  $ wi ph (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3o ps th et) (w3o ch ta ze)) $;

--| Deduction joining 3 equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3anbi123d (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a ps th et) (w3a ch ta ze)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining and adding a conjunct to equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi12d (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a ps th et) (w3a ch ta et)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining and adding a conjunct to equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi13d (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a ps et th) (w3a ch et ta)) $;

--| Deduction conjoining and adding a conjunct to equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi23d (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a et ps th) (w3a et ch ta)) $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi1d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a ps th ta) (w3a ch th ta)) $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi2d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a th ps ta) (w3a th ch ta)) $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
theorem _3anbi3d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (w3a th ta ps) (w3a th ta ch)) $;

--| Deduction joining 3 implications to form implication of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem _3anim123d (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3a ps th et) (w3a ch ta ze)) $;

--| Deduction joining 3 implications to form implication of disjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1997.)
theorem _3orim123d (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3o ps th et) (w3o ch ta ze)) $;

--| Disjoin antecedents and consequents of three premises. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jul-2026.)
theorem _3orim123da (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (w3o ps th et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph et) ze $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o ch ta ze) $;

--| Rearrangement of 6 conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1995.)
theorem an6 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wb (wa (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a th ta et)) (w3a (wa ph th) (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Analogue of ~ an4 for triple conjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3an6 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wb (w3a (wa ph ps) (wa ch th) (wa ta et)) (wa (w3a ph ch ta) (w3a ps th et)) $;

--| Analogue of ~ or4 for triple conjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Mar-2011.)
theorem _3or6 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wb (w3o (wo ph ps) (wo ch th) (wo ta et)) (wo (w3o ph ch ta) (w3o ps th et)) $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 21-Nov-1994.)
theorem mp3an1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ch) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 21-Nov-1994.)
theorem mp3an2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 21-Nov-1994.)
theorem mp3an3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jul-2005.)
theorem mp3an12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ch th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jul-2005.)
theorem mp3an13 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ps th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jul-2005.)
theorem mp3an23 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jul-2005.)
theorem mp3an1i (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3a ps ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch th) ta) $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem mp3anl1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ch) th) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem mp3anl2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem mp3anl3 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) th) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2006.)
theorem mp3anr1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ch th)) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-2006.)
theorem mp3anr2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps th)) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2007.)
theorem mp3anr3 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) ta $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem mp3an (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.)
theorem mpd3an3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| An inference based on modus ponens. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.)
theorem mpd3an23 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| A deduction based on modus ponens. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem mp3and (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3a ps ch th) ta) $ >
  $ wi ph ta $;

--| ~ mp3an with antecedents in standard conjunction form and with one hypothesis an implication. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem mp3an12i (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ta $ >
  $ wi ch ta $;

--| ~ mp3an with antecedents in standard conjunction form and with two hypotheses which are implications. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem mp3an2i (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ps th $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ch th) ta $ >
  $ wi ps ta $;

--| ~ mp3an with antecedents in standard conjunction form and with two hypotheses which are implications. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem mp3an3an (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ch ta) et $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) et $;

--| An elimination deduction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 17-Oct-2017.)
theorem mp3an2ani (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ch ta) et $ >
  $ wi (wa ps th) et $;

--| Infer implication from a logical equivalence. Similar to ~ biimpa . (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem biimp3a (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Infer implication from a logical equivalence. Similar to ~ biimpar . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2009.)
theorem biimp3ar (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ch $;

--| Inference that undistributes a triple conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Apr-2007.)
theorem _3anandis (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (w3a (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch) (wa ph th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ta $;

--| Inference that undistributes a triple conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2006.)
theorem _3anandirs (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (w3a (wa ph th) (wa ps th) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ta $;

--| Deduction for elimination by cases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Aug-2009.)
theorem ecase13d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o ch ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction for elimination by cases. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-1994.)
theorem ecase23d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduction for elimination by cases. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jul-2026.)
theorem ecase33d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Inference for elimination by cases. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jul-2005.)
theorem _3ecase (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) th $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) th $ >
  $ wi (wn ch) th $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ th $;

--| A disjunction is equivalent to a threefold disjunction with single falsehood, analogous to ~ biorf . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Sep-2017.)
theorem _3bior1fd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wo ch ps) (w3o th ch ps)) $;

--| A disjunction is equivalent to a threefold disjunction with single falsehood of a conjunction. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Sep-2017.)
theorem _3bior1fand (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wo ch ps) (w3o (wa th ta) ch ps)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its threefold disjunction with double falsehood, analogous to ~ biorf . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Sep-2017.)
theorem _3bior2fd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (w3o th ch ps)) $;

--| A conjunction is equivalent to a threefold conjunction with single truth, analogous to ~ biantrud . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-Sep-2017.)
theorem _3biant1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ch ps) (w3a th ch ps)) $;

--| Introduction of a triple conjunct inside a contradiction. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem intn3an1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (w3a ps ch th)) $;

--| Introduction of a triple conjunct inside a contradiction. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem intn3an2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (w3a ch ps th)) $;

--| Introduction of a triple conjunct inside a contradiction. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem intn3an3d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (w3a ch th ps)) $;

--| Distribution of conjunction over threefold conjunction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Apr-2019.)
theorem an3andi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) (w3a (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch) (wa ph th)) $;

--| Rearrange a 9-fold conjunction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2024.)
theorem an33rean (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh: wff):
  $ wb (w3a (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a th ta et) (w3a ze si rh)) (wa (w3a ph ta rh) (w3a (wa ps th) (wa et si) (wa ch ze))) $;

--| Partial elimination of a triple disjunction by denial of a disjunct. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 8-Oct-2025.)
theorem _3orel2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wi (w3o ph ps ch) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Partial elimination of a triple disjunction by denial of a disjunct. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Mar-2011.)
theorem _3orel3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ch) (wi (w3o ph ps ch) (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Elimination of two disjuncts in a triple disjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem _3orel13 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (wn ch)) (wi (w3o ph ps ch) ps) $;

--| Triple negated disjunction introduction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem _3pm3_2ni (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wn (w3o ph ps ch) $;

--| Inference exchanging the last antecedent with the second one. See also ~ an32s . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2025.)
theorem an42ds (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph th) ch) ps) ta $;

--| Extend wff definition to include alternative denial ("nand").
term wnan (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Define incompatibility, or alternative denial ("not-and" or "nand"). See ~ dfnan2 for an alternative. This is also called the Sheffer stroke, represented by a vertical bar, but we use a different symbol to avoid ambiguity with other uses of the vertical bar. In the second edition of Principia Mathematica (1927), Russell and Whitehead used the Sheffer stroke and suggested it as a replacement for the "or" and "not" operations of the first edition. However, in practice, "or" and "not" are more widely used. After we define the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ) and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. -/\ T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ trunantru ), ` ( ( T. -/\ F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ trunanfal ), ` ( ( F. -/\ T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falnantru ), and ` ( ( F. -/\ F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falnanfal ). Contrast with ` /\ ` ( ~ df-an ), ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ), ` -> ` ( ~ wi ), and ` \/_ ` ( ~ df-xor ). (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.)
axiom df_nan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph ps) (wn (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Conjunction in terms of alternative denial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem nanan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wn (wnan ph ps)) $;

--| Alternative denial in terms of our primitive connectives (implication and negation). (Contributed by WL, 26-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfnan2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph ps) (wi ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Alternative denial in terms of disjunction and negation. This explains the name "alternative denial". (Contributed by BJ, 19-Oct-2022.)
theorem nanor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph ps) (wo (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Alternative denial is commutative. Remark: alternative denial is not associative, see ~ nanass . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Jun-2020.)
theorem nancom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph ps) (wnan ps ph) $;

--| Nested alternative denials. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Jun-2020.)
theorem nannan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph (wnan ps ch)) (wi ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Implication in terms of alternative denial. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.)
theorem nanim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wnan ph (wnan ps ps)) $;

--| Negation in terms of alternative denial. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.) Use ~ dfnan2 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 26-Jun-2020.)
theorem nannot (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wnan ph ph) $;

--| Biconditional in terms of alternative denial. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2020.)
theorem nanbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wnan (wnan ph ps) (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wnan ps ps))) $;

--| Introduce a right anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 1-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2020.)
theorem nanbi1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wnan ph ch) (wnan ps ch)) $;

--| Introduce a left anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 1-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wnan ch ph) (wnan ch ps)) $;

--| Join two logical equivalences with anti-conjunction. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wb ph ps) (wb ch th)) (wb (wnan ph ch) (wnan ps th)) $;

--| Introduce a right anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wnan ph ch) (wnan ps ch) $;

--| Introduce a left anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wnan ch ph) (wnan ch ps) $;

--| Join two logical equivalences with anti-conjunction. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wnan ph ch) (wnan ps th) $;

--| Introduce a right anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnan ps th) (wnan ch th)) $;

--| Introduce a left anti-conjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by SF, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnan th ps) (wnan th ch)) $;

--| Join two logical equivalences with anti-conjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nanbi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnan ps th) (wnan ch ta)) $;

--| A characterization of when an expression involving alternative denials associates. Remark: alternative denial is commutative, see ~ nancom . (Contributed by Richard Penner, 29-Feb-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2022.)
theorem nanass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ch) (wb (wnan (wnan ph ps) ch) (wnan ph (wnan ps ch))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include exclusive disjunction ("xor").
term wxo (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Define exclusive disjunction (logical "xor"). Return true if either the left or right, but not both, are true. After we define the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ) and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. \/_ T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ truxortru ), ` ( ( T. \/_ F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ truxorfal ), ` ( ( F. \/_ T. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falxortru ), and ` ( ( F. \/_ F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ falxorfal ). Contrast with ` /\ ` ( ~ df-an ), ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ), ` -> ` ( ~ wi ), and ` -/\ ` ( ~ df-nan ). (Contributed by FL, 22-Nov-2010.)
axiom df_xor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo ph ps) (wn (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Two ways to write XNOR (exclusive not-or). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem xnor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wxo ph ps)) $;

--| The connector ` \/_ ` is commutative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2024.)
theorem xorcom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo ph ps) (wxo ps ph) $;

--| The connector ` \/_ ` is associative. (Contributed by FL, 22-Nov-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 8-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem xorass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wxo (wxo ph ps) ch) (wxo ph (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| This tautology shows that xor is really exclusive. (Contributed by FL, 22-Nov-2010.)
theorem excxor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo ph ps) (wo (wa ph (wn ps)) (wa (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| Two ways to express "exclusive or". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem xor2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo ph ps) (wa (wo ph ps) (wn (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Exclusive disjunction implies disjunction ("XOR implies OR"). (Contributed by BJ, 19-Apr-2019.)
theorem xoror (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wxo ph ps) (wo ph ps) $;

--| Exclusive disjunction implies alternative denial ("XOR implies NAND"). (Contributed by BJ, 19-Apr-2019.)
theorem xornan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wxo ph ps) (wn (wa ph ps)) $;

--| XOR implies NAND (written with the ` -/\ ` connector). (Contributed by BJ, 19-Apr-2019.)
theorem xornan2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wxo ph ps) (wnan ph ps) $;

--| The connector ` \/_ ` is negated under negation of one argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2020.)
theorem xorneg2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo ph (wn ps)) (wn (wxo ph ps)) $;

--| The connector ` \/_ ` is negated under negation of one argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2020.)
theorem xorneg1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo (wn ph) ps) (wn (wxo ph ps)) $;

--| The connector ` \/_ ` is unchanged under negation of both arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem xorneg (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wxo (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wxo ph ps) $;

--| Equality property for exclusive disjunction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2024.)
theorem xorbi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wxo ph ch) (wxo ps th) $;

--| Equality property for exclusive disjunction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem xorbi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wxo ps th) (wxo ch ta)) $;

--| Conjunction distributes over exclusive-or. In intuitionistic logic this assertion is also true, even though ~ xordi does not necessarily hold, in part because the usual definition of xor is subtly different in intuitionistic logic. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Oct-2018.)
theorem anxordi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wxo ps ch)) (wxo (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Exclusive-or variant of the law of the excluded middle ( ~ exmid ). This statement is ancient, going back to at least Stoic logic. This statement does not necessarily hold in intuitionistic logic. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 23-Feb-2019.)
theorem xorexmid (ph: wff):
  $ wxo ph (wn ph) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include joint denial ("nor").
term wnor (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Define joint denial ("not-or" or "nor"). After we define the constant true ` T. ` ( ~ df-tru ) and the constant false ` F. ` ( ~ df-fal ), we will be able to prove these truth table values: ` ( ( T. -\/ T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ trunortru ), ` ( ( T. -\/ F. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ trunorfal ), ` ( ( F. -\/ T. ) <-> F. ) ` ( ~ falnortru ), and ` ( ( F. -\/ F. ) <-> T. ) ` ( ~ falnorfal ). Contrast with ` /\ ` ( ~ df-an ), ` \/ ` ( ~ df-or ), ` -> ` ( ~ wi ), ` -/\ ` ( ~ df-nan ), and ` \/_ ` ( ~ df-xor ). (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.)
axiom df_nor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnor ph ps) (wn (wo ph ps)) $;

--| The connector ` -\/ ` is commutative. (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Apr-2024.)
theorem norcom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnor ph ps) (wnor ps ph) $;

--| ` -. ` is expressible via ` -\/ ` . (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2023.)
theorem nornot (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wnor ph ph) $;

--| ` /\ ` is expressible via ` -\/ ` . (Contributed by Remi, 26-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2023.)
theorem noran (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wnor (wnor ph ph) (wnor ps ps)) $;

--| ` \/ ` is expressible via ` -\/ ` . (Contributed by Remi, 26-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2023.)
theorem noror (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph ps) (wnor (wnor ph ps) (wnor ph ps)) $;

--| This lemma shows the equivalence of two expressions, used in ~ norass . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 18-Dec-2023.)
theorem norasslem1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo ph ps) ch) (wo (wnor ph ps) ch) $;

--| This lemma specializes ~ biimt suitably for the proof of ~ norass . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 18-Dec-2023.)
theorem norasslem2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wi (wo ph ch) ps)) $;

--| This lemma specializes ~ biorf suitably for the proof of ~ norass . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 18-Dec-2023.)
theorem norasslem3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (wi ps ch) (wi (wo ph ps) ch)) $;

--| A characterization of when an expression involving joint denials associates. This is identical to the case when alternative denial is associative, see ~ nanass . Remark: Like alternative denial, joint denial is also commutative, see ~ norcom . (Contributed by RP, 29-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Dec-2023.)
theorem norass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ch) (wb (wnor (wnor ph ps) ch) (wnor ph (wnor ps ch))) $;

pure sort setvar;

--| Extend wff definition to include the universal quantifier ("for all"). ` A. x ph ` is read " ` ph ` (phi) is true for all ` x ` ". Typically, in its final application ` ph ` would be replaced with a wff containing a (free) occurrence of the variable ` x ` , for example ` x = y ` . In a universe with a finite number of objects, "for all" is equivalent to a big conjunction (AND) with one wff for each possible case of ` x ` . When the universe is infinite (as with set theory), such a propositional-calculus equivalent is not possible because an infinitely long formula has no meaning, but conceptually the idea is the same.
term wal {x: setvar} (ph: wff x): wff;

sort class;

--| This syntax construction states that a variable ` x ` , which has been declared to be a setvar variable by $f statement vx, is also a class expression. This can be justified informally as follows. We know that the class builder ` { y | y e. x } ` is a class by ~ cab . Since (when ` y ` is distinct from ` x ` ) we have ` x = { y | y e. x } ` by ~ cvjust , we can argue that the syntax " ` class x ` " can be viewed as an abbreviation for " ` class { y | y e. x } ` ". See the discussion under the definition of class in [Jech] p. 4 showing that "Every set can be considered to be a class". While it is tempting and perhaps occasionally useful to view ~ cv as a "type conversion" from a setvar variable to a class variable, keep in mind that ~ cv is intrinsically no different from any other class-building syntax such as ~ cab , ~ cun , or ~ c0 . For a general discussion of the theory of classes and the role of ~ cv , see ~ mmset.html#class . (The description above applies to set theory, not predicate calculus. The purpose of introducing ` class x ` here, and not in set theory where it belongs, is to allow to express, i.e., "prove", the ~ weq of predicate calculus from the ~ wceq of set theory, so that we do not overload the ` = ` connective with two syntax definitions. This is done to prevent ambiguity that would complicate some Metamath parsers.)
term cv (x1: setvar): class;

--| Extend wff definition to include class equality. For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . (The purpose of introducing ` wff A = B ` here, and not in set theory where it belongs, is to allow to express, i.e., "prove", the ~ weq of predicate calculus in terms of the ~ wceq of set theory, so that we do not "overload" the ` = ` connective with two syntax definitions. This is done to prevent ambiguity that would complicate some Metamath parsers. For example, some parsers - although not the Metamath program - stumble on the fact that the ` = ` in ` x = y ` could be the ` = ` of either ~ weq or ~ wceq , although mathematically it makes no difference. The class variables ` A ` and ` B ` are introduced temporarily for the purpose of this definition but otherwise not used in predicate calculus. See ~ df-cleq for more information on the set theory usage of ~ wceq .)
term wceq (A B: class): wff;

--| The constant ` T. ` is a wff.
term wtru: wff;

--| Soundness justification theorem for ~ df-tru . Instance of ~ monothetic . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2013.) (Revised by NM, 11-Jul-2019.)
theorem trujust {x2 y: setvar}:
  $ wb (wi (wal x2 (wceq (cv x2) (cv x2))) (wal x2 (wceq (cv x2) (cv x2)))) (wi (wal y (wceq (cv y) (cv y))) (wal y (wceq (cv y) (cv y)))) $;

--| Definition of the truth value "true", or "verum", denoted by ` T. ` . In this definition, an instance of ~ id is used as the definiens, although any tautology, such as an axiom, can be used in its place. This particular instance of ~ id was chosen so this definition can be checked by the same algorithm that is used for predicate calculus. This definition should be referenced directly only by ~ tru , and other proofs should use ~ tru instead of this definition, since there are many alternate ways to define ` T. ` . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Oct-2010.) (Revised by NM, 11-Jul-2019.) Use ~ tru instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_tru {x2: setvar}:
  $ wb wtru (wi (wal x2 (wceq (cv x2) (cv x2))) (wal x2 (wceq (cv x2) (cv x2)))) $;

--| The truth value ` T. ` is provable. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem tru:
  $ wtru $;

--| An alternate definition of "true" (see comment of ~ df-tru ). The associated justification theorem is ~ monothetic . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Oct-2010.) (Revised by BJ, 12-Jul-2019.) Use ~ tru instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dftru2 (ph: wff):
  $ wb wtru (wi ph ph) $;

--| A proposition is equivalent to it being implied by ` T. ` . Closed form of ~ mptru . Dual of ~ dfnot . It is to ~ tbtru what ~ a1bi is to ~ tbt . (Contributed by BJ, 26-Oct-2019.)
theorem trut (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph (wi wtru ph) $;

--| Eliminate ` T. ` as an antecedent. A proposition implied by ` T. ` is true. This is modus ponens ~ ax-mp when the minor hypothesis is ` T. ` (which holds by ~ tru ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem mptru (ph: wff):
  $ wi wtru ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| A proposition is equivalent to itself being equivalent to ` T. ` . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 14-Aug-2011.)
theorem tbtru (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb ph wtru) $;

--| A theorem is equivalent to truth. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem bitru (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb ph wtru $;

--| Anything implies ` T. ` . Dual statement of ~ falim . Deduction form of ~ tru . Note on naming: in 2022, the theorem now known as ~ mptru was renamed from trud so if you are reading documentation written before that time, references to trud refer to what is now ~ mptru . (Contributed by FL, 20-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 1-Aug-2011.)
theorem trud (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph wtru $;

--| True can be removed from a conjunction. (Contributed by FL, 20-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Jul-2019.)
theorem truan (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wa wtru ph) ph $;

--| The constant ` F. ` is a wff.
term wfal: wff;

--| Definition of the truth value "false", or "falsum", denoted by ` F. ` . See also ~ df-tru . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
axiom df_fal:
  $ wb wfal (wn wtru) $;

--| The truth value ` F. ` is refutable. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 11-Mar-2012.)
theorem fal:
  $ wn wfal $;

--| The negation of a proposition is equivalent to itself being equivalent to ` F. ` . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 14-Aug-2011.)
theorem nbfal (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wb ph wfal) $;

--| A contradiction is equivalent to falsehood. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem bifal (ph: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ph wfal $;

--| The truth value ` F. ` implies anything. Also called the "principle of explosion", or "ex falso [[sequitur]] quodlibet" (Latin for "from falsehood, anything [[follows]]"). Dual statement of ~ trud . (Contributed by FL, 20-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 1-Aug-2011.)
theorem falim (ph: wff):
  $ wi wfal ph $;

--| The truth value ` F. ` implies anything. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem falimd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph wfal) ps $;

--| Given falsum ` F. ` , we can define the negation of a wff ` ph ` as the statement that ` F. ` follows from assuming ` ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Jul-2019.)
theorem dfnot (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wi ph wfal) $;

--| Negation introduction rule from natural deduction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem inegd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) wfal $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Deduction based on reductio ad absurdum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem efald (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) wfal $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| If a wff and its negation are provable, then falsum is provable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem pm2_21fal (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph wfal $;

--| A ` -> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) An alternate proof is possible using ~ trud instead of ~ id but the principle of identity ~ id is more basic, and the present proof indicates that the result still holds in relevance logic. (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem truimtru:
  $ wb (wi wtru wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` -> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem truimfal:
  $ wb (wi wtru wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` -> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) An alternate proof is possible using ~ falim instead of ~ trud but the present proof using ~ trud emphasizes that the result does not require the principle of explosion. (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem falimtru:
  $ wb (wi wfal wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` -> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) An alternate proof is possible using ~ falim instead of ~ id but the present proof using ~ id emphasizes that the result does not require the principle of explosion. (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem falimfal:
  $ wb (wi wfal wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` -. ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem nottru:
  $ wb (wn wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` -. ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem notfal:
  $ wb (wn wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` <-> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem trubitru:
  $ wb (wb wtru wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` <-> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2020.)
theorem falbitru:
  $ wb (wb wfal wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` <-> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2020.)
theorem trubifal:
  $ wb (wb wtru wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` <-> ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem falbifal:
  $ wb (wb wfal wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` /\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem truantru:
  $ wb (wa wtru wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` /\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem truanfal:
  $ wb (wa wtru wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` /\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem falantru:
  $ wb (wa wfal wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` /\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem falanfal:
  $ wb (wa wfal wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` \/ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem truortru:
  $ wb (wo wtru wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` \/ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem truorfal:
  $ wb (wo wtru wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` \/ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.)
theorem falortru:
  $ wb (wo wfal wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` \/ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem falorfal:
  $ wb (wo wfal wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` -/\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem trunantru:
  $ wb (wnan wtru wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` -/\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 23-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2020.)
theorem trunanfal:
  $ wb (wnan wtru wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` -/\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 23-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem falnantru:
  $ wb (wnan wfal wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` -/\ ` identity. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 22-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem falnanfal:
  $ wb (wnan wfal wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` \/_ ` identity. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-May-2015.)
theorem truxortru:
  $ wb (wxo wtru wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` \/_ ` identity. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-May-2015.)
theorem truxorfal:
  $ wb (wxo wtru wfal) wtru $;

--| A ` \/_ ` identity. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 9-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2020.)
theorem falxortru:
  $ wb (wxo wfal wtru) wtru $;

--| A ` \/_ ` identity. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 9-May-2015.)
theorem falxorfal:
  $ wb (wxo wfal wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` -\/ ` identity. (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Dec-2023.)
theorem trunortru:
  $ wb (wnor wtru wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` -\/ ` identity. (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Dec-2023.)
theorem trunorfal:
  $ wb (wnor wtru wfal) wfal $;

--| A ` -\/ ` identity. (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.)
theorem falnortru:
  $ wb (wnor wfal wtru) wfal $;

--| A ` -\/ ` identity. (Contributed by Remi, 25-Oct-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Dec-2023.)
theorem falnorfal:
  $ wb (wnor wfal wfal) wtru $;

--| Syntax for the "sum" output of the full adder. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
term whad (ph ps ch: wff): wff;

--| Definition of the "sum" output of the full adder (triple exclusive disjunction, or XOR3, or testing whether an odd number of parameters are true). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_had (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (wxo (wxo ph ps) ch) $;

--| Equality theorem for the adder sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadbi123d (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (whad ps th et) (whad ch ta ze)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the adder sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadbi123i (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb ta et $ >
  $ wb (whad ph ch ta) (whad ps th et) $;

--| Associative law for the adder sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (wxo ph (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| The adder sum is the same as the triple biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (wb (wb ph ps) ch) $;

--| Commutative law for the adder sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Dec-2023.)
theorem hadcoma (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (whad ps ph ch) $;

--| Commutative law for the adders sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadcomb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (whad ph ch ps) $;

--| Rotation law for the adder sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem hadrot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (whad ps ch ph) $;

--| The adder sum distributes over negation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem hadnot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn (whad ph ps ch)) (whad (wn ph) (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| If the first input is true, then the adder sum is equivalent to the biconditionality of the other two inputs, and conversely. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.) Strengthen to a biconditional. (Revised by BJ, 10-Aug-2026.)
theorem had1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb (whad ph ps ch) (wb ps ch)) $;

--| If the first input is false, then the adder sum is equivalent to the exclusive disjunction of the other two inputs, and conversely. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2020.) Strengthen to a biconditional. (Revised by BJ, 10-Aug-2026.)
theorem had0 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wb (whad ph ps ch) (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ had1 as of 10-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem had1OLD (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (whad ph ps ch) (wb ps ch)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ had0 as of 10-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem had0OLD (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (whad ph ps ch) (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| The value of the adder sum is, if the first input is true, the biconditionality, and if the first input is false, the exclusive disjunction, of the other two inputs. (Contributed by BJ, 11-Aug-2020.)
theorem hadifp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (wif ph (wb ps ch) (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hadifp as of 10-Aug-2026. (Contributed by BJ, 11-Aug-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hadifpOLD (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (whad ph ps ch) (wif ph (wb ps ch) (wxo ps ch)) $;

--| Syntax for the "carry" output of the full adder. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
term wcad (ph ps ch: wff): wff;

--| Definition of the "carry" output of the full adder. It is true when at least two arguments are true, so it is equal to the "majority" function on three variables. See ~ cador and ~ cadan for alternate definitions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_cad (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ch (wxo ph ps))) $;

--| The adder carry in disjunctive normal form. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem cador (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (w3o (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| The adder carry in conjunctive normal form. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Sep-2018.)
theorem cadan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (w3a (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the adder carry. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cadbi123d (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcad ps th et) (wcad ch ta ze)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the adder carry. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cadbi123i (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb ta et $ >
  $ wb (wcad ph ch ta) (wcad ps th et) $;

--| Commutative law for the adder carry. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cadcoma (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wcad ps ph ch) $;

--| Commutative law for the adder carry. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem cadcomb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wcad ph ch ps) $;

--| Rotation law for the adder carry. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cadrot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wcad ps ch ph) $;

--| The adder carry distributes over negation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem cadnot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wcad ph ps ch)) (wcad (wn ph) (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| If (at least) two inputs are true, then the adder carry is true. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cad11 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcad ph ps ch) $;

--| If one input is true, then the adder carry is true exactly when at least one of the other two inputs is true. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem cad1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ch (wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wo ph ps)) $;

--| If one input is false, then the adder carry is true exactly when both of the other two inputs are true. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Sep-2024.)
theorem cad0 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ch) (wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wa ph ps)) $;

--| The value of the carry is, if the input carry is true, the disjunction, and if the input carry is false, the conjunction, of the other two inputs. (Contributed by BJ, 8-Oct-2019.)
theorem cadifp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wcad ph ps ch) (wif ch (wo ph ps) (wa ph ps)) $;

--| The adder carry is true as soon as its first two inputs are the truth constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem cadtru (ph: wff):
  $ wcad wtru wtru ph $;

--| A single axiom for minimal implicational calculus, due to Meredith. Other single axioms of the same length are known, but it is thought to be the minimal length. Among single axioms of this length, it is the one with simplest antecedents (i.e., in the corresponding ordering of binary trees which first compares left subtrees, it is the first one). (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.)
theorem minimp (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wi (wi th ps) (wi ch ta)) (wi ps ta))) $;

--| Derivation of Syll-Simp ( ~ jarr ) from ~ ax-mp and ~ minimp . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem minimp_syllsimp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-1 from ~ ax-mp and ~ minimp . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem minimp_ax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Derivation of a commuted form of ~ ax-2 from ~ ax-mp and ~ minimp . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem minimp_ax2c (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ ax-mp and ~ minimp . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem minimp_ax2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ pm2.43 (also called "hilbert" or W) from ~ ax-mp and ~ minimp . It uses the classical derivation from ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 written DD22D21 in D-notation (see head comment for an explanation) and shortens the proof using ~ mp2 (which only requires ~ ax-mp ). (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem minimp_pm2_43 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| The shortest single axiom for implicational calculus, due to Lukasiewicz. It is now known to be the _unique_ shortest axiom. The axiom is proved here starting from { ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 and ~ peirce }. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 18-Jul-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wi ch ph) (wi th ph)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step4 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 4 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCpqpCsp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) (wi ch ph) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step8 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ ax-1 ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 8 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCsqpCqp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-ax1 ( ~ ax-1 ) from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . Lukasiewicz was used to construct this proof. Every formula corresponding to a detachment step was converted to its corresponding Metamath formula. mmj2 was used to unify each formula using ~ ax-mp , which in turn produced this proof. With extremely high confidence, this result shows that the Lukasiewicz proof of ~ ax-1 (step 27) is correct and that Metamath correctly verifies the proof. The same comments apply to the proofs that follow this one. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_ax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step15 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 15 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCrqCspCCrpCsp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step15 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch th)) (wi (wi ph th) (wi ch th)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step18 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 18 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCCrpCspCCCpqrtCuCCCpqrt' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step18 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch ps)) (wi (wi (wi ps th) ph) ta)) (wi et (wi (wi (wi ps th) ph) ta)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step19 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 19 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCCspqCrpCCCpqrCsp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step19 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi th ps)) (wi (wi (wi ps ch) th) (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step20 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 20 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCCrppCspCCCpqrCsp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step20 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) (wi ch ps)) (wi (wi (wi ps th) ph) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step21 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in the proof of ~ imim1 from ~ impsingle . It is Step 21 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCCCprqqCCqrCpr' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step21 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) ch) (wi (wi ch ps) (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step22 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in proofs of ~ imim1 and ~ peirce from ~ impsingle . It is Step 22 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'Cpp' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step22 (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph ph $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-step25 from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is used as a lemma in the proof of ~ imim1 from ~ impsingle . It is Step 25 in Lukasiewicz, where it appears as 'CCpqCCCprqq' using parenthesis-free prefix notation. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_step25 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) ps) ps) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-imim1 ( ~ imim1 ) from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is step 29 in Lukasiewicz. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_imim1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of impsingle-peirce ( ~ peirce ) from ~ ax-mp and ~ impsingle . It is step 28 in Lukasiewicz. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 2-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem impsingle_peirce (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-2 from the Tarski-Bernays axiom set { ~ ax-1 , ~ imim1 , ~ peirce }. Note that no inference rule other than ~ ax-mp is used in this proof. This proof establishes a circle of equivalence: From { ~ impsingle }, { ~ ax-1 , ~ imim1 , ~ peirce } was proved. From { ~ ax-1 , ~ imim1 , ~ peirce }, { ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 and ~ peirce } was proved. From { ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 and ~ peirce }, { ~ impsingle } was proved. Therefore, the theorems that can be proved by selecting any one of these three distinct axiom sets must be equivalent. Prover9 and mmj2 were used to help constuct this proof. (Contributed by Larry Lesyna and Jeffrey P. Machado, 1-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tarski_bernays_ax2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Carew Meredith's sole axiom for propositional calculus. This amazing formula is thought to be the shortest possible single axiom for propositional calculus with inference rule ~ ax-mp , where negation and implication are primitive. Here we prove Meredith's axiom from ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 , and ~ ax-3 . Then from it we derive the Lukasiewicz axioms ~ luk-1 , ~ luk-2 , and ~ luk-3 . Using these we finally rederive our axioms as ~ ax1 , ~ ax2 , and ~ ax3 , thus proving the equivalence of all three systems. C. A. Meredith, "Single Axioms for the Systems (C,N), (C,O) and (A,N) of the Two-Valued Propositional Calculus", _The Journal of Computing Systems_ vol. 1 (1953), pp. 155-164. Meredith claimed to be close to a proof that this axiom is the shortest possible, but the proof was apparently never completed. An obscure Irish lecturer, Meredith (1904-1976) became enamored with logic somewhat late in life after attending talks by Lukasiewicz and produced many remarkable results such as this axiom. From his obituary: "He did logic whenever time and opportunity presented themselves, and he did it on whatever materials came to hand: in a pub, his favored pint of porter within reach, he would use the inside of cigarette packs to write proofs for logical colleagues." (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-May-2013.)
theorem meredith (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ch) (wn th))) ch) ta) (wi (wi ta ph) (wi th ph)) $;

--| Step 3 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (The step numbers refer to Meredith's original paper.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem1 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ch (wi (wn ph) ps)) ta) (wi ph ta) $;

--| Step 4 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem2 (ph ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ph) ch) (wi th ch) $;

--| Step 7 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ps ch) ph) (wi ch ph) $;

--| Step 8 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem4 (ph th ta: wff):
  $ wi ta (wi (wi ta ph) (wi th ph)) $;

--| Step 11 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wn (wn ph)) ps) $;

--| Step 12 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem6 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ch (wi (wi (wi ps ch) ph) (wi th ph)) $;

--| Between steps 14 and 15 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem7 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi (wi ps ch) th) (wi (wi (wi ch ta) (wi (wn th) (wn ps))) th)) $;

--| Step 15 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem8 (ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ps ch) th) (wi (wi (wi ch ta) (wi (wn th) (wn ps))) th) $;

--| Step 18 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch (wi th (wi ps ta)))) (wi et (wi ch (wi th (wi ps ta)))) $;

--| Step 19 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem10 (ph ps th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi th (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Step 20 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem11 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Step 28 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem12 (ph ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi th (wi (wn (wn ch)) ch)) ph) ph $;

--| Step 35 of Meredith's proof of Lukasiewicz axioms from his sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merlem13 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi th (wi (wn (wn ch)) ch)) (wn (wn ph))) ps) $;

--| 1 of 3 axioms for propositional calculus due to Lukasiewicz, derived from Meredith's sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luk_1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| 2 of 3 axioms for propositional calculus due to Lukasiewicz, derived from Meredith's sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luk_2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| 3 of 3 axioms for propositional calculus due to Lukasiewicz, derived from Meredith's sole axiom. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luk_3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 23-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ps)) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) th) (wi ps th)) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) ch) (wi th ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph) ps) ps $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem6 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive standard propositional axioms from Lukasiewicz'. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem luklem8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ch ph) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Standard propositional axiom derived from Lukasiewicz axioms. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Standard propositional axiom derived from Lukasiewicz axioms. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Standard propositional axiom derived from Lukasiewicz axioms. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2002.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wi ps ph) $;

--| This theorem "defines" implication in terms of 'nand'. Analogous to ~ nanim . In a pure (standalone) treatment of Nicod's axiom, this theorem would be changed to a definition ($a statement). (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_dfim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan (wnan ph (wnan ps ps)) (wi ph ps)) (wnan (wnan (wnan ph (wnan ps ps)) (wnan ph (wnan ps ps))) (wnan (wi ph ps) (wi ph ps))) $;

--| This theorem "defines" negation in terms of 'nand'. Analogous to ~ nannot . In a pure (standalone) treatment of Nicod's axiom, this theorem would be changed to a definition ($a statement). (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_dfneg (ph: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wn ph)) (wnan (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wnan ph ph)) (wnan (wn ph) (wn ph))) $;

--| Derive Nicod's rule of modus ponens using 'nand', from the standard one. Although the major and minor premise together also imply ` ch ` , this form is necessary for useful derivations from ~ nic-ax . In a pure (standalone) treatment of Nicod's axiom, this theorem would be changed to an axiom ($a statement). (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_mp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ps) $ >
  $ ps $;

--| A direct proof of ~ nic-mp . (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_mpALT (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ps) $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Nicod's axiom derived from the standard ones. See _Introduction to Mathematical Philosophy_ by B. Russell, p. 152. Like ~ meredith , the usual axioms can be derived from this and vice versa. Unlike ~ meredith , Nicod uses a different connective ('nand'), so another form of modus ponens must be used in proofs, e.g., ` { ` ~ nic-ax , ~ nic-mp ` } ` is equivalent to ` { ` ~ luk-1 , ~ luk-2 , ~ luk-3 , ~ ax-mp ` } ` . In a pure (standalone) treatment of Nicod's axiom, this theorem would be changed to an axiom ($a statement). (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 19-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_ax (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) (wnan (wnan ta (wnan ta ta)) (wnan (wnan th ch) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)))) $;

--| A direct proof of ~ nic-ax . (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_axALT (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) (wnan (wnan ta (wnan ta ta)) (wnan (wnan th ch) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)))) $;

--| Inference for ~ nic-mp using ~ nic-ax as major premise. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_imp (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ps) $ >
  $ wnan (wnan th ch) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nic-id . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_idlem1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan th (wnan ta (wnan ta ta))) (wnan (wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) th) (wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) th)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nic-id . Inference used by ~ nic-id . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_idlem2 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wnan et (wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) th) $ >
  $ wnan (wnan th (wnan ta (wnan ta ta))) et $;

--| Theorem ~ id expressed with ` -/\ ` . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_id (ta: wff):
  $ wnan ta (wnan ta ta) $;

--| The connector ` -/\ ` is symmetric. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_swap (ph th: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan th ph) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)) $;

--| Inference version of ~ nic-swap . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_isw1 (ph th: wff):
  $ wnan th ph $ >
  $ wnan ph th $;

--| Inference for swapping nested terms. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_isw2 (ph ps th: wff):
  $ wnan ps (wnan th ph) $ >
  $ wnan ps (wnan ph th) $;

--| Inference version of ~ nic-imp using right-handed term. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_iimp1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ps) $ >
  $ wnan th ch $ >
  $ wnan th ph $;

--| Inference version of ~ nic-imp using left-handed term. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_iimp2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph ps) (wnan ch ch) $ >
  $ wnan th ph $ >
  $ wnan th (wnan ch ch) $;

--| Inference to remove the trailing term. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_idel (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ps) $ >
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ch) $;

--| Chained inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_ich (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wnan ph (wnan ps ps) $ >
  $ wnan ps (wnan ch ch) $ >
  $ wnan ph (wnan ch ch) $;

--| Double the terms. Since doubling is the same as negation, this can be viewed as a contraposition inference. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_idbl (ph ps: wff):
  $ wnan ph (wnan ps ps) $ >
  $ wnan (wnan ps ps) (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wnan ph ph)) $;

--| Biconditional justification from Nicod's axiom. For nic-* definitions, the biconditional connective is not used. Instead, definitions are made based on this form. ~ nic-bi1 and ~ nic-bi2 are used to convert the definitions into usable theorems about one side of the implication. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_bijust (ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ta ta) (wnan (wnan ta ta) (wnan ta ta)) $;

--| Inference to extract one side of an implication from a definition. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_bi1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph ps) (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wnan ps ps)) $ >
  $ wnan ph (wnan ps ps) $;

--| Inference to extract the other side of an implication from a 'biconditional' definition. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_bi2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph ps) (wnan (wnan ph ph) (wnan ps ps)) $ >
  $ wnan ps (wnan ph ph) $;

--| Derive the standard modus ponens from ~ nic-mp . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_stdmp (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Proof of ~ luk-1 from ~ nic-ax and ~ nic-mp (and Definitions ~ nic-dfim and ~ nic-dfneg ). Note that the standard axioms ~ ax-1 , ~ ax-2 , and ~ ax-3 are proved from the Lukasiewicz axioms by Theorems ~ ax1 , ~ ax2 , and ~ ax3 . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_luk1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Proof of ~ luk-2 from ~ nic-ax and ~ nic-mp . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_luk2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| Proof of ~ luk-3 from ~ nic-ax and ~ nic-mp . (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 18-Nov-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nic_luk3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| This alternative axiom for propositional calculus using the Sheffer Stroke was discovered by Lukasiewicz in his Selected Works. It improves on Nicod's axiom by reducing its number of variables by one. This axiom also uses ~ nic-mp for its constructions. Here, the axiom is proved as a substitution instance of ~ nic-ax . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 31-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lukshef_ax1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) (wnan (wnan th (wnan th th)) (wnan (wnan th ch) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ renicax . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lukshefth1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan (wnan (wnan ta ps) (wnan (wnan ph ta) (wnan ph ta))) (wnan th (wnan th th))) (wnan ph (wnan ps ch)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ renicax . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lukshefth2 (th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ta th) (wnan (wnan th ta) (wnan th ta)) $;

--| A rederivation of ~ nic-ax from ~ lukshef-ax1 , proving that ~ lukshef-ax1 with ~ nic-mp can be used as a complete axiomatization of propositional calculus. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 31-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem renicax (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wnan (wnan ph (wnan ch ps)) (wnan (wnan ta (wnan ta ta)) (wnan (wnan th ch) (wnan (wnan ph th) (wnan ph th)))) $;

--| Justification for ~ tbw-negdf . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 15-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_bijust (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ph) wfal)) wfal) $;

--| The definition of negation, in terms of ` -> ` and ` F. ` . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 15-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_negdf (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wn ph) (wi ph wfal)) (wi (wi (wi ph wfal) (wn ph)) wfal)) wfal $;

--| The first of four axioms in the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_ax1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| The second of four axioms in the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_ax2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| The third of four axioms in the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_ax3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph $;

--| The fourth of four axioms in the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg system. This axiom was added to the Tarski-Bernays axiom system (see ~ tb-ax1 , ~ tb-ax2 , and ~ tb-ax3 ) by Wajsberg for completeness. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbw_ax4 (ph: wff):
  $ wi wfal ph $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwsyl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwlem1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwlem2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps wfal)) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) th) (wi ps th)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwlem3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi ph wfal) ph) ph) ps) ps $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwlem4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph wfal) ps) (wi (wi ps wfal) ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tbwlem5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph (wi ps wfal)) wfal) ph $;

--| ~ luk-1 derived from the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1luk1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| ~ luk-2 derived from the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1luk2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| ~ luk-3 derived from the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms. This theorem, along with ~ re1luk1 and ~ re1luk2 proves that ~ tbw-ax1 , ~ tbw-ax2 , ~ tbw-ax3 , and ~ tbw-ax4 , with ~ ax-mp can be used as a complete axiom system for all of propositional calculus. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1luk3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| A single axiom for propositional calculus discovered by C. A. Meredith. This axiom is worthy of note, due to it having only 19 symbols, not counting parentheses. The more well-known ~ meredith has 21 symbols, sans parentheses. See ~ merco2 for another axiom of equal length. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch wfal)) th) ta) (wi (wi ta ph) (wi ch ph)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem1 (ph ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi wfal ch) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax4 rederived from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem retbwax4 (ph: wff):
  $ wi wfal ph $;

--| ~ tbw-ax2 rederived from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem retbwax2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem2 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wi (wi ps ta) (wi ph wfal)) ch) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch wfal)) (wi ch ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem5 (ph ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph wfal) ch) ta) (wi ph ta) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem6 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ch (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi (wi ps ch) ps) ps) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax3 rederived from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem retbwax3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps (wi ps ch)) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem9 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem10 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ta ch)) ph) (wi th ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem11 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ch (wi ph ta)) wfal) ps) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem12 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ch (wi ph ta)) ph) ps) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem13 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ch ps)) ta) (wi ph ta) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem14 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) ch) (wi ph ch) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem15 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem16 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) ta) (wi (wi ph ch) ta) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem17 (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ch) ta) (wi (wi ph ch) ta) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco1lem18 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ps ph) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax1 rederived from ~ merco1 . This theorem, along with ~ retbwax2 , ~ retbwax3 , and ~ retbwax4 , shows that ~ merco1 with ~ ax-mp can be used as a complete axiomatization of propositional calculus. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Sep-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem retbwax1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| A single axiom for propositional calculus discovered by C. A. Meredith. This axiom has 19 symbols, sans auxiliaries. See notes in ~ merco1 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 7-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem merco2 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi wfal ch) th)) (wi (wi th ph) (wi ta (wi et ph))) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps (wi th ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) (wi ch (wi th ph)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem4 (ph ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wi th (wi et ph)) (wi (wi (wi th ch) ph) (wi ta (wi et ph))) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem5 (ph ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi th (wi (wi th ph) (wi ta (wi ch ph))) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem6 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps (wi ph ch))) (wi ps (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem7 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) (wi th ps)) (wi th ps)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Tarski-Bernays-Wajsberg axioms from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mercolem8 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps (wi ph ch)) (wi ta (wi th (wi ph ch)))) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax1 rederived from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1tbw1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax2 rederived from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1tbw2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| ~ tbw-ax3 rederived from ~ merco2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1tbw3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph $;

--| ~ tbw-ax4 rederived from ~ merco2 . This theorem, along with ~ re1tbw1 , ~ re1tbw2 , and ~ re1tbw3 , shows that ~ merco2 , along with ~ ax-mp , can be used as a complete axiomatization of propositional calculus. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 16-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1tbw4 (ph: wff):
  $ wi wfal ph $;

--| Justification for ~ rb-imdf . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_bijust (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wo (wn (wo (wn ph) ps)) (wn (wo (wn ps) ph)))) $;

--| The definition of implication, in terms of ` \/ ` and ` -. ` . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 17-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_imdf (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wo (wn (wo (wn (wi ph ps)) (wo (wn ph) ps))) (wn (wo (wn (wo (wn ph) ps)) (wi ph ps)))) $;

--| Modus ponens for ` { \/ , -. } ` axiom systems. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 12-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem anmp (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wo (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| The first of four axioms in the Russell-Bernays axiom system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_ax1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo (wn ps) ch)) (wo (wn (wo ph ps)) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| The second of four axioms in the Russell-Bernays axiom system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_ax2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo ph ps)) (wo ps ph) $;

--| The third of four axioms in the Russell-Bernays axiom system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_ax3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wo (wn ph) (wo ps ph) $;

--| The fourth of four axioms in the Russell-Bernays axiom system. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rb_ax4 (ph: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo ph ph)) ph $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rbsyl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wo (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wo ph ps $ >
  $ wo ph ch $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wo (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wo (wn ch) th $ >
  $ wo (wn (wo ph ch)) (wo ps th) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo ch ph)) (wo ch (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo ch ph)) (wo (wo ch ps) ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 18-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem4 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wo (wn ph) th $ >
  $ wo (wn ps) ta $ >
  $ wo (wn ch) et $ >
  $ wo (wn (wo (wo ph ps) ch)) (wo (wo et ta) th) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wo (wn (wo (wn (wn ph)) ps)) (wo (wn (wn ps)) ph) $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem6 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wo (wn (wo (wn ph) ps)) (wn (wo (wn ps) ph))) $ >
  $ wo (wn ph) ps $;

--| Used to rederive the Lukasiewicz axioms from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rblem7 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wo (wn (wo (wn ph) ps)) (wn (wo (wn ps) ph))) $ >
  $ wo (wn ps) ph $;

--| ~ ax-mp derived from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re1axmp (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| ~ luk-1 derived from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re2luk1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| ~ luk-2 derived from Russell-Bernays'. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re2luk2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph $;

--| ~ luk-3 derived from Russell-Bernays'. This theorem, along with ~ re1axmp , ~ re2luk1 , and ~ re2luk2 shows that ~ rb-ax1 , ~ rb-ax2 , ~ rb-ax3 , and ~ rb-ax4 , along with ~ anmp , can be used as a complete axiomatization of propositional calculus. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 19-Aug-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem re2luk3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Modus ponendo tollens 1, one of the "indemonstrables" in Stoic logic. See rule 1 on [Lopez-Astorga] p. 12 , rule 1 on [Sanford] p. 40, and rule A3 in [Hitchcock] p. 5. Sanford describes this rule second (after ~ mptxor ) as a "safer, and these days much more common" version of modus ponendo tollens because it avoids confusion between inclusive-or and exclusive-or. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 3-Jul-2016.)
theorem mptnan (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wn (wa ph ps) $ >
  $ wn ps $;

--| Modus ponendo tollens 2, one of the "indemonstrables" in Stoic logic. Note that this uses exclusive-or ` \/_ ` . See rule 2 on [Lopez-Astorga] p. 12 , rule 4 on [Sanford] p. 39 and rule A4 in [Hitchcock] p. 5 . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 3-Jul-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 19-Apr-2019.)
theorem mptxor (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wxo ph ps $ >
  $ wn ps $;

--| Modus tollendo ponens (inclusive-or version), aka disjunctive syllogism. This is similar to ~ mtpxor , one of the five original "indemonstrables" in Stoic logic. However, in Stoic logic this rule used exclusive-or, while the name modus tollendo ponens often refers to a variant of the rule that uses inclusive-or instead. The rule says, "if ` ph ` is not true, and ` ph ` or ` ps ` (or both) are true, then ` ps ` must be true". An alternate phrasing is: "once you eliminate the impossible, whatever remains, no matter how improbable, must be the truth". -- Sherlock Holmes (Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, 1890: The Sign of the Four, ch. 6). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 3-Jul-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Nov-2017.)
theorem mtpor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wo ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Modus tollendo ponens (original exclusive-or version), aka disjunctive syllogism, similar to ~ mtpor , one of the five "indemonstrables" in Stoic logic. The rule says: "if ` ph ` is not true, and either ` ph ` or ` ps ` (exclusively) are true, then ` ps ` must be true". Today the name "modus tollendo ponens" often refers to a variant, the inclusive-or version as defined in ~ mtpor . See rule 3 on [Lopez-Astorga] p. 12 (note that the "or" is the same as ~ mptxor , that is, it is exclusive-or ~ df-xor ), rule 3 of [Sanford] p. 39 (where it is not as clearly stated which kind of "or" is used but it appears to be in the same sense as ~ mptxor ), and rule A5 in [Hitchcock] p. 5 (exclusive-or is expressly used). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 4-Jul-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 19-Apr-2019.)
theorem mtpxor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wxo ph ps $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 1 (part a). The first thema of the four Stoic logic themata, in its basic form, was: "When from two (assertibles) a third follows, then from either of them together with the contradictory of the conclusion the contradictory of the other follows." (Apuleius Int. 209.9-14), see [Bobzien] p. 117 and ~ https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-ancient/ We will represent thema 1 as two very similar rules ~ stoic1a and ~ stoic1b to represent each side. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 16-Feb-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-May-2020.)
theorem stoic1a (ph ps th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn th)) (wn ps) $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 1 (part b). The other part of thema 1 of Stoic logic; see ~ stoic1a . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 16-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic1b (ph ps th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wn th)) (wn ph) $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 2 version a. Statement T2 of [Bobzien] p. 117 shows a reconstructed version of Stoic logic thema 2 as follows: "When from two assertibles a third follows, and from the third and one (or both) of the two another follows, then this other follows from the first two." Bobzien uses constructs such as ` ph , ps |- ch ` ; in Metamath we will represent that construct as ` ph /\ ps -> ch ` . This version a is without the phrase "or both"; see ~ stoic2b for the version with the phrase "or both". We already have this rule as ~ syldan , so here we show the equivalence and discourage its use. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic2a (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 2 version b. See ~ stoic2a . Version b is with the phrase "or both". We already have this rule as ~ mpd3an3 , so here we prove the equivalence and discourage its use. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic2b (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 3. Statement T3 of [Bobzien] p. 116-117 discusses Stoic logic Thema 3. "When from two (assemblies) a third follows, and from the one that follows (i.e., the third) together with another, external assumption, another follows, then that other follows from the first two and the externally co-assumed one. (Simp. Cael. 237.2-4)" (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic3 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ch th) ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ta $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 4 version a. Statement T4 of [Bobzien] p. 117 shows a reconstructed version of Stoic logic Thema 4: "When from two assertibles a third follows, and from the third and one (or both) of the two and one (or more) external assertible(s) another follows, then this other follows from the first two and the external(s)." We use ` th ` to represent the "external" assertibles. This is version a, which is without the phrase "or both"; see ~ stoic4b for the version with the phrase "or both". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic4a (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ch ph th) ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ta $;

--| Stoic logic Thema 4 version b. This is version b, which is with the phrase "or both". See ~ stoic4a for more information. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Feb-2019.)
theorem stoic4b (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ch ph ps) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ta $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the existential quantifier ("there exists").
term wex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): wff;

--| Define existential quantification. ` E. x ph ` means "there exists at least one set ` x ` such that ` ph ` is true". Dual of ~ alex . See also the dual pair ~ alnex / ~ exnal . Definition of [Margaris] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
axiom df_ex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wn (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| Universal quantification of negation is equivalent to negation of existential quantification. Dual of ~ exnal (but does not depend on ~ ax-4 contrary to it). See also the dual pair ~ df-ex / ~ alex . Theorem 19.7 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem alnex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wn ph)) (wn (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| An equivalence between an implication with an existentially quantified antecedent and an implication with a universally quantified consequent. An interesting case is when the same formula is substituted for both ` ph ` and ` ps ` , since then both implications express a type of nonfreeness. See also ~ alimex . (Contributed by BJ, 12-May-2019.)
theorem eximal {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wi (wex x3 ph) ps) (wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the not-free predicate.
term wnf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): wff;

--| Define the not-free predicate for wffs. This is read " ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` ". Not-free means that the value of ` x ` cannot affect the value of ` ph ` , e.g., any occurrence of ` x ` in ` ph ` is effectively bound by a "for all" or something that expands to one (such as "there exists"). In particular, substitution for a variable not free in a wff does not affect its value ( ~ sbf ). An example of where this is used is ~ stdpc5 . See ~ nf5 for an alternate definition which involves nested quantifiers on the same variable. Not-free is a commonly used constraint, so it is useful to have a notation for it. Surprisingly, there is no common formal notation for it, so here we devise one. Our definition lets us work with the not-free notion within the logic itself rather than as a metalogical side condition. To be precise, our definition really means "effectively not free", because it is slightly less restrictive than the usual textbook definition for "not free" (which considers syntactic freedom). For example, ` x ` is effectively not free in the formula ` x = x ` (even though ` x ` is syntactically free in it, so would be considered free in the usual textbook definition) because the value of ` x ` in the formula ` x = x ` does not affect the truth of that formula (and thus substitutions will not change the result), see ~ nfequid . This definition of "not free" tightly ties to the quantifier ` A. x ` . At this state (no axioms restricting quantifiers yet) "nonfree" appears quite arbitrary. Its intended semantics expresses single-valuedness (constness) across a parameter, but is only evolved as much as later axioms assign properties to quantifiers. It seems the definition here is best suited in situations, where axioms are only partially in effect. In particular, this definition more easily carries over to other logic models with weaker axiomization. The reverse implication of the definiens (the right hand side of the biconditional) always holds, see ~ 19.2 . This predicate only applies to wffs. See ~ df-nfc for a not-free predicate for class variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) Convert to definition. (Revised by BJ, 6-May-2019.)
axiom df_nf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Alternate definition of nonfreeness. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nf2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wo (wal x3 ph) (wn (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Alternate definition of nonfreeness. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nf3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wo (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| Alternate definition of nonfreeness. This definition uses only primitive symbols ( ` -> , -. , A. ` ). (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nf4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| Deduce that ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` from the definition. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| Consequence of the definition of not-free. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfri {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Deduce that ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` in a context. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Consequence of the definition of not-free in a context. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 15-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfrd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ nfth . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 19-Aug-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem nftht {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Closed form of ~ nfnth . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem nfntht {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 ph)) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Closed form of ~ nfnth . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem nfntht2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wn ph)) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Rule of (universal) generalization. In our axiomatization, this is the only postulated (that is, axiomatic) rule of inference of predicate calculus (together with the rule of modus ponens ~ ax-mp of propositional calculus). See, e.g., Rule 2 of [Hamilton] p. 74. This rule says that if something is unconditionally true, then it is true for all values of a variable. For example, if we have proved ` x = x ` , then we can conclude ` A. x x = x ` or even ` A. y x = x ` . Theorem ~ altru shows the special case ` A. x T. ` . The converse rule of inference ~ spi (universal instantiation, or universal specialization) shows that we can also go the other way: in other words, we can add or remove universal quantifiers from the beginning of any theorem as required. Note that the closed form ` ( ph -> A. x ph ) ` need not hold (but may hold in special cases, see ~ ax-5 ). (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
axiom ax_gen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal x3 ph $;

--| Generalization applied twice. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1998.)
theorem gen2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal x3 (wal y1 ph) $;

--| Generalization applied twice. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1998.)
theorem gen2_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal x3 (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Modus ponens combined with generalization. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-1994.)
theorem mpg {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Modus ponens on biconditional combined with generalization. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 28-Oct-2008.)
theorem mpgbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) ps $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Modus ponens on biconditional combined with generalization. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Stefan Allan, 28-Oct-2008.)
theorem mpgbir {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Generalization rule for negated wff. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.)
theorem nex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn (wex x3 ph) $;

--| No variable is (effectively) free in a theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| No variable is (effectively) free in a non-theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Dec-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfnth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| No variable is (effectively) free in a theorem. This and later "hypothesis-building" lemmas, with labels starting "hb...", allow to construct proofs of formulas of the form ` |- ( ph -> A. x ph ) ` from smaller formulas of this form. These are useful for constructing hypotheses that state " ` x ` is (effectively) not free in ` ph ` ". (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) This hb* idiom is generally being replaced by the nf* idiom (see ~ nfth ), but keeps its interest in some cases. (Revised by BJ, 23-Sep-2022.)
theorem hbth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $;

--| The true constant has no free variables. (This can also be proven in one step with ~ nfv , but this proof does not use ~ ax-5 .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.)
theorem nftru {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnf x3 wtru $;

--| The false constant has no free variables (see ~ nftru ). (Contributed by BJ, 6-May-2019.)
theorem nffal {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnf x3 wfal $;

--| Version of ~ sp when ` ph ` is true. Instance of ~ a1i . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.)
theorem sptruw {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| For all sets, ` T. ` is true. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Sep-2011.)
theorem altru {x3: setvar}:
  $ wal x3 wtru $;

--| For all sets, ` -. F. ` is true. (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Sep-2011.)
theorem alfal {x3: setvar}:
  $ wal x3 (wn wfal) $;

--| Axiom of Quantified Implication. Axiom C4 of [Monk2] p. 105 and Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90. It is restated as ~ alim for labeling consistency. It should be used only by ~ alim . (Contributed by NM, 21-May-2008.) Use ~ alim instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_4 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Restatement of Axiom ~ ax-4 , for labeling consistency. It should be the only theorem using ~ ax-4 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem alim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference quantifying both antecedent and consequent. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.)
theorem alimi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps) $;

--| Inference doubly quantifying both antecedent and consequent. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _2alimi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Add an antecedent in a universally quantified formula. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2018.)
theorem ala1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ al2imi . Version of ~ alim for a nested implication. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem al2im {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wi ps ch))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch))) $;

--| Inference quantifying antecedent, nested antecedent, and consequent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem al2imi {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Variant of ~ al2imi with conjunctive antecedent. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem alanimi {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 ch) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ alim . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.)
theorem alimdh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Theorem 19.15 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.)
theorem albi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference adding universal quantifier to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.)
theorem albii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps) $;

--| Inference adding two universal quantifiers to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1997.)
theorem _2albii {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Inference adding three universal quantifiers to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 10-Aug-2018.)
theorem _3albii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z ph))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z ps))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ sylg . (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2019.)
theorem sylgt {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ch))) $;

--| A syllogism combined with generalization. Inference associated with ~ sylgt . General form of ~ alrimih . (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-1993.) Extract from proof of ~ alrimih . (Revised by BJ, 4-Oct-2019.)
theorem sylg {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ch) $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21h . Instance of ~ sylg . (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-1993.)
theorem alrimih {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. See ~ hbxfreq for equality version. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem hbxfrbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Universal quantifier in terms of existential quantifier and negation. Dual of ~ df-ex . See also the dual pair ~ alnex / ~ exnal . Theorem 19.6 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem alex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wn (wex x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| Existential quantification of negation is equivalent to negation of universal quantification. Dual of ~ alnex . See also the dual pair ~ df-ex / ~ alex . Theorem 19.14 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem exnal {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wn ph)) (wn (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Part of theorem *11.5 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2nalexn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wn (wal x3 (wal y1 ph))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wn ph))) $;

--| Theorem *11.22 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 160. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2exnaln {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wn (wal x3 (wal y1 (wn ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *11.22 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 160. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2exnaln_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex x3 ph)) (wn (wal x3 (wal x3 (wn ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *11.25 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 160. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2nexaln {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wn (wex x3 (wex y1 ph))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wn ph))) $;

--| An equivalence between an implication with a universally quantified consequent and an implication with an existentially quantified antecedent. An interesting case is when the same formula is substituted for both ` ph ` and ` ps ` , since then both implications express a type of nonfreeness. See also ~ eximal . (Contributed by BJ, 12-May-2019.)
theorem alimex {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) (wi (wex x3 (wn ps)) (wn ph)) $;

--| A variant of ~ al2imi : instead of applying ` A. x ` quantifiers to the final implication, replace them with ` E. x ` . A shorter proof is possible using ~ nfa1 , ~ sps and ~ eximd , but it depends on more axioms. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 18-Aug-2019.)
theorem aleximi {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wi (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Biconditional form of ~ aleximi . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Nov-2020.)
theorem alexbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem exim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference adding existential quantifier to antecedent and consequent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem eximi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Inference adding two existential quantifiers to antecedent and consequent. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _2eximi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ eximi . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Feb-2018.)
theorem eximii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wex x3 ps $;

--| Add an antecedent in an existentially quantified formula. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2018.)
theorem exa1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Theorem 19.38 of [Margaris] p. 90. The converse holds under nonfreeness conditions, see ~ 19.38a and ~ 19.38b . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) Allow a shortening of ~ 19.21t . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem _19_38 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Under a nonfreeness hypothesis, the implication ~ 19.38 can be strengthened to an equivalence. See also ~ 19.38b . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Nov-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Jul-2022.)
theorem _19_38a {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wb (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Under a nonfreeness hypothesis, the implication ~ 19.38 can be strengthened to an equivalence. See also ~ 19.38a . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Nov-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Jul-2022.)
theorem _19_38b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ps) (wb (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Quantified implication in terms of quantified negation of conjunction. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem imnang {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps))) (wal x3 (wn (wa ph ps))) $;

--| A transformation of quantifiers and logical connectives. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.)
theorem alinexa {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps))) (wn (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Existential quantification of a conjunction expressed with only primitive symbols ( ` -> ` , ` -. ` , ` A. ` ). (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) State the most general instance. (Revised by BJ, 29-Sep-2019.)
theorem exnalimn {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wn (wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)))) $;

--| A relationship between two quantifiers and negation. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem alexn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wex y1 (wn ph))) (wn (wex x3 (wal y1 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *11.51 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Sep-2014.)
theorem _2exnexn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wn (wal x3 (wex y1 (wn ph)))) $;

--| Theorem 19.18 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem exbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference adding existential quantifier to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-1994.)
theorem exbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Inference adding two existential quantifiers to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-1995.)
theorem _2exbii {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Inference adding three existential quantifiers to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1995.)
theorem _3exbii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ph))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ps))) $;

--| Equivalence theorem for the nonfreeness predicate. Closed form of ~ nfbii . (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 10-Apr-2018.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 6-May-2019.)
theorem nfbiit {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wnf x3 ph) (wnf x3 ps)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the nonfreeness predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfxfr {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nfxfrd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| A variable is nonfree in a proposition if and only if it is so in its negation. (Contributed by BJ, 6-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Oct-2024.)
theorem nfnbi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wnf x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| If a variable is nonfree in a proposition, then it is nonfree in its negation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Dec-2017.) (Revised by BJ, 24-Jul-2019.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfnt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wnf x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ nfnt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wn ph) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ nfnt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nfnd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wn ps)) $;

--| A transformation of quantifiers and logical connectives. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Sep-2014.)
theorem exanali {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph (wn ps))) (wn (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.521 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2exanali {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wn (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph (wn ps))))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Commutation of conjunction inside an existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem exancom {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wex x3 (wa ps ph)) $;

--| Place a conjunct in the scope of an existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jan-2018.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by BJ, 7-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Nov-2022.) Expand hypothesis. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 19-Jun-2023.)
theorem exan {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ps) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21h . (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem alrimdh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction from Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-1996.)
theorem eximdh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction for generalization rule for negated wff. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.)
theorem nexdh {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for universal quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem albidh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for existential quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem exbidh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Simplification of an existentially quantified conjunction. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 25-Sep-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 29-Jun-2011.)
theorem exsimpl {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Simplification of an existentially quantified conjunction. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 25-Sep-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 29-Jun-2011.)
theorem exsimpr {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.26 of [Margaris] p. 90. Also Theorem *10.22 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 147. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem _19_26 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem ~ 19.26 with two quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _19_26_2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem ~ 19.26 with triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2011.)
theorem _19_26_3an {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (w3a ph ps ch)) (w3a (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , it is not free in ` ( ph /\ ps ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by SN and Sky Winter, 22-Oct-2026.)
theorem hban {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , ` ps ` , and ` ch ` , it is not free in ` ( ph /\ ps /\ ch ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by SN and Sky Winter, 23-Oct-2026.)
theorem hb3an {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wal x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wal x3 (w3a ph ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem 19.29 of [Margaris] p. 90. See also ~ 19.29r . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem _19_29 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Variation of ~ 19.29 . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2020.)
theorem _19_29r {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Variation of ~ 19.29r with double quantification. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _19_29r2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Variation of ~ 19.29 with mixed quantification. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-2005.)
theorem _19_29x {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wex y1 ps))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem 19.35 of [Margaris] p. 90. This theorem is useful for moving an implication (in the form of the right-hand side) into the scope of a single existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem _19_35 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.35 . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_35i {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.35 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_35ri {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wi ph ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.25 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_25 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal y1 (wex x3 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex y1 (wal x3 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ps))) $;

--| Theorem 19.30 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _19_30 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.43 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem _19_43 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Obsolete proof of ~ 19.43 . Do not delete as it is referenced on the ~ mmrecent.html page and in ~ conventions-labels . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _19_43OLD {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.33 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_33 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wo (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) $;

--| The antecedent provides a condition implying the converse of ~ 19.33 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jul-2014.)
theorem _19_33b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps))) (wb (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps))) $;

--| Theorem 19.40 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem _19_40 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem *11.42 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 163. Theorem 19.40 of [Margaris] p. 90 with two quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_40_2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| The antecedent provides a condition implying the converse of ~ 19.40 . This is to ~ 19.40 what ~ 19.33b is to ~ 19.33 . (Contributed by BJ, 6-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2020.)
theorem _19_40b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wo (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wb (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Split a biconditional and distribute quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem albiim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wa (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wal x3 (wi ps ph))) $;

--| Split a biconditional and distribute two quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _2albiim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wb ph ps))) (wa (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ps ph)))) $;

--| Add/remove a conjunct in the scope of an existential quantifier. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 3-Jul-2006.)
theorem exintrbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Introduce a conjunct in the scope of an existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem exintr {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Universally quantified and uncurried (imported) form of syllogism. Theorem *10.3 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 150. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem alsyl {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wal x3 (wi ps ch))) (wal x3 (wi ph ch)) $;

--| If in a context ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` and ` ch ` , then it is not free in ` ( ps -> ch ) ` . Deduction form of ~ nfim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Dec-2017.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.) Eliminate curried form of ~ nfimt . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2022.)
theorem nfimd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ nfim and ~ nfimd . (Contributed by BJ, 20-Oct-2021.) Eliminate curried form, former name nfimt2. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Jul-2022.)
theorem nfimt {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wnf x3 ph) (wnf x3 ps)) (wnf x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph -> ps ) ` . Inference associated with ~ nfimt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wi ph ps) $;

--| If in a context ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` and ` ch ` , then it is not free in ` ( ps /\ ch ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfand {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction form of bound-variable hypothesis builder ~ nf3an . (Contributed by NM, 17-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2016.)
theorem nf3and {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch th: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (w3a ps ch th)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph /\ ps ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jan-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfan {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wa ph ps) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph -/\ ps ) ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nfnan {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wnan ph ps) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , ` ps ` , and ` ch ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph /\ ps /\ ch ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nf3an {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 (w3a ph ps ch) $;

--| If in a context ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` and ` ch ` , then it is not free in ` ( ps <-> ch ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Dec-2017.)
theorem nfbid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wb ps ch)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph <-> ps ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.)
theorem nfbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wb ph ps) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph \/ ps ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfor {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wo ph ps) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , ` ps ` , and ` ch ` , then it is not free in ` ( ph \/ ps \/ ch ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nf3or {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 (w3o ph ps ch) $;

--| Two characterizations of the empty domain. (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 5-Feb-2024.)
theorem empty {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb (wn (wex x3 wtru)) (wal x3 wfal) $;

--| On the empty domain, any existentially quantified formula is false. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 21-Jan-2024.)
theorem emptyex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 wtru)) (wn (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| On the empty domain, any universally quantified formula is true. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem emptyal {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 wtru)) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| On the empty domain, any variable is effectively nonfree in any formula. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem emptynf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 wtru)) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Axiom of Distinctness. This axiom quantifies a variable over a formula in which it does not occur. Axiom C5 in [Megill] p. 444 (p. 11 of the preprint). Also appears as Axiom B6 (p. 75) of system S2 of [Tarski] p. 77 and Axiom C5-1 of [Monk2] p. 113. (See comments in ~ ax5ALT about the logical redundancy of ~ ax-5 in the presence of our obsolete axioms.) This axiom essentially says that if ` x ` does not occur in ` ph ` , i.e. ` ph ` does not depend on ` x ` in any way, then we can add the quantifier ` A. x ` to ` ph ` with no further assumptions. By ~ sp , we can also remove the quantifier (unconditionally). For an explanation of disjoint variable conditions, see ~ https://us.metamath.org/mpeuni/mmset.html#distinct . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
axiom ax_5 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-5 with antecedent. Useful in proofs of deduction versions of bound-variable hypothesis builders. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-2013.)
theorem ax5d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| A rephrasing of ~ ax-5 using the existential quantifier. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem ax5e {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ph $;

--| If a formula holds for some value of a variable not occurring in it, then it holds for all values of that variable. (Contributed by BJ, 28-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax5ea {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| If ` x ` is not present in ` ph ` , then ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Definition change. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| ~ nfv with antecedent. Useful in proofs of deduction versions of bound-variable hypothesis builders such as ~ nfimd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfvd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ alim . See ~ alimdh and ~ alimd for versions without a distinct variable condition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.)
theorem alimdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ exim . See ~ eximdh and ~ eximd for versions without a distinct variable condition. (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-1994.)
theorem eximdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90 with two quantifiers, see ~ alim . (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-2004.)
theorem _2alimdv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ch))) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90 with two quantifiers, see ~ exim . (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1995.)
theorem _2eximdv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ps)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ch))) $;

--| Formula-building rule for universal quantifier (deduction form). See also ~ albidh and ~ albid . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem albidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for existential quantifier (deduction form). See also ~ exbidh and ~ exbid . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem exbidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| An equality theorem for nonfreeness. See ~ nfbidf for a version without disjoint variable condition but requiring more axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-6 , ~ ax-7 , ~ ax-12 by adapting proof of ~ nfbidf . (Revised by BJ, 25-Sep-2022.)
theorem nfbidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnf x3 ps) (wnf x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for two universal quantifiers (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1997.)
theorem _2albidv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ch))) $;

--| Formula-building rule for two existential quantifiers (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 1-May-1995.)
theorem _2exbidv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ps)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ch))) $;

--| Formula-building rule for three existential quantifiers (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 1-May-1995.)
theorem _3exbidv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ps))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ch)))) $;

--| Formula-building rule for four existential quantifiers (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1995.)
theorem _4exbidv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w ps)))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w ch))))) $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21v . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem alrimiv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21v . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.)
theorem alrimivv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21v . (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.)
theorem alrimdv {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff) (ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.23 . See ~ exlimi for a more general version requiring more axioms. This inference, along with its many variants such as ~ rexlimdv , is used to implement a metatheorem called "Rule C" that is given in many logic textbooks. See, for example, Rule C in [Mendelson] p. 81, Rule C in [Margaris] p. 40, or Rule C in Hirst and Hirst's _A Primer for Logic and Proof_ p. 59 (PDF p. 65) at ~ http://www.appstate.edu/~~hirstjl/primer/hirst.pdf . In informal proofs, the statement "Let ` C ` be an element such that..." almost always means an implicit application of Rule C. In essence, Rule C states that if we can prove that some element ` x ` exists satisfying a wff, i.e. ` E. x ph ( x ) ` where ` ph ( x ) ` has ` x ` free, then we can use ` ph ( C ) ` as a hypothesis for the proof where ` C ` is a new (fictitious) constant not appearing previously in the proof, nor in any axioms used, nor in the theorem to be proved. The purpose of Rule C is to get rid of the existential quantifier. We cannot do this in Metamath directly. Instead, we use the original ` ph ` (containing ` x ` ) as an antecedent for the main part of the proof. We eventually arrive at ` ( ph -> ps ) ` where ` ps ` is the theorem to be proved and does not contain ` x ` . Then we apply ~ exlimiv to arrive at ` ( E. x ph -> ps ) ` . Finally, we separately prove ` E. x ph ` and detach it with modus ponens ~ ax-mp to arrive at the final theorem ` ps ` , see ~ exlimiiv . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-6 and ~ ax-8 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem exlimiv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ps $;

--| Inference (Rule C) associated with ~ exlimiv . (Contributed by BJ, 19-Dec-2020.)
theorem exlimiiv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1995.)
theorem exlimivv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) ps $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-1994.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-6 , ~ ax-7 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem exlimdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.)
theorem exlimdvv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ps)) ch) $;

--| Existential elimination rule of natural deduction (Rule C, explained in ~ exlimiv ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2016.)
theorem exlimddv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction for generalization rule for negated wff. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem nexdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Quantification of two variables over a formula in which they do not occur. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 12-Apr-2011.)
theorem _2ax5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Version of ~ stdpc5 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Mar-2020.) Revised to shorten ~ 19.21v . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem stdpc5v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.21 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. _Notational convention_: We sometimes suffix with "v" the label of a theorem using a distinct variable ("dv") condition instead of a nonfreeness hypothesis such as ` F/ x ph ` . Conversely, we sometimes suffix with "f" the label of a theorem introducing such a nonfreeness hypothesis ("f" stands for "not free in", see ~ df-nf ) instead of a disjoint variable condition. For instance, ~ 19.21v versus ~ 19.21 and ~ vtoclf versus ~ vtocl . Note that "not free in" is less restrictive than "does not occur in". Note that the version with a disjoint variable condition is easily proved from the version with the corresponding nonfreeness hypothesis, by using ~ nfv . However, the dv version can often be proved from fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem _19_21v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.32 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem _19_32v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.31 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem _19_31v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.23 with a disjoint variable condition instead of a nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jun-1998.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Jan-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-6 . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 15-Apr-2022.)
theorem _19_23v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem ~ 19.23v extended to two variables. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2004.)
theorem _19_23vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ pm11.53 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem pm11_53v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| One direction of ~ 19.36v that can be proven without ~ ax-6 . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 16-Apr-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2024.)
theorem _19_36imv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.36v . Version of ~ 19.36i with a disjoint variable condition. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 17-Jan-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-6 . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 15-Apr-2022.)
theorem _19_36iv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wex x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| One direction of ~ 19.37v that can be proven without ~ ax-6 . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 16-Apr-2022.)
theorem _19_37imv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.37v . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-6 . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 15-Apr-2022.)
theorem _19_37iv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.41 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-6 . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 15-Apr-2022.)
theorem _19_41v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.41 with two quantifiers and a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1995.)
theorem _19_41vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.41 with three quantifiers and a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1995.)
theorem _19_41vvv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wa ph ps)))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ph))) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.41 with four quantifiers and a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by FL, 14-Jul-2007.)
theorem _19_41vvvv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex w (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wa ph ps))))) (wa (wex w (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ph)))) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.42 with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_42v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Distribution of existential quantifiers. See also ~ exdistrv . (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.)
theorem exdistr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wex x3 (wa ph (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Distribute a pair of existential quantifiers (over disjoint variables) over a conjunction. Combination of ~ 19.41v and ~ 19.42v . For a version with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms, see ~ eeanv . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.)
theorem exdistrv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Distribute two pairs of existential quantifiers (over disjoint variables) over a conjunction. For a version with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms, see ~ ee4anv . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Jan-2023.)
theorem _4exdistrv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex z (wex y1 (wex w (wa ph ps))))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps))) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.42 with two quantifiers and a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-1995.)
theorem _19_42vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa ph (wex x3 (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Distribution of existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1995.)
theorem exdistr2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wa ph ps)))) (wex x3 (wa ph (wex y1 (wex z ps)))) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.42 with three quantifiers and a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Aug-2023.)
theorem _19_42vvv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wa ph ps)))) (wa ph (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ps)))) $;

--| Distribution of existential quantifiers in a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3exdistr {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (w3a ph ps ch)))) (wex x3 (wa ph (wex y1 (wa ps (wex z ch))))) $;

--| Distribution of existential quantifiers in a quadruple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jan-2018.)
theorem _4exdistr {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff x3 y1 z) (th: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)))))) (wex x3 (wa ph (wex y1 (wa ps (wex z (wa ch (wex w th))))))) $;

--| Specialization, with additional weakening (compared to ~ 19.2 ) to allow bundling of ` x ` and ` y ` . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem speimfw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ speimfw (longer compressed proof, but fewer essential steps). (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Aug-2017.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem speimfwALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Specialization, with additional weakening (compared to ~ sp ) to allow bundling of ` x ` and ` y ` . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Aug-2017.)
theorem spimfw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wi (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Inference that has ~ ax-12 (without ` A. y ` ) as its conclusion. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. The hypotheses may be eliminable without using ~ ax-12 in special cases. Proof similar to Lemma 16 of [Tarski] p. 70. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2008.)
theorem ax12i {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Axiom of Existence. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. This axiom tells us that at least one thing exists. In this form (not requiring that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct) it was used in an axiom system of Tarski (see Axiom B7' in footnote 1 of [KalishMontague] p. 81.) It is equivalent to axiom scheme C10' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint); the equivalence is established by ~ axc10 and ~ ax6fromc10 . A more convenient form of this axiom is ~ ax6e , which has additional remarks. Raph Levien proved the independence of this axiom from the other logical axioms on 12-Apr-2005. See item 16 at ~ https://us.metamath.org/award2003.html . ~ ax-6 can be proved from the weaker version ~ ax6v requiring that the variables be distinct; see Theorem ~ ax6 . ~ ax-6 can also be proved from the Axiom of Separation (in the form that we use that axiom, where free variables are not universally quantified). See Theorem ~ ax6vsep . Except by ~ ax6v , this axiom should not be referenced directly. Instead, use Theorem ~ ax6 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_6 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Axiom B7 of [Tarski] p. 75, which requires that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct. This trivial proof is intended merely to weaken Axiom ~ ax-6 by adding a distinct variable restriction ($d). From here on, ~ ax-6 should not be referenced directly by any other proof, so that Theorem ~ ax6 will show that we can recover ~ ax-6 from this weaker version if it were an axiom (as it is in the case of Tarski). Note: Introducing ` x , y ` as a distinct variable group "out of the blue" with no apparent justification has puzzled some people, but it is perfectly sound. All we are doing is adding an additional prerequisite, similar to adding an unnecessary logical hypothesis, that results in a weakening of the theorem. This means that any _future_ theorem that references ~ ax6v must have a $d specified for the two variables that get substituted for ` x ` and ` y ` . The $d does not propagate "backwards", i.e., it does not impose a requirement on ~ ax-6 . When possible, use of this theorem rather than ~ ax6 is preferred since its derivation is much shorter and requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem ax6v {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| At least one individual exists. Weaker version of ~ ax6e . When possible, use of this theorem rather than ~ ax6e is preferred since its derivation is much shorter and requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2017.)
theorem ax6ev {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Specialization. Lemma 8 of [KalishMontague] p. 87. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Aug-2017.)
theorem spimw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Existential introduction, using implicit substitution. Compare Lemma 14 of [Tarski] p. 70. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Oct-2023.)
theorem spimew {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Inference from existential specialization. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) Use ~ spimew . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Oct-2023.)
theorem speiv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $;

--| Version of ~ spei with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 (neither ~ ax-7 nor ~ ax-12 ). (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem speivw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $;

--| Rule of existential generalization, similar to universal generalization ~ ax-gen , but valid only if an individual exists. Its proof requires ~ ax-6 in our axiomatization but the equality predicate does not occur in its statement. Some fundamental theorems of predicate calculus can be proven from ~ ax-gen , ~ ax-4 and this theorem alone, not requiring ~ ax-7 or excessive distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem exgen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $;

--| There exists a variable such that ` T. ` holds; that is, there exists a variable. This corresponds under the standard translation to one of the formulations of the modal axiom (D), the other being ~ 19.2 . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 12-May-2019.)
theorem extru {x3: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 wtru $;

--| Theorem 19.2 of [Margaris] p. 89. This corresponds to the axiom (D) of modal logic (the other standard formulation being ~ extru ). Note: This proof is very different from Margaris' because we only have Tarski's FOL axiom schemes available at this point. See the later ~ 19.2g for a more conventional proof of a more general result, which uses additional axioms. The reverse implication is the defining property of effective nonfreeness (see ~ df-nf ). (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2017.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-7 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem _19_2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ 19.2 . (Contributed by BJ, 12-May-2019.)
theorem _19_2d {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Weak version of ~ 19.8a and instance of ~ 19.2d . (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem _19_8w {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Weak version of ~ sp . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Aug-2017.)
theorem spnfw {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wal x3 (wn ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Version of ~ sp when ` ph ` is false. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Dec-2017.)
theorem spfalw {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Version of ~ sp when ` x ` does not occur in ` ph ` . Converse of ~ ax-5 . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.) Shorten ~ 19.3v . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem spvw {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Version of ~ 19.3 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Any formula can be universally quantified using a variable which it does not contain. See also ~ 19.9v . (Contributed by Anthony Hart, 13-Sep-2011.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-7 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem _19_3v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Version of ~ 19.8a with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Converse of ~ ax5e . (Contributed by BJ, 12-Mar-2020.)
theorem _19_8v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.9 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Any formula can be existentially quantified using a variable which it does not contain. See also ~ 19.3v . (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1995.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-7 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem _19_9v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) ph $;

--| Existential introduction, using implicit substitution. This is to ~ spimew what ~ spimvw is to ~ spimw . Version of ~ spimev and ~ spimefv with an additional disjoint variable condition, using only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 17-Mar-2020.)
theorem spimevw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| A weak form of specialization. Lemma 8 of [KalishMontague] p. 87. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. For stronger forms using more axioms, see ~ spimv and ~ spimfv . (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem spimvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Generalization of antecedent. A trivial weak version of ~ sps avoiding ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by SN, 13-Nov-2025.) (Proof shortened by WL, 19-Nov-2025.)
theorem spsv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Specialization, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ spv with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-7 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem spvv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Implicit substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` into a theorem. Version of ~ chvarv with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 20-Apr-1994.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem chvarvv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Theorem 19.39 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_39 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wi (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.24 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_24 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.34 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_34 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wo (wal x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.36 with a disjoint variable condition instead of a nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 17-Jan-2020.)
theorem _19_36v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.12vv with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. See also ~ 19.12 . (Contributed by BJ, 18-Mar-2020.)
theorem _19_12vvv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wal y1 (wex x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.27 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jun-2004.)
theorem _19_27v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.28 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem _19_28v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.37 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_37v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.44 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_44v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.45 with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_45v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ equs4 with a disjoint variable condition, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem equs4v {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Version of ~ equs4v with its consequence simplified by ~ exsimpr . (Contributed by BJ, 9-Nov-2021.)
theorem alequexv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| One direction of the equivalence in ~ exsb is based on fewer axioms. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2023.)
theorem exsbim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| If a formula does not contain a variable ` x ` , then it is equivalent to the corresponding prototype of substitution with a fresh variable (see ~ sb6 ). (Contributed by BJ, 23-Jul-2023.)
theorem equsv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ph $;

--| Version of ~ equsalv with a disjoint variable condition, and of ~ equsal with two disjoint variable conditions, which requires fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsexvw . (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem equsalvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| Version of ~ equsexv with a disjoint variable condition, and of ~ equsex with two disjoint variable conditions, which requires fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsalvw . (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2023.)
theorem equsexvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. Part of Lemma 7 of [KalishMontague] p. 86. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.)
theorem cbvaliw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. Part of Lemma 7 of [KalishMontague] p. 86. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem cbvalivw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Axiom of Equality. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. It states that equality is a right-Euclidean binary relation (this is similar, but not identical, to being transitive, which is proved as ~ equtr ). This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom C7 of [Monk2] p. 105 and Axiom Scheme C8' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). The equality symbol was invented in 1557 by Robert Recorde. He chose a pair of parallel lines of the same length because "noe .2. thynges, can be moare equalle". We prove in ~ ax7 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax7v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-7 should be ~ ax7v . See the comment of ~ ax7v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_7 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Axiom of Equality. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. It states that equality is a right-Euclidean binary relation (this is similar, but not identical, to being transitive, which is proved as ~ equtr ). This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom C7 of [Monk2] p. 105 and Axiom Scheme C8' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). The equality symbol was invented in 1557 by Robert Recorde. He chose a pair of parallel lines of the same length because "noe .2. thynges, can be moare equalle". We prove in ~ ax7 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax7v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-7 should be ~ ax7v . See the comment of ~ ax7v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_7_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Axiom of Equality. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. It states that equality is a right-Euclidean binary relation (this is similar, but not identical, to being transitive, which is proved as ~ equtr ). This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom C7 of [Monk2] p. 105 and Axiom Scheme C8' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). The equality symbol was invented in 1557 by Robert Recorde. He chose a pair of parallel lines of the same length because "noe .2. thynges, can be moare equalle". We prove in ~ ax7 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax7v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-7 should be ~ ax7v . See the comment of ~ ax7v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_7_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-7 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-7 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 , from which ~ ax-7 is then rederived as ~ ax7 , which shows that either ~ ax7v or the conjunction of ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 is sufficient. In ~ ax7v , it is still allowed to substitute the same variable for ` x ` and ` z ` , or the same variable for ` y ` and ` z ` . Therefore, ~ ax7v "bundles" (a term coined by Raph Levien) its "principal instance" ` ( x = y -> ( x = z -> y = z ) ) ` with ` x , y , z ` distinct, and its "degenerate instances" ` ( x = y -> ( x = x -> y = x ) ) ` and ` ( x = y -> ( x = y -> y = y ) ) ` with ` x , y ` distinct. These degenerate instances are for instance used in the proofs of ~ equcomiv and ~ equid respectively. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax7v (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-7 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-7 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 , from which ~ ax-7 is then rederived as ~ ax7 , which shows that either ~ ax7v or the conjunction of ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 is sufficient. In ~ ax7v , it is still allowed to substitute the same variable for ` x ` and ` z ` , or the same variable for ` y ` and ` z ` . Therefore, ~ ax7v "bundles" (a term coined by Raph Levien) its "principal instance" ` ( x = y -> ( x = z -> y = z ) ) ` with ` x , y , z ` distinct, and its "degenerate instances" ` ( x = y -> ( x = x -> y = x ) ) ` and ` ( x = y -> ( x = y -> y = y ) ) ` with ` x , y ` distinct. These degenerate instances are for instance used in the proofs of ~ equcomiv and ~ equid respectively. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax7v_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-7 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-7 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 , from which ~ ax-7 is then rederived as ~ ax7 , which shows that either ~ ax7v or the conjunction of ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 is sufficient. In ~ ax7v , it is still allowed to substitute the same variable for ` x ` and ` z ` , or the same variable for ` y ` and ` z ` . Therefore, ~ ax7v "bundles" (a term coined by Raph Levien) its "principal instance" ` ( x = y -> ( x = z -> y = z ) ) ` with ` x , y , z ` distinct, and its "degenerate instances" ` ( x = y -> ( x = x -> y = x ) ) ` and ` ( x = y -> ( x = y -> y = y ) ) ` with ` x , y ` distinct. These degenerate instances are for instance used in the proofs of ~ equcomiv and ~ equid respectively. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax7 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax7v_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7v1 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7v1_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7v2 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7v2_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Identity law for equality. Lemma 2 of [KalishMontague] p. 85. See also Lemma 6 of [Tarski] p. 68. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2005.) (Revised by NM, 9-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem equid (x3: setvar):
  $ wceq (cv x3) (cv x3) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for ` x = x ` . This theorem tells us that any variable, including ` x ` , is effectively not free in ` x = x ` , even though ` x ` is technically free according to the traditional definition of free variable. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jan-2011.) (Revised by NM, 21-Aug-2017.)
theorem nfequid {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wnf y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| Weaker form of ~ equcomi with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This is an intermediate step and ~ equcomi is fully recovered later. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem equcomiv (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) $;

--| A commuted form of ~ ax6ev . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax6evr {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-7 from ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 (and earlier axioms), proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-7 . Note that the weakened version of ~ ax-7 obtained by adding a disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` (resp. on ` y , z ` ) does not permit, together with the other axioms, to prove reflexivity (resp. symmetry). (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-7 from ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 (and earlier axioms), proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-7 . Note that the weakened version of ~ ax-7 obtained by adding a disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` (resp. on ` y , z ` ) does not permit, together with the other axioms, to prove reflexivity (resp. symmetry). (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7_b (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-7 from ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 (and earlier axioms), proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-7 . Note that the weakened version of ~ ax-7 obtained by adding a disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` (resp. on ` y , z ` ) does not permit, together with the other axioms, to prove reflexivity (resp. symmetry). (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7_b1 (x3 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-7 from ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 (and earlier axioms), proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-7 . Note that the weakened version of ~ ax-7 obtained by adding a disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` (resp. on ` y , z ` ) does not permit, together with the other axioms, to prove reflexivity (resp. symmetry). (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7_b2 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-7 from ~ ax7v1 and ~ ax7v2 (and earlier axioms), proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax7v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-7 . Note that the weakened version of ~ ax-7 obtained by adding a disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` (resp. on ` y , z ` ) does not permit, together with the other axioms, to prove reflexivity (resp. symmetry). (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax7_b3 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Commutative law for equality. Equality is a symmetric relation. Lemma 3 of [KalishMontague] p. 85. See also Lemma 7 of [Tarski] p. 69. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem equcomi (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) $;

--| Commutative law for equality. Equality is a symmetric relation. Lemma 3 of [KalishMontague] p. 85. See also Lemma 7 of [Tarski] p. 69. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem equcomi_b (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| Commutative law for equality. Equality is a symmetric relation. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-1993.)
theorem equcom (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ equcom , symmetry of equality. For the versions for classes, see ~ eqcom and ~ eqcomd . (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.)
theorem equcomd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) $;

--| An inference commuting equality in antecedent. Used to eliminate the need for a syllogism. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem equcoms {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) ph $;

--| An inference commuting equality in antecedent. Used to eliminate the need for a syllogism. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.)
theorem equcoms_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) ph $;

--| A transitive law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtr {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtr_b {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtr_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtr_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. Lemma L17 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtrr {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. Lemma L17 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtrr_b {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A transitive law for equality. Lemma L17 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1993.)
theorem equtrr_b1 {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv z))) $;

--| Commuted version of ~ equeucl (equality is left-Euclidean). (Contributed by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeuclr {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Commuted version of ~ equeucl (equality is left-Euclidean). (Contributed by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeuclr_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| Commuted version of ~ equeucl (equality is left-Euclidean). (Contributed by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeuclr_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Equality is a left-Euclidean binary relation. (Right-Euclideanness is stated in ~ ax-7 .) Curried (exported) form of ~ equtr2 . (Contributed by BJ, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeucl {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Equality is a left-Euclidean binary relation. (Right-Euclideanness is stated in ~ ax-7 .) Curried (exported) form of ~ equtr2 . (Contributed by BJ, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeucl_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| Equality is a left-Euclidean binary relation. (Right-Euclideanness is stated in ~ ax-7 .) Curried (exported) form of ~ equtr2 . (Contributed by BJ, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem equeucl_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Dec-2017.)
theorem equequ1 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Dec-2017.)
theorem equequ1_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Dec-2017.)
theorem equequ1_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equequ2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equequ2_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equivalence law for equality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 12-Apr-2021.)
theorem equequ2_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Equality is a left-Euclidean binary relation. Uncurried (imported) form of ~ equeucl . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem equtr2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Equality is a left-Euclidean binary relation. Uncurried (imported) form of ~ equeucl . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem equtr2_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| One of the two equality axioms of standard predicate calculus, called reflexivity of equality. (The other one is ~ stdpc7 .) Axiom 6 of [Mendelson] p. 95. Mendelson doesn't say why he prepended the redundant quantifier, but it was probably to be compatible with free logic (which is valid in the empty domain). (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-2005.)
theorem stdpc6 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| A variable introduction law for equality. Lemma 15 of [Monk2] p. 109. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-1993.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jun-2019.) Move the quantified variable ( ` z ` ) to the left of the equality signs. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2022.)
theorem equvinv {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A variable introduction law for equality. Lemma 15 of [Monk2] p. 109. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-1993.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jun-2019.) Move the quantified variable ( ` z ` ) to the left of the equality signs. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2022.)
theorem equvinv_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) $;

--| A modified version of the forward implication of ~ equvinv adapted to common usage. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.)
theorem equvinva {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| A modified version of the forward implication of ~ equvinv adapted to common usage. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.)
theorem equvinva_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) $;

--| A biconditional form of ~ equvel with disjoint variable conditions and proved from Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 2-Jun-2011.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Apr-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jul-2022.)
theorem equvelv {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wal z (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| An equivalence between two ways of expressing ~ ax-13 . See the comment for ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-May-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Feb-2018.) (Revised by BJ, 15-Sep-2020.)
theorem ax13b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) ph)) (wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) ph))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ sp . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. Lemma 9 of [KalishMontague] p. 87. This may be the best we can do with minimal distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem spfw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wal y1 (wn ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Weak version of the specialization scheme ~ sp . Lemma 9 of [KalishMontague] p. 87. While it appears that ~ sp in its general form does not follow from Tarski's FOL axiom schemes, from this theorem we can prove any _instance_ of ~ sp having mutually distinct setvar variables and no wff metavariables (see ~ ax12wdemo for an example of the procedure to eliminate the hypothesis). Other approximations of ~ sp are ~ spfw (minimal distinct variable requirements), ~ spnfw (when ` x ` is not free in ` -. ph ` ), ~ spvw (when ` x ` does not appear in ` ph ` ), ~ sptruw (when ` ph ` is true), ~ spfalw (when ` ph ` is false), and ~ spvv (where ` ph ` is changed into ` ps ` ). (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Feb-2018.)
theorem spw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem cbvalw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 ps) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wal y1 (wn ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. See ~ cbvalv for a version with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Feb-2018.)
theorem cbvalvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. See ~ cbvexv for a version with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.)
theorem cbvexvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change the bound variable in a universal quantifier with implicit substitution. Deduction form. Version of ~ cbvaldva with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.) Reduce axiom usage, along an idea of GG. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Feb-2024.)
theorem cbvaldvaw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change the bound variable in an existential quantifier with implicit substitution. Deduction form. Version of ~ cbvexdva with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Feb-2024.)
theorem cbvexdvaw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbval2vv with more disjoint variable conditions, which requires fewer axioms . (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2005.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbval2vw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal z (wal w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvex2vv with more disjoint variable conditions, which requires fewer axioms . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbvex2vw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvex4v with more disjoint variable conditions, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbvex4vw {x3 y1 z w v u f g: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w f g) (ps: wff z w v u) (ch: wff x3 y1 v u f g):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv v)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv u))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv f)) (wceq (cv w) (cv g))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w ph)))) (wex v (wex u (wex f (wex g ch)))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ ax-11 . See ~ alcomw for the biconditional form. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem alcomimw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ excomim . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Jun-2025.)
theorem excomimw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ alcom and biconditional form of ~ alcomimw . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Dec-2024.)
theorem alcomw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z w) (ch: wff x3 z w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ alcom and biconditional form of ~ alcomimw . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Dec-2024.)
theorem alcomw_b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ excom and biconditional form of ~ excomimw . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by TM, 24-Jan-2026.)
theorem excomw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z w) (ch: wff x3 z w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ excom and biconditional form of ~ excomimw . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by TM, 24-Jan-2026.)
theorem excomw_b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ ax-10 from which we can prove any ~ ax-10 instance not involving wff variables or bundling. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Feb-2018.)
theorem hbn1fw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 ps) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wal y1 (wn ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal y1 ps)) (wal x3 (wn (wal y1 ps))) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ hbn1 . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.)
theorem hbn1w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ hba1 . See comments for ~ ax10w . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem hba1w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ hbe1 . See comments for ~ ax10w . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.)
theorem hbe1w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ hbal . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. Unlike ~ hbal , this theorem requires that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct, i.e., not be bundled. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2017.)
theorem hbalw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| If a formula is true, then it is true for at least one instance. This is to ~ 19.8a what ~ spw is to ~ sp . (Contributed by SN, 26-Sep-2024.)
theorem _19_8aw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Existential quantification over a given variable is idempotent. Weak version of ~ bj-exexbiex , requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by GG, 4-Nov-2024.)
theorem exexw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| A special instance of ~ sp applied to an equality with a disjoint variable condition. Unlike the more general ~ sp , we can prove this without ~ ax-12 . Instance of ~ aeveq . The antecedent ` A. x x = y ` with distinct ` x ` and ` y ` is a characteristic of a degenerate universe, in which just one object exists. Actually more than one object may still exist, but if so, we give up on equality as a discriminating term. Separating this degenerate case from a richer universe, where inequality is possible, is a common proof idea. The name of this theorem follows a convention, where the condition ` A. x x = y ` is denoted by 'aev', a shorthand for 'all equal, with a distinct variable condition'. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 14-Mar-2021.)
theorem spaev {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Change bound variable in an equality with a disjoint variable condition. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Revised by BJ, 18-Jun-2019.)
theorem cbvaev {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Change bound variable in an equality with a disjoint variable condition. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Revised by BJ, 18-Jun-2019.)
theorem cbvaev_b {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aevlem . Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 8-Jul-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 14-Mar-2021.) Extract from proof of a former lemma for ~ axc11n and add DV condition to reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem0 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem {x3 y1 z t: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem_b {x3 y1 t: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem_b2 {x3 y1 t: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem_b3 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aev and ~ axc16g . Change free and bound variables. Instance of ~ aev . (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem aevlem_b4 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| The antecedent ` A. x x = y ` with a disjoint variable condition (typical of a one-object universe) forces equality of everything. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aeveq {x3 y1 z: setvar} (t: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv z) (cv t)) $;

--| The antecedent ` A. x x = y ` with a disjoint variable condition (typical of a one-object universe) forces equality of everything. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aeveq_b {x3 y1: setvar} (t: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) $;

--| The antecedent ` A. x x = y ` with a disjoint variable condition (typical of a one-object universe) forces equality of everything. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aeveq_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (t: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) $;

--| The antecedent ` A. x x = y ` with a disjoint variable condition (typical of a one-object universe) forces equality of everything. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aeveq_b2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv z) (cv z)) $;

--| A "distinctor elimination" lemma with no disjoint variable conditions on variables in the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Sep-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , inspired by an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (u: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv t) (cv u))) $;

--| A "distinctor elimination" lemma with no disjoint variable conditions on variables in the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Sep-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , inspired by an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| A "distinctor elimination" lemma with no disjoint variable conditions on variables in the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Sep-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , inspired by an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (u: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv u))) $;

--| A "distinctor elimination" lemma with no disjoint variable conditions on variables in the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Sep-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , inspired by an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev_b2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| A "distinctor elimination" lemma with no disjoint variable conditions on variables in the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Sep-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , inspired by an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev_b3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (u: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv u))) $;

--| A version of ~ aev with two universal quantifiers in the consequent. One can prove similar statements with arbitrary numbers of universal quantifiers in the consequent (the series begins with ~ aeveq , ~ aev , ~ aev2 ). Using ~ aev and ~ alrimiv , one can actually prove (with no more axioms) any scheme of the form ` ( A. x x = y -> ` PHI) , DV ` ( x , y ) ` where PHI involves only setvar variables and the connectors ` -> ` , ` <-> ` , ` /\ ` , ` \/ ` , ` T. ` , ` = ` , ` A. ` , ` E. ` , ` E* ` , ` E! ` , ` F/ ` . An example is given by ~ aevdemo . This list cannot be extended to ` -. ` or ` F. ` since the scheme ` A. x x = y ` is consistent with ~ ax-mp , ~ ax-gen , ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-13 (as the one-element universe shows), so for instance ` ( A. x x = y -> F. ) , ` DV ` ( x , y ) ` is not provable from these axioms alone (indeed, ~ dtru uses non-logical axioms as well). (Contributed by BJ, 23-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev2 {x3 y1 z u t: setvar} (v: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wal t (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))) $;

--| A version of ~ aev with two universal quantifiers in the consequent. One can prove similar statements with arbitrary numbers of universal quantifiers in the consequent (the series begins with ~ aeveq , ~ aev , ~ aev2 ). Using ~ aev and ~ alrimiv , one can actually prove (with no more axioms) any scheme of the form ` ( A. x x = y -> ` PHI) , DV ` ( x , y ) ` where PHI involves only setvar variables and the connectors ` -> ` , ` <-> ` , ` /\ ` , ` \/ ` , ` T. ` , ` = ` , ` A. ` , ` E. ` , ` E* ` , ` E! ` , ` F/ ` . An example is given by ~ aevdemo . This list cannot be extended to ` -. ` or ` F. ` since the scheme ` A. x x = y ` is consistent with ~ ax-mp , ~ ax-gen , ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-13 (as the one-element universe shows), so for instance ` ( A. x x = y -> F. ) , ` DV ` ( x , y ) ` is not provable from these axioms alone (indeed, ~ dtru uses non-logical axioms as well). (Contributed by BJ, 23-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev2_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A version of ~ aev with two universal quantifiers in the consequent. One can prove similar statements with arbitrary numbers of universal quantifiers in the consequent (the series begins with ~ aeveq , ~ aev , ~ aev2 ). Using ~ aev and ~ alrimiv , one can actually prove (with no more axioms) any scheme of the form ` ( A. x x = y -> ` PHI) , DV ` ( x , y ) ` where PHI involves only setvar variables and the connectors ` -> ` , ` <-> ` , ` /\ ` , ` \/ ` , ` T. ` , ` = ` , ` A. ` , ` E. ` , ` E* ` , ` E! ` , ` F/ ` . An example is given by ~ aevdemo . This list cannot be extended to ` -. ` or ` F. ` since the scheme ` A. x x = y ` is consistent with ~ ax-mp , ~ ax-gen , ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-13 (as the one-element universe shows), so for instance ` ( A. x x = y -> F. ) , ` DV ` ( x , y ) ` is not provable from these axioms alone (indeed, ~ dtru uses non-logical axioms as well). (Contributed by BJ, 23-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev2_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A version of ~ aev with two universal quantifiers in the consequent. One can prove similar statements with arbitrary numbers of universal quantifiers in the consequent (the series begins with ~ aeveq , ~ aev , ~ aev2 ). Using ~ aev and ~ alrimiv , one can actually prove (with no more axioms) any scheme of the form ` ( A. x x = y -> ` PHI) , DV ` ( x , y ) ` where PHI involves only setvar variables and the connectors ` -> ` , ` <-> ` , ` /\ ` , ` \/ ` , ` T. ` , ` = ` , ` A. ` , ` E. ` , ` E* ` , ` E! ` , ` F/ ` . An example is given by ~ aevdemo . This list cannot be extended to ` -. ` or ` F. ` since the scheme ` A. x x = y ` is consistent with ~ ax-mp , ~ ax-gen , ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-13 (as the one-element universe shows), so for instance ` ( A. x x = y -> F. ) , ` DV ` ( x , y ) ` is not provable from these axioms alone (indeed, ~ dtru uses non-logical axioms as well). (Contributed by BJ, 23-Mar-2021.)
theorem aev2_b2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Version of ~ hbae with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Instance of ~ aev2 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Mar-2021.)
theorem hbaev {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Version of ~ hbae with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Instance of ~ aev2 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Mar-2021.)
theorem hbaev_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Version of ~ hbae with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. Instance of ~ aev2 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Mar-2021.)
theorem hbaev_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| If some set variables can assume different values, then any two distinct set variables cannot always be the same. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Aug-2019.)
theorem naev {x3 v u: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wn (wal u (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))) $;

--| If some set variables can assume different values, then any two distinct set variables cannot always be the same. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Aug-2019.)
theorem naev_b {x3 v: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv v)))) $;

--| If some set variables can assume different values, then any two distinct set variables cannot always be the same. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Aug-2019.)
theorem naev_b1 {x3 v u: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv u)))) (wn (wal u (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))) $;

--| Generalization of ~ hbnaev . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-Apr-2021.)
theorem naev2 {x3 z u t: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal z (wn (wal t (wceq (cv t) (cv u))))) $;

--| Generalization of ~ hbnaev . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-Apr-2021.)
theorem naev2_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wal z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) $;

--| Generalization of ~ hbnaev . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-Apr-2021.)
theorem naev2_b1 {x3 z u: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv u)))) (wal z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv u))))) $;

--| Any variable is free in ` -. A. x x = y ` , if ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct. This condition is dropped in ~ hbnae , at the expense of more axiom dependencies. Instance of ~ naev2 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Apr-2021.)
theorem hbnaev {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Any variable is free in ` -. A. x x = y ` , if ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct. This condition is dropped in ~ hbnae , at the expense of more axiom dependencies. Instance of ~ naev2 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Apr-2021.)
theorem hbnaev_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal y1 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Justification theorem for ~ df-sb proved from Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem sbjust {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (t: setvar):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) (wal z (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $;

--| Justification theorem for ~ df-sb proved from Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem sbjust_b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) (wal z (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include proper substitution. Read: "the wff that results when ` y ` is properly substituted for ` x ` in wff ` ph ` ". (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-2006.)
term wsb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar): wff;

--| Define proper substitution. For our notation, we use ` [ t / x ] ph ` to mean "the wff that results from the proper substitution of ` t ` for ` x ` in the wff ` ph ` ". That is, ` t ` properly replaces ` x ` . For example, ` [ t / x ] z e. x ` is the same as ` z e. t ` (when ` x ` and ` z ` are distinct), as shown in ~ elsb2 . Our notation was introduced in Haskell B. Curry's _Foundations of Mathematical Logic_ (1977), p. 316 and is frequently used in textbooks of lambda calculus and combinatory logic. This notation improves the common but ambiguous notation, " ` ph ( t ) ` is the wff that results when ` t ` is properly substituted for ` x ` in ` ph ( x ) ` ". For example, if the original ` ph ( x ) ` is ` x = t ` , then ` ph ( t ) ` is ` t = t ` , from which we obtain that ` ph ( x ) ` is ` x = x ` . So what exactly does ` ph ( x ) ` mean? Curry's notation solves this problem. A very similar notation, namely ` ( y | x ) ph ` , was introduced in Bourbaki's Set Theory (Chapter 1, Description of Formal Mathematic, 1953). In most books, proper substitution has a somewhat complicated recursive definition with multiple cases based on the occurrences of free and bound variables in the wff. Instead, we use a single formula that is exactly equivalent and gives us a direct definition. We later prove that our definition has the properties we expect of proper substitution (see Theorems ~ sbequ , ~ sbcom2 and ~ sbid2v ). Note that our definition is valid even when ` x ` and ` t ` are replaced with the same variable, as ~ sbid shows. We achieve this by applying twice Tarski's definition ~ sb6 which is valid for disjoint variables, and introducing a dummy variable ` y ` which isolates ` x ` from ` t ` , as in ~ dfsb7 with respect to ~ sb5 . We can also achieve this by having ` x ` free in the first conjunct and bound in the second, as the alternate definition ~ dfsb1 shows. Another version that mixes free and bound variables is ~ dfsb3 . When ` x ` and ` t ` are distinct, we can express proper substitution with the simpler expressions of ~ sb5 and ~ sb6 . Note that the occurrences of a given variable in the definiens are either all bound ( ` x , y ` ) or all free ( ` t ` ). Also note that the definiens uses only primitive symbols. This double level definition will make several proofs using it appear as doubled. Alternately, one could often first prove as a lemma the same theorem with a disjoint variable condition on the substitute and the substituted variables, and then prove the original theorem by applying this lemma twice in a row. The hypothesis asserts that the definition is independent of the particular choice of the dummy variable ` y ` . Without this hypothesis, ~ sbjust would be derivable from propositional axioms alone: one could apply the definiens for ` [ t / x ] ph ` twice, using different dummy variables ` y ` and ` z ` , and then invoke ~ bitr3i to establish their equivalence. This would jeopardize the independence of axioms, as demonstrated in an analoguous situation involving ~ df-ss to prove ~ ax-8 (see ~ in-ax8 ). Prefer ~ dfsb unless you can prove the hypothesis from fewer axioms in special cases, see ~ sbt . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) Revised from the original definition ~ dfsb1 . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) Add the justification hypothesis. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
axiom df_sb {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) (wal z (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| Define proper substitution. For our notation, we use ` [ t / x ] ph ` to mean "the wff that results from the proper substitution of ` t ` for ` x ` in the wff ` ph ` ". That is, ` t ` properly replaces ` x ` . For example, ` [ t / x ] z e. x ` is the same as ` z e. t ` (when ` x ` and ` z ` are distinct), as shown in ~ elsb2 . Our notation was introduced in Haskell B. Curry's _Foundations of Mathematical Logic_ (1977), p. 316 and is frequently used in textbooks of lambda calculus and combinatory logic. This notation improves the common but ambiguous notation, " ` ph ( t ) ` is the wff that results when ` t ` is properly substituted for ` x ` in ` ph ( x ) ` ". For example, if the original ` ph ( x ) ` is ` x = t ` , then ` ph ( t ) ` is ` t = t ` , from which we obtain that ` ph ( x ) ` is ` x = x ` . So what exactly does ` ph ( x ) ` mean? Curry's notation solves this problem. A very similar notation, namely ` ( y | x ) ph ` , was introduced in Bourbaki's Set Theory (Chapter 1, Description of Formal Mathematic, 1953). In most books, proper substitution has a somewhat complicated recursive definition with multiple cases based on the occurrences of free and bound variables in the wff. Instead, we use a single formula that is exactly equivalent and gives us a direct definition. We later prove that our definition has the properties we expect of proper substitution (see Theorems ~ sbequ , ~ sbcom2 and ~ sbid2v ). Note that our definition is valid even when ` x ` and ` t ` are replaced with the same variable, as ~ sbid shows. We achieve this by applying twice Tarski's definition ~ sb6 which is valid for disjoint variables, and introducing a dummy variable ` y ` which isolates ` x ` from ` t ` , as in ~ dfsb7 with respect to ~ sb5 . We can also achieve this by having ` x ` free in the first conjunct and bound in the second, as the alternate definition ~ dfsb1 shows. Another version that mixes free and bound variables is ~ dfsb3 . When ` x ` and ` t ` are distinct, we can express proper substitution with the simpler expressions of ~ sb5 and ~ sb6 . Note that the occurrences of a given variable in the definiens are either all bound ( ` x , y ` ) or all free ( ` t ` ). Also note that the definiens uses only primitive symbols. This double level definition will make several proofs using it appear as doubled. Alternately, one could often first prove as a lemma the same theorem with a disjoint variable condition on the substitute and the substituted variables, and then prove the original theorem by applying this lemma twice in a row. The hypothesis asserts that the definition is independent of the particular choice of the dummy variable ` y ` . Without this hypothesis, ~ sbjust would be derivable from propositional axioms alone: one could apply the definiens for ` [ t / x ] ph ` twice, using different dummy variables ` y ` and ` z ` , and then invoke ~ bitr3i to establish their equivalence. This would jeopardize the independence of axioms, as demonstrated in an analoguous situation involving ~ df-ss to prove ~ ax-8 (see ~ in-ax8 ). Prefer ~ dfsb unless you can prove the hypothesis from fewer axioms in special cases, see ~ sbt . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) Revised from the original definition ~ dfsb1 . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) Add the justification hypothesis. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
axiom df_sb_b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) (wal z (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph x3) (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| Simplify definition ~ df-sb by removing its provable hypothesis. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 5-Feb-2026.)
theorem dfsb {x3 y1 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| Simplify definition ~ df-sb by removing its provable hypothesis. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 5-Feb-2026.)
theorem dfsb_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph x3) (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| In the case of ~ sbt , the hypothesis in ~ df-sb is derivable from propositional axioms and ~ ax-gen alone. The essential proof step is presented in this lemma. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
theorem sbtlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (t: setvar):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| In the case of ~ sbt , the hypothesis in ~ df-sb is derivable from propositional axioms and ~ ax-gen alone. The essential proof step is presented in this lemma. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
theorem sbtlem_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| A substitution into a theorem yields a theorem. See ~ sbtALT for a shorter proof requiring more axioms. See ~ chvar and ~ chvarv for versions using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 6-Jul-2023.) Revise ~ df-sb again. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
theorem sbt {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wsb x3 ph t $;

--| A substitution into a theorem yields a theorem. See ~ sbtALT for a shorter proof requiring more axioms. See ~ chvar and ~ chvarv for versions using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jul-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 6-Jul-2023.) Revise ~ df-sb again. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2026.)
theorem sbt_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wsb x3 ph x3 $;

--| The result of substituting in the truth constant "true" is true. (Contributed by BJ, 2-Sep-2023.)
theorem sbtru {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wsb x3 wtru y1 $;

--| The specialization axiom of standard predicate calculus. It states that if a statement ` ph ` holds for all ` x ` , then it also holds for the specific case of ` t ` (properly) substituted for ` x ` . Translated to traditional notation, it can be read: " ` A. x ph ( x ) -> ph ( t ) ` , provided that ` t ` is free for ` x ` in ` ph ( x ) ` ". Axiom 4 of [Mendelson] p. 69. See also ~ spsbc and ~ rspsbc . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.)
theorem stdpc4 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wsb x3 ph t) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ sbt , shorter but using additional axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbtALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wsb x3 ph y1 $;

--| A double specialization using explicit substitution. This is Theorem PM*11.1 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 159. See ~ stdpc4 for the analogous single specialization. See ~ 2sp for another double specialization. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2stdpc4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Remove dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbi1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Remove dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbi1_b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) x3) (wi (wsb x3 ph x3) (wsb x3 ps x3)) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. Closed form of ~ sbimi . Specialization of implication. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem spsbim {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wsb x3 ph t) (wsb x3 ps t)) $;

--| Biconditional property for substitution. Closed form of ~ sbbii . Specialization of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.)
theorem spsbbi {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wsb x3 ps t)) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jun-1998.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbimi {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 t):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph t) (wsb x3 ps t) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jun-1998.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbimi_b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph x3) (wsb x3 ps x3) $;

--| Distribute substitution over implication. Compare ~ al2imi . (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem sb2imi {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 t):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph t) (wi (wsb x3 ps t) (wsb x3 ch t)) $;

--| Infer substitution into both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbbii {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 t):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wsb x3 ps t) $;

--| Infer double substitution into both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sbbii {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 u t):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph u) t) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ps u) t) $;

--| Infer double substitution into both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sbbii_b {x3 y1 u: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 u):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph u) y1) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ps u) y1) $;

--| Deduction substituting both sides of an implication, with ` ph ` and ` x ` disjoint. See also ~ sbimd . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 6-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbimdv {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (ps ch: wff x3 t):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wsb x3 ps t) (wsb x3 ch t)) $;

--| Deduction substituting both sides of a biconditional, with ` ph ` and ` x ` disjoint. See also ~ sbbid . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 6-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbbidv {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (ps ch: wff x3 t):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps t) (wsb x3 ch t)) $;

--| Conjunction inside and outside of a substitution are equivalent. Compare ~ 19.26 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem sban {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wa ph ps) y1) (wa (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Threefold conjunction inside and outside of a substitution are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2006.)
theorem sb3an {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (w3a ph ps ch) y1) (w3a (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1) (wsb x3 ch y1)) $;

--| Existential generalization: if a proposition is true for a specific instance, then there exists an instance where it is true. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-May-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 11-Jul-2023.)
theorem spsbe {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph t) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Equality property for substitution, from Tarski's system. Used in proof of Theorem 9.7 in [Megill] p. 449 (p. 16 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 30-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbequ {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wsb z ph x3) (wsb z ph y1)) $;

--| Equality property for substitution, from Tarski's system. Used in proof of Theorem 9.7 in [Megill] p. 449 (p. 16 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 30-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbequ_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wsb x3 ph x3) (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Equality property for substitution, from Tarski's system. Used in proof of Theorem 9.7 in [Megill] p. 449 (p. 16 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 30-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbequ_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wsb y1 ph x3) (wsb y1 ph y1)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ sbequ as of 10-Aug-2026, as per ~ conventions , paragraph "Biconditional". (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 7-Jul-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbequiOLD {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wsb z ph x3) (wsb z ph y1)) $;

--| Alternate definition of substitution when variables are disjoint. Compare Theorem 6.2 of [Quine] p. 40. Also proved as Lemmas 16 and 17 of [Tarski] p. 70. The implication "to the left" also holds without a disjoint variable condition ( ~ sb2 ). Theorem ~ sb6f replaces the disjoint variable condition with a nonfreeness hypothesis. Theorem ~ sb4b replaces it with a distinctor antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Sep-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) Remove use of ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 7-Jul-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Jul-2023.)
theorem sb6 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph)) $;

--| Equivalence for double substitution. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _2sb6 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ph))) $;

--| One direction of ~ sb5 , provable from fewer axioms. Version of ~ sb1 with a disjoint variable condition using fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem sb1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Substitution for a variable not occurring in a proposition. See ~ sbf for a version without disjoint variable condition on ` x , ph ` . If one adds a disjoint variable condition on ` x , t ` , then ~ sbv can be proved directly by chaining ~ equsv with ~ sb6 . (Contributed by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbv {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) ph $;

--| Commutativity law for substitution. This theorem was incorrectly used as our previous version of ~ pm11.07 but may still be useful. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jan-2018.)
theorem sbcom4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 w):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb z ph y1) w) (wsb x3 (wsb z ph w) y1) $;

--| Axiom *11.07 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 159. The original reads: *11.07 "Whatever possible argument ` x ` may be, ` ph ( x , y ) ` is true whatever possible argument ` y ` may be" implies the corresponding statement with ` x ` and ` y ` interchanged except in " ` ph ( x , y ) ` ". Under our formalism this appears to correspond to ~ idi and not to ~ sbcom4 as earlier thought. See ~ https://groups.google.com/g/metamath/c/iS0fOvSemC8/m/M1zTH8wxCAAJ . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pm11_07 (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Substitution in an implication with a variable not free in the antecedent affects only the consequent. Version of ~ sbrim based on fewer axioms, but with more disjoint variable conditions. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jan-2024.) Remove DV condition. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jun-2026.)
theorem sbrimvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi ph (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ sbrimvw as of 5-Jun-2026. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jan-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbrimvwOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi ph (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| An equivalence of substitutions (as in ~ sbbii ) allowing the additional information that ` x = t ` . Version of ~ sbiev and ~ sbievw without a disjoint variable condition on ` ps ` , useful for substituting only part of ` ph ` . (Contributed by SN, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem sbbiiev {x3 t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wsb x3 ps t) $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution. Version of ~ sbie and ~ sbiev with more disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by BJ, 18-Jul-2023.) (Proof shortened by SN, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem sbievw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution (deduction version of ~ sbievw ). Version of ~ sbied and ~ sbiedv with more disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by GG, 29-Jan-2024.)
theorem sbiedvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps y1) ch) $;

--| Conversion of double implicit substitution to explicit substitution. Version of ~ 2sbiev with more disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem _2sbievw {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 u t) (ps: wff u t):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv u))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph u) t) ps $;

--| Substituting ` y ` for ` x ` and then ` z ` for ` y ` is equivalent to substituting ` z ` for both ` x ` and ` y ` . Version of ~ sbcom3 with a disjoint variable condition using fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1997.) (Revised by Giovanni Mascellani, 8-Apr-2018.) (Revised by BJ, 30-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2023.)
theorem sbcom3vv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph y1) z) (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph z) z) $;

--| ~ sbievw applied twice, avoiding a DV condition on ` x ` , ` y ` . Based on proofs by Wolf Lammen. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbievw2 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1 w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)) (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Version of ~ sbco2 with disjoint variable conditions and fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem sbco2vv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ph z) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Change the bound variable (i.e. the substituted one) in wff's linked by implicit substitution. The proof was extracted from a former ~ cbvabv version. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem cbvsbv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (ps: wff y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph z) (wsb y1 ps z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbco4 . It replaces the temporary variable ` v ` with another temporary variable ` w ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by SN, 3-Sep-2025.)
theorem sbco4lem {x3 y1 w v: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb v (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph v) y1) x3) (wsb w (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) y1) x3) $;

--| Two ways of exchanging two variables. Both sides of the biconditional exchange ` x ` and ` y ` , either via two temporary variables ` u ` and ` v ` , or a single temporary ` w ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2018.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by SN, 3-Sep-2025.)
theorem sbco4 {x3 y1 w v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb u (wsb v (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph v) u) x3) y1) (wsb w (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) y1) x3) $;

--| Substitution in an equality. (Contributed by Raph Levien and FL, 4-Dec-2005.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 23-Jul-2023.)
theorem equsb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) y1) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) $;

--| Substitution in an equality. (Contributed by Raph Levien and FL, 4-Dec-2005.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 23-Jul-2023.)
theorem equsb3_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) y1) (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)) $;

--| Substitution applied to the atomic wff with equality. Variant of ~ equsb3 . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Sep-2023.)
theorem equsb3r {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) y1) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) $;

--| Substitution applied to an atomic wff. Version of ~ equsb1 with a disjoint variable condition, which neither requires ~ ax-12 nor ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 11-Sep-2019.) Remove dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-May-2023.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 19-Jun-2023.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 11-Jul-2023.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 22-Jul-2023.)
theorem equsb1v {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wsb x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) y1 $;

--| Any substitution in an always false formula is false. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 3-May-2023.)
theorem nsb {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wn ph)) (wn (wsb x3 ph t)) $;

--| One direction of ~ sbn , using fewer axioms. Compare ~ 19.2 . (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 18-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbn1 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wn ph) t) (wn (wsb x3 ph t)) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the membership connective between classes. For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . The purpose of introducing ` wff A e. B ` here is to allow to prove the ~ wel of predicate calculus in terms of the ~ wcel of set theory, so that we do not overload the ` e. ` connective with two syntax definitions. This is done to prevent ambiguity that would complicate some Metamath parsers. The class variables ` A ` and ` B ` are introduced temporarily for the purpose of this definition but otherwise not used in predicate calculus. See ~ df-clab for more information on the set theory usage of ~ wcel .
term wcel (A1 B1: class): wff;

--| Axiom of Left Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the left-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C12' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). "Non-logical" means that the predicate is not a primitive of predicate calculus proper but instead is an extension to it. "Binary" means that the predicate has two arguments. In a system of predicate calculus with equality, like ours, equality is not usually considered to be a non-logical predicate. In systems of predicate calculus without equality, it typically would be. We prove in ~ ax8 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax8v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-8 should be ~ ax8v . See the comment of ~ ax8v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_8 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Axiom of Left Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the left-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C12' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). "Non-logical" means that the predicate is not a primitive of predicate calculus proper but instead is an extension to it. "Binary" means that the predicate has two arguments. In a system of predicate calculus with equality, like ours, equality is not usually considered to be a non-logical predicate. In systems of predicate calculus without equality, it typically would be. We prove in ~ ax8 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax8v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-8 should be ~ ax8v . See the comment of ~ ax8v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_8_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Axiom of Left Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the left-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C12' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). "Non-logical" means that the predicate is not a primitive of predicate calculus proper but instead is an extension to it. "Binary" means that the predicate has two arguments. In a system of predicate calculus with equality, like ours, equality is not usually considered to be a non-logical predicate. In systems of predicate calculus without equality, it typically would be. We prove in ~ ax8 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax8v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-8 should be ~ ax8v . See the comment of ~ ax8v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_8_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-8 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-8 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , from which ~ ax-8 is then rederived as ~ ax8 , which shows that either ~ ax8v or the conjunction of ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax8v (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-8 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-8 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , from which ~ ax-8 is then rederived as ~ ax8 , which shows that either ~ ax8v or the conjunction of ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax8v_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-8 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-8 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , from which ~ ax-8 is then rederived as ~ ax8 , which shows that either ~ ax8v or the conjunction of ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax8 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax8v_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax8v1 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax8v1_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax8v2 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax8v2_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-8 from ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-8 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax8 {x3: setvar} (y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-8 from ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-8 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax8_b {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-8 from ~ ax8v1 and ~ ax8v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax8v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-8 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax8_b1 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ1 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ1_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ1_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Substitution for the first argument of the non-logical predicate in an atomic formula. See ~ elsb2 for substitution for the second argument. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem elsb1 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) y1) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) $;

--| When the class variables in Definition ~ df-clel are replaced with setvar variables, this theorem of predicate calculus is the result. This theorem provides part of the justification for the consistency of that definition, which "overloads" the setvar variables in ~ wel with the class variables in ~ wcel . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2004.) Revised to use ~ equsexvw in order to remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-13 . Note that there is no disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , that is, on the variables of the left-hand side, as should be the case for definitions. (Revised by BJ, 29-Dec-2020.)
theorem cleljust {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| When the class variables in Definition ~ df-clel are replaced with setvar variables, this theorem of predicate calculus is the result. This theorem provides part of the justification for the consistency of that definition, which "overloads" the setvar variables in ~ wel with the class variables in ~ wcel . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2004.) Revised to use ~ equsexvw in order to remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-13 . Note that there is no disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , that is, on the variables of the left-hand side, as should be the case for definitions. (Revised by BJ, 29-Dec-2020.)
theorem cleljust_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Axiom of Right Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the right-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C13' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). We prove in ~ ax9 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax9v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-9 should be ~ ax9v . See the comment of ~ ax9v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_9 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Axiom of Right Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the right-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C13' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). We prove in ~ ax9 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax9v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-9 should be ~ ax9v . See the comment of ~ ax9v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_9_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Axiom of Right Equality for Binary Predicate. One of the equality and substitution axioms for a non-logical predicate in our predicate calculus with equality. It substitutes equal variables into the right-hand side of an arbitrary binary predicate ` e. ` , which we will use for the set membership relation when set theory is introduced. This axiom scheme is a sub-scheme of Axiom Scheme B8 of system S2 of [Tarski], p. 75, whose general form cannot be represented with our notation. Also appears as Axiom scheme C13' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). We prove in ~ ax9 that this axiom can be recovered from its weakened version ~ ax9v where ` x ` and ` y ` are assumed to be disjoint variables. In particular, the only theorem referencing ~ ax-9 should be ~ ax9v . See the comment of ~ ax9v for more details on these matters. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_9_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-9 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-9 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , from which ~ ax-9 is then rederived as ~ ax9 , which shows that either ~ ax9v or the conjunction of ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax9v (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-9 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-9 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , from which ~ ax-9 is then rederived as ~ ax9 , which shows that either ~ ax9v or the conjunction of ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax9v_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Weakened version of ~ ax-9 , with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . This should be the only proof referencing ~ ax-9 , and it should be referenced only by its two weakened versions ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , from which ~ ax-9 is then rederived as ~ ax9 , which shows that either ~ ax9v or the conjunction of ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) Use ~ ax9 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax9v_b1 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax9v1 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| First of two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` , see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax9v1_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax9v2 (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Second of two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , with an extra disjoint variable condition on ` y , z ` see comments there. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.)
theorem ax9v2_b (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-9 from ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-9 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax9 {x3: setvar} (y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-9 from ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-9 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax9_b {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-9 from ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-9 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax9_b1 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-9 from ~ ax9v1 and ~ ax9v2 , proving sufficiency of the conjunction of the latter two weakened versions of ~ ax9v , which is itself a weakened version of ~ ax-9 . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Apr-2021.)
theorem ax9_b2 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ2 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ2_b {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ2_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem elequ2_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| A form of ~ elequ2 with a universal quantifier. Its converse is the axiom of extensionality ~ ax-ext . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem elequ2g {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A form of ~ elequ2 with a universal quantifier. Its converse is the axiom of extensionality ~ ax-ext . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem elequ2g_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Substitution for the second argument of the non-logical predicate in an atomic formula. See ~ elsb1 for substitution for the first argument. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 3-Apr-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Jul-2023.)
theorem elsb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) y1) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate, which combines ~ elequ1 and ~ elequ2 . The analogous theorems for class terms are ~ eleq1 , ~ eleq2 , and ~ eleq12 respectively. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Sep-2019.)
theorem elequ12 {x3 y1 z t: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv z) (cv t))) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv t))) $;

--| An identity law for the non-logical predicate, which combines ~ elequ1 and ~ elequ2 . The analogous theorems for class terms are ~ eleq1 , ~ eleq2 , and ~ eleq12 respectively. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Sep-2019.)
theorem elequ12_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) $;

--| The FOL statement used in the standard proof of Russell's paradox ~ ru . (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) Extract from proof of ~ ru and reduce axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 12-Oct-2019.)
theorem ru0 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Tarski's system uses the weaker ~ ax6v instead of the bundled ~ ax-6 , so here we show that the degenerate case of ~ ax-6 can be derived. Even though ~ ax-6 is in the list of axioms used, recall that in set.mm, the only statement referencing ~ ax-6 is ~ ax6v . We later rederive from ~ ax6v the bundled form as ~ ax6 with the help of the auxiliary axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax6dgen {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ ax-10 from which we can prove any ~ ax-10 instance not involving wff variables or bundling. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. It is an alias of ~ hbn1w introduced for labeling consistency. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2017.) Use ~ hbn1w instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax10w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ ax-11 from which we can prove any ~ ax-11 instance not involving wff variables or bundling. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. Unlike ~ ax-11 , this theorem requires that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct i.e. are not bundled. It is an alias of ~ alcomimw introduced for labeling consistency. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.) Use ~ alcomimw instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax11w {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-11 where bundled variables ` x ` and ` y ` have a common substitution. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax11dgen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Lemma for weak version of ~ ax-12 . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. In some cases, this lemma may lead to shorter proofs than ~ ax12w . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax12wlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ ax-12 from which we can prove any ~ ax-12 instance not involving wff variables or bundling. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. An instance of the first hypothesis will normally require that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct (unless ` x ` does not occur in ` ph ` ). For an example of how the hypotheses can be eliminated when we substitute an expression without wff variables for ` ph ` , see ~ ax12wdemo . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax12w {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-12 where bundled variables ` x ` and ` y ` have a common substitution. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax12dgen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) ph))) $;

--| Example of an application of ~ ax12w that results in an instance of ~ ax-12 for a contrived formula with mixed free and bound variables, ` ( x e. y /\ A. x z e. x /\ A. y A. z y e. x ) ` , in place of ` ph ` . The proof illustrates bound variable renaming with ~ cbvalvw to obtain fresh variables to avoid distinct variable clashes. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 14-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax12wdemo {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wal y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) (wal y1 (wal z (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) (wal y1 (wal z (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))))) $;

--| Weak version (principal instance) of ~ ax-13 . (Because ` y ` and ` z ` don't need to be distinct, this actually bundles the principal instance and the degenerate instance ` ( -. x = y -> ( y = y -> A. x y = y ) ) ` .) Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. The proof is trivial but is included to complete the set ~ ax10w , ~ ax11w , and ~ ax12w . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax13w {x3: setvar} (y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-13 where bundled variables ` x ` and ` y ` have a common substitution. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax13dgen1 {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-13 where bundled variables ` x ` and ` z ` have a common substitution. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax13dgen2 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-13 where bundled variables ` y ` and ` z ` have a common substitution. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.)
theorem ax13dgen3 {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Degenerate instance of ~ ax-13 where bundled variables ` x ` , ` y ` , and ` z ` have a common substitution. Therefore, also a degenerate instance of ~ ax13dgen1 , ~ ax13dgen2 , and ~ ax13dgen3 . Also an instance of the intuitionistic tautology ~ pm2.21 . Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2017.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem ax13dgen4 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Axiom of Quantified Negation. Axiom C5-2 of [Monk2] p. 113. This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax10w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve scheme completeness. It means that ` x ` is not free in ` -. A. x ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 21-May-2008.) Use its alias ~ hbn1 instead if you must use it. Any theorem in first-order logic (FOL) that contains only set variables that are all mutually distinct, and has no wff variables, can be proved *without* using ~ ax-10 through ~ ax-13 , by invoking ~ ax10w through ~ ax13w . We encourage proving theorems *without* ~ ax-10 through ~ ax-13 and moving them up to the ~ ax-4 through ~ ax-9 section. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_10 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-10 to be used instead of it. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem hbn1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` E. x ph ` . Corresponds to the axiom (5) of modal logic (see also ~ modal5 ). (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.)
theorem hbe1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Dual statement of ~ hbe1 . Modified version of ~ axc7e with a universally quantified consequent. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem hbe1a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Since the converse holds by ~ a1i , this inference shows that we can represent a not-free hypothesis with either ` F/ x ph ` (inference form) or ` ( ph -> F/ x ph ) ` (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2019.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by SN and Sky Winter, 23-Oct-2026.)
theorem nf5di {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ph) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| One direction of ~ nf5 can be proved with a smaller footprint on axiom usage. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem nf5_1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ph))) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Deduce that ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` from the definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nf5i {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| Deduce that ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` in a context. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem nf5dh {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Apply the definition of not-free in a context. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jul-2022.)
theorem nf5dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Version of ~ nfnae with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Sep-2024.)
theorem nfnaew {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wnf z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Version of ~ nfnae with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Sep-2024.)
theorem nfnaew_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnf y1 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` E. x ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfe1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wex x3 ph) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` A. x ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfa1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wal x3 ph) $;

--| A convenience theorem particularly designed to remove dependencies on ~ ax-11 in conjunction with distinctors. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 2-Sep-2018.)
theorem nfna1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wn (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Lemma 23 of [Monk2] p. 114. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nfia1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` F/ x ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfnf1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| The analogue in our predicate calculus of axiom (5) of modal logic S5. See also ~ hbe1 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2005.)
theorem modal5 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn ph))) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 (wn ph)))) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Shorten ~ nfs1v and ~ hbs1 combined. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2022.)
theorem nfs1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Axiom of Quantifier Commutation. This axiom says universal quantifiers can be swapped. Axiom scheme C6' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). Also appears as Lemma 12 of [Monk2] p. 109 and Axiom C5-3 of [Monk2] p. 113. This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax11w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve metalogical completeness. Use its weak version ~ alcomimw when it allows to avoid dependence on ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
axiom ax_11 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Axiom of Quantifier Commutation. This axiom says universal quantifiers can be swapped. Axiom scheme C6' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). Also appears as Lemma 12 of [Monk2] p. 109 and Axiom C5-3 of [Monk2] p. 113. This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax11w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve metalogical completeness. Use its weak version ~ alcomimw when it allows to avoid dependence on ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
axiom ax_11_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Swap quantifiers in an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.)
theorem alcoms {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) ps $;

--| Theorem 19.5 of [Margaris] p. 89. Use its weak version ~ alcomw when it allows to avoid dependence on ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.)
theorem alcom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Theorem *11.21 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 160. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem alrot3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z ph))) (wal y1 (wal z (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| Rotate four universal quantifiers twice. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by Fan Zheng, 6-Jun-2016.)
theorem alrot4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wal w ph)))) (wal z (wal w (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)))) $;

--| Theorem 19.11 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-5 , ~ ax-6 , ~ ax-7 , ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Jan-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem excom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| One direction of Theorem 19.11 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-5 , ~ ax-6 , ~ ax-7 , ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Jan-2018.)
theorem excomim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Swap 1st and 3rd existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.)
theorem excom13 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ph))) (wex z (wex y1 (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Rotate existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1995.)
theorem exrot3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z ph))) (wex y1 (wex z (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Rotate existential quantifiers twice. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.)
theorem exrot4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w ph)))) (wex z (wex w (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)))) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , it is not free in ` A. y ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem hbal {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Deduction form of bound-variable hypothesis builder ~ hbal . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.)
theorem hbald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal y1 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal y1 ps) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Move universal quantifier in and out of substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Sep-2018.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbal {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wal x3 ph) z) (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph z)) $;

--| Move universal quantifier in and out of substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Sep-2018.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbal_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wal x3 ph) y1) (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph y1)) $;

--| Quantify with new variable inside substitution. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem sbalv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wal z ph) y1) (wal z ps) $;

--| If ` z ` is not free in ` ph ` , it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` y ` and ` z ` are distinct. Version of ~ hbsb with a disjoint variable condition, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) Remove dependencies on axioms. (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-May-2025.)
theorem hbsbw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal z ph) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal z (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| If ` z ` is not free in ` ph ` , it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` y ` and ` z ` are distinct. Version of ~ hbsb with a disjoint variable condition, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) Remove dependencies on axioms. (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-May-2025.)
theorem hbsbw_b {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z):
  $ wi ph (wal z ph) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph x3) (wal z (wsb x3 ph x3)) $;

--| Commutativity law for substitution. Used in proof of Theorem 9.7 of [Megill] p. 449 (p. 16 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1997.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Dec-2022.)
theorem sbcom2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ph y1) w) (wsb x3 (wsb z ph w) y1) $;

--| Lemma 24 of [Monk2] p. 114. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfa2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Version of ~ nfex with the existential dual to the 'h' hypothesis, avoiding ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by SN, 11-Feb-2026.)
theorem nfexhe {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wex y1 ph) $;

--| An inner universal quantifier's variable is bound. (Contributed by SN, 11-Feb-2026.)
theorem nfexa2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 (wex y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Axiom of Substitution. One of the 5 equality axioms of predicate calculus. The final consequent ` A. x ( x = y -> ph ) ` is a way of expressing " ` y ` substituted for ` x ` in wff ` ph ` " (cf. ~ sb6 ). It is based on Lemma 16 of [Tarski] p. 70 and Axiom C8 of [Monk2] p. 105, from which it can be proved by cases. The original version of this axiom was ~ ax-c15 and was replaced with this shorter ~ ax-12 in Jan. 2007. The old axiom is proved from this one as Theorem ~ axc15 . Conversely, this axiom is proved from ~ ax-c15 as Theorem ~ ax12 . Juha Arpiainen proved the metalogical independence of this axiom (in the form of the older axiom ~ ax-c15 ) from the others on 19-Jan-2006. See item 9a at ~ https://us.metamath.org/award2003.html . See ~ ax12v and ~ ax12v2 for other equivalents of this axiom that (unlike this axiom) have distinct variable restrictions. This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax12w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve scheme completeness. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Axiom of Substitution. One of the 5 equality axioms of predicate calculus. The final consequent ` A. x ( x = y -> ph ) ` is a way of expressing " ` y ` substituted for ` x ` in wff ` ph ` " (cf. ~ sb6 ). It is based on Lemma 16 of [Tarski] p. 70 and Axiom C8 of [Monk2] p. 105, from which it can be proved by cases. The original version of this axiom was ~ ax-c15 and was replaced with this shorter ~ ax-12 in Jan. 2007. The old axiom is proved from this one as Theorem ~ axc15 . Conversely, this axiom is proved from ~ ax-c15 as Theorem ~ ax12 . Juha Arpiainen proved the metalogical independence of this axiom (in the form of the older axiom ~ ax-c15 ) from the others on 19-Jan-2006. See item 9a at ~ https://us.metamath.org/award2003.html . See ~ ax12v and ~ ax12v2 for other equivalents of this axiom that (unlike this axiom) have distinct variable restrictions. This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax12w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve scheme completeness. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_12_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) ph))) $;

--| This is essentially Axiom ~ ax-12 weakened by additional restrictions on variables. Besides ~ axc11r , this theorem should be the only one referencing ~ ax-12 directly. Both restrictions on variables have their own value. If for a moment we assume ` x ` could be set to ` y ` , then, after elimination of the tautology ` y = y ` , immediately we have ` ph -> A. y ph ` for all ` ph ` and ` y ` , that is ~ ax-5 , a degenerate result. The second restriction is not necessary, but a simplification that makes the following interpretation easier to see. Since ` ph ` textually at most depends on ` x ` , we can look at it at some given 'fixed' ` y ` . This theorem now states that the truth value of ` ph ` will stay constant, as long as we 'vary ` x ` around ` y ` ' only such that ` x = y ` still holds. Or in other words, equality is the finest grained logical expression. If you cannot differ two sets by ` = ` , you won't find a whatever sophisticated expression that does. One might wonder how the described variation of ` x ` is possible at all. Note that Metamath is a text processor that easily sees a difference between text chunks ` { x | -. x = x } ` and ` { y | -. y = y } ` . Our usual interpretation is to abstract from textual variations of the same set, but we are free to interpret Metamath's formalism differently, and in fact let ` x ` run through all textual representations of sets. Had we allowed ` ph ` to depend also on ` y ` , this idea is both harder to see, and it is less clear that this extra freedom introduces effects not covered by other axioms. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Aug-2020.)
theorem ax12v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| It is possible to remove any restriction on ` ph ` in ~ ax12v . Same as Axiom C8 of [Monk2] p. 105. Use ~ ax12v instead when sufficient. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 and ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 15-Dec-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem ax12v2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax12v2 rewritten to use an existential quantifier. One direction of ~ sbalex without the universal quantifier, avoiding ~ ax-10 . (Contributed by SN, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem ax12ev2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) $;

--| A commuted form of ~ ax12ev2 . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Sep-2026.)
theorem ax12ev2c {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ph) $;

--| If a wff is true, it is true for at least one instance. Special case of Theorem 19.8 of [Margaris] p. 89. See ~ 19.8v for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-1993.) Allow a shortening of ~ sp . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jan-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem _19_8a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ph) $;

--| If a wff is true, it is true for at least one instance. Deduction form of ~ 19.8a . (Contributed by DAW, 13-Feb-2017.)
theorem _19_8ad {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Specialization. A universally quantified wff implies the wff without a quantifier. Axiom scheme B5 of [Tarski] p. 67 (under his system S2, defined in the last paragraph on p. 77). Also appears as Axiom scheme C5' in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint). This corresponds to the axiom (T) of modal logic. For the axiom of specialization presented in many logic textbooks, see Theorem ~ stdpc4 . This theorem shows that our obsolete axiom ~ ax-c5 can be derived from the others. The proof uses ideas from the proof of Lemma 21 of [Monk2] p. 114. It appears that this scheme cannot be derived directly from Tarski's axioms without auxiliary axiom scheme ~ ax-12 . It is thought the best we can do using only Tarski's axioms is ~ spw . Also see ~ spvw where ` x ` and ` ph ` are disjoint, using fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-2008.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 24-Jan-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jan-2018.)
theorem sp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Inference rule of universal instantiation, or universal specialization. Converse of the inference rule of (universal) generalization ~ ax-gen . Contrary to the rule of generalization, its closed form is valid, see ~ sp . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem spi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Generalization of antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-1993.)
theorem sps {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| A double specialization (see ~ sp ). Another double specialization, closer to PM*11.1, is ~ 2stdpc4 . (Contributed by BJ, 15-Sep-2018.)
theorem _2sp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) ph $;

--| Deduction generalizing antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-1994.)
theorem spsd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Theorem 19.2 of [Margaris] p. 89, generalized to use two setvar variables. Use ~ 19.2 when sufficient. (Contributed by Mel L. O'Cat, 31-Mar-2008.)
theorem _19_2g {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wex y1 ph) $;

--| Inference form of ~ 19.21 and also deduction form of ~ sp . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem _19_21bi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Inference removing two universal quantifiers. Version of ~ 19.21bi with two quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Apr-1994.)
theorem _19_21bbi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_23bi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Inference associated with the contrapositive of ~ 19.8a . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 26-Jul-2009.)
theorem nexr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wn (wex x3 ph) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Quantified excluded middle (see ~ exmid ). Also known as the drinker paradox (if ` ph ( x ) ` is interpreted as " ` x ` drinks", then this theorem tells that there exists a person such that, if this person drinks, then everyone drinks). Exercise 9.2a of Boolos, p. 111, _Computability and Logic_. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2000.)
theorem qexmid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Consequence of the definition of not-free. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Sep-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2023.)
theorem nf5r {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Consequence of the definition of not-free. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Mar-2023.)
theorem nf5ri {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Consequence of the definition of not-free in a context. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nf5rd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ spimev . Version of ~ spimed with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ spime for a non-deduction version. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem spimedv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ch (wnf x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ spime with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem spimefv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| A closed form of ~ nfim . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfim1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wi ph ps) $;

--| A closed form of ~ nfan . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Jul-2022.)
theorem nfan1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wa ph ps) $;

--| Closed form of ~ 19.3 and version of ~ 19.9t with a universal quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2020.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 9-Oct-2022.)
theorem _19_3t {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wb (wal x3 ph) ph) $;

--| A wff may be quantified with a variable not free in it. Version of ~ 19.9 with a universal quantifier. Theorem 19.3 of [Margaris] p. 89. See ~ 19.3v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem _19_3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| A deduction version of one direction of ~ 19.9 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) Revised to shorten other proofs. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jul-2020.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Jul-2022.)
theorem _19_9d {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ps (wnf x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wex x3 ph) ph) $;

--| Closed form of ~ 19.9 and version of ~ 19.3t with an existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem _19_9t {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wb (wex x3 ph) ph) $;

--| A wff may be existentially quantified with a variable not free in it. Version of ~ 19.3 with an existential quantifier. Theorem 19.9 of [Margaris] p. 89. See ~ 19.9v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by FL, 24-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Dec-2017.) Revised to shorten other proofs. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem _19_9 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) ph $;

--| Closed form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.21 . (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2018.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 3-Nov-2021.)
theorem _19_21t {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps))) $;

--| Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. The hypothesis can be thought of as " ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` ". See ~ 19.21v for a version requiring fewer axioms. See also ~ 19.21h . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem _19_21 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| An axiom scheme of standard predicate calculus that emulates Axiom 5 of [Mendelson] p. 69. The hypothesis ` F/ x ph ` can be thought of as emulating " ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` ". With this definition, the meaning of "not free" is less restrictive than the usual textbook definition; for example ` x ` would not (for us) be free in ` x = x ` by ~ nfequid . This theorem scheme can be proved as a metatheorem of Mendelson's axiom system, even though it is slightly stronger than his Axiom 5. See ~ stdpc5v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem stdpc5 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.21 with two quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2005.)
theorem _19_21_2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi ph (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Closed form of Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Aug-2020.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem _19_23t {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ps) (wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.23v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem _19_23 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ alim . See ~ alimdh , ~ alimdv for variants requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem alimd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Inference form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.21 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem alrimi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.21 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem alrimdd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ 19.21 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem alrimd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90, see ~ exim . (Contributed by NM, 29-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem eximd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.23 . See ~ exlimiv for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem exlimi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ps $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.9 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jan-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jan-2018.)
theorem exlimd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Existential elimination rule of natural deduction. (Contributed by ML, 17-Jul-2020.) Shorten ~ exlimdd . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 3-Sep-2023.)
theorem exlimimdd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Existential elimination rule of natural deduction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Sep-2023.)
theorem exlimdd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction for generalization rule for negated wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nexd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for universal quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem albid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for existential quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem exbid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex x3 ch)) $;

--| An equality theorem for effectively not free. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem nfbidf {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnf x3 ps) (wnf x3 ch)) $;

--| Theorem 19.16 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_16 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.17 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_17 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.27 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.27v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_27 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.28 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.28v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-May-2025.)
theorem _19_28 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.19 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_19 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.36 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.36v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_36 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.36 . See ~ 19.36iv for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_36i {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wex x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Theorem 19.37 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.37v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _19_37 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.32 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.32v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem _19_32 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.31 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.31v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem _19_31 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.41 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.41v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Jan-2018.)
theorem _19_41 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.42 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.42v for a version requiring fewer axioms. See ~ exan for an immediate version. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem _19_42 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.44 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.44v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_44 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem 19.45 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.45v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.)
theorem _19_45 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Specialization, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ spim with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ spimvw for a version with two disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms, and ~ spimv for another variant. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem spimfv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Implicit substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` into a theorem. Version of ~ chvar with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Raph Levien, 9-Jul-2003.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem chvarfv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Version of ~ cbv3 with two disjoint variable conditions, which does not require ~ ax-11 nor ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem cbv3v2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Equivalence of two ways to express proper substitution of a setvar for another setvar disjoint from it in a formula. This proof of their equivalence does not use ~ df-sb . That both sides of the biconditional express proper substitution is proved by ~ sb5 and ~ sb6 . The implication "to the left" is ~ equs4v and does not require ~ ax-10 nor ~ ax-12 . It also holds without disjoint variable condition if we allow more axioms (see ~ equs4 ). Theorem 6.2 of [Quine] p. 40. Theorem ~ equs5 replaces the disjoint variable condition with a distinctor antecedent. Theorem ~ equs45f replaces the disjoint variable condition on ` x , t ` with the nonfreeness hypothesis of ` t ` in ` ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Apr-2008.) Revised to use ~ equsexv in place of ~ equsex in order to remove dependency on ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ, 20-Dec-2020.) Revise to remove dependency on ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 21-Sep-2024.) (Proof shortened by SN, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem sbalex {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph)) $;

--| Version of ~ sb4a with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . The distinctor antecedent from ~ sb4b is replaced by a disjoint variable condition in this theorem. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Revised by BJ, 15-Dec-2023.)
theorem sb4av {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wal t ph) t) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph)) $;

--| Deduction substituting both sides of an implication. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2022.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 9-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbimd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wsb x3 ps y1) (wsb x3 ch y1)) $;

--| Deduction substituting both sides of a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 and ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2022.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 11-Jul-2023.)
theorem sbbid {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps y1) (wsb x3 ch y1)) $;

--| Deduction doubly substituting both sides of a biconditional. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sbbid {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1 u t):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ps u) t) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ch u) t)) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbequ1 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (wi ph (wsb x3 ph t)) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbequ1_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi ph (wsb x3 ph x3)) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Feb-2024.)
theorem sbequ2 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (wi (wsb x3 ph t) ph) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 22-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Feb-2024.)
theorem sbequ2_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wi (wsb x3 ph x3) ph) $;

--| One of the two equality axioms of standard predicate calculus, called substitutivity of equality. (The other one is ~ stdpc6 .) Translated to traditional notation, it can be read: " ` x = y -> ( ph ( x , x ) -> ph ( x , y ) ) ` , provided that ` y ` is free for ` x ` in ` ph ( x , x ) ` ". Axiom 7 of [Mendelson] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2005.)
theorem stdpc7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wsb y1 ph x3) ph) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbequ12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbequ12_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wb ph (wsb x3 ph x3)) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 21-Jun-2011.)
theorem sbequ12r {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wsb y1 ph x3) ph) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 21-Jun-2011.)
theorem sbequ12r_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wb (wsb x3 ph x3) ph) $;

--| Elimination of substitution. Also see ~ sbel2x . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Aug-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 20-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbelx {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1):
  $ wb ph (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wsb y1 ph x3))) $;

--| An equality theorem for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Jun-2019.)
theorem sbequ12a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb y1 ph x3)) $;

--| An identity theorem for substitution. Remark 9.1 in [Megill] p. 447 (p. 15 of the preprint). (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Sep-2018.)
theorem sbid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph x3) ph $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Version of ~ sbco with a disjoint variable condition using fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by GG, 7-Aug-2023.) (Proof shortened by SN, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem sbcov {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph x3) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Equivalence for substitution. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Sep-2018.)
theorem sb6a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wsb y1 ph x3))) $;

--| Reverting substitution yields the original expression. Based on fewer axioms than ~ sbid2v , at the expense of an extra distinct variable condition. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 5-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbid2vw {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb t ph x3) t) ph $;

--| Generalization of ~ axc16 . Use the latter when sufficient. This proof only requires, on top of { ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-7 }, Theorem ~ ax12v . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Jul-2021.) Shorten ~ axc11rv . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc16g {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal z ph)) $;

--| Generalization of ~ axc16 . Use the latter when sufficient. This proof only requires, on top of { ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-7 }, Theorem ~ ax12v . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Jul-2021.) Shorten ~ axc11rv . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc16g_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Generalization of ~ axc16 . Use the latter when sufficient. This proof only requires, on top of { ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-7 }, Theorem ~ ax12v . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 , along an idea of BJ. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) (Revised by BJ, 7-Jul-2021.) Shorten ~ axc11rv . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc16g_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Proof of older axiom ~ ax-c16 . (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2006.) (Revised by NM, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem axc16 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Biconditional strengthening of ~ axc16g . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.)
theorem axc16gb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph (wal z ph)) $;

--| If ~ dtru is false, then there is only one element in the universe, so everything satisfies ` F/ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 14-Jun-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc16nf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wnf z ph) $;

--| If ~ dtru is false, then there is only one element in the universe, so everything satisfies ` F/ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 14-Jun-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc16nf_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wnf x3 ph) $;

--| Version of ~ axc11 with a disjoint variable condition on ` x ` and ` y ` , which is provable, on top of { ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-7 }, from ~ ax12v (contrary to ~ axc11 which seems to require the full ~ ax-12 and ~ ax-13 ). (Contributed by NM, 16-May-2008.) (Revised by BJ, 6-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc11v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Version of ~ axc11r with a disjoint variable condition on ` x ` and ` y ` , which is provable, on top of { ~ ax-1 -- ~ ax-7 }, from ~ ax12v (contrary to ~ axc11 which seems to require the full ~ ax-12 and ~ ax-13 , and to ~ axc11r which seems to require the full ~ ax-12 ). (Contributed by BJ, 6-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem axc11rv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem drsb2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsb z ph x3) (wsb z ph y1)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem drsb2_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsb x3 ph x3) (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem drsb2_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsb y1 ph x3) (wsb y1 ph y1)) $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Version of ~ equsal with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsalvw for a version with two disjoint variable conditions requiring fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsexv . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem equsalv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Version of ~ equsex with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsexvw for a version with two disjoint variable conditions requiring fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsalv . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem equsexv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| Substitution has no effect on a nonfree variable. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-May-2018.)
theorem sbft {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wnf x3 ph) (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ph) $;

--| Substitution for a variable not free in a wff does not affect it. For a version requiring disjoint variables but fewer axioms, see ~ sbv . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.)
theorem sbf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ph $;

--| Substitution has no effect on a bound variable. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jul-2005.)
theorem sbf2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wal x3 ph) y1) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Substitution for a variable not free in a wff does not affect it. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbh {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ph $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem hbs1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfs1f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Alternate definition of substitution when variables are disjoint. Similar to Theorem 6.1 of [Quine] p. 40. The implication "to the right" is ~ sb1v and even needs no disjoint variable condition, see ~ sb1 . Theorem ~ sb5f replaces the disjoint variable condition with a nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Sep-2023.)
theorem sb5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| A property related to substitution that replaces the distinctor from ~ equs5 to a disjoint variable condition. Version of ~ equs5a with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See also ~ sbalex . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Revised by GG, 15-Dec-2023.)
theorem equs5av {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 ph))) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Equivalence for double substitution. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.)
theorem _2sb5 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ph))) $;

--| An alternate definition of proper substitution ~ df-sb . By introducing a dummy variable ` y ` in the definiens, we are able to eliminate any distinct variable restrictions among the variables ` t ` , ` x ` , and ` ph ` of the definiendum. No distinct variable conflicts arise because ` y ` effectively insulates ` t ` from ` x ` . To achieve this, we use a chain of two substitutions in the form of ~ sb5 , first ` y ` for ` x ` then ` t ` for ` y ` . Compare Definition 2.1'' of [Quine] p. 17, which is obtained from this theorem by applying ~ df-clab . Theorem ~ sb7h provides a version where ` ph ` and ` y ` don't have to be distinct. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2004.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 25-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Sep-2023.)
theorem dfsb7 {x3 y1 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| Negation inside and outside of substitution are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Apr-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by BJ, 25-Dec-2020.)
theorem sbn {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wn ph) t) (wn (wsb x3 ph t)) $;

--| Move existential quantifier in and out of substitution. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2003.)
theorem sbex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wex x3 ph) z) (wex x3 (wsb y1 ph z)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ~ df-nf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) ~ df-nf changed. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2021.)
theorem nf5 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ph))) $;

--| An alternate definition of ~ df-nf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nf6 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wex x3 ph) ph)) $;

--| Deduce that ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` in a context. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nf5d {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ nf5di as of 23-Oct-2026. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jul-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nf5diOLD {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ph) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $;

--| A wff may be existentially quantified with a variable not free in it. Theorem 19.9 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by FL, 24-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jan-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem _19_9h {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) ph $;

--| Theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90. The hypothesis can be thought of as " ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` ". See also ~ 19.21 and ~ 19.21v . (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2018.)
theorem _19_21h {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90. See ~ 19.23 . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2018.)
theorem _19_23h {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 19.23 . See ~ exlimiv for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2018.)
theorem exlimih {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ps $;

--| Deduction form of Theorem 19.9 of [Margaris] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-1997.)
theorem exlimdh {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ch (wal x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wex x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Version of ~ equsalh with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Jul-2022.)
theorem equsalhw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Version of ~ equsexh with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem equsexhv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` A. x ph ` . This corresponds to the axiom (4) of modal logic. Example in Appendix in [Megill] p. 450 (p. 19 of the preprint). Also Lemma 22 of [Monk2] p. 114. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Oct-2021.)
theorem hba1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Closed theorem version of bound-variable hypothesis builder ~ hbn . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Oct-2021.)
theorem hbnt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ph))) (wi (wn ph) (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , it is not free in ` -. ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem hbn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wal x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| Deduction form of bound-variable hypothesis builder ~ hbn . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.)
theorem hbnd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ps))) $;

--| A closed form of ~ hbim . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.)
theorem hbim1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Deduction form of bound-variable hypothesis builder ~ hbim . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2018.)
theorem hbimd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wal x3 ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) (wal x3 (wi ps ch))) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` and ` ps ` , it is not free in ` ( ph -> ps ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mel L. O'Cat, 3-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2018.)
theorem hbim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hban as of 22-Oct-2026. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbanOLD {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wal x3 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hb3an as of 23-Oct-2026. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jan-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hb3anOLD {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wal x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wal x3 (w3a ph ps ch)) $;

--| Introduction of implication into substitution. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbi2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) $;

--| Implication inside and outside of a substitution are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Substitution in an implication with a variable not free in the antecedent affects only the consequent. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 20-Nov-2024.)
theorem sbrim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi ph (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Substitution in an implication with a variable not free in the consequent affects only the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.)
theorem sblim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wi ph ps) y1) (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) ps) $;

--| Disjunction inside and outside of a substitution are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2002.)
theorem sbor {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wo ph ps) y1) (wo (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Equivalence inside and outside of a substitution are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.)
theorem sbbi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wb ph ps) y1) (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Introduce left biconditional inside of a substitution. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.)
theorem sblbis {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wb ch ph) y1) (wb (wsb x3 ch y1) ps) $;

--| Introduce right biconditional inside of a substitution. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem sbrbis {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wb ph ch) y1) (wb ps (wsb x3 ch y1)) $;

--| Introduce right biconditional inside of a substitution. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.)
theorem sbrbif {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wb ph ch) y1) (wb ps ch) $;

--| Move nonfree predicate in and out of substitution; see ~ sbal and ~ sbex . (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-May-2025.)
theorem sbnf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wnf x3 ph) z) (wnf x3 (wsb y1 ph z)) $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution. Version of ~ sbie with a disjoint variable condition, not requiring ~ ax-13 . See ~ sbievw for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Jan-2023.) Remove dependence on ~ ax-10 and shorten proof. (Revised by BJ, 18-Jul-2023.) (Proof shortened by SN, 24-Jul-2025.)
theorem sbiev {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution (deduction version of ~ sbiev ). Version of ~ sbied with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem sbiedw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps y1) ch) $;

--| Show that the original axiom ~ ax-c7 can be derived from ~ ax-10 ( ~ hbn1 ), ~ sp and propositional calculus. See ~ ax10fromc7 for the rederivation of ~ ax-10 from ~ ax-c7 . Normally, ~ axc7 should be used rather than ~ ax-c7 , except by theorems specifically studying the latter's properties. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-2008.)
theorem axc7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph)))) ph $;

--| Abbreviated version of ~ axc7 using the existential quantifier. Corresponds to the dual of Axiom (B) of modal logic. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Jul-2022.)
theorem axc7e {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wal x3 ph)) ph $;

--| The analogue in our predicate calculus of the Brouwer axiom (B) of modal logic S5. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2005.)
theorem modal_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 (wn ph)))) $;

--| A closed version of ~ 19.9h . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Mar-2018.)
theorem _19_9ht {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ph))) (wi (wex x3 ph) ph) $;

--| Show that the original axiom ~ ax-c4 can be derived from ~ ax-4 ( ~ alim ), ~ ax-10 ( ~ hbn1 ), ~ sp and propositional calculus. See ~ ax4fromc4 for the rederivation of ~ ax-4 from ~ ax-c4 . Part of the proof is based on the proof of Lemma 22 of [Monk2] p. 114. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem axc4 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Inference version of ~ axc4 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1993.)
theorem axc4i {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` A. y ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfal {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wal y1 ph) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` E. y ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Dec-2017.) Reduce symbol count in ~ nfex , ~ hbex . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wex y1 ph) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` E. y ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) Reduce symbol count in ~ nfex , ~ hbex . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2021.)
theorem hbex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wex y1 ph) (wal x3 (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` F/ y ph ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Dec-2017.)
theorem nfnf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wnf y1 ph) $;

--| Theorem 19.12 of [Margaris] p. 89. Assuming the converse is a mistake sometimes made by beginners! But sometimes the converse does hold, as in ~ 19.12vv and ~ r19.12sn . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2018.)
theorem _19_12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ nfal . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| If ` x ` is not free in ` ps ` , then it is not free in ` E. y ps ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2016.)
theorem nfexd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| If ` z ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` z ` is disjoint from both ` x ` and ` y ` . Version of ~ nfsb with an additional disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Feb-2023.) Remove disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 13-Aug-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2024.)
theorem nfsbv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf z (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Version of ~ sbco2 with disjoint variable conditions but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem sbco2v {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ph z) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Distribute universal quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 21-Nov-2024.)
theorem aaan {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Distribute existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 8-Aug-1994.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 21-Nov-2024.)
theorem eeor {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wo ph ps))) (wo (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbv3 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem cbv3v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbv1 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem cbv1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbv2 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbv2w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Deduction used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvald with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbvaldw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Deduction used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvexd with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbvexdw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbv3h with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , which does not require ~ ax-13 . Was used in a proof of ~ axc11n (but of independent interest). (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Nov-2020.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 30-Nov-2020.)
theorem cbv3hv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal y1 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbval with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbvalvw for a version with two more disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms, and ~ cbvalv for another variant. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem cbvalv1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvex with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbvexvw for a version with two disjoint variable conditions, requiring fewer axioms, and ~ cbvexv for another variant. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 31-May-2019.)
theorem cbvexv1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbval2 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2003.) (Revised by BJ, 16-Jun-2019.) (Proof shortened by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem cbval2v {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal z (wal w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvex2 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Revised by BJ, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem cbvex2v {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps)) $;

--| Proof of ~ dvelimh without using ~ ax-13 but with additional distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2002.) (Revised by Andrew Salmon, 21-Jul-2011.) (Revised by NM, 1-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Dec-2018.)
theorem dvelimhw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal z ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem *11.53 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. See ~ pm11.53v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_53 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Special case of ~ 19.12 where its converse holds. See ~ 19.12vvv for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2001.) (Revised by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem _19_12vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wal y1 (wex x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Distribute existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.)
theorem eean {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Distribute a pair of existential quantifiers over a conjunction. Combination of ~ 19.41v and ~ 19.42v . For a version requiring fewer axioms but with additional disjoint variable conditions, see ~ exdistrv . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.)
theorem eeanv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Distribute three existential quantifiers over a conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) Reduce distinct variable restrictions. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jan-2018.)
theorem eeeanv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (ch: wff z):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (w3a ph ps ch)))) (w3a (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps) (wex z ch)) $;

--| Distribute two pairs of existential quantifiers over a conjunction. For a version requiring fewer axioms but with additional disjoint variable conditions, see ~ 4exdistrv . (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.) Remove disjoint variable conditions on ` y , z ` and ` x , w ` . (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem ee4anv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w (wa ph ps))))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps))) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ ee4anv as of 26-Oct-2025. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee4anvOLD {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w (wa ph ps))))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps))) $;

--| Substitution of variable in universal quantifier. Version of ~ sb8f with a disjoint variable condition replacing the nonfree hypothesis ` F/ y ph ` , not requiring ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by SN, 5-Dec-2024.)
theorem sb8v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Substitution of variable in universal quantifier. Version of ~ sb8 with a disjoint variable condition, not requiring ~ ax-10 or ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by SN, 5-Dec-2024.)
theorem sb8f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Substitution of variable in existential quantifier. Version of ~ sb8e with a disjoint variable condition, not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2023.)
theorem sb8ef {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| An equivalent expression for double existence. Version of ~ 2sb8e with more disjoint variable conditions, not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jan-2023.)
theorem _2sb8ef {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z))) $;

--| Reversed substitution. Version of ~ sb6rf requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Feb-2023.)
theorem sb6rfv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing " ` x ` is (effectively) not free in ` ph ` ". (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 14-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2018.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Jan-2023.)
theorem sbnf2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wnf x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal z (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ph z)))) $;

--| An equivalent expression for existence. One direction ( ~ exsbim ) needs fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2005.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2022.)
theorem exsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| An equivalent expression for double existence. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Sep-2018.)
theorem _2exsb {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ph))))) $;

--| Reversal of substitution. (Contributed by AV, 6-Aug-2023.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Sep-2023.)
theorem sbbib {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wal y1 (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps)) (wal x3 (wb ph (wsb y1 ps x3))) $;

--| Reversal of substitution. (Contributed by AV, 6-Aug-2023.)
theorem sbbibvv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps)) (wal x3 (wb ph (wsb y1 ps x3))) $;

--| Change the bound variable (i.e. the substituted one) in wff's linked by implicit substitution. The proof was part of a former ~ cbvabw version. (Contributed by GG and WL, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem cbvsbvf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph z) (wsb y1 ps z) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ cleljust . It is kept here and should not be modified because it is referenced on the Metamath Proof Explorer Home Page (mmset.html) as an example of how disjoint variable conditions are inherited by substitutions. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2004.) (Revised by BJ, 29-Dec-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cleljustALT {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ cleljust . Compared with ~ cleljustALT , it uses ~ nfv followed by ~ equsexv instead of ~ ax-5 followed by ~ equsexhv , so it uses the idiom ` F/ x ph ` instead of ` ph -> A. x ph ` to express nonfreeness. This style is generally preferred for later theorems. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Dec-2016.) (Revised by BJ, 29-Dec-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cleljustALT2 {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ equs5a . Uses ~ ax-12 but not ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs5aALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 ph))) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ equs5e . Uses ~ ax-12 but not ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jan-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs5eALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex y1 ph))) $;

--| Same as ~ axc11 but with reversed antecedent. Note the use of ~ ax-12 (and not merely ~ ax12v as in ~ axc11rv ). This theorem is mostly used to eliminate conditions requiring set variables be distinct (cf. ~ cbvaev and ~ aecom , for example) in proofs. In practice, theorems beyond elementary set theory do not really benefit from such eliminations. As of 2024, it is used in conjunction with ~ ax-13 only, and like that, it should be applied only in niches where indispensable. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2015.)
theorem axc11r {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Same as ~ axc11 but with reversed antecedent. Note the use of ~ ax-12 (and not merely ~ ax12v as in ~ axc11rv ). This theorem is mostly used to eliminate conditions requiring set variables be distinct (cf. ~ cbvaev and ~ aecom , for example) in proofs. In practice, theorems beyond elementary set theory do not really benefit from such eliminations. As of 2024, it is used in conjunction with ~ ax-13 only, and like that, it should be applied only in niches where indispensable. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2015.)
theorem axc11r_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Version of ~ dral1 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . Remark: the corresponding versions for ~ dral2 and ~ drex2 are instances of ~ albidv and ~ exbidv respectively. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-1994.) (Revised by BJ, 17-Jun-2019.) Base the proof on ~ ax12v . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Mar-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem dral1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Version of ~ drex1 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) (Revised by BJ, 17-Jun-2019.)
theorem drex1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Version of ~ drnf1 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (Revised by BJ, 17-Jun-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem drnf1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wnf x3 ph) (wnf y1 ps)) $;

--| Axiom of Quantified Equality. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. An equivalent way to express this axiom that may be easier to understand is ` ( -. x = y -> ( -. x = z -> ( y = z -> A. x y = z ) ) ) ` (see ~ ax13b ). Recall that in the intended interpretation, our variables are metavariables ranging over the variables of predicate calculus (the object language). In order for the first antecedent ` -. x = y ` to hold, ` x ` and ` y ` must have different values and thus cannot be the same object-language variable (so they are effectively "distinct variables" even though no $d is present). Similarly, ` x ` and ` z ` cannot be the same object-language variable. Therefore, ` x ` will not occur in the wff ` y = z ` when the first two antecedents hold, so analogous to ~ ax-5 , the conclusion ` ( y = z -> A. x y = z ) ` follows. Note that ~ ax-5 cannot prove this because its distinct variable ($d) requirement is not satisfied directly but only indirectly (outside of Metamath) by the argument above. The original version of this axiom was ~ ax-c9 and was replaced with this shorter ~ ax-13 in December 2015. The old axiom is proved from this one as Theorem ~ axc9 . The primary purpose of this axiom is to provide a way to introduce the quantifier ` A. x ` on ` y = z ` even when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. In this case, the first antecedent becomes ` -. x = x ` and the axiom still holds. This axiom is mostly used to eliminate conditions requiring set variables be distinct (cf. ~ ax6ev and ~ ax6e , for example) in proofs. In practice, theorems beyond elementary set theory do not really benefit from such eliminations, so direct or indirect application of this axiom is discouraged now. You need to explicitly confirm its use in case you see a sensible application in a niche. After some assisting contributions by others over the years, it was in particular the extensive work of Gino Giotto in 2024 that helped reducing dependencies on this axiom on a large scale. Although this version is shorter, the original version ~ axc9 may be more practical to work with because of the "distinctor" form of its antecedents. A typical application of ~ axc9 is in ~ dvelimh which converts a distinct variable pair to the distinctor antecedent ` -. A. x x = y ` . In particular, it is conjectured that it is not possible to prove ~ ax6 from ~ ax6v without this axiom. This axiom can be weakened if desired by adding distinct variable restrictions on pairs ` x , z ` and ` y , z ` . To show that, we add these restrictions to Theorem ~ ax13v and use only ~ ax13v for further derivations. Thus, ~ ax13v should be the only theorem referencing this axiom. Other theorems can reference either ~ ax13v (preferred) or ~ ax13 (if the stronger form is needed). This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax13w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve scheme completeness (i.e. so that all possible cases of bundling can be proved; see text linked at ~ mmtheorems.html#ax6dgen ). It is not known whether this axiom can be derived from the others. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_13 {x3: setvar} (y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Axiom of Quantified Equality. One of the equality and substitution axioms of predicate calculus with equality. An equivalent way to express this axiom that may be easier to understand is ` ( -. x = y -> ( -. x = z -> ( y = z -> A. x y = z ) ) ) ` (see ~ ax13b ). Recall that in the intended interpretation, our variables are metavariables ranging over the variables of predicate calculus (the object language). In order for the first antecedent ` -. x = y ` to hold, ` x ` and ` y ` must have different values and thus cannot be the same object-language variable (so they are effectively "distinct variables" even though no $d is present). Similarly, ` x ` and ` z ` cannot be the same object-language variable. Therefore, ` x ` will not occur in the wff ` y = z ` when the first two antecedents hold, so analogous to ~ ax-5 , the conclusion ` ( y = z -> A. x y = z ) ` follows. Note that ~ ax-5 cannot prove this because its distinct variable ($d) requirement is not satisfied directly but only indirectly (outside of Metamath) by the argument above. The original version of this axiom was ~ ax-c9 and was replaced with this shorter ~ ax-13 in December 2015. The old axiom is proved from this one as Theorem ~ axc9 . The primary purpose of this axiom is to provide a way to introduce the quantifier ` A. x ` on ` y = z ` even when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. In this case, the first antecedent becomes ` -. x = x ` and the axiom still holds. This axiom is mostly used to eliminate conditions requiring set variables be distinct (cf. ~ ax6ev and ~ ax6e , for example) in proofs. In practice, theorems beyond elementary set theory do not really benefit from such eliminations, so direct or indirect application of this axiom is discouraged now. You need to explicitly confirm its use in case you see a sensible application in a niche. After some assisting contributions by others over the years, it was in particular the extensive work of Gino Giotto in 2024 that helped reducing dependencies on this axiom on a large scale. Although this version is shorter, the original version ~ axc9 may be more practical to work with because of the "distinctor" form of its antecedents. A typical application of ~ axc9 is in ~ dvelimh which converts a distinct variable pair to the distinctor antecedent ` -. A. x x = y ` . In particular, it is conjectured that it is not possible to prove ~ ax6 from ~ ax6v without this axiom. This axiom can be weakened if desired by adding distinct variable restrictions on pairs ` x , z ` and ` y , z ` . To show that, we add these restrictions to Theorem ~ ax13v and use only ~ ax13v for further derivations. Thus, ~ ax13v should be the only theorem referencing this axiom. Other theorems can reference either ~ ax13v (preferred) or ~ ax13 (if the stronger form is needed). This axiom scheme is logically redundant (see ~ ax13w ) but is used as an auxiliary axiom scheme to achieve scheme completeness (i.e. so that all possible cases of bundling can be proved; see text linked at ~ mmtheorems.html#ax6dgen ). It is not known whether this axiom can be derived from the others. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_13_b {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) $;

--| A weaker version of ~ ax-13 with distinct variable restrictions on pairs ` x , z ` and ` y , z ` . In order to show (with ~ ax13 ) that this weakening is still adequate, this should be the only theorem referencing ~ ax-13 directly. Had we additionally required ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct, too, this theorem would have been a direct consequence of ~ ax-5 . So essentially this theorem states, that a distinct variable condition can be replaced with an inequality between set variables. Preferably, use the version ~ ax13w to avoid the propagation of ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13v {x3: setvar} (y1 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| A weaker version of ~ ax-13 with distinct variable restrictions on pairs ` x , z ` and ` y , z ` . In order to show (with ~ ax13 ) that this weakening is still adequate, this should be the only theorem referencing ~ ax-13 directly. Had we additionally required ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct, too, this theorem would have been a direct consequence of ~ ax-5 . So essentially this theorem states, that a distinct variable condition can be replaced with an inequality between set variables. Preferably, use the version ~ ax13w to avoid the propagation of ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13v_b {x3: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) $;

--| A version of ~ ax13v with one distinct variable restriction dropped. For convenience, ` y ` is kept on the right side of equations. The proof of ~ ax13 bases on ideas from NM, 24-Dec-2015. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13lem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A version of ~ ax13v with one distinct variable restriction dropped. For convenience, ` y ` is kept on the right side of equations. The proof of ~ ax13 bases on ideas from NM, 24-Dec-2015. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13lem1_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Derive ~ ax-13 from ~ ax13v and Tarski's FOL. This shows that the weakening in ~ ax13v is still sufficient for a complete system. Preferably, use the weaker ~ ax13w to avoid the propagation of ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Jan-2018.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jun-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nfeqf2 . This lemma is equivalent to ~ ax13v with one distinct variable constraint removed. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Oct-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13lem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nfeqf2 . This lemma is equivalent to ~ ax13v with one distinct variable constraint removed. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Oct-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13lem2_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| An equation between setvar is free of any other setvar. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-Jun-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2022.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equation between setvar is free of any other setvar. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 9-Jun-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2022.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf2_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wnf x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| Quantifier introduction when one pair of variables is distinct. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Revised by NM, 20-Jul-2015.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dveeq2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| An equation between setvar is free of any other setvar. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| An equation between setvar is free of any other setvar. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf1_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wnf x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) $;

--| Quantifier introduction when one pair of variables is distinct. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dveeq1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Quantifier introduction when one pair of variables is distinct. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dveeq1_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) $;

--| A variable is effectively not free in an equality if it is not either of the involved variables. ` F/ ` version of ~ ax-c9 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wnf z (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A variable is effectively not free in an equality if it is not either of the involved variables. ` F/ ` version of ~ ax-c9 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))))) (wnf z (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| A variable is effectively not free in an equality if it is not either of the involved variables. ` F/ ` version of ~ ax-c9 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A variable is effectively not free in an equality if it is not either of the involved variables. ` F/ ` version of ~ ax-c9 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeqf_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1))))) (wnf y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c9 from the shorter ~ ax-13 . Usage is discouraged to avoid uninformed ~ ax-13 propagation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2015.) (Revised by NM, 24-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc9 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c9 from the shorter ~ ax-13 . Usage is discouraged to avoid uninformed ~ ax-13 propagation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2015.) (Revised by NM, 24-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc9_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wal z (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c9 from the shorter ~ ax-13 . Usage is discouraged to avoid uninformed ~ ax-13 propagation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2015.) (Revised by NM, 24-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc9_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c9 from the shorter ~ ax-13 . Usage is discouraged to avoid uninformed ~ ax-13 propagation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2015.) (Revised by NM, 24-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc9_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| At least one individual exists. This is not a theorem of free logic, which is sound in empty domains. For such a logic, we would add this theorem as an axiom of set theory (Axiom 0 of [Kunen] p. 10). In the system consisting of ~ ax-4 through ~ ax-9 , all axioms other than ~ ax-6 are believed to be theorems of free logic, although the system without ~ ax-6 is not complete in free logic. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . It is preferred to use ~ ax6ev when it is sufficient. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) Shortened after ~ ax13lem1 became available. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax6e {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Theorem showing that ~ ax-6 follows from the weaker version ~ ax6v . (Even though this theorem depends on ~ ax-6 , all references of ~ ax-6 are made via ~ ax6v . An earlier version stated ~ ax6v as a separate axiom, but having two axioms caused some confusion.) This theorem should be referenced in place of ~ ax-6 so that all proofs can be traced back to ~ ax6v . When possible, use the weaker ~ ax6v rather than ~ ax6 since the ~ ax6v derivation is much shorter and requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by NM, 25-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Feb-2018.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ ax6v instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax6 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Show that the original axiom ~ ax-c10 can be derived from ~ ax6 and ~ axc7 (on top of propositional calculus, ~ ax-gen , and ~ ax-4 ). See ~ ax6fromc10 for the rederivation of ~ ax6 from ~ ax-c10 . Normally, ~ axc10 should be used rather than ~ ax-c10 , except by theorems specifically studying the latter's properties. See ~ bj-axc10v for a weaker version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc10 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal x3 ph))) ph $;

--| Closed theorem form of ~ spim . (Contributed by NM, 15-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Mar-2023.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spimt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wnf x3 ps) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps)))) (wi (wal x3 ph) ps) $;

--| Specialization, using implicit substitution. Compare Lemma 14 of [Tarski] p. 70. The ~ spim series of theorems requires that only one direction of the substitution hypothesis hold. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ spimw for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Deduction version of ~ spime . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Feb-2018.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ spimedv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spimed {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ch (wnf x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Existential introduction, using implicit substitution. Compare Lemma 14 of [Tarski] p. 70. See ~ spimew and ~ spimevw for weaker versions requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Mar-2018.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ spimefv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spime {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| A version of ~ spim with a distinct variable requirement instead of a bound-variable hypothesis. See ~ spimfv and ~ spimvw for versions requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1993.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ spimvw instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spimv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ spimv . Note that it requires only ~ ax-1 through ~ ax-5 together with ~ ax6e . Currently, proofs derive from ~ ax6v , but if ~ ax-6 could be used instead, this proof would reduce axiom usage. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by BJ, 29-Nov-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spimvALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Distinct-variable version of ~ spime . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ spimevw instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spimev {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Specialization, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ spvv if possible. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ps $;

--| Inference from existential specialization, using implicit substitution. Remove a distinct variable constraint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ speiv if possible. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spei {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $;

--| Implicit substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` into a theorem. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ chvarfv if possible. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 9-Jul-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem chvar {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Implicit substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` into a theorem. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ chvarvv if possible. (Contributed by NM, 20-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem chvarv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution, that does not use ~ ax-c9 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbv3v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ cbvalw , ~ cbvalvw , ~ cbvalv1 for versions requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ cbvexvw , ~ cbvexv1 for weaker versions requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbvalvw for a version requiring fewer axioms, to be preferred when sufficient. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 and shorten proof. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvalv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbvexvw for a version requiring fewer axioms, to be preferred when sufficient. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 and shorten proof. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvexv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbv1v with disjoint variable conditions, not depending on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) Format hypotheses to common style. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbv2w with disjoint variable conditions, not depending on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) Format hypotheses to common style, avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Sep-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbv3hv if possible. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv3h {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal y1 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv1h {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal y1 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wal x3 ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wi (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbv2h {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wal y1 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wal x3 ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Deduction used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution, particularly useful in conjunction with ~ dvelim . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ cbvaldw for a version with ` x , y ` disjoint, not depending on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Deduction used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution, particularly useful in conjunction with ~ dvelim . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvexdw if possible. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvexd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change the bound variable in a universal quantifier with implicit substitution. Deduction form. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvaldvaw if possible. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvaldva {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wal x3 ps) (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change the bound variable in an existential quantifier with implicit substitution. Deduction form. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvexdvaw if possible. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvexdva {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wex x3 ps) (wex y1 ch)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbval2v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbval2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal z (wal w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvex2v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvex2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbval2vw if possible. (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2005.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Jul-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbval2vv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal z (wal w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvex2vw if possible. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Jul-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvex2vv {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvex4vw if possible. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvex4v {x3 y1 z w v u f1 g1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w f1 g1) (ps: wff z w v u) (ch: wff x3 y1 v u f1 g1):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv v)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv u))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv f1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv g1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w ph)))) (wex v (wex u (wex f1 (wex g1 ch)))) $;

--| Lemma used in proofs of implicit substitution properties. The converse requires either a disjoint variable condition ( ~ sbalex ) or a nonfreeness hypothesis ( ~ equs45f ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equs4v for a weaker version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2014.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Feb-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsalvw and ~ equsalv for versions with disjoint variable conditions proved from fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsex . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Feb-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsal {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsexvw and ~ equsexv for versions with disjoint variable conditions proved from fewer axioms. See also the dual form ~ equsal . See ~ equsexALT for an alternate proof. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Feb-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ equsex . This proves the result directly, instead of as a corollary of ~ equsal via ~ equs4 . Note in particular that only existential quantifiers appear in the proof and that the only step requiring ~ ax-13 is ~ ax6e . This proof mimics that of ~ equsal (in particular, note that ~ pm5.32i , ~ exbii , ~ 19.41 , ~ mpbiran correspond respectively to ~ pm5.74i , ~ albii , ~ 19.23 , ~ a1bi ). (Contributed by BJ, 20-Aug-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsexALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsalhw for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsalh {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| An equivalence related to implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equsexhv for a version with a disjoint variable condition which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsexh {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ps $;

--| Derivation of set.mm's original ~ ax-c15 from ~ ax-c11n and the shorter ~ ax-12 that has replaced it. Theorem ~ ax12 shows the reverse derivation of ~ ax-12 from ~ ax-c15 . Normally, ~ axc15 should be used rather than ~ ax-c15 , except by theorems specifically studying the latter's properties. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Mar-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc15 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| Rederivation of Axiom ~ ax-12 from ~ ax12v (used only via ~ sp ), ~ axc11r , and ~ axc15 (on top of Tarski's FOL). Since this version depends on ~ ax-13 , usage of the weaker ~ ax12v , ~ ax12w , ~ ax12i are preferred. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-2007.) Proof uses contemporary axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 4-Jul-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| A bidirectional version of ~ axc15 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-2006.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax12b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ ax13 from FOL, ~ sp , and ~ axc9 . (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Jan-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax13ALT {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c11n from others. Commutation law for identical variable specifiers. The antecedent and consequent are true when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. Lemma L12 in [Megill] p. 445 (p. 12 of the preprint). If a disjoint variable condition is added on ` x ` and ` y ` , then this becomes an instance of ~ aevlem . Use ~ aecom instead when this does not lengthen the proof. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Revised by NM, 7-Nov-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Mar-2018.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jul-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc11n {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Derive set.mm's original ~ ax-c11n from others. Commutation law for identical variable specifiers. The antecedent and consequent are true when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. Lemma L12 in [Megill] p. 445 (p. 12 of the preprint). If a disjoint variable condition is added on ` x ` and ` y ` , then this becomes an instance of ~ aevlem . Use ~ aecom instead when this does not lengthen the proof. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (Revised by NM, 7-Nov-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Mar-2018.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 29-Mar-2021.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Jul-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc11n_b {x3: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| Commutation law for identical variable specifiers. Both sides of the biconditional are true when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) Change to a biconditional. (Revised by BJ, 26-Sep-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem aecom {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Commutation law for identical variable specifiers. Both sides of the biconditional are true when ` x ` and ` y ` are substituted with the same variable. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) Change to a biconditional. (Revised by BJ, 26-Sep-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem aecom_b {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| A commutation rule for identical variable specifiers. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem aecoms {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) ph $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) ph $;

--| A commutation rule for identical variable specifiers. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem aecoms_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) ph $;

--| A commutation rule for distinct variable specifiers. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem naecoms {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ph $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) ph $;

--| Show that ~ ax-c11 can be derived from ~ ax-c11n in the form of ~ axc11n . Normally, ~ axc11 should be used rather than ~ ax-c11 , except by theorems specifically studying the latter's properties. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ axc11v when possible. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc11 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Show that ~ ax-c11 can be derived from ~ ax-c11n in the form of ~ axc11n . Normally, ~ axc11 should be used rather than ~ ax-c11 , except by theorems specifically studying the latter's properties. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ axc11v when possible. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc11_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbae {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbae_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wal z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbae_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbae_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Lemma L19 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbnaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbnae {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Lemma L19 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbnaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbnae_b {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Lemma L19 in [Megill] p. 446 (p. 14 of the preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ hbnaev when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbnae_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal y1 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfae {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wnf z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfae_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wnf z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfae_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnf x3 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfae_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnf y1 (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfnaew when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfnae {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wnf z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfnaew when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfnae_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wnf z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfnaew when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfnae_b1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnf x3 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| All variables are effectively bound in a distinct variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfnaew when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfnae_b2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnf y1 (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Rule that applies ~ hbnae to antecedent. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbnaes {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) ph $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ph $;

--| Inference with ~ axc16 as its conclusion. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ axc16 instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc16i {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 z) (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ axc16nf , shorter but requiring ~ ax-11 and ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc16nfALT {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wnf z ph) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of ~ albidv is preferred, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) Allow a shortening of ~ dral1 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Mar-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dral2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wal z ph) (wal z ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of ~ albidv is preferred, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) Allow a shortening of ~ dral1 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Mar-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dral2_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ dral1v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-1994.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dral1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dral1 , shorter but requiring ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dral1ALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ drex1v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drex1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of ~ exbidv is preferred, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drex2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wex z ph) (wex z ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of ~ exbidv is preferred, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drex2_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ drnf1v instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drnf1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wnf x3 ph) (wnf y1 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-May-2018.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfbidv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drnf2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wnf z ph) (wnf z ps)) $;

--| Variation on ~ nfald which adds the hypothesis that ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct in the inner subproof. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfald instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfald2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Variation on ~ nfexd which adds the hypothesis that ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct in the inner subproof. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfexd instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfexd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wex y1 ps)) $;

--| Distribution of existential quantifiers, with a bound-variable hypothesis saying that ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` , but ` x ` can be free in ` ph ` (and there is no distinct variable condition on ` x ` and ` y ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-May-2018.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ exdistr instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem exdistrf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf y1 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wex x3 (wa ph (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelimv without any variable restrictions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelimv without any variable restrictions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimf_b {x3 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ dvelimf . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimdf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf z ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 ch)) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelim without any variable restrictions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ dvelimhw for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2002.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimh {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal z ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| This theorem can be used to eliminate a distinct variable restriction on ` x ` and ` z ` and replace it with the "distinctor" ` -. A. x x = y ` as an antecedent. ` ph ` normally has ` z ` free and can be read ` ph ( z ) ` , and ` ps ` substitutes ` y ` for ` z ` and can be read ` ph ( y ) ` . We do not require that ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct: if they are not, the distinctor will become false (in multiple-element domains of discourse) and "protect" the consequent. To obtain a closed-theorem form of this inference, prefix the hypotheses with ` A. x A. z ` , conjoin them, and apply ~ dvelimdf . Other variants of this theorem are ~ dvelimh (with no distinct variable restrictions) and ~ dvelimhw (that avoids ~ ax-13 ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ dvelimhw for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelim {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Similar to ~ dvelim with first hypothesis replaced by a distinct variable condition. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ dvelimhw for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Apr-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi ps (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelim using "not free" notation. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimnf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelim using "not free" notation. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimnf_b {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wnf x3 ps) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dveeq2 , shorter but requiring ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Revised by NM, 20-Jul-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dveeq2ALT {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A variable introduction law for equality. Lemma 15 of [Monk2] p. 109, however we do not require ` z ` to be distinct from ` x ` and ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ equvinv for a shorter proof requiring fewer axioms when ` z ` is required to be distinct from ` x ` and ` y ` . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equvini {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A variable elimination law for equality with no distinct variable requirements. Compare ~ equvini . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ equvelv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equvel {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| A property related to substitution that unlike ~ equs5 does not require a distinctor antecedent. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . This proof uses ~ ax12 , see ~ equs5aALT for an alternative one using ~ ax-12 but not ~ ax13 . Usage of the weaker ~ equs5av is preferred, which uses ~ ax12v2 , but not ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs5a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 ph))) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| A property related to substitution that unlike ~ equs5 does not require a distinctor antecedent. This proof uses ~ ax12 , see ~ equs5eALT for an alternative one using ~ ax-12 but not ~ ax13 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jan-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs5e {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex y1 ph))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing substitution when ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` . The implication "to the left" is ~ equs4 and does not require the nonfreeness hypothesis. Theorem ~ sbalex replaces the nonfreeness hypothesis with a disjoint variable condition and ~ equs5 replaces it with a distinctor antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ sbalex instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs45f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Lemma used in proofs of substitution properties. If there is a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , then ~ sbalex can be used instead; if ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` , then ~ equs45f can be used. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 1-Oct-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equs5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wb (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Quantifier introduction when one pair of variables is disjoint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dveel1 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| Quantifier introduction when one pair of variables is disjoint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dveel2 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Axiom ~ ax-c14 is redundant if we assume ~ ax-5 . Remark 9.6 in [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of the preprint), regarding axiom scheme C14'. Note that ` w ` is a dummy variable introduced in the proof. Its purpose is to satisfy the distinct variable requirements of ~ dveel2 and ~ ax-5 . By the end of the proof it has vanished, and the final theorem has no distinct variable requirements. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Jun-1995.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc14 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Equivalence involving substitution for a variable not free. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of ~ sb6 is preferred, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb6x {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Substitution does not change an identical variable specifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbequ5 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wb (wsb z (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) w) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Substitution does not change a distinctor. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbequ6 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wb (wsb z (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) w) (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Reversed substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb5rf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| Reversed substitution. For a version requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ sb6rfv . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ sb6rfv if possible. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb6rf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wal y1 (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ ax12v2 , shorter, but depending on more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem ax12vALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| We can always find values matching ` x ` and ` y ` , as long as they are represented by distinct variables. This theorem merges two ~ ax6e instances ` E. z z = x ` and ` E. w w = y ` into a common expression. Alan Sare contributed a variant of this theorem with distinct variable conditions before, see ~ ax6e2nd . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2ax6elem {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal w (wceq (cv w) (cv z)))) (wex z (wex w (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))))) $;

--| We can always find values matching ` x ` and ` y ` , as long as they are represented by distinct variables. Version of ~ 2ax6elem with a distinct variable constraint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 28-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2ax6e {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex z (wex w (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Reversed double substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove distinct variable constraints. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2sb5rf {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wex z (wex w (wa (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z)))) $;

--| Reversed double substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) Remove variable constraints. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Apr-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2sb6rf {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z)))) $;

--| Elimination of double substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbel2x {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w):
  $ wb ph (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wsb w (wsb z ph x3) y1)))) $;

--| Simplified definition of substitution when variables are distinct. Version of ~ sb6 with a distinctor antecedent. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1997.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 21-Feb-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb4b {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)))) (wb (wsb x3 ph t) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph))) $;

--| Simplified definition of substitution when variables are distinct. This is the biconditional strengthening of ~ sb3 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2018.) Shorten ~ sb3 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 21-Feb-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb3b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| One direction of a simplified definition of substitution when variables are distinct. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Feb-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| One direction of a simplified definition of substitution. The converse requires either a disjoint variable condition ( ~ sb5 ) or a nonfreeness hypothesis ( ~ sb5f ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ sb1v when possible. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 21-Feb-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| One direction of a simplified definition of substitution. The converse requires either a disjoint variable condition ( ~ sb6 ) or a nonfreeness hypothesis ( ~ sb6f ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1993.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 26-Jul-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| A version of one implication of ~ sb4b that does not require a distinctor antecedent. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ sb4av when possible. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb4a {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wal t ph) t) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) ph)) $;

--| Alternate definition of substitution. Remark 9.1 in [Megill] p. 447 (p. 15 of the preprint). This was the original definition before ~ df-sb . Note that it does not require dummy variables in its definiens; this is done by having ` x ` free in the first conjunct and bound in the second. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 9-Jul-2023.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 29-Jul-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfsb1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wa (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbsb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfs1v when sufficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnf x3 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Special case of a bound-variable hypothesis builder for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbsb2a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wal y1 ph) y1) (wal x3 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| One direction of a simplified definition of substitution that unlike ~ sb4b does not require a distinctor antecedent. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb4e {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex y1 ph))) $;

--| Special case of a bound-variable hypothesis builder for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbsb2e {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wsb x3 (wex y1 ph) y1)) $;

--| If ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` , ` x ` is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ bj-hbsb3v for a weaker version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbsb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal y1 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| If ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` , ` x ` is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ nfs1v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfs1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ axc16 , shorter but requiring ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-13 and using ~ df-nf and ~ df-sb . (Contributed by NM, 17-May-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc16ALT {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ axc16g that uses ~ df-sb and requires ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc16gALT {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi ph (wal z ph)) $;

--| Substitution applied to an atomic wff. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ equsb1v if possible. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsb1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wsb x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) y1 $;

--| Substitution applied to an atomic wff. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ equsb1v for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem equsb2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wsb x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) y1 $;

--| An alternate definition of proper substitution that, like ~ dfsb1 , mixes free and bound variables to avoid distinct variable requirements. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 17-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfsb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wo (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| An alternate definition of proper substitution ~ df-sb that uses only primitive connectives (no defined terms) on the right-hand side. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfsb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wi (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Part of Theorem 9.4 of [Megill] p. 448 (p. 16 of preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drsb1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsb x3 ph z) (wsb y1 ph z)) $;

--| In the case of two successive substitutions for two always equal variables, the second substitution has no effect. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ and WL, 9-Aug-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb2ae {x3 y1 v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 v u):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph v) u) (wsb y1 ph v)) $;

--| Equivalence for substitution when ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` . The implication "to the left" is ~ sb2 and does not require the nonfreeness hypothesis. Theorem ~ sb6 replaces the nonfreeness hypothesis with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` and requires fewer axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb6f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| Equivalence for substitution when ` y ` is not free in ` ph ` . The implication "to the right" is ~ sb1 and does not require the nonfreeness hypothesis. Theorem ~ sb5 replaces the nonfreeness hypothesis with a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` and requires fewer axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb5f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) $;

--| A variable not free in a proposition remains so after substitution in that proposition with a distinct variable (closed form of ~ nfsb4 ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsb4t {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wnf z ph)) (wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wnf z (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| A variable not free in a proposition remains so after substitution in that proposition with a distinct variable (inference associated with ~ nfsb4t ). Theorem ~ nfsb replaces the distinctor antecedent with a disjoint variable condition. See ~ nfsbv for a weaker version of ~ nfsb not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfsbv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsb4 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wnf z (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Elimination of equality from antecedent after substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2018.) Revise ~ df-sb . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jul-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbequ8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) y1) $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution. For versions requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ sbiev and ~ sbievw . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jul-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbie {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) ps $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution (deduction version of ~ sbie ) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ sbiedw , ~ sbiedvw for variants using disjoint variables, but requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Jun-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbied {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps y1) ch) $;

--| Conversion of implicit substitution to explicit substitution (deduction version of ~ sbie ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ sbiedvw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jan-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbiedv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps y1) ch) $;

--| Conversion of double implicit substitution to explicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ 2sbievw for a version with extra disjoint variables, but based on fewer axioms. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2sbiev {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 u t) (ps: wff u t):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv u))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph u) t) ps $;

--| Substituting ` y ` for ` x ` and then ` z ` for ` y ` is equivalent to substituting ` z ` for both ` x ` and ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring a disjoint variable, but fewer axioms, see ~ sbcom3vv . (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 8-Apr-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbcom3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph y1) z) (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph z) z) $;

--| Substituting ` y ` for ` x ` and then ` z ` for ` y ` is equivalent to substituting ` z ` for both ` x ` and ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring a disjoint variable, but fewer axioms, see ~ sbcom3vv . (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 8-Apr-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbcom3_b {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb x3 ph x3) z) (wsb x3 (wsb x3 ph z) z) $;

--| Substituting ` y ` for ` x ` and then ` z ` for ` y ` is equivalent to substituting ` z ` for both ` x ` and ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring a disjoint variable, but fewer axioms, see ~ sbcom3vv . (Contributed by Giovanni Mascellani, 8-Apr-2018.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbcom3_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph y1) x3) (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph x3) x3) $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ sbcov for a version with a disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbco {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph x3) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| An identity law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ sbid2vw for a weaker version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbid2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph x3) y1) ph $;

--| An identity law for substitution. Used in proof of Theorem 9.7 of [Megill] p. 449 (p. 16 of the preprint). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ sbid2vw for a version with an extra disjoint variable condition requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbid2v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph x3) y1) ph $;

--| An idempotent law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jul-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbidm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wsb x3 ph y1) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| A composition law for substitution. For versions requiring fewer axioms, but more disjoint variable conditions, see ~ sbco2v and ~ sbco2vv . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbco2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ph z) y1) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbco2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf z ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ps z) y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| A composition law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbco3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb y1 (wsb x3 ph y1) z) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph x3) z) $;

--| A commutativity law for substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Check out ~ sbcom3vv for a version requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1997.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbcom {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb z (wsb x3 ph y1) y1) (wsb x3 (wsb z ph y1) y1) $;

--| Partially closed form of ~ sbtr . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbtrt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) ph $;

--| A partial converse to ~ sbt . If the substitution of a variable for a nonfree one in a wff gives a theorem, then the original wff is a theorem. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by BJ, 15-Sep-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbtr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wsb x3 ph y1 $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Substitution of variable in universal quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ sb8f . (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jan-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Substitution of variable in existential quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ sb8ef . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jan-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb8e {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Commutation of quantification and substitution variables. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) Allow a shortening of ~ sb9i . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb9 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph x3)) (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Commutation of quantification and substitution variables. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb9i {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph x3)) (wal y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Two ways of expressing " ` x ` is (effectively) not free in ` ph ` ". Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 29-May-2009.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbhb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wi ph (wal x3 ph)) (wal y1 (wi ph (wsb x3 ph y1))) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ nfsb . (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2013.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfsbv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsbd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf z ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf z (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| If ` z ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` y ` and ` z ` are distinct. See ~ nfsbv for a version with an additional disjoint variable condition on ` x , z ` but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Feb-2024.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfsbv instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf z (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| If ` z ` is not free in ` ph ` , then it is not free in ` [ y / x ] ph ` when ` y ` and ` z ` are distinct. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ hbsbw instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbsb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal z ph) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wal z (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| This version of ~ dfsb7 does not require that ` ph ` and ` z ` be disjoint. This permits it to be used as a definition for substitution in a formalization that omits the logically redundant axiom ~ ax-5 , i.e., that does not have the concept of a variable not occurring in a formula. (Definition ~ dfsb1 is also suitable, but its mixing of free and bound variables is distasteful to some logicians.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb7f {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $;

--| This version of ~ dfsb7 does not require that ` ph ` and ` z ` be disjoint. This permits it to be used as a definition for substitution in a formalization that omits the logically redundant axiom ~ ax-5 , i.e., that does not have the concept of a variable not occurring in a formula. (Definition ~ dfsb1 is also suitable, but its mixing of free and bound variables is distasteful to some logicians.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb7h {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal z ph) $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 ph y1) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)))) $;

--| Hao Wang's identity axiom P6 in Irving Copi, _Symbolic Logic_ (5th ed., 1979), p. 328. In traditional predicate calculus, this is a sole axiom for identity from which the usual ones can be derived. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 9-May-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb10f {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wsb z ph y1) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wsb z ph x3))) $;

--| Check out ~ sbal for a version not dependent on ~ ax-13 . A theorem used in elimination of disjoint variable restriction on ` x ` and ` z ` by replacing it with a distinctor ` -. A. x x = z ` . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem sbal1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wb (wsb y1 (wal x3 ph) z) (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph z))) $;

--| Move quantifier in and out of substitution. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) Remove a distinct variable constraint. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Dec-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Sep-2023.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ sbal instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sbal2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wb (wsb y1 (wal x3 ph) z) (wal x3 (wsb y1 ph z))) $;

--| An equivalent expression for double existence. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring more disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ 2sb8ef . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 2-Nov-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2sb8e {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z))) $;

--| An elementary proof of ~ moeu in disguise, connecting an expression characterizing uniqueness ( ~ df-mo ) to that of existential uniqueness ( ~ eu6 ). No particular order of definition is required, as one can be derived from the other. This is shown here and in ~ dfeumo . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-May-2019.)
theorem dfmoeu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wi (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| An elementary proof showing the reverse direction of ~ dfmoeu . Here the characterizing expression of existential uniqueness ( ~ eu6 ) is derived from that of uniqueness ( ~ df-mo ). (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2023.)
theorem dfeumo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the at-most-one quantifier ("there exists at most one ` x ` such that ` ph ` ").
term wmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): wff;

--| Soundness justification theorem for ~ df-mo . (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2010.) Added this theorem by adapting the proof of ~ eujust . (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.)
theorem mojust {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wex z (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) $;

--| Define the at-most-one quantifier. The expression ` E* x ph ` is read "there exists at most one ` x ` such that ` ph ` ". This is also called the "uniqueness quantifier" but that expression is also used for the unique existential quantifier ~ df-eu , therefore we avoid that ambiguous name. Notation of [BellMachover] p. 460, whose definition we show as ~ mo3 . For other possible definitions see ~ moeu and ~ mo4 . Note that the definiens does not express "at-most-one" in the empty domain. Since the hypothesis relies on ~ ax-6 , this case is excluded anyway. Nevertheless, it was suggested to begin with the definition of uniqueness ( ~ eu6 ) and then define the at-most-one quantifier via ~ moeu . Both ~ eu6 and ~ moeu remain valid in the empty domain. The hypothesis asserts that the definition is independent of the particular choice of the dummy variable ` y ` . Without this hypothesis, ~ mojust would be derivable from propositional axioms alone: one could apply the definiens for ` E* x ph ` twice, using different dummy variables ` y ` and ` z ` , and then invoke ~ bitr3i to establish their equivalence. This would jeopardize the independence of axioms, as demonstrated in an analoguous situation involving ~ df-ss to prove ~ ax-8 (see ~ in-ax8 ). Prefer ~ dfmo unless you can prove the hypothesis from fewer axioms in special cases. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-May-2019.) Make this the definition (which used to be ~ moeu , while this definition was then proved as ~ dfmo ). (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.)
axiom df_mo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wex z (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Simplify definition ~ df-mo by removing its provable hypothesis. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 15-Feb-2026.)
theorem dfmo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Nonexistence implies uniqueness. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2022.)
theorem nexmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 ph)) (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| Any proposition holds for some ` x ` or holds for at most one ` x ` . (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) Shorten proof and avoid ~ df-eu . (Revised by BJ, 14-Oct-2022.)
theorem exmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wo (wex x3 ph) (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| Absorption of existence condition by uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2002.) Shorten proof and avoid ~ df-eu . (Revised by BJ, 14-Oct-2022.)
theorem moabs {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wi (wex x3 ph) (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| The at-most-one quantifier reverses implication. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-1995.)
theorem moim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| The at-most-one quantifier reverses implication. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2006.)
theorem moimi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| The at-most-one quantifier reverses implication, deduction form. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Feb-2017.)
theorem moimdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wmo x3 ch) (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Equivalence theorem for the at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2023.)
theorem mobi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wmo x3 ph) (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for the at-most-one quantifier (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2016.)
theorem mobii {x3: setvar} (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 ch) $;

--| Formula-building rule for the at-most-one quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) Reduce axiom dependencies and shorten proof. (Revised by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem mobidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for the at-most-one quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ, 14-Oct-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Feb-2023.)
theorem mobid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 ch)) $;

--| If an implication holds for at most one value, then its consequent holds for at most one value. See also ~ ala1 and ~ exa1 . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Dec-2018.)
theorem moa1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wmo x3 (wi ph ps)) (wmo x3 ps) $;

--| "At most one" is still the case when a conjunct is added. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-1995.)
theorem moan {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wmo x3 ph) (wmo x3 (wa ps ph)) $;

--| "At most one" is still true when a conjunct is added, inference form. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.)
theorem moani {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wmo x3 ph $ >
  $ wmo x3 (wa ps ph) $;

--| "At most one" is still the case when a disjunct is removed. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem moor {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wmo x3 (wo ph ps)) (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| "At most one" imports disjunction to conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.)
theorem mooran1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wo (wmo x3 ph) (wmo x3 ps)) (wmo x3 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| "At most one" exports disjunction to conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.)
theorem mooran2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wmo x3 (wo ph ps)) (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) Adapt to new definition. (Revised by BJ, 1-Oct-2022.)
theorem nfmo1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfmodv for a version replacing the distinctor with a disjoint variable condition, not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2016.) Avoid ~ df-eu . (Revised by BJ, 14-Oct-2022.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfmod2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wmo y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. See ~ nfmod for a version without disjoint variable conditions but requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2016.) (Revised by BJ, 28-Jan-2023.)
theorem nfmodv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wmo y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. See ~ nfmo for a version without disjoint variable conditions but requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem nfmov {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wmo y1 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. Deduction version of ~ nfmo . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfmodv when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfmod {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wmo y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the at-most-one quantifier. Note that ` x ` and ` y ` need not be disjoint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfmov when possible. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfmo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wmo y1 ph) $;

--| Version of ~ df-mo with disjoint variable condition replaced by nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) Extract ~ dfmo from this proof, and prove ~ mof from it (as of 30-Sep-2022, directly from ~ df-mo ). (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 28-May-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2022.)
theorem mof {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the at-most-one quantifier. Definition of [BellMachover] p. 460, except that definition has the side condition that ` y ` not occur in ` ph ` in place of our hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Aug-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ and WL, 29-Jan-2023.)
theorem mo3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Equivalent definitions of "there exists at most one". (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Dec-2018.)
theorem mo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| At-most-one quantifier expressed using implicit substitution. This theorem is also a direct consequence of ~ mo4f , but this proof is based on fewer axioms. Note: Swapping both ` x ` , ` y ` and ` ph ` , ` ps ` preserves the hypothesis and DV conditions, so we also have the conclusion ` |- ( E* y ps <-> A. y A. x ( ( ps /\ ph ) -> y = x ) ) ` . But under the hypothesis and DV condition, we also have ` |- ( E* x ph <-> E* y ps ) ` (that is a proof step). Therefore, since ` |- ( ( ph /\ ps ) <-> ( ps <-> ph ) ) ` ( ~ ancom ) and ` |- ( x = y <-> y = x ) ` ( ~ equcom ) , we obtain, still under the hypothesis and DV condition, ` |- ( A. x A. y ( ( ph /\ ps ) -> x = y ) <-> ` ` A. y A. x ( ( ph /\ ps ) -> x = y ) ) ` ; in other words, in this special case we can swap universal quantifiers as in ~ alcom without requiring ~ ax-11 . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Oct-2023.)
theorem mo4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| At-most-one quantifier expressed using implicit substitution. Note that the disjoint variable condition on ` y , ph ` can be replaced by the nonfreeness hypothesis ` |- F/ y ph ` with essentially the same proof. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jan-2023.)
theorem mo4f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the unique existential quantifier ("there exists a unique ` x ` such that ` ph ` ").
term weu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): wff;

--| Define the existential uniqueness quantifier. This expresses unique existence, or existential uniqueness, which is the conjunction of existence ( ~ df-ex ) and uniqueness ( ~ df-mo ). The expression ` E! x ph ` is read "there exists exactly one ` x ` such that ` ph ` " or "there exists a unique ` x ` such that ` ph ` ". This is also called the "uniqueness quantifier" but that expression is also used for the at-most-one quantifier ~ df-mo , therefore we avoid that ambiguous name. Definition 10.1 of [BellMachover] p. 97; also Definition *14.02 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 175. Other possible definitions are given by ~ eu1 , ~ eu2 , ~ eu3v , and ~ eu6 . As for double unique existence, beware that the expression ` E! x E! y ph ` means "there exists a unique ` x ` such that there exists a unique ` y ` such that ` ph ` " which is a weaker property than "there exists exactly one ` x ` and one ` y ` such that ` ph ` " (see ~ 2eu4 ). (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) Make this the definition (which used to be ~ eu6 , while this definition was then proved as ~ dfeu ). (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.)
axiom df_eu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| An alternate way to express existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jul-1994.) Replace a nonfreeness hypothesis with a disjoint variable condition on ` ph ` , ` y ` to reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 29-May-2019.)
theorem eu3v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Soundness justification theorem for ~ eu6 when this was the definition of the unique existential quantifier (note that ` y ` and ` z ` need not be disjoint, although the weaker theorem with that disjoint variable condition added would be enough to justify the soundness of the definition). See ~ eujustALT for a proof that provides an example of how it can be achieved through the use of ~ dvelim . (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.)
theorem eujust {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wex z (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ eujust illustrating the use of ~ dvelim . (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2010.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem eujustALT {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wex z (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) $;

--| Lemma of ~ eu6im . A dissection of an idiom characterizing existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) This used to be the definition of the unique existential quantifier, while ~ df-eu was then proved as ~ dfeu . (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2023.) Extract common proof lines. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 3-Mar-2023.)
theorem eu6lem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wa (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph))) (wex z (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the unique existential quantifier ~ df-eu not using the at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) This used to be the definition of the unique existential quantifier, while ~ df-eu was then proved as ~ dfeu . (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2023.) Remove use of ~ ax-11 . (Revised by SN, 21-Sep-2023.)
theorem eu6 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| One direction of ~ eu6 needs fewer axioms. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2023.)
theorem eu6im {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (weu x3 ph) $;

--| Version of ~ eu6 with disjoint variable condition replaced by nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Oct-2018.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 16-Oct-2022.)
theorem euf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Existential uniqueness implies existence. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem euex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wex x3 ph) $;

--| Existential uniqueness implies uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.)
theorem eumo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wmo x3 ph) $;

--| Uniqueness inferred from existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-1995.)
theorem eumoi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ weu x3 ph $ >
  $ wmo x3 ph $;

--| Existence implies that uniqueness is equivalent to unique existence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem exmoeub {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wb (wmo x3 ph) (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Existence is equivalent to uniqueness implying existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Dec-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem exmoeu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wi (wmo x3 ph) (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Uniqueness implies that existence is equivalent to unique existence. (Contributed by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem moeuex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wmo x3 ph) (wb (wex x3 ph) (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Uniqueness is equivalent to existence implying unique existence. Alternate definition of the at-most-one quantifier, in terms of the existential quantifier and the unique existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) This used to be the definition of the at-most-one quantifier, while ~ df-mo was then proved as ~ dfmo2 . (Revised by BJ, 30-Sep-2022.)
theorem moeu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wi (wex x3 ph) (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Equivalence theorem for the unique existential quantifier. Theorem *14.271 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem eubi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (weu x3 ph) (weu x3 ps)) $;

--| Introduce unique existential quantifier to both sides of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2016.)
theorem eubii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu x3 ps) $;

--| Formula-building rule for unique existential quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) Reduce axiom dependencies and shorten proof. (Revised by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem eubidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (weu x3 ps) (weu x3 ch)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for the unique existential quantifier (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Feb-2023.)
theorem eubid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (weu x3 ps) (weu x3 ch)) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ nfeu1 with a shorter proof but using ~ ax-12 . Bound-variable hypothesis builder for uniqueness. See also ~ nfeu1 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeu1ALT {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (weu x3 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for uniqueness. See ~ nfeu1ALT for a shorter proof using ~ ax-12 . This proof illustrates the systematic way of proving nonfreeness in a defined expression: consider the definiens as a tree whose nodes are its subformulas, and prove by tree-induction the nonfreeness of each node, starting from the leaves (generally using ~ nfv or nf* theorems for previously defined expressions) and up to the root. Here, the definiens is a conjunction of two previously defined expressions, which automatically yields the present proof. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Revised by BJ, 2-Oct-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem nfeu1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 (weu x3 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for uniqueness. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Oct-2018.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 14-Oct-2022.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use ~ nfeudw instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeud2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (weu y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the unique existential quantifier. Deduction version of ~ nfeu . Version of ~ nfeud with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfeudw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (weu y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the unique existential quantifier. Deduction version of ~ nfeu . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfeudw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeud {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (weu y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the unique existential quantifier. Version of ~ nfeu with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfeuw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (weu y1 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the unique existential quantifier. Note that ` x ` and ` y ` need not be disjoint. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfeuw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfeu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (weu y1 ph) $;

--| Rederive ~ df-eu from the old definition ~ eu6 . (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ df-eu instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfeu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| Rederive ~ df-mo from the old definition ~ moeu . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-May-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ dfmo instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfmo2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| There exists a unique set equal to a given set. Special case of ~ eueqi proved using only predicate calculus. The proof needs ` y = z ` be free of ` x ` . This is ensured by having ` x ` and ` y ` be distinct. Alternately, a distinctor ` -. A. x x = y ` could have been used instead. See ~ eueq and ~ eueqi for classes. (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 4-Dec-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Sep-2019.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 1-Mar-2023.)
theorem euequ {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ weu x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Lemma. Factor out the common proof skeleton of ~ sb8euv and ~ sb8eu . Variable substitution in unique existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Aug-2019.) Factor out common proof lines. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Feb-2023.)
theorem sb8eulem {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 (wsb x3 ph w) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Variable substitution in unique existential quantifier. Version of ~ sb8eu requiring more disjoint variables, but fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Feb-2023.)
theorem sb8euv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Variable substitution in unique existential quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . For a version requiring more disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ sb8euv . (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Aug-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb8eu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Variable substitution for the at-most-one quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Jun-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sb8mo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wmo y1 (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. See ~ cbvmo and ~ cbvmow for versions with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Revised by GG, 30-Sep-2024.)
theorem cbvmovw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wmo y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvmo with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.)
theorem cbvmow {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wmo y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbvmow , ~ cbvmovw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Andrew Salmon, 8-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Jan-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvmo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wmo y1 ps) $;

--| Change bound variable. Uses only Tarski's FOL axiom schemes. See ~ cbveu for a version with fewer disjoint variable conditions but requiring more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.) (Revised by GG, 30-Sep-2024.)
theorem cbveuvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 ps) $;

--| Version of ~ cbveu with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.) (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.)
theorem cbveuw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ cbveuw , ~ cbveuvw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbveu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 ps) $;

--| Alternative proof of ~ cbveu . Since ~ df-eu combines two other quantifiers, one can base this theorem on their associated 'change bounded variable' kind of theorems as well. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jan-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbveuALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 ps) $;

--| An alternate way of defining existential uniqueness. Definition 6.10 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Dec-2018.)
theorem eu2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| An alternate way to express uniqueness used by some authors. Exercise 2(b) of [Margaris] p. 110. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 29-Oct-2018.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 7-Feb-2023.)
theorem eu1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Introduce a disjunct into a unique existential quantifier. For a version requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ euorv . (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2005.)
theorem euor {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (weu x3 ps)) (weu x3 (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Introduce a disjunct into a unique existential quantifier. Version of ~ euor requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem euorv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (weu x3 ps)) (weu x3 (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Introduce or eliminate a disjunct in a unique existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Dec-2018.)
theorem euor2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 ph)) (wb (weu x3 (wo ph ps)) (weu x3 ps)) $;

--| Substitution into an at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem sbmo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wmo z ph) y1) (wmo z (wsb x3 ph y1)) $;

--| Uniqueness using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-1995.)
theorem eu4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Existential uniqueness implies uniqueness through reverse implication. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-1995.)
theorem euimmo {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (weu x3 ps) (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| Add unique existential quantifiers to an implication. Note the reversed implication in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Oct-2023.)
theorem euim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) (wi (weu x3 ps) (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Factor out the common proof skeleton of ~ moanimv and ~ moanim . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Dec-2018.) Factor out common proof lines. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Feb-2023.)
theorem moanimlem {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb (wmo x3 ps) (wmo x3 (wa ph ps))) $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) ph $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 (wa ph ps)) (wi ph (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Introduction of a conjunct into an at-most-one quantifier. Version of ~ moanim requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) Reduce axiom usage . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 8-Feb-2023.)
theorem moanimv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 (wa ph ps)) (wi ph (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Introduction of a conjunct into "at most one" quantifier. For a version requiring disjoint variables, but fewer axioms, see ~ moanimv . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Dec-2018.)
theorem moanim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wmo x3 (wa ph ps)) (wi ph (wmo x3 ps)) $;

--| Introduction of a conjunct into unique existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Dec-2018.)
theorem euan {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (weu x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (weu x3 ps)) $;

--| Nested at-most-one quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jan-2006.)
theorem moanmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wmo x3 (wa ph (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| Nested at-most-one and unique existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jan-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Dec-2018.)
theorem moaneu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wmo x3 (wa ph (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Introduction of a conjunct into unique existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem euanv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 (wa ph ps)) (wa ph (weu x3 ps)) $;

--| "At most one" picks a variable value, eliminating an existential quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jan-1997.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2019.)
theorem mopick {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Factor out the proof skeleton of ~ moexex and ~ moexexvw . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem moexexlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 (wmo x3 ph) $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wmo y1 (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wmo y1 ps))) (wmo y1 (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Double quantification with "at most one". (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.)
theorem _2moexv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wmo x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| "At most one" double quantification. Version of ~ moexexv with an additional disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-1997.) (Revised by GG, 22-Aug-2023.) Factor out common proof lines with ~ moexex . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem moexexvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wmo y1 ps))) (wmo y1 (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| A condition allowing to swap an existential quantifier and at at-most-one quantifier. Version of ~ 2moswap with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) (Revised by GG, 22-Aug-2023.) Factor out common proof lines with ~ moexexvw . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem _2moswapv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wi (wmo x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wmo y1 (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| A condition allowing to swap an existential quantifier and a unique existential quantifier. Version of ~ 2euswap with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) (Revised by GG, 22-Aug-2023.)
theorem _2euswapv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wi (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Double quantification with existential uniqueness. Version of ~ 2euex with ` x ` and ` y ` distinct, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem _2euexv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness implies double unique existential quantification. Version of ~ 2exeu with ` x ` and ` y ` distinct, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem _2exeuv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) $;

--| Existential uniqueness "picks" a variable value for which another wff is true. If there is only one thing ` x ` such that ` ph ` is true, and there is also an ` x ` (actually the same one) such that ` ph ` and ` ps ` are both true, then ` ph ` implies ` ps ` regardless of ` x ` . This theorem can be useful for eliminating existential quantifiers in a hypothesis. Compare Theorem *14.26 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-1994.)
theorem eupick {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (weu x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Version of ~ eupick with closed formulas. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2008.)
theorem eupicka {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (weu x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Existential uniqueness "pick" showing wff equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Dec-2018.)
theorem eupickb {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (w3a (weu x3 ph) (weu x3 ps) (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) (wb ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *14.26 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Dec-2018.)
theorem eupickbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| "At most one" can show the existence of a common value. In this case we can infer existence of conjunction from a conjunction of existence, and it is one way to achieve the converse of ~ 19.40 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.)
theorem mopick2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wmo x3 ph) (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ch))) (wex x3 (w3a ph ps ch)) $;

--| "At most one" double quantification. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the version ~ moexexvw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Dec-2018.) Factor out common proof lines with ~ moexexvw . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem moexex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wmo y1 ps))) (wmo y1 (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| "At most one" double quantification. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ moexexvw when possible. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-1997.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem moexexv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wmo x3 ph) (wal x3 (wmo y1 ps))) (wmo y1 (wex x3 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Double quantification with "at most one". Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2moexv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2moex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wmo x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wmo x3 ph)) $;

--| Double quantification with existential uniqueness. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2euexv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2euex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| Nested unique existential quantifier and at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.)
theorem _2eumo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wmo x3 (weu y1 ph)) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.)
theorem _2eu2ex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| A condition allowing to swap an existential quantifier and at at-most-one quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2moswapv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2moswap {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wi (wmo x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wmo y1 (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| A condition allowing to swap an existential quantifier and a unique existential quantifier. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2euswapv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2euswap {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wi (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness implies double unique existential quantification. The converse does not hold. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2exeuv when possible. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2exeu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) $;

--| Two ways of expressing "there exists at most one ordered pair ` <. x , y >. ` such that ` ph ( x , y ) ` holds. Note that this is not equivalent to ` E* x E* y ph ` . See also ~ 2mo . This is the analogue of ~ 2eu4 for existential uniqueness. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 26-Oct-2019.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 3-Jan-2023.)
theorem _2mo2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa (wmo x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wmo y1 (wex x3 ph))) (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing "there exists at most one ordered pair ` <. x , y >. ` such that ` ph ( x , y ) ` holds. See also ~ 2mo2 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Nov-2019.)
theorem _2mo {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa ph (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z)) (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Double "there exists at most one", using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-May-2025.)
theorem _2mos {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff z w):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa ph ps) (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness. This theorem shows a condition under which a "naive" definition matches the correct one. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ 2eu1v when possible. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Apr-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2eu1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wb (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph)))) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness. This theorem shows a condition under which a "naive" definition matches the correct one. Version of ~ 2eu1 with ` x ` and ` y ` distinct, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem _2eu1v {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph)) (wb (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph)))) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2eu2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu y1 (wex x3 ph)) (wb (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (weu x3 (wex y1 ph))) $;

--| Double existential uniqueness. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Apr-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2eu3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wo (wmo x3 ph) (wmo y1 ph)))) (wb (wa (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (weu y1 (weu x3 ph))) (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph)))) $;

--| This theorem provides us with a definition of double existential uniqueness ("exactly one ` x ` and exactly one ` y ` "). Naively one might think (incorrectly) that it could be defined by ` E! x E! y ph ` . See ~ 2eu1 for a condition under which the naive definition holds and ~ 2exeu for a one-way implication. See ~ 2eu5 and ~ 2eu8 for alternate definitions. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2001.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Sep-2019.)
theorem _2eu4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))))))) $;

--| An alternate definition of double existential uniqueness (see ~ 2eu4 ). A mistake sometimes made in the literature is to use ` E! x E! y ` to mean "exactly one ` x ` and exactly one ` y ` ". (For example, see Proposition 7.53 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 53.) It turns out that this is actually a weaker assertion, as can be seen by expanding out the formal definitions. This theorem shows that the erroneous definition can be repaired by conjoining ` A. x E* y ph ` as an additional condition. The correct definition apparently has never been published. ( ` E* ` means "there exists at most one".) (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2023.)
theorem _2eu5 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa (weu x3 (weu y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wmo y1 ph))) (wa (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))))))) $;

--| Two equivalent expressions for double existential uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Oct-2019.)
theorem _2eu6 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) (wex z (wex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wb ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Two equivalent expressions for double existential uniqueness. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2eu7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa (weu x3 (wex y1 ph)) (weu y1 (wex x3 ph))) (weu x3 (weu y1 (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ph)))) $;

--| Two equivalent expressions for double existential uniqueness. Curiously, we can put ` E! ` on either of the internal conjuncts but not both. We can also commute ` E! x E! y ` using ~ 2eu7 . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 20-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2eu8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (weu x3 (weu y1 (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ph)))) (weu x3 (weu y1 (wa (weu x3 ph) (wex y1 ph)))) $;

--| Two ways to express "exactly one thing exists". To paraphrase the statement and explain the label: there Exists a Unique thing if and only if for All ` x ` , ` x ` Equals some given (and disjoint) ` y ` . Both sides are false in set theory, see Theorems ~ neutru and ~ dtru . (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) State the theorem using truth constant ` T. ` . (Revised by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2023.)
theorem euae {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (weu x3 wtru) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Two ways to express "exactly one thing exists". The left-hand side requires only one variable to express this. Both sides are false in set theory, see Theorem ~ dtru . (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 7-Oct-2022.)
theorem exists1 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (weu x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A condition implying that at least two things exist. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.) Reduce axiom usage. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-Mar-2023.)
theorem exists2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wn ph))) (wn (weu x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) $;

--| "Barbara", one of the fundamental syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and all ` ch ` is ` ph ` , therefore all ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, AAA-1: MaP and SaM therefore SaP. For example, given "All men are mortal" and "Socrates is a man", we can prove "Socrates is mortal". If H is the set of men, M is the set of mortal beings, and S is Socrates, these word phrases can be represented as ` A. x ( x e. H -> x e. M ) ` (all men are mortal) and ` A. x ( x = S -> x e. H ) ` (Socrates is a man) therefore ` A. x ( x = S -> x e. M ) ` (Socrates is mortal). Russell and Whitehead note that "the syllogism in Barbara [[ ~ barbara ] is derived from [[ ~ syl ]" (quote after Theorem *2.06 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101). Most of the proof is in ~ alsyl . There are a legion of sources for Barbara, including ~ http://www.friesian.com/aristotl.htm , ~ http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-logic/ , and ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 24-Aug-2016.)
theorem barbara {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ps) $;

--| "Celarent", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and all ` ch ` is ` ph ` , therefore no ` ch ` is ` ps ` . Instance of ~ barbara . In Aristotelian notation, EAE-1: MeP and SaM therefore SeP. For example, given the "No reptiles have fur" and "All snakes are reptiles", therefore "No snakes have fur". Example from ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 24-Aug-2016.)
theorem celarent {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ps)) $;

--| "Darii", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ch ` is ` ph ` , therefore some ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, AII-1: MaP and SiM therefore SiP. For example, given "All rabbits have fur" and "Some pets are rabbits", therefore "Some pets have fur". Example from ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . See ~ dariiALT for a shorter proof requiring more axioms. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 24-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem darii {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ darii , shorter but using more axioms. This shows how the use of ~ spi may shorten some proofs of the Aristotelian syllogisms, even though this adds axiom dependencies. Note that ~ spi is the inference associated with ~ sp , which corresponds to the axiom (T) of modal logic. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) Added precisions on axiom usage. (Revised by BJ, 27-Sep-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dariiALT {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| "Ferio" ("Ferioque"), one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ch ` is ` ph ` , therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` . Instance of ~ darii . In Aristotelian notation, EIO-1: MeP and SiM therefore SoP. For example, given "No homework is fun" and "Some reading is homework", therefore "Some reading is not fun". This is essentially a logical axiom in Aristotelian logic. Example from ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 24-Aug-2016.)
theorem ferio {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ barbari and the other Aristotelian syllogisms with existential assumption. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem barbarilem {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ps) $;

--| "Barbari", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ch ` is ` ph ` , and some ` ch ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, AAI-1: MaP and SaM therefore SiP. For example, given "All men are mortal", "All Greeks are men", and "Greeks exist", therefore "Some Greeks are mortal". Note the existence hypothesis (to prove the "some" in the conclusion). Example from ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem barbari {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ barbari , shorter but using more axioms. See comment of ~ dariiALT . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem barbariALT {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| "Celaront", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ch ` is ` ph ` , and some ` ch ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` . Instance of ~ barbari . In Aristotelian notation, EAO-1: MeP and SaM therefore SoP. For example, given "No reptiles have fur", "All snakes are reptiles", and "Snakes exist", prove "Some snakes have no fur". Note the existence hypothesis. Example from ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.)
theorem celaront {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $;

--| "Cesare", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and all ` ch ` is ` ps ` , therefore no ` ch ` is ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, EAE-2: PeM and SaM therefore SeP. Related to ~ celarent . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem cesare {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Camestres", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and no ` ch ` is ` ps ` , therefore no ` ch ` is ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, AEE-2: PaM and SeM therefore SeP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem camestres {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Festino", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ch ` is ` ps ` , therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, EIO-2: PeM and SiM therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 25-Nov-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem festino {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ festino , shorter but using more axioms. See comment of ~ dariiALT . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem festinoALT {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Baroco", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` , therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, AOO-2: PaM and SoM therefore SoP. For example, "All informative things are useful", "Some websites are not useful", therefore "Some websites are not informative". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem baroco {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ festino , shorter but using more axioms. See comment of ~ dariiALT . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem barocoALT {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Cesaro", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ch ` is ` ps ` , and ` ch ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, EAO-2: PeM and SaM therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem cesaro {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Camestros", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , no ` ch ` is ` ps ` , and ` ch ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, AEO-2: PaM and SeM therefore SoP. For example, "All horses have hooves", "No humans have hooves", and humans exist, therefore "Some humans are not horses". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem camestros {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Datisi", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ph ` is ` ch ` , therefore some ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, AII-3: MaP and MiS therefore SiP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Shorten and reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem datisi {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| "Disamis", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. Some ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and all ` ph ` is ` ch ` , therefore some ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, IAI-3: MiP and MaS therefore SiP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem disamis {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| "Ferison", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and some ` ph ` is ` ch ` , therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` . Instance of ~ datisi . In Aristotelian notation, EIO-3: MeP and MiS therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem ferison {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $;

--| "Bocardo", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. Some ` ph ` is not ` ps ` , and all ` ph ` is ` ch ` , therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` . Instance of ~ disamis . In Aristotelian notation, OAO-3: MoP and MaS therefore SoP. For example, "Some cats have no tails", "All cats are mammals", therefore "Some mammals have no tails". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem bocardo {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wa ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $;

--| "Darapti", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ph ` is ` ch ` , and some ` ph ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is ` ps ` . In Aristotelian notation, AAI-3: MaP and MaS therefore SiP. For example, "All squares are rectangles" and "All squares are rhombuses", therefore "Some rhombuses are rectangles". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem darapti {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ darapti , shorter but using more axioms. See comment of ~ dariiALT . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 27-Aug-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem daraptiALT {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ps) $;

--| "Felapton", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ph ` is ` ch ` , and some ` ph ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ps ` . Instance of ~ darapti . In Aristotelian notation, EAO-3: MeP and MaS therefore SoP. For example, "No flowers are animals" and "All flowers are plants", therefore "Some plants are not animals". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.)
theorem felapton {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ps)) $;

--| "Calemes", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and no ` ps ` is ` ch ` , therefore no ` ch ` is ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, AEE-4: PaM and MeS therefore SeP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem calemes {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Dimatis", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. Some ` ph ` is ` ps ` , and all ` ps ` is ` ch ` , therefore some ` ch ` is ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, IAI-4: PiM and MaS therefore SiP. For example, "Some pets are rabbits", "All rabbits have fur", therefore "Some fur bearing animals are pets". Like ~ darii with positions interchanged. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Shorten and reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem dimatis {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wex x3 (wa ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ph) $;

--| "Fresison", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` (PeM), and some ` ps ` is ` ch ` (MiS), therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` (SoP). In Aristotelian notation, EIO-4: PeM and MiS therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Shorten and reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem fresison {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Calemos", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` (PaM), no ` ps ` is ` ch ` (MeS), and ` ch ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` (SoP). In Aristotelian notation, AEO-4: PaM and MeS therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Shorten and reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem calemos {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wex x3 ch $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Fesapo", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. No ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ps ` is ` ch ` , and ` ps ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is not ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, EAO-4: PeM and MaS therefore SoP. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem fesapo {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 ps $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch (wn ph)) $;

--| "Bamalip", one of the syllogisms of Aristotelian logic. All ` ph ` is ` ps ` , all ` ps ` is ` ch ` , and ` ph ` exist, therefore some ` ch ` is ` ph ` . In Aristotelian notation, AAI-4: PaM and MaS therefore SiP. Very similar to ~ barbari . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 28-Aug-2016.) Shorten and reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 16-Sep-2022.)
theorem bamalip {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wal x3 (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wal x3 (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wex x3 ph $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa ch ph) $;

--| Left 'and' elimination (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ia1). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axia1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ph $;

--| Right 'and' elimination (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ia2). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axia2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ps $;

--| 'And' introduction (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ia3). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axia3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wa ph ps)) $;

--| 'Not' introduction (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-in1). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axin1 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| 'Not' elimination (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-in2). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axin2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Definition of 'or' (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-io). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axio (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo ph ch) ps) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Specialization (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-4). This is just ~ sp by another name. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axi4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Converse of ~ axc4 (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-i5r). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.)
theorem axi5r {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) ps)) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` A. x ph ` (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ial). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axial {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is not free in ` E. x ph ` (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ie1). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axie1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| A key property of existential quantification (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-ie2). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.)
theorem axie2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ps (wal x3 ps))) (wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wi (wex x3 ph) ps)) $;

--| Axiom of existence (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-i9). In classical logic, this is equivalent to ~ ax-6 but in intuitionistic logic it needs to be stated using the existential quantifier. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axi9 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Axiom of Quantifier Substitution (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-10). This is just ~ axc11n by another name. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axi10 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Axiom of Quantifier Introduction (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-i12). In classical logic, this is mostly a restatement of ~ axc9 (with one additional quantifier). But in intuitionistic logic, changing the negations and implications to disjunctions makes it stronger. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Apr-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axi12 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wo (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (wo (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) (wal z (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Axiom of Bundling (intuitionistic logic axiom ax-bnd). In classical logic, this and ~ axi12 are fairly straightforward consequences of ~ axc9 . But in intuitionistic logic, it is not easy to add the extra ` A. x ` to ~ axi12 and so we treat the two as separate axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Mar-2018.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Apr-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axbnd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wo (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (wo (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) (wal x3 (wal z (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Axiom of extensionality. An axiom of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory. It states that two sets are identical if they contain the same elements. Axiom Ext of [BellMachover] p. 461. Its converse is a theorem of predicate logic, ~ elequ2g . Set theory can also be formulated with a _single_ primitive predicate ` e. ` on top of traditional predicate calculus _without_ equality. In that case the Axiom of Extensionality becomes ` ( A. w ( w e. x <-> w e. y ) -> ( x e. z -> y e. z ) ) ` , and equality ` x = y ` is _defined_ as ` A. w ( w e. x <-> w e. y ) ` . All of the usual axioms of equality then become theorems of set theory. See, for example, Axiom 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 8. To use the above "equality-free" version of Extensionality with Metamath's predicate calculus axioms, we would rewrite all axioms involving equality with equality expanded according to the above definition. Some of those axioms may be provable from ax-ext and would become redundant, but this hasn't been studied carefully. _General remarks_: Our set theory axioms are presented using defined connectives ( ` <-> ` , ` E. ` , etc.) for convenience. However, it is implicitly understood that the actual axioms use only the primitive connectives ` -> ` , ` -. ` , ` A. ` , ` = ` , and ` e. ` . It is straightforward to establish the equivalence between the actual axioms and the ones we display, and we will not do so. It is important to understand that strictly speaking, all of our set theory axioms are really schemes that represent an infinite number of actual axioms. This is inherent in the design of Metamath ("metavariable math"), which manipulates only metavariables. For example, the metavariable ` x ` in ~ ax-ext can represent any actual variable _v1_, _v2_, _v3_,... . Distinct variable restrictions ($d) prevent us from substituting say _v1_ for both ` x ` and ` z ` . This is in contrast to typical textbook presentations that present actual axioms (except for Replacement ~ ax-rep , which involves a wff metavariable). In practice, though, the theorems and proofs are essentially the same. The $d restrictions make each of the infinite axioms generated by the ~ ax-ext scheme exactly logically equivalent to each other and in particular to the actual axiom of the textbook version. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1993.)
axiom ax_ext {z: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| The axiom of extensionality ( ~ ax-ext ) restated so that it postulates the existence of a set ` z ` given two arbitrary sets ` x ` and ` y ` . This way to express it follows the general idea of the other ZFC axioms, which is to postulate the existence of sets given other sets. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2003.)
theorem axexte {z: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wex z (wi (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| A generalization of the axiom of extensionality in which ` x ` and ` y ` need not be distinct. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ, 12-Jul-2019.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Dec-2019.)
theorem axextg {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| A generalization of the axiom of extensionality in which ` x ` and ` y ` need not be distinct. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ, 12-Jul-2019.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 9-Dec-2019.)
theorem axextg_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| A bidirectional version of the axiom of extensionality. Although this theorem looks like a definition of equality, it requires the axiom of extensionality for its proof under our axiomatization. See the comments for ~ ax-ext and ~ df-cleq . (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2008.)
theorem axextb {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A bidirectional version of the axiom of extensionality. Although this theorem looks like a definition of equality, it requires the axiom of extensionality for its proof under our axiomatization. See the comments for ~ ax-ext and ~ df-cleq . (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2008.)
theorem axextb_b {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) $;

--| There exists at most one set with prescribed elements. Theorem 1.1 of [BellMachover] p. 462. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2019.) Use the at-most-one quantifier. (Revised by BJ, 17-Sep-2022.)
theorem axextmo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wmo x3 (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) ph)) $;

--| There exists at most one empty set. With either ~ axnul or ~ axnulALT or ~ ax-nul , this proves that there exists a unique empty set. In practice, once the language of classes is available, we use the stronger characterization among classes ~ eq0 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2007.) Use the at-most-one quantifier. (Revised by BJ, 17-Sep-2022.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Apr-2023.)
theorem nulmo {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wmo x3 (wal y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Introduce the class abstraction (or class builder) notation: ` { x | ph } ` is the class of sets ` x ` such that ` ph ( x ) ` is true. A setvar variable can be expressed as a class abstraction per Theorem ~ cvjust , justifying the substitution of class variables for setvar variables via the use of ~ cv .
term cab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): class;

--| Define class abstractions, that is, classes of the form ` { y | ph } ` , which is read "the class of sets ` y ` such that ` ph ( y ) ` ". A few remarks are in order: 1. The axiomatic statement ~ df-clab does not define the class abstraction ` { y | ph } ` itself, that is, it does not have the form ` |- { y | ph } = ... ` that a standard definition should have (for a good reason: equality itself has not yet been defined or axiomatized for class abstractions; it is defined later in ~ df-cleq ). Instead, ~ df-clab has the form ` |- ( x e. { y | ph } <-> ... ) ` , meaning that it only defines what it means for a setvar to be a member of a class abstraction. As a consequence, one can say that ~ df-clab defines class abstractions if and only if a class abstraction is completely determined by which elements belong to it, which is the content of the axiom of extensionality ~ ax-ext . Therefore, ~ df-clab can be considered a definition only in systems that can prove ~ ax-ext (and the necessary first-order logic). 2. As in all definitions, the definiendum (the left-hand side of the biconditional) has no disjoint variable conditions. In particular, the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` need not be distinct, and the formula ` ph ` may depend on both ` x ` and ` y ` . This is necessary, as with all definitions, since if there was for instance a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , then one could not do anything with expressions like ` x e. { x | ph } ` which are sometimes useful to shorten proofs (because of ~ abid ). Most often, however, ` x ` does not occur in ` { y | ph } ` and ` y ` is free in ` ph ` . 3. Remark 1 stresses that ~ df-clab does not have the standard form of a definition for a class, but one could be led to think it has the standard form of a definition for a formula. However, it also fails that test since the membership predicate ` e. ` has already appeared earlier (outside of syntax e.g. in ~ ax-8 ). Indeed, the definiendum extends, or "overloads", the membership predicate ` e. ` from formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` setvar" to formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` class abstraction". This is possible because of ~ wcel and ~ cab , and it can be called an "extension" of the membership predicate because of ~ wel , whose proof uses ~ cv . An _a posteriori_ justification for ~ cv is given by ~ cvjust , stating that every setvar can be written as a class abstraction (though conversely not every class abstraction is a set, as illustrated by Russell's paradox ~ ru ). 4. Proof techniques. Because class variables can be substituted with compound expressions and setvar variables cannot, it is often useful to convert a theorem containing a free setvar variable to a more general version with a class variable. This is done with theorems such as ~ vtoclg which is used, for example, to convert ~ elirrv to ~ elirr . 5. Definition or axiom? The question arises with the three axiomatic statements introducing classes, ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , and ~ df-clel , to decide if they qualify as definitions or if they should be called axioms. Under the strict definition of "definition" (see ~ conventions ), they are not definitions (see Remarks 1 and 3 above, and similarly for ~ df-cleq and ~ df-clel ). One could be less strict and decide to call "definition" every axiomatic statement which provides an eliminable and conservative extension of the considered axiom system. But the notion of conservativity may be given two different meanings in set.mm, due to the difference between the "scheme level" of set.mm and the "object level" of classical treatments. For a proof that these three axiomatic statements yield an eliminable and weakly (that is, object-level) conservative extension of FOL= plus ~ ax-ext , see Appendix of [Levy] p. 357. 6. References and history. The concept of class abstraction dates back to at least Frege, and is used by Whitehead and Russell. This definition is Definition 2.1 of [Quine] p. 16 and Axiom 4.3.1 of [Levy] p. 12. It is called the "axiom of class comprehension" by [Levy] p. 358, who treats the theory of classes as an extralogical extension to predicate logic and set theory axioms. He calls the construction ` { y | ph } ` a "class term". For a full description of how classes are introduced and how to recover the primitive language, see the books of Quine and Levy (and the comment of ~ eqabb for a quick overview). For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 19-Aug-2023.)
axiom df_clab {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (x3: setvar):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cab y1 ph)) (wsb y1 ph x3) $;

--| Define class abstractions, that is, classes of the form ` { y | ph } ` , which is read "the class of sets ` y ` such that ` ph ( y ) ` ". A few remarks are in order: 1. The axiomatic statement ~ df-clab does not define the class abstraction ` { y | ph } ` itself, that is, it does not have the form ` |- { y | ph } = ... ` that a standard definition should have (for a good reason: equality itself has not yet been defined or axiomatized for class abstractions; it is defined later in ~ df-cleq ). Instead, ~ df-clab has the form ` |- ( x e. { y | ph } <-> ... ) ` , meaning that it only defines what it means for a setvar to be a member of a class abstraction. As a consequence, one can say that ~ df-clab defines class abstractions if and only if a class abstraction is completely determined by which elements belong to it, which is the content of the axiom of extensionality ~ ax-ext . Therefore, ~ df-clab can be considered a definition only in systems that can prove ~ ax-ext (and the necessary first-order logic). 2. As in all definitions, the definiendum (the left-hand side of the biconditional) has no disjoint variable conditions. In particular, the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` need not be distinct, and the formula ` ph ` may depend on both ` x ` and ` y ` . This is necessary, as with all definitions, since if there was for instance a disjoint variable condition on ` x , y ` , then one could not do anything with expressions like ` x e. { x | ph } ` which are sometimes useful to shorten proofs (because of ~ abid ). Most often, however, ` x ` does not occur in ` { y | ph } ` and ` y ` is free in ` ph ` . 3. Remark 1 stresses that ~ df-clab does not have the standard form of a definition for a class, but one could be led to think it has the standard form of a definition for a formula. However, it also fails that test since the membership predicate ` e. ` has already appeared earlier (outside of syntax e.g. in ~ ax-8 ). Indeed, the definiendum extends, or "overloads", the membership predicate ` e. ` from formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` setvar" to formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` class abstraction". This is possible because of ~ wcel and ~ cab , and it can be called an "extension" of the membership predicate because of ~ wel , whose proof uses ~ cv . An _a posteriori_ justification for ~ cv is given by ~ cvjust , stating that every setvar can be written as a class abstraction (though conversely not every class abstraction is a set, as illustrated by Russell's paradox ~ ru ). 4. Proof techniques. Because class variables can be substituted with compound expressions and setvar variables cannot, it is often useful to convert a theorem containing a free setvar variable to a more general version with a class variable. This is done with theorems such as ~ vtoclg which is used, for example, to convert ~ elirrv to ~ elirr . 5. Definition or axiom? The question arises with the three axiomatic statements introducing classes, ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , and ~ df-clel , to decide if they qualify as definitions or if they should be called axioms. Under the strict definition of "definition" (see ~ conventions ), they are not definitions (see Remarks 1 and 3 above, and similarly for ~ df-cleq and ~ df-clel ). One could be less strict and decide to call "definition" every axiomatic statement which provides an eliminable and conservative extension of the considered axiom system. But the notion of conservativity may be given two different meanings in set.mm, due to the difference between the "scheme level" of set.mm and the "object level" of classical treatments. For a proof that these three axiomatic statements yield an eliminable and weakly (that is, object-level) conservative extension of FOL= plus ~ ax-ext , see Appendix of [Levy] p. 357. 6. References and history. The concept of class abstraction dates back to at least Frege, and is used by Whitehead and Russell. This definition is Definition 2.1 of [Quine] p. 16 and Axiom 4.3.1 of [Levy] p. 12. It is called the "axiom of class comprehension" by [Levy] p. 358, who treats the theory of classes as an extralogical extension to predicate logic and set theory axioms. He calls the construction ` { y | ph } ` a "class term". For a full description of how classes are introduced and how to recover the primitive language, see the books of Quine and Levy (and the comment of ~ eqabb for a quick overview). For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 19-Aug-2023.)
axiom df_clab_b {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1):
  $ wb (wcel (cv y1) (cab y1 ph)) (wsb y1 ph y1) $;

--| Extension (in the sense of Remark 3 of the comment of ~ df-clab ) of ~ elequ1 from formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` setvar" to formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` class abstraction". This extension does not require ~ ax-8 contrary to ~ elequ1 , but recall from Remark 3 of the comment of ~ df-clab that it can be considered an extension only because of ~ cvjust , which does require ~ ax-8 . This is an instance of ~ eleq1w where the containing class is a class abstraction, and contrary to it, it can be proved without ~ df-clel . See also ~ eleq1 for general classes. The straightforward yet important fact that this statement can be proved from FOL= plus ~ df-clab (hence without ~ ax-ext , ~ df-cleq or ~ df-clel ) was stressed by Mario Carneiro. (Contributed by BJ, 17-Aug-2023.)
theorem eleq1ab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cab z ph)) (wcel (cv y1) (cab z ph))) $;

--| Extension (in the sense of Remark 3 of the comment of ~ df-clab ) of ~ elequ1 from formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` setvar" to formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` class abstraction". This extension does not require ~ ax-8 contrary to ~ elequ1 , but recall from Remark 3 of the comment of ~ df-clab that it can be considered an extension only because of ~ cvjust , which does require ~ ax-8 . This is an instance of ~ eleq1w where the containing class is a class abstraction, and contrary to it, it can be proved without ~ df-clel . See also ~ eleq1 for general classes. The straightforward yet important fact that this statement can be proved from FOL= plus ~ df-clab (hence without ~ ax-ext , ~ df-cleq or ~ df-clel ) was stressed by Mario Carneiro. (Contributed by BJ, 17-Aug-2023.)
theorem eleq1ab_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cab y1 ph)) (wcel (cv y1) (cab y1 ph))) $;

--| Extension of ~ cleljust from formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` setvar" to formulas of the form "setvar ` e. ` class abstraction". This is an instance of ~ dfclel where the containing class is a class abstraction. The same remarks as for ~ eleq1ab apply. (Contributed by BJ, 8-Nov-2021.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 19-May-2023.)
theorem cleljustab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cab y1 ph)) (wex z (wa (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph)))) $;

--| Simplification of class abstraction notation when the free and bound variables are identical. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem abid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) (cab x3 ph)) ph $;

--| A standard theorem of predicate calculus ( ~ stdpc4 ) expressed using class abstractions. Closed form of ~ vexw . (Contributed by BJ, 14-Jun-2019.)
theorem vexwt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 ph)) $;

--| If ` ph ` is a theorem, then any set belongs to the class ` { x | ph } ` . Therefore, ` { x | ph } ` is "a" universal class. This is the closest one can get to defining a universal class, or proving ~ vex , without using ~ ax-ext . Note that this theorem has no disjoint variable condition and does not use ~ df-clel nor ~ df-cleq either: only propositional logic and ~ ax-gen and ~ df-clab . This is ~ sbt expressed using class abstractions. Without ~ ax-ext , one cannot define "the" universal class, since one could not prove for instance the justification theorem ` { x | T. } = { y | T. } ` (see ~ vjust ). Indeed, in order to prove any equality of classes, one needs ~ df-cleq , which has ~ ax-ext as a hypothesis. Therefore, the classes ` { x | T. } ` , ` { y | ( ph -> ph ) } ` , ` { z | ( A. t t = t -> A. t t = t ) } ` and countless others are all universal classes whose equality cannot be proved without ~ ax-ext . Once ~ dfcleq is available, we will define "the" universal class in ~ df-v . Its degenerate instance is also a simple consequence of ~ abid (using ~ mpbir ). (Contributed by BJ, 13-Jun-2019.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 25-Apr-2023.)
theorem vexw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 ph) $;

--| If ` ph ` is a theorem, then any set belongs to the class ` { x | ph } ` . Therefore, ` { x | ph } ` is "a" universal class. This is the closest one can get to defining a universal class, or proving ~ vex , without using ~ ax-ext . Note that this theorem has no disjoint variable condition and does not use ~ df-clel nor ~ df-cleq either: only propositional logic and ~ ax-gen and ~ df-clab . This is ~ sbt expressed using class abstractions. Without ~ ax-ext , one cannot define "the" universal class, since one could not prove for instance the justification theorem ` { x | T. } = { y | T. } ` (see ~ vjust ). Indeed, in order to prove any equality of classes, one needs ~ df-cleq , which has ~ ax-ext as a hypothesis. Therefore, the classes ` { x | T. } ` , ` { y | ( ph -> ph ) } ` , ` { z | ( A. t t = t -> A. t t = t ) } ` and countless others are all universal classes whose equality cannot be proved without ~ ax-ext . Once ~ dfcleq is available, we will define "the" universal class in ~ df-v . Its degenerate instance is also a simple consequence of ~ abid (using ~ mpbir ). (Contributed by BJ, 13-Jun-2019.) Reduce axiom dependencies. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 25-Apr-2023.)
theorem vexw_b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wcel (cv x3) (cab x3 ph) $;

--| Every setvar is a member of ` { x | T. } ` , which is therefore "a" universal class. Once class extensionality ~ dfcleq is available, we can say "the" universal class (see ~ df-v ). This is ~ sbtru expressed using class abstractions. (Contributed by BJ, 2-Sep-2023.)
theorem vextru {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 wtru) $;

--| Every setvar is a member of ` { x | T. } ` , which is therefore "a" universal class. Once class extensionality ~ dfcleq is available, we can say "the" universal class (see ~ df-v ). This is ~ sbtru expressed using class abstractions. (Contributed by BJ, 2-Sep-2023.)
theorem vextru_b {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cv x3) (cab x3 wtru) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Remove use of ~ ax-12 . (Revised by SN, 20-Sep-2023.)
theorem nfsab1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 ph)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2024.)
theorem hbab1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cab x3 ph))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ hbabg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem hbab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ hbabg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem hbab_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) (cab y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cab y1 ph))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ hbab for a version with more disjoint variable conditions, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hbabg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfsabg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfsab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfsabg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfsab_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cab y1 ph)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfsab for a version with more disjoint variable conditions, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfsabg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv z) (cab y1 ph)) $;

--| Define the equality connective between classes. Definition 2.7 of [Quine] p. 18. Also Definition 4.5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 13; Chapter 4 provides its justification and methods for eliminating it. Note that its elimination will not necessarily result in a single wff in the original language but possibly a "scheme" of wffs. The hypotheses express that all instances of the conclusion where class variables are replaced with setvar variables hold. Therefore, this definition merely extends to class variables something that is true for setvar variables, hence is conservative. This is only a proof sketch of conservativity; for details see Appendix of [Levy] p. 357. This is the reason why we call this axiomatic statement a "definition", even though it does not have the usual form of a definition. If we required a definition to have the usual form, we would call ~ df-cleq an axiom. See also comments under ~ df-clab , ~ df-clel , and ~ eqabb . In the form of ~ dfcleq , this is called the "axiom of extensionality" by [Levy] p. 338, who treats the theory of classes as an extralogical extension to our logic and set theory axioms. While the three class definitions ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , and ~ df-clel are eliminable and conservative and thus meet the requirements for sound definitions, they are technically axioms in that they do not satisfy the requirements for the current definition checker. The proofs of conservativity require external justification that is beyond the scope of the definition checker. For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_cleq {x3 y1 z v u t: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wal u (wb (wcel (cv u) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv u) (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wb (wceq (cv t) (cv t)) (wal v (wb (wcel (cv v) (cv t)) (wcel (cv v) (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| The defining characterization of class equality. It is proved, over Tarski's FOL, from the axiom of (set) extensionality ( ~ ax-ext ) and the definition of class equality ( ~ df-cleq ). Its forward implication is called "class extensionality". Remark: the proof uses ~ axextb to prove also the hypothesis of ~ df-cleq that is a degenerate instance, but it could be proved also from minimal propositional calculus and { ~ ax-gen , ~ equid }. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.)
theorem dfcleq {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Every set is a class. Proposition 4.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 13. This theorem shows that a setvar variable can be expressed as a class abstraction. This provides a motivation for the class syntax construction ~ cv , which allows to substitute a setvar variable for a class variable. See also ~ cab and ~ df-clab . Note that this is not a rigorous justification, because ~ cv is used as part of the proof of this theorem, but a careful argument can be made outside of the formalism of Metamath, for example as is done in Chapter 4 of Takeuti and Zaring. See also the discussion under the definition of class in [Jech] p. 4 showing that "Every set can be considered to be a class." See ~ abid1 for the version of ~ cvjust extended to classes. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2006.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 4-May-2023.)
theorem cvjust {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wceq (cv x3) (cab y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) $;

--| Proof of ~ ax-9 from Tarski's FOL and ~ dfcleq . For a version not using ~ ax-8 either, see ~ eleq2w2 . This shows that ~ dfcleq is too powerful to be used as a definition instead of ~ df-cleq . Note that ~ ax-ext is also a direct consequence of ~ dfcleq (as an instance of its forward implication). (Contributed by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax9ALT {z: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) $;

--| A weaker version of ~ eleq2 (but stronger than ~ ax-9 and ~ elequ2 ) that uses ~ ax-12 to avoid ~ ax-8 and ~ df-clel . Compare ~ eleq2w , whose setvars appear where the class variables are in this theorem, and vice versa. (Contributed by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.) Strengthen from setvar variables to class variables. (Revised by WL and SN, 23-Aug-2024.)
theorem eleq2w2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) $;

--| Infer equality of classes from equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqriv {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wceq A2 B2 $;

--| Deduce equality of classes from equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1996.)
theorem eqrdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Deduce equality of classes from an equivalence of membership that depends on the membership variable. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqrdav {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Law of identity (reflexivity of class equality). Theorem 6.4 of [Quine] p. 41. This is part of Frege's eighth axiom per Proposition 54 of [Frege1879] p. 50; see also ~ biid . An early mention of this law can be found in Aristotle, _Metaphysics_, Z.17, 1041a10-20. (Thanks to Stefan Allan and BJ for this information.) (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 14-Oct-2017.)
theorem eqid (A2: class):
  $ wceq A2 A2 $;

--| Class identity law with antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 21-Aug-2008.)
theorem eqidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 A2) $;

--| Deduction from equality to equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 5-Dec-2019.)
theorem eqeq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ eqeq1d , shorter but requiring ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem eqeq1dALT (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Equality implies equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqeq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Inference from equality to equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.)
theorem eqeq1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 C) $;

--| Deduction from commutative law for class equality. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.) Allow shortening of ~ eqcom . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 A2) $;

--| Commutative law for class equality. Theorem 6.5 of [Quine] p. 41. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wceq B2 A2) $;

--| Inference applying commutative law for class equality to an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqcoms (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq B2 A2) ph $;

--| Inference from commutative law for class equality. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem eqcomi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 A2 $;

--| Commute an inequality. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem neqcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq B2 A2)) $;

--| Deduction from equality to equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1993.) Allow shortening of ~ eqeq2 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqeq2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq C A2) (wceq C B2)) $;

--| Equality implies equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqeq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wceq C A2) (wceq C B2)) $;

--| Inference from equality to equivalence of equalities. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem eqeq2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wceq C A2) (wceq C B2) $;

--| A useful inference for substituting definitions into an equality. See also ~ eqeqan12dALT . (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) Shorten other proofs. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2024.)
theorem eqeqan12d (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| A useful inference for substituting definitions into an equality. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.)
theorem eqeqan12rd (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| A useful inference for substituting definitions into an equality. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2024.)
theorem eqeq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Equality relationship among four classes. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Oct-2024.)
theorem eqeq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| A useful inference for substituting definitions into an equality. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqeq12i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ eqeqan12d . This proof has one more step but one fewer essential step. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem eqeqan12dALT (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Transitive law for class equality. Proposition 4.7(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 13. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jan-2004.)
theorem eqtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq B2 C)) (wceq A2 C) $;

--| A transitive law for class equality. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Oct-2024.)
theorem eqtr2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq A2 C)) (wceq B2 C) $;

--| A transitive law for class equality. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Oct-2024.)
theorem eqtr3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 C)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| An equality transitivity inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem eqtri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $;

--| An equality transitivity inference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-1995.)
theorem eqtr2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $;

--| An equality transitivity inference. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1994.)
theorem eqtr3i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $;

--| An equality transitivity inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem eqtr4i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1993.)
theorem _3eqtri (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq A2 D $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtrri (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq D A2 $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3eqtr2i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq A2 D $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr2ri (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq D A2 $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr3i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 D $ >
  $ wceq C D $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2004.)
theorem _3eqtr3ri (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 D $ >
  $ wceq D C $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr4i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $ >
  $ wceq D B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $;

--| An inference from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr4ri (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $ >
  $ wceq D B2 $ >
  $ wceq D C $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqtrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1999.)
theorem eqtr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $;

--| An equality transitivity equality deduction. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-1995.)
theorem eqtr3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity equality deduction. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-1995.)
theorem eqtr4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.)
theorem _3eqtrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 D) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtrrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D A2) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3eqtr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 D) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2006.)
theorem _3eqtr2rd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D A2) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jan-2006.)
theorem _3eqtr3rd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D C) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A deduction from three chained equalities. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.)
theorem _3eqtr4rd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqtrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-1998.)
theorem eqtr2id (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem eqtr3id (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq B2 A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-1998.)
theorem eqtr3di (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqtrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-1998.)
theorem eqtr2di (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqtr4di (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-1998.)
theorem eqtr4id (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 8-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem sylan9eq (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jun-2007.)
theorem sylan9req (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq A2 C) $;

--| An equality transitivity deduction. (Contributed by NM, 8-May-1994.)
theorem sylan9eqr (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps ph) (wceq A2 C) $;

--| A chained equality inference, useful for converting from definitions. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1994.)
theorem _3eqtr3g (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 D $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A chained equality inference, useful for converting from definitions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2015.)
theorem _3eqtr3a (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A chained equality inference, useful for converting to definitions. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem _3eqtr4g (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $ >
  $ wceq D B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A chained equality inference, useful for converting to definitions. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _3eqtr4a (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $;

--| A compound transitive inference for class equality. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-2004.)
theorem eq2tri (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 C) (wceq D F) $ >
  $ wi (wceq B2 D) (wceq C G) $ >
  $ wb (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 F)) (wa (wceq B2 D) (wceq A2 G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iseqsetv-cleq . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 17-Aug-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem iseqsetvlem {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (wex z (wceq (cv z) A2)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ iseqsetv-clel . The expression ` E. x x = A ` does not depend on a particular choice of the set variable. The proof here avoids ~ df-clab , ~ df-clel and ~ ax-8 , but instead is based on ~ ax-9 , ~ ax-ext and ~ df-cleq . In particular it still accepts ` x e. A ` being a primitive syntax term, not assuming any specific semantics (like elementhood in some form). Use it in contexts where you want to avoid ~ df-clab , or you need ~ df-cleq anyway. See the alternative version , not using ~ df-cleq or ~ ax-ext or ~ ax-9 . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-Aug-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem iseqsetv_cleq {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (wex y1 (wceq (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Equivalent formulas yield equal class abstractions (closed form). This is the backward implication of ~ abbib , proved from fewer axioms, and hence is independently named. (Contributed by BJ and WL and SN, 20-Aug-2023.)
theorem abbi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab x3 ps)) $;

--| Equivalent wff's yield equal class abstractions (deduction form). (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1993.) Avoid ~ ax-12 , based on an idea of Steven Nguyen. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.)
theorem abbidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cab x3 ps) (cab x3 ch)) $;

--| Equivalent wff's yield equal class abstractions (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , and ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 3-May-2023.)
theorem abbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab x3 ps) $;

--| Equivalent wff's yield equal class abstractions (deduction form, with nonfreeness hypothesis). (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-10 and ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.)
theorem abbid {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cab x3 ps) (cab x3 ch)) $;

--| Equal class abstractions require equivalent formulas, and conversely. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-8 and ~ df-clel (by avoiding use of ~ cleqh ). (Revised by BJ, 23-Jun-2019.) Reverse the biconditional. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 23-Feb-2025.) (Proof shortened by SN, 22-Oct-2026.)
theorem abbib {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ abbib as of 22-Oct-2026. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem abbibOLD {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab x3 ps)) (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvab with disjoint variable conditions requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) Require ` x ` , ` y ` be disjoint to avoid ~ ax-11 and ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 4-Dec-2022.)
theorem cbvabv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Version of ~ cbvab with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.)
theorem cbvabw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab y1 ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Usage of the weaker ~ cbvabw and ~ cbvabv are preferred. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 ph) (cab y1 ps) $;

--| Version of ~ eqabb using implicit substitution, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by GG and AV, 18-Sep-2024.)
theorem eqabbw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cab x3 ph)) (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) A2) ps)) $;

--| Version of ~ eqabcb using implicit substitution, which requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by TM, 24-Jan-2026.)
theorem eqabcbw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wceq (cab x3 ph) A2) (wal y1 (wb ps (wcel (cv y1) A2))) $;

--| Define the membership connective between classes. Theorem 6.3 of [Quine] p. 41, or Proposition 4.6 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 13, which we adopt as a definition. See these references for its metalogical justification. The hypotheses express that all instances of the conclusion where class variables are replaced with setvar variables hold. Therefore, this definition merely extends to class variables something that is true for setvar variables, hence is conservative. This is only a proof sketch of conservativity; for details see Appendix of [Levy] p. 357. This is the reason why we call this axiomatic statement a "definition", even though it does not have the usual form of a definition. If we required a definition to have the usual form, we would call ~ df-clel an axiom. See also comments under ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , and ~ eqabb . Alternate characterizations of ` A e. B ` when either ` A ` or ` B ` is a set are given by ~ clel2g , ~ clel3g , and ~ clel4g . This is called the "axiom of membership" by [Levy] p. 338, who treats the theory of classes as an extralogical extension to our logic and set theory axioms. While the three class definitions ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , and ~ df-clel are eliminable and conservative and thus meet the requirements for sound definitions, they are technically axioms in that they do not satisfy the requirements for the current definition checker. The proofs of conservativity require external justification that is beyond the scope of the definition checker. For a general discussion of the theory of classes, see ~ mmset.html#class . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 27-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_clel {x3 y1 z v u t: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wex u (wa (wceq (cv u) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv u) (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cv t) (cv t)) (wex v (wa (wceq (cv v) (cv t)) (wcel (cv v) (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 B2) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Characterization of the elements of a class. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Jun-2019.)
theorem dfclel {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 B2) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| If a class contains another class, then it contains some set. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 25-Sep-2011.) Avoid ~ ax-9 , ~ ax-ext , ~ df-clab . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 30-Nov-2024.)
theorem elex2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) B2)) $;

--| Weak version of ~ isset . (Contributed by BJ, 24-Apr-2024.)
theorem issettru {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (wcel A2 (cab y1 wtru)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ iseqsetv-cleq . The expression ` E. x x = A ` does not depend on a particular choice of the set variable. Use this theorem in contexts where ~ df-cleq or ~ ax-ext is not referenced elsewhere in your proof. It is proven from a specific implementation (class builder, axiom ~ df-clab ) of the primitive term ` x e. A ` . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Apr-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem iseqsetv_clel {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (wex y1 (wceq (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ elisset and ~ isset . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) Extract from the proof of ~ isset . (Revised by WL, 2-Feb-2025.)
theorem issetlem {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wcel (cv x3) V $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| An element of a class exists. Version of ~ elisset with a disjoint variable condition on ` V , x ` , avoiding ~ df-clab . Prefer its use over ~ elisset when sufficient (for instance in usages where ` x ` is a dummy variable). (Contributed by BJ, 14-Sep-2019.)
theorem elissetv {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| An element of a class exists. Use ~ elissetv instead when sufficient (for instance in usages where ` x ` is a dummy variable). (Contributed by NM, 1-May-1995.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by BJ, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem elisset {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| Weaker version of ~ eleq1 (but more general than ~ elequ1 ) not depending on ~ ax-ext nor ~ df-cleq . Note that this provides a proof of ~ ax-8 from Tarski's FOL and ~ dfclel (simply consider an instance where ` A ` is replaced by a setvar and deduce the forward implication by ~ biimpd ), which shows that ~ dfclel is too powerful to be used as a definition instead of ~ df-clel . (Contributed by BJ, 24-Jun-2019.)
theorem eleq1w {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Weaker version of ~ eleq2 (but more general than ~ elequ2 ) not depending on ~ ax-ext nor ~ df-cleq . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Sep-2019.)
theorem eleq2w {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) $;

--| Deduction from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) Allow shortening of ~ eleq1 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Nov-2019.)
theorem eleq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C)) $;

--| Deduction from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1993.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 5-Dec-2019.)
theorem eleq2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C A2) (wcel C B2)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ eleq2d , shorter at the expense of requiring ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-1993.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem eleq2dALT (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C A2) (wcel C B2)) $;

--| Equality implies equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Nov-2019.)
theorem eleq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C)) $;

--| Equality implies equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Nov-2019.)
theorem eleq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wcel C A2) (wcel C B2)) $;

--| Equality implies equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-1999.)
theorem eleq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) $;

--| Inference from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eleq1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C) $;

--| Inference from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
theorem eleq2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wcel C A2) (wcel C B2) $;

--| Inference from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-1994.)
theorem eleq12i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D) $;

--| Deduction from equality to equivalence of membership. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-1994.)
theorem eleq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) $;

--| A transitive-type law relating membership and equality. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1994.)
theorem eleq1a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wceq C A2) (wcel C B2)) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqeltri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 C $ >
  $ wcel A2 C $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.)
theorem eqeltrri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 C $ >
  $ wcel B2 C $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.)
theorem eleqtri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wcel A2 C $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-1993.)
theorem eleqtrri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 C $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation, deduction form. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 10-Dec-2002.)
theorem eqeltrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| Deduction that substitutes equal classes into membership. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem eqeltrrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $;

--| Deduction that substitutes equal classes into membership. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem eleqtrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| Deduction that substitutes equal classes into membership. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem eleqtrrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eqeltrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eqeltrrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq B2 A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eleqtrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eleqtrrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eqeltrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wcel B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eqeltrrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 A2) $ >
  $ wcel B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem eleqtrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| A membership and equality inference. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2005.)
theorem eleqtrrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem _3eltr3i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 D $ >
  $ wcel C D $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem _3eltr4i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $ >
  $ wceq D B2 $ >
  $ wcel C D $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem _3eltr3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C D) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem _3eltr4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C D) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem _3eltr3g (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq A2 C $ >
  $ wceq B2 D $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C D) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem _3eltr4g (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C A2 $ >
  $ wceq D B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C D) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into a membership antecedent. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem eleq2s (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) ph $ >
  $ wceq C B2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) ph $;

--| If a class is not an element of another class, an equal class is also not an element. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem eqneltri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wn (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 C) $;

--| If a class is not an element of another class, an equal class is also not an element. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem eqneltrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 C)) $;

--| If a class is not an element of another class, an equal class is also not an element. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqneltrrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel B2 C)) $;

--| If a class is not an element of another class, it is also not an element of an equal class. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem neleqtrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C B2)) $;

--| If a class is not an element of another class, it is also not an element of an equal class. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Nov-2019.)
theorem neleqtrrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C A2)) $;

--| A way of showing two classes are not equal. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-1997.)
theorem nelneq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wn (wcel B2 C))) (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| A way of showing two classes are not equal. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-2002.)
theorem nelneq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wn (wcel A2 C))) (wn (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Substitution for the left-hand side in an equality. Class version of ~ equsb3 . (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 28-Apr-2010.)
theorem eqsb1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wceq (cv x3) A2) y1) (wceq (cv y1) A2) $;

--| Substitution for the first argument of the membership predicate in an atomic formula (class version of ~ elsb1 ). (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 28-Apr-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.)
theorem clelsb1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2) y1) (wcel (cv y1) A2) $;

--| Substitution for the second argument of the membership predicate in an atomic formula (class version of ~ elsb2 ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Nov-2018.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem clelsb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1):
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) y1) (wcel A2 (cv y1)) $;

--| Establish equality between classes, using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions as in ~ dfcleq . See also ~ cleqf . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Nov-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 . (Revised by BJ, 30-Nov-2020.)
theorem cleqh {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) B2)) $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. See ~ hbxfrbi for equivalence version. (Contributed by NM, 21-Aug-2007.)
theorem hbxfreq {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Change the free variable of a hypothesis builder. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ hblemg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem hblem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 z):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) A2)) $;

--| Change the free variable of a hypothesis builder. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ hblem for a version with more disjoint variable conditions, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hblemg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 z):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) A2)) $;

--| Deduction from a wff to a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.)
theorem eqabdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cab x3 ps)) $;

--| Deduction from a wff to a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqabcdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cab x3 ps) A2) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 6-May-2023.)
theorem eqabi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab x3 ph) $;

--| Every class is equal to a class abstraction (the class of sets belonging to it). Theorem 5.2 of [Quine] p. 35. This is a generalization to classes of ~ cvjust . The proof does not rely on ~ cvjust , so ~ cvjust could be proved as a special instance of it. Note however that ~ abid1 necessarily relies on ~ df-clel , whereas ~ cvjust does not. This theorem requires ~ ax-ext , ~ df-clab , ~ df-cleq , ~ df-clel , but to prove that any specific class term not containing class variables is a setvar or is equal to a class abstraction does not require these $a-statements. This last fact is a metatheorem, consequence of the fact that the only $a-statements with typecode ` class ` are ~ cv , ~ cab , and statements corresponding to defined class constructors. Note on the simultaneous presence in set.mm of this ~ abid1 and its commuted form ~ abid2 : It is rare that two forms so closely related both appear in set.mm. Indeed, such equalities are generally used in later proofs as parts of transitive inferences, and with the many variants of ~ eqtri (search for *eqtr*), it would be rare that either one would shorten a proof compared to the other. There is typically a choice between what we call a "definitional form", where the shorter expression is on the LHS (left-hand side), and a "computational form", where the shorter expression is on the RHS (right-hand side). An example is ~ df-2 versus ~ 1p1e2 . We do not need ~ 1p1e2 , but because it occurs "naturally" in computations, it can be useful to have it directly, together with a uniform set of 1-digit operations like ~ 1p2e3 , etc. In most cases, we do not need both a definitional and a computational forms. A definitional form would favor consistency with genuine definitions, while a computational form is often more natural. The situation is similar with biconditionals in propositional calculus: see for instance ~ pm4.24 and ~ anidm , while other biconditionals generally appear in a single form (either definitional, but more often computational). In the present case, the equality is important enough that both ~ abid1 and ~ abid2 are in set.mm. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-1993.) (Revised by BJ, 10-Nov-2020.)
theorem abid1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| A simplification of class abstraction. Commuted form of ~ abid1 . See comments there. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-1993.)
theorem abid2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) A2 $;

--| One direction of ~ eqabb is provable from fewer axioms. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 13-Feb-2025.)
theorem eqab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)) (wceq A2 (cab x3 ph)) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction (also called a class builder). Theorem 5.1 of [Quine] p. 34. This theorem shows the relationship between expressions with class abstractions and expressions with class variables. Note that ~ abbib and its relatives are among those useful for converting theorems with class variables to equivalent theorems with wff variables, by first substituting a class abstraction for each class variable. Class variables can always be eliminated from a theorem to result in an equivalent theorem with wff variables, and vice-versa. The idea is roughly as follows. To convert a theorem with a wff variable ` ph ` (that has a free variable ` x ` ) to a theorem with a class variable ` A ` , we substitute ` x e. A ` for ` ph ` throughout and simplify, where ` A ` is a new class variable not already in the wff. An example is the conversion of ~ sepgi to ~ inex1 (look at the instance of ~ sepgi that occurs in the proof of ~ inex1 ). Conversely, to convert a theorem with a class variable ` A ` to one with ` ph ` , we substitute ` { x | ph } ` for ` A ` throughout and simplify, where ` x ` and ` ph ` are new setvar and wff variables not already in the wff. Examples include ~ dfsymdif2 and ~ cp ; the latter derives a formula containing wff variables from substitution instances of the class variables in its equivalent formulation ~ cplem2 . For more information on class variables, see Quine pp. 15-21 and/or Takeuti and Zaring pp. 10-13. Usage of ~ eqabbw is preferred since it requires fewer axioms. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Feb-2025.)
theorem eqabb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cab x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction. Commuted form of ~ eqabb . (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-1993.)
theorem eqabcb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cab x3 ph) A2) (wal x3 (wb ph (wcel (cv x3) A2))) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction (deduction form of ~ eqabb ). (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-1995.)
theorem eqabrd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cab x3 ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqabri {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cab x3 ph) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction (inference form). (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Nov-2019.)
theorem eqabcri {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cab x3 ph) A2 $ >
  $ wb ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $;

--| Membership of a class variable in a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 23-Dec-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-11 , see ~ sbc5ALT for more details. (Revised by SN, 2-Sep-2024.)
theorem clelab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) ph)) $;

--| Membership of a class abstraction in another class. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jan-2006.)
theorem clabel {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (wcel (cab x3 ph) A2) (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)))) $;

--| The right-hand side of the second equality is a way of representing proper substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` into a class variable. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.)
theorem sbab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 (cab z (wsb x3 (wcel (cv z) A2) y1))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include the not-free predicate for classes.
term wnfc {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3): wff;

--| Justification theorem for ~ df-nfc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfcjust {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (wal y1 (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wal z (wnf x3 (wcel (cv z) A2))) $;

--| Define the not-free predicate for classes. This is read " ` x ` is not free in ` A ` ". Not-free means that the value of ` x ` cannot affect the value of ` A ` , e.g., any occurrence of ` x ` in ` A ` is effectively bound by a "for all" or something that expands to one (such as "there exists"). It is defined in terms of the not-free predicate ~ df-nf for wffs; see that definition for more information. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
axiom df_nfc {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (wnfc x3 A2) (wal y1 (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2))) $;

--| Deduce that a class ` A ` does not have ` x ` free in it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfci {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2) $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $;

--| Deduce that a class ` A ` does not have ` x ` free in it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcii {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $;

--| Consequence of the not-free predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Drop ~ ax-12 but use ~ ax-8 , ~ df-clel , and avoid a DV condition on ` y ` , ` A ` . (Revised by SN, 3-Jun-2024.)
theorem nfcr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wnfc x3 A2) (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ nfcr . Avoids ~ ax-8 but uses ~ ax-12 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem nfcrALT {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wnfc x3 A2) (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Consequence of the not-free predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 . (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-12 (adopting Wolf Lammen's 13-May-2023 proof). (Revised by SN, 3-Jun-2024.)
theorem nfcri {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2) $;

--| Deduce that a class ` A ` does not have ` x ` free in it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $;

--| Consequence of the not-free predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcrd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Consequence of the not-free predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 . (Revised by GG, 23-May-2024.)
theorem nfcrii {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| An equality theorem for effectively not free. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-8 and ~ df-clel . (Revised by WL and SN, 23-Aug-2024.)
theorem nfceqdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wnfc x3 A2) (wnfc x3 B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for class not-free. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-12 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 19-Jun-2023.)
theorem nfceqi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wnfc x3 A2) (wnfc x3 B2) $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcxfr {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $;

--| A utility lemma to transfer a bound-variable hypothesis builder into a definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcxfrd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $;

--| If ` x ` is disjoint from ` A ` , then ` x ` is not free in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfcv {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $;

--| If ` x ` is disjoint from ` A ` , then ` x ` is not free in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfcvd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfab1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wnfc x3 (cab x3 ph) $;

--| The setvar ` x ` is bound in ` F/_ x A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.)
theorem nfnfc1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wnfc x3 A2) $;

--| Substitution for the first argument of the membership predicate in an atomic formula (class version of ~ elsb1 ). Version of ~ clelsb1f with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 28-Apr-2010.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.)
theorem clelsb1fw {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2) y1) (wcel (cv y1) A2) $;

--| Substitution for the first argument of the membership predicate in an atomic formula (class version of ~ elsb1 ). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ clelsb1fw not requiring ~ ax-13 , but extra disjoint variables. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 28-Apr-2010.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Mar-2017.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-May-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem clelsb1f {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2) y1) (wcel (cv y1) A2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfabg for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cab y1 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfab for a version with more disjoint variable conditions, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfabg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cab y1 ph) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2016.) Add disjoint variable condition to avoid ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfaba1g for a less restrictive version requiring more axioms. (Revised by GG, 20-Jan-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-12 . (Revised by SN, 14-May-2025.)
theorem nfaba1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 (cab y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfaba1 for a version with a disjoint variable condition, but not requiring ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfaba1g {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 (cab y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for equality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfeqd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for elementhood. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfeld {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 (wcel A2 B2)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for ` F/_ y A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-Dec-2019.)
theorem nfnfc {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wnfc y1 A2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for equality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.)
theorem nfeq {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for elementhood. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2019.)
theorem nfel {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel A2 B2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for equality, special case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfeq1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for elementhood, special case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfel1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel A2 B2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for equality, special case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfeq2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for elementhood, special case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfel2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wcel A2 B2) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-8 , ~ ax-11 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drnfc1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wnfc x3 A2) (wnfc y1 B2)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for use with the Distinctor Reduction Theorem. Proof revision is marked as discouraged because the minimizer replaces ~ albidv with ~ dral2 , leading to a one byte longer proof. However feel free to manually edit it according to conventions. (TODO: ~ dral2 depends on ~ ax-13 , hence its usage during minimizing is discouraged. Check in the long run whether this is a permanent restriction). Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-8 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Sep-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drnfc2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wnfc z A2) (wnfc z B2)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Version of ~ nfabd with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 10-Jan-2024.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem nfabdw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cab y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfabdw when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-9 and ~ ax-ext . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 23-May-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfabd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cab y1 ps)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a class abstraction. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfabd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wnf x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cab y1 ps)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ dvelimc . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimdc {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnfc x3 B2)) $;

--| Version of ~ dvelim for classes. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dvelimc {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc z B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnfc x3 B2) $;

--| If ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct, then ` x ` is not free in ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfcv for a version that replaces the distinctor with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-ext . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfcvf {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnfc x3 (cv y1)) $;

--| If ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct, then ` x ` is not free in ` y ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfcv for a version that replaces the distinctor with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-ext . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfcvf_b {x3: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wnfc x3 (cv x3)) $;

--| If ` x ` and ` y ` are distinct, then ` y ` is not free in ` x ` . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . See ~ nfcv for a version that replaces the distinctor with a disjoint variable condition, requiring fewer axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfcvf2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wnfc y1 (cv x3)) $;

--| Establish equality between classes, using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions as in ~ dfcleq . See also ~ cleqh . (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-10 . (Revised by GG, 20-Aug-2023.)
theorem cleqf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Equality of a class variable and a class abstraction. In this version, the fact that ` x ` is a nonfree variable in ` A ` is explicitly stated as a hypothesis. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-May-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2023.)
theorem eqabf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cab x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)) $;

--| A simplification of class abstraction. Theorem 5.2 of [Quine] p. 35. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Feb-2025.)
theorem abid2f {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) A2 $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ abid2f as of 26-Feb-2025. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem abid2fOLD {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) A2 $;

--| Theorem to move a substitution in and out of a class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem sbabel {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wsb x3 (wcel (cab z ph) A2) y1) (wcel (cab z (wsb x3 ph y1)) A2) $;

--| Extend wff notation to include inequality.
term wne (A2 B2: class): wff;

--| Define inequality. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1993.)
axiom df_ne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wne A2 B2) (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ df-ne . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Jul-2018.)
theorem neii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wne A2 B2 $ >
  $ wn (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ df-ne . (Contributed by BJ, 7-Jul-2018.)
theorem neir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wne A2 B2 $;

--| Negation of inequality. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem nne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wn (wne A2 B2)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Deduction eliminating inequality definition. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem neneqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| From inequality to non-equality. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem neneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| If it is not the case that two classes are equal, then they are unequal. Converse of ~ neneqd . One-way deduction form of ~ df-ne . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.) Allow a shortening of ~ necon3bi . (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem neqned (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $;

--| From non-equality to inequality. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem neqne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wceq A2 B2)) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| No class is unequal to itself. Inequality is irreflexive. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Jan-2015.)
theorem neirr (A2: class):
  $ wn (wne A2 A2) $;

--| Excluded middle with equality and inequality. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2019.)
theorem exmidne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wo (wceq A2 B2) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| A contradiction concerning equality implies anything. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-Jan-2018.)
theorem eqneqall (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wi (wne A2 B2) ph) $;

--| Law of noncontradiction with equality and inequality. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Dec-2019.)
theorem nonconne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive law deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon3ad (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) (wn ps)) $;

--| Contrapositive law deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem necon3bd (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon2ad (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2007.)
theorem necon2bd (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wne A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wn ps)) $;

--| Contrapositive deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon1ad (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) ps) $;

--| Contrapositive deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon1bd (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon4ad (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon4bd (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wne A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) ps) $;

--| Contrapositive law deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-2006.)
theorem necon3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wne C D) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive law deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2008.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem necon1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) (wceq C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wne C D) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2008.)
theorem necon2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wne C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq C D) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem necon4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wi (wne A2 B2) (wne C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq C D) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 23-May-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem necon3ai (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) (wn ph) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon3bi (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon1ai (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) ph $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon1bi (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon2ai (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wn ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2007.)
theorem necon2bi (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wn ph) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon4ai (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) (wn ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon3i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wne C D) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.)
theorem necon1i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wne C D) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.)
theorem necon2i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wne C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq C D) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| Contrapositive inference for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon4i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 B2) (wne C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq C D) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Deduction from equality to inequality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2007.)
theorem necon3abid (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 B2) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wne A2 B2) (wn ps)) $;

--| Deduction from equality to inequality. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-2007.)
theorem necon3bbid (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Contrapositive deduction for inequality. (Contributed by NM, 21-Aug-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2019.)
theorem necon1abid (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
