  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) G) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F) G) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv x3) (cv z) G) F) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 F) C G) (co (co A2 C G) (co B2 C G) F) $;

--| Lemma used by real number construction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.)
theorem caovdilem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F G H: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) G) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F) G) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv x3) (cv z) G) F) $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (cv z) G) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) G) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (co A2 C G) (co B2 D G) F) H G) (co (co A2 (co C H G) G) (co B2 (co D H G) G) F) $;

--| Lemma used in real number construction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.)
theorem caovlem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C D R F G H: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) G) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F) G) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv x3) (cv z) G) F) $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (cv z) G) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) G) $ >
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) F) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (cv z) F) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F) F) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (co (co A2 C G) (co B2 D G) F) H G) (co (co (co A2 D G) (co B2 C G) F) R G) F) (co (co A2 (co (co C H G) (co D R G) F) G) (co B2 (co (co C R G) (co D H G) F) G) F) $;

--| Uniqueness of inverse element in commutative, associative operation with identity. Remark in proof of Proposition 9-2.4 of [Gleason] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1996.)
theorem caovmo {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wcel B2 S $ >
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wn (wcel c0 S) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) F) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (cv z) F) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F) F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) S) (wceq (co (cv x3) B2 F) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wmo w (wceq (co A2 (cv w) F) B2) $;

--| Substitute an operation value into an existential quantifier over an image. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem imaeqexov {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1 z) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wss (cxp B2 C) A2)) (wb (wrex x3 ph (cima F (cxp B2 C))) (wrex y1 (wrex z ps C) B2)) $;

--| Substitute an operation value into a universal quantifier over an image. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem imaeqalov {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1 z) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) F)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wss (cxp B2 C) A2)) (wb (wral x3 ph (cima F (cxp B2 C))) (wral y1 (wral z ps C) B2)) $;

--| The value of an operation given by a maps-to rule is the empty set if the arguments are not contained in the base sets of the rule. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpondm0 {x3 y1: setvar} (C F V W: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y C) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y))) (wceq (co V W F) c0) $;

--| If a two-parameter class is not empty, constrain the implicit pair. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem elmpocl {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C S T F X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co S T F)) (wa (wcel S A2) (wcel T B2)) $;

--| If a two-parameter class is not empty, the first argument is in its nominal domain. (Contributed by FL, 15-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem elmpocl1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C S T F X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co S T F)) (wcel S A2) $;

--| If a two-parameter class is not empty, the second argument is in its nominal domain. (Contributed by FL, 15-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem elmpocl2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C S T F X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co S T F)) (wcel T B2) $;

--| Utility lemma for two-parameter classes. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.) Variant of ~ elovmpo in deduction form. (Revised by AV, 20-Apr-2025.)
theorem elovmpod {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class a b) (D: class) (E O V: class a b) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo a b A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv a) X) (wceq (cv b) Y)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel E (co X Y O)) (wcel E D)) $;

--| Utility lemma for two-parameter classes. _EDITORIAL_: can simplify ~ islmhm . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.)
theorem elovmpo {a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class a b) (E F X Y: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo a b A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv a) X) (wceq (cv b) Y)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co X Y D)) (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel F E)) $;

--| Implications for the value of an operation, defined by the maps-to notation with a class abstraction as a result, having an element. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Jul-2018.)
theorem elovmporab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (M: class) (O: class x3 y1 z) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (crab z ph M)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) (wcel M cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Z (co X Y O)) (w3a (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv) (wcel Z M)) $;

--| Implications for the value of an operation, defined by the maps-to notation with a class abstraction as a result, having an element. Here, the base set of the class abstraction depends on the first operand. Version of ~ elovmporab1 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Jul-2018.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 26-Jan-2024.)
theorem elovmporab1w {x3 y1 z m: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z m) (M: class m) (O: class x3 y1 z m) (X Y: class m) (Z: class x3 y1 m):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (crab z ph (csb m (cv x3) M))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) (wcel (csb m X M) cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Z (co X Y O)) (w3a (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv) (wcel Z (csb m X M))) $;

--| Implications for the value of an operation, defined by the maps-to notation with a class abstraction as a result, having an element. Here, the base set of the class abstraction depends on the first operand. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ elovmporab1w when possible. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Jul-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elovmporab1 {x3 y1 z m: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z m) (M: class m) (O: class x3 y1 z m) (X Y: class m) (Z: class x3 y1 m):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (crab z ph (csb m (cv x3) M))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) (wcel (csb m X M) cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Z (co X Y O)) (w3a (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv) (wcel Z (csb m X M))) $;

--| If the operation value of the operation value of two nested maps-to notation is not empty, all involved arguments belong to the corresponding base classes of the maps-to notations. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2021.)
theorem _2mpo0 {x3 y1 t s1: setvar} (A2 B2: class t s1) (C D: class x3 y1) (S T E F O X Y: class x3 y1 t s1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 E) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y O) (cmpo s1 t C D F)) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel S C) (wcel T D)))) (wceq (co S T (co X Y O)) c0) $;

--| Any function to sets of ordered pairs produces a relation on function value unconditionally. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem relmptopab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class y1 z) (B2 F: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (copab y1 z ph)) $ >
  $ wrel (cfv B2 F) $;

--| Describe an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem f1ocnvd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (F W X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) C)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 B2 D))) $;

--| Describe an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem f1od {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (F W X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) C)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $;

--| Describe an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem f1ocnv2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wb (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq (cv y1) C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 B2 D))) $;

--| Describe an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem f1o2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wb (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq (cv y1) C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $;

--| A one-to-one mapping induces a one-to-one mapping on power sets. This version of ~ f1opw avoids the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem f1opw2 {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv a)) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima F (cv b)) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cpw A2) (cpw B2) (cmpt b (cpw A2) (cima F (cv b)))) $;

--| A one-to-one mapping induces a one-to-one mapping on power sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem f1opw {b: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wf1o (cpw A2) (cpw B2) (cmpt b (cpw A2) (cima F (cv b)))) $;

--| If the value of a function which is the result of an operation defined by the maps-to notation is not empty, the operands must be sets. Remark: a function which is the result of an operation can be regared as operation with 3 operands - therefore the abbreviation "mpt3" is used in the label. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2019.)
theorem elovmpt3imp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 M O X Y Z: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt z M B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv Z (co X Y O))) (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) $;

--| The value of an operation defined by the maps-to notation with a function into a class abstraction as a result. The domain of the function and the base set of the class abstraction may depend on the operands, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2018.) (Revised by AV, 16-May-2019.)
theorem ovmpt3rab1 {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z a) (U: class z a) (K: class a) (L: class z) (M: class x3 y1 z a) (N: class x3 y1 z) (O: class x3 y1 z a) (V W: class z a) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt z M (crab a ph N))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq M K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq N L) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y W) (wcel K U)) (wceq (co X Y O) (cmpt z K (crab a ps L))) $;

--| If the value of a function which is the result of an operation defined by the maps-to notation is not empty, the operands and the argument of the function must be sets. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2019.)
theorem ovmpt3rabdm {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z a) (T: class x3 y1 a) (U K: class a) (L: class) (M: class x3 y1 z a) (N: class x3 y1 z) (O: class x3 y1 z a) (V W: class a) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt z M (crab a ph N))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq M K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq N L) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y W) (wcel K U)) (wcel L T)) (wceq (cdm (co X Y O)) K) $;

--| Implications for the value of an operation defined by the maps-to notation with a function into a class abstraction as a result having an element. The domain of the function and the base set of the class abstraction may depend on the operands, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2018.) (Revised by AV, 16-May-2019.)
theorem elovmpt3rab1 {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z a) (A2: class x3 y1 z) (T: class x3 y1 a) (U K: class a) (L: class) (M: class x3 y1 z a) (N: class x3 y1 z) (O: class x3 y1 z a) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt z M (crab a ph N))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq M K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wceq N L) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K U) (wcel L T)) (wi (wcel A2 (cfv Z (co X Y O))) (wa (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) (wa (wcel Z K) (wcel A2 L)))) $;

--| Implications for the value of an operation defined by the maps-to notation with a class abstraction as a result having an element. (Contributed by AV, 17-Jul-2018.) (Revised by AV, 16-May-2019.)
theorem elovmpt3rab {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z a) (A2: class x3 y1 z) (T: class x3 y1 a) (U: class a) (M N: class) (O: class x3 y1 z a) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt z M (crab a ph N))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M U) (wcel N T)) (wi (wcel A2 (cfv Z (co X Y O))) (wa (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) (wa (wcel Z M) (wcel A2 N)))) $;

--| Syntax for maps-to notation for functions with three arguments.
term cmpt3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3 y1 z): class;

--| Define maps-to notation for functions with three arguments. See ~ df-mpt and ~ df-mpo for functions with one and two arguments respectively. This definition is analogous to ~ bj-dfmpoa . (Contributed by BJ, 11-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_mpt3 {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) (copab s1 t (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wa (wceq (cv s1) (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (cv t) D)) C) B2) A2)) $;

--| Express a three-argument function as a one-argument function, or vice-versa. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Sep-2026.)
theorem mpt3mpt {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class w) (E: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq D E) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpt w (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) D) (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C E) $;

--| An equality deduction for maps-to notation. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Sep-2026.)
theorem mpt3eqdv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class x3 y1 z) (E: class y1 z) (F: class x3 z) (G: class x3 y1) (H: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) C))) (wceq D H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) (cmpt3 x3 y1 z E F G H)) $;

--| A function in maps-to notation with three arguments is a function. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Sep-2026.)
theorem funmpt3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 y1) (C D: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wfun (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) $;

--| Value of a three-argument function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Sep-2026.)
theorem mpt3fvd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D F V: class x3 y1 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq X (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)))) (wceq Y D) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X F) Y) $;

--| Value of a three-argument function in maps-to notation at an ordered triple. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Sep-2026.)
theorem mpt3fvotd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3 y1 z) (R S T: class) (F V: class x3 y1 z) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cotp R S T) (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)))) (wceq Y D) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cotp R S T) F) Y) $;

--| Value of a three-argument function in maps-to notation at an ordered triple. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Sep-2026.)
theorem mpt3fvot2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3 y1 z) (R S T: class) (F K L: class x3 y1 z) (M: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel R A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq R (cv x3))) (wceq K D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq S (cv y1))) (wceq L K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq T (cv z))) (wceq M L) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt3 x3 y1 z A2 B2 C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cotp R S T) F) M) $;

--| Extend class notation to include mapping of an operation to a function operation.
term cof (R: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include mapping of a binary relation to a function relation.
term cofr (R: class): class;

--| Define the function operation map. The definition is designed so that if ` R ` is a binary operation, then ` oF R ` is the analogous operation on functions which corresponds to applying ` R ` pointwise to the values of the functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_of {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (cof R) (cmpo f2 g2 cvv cvv (cmpt x3 (cin (cdm (cv f2)) (cdm (cv g2))) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) R))) $;

--| Define the function relation map. The definition is designed so that if ` R ` is a binary relation, then ` oR R ` is the analogous relation on functions which is true when each element of the left function relates to the corresponding element of the right function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_ofr {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (cofr R) (copab f2 g2 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) R) (cin (cdm (cv f2)) (cdm (cv g2))))) $;

--| Equality theorem for function operation, deduction form. (Contributed by SN, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem ofeqd (ph: wff) (R S: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cof R) (cof S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofeq (R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cof R) (cof S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofreq (R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cofr R) (cofr S)) $;

--| A function operation restricted to a set is a set. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofexg (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cres (cof R) A2) cvv) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem nfof {x3: setvar} (R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cof R) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for function relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem nfofr {x3: setvar} (R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cofr R) $;

--| Value of a relation applied to two functions. Originally part of ~ ofrfval , this version assumes the functions are sets rather than their domains, avoiding ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by SN, 5-Aug-2024.)
theorem ofrfvalg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D: class x3) (R S F G: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wral x3 (wbr C D R) S)) $;

--| Value of an operation applied to two functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem offval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D: class x3) (R S F G: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpt x3 S (co C D R))) $;

--| Value of a relation applied to two functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofrfval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D: class x3) (R S F G: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wral x3 (wbr C D R) S)) $;

--| Evaluate a function operation at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S F G V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X G) D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X S)) (wceq (cfv X (co F G (cof R))) (co C D R)) $;

--| Exhibit a function relation at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofrval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S F G V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X G) D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wcel X S)) (wbr C D R) $;

--| The function operation produces a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem offn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S F G V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) S $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (co F G (cof R)) S) $;

--| The function operation produces a function. (Contributed by SN, 23-Jul-2024.)
theorem offun (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F G V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (co F G (cof R))) $;

--| The function operation expressed as a mapping. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jun-2017.)
theorem offval2f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3) (R: class) (F G V W X: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C R))) $;

--| Value of a restriction of the function operation map. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2014.)
theorem ofmresval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cres (cof R) (cxp A2 B2))) (co F G (cof R))) $;

--| Function value of a pointwise composition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2015.)
theorem fnfvof (A2 R F G V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G A2)) (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel X A2))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G (cof R))) (co (cfv X F) (cfv X G) R)) $;

--| The function operation produces a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem off {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3 y1) (R S T U F: class) (G: class x3) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) T))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 T G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) C $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C U (co F G (cof R))) $;

--| Restrict the operands of a function operation to the same domain as that of the operation itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem ofres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R F G V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) C $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (co (cres F C) (cres G C) (cof R))) $;

--| The function operation expressed as a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem offval2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (R: class) (F G V W X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C R))) $;

--| The function relation acting on maps. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofrfval2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (R: class) (F G V W X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C R) A2)) $;

--| The function operation expressed as a mapping with function values. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
theorem offvalfv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R F G: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) R))) $;

--| Value of a pointwise operation on two functions defined using maps-to notation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Oct-2014.)
theorem ofmpteq {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (R: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wfn (cmpt x3 A2 B2) A2) (wfn (cmpt x3 A2 C) A2)) (wceq (co (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (cmpt x3 A2 C) (cof R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C R))) $;

--| The composition of a _homomorphism_ with a function operation. (Contributed by SN, 20-May-2025.)
theorem coof {b c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class b c) (B2 R S F G H: class) (V: class b c):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) B2))) (wcel (co (cv b) (cv c) R) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) B2))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv b) (cv c) R) H) (co (cfv (cv b) H) (cfv (cv c) H) S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom H (co F G (cof R))) (co (ccom H F) (ccom H G) (cof S))) $;

--| The composition of a function operation with another function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.)
theorem ofco (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R F G H V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D C H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) C $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom (co F G (cof R)) H) (co (ccom F H) (ccom G H) (cof R))) $;

--| Convert an identity of the operation to the analogous identity on the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem offveq {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (R F G H: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (co B2 C R) (cfv (cv x3) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) H) $;

--| Equivalent expressions for equality with a function operation. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.)
theorem offveqb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (R F G H: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq H (co F G (cof R))) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) H) (co B2 C R)) A2)) $;

--| Left operation by a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofc1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R F V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) F (cof R))) (co B2 C R)) $;

--| Right operation by a constant. (Contributed by NM, 7-Oct-2014.)
theorem ofc2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R F V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X (co F (cxp A2 (csn B2)) (cof R))) (co C B2 R)) $;

--| Function operation on two constant functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofc12 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) (cxp A2 (csn C)) (cof R)) (cxp A2 (csn (co B2 C R)))) $;

--| Transfer a reflexive law to the function relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofref {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R S F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F F (cofr R)) $;

--| Transfer a left inverse law to the function operation. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2014.)
theorem caofinvl {x3 v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 R: class v) (S F: class) (G: class v) (N: class) (V W: class x3 v):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S S N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt v A2 (cfv (cfv (cv v) F) N))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cv x3) R) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (cof R)) (cxp A2 (csn B2))) $;

--| Transfer a left identity law to the function operation. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2014.)
theorem caofid0l {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 R S F: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co B2 (cv x3) R) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) F (cof R)) F) $;

--| Transfer a right identity law to the function operation. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2014.)
theorem caofid0r {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 R S F: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co (cv x3) B2 R) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (cxp A2 (csn B2)) (cof R)) F) $;

--| Transfer a right absorption law to the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofid1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C R S F: class) (V W X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co (cv x3) B2 R) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (cxp A2 (csn B2)) (cof R)) (cxp A2 (csn C))) $;

--| Transfer a right absorption law to the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofid2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C R S F: class) (V W X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co B2 (cv x3) R) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) F (cof R)) (cxp A2 (csn C))) $;

--| Transfer a commutative law to the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofcom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (R S F G: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (co G F (cof R))) $;

--| Transfer a cancellation/identity law to the function operation. (Contributed by SN, 16-Oct-2025.)
theorem caofidlcan {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (R S F G: class) (V: class x3 y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co F G (cof R)) G) (wceq F (cxp A2 (csn c_0)))) $;

--| Transfer a relation subset law to the function relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofrss {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (R S T F G: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wbr F G (cofr T))) $;

--| Transfer an associative law to the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofass {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1 z) (P R S T F G H O: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (cv z) T) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) P) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co F G (cof R)) H (cof T)) (co F (co G H (cof P)) (cof O))) $;

--| Transfer a transitivity law to the function relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem caoftrn {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1 z) (R S T U F G H: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) T)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr F G (cofr R)) (wbr G H (cofr T))) (wbr F H (cofr U))) $;

--| Transfer a distributive law to the function operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem caofdi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S T F G H K O: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 K F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) R) T) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) T) (co (cv x3) (cv z) T) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (co G H (cof R)) (cof T)) (co (co F G (cof T)) (co F H (cof T)) (cof O))) $;

--| Transfer a reverse distributive law to the function operation. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2014.)
theorem caofdir {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S T F G H K O: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 K F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) K))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (cv z) T) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) T) (co (cv y1) (cv z) T) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co G H (cof R)) F (cof T)) (co (co G F (cof T)) (co H F (cof T)) (cof O))) $;

--| Transfer ~ nncan -shaped laws to vectors of numbers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem caonncan {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (S: class) (I: class x3 y1) (M: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I S A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I S B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) M) M) (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co A2 B2 (cof M)) (cof M)) B2) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the reified proper subset relation.
term crpss: class;

--| Define a relation which corresponds to proper subsethood ~ df-pss on sets. This allows to use proper subsethood with general concepts that require relations, such as strict ordering, see ~ sorpss . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_rpss {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq crpss (copab x3 y1 (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| The proper subset relation is a relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem relrpss:
  $ wrel crpss $;

--| The proper subset relation on sets is the same as class proper subsethood. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem brrpssg (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wbr A2 B2 crpss) (wpss A2 B2)) $;

--| The proper subset relation on sets is the same as class proper subsethood. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem brrpss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 crpss) (wpss A2 B2) $;

--| Every class is partially ordered by proper subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem porpss (A2: class):
  $ wpo A2 crpss $;

--| Express strict ordering under proper subsets, i.e. the notion of a chain of sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem sorpss {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wor A2 crpss) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wss (cv y1) (cv x3))) A2) A2) $;

--| Property of a chain of sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem sorpssi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 crpss) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2))) (wo (wss B2 C) (wss C B2)) $;

--| A chain of sets is closed under binary union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem sorpssun (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 crpss) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2))) (wcel (cun B2 C) A2) $;

--| A chain of sets is closed under binary intersection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem sorpssin (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 crpss) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2))) (wcel (cin B2 C) A2) $;

--| In a chain of sets, a maximal element is the union of the chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem sorpssuni {v u: setvar} (Y: class):
  $ wi (wor Y crpss) (wb (wrex u (wral v (wn (wpss (cv u) (cv v))) Y) Y) (wcel (cuni Y) Y)) $;

--| In a chain of sets, a minimal element is the intersection of the chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem sorpssint {v u: setvar} (Y: class):
  $ wi (wor Y crpss) (wb (wrex u (wral v (wn (wpss (cv v) (cv u))) Y) Y) (wcel (cint Y) Y)) $;

--| The componentwise complement of a chain of sets is also a chain of sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem sorpsscmpl {u: setvar} (A2 Y: class):
  $ wi (wor Y crpss) (wor (crab u (wcel (cdif A2 (cv u)) Y) (cpw A2)) crpss) $;

--| Axiom of Union. An axiom of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory. It states that a set ` y ` exists that includes the union of a given set ` x ` i.e. the collection of all members of the members of ` x ` . The variant ~ axun2 states that the union itself exists. A version with the standard abbreviation for union is ~ uniex2 . A version using class notation is ~ uniex . The union of a class ~ df-uni should not be confused with the union of two classes ~ df-un . Their relationship is shown in ~ unipr . (Contributed by NM, 23-Dec-1993.)
axiom ax_un {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Axiom of Union expressed with the fewest number of different variables. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2003.) Use ~ ax-un instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfun {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| A variant of the Axiom of Union ~ ax-un . For any set ` x ` , there exists a set ` y ` whose members are exactly the members of the members of ` x ` i.e. the union of ` x ` . Axiom Union of [BellMachover] p. 466. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2006.)
theorem axun2 {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Union using the standard abbreviation for union. Given any set ` x ` , its union ` y ` exists. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2006.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 14-Jul-2026.)
theorem uniex2 {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cuni (cv x3))) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ uniex2 as of 14-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2006.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem uniex2OLD {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cuni (cv x3))) $;

--| The union of a setvar is a set. (Contributed by BJ, 3-May-2021.) (Revised by BJ, 6-Apr-2024.)
theorem vuniex (x3: setvar):
  $ wcel (cuni (cv x3)) cvv $;

--| The ZF Axiom of Union in class notation, in the form of a theorem instead of an inference. We use the antecedent ` A e. V ` instead of ` A e. _V ` to make the theorem more general and thus shorten some proofs; obviously the universal class constant ` _V ` is one possible substitution for class variable ` V ` . (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-1994.)
theorem uniexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cuni A2) cvv) $;

--| The Axiom of Union in class notation. This says that if ` A ` is a set i.e. ` A e. _V ` (see ~ isset ), then the union of ` A ` is also a set. Same as Axiom 3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 16. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1993.)
theorem uniex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cuni A2) cvv $;

--| Deduction version of the ZF Axiom of Union in class notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem uniexd (ph: wff) (A2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) cvv) $;

--| The union of two sets is a set. Corollary 5.8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 16. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.) Prove ~ unexg first and then ~ unex and ~ unexb from it. (Revised by BJ, 21-Jul-2025.)
theorem unexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The union of two sets is a set. Corollary 5.8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 16. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jul-1994.)
theorem unex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv $;

--| An unordered triple of classes exists. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1994.)
theorem tpex (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel (ctp A2 B2 C) cvv $;

--| Existence of union is equivalent to existence of its components. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jun-1998.)
theorem unexb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two sets is a set. Proposition 6.2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 23. See also ~ xpexgALT . (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.)
theorem xpexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two sets is a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem xpexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The Cartesian product of three sets is a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-Feb-2018.)
theorem _3xpexg (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wcel (cxp (cxp V V) V) cvv) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two sets is a set. Proposition 6.2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 23. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.)
theorem xpex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv $;

--| The union of two sets is a set. (Contributed by SN, 16-Jul-2024.)
theorem unexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The Cartesian square of a set is a set. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
theorem sqxpexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cxp A2 A2) cvv) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a class abstraction to be a proper class. The class ` F ` can be thought of as an expression in ` x ` and the abstraction appearing in the statement as the class of values ` F ` as ` x ` varies through ` A ` . Assuming the antecedents, if that class is a set, then so is the "domain" ` A ` . The converse holds without antecedent, see ~ abrexexg . Note that the second antecedent ` A. x e. A x e. F ` cannot be translated to ` A C_ F ` since ` F ` may depend on ` x ` . In applications, one may take ` F = { x } ` or ` F = ~P x ` (see ~ snnex and ~ pwnex respectively, proved from ~ abnex , which is a consequence of ~ abnexg with ` A = _V ` ). (Contributed by BJ, 2-Dec-2021.)
theorem abnexg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wa (wcel F V) (wcel (cv x3) F)) A2) (wi (wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) F) A2)) W) (wcel A2 cvv)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a class abstraction to be a proper class. Lemma for ~ snnex and ~ pwnex . See the comment of ~ abnexg . (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2021.)
theorem abnex {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel F V) (wcel (cv x3) F))) (wn (wcel (cab y1 (wex x3 (wceq (cv y1) F))) cvv)) $;

--| The class of all singletons is a proper class. See also ~ pwnex . (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2008.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 7-Dec-2008.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 5-Dec-2021.)
theorem snnex {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csn (cv y1))))) cvv $;

--| The class of all power sets is a proper class. See also ~ snnex . (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2021.)
theorem pwnex {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cpw (cv y1))))) cvv $;

--| If the subtrahend of a class difference exists, then the minuend exists iff the difference exists. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-2003.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.)
theorem difex2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) cvv)) $;

--| If the difference of a class and a singleton is a set, the class itself is a set. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem difsnexi (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cdif N (csn K)) cvv) (wcel N cvv) $;

--| Expression for double union that moves union into a class abstraction. (Contributed by FL, 28-May-2007.)
theorem uniuni {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (cuni A2)) (cuni (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cuni (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) A2))))) $;

--| Converse of the Axiom of Union. Note that it does not require ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem uniexr (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cuni A2) V) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The Axiom of Union and its converse. A class is a set iff its union is a set. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem uniexb (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel (cuni A2) cvv) $;

--| Converse of the Axiom of Power Sets. Note that it does not require ~ ax-pow . (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem pwexr (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cpw A2) V) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The Axiom of Power Sets and its converse. A class is a set iff its power class is a set. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem pwexb (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel (cpw A2) cvv) $;

--| A class belongs to a double power class if and only if its union belongs to the power class. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem elpwpwel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cpw (cpw B2))) (wcel (cuni A2) (cpw B2)) $;

--| Membership in a power class difference. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2007.)
theorem eldifpw (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cpw B2)) (wn (wss C B2))) (wcel (cun A2 C) (cdif (cpw (cun B2 C)) (cpw B2))) $;

--| Membership in the power class of a union. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2007.)
theorem elpwun (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cpw (cun B2 C))) (wcel (cdif A2 C) (cpw B2)) $;

--| Power classes are closed under union. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2024.)
theorem pwuncl (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cpw X)) (wcel B2 (cpw X))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cpw X)) $;

--| An indexed union of a power class in terms of the power class of the union of its index. Part of Exercise 24(b) of [Enderton] p. 33. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2003.)
theorem iunpw {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cuni A2)) A2) (wceq (cpw (cuni A2)) (ciun x3 A2 (cpw (cv x3)))) $;

--| A well-founded relation has no 3-cycle loops. Special case of Proposition 6.23 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 30. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2015.)
theorem fr3nr (A2 B2 C D R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wn (w3a (wbr B2 C R) (wbr C D R) (wbr D B2 R))) $;

--| A well-founded class contains no 3-cycle loops. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2015.)
theorem epne3 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 cep) (w3a (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wn (w3a (wcel B2 C) (wcel C D) (wcel D B2))) $;

--| Alternate definition of well-ordering. Definition 6.24(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 30. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-1997.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.)
theorem dfwe2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wwe A2 R) (wa (wfr A2 R) (wral x3 (wral y1 (w3o (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2)) $;

--| The membership relation well-orders the class of ordinal numbers. This proof does not require the axiom of regularity. Proposition 4.8(g) of [Mendelson] p. 244. For a shorter proof requiring ~ ax-un , see ~ epweonALT . (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2003.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Nov-2024.)
theorem epweon:
  $ wwe con0 cep $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ epweon , shorter but requiring ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem epweonALT:
  $ wwe con0 cep $;

--| The class of all ordinal numbers is ordinal. Proposition 7.12 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 38, but without using the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-1994.)
theorem ordon:
  $ word con0 $;

--| No set contains all ordinal numbers. Proposition 7.13 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 38, but without using the Axiom of Regularity. This is also known as the Burali-Forti paradox (remark in [Enderton] p. 194). In 1897, Cesare Burali-Forti noticed that since the "set" of all ordinal numbers is an ordinal class ( ~ ordon ), it must be both an element of the set of all ordinal numbers yet greater than every such element. ZF set theory resolves this paradox by not allowing the class of all ordinal numbers to be a set (so instead it is a proper class). Here we prove the denial of its existence. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.)
theorem onprc:
  $ wn (wcel con0 cvv) $;

--| The union of a class of ordinal numbers is ordinal. Proposition 7.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 40. Lemma 2.7 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.)
theorem ssorduni (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (word (cuni A2)) $;

--| The union of a set of ordinal numbers is an ordinal number. Theorem 9 of [Suppes] p. 132. Lemma 2.7 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2003.)
theorem ssonuni (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wss A2 con0) (wcel (cuni A2) con0)) $;

--| The union of a set of ordinal numbers is an ordinal number. Corollary 7N(d) of [Enderton] p. 193. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2003.)
theorem ssonunii (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wcel (cuni A2) con0) $;

--| A way to express the ordinal property of a class in terms of the class of ordinal numbers. Corollary 7.14 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 38 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jun-2003.)
theorem ordeleqon (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (wo (wcel A2 con0) (wceq A2 con0)) $;

--| Any ordinal class is a subclass of the class of ordinal numbers. Corollary 7.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 38. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.)
theorem ordsson (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wss A2 con0) $;

--| A class is ordinal iff it is a subclass of ` On ` and transitive. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem dford5 (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (wa (wss A2 con0) (wtr A2)) $;

--| An ordinal number is a subset of the class of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jun-1994.)
theorem onss (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wss A2 con0) $;

--| The predecessor of an ordinal under ` _E ` and ` On ` is itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Oct-2024.)
theorem predon (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cpred con0 cep A2) A2) $;

--| Two ways of saying a class of ordinals is unbounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem ssonprc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wb (wnel A2 cvv) (wceq (cuni A2) con0)) $;

--| The union of an ordinal number is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem onuni (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (cuni A2) con0) $;

--| The union of an ordinal class is ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2003.)
theorem orduni (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (word (cuni A2)) $;

--| The intersection (infimum) of a nonempty class of ordinal numbers belongs to the class. Compare Exercise 4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 45. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-1997.)
theorem onint (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) A2) $;

--| The intersection of a class of ordinal numbers is zero iff the class contains zero. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.)
theorem onint0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wb (wceq (cint A2) c0) (wcel c0 A2)) $;

--| A nonempty class of ordinal numbers has the smallest member. Exercise 9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 40. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2003.)
theorem onssmin {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2) $;

--| If a property is true for some ordinal number, it is true for a minimal ordinal number. This version uses explicit substitution. Theorem Schema 62 of [Suppes] p. 228. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2003.)
theorem onminesb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wrex x3 ph con0) (wsbc x3 ph (cint (crab x3 ph con0))) $;

--| If a property is true for some ordinal number, it is true for a minimal ordinal number. This version uses implicit substitution. Theorem Schema 62 of [Suppes] p. 228. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2003.)
theorem onminsb {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cint (crab x3 ph con0))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wrex x3 ph con0) ps $;

--| The intersection of a nonempty collection of ordinal numbers is an ordinal number. Compare Exercise 6 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 44. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1997.)
theorem oninton (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) con0) $;

--| The intersection of a class of ordinal numbers exists iff it is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2003.)
theorem onintrab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wcel (cint (crab x3 ph con0)) cvv) (wcel (cint (crab x3 ph con0)) con0) $;

--| An existence condition equivalent to an intersection's being an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2003.)
theorem onintrab2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph con0) (wcel (cint (crab x3 ph con0)) con0) $;

--| No member of a set of ordinal numbers belongs to its minimum. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-1997.)
theorem onnmin (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wcel B2 A2)) (wn (wcel B2 (cint A2))) $;

--| An ordinal number smaller than the minimum of a set of ordinal numbers does not have the property determining that set. ` ps ` is the wff resulting from the substitution of ` A ` for ` x ` in wff ` ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.)
theorem onnminsb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wi (wcel A2 (cint (crab x3 ph con0))) (wn ps)) $;

--| A way to show that an ordinal number equals the minimum of a nonempty collection of ordinal numbers: it must be in the collection, and it must not be larger than any member of the collection. (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2003.)
theorem oneqmin {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 con0) (wne B2 c0)) (wb (wceq A2 (cint B2)) (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) B2)) A2))) $;

--| The union of a set of ordinals is equal to the intersection of its upper bounds. Problem 2.5(ii) of [BellMachover] p. 471. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2003.)
theorem uniordint {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wceq (cuni A2) (cint (crab x3 (wral y1 (wss (cv y1) (cv x3)) A2) con0))) $;

--| If a wff is true for an ordinal number, then there is the smallest ordinal number for which it is true. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-1997.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Nov-2016.)
theorem onminex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wrex x3 ph con0) (wrex x3 (wa ph (wral y1 (wn ps) (cv x3))) con0) $;

--| The class of all ordinal numbers is its own successor. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2003.)
theorem sucon:
  $ wceq (csuc con0) con0 $;

--| A successor exists iff its class argument exists. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-1998.)
theorem sucexb (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel (csuc A2) cvv) $;

--| The successor of a set is a set (generalization). (Contributed by NM, 5-Jun-1994.)
theorem sucexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (csuc A2) cvv) $;

--| The successor of a set is a set. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.)
theorem sucex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (csuc A2) cvv $;

--| The minimum of a class of ordinal numbers is less than the minimum of that class with its minimum removed. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2003.)
theorem onmindif2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) (cint (cdif A2 (csn (cint A2))))) $;

--| The successor of an ordinal class is an ordinal class. Remark 1.5 of [Schloeder] p. 1. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-1994.) Extract and adapt from a subproof of ~ onsuc . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Jan-2025.)
theorem ordsuci (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (word (csuc A2)) $;

--| If the successor of an ordinal number exists, it is an ordinal number. This variation of ~ onsuc does not require ~ ax-un . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 30-Nov-2024.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Jan-2025.)
theorem sucexeloni (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (csuc A2) V)) (wcel (csuc A2) con0) $;

--| The successor of an ordinal number is an ordinal number. Closed form of ~ onsuci . Forward implication of ~ onsucb . Proposition 7.24 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 41. Remark 1.5 of [Schloeder] p. 1. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by BTernaryTau, 30-Nov-2024.)
theorem onsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (csuc A2) con0) $;

--| A class is ordinal if and only if its successor is ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.)
theorem ordsuc (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (word (csuc A2)) $;

--| The collection of ordinals in the power class of an ordinal is its successor. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2005.)
theorem ordpwsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wceq (cin (cpw A2) con0) (csuc A2)) $;

--| The collection of ordinal numbers in the power set of an ordinal number is its successor. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2004.)
theorem onpwsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cin (cpw A2) con0) (csuc A2)) $;

--| A class is an ordinal number if and only if its successor is an ordinal number. Biconditional form of ~ onsuc . (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2003.)
theorem onsucb (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (csuc A2) con0) $;

--| The successor of an element of an ordinal class is a subset of it. Lemma 1.14 of [Schloeder] p. 2. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1998.)
theorem ordsucss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (word B2) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wss (csuc A2) B2)) $;

--| An ordinal number is a proper subset of its successor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem onpsssuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wpss A2 (csuc A2)) $;

--| A set belongs to an ordinal iff its successor is a subset of the ordinal. Exercise 8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2003.)
theorem ordelsuc (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (word B2)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wss (csuc A2) B2)) $;

--| The successor of an ordinal number is the smallest larger ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2003.)
theorem onsucmin {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (csuc A2) (cint (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) con0))) $;

--| Membership is inherited by successors. Generalization of Exercise 9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-1998.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.)
theorem ordsucelsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (word B2) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (csuc A2) (csuc B2))) $;

--| The subclass relationship between two ordinal classes is inherited by their successors. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.)
theorem ordsucsssuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (word A2) (word B2)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (csuc A2) (csuc B2))) $;

--| Given an element ` A ` of the union of an ordinal ` B ` , ` suc A ` is an element of ` B ` itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Mar-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem ordsucuniel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (word B2) (wb (wcel A2 (cuni B2)) (wcel (csuc A2) B2)) $;

--| The successor of the maximum (i.e. union) of two ordinals is the maximum of their successors. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2003.)
theorem ordsucun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (word A2) (word B2)) (wceq (csuc (cun A2 B2)) (cun (csuc A2) (csuc B2))) $;

--| The maximum of two ordinals is equal to one of them. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordunpr (B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel (cun B2 C) (cpr B2 C)) $;

--| The maximum of two ordinals belongs to a third if each of them do. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordunel (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (word A2) (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cun B2 C) A2) $;

--| A class of ordinal numbers is a subclass of the successor of its union. Similar to Proposition 7.26 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 41. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2003.)
theorem onsucuni (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wss A2 (csuc (cuni A2))) $;

--| An ordinal class is a subclass of the successor of its union. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2003.)
theorem ordsucuni (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wss A2 (csuc (cuni A2))) $;

--| An ordinal class is either its union or the successor of its union. If we adopt the view that zero is a limit ordinal, this means every ordinal class is either a limit or a successor. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2003.)
theorem orduniorsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wo (wceq A2 (cuni A2)) (wceq A2 (csuc (cuni A2)))) $;

--| The class of all ordinal numbers is its own union. Exercise 11 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 40. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-2003.)
theorem unon:
  $ wceq (cuni con0) con0 $;

--| An ordinal class is equal to the union of its successor. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem ordunisuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wceq (cuni (csuc A2)) A2) $;

--| The union of the ordinal subsets of an ordinal number is that number. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2005.)
theorem orduniss2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wceq (cuni (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) A2) con0)) A2) $;

--| A successor ordinal is the successor of its union. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem onsucuni2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wceq A2 (csuc B2))) (wceq (csuc (cuni A2)) A2) $;

--| The successor of an ordinal class contains the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1995.)
theorem _0elsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wcel c0 (csuc A2)) $;

--| The class of ordinal numbers is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-1995.)
theorem limon:
  $ wlim con0 $;

--| An ordinal number is either its own union (if zero or a limit ordinal) or the successor of its union. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jun-1994.) Put in closed form. (Revised by BJ, 11-Jan-2025.)
theorem onuniorsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wo (wceq A2 (cuni A2)) (wceq A2 (csuc (cuni A2)))) $;

--| An ordinal number is a subset of ` On ` . (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1994.)
theorem onssi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wss A2 con0 $;

--| The successor of an ordinal number is an ordinal number. Inference associated with ~ onsuc and ~ onsucb . Corollary 7N(c) of [Enderton] p. 193. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jun-1994.)
theorem onsuci (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wcel (csuc A2) con0 $;

--| An ordinal is equal to its union if and only if it is not the successor of an ordinal. A closed-form generalization of this result is ~ orduninsuc . (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
theorem onuninsuci {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cuni A2)) (wn (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) con0)) $;

--| A set belongs to an ordinal number iff its successor is a subset of the ordinal number. Exercise 8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1995.)
theorem onsucssi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 con0 $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 B2) (wss (csuc A2) B2) $;

--| A successor is not a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem nlimsucg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wn (wlim (csuc A2))) $;

--| An ordinal class is equal to its union if and only if it is not the successor of an ordinal. Closed-form generalization of ~ onuninsuci . (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
theorem orduninsuc {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wceq A2 (cuni A2)) (wn (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) con0))) $;

--| An ordinal equal to its union contains the successor of each of its members. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2005.)
theorem ordunisuc2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wceq A2 (cuni A2)) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2)) $;

--| An ordinal is zero, a successor ordinal, or a limit ordinal. Remark 1.12 of [Schloeder] p. 2. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2003.)
theorem ordzsl {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (w3o (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) con0) (wlim A2)) $;

--| An ordinal number is zero, a successor ordinal, or a limit ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2003.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem onzsl {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 con0) (w3o (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) con0) (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wlim A2))) $;

--| An alternate definition of a limit ordinal, which is any ordinal that is neither zero nor a successor. (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem dflim3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wlim A2) (wa (word A2) (wn (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) con0)))) $;

--| An alternate definition of a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2005.)
theorem dflim4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wlim A2) (w3a (word A2) (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2)) $;

--| The successor of a member of a limit ordinal is also a member. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2003.)
theorem limsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wb (wcel B2 A2) (wcel (csuc B2) A2)) $;

--| A class includes a limit ordinal iff the successor of the class includes it. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem limsssuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss A2 (csuc B2))) $;

--| Two ways to express the class of non-limit ordinal numbers. Part of Definition 7.27 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42, who use the symbol K_I for this class. (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2004.)
theorem nlimon {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (crab x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) con0)) con0) (crab x3 (wn (wlim (cv x3))) con0) $;

--| The union of a nonempty class of limit ordinals is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2005.)
theorem limuni3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wlim (cv x3)) A2)) (wlim (cuni A2)) $;

--| The Principle of Transfinite Induction. Theorem 7.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 39. This principle states that if ` A ` is a class of ordinal numbers with the property that every ordinal number included in ` A ` also belongs to ` A ` , then every ordinal number is in ` A ` . See Theorem ~ tfindes or ~ tfinds for the version involving basis and induction hypotheses. (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
theorem tfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wral x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) A2)) con0)) (wceq A2 con0) $;

--| A closed form of ~ tfis . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem tfisg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wi (wral y1 (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (cv x3)) ph) con0) (wral x3 ph con0) $;

--| Transfinite Induction Schema. If all ordinal numbers less than a given number ` x ` have a property (induction hypothesis), then all ordinal numbers have the property (conclusion). Exercise 25 of [Enderton] p. 200. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Nov-2016.)
theorem tfis {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi (wral y1 (wsb x3 ph y1) (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) ph $;

--| Transfinite Induction Schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1994.)
theorem tfis2f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) ph $;

--| Transfinite Induction Schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1994.)
theorem tfis2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) ph $;

--| Transfinite Induction Schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2003.)
theorem tfis3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) ch $;

--| A transfinite induction scheme in "implicit" form where the induction is done on an object derived from the object of interest. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem tfisi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch th: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (T: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T con0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel R con0) (wss R T)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel S R) ch))) ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq R T) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Principle of Transfinite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last three are the basis, the induction step for successors, and the induction step for limit ordinals. Theorem Schema 4 of [Suppes] p. 197. Theorem 1.19 of [Schloeder] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem tfinds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1) (th: wff x3 y1) (ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wlim (cv x3)) (wi (wral y1 ch (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) ta $;

--| Transfinite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last three are the basis, the induction step for successors, and the induction step for limit ordinals. The basis of this version is an arbitrary ordinal ` B ` instead of zero. Remark in [TakeutiZaring] p. 57. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2004.)
theorem tfindsg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss B2 (cv y1))) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wlim (cv x3)) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss B2 (cv x3))) (wi (wral y1 (wi (wss B2 (cv y1)) ch) (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss B2 A2)) ta $;

--| Transfinite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last three are the basis, the induction step for successors, and the induction step for limit ordinals. The basis of this version is an arbitrary ordinal ` suc B ` instead of zero. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2005.) Remove unnecessary distinct variable conditions. (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.)
theorem tfindsg2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc B2)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wcel B2 (cv y1))) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wlim (cv x3)) (wcel B2 (cv x3))) (wi (wral y1 (wi (wcel B2 (cv y1)) ch) (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 A2)) ta $;

--| Transfinite Induction with explicit substitution. The first hypothesis is the basis, the second is the induction step for successors, and the third is the induction step for limit ordinals. Theorem Schema 4 of [Suppes] p. 197. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2004.)
theorem tfindes {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wsbc x3 ph c0 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph (csuc (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wi (wral x3 ph (cv y1)) (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) ph $;

--| Transfinite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first three hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last three are the basis and the induction hypotheses (for successor and limit ordinals respectively). Theorem Schema 4 of [Suppes] p. 197. The wff ` ta ` is an auxiliary antecedent to help shorten proofs using this theorem. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2004.)
theorem tfinds2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff y1) (ta: wff):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi ta ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wi ta (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wlim (cv x3)) (wi ta (wi (wral y1 ch (cv x3)) ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wi ta ph) $;

--| Principle of Transfinite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last three are the basis, the induction step for successors, and the induction step for limit ordinals. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 21-Jun-2011.)
theorem tfinds3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff y1) (th: wff x3 y1) (ta: wff y1) (et: wff) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi et ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wi et (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wlim (cv x3)) (wi et (wi (wral y1 ch (cv x3)) ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wi et ta) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of natural numbers.
term com: class;

--| Define the class of natural numbers, which are all ordinal numbers that are less than every limit ordinal, i.e., all finite ordinals. Our definition is a variant of the Definition of N of [BellMachover] p. 471. See ~ dfom2 for an alternate definition. Later, when we assume the Axiom of Infinity, we show ` _om ` is a set in ~ omex , and ` _om ` can then be defined per ~ dfom3 (the smallest inductive set) and ~ dfom4 . _Note_: the natural numbers ` _om ` are a subset of the ordinal numbers ~ df-on . Later, when we define complex numbers, we will be able to also define a subset of the complex numbers ( ~ df-nn ) with analogous properties and operations, but they will be different sets. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1994.)
axiom df_om {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (crab x3 (wal y1 (wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)))) con0) $;

--| An alternate definition of the set of natural numbers ` _om ` . Definition 7.28 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42, who use the symbol K_I for the restricted class abstraction of non-limit ordinal numbers (see ~ nlimon ). (Contributed by NM, 1-Nov-2004.)
theorem dfom2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (crab x3 (wss (csuc (cv x3)) (crab y1 (wn (wlim (cv y1))) con0)) con0) $;

--| Membership in omega. The left conjunct can be eliminated if we assume the Axiom of Infinity; see ~ elom3 . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1994.)
theorem elom {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 com) (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wal x3 (wi (wlim (cv x3)) (wcel A2 (cv x3))))) $;

--| Omega is a subset of ` On ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem omsson:
  $ wss com con0 $;

--| The class of natural numbers is a subclass of any (infinite) limit ordinal. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 44. Remarkably, our proof does not require the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem limomss (A2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wss com A2) $;

--| A natural number is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-1994.)
theorem nnon (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel A2 con0) $;

--| A natural number is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-1994.)
theorem nnoni (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel A2 con0 $;

--| A natural number is ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-1995.)
theorem nnord (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (word A2) $;

--| The class of finite ordinals ` _om ` is a transitive class. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem trom:
  $ wtr com $;

--| The class of finite ordinals ` _om ` is ordinal. Theorem 7.32 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. Theorem 1.22 of [Schloeder] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem ordom:
  $ word com $;

--| A member of a natural number is a natural number. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1998.)
theorem elnn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel A2 com) $;

--| The class of natural numbers ` _om ` is either an ordinal number (if we accept the Axiom of Infinity) or the proper class of all ordinal numbers (if we deny the Axiom of Infinity). Remark in [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1998.)
theorem omon:
  $ wo (wcel com con0) (wceq com con0) $;

--| Omega is an ordinal number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2013.)
theorem omelon2:
  $ wi (wcel com cvv) (wcel com con0) $;

--| A natural number is not a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.)
theorem nnlim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wn (wlim A2)) $;

--| The class of natural numbers is a subclass of the class of non-limit ordinal numbers. Exercise 4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 2-Nov-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem omssnlim {x3: setvar}:
  $ wss com (crab x3 (wn (wlim (cv x3))) con0) $;

--| Omega is a limit ordinal. Theorem 2.8 of [BellMachover] p. 473. Theorem 1.23 of [Schloeder] p. 4. Our proof, however, does not require the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem limom:
  $ wlim com $;

--| A class belongs to omega iff its successor does. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-1995.)
theorem peano2b (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 com) (wcel (csuc A2) com) $;

--| A nonzero natural number is a successor. (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
theorem nnsuc {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) com) $;

--| A natural number is not the successor of itself. (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2023.)
theorem omsucne (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wne A2 (csuc A2)) $;

--| An ordinal subclass of non-limit ordinals is a class of natural numbers. Exercise 7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 2-Nov-2004.)
theorem ssnlim {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (word A2) (wss A2 (crab x3 (wn (wlim (cv x3))) con0))) (wss A2 com) $;

--| Strong (or "total") induction principle over the finite ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Oct-2024.)
theorem omsinds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) com) (wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) ch $;

--| The union of two finite ordinals is a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Mar-2025.)
theorem omun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) com) $;

--| Zero is a natural number. One of Peano's five postulates for arithmetic. Proposition 7.30(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. Note: Unlike most textbooks, our proofs of ~ peano1 through ~ peano5 do not use the Axiom of Infinity. Unlike Takeuti and Zaring, they also do not use the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1994.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem peano1:
  $ wcel c0 com $;

--| The successor of any natural number is a natural number. One of Peano's five postulates for arithmetic. Proposition 7.30(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2003.)
theorem peano2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel (csuc A2) com) $;

--| The successor of any natural number is not zero. One of Peano's five postulates for arithmetic. Proposition 7.30(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2003.) Avoid ~ ax-nul . (Revised by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem peano3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wne (csuc A2) c0) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ peano3 as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem peano3OLD (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wne (csuc A2) c0) $;

--| Two natural numbers are equal iff their successors are equal, i.e. the successor function is one-to-one. One of Peano's five postulates for arithmetic. Proposition 7.30(4) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2003.)
theorem peano4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wceq (csuc A2) (csuc B2)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The induction postulate: any class containing zero and closed under the successor operation contains all natural numbers. One of Peano's five postulates for arithmetic. Proposition 7.30(5) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 43, except our proof does not require the Axiom of Infinity. The more traditional statement of mathematical induction as a theorem schema, with a basis and an induction step, is derived from this theorem as Theorem ~ findes . (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-12 . (Revised by GG, 3-Oct-2024.)
theorem peano5 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2)) com)) (wss com A2) $;

--| A natural number is either 0 or a successor. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1998.)
theorem nn0suc {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) com)) $;

--| The Principle of Finite Induction (mathematical induction). Corollary 7.31 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. The simpler hypothesis shown here was suggested in an email from "Colin" on 1-Oct-2001. The hypothesis states that ` A ` is a set of natural numbers, zero belongs to ` A ` , and given any member of ` A ` the member's successor also belongs to ` A ` . The conclusion is that every natural number is in ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-May-2024.)
theorem find {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ w3a (wss A2 com) (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wceq A2 com $;

--| Principle of Finite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last two are the basis and the induction step. Theorem Schema 22 of [Suppes] p. 136. This is Metamath 100 proof #74. (Contributed by NM, 14-Apr-1995.)
theorem finds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) com) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) ta $;

--| Principle of Finite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first four hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last two are the basis and the induction step. The basis of this version is an arbitrary natural number ` B ` instead of zero. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1995.)
theorem findsg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 com) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss B2 (cv y1))) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss B2 A2)) ta $;

--| Principle of Finite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first three hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last two are the basis and the induction step. Theorem Schema 22 of [Suppes] p. 136. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-2002.)
theorem finds2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff y1) (ta: wff):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi ta ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) com) (wi ta (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) com) (wi ta ph) $;

--| Principle of Finite Induction (inference schema), using implicit substitutions. The first three hypotheses establish the substitutions we need. The last two are the basis and the induction step. Theorem Schema 22 of [Suppes] p. 136. (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-2006.)
theorem finds1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) com) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) com) ph $;

--| Finite induction with explicit substitution. The first hypothesis is the basis and the second is the induction step. Theorem Schema 22 of [Suppes] p. 136. See ~ tfindes for the transfinite version. This is an alternative for Metamath 100 proof #74. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 9-Jul-2003.)
theorem findes {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wsbc x3 ph c0 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) com) (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph (csuc (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) com) ph $;

--| The domain of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-1995.)
theorem dmexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cdm A2) cvv) $;

--| The range of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. Similar to Lemma 3D of [Enderton] p. 41. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1995.)
theorem rnexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crn A2) cvv) $;

--| The domain of a set is a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem dmexd (ph: wff) (A2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdm A2) cvv) $;

--| If a function is a set, its domain is a set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-May-2024.)
theorem fndmexd (ph: wff) (D F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cvv) $;

--| If a mapping is a set, its domain is a set. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem dmfex (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F C) (wf A2 B2 F)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The domain of a function is a set iff the function is a set. (Contributed by AV, 8-Aug-2024.)
theorem fndmexb (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel F cvv)) $;

--| The domain of a function is a set iff the function is a set. (Contributed by AV, 8-Aug-2024.)
theorem fdmexb (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel F cvv)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dmfex : shorter but using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 17-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by AV, 23-Aug-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmfexALT (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F C) (wf A2 B2 F)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The domain of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jul-2008.)
theorem dmex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cdm A2) cvv $;

--| The range of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. Similar to Lemma 3D of [Enderton] p. 41. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jul-2008.)
theorem rnex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (crn A2) cvv $;

--| The identity function is a proper class. This means, for example, that we cannot use it as a member of the class of continuous functions unless it is restricted to a set, as in ~ idcn . (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2007.)
theorem iprc:
  $ wn (wcel cid cvv) $;

--| The existence of a restricted identity function, proved without using the Axiom of Replacement (unlike ~ resfunexg ). (Contributed by NM, 13-Jan-2007.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem resiexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cres cid A2) cvv) $;

--| The image of a set is a set. Theorem 3.17 of [Monk1] p. 39. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-1995.)
theorem imaexg (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cima A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The image of a set is a set. Theorem 3.17 of [Monk1] p. 39. (Contributed by JJ, 24-Sep-2021.)
theorem imaex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cima A2 B2) cvv $;

--| The range of a set is a set. Deduction version of ~ rnexd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2025.)
theorem rnexd (ph: wff) (A2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn A2) cvv) $;

--| The image of a set is a set. Deduction version of ~ imaexg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2025.)
theorem imaexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Any set relation is set-like. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2015.)
theorem exse2 (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wse A2 R) $;

--| If a Cartesian product is a set, one of its components must be a set. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.)
theorem xpexr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cxp A2 B2) C) (wo (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| If a nonempty Cartesian product is a set, so are both of its components. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.)
theorem xpexr2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cxp A2 B2) C) (wne (cxp A2 B2) c0)) (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| A condition where the converse of ~ xpex holds as well. Corollary 6.9(2) in [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 13-Nov-2011.)
theorem xpexcnv (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne B2 c0) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| If the relation in a strict order is a set, then the base field is also a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem soex (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel R V)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Membership in a Cartesian product. This version requires no quantifiers or dummy variables. See also ~ elxp5 , ~ elxp6 , and ~ elxp7 . (Contributed by NM, 17-Feb-2004.)
theorem elxp4 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wa (wceq A2 (cop (cuni (cdm (csn A2))) (cuni (crn (csn A2))))) (wa (wcel (cuni (cdm (csn A2))) B2) (wcel (cuni (crn (csn A2))) C))) $;

--| Membership in a Cartesian product requiring no quantifiers or dummy variables. Provides a slightly shorter version of ~ elxp4 when the double intersection does not create class existence problems (caused by ~ int0 ). (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.)
theorem elxp5 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wa (wceq A2 (cop (cint (cint A2)) (cuni (crn (csn A2))))) (wa (wcel (cint (cint A2)) B2) (wcel (cuni (crn (csn A2))) C))) $;

--| The converse of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1998.)
theorem cnvexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (ccnv A2) cvv) $;

--| The converse of a set is a set. Corollary 6.8(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2003.)
theorem cnvex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (ccnv A2) cvv $;

--| A relation is a set iff its converse is a set. (Contributed by FL, 3-Mar-2007.)
theorem relcnvexb (R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wb (wcel R cvv) (wcel (ccnv R) cvv)) $;

--| If the range of a 1-1 onto function is a set, the function itself is a set. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2019.)
theorem f1oexrnex (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wcel B2 V)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| There is a one-to-one onto function from a set to a second set iff there is a one-to-one onto function from the second set to the first set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Sep-2018.)
theorem f1oexbi {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wex f2 (wf1o A2 B2 (cv f2))) (wex g2 (wf1o B2 A2 (cv g2))) $;

--| The composition of two sets is a set. (Contributed by NM, 19-Mar-1998.)
theorem coexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (ccom A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The composition of two sets is a set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2003.)
theorem coex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (ccom A2 B2) cvv $;

--| The composition of two sets is a set. (Contributed by SN, 7-Feb-2025.)
theorem coexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The union of a chain (with respect to inclusion) of single-rooted sets is single-rooted. (See ~ funcnv for "single-rooted" definition.) (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-2004.)
theorem funcnvuni {f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral f2 (wa (wfun (ccnv (cv f2))) (wral g2 (wo (wss (cv f2) (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) (cv f2))) A2)) A2) (wfun (ccnv (cuni A2))) $;

--| The union of a chain (with respect to inclusion) of one-to-one functions is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-2004.)
theorem fun11uni {f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral f2 (wa (wa (wfun (cv f2)) (wfun (ccnv (cv f2)))) (wral g2 (wo (wss (cv f2) (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) (cv f2))) A2)) A2) (wa (wfun (cuni A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cuni A2)))) $;

--| Extension of an injection which is a restriction of a function. (Contributed by AV, 3-Oct-2025.)
theorem resf1extb (A2 B2 C F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel X (cdif A2 C)) (wss C A2)) (wb (wa (wf1 C B2 (cres F C)) (wnel (cfv X F) (cima F C))) (wf1 (cun C (csn X)) B2 (cres F (cun C (csn X))))) $;

--| Extension of an injection which is a restriction of a function. (Contributed by AV, 3-Oct-2025.)
theorem resf1ext2b (A2 B2 C F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel X (cdif A2 C)) (wss C A2)) (wb (wa (wfun (ccnv (cres F C))) (wnel (cfv X F) (cima F C))) (wfun (ccnv (cres F (cun C (csn X)))))) $;

--| A function with bounded domain and codomain is a set. This version of ~ fex is proven without the Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep , but depends on ~ ax-un , which is not required for the proof of ~ fex . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem fex2 (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Existence of a set of functions. In contrast to ~ fabex or ~ fabexg , the condition in the class abstraction does not contain the function explicitly, but the function can be derived from it. Therefore, this theorem is also applicable for more special functions like one-to-one, onto or one-to-one onto functions. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2025.)
theorem fabexd {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff f2) (V W: class f2) (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf X Y (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cab f2 ps) cvv) $;

--| Existence of a set of functions. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem fabexg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C D F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Existence of a set of functions. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2007.)
theorem fabex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) ph)) $ >
  $ wcel F cvv $;

--| The class of all functions mapping one set to another is a set. Remark after Definition 10.24 of [Kunen] p. 31. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 4-Dec-2003.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Jun-2025.)
theorem mapex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wcel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| The class of all 1-1-onto functions mapping one set to another is a set. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem f1oabexg {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2 B2: class) (C D F: class f2):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf1o A2 B2 (cv f2)) ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fiun and ~ f1iun . Formerly part of ~ f1iun . (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2023.)
theorem fiunlem {x3 y1 z v: setvar} (u: setvar) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1 z) (D S: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wf D S B2) (wral y1 (wo (wss B2 C) (wss C B2)) A2)) (wceq (cv u) B2)) (wral v (wo (wss (cv u) (cv v)) (wss (cv v) (cv u))) (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) B2) A2))) $;

--| The union of a chain (with respect to inclusion) of functions is a function. Analogous to ~ f1iun . (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2023.)
theorem fiun {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1) (D: class x3 y1) (S: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wa (wf D S B2) (wral y1 (wo (wss B2 C) (wss C B2)) A2)) A2) (wf (ciun x3 A2 D) S (ciun x3 A2 B2)) $;

--| The union of a chain (with respect to inclusion) of one-to-one functions is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem f1iun {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1) (D: class x3 y1) (S: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wa (wf1 D S B2) (wral y1 (wo (wss B2 C) (wss C B2)) A2)) A2) (wf1 (ciun x3 A2 D) S (ciun x3 A2 B2)) $;

--| The function value of an indexed union is the value of one of the indexed functions. (Contributed by AV, 4-Nov-2023.)
theorem fviunfun {i: setvar} (U: class i) (F I J: class) (X: class i):
  $ wceq U (ciun i I (cfv (cv i) F)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfun U) (wcel J I) (wcel X (cdm (cfv J F)))) (wceq (cfv X U) (cfv X (cfv J F))) $;

--| Relationship between a mapping and an onto mapping. Figure 38 of [Enderton] p. 145. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1998.)
theorem ffoss {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wb (wf A2 B2 F) (wex x3 (wa (wfo A2 (cv x3) F) (wss (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Relationship between one-to-one and one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1998.)
theorem f11o {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wb (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wex x3 (wa (wf1o A2 (cv x3) F) (wss (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ resfunexg , shorter but requiring ~ ax-pow and ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-1995.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem resfunexgALT (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun A2) (wcel B2 C)) (wcel (cres A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Existence of a composition when the first member is a function. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2007.)
theorem cofunexg (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun A2) (wcel B2 C)) (wcel (ccom A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Existence of a composition when the second member is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2007.)
theorem cofunex2g (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wfun (ccnv B2))) (wcel (ccom A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ fnex , derived using the Axiom of Replacement in the form of ~ funimaexg . This version uses ~ ax-pow and ~ ax-un , whereas ~ fnex does not. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fnexALT (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 B2)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Weak version of ~ funex that holds without ~ ax-rep . If the domain and codomain of a function exist, so does the function. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem funexw (B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun F) (wcel (cdm F) B2) (wcel (crn F) C)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Weak version of ~ mptex that holds without ~ ax-rep . If the domain and codomain of a function given by maps-to notation are sets, the function is a set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mptexw {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2 $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cvv $;

--| If the domain of a function exists, so does its range. Part of Theorem 4.15(v) of [Monk1] p. 46. This theorem is derived using the Axiom of Replacement in the form of ~ funex . (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-1995.)
theorem funrnex (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cdm F) B2) (wi (wfun F) (wcel (crn F) cvv)) $;

--| Obsolete proof of ~ zfrep6 as of 5-Apr-2026. (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfrep6OLD {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wral x3 (weu y1 ph) (cv z)) (wex w (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv w)) (cv z))) $;

--| If the domain of an onto function exists, so does its codomain. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.)
theorem focdmex (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) (wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| If the codomain of a one-to-one function exists, so does its domain. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2004.)
theorem f1dmex (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wcel B2 C)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The codomain/range of a 1-1 onto function is a set iff its domain is a set. (Contributed by AV, 21-Mar-2019.)
theorem f1ovv (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| Existence of the class of values of a set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-1995.)
theorem fvclex {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F)))) cvv $;

--| Existence of the class of values of a restricted class. (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem fvresex {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cres F A2))))) cvv $;

--| Existence of a class abstraction of existentially restricted sets. The class ` B ` can be thought of as an expression in ` x ` (which is typically a free variable in the class expression substituted for ` B ` ) and the class abstraction appearing in the statement as the class of values ` B ` as ` x ` varies through ` A ` . If the "domain" ` A ` is a set, then the abstraction is also a set. Therefore, this statement is a kind of Replacement. This can be seen by tracing back through the path ~ axrep6g , ~ axrep6 , ~ ax-rep . See also ~ abrexex2g . There are partial converses under additional conditions, see for instance ~ abnexg . (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-pr , ~ ax-un and shorten proof. (Revised by SN, 11-Dec-2024.)
theorem abrexexg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2)) cvv) $;

--| Existence of a class abstraction of existentially restricted sets. See the comment of ~ abrexexg . See also ~ abrexex2 . (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem abrexex {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2)) cvv $;

--| The existence of an indexed union. ` x ` is normally a free-variable parameter in ` B ` . (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.)
theorem iunexg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel B2 W) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Existence of an existentially restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem abrexex2g {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel (cab y1 ph) W) A2)) (wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 ph A2)) cvv) $;

--| Existence of an ordered pair abstraction, deduction version. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 9-Aug-2024.)
theorem opabex3d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cab y1 ps) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps)) cvv) $;

--| Existence of an ordered pair abstraction if the second components are elements of a set. (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2023.) (Revised by AV, 9-Aug-2024.)
theorem opabex3rd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (cab x3 ps) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) ps)) cvv) $;

--| Existence of an ordered pair abstraction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem opabex3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cab y1 ph) cvv) $ >
  $ wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)) cvv $;

--| The existence of an indexed union. ` x ` is normally a free-variable parameter in the class expression substituted for ` B ` , which can be read informally as ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.)
theorem iunex {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Existence of an existentially restricted class abstraction. ` ph ` normally has free-variable parameters ` x ` and ` y ` . See also ~ abrexex . (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2004.)
theorem abrexex2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 ph) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 ph A2)) cvv $;

--| Existence of a class abstraction with an existentially quantified expression. Both ` x ` and ` y ` can be free in ` ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-2006.)
theorem abexssex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 ph) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) ph))) cvv $;

--| A condition where a class abstraction continues to exist after its wff is existentially quantified. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-2007.)
theorem abexex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 ph) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab y1 (wex x3 ph)) cvv $;

--| Alternate proof of one direction of ~ f1owe , more direct since not using the isomorphism predicate, but requiring ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1997.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem f1oweALT {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class x3 y1) (S F: class):
  $ wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) S)) $ >
  $ wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wi (wwe B2 S) (wwe A2 R)) $;

--| Thus, there is at most one isomorphism between any two well-ordered sets. TODO: Shorten ~ finnisoeu . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem wemoiso {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wwe A2 R) (wmo f2 (wiso A2 B2 R S (cv f2))) $;

--| Thus, there is at most one isomorphism between any two well-ordered sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem wemoiso2 {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wwe B2 S) (wmo f2 (wiso A2 B2 R S (cv f2))) $;

--| Existence of an operator abstraction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by AV, 9-Aug-2024.)
theorem oprabexd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (F V W: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wmo z ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) ps))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2004.)
theorem oprabex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wmo z ph) $ >
  $ wceq F (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) ph)) $ >
  $ wcel F cvv $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction (special case). (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2004.)
theorem oprabex3 {x3 y1 z w v u f2: setvar} (R: class w v u f2) (F: class x3 y1 z w v u f2) (H: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp H H)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp H H))) (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv w) (cv v))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv u) (cv f2)))) (wceq (cv z) R)))))))) $ >
  $ wcel F cvv $;

--| Existence of an existentially restricted operation abstraction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.)
theorem oprabrexex2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (coprab x3 y1 z ph) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex w ph A2)) cvv $;

--| Existence of a class abstraction of existentially restricted sets. Variables ` x ` and ` y ` are normally free-variable parameters in the class expression substituted for ` C ` , which can be thought of as ` C ( x , y ) ` . See comments for ~ abrexex . (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2011.)
theorem ab2rexex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) C) B2) A2)) cvv $;

--| Existence of an existentially restricted class abstraction. ` ph ` normally has free-variable parameters ` x ` , ` y ` , and ` z ` . Compare ~ abrexex2 . (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2011.)
theorem ab2rexex2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab z ph) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2)) cvv $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ xpexg requiring Replacement ( ~ ax-rep ) but not Power Set ( ~ ax-pow ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem xpexgALT (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| General value of ` ( F oF R G ) ` with no assumptions on functionality of ` F ` and ` G ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem offval3 {x3: setvar} (R F G V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpt x3 (cin (cdm F) (cdm G)) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) R))) $;

--| Pointwise combination commutes with restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem offres (D R F G V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wceq (cres (co F G (cof R)) D) (co (cres F D) (cres G D) (cof R))) $;

--| Equivalent expressions for a restriction of the function operation map. Unlike ` oF R ` which is a proper class, ` ( oF R |`` ( A X. B ) ) ` can be a set by ~ ofmresex , allowing it to be used as a function or structure argument. By ~ ofmresval , the restricted operation map values are the same as the original values, allowing theorems for ` oF R ` to be reused. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2014.)
theorem ofmres {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cres (cof R) (cxp A2 B2)) (cmpo f2 g2 A2 B2 (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cof R))) $;

--| Existence of a restriction of the function operation map. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2014.)
theorem ofmresex (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres (cof R) (cxp A2 B2)) cvv) $;

--| The converse of a mapping of subsets to their image of a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2025.)
theorem mptcnfimad {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (U: class x3 y1) (F: class) (M: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 A2 (cima F (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V W F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn M) (cpw W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccnv M) (cmpt y1 (crn M) (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the first member an ordered pair function.
term c1st: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the second member an ordered pair function.
term c2nd: class;

--| Define a function that extracts the first member, or abscissa, of an ordered pair. Theorem ~ op1st proves that it does this. For example, ` ( 1st `` <. 3 , 4 >. ) = 3 ` . Equivalent to Definition 5.13 (i) of [Monk1] p. 52 (compare ~ op1sta and ~ op1stb ). The notation is the same as Monk's. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2004.)
axiom df_1st {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq c1st (cmpt x3 cvv (cuni (cdm (csn (cv x3))))) $;

--| Define a function that extracts the second member, or ordinate, of an ordered pair. Theorem ~ op2nd proves that it does this. For example, ` ( 2nd `` <. 3 , 4 >. ) = 4 ` . Equivalent to Definition 5.13 (ii) of [Monk1] p. 52 (compare ~ op2nda and ~ op2ndb ). The notation is the same as Monk's. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2004.)
axiom df_2nd {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq c2nd (cmpt x3 cvv (cuni (crn (csn (cv x3))))) $;

--| The value of the function that extracts the first member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem _1stval (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 c1st) (cuni (cdm (csn A2))) $;

--| The value of the function that extracts the second member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem _2ndval (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) (cuni (crn (csn A2))) $;

--| Value of the first-member function at non-pairs. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem _1stnpr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv))) (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) c0) $;

--| Value of the second-member function at non-pairs. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem _2ndnpr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv))) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) c0) $;

--| The value of the first-member function at the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2007.)
theorem _1st0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 c1st) c0 $;

--| The value of the second-member function at the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2007.)
theorem _2nd0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 c2nd) c0 $;

--| Extract the first member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2004.)
theorem op1st (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) c1st) A2 $;

--| Extract the second member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2004.)
theorem op2nd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) c2nd) B2 $;

--| Extract the first member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem op1std (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq C (cop A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv C c1st) A2) $;

--| Extract the second member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem op2ndd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq C (cop A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv C c2nd) B2) $;

--| Extract the first member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2005.)
theorem op1stg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) c1st) A2) $;

--| Extract the second member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2005.)
theorem op2ndg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) c2nd) B2) $;

--| Extract the first member of an ordered triple. (Due to infrequent usage, it isn't worthwhile at this point to define special extractors for triples, so we reuse the ordered pair extractors for ~ ot1stg , ~ ot2ndg , ~ ot3rdg .) (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2015.)
theorem ot1stg (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cotp A2 B2 C) c1st) c1st) A2) $;

--| Extract the second member of an ordered triple. (See ~ ot1stg comment.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2015.)
theorem ot2ndg (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cotp A2 B2 C) c1st) c2nd) B2) $;

--| Extract the third member of an ordered triple. (See ~ ot1stg comment.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem ot3rdg (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wceq (cfv (cotp A2 B2 C) c2nd) C) $;

--| Alternate value of the function that extracts the first member of an ordered pair. Definition 5.13 (i) of [Monk1] p. 52. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2006.)
theorem _1stval2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv)) (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) (cint (cint A2))) $;

--| Alternate value of the function that extracts the second member of an ordered pair. Definition 5.13 (ii) of [Monk1] p. 52. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2006.)
theorem _2ndval2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv)) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) (cint (cint (cint (ccnv (csn A2)))))) $;

--| The components of an ordered triple. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Mar-2018.)
theorem oteqimp (A2 B2 C T X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wceq T (cotp A2 B2 C)) (wi (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y) (wcel C Z)) (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv T c1st) c1st) A2) (wceq (cfv (cfv T c1st) c2nd) B2) (wceq (cfv T c2nd) C))) $;

--| The ` 1st ` function maps the universe onto the universe. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem fo1st:
  $ wfo cvv cvv c1st $;

--| The ` 2nd ` function maps the universe onto the universe. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem fo2nd:
  $ wfo cvv cvv c2nd $;

--| Uniqueness condition for the binary relation ` 1st ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jul-2020.) Revised to remove sethood hypothesis on ` C ` . (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 17-Jan-2022.)
theorem br1steqg (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C c1st) (wceq C A2)) $;

--| Uniqueness condition for the binary relation ` 2nd ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jul-2020.) Revised to remove sethood hypothesis on ` C ` . (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 17-Jan-2022.)
theorem br2ndeqg (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C c2nd) (wceq C B2)) $;

--| Mapping of a restriction of the ` 1st ` (first member of an ordered pair) function. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem f1stres (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf (cxp A2 B2) A2 (cres c1st (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Mapping of a restriction of the ` 2nd ` (second member of an ordered pair) function. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem f2ndres (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf (cxp A2 B2) B2 (cres c2nd (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Onto mapping of a restriction of the ` 1st ` (first member of an ordered pair) function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2008.)
theorem fo1stres (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne B2 c0) (wfo (cxp A2 B2) A2 (cres c1st (cxp A2 B2))) $;

--| Onto mapping of a restriction of the ` 2nd ` (second member of an ordered pair) function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2008.)
theorem fo2ndres (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wfo (cxp A2 B2) B2 (cres c2nd (cxp A2 B2))) $;

--| Value of an alternate definition of the ` 1st ` function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem _1st2val {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 (coprab x3 y1 z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (cfv A2 c1st) $;

--| Value of an alternate definition of the ` 2nd ` function. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem _2nd2val {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 (coprab x3 y1 z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cfv A2 c2nd) $;

--| Composition of the first member function with another function. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2007.)
theorem _1stcof (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 (cxp B2 C) F) (wf A2 B2 (ccom c1st F)) $;

--| Composition of the second member function with another function. (Contributed by FL, 15-Oct-2012.)
theorem _2ndcof (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 (cxp B2 C) F) (wf A2 C (ccom c2nd F)) $;

--| Location of the first element of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem xp1st (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wcel (cfv A2 c1st) B2) $;

--| Location of the second element of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem xp2nd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wcel (cfv A2 c2nd) C) $;

--| Membership in a Cartesian product. This version requires no quantifiers or dummy variables. See also ~ elxp4 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2004.)
theorem elxp6 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wa (wceq A2 (cop (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd))) (wa (wcel (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wcel (cfv A2 c2nd) C))) $;

--| Membership in a Cartesian product. This version requires no quantifiers or dummy variables. See also ~ elxp4 . (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2006.)
theorem elxp7 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv)) (wa (wcel (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wcel (cfv A2 c2nd) C))) $;

--| Equality with an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2014.)
theorem eqopi (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp V W)) (wa (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) C))) (wceq A2 (cop B2 C)) $;

--| Representation of Cartesian product based on ordered pair component functions. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2006.)
theorem xp2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cxp A2 B2) (crab x3 (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c1st) A2) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) B2)) (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| The membership relation for a Cartesian product is inherited by union. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2006.)
theorem unielxp (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wcel (cuni A2) (cuni (cxp B2 C))) $;

--| Reconstruction of a member of a Cartesian product in terms of its ordered pair components. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2013.)
theorem _1st2nd2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wceq A2 (cop (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd))) $;

--| Reconstruction of an ordered pair in terms of its components. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-2014.)
theorem _1st2ndb (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv)) (wceq A2 (cop (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd))) $;

--| An ordered pair theorem for members of Cartesian products. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jun-2007.)
theorem xpopth (A2 B2 C D R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp C D)) (wcel B2 (cxp R S))) (wb (wa (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c1st)) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) (cfv B2 c2nd))) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Two ways to express equality with an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem eqop (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp V W)) (wb (wceq A2 (cop B2 C)) (wa (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) C))) $;

--| Two ways to express equality with an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-2014.)
theorem eqop2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cop B2 C)) (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cvv cvv)) (wa (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) C))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing that an element is the first member of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2014.)
theorem op1steq {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp V W)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 c1st) B2) (wex x3 (wceq A2 (cop B2 (cv x3))))) $;

--| There is a unique ordered pair fulfilling a wff iff its components fulfil a corresponding wff. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem opreuopreu {p a b: setvar} (ph: wff p) (ps: wff p a b) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv a) (cfv (cv p) c1st)) (wceq (cv b) (cfv (cv p) c2nd))) (wb ps ph) $ >
  $ wb (wreu p ph (cxp A2 B2)) (wreu p (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ps))) (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| A member of a nested Cartesian product is an ordered triple. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Feb-2018.)
theorem el2xptp0 (A2 U V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X U) (wcel Y V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wa (wcel A2 (cxp (cxp U V) W)) (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c1st) X) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c2nd) Y) (wceq (cfv A2 c2nd) Z))) (wceq A2 (cotp X Y Z))) $;

--| Version of ~ elrel for triple Cartesian products. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 1-Feb-2025.)
theorem el2xpss {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class) (R: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 R) (wss R (cxp (cxp B2 C) D))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wceq A2 (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)))))) $;

--| Swap the members of an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem _2nd1st (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp B2 C)) (wceq (cuni (ccnv (csn A2))) (cop (cfv A2 c2nd) (cfv A2 c1st))) $;

--| Reconstruction of a member of a relation in terms of its ordered pair components. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-2006.)
theorem _1st2nd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel B2) (wcel A2 B2)) (wceq A2 (cop (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd))) $;

--| The first ordered pair component of a member of a relation belongs to the domain of the relation. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2006.)
theorem _1stdm (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel R) (wcel A2 R)) (wcel (cfv A2 c1st) (cdm R)) $;

--| The second ordered pair component of a member of a relation belongs to the range of the relation. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2006.)
theorem _2ndrn (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel R) (wcel A2 R)) (wcel (cfv A2 c2nd) (crn R)) $;

--| Express an element of a relation as a relationship between first and second components. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2016.)
theorem _1st2ndbr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel B2) (wcel A2 B2)) (wbr (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd) B2) $;

--| Two ways of expressing membership in the domain of a relation. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem releldm2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wb (wcel B2 (cdm A2)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) B2) A2)) $;

--| An expression for the domain of a relation. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2013.) (Proof shortened by Sky Winter, 22-Oct-2026.)
theorem reldm {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wceq (cdm A2) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) c1st)))) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ reldm as of 22-Oct-2026. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem reldmOLD {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wceq (cdm A2) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) c1st)))) $;

--| One way of expressing membership in the difference of domains of two nested relations. (Contributed by AV, 26-Oct-2023.)
theorem releldmdifi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel A2) (wss B2 A2)) (wi (wcel C (cdif (cdm A2) (cdm B2))) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) (cdif A2 B2))) $;

--| The function value for the first component of an ordered pair is the second component of the ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2023.)
theorem funfv1st2nd (F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel X F)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X c1st) F) (cfv X c2nd)) $;

--| If the first component of an element of a function is in the domain of a subset of the function, the element is a member of this subset. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2023.)
theorem funelss (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun A2) (wss B2 A2) (wcel X A2)) (wi (wcel (cfv X c1st) (cdm B2)) (wcel X B2)) $;

--| Two ways of expressing membership in the difference of domains of two nested functions. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2023.)
theorem funeldmdif {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun A2) (wss B2 A2)) (wb (wcel C (cdif (cdm A2) (cdm B2))) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) (cdif A2 B2))) $;

--| Equality theorem for substitution of a class for an ordered pair (analogue of ~ sbceq1a that avoids the existential quantifiers of ~ copsexg ). (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem sbcopeq1a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq A2 (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph (cfv A2 c2nd)) (cfv A2 c1st)) ph) $;

--| Equality theorem for substitution of a class ` A ` for an ordered pair ` <. x , y >. ` in ` B ` (analogue of ~ csbeq1a ). (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem csbopeq1a {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq A2 (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (csb x3 (cfv A2 c1st) (csb y1 (cfv A2 c2nd) B2)) B2) $;

--| Equality theorem for substitution of a class for an ordered triple. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem sbcoteq1a {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq A2 (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wb (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 (wsbc z ph (cfv A2 c2nd)) (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c2nd)) (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c1st)) ph) $;

--| A way to define an ordered-pair class abstraction without using existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfopab2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wceq (copab x3 y1 ph) (crab z (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph (cfv (cv z) c2nd)) (cfv (cv z) c1st)) (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| A way to define an operation class abstraction without using existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfoprab3s {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z ph) (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cxp cvv cvv)) (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph (cfv (cv w) c2nd)) (cfv (cv w) c1st)))) $;

--| Operation class abstraction expressed without existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2008.)
theorem dfoprab3 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cxp cvv cvv)) ph)) (coprab x3 y1 z ps) $;

--| Operation class abstraction expressed without existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfoprab4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cxp A2 B2)) ph)) (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) ps)) $;

--| Operation class abstraction expressed without existential quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Dec-2008.) Remove unnecessary distinct variable conditions. (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfoprab4f {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cxp A2 B2)) ph)) (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) ps)) $;

--| Condition for an operation to be a set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jun-2019.)
theorem opabex2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cv y1) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 ps) cvv) $;

--| An ordered-pair class abstraction which does not depend on the first abstraction variable is a proper class. There must be, however, at least one set which satisfies the restricting wff. (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2020.)
theorem opabn1stprc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1):
  $ wi (wex y1 ph) (wnel (copab x3 y1 ph) cvv) $;

--| The property of a uniquely specified ordered pair. The proof uses properties of the ` iota ` description binder. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem opiota {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z) (A2 B2 C D: class) (I X Y: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq I (cio z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) ph) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv I c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv I c2nd) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) D) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (weu z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) ph) B2) A2)) (wb (w3a (wcel C A2) (wcel D B2) ch) (wa (wceq C X) (wceq D Y))) $;

--| The converse of a class abstraction of nested ordered pairs. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Feb-2022.)
theorem cnvoprab {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (ps: wff z a):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel (cv a) (cxp cvv cvv)) $ >
  $ wceq (ccnv (coprab x3 y1 z ph)) (copab z a ps) $;

--| Define the Cartesian product of three classes. Compare ~ df-xp . (Contributed by FL, 6-Nov-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2015.)
theorem dfxp3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) (coprab x3 y1 z (w3a (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) C))) $;

--| A consequence of membership in an ordered-pair class abstraction, using ordered pair extractors. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-2006.)
theorem elopabi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv A2 c1st)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv A2 c2nd)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (copab x3 y1 ph)) ch $;

--| A consequence of membership in an operation class abstraction, using ordered pair extractors. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2006.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.)
theorem eloprabi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1 z) (th: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c1st)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cfv A2 c1st) c2nd)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv A2 c2nd)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (coprab x3 y1 z ph)) th $;

--| Express a two-argument function as a one-argument function, or vice-versa. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem mpomptsx {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (cmpt z (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) (csb x3 (cfv (cv z) c1st) (csb y1 (cfv (cv z) c2nd) C))) $;

--| Express a two-argument function as a one-argument function, or vice-versa. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2015.)
theorem mpompts {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (cmpt z (cxp A2 B2) (csb x3 (cfv (cv z) c1st) (csb y1 (cfv (cv z) c2nd) C))) $;

--| The domain of a mapping is a subset of its base class. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dmmpossx {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) $;

--| Functionality, domain and codomain of a class given by the maps-to notation, where ` B ( x ) ` is not constant but depends on ` x ` . (Contributed by NM, 29-Dec-2014.)
theorem fmpox {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C D) B2) A2) (wf (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) D F) $;

--| Functionality, domain and range of a class given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.)
theorem fmpo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C D) B2) A2) (wf (cxp A2 B2) D F) $;

--| Functionality and domain of a class given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.)
theorem fnmpo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C F V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) B2) A2) (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Functionality and domain of a class given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.)
theorem fnmpoi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wfn F (cxp A2 B2) $;

--| Domain of a class given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.)
theorem dmmpo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp A2 B2) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the mapping operation; deduction form. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem fmpodg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cxp A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R S F) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the mapping operation; deduction form. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 30-Sep-2025.)
theorem fmpod {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 B2) S F) $;

--| An operation's value belongs to its range. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem ovmpoelrn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (M: class) (O X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C M) B2) A2) (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y O) M) $;

--| Domain of an operation given by the maps-to notation, closed form of ~ dmmpo . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Feb-2019.) (Proof shortened by Lammen, 29-May-2024.)
theorem dmmpoga {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3 y1) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) B2) A2) (wceq (cdm F) (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Domain of an operation given by the maps-to notation, closed form of ~ dmmpo . Caution: This theorem is only valid in the very special case where the value of the mapping is a constant! (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jun-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Feb-2019.)
theorem dmmpog {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wceq (cdm F) (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction (version for dependent domains). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem mpoexxg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C R S F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 R) (wral x3 (wcel B2 S) A2)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction (special case). (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem mpoexg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C R S F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 R) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| If the domain of an operation given by maps-to notation is a set, the operation is a set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.)
theorem mpoexga {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) cvv) $;

--| Weak version of ~ mpoex that holds without ~ ax-rep . If the domain and codomain of an operation given by maps-to notation are sets, the operation is a set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-Aug-2023.)
theorem mpoexw {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C D) B2) A2 $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) cvv $;

--| If the domain of an operation given by maps-to notation is a set, the operation is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem mpoex {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) cvv $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jun-2026.)
theorem mpoexd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) cvv) $;

--| The operation value of a function value of a collection of ordered pairs of elements related in two ways. (Contributed by Alexander van Vekens, 8-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Jan-2021.) Add disjoint variable condition on ` D , f , h ` to remove hypotheses; avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by SN, 7-Apr-2025.)
theorem mptmpoopabbrd {f2 g2 h a b: setvar} (ph: wff g2 a b) (ch: wff f2 g2 h a b) (th: wff f2 g2 h) (ta: wff f2 h a b) (A2 B2: class f2 h) (D G: class) (M: class f2 g2 h a b) (W: class f2 h a b) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv G A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv G B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv a) X) (wceq (cv b) Y)) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv g2) G) (wb ch ta) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpo a b (cfv (cv g2) A2) (cfv (cv g2) B2) (copab f2 h (wa ch (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (cfv (cv g2) D)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y (cfv G M)) (copab f2 h (wa th (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (cfv G D))))) $;

--| The operation value of a function value of a collection of ordered pairs of related elements. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Jan-2021.) Add disjoint variable condition on ` D , f , h ` to remove hypotheses. (Revised by SN, 13-Dec-2024.)
theorem mptmpoopabovd {f2 g2 h a b: setvar} (ph: wff g2 a b) (A2 B2 C: class f2 h) (D G: class) (M: class f2 g2 h a b) (W: class f2 h a b) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv G A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv G B2)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpo a b (cfv (cv g2) A2) (cfv (cv g2) B2) (copab f2 h (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (co (cv a) (cv b) (cfv (cv g2) C))) (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (cfv (cv g2) D)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y (cfv G M)) (copab f2 h (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (co X Y (cfv G C))) (wbr (cv f2) (cv h) (cfv G D))))) $;

--| If the operation value of the operation value of two nested maps-to notation is not empty, all involved arguments belong to the corresponding base classes of the maps-to notations. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2021.)
theorem el2mpocsbcl {x3 y1 t s1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (S T: class x3 y1 t s1) (U: class t s1) (E O: class x3 y1 t s1) (V: class t s1) (W: class x3 y1 t s1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cmpo s1 t C D E)) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel C U) (wcel D V)) B2) A2) (wi (wcel W (co S T (co X Y O))) (wa (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel S (csb x3 X (csb y1 Y C))) (wcel T (csb x3 X (csb y1 Y D)))))) $;

--| If the operation value of the operation value of two nested maps-to notation is not empty, all involved arguments belong to the corresponding base classes of the maps-to notations. Using implicit substitution. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2021.)
theorem el2mpocl {x3 y1 t s1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (S T: class x3 y1 t s1) (U: class t s1) (E: class x3 y1 t s1) (F G: class t s1) (O: class x3 y1 t s1) (V: class t s1) (W: class x3 y1 t s1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cmpo s1 t C D E)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wa (wceq C F) (wceq D G)) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel C U) (wcel D V)) B2) A2) (wi (wcel W (co S T (co X Y O))) (wa (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel S F) (wcel T G)))) $;

--| A function with a Cartesian product as domain is a mapping with two arguments defined by its operation values. (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2019.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2022.)
theorem fnmpoovd {i j a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class a b) (D: class i j) (U: class i j a b) (M: class a b) (V: class i j a b):
  $ wi ph (wfn M (cxp A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv i) (cv a)) (wceq (cv j) (cv b))) (wceq D C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) A2) (wcel (cv j) B2)) (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq M (cmpo a b A2 B2 C)) (wral i (wral j (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) M) D) B2) A2)) $;

--| The function operation expressed as a mapping, variation of ~ offval2 . (Contributed by SO, 15-Jul-2018.)
theorem offval22 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (R: class) (F G V W X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (co C D R))) $;

--| If a binary relation holds for the result of an operation, the operands are in the domain of the operation. (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2020.)
theorem brovpreldm (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (wbr D E (co B2 C A2)) (wcel (cop B2 C) (cdm A2)) $;

--| If a binary relation holds for the result of an operation which is a result of an operation, the involved classes are sets. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Dec-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem bropopvvv {v e f2 p a b: setvar} (ph: wff v e f2 p a b) (ps: wff f2 p a b) (th: wff v e f2 p a b) (A2 B2 P: class v e f2 p a b) (E: class) (F O: class v e f2 p a b) (V: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpo v e cvv cvv (cmpo a b (cv v) (cv v) (copab f2 p ph))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wceq (cv e) E)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V))) (wceq (co A2 B2 (co V E O)) (copab f2 p th)) $ >
  $ wi (wbr F P (co A2 B2 (co V E O))) (w3a (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) (wa (wcel F cvv) (wcel P cvv)) (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bropfvvvv . (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2020.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jan-2021.)
theorem bropfvvvvlem {e a b c d: setvar} (ph th: wff e a b c d) (A2 B2 C D S T: class e a b c d) (U: class e b c d) (E O V W: class e a b c d):
  $ wceq O (cmpt a U (cmpo b c V W (copab d e ph))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 S) (wcel C T)) (wceq (co B2 C (cfv A2 O)) (copab d e th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cop B2 C) (cxp S T)) (wbr D E (co B2 C (cfv A2 O)))) (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wa (wcel B2 S) (wcel C T)) (wa (wcel D cvv) (wcel E cvv))) $;

--| If a binary relation holds for the result of an operation which is a function value, the involved classes are sets. (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2020.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jan-2021.)
theorem bropfvvvv {e a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e a b c d) (ps: wff e b c d) (th: wff e a b c d) (A2: class) (B2 C D: class e a b c d) (S T: class e d) (U: class e b c d) (E O V W X Y: class e a b c d):
  $ wceq O (cmpt a U (cmpo b c V W (copab d e ph))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 S) (wcel C T)) (wceq (co B2 C (cfv A2 O)) (copab d e th)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wceq V S) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wceq W T) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S X) (wcel T Y)) (wi (wbr D E (co B2 C (cfv A2 O))) (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wa (wcel B2 S) (wcel C T)) (wa (wcel D cvv) (wcel E cvv)))) $;

--| If all the values of the mapping are subsets of a class ` X ` , then so is any evaluation of the mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-un and shorten proof. (Revised by Zhi Wang, 16-Sep-2026.)
theorem ovmptss {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2 C E F G: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss C X) B2) A2) (wss (co E G F) X) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ ovmptss as of 16-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ovmptssOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C E F G: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss C X) B2) A2) (wss (co E G F) X) $;

--| Any function to sets of ordered pairs produces a relation on function value unconditionally. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem relmpoopab {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class z w) (B2: class x3 z w) (C D F: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (copab z w ph)) $ >
  $ wrel (co C D F) $;

--| Composition of two functions. Variation of ~ fmptco when the second function has two arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem fmpoco {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2: class z) (C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class z) (T: class x3 y1) (F G: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel R C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z C S)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) R) (wceq S T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom G F) (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 T)) $;

--| Composition of a function with an operator abstraction. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Sep-2015.)
theorem oprabco {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (F G: class x3 y1) (H: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel C D) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cfv C H)) $ >
  $ wi (wfn H D) (wceq G (ccom H F)) $;

--| Composition of operator abstractions. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 23-Apr-2013.)
theorem oprab2co {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (R S: class) (F G: class x3 y1) (M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel C R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel D S) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cop C D)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (co C D M)) $ >
  $ wi (wfn M (cxp R S)) (wceq G (ccom M F)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a binary function expressed in maps-to notation to be bijective. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Aug-2017.)
theorem f1od2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (F: class x3 y1 z) (I J: class z) (W X Y: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) D)) (wa (wcel I X) (wcel J Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wceq (cv z) C)) (wa (wcel (cv z) D) (wa (wceq (cv x3) I) (wceq (cv y1) J)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 B2) D F) $;

--| An alternate possible definition of the ` 1st ` function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem df1st2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| An alternate possible definition of the ` 2nd ` function. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem df2nd2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| The mapping of a restriction of the ` 1st ` function to a constant function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2008.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem _1stconst (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wf1o (cxp A2 (csn B2)) A2 (cres c1st (cxp A2 (csn B2)))) $;

--| The mapping of a restriction of the ` 2nd ` function to a converse constant function. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem _2ndconst (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wf1o (cxp (csn A2) B2) B2 (cres c2nd (cxp (csn A2) B2))) $;

--| Alternate definition for the maps-to notation ~ df-mpo (although it requires that ` C ` be a set). (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfmpo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (ciun x3 A2 (ciun y1 B2 (csn (cop (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) C)))) $;

--| An operation (in maps-to notation) on two singletons. (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2019.)
theorem mposn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (U E: class) (F: class x3 y1) (V W: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (csn A2) (csn B2) C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq D E) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel E U)) (wceq F (csn (cop (cop A2 B2) E))) $;

--| Composition with ` ``' ( 2nd |`` ( { C } X. _V ) ) ` turns any binary operation ` F ` with a constant first operand into a function ` G ` of the second operand only. This transformation is called "currying". (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2014.)
theorem curry1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp (csn C) cvv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel C A2)) (wceq G (cmpt x3 B2 (co C (cv x3) F))) $;

--| The value of a curried function with a constant first argument. (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem curry1val (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp (csn C) cvv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel C A2)) (wceq (cfv D G) (co C D F)) $;

--| Functionality of a curried function with a constant first argument. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-2008.)
theorem curry1f (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp (csn C) cvv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf (cxp A2 B2) D F) (wcel C A2)) (wf B2 D G) $;

--| Composition with ` ``' ( 1st |`` ( _V X. { C } ) ) ` turns any binary operation ` F ` with a constant second operand into a function ` G ` of the first operand only. This transformation is called "currying". (If this becomes frequently used, we can introduce a new notation for the hypothesis.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2008.)
theorem curry2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv (csn C))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel C B2)) (wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cv x3) C F))) $;

--| Functionality of a curried function with a constant second argument. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2008.)
theorem curry2f (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv (csn C))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf (cxp A2 B2) D F) (wcel C B2)) (wf A2 D G) $;

--| The value of a curried function with a constant second argument. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2008.)
theorem curry2val (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom F (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv (csn C))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel C B2)) (wceq (cfv D G) (co D C F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnvf1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2014.)
theorem cnvf1olem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel A2) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wceq C (cuni (ccnv (csn B2)))))) (wa (wcel C (ccnv A2)) (wceq B2 (cuni (ccnv (csn C))))) $;

--| Describe a function that maps the elements of a set to its converse bijectively. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2014.)
theorem cnvf1o {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wf1o A2 (ccnv A2) (cmpt x3 A2 (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpar . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fparlem1 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cima (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (csn (cv x3))) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) cvv) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpar . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fparlem2 {y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cima (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (csn (cv y1))) (cxp cvv (csn (cv y1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpar . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fparlem3 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wceq (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom F (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (cxp (csn (cv x3)) cvv) (cxp (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)) cvv)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpar . (Contributed by NM, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fparlem4 {y1: setvar} (B2 G: class):
  $ wi (wfn G B2) (wceq (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom G (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ciun y1 B2 (cxp (cxp cvv (csn (cv y1))) (cxp cvv (csn (cfv (cv y1) G)))))) $;

--| Merge two functions in parallel. Use as the second argument of a composition with a binary operation to build compound functions such as ` ( x e. ( 0 [,) +oo ) , y e. RR |-> ` ` ( ( sqrt `` x ) + ( sin `` y ) ) ) ` , see also ~ ex-fpar . (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fpar {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class) (H: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq H (cin (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom F (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom G (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G B2)) (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $;

--| A function that can be used to feed a common value to both operands of an operation. Use as the second argument of a composition with the function of ~ fpar in order to build compound functions such as ` ( x e. ( 0 [,) +oo ) |-> ( ( sqrt `` x ) + ( sin `` x ) ) ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2007.) Replace use of ~ dfid2 with ~ df-id . (Revised by BJ, 31-Dec-2023.)
theorem fsplit {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (ccnv (cres c1st cid)) (cmpt x3 cvv (cop (cv x3) (cv x3))) $;

--| Merge two functions with a common argument in parallel. Combination of ~ fsplit and ~ fpar . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jan-2024.)
theorem fsplitfpar {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wceq H (cin (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom F (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom G (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cres (ccnv (cres c1st cid)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G A2)) (wceq (ccom H S) (cmpt x3 A2 (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G)))) $;

--| Express the function operation map ` oF ` by the functions defined in ~ fsplit and ~ fpar . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2024.)
theorem offsplitfpar (A2 C c_pl S F G H V W: class):
  $ wceq H (cin (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom F (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom G (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cres (ccnv (cres c1st cid)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G A2)) (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wa (wfn c_pl C) (wss (cxp (crn F) (crn G)) C))) (wceq (ccom c_pl (ccom H S)) (co F G (cof c_pl))) $;

--| The ` 2nd ` (second component of an ordered pair) function restricted to a function ` F ` is a function from ` F ` into the codomain of ` F ` . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 4-Feb-2018.)
theorem f2ndf (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wf F B2 (cres c2nd F)) $;

--| The ` 2nd ` (second component of an ordered pair) function restricted to a function ` F ` is a function from ` F ` onto the range of ` F ` . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 4-Feb-2018.)
theorem fo2ndf (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wfo F (crn F) (cres c2nd F)) $;

--| The ` 2nd ` (second component of an ordered pair) function restricted to a one-to-one function ` F ` is a one-to-one function from ` F ` onto the range of ` F ` . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 4-Feb-2018.)
theorem f1o2ndf1 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wf1o F (crn F) (cres c2nd F)) $;

--| Value of an operation precomposed with the projection on the first component. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2014.) Generalize to closed form. (Revised by BJ, 27-Oct-2024.)
theorem opco1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 (ccom F c1st)) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| Value of an operation precomposed with the projection on the second component. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2024.)
theorem opco2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 (ccom F c2nd)) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Inference form of ~ opco1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem opco1i (B2 C F: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 C (ccom F c1st)) (cfv B2 F) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a binary function expressed in maps-to notation to be bijective. (Contributed by SN, 11-Mar-2025.)
theorem mpof1o2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (F: class x3 y1 z) (I J: class z):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) D)) (wcel I A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) D)) (wcel J B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (cv z) D))) (wb (wa (wceq (cv x3) I) (wceq (cv y1) J)) (wceq (cv z) C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 B2) D F) $;

--| A lexicographical ordering of two well-founded classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Oct-2014.)
theorem frxp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (wfr B2 S)) (wfr (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Lemma for lexicographical ordering theorems. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Mar-2011.)
theorem xporderlem {x3 y1 a b c d: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class a b c d) (T: class x3 y1 a b c d):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)) T) (wa (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv c) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv d) B2))) (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv c) R) (wa (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wbr (cv b) (cv d) S)))) $;

--| Lemma for lexicographical ordering theorems. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Mar-2011.)
theorem xporderlem_b {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class a b) (T: class x3 y1 a b):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv a) (cv b)) T) (wa (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv a) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv b) B2))) (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv a) R) (wa (wceq (cv a) (cv a)) (wbr (cv b) (cv b) S)))) $;

--| A lexicographical ordering of two posets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.)
theorem poxp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 R) (wpo B2 S)) (wpo (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| A lexicographical ordering of two strictly ordered classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.)
theorem soxp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wor B2 S)) (wor (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| A lexicographical ordering of two well-ordered classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.)
theorem wexp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wwe B2 S)) (wwe (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fnwe . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem fnwelem {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff y1 v u) (A2 B2: class) (Q: class z v u) (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z v u) (F: class) (G: class z v u):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) S))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima F (cv w)) cvv) $ >
  $ wceq Q (copab u v (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cxp B2 A2)) (wcel (cv v) (cxp B2 A2))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) S))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z A2 (cop (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 T) $;

--| A variant on lexicographic order, which sorts first by some function of the base set, and then by a "backup" well-ordering when the function value is equal on both elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem fnwe {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) S))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima F (cv w)) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fnwe2 . Substitute variables. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnwe2lem1 {x3 y1 z a b: setvar} (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z) (U: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (wceq S U) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) U)))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cv a) (cv b) T) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F)) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) (csb z (cfv (cv a) F) S)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fnwe2 . Substitution in well-ordering hypothesis. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnwe2lem2 {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (ph: wff a) (A2: class) (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z) (U: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (wceq S U) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) U)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wwe (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2) U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) A2)) (wwe (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv a) F)) A2) (csb z (cfv (cv a) F) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fnwe2 . An element which is in a minimal fiber and minimal within its fiber is minimal globally; thus ` T ` is well-founded. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnwe2lem3 {x3 y1 z a b c: setvar} (ph: wff a) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z) (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z) (U: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (wceq S U) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) U)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wwe (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 (cres F A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cv a) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cv a) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wral c (wn (wbr (cv c) (cv b) T)) (cv a)) (cv a)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fnwe2 . Trichotomy. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnwe2lem4 {x3 y1 z a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z) (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z) (U: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (wceq S U) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) U)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wwe (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 (cres F A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv a) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv b) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o (wbr (cv a) (cv b) T) (wceq (cv a) (cv b)) (wbr (cv b) (cv a) T)) $;

--| A well-ordering can be constructed on a partitioned set by patching together well-orderings on each partition using a well-ordering on the partitions themselves. Similar to ~ fnwe but does not require the within-partition ordering to be globally well. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnwe2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z) (R S: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z) (U: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (wceq S U) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) U)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wwe (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 (cres F A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 T) $;

--| Condition for the well-order in ~ fnwe to be set-like. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem fnse {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class w) (B2: class x3 y1) (R: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) S))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv w)) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 T) $;

--| Value of a function on ordered pairs with values expressed as ordered pairs. Note that ` F ` and ` G ` are the projections of ` H ` to the first and second coordinate respectively. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Dec-2019.)
theorem fvproj {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 F G: class) (H X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cop X Y) H) (cop (cfv X F) (cfv Y G))) $;

--| Image of a cartesian product for a function on ordered pairs with values expressed as ordered pairs. Note that ` F ` and ` G ` are the projections of ` H ` to the first and second coordinate respectively. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Dec-2019.)
theorem fimaproj {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 F G H: class) (X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima H (cxp X Y)) (cxp (cima F X) (cima G Y))) $;

--| A version of ~ ralxp with explicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem ralxpes {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (A2: class z) (B2: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wsbc y1 (wsbc z ph (cfv (cv x3) c2nd)) (cfv (cv x3) c1st)) (cxp A2 B2)) (wral y1 (wral z ph B2) A2) $;

--| Restricted for all over a triple Cartesian product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem ralxp3f {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cotp (cv y1) (cv z) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 ph (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral w ps C) B2) A2) $;

--| Restricted for all over a triple Cartesian product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Feb-2025.)
theorem ralxp3 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1 z w) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cotp (cv y1) (cv z) (cv w))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 ph (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral w ps C) B2) A2) $;

--| Restricted for-all over a triple Cartesian product with explicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem ralxp3es {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z w) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wsbc y1 (wsbc z (wsbc w ph (cfv (cv x3) c2nd)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st)) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral w ph C) B2) A2) $;

--| Special case of founded partial induction over a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem frpoins3xpg {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 w) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z w) (th: wff y1 z w) (ta: wff x3 z w) (A2 B2 R: class) (X: class z w) (Y: class x3 z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi (wal z (wal w (wi (wcel (cop (cv z) (cv w)) (cpred (cxp A2 B2) R (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ch))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) Y) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr (cxp A2 B2) R) (wpo (cxp A2 B2) R) (wse (cxp A2 B2) R)) (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2))) ta $;

--| Special case of founded partial recursion over a triple Cartesian product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem frpoins3xp3g {x3 y1 z w u t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z u t) (ps: wff y1 z w u) (ch: wff x3 z w t) (th: wff w u t) (ta: wff y1 z w u t) (et: wff x3 z w u t) (ze: wff x3 y1 w u t) (A2 B2 C R: class) (X: class w u t) (Y: class x3 w u t) (Z: class x3 y1 w u t):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) C)) (wi (wal w (wal t (wal u (wi (wcel (cotp (cv w) (cv t) (cv u)) (cpred (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) R (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)))) th)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv t)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv u)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) Y) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) Z) (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) R) (wpo (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) R) (wse (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) R)) (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z C))) ze $;

--| Lemma for Cartesian product ordering. Calculate the value of the Cartesian product relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord2lem {x3 y1 a b c d: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class a b c d) (T: class x3 y1 a b c d):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)) T) (w3a (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv c) A2) (wcel (cv d) B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv c) R) (wceq (cv a) (cv c))) (wo (wbr (cv b) (cv d) S) (wceq (cv b) (cv d))) (wo (wne (cv a) (cv c)) (wne (cv b) (cv d))))) $;

--| Lemma for Cartesian product ordering. Calculate the value of the Cartesian product relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord2lem_b {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class a b) (T: class x3 y1 a b):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv a) (cv b)) T) (w3a (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv a) R) (wceq (cv a) (cv a))) (wo (wbr (cv b) (cv b) S) (wceq (cv b) (cv b))) (wo (wne (cv a) (cv a)) (wne (cv b) (cv b))))) $;

--| Another way of partially ordering a Cartesian product of two classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem poxp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Another way of giving a well-founded order to a Cartesian product of two classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem frxp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Calculate the predecessor class in ~ frxp2 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord2pred {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (T X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (cpred (cxp A2 B2) T (cop X Y)) (cdif (cxp (cun (cpred A2 R X) (csn X)) (cun (cpred B2 S Y) (csn Y))) (csn (cop X Y)))) $;

--| Condition for the relation in ~ frxp2 to be set-like. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem sexp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Induction over the Cartesian product ordering. Note that the substitutions cover all possible cases of membership in the predecessor class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord2indlem {x3 y1 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a b d) (ps: wff x3 y1 b c) (ch: wff x3 y1 a c d) (th: wff x3 y1 a b d) (ta: wff x3 y1 b c d) (et: wff x3 y1 a c d) (A2 B2: class) (R S: class a b) (T: class x3 y1) (X: class x3 y1 c d) (Y: class x3 y1 a c d):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp A2 B2)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wfr A2 R $ >
  $ wpo A2 R $ >
  $ wse A2 R $ >
  $ wfr B2 S $ >
  $ wpo B2 S $ >
  $ wse B2 S $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv d)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wi (w3a (wral c (wral d ch (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral c ps (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d th (cpred B2 S (cv b)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) et $;

--| Induction over the Cartesian product ordering. Note that the substitutions cover all possible cases of membership in the predecessor class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord2ind {a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff a b d) (ps: wff b c) (ch: wff a c d) (th: wff a b d) (ta: wff b c d) (et: wff a c d) (A2 B2 R S: class) (X: class c d) (Y: class a c d):
  $ wfr A2 R $ >
  $ wpo A2 R $ >
  $ wse A2 R $ >
  $ wfr B2 S $ >
  $ wpo B2 S $ >
  $ wse B2 S $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv d)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wi (w3a (wral c (wral d ch (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral c ps (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d th (cpred B2 S (cv b)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) et $;

--| Lemma for triple ordering. Calculate the value of the relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord3lem {x3 y1 e f2 a b c d: setvar} (A2 B2 C R S T: class e f2 a b c d) (U: class x3 y1 e f2 a b c d):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cotp (cv a) (cv b) (cv c)) (cotp (cv d) (cv e) (cv f2)) U) (w3a (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) C)) (w3a (wcel (cv d) A2) (wcel (cv e) B2) (wcel (cv f2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv d) R) (wceq (cv a) (cv d))) (wo (wbr (cv b) (cv e) S) (wceq (cv b) (cv e))) (wo (wbr (cv c) (cv f2) T) (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)))) (w3o (wne (cv a) (cv d)) (wne (cv b) (cv e)) (wne (cv c) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Lemma for triple ordering. Calculate the value of the relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpord3lem_b {x3 y1 e f2 a: setvar} (A2 B2 C R S T: class e f2 a) (U: class x3 y1 e f2 a):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cotp (cv a) (cv e) (cv f2)) (cotp (cv a) (cv e) (cv f2)) U) (w3a (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv e) B2) (wcel (cv f2) C)) (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv e) B2) (wcel (cv f2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cv a) (cv a) R) (wceq (cv a) (cv a))) (wo (wbr (cv e) (cv e) S) (wceq (cv e) (cv e))) (wo (wbr (cv f2) (cv f2) T) (wceq (cv f2) (cv f2)))) (w3o (wne (cv a) (cv a)) (wne (cv e) (cv e)) (wne (cv f2) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Triple Cartesian product partial ordering. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem poxp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) U) $;

--| Give well-foundedness over a triple Cartesian product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem frxp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) U) $;

--| Calculate the predecsessor class for the triple order. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jan-2025.)
theorem xpord3pred {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C R S T: class) (U X Y Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z C)) (wceq (cpred (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) U (cotp X Y Z)) (cdif (cxp (cxp (cun (cpred A2 R X) (csn X)) (cun (cpred B2 S Y) (csn Y))) (cun (cpred C T Z) (csn Z))) (csn (cotp X Y Z)))) $;

--| Show that the triple order is set-like. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Aug-2024.)
theorem sexp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) U) $;

--| Induction over the triple Cartesian product ordering. Note that the substitutions cover all possible cases of membership in the predecessor class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Feb-2025.)
theorem xpord3inddlem {x3 y1 e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 e f2 a b c) (ps: wff x3 y1 f2 b c d) (ch: wff x3 y1 e a c d) (th: wff x3 y1 e f2 d) (ta: wff x3 y1 e f2 a b c) (et ze: wff x3 y1 f2 a b c d) (si: wff x3 y1 e a b c d) (rh: wff x3 y1 e f2 b c d) (mu: wff x3 y1 e f2 a c d) (la: wff x3 y1 e f2 a b d) (ka: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C: class) (R S T: class a b c) (U: class x3 y1) (X: class x3 y1 e f2 d) (Y: class x3 y1 e f2 a d) (Z: class x3 y1 e f2 a b d):
  $ wceq U (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C)) (wa (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st) R) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) c1st) c2nd))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) T) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ka (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ka (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ka (wcel Z C) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr C T) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo C T) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse C T) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb et ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ze th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb si ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph rh) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb rh mu) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) Z) (wb mu la) $ >
  $ wi (wa ka (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) C))) (wi (w3a (w3a (wral d (wral e (wral f2 th (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral e ch (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral f2 ze (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred A2 R (cv a)))) (w3a (wral d ps (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral e (wral f2 ta (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (wral e si (cpred B2 S (cv b)))) (wral f2 et (cpred C T (cv c)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi ka la $;

--| Induction over the triple Cartesian product ordering. Note that the substitutions cover all possible cases of membership in the predecessor class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Feb-2025.)
theorem xpord3indd {e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 a b c) (ps: wff f2 b c d) (ch: wff e a c d) (th: wff e f2 d) (ta: wff e f2 a b c) (et ze: wff f2 a b c d) (si: wff e a b c d) (rh: wff e f2 b c d) (mu: wff e f2 a c d) (la: wff e f2 a b d) (ka: wff) (A2 B2 C R S T: class) (X: class e f2 d) (Y: class e f2 a d) (Z: class e f2 a b d):
  $ wi ka (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ka (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ka (wcel Z C) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ka (wfr C T) $ >
  $ wi ka (wpo C T) $ >
  $ wi ka (wse C T) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb et ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ze th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb si ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph rh) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb rh mu) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) Z) (wb mu la) $ >
  $ wi (wa ka (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) C))) (wi (w3a (w3a (wral d (wral e (wral f2 th (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral e ch (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral f2 ze (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred A2 R (cv a)))) (w3a (wral d ps (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral e (wral f2 ta (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (wral e si (cpred B2 S (cv b)))) (wral f2 et (cpred C T (cv c)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi ka la $;

--| Induction over the triple Cartesian product ordering. Note that the substitutions cover all possible cases of membership in the predecessor class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Sep-2024.)
theorem xpord3ind {e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 a b c) (ps: wff f2 b c d) (ch: wff e a c d) (th: wff e f2 d) (ta: wff e f2 a b c) (et ze: wff f2 a b c d) (si: wff e a b c d) (rh: wff e f2 b c d) (mu: wff e f2 a c d) (la: wff e f2 a b d) (A2 B2 C R S T X Y Z: class):
  $ wfr A2 R $ >
  $ wpo A2 R $ >
  $ wse A2 R $ >
  $ wfr B2 S $ >
  $ wpo B2 S $ >
  $ wse B2 S $ >
  $ wfr C T $ >
  $ wpo C T $ >
  $ wse C T $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb et ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ze th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb si ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph rh) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb rh mu) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) Z) (wb mu la) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv a) A2) (wcel (cv b) B2) (wcel (cv c) C)) (wi (w3a (w3a (wral d (wral e (wral f2 th (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral e ch (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral d (wral f2 ze (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred A2 R (cv a)))) (w3a (wral d ps (cpred A2 R (cv a))) (wral e (wral f2 ta (cpred C T (cv c))) (cpred B2 S (cv b))) (wral e si (cpred B2 S (cv b)))) (wral f2 et (cpred C T (cv c)))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z C)) la $;

--| Lemma for ~ poseq and ~ soseq . The function value of a sequence is either in ` A ` or null. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem orderseqlem {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 f2) (G: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wf (cv x3) A2 (cv f2)) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G F) (wcel (cfv X G) (cun A2 (csn c0))) $;

--| A partial ordering of ordinal sequences. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem poseq {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class y1 g2) (R: class y1) (S: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (F: class y1):
  $ wpo (cun A2 (csn c0)) R $ >
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wf (cv x3) A2 (cv f2)) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) F) (wcel (cv g2) F)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))) (cv x3)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) R)) con0))) $ >
  $ wpo F S $;

--| A linear ordering of ordinal sequences. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Jun-2011.)
theorem soseq {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class g2) (R: class y1) (S: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (F: class y1):
  $ wor (cun A2 (csn c0)) R $ >
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wf (cv x3) A2 (cv f2)) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) F) (wcel (cv g2) F)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))) (cv x3)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) R)) con0))) $ >
  $ wn (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wor F S $;

--| Extend class definition to include the support of functions.
term csupp: class;

--| Define the support of a function against a "zero" value. According to Wikipedia ("Support (mathematics)", 31-Mar-2019, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Support_(mathematics) ) "In mathematics, the support of a real-valued function f is the subset of the domain containing those elements which are not mapped to zero." and "The notion of support also extends in a natural way to functions taking values in more general sets than R [the real numbers] and to other objects." The following definition allows for such extensions, being applicable for any sets (which usually are functions) and any element (even not necessarily from the range of the function) regarded as "zero". (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
axiom df_supp {x3 z i: setvar}:
  $ wceq csupp (cmpo x3 z cvv cvv (crab i (wne (cima (cv x3) (csn (cv i))) (csn (cv z))) (cdm (cv x3)))) $;

--| The value of the operation constructing the support of a function. (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppval {i: setvar} (V W: class i) (X Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co X Z csupp) (crab i (wne (cima X (csn (cv i))) (csn Z)) (cdm X))) $;

--| The support of a class is empty if either the class or the "zero" is a proper class. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem supp0prc (X Z: class):
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Z cvv))) (wceq (co X Z csupp) c0) $;

--| The value of the operation constructing the support of a function expressed by binary relations. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by Sky Winter, 14-Oct-2026.)
theorem suppvalbr {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (V W: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co R Z csupp) (cab x3 (wa (wex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wex y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wne (cv y1) Z)))))) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ suppvalbr as of 14-Oct-2026. The value of the operation constructing the support of a function expressed by binary relations. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem suppvalbrOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (V W: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co R Z csupp) (cab x3 (wa (wex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wex y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wne (cv y1) Z)))))) $;

--| The support of the empty set is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem supp0 (W Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z W) (wceq (co c0 Z csupp) c0) $;

--| The value of the operation constructing the support of a function. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppval1 {i: setvar} (V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun X) (wcel X V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co X Z csupp) (crab i (wne (cfv (cv i) X) Z) (cdm X))) $;

--| The value of the operation constructing the support of a function with a given domain. This version of ~ suppvalfn assumes ` F ` is a set rather than its domain ` X ` , avoiding ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by SN, 5-Aug-2024.)
theorem suppvalfng {i: setvar} (F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F X) (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (crab i (wne (cfv (cv i) F) Z) X)) $;

--| The value of the operation constructing the support of a function with a given domain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 22-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppvalfn {i: setvar} (F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F X) (wcel X V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (crab i (wne (cfv (cv i) F) Z) X)) $;

--| An element of the support of a function with a given domain. This version of ~ elsuppfn assumes ` F ` is a set rather than its domain ` X ` , avoiding ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by SN, 5-Aug-2024.)
theorem elsuppfng (S F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F X) (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wcel S (co F Z csupp)) (wa (wcel S X) (wne (cfv S F) Z))) $;

--| An element of the support of a function with a given domain. (Contributed by AV, 27-May-2019.)
theorem elsuppfn (S F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F X) (wcel X V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wcel S (co F Z csupp)) (wa (wcel S X) (wne (cfv S F) Z))) $;

--| Function value is zero outside of its support. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2024.)
theorem fvdifsupp (ph: wff) (A2 F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdif A2 (co F Z csupp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X F) Z) $;

--| The support of functions "defined" by inverse images expressed by binary relations. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2019.)
theorem cnvimadfsn {x3 y1: setvar} (R Z: class):
  $ wceq (cima (ccnv R) (cdif cvv (csn Z))) (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wne (cv y1) Z)))) $;

--| The support of functions "defined" by inverse images is a subset of the support defined by ~ df-supp . (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppimacnvss (R V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wss (cima (ccnv R) (cdif cvv (csn Z))) (co R Z csupp)) $;

--| Support sets of functions expressed by inverse images. (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 7-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppimacnv (R V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wceq (co R Z csupp) (cima (ccnv R) (cdif cvv (csn Z)))) $;

--| Two ways of writing the support of a function with known codomain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem fsuppeq (S F I V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel Z W)) (wi (wf I S F) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (cima (ccnv F) (cdif S (csn Z))))) $;

--| Version of ~ fsuppeq avoiding ~ ax-rep by assuming ` F ` is a set rather than its domain ` I ` . (Contributed by SN, 30-Jul-2024.)
theorem fsuppeqg (S F I V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wi (wf I S F) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (cima (ccnv F) (cdif S (csn Z))))) $;

--| The support of a function is a subset of the function's domain. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2019.)
theorem suppssdm (F Z: class):
  $ wss (co F Z csupp) (cdm F) $;

--| The support of a singleton of an ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppsnop (U F V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq F (csn (cop X Y)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y W) (wcel Z U)) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (cif (wceq Y Z) c0 (csn X))) $;

--| The support of a singleton containing an ordered pair is a subset of the singleton containing the first element of the ordered pair, i.e. it is empty or the singleton itself. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2019.)
theorem snopsuppss (X Y Z: class):
  $ wss (co (csn (cop X Y)) Z csupp) (csn X) $;

--| If the function value for a given argument is not empty, the argument belongs to the support of the function with the empty set as zero. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem fvn0elsupp (B2 G V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel X B2)) (wa (wfn G B2) (wne (cfv X G) c0))) (wcel X (co G c0 csupp)) $;

--| The function value for a given argument is not empty iff the argument belongs to the support of the function with the empty set as zero. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem fvn0elsuppb (B2 G V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 V) (wcel X B2) (wfn G B2)) (wb (wne (cfv X G) c0) (wcel X (co G c0 csupp))) $;

--| Existential quantification restricted to a support. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 27-May-2019.)
theorem rexsupp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F X) (wcel X V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wrex x3 ph (co F Z csupp)) (wrex x3 (wa (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) Z) ph) X)) $;

--| The support of the restriction of a function is a subset of the support of the function itself. (Contributed by AV, 22-Apr-2019.)
theorem ressuppss (B2 F V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wss (co (cres F B2) Z csupp) (co F Z csupp)) $;

--| The support of a class/function is a subset of the support of the union of this class/function with another class/function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2019.)
theorem suppun (ph: wff) (F G V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F Z csupp) (co (cun F G) Z csupp)) $;

--| The support of the restriction of a function is a subset of the support of the function itself. (Contributed by AV, 22-Apr-2019.)
theorem ressuppssdif (B2 F V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wss (co F Z csupp) (cun (co (cres F B2) Z csupp) (cdif (cdm F) B2))) $;

--| The support of a function in maps-to notation with a class difference. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem mptsuppdifd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 F V W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F Z csupp) (crab x3 (wcel B2 (cdif cvv (csn Z))) A2)) $;

--| The support of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem mptsuppd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 U F V W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F Z csupp) (crab x3 (wne B2 Z) A2)) $;

--| The support of an extended function is the same as the original. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem extmptsuppeq {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (W X: class n) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq X Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cmpt n A2 X) Z csupp) (co (cmpt n B2 X) Z csupp)) $;

--| The support of a function which has the same zero values (in its domain) as another function is a subset of the support of this other function. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Jun-2022.)
theorem suppfnss {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F G: class) (V W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G B2)) (w3a (wss A2 B2) (wcel B2 V) (wcel Z W))) (wi (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) Z) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) Z)) A2) (wss (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp))) $;

--| The support of a function which is a subset of another function is a subset of the support of this other function. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem funsssuppss (F G V Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun G) (wss F G) (wcel G V)) (wss (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp)) $;

--| Two ways to express restriction of a support set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem fnsuppres (A2 B2 F V W Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F (cun A2 B2)) (wa (wcel F W) (wcel Z V)) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wb (wss (co F Z csupp) A2) (wceq (cres F B2) (cxp B2 (csn Z)))) $;

--| The support of a function is empty iff it is identically zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem fnsuppeq0 (A2 F V W Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 W) (wcel Z V)) (wb (wceq (co F Z csupp) c0) (wceq F (cxp A2 (csn Z)))) $;

--| The support of a constant function with value zero is empty. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem fczsupp0 (B2 Z: class):
  $ wceq (co (cxp B2 (csn Z)) Z csupp) c0 $;

--| Show that the support of a function is contained in a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by SN, 5-Aug-2024.)
theorem suppss {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k) (F W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cdif A2 W))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F Z csupp) W) $;

--| A function is zero outside its support. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem suppssr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F Z csupp) W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (cdif A2 W))) (wceq (cfv X F) Z) $;

--| A function is zero outside its support. Version of ~ suppssr avoiding ~ ax-rep by assuming ` F ` is a set rather than its domain ` A ` . (Contributed by SN, 5-May-2024.)
theorem suppssrg (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F V W X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F Z csupp) W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (cdif A2 W))) (wceq (cfv X F) Z) $;

--| Formula building theorem for support restrictions: operator with left annihilator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by SN, 11-Apr-2025.)
theorem suppssov1 {x3 v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 v) (B2: class x3) (D: class v) (R: class x3) (L: class x3 v) (O: class x3) (V W: class x3 v) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D A2) Y csupp) L) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) R)) (wceq (co Y (cv v) O) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D (co A2 B2 O)) Z csupp) L) $;

--| Formula building theorem for support restrictions: operator with right annihilator. (Contributed by SN, 11-Apr-2025.)
theorem suppssov2 {x3 v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 v) (D: class v) (R: class x3) (L: class x3 v) (O: class x3) (V W: class x3 v) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D B2) Y csupp) L) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) R)) (wceq (co (cv v) Y O) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D (co A2 B2 O)) Z csupp) L) $;

--| Formula building theorem for support restrictions: vector operation with left annihilator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem suppssof1 {v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class v) (B2: class) (D: class v) (R: class) (U L: class v) (O: class) (V W: class v) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 Y csupp) L) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) R)) (wceq (co Y (cv v) O) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D V A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D R B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co A2 B2 (cof O)) Z csupp) L) $;

--| Show that the support of a function is contained in a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem suppss2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 V: class k) (W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cdif A2 W))) (wceq B2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt k A2 B2) Z csupp) W) $;

--| Show that the support of a function is a subset of a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2019.)
theorem suppsssn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 V: class k) (W Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv k) A2) (wne (cv k) W)) (wceq B2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt k A2 B2) Z csupp) (csn W)) $;

--| Formula building theorem for support restriction, on a function which preserves zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem suppssfv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (D: class) (U F L V: class x3) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D A2) Y csupp) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y F) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cmpt x3 D (cfv A2 F)) Z csupp) L) $;

--| Condition for the support of a function operation to be a subset of the union of the supports of the left and right function terms. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 28-Aug-2023.)
theorem suppofssd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Z Z X) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co F G (cof X)) Z csupp) (cun (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp))) $;

--| Condition for the support of a function operation to be a subset of the support of the left function term. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2019.)
theorem suppofss1d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class) (V: class x3) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co Z (cv x3) X) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co F G (cof X)) Z csupp) (co F Z csupp)) $;

--| Condition for the support of a function operation to be a subset of the support of the right function term. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2019.)
theorem suppofss2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class) (V: class x3) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) Z X) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co F G (cof X)) Z csupp) (co G Z csupp)) $;

--| The support of the composition of two functions is the inverse image by the inner function of the support of the outer function. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2019.) Extract this statement from the proof of ~ supp0cosupp0 . (Revised by SN, 15-Sep-2023.)
theorem suppco (F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wceq (co (ccom F G) Z csupp) (cima (ccnv G) (co F Z csupp))) $;

--| The support of the composition of two functions is a subset of the support of the inner function if the outer function preserves zero. Compare ~ suppssfv , which has a sethood condition on ` A ` instead of ` B ` . (Contributed by SN, 25-May-2024.)
theorem suppcoss (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V W Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (ccom F G) Z csupp) (co G Y csupp)) $;

--| The support of the composition of two functions is empty if the support of the outer function is empty. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2019.)
theorem supp0cosupp0 (F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wi (wceq (co F Z csupp) c0) (wceq (co (ccom F G) Z csupp) c0)) $;

--| The image of the support of the composition of two functions is the support of the outer function. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2019.)
theorem imacosupp (F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wi (wa (wfun G) (wss (co F Z csupp) (crn G))) (wceq (cima G (co (ccom F G) Z csupp)) (co F Z csupp))) $;

--| Membership in a union of Cartesian products, using bound-variable hypothesis for ` E ` instead of distinct variable conditions as in ~ opeliunxp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2020.)
theorem opeliunxp2f {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (E: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 E $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cop C D) (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2))) (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D E)) $;

--| If there is an element of the value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, then the first argument is an element of the first component of the domain and the second argument is an element of the second component of the domain depending on the first argument. (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2020.)
theorem mpoxeldm {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3) (R F N: class x3 y1) (X Y: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 C D R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (co X Y F)) (wa (wcel X C) (wcel Y (csb x3 X D))) $;

--| If the first argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule is not an element of the first component of the domain or the second argument is not an element of the second component of the domain depending on the first argument, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2020.)
theorem mpoxneldm {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3) (R F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 C D R) $ >
  $ wi (wo (wnel X C) (wnel Y (csb x3 X D))) (wceq (co X Y F) c0) $;

--| If there is an element of the value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument, then the second argument is an element of the first component of the first argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopn0yelv {x3 y1: setvar} (C F: class x3 y1) (K: class y1) (N: class x3 y1) (V W: class y1) (X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y)) (wi (wcel N (co (cop V W) K F)) (wcel K V)) $;

--| If the second argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument is not element of the first component of the first argument, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopynvov0g {x3 y1: setvar} (C F: class x3 y1) (K V W: class y1) (X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y)) (wnel K V)) (wceq (co (cop V W) K F) c0) $;

--| If the first argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument, is not an ordered pair, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopxnop0 {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (F K V: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel V (cxp cvv cvv))) (wceq (co V K F) c0) $;

--| If the first argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument, is the empty set, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopx0ov0 {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (F K: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wceq (co c0 K F) c0 $;

--| If the components of the first argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument, are not sets, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopxprcov0 {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (F K V W: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel W cvv))) (wceq (co (cop V W) K F) c0) $;

--| If the second argument of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument is not element of the first component of the first argument, then the value of the operation is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopynvov0 {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (F K V W: class y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) C) $ >
  $ wi (wnel K V) (wceq (co (cop V W) K F) c0) $;

--| Value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopoveq {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 n) (F: class x3 y1 n) (K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (crab n ph (cfv (cv x3) c1st))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y)) (wcel K V)) (wceq (co (cop V W) K F) (crab n (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph K) (cop V W)) V)) $;

--| Element of the value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens and Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopovel {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 n) (F: class x3 y1 n) (K: class) (N: class n) (V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (crab n ph (cfv (cv x3) c1st))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y)) (wb (wcel N (co (cop V W) K F)) (w3a (wcel K V) (wcel N V) (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 (wsbc n ph N) K) (cop V W)))) $;

--| Value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, where the first argument is a pair and the base set of the second argument is the first component of the first argument, deduction version. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem mpoxopoveqd {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 n) (F: class y1 n) (K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cvv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (crab n ph (cfv (cv x3) c1st))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wa (wcel V X) (wcel W Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wn (wcel K V))) (wceq (crab n (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph K) (cop V W)) V) c0) $ >
  $ wi ps (wceq (co (cop V W) K F) (crab n (wsbc x3 (wsbc y1 ph K) (cop V W)) V)) $;

--| A binary relation of the value of an operation given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-Oct-2017.)
theorem brovex {x3 y1: setvar} (C P E F O V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) (wrel (co V E O)) $ >
  $ wi (wbr F P (co V E O)) (wa (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) (wa (wcel F cvv) (wcel P cvv))) $;

--| A binary relation of the value of an operation given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-Oct-2017.)
theorem brovmpoex {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (P E F O V: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (copab z w ph)) $ >
  $ wi (wbr F P (co V E O)) (wa (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) (wa (wcel F cvv) (wcel P cvv))) $;

--| The extension of a binary relation which is the value of an operation given in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2019.)
theorem sprmpod {x3 y1 v e: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch th: wff x3 y1 v e) (R: class x3 y1) (E: class) (M: class x3 y1 v e) (V: class):
  $ wceq M (cmpo v e cvv cvv (copab x3 y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co (cv v) (cv e) R)) ch))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wceq (cv v) V) (wceq (cv e) E)) (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel V cvv) (wcel E cvv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co V E R)) th))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 th) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co V E M) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co V E R)) ps))) $;

--| The transposition of a function.
term ctpos (F: class): class;

--| Define the transposition of a function, which is a function ` G = tpos F ` satisfying ` G ( x , y ) = F ( y , x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_tpos {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (ctpos F) (ccom F (cmpt x3 (cun (ccnv (cdm F)) (csn c0)) (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Subset theorem for transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposss (F G: class):
  $ wi (wss F G) (wss (ctpos F) (ctpos G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposeq (F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (ctpos F) (ctpos G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.)
theorem tposeqd (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ctpos F) (ctpos G)) $;

--| The transposition is a subset of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem tposssxp (F: class):
  $ wss (ctpos F) (cxp (cun (ccnv (cdm F)) (csn c0)) (crn F)) $;

--| The transposition is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem reltpos (F: class):
  $ wrel (ctpos F) $;

--| Value of the transposition at an ordered pair ` <. A , B >. ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem brtpos2 (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wbr A2 B2 (ctpos F)) (wa (wcel A2 (cun (ccnv (cdm F)) (csn c0))) (wbr (cuni (ccnv (csn A2))) B2 F))) $;

--| The behavior of ` tpos ` when the left argument is the empty set (which is not an ordered pair but is the "default" value of an ordered pair when the arguments are proper classes). This allows to eliminate sethood hypotheses on ` A , B ` in ~ brtpos . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem brtpos0 (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wbr c0 A2 (ctpos F)) (wbr c0 A2 F)) $;

--| Necessary and sufficient condition for ` dom tpos F ` to be a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem reldmtpos (F: class):
  $ wb (wrel (cdm (ctpos F))) (wn (wcel c0 (cdm F))) $;

--| The transposition swaps arguments of a three-parameter relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem brtpos (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C (ctpos F)) (wbr (cop B2 A2) C F)) $;

--| The transposition swaps the first two elements in a collection of ordered triples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.)
theorem ottpos (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel (cotp A2 B2 C) (ctpos F)) (wcel (cotp B2 A2 C) F)) $;

--| The transposition swaps arguments of a three-parameter relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2015.)
theorem relbrtpos (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wrel F) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C (ctpos F)) (wbr (cop B2 A2) C F)) $;

--| The domain of ` tpos F ` when ` dom F ` is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem dmtpos (F: class):
  $ wi (wrel (cdm F)) (wceq (cdm (ctpos F)) (ccnv (cdm F))) $;

--| The range of ` tpos F ` when ` dom F ` is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem rntpos (F: class):
  $ wi (wrel (cdm F)) (wceq (crn (ctpos F)) (crn F)) $;

--| The transposition of a set is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposexg (F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wcel (ctpos F) cvv) $;

--| The transposition swaps the arguments in a two-argument function. When ` F ` is a matrix, which is to say a function from ` ( 1 ... m ) X. ( 1 ... n ) ` to ` RR ` or some ring, ` tpos F ` is the transposition of ` F ` , which is where the name comes from. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem ovtpos (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 (ctpos F)) (co B2 A2 F) $;

--| The transposition of a function is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposfun (F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wfun (ctpos F)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` tpos ` when ` F ` has relational domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem dftpos2 {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wrel (cdm F)) (wceq (ctpos F) (ccom F (cmpt x3 (ccnv (cdm F)) (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3))))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` tpos ` when ` F ` has relational domain. Compare ~ df-cnv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem dftpos3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wrel (cdm F)) (wceq (ctpos F) (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv z) F))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` tpos ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem dftpos4 {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (ctpos F) (ccom F (cmpt x3 (cun (cxp cvv cvv) (csn c0)) (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Value of the double transposition for a general class ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem tpostpos (F: class):
  $ wceq (ctpos (ctpos F)) (cin F (cxp (cun (cxp cvv cvv) (csn c0)) cvv)) $;

--| Value of the double transposition for a relation on triples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem tpostpos2 (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel F) (wrel (cdm F))) (wceq (ctpos (ctpos F)) F) $;

--| The domain of a transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposfn2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wi (wfn F A2) (wfn (ctpos F) (ccnv A2))) $;

--| Condition for a surjective transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposfo2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wfo (ccnv A2) B2 (ctpos F))) $;

--| The domain and codomain of a transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposf2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wf (ccnv A2) B2 (ctpos F))) $;

--| Condition for an injective transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposf12 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wi (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wf1 (ccnv A2) B2 (ctpos F))) $;

--| Condition of a bijective transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposf1o2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wrel A2) (wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wf1o (ccnv A2) B2 (ctpos F))) $;

--| The domain and codomain/range of a transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposfo (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wfo (cxp A2 B2) C F) (wfo (cxp B2 A2) C (ctpos F)) $;

--| The domain and codomain of a transposition. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposf (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf (cxp A2 B2) C F) (wf (cxp B2 A2) C (ctpos F)) $;

--| Functionality of a transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tposfn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wfn (ctpos F) (cxp B2 A2)) $;

--| Transposition of the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tpos0:
  $ wceq (ctpos c0) c0 $;

--| Transposition of a composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tposco (F G: class):
  $ wceq (ctpos (ccom F G)) (ccom F (ctpos G)) $;

--| Two ways to say a function is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tpossym {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F (cxp A2 A2)) (wb (wceq (ctpos F) F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) F)) A2) A2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposeqi (F G: class):
  $ wceq F G $ >
  $ wceq (ctpos F) (ctpos G) $;

--| A transposition is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposex (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wcel (ctpos F) cvv $;

--| Hypothesis builder for transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem nftpos {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (ctpos F) $;

--| Transposition of a class of ordered triples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposoprab {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (F: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (coprab x3 y1 z ph) $ >
  $ wceq (ctpos F) (coprab y1 x3 z ph) $;

--| Transposition of a two-argument mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem tposmpo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (ctpos F) (cmpo y1 x3 B2 A2 C) $;

--| The transposition of a constant operation using the relation representation. (Contributed by SO, 11-Jul-2018.)
theorem tposconst (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (ctpos (cxp (cxp A2 B2) (csn C))) (cxp (cxp B2 A2) (csn C)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the currying function.
term ccur (A2: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include the uncurrying function.
term cunc (A2: class): class;

--| Define the currying of ` F ` , which splits a function of two arguments into a function of the first argument, producing a function over the second argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_cur {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (ccur F) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cdm F)) (copab y1 z (wbr (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) F))) $;

--| Define the uncurrying of ` F ` , which takes a function producing functions, and transforms it into a two-argument function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_unc {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (cunc F) (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| The currying of an operation given in maps-to notation, splitting the operation (function of two arguments) into a function of the first argument, producing a function over the second argument. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2019.)
theorem mpocurryd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 y1) (F V X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) Y) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccur F) (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y C))) $;

--| The value of a curried operation given in maps-to notation is a function over the second argument of the original operation. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2019.)
theorem mpocurryvald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (F V: class) (W: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) Y) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (ccur F)) (cmpt y1 Y (csb x3 A2 C))) $;

--| The value of the value of a curried operation given in maps-to notation is the operation value of the original operation. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2019.)
theorem fvmpocurryd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3 y1) (V: class) (W: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) Y) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv A2 (ccur F))) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Extend class notation with undefined value function.
term cund: class;

--| Define the undefined value function, whose value at set ` s ` is guaranteed not to be a member of ` s ` (see ~ pwuninel ). (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2011.)
axiom df_undef {s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cund (cmpt s1 cvv (cpw (cuni (cv s1)))) $;

--| Proof of ~ pwuninel under the assumption that the union of the given class is a set, avoiding ~ ax-pr and ~ ax-un . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem pwuninel2 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cuni A2) V) (wn (wcel (cpw (cuni A2)) A2)) $;

--| The powerclass of the union of a class does not belong to that class. This theorem provides a way of constructing a new set that does not belong to a given set. See also ~ pwuninel2 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-pr and ~ ax-un . (Revised by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem pwuninel (A2: class):
  $ wn (wcel (cpw (cuni A2)) A2) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ pwuninel as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pwuninelOLD (A2: class):
  $ wn (wcel (cpw (cuni A2)) A2) $;

--| Value of the undefined value function. Normally we will not reference the explicit value but will use ~ undefnel instead. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem undefval (S V: class):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq (cfv S cund) (cpw (cuni S))) $;

--| The undefined value generated from a set is not a member of the set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2011.)
theorem undefnel2 (S V: class):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wn (wcel (cfv S cund) S)) $;

--| The undefined value generated from a set is not a member of the set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2011.)
theorem undefnel (S V: class):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wnel (cfv S cund) S) $;

--| The undefined value generated from a set is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2018.)
theorem undefne0 (S V: class):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wne (cfv S cund) c0) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the well-founded recursion generator. See ~ df-frecs .
term cfrecs (A2 R F: class): class;

--| This is the definition for the well-founded recursion generator. Similar to ~ df-wrecs and ~ df-recs , it is a direct definition form of normally recursive relationships. Unlike the former two definitions, it only requires a well-founded set-like relationship for its properties, not a well-ordered relationship. This proof requires either a partial order or the axiom of infinity. We develop the theorems twice, once with a partial order and once without. The second development occurs later in the database, after ~ ax-inf has been introduced. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Dec-2021.)
axiom df_frecs {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq (cfrecs A2 R F) (cuni (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) F)) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the well-founded recursion generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Dec-2021.)
theorem frecseq123 (A2 B2 R S F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq R S) (wceq A2 B2) (wceq F G)) (wceq (cfrecs A2 R F) (cfrecs B2 S G)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the well-founded recursion generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Dec-2021.)
theorem nffrecs {x3: setvar} (A2 R F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cfrecs A2 R F) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of the well-founded recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem csbfrecsg {x3: setvar} (A2 D R F V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (cfrecs D R F)) (cfrecs (csb x3 A2 D) (csb x3 A2 R) (csb x3 A2 F))) $;

--| Functions defined by well-founded recursion over a partial order are identical up to relation, domain, and characteristic function. This version of ~ frr3g does not require infinity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem fpr3g {y1: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn F A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2)) (wa (wfn G A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cres G (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2))) (wceq F G) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. The final item we are interested in is the union of acceptable functions ` B ` . This lemma just changes bound variables for later use. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2012.)
theorem frrlem1 {x3 y1 z w f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z w f2 g2) (R G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab g2 (wex z (w3a (wfn (cv g2) (cv z)) (wa (wss (cv z) A2) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) (cv z)) (cv z))) (wral w (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv g2)) (co (cv w) (cres (cv g2) (cpred A2 R (cv w))) G)) (cv z))))) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. An acceptable function is a function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2012.)
theorem frrlem2 {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (R G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wfun (cv g2)) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. An acceptable function's domain is a subset of ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2012.)
theorem frrlem3 {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (R G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wss (cdm (cv g2)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Properties of the restriction of an acceptable function to the domain of another acceptable function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2012.)
theorem frrlem4 {x3 y1 f2 g2 h a: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2 g2 h) (R G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2)) (wa (wfn (cres (cv g2) (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h)))) (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h)))) (wral a (wceq (cfv (cv a) (cres (cv g2) (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h))))) (co (cv a) (cres (cres (cv g2) (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h)))) (cpred (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h))) R (cv a))) G)) (cin (cdm (cv g2)) (cdm (cv h))))) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. State the well-founded recursion generator in terms of the acceptable functions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Aug-2022.)
theorem frrlem5 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1 f2) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wceq F (cuni B2) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. The well-founded recursion generator is a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Aug-2022.)
theorem frrlem6 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1 f2) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wrel F $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. The well-founded recursion generator's domain is a subclass of ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Aug-2022.)
theorem frrlem7 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 f2) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1 f2) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) A2 $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. ` dom F ` is closed under predecessor classes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem8 {x3 y1 z f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z f2) (R: class) (F: class x3 z f2) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) (cdm F)) (wss (cpred A2 R (cv z)) (cdm F)) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Show that the well-founded recursive generator produces a function. Hypothesis three will be eliminated using different induction rules depending on if we use partial orders or the axiom of infinity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Aug-2022.)
theorem frrlem9 {x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class v u g2 h) (B2: class x3 y1 v u f2) (R: class v u g2 h) (F: class y1 g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Under the compatibility hypothesis, compute the value of ` F ` within its domain. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem10 {x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class v u g2 h) (B2: class x3 y1 v u f2) (R: class v u g2 h) (F: class y1 g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) (cdm F))) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. For the next several theorems we will be aiming to prove that ` dom F = A ` . To do this, we set up a function ` C ` that supposedly contains an element of ` A ` that is not in ` dom F ` and we show that the element must be in ` dom F ` . Our choice of what to restrict ` F ` to depends on if we assume partial orders or the axiom of infinity. To begin with, we establish the functionality of ` C ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem11 {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class v u g2 h) (B2: class x3 y1 v u f2) (C: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (R: class v u g2 h) (S: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (F: class y1 g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (cres F S) (csn (cop (cv z) (co (cv z) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) (cdif A2 (cdm F)))) (wfn C (cun (cin S (cdm F)) (csn (cv z)))) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Next, we calculate the value of ` C ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem12 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1 w) (A2: class v u g2) (B2: class y1 w v u f2) (C: class x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 h) (R: class v u g2 h) (S: class x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 h) (F: class w g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (cres F S) (csn (cop (cv z) (co (cv z) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wss (cpred A2 R (cv z)) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) S) S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv z) (cdif A2 (cdm F))) (wcel (cv w) (cun (cin S (cdm F)) (csn (cv z))))) (wceq (cfv (cv w) C) (co (cv w) (cres C (cpred A2 R (cv w))) G)) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Assuming that ` S ` is a subset of ` A ` and that ` z ` is ` R ` -minimal, then ` C ` is an acceptable function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem13 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class v u g2) (B2: class y1 w v u f2) (C: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (R: class v u g2 h) (S: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (F: class g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (cres F S) (csn (cop (cv z) (co (cv z) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wss (cpred A2 R (cv z)) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) S) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wss S A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv z) (cdif A2 (cdm F))) (wceq (cpred (cdif A2 (cdm F)) R (cv z)) c0))) (wcel C B2) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion. Finally, we tie all these threads together and show that ` dom F = A ` when given the right ` S ` . Specifically, we prove that there can be no ` R ` -minimal element of ` ( A \ dom F ) ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Dec-2022.)
theorem frrlem14 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class v u g2) (B2: class y1 w v u f2) (C: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (R: class v u g2 h) (S: class x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 h) (F: class g2 h) (G: class v u g2 h):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (cres F S) (csn (cop (cv z) (co (cv z) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfr A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wss (cpred A2 R (cv z)) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) S) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wss S A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wne (cdif A2 (cdm F)) c0)) (wrex z (wceq (cpred (cdif A2 (cdm F)) R (cv z)) c0) (cdif A2 (cdm F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm F) A2) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion with a partial order. Two acceptable functions are compatible. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem fprlem1 {x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $;

--| Lemma for well-founded recursion with a partial order. Establish a subset relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem fprlem2 {z w: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) (cpred A2 R (cv z))) (cpred A2 R (cv z))) $;

--| Weak version of ~ fpr2 which is useful for proofs that avoid the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem fpr2a (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X (cdm F))) (wceq (cfv X F) (co X (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) G)) $;

--| Law of well-founded recursion over a partial order, part one. Establish the functionality and domain of the recursive function generator. Note that by requiring a partial order we can avoid using the axiom of infinity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem fpr1 (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wfn F A2) $;

--| Law of well-founded recursion over a partial order, part two. Now we establish the value of ` F ` within ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem fpr2 (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (co X (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) G)) $;

--| Law of well-founded recursion over a partial order, part three. Finally, we show that ` F ` is unique. We do this by showing that any function ` H ` with the same properties we proved of ` F ` in ~ fpr1 and ~ fpr2 is identical to ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem fpr3 {z: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn H A2) (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) H) (co (cv z) (cres H (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)) A2))) (wceq F H) $;

--| Show without using the axiom of replacement that the well-founded recursion generator gives a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem frrrel (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wrel F $;

--| Show without using the axiom of replacement that the domain of the well-founded recursion generator is a subclass of ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem frrdmss (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) A2 $;

--| Show without using the axiom of replacement that for a "function" defined by well-founded recursion, the predecessor class of an element of its domain is a subclass of its domain. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem frrdmcl (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cdm F)) (wss (cpred A2 R X) (cdm F)) $;

--| A "function" defined by well-founded recursion is indeed a function when the relation is a partial order. Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem fprfung (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wfun F) $;

--| The restriction of a function defined by well-founded recursion to the predecessor of an element of its domain is a set. Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem fprresex (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfr A2 R) (wpo A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X (cdm F))) (wcel (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) cvv) $;

--| Declare syntax for the well-ordered recursive function generator.
term cwrecs (A2 R F: class): class;

--| Define the well-ordered recursive function generator. This function takes the usual expressions from recursion theorems and forms a unified definition. Specifically, given a function ` F ` , a relation ` R ` , and a base set ` A ` , this definition generates a function ` G = wrecs ( R , A , F ) ` that has property that, at any point ` x e. A ` , ` ( G `` x ) = ( F `` ( G |`` Pred ( R , A , x ) ) ) ` . See ~ wfr1 , ~ wfr2 , and ~ wfr3 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2018.) (Revised by BJ, 27-Oct-2024.)
axiom df_wrecs (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq (cwrecs A2 R F) (cfrecs A2 R (ccom F c2nd)) $;

--| General equality theorem for the well-ordered recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem wrecseq123 (A2 B2 R S F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq R S) (wceq A2 B2) (wceq F G)) (wceq (cwrecs A2 R F) (cwrecs B2 S G)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the well-ordered recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem nfwrecs {x3: setvar} (A2 R F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cwrecs A2 R F) $;

--| Equality theorem for the well-ordered recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2018.)
theorem wrecseq1 (A2 R S F: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cwrecs A2 R F) (cwrecs A2 S F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the well-ordered recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2018.)
theorem wrecseq2 (A2 B2 R F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cwrecs A2 R F) (cwrecs B2 R F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the well-ordered recursive function generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2018.)
theorem wrecseq3 (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (cwrecs A2 R F) (cwrecs A2 R G)) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of the well-founded recursive function generator. (Contributed by ML, 25-Oct-2020.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem csbwrecsg {x3: setvar} (A2 D R F V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (cwrecs D R F)) (cwrecs (csb x3 A2 D) (csb x3 A2 R) (csb x3 A2 F))) $;

--| Functions defined by well-ordered recursion are identical up to relation, domain, and characteristic function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Feb-2011.)
theorem wfr3g {y1: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn F A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2)) (wa (wfn G A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) G) (cfv (cres G (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2))) (wceq F G) $;

--| The well-ordered recursion generator generates a relation. Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfrrel (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wrel F $;

--| The domain of the well-ordered recursion generator is a subclass of ` A ` . Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfrdmss (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) A2 $;

--| The predecessor class of an element of the well-ordered recursion generator's domain is a subset of its domain. Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfrdmcl (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cdm F)) (wss (cpred A2 R X) (cdm F)) $;

--| The "function" generated by the well-ordered recursion generator is indeed a function. Avoids the axiom of replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 17-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfrfun (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wfun F) $;

--| Show without using the axiom of replacement that the restriction of the well-ordered recursion generator to a predecessor class is a set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfrresex (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X (cdm F))) (wcel (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) cvv) $;

--| A weak version of ~ wfr2 which is useful for proofs that avoid the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfr2a (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X (cdm F))) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) G)) $;

--| The Principle of Well-Ordered Recursion, part 1 of 3. We start with an arbitrary function ` G ` . Then, using a base class ` A ` and a set-like well-ordering ` R ` of ` A ` , we define a function ` F ` . This function is said to be defined by "well-ordered recursion". The purpose of these three theorems is to demonstrate the properties of ` F ` . We begin by showing that ` F ` is a function over ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfr1 (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wfn F A2) $;

--| The Principle of Well-Ordered Recursion, part 2 of 3. Next, we show that the value of ` F ` at any ` X e. A ` is ` G ` applied to all "previous" values of ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem wfr2 (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) G)) $;

--| The principle of Well-Ordered Recursion, part 3 of 3. Finally, we show that ` F ` is unique. We do this by showing that any function ` H ` with the same properties we proved of ` F ` in ~ wfr1 and ~ wfr2 is identical to ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem wfr3 {z: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wceq F (cwrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn H A2) (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) H) (cfv (cres H (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)) A2))) (wceq F H) $;

--| The indexed union of a set of ordinal numbers ` B ( x ) ` is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.)
theorem iunon {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel B2 con0) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) con0) $;

--| The nonempty indexed intersection of a class of ordinal numbers ` B ( x ) ` is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.)
theorem iinon {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wral x3 (wcel B2 con0) A2) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) con0) $;

--| A property of functions on ordinal numbers. Generalization of Theorem Schema 8E of [Enderton] p. 218. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.)
theorem onfununi {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class) (T: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (ciun x3 (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wss (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S T) (wss S con0) (wne S c0)) (wceq (cfv (cuni S) F) (ciun x3 S (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| A variant of ~ onfununi for operations. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem onovuni {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 S: class) (T: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wceq (co A2 (cv y1) F) (ciun x3 (cv y1) (co A2 (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wss (co A2 (cv x3) F) (co A2 (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S T) (wss S con0) (wne S c0)) (wceq (co A2 (cuni S) F) (ciun x3 S (co A2 (cv x3) F))) $;

--| A variant of ~ onovuni with indexed unions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.)
theorem onoviun {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (T: class x3 y1 z) (F K: class) (L: class z):
  $ wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wceq (co A2 (cv y1) F) (ciun x3 (cv y1) (co A2 (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wcel (cv y1) con0) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wss (co A2 (cv x3) F) (co A2 (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K T) (wral z (wcel L con0) K) (wne K c0)) (wceq (co A2 (ciun z K L) F) (ciun z K (co A2 L F))) $;

--| There are no length ` _om ` decreasing sequences in the ordinals. See also ~ noinfep for a stronger version assuming Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-May-2015.)
theorem onnseq {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cfv c0 F) con0) (wrex x3 (wn (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F))) com) $;

--| Introduce the strictly monotone ordinal function. A strictly monotone function is one that is constantly increasing across the ordinals.
term wsmo (A2: class): wff;

--| Definition of a strictly monotone ordinal function. Definition 7.46 in [TakeutiZaring] p. 50. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Nov-2011.)
axiom df_smo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wsmo A2) (w3a (wf (cdm A2) con0 A2) (word (cdm A2)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) A2) (cfv (cv y1) A2))) (cdm A2)) (cdm A2))) $;

--| Alternate definition of a strictly monotone ordinal function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Mar-2013.)
theorem dfsmo2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wb (wsmo F) (w3a (wf (cdm F) con0 F) (word (cdm F)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (cv x3)) (cdm F))) $;

--| Conditions for which ` A ` is a strictly monotone ordinal function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Nov-2011.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 19-May-2023.)
theorem issmo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wf B2 con0 A2 $ >
  $ word B2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) A2) (cfv (cv y1) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm A2) B2 $ >
  $ wsmo A2 $;

--| Alternate definition of a strictly monotone ordinal function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2013.)
theorem issmo2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wi (w3a (wss B2 con0) (word A2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (cv x3)) A2)) (wsmo F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for strictly monotone functions. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Nov-2011.)
theorem smoeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wsmo A2) (wsmo B2)) $;

--| The domain of a strictly monotone function is an ordinal. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Nov-2011.)
theorem smodm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wsmo A2) (word (cdm A2)) $;

--| A strictly monotone function restricted to an ordinal remains strictly monotone. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2016.)
theorem smores (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wsmo A2) (wcel B2 (cdm A2))) (wsmo (cres A2 B2)) $;

--| A strictly monotone function restricted to an ordinal remains strictly monotone. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem smores3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wsmo (cres A2 B2)) (wcel C (cin (cdm A2) B2)) (word B2)) (wsmo (cres A2 C)) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function restricted to an ordinal is still monotone. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Mar-2013.)
theorem smores2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wsmo F) (word A2)) (wsmo (cres F A2)) $;

--| The domain of a strictly monotone ordinal function is an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2013.)
theorem smodm2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wsmo F)) (word A2) $;

--| The function values of a strictly monotone ordinal function are ordinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2013.)
theorem smofvon2 (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wsmo F) (wcel (cfv B2 F) con0) $;

--| The identity relation restricted to the ordinals is a strictly monotone function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Nov-2011.)
theorem iordsmo (A2: class):
  $ word A2 $ >
  $ wsmo (cres cid A2) $;

--| The empty set is a strictly monotone ordinal function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 20-Nov-2011.)
theorem smo0:
  $ wsmo c0 $;

--| If ` B ` is a strictly monotone ordinal function, and ` A ` is in the domain of ` B ` , then the value of the function at ` A ` is an ordinal. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 20-Nov-2011.)
theorem smofvon (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wsmo B2) (wcel A2 (cdm B2))) (wcel (cfv A2 B2) con0) $;

--| If ` x ` is less than ` y ` then a strictly monotone function's value will be strictly less at ` x ` than at ` y ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 22-Nov-2011.)
theorem smoel (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wsmo B2) (wcel A2 (cdm B2)) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cfv C B2) (cfv A2 B2)) $;

--| The value of a strictly monotone ordinal function contains its indexed union. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 22-Nov-2011.)
theorem smoiun {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wsmo B2) (wcel A2 (cdm B2))) (wss (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) B2)) (cfv A2 B2)) $;

--| If ` F ` is an isomorphism from an ordinal ` A ` onto ` B ` , which is a subset of the ordinals, then ` F ` is a strictly monotonic function. Exercise 3 in [TakeutiZaring] p. 50. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-Nov-2011.)
theorem smoiso (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wiso A2 B2 cep cep F) (word A2) (wss B2 con0)) (wsmo F) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function preserves the membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2013.)
theorem smoel2 (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfn F A2) (wsmo F)) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C B2))) (wcel (cfv C F) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem smo11 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wsmo F)) (wf1 A2 B2 F) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function preserves strict ordering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Mar-2013.)
theorem smoord (A2 C D F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfn F A2) (wsmo F)) (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wb (wcel C D) (wcel (cfv C F) (cfv D F))) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function preserves weak ordering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Mar-2013.)
theorem smoword (A2 C D F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfn F A2) (wsmo F)) (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wb (wss C D) (wss (cfv C F) (cfv D F))) $;

--| A strictly monotone ordinal function is greater than or equal to its argument. Exercise 1 in [TakeutiZaring] p. 50. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 23-Nov-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem smogt (A2 C F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wsmo F) (wcel C A2)) (wss C (cfv C F)) $;

--| The codomain of a strictly monotone ordinal function dominates the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2013.)
theorem smocdmdom (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wsmo F) (word B2)) (wss A2 B2) $;

--| The strictly monotone ordinal functions are also isomorphisms of subclasses of ` On ` equipped with the membership relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.)
theorem smoiso2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (word A2) (wss B2 con0)) (wb (wa (wfo A2 B2 F) (wsmo F)) (wiso A2 B2 cep cep F)) $;

--| Notation for a function defined by strong transfinite recursion.
term crecs (F: class): class;

--| Define a function ` recs ( F ) ` on ` On ` , the class of ordinal numbers, by transfinite recursion given a rule ` F ` which sets the next value given all values so far. See ~ df-rdg for more details on why this definition is desirable. Unlike ~ df-rdg which restricts the update rule to use only the previous value, this version allows the update rule to use _all_ previous values, which is why it is described as "strong", although it is actually more primitive. See ~ recsfnon and ~ recsval for the primary contract of this definition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2020.)
axiom df_recs (F: class):
  $ wceq (crecs F) (cwrecs con0 cep F) $;

--| The old definition of transfinite recursion. This version is preferred for development, as it demonstrates the properties of transfinite recursion without relying on well-ordered recursion. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2020.) (Proof revised by Scott Fenton, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem dfrecs3 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (crecs F) (cuni (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0))) $;

--| Equality theorem for ` recs ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem recseq (F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (crecs F) (crecs G)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for ` recs ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem nfrecs {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (crecs F) $;

--| A technical lemma for transfinite recursion. Compare Lemma 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 47. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2019.)
theorem tfrlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wfun F) (wss A2 (cdm F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wfun G) (wss A2 (cdm G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cres F (cv x3)) B2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cres G (cv x3)) B2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Let ` A ` be the class of "acceptable" functions. The final thing we're interested in is the union of all these acceptable functions. This lemma just changes some bound variables in ` A ` for later use. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.)
theorem tfrlem3a {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z w f2) (F G: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wcel G cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel G A2) (wrex z (wa (wfn G (cv z)) (wral w (wceq (cfv (cv w) G) (cfv (cres G (cv w)) F)) (cv z))) con0) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Let ` A ` be the class of "acceptable" functions. The final thing we're interested in is the union of all these acceptable functions. This lemma just changes some bound variables in ` A ` for later use. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.)
theorem tfrlem3 {x3 y1 z w f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z w f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab g2 (wrex z (wa (wfn (cv g2) (cv z)) (wral w (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv g2)) (cfv (cres (cv g2) (cv w)) F)) (cv z))) con0)) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. ` A ` is the class of all "acceptable" functions, and ` F ` is their union. First we show that an acceptable function is in fact a function. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.)
theorem tfrlem4 {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv g2) A2) (wfun (cv g2)) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. The values of two acceptable functions are the same within their domains. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2019.)
theorem tfrlem5 {x3 y1 f2 g2 h: setvar} (v u: setvar) (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv g2) A2) (wcel (cv h) A2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. The definition ` recs ` is the union of all acceptable functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem recsfval {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq (crecs F) (cuni A2) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. The union of all acceptable functions is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 8-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-nul , ~ ax-pr , ~ ax-sep and ~ ax-un . (Revised by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem tfrlem6 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wrel (crecs F) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ tfrlem6 as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by NM, 8-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tfrlem6OLD {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wrel (crecs F) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. The union of all acceptable functions is a function. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2019.)
theorem tfrlem7 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wfun (crecs F) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. The domain of ` recs ` is an ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Alan Sare, 11-Mar-2008.)
theorem tfrlem8 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ word (cdm (crecs F)) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Here we compute the value of ` recs ` (the union of all acceptable functions). (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-1994.)
theorem tfrlem9 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (B2 F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (crecs F))) (wceq (cfv B2 (crecs F)) (cfv (cres (crecs F) B2) F)) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Without using ~ ax-rep , show that all the restrictions of ` recs ` are sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem tfrlem9a {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (B2 F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (crecs F))) (wcel (cres (crecs F) B2) cvv) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. We define class ` C ` by extending ` recs ` with one ordered pair. We will assume, falsely, that domain of ` recs ` is a member of, and thus not equal to, ` On ` . Using this assumption we will prove facts about ` C ` that will lead to a contradiction in ~ tfrlem14 , thus showing the domain of ` recs ` does in fact equal ` On ` . Here we show (under the false assumption) that ` C ` is a function extending the domain of ` recs ( F ) ` by one. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem tfrlem10 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (crecs F) (csn (cop (cdm (crecs F)) (cfv (crecs F) F)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cdm (crecs F)) con0) (wfn C (csuc (cdm (crecs F)))) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Compute the value of ` C ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem tfrlem11 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (B2 C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (crecs F) (csn (cop (cdm (crecs F)) (cfv (crecs F) F)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cdm (crecs F)) con0) (wi (wcel B2 (csuc (cdm (crecs F)))) (wceq (cfv B2 C) (cfv (cres C B2) F))) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Show ` C ` is an acceptable function. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem tfrlem12 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun (crecs F) (csn (cop (cdm (crecs F)) (cfv (crecs F) F)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (crecs F) cvv) (wcel C A2) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. If ` recs ` is a set function, then ` C ` is acceptable, and thus a subset of ` recs ` , but ` dom C ` is bigger than ` dom recs ` . This is a contradiction, so ` recs ` must be a proper class function. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem tfrlem13 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wn (wcel (crecs F) cvv) $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Assuming ~ ax-rep , ` dom recs e. _V <-> recs e. _V ` , so since ` dom recs ` is an ordinal, it must be equal to ` On ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem tfrlem14 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm (crecs F)) con0 $;

--| Lemma for transfinite recursion. Without assuming ~ ax-rep , we can show that all proper initial subsets of ` recs ` are sets, while nothing larger is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem tfrlem15 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (B2 F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wcel B2 (cdm (crecs F))) (wcel (cres (crecs F) B2) cvv)) $;

--| Lemma for finite recursion. Without assuming ~ ax-rep , we can show that the domain of the constructed function is a limit ordinal, and hence contains all the finite ordinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem tfrlem16 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 f2) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) F)) (cv x3))) con0)) $ >
  $ wlim (cdm (crecs F)) $;

--| A weak version of ~ tfr1 which is useful for proofs that avoid the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem tfr1a (F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wa (wfun F) (wlim (cdm F)) $;

--| A weak version of ~ tfr2 which is useful for proofs that avoid the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem tfr2a (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv (cres F A2) G)) $;

--| Without assuming ~ ax-rep , we can show that all proper initial subsets of ` recs ` are sets, while nothing larger is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem tfr2b (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wcel (cres F A2) cvv)) $;

--| Principle of Transfinite Recursion, part 1 of 3. Theorem 7.41(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 47. We start with an arbitrary class ` G ` , normally a function, and define a class ` A ` of all "acceptable" functions. The final function we're interested in is the union ` F = recs ( G ) ` of them. ` F ` is then said to be defined by transfinite recursion. The purpose of the 3 parts of this theorem is to demonstrate properties of ` F ` . In this first part we show that ` F ` is a function whose domain is all ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem tfr1 (F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wfn F con0 $;

--| Principle of Transfinite Recursion, part 2 of 3. Theorem 7.41(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 47. Here we show that the function ` F ` has the property that for any function ` G ` whatsoever, the "next" value of ` F ` is ` G ` recursively applied to all "previous" values of ` F ` . (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem tfr2 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv (cres F A2) G)) $;

--| Principle of Transfinite Recursion, part 3 of 3. Theorem 7.41(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 47. Finally, we show that ` F ` is unique. We do this by showing that any class ` B ` with the same properties of ` F ` that we showed in parts 1 and 2 is identical to ` F ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem tfr3 {x3: setvar} (B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn B2 con0) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) B2) (cfv (cres B2 (cv x3)) G)) con0)) (wceq B2 F) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ tfr1 using well-ordered recursion. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem tfr1ALT (F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wfn F con0 $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ tfr2 using well-ordered recursion. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem tfr2ALT (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv (cres F A2) G)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ tfr3 using well-ordered recursion. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem tfr3ALT {x3: setvar} (B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn B2 con0) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) B2) (cfv (cres B2 (cv x3)) G)) con0)) (wceq B2 F) $;

--| Strong transfinite recursion defines a function on ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem recsfnon (F: class):
  $ wfn (crecs F) con0 $;

--| Strong transfinite recursion in terms of all previous values. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem recsval (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 (crecs F)) (cfv (cres (crecs F) A2) F)) $;

--| The ordered pair abstraction ` G ` defined in the hypothesis is a function. This was a lemma for ~ tz7.44-1 , ~ tz7.44-2 , and ~ tz7.44-3 when they used that definition of ` G ` . Now, they use the maps-to ~ df-mpt idiom so this lemma is not needed anymore, but is kept in case other applications (for instance in intuitionistic set theory) need it. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.)
theorem tz7_44lem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (G: class x3 y1) (H: class x3):
  $ wceq G (copab x3 y1 (w3o (wa (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (wa (wn (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wlim (cdm (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3)) H))) (wa (wlim (cdm (cv x3))) (wceq (cv y1) (cuni (crn (cv x3))))))) $ >
  $ wfun G $;

--| The value of ` F ` at ` (/) ` . Part 1 of Theorem 7.44 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem tz7_44_1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class y1) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) A2 (cif (wlim (cdm (cv x3))) (cuni (crn (cv x3))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3)) H)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) X) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cres F (cv y1)) G)) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel c0 X) (wceq (cfv c0 F) A2) $;

--| The value of ` F ` at a successor ordinal. Part 2 of Theorem 7.44 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) Remove unnecessary distinct variable conditions. (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem tz7_44_21 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class y1) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) A2 (cif (wlim (cdm (cv x3))) (cuni (crn (cv x3))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3)) H)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) X) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cres F (cv y1)) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cres F (cv y1)) cvv) $ >
  $ wfn F X $ >
  $ word X $ >
  $ wi (wcel (csuc B2) X) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) (cfv (cfv B2 F) H)) $;

--| The value of ` F ` at a limit ordinal. Part 3 of Theorem 7.44 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 19-Jun-2012.)
theorem tz7_44_31 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class y1) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) A2 (cif (wlim (cdm (cv x3))) (cuni (crn (cv x3))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3)) H)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) X) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cres F (cv y1)) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cres F (cv y1)) cvv) $ >
  $ wfn F X $ >
  $ word X $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 X) (wlim B2)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cuni (cima F B2))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the recursive definition generator, with characteristic function ` F ` and initial value ` I ` .
term crdg (F I: class): class;

--| Define a recursive definition generator on ` On ` (the class of ordinal numbers) with characteristic function ` F ` and initial value ` I ` . This combines functions ` F ` in ~ tfr1 and ` G ` in ~ tz7.44-1 into one definition. This rather amazing operation allows to define, with compact direct definitions, functions that are usually defined in textbooks only with indirect self-referencing recursive definitions. A recursive definition requires advanced metalogic to justify - in particular, eliminating a recursive definition is very difficult and often not even shown in textbooks. On the other hand, the elimination of a direct definition is a matter of simple mechanical substitution. The price paid is the daunting complexity of our ` rec ` operation (especially when ~ df-recs that it is built on is also eliminated). But once we get past this hurdle, definitions that would otherwise be recursive become relatively simple, as in for example ~ oav , from which we prove the recursive textbook definition as Theorems ~ oa0 , ~ oasuc , and ~ oalim (with the help of Theorems ~ rdg0 , ~ rdgsuc , and ~ rdglim2a ). We can also restrict the ` rec ` operation to define otherwise recursive functions on the natural numbers ` _om ` ; see ~ fr0g and ~ frsuc . Our ` rec ` operation apparently does not appear in published literature, although closely related is Definition 25.2 of [Quine] p. 177, which he uses to "turn...a recursion into a genuine or direct definition" (p. 174). Note that the ` if ` operations (see ~ df-if ) select cases based on whether the domain of ` g ` is zero, a successor, or a limit ordinal. An important use of this definition is in the recursive sequence generator ~ df-seq on the natural numbers (as a subset of the complex numbers), allowing us to define, with direct definitions, recursive infinite sequences such as the factorial function ~ df-fac and integer powers ~ df-exp . _Note: We introduce_ ` rec ` _with the philosophical goal of being_ _able to eliminate all definitions with direct mechanical substitution_ _and to verify easily the soundness of definitions. Metamath itself_ _has no built-in technical limitation that prevents multiple-part_ _recursive definitions in the traditional textbook style_. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
axiom df_rdg {g2: setvar} (F I: class):
  $ wceq (crdg F I) (crecs (cmpt g2 cvv (cif (wceq (cv g2) c0) I (cif (wlim (cdm (cv g2))) (cuni (crn (cv g2))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv g2))) (cv g2)) F))))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem rdgeq1 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (crdg F A2) (crdg G A2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem rdgeq2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (crdg F A2) (crdg F B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Apr-2012.)
theorem rdgeq12 (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq F G) (wceq A2 B2)) (wceq (crdg F A2) (crdg G B2)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem nfrdg {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (crdg F A2) $;

--| Lemma used with the recursive definition generator. This is a trivial lemma that just changes bound variables for later use. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.)
theorem rdglem1 {x3 y1 z w f2 g2: setvar} (G: class):
  $ wceq (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) G)) (cv x3))) con0)) (cab g2 (wrex z (wa (wfn (cv g2) (cv z)) (wral w (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv g2)) (cfv (cres (cv g2) (cv w)) G)) (cv z))) con0)) $;

--| Lemma used with the recursive definition generator. This is a trivial lemma that just changes bound variables for later use. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.)
theorem rdglem1_b {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (G: class):
  $ wceq (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) G)) (cv x3))) con0)) (cab g2 (wrex z (wa (wfn (cv g2) (cv z)) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cres (cv g2) (cv x3)) G)) (cv z))) con0)) $;

--| The recursive definition generator is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdgfun (A2 F: class):
  $ wfun (crdg F A2) $;

--| The domain of the recursive definition generator is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdgdmlim (A2 F: class):
  $ wlim (cdm (crdg F A2)) $;

--| The recursive definition generator is a function on ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem rdgfnon (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn (crdg F A2) con0 $;

--| Value of the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem rdgvalg {g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (crdg F A2))) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (cfv (cres (crdg F A2) B2) (cmpt g2 cvv (cif (wceq (cv g2) c0) A2 (cif (wlim (cdm (cv g2))) (cuni (crn (cv g2))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv g2))) (cv g2)) F)))))) $;

--| Value of the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem rdgval {g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (cfv (cres (crdg F A2) B2) (cmpt g2 cvv (cif (wceq (cv g2) c0) A2 (cif (wlim (cdm (cv g2))) (cuni (crn (cv g2))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv g2))) (cv g2)) F)))))) $;

--| The initial value of the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdg0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 (crdg F A2)) A2 $;

--| The initial segments of the recursive definition generator are sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdgseg (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (crdg F A2))) (wcel (cres (crdg F A2) B2) cvv) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a successor. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdgsucg (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (crdg F A2))) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (crdg F A2)) (cfv (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) F)) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a successor. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdgsuc (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (crdg F A2)) (cfv (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) F)) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdglimg (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdm (crdg F A2))) (wlim B2)) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (cuni (cima (crdg F A2) B2))) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem rdglim (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2)) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (cuni (cima (crdg F A2) B2))) $;

--| The initial value of the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-1995.)
theorem rdg0g (A2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) (wceq (cfv c0 (crdg F A2)) A2) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a successor (special case where the characteristic function uses the map operation). (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem rdgsucmptf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel D V)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) D) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a successor (special case where the characteristic function is an ordered-pair class abstraction and where the mapping class ` D ` is a proper class). This is a technical lemma that can be used together with ~ rdgsucmptf to help eliminate redundant sethood antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem rdgsucmptnf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel D cvv)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) c0) $;

--| This version of ~ rdgsucmpt avoids the not-free hypothesis of ~ rdgsucmptf by using two substitutions instead of one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem rdgsucmpt2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (E: class y1) (F V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wceq E C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq E D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel D V)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) D) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a successor (special case where the characteristic function uses the map operation). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2013.)
theorem rdgsucmpt {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel D V)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) D) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a limit ordinal, in terms of the union of all smaller values. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.)
theorem rdglim2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2)) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (cuni (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (crdg F A2))) B2)))) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at a limit ordinal, in terms of indexed union of all smaller values. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jun-1998.)
theorem rdglim2a {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2)) (wceq (cfv B2 (crdg F A2)) (ciun x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) (crdg F A2)))) $;

--| If ` A ` is a proper class, then the recursive function generator at ` (/) ` is the empty set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem rdg0n (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (cfv c0 (crdg F A2)) c0) $;

--| The function generated by finite recursive definition generation is a function on omega. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem frfnom (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn (cres (crdg F A2) com) com $;

--| The initial value resulting from finite recursive definition generation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-1996.)
theorem fr0g (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wceq (cfv c0 (cres (crdg F A2) com)) A2) $;

--| The successor value resulting from finite recursive definition generation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem frsuc (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (cres (crdg F A2) com)) (cfv (cfv B2 (cres (crdg F A2) com)) F)) $;

--| The successor value resulting from finite recursive definition generation (special case where the generation function is expressed in maps-to notation). (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 2-Nov-2011.)
theorem frsucmpt {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) com) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 com) (wcel D V)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) D) $;

--| The value of the finite recursive definition generator at a successor (special case where the characteristic function is a mapping abstraction and where the mapping class ` D ` is a proper class). This is a technical lemma that can be used together with ~ frsucmpt to help eliminate redundant sethood antecedents. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem frsucmptn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) com) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel D cvv)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) c0) $;

--| The successor value resulting from finite recursive definition generation (special case where the generation function is expressed in maps-to notation), using double-substitution instead of a bound variable condition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem frsucmpt2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (E: class y1) (F V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) com) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wceq E C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq E D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 com) (wcel D V)) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) F) D) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a function on ordinals to be one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.) Extract from ~ tz7.48lem and generalize statement. (Revised by Matthew House, 6-Sep-2026.)
theorem onelfvnef1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wss A2 con0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) A2) A2)) (wf1 A2 B2 F) $;

--| A way of showing an ordinal function is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.) Extract ~ onelfvnef1 . (Proof shortened by Matthew House, 6-Sep-2026.)
theorem tz7_48lem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wn (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cv x3)) A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cres F A2))) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ tz7.48lem as of 6-Sep-2026. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tz7_48lemOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wn (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cv x3)) A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cres F A2))) $;

--| Proposition 7.48(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 51. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 5-May-2013.)
theorem tz7_48_2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3)))) con0) (wfun (ccnv F)) $;

--| Proposition 7.48(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 51. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.)
theorem tz7_48_11 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3)))) con0) (wss (crn F) A2) $;

--| Proposition 7.48(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 51. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1997.)
theorem tz7_48_3 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3)))) con0) (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) $;

--| Proposition 7.49 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 51. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2013.)
theorem tz7_49 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wb ph (wral x3 (wi (wne (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3))) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3))))) con0) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) ph) (wrex x3 (w3a (wral y1 (wne (cdif A2 (cima F (cv y1))) c0) (cv x3)) (wceq (cima F (cv x3)) A2) (wfun (ccnv (cres F (cv x3))))) con0) $;

--| Corollary of Proposition 7.49 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 51. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2013.)
theorem tz7_49c {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wfn F con0 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3))) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cdif A2 (cima F (cv x3))))) con0)) (wrex x3 (wf1o (cv x3) A2 (cres F (cv x3))) con0) $;

--| Extend class notation to include index-aware recursive definitions.
term cseqom (F I: class): class;

--| Index-aware recursive definitions over ` _om ` . A mashup of ~ df-rdg and ~ df-seq , this allows for recursive definitions that use an index in the recursion in cases where Infinity is not admitted. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_seqom {v i: setvar} (F I: class):
  $ wceq (cseqom F I) (cima (crdg (cmpo i v com cvv (cop (csuc (cv i)) (co (cv i) (cv v) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) com) $;

--| Lemma for ` seqom ` . Change bound variables. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem seqomlem0 {a b c d: setvar} (F I: class):
  $ wceq (crdg (cmpo a b com cvv (cop (csuc (cv a)) (co (cv a) (cv b) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) (crdg (cmpo c d com cvv (cop (csuc (cv c)) (co (cv c) (cv d) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) $;

--| Lemma for ` seqom ` . The underlying recursion generates a sequence of pairs with the expected first values. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2015.)
theorem seqomlem1 {v i: setvar} (A2 Q F: class) (I: class v i):
  $ wceq Q (crdg (cmpo i v com cvv (cop (csuc (cv i)) (co (cv i) (cv v) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv A2 Q) (cop A2 (cfv (cfv A2 Q) c2nd))) $;

--| Lemma for ` seqom ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2015.)
theorem seqomlem2 {v i: setvar} (Q F: class) (I: class v i):
  $ wceq Q (crdg (cmpo i v com cvv (cop (csuc (cv i)) (co (cv i) (cv v) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) $ >
  $ wfn (cima Q com) com $;

--| Lemma for ` seqom ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem seqomlem3 {v i: setvar} (Q F: class) (I: class v i):
  $ wceq Q (crdg (cmpo i v com cvv (cop (csuc (cv i)) (co (cv i) (cv v) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 (cima Q com)) (cfv I cid) $;

--| Lemma for ` seqom ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2015.)
theorem seqomlem4 {v i: setvar} (A2 Q F: class) (I: class v i):
  $ wceq Q (crdg (cmpo i v com cvv (cop (csuc (cv i)) (co (cv i) (cv v) F))) (cop c0 (cfv I cid))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) (cima Q com)) (co A2 (cfv A2 (cima Q com)) F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for ` seqom ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem seqomeq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wceq (cseqom A2 C) (cseqom B2 D)) $;

--| An index-aware recursive definition defines a function on the natural numbers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fnseqom (F G I: class):
  $ wceq G (cseqom F I) $ >
  $ wfn G com $;

--| Value of an index-aware recursive definition at 0. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem seqom0g (F G I V: class):
  $ wceq G (cseqom F I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv c0 G) I) $;

--| Value of an index-aware recursive definition at a successor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem seqomsuc (A2 F G I: class):
  $ wceq G (cseqom F I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) G) (co A2 (cfv A2 G) F)) $;

--| Membership is inherited by successors for natural numbers. (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2023.)
theorem omsucelsucb (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N com) (wcel (csuc N) (csuc com)) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal number 1.
term c1o: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal number 2.
term c2o: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal number 3.
term c3o: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal number 4.
term c4o: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal addition operation.
term coa: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal multiplication operation.
term comu: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the ordinal exponentiation operation.
term coe: class;

--| Define the ordinal number 1. Definition 2.1 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.)
axiom df_1o:
  $ wceq c1o (csuc c0) $;

--| Define the ordinal number 2. Lemma 3.17 of [Schloeder] p. 10. (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
axiom df_2o:
  $ wceq c2o (csuc c1o) $;

--| Define the ordinal number 3. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_3o:
  $ wceq c3o (csuc c2o) $;

--| Define the ordinal number 4. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_4o:
  $ wceq c4o (csuc c3o) $;

--| Define the ordinal addition operation. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1995.)
axiom df_oadd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq coa (cmpo x3 y1 con0 con0 (cfv (cv y1) (crdg (cmpt z cvv (csuc (cv z))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define the ordinal multiplication operation. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.)
axiom df_omul {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq comu (cmpo x3 y1 con0 con0 (cfv (cv y1) (crdg (cmpt z cvv (co (cv z) (cv x3) coa)) c0))) $;

--| Define the ordinal exponentiation operation. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2004.)
axiom df_oexp {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq coe (cmpo x3 y1 con0 con0 (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cdif c1o (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) (crdg (cmpt z cvv (co (cv z) (cv x3) comu)) c1o)))) $;

--| Expanded value of the ordinal number 1. Definition 2.1 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2002.)
theorem df1o2:
  $ wceq c1o (csn c0) $;

--| Expanded value of the ordinal number 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem df2o3:
  $ wceq c2o (cpr c0 c1o) $;

--| Expanded value of the ordinal number 2. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2004.)
theorem df2o2:
  $ wceq c2o (cpr c0 (csn c0)) $;

--| Ordinal 1 is a set. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Jul-2022.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-un . (Revised by Zhi Wang, 19-Sep-2024.)
theorem _1oex:
  $ wcel c1o cvv $;

--| ` 1o ` is an element of ` { (/) , 1o } ` . (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _1oelpr:
  $ wcel c1o (cpr c0 c1o) $;

--| ` 2o ` is a set. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Apr-2019.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-12 , ~ ax-un . (Proof shortened by Zhi Wang, 19-Sep-2024.)
theorem _2oex:
  $ wcel c2o cvv $;

--| Ordinal 1 is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Nov-2024.)
theorem _1on:
  $ wcel c1o con0 $;

--| Ordinal 2 is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Nov-2024.)
theorem _2on:
  $ wcel c2o con0 $;

--| Ordinal two is not zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem _2on0:
  $ wne c2o c0 $;

--| Ordinal 3 is an ordinal class. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.)
theorem ord3:
  $ word c3o $;

--| Ordinal 3 is an ordinal number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem _3on:
  $ wcel c3o con0 $;

--| Ordinal 4 is an ordinal number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem _4on:
  $ wcel c4o con0 $;

--| Ordinal one is not equal to ordinal zero. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _1n0:
  $ wne c1o c0 $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ 1n0 as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1n0OLD:
  $ wne c1o c0 $;

--| 1 is not a limit ordinal. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem nlim1:
  $ wn (wlim c1o) $;

--| 2 is not a limit ordinal. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem nlim2:
  $ wn (wlim c2o) $;

--| Cartesian products with the singletons of ordinals 0 and 1 are disjoint. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-2007.)
theorem xp01disj (A2 C: class):
  $ wceq (cin (cxp A2 (csn c0)) (cxp C (csn c1o))) c0 $;

--| Cartesian products with the singletons of ordinals 0 and 1 are disjoint. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jul-2023.)
theorem xp01disjl (A2 C: class):
  $ wceq (cin (cxp (csn c0) A2) (cxp (csn c1o) C)) c0 $;

--| Two ways to express that an ordinal class is positive. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem ordgt0ge1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wcel c0 A2) (wss c1o A2)) $;

--| An ordinal greater than or equal to 1 is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem ordge1n0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wss c1o A2) (wne A2 c0)) $;

--| Membership in ordinal one. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2005.)
theorem el1o (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 c1o) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| An ordinal that is not 0 or 1 contains 1. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem ord1eln01 (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wcel c1o A2) (wa (wne A2 c0) (wne A2 c1o))) $;

--| An ordinal that is not 0, 1, or 2 contains 2. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem ord2eln012 (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wcel c2o A2) (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wne A2 c1o) (wne A2 c2o))) $;

--| A limit ordinal contains 1. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem _1ellim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wcel c1o A2) $;

--| A limit ordinal contains 2. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem _2ellim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wcel c2o A2) $;

--| Two ways to say that ` A ` is a nonzero number of the set ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem dif1o (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdif B2 c1o)) (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wne A2 c0)) $;

--| Two ways to say that ` A ` is a nonzero ordinal number. Lemma 1.10 of [Schloeder] p. 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem ondif1 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c1o)) (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel c0 A2)) $;

--| Two ways to say that ` A ` is an ordinal greater than one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem ondif2 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel c1o A2)) $;

--| Closure of the pair swapping function on ` 2o ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem _2oconcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 c2o) (wcel (cdif c1o A2) c2o) $;

--| Ordinal zero is less than ordinal one. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2005.)
theorem _0lt1o:
  $ wcel c0 c1o $;

--| An ordinal greater than one is greater than zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem dif20el (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel c0 A2) $;

--| The empty set is a well-ordering of ordinal one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem _0we1:
  $ wwe c1o c0 $;

--| Lemma for relations which assert the existence of a witness in a two-parameter set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem brwitnlem (A2 B2 R O X: class):
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv O) (cdif cvv c1o)) $ >
  $ wfn O X $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wne (co A2 B2 O) c0) $;

--| Functionality and domain of ordinal addition. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem fnoa:
  $ wfn coa (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Functionality and domain of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem fnom:
  $ wfn comu (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Functionality and domain of ordinal exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem fnoe:
  $ wfn coe (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Value of ordinal addition. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oav {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (csuc (cv x3))) A2))) $;

--| Value of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem omv {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 coa)) c0))) $;

--| A helper lemma for ~ oe0 and others. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.)
theorem oe0lem (ph ps: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq A2 c0)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) ph) (wcel c0 A2)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) ph) ps $;

--| Value of ordinal exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem oev {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (cif (wceq A2 c0) (cdif c1o B2) (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)) c1o)))) $;

--| Value of ordinal exponentiation at a nonzero base. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oevn0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel c0 A2)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)) c1o))) $;

--| Addition with zero. Proposition 8.3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 57. Definition 2.3 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oa0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c0 coa) A2) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with zero. Definition 8.15(a) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62. Definition 2.5 of [Schloeder] p. 4. See ~ om0x for a way to remove the antecedent ` A e. On ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem om0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c0 comu) c0) $;

--| Value of zero raised to an ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oe0m (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c0 A2 coe) (cdif c1o A2)) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with zero. Definition 8.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62. Unlike ~ om0 , this version works whether or not ` A ` is an ordinal. However, since it is an artifact of our particular function value definition outside the domain, we will not use it in order to be conventional and present it only as a curiosity. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem om0x (A2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 c0 comu) c0 $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with zero base and zero exponent. Proposition 8.31 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.)
theorem oe0m0:
  $ wceq (co c0 c0 coe) c1o $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with zero base and nonzero exponent. Proposition 8.31(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67 and its converse. Definition 2.6 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2005.)
theorem oe0m1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wb (wcel c0 A2) (wceq (co c0 A2 coe) c0)) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with zero exponent. Definition 8.30 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. Definition 2.6 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oe0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c0 coe) c1o) $;

--| Alternate value of ordinal exponentiation. Compare ~ oev . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oev2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (cin (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)) c1o)) (cun (cdif cvv (cint A2)) (cint B2)))) $;

--| Addition with successor. Definition 8.1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 56. Definition 2.3 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oasuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coa) (csuc (co A2 B2 coa))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oesuc . (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem oesuclem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wlim X $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)) c1o)) (cfv (cfv B2 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)) c1o)) (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) A2 comu)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) A2 comu)) $;

--| Multiplication with successor. Definition 8.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62. Definition 2.5 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem omsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) A2 coa)) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with a successor exponent. Definition 8.30 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. Definition 2.6 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oesuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) A2 comu)) $;

--| Addition with successor. Theorem 4I(A2) of [Enderton] p. 79. (Note that this version of ~ oasuc does not need Replacement.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem onasuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coa) (csuc (co A2 B2 coa))) $;

--| Multiplication with successor. Theorem 4J(A2) of [Enderton] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem onmsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) A2 coa)) $;

--| Exponentiation with a successor exponent. Definition 8.30 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem onesuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) A2 comu)) $;

--| Addition with 1 is same as successor. Proposition 4.34(a) of [Mendelson] p. 266. Remark 2.4 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem oa1suc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c1o coa) (csuc A2)) $;

--| Ordinal addition with a limit ordinal. Definition 8.1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 56. Definition 2.3 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oalim {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (ciun x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coa))) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with a limit ordinal. Definition 8.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62. Definition 2.5 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem omlim {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) (ciun x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) comu))) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with a limit exponent and nonzero base. Definition 8.30 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. Definition 2.6 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem oelim {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wcel c0 A2)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (ciun x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coe))) $;

--| Closure law for ordinal addition. Proposition 8.2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 57. Remark 2.8 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1995.)
theorem oacl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 coa) con0) $;

--| Closure law for ordinal multiplication. Proposition 8.16 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 57. Remark 2.8 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem omcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 comu) con0) $;

--| Closure law for ordinal exponentiation. Remark 2.8 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem oecl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 coe) con0) $;

--| Ordinal addition with zero. Proposition 8.3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 57. Lemma 2.14 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1995.)
theorem oa0r (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c0 A2 coa) A2) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with zero. Proposition 8.18(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem om0r (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c0 A2 comu) c0) $;

--| 1 + 1 = 2 for ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Feb-2004.)
theorem o1p1e2:
  $ wceq (co c1o c1o coa) c2o $;

--| 2 + 2 = 4 for ordinal numbers. Ordinal numbers are modeled as Von Neumann ordinals; see ~ df-suc . For the usual proof using complex numbers, see ~ 2p2e4 . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2021.) Avoid ~ ax-rep , from a comment by Sophie. (Revised by SN, 23-Mar-2024.)
theorem o2p2e4:
  $ wceq (co c2o c2o coa) c4o $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with 1. Proposition 8.18(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Lemma 2.15 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.)
theorem om1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c1o comu) A2) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication with 1. Proposition 8.18(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Lemma 2.15 of [Schloeder] p. 5. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem om1r (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c1o A2 comu) A2) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with an exponent of 1. Lemma 2.16 of [Schloeder] p. 6. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2005.)
theorem oe1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c1o coe) A2) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with a base of 1. Proposition 8.31(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. Lemma 2.17 of [Schloeder] p. 6. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2005.)
theorem oe1m (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c1o A2 coe) c1o) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal addition. Proposition 8.4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 58. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal addition. Proposition 8.4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 58 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Left cancellation law for ordinal addition. Corollary 8.5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 58. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2004.)
theorem oacan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (co A2 C coa)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal addition. (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem oaword (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal addition. Proposition 8.7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 59. (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2004.)
theorem oawordri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C coa) (co B2 C coa))) $;

--| An ordinal is less than its sum with a nonzero ordinal. Theorem 18 of [Suppes] p. 209 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaord1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel c0 B2) (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 coa))) $;

--| An ordinal is less than or equal to its sum with another. Part of Exercise 5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62. Lemma 3.2 of [Schloeder] p. 7. (For the other part see ~ oaord1 .) (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaword1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| An ordinal is less than or equal to its sum with another. Theorem 21 of [Suppes] p. 209. Lemma 3.3 of [Schloeder] p. 7. (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaword2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 (co B2 A2 coa)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oawordex . (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2004.)
theorem oawordeulem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 con0 $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wss B2 (co A2 (cv y1) coa)) con0) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wreu x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) con0) $;

--| Existence theorem for weak ordering of ordinal sum. Proposition 8.8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 59. (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2004.)
theorem oawordeu {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 B2)) (wreu x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) con0) $;

--| Existence theorem for weak ordering of ordinal sum. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem oawordexr {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) con0)) (wss A2 B2) $;

--| Existence theorem for weak ordering of ordinal sum. Proposition 8.8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 59 and its converse. See ~ oawordeu for uniqueness. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem oawordex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) con0)) $;

--| Existence theorem for ordering of ordinal sum. Similar to Proposition 4.34(f) of [Mendelson] p. 266 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaordex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel c0 (cv x3)) (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2)) con0)) $;

--| An ordinal sum is zero iff both of its arguments are zero. Lemma 3.10 of [Schloeder] p. 8. (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2004.)
theorem oa00 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) c0) (wa (wceq A2 c0) (wceq B2 c0))) $;

--| The ordinal sum with a limit ordinal is a limit ordinal. Proposition 8.11 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 60. Lemma 3.4 of [Schloeder] p. 7. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2004.)
theorem oalimcl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wlim (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| Ordinal addition is associative. Theorem 25 of [Suppes] p. 211. Theorem 4.2 of [Schloeder] p. 11. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 coa) C coa) (co A2 (co B2 C coa) coa)) $;

--| Recursive definition of ordinal addition. Exercise 25 of [Enderton] p. 240. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem oarec {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (cun A2 (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coa))))) $;

--| Left addition by a constant is a bijection from ordinals to ordinals greater than the constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem oaf1o {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wf1o con0 (cdif con0 A2) (cmpt x3 con0 (co A2 (cv x3) coa))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oacomf1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem oacomf1olem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 (cv x3) coa)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wf1o A2 (crn F) F) (wceq (cin (crn F) B2) c0)) $;

--| Define a bijection from ` A +o B ` to ` B +o A ` . Thus, the two are equinumerous even if they are not equal (which sometimes occurs, e.g., ~ oancom ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem oacomf1o {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cun (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wf1o (co A2 B2 coa) (co B2 A2 coa) F) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal multiplication. Half of Proposition 8.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Lemma 3.15 of [Schloeder] p. 9. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem omordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel c0 C)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2004.)
theorem omord2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel c0 C)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal multiplication. Proposition 8.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Theorem 3.16 of [Schloeder] p. 9. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem omord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel c0 C)) (wcel (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Left cancellation law for ordinal multiplication. Proposition 8.20 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2004.)
theorem omcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel c0 A2)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) (co A2 C comu)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem omword (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel c0 C)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem omwordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal multiplication. Proposition 8.21 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. (Contributed by NM, 20-Dec-2004.)
theorem omwordri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C comu) (co B2 C comu))) $;

--| An ordinal is less than or equal to its product with another. Lemma 3.11 of [Schloeder] p. 8. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem omword1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel c0 B2)) (wss A2 (co A2 B2 comu)) $;

--| An ordinal is less than or equal to its product with another. Lemma 3.12 of [Schloeder] p. 9. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem omword2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel c0 B2)) (wss A2 (co B2 A2 comu)) $;

--| The product of two ordinal numbers is zero iff at least one of them is zero. Proposition 8.22 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 21-Dec-2004.)
theorem om00 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) c0) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wceq B2 c0))) $;

--| The product of two nonzero ordinal numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2004.)
theorem om00el (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel c0 (co A2 B2 comu)) (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wcel c0 B2))) $;

--| Ordering involving the product of a limit ordinal. Proposition 8.23 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2004.)
theorem omordlim {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 D) (wlim B2))) (wcel C (co A2 B2 comu))) (wrex x3 (wcel C (co A2 (cv x3) comu)) B2) $;

--| The product of any nonzero ordinal with a limit ordinal is a limit ordinal. Proposition 8.24 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2004.)
theorem omlimcl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wcel c0 A2)) (wlim (co A2 B2 comu)) $;

--| Distributive law for ordinal arithmetic (left-distributivity). Proposition 8.25 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 64. Theorem 4.3 of [Schloeder] p. 12. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-2004.)
theorem odi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C coa) comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) (co A2 C comu) coa)) $;

--| Multiplication of ordinal numbers is associative. Theorem 8.26 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 65. Theorem 4.4 of [Schloeder] p. 13. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2004.)
theorem omass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 comu) C comu) (co A2 (co B2 C comu) comu)) $;

--| If an ordinal number is even, its successor is odd. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-2006.)
theorem oneo (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wceq C (co c2o A2 comu))) (wn (wceq (csuc C) (co c2o B2 comu))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omeu : existence part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem omeulem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (co (co A2 (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa) B2) A2) con0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omeu : uniqueness part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem omeulem2 (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C A2)) (wa (wcel D con0) (wcel E A2))) (wi (wo (wcel B2 D) (wa (wceq B2 D) (wcel C E))) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 comu) C coa) (co (co A2 D comu) E coa))) $;

--| An ordered pair-like theorem for ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem omopth2 (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C A2)) (wa (wcel D con0) (wcel E A2))) (wb (wceq (co (co A2 B2 comu) C coa) (co (co A2 D comu) E coa)) (wa (wceq B2 D) (wceq C E))) $;

--| The division algorithm for ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem omeu {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wne A2 c0)) (weu z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (co (co A2 (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa) B2)) A2) con0)) $;

--| Two ways to double an ordinal. (Contributed by RP, 3-Jan-2025.)
theorem om2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 A2 coa) (co A2 c2o comu)) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with a nonzero base is nonzero. Proposition 8.32 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2005.)
theorem oen0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel c0 A2)) (wcel c0 (co A2 B2 coe)) $;

--| Ordering law for ordinal exponentiation. Proposition 8.33 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.)
theorem oeordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C (cdif con0 c2o))) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coe) (co C B2 coe))) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal exponentiation. Corollary 8.34 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 68 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.)
theorem oeord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C (cdif con0 c2o))) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coe) (co C B2 coe))) $;

--| Left cancellation law for ordinal exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.)
theorem oecan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (co A2 C coe)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.)
theorem oeword (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C (cdif con0 c2o))) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 coe) (co C B2 coe))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal exponentiation. Lemma 3.19 of [Schloeder] p. 10. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.)
theorem oewordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wcel c0 C)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 coe) (co C B2 coe))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal exponentiation. Proposition 8.35 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 68. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.)
theorem oewordri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C coe) (co B2 C coe))) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation compared to its exponent. Proposition 8.37 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 68. Lemma 3.20 of [Schloeder] p. 10. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.)
theorem oeworde (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss B2 (co A2 B2 coe)) $;

--| Ordering property of ordinal exponentiation with a successor exponent. Corollary 8.36 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 68. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jan-2005.)
theorem oeordsuc (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co A2 (csuc C) coe) (co B2 (csuc C) coe))) $;

--| Ordinal exponentiation with a limit exponent. Part of Exercise 4.36 of [Mendelson] p. 250. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2005.)
theorem oelim2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wceq (co A2 B2 coe) (ciun x3 (cdif B2 c1o) (co A2 (cv x3) coe))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oeoa . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.)
theorem oeoalem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wcel c0 A2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 con0 $ >
  $ wi (wcel C con0) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C coa) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) (co A2 C coe) comu)) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for ordinal exponentiation. Theorem 8R of [Enderton] p. 238. Also Proposition 8.41 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 69. Theorem 4.7 of [Schloeder] p. 14. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.)
theorem oeoa (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C coa) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) (co A2 C coe) comu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oeoe . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.)
theorem oeoelem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 con0 $ >
  $ wcel c0 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 coe) C coe) (co A2 (co B2 C comu) coe)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for ordinal exponentiation. Theorem 8S of [Enderton] p. 238. Also Proposition 8.42 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 70. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-May-2009.)
theorem oeoe (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 coe) C coe) (co A2 (co B2 C comu) coe)) $;

--| The ordinal exponential with a limit ordinal is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem oelimcl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wa (wcel B2 C) (wlim B2))) (wlim (co A2 B2 coe)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oeeu . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem oeeulem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cuni (cint (crab x3 (wcel B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coe)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel B2 (cdif con0 c1o))) (w3a (wcel X con0) (wss (co A2 X coe) B2) (wcel B2 (co A2 (csuc X) coe))) $;

--| The division algorithm for ordinal exponentiation. (This version of ~ oeeu gives an explicit expression for the unique solution of the equation, in terms of the solution ` P ` to ~ omeu .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem oeeui {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D P E: class x3 y1 z w) (X: class x3) (Y Z: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq X (cuni (cint (crab x3 (wcel B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coe)) con0))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cio w (wrex y1 (wrex z (wa (wceq (cv w) (cop (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 X coe) (cv y1) comu) (cv z) coa) B2)) (co A2 X coe)) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv P c2nd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel B2 (cdif con0 c1o))) (wb (wa (w3a (wcel C con0) (wcel D (cdif A2 c1o)) (wcel E (co A2 C coe))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 C coe) D comu) E coa) B2)) (w3a (wceq C X) (wceq D Y) (wceq E Z))) $;

--| The division algorithm for ordinal exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem oeeu {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel B2 (cdif con0 c1o))) (weu w (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wa (wceq (cv w) (cotp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 (cv x3) coe) (cv y1) comu) (cv z) coa) B2)) (co A2 (cv x3) coe)) (cdif A2 c1o)) con0)) $;

--| Addition with zero. Theorem 4I(A1) of [Enderton] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.)
theorem nna0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co A2 c0 coa) A2) $;

--| Multiplication with zero. Theorem 4J(A1) of [Enderton] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.)
theorem nnm0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co A2 c0 comu) c0) $;

--| Addition with successor. Theorem 4I(A2) of [Enderton] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnasuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coa) (csuc (co A2 B2 coa))) $;

--| Multiplication with successor. Theorem 4J(A2) of [Enderton] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) A2 coa)) $;

--| Exponentiation with a successor exponent. Definition 8.30 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 67. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnesuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) coe) (co (co A2 B2 coe) A2 comu)) $;

--| Addition to zero. Remark in proof of Theorem 4K(2) of [Enderton] p. 81. Note: In this and later theorems, we deliberately avoid the more general ordinal versions of these theorems (in this case ~ oa0r ) so that we can avoid ~ ax-rep , which is not needed for finite recursive definitions. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem nna0r (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co c0 A2 coa) A2) $;

--| Multiplication with zero. Exercise 16 of [Enderton] p. 82. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnm0r (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co c0 A2 comu) c0) $;

--| Closure of addition of natural numbers. Proposition 8.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 59. Theorem 2.20 of [Schloeder] p. 6. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem nnacl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel (co A2 B2 coa) com) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of natural numbers. Proposition 8.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Theorem 2.20 of [Schloeder] p. 6. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem nnmcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel (co A2 B2 comu) com) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of natural numbers. Proposition 8.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63. Theorem 2.20 of [Schloeder] p. 6. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem nnecl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel (co A2 B2 coe) com) $;

--| ` _om ` is closed under addition. Inference form of ~ nnacl . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2012.)
theorem nnacli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 coa) com $;

--| ` _om ` is closed under multiplication. Inference form of ~ nnmcl . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2012.)
theorem nnmcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 comu) com $;

--| Reverse closure law for addition of natural numbers. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 62 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2004.)
theorem nnarcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel (co A2 B2 coa) com) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com))) $;

--| Addition of natural numbers is commutative. Theorem 4K(2) of [Enderton] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnacom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (co B2 A2 coa)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition. Proposition 8.4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 58, limited to natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Ordering property of addition. Proposition 8.4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 58, limited to natural numbers, and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 7-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Ordering property of addition of natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2002.)
theorem nnaordr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co A2 C coa) (co B2 C coa))) $;

--| Adding to both sides of an inequality in ` _om ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2012.)
theorem nnawordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C coa) (co B2 C coa))) $;

--| Addition of natural numbers is associative. Theorem 4K(1) of [Enderton] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 coa) C coa) (co A2 (co B2 C coa) coa)) $;

--| Distributive law for natural numbers (left-distributivity). Theorem 4K(3) of [Enderton] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nndi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C coa) comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) (co A2 C comu) coa)) $;

--| Multiplication of natural numbers is associative. Theorem 4K(4) of [Enderton] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 comu) C comu) (co A2 (co B2 C comu) comu)) $;

--| Multiplication with successor. Exercise 16 of [Enderton] p. 82. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem nnmsucr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co (csuc A2) B2 comu) (co (co A2 B2 comu) B2 coa)) $;

--| Multiplication of natural numbers is commutative. Theorem 4K(5) of [Enderton] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem nnmcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) (co B2 A2 comu)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of addition. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaword (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 coa) (co C B2 coa))) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition of natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnacan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) (co A2 C coa)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of addition. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaword1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss A2 (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of addition. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2002.)
theorem nnaword2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss A2 (co B2 A2 coa)) $;

--| Ordering property of multiplication. Half of Proposition 8.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63, limited to natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wcel c0 C)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Ordering property of multiplication. Proposition 8.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63, limited to natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wb (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel c0 C)) (wcel (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmword (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wcel c0 C)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication of natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wcel c0 A2)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 comu) (co A2 C comu)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Weak ordering property of multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmwordi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 comu) (co C B2 comu))) $;

--| Weak ordering property of ordinal multiplication. Proposition 8.21 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 63, limited to natural numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnmwordri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wcel C com)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C comu) (co B2 C comu))) $;

--| Equivalence for weak ordering of natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnawordex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) com)) $;

--| Equivalence for ordering. Compare Exercise 23 of [Enderton] p. 88. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnaordex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel c0 (cv x3)) (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2)) com)) $;

--| Equivalence for ordering. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2025.)
theorem nnaordex2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (csuc (cv x3)) coa) B2) com)) $;

--| The ordinal 1 is a natural number. For a shorter proof using Peano's postulates that depends on ~ ax-un , see ~ 1onnALT . Lemma 2.2 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem _1onn:
  $ wcel c1o com $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ 1onn using Peano's postulates that depends on ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1onnALT:
  $ wcel c1o com $;

--| The ordinal 2 is a natural number. For a shorter proof using Peano's postulates that depends on ~ ax-un , see ~ 2onnALT . (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem _2onn:
  $ wcel c2o com $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ 2onn using Peano's postulates that depends on ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2onnALT:
  $ wcel c2o com $;

--| The ordinal 3 is a natural number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem _3onn:
  $ wcel c3o com $;

--| The ordinal 4 is a natural number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem _4onn:
  $ wcel c4o com $;

--| Ordinal one is not ordinal two. Analogous to ~ 1ne2 . (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2023.)
theorem _1one2o:
  $ wne c1o c2o $;

--| Lemma for ~ oaabs . (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2004.)
theorem oaabslem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel com con0) (wcel A2 com)) (wceq (co A2 com coa) com) $;

--| Ordinal addition absorbs a natural number added to the left of a transfinite number. Proposition 8.10 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 59. (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem oaabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss com B2)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) B2) $;

--| The absorption law ~ oaabs is also a property of higher powers of ` _om ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem oaabs2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (co com C coe)) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss (co com C coe) B2)) (wceq (co A2 B2 coa) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omabs . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem omabslem (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel com con0) (wcel A2 com) (wcel c0 A2)) (wceq (co A2 com comu) com) $;

--| Ordinal multiplication is also absorbed by powers of ` _om ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem omabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel c0 A2)) (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel c0 B2))) (wceq (co A2 (co com B2 coe) comu) (co com B2 coe)) $;

--| Multiply an element of ` _om ` by ` 1o ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnm1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co A2 c1o comu) A2) $;

--| Multiply an element of ` _om ` by ` 2o ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnm2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co A2 c2o comu) (co A2 A2 coa)) $;

--| Multiply an element of ` _om ` by ` 2o ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nn2m (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (co c2o A2 comu) (co A2 A2 coa)) $;

--| If a natural number is even, its successor is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnneo (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wceq C (co c2o A2 comu))) (wn (wceq (csuc C) (co c2o B2 comu))) $;

--| A natural number is even iff its successor is odd. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem nneob {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wb (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) com) (wn (wrex x3 (wceq (csuc A2) (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) com))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omsmo . (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 1-Jan-2014.)
theorem omsmolem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) com) (wi (wa (wa (wss A2 con0) (wf com A2 F)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F)) com)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| A strictly monotonic ordinal function on the set of natural numbers is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 1-Jan-2014.)
theorem omsmo {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss A2 con0) (wf com A2 F)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F)) com)) (wf1 com A2 F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omopthi . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem omopthlem1 (A2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel C com $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) (wcel (co (co A2 A2 comu) (co A2 c2o comu) coa) (co C C comu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omopthi . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem omopthlem2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wcel C com $ >
  $ wcel D com $ >
  $ wi (wcel (co A2 B2 coa) C) (wn (wceq (co (co C C comu) D coa) (co (co (co A2 B2 coa) (co A2 B2 coa) comu) B2 coa))) $;

--| An ordered pair theorem for ` _om ` . Theorem 17.3 of [Quine] p. 124. This proof is adapted from ~ nn0opthi . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem omopthi (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wcel C com $ >
  $ wcel D com $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 coa) (co A2 B2 coa) comu) B2 coa) (co (co (co C D coa) (co C D coa) comu) D coa)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| An ordered pair theorem for finite integers. Analogous to ~ nn0opthi . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 1-May-2012.)
theorem omopth (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wa (wcel C com) (wcel D com))) (wb (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 coa) (co A2 B2 coa) comu) B2 coa) (co (co (co C D coa) (co C D coa) comu) D coa)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| There is at most one left additive inverse for natural number addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem nnasmo {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wrmo x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) coa) B2) com) $;

--| Condition for membership in the difference of ` _om ` and a nonzero finite ordinal. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Oct-2024.)
theorem eldifsucnn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wb (wcel B2 (cdif com (csuc A2))) (wrex x3 (wceq B2 (csuc (cv x3))) (cdif com A2))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for natural ordinal addition. See ~ df-nadd .
term cnadd: class;

--| Define natural ordinal addition. This is a commutative form of addition over the ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
axiom df_nadd {x3 y1 z w a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnadd (cfrecs (cxp con0 con0) (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (cmpo z a cvv cvv (cint (crab w (wa (wss (cima (cv a) (cxp (csn (cfv (cv z) c1st)) (cfv (cv z) c2nd))) (cv w)) (wss (cima (cv a) (cxp (cfv (cv z) c1st) (csn (cfv (cv z) c2nd)))) (cv w))) con0)))) $;

--| Show that double recursion over ordinals yields a function over pairs of ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Sep-2024.)
theorem on2recsfn {x3 y1: setvar} (F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs (cxp con0 con0) (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) G) $ >
  $ wfn F (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Calculate the value of the double ordinal recursion operator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Sep-2024.)
theorem on2recsov {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs (cxp con0 con0) (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) (co (cop A2 B2) (cres F (cdif (cxp (csuc A2) (csuc B2)) (csn (cop A2 B2)))) G)) $;

--| Double induction over ordinal numbers. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem on2ind {a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff a b d) (ps: wff b c) (ch: wff a c d) (th: wff a b d) (ta: wff b c d) (et: wff a c d) (X: class c d) (Y: class a c d):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv d)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv c)) (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv a) con0) (wcel (cv b) con0)) (wi (w3a (wral c (wral d ch (cv b)) (cv a)) (wral c ps (cv a)) (wral d th (cv b))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X con0) (wcel Y con0)) et $;

--| Triple induction over ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Sep-2024.)
theorem on3ind {e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 a b c) (ps: wff f2 b c d) (ch: wff e a c d) (th: wff e f2 d) (ta: wff e f2 a b c) (et ze: wff f2 a b c d) (si: wff e a b c d) (rh: wff e f2 b c d) (mu: wff e f2 a c d) (la: wff e f2 a b d) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb et ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ze th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb si ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) X) (wb ph rh) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) Y) (wb rh mu) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) Z) (wb mu la) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv a) con0) (wcel (cv b) con0) (wcel (cv c) con0)) (wi (w3a (w3a (wral d (wral e (wral f2 th (cv c)) (cv b)) (cv a)) (wral d (wral e ch (cv b)) (cv a)) (wral d (wral f2 ze (cv c)) (cv a))) (w3a (wral d ps (cv a)) (wral e (wral f2 ta (cv c)) (cv b)) (wral e si (cv b))) (wral f2 et (cv c))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X con0) (wcel Y con0) (wcel Z con0)) la $;

--| Cofinality theorem for ordinals. If ` A ` is cofinal with ` B ` and ` B ` precedes ` C ` , then ` A ` precedes ` C ` . Compare ~ cofslts for surreals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem coflton {x3 y1 z w a c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class y1 z w a) (B2: class w a c) (C: class x3 y1 a c):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wral w (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) C) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral c (wcel (cv a) (cv c)) C) A2) $;

--| Cofinality theorem for ordinals. If ` A ` is cofinal with ` B ` and the upper bound of ` A ` dominates ` B ` , then their upper bounds are equal. Compare with ~ cofcut1 for surreals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem cofon1 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 w) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cpw con0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cint (crab z (wss A2 (cv z)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cint (crab z (wss A2 (cv z)) con0)) (cint (crab w (wss B2 (cv w)) con0))) $;

--| Cofinality theorem for ordinals. If ` A ` and ` B ` are mutually cofinal, then their upper bounds agree. Compare ~ cofcut2 for surreals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem cofon2 {x3 y1 z w a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class y1) (B2: class w):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cpw con0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cpw con0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cv w)) A2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cint (crab a (wss A2 (cv a)) con0)) (cint (crab b (wss B2 (cv b)) con0))) $;

--| Inverse cofinality law for ordinals. Contrast with ~ cofcutr for surreals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem cofonr {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 X: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cint (crab x3 (wss X (cv x3)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wrex z (wss (cv y1) (cv z)) X) A2) $;

--| Natural addition is a function over pairs of ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddfn:
  $ wfn cnadd (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Lemma for ordinal addition closure. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddcllem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel (co A2 B2 cnadd) con0) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (wa (wss (cima cnadd (cxp (csn A2) B2)) (cv x3)) (wss (cima cnadd (cxp A2 (csn B2))) (cv x3))) con0)))) $;

--| Closure law for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cnadd) con0) $;

--| The value of natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddov {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (wa (wss (cima cnadd (cxp (csn A2) B2)) (cv x3)) (wss (cima cnadd (cxp A2 (csn B2))) (cv x3))) con0))) $;

--| Alternate expression for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddov2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (wa (wral y1 (wcel (co A2 (cv y1) cnadd) (cv x3)) B2) (wral z (wcel (co (cv z) B2 cnadd) (cv x3)) A2)) con0))) $;

--| Alternate expression for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddov2_b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (wa (wral y1 (wcel (co A2 (cv y1) cnadd) (cv x3)) B2) (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv y1) B2 cnadd) (cv x3)) A2)) con0))) $;

--| Closure law for natural addition. Deduction version. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2025.)
theorem naddcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cnadd) con0) $;

--| Alternate expression for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddov3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (wss (cun (cima cnadd (cxp (csn A2) B2)) (cima cnadd (cxp A2 (csn B2)))) (cv x3)) con0))) $;

--| Function statement for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddf:
  $ wf (cxp con0 con0) con0 cnadd $;

--| Natural addition is commutative. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co B2 A2 cnadd)) $;

--| Ordinal zero is the additive identity for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem naddrid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c0 cnadd) A2) $;

--| Ordinal zero is the additive identity for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Feb-2025.)
theorem naddlid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c0 A2 cnadd) A2) $;

--| Ordinal less-than-or-equal is preserved by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddssim (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C cnadd) (co B2 C cnadd))) $;

--| Ordinal less-than is preserved by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddelim (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co A2 C cnadd) (co B2 C cnadd))) $;

--| Ordinal less-than is not affected by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddel1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co A2 C cnadd) (co B2 C cnadd))) $;

--| Ordinal less-than is not affected by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddel2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (co C A2 cnadd) (co C B2 cnadd))) $;

--| Ordinal less-than-or-equal is not affected by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddss1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C cnadd) (co B2 C cnadd))) $;

--| Ordinal less-than-or-equal is not affected by natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem naddss2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co C A2 cnadd) (co C B2 cnadd))) $;

--| Weak-ordering principle for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddword1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 (co A2 B2 cnadd)) $;

--| Weak-ordering principle for natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem naddword2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 (co B2 A2 cnadd)) $;

--| Uniformity theorem for natural addition. If ` A ` is the upper bound of ` X ` and ` B ` is the upper bound of ` Y ` , then ` ( A +no B ) ` can be expressed in terms of ` X ` and ` Y ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddunif {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3) (X: class y1) (Y: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cint (crab x3 (wss X (cv x3)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cint (crab y1 (wss Y (cv y1)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cint (crab z (wss (cun (cima cnadd (cxp X (csn B2))) (cima cnadd (cxp (csn A2) Y))) (cv z)) con0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ naddass . Expand out the expression for natural addition of three arguments. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddasslem1 {x3 a b c: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (w3a (wral a (wcel (co (co (cv a) B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (cv x3)) A2) (wral b (wcel (co (co A2 (cv b) cnadd) C cnadd) (cv x3)) B2) (wral c (wcel (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) (cv c) cnadd) (cv x3)) C)) con0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ naddass . Expand out the expression for natural addition of three arguments. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddasslem2 {x3 a b c: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnadd) (cint (crab x3 (w3a (wral a (wcel (co (cv a) (co B2 C cnadd) cnadd) (cv x3)) A2) (wral b (wcel (co A2 (co (cv b) C cnadd) cnadd) (cv x3)) B2) (wral c (wcel (co A2 (co B2 (cv c) cnadd) cnadd) (cv x3)) C)) con0))) $;

--| Natural ordinal addition is associative. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnadd)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Jan-2025.)
theorem nadd32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (co (co A2 C cnadd) B2 cnadd)) $;

--| Rearragement of terms in a quadruple sum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Feb-2025.)
theorem nadd4 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel C con0) (wcel D con0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co C D cnadd) cnadd) (co (co A2 C cnadd) (co B2 D cnadd) cnadd)) $;

--| Rearragement of terms in a quadruple sum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Feb-2025.)
theorem nadd42 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel C con0) (wcel D con0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co C D cnadd) cnadd) (co (co A2 C cnadd) (co D B2 cnadd) cnadd)) $;

--| Natural addition to both sides of ordinal less-than. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Feb-2025.)
theorem naddel12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C con0) (wcel D con0)) (wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co C D cnadd))) $;

--| Natural addition with successor. (Contributed by RP, 1-Jan-2025.)
theorem naddsuc2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 (csuc B2) cnadd) (csuc (co A2 B2 cnadd))) $;

--| Natural addition of a natural is the same as regular addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Aug-2025.)
theorem naddoa (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| The naturals are closed under natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Aug-2025.)
theorem omnaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cnadd) com) $;

--| Extend the definition of a wff to include the equivalence predicate.
term wer (A2 R: class): wff;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include equivalence class.
term cec (A2 R: class): class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include quotient set.
term cqs (A2 R: class): class;

--| Define the equivalence relation predicate. Our notation is not standard. A formal notation doesn't seem to exist in the literature; instead only informal English tends to be used. The present definition, although somewhat cryptic, nicely avoids dummy variables. In ~ dfer2 we derive a more typical definition. We show that an equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive in ~ erref , ~ ersymb , and ~ ertr . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Nov-2015.)
axiom df_er (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wer A2 R) (w3a (wrel R) (wceq (cdm R) A2) (wss (cun (ccnv R) (ccom R R)) R)) $;

--| Alternate definition of equivalence predicate. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfer2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wb (wer A2 R) (w3a (wrel R) (wceq (cdm R) A2) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wa (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R))))))) $;

--| Define the ` R ` -coset of ` A ` . Exercise 35 of [Enderton] p. 61. This is called the equivalence class of ` A ` modulo ` R ` when ` R ` is an equivalence relation (i.e. when ` Er R ` ; see ~ dfer2 ). In this case, ` A ` is a representative (member) of the equivalence class ` [ A ] R ` , which contains all sets that are equivalent to ` A ` . Definition of [Enderton] p. 57 uses the notation ` [ A ] ` (subscript) ` R ` , although we simply follow the brackets by ` R ` since we don't have subscripted expressions. For an alternate definition, see ~ dfec2 . (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
axiom df_ec (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cec A2 R) (cima R (csn A2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` R ` -coset of ` A ` . Definition 34 of [Suppes] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-1997.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem dfec2 {y1: setvar} (A2 R: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cec A2 R) (cab y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) R))) $;

--| An equivalence class modulo a set is a set. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-1995.)
theorem ecexg (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R B2) (wcel (cec A2 R) cvv) $;

--| A nonempty equivalence class implies the representative is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ecexr (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cec B2 R)) (wcel B2 cvv) $;

--| Define quotient set. ` R ` is usually an equivalence relation. Definition of [Enderton] p. 58. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
axiom df_qs {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cqs A2 R) (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv x3) R)) A2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of quotient set. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 7-Jun-2023.)
theorem dfqs2 {x3: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cqs A2 R) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cec (cv x3) R))) $;

--| Equality theorem for equivalence predicate. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ereq1 (A2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wb (wer A2 R) (wer A2 S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for equivalence predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ereq2 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wer A2 R) (wer B2 R)) $;

--| An equivalence relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem errel (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wrel R) $;

--| The domain of an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erdm (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wceq (cdm R) A2) $;

--| Elementhood in the field of an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ercl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $;

--| An equivalence relation is symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ersym (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 R) $;

--| Elementhood in the field of an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ercl2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $;

--| An equivalence relation is symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ersymb (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 A2 R)) $;

--| An equivalence relation is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ertr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 C R)) (wbr A2 C R)) $;

--| A transitivity relation for equivalences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ertrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C R) $;

--| A transitivity relation for equivalences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ertr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C A2 R) $;

--| A transitivity relation for equivalences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ertr3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C R) $;

--| A transitivity relation for equivalences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ertr4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C R) $;

--| An equivalence relation is reflexive on its field. Compare Theorem 3M of [Enderton] p. 56. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erref (ph: wff) (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 A2 R) $;

--| The converse of an equivalence relation is itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ercnv (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wceq (ccnv R) R) $;

--| The range and domain of an equivalence relation are equal. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 11-Oct-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem errn (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wceq (crn R) A2) $;

--| An equivalence relation is a subset of the cartesian product of the field. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erssxp (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wss R (cxp A2 A2)) $;

--| An equivalence relation is a set if its domain is a set. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 15-Oct-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erex (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel R cvv)) $;

--| An equivalence relation is a set if and only if its domain is a set. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 15-Oct-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erexb (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wer A2 R) (wb (wcel R cvv) (wcel A2 cvv)) $;

--| A reflexive, symmetric, transitive relation is an equivalence relation on its domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem iserd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wrel R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R))) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer A2 R) $;

--| A reflexive, symmetric, transitive relation is an equivalence relation on its domain. Inference version of ~ iserd , which avoids the need to provide a "dummy antecedent" ` ph ` if there is no natural one to choose. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2021.)
theorem iseri {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wrel R $ >
  $ wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wer A2 R $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ iseri , avoiding the usage of ~ mptru and ` T. ` as antecedent by using ~ ax-mp and one of the hypotheses as antecedent. This results, however, in a slightly longer proof. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem iseriALT {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wrel R $ >
  $ wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) R) $ >
  $ wer A2 R $;

--| Conditions for a reflexive, symmetric and transitive binary relation to be an equivalence relation over a class ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2025.)
theorem brinxper {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_sm V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) V) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) V) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_sm) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) V) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_sm) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_sm)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wer V (cin c_sm (cxp V V)) $;

--| Evaluate the incomparability relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem brdifun (A2 B2 R c_lt X: class):
  $ wceq R (cdif (cxp X X) (cun c_lt (ccnv c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wn (wo (wbr A2 B2 c_lt) (wbr B2 A2 c_lt)))) $;

--| Incomparability under a strict weak partial order is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem swoer {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (R: class x3 y1 z) (c_lt X: class):
  $ wceq R (cdif (cxp X X) (cun c_lt (ccnv c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_lt) (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_lt) (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_lt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $;

--| The incomparability equivalence relation is compatible with the original order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem swoord1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class x3 y1 z) (c_lt X: class):
  $ wceq R (cdif (cxp X X) (cun c_lt (ccnv c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_lt) (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_lt) (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_lt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 C c_lt) (wbr B2 C c_lt)) $;

--| The incomparability equivalence relation is compatible with the original order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem swoord2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class x3 y1 z) (c_lt X: class):
  $ wceq R (cdif (cxp X X) (cun c_lt (ccnv c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_lt) (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_lt) (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_lt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr C A2 c_lt) (wbr C B2 c_lt)) $;

--| If the incomparability relation is equivalent to equality in a subset, then the partial order strictly orders the subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem swoso {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (R: class x3 y1 z) (c_lt X: class) (Y: class z):
  $ wceq R (cdif (cxp X X) (cun c_lt (ccnv c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_lt) (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_lt) (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_lt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_lt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) Y) (wcel (cv y1) Y) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor Y c_lt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eqer . (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Dec-2016.)
theorem eqerlem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class x3 z w) (B2: class y1 z w) (R: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cv z) (cv w) R) (wceq (csb x3 (cv z) A2) (csb x3 (cv w) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eqer . (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Dec-2016.)
theorem eqerlem_b {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 z) (B2: class y1 z) (R: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cv z) (cv z) R) (wceq (csb x3 (cv z) A2) (csb x3 (cv z) A2)) $;

--| Equivalence relation involving equality of dependent classes ` A ( x ) ` and ` B ( y ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem eqer {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (R: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wer cvv R $;

--| The identity relation is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1998.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ider:
  $ wer cvv cid $;

--| The empty set is an equivalence relation on the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem _0er:
  $ wer c0 c0 $;

--| Equality theorem for equivalence class. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem eceq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cec A2 C) (cec B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for equivalence class (deduction form). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Dec-2019.)
theorem eceq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cec A2 C) (cec B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for equivalence class. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem eceq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cec C A2) (cec C B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the ` A ` -coset and ` B ` -coset of ` C ` , inference version. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 11-May-2021.)
theorem eceq2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cec C A2) (cec C B2) $;

--| Equality theorem for the ` A ` -coset and ` B ` -coset of ` C ` , deduction version. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 23-Apr-2021.)
theorem eceq2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cec C A2) (cec C B2)) $;

--| Membership in an equivalence class. Theorem 72 of [Suppes] p. 82. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem elecg (A2 B2 R V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel A2 (cec B2 R)) (wbr B2 A2 R)) $;

--| All elements are in their own equivalence class. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2025.)
theorem ecref (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wer X R) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel A2 (cec A2 R)) $;

--| Membership in an equivalence class. Theorem 72 of [Suppes] p. 82. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem elec (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cec B2 R)) (wbr B2 A2 R) $;

--| Membership in an equivalence class when ` R ` is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem relelec (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wb (wcel A2 (cec B2 R)) (wbr B2 A2 R)) $;

--| Elementhood in the restricted coset of ` B ` . (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 21-Sep-2018.)
theorem elecres (A2 B2 C R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel C (cec B2 (cres R A2))) (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wbr B2 C R))) $;

--| The restricted coset of ` B ` when ` B ` is an element of the restriction. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 16-Oct-2018.)
theorem elecreseq (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wceq (cec B2 (cres R A2)) (cec B2 R)) $;

--| Condition for a coset to be a set. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 4-May-2019.)
theorem elecex (A2 B2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cres R A2) V) (wi (wcel B2 A2) (wcel (cec B2 R) cvv)) $;

--| An equivalence class is a subset of the domain. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ecss (ph: wff) (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cec A2 R) X) $;

--| A representative of a nonempty equivalence class belongs to the domain of the equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ecdmn0 (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm R)) (wne (cec A2 R) c0) $;

--| Equality of equivalence classes implies equivalence of domain membership. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem ereldm (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) $;

--| Basic property of equivalence relations. Theorem 73 of [Suppes] p. 82. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.)
theorem erth (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wceq (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R))) $;

--| Basic property of equivalence relations. Compare Theorem 73 of [Suppes] p. 82. Assumes membership of the second argument in the domain. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.)
theorem erth2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wceq (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R))) $;

--| Basic property of equivalence relations. Part of Lemma 3N of [Enderton] p. 57. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem erthi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R)) $;

--| Equivalence classes do not overlap. In other words, two equivalence classes are either equal or disjoint. Theorem 74 of [Suppes] p. 83. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem erdisj (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi (wer X R) (wo (wceq (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R)) (wceq (cin (cec A2 R) (cec B2 R)) c0)) $;

--| An equivalence class modulo the identity relation is a singleton. (Contributed by NM, 24-Oct-2004.)
theorem ecidsn (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cec A2 cid) (csn A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem qseq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cqs A2 C) (cqs B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem qseq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cqs C A2) (cqs C B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set, inference form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 3-Jun-2021.)
theorem qseq2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cqs C A2) (cqs C B2) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set, deduction form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 27-May-2021.)
theorem qseq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cqs A2 C) (cqs B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set, deduction form. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 27-May-2021.)
theorem qseq2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cqs C A2) (cqs C B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for quotient set. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 17-Apr-2019.)
theorem qseq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wceq (cqs A2 C) (cqs B2 D)) $;

--| Quotient set with the empty set. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 14-Sep-2019.)
theorem _0qs (R: class):
  $ wceq (cqs c0 R) c0 $;

--| Closed form of ~ elqs . (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 12-Oct-2010.)
theorem elqsg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R)) (wrex x3 (wceq B2 (cec (cv x3) R)) A2)) $;

--| Membership in a quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem elqs {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R)) (wrex x3 (wceq B2 (cec (cv x3) R)) A2) $;

--| Membership in a quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem elqsi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R)) (wrex x3 (wceq B2 (cec (cv x3) R)) A2) $;

--| Membership in a quotient set by an equivalence class according to ` .~ ` . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Apr-2018.) (Revised by AV, 30-Apr-2021.)
theorem elqsecl {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (c_sm: class) (W X: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wb (wcel B2 (cqs W c_sm)) (wrex x3 (wceq B2 (cab y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_sm))) W)) $;

--| Membership of an equivalence class in a quotient set. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem ecelqs (A2 B2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cres R A2) V) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel (cec B2 R) (cqs A2 R)) $;

--| Membership of an equivalence class in a quotient set. More restrictive antecedent; kept for backward compatibility; for new work, prefer ~ ecelqs . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Nov-2025.)
theorem ecelqsw (A2 B2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel (cec B2 R) (cqs A2 R)) $;

--| Membership of an equivalence class in a quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ecelqsi (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wcel (cec B2 R) (cqs A2 R)) $;

--| "Closure" law for equivalence class of ordered pairs. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-1996.)
theorem ecopqsi (A2 B2 C R S: class):
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (cqs (cxp A2 A2) R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cec (cop B2 C) R) S) $;

--| A quotient set exists. (Contributed by FL, 19-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem qsexg (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cqs A2 R) cvv) $;

--| A quotient set exists. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1995.)
theorem qsex (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cqs A2 R) cvv $;

--| The union of a quotient set, like ~ uniqsw but with a weaker antecedent: only the restriction of ` R ` by ` A ` needs to be a set, not ` R ` itself, see e.g. ~ cnvepima . (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 20-Jun-2019.)
theorem uniqs (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cres R A2) V) (wceq (cuni (cqs A2 R)) (cima R A2)) $;

--| The union of a quotient set. More restrictive antecedent; kept for backward compatibility; for new work, prefer ~ uniqs . (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2008.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Nov-2025.)
theorem uniqsw (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq (cuni (cqs A2 R)) (cima R A2)) $;

--| A quotient set is a set of subsets of the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem qsss (ph: wff) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wer A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cqs A2 R) (cpw A2)) $;

--| The union of a quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem uniqs2 (ph: wff) (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi ph (wer A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (cqs A2 R)) A2) $;

--| The singleton of a coset is the singleton quotient. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 25-Mar-2019.)
theorem snecg (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csn (cec A2 R)) (cqs (csn A2) R)) $;

--| The singleton of an equivalence class. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem snec (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (csn (cec A2 R)) (cqs (csn A2) R) $;

--| Equivalence class in terms of quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1999.)
theorem ecqs (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cec A2 R) (cuni (cqs (csn A2) R)) $;

--| A set is equal to its coset under the converse membership relation. (Note: the converse membership relation is not an equivalence relation.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ecid (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cec A2 (ccnv cep)) A2 $;

--| A set is equal to its quotient set modulo the converse membership relation. (Note: the converse membership relation is not an equivalence relation.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem qsid (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cqs A2 (ccnv cep)) A2 $;

--| Implicit substitution of class for equivalence class. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ectocld {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch: wff) (A2 B2 R: class) (S: class x3):
  $ wceq S (cqs B2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cv x3) R) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa ch (wcel (cv x3) B2)) ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ch (wcel A2 S)) ps $;

--| Implicit substitution of class for equivalence class. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem ectocl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2 B2 R: class) (S: class x3):
  $ wceq S (cqs B2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cv x3) R) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) ps $;

--| A quotient set does not contain the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.)
theorem elqsn0 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cdm R) A2) (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R))) (wne B2 c0) $;

--| Membership of an equivalence class in a quotient set. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1995.)
theorem ecelqsdm (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cdm R) A2) (wcel (cec B2 R) (cqs A2 R))) (wcel B2 A2) $;

--| ` R ` -coset of ` B ` in a quotient set, biconditional version. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 17-Apr-2019.) (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem ecelqsdmb (A2 B2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cres R A2) V) (wceq (cdm R) A2)) (wb (wcel (cec B2 R) (cqs A2 R)) (wcel B2 A2)) $;

--| ` R ` -coset in its domain quotient. This is the bridge between ` A ` in the domain and its block ` [ A ] R ` in its domain quotient. (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 17-Apr-2019.) (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem eceldmqs (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wb (wcel (cec A2 R) (cqs (cdm R) R)) (wcel A2 (cdm R))) $;

--| A Cartesian square is an equivalence relation (in general, it is not a poset). (Contributed by FL, 31-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpider (A2: class):
  $ wer A2 (cxp A2 A2) $;

--| The intersection of a nonempty family of equivalence relations is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem iiner {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (R: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wer B2 R) A2)) (wer B2 (ciin x3 A2 R)) $;

--| The relative intersection of a family of equivalence relations is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem riiner {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (R: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wer B2 R) A2) (wer B2 (cin (cxp B2 B2) (ciin x3 A2 R))) $;

--| A restricted equivalence relation is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem erinxp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wer A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer B2 (cin R (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| Restrict the relation in an equivalence class to a base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem ecinxp (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss (cima R A2) A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wceq (cec B2 R) (cec B2 (cin R (cxp A2 A2)))) $;

--| Restrict the equivalence relation in a quotient set to the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem qsinxp (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wss (cima R A2) A2) (wceq (cqs A2 R) (cqs A2 (cin R (cxp A2 A2)))) $;

--| Members of a quotient set do not overlap. (Contributed by Rodolfo Medina, 12-Oct-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem qsdisj (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cqs A2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq B2 C) (wceq (cin B2 C) c0)) $;

--| A quotient set is a disjoint set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.)
theorem qsdisj2 {x3: setvar} (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi (wer X R) (wdisj x3 (cqs A2 R) (cv x3)) $;

--| If an element of a quotient set contains a given element, it is equal to the equivalence class of the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem qsel (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wer X R) (wcel B2 (cqs A2 R)) (wcel C B2)) (wceq B2 (cec C R)) $;

--| Class union distributes over the intersection of two subclasses of a quotient space. Compare ~ uniin . (Contributed by FL, 25-May-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem uniinqs (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wer X R $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 (cqs A2 R)) (wss C (cqs A2 R))) (wceq (cuni (cin B2 C)) (cin (cuni B2) (cuni C))) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about a function lift. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (cec (cv x3) R) (cqs X R)) $;

--| ` F ` , a function lift, is a subset of ` R X. S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftrel {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cxp (cqs X R) Y)) $;

--| Elementhood in the relation ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftel {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (C D R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cec C R) D F) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr C (cv x3) R) (wceq D A2)) X)) $;

--| Elementhood in the relation ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftel1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wbr (cec (cv x3) R) A2 F) $;

--| The function ` F ` is the unique function defined by ` F `` [ x ] = A ` , provided that the well-definedness condition holds. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftfun {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (R: class) (F: class x3) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wfun F) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wceq A2 B2))))) $;

--| The function ` F ` is the unique function defined by ` F `` [ x ] = A ` , provided that the well-definedness condition holds. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftfund {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (R: class) (F: class x3) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $;

--| The function ` F ` is the unique function defined by ` F `` [ x ] = A ` , provided that the well-definedness condition holds. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftfuns {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (F: class x3) (V: class x3 y1 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wfun F) (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) (wceq (csb x3 (cv y1) A2) (csb x3 (cv z) A2)))))) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wfun F) (wf (cqs X R) Y F)) $;

--| The value of the function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 3-Aug-2024.)
theorem qliftval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C R: class) (F V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 X (cop (cec (cv x3) R) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer X R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C X)) (wceq (cfv (cec C R) F) B2) $;

--| Implicit substitution of class for equivalence class of ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem ecoptocl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class) (S: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cqs (cxp B2 C) R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) R) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) C)) ph $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) ps $;

--| Implicit substitution of classes for equivalence classes of ordered pairs. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-1995.)
theorem _2ecoptocl {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (ps: wff z w) (ch: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (C D R: class) (S: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cqs (cxp C D) R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) R) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) R) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) C) (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wa (wcel (cv z) C) (wcel (cv w) D))) ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) ch $;

--| Implicit substitution of classes for equivalence classes of ordered pairs. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.)
theorem _3ecoptocl {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v u) (ps: wff z w v u) (ch: wff x3 y1 v u) (th: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3 y1 z w) (D R: class) (S: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cqs (cxp D D) R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) R) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) R) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) R) C) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wa (wcel (cv z) D) (wcel (cv w) D)) (wa (wcel (cv v) D) (wcel (cv u) D))) ph $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S) (wcel C S)) th $;

--| Binary relation on a quotient set. Lemma for real number construction. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1996.)
theorem brecop {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D c_sm: class) (G: class x3 y1) (H: class z w v u) (c_le: class x3 y1 z w v u):
  $ wcel c_sm cvv $ >
  $ wer (cxp G G) c_sm $ >
  $ wceq H (cqs (cxp G G) c_sm) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) H) (wcel (cv y1) H)) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm))) ph))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) G) (wcel (cv w) G)) (wa (wcel A2 G) (wcel B2 G))) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) G) (wcel (cv u) G)) (wa (wcel C G) (wcel D G)))) (wi (wa (wceq (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) (cec (cop A2 B2) c_sm)) (wceq (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm) (cec (cop C D) c_sm))) (wb ph ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 G) (wcel B2 G)) (wa (wcel C G) (wcel D G))) (wb (wbr (cec (cop A2 B2) c_sm) (cec (cop C D) c_sm) c_le) ps) $;

--| Binary relation on a quotient set. Lemma for real number construction. Eliminates antecedent from last hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-1996.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2022.)
theorem brecop2 (A2 B2 C D c_pl c_sm R G H c_le: class):
  $ wceq (cdm c_sm) (cxp G G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cqs (cxp G G) c_sm) $ >
  $ wss R (cxp H H) $ >
  $ wss c_le (cxp G G) $ >
  $ wn (wcel c0 G) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm c_pl) (cxp G G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 G) (wcel B2 G)) (wa (wcel C G) (wcel D G))) (wb (wbr (cec (cop A2 B2) c_sm) (cec (cop C D) c_sm) R) (wbr (co A2 D c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl) c_le)) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cec (cop A2 B2) c_sm) (cec (cop C D) c_sm) R) (wbr (co A2 D c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl) c_le) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erov and ~ eroprf . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2014.)
theorem eroveu {z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class z u t s1 r q p) (c_pl R S T: class) (U: class z u t s1 r q p) (J K: class u t s1 r) (V W: class z u t s1 r q p) (X Y: class) (Z: class z u t s1 r q p):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq K (cqs B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer W T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 B2) C c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) B2) (wcel (cv u) B2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) R) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) S)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) T)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel X J) (wcel Y K))) (weu z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq X (cec (cv p) R)) (wceq Y (cec (cv q) S))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) T))) B2) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erov and ~ eroprf . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem erovlem {x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (J K: class u t s1 r) (V W Z: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq K (cqs B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer W T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 B2) C c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) B2) (wcel (cv u) B2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) R) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) S)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) T)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) R)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) S))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) T))) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pd (cmpo x3 y1 J K (cio z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) R)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) S))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) T))) B2) A2)))) $;

--| The value of an operation defined on equivalence classes. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem erov {x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (P c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (Q R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (J K: class u t s1 r) (V W: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (X Y: class x3 y1) (Z: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq K (cqs B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer W T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 B2) C c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) B2) (wcel (cv u) B2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) R) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) S)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) T)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) R)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) S))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) T))) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S Y) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel P A2) (wcel Q B2)) (wceq (co (cec P R) (cec Q S) c_pd) (cec (co P Q c_pl) T)) $;

--| Functionality of an operation defined on equivalence classes. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2014.)
theorem eroprf {x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (R S T: class) (U: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (J K L: class u t s1 r) (V W: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (X Y: class x3 y1) (Z: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq K (cqs B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer W T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 B2) C c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) B2) (wcel (cv u) B2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) R) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) S)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) T)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) R)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) S))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) T))) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S Y) $ >
  $ wceq L (cqs C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp J K) L c_pd) $;

--| The value of an operation defined on equivalence classes. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.)
theorem erov2 {x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 P c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (Q c_sm: class) (U: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (J: class u t s1 r) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 c_sm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) c_sm)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) c_sm))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) c_sm))) A2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 A2) A2 c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) A2) (wcel (cv u) A2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) c_sm) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel P A2) (wcel Q A2)) (wceq (co (cec P c_sm) (cec Q c_sm) c_pd) (cec (co P Q c_pl) c_sm)) $;

--| Functionality of an operation defined on equivalence classes. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.)
theorem eroprf2 {x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (c_sm: class) (U: class x3 y1 z u t s1 r q p) (J: class u t s1 r) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cqs A2 c_sm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) c_sm)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) c_sm))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) c_sm))) A2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer U c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 A2) A2 c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv r) A2) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wa (wcel (cv t) A2) (wcel (cv u) A2)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) c_sm) (wbr (cv t) (cv u) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv r) (cv t) c_pl) (co (cv s1) (cv u) c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp J J) J c_pd) $;

--| This is the first of several theorems about equivalence relations of the kind used in construction of fractions and signed reals, involving operations on equivalent classes of ordered pairs. This theorem expresses the relation ` .~ ` (specified by the hypothesis) in terms of its operation ` F ` . (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1995.)
theorem ecopoveq {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C D c_pl: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 z w v u) (S: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp S S)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp S S))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) c_pl) (co (cv w) (cv v) c_pl))))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wa (wcel C S) (wcel D S))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_sm) (wceq (co A2 D c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl))) $;

--| Assuming the operation ` F ` is commutative, show that the relation ` .~ ` , specified by the first hypothesis, is symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ecopovsym {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v u) (c_pl: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 z w v u) (S: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp S S)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp S S))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) c_pl) (co (cv w) (cv v) c_pl))))))))) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 c_sm) (wbr B2 A2 c_sm) $;

--| Assuming that operation ` F ` is commutative (second hypothesis), closed (third hypothesis), associative (fourth hypothesis), and has the cancellation property (fifth hypothesis), show that the relation ` .~ ` , specified by the first hypothesis, is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ecopovtrn {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3 y1 z w v u) (c_pl: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 z w v u) (S: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp S S)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp S S))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) c_pl) (co (cv w) (cv v) c_pl))))))))) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wi (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 c_sm) (wbr B2 C c_sm)) (wbr A2 C c_sm) $;

--| Assuming that operation ` F ` is commutative (second hypothesis), closed (third hypothesis), associative (fourth hypothesis), and has the cancellation property (fifth hypothesis), show that the relation ` .~ ` , specified by the first hypothesis, is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem ecopover {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (c_pl: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 z w v u) (S: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp S S)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp S S))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) c_pl) (co (cv w) (cv v) c_pl))))))))) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wi (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $ >
  $ wer (cxp S S) c_sm $;

--| Equality of equivalence relation in terms of an operation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-1996.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem eceqoveq {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D c_pl: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1) (S: class):
  $ wer (cxp S S) c_sm $ >
  $ wceq (cdm c_pl) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wn (wcel c0 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wa (wcel C S) (wcel D S))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_sm) (wceq (co A2 D c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel C S)) (wb (wceq (cec (cop A2 B2) c_sm) (cec (cop C D) c_sm)) (wceq (co A2 D c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl))) $;

--| Lemma used to transfer a commutative law via an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1995.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 4-Jun-2013.)
theorem ecovcom {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3 y1) (D: class x3 y1 z w) (c_pl c_sm S: class) (G H J: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq C (cqs (cxp S S) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop D G) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop H J) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wceq D H $ >
  $ wceq G J $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co B2 A2 c_pl)) $;

--| Lemma used to transfer an associative law via an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 4-Jun-2013.)
theorem ecovass {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (c_pl: class) (Q: class x3 y1 z w v u) (c_sm S: class) (G H J K L M N: class x3 y1 z w v u):
  $ wceq D (cqs (cxp S S) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop G H) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop N Q) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G S) (wcel H S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop G H) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop J K) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel N S) (wcel Q S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop N Q) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop L M) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S))) (wa (wcel G S) (wcel H S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wa (wcel N S) (wcel Q S)) $ >
  $ wceq J L $ >
  $ wceq K M $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 D) (wcel B2 D) (wcel C D)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) C c_pl) (co A2 (co B2 C c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| Lemma used to transfer a distributive law via an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 4-Jun-2013.)
theorem ecovdi {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3 y1 z) (D: class x3 y1) (c_pl c_sm S c_x: class) (H J K L M N W X Y Z: class x3 y1 z w v u):
  $ wceq D (cqs (cxp S S) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop M N) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel M S) (wcel N S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop M N) c_sm) c_x) (cec (cop H J) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) c_sm) c_x) (cec (cop W X) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) c_sm) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) c_sm) c_x) (cec (cop Y Z) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel W S) (wcel X S)) (wa (wcel Y S) (wcel Z S))) (wceq (co (cec (cop W X) c_sm) (cec (cop Y Z) c_sm) c_pl) (cec (cop K L) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wa (wcel M S) (wcel N S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S))) (wa (wcel W S) (wcel X S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv v) S) (wcel (cv u) S))) (wa (wcel Y S) (wcel Z S)) $ >
  $ wceq H K $ >
  $ wceq J L $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 D) (wcel B2 D) (wcel C D)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C c_pl) c_x) (co (co A2 B2 c_x) (co A2 C c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the mapping operation. (Read for ` A ^m B ` , "the set of all functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` .)
term cmap: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the partial mapping operation. (Read for ` A ^pm B ` , "the set of all partial functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` .)
term cpm: class;

--| Define the mapping operation or set exponentiation. The set of all functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` is written ` ( A ^m B ) ` (see ~ mapval ). Many authors write ` A ` followed by ` B ` as a superscript for this operation and rely on context to avoid confusion other exponentiation operations (e.g., Definition 10.42 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 95). Other authors show ` B ` as a prefixed superscript, which is read " ` A ` pre ` B ` " (e.g., definition of [Enderton] p. 52). Definition 8.21 of [Eisenberg] p. 125 uses the notation Map( ` B ` , ` A ` ) for our ` ( A ^m B ) ` . The up-arrow is used by Donald Knuth for iterated exponentiation (_Science_ 194, 1235-1242, 1976). We adopt the first case of his notation (simple exponentiation) and subscript it with _m_ to distinguish it from other kinds of exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.)
axiom df_map {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmap (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cab f2 (wf (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv f2)))) $;

--| Define the partial mapping operation. A partial function from ` B ` to ` A ` is a function from a subset of ` B ` to ` A ` . The set of all partial functions from ` B ` to ` A ` is written ` ( A ^pm B ) ` (see ~ pmvalg ). A notation for this operation apparently does not appear in the literature. We use ` ^pm ` to distinguish it from the less general set exponentiation operation ` ^m ` ( ~ df-map ). See ~ mapsspm for its relationship to set exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.)
axiom df_pm {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpm (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (crab f2 (wfun (cv f2)) (cpw (cxp (cv y1) (cv x3))))) $;

--| When ` A ` is a proper class, the class of all functions mapping ` A ` to ` B ` is empty. Exercise 4.41 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.)
theorem mapprc {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) c0) $;

--| The class of all partial functions from one set to another is a set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.)
theorem pmex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wcel (cab f2 (wa (wfun (cv f2)) (wss (cv f2) (cxp A2 B2)))) cvv) $;

--| Set exponentiation has a universal domain. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem fnmap:
  $ wfn cmap (cxp cvv cvv) $;

--| Partial function exponentiation has a universal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem fnpm:
  $ wfn cpm (cxp cvv cvv) $;

--| Set exponentiation is a well-behaved binary operator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem reldmmap:
  $ wrel (cdm cmap) $;

--| The value of set exponentiation. ` ( A ^m B ) ` is the set of all functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` . Definition 10.24 of [Kunen] p. 24. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem mapvalg {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmap) (cab f2 (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)))) $;

--| The value of the partial mapping operation. ` ( A ^pm B ) ` is the set of all partial functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2013.)
theorem pmvalg {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cpm) (crab f2 (wfun (cv f2)) (cpw (cxp B2 A2)))) $;

--| The value of set exponentiation (inference version). ` ( A ^m B ) ` is the set of all functions that map from ` B ` to ` A ` . Definition 10.24 of [Kunen] p. 24. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.)
theorem mapval {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmap) (cab f2 (wf B2 A2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Membership relation for set exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem elmapg (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cmap)) (wf B2 A2 C)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ elmapg . (Contributed by BJ, 11-Apr-2020.)
theorem elmapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cmap)) (wf B2 A2 C)) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ elmapd . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jul-2024.)
theorem elmapdd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cmap)) $;

--| The empty set is the only map with empty domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2021.)
theorem mapdm0 (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (co B2 c0 cmap) (csn c0)) $;

--| The predicate "is a partial function". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem elpmg (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wa (wfun C) (wss C (cxp B2 A2)))) $;

--| The predicate "is a partial function". (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem elpm2g (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wa (wf (cdm F) A2 F) (wss (cdm F) B2))) $;

--| Sufficient condition for being a partial function. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem elpm2r (A2 B2 C F V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wa (wf C A2 F) (wss C B2))) (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) $;

--| A partial function is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2015.)
theorem elpmi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wa (wf (cdm F) A2 F) (wss (cdm F) B2)) $;

--| A partial function is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem pmfun (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wfun F) $;

--| Eliminate antecedent for mapping theorems: domain can be taken to be a set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Oct-2014.)
theorem elmapex (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (wa (wcel B2 cvv) (wcel C cvv)) $;

--| A mapping is a function, forward direction only with superfluous antecedent removed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Oct-2014.)
theorem elmapi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (wf C B2 A2) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ elmapd . Reverse direction of ~ elmapdd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Oct-2025.) Removed redundant hypotheses. (Revised by SN, 30-Aug-2026.)
theorem elmaprd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elmaprd as of 30-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Oct-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elmaprdOLD (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| If ` B ` is a set, the value of the set exponentiation ` ( B ^m A ) ` is the class of all functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . Generalisation of ~ mapvalg (which does not require ~ ax-rep ) to arbitrary domains. Note that the class ` { f | f : A --> B } ` can only contain set-functions, as opposed to arbitrary class-functions. When ` A ` is a proper class, there can be no set-functions on it, so the above class is empty (see also ~ fsetdmprc0 ), hence a set. In this case, both sides of the equality in this theorem are the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 8-Aug-2024.)
theorem mapfset {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $;

--| The value of the set exponentiation ` ( B ^m A ) ` is a subset of the class of functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2024.)
theorem mapssfset {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co B2 A2 cmap) (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| The value of the set exponentiation ` ( B ^m A ) ` is a superset of the set of all functions from ` A ` onto ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 7-Aug-2024.)
theorem mapfoss {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (cab f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2))) (co B2 A2 cmap) $;

--| The class of all functions from ` A ` into ` B ` is a subclass of the power class of the cartesion product of ` A ` and ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsspwxp {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) (cpw (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| The set of functions from the empty set is the singleton containing the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fset0 {f2: setvar} (B2: class f2):
  $ wceq (cab f2 (wf c0 B2 (cv f2))) (csn c0) $;

--| The set of functions with a proper class as domain is empty. (Contributed by AV, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem fsetdmprc0 {f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wnel A2 cvv) (wceq (cab f2 (wfn (cv f2) A2)) c0) $;

--| The set of functions between two classes exists if the codomain exists. Generalization of ~ mapex to arbitrary domains. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2024.)
theorem fsetex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wcel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| The set of injections between two classes exists if the codomain exists. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2024.)
theorem f1setex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wcel (cab f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| The set of surjections between two classes exists (without any precondition). (Contributed by AV, 8-Aug-2024.)
theorem fosetex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (cab f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv $;

--| The set of bijections between two classes exists. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2024.) (Revised by AV, 8-Aug-2024.) (Proof shortened by SN, 22-Aug-2024.)
theorem f1osetex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (cab f2 (wf1o A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv $;

--| The class of functions with a given domain and a given codomain is mapped, through evaluation at a point of the domain, into the codomain. (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetfcdm {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S: class f2 g2) (F X: class f2):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt g2 F (cfv X (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X A2) (wf F B2 S) $;

--| The class of functions with a given domain that is a set and a given codomain is mapped, through evaluation at a point of the domain, onto the codomain. (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetfocdm {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S: class f2) (F V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt g2 F (cfv X (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel X A2)) (wfo F B2 S) $;

--| The class of all functions from a nonempty set ` A ` into a proper class ` B ` is not a set. If one of the preconditions is not fufilled, then ` { f | f : A --> B } ` is a set, see ~ fsetdmprc0 for ` A e/ _V ` , ~ fset0 for ` A = (/) ` , and ~ fsetex for ` B e. _V ` , see also ~ fsetexb . (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetprcnex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wne A2 c0)) (wnel B2 cvv)) (wnel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| The class of all functions from a nonempty set ` A ` into a class ` B ` is a set iff ` B ` is a set . (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetcdmex {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wne A2 c0)) (wb (wcel B2 cvv) (wcel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv)) $;

--| The class of all functions from a class ` A ` into a class ` B ` is a set iff ` B ` is a set or ` A ` is not a set or ` A ` is empty. (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetexb {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) (w3o (wnel A2 cvv) (wceq A2 c0) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| A mapping is a function with the appropriate domain. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
theorem elmapfn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (wfn A2 C) $;

--| A mapping is always a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-May-2015.)
theorem elmapfun (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (wfun A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for currying. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem cureq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (ccur A2) (ccur B2)) $;

--| Functional property of currying. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem curf (A2 B2 C F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf (cxp A2 B2) C F) (wcel B2 (cdif V (csn c0))) (wcel C W)) (wf A2 (co C B2 cmap) (ccur F)) $;

--| Functional property of uncurrying. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem uncf (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 (co C B2 cmap) F) (wf (cxp A2 B2) C (cunc F)) $;

--| Value of currying. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem curfv (A2 B2 F V W X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfn F (cxp V W)) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel W X)) (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv A2 (ccur F))) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Value of uncurrying. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem uncov (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co A2 B2 (cunc F)) (cfv B2 (cfv A2 F))) $;

--| A restricted mapping is a mapping. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem elmapssres (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (wss D C)) (wcel (cres A2 D) (co B2 D cmap)) $;

--| A restricted mapping is a mapping. EDITORIAL: Could be used to shorten ~ elpm2r with some reordering involving ~ mapsspm . (Contributed by SN, 11-Mar-2025.)
theorem elmapssresd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 C cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres A2 D) (co B2 D cmap)) $;

--| A total function is a partial function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem fpmg (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wf A2 B2 F)) (wcel F (co B2 A2 cpm)) $;

--| Subset relation for the set of partial functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem pmss12g (A2 B2 C D V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss A2 C) (wss B2 D)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D W))) (wss (co A2 B2 cpm) (co C D cpm)) $;

--| Elementhood of a restricted function in the set of partial functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem pmresg (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel F (co A2 C cpm))) (wcel (cres F B2) (co A2 B2 cpm)) $;

--| Membership relation for set exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2003.)
theorem elmap (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 cmap)) (wf B2 A2 F) $;

--| Alternate expression for the value of set exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2007.)
theorem mapval2 {f2: setvar} (A2: class f2) (B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmap) (cin (cpw (cxp B2 A2)) (cab f2 (wfn (cv f2) B2))) $;

--| The predicate "is a partial function". (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem elpm (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wa (wfun F) (wss F (cxp B2 A2))) $;

--| The predicate "is a partial function". (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem elpm2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wa (wf (cdm F) A2 F) (wss (cdm F) B2)) $;

--| A total function is a partial function. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem fpm (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel F (co B2 A2 cpm)) $;

--| Set exponentiation is a subset of partial maps. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem mapsspm (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cmap) (co A2 B2 cpm) $;

--| Partial maps are a subset of the power set of the Cartesian product of its arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem pmsspw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cpm) (cpw (cxp B2 A2)) $;

--| Set exponentiation is a subset of the power set of the Cartesian product of its arguments. (Contributed by NM, 8-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapsspw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cmap) (cpw (cxp B2 A2)) $;

--| The value of a function that maps from ` B ` to ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem mapfvd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M X: class):
  $ wceq M (co A2 B2 cmap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X F) A2) $;

--| ~ fresaun transposed to mappings. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-May-2015.)
theorem elmapresaun (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co C A2 cmap)) (wcel G (co C B2 cmap)) (wceq (cres F (cin A2 B2)) (cres G (cin A2 B2)))) (wcel (cun F G) (co C (cun A2 B2) cmap)) $;

--| Special case of ~ fvmpt for operator theorems. (Contributed by NM, 27-Nov-2007.)
theorem fvmptmap {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D R: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co R D cmap) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wf D R A2) (wceq (cfv A2 F) C) $;

--| Set exponentiation with an empty exponent (ordinal number 0) is ordinal number 1. Exercise 4.42(a) of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Jul-2022.)
theorem map0e (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (co A2 c0 cmap) c1o) $;

--| Set exponentiation with an empty base is the empty set, provided the exponent is nonempty. Theorem 96 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem map0b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wceq (co c0 A2 cmap) c0) $;

--| Set exponentiation is empty iff the base is empty and the exponent is not empty. Theorem 97 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem map0g (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmap) c0) (wa (wceq A2 c0) (wne B2 c0))) $;

--| The set of mappings of the empty set to the empty set is the singleton containing the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2024.)
theorem _0map0sn0:
  $ wceq (co c0 c0 cmap) (csn c0) $;

--| The value of set exponentiation with a singleton exponent. Theorem 98 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem mapsnd {y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class y1 f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (csn B2) cmap) (cab f2 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv f2) (csn (cop B2 (cv y1)))) A2))) $;

--| Set exponentiation is empty iff the base is empty and the exponent is not empty. Theorem 97 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.)
theorem map0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmap) c0) (wa (wceq A2 c0) (wne B2 c0)) $;

--| The value of set exponentiation with a singleton exponent. Theorem 98 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem mapsn {y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (csn B2) cmap) (cab f2 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv f2) (csn (cop B2 (cv y1)))) A2)) $;

--| Subset inheritance for set exponentiation. Theorem 99 of [Suppes] p. 89. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapss (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap)) $;

--| Functionality of the diagonal map. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem fdiagfn {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (F: class x3) (I V W: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cxp I (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel I W)) (wf B2 (co B2 I cmap) F) $;

--| Functionality of the diagonal map. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem fvdiagfn {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (F: class x3) (I W X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cxp I (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I W) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cxp I (csn X))) $;

--| Every singleton map is a constant function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapsnconst (B2 S F X: class):
  $ wceq S (csn X) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co B2 S cmap)) (wceq F (cxp S (csn (cfv X F)))) $;

--| Expression for the inverse of the canonical map between a set and its set of singleton functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapsncnv {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq S (csn X) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co B2 S cmap) (cfv X (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 B2 (cxp S (csn (cv y1)))) $;

--| Explicit bijection between a set and its singleton functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapsnf1o2 {x3: setvar} (B2 S: class) (F X: class x3):
  $ wceq S (csn X) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co B2 S cmap) (cfv X (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wf1o (co B2 S cmap) B2 F $;

--| Explicit bijection in the reverse of ~ mapsnf1o2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapsnf1o3 {y1: setvar} (B2 S: class) (F: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq S (csn X) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 B2 (cxp S (csn (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wf1o B2 (co B2 S cmap) F $;

--| Quantification over functions in terms of quantification over values and punctured functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-May-2015.)
theorem ralxpmap {y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (ps: wff y1 g2) (S T J: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv f2) (cun (cv g2) (csn (cop J (cv y1))))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J T) (wb (wral f2 ph (co S T cmap)) (wral y1 (wral g2 ps (co S (cdif T (csn J)) cmap)) S)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include infinite Cartesian products.
term cixp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3): class;

--| Definition of infinite Cartesian product of [Enderton] p. 54. Enderton uses a bold "X" with ` x e. A ` written underneath or as a subscript, as does Stoll p. 47. Some books use a capital pi, but we will reserve that notation for products of numbers. Usually ` B ` represents a class expression containing ` x ` free and thus can be thought of as ` B ( x ) ` . Normally, ` x ` is not free in ` A ` , although this is not a requirement of the definition. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
axiom df_ixp {x3 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cab f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Eliminate the expression ` { x | x e. A } ` in ~ df-ixp , under the assumption that ` A ` and ` x ` are disjoint. This way, we can say that ` x ` is bound in ` X_ x e. A B ` even if it appears free in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2016.)
theorem dfixp {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cab f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| The value of an infinite Cartesian product with a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 3-Dec-2018.)
theorem ixpsnval {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 V: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cixp x3 (csn X) B2) (cab f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (csn X)) (wcel (cfv X (cv f2)) (csb x3 X B2))))) $;

--| Membership in an infinite Cartesian product. See ~ df-ixp for discussion of the notation. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem elixp2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (w3a (wcel F cvv) (wfn F A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| Projection of a factor of an indexed Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2016.)
theorem fvixp {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cfv C F) D) $;

--| A nuple is a function. (Contributed by FL, 6-Jun-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2014.)
theorem ixpfn {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 F: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wfn F A2) $;

--| Membership in an infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem elixp {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wa (wfn F A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| Membership in an infinite Cartesian product of a constant ` B ` . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2008.)
theorem elixpconst {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| Infinite Cartesian product of a constant ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem ixpconstg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $;

--| Infinite Cartesian product of a constant ` B ` . (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem ixpconst {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co B2 A2 cmap) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem ixpeq1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2016.)
theorem ixpeq1d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Subclass theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2016.)
theorem ss2ixp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wss B2 C) A2) (wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem ixpeq2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wceq B2 C) A2) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2016.)
theorem ixpeq2dva {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2016.)
theorem ixpeq2dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Change bound variable in an indexed Cartesian product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 20-Jun-2011.)
theorem cbvixp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc y1 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp y1 A2 C) $;

--| Change bound variable in an indexed Cartesian product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem cbvixpv {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp y1 A2 C) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for indexed Cartesian product. Version of ~ nfixp with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 26-Jan-2024.)
theorem nfixpw {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 (cixp x3 A2 B2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for indexed Cartesian product. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use the weaker ~ nfixpw when possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nfixp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 (cixp x3 A2 B2) $;

--| The index variable in an indexed Cartesian product is not free. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfixp1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 (cixp x3 A2 B2) $;

--| A cartesian product of proper-class many sets is empty, because any function in the cartesian product has to be a set with domain ` A ` , which is not possible for a proper class domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem ixpprc {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0) $;

--| A member of an infinite Cartesian product maps to the indexed union of the product argument. Remark in [Enderton] p. 54. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem ixpf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wf A2 (ciun x3 A2 B2) F) $;

--| The union of an infinite Cartesian product is included in a Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem uniixp {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wss (cuni (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (cxp A2 (ciun x3 A2 B2)) $;

--| The existence of an infinite Cartesian product. ` x ` is normally a free-variable parameter in ` B ` . Remark in Enderton p. 54. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem ixpexg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel B2 V) A2) (wcel (cixp x3 A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The intersection of two infinite Cartesian products. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ixpin {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 (cin B2 C)) (cin (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| The indexed intersection of a collection of infinite Cartesian products. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2015.)
theorem ixpiin {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wne B2 c0) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 (ciin y1 B2 C)) (ciin y1 B2 (cixp x3 A2 C))) $;

--| The intersection of a collection of infinite Cartesian products. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ixpint {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wne B2 c0) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 (cint B2)) (ciin y1 B2 (cixp x3 A2 (cv y1)))) $;

--| An infinite Cartesian product with an empty index set. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-2007.)
theorem ixp0x {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cixp x3 c0 A2) (csn c0) $;

--| An infinite Cartesian product is a subset of set exponentiation. This version of ~ ixpssmapg avoids ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem ixpssmap2g {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) V) (wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co (ciun x3 A2 B2) A2 cmap)) $;

--| An infinite Cartesian product is a subset of set exponentiation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem ixpssmapg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel B2 V) A2) (wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co (ciun x3 A2 B2) A2 cmap)) $;

--| Membership of the empty set in an infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem _0elixp {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wcel c0 (cixp x3 c0 A2) $;

--| The infinite Cartesian product of a family ` B ( x ) ` with an empty member is empty. The converse of this theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice, see ~ ac9 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2016.)
theorem ixpn0 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wne (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0) (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) $;

--| The infinite Cartesian product of a family ` B ( x ) ` with an empty member is empty. The converse of this theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice, see ~ ac9 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2016.)
theorem ixp0 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wceq B2 c0) A2) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0) $;

--| An infinite Cartesian product is a subset of set exponentiation. Remark in [Enderton] p. 54. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem ixpssmap {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co (ciun x3 A2 B2) A2 cmap) $;

--| Restriction of an element of an infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by FL, 7-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2014.)
theorem resixp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 A2) (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 C))) (wcel (cres F B2) (cixp x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Union of two projections of a cartesian product. (Contributed by FL, 7-Nov-2011.)
theorem undifixp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cixp x3 B2 C)) (wcel G (cixp x3 (cdif A2 B2) C)) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel (cun F G) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Condition for an explicit member of an indexed product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Jan-2015.)
theorem mptelixpg {x3: setvar} (I: class) (J K V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 I J) (cixp x3 I K)) (wral x3 (wcel J K) I)) $;

--| Restriction of elements of an infinite Cartesian product creates a surjection, if the original Cartesian product is nonempty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem resixpfo {x3 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (F: class x3 f2):
  $ wceq F (cmpt f2 (cixp x3 A2 C) (cres (cv f2) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 A2) (wne (cixp x3 A2 C) c0)) (wfo (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 C) F) $;

--| Membership in a class of singleton functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem elixpsn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel F (cixp x3 (csn A2) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq F (csn (cop A2 (cv y1)))) B2)) $;

--| A bijection between a class and single-point functions to it. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem ixpsnf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (I V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cxp (csn I) (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wf1o A2 (cixp y1 (csn I) A2) F) $;

--| A bijection between a set and single-point functions to it. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem mapsnf1o {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (I V W: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cxp (csn I) (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel I W)) (wf1o A2 (co A2 (csn I) cmap) F) $;

--| A rectangular subset of a rectangular set can be recovered as the relative intersection of single-axis restrictions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem boxriin {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (I: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wss A2 B2) I) (wceq (cixp x3 I A2) (cin (cixp x3 I B2) (ciin y1 I (cixp x3 I (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2 B2))))) $;

--| The relative complement of a box set restricted on one axis. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem boxcutc {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class k) (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wral k (wss C B2) A2)) (wceq (cdif (cixp k A2 B2) (cixp k A2 (cif (wceq (cv k) X) C B2))) (cixp k A2 (cif (wceq (cv k) X) (cdif B2 C) B2))) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the equinumerosity relation ("approximately equals" symbol)
term cen: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the dominance relation (curly "less than or equal to")
term cdom: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the strict dominance relation (curly less-than)
term csdm: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of all finite sets.
term cfn: class;

--| Define the equinumerosity relation. Definition of [Enderton] p. 129. We define ` ~~ ` to be a binary relation rather than a connective, so its arguments must be sets to be meaningful. This is acceptable because we do not consider equinumerosity for proper classes. We derive the usual definition as ~ bren . (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
axiom df_en {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cen (copab x3 y1 (wex f2 (wf1o (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2)))) $;

--| Define the dominance relation. For an alternate definition see ~ dfdom2 . Compare Definition of [Enderton] p. 145. Typical textbook definitions are derived as ~ brdom and ~ domen . (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
axiom df_dom {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdom (copab x3 y1 (wex f2 (wf1 (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2)))) $;

--| Define the strict dominance relation. Alternate possible definitions are derived as ~ brsdom and ~ brsdom2 . Definition 3 of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1998.)
axiom df_sdom:
  $ wceq csdm (cdif cdom cen) $;

--| Define the (proper) class of all finite sets. Similar to Definition 10.29 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91, whose "Fin(a)" corresponds to our " ` a e. Fin ` ". This definition is meaningful whether or not we accept the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 . If we accept Infinity, we can also express ` A e. Fin ` by ` A ~< _om ` (Theorem ~ isfinite .) (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.)
axiom df_fin {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfn (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cen) com)) $;

--| Equinumerosity is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
theorem relen:
  $ wrel cen $;

--| Dominance is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
theorem reldom:
  $ wrel cdom $;

--| Strict dominance is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1998.)
theorem relsdom:
  $ wrel csdm $;

--| If two classes are equinumerous, both classes are sets. (Contributed by AV, 21-Mar-2019.)
theorem encv (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| Equinumerosity relation. This variation of ~ bren does not require the Axiom of Union. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.) Extract from a subproof of ~ bren . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem breng {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wex f2 (wf1o A2 B2 (cv f2)))) $;

--| Equinumerosity relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.) Extract ~ breng as an intermediate result. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem bren {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wex f2 (wf1o A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Dominance relation. This variation of ~ brdomg does not require the Axiom of Union. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.) Extract from a subproof of ~ brdomg . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem brdom2g {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2)))) $;

--| Dominance relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.) Extract ~ brdom2g as an intermediate result. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem brdomg {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2)))) $;

--| Dominance relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem brdomi {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Dominance relation. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.)
theorem brdom {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Dominance in terms of equinumerosity. Example 1 of [Enderton] p. 146. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.)
theorem domen {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cen) (wss (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Dominance in terms of equinumerosity, with the sethood requirement expressed as an antecedent. Example 1 of [Enderton] p. 146. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.)
theorem domeng {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cen) (wss (cv x3) B2)))) $;

--| A countable set is a set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem ctex (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The domain and range of a one-to-one, onto set function are equinumerous. This variation of ~ f1oeng does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets nor the Axiom of Union. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem f1oen4g (A2 B2 F V W X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 W) (wcel B2 X)) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| The domain of a one-to-one set function is dominated by its codomain when the latter is a set. This variation of ~ f1domg does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets nor the Axiom of Union. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem f1dom4g (A2 B2 F V W X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 W) (wcel B2 X)) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| The domain and range of a one-to-one, onto set function are equinumerous. This variation of ~ f1oeng does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem f1oen3g (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| The domain of a one-to-one set function is dominated by its codomain when the latter is a set. This variation of ~ f1domg does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1dom3g (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel B2 W) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| The domain and range of a one-to-one, onto function are equinumerous. This variation of ~ f1oeng does not require the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem f1oen2g (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| The domain of a one-to-one function is dominated by its codomain. This variation of ~ f1domg does not require the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.) (Proof shortened by BTernaryTau, 25-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1dom2g (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| The domain and range of a one-to-one, onto function are equinumerous. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jun-1998.)
theorem f1oeng (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| The domain of a one-to-one function is dominated by its codomain. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2004.)
theorem f1domg (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wi (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| The domain and range of a one-to-one, onto function are equinumerous. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jun-1998.)
theorem f1oen (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| The domain of a one-to-one function is dominated by its codomain. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jun-1998.)
theorem f1dom (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Strict dominance relation, meaning " ` B ` is strictly greater in size than ` A ` ". Definition of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jun-1998.)
theorem brsdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr A2 B2 cen))) $;

--| Express " ` A ` is finite". Definition 10.29 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91 (whose " ` Fin ` " is a predicate instead of a class). (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.)
theorem isfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) cen) com) $;

--| Equinumerosity implies dominance. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1998.) (Proof shortened by TM, 10-Feb-2026.)
theorem enssdom:
  $ wss cen cdom $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ enssdom as of 10-Feb-2026. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1998.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enssdomOLD:
  $ wss cen cdom $;

--| Alternate definition of dominance. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-1998.)
theorem dfdom2:
  $ wceq cdom (cun csdm cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity implies dominance. Theorem 15 of [Suppes] p. 94. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1998.)
theorem endom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Strict dominance implies dominance. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-1998.)
theorem sdomdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Strict dominance implies non-equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-1998.)
theorem sdomnen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wn (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Dominance in terms of strict dominance and equinumerosity. Theorem 22(iv) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-1998.)
theorem brdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wo (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Equinumerosity expressed in terms of dominance and strict dominance. (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.)
theorem bren2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr A2 B2 csdm))) $;

--| Equinumerosity is reflexive. Theorem 1 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem enrefg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 A2 cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity is reflexive. Theorem 1 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem enref (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr A2 A2 cen $;

--| Equality implies equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.)
theorem eqeng (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Dominance is reflexive. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jun-1998.)
theorem domrefg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 A2 cdom) $;

--| Equinumerosity inference from an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.) (Revised by AV, 4-Aug-2024.)
theorem en2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (V W X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel D Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) C)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity inference from an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.) (Revised by AV, 4-Aug-2024.)
theorem en3d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel D A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq (cv y1) C))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity inference from an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.)
theorem en2i {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel D cvv) $ >
  $ wb (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) C)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) D)) $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $;

--| Equinumerosity inference from an implicit one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2004.)
theorem en3i {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel D A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wb (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq (cv y1) C)) $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $;

--| A mapping (first hypothesis) that is one-to-one (second hypothesis) implies its domain is dominated by its codomain. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2004.)
theorem dom2lem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wb (wceq C D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 A2 B2 (cmpt x3 A2 C)) $;

--| A mapping (first hypothesis) that is one-to-one (second hypothesis) implies its domain is dominated by its codomain. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2013.)
theorem dom2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (R: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wb (wceq C D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel B2 R) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| A mapping (first hypothesis) that is one-to-one (second hypothesis) implies its domain is dominated by its codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2013.)
theorem dom3d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wb (wceq C D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| A mapping (first hypothesis) that is one-to-one (second hypothesis) implies its domain is dominated by its codomain. ` C ` and ` D ` can be read ` C ( x ) ` and ` D ( y ) ` , as can be inferred from their distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.)
theorem dom2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wb (wceq C D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| A mapping (first hypothesis) that is one-to-one (second hypothesis) implies its domain is dominated by its codomain. ` C ` and ` D ` can be read ` C ( x ) ` and ` D ( y ) ` , as can be inferred from their distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2013.)
theorem dom3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1) (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wb (wceq C D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Equality implies equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem idssen:
  $ wss cid cen $;

--| If ` A ` is a subset of ` B ` and ` C ` dominates ` B ` , then ` C ` also dominates ` A ` . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem domssl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| If ` C ` is a superset of ` B ` and ` B ` dominates ` A ` , then ` C ` also dominates ` A ` . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem domssr (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C V) (wss B2 C) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| A set dominates its subsets. Theorem 16 of [Suppes] p. 94. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ssdomg (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| Equinumerosity is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by NM, 19-Mar-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem ener:
  $ wer cvv cen $;

--| Symmetry of equinumerosity. Theorem 2 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ensymb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 A2 cen) $;

--| Symmetry of equinumerosity. Theorem 2 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ensym (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 A2 cen) $;

--| Symmetry of equinumerosity. Theorem 2 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem ensymi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr B2 A2 cen $;

--| Symmetry of equinumerosity. Deduction form of ~ ensym . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ensymd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cen) $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity. Theorem 3 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jun-1998.)
theorem entr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C cen) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance relation. Theorem 17 of [Suppes] p. 94. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem domtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| A chained equinumerosity inference. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem entri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr B2 C cen $ >
  $ wbr A2 C cen $;

--| A chained equinumerosity inference. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem entr2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr B2 C cen $ >
  $ wbr C A2 cen $;

--| A chained equinumerosity inference. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem entr3i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr A2 C cen $ >
  $ wbr B2 C cen $;

--| A chained equinumerosity inference. (Contributed by NM, 25-Sep-2004.)
theorem entr4i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr C B2 cen $ >
  $ wbr A2 C cen $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity and dominance. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-1998.)
theorem endomtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance and equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-1998.)
theorem domentr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| If a function is one-to-one, then the image of a subset of its domain under it is equinumerous to the subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem f1imaeng (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wss C A2) (wcel C V)) (wbr (cima F C) C cen) $;

--| If a function is one-to-one, then the image of a subset of its domain under it is equinumerous to the subset. (This version of ~ f1imaeng does not need ~ ax-rep .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem f1imaen2g (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wss C A2) (wcel C V))) (wbr (cima F C) C cen) $;

--| If a set function is one-to-one, then a subset of its domain is equinumerous to the image of that subset. (This version of ~ f1imaeng does not need ~ ax-rep nor ~ ax-pow .) (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem f1imaen3g (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wss C A2) (wcel F V)) (wbr C (cima F C) cen) $;

--| If a function is one-to-one, then the image of a subset of its domain under it is equinumerous to the subset. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-2004.)
theorem f1imaen (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wss C A2)) (wbr (cima F C) C cen) $;

--| The empty set is equinumerous only to itself. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 88. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem en0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c0 cen) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ en0 , depending on ~ ax-pow and ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1998.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem en0ALT (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c0 cen) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| The empty set is equinumerous only to itself. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem en0r (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr c0 A2 cen) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| A singleton is equinumerous to ordinal one. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2002.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem ensn1 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (csn A2) c1o cen $;

--| A singleton is equinumerous to ordinal one. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2004.)
theorem ensn1g (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (csn A2) c1o cen) $;

--| ` { A , A } ` has only one element. (Contributed by FL, 15-Feb-2010.)
theorem enpr1g (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cpr A2 A2) c1o cen) $;

--| A set is equinumerous to ordinal one iff it is a singleton. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem en1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c1o cen) (wex x3 (wceq A2 (csn (cv x3)))) $;

--| A set is equinumerous to ordinal one iff it is a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 24-Sep-2024.)
theorem en1b (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c1o cen) (wceq A2 (csn (cuni A2))) $;

--| Two ways to express "exactly one". (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Oct-2014.)
theorem reuen1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wreu x3 ph A2) (wbr (crab x3 ph A2) c1o cen) $;

--| Two ways to express "exactly one". (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Oct-2014.)
theorem euen1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wbr (cab x3 ph) c1o cen) $;

--| Two ways to express " ` A ` has a unique element". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem euen1b {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c1o cen) (weu x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| If a function is equinumerous to ordinal 1, then its converse is also a function. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Oct-2023.)
theorem funen1cnv (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wbr F c1o cen)) (wfun (ccnv F)) $;

--| A singleton contains its sole element. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 24-Sep-2024.)
theorem en1uniel (S: class):
  $ wi (wbr S c1o cen) (wcel (cuni S) S) $;

--| A set that dominates ordinal 2 has at least 2 different members. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-2004.)
theorem _2dom {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr c2o A2 cdom) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| A function is equinumerous to its domain. Exercise 4 of [Suppes] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem fundmen (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wfun F) (wbr (cdm F) F cen) $;

--| A function is equinumerous to its domain. Exercise 4 of [Suppes] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2013.)
theorem fundmeng (F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wfun F)) (wbr (cdm F) F cen) $;

--| A relational set is equinumerous to its converse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2014.)
theorem cnven (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel A2) (wcel A2 V)) (wbr A2 (ccnv A2) cen) $;

--| If a set is countable, so is its converse. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnvct (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (ccnv A2) com cdom) $;

--| A function is equinumerate to its domain. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem fndmeng (A2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 C)) (wbr A2 F cen) $;

--| Set exponentiation to a singleton exponent is equinumerous to its base. Exercise 4.43 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem mapsnend (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 (csn B2) cmap) A2 cen) $;

--| Set exponentiation to a singleton exponent is equinumerous to its base. Exercise 4.43 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem mapsnen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (co A2 (csn B2) cmap) A2 cen $;

--| Set exponentiation: a singleton to any set is equinumerous to that singleton. (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2003.) (Revised by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem snmapen (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (co (csn A2) B2 cmap) (csn A2) cen) $;

--| Set exponentiation: a singleton to any set is equinumerous to ordinal 1. (Proposed by BJ, 17-Jul-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem snmapen1 (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (co (csn A2) B2 cmap) c1o cen) $;

--| Set exponentiation: ordinal 1 to any set is equinumerous to ordinal 1. Exercise 4.42(b) of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2003.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem map1 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (co c1o A2 cmap) c1o cen) $;

--| Two singletons are equinumerous. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 31-Jul-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 25-Sep-2024.)
theorem en2sn (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wbr (csn A2) (csn B2) cen) $;

--| The empty set is finite. (Contributed by FL, 14-Jul-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-10 , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem _0fi:
  $ wcel c0 cfn $;

--| A singleton is finite. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2002.) (Proof shortened by BTernaryTau, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem snfi (A2: class):
  $ wcel (csn A2) cfn $;

--| The class of finite sets is a proper class. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Oct-2008.)
theorem fiprc:
  $ wnel cfn cvv $;

--| Equinumerosity of union of disjoint sets. Theorem 4 of [Suppes] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem unen (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr C D cen)) (wa (wceq (cin A2 C) c0) (wceq (cin B2 D) c0))) (wbr (cun A2 C) (cun B2 D) cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity is reflexive for finite ordinals, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ enrefg ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 31-Jul-2024.)
theorem enrefnn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wbr A2 A2 cen) $;

--| Two proper unordered pairs are equinumerous. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Dec-2024.)
theorem en2prd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D) cen) $;

--| A pair with distinct elements is equinumerous to ordinal two. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Dec-2024.)
theorem enpr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cpr A2 B2) c2o cen) $;

--| Any subset of a countable set is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem ssct (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wbr B2 com cdom)) (wbr A2 com cdom) $;

--| All decrements of a set are equinumerous. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem difsnen (A2 B2 V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr (cdif X (csn A2)) (cdif X (csn B2)) cen) $;

--| Dominance over a set with one element removed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem domdifsn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr A2 (cdif B2 (csn C)) cdom) $;

--| A set is equinumerous to its Cartesian product with a singleton. Proposition 4.22(c) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpsnen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (cxp A2 (csn B2)) A2 cen $;

--| A set is equinumerous to its Cartesian product with a singleton. Proposition 4.22(c) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2004.)
theorem xpsneng (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cxp A2 (csn B2)) A2 cen) $;

--| One times a cardinal number. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xp1en (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cxp A2 c1o) A2 cen) $;

--| Any two sets are equinumerous to two disjoint sets. Exercise 4.39 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2004.)
theorem endisj {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) (wbr (cv y1) B2 cen)) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0))) $;

--| Dominance law for union. Proposition 4.24(a) of [Mendelson] p. 257. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Dec-2024.)
theorem undom (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr C D cdom)) (wceq (cin B2 D) c0)) (wbr (cun A2 C) (cun B2 D) cdom) $;

--| The canonical bijection from ` ( A X. B ) ` to ` ( B X. A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2014.)
theorem xpcomf1o {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cxp A2 B2) (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp A2 B2) (cxp B2 A2) F $;

--| Composition with the bijection of ~ xpcomf1o swaps the arguments to a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem xpcomco {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z) (F: class x3) (G: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cxp A2 B2) (cuni (ccnv (csn (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo y1 z B2 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (ccom G F) (cmpo z y1 A2 B2 C) $;

--| Commutative law for equinumerosity of Cartesian product. Proposition 4.22(d) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpcomen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (cxp A2 B2) (cxp B2 A2) cen $;

--| Commutative law for equinumerosity of Cartesian product. Proposition 4.22(d) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2006.)
theorem xpcomeng (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cxp A2 B2) (cxp B2 A2) cen) $;

--| A set is equinumerous to its Cartesian product with a singleton on the left. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpsnen2g (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cxp (csn A2) B2) B2 cen) $;

--| Associative law for equinumerosity of Cartesian product. Proposition 4.22(e) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpassen (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wbr (cxp (cxp A2 B2) C) (cxp A2 (cxp B2 C)) cen $;

--| Dominance law for Cartesian product. Proposition 10.33(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpdom2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr (cxp C A2) (cxp C B2) cdom) $;

--| Dominance law for Cartesian product. Theorem 6L(c) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpdom2g (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) (wbr (cxp C A2) (cxp C B2) cdom) $;

--| Dominance law for Cartesian product. Theorem 6L(c) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpdom1g (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) (wbr (cxp A2 C) (cxp B2 C) cdom) $;

--| A set is dominated by its Cartesian product with a nonempty set. Exercise 6 of [Suppes] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpdom3 (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wne B2 c0)) (wbr A2 (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| Dominance law for Cartesian product. Theorem 6L(c) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by NM, 29-Mar-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem xpdom1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr (cxp A2 C) (cxp B2 C) cdom) $;

--| A singleton cancellation law for dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-May-2015.)
theorem domunsncan (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wn (wcel A2 X)) (wn (wcel B2 Y))) (wb (wbr (cun (csn A2) X) (cun (csn B2) Y) cdom) (wbr X Y cdom)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ omxpen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem omxpenlem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 B2 A2 (co (co A2 (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wf1o (cxp B2 A2) (co A2 B2 comu) F) $;

--| The cardinal and ordinal products are always equinumerous. Exercise 10 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 89. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2013.)
theorem omxpen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wbr (co A2 B2 comu) (cxp A2 B2) cen) $;

--| Construct an explicit bijection from ` A .o B ` to ` B .o A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem omf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 B2 A2 (co (co A2 (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 A2 (co (co B2 (cv y1) comu) (cv x3) coa)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wf1o (co A2 B2 comu) (co B2 A2 comu) (ccom G (ccnv F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pw2f1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2014.)
theorem pw2f1olem {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 C S: class) (G V W: class z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel S (cpw A2)) (wceq G (cmpt z A2 (cif (wcel (cv z) S) C B2)))) (wa (wcel G (co (cpr B2 C) A2 cmap)) (wceq S (cima (ccnv G) (csn C))))) $;

--| The power set of a set is equinumerous to set exponentiation with an unordered pair base of ordinal 2. Generalized from Proposition 10.44 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 96. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2014.)
theorem pw2f1o {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 C: class) (F V W: class x3 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cmpt z A2 (cif (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) C B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cpw A2) (co (cpr B2 C) A2 cmap) F) $;

--| The power set of a set is equinumerous to set exponentiation with a base of ordinal ` 2o ` . (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem pw2eng (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cpw A2) (co c2o A2 cmap) cen) $;

--| The power set of a set is equinumerous to set exponentiation with a base of ordinal 2. Proposition 10.44 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 96. This is Metamath 100 proof #52. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem pw2en (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (cpw A2) (co c2o A2 cmap) cen $;

--| Covering implies injection on power sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem fopwdom (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wfo A2 B2 F)) (wbr (cpw B2) (cpw A2) cdom) $;

--| Given two equipollent sets, a bijection can always be chosen which fixes a single point. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem enfixsn {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y) (wbr X Y cen)) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o X Y (cv f2)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cv f2)) B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem1 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wss (cuni D) (cdif A2 (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cuni D))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem2 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2) (wss (cdif A2 (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cuni D))))) (cuni D)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem3 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2) (wceq (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cuni D)))) (cdif A2 (cuni D))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem4 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wceq (cdm (cv g2)) B2) (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cv g2)))) (wceq (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D))) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cuni D)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem5 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cdm (cv f2)) A2) (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2)) (wceq (cdm H) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem6 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss (crn (cv f2)) B2) (wa (wa (wceq (cdm (cv g2)) B2) (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cv g2))))) (wceq (crn H) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem7 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun (cv f2)) (wfun (ccnv (cv g2)))) (wfun H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem8 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun (ccnv (cv f2))) (wa (wa (wa (wfun (cv g2)) (wceq (cdm (cv g2)) B2)) (wss (crn (cv g2)) A2)) (wfun (ccnv (cv g2))))) (wfun (ccnv H)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem9 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wf1 B2 A2 (cv g2))) (wf1o A2 B2 H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbth . (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthlem10 {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Schroeder-Bernstein Theorem. Theorem 18 of [Suppes] p. 95. This theorem states that if set ` A ` is smaller (has lower cardinality) than ` B ` and vice-versa, then ` A ` and ` B ` are equinumerous (have the same cardinality). The interesting thing is that this can be proved without invoking the Axiom of Choice, as we do here. The theorem can also be proved from the axiom of choice and the linear order of the cardinal numbers, but our development does not provide the linear order of cardinal numbers until much later and in ways that depend on Schroeder-Bernstein. The main proof consists of lemmas ~ sbthlem1 through ~ sbthlem10 ; this final piece mainly changes bound variables to eliminate the hypotheses of ~ sbthlem10 . We follow closely the proof in Suppes, which you should consult to understand our proof at a higher level. Note that Suppes' proof, which is credited to J. M. Whitaker, does not require the Axiom of Infinity. In the Intuitionistic Logic Explorer (ILE) the Schroeder-Bernstein Theorem has been proven equivalent to the law of the excluded middle (LEM), and in ILE the LEM is not accepted as necessarily true; see ~ https://us.metamath.org/ileuni/exmidsbth.html . This is Metamath 100 proof #25. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-1998.)
theorem sbth (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Schroeder-Bernstein Theorem and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-1998.)
theorem sbthb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Schroeder-Bernstein Theorem in class form. (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-1998.)
theorem sbthcl:
  $ wceq cen (cin cdom (ccnv cdom)) $;

--| Alternate definition of strict dominance. Compare Definition 3 of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1998.)
theorem dfsdom2:
  $ wceq csdm (cdif cdom (ccnv cdom)) $;

--| Alternate definition of strict dominance. Definition 3 of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-2004.)
theorem brsdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cdom))) $;

--| Strict dominance is asymmetric. Theorem 21(ii) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-1998.)
theorem sdomnsym (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Theorem 22(i) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-1998.)
theorem domnsym (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Any set dominates the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem _0domg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr c0 A2 cdom) $;

--| A set dominated by the empty set is empty. (Contributed by NM, 22-Nov-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem dom0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c0 cdom) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| A set strictly dominates the empty set iff it is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem _0sdomg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wbr c0 A2 csdm) (wne A2 c0)) $;

--| Any set dominates the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem _0dom (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr c0 A2 cdom $;

--| A set strictly dominates the empty set iff it is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-2004.)
theorem _0sdom (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr c0 A2 csdm) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| The empty set does not strictly dominate any set. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem sdom0 (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr A2 c0 csdm) $;

--| Transitivity of strict dominance and dominance. Theorem 22(iii) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem sdomdomtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Transitivity of strict dominance and equinumerosity. Exercise 11 of [Suppes] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.)
theorem sdomentr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance and strict dominance. Theorem 22(ii) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem domsdomtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C csdm)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity and strict dominance. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ensdomtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C csdm)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Strict dominance is irreflexive. Theorem 21(i) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-1998.)
theorem sdomirr (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr A2 A2 csdm) $;

--| Strict dominance is transitive. Theorem 21(iii) of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jun-1998.)
theorem sdomtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 C csdm)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Strict dominance has no 2-cycle loops. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-2008.)
theorem sdomn2lp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2003.)
theorem enen1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr A2 C cen) (wbr B2 C cen)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2003.)
theorem enen2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr C A2 cen) (wbr C B2 cen)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and dominance. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2003.)
theorem domen1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr A2 C cdom) (wbr B2 C cdom)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and dominance. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2003.)
theorem domen2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr C A2 cdom) (wbr C B2 cdom)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and strict dominance. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2003.)
theorem sdomen1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr A2 C csdm) (wbr B2 C csdm)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and strict dominance. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2003.)
theorem sdomen2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr C A2 csdm) (wbr C B2 csdm)) $;

--| Dominance is trichotomous in the restricted case of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Oct-2009.)
theorem domtriord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm))) $;

--| For ordinals, strict dominance implies membership. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem sdomel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wcel A2 B2)) $;

--| The difference of a set from a smaller set cannot be empty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2013.)
theorem sdomdif (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wne (cdif B2 A2) c0) $;

--| An ordinal with more elements of some type is larger. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem onsdominel (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wbr (cin A2 C) (cin B2 C) csdm)) (wcel A2 B2) $;

--| Dominance over a set with one element added. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem domunsn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr (cun A2 (csn C)) B2 cdom) $;

--| There exists a mapping from a set onto any (nonempty) set that it dominates. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.)
theorem fodomr {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c0 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wex f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Injection of sets implies injection on power sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem pwdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr (cpw A2) (cpw B2) cdom) $;

--| Cantor's Theorem. No set is equinumerous to its power set. Specifically, any set has a cardinality (size) strictly less than the cardinality of its power set. For example, the cardinality of real numbers is the same as the cardinality of the power set of integers, so real numbers cannot be put into a one-to-one correspondence with integers. Theorem 23 of [Suppes] p. 97. For the function version, see ~ canth . This is Metamath 100 proof #63. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.)
theorem canth2 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr A2 (cpw A2) csdm $;

--| Cantor's theorem with the sethood requirement expressed as an antecedent. Theorem 23 of [Suppes] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2003.)
theorem canth2g (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 (cpw A2) csdm) $;

--| The power set of the power set of the union of a set does not belong to the set. This theorem provides a way of constructing a new set that doesn't belong to a given set. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jun-2008.)
theorem _2pwuninel (A2: class):
  $ wn (wcel (cpw (cpw (cuni A2))) A2) $;

--| No set equals the power set of its power set. (Contributed by NM, 17-Nov-2008.)
theorem _2pwne (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wne (cpw (cpw A2)) A2) $;

--| A stronger form of ~ pwuninel . We can use ~ pwuninel , ~ 2pwuninel to create one or two sets disjoint from a given set ` A ` , but here we show that in fact such constructions exist for arbitrarily large disjoint extensions, which is to say that for any set ` B ` we can construct a set ` x ` that is equinumerous to it and disjoint from ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.)
theorem disjen (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wa (wceq (cin A2 (cxp B2 (csn (cpw (cuni (crn A2)))))) c0) (wbr (cxp B2 (csn (cpw (cuni (crn A2))))) B2 cen)) $;

--| Existence version of ~ disjen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.)
theorem disjenex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cin A2 (cv x3)) c0) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cen))) $;

--| A corollary of ~ disjenex . If ` F ` is an injection from ` A ` to ` B ` then ` G ` is a right inverse of ` F ` from ` B ` to a superset of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem domss2 (A2 B2 F G V W: class):
  $ wceq G (ccnv (cun F (cres c1st (cxp (cdif B2 (crn F)) (csn (cpw (cuni (crn A2)))))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (w3a (wf1o B2 (crn G) G) (wss A2 (crn G)) (wceq (ccom G F) (cres cid A2))) $;

--| A corollary of ~ disjenex . If ` F ` is an injection from ` A ` to ` B ` then there is a right inverse ` g ` of ` F ` from ` B ` to a superset of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem domssex2 {g2: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V W: class g2):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1 B2 cvv (cv g2)) (wceq (ccom (cv g2) F) (cres cid A2)))) $;

--| Weakening of ~ domssex2 to forget the functions in favor of dominance and equinumerosity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem domssex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wbr B2 (cv x3) cen))) $;

--| Construct a bijection on a Cartesian product given bijections on the factors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem xpf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (C: class) (D X: class x3) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 (cmpt x3 A2 X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C D (cmpt y1 C Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 C) (cxp B2 D) (cmpo x3 y1 A2 C (cop X Y))) $;

--| Equinumerosity law for Cartesian product. Proposition 4.22(b) of [Mendelson] p. 254. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpen (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr C D cen)) (wbr (cxp A2 C) (cxp B2 D) cen) $;

--| Two set exponentiations are equinumerous when their bases and exponents are equinumerous. Theorem 6H(c) of [Enderton] p. 139. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapen (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr C D cen)) (wbr (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 D cmap) cen) $;

--| Order-preserving property of set exponentiation. Theorem 6L(c) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem mapdom1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap) cdom) $;

--| Equinumerosity law for double set exponentiation. Proposition 10.45 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem mapxpen (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wbr (co (co A2 B2 cmap) C cmap) (co A2 (cxp B2 C) cmap) cen) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xpmapen . (Contributed by NM, 1-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpmapenlem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D R: class x3) (S: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt z C (cfv (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) c1st)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt z C (cfv (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt z C (cop (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))) $ >
  $ wbr (co (cxp A2 B2) C cmap) (cxp (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap)) cen $;

--| Equinumerosity law for set exponentiation of a Cartesian product. Exercise 4.47 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem xpmapen (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wbr (co (cxp A2 B2) C cmap) (cxp (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap)) cen $;

--| Equinumerosity law for set exponentiation of a disjoint union. Exercise 4.45 of [Mendelson] p. 255. (Contributed by NM, 23-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapunen (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wbr (co C (cun A2 B2) cmap) (cxp (co C A2 cmap) (co C B2 cmap)) cen) $;

--| A cardinal power with exponent 2 is equivalent to a Cartesian product with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem map2xp (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (co A2 c2o cmap) (cxp A2 A2) cen) $;

--| Order-preserving property of set exponentiation. Theorem 6L(d) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by NM, 23-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapdom2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wa (wceq A2 c0) (wceq C c0)))) (wbr (co C A2 cmap) (co C B2 cmap) cdom) $;

--| Set exponentiation dominates the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Jul-2022.)
theorem mapdom3 (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wne B2 c0)) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cmap) cdom) $;

--| If two sets are equinumerous, then their power sets are equinumerous. Proposition 10.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 87. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem pwen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr (cpw A2) (cpw B2) cen) $;

--| Equinumerosity of equinumerous subsets of a set. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem ssenen {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) C cen))) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) B2) (wbr (cv x3) C cen))) cen) $;

--| A limit ordinal is equinumerous to a proper subset of itself. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem limenpsi (A2 V: class):
  $ wlim A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 (cdif A2 (csn c0)) cen) $;

--| A limit ordinal is equinumerous to its successor. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem limensuci (A2 V: class):
  $ wlim A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 (csuc A2) cen) $;

--| A limit ordinal is equinumerous to its successor. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem limensuc (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wlim A2)) (wbr A2 (csuc A2) cen) $;

--| Any infinite ordinal is equinumerous to its successor. Exercise 7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 88. Proved without the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem infensuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wss com A2)) (wbr A2 (csuc A2) cen) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rexdif1en and ~ dif1en . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 18-Aug-2024.) Generalize to all ordinals and add a sethood requirement to avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem dif1enlem (A2 F M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 W) (wcel M con0)) (wf1o A2 (csuc M) F)) (wbr (cdif A2 (csn (cfv M (ccnv F)))) M cen) $;

--| If a set is equinumerous to a nonzero ordinal, then there exists an element in that set such that removing it leaves the set equinumerous to the predecessor of that ordinal. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 26-Aug-2024.) Generalize to all ordinals and avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem rexdif1en {x3: setvar} (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M con0) (wbr A2 (csuc M) cen)) (wrex x3 (wbr (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) M cen) A2) $;

--| If a set ` A ` is equinumerous to the successor of an ordinal ` M ` , then ` A ` with an element removed is equinumerous to ` M ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 26-Aug-2024.) Generalize to all ordinals. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.)
theorem dif1en (A2 M X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M con0) (wbr A2 (csuc M) cen) (wcel X A2)) (wbr (cdif A2 (csn X)) M cen) $;

--| If a set ` A ` is equinumerous to the successor of a natural number ` M ` , then ` A ` with an element removed is equinumerous to ` M ` . See also ~ dif1ennnALT . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.)
theorem dif1ennn (A2 M X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M com) (wbr A2 (csuc M) cen) (wcel X A2)) (wbr (cdif A2 (csn X)) M cen) $;

--| Schema for induction on the cardinality of a finite set. The inductive hypothesis is that the result is true on the given set with any one element removed. The result is then proven to be true for all finite sets. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem findcard {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1 z) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cdif (cv y1) (csn (cv z)))) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wi (wral z ch (cv y1)) th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) ta $;

--| Schema for induction on the cardinality of a finite set. The inductive step shows that the result is true if one more element is added to the set. The result is then proven to be true for all finite sets. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 8-Jul-2010.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 26-Aug-2024.)
theorem findcard2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (ch th ta: wff y1 z) (A2: class y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cun (cv y1) (csn (cv z)))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) ta $;

--| Variation of ~ findcard2 requiring that the element added in the induction step not be a member of the original set. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 30-Nov-2012.)
theorem findcard2s {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1 z) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cun (cv y1) (csn (cv z)))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) ta $;

--| Deduction version of ~ findcard2 . (Contributed by SO, 16-Jul-2018.)
theorem findcard2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch th ta et: wff y1 z) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cun (cv y1) (csn (cv z)))) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wcel (cv z) (cdif A2 (cv y1))))) (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph et $;

--| Natural numbers are finite sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem nnfi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| A proper subset of a natural number is equinumerous to some smaller number. Lemma 6F of [Enderton] p. 137. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 31-Jul-2024.)
theorem pssnn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wpss B2 A2)) (wrex x3 (wbr B2 (cv x3) cen) A2) $;

--| A subset of a natural number is finite. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1998.) (Proof shortened by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem ssnnfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| The union of two finite sets is finite. Part of Corollary 6K of [Enderton] p. 144. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2002.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Aug-2024.)
theorem unfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| The union of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem unfid (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| A subset of a finite set is finite. Corollary 6G of [Enderton] p. 138. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-pow , see ~ ssfiALT . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 12-Aug-2024.)
theorem ssfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ ssfi using ~ ax-pow . (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-1998.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ssfiALT (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| If ` A ` is finite, ` ( A \ B ) ` is finite. (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem diffi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| If a set is finite, its converse is as well. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem cnvfi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (ccnv A2) cfn) $;

--| Every element of the power set of ` A ` is finite if and only if ` A ` is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem pwssfi (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wss (cpw A2) cfn)) $;

--| A version of ~ fnex for finite sets that does not require Replacement or Power Sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem fnfi (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel F cfn) $;

--| If the domain of a one-to-one, onto function is finite, then the domain and range of the function are equinumerous. This theorem is proved without using the Axiom of Replacement or the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ f1oeng ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1oenfi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| If the range of a one-to-one, onto function is finite, then the domain and range of the function are equinumerous. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1oenfirn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cfn) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| If the codomain of a one-to-one function is finite, then the function's domain is dominated by its codomain. This theorem is proved without using the Axiom of Replacement or the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ f1domg ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 25-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1domfi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cfn) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| If the domain of a one-to-one function is finite, then the function's domain is dominated by its codomain when the latter is a set. This theorem is proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ f1dom2g ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem f1domfi2 (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 V) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Equinumerosity is reflexive for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ enrefg ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 8-Sep-2024.)
theorem enreffi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 A2 cen) $;

--| Symmetry of equinumerosity for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ ensymb ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 9-Sep-2024.)
theorem ensymfib (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 A2 cen)) $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ entr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 10-Sep-2024.)
theorem entrfil (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C cen) $;

--| A set equinumerous to a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem enfii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| Equinumerous sets have the same finiteness. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-pow , see ~ enfiALT . (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem enfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ enfi using ~ ax-pow . (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enfiALT (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| A set dominated by a finite set is finite. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem domfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ entr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem entrfi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C cen) $;

--| Transitivity of equinumerosity for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ entr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem entrfir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 C cen)) (wbr A2 C cen) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance relation when ` A ` is finite, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ domtr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem domtrfil (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance relation when ` B ` is finite, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ domtr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem domtrfi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance relation for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ domtr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem domtrfir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C cdom) $;

--| If a function is one-to-one, then the image of a finite subset of its domain under it is equinumerous to the subset. This theorem is proved without using the Axiom of Replacement or the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ f1imaeng ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 29-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1imaenfi (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wss C A2) (wcel C cfn)) (wbr (cima F C) C cen) $;

--| A finite set dominates its subsets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ ssdomg ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem ssdomfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 cfn) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| A set dominates its finite subsets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ ssdomg ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 24-Nov-2024.)
theorem ssdomfi2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 V) (wss A2 B2)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sbthfi . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 4-Nov-2024.)
theorem sbthfilem {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D H: class f2 g2):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cima (cv g2) (cdif B2 (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)))) (cdif A2 (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cres (cv f2) (cuni D)) (cres (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif A2 (cuni D)))) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Schroeder-Bernstein Theorem for finite sets, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ sbth ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 4-Nov-2024.)
theorem sbthfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| If a set dominates a finite set, it cannot also be strictly dominated by the finite set. This theorem is proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ domnsym ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 22-Nov-2024.)
theorem domnsymfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Transitivity of strict dominance and dominance when ` A ` is finite, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ sdomdomtr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 25-Nov-2024.)
theorem sdomdomtrfi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 C cdom)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Transitivity of dominance and strict dominance when ` A ` is finite, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ domsdomtr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 25-Nov-2024.)
theorem domsdomtrfi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 C csdm)) (wbr A2 C csdm) $;

--| Strict dominance of a set over another set implies dominance over its successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Dec-2024.)
theorem sucdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr (csuc A2) B2 cdom) $;

--| Lemma for Pigeonhole Principle. A natural number is equinumerous to its successor minus any element of the successor. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Sep-2024.)
theorem phplem1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 (csuc A2))) (wbr A2 (cdif (csuc A2) (csn B2)) cen) $;

--| Lemma for Pigeonhole Principle. Equinumerosity of successors implies equinumerosity of the original natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Nov-2024.)
theorem phplem2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wi (wbr (csuc A2) (csuc B2) cen) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Two equinumerous natural numbers are equal. Proposition 10.20 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90 and its converse. Also compare Corollary 6E of [Enderton] p. 136. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem nneneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Pigeonhole Principle. A natural number is not equinumerous to a proper subset of itself. Theorem (Pigeonhole Principle) of [Enderton] p. 134. The theorem is so-called because you can't put _n_ + 1 pigeons into _n_ holes (if each hole holds only one pigeon). The proof consists of ~ phplem1 , ~ phplem2 , ~ nneneq , and this final piece of the proof. (Contributed by NM, 29-May-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 18-Nov-2024.)
theorem php (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wpss B2 A2)) (wn (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Corollary of Pigeonhole Principle. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 20-Nov-2024.)
theorem php2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wpss B2 A2)) (wbr B2 A2 csdm) $;

--| Corollary of Pigeonhole Principle. If ` A ` is finite and ` B ` is a proper subset of ` A ` , the ` B ` is strictly less numerous than ` A ` . Stronger version of Corollary 6C of [Enderton] p. 135. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 26-Nov-2024.)
theorem php3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wpss B2 A2)) (wbr B2 A2 csdm) $;

--| Corollary of the Pigeonhole Principle ~ php : a natural number is strictly dominated by its successor. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2004.)
theorem php4 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wbr A2 (csuc A2) csdm) $;

--| Corollary of the Pigeonhole Principle ~ php : a natural number is not equinumerous to its successor. Corollary 10.21(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2004.)
theorem php5 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wn (wbr A2 (csuc A2) cen)) $;

--| Corollary of the Pigeonhole Principle using equality. Strengthening of ~ php expressed without negation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 28-Nov-2024.)
theorem phpeqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Cardinal ordering agrees with ordinal number ordering when the smaller number is a natural number. Compare with ~ nndomo when both are natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-1998.) Generalize from ~ nndomo . (Revised by RP, 5-Nov-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 29-Nov-2024.)
theorem nndomog (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wss A2 B2)) $;

--| An ordinal number equinumerous to a natural number is equal to it. Proposition 10.22 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 2-Dec-2024.)
theorem onomeneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| An ordinal number is finite iff it is a natural number. Proposition 10.32 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2004.)
theorem onfin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 com)) $;

--| A generalization of ~ onfin to include the class of all ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2026.)
theorem ordfin (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 com)) $;

--| A set is a natural number iff it is a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2013.)
theorem onfin2:
  $ wceq com (cin con0 cfn) $;

--| Cardinal ordering agrees with natural number ordering. Example 3 of [Enderton] p. 146. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-1998.)
theorem nndomo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wss A2 B2)) $;

--| Cardinal ordering agrees with natural number ordering. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-1998.)
theorem nnsdomo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wpss A2 B2)) $;

--| Strict dominance of a set over a natural number is the same as dominance over its successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Dec-2024.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Jan-2025.)
theorem sucdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr (csuc A2) B2 cdom)) $;

--| A singleton ` { A } ` is never equinumerous with the ordinal number 2. This holds for proper singletons ( ` A e. _V ` ) as well as for singletons being the empty set ( ` A e/ _V ` ). (Contributed by AV, 6-Aug-2019.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem snnen2o (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr (csn A2) c2o cen) $;

--| Strict dominance over 0 is the same as dominance over 1. For a shorter proof requiring ~ ax-un , see 0sdom1domALT . (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Dec-2024.)
theorem _0sdom1dom (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr c0 A2 csdm) (wbr c1o A2 cdom) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 0sdom1dom , shorter but requiring ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0sdom1domALT (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr c0 A2 csdm) (wbr c1o A2 cdom) $;

--| Ordinal 1 is strictly dominated by ordinal 2. For a shorter proof requiring ~ ax-un , see ~ 1sdom2ALT . (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2007.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 8-Dec-2024.)
theorem _1sdom2:
  $ wbr c1o c2o csdm $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 1sdom2 , shorter but requiring ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1sdom2ALT:
  $ wbr c1o c2o csdm $;

--| A set has less than one member iff it is empty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Oct-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 12-Dec-2024.)
theorem sdom1 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c1o csdm) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| Two ways to express "at most one". (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Oct-2014.)
theorem modom {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wmo x3 ph) (wbr (cab x3 ph) c1o cdom) $;

--| Two ways to express "at most one". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem modom2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr A2 c1o cdom) $;

--| A set that has at least 2 different members dominates ordinal 2. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2024.)
theorem rex2dom {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2)) (wbr c2o A2 cdom) $;

--| Strict dominance over 1 is the same as dominance over 2. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Dec-2024.)
theorem _1sdom2dom (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr c2o A2 cdom) $;

--| A set that strictly dominates ordinal 1 has at least 2 different members. (Closely related to ~ 2dom .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2024.)
theorem _1sdom {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unxpdom . (Trivial substitution proof.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem unxpdomlem1 {x3 z t m n s1 a b: setvar} (F: class x3 t m n s1 a b) (G: class x3 z t m n s1 a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cun (cv a) (cv b)) G) $ >
  $ wceq G (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) (cop (cv x3) (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv m)) (cv t) (cv s1))) (cop (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (cv n) (cv m)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) (cun (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (cfv (cv z) F) (cif (wcel (cv z) (cv a)) (cop (cv z) (cif (wceq (cv z) (cv m)) (cv t) (cv s1))) (cop (cif (wceq (cv z) (cv t)) (cv n) (cv m)) (cv z)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unxpdom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem unxpdomlem2 {x3 z w t m n s1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z w t m n s1 a b) (F: class x3 t m n s1 a b) (G: class x3 z w t m n s1 a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cun (cv a) (cv b)) G) $ >
  $ wceq G (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) (cop (cv x3) (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv m)) (cv t) (cv s1))) (cop (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (cv n) (cv m)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv w) (cun (cv a) (cv b))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cv m) (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cv s1) (cv t))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv a)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cv a))))) (wn (wceq (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv w) F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unxpdom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem unxpdomlem3 {x3 t m n s1 a b: setvar} (F G: class x3 t m n s1 a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cun (cv a) (cv b)) G) $ >
  $ wceq G (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) (cop (cv x3) (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv m)) (cv t) (cv s1))) (cop (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv t)) (cv n) (cv m)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr c1o (cv a) csdm) (wbr c1o (cv b) csdm)) (wbr (cun (cv a) (cv b)) (cxp (cv a) (cv b)) cdom) $;

--| Cartesian product dominates union for sets with cardinality greater than 1. Proposition 10.36 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem unxpdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr c1o B2 csdm)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| Corollary of ~ unxpdom . (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2004.)
theorem unxpdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) (cxp A2 A2) cdom) $;

--| Cartesian product dominates successor for set with cardinality greater than 1. Proposition 10.38 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93 (but generalized to arbitrary sets, not just ordinals). (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem sucxpdom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr (csuc A2) (cxp A2 A2) cdom) $;

--| A set equinumerous to a proper subset of itself is infinite. Corollary 6D(a) of [Enderton] p. 136. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1998.)
theorem pssinf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wpss A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) (wn (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| A finite set is equal to its subset if they are equinumerous. (Contributed by FL, 11-Aug-2008.)
theorem fisseneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wss A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| The set of natural numbers is infinite. Corollary 6D(b) of [Enderton] p. 136. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jun-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 2-Jan-2025.)
theorem ominf:
  $ wn (wcel com cfn) $;

--| Any set that is not finite is literally infinite, in the sense that it contains subsets of arbitrarily large finite cardinality. (It cannot be proven that the set has _countably infinite_ subsets unless AC is invoked.) The proof does not require the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 2-Jan-2025.)
theorem isinf {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wral n (wex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv n) cen))) com) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fineqv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fineqvlem (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wbr com (cpw (cpw A2)) cdom) $;

--| If the Axiom of Infinity is denied, then all sets are finite (which implies the Axiom of Choice). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2015.)
theorem fineqv:
  $ wb (wn (wcel com cvv)) (wceq cfn cvv) $;

--| The components of a nonempty finite Cartesian product are finite. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpfir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cfn) (wne (cxp A2 B2) c0)) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| A subset of a finite set is finite, deduction version of ~ ssfi . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem ssfid (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| The intersection of two sets is finite if one of them is. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2017.)
theorem infi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cin A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| A restricted class built from a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2017.)
theorem rabfi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (crab x3 ph A2) cfn) $;

--| The restriction of a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Jan-2018.)
theorem finresfin (B2 E: class):
  $ wi (wcel E cfn) (wcel (cres E B2) cfn) $;

--| Any injection from one finite set to another of equal size must be a bijection. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 5-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Jan-2025.)
theorem f1finf1o (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wb (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wf1o A2 B2 F)) $;

--| If a class is not finite, then it contains at least one element. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Jan-2018.)
theorem nfielex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| A set with one element is a singleton. (Contributed by FL, 18-Aug-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 4-Jan-2025.)
theorem en1eqsn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wbr B2 c1o cen)) (wceq B2 (csn A2)) $;

--| A set containing an element has exactly one element iff it is a singleton. Formerly part of proof for ~ rngen1zr . (Contributed by FL, 13-Feb-2010.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jan-2020.)
theorem en1eqsnbi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wbr B2 c1o cen) (wceq B2 (csn A2))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dif1ennn using ~ ax-pow . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dif1ennnALT (A2 M X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M com) (wbr A2 (csuc M) cen) (wcel X A2)) (wbr (cdif A2 (csn X)) M cen) $;

--| Lemma for uses of ~ enp1i . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem enp1ilem {x3: setvar} (A2 S T: class x3):
  $ wceq T (cun (csn (cv x3)) S) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wi (wceq (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) S) (wceq A2 T)) $;

--| Proof induction for ~ en2 and related theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.) Generalize to all ordinals and avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 6-Jan-2025.)
theorem enp1i {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ word M $ >
  $ wceq N (csuc M) $ >
  $ wi (wbr (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) M cen) ph $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 N cen) (wex x3 ps) $;

--| A set equinumerous to ordinal 2 is an unordered pair. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem en2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 c2o cen) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wceq A2 (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| A set equinumerous to ordinal 3 is a triple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem en3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 c3o cen) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wceq A2 (ctp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)))))) $;

--| A set equinumerous to ordinal 4 is a quadruple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2016.)
theorem en4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 c4o cen) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wex z (wex w (wceq A2 (cun (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cpr (cv z) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Schema for strong induction on the cardinality of a finite set. The inductive hypothesis is that the result is true on any proper subset. The result is then proven to be true for all finite sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Jan-2025.)
theorem findcard3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ch ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wi (wal x3 (wi (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) ta $;

--| A version of ~ ac6s for finite sets. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 26-Jun-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem ac6sfi {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2)) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| A partial order is well-founded on a finite set. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 18-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem frfi (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wfr A2 R) $;

--| A finite set has a maximum under a total order. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 18-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem fimax2g {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| A finite set has a maximum under a total order. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem fimaxg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma showing existence and closure of supremum of a finite set. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem fisupg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) A2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| A total order on a finite set is a well-order. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 18-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem wofi (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wwe A2 R) $;

--| The maximum of a finite collection of ordinals is in the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem ordunifi (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 con0) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cuni A2) A2) $;

--| The union (supremum) of a finite set of ordinals less than a nonzero ordinal class is an element of that ordinal class. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 15-Jan-2026.)
theorem fissorduni (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 B2) (wa (word B2) (wne B2 c0))) (wcel (cuni A2) B2) $;

--| The union (supremum) of a finite set of finite ordinals is a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnunifi (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S com) (wcel S cfn)) (wcel (cuni S) com) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unbnn . After removing the successor of an element from an unbounded set of natural numbers, the intersection of the result belongs to the original unbounded set. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2003.)
theorem unblem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss B2 com) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) com)) (wcel A2 B2)) (wcel (cint (cdif B2 (csuc A2))) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unbnn . The value of the function ` F ` belongs to the unbounded set of natural numbers ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2003.)
theorem unblem2 {x3 z w v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (cdif A2 (csuc (cv x3))))) (cint A2)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 com) (wral w (wrex v (wcel (cv w) (cv v)) A2) com)) (wi (wcel (cv z) com) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unbnn . The value of the function ` F ` is less than its value at a successor. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2003.)
theorem unblem3 {x3 z w v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (cdif A2 (csuc (cv x3))))) (cint A2)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 com) (wral w (wrex v (wcel (cv w) (cv v)) A2) com)) (wi (wcel (cv z) com) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (csuc (cv z)) F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unbnn . The function ` F ` maps the set of natural numbers one-to-one to the set of unbounded natural numbers ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2003.)
theorem unblem4 {x3 w v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (cdif A2 (csuc (cv x3))))) (cint A2)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 com) (wral w (wrex v (wcel (cv w) (cv v)) A2) com)) (wf1 com A2 F) $;

--| Any unbounded subset of natural numbers is equinumerous to the set of all natural numbers. Part of the proof of Theorem 42 of [Suppes] p. 151. See ~ unbnn3 for a stronger version without the first assumption. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2003.)
theorem unbnn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel com cvv) (wss A2 com) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) com)) (wbr A2 com cen) $;

--| Version of ~ unbnn that does not require a strict upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.)
theorem unbnn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel com cvv) (wss A2 com) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) com)) (wbr A2 com cen) $;

--| Any set strictly dominated by the class of natural numbers is finite. Sufficiency part of Theorem 42 of [Suppes] p. 151. This theorem does not require the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.)
theorem isfinite2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com csdm) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| Omega strictly dominates a natural number. Example 3 of [Enderton] p. 146. In order to avoid the Axiom of Infinity, we include it as part of the antecedent. See ~ nnsdom for the version without this sethood requirement. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-1998.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Jan-2025.)
theorem nnsdomg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel com cvv) (wcel A2 com)) (wbr A2 com csdm) $;

--| A set is finite iff it is strictly dominated by the class of natural number. Theorem 42 of [Suppes] p. 151. In order to avoid the Axiom of infinity, we include it as a hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem isfiniteg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel com cvv) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 com csdm)) $;

--| An infinite set strictly dominates a natural number. (Contributed by NM, 22-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Jan-2025.)
theorem infsdomnn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr com A2 cdom) (wcel B2 com)) (wbr B2 A2 csdm) $;

--| An infinite set is not empty. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-un , see ~ infn0ALT . (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 8-Jan-2025.)
theorem infn0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ infn0 using ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem infn0ALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| This (useless) theorem, which was proved without the Axiom of Infinity, demonstrates an artifact of our definition of binary relation, which is meaningful only when its arguments exist. In particular, the antecedent cannot be satisfied unless ` _om ` exists. (Contributed by NM, 13-Nov-2003.)
theorem fin2inf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com csdm) (wcel com cvv) $;

--| Lemma for proving that the union of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem unfilem1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq (crn F) (cdif (co A2 B2 coa) A2) $;

--| Lemma for proving that the union of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem unfilem2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 com $ >
  $ wcel B2 com $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (co A2 (cv x3) coa)) $ >
  $ wf1o B2 (cdif (co A2 B2 coa) A2) F $;

--| Lemma for proving that the union of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem unfilem3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wbr B2 (cdif (co A2 B2 coa) A2) cen) $;

--| If a union is finite, the operands are finite. Converse of ~ unfi . (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem unfir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cun A2 B2) cfn) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| A union is finite if and only if the operands are finite. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem unfib (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cun A2 B2) cfn) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) $;

--| The union of two finite sets is finite. Part of Corollary 6K of [Enderton] p. 144. This version of ~ unfi is useful only if we assume the Axiom of Infinity (see comments in ~ fin2inf ). (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem unfi2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com csdm) (wbr B2 com csdm)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) com csdm) $;

--| An infinite set ` A ` minus a finite set is infinite. (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem difinf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wn (wcel (cdif A2 B2) cfn)) $;

--| An onto function implies dominance of domain over range, for finite sets. Unlike ~ fodomg for arbitrary sets, this theorem does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets nor the Axiom of Choice for its proof. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 20-Jun-2025.)
theorem fodomfi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wfo A2 B2 F)) (wbr B2 A2 cdom) $;

--| If an onto function has a finite domain, its codomain/range is finite. Theorem 37 of [Suppes] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2007.)
theorem fofi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wfo A2 B2 F)) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| If a 1-to-1 function has a finite codomain its domain is finite. (Contributed by FL, 31-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem f1fi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cfn) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| Images of finite sets are finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem imafi (F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel X cfn)) (wcel (cima F X) cfn) $;

--| If the power set of a set is finite, then the set itself is finite. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 7-Sep-2024.)
theorem pwfir (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cpw B2) cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfi . (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2007.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Sep-2024.)
theorem pwfilem {x3 b c: setvar} (F: class x3 b c):
  $ wceq F (cmpt c (cpw (cv b)) (cun (cv c) (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cpw (cv b)) cfn) (wcel (cpw (cun (cv b) (csn (cv x3)))) cfn) $;

--| The power set of a finite set is finite and vice-versa. Theorem 38 of [Suppes] p. 104 and its converse, Theorem 40 of [Suppes] p. 105. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2007.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 7-Sep-2024.)
theorem pwfi (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cpw A2) cfn) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 10-Jan-2025.)
theorem xpfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| The Cartesian product of three finite sets is a finite set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem _3xpfi (V: class):
  $ wi (wcel V cfn) (wcel (cxp (cxp V V) V) cfn) $;

--| A finite set union cancellation law for dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-May-2015.)
theorem domunfican (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr B2 A2 cen)) (wa (wceq (cin A2 X) c0) (wceq (cin B2 Y) c0))) (wb (wbr (cun A2 X) (cun B2 Y) cdom) (wbr X Y cdom)) $;

--| Every infinite set has a denumerable subset. Similar to Exercise 8 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91. (However, we need neither AC nor the Axiom of Infinity because of the way we express "infinite" in the antecedent.) (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.)
theorem infcntss {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) com cen))) $;

--| An unordered pair is finite. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-un , see ~ prfiALT . (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-11 , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem prfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (cpr A2 B2) cfn $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ prfi using ~ ax-un . (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prfiALT (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (cpr A2 B2) cfn $;

--| An unordered triple is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Sep-2013.)
theorem tpfi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel (ctp A2 B2 C) cfn $;

--| Equivalent ways of stating the finite intersection property. We show two ways of saying, "the intersection of elements in every finite nonempty subcollection of ` A ` is in ` A ` ". This theorem is applicable to a topology, which (among other axioms) is closed under finite intersections. Some texts use the left-hand version of this axiom and others the right-hand version, but as our proof here shows, their "intuitively obvious" equivalence can be non-trivial to establish formally. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2002.) Use a separate setvar for the right-hand side and avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 14-Jan-2025.)
theorem fiint {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2) A2) (wal z (wi (w3a (wss (cv z) A2) (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cv z) cfn)) (wcel (cint (cv z)) A2))) $;

--| Equivalent ways of stating the finite intersection property. We show two ways of saying, "the intersection of elements in every finite nonempty subcollection of ` A ` is in ` A ` ". This theorem is applicable to a topology, which (among other axioms) is closed under finite intersections. Some texts use the left-hand version of this axiom and others the right-hand version, but as our proof here shows, their "intuitively obvious" equivalence can be non-trivial to establish formally. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2002.) Use a separate setvar for the right-hand side and avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 14-Jan-2025.)
theorem fiint_b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2) A2) (wal x3 (wi (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wne (cv x3) c0) (wcel (cv x3) cfn)) (wcel (cint (cv x3)) A2))) $;

--| There exists a mapping from a finite set onto any nonempty set that it dominates, proved without using the Axiom of Power Sets (unlike ~ fodomr ). (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 23-Jun-2025.)
theorem fodomfir {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr c0 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wex f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Equivalence of an onto mapping and dominance for a nonempty finite set. Unlike ~ fodomb for arbitrary sets, this theorem does not require the Axiom of Replacement nor the Axiom of Power Sets nor the Axiom of Choice for its proof. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.) Avoid ~ ax-pow . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Jun-2025.)
theorem fodomfib {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wb (wa (wne A2 c0) (wex f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2)))) (wa (wbr c0 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 cdom))) $;

--| Any surjection from one finite set to another of equal size must be a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Aug-2014.)
theorem fofinf1o (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfo A2 B2 F) (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wf1o A2 B2 F) $;

--| Any function from a finite set onto the same set must be a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2021.)
theorem rneqdmfinf1o (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wfn F A2) (wceq (crn F) A2)) (wf1o A2 A2 F) $;

--| Any finite set dominates its domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem fidomdm (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F cfn) (wbr (cdm F) F cdom) $;

--| The domain of a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Sep-2013.)
theorem dmfi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cdm A2) cfn) $;

--| A function is finite if and only if its domain is finite. (Contributed by AV, 10-Jan-2020.)
theorem fundmfibi (F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wb (wcel F cfn) (wcel (cdm F) cfn)) $;

--| The restriction of a function to a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Feb-2018.)
theorem resfnfinfin (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wcel (cres F B2) cfn) $;

--| A restricted identity function is finite iff the restricting class is finite. (Contributed by AV, 10-Jan-2020.)
theorem residfi (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cres cid A2) cfn) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ cnvfi using ~ ax-pow . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cnvfiALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (ccnv A2) cfn) $;

--| The range of a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2014.)
theorem rnfi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (crn A2) cfn) $;

--| A one-to-one function whose domain is a set is finite if and only if its range is finite. See also ~ f1vrnfibi . (Contributed by AV, 10-Jan-2020.)
theorem f1dmvrnfibi (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wb (wcel F cfn) (wcel (crn F) cfn)) $;

--| A one-to-one function which is a set is finite if and only if its range is finite. See also ~ f1dmvrnfibi . (Contributed by AV, 10-Jan-2020.)
theorem f1vrnfibi (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wf1 A2 B2 F)) (wb (wcel F cfn) (wcel (crn F) cfn)) $;

--| The finite union of finite sets is finite. Exercise 13 of [Enderton] p. 144. This is the indexed union version of ~ unifi . Note that ` B ` depends on ` x ` , i.e. can be thought of as ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem iunfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel B2 cfn) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| The finite union of finite sets is finite. Exercise 13 of [Enderton] p. 144. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem unifi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 cfn)) (wcel (cuni A2) cfn) $;

--| The finite union of finite sets is finite. Exercise 13 of [Enderton] p. 144. This version of ~ unifi is useful only if we assume the Axiom of Infinity (see comments in ~ fin2inf ). (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2006.)
theorem unifi2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com csdm) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) com csdm) A2)) (wbr (cuni A2) com csdm) $;

--| If an infinite set ` A ` is included in the underlying set of a finite cover ` B ` , then there exists a set of the cover that contains an infinite number of element of ` A ` . (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.)
theorem infssuni {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel B2 cfn) (wss A2 (cuni B2))) (wrex x3 (wn (wcel (cin A2 (cv x3)) cfn)) B2) $;

--| The union of the range of a function from a finite set into the class of finite sets is finite. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem unirnffid (ph: wff) (T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf T cfn F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (crn F)) cfn) $;

--| Set exponentiation of finite sets is finite. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem mapfi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmap) cfn) $;

--| A Cartesian product of finitely many finite sets is finite. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem ixpfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel B2 cfn) A2)) (wcel (cixp x3 A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| A Cartesian product of finite sets such that all but finitely many are singletons is finite. (Note that ` B ( x ) ` and ` D ( x ) ` are both possibly dependent on ` x ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem ixpfi2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif A2 C))) (wss B2 (csn D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp x3 A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| A finite mapping set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem mptfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| An image set from a finite set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2014.)
theorem abrexfi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2)) cfn) $;

--| Any preimage by a mapping with a finite domain is finite. In contrast to ~ fisuppfi , the range of the mapping need not be known. (Contributed by AV, 21-Dec-2018.)
theorem cnvimamptfin {p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (N: class) (X Y: class p):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv (cmpt p N X)) Y) cfn) $;

--| Membership in a class of finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfpw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cin (cpw B2) cfn)) (wa (wss A2 B2) (wcel A2 cfn)) $;

--| A set is the union of its finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem unifpw (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (cin (cpw A2) cfn)) A2 $;

--| A one-to-one mapping induces a one-to-one mapping on finite subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem f1opwfi {b: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wf1o (cin (cpw A2) cfn) (cin (cpw B2) cfn) (cmpt b (cin (cpw A2) cfn) (cima F (cv b)))) $;

--| A finite subset of a union is covered by finitely many elements. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem fissuni {c: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 (cuni B2)) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wrex c (wss A2 (cuni (cv c))) (cin (cpw B2) cfn)) $;

--| Given a finite subset ` A ` of the range of a function, there exists a finite subset of the domain whose image is ` A ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem fipreima {c: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F B2) (wss A2 (crn F)) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wrex c (wceq (cima F (cv c)) A2) (cin (cpw B2) cfn)) $;

--| A finite subset of the union of a superset chain is a subset of some element of the chain. A useful preliminary result for ~ alexsub and others. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem finsschain {z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wne A2 c0) (wor A2 crpss)) (wa (wcel B2 cfn) (wss B2 (cuni A2)))) (wrex z (wss B2 (cv z)) A2) $;

--| If for every element of a finite indexing set ` A ` there exists a corresponding element of another set ` B ` , then there exists a finite subset of ` B ` consisting only of those elements which are indexed by ` A ` . Proven without the Axiom of Choice, unlike ~ indexdom . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem indexfi {x3 y1 c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (M: class x3 y1 c):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 M) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2)) (wrex c (w3a (wss (cv c) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv c)) A2) (wral y1 (wrex x3 ph A2) (cv c))) cfn) $;

--| The image by a finite set is finite. See also ~ imafi . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2020.)
theorem imafi2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cima A2 B2) cfn) $;

--| If a union is finite, then all its elements are finite. See ~ unifi . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Aug-2017.)
theorem unifi3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cuni A2) cfn) (wss A2 cfn) $;

--| A transfinite sequence is infinite iff its domain is greater than or equal to omega. Theorem 5 in Grzegorz Bancerek, "Epsilon Numbers and Cantor Normal Form", Formalized Mathematics, Vol. 17, No. 4, Pages 249&ndash;256, 2009. DOI: 10.2478/v10037-009-0032-8 (Contributed by RP, 1-Mar-2025.)
theorem tfsnfin2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn A2 B2) (word B2)) (wb (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wss com B2)) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the predicate to be a finitely supported function.
term cfsupp: class;

--| Define the property of a function to be finitely supported (in relation to a given zero). (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2019.)
axiom df_fsupp {z r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfsupp (copab r z (wa (wfun (cv r)) (wcel (co (cv r) (cv z) csupp) cfn))) $;

--| The property of a function to be finitely supported is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2019.)
theorem relfsupp:
  $ wrel cfsupp $;

--| A proper class is never finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2019.)
theorem relprcnfsupp (A2 Z: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wn (wbr A2 Z cfsupp)) $;

--| The property of a class to be a finitely supported function (in relation to a given zero). (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2019.)
theorem isfsupp (R V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wbr R Z cfsupp) (wa (wfun R) (wcel (co R Z csupp) cfn))) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ isfsupp . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jul-2024.)
theorem isfsuppd (ph: wff) (R V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co R Z csupp) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr R Z cfsupp) $;

--| The property of a function to be finitely supported (in relation to a given zero). (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2019.)
theorem funisfsupp (R V W Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun R) (wcel R V) (wcel Z W)) (wb (wbr R Z cfsupp) (wcel (co R Z csupp) cfn)) $;

--| Implications of a class being a finitely supported function (in relation to a given zero). (Contributed by AV, 26-May-2019.)
theorem fsuppimp (R Z: class):
  $ wi (wbr R Z cfsupp) (wa (wfun R) (wcel (co R Z csupp) cfn)) $;

--| A finitely supported function is a function with a finite support. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppimpd (ph: wff) (F Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F Z csupp) cfn) $;

--| A finitely supported function is a function. (Contributed by SN, 8-Mar-2025.)
theorem fsuppfund (ph: wff) (F Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $;

--| A function on a finite set is finitely supported. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem fisuppfi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) C) cfn) $;

--| If a function is zero outside of a finite set, it has finite support. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-May-2024.)
theorem finnzfsuppd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (D U: class x3) (F: class) (V: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wo (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| The support of a function with a finite domain is always finite. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem fdmfisuppfi (ph: wff) (D R F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf D R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F Z csupp) cfn) $;

--| A function with a finite domain is always finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.)
theorem fdmfifsupp (ph: wff) (D R F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf D R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| A mapping with a finite domain is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppmptdm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (W Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| The support of a function with a finite domain is always finite. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.)
theorem fndmfisuppfi (ph: wff) (D F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F Z csupp) cfn) $;

--| A function with a finite domain is always finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.)
theorem fndmfifsupp (ph: wff) (D F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| If two functions have the same support, one function is finitely supported iff the other one is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem suppeqfsuppbi (U F G V Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel F U) (wfun F)) (wa (wcel G V) (wfun G))) (wi (wceq (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp)) (wb (wbr F Z cfsupp) (wbr G Z cfsupp))) $;

--| If the support of a function is a subset of a finite set, the function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jul-2019.)
theorem suppssfifsupp (F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G V) (wfun G) (wcel Z W)) (wa (wcel F cfn) (wss (co G Z csupp) F))) (wbr G Z cfsupp) $;

--| If the support of a function is a subset of the support of a finitely supported function, the function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Jul-2019.)
theorem fsuppsssupp (F G V Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G V) (wfun G)) (wa (wbr F Z cfsupp) (wss (co G Z csupp) (co F Z csupp)))) (wbr G Z cfsupp) $;

--| If the support of a function is a subset of a finite support, it is finite. Deduction associated with ~ fsuppsssupp . (Contributed by SN, 6-Mar-2025.)
theorem fsuppsssuppgd (ph: wff) (F G O V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F O cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G Z csupp) (co F O csupp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G Z cfsupp) $;

--| A subset of a finitely supported function is a finitely supported function. (Contributed by SN, 8-Mar-2025.)
theorem fsuppss (ph: wff) (F G Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| Formula building theorem for finite support: operator with left annihilator. Finite support version of ~ suppssov1 . (Contributed by SN, 26-Apr-2025.)
theorem fsuppssov1 {x3 v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 v) (B2: class x3) (D: class v) (R O: class x3) (V: class v) (W: class x3 v) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 D A2) Y cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) R)) (wceq (co Y (cv v) O) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 D (co A2 B2 O)) Z cfsupp) $;

--| The cartesian product of two finitely supported functions is finite. (Contributed by AV, 17-Jul-2019.)
theorem fsuppxpfi (F G Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr F Z cfsupp) (wbr G Z cfsupp)) (wcel (cxp (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp)) cfn) $;

--| A constant function with value zero is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem fczfsuppd (ph: wff) (B2 V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cxp B2 (csn Z)) Z cfsupp) $;

--| The union of two finitely supported functions is finitely supported (but not necessarily a function!). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppun (ph: wff) (F G Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cun F G) Z csupp) cfn) $;

--| The union of the support of two finitely supported functions is finite. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2019.)
theorem fsuppunfi (ph: wff) (F G Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun (co F Z csupp) (co G Z csupp)) cfn) $;

--| If the union of two classes/functions is a function, this union is finitely supported iff the two functions are finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppunbi (ph: wff) (F G Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun (cun F G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cun F G) Z cfsupp) (wa (wbr F Z cfsupp) (wbr G Z cfsupp))) $;

--| The empty set is a finitely supported function. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2019.)
theorem _0fsupp (V Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z V) (wbr c0 Z cfsupp) $;

--| A singleton containing an ordered pair is a finitely supported function. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2019.)
theorem snopfsupp (U V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y W) (wcel Z U)) (wbr (csn (cop X Y)) Z cfsupp) $;

--| Finite support for a function extended by a singleton. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jul-2019.)
theorem funsnfsupp (F V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y W)) (wa (wfun F) (wnel X (cdm F)))) (wb (wbr (cun F (csn (cop X Y))) Z cfsupp) (wbr F Z cfsupp)) $;

--| The restriction of a finitely supported function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2019.)
theorem fsuppres (ph: wff) (F V X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cres F X) Z cfsupp) $;

--| The restriction of a mapping function has finite support if that function has finite support. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2024.)
theorem fmptssfisupp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 A2 B2) Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 C B2) Z cfsupp) $;

--| If the support of the restriction of a function by a set which, subtracted from the domain of the function so that its difference is finite, the support of the function itself is finite. (Contributed by AV, 22-Apr-2019.)
theorem ressuppfi (ph: wff) (B2 F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif (cdm F) B2) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cres F B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G Z csupp) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F Z csupp) cfn) $;

--| If the restriction of a function by a set which, subtracted from the domain of the function so that its difference is finitely supported, the function itself is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 27-May-2019.)
theorem resfsupp (ph: wff) (B2 F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif (cdm F) B2) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cres F B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| The restriction of a function to a finite set is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 12-Dec-2019.)
theorem resfifsupp (ph: wff) (F V X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cres F X) Z cfsupp) $;

--| Two ways of saying that a function with known codomain is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2019.)
theorem ffsuppbi (S F I V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel Z W)) (wi (wf I S F) (wb (wbr F Z cfsupp) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cdif S (csn Z))) cfn))) $;

--| A function mapping an argument to either a value of a finitely supported function or zero is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppmptif {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 D: class k) (F: class) (V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k A2 (cif (wcel (cv k) D) (cfv (cv k) F) Z)) Z cfsupp) $;

--| A function mapping all but one arguments to zero is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2019.)
theorem sniffsupp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class x3) (I: class) (V W: class x3) (X c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 I (cif (wceq (cv x3) X) A2 c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fsuppco . Formula building theorem for finite supports: rearranging the index set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem fsuppcolem (ph: wff) (F G X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cvv (csn Z))) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 X Y G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv (ccom F G)) (cdif cvv (csn Z))) cfn) $;

--| The composition of a 1-1 function with a finitely supported function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2019.)
theorem fsuppco (ph: wff) (F G V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 X Y G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom F G) Z cfsupp) $;

--| The composition of a function which maps the zero to zero with a finitely supported function is finitely supported. This is not only a special case of ~ fsuppcor because it does not require that the "zero" is an element of the range of the finitely supported function. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppco2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F G V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z G) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom G F) Z cfsupp) $;

--| The composition of a function which maps the zero of the range of a finitely supported function to the zero of its range with this finitely supported function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem fsuppcor (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D U F G V W c_0 Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z G) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom G F) c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ mapfien . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2024.)
theorem mapfienlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class) (S T U: class x3) (f2: setvar) (F G: class) (V: class x3) (W: class) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) W cfsupp) (co D C cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv Z G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) S)) (wbr (ccom G (ccom (cv f2) F)) W cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ mapfien . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2024.)
theorem mapfienlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class) (S T U: class x3) (g2: setvar) (F G: class) (V: class x3) (W: class) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) W cfsupp) (co D C cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv Z G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv g2) T)) (wbr (ccom (ccom (ccnv G) (cv g2)) (ccnv F)) Z cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ mapfien . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2024.)
theorem mapfienlem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class) (S T U: class x3) (g2: setvar) (F G: class) (V: class x3) (W: class) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) W cfsupp) (co D C cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv Z G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv g2) T)) (wcel (ccom (ccom (ccnv G) (cv g2)) (ccnv F)) S) $;

--| A bijection of the base sets induces a bijection on the set of finitely supported functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2024.)
theorem mapfien {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class f2) (S T: class x3) (U: class x3 f2) (F G: class) (V: class x3 f2) (W: class f2) (X Y: class x3 f2) (Z: class f2):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) W cfsupp) (co D C cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv Z G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o S T (cmpt f2 S (ccom G (ccom (cv f2) F)))) $;

--| Equinumerousity relation for sets of finitely supported functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem mapfien2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class) (S T: class x3) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c_0 cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) W cfsupp) (co D C cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cen) $;

--| Extend class notation with the function whose value is the class of finite intersections of the elements of a given set.
term cfi: class;

--| Function whose value is the class of finite intersections of the elements of the argument. Note that the empty intersection being the universal class, hence a proper class, it cannot be an element of that class. Therefore, the function value is the class of nonempty finite intersections of elements of the argument (see ~ elfi2 ). (Contributed by FL, 27-Apr-2008.)
axiom df_fi {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfi (cmpt x3 cvv (cab z (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (cint (cv y1))) (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn)))) $;

--| The set of all the finite intersections of the elements of ` A ` . (Contributed by FL, 27-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fival {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cint (cv x3))) (cin (cpw A2) cfn)))) $;

--| Specific properties of an element of ` ( fi `` B ) ` . (Contributed by FL, 27-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem elfi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cfi)) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cint (cv x3))) (cin (cpw B2) cfn))) $;

--| The empty intersection need not be considered in the set of finite intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem elfi2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cfi)) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cint (cv x3))) (cdif (cin (cpw B2) cfn) (csn c0)))) $;

--| Sufficient condition for an element of ` ( fi `` B ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem elfir (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (w3a (wss A2 B2) (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn))) (wcel (cint A2) (cfv B2 cfi)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for the intersection of the range of a function to be in the set of finite intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem intrnfi (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn))) (wcel (cint (crn F)) (cfv B2 cfi)) $;

--| An indexed intersection of elements of ` C ` is an element of the finite intersections of ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem iinfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (w3a (wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2) (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn))) (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) (cfv C cfi)) $;

--| The intersection of two sets is a finite intersection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem inelfi (A2 B2 V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cfv X cfi)) $;

--| Any element of a set ` A ` is the intersection of a finite subset of ` A ` . (Contributed by FL, 27-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem ssfii (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 (cfv A2 cfi)) $;

--| The set of finite intersections of the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem fi0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cfi) c0 $;

--| A set is empty iff the class of all the finite intersections of that set is empty. (Contributed by FL, 27-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fieq0 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wceq A2 c0) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) c0)) $;

--| The elements of ` ( fi `` C ) ` are closed under finite intersection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fiin (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv C cfi)) (wcel B2 (cfv C cfi))) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cfv C cfi)) $;

--| The set of finite intersections is the smallest set that contains ` A ` and is closed under pairwise intersection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem dffi2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) (cint (cab z (wa (wss A2 (cv z)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)))))) $;

--| Subset relationship for function ` fi ` . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fiss (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (cfv A2 cfi) (cfv B2 cfi)) $;

--| A set which is closed under pairwise intersection is closed under finite intersection. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem inficl {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2) A2) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) A2)) $;

--| The set of finite intersections of a set is contained in the powerset of the union of the elements of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem fipwuni (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfv A2 cfi) (cpw (cuni A2)) $;

--| A singleton is closed under finite intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem fisn (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (csn A2) cfi) (csn A2) $;

--| The union of the finite intersections of a set is simply the union of the set itself. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fiuni (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cuni A2) (cuni (cfv A2 cfi))) $;

--| If a set is a family of subsets of some base set, then so is its finite intersection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fipwss (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 (cpw X)) (wss (cfv A2 cfi) (cpw X)) $;

--| A finite intersection of elements taken from a union of collections. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 15-Nov-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Nov-2013.)
theorem elfiun {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 D) (wcel C K)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv (cun B2 C) cfi)) (w3o (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cfi)) (wcel A2 (cfv C cfi)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (cin (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cfv C cfi)) (cfv B2 cfi)))) $;

--| The set of finite intersections can be "constructed" inductively by iterating binary intersection ` _om ` -many times. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem dffi3 {y1 z u: setvar} (A2 R V: class z u):
  $ wceq R (cmpt u cvv (crn (cmpo y1 z (cv u) (cv u) (cin (cv y1) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) (cuni (cima (crdg R A2) com))) $;

--| Describe a surjection from nonempty finite sets to finite intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem fifo {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class y1) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 (cdif (cin (cpw A2) cfn) (csn c0)) (cint (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wfo (cdif (cin (cpw A2) cfn) (csn c0)) (cfv A2 cfi) F) $;

--| Core induction for Philip Hall's marriage theorem. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha1lem {e c d: setvar} (A2: class) (b: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wi (wcel (cv b) cfn) (wral c (wi (wral d (wbr (cv d) (cima (cv c) (cv d)) cdom) (cpw A2)) (wrex e (wf1 A2 cvv (cv e)) (cpw (cv c)))) (cpw (cxp A2 (cv b))))) $;

--| (Philip) Hall's marriage theorem, sufficiency: a finite relation contains an injection if there is no subset of its domain which would be forced to violate the pigeonhole principle. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha1 {f2 d: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class f2 d) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (cxp A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wss (cv d) A2)) (wbr (cv d) (cima C (cv d)) cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wf1 A2 B2 (cv f2)) (cpw C)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ marypha2 . Properties of the used relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha2lem1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (T: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wss T (cxp A2 (cuni (crn F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ marypha2 . Properties of the used relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha2lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ marypha2 . Properties of the used relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha2lem3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (T: class x3) (F G: class):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wfn G A2)) (wb (wss G T) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ marypha2 . Properties of the used relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha2lem4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (T: class x3) (F X: class):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wss X A2)) (wceq (cima T X) (cuni (cima F X))) $;

--| Version of ~ marypha1 using a functional family of sets instead of a relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem marypha2 {x3 g2 d: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cfn F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wss (cv d) A2)) (wbr (cv d) (cuni (cima F (cv d))) cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (wa (wf1 A2 cvv (cv g2)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include supremum of class ` A ` . Here ` R ` is ordinarily a relation that strictly orders class ` B ` . For example, ` R ` could be 'less than' and ` B ` could be the set of real numbers.
term csup (A2 B2 R: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include infimum of class ` A ` . Here ` R ` is ordinarily a relation that strictly orders class ` B ` . For example, ` R ` could be 'less than' and ` B ` could be the set of real numbers.
term cinf (A2 B2 R: class): class;

--| Define the supremum of class ` A ` . It is meaningful when ` R ` is a relation that strictly orders ` B ` and when the supremum exists. For example, ` R ` could be 'less than', ` B ` could be the set of real numbers, and ` A ` could be the set of all positive reals whose square is less than 2; in this case the supremum is defined as the square root of 2 per ~ sqrtval . See ~ dfsup2 for alternate definition not requiring dummy variables. (Contributed by NM, 22-May-1999.)
axiom df_sup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (csup A2 B2 R) (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) A2)) B2)) B2)) $;

--| Define the infimum of class ` A ` . It is meaningful when ` R ` is a relation that strictly orders ` B ` and when the infimum exists. For example, ` R ` could be 'less than', ` B ` could be the set of real numbers, and ` A ` could be the set of all positive reals; in this case the infimum is 0. The infimum is defined as the supremum using the converse ordering relation. In the given example, 0 is the supremum of all reals (greatest real number) for which all positive reals are greater. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_inf (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cinf A2 B2 R) (csup A2 B2 (ccnv R)) $;

--| Quantifier-free definition of supremum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
theorem dfsup2 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (csup B2 A2 R) (cuni (cdif A2 (cun (cima (ccnv R) B2) (cima R (cdif A2 (cima (ccnv R) B2)))))) $;

--| Equality theorem for supremum. (Contributed by NM, 22-May-1999.)
theorem supeq1 (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wceq B2 C) (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) (csup C A2 R)) $;

--| Equality deduction for supremum. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem supeq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) (csup C A2 R)) $;

--| Equality inference for supremum. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem supeq1i (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (csup B2 A2 R) (csup C A2 R) $;

--| Equality theorem for supremum. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem supeq2 (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wceq B2 C) (wceq (csup A2 B2 R) (csup A2 C R)) $;

--| Equality theorem for supremum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem supeq3 (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (csup A2 B2 R) (csup A2 B2 S)) $;

--| Equality deduction for supremum. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Jan-2015.)
theorem supeq123d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 B2 C) (csup D E F)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for supremum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem nfsup {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (csup A2 B2 R) $;

--| Any class ` B ` has at most one supremum in ` A ` (where ` R ` is interpreted as 'less than'). (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supmo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrmo x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| A supremum is a set. (Contributed by NM, 22-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup B2 A2 R) cvv) $;

--| A supremum is unique. Similar to Theorem I.26 of [Apostol] p. 24 (but for suprema in general). (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.)
theorem supeu {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| Alternate expression for the supremum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Sep-2017.)
theorem supval2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) (crio x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for an element to be equal to the supremum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2015.)
theorem eqsup {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class z) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3a (wcel C A2) (wral y1 (wn (wbr C (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) C R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) C)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for an element to be equal to the supremum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2015.)
theorem eqsupd {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class z) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wn (wbr C (cv y1) R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wbr (cv y1) C R))) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) C) $;

--| A supremum belongs to its base class (closure law). See also ~ supub and ~ suplub . (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.)
theorem supcl {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup B2 A2 R) A2) $;

--| A supremum is an upper bound. See also ~ supcl and ~ suplub . This proof demonstrates how to expand an iota-based definition ( ~ df-iota ) using ~ riotacl2 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel C B2) (wn (wbr (csup B2 A2 R) C R))) $;

--| A supremum is the least upper bound. See also ~ supcl and ~ supub . (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem suplub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wbr C (csup B2 A2 R) R)) (wrex z (wbr C (cv z) R) B2)) $;

--| Bidirectional form of ~ suplub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suplub2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C A2)) (wb (wbr C (csup B2 A2 R) R) (wrex z (wbr C (cv z) R) B2)) $;

--| An upper bound is not less than the supremum. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2004.)
theorem supnub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wral z (wn (wbr C (cv z) R)) B2)) (wn (wbr C (csup B2 A2 R) R))) $;

--| Inequality deduction for supremum of a subset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Mar-2017.)
theorem supssd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) C)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (csup C A2 R) (csup B2 A2 R) R)) $;

--| A supremum is a set. (Contributed by NM, 22-May-1999.)
theorem supex (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wor A2 R $ >
  $ wcel (csup B2 A2 R) cvv $;

--| The supremum under an empty base set is always the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem sup00 (B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (csup B2 c0 R) c0 $;

--| The supremum of an empty set is the smallest element of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem sup0riota {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wor A2 R) (wceq (csup c0 A2 R) (crio x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of an empty set under a base set which has a unique smallest element is the smallest element of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem sup0 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wa (wcel X A2) (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) X R)) A2)) (wreu x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2)) (wceq (csup c0 A2 R) X) $;

--| The greatest element of a set is its supremum. Note that the converse is not true; the supremum might not be an element of the set considered. (Contributed by Jeff Hoffman, 17-Jun-2008.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem supmax {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wn (wbr C (cv y1) R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup B2 A2 R) C) $;

--| A finite set satisfies the conditions to have a supremum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fisup2g {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wne B2 c0) (wss B2 A2))) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) B2)) A2)) B2) $;

--| A nonempty finite set contains its supremum. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 9-May-2011.)
theorem fisupcl (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wne B2 c0) (wss B2 A2))) (wcel (csup B2 A2 R) B2) $;

--| The supremum of a finite set is greater than or equal to all the elements of the set. (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2019.)
theorem supgtoreq (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R S: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (csup B2 A2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr C S R) (wceq C S)) $;

--| The supremum of a pair. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem suppr (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2)) (wceq (csup (cpr B2 C) A2 R) (cif (wbr C B2 R) B2 C)) $;

--| The supremum of a singleton. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.)
theorem supsn (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel B2 A2)) (wceq (csup (csn B2) A2 R) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ supiso . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supisolem {y1 z w v: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z v) (A2 B2 C: class) (D R: class v) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wiso A2 B2 R S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel D A2)) (wb (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr D (cv y1) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) D R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) C)) A2)) (wa (wral w (wn (wbr (cfv D F) (cv w) S)) (cima F C)) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cfv D F) S) (wrex v (wbr (cv w) (cv v) S) (cima F C))) B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ supiso . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supisoex {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z v) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class v) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wiso A2 B2 R S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) C)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wa (wral w (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv w) S)) (cima F C)) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv u) S) (wrex v (wbr (cv w) (cv v) S) (cima F C))) B2)) B2) $;

--| Image of a supremum under an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem supiso {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wiso A2 B2 R S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) C)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup (cima F C) B2 S) (cfv (csup C A2 R) F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq1 (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wceq B2 C) (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) (cinf C A2 R)) $;

--| Equality deduction for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) (cinf C A2 R)) $;

--| Equality inference for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq1i (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) (cinf C A2 R) $;

--| Equality theorem for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq2 (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wceq B2 C) (wceq (cinf A2 B2 R) (cinf A2 C R)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq3 (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cinf A2 B2 R) (cinf A2 B2 S)) $;

--| Equality deduction for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infeq123d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf A2 B2 C) (cinf D E F)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem nfinf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cinf A2 B2 R) $;

--| An infimum is a set. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cinf B2 A2 R) cvv) $;

--| Sufficient condition for an element to be equal to the infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem eqinf {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (w3a (wcel C A2) (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) C R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr C (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) C)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for an element to be equal to the infimum. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem eqinfd {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wn (wbr (cv y1) C R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wbr C (cv y1) R))) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) C) $;

--| Alternate expression for the infimum. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2020.)
theorem infval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) (crio x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infcl , ~ inflb , ~ infglb , etc. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infcllem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (ccnv R))) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (ccnv R)) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (ccnv R)) B2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| An infimum belongs to its base class (closure law). See also ~ inflb and ~ infglb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infcl {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cinf B2 A2 R) A2) $;

--| An infimum is a lower bound. See also ~ infcl and ~ infglb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem inflb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel C B2) (wn (wbr C (cinf B2 A2 R) R))) $;

--| An infimum is the greatest lower bound. See also ~ infcl and ~ inflb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infglb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wbr (cinf B2 A2 R) C R)) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) C R) B2)) $;

--| Bidirectional form of ~ infglb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infglbb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C A2)) (wb (wbr (cinf B2 A2 R) C R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) C R) B2)) $;

--| A lower bound is not greater than the infimum. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infnlb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wral z (wn (wbr (cv z) C R)) B2)) (wn (wbr (cinf B2 A2 R) C R))) $;

--| Inequality deduction for infimum of a subset. (Contributed by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem infssd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) C)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cinf C A2 R) (cinf B2 A2 R) R)) $;

--| An infimum is a set. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infex (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wor A2 R $ >
  $ wcel (cinf B2 A2 R) cvv $;

--| The smallest element of a set is its infimum. Note that the converse is not true; the infimum might not be an element of the set considered. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem infmin {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wn (wbr (cv y1) C R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf B2 A2 R) C) $;

--| Any class ` B ` has at most one infimum in ` A ` (where ` R ` is interpreted as 'less than'). (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem infmo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrmo x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| An infimum is unique. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem infeu {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| A finite set has a minimum under a total order. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem fimin2g {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| A finite set has a minimum under a total order. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem fiming {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma showing existence and closure of infimum of a finite set. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem fiinfg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) A2)) A2)) A2) $;

--| A finite set satisfies the conditions to have an infimum. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem fiinf2g {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wne B2 c0) (wss B2 A2))) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) B2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) B2)) A2)) B2) $;

--| A nonempty finite set contains its infimum. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem fiinfcl (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 cfn) (wne B2 c0) (wss B2 A2))) (wcel (cinf B2 A2 R) B2) $;

--| The infimum of a finite set is less than or equal to all the elements of the set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infltoreq (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R S: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cinf B2 A2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr S C R) (wceq C S)) $;

--| The infimum of a pair. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infpr (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2)) (wceq (cinf (cpr B2 C) A2 R) (cif (wbr B2 C R) B2 C)) $;

--| The infimum of a proper pair is less than the supremum of this pair. (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem infsupprpr (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (w3a (wcel B2 A2) (wcel C A2) (wne B2 C))) (wbr (cinf (cpr B2 C) A2 R) (csup (cpr B2 C) A2 R) R) $;

--| The infimum of a singleton. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.)
theorem infsn (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel B2 A2)) (wceq (cinf (csn B2) A2 R) B2) $;

--| The infimum regarding an empty base set is always the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem inf00 (B2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cinf B2 c0 R) c0 $;

--| The infimum of an empty set under a base set which has a unique greatest element is the greatest element of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infempty {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor A2 R) (wa (wcel X A2) (wral y1 (wn (wbr X (cv y1) R)) A2)) (wreu x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2)) (wceq (cinf c0 A2 R) X) $;

--| Image of an infimum under an isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infiso {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wiso A2 B2 R S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) C) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) C)) A2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf (cima F C) B2 S) (cfv (cinf C A2 R) F)) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the canonical order isomorphism to an ordinal.
term coi (A2 R: class): class;

--| Define the canonical order isomorphism from the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` to an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
axiom df_oi {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq (coi A2 R) (cif (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (cres (crecs (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2)))) (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima (crecs (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2)))) (cv x3))) A2) con0)) c0) $;

--| Rewrite ~ df-oi with abbreviations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem dfoi {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (F: class x3 w v u t h j) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j):
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq (coi A2 R) (cif (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (cres F (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0)) c0) $;

--| Equality theorem for ordinal isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oieq1 (A2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (coi A2 R) (coi A2 S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for ordinal isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oieq2 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (coi A2 R) (coi B2 R)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for ordinal isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfoi {x3: setvar} (A2 R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (coi A2 R) $;

--| Generalize ~ ordiso to proper classes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordiso2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wiso A2 B2 cep cep F) (word A2) (word B2)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Order-isomorphic ordinal numbers are equal. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 16-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordiso {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wex f2 (wiso A2 B2 cep cep (cv f2)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypecbv {y1 w v u f2 h i j s1 r: setvar} (A2: class f2 i) (C: class y1 w h i j) (R: class) (F G: class y1 w v u f2 h i j s1 r):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio s1 (wral r (wn (wbr (cv r) (cv s1) R)) (crab y1 (wral i (wbr (cv i) (cv y1) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2)) (crab y1 (wral i (wbr (cv i) (cv y1) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2)))) F $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem1 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (cres F T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem2 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (word T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem3 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (M: class) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M (cin T (cdm F)))) (wcel (cfv M F) (crab v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (cima F M)) A2)) (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (cima F M)) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem4 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cin T (cdm F)) A2 O) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem5 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (word (cdm O)) (wf (cdm O) A2 O)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem6 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (M: class) (N: class x3 z v t h) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M (cdm O))) (wi (wcel N M) (wbr (cfv N O) (cfv M O) R)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . ` ran O ` is an initial segment of ` A ` under the well-order ` R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem7 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (M: class) (N: class x3 z v t h) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel N A2)) (wcel M (cdm O))) (wo (wbr (cfv M O) N R) (wcel N (crn O))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem8 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (cdm O) (crn O) cep R O) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . Either the function ` OrdIso ` is an isomorphism onto all of ` A ` , or ` OrdIso ` is not a set, which by ~ oif implies that either ` ran O C_ A ` is a proper class or ` dom O = On ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2024.)
theorem ordtypelem9 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j) (V: class x3 z w v u t h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (cdm O) A2 cep R O) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtype . Using ~ ax-rep , exclude the possibility that ` O ` is a proper class and does not enumerate all of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtypelem10 {x3 z w v u t h j: setvar} (ph: wff z w v u h j) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w t h j) (R: class) (T: class x3 z w v u t h j) (F: class) (G: class x3 z w v u t h j) (O: class z w h j):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab w (wral j (wbr (cv j) (cv w) R) (crn (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt h cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) R)) C) C)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab x3 (wrex t (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv t) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) con0) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (cdm O) A2 cep R O) $;

--| Definition of the ordinal isomorphism when its arguments are not meaningful. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem oi0 (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R))) (wceq F c0) $;

--| The order type of the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` is an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem oicl (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ word (cdm F) $;

--| The order isomorphism of the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oif (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wf (cdm F) A2 F $;

--| The order isomorphism of the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` is an isomorphism onto ` ran O ` (which is a subset of ` A ` by ~ oif ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem oiiso2 (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wiso (cdm F) (crn F) cep R F) $;

--| For any set-like well-ordered class, there is an isomorphic ordinal number called its order type. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtype (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wiso (cdm F) A2 cep R F) $;

--| ` ran F ` is an initial segment of ` A ` under the well-order ` R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem oiiniseg (A2 R F M N: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wcel N A2) (wcel M (cdm F)))) (wo (wbr (cfv M F) N R) (wcel N (crn F))) $;

--| For any set-like well-ordered class, if the order isomorphism exists (is a set), then it maps some ordinal onto ` A ` isomorphically. Otherwise, ` F ` is a proper class, which implies that either ` ran F C_ A ` is a proper class or ` dom F = On ` . This weak version of ~ ordtype does not require the Axiom of Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem ordtype2 (A2 R F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R) (wcel F cvv)) (wiso (cdm F) A2 cep R F) $;

--| The order isomorphism on a set is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem oiexg (A2 R F V: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| The order type of the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` is an ordinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oion (A2 R F V: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cdm F) con0) $;

--| The order isomorphism of the well-order ` R ` on ` A ` is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oiiso (A2 R F V: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wwe A2 R)) (wiso (cdm F) A2 cep R F) $;

--| The order type of a well-ordered set is equinumerous to the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem oien (A2 R F V: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wwe A2 R)) (wbr (cdm F) A2 cen) $;

--| Uniqueness of the unique ordinal isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem oieu (A2 B2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wb (wa (word B2) (wiso B2 A2 cep R G)) (wa (wceq B2 (cdm F)) (wceq G F))) $;

--| When ` A ` is a subclass of ` On ` , ` F ` is a strictly monotone ordinal functions, and it is also complete (it is an isomorphism onto all of ` A ` ). The proof avoids ~ ax-rep (the second statement is trivial under ~ ax-rep ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem oismo (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (coi A2 cep) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 con0) (wa (wsmo F) (wceq (crn F) A2)) $;

--| The order type of an ordinal under the ` e. ` order is itself, and the order isomorphism is the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem oiid (A2: class):
  $ wi (word A2) (wceq (coi A2 cep) (cres cid A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hartogs . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem hartogslem1 {x3 y1 z w t f2 s1 r: setvar} (A2: class z w t s1) (R: class y1 z w t f2 s1) (F: class x3 y1 z w t f2 s1 r) (V: class x3 z w t f2 s1):
  $ wceq F (copab r y1 (wa (wa (w3a (wss (cdm (cv r)) A2) (wss (cres cid (cdm (cv r))) (cv r)) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cdm (cv r)) (cdm (cv r))))) (wwe (cdm (cv r)) (cdif (cv r) cid))) (wceq (cv y1) (cdm (coi (cdm (cv r)) (cdif (cv r) cid)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab s1 t (wrex w (wrex z (wa (wa (wceq (cv s1) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) (wceq (cv t) (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)))) (wbr (cv w) (cv z) cep)) (cv y1)) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ w3a (wss (cdm F) (cpw (cxp A2 A2))) (wfun F) (wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (crn F) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cdom) con0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hartogs . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2013.)
theorem hartogslem2 {x3 y1 z w t f2 s1 r: setvar} (A2: class z w t s1) (R: class y1 z w t f2 s1) (F: class x3 y1 z w t f2 s1 r) (V: class x3 z w t f2 s1):
  $ wceq F (copab r y1 (wa (wa (w3a (wss (cdm (cv r)) A2) (wss (cres cid (cdm (cv r))) (cv r)) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cdm (cv r)) (cdm (cv r))))) (wwe (cdm (cv r)) (cdif (cv r) cid))) (wceq (cv y1) (cdm (coi (cdm (cv r)) (cdif (cv r) cid)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab s1 t (wrex w (wrex z (wa (wa (wceq (cv s1) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) (wceq (cv t) (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)))) (wbr (cv w) (cv z) cep)) (cv y1)) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cdom) con0) cvv) $;

--| The class of ordinals dominated by a given set is an ordinal. A shorter (when taking into account lemmas ~ hartogslem1 and ~ hartogslem2 ) proof can be given using the axiom of choice, see ~ ondomon . As its label indicates, this result is used to justify the definition of the Hartogs function ~ df-har . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem hartogs {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cdom) con0) con0) $;

--| The only sets which are well-ordered forwards and backwards are finite sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2015.)
theorem wofib (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wa (wwe A2 R) (wwe A2 (ccnv R))) $;

--| Value of the lexicographic order on a sequence space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem wemaplem1 {x3 y1 z w a b: setvar} (A2 P Q R S: class) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (V W: class x3 y1 z w a b):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P V) (wcel Q W)) (wb (wbr P Q T) (wrex a (wa (wbr (cfv (cv a) P) (cfv (cv a) Q) S) (wral b (wi (wbr (cv b) (cv a) R) (wceq (cfv (cv b) P) (cfv (cv b) Q))) A2)) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wemapso . Transitivity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jul-2024.)
theorem wemaplem2 {x3 y1 z w a b c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w a b c) (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (P Q R S: class) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (X: class):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv a) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cv a) P) (cfv (cv a) X) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral c (wi (wbr (cv c) (cv a) R) (wceq (cfv (cv c) P) (cfv (cv c) X))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv b) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cv b) X) (cfv (cv b) Q) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral c (wi (wbr (cv c) (cv b) R) (wceq (cfv (cv c) X) (cfv (cv c) Q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P Q T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wemapso . Transitivity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jul-2024.)
theorem wemaplem3 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (P Q R S: class) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (X: class):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wpo B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P X T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Q T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P Q T) $;

--| Construct lexicographic order on a function space based on a well-ordering of the indices and a total ordering of the values. Without totality on the values or least differing indices, the best we can prove here is a partial order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jul-2024.)
theorem wemappo {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (R S: class) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wpo B2 S)) (wpo (co B2 A2 cmap) T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wemapso . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jul-2024.)
theorem wemapsolem {x3 y1 z w a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (R S: class) (T U: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wss U (co B2 A2 cmap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor B2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) U) (wcel (cv b) U)) (wne (cv a) (cv b)))) (wrex c (wral d (wn (wbr (cv d) (cv c) R)) (cdm (cdif (cv a) (cv b)))) (cdm (cdif (cv a) (cv b)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor U T) $;

--| Construct lexicographic order on a function space based on a well-ordering of the indices and a total ordering of the values. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jul-2024.)
theorem wemapso {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (R S: class) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wor B2 S)) (wor (co B2 A2 cmap) T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wemapso2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2019.)
theorem wemapso2lem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (R S: class) (T U V W: class x3 y1 z w) (Z: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wor A2 R) (wor B2 S)) (wcel Z W)) (wor U T) $;

--| An alternative to having a well-order on ` R ` in ~ wemapso is to restrict the function set to finitely-supported functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2019.)
theorem wemapso2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1 z w) (R S: class) (T U V: class x3 y1 z w) (Z: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wor A2 R) (wor B2 S)) (wor U T) $;

--| The alternate definition of the cardinal of a set given in ~ cardval2 always gives a set, and indeed an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2013.)
theorem card2on {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 csdm) con0) con0 $;

--| The alternate definition of the cardinal of a set given in ~ cardval2 has the curious property that for non-numerable sets (for which ~ ndmfv yields ` (/) ` ), it still evaluates to a nonempty set, and indeed it contains ` _om ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem card2inf {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wn (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 cen) con0)) (wss com (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 csdm) con0)) $;

--| Class symbol for the Hartogs function.
term char: class;

--| Define the Hartogs function as mapping a set to the class of ordinals it dominates. That class is an ordinal by ~ hartogs , which is used in ~ harf . The _Hartogs number_ of a set is the least ordinal not dominated by that set. Theorem ~ harval2 proves that the Hartogs function actually gives the Hartogs number for well-orderable sets. The Hartogs number of an ordinal is its cardinal successor. This is proved for finite ordinal in ~ harsucnn . Traditionally, the Hartogs number of a set ` X ` is written ` aleph ( X ) ` , and its cardinal successor, ` X + ` ; we use functional notation for this, and cannot use the aleph symbol because it is taken for the enumerating function of the infinite initial ordinals ~ df-aleph . Some authors define the Hartogs number of a set to be the least *infinite* ordinal which does not inject into it, thus causing the range to consist only of alephs. We use the simpler definition where the value can be any successor cardinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_har {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq char (cmpt x3 cvv (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cdom) con0)) $;

--| Functionality of the Hartogs function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem harf:
  $ wf cvv con0 char $;

--| Values of the Hartogs function are ordinals (closure of the Hartogs function in the ordinals). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem harcl (X: class):
  $ wcel (cfv X char) con0 $;

--| Function value of the Hartogs function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem harval {y1: setvar} (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X char) (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) X cdom) con0)) $;

--| The Hartogs number of a set contains exactly the ordinals that set dominates. Combined with ~ harcl , this implies that the Hartogs number of a set is greater than all ordinals that set dominates. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem elharval (X Y: class):
  $ wb (wcel Y (cfv X char)) (wa (wcel Y con0) (wbr Y X cdom)) $;

--| The Hartogs number of a set does not inject into that set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem harndom (X: class):
  $ wn (wbr (cfv X char) X cdom) $;

--| Weak ordering property of the Hartogs function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem harword (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cdom) (wss (cfv X char) (cfv Y char)) $;

--| Class symbol for the weak dominance relation.
term cwdom: class;

--| A set is _weakly dominated_ by a "larger" set if the "larger" set can be mapped onto the "smaller" set or the smaller set is empty, or equivalently, if the smaller set can be placed into bijection with some partition of the larger set. Dominance ( ~ df-dom ) implies weak dominance (over ZF). The principle asserting the converse is known as the partition principle and is independent of ZF. Theorem ~ fodom proves that the axiom of choice implies the partition principle (over ZF). It is not known whether the partition principle is equivalent to the axiom of choice (over ZF), although it is know to imply dependent choice. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_wdom {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cwdom (copab x3 y1 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wex z (wfo (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv z))))) $;

--| Weak dominance is a relation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem relwdom:
  $ wrel cwdom $;

--| Property of weak dominance (definitional form). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem brwdom {z: setvar} (V: class z) (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel Y V) (wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wo (wceq X c0) (wex z (wfo Y X (cv z))))) $;

--| Property of weak dominance, forward direction only. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem brwdomi {z: setvar} (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cwdom) (wo (wceq X c0) (wex z (wfo Y X (cv z)))) $;

--| Weak dominance over nonempty sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem brwdomn0 {z: setvar} (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wne X c0) (wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wex z (wfo Y X (cv z)))) $;

--| Any set weakly dominates the empty set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem _0wdom (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wbr c0 X cwdom) $;

--| An onto function implies weak dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem fowdom (F V X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wfo Y X F)) (wbr X Y cwdom) $;

--| Reflexivity of weak dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem wdomref (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wbr X X cwdom) $;

--| Alternate characterization of the weak dominance predicate which does not require special treatment of the empty set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem brwdom2 {y1 z: setvar} (V: class y1 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel Y V) (wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wrex y1 (wex z (wfo (cv y1) X (cv z))) (cpw Y))) $;

--| Weak dominance is implied by dominance in the usual sense. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem domwdom (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cdom) (wbr X Y cwdom) $;

--| Transitivity of weak dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem wdomtr (X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr X Y cwdom) (wbr Y Z cwdom)) (wbr X Z cwdom) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and weak dominance. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem wdomen1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr A2 C cwdom) (wbr B2 C cwdom)) $;

--| Equality-like theorem for equinumerosity and weak dominance. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem wdomen2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wbr C A2 cwdom) (wbr C B2 cwdom)) $;

--| Weak dominance strengthens to usual dominance on the power sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem wdompwdom (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cwdom) (wbr (cpw X) (cpw Y) cdom) $;

--| Cantor's Theorem, stated using weak dominance (this is actually a stronger statement than ~ canth2 , equivalent to ~ canth ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem canthwdom (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr (cpw A2) A2 cwdom) $;

--| Deduce weak dominance from an implicit onto function (stated in a way which avoids ~ ax-rep ). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem wdom2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) X) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) $;

--| Deduce weak dominance from an implicit onto function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem wdomd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (W: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) X) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) $;

--| Condition for weak dominance with a condition reminiscent of ~ wdomd . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem brwdom3 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (V W: class x3 y1 f2) (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y W)) (wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wex f2 (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) Y) X))) $;

--| Weak dominance implies existence of a covering function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem brwdom3i {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cwdom) (wex f2 (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) Y) X)) $;

--| Weak dominance of a (disjoint) union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem unwdomg (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) (wbr C D cwdom) (wceq (cin B2 D) c0)) (wbr (cun A2 C) (cun B2 D) cwdom) $;

--| Weak dominance of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem xpwdomg (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) (wbr C D cwdom)) (wbr (cxp A2 C) (cxp B2 D) cwdom) $;

--| A set is weakly dominant over its image under any function. This version of ~ wdomimag is stated so as to avoid ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem wdomima2g (A2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun F) (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cima F A2) W)) (wbr (cima F A2) A2 cwdom) $;

--| A set is weakly dominant over its image under any function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem wdomimag (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 V)) (wbr (cima F A2) A2 cwdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ unxpwdom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem unxpwdom2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cxp A2 A2) (cun B2 C) cen) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) (wbr A2 C cdom)) $;

--| If a Cartesian product is dominated by a union, then the base set is either weakly dominated by one factor of the union or dominated by the other. Extracted from Lemma 2.3 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 420. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem unxpwdom (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cxp A2 A2) (cun B2 C) cdom) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) (wbr A2 C cdom)) $;

--| Describe an onto function from the indexed cartesian product to the indexed union. Together with ~ ixpssmapg this shows that ` U_ x e. A B ` and ` X_ x e. A B ` have closely linked cardinalities. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem ixpiunwdom {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V W: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) W) (wne (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0)) (wbr (ciun x3 A2 B2) (cxp (cixp x3 A2 B2) A2) cwdom) $;

--| The value of the Hartogs function at a set ` X ` is weakly dominated by ` ~P ( X X. X ) ` . This follows from a more precise analysis of the bound used in ~ hartogs to prove that ` ( har `` X ) ` is an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem harwdom (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wbr (cfv X char) (cpw (cxp X X)) cwdom) $;

--| Axiom of Regularity. An axiom of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory. Also called the Axiom of Foundation. A rather non-intuitive axiom that denies more than it asserts, it states (in the form of ~ zfreg ) that every nonempty set contains a set disjoint from itself. One consequence is that it denies the existence of a set containing itself ( ~ elirrv ). A stronger version that works for proper classes is proved as ~ zfregs . (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1993.)
axiom ax_reg {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wex y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Regularity expressed more compactly. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2003.)
theorem axreg2 {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Regularity with class variables. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1994.) Replace sethood hypothesis with sethood antecedent. (Revised by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.) Avoid ~ ax-10 and ~ ax-12 . (Revised by TM, 31-Dec-2025.)
theorem zfregcl {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ zfregcl as of 31-Dec-2025. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1994.) Replace sethood hypothesis with sethood antecedent. (Revised by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfregclOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| The Axiom of Regularity using abbreviations. Axiom 6 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 21. This is called the "weak form". Axiom Reg of [BellMachover] p. 480. There is also a "strong form", not requiring that ` A ` be a set, that can be proved with more difficulty (see ~ zfregs ). (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1995.) Replace sethood hypothesis with sethood antecedent. (Revised by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.)
theorem zfreg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cin (cv x3) A2) c0) A2) $;

--| The membership relation is irreflexive: no set is a member of itself. Theorem 105 of [Suppes] p. 54. This is trivial to prove from ~ zfregfr and ~ efrirr (see ~ elirrvALT ), but this proof is direct from ~ ax-reg . (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) Reduce axiom dependencies and make use of ~ ax-reg directly. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 27-Dec-2025.) Avoid ~ ax-pr . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 21-May-2026.) (Proof shortened by Matthew House, 23-May-2026.)
theorem elirrv {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elirrv as of 21-May-2026. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) Reduce axiom dependencies and make use of ~ ax-reg directly. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 27-Dec-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elirrvOLD {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elirrv as of 27-Dec-2025. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elirrvOLDOLD {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| No class is a member of itself. Exercise 6 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 22. Theorem 1.9(i) of [Schloeder] p. 1. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jul-2011.)
theorem elirr (A2: class):
  $ wn (wcel A2 A2) $;

--| A class is not equal to any of its elements. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2022.)
theorem elneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| A class is not an element of and equal to a class at the same time. Variant of ~ elneq analogously to ~ elnotel and ~ en2lp . (Proposed by BJ, 18-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2022.) (Proof shortened by TM, 31-Dec-2025.) (Proof shortened by SN, 22-Apr-2026.)
theorem nelaneq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ nelaneq as of 22-Apr-2026. (Proposed by BJ, 18-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2022.) (Proof shortened by TM, 31-Dec-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nelaneqOLD (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ nelaneq as of 31-Dec-2025. (Proposed by BJ, 18-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nelaneqOLDOLD (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The membership relation and the identity relation are disjoint. Variable-free version of ~ nelaneq . (Proposed by BJ, 18-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2022.)
theorem epinid0:
  $ wceq (cin cep cid) c0 $;

--| A class is equal to its successor iff it is a proper class (assuming the Axiom of Regularity). (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-2004.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by SN, 22-Apr-2026.)
theorem sucprcreg (A2: class):
  $ wb (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (csuc A2) A2) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ sucprcreg as of 31-Dec-2025. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-2004.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 16-Apr-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sucprcregOLD (A2: class):
  $ wb (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (csuc A2) A2) $;

--| The Russell class is equal to the universe ` _V ` . Exercise 5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 22. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 4-Oct-2008.)
theorem ruv {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cab x3 (wnel (cv x3) (cv x3))) cvv $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ ru , simplified using (indirectly) the Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 4-Oct-2008.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ruALT {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wnel (cv x3) (cv x3))) cvv $;

--| A class is disjoint from its singleton. A consequence of regularity. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.) (Revised by BJ, 4-Apr-2019.)
theorem disjcsn (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cin A2 (csn A2)) c0 $;

--| The membership relation is well-founded on any class. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1995.)
theorem zfregfr (A2: class):
  $ wfr A2 cep $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ elirrv , shorter but using more axioms. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 28-Dec-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elirrvALT (x3: setvar):
  $ wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| No class has 2-cycle membership loops. Theorem 7X(b) of [Enderton] p. 206. (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem en2lp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 A2)) $;

--| Two classes are not elements of each other simultaneously. This is just a rewriting of ~ en2lp and serves as an example in the context of Godel codes, see ~ elnanelprv . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elnanel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnan (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 A2) $;

--| The membership (epsilon) relation and its converse are disjoint, i.e., ` _E ` is an asymmetric relation. Variable-free version of ~ en2lp . (Proposed by BJ, 18-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2022.)
theorem cnvepnep:
  $ wceq (cin (ccnv cep) cep) c0 $;

--| The membership (epsilon) relation is not symmetric. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2022.)
theorem epnsym:
  $ wne (ccnv cep) cep $;

--| A class cannot be an element of one of its elements. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2022.)
theorem elnotel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wn (wcel B2 A2)) $;

--| A class cannot be an element of one of its elements. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2022.)
theorem elnel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wnel B2 A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ en3lp . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Oct-2011.)
theorem en3lplem1 (x3: setvar) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 C) (wcel C A2)) (wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wne (cin (cv x3) (ctp A2 B2 C)) c0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ en3lp . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Oct-2011.)
theorem en3lplem2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 C) (wcel C A2)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (ctp A2 B2 C)) (wne (cin (cv x3) (ctp A2 B2 C)) c0)) $;

--| No class has 3-cycle membership loops. This proof was automatically generated from the virtual deduction proof ~ en3lpVD using a translation program. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 24-Oct-2011.)
theorem en3lp (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wn (w3a (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 C) (wcel C A2)) $;

--| Equality of two unordered pairs when one member of each pair contains the other member. Closed form of ~ preleq . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2022.)
theorem preleqg (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C D)) (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D))) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Equality of two unordered pairs when one member of each pair contains the other member. (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-1996.) (Revised by AV, 15-Jun-2022.)
theorem preleq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel C D)) (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D))) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ preleq , not based on ~ preleqg : Equality of two unordered pairs when one member of each pair contains the other member. (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-1996.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem preleqALT (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel C D)) (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D))) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Theorem for alternate representation of ordered pairs, requiring the Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg (via the ~ preleq step). See ~ df-op for a description of other ordered pair representations. Exercise 34 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-1996.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Jun-2022.)
theorem opthreg (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (cpr A2 (cpr A2 B2)) (cpr C (cpr C D))) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| The successor operation behaves like a one-to-one function (assuming the Axiom of Regularity). Exercise 35 of [Enderton] p. 208 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2003.)
theorem suc11reg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (csuc A2) (csuc B2)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Assuming ~ ax-reg , an ordinal is a transitive class on which inclusion satisfies trichotomy. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Oct-2010.)
theorem dford2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (wa (wtr A2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (w3o (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) A2) A2)) $;

--| Existence of ` _om ` implies our axiom of infinity ~ ax-inf . The proof shows that the especially contrived class " ` ran ( rec ( ( v e. _V |-> suc v ) , x ) |`` _om ) ` " exists, is a subset of its union, and contains a given set ` x ` (and thus is nonempty). Thus, it provides an example demonstrating that a set ` y ` exists with the necessary properties demanded by ~ ax-inf . (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-1996.) Revised to closed form. (Revised by BJ, 20-May-2024.)
theorem inf0 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (V: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wcel com V) (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)))))))) $;

--| Variation of Axiom of Infinity (using ~ zfinf as a hypothesis). Axiom of Infinity in [FreydScedrov] p. 283. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-1996.) (Revised by David Abernethy, 1-Oct-2013.)
theorem inf1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $;

--| Variation of Axiom of Infinity. There exists a nonempty set that is a subset of its union (using ~ zfinf as a hypothesis). Abbreviated version of the Axiom of Infinity in [FreydScedrov] p. 283. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lema {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 G)) (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wss (cin A2 (cv x3)) B2)) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lemb {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 F) c0 $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lemc {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) F) (cfv (cfv A2 F) G)) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lemd {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss (cfv A2 F) (cv x3)) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem1 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss (cfv A2 F) (cfv (csuc A2) F)) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem2 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wi (wcel A2 com) (wne (cfv A2 F) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. In the proof, we invoke the Axiom of Regularity in the form of ~ zfreg . (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem3 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wi (wcel A2 com) (wne (cfv A2 F) (cfv (csuc A2) F))) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem4 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wi (wcel A2 com) (wpss (cfv A2 F) (cfv (csuc A2) F))) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem5 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 A2)) (wpss (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F))) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3lem6 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wf1 com (cpw (cv x3)) F) $;

--| Lemma for our Axiom of Infinity => standard Axiom of Infinity. See ~ inf3 for detailed description. In the proof, we invoke the Axiom of Replacement in the form of ~ f1dmex . (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2013.)
theorem inf3lem7 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 cvv (crab w (wss (cin (cv w) (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg G c0) com) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wcel com cvv) $;

--| Our Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf implies the standard Axiom of Infinity. The hypothesis is a variant of our Axiom of Infinity provided by ~ inf2 , and the conclusion is the version of the Axiom of Infinity shown as Axiom 7 in [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. (Other standard versions are proved later as ~ axinf2 and ~ zfinf2 .) The main proof is provided by ~ inf3lema through ~ inf3lem7 , and this final piece eliminates the auxiliary hypothesis of ~ inf3lem7 . This proof is due to Ian Sutherland, Richard Heck, and Norman Megill and was posted on Usenet as shown below. Although the result is not new, the authors were unable to find a published proof. <HTML> <PRE> (As posted to sci.logic on 30-Oct-1996, with annotations added.) Theorem: The statement "There exists a nonempty set that is a subset of its union" implies the Axiom of Infinity. Proof: Let X be a nonempty set which is a subset of its union; the latter property is equivalent to saying that for any y in X, there exists a z in X such that y is in z. Define by finite recursion a function F:omega-->(power X) such that F_0 = 0 (See ~ inf3lemb .) F_n+1 = {y&lt;X | y^X subset F_n} (See ~ inf3lemc .) Note: ^ means intersect, &lt; means \in ("element of"). (Finite recursion as typically done requires the existence of omega; to avoid this we can just use transfinite recursion restricted to omega. F is a class-term that is not necessarily a set at this point.) Lemma 1. F_n subset F_n+1. (See ~ inf3lem1 .) Proof: By induction: F_0 subset F_1. If y &lt; F_n+1, then y^X subset F_n, so if F_n subset F_n+1, then y^X subset F_n+1, so y &lt; F_n+2. Lemma 2. F_n =/= X. (See ~ inf3lem2 .) Proof: By induction: F_0 =/= X because X is not empty. Assume F_n =/= X. Then there is a y in X that is not in F_n. By definition of X, there is a z in X that contains y. Suppose F_n+1 = X. Then z is in F_n+1, and z^X contains y, so z^X is not a subset of F_n, contrary to the definition of F_n+1. Lemma 3. F_n =/= F_n+1. (See ~ inf3lem3 .) Proof: Using the identity y^X subset F_n &lt;-> y^(X-F_n) = 0, we have F_n+1 = {y&lt;X | y^(X-F_n) = 0}. Let q = {y&lt;X-F_n | y^(X-F_n) = 0}. Then q subset F_n+1. Since X-F_n is not empty by Lemma 2 and q is the set of \in-minimal elements of X-F_n, by Foundation q is not empty, so q and therefore F_n+1 have an element not in F_n. Lemma 4. F_n proper_subset F_n+1. (See ~ inf3lem4 .) Proof: Lemmas 1 and 3. Lemma 5. F_m proper_subset F_n, m &lt; n. (See ~ inf3lem5 .) Proof: Fix m and use induction on n > m. Basis: F_m proper_subset F_m+1 by Lemma 4. Induction: Assume F_m proper_subset F_n. Then since F_n proper_subset F_n+1, F_m proper_subset F_n+1 by transitivity of proper subset. By Lemma 5, F_m =/= F_n for m =/= n, so F is 1-1. (See ~ inf3lem6 .) Thus, the inverse of F is a function with range omega and domain a subset of power X, so omega exists by Replacement. (See ~ inf3lem7 .) Q.E.D. </PRE> </HTML> (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1996.)
theorem inf3 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wne (cv x3) c0) (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| Half of ~ infeq5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem infeq5i {x3: setvar}:
  $ wi (wcel com cvv) (wex x3 (wpss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) $;

--| The statement "there exists a set that is a proper subset of its union" is equivalent to the Axiom of Infinity (shown on the right-hand side in the form of ~ omex .) The left-hand side provides us with a very short way to express the Axiom of Infinity using only elementary symbols. This proof of equivalence does not depend on the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem infeq5 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb (wex x3 (wpss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3)))) (wcel com cvv) $;

--| Axiom of Infinity. An axiom of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory. This axiom is the gateway to "Cantor's paradise" (an expression coined by Hilbert). It asserts that given a starting set ` x ` , an infinite set ` y ` built from it exists. Although our version is apparently not given in the literature, it is similar to, but slightly shorter than, the Axiom of Infinity in [FreydScedrov] p. 283 (see ~ inf1 and ~ inf2 ). More standard versions, which essentially state that there exists a set containing all the natural numbers, are shown as ~ zfinf2 and ~ omex and are based on the (nontrivial) proof of ~ inf3 . This version has the advantage that when expanded to primitives, it has fewer symbols than the standard version ~ ax-inf2 . Theorem ~ inf0 shows the reverse derivation of our axiom from a standard one. Theorem ~ inf5 shows a very short way to state this axiom. The standard version of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 requires this axiom along with Regularity ~ ax-reg for its derivation (as Theorem ~ axinf2 below). In order to more easily identify the normal uses of Regularity, we will usually reference ~ ax-inf2 instead of this one. The derivation of this axiom from ~ ax-inf2 is shown by Theorem ~ axinf . Proofs should normally use the standard version ~ ax-inf2 instead of this axiom. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1993.)
axiom ax_inf {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Infinity expressed with the fewest number of different variables. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2003.)
theorem zfinf {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $;

--| A standard version of Axiom of Infinity, expanded to primitives, derived from our version of Infinity ~ ax-inf and Regularity ~ ax-reg . This theorem should not be referenced in any proof. Instead, use ~ ax-inf2 below so that the ordinary uses of Regularity can be more easily identified. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-1996.)
theorem axinf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wal w (wb (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wo (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))))))))) $;

--| A standard version of Axiom of Infinity of ZF set theory. In English, it says: there exists a set that contains the empty set and the successors of all of its members. Theorem ~ zfinf2 shows it converted to abbreviations. This axiom was derived as Theorem ~ axinf2 above, using our version of Infinity ~ ax-inf and the Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg . We will reference ~ ax-inf2 instead of ~ axinf2 so that the ordinary uses of Regularity can be more easily identified. The reverse derivation of ~ ax-inf from ~ ax-inf2 is shown by Theorem ~ axinf . (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-1996.)
axiom ax_inf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wal w (wb (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wo (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))))))))) $;

--| A standard version of the Axiom of Infinity, using definitions to abbreviate. Axiom Inf of [BellMachover] p. 472. (See ~ ax-inf2 for the unabbreviated version.) (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.)
theorem zfinf2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wcel c0 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (csuc (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) $;

--| The existence of omega (the class of natural numbers). Axiom 7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 43. Remark 1.21 of [Schloeder] p. 3. This theorem is proved assuming the Axiom of Infinity and in fact is equivalent to it, as shown by the reverse derivation ~ inf0 . A finitist (someone who doesn't believe in infinity) could, without contradiction, replace the Axiom of Infinity by its denial ` -. _om e. _V ` ; this would lead to ` _om = On ` by ~ omon and ` Fin = _V ` (the universe of all sets) by ~ fineqv . The finitist could still develop natural number, integer, and rational number arithmetic but would be denied the real numbers (as well as much of the rest of mathematics). In deference to the finitist, much of our development is done, when possible, without invoking the Axiom of Infinity; an example is Peano's axioms ~ peano1 through ~ peano5 (which many textbooks prove more easily assuming Infinity). (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-1994.)
theorem omex:
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| The first version of the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf proved from the second version ~ ax-inf2 . Note that we didn't use ~ ax-reg , unlike the other direction ~ axinf2 . (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2009.)
theorem axinf {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| The statement "there exists a set that is a proper subset of its union" is equivalent to the Axiom of Infinity (see Theorem ~ infeq5 ). This provides us with a very compact way to express the Axiom of Infinity using only elementary symbols. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jun-2005.)
theorem inf5 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wpss (cv x3) (cuni (cv x3))) $;

--| Omega is an ordinal number. Theorem 1.22 of [Schloeder] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1998.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2013.)
theorem omelon:
  $ wcel com con0 $;

--| The class of natural numbers ` _om ` can be defined as the intersection of all inductive sets (which is the smallest inductive set, since inductive sets are closed under intersection), which is valid provided we assume the Axiom of Infinity. Definition 6.3 of [Eisenberg] p. 82. Definition 1.20 of [Schloeder] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-1994.)
theorem dfom3 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (cint (cab x3 (wa (wcel c0 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (csuc (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))))) $;

--| A simplification of ~ elom assuming the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-2003.)
theorem elom3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 com) (wal x3 (wi (wlim (cv x3)) (wcel A2 (cv x3)))) $;

--| A simplification of ~ df-om assuming the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-2003.)
theorem dfom4 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (cab x3 (wal y1 (wi (wlim (cv y1)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| ` _om ` is the smallest limit ordinal and can be defined as such (although the Axiom of Infinity is needed to ensure that at least one limit ordinal exists). Theorem 1.23 of [Schloeder] p. 4. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem dfom5 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (cint (cab x3 (wlim (cv x3)))) $;

--| Ordinal addition is not commutative. This theorem shows a counterexample. Remark in [TakeutiZaring] p. 60. (Contributed by NM, 10-Dec-2004.)
theorem oancom:
  $ wne (co c1o com coa) (co com c1o coa) $;

--| A set is finite iff it is strictly dominated by the class of natural number. Theorem 42 of [Suppes] p. 151. The Axiom of Infinity is used for the forward implication. (Contributed by FL, 16-Apr-2011.)
theorem isfinite (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 com csdm) $;

--| A finite set is countable (weaker version of ~ isfinite ). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2018.)
theorem fict (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr A2 com cdom) $;

--| A natural number is strictly dominated by the set of natural numbers. Example 3 of [Enderton] p. 146. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2003.)
theorem nnsdom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wbr A2 com csdm) $;

--| Omega is equinumerous to a proper subset of itself. Example 13.2(4) of [Eisenberg] p. 216. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2003.)
theorem omenps:
  $ wbr com (cdif com (csn c0)) cen $;

--| The set of natural numbers is equinumerous to its successor. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem omensuc:
  $ wbr com (csuc com) cen $;

--| Removing a singleton from an infinite set does not change the cardinality of the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem infdifsn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr (cdif A2 (csn B2)) A2 cen) $;

--| Removing a finite set from an infinite set does not change the cardinality of the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem infdiffi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr com A2 cdom) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wbr (cdif A2 B2) A2 cen) $;

--| Any unbounded subset of natural numbers is equinumerous to the set of all natural numbers. This version of ~ unbnn eliminates its hypothesis by assuming the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by NM, 4-May-2005.)
theorem unbnn3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 com) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) com)) (wbr A2 com cen) $;

--| Using the Axiom of Regularity in the form ~ zfregfr , show that there are no infinite descending ` e. ` -chains. Proposition 7.34 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 44. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem noinfep {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wrex x3 (wnel (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com $;

--| Extend class notation with the Cantor normal form function.
term ccnf: class;

--| Define the Cantor normal form function, which takes as input a finitely supported function from ` y ` to ` x ` and outputs the corresponding member of the ordinal exponential ` x ^o y ` . The content of the original Cantor Normal Form theorem is that for ` x = _om ` this function is a bijection onto ` _om ^o y ` for any ordinal ` y ` (or, since the function restricts naturally to different ordinals, the statement that the composite function is a bijection to ` On ` ). More can be said about the function, however, and in particular it is an order isomorphism for a certain easily defined well-ordering of the finitely supported functions, which gives an alternate definition ~ cantnffval2 of this function in terms of ~ df-oi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_cnf {x3 y1 z f2 g2 h k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccnf (cmpo x3 y1 con0 con0 (cmpt f2 (crab g2 (wbr (cv g2) c0 cfsupp) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmap)) (csb h (coi (co (cv f2) c0 csupp) cep) (cfv (cdm (cv h)) (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) (cv h)) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv h)) (cv f2)) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0))))) $;

--| The value of the Cantor normal form function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnffval {z f2 g2 h k: setvar} (ph: wff z f2 g2 h k) (A2 B2: class) (S: class z g2 h k):
  $ wceq S (crab g2 (wbr (cv g2) c0 cfsupp) (co A2 B2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 ccnf) (cmpt f2 S (csb h (coi (co (cv f2) c0 csupp) cep) (cfv (cdm (cv h)) (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) (cv h)) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv h)) (cv f2)) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0))))) $;

--| The domain of the Cantor normal form function (in later lemmas we will use ` dom ( A CNF B ) ` to abbreviate "the set of finitely supported functions from ` B ` to ` A ` "). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfdm {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (A2 B2: class) (S: class g2):
  $ wceq S (crab g2 (wbr (cv g2) c0 cfsupp) (co A2 B2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . The function appearing in ~ cantnfval is unconditionally a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2015.)
theorem cantnfvalf {z k: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D F: class z k):
  $ wceq F (cseqom (cmpo k z A2 B2 (co C D coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wf com con0 F $;

--| Elementhood in the set of finitely supported functions from ` B ` to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfs (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F S) (wa (wf B2 A2 F) (wbr F c0 cfsupp))) $;

--| Basic properties of the order isomorphism ` G ` used later. The support of an ` F e. S ` is a finite subset of ` A ` , so it is well-ordered by ` _E ` and the order isomorphism has domain a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wwe (co F c0 csupp) cep) (wcel (cdm G) com)) $;

--| The value of the Cantor normal form function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfval {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F G: class) (H: class z k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (cfv (cdm G) H)) $;

--| Alternate expression for the value of the Cantor normal form function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfval2 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F G: class) (H: class z k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (cfv (cdm G) (cseqom (cmpo k z (cdm G) con0 (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0))) $;

--| The value of the recursive function ` H ` at a successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfsuc {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K com)) (wceq (cfv (csuc K) H) (co (co (co A2 (cfv K G) coe) (cfv (cfv K G) F) comu) (cfv K H) coa)) $;

--| A lower bound on the ` CNF ` function. Since ` ( ( A CNF B ) `` F ) ` is defined as the sum of ` ( A ^o x ) .o ( F `` x ) ` over all ` x ` in the support of ` F ` , it is larger than any of these terms (and all other terms are zero, so we can extend the statement to all ` C e. B ` instead of just those ` C ` in the support). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfle {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (S F G: class) (H: class z k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co A2 C coe) (cfv C F) comu) (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $;

--| An upper bound on the partial sums of the ` CNF ` function. Since each term dominates all previous terms, by induction we can bound the whole sum with any exponent ` A ^o C ` where ` C ` is larger than any exponent ` ( G `` x ) , x e. K ` which has been summed so far. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 29-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnflt {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (S F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (csuc (cdm G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima G K) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv K H) (co A2 C coe)) $;

--| An upper bound on the ` CNF ` function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 29-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnflt2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C S F: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F c0 csupp) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (co A2 C coe)) $;

--| The ` CNF ` function is a function from finitely supported functions from ` B ` to ` A ` , to the ordinal exponential ` A ^o B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem cantnff (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S (co A2 B2 coe) (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $;

--| The value of the zero function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem cantnf0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cxp B2 (csn c0)) (co A2 B2 ccnf)) c0) $;

--| A function is finitely supported from ` B ` to ` A ` iff the extended function is finitely supported from ` D ` to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem cantnfrescl {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D: class) (S T X: class n):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (cdif D B2))) (wceq X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cdm (co A2 D ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt n B2 X) S) (wcel (cmpt n D X) T)) $;

--| The ` CNF ` function respects extensions of the domain to a larger ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem cantnfres {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D: class) (S T X: class n):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (cdif D B2))) (wceq X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cdm (co A2 D ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt n B2 X) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt n B2 X) (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (cfv (cmpt n D X) (co A2 D ccnf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnfp1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfp1lem1 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (F: class t) (G X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G c0 csupp) X) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t B2 (cif (wceq (cv t) X) Y (cfv (cv t) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnfp1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem cantnfp1lem2 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (F: class t) (G: class) (O: class t) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G c0 csupp) X) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t B2 (cif (wceq (cv t) X) Y (cfv (cv t) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 Y) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm O) (csuc (cuni (cdm O)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnfp1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnfp1lem3 {z t k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (F: class t) (G: class) (H: class z t k) (K: class) (M: class z t k) (O: class t) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G c0 csupp) X) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t B2 (cif (wceq (cv t) X) Y (cfv (cv t) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 Y) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) O) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) O) F) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq K (coi (co G c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq M (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) K) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) K) G) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (co (co (co A2 X coe) Y comu) (cfv G (co A2 B2 ccnf)) coa)) $;

--| If ` F ` is created by adding a single term ` ( F `` X ) = Y ` to ` G ` , where ` X ` is larger than any element of the support of ` G ` , then ` F ` is also a finitely supported function and it is assigned the value ` ( ( A ^o X ) .o Y ) +o z ` where ` z ` is the value of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnfp1 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (F: class t) (G X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G c0 csupp) X) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t B2 (cif (wceq (cv t) X) Y (cfv (cv t) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel F S) (wceq (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (co (co (co A2 X coe) Y comu) (cfv G (co A2 B2 ccnf)) coa))) $;

--| The relation ` T ` is a strict order on ` S ` (a corollary of ~ wemapso2 ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem oemapso {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor S T) $;

--| Value of the relation ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem oemapval {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G T) (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv z) G)) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (cv w) G))) B2)) B2)) $;

--| If ` F < G ` , then there is some ` z ` witnessing this, but we can say more and in fact there is a definable expression ` X ` that also witnesses ` F < G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem oemapvali {x3 y1 z w c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel (cfv X F) (cfv X G)) (wral w (wi (wcel X (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (cv w) G))) B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 2-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnflem1a {x3 y1 z w c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G c0 csupp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 2-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnflem1b {x3 y1 z w c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (u: setvar) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (O X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co G c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (csuc (cv u)) (cdm O)) (wss (cfv X (ccnv O)) (cv u)))) (wss X (cfv (cv u) O)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 2-Jul-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem cantnflem1c {x3 y1 z w c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (u: setvar) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (O X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co G c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph (wa (wcel (csuc (cv u)) (cdm O)) (wss (cfv X (ccnv O)) (cv u)))) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wa (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv u) O) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cv x3) (co G c0 csupp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 2-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnflem1d {x3 y1 z w k c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (H: class z w k c) (O X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co G c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) O) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) O) G) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 B2 (cif (wss (cv x3) X) (cfv (cv x3) F) c0)) (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (cfv (csuc (cfv X (ccnv O))) H)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . This part of the proof is showing uniqueness of the Cantor normal form. We already know that the relation ` T ` is a strict order, but we haven't shown it is a well-order yet. But being a strict order is enough to show that two distinct ` F , G ` are ` T ` -related as ` F < G ` or ` G < F ` , and WLOG assuming that ` F < G ` , we show that ` CNF ` respects this order and maps these two to different ordinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 2-Jul-2019.)
theorem cantnflem1 {x3 y1 z w k c: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F G: class) (H: class z w k c) (O X: class c):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G T) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (crab c (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv c) G)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (co G c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co (co (co A2 (cfv (cv k) O) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) O) G) comu) (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co A2 B2 ccnf)) (cfv G (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem cantnflem2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2 C: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 coe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (crn (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c2o)) (wcel C (cdif con0 c1o))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . Here we show existence of Cantor normal forms. Assuming (by transfinite induction) that every number less than ` C ` has a normal form, we can use ~ oeeu to factor ` C ` into the form ` ( ( A ^o X ) .o Y ) +o Z ` where ` 0 < Y < A ` and ` Z < ( A ^o X ) ` (and a fortiori ` X < B ` ). Then since ` Z < ( A ^o X ) <_ ( A ^o X ) .o Y <_ C ` , ` Z ` has a normal form, and by appending the term ` ( A ^o X ) .o Y ` using ~ cantnfp1 we get a normal form for ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem cantnflem3 {x3 y1 z w t a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff w a b c d) (A2: class) (B2: class a b d) (C: class t) (P: class x3 y1 z w t a b c d) (S: class w a b d) (T: class x3 y1 z w a b d) (F: class t a b c d) (G: class a b d) (X: class c) (Y: class a b c d) (Z: class w a b c d):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 coe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (crn (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cint (crab c (wcel C (co A2 (cv c) coe)) con0))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cio d (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wceq (cv d) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 X coe) (cv a) comu) (cv b) coa) C)) (co A2 X coe)) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv P c2nd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G (co A2 B2 ccnf)) Z) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t B2 (cif (wceq (cv t) X) Y (cfv (cv t) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (crn (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cantnf . Complete the induction step of ~ cantnflem3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-May-2015.)
theorem cantnflem4 {x3 y1 z w a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff w a b c d) (A2: class) (B2: class a b d) (C: class) (P: class x3 y1 z w a b c d) (S: class w a b d) (T: class x3 y1 z w a b d) (X: class c) (Y: class a b c d) (Z: class w a b c d):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 coe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (crn (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cint (crab c (wcel C (co A2 (cv c) coe)) con0))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cio d (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wceq (cv d) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 X coe) (cv a) comu) (cv b) coa) C)) (co A2 X coe)) con0)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv P c2nd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (crn (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $;

--| The Cantor Normal Form theorem. The function ` ( A CNF B ) ` , which maps a finitely supported function from ` B ` to ` A ` to the sum ` ( ( A ^o f ( a 1 ) ) o. a 1 ) +o ` ` ( ( A ^o f ( a 2 ) ) o. a 2 ) +o ... ` over all indices ` a < B ` such that ` f ( a ) ` is nonzero, is an order isomorphism from the ordering ` T ` of finitely supported functions to the set ` ( A ^o B ) ` under the natural order. Setting ` A = _om ` and letting ` B ` be arbitrarily large, the surjectivity of this function implies that every ordinal has a Cantor normal form (and injectivity, together with coherence ~ cantnfres , implies that such a representation is unique). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem cantnf {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso S (co A2 B2 coe) T cep (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $;

--| The lexicographic order on a function space of ordinals gives a well-ordering with order type equal to the ordinal exponential. This provides an alternate definition of the ordinal exponential. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem oemapwe {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wwe S T) (wceq (cdm (coi S T)) (co A2 B2 coe))) $;

--| An alternate definition of ~ df-cnf which relies on ~ cantnf . (Note that although the use of ` S ` seems self-referential, one can use ~ cantnfdm to eliminate it.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.)
theorem cantnffval2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class) (S: class w) (T: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1))) (wral w (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) B2)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 ccnf) (ccnv (coi S T))) $;

--| Simplify the isomorphism of ~ cantnf to simple bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem cantnff1o (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o S (co A2 B2 coe) (co A2 B2 ccnf)) $;

--| Construct lexicographic order on a function space based on a reverse well-ordering of the indices and a well-ordering of the values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem wemapwe {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2: class) (B2: class z w) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class x3 y1 w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (U: class z w) (F: class) (G: class z w) (Z: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) S) (wral w (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv w) R) (wceq (cfv (cv w) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv w) (cv y1)))) A2)) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) Z cfsupp) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wceq F (coi A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi B2 S) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv c0 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe U T) $;

--| A bijection of the base sets induces a bijection on ordinal exponentials. (The assumption ` ( F `` (/) ) = (/) ` can be discharged using ~ fveqf1o .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem oef1o {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G: class) (H K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif con0 c1o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 F) c0) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt y1 (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c0 cfsupp) (co A2 B2 cmap)) (ccom F (ccom (cv y1) (ccnv G)))) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccom (co C D ccnf) K) (ccnv (co A2 B2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co A2 B2 coe) (co C D coe) H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnfcom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcomlem {x3 z f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z f2 k) (A2: class f2) (B2: class x3 z f2 k) (S: class x3 f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (I: class f2) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M: class z f2 k) (O: class x3 z f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O (co com (cfv I G) coe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv I H) O (cfv I T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (csuc I) H) (co (co com (cfv I G) coe) (cfv (cfv I G) F) comu) (cfv (csuc I) T)) $;

--| Any ordinal ` B ` is equinumerous to the leading term of its Cantor normal form. Here we show that bijection explicitly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom {x3 z f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z f2 k) (A2: class f2) (B2: class x3 z f2 k) (S: class x3 f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (I: class f2) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M: class z f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (csuc I) H) (co (co com (cfv I G) coe) (cfv (cfv I G) F) comu) (cfv (csuc I) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnfcom2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom2lem {x3 z f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class f2) (B2: class x3 z f2 k) (S: class x3 f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M W: class z f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (cuni (cdm G)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm G) (csuc (cuni (cdm G)))) $;

--| Any nonzero ordinal ` B ` is equinumerous to the leading term of its Cantor normal form. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom2 {x3 z f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class f2) (B2: class x3 z f2 k) (S: class x3 f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M W: class z f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (cuni (cdm G)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 (co (co com W coe) (cfv W F) comu) (cfv (cdm G) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnfcom3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 4-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom3lem {x3 z f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class f2) (B2: class x3 z f2 k) (S: class x3 f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M W: class z f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (cuni (cdm G)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss com B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cdif con0 c1o)) $;

--| Any infinite ordinal ` B ` is equinumerous to a power of ` _om ` . (We are being careful here to show explicit bijections rather than simple equinumerosity because we want a uniform construction for ~ cnfcom3c .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 4-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom3 {x3 z v u f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class v u f2) (B2: class x3 z v u f2 k) (S: class x3 v u f2) (T: class x3 f2 k) (F G: class) (H: class z k) (K: class x3 z f2 k) (M: class z v u f2 k) (N: class x3 z v u f2 k) (W: class z f2 k) (X Y: class x3 z v u f2 k):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co com A2 coe)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv B2 (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (cuni (cdm G)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss com B2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpo u v (cfv W F) (co com W coe) (co (co (cfv W F) (cv v) comu) (cv u) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpo u v (cfv W F) (co com W coe) (co (co (co com W coe) (cv u) comu) (cv v) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq N (ccom (ccom X (ccnv Y)) (cfv (cdm G) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 (co com W coe) N) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnfcom3c . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 4-Jul-2019.)
theorem cnfcom3clem {x3 z w v u f2 g2 k b: setvar} (A2: class f2) (S: class x3 w v u f2 g2 b) (T: class x3 w f2 g2 k b) (F G: class w g2 b) (H: class z w g2 k b) (K: class x3 z w f2 g2 k b) (L: class x3 z v u f2 k b) (M: class z w v u f2 g2 k b) (N: class x3 z w v u f2 g2 k b) (W: class z f2 g2 k b) (X Y: class x3 z w v u f2 g2 k b):
  $ wceq S (cdm (co com A2 ccnf)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv (cv b) (ccnv (co com A2 ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (co F c0 csupp) cep) $ >
  $ wceq H (cseqom (cmpo k z cvv cvv (co M (cv z) coa)) c0) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv K) c0) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co com (cfv (cv k) G) coe) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F) comu) $ >
  $ wceq K (cun (cmpt x3 M (co (cdm (cv f2)) (cv x3) coa)) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co M (cv x3) coa)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (cuni (cdm G)) G) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpo u v (cfv W F) (co com W coe) (co (co (cfv W F) (cv v) comu) (cv u) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpo u v (cfv W F) (co com W coe) (co (co (co com W coe) (cv u) comu) (cv v) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq N (ccom (ccom X (ccnv Y)) (cfv (cdm G) T)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt b (co com A2 coe) N) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex g2 (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wrex w (wf1o (cv b) (co com (cv w) coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv g2))) (cdif con0 c1o))) A2)) $;

--| Wrap the construction of ~ cnfcom3 into an existential quantifier. For any ` _om C_ b ` , there is a bijection from ` b ` to some power of ` _om ` . Furthermore, this bijection is _canonical_ , which means that we can find a single function ` g ` which will give such bijections for every ` b ` less than some arbitrarily large bound ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem cnfcom3c {w g2 b: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex g2 (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wrex w (wf1o (cv b) (co com (cv w) coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv g2))) (cdif con0 c1o))) A2)) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the transitive closure of a class.
term cttrcl (R: class): class;

--| Define the transitive closure of a class. This is the smallest relation containing ` R ` (or more precisely, the relation ` ( R |`` _V ) ` induced by ` R ` ) and having the transitive property. Definition from [Levy] p. 59, who denotes it as ` R * ` and calls it the "ancestral" of ` R ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
axiom df_ttrcl {x3 y1 f2 m n: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (cttrcl R) (copab x3 y1 (wrex n (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (csuc (cv n))) (wa (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) (cv y1))) (wral m (wbr (cfv (cv m) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv m)) (cv f2)) R) (cv n)))) (cdif com c1o))) $;

--| Equality theorem for transitive closure. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrcleq (R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cttrcl R) (cttrcl S)) $;

--| Bound variable hypothesis builder for transitive closure. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem nfttrcld {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (R: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cttrcl R)) $;

--| Bound variable hypothesis builder for transitive closure. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem nfttrcl {x3: setvar} (R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cttrcl R) $;

--| The transitive closure of a class is a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem relttrcl (R: class):
  $ wrel (cttrcl R) $;

--| Characterization of elements of the transitive closure of a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem brttrcl {f2 n a: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (cttrcl R)) (wrex n (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (csuc (cv n))) (wa (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) B2)) (wral a (wbr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv a)) (cv f2)) R) (cv n)))) (cdif com c1o)) $;

--| Characterization of elements of the transitive closure of a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Aug-2024.)
theorem brttrcl2 {f2 n a: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (cttrcl R)) (wrex n (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (csuc (csuc (cv n)))) (wa (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) A2) (wceq (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) B2)) (wral a (wbr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv a)) (cv f2)) R) (csuc (cv n))))) com) $;

--| If ` R ` is a relation, then it is a subclass of its transitive closure. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem ssttrcl (R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wss R (cttrcl R)) $;

--| The transitive closure of a class is transitive. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrcltr (R: class):
  $ wss (ccom (cttrcl R) (cttrcl R)) (cttrcl R) $;

--| The transitive closure of ` R ` restricted to ` _V ` is the same as the transitive closure of ` R ` itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclresv (R: class):
  $ wceq (cttrcl (cres R cvv)) (cttrcl R) $;

--| Composition law for the transitive closure of a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclco (R: class):
  $ wss (ccom (cttrcl R) R) (cttrcl R) $;

--| Composition law for the transitive closure of a relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Oct-2024.)
theorem cottrcl (R: class):
  $ wss (ccom R (cttrcl R)) (cttrcl R) $;

--| If ` R ` is a subclass of ` S ` and ` S ` is transitive, then the transitive closure of ` R ` is a subclass of ` S ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclss (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss R S) (wss (ccom S S) S)) (wss (cttrcl R) S) $;

--| The domain of a transitive closure is the same as the domain of the original class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem dmttrcl (R: class):
  $ wceq (cdm (cttrcl R)) (cdm R) $;

--| The range of a transitive closure is the same as the range of the original class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem rnttrcl (R: class):
  $ wceq (crn (cttrcl R)) (crn R) $;

--| If ` R ` is a set, then so is ` t++ R ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclexg (R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wcel (cttrcl R) cvv) $;

--| When ` R ` is a set and a relation, then its transitive closure can be defined by an intersection. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2024.)
theorem dfttrcl2 {z: setvar} (R: class) (V: class z):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wrel R)) (wceq (cttrcl R) (cint (cab z (wa (wss R (cv z)) (wss (ccom (cv z) (cv z)) (cv z)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttrclse . Show that all finite ordinal function values of ` F ` are subsets of ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclselem1 {w b: setvar} (A2 R: class) (F N: class w b) (X: class w):
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt b cvv (ciun w (cv b) (cpred A2 R (cv w)))) (cpred A2 R X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wss (cfv N F) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttrclse . Show that a ` suc N ` element long chain gives membership in the ` N ` -th predecessor class and vice-versa. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclselem2 {y1 w f2 a b: setvar} (A2 R: class) (F: class w f2 a b) (N: class w b) (X: class w a):
  $ wceq F (crdg (cmpt b cvv (ciun w (cv b) (cpred A2 R (cv w)))) (cpred A2 R X)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N com) (wse A2 R) (wcel X A2)) (wb (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (csuc (csuc N))) (wa (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (wceq (cfv (csuc N) (cv f2)) X)) (wral a (wbr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv a)) (cv f2)) (cres R A2)) (csuc N)))) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv N F))) $;

--| If ` R ` is set-like over ` A ` , then the transitive closure of the restriction of ` R ` to ` A ` is set-like over ` A ` . This theorem requires the axioms of infinity and replacement for its proof. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem ttrclse (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wse A2 R) (wse A2 (cttrcl (cres R A2))) $;

--| For any set ` A ` , show the properties of its transitive closure ` C ` . Similar to Theorem 9.1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 73 except that we show an explicit expression for the transitive closure rather than just its existence. See ~ tz9.1 for an abbreviated version showing existence. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem trcl {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class z) (C: class x3 y1 z) (F: class z):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cun (cv z) (cuni (cv z)))) A2) com) $ >
  $ wceq C (ciun y1 com (cfv (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ w3a (wss A2 C) (wtr C) (wal x3 (wi (wa (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wtr (cv x3))) (wss C (cv x3)))) $;

--| Every set has a transitive closure (the smallest transitive extension). Theorem 9.1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 73. See ~ trcl for an explicit expression for the transitive closure. Apparently open problems are whether this theorem can be proved without the Axiom of Infinity; if not, then whether it implies Infinity; and if not, what is the "property" that Infinity has that the other axioms don't have that is weaker than Infinity itself? (Added 22-Mar-2011) The following article seems to answer the first question, that it can't be proved without Infinity, in the affirmative: Mancini, Antonella and Zambella, Domenico (2001). "A note on recursive models of set theories." _Notre Dame Journal of Formal Logic_, 42(2):109-115. (Thanks to Scott Fenton.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2003.)
theorem tz9_1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wex x3 (w3a (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wtr (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wa (wss A2 (cv y1)) (wtr (cv y1))) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Alternate expression for the existence of transitive closures ~ tz9.1 : the intersection of all transitive sets containing ` A ` is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem tz9_1c {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cint (cab x3 (wa (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wtr (cv x3))))) cvv $;

--| The strong form of the Axiom of Regularity (no sethood requirement on ` A ` ), with the axiom itself present as an antecedent. See also ~ zfregs . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem epfrs {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 cep) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cin (cv x3) A2) c0) A2) $;

--| The strong form of the Axiom of Regularity, which does not require that ` A ` be a set. Axiom 6' of [TakeutiZaring] p. 21. See also ~ epfrs . (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2003.)
theorem zfregs {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq (cin (cv x3) A2) c0) A2) $;

--| Alternate strong form of the Axiom of Regularity. Not every element of a nonempty class contains some element of that class. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 24-Oct-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2013.)
theorem zfregs2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wn (wral x3 (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) A2)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the transitive closure function.
term ctc: class;

--| The transitive closure function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
axiom df_tc {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctc (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (cab y1 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wtr (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Value of the transitive closure function. (The fact that this intersection exists is a non-trivial fact that depends on ~ ax-inf ; see ~ tz9.1 .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcvalg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 ctc) (cint (cab x3 (wa (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wtr (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Defining property of the transitive closure function: it contains its argument as a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcid (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 (cfv A2 ctc)) $;

--| Defining property of the transitive closure function: it is transitive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tctr (A2: class):
  $ wtr (cfv A2 ctc) $;

--| Defining property of the transitive closure function: it is a subset of any transitive class containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcmin (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wtr B2)) (wss (cfv A2 ctc) B2)) $;

--| A variant of the definition of the transitive closure function, using instead the smallest transitive set containing ` A ` as a member, gives almost the same set, except that ` A ` itself must be added because it is not usually a member of ` ( TC `` A ) ` (and it is never a member if ` A ` is well-founded). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tc2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cun (cfv A2 ctc) (csn A2)) (cint (cab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wtr (cv x3))))) $;

--| The transitive closure of a singleton. Proof suggested by G&eacute;rard Lang. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.)
theorem tcsni (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csn A2) ctc) (cun (cfv A2 ctc) (csn A2)) $;

--| The transitive closure function inherits the subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wss B2 A2) (wss (cfv B2 ctc) (cfv A2 ctc)) $;

--| The transitive closure function converts the element relation to the subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wss (cfv B2 ctc) (cfv A2 ctc)) $;

--| The transitive closure function is idempotent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcidm (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ctc) ctc) (cfv A2 ctc) $;

--| The transitive closure of the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.)
theorem tc0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 ctc) c0 $;

--| The transitive closure is empty iff its argument is. Proof suggested by G&eacute;rard Lang. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.)
theorem tc00 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ctc) c0) (wceq A2 c0)) $;

--| Set (epsilon) induction. Theorem 5.22 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 21. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2003.)
theorem setind {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wceq A2 cvv) $;

--| Set (epsilon) induction, stated compactly. Given as a homework problem in 1992 by George Boolos (1940-1996). (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2003.)
theorem setind2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss (cpw A2) A2) (wceq A2 cvv) $;

--| Principle of set induction (or ` _E ` -induction). If a property passes from all elements of ` x ` to ` x ` itself, then it holds for all ` x ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Mar-2011.)
theorem setinds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wral y1 (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (cv x3)) ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| ` _E ` induction schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem setinds2f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| ` _E ` induction schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Mar-2011.)
theorem setinds2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral y1 ps (cv x3)) ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Every (possibly proper) subclass of a class ` A ` with a well-founded set-like relation ` R ` has a minimal element. This is a very strong generalization of ~ tz6.26 and ~ tz7.5 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 27-Nov-2024.)
theorem frmin {y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wss B2 A2) (wne B2 c0))) (wrex y1 (wceq (cpred B2 R (cv y1)) c0) B2) $;

--| A subclass of a well-founded class ` A ` with the property that whenever it contains all predecessors of an element of ` A ` it also contains that element, is equal to ` A ` . Compare ~ wfi and ~ tfi , which are special cases of this theorem that do not require the axiom of infinity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frind {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wss B2 A2) (wral y1 (wi (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) A2))) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Well-Founded Induction Schema. If a property passes from all elements less than ` y ` of a well-founded class ` A ` to ` y ` itself (induction hypothesis), then the property holds for all elements of ` A ` . Theorem 5.6(ii) of [Levy] p. 64. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frinsg {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wi (wral z (wsbc y1 ph (cv z)) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wral y1 ph A2) $;

--| Well-Founded Induction Schema. If a property passes from all elements less than ` y ` of a well-founded class ` A ` to ` y ` itself (induction hypothesis), then the property holds for all elements of ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frins {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class):
  $ wfr A2 R $ >
  $ wse A2 R $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wi (wral z (wsbc y1 ph (cv z)) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) ph $;

--| Well-Founded Induction schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem frins2f {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wi (wral z ps (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) ph) $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wral y1 ph A2) $;

--| Well-Founded Induction schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frins2 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps: wff z) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wi (wral z ps (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wral y1 ph A2) $;

--| Well-Founded Induction schema, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frins3 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (ps ch: wff z) (A2: class) (B2: class z) (R: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wi (wral z ps (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel B2 A2)) ch $;

--| Functions defined by well-founded recursion are identical up to relation, domain, and characteristic function. General version of ~ frr3 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem frr3g {y1: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn F A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv y1) (cres F (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2)) (wa (wfn G A2) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cres G (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) H)) A2))) (wceq F G) $;

--| Lemma for general well-founded recursion. Two acceptable functions are compatible. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem frrlem15 {x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1 v u f2 g2 h) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wex x3 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wral y1 (wss (cpred A2 R (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cres (cv f2) (cpred A2 R (cv y1))) G)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wcel (cv g2) B2) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv u) (cv g2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv v) (cv h))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) $;

--| Lemma for general well-founded recursion. Establish a subset relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.) Revised notion of transitive closure. (Revised by Scott Fenton, 1-Dec-2024.)
theorem frrlem16 {w: setvar} (z: setvar) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wral w (wss (cpred A2 R (cv w)) (cpred A2 (cttrcl (cres R A2)) (cv z))) (cpred A2 (cttrcl (cres R A2)) (cv z))) $;

--| Law of general well-founded recursion, part one. This theorem and the following two drop the partial order requirement from ~ fpr1 , ~ fpr2 , and ~ fpr3 , which requires using the axiom of infinity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem frr1 (A2 R F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wfn F A2) $;

--| Law of general well-founded recursion, part two. Now we establish the value of ` F ` within ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem frr2 (A2 R F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (co X (cres F (cpred A2 R X)) G)) $;

--| Law of general well-founded recursion, part three. Finally, we show that ` F ` is unique. We do this by showing that any function ` H ` with the same properties we proved of ` F ` in ~ frr1 and ~ frr2 is identical to ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Sep-2023.)
theorem frr3 {z: setvar} (A2 R F G H: class):
  $ wceq F (cfrecs A2 R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wfr A2 R) (wse A2 R)) (wa (wfn H A2) (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) H) (co (cv z) (cres H (cpred A2 R (cv z))) G)) A2))) (wceq F H) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the cumulative hierarchy of sets function.
term cr1: class;

--| Extend class definition to include rank function.
term crnk: class;

--| Define the cumulative hierarchy of sets function, using Takeuti and Zaring's notation ( ` R1 ` ). It is also called the Von Neumann hierarchy of sets function. Starting with the empty set, this function builds up layers of sets where the next layer is the power set of the previous layer (and the union of all previous layers when the argument is a limit ordinal). Using the axiom of regularity, we can show that every set belongs to one of the layers of this hierarchy (see ~ tz9.13 ). Our definition expresses Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76 in a closed form, from which we derive the recursive definition as Theorems ~ r10 , ~ r1suc , and ~ r1lim . Theorem ~ r1val1 shows a recursive definition that works for all values, and Theorems ~ r1val2 and ~ r1val3 show the value expressed in terms of rank. Other notations for this function are _R_ with the argument as a subscript (Equation 3.1 of [BellMachover] p. 477), ` _V ` with a subscript (Definition of [Enderton] p. 202), _M_ with a subscript (Definition 15.19 of [Monk1] p. 113), the capital Greek letter psi (Definition of [Mendelson] p. 281), and bold-face R (Definition 2.1 of [Kunen] p. 95). The values of this function, that is, the layers of this hierarchy, are called "stages" to emphasize the process-like nature of this function. Some sources also use the term "level". (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2003.)
axiom df_r1 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cr1 (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (cpw (cv x3))) c0) $;

--| Define the rank function. The rank of a set is the smallest ordinal such that the stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets at that ordinal includes that set, or equivalently the smallest ordinal such that the stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets at the successor of that ordinal contains that set. This definition uses that second characterization, while the first is proven in ~ rankval2 . See ~ rankval , ~ rankval2 , ~ rankval3 , or ~ rankval4 for its value. The rank is therefore a kind of "inverse" of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function, in a sense made precise in ~ rankid and ~ rankr1a . Based on Definition 9.14 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2003.)
axiom df_rank {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq crnk (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) cr1)) con0))) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of sets function is a function on a limit ordinal. (This weak form of ~ r1fnon avoids ~ ax-rep .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ r1fun or ~ r1dmlim instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem r1funlimOLD:
  $ wa (wfun cr1) (wlim (cdm cr1)) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of sets function is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) Extract this statement from its conjunction with ~ r1dmlim . (Revised by BJ, 27-Sep-2026.)
theorem r1fun:
  $ wfun cr1 $;

--| The domain of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function is a limit ordinal. This weak form of ~ r1fnon avoids ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) Extract this statement from its conjunction with ~ r1fun . (Revised by BJ, 27-Sep-2026.)
theorem r1dmlim:
  $ wlim (cdm cr1) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of sets function is a function on the class of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem r1fnon:
  $ wfn cr1 con0 $;

--| The range of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function. This is the class of all stages of the cumulative hierarchy of sets, not the class of all sets in some stage of the cumulative hierarchy (that is ` U. ran R1 ` ). This statement does not require the axiom of replacement by avoiding using ~ r1fnon . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Sep-2026.)
theorem rnr1:
  $ wceq (crn cr1) (cima cr1 con0) $;

--| Value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at ` (/) ` . Part of Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem r10:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cr1) c0 $;

--| Value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at a successor ordinal. Part of Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1sucg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) cr1) (cpw (cfv A2 cr1))) $;

--| Value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at a successor ordinal. Use ~ r1sucg (which does not require ~ ax-rep ) when sufficient. Part of Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem r1suc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) cr1) (cpw (cfv A2 cr1))) $;

--| Value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at a limit ordinal. Part of Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1limg {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wlim A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cr1) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1))) $;

--| Value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at a limit ordinal. Use ~ r1limg (which does not require ~ ax-rep ) when sufficient. Part of Definition 9.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 76. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1lim {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wlim A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cr1) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1))) $;

--| The first ` _om ` stages of the cumulative hierarchy of sets are all finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2013.)
theorem r1fin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) cfn) $;

--| Each stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets is strictly larger than any previous stage. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2013.)
theorem r1sdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 A2)) (wbr (cfv B2 cr1) (cfv A2 cr1) csdm) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of sets is a one-to-one function. Use ~ r1fun or ~ r1fnon (which require fewer axioms) when sufficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2013.)
theorem r111:
  $ wf1 con0 cvv cr1 $;

--| Each stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets is transitive. Lemma 7T of [Enderton] p. 202. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1tr (A2: class):
  $ wtr (cfv A2 cr1) $;

--| Each stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets includes its union, that is, is transitive. JFM CLASSES1 th. 40. (Contributed by FL, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem r1tr2 (A2: class):
  $ wss (cuni (cfv A2 cr1)) (cfv A2 cr1) $;

--| Ordering relation for the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Part of Proposition 9.10(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 77. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2003.)
theorem r1ordg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm cr1)) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| Ordering relation for the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Part of Theorem 3.3(i) of [BellMachover] p. 478. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.)
theorem r1ord3g (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wcel B2 (cdm cr1))) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| Ordering relation for the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Part of Proposition 9.10(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 77. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1ord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| Ordering relation for the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Part of Proposition 9.10(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 77. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.)
theorem r1ord2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| Ordering relation for the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Part of Theorem 3.3(i) of [BellMachover] p. 478. (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.)
theorem r1ord3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| A stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets is included in its successor stage. JFM CLASSES1 Th. 39. (Contributed by FL, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem r1sssuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wss (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv (csuc A2) cr1)) $;

--| Each stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets is closed under subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1pwss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wss (cpw A2) (cfv B2 cr1)) $;

--| Each stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets is closed under subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1sscl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wss C A2)) (wcel C (cfv B2 cr1)) $;

--| The value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function expressed recursively. Theorem 7Q of [Enderton] p. 202. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1val1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wceq (cfv A2 cr1) (ciun x3 A2 (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cr1)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tz9.12 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem tz9_12lem1 {z v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z v):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cvv (cint (crab v (wcel (cv z) (cfv (cv v) cr1)) con0))) $ >
  $ wss (cima F A2) con0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ tz9.12 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.)
theorem tz9_12lem2 {z v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z v):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cvv (cint (crab v (wcel (cv z) (cfv (cv v) cr1)) con0))) $ >
  $ wcel (csuc (cuni (cima F A2))) con0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ tz9.12 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem tz9_12lem3 {x3 y1 z v: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z v):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cvv (cint (crab v (wcel (cv z) (cfv (cv v) cr1)) con0))) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) cr1)) con0) A2) (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (csuc (cuni (cima F A2)))) cr1)) $;

--| A set is well-founded if all of its elements are well-founded. Proposition 9.12 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 78. The main proof consists of ~ tz9.12lem1 through ~ tz9.12lem3 . (Contributed by NM, 22-Sep-2003.)
theorem tz9_12 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) cr1)) con0) A2) (wrex y1 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv y1) cr1)) con0) $;

--| Every set is well-founded, assuming the Axiom of Regularity. In other words, every set belongs to a stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets. Proposition 9.13 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 78. (Contributed by NM, 23-Sep-2003.)
theorem tz9_13 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) con0 $;

--| Every set is well-founded, assuming the Axiom of Regularity. Proposition 9.13 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 78. This variant of ~ tz9.13 expresses the class existence requirement as an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.)
theorem tz9_13g {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) con0) $;

--| Two ways of expressing that a set is well-founded. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem rankwflemb {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ rankwflemb as of 29-Sep-2026. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rankwflembOLD {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the ` rank ` function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2013.)
theorem rankf:
  $ wf (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) con0 crnk $;

--| The rank of a set is an ordinal number. Proposition 9.15(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2013.)
theorem rankon (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 crnk) con0 $;

--| Any element of (any stage of) the cumulative hierarchy of sets is well-founded (recall that ` U. ( R1 " On ) ` is the class of well-founded sets). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1elwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| Value of the rank function. Definition 9.14 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79 (proved as a theorem from our definition). This variant of ~ rankval does not use the axiom of regularity, and so requires the assumption that ` A ` is in the range of ` R1 ` . (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem rankvalb {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0))) $;

--| One direction of ~ rankr1a . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1ai (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) B2) $;

--| Value of the rank function at a non-well-founded set. (The antecedent is always false under Foundation, by ~ unir1 , unless ` A ` is a proper class.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem rankvaln (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) c0) $;

--| Identity law for the rank function. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem rankidb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cfv A2 crnk)) cr1)) $;

--| A rank is a member of the cumulative hierarchy of sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankdmr1 (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cdm cr1) $;

--| A version of ~ rankr1a that is suitable without assuming Regularity or Replacement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1ag (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cdm cr1))) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) B2)) $;

--| A relationship between rank and ` R1 ` . See ~ rankr1ag for the membership version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1bg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cdm cr1))) (wb (wss A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) B2)) $;

--| Any set is a subset of the hierarchy of its rank. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1rankidb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wss A2 (cfv (cfv A2 crnk) cr1)) $;

--| The range of the ` R1 ` function is transitive. Lemma 2.10 of [Kunen] p. 97. One direction of ~ r1elss that doesn't need ` A ` to be a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1elssi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wss A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| The range of the ` R1 ` function is transitive. Lemma 2.10 of [Kunen] p. 97. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1elss (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wss A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A power set is well-founded iff the base set is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem pwwf (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (cpw A2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A subset of a well-founded set is well-founded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem sswf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A singleton is well-founded if its element is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem snwf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (csn A2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A binary union is well-founded iff its elements are. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem unwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| An unordered pair is well-founded if its elements are. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem prwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| An ordered pair is well-founded if its elements are. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem opwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wcel (cop A2 B2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of sets covers the universe. Proposition 4.45 (b) to (a) of [Mendelson] p. 281. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem unir1:
  $ wceq (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) cvv $;

--| Every set belongs to some stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets, expressed using an indexed union. Lemma 9.3 of [Jech] p. 71. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem jech9_3 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (ciun x3 con0 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) cvv $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ jech9.3 as of 29-Sep-2026. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem jech9_3OLD {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (ciun x3 con0 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) cvv $;

--| Every set is well-founded, assuming the axiom of regularity. Proposition 9.13 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 78. This variant of ~ tz9.13g is useful in proofs of theorems about the rank function. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.)
theorem rankwflem {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0) $;

--| Value of the rank function. Definition 9.14 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79 (proved as a theorem from our definition). (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem rankval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0)) $;

--| Value of the rank function. Definition 9.14 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79 (proved as a theorem from our definition). This variant of ~ rankval expresses the class existence requirement as an antecedent instead of a hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2003.)
theorem rankvalg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cr1)) con0))) $;

--| Value of an alternate definition of the rank function. Definition of [BellMachover] p. 478. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2003.)
theorem rankval2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wss A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) con0))) $;

--| A union is well-founded iff the base set is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem uniwf (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (cuni A2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rankr1c . (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1clem (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cdm cr1))) (wb (wn (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1))) (wss B2 (cfv A2 crnk))) $;

--| A relationship between the rank function and the cumulative hierarchy of sets function ` R1 ` . Proposition 9.15(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1c (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wb (wceq B2 (cfv A2 crnk)) (wa (wn (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1))) (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc B2) cr1)))) $;

--| A relationship between the rank function and the cumulative hierarchy of sets function ` R1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankidn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wn (wcel A2 (cfv (cfv A2 crnk) cr1))) $;

--| The rank of a power set. Part of Exercise 30 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jun-2013.)
theorem rankpwi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv (cpw A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk))) $;

--| The membership relation is inherited by the rank function. Proposition 9.16 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankelb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| A well-founded set is not a member of itself. This proof does not require the axiom of regularity, unlike ~ elirr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wfelirr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wn (wcel A2 A2)) $;

--| Value of an alternate definition of the rank function. Definition of [BellMachover] p. 478. This variant of ~ rankval2 does not use Regularity, and so requires the assumption that ` A ` is in the range of ` R1 ` . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.)
theorem rankval2b {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wss A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) con0))) $;

--| The value of the rank function expressed recursively: the rank of a set is the smallest ordinal number containing the ranks of all members of the set. Proposition 9.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankval3b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crnk) (cv x3)) A2) con0))) $;

--| The rank of a singleton. Theorem 15.17(v) of [Monk1] p. 112. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem ranksnb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rankonid . (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem rankonidlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) A2)) $;

--| The rank of an ordinal number is itself. Proposition 9.18 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankonid (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) A2) $;

--| The ordinals are all well-founded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem onwf:
  $ wss con0 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $;

--| Each stage in the cumulative hierarchy is well-founded. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.)
theorem r1wf (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cr1) (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $;

--| An element of a well-founded set is well-founded. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2025.)
theorem elwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| Initial segments of the ordinals are contained in initial segments of the cumulative hierarchy. (Contributed by FL, 20-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem onssr1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wss A2 (cfv A2 cr1)) $;

--| A relationship between the rank function and the cumulative hierarchy of sets function ` R1 ` . Proposition 9.15(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1g (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wceq B2 (cfv A2 crnk)) (wa (wn (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1))) (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc B2) cr1)))) $;

--| Identity law for the rank function. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankid (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel A2 (cfv (csuc (cfv A2 crnk)) cr1) $;

--| A relationship between the rank function and the cumulative hierarchy of sets function ` R1 ` . Proposition 9.15(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq B2 (cfv A2 crnk)) (wa (wn (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1))) (wcel A2 (cfv (csuc B2) cr1))) $;

--| A relationship between an ordinal number less than or equal to a rank, and the cumulative hierarchy of sets ` R1 ` . Proposition 9.15(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem ssrankr1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wss B2 (cfv A2 crnk)) (wn (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)))) $;

--| A relationship between rank and ` R1 ` , clearly equivalent to ~ ssrankr1 and friends through trichotomy, but in Raph's opinion considerably more intuitive. See ~ rankr1b for the subset version. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 29-May-2004.)
theorem rankr1a (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) B2)) $;

--| The value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function expressed in terms of rank. Definition 15.19 of [Monk1] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.)
theorem r1val2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 cr1) (cab x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) crnk) A2))) $;

--| The value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function expressed in terms of rank. Theorem 15.18 of [Monk1] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1val3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 cr1) (ciun x3 A2 (cpw (cab y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crnk) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| The membership relation is inherited by the rank function. Proposition 9.16 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)) $;

--| The membership relation is inherited by the rank function. Closed form of ~ rankel . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.)
theorem rankelg (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel A2 B2)) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)) $;

--| The value of the rank function expressed recursively: the rank of a set is the smallest ordinal number containing the ranks of all members of the set. Proposition 9.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankval3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cint (crab x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crnk) (cv x3)) A2) con0)) $;

--| Any class whose elements have bounded rank is a set. Proposition 9.19 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.)
theorem bndrank {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) crnk) (cv x3)) A2) con0) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The elements of a proper class have unbounded rank. Exercise 2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.)
theorem unbndrank {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) A2) con0) $;

--| The rank of the powerset is the successor of the rank. Part of Exercise 30 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by NM, 22-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankpw (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cpw A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk)) $;

--| The rank of the powerset is the successor of the rank. Closed form of ~ rankpw . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.)
theorem rankpwg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cpw A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk))) $;

--| The rank of a set belongs to a limit ordinal iff the rank of its power set does. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem ranklim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wlim B2) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) B2) (wcel (cfv (cpw A2) crnk) B2)) $;

--| A set is in a given stage of the cumulative hierarchy of sets if and only if its powerset is in the successor stage. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 29-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1pw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cpw A2) (cfv (csuc B2) cr1))) $;

--| Alternate shorter proof of ~ r1pw based on the additional axioms ~ ax-reg and ~ ax-inf2 . (Contributed by Raph Levien, 29-May-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem r1pwALT (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cpw A2) (cfv (csuc B2) cr1))) $;

--| The cumulative hierarchy of a limit ordinal is closed under power set. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 29-May-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1pwcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wlim B2) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wcel (cpw A2) (cfv B2 cr1))) $;

--| The subset relation is inherited by the rank function. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankssb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| The subset relation is inherited by the rank function. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)) $;

--| The rank of the union of two sets. Theorem 15.17(iii) of [Monk1] p. 112. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankunb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| The rank of an unordered pair. Part of Exercise 30 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem rankprb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq (cfv (cpr A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)))) $;

--| The rank of an ordered pair. Part of Exercise 4 of [Kunen] p. 107. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem rankopb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (csuc (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))))) $;

--| The value of the rank function expressed recursively: the rank of a set is the smallest ordinal number containing the ranks of all members of the set. Proposition 9.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 79. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem rankuni2b {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv (cuni A2) crnk) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) crnk))) $;

--| The rank of a singleton. Theorem 15.17(v) of [Monk1] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem ranksn (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csn A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk)) $;

--| The rank of a union. Part of Theorem 15.17(iv) of [Monk1] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankuni2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cuni A2) crnk) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) crnk)) $;

--| The rank of the union of two sets. Theorem 15.17(iii) of [Monk1] p. 112. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)) $;

--| The rank of an unordered pair. Part of Exercise 30 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cpr A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| The rank of an ordered pair. Part of Exercise 4 of [Kunen] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankop (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (csuc (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk)))) $;

--| The rank of the union of two sets. Closed form of ~ rankun . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.)
theorem rankung (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| The rank of a singleton. Closed form of ~ ranksn . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.)
theorem ranksng (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk))) $;

--| Any set is a subset of the hierarchy of its rank. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem r1rankid (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 (cfv (cfv A2 crnk) cr1)) $;

--| A set is empty iff its rank is empty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankeq0b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wb (wceq A2 c0) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) c0)) $;

--| A set is empty iff its rank is empty. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankeq0 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 c0) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) c0) $;

--| The rank of the hierarchy of an ordinal number is itself. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1id (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cr1)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cr1) crnk) A2) $;

--| The rank of a union. Part of Exercise 4 of [Kunen] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankuni (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cuni A2) crnk) (cuni (cfv A2 crnk)) $;

--| The rank of a set is the supremum of the successors of the ranks of its members. Exercise 9.1 of [Jech] p. 72. Also a special case of Theorem 7V(b) of [Enderton] p. 204. This variant of ~ rankval4 does not use Regularity, and so requires the assumption that ` A ` is in the range of ` R1 ` . (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.)
theorem rankval4b {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (ciun x3 A2 (csuc (cfv (cv x3) crnk)))) $;

--| A relationship between rank and ` R1 ` . See ~ rankr1a for the membership version. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankr1b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wss A2 (cfv B2 cr1)) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) B2)) $;

--| The rank of a successor. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem ranksuc (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csuc A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv A2 crnk)) $;

--| Upper bound of the rank of a union. Part of Exercise 30 of [Enderton] p. 207. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2003.)
theorem rankuniss (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wss (cfv (cuni A2) crnk) (cfv A2 crnk) $;

--| The rank of a set is the supremum of the successors of the ranks of its members. Exercise 9.1 of [Jech] p. 72. Also a special case of Theorem 7V(b) of [Enderton] p. 204. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2003.)
theorem rankval4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (ciun x3 A2 (csuc (cfv (cv x3) crnk))) $;

--| The rank of a set is bounded by a bound for the successor of its members. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankbnd {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wss (csuc (cfv (cv x3) crnk)) B2) A2) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) B2) $;

--| The rank of a set is bounded by the successor of a bound for its members. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankbnd2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wral x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) B2) A2) (wss (cfv A2 crnk) (csuc B2))) $;

--| A relationship that can be used for computation of rank. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankc1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cuni A2) crnk)) A2) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv (cuni A2) crnk)) $;

--| A relationship that can be used for computation of rank. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankc2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cuni A2) crnk)) A2) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (csuc (cfv (cuni A2) crnk))) $;

--| Rank membership is inherited by union. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankelun (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv C crnk)) (wcel (cfv B2 crnk) (cfv D crnk))) (wcel (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (cfv (cun C D) crnk)) $;

--| Rank membership is inherited by unordered pairs. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem rankelpr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv C crnk)) (wcel (cfv B2 crnk) (cfv D crnk))) (wcel (cfv (cpr A2 B2) crnk) (cfv (cpr C D) crnk)) $;

--| Rank membership is inherited by ordered pairs. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankelop (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv C crnk)) (wcel (cfv B2 crnk) (cfv D crnk))) (wcel (cfv (cop A2 B2) crnk) (cfv (cop C D) crnk)) $;

--| A lower bound on the rank of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxpl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wne (cxp A2 B2) c0) (wss (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk)) $;

--| An upper bound on the rank of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxpu (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wss (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (csuc (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk))) $;

--| An upper bound on the rank of a function. (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 5-Aug-2018.)
theorem rankfu (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wss (cfv F crnk) (csuc (csuc (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)))) $;

--| An upper bound on the rank of set exponentiation. (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 5-Aug-2018.)
theorem rankmapu (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wss (cfv (co A2 B2 cmap) crnk) (csuc (csuc (csuc (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)))) $;

--| The rank of a Cartesian product when the rank of the union of its arguments is a limit ordinal. Part of Exercise 4 of [Kunen] p. 107. See ~ rankxpsuc for the successor case. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxplim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wlim (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)) (wne (cxp A2 B2) c0)) (wceq (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk) (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)) $;

--| If the rank of a Cartesian product is a limit ordinal, so is the rank of the union of its arguments. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxplim2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wlim (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk)) (wlim (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)) $;

--| The rank of a Cartesian product is a limit ordinal iff its union is. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxplim3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wlim (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk)) (wlim (cuni (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk))) $;

--| The rank of a Cartesian product when the rank of the union of its arguments is a successor ordinal. Part of Exercise 4 of [Kunen] p. 107. See ~ rankxplim for the limit ordinal case. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2006.)
theorem rankxpsuc (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk) (csuc C)) (wne (cxp A2 B2) c0)) (wceq (cfv (cxp A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (csuc (cfv (cun A2 B2) crnk)))) $;

--| The transitive closure function is well-founded if its argument is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcwf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (cfv A2 ctc) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| This theorem expresses two different facts from the two subset implications in this equality. In the forward direction, it says that the transitive closure has members of every rank below ` A ` . Stated another way, to construct a set at a given rank, you have to climb the entire hierarchy of ordinals below ` ( rank `` A ) ` , constructing at least one set at each level in order to move up the ranks. In the reverse direction, it says that every member of ` ( TC `` A ) ` has a rank below the rank of ` A ` , since intuitively it contains only the members of ` A ` and the members of those and so on, but nothing "bigger" than ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem tcrank (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) (cima crnk (cfv A2 ctc))) $;

--| If all elements of a finite well-founded set have a rank less than a limit ordinal, then the rank of that set is also less than the limit ordinal. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.)
theorem rankfilimbi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wa (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) crnk) B2) A2) (wlim B2))) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) B2) $;

--| If all elements of a finite set appear in the cumulative hierarchy prior to a limit ordinal, then that set also appears in the cumulative hierarchy prior to the limit ordinal. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.)
theorem r1filimi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cuni (cima cr1 B2))) A2) (wlim B2)) (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 B2))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of hereditarily finite sets.
term chf: class;

--| Define the class of sets belonging to the finite stages of the cumulative hierarchy of sets. This is the class of sets of finite rank by ~ elhf2 . They are called the hereditarily finite sets since they are the finite sets whose members are hereditarily finite, as proved in ~ elhf3 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jul-2015.)
axiom df_hf:
  $ wceq chf (cuni (cima cr1 com)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the class of hereditarily finite sets as the value of the cumulative hierarchy of sets function at ` _om ` . This characterization is simpler but requires the axiom of infinity to hold. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 25-Jan-2026.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.)
theorem dfhf2:
  $ wceq chf (cfv com cr1) $;

--| Membership in the hereditarily finite sets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jul-2015.) Reduce axiom usage and shorten proof. (Revised by BJ, 27-Sep-2026.)
theorem elhf {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) com) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elhf as of 27-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elhfOLD {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv x3) cr1)) com) $;

--| Hereditarily finite sets are finite sets. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2025.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 8-Sep-2026.)
theorem hffi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| Alternate form of membership in the hereditarily finite sets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jul-2015.)
theorem elhf2 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) com) $;

--| Hereditarily finiteness via rank. Closed form of ~ elhf2 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.)
theorem elhf2g (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) com)) $;

--| A set is hereditarily finite iff it is finite and all of its elements are hereditarily finite. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 19-Jan-2026.) Use ` HF ` . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem elhf4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (cv x3) chf) A2)) $;

--| A set is hereditarily finite if and only if it is finite and all its members are hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 8-Sep-2026.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem elhf3 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 chf)) $;

--| Any member of a hereditarily finite set is itself a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfelhf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel A2 chf) $;

--| Any subset of a hereditarily finite set is itself a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfsshf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel A2 chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elhf3 as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfelhfOLD (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel A2 chf) $;

--| The empty set is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem _0hf:
  $ wcel c0 chf $;

--| The union of two hereditarily finite sets is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hfun as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfunOLD (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) chf) $;

--| The singleton of a hereditarily finite set is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfsn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (csn A2) chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hfsn as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfsnOLD (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (csn A2) chf) $;

--| Adjoining one hereditarily finite set to a hereditarily finite set results in a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2015.)
theorem hfadj (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel (cun A2 (csn B2)) chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ elhf3 as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 8-Sep-2026.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elhf3OLD (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 chf) (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 chf)) $;

--| The union of a hereditarily finite set is hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfuni (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cuni A2) chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hfuni as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfuniOLD (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cuni A2) chf) $;

--| The power class of a hereditarily finite set is hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-reg , ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 17-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfpw (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cpw A2) chf) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hfpw as of 17-Sep-2026. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfpwOLD (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cpw A2) chf) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Scott's trick operation.
term cscott (A2: class): class;

--| Define an operation for Scott's trick, expressed as in Equation 9.3 of [Jech] p. 72. Scott's trick collects all sets that have a certain property and are of the smallest possible rank. The resulting collection is guaranteed to be a set (see ~ scottex ). Under this definition, the property in question is represented by membership in a class. See ~ scottab for the case where a wff (meta)variable is used to represent the property. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
axiom df_scott {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cscott A2) (crab x3 (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) A2) A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for the Scott operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
theorem scotteqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cscott A2) (cscott B2)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ scotteqd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
theorem scotteq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cscott A2) (cscott B2)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the Scott operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem nfscott {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cscott A2) $;

--| Scott's trick produces a set. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 18-Jul-2026.)
theorem scottex (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cscott A2) cvv $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ scottex as of 18-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scottexOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel (crab x3 (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) A2) A2) cvv $;

--| Scott's trick produces a subset of the input class. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottss (A2: class):
  $ wss (cscott A2) A2 $;

--| Applying Scott's trick to the empty set leaves it unchanged. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 3-Jul-2026.)
theorem scott0:
  $ wceq (cscott c0) c0 $;

--| Applying Scott's trick yields the empty set iff it was applied to the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 19-Jul-2026.)
theorem scott0b (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq A2 c0) (wceq (cscott A2) c0) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ scott0b as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scott0OLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq A2 c0) (wceq (crab x3 (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) A2) A2) c0) $;

--| Value of the Scott operation at a class abstraction. Variant of ~ scottab with a nonfreeness hypothesis instead of a disjoint variable condition. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottabf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (cscott (cab x3 ph)) (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi ps (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $;

--| Value of the Scott operation at a class abstraction. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (cscott (cab x3 ph)) (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi ps (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $;

--| Value of the Scott operation at a class abstraction. Variant of ~ scottab using explicit substitution. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottabes {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (cscott (cab x3 ph)) (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $;

--| An element of the output of the Scott operation applied to a class abstraction satisfies the class abstraction's predicate. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-Aug-2023.)
theorem elscottab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) (cscott (cab x3 ph))) ps $;

--| Obsolete theorem as of 27-Jul-2026. Use ~ scottex with a class abstraction instead. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scottexsOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wcel (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) cvv $;

--| Theorem scheme version of ~ scott0b . The collection of all ` x ` of minimum rank such that ` ph ( x ) ` is true, is not empty iff there is an ` x ` such that ` ph ( x ) ` holds. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 22-Jul-2026.)
theorem scott0bs {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wne (cscott (cab x3 ph)) c0) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ scott0bs as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scott0bsOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wne (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) c0) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ scottex as of 18-Jul-2026. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scottex2OLD (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cscott A2) cvv $;

--| The Scott operation sends inhabited classes to inhabited sets. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem scotteld {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cscott A2))) $;

--| Property of a Scott's trick set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottelrankd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cscott A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cscott A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 crnk) (cfv C crnk)) $;

--| Rank of a nonempty Scott's trick set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem scottrankd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cscott A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cscott A2) crnk) (csuc (cfv B2 crnk))) $;

--| Lemma for the Collection Principle ~ cp . (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 19-Jul-2026.)
theorem cplem1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq C (ciun x3 A2 (cscott B2)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wi (wne B2 c0) (wne (cin B2 C) c0)) A2 $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ cplem1 as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cplem1OLD {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq C (crab y1 (wral z (wss (cfv (cv y1) crnk) (cfv (cv z) crnk)) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq D (ciun x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wi (wne B2 c0) (wne (cin B2 D) c0)) A2 $;

--| Lemma for the Collection Principle ~ cp . (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 19-Jul-2026.)
theorem cplem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wex y1 (wral x3 (wi (wne B2 c0) (wne (cin B2 (cv y1)) c0)) A2) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ cplem2 as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cplem2OLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wex y1 (wral x3 (wi (wne B2 c0) (wne (cin B2 (cv y1)) c0)) A2) $;

--| Collection Principle. This remarkable theorem scheme is in effect a very strong generalization of the Axiom of Replacement. The proof makes use of Scott's trick ~ df-scott to collapse a proper class into a set of minimum rank. The wff ` ph ` can be thought of as ` ph ( x , y ) ` . Scheme "Collection Principle" of [Jech] p. 72. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2003.)
theorem cp {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wex w (wral x3 (wi (wex y1 ph) (wrex y1 ph (cv w))) (cv z)) $;

--| A very strong generalization of the Axiom of Replacement (compare ~ zfrep6 ), derived from the Collection Principle ~ cp . Its strength lies in the rather profound fact that ` ph ( x , y ) ` does not have to be a "function-like" wff, as it does in the standard Axiom of Replacement. This theorem is sometimes called the Boundedness Axiom. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2004.)
theorem bnd {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv z)) (wex w (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv w)) (cv z))) $;

--| A variant of the Boundedness Axiom ~ bnd that picks a subset ` z ` out of a possibly proper class ` B ` in which a property is true. (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2004.)
theorem bnd2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wex z (wa (wss (cv z) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv z)) A2))) $;

--| The collection of all sets equinumerous to a set ` A ` and having the least possible rank is a set. This is the part of the justification of the definition of kard of [Enderton] p. 222. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2003.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 19-Jul-2026.)
theorem kardex {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wcel (cscott (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen))) cvv $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ kardex as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem kardexOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cab x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) A2 cen) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) cvv $;

--| If we allow the Axiom of Regularity, we can avoid the Axiom of Choice by defining the cardinal number of a set as the set of all sets equinumerous to it and having the least possible rank. This theorem proves the equinumerosity relationship for this definition (compare ~ carden ). The hypotheses correspond to the definition of kard of [Enderton] p. 222 (which we don't define separately since currently we do not use it elsewhere). This theorem along with ~ kardex justify the definition of kard. The restriction to the least rank prevents the proper class that would result from ` { x | x ~~ A } ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2003.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2022.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 19-Jul-2026.)
theorem karden {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq C (cscott (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cscott (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) B2 cen))) $ >
  $ wb (wceq C D) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ karden as of 19-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2003.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem kardenOLD {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) A2 cen) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cab x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) B2 cen) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) B2 cen) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $ >
  $ wb (wceq C D) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| Lemma for defining an emulation of Hilbert's epsilon. Hilbert's epsilon is described at ~ http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epsilon-calculus/ . This theorem is equivalent to Hilbert's "transfinite axiom", described on that page, with the additional ` R We A ` antecedent. The element ` B ` is the epsilon that the theorem emulates. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.)
theorem htalem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crio x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wwe A2 R) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel B2 A2) $;

--| A ZFC emulation of Hilbert's transfinite axiom. The set ` B ` has the properties of Hilbert's epsilon, except that it also depends on a well-ordering ` R ` . This theorem arose from discussions with Raph Levien on 5-Mar-2004 about translating the HOL proof language, which uses Hilbert's epsilon. See ~ https://us.metamath.org/downloads/choice.txt (copy of obsolete link ~ http://ghilbert.org/choice.txt) and ~ https://us.metamath.org/downloads/megillaward2005he.pdf . Hilbert's epsilon is described at ~ http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epsilon-calculus/ . This theorem differs from Hilbert's transfinite axiom described on that page in that it requires ` R We A ` as an antecedent. Class ` A ` collects the sets of the least rank for which ` ph ( x ) ` is true. Class ` B ` , which emulates Hilbert's epsilon, is the minimum element in a well-ordering ` R ` on ` A ` . If a well-ordering ` R ` on ` A ` can be expressed in a closed form, as might be the case if we are working with say natural numbers, we can eliminate the antecedent with modus ponens, giving us the exact equivalent of Hilbert's transfinite axiom. Otherwise, we replace ` R ` with a dummy setvar variable, say ` w ` , and attach ` w We A ` as an antecedent in each step of the ZFC version of the HOL proof until the epsilon is eliminated. At that point, ` B ` (which will have ` w ` as a free variable) will no longer be present, and we can eliminate ` w We A ` by applying ~ exlimiv and ~ weth , using ~ scottex to establish the existence of ` A ` . For a version of this theorem scheme using class (meta)variables instead of wff (meta)variables, see ~ htalem . (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.) Use the ` Scott ` operation. (Revised by BTernaryTau, 22-Jul-2026.)
theorem hta {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 y1 z) (R: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cscott (cab x3 ph)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crio y1 (wral z (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wwe A2 R) (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph B2)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ hta as of 22-Jul-2026. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem htaOLD {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z w) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class x3 y1 z w) (R: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab x3 (wa ph (wal y1 (wi (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (wss (cfv (cv x3) crnk) (cfv (cv y1) crnk)))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crio z (wral w (wn (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R)) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wwe A2 R) (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph B2)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include a set defined by transfinite recursion.
term csetrecs (F: class): class;

--| Define a class ` setrecs ( F ) ` by transfinite recursion, where ` ( F `` x ) ` is the set of new elements to add to the class given the set ` x ` of elements in the class so far. We do not need a base case, because we can start with the empty set, which is vacuously a subset of ` setrecs ( F ) ` . The goal of this definition is to construct a class fulfilling Theorems ~ setrec1 and ~ setrec2v , which give a more intuitive idea of the meaning of ` setrecs ` . Unlike ` wrecs ` , ` setrecs ` is well-defined for any ` F ` and meaningful for any function ` F ` . For example, see Theorem ~ onsetrec for how the class ` On ` is defined recursively using the successor function. The definition works by building subsets of the desired class and taking the union of those subsets. To find such a collection of subsets, consider an arbitrary set ` z ` , and consider the result when applying ` F ` to any subset ` w C_ z ` . Remember that ` F ` can be any function, and in general we are interested in functions that give outputs that are larger than their inputs, so we have no reason to expect the outputs to be within ` z ` . However, if we restrict the domain of ` F ` to a given set ` y ` , the resulting range will be a set. Therefore, with this restricted ` F ` , it makes sense to consider sets ` z ` that are closed under ` F ` applied to its subsets. Now we can test whether a given set ` y ` is recursively generated by ` F ` . If every set ` z ` that is closed under ` F ` contains ` y ` , that means that every member of ` y ` must eventually be generated by ` F ` . On the other hand, if some such ` z ` does not contain a certain element of ` y ` , then that element can be avoided even if we apply ` F ` in every possible way to previously generated elements. Note that such an omitted element might be eventually recursively generated by ` F ` , but not through the elements of ` y ` . In this case, ` y ` would fail the condition in the definition, but the omitted element would still be included in some larger ` y ` . For example, if ` F ` is the successor function, the set ` { (/) , 2o } ` would fail the condition since ` 2o ` is not an element of the successor of ` (/) ` or ` { (/) } ` . Remember that we are applying ` F ` to subsets of ` y ` , not elements of ` y ` . In fact, even the set ` { 1o } ` fails the condition, since the only subset of previously generated elements is ` (/) ` , and ` suc (/) ` does not have ` 1o ` as an element. However, we can let ` y ` be any ordinal, since each of its elements is generated by starting with ` (/) ` and repeatedly applying the successor function. A similar definition I initially used for ` setrecs ( F ) ` was ` setrecs ( F ) = U. ran recs ( ( g e. _V |-> ( F `` U. ran g ) ) ) ` . I had initially tried and failed to find an elementary definition, and I had proven theorems analogous to ~ setrec1 and ~ setrec2v using the old definition before I found the new one. I decided to change definitions for two reasons. First, as John Horton Conway noted in the Appendix to Part Zero of On Numbers and Games, mathematicians should not be caught up in any particular formalization, such as ZF set theory. Instead, they should work under whatever framework best suits the problem, and the formal bases used for different problems can be shown to be equivalent. Thus, Conway preferred defining surreal numbers as equivalence classes of surreal number forms, rather than sign-expansions. Although sign-expansions are easier to implement in ZF set theory, Conway argued that "formalisation within some particular axiomatic set theory is irrelevant". Furthermore, one of the most remarkable properties of the theory of surreal numbers is that it generates so much from almost nothing. Using sign-expansions as the formal definition destroys the beauty of surreal numbers, because ordinals are already built in. For this reason, I replaced the old definition of ` setrecs ` , which also relied heavily on ordinal numbers. On the other hand, both surreal numbers and the elementary definition of ` setrecs ` immediately generate the ordinal numbers from a (relatively) very simple set-theoretical basis. Second, although it is still complicated to formalize the theory of recursively generated sets within ZF set theory, it is actually simpler and more natural to do so with set theory directly than with the theory of ordinal numbers. As Conway wrote, indexing the "birthdays" of sets is and should be unnecessary. Using an elementary definition for ` setrecs ` removes the reliance on the previously developed theory of ordinal numbers, allowing proofs to be simpler and more direct. Formalizing surreal numbers within Metamath is probably still not in the spirit of Conway. He said that "attempts to force arbitrary theories into a single formal straitjacket... produce unnecessarily cumbrous and inelegant contortions." Nevertheless, Metamath has proven to be much more versatile than it seems at first, and I think the theory of surreal numbers can be natural while fitting well into the Metamath framework. The difficulty in writing a definition in Metamath for ` setrecs ( F ) ` is that the necessary properties to prove are self-referential (see ~ setrec1 and ~ setrec2v ), so we cannot simply write the properties we want inside a class abstraction as with most definitions. As noted in the comment of ~ df-rdg , this is not actually a requirement of the Metamath language, but we would like to be able to eliminate all definitions by direct mechanical substitution. We cannot define ` setrecs ` using a class abstraction directly, because nothing about its individual elements tells us whether they are in the set. We need to know about previous elements first. One way of getting around this problem without indexing is by defining ` setrecs ( F ) ` as a union or intersection of suitable sets. Thus, instead of using a class abstraction for the elements of ` setrecs ( F ) ` , which seems to be impossible, we can use a class abstraction for supersets or subsets of ` setrecs ( F ) ` , which "know" about multiple individual elements at a time. Note that we cannot define ` setrecs ( F ) ` as an intersection of sets, because in general it is a proper class, so any supersets would also be proper classes. However, a proper class can be a union of sets, as long as the collection of such sets is a proper class. Therefore, it is feasible to define ` setrecs ( F ) ` as a union of a class abstraction. If ` setrecs ( F ) = U. A ` , the elements of A must be subsets of ` setrecs ( F ) ` which together include everything recursively generated by ` F ` . We can do this by letting ` A ` be the class of sets ` x ` whose elements are all recursively generated by ` F ` . One necessary condition is that each element of a given ` x e. A ` must be generated by ` F ` when applied to a previous element ` y e. A ` . In symbols, ` A. x e. A E. y e. A ( y C_ x /\ x C_ ( F `` y ) ) } ` . However, this is not sufficient. All fixed points ` x ` of ` F ` will satisfy this condition whether they should be in ` setrecs ( F ) ` or not. If we replace the subset relation with the proper subset relation, ` x ` cannot be the empty set, even though the empty set should be in ` A ` . Therefore this condition cannot be used in the definition, even if we can find a way to avoid making it circular. A better strategy is to find a necessary and sufficient condition for all the elements of a set ` y e. A ` to be generated by ` F ` when applied only to sets of previously generated elements within ` y ` . For example, taking ` F ` to be the successor function, we can let ` A = On ` rather than ` ~P On ` , and we will still have ` U. A = On ` as required. This gets rid of the circularity of the definition, since we should have a condition to test whether a given set ` y ` is in ` A ` without knowing about any of the other elements of ` A ` . The definition I ended up using accomplishes this using induction: ` A ` is defined as the class of sets ` y ` for which a sort of induction on the elements of ` y ` holds. However, when creating a definition for ` setrecs ` that did not rely on ordinal numbers, I tried at first to write a definition using the well-founded relation predicate, ` Fr ` . I thought that this would be simple to do once I found a suitable definition using induction, just as the least- element principle is equivalent to induction on the positive integers. If we let ` R = { <. a , b >. | ( F `` a ) C_ b } ` , then ` ( R Fr A <-> A. x ( ( x C_ A /\ x =/= (/) ) ` ` -> E. y e. x A. z e. x -. ( F `` z ) C_ y ) ) ` . On 22-Jul-2020 I came up with the following definition (Version 1) phrased in terms of induction: ` U. { y | A. z ` ` ( A. w ( w C_ y -> ( w e. z -> ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) -> y e. z ) } ` In Aug-2020 I came up with an equivalent definition with the goal of phrasing it in terms of the relation ` Fr ` . It is the contrapositive of the previous one with ` z ` replaced by its complement. ` U. { y | A. z ` ` ( y e. z -> E. w ( w C_ y /\ ( F `` w ) e. z /\ -. w e. z ) ) } ` These definitions didn't work because the induction didn't "get off the ground." If ` z ` does not contain the empty set, the condition ` ( A. w ... y e. z ` fails, so ` y = (/) ` doesn't get included in ` A ` even though it should. This could be fixed by adding the base case as a separate requirement, but the subtler problem would remain that rather than a set of "acceptable" sets, what we really need is a collection ` z ` of all individuals that have been generated so far. So one approach is to replace every occurrence of ` e. z ` with ` C_ z ` , making ` z ` a set of individuals rather than a family of sets. That solves this problem, but it complicates the foundedness version of the definition, which looked cleaner in Version 1. There was another problem with Version 1. If we let ` F ` be the power set function, then the induction in the inductive version works for ` z ` being the class of transitive sets, restricted to subsets of ` y ` . Therefore, ` y ` must be transitive by definition of ` z ` . This doesn't affect the union of all such ` y ` , but it may or may not be desirable. The problem is that ` F ` is only applied to transitive sets, because of the strong requirement ` w e. z ` , so the definition requires the additional constraint ` ( a C_ b -> ( F `` a ) C_ ( F `` b ) ) ` in order to work. This issue can also be avoided by replacing ` e. z ` with ` C_ z ` . The induction version of the result is used in the final definition. Version 2: (18-Aug-2020) Induction: ` U. { y | A. z ` ` ( A. w ( w C_ y -> ( w C_ z -> ( F `` w ) C_ z ) ) -> y C_ z ) } ` Foundedness: ` U. { y | A. z ( y i^i z =/= (/) -> ` ` E. w ( w C_ y /\ w i^i z = (/) /\ ( F `` w ) i^i z =/= (/) ) ) } ` In the induction version, not only does ` z ` include all the elements of ` y ` , but it must include the elements of ` ( F `` w ) ` for ` w C_ ( y i^i z ) ` even if those elements of ` ( F `` w ) ` are not in ` y ` . We shouldn't care about any of the elements of ` z ` outside ` y ` , but this detail doesn't affect the correctness of the definition. If we replaced ` ( F `` w ) ` in the definition by ` ( ( F `` w ) i^i y ) ` , we would get the same class for ` setrecs ( F ) ` . Suppose we could find a ` z ` for which the condition fails for a given ` y ` under the changed definition. Then the antecedent would be true, but ` y C_ z ` would be false. We could then simply add all elements of ` ( F `` w ) ` outside of ` y ` for any ` w C_ y ` , which we can do because all the classes involved are sets. This is not trivial and requires the axioms of union, power set, and replacement. However, the expanded ` z ` fails the condition under the Metamath definition. The other direction is easier. If a certain ` z ` fails the Metamath definition, then all ` ( F `` w ) C_ z ` for ` w C_ ( y i^i z ) ` , and in particular ` ( ( F `` w ) i^i y ) C_ z ` . The foundedness version is starting to look more like ~ ax-reg ! We want to take advantage of the preexisting relation ` Fr ` , which seems closely related to our foundedness definition. Since we only care about the elements of ` z ` which are subsets of ` y ` , we can restrict ` z ` to ` y ` in the foundedness definition. Furthermore, instead of quantifying over ` w ` , quantify over the elements ` v e. z ` overlapping with ` w ` . Versions 3, 4, and 5 are all equivalent to Version 2. Version 3 - Foundedness (5-Sep-2020): ` U. { y | A. z ( ( z C_ y /\ z =/= (/) ) ` ` -> E. v e. z E. w ( w C_ y /\ w i^i z = (/) /\ v e. ( F `` w ) ) ) } ` Now, if we replace ` ( F `` w ) ` by ` ( ( F `` w ) i^i y ) ` , we do not change the definition. We already know that ` v e. y ` since ` v e. z ` and ` z C_ y ` . All we need to show in order to prove that this change leads to an equivalent definition is to find To make our definition look exactly like ~ df-fr , we add another variable ` u ` representing the nonexistent element of ` w ` in ` z ` . Version 4 - Foundedness (6-Sep-2020): ` U. { y | A. z ( ( z C_ y /\ z =/= (/) ) -> ` ` E. v e. z E. w A. u e. z ( w C_ y /\ -. u e. w /\ v e. ( F `` w ) ) ` This is so close to ~ df-fr ; the only change needed is to switch ` E. w ` with ` A. u e. z ` . Unfortunately, I couldn't find any way to switch the quantifiers without interfering with the definition. Maybe there is a definition equivalent to this one that uses ` Fr ` , but I couldn't find one. Yet, we can still find a remarkable similarity between Foundedness Version 2 and ~ ax-reg . Rather than a disjoint element of ` z ` , there's a disjoint coverer of an element of ` z ` . Finally, here's a different dead end I followed: To clean up our foundedness definition, we keep ` z ` as a family of sets ` y ` but allow ` w ` to be any subset of ` U. z ` in the induction. With this stronger induction, we can also allow for the stronger requirement ` ~P y C_ z ` rather than only ` y e. z ` . This will help improve the foundedness version. Version 1.1 (28-Aug-2020) Induction: ` U. { y | A. z ( A. w ` ` ( w C_ y -> ( w C_ U. z -> ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) -> ~P y C_ z ) } ` Foundedness: ` U. { y | A. z ( E. a ( a C_ y /\ a e. z ) ` ` -> E. w ( w C_ y /\ w i^i |^| z = (/) /\ ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) } ` ( Edit (Aug 31) - this isn't true! Nothing forces the subset of an element of ` z ` to be in ` z ` . Version 2 does not have this issue. ) Similarly, we could allow ` w ` to be any subset of any element of ` z ` rather than any subset of ` U. z ` . I think this has the same problem. We want to take advantage of the preexisting relation ` Fr ` , which seems closely related to our foundedness definition. Since we only care about the elements of ` z ` which are subsets of ` y ` , we can restrict ` z ` to ` ~P y ` in the foundedness definition: Version 1.2 (31-Aug-2020) Foundedness: ` U. { y | A. z ( ( z C_ ~P y /\ z =/= (/) ) ` ` -> E. w ( w e. ~P y /\ w i^i |^| z = (/) /\ ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) } ` Now this looks more like ~ df-fr ! The last step necessary to be able to use ` Fr ` directly in our definition is to replace ` ( F `` w ) ` with its own setvar variable, corresponding to ` y ` in ~ df-fr . This definition is incorrect, though, since there's nothing forcing the subset of an element of ` z ` to be in ` z ` . Version 1.3 (31-Aug-2020) Induction: ` U. { y | A. z ( A. w ( w C_ y ` ` -> ( w C_ U. z -> ( w e. z /\ ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) ) -> ~P y C_ z ) } ` Foundedness: ` U. { y | A. z ( ( z C_ ~P y /\ z =/= (/) ) -> E. w ( w e. ~P y ` ` /\ w i^i |^| z = (/) /\ ( w e. z \/ ( F `` w ) e. z ) ) ) } ` ` z ` must contain the supersets of each of its elements in the foundedness version, and we can't make any restrictions on ` z ` or ` F ` , so this doesn't work. Let's try letting R be the covering relation ` R = { <. a , b >. | b e. ( F `` a ) } ` to solve the transitivity issue (i.e. that if ` F ` is the power set relation, ` A ` consists only of transitive sets). The set ` ( F `` w ) ` corresponds to the variable ` y ` in ~ df-fr . Thus, in our case, ~ df-fr is equivalent to ` ( R Fr A <-> A. z ( ( z C_ A /\ z =/= (/) ) ` ` -> E. w ( ( F `` w ) e. z /\ -. E. v e. z v R ( F `` w ) ) ) ` . Substituting our relation ` R ` gives ` ( R Fr A <-> A. z ( ( z C_ A /\ z =/= (/) ) -> ` ` E. w ( ( F `` w ) e. z /\ -. E. v e. z ( F `` w ) e. ( F `` v ) ) ) ` This doesn't work for non-injective ` F ` because we need all ` z ` to be straddlers, but we don't necessarily need all-straddlers; loops within z are fine for non-injective F. Consider the foundedness form of Version 1. We want to show ` -. w e. z <-> A. v e. z -. v R ( F `` w ) ` so we can replace one with the other. Negate both sides: ` w e. z <-> E. v e. z v R ( F `` w ) ` If ` F ` is injective, then we should be able to pick a suitable R, being careful about the above problem for some F (for example z = transitivity) when changing the antecedent y e. z' to z =/= (/). If we're clever, we can get rid of the injectivity requirement. The forward direction of the above equivalence always holds, but the key is that although the backwards direction doesn't hold in general, we can always find some z' where it doesn't work for ` w ` itself. If there exists a z' where the version with the w condition fails, then there exists a z' where the version with the v condition also fails. However, Version 1 is not a correct definition, so this doesn't work either. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 18-Aug-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_setrecs {y1 z w: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq (csetrecs F) (cuni (cab y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv y1)) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss (cv y1) (cv z)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec1 . This is a utility theorem showing the equivalence of the statement ` X e. Y ` and its expanded form. The proof uses ~ elabg and equivalence theorems. Variable ` Y ` is the class of sets ` y ` that are recursively generated by the function ` F ` . In other words, ` y e. Y ` iff by starting with the empty set and repeatedly applying ` F ` to subsets ` w ` of our set, we will eventually generate all the elements of ` Y ` . In this theorem, ` X ` is any element of ` Y ` , and ` V ` is any class. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 16-Oct-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec1lem1 {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z w) (F: class z w) (V: class y1 z w) (X: class) (Y: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq Y (cab y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv y1)) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss (cv y1) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X Y) (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) X) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss X (cv z))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec1 . If a family of sets are all recursively generated by ` F ` , so is their union. In this theorem, ` X ` is a family of sets which are all elements of ` Y ` , and ` V ` is any class. Use ~ dfss3 , equivalence and equality theorems, and unissb at the end. Sandwich with applications of setrec1lem1. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 24-Jan-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec1lem2 {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z w) (F: class z w) (V: class y1 z w) (X: class) (Y: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq Y (cab y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv y1)) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss (cv y1) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni X) Y) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bnd2 . (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 19-Jan-2021.)
theorem bnd2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex y1 ps B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex z (wa (wss (cv z) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ps (cv z)) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec1 . If each element ` a ` of ` A ` is covered by a set ` x ` recursively generated by ` F ` , then there is a single such set covering all of ` A ` . The set is constructed explicitly using ~ setrec1lem2 . It turns out that ` x = A ` also works, i.e., given the hypotheses it is possible to prove that ` A e. Y ` . I don't know if proving this fact directly using ~ setrec1lem1 would be any easier than the current proof using ~ setrec1lem2 , and it would only slightly simplify the proof of ~ setrec1 . Other than the use of ~ bnd2d , this is a purely technical theorem for rearranging notation from that of ~ setrec1lem2 to that of ~ setrec1 . (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 20-Jan-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec1lem3 {x3 y1 z w a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w a) (A2: class y1 z w) (F: class z w a) (Y: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq Y (cab y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv y1)) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss (cv y1) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv a) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) Y))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wa (wss A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) Y))) $;

--| A version of ~ spcdv where ` ps ` and ` ch ` are direct substitutions of each other. This theorem is useful because it does not require ` ph ` and ` x ` to be distinct variables. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 12-Apr-2020.)
theorem spcdvw {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec1 . If ` X ` is recursively generated by ` F ` , then so is ` X u. ( F `` A ) ` . In the proof of ~ setrec1 , the following is substituted for this theorem's ` ph ` : ` ( ph /\ ( A C_ x /\ x e. { y | A. z ( A. w ` ` ( w C_ y -> ( w C_ z -> ( F `` w ) C_ z ) ) -> y C_ z ) } ) ) ` Therefore, we cannot declare ` z ` to be a distinct variable from ` ph ` , since we need it to appear as a bound variable in ` ph ` . This theorem can be proven without the hypothesis ` F/ z ph ` , but the proof would be harder to read because theorems in deduction form would be interrupted by theorems like ~ eximi , making the antecedent of each line something more complicated than ` ph ` . The proof of ~ setrec1lem2 could similarly be made easier to read by adding the hypothesis ` F/ z ph ` , but I had already finished the proof and decided to leave it as is. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 26-Nov-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec1lem4 {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z w) (A2 F X: class) (Y: class y1 z w):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv y1)) (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv w) F) (cv z))))) (wss (cv y1) (cv z))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun X (cfv A2 F)) Y) $;

--| This is the first of two fundamental theorems about set recursion from which all other facts will be derived. It states that the class ` setrecs ( F ) ` is closed under ` F ` . This effectively sets the actual value of ` setrecs ( F ) ` as a lower bound for ` setrecs ( F ) ` , as it implies that any set generated by successive applications of ` F ` is a member of ` B ` . This theorem "gets off the ground" because we can start by letting ` A = (/) ` , and the hypotheses of the theorem will hold trivially. Variable ` B ` represents an abbreviation of ` setrecs ( F ) ` or another name of ` setrecs ( F ) ` (for an example of the latter, see theorem setrecon). Proof summary: Assume that ` A C_ B ` , meaning that all elements of ` A ` are in some set recursively generated by ` F ` . Then by ~ setrec1lem3 , ` A ` is a subset of some set recursively generated by ` F ` . (It turns out that ` A ` itself is recursively generated by ` F ` , but we don't need this fact. See the comment to ~ setrec1lem3 .) Therefore, by ~ setrec1lem4 , ` ( F `` A ) ` is a subset of some set recursively generated by ` F ` . Thus, by ~ ssuni , it is a subset of the union of all sets recursively generated by ` F ` . See ~ df-setrecs for a detailed description of how the ` setrecs ` definition works. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 9-Oct-2020.)
theorem setrec1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (csetrecs F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv A2 F) B2) $;

--| This is the second of two fundamental theorems about set recursion from which all other facts will be derived. It states that the class ` setrecs ( F ) ` is a subclass of all classes ` C ` that are closed under ` F ` . Taken together, Theorems ~ setrec1 and ~ setrec2v say that ` setrecs ( F ) ` is the minimal class closed under ` F ` . We express this by saying that if ` F ` respects the ` C_ ` relation and ` C ` is closed under ` F ` , then ` B C_ C ` . By substituting strategically constructed classes for ` C ` , we can easily prove many useful properties. Although this theorem cannot show equality between ` B ` and ` C ` , if we intend to prove equality between ` B ` and some particular class (such as ` On ` ), we first apply this theorem, then the relevant induction theorem (such as ~ tfi ) to the other class. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 15-Feb-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec2fun {a: setvar} (ph: wff a) (B2: class a) (C: class) (F: class a):
  $ wnfc a F $ >
  $ wceq B2 (csetrecs F) $ >
  $ wfun F $ >
  $ wi ph (wal a (wi (wss (cv a) C) (wss (cfv (cv a) F) C))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec2 . The functional part of ` F ` has the same values as ` F ` . (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 4-Mar-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec2lem1 {x3 y1 a: setvar} (F: class a):
  $ wceq (cfv (cv a) (cres F (cab x3 (weu y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F))))) (cfv (cv a) F) $;

--| Alternate definition of function, using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 14-Mar-2021.)
theorem dffun3f {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc z A2 $ >
  $ wb (wfun A2) (wa (wrel A2) (wal x3 (wex z (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) A2) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setrec2 . The functional part of ` F ` is a function. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 6-Mar-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem setrec2lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wfun (cres F (cab x3 (weu y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| This is the second of two fundamental theorems about set recursion from which all other facts will be derived. It states that the class ` setrecs ( F ) ` is a subclass of all classes ` C ` that are closed under ` F ` . Taken together, Theorems ~ setrec1 and ~ setrec2v uniquely determine ` setrecs ( F ) ` to be the minimal class closed under ` F ` . We express this by saying that if ` F ` respects the ` C_ ` relation and ` C ` is closed under ` F ` , then ` B C_ C ` . By substituting strategically constructed classes for ` C ` , we can easily prove many useful properties. Although this theorem cannot show equality between ` B ` and ` C ` , if we intend to prove equality between ` B ` and some particular class (such as ` On ` ), we first apply this theorem, then the relevant induction theorem (such as ~ tfi ) to the other class. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 2-Sep-2021.)
theorem setrec2 {a: setvar} (ph: wff a) (B2: class a) (C: class) (F: class a):
  $ wnfc a F $ >
  $ wceq B2 (csetrecs F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal a (wi (wss (cv a) C) (wss (cfv (cv a) F) C))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $;

--| Version of ~ setrec2 with a disjoint variable condition instead of a nonfreeness hypothesis. (Contributed by Emmett Weisz, 6-Mar-2021.)
theorem setrec2v {a: setvar} (ph: wff a) (B2: class a) (C F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (csetrecs F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal a (wi (wss (cv a) C) (wss (cfv (cv a) F) C))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $;

--| Extend class notation to include disjoint union of two classes.
term cdju (A2 B2: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include left injection of a disjoint union.
term cinl: class;

--| Extend class notation to include right injection of a disjoint union.
term cinr: class;

--| Disjoint union of two classes. This is a way of creating a set which contains elements corresponding to each element of ` A ` or ` B ` , tagging each one with whether it came from ` A ` or ` B ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Jun-2022.)
axiom df_dju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cdju A2 B2) (cun (cxp (csn c0) A2) (cxp (csn c1o) B2)) $;

--| Left injection of a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2022.)
axiom df_inl {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cinl (cmpt x3 cvv (cop c0 (cv x3))) $;

--| Right injection of a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2022.)
axiom df_inr {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cinr (cmpt x3 cvv (cop c1o (cv x3))) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jun-2022.)
theorem djueq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wceq (cdju A2 C) (cdju B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jun-2022.)
theorem djueq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cdju A2 C) (cdju B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jun-2022.)
theorem djueq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cdju C A2) (cdju C B2)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jun-2022.)
theorem nfdju {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cdju A2 B2) $;

--| The disjoint union of sets is a set. For a shorter proof using ~ djuss see ~ djuexALT . (Contributed by AV, 28-Jun-2022.)
theorem djuex (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cdju A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The disjoint union of two classes is a set iff both classes are sets. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Sep-2023.)
theorem djuexb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) (wcel (cdju A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Left closure of disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Jun-2022.)
theorem djulcl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C A2) (wcel (cfv C cinl) (cdju A2 B2)) $;

--| Right closure of disjoint union. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Jun-2022.)
theorem djurcl (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C B2) (wcel (cfv C cinr) (cdju A2 B2)) $;

--| The left injection function on all sets is one to one and onto. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jun-2022.)
theorem djulf1o:
  $ wf1o cvv (cxp (csn c0) cvv) cinl $;

--| The right injection function on all sets is one to one and onto. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jun-2022.)
theorem djurf1o:
  $ wf1o cvv (cxp (csn c1o) cvv) cinr $;

--| The left injection restricted to the left class of a disjoint union is a function from the left class into the disjoint union. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem inlresf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf A2 (cdju A2 B2) (cres cinl A2) $;

--| The left injection restricted to the left class of a disjoint union is an injective function from the left class into the disjoint union. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jun-2022.)
theorem inlresf1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf1 A2 (cdju A2 B2) (cres cinl A2) $;

--| The right injection restricted to the right class of a disjoint union is a function from the right class into the disjoint union. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem inrresf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf B2 (cdju A2 B2) (cres cinr B2) $;

--| The right injection restricted to the right class of a disjoint union is an injective function from the right class into the disjoint union. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jun-2022.)
theorem inrresf1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wf1 B2 (cdju A2 B2) (cres cinr B2) $;

--| The images of any classes under right and left injection produce disjoint sets. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Jun-2022.)
theorem djuin (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cin (cima cinl A2) (cima cinr B2)) c0 $;

--| A member of a disjoint union can be mapped from one of the classes which produced it. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jun-2022.)
theorem djur {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cdju A2 B2)) (wo (wrex x3 (wceq C (cfv (cv x3) cinl)) A2) (wrex x3 (wceq C (cfv (cv x3) cinr)) B2)) $;

--| A disjoint union is a subclass of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jun-2022.)
theorem djuss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (cdju A2 B2) (cxp (cpr c0 c1o) (cun A2 B2)) $;

--| The union of a disjoint union and its inversion is the Cartesian product of an unordered pair and the union of the left and right classes of the disjoint unions. (Proposed by GL, 4-Jul-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2022.)
theorem djuunxp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cun (cdju A2 B2) (cdju B2 A2)) (cxp (cpr c0 c1o) (cun A2 B2)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ djuex , which is shorter, but based indirectly on the definitions of ` inl ` and ` inr ` . (Proposed by BJ, 28-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 28-Jun-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem djuexALT (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cdju A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| The first component of an element of a disjoint union is either ` (/) ` or ` 1o ` . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jun-2022.)
theorem eldju1st (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cdju A2 B2)) (wo (wceq (cfv X c1st) c0) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c1o)) $;

--| The second component of an element of a disjoint union is an element of the left class of the disjoint union if its first component is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jun-2022.)
theorem eldju2ndl (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cdju A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c0)) (wcel (cfv X c2nd) A2) $;

--| The second component of an element of a disjoint union is an element of the right class of the disjoint union if its first component is not the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jun-2022.)
theorem eldju2ndr (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cdju A2 B2)) (wne (cfv X c1st) c0)) (wcel (cfv X c2nd) B2) $;

--| The disjoint union of two classes is the union of the images of those two classes under right and left injection. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jun-2022.)
theorem djuun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cun (cima cinl A2) (cima cinr B2)) (cdju A2 B2) $;

--| The first component of the value of a left injection is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem _1stinl (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv (cfv X cinl) c1st) c0) $;

--| The second component of the value of a left injection is its argument. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem _2ndinl (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv (cfv X cinl) c2nd) X) $;

--| The first component of the value of a right injection is ` 1o ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem _1stinr (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv (cfv X cinr) c1st) c1o) $;

--| The second component of the value of a right injection is its argument. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem _2ndinr (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv (cfv X cinr) c2nd) X) $;

--| The mapping of an element of the disjoint union to the value of the corresponding function is a function. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jun-2022.)
theorem updjudhf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (F G H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (cdju A2 B2) (cif (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c0) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdju A2 B2) C H) $;

--| The composition of the mapping of an element of the disjoint union to the value of the corresponding function and the left injection equals the first function. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem updjudhcoinlf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (cdju A2 B2) (cif (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c0) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom H (cres cinl A2)) F) $;

--| The composition of the mapping of an element of the disjoint union to the value of the corresponding function and the right injection equals the second function. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2022.)
theorem updjudhcoinrg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (cdju A2 B2) (cif (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c0) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom H (cres cinr B2)) G) $;

--| Universal property of the disjoint union. This theorem shows that the disjoint union, together with the left and right injections ~ df-inl and ~ df-inr , is the _coproduct_ in the category of sets, see Wikipedia "Coproduct", ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coproduct (25-Aug-2023). This is a special case of Example 1 of coproducts in Section 10.67 of [Adamek] p. 185. (Proposed by BJ, 25-Jun-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 28-Jun-2022.)
theorem updjud {h: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class) (V W: class h):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (weu h (w3a (wf (cdju A2 B2) C (cv h)) (wceq (ccom (cv h) (cres cinl A2)) F) (wceq (ccom (cv h) (cres cinr B2)) G))) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the cardinal size function.
term ccrd: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the aleph function.
term cale: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the cofinality function.
term ccf: class;

--| The axiom of choice for limited-length sequences.
term wacn (A2: class): class;

--| Define the cardinal number function. The cardinal number of a set is the least ordinal number equinumerous to it. In other words, it is the "size" of the set. Definition of [Enderton] p. 197. See ~ cardval for its value and ~ cardval2 for a simpler version of its value. The principal theorem relating cardinality to equinumerosity is ~ carden . Our notation is from Enderton. Other textbooks often use a double bar over the set to express this function. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.)
axiom df_card {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccrd (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cen) con0))) $;

--| Define the aleph function. Our definition expresses Definition 12 of [Suppes] p. 229 in a closed form, from which we derive the recursive definition as Theorems ~ aleph0 , ~ alephsuc , and ~ alephlim . The aleph function provides a one-to-one, onto mapping from the ordinal numbers to the infinite cardinal numbers. Roughly, any aleph is the smallest infinite cardinal number whose size is strictly greater than any aleph before it. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.)
axiom df_aleph:
  $ wceq cale (crdg char com) $;

--| Define the cofinality function. Definition B of Saharon Shelah, _Cardinal Arithmetic_ (1994), p. xxx (Roman numeral 30). See ~ cfval for its value and a description. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2004.)
axiom df_cf {x3 y1 z v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccf (cmpt x3 con0 (cint (cab y1 (wex z (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv z) ccrd)) (wa (wss (cv z) (cv x3)) (wral v (wrex u (wss (cv v) (cv u)) (cv z)) (cv x3)))))))) $;

--| Define a local and length-limited version of the axiom of choice. The definition of the predicate ` X e. AC_ A ` is that for all families of nonempty subsets of ` X ` indexed on ` A ` (i.e. functions ` A --> ~P X \ { (/) } ` ), there is a function which selects an element from each set in the family. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_acn {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (wacn A2) (cab x3 (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wral f2 (wex g2 (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) A2)) (co (cdif (cpw (cv x3)) (csn c0)) A2 cmap)))) $;

--| The cardinality function is a function with domain the well-orderable sets. Assuming AC, this is the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem cardf2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wf (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cen) con0)) con0 ccrd $;

--| The cardinal number of a set is an ordinal number. Proposition 10.6(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem cardon (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) con0 $;

--| A way to express well-orderability without bound or distinct variables. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem isnum2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) con0) $;

--| A set equinumerous to an ordinal is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem isnumi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Equinumerous sets are equi-numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem ennum (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) $;

--| Every finite set is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem finnum (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Every ordinal number is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem onenon (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| A Tarski set is well-orderable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem tskwe {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 csdm) (cpw A2)) A2)) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The cartesian product of numerable sets is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpnum (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| An alternate definition of the value of ` ( card `` A ) ` that does not require AC to prove. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardval3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cint (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) con0))) $;

--| Any numerable set is equinumerous to its cardinal number. Proposition 10.5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardid2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| A set is numerable iff it is equinumerous with its cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem isnum3 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| The value of the cardinal number function with an ordinal number as its argument. Unlike ~ cardval , this theorem does not require the Axiom of Choice. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem oncardval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cint (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) con0))) $;

--| Any ordinal number is equinumerous to its cardinal number. Unlike ~ cardid , this theorem does not require the Axiom of Choice. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2004.)
theorem oncardid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| The cardinal of an ordinal number is less than or equal to the ordinal number. Proposition 10.6(3) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.)
theorem cardonle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wss (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) $;

--| The cardinality of the empty set is the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2003.)
theorem card0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 ccrd) c0 $;

--| The cardinality function is idempotent. Proposition 10.11 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardidm (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccrd) ccrd) (cfv A2 ccrd) $;

--| A set is a cardinal number iff it equals its own cardinal number. Proposition 10.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.)
theorem oncard {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wceq A2 (cfv (cv x3) ccrd))) (wceq A2 (cfv A2 ccrd)) $;

--| The cardinal number of a finite set is a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2013.)
theorem ficardom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) com) $;

--| A finite set is equinumerous to its cardinal number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2013.)
theorem ficardid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| The cardinality of a natural number is the number. Corollary 10.23 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardnn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) $;

--| The empty set is the only numerable set with cardinality zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardnueq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) c0) (wceq A2 c0)) $;

--| No member of a cardinal number of a set is equinumerous to the set. Proposition 10.6(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wn (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| If two sets have equal nonzero cardinalities, then they are equinumerous. This assertion and ~ carden2b are meant to replace ~ carden in ZF without AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jan-2013.)
theorem carden2a (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wne (cfv A2 ccrd) c0)) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| If two sets are equinumerous, then they have equal cardinalities. (This assertion and ~ carden2a are meant to replace ~ carden in ZF without AC.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem carden2b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) $;

--| A set has cardinality one iff it is a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2013.)
theorem card1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) c1o) (wex x3 (wceq A2 (csn (cv x3)))) $;

--| A singleton has cardinality one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardsn (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) ccrd) c1o) $;

--| Two sets have the dominance relationship if their cardinalities have the subset relationship and one is numerable. See also ~ carddom , which uses AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem carddomi2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 V)) (wi (wss (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| A set strictly dominates if its cardinal strictly dominates. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem sdomsdomcardi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 (cfv B2 ccrd) csdm) (wbr A2 B2 csdm) $;

--| An infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal. Equivalent to Exercise 4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardlim (A2: class):
  $ wb (wss com (cfv A2 ccrd)) (wlim (cfv A2 ccrd)) $;

--| A cardinal strictly dominates its members. Equivalent to Proposition 10.37 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93. This is half of the assertion ~ cardsdomel and can be proven without the AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardsdomelir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 csdm) $;

--| A cardinal strictly dominates its members. Equivalent to Proposition 10.37 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2015.)
theorem cardsdomel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ccrd))) $;

--| Two ways to express the property of being a cardinal number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.)
theorem iscard {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 csdm) A2)) $;

--| Two ways to express the property of being a cardinal number. Definition 8 of [Suppes] p. 225. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2013.)
theorem iscard2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wral x3 (wi (wbr A2 (cv x3) cen) (wss A2 (cv x3))) con0)) $;

--| Two numerable sets have the dominance relationship iff their cardinalities have the subset relationship. See also ~ carddom , which uses AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem carddom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wb (wss (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| The class of ordinal numbers dominated by a set is a cardinal number. Theorem 59 of [Suppes] p. 228. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem harcard (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 char) ccrd) (cfv A2 char) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cardprc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem cardprclem {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| The class of all cardinal numbers is not a set (i.e. is a proper class). Theorem 19.8 of [Eisenberg] p. 310. In this proof (which does not use AC), we cannot use Cantor's construction ~ canth3 to ensure that there is always a cardinal larger than a given cardinal, but we can use Hartogs' construction ~ hartogs to construct (effectively) ` ( aleph `` suc A ) ` from ` ( aleph `` A ) ` , which achieves the same thing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardprc {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3))) cvv $;

--| The union of a set of cardinals is a cardinal. Theorem 18.14 of [Monk1] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jan-2013.)
theorem carduni {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cuni A2) ccrd) (cuni A2))) $;

--| The indexed union of a set of cardinals is a cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.)
theorem cardiun {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wral x3 (wceq (cfv B2 ccrd) B2) A2) (wceq (cfv (ciun x3 A2 B2) ccrd) (ciun x3 A2 B2))) $;

--| If ` A ` is equinumerous to a natural number, then that number is its cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardennn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) B2) $;

--| The cardinality of the successor of an infinite ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2013.)
theorem cardsucinf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wss com A2)) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) ccrd) (cfv A2 ccrd)) $;

--| The cardinality of the successor of a finite ordinal (natural number). This theorem does not hold for infinite ordinals; see ~ cardsucinf . (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.)
theorem cardsucnn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) ccrd) (csuc (cfv A2 ccrd))) $;

--| The set of natural numbers is a cardinal number. Theorem 18.11 of [Monk1] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2003.)
theorem cardom:
  $ wceq (cfv com ccrd) com $;

--| Two numerable sets are equinumerous iff their cardinal numbers are equal. Unlike ~ carden , the Axiom of Choice is not required. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2013.)
theorem carden2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| A numerable set is strictly dominated by another iff their cardinalities are strictly ordered. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardsdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 csdm)) $;

--| Trichotomy of dominance for numerable sets (does not use AC). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem domtri2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm))) $;

--| Strict dominance and elementhood are the same for finite ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnsdomel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 csdm)) $;

--| An alternate version of the value of the cardinal number of a set. Compare ~ cardval . This theorem could be used to give a simpler definition of ` card ` in place of ~ df-card . It apparently does not occur in the literature. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2003.)
theorem cardval2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 csdm) con0)) $;

--| An infinite set contains subsets equinumerous to every finite set. Extension of ~ isinf from finite ordinals to all finite sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Oct-2014.)
theorem isinffi {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wex f2 (wf1 B2 A2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Trichotomy of dominance without AC when one set is finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem fidomtri (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm))) $;

--| Trichotomy of dominance without AC when one set is finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem fidomtri2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm))) $;

--| The Hartogs number of a well-orderable set strictly dominates the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem harsdom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 char) csdm) $;

--| Any well-orderable set is strictly dominated by an ordinal number. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem onsdom {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0) $;

--| An alternate expression for the Hartogs number of a well-orderable set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem harval2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wceq (cfv A2 char) (cint (crab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0))) $;

--| The next cardinal after a finite ordinal is the successor ordinal. (Contributed by RP, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem harsucnn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv A2 char) (csuc A2)) $;

--| The smallest ordinal that strictly dominates a set is a cardinal, if it exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem cardmin2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0) (wceq (cfv (cint (crab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0)) ccrd) (cint (crab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0))) $;

--| In Theorem *54.43 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 360, the number 1 is defined as the collection of all sets with cardinality 1 (i.e. all singletons; see ~ card1 ), so that their ` A e. 1 ` means, in our notation, ` A e. { x | ( card `` x ) = 1o } ` . Here we show that this is equivalent to ` A ~~ 1o ` so that we can use the latter more convenient notation in ~ pm54.43 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2013.)
theorem pm54_43lem {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c1o cen) (wcel A2 (cab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) c1o))) $;

--| Theorem *54.43 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 360. "From this proposition it will follow, when arithmetical addition has been defined, that 1+1=2." See ~ http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principia_Mathematica#Quotations . This theorem states that two sets of cardinality 1 are disjoint iff their union has cardinality 2. Whitehead and Russell define 1 as the collection of all sets with cardinality 1 (i.e. all singletons; see ~ card1 ), so that their ` A e. 1 ` means, in our notation, ` A e. { x | ( card `` x ) = 1o } ` which is the same as ` A ~~ 1o ` by ~ pm54.43lem . We do not have several of their earlier lemmas available (which would otherwise be unused by our different approach to arithmetic), so our proof is longer. (It is also longer because we must show every detail.) Theorem ~ dju1p1e2 shows the derivation of 1+1=2 for cardinal numbers from this theorem. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2007.)
theorem pm54_43 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 c1o cen) (wbr B2 c1o cen)) (wb (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) (wbr (cun A2 B2) c2o cen)) $;

--| An unordered pair with distinct elements is equinumerous to ordinal two. This is a closed-form version of ~ enpr2d . (Contributed by FL, 17-Aug-2008.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2024.)
theorem enpr2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D) (wne A2 B2)) (wbr (cpr A2 B2) c2o cen) $;

--| If an unordered pair has two elements, then they are different. (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2010.) Avoid ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-un . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 30-Dec-2024.)
theorem pr2ne (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wb (wbr (cpr A2 B2) c2o cen) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| An unordered pair has at most two elements. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.)
theorem prdom2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wbr (cpr A2 B2) c2o cdom) $;

--| Building a set with two elements. (Contributed by FL, 11-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem en2eqpr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr C c2o cen) (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C)) (wi (wne A2 B2) (wceq C (cpr A2 B2))) $;

--| Express a set of pair cardinality as the unordered pair of a given element and the other element. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem en2eleq (P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X P) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wceq P (cpr X (cuni (cdif P (csn X))))) $;

--| Taking the other element twice in a pair gets back to the original element. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem en2other2 (P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X P) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wceq (cuni (cdif P (csn (cuni (cdif P (csn X)))))) X) $;

--| The cardinality of a nonempty finite set is one greater than the cardinality of the set with one element removed. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem dif1card (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (csuc (cfv (cdif A2 (csn X)) ccrd))) $;

--| Lexicographical order is a well-ordering of ` On X. On ` . Proposition 7.56(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 54. Note that unlike ~ r0weon , this order is _not_ set-like, as the preimage of ` <. 1o , (/) >. ` is the proper class ` ( { (/) } X. On ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem leweon {x3 y1: setvar} (L: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq L (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0))) (wo (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))))) $ >
  $ wwe (cxp con0 con0) L $;

--| A set-like well-ordering of the class of ordinal pairs. Proposition 7.58(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 54. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem r0weon {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 z w) (L: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq L (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0))) (wo (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab z w (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv w) (cxp con0 con0))) (wo (wcel (cun (cfv (cv z) c1st) (cfv (cv z) c2nd)) (cun (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cfv (cv w) c2nd))) (wa (wceq (cun (cfv (cv z) c1st) (cfv (cv z) c2nd)) (cun (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cfv (cv w) c2nd))) (wbr (cv z) (cv w) L))))) $ >
  $ wa (wwe (cxp con0 con0) R) (wse (cxp con0 con0) R) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infxpen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem infxpenlem {x3 y1 z w m a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 m a) (A2: class x3 y1 z w m) (Q: class x3 y1 w m a) (R: class x3 y1 z w m a) (J: class x3 y1 z m a) (L: class x3 y1 m a) (M: class x3 y1 w a):
  $ wceq L (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0))) (wo (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab z w (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv w) (cxp con0 con0))) (wo (wcel (cun (cfv (cv z) c1st) (cfv (cv z) c2nd)) (cun (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cfv (cv w) c2nd))) (wa (wceq (cun (cfv (cv z) c1st) (cfv (cv z) c2nd)) (cun (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cfv (cv w) c2nd))) (wbr (cv z) (cv w) L))))) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cin R (cxp (cxp (cv a) (cv a)) (cxp (cv a) (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wb ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) con0) (wral m (wi (wss com (cv m)) (wbr (cxp (cv m) (cv m)) (cv m) cen)) (cv a))) (wa (wss com (cv a)) (wral m (wbr (cv m) (cv a) csdm) (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cun (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cfv (cv w) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wceq J (coi (cxp (cv a) (cv a)) Q) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wss com A2)) (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| Every infinite ordinal is equinumerous to its Cartesian square. Proposition 10.39 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 94, whose proof we follow closely. The key idea is to show that the relation ` R ` is a well-ordering of ` ( On X. On ) ` with the additional property that ` R ` -initial segments of ` ( x X. x ) ` (where ` x ` is a limit ordinal) are of cardinality at most ` x ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem infxpen (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wss com A2)) (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| The Cartesian product of omega (the set of ordinal natural numbers) with itself is equinumerous to omega. Exercise 1 of [Enderton] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem xpomen:
  $ wbr (cxp com com) com cen $;

--| The cartesian product of two countable sets is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Sep-2017.)
theorem xpct (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr B2 com cdom)) (wbr (cxp A2 B2) com cdom) $;

--| Every infinite well-orderable set is equinumerous to its Cartesian square. This theorem provides the basis for infinite cardinal arithmetic. Proposition 10.40 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 95. See also ~ infxpidm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infxpidm2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| A canonical version of ~ infxpen , by a completely different approach (although it uses ~ infxpen via ~ xpomen ). Using Cantor's normal form, we can show that ` A ^o B ` respects equinumerosity ( ~ oef1o ), so that all the steps of ` ( _om ^ W ) x. ( _om ^ W ) ~~ _om ^ ( 2 W ) ~~ ` ` ( _om ^ 2 ) ^ W ~~ _om ^ W ` can be verified using bijections to do the ordinal commutations. (The assumption on ` N ` can be satisfied using ~ cnfcom3c .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem infxpenc {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2: class z w) (T: class x3 y1 z w) (F: class z w) (G H J K L: class x3 y1 z w) (N: class z w) (W: class) (X Y: class z w) (Z: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss com A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cdif con0 c1o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co com c2o coe) com F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 F) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 (co com W coe) N) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt y1 (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c0 cfsupp) (co (co com c2o coe) W cmap)) (ccom F (ccom (cv y1) (ccnv (cres cid W))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccom (co com W ccnf) K) (ccnv (co (co com c2o coe) W ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c0 cfsupp) (co com (co W c2o comu) cmap)) (ccom (cres cid com) (ccom (cv y1) (ccnv (ccom Y (ccnv X)))))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpo z w c2o W (co (co W (cv z) comu) (cv w) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpo z w c2o W (co (co c2o (cv w) comu) (cv z) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq J (ccom (ccom (co com (co c2o W comu) ccnf) L) (ccnv (co com (co W c2o comu) ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpo x3 y1 (co com W coe) (co com W coe) (co (co (co com W coe) (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo x3 y1 A2 A2 (cop (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N))) $ >
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccnv N) (ccom (ccom (ccom H J) Z) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 A2) A2 G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infxpenc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem infxpenc2lem1 {x3 w n b: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class) (W: class n b):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wrex w (wf1o (cv b) (co com (cv w) coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (cdif con0 c1o))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdif con0 c1o) (co com (cv x3) coe)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv b) A2) (wss com (cv b)))) (wa (wcel W (cdif con0 c1o)) (wf1o (cv b) (co com W coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv n)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infxpenc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem infxpenc2lem2 {x3 y1 z w g2 n b: setvar} (ph: wff z g2 n) (A2: class z) (T: class x3 y1 z w g2 n b) (F: class z w n b) (G: class x3 y1 z w n b) (H J K L: class x3 y1 z w g2 n b) (W: class n b) (X Y: class z w g2 n b) (Z: class x3 y1 z w g2 n b):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wrex w (wf1o (cv b) (co com (cv w) coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (cdif con0 c1o))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdif con0 c1o) (co com (cv x3) coe)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co com c2o coe) com F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 F) c0) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt y1 (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c0 cfsupp) (co (co com c2o coe) W cmap)) (ccom F (ccom (cv y1) (ccnv (cres cid W))))) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccom (co com W ccnf) K) (ccnv (co (co com c2o coe) W ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c0 cfsupp) (co com (co W c2o comu) cmap)) (ccom (cres cid com) (ccom (cv y1) (ccnv (ccom Y (ccnv X)))))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpo z w c2o W (co (co W (cv z) comu) (cv w) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpo z w c2o W (co (co c2o (cv w) comu) (cv z) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq J (ccom (ccom (co com (co c2o W comu) ccnf) L) (ccnv (co com (co W c2o comu) ccnf))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpo x3 y1 (co com W coe) (co com W coe) (co (co (co com W coe) (cv x3) comu) (cv y1) coa)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo x3 y1 (cv b) (cv b) (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv b) (cv n))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccnv (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (ccom (ccom (ccom H J) Z) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wf1o (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cfv (cv b) (cv g2)))) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infxpenc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem infxpenc2lem3 {x3 w g2 n b: setvar} (ph: wff g2 n) (A2: class) (F: class w n b) (W: class n b):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wrex w (wf1o (cv b) (co com (cv w) coe) (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (cdif con0 c1o))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv n))) (ccnv (cmpt x3 (cdif con0 c1o) (co com (cv x3) coe)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co com c2o coe) com F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 F) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wf1o (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cfv (cv b) (cv g2)))) A2)) $;

--| Existence form of ~ infxpenc . A "uniform" or "canonical" version of ~ infxpen , asserting the existence of a single function ` g ` that simultaneously demonstrates product idempotence of all ordinals below a given bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2015.)
theorem infxpenc2 {g2 b: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex g2 (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wf1o (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cfv (cv b) (cv g2)))) A2)) $;

--| The union ` U_ n e. C ( A ^m n ) ` is a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.) (Revised by NM, 16-Jun-2017.)
theorem iunmapdisj {n: setvar} (A2: class n) (B2: class) (C: class n):
  $ wrmo n (wcel B2 (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) C $;

--| Lemma for ~ fseqen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fseqenlem1 {x3 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3 f2 n) (F: class) (G V: class x3 f2 n):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 A2) A2 F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cseqom (cmpo n f2 cvv cvv (cmpt x3 (co A2 (csuc (cv n)) cmap) (co (cfv (cres (cv x3) (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv n) (cv x3)) F))) (csn (cop c0 B2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C com)) (wf1 (co A2 C cmap) A2 (cfv C G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fseqen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fseqenlem2 {x3 y1 f2 k n: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 f2 k n) (F: class y1 k) (G: class x3 f2 n) (K V: class x3 y1 f2 k n):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp A2 A2) A2 F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cseqom (cmpo n f2 cvv cvv (cmpt x3 (co A2 (csuc (cv n)) cmap) (co (cfv (cres (cv x3) (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv n) (cv x3)) F))) (csn (cop c0 B2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt y1 (ciun k com (co A2 (cv k) cmap)) (cop (cdm (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cdm (cv y1)) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (ciun k com (co A2 (cv k) cmap)) (cxp com A2) K) $;

--| One half of ~ fseqen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem fseqdom {n: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class n):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cxp com A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) cdom) $;

--| A set that is equinumerous to its Cartesian product is equinumerous to the set of finite sequences on it. (This can be proven more easily using some choice but this proof avoids it.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem fseqen {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) (wne A2 c0)) (wbr (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) (cxp com A2) cen) $;

--| The collection of finite subsets of a set dominates the set. (We use the weaker sethood assumption ` ( ~P A i^i Fin ) e. _V ` because this theorem also implies that ` A ` is a set if ` ~P A i^i Fin ` is.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem infpwfidom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cin (cpw A2) cfn) cvv) (wbr A2 (cin (cpw A2) cfn) cdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac8a . If the power set of a set has a choice function, then the set is numerable. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem dfac8alem {y1 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class y1 f2) (F: class g2) (G: class y1 f2 g2):
  $ wceq F (crecs G) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt f2 cvv (cfv (cdif A2 (crn (cv f2))) (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) (wi (wex g2 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv y1) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cv y1))) (cpw A2))) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd))) $;

--| Numeration theorem: every set with a choice function on its power set is numerable. With AC, this reduces to the statement that every set is numerable. Similar to Theorem 10.3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 84. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem dfac8a {y1 h: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wex h (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv y1) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv h)) (cv y1))) (cpw A2))) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd))) $;

--| The well-ordering theorem: every numerable set is well-orderable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem dfac8b {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wex x3 (wwe A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac8c . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2013.)
theorem dfac8clem {z f2 s1 r a b: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class z f2 a b) (F: class s1 r a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt s1 (cdif A2 (csn c0)) (crio a (wral b (wn (wbr (cv b) (cv a) (cv r))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wex r (wwe (cuni A2) (cv r))) (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) A2))) $;

--| If the union of a set is well-orderable, then the set has a choice function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem dfac8c {z f2 r: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class z f2):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wex r (wwe (cuni A2) (cv r))) (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) A2))) $;

--| A proof of the well-ordering theorem ~ weth , an Axiom of Choice equivalent, restricted to sets dominated by some ordinal (in particular finite sets and countable sets), proven in ZF without AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem ac10ct {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrex y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) cdom) con0) (wex x3 (wwe A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| A set is numerable iff it can be well-ordered. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem ween {r: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wex r (wwe A2 (cv r))) $;

--| A version of ~ ac5b with the choice as a hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem ac5num {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cuni A2) (cdm ccrd)) (wn (wcel c0 A2))) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 (cuni A2) (cv f2)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) A2))) $;

--| If a set is dominated by an ordinal, then it is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem ondomen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| A set dominated by a numerable set is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem numdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| A subset of a numerable set is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ssnum (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| All subsets of the ordinals are numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem onssnum (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 con0)) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Indirect strong induction on the cardinality of a finite or numerable set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem indcardi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch th: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (T: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cdm ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr R T cdom) (wal y1 (wi (wbr S R csdm) ch))) ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq R T) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Reverse closure for the choice set predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acnrcl (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Equality theorem for the choice set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acneq (A2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 C) (wceq (wacn A2) (wacn C)) $;

--| The property of being a choice set of length ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem isacn {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2) (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel A2 W)) (wb (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wral f2 (wex g2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) A2)) (co (cdif (cpw X) (csn c0)) A2 cmap))) $;

--| The property of being a choice set of length ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acni {x3 g2: setvar} (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wf A2 (cdif (cpw X) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv x3) F)) A2)) $;

--| The property of being a choice set of length ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acni2 {x3 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wral x3 (wa (wss B2 X) (wne B2 c0)) A2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf A2 X (cv g2)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| The property of being a choice set of length ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acni3 {x3 y1 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 g2) (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph X) A2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf A2 X (cv g2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Construct a mapping satisfying the consequent of ~ isacn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acnlem {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 f2) (V: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) A2)) (wex g2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) A2)) $;

--| A well-orderable set has choice sequences of every length. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem numacn (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wcel X (cdm ccrd)) (wcel X (wacn A2))) $;

--| Every set has finite choice sequences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem finacn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq (wacn A2) cvv) $;

--| A set with long choice sequences also has shorter choice sequences, where "shorter" here means the new index set is dominated by the old index set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acndom (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wi (wcel X (wacn B2)) (wcel X (wacn A2))) $;

--| A set ` X ` which has choice sequences on it of length ` ~P X ` is well-orderable (and hence has choice sequences of every length). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acnnum (X: class):
  $ wb (wcel X (wacn (cpw X))) (wcel X (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The class of choice sets of length ` A ` is a cardinal invariant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acnen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wceq (wacn A2) (wacn B2)) $;

--| A set smaller than one with choice sequences of length ` A ` also has choice sequences of length ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acndom2 (A2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cdom) (wi (wcel Y (wacn A2)) (wcel X (wacn A2))) $;

--| The class of sets with choice sequences of length ` A ` is a cardinal invariant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acnen2 (A2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (wbr X Y cen) (wb (wcel X (wacn A2)) (wcel Y (wacn A2))) $;

--| A version of ~ fodom that doesn't require the Axiom of Choice ~ ax-ac . If ` A ` has choice sequences of length ` B ` , then any surjection from ` A ` to ` B ` can be inverted to an injection the other way. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem fodomacn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (wacn B2)) (wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| A version of ~ fodom that doesn't require the Axiom of Choice ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fodomnum (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| A surjection maps numerable sets to numerable sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem fonum (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wfo A2 B2 F)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| A surjection maps numerable sets to numerable sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem numwdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr B2 A2 cwdom)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Onto functions define dominance when a finite number of choices need to be made. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Feb-2015.)
theorem fodomfi2 (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 cfn) (wfo A2 B2 F)) (wbr B2 A2 cdom) $;

--| Weak dominance agrees with normal for finite left sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem wdomfil (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel X cfn) (wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wbr X Y cdom)) $;

--| Any infinite well-orderable set is equinumerous to its set of finite subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem infpwfien (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wbr (cin (cpw A2) cfn) A2 cen) $;

--| The set of finite intersections of an infinite well-orderable set is equinumerous to the set itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem inffien (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wbr (cfv A2 cfi) A2 cen) $;

--| Weak dominance agrees with normal for numerable right sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem wdomnumr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cwdom) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| The aleph function is a function on the class of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem alephfnon:
  $ wfn cale con0 $;

--| The first infinite cardinal number, discovered by Georg Cantor in 1873, has the same size as the set of natural numbers ` _om ` (and under our particular definition is also equal to it). In the literature, the argument of the aleph function is often written as a subscript, and the first aleph is written ` aleph `_0. Exercise 3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91. Also Definition 12(i) of [Suppes] p. 228. From Mosh&eacute Machover, _Set Theory, Logic, and Their Limitations_, p. 95: "Aleph ... the first letter in the Hebrew alphabet ... is also the first letter of the Hebrew word ... (_einsoph_, meaning infinity), which is a cabbalistic appellation of the deity. The notation is due to Cantor, who was deeply interested in mysticism." (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem aleph0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cale) com $;

--| Value of the aleph function at a limit ordinal. Definition 12(iii) of [Suppes] p. 91. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem alephlim {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wlim A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cale) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) cale))) $;

--| Value of the aleph function at a successor ordinal. Definition 12(ii) of [Suppes] p. 91. Here we express the successor aleph in terms of the Hartogs function ~ df-har , which gives the smallest ordinal that strictly dominates its argument (or the supremum of all ordinals that are dominated by the argument). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cfv (cfv A2 cale) char)) $;

--| An aleph is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem alephon (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cale) con0 $;

--| Every aleph is a cardinal number. Theorem 65 of [Suppes] p. 229. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephcard (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cale) ccrd) (cfv A2 cale) $;

--| No cardinal can be sandwiched between an aleph and its successor aleph. Theorem 67 of [Suppes] p. 229. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephnbtwn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cfv B2 ccrd) B2) (wn (wa (wcel (cfv A2 cale) B2) (wcel B2 (cfv (csuc A2) cale)))) $;

--| No set has equinumerosity between an aleph and its successor aleph. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephnbtwn2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wa (wbr (cfv A2 cale) B2 csdm) (wbr B2 (cfv (csuc A2) cale) csdm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alephordi . (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephordilem1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) (cfv (csuc A2) cale) csdm) $;

--| Strict ordering property of the aleph function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephordi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale) csdm)) $;

--| Ordering property of the aleph function. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale) csdm)) $;

--| Ordering property of the aleph function. Theorem 8A(a) of [Enderton] p. 213 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephord2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale))) $;

--| Ordering property of the aleph function. Theorem 66 of [Suppes] p. 229. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2003.)
theorem alephord2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale))) $;

--| Ordering property of the aleph function. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephord3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale))) $;

--| A set dominated by an aleph is strictly dominated by its successor aleph and vice-versa. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephsucdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 con0) (wb (wbr A2 (cfv B2 cale) cdom) (wbr A2 (cfv (csuc B2) cale) csdm)) $;

--| An alternate representation of a successor aleph. The aleph function is the function obtained from the ~ hartogs function by transfinite recursion, starting from ` _om ` . Using this theorem we could define the aleph function with ` { z e. On | z ~<_ x } ` in place of ` |^| { z e. On | x ~< z } ` in ~ df-aleph . (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephsuc2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale) cdom) con0)) $;

--| Relationship between inclusion of ordinal numbers and dominance of infinite initial ordinals. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 23-Oct-2009.)
theorem alephdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale) cdom)) $;

--| Every aleph is greater than or equal to the set of natural numbers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephgeom (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 con0) (wss com (cfv A2 cale)) $;

--| Every aleph is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephislim (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 con0) (wlim (cfv A2 cale)) $;

--| The aleph function is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem aleph11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The aleph function is a one-to-one mapping from the ordinals to the infinite cardinals. See also ~ alephf1ALT . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephf1:
  $ wf1 con0 con0 cale $;

--| If an ordinal is smaller than an initial ordinal, it is strictly dominated by it. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem alephsdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 cale)) (wbr A2 (cfv B2 cale) csdm)) $;

--| A dominated initial ordinal is included. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Oct-2009.)
theorem alephdom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wb (wss (cfv A2 cale) B2) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) B2 cdom)) $;

--| The argument of the aleph function is less than or equal to its value. Exercise 2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91. (Later, in ~ alephfp2 , we will that equality can sometimes hold.) (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wss A2 (cfv A2 cale)) $;

--| Given any transfinite cardinal number ` A ` , there is exactly one aleph that is equal to it. Here we compute that aleph _explicitly_. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem cardaleph {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss com A2) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2)) (wceq A2 (cfv (cint (crab x3 (wss A2 (cfv (cv x3) cale)) con0)) cale)) $;

--| Every transfinite cardinal is an aleph and vice-versa. Theorem 8A(b) of [Enderton] p. 213 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2003.)
theorem cardalephex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss com A2) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cfv (cv x3) cale)) con0)) $;

--| An infinite numerable set is equinumerous to an infinite initial ordinal. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 23-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infenaleph {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) (crn cale)) $;

--| Two ways to express the property of being a transfinite cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.)
theorem isinfcard (A2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wss com A2) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2)) (wcel A2 (crn cale)) $;

--| Two ways to express the property of being a cardinal number. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.)
theorem iscard3 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) (wcel A2 (cun com (crn cale))) $;

--| Two ways to express the class of all cardinal numbers, which consists of the finite ordinals in ` _om ` plus the transfinite alephs. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2004.)
theorem cardnum {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3))) (cun com (crn cale)) $;

--| An infinite initial ordinal is characterized by the property of being initial - that is, it is a subset of any dominating ordinal. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 29-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem alephinit {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wss com A2)) (wb (wcel A2 (crn cale)) (wral x3 (wi (wbr A2 (cv x3) cdom) (wss A2 (cv x3))) con0)) $;

--| The union of the image of a mapping to cardinals is a cardinal. Proposition 11.16 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2004.)
theorem carduniima (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wf A2 (cun com (crn cale)) F) (wcel (cuni (cima F A2)) (cun com (crn cale)))) $;

--| If a mapping to cardinals has an infinite value, then the union of its image is an infinite cardinal. Corollary 11.17 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2004.)
theorem cardinfima {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wa (wf A2 (cun com (crn cale)) F) (wrex x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (crn cale)) A2)) (wcel (cuni (cima F A2)) (crn cale))) $;

--| Aleph is an order isomorphism of the class of ordinal numbers onto the class of infinite cardinals. Definition 10.27 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem alephiso {x3: setvar}:
  $ wiso con0 (cab x3 (wa (wss com (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3)))) cep cep cale $;

--| The class of all transfinite cardinal numbers (the range of the aleph function) is a proper class. Proposition 10.26 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 90. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephprc:
  $ wn (wcel (crn cale) cvv) $;

--| The class of transfinite cardinals (the range of the aleph function) is a subclass of the class of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephsson:
  $ wss (crn cale) con0 $;

--| The union of the class of transfinite cardinals (the range of the aleph function) is the class of ordinal numbers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2003.)
theorem unialeph:
  $ wceq (cuni (crn cale)) con0 $;

--| The aleph function is strictly monotone. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Mar-2013.)
theorem alephsmo:
  $ wsmo cale $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ alephf1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Mar-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem alephf1ALT:
  $ wf1 con0 con0 cale $;

--| Lemma for ~ alephfp . (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2004.)
theorem alephfplem1 (H: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg cale com) com) $ >
  $ wcel (cfv c0 H) (crn cale) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alephfp . (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2004.)
theorem alephfplem2 (w: setvar) (H: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg cale com) com) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv w) com) (wceq (cfv (csuc (cv w)) H) (cfv (cfv (cv w) H) cale)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alephfp . (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2004.)
theorem alephfplem3 {v: setvar} (H: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg cale com) com) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv v) com) (wcel (cfv (cv v) H) (crn cale)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alephfp . (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2004.)
theorem alephfplem4 (H: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg cale com) com) $ >
  $ wcel (cuni (cima H com)) (crn cale) $;

--| The aleph function has a fixed point. Similar to Proposition 11.18 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 104, except that we construct an actual example of a fixed point rather than just showing its existence. See ~ alephfp2 for an abbreviated version just showing existence. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephfp (H: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg cale com) com) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cuni (cima H com)) cale) (cuni (cima H com)) $;

--| The aleph function has at least one fixed point. Proposition 11.18 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 104. See ~ alephfp for an actual example of a fixed point. Compare the inequality ~ alephle that holds in general. Note that if ` x ` is a fixed point, then ` aleph `` aleph `` aleph `` ` ... ` aleph `` x = x ` . (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephfp2 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cale) (cv x3)) con0 $;

--| An alternate way to express the value of the aleph function: it is the least infinite cardinal different from all values at smaller arguments. Definition of aleph in [Enderton] p. 212 and definition of aleph in [BellMachover] p. 490 . (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephval3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 cale) (cint (cab x3 (w3a (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3)) (wss com (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) cale))) A2))))) $;

--| The power set of an aleph is not strictly dominated by the successor aleph. (The Generalized Continuum Hypothesis says they are equinumerous, see ~ gch3 or ~ gchaleph2 .) The transposed form ~ alephsucpw cannot be proven without the AC, and is in fact equivalent to it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem alephsucpw2 (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) (cfv (csuc A2) cale) csdm) $;

--| Power rule for cardinal arithmetic. Theorem 11.21 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 106. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2015.)
theorem mappwen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com B2 cdom)) (wa (wbr c2o A2 cdom) (wbr A2 (cpw B2) cdom))) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmap) (cpw B2) cen) $;

--| A finite totally ordered set has a unique order isomorphism to a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem finnisoeu {f2: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 R) (wcel A2 cfn)) (weu f2 (wiso (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cep R (cv f2))) $;

--| Countability of a countable union of finite sets with a strict (not globally well) order fulfilling the choice role. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem iunfictbso (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr A2 com cdom) (wss A2 cfn) (wor (cuni A2) B2)) (wbr (cuni A2) com cdom) $;

--| Wff for an abbreviation of the axiom of choice.
term wac: wff;

--| The expression ` CHOICE ` will be used as a readable shorthand for any form of the axiom of choice; all concrete forms are long, cryptic, have dummy variables, or all three, making it useful to have a short name. Similar to the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49. There is a slight problem with taking the exact form of ~ ax-ac as our definition, because the equivalence to more standard forms ( ~ dfac2 ) requires the Axiom of Regularity, which we often try to avoid. Thus, we take the first of the "textbook forms" as the definition and derive the form of ~ ax-ac itself as ~ dfac0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_ac {x3 f2: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex f2 (wa (wss (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wfn (cv f2) (cdm (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice ~ ax-ac . The proof uses neither AC nor the Axiom of Regularity. The right-hand side expresses our AC with the fewest number of different variables. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem aceq1 {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wb (wex y1 (wral z (wral w (wreu v (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv u)) (wcel (cv v) (cv u))) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex x3 (wal z (wb (wex x3 (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))))))))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The proof uses neither AC nor the Axiom of Regularity. The right-hand side is our original ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem aceq0 {x3 y1 z w v u t: setvar}:
  $ wb (wex y1 (wral z (wral w (wreu v (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv u)) (wcel (cv v) (cv u))) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex v (wal u (wb (wex t (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv t))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv t)) (wcel (cv t) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))))))))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The proof uses neither AC nor the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem aceq2 {y1 z w v u: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wral z (wral w (wreu v (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv u)) (wcel (cv v) (cv u))) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wreu w (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv v)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v))) (cv y1)) (cv z))) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac3 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem aceq3lem {x3 y1 z w u f2: setvar} (F: class x3 y1 z w u f2):
  $ wceq F (cmpt w (cdm (cv y1)) (cfv (cab u (wbr (cv w) (cv u) (cv y1))) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3)))) (wex f2 (wa (wss (cv f2) (cv y1)) (wfn (cv f2) (cdm (cv y1))))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The left-hand side is defined as the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49. The right-hand side is the Axiom of Choice of [TakeutiZaring] p. 83. The proof does not depend on AC. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem dfac3 {x3 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The right-hand side is Axiom AC of [BellMachover] p. 488. The proof does not depend on AC. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem dfac4 {x3 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac5 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2004.)
theorem dfac5lem1 {w v g2: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cxp (csn (cv w)) (cv w)) (cv y1)))) (weu g2 (wa (wcel (cv g2) (cv w)) (wcel (cop (cv w) (cv g2)) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac5 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2004.)
theorem dfac5lem2 {w u t h: setvar} (A2: class u t h) (g2: setvar):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wa (wne (cv u) c0) (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cv t))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cop (cv w) (cv g2)) (cuni A2)) (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv h)) (wcel (cv g2) (cv w))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac5 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2004.)
theorem dfac5lem3 {w u t h: setvar} (A2: class u t h):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wa (wne (cv u) c0) (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cv t))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cxp (csn (cv w)) (cv w)) A2) (wa (wne (cv w) c0) (wcel (cv w) (cv h))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac5 . (Contributed by NM, 11-Apr-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 23-Jun-2025.)
theorem dfac5lem4 {x3 y1 z w v u t h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v u t h) (A2: class v u t h):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wa (wne (cv u) c0) (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cv t))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wb ph (wal x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac5 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2004.)
theorem dfac5lem5 {x3 y1 z w v u t f2 h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v u t f2 h) (A2: class v u t f2 h) (B2: class x3 y1 v u t h):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wa (wne (cv u) c0) (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cv t))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wb ph (wal x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cin (cuni A2) (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wral w (wi (wne (cv w) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv w) (cv f2)) (cv w))) (cv h))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The right-hand side is Theorem 6M(4) of [Enderton] p. 151 and asserts that given a family of mutually disjoint nonempty sets, a set exists containing exactly one member from each set in the family. The proof does not depend on AC. (Contributed by NM, 11-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dfac5 {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Our Axiom of Choice (in the form of ~ ac3 ) implies the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49. The proof uses neither AC nor the Axiom of Regularity. See ~ dfac2b for the converse (which does use the Axiom of Regularity). (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem dfac2a {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wreu w (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv v)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v))) (cv y1)) (cv z))) (cv x3)))) wac $;

--| Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49 implies our Axiom of Choice (in the form of ~ ac3 ). The proof does not make use of AC. Note that the Axiom of Regularity is used by the proof. Specifically, ~ elneq and ~ preleq that are referenced in the proof each make use of Regularity for their derivations. (The reverse implication can be derived without using Regularity; see ~ dfac2a .) (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jun-2022.)
theorem dfac2b {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wi wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wreu w (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv v)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v))) (cv y1)) (cv z))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49 corresponds to our Axiom of Choice (in the form of ~ ac3 ). The proof does not make use of AC, but the Axiom of Regularity is used (by applying ~ dfac2b ). (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jun-2022.)
theorem dfac2 {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wreu w (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv v)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v))) (cv y1)) (cv z))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Equivalence of the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49 and our Axiom of Choice (in the form of ~ ac2 ). The proof does not depend on AC but does depend on the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dfac7 {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wral z (wral w (wreu v (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv u)) (wcel (cv v) (cv u))) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice. The proof uses the Axiom of Regularity. The right-hand side is our original ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dfac0 {x3 y1 z w v u t: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex v (wal u (wb (wex t (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv t))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv t)) (wcel (cv t) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))))) $;

--| Equivalence of two versions of the Axiom of Choice ~ ax-ac . The proof uses the Axiom of Regularity. The right-hand side expresses our AC with the fewest number of different variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dfac1 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex x3 (wal z (wb (wex x3 (wa (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))))))))) $;

--| A proof of the equivalency of the well-ordering theorem ~ weth and the axiom of choice ~ ac7 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem dfac8 {x3 r: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex r (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) $;

--| Equivalence of the axiom of choice with a statement related to ~ ac9 ; definition AC3 of [Schechter] p. 139. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem dfac9 {x3 f2: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal f2 (wi (wa (wfun (cv f2)) (wnel c0 (crn (cv f2)))) (wne (cixp x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) c0))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice equivalent: the cardinality function measures every set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem dfac10:
  $ wb wac (wceq (cdm ccrd) cvv) $;

--| Axiom of Choice equivalent: every set is equinumerous to an ordinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem dfac10c {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cen) con0)) $;

--| Axiom of Choice equivalent: every set is equinumerous to an ordinal (quantifier-free short cryptic version alluded to in ~ df-ac ). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem dfac10b:
  $ wb wac (wceq (cima cen con0) cvv) $;

--| A choice equivalent: every set has choice sets of every length. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acacni (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa wac (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (wacn A2) cvv) $;

--| A choice equivalent: every set has choice sets of every length. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem dfacacn {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wceq (wacn (cv x3)) cvv)) $;

--| The axiom of choice holds iff every set has choice sequences as long as itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfac13 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wcel (cv x3) (wacn (cv x3)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac12 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12lem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (C F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw (cfv (cfv A2 cr1) char)) con0 F) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cfv (cdm (cv x3)) cr1) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv x3)) (cuni (cdm (cv x3)))) (co (co (csuc (cuni (crn (cuni (crn (cv x3)))))) (cfv (cv y1) crnk) comu) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (csuc (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) (cv x3))) coa) (cfv (cima (ccom (ccnv (coi (crn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) cep)) (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) (cv y1)) F))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccnv (coi (crn (cfv (cuni C) G)) cep)) (cfv (cuni C) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C G) (cmpt y1 (cfv C cr1) (cif (wceq C (cuni C)) (co (co (csuc (cuni (crn (cuni (cima G C))))) (cfv (cv y1) crnk) comu) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (csuc (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) G)) coa) (cfv (cima H (cv y1)) F)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac12 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12lem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (C F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw (cfv (cfv A2 cr1) char)) con0 F) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cfv (cdm (cv x3)) cr1) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv x3)) (cuni (cdm (cv x3)))) (co (co (csuc (cuni (crn (cuni (crn (cv x3)))))) (cfv (cv y1) crnk) comu) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (csuc (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) (cv x3))) coa) (cfv (cima (ccom (ccnv (coi (crn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) cep)) (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) (cv y1)) F))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccnv (coi (crn (cfv (cuni C) G)) cep)) (cfv (cuni C) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wf1 (cfv (cv z) cr1) con0 (cfv (cv z) G)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cfv C cr1) con0 (cfv C G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac12 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12lem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw (cfv (cfv A2 cr1) char)) con0 F) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cfv (cdm (cv x3)) cr1) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv x3)) (cuni (cdm (cv x3)))) (co (co (csuc (cuni (crn (cuni (crn (cv x3)))))) (cfv (cv y1) crnk) comu) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (csuc (cfv (cv y1) crnk)) (cv x3))) coa) (cfv (cima (ccom (ccnv (coi (crn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) cep)) (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) (cv y1)) F))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The axiom of choice holds iff every ordinal has a well-orderable powerset. This version of ~ dfac12 does not assume the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12r {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb (wral x3 (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) (cdm ccrd)) con0) (wss (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Equivalence of ~ dfac12 and ~ dfac12a , without using Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12k {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wral x3 (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) (cdm ccrd)) con0) (wral y1 (wcel (cpw (cfv (cv y1) cale)) (cdm ccrd)) con0) $;

--| The axiom of choice holds iff every ordinal has a well-orderable powerset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12a {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wral x3 (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) (cdm ccrd)) con0) $;

--| The axiom of choice holds iff every aleph has a well-orderable powerset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2015.)
theorem dfac12 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wral x3 (wcel (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cale)) (cdm ccrd)) con0) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, 1 => 2. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem1 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (ps: wff y1 z w):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w ph (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z ps (cv x3))))) (wal x3 (wi (wral z (wral w ph (cv x3)) (cv x3)) (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) ps) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem2 {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (x3: setvar):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wral z (wi ph (weu w (wcel (cv w) (cin (cv z) (cv y1))))) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wral z (wi ph (weu w (wcel (cv w) (cin (cv z) (cv y1))))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. The right-hand side is part of the hypothesis of 4. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem3 {w v: setvar} (x3 z: setvar):
  $ wb (wne (cdif (cv z) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv z))))) c0) (wrex v (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wn (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv w))))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem4 {w: setvar} (x3 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wne (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cin (cdif (cv z) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv z))))) (cv w)) c0) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem5 {w: setvar} (x3 z: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wne (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cin (cdif (cv z) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv z))))) (cdif (cv w) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv w)))))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 4 => 1. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem6 {z w v: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (x3: setvar) (A2: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi ph (wceq A2 c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wral z (wrex v (wral w (wi ph (wn (wcel (cv v) A2))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) (cv x3)) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 4 => 1. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem7 {z w v: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wn (wrex z (wral v (wrex w (wa (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv w)))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4 1 <=> 4. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem8 {y1 z w: setvar} (ps: wff y1 z w) (u: setvar):
  $ wb (wi (wn (wrex z (wral w ps (cv z)) (cv u))) (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (weu w (wcel (cv w) (cin (cv z) (cv y1))))) (cv u)))) (wo (wrex z (wral w ps (cv z)) (cv u)) (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv u))) (wral z (weu w (wcel (cv w) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv u))))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem9 {x3 z w u t: setvar} (A2: class x3 u t):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cdif (cv t) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv t)))))) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) A2) A2 $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem10 {x3 y1 z w u t h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w u t) (A2: class x3 u t):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cdif (cv t) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv t)))))) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wal h (wi (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv h)) (cv h)) (wex y1 (wral z ph (cv h))))) (wex y1 (wral z ph A2)) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem11 {x3 z u t: setvar} (A2: class x3 u t):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cdif (cv t) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv t)))))) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cuni A2)) (cdif (cv z) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv z)))))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 3 => 4. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2004.)
theorem kmlem12 {x3 z v u t: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class x3 u t):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cdif (cv t) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv t)))))) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wral z (wne (cdif (cv z) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv z))))) c0) (cv x3)) (wi (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1))))) A2) (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cin (cv y1) (cuni A2)))))) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4 1 <=> 4. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem13 {x3 y1 z w v u t: setvar} (A2: class x3 u t):
  $ wceq A2 (cab u (wrex t (wceq (cv u) (cdif (cv t) (cuni (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv t)))))) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) (wal x3 (wi (wn (wrex z (wral v (wrex w (wa (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv w)))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1))))) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 5 <=> 4. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem14 {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z w v u):
  $ wb ph (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wne (cv y1) (cv v))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v)))) $ >
  $ wb ps (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))))) $ >
  $ wb ch (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wb (wrex z (wral v (wrex w (wa (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv w)))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) (cv x3)) (wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) ph))))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4, part of 5 <=> 4. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem15 {x3 y1 z v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z v) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z v u):
  $ wb ph (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wne (cv y1) (cv v))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v)))) $ >
  $ wb ps (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))))) $ >
  $ wb ch (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wb (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) ch) (wal z (wex v (wal u (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) ps)))) $;

--| Lemma for 5-quantifier AC of Kurt Maes, Th. 4 5 <=> 4. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2004.)
theorem kmlem16 {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z w v u):
  $ wb ph (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wne (cv y1) (cv v))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v)))) $ >
  $ wb ps (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))))) $ >
  $ wb ch (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wb (wo (wrex z (wral v (wrex w (wa (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv w)))) (cv x3)) (cv z)) (cv x3)) (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) ch))) (wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wo (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) ph) (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) ps)))))) $;

--| Equivalence of the Axiom of Choice and Maes' AC ~ ackm . The proof consists of lemmas ~ kmlem1 through ~ kmlem16 and this final theorem. AC is not used for the proof. Note: bypassing the first step (i.e., replacing ~ dfac5 with ~ biid ) establishes the AC equivalence shown by Maes' writeup. The left-hand-side AC shown here was chosen because it is shorter to display. (Contributed by NM, 13-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dfackm {x3 y1 z v u: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wo (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wn (wceq (cv y1) (cv v)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v))))) (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))))))) $;

--| Cardinal addition dominates union. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2023.)
theorem undjudom (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) (cdju A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| Equinumerosity of a disjoint union and a union of two disjoint sets. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2007.)
theorem endjudisj (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (cun A2 B2) cen) $;

--| Disjoint unions of equinumerous sets are equinumerous. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djuen (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr C D cen)) (wbr (cdju A2 C) (cdju B2 D) cen) $;

--| Disjoint union is equinumerous to union for disjoint sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 19-Aug-2023.)
theorem djuenun (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr C D cen) (wceq (cin B2 D) c0)) (wbr (cdju A2 C) (cun B2 D) cen) $;

--| Cardinal addition with cardinal one (which is the same as ordinal one). Used in proof of Theorem 6J of [Enderton] p. 143. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem dju1en (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wn (wcel A2 A2))) (wbr (cdju A2 c1o) (csuc A2) cen) $;

--| Adding and subtracting one gives back the original cardinality. Similar to ~ pncan for cardinalities. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 20-Aug-2023.)
theorem dju1dif (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 (cdju A2 c1o))) (wbr (cdif (cdju A2 c1o) (csn B2)) A2 cen) $;

--| 1+1=2 for cardinal number addition, derived from ~ pm54.43 as promised. Theorem *110.643 of _Principia Mathematica_, vol. II, p. 86, which adds the remark, "The above proposition is occasionally useful." Whitehead and Russell define cardinal addition on collections of all sets equinumerous to 1 and 2 (which for us are proper classes unless we restrict them as in ~ karden ), but after applying definitions, our theorem is equivalent. Because we use a disjoint union for cardinal addition (as explained in the comment at the top of this section), we use ` ~~ ` instead of =. See ~ dju1p1e2ALT for a shorter proof that doesn't use ~ pm54.43 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2007.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dju1p1e2:
  $ wbr (cdju c1o c1o) c2o cen $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ dju1p1e2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dju1p1e2ALT:
  $ wbr (cdju c1o c1o) c2o cen $;

--| Cardinal addition with cardinal zero (the empty set). Part (a1) of proof of Theorem 6J of [Enderton] p. 143. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem dju0en (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cdju A2 c0) A2 cen) $;

--| Two times a cardinal number. Exercise 4.56(g) of [Mendelson] p. 258. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xp2dju (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cxp c2o A2) (cdju A2 A2) $;

--| Commutative law for cardinal addition. Exercise 4.56(c) of [Mendelson] p. 258. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djucomen (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (cdju B2 A2) cen) $;

--| Associative law for cardinal addition. Exercise 4.56(c) of [Mendelson] p. 258. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djuassen (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wbr (cdju (cdju A2 B2) C) (cdju A2 (cdju B2 C)) cen) $;

--| Cardinal multiplication distributes over cardinal addition. Theorem 6I(3) of [Enderton] p. 142. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem xpdjuen (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wbr (cxp A2 (cdju B2 C)) (cdju (cxp A2 B2) (cxp A2 C)) cen) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for cardinal arithmetic. Theorem 6I(4) of [Enderton] p. 142. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem mapdjuen (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C X)) (wbr (co A2 (cdju B2 C) cmap) (cxp (co A2 B2 cmap) (co A2 C cmap)) cen) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for cardinal arithmetic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem pwdjuen (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr (cpw (cdju A2 B2)) (cxp (cpw A2) (cpw B2)) cen) $;

--| Ordering law for cardinal addition. Exercise 4.56(f) of [Mendelson] p. 258. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 1-Sep-2023.)
theorem djudom1 (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wcel C V)) (wbr (cdju A2 C) (cdju B2 C) cdom) $;

--| Ordering law for cardinal addition. Theorem 6L(a) of [Enderton] p. 149. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djudom2 (A2 B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wcel C V)) (wbr (cdju C A2) (cdju C B2) cdom) $;

--| A set is dominated by its disjoint union with another. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djudoml (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wbr A2 (cdju A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| Cartesian product dominates disjoint union for sets with cardinality greater than 1. Similar to Proposition 10.36 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem djuxpdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr c1o B2 csdm)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| The disjoint union of two finite sets is finite. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2004.)
theorem djufi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com csdm) (wbr B2 com csdm)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) com csdm) $;

--| Any partition of omega into two pieces (which may be disjoint) contains an infinite subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2013.)
theorem cdainflem (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cun A2 B2) com cen) (wo (wbr A2 com cen) (wbr B2 com cen)) $;

--| A set is infinite iff the cardinal sum with itself is infinite. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djuinf (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr com (cdju A2 A2) cdom) $;

--| An infinite set is equinumerous to itself added with one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem infdju1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr (cdju A2 c1o) A2 cen) $;

--| The sum of a powerset with itself is equipotent to the successor powerset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem pwdju1 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cdju (cpw A2) (cpw A2)) (cpw (cdju A2 c1o)) cen) $;

--| If the natural numbers inject into ` A ` , then ` ~P A ` is idempotent under cardinal sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem pwdjuidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr (cdju (cpw A2) (cpw A2)) (cpw A2) cen) $;

--| If ` A ` is idempotent under cardinal sum and ` B ` is dominated by the power set of ` A ` , then so is the cardinal sum of ` A ` and ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem djulepw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr (cdju A2 A2) A2 cen) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cdom)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (cpw A2) cdom) $;

--| The cardinal and ordinal sums are always equinumerous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 7-Sep-2023.)
theorem onadju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wbr (co A2 B2 coa) (cdju A2 B2) cen) $;

--| The cardinal sum is equinumerous to an ordinal sum of the cardinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardadju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (co (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd) coa) cen) $;

--| The disjoint union of two numerable sets is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem djunum (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wcel (cdju A2 B2) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The union of two numerable sets is numerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem unnum (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The cardinal and ordinal sums of finite ordinals are equal. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-rep , see ~ nnadjuALT . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 2-Jul-2024.)
theorem nnadju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (cfv (cdju A2 B2) ccrd) (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ nnadju using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nnadjuALT (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (cfv (cdju A2 B2) ccrd) (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| The disjoint union of finite sets is equinumerous to the ordinal sum of the cardinalities of those sets. (Contributed by BTernaryTau, 3-Jul-2024.)
theorem ficardadju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (co (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd) coa) cen) $;

--| The cardinality of the union of disjoint, finite sets is the ordinal sum of their cardinalities. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 5-Jun-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 3-Jul-2024.)
theorem ficardun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) ccrd) (co (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd) coa)) $;

--| The cardinality of the union of finite sets is at most the ordinal sum of their cardinalities. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 3-Jul-2024.)
theorem ficardun2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wss (cfv (cun A2 B2) ccrd) (co (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd) coa)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ domtriom . This is the equinumerosity version of the algebraic identity ` sum_ k e. n ( 2 ^ k ) = ( 2 ^ n ) - 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2013.)
theorem pwsdompw {k n: setvar} (B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv n) com) (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) B2) (cpw (cv k)) cen) (csuc (cv n)))) (wbr (ciun k (cv n) (cfv (cv k) B2)) (cfv (cv n) B2) csdm) $;

--| The union of two countable sets is countable. (Contributed by FL, 25-Aug-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem unctb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr B2 com cdom)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) com cdom) $;

--| Absorption law for addition to an infinite cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infdjuabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) A2 cen) $;

--| An infinite set is equinumerous to its union with a smaller one. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infunabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr (cun A2 B2) A2 cen) $;

--| The sum of two cardinal numbers is their maximum, if one of them is infinite. Proposition 10.41 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infdju (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wbr (cdju A2 B2) (cun A2 B2) cen) $;

--| The cardinality of an infinite set does not change after subtracting a strictly smaller one. Example in [Enderton] p. 164. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infdif (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) (wbr (cdif A2 B2) A2 cen) $;

--| Cardinality ordering for an infinite class difference. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infdif2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wb (wbr (cdif A2 B2) B2 cdom) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| Dominance law for multiplication with an infinite cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infxpdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr (cxp A2 B2) A2 cdom) $;

--| Absorption law for multiplication with an infinite cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infxpabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wa (wne B2 c0) (wbr B2 A2 cdom))) (wbr (cxp A2 B2) A2 cen) $;

--| The union of two sets that are strictly dominated by the infinite set ` X ` is also dominated by ` X ` . This version of ~ infunsdom assumes additionally that ` A ` is the smaller of the two. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem infunsdom1 (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com X cdom)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 X csdm))) (wbr (cun A2 B2) X csdm) $;

--| The union of two sets that are strictly dominated by the infinite set ` X ` is also strictly dominated by ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem infunsdom (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com X cdom)) (wa (wbr A2 X csdm) (wbr B2 X csdm))) (wbr (cun A2 B2) X csdm) $;

--| Absorption law for multiplication with an infinite cardinal. Equivalent to Proposition 10.41 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2015.)
theorem infxp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wa (wcel B2 (cdm ccrd)) (wne B2 c0))) (wbr (cxp A2 B2) (cun A2 B2) cen) $;

--| A property of dominance over a powerset, and a main lemma for ~ gchac . Similar to Lemma 2.3 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 420. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem pwdjudom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cpw (cdju A2 A2)) (cdju A2 B2) cdom) (wbr (cpw A2) B2 cdom) $;

--| Every infinite set has an equinumerous proper subset, proved without AC or Infinity. Exercise 7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 91. See also ~ infpssALT . (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem infpss {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen))) $;

--| An exponentiation law for infinite cardinals. Similar to Lemma 6.2 of [Jech] p. 43. Although this version of ~ infmap avoids the axiom of choice, it requires the powerset of an infinite set to be well-orderable and so is usually not applicable. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem infmap2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmap) (cdm ccrd))) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmap) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cen))) cen) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij2lem1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss (cpw A2) (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 B2)) (wceq (cin B2 (csuc A2)) (cin B2 A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wceq (cin B2 (csuc A2)) (cun (csn A2) (cin B2 A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem4 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel (csn A2) (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Jul-2022.)
theorem ackbij1lem5 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (cpw (csuc A2)) ccrd) (co (cfv (cpw A2) ccrd) (cfv (cpw A2) ccrd) coa)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem6 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv (ciun y1 A2 (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem8 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) F) (cfv (cpw A2) ccrd)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem9 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) F) (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) coa)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem10 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wf (cin (cpw com) cfn) com F $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem11 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem12 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem13 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 F) c0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem14 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) F) (csuc (cfv A2 F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem15 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (c: setvar):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn))) (w3a (wcel (cv c) com) (wcel (cv c) A2) (wn (wcel (cv c) B2)))) (wn (wceq (cfv (cin A2 (csuc (cv c))) F) (cfv (cin B2 (csuc (cv c))) F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem16 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wcel B2 (cin (cpw com) cfn))) (wi (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem17 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wf1 (cin (cpw com) cfn) com F $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1lem18 {x3 y1 b: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (cpw com) cfn)) (wrex b (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (csuc (cfv A2 F))) (cin (cpw com) cfn)) $;

--| The Ackermann bijection, part 1: each natural number can be uniquely coded in binary as a finite set of natural numbers and conversely. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wf1o (cin (cpw com) cfn) com F $;

--| The Ackermann bijection, part 1b: the bijection from ~ ackbij1 restricts naturally to the powers of particular naturals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij1b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cima F (cpw A2)) (cfv (cpw A2) ccrd)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij2lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cpw (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv (cima (cv x3) (cv y1)) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wf1o (cfv A2 cr1) (cfv (cfv A2 cr1) ccrd) (cfv A2 (crdg G c0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij2lem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cpw (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv (cima (cv x3) (cv y1)) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss (cfv A2 (crdg G c0)) (cfv (csuc A2) (crdg G c0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ackbij2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.)
theorem ackbij2lem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cpw (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv (cima (cv x3) (cv y1)) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss B2 A2)) (wss (cfv B2 (crdg G c0)) (cfv A2 (crdg G c0))) $;

--| The Ackermann bijection, part 2: hereditarily finite sets can be represented by recursive binary notation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.)
theorem ackbij2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F G H: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw com) cfn) (cfv (ciun y1 (cv x3) (cxp (csn (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1)))) ccrd)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (cmpt y1 (cpw (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv (cima (cv x3) (cv y1)) F))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cuni (cima (crdg G c0) com)) $ >
  $ wf1o chf com H $;

--| The set of hereditarily finite sets is countable. See ~ ackbij2 for an explicit bijection that works without Infinity. See also ~ hfomALT . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 18-Nov-2014.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfom:
  $ wbr chf com cen $;

--| A set is countable iff its collection of finite intersections is countable. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fictb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (cfv A2 cfi) com cdom)) $;

--| A lemma used to simplify cofinality computations, showing the existence of the cardinal of an unbounded subset of a set ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.) Avoid ~ ax-11 . (Revised by BTernaryTau, 25-Jul-2025.)
theorem cflem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) ccrd)) (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cv w)) (cv y1)) A2))))) $;

--| Value of the cofinality function. Definition B of Saharon Shelah, _Cardinal Arithmetic_ (1994), p. xxx (Roman numeral 30). The cofinality of an ordinal number ` A ` is the cardinality (size) of the smallest unbounded subset ` y ` of the ordinal number. Unbounded means that for every member of ` A ` , there is a member of ` y ` that is at least as large. Cofinality is a measure of how "reachable from below" an ordinal is. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cfval {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) (cint (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) ccrd)) (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cv w)) (cv y1)) A2))))))) $;

--| Cofinality is a function on the class of ordinal numbers to the class of cardinal numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cff:
  $ wf con0 con0 ccf $;

--| An upper bound on cofinality. (Contributed by NM, 25-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cfub {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfv A2 ccf) (cint (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) ccrd)) (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wss A2 (cuni (cv y1)))))))) $;

--| Value of the cofinality function at a limit ordinal. Part of Definition of cofinality of [Enderton] p. 257. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-2004.)
theorem cflm {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wlim A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) (cint (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) ccrd)) (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wceq A2 (cuni (cv y1))))))))) $;

--| Value of the cofinality function at 0. Exercise 2 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 102. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2004.)
theorem cf0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 ccf) c0 $;

--| Cofinality is a cardinal number. Proposition 11.11 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 103. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cardcf (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccf) ccrd) (cfv A2 ccf) $;

--| Cofinality is bounded by the cardinality of its argument. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cflecard (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfv A2 ccf) (cfv A2 ccrd) $;

--| Cofinality is bounded by its argument. Exercise 1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 102. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cfle (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfv A2 ccf) A2 $;

--| The cofinality of any set is an ordinal (although it only makes sense when ` A ` is an ordinal). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) Avoid ~ ax-pow and ~ ax-un . (Revised by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem cfon (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 ccf) con0 $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ cfon as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cfonOLD (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 ccf) con0 $;

--| Only the ordinal zero has cofinality zero. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfeq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) c0) (wceq A2 c0)) $;

--| Value of the cofinality function at a successor ordinal. Exercise 3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 102. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) ccf) c1o) $;

--| There is always a map from ` ( cf `` A ) ` to ` A ` (this is a stronger condition than the definition, which only presupposes a map from some ` y ~~ ( cf `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cff1 {z w f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex f2 (wa (wf1 (cfv A2 ccf) A2 (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) (cfv A2 ccf)) A2))) $;

--| If there is a cofinal map from ` B ` to ` A ` , then ` B ` is at least ` ( cf `` A ) ` . This theorem and ~ cff1 motivate the picture of ` ( cf `` A ) ` as the greatest lower bound of the domain of cofinal maps into ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfflb {z w f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wex f2 (wa (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) B2) A2))) (wss (cfv A2 ccf) B2)) $;

--| Another expression for the cofinality function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfval2 {x3 z w: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) (ciin x3 (crab x3 (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cv w)) (cv x3)) A2) (cpw A2)) (cfv (cv x3) ccrd))) $;

--| A simpler expression for the cofinality predicate, at a limit ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem coflim {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wlim A2) (wss B2 A2)) (wb (wceq (cuni B2) A2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) A2)) $;

--| Another expression for the cofinality function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cflim3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) (ciin x3 (crab x3 (wceq (cuni (cv x3)) A2) (cpw A2)) (cfv (cv x3) ccrd))) $;

--| The cofinality function is a limit ordinal iff its argument is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cflim2 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wlim A2) (wlim (cfv A2 ccf)) $;

--| Value of the cofinality function at omega (the set of natural numbers). Exercise 4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 102. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2015.)
theorem cfom:
  $ wceq (cfv com ccf) com $;

--| There is a cofinal subset of ` A ` of cardinality ` ( cf `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem cfss {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wex x3 (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 ccf) cen) (wceq (cuni (cv x3)) A2))) $;

--| Any cofinal subset of ` A ` is at least as large as ` ( cf `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem cfslb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wlim A2) (wss B2 A2) (wceq (cuni B2) A2)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccf) B2 cdom) $;

--| Any subset of ` A ` smaller than its cofinality has union less than ` A ` . (This is the contrapositive to ~ cfslb .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem cfslbn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wlim A2) (wss B2 A2) (wbr B2 (cfv A2 ccf) csdm)) (wcel (cuni B2) A2) $;

--| Any small collection of small subsets of ` A ` cannot have union ` A ` , where "small" means smaller than the cofinality. This is a stronger version of ~ cfslb . This is a common application of cofinality: under AC, ` ( aleph `` 1 ) ` is regular, so it is not a countable union of countable sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem cfslb2n {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wlim A2) (wral x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 ccf) csdm)) B2)) (wi (wbr B2 (cfv A2 ccf) csdm) (wne (cuni B2) A2)) $;

--| Any cofinal map implies the existence of a strictly monotone cofinal map with a domain no larger than the original. Proposition 11.7 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 101. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.)
theorem cofsmo {x3 y1 z w v f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class g2) (C: class x3 y1 z w f2 g2) (K: class x3 z f2 g2) (O: class y1 w f2):
  $ wceq C (crab y1 (wral w (wcel (cfv (cv w) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) (cv y1)) B2) $ >
  $ wceq K (cint (crab x3 (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi C cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (word A2) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wex f2 (wa (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) B2) A2))) (wrex x3 (wex g2 (w3a (wf (cv x3) A2 (cv g2)) (wsmo (cv g2)) (wral z (wrex v (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv v) (cv g2))) (cv x3)) A2))) (csuc B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cfsmo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfsmolem {z w t f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class w g2) (G: class t g2):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cvv (cun (cfv (cdm (cv z)) (cv g2)) (ciun t (cdm (cv z)) (csuc (cfv (cv t) (cv z)))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crecs F) (cfv A2 ccf)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex f2 (w3a (wf (cfv A2 ccf) A2 (cv f2)) (wsmo (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) (cfv A2 ccf)) A2))) $;

--| The map in ~ cff1 can be assumed to be a strictly monotone ordinal function without loss of generality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2013.)
theorem cfsmo {z w f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wex f2 (w3a (wf (cfv A2 ccf) A2 (cv f2)) (wsmo (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) (cfv A2 ccf)) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cfcof , showing subset relation in one direction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2014.)
theorem cfcoflem {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wex f2 (w3a (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)) (wsmo (cv f2)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) B2) A2))) (wss (cfv A2 ccf) (cfv B2 ccf))) $;

--| If there is a cofinal map from ` B ` to ` A ` and another from ` C ` to ` A ` , then there is also a cofinal map from ` C ` to ` B ` . Proposition 11.9 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 102. A limited form of transitivity for the "cof" relation. This is really a lemma for ~ cfcof . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2013.)
theorem coftr {x3 y1 z w t f2 g2 h n s1: setvar} (A2: class y1 t h n) (B2: class z) (C: class x3 y1 n) (H: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 n):
  $ wceq H (cmpt t C (cint (crab n (wss (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) B2))) $ >
  $ wi (wex f2 (w3a (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)) (wsmo (cv f2)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) B2) A2))) (wi (wex g2 (wa (wf C A2 (cv g2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv g2))) C) A2))) (wex h (wa (wf C B2 (cv h)) (wral s1 (wrex w (wss (cv s1) (cfv (cv w) (cv h))) C) B2)))) $;

--| If there is a cofinal map from ` A ` to ` B ` , then they have the same cofinality. This was used as Definition 11.1 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 100, who defines an equivalence relation cof ` ( A , B ) ` and defines our ` cf ( B ) ` as the minimum ` B ` such that cof ` ( A , B ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.)
theorem cfcof {z w f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wi (wex f2 (w3a (wf B2 A2 (cv f2)) (wsmo (cv f2)) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2))) B2) A2))) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) (cfv B2 ccf))) $;

--| The cofinality function is idempotent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem cfidm (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccf) ccf) (cfv A2 ccf) $;

--| The cofinality of a limit aleph is the same as the cofinality of its argument, so if ` ( aleph `` A ) < A ` , then ` ( aleph `` A ) ` is singular. Conversely, if ` ( aleph `` A ) ` is regular (i.e. weakly inaccessible), then ` ( aleph `` A ) = A ` , so ` A ` has to be rather large (see ~ alephfp ). Proposition 11.13 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 103. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem alephsing (A2: class):
  $ wi (wlim A2) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cale) ccf) (cfv A2 ccf)) $;

--| The range of a single-step monotone function from ` _om ` into a partially ordered set is a chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Nov-2014.)
theorem sornom {a: setvar} (R F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F com) (wral a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (csuc (cv a)) F) R) (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (csuc (cv a)) F))) com) (wpo (crn F) R)) (wor (crn F) R) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of Ia-finite sets.
term cfin1a: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of II-finite sets.
term cfin2: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of IV-finite sets.
term cfin4: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of III-finite sets.
term cfin3: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of V-finite sets.
term cfin5: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of VI-finite sets.
term cfin6: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of VII-finite sets.
term cfin7: class;

--| A set is Ia-finite iff it is not the union of two I-infinite sets. Equivalent to definition Ia of [Levy58] p. 2. A I-infinite Ia-finite set is also known as an amorphous set. This is the second of Levy's eight definitions of finite set. Levy's I-finite is equivalent to our ~ df-fin and not repeated here. These eight definitions are equivalent with Choice but strictly decreasing in strength in models where Choice fails; conversely, they provide a series of increasingly stronger notions of infiniteness. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin1a {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin1a (cab x3 (wral y1 (wo (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) cfn)) (cpw (cv x3)))) $;

--| A set is II-finite (Tarski finite) iff every nonempty chain of subsets contains a maximum element. Definition II of [Levy58] p. 2. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin2 (cab x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wne (cv y1) c0) (wor (cv y1) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv y1)) (cv y1))) (cpw (cpw (cv x3))))) $;

--| A set is IV-finite (Dedekind finite) iff it has no equinumerous proper subset. Definition IV of [Levy58] p. 3. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin4 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin4 (cab x3 (wn (wex y1 (wa (wpss (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cen))))) $;

--| A set is III-finite (weakly Dedekind finite) iff its power set is Dedekind finite. Definition III of [Levy58] p. 2. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin3 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin3 (cab x3 (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) cfin4)) $;

--| A set is V-finite iff it behaves finitely under ` |_| ` . Definition V of [Levy58] p. 3. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin5 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin5 (cab x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wbr (cv x3) (cdju (cv x3) (cv x3)) csdm))) $;

--| A set is VI-finite iff it behaves finitely under ` X. ` . Definition VI of [Levy58] p. 4. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin6 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin6 (cab x3 (wo (wbr (cv x3) c2o csdm) (wbr (cv x3) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)) csdm))) $;

--| A set is VII-finite iff it cannot be infinitely well-ordered. Equivalent to definition VII of [Levy58] p. 4. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_fin7 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin7 (cab x3 (wn (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cen) (cdif con0 com)))) $;

--| Definition of a Ia-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin1a {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin1a) (wral y1 (wo (wcel (cv y1) cfn) (wcel (cdif A2 (cv y1)) cfn)) (cpw A2))) $;

--| Property of a Ia-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin1ai (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfin1a) (wss X A2)) (wo (wcel X cfn) (wcel (cdif A2 X) cfn)) $;

--| Definition of a II-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin2 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin2) (wral y1 (wi (wa (wne (cv y1) c0) (wor (cv y1) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv y1)) (cv y1))) (cpw (cpw A2)))) $;

--| Property of a II-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cfin2) (wss B2 (cpw A2))) (wa (wne B2 c0) (wor B2 crpss))) (wcel (cuni B2) B2) $;

--| Definition of a III-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin3 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfin3) (wcel (cpw A2) cfin4) $;

--| Definition of a IV-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin4 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin4) (wn (wex y1 (wa (wpss (cv y1) A2) (wbr (cv y1) A2 cen))))) $;

--| Infer that a set is IV-infinite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin4i (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wpss X A2) (wbr X A2 cen)) (wn (wcel A2 cfin4)) $;

--| Definition of a V-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin5 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfin5) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wbr A2 (cdju A2 A2) csdm)) $;

--| Definition of a VI-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin6 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfin6) (wo (wbr A2 c2o csdm) (wbr A2 (cxp A2 A2) csdm)) $;

--| Definition of a VII-finite set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin7 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin7) (wn (wrex y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) cen) (cdif con0 com)))) $;

--| A set with less than two elements has 0 or 1. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem sdom2en01 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 c2o csdm) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wbr A2 c1o cen)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infpssr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem infpssrlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crdg (ccnv F) C) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 G) C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infpssr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem infpssrlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G M: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crdg (ccnv F) C) com) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M com) (wceq (cfv (csuc M) G) (cfv (cfv M G) (ccnv F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infpssr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem infpssrlem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crdg (ccnv F) C) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf com A2 G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infpssr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem infpssrlem4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crdg (ccnv F) C) com) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel M com) (wcel N M)) (wne (cfv M G) (cfv N G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infpssr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem infpssrlem5 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G V: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (crdg (ccnv F) C) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr com A2 cdom)) $;

--| Dedekind infinity implies existence of a denumerable subset: take a single point witnessing the proper subset relation and iterate the embedding. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem infpssr (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wpss X A2) (wbr X A2 cen)) (wbr com A2 cdom) $;

--| Dedekind finite is a cardinal property. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin4en1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wi (wcel A2 cfin4) (wcel B2 cfin4)) $;

--| Dedekind finite sets have Dedekind finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem ssfin4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfin4) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cfin4) $;

--| A set dominated by a Dedekind finite set is Dedekind finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem domfin4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfin4) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wcel B2 cfin4) $;

--| ` _om ` is Dedekind infinite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem ominf4:
  $ wn (wcel com cfin4) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ infpss , shorter but requiring Replacement ( ~ ax-rep ). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem infpssALT {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wex x3 (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen))) $;

--| Alternate definition of IV-finite sets: they lack a denumerable subset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin4_2 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin4) (wn (wbr com A2 cdom))) $;

--| Alternate definition of IV-finite sets: they are strictly dominated by their successors. (Thus, the proper subset referred to in ~ isfin4 can be assumed to be only a singleton smaller than the original.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem isfin4p1 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfin4) (wbr A2 (cdju A2 c1o) csdm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin2-2 . The componentwise complement of a nonempty collection of sets is nonempty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin23lem7 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 (cpw A2)) (wne B2 c0)) (wne (crab x3 (wcel (cdif A2 (cv x3)) B2) (cpw A2)) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin2-2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin23lem11 {x3 z w v c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z w c) (ps: wff z w v c) (ch: wff x3 w v c) (th: wff x3 z v c) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) (cdif A2 (cv v))) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv v) A2)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wss B2 (cpw A2)) (wi (wrex x3 (wral w (wn ph) (crab c (wcel (cdif A2 (cv c)) B2) (cpw A2))) (crab c (wcel (cdif A2 (cv c)) B2) (cpw A2))) (wrex z (wral v (wn ps) B2) B2)) $;

--| A II-finite set contains minimal elements for every nonempty chain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin2i2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cfin2) (wss B2 (cpw A2))) (wa (wne B2 c0) (wor B2 crpss))) (wcel (cint B2) B2) $;

--| ` Fin2 ` expressed in terms of minimal elements. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin2_2 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin2) (wral y1 (wi (wa (wne (cv y1) c0) (wor (cv y1) crpss)) (wcel (cint (cv y1)) (cv y1))) (cpw (cpw A2)))) $;

--| A subset of a II-finite set is II-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem ssfin2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfin2) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cfin2) $;

--| II-finiteness is a cardinal property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem enfin2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wi (wcel A2 cfin2) (wcel B2 cfin2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . In a class of ordinals, each element is fully identified by those of its predecessors which also belong to the class. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem24 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (word A2) (wss B2 A2)) (wa (wcel C B2) (wcel D B2))) (wb (wceq (cin C B2) (cin D B2)) (wceq C D)) $;

--| In a chain of finite sets, dominance and subset coincide. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.)
theorem fincssdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) (wo (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 A2))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wss A2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . In a chain of finite sets, equinumerosity is equivalent to equality. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem25 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) (wo (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 A2))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23lem22 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem26 {i j: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss S com) (wn (wcel S cfn))) (wcel (cv i) com)) (wrex j (wbr (cin (cv j) S) (cv i) cen) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23lem22 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem23 {i j: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss S com) (wn (wcel S cfn))) (wcel (cv i) com)) (wreu j (wbr (cin (cv j) S) (cv i) cen) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 but could be used elsewhere if we find a good name for it. Explicit construction of a bijection (actually an isomorphism, see ~ fin23lem27 ) between an infinite subset of ` _om ` and ` _om ` itself. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem22 {i j: setvar} (C: class i j) (S: class):
  $ wceq C (cmpt i com (crio j (wbr (cin (cv j) S) (cv i) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S com) (wn (wcel S cfn))) (wf1o com S C) $;

--| The mapping constructed in ~ fin23lem22 is in fact an isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem27 {i j: setvar} (C: class i j) (S: class):
  $ wceq C (cmpt i com (crio j (wbr (cin (cv j) S) (cv i) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S com) (wn (wcel S cfn))) (wiso com S cep cep C) $;

--| Property of a III-finite set (descending sequence version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem isfin3ds {x3 f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class) (F V: class x3 f2 g2 a b):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral b (wss (cfv (csuc (cv b)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv b) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 F) (wral f2 (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv f2))) (crn (cv f2)))) (co (cpw A2) com cmap))) $;

--| A subset of a III-finite set is III-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.)
theorem ssfin3ds {g2 a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class g2 a b):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral b (wss (cfv (csuc (cv b)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv b) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 F) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 F) $;

--| The beginning of the proof that every II-finite set (every chain of subsets has a maximal element) is III-finite (has no denumerable collection of subsets). This first section is dedicated to the construction of ` U ` and its intersection. First, the value of ` U ` at a successor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem12 {u t i: setvar} (A2 U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) U) (cif (wceq (cin (cfv A2 (cv t)) (cfv A2 U)) c0) (cfv A2 U) (cin (cfv A2 (cv t)) (cfv A2 U)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Each step of ` U ` is a decrease. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem13 {u t i: setvar} (A2 U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss (cfv (csuc A2) U) (cfv A2 U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` U ` will never evolve to an empty set if it did not start with one. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem14 {u t i: setvar} (A2 U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wne (cuni (crn (cv t))) c0)) (wne (cfv A2 U) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` U ` is a monotone function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem15 {u t i: setvar} (A2: class t) (B2: class u t i) (U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wss B2 A2)) (wss (cfv A2 U) (cfv B2 U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` U ` ranges over the original set; in particular ` ran U ` is a set, although we do not assume here that ` U ` is. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem16 {u t i: setvar} (U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (crn U)) (cuni (crn (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The first set in ` U ` to see an input set is either contained in it or disjoint from it. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem19 {u t i: setvar} (A2 U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wo (wss (cfv (csuc A2) U) (cfv A2 (cv t))) (wceq (cin (cfv (csuc A2) U) (cfv A2 (cv t))) c0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` X ` is either contained in or disjoint from all input sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem20 {u t i: setvar} (A2 U: class t):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wo (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv A2 (cv t))) (wceq (cin (cint (crn U)) (cfv A2 (cv t))) c0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . By ? Fin3DS ? , ` U ` achieves its minimum ( ` X ` in the synopsis above, but we will not be assigning a symbol here). TODO: Fix comment; math symbol Fin3DS does not exist. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin23lem17 {x3 u t g2 i a: setvar} (U: class x3 t g2) (F: class x3 u g2 i) (V: class x3 u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cuni (crn (cv t))) F) (wf1 com V (cv t))) (wcel (cint (crn U)) (crn U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` X ` is not empty. We only need here that ` t ` has at least one set in its range besides ` (/) ` ; the much stronger hypothesis here will serve as our induction hypothesis though. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem fin23lem21 {x3 u t g2 i a: setvar} (U: class x3 t g2) (F: class x3 u g2 i) (V: class x3 u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cuni (crn (cv t))) F) (wf1 com V (cv t))) (wne (cint (crn U)) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is also one-to-one. This preserves the induction invariant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem28 {x3 z w v u t g2 i a: setvar} (P: class v u t g2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t g2 i a) (R: class x3 z w t g2) (U: class x3 w t g2) (F: class x3 z w v u g2 i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wf1 com cvv (cv t)) (wf1 com cvv Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is also one-to-one. This preserves the induction invariant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem28_b {x3 z w v u t i a: setvar} (P: class v u t i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t i a) (R: class x3 z w t) (U: class x3 w t) (F: class x3 z w v u i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab w (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv w)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wf1 com cvv (cv t)) (wf1 com cvv Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is built from the same elements as the previous sequence. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem29 {x3 z w v u t g2 i a: setvar} (P: class v u t g2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t g2 i a) (R: class x3 z w t g2) (U: class x3 w t g2) (F: class x3 z w v u g2 i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wss (cuni (crn Z)) (cuni (crn (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is built from the same elements as the previous sequence. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem29_b {x3 z w v u t i a: setvar} (P: class v u t i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t i a) (R: class x3 z w t) (U: class x3 w t) (F: class x3 z w v u i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab w (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv w)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wss (cuni (crn Z)) (cuni (crn (cv t))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is disjoint from the common set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem30 {x3 z w v u t g2 i a: setvar} (P: class v u t g2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t g2 i a) (R: class x3 z w t g2) (U: class x3 w t g2) (F: class x3 z w v u g2 i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wfun (cv t)) (wceq (cin (cuni (crn Z)) (cint (crn U))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is disjoint from the common set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem30_b {x3 z w v u t i a: setvar} (P: class v u t i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t i a) (R: class x3 z w t) (U: class x3 w t) (F: class x3 z w v u i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab w (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv w)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wfun (cv t)) (wceq (cin (cuni (crn Z)) (cint (crn U))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is has a strictly smaller range than the previous sequence. This will be iterated to build an unbounded chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem31 {x3 z w v u t g2 i a: setvar} (P: class v u t g2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t g2 i a) (R: class x3 z w t g2) (U: class x3 w t g2) (F: class x3 z w v u g2 i) (G: class z w v u i) (V Z: class x3 z w v u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 com V (cv t)) (wcel G F) (wss (cuni (crn (cv t))) G)) (wpss (cuni (crn Z)) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The residual is has a strictly smaller range than the previous sequence. This will be iterated to build an unbounded chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem31_b {x3 z w v u t i a: setvar} (P: class v u t i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t i a) (R: class x3 z w t) (U: class x3 w t) (F: class x3 z w v u i) (G: class z v u i) (V Z: class x3 z w v u t i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab w (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv w)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf1 com V (cv t)) (wcel G F) (wss (cuni (crn (cv t))) G)) (wpss (cuni (crn Z)) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Wrap the previous construction into a function to hide the hypotheses. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem32 {x3 z w v u t f2 g2 i a b: setvar} (P: class v u t f2 g2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t f2 g2 i a b) (R: class x3 z w t f2 g2) (U: class x3 w t f2 g2) (F: class x3 z w v u f2 g2 i b) (G: class z w v u i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t g2 i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G F) (wex f2 (wal b (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv b)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv b))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv b) (cv f2))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv f2)))) (cuni (crn (cv b)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Wrap the previous construction into a function to hide the hypotheses. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem32_b {x3 z w v u t f2 i a b: setvar} (P: class v u t f2 i) (Q: class x3 z w v u t f2 i a b) (R: class x3 z w t f2) (U: class x3 w t f2) (F: class x3 z w v u f2 i b) (G: class z v u i) (Z: class x3 z w v u t i):
  $ wceq U (cseqom (cmpo i u com cvv (cif (wceq (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)) c0) (cv u) (cin (cfv (cv i) (cv t)) (cv u)))) (cuni (crn (cv t)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cab w (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv w)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab v (wss (cint (crn U)) (cfv (cv v) (cv t))) com) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) P) (cv w) cen) P)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt w com (crio x3 (wbr (cin (cv x3) (cdif com P)) (cv w) cen) (cdif com P))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cif (wcel P cfn) (ccom (cv t) R) (ccom (cmpt z P (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cv t)) (cint (crn U)))) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G F) (wex f2 (wal b (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv b)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv b))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv b) (cv f2))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv f2)))) (cuni (crn (cv b)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Discharge hypotheses. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem33 {x3 f2 g2 a b: setvar} (F: class x3 f2 g2 b) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G F) (wex f2 (wal b (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv b)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv b))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv b) (cv f2))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv b) (cv f2)))) (cuni (crn (cv b)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Establish induction invariants on ` Y ` which parameterizes our contradictory chain of subsets. In this section, ` h ` is the hypothetically assumed family of subsets, ` g ` is the ground set, and ` i ` is the induction function constructed in the previous section. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem34 {x3 g2 h i j a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 g2 h i) (A2: class x3 g2 h i) (F: class x3 g2 h i j) (G: class) (Y: class x3 g2 h i):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 com cvv (cv h)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (crn (cv h))) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal j (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv j)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv j))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv j) (cv i))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv j) (cv i)))) (cuni (crn (cv j))))))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres (crdg (cv i) (cv h)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 com)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv A2 Y)) (wss (cuni (crn (cfv A2 Y))) G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Strict order property of ` Y ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem35 {x3 g2 h i j a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 g2 h i) (A2: class x3 g2 h i) (F: class x3 g2 h i j) (G: class) (Y: class x3 g2 h i):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 com cvv (cv h)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (crn (cv h))) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal j (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv j)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv j))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv j) (cv i))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv j) (cv i)))) (cuni (crn (cv j))))))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres (crdg (cv i) (cv h)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 com)) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (csuc A2) Y))) (cuni (crn (cfv A2 Y)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Weak order property of ` Y ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem36 {x3 g2 h i j a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 g2 h i) (A2: class x3 g2 h i) (B2 F: class x3 g2 h i j) (G: class) (Y: class x3 g2 h i):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 com cvv (cv h)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (crn (cv h))) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal j (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv j)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv j))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv j) (cv i))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv j) (cv i)))) (cuni (crn (cv j))))))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres (crdg (cv i) (cv h)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wa (wss B2 A2) ph)) (wss (cuni (crn (cfv A2 Y))) (cuni (crn (cfv B2 Y)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . The contradictory chain has no minimum. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin23lem38 {x3 g2 h i j a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 g2 h i) (F: class x3 g2 h i j b) (G: class) (Y: class x3 g2 h i):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 com cvv (cv h)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (crn (cv h))) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal j (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv j)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv j))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv j) (cv i))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv j) (cv i)))) (cuni (crn (cv j))))))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres (crdg (cv i) (cv h)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn (cmpt b com (cuni (crn (cfv (cv b) Y)))))) (crn (cmpt b com (cuni (crn (cfv (cv b) Y))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . Thus, we have that ` g ` could not have been in ` F ` after all. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem39 {x3 g2 h i j a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 g2 h i) (F: class x3 g2 h i j) (G: class) (Y: class x3 g2 h i):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 com cvv (cv h)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (crn (cv h))) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal j (wi (wa (wf1 com cvv (cv j)) (wss (cuni (crn (cv j))) G)) (wa (wf1 com cvv (cfv (cv j) (cv i))) (wpss (cuni (crn (cfv (cv j) (cv i)))) (cuni (crn (cv j))))))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres (crdg (cv i) (cv h)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel G F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . ` Fin2 ` sets satisfy the descending chain condition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem40 {x3 g2 a: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin2) (wcel A2 F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin23 . A set which satisfies the descending sequence condition must be III-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin23lem41 {x3 g2 a: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 F) (wcel A2 cfin3) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Derive weak ordering property. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wa (wss B2 A2) ph)) (wss (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Non-minimum implies that there is always another decrease. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem2 {x3 a: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 com)) (wrex a (wa (wcel A2 (cv a)) (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv a)) F) (cfv (cv a) F))) com) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Being a chain, difference sets are disjoint (one case). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wa (wcel B2 A2) ph)) (wceq (cin (cdif (cfv A2 F) (cfv (csuc A2) F)) (cdif (cfv B2 F) (cfv (csuc B2) F))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Being a chain, difference sets are disjoint. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wne A2 B2)) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com))) (wceq (cin (cdif (cfv A2 F) (cfv (csuc A2) F)) (cdif (cfv B2 F) (cfv (csuc B2) F))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . There are infinite decrease points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Each K value is nonempty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem6 {x3 y1 w v u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class v u) (S: class) (F: class v u) (G: class x3 y1 w v u) (J: class v u) (K: class w v u):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt u com (crio v (wbr (cin (cv v) S) (cv u) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (cmpt w S (cdif (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (csuc (cv w)) F))) J) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 com)) (wne (cfv A2 K) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Different K values are disjoint. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem7 {x3 y1 w v u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class v u) (B2: class y1 v u) (S: class) (F: class v u) (G: class x3 y1 w v u) (J: class v u) (K: class w v u):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt u com (crio v (wbr (cin (cv v) S) (cv u) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (cmpt w S (cdif (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (csuc (cv w)) F))) J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wne A2 B2)) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com))) (wceq (cin (cfv A2 K) (cfv B2 K)) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . K sets are subsets of the base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf32lem8 {x3 y1 w v u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class v u) (S: class) (F: class v u) (G: class x3 y1 w v u) (J: class v u) (K: class w v u):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt u com (crio v (wbr (cin (cv v) S) (cv u) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (cmpt w S (cdif (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (csuc (cv w)) F))) J) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 com)) (wss (cfv A2 K) G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Construction of the onto function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem isf32lem9 {x3 y1 w v u t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class) (F: class v u t s1) (G: class x3 y1 w v u s1) (J: class v u) (K: class w v u) (L: class y1 w v u t s1):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt u com (crio v (wbr (cin (cv v) S) (cv u) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (cmpt w S (cdif (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (csuc (cv w)) F))) J) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt t G (cio s1 (wa (wcel (cv s1) com) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv s1) K))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo G com L) $;

--| Lemma for isfin3-2 . Write in terms of weak dominance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf32lem10 {x3 y1 w v u t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class) (F: class v u t s1) (G: class x3 y1 w v u s1) (J: class v u) (K: class w v u) (L: class y1 w v u t s1) (V: class x3 y1 w v u t s1):
  $ wi ph (wf com (cpw G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wpss (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) com) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt u com (crio v (wbr (cin (cv v) S) (cv u) cen) S)) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (cmpt w S (cdif (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (csuc (cv w)) F))) J) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt t G (cio s1 (wa (wcel (cv s1) com) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv s1) K))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel G V) (wbr com G cwdom)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . Remove hypotheses from ~ isf32lem10 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf32lem11 {b: setvar} (F G: class) (V: class b):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (w3a (wf com (cpw G) F) (wral b (wss (cfv (csuc (cv b)) F) (cfv (cv b) F)) com) (wn (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F))))) (wbr com G cwdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf32lem12 {x3 g2 a: setvar} (F: class x3 a) (G: class) (V: class x3 g2 a):
  $ wceq F (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wi (wn (wbr com G cwdom)) (wcel G F)) $;

--| One half of ~ isfin3-2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jun-2015.)
theorem isfin32i (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin3) (wn (wbr com A2 cwdom)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf33lem {x3 g2 a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfin3 (cab g2 (wral a (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv a))) (crn (cv a)))) (co (cpw (cv g2)) com cmap))) $;

--| Weakly Dedekind-infinite sets are exactly those which can be mapped onto ` _om ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin3_2 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin3) (wn (wbr com A2 cwdom))) $;

--| Weakly Dedekind-infinite sets are exactly those with an ` _om ` -indexed descending chain of subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem isfin3_3 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin3) (wral f2 (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) com) (wcel (cint (crn (cv f2))) (crn (cv f2)))) (co (cpw A2) com cmap))) $;

--| Inference from ~ isfin3-3 . (This is actually a bit stronger than ~ isfin3-3 because it does not assume ` F ` is a set and does not use the Axiom of Infinity either.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin33i {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfin3) (wf com (cpw A2) F) (wral x3 (wss (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) com)) (wcel (cint (crn F)) (crn F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ compsscnv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem compsscnvlem {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cpw A2)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif A2 (cv x3)))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cpw A2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cdif A2 (cv y1)))) $;

--| Complementation on a power set lattice is an involution. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem compsscnv {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq (ccnv F) F $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf34lem1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cdif A2 X)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf34lem2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wf (cpw A2) (cpw A2) F) $;

--| Complementation is an antiautomorphism on power set lattices. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem compssiso {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wiso (cpw A2) (cpw A2) crpss (ccnv crpss) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf34lem3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss X (cpw A2))) (wceq (cima F (cima F X)) X) $;

--| Express image under of the complementation isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem compss {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq (cima F G) (crab y1 (wcel (cdif A2 (cv y1)) G) (cpw A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf34lem4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wa (wss X (cpw A2)) (wne X c0))) (wceq (cfv (cuni X) F) (cint (cima F X))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf34lem5 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wa (wss X (cpw A2)) (wne X c0))) (wceq (cfv (cint X) F) (cuni (cima F X))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem isf34lem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfin3) (wf com (cpw A2) G) (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) G) (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) G)) com)) (wcel (cuni (crn G)) (crn G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isf34lem6 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class f2):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin3) (wral f2 (wi (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv y1)) (cv f2))) com) (wcel (cuni (crn (cv f2))) (crn (cv f2)))) (co (cpw A2) com cmap))) $;

--| Inference from ~ isfin3-4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin34i {x3: setvar} (A2 G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfin3) (wf com (cpw A2) G) (wral x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) G)) com)) (wcel (cuni (crn G)) (crn G)) $;

--| Weakly Dedekind-infinite sets are exactly those with an ` _om ` -indexed ascending chain of subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin3_4 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin3) (wral f2 (wi (wral x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) (cv f2))) com) (wcel (cuni (crn (cv f2))) (crn (cv f2)))) (co (cpw A2) com cmap))) $;

--| Every I-finite set is Ia-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin11a (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 cfin1a) $;

--| Ia-finiteness is a cardinal property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem enfin1ai (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wi (wcel A2 cfin1a) (wcel B2 cfin1a)) $;

--| A set is finite in the usual sense iff the power set of its power set is Dedekind finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin1_2 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cpw (cpw A2)) cfin4) $;

--| A set is I-finite iff every system of subsets contains a maximal subset. Definition I of [Levy58] p. 2. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin1_3 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wfr (cpw A2) (ccnv crpss))) $;

--| A set is I-finite iff every system of subsets contains a minimal subset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin1_4 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wfr (cpw A2) crpss)) $;

--| Compact quantifier-free version of the standard definition ~ df-fin . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem dffin1_5:
  $ wceq cfn (cima cen com) $;

--| Every II-finite set (every chain of subsets has a maximal element) is III-finite (has no denumerable collection of subsets). The proof here is the only one I could find, from ~ http://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/fm/fm6/fm619.pdf p.94 (writeup by Tarski, credited to Kuratowski). Translated into English and modern notation, the proof proceeds as follows (variables renamed for uniqueness): Suppose for a contradiction that ` A ` is a set which is II-finite but not III-finite. For any countable sequence of distinct subsets ` T ` of ` A ` , we can form a decreasing sequence of nonempty subsets ` ( U `` T ) ` by taking finite intersections of initial segments of ` T ` while skipping over any element of ` T ` which would cause the intersection to be empty. By II-finiteness (as ~ fin2i2 ) this sequence contains its intersection, call it ` Y ` ; since by induction every subset in the sequence ` U ` is nonempty, the intersection must be nonempty. Suppose that an element ` X ` of ` T ` has nonempty intersection with ` Y ` . Thus, said element has a nonempty intersection with the corresponding element of ` U ` , therefore it was used in the construction of ` U ` and all further elements of ` U ` are subsets of ` X ` , thus ` X ` contains the ` Y ` . That is, all elements of ` X ` either contain ` Y ` or are disjoint from it. Since there are only two cases, there must exist an infinite subset of ` T ` which uniformly either contain ` Y ` or are disjoint from it. In the former case we can create an infinite set by subtracting ` Y ` from each element. In either case, call the result ` Z ` ; this is an infinite set of subsets of ` A ` , each of which is disjoint from ` Y ` and contained in the union of ` T ` ; the union of ` Z ` is strictly contained in the union of ` T ` , because only the latter is a superset of the nonempty set ` Y ` . The preceding four steps may be iterated a countable number of times starting from the assumed denumerable set of subsets to produce a denumerable sequence ` B ` of the ` T ` sets from each stage. Great caution is required to avoid ~ ax-dc here; in particular an effective version of the pigeonhole principle (for aleph-null pigeons and 2 holes) is required. Since a denumerable set of subsets is assumed to exist, we can conclude ` _om e. _V ` without the axiom. This ` B ` sequence is strictly decreasing, thus it has no minimum, contradicting the first assumption. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin23 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin2) (wcel A2 cfin3) $;

--| Every III-finite set is IV-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.)
theorem fin34 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin3) (wcel A2 cfin4) $;

--| Alternate definition of V-finite which emphasizes the idempotent behavior of V-infinite sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin5_2 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin5) (wn (wa (wne A2 c0) (wbr A2 (cdju A2 A2) cen)))) $;

--| Every IV-finite set is V-finite: if we can pack two copies of the set into itself, we can certainly leave space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem fin45 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin4) (wcel A2 cfin5) $;

--| Every V-finite set is VI-finite because multiplication dominates addition for cardinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin56 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin5) (wcel A2 cfin6) $;

--| Every I-finite set is VII-finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin17 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 cfin7) $;

--| Every VI-finite set is VII-finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin67 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin6) (wcel A2 cfin7) $;

--| A set is VII-finite iff it is non-well-orderable or finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem isfin7_2 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfin7) (wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wcel A2 cfn))) $;

--| A well-orderable set is VII-finite iff it is I-finite. Thus, even without choice, on the class of well-orderable sets all eight definitions of finite set coincide. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem fin71num (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wb (wcel A2 cfin7) (wcel A2 cfn)) $;

--| Class form of ~ isfin7-2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem dffin7_2:
  $ wceq cfin7 (cun cfn (cdif cvv (cdm ccrd))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice equivalent: the VII-finite sets are the same as I-finite sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem dfacfin7:
  $ wb wac (wceq cfin7 cfn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem1 {x3: setvar} (A2 S: class x3):
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 con0 (csuc (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (cfv A2 S) (csuc A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . The successor operation on the ordinal numbers is injective or one-to-one. Lemma 1.17 of [Schloeder] p. 2. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem2 {x3: setvar} (S: class x3):
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 con0 (csuc (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wf1 con0 con0 S $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem3 {x3: setvar} (A2 E: class x3):
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 com (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv A2 E) (co c2o A2 comu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem4 {x3: setvar} (E: class x3):
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 com (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) $ >
  $ wf1 com com E $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem5 {x3: setvar} (A2 E: class x3):
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 com (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wb (wcel A2 (crn E)) (wn (wcel (csuc A2) (crn E)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . Establish that ` _om ` can be broken into two equipollent pieces. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem6 {x3: setvar} (S E: class x3):
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 com (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 con0 (csuc (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wf1o (crn E) (cdif com (crn E)) (cres S (crn E)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . Split a III-infinite set in two pieces. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (S: class x3 y1) (E: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 com (co c2o (cv x3) comu)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 con0 (csuc (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral y1 (wo (wcel (cv y1) cfin3) (wcel (cdif A2 (cv y1)) cfin3)) (cpw A2))) (wcel A2 cfin3) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . Split a III-infinite set in two pieces. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem8 {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wo (wcel (cv x3) cfin3) (wcel (cdif A2 (cv x3)) cfin3)) (cpw A2))) (wcel A2 cfin3) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . In a chain of finite sets, initial segments are finite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem9 {b: setvar} (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wor X crpss) (wss X cfn) (wcel A2 com)) (wcel (crab b (wbr (cv b) A2 cdom) X) cfn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . A nonempty finite union of members of a chain is a member of the chain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem10 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn) (wor A2 crpss)) (wcel (cuni A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.)
theorem fin1a2lem11 {b c: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wor A2 crpss) (wss A2 cfn)) (wceq (crn (cmpt b com (cuni (crab c (wbr (cv c) (cv b) cdom) A2)))) (cun A2 (csn c0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin1a2lem12 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 (cpw B2)) (wor A2 crpss) (wn (wcel (cuni A2) A2))) (wa (wss A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0))) (wn (wcel B2 cfin3)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin1a2lem13 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 (cpw B2)) (wor A2 crpss) (wn (wcel (cuni A2) A2))) (wa (wn (wcel C cfn)) (wcel C A2))) (wn (wcel (cdif B2 C) cfin2)) $;

--| Weak theorem which skips Ia but has a trivial proof, needed to prove ~ fin1a2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin12 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 cfin2) $;

--| An II-infinite set can have an I-infinite part broken off and remain II-infinite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin1a2s {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wo (wcel (cv x3) cfn) (wcel (cdif A2 (cv x3)) cfin2)) (cpw A2))) (wcel A2 cfin2) $;

--| Every Ia-finite set is II-finite. Theorem 1 of [Levy58], p. 3. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem fin1a2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfin1a) (wcel A2 cfin2) $;

--| Function value of iterated unions. _EDITORIAL_: The iterated unions and order types of ordered sets are split out here because they could conceivably be independently useful. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem itunifval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 U) (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) A2) com)) $;

--| Functionality of the iterated union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem itunifn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wfn (cfv A2 U) com) $;

--| A zero-fold iterated union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem ituni0 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv c0 (cfv A2 U)) A2) $;

--| Successor iterated union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem itunisuc {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (cfv A2 U)) (cuni (cfv B2 (cfv A2 U))) $;

--| Each union iterate is a member of the transitive closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem itunitc1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wss (cfv B2 (cfv A2 U)) (cfv A2 ctc) $;

--| The union of all union iterates creates the transitive closure; compare ~ trcl . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem itunitc {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 ctc) (cuni (crn (cfv A2 U))) $;

--| Unwrap an iterated union from the "other end". (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem ituniiun {x3 y1 a: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (U: class x3 y1) (V: class x3 y1 a):
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (csuc B2) (cfv A2 U)) (ciun a A2 (cfv B2 (cfv (cv a) U)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Properties of the recurrent sequence of ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem7 {z: setvar} (H: class z) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 H) (cfv (cpw X) char) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Properties of the recurrent sequence of ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem8 {z: setvar} (H: class z) (X: class) (a: setvar):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv a) com) (wceq (cfv (csuc (cv a)) H) (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cfv (cv a) H))) char)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Properties of the recurrent sequence of ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem9 {z: setvar} (H: class z) (X: class) (a: setvar):
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv a) com) (wcel (cfv (cv a) H) con0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Bound the order type of a limited-cardinality set of ordinals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem1 (A2 B2 O: class):
  $ wceq O (coi A2 cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 con0) (wbr A2 B2 cwdom)) (wcel (cdm O) (cfv (cpw B2) char)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Bound the order type of a union of sets of ordinals, each of limited order type. Vaguely reminiscent of ~ unictb but use of order types allows to canonically choose the sub-bijections, removing the choice requirement. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem hsmexlem2 {a: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class a) (C: class) (F G V: class a):
  $ wceq F (coi B2 cep) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (ciun a A2 B2) cep) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel C con0) (wral a (wa (wcel B2 (cpw con0)) (wcel (cdm F) C)) A2)) (wcel (cdm G) (cfv (cpw (cxp A2 C)) char)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Clear ` I ` hypothesis and extend previous result by dominance. Note that this could be substantially strengthened, e.g., using the weak Hartogs function, but all we need here is that there be *some* dominating ordinal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem3 {a: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class a) (C: class) (D F G: class a):
  $ wceq F (coi B2 cep) $ >
  $ wceq G (coi (ciun a A2 B2) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wbr A2 D cwdom) (wcel C con0)) (wral a (wa (wcel B2 (cpw con0)) (wcel (cdm F) C)) A2)) (wcel (cdm G) (cfv (cpw (cxp D C)) char)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . The core induction, establishing bounds on the order types of iterated unions of the initial set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem4 {x3 y1 z a b c d: setvar} (S U: class x3 y1 z a b) (H: class x3 y1 z b) (O: class x3 y1 z a b c d) (X: class x3 y1 c d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab a (wral b (wbr (cv b) X cdom) (cfv (csn (cv a)) ctc)) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (cima crnk (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv d) U))) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv c) com) (wcel (cv d) S)) (wcel (cdm O) (cfv (cv c) H)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . Combining the above constraints, along with ~ itunitc and ~ tcrank , gives an effective constraint on the rank of ` S ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem5 {x3 y1 z a b c d: setvar} (S U: class x3 y1 z a b) (H: class x3 y1 z b) (O: class x3 y1 z a b c d) (X: class x3 y1 c d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab a (wral b (wbr (cv b) X cdom) (cfv (csn (cv a)) ctc)) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (cima crnk (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv d) U))) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv d) S) (wcel (cfv (cv d) crnk) (cfv (cpw (cxp com (cuni (crn H)))) char)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hsmex . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmexlem6 {x3 y1 z a b c d: setvar} (S U: class x3 y1 z a b) (H: class x3 y1 z b) (O: class x3 y1 z a b c d) (X: class x3 y1 c d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wceq H (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cfv (cpw (cxp X (cv z))) char)) (cfv (cpw X) char)) com) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cuni (cv y1))) (cv x3)) com)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab a (wral b (wbr (cv b) X cdom) (cfv (csn (cv a)) ctc)) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (cima crnk (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv d) U))) cep) $ >
  $ wcel S cvv $;

--| The collection of hereditarily size-limited well-founded sets comprise a set. The proof is that of Randall Holmes at ~ http://math.boisestate.edu/~~holmes/holmes/hereditary.pdf , with modifications to use Hartogs' theorem instead of the weak variant (inconsequentially weakening some intermediate results), and making the well-foundedness condition explicit to avoid a direct dependence on ~ ax-reg . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmex {x3 s1: setvar} (V: class x3 s1) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (crab s1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) X cdom) (cfv (csn (cv s1)) ctc)) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) cvv) $;

--| The set of hereditary size-limited sets, assuming ~ ax-reg . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmex2 {x3 s1: setvar} (V: class x3 s1) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cab s1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) X cdom) (cfv (csn (cv s1)) ctc))) cvv) $;

--| The set of hereditary size-limited sets, assuming ~ ax-reg , using strict comparison (an easy corollary by separation). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem hsmex3 {x3 s1: setvar} (V: class x3 s1) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cab s1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) X csdm) (cfv (csn (cv s1)) ctc))) cvv) $;

--| The axiom of countable choice (CC), also known as the axiom of denumerable choice. It is clearly a special case of ~ ac5 , but is weak enough that it can be proven using DC (see ~ axcc ). It is, however, strictly stronger than ZF and cannot be proven in ZF. It states that any countable collection of nonempty sets must have a choice function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
axiom ax_cc {z f2: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wbr (cv x3) com cen) (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axcc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2013.)
theorem axcc2lem {f2 g2 n: setvar} (A2: class g2) (F: class n) (G: class f2) (K: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq K (cmpt n com (cif (wceq (cfv (cv n) F) c0) (csn c0) (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n com (cxp (csn (cv n)) (cfv (cv n) K))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n com (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) (cv f2)) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wex g2 (wa (wfn (cv g2) com) (wral n (wi (wne (cfv (cv n) F) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv n) F))) com)) $;

--| A possibly more useful version of ax-cc using sequences instead of countable sets. The Axiom of Infinity is needed to prove this, and indeed this implies the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2013.)
theorem axcc2 {g2 n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wex g2 (wa (wfn (cv g2) com) (wral n (wi (wne (cfv (cv n) F) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv n) F))) com)) $;

--| A possibly more useful version of ~ ax-cc using sequences ` F ( n ) ` instead of countable sets. The Axiom of Infinity is needed to prove this, and indeed this implies the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2014.)
theorem axcc3 {f2 n: setvar} (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wbr N com cen $ >
  $ wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) N) (wral n (wi (wne F c0) (wcel (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) F)) N)) $;

--| A version of ~ axcc3 that uses wffs instead of classes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2013.)
theorem axcc4 {x3 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (ps: wff f2 n) (A2: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr N com cen $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral n (wrex x3 ph A2) N) (wex f2 (wa (wf N A2 (cv f2)) (wral n ps N))) $;

--| An ~ ax-cc equivalent: every set has choice sets of length ` _om ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem acncc:
  $ wceq (wacn com) cvv $;

--| Relax the constraint on ~ axcc4 to dominance instead of equinumerosity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2014.)
theorem axcc4dom {x3 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (ps: wff f2 n) (A2: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr N com cdom) (wral n (wrex x3 ph A2) N)) (wex f2 (wa (wf N A2 (cv f2)) (wral n ps N))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ domtriom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
theorem domtriomlem {y1 k n b: setvar} (A2: class k) (B2: class y1 k n) (C: class y1 b):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab y1 (wa (wss (cv y1) A2) (wbr (cv y1) (cpw (cv n)) cen))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpt n com (cdif (cfv (cv n) (cv b)) (ciun k (cv n) (cfv (cv k) (cv b))))) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wbr com A2 cdom) $;

--| Trichotomy of equinumerosity for ` _om ` , proven using countable choice. Equivalently, all Dedekind-finite sets (as in ~ isfin4-2 ) are finite in the usual sense and conversely. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
theorem domtriom (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr com A2 cdom) (wn (wbr A2 com csdm)) $;

--| Under countable choice, the IV-finite sets (Dedekind-finite) coincide with I-finite (finite in the usual sense) sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2015.)
theorem fin41:
  $ wceq cfin4 cfn $;

--| A nonempty set that is a subset of its union is infinite. This version is proved from ~ ax-cc . See ~ dominfac for a version proved from ~ ax-ac . The axiom of Regularity is used for this proof, via ~ inf3lem6 , and its use is necessary: otherwise the set ` A = { A } ` or ` A = { (/) , A } ` (where the second example even has nonempty well-founded part) provides a counterexample. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2013.)
theorem dominf (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wss A2 (cuni A2))) (wbr com A2 cdom) $;

--| Dependent Choice. Axiom DC1 of [Schechter] p. 149. This theorem is weaker than the Axiom of Choice but is stronger than Countable Choice. It shows the existence of a sequence whose values can only be shown to exist (but cannot be constructed explicitly) and also depend on earlier values in the sequence. Dependent choice is equivalent to the statement that every (nonempty) pruned tree has a branch. This axiom is redundant in ZFC; see ~ axdc . But ZF+DC is strictly weaker than ZF+AC, so this axiom provides for theorems that do not need the full power of AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
axiom ax_dc {y1 z f2 n: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wex y1 (wex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wss (crn (cv x3)) (cdm (cv x3)))) (wex f2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) com)) $;

--| The Axiom of Dependent Choice implies Infinity, the way we have stated it. Thus, we have _Inf+AC_ implies _DC_ and _DC_ implies _Inf_, but _AC_ does not imply _Inf_. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
theorem dcomex:
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc2 . We construct a relation ` R ` based on ` F ` such that ` x R y ` iff ` y e. ( F `` x ) ` , and show that the "function" described by ~ ax-dc can be restricted so that it is a real function (since the stated properties only show that it is the superset of a function). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2015.)
theorem axdc2lem {x3 y1 g2 h k: setvar} (A2: class k) (R: class y1 g2) (F: class k) (G: class h):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 com (cfv (cv x3) (cv h))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wf A2 (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (wa (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| An apparent strengthening of ~ ax-dc (but derived from it) which shows that there is a denumerable sequence ` g ` for any function that maps elements of a set ` A ` to nonempty subsets of ` A ` such that ` g ( x + 1 ) e. F ( g ( x ) ) ` for all ` x e. _om ` . The finitistic version of this can be proven by induction, but the infinite version requires this new axiom. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc2 {g2 k: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wf A2 (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (wa (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| The class ` S ` of finite approximations to the DC sequence is a set. (We derive here the stronger statement that ` S ` is a subset of a specific set, namely ` ~P ( _om X. A ) ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2013.) Remove unnecessary distinct variable conditions. (Revised by David Abernethy, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem axdc3lem {k n s1: setvar} (A2: class k) (C S F: class k n s1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (cab s1 (wrex n (w3a (wf (csuc (cv n)) A2 (cv s1)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv s1)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv s1)) F)) (cv n))) com)) $ >
  $ wcel S cvv $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc3 . We have constructed a "candidate set" ` S ` , which consists of all finite sequences ` s ` that satisfy our property of interest, namely ` s ( x + 1 ) e. F ( s ( x ) ) ` on its domain, but with the added constraint that ` s ( 0 ) = C ` . These sets are possible "initial segments" of the _infinite_ sequence satisfying these constraints, but we can leverage the standard ~ ax-dc (with no initial condition) to select a sequence of ever-lengthening finite sequences, namely ` ( h `` n ) : m --> A ` (for some integer ` m ` ). We let our "choice" function select a sequence whose domain is one more than the last one, and agrees with the previous one on its domain. Thus, the application of vanilla ~ ax-dc yields a sequence of sequences whose domains increase without bound, and whose union is a function which has all the properties we want. In this lemma, we show that given the sequence ` h ` , we can construct the sequence ` g ` that we are after. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc3lem2 {x3 y1 g2 h k n s1: setvar} (A2 C: class x3 y1 k) (S: class g2 h n) (F: class x3 y1 k) (G: class x3 y1 g2 h n s1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (cab s1 (wrex n (w3a (wf (csuc (cv n)) A2 (cv s1)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv s1)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv s1)) F)) (cv n))) com)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (crab y1 (wa (wceq (cdm (cv y1)) (csuc (cdm (cv x3)))) (wceq (cres (cv y1) (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) S)) $ >
  $ wi (wex h (wa (wf com S (cv h)) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv h)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv h)) G)) com))) (wex g2 (w3a (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv g2)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| Simple substitution lemma for ~ axdc3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc3lem3 {k m n s1: setvar} (A2: class k) (B2: class) (C: class k) (S: class k m n s1) (F: class k):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (cab s1 (wrex n (w3a (wf (csuc (cv n)) A2 (cv s1)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv s1)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv s1)) F)) (cv n))) com)) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel B2 S) (wrex m (w3a (wf (csuc (cv m)) A2 B2) (wceq (cfv c0 B2) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) B2) (cfv (cfv (cv k) B2) F)) (cv m))) com) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc3 . We have constructed a "candidate set" ` S ` , which consists of all finite sequences ` s ` that satisfy our property of interest, namely ` s ( x + 1 ) e. F ( s ( x ) ) ` on its domain, but with the added constraint that ` s ( 0 ) = C ` . These sets are possible "initial segments" of the _infinite_ sequence satisfying these constraints, but we can leverage the standard ~ ax-dc (with no initial condition) to select a sequence of ever-lengthening finite sequences, namely ` ( h `` n ) : m --> A ` (for some integer ` m ` ). We let our "choice" function select a sequence whose domain is one more than the last one, and agrees with the previous one on its domain. Thus, the application of vanilla ~ ax-dc yields a sequence of sequences whose domains increase without bound, and whose union is a function which has all the properties we want. In this lemma, we show that ` S ` is nonempty, and that ` G ` always maps to a nonempty subset of ` S ` , so that we can apply ~ axdc2 . See ~ axdc3lem2 for the rest of the proof. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc3lem4 {x3 y1 g2 k n s1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (C: class x3 y1) (S: class g2 n) (F: class y1) (G: class x3 y1 g2 n s1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (cab s1 (wrex n (w3a (wf (csuc (cv n)) A2 (cv s1)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv s1)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv s1)) F)) (cv n))) com)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (crab y1 (wa (wceq (cdm (cv y1)) (csuc (cdm (cv x3)))) (wceq (cres (cv y1) (cdm (cv x3))) (cv x3))) S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wf A2 (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (w3a (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv g2)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| Dependent Choice. Axiom DC1 of [Schechter] p. 149, with the addition of an initial value ` C ` . This theorem is weaker than the Axiom of Choice but is stronger than Countable Choice. It shows the existence of a sequence whose values can only be shown to exist (but cannot be constructed explicitly) and also depend on earlier values in the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc3 {g2 k: setvar} (A2 C F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wf A2 (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (w3a (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv g2)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem axdc4lem {x3 g2 k n: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 n) (F: class k) (G: class x3 g2 n):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo n x3 com A2 (cxp (csn (csuc (cv n))) (co (cv n) (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wf (cxp com A2) (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (w3a (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv g2)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| A more general version of ~ axdc3 that allows the function ` F ` to vary with ` k ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc4 {g2 k: setvar} (A2 C F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wf (cxp com A2) (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) F)) (wex g2 (w3a (wf com A2 (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv g2)) C) (wral k (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv k)) (cv g2)) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) com))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axcc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem axcclem {x3 y1 z w f2 g2 h n: setvar} (A2: class x3 g2) (F: class x3 y1 w f2 g2 n) (G: class x3 y1 w f2 h n):
  $ wceq A2 (cdif (cv x3) (csn c0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo n y1 com (cuni A2) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt w A2 (cfv (csuc (cfv (cv w) (ccnv (cv f2)))) (cv h))) $ >
  $ wi (wbr (cv x3) com cen) (wex g2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv g2)) (cv z))) (cv x3))) $;

--| Although CC can be proven trivially using ~ ac5 , we prove it here using DC. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2013.)
theorem axcc {x3 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wi (wbr (cv x3) com cen) (wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice. The Axiom of Choice (AC) is usually considered an extension of ZF set theory rather than a proper part of it. It is sometimes considered philosophically controversial because it asserts the existence of a set without telling us what the set is. ZF set theory that includes AC is called ZFC. The unpublished version given here says that given any set ` x ` , there exists a ` y ` that is a collection of unordered pairs, one pair for each nonempty member of ` x ` . One entry in the pair is the member of ` x ` , and the other entry is some arbitrary member of that member of ` x ` . See the rewritten version ~ ac3 for a more detailed explanation. Theorem ~ ac2 shows an equivalent written compactly with restricted quantifiers. This version was specifically crafted to be short when expanded to primitives. Kurt Maes' 5-quantifier version ~ ackm is slightly shorter when the biconditional of ~ ax-ac is expanded into implication and negation. In ~ axac3 we allow the constant ` CHOICE ` to represent the Axiom of Choice; this simplifies the representation of theorems like ~ gchac (the Generalized Continuum Hypothesis implies the Axiom of Choice). Standard textbook versions of AC are derived as ~ ac8 , ~ ac5 , and ~ ac7 . The Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg (among others) is used to derive our version from the standard ones; this reverse derivation is shown as Theorem ~ dfac2b . Equivalents to AC are the well-ordering theorem ~ weth and Zorn's lemma ~ zorn . See ~ ac4 for comments about stronger versions of AC. In order to avoid uses of ~ ax-reg for derivation of AC equivalents, we provide ~ ax-ac2 (due to Kurt Maes), which is equivalent to the standard AC of textbooks. The derivation of ~ ax-ac2 from ~ ax-ac is shown by Theorem ~ axac2 , and the reverse derivation by ~ axac . Therefore, new proofs should normally use ~ ax-ac2 instead. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-1996.)
axiom ax_ac {y1 z w v u t: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex v (wal u (wb (wex t (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv t))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv t)) (wcel (cv t) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice expressed with the fewest number of different variables. The penultimate step shows the logical equivalence to ~ ax-ac . (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2003.)
theorem zfac {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice equivalent. By using restricted quantifiers, we can express the Axiom of Choice with a single explicit conjunction. (If you want to figure it out, the rewritten equivalent ~ ac3 is easier to understand.) Note: ~ aceq0 shows the logical equivalence to ~ ax-ac . (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-1996.)
theorem ac2 {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wral z (wral w (wreu v (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv u)) (wcel (cv v) (cv u))) (cv y1)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv x3)) $;

--| Axiom of Choice using abbreviations. The logical equivalence to ~ ax-ac can be established by chaining ~ aceq0 and ~ aceq2 . A standard textbook version of AC is derived from this one in ~ dfac2a , and this version of AC is derived from the textbook version in ~ dfac2b , showing their logical equivalence (see ~ dfac2 ). The following sketch will help you understand this version of the axiom. Given any set ` x ` , the axiom says that there exists a ` y ` that is a collection of unordered pairs, one pair for each nonempty member of ` x ` . One entry in the pair is the member of ` x ` , and the other entry is some arbitrary member of that member of ` x ` . Using the Axiom of Regularity, we can show that ` y ` is really a set of _ordered_ pairs, very similar to the ordered pair construction ~ opthreg . The key theorem for this (used in the proof of ~ dfac2b ) is ~ preleq . With this modified definition of ordered pair, it can be seen that ` y ` is actually a choice function on the members of ` x ` . For example, suppose ` x = { { 1 , 2 } , { 1 , 3 } , { 2 , 3 , 4 } } ` . Let us try ` y = { { { 1 , 2 } , 1 } , { { 1 , 3 } , 1 } , ` ` { { 2 , 3 , 4 } , 2 } } ` . For the member (of ` x ` ) ` z = { 1 , 2 } ` , the only assignment to ` w ` and ` v ` that satisfies the axiom is ` w = 1 ` and ` v = { { 1 , 2 } , 1 } ` , so there is exactly one ` w ` as required. We verify the other two members of ` x ` similarly. Thus, ` y ` satisfies the axiom. Using our modified ordered pair definition, we can say that ` y ` corresponds to the choice function ` { <. { 1 , 2 } , 1 >. , <. { 1 , 3 } , 1 >. , ` ` <. { 2 , 3 , 4 } , 2 >. } ` . Of course other choices for ` y ` will also satisfy the axiom, for example ` y = { { { 1 , 2 } , 2 } , { { 1 , 3 } , 1 } , ` ` { { 2 , 3 , 4 } , 4 } } ` . What AC tells us is that there exists at least one such ` y ` , but it doesn't tell us which one. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-1996.)
theorem ac3 {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wreu w (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv v)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v))) (cv y1)) (cv z))) (cv x3)) $;

--| In order to avoid uses of ~ ax-reg for derivation of AC equivalents, we provide ~ ax-ac2 , which is equivalent to the standard AC of textbooks. This appears to be the shortest known equivalent to the standard AC when expressed in terms of set theory primitives. It was found by Kurt Maes as Theorem ~ ackm . We removed the leading quantifier to make it slightly shorter, since we have ~ ax-gen available. The derivation of ~ ax-ac2 from ~ ax-ac is shown by Theorem ~ axac2 , and the reverse derivation by ~ axac . Note that we use ~ ax-reg to derive ~ ax-ac from ~ ax-ac2 , but not to derive ~ ax-ac2 from ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2016.)
axiom ax_ac2 {y1 z v u: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wo (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wn (wceq (cv y1) (cv v)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v))))) (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))))) $;

--| This theorem asserts that the constant ` CHOICE ` is a theorem, thus eliminating it as a hypothesis while assuming ~ ax-ac2 as an axiom. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.) (Revised by NM, 20-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem axac3:
  $ wac $;

--| A remarkable equivalent to the Axiom of Choice that has only five quantifiers (when expanded to use only the primitive predicates ` = ` and ` e. ` and in prenex normal form), discovered and proved by Kurt Maes. This establishes a new record, reducing from 6 to 5 the largest number of quantified variables needed by any ZFC axiom. The ZF-equivalence to AC is shown by Theorem ~ dfackm . Maes found this version of AC in April 2004 (replacing a longer version, also with five quantifiers, that he found in November 2003). See Kurt Maes, "A 5-quantifier ( ` e. , = ` )-expression ZF-equivalent to the Axiom of Choice", ~ https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.0705.3162 . The original FOM posts are: ~ https://fomarchive.ugent.be/2003-November/007631.html ~ https://fomarchive.ugent.be/2003-November/007641.html . (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem ackm {x3 y1 z v u: setvar}:
  $ wal x3 (wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wo (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wn (wceq (cv y1) (cv v)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v))))) (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))))))))))) $;

--| Derive ~ ax-ac2 from ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem axac2 {x3 y1 z v u: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wex v (wal u (wo (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv x3)) (wn (wceq (cv y1) (cv v)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv v))))) (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wcel (cv u) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))))) $;

--| Derive ~ ax-ac from ~ ax-ac2 . Note that ~ ax-reg is used by the proof. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem axac {x3 y1 z w v u t: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex v (wal u (wb (wex t (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv t))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv t)) (wcel (cv t) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))) $;

--| Apply a choice equivalent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2015.)
theorem axaci {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb wac (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| All sets are well-orderable under choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardeqv:
  $ wceq (cdm ccrd) cvv $;

--| All sets are well-orderable under choice. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Feb-2015.)
theorem numth3 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Numeration theorem: any set is equinumerous to some ordinal (using AC). Theorem 10.3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 84. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2003.)
theorem numth2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) con0 $;

--| Numeration theorem: every set can be put into one-to-one correspondence with some ordinal (using AC). Theorem 10.3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 84. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jan-2015.)
theorem numth {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wex f2 (wf1o (cv x3) A2 (cv f2))) con0 $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent similar to the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2004.)
theorem ac7 {x3 f2: setvar}:
  $ wex f2 (wa (wss (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wfn (cv f2) (cdm (cv x3)))) $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent similar to the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.)
theorem ac7g {f2: setvar} (A2: class f2) (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R A2) (wex f2 (wa (wss (cv f2) R) (wfn (cv f2) (cdm R)))) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. We do not insist that ` f ` be a function. However, Theorem ~ ac5 , derived from this one, shows that this form of the axiom does imply that at least one such set ` f ` whose existence we assert is in fact a function. Axiom of Choice of [TakeutiZaring] p. 83. Takeuti and Zaring call this "weak choice" in contrast to "strong choice" ` E. F A. z ( z =/= (/) -> ( F `` z ) e. z ) ` , which asserts the existence of a universal choice function but requires second-order quantification on (proper) class variable ` F ` and thus cannot be expressed in our first-order formalization. However, it has been shown that ZF plus strong choice is a conservative extension of ZF plus weak choice. See Ulrich Felgner, "Comparison of the axioms of local and universal choice", _Fundamenta Mathematica_, 71, 43-62 (1971). Weak choice can be strengthened in a different direction to choose from a collection of proper classes; see ~ ac6s5 . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-1996.)
theorem ac4 {x3 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wex f2 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z))) (cv x3)) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice (class version). (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1997.)
theorem ac4c {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wex f2 (wral x3 (wi (wne (cv x3) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3))) A2) $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent: there exists a function ` f ` (called a choice function) with domain ` A ` that maps each nonempty member of the domain to an element of that member. Axiom AC of [BellMachover] p. 488. Note that the assertion that ` f ` be a function is not necessary; see ~ ac4 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1999.)
theorem ac5 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cv x3) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3))) A2)) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1999.)
theorem ac5b {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wne (cv x3) c0) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 (cuni A2) (cv f2)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) A2))) $;

--| A version of ~ ac6 which takes the choice as a hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem ac6num {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (V: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 (crab y1 ph B2)) (cdm ccrd)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2)) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. This is useful for proving that there exists, for example, a sequence mapping natural numbers to members of a larger set ` B ` , where ` ph ` depends on ` x ` (the natural number) and ` y ` (to specify a member of ` B ` ). A stronger version of this theorem, ~ ac6s , allows ` B ` to be a proper class. Unlike ~ ac6sg , which allows ` B ` to be a proper class and so uses the Collection principle ~ bnd2 , this needs neither ~ ax-reg nor ~ ax-inf2 . (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.) Replace hypotheses with antecedents. (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem ac6 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (V W: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2)) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. ` B ` is a collection ` B ( x ) ` of nonempty sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem ac6c4 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. ` B ` is a collection ` B ( x ) ` of nonempty sets. Remark after Theorem 10.46 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 98. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem ac6c5 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wex f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2)) $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent: the infinite Cartesian product of nonempty classes is nonempty. Axiom of Choice (second form) of [Enderton] p. 55 and its converse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2013.)
theorem ac9 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wne (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. Using the Boundedness Axiom ~ bnd2 , we derive this strong version of ~ ac6 that doesn't require ` B ` to be a set. (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2004.)
theorem ac6s {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Equivalent of Axiom of Choice. Contrapositive of ~ ac6s . (Contributed by NM, 10-Jun-2007.)
theorem ac6n {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wal f2 (wi (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex x3 ps A2))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 ph B2) A2) $;

--| Generalization of the Axiom of Choice to classes. Slightly strengthened version of ~ ac6s3 . (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem ac6s2 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Generalization of the Axiom of Choice to classes. Theorem 10.46 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2004.)
theorem ac6s3 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) A2) (wex f2 (wral x3 ps A2)) $;

--| ~ ac6s with sethood as antecedent. (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem ac6sg {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (V: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2)))) $;

--| Version of ~ ac6 with bound-variable hypothesis. (Contributed by NM, 2-Mar-2008.)
theorem ac6sf {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff x3 y1 f2) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Generalization of the Axiom of Choice to proper classes. ` B ` is a collection ` B ( x ) ` of nonempty, possible proper classes. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem ac6s4 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Generalization of the Axiom of Choice to proper classes. ` B ` is a collection ` B ( x ) ` of nonempty, possible proper classes. Remark after Theorem 10.46 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2006.)
theorem ac6s5 {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wex f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2)) $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent. Given a family ` x ` of mutually disjoint nonempty sets, there exists a set ` y ` containing exactly one member from each set in the family. Theorem 6M(4) of [Enderton] p. 151. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-2004.)
theorem ac8 {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3))) $;

--| An Axiom of Choice equivalent: the infinite Cartesian product of nonempty classes is nonempty. Axiom of Choice (second form) of [Enderton] p. 55 and its converse. This is a stronger version of the axiom in Enderton, with no existence requirement for the family of classes ` B ( x ) ` (achieved via the Collection Principle ~ cp ). (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2006.)
theorem ac9s {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) (wne (cixp x3 A2 B2) c0) $;

--| Any set is strictly dominated by some ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.)
theorem numthcor {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0) $;

--| Well-ordering theorem: any set ` A ` can be well-ordered. This is an equivalent of the Axiom of Choice. Theorem 6 of [Suppes] p. 242. First proved by Ernst Zermelo (the "Z" in ZFC) in 1904. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem weth {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wwe A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem1 {x3 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wa (wwe A2 (cv w)) (wne D c0))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) D) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem2 {x3 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wa (wwe A2 (cv w)) (wne D c0))) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) R)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem3 {x3 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 R) (wa (wcel (cv x3) con0) (wa (wwe A2 (cv w)) (wne D c0)))) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wn (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem4 {x3 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 R) (wwe A2 (cv w))) (wrex x3 (wceq D c0) con0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem5 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w) (H: class y1 z w g2):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv y1))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 (cv w)) (wcel (cv x3) con0)) (wral y1 (wne H c0) (cv x3))) (wss (cima F (cv x3)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem6 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w u f2 g2) (D: class x3 z w g2) (R: class) (F: class w) (H: class y1 z w g2):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv y1))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wpo A2 R) (wi (wa (wa (wwe A2 (cv w)) (wcel (cv x3) con0)) (wral y1 (wne H c0) (cv x3))) (wor (cima F (cv x3)) R)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zorn2 . (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2lem7 {x3 y1 z w v u f2 g2 s1 r a b: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w u f2 g2 s1 r a b) (D: class x3 z w g2 s1 r) (R: class) (F: class w) (H: class y1 z w g2):
  $ wceq F (crecs (cmpt f2 cvv (crio v (wral u (wn (wbr (cv u) (cv v) (cv w))) C) C))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (crn (cv f2))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv x3))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab z (wral g2 (wbr (cv g2) (cv z) R) (cima F (cv y1))) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wpo A2 R) (wal s1 (wi (wa (wss (cv s1) A2) (wor (cv s1) R)) (wrex a (wral r (wo (wbr (cv r) (cv a) R) (wceq (cv r) (cv a))) (cv s1)) A2)))) (wrex a (wral b (wn (wbr (cv a) (cv b) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| Zorn's Lemma of [Monk1] p. 117. This version of ~ zorn2 avoids the Axiom of Choice by assuming that ` A ` is well-orderable. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2g {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wpo A2 R) (wal w (wi (wa (wss (cv w) A2) (wor (cv w) R)) (wrex x3 (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (cv w)) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| Zorn's Lemma. If the union of every chain (with respect to inclusion) in a set belongs to the set, then the set contains a maximal element. Theorem 6M of [Enderton] p. 151. This version of ~ zorn avoids the Axiom of Choice by assuming that ` A ` is well-orderable. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorng {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wal z (wi (wa (wss (cv z) A2) (wor (cv z) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv z)) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| Variant of Zorn's lemma ~ zorng in which ` (/) ` , the union of the empty chain, is not required to be an element of ` A ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 5-Jan-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zornn0g {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wne A2 c0) (wal z (wi (w3a (wss (cv z) A2) (wne (cv z) c0) (wor (cv z) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv z)) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| Zorn's Lemma of [Monk1] p. 117. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice and states that every partially ordered set ` A ` (with an ordering relation ` R ` ) in which every totally ordered subset has an upper bound, contains at least one maximal element. The main proof consists of lemmas ~ zorn2lem1 through ~ zorn2lem7 ; this final piece mainly changes bound variables to eliminate the hypotheses of ~ zorn2lem7 . (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem zorn2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 R) (wal w (wi (wa (wss (cv w) A2) (wor (cv w) R)) (wrex x3 (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) (cv w)) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) A2) A2) $;

--| Zorn's Lemma. If the union of every chain (with respect to inclusion) in a set belongs to the set, then the set contains a maximal element. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. Theorem 6M of [Enderton] p. 151. See ~ zorn2 for a version with general partial orderings. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-2004.)
theorem zorn {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wal z (wi (wa (wss (cv z) A2) (wor (cv z) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv z)) A2))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| Variant of Zorn's lemma ~ zorn in which ` (/) ` , the union of the empty chain, is not required to be an element of ` A ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 5-Jan-2011.)
theorem zornn0 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wal z (wi (w3a (wss (cv z) A2) (wne (cv z) c0) (wor (cv z) crpss)) (wcel (cuni (cv z)) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . Expand out the property of being an element of a property of finite character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C A2) (wss (cin (cpw C) cfn) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . A property of finite character is closed under subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel C A2) (wss D C))) (wcel D A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem3 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt z cvv (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) (cuni (cdm (cv z)))) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) c0) B2 (cuni (crn (cv z)))) (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F)) c0))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C con0)) (wceq (cfv C G) (cif (wceq C (cuni C)) (cif (wceq C c0) B2 (cuni (cima G C))) (cun (cfv (cuni C) G) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni C) G) (csn (cfv (cuni C) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni C) F)) c0)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem4 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt z cvv (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) (cuni (cdm (cv z)))) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) c0) B2 (cuni (crn (cv z)))) (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F)) c0))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 G) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . The ` G ` function forms a (transfinitely long) chain of inclusions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem5 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class z) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt z cvv (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) (cuni (cdm (cv z)))) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) c0) B2 (cuni (crn (cv z)))) (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F)) c0))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel C con0) (wcel D con0) (wss C D))) (wss (cfv C G) (cfv D G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem6 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt z cvv (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) (cuni (cdm (cv z)))) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) c0) B2 (cuni (crn (cv z)))) (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F)) c0))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C (csuc (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd)))) (wcel (cfv C G) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ttukey . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeylem7 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (F: class y1) (G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv (cdif (cuni A2) B2) ccrd) (cdif (cuni A2) B2) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crecs (cmpt z cvv (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) (cuni (cdm (cv z)))) (cif (wceq (cdm (cv z)) c0) B2 (cuni (crn (cv z)))) (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (cif (wcel (cun (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) (cv z)) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F))) A2) (csn (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv z))) F)) c0))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wss B2 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2)) A2) $;

--| The Teichm&uuml;ller-Tukey Lemma ~ ttukey with a slightly stronger conclusion: we can set up the maximal element of ` A ` so that it also contains some given ` B e. A ` as a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukey2g {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cuni A2) (cdm ccrd)) (wcel B2 A2) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wa (wss B2 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2)) A2) $;

--| The Teichm&uuml;ller-Tukey Lemma ~ ttukey stated with the "choice" as an antecedent (the hypothesis ` U. A e. dom card ` says that ` U. A ` is well-orderable). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukeyg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cuni A2) (cdm ccrd)) (wne A2 c0) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| The Teichm&uuml;ller-Tukey Lemma, an Axiom of Choice equivalent. If ` A ` is a nonempty collection of finite character, then ` A ` has a maximal element with respect to inclusion. Here "finite character" means that ` x e. A ` iff every finite subset of ` x ` is in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem ttukey {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wss (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn) A2)))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1))) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdclem {x3 y1 z g2 s1: setvar} (F K: class x3 g2 s1):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cfv (cab z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv x3))) (cv g2))) (cv s1)) com) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv y1) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cv y1))) (cpw (cdm (cv x3)))) (wss (crn (cv x3)) (cdm (cv x3))) (wex z (wbr (cfv K F) (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wi (wcel K com) (wbr (cfv K F) (cfv (csuc K) F) (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ axdc . Using the full Axiom of Choice, we can construct a choice function ` g ` on ` ~P dom x ` . From this, we can build a sequence ` F ` starting at any value ` s e. dom x ` by repeatedly applying ` g ` to the set ` ( F `` x ) ` (where ` x ` is the value from the previous iteration). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdclem2 {x3 y1 z f2 g2 n s1: setvar} (F: class x3 g2 s1):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt y1 cvv (cfv (cab z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv x3))) (cv g2))) (cv s1)) com) $ >
  $ wi (wex z (wbr (cv s1) (cv z) (cv x3))) (wi (wss (crn (cv x3)) (cdm (cv x3))) (wex f2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) com))) $;

--| This theorem derives ~ ax-dc using ~ ax-ac and ~ ax-inf . Thus, _AC_ implies _DC_, but not vice-versa (so that ZFC is strictly stronger than ZF+DC). (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jan-2013.)
theorem axdc {x3 y1 z f2 n: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wex y1 (wex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wss (crn (cv x3)) (cdm (cv x3)))) (wex f2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) com)) $;

--| An onto function implies dominance of domain over range. Lemma 10.20 of [Kunen] p. 30. This theorem uses the axiom of choice ~ ac7g . The axiom of choice is not needed for finite sets, see ~ fodomfi . See also ~ fodomnum . (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 20-May-2024.)
theorem fodomg (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| An onto function implies dominance of domain over range. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.)
theorem fodom (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wbr B2 A2 cdom) $;

--| The domain of a countable set is countable. The proof uses ~ fodomnum rather than ~ fodomg , and so does not require ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Vincent Gonzalez, 24-Aug-2026.)
theorem dmct (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (cdm A2) com cdom) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ dmct as of 26-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmctOLD (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (cdm A2) com cdom) $;

--| The range of a countable set is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem rnct (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (crn A2) com cdom) $;

--| Equivalence of an onto mapping and dominance for a nonempty set. Proposition 10.35 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 93. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-2004.)
theorem fodomb {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wne A2 c0) (wex f2 (wfo A2 B2 (cv f2)))) (wa (wbr c0 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| When assuming AC, weak and usual dominance coincide. It is not known if this is an AC equivalent. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem wdomac (X Y: class):
  $ wb (wbr X Y cwdom) (wbr X Y cdom) $;

--| Equivalence to a dominance relation. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2007.)
theorem brdom3 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wa (wal x3 (wmo y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2)))) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| An equivalence to a dominance relation. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-2007.)
theorem brdom5 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wa (wral x3 (wmo y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2))) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| An equivalence to a dominance relation. (Contributed by NM, 28-Mar-2007.) (Revised by NM, 16-Jun-2017.)
theorem brdom4 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wa (wral x3 (wrmo y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2)) A2) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| An equivalence to a dominance relation for disjoint sets. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-2007.) (Revised by NM, 16-Jun-2017.)
theorem brdom7disj {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) c0 $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wa (wral x3 (wrmo y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv f2)) A2) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cpr (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| An equivalence to a dominance relation for disjoint sets. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-2007.)
theorem brdom6disj {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cin A2 B2) c0 $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wex f2 (wa (wral x3 (wmo y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv f2))) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cpr (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Once we allow AC, the "strongest" definition of finite set becomes equivalent to the "weakest" and the entire hierarchy collapses. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Oct-2014.)
theorem fin71ac:
  $ wceq cfin7 cfn $;

--| An image of a function under a set is dominated by the set. Proposition 10.34 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 92. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2004.)
theorem imadomg (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wfun F) (wbr (cima F A2) A2 cdom)) $;

--| A version of ~ imadomg that does not require the axiom of choice ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Vincent Gonzalez, 25-Aug-2026.)
theorem imadomnum (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wi (wfun F) (wbr (cima F A2) A2 cdom)) $;

--| The image by a function of a countable set is countable. The proof uses ~ imadomnum rather than ~ imadomg , and so does not require ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2018.) (Revised by Vincent Gonzalez, 25-Aug-2026.)
theorem fimact (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wfun F)) (wbr (cima F A2) com cdom) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ fimact as of 26-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fimactOLD (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wfun F)) (wbr (cima F A2) com cdom) $;

--| A version of ~ fnrndomg that does not require the axiom of choice ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Vincent Gonzalez, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem fnrndomnum (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm ccrd)) (wi (wfn F A2) (wbr (crn F) A2 cdom)) $;

--| The range of a function is dominated by its domain. This theorem requires the axiom of choice ~ ax-ac2 ; see ~ fnrndomnum for a version that does not. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2004.) (Proof shortened by Vincent Gonzalez, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem fnrndomg (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wfn F A2) (wbr (crn F) A2 cdom)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ fnrndomg as of 18-Aug-2026. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2004.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fnrndomgOLD (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi (wfn F A2) (wbr (crn F) A2 cdom)) $;

--| If the domain of a function is countable, the function is countable. The proof uses ~ fnrndomnum rather than ~ fnrndomg , and so does not require ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Vincent Gonzalez, 24-Aug-2026.)
theorem fnct (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wbr A2 com cdom)) (wbr F com cdom) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ fnct as of 26-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fnctOLD (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wbr A2 com cdom)) (wbr F com cdom) $;

--| A countable mapping set is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem mptct {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (cmpt x3 A2 B2) com cdom) $;

--| Existence of an onto function from a disjoint union to a union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2014.)
theorem iunfo {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 T: class x3):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) $ >
  $ wfo T (ciun x3 A2 B2) (cres c2nd T) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of a disjoint indexed union, with explicit choice principles. ` B ` depends on ` x ` and should be thought of as ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem iundom2g {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (T: class x3):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun x3 A2 (co C B2 cmap)) (wacn A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cdom) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr T (cxp A2 C) cdom) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of an indexed union, with explicit choice principles. ` B ` depends on ` x ` and should be thought of as ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem iundomg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (T: class x3):
  $ wceq T (ciun x3 A2 (cxp (csn (cv x3)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun x3 A2 (co C B2 cmap)) (wacn A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cdom) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp A2 C) (wacn (ciun x3 A2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ciun x3 A2 B2) (cxp A2 C) cdom) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of an indexed union. ` C ` depends on ` x ` and should be thought of as ` C ( x ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2006.)
theorem iundom {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wbr C B2 cdom) A2)) (wbr (ciun x3 A2 C) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of a union. Theorem 10.47 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem unidom {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) B2 cdom) A2)) (wbr (cuni A2) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of the union of an image. Theorem 10.48 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 99. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2006.)
theorem uniimadom {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) B2 cdom) A2)) (wbr (cuni (cima F A2)) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| An upper bound for the cardinality of the union of an image. Theorem 10.48 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 99. This version of ~ uniimadom uses a bound-variable hypothesis in place of a distinct variable condition. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2006.)
theorem uniimadomf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) B2 cdom) A2)) (wbr (cuni (cima F A2)) (cxp A2 B2) cdom) $;

--| The value of the cardinal number function. Definition 10.4 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. See ~ cardval2 for a simpler version of its value. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cint (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cen) con0)) $;

--| Any set is equinumerous to its cardinal number. Proposition 10.5 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardid (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen $;

--| Any set is equinumerous to its cardinal number. Closed theorem form of ~ cardid . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem cardidg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| Any set is equinumerous to its cardinal number. Deduction form of ~ cardid . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem cardidd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 ccrd) A2 cen) $;

--| The cardinality function is a function with domain the well-orderable sets. Assuming AC, this is the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2013.)
theorem cardf:
  $ wf cvv con0 ccrd $;

--| Two sets are equinumerous iff their cardinal numbers are equal. This important theorem expresses the essential concept behind "cardinality" or "size". This theorem appears as Proposition 10.10 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 85, Theorem 7P of [Enderton] p. 197, and Theorem 9 of [Suppes] p. 242 (among others). The Axiom of Choice is required for its proof. Related theorems are ~ hasheni and the finite-set-only ~ hashen . This theorem is also known as Hume's Principle. Gottlob Frege's two-volume _Grundgesetze der Arithmetik_ used his Basic Law V to prove this theorem. Unfortunately Basic Law V caused Frege's system to be inconsistent because it was subject to Russell's paradox (see ~ ru ). Later scholars have found that Frege primarily used Basic Law V to Hume's Principle. If Basic Law V is replaced by Hume's Principle in Frege's system, much of Frege's work is restored. _Grundgesetze der Arithmetik_, once Basic Law V is replaced, proves "Frege's theorem" (the Peano axioms of arithmetic can be derived in second-order logic from Hume's principle). See ~ https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frege-theorem . We take a different approach, using first-order logic and ZFC, to prove the Peano axioms of arithmetic. The theory of cardinality can also be developed without AC by introducing "card" as a primitive notion and stating this theorem as an axiom, as is done with the axiom for cardinal numbers in [Suppes] p. 111. Finally, if we allow the Axiom of Regularity, we can avoid AC by defining the cardinal number of a set as the set of all sets equinumerous to it and having the least possible rank (see ~ karden ). (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.)
theorem carden (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 cen)) $;

--| Only the empty set has cardinality zero. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-2004.)
theorem cardeq0 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) c0) (wceq A2 c0)) $;

--| Equinumerosity of a set with a new element added. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.)
theorem unsnen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel B2 A2)) (wbr (cun A2 (csn B2)) (csuc (cfv A2 ccrd)) cen) $;

--| Two sets have the dominance relationship iff their cardinalities have the subset relationship. Equation i of [Quine] p. 232. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem carddom (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wss (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 cdom)) $;

--| Two sets have the strict dominance relationship iff their cardinalities have the membership relationship. Corollary 19.7(2) of [Eisenberg] p. 310. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem cardsdom (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv B2 ccrd)) (wbr A2 B2 csdm)) $;

--| Trichotomy law for dominance and strict dominance. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem domtri (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wn (wbr B2 A2 csdm))) $;

--| Trichotomy of equinumerosity and strict dominance. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. Theorem 8 of [Suppes] p. 242. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.)
theorem entric (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (w3o (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Trichotomy of dominance and strict dominance. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.)
theorem entri2 (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 csdm)) $;

--| Trichotomy of dominance. This theorem is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice. Part of Proposition 4.42(d) of [Mendelson] p. 275. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2004.)
theorem entri3 (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| A set strictly dominates iff its cardinal strictly dominates. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2003.)
theorem sdomsdomcard (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr A2 (cfv B2 ccrd) csdm) $;

--| Cantor's theorem in terms of cardinals. This theorem tells us that no matter how large a cardinal number is, there is a still larger cardinal number. Theorem 18.12 of [Monk1] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2003.)
theorem canth3 (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) (cfv (cpw A2) ccrd)) $;

--| Every infinite class is equinumerous to its Cartesian square. This theorem, which is equivalent to the axiom of choice over ZF, provides the basis for infinite cardinal arithmetic. Proposition 10.40 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 95. This is a corollary of ~ infxpen (used via ~ infxpidm2 ). (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem infxpidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| The class of ordinals dominated by a given set is an ordinal. Theorem 56 of [Suppes] p. 227. This theorem can be proved without the axiom of choice, see ~ hartogs . (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ hartogs instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ondomon {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cdom) con0) con0) $;

--| The smallest ordinal that strictly dominates a set is a cardinal. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem cardmin {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cint (crab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0)) ccrd) (cint (crab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) con0))) $;

--| A set is finite iff its cardinal is a natural number. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem ficard (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cfv A2 ccrd) com)) $;

--| Equivalence between two infiniteness criteria for sets. To avoid the axiom of infinity, we include it as a hypothesis. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Feb-2026.)
theorem infinfg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel com cvv) (wcel A2 B2)) (wb (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) $;

--| Equivalence between two infiniteness criteria for sets. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 20-Feb-2026.)
theorem infinf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) (wbr com A2 cdom)) $;

--| The union of the range of a function from an infinite set into the class of finite sets is dominated by its domain. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem unirnfdomd (ph: wff) (T F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wf T cfn F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel T cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cuni (crn F)) T cdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ konigth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2013.)
theorem konigthlem {e f2 i a: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class f2 i) (P S: class i) (E: class e f2) (M: class e i) (N: class i):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (ciun i A2 (cfv (cv i) M)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cixp i A2 (cfv (cv i) N)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt i A2 (cmpt a (cfv (cv i) M) (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv a) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt i A2 (cfv (cv i) (cv e))) $ >
  $ wi (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) M) (cfv (cv i) N) csdm) A2) (wbr S P csdm) $;

--| Konig's Theorem. If ` m ( i ) ~< n ( i ) ` for all ` i e. A ` , then ` sum_ i e. A m ( i ) ~< prod_ i e. A n ( i ) ` , where the sums and products stand in for disjoint union and infinite cartesian product. The version here is proven with unions rather than disjoint unions for convenience, but the version with disjoint unions is clearly a special case of this version. The Axiom of Choice is needed for this proof, but it contains AC as a simple corollary (letting ` m ( i ) = (/) ` , this theorem says that an infinite cartesian product of nonempty sets is nonempty), so this is an AC equivalent. Theorem 11.26 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 107. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2013.)
theorem konigth {i: setvar} (A2: class) (P S M N: class i):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq S (ciun i A2 (cfv (cv i) M)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cixp i A2 (cfv (cv i) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) M) (cfv (cv i) N) csdm) A2) (wbr S P csdm) $;

--| The power set of an aleph dominates the successor aleph. (The Generalized Continuum Hypothesis says they are equinumerous, see ~ gch3 or ~ gchaleph2 .) (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2005.)
theorem alephsucpw (A2: class):
  $ wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) cdom $;

--| The set exponentiation of 2 to the aleph-zero has cardinality of at least aleph-one. (If we were to assume the Continuum Hypothesis, their cardinalities would be the same.) (Contributed by NM, 7-Jul-2004.)
theorem aleph1:
  $ wbr (cfv c1o cale) (co c2o (cfv c0 cale) cmap) cdom $;

--| An alternate way to express the value of the aleph function for nonzero arguments. Theorem 64 of [Suppes] p. 229. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2003.)
theorem alephval2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel c0 A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cale) (cint (crab x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) cale) (cv x3) csdm) A2) con0))) $;

--| A nonempty set that is a subset of its union is infinite. This version is proved from ~ ax-ac . See ~ dominf for a version proved from ~ ax-cc . (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-2007.)
theorem dominfac (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wss A2 (cuni A2))) (wbr com A2 cdom) $;

--| The countable union of countable sets is countable (indexed union version of ~ unictb ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2014.)
theorem iunctb {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wral x3 (wbr B2 com cdom) A2)) (wbr (ciun x3 A2 B2) com cdom) $;

--| The countable union of countable sets is countable. Theorem 6Q of [Enderton] p. 159. See ~ iunctb for indexed union version. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2006.)
theorem unictb {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) A2)) (wbr (cuni A2) com cdom) $;

--| An exponentiation law for infinite cardinals. Similar to Lemma 6.2 of [Jech] p. 43. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem infmap {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr com A2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmap) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cen))) cen) $;

--| The sum of two alephs is their maximum. Equation 6.1 of [Jech] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem alephadd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbr (cdju (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale)) (cun (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale)) cen $;

--| The product of two alephs is their maximum. Equation 6.1 of [Jech] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem alephmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wbr (cxp (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale)) (cun (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale)) cen) $;

--| An exponentiation law for alephs. Lemma 6.1 of [Jech] p. 42. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem alephexp1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wss A2 B2)) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cale) (cfv B2 cale) cmap) (co c2o (cfv B2 cale) cmap) cen) $;

--| An alternate representation of a successor aleph. Compare ~ alephsuc and ~ alephsuc2 . Equality can be obtained by taking the ` card ` of the right-hand side then using ~ alephcard and ~ carden . (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.)
theorem alephsuc3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale) cen) con0) cen) $;

--| An expression equinumerous to 2 to an aleph power. The proof equates the two laws for cardinal exponentiation ~ alephexp1 (which works if the base is less than or equal to the exponent) and ~ infmap (which works if the exponent is less than or equal to the base). They can be equated only when the base is equal to the exponent, and this is the result. (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2004.)
theorem alephexp2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wbr (co c2o (cfv A2 cale) cmap) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale)) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale) cen))) cen) $;

--| A successor aleph is regular. Theorem 11.15 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 103. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2013.)
theorem alephreg (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv (csuc A2) cale) ccf) (cfv (csuc A2) cale) $;

--| A corollary of Konig's Theorem ~ konigth . Theorem 11.28 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 108. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.)
theorem pwcfsdom {y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (H: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq H (cmpt y1 (cfv (cfv A2 cale) ccf) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) char)) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv A2 cale) (co (cfv A2 cale) (cfv (cfv A2 cale) ccf) cmap) csdm $;

--| A corollary of Konig's Theorem ~ konigth . Theorem 11.29 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 108. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2013.)
theorem cfpwsdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wbr c2o B2 cdom) (wbr (cfv A2 cale) (cfv (cfv (co B2 (cfv A2 cale) cmap) ccrd) ccf) csdm) $;

--| From ~ canth2 , we know that ` ( aleph `` 0 ) < ( 2 ^ _om ) ` , but we cannot prove that ` ( 2 ^ _om ) = ( aleph `` 1 ) ` (this is the Continuum Hypothesis), nor can we prove that it is less than any bound whatsoever (i.e. the statement ` ( aleph `` A ) < ( 2 ^ _om ) ` is consistent for any ordinal ` A ` ). However, we can prove that ` ( 2 ^ _om ) ` is not equal to ` ( aleph `` _om ) ` , nor ` ( aleph `` ( aleph `` _om ) ) ` , on cofinality grounds, because by Konig's Theorem ~ konigth (in the form of ~ cfpwsdom ), ` ( 2 ^ _om ) ` has uncountable cofinality, which eliminates limit alephs like ` ( aleph `` _om ) ` . (The first limit aleph that is _not_ eliminated is ` ( aleph `` ( aleph `` 1 ) ) ` , which has cofinality ` ( aleph `` 1 ) ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2013.)
theorem alephom:
  $ wne (cfv (co c2o com cmap) ccrd) (cfv com cale) $;

--| The beth function is strictly monotone. This function is not strictly the beth function, but rather beth_A is the same as ` ( card `` ( R1 `` ( _om +o A ) ) ) ` , since conventionally we start counting at the first infinite level, and ignore the finite levels. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jun-2015.)
theorem smobeth:
  $ wsmo (ccom ccrd cr1) $;

--| A lemma for proving conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nd1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)))) $;

--| A lemma for proving conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nd2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal x3 (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A lemma for proving conditionless ZFC axioms. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.)
theorem nd3 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A lemma for proving conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nd4 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal z (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Extensionality with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2003.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axextnd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wi (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Replacement with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axrepndlem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) (wex x3 (wi (wex y1 (wal z (wi ph (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 ph))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Replacement with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axrepndlem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))))) (wex x3 (wi (wex y1 (wal z (wi ph (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal y1 ph))))))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Replacement with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axrepnd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wex x3 (wi (wex y1 (wal z (wi ph (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wal z (wb (wal y1 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) (wex x3 (wa (wal z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 ph)))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Union with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axunndlem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Union with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axunnd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Power Sets with no distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.)
theorem axpowndlem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wex z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Power Sets with no distinct variable conditions. Revised to remove a redundant antecedent from the consequence. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Dec-2016.) (Revised and shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpowndlem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wex z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Power Sets with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Jun-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpowndlem3 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wex z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Power Sets with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpowndlem4 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) (wi (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wex z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))))))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Power Sets with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpownd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wex z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Regularity with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axregndlem1 {x3 z: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Regularity with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axregndlem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Regularity with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Aug-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axregnd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Infinity with no distinct variable conditions. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2002.)
theorem axinfndlem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))))))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Infinity with no distinct variable conditions. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2002.)
theorem axinfnd {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axacndlem1 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axacndlem2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axacndlem3 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) (wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.)
theorem axacndlem4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Lemma for the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.)
theorem axacndlem5 {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| A version of the Axiom of Choice with no distinct variable conditions. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Dec-2016.)
theorem axacnd {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))) (wex w (wal y1 (wb (wex w (wa (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wcel (cv z) (cv w))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Extensionality ~ ax-ext , reproved from conditionless ZFC version and predicate calculus. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndext {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndrep {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wal w (wex y1 (wal z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))))) (wex y1 (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wal y1 ph)))))) $;

--| Axiom of Union ~ ax-un , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndun {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Axiom of Power Sets ~ ax-pow , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. The proof uses the "Axiom of Twoness" ~ dtru . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndpow {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndreg {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wex y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. Since we have already reproved Extensionality, Replacement, and Power Sets above, we are justified in referencing Theorem ~ el in the proof. (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.)
theorem zfcndinf {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Axiom of Choice ~ ax-ac , reproved from conditionless ZFC axioms. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-2003.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem zfcndac {x3 y1 z w v u t: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wex v (wal u (wb (wex t (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv t))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv t)) (wcel (cv t) (cv y1))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v)))))))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the collection of sets that satisfy the GCH.
term cgch: class;

--| Define the collection of "GCH-sets", or sets for which the generalized continuum hypothesis holds. In this language the generalized continuum hypothesis can be expressed as ` GCH = _V ` . A set ` x ` satisfies the generalized continuum hypothesis if it is finite or there is no set ` y ` strictly between ` x ` and its powerset in cardinality. The continuum hypothesis is equivalent to ` _om e. GCH ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
axiom df_gch {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgch (cun cfn (cab x3 (wal y1 (wn (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) csdm) (wbr (cv y1) (cpw (cv x3)) csdm)))))) $;

--| Elementhood in the collection of GCH-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem elgch {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 cgch) (wo (wcel A2 cfn) (wal x3 (wn (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) csdm) (wbr (cv x3) (cpw A2) csdm)))))) $;

--| A finite set is a GCH-set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem fingch:
  $ wss cfn cgch $;

--| The only GCH-sets which have other sets between it and its power set are finite sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cgch) (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) csdm)) (wcel A2 cfn) $;

--| If ` A <_ B < ~P A ` , and ` A ` is an infinite GCH-set, then ` A = B ` in cardinality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchen1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) csdm))) (wbr A2 B2 cen) $;

--| If ` A < B <_ ~P A ` , and ` A ` is an infinite GCH-set, then ` B = ~P A ` in cardinality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchen2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wa (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cdom))) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cen) $;

--| If ` A <_ B <_ ~P A ` , and ` A ` is an infinite GCH-set, then either ` A = B ` or ` B = ~P A ` in cardinality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchor (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cdom))) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cen)) $;

--| The property of being a GCH-set is a cardinal invariant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem engch (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wb (wcel A2 cgch) (wcel B2 cgch)) $;

--| Under certain conditions, a GCH-set can demonstrate trichotomy of dominance. Lemma for ~ gchac . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchdomtri (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cgch) (wbr (cdju A2 A2) A2 cen) (wbr B2 (cpw A2) cdom)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cdom) (wbr B2 A2 cdom)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jun-2015.)
theorem fpwwe2cbv {x3 y1 z v u s1 r a: setvar} (A2: class y1 v u) (F: class) (W: class x3 y1 z v u s1 r a):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq W (copab a s1 (wa (wa (wss (cv a) A2) (wss (cv s1) (cxp (cv a) (cv a)))) (wa (wwe (cv a) (cv s1)) (wral z (wsbc v (wceq (co (cv v) (cin (cv s1) (cxp (cv v) (cv v))) F) (cv z)) (cima (ccnv (cv s1)) (csn (cv z)))) (cv a))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem fpwwe2lem1 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (A2: class y1 u) (F W: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wss W (cxp (cpw A2) (cpw (cxp A2 A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem2 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr X R W) (wa (wa (wss X A2) (wss R (cxp X X))) (wa (wwe X R) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin R (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv R) (csn (cv y1)))) X)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem3 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (B2: class x3 r) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co (cima (ccnv R) (csn B2)) (cin R (cxp (cima (ccnv R) (csn B2)) (cima (ccnv R) (csn B2)))) F) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.) (Proof shortened by Matthew House, 10-Sep-2025.)
theorem fpwwe2lem4 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss X A2) (wss R (cxp X X)) (wwe X R))) (wcel (co X R F) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem5 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (B2: class x3 r) (C: class x3 y1 u r) (R S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y S W) $ >
  $ wceq M (coi X R) $ >
  $ wceq N (coi Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres M B2) (cres N B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr C (cfv B2 M) R)) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel C Y) (wceq (cfv C (ccnv M)) (cfv C (ccnv N)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem6 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (B2: class x3 r) (C D: class x3 y1 u r) (R S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y S W) $ >
  $ wceq M (coi X R) $ >
  $ wceq N (coi Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres M B2) (cres N B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr C (cfv B2 M) R)) (wa (wbr C (cfv B2 N) S) (wi (wbr D (cfv B2 M) R) (wb (wbr C D R) (wbr C D S)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . Show by induction that the two isometries ` M ` and ` N ` agree on their common domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 23-Sep-2022.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem7 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y S W) $ >
  $ wceq M (coi X R) $ >
  $ wceq N (coi Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm M) (cdm N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (cres N (cdm M))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . Given two well-orders ` <. X , R >. ` and ` <. Y , S >. ` of parts of ` A ` , one is an initial segment of the other. (The ` O C_ P ` hypothesis is in order to break the symmetry of ` X ` and ` Y ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 23-Sep-2022.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem8 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y S W) $ >
  $ wceq M (coi X R) $ >
  $ wceq N (coi Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm M) (cdm N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss X Y) (wceq R (cin S (cxp Y X)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . Given two well-orders ` <. X , R >. ` and ` <. Y , S >. ` of parts of ` A ` , one is an initial segment of the other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem9 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R S F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wa (wss X Y) (wceq R (cin S (cxp Y X)))) (wa (wss Y X) (wceq S (cin R (cxp X Y))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem10 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm W) (cpw (cxp X X)) W) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 23-Sep-2022.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem11 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdm W)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2lem12 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (cfv X W) F) X) $;

--| Given any function ` F ` from well-orderings of subsets of ` A ` to ` A ` , there is a unique well-ordered subset ` <. X , ( W `` X ) >. ` which "agrees" with ` F ` in the sense that each initial segment maps to its upper bound, and such that the entire set maps to an element of the set (so that it cannot be extended without losing the well-ordering). This theorem can be used to prove ~ dfac8a . Theorem 1.1 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 415. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe2 {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 u r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wbr Y R W) (wcel (co Y R F) Y)) (wa (wceq Y X) (wceq R (cfv X W)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem fpwwecbv {x3 y1 z s1 r a: setvar} (A2: class y1 z) (F: class) (W: class x3 y1 z s1 r a):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq W (copab a s1 (wa (wa (wss (cv a) A2) (wss (cv s1) (cxp (cv a) (cv a)))) (wa (wwe (cv a) (cv s1)) (wral z (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv s1)) (csn (cv z))) F) (cv z)) (cv a))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fpwwe . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwelem {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 r) (W X: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr X R W) (wa (wa (wss X A2) (wss R (cxp X X))) (wa (wwe X R) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv R) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) X)))) $;

--| Given any function ` F ` from the powerset of ` A ` to ` A ` , ~ canth2 gives that the function is not injective, but we can say rather more than that. There is a unique well-ordered subset ` <. X , ( W `` X ) >. ` which "agrees" with ` F ` in the sense that each initial segment maps to its upper bound, and such that the entire set maps to an element of the set (so that it cannot be extended without losing the well-ordering). This theorem can be used to prove ~ dfac8a . Theorem 1.1 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 415. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2024.)
theorem fpwwe {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (R F: class) (V: class x3 y1 r) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin (cpw A2) (cdm ccrd)))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wbr Y R W) (wcel (cfv Y F) Y)) (wa (wceq Y X) (wceq R (cfv X W)))) $;

--| An "effective" form of Cantor's theorem ~ canth . For any function ` F ` from the powerset of ` A ` to ` A ` , there are two definable sets ` B ` and ` C ` which witness non-injectivity of ` F ` . Corollary 1.3 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 416. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem canth4 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 r) (D F V W: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv (cfv B2 W)) (csn (cfv B2 F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf D A2 F) (wss (cin (cpw A2) (cdm ccrd)) D)) (w3a (wss B2 A2) (wpss C B2) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ canthnum . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-May-2015.)
theorem canthnumlem {x3 y1 r: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 r) (F V W: class):
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) F) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv (cfv B2 W)) (csn (cfv B2 F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wn (wf1 (cin (cpw A2) (cdm ccrd)) A2 F)) $;

--| The set of well-orderable subsets of a set ` A ` strictly dominates ` A ` . A stronger form of ~ canth2 . Corollary 1.4(a) of [KanamoriPincus] p. 417. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-May-2015.)
theorem canthnum (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 (cin (cpw A2) (cdm ccrd)) csdm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ canthwe . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem canthwelem {x3 y1 u r: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 u) (F O V W: class):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 r (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wsbc u (wceq (co (cv u) (cin (cv r) (cxp (cv u) (cv u))) F) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1)))) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv (cfv B2 W)) (csn (co B2 (cfv B2 W) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wn (wf1 O A2 F)) $;

--| The set of well-orders of a set ` A ` strictly dominates ` A ` . A stronger form of ~ canth2 . Corollary 1.4(b) of [KanamoriPincus] p. 417. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem canthwe {x3 r: setvar} (A2 O V: class):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 r (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 O csdm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ canthp1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem canthp1lem1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr (cdju A2 c2o) (cpw A2) cdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ canthp1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem canthp1lem2 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (F G: class x3 y1 r) (H W: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr c1o A2 csdm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cpw A2) (cdju A2 c1o) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cdif (cdju A2 c1o) (csn (cfv A2 F))) A2 G) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom (ccom G F) (cmpt x3 (cpw A2) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) c0 (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wa (wwe (cv x3) (cv r)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv r)) (csn (cv y1))) H) (cv y1)) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A slightly stronger form of Cantor's theorem: For ` 1 < n ` , ` n + 1 < 2 ^ n ` . Corollary 1.6 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 417. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem canthp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr c1o A2 csdm) (wbr (cdju A2 c1o) (cpw A2) csdm) $;

--| The exclusion of finite sets from consideration in ~ df-gch is necessary, because otherwise finite sets larger than a singleton would violate the GCH property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2015.)
theorem finngch (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wbr c1o A2 csdm)) (wa (wbr A2 (cdju A2 c1o) csdm) (wbr (cdju A2 c1o) (cpw A2) csdm)) $;

--| An infinite GCH-set is idempotent under cardinal successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2015.)
theorem gchdju1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wbr (cdju A2 c1o) A2 cen) $;

--| An infinite GCH-set is Dedekind-infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchinf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wbr com A2 cdom) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseq . Derive a contradiction by diagonalization. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwfseqlem1 {x3 w n r: setvar} (ph: wff w) (ps: wff x3 w r) (A2: class w) (D: class x3 w r) (G: class x3 n r) (H: class w n) (K: class x3 n r) (X: class x3 w n r):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv x3) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf1 (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)) (cv x3) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (crn G)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cfv (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (ccnv G))))) (cv x3)) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel D (cdif (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2014.) (Revised by AV, 18-Sep-2021.)
theorem pwfseqlem2 {x3 z w n r: setvar} (ph: wff w) (ps: wff x3 w r) (A2: class w) (D: class x3 w r) (R F: class x3 z w n r) (G: class x3 z n r) (H: class w n) (K: class x3 z n r) (V: class z w n) (X Y: class x3 z w n r):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv x3) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf1 (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)) (cv x3) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (crn G)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cfv (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (ccnv G))))) (cv x3)) G) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 r cvv cvv (cif (wcel (cv x3) cfn) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) H) (cfv (cint (crab z (wn (wcel (cfv (cv z) D) (cv x3))) com)) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Y cfn) (wcel R V)) (wceq (co Y R F) (cfv (cfv Y ccrd) H)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseq . Using the construction ` D ` from ~ pwfseqlem1 , produce a function ` F ` that maps any well-ordered infinite set to an element outside the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwfseqlem3 {x3 z w n r: setvar} (ph: wff w) (ps: wff x3 w r) (A2: class w) (D: class x3 w r) (F: class x3 z w n r) (G: class x3 z n r) (H: class w n) (K: class x3 z n r) (X: class x3 z w n r):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv x3) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf1 (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)) (cv x3) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (crn G)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cfv (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (ccnv G))))) (cv x3)) G) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 r cvv cvv (cif (wcel (cv x3) cfn) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) H) (cfv (cint (crab z (wn (wcel (cfv (cv z) D) (cv x3))) com)) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) F) (cdif A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseqlem4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem pwfseqlem4a {x3 z w n s1 r a: setvar} (ph: wff w) (ps: wff x3 w s1 r a) (A2: class w) (D: class x3 w s1 r a) (F: class x3 z w n r) (G: class x3 z n s1 r a) (H: class w n) (K: class x3 z n s1 r a) (X: class x3 z w n s1 r a):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv x3) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf1 (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)) (cv x3) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (crn G)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cfv (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (ccnv G))))) (cv x3)) G) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 r cvv cvv (cif (wcel (cv x3) cfn) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) H) (cfv (cint (crab z (wn (wcel (cfv (cv z) D) (cv x3))) com)) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wss (cv a) A2) (wss (cv s1) (cxp (cv a) (cv a))) (wwe (cv a) (cv s1)))) (wcel (co (cv a) (cv s1) F) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseq . Derive a final contradiction from the function ` F ` in ~ pwfseqlem3 . Applying ~ fpwwe2 to it, we get a certain maximal well-ordered subset ` Z ` , but the defining property ` ( Z F ( W `` Z ) ) e. Z ` contradicts our assumption on ` F ` , so we are reduced to the case of ` Z ` finite. This too is a contradiction, though, because ` Z ` and its preimage under ` ( W `` Z ) ` are distinct sets of the same cardinality and in a subset relation, which is impossible for finite sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Matthew House, 10-Sep-2025.)
theorem pwfseqlem4 {x3 z w v n s1 r a b: setvar} (ph: wff w) (ps: wff x3 w v s1 r a b) (A2: class w v b) (D: class x3 w v s1 r a b) (F: class x3 z w n r) (G: class x3 z v n s1 r a b) (H: class w n) (K: class x3 z v n s1 r a b) (W: class x3 z w n r) (X: class x3 z w v n s1 r a b) (Z: class x3 z w n r):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3))) (wwe (cv x3) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv x3) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wf1 (ciun n com (co (cv x3) (cv n) cmap)) (cv x3) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (crn G)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cfv (cfv (cv w) (ccnv K)) (ccnv G))))) (cv x3)) G) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 r cvv cvv (cif (wcel (cv x3) cfn) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) H) (cfv (cint (crab z (wn (wcel (cfv (cv z) D) (cv x3))) com)) D))) $ >
  $ wceq W (copab a s1 (wa (wa (wss (cv a) A2) (wss (cv s1) (cxp (cv a) (cv a)))) (wa (wwe (cv a) (cv s1)) (wral b (wsbc v (wceq (co (cv v) (cin (cv s1) (cxp (cv v) (cv v))) F) (cv b)) (cima (ccnv (cv s1)) (csn (cv b)))) (cv a))))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cuni (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ pwfseq . Although in some ways ~ pwfseqlem4 is the "main" part of the proof, one last aspect which makes up a remark in the original text is by far the hardest part to formalize. The main proof relies on the existence of an injection ` K ` from the set of finite sequences on an infinite set ` x ` to ` x ` . Now this alone would not be difficult to prove; this is mostly the claim of ~ fseqen . However, what is needed for the proof is a _canonical_ injection on these sets, so we have to start from scratch pulling together explicit bijections from the lemmas. If one attempts such a program, it will mostly go through, but there is one key step which is inherently nonconstructive, namely the proof of ~ infxpen . The resolution is not obvious, but it turns out that reversing an infinite ordinal's Cantor normal form absorbs all the non-leading terms ( ~ cnfcom3c ), which can be used to construct a pairing function explicitly using properties of the ordinal exponential ( ~ infxpenc ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwfseqlem5 {x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r b: setvar} (ph: wff v u f2) (ps: wff v u t f2 r b) (A2: class x3 y1 v u f2 k) (P: class y1 v u t n r b) (Q: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r b) (S: class x3 v u t f2 k r b) (T: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r b) (G: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k r) (H: class x3 y1 v u f2 k n) (I: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r b) (K: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k r) (N: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r) (O: class t f2 k n r) (X: class x3 y1 v u t f2 k n r b):
  $ wi ph (wf1 (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com X H) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (w3a (wss (cv t) A2) (wss (cv r) (cxp (cv t) (cv t))) (wwe (cv t) (cv r))) (wbr com (cv t) cdom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wi (wss com (cv b)) (wf1o (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cfv (cv b) N))) (cfv (cpw A2) char)) $ >
  $ wceq O (coi (cv t) (cv r)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo u v (cdm O) (cdm O) (cop (cfv (cv u) O) (cfv (cv v) O))) $ >
  $ wceq P (ccom (ccom O (cfv (cdm O) N)) (ccnv T)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseqom (cmpo k f2 cvv cvv (cmpt x3 (co (cv t) (csuc (cv k)) cmap) (co (cfv (cres (cv x3) (cv k)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv x3)) P))) (csn (cop c0 (cfv c0 O)))) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt y1 (ciun n com (co (cv t) (cv n) cmap)) (cop (cdm (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cdm (cv y1)) S)))) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 y1 com (cv t) (cop (cfv (cv x3) O) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq K (ccom (ccom P I) Q) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| The powerset of a Dedekind-infinite set does not inject into the set of finite sequences. The proof is due to Halbeisen and Shelah. Proposition 1.7 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 418. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwfseq {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wn (wbr (cpw A2) (ciun n com (co A2 (cv n) cmap)) cdom)) $;

--| The powerset of a Dedekind-infinite set does not inject into its Cartesian product with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Jul-2022.)
theorem pwxpndom2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wn (wbr (cpw A2) (cdju A2 (cxp A2 A2)) cdom)) $;

--| The powerset of a Dedekind-infinite set does not inject into its Cartesian product with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwxpndom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wn (wbr (cpw A2) (cxp A2 A2) cdom)) $;

--| The powerset of a Dedekind-infinite set does not inject into its cardinal sum with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem pwdjundom (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr com A2 cdom) (wn (wbr (cpw A2) (cdju A2 A2) cdom)) $;

--| An infinite GCH-set is idempotent under cardinal sum. Part of Lemma 2.2 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 419. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchdjuidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wbr (cdju A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| An infinite GCH-set is idempotent under cardinal product. Part of Lemma 2.2 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 419. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchxpidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cgch) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wbr (cxp A2 A2) A2 cen) $;

--| A relationship between dominance over the powerset and strict dominance when the sets involved are infinite GCH-sets. Proposition 3.1 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 421. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchpwdom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr com A2 cdom) (wcel A2 cgch) (wcel B2 cgch)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 csdm) (wbr (cpw A2) B2 cdom)) $;

--| If ` ( aleph `` A ) ` is a GCH-set and its powerset is well-orderable, then the successor aleph ` ( aleph `` suc A ) ` is equinumerous to the powerset of ` ( aleph `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchaleph (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (cfv A2 cale) cgch) (wcel (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) (cdm ccrd))) (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) cen) $;

--| If ` ( aleph `` A ) ` and ` ( aleph `` suc A ) ` are GCH-sets, then the successor aleph ` ( aleph `` suc A ) ` is equinumerous to the powerset of ` ( aleph `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchaleph2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel (cfv A2 cale) cgch) (wcel (cfv (csuc A2) cale) cgch)) (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) cen) $;

--| If ` A + ~~ ~P A ` , then ` A ` is a GCH-set. The much simpler converse to ~ gchhar . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jun-2015.)
theorem hargch (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cfv A2 char) (cpw A2) cen) (wcel A2 cgch) $;

--| If ` ( aleph `` suc A ) ` is equinumerous to the powerset of ` ( aleph `` A ) ` , then ` ( aleph `` A ) ` is a GCH-set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem alephgch (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cpw (cfv A2 cale)) cen) (wcel (cfv A2 cale) cgch) $;

--| It is sufficient to require that all alephs are GCH-sets to ensure the full generalized continuum hypothesis. (The proof uses the Axiom of Regularity.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gch2:
  $ wb (wceq cgch cvv) (wss (crn cale) cgch) $;

--| An equivalent formulation of the generalized continuum hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gch3 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wb (wceq cgch cvv) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (csuc (cv x3)) cale) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cale)) cen) con0) $;

--| The equivalence of two versions of the Generalized Continuum Hypothesis. The right-hand side is the standard version in the literature. The left-hand side is a version devised by Kannan Nambiar, which he calls the Axiom of Combinatorial Sets. For the notation and motivation behind this axiom, see his paper, "Derivation of Continuum Hypothesis from Axiom of Combinatorial Sets", available at ~ http://www.e-atheneum.net/science/derivation&#95;ch.pdf . The equivalence of the two sides provides a negative answer to Open Problem 2 in ~ http://www.e-atheneum.net/science/open&#95;problem&#95;print.pdf . The key idea in the proof below is to equate both sides of ~ alephexp2 to the successor aleph using ~ enen2 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2004.)
theorem gch_kn {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wb (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale)) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv A2 cale) cen))) cen) (wbr (cfv (csuc A2) cale) (co c2o (cfv A2 cale) cmap) cen)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gchac (obsolete, used in Sierpi&#324;ski's proof). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchaclem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr com A2 cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpw C) cgch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr A2 C cdom) (wi (wbr B2 (cpw C) cdom) (wbr (cpw A2) B2 cdom))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr A2 (cpw C) cdom) (wi (wbr B2 (cpw (cpw C)) cdom) (wbr (cpw A2) B2 cdom))) $;

--| A "local" form of ~ gchac . If ` A ` and ` ~P A ` are GCH-sets, then the Hartogs number of ` A ` is ` ~P A ` (so ` ~P A ` and a fortiori ` A ` are well-orderable). The proof is due to Specker. Theorem 2.1 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 419. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2015.)
theorem gchhar (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr com A2 cdom) (wcel A2 cgch) (wcel (cpw A2) cgch)) (wbr (cfv A2 char) (cpw A2) cen) $;

--| A "local" form of ~ gchac . If ` A ` and ` ~P A ` are GCH-sets, then ` ~P A ` is well-orderable. The proof is due to Specker. Theorem 2.1 of [KanamoriPincus] p. 419. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchacg (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr com A2 cdom) (wcel A2 cgch) (wcel (cpw A2) cgch)) (wcel (cpw A2) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The Generalized Continuum Hypothesis implies the Axiom of Choice. The original proof is due to Sierpi&#324;ski (1947); we use a refinement of Sierpi&#324;ski's result due to Specker. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2015.)
theorem gchac:
  $ wi (wceq cgch cvv) wac $;

--| The class of weak inaccessibles.
term cwina: class;

--| The class of strong inaccessibles.
term cina: class;

--| An ordinal is weakly inaccessible iff it is a regular limit cardinal. Note that our definition allows ` _om ` as a weakly inaccessible cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
axiom df_wina {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cwina (cab x3 (w3a (wne (cv x3) c0) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccf) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) csdm) (cv x3)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| An ordinal is strongly inaccessible iff it is a regular strong limit cardinal, which is to say that it dominates the powersets of every smaller ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
axiom df_ina {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cina (cab x3 (w3a (wne (cv x3) c0) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccf) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wbr (cpw (cv y1)) (cv x3) csdm) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Conditions of weak inaccessibility. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
theorem elwina {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cwina) (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) A2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) csdm) A2) A2)) $;

--| Conditions of strong inaccessibility. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
theorem elina {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cina) (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wceq (cfv A2 ccf) A2) (wral x3 (wbr (cpw (cv x3)) A2 csdm) A2)) $;

--| A weakly inaccessible cardinal is an ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winaon (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cwina) (wcel A2 con0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ inawina . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2014.)
theorem inawinalem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wi (wral x3 (wbr (cpw (cv x3)) A2 csdm) A2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) csdm) A2) A2)) $;

--| Every strongly inaccessible cardinal is weakly inaccessible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem inawina (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cina) (wcel A2 cwina) $;

--| ` _om ` is a strongly inaccessible cardinal. (Many definitions of "inaccessible" explicitly disallow ` _om ` as an inaccessible cardinal, but this choice allows to reuse our results for inaccessibles for ` _om ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem omina:
  $ wcel com cina $;

--| A weakly inaccessible cardinal is a cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winacard (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cwina) (wceq (cfv A2 ccrd) A2) $;

--| A weakly inaccessible cardinal is infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winainflem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 con0) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) csdm) A2) A2)) (wss com A2) $;

--| A weakly inaccessible cardinal is infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winainf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cwina) (wss com A2) $;

--| A weakly inaccessible cardinal is a limit ordinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winalim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cwina) (wlim A2) $;

--| A nontrivial weakly inaccessible cardinal is a limit aleph. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winalim2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cwina) (wne A2 com)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cale) A2) (wlim (cv x3)))) $;

--| A nontrivial weakly inaccessible cardinal is a fixed point of the aleph function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2014.)
theorem winafp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cwina) (wne A2 com)) (wceq (cfv A2 cale) A2) $;

--| This theorem, which states that a nontrivial inaccessible cardinal is its own aleph number, is stated here in inference form, where the assumptions are in the hypotheses rather than an antecedent. Often, we use ~ dedth to turn this type of statement into the closed form statement ~ winafp , but in this case, since it is consistent with ZFC that there are no nontrivial inaccessible cardinals, it is not possible to prove ~ winafp using this theorem and ~ dedth , in ZFC. (You can prove this if you use ~ ax-groth , though.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2014.)
theorem winafpi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cwina $ >
  $ wne A2 com $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 cale) A2 $;

--| Assuming the GCH, weakly and strongly inaccessible cardinals coincide. Theorem 11.20 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 106. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2015.)
theorem gchina:
  $ wi (wceq cgch cvv) (wceq cwina cina) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of all weak universes.
term cwun: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the map whose value is the smallest weak universe of which the given set is a subset.
term cwunm: class;

--| The class of all weak universes. A weak universe is a nonempty transitive class closed under union, pairing, and powerset. The advantage of weak universes over Grothendieck universes is that one can prove that every set is contained in a weak universe in ZF (see ~ uniwun ) whereas the analogue for Grothendieck universes requires ~ ax-groth (see ~ grothtsk ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_wun {x3 y1 u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cwun (cab u (w3a (wtr (cv u)) (wne (cv u) c0) (wral x3 (w3a (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv u)) (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) (cv u)) (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv u)) (cv u))) (cv u)))) $;

--| A function that maps a set ` x ` to the smallest weak universe that contains the elements of the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_wunc {x3 u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cwunm (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (crab u (wss (cv x3) (cv u)) cwun))) $;

--| Properties of a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem iswun {x3 y1: setvar} (U: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wb (wcel U cwun) (w3a (wtr U) (wne U c0) (wral x3 (w3a (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) U) (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) U) (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) U) U)) U))) $;

--| A weak universe is transitive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuntr (U: class):
  $ wi (wcel U cwun) (wtr U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wununi (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under powerset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunpw (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpw A2) U) $;

--| The elements of a weak universe are also subsets of it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunelss (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under pairing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunpr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under binary union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunun (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under unordered triple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuntp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ctp A2 B2 C) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under subsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunss (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under binary intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunin (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cin A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under class difference. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wundif (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under nonempty intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunint (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under singletons. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunsn (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csn A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under successors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunsuc (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csuc A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe contains the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wun0 (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 U) $;

--| A weak universe is infinite, because it contains all the finite levels of the cumulative hierarchy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunr1om (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima cr1 com) U) $;

--| A weak universe contains all the finite ordinals, and hence is infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunom (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss com U) $;

--| A weak universe contains all finite sets with elements drawn from the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunfi (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under ordered pairs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunop (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under ordered triples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunot (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp A2 B2 C) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under cartesian products. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunxp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under partial mappings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunpm (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cpm) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under mappings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunmap (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmap) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under functions with known domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the domain operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wundm (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdm A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the range operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunrn (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the converse operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncnv (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccnv A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under restrictions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the function value operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunfv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 A2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunco (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom A2 B2) U) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under transposition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuntpos (ph: wff) (A2 U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ctpos A2) U) $;

--| The intersection of a collection of weak universes is a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem intwun (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cwun) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) cwun) $;

--| Each limit stage in the cumulative hierarchy is a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem r1limwun (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wlim A2)) (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) cwun) $;

--| The stages of the cumulative hierarchy of sets that are weak universes are exactly the stages at the limit ordinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem r1wunlim (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) cwun) (wlim A2)) $;

--| Construct a weak universe from a given set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunex2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 U F V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cun (cun (cv z) (cuni (cv z))) (ciun x3 (cv z) (cun (cpr (cpw (cv x3)) (cuni (cv x3))) (crn (cmpt y1 (cv z) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)))))))) (cun A2 c1o)) com) $ >
  $ wceq U (cuni (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wa (wcel U cwun) (wss A2 U)) $;

--| Construct a weak universe from a given set. See also ~ wunex2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunex {u: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class u):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wrex u (wss A2 (cv u)) cwun) $;

--| Every set is contained in a weak universe. This is the analogue of ~ grothtsk for weak universes, but it is provable in ZF without the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom, contrary to ~ grothtsk . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem uniwun:
  $ wceq (cuni cwun) cvv $;

--| Construct a weak universe from a given set. This version of ~ wunex has a simpler proof, but requires the axiom of regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunex3 (A2 U V: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv (co (cfv A2 crnk) com coa) cr1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wa (wcel U cwun) (wss A2 U)) $;

--| Value of the weak universe closure operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncval {u: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 cwunm) (cint (crab u (wss A2 (cv u)) cwun))) $;

--| The weak universe closure of a set contains the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncid (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 (cfv A2 cwunm)) $;

--| The weak universe closure of a set is a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunccl (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cfv A2 cwunm) cwun) $;

--| The weak universe closure is a subset of any other weak universe containing the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncss (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cwun) (wss A2 U)) (wss (cfv A2 cwunm) U) $;

--| The weak universe closure is idempotent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncidm (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cwunm) cwunm) (cfv A2 cwunm)) $;

--| Our earlier expression for a containing weak universe is in fact the weak universe closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncval2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class z) (U F: class x3 y1 z) (V: class z):
  $ wceq F (cres (crdg (cmpt z cvv (cun (cun (cv z) (cuni (cv z))) (ciun x3 (cv z) (cun (cpr (cpw (cv x3)) (cuni (cv x3))) (crn (cmpt y1 (cv z) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)))))))) (cun A2 c1o)) com) $ >
  $ wceq U (cuni (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 cwunm) U) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of all Tarski classes.
term ctsk: class;

--| The class of all Tarski classes. Tarski classes is a phrase coined by Grzegorz Bancerek in his article _Tarski's Classes and Ranks_, Journal of Formalized Mathematics, Vol 1, No 3, May-August 1990. A Tarski class is a set whose existence is ensured by Tarski's Axiom A (see ~ ax-groth and the equivalent axioms). Axiom A was first presented in Tarski's article _Ueber unerreichbare Kardinalzahlen_. Tarski introduced Axiom A to allow reasoning with inaccessible cardinals in ZFC. Later, Grothendieck introduced the concept of (Grothendieck) universes and showed they were exactly transitive Tarski classes. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.)
axiom df_tsk {y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctsk (cab y1 (wa (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv y1)) (wrex w (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cen) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (cpw (cv y1))))) $;

--| Properties of a Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.)
theorem eltskg {z w: setvar} (T: class) (V: class z w):
  $ wi (wcel T V) (wb (wcel T ctsk) (wa (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) T) (wrex w (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) T)) T) (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) T cen) (wcel (cv z) T)) (cpw T)))) $;

--| Properties of a Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem eltsk2g {z: setvar} (T: class) (V: class z):
  $ wi (wcel T V) (wb (wcel T ctsk) (wa (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) T) (wcel (cpw (cv z)) T)) T) (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) T cen) (wcel (cv z) T)) (cpw T)))) $;

--| First axiom of a Tarski class. The subsets of an element of a Tarski class belong to the class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskpwss (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T)) (wss (cpw A2) T) $;

--| Second axiom of a Tarski class. The powerset of an element of a Tarski class belongs to the class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskpw (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (cpw A2) T) $;

--| Third axiom of a Tarski class. A subset of a Tarski class is either equipotent to the class or an element of the class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tsken (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wss A2 T)) (wo (wbr A2 T cen) (wcel A2 T)) $;

--| The empty set is a (transitive) Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.)
theorem _0tsk:
  $ wcel c0 ctsk $;

--| An element of a Tarski class is strictly dominated by the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 1. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2013.)
theorem tsksdom (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T)) (wbr A2 T csdm) $;

--| A part of a Tarski class strictly dominated by the class is an element of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 2. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskssel (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wss A2 T) (wbr A2 T csdm)) (wcel A2 T) $;

--| The subsets of an element of a Tarski class belong to the class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskss (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 T) $;

--| The intersection of two elements of a Tarski class belongs to the class. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskin (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) T) $;

--| A singleton of an element of a Tarski class belongs to the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 2 (partly). (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2013.)
theorem tsksn (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (csn A2) T) $;

--| A transitive element of a Tarski class is a part of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 8. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tsktrss (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wtr A2) (wcel A2 T)) (wss A2 T) $;

--| If an element of a Tarski class is an ordinal number, its successor is an element of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 6 (partly). (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tsksuc (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 con0) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (csuc A2) T) $;

--| A nonempty Tarski class contains the empty set. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2013.)
theorem tsk0 (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wcel c0 T) $;

--| One is an element of a nonempty Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.)
theorem tsk1 (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wcel c1o T) $;

--| Two is an element of a nonempty Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tsk2 (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wcel c2o T) $;

--| If a Tarski class is not empty, it has more than two elements. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.)
theorem _2domtsk (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wbr c2o T csdm) $;

--| A nonempty Tarski class is infinite, because it contains all the finite levels of the cumulative hierarchy. (This proof does not use ~ ax-inf .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskr1om (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wss (cima cr1 com) T) $;

--| A nonempty Tarski class contains the whole finite cumulative hierarchy. (This proof does not use ~ ax-inf .) (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2011.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.)
theorem tskhf (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wss chf T) $;

--| A nonempty Tarski class is infinite. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.)
theorem tskinf (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wbr com T cdom) $;

--| If ` A ` and ` B ` are members of a Tarski class, their unordered pair is also an element of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 3 (partly). (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskpr (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) T) $;

--| If ` A ` and ` B ` are members of a Tarski class, their ordered pair is also an element of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 4. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.)
theorem tskop (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wcel (cop A2 B2) T) $;

--| A Cartesian product of two parts of a Tarski class is a part of the class. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskxpss (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wss A2 T) (wss B2 T)) (wss (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| A Tarski class is well-orderable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskwe2 (T: class):
  $ wi (wcel T ctsk) (wcel T (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| The intersection of a collection of Tarski classes is a Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 17-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem inttsk (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 ctsk) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) ctsk) $;

--| ` ( R1 `` A ) ` for ` A ` a strongly inaccessible cardinal is equipotent to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2013.)
theorem inar1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cina) (wbr (cfv A2 cr1) A2 cen) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ hfom , shorter as a consequence of ~ inar1 , but requiring AC. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2013.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem hfomALT:
  $ wbr chf com cen $;

--| Any set must be at least as large as the cofinality of its rank, because the ranks of the elements of ` A ` form a cofinal map into ` ( rank `` A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2013.)
theorem rankcf (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr A2 (cfv (cfv A2 crnk) ccf) csdm) $;

--| ` ( R1 `` A ) ` for ` A ` a strongly inaccessible cardinal is a Tarski class. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem inatsk (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cina) (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) ctsk) $;

--| The set of hereditarily finite sets is a Tarski class. (The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom is not needed for this theorem.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.) Restate using the defined ` HF ` symbol. (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 24-Sep-2026.)
theorem hftsk:
  $ wcel chf ctsk $;

--| A Tarski class contains all ordinals smaller than it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskord (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wcel A2 con0) (wbr A2 T csdm)) (wcel A2 T) $;

--| An even more direct relationship than ~ r1tskina to get an inaccessible cardinal out of a Tarski class: the size of any nonempty Tarski class is an inaccessible cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskcard (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wne T c0)) (wcel (cfv T ccrd) cina) $;

--| There is a direct relationship between transitive Tarski classes and inaccessible cardinals: the stages of the cumulative hierarchy of sets that are Tarski classes are exactly the stages at the strongly inaccessible cardinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem r1tskina (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) ctsk) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wcel A2 cina))) $;

--| The union of an element of a transitive Tarski class is in the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskuni (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (cuni A2) T) $;

--| A nonempty transitive Tarski class is a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem tskwun (T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T) (wne T c0)) (wcel T cwun) $;

--| The intersection of an element of a transitive Tarski class is an element of the class. (Contributed by FL, 17-Apr-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskint (A2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel A2 T) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) T) $;

--| The union of two elements of a transitive Tarski class is in the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskun (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) T) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two elements of a transitive Tarski class is an element of the class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 67 (partly). (Contributed by FL, 15-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskxp (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) T) $;

--| Set exponentiation is an element of a transitive Tarski class. JFM CLASSES2 th. 67 (partly). (Contributed by FL, 15-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskmap (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmap) T) $;

--| A transitive Tarski class is closed under small unions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jun-2013.)
theorem tskurn (A2 T F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel A2 T) (wf A2 T F)) (wcel (cuni (crn F)) T) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of all Grothendieck universes.
term cgru: class;

--| A Grothendieck universe is a set that is closed with respect to all the operations that are common in set theory: pairs, powersets, unions, intersections, Cartesian products etc. Grothendieck and alii, S&eacute;minaire de G&eacute;om&eacute;trie Alg&eacute;brique 4, Expos&eacute; I, p. 185. It was designed to give a precise meaning to the concepts of categories of sets, groups... (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
axiom df_gru {x3 y1 u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgru (cab u (wa (wtr (cv u)) (wral x3 (w3a (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) (cv u)) (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv u)) (cv u)) (wral y1 (wcel (cuni (crn (cv y1))) (cv u)) (co (cv u) (cv x3) cmap))) (cv u)))) $;

--| Properties of a Grothendieck universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem elgrug {x3 y1: setvar} (U: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wb (wcel U cgru) (wa (wtr U) (wral x3 (w3a (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) U) (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) U) U) (wral y1 (wcel (cuni (crn (cv y1))) U) (co U (cv x3) cmap))) U))) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe is transitive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem grutr (U: class):
  $ wi (wcel U cgru) (wtr U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe is transitive, so each element is a subset of the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruelss (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U)) (wss A2 U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains the powerset of each of its members. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem grupw (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U)) (wcel (cpw A2) U) $;

--| Any subset of an element of a Grothendieck universe is also an element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruss (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains pairs derived from its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem grupr (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains the range of any function which takes values in the universe (see ~ gruiun for a more intuitive version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruurn (A2 U F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wf A2 U F)) (wcel (cuni (crn F)) U) $;

--| If ` B ( x ) ` is a family of elements of ` U ` and the index set ` A ` is an element of ` U ` , then the indexed union ` U_ x e. A B ` is also an element of ` U ` , where ` U ` is a Grothendieck universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruiun {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wral x3 (wcel B2 U) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains unions of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruuni (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U)) (wcel (cuni A2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains the range of any function which takes values in the universe (see ~ gruiun for a more intuitive version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem grurn (A2 U F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wf A2 U F)) (wcel (crn F) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains image sets drawn from its members. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruima (A2 U F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wfun F) (wss (cima F A2) U)) (wi (wcel A2 U) (wcel (cima F A2) U)) $;

--| Any element of an element of a Grothendieck universe is also an element of the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruel (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel B2 U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains the singletons of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem grusn (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U)) (wcel (csn A2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains ordered pairs of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruop (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) (wcel (cop A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains binary unions of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruun (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains binary cartesian products of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruxp (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains all powers of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem grumap (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmap) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains indexed cartesian products of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruixp {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wral x3 (wcel B2 U) A2)) (wcel (cixp x3 A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains indexed intersections of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruiin {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wrex x3 (wcel B2 U) A2)) (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains all functions on its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruf (A2 U F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 U) (wf A2 U F)) (wcel F U) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains all subsets of itself that are equipotent to an element of the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruen (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wss A2 U) (wa (wcel B2 U) (wbr B2 A2 cen))) (wcel A2 U) $;

--| A nonempty Grothendieck universe is a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem gruwun (U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wne U c0)) (wcel U cwun) $;

--| The intersection of a family of universes is a universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem intgru (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cgru) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cint A2) cgru) $;

--| The intersection of a universe with a class that acts like a universe is another universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem ingru {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wtr A2) (wral x3 (w3a (wcel (cpw (cv x3)) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2) (wal y1 (wi (wf (cv x3) A2 (cv y1)) (wcel (cuni (crn (cv y1))) A2)))) A2)) (wi (wcel U cgru) (wcel (cin U A2) cgru)) $;

--| The wellfounded part of a universe is another universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem wfgru (U: class):
  $ wi (wcel U cgru) (wcel (cin U (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) cgru) $;

--| Each ordinal that is comparable with an element of the universe is in the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem grudomon (A2 B2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U cgru) (wcel A2 con0) (wa (wcel B2 U) (wbr A2 B2 cdom))) (wcel A2 U) $;

--| If a Grothendieck universe ` U ` is nonempty, then the height of the ordinals in ` U ` is a strongly inaccessible cardinal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem gruina (A2 U: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cin U con0) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wne U c0)) (wcel A2 cina) $;

--| A characterization of Grothendieck universes, part 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jun-2013.)
theorem grur1a (A2 U: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cin U con0) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U cgru) (wss (cfv A2 cr1) U) $;

--| A characterization of Grothendieck universes, part 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem grur1 (A2 U: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cin U con0) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cgru) (wcel U (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq U (cfv A2 cr1)) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are the same as transitive Tarski classes, part one: a transitive Tarski class is a universe. (The hard work is in ~ tskuni .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem grutsk1 (T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel T ctsk) (wtr T)) (wcel T cgru) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are the same as transitive Tarski classes. (The proof in the forward direction requires Foundation.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem grutsk {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgru (crab x3 (wtr (cv x3)) ctsk) $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom. For every set ` x ` there is an inaccessible cardinal ` y ` such that ` y ` is not in ` x ` . The addition of this axiom to ZFC set theory provides a framework for category theory, thus for all practical purposes giving us a complete foundation for "all of mathematics". This version of the axiom is used by the Mizar project ( ~ http://www.mizar.org/JFM/Axiomatics/tarski.html ). Unlike the ZFC axioms, this axiom is very long when expressed in terms of primitive symbols (see ~ grothprim ). An open problem is finding a shorter equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.)
axiom ax_groth {y1 z w v: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)))) (wrex w (wal v (wi (wss (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wo (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cen) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck axiom using abbreviations. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-2009.)
theorem axgroth5 {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv y1)) (wrex w (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wral z (wo (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cen) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (cpw (cv y1)))) $;

--| Alternate version of the Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.)
theorem axgroth2 {y1 z w v: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)))) (wrex w (wal v (wi (wss (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wo (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cdom) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Derive the Axiom of Power Sets ~ ax-pow from the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom ~ ax-groth . That it follows is mentioned by Bob Solovay at ~ https://fomarchive.ugent.be/2008-March/012783.html . Note that ~ ax-pow is not used by the proof. (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 22-Jun-2009.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grothpw {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Derive the Axiom of Power Sets from the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom ~ ax-groth . Note that ~ ax-pow is not used by the proof. Use ~ axpweq to obtain ~ ax-pow . Use ~ pwex or ~ pwexg instead. (Contributed by G&eacute;rard Lang, 22-Jun-2009.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grothpwex (x3: setvar):
  $ wcel (cpw (cv x3)) cvv $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck axiom using abbreviations. This version is called Tarski's axiom: given a set ` x ` , there exists a set ` y ` containing ` x ` , the subsets of the members of ` y ` , the power sets of the members of ` y ` , and the subsets of ` y ` of cardinality less than that of ` y ` . (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-2009.)
theorem axgroth6 {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv y1)) (wcel (cpw (cv z)) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wral z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) csdm) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (cpw (cv y1)))) $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom implies the Axiom of Infinity (in the form of ~ omex ). Note that our proof depends on neither the Axiom of Infinity nor Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2013.) Use ~ omex instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grothomex:
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom implies the Axiom of Choice (in the form of ~ cardeqv ). This can be put in a more conventional form via ~ ween and ~ dfac8 . Note that the mere existence of strongly inaccessible cardinals doesn't imply AC, but rather the particular form of the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom (see ~ https://fomarchive.ugent.be/2008-March/012783.html ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grothac:
  $ wceq (cdm ccrd) cvv $;

--| Alternate version of the Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom. ~ ax-cc is used to derive this version. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2007.)
theorem axgroth3 {y1 z w v: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)))) (wrex w (wal v (wi (wss (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))) (cv y1))) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wo (wbr (cdif (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv z) cdom) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Alternate version of the Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom. ~ ax-ac is used to derive this version. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2007.)
theorem axgroth4 {y1 z w v: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wrex v (wal w (wi (wss (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cin (cv y1) (cv v))))) (cv y1)) (cv y1)) (wal z (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wo (wbr (cdif (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv z) cdom) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ grothprim . Expand the membership of an unordered pair into primitives. (Contributed by NM, 29-Mar-2007.)
theorem grothprimlem {g2 h: setvar} (w v u: setvar):
  $ wb (wcel (cpr (cv u) (cv v)) (cv w)) (wex g2 (wa (wcel (cv g2) (cv w)) (wal h (wb (wcel (cv h) (cv g2)) (wo (wceq (cv h) (cv u)) (wceq (cv h) (cv v))))))) $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom ~ ax-groth expanded into set theory primitives using 163 symbols (allowing the defined symbols ` /\ ` , ` \/ ` , ` <-> ` , and ` E. ` ). An open problem is whether a shorter equivalent exists (when expanded to primitives). (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2007.)
theorem grothprim {y1 z w v u t g2 h: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wa (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex v (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wal w (wi (wal u (wi (wcel (cv u) (cv w)) (wcel (cv u) (cv z)))) (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv w) (cv v)))))))) (wex w (wi (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1))) (wo (wal v (wa (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wex t (wal u (wi (wex g2 (wa (wcel (cv g2) (cv w)) (wal h (wb (wcel (cv h) (cv g2)) (wo (wceq (cv h) (cv v)) (wceq (cv h) (cv u))))))) (wceq (cv u) (cv t)))))) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wo (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wex u (wa (wcel (cv u) (cv z)) (wex g2 (wa (wcel (cv g2) (cv w)) (wal h (wb (wcel (cv h) (cv g2)) (wo (wceq (cv h) (cv u)) (wceq (cv h) (cv v))))))))))))) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))))))) $;

--| The Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom, using abbreviations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2013.)
theorem grothtsk:
  $ wceq (cuni ctsk) cvv $;

--| An equivalent to the Tarski-Grothendieck Axiom: there is a proper class of inaccessible cardinals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jun-2013.)
theorem inaprc:
  $ wnel cina cvv $;

--| Extend class definition to include the map whose value is the smallest Tarski class.
term ctskm: class;

--| A function that maps a set ` x ` to the smallest Tarski class that contains the set. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.)
axiom df_tskm {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctskm (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) ctsk))) $;

--| Value of our tarski map. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskmval {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 ctskm) (cint (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) ctsk))) $;

--| The set ` A ` is an element of the smallest Tarski class that contains ` A ` . CLASSES1 th. 5. (Contributed by FL, 30-Dec-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskmid (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel A2 (cfv A2 ctskm)) $;

--| A Tarski class that contains ` A ` is a Tarski class. (Contributed by FL, 17-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem tskmcl (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 ctskm) ctsk $;

--| Being a part of ` ( tarskiMap `` A ) ` . (Contributed by FL, 17-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.)
theorem sstskm {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wss B2 (cfv A2 ctskm)) (wral x3 (wi (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wss B2 (cv x3))) ctsk)) $;

--| Belonging to ` ( tarskiMap `` A ) ` . (Contributed by FL, 17-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem eltskm {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel B2 (cfv A2 ctskm)) (wral x3 (wi (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel B2 (cv x3))) ctsk)) $;

--| The set of positive integers, which is the set of natural numbers ` _om ` with 0 removed. _Note: This is the start of the Dedekind-cut construction of real and_ _complex numbers. The last lemma of the construction is_ ~ mulcnsrec . _The actual set of Dedekind cuts is defined by_ ~ df-np .
term cnpi: class;

--| Positive integer addition.
term cpli: class;

--| Positive integer multiplication.
term cmi: class;

--| Positive integer ordering relation.
term clti: class;

--| Positive pre-fraction addition.
term cplpq: class;

--| Positive pre-fraction multiplication.
term cmpq: class;

--| Positive pre-fraction ordering relation.
term cltpq: class;

--| Equivalence class used to construct positive fractions.
term ceq: class;

--| Set of positive fractions.
term cnq: class;

--| The positive fraction constant 1.
term c1q: class;

--| Positive fraction equivalence class.
term cerq: class;

--| Positive fraction addition.
term cplq: class;

--| Positive fraction multiplication.
term cmq: class;

--| Positive fraction reciprocal operation.
term crq: class;

--| Positive fraction ordering relation.
term cltq: class;

--| Set of positive reals.
term cnp: class;

--| Positive real constant 1.
term c1p: class;

--| Positive real addition.
term cpp: class;

--| Positive real multiplication.
term cmp: class;

--| Positive real ordering relation.
term cltp: class;

--| Equivalence class used to construct signed reals.
term cer: class;

--| Set of signed reals.
term cnr: class;

--| The signed real constant 0.
term c0r: class;

--| The signed real constant 1.
term c1r: class;

--| The signed real constant -1.
term cm1r: class;

--| Signed real addition.
term cplr: class;

--| Signed real multiplication.
term cmr: class;

--| Signed real ordering relation.
term cltr: class;

--| Define the class of positive integers. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ni:
  $ wceq cnpi (cdif com (csn c0)) $;

--| Define addition on positive integers. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_pli:
  $ wceq cpli (cres coa (cxp cnpi cnpi)) $;

--| Define multiplication on positive integers. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mi:
  $ wceq cmi (cres comu (cxp cnpi cnpi)) $;

--| Define 'less than' on positive integers. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_lti:
  $ wceq clti (cin cep (cxp cnpi cnpi)) $;

--| Membership in the class of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elni (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cnpi) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wne A2 c0)) $;

--| Membership in the class of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 27-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elni2 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cnpi) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel c0 A2)) $;

--| A positive integer is a natural number. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pinn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel A2 com) $;

--| A positive integer is an ordinal number. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pion (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel A2 con0) $;

--| A positive integer is ordinal. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem piord (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (word A2) $;

--| The class of positive integers is a set. (Contributed by NM, 15-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem niex:
  $ wcel cnpi cvv $;

--| The empty set is not a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0npi:
  $ wn (wcel c0 cnpi) $;

--| Ordinal 'one' is a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1pi:
  $ wcel c1o cnpi $;

--| Positive integer addition in terms of ordinal addition. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addpiord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cpli) (co A2 B2 coa)) $;

--| Positive integer multiplication in terms of ordinal multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulpiord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmi) (co A2 B2 comu)) $;

--| 1 is an identity element for multiplication on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulidpi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wceq (co A2 c1o cmi) A2) $;

--| Positive integer 'less than' in terms of ordinal membership. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltpiord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clti) (wcel A2 B2)) $;

--| Positive integer 'less than' is a strict ordering. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-1996.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltsopi:
  $ wor cnpi clti $;

--| Positive integer 'less than' is a relation on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrelpi:
  $ wss clti (cxp cnpi cnpi) $;

--| Domain of addition on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmaddpi:
  $ wceq (cdm cpli) (cxp cnpi cnpi) $;

--| Domain of multiplication on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmmulpi:
  $ wceq (cdm cmi) (cxp cnpi cnpi) $;

--| Closure of addition of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclpi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cpli) cnpi) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulclpi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmi) cnpi) $;

--| Addition of positive integers is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcompi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cpli) (co B2 A2 cpli) $;

--| Addition of positive integers is associative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addasspi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cpli) C cpli) (co A2 (co B2 C cpli) cpli) $;

--| Multiplication of positive integers is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcompi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmi) (co B2 A2 cmi) $;

--| Multiplication of positive integers is associative. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulasspi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmi) C cmi) (co A2 (co B2 C cmi) cmi) $;

--| Multiplication of positive integers is distributive. (Contributed by NM, 21-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrpi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cpli) cmi) (co (co A2 B2 cmi) (co A2 C cmi) cpli) $;

--| Addition cancellation law for positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcanpi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cpli) (co A2 C cpli)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Multiplication cancellation law for positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcanpi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmi) (co A2 C cmi)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| There is no identity element for addition on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addnidpi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wn (wceq (co A2 B2 cpli) A2)) $;

--| Ordering on positive integers in terms of existence of sum. (Contributed by NM, 15-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexpi {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clti) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cpli) B2) cnpi)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition for positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 7-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltapi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnpi) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clti) (wbr (co C A2 cpli) (co C B2 cpli) clti)) $;

--| Ordering property of multiplication for positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltmpi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnpi) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clti) (wbr (co C A2 cmi) (co C B2 cmi) clti)) $;

--| One is less than two (one plus one). (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1lt2pi:
  $ wbr c1o (co c1o c1o cpli) clti $;

--| No positive integer is less than one. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nlt1pi (A2: class):
  $ wn (wbr A2 c1o clti) $;

--| Principle of Finite Induction on positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem indpi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1o) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1o cpli)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cnpi) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) ta $;

--| Define pre-addition on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. This "pre-addition" operation works directly with ordered pairs of integers. The actual positive fraction addition ` +Q ` ( ~ df-plq ) works with the equivalence classes of these ordered pairs determined by the equivalence relation ` ~Q ` ( ~ df-enq ). (Analogous remarks apply to the other "pre-" operations in the complex number construction that follows.) From Proposition 9-2.3 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_plpq {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cplpq (cmpo x3 y1 (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cop (co (co (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cmi) (co (cfv (cv y1) c1st) (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) cmi) cpli) (co (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cmi))) $;

--| Define pre-multiplication on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.4 of [Gleason] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mpq {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmpq (cmpo x3 y1 (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cop (co (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) cmi) (co (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cmi))) $;

--| Define pre-ordering relation on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. Similar to Definition 5 of [Suppes] p. 162. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ltpq {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cltpq (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wbr (co (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cmi) (co (cfv (cv y1) c1st) (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) cmi) clti))) $;

--| Define equivalence relation for positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.1 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_enq {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceq (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) cmi) (co (cv w) (cv v) cmi))))))))) $;

--| Define class of positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.2 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_nq {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnq (crab x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) ceq) (wn (wbr (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) clti))) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) $;

--| Define a convenience function that "reduces" a fraction to lowest terms. Note that in this form, it is not obviously a function; we prove this in ~ nqerf . (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_erq:
  $ wceq cerq (cin ceq (cxp (cxp cnpi cnpi) cnq)) $;

--| Define addition on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.3 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_plq:
  $ wceq cplq (cres (ccom cerq cplpq) (cxp cnq cnq)) $;

--| Define multiplication on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.4 of [Gleason] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mq:
  $ wceq cmq (cres (ccom cerq cmpq) (cxp cnq cnq)) $;

--| Define positive fraction constant 1. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.2 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_1nq:
  $ wceq c1q (cop c1o c1o) $;

--| Define reciprocal on positive fractions. It means the same thing as one divided by the argument (although we don't define full division since we will never need it). This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-2.5 of [Gleason] p. 119, who uses an asterisk to denote this unary operation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_rq:
  $ wceq crq (cima (ccnv cmq) (csn c1q)) $;

--| Define ordering relation on positive fractions. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. Similar to Definition 5 of [Suppes] p. 162. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ltnq:
  $ wceq cltq (cin cltpq (cxp cnq cnq)) $;

--| Equivalence relation for positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enqbreq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wa (wcel C cnpi) (wcel D cnpi))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ceq) (wceq (co A2 D cmi) (co B2 C cmi))) $;

--| Equivalence relation for positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enqbreq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 ceq) (wceq (co (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c2nd) cmi) (co (cfv B2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd) cmi))) $;

--| The equivalence relation for positive fractions is an equivalence relation. Proposition 9-2.1 of [Gleason] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enqer:
  $ wer (cxp cnpi cnpi) ceq $;

--| The equivalence relation for positive fractions exists. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enqex:
  $ wcel ceq cvv $;

--| The class of positive fractions exists. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqex:
  $ wcel cnq cvv $;

--| The empty set is not a positive fraction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0nnq:
  $ wn (wcel c0 cnq) $;

--| Each positive fraction is an ordered pair of positive integers (the numerator and denominator, in "lowest terms". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elpqn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) $;

--| Positive fraction 'less than' is a relation on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrelnq:
  $ wss cltq (cxp cnq cnq) $;

--| The representatives of positive integers as positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pinq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel (cop A2 c1o) cnq) $;

--| The positive fraction 'one'. (Contributed by NM, 29-Oct-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1nq:
  $ wcel c1q cnq $;

--| There is a unique element of ` Q. ` equivalent to each element of ` N. X. N. ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqereu {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wreu x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 ceq) cnq) $;

--| Corollary of ~ nqereu : the function ` /Q ` is actually a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqerf:
  $ wf (cxp cnpi cnpi) cnq cerq $;

--| Corollary of ~ nqereu : closure of ` /Q ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqercl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel (cfv A2 cerq) cnq) $;

--| Any member of ` ( N. X. N. ) ` relates to the representative of its equivalence class. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqerrel (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 cerq) ceq) $;

--| Corollary of ~ nqereu : the function ` /Q ` acts as the identity on members of ` Q. ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqerid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wceq (cfv A2 cerq) A2) $;

--| Corollary of ~ nqereu : if two fractions are both reduced and equivalent, then they are equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enqeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq) (wbr A2 B2 ceq)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| The function ` /Q ` acts as a substitute for equivalence classes, and it satisfies the fundamental requirement for equivalence representatives: the representatives are equal iff the members are equivalent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqereq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 ceq) (wceq (cfv A2 cerq) (cfv B2 cerq))) $;

--| Addition of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addpipq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cplpq) (cop (co (co (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c2nd) cmi) (co (cfv B2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd) cmi) cpli) (co (cfv A2 c2nd) (cfv B2 c2nd) cmi))) $;

--| Addition of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addpipq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wa (wcel C cnpi) (wcel D cnpi))) (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) cplpq) (cop (co (co A2 D cmi) (co C B2 cmi) cpli) (co B2 D cmi))) $;

--| Addition of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addpqnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cplq) (cfv (co A2 B2 cplpq) cerq)) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulpipq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmpq) (cop (co (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c1st) cmi) (co (cfv A2 c2nd) (cfv B2 c2nd) cmi))) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulpipq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi)) (wa (wcel C cnpi) (wcel D cnpi))) (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) cmpq) (cop (co A2 C cmi) (co B2 D cmi))) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulpqnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmq) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmpq) cerq)) $;

--| Ordering of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ordpipq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) cltpq) (wbr (co A2 D cmi) (co C B2 cmi) clti) $;

--| Ordering of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ordpinq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wbr (co (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c2nd) cmi) (co (cfv B2 c1st) (cfv A2 c2nd) cmi) clti)) $;

--| Closure of addition on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addpqf:
  $ wf (cxp (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (cxp cnpi cnpi) cplpq $;

--| Closure of addition on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cplq) cnq) $;

--| Closure of multiplication on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulpqf:
  $ wf (cxp (cxp cnpi cnpi) (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (cxp cnpi cnpi) cmpq $;

--| Closure of multiplication on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulclnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmq) cnq) $;

--| Domain of addition on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addnqf:
  $ wf (cxp cnq cnq) cnq cplq $;

--| Domain of multiplication on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulnqf:
  $ wf (cxp cnq cnq) cnq cmq $;

--| Addition of positive fractions is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcompq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cplpq) (co B2 A2 cplpq) $;

--| Addition of positive fractions is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcomnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cplq) (co B2 A2 cplq) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcompq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmpq) (co B2 A2 cmpq) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcomnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmq) (co B2 A2 cmq) $;

--| Lemma for ~ adderpq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adderpqlem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel C (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 ceq) (wbr (co A2 C cplpq) (co B2 C cplpq) ceq)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mulerpq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulerpqlem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel B2 (cxp cnpi cnpi)) (wcel C (cxp cnpi cnpi))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 ceq) (wbr (co A2 C cmpq) (co B2 C cmpq) ceq)) $;

--| Addition is compatible with the equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adderpq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co (cfv A2 cerq) (cfv B2 cerq) cplq) (cfv (co A2 B2 cplpq) cerq) $;

--| Multiplication is compatible with the equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulerpq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co (cfv A2 cerq) (cfv B2 cerq) cmq) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmpq) cerq) $;

--| Addition of positive fractions is associative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addassnq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cplq) C cplq) (co A2 (co B2 C cplq) cplq) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions is associative. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulassnq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmq) C cmq) (co A2 (co B2 C cmq) cmq) $;

--| Lemma for distributive law: cancellation of common factor. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcanenq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cnpi) (wcel B2 cnpi) (wcel C cnpi)) (wbr (cop (co A2 B2 cmi) (co A2 C cmi)) (cop B2 C) ceq) $;

--| Multiplication of positive fractions is distributive. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrnq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cplq) cmq) (co (co A2 B2 cmq) (co A2 C cmq) cplq) $;

--| The equivalence class of ratio 1. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1nqenq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnpi) (wbr c1q (cop A2 A2) ceq) $;

--| Multiplication identity element for positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 3-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulidnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wceq (co A2 c1q cmq) A2) $;

--| Relationship between reciprocal and multiplication on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recmulnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 crq) B2) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmq) c1q)) $;

--| A positive fraction times its reciprocal is 1. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recidnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 crq) cmq) c1q) $;

--| Closure law for positive fraction reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recclnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel (cfv A2 crq) cnq) $;

--| Reciprocal of reciprocal of positive fraction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recrecnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 crq) crq) A2) $;

--| Domain of reciprocal on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmrecnq:
  $ wceq (cdm crq) cnq $;

--| 'Less than' is a strict ordering on positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltsonq:
  $ wor cnq cltq $;

--| Compatibility of ordering on equivalent fractions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lterpq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cltpq) (wbr (cfv A2 cerq) (cfv B2 cerq) cltq) $;

--| Ordering property of addition for positive fractions. Proposition 9-2.6(ii) of [Gleason] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltanq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnq) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wbr (co C A2 cplq) (co C B2 cplq) cltq)) $;

--| Ordering property of multiplication for positive fractions. Proposition 9-2.6(iii) of [Gleason] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltmnq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnq) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wbr (co C A2 cmq) (co C B2 cmq) cltq)) $;

--| One is less than two (one plus one). (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1lt2nq:
  $ wbr c1q (co c1q c1q cplq) cltq $;

--| The sum of two fractions is greater than one of them. (Contributed by NM, 14-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltaddnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel B2 cnq)) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cplq) cltq) $;

--| Ordering on positive fractions in terms of existence of sum. Definition in Proposition 9-2.6 of [Gleason] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexnq {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 cnq) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cplq) B2))) $;

--| One-half of any positive fraction exists. Lemma for Proposition 9-2.6(i) of [Gleason] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem halfnq {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wex x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv x3) cplq) A2)) $;

--| The is no smallest positive fraction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nsmallnq {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wex x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cltq)) $;

--| There exists a number between any two positive fractions. Proposition 9-2.6(i) of [Gleason] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltbtwnnq {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cltq) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cltq))) $;

--| Ordering property of reciprocal for positive fractions. Proposition 9-2.6(iv) of [Gleason] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cltq) (wbr (cfv B2 crq) (cfv A2 crq) cltq) $;

--| For any fraction, there is an integer that is greater than it. This is also known as the "archimedean property". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem archnq {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cop (cv x3) c1o) cltq) cnpi) $;

--| Define the set of positive reals. A "Dedekind cut" is a partition of the positive rational numbers into two classes such that all the numbers of one class are less than all the numbers of the other. A positive real is defined as the lower class of a Dedekind cut. Definition 9-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 121. (Note: This is a "temporary" definition used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction.) (Contributed by NM, 31-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_np {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnp (cab x3 (wa (wa (wpss c0 (cv x3)) (wpss (cv x3) cnq)) (wral y1 (wa (wal z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cltq) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltq) (cv x3))) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define the positive real constant 1. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. Definition of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_1p {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq c1p (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) c1q cltq)) $;

--| Define addition on positive reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-3.5 of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_plp {x3 y1 w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpp (cmpo x3 y1 cnp cnp (cab w (wrex v (wrex u (wceq (cv w) (co (cv v) (cv u) cplq)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define multiplication on positive reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-3.7 of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mp {x3 y1 w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmp (cmpo x3 y1 cnp cnp (cab w (wrex v (wrex u (wceq (cv w) (co (cv v) (cv u) cmq)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define ordering on positive reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-3.2 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ltp {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cltp (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cnp) (wcel (cv y1) cnp)) (wpss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| The class of positive reals is a set. (Contributed by NM, 31-Oct-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem npex:
  $ wcel cnp cvv $;

--| Membership in positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elnp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cnp) (wa (wa (wpss c0 A2) (wpss A2 cnq)) (wral x3 (wa (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltq) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) A2)) A2)) $;

--| Membership in positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elnpi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cnp) (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cvv) (wpss c0 A2) (wpss A2 cnq)) (wral x3 (wa (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltq) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) A2)) A2)) $;

--| A positive real is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prn0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| A positive real is a subset of the positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prpssnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wpss A2 cnq) $;

--| A positive real is a set of positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elprnq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel B2 cnq) $;

--| The empty set is not a positive real. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0npr:
  $ wn (wcel c0 cnp) $;

--| A positive real is closed downwards under the positive fractions. Definition 9-3.1 (ii) of [Gleason] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prcdnq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 A2)) (wi (wbr C B2 cltq) (wcel C A2)) $;

--| A positive fraction not in a positive real is an upper bound. Remark (1) of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel C cnq)) (wi (wn (wcel C A2)) (wbr B2 C cltq)) $;

--| A positive real has no largest member. Definition 9-3.1(iii) of [Gleason] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prnmax {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 A2)) (wrex x3 (wbr B2 (cv x3) cltq) A2) $;

--| A simplifying observation, and an indication of why any attempt to develop a theory of the real numbers without the Axiom of Infinity is doomed to failure: since every member of ` P. ` is an infinite set, the negation of Infinity implies that ` P. ` , and hence ` RR ` , is empty. (Note that this proof, which used the fact that Dedekind cuts have no maximum, could just as well have used that they have no minimum, since they are downward-closed by ~ prcdnq and ~ nsmallnq ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem npomex (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel com cvv) $;

--| A positive real has no largest member. Addition version. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prnmadd {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 A2)) (wex x3 (wcel (co B2 (cv x3) cplq) A2)) $;

--| Positive real 'less than' is a relation on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrelpr:
  $ wss cltp (cxp cnp cnp) $;

--| Value of general operation (addition or multiplication) on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpv {x3 y1 z w v f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (F: class x3 y1 z w v h) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) (cab f2 (wrex g2 (wrex h (wceq (cv f2) (co (cv g2) (cv h) G)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Membership in value of general operation (addition or multiplication) on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpelv {x3 y1 z w v g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (C: class x3 y1 z w v) (F: class x3 y1 z w v h) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 F)) (wrex g2 (wrex h (wceq C (co (cv g2) (cv h) G)) B2) A2)) $;

--| Pre-closure law for general operation on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpprecl {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (C D F: class x3 y1 z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D B2)) (wcel (co C D G) (co A2 B2 F))) $;

--| Domain of general operation on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpdm {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (F: class x3 y1 z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp cnp cnp) $;

--| The result of an operation on positive reals is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 28-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpn0 {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (F: class x3 y1 z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wpss c0 (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| The result of an operation on positive reals is a subset of the positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpss {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (F: class x3 y1 z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wss (co A2 B2 F) cnq) $;

--| The result of an operation on positive reals is different from the set of positive fractions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpnnp {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1 z) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) cnq) (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) (wbr (co (cv z) (cv x3) G) (co (cv z) (cv y1) G) cltq)) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wn (wceq (co A2 B2 F) cnq)) $;

--| Downward closure of an operation on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpcd {x3 y1 z w v g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (f2: setvar) (F: class x3 y1 z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wa (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wcel (cv x3) cnq)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv g2) (cv h) G) cltq) (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wi (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 B2 F)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv f2) cltq) (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 F)))) $;

--| An operation on positive reals has no largest member. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpnmax {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (A2 B2: class w v) (f2: setvar) (F: class z w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv v) cnq) (wb (wbr (cv z) (cv w) cltq) (wbr (co (cv v) (cv z) G) (co (cv v) (cv w) G) cltq)) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv z) (cv w) G) (co (cv w) (cv z) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wi (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 B2 F)) (wrex x3 (wbr (cv f2) (cv x3) cltq) (co A2 B2 F))) $;

--| Closure of an operation on reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpcl {x3 y1 z w v f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class z) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv h) cnq) (wb (wbr (cv f2) (cv g2) cltq) (wbr (co (cv h) (cv f2) G) (co (cv h) (cv g2) G) cltq)) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wa (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wcel (cv x3) cnq)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv g2) (cv h) G) cltq) (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 F) cnp) $;

--| Associativity of an operation on reals. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem genpass {x3 y1 z w v f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class w v) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo w v cnp cnp (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cv v)) (cv w)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cnq) (wcel (cv z) cnq)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) G) cnq) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp cnp cnp) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv f2) cnp) (wcel (cv g2) cnp)) (wcel (co (cv f2) (cv g2) F) cnp) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (cv f2) (cv g2) G) (cv h) G) (co (cv f2) (co (cv g2) (cv h) G) G) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 F) C F) (co A2 (co B2 C F) F) $;

--| Value of addition on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 28-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem plpv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cpp) (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) cplq)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Value of multiplication on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 28-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mpv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmp) (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) cmq)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Domain of addition on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmplp:
  $ wceq (cdm cpp) (cxp cnp cnp) $;

--| Domain of multiplication on positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmmp:
  $ wceq (cdm cmp) (cxp cnp cnp) $;

--| The canonical embedding of the rationals into the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nqpr {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnq) (wcel (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cltq)) cnp) $;

--| The positive real number 'one'. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1pr:
  $ wcel c1p cnp $;

--| Lemma to prove downward closure in positive real addition. Part of proof of Proposition 9-3.5 of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclprlem1 {g2: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class g2) (h: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cv x3) cnq)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv g2) (cv h) cplq) cltq) (wcel (co (co (cv x3) (cfv (co (cv g2) (cv h) cplq) crq) cmq) (cv g2) cmq) A2)) $;

--| Lemma to prove downward closure in positive real addition. Part of proof of Proposition 9-3.5 of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclprlem2 {x3 g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class g2 h):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wa (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wcel (cv x3) cnq)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv g2) (cv h) cplq) cltq) (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cpp))) $;

--| Closure of addition on positive reals. First statement of Proposition 9-3.5 of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cpp) cnp) $;

--| Lemma to prove downward closure in positive real multiplication. Part of proof of Proposition 9-3.7 of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 14-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulclprlem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (g2 h: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wa (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel (cv h) B2))) (wcel (cv x3) cnq)) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv g2) (cv h) cmq) cltq) (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cmp))) $;

--| Closure of multiplication on positive reals. First statement of Proposition 9-3.7 of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulclpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmp) cnp) $;

--| Addition of positive reals is commutative. Proposition 9-3.5(ii) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 19-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcompr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cpp) (co B2 A2 cpp) $;

--| Addition of positive reals is associative. Proposition 9-3.5(i) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addasspr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cpp) C cpp) (co A2 (co B2 C cpp) cpp) $;

--| Multiplication of positive reals is commutative. Proposition 9-3.7(ii) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 19-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcompr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmp) (co B2 A2 cmp) $;

--| Multiplication of positive reals is associative. Proposition 9-3.7(i) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulasspr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmp) C cmp) (co A2 (co B2 C cmp) cmp) $;

--| Lemma for distributive law for positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 1-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrlem1pr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel C cnp)) (wss (co A2 (co B2 C cpp) cmp) (co (co A2 B2 cmp) (co A2 C cmp) cpp)) $;

--| Lemma for distributive law for positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrlem4pr {x3 y1 z f2: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel C cnp)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv z) C)))) (wcel (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmq) (co (cv f2) (cv z) cmq) cplq) (co A2 (co B2 C cpp) cmp)) $;

--| Lemma for distributive law for positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrlem5pr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp) (wcel C cnp)) (wss (co (co A2 B2 cmp) (co A2 C cmp) cpp) (co A2 (co B2 C cpp) cmp)) $;

--| Multiplication of positive reals is distributive. Proposition 9-3.7(iii) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrpr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cpp) cmp) (co (co A2 B2 cmp) (co A2 C cmp) cpp) $;

--| 1 is an identity element for positive real multiplication. Theorem 9-3.7(iv) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1idpr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wceq (co A2 c1p cmp) A2) $;

--| Positive real 'less than' in terms of proper subset. (Contributed by NM, 20-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltprord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltp) (wpss A2 B2)) $;

--| Proper subset is a linear ordering on positive reals. Part of Proposition 9-3.3 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem psslinpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (w3o (wpss A2 B2) (wceq A2 B2) (wpss B2 A2)) $;

--| Positive real 'less than' is a strict ordering. Part of Proposition 9-3.3 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltsopr:
  $ wor cnp cltp $;

--| Lemma 9-3.4 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 25-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prlem934 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wrex x3 (wn (wcel (co (cv x3) B2 cplq) A2)) A2) $;

--| The sum of two positive reals is greater than one of them. Proposition 9-3.5(iii) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltaddpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cpp) cltp) $;

--| The sum of two positive reals is greater than one of them. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltaddpr2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnp) (wi (wceq (co A2 B2 cpp) C) (wbr A2 C cltp)) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cnp) (wi (wpss A2 B2) (wne C c0)) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cnp) (wpss C cnq) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cnp) (wi (wcel (cv x3) C) (wal z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) cltq) (wcel (cv z) C)))) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem4 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cnp) (wi (wcel (cv x3) C) (wex z (wa (wcel (cv z) C) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) cltq)))) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cnp) (wpss A2 B2)) (wcel C cnp) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem6 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wpss A2 B2)) (wss (co A2 C cpp) B2) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexprlem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1):
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wn (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cplq) B2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wpss A2 B2)) (wss B2 (co A2 C cpp)) $;

--| Proposition 9-3.5(iv) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltexpri {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cltp) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cpp) B2) cnp) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.5(v) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltaprlem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnp) (wi (wbr A2 B2 cltp) (wbr (co C A2 cpp) (co C B2 cpp) cltp)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition. Proposition 9-3.5(v) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltapr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnp) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltp) (wbr (co C A2 cpp) (co C B2 cpp) cltp)) $;

--| Addition cancellation law for positive reals. Proposition 9-3.5(vi) of [Gleason] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcanpr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wi (wceq (co A2 B2 cpp) (co A2 C cpp)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Lemma 9-3.6 of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prlem936 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wbr c1q B2 cltq)) (wrex x3 (wn (wcel (co (cv x3) B2 cmq) A2)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.7 of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem reclem2pr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) (wn (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crq) A2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.7(v) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem reclem3pr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) (wn (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crq) A2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wss c1p (co A2 B2 cmp)) $;

--| Lemma for Proposition 9-3.7(v) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem reclem4pr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltq) (wn (wcel (cfv (cv y1) crq) A2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmp) c1p) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive real exists. Part of Proposition 9-3.7(v) of [Gleason] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recexpr {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnp) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmp) c1p) cnp) $;

--| The union of a nonempty, bounded set of positive reals is a positive real. Part of Proposition 9-3.3 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem suplem1pr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltp) A2) cnp)) (wcel (cuni A2) cnp) $;

--| The union of a set of positive reals (if a positive real) is its supremum (the least upper bound). Part of Proposition 9-3.3 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem suplem2pr {z: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cnp) (wa (wi (wcel (cv y1) A2) (wn (wbr (cuni A2) (cv y1) cltp))) (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cuni A2) cltp) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltp) A2))) $;

--| The union of a nonempty, bounded set of positive reals has a supremum. Part of Proposition 9-3.3 of [Gleason] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem supexpr {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltp) A2) cnp)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltp)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltp) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltp) A2)) cnp)) cnp) $;

--| Define equivalence relation for signed reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_enr {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cer (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp cnp cnp)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp cnp cnp))) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv v) (cv u)))) (wceq (co (cv z) (cv u) cpp) (co (cv w) (cv v) cpp))))))))) $;

--| Define class of signed reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.2 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_nr:
  $ wceq cnr (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer) $;

--| Define addition on signed reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_plr {x3 y1 z w v u f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cplr (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cnr) (wcel (cv y1) cnr)) (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cop (cv w) (cv v)) cer)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cop (cv u) (cv f2)) cer))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (cop (co (cv w) (cv u) cpp) (co (cv v) (cv f2) cpp)) cer))))))))) $;

--| Define multiplication on signed reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mr {x3 y1 z w v u f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmr (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cnr) (wcel (cv y1) cnr)) (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cop (cv w) (cv v)) cer)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cop (cv u) (cv f2)) cer))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (cop (co (co (cv w) (cv u) cmp) (co (cv v) (cv f2) cmp) cpp) (co (co (cv w) (cv f2) cmp) (co (cv v) (cv u) cmp) cpp)) cer))))))))) $;

--| Define ordering relation on signed reals. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.4 of [Gleason] p. 127. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ltr {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cltr (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cnr) (wcel (cv y1) cnr)) (wex z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cop (cv z) (cv w)) cer)) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cop (cv v) (cv u)) cer))) (wbr (co (cv z) (cv u) cpp) (co (cv w) (cv v) cpp) cltp)))))))) $;

--| Define signed real constant 0. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.2 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_0r:
  $ wceq c0r (cec (cop c1p c1p) cer) $;

--| Define signed real constant 1. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. From Proposition 9-4.2 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_1r:
  $ wceq c1r (cec (cop (co c1p c1p cpp) c1p) cer) $;

--| Define signed real constant -1. This is a "temporary" set used in the construction of complex numbers ~ df-c , and is intended to be used only by the construction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_m1r:
  $ wceq cm1r (cec (cop c1p (co c1p c1p cpp)) cer) $;

--| The equivalence relation for signed reals is an equivalence relation. Proposition 9-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enrer:
  $ wer (cxp cnp cnp) cer $;

--| The class of signed reals is a set. Note that a shorter proof is possible using ~ qsex (and not requiring ~ enrer ), but it would add a dependency on ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) Extract proof from that of ~ axcnex . (Revised by BJ, 4-Feb-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nrex1:
  $ wcel cnr cvv $;

--| Equivalence relation for signed reals in terms of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enrbreq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) cer) (wceq (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp))) $;

--| Equivalence class equality of positive fractions in terms of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 29-Nov-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enreceq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wb (wceq (cec (cop A2 B2) cer) (cec (cop C D) cer)) (wceq (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp))) $;

--| The equivalence relation for signed reals exists. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem enrex:
  $ wcel cer cvv $;

--| Signed real 'less than' is a relation on signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrelsr:
  $ wss cltr (cxp cnr cnr) $;

--| Lemma showing compatibility of addition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcmpblnr (A2 B2 C D R S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wa (wa (wcel F cnp) (wcel G cnp)) (wa (wcel R cnp) (wcel S cnp)))) (wi (wa (wceq (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp)) (wceq (co F S cpp) (co G R cpp))) (wbr (cop (co A2 F cpp) (co B2 G cpp)) (cop (co C R cpp) (co D S cpp)) cer)) $;

--| Lemma used in lemma showing compatibility of multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcmpblnrlem (A2 B2 C D R S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp)) (wceq (co F S cpp) (co G R cpp))) (wceq (co (co D F cmp) (co (co (co A2 F cmp) (co B2 G cmp) cpp) (co (co C S cmp) (co D R cmp) cpp) cpp) cpp) (co (co D F cmp) (co (co (co A2 G cmp) (co B2 F cmp) cpp) (co (co C R cmp) (co D S cmp) cpp) cpp) cpp)) $;

--| Lemma showing compatibility of multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcmpblnr (A2 B2 C D R S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wa (wa (wcel F cnp) (wcel G cnp)) (wa (wcel R cnp) (wcel S cnp)))) (wi (wa (wceq (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp)) (wceq (co F S cpp) (co G R cpp))) (wbr (cop (co (co A2 F cmp) (co B2 G cmp) cpp) (co (co A2 G cmp) (co B2 F cmp) cpp)) (cop (co (co C R cmp) (co D S cmp) cpp) (co (co C S cmp) (co D R cmp) cpp)) cer)) $;

--| Decomposing signed reals into positive reals. Lemma for ~ addsrpr and ~ mulsrpr . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Dec-2019.)
theorem prsrlem1 (w v u t: setvar) (A2 B2: class) (f2 g2 h s1: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer)) (wcel B2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer))) (wa (wa (wceq A2 (cec (cop (cv w) (cv v)) cer)) (wceq B2 (cec (cop (cv u) (cv t)) cer))) (wa (wceq A2 (cec (cop (cv s1) (cv f2)) cer)) (wceq B2 (cec (cop (cv g2) (cv h)) cer))))) (wa (wa (wa (wa (wcel (cv w) cnp) (wcel (cv v) cnp)) (wa (wcel (cv s1) cnp) (wcel (cv f2) cnp))) (wa (wa (wcel (cv u) cnp) (wcel (cv t) cnp)) (wa (wcel (cv g2) cnp) (wcel (cv h) cnp)))) (wa (wceq (co (cv w) (cv f2) cpp) (co (cv v) (cv s1) cpp)) (wceq (co (cv u) (cv h) cpp) (co (cv t) (cv g2) cpp)))) $;

--| There is at most one result from adding signed reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Dec-2019.)
theorem addsrmo {z w v u t: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer)) (wcel B2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer))) (wmo z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex t (wa (wa (wceq A2 (cec (cop (cv w) (cv v)) cer)) (wceq B2 (cec (cop (cv u) (cv t)) cer))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (cop (co (cv w) (cv u) cpp) (co (cv v) (cv t) cpp)) cer)))))))) $;

--| There is at most one result from multiplying signed reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Dec-2019.)
theorem mulsrmo {z w v u t: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer)) (wcel B2 (cqs (cxp cnp cnp) cer))) (wmo z (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex t (wa (wa (wceq A2 (cec (cop (cv w) (cv v)) cer)) (wceq B2 (cec (cop (cv u) (cv t)) cer))) (wceq (cv z) (cec (cop (co (co (cv w) (cv u) cmp) (co (cv v) (cv t) cmp) cpp) (co (co (cv w) (cv t) cmp) (co (cv v) (cv u) cmp) cpp)) cer)))))))) $;

--| Addition of signed reals in terms of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addsrpr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wceq (co (cec (cop A2 B2) cer) (cec (cop C D) cer) cplr) (cec (cop (co A2 C cpp) (co B2 D cpp)) cer)) $;

--| Multiplication of signed reals in terms of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulsrpr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnp) (wcel B2 cnp)) (wa (wcel C cnp) (wcel D cnp))) (wceq (co (cec (cop A2 B2) cer) (cec (cop C D) cer) cmr) (cec (cop (co (co A2 C cmp) (co B2 D cmp) cpp) (co (co A2 D cmp) (co B2 C cmp) cpp)) cer)) $;

--| Ordering of signed reals in terms of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 20-Feb-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltsrpr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wb (wbr (cec (cop A2 B2) cer) (cec (cop C D) cer) cltr) (wbr (co A2 D cpp) (co B2 C cpp) cltp) $;

--| Greater than zero in terms of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem gt0srpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr c0r (cec (cop A2 B2) cer) cltr) (wbr B2 A2 cltp) $;

--| The empty set is not a signed real. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0nsr:
  $ wn (wcel c0 cnr) $;

--| The constant ` 0R ` is a signed real. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0r:
  $ wcel c0r cnr $;

--| The constant ` 1R ` is a signed real. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1sr:
  $ wcel c1r cnr $;

--| The constant ` -1R ` is a signed real. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem m1r:
  $ wcel cm1r cnr $;

--| Closure of addition on signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Jul-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addclsr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cplr) cnr) $;

--| Closure of multiplication on signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulclsr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmr) cnr) $;

--| Domain of addition on signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmaddsr:
  $ wceq (cdm cplr) (cxp cnr cnr) $;

--| Domain of multiplication on signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dmmulsr:
  $ wceq (cdm cmr) (cxp cnr cnr) $;

--| Addition of signed reals is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcomsr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cplr) (co B2 A2 cplr) $;

--| Addition of signed reals is associative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addasssr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cplr) C cplr) (co A2 (co B2 C cplr) cplr) $;

--| Multiplication of signed reals is commutative. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcomsr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmr) (co B2 A2 cmr) $;

--| Multiplication of signed reals is associative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulasssr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmr) C cmr) (co A2 (co B2 C cmr) cmr) $;

--| Multiplication of signed reals is distributive. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem distrsr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cplr) cmr) (co (co A2 B2 cmr) (co A2 C cmr) cplr) $;

--| Minus one plus one is zero for signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem m1p1sr:
  $ wceq (co cm1r c1r cplr) c0r $;

--| Minus one times minus one is plus one for signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem m1m1sr:
  $ wceq (co cm1r cm1r cmr) c1r $;

--| Signed real 'less than' is a strict ordering. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltsosr:
  $ wor cnr cltr $;

--| 0 is less than 1 for signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0lt1sr:
  $ wbr c0r c1r cltr $;

--| 1 and 0 are distinct for signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1ne0sr:
  $ wn (wceq c1r c0r) $;

--| The signed real number 0 is an identity element for addition of signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0idsr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnr) (wceq (co A2 c0r cplr) A2) $;

--| 1 is an identity element for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1idsr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnr) (wceq (co A2 c1r cmr) A2) $;

--| A signed real times 0 is 0. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _00sr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnr) (wceq (co A2 c0r cmr) c0r) $;

--| Ordering property of addition. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltasr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cnr) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltr) (wbr (co C A2 cplr) (co C B2 cplr) cltr)) $;

--| A signed real plus its negative is zero. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pn0sr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnr) (wceq (co A2 (co A2 cm1r cmr) cplr) c0r) $;

--| Existence of negative signed real. Part of Proposition 9-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem negexsr {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cnr) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cplr) c0r) cnr) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive signed real exists. Part of Proposition 9-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recexsrlem {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbr c0r A2 cltr) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmr) c1r) cnr) $;

--| The sum of two positive signed reals is positive. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addgt0sr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c0r A2 cltr) (wbr c0r B2 cltr)) (wbr c0r (co A2 B2 cplr) cltr) $;

--| The product of two positive signed reals is positive. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulgt0sr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr c0r A2 cltr) (wbr c0r B2 cltr)) (wbr c0r (co A2 B2 cmr) cltr) $;

--| The square of a nonzero signed real is positive. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sqgt0sr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wne A2 c0r)) (wbr c0r (co A2 A2 cmr) cltr) $;

--| The reciprocal of a nonzero signed real exists. Part of Proposition 9-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem recexsr {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wne A2 c0r)) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmr) c1r) cnr) $;

--| Mapping from positive signed reals to positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mappsrpr (A2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cnr $ >
  $ wb (wbr (co C cm1r cplr) (co C (cec (cop A2 c1p) cer) cplr) cltr) (wcel A2 cnp) $;

--| Mapping of order from positive signed reals to positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltpsrpr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cnr $ >
  $ wb (wbr (co C (cec (cop A2 c1p) cer) cplr) (co C (cec (cop B2 c1p) cer) cplr) cltr) (wbr A2 B2 cltp) $;

--| Equivalence for positive signed real. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem map2psrpr {x3: setvar} (A2 C: class):
  $ wcel C cnr $ >
  $ wb (wbr (co C cm1r cplr) A2 cltr) (wrex x3 (wceq (co C (cec (cop (cv x3) c1p) cer) cplr) A2) cnp) $;

--| Lemma for supremum theorem. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem supsrlem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab w (wcel (co C (cec (cop (cv w) c1p) cer) cplr) A2)) $ >
  $ wcel C cnr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C A2) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltr) A2) cnr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltr)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltr) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltr) A2)) cnr)) cnr) $;

--| A nonempty, bounded set of signed reals has a supremum. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem supsr {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltr) A2) cnr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltr)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltr) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltr) A2)) cnr)) cnr) $;

--| Class of complex numbers.
term cc: class;

--| Class of real numbers.
term cr: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the complex number 0.
term cc0: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the complex number 1.
term c1: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the complex number i.
term ci: class;

--| Addition on complex numbers.
term caddc: class;

--| 'Less than' predicate (defined over real subset of complex numbers).
term cltrr: class;

--| Multiplication on complex numbers. The token ` x. ` is a center dot.
term cmul: class;

--| Define the set of complex numbers. The 23 axioms for complex numbers start at ~ axresscn . (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_c:
  $ wceq cc (cxp cnr cnr) $;

--| Define the complex number 0. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_0:
  $ wceq cc0 (cop c0r c0r) $;

--| Define the complex number 1. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_1:
  $ wceq c1 (cop c1r c0r) $;

--| Define the complex number ` _i ` (the imaginary unit). (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_i:
  $ wceq ci (cop c0r c1r) $;

--| Define the set of real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_r:
  $ wceq cr (cxp cnr (csn c0r)) $;

--| Define addition over complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_add {x3 y1 z w v u f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq caddc (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cc) (wcel (cv y1) cc)) (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv w) (cv v))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv u) (cv f2)))) (wceq (cv z) (cop (co (cv w) (cv u) cplr) (co (cv v) (cv f2) cplr)))))))))) $;

--| Define multiplication over complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mul {x3 y1 z w v u f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmul (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cc) (wcel (cv y1) cc)) (wex w (wex v (wex u (wex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv w) (cv v))) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv u) (cv f2)))) (wceq (cv z) (cop (co (co (cv w) (cv u) cmr) (co cm1r (co (cv v) (cv f2) cmr) cmr) cplr) (co (co (cv v) (cv u) cmr) (co (cv w) (cv f2) cmr) cplr)))))))))) $;

--| Define 'less than' on the real subset of complex numbers. Proofs should typically use ` < ` instead; see ~ df-ltxr . (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_lt {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cltrr (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wcel (cv y1) cr)) (wex z (wex w (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cv z) c0r)) (wceq (cv y1) (cop (cv w) c0r))) (wbr (cv z) (cv w) cltr)))))) $;

--| Ordered pair membership in the class of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem opelcn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cop A2 B2) cc) (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) $;

--| Ordered pair membership in class of real subset of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem opelreal (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cop A2 c0r) cr) (wcel A2 cnr) $;

--| Membership in class of real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elreal {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cr) (wrex x3 (wceq (cop (cv x3) c0r) A2) cnr) $;

--| Ordered pair membership in the class of complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem elreal2 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel (cfv A2 c1st) cnr) (wceq A2 (cop (cfv A2 c1st) c0r))) $;

--| The empty set is not a complex number. Note: do not use this after the real number axioms are developed, since it is a construction-dependent property. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0ncn:
  $ wn (wcel c0 cc) $;

--| 'Less than' is a relation on real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltrelre:
  $ wss cltrr (cxp cr cr) $;

--| Addition of complex numbers in terms of signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcnsr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wa (wcel C cnr) (wcel D cnr))) (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) caddc) (cop (co A2 C cplr) (co B2 D cplr))) $;

--| Multiplication of complex numbers in terms of signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcnsr (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wa (wcel C cnr) (wcel D cnr))) (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) cmul) (cop (co (co A2 C cmr) (co cm1r (co B2 D cmr) cmr) cplr) (co (co B2 C cmr) (co A2 D cmr) cplr))) $;

--| Equality of real numbers in terms of intermediate signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem eqresr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (cop A2 c0r) (cop B2 c0r)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Addition of real numbers in terms of intermediate signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addresr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wceq (co (cop A2 c0r) (cop B2 c0r) caddc) (cop (co A2 B2 cplr) c0r)) $;

--| Multiplication of real numbers in terms of intermediate signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulresr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wceq (co (cop A2 c0r) (cop B2 c0r) cmul) (cop (co A2 B2 cmr) c0r)) $;

--| Ordering of real subset of complex numbers in terms of signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltresr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 c0r) (cop B2 c0r) cltrr) (wbr A2 B2 cltr) $;

--| Ordering of real subset of complex numbers in terms of signed reals. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ltresr2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wbr (cfv A2 c1st) (cfv B2 c1st) cltr)) $;

--| Technical trick to permit reuse of previous lemmas to prove arithmetic operation laws in ` CC ` from those in ` R. ` . The trick involves ~ qsid , which shows that the coset of the converse membership relation (which is not an equivalence relation) acts as an identity divisor for the quotient set operation. This lets us "pretend" that ` CC ` is a quotient set, even though it is not (compare ~ df-c ), and allows to reuse some of the equivalence class lemmas we developed for the transition from positive reals to signed reals, etc. (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dfcnqs:
  $ wceq cc (cqs (cxp cnr cnr) (ccnv cep)) $;

--| Technical trick to permit re-use of some equivalence class lemmas for operation laws. See ~ dfcnqs and ~ mulcnsrec . (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem addcnsrec (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wa (wcel C cnr) (wcel D cnr))) (wceq (co (cec (cop A2 B2) (ccnv cep)) (cec (cop C D) (ccnv cep)) caddc) (cec (cop (co A2 C cplr) (co B2 D cplr)) (ccnv cep))) $;

--| Technical trick to permit re-use of some equivalence class lemmas for operation laws. The trick involves ~ ecid , which shows that the coset of the converse membership relation (which is not an equivalence relation) leaves a set unchanged. See also ~ dfcnqs . _Note: This is the last lemma (from which the axioms will be derived) in_ _the construction of real and complex numbers. The construction starts_ _at_ ~ cnpi . (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcnsrec (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cnr) (wcel B2 cnr)) (wa (wcel C cnr) (wcel D cnr))) (wceq (co (cec (cop A2 B2) (ccnv cep)) (cec (cop C D) (ccnv cep)) cmul) (cec (cop (co (co A2 C cmr) (co cm1r (co B2 D cmr) cmr) cplr) (co (co B2 C cmr) (co A2 D cmr) cplr)) (ccnv cep))) $;

--| Addition is an operation on the complex numbers. This theorem can be used as an alternate axiom for complex numbers in place of the less specific ~ axaddcl . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-addf . (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axaddf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc caddc $;

--| Multiplication is an operation on the complex numbers. This is the construction-dependent version of ~ ax-mulf and it should not be referenced outside the construction. We generally prefer to develop our theory using the less specific ~ mulcl . (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axmulf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc cmul $;

--| The complex numbers form a set. This axiom is redundant in the presence of the other axioms (see ~ cnexALT ), but the proof requires the axiom of replacement, while the derivation from the construction here does not. Thus, we can avoid ~ ax-rep in later theorems by invoking Axiom ~ ax-cnex instead of ~ cnexALT . Use ~ cnex instead. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axcnex:
  $ wcel cc cvv $;

--| The real numbers are a subset of the complex numbers. Axiom 1 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-resscn . (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axresscn:
  $ wss cr cc $;

--| 1 is a complex number. Axiom 2 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1cn . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax1cn:
  $ wcel c1 cc $;

--| ` _i ` is a complex number. Axiom 3 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-icn . (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axicn:
  $ wcel ci cc $;

--| Closure law for addition of complex numbers. Axiom 4 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ addcl . (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cc) $;

--| Closure law for addition in the real subfield of complex numbers. Axiom 5 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addrcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ readdcl . (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axaddrcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of complex numbers. Axiom 6 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ mulcl . (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axmulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cc) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication in the real subfield of complex numbers. Axiom 7 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulrcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ remulcl . (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1996.)
theorem axmulrcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr) $;

--| Multiplication of complex numbers is commutative. Axiom 8 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulcom be used later. Instead, use ~ mulcom . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axmulcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| Addition of complex numbers is associative. This theorem transfers the associative laws for the real and imaginary signed real components of complex number pairs, to complex number addition itself. Axiom 9 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addass be used later. Instead, use ~ addass . (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axaddass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Multiplication of complex numbers is associative. Axiom 10 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-mulass . (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axmulass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Distributive law for complex numbers (left-distributivity). Axiom 11 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-distr be used later. Instead, use ~ adddi . (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axdistr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| i-squared equals -1 (expressed as i-squared plus 1 is 0). Axiom 12 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-i2m1 . (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axi2m1:
  $ wceq (co (co ci ci cmul) c1 caddc) cc0 $;

--| 1 and 0 are distinct. Axiom 13 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1ne0 . (Contributed by NM, 19-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax1ne0:
  $ wne c1 cc0 $;

--| ` 1 ` is an identity element for real multiplication. Axiom 14 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Weakened from the original axiom in the form of statement in ~ mulrid , based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1rid . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax1rid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) A2) $;

--| Existence of negative of real number. Axiom 15 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-rnegex . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axrnegex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) cc0) cr) $;

--| Existence of reciprocal of nonzero real number. Axiom 16 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-rrecex . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axrrecex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) c1) cr) $;

--| A complex number can be expressed in terms of two reals. Definition 10-1.1(v) of [Gleason] p. 130. Axiom 17 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-cnre . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axcnre {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) cr) cr) $;

--| Ordering on reals satisfies strict trichotomy. Axiom 18 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axlttri . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-lttri . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpre_lttri (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wn (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 cltrr)))) $;

--| Ordering on reals is transitive. Axiom 19 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axlttrn . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-lttrn . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpre_lttrn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wbr B2 C cltrr)) (wbr A2 C cltrr)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition on reals. Axiom 20 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axltadd . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-ltadd . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpre_ltadd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) cltrr)) $;

--| The product of two positive reals is positive. Axiom 21 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axmulgt0 . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpre_mulgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cltrr) (wbr cc0 B2 cltrr)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) cltrr)) $;

--| A nonempty, bounded-above set of reals has a supremum. Axiom 22 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version with ordering on extended reals is ~ axsup . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-sup . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axpre_sup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltrr) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltrr)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltrr) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltrr) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| A weak universe containing ` _om ` contains the complex number construction. This theorem is construction-dependent in the literal sense, but will also be satisfied by any other reasonable implementation of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem wuncn (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc U) $;

--| The complex numbers form a set. This axiom is redundant in the presence of the other axioms (see ~ cnexALT ), but the proof requires the axiom of replacement, while the derivation from the construction here does not. Thus, we can avoid ~ ax-rep in later theorems by invoking Axiom ~ ax-cnex instead of ~ cnexALT . Use ~ cnex instead. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_cnex:
  $ wcel cc cvv $;

--| The real numbers are a subset of the complex numbers. Axiom 1 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-resscn . (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 12-Aug-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_resscn:
  $ wss cr cc $;

--| 1 is a complex number. Axiom 2 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1cn . (Contributed by NM, 12-Apr-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_1cn:
  $ wcel c1 cc $;

--| ` _i ` is a complex number. Axiom 3 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-icn . (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_icn:
  $ wcel ci cc $;

--| Closure law for addition of complex numbers. Axiom 4 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ addcl . (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_addcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cc) $;

--| Closure law for addition in the real subfield of complex numbers. Axiom 5 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addrcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ readdcl . (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_addrcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of complex numbers. Axiom 6 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ mulcl . (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_mulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cc) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication in the real subfield of complex numbers. Axiom 7 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulrcl be used later. Instead, in most cases use ~ remulcl . (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1996.)
theorem ax_mulrcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr) $;

--| Multiplication of complex numbers is commutative. Axiom 8 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-mulcom be used later. Instead, use ~ mulcom . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_mulcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| Addition of complex numbers is associative. This theorem transfers the associative laws for the real and imaginary signed real components of complex number pairs, to complex number addition itself. Axiom 9 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-addass be used later. Instead, use ~ addass . (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_addass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Multiplication of complex numbers is associative. Axiom 10 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-mulass . (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_mulass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Distributive law for complex numbers (left-distributivity). Axiom 11 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly, nor should the proven axiom ~ ax-distr be used later. Instead, use ~ adddi . (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-1995.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_distr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| i-squared equals -1 (expressed as i-squared plus 1 is 0). Axiom 12 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-i2m1 . (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_i2m1:
  $ wceq (co (co ci ci cmul) c1 caddc) cc0 $;

--| 1 and 0 are distinct. Axiom 13 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1ne0 . (Contributed by NM, 19-Mar-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_1ne0:
  $ wne c1 cc0 $;

--| ` 1 ` is an identity element for real multiplication. Axiom 14 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Weakened from the original axiom in the form of statement in ~ mulrid , based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-1rid . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_1rid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) A2) $;

--| Existence of negative of real number. Axiom 15 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-rnegex . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_rnegex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) cc0) cr) $;

--| Existence of reciprocal of nonzero real number. Axiom 16 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-rrecex . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_rrecex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) c1) cr) $;

--| A complex number can be expressed in terms of two reals. Definition 10-1.1(v) of [Gleason] p. 130. Axiom 17 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-cnre . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_cnre {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) cr) cr) $;

--| Ordering on reals satisfies strict trichotomy. Axiom 18 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axlttri . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-lttri . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_pre_lttri (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wn (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 cltrr)))) $;

--| Ordering on reals is transitive. Axiom 19 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axlttrn . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-lttrn . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_pre_lttrn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wbr B2 C cltrr)) (wbr A2 C cltrr)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition on reals. Axiom 20 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axltadd . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-ltadd . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_pre_ltadd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 cltrr) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) cltrr)) $;

--| The product of two positive reals is positive. Axiom 21 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version for extended reals is ~ axmulgt0 . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 . (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1996.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_pre_mulgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cltrr) (wbr cc0 B2 cltrr)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) cltrr)) $;

--| A nonempty, bounded-above set of reals has a supremum. Axiom 22 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. Note: The more general version with ordering on extended reals is ~ axsup . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-pre-sup . (Contributed by NM, 19-May-1996.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_pre_sup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltrr) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltrr)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cltrr) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cltrr) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| Addition is an operation on the complex numbers. This theorem can be used as an alternate axiom for complex numbers in place of the less specific ~ axaddcl . This construction-dependent theorem should not be referenced directly; instead, use ~ ax-addf . (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_addf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc caddc $;

--| Multiplication is an operation on the complex numbers. This is the construction-dependent version of ~ ax-mulf and it should not be referenced outside the construction. We generally prefer to develop our theory using the less specific ~ mulcl . (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax_mulf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc cmul $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-cnex . See also ~ cnexALT . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem cnex:
  $ wcel cc cvv $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-addcl , for naming consistency with ~ addcli . Use this theorem instead of ~ ax-addcl or ~ axaddcl . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem addcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cc) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-addrcl , for naming consistency with ~ readdcli . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem readdcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-mulcl , for naming consistency with ~ mulcli . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem mulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cc) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-mulrcl , for naming consistency with ~ remulcli . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem remulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-mulcom , for naming consistency with ~ mulcomi . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem mulcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-addass , for naming consistency with ~ addassi . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem addass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-mulass , for naming consistency with ~ mulassi . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem mulass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-distr , for naming consistency with ~ adddii . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem adddi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| A real number is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1999.)
theorem recn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| The real numbers form a set. See also ~ reexALT . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem reex:
  $ wcel cr cvv $;

--| Reals are a subset of the pair of real and complex numbers. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem reelprrecn:
  $ wcel cr (cpr cr cc) $;

--| Complex numbers are a subset of the pair of real and complex numbers . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem cnelprrecn:
  $ wcel cc (cpr cr cc) $;

--| Addition is an operation on complex numbers. Version of ~ ax-addf using maps-to notation, proved from the axioms of set theory and ~ ax-addcl . (Contributed by GG, 31-Mar-2025.)
theorem mpoaddf {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc)) $;

--| Multiplication is an operation on complex numbers. Version of ~ ax-mulf using maps-to notation, proved from the axioms of set theory and ~ ax-mulcl . (Contributed by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem mpomulf {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul)) $;

--| Hypothesis for weak deduction theorem to eliminate ` A =/= 0 ` . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.)
theorem elimne0 (A2: class):
  $ wne (cif (wne A2 cc0) A2 c1) cc0 $;

--| Distributive law for complex numbers (right-distributivity). (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2004.)
theorem adddir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Zero is a complex number. See also ~ 0cnALT . (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2005.)
theorem _0cn:
  $ wcel cc0 cc $;

--| Zero is a complex number, deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0cnd (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 cc) $;

--| Zero is a set. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Jul-2016.)
theorem c0ex:
  $ wcel cc0 cvv $;

--| 0 is an element of ` { 0 , 1 } ` . (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _0elpr01:
  $ wcel cc0 (cpr cc0 c1) $;

--| One is a complex number, deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 6-Dec-2018.)
theorem _1cnd (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wcel c1 cc) $;

--| One is a set. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Jul-2016.)
theorem _1ex:
  $ wcel c1 cvv $;

--| 1 is an element of ` { 0 , 1 } ` . (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _1elpr01:
  $ wcel c1 (cpr cc0 c1) $;

--| Alias for ~ ax-cnre , for naming consistency. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem cnre {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) cr) cr) $;

--| The number 1 is an identity element for multiplication. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mulrid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) A2) $;

--| Identity law for multiplication. See ~ mulrid for commuted version. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-1999.)
theorem mullid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co c1 A2 cmul) A2) $;

--| The number 1 is real. This used to be one of our postulates for complex numbers, but Eric Schmidt discovered that it could be derived from a weaker postulate, ~ ax-1cn , by exploiting properties of the imaginary unit ` _i ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem _1re:
  $ wcel c1 cr $;

--| The number 1 is real, deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 6-Dec-2018.)
theorem _1red (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wcel c1 cr) $;

--| The number 0 is real. Remark: the first step could also be ~ ax-icn . See also ~ 0reALT . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 21-May-2007.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 11-Oct-2022.)
theorem _0re:
  $ wcel cc0 cr $;

--| The number 0 is real, deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 6-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0red (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 cr) $;

--| The pair ` { 0 , 1 } ` is a subset of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Aug-2017.)
theorem pr01ssre:
  $ wss (cpr cc0 c1) cr $;

--| Identity law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1995.)
theorem mulridi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) A2 $;

--| Identity law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1995.)
theorem mullidi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co c1 A2 cmul) A2 $;

--| Closure law for addition. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem addcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cc $;

--| Closure law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem mulcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cc $;

--| Commutative law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem mulcomi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul) $;

--| Commutative law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem mulcomli (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 A2 cmul) C $;

--| Associative law for addition. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem addassi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) $;

--| Associative law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem mulassi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) $;

--| Distributive law (left-distributivity). (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem adddii (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul) caddc) $;

--| Distributive law (right-distributivity). (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1995.)
theorem adddiri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) caddc) $;

--| A real number is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-1995.)
theorem recni (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel A2 cc $;

--| Closure law for addition of reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jan-1997.)
theorem readdcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jan-1997.)
theorem remulcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr $;

--| Identity law for multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulridd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) A2) $;

--| Identity law for multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mullidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 A2 cmul) A2) $;

--| Closure law for addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cc) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cc) $;

--| Commutative law for multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| Associative law for addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addassd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Associative law for multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulassd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Distributive law (left-distributivity). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem adddid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Distributive law (right-distributivity). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem adddird (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Distributive law, plus 1 version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem adddirp1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 c1 caddc) B2 cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Join AB+CB into (A+C) on LHS. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 26-Oct-2019.)
theorem joinlmuladdmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) caddc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 C caddc) B2 cmul) D) $;

--| Deduction from real number to complex number. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-1999.)
theorem recnd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| Closure law for addition of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem readdcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem remulcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr) $;

--| Plus infinity.
term cpnf: class;

--| Minus infinity.
term cmnf: class;

--| The set of extended reals (includes plus and minus infinity).
term cxr: class;

--| 'Less than' predicate (extended to include the extended reals).
term clt: class;

--| Extend wff notation to include the 'less than or equal to' relation.
term cle: class;

--| Define plus infinity. Note that the definition is arbitrary, requiring only that ` +oo ` be a set not in ` RR ` and different from ` -oo ` ( ~ df-mnf ). We use ` ~P U. CC ` to make it independent of the construction of ` CC ` , and Cantor's Theorem will show that it is different from any member of ` CC ` and therefore ` RR ` . See ~ pnfnre , ~ mnfnre , and ~ pnfnemnf . A simpler possibility is to define ` +oo ` as ` CC ` and ` -oo ` as ` { CC } ` , but that approach requires the Axiom of Regularity to show that ` +oo ` and ` -oo ` are different from each other and from all members of ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_pnf:
  $ wceq cpnf (cpw (cuni cc)) $;

--| Define minus infinity as the power set of plus infinity. Note that the definition is arbitrary, requiring only that ` -oo ` be a set not in ` RR ` and different from ` +oo ` (see ~ mnfnre and ~ pnfnemnf ). (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mnf:
  $ wceq cmnf (cpw cpnf) $;

--| Define the set of extended reals that includes plus and minus infinity. Definition 12-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 173. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
axiom df_xr:
  $ wceq cxr (cun cr (cpr cpnf cmnf)) $;

--| Define 'less than' on the set of extended reals. Definition 12-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 173. Note that in our postulates for complex numbers, ` <RR ` is primitive and not necessarily a relation on ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
axiom df_ltxr {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clt (cun (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wcel (cv y1) cr) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cltrr))) (cun (cxp (cun cr (csn cmnf)) (csn cpnf)) (cxp (csn cmnf) cr))) $;

--| Define 'less than or equal to' on the extended real subset of complex numbers. Theorem ~ leloe relates it to 'less than' for reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
axiom df_le:
  $ wceq cle (cdif (cxp cxr cxr) (ccnv clt)) $;

--| Plus infinity is not a real number. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem pnfnre:
  $ wnel cpnf cr $;

--| Plus infinity is not a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem pnfnre2:
  $ wn (wcel cpnf cr) $;

--| Minus infinity is not a real number. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem mnfnre:
  $ wnel cmnf cr $;

--| The standard reals are a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem ressxr:
  $ wss cr cxr $;

--| The Cartesian product of standard reals are a subset of the Cartesian product of extended reals. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem rexpssxrxp:
  $ wss (cxp cr cr) (cxp cxr cxr) $;

--| A standard real is an extended real. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem rexr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| Zero is an extended real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2014.)
theorem _0xr:
  $ wcel cc0 cxr $;

--| No (finite) real equals plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem renepnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cpnf) $;

--| No real equals minus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem renemnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cmnf) $;

--| A standard real is an extended real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rexrd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| No (finite) real equals plus infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem renepnfd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cpnf) $;

--| No real equals minus infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem renemnfd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cmnf) $;

--| Plus infinity exists. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.) (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 7-Dec-2022.)
theorem pnfex:
  $ wcel cpnf cvv $;

--| Plus infinity belongs to the set of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 29-Aug-2011.)
theorem pnfxr:
  $ wcel cpnf cxr $;

--| Plus and minus infinity are different elements of ` RR* ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem pnfnemnf:
  $ wne cpnf cmnf $;

--| Minus and plus infinity are different. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem mnfnepnf:
  $ wne cmnf cpnf $;

--| Minus infinity belongs to the set of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 29-Aug-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem mnfxr:
  $ wcel cmnf cxr $;

--| A standard real is an extended real (inference form.) (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem rexri (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel A2 cxr $;

--| ` 1 ` is an extended real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem _1xr:
  $ wcel c1 cxr $;

--| The reals and the infinities are disjoint. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem renfdisj:
  $ wceq (cin cr (cpr cpnf cmnf)) c0 $;

--| "Less than" is a relation on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ltrelxr:
  $ wss clt (cxp cxr cxr) $;

--| "Less than" is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem ltrel:
  $ wrel clt $;

--| "Less than or equal to" is a relation on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem lerelxr:
  $ wss cle (cxp cxr cxr) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" is a relation. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem lerel:
  $ wrel cle $;

--| "Less than or equal to" expressed in terms of "less than", for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem xrlenlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" expressed in terms of "less than", for extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xrlenltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| "Less than" expressed in terms of "less than or equal to", for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.)
theorem xrltnle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| 'Less than' in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem xrltnled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| "Not less than" implies "less than or equal to". (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem xrnltled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| The three (non-exclusive) possibilities implied by a subset of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem ssxr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (w3o (wss A2 cr) (wcel cpnf A2) (wcel cmnf A2)) $;

--| The standard less-than ` <RR ` and the extended real less-than ` < ` are identical when restricted to the non-extended reals ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ltxrlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 cltrr)) $;

--| Ordering on reals satisfies strict trichotomy. Axiom 18 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. (This restates ~ ax-pre-lttri with ordering on the extended reals.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem axlttri (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 clt)))) $;

--| Ordering on reals is transitive. Axiom 19 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. This restates ~ ax-pre-lttrn with ordering on the extended reals. New proofs should use ~ lttr instead for naming consistency. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem axlttrn (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| Ordering property of addition on reals. Axiom 20 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. (This restates ~ ax-pre-ltadd with ordering on the extended reals.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem axltadd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) clt)) $;

--| The product of two positive reals is positive. Axiom 21 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. (This restates ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 with ordering on the extended reals.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem axmulgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| A nonempty, bounded-above set of reals has a supremum. Axiom 22 of 22 for real and complex numbers, derived from ZF set theory. (This restates ~ ax-pre-sup with ordering on the extended reals.) (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.)
theorem axsup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) clt) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| Alias for ~ axlttrn , for naming consistency with ~ lttri . New proofs should generally use this instead of ~ ax-pre-lttrn . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem lttr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| The product of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2008.)
theorem mulgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' expressed in terms of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1999.)
theorem lenlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jul-2005.)
theorem ltnle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| 'Less than' is a strict ordering. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-1997.)
theorem ltso:
  $ wor cr clt $;

--| 'Greater than' is a strict ordering. (Contributed by JJ, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem gtso:
  $ wor cr (ccnv clt) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-1999.)
theorem lttri2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wne A2 B2) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Trichotomy law for 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-1999.)
theorem lttri3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)))) $;

--| Trichotomy law for 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem lttri4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (w3o (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Trichotomy law. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem letri3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' expressed in terms of 'less than' or 'equals'. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1999.)
theorem leloe (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq A2 B2))) $;

--| Equality in terms of 'less than or equal to', 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2001.)
theorem eqlelt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)))) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.)
theorem ltle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' implies 'less than' is not 'equals'. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
theorem leltne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wne B2 A2)) $;

--| Transitive law. (Contributed by NM, 23-May-1999.)
theorem lelttr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| Transitive law, weaker form of ~ lelttr . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2018.)
theorem leltletr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C cle)) $;

--| Transitive law. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.)
theorem ltletr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| Transitive law, weaker form of ~ ltletr . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2018.)
theorem ltleletr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C cle)) $;

--| Transitive law. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-1999.)
theorem letr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' is irreflexive. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem ltnr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wn (wbr A2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' is reflexive. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem leid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 A2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem ltne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wne B2 A2) $;

--| 'Less than' is not symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.)
theorem ltnsym (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| 'Less than' is antisymmetric and irreflexive. (Contributed by NM, 13-Aug-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem ltnsym2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wn (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Trichotomy law. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem letric (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1999.)
theorem ltlen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wne B2 A2))) $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2005.)
theorem eqle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wceq A2 B2)) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eqled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltadd2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) clt)) $;

--| A nonzero nonnegative number is positive. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2007.)
theorem ne0gt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wb (wne A2 cc0) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) $;

--| Ordering elimination by cases. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jul-2007.)
theorem lecasei (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr A2 B2 cle)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr B2 A2 cle)) ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Trichotomy law. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2005.)
theorem lelttric (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Ordering elimination by cases. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jul-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltlecasei (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr A2 B2 clt)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr B2 A2 cle)) ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| 'Less than' is irreflexive. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem ltnri (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wn (wbr A2 A2 clt) $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 23-May-1999.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 20-Mar-2018.)
theorem eqlei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 20-Mar-2018.)
theorem eqlei2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wceq B2 A2) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Sep-2013.)
theorem gtneii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 clt $ >
  $ wne B2 A2 $;

--| 'Greater than' implies not equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem ltneii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 clt $ >
  $ wne A2 B2 $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-1997.)
theorem lttri2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wne A2 B2) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem lttri3i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem letri3i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' in terms of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem leloei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1999.)
theorem ltleni (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wne B2 A2)) $;

--| 'Less than' is not symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1999.)
theorem ltnsymi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' in terms of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-1999.)
theorem lenlti (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jul-2005.)
theorem ltnlei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem ltlei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal to' (inference). (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-1999.)
theorem ltleii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 clt $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 cle $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem ltnei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wne B2 A2) $;

--| Trichotomy law for 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem letrii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than' is transitive. Theorem I.17 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem lttri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to', 'less than' transitive law. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem lelttri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| 'Less than', 'less than or equal to' transitive law. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem ltletri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem letri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C cle) $;

--| Extended trichotomy law for 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2000.)
theorem le2tri3i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wb (w3a (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr C A2 cle)) (w3a (wceq A2 B2) (wceq B2 C) (wceq C A2)) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-1997.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem ltadd2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) clt) $;

--| The product of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem mulgt0i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| The product of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1999.)
theorem mulgt0ii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt $;

--| 'Less than' is irreflexive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltnrd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem gtned (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 A2) $;

--| 'Greater than' implies not equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltned (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $;

--| A nonzero nonnegative number is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ne0gt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| Ordering on reals satisfies strict trichotomy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lttrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 clt)))) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lttri2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wne A2 B2) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lttri3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)))) $;

--| Trichotomy law for 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem lttri4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq A2 B2) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem letri3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' in terms of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem leloed (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wo (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq A2 B2))) $;

--| Equality in terms of 'less than or equal to', 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2001.)
theorem eqleltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)))) $;

--| 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltlend (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wne B2 A2))) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' in terms of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lenltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| 'Less than' in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltnled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltnsymd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Not less than ' implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem nltled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' implies 'not less than'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lensymd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Trichotomy law for 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem letrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' implies 'less than' is not 'equals'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem leltned (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wne B2 A2)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' and 'not equals' implies 'less than'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem leneltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $;

--| The product of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulgt0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltadd2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) clt)) $;

--| Transitive law deduction for 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2005.)
theorem letrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $;

--| Transitive law deduction for 'less than or equal to', 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2006.)
theorem lelttrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltadd2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) clt) $;

--| Transitive law deduction for 'less than', 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2006.)
theorem ltletrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Transitive law deduction for 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2006.)
theorem lttrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| If a number is less than another number, and the other number is less than or equal to a third number, the first number is less than the third number. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 24-Mar-2018.)
theorem lelttrdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| The Dedekind cut theorem. This theorem, which may be used to replace ~ ax-pre-sup with appropriate adjustments, states that, if ` A ` completely preceeds ` B ` , then there is some number separating the two of them. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2013.)
theorem dedekind {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wss B2 cr) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt) B2) A2)) (wrex z (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) cle) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle)) B2) A2) cr) $;

--| The Dedekind cut theorem, with the hypothesis weakened to only require non-strict less than. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jul-2013.)
theorem dedekindle {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wss B2 cr) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) B2) A2)) (wrex z (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) cle) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle)) B2) A2) cr) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem mul12 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co B2 (co A2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-1999.)
theorem mul32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) B2 cmul)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul31 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co (co C B2 cmul) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 factors. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-1999.)
theorem mul4 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 factors: swap the right factors in the factors of a product of two products. (Contributed by AV, 4-Mar-2023.)
theorem mul4r (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) cmul) (co (co A2 D cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cmul)) $;

--| A simple product of sums expansion. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2005.)
theorem muladd11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 caddc) (co c1 B2 caddc) cmul) (co (co c1 A2 caddc) (co B2 (co A2 B2 cmul) caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Two times a number. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem _1p1times (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (co c1 c1 caddc) A2 cmul) (co A2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| A theorem for complex numbers analogous the second Peano postulate ~ peano2nn . (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2005.)
theorem peano2cn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cc) $;

--| A theorem for reals analogous the second Peano postulate ~ peano2nn . (Contributed by NM, 5-Jul-2005.)
theorem peano2re (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cr) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition over the reals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem readdcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wceq (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| ` 0 ` is its own additive identity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem _00id:
  $ wceq (co cc0 cc0 caddc) cc0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ mul02 . If any real does not produce ` 0 ` when multiplied by ` 0 ` , then any complex is equal to double itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul02lem1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne (co cc0 A2 cmul) cc0)) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq B2 (co B2 B2 caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mul02 . Zero times a real is zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul02lem2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (co cc0 A2 cmul) cc0) $;

--| Multiplication by ` 0 ` . Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1999.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul02 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc0 A2 cmul) cc0) $;

--| Multiplication by ` 0 ` . Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul01 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 cc0 cmul) cc0) $;

--| ` 0 ` is an additive identity. This used to be one of our complex number axioms, until it was found to be dependent on the others. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addrid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 cc0 caddc) A2) $;

--| Existence of the negative of a complex number. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 21-May-2007.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem cnegex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) cc0) cc) $;

--| Existence of a left inverse for addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem cnegex2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wrex x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) A2 caddc) cc0) cc) $;

--| ` 0 ` is a left identity for addition. This used to be one of our complex number axioms, until it was discovered that it was dependent on the others. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addlid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc0 A2 caddc) A2) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. Theorem I.1 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 22-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addcan2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Addition is commutative. This used to be one of our complex number axioms, until it was found to be dependent on the others. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| ` 0 ` is an additive identity. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addridi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 cc0 caddc) A2 $;

--| ` 0 ` is a left identity for addition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addlidi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co cc0 A2 caddc) A2 $;

--| Multiplication by 0. Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.)
theorem mul02i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co cc0 A2 cmul) cc0 $;

--| Multiplication by ` 0 ` . Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-1994.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem mul01i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 cc0 cmul) cc0 $;

--| Addition is commutative. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addcomi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc) $;

--| Addition is commutative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem addcomli (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 A2 caddc) C $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. Theorem I.1 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addcani (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) (wceq B2 C) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. Theorem I.1 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-2003.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.)
theorem addcan2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first two factors in a triple product. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem mul12i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co B2 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two factors in a triple product. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem mul32i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) B2 cmul) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 factors. (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1995.)
theorem mul4i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wcel D cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cmul) $;

--| Multiplication by 0. Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul02d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 A2 cmul) cc0) $;

--| Multiplication by ` 0 ` . Theorem I.6 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul01d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 cc0 cmul) cc0) $;

--| ` 0 ` is an additive identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addridd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 cc0 caddc) A2) $;

--| ` 0 ` is a left identity for addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addlidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 A2 caddc) A2) $;

--| Addition is commutative. Based on ideas by Eric Schmidt. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jan-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. Theorem I.1 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addcand (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Cancellation law for addition. Theorem I.1 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addcan2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Cancelling a term on the left-hand side of a sum in an equality. Consequence of ~ addcand . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem addcanad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $;

--| Cancelling a term on the right-hand side of a sum in an equality. Consequence of ~ addcan2d . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem addcan2ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Introducing a term on the left-hand side of a sum in a negated equality. Contrapositive of ~ addcanad . Consequence of ~ addcand . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem addneintrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) $;

--| Introducing a term on the right-hand side of a sum in a negated equality. Contrapositive of ~ addcan2ad . Consequence of ~ addcan2d . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem addneintr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first two factors in a triple product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co B2 (co A2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two factors in a triple product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul32d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) B2 cmul)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul31d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co (co C B2 cmul) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 factors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cmul)) $;

--| A simple product of sums expansion. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem muladd11r (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 c1 caddc) (co B2 c1 caddc) cmul) (co (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 B2 caddc) caddc) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Commute RHS addition, in deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem comraddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (co B2 C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (co C B2 caddc)) $;

--| Commute RHS addition. See ~ addcomli to commute addition on LHS. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem comraddi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co B2 C caddc) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co C B2 caddc) $;

--| Adding a negative number to another number decreases it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ltaddneg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 clt) (wbr (co B2 A2 caddc) B2 clt)) $;

--| Adding a negative number to another number decreases it. (Contributed by AV, 19-Mar-2021.)
theorem ltaddnegr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) B2 clt)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2004.)
theorem add12 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) (co B2 (co A2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by NM, 13-Nov-1999.)
theorem add32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum, rearranging the parentheses. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-May-2007.)
theorem add32r (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by NM, 13-Nov-1999.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem add4 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 D caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2005.)
theorem add42 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co D B2 caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-1997.)
theorem add12i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) (co B2 (co A2 C caddc) caddc) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-1997.)
theorem add32i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) B2 caddc) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.)
theorem add4i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wcel D cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 D caddc) caddc) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-1999.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem add42i (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wcel D cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co D B2 caddc) caddc) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem add12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) (co B2 (co A2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem add32d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem add4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 D caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem add42d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co D B2 caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include subtraction.
term cmin: class;

--| Extend class notation to include unary minus. The symbol ` -u ` is not a class by itself but part of a compound class definition. We do this rather than making it a formal function since it is so commonly used. Note: We use different symbols for unary minus ( ` -u ` ) and subtraction ~ cmin ( ` - ` ) to prevent syntax ambiguity. For example, looking at the syntax definition ~ co , if we used the same symbol then " ` ( - A - B ) ` " could mean either " ` - A ` " minus " ` B ` ", or it could represent the (meaningless) operation of classes " ` - ` " and " ` - B ` " connected with "operation" " ` A ` ". On the other hand, " ` ( -u A - B ) ` " is unambiguous.
term cneg (A2: class): class;

--| Define subtraction. Theorem ~ subval shows its value (and describes how this definition works), Theorem ~ subaddi relates it to addition, and Theorems ~ subcli and ~ resubcli prove its closure laws. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1994.)
axiom df_sub {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmin (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (crio z (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv z) caddc) (cv x3)) cc)) $;

--| Define the negative of a number (unary minus). We use different symbols for unary minus ( ` -u ` ) and subtraction ( ` - ` ) to prevent syntax ambiguity. See ~ cneg for a discussion of this. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1995.)
axiom df_neg (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cneg A2) (co cc0 A2 cmin) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 0cn which does not reference ~ ax-1cn . (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 7-Jan-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0cnALT:
  $ wcel cc0 cc $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 0cnALT which is shorter, but depends on ~ ax-8 , ~ ax-13 , ~ ax-sep , ~ ax-nul , ~ ax-pow , ~ ax-pr , ~ ax-un , and every complex number axiom except ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 and ~ ax-pre-sup . (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0cnALT2:
  $ wcel cc0 cc $;

--| Existential uniqueness of negatives. Theorem I.2 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 22-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem negeu {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wreu x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) B2) cc) $;

--| Value of subtraction, which is the (unique) element ` x ` such that ` B + x = A ` . (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Nov-2013.)
theorem subval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) (crio x3 (wceq (co B2 (cv x3) caddc) A2) cc)) $;

--| Equality theorem for negatives. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1995.)
theorem negeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cneg A2) (cneg B2)) $;

--| Equality inference for negatives. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-1995.)
theorem negeqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cneg A2) (cneg B2) $;

--| Equality deduction for negatives. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem negeqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cneg A2) (cneg B2)) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ nfneg . (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfnegd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cneg A2)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the negative of a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jun-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2016.)
theorem nfneg {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cneg A2) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of the negative of a number. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem csbnegg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (cneg B2)) (cneg (csb x3 A2 B2))) $;

--| A negative is a set. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2005.)
theorem negex (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cneg A2) cvv $;

--| Closure law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Dec-2013.)
theorem subcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) cc) $;

--| Closure law for negative. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-2003.)
theorem negcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cneg A2) cc) $;

--| ` -u _i ` is a complex number. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Dec-2018.)
theorem negicn:
  $ wcel (cneg ci) cc $;

--| Subtraction is an operation on the complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem subf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc cmin $;

--| Relationship between subtraction and addition. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Dec-2013.)
theorem subadd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) C) (wceq (co B2 C caddc) A2)) $;

--| Relationship between subtraction and addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Jul-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem subadd2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) C) (wceq (co C B2 caddc) A2)) $;

--| Swap subtrahend and result of subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2007.)
theorem subsub23 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) C) (wceq (co A2 C cmin) B2)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem pncan (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) B2 cmin) A2) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 17-Apr-2005.)
theorem pncan2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) A2 cmin) B2) $;

--| Subtraction and addition of equals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Mar-2005.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 8-Jan-2023.)
theorem pncan3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 A2 cmin) caddc) B2) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem npcan (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) B2 caddc) A2) $;

--| Associative-type law for addition and subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-2003.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem addsubass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmin) (co A2 (co B2 C cmin) caddc)) $;

--| Law for addition and subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2001.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem addsub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmin) (co (co A2 C cmin) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for addition and subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.)
theorem subadd23 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C caddc) (co A2 (co C B2 cmin) caddc)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for addition and subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.)
theorem addsub12 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmin) caddc) (co B2 (co A2 C cmin) caddc)) $;

--| Law for subtraction and addition. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2005.)
theorem _2addsub (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) D cmin) (co (co (co A2 C caddc) D cmin) B2 caddc)) $;

--| Relation between sums and differences. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 17-Jun-2010.)
theorem addsubeq4 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc)) (wceq (co C A2 cmin) (co B2 D cmin))) $;

--| Subtraction and addition of equals. Almost but not exactly the same as ~ pncan3i and ~ pncan , this order happens often when applying "operations to both sides" so create a theorem specifically for it. A deduction version of this is available as ~ pncand . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem pncan3oi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) B2 cmin) A2 $;

--| Move the right term in a sum on the RHS to the LHS. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem mvrraddi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co B2 C caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 C cmin) B2 $;

--| Move the left term in a sum on the RHS to the LHS. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem mvrladdi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co B2 C caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) C $;

--| Move the left term in a sum on the LHS to the RHS. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem mvlladdi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co C A2 cmin) $;

--| Subtraction of a number from itself. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem subid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 A2 cmin) cc0) $;

--| Identity law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem subid1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 cc0 cmin) A2) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.)
theorem npncan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co B2 C cmin) caddc) (co A2 C cmin)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2005.)
theorem nppcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C caddc) B2 caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction: ((a-b)-c)+b = a-c holds for complex numbers a,b,c. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 24-Mar-2018.)
theorem nnpcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C cmin) B2 caddc) (co A2 C cmin)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2015.)
theorem nppcan3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 caddc) caddc) (co A2 C caddc)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.)
theorem subcan2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| If the difference between two numbers is zero, they are equal. (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-1999.)
theorem subeq0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) cc0) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

