  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin) cle)) $;

--| Subtraction of both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lesub2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C B2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) cle)) $;

--| Subtraction from both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltsub1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin) clt)) $;

--| Subtraction of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltsub2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C B2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) clt)) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. Theorem I.18 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltadd1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc) clt) $;

--| Subtraction from both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltsub1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin) clt) $;

--| Subtraction of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltsub2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C B2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) clt) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem leadd1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc) cle) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem leadd2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 caddc) (co C B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| Subtraction from both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem lesub1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin) cle) $;

--| Subtraction of both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem lesub2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C B2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) cle) $;

--| The result of subtracting a number less than or equal to an intermediate number from a number greater than or equal to a third number increased by the intermediate number is greater than or equal to the third number. (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.)
theorem lesub3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co X C caddc) A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (co A2 B2 cmin) cle) $;

--| Adding both side of two inequalities. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.) (Proof shortened by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem le2addd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) cle) $;

--| Subtracting both sides of two 'less than or equal to' relations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem le2subd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 D cmin) (co C B2 cmin) cle) $;

--| Adding both sides of two orderings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltleaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) clt) $;

--| Adding both sides of two orderings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem leltaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) clt) $;

--| Adding both side of two inequalities. Theorem I.25 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lt2addd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) clt) $;

--| Subtracting both sides of two 'less than' relations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lt2subd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 D cmin) (co C B2 cmin) clt) $;

--| Condition for a positive sum. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem possumd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 caddc) clt) (wbr (cneg B2) A2 clt)) $;

--| When a subtraction gives a negative result. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sublt0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cmin) cc0 clt) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| Addition and subtraction on one side of 'less than'. (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2018.)
theorem ltaddsublt (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wbr (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmin) A2 clt)) $;

--| One is less than or equal to one. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 16-Jul-2016.)
theorem _1le1:
  $ wbr c1 c1 cle $;

--| ` _i ` times itself is minus 1. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem ixi:
  $ wceq (co ci ci cmul) (cneg c1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ recex . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 23-May-2007.)
theorem recextlem1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) cmin) cmul) (co (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ recex . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 23-May-2007.)
theorem recextlem2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wne (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cc0)) (wne (co (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) caddc) cc0) $;

--| Existence of reciprocal of nonzero complex number. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 22-May-2007.)
theorem recex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) c1) cc) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication. Theorem I.7 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcand (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication. Theorem I.7 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcan2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Cancellation of a nonzero factor on the left in an equation. One-way deduction form of ~ mulcand . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem mulcanad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Cancellation of a nonzero factor on the right in an equation. One-way deduction form of ~ mulcan2d . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem mulcan2ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication (full theorem form). Theorem I.7 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulcan2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Cancellation law for multiplication. Theorem I.7 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jan-1995.)
theorem mulcani (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wne C cc0 $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| If a product is zero, one of its factors must be zero. Theorem I.11 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul0or (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) (wo (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0))) $;

--| The product of two nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem mulne0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wa (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0)) $;

--| The product of two nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2007.)
theorem mulne0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) $;

--| The product of two nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-1995.)
theorem mulne0i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wne B2 cc0 $ >
  $ wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0 $;

--| Property of numbers whose product equals their sum. Equation 5 of [Kreyszig] p. 12. (Contributed by NM, 13-Nov-2006.)
theorem muleqadd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 B2 caddc)) (wceq (co (co A2 c1 cmin) (co B2 c1 cmin) cmul) c1)) $;

--| Existential uniqueness of reciprocals. Theorem I.8 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem receu {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wreu x3 (wceq (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) A2) cc) $;

--| Multiplication maps nonzero complex numbers to nonzero complex numbers. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 23-Feb-2007.)
theorem mulnzcnf:
  $ wf (cxp (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cres cmul (cxp (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) $;

--| If a product is zero, one of its factors must be zero. Theorem I.11 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 7-Oct-1999.)
theorem mul0ori (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) (wo (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0)) $;

--| If a product is zero, one of its factors must be zero. Theorem I.11 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mul0ord (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) (wo (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0))) $;

--| A number is zero iff its square is zero (where square is represented using multiplication). (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem msq0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 A2 cmul) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| A number is zero iff its square is zero (where square is represented using multiplication). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem msq0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 A2 cmul) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| The product of two nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulne0bd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0)) $;

--| The product of two nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem mulne0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) $;

--| A generalized form of the cancellation law for multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulcan1g (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 C cmul)) (wo (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 C))) $;

--| A generalized form of the cancellation law for multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulcan2g (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul)) (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C cc0))) $;

--| A factor of a nonzero complex number is nonzero. Partial converse of ~ mulne0d and consequence of ~ mulne0bd . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem mulne0bad (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| A factor of a nonzero complex number is nonzero. Partial converse of ~ mulne0d and consequence of ~ mulne0bd . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem mulne0bbd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $;

--| Extend class notation to include division.
term cdiv: class;

--| Define division. Theorem ~ divmuli relates it to multiplication, and ~ divcli and ~ redivcli prove its closure laws. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-1995.) Use ~ divval instead. (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_div {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdiv (cmpo x3 y1 cc (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (crio z (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv z) cmul) (cv x3)) cc)) $;

--| You can't divide by zero, because division explicitly excludes zero from the domain of the function. Thus, by the definition of function value, it evaluates to the empty set. (This theorem is for information only and normally is not referenced by other proofs. To be meaningful, it assumes that ` (/) ` is not a complex number, which depends on the particular complex number construction that is used.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by SN, 5-Jun-2025.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1div0:
  $ wceq (co c1 cc0 cdiv) c0 $;

--| Value of division: if ` A ` and ` B ` are complex numbers with ` B ` nonzero, then ` ( A / B ) ` is the (unique) complex number such that ` ( B x. x ) = A ` . (Contributed by NM, 8-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem divval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (crio x3 (wceq (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) A2) cc)) $;

--| Relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem divmul (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) B2) (wceq (co C B2 cmul) A2)) $;

--| Relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2006.)
theorem divmul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) B2) (wceq A2 (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2006.)
theorem divmul3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) B2) (wceq A2 (co B2 C cmul))) $;

--| Closure law for division. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2001.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem divcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem reccl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divcan2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cmul) A2) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jun-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divcan1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) B2 cmul) A2) $;

--| A ratio is zero iff the numerator is zero. (Contributed by NM, 20-Apr-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem diveq0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| The ratio of nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divne0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wne A2 cc0) (wne (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc0)) $;

--| The ratio of nonzero numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2007.)
theorem divne0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wne (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc0) $;

--| The reciprocal of a nonzero number is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem recne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wne (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc0) $;

--| Multiplication of a number and its reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 25-Oct-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem recid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cmul) c1) $;

--| Multiplication of a number and its reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jun-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem recid2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) A2 cmul) c1) $;

--| Relationship between division and reciprocal. Theorem I.9 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divrec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co A2 (co c1 B2 cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Relationship between division and reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2006.)
theorem divrec2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co (co c1 B2 cdiv) A2 cmul)) $;

--| An associative law for division. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem divass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem div23 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem div32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cmul) (co A2 (co C B2 cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by NM, 22-Apr-2005.)
theorem div13 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cmul) (co (co C B2 cdiv) A2 cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem div12 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cmul) (co B2 (co A2 C cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| An associative law for division and multiplication. (Contributed by AV, 10-Jul-2021.)
theorem divmulass (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 (co B2 D cdiv) cmul) C cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| An associative/commutative law for division and multiplication. (Contributed by AV, 10-Jul-2021.)
theorem divmulasscom (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 (co B2 D cdiv) cmul) C cmul) (co B2 (co (co A2 C cmul) D cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Distribution of division over addition. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divdir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) caddc)) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divcan3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co B2 A2 cmul) B2 cdiv) A2) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2005.)
theorem divcan4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) B2 cdiv) A2) $;

--| One-to-one relationship for division. (Contributed by NM, 20-Apr-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.) (Proof shortened by SN, 9-Jul-2025.)
theorem div11 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Equality in terms of unit ratio. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem diveq1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) c1) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| A number divided by itself is one. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.) (Proof shortened by SN, 9-Jul-2025.)
theorem divid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 A2 cdiv) c1) $;

--| Division into zero is zero. (Contributed by NM, 14-Mar-2005.) (Proof shortened by SN, 9-Jul-2025.)
theorem div0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co cc0 A2 cdiv) cc0) $;

--| A number divided by 1 is itself. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem div1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c1 cdiv) A2) $;

--| 1 divided by 1 is 1. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Dec-2018.)
theorem _1div1e1:
  $ wceq (co c1 c1 cdiv) c1 $;

--| Move negative sign inside of a division. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2004.)
theorem divneg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (cneg (co A2 B2 cdiv)) (co (cneg A2) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Distribution of division over addition with a multiplication. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2021.)
theorem muldivdir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co (co C A2 cmul) B2 caddc) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) caddc)) $;

--| Distribution of division over subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Mar-2005.)
theorem divsubdir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cmin)) $;

--| Distribution of division over addition with a multiplication. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2025.)
theorem muldivdid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C caddc) B2 cdiv) (co A2 (co C B2 cdiv) caddc)) $;

--| Bring a term in a subtraction into the numerator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jul-2013.)
theorem subdivcomb1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co (co C A2 cmul) B2 cmin) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cmin)) $;

--| Bring a term in a subtraction into the numerator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jul-2013.)
theorem subdivcomb2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 (co C B2 cmul) cmin) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cmin)) $;

--| A number is equal to the reciprocal of its reciprocal. Theorem I.10 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem recrec (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cdiv) A2) $;

--| Reciprocal is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem rec11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wb (wceq (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co c1 B2 cdiv)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Mutual reciprocals. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.)
theorem rec11r (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wb (wceq (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2) (wceq (co c1 B2 cdiv) A2)) $;

--| Multiplication of two ratios. Theorem I.14 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.)
theorem divmuldiv (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Division of two ratios. Theorem I.15 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem divdivdiv (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cdiv) (co (co A2 D cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Cancellation of common factor in a ratio. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2006.)
theorem divcan5 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cdiv) (co A2 B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Swap the denominators in the product of two ratios. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-2005.)
theorem divmul13 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv) cmul) (co (co B2 C cdiv) (co A2 D cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Swap the numerators in the product of two ratios. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-2005.)
theorem divmul24 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv) cmul) (co (co A2 D cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Cross-multiply in an equality of ratios. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2014.)
theorem divmuleq (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv)) (wceq (co A2 D cmul) (co B2 C cmul))) $;

--| The reciprocal of a ratio. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem recdiv (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co c1 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cdiv) (co B2 A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Cancellation of inverted fractions. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2007.)
theorem divcan6 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co B2 A2 cdiv) cmul) c1) $;

--| Swap denominators in a division. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem divdiv32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Cancel equal divisors in a division. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 29-Sep-2013.)
theorem divcan7 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cdiv) (co A2 B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Cancellation law for division and multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2013.) (Proof shortened by Fan Zheng, 3-Jul-2016.)
theorem dmdcan (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cmul) (co C B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Division into a fraction. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2007.)
theorem divdiv1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Division by a fraction. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-2008.)
theorem divdiv2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cdiv) (co (co A2 C cmul) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Division into a reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2007.)
theorem recdiv2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2 cdiv) (co c1 (co A2 B2 cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Cancellation in a double division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem ddcan (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co A2 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cdiv) B2) $;

--| Addition of two ratios. Theorem I.13 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem divadddiv (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv) caddc) (co (co (co A2 D cmul) (co B2 C cmul) caddc) (co C D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Subtraction of two ratios. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem divsubdiv (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0)) (wa (wcel D cc) (wne D cc0)))) (wceq (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 D cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co A2 D cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cmin) (co C D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Two numbers whose reciprocals sum to 1 are called "conjugates" and satisfy this relationship. Equation 5 of [Kreyszig] p. 12. (Contributed by NM, 12-Nov-2006.)
theorem conjmul (P Q: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel P cc) (wne P cc0)) (wa (wcel Q cc) (wne Q cc0))) (wb (wceq (co (co c1 P cdiv) (co c1 Q cdiv) caddc) c1) (wceq (co (co P c1 cmin) (co Q c1 cmin) cmul) c1)) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem rereccl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Closure law for division of reals. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem redivcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| A number equal to its negative is zero. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem eqneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq A2 (cneg A2)) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| A complex number equals its negative iff it is zero. Deduction form of ~ eqneg . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem eqnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 (cneg A2)) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| If a complex number equals its own negative, it is zero. One-way deduction form of ~ eqneg . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem eqnegad (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cneg A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Quotient of two negatives. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Nov-2012.)
theorem div2neg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (cneg A2) (cneg B2) cdiv) (co A2 B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Move negative sign inside of a division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem divneg2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (cneg (co A2 B2 cdiv)) (co A2 (cneg B2) cdiv)) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem recclzi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 cc0) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc) $;

--| The reciprocal of a nonzero number is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem recne0zi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 cc0) (wne (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc0) $;

--| Multiplication of a number and its reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 14-May-1999.)
theorem recidzi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 cc0) (wceq (co A2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cmul) c1) $;

--| A number divided by 1 is itself. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2002.)
theorem div1i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c1 cdiv) A2 $;

--| A number equal to its negative is zero. (Contributed by NM, 29-May-1999.)
theorem eqnegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 (cneg A2)) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem reccli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc $;

--| Multiplication of a number and its reciprocal. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1995.)
theorem recidi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cmul) c1 $;

--| A number is equal to the reciprocal of its reciprocal. Theorem I.10 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1995.)
theorem recreci (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cdiv) A2 $;

--| A number divided by itself is one. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1995.)
theorem dividi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 A2 cdiv) c1 $;

--| Division into zero is zero. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1999.)
theorem div0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co cc0 A2 cdiv) cc0 $;

--| Closure law for division. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem divclzi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem divcan1zi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) B2 cmul) A2) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1999.)
theorem divcan2zi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cmul) A2) $;

--| Relationship between division and reciprocal. Theorem I.9 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-1999.)
theorem divreczi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co A2 (co c1 B2 cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Eliminates a hypothesis of ~ divcan3i with the weak deduction theorem.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2004.)
theorem divcan3zi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co (co B2 A2 cmul) B2 cdiv) A2) $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-1999.)
theorem divcan4zi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) B2 cdiv) A2) $;

--| Reciprocal is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1999.)
theorem rec11i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wceq (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co c1 B2 cdiv)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Closure law for division. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-1995.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem divcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne B2 cc0 $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-1995.)
theorem divcan2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne B2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cmul) A2 $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1999.)
theorem divcan1i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne B2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) B2 cmul) A2 $;

