theorem divdiv1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Division by a fraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divdiv2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cdiv) (co (co A2 C cmul) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) B2) (wceq A2 (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmul3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) B2) (wceq A2 (co B2 C cmul))) $;

--| An associative law for division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divassd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cdiv) (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem div12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cmul) (co B2 (co A2 C cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A commutative/associative law for division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem div23d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cmul)) $;

--| Distribution of division over addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divdird (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) caddc)) $;

--| Distribution of division over subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divsubdird (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C cdiv) (co (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cmin)) $;

--| One-to-one relationship for division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem div11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Multiplication of two ratios. Theorem I.14 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmuldivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Swap denominators of two ratios. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmul13d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cmul) (co (co C B2 cdiv) (co A2 D cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Swap the numerators in the product of two ratios. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmul24d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cmul) (co (co A2 D cdiv) (co C B2 cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Addition of two ratios. Theorem I.13 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divadddivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) caddc) (co (co (co A2 D cmul) (co C B2 cmul) caddc) (co B2 D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Subtraction of two ratios. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divsubdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co A2 D cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cmin) (co B2 D cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Cross-multiply in an equality of ratios. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divmuleqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv)) (wceq (co A2 D cmul) (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Division of two ratios. Theorem I.15 of [Apostol] p. 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem divdivdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cdiv) (co (co A2 D cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| If two complex numbers are equal, their quotient is one. One-way deduction form of ~ diveq1 . Converse of ~ diveq1d . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem diveq1bd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) c1) $;

--| Swap the order of subtraction in a division. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem div2sub (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (w3a (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc) (wne C D))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cmin) cdiv) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co D C cmin) cdiv)) $;

--| Swap subtrahend and minuend inside the numerator and denominator of a fraction. Deduction form of ~ div2sub . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem div2subd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cmin) cdiv) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co D C cmin) cdiv)) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem rereccld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Closure law for division of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem redivcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Subtraction of reciprocals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jan-2017.)
theorem subrecd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co c1 B2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Subtraction of reciprocals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem subrec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co c1 B2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Subtraction of reciprocals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jan-2017.)
theorem subreci (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wne B2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co c1 B2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmul) cdiv) $;

--| Move the left term in a product on the LHS to the RHS, deduction form. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem mvllmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (co C A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Move the left term in a product on the LHS to the RHS, inference form. Uses ~ divcan4i . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Oct-2018.)
theorem mvllmuli (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co C A2 cdiv) $;

--| Left-division. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem ldiv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) C) (wceq A2 (co C B2 cdiv))) $;

--| Right-division. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem rdiv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) C) (wceq B2 (co C A2 cdiv))) $;

--| A division law. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem mdiv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 (co C B2 cdiv)) (wceq B2 (co C A2 cdiv))) $;

--| Solution of a (scalar) linear equation. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Jun-2019.)
theorem lineq (ph: wff) (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co A2 X cmul) B2 caddc) Y) (wceq X (co (co Y B2 cmin) A2 cdiv))) $;

--| Hypothesis for weak deduction theorem to eliminate ` 0 < A ` . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.)
theorem elimgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wbr cc0 (cif (wbr cc0 A2 clt) A2 c1) clt $;

--| Hypothesis for weak deduction theorem to eliminate ` 0 <_ A ` . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1999.)
theorem elimge0 (A2: class):
  $ wbr cc0 (cif (wbr cc0 A2 cle) A2 cc0) cle $;

--| A number is less than itself plus 1. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.)
theorem ltp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) clt) $;

--| A number is less than or equal to itself plus 1. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2006.)
theorem lep1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| A number minus 1 is less than itself. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2006.)
theorem ltm1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| A number minus 1 is less than or equal to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem lem1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 cle) $;

--| A transitive property of 'less than or equal' and plus 1. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-2005.)
theorem letrp1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| A transitive property of plus 1 and 'less than or equal'. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-2005.)
theorem p1le (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr (co A2 c1 caddc) B2 cle)) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number is positive. Exercise 4 of [Apostol] p. 21. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem recgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| Infer that a multiplicand is positive from a nonnegative multiplier and positive product. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem prodgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt))) (wbr cc0 B2 clt) $;

--| Infer that a multiplier is positive from a nonnegative multiplicand and positive product. (Contributed by NM, 24-Apr-2005.)
theorem prodgt02 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 B2 cle) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt))) (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ltmul1 . Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul1a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2005.)
theorem ltmul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2005.)
theorem lemul1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cle)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-2005.)
theorem lemul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a nonnegative number. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2005.)
theorem lemul1a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C cle))) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cle) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem lemul2a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C cle))) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Comparison of product of two positive numbers. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2005.)
theorem ltmul12a (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 clt))) (wa (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 C cle) (wbr C D clt)))) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) clt) $;

--| Comparison of product of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2008.)
theorem lemul12b (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wa (wcel D cr) (wbr cc0 D cle)))) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr C D cle)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cle)) $;

--| Comparison of product of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2008.)
theorem lemul12a (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C cle)) (wcel D cr))) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr C D cle)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cle)) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than 1. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2005.)
theorem ltmulgt11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wb (wbr c1 B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than 1. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2005.)
theorem ltmulgt12 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wb (wbr c1 B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 A2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| The product of two numbers greater than 1 is greater than 1. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2005.) (Proof shortened by SN, 29-Jun-2025.)
theorem mulgt1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr c1 B2 clt))) (wbr c1 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2005.)
theorem lemulge11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr c1 B2 cle))) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.)
theorem lemulge12 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr c1 B2 cle))) (wbr A2 (co B2 A2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltdiv1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Division of both sides of a less than or equal to relation by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-2004.)
theorem lediv1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2005.)
theorem gt0div (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wb (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Division of a nonnegative number by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2005.)
theorem ge0div (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wb (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The ratio of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-1999.)
theorem divgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-1999.)
theorem divge0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| A condition for multiplication to be nonnegative. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulge0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) cle) (wo (wa (wbr A2 cc0 cle) (wbr B2 cc0 cle)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)))) $;

--| A condition for multiplication to be nonpositive. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulle0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0 cle) (wo (wa (wbr A2 cc0 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr B2 cc0 cle)))) $;

--| A condition for multiplication of subtraction to be nonpositive. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulsuble0b (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) cmul) cc0 cle) (wo (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wa (wbr C B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle)))) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltmuldiv (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-2004.)
theorem ltmuldiv2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co C A2 cmul) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-2004.)
theorem ltdivmul (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co C B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2005.)
theorem ledivmul (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co C B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem ltdivmul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2006.)
theorem lt2mul2div (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wa (wcel C cr) (wa (wcel D cr) (wbr cc0 D clt)))) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) clt) (wbr (co A2 D cdiv) (co C B2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2005.)
theorem ledivmul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cmul) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2006.)
theorem lemuldiv (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2006.)
theorem lemuldiv2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co C A2 cmul) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem ltrec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lerec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lt2msq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lt2msq1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wbr (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| Two nonnegative numbers compare the same as their squares. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 8-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem lt2msq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-2005.)
theorem ltdiv2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Reciprocal swap in a 'less than' relation. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem ltrec1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wb (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2 clt) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) A2 clt)) $;

--| Reciprocal swap in a 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2005.)
theorem lerec2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wb (wbr A2 (co c1 B2 cdiv) cle) (wbr B2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Invert ratios of positive numbers and swap their ordering. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2006.)
theorem ledivdiv (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wa (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt)) (wa (wcel D cr) (wbr cc0 D clt)))) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cle) (wbr (co D C cdiv) (co B2 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2006.)
theorem lediv2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-1999.)
theorem ltdiv23 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt)) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-2005.)
theorem lediv23 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C clt))) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cle) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle)) $;

--| Comparison of ratio of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-2005.)
theorem lediv12a (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) (wa (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 C clt) (wbr C D cle)))) (wbr (co A2 D cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cle) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than or equal to' into a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem lediv2a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C cle))) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number less than 1 is greater than 1. (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2005.)
theorem reclt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wb (wbr A2 c1 clt) (wbr c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number greater than 1 is less than 1. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2005.)
theorem recgt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wb (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) c1 clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of a number greater than 1 is positive and less than 1. (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2005.)
theorem recgt1i (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wa (wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) c1 clt)) $;

--| Construct a number less than 1 from any nonnegative number. (Contributed by NM, 30-Dec-2005.)
theorem recp1lt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr (co A2 (co c1 A2 caddc) cdiv) c1 clt) $;

--| Given a positive number ` A ` , construct a new positive number less than both ` A ` and 1. (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-2005.)
theorem recreclt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wbr (co c1 (co c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) caddc) cdiv) c1 clt) (wbr (co c1 (co c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) caddc) cdiv) A2 clt)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem le2msq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| The square of a nonnegative number is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem msq11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wceq (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" and division relation. (Lemma for computing upper bounds of products. The "+ 1" prevents division by zero.) (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2005.)
theorem ledivp1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wbr (co (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) cdiv) B2 cmul) A2 cle) $;

--| If a nonnegative number is less than any positive number, it is zero. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-2006.)
theorem squeeze0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) (wbr A2 (cv x3) clt)) cr)) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| A number is less than itself plus 1. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.)
theorem ltp1i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) clt $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number is positive. Exercise 4 of [Apostol] p. 21. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.)
theorem recgt0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number is positive. Exercise 4 of [Apostol] p. 21. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.)
theorem recgt0ii (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt $;

--| Infer that a multiplicand is positive from a nonnegative multiplier and positive product. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.)
theorem prodgt0i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt)) (wbr cc0 B2 clt) $;

--| The ratio of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem divgt0i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1999.)
theorem divge0i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1999.)
theorem ltreci (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1999.)
theorem lereci (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem lt2msqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem le2msqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| The square of a nonnegative number is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.)
theorem msq11i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wceq (co A2 A2 cmul) (co B2 B2 cmul)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The ratio of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem divgt0i2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 clt $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1999.)
theorem ltrecii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 clt $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The ratio of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1999.)
theorem divgt0ii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem ltmul1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem ltdiv1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-1999.)
theorem ltmuldivi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem ltmul2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem lemul1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cle)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1999.)
theorem lemul2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 C clt) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.)
theorem ltdiv23i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 B2 clt) (wbr cc0 C clt)) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" and division relation. (Lemma for computing upper bounds of products. The "+ 1" prevents division by zero.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2005.)
theorem ledivp1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 C cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 (co C c1 caddc) cdiv) cle)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 cle) $;

--| Less-than and division relation. (Lemma for computing upper bounds of products. The "+ 1" prevents division by zero.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2005.)
theorem ltdivp1i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 C cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 (co C c1 caddc) cdiv) clt)) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 clt) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than'. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.)
theorem ltdiv23ii (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 C clt $ >
  $ wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Paul Chapman, 25-Jan-2008.)
theorem ltmul1ii (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 C clt $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-1999.)
theorem ltdiv1ii (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel C cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 C clt $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) clt) $;

--| A number is less than itself plus 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltp1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) clt) $;

--| A number is less than or equal to itself plus 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lep1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| A number minus 1 is less than itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltm1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| A number minus 1 is less than or equal to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lem1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 cle) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number is positive. Exercise 4 of [Apostol] p. 21. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem recgt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The ratio of two positive numbers is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem divgt0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The product of two numbers greater than 1 is greater than 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem mulgt1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemulge11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemulge12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co B2 A2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul1ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cle) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul2ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Comparison of product of two positive numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul12ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) clt) $;

--| Comparison of product of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul12ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cle) $;

--| Comparison of product of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul12bd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) cle) $;

--| A finite set of real numbers has a maximum. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem fimaxre {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) A2) $;

--| A nonempty finite set of real numbers has an upper bound. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 27-May-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2014.)
theorem fimaxre2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $;

--| A nonempty finite set of real numbers has a maximum (image set version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2014.)
theorem fimaxre3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral y1 (wcel B2 cr) A2)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $;

--| A nonempty finite set of real numbers has a minimum. Analogous to ~ fimaxre . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem fiminre {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) A2) $;

--| A nonempty finite set of real numbers is bounded below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fiminre2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $;

--| The negation of a finite set of real numbers is finite. (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2020.)
theorem negfi {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (crab n (wcel (cneg (cv n)) A2) cr) cfn) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, it contains a unique lower bound. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2005.)
theorem lbreu {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S)) (wreu x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, it contains a unique lower bound that belongs to the set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem lbcl {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S)) (wcel (crio x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S) S) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, the lower bound is less than or equal to all members of the set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem lble {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S) (wcel A2 S)) (wbr (crio x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S) A2 cle) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, the lower bound is its infimum. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem lbinf {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S)) (wceq (cinf S cr clt) (crio x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S)) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, it contains its infimum. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem lbinfcl {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S)) (wcel (cinf S cr clt) S) $;

--| If a set of reals contains a lower bound, its infimum is less than or equal to all members of the set. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem lbinfle {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) S) S) (wcel A2 S)) (wbr (cinf S cr clt) A2 cle) $;

--| A nonempty, bounded-above set of reals has a supremum. Stronger version of completeness axiom (it has a slightly weaker antecedent). (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-1997.)
theorem sup2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wo (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3))) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) clt) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| A version of the completeness axiom for reals. Dual of ~ infm3 . (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.)
theorem sup3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) clt) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infm3 . (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-2005.)
theorem infm3lem {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cneg (cv y1))) cr) $;

--| The completeness axiom for reals in terms of infimum: a nonempty, bounded-below set of reals has an infimum. Dual of ~ sup3 . (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-2005.)
theorem infm3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) clt) A2)) cr)) cr) $;

--| Closure of supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.)
theorem suprcl {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel (csup A2 cr clt) cr) $;

--| A member of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to the set's upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2004.)
theorem suprub {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 A2)) (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) cle) $;

--| Natural deduction form of ~ suprubd . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem suprubd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) cle) $;

--| Natural deduction form of ~ suprcl . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem suprcld {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup A2 cr clt) cr) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is the least upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprlub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt) (wrex z (wbr B2 (cv z) clt) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is the least upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprlub_b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt) (wrex y1 (wbr B2 (cv y1) clt) A2)) $;

--| An upper bound is not less than the supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprnub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wn (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt)) (wral z (wn (wbr B2 (cv z) clt)) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprleub {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cle) (wral z (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprleub_b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cle) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprleub_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cle) (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| The supremum function distributes over addition in a sense similar to that in ~ supmul1 . (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 25-Sep-2017.)
theorem supaddc {x3 y1 z v: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 z v):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) B2 caddc)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csup A2 cr clt) B2 caddc) (csup C cr clt)) $;

--| The supremum function distributes over addition in a sense similar to that in ~ supmul . (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 26-Sep-2017.)
theorem supadd {x3 y1 z v b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 z v b):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) B2) cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wrex b (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) (cv b) caddc)) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csup A2 cr clt) (csup B2 cr clt) caddc) (csup C cr clt)) $;

--| The supremum function distributes over multiplication, in the sense that ` A x. ( sup B ) = sup ( A x. B ) ` , where ` A x. B ` is shorthand for ` { A x. b | b e. B } ` and is defined as ` C ` below. This is the simple version, with only one set argument; see ~ supmul for the more general case with two set arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2013.)
theorem supmul1 {x3 y1 z v: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z v) (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (C: class y1 z v):
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wceq (cv z) (co A2 (cv v) cmul)) B2)) $ >
  $ wb ph (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) B2)) (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wne B2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) B2) cr))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (csup B2 cr clt) cmul) (csup C cr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ supmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2013.)
theorem supmullem1 {x3 y1 z w v b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 v) (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 z v b):
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wrex b (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) (cv b) cmul)) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wb ph (w3a (wa (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) A2) (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) B2)) (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wne B2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) B2) cr))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral w (wbr (cv w) (co (csup A2 cr clt) (csup B2 cr clt) cmul) cle) C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ supmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2013.)
theorem supmullem2 {x3 y1 z w v b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 v) (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 z v b):
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wrex b (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) (cv b) cmul)) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wb ph (w3a (wa (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) A2) (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) B2)) (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wne B2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) B2) cr))) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wss C cr) (wne C c0) (wrex x3 (wral w (wbr (cv w) (cv x3) cle) C) cr)) $;

--| The supremum function distributes over multiplication, in the sense that ` ( sup A ) x. ( sup B ) = sup ( A x. B ) ` , where ` A x. B ` is shorthand for ` { a x. b | a e. A , b e. B } ` and is defined as ` C ` below. We made use of this in our definition of multiplication in the Dedekind cut construction of the reals (see ~ df-mp ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem supmul {x3 y1 z v b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 v) (A2 B2: class) (C: class y1 z v b):
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wrex b (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) (cv b) cmul)) B2) A2)) $ >
  $ wb ph (w3a (wa (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) A2) (wral x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) B2)) (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wne B2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) B2) cr))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csup A2 cr clt) (csup B2 cr clt) cmul) (csup C cr clt)) $;

--| A version of the completeness axiom for reals. (Contributed by NM, 23-Aug-1999.)
theorem sup3ii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) clt) A2)) cr)) cr $;

--| Closure of supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-1999.)
theorem suprclii {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wcel (csup A2 cr clt) cr $;

--| A member of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to the set's upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-1999.)
theorem suprubii {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) cle) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is the least upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprlubii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cr) (wb (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt) (wrex z (wbr B2 (cv z) clt) A2)) $;

--| An upper bound is not less than the supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprnubii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cr) (wb (wn (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt)) (wral z (wn (wbr B2 (cv z) clt)) A2)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2014.)
theorem suprleubii {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cr) (wb (wbr (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cle) (wral z (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| The negative of the unique real such that ` ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Jun-2005.)
theorem riotaneg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cneg (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wreu x3 ph cr) (wceq (crio x3 ph cr) (cneg (crio y1 ps cr))) $;

--| Negation is an order anti-isomorphism of the real numbers, which is its own inverse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem negiso {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cneg (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wa (wiso cr cr clt (ccnv clt) F) (wceq (ccnv F) F) $;

--| The infimum of a set of reals ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem dfinfre {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq (cinf A2 cr clt) (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) clt) A2)) cr)) cr))) $;

--| Closure of infimum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infrecl {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel (cinf A2 cr clt) cr) $;

--| The infimum of a set of reals ` A ` is the negative of the supremum of the negatives of its elements. The antecedent ensures that ` A ` is nonempty and has a lower bound. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-2005.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infrenegsup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wceq (cinf A2 cr clt) (cneg (csup (crab z (wcel (cneg (cv z)) A2) cr) cr clt))) $;

--| Any lower bound of a nonempty set of real numbers is less than or equal to its infimum. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2013.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem infregelb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr B2 (cinf A2 cr clt) cle) (wral z (wbr B2 (cv z) cle) A2)) $;

--| Any lower bound of a nonempty set of real numbers is less than or equal to its infimum. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2013.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem infregelb_b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr B2 (cinf A2 cr clt) cle) (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) A2)) $;

--| Any lower bound of a nonempty set of real numbers is less than or equal to its infimum. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2013.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem infregelb_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr B2 (cinf A2 cr clt) cle) (wral y1 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2)) $;

--| If a nonempty set of real numbers has a lower bound, its infimum is less than or equal to any of its elements. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 15-Sep-2013.) (Revised by AV, 4-Sep-2020.)
theorem infrelb {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) B2) cr) (wcel A2 B2)) (wbr (cinf B2 cr clt) A2 cle) $;

--| The infimum of a finite set of reals is less than or equal to any of its elements. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem infrefilb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss B2 cr) (wcel B2 cfn) (wcel A2 B2)) (wbr (cinf B2 cr clt) A2 cle) $;

--| The supremum of a finite set of real numbers is greater than or equal to all the real numbers of the set. (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2019.)
theorem supfirege (ph: wff) (B2 C S: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (csup B2 cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C S cle) $;

--| -1 is a complex number. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Jul-2016.)
theorem neg1cn:
  $ wcel (cneg c1) cc $;

--| -1 is a real number. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 5-Dec-2018.)
theorem neg1rr:
  $ wcel (cneg c1) cr $;

--| -1 is nonzero. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem neg1ne0:
  $ wne (cneg c1) cc0 $;

--| -1 is less than 0. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem neg1lt0:
  $ wbr (cneg c1) cc0 clt $;

--| ` -u -u 1 ` is 1. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem negneg1e1:
  $ wceq (cneg (cneg c1)) c1 $;

--| The imaginary unit ` _i ` is not a real number. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1999.)
theorem inelr:
  $ wn (wcel ci cr) $;

--| A real number times the imaginary unit is real only if the number is 0. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem rimul (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co ci A2 cmul) cr)) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| The representation of complex numbers in terms of real and imaginary parts is unique. Proposition 10-1.3 of [Gleason] p. 130. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem cru (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr))) (wb (wceq (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) (co C (co ci D cmul) caddc)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| The real representation of complex numbers is nonzero iff one of its terms is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem crne0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wo (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) (wne (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| The real part of a complex number is unique. Proposition 10-1.3 of [Gleason] p. 130. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem creur {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wreu x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) cr) cr) $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number is unique. Proposition 10-1.3 of [Gleason] p. 130. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem creui {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wreu y1 (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) cr) cr) $;

--| The complex conjugate of a complex number is unique. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem cju {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wreu x3 (wa (wcel (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) cr) (wcel (co ci (co A2 (cv x3) cmin) cmul) cr)) cc) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ subeq0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofsubeq0 (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G)) (wb (wceq (co F G (cof cmin)) (cxp A2 (csn cc0))) (wceq F G)) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ negsub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofnegsub (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G)) (wceq (co F (co (cxp A2 (csn (cneg c1))) G (cof cmul)) (cof caddc)) (co F G (cof cmin))) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ subge0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofsubge0 (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cr F) (wf A2 cr G)) (wb (wbr (cxp A2 (csn cc0)) (co F G (cof cmin)) (cofr cle)) (wbr G F (cofr cle))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the indicator function generator.
term cind: class;

--| Define the indicator function generator. It generates an indicator function ` ( ( _Ind `` O ) `` A ) ` for a given domain ` O ` and a given subset ` A ` of the domain, see ~ indval . In contrast to the definitions and notations in Wikipedia and [Lang2] p. 3, the domain and the subset are always mentioned explicitly. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_ind {x3 o a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cind (cmpt o cvv (cmpt a (cpw (cv o)) (cmpt x3 (cv o) (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) c1 cc0)))) $;

--| Value of the indicator function generator with domain ` O ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Aug-2017.)
theorem indv {x3 a: setvar} (O: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq (cfv O cind) (cmpt a (cpw O) (cmpt x3 O (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) c1 cc0)))) $;

--| Value of the indicator function generator for a set ` A ` and a domain ` O ` , i.e., an indicator function for a given domain ` O ` and a given subset ` A ` of the domain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2017.)
theorem indval {x3: setvar} (A2 O: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel O V) (wss A2 O)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv O cind)) (cmpt x3 O (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) c1 cc0))) $;

--| The indicator function generator does not generate a (meaningful) indicator function for a class which is not a subset of the domain. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2026.)
theorem indval0 (A2 O: class):
  $ wi (wn (wss A2 O)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv O cind)) c0) $;

--| Alternate value of the indicator function generator. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2017.)
theorem indval2 (A2 O V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel O V) (wss A2 O)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv O cind)) (cun (cxp A2 (csn c1)) (cxp (cdif O A2) (csn cc0)))) $;

--| An indicator function as a function with domain and codomain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Aug-2017.)
theorem indf (A2 O V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel O V) (wss A2 O)) (wf O (cpr cc0 c1) (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) $;

--| Value of the indicator function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Aug-2017.)
theorem indfval (A2 O V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel O V) (wss A2 O) (wcel X O)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) (cif (wcel X A2) c1 cc0)) $;

--| The range of the indicator function is a subset of ` RR ` . (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2026.)
theorem fvindre (A2 O X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel O cfn) (wss A2 O)) (wcel X O)) (wcel (cfv X (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) cr) $;

--| Value of the indicator function where it is ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Aug-2017.)
theorem ind1 (A2 O V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel O V) (wss A2 O) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) c1) $;

--| Value of the indicator function where it is ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Aug-2017.)
theorem ind0 (A2 O V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel O V) (wss A2 O) (wcel X (cdif O A2))) (wceq (cfv X (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) cc0) $;

--| Value of the indicator function where it is ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2017.)
theorem ind1a (A2 O V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel O V) (wss A2 O) (wcel X O)) (wb (wceq (cfv X (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) c1) (wcel X A2)) $;

--| Indicator of the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2026.)
theorem indconst0 (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq (cfv c0 (cfv O cind)) (cxp O (csn cc0))) $;

--| Indicator of the whole set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2026.)
theorem indconst1 (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq (cfv O (cfv O cind)) (cxp O (csn c1))) $;

--| Preimage of the singleton ` { 1 } ` by the indicator function. See ~ i1f1lem . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Aug-2017.)
theorem indpi1 (A2 O V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel O V) (wss A2 O)) (wceq (cima (ccnv (cfv A2 (cfv O cind))) (csn c1)) A2) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of positive integers.
term cn: class;

--| Define the set of positive integers. Some authors, especially in analysis books, call these the natural numbers, whereas other authors choose to include 0 in their definition of natural numbers. Note that ` NN ` is a subset of complex numbers ( ~ nnsscn ), in contrast to the more elementary ordinal natural numbers ` _om ` , ~ df-om ). See ~ nnind for the principle of mathematical induction. See ~ df-n0 for the set of nonnegative integers ` NN0 ` . See ~ dfn2 for ` NN ` defined in terms of ` NN0 ` . This is a technical definition that helps us avoid the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 in certain proofs. For a more conventional and intuitive definition ("the smallest set of reals containing ` 1 ` as well as the successor of every member") see ~ dfnn3 (or its slight variant ~ dfnn2 ). (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2014.)
axiom df_nn {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cn (cima (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) c1) com) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ nnex , more direct, that makes use of ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem nnexALT:
  $ wcel cn cvv $;

--| Peano's inductive postulate. Theorem I.36 (principle of mathematical induction) of [Apostol] p. 34. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem peano5nni {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel c1 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) A2) A2)) (wss cn A2) $;

--| The positive integers are a subset of the reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.)
theorem nnssre:
  $ wss cn cr $;

--| The positive integers are a subset of the complex numbers. Remark: this could also be proven from ~ nnssre and ~ ax-resscn at the cost of using more axioms. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 4-Oct-2022.)
theorem nnsscn:
  $ wss cn cc $;

--| The set of positive integers exists. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem nnex:
  $ wcel cn cvv $;

--| A positive integer is a real number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem nnre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A positive integer is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem nncn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| A positive integer is a real number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem nnrei (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cr $;

--| A positive integer is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 4-Oct-2022.)
theorem nncni (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cc $;

--| Peano postulate: 1 is a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem _1nn:
  $ wcel c1 cn $;

--| Peano postulate: a successor of a positive integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem peano2nn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cn) $;

--| Alternate definition of the set of positive integers. This was our original definition, before the current ~ df-nn replaced it. This definition requires the axiom of infinity to ensure it has the properties we expect. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2014.)
theorem dfnn2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cn (cint (cab x3 (wa (wcel c1 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) (cv x3)) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the set of positive integers. Definition of positive integers in [Apostol] p. 22. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jul-2005.)
theorem dfnn3 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cn (cint (cab x3 (w3a (wss (cv x3) cr) (wcel c1 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) (cv x3)) (cv x3))))) $;

--| A positive integer is a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A positive integer is a complex number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nncnd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| Peano postulate: a successor of a positive integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem peano2nnd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cn) $;

--| Principle of Mathematical Induction (inference schema). The first four hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the last two are the basis and the induction step. See ~ nnaddcl for an example of its use. See ~ nn0ind for induction on nonnegative integers and ~ uzind , ~ uzind4 for induction on an arbitrary upper set of integers. See ~ indstr for strong induction. See also ~ nnindALT . This is an alternative for Metamath 100 proof #74. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-1997.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.)
theorem nnind {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cn) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) ta $;

--| Principle of Mathematical Induction (inference schema). The last four hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the first two are the induction step and the basis. This ALT version of ~ nnind has a different hypothesis order. It may be easier to use with the Metamath program Proof Assistant, because "MM-PA> ASSIGN LAST" will be applied to the substitution instances first. We may eventually use this one as the official version. You may use either version. After the proof is complete, the ALT version can be changed to the non-ALT version with "MM-PA> MINIMIZE__WITH nnind / MAY_GROW". (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem nnindALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cn) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) ta $;

--| Principle of Mathematical Induction (inference schema) on integers, a deduction version. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem nnindd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch th ta et: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) cn)) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 cn)) et $;

--| Every positive integer is one or a successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2014.)
theorem nn1m1nn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wo (wceq A2 c1) (wcel (co A2 c1 cmin) cn)) $;

--| If a statement holds for 1 and also holds for a successor, it holds for all positive integers. The first three hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the last two show that it holds for 1 and for a successor. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2014.)
theorem nn1suc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff y1) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cn) ch $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) th $;

--| Closure of addition of positive integers, proved by induction on the second addend. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-1997.)
theorem nnaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cn) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-1997.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-mulcom and ~ ax-mulass . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 24-Sep-2022.)
theorem nnmulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cn) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem nnmulcli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cn $;

--| Addition with 1 is commutative for natural numbers. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 9-Dec-2022.)
theorem nnadd1com (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (co A2 c1 caddc) (co c1 A2 caddc)) $;

--| Addition is commutative for natural numbers. Uses fewer axioms than ~ addcom . (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 9-Dec-2022.)
theorem nnaddcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| Version of ~ addcomli for natural numbers. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 1-Aug-2023.)
theorem nnaddcomli (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 A2 caddc) C $;

--| "Minus times minus is plus, The reason for this we need not discuss." (W. H. Auden, as quoted in M. Guillen "Bridges to Infinity", p. 64, see also Metamath Book, section 1.1.1, p. 5) This statement, formalized to "The product of two negative integers is a positive integer", is proved by the following theorem, therefore it actually need not be discussed anymore. "The reason for this" is that ` ( -u A x. -u B ) = ( A x. B ) ` for all complex numbers ` A ` and ` B ` because of ~ mul2neg , ` A ` and ` B ` are complex numbers because of ~ nncn , and ` ( A x. B ) e. NN ` because of ~ nnmulcl . This also holds for positive reals, see ~ rpmtmip . Note that the opposites ` -u A ` and ` -u B ` of the positive integers ` A ` and ` B ` are negative integers. (Contributed by AV, 23-Dec-2022.)
theorem nnmtmip (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co (cneg A2) (cneg B2) cmul) cn) $;

--| There exists a positive integer greater than or equal to any two others. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1999.)
theorem nn2ge {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cle) (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle)) cn) $;

--| A positive integer is one or greater. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.)
theorem nnge1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr c1 A2 cle) $;

--| A positive integer is greater than one iff it is not equal to one. (Contributed by NM, 7-Oct-2004.)
theorem nngt1ne1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wne A2 c1)) $;

--| A positive integer is less than or equal to one iff it is equal to one. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-2005.)
theorem nnle1eq1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wbr A2 c1 cle) (wceq A2 c1)) $;

--| A positive integer is positive. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.)
theorem nngt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| A positive integer is not less than one. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jan-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnnlt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wn (wbr A2 c1 clt)) $;

--| A positive integer is not less than or equal to zero. (Contributed by AV, 13-May-2020.)
theorem nnnle0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wn (wbr A2 cc0 cle)) $;

--| A positive integer is nonzero. See ~ nnne0ALT for a shorter proof using ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 . This proof avoids ~ 0lt1 , and thus ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 , by splitting ~ ax-1ne0 into the two separate cases ` 0 < 1 ` and ` 1 < 0 ` . (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-1999.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 30-Jan-2023.)
theorem nnne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| No positive integer is equal to its negation. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2023.)
theorem nnneneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wne A2 (cneg A2)) $;

--| Zero is not a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 . (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 30-Jan-2023.)
theorem _0nnn:
  $ wn (wcel cc0 cn) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 0nnn , which requires ~ ax-pre-mulgt0 but is not based on ~ nnne0 (and which can therefore be used in ~ nnne0ALT ). (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _0nnnALT:
  $ wn (wcel cc0 cn) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ nnne0 . A positive integer is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 27-Sep-1999.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem nnne0ALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| A positive integer is positive (inference version). (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-1999.)
theorem nngt0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $;

--| A positive integer is nonzero (inference version). (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.)
theorem nnne0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $;

--| The quotient of a real and a positive integer is real. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem nndivre (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn)) (wcel (co A2 N cdiv) cr) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive integer is real. (Contributed by NM, 8-Feb-2008.)
theorem nnrecre (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co c1 N cdiv) cr) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive integer is positive. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-1999.)
theorem nnrecgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr cc0 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| Subtraction of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2014.)
theorem nnsub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wcel (co B2 A2 cmin) cn)) $;

--| Subtraction of positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2001.)
theorem nnsubi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wcel (co B2 A2 cmin) cn) $;

--| Two ways to express " ` A ` divides ` B ` " for positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2004.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2014.)
theorem nndiv {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wb (wrex x3 (wceq (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) B2) cn) (wcel (co B2 A2 cdiv) cn)) $;

--| Transitive property of divisibility: if ` A ` divides ` B ` and ` B ` divides ` C ` , then ` A ` divides ` C ` . Typically, ` C ` would be an integer, although the theorem holds for complex ` C ` . (Contributed by NM, 3-May-2005.)
theorem nndivtr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wcel C cc)) (wa (wcel (co B2 A2 cdiv) cn) (wcel (co C B2 cdiv) cn))) (wcel (co C A2 cdiv) cn) $;

--| A positive integer is one or greater. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnge1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $;

--| A positive integer is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nngt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| A positive integer is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnne0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive integer is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnrecred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Closure of addition of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnaddcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cn) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nnmulcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cn) $;

--| A positive integer is one or greater. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.)
theorem nndivred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 1t1e1 using a different set of axioms (add ~ ax-mulrcl , ~ ax-i2m1 , ~ ax-1ne0 , ~ ax-rrecex and remove ~ ax-resscn , ~ ax-mulcom , ~ ax-mulass , ~ ax-distr ). (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 20-Sep-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _1t1e1ALT:
  $ wceq (co c1 c1 cmul) c1 $;

--| Right-distributivity for natural numbers without ~ ax-mulcom . (Contributed by SN, 5-Feb-2024.)
theorem nnadddir (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wcel C cn)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Multiplication with 1 is commutative for natural numbers, without ~ ax-mulcom . Since ` ( A x. 1 ) ` is ` A ` by ~ ax-1rid , this is equivalent to ~ remullid for natural numbers, but using fewer axioms (avoiding ~ ax-resscn , ~ ax-addass , ~ ax-mulass , ~ ax-rnegex , ~ ax-pre-lttri , ~ ax-pre-lttrn , ~ ax-pre-ltadd ). (Contributed by SN, 5-Feb-2024.)
theorem nnmul1com (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (co c1 A2 cmul) (co A2 c1 cmul)) $;

--| Multiplication is commutative for natural numbers. (Contributed by SN, 5-Feb-2024.)
theorem nnmulcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co B2 A2 cmul)) $;

