theorem _9m1e8:
  $ wceq (co c9 c1 cmin) c8 $;

--| Two plus two equals four. For more information, see "2+2=4 Trivia" on the Metamath Proof Explorer Home Page: ~ mmset.html#trivia . This proof is simple, but it depends on many other proof steps because 2 and 4 are complex numbers and thus it depends on our construction of complex numbers. The proof ~ o2p2e4 is similar but proves ` 2 + 2 = 4 ` using ordinal natural numbers (finite integers starting at 0), so that proof depends on fewer intermediate steps. (Contributed by NM, 27-May-1999.)
theorem _2p2e4:
  $ wceq (co c2 c2 caddc) c4 $;

--| Two times a number. (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2times (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co c2 A2 cmul) (co A2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| A number times 2. (Contributed by NM, 16-Oct-2007.)
theorem times2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c2 cmul) (co A2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| Two times a number. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1999.)
theorem _2timesi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co c2 A2 cmul) (co A2 A2 caddc) $;

--| A number times 2. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2004.)
theorem times2i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c2 cmul) (co A2 A2 caddc) $;

--| Two times a complex number minus the number itself results in the number itself. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem _2txmxeqx (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X cc) (wceq (co (co c2 X cmul) X cmin) X) $;

--| 2 divided by 2 is 1. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _2div2e1:
  $ wceq (co c2 c2 cdiv) c1 $;

--| 2 + 1 = 3. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2p1e3:
  $ wceq (co c2 c1 caddc) c3 $;

--| 1 + 2 = 3. For a shorter proof using ~ addcomli , see ~ 1p2e3ALT . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.) Reduce dependencies on axioms. (Revised by Steven Nguyen, 12-Dec-2022.)
theorem _1p2e3:
  $ wceq (co c1 c2 caddc) c3 $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 1p2e3 , shorter but using more axioms. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _1p2e3ALT:
  $ wceq (co c1 c2 caddc) c3 $;

--| 3 + 1 = 4. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2015.)
theorem _3p1e4:
  $ wceq (co c3 c1 caddc) c4 $;

--| 4 + 1 = 5. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2015.)
theorem _4p1e5:
  $ wceq (co c4 c1 caddc) c5 $;

