  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4sq . Inductive step, odd prime case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem _4sqlem18 {x3 y1 z w i n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w i) (P S: class x3 y1 z w) (T: class x3 y1 z w i n) (M: class x3 y1 z w) (N: class x3 y1 z w i):
  $ wceq S (cab n (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (wceq (cv n) (co (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) (co (co (cv z) c2 cexp) (co (cv w) c2 cexp) caddc) caddc)) cz) cz) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co cc0 (co c2 N cmul) cfz) S) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab i (wcel (co (cv i) P cmul) S) cn) $ >
  $ wceq M (cinf T cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4sq . The proof is by strong induction - we show that if all the integers less than ` k ` are in ` S ` , then ` k ` is as well. In this part of the proof we do the induction argument and dispense with all the cases except the odd prime case, which is sent to ~ 4sqlem18 . If ` k ` is ` 0 , 1 , 2 ` , we show ` k e. S ` directly; otherwise if ` k ` is composite, ` k ` is the product of two numbers less than it (and hence in ` S ` by assumption), so by ~ mul4sq ` k e. S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _4sqlem19 {x3 y1 z w n: setvar} (S: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wceq S (cab n (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (wceq (cv n) (co (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) (co (co (cv z) c2 cexp) (co (cv w) c2 cexp) caddc) caddc)) cz) cz) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wceq cn0 S $;

--| Lagrange's four-square theorem, or Bachet's conjecture: every nonnegative integer is expressible as a sum of four squares. This is Metamath 100 proof #19. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2014.)
theorem _4sq {a b c d: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cn0) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wceq A2 (co (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) (co (co (cv c) c2 cexp) (co (cv d) c2 cexp) caddc) caddc)) cz) cz) cz) cz) $;

--| The arithmetic progression function.
term cvdwa: class;

--| The monochromatic arithmetic progression predicate.
term cvdwm: class;

--| The polychromatic arithmetic progression predicate.
term cvdwp: class;

--| Define the arithmetic progression function, which takes as input a length ` k ` , a start point ` a ` , and a step ` d ` and outputs the set of points in this progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_vdwap {k m a d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvdwa (cmpt k cn0 (cmpo a d cn cn (crn (cmpt m (co cc0 (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfz) (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc))))) $;

--| Define the "contains a monochromatic AP" predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_vdwmc {f2 k c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvdwm (copab k f2 (wex c (wne (cin (crn (cfv (cv k) cvdwa)) (cpw (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) c0))) $;

--| Define the "contains a polychromatic collection of APs" predicate. See ~ vdwpc for more information. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_vdwpc {f2 i k m a d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvdwp (coprab m k f2 (wrex a (wrex d (wa (wral i (wss (co (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) (cfv (cv k) cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cfv (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) (cv f2))))) (co c1 (cv m) cfz)) (wceq (cfv (crn (cmpt i (co c1 (cv m) cfz) (cfv (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) (cv f2)))) chash) (cv m))) (co cn (co c1 (cv m) cfz) cmap)) cn)) $;

--| Define the arithmetic progression function, which takes as input a length ` k ` , a start point ` a ` , and a step ` d ` and outputs the set of points in this progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwapfval {m a d: setvar} (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cn0) (wceq (cfv K cvdwa) (cmpo a d cn cn (crn (cmpt m (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz) (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc))))) $;

--| The arithmetic progression function is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwapf (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cn0) (wf (cxp cn cn) (cpw cn) (cfv K cvdwa)) $;

--| Value of the arithmetic progression function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwapval {m: setvar} (A2 D K X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn0) (wcel A2 cn) (wcel D cn)) (wb (wcel X (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa))) (wrex m (wceq X (co A2 (co (cv m) D cmul) caddc)) (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| Remove the first element of an arithmetic progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwapun (A2 D K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn0) (wcel A2 cn) (wcel D cn)) (wceq (co A2 D (cfv (co K c1 caddc) cvdwa)) (cun (csn A2) (co (co A2 D caddc) D (cfv K cvdwa)))) $;

--| The first element of an arithmetic progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwapid1 (A2 D K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn) (wcel A2 cn) (wcel D cn)) (wcel A2 (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa))) $;

--| Value of a length-1 arithmetic progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwap0 (A2 D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel D cn)) (wceq (co A2 D (cfv cc0 cvdwa)) c0) $;

--| Value of a length-1 arithmetic progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwap1 (A2 D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel D cn)) (wceq (co A2 D (cfv c1 cvdwa)) (csn A2)) $;

--| The predicate " The ` <. R , N >. ` -coloring ` F ` contains a monochromatic AP of length ` K ` ". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwmc {a c d: setvar} (ph: wff a d) (R: class a c d) (F K: class) (X: class a c d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr K F cvdwm) (wex c (wrex a (wrex d (wss (co (cv a) (cv d) (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) cn) cn))) $;

--| Expand out the definition of an arithmetic progression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwmc2 {m a c d: setvar} (ph: wff m) (A2: class m a c d) (R: class m) (F K: class) (X: class m a c d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr K F cvdwm) (wrex c (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn) R)) $;

--| The predicate " The coloring ` F ` contains a polychromatic ` M ` -tuple of AP's of length ` K ` ". A polychromatic ` M ` -tuple of AP's is a set of AP's with the same base point but different step lengths, such that each individual AP is monochromatic, but the AP's all have mutually distinct colors. (The common basepoint is not required to have the same color as any of the AP's.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwpc {i a d: setvar} (ph: wff i a d) (R: class i a d) (F: class) (J: class a) (K M: class) (X: class i a d):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 M cfz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cop M K) F cvdwp) (wrex a (wrex d (wa (wral i (wss (co (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) F)))) J) (wceq (cfv (crn (cmpt i J (cfv (co (cv a) (cfv (cv i) (cv d)) caddc) F))) chash) M)) (co cn J cmap)) cn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem1 {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R F I K M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 W cfz) R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) cn D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wss (co (co A2 (cfv (cv i) D) caddc) (cfv (cv i) D) (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (co A2 (cfv (cv i) D) caddc) F)))) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv (co A2 (cfv I D) caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co K c1 caddc) F cvdwm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (R F G K M N W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv (co W N caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv x3) N caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr K G cvdwm) (wbr K F cvdwm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co c1 V cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co c1 W cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co B2 (co W (co (co A2 c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (R: class) (F: class x3 y1) (H V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz) R H) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 V cfz) (cmpt y1 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) (co W (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 V cfz) (co R (co c1 W cfz) cmap) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem5 {x3 y1 i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i) (P: class y1 j) (R: class j) (T: class y1 j) (E: class) (F: class x3 y1 i j) (G: class) (H: class j) (J: class y1) (K M: class) (V: class i j) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz) R H) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 V cfz) (cmpt y1 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) (co W (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 W cfz) R G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) cn E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wss (co (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) (cfv (cv i) E) (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cfv (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) G)))) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (crn J) chash) M) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 (co W (co (co A2 (co V D cmin) caddc) c1 cmin) cmul) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt j (co c1 (co M c1 caddc) cfz) (co (cif (wceq (cv j) (co M c1 caddc)) cc0 (cfv (cv j) E)) (co W D cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem6 {x3 y1 i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i) (P: class y1 j) (R: class j) (T: class y1 j) (E: class) (F: class x3 y1 i j) (G: class) (H: class j) (J: class y1) (K M: class) (V: class i j) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz) R H) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 V cfz) (cmpt y1 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) (co W (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 W cfz) R G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) cn E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wss (co (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) (cfv (cv i) E) (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cfv (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) G)))) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (co B2 (cfv (cv i) E) caddc) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (crn J) chash) M) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 (co W (co (co A2 (co V D cmin) caddc) c1 cmin) cmul) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt j (co c1 (co M c1 caddc) cfz) (co (cif (wceq (cv j) (co M c1 caddc)) cc0 (cfv (cv j) E)) (co W D cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr (cop (co M c1 caddc) K) H cvdwp) (wbr (co K c1 caddc) G cvdwm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G H K M V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz) R H) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 V cfz) (cmpt y1 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) (co W (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 W cfz) R G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr (cop M K) G cvdwp) (wo (wbr (cop (co M c1 caddc) K) H cvdwp) (wbr (co K c1 caddc) G cvdwm))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwlem8 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C D: class) (R: class x3) (F: class) (G: class x3) (K W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co c2 W cmul) cfz) R F) $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 D (cfv K cvdwa)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn C))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv x3) W caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop c1 K) F cvdwp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwlem9 {x3 y1 f2 g2 n s1: setvar} (ph: wff f2 s1) (R: class) (F: class n s1) (H: class f2 n s1) (K: class) (M: class s1) (V W: class g2 n s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wrex n (wral f2 (wbr K (cv f2) cvdwm) (co (cv s1) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral g2 (wo (wbr (cop M K) (cv g2) cvdwp) (wbr (co K c1 caddc) (cv g2) cvdwm)) (co R (co c1 W cfz) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wbr K (cv f2) cvdwm) (co (co R (co c1 W cfz) cmap) (co c1 V cfz) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co W (co c2 V cmul) cmul) cfz) R H) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 V cfz) (cmpt y1 (co c1 W cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) (co W (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) V caddc) cmul) caddc) H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr (cop (co M c1 caddc) K) H cvdwp) (wbr (co K c1 caddc) H cvdwm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . Set up secondary induction on ` M ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwlem10 {f2 n s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (R K: class) (M: class s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wrex n (wral f2 (wbr K (cv f2) cvdwm) (co (cv s1) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral f2 (wo (wbr (cop M K) (cv f2) cvdwp) (wbr (co K c1 caddc) (cv f2) cvdwm)) (co R (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwlem11 {f2 n s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (R K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wrex n (wral f2 (wbr K (cv f2) cvdwm) (co (cv s1) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral f2 (wbr (co K c1 caddc) (cv f2) cvdwm) (co R (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . ` K = 2 ` base case of induction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwlem12 (ph: wff) (R F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co (cfv R chash) c1 caddc) cfz) R F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 F cvdwm)) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdw . Main induction on ` K ` ; ` K = 0 ` , ` K = 1 ` base cases. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem vdwlem13 {f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (R K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral f2 (wbr K (cv f2) cvdwm) (co R (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Van der Waerden's theorem. For any finite coloring ` R ` and integer ` K ` , there is an ` N ` such that every coloring function from ` 1 ... N ` to ` R ` contains a monochromatic arithmetic progression (which written out in full means that there is a color ` c ` and base, increment values ` a , d ` such that all the numbers ` a , a + d , ... , a + ( k - 1 ) d ` lie in the preimage of ` { c } ` , i.e. they are all in ` 1 ... N ` and ` f ` evaluated at each one yields ` c ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdw {f2 m n a c d: setvar} (R: class m) (K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cfn) (wcel K cn0)) (wrex n (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn) R) (co R (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Corollary of ~ vdw , and lemma for ~ vdwnn . If ` F ` is a coloring of the integers, then there are arbitrarily long monochromatic APs in ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwnnlem1 {m a c d: setvar} (R: class m) (F K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cfn) (wf cn R F) (wcel K cn0)) (wrex c (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn) R) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdwnn . The set of all "bad" ` k ` for the theorem is upwards-closed, because a long AP implies a short AP. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwnnlem2 {k m a c d: setvar} (ph: wff k m) (A2 B2: class c) (R: class k m) (S: class c) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn R F) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab k (wn (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn)) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz))) (wi (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vdwnn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Sep-2020.)
theorem vdwnnlem3 {k m a c d: setvar} (ph: wff k m) (R: class k m) (S: class c) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn R F) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab k (wn (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral c (wne S c0) R) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Van der Waerden's theorem, infinitary version. For any finite coloring ` F ` of the positive integers, there is a color ` c ` that contains arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2014.)
theorem vdwnn {k m a c d: setvar} (R: class k m a d) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cfn) (wf cn R F)) (wrex c (wral k (wrex a (wrex d (wral m (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv m) (cv d) cmul) caddc) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv c)))) (co cc0 (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfz)) cn) cn) cn) R) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Ramsey number function.
term cram: class;

--| The set ` T ` of numbers with the Ramsey number property is upward-closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramtlecl {n s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (T M: class):
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) ph)) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M T) (wss (cfv M cuz) T) $;

--| Define the Ramsey number function. The input is a number ` m ` for the size of the edges of the hypergraph, and a tuple ` r ` from the finite color set to lower bounds for each color. The Ramsey number ` ( M Ramsey R ) ` is the smallest number such that for any set ` S ` with ` ( M Ramsey R ) <_ # S ` and any coloring ` F ` of the set of ` M ` -element subsets of ` S ` (with color set ` dom R ` ), there is a color ` c e. dom R ` and a subset ` x C_ S ` such that ` R ( c ) <_ # x ` and all the hyperedges of ` x ` (that is, subsets of ` x ` of size ` M ` ) have color ` c ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_ram {x3 y1 f2 m n s1 r c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cram (cmpo m r cn0 cvv (cinf (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) (cv r)) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wral y1 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv y1) chash) (cv m)) (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv c))) (cpw (cv x3)))) (cpw (cv s1))) (cdm (cv r))) (co (cdm (cv r)) (crab y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) chash) (cv m)) (cpw (cv s1))) cmap)))) cn0) cxr clt)) $;

--| Value of the "binomial set", the set of all ` N ` -element subsets of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem hashbcval {x3 i a b: setvar} (A2: class b) (C: class i a b) (N: class b) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 N C) (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) N) (cpw A2))) $;

--| The binomial set is a finite set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem hashbccl {i a b: setvar} (A2: class b) (C: class i a b) (N: class b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N C) cfn) $;

--| Subset relation for the binomial set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem hashbcss {i a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class b) (C: class i a b) (N: class b) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2) (wcel N cn0)) (wss (co B2 N C) (co A2 N C)) $;

--| The set of subsets of size zero is the singleton of the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem hashbc0 {i a b: setvar} (A2: class b) (C V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (co A2 cc0 C) (csn c0)) $;

--| The size of the binomial set is the binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem hashbc2 {i a b: setvar} (A2: class b) (C: class i a b) (N: class b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N C) chash) (co (cfv A2 chash) N cbc)) $;

--| There are no subsets of the empty set with size greater than zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem _0hashbc {i a b: setvar} (C: class i a b) (N: class b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co c0 N C) c0) $;

--| The value of the Ramsey number function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramval {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (C: class i n s1 a b) (R: class i a b) (T: class x3 f2 i n s1 a b c) (F: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wceq (co M F cram) (cinf T cxr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for extended real closure of the Ramsey number function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramcl2lem {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (C: class i n s1 a b) (R: class i a b) (T: class x3 f2 i n s1 a b c) (F: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wceq (co M F cram) (cif (wceq T c0) cpnf (cinf T cr clt))) $;

--| The Ramsey number has the Ramsey number property if any number does. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramtcl {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (C: class i n s1 a b) (R: class i a b) (T: class x3 f2 i n s1 a b c) (F: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wb (wcel (co M F cram) T) (wne T c0)) $;

--| The Ramsey number is an integer iff there is a number with the Ramsey number property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramtcl2 {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (C: class i n s1 a b) (R: class i a b) (T: class x3 f2 i n s1 a b c) (F: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wb (wcel (co M F cram) cn0) (wne T c0)) $;

--| The Ramsey number is a lower bound on the set of all numbers with the Ramsey number property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramtub {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 b c) (C: class i n s1 a b) (R: class i a b) (T: class x3 f2 i n s1 a b c) (F: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wcel A2 T)) (wbr (co M F cram) A2 cle) $;

--| The Ramsey number is a lower bound on the set of all numbers with the Ramsey number property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramub {x3 f2 i s1 a b c: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C R F: class i a b) (M: class) (N: class b) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wbr N (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wf (co (cv s1) M C) R (cv f2)))) (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M F cram) N cle) $;

--| It is sufficient to check the Ramsey property on finite sets of size equal to the upper bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramub2 {x3 f2 i s1 a b c: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C R F: class i a b) (M: class) (N: class b) (V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) N) (wf (co (cv s1) M C) R (cv f2)))) (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M F cram) N cle) $;

--| The defining property of a Ramsey number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem rami {x3 i a b c: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C R S F G: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class i a b) (W: class x3 i a b c):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co M F cram) cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M F cram) (cfv S chash) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co S M C) R G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw S)) R) $;

--| The Ramsey number is either a nonnegative integer or plus infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem ramcl2 (R F M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wcel (co M F cram) (cun cn0 (csn cpnf))) $;

--| The Ramsey number is an extended real number. (This theorem does not imply Ramsey's theorem, unlike ~ ramcl .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramxrcl (R F M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wcel (co M F cram) cxr) $;

--| If the Ramsey number is upper bounded, then it is an integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramubcl (A2 R F M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wbr (co M F cram) A2 cle))) (wcel (co M F cram) cn0) $;

--| Establish a lower bound on a Ramsey number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramlb {x3 i a b c: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C R F G: class i a b) (M: class) (N V: class i a b):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (co c1 N cfz) M C) R G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv c) R) (wss (cv x3) (co c1 N cfz)))) (wi (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv c)))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv (cv c) F) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N (co M F cram) clt) $;

--| The Ramsey number when ` M = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem _0ram {x3 y1: setvar} (R F: class) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel R V) (wne R c0) (wf R cn0 F)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) (crn F)) cz)) (wceq (co cc0 F cram) (csup (crn F) cr clt)) $;

--| The Ramsey number when ` M = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem _0ram2 (R F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cfn) (wne R c0) (wf R cn0 F)) (wceq (co cc0 F cram) (csup (crn F) cr clt)) $;

--| The Ramsey number when ` R = (/) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem ram0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wceq (co M c0 cram) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ramcl : Existence of the Ramsey number when ` M = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem _0ramcl (R F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cfn) (wf R cn0 F)) (wcel (co cc0 F cram) cn0) $;

--| The Ramsey number when ` F ` has value zero for some color ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramz2 (C R F M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel R V) (wf R cn0 F)) (wa (wcel C R) (wceq (cfv C F) cc0))) (wceq (co M F cram) cc0) $;

--| The Ramsey number when ` F ` is the zero function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramz (R M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R V) (wne R c0)) (wceq (co M (cxp R (csn cc0)) cram) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ramub1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramub1lem1 {x3 y1 z u i a b: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C: class i a b) (D: class y1 z i a b) (R: class i a b) (S: class b) (E: class y1 u i a b) (F: class i a b) (G: class b) (H K: class i a b) (M: class) (V: class y1 u b) (W: class x3 y1 z b) (X: class b):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 R (co M (cmpt y1 R (cif (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) c1 cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F))) cram)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) cn0) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S chash) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co S M C) R K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt u (co (cdif S (csn X)) (co M c1 cmin) C) (cfv (cun (cv u) (csn X)) K)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss W (cdif S (csn X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv D G) (cfv W chash) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co W (co M c1 cmin) C) (cima (ccnv H) (csn D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cif (wceq E D) (co (cfv D F) c1 cmin) (cfv E F)) (cfv V chash) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co V M C) (cima (ccnv K) (csn E))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv E F) (cfv (cv z) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv z) M C) (cima (ccnv K) (csn E)))) (cpw S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ramub1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramub1lem2 {x3 y1 z u i a b c: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (C R: class i a b) (S: class b) (F: class i a b) (G: class b) (H K: class i a b) (M: class) (X: class b):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 R (co M (cmpt y1 R (cif (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) c1 cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F))) cram)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) cn0) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S chash) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co S M C) R K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt u (co (cdif S (csn X)) (co M c1 cmin) C) (cfv (cun (cv u) (csn X)) K)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wrex z (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv z) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv z) M C) (cima (ccnv K) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw S)) R) $;

--| Inductive step for Ramsey's theorem, in the form of an explicit upper bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramub1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (R F G M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 R (co M (cmpt y1 R (cif (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) c1 cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F))) cram)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf R cn0 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M F cram) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) G cram) c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| Ramsey's theorem: the Ramsey number is an integer for every finite coloring and set of upper bounds. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramcl (R F M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R cfn) (wf R cn0 F)) (wcel (co M F cram) cn0) $;

--| Ramsey's theorem with the definition of ` Ramsey ` ( ~ df-ram ) eliminated. If ` M ` is an integer, ` R ` is a specified finite set of colors, and ` F : R --> NN0 ` is a set of lower bounds for each color, then there is an ` n ` such that for every set ` s ` of size greater than ` n ` and every coloring ` f ` of the set ` ( s C M ) ` of all ` M ` -element subsets of ` s ` , there is a color ` c ` and a subset ` x C_ s ` such that ` x ` is larger than ` F ( c ) ` and the ` M ` -element subsets of ` x ` are monochromatic with color ` c ` . This is the hypergraph version of Ramsey's theorem; the version for simple graphs is the case ` M = 2 ` . This is Metamath 100 proof #31. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem ramsey {x3 f2 i n s1 a b c: setvar} (C: class i n s1 a b) (R F: class i a b) (M: class):
  $ wceq C (cmpo a i cvv cn0 (crab b (wceq (cfv (cv b) chash) (cv i)) (cpw (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel R cfn) (wf R cn0 F)) (wrex n (wal s1 (wi (wbr (cv n) (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wral f2 (wrex c (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (wss (co (cv x3) M C) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv c))))) (cpw (cv s1))) R) (co R (co (cv s1) M C) cmap)))) cn0) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the primorial of nonnegative integers.
term cprmo: class;

--| Define the primorial function on nonnegative integers as the product of all prime numbers less than or equal to the integer. For example, ` ( #p `` 1 0 ) = 2 x. 3 x. 5 x. 7 = 2 1 0 ` (see ~ ex-prmo ). In the literature, the primorial function is written as a postscript hash: 6# = 30. In contrast to ~ prmorcht , where the primorial function is defined by using the sequence builder ( ` P = seq 1 ( x. , F ) ` ), the more specialized definition of a product of a series is used here. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
axiom df_prmo {k n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprmo (cmpt n cn0 (cprod k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (cif (wcel (cv k) cprime) (cv k) c1))) $;

--| Value of the primorial function for nonnegative integers. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmoval {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N cprmo) (cprod k (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wcel (cv k) cprime) (cv k) c1))) $;

--| Closure of the primorial function. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmocl (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N cprmo) cn) $;

--| The primorial function is nonzero. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmone0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wne (cfv N cprmo) cc0) $;

--| The primorial of 0. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cprmo) c1 $;

--| The primorial of 1. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cprmo) c1 $;

--| The primorial of a successor. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmop1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cprmo) (cif (wcel (co N c1 caddc) cprime) (co (cfv N cprmo) (co N c1 caddc) cmul) (cfv N cprmo))) $;

--| Value of the primorial function expressed recursively. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmonn2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cprmo) (cif (wcel N cprime) (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cprmo) N cmul) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cprmo))) $;

--| The primorial of 2. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cprmo) c2 $;

--| The primorial of 3. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo3:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 cprmo) c6 $;

--| The primorial of a number is divisible by each prime less than or equal to the number. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmdvdsprmo {p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wral p (wi (wbr (cv p) N cle) (wbr (cv p) (cfv N cprmo) cdvds)) cprime) $;

--| The primorial of a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number is divisible by a prime less than or equal to the number. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmdvdsprmop {p: setvar} (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wrex p (w3a (wbr (cv p) N cle) (wbr (cv p) I cdvds) (wbr (cv p) (co (cfv N cprmo) I caddc) cdvds)) cprime) $;

--| The value of the prime selection function is in a finite sequence of integers if the argument is in this finite sequence of integers. (Contributed by AV, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem fvprmselelfz {m: setvar} (F: class m) (N X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt m cn (cif (wcel (cv m) cprime) (cv m) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X (co c1 N cfz))) (wcel (cfv X F) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| The greatest common divisor of two values of the prime selection function for different arguments is 1. (Contributed by AV, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem fvprmselgcd1 {m: setvar} (F: class m) (N X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt m cn (cif (wcel (cv m) cprime) (cv m) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X (co c1 N cfz)) (wcel Y (co c1 N cfz)) (wne X Y)) (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) cgcd) c1) $;

--| The primorial of a positive integer is less than or equal to the factorial of the integer. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmolefac (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cfv N cprmo) (cfv N cfa) cle) $;

--| The primorial of a nonnegative integer divides the least common multiple of all positive integers less than or equal to the integer. (Contributed by AV, 19-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmodvdslcmf (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cfv N cprmo) (cfv (co c1 N cfz) clcmf) cdvds) $;

--| The primorial of a positive integer is less than or equal to the least common multiple of all positive integers less than or equal to the integer. (Contributed by AV, 19-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmolelcmf (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cfv N cprmo) (cfv (co c1 N cfz) clcmf) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap : The factorial of a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number is divisible by that integer. (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem1 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wbr I (co (cfv N cfa) I caddc) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap : The factorial of a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number are not coprime. (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem2 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wbr c1 (co (co (cfv N cfa) I caddc) I cgcd) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgaplcm : The least common multiple of all positive integers less than or equal to a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number is divisible by that integer. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 27-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplcmlem1 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wbr I (co (cfv (co c1 N cfz) clcmf) I caddc) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgaplcm : The least common multiple of all positive integers less than or equal to a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number are not coprime. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 27-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplcmlem2 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wbr c1 (co (co (cfv (co c1 N cfz) clcmf) I caddc) I cgcd) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem3 {x3 y1 p: setvar} (A2: class p) (N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (crab p (wbr (cv p) N clt) cprime) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap . (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem4 {x3 y1 p: setvar} (A2: class p) (P N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (crab p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) P cle)) cprime) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel P cprime) (wbr N P clt)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap : for each integer greater than 2 there is a smaller prime closest to this integer, i.e. there is a smaller prime and no other prime is between this prime and the integer. (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem5 {z p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wrex p (wa (wbr (cv p) N clt) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) N cfzo))) cprime) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap : for each positive integer there is a greater prime closest to this integer, i.e. there is a greater prime and no other prime is between this prime and the integer. (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem6 {z p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co N c1 caddc) (cv p) cfzo))) cprime) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap . (Contributed by AV, 12-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Jul-2022.)
theorem prmgaplem7 {z i q p: setvar} (ph: wff i) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cn cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr c1 (co (co (cfv N F) (cv i) caddc) (cv i) cgcd) clt) (co c2 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex p (wrex q (w3a (wbr (cv p) (co (cfv N F) c2 caddc) clt) (wbr (co (cfv N F) N caddc) (cv q) clt) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cv q) cfzo))) cprime) cprime) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgap . (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgaplem8 {z i q p: setvar} (ph: wff i) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cn cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr c1 (co (co (cfv N F) (cv i) caddc) (cv i) cgcd) clt) (co c2 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wbr N (co (cv q) (cv p) cmin) cle) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cv q) cfzo))) cprime) cprime) $;

--| The prime gap theorem: for each positive integer there are (at least) two successive primes with a difference ("gap") at least as big as the given integer. (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgap {z n q p: setvar}:
  $ wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wbr (cv n) (co (cv q) (cv p) cmin) cle) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cv q) cfzo))) cprime) cprime) cn $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ prmgap : in contrast to ~ prmgap , where the gap starts at n! , the factorial of n, the gap starts at the least common multiple of all positive integers less than or equal to n. (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 27-Aug-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prmgaplcm {z n q p: setvar}:
  $ wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wbr (cv n) (co (cv q) (cv p) cmin) cle) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cv q) cfzo))) cprime) cprime) cn $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmgapprmo : The primorial of a number plus an integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to the number are not coprime. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmgapprmolem (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c2 N cfz))) (wbr c1 (co (co (cfv N cprmo) I caddc) I cgcd) clt) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ prmgap : in contrast to ~ prmgap , where the gap starts at n! , the factorial of n, the gap starts at n#, the primorial of n. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem prmgapprmo {z n q p: setvar}:
  $ wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wbr (cv n) (co (cv q) (cv p) cmin) cle) (wral z (wnel (cv z) cprime) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cv q) cfzo))) cprime) cprime) cn $;

--| Divisibility by two is obvious in base 10. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem dec2dvds (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 c2 cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq D (co C c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c2 (cdc A2 D) cdvds) $;

--| Divisibility by five is obvious in base 10. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem dec5dvds (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn $ >
  $ wbr B2 c5 clt $ >
  $ wn (wbr c5 (cdc A2 B2) cdvds) $;

--| Divisibility by five is obvious in base 10. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem dec5dvds2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn $ >
  $ wbr B2 c5 clt $ >
  $ wceq (co c5 B2 caddc) C $ >
  $ wn (wbr c5 (cdc A2 C) cdvds) $;

--| A decimal number greater than 10 and ending with five is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem dec5nprm (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wn (wcel (cdc A2 c5) cprime) $;

--| A decimal number greater than 10 and ending with an even digit is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem dec2nprm (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 c2 cmul) C $ >
  $ wn (wcel (cdc A2 C) cprime) $;

--| Add exponents in a power mod calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.)
theorem modxai (A2 B2 C D E K L M N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cz $ >
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wcel C cn0 $ >
  $ wcel L cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cexp) N cmo) (co K N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 C cexp) N cmo) (co L N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 C caddc) E $ >
  $ wceq (co (co D N cmul) M caddc) (co K L cmul) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 E cexp) N cmo) (co M N cmo) $;

--| Double exponents in a power mod calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Feb-2014.)
theorem mod2xi (A2 B2 D E K M N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cz $ >
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cexp) N cmo) (co K N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co c2 B2 cmul) E $ >
  $ wceq (co (co D N cmul) M caddc) (co K K cmul) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 E cexp) N cmo) (co M N cmo) $;

--| Add one to an exponent in a power mod calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Feb-2014.)
theorem modxp1i (A2 B2 D E K M N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cz $ >
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cexp) N cmo) (co K N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 c1 caddc) E $ >
  $ wceq (co (co D N cmul) M caddc) (co K A2 cmul) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 E cexp) N cmo) (co M N cmo) $;

--| Version of ~ mod2xi where ` A ^ B ` does not equal ` K ` (mod ` N ` ) but instead the negative of ` K ` (mod ` N ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Feb-2014.)
theorem mod2xnegi (A2 B2 D E K L M N: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cz $ >
  $ wcel K cn $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wcel L cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cexp) N cmo) (co L N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co c2 B2 cmul) E $ >
  $ wceq (co L K caddc) N $ >
  $ wceq (co (co D N cmul) M caddc) (co K K cmul) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 E cexp) N cmo) (co M N cmo) $;

--| Subtract from within a mod calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem modsubi (A2 B2 K M N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 N cmo) (co K N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co M B2 caddc) K $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) N cmo) (co M N cmo) $;

--| Calculate a GCD via Euclid's algorithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.)
theorem gcdi (R G K M N: class):
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel R cn0 $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co N R cgcd) G $ >
  $ wceq (co (co K N cmul) R caddc) M $ >
  $ wceq (co M N cgcd) G $;

--| Calculate a GCD via Euclid's algorithm. Theorem 5.6 in [ApostolNT] p. 109. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.)
theorem gcdmodi (R G K N: class):
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel R cn0 $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq (co K N cmo) (co R N cmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co N R cgcd) G $ >
  $ wceq (co K N cgcd) G $;

--| Calculate an integer power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem numexp0 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 cc0 cexp) c1 $;

--| Calculate an integer power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem numexp1 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c1 cexp) A2 $;

--| Calculate an integer power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem numexpp1 (A2 C M N: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co M c1 caddc) N $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 M cexp) A2 cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 N cexp) C $;

--| Double an integer power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem numexp2x (A2 C D M N: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co c2 M cmul) N $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 M cexp) D $ >
  $ wceq (co D D cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 N cexp) C $;

--| Split a decimal number into two parts. Base case: ` N = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 8-Sep-2021.)
theorem decsplit0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) cc0 cexp) cmul) B2 caddc) (co A2 B2 caddc) $;

--| Split a decimal number into two parts. Base case: ` N = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 8-Sep-2021.)
theorem decsplit0 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) cc0 cexp) cmul) cc0 caddc) A2 $;

--| Split a decimal number into two parts. Base case: ` N = 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 8-Sep-2021.)
theorem decsplit1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) c1 cexp) cmul) B2 caddc) (cdc A2 B2) $;

--| Split a decimal number into two parts. Inductive step. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 8-Sep-2021.)
theorem decsplit (A2 B2 C D M N: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co M c1 caddc) N $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) M cexp) cmul) B2 caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) N cexp) cmul) (cdc B2 D) caddc) (cdc C D) $;

--| The Karatsuba multiplication algorithm. If ` X ` and ` Y ` are decomposed into two groups of digits of length ` M ` (only the lower group is known to be this size but the algorithm is most efficient when the partition is chosen near the middle of the digit string), then ` X Y ` can be written in three groups of digits, where each group needs only one multiplication. Thus, we can halve both inputs with only three multiplications on the smaller operands, yielding an asymptotic improvement of n^(log_2 3) instead of n^2 for the "naive" algorithm ~ decmul1c . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem karatsuba (A2 B2 C D R S T M W X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel C cn0 $ >
  $ wcel D cn0 $ >
  $ wcel S cn0 $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 C cmul) R $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 D cmul) T $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) cmul) (co (co R S caddc) T caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co (cdc c1 cc0) M cexp) cmul) B2 caddc) X $ >
  $ wceq (co (co C (co (cdc c1 cc0) M cexp) cmul) D caddc) Y $ >
  $ wceq (co (co R (co (cdc c1 cc0) M cexp) cmul) S caddc) W $ >
  $ wceq (co (co W (co (cdc c1 cc0) M cexp) cmul) T caddc) Z $ >
  $ wceq (co X Y cmul) Z $;

--| Two to the fourth power is 16. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2exp4:
  $ wceq (co c2 c4 cexp) (cdc c1 c6) $;

--| Two to the fifth power is 32. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem _2exp5:
  $ wceq (co c2 c5 cexp) (cdc c3 c2) $;

--| Two to the sixth power is 64. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem _2exp6:
  $ wceq (co c2 c6 cexp) (cdc c6 c4) $;

--| Two to the seventh power is 128. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem _2exp7:
  $ wceq (co c2 c7 cexp) (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c8) $;

--| Two to the eighth power is 256. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2exp8:
  $ wceq (co c2 c8 cexp) (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) $;

--| Two to the eleventh power is 2048. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem _2exp11:
  $ wceq (co c2 (cdc c1 c1) cexp) (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 cc0) c4) c8) $;

--| Two to the sixteenth power is 65536. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2exp16:
  $ wceq (co c2 (cdc c1 c6) cexp) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) $;

--| Three to the third power is 27. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _3exp3:
  $ wceq (co c3 c3 cexp) (cdc c2 c7) $;

--| The factorial grows faster than two to the power ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2016.)
theorem _2expltfac (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wbr (co c2 N cexp) (cfv N cfa) clt) $;

--| If cyclically shifting a word of length being a prime number by a number of positions which is not divisible by the prime number results in the word itself, the word is a "repeated symbol word". (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwsidrepsw (L V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) (wi (w3a (wcel L cz) (wne (co L (cfv W chash) cmo) cc0) (wceq (co W L ccsh) W)) (wceq W (co (cfv cc0 W) (cfv W chash) creps))) $;

--| If cyclically shifting a word of length being a prime number results in the word itself, the shift must be either by 0 (modulo the length of the word) or the word must be a "repeated symbol word". (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwsidrepswmod0 (L V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime) (wcel L cz)) (wi (wceq (co W L ccsh) W) (wo (wceq (co L (cfv W chash) cmo) cc0) (wceq W (co (cfv cc0 W) (cfv W chash) creps)))) $;

--| If cyclically shifting a word of length being a prime number not consisting of identical symbols by at least one position (and not by as many positions as the length of the word), the result will not be the word itself. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 8-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwshashlem1 {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (L V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wrex i (wne (cfv (cv i) W) (cfv cc0 W)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wcel L (co c1 (cfv W chash) cfzo))) (wne (co W L ccsh) W) $;

--| If cyclically shifting a word of length being a prime number and not of identical symbols by different numbers of positions, the resulting words are different. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem cshwshashlem2 {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (K L V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wrex i (wne (cfv (cv i) W) (cfv cc0 W)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo))) (wi (w3a (wcel L (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wcel K (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wbr K L clt)) (wne (co W L ccsh) (co W K ccsh))) $;

--| If cyclically shifting a word of length being a prime number and not of identical symbols by different numbers of positions, the resulting words are different. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem cshwshashlem3 {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (K L V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wrex i (wne (cfv (cv i) W) (cfv cc0 W)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo))) (wi (w3a (wcel L (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wcel K (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wne K L)) (wne (co W L ccsh) (co W K ccsh))) $;

--| The singletons resulting by cyclically shifting a given word of length being a prime number and not consisting of identical symbols is a disjoint collection. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem cshwsdisj {i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (V: class n) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wrex i (wne (cfv (cv i) W) (cfv cc0 W)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo))) (wdisj n (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (csn (co W (cv n) ccsh))) $;

--| The set of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting a given word is an indexed union. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 8-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwsiun {w n: setvar} (M: class w n) (V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cword V)) (wceq M (ciun n (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (csn (co W (cv n) ccsh)))) $;

--| The class of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting a given word is a set. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 8-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwsex {w n: setvar} (M: class w n) (V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel M cvv) $;

--| The size of the set of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting an empty word is 0. (Contributed by AV, 8-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshws0 {w n: setvar} (M: class w n) (V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq W c0) (wceq (cfv M chash) cc0) $;

--| The size of the set of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting a nonempty "repeated symbol word" is 1. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 8-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwrepswhash1 {w n: setvar} (A2: class) (M: class w n) (N V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel N cn) (wceq W (co A2 N creps))) (wceq (cfv M chash) c1) $;

--| If a word (not consisting of identical symbols) has a length being a prime number, the size of the set of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting the original word equals the length of the original word. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwshashnsame {w i n: setvar} (M: class w i n) (V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) (wi (wrex i (wne (cfv (cv i) W) (cfv cc0 W)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wceq (cfv M chash) (cfv W chash))) $;

--| If a word has a length being a prime number, the size of the set of (different!) words resulting by cyclically shifting the original word equals the length of the original word or 1. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Nov-2018.)
theorem cshwshash {w n: setvar} (M: class w n) (V W: class):
  $ wceq M (crab w (wrex n (wceq (co W (cv n) ccsh) (cv w)) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (cword V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W (cword V)) (wcel (cfv W chash) cprime)) (wo (wceq (cfv M chash) (cfv W chash)) (wceq (cfv M chash) c1)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmlem1a and ~ prmlem2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem prmlem0 {x3: setvar} (K M: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds)) (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) N cle)) (wn (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cprime) (wn (wbr K N cdvds)) $ >
  $ wceq (co K c2 caddc) M $ >
  $ wi (wa (wn (wbr c2 K cdvds)) (wcel (cv x3) (cfv K cuz))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) N cle)) (wn (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prmlem1 and ~ prmlem2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem prmlem1a {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wbr c1 N clt $ >
  $ wn (wbr c2 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c3 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wn (wbr c2 c5 cdvds)) (wcel (cv x3) (cfv c5 cuz))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) N cle)) (wn (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds))) $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| A quick proof skeleton to show that the numbers less than 25 are prime, by trial division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem prmlem1 (N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wbr c1 N clt $ >
  $ wn (wbr c2 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c3 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wbr N (cdc c2 c5) clt $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| 5 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _5prm:
  $ wcel c5 cprime $;

--| 6 is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem _6nprm:
  $ wn (wcel c6 cprime) $;

--| 7 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7prm:
  $ wcel c7 cprime $;

--| 8 is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem _8nprm:
  $ wn (wcel c8 cprime) $;

--| 9 is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem _9nprm:
  $ wn (wcel c9 cprime) $;

--| 10 is not a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by AV, 6-Sep-2021.) (Proof shortened by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _10nprm:
  $ wn (wcel (cdc c1 cc0) cprime) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ 10nprm as of 10-Jun-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by AV, 6-Sep-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _10nprmOLD:
  $ wn (wcel (cdc c1 cc0) cprime) $;

--| 11 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _11prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c1 c1) cprime $;

--| 13 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _13prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c1 c3) cprime $;

--| 17 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _17prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c1 c7) cprime $;

--| 19 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _19prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c1 c9) cprime $;

--| 23 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _23prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c2 c3) cprime $;

--| Our last proving session got as far as 25 because we started with the two "bootstrap" primes 2 and 3, and the next prime is 5, so knowing that 2 and 3 are prime and 4 is not allows to cover the numbers less than ` 5 ^ 2 = 2 5 ` . Additionally, nonprimes are "easy", so we can extend this range of known prime/nonprimes all the way until 29, which is the first prime larger than 25. Thus, in this lemma we extend another blanket out to ` 2 9 ^ 2 = 8 4 1 ` , from which we can prove even more primes. If we wanted, we could keep doing this, but the goal is Bertrand's postulate, and for that we only need a few large primes - we don't need to find them all, as we have been doing thus far. So after this blanket runs out, we'll have to switch to another method (see ~ 1259prm ). As a side note, you can see the pattern of the primes in the indentation pattern of this lemma! (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem prmlem2 (N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wbr N (cdc (cdc c8 c4) c1) clt $ >
  $ wbr c1 N clt $ >
  $ wn (wbr c2 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c3 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c5 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr c7 N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr (cdc c1 c1) N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr (cdc c1 c3) N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr (cdc c1 c7) N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr (cdc c1 c9) N cdvds) $ >
  $ wn (wbr (cdc c2 c3) N cdvds) $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| 37 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _37prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c3 c7) cprime $;

--| 43 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _43prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c4 c3) cprime $;

--| 83 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _83prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c8 c3) cprime $;

--| 139 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _139prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c1 c3) c9) cprime $;

--| 163 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _163prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c1 c6) c3) cprime $;

--| 317 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _317prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c3 c1) c7) cprime $;

--| 631 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _631prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c6 c3) c1) cprime $;

--| The primorial of 4. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo4:
  $ wceq (cfv c4 cprmo) c6 $;

--| The primorial of 5. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo5:
  $ wceq (cfv c5 cprmo) (cdc c3 cc0) $;

--| The primorial of 6. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem prmo6:
  $ wceq (cfv c6 cprmo) (cdc c3 cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1259prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 1 6 = 5 2 N + 6 8 == 6 8 ` and ` 2 ^ 1 7 == 6 8 x. 2 = 1 3 6 ` in this lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _1259lem1 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc c1 c7) cexp) N cmo) (co (cdc (cdc c1 c3) c6) N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1259prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 3 4 = ( 2 ^ 1 7 ) ^ 2 == 1 3 6 ^ 2 == 1 4 N + 8 7 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem _1259lem2 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc c3 c4) cexp) N cmo) (co (cdc (cdc c8 c7) cc0) N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1259prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 3 8 = 2 ^ 3 4 x. 2 ^ 4 == 8 7 0 x. 1 6 = 1 1 N + 7 1 ` and ` 2 ^ 7 6 = ( 2 ^ 3 4 ) ^ 2 == 7 1 ^ 2 = 4 N + 5 == 5 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _1259lem3 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc c7 c6) cexp) N cmo) (co c5 N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1259prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 3 0 6 = ( 2 ^ 7 6 ) ^ 4 x. 4 == 5 ^ 4 x. 4 = 2 N - 1 8 ` , ` 2 ^ 6 1 2 = ( 2 ^ 3 0 6 ) ^ 2 == 1 8 ^ 2 = 3 2 4 ` , ` 2 ^ 6 2 9 = 2 ^ 6 1 2 x. 2 ^ 1 7 == 3 2 4 x. 1 3 6 = 3 5 N - 1 ` and finally ` 2 ^ ( N - 1 ) = ( 2 ^ 6 2 9 ) ^ 2 == 1 ^ 2 = 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _1259lem4 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) N cmo) (co c1 N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1259prm . Calculate the GCD of ` 2 ^ 3 4 - 1 == 8 6 9 ` with ` N = 1 2 5 9 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _1259lem5 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (co c2 (cdc c3 c4) cexp) c1 cmin) N cgcd) c1 $;

--| 1259 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _1259prm (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c5) c9) $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2503prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 1 8 = 5 1 2 ^ 2 = 1 0 4 N + 1 8 3 2 == 1 8 3 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _2503lem1 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c5) cc0) c3) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc c1 c8) cexp) N cmo) (co (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c8) c3) c2) N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2503prm . Calculate a power mod. We calculate ` 2 ^ 1 9 = 2 ^ 1 8 x. 2 == 1 8 3 2 x. 2 = N + 1 1 6 1 ` , ` 2 ^ 3 8 = ( 2 ^ 1 9 ) ^ 2 == 1 1 6 1 ^ 2 = 5 3 8 N + 1 3 0 7 ` , ` 2 ^ 3 9 = 2 ^ 3 8 x. 2 == 1 3 0 7 x. 2 = N + 1 1 1 ` , ` 2 ^ 7 8 = ( 2 ^ 3 9 ) ^ 2 == 1 1 1 ^ 2 = 5 N - 1 9 4 ` , ` 2 ^ 1 5 6 = ( 2 ^ 7 8 ) ^ 2 == 1 9 4 ^ 2 = 1 5 N + 9 1 ` , ` 2 ^ 3 1 2 = ( 2 ^ 1 5 6 ) ^ 2 == 9 1 ^ 2 = 3 N + 7 7 2 ` , ` 2 ^ 6 2 4 = ( 2 ^ 3 1 2 ) ^ 2 == 7 7 2 ^ 2 = 2 3 8 N + 2 7 0 ` , ` 2 ^ 1 2 4 8 = ( 2 ^ 6 2 4 ) ^ 2 == 2 7 0 ^ 2 = 2 9 N + 3 1 3 ` , ` 2 ^ 1 2 5 1 = 2 ^ 1 2 4 8 x. 8 == 3 1 3 x. 8 = N + 1 ` and finally ` 2 ^ ( N - 1 ) = ( 2 ^ 1 2 5 1 ) ^ 2 == 1 ^ 2 = 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _2503lem2 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c5) cc0) c3) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) N cmo) (co c1 N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2503prm . Calculate the GCD of ` 2 ^ 1 8 - 1 == 1 8 3 1 ` with ` N = 2 5 0 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem _2503lem3 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c5) cc0) c3) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (co c2 (cdc c1 c8) cexp) c1 cmin) N cgcd) c1 $;

--| 2503 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2503prm (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c5) cc0) c3) $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4001prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 1 2 = 4 0 9 6 = N + 9 5 ` , ` 2 ^ 2 4 = ( 2 ^ 1 2 ) ^ 2 == 9 5 ^ 2 = 2 N + 1 0 2 3 ` , ` 2 ^ 2 5 = 2 ^ 2 4 x. 2 == 1 0 2 3 x. 2 = 2 0 4 6 ` , ` 2 ^ 5 0 = ( 2 ^ 2 5 ) ^ 2 == 2 0 4 6 ^ 2 = 1 0 4 6 N + 1 0 7 0 ` , ` 2 ^ 1 0 0 = ( 2 ^ 5 0 ) ^ 2 == 1 0 7 0 ^ 2 = 2 8 6 N + 6 1 4 ` and ` 2 ^ 2 0 0 = ( 2 ^ 1 0 0 ) ^ 2 == 6 1 4 ^ 2 = 9 4 N + 9 0 2 ` ` == 9 0 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _4001lem1 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) cc0) c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc (cdc c2 cc0) cc0) cexp) N cmo) (co (cdc (cdc c9 cc0) c2) N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4001prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 4 0 0 = ( 2 ^ 2 0 0 ) ^ 2 == 9 0 2 ^ 2 = 2 0 3 N + 1 4 0 1 ` and ` 2 ^ 8 0 0 = ( 2 ^ 4 0 0 ) ^ 2 == 1 4 0 1 ^ 2 = 4 9 0 N + 2 3 1 1 ` ` == 2 3 1 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _4001lem2 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) cc0) c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc (cdc c8 cc0) cc0) cexp) N cmo) (co (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c3) c1) c1) N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4001prm . Calculate a power mod. In decimal, we calculate ` 2 ^ 1 0 0 0 = 2 ^ 8 0 0 x. 2 ^ 2 0 0 == 2 3 1 1 x. 9 0 2 ` ` = 5 2 1 N + 1 ` and finally ` 2 ^ ( N - 1 ) = ( 2 ^ 1 0 0 0 ) ^ 4 == 1 ^ 4 = 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _4001lem3 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) cc0) c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) N cmo) (co c1 N cmo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 4001prm . Calculate the GCD of ` 2 ^ 8 0 0 - 1 == 2 3 1 0 ` with ` N = 4 0 0 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _4001lem4 (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) cc0) c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co (co c2 (cdc (cdc c8 cc0) cc0) cexp) c1 cmin) N cgcd) c1 $;

--| 4001 is a prime number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _4001prm (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) cc0) c1) $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of structures with components numbered below ` A ` .
term cstr: class;

--| Define a structure with components in ` M ... N ` . This is not a requirement for groups, posets, etc., but it is a useful assumption for component extraction theorems. As mentioned in the section header, an "extensible structure should be implemented as a function (a set of ordered pairs)". The current definition, however, is less restrictive: it allows for classes which contain the empty set ` (/) ` to be extensible structures. Because of ~ 0nelfun , such classes cannot be functions. Without the empty set, however, a structure must be a function, see ~ structn0fun : ` F Struct X -> Fun ( F \ { (/) } ) ` . Allowing an extensible structure to contain the empty set ensures that expressions like ` { <. A , B >. , <. C , D >. } ` are structures without asserting or implying that ` A ` , ` B ` , ` C ` and ` D ` are sets (if ` A ` or ` B ` is a proper class, then ` <. A , B >. = (/) ` , see ~ opprc ). This is used critically in ~ strle1 , ~ strle2 , ~ strle3 and ~ strleun to avoid sethood hypotheses on the "payload" sets: without this, ~ ipsstr and theorems like it will have many sethood assumptions, and may not even be usable in the empty context. Instead, the sethood assumption is deferred until it is actually needed, e.g., ~ ipsbase , which requires that the base set be a set but not any of the other components. Usually, a concrete structure like ` CCfld ` does not contain the empty set, and therefore is a function, see ~ cnfldfun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_struct {x3 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cstr (copab f2 x3 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cin cle (cxp cn cn))) (wfun (cdif (cv f2) (csn c0))) (wss (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) cfz)))) $;

--| The structure relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem brstruct:
  $ wrel cstr $;

--| The property of being a structure with components in ` ( 1st `` X ) ... ( 2nd `` X ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem isstruct2 (F X: class):
  $ wb (wbr F X cstr) (w3a (wcel X (cin cle (cxp cn cn))) (wfun (cdif F (csn c0))) (wss (cdm F) (cfv X cfz))) $;

--| A structure is a set. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2021.)
theorem structex (G X: class):
  $ wi (wbr G X cstr) (wcel G cvv) $;

--| A structure without the empty set is a function. (Contributed by AV, 13-Nov-2021.)
theorem structn0fun (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wfun (cdif F (csn c0))) $;

--| The property of being a structure with components in ` M ... N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem isstruct (F M N: class):
  $ wb (wbr F (cop M N) cstr) (w3a (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn) (wbr M N cle)) (wfun (cdif F (csn c0))) (wss (cdm F) (co M N cfz))) $;

--| Two ways to express the relational part of a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem structcnvcnv (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wceq (ccnv (ccnv F)) (cdif F (csn c0))) $;

--| The converse of the converse of a structure is a function. Closed form of ~ structfun . (Contributed by AV, 12-Nov-2021.)
theorem structfung (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wfun (ccnv (ccnv F))) $;

--| Convert between two kinds of structure closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Nov-2021.)
theorem structfun (F X: class):
  $ wbr F X cstr $ >
  $ wfun (ccnv (ccnv F)) $;

--| Convert between two kinds of structure closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem structfn (F M N: class):
  $ wbr F (cop M N) cstr $ >
  $ wa (wfun (ccnv (ccnv F))) (wss (cdm F) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Combine two structures into one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem strleun (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wbr F (cop A2 B2) cstr $ >
  $ wbr G (cop C D) cstr $ >
  $ wbr B2 C clt $ >
  $ wbr (cun F G) (cop A2 D) cstr $;

--| Make a structure from a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem strle1 (A2 I X: class):
  $ wcel I cn $ >
  $ wceq A2 I $ >
  $ wbr (csn (cop A2 X)) (cop I I) cstr $;

--| Make a structure from a pair. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem strle2 (A2 B2 I J X Y: class):
  $ wcel I cn $ >
  $ wceq A2 I $ >
  $ wbr I J clt $ >
  $ wcel J cn $ >
  $ wceq B2 J $ >
  $ wbr (cpr (cop A2 X) (cop B2 Y)) (cop I J) cstr $;

--| Make a structure from a triple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem strle3 (A2 B2 C I J K X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel I cn $ >
  $ wceq A2 I $ >
  $ wbr I J clt $ >
  $ wcel J cn $ >
  $ wceq B2 J $ >
  $ wbr J K clt $ >
  $ wcel K cn $ >
  $ wceq C K $ >
  $ wbr (ctp (cop A2 X) (cop B2 Y) (cop C Z)) (cop I K) cstr $;

--| A special version of class substitution commonly used for structures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Mar-2019.) (Revised by SN, 2-Mar-2025.)
theorem sbcie2s {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (ps: wff) (w: setvar) (A2 B2: class a b) (E: class) (F: class a) (W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv W E) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W F) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv a) A2) (wceq (cv b) B2)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) W) (wb (wsbc a (wsbc b ph (cfv (cv w) F)) (cfv (cv w) E)) ps) $;

--| A special version of class substitution commonly used for structures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Mar-2019.)
theorem sbcie3s {a b c: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff a b c) (w: setvar) (A2 B2 C: class a b c) (E: class) (F: class a) (G: class a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv W E) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W F) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv W G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wceq (cv a) A2) (wceq (cv b) B2) (wceq (cv c) C)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv w) W) (wb (wsbc a (wsbc b (wsbc c ps (cfv (cv w) G)) (cfv (cv w) F)) (cfv (cv w) E)) ph) $;

--| Set components of a structure.
term csts: class;

--| Set a component of an extensible structure. This function is useful for taking an existing structure and "overriding" one of its components. For example, ~ df-ress adjusts the base set to match its second argument, which has the effect of making subgroups, subspaces, subrings etc. from the original structures. Or ~ df-mgp , which takes a ring and overrides its addition operation with the multiplicative operation, so that we can consider the "multiplicative group" using group and monoid theorems, which expect the operation to be in the ` +g ` slot instead of the ` .r ` slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_sets {e s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csts (cmpo s1 e cvv cvv (cun (cres (cv s1) (cdif cvv (cdm (csn (cv e))))) (csn (cv e)))) $;

--| The structure override operator is a proper operator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem reldmsets:
  $ wrel (cdm csts) $;

--| Value of the structure replacement function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem setsvalg (A2 S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel A2 W)) (wceq (co S A2 csts) (cun (cres S (cdif cvv (cdm (csn A2)))) (csn A2))) $;

--| Value of the structure replacement function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem setsval (A2 B2 S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) (cun (cres S (cdif cvv (csn A2))) (csn (cop A2 B2)))) $;

--| The value of the structure replacement function for its first argument is its second argument. (Contributed by SO, 12-Jul-2018.)
theorem fvsetsid (U F V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel X W) (wcel Y U)) (wceq (cfv X (co F (cop X Y) csts)) Y) $;

--| The structure replacement function is a function. (Contributed by SO, 12-Jul-2018.)
theorem fsets (A2 B2 F V X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel F V) (wf A2 B2 F)) (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wf A2 B2 (co F (cop X Y) csts)) $;

--| The domain of a structure with replacement is the domain of the original structure extended by the index of the replacement. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2021.)
theorem setsdm (E G I V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (cdm (co G (cop I E) csts)) (cun (cdm G) (csn I))) $;

--| A structure with replacement is a function if the original structure is a function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2021.)
theorem setsfun (U E G I V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G V) (wfun G)) (wa (wcel I U) (wcel E W))) (wfun (co G (cop I E) csts)) $;

--| A structure with replacement without the empty set is a function if the original structure without the empty set is a function. This variant of ~ setsfun is useful for proofs based on ~ isstruct2 which requires ` Fun ( F \ { (/) } ) ` for ` F ` to be an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2021.)
theorem setsfun0 (U E G I V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G V) (wfun (cdif G (csn c0)))) (wa (wcel I U) (wcel E W))) (wfun (cdif (co G (cop I E) csts) (csn c0))) $;

--| The value of the structure replacement function (without the empty set) is a function if the structure (without the empty set) is a function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2021.) (Revised by AV, 16-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsn0fun (ph: wff) (S U E I W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr S X cstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (cdif (co S (cop I E) csts) (csn c0))) $;

--| An extensible structure with a replaced slot is an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 14-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsstruct2 (E G I V X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wbr G X cstr) (wcel E V) (wcel I cn)) (wceq Y (cop (cif (wbr I (cfv X c1st) cle) I (cfv X c1st)) (cif (wbr I (cfv X c2nd) cle) (cfv X c2nd) I)))) (wbr (co G (cop I E) csts) Y cstr) $;

--| An extensible structure with a replaced slot is an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 14-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsexstruct2 {y1: setvar} (E G I: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr G X cstr) (wcel E V) (wcel I cn)) (wex y1 (wbr (co G (cop I E) csts) (cv y1) cstr)) $;

--| An extensible structure with a replaced slot is an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2021.) (Revised by AV, 14-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsstruct (E G I M N V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel E V) (wcel I (cfv M cuz)) (wbr G (cop M N) cstr)) (wbr (co G (cop I E) csts) (cop M (cif (wbr I N cle) N I)) cstr) $;

--| Closure of structure replacement in a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunsets (ph: wff) (A2 S U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S A2 csts) U) $;

--| The structure replacement function does not affect the value of ` S ` away from ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem setsres (A2 B2 S V: class):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq (cres (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) (cdif cvv (csn A2))) (cres S (cdif cvv (csn A2)))) $;

--| Replacing the same components twice yields the same as the second setting only. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem setsabs (A2 B2 C S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel C W)) (wceq (co (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) (cop A2 C) csts) (co S (cop A2 C) csts)) $;

--| Different components can be set in any order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem setscom (A2 B2 C D S V W X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S V) (wne A2 B2)) (wa (wcel C W) (wcel D X))) (wceq (co (co S (cop A2 C) csts) (cop B2 D) csts) (co (co S (cop B2 D) csts) (cop A2 C) csts)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the slot function.
term cslot (A2: class): class;

--| Define the slot extractor for extensible structures. The class ` Slot A ` is a function whose argument can be any set, although it is meaningful only if that set is a member of an extensible structure (such as a partially ordered set ( ~ df-poset ) or a group ( ~ df-grp )). Note that ` Slot A ` is implemented as "evaluation at ` A ` ". That is, ` ( Slot A `` S ) ` is defined to be ` ( S `` A ) ` , where ` A ` will typically be an index (which is implemented as a small natural number) of a component of an extensible structure ` S ` . Each extensible structure is a function defined on specific (natural number) "slots", and the function ` Slot A ` extracts the structure's component as a function value at a particular slot (with index ` A ` ). The special "structure" ` ndx ` , defined as the identity function restricted to ` NN ` , can be used to extract the number ` A ` from a slot, since ` ( Slot A `` ndx ) = A ` (see ~ ndxarg ). This is typically used to refer to the number of a slot when defining structures without having to expose the detail of what that number is (for instance, we use the expression ` ( Base `` ndx ) ` in theorems and proofs instead of its hard-coded, numeric value 1), and discourage using the specific definition of slot extractors like ` Base = Slot 1 ` (see ~ df-base ). Actually, these definitions are used in two basic theorems named *id (theorems of the form ` C = Slot ( C `` ndx ) ` ) and *ndx (theorems of the form ` ( C `` ndx ) = N ` ) only (see, for example, ~ baseid and ~ basendx ), except additionally in the discouraged theorem ~ baseval to demonstrate the representations of the value of the base set extractor. The *id theorems are implementation independent equivalents of the definitions by the means of ~ ndxid , but the *ndx theorems still depend on the hard-coded values of the indices. Therefore, the usage of these *ndx theorems is also discouraged (for more details see the section header comment ~ mmtheorems.html#cnx ). Example: The group operation is the second component, i.e., the component in the second slot, of a group-like structure ` G = { <. ( Base `` ndx ) , B >. , <. ( +g `` ndx ) , .+ >. } ` . The slot extractor ` +g = Slot 2 ` (see ~ df-plusg ) applied on the structure ` G ` provides the group operation ` .+ = ( +g `` G ) ` . Expanding the definitions, we get ` .+ = ( Slot 2 `` G ) = ( G `` 2 ) = ( G `` ( +g `` ndx ) ) ` (for the last equation, see ~ plusgndx ). The class ` Slot ` cannot be defined as ` ( x e. _V |-> ( f e. _V |-> ( f `` x ) ) ) ` because each ` Slot A ` is a function on the proper class ` _V ` so is itself a proper class, and the values of functions are sets ( ~ fvex ). It is necessary to allow proper classes as values of ` Slot A ` since for instance the class of all (base sets of) groups is proper. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_slot {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cslot A2) (cmpt x3 cvv (cfv A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the ` Slot ` construction. The converse holds if ` A ` (or ` B ` ) is a set. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Dec-2021.)
theorem sloteq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cslot A2) (cslot B2)) $;

--| A slot is a function on sets, treated as structures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem slotfn (E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wfn E cvv $;

--| Deduction version of ~ strfvn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem strfvnd (ph: wff) (S E N V: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S E) (cfv N S)) $;

--| Value of a structure component extractor ` E ` . Normally, ` E ` is a defined constant symbol such as ` Base ` ( ~ df-base ) and ` N ` is the index of the component. ` S ` is a structure, i.e. a specific member of a class of structures such as ` Poset ` ( ~ df-poset ) where ` S e. Poset ` . Hint: Do not substitute ` N ` by a specific (positive) integer to be independent of a hard-coded index value. Often, ` ( E `` ndx ) ` can be used instead of ` N ` . Alternatively, use ~ strfv instead of ~ strfvn . (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem strfvn (S E N: class):
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv S E) (cfv N S) $;

--| A structure component extractor produces a value which is contained in a set dependent on ` S ` , but not ` E ` . This is sometimes useful for showing sethood. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem strfvss (S E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wss (cfv S E) (cuni (crn S)) $;

--| Closure of a structure index in a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunstr (ph: wff) (S U E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S E) U) $;

--| All components of the empty set are empty sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem str0 (F I: class):
  $ wceq F (cslot I) $ >
  $ wceq c0 (cfv c0 F) $;

--| Structure slot extractors cannot distinguish between proper classes and ` (/) ` , so they can be protected using the identity function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem strfvi (S E N X: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv S E) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv (cfv S cid) E) $;

--| Lemma for showing the equality of values for functions like slot extractors ` E ` at a proper class. Extracted from several former proofs of lemmas like ~ zlmlem . (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem fveqprc (E F X Y: class):
  $ wceq (cfv c0 E) c0 $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv X F) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel X cvv)) (wceq (cfv X E) (cfv Y E)) $;

--| Lemma for showing the equality of values for functions like slot extractors ` E ` at a proper class. Extracted from several former proofs of lemmas like ~ resvlem . (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem oveqprc (E O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq (cfv c0 E) c0 $ >
  $ wceq Z (co X Y O) $ >
  $ wrel (cdm O) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wcel X cvv)) (wceq (cfv X E) (cfv Z E)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the structure component index extractor.
term cnx: class;

--| Define the structure component index extractor. See Theorem ~ ndxarg to understand its purpose. The restriction to ` NN ` ensures that ` ndx ` is a set. The restriction to some set is necessary since ` _I ` is a proper class. In principle, we could have chosen ` CC ` or (if we revise all structure component definitions such as ~ df-base ) another set such as the set of finite ordinals ` _om ` ( ~ df-om ). (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.)
axiom df_ndx:
  $ wceq cnx (cres cid cn) $;

--| Closure of the index extractor in an infinite weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem wunndx (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cnx U) $;

--| Get the numeric argument from a defined structure component extractor such as ~ df-base . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem ndxarg (E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cnx E) N $;

--| A structure component extractor is defined by its own index. This theorem, together with ~ strfv below, is useful for avoiding direct reference to the hard-coded numeric index in component extractor definitions, such as the ` 1 ` in ~ df-base and the ` ; 1 0 ` in ~ df-ple , making it easier to change should the need arise. For example, we can refer to a specific poset with base set ` B ` and order relation ` L ` using ` { <. ( Base `` ndx ) , B >. , ` ` <. ( le `` ndx ) , L >. } ` rather than ` { <. 1 , B >. , ` ` <. ; 1 0 , L >. } ` . The latter, while shorter to state, requires revision if we later change ` ; 1 0 ` to some other number, and it may also be harder to remember. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 27-Dec-2021.)
theorem ndxid (E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $;

--| The value of a structure component extractor is the value of the corresponding slot of the structure. (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) Use ~ strfvnd directly with ` N ` set to ` ( E `` ndx ) ` if possible.
theorem strndxid (ph: wff) (S E N V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv cnx E) S) (cfv S E)) $;

--| Value of the structure replacement function, deduction version. Hint: Do not substitute ` N ` by a specific (positive) integer to be independent of a hard-coded index value. Often, ` ( E `` ndx ) ` can be used instead of ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2020.) (Revised by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem setsidvald (ph: wff) (S E N V: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co S (cop N (cfv S E)) csts)) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ strfv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem strfvd (ph: wff) (C S E V: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop (cfv cnx E) C) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv S E)) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ strfv2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem strfv2d (ph: wff) (C S E V W: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (ccnv (ccnv S))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop (cfv cnx E) C) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv S E)) $;

--| A variation on ~ strfv to avoid asserting that ` S ` itself is a function, which involves sethood of all the ordered pair components of ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem strfv2 (C S E V: class):
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wfun (ccnv (ccnv S)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wcel (cop (cfv cnx E) C) S $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wceq C (cfv S E)) $;

--| Extract a structure component ` C ` (such as the base set) from a structure ` S ` (such as a member of ` Poset ` , ~ df-poset ) with a component extractor ` E ` (such as the base set extractor ~ df-base ). By virtue of ~ ndxid , this can be done without having to refer to the hard-coded numeric index of ` E ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem strfv (C S E V X: class):
  $ wbr S X cstr $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wss (csn (cop (cfv cnx E) C)) S $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wceq C (cfv S E)) $;

--| Variant on ~ strfv for large structures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2017.)
theorem strfv3 (ph: wff) (A2 C S U E V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U S) $ >
  $ wbr S X cstr $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wss (csn (cop (cfv cnx E) C)) S $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv U E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ strss . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem strssd (ph: wff) (C S T E V: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop (cfv cnx E) C) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv T E) (cfv S E)) $;

--| Propagate component extraction to a structure ` T ` from a subset structure ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2014.)
theorem strss (C S T E: class):
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wfun T $ >
  $ wss S T $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wcel (cop (cfv cnx E) C) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv T E) (cfv S E) $;

--| Value of the structure replacement function at a replaced index. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem setsid (A2 C E V W: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W A2) (wcel C V)) (wceq C (cfv (co W (cop (cfv cnx E) C) csts) E)) $;

--| Value of the structure replacement function at an untouched index. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 7-Nov-2024.)
theorem setsnid (C D E W: class):
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) D $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W E) (cfv (co W (cop D C) csts) E) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all base set extractors.
term cbs: class;

--| Define the base set (also called underlying set, ground set, carrier set, or carrier) extractor for extensible structures. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ baseid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_base:
  $ wceq cbs (cslot c1) $;

--| Value of the base set extractor. (Normally it is preferred to work with ` ( Base `` ndx ) ` rather than the hard-coded ` 1 ` in order to make structure theorems portable. This is an example of how to obtain it when needed.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.)
theorem baseval (K: class):
  $ wcel K cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv c1 K) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-base . (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.)
theorem baseid:
  $ wceq cbs (cslot (cfv cnx cbs)) $;

--| The base set extractor is a function on ` _V ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Jul-2015.)
theorem basfn:
  $ wfn cbs cvv $;

--| The base set of the empty structure. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 7-Jul-2016.)
theorem base0:
  $ wceq c0 (cfv c0 cbs) $;

--| Utility theorem: reverse closure for any structure defined as a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem elbasfv (B2 S F X Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv Z F) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wcel Z cvv) $;

--| Utility theorem: reverse closure for any structure defined as a two-argument function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2015.)
theorem elbasov (A2 B2 S O X Y: class):
  $ wrel (cdm O) $ >
  $ wceq S (co X Y O) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wa (wcel X cvv) (wcel Y cvv)) $;

--| Partial reverse closure for any structure defined as a two-argument function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Dec-2019.)
theorem strov2rcl (B2 R S F I X: class):
  $ wceq S (co I R F) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wrel (cdm F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wcel I cvv) $;

--| Index value of the base set extractor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2013.) Use of this theorem is discouraged since the particular value ` 1 ` for the index is an implementation detail, see section header comment ~ mmtheorems.html#cnx for more information. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem basendx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cbs) c1 $;

--| The index value of the base set extractor is a positive integer. This property should be ensured for every concrete coding because otherwise it could not be used in an extensible structure (slots must be positive integers). (Contributed by AV, 23-Sep-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cbs) cn $;

--| The index of the base set is an element in a weak universe containing the natural numbers. Formerly part of proof for ~ 1strwun . (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.) (Revised by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem basndxelwund (ph: wff) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv cnx cbs) U) $;

--| The pair of the base index and another index is a subset of the domain of the structure obtained by replacing/adding a slot at the other index in a structure having a base slot. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2021.) (Revised by AV, 16-Nov-2021.)
theorem basprssdmsets (ph: wff) (S U E I W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr S X cstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv cnx cbs) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cpr (cfv cnx cbs) I) (cdm (co S (cop I E) csts))) $;

--| The base set of a structure with a base set. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2021.)
theorem opelstrbas (ph: wff) (S V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr S X cstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop (cfv cnx cbs) V) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv S cbs)) $;

--| A constructed one-slot structure. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2024.)
theorem _1strstr (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2)) $ >
  $ wbr G (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cbs)) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed one-slot structure. (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Nov-2024.)
theorem _1strbas (B2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $;

--| A constructed one-slot structure in a weak universe containing the index of the base set extractor. (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.)
theorem _1strwunbndx (ph: wff) (B2 U G: class):
  $ wceq G (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv cnx cbs) U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 U)) (wcel G U) $;

--| A constructed one-slot structure in a weak universe. (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem _1strwun (ph: wff) (B2 U G: class):
  $ wceq G (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 U)) (wcel G U) $;

--| A constructed two-slot structure not depending on the hard-coded index value of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 22-Sep-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem _2strstr (B2 c_pl G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop N c_pl)) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) N clt $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wbr G (cop (cfv cnx cbs) N) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed two-slot structure not depending on the hard-coded index value of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 22-Sep-2020.)
theorem _2strbas (B2 c_pl G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop N c_pl)) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) N clt $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $;

--| The other slot of a constructed two-slot structure not depending on the hard-coded index value of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 22-Sep-2020.)
theorem _2strop (B2 c_pl E G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop N c_pl)) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) N clt $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot N) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl V) (wceq c_pl (cfv G E)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the extensible structure builder restriction operator.
term cress: class;

--| Define a multifunction restriction operator for extensible structures, which can be used to turn statements about rings into statements about subrings, modules into submodules, etc. This definition knows nothing about individual structures and merely truncates the ` Base ` set while leaving operators alone; individual kinds of structures will need to handle this behavior, by ignoring operators' values outside the range (like ` Ring ` ), defining a function using the base set and applying that (like ` TopGrp ` ), or explicitly truncating the slot before use (like ` MetSp ` ). (Credit for this operator goes to Mario Carneiro.) See ~ ressbas for the altered base set, and ~ resseqnbas ( ~ subrg0 , ~ ressplusg , ~ subrg1 , ~ ressmulr ) for the (un)altered other operations. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_ress {x3 w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cress (cmpo w x3 cvv cvv (cif (wss (cfv (cv w) cbs) (cv x3)) (cv w) (co (cv w) (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cin (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cbs))) csts))) $;

--| The structure restriction is a proper operator, so it can be used with ~ ovprc1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem reldmress:
  $ wrel (cdm cress) $;

--| Value of structure restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressval (A2 B2 R W X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W X) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq R (cif (wss B2 A2) W (co W (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cin A2 B2)) csts))) $;

--| General behavior of trivial restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressid2 (A2 B2 R W X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss B2 A2) (wcel W X) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq R W) $;

--| Value of nontrivial structure restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressval2 (A2 B2 R W X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wn (wss B2 A2)) (wcel W X) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq R (co W (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cin A2 B2)) csts)) $;

--| Base set of a structure restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 7-Nov-2024.)
theorem ressbas (A2 B2 R V W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cin A2 B2) (cfv R cbs)) $;

--| The base set of a restriction to ` A ` is a subset of ` A ` and the base set ` B ` of the original structure. (Contributed by SN, 10-Jan-2025.)
theorem ressbasssg (A2 B2 R W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wss (cfv R cbs) (cin A2 B2) $;

--| Base set of a structure restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem ressbas2 (A2 B2 R W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wceq A2 (cfv R cbs)) $;

--| The base set of a restriction is a subset of the base set of the original structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by SN, 25-Feb-2025.)
theorem ressbasss (A2 B2 R W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wss (cfv R cbs) B2 $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ ressbas as of 25-Feb-2025. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem ressbasssOLD (A2 B2 R W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wss (cfv R cbs) B2 $;

--| The base set of a restriction to ` A ` is a subset of ` A ` . (Contributed by SN, 10-Jan-2025.)
theorem ressbasss2 (A2 R W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wss (cfv R cbs) A2 $;

--| The components of an extensible structure except the base set remain unchanged on a structure restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 19-Oct-2024.)
theorem resseqnbas (A2 C R E V W: class):
  $ wceq R (co W A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv W E) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) (cfv cnx cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq C (cfv R E)) $;

--| All restrictions of the empty set are trivial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem ress0 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (co c0 A2 cress) c0 $;

--| Behavior of trivial restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressid (B2 W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W X) (wceq (co W B2 cress) W) $;

--| Restriction only cares about the part of the second set which intersects the base of the first. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressinbas (A2 B2 W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (co W A2 cress) (co W (cin A2 B2) cress)) $;

--| Value of structure restriction, deduction version. (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2020.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2022.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem ressval3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S E V: class):
  $ wceq R (co S A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv cnx cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co S (cop E A2) csts)) $;

--| Restriction composition law. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem ressress (A2 B2 W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y)) (wceq (co (co W A2 cress) B2 cress) (co W (cin A2 B2) cress)) $;

--| Restriction absorption law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem ressabs (A2 B2 W X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wss B2 A2)) (wceq (co (co W A2 cress) B2 cress) (co W B2 cress)) $;

--| Closure of structure restriction in a weak universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem wunress (ph: wff) (A2 U W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co W A2 cress) U) $;

--| Extend class notation with group (addition) operation.
term cplusg: class;

--| Extend class notation with ring multiplication.
term cmulr: class;

--| Extend class notation with involution.
term cstv: class;

--| Extend class notation with scalar field.
term csca: class;

--| Extend class notation with scalar product.
term cvsca: class;

--| Extend class notation with Hermitian form (inner product).
term cip: class;

--| Extend class notation with the topology component of a topological space.
term cts: class;

--| Extend class notation with "less than or equal to" for posets.
term cple: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of orthocomplementation extractors.
term coc: class;

--| Extend class notation with the metric space distance function.
term cds: class;

--| Extend class notation with the uniform structure.
term cunif: class;

--| Extend class notation with the hom-set structure.
term chom: class;

--| Extend class notation with the composition operation.
term cco: class;

--| Define group operation. In the context of less restrictive structures, this operation is also called magma, semigroup or monoid operation. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ plusgid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_plusg:
  $ wceq cplusg (cslot c2) $;

--| Define ring multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ mulrid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_mulr:
  $ wceq cmulr (cslot c3) $;

--| Define the involution function of a *-ring. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ starvid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_starv:
  $ wceq cstv (cslot c4) $;

--| Define scalar field component of a vector space ` v ` . (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ scaid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_sca:
  $ wceq csca (cslot c5) $;

--| Define scalar product. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ vscaid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_vsca:
  $ wceq cvsca (cslot c6) $;

--| Define Hermitian form (inner product). (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ ipid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ip:
  $ wceq cip (cslot c8) $;

--| Define the topology component of a topological space (structure). (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ tsetid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_tset:
  $ wceq cts (cslot c9) $;

--| Define "less than or equal to" ordering extractor for posets and related structures. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.) Use its index-independent form ~ pleid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ple:
  $ wceq cple (cslot (cdc c1 cc0)) $;

--| Define the orthocomplementation extractor for posets and related structures. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ ocid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ocomp:
  $ wceq coc (cslot (cdc c1 c1)) $;

--| Define the distance function component of a metric space (structure). (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ dsid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ds:
  $ wceq cds (cslot (cdc c1 c2)) $;

--| Define the uniform structure component of a uniform space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) Use its index-independent form ~ unifid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_unif:
  $ wceq cunif (cslot (cdc c1 c3)) $;

--| Define the hom-set component of a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) Use its index-independent form ~ homid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_hom:
  $ wceq chom (cslot (cdc c1 c4)) $;

--| Define the composition operation of a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) Use its index-independent form ~ ccoid instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_cco:
  $ wceq cco (cslot (cdc c1 c5)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-plusg slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem plusgndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cplusg) c2 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-plusg . (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.)
theorem plusgid:
  $ wceq cplusg (cslot (cfv cnx cplusg)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem plusgndxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cplusg) cn $;

--| The index of the slot for the base set is less than the index of the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxltplusgndx:
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cplusg) clt $;

--| The slot for the base set is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 14-Nov-2021.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| A constructed group is a structure. Version not depending on the implementation of the indices. (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2024.)
theorem grpstr (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wbr G (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cplusg)) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 27-Oct-2024.)
theorem grpbase (B2 c_pl G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $;

--| The operation of a constructed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 27-Oct-2024.)
theorem grpplusg (B2 c_pl G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl V) (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $;

--| ` +g ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressplusg (A2 c_pl G H V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_pl (cfv H cplusg)) $;

--| The base of an explicitly given group. Note: This theorem has hard-coded structure indices for demonstration purposes. It is not intended for general use; use ~ grpbase instead. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2012.)
theorem grpbasex (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel c_pl cvv $ >
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop c1 B2) (cop c2 c_pl)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $;

--| The operation of an explicitly given group. Note: This theorem has hard-coded structure indices for demonstration purposes. It is not intended for general use; use ~ grpplusg instead. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2012.)
theorem grpplusgx (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel c_pl cvv $ >
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop c1 B2) (cop c2 c_pl)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-mulr slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulrndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cmulr) c3 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-mulr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2013.)
theorem mulridx:
  $ wceq cmulr (cslot (cfv cnx cmulr)) $;

--| The slot for the base set is not the slot for the ring (multiplication) operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slot for the group (addition) operation is not the slot for the ring (multiplication) operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem plusgndxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cplusg) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| A constructed ring is a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem rngstr (B2 c_pl R c_x: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) $ >
  $ wbr R (cop c1 c3) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem rngbase (B2 c_pl R c_x V: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv R cbs)) $;

--| The additive operation of a constructed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem rngplusg (B2 c_pl R c_x V: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl V) (wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg)) $;

--| The multiplicative operation of a constructed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem rngmulr (B2 c_pl R c_x V: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x V) (wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-starv slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem starvndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cstv) c4 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-starv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem starvid:
  $ wceq cstv (cslot (cfv cnx cstv)) $;

--| The slot for the involution function is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ ressstarv . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem starvndxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the involution function is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ ressstarv . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem starvndxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the involution function is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ ressstarv . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem starvndxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| ` .r ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.)
theorem ressmulr (A2 R S c_x V: class):
  $ wceq S (co R A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_x (cfv S cmulr)) $;

--| ` *r ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2015.)
theorem ressstarv (A2 R S c_as V: class):
  $ wceq S (co R A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cstv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_as (cfv S cstv)) $;

--| A constructed star ring is a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem srngstr (B2 c_pl R c_x c_as: class):
  $ wceq R (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cstv) c_as))) $ >
  $ wbr R (cop c1 c4) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed star ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem srngbase (B2 c_pl R c_x c_as X: class):
  $ wceq R (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cstv) c_as))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wceq B2 (cfv R cbs)) $;

--| The addition operation of a constructed star ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem srngplusg (B2 c_pl R c_x c_as X: class):
  $ wceq R (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cstv) c_as))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl X) (wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg)) $;

--| The multiplication operation of a constructed star ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem srngmulr (B2 c_pl R c_x c_as X: class):
  $ wceq R (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cstv) c_as))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x X) (wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr)) $;

--| The involution function of a constructed star ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem srnginvl (B2 c_pl R c_x c_as X: class):
  $ wceq R (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cstv) c_as))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_as X) (wceq c_as (cfv R cstv)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-sca slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem scandx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx csca) c5 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of scalar ~ df-sca . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem scaid:
  $ wceq csca (cslot (cfv cnx csca)) $;

--| The slot for the scalar is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.)
theorem scandxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx csca) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the scalar field is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ mgpsca . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem scandxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx csca) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the scalar field is not the slot for the ring (multiplication) operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ mgpsca . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem scandxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx csca) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-vsca slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem vscandx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cvsca) c6 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of scalar product ~ df-vsca . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem vscaid:
  $ wceq cvsca (cslot (cfv cnx cvsca)) $;

--| The slot for the scalar product is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ rmodislmod . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem vscandxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cvsca) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the scalar product is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ rmodislmod . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem vscandxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cvsca) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the scalar product is not the slot for the ring (multiplication) operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ rmodislmod . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem vscandxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cvsca) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slot for the scalar product is not the slot for the scalar field in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ rmodislmod . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem vscandxnscandx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cvsca) (cfv cnx csca) $;

--| A constructed left module or left vector space is a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmodstr (B2 c_pl c_x F W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) F)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x))) $ >
  $ wbr W (cop c1 c6) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmodbase (B2 c_pl c_x F W X: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) F)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wceq B2 (cfv W cbs)) $;

--| The additive operation of a constructed left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmodplusg (B2 c_pl c_x F W X: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) F)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl X) (wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg)) $;

--| The set of scalars of a constructed left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmodsca (B2 c_pl c_x F W X: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) F)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F X) (wceq F (cfv W csca)) $;

--| The scalar product operation of a constructed left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmodvsca (B2 c_pl c_x F W X: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) F)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x X) (wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-ip slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ipndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cip) c8 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-ip . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem ipid:
  $ wceq cip (cslot (cfv cnx cip)) $;

--| The slot for the inner product is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.)
theorem ipndxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cip) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the inner product is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem ipndxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cip) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the inner product is not the slot for the ring (multiplication) operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ mgpsca . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem ipndxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cip) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slot for the scalar is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ srasca and ~ sravsca . (Contributed by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifipndx:
  $ wa (wne (cfv cnx cvsca) (cfv cnx cip)) (wne (cfv cnx csca) (cfv cnx cip)) $;

--| Lemma to shorten proofs of ~ ipsbase through ~ ipsvsca . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsstr (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wbr A2 (cop c1 c8) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsbase (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs)) $;

--| The additive operation of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsaddg (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl V) (wceq c_pl (cfv A2 cplusg)) $;

--| The multiplicative operation of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsmulr (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_xp V) (wceq c_xp (cfv A2 cmulr)) $;

--| The set of scalars of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipssca (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq S (cfv A2 csca)) $;

--| The scalar product operation of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsvsca (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x V) (wceq c_x (cfv A2 cvsca)) $;

--| The multiplicative operation of a constructed inner product space. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ipsip (A2 B2 c_pl S c_x c_xp I V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq I (cfv A2 cip)) $;

--| ` Scalar ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem resssca (A2 F G H V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq F (cfv H csca)) $;

--| ` .s ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem ressvsca (A2 c_x G H V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_x (cfv H cvsca)) $;

--| The inner product is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ressip (A2 G H c_xi V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv G cip) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_xi (cfv H cip)) $;

--| A constructed pre-Hilbert space is a structure. Starting from ~ lmodstr (which has 4 members), we chain ~ strleun once more, adding an ordered pair to the function, to get all 5 members. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlstr (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wbr H (cop c1 c8) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlbase (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi X: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wceq B2 (cfv H cbs)) $;

--| The additive operation of a constructed pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlplusg (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi X: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl X) (wceq c_pl (cfv H cplusg)) $;

--| The ring of scalars of a constructed pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlsca (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi X: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T X) (wceq T (cfv H csca)) $;

--| The scalar product operation of a constructed pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlvsca (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi X: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x X) (wceq c_x (cfv H cvsca)) $;

--| The inner product (Hermitian form) operation of a constructed pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem phlip (B2 c_pl T c_x H c_xi X: class):
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx csca) T)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_xi X) (wceq c_xi (cfv H cip)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-tset slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem tsetndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cts) c9 $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-tset . (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.)
theorem tsetid:
  $ wceq cts (cslot (cfv cnx cts)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tsetndxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cts) cn $;

--| The index of the slot for the base set is less than the index of the slot for the topology in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxlttsetndx:
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cts) clt $;

--| The slot for the topology is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tsetndxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the topology is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ oppgtset . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem tsetndxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the topology is not the slot for the ring multiplication operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tsetndxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slot for the topology is not the slot for the involution in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ cnfldfunALT . (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem tsetndxnstarvndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cstv) $;

--| The slots ` Scalar ` , ` .s ` and ` .i ` are different from the slot ` TopSet ` . Formerly part of ~ sralem and proofs using it. (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem slotstnscsi:
  $ w3a (wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx csca)) (wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cvsca)) (wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cip)) $;

--| A constructed topological group is a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem topgrpstr (B2 c_pl J W: class):
  $ wceq W (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wbr W (cop c1 c9) cstr $;

--| The base set of a constructed topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem topgrpbas (B2 c_pl J W X: class):
  $ wceq W (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wceq B2 (cfv W cbs)) $;

--| The additive operation of a constructed topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem topgrpplusg (B2 c_pl J W X: class):
  $ wceq W (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl X) (wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg)) $;

--| The topology of a constructed topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem topgrptset (B2 c_pl J W X: class):
  $ wceq W (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J X) (wceq J (cfv W cts)) $;

--| ` TopSet ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem resstset (A2 G H J V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G cts) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq J (cfv H cts)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-ple slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem plendx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cple) (cdc c1 cc0) $;

--| Utility theorem: self-referencing, index-independent form of ~ df-ple . (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2012.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem pleid:
  $ wceq cple (cslot (cfv cnx cple)) $;

--| The index value of the order slot is a positive integer. This property should be ensured for every concrete coding because otherwise it could not be used in an extensible structure (slots must be positive integers). (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2024.)
theorem plendxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cple) cn $;

--| The index value of the ` Base ` slot is less than the index value of the ` le ` slot. (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxltplendx:
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cple) clt $;

--| The slot for the order is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Oct-2024.)
theorem plendxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the "less than or equal to" ordering is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ oppgle . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem plendxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the "less than or equal to" ordering is not the slot for the ring multiplication operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ opsrmulr . (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem plendxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slot for the "less than or equal to" ordering is not the slot for the scalar in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ opsrsca . (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem plendxnscandx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx csca) $;

--| The slot for the "less than or equal to" ordering is not the slot for the scalar product in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ opsrvsca . (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem plendxnvscandx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cvsca) $;

--| The index of the slot for the distance is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ cnfldfunALT . (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifplendx:
  $ wa (wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cple)) (wne (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cnx cple)) $;

--| Functionality of a topological ordered space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem otpsstr (B2 J K c_le: class):
  $ wceq K (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le)) $ >
  $ wbr K (cop c1 (cdc c1 cc0)) cstr $;

--| The base set of a topological ordered space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem otpsbas (B2 J K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq K (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $;

--| The open sets of a topological ordered space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem otpstset (B2 J K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq K (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J V) (wceq J (cfv K cts)) $;

--| The order of a topological ordered space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem otpsle (B2 J K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq K (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_le V) (wceq c_le (cfv K cple)) $;

--| ` le ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2015.)
theorem ressle (A2 K c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (co K A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_le (cfv W cple)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-ocomp slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Oct-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ocndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx coc) (cdc c1 c1) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-ocomp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Oct-2015.)
theorem ocid:
  $ wceq coc (cslot (cfv cnx coc)) $;

--| The slot for the orthocomplementation is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ thlbas . (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem basendxnocndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx coc) $;

--| The slot for the orthocomplementation is not the slot for the order in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ thlle . (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem plendxnocndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx coc) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-ds slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem dsndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cds) (cdc c1 c2) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-ds . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem dsid:
  $ wceq cds (cslot (cfv cnx cds)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the distance in an extensible structure is a positive integer. Formerly part of proof for ~ tmslem . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem dsndxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cds) cn $;

--| The index of the slot for the base set is less than the index of the slot for the distance in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ tmslem . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxltdsndx:
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cds) clt $;

--| The slot for the distance is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem dsndxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the distance function is not the slot for the group operation in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ mgpds . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem dsndxnplusgndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cplusg) $;

--| The slot for the distance function is not the slot for the ring multiplication operation in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem dsndxnmulrndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cmulr) $;

--| The slots ` Scalar ` , ` .s ` and ` .i ` are different from the slot ` dist ` . Formerly part of ~ sralem and proofs using it. (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem slotsdnscsi:
  $ w3a (wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx csca)) (wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cvsca)) (wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cip)) $;

--| The slot for the distance function is not the slot for the topology in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ tngds . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem dsndxntsetndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cts) $;

--| The index of the slot for the distance is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ cnfldfunALT . (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifdsndx:
  $ wa (wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cds)) (wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cds)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-unif slot. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem unifndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cunif) (cdc c1 c3) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-unif . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem unifid:
  $ wceq cunif (cslot (cfv cnx cunif)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the uniform set in an extensible structure is a positive integer. Formerly part of proof for ~ tuslem . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem unifndxnn:
  $ wcel (cfv cnx cunif) cn $;

--| The index of the slot for the base set is less than the index of the slot for the uniform set in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ tuslem . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem basendxltunifndx:
  $ wbr (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cunif) clt $;

--| The slot for the uniform set is not the slot for the base set in an extensible structure. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2024.)
theorem unifndxnbasendx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cunif) (cfv cnx cbs) $;

--| The slot for the uniform set is not the slot for the topology in an extensible structure. Formerly part of proof for ~ tuslem . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem unifndxntsetndx:
  $ wne (cfv cnx cunif) (cfv cnx cts) $;

--| The index of the slot for the uniform set is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ cnfldfunALT . (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifunifndx:
  $ wa (w3a (wne (cfv cnx cplusg) (cfv cnx cunif)) (wne (cfv cnx cmulr) (cfv cnx cunif)) (wne (cfv cnx cstv) (cfv cnx cunif))) (wa (wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cunif)) (wne (cfv cnx cds) (cfv cnx cunif))) $;

--| ` UnifSet ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Dec-2017.)
theorem ressunif (A2 U G H V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv G cunif) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq U (cfv H cunif)) $;

--| Functionality of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem odrngstr (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wbr W (cop c1 (cdc c1 c2)) cstr $;

--| The base set of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngbas (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq B2 (cfv W cbs)) $;

--| The addition operation of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngplusg (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_pl V) (wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg)) $;

--| The multiplication operation of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngmulr (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_x V) (wceq c_x (cfv W cmulr)) $;

--| The open sets of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngtset (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J V) (wceq J (cfv W cts)) $;

--| The order of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngle (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_le V) (wceq c_le (cfv W cple)) $;

--| The metric of an ordered metric ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem odrngds (B2 D c_pl c_x J c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_x)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wceq D (cfv W cds)) $;

--| ` dist ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ressds (A2 D G H V: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq D (cfv H cds)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-hom slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem homndx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx chom) (cdc c1 c4) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-hom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.)
theorem homid:
  $ wceq chom (cslot (cfv cnx chom)) $;

--| Index value of the ~ df-cco slot. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ccondx:
  $ wceq (cfv cnx cco) (cdc c1 c5) $;

--| Utility theorem: index-independent form of ~ df-cco . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.)
theorem ccoid:
  $ wceq cco (cslot (cfv cnx cco)) $;

--| The slots ` Base ` , ` Hom ` and ` comp ` are different. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem slotsbhcdif:
  $ w3a (wne (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx chom)) (wne (cfv cnx cbs) (cfv cnx cco)) (wne (cfv cnx chom) (cfv cnx cco)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the "less than or equal to" ordering is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ prstcleval . (Contributed by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifplendx2:
  $ wa (wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx cco)) (wne (cfv cnx cple) (cfv cnx chom)) $;

--| The index of the slot for the orthocomplementation is not the index of other slots. Formerly part of proof for ~ prstcocval . (Contributed by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem slotsdifocndx:
  $ wa (wne (cfv cnx coc) (cfv cnx cco)) (wne (cfv cnx coc) (cfv cnx chom)) $;

--| ` Hom ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem resshom (A2 C D H V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq H (cfv D chom)) $;

--| ` comp ` is unaffected by restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem ressco (A2 C D c_x V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq c_x (cfv D cco)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the function returning a subspace topology.
term crest: class;

--| Extend class notation with the topology extractor function.
term ctopn: class;

--| Function returning the subspace topology induced by the topology ` y ` and the set ` x ` . (Contributed by FL, 20-Sep-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
axiom df_rest {x3 y1 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq crest (cmpo j x3 cvv cvv (crn (cmpt y1 (cv j) (cin (cv y1) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Define the topology extractor function. This differs from ~ df-tset when a structure has been restricted using ~ df-ress ; in this case the ` TopSet ` component will still have a topology over the larger set, and this function fixes this by restricting the topology as well. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_topn {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctopn (cmpt w cvv (co (cfv (cv w) cts) (cfv (cv w) cbs) crest)) $;

--| The subspace topology operator is a function on pairs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem restfn:
  $ wfn crest (cxp cvv cvv) $;

--| The topology extractor function is a function on the universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topnfn:
  $ wfn ctopn cvv $;

--| The subspace topology induced by the topology ` J ` on the set ` A ` . (Contributed by FL, 20-Sep-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem restval {x3: setvar} (A2 J: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel A2 W)) (wceq (co J A2 crest) (crn (cmpt x3 J (cin (cv x3) A2)))) $;

--| The predicate "is an open set of a subspace topology". (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem elrest {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 J: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J B2 crest)) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cin (cv x3) B2)) J)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for being an open set in a subspace. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem elrestr (A2 S J V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wcel S W) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel (cin A2 S) (co J S crest)) $;

--| Value of the structure restriction when the topology input is empty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem _0rest (A2: class):
  $ wceq (co c0 A2 crest) c0 $;

--| The subspace topology over a subset of the base set is the original topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem restid2 (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss J (cpw A2))) (wceq (co J A2 crest) J) $;

--| The subspace topology is a collection of subsets of the restriction set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem restsspw (A2 J: class):
  $ wss (co J A2 crest) (cpw A2) $;

--| The finite intersections operator commutes with restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem firest (A2 J: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (co J A2 crest) cfi) (co (cfv J cfi) A2 crest) $;

--| The subspace topology of the base set is the original topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem restid (J V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J V) (wceq (co J X crest) J) $;

--| Value of the topology extractor function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topnval (B2 J W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W cts) $ >
  $ wceq (co J B2 crest) (cfv W ctopn) $;

--| Value of the topology extractor function when the topology is defined over the same set as the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topnid (B2 J W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W cts) $ >
  $ wi (wss J (cpw B2)) (wceq J (cfv W ctopn)) $;

--| The topology extractor function depends only on the base and topology components. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.)
theorem topnpropd (ph: wff) (K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cts) (cfv L cts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $;

--| Extend class notation with a function that converts a basis to its corresponding topology.
term ctg: class;

--| Extend class notation with a function whose value is a product topology.
term cpt: class;

--| Extend class notation with group identity element.
term c0g: class;

--| Extend class notation to include finitely supported group sums.
term cgsu: class;

--| Define group identity element. Remark: this definition is required here because the symbol ` 0g ` is already used in ~ df-gsum . The related theorems are provided later, see ~ grpidval . (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2011.)
axiom df_0g {x3 e g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq c0g (cmpt g2 cvv (cio e (wa (wcel (cv e) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cv x3))) (cfv (cv g2) cbs))))) $;

--| Define a finite group sum (also called "iterated sum") of a structure. Given ` G gsum F ` where ` F : A --> ( Base `` G ) ` , the set of indices is ` A ` and the values are given by ` F ` at each index. A group sum over a multiplicative group may be viewed as a product. The definition is meaningful in different contexts, depending on the size of the index set ` A ` and each demanding different properties of ` G ` . 1. If ` A = (/) ` and ` G ` has an identity element, then the sum equals this identity. See ~ gsum0 . 2. If ` A = ( M ... N ) ` and ` G ` is any magma, then the sum is the sum of the elements, evaluated left-to-right, i.e., ` ( ( F `` 1 ) + ( F `` 2 ) ) + ( F `` 3 ) ` , etc. See ~ gsumval2 and ~ gsumnunsn . 3. If ` A ` is a finite set (or is nonzero for finitely many indices) and ` G ` is a commutative monoid, then the sum adds up these elements in some order, which is then uniquely defined. See ~ gsumval3 . 4. If ` A ` is an infinite set and ` G ` is a Hausdorff topological group, then there is a meaningful sum, but ` gsum ` cannot handle this case. See ~ df-tsms . Remark: this definition is required here because the symbol ` gsum ` is already used in ~ df-prds and ~ df-imas . The related theorems are provided later, see ~ gsumvalx . (Contributed by FL, 5-Sep-2010.) (Revised by FL, 17-Oct-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_gsum {x3 y1 w f2 g2 m n o: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgsu (cmpo w f2 cvv cvv (csb o (crab x3 (wral y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cplusg)) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cplusg)) (cv y1))) (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cif (wss (crn (cv f2)) (cv o)) (cfv (cv w) c0g) (cif (wcel (cdm (cv f2)) (crn cfz)) (cio x3 (wex m (wrex n (wa (wceq (cdm (cv f2)) (co (cv m) (cv n) cfz)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cseq (cfv (cv w) cplusg) (cv f2) (cv m))))) (cfv (cv m) cuz)))) (cio x3 (wex g2 (wsbc y1 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (cfv (cv y1) chash) cfz) (cv y1) (cv g2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) chash) (cseq (cfv (cv w) cplusg) (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2)) c1)))) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cdif cvv (cv o)))))))))) $;

--| Define a function that converts a basis to its corresponding topology. Equivalent to the definition of a topology generated by a basis in [Munkres] p. 78 (see ~ tgval2 ). The first use of this definition is ~ tgval but the token is used in ~ df-pt . See ~ tgval3 for an alternate expression for the value. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.)
axiom df_topgen {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctg (cmpt x3 cvv (cab y1 (wss (cv y1) (cuni (cin (cv x3) (cpw (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Define the product topology on a collection of topologies. For convenience, it is defined on arbitrary collections of sets, expressed as a function from some index set to the subbases of each factor space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_pt {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpt (cmpt f2 cvv (cfv (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) (cdm (cv f2))) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)))) (cdif (cdm (cv f2)) (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) ctg)) $;

--| The function constructing structure products.
term cprds: class;

--| The function constructing structure powers.
term cpws: class;

--| Define a structure product. This can be a product of groups, rings, modules, or ordered topological fields; any unused components will have garbage in them but this is usually not relevant for the purpose of inheriting the structures present in the factors. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
axiom df_prds {x3 v e f2 g2 h s1 r a c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprds (cmpo s1 r cvv cvv (csb v (cixp x3 (cdm (cv r)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cbs)) (csb h (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (cixp x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) chom)))) (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv v)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cplusg))))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cmulr)))))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) (cv s1)) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo f2 g2 (cfv (cv s1) cbs) (cv v) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cv f2) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cvsca))))) (cop (cfv cnx cip) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (co (cv s1) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cip))) cgsu))))) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (ccom ctopn (cv r)) cpt)) (cop (cfv cnx cple) (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cv v)) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cple)) (cdm (cv r)))))) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cds)))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt)))) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cv h)) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo a c (cxp (cv v) (cv v)) (cv v) (cmpo d e (co (cfv (cv a) c2nd) (cv c) (cv h)) (cfv (cv a) (cv h)) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv d)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv e)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) (cv c)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) cco))))))))))))) $;

--| The structure product is a well-behaved binary operator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem reldmprds:
  $ wrel (cdm cprds) $;

--| Define a structure power, which is just a structure product where all the factors are the same. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_pws {i r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpws (cmpo r i cvv cvv (co (cfv (cv r) csca) (cxp (cv i) (csn (cv r))) cprds)) $;

--| Lemma for structure products. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsbasex {x3: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cixp x3 (cdm R) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cbs)) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $;

--| An image structure value is a structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imasvalstr (B2 D c_pl S c_x c_xp U c_xi L O: class):
  $ wceq U (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) L) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D))) $ >
  $ wbr U (cop c1 (cdc c1 c2)) cstr $;

--| Structure product value is a structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem prdsvalstr (B2 D c_pl S c_xb c_x c_xp H c_xi L O: class):
  $ wbr (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) L) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_xb)))) (cop c1 (cdc c1 c5)) cstr $;

--| Lemma for ~ prdsbas and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2024.)
theorem prdsbaslem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D c_pl S c_xb T c_x c_xp U E H c_xi L O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) L) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_xb))))) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv U E) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wss (csn (cop (cfv cnx E) T)) (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) L) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_xb)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prdsval . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) Extracted from the former proof of ~ prdsval , dependency on ~ df-hom removed. (Revised by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem prdsvallem {x3 v f2 g2 r: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpo f2 g2 (cv v) (cv v) (cixp x3 (cdm (cv r)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) chom)))) cvv $;

--| Value of the structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsval {x3 e f2 g2 a c d: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3) (D P c_pl: class x3 e f2 g2 a c d) (R S: class) (c_xb c_x c_xp: class x3 e f2 g2 a c d) (H: class x3 f2 g2) (c_xi: class x3 e f2 g2 a c d) (I: class e f2 g2 a c d) (K c_le O W Z: class x3 e f2 g2 a c d):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cixp x3 I (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cplusg))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xp (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cmulr))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo f2 g2 K B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cv f2) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cvsca))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xi (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (co S (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cip))) cgsu))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (cfv (ccom ctopn R) cpt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_le (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) B2) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cple)) I)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cds)))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cixp x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) chom))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (cmpo a c (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo d e (co (cfv (cv a) c2nd) (cv c) H) (cfv (cv a) H) (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv d)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv e)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) (cv c)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cco))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xp)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) S) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_x) (cop (cfv cnx cip) c_xi))) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_xb))))) $;

--| Scalar ring of a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdssca (ph: wff) (P R S V W: class):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cfv P csca)) $;

--| Base set of a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsbas {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S I: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cixp x3 I (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cbs))) $;

--| Addition in a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsplusg {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P: class) (c_pl: class x3 f2 g2) (R S I: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv P cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cplusg))))) $;

--| Multiplication in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsmulr {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S: class) (c_x: class x3 f2 g2) (I: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cmulr))))) $;

--| Scalar multiplication in a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsvsca {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S: class) (c_x: class x3 f2 g2) (I: class) (K: class x3) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo f2 g2 K B2 (cmpt x3 I (co (cv f2) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cvsca))))) $;

--| Inner product in a structure product. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsip {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S: class) (c_xi: class x3 f2 g2) (I: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv P cip) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xi (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (co S (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cip))) cgsu))) $;

--| Structure product weak ordering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsle {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S I: class) (c_le V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv P cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_le (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) B2) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cple)) I)))) $;

--| Closure of the order relation on a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsless (ph: wff) (B2 P R S I c_le V W: class):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv P cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss c_le (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Structure product distance function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsds {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3 f2 g2) (P R S I: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv P cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cds)))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt))) $;

--| Structure product distance function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdsdsfn (ph: wff) (B2 D P R S I V W: class):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv P cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn D (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Structure product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdstset (ph: wff) (B2 P R S I O V W: class):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv P cts) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (cfv (ccom ctopn R) cpt)) $;

--| Structure product hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdshom {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P R S: class) (H: class x3 f2 g2) (I: class) (V W: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv P chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cixp x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) chom))))) $;

--| Structure product composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by Zhi Wang, 18-Aug-2024.)
theorem prdsco {x3 e a c d: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (P: class e a c d) (R S: class) (c_xb: class x3 e a c d) (H: class x3) (I: class) (V W: class x3 e a c d):
  $ wceq P (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm R) I) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv P chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (cmpo a c (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo d e (co (cfv (cv a) c2nd) (cv c) H) (cfv (cv a) H) (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv d)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv e)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv a) c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) (cv c)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cco))))))) $;

--| The base set of a structure product is an indexed set product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsbas2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cixp x3 I (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cbs))) $;

--| A constructed tuple is a point in a structure product iff each coordinate is in the proper base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsbasmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S: class) (U: class x3) (I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 I U) B2) (wral x3 (wcel U (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cbs)) I)) $;

--| Points in the structure product are functions; use this with ~ dffn5 to establish equalities. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsbasfn (ph: wff) (B2 R S T I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn T I) $;

--| Each point in a structure product restricts on each coordinate to the relevant base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsbasprj (ph: wff) (B2 R S T I J V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv J T) (cfv (cfv J R) cbs)) $;

--| Value of a componentwise sum in a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsplusgval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3) (R S F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G c_pl) (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cplusg)))) $;

--| Value of a structure product sum at a single coordinate. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsplusgfval (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R S F G I J V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co F G c_pl)) (co (cfv J F) (cfv J G) (cfv (cfv J R) cplusg))) $;

--| Value of a componentwise ring product in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsmulrval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S: class) (c_x: class x3) (F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G c_x) (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cmulr)))) $;

--| Value of a structure product's ring product at a single coordinate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsmulrfval (ph: wff) (B2 R S c_x F G I J V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co F G c_x)) (co (cfv J F) (cfv J G) (cfv (cfv J R) cmulr))) $;

--| Value of the product ordering in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsleval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S F G I: class) (c_le: class x3) (V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv Y cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G c_le) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cple)) I)) $;

--| Value of the metric in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsdsval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3) (R S F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G D) (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cds)))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Scalar multiplication in a structure product is pointwise. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsvscaval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S: class) (c_x: class x3) (F G I: class) (K: class x3) (V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G c_x) (cmpt x3 I (co F (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cvsca)))) $;

--| Scalar multiplication of a single coordinate in a structure product. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsvscafval (ph: wff) (B2 R S c_x F G I J K V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co F G c_x)) (co F (cfv J G) (cfv (cfv J R) cvsca))) $;

--| The base set of an indexed structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdsbas3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 R S: class x3) (I: class) (K V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel R X) I) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cixp x3 I K)) $;

--| A constructed tuple is a point in a structure product iff each coordinate is in the proper base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdsbasmpt2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 R S U: class x3) (I: class) (K V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel R X) I) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 I U) B2) (wral x3 (wcel U K) I)) $;

--| An element of the base has projections closed in the factors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsbascl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 R S: class x3) (F I: class) (K V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel R X) I) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) K) I) $;

--| Value of the metric in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsdsval2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 D R S E: class x3) (F G I: class) (V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel R X) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G D) (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) E))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Value of the metric in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsdsval3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 D R S E: class x3) (F G I: class) (K V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel R X) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp K K)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G D) (csup (cun (crn (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) E))) (csn cc0)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Value of a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsval (R F I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wceq Y (co F (cxp I (csn R)) cprds)) $;

--| Base set of a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsbas (B2 R I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wceq (co B2 I cmap) (cfv Y cbs)) $;

--| Membership in the base set of a structure power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwselbasb (B2 R I V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R W) (wcel I Z)) (wb (wcel X V) (wf I B2 X)) $;

--| An element of a structure power is a function from the index set to the base set of the structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2015.)
theorem pwselbas (ph: wff) (B2 R I V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I B2 X) $;

--| The reverse direction of ~ pwselbasb : a function between the index and base set of a structure is an element of the structure power. (Contributed by SN, 29-Jul-2024.)
theorem pwselbasr (ph: wff) (B2 R I V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $;

--| Value of addition in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsplusgval (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl c_pb R F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G c_pb) (co F G (cof c_pl))) $;

--| Value of multiplication in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsmulrval (ph: wff) (B2 R c_xb c_x F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G c_xb) (co F G (cof c_x))) $;

--| Ordering in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pwsle (B2 R I c_le O V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv Y cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wceq c_le (cin (cofr O) (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| Ordering in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pwsleval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R F G I: class) (c_le: class x3) (O V W: class) (Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv Y cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G c_le) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) O) I)) $;

--| Scalar multiplication in a structure power is pointwise. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsvscafval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R c_xb c_x F I K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 X c_xb) (co (cxp I (csn A2)) X (cof c_x))) $;

--| Scalar multiplication of a single coordinate in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsvscaval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R c_xb c_x F I J K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co A2 X c_xb)) (co A2 (cfv J X) c_x)) $;

--| The ring of scalars of a structure power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwssca (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wceq S (cfv Y csca)) $;

--| Membership of diagonal elements in the structure power base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsdiagel (A2 B2 C R I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wcel A2 B2)) (wcel (cxp I (csn A2)) C) $;

--| Triviality of singleton powers: set equipollence. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwssnf1o {x3: setvar} (B2 C R: class) (F: class x3) (I: class) (V: class x3) (W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R (csn I) cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cxp (csn I) (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I W)) (wf1o B2 C F) $;

--| Extend class notation with the order topology.
term cordt: class;

--| Extend class notation with the extended real number structure.
term cxrs: class;

--| Define the order topology, given an order ` <_ ` , written as ` r ` below. A closed subbasis for the order topology is given by the closed rays ` [ y , +oo ) = { z e. X | y <_ z } ` and ` ( -oo , y ] = { z e. X | z <_ y } ` , along with ` ( -oo , +oo ) = X ` itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_ordt {x3 y1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cordt (cmpt r cvv (cfv (cfv (cun (csn (cdm (cv r))) (crn (cun (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) (cdm (cv r)))) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv r)) (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r))) (cdm (cv r))))))) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| The extended real number structure. Unlike ~ df-cnfld , the extended real numbers do not have good algebraic properties, so this is not actually a group or anything higher, even though it has just as many operations as ~ df-cnfld . The main interest in this structure is in its ordering, which is complete and compact. The metric described here is an extension of the absolute value metric, but it is not itself a metric because ` +oo ` is infinitely far from all other points. The topology is based on the order and not the extended metric (which would make ` +oo ` an isolated point since there is nothing else in the ` 1 ` -ball around it). All components of this structure agree with ` CCfld ` when restricted to ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_xrs {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxrs (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cxr) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) cxad) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) cxmu)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv cle cordt)) (cop (cfv cnx cple) cle) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (cmpo x3 y1 cxr cxr (cif (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (co (cv y1) (cxne (cv x3)) cxad) (co (cv x3) (cxne (cv y1)) cxad)))))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the quotient topology function.
term cqtop: class;

--| Image structure function.
term cimas: class;

--| Quotient structure function.
term cqus: class;

--| Binary product structure function.
term cxps: class;

--| Define the quotient topology given a function ` f ` and topology ` j ` on the domain of ` f ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_qtop {f2 j s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqtop (cmpo j f2 cvv cvv (crab s1 (wcel (cin (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cv s1)) (cuni (cv j))) (cv j)) (cpw (cima (cv f2) (cuni (cv j)))))) $;

--| Define an image structure, which takes a structure and a function on the base set, and maps all the operations via the function. For this to work properly ` f ` must either be injective or satisfy the well-definedness condition ` f ( a ) = f ( c ) /\ f ( b ) = f ( d ) -> ` ` f ( a + b ) = f ( c + d ) ` for each relevant operation. Note that although we call this an "image" by association to ~ df-ima , in order to keep the definition simple we consider only the case when the domain of ` F ` is equal to the base set of ` R ` . Other cases can be achieved by restricting ` F ` (with ~ df-res ) and/or ` R ` ( with ~ df-ress ) to their common domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
axiom df_imas {x3 y1 v f2 g2 h i n r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cimas (cmpo f2 r cvv cvv (csb v (cfv (cv r) cbs) (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (crn (cv f2))) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ciun p (cv v) (ciun q (cv v) (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv q) (cv f2))) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) (cfv (cv r) cplusg)) (cv f2))))))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (ciun p (cv v) (ciun q (cv v) (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv q) (cv f2))) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) (cfv (cv r) cmulr)) (cv f2)))))))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) (cfv (cv r) csca)) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (ciun q (cv v) (cmpo p x3 (cfv (cfv (cv r) csca) cbs) (csn (cfv (cv q) (cv f2))) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) (cfv (cv r) cvsca)) (cv f2))))) (cop (cfv cnx cip) (ciun p (cv v) (ciun q (cv v) (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv q) (cv f2))) (co (cv p) (cv q) (cfv (cv r) cip))))))))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) (co (cfv (cv r) ctopn) (cv f2) cqtop)) (cop (cfv cnx cple) (ccom (ccom (cv f2) (cfv (cv r) cple)) (ccnv (cv f2)))) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (cmpo x3 y1 (crn (cv f2)) (crn (cv f2)) (cinf (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) (cv f2)) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) (cv f2))) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp (cv v) (cv v)) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) (co cxrs (ccom (cfv (cv r) cds) (cv g2)) cgsu)))) cxr clt))))))) $;

--| Define a quotient ring (or quotient group), which is a special case of an image structure ~ df-imas where the image function is ` x |-> [ x ] e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_qus {x3 e r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqus (cmpo r e cvv cvv (co (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv r) cbs) (cec (cv x3) (cv e))) (cv r) cimas)) $;

--| Define a binary product on structures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
axiom df_xps {x3 y1 s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxps (cmpo r s1 cvv cvv (co (ccnv (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv r) cbs) (cfv (cv s1) cbs) (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1))))) (co (cfv (cv r) csca) (cpr (cop c0 (cv r)) (cop c1o (cv s1))) cprds) cimas)) $;

--| Value of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasval {x3 y1 g2 h i n q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D c_pl c_pb: class x3 y1 g2 h i n q p) (R: class) (c_xb c_x c_xp c_xo U E: class x3 y1 g2 h i n q p) (F: class) (G c_xi I J K c_le N O: class x3 y1 g2 h i n q p) (V: class x3 y1 g2 i n) (Z: class x3 y1 g2 h i n q p):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv R cip) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_xp) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xo (ciun q V (cmpo p x3 K (csn (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_xi)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (co J F cqtop)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cinf (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) F) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cv y1)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) F)) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp V V) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) (co cxrs (ccom E (cv g2)) cgsu)))) cxr clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_le (ccom (ccom F N) (ccnv F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pb) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) c_xb)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx csca) G) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) c_xo) (cop (cfv cnx cip) I))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cts) O) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)))) $;

--| The base set of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasbas (ph: wff) (B2 R U F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv U cbs)) $;

--| The distance function of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasds {x3 y1 g2 h i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class g2 h i n) (D: class x3 y1 g2 h i n) (R: class) (U: class y1 g2 h i n) (E: class g2 h i n) (F: class) (V: class x3 y1 i n) (Z: class x3 y1 g2 h i n):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cinf (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) F) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cv y1)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) F)) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp V V) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) (co cxrs (ccom E (cv g2)) cgsu)))) cxr clt))) $;

--| The distance function is a function on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasdsfn (ph: wff) (B2 D R U E F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn D (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The distance function of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasdsval {g2 h i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class g2 h i n) (R: class) (S: class h i n) (U E: class g2 h i n) (F: class) (V: class i n) (X Y: class g2 i) (Z: class g2 h i n):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) F) X) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) F) Y) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) F)) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp V V) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y D) (cinf (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 S (co cxrs (ccom E (cv g2)) cgsu)))) cxr clt)) $;

--| The distance function of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasdsval2 {g2 h i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class g2 h i n) (R: class) (S: class h i n) (T U E: class g2 h i n) (F: class) (V: class i n) (X Y: class g2 i) (Z: class g2 h i n):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) F) X) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) F) Y) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) F)) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp V V) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cres E (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y D) (cinf (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 S (co cxrs (ccom T (cv g2)) cgsu)))) cxr clt)) $;

--| The group operation in an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imasplusg {q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl c_pb: class q p) (R: class) (U: class q p) (F V: class) (Z: class q p):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F)))))) $;

--| The ring multiplication in an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imasmulr {q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class q p) (R: class) (c_xb c_x U: class q p) (F V: class) (Z: class q p):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $;

--| The scalar field of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imassca (ph: wff) (B2 R U F G V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cfv U csca)) $;

--| The scalar multiplication operation of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imasvsca {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class q p) (R: class) (c_xb c_x: class x3 q p) (U: class q p) (F: class) (G: class x3 q p) (K: class q) (V: class x3) (Z: class x3 q p):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun q V (cmpo p x3 K (csn (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))) $;

--| The inner product of an image structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem imasip {q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class q p) (R: class) (U: class q p) (F: class) (c_xi I: class q p) (V: class) (Z: class q p):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv R cip) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv U cip) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_xi)))))) $;

--| The topology of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imastset (ph: wff) (B2 R U F J O V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv U cts) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| The ordering of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasle (ph: wff) (B2 R U F c_le N V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv U cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_le (ccom (ccom F N) (ccnv F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ f1ocpbl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem f1ocpbllem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o V X F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wb (wa (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv D F))) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| An injection is compatible with any operations on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem f1ocpbl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl F V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o V X F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv D F))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) F) (cfv (co C D c_pl) F))) $;

--| An injection is compatible with any operations on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1ovscpbl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C c_pl F K V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o V X F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wi (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) F) (cfv (co A2 C c_pl) F))) $;

--| An injection is compatible with any relations on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem f1olecpbl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F N V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o V X F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv D F))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 N) (wbr C D N))) $;

--| The image structure operation is a function if the original operation is compatible with the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasaddfnlem {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn c_xb (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The operation of an image structure is defined to distribute over the mapping function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasaddvallem {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (F V: class) (X: class q a b) (Y: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_xb) (cfv (co X Y c_x) F)) $;

--| The image set operations are closed if the original operation is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasaddflem {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_xb) $;

--| The image structure's group operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem imasaddfn {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn c_xb (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The value of an image structure's group operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasaddval {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (X: class q a b) (Y: class a b) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_xb) (cfv (co X Y c_x) F)) $;

--| The image structure's group operation is closed in the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasaddf {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_xb) $;

--| The image structure's ring multiplication is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmulfn {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn c_xb (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The value of an image structure's ring multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmulval {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (X: class q a b) (Y: class a b) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_xb) (cfv (co X Y c_x) F)) $;

--| The image structure's ring multiplication is closed in the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmulf {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (F V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_xb) $;

--| The image structure's scalar multiplication is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasvscafn {q p a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a) (U: class q p a) (F: class) (G: class q p a) (K V: class) (Z: class q p a):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv p) K) (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv p) (cv a) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn c_xb (cxp K B2)) $;

--| The value of an image structure's scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasvscaval {q p a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a) (U: class q p a) (F: class) (G: class q p a) (K V: class) (X: class q a) (Y: class a) (Z: class q p a):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv p) K) (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv p) (cv a) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X K) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co X (cfv Y F) c_xb) (cfv (co X Y c_x) F)) $;

--| The image structure's scalar multiplication is closed in the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasvscaf {q p a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R: class a) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a) (U: class q p a) (F: class) (G: class q p a) (K V: class) (Z: class q p a):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv p) K) (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv p) (cv a) c_x) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) K) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp K B2) B2 c_xb) $;

--| The order relation defined on an image set is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasless (ph: wff) (B2 R U F c_le V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv U cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss c_le (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The value of the image structure's ordering when the order is compatible with the mapping function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasleval {a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R U: class a b c d) (F: class) (c_le: class a b) (N V: class) (X: class a b) (Y: class a b c) (Z: class a b c d):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv U cple) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv c) V) (wcel (cv d) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv c) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv d) F))) (wb (wbr (cv a) (cv b) N) (wbr (cv c) (cv d) N))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wb (wbr (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_le) (wbr X Y N)) $;

--| Value of a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm R: class) (U F: class x3) (V: class) (W Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $;

--| The function in ~ qusval is a surjection onto a quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem quslem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm R: class) (U F: class x3) (V: class) (W Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V (cqs V c_sm) F) $;

--| Restrict the equivalence relation in a quotient structure to the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusin (ph: wff) (c_sm R U V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima c_sm V) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R (cin c_sm (cxp V V)) cqus)) $;

--| Base set of a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusbas (ph: wff) (c_sm R U V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cqs V c_sm) (cfv U cbs)) $;

--| The scalar field of a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem quss (ph: wff) (c_sm R U K V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv U csca)) $;

--| Value of the function in ~ qusval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2024.)
theorem divsfval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 c_sm: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cec A2 c_sm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ercpbl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2024.)
theorem ercpbllem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_sm: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) (wbr A2 B2 c_sm)) $;

--| Translate the function compatibility relation to a quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2024.)
theorem ercpbl {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class a) (C D: class a b) (c_pl: class) (c_sm: class a b) (F: class x3 a b) (V: class) (W: class x3 a b):
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V))) (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr A2 C c_sm) (wbr B2 D c_sm)) (wbr (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv D F))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) F) (cfv (co C D c_pl) F))) $;

--| Translate the relation compatibility relation to a quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2024.)
theorem erlecpbl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_sm: class) (F N: class x3) (V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr A2 C c_sm) (wbr B2 D c_sm)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 N) (wbr C D N))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv C F)) (wceq (cfv B2 F) (cfv D F))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 N) (wbr C D N))) $;

--| Value of an operation defined on a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusaddvallem {x3 q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class x3) (c_x: class a b) (U: class x3 q p a b) (F: class x3) (V: class) (X Y: class a b) (Z: class x3 q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cec X c_sm) (cec Y c_sm) c_xb) (cec (co X Y c_x) c_sm)) $;

--| The operation of a quotient structure is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusaddflem {x3 q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class x3) (c_x: class a b) (U: class x3 q p a b) (F: class x3) (V: class) (Z: class x3 q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (ciun p V (ciun q V (csn (cop (cop (cfv (cv p) F) (cfv (cv q) F)) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) F)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp (cqs V c_sm) (cqs V c_sm)) (cqs V c_sm) c_xb) $;

--| The addition in a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusaddval {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (V: class) (X Y: class a b) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cec X c_sm) (cec Y c_sm) c_xb) (cec (co X Y c_x) c_sm)) $;

--| The addition in a quotient structure as a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusaddf {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp (cqs V c_sm) (cqs V c_sm)) (cqs V c_sm) c_xb) $;

--| The multiplication in a quotient structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusmulval {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (V: class) (X Y: class a b) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cec X c_sm) (cec Y c_sm) c_xb) (cec (co X Y c_x) c_sm)) $;

--| The multiplication in a quotient structure as a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem qusmulf {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm: class) (R: class a b) (c_xb: class) (c_x: class a b) (U: class q p a b) (V: class) (Z: class q p a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv q) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv U cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp (cqs V c_sm) (cqs V c_sm)) (cqs V c_sm) c_xb) $;

--| Function with a domain of ` 2o ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
theorem fnpr2o (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wfn (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) c2o) $;

--| Biconditional version of ~ fnpr2o . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-Sep-2023.)
theorem fnpr2ob (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) (wfn (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) c2o) $;

--| The value of a function with a domain of (at most) two elements. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
theorem fvpr0o (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv c0 (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2))) A2) $;

--| The value of a function with a domain of (at most) two elements. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
theorem fvpr1o (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv c1o (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2))) B2) $;

--| The value of the pair function at an element of ` 2o ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fvprif (A2 B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel C c2o)) (wceq (cfv C (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2))) (cif (wceq C c0) A2 B2)) $;

--| Elementhood in the target space of the function ` F ` appearing in ~ xpsval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsfrnel {k: setvar} (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wb (wcel G (cixp k c2o (cif (wceq (cv k) c0) A2 B2))) (w3a (wfn G c2o) (wcel (cfv c0 G) A2) (wcel (cfv c1o G) B2)) $;

--| A function on ` 2o ` is determined by its values at zero and one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsfeq (G: class):
  $ wi (wfn G c2o) (wceq (cpr (cop c0 (cfv c0 G)) (cop c1o (cfv c1o G))) G) $;

--| Elementhood in the target space of the function ` F ` appearing in ~ xpsval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsfrnel2 {k: setvar} (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cpr (cop c0 X) (cop c1o Y)) (cixp k c2o (cif (wceq (cv k) c0) A2 B2))) (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) $;

--| Equivalent condition for the pair function to be a proper function on ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpscf (A2 X Y: class):
  $ wb (wf c2o A2 (cpr (cop c0 X) (cop c1o Y))) (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y A2)) $;

--| The value of the function appearing in ~ xpsval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsfval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y F) (cpr (cop c0 X) (cop c1o Y))) $;

--| The function appearing in ~ xpsval is a bijection from the cartesian product to the indexed cartesian product indexed on the pair ` 2o = { (/) , 1o } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsff1o {x3 y1 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp A2 B2) (cixp k c2o (cif (wceq (cv k) c0) A2 B2)) F $;

--| A short expression for the indexed cartesian product on two indices. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsfrn {x3 y1 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wceq (crn F) (cixp k c2o (cif (wceq (cv k) c0) A2 B2)) $;

--| The function appearing in ~ xpsval is a bijection from the cartesian product to the indexed cartesian product indexed on the pair ` 2o = { (/) , 1o } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem xpsff1o2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp A2 B2) (crn F) F $;

--| Value of the binary structure product function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
theorem xpsval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (R: class y1) (S T U F G V: class x3 y1) (W: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S)) cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (co (ccnv F) U cimas)) $;

--| The indexed structure product that appears in ~ xpsval has the same base as the target of the function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Jim Kingdon, 25-Sep-2023.)
theorem xpsrnbas {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (R: class y1) (S T U F G V: class x3 y1) (W: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S)) cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cfv U cbs)) $;

--| The base set of the binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsbas (ph: wff) (R S T V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxp X Y) (cfv T cbs)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xpsadd and ~ xpsmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsaddlem {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D: class) (R: class y1) (S: class x3 y1) (c_xb T: class x3 y1 k) (c_x c_xp: class) (U: class x3 y1) (E F V: class x3 y1 k) (W: class y1 k) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 C c_x) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co B2 D c_xp) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R E) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S E) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv T E) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv R csca) (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S)) cprds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) (crn F)) (wcel (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D)) (crn F))) (wceq (co (cfv (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) (ccnv F)) (cfv (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D)) (ccnv F)) c_xb) (cfv (co (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D)) (cfv U E)) (ccnv F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S)) c2o) (wcel (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) (cfv U cbs)) (wcel (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D)) (cfv U cbs))) (wceq (co (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2)) (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D)) (cfv U E)) (cmpt k c2o (co (cfv (cv k) (cpr (cop c0 A2) (cop c1o B2))) (cfv (cv k) (cpr (cop c0 C) (cop c1o D))) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S))) E)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_xb) (cop (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 D c_xp))) $;

--| Value of the addition operation in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsadd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S c_xb T c_x c_xp V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 C c_x) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co B2 D c_xp) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_xb) (cop (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 D c_xp))) $;

--| Value of the multiplication operation in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsmul (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S c_xb T c_x c_xp V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 C c_x) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co B2 D c_xp) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv T cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_xb) (cop (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 D c_xp))) $;

--| Value of the scalar field of a binary structure product. For concreteness, we choose the scalar field to match the left argument, but in most cases where this slot is meaningful both factors will have the same scalar field, so that it doesn't matter which factor is chosen. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpssca (ph: wff) (R S T G V W: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cfv T csca)) $;

--| Value of the scalar multiplication function in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsvsca (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R S c_xb T c_x c_xp G K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv T cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 c_x) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 C c_xp) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (cop B2 C) c_xb) (cop (co A2 B2 c_x) (co A2 C c_xp))) $;

--| Closure of the ordering in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsless (ph: wff) (R S T c_le V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv T cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss c_le (cxp (cxp X Y) (cxp X Y))) $;

--| Value of the ordering in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsle (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S T c_le M N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv T cple) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv S cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_le) (wa (wbr A2 C M) (wbr B2 D N))) $;

--| The class of Moore systems.
term cmre: class;

--| The class function generating Moore closures.
term cmrc: class;

--| ` mrInd ` is a class function which takes a Moore system to its set of independent sets.
term cmri: class;

--| The class of algebraic closure (Moore) systems.
term cacs: class;

--| Define a _Moore collection_, which is a family of subsets of a base set which preserve arbitrary intersection. Elements of a Moore collection are termed _closed_; Moore collections generalize the notion of closedness from topologies ( ~ cldmre ) and vector spaces ( ~ lssmre ) to the most general setting in which such concepts make sense. Definition of Moore collection of sets in [Schechter] p. 78. A Moore collection may also be called a _closure system_ (Section 0.6 in [Gratzer] p. 23.) The name _Moore collection_ is after Eliakim Hastings Moore, who discussed these systems in Part I of [Moore] p. 53 to 76. See ~ ismre , ~ mresspw , ~ mre1cl and ~ mreintcl for the major properties of a Moore collection. Note that a Moore collection uniquely determines its base set ( ~ mreuni ); as such the disjoint union of all Moore collections is sometimes considered as ` U. ran Moore ` , justified by ~ mreunirn . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.) (Revised by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
axiom df_mre {x3 s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmre (cmpt x3 cvv (crab c (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv c)) (wral s1 (wi (wne (cv s1) c0) (wcel (cint (cv s1)) (cv c))) (cpw (cv c)))) (cpw (cpw (cv x3))))) $;

--| Define the _Moore closure_ of a generating set, which is the smallest closed set containing all generating elements. Definition of Moore closure in [Schechter] p. 79. This generalizes topological closure ( ~ mrccls ) and linear span ( ~ mrclsp ). A Moore closure operation ` N ` is (1) extensive, i.e., ` x C_ ( N `` x ) ` for all subsets ` x ` of the base set ( ~ mrcssid ), (2) isotone, i.e., ` x C_ y ` implies that ` ( N `` x ) C_ ( N `` y ) ` for all subsets ` x ` and ` y ` of the base set ( ~ mrcss ), and (3) idempotent, i.e., ` ( N `` ( N `` x ) ) = ( N `` x ) ` for all subsets ` x ` of the base set ( ~ mrcidm .) Operators satisfying these three properties are in bijective correspondence with Moore collections, so these properties may be used to give an alternate characterization of a Moore collection by providing a closure operation ` N ` on the set of subsets of a given base set which satisfies (1), (2), and (3); the closed sets can be recovered as those sets which equal their closures (Section 4.5 in [Schechter] p. 82.) (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) (Revised by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
axiom df_mrc {x3 s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmrc (cmpt c (cuni (crn cmre)) (cmpt x3 (cpw (cuni (cv c))) (cint (crab s1 (wss (cv x3) (cv s1)) (cv c))))) $;

--| In a Moore system, a set is _independent_ if no element of the set is in the closure of the set with the element removed (Section 0.6 in [Gratzer] p. 27; Definition 4.1.1 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 83.) ` mrInd ` is a class function which takes a Moore system to its set of independent sets. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
axiom df_mri {x3 s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmri (cmpt c (cuni (crn cmre)) (crab s1 (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv x3))) (cfv (cv c) cmrc)))) (cv s1)) (cpw (cuni (cv c))))) $;

--| An important subclass of Moore systems are those which can be interpreted as closure under some collection of operators of finite arity (the collection itself is not required to be finite). These are termed _algebraic closure systems_; similar to definition (A) of an algebraic closure system in [Schechter] p. 84, but to avoid the complexity of an arbitrary mixed collection of functions of various arities (especially if the axiom of infinity ~ omex is to be avoided), we consider a single function defined on finite sets instead. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
axiom df_acs {x3 f2 s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cacs (cmpt x3 cvv (crab c (wex f2 (wa (wf (cpw (cv x3)) (cpw (cv x3)) (cv f2)) (wral s1 (wb (wcel (cv s1) (cv c)) (wss (cuni (cima (cv f2) (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn))) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv x3))))) (cfv (cv x3) cmre))) $;

--| Property of being a Moore collection on some base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ismre {s1: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (w3a (wss C (cpw X)) (wcel X C) (wral s1 (wi (wne (cv s1) c0) (wcel (cint (cv s1)) C)) (cpw C))) $;

--| The Moore collection generator is a well-behaved function. Analogue for Moore collections of ~ fntopon for topologies. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem fnmre:
  $ wfn cmre cvv $;

--| A Moore collection is a subset of the power of the base set; each closed subset of the system is actually a subset of the base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mresspw (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss C (cpw X)) $;

--| A Moore-closed subset is a subset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mress (C S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel S C)) (wss S X) $;

--| In any Moore collection the base set is closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mre1cl (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel X C) $;

--| A nonempty collection of closed sets has a closed intersection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mreintcl (C S X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss S C) (wne S c0)) (wcel (cint S) C) $;

--| A nonempty indexed intersection of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem mreiincl {y1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class y1) (I X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wne I c0) (wral y1 (wcel S C) I)) (wcel (ciin y1 I S) C) $;

--| The relative intersection of a set of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem mrerintcl (C S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss S C)) (wcel (cin X (cint S)) C) $;

--| The relative intersection of a family of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem mreriincl {y1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class y1) (I X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral y1 (wcel S C) I)) (wcel (cin X (ciin y1 I S)) C) $;

--| Two closed sets have a closed intersection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mreincl (A2 B2 C X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) C) $;

--| Since the entire base set of a Moore collection is the greatest element of it, the base set can be recovered from a Moore collection by set union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mreuni (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wceq (cuni C) X) $;

--| Two ways to express the notion of being a Moore collection on an unspecified base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mreunirn (C: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (cuni (crn cmre))) (wcel C (cfv (cuni C) cmre)) $;

--| Properties that determine a Moore collection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ismred {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss C (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv s1) C) (wne (cv s1) c0)) (wcel (cint (cv s1)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) $;

--| Properties that determine a Moore collection, using restricted intersection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem ismred2 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss C (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wss (cv s1) C)) (wcel (cin X (cint (cv s1))) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) $;

--| The Moore collections of subsets of a space, viewed as a kind of subset of the power set, form a Moore collection in their own right on the power set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem mremre (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cfv X cmre) (cfv (cpw X) cmre)) $;

--| The subcollection of a closed set system below a given closed set is itself a closed set system. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem submre (A2 C X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel A2 C)) (wcel (cin C (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 cmre)) $;

--| The ordering of the extended real number structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrsle:
  $ wceq cle (cfv cxrs cple) $;

--| The "less than or equal to" relation in the extended real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Mar-2018.)
theorem xrge0le:
  $ wceq cle (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) cple) $;

--| The base set of the extended real number structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrsbas:
  $ wceq cxr (cfv cxrs cbs) $;

--| The base of the extended nonnegative real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem xrge0base:
  $ wceq (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) cbs) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the function expression for Moore closures. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcflem {x3 s1: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wf (cpw X) C (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (cint (crab s1 (wss (cv x3) (cv s1)) C)))) $;

--| Moore-closure is a well-behaved function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem fnmrc:
  $ wfn cmrc (cuni (crn cmre)) $;

--| Value of the function expression for the Moore closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcfval {x3 s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (cint (crab s1 (wss (cv x3) (cv s1)) C)))) $;

--| The Moore closure is a function mapping arbitrary subsets to closed sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcf (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wf (cpw X) C F) $;

--| Evaluation of the Moore closure of a set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Fan Zheng, 6-Jun-2016.)
theorem mrcval {s1: setvar} (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X)) (wceq (cfv U F) (cint (crab s1 (wss U (cv s1)) C))) $;

--| The Moore closure of a set is a closed set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrccl (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X)) (wcel (cfv U F) C) $;

--| The Moore closure of a singleton is a closed set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcsncl (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel U X)) (wcel (cfv (csn U) F) C) $;

--| The closure of a closed set is itself. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcid (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel U C)) (wceq (cfv U F) U) $;

--| The closure of a set is a subset of the base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcssv (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss (cfv U F) X) $;

--| A set is closed iff it is equal to its closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcidb (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wb (wcel U C) (wceq (cfv U F) U)) $;

--| Closure preserves subset ordering. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcss (C U F V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U V) (wss V X)) (wss (cfv U F) (cfv V F)) $;

--| The closure of a set is a superset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcssid (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X)) (wss U (cfv U F)) $;

--| A set is closed iff it contains its closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem mrcidb2 (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X)) (wb (wcel U C) (wss (cfv U F) U)) $;

--| The closure operation is idempotent. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcidm (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv U F) F) (cfv U F)) $;

--| The closure is the minimal closed set; any closed set which contains the generators is a superset of the closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcsscl (C U F V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U V) (wcel V C)) (wss (cfv U F) V) $;

--| Idempotence of closure under a general union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcuni (C U F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U (cpw X))) (wceq (cfv (cuni U) F) (cfv (cuni (cima F U)) F)) $;

--| Idempotence of closure under a pair union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrcun (C U F V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U X) (wss V X)) (wceq (cfv (cun U V) F) (cfv (cun (cfv U F) (cfv V F)) F)) $;

--| The Moore closure of a set is a subset of the base. Deduction form of ~ mrcssv . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrcssvd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 N) X) $;

--| Moore closure preserves subset ordering. Deduction form of ~ mrcss . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrcssd (ph: wff) (A2 U N V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv U N) (cfv V N)) $;

--| A set is contained in its Moore closure. Deduction form of ~ mrcssid . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrcssidd (ph: wff) (A2 U N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (cfv U N)) $;

--| Moore closure is idempotent. Deduction form of ~ mrcidm . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrcidmd (ph: wff) (A2 U N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv U N) N) (cfv U N)) $;

--| In a Moore system, if a set is between another set and its closure, the two sets have the same closure. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mressmrcd (ph: wff) (A2 S T N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $;

--| In a closure system which is cut off above some level, closures below that level act as normal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem submrc (C D U F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cin C (cpw D)) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel D C) (wss U D)) (wceq (cfv U G) (cfv U F)) $;

--| In a Moore system, if ` Y ` is a member of ` S ` , ` ( S \ { Y } ) ` and ` S ` have the same closure if and only if ` Y ` is in the closure of ` ( S \ { Y } ) ` . Used in the proof of ~ mrieqvd and ~ mrieqv2d . Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrieqvlemd (ph: wff) (A2 S N X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel Y (cfv (cdif S (csn Y)) N)) (wceq (cfv (cdif S (csn Y)) N) (cfv S N))) $;

--| Value of the set of independent sets of a Moore system. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrisval {x3 s1: setvar} (A2: class) (I N: class x3 s1) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) (wceq I (crab s1 (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv x3))) N))) (cv s1)) (cpw X))) $;

--| Criterion for a set to be an independent set of a Moore system. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ismri {x3: setvar} (A2 S: class) (I N X: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) (wb (wcel S I) (wa (wss S X) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) N))) S))) $;

--| Criterion for a subset of the base set in a Moore system to be independent. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ismri2 {x3: setvar} (A2 S: class) (I N X: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S I) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) N))) S)) $;

--| Criterion for a subset of the base set in a Moore system to be independent. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ismri2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 S: class) (I N X: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S I) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) N))) S)) $;

--| Definition of independence of a subset of the base set in a Moore system. One-way deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ismri2dd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 S: class) (I N X: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) N))) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $;

--| An independent set of a Moore system is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mriss (A2 S I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) (wcel S I)) (wss S X) $;

--| An independent set of a Moore system is a subset of the base set. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrissd (ph: wff) (A2 S I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $;

--| Consequence of a set in a Moore system being independent. Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem ismri2dad (ph: wff) (A2 S I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel Y (cfv (cdif S (csn Y)) N))) $;

--| In a Moore system, a set is independent if and only if, for all elements of the set, the closure of the set with the element removed is unequal to the closure of the original set. Part of Proposition 4.1.3 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 83. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrieqvd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class) (I N X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S I) (wral x3 (wne (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) N) (cfv S N)) S)) $;

--| In a Moore system, a set is independent if and only if all its proper subsets have closure properly contained in the closure of the set. Part of Proposition 4.1.3 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 83. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrieqv2d {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class s1) (S I N: class) (X: class s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S I) (wal s1 (wi (wpss (cv s1) S) (wpss (cfv (cv s1) N) (cfv S N))))) $;

--| In a Moore system, if an independent set is between a set and its closure, the two sets are equal (since the two sets must have equal closures by ~ mressmrcd , and so are equal by ~ mrieqv2d .) (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrissmrcd (ph: wff) (A2 S T I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S T) $;

--| In a Moore system, subsets of independent sets are independent. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mrissmrid (ph: wff) (A2 S T I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T I) $;

--| In a Moore system, the closure operator is said to have the _exchange property_ if, for all elements ` y ` and ` z ` of the base set and subsets ` S ` of the base set such that ` z ` is in the closure of ` ( S u. { y } ) ` but not in the closure of ` S ` , ` y ` is in the closure of ` ( S u. { z } ) ` (Definition 3.1.9 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 57 to 58.) This theorem allows to construct substitution instances of this definition. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexd {y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S N: class) (V: class y1 z s1) (X: class z) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z (cfv (cun S (csn Y)) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel Z (cfv S N))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv (cun S (csn Z)) N)) $;

--| In a Moore system whose closure operator has the exchange property, if a set is independent and an element is not in its closure, then adding the element to the set gives another independent set. Lemma 4.1.5 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 84. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexmrid {y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z s1) (S: class) (I: class y1 z s1) (N: class) (X: class z) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel Y (cfv S N))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun S (csn Y)) I) $;

--| This lemma is used to generate substitution instances of the induction hypothesis in ~ mreexexd . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexexlemd {v u t i j: setvar} (ph: wff v u t i) (F G H: class v u t i) (I: class) (J: class v u t i j) (K N X: class i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel X J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cfv (cun G H) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun F H) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr F K cen) (wbr G K cen)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal t (wral u (wral v (wi (w3a (wo (wbr (cv u) K cen) (wbr (cv v) K cen)) (wss (cv u) (cfv (cun (cv v) (cv t)) N)) (wcel (cun (cv u) (cv t)) I)) (wrex i (wa (wbr (cv u) (cv i) cen) (wcel (cun (cv i) (cv t)) I)) (cpw (cv v)))) (cpw (cdif X (cv t)))) (cpw (cdif X (cv t))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wa (wbr F (cv j) cen) (wcel (cun (cv j) H) I)) (cpw G)) $;

--| Used in ~ mreexexlem4d to prove the induction step in ~ mreexexd . See the proof of Proposition 4.2.1 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 86 to 87. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexexlem2d {y1 z g2 s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z g2 s1) (F G H: class) (I: class y1 z g2 s1) (N: class) (X: class z g2) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cfv (cun G H) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun F H) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wa (wn (wcel (cv g2) (cdif F (csn Y)))) (wcel (cun (cdif F (csn Y)) (cun H (csn (cv g2)))) I)) G) $;

--| Base case of the induction in ~ mreexexd . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexexlem3d {y1 z i s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z i s1) (A2: class y1 z i s1) (F G H I: class y1 z s1) (N X: class y1 z i s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cfv (cun G H) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun F H) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq F c0) (wceq G c0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex i (wa (wbr F (cv i) cen) (wcel (cun (cv i) H) I)) (cpw G)) $;

--| Induction step of the induction in ~ mreexexd . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexexlem4d {y1 z f2 g2 h j s1: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2 h j) (A2: class y1 z f2 g2 h j s1) (F G H: class f2 g2 h) (I: class y1 z s1) (L: class y1 z j s1) (N: class j) (X: class z j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cfv (cun G H) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun F H) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal h (wral f2 (wral g2 (wi (w3a (wo (wbr (cv f2) L cen) (wbr (cv g2) L cen)) (wss (cv f2) (cfv (cun (cv g2) (cv h)) N)) (wcel (cun (cv f2) (cv h)) I)) (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv j) cen) (wcel (cun (cv j) (cv h)) I)) (cpw (cv g2)))) (cpw (cdif X (cv h)))) (cpw (cdif X (cv h))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wbr F (csuc L) cen) (wbr G (csuc L) cen)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wa (wbr F (cv j) cen) (wcel (cun (cv j) H) I)) (cpw G)) $;

--| Exchange-type theorem. In a Moore system whose closure operator has the exchange property, if ` F ` and ` G ` are disjoint from ` H ` , ` ( F u. H ) ` is independent, ` F ` is contained in the closure of ` ( G u. H ) ` , and either ` F ` or ` G ` is finite, then there is a subset ` q ` of ` G ` equinumerous to ` F ` such that ` ( q u. H ) ` is independent. This implies the case of Proposition 4.2.1 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 86 where either ` ( A \ B ) ` or ` ( B \ A ) ` is finite. The theorem is proven by induction using ~ mreexexlem3d for the base case and ~ mreexexlem4d for the induction step. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) Remove dependencies on ~ ax-rep and ~ ax-ac2 . (Revised by Brendan Leahy, 2-Jun-2021.)
theorem mreexexd {y1 z s1 q: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z s1 q) (F G H: class y1 z s1) (I: class) (N X: class q):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G (cdif X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cfv (cun G H) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun F H) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel F cfn) (wcel G cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex q (wa (wbr F (cv q) cen) (wcel (cun (cv q) H) I)) (cpw G)) $;

--| In a Moore system whose closure operator has the exchange property, if ` S ` is independent and contained in the closure of ` T ` , and either ` S ` or ` T ` is finite, then ` T ` dominates ` S ` . This is an immediate consequence of ~ mreexexd . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexdomd {y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S T: class y1 z s1) (I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel S cfn) (wcel T cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cdom) $;

--| In a Moore system whose closure operator has the exchange property, if two independent sets have equal closure and one is finite, then they are equinumerous. Proven by using ~ mreexdomd twice. This implies a special case of Theorem 4.2.2 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 87. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem mreexfidimd {y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S T: class y1 z s1) (I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cen) $;

--| A set is an algebraic closure system iff it is specified by some function of the finite subsets, such that a set is closed iff it does not expand under the operation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs {f2 s1: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wex f2 (wa (wf (cpw X) (cpw X) (cv f2)) (wral s1 (wb (wcel (cv s1) C) (wss (cuni (cima (cv f2) (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn))) (cv s1))) (cpw X))))) $;

--| Algebraic closure systems are closure systems. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsmre (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) $;

--| In the definition of an algebraic closure system, we may always take the operation being closed over as the Moore closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs2 {y1 s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wb (wcel (cv s1) C) (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) F) (cv s1)) (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn))) (cpw X))) $;

--| A set is closed in an algebraic closure system iff it contains all closures of finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfiel {y1: setvar} (C S F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wb (wcel S C) (wa (wss S X) (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) F) S) (cin (cpw S) cfn)))) $;

--| A set is closed in an algebraic closure system iff it contains all closures of finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfiel2 {y1: setvar} (C S F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S C) (wral y1 (wss (cfv (cv y1) F) S) (cin (cpw S) cfn))) $;

--| An algebraic closure system is also a Moore system. Deduction form of ~ acsmre . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsmred (ph: wff) (A2 X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cmre)) $;

--| A closure system determined by a function is a closure system and algebraic. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs1i {s1: setvar} (F: class) (V: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wf (cpw X) (cpw X) F)) (wcel (crab s1 (wss (cuni (cima F (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn))) (cv s1)) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Algebraicity is a composable property; combining several algebraic closure properties gives another. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem mreacs (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cfv X cacs) (cfv (cpw X) cmre)) $;

--| Algebraicity of a conditional point closure condition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfn {a: setvar} (T K V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X V) (wcel K X)) (wa (wss T X) (wcel T cfn))) (wcel (crab a (wi (wss T (cv a)) (wcel K (cv a))) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Algebraicity of a point closure condition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfn0 {a: setvar} (K V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel K X)) (wcel (crab a (wcel K (cv a)) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Algebraicity of a one-argument closure condition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfn1 {a b: setvar} (E: class b) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wral b (wcel E X) X)) (wcel (crab a (wral b (wcel E (cv a)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Algebraicity of a one-argument closure condition with additional constant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfn1c {a b c: setvar} (E: class b c) (K V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wral b (wral c (wcel E X) X) K)) (wcel (crab a (wral b (wral c (wcel E (cv a)) (cv a)) K) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Algebraicity of a two-argument closure condition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsfn2 {a b c: setvar} (E: class b c) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wral b (wral c (wcel E X) X) X)) (wcel (crab a (wral b (wral c (wcel E (cv a)) (cv a)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) (cfv X cacs)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of categories.
term ccat: class;

--| Extend class notation with the identity arrow of a category.
term ccid: class;

--| Extend class notation to include functionalized Hom-set extractor.
term chomf: class;

--| Extend class notation to include functionalized composition operation.
term ccomf: class;

--| A category is an abstraction of a structure (a group, a topology, an order...) Category theory consists in finding new formulation of the concepts associated with those structures (product, substructure...) using morphisms instead of the belonging relation. That trick has the interesting property that heterogeneous structures like topologies or groups for instance become comparable. Definition in [Lang] p. 53, without the axiom CAT 1, i.e., pairwise disjointness of hom-sets ( ~ cat1 ). See ~ setc2obas and ~ setc2ohom for a counterexample. In contrast to definition 3.1 of [Adamek] p. 21, where "A category is a quadruple A = (O, hom, id, o)", a category is defined as an extensible structure consisting of three slots: the objects "O" ( ` ( Base `` c ) ` ), the morphisms "hom" ( ` ( Hom `` c ) ` ) and the composition law "o" ( ` ( comp `` c ) ` ). The identities "id" are defined by their properties related to morphisms and their composition, see condition 3.1(b) in [Adamek] p. 21 and ~ df-cid . (Note: in category theory morphisms are also called arrows.) (Contributed by FL, 24-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_cat {x3 y1 z w f2 g2 h k o b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccat (cab c (wsbc b (wsbc h (wsbc o (wral x3 (wa (wrex g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) (cv o))) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv h))) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) (cv o))) (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h)))) (cv b)) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv h))) (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wa (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cv o))) (co (cv x3) (cv z) (cv h))) (wral w (wral k (wceq (co (co (cv k) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv w) (cv o))) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv w) (cv o))) (co (cv k) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cv o))) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) (cv w) (cv o)))) (co (cv z) (cv w) (cv h))) (cv b))) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv h))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h))) (cv b)) (cv b))) (cv b)) (cfv (cv c) cco)) (cfv (cv c) chom)) (cfv (cv c) cbs))) $;

--| Define the category identity arrow. Since it is uniquely defined when it exists, we do not need to add it to the data of the category, and instead extract it by uniqueness. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_cid {x3 y1 f2 g2 h o b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccid (cmpt c ccat (csb b (cfv (cv c) cbs) (csb h (cfv (cv c) chom) (csb o (cfv (cv c) cco) (cmpt x3 (cv b) (crio g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) (cv o))) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv h))) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) (cv o))) (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h)))) (cv b)) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv h)))))))) $;

--| Define the functionalized Hom-set operator, which is exactly like ` Hom ` but is guaranteed to be a function on the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_homf {x3 y1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq chomf (cmpt c cvv (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cfv (cv c) cbs) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv c) chom)))) $;

--| Define the functionalized composition operator, which is exactly like ` comp ` but is guaranteed to be a function of the proper type. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_comf {x3 y1 f2 g2 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccomf (cmpt c cvv (cmpo x3 y1 (cxp (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cv y1) (cfv (cv c) chom)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) chom)) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv c) cco)))))) $;

--| The predicate "is a category". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem iscat {x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k: setvar} (B2 C c_x H: class) (V: class x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel C ccat) (wral x3 (wa (wrex g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) H)) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H))) B2) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) H)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wa (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) H)) (wral w (wral k (wceq (co (co (cv k) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x)) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv w) c_x)) (co (cv k) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x))) (co (cv z) (cv w) H)) B2)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2)) B2)) $;

--| Properties that determine a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem iscatd {x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (c_x: class x3 y1 z w f2 k) (c_1: class x3 z w k) (H: class x3 y1 z) (V: class x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cfv C chom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel c_1 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) H)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) H)))) (wceq (co c_1 (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) c_x)) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)))) (wceq (co (cv f2) c_1 (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)))) (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) H)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv z) B2) (wcel (cv w) B2))) (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)) (wcel (cv k) (co (cv z) (cv w) H)))) (wceq (co (co (cv k) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x)) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv w) c_x)) (co (cv k) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $;

--| Each object in a category has an associated identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catidex {y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff y1 f2) (B2 C c_x H X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) X) X c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) X H)) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop X X) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co X (cv y1) H))) B2) (co X X H)) $;

--| Each object in a category has a unique identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catideu {y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff y1 f2) (B2 C c_x H X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) X) X c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) X H)) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop X X) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co X (cv y1) H))) B2) (co X X H)) $;

--| Each object in a category has an associated identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cidfval {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x: class) (c_1: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_1 (cmpt x3 B2 (crio g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) H)) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H))) B2) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) H)))) $;

--| Each object in a category has an associated identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem cidval {y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x: class) (c_1: class y1 f2 g2) (H X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) (crio g2 (wral y1 (wa (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) X) X c_x)) (cv f2)) (co (cv y1) X H)) (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop X X) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) (co X (cv y1) H))) B2) (co X X H))) $;

--| The identity arrow construction is a function on categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2017.)
theorem cidffn:
  $ wfn ccid ccat $;

--| The identity arrow operator is a function from objects to arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem cidfn (B2 C c_1: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wfn c_1 B2) $;

--| Deduce the identity arrow in a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catidd {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class y1 f2) (C: class) (c_x: class x3 y1 f2) (c_1: class x3) (H: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cfv C chom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel c_1 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) H)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) H)))) (wceq (co c_1 (cv f2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)) (cv x3) c_x)) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)))) (wceq (co (cv f2) c_1 (co (cop (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt x3 B2 c_1)) $;

--| Version of ~ iscatd with a uniform assumption list, for increased proof sharing capabilities. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem iscatd2 {x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k) (B2: class) (C: class x3 f2) (c_x: class) (c_1: class y1) (H: class) (V: class x3 y1 z w f2 g2 k):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cfv C chom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wb ps (w3a (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv z) B2) (wcel (cv w) B2)) (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)) (wcel (cv k) (co (cv z) (cv w) H)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel c_1 (co (cv y1) (cv y1) H)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co c_1 (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv y1) c_x)) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co (cv g2) c_1 (co (cop (cv y1) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (cv g2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) H)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co (co (cv k) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x)) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv w) c_x)) (co (cv k) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) (cv w) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt y1 B2 c_1))) $;

--| Each object in a category has an associated identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catidcl (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 H X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X c_1) (co X X H)) $;

--| Left identity property of an identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catlid (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x c_1 F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv Y c_1) F (co (cop X Y) Y c_x)) F) $;

--| Right identity property of an identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catrid (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x c_1 F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (cfv X c_1) (co (cop X X) Y c_x)) F) $;

--| Closure of a composition arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcocl (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co X Z H)) $;

--| Associativity of composition in a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem catass (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G H K W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co Z W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co K G (co (cop Y Z) W c_x)) F (co (cop X Y) W c_x)) (co K (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co (cop X Z) W c_x))) $;

--| Composition of non-empty hom-sets is non-empty. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 18-Sep-2024.)
theorem catcone0 (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co X Y H) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co Y Z H) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co X Z H) c0) $;

--| Any structure with an empty set of objects is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem _0catg (C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (wceq c0 (cfv C cbs))) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The empty set is a category, the _empty category_, see example 3.3(4.c) in [Adamek] p. 24. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem _0cat:
  $ wcel c0 ccat $;

--| Value of the functionalized Hom-set operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Mar-2024.)
theorem homffval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1) (H: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) $;

--| If the Hom-set operation is a function it is equal to the corresponding functionalized Hom-set operation. (Contributed by AV, 1-Mar-2020.)
theorem fnhomeqhomf (B2 C F H: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi (wfn H (cxp B2 B2)) (wceq F H) $;

--| Value of the functionalized Hom-set operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem homfval (ph: wff) (B2 C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y F) (co X Y H)) $;

--| The functionalized Hom-set operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem homffn (B2 C F: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wfn F (cxp B2 B2) $;

--| Condition for two categories with the same base to have the same hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem homfeq {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C D H J: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv D cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J)) B2) B2)) $;

--| If two structures have the same ` Hom ` slot, they have the same Hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem homfeqd (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C cbs) (cfv D cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chom) (cfv D chom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $;

--| Deduce equality of base sets from equality of Hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem homfeqbas (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C cbs) (cfv D cbs)) $;

--| Value of the functionalized Hom-set operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem homfeqval (ph: wff) (B2 C D H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (co X Y J)) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Mar-2024.)
theorem comfffval {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (B2: class f2 g2) (C: class) (c_x H: class y1) (O: class x3 y1 f2 g2):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cv y1) H) (cfv (cv x3) H) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_x)))) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comffval {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x H: class) (O: class f2 g2) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop X Y) Z O) (cmpo g2 f2 (co Y Z H) (co X Y H) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)))) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfval (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G H O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z O)) (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x))) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfffval2 {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (B2 C: class) (c_x: class y1) (H O: class x3 y1 f2 g2):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpo x3 y1 (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cv y1) H) (cfv (cv x3) H) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_x)))) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comffval2 {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x: class) (H O: class f2 g2) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop X Y) Z O) (cmpo g2 f2 (co Y Z H) (co X Y H) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)))) $;

--| Value of the functionalized composition operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfval2 (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G H O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z O)) (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x))) $;

--| The functionalized composition operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfffn (B2 C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wfn O (cxp (cxp B2 B2) B2) $;

--| The functionalized composition operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comffn (ph: wff) (B2 C H O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C ccomf) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (co (cop X Y) Z O) (cxp (co Y Z H) (co X Y H))) $;

--| Condition for two categories with the same hom-sets to have the same composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfeq {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (c_xb c_x H: class z):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv D cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_xb))) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| Condition for two categories with the same hom-sets to have the same composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfeqd (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C cco) (cfv D cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $;

--| Equality of two compositions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem comfeqval (ph: wff) (B2 C D c_xb c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_xb))) $;

--| Two structures with the same base, hom-sets and composition operation are either both categories or neither. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem catpropd (ph: wff) (C D V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C ccat) (wcel D ccat)) $;

--| Two structures with the same base, hom-sets and composition operation have the same identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2017.)
theorem cidpropd (ph: wff) (C D V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cfv D ccid)) $;

--| The opposite category operation.
term coppc: class;

--| Define an opposite category, which is the same as the original category but with the direction of arrows the other way around. Definition 3.5 of [Adamek] p. 25. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_oppc {z u f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq coppc (cmpt f2 cvv (co (co (cv f2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (ctpos (cfv (cv f2) chom))) csts) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo u z (cxp (cfv (cv f2) cbs) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) (cfv (cv f2) cbs) (ctpos (co (cop (cv z) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv f2) cco))))) csts)) $;

--| Value of the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcval {z u: setvar} (B2: class z u) (C: class) (c_x H O V: class z u):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wceq O (co (co C (cop (cfv cnx chom) (ctpos H)) csts) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo u z (cxp B2 B2) B2 (ctpos (co (cop (cv z) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) (cfv (cv u) c1st) c_x)))) csts)) $;

--| Hom-sets of the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem oppchomfval (C H O: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq (ctpos H) (cfv O chom) $;

--| Hom-sets of the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppchom (C H O X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq (co X Y (cfv O chom)) (co Y X H) $;

--| Composition in the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppccofval (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop X Y) Z (cfv O cco)) (ctpos (co (cop Z Y) X c_x))) $;

--| Composition in the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcco (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z (cfv O cco))) (co F G (co (cop Z Y) X c_x))) $;

--| Base set of an opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem oppcbas (B2 C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv O cbs) $;

--| Lemma for ~ oppccat . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppccatid (C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wa (wcel O ccat) (wceq (cfv O ccid) (cfv C ccid))) $;

--| Hom-sets of the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppchomf (C H O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq (ctpos H) (cfv O chomf) $;

--| Identity function of an opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcid (B2 C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv O ccid) B2) $;

--| An opposite category is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppccat (C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wcel O ccat) $;

--| The double opposite category has the same objects as the original category. Intended for use with property lemmas such as ~ monpropd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem _2oppcbas (B2 C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv (cfv O coppc) cbs) $;

--| The double opposite category has the same morphisms as the original category. Intended for use with property lemmas such as ~ monpropd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem _2oppchomf (C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv (cfv O coppc) chomf) $;

--| The double opposite category has the same composition as the original category. Intended for use with property lemmas such as ~ monpropd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem _2oppccomf (C O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv (cfv O coppc) ccomf) $;

--| If two categories have the same hom-sets, so do their opposites. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppchomfpropd (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv C coppc) chomf) (cfv (cfv D coppc) chomf)) $;

--| If two categories have the same hom-sets and composition, so do their opposites. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppccomfpropd (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv C coppc) ccomf) (cfv (cfv D coppc) ccomf)) $;

--| ` oppCat ` restricted to ` Cat ` is a function from ` Cat ` to ` Cat ` . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem oppccatf:
  $ wf ccat ccat (cres coppc ccat) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all monomorphisms.
term cmon: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all epimorphisms.
term cepi: class;

--| Function returning the monomorphisms of the category ` c ` . JFM CAT_1 def. 10. (Contributed by FL, 5-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_mon {x3 y1 z f2 g2 h b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmon (cmpt c ccat (csb b (cfv (cv c) cbs) (csb h (cfv (cv c) chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv b) (cv b) (crab f2 (wral z (wfun (ccnv (cmpt g2 (co (cv z) (cv x3) (cv h)) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv y1) (cfv (cv c) cco)))))) (cv b)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h))))))) $;

--| Function returning the epimorphisms of the category ` c ` . JFM CAT_1 def. 11. (Contributed by FL, 8-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_epi {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cepi (cmpt c ccat (ctpos (cfv (cfv (cv c) coppc) cmon))) $;

--| Definition of a monomorphism in a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem monfval {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x H: class) (M: class x3 y1 z g2):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (crab f2 (wral z (wfun (ccnv (cmpt g2 (co (cv z) (cv x3) H) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cop (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv y1) c_x))))) B2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)))) $;

--| Definition of a monomorphism in a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem ismon {z g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F H: class) (M: class z g2) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y M)) (wa (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wral z (wfun (ccnv (cmpt g2 (co (cv z) X H) (co F (cv g2) (co (cop (cv z) X) Y c_x))))) B2))) $;

--| Write out the monomorphism property directly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem ismon2 {z g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F H: class) (M: class z g2 h) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y M)) (wa (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wral z (wral g2 (wral h (wi (wceq (co F (cv g2) (co (cop (cv z) X) Y c_x)) (co F (cv h) (co (cop (cv z) X) Y c_x))) (wceq (cv g2) (cv h))) (co (cv z) X H)) (co (cv z) X H)) B2))) $;

--| A monomorphism is a morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem monhom (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x H M X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y M) (co X Y H)) $;

--| Property of a monomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem moni (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G H K M X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Z X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co Z X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co F G (co (cop Z X) Y c_x)) (co F K (co (cop Z X) Y c_x))) (wceq G K)) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same monomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem monpropd (ph: wff) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C cmon) (cfv D cmon)) $;

--| A monomorphism in the opposite category is an epimorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcmon (ph: wff) (C E M O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv O cmon) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y M) (co Y X E)) $;

--| An epimorphism in the opposite category is a monomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcepi (ph: wff) (C E M O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv O cepi) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y E) (co Y X M)) $;

--| Definition of an epimorphism in a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem isepi {z g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x: class) (E: class z g2) (F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y E)) (wa (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wral z (wfun (ccnv (cmpt g2 (co Y (cv z) H) (co (cv g2) F (co (cop X Y) (cv z) c_x))))) B2))) $;

--| Write out the epimorphism property directly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem isepi2 {z g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff h) (B2 C: class h) (c_x: class) (E: class z g2 h) (F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y E)) (wa (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wral z (wral g2 (wral h (wi (wceq (co (cv g2) F (co (cop X Y) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv h) F (co (cop X Y) (cv z) c_x))) (wceq (cv g2) (cv h))) (co Y (cv z) H)) (co Y (cv z) H)) B2))) $;

--| An epimorphism is a morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem epihom (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x E H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y E) (co X Y H)) $;

--| Property of an epimorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem epii (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x E F G H K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co K F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x))) (wceq G K)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the sections of a morphism.
term csect: class;

--| Extend class notation with the inverses of a morphism.
term cinv: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all isomorphisms.
term ciso: class;

--| Function returning the section relation in a category. Given arrows ` f : X --> Y ` and ` g : Y --> X ` , we say ` f Sect g ` , that is, ` f ` is a section of ` g ` , if ` g o. f = 1 `` X ` . If there there is an arrow ` g ` with ` f Sect g ` , the arrow ` f ` is called a _section_, see definition 7.19 of [Adamek] p. 106. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_sect {x3 y1 f2 g2 h c: setvar}:
  $ wceq csect (cmpt c ccat (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cfv (cv c) cbs) (copab f2 g2 (wsbc h (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h))) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv h)))) (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) cco))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) ccid)))) (cfv (cv c) chom))))) $;

--| The inverse relation in a category. Given arrows ` f : X --> Y ` and ` g : Y --> X ` , we say ` g Inv f ` , that is, ` g ` is an inverse of ` f ` , if ` g ` is a section of ` f ` and ` f ` is a section of ` g ` . Definition 3.8 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_inv {x3 y1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cinv (cmpt c ccat (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cin (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv c) csect)) (ccnv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) csect)))))) $;

--| Function returning the isomorphisms of the category ` c ` . Definition 3.8 of [Adamek] p. 28, and definition in [Lang] p. 54. (Contributed by FL, 9-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_iso {x3 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ciso (cmpt c ccat (ccom (cmpt x3 cvv (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv (cv c) cinv))) $;

--| Value of the section operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) Removed redundant hypotheses. (Revised by Zhi Wang, 27-Oct-2025.)
theorem sectffval {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2 g2) (C: class) (S: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (c_x c_1 H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) H))) (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv x3) c_x)) (cfv (cv x3) c_1)))))) $;

--| Value of the section relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectfval {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2 g2) (C: class) (S: class f2 g2) (c_x c_1 H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y S) (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co X Y H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co Y X H))) (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop X Y) X c_x)) (cfv X c_1))))) $;

--| The section relation is a relation between morphisms from ` X ` to ` Y ` and morphisms from ` Y ` to ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectss (ph: wff) (B2 C S c_x c_1 H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y S) (cxp (co X Y H) (co Y X H))) $;

--| The property " ` F ` is a section of ` G ` ". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem issect (ph: wff) (B2 C S c_x c_1 F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y S)) (w3a (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wcel G (co Y X H)) (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) X c_x)) (cfv X c_1)))) $;

--| Property of being a section. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem issect2 (ph: wff) (B2 C S c_x c_1 F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y X H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y S)) (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) X c_x)) (cfv X c_1))) $;

--| If ` G ` is a section of ` F ` and ` F ` is a section of ` H ` , then ` G = H ` . Proposition 3.10 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectcan (ph: wff) (B2 C S F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G F (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F H (co Y X S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G H) $;

--| Composition of two sections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectco (ph: wff) (B2 C S c_x F G H K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H K (co Y Z S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co H F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co G K (co (cop Z Y) X c_x)) (co X Z S)) $;

--| Function value of the function returning the isomorphisms of a category. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2017.)
theorem isofval {x3: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ciso) (ccom (cmpt x3 cvv (cdm (cv x3))) (cfv C cinv))) $;

--| Value of the inverse relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) Removed redundant hypotheses. (Revised by Zhi Wang, 27-Oct-2025.)
theorem invffval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C S: class) (N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cin (co (cv x3) (cv y1) S) (ccnv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) S))))) $;

--| Value of the inverse relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invfval (ph: wff) (B2 C S N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y N) (cin (co X Y S) (ccnv (co Y X S)))) $;

--| Value of the inverse relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem isinv (ph: wff) (B2 C S F G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y N)) (wa (wbr F G (co X Y S)) (wbr G F (co Y X S)))) $;

--| The inverse relation is a relation between morphisms ` F : X --> Y ` and their inverses ` G : Y --> X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invss (ph: wff) (B2 C H N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y N) (cxp (co X Y H) (co Y X H))) $;

--| The inverse relation is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invsym (ph: wff) (B2 C F G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y N)) (wbr G F (co Y X N))) $;

--| The inverse relation is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invsym2 (ph: wff) (B2 C N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccnv (co X Y N)) (co Y X N)) $;

--| The inverse relation is a function, which is to say that every morphism has at most one inverse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invfun (ph: wff) (B2 C N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (co X Y N)) $;

--| The isomorphisms are the domain of the inverse relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 21-May-2020.)
theorem isoval (ph: wff) (B2 C I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y I) (cdm (co X Y N))) $;

--| If ` G ` is an inverse to ` F ` , then ` F ` is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem inviso1 (ph: wff) (B2 C F G I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $;

--| If ` G ` is an inverse to ` F ` , then ` G ` is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem inviso2 (ph: wff) (B2 C F G I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y X I)) $;

--| The inverse relation is a function from isomorphisms to isomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invf (ph: wff) (B2 C I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co X Y I) (co Y X I) (co X Y N)) $;

--| The inverse relation is a bijection from isomorphisms to isomorphisms. This means that every isomorphism ` F e. ( X I Y ) ` has a unique inverse, denoted by ` ( ( Inv `` C ) `` F ) ` . Remark 3.12 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invf1o (ph: wff) (B2 C I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co X Y I) (co Y X I) (co X Y N)) $;

--| The inverse of the inverse of an isomorphism is itself. Proposition 3.14(1) of [Adamek] p. 29. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invinv (ph: wff) (B2 C F I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv F (co X Y N)) (co Y X N)) F) $;

--| The composition of two isomorphisms is an isomorphism, and the inverse is the composition of the individual inverses. Proposition 3.14(2) of [Adamek] p. 29. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem invco (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G I N X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co (cfv F (co X Y N)) (cfv G (co Y Z N)) (co (cop Z Y) X c_x)) (co X Z N)) $;

--| Alternate definition of an isomorphism of a category, according to definition 3.8 in [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2020.)
theorem dfiso2 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class g2) (C c_1 F H I c_as X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (co (cop X Y) X (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (co (cop Y X) Y (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y I)) (wrex g2 (wa (wceq (co (cv g2) F c_op) (cfv X c_1)) (wceq (co F (cv g2) c_as) (cfv Y c_1))) (co Y X H))) $;

--| Alternate definition of an isomorphism of a category as a section in both directions. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2020.)
theorem dfiso3 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class g2) (C: class) (S: class g2) (F H I X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y I)) (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (co Y X S)) (wbr F (cv g2) (co X Y S))) (co Y X H))) $;

--| If there are two inverses of a morphism, these inverses are equal. Corollary 3.11 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2020.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2022.)
theorem inveq (ph: wff) (B2 C F G K N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr F G (co X Y N)) (wbr F K (co X Y N))) (wceq G K)) $;

--| The function value of the function returning the isomorphisms of a category is a function over the Cartesian square of the base set of the category. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem isofn (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wfn (cfv C ciso) (cxp (cfv C cbs) (cfv C cbs))) $;

--| An isomorphism is a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isohom (ph: wff) (B2 C H I X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y I) (co X Y H)) $;

--| The composition of two isomorphisms is an isomorphism. Proposition 3.14(2) of [Adamek] p. 29. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem isoco (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x F G I X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co X Z I)) $;

--| A section in the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcsect (ph: wff) (B2 C S T F G O X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv O csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y T)) (wbr G F (co X Y S))) $;

--| A section in the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcsect2 (ph: wff) (B2 C S T O X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv O csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y T) (ccnv (co X Y S))) $;

--| An inverse in the opposite category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppcinv (ph: wff) (B2 C I J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv O cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y J) (co Y X I)) $;

--| An isomorphism in the opposite category. See also remark 3.9 in [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem oppciso (ph: wff) (B2 C I J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv O ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y J) (co Y X I)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a section of ` G ` , then ` F ` is a monomorphism. Proposition 7.35 of [Adamek] p. 110. A monomorphism that arises from a section is also known as a _split monomorphism_. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectmon (ph: wff) (B2 C S F G M X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y M)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a monomorphism and ` G ` is a section of ` F ` , then ` G ` is an inverse of ` F ` and they are both isomorphisms. This is also stated as "a monomorphism which is also a split epimorphism is an isomorphism". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem monsect (ph: wff) (B2 C S F G M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G F (co Y X S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y N)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a section of ` G ` , then ` G ` is an epimorphism. Proposition 7.42 of [Adamek] p. 112. An epimorphism that arises from a section is also known as a _split epimorphism_. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem sectepi (ph: wff) (B2 C S E F G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y X E)) $;

--| If ` F ` is an epimorphism and ` F ` is a section of ` G ` , then ` G ` is an inverse of ` F ` and they are both isomorphisms. This is also stated as "an epimorphism which is also a split monomorphism is an isomorphism". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem episect (ph: wff) (B2 C S E F G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co X Y N)) $;

--| The identity is a section of itself. (Contributed by AV, 8-Apr-2020.)
theorem sectid (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X I) (cfv X I) (co X X (cfv C csect))) $;

--| The inverse of the identity is the identity. (Contributed by AV, 8-Apr-2020.)
theorem invid (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X I) (cfv X I) (co X X (cfv C cinv))) $;

--| The identity is an isomorphism. Example 3.13 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 8-Apr-2020.)
theorem idiso (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X I) (co X X (cfv C ciso))) $;

--| The inverse of the identity is the identity. Example 3.13 of [Adamek] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2020.)
theorem idinv (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv X I) (co X X (cfv C cinv))) (cfv X I)) $;

--| The inverse of an isomorphism ` F ` (which is unique because of ~ invf and is therefore denoted by ` ( ( X N Y ) `` F ) ` , see also remark 3.12 in [Adamek] p. 28) is invers to the isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2020.)
theorem invisoinvl (ph: wff) (B2 C F I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F (co X Y N)) F (co Y X N)) $;

--| The inverse of an isomorphism is invers to the isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2020.)
theorem invisoinvr (ph: wff) (B2 C F I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cfv F (co X Y N)) (co X Y N)) $;

--| The inverse of an isomorphism composed with the isomorphism is the identity. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem invcoisoid (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 F I N X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (co (cop X Y) X (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv F (co X Y N)) F c_op) (cfv X c_1)) $;

--| The inverse of an isomorphism composed with the isomorphism is the identity. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2020.)
theorem isocoinvid (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 F I N X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (co (cop Y X) Y (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (cfv F (co X Y N)) c_op) (cfv Y c_1)) $;

--| Right cancellation of an inverse of an isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem rcaninv (ph: wff) (B2 C R F G H N X Y c_op Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co Y X (cfv C ciso))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z (cfv C chom))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co Y Z (cfv C chom))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv F (co Y X N)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (co (cop X Y) Z (cfv C cco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (co G R c_op) (co H R c_op)) (wceq G H)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category isomorphism relation.
term ccic: class;

--| Function returning the set of isomorphic objects for each category ` c ` . Definition 3.15 of [Adamek] p. 29. Analogous to the definition of the group isomorphism relation ` ~=g ` , see ~ df-gic . (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_cic {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccic (cmpt c ccat (co (cfv (cv c) ciso) c0 csupp)) $;

--| The set of isomorphic objects of the category ` c ` . (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem cicfval (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccic) (co (cfv C ciso) c0 csupp)) $;

--| The relation "is isomorphic to" for categories. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem brcic (ph: wff) (B2 C I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr X Y (cfv C ccic)) (wne (co X Y I) c0)) $;

--| Objects ` X ` and ` Y ` in a category are isomorphic provided that there is an isomorphism ` f : X --> Y ` , see definition 3.15 of [Adamek] p. 29. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem cic {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2 C: class f2) (I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr X Y (cfv C ccic)) (wex f2 (wcel (cv f2) (co X Y I)))) $;

--| Prove that two objects are isomorphic by an explicit isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem brcici (ph: wff) (B2 C F I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Isomorphism is reflexive. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cicref (C O: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wcel O (cfv C cbs))) (wbr O O (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Isomorphism implies the left side is an object. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem ciclcl (C R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wbr R S (cfv C ccic))) (wcel R (cfv C cbs)) $;

--| Isomorphism implies the right side is an object. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cicrcl (C R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wbr R S (cfv C ccic))) (wcel S (cfv C cbs)) $;

--| Isomorphism is symmetric. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cicsym (C R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wbr R S (cfv C ccic))) (wbr S R (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Isomorphism is transitive. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cictr (C R S T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C ccat) (wbr R S (cfv C ccic)) (wbr S T (cfv C ccic))) (wbr R T (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Isomorphism is an equivalence relation on objects of a category. Remark 3.16 in [Adamek] p. 29. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cicer (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wer (cfv C cbs) (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the subset relation for subcategories.
term cssc: class;

--| Extend class notation to include category restriction (which is like structure restriction but also allows limiting the collection of morphisms).
term cresc: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the collection of subcategories of a category.
term csubc: class;

--| Define the subset relation for subcategories. Despite the name, this is not really a "category-aware" definition, which is to say it makes no explicit references to homsets or composition; instead this is a subset-like relation on the functions that are used as subcategory specifications in ~ df-subc , which makes it play an analogous role to the subset relation applied to the subgroups of a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_ssc {x3 t h j s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cssc (copab h j (wex t (wa (wfn (cv j) (cxp (cv t) (cv t))) (wrex s1 (wcel (cv h) (cixp x3 (cxp (cv s1) (cv s1)) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) (cv j))))) (cpw (cv t)))))) $;

--| Define the restriction of a category to a given set of arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_resc {h c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cresc (cmpo c h cvv cvv (co (co (cv c) (cdm (cdm (cv h))) cress) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cv h)) csts)) $;

--| ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` is the set of all the subcategory specifications of the category ` C ` . Like ~ df-subg , this is not actually a collection of categories (as in definition 4.1(a) of [Adamek] p. 48), but only sets which when given operations from the base category (using ~ df-resc ) form a category. All the objects and all the morphisms of the subcategory belong to the supercategory. The identity of an object, the domain and the codomain of a morphism are the same in the subcategory and the supercategory. The composition of the subcategory is a restriction of the composition of the supercategory. (Contributed by FL, 17-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_subc {x3 y1 z f2 g2 h s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq csubc (cmpt c ccat (cab h (wa (wbr (cv h) (cfv (cv c) chomf) cssc) (wsbc s1 (wral x3 (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) ccid)) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv h))) (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cfv (cv c) cco))) (co (cv x3) (cv z) (cv h))) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv h))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cdm (cdm (cv h))))))) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscrel:
  $ wrel cssc $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem brssc {x3 t s1: setvar} (H J: class):
  $ wb (wbr H J cssc) (wex t (wa (wfn J (cxp (cv t) (cv t))) (wrex s1 (wcel H (cixp x3 (cxp (cv s1) (cv s1)) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) J)))) (cpw (cv t))))) $;

--| An analogue of ~ pwex for the subcategory subset relation: The collection of subcategory subsets of a given set ` J ` is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscpwex {h: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wcel (cab h (wbr (cv h) J cssc)) cvv $;

--| Reverse closure for the subcategory predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcrcl (C H: class):
  $ wi (wcel H (cfv C csubc)) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is defined on functions with square domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscfn1 (ph: wff) (S H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr H J cssc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cdm (cdm H))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is defined on functions with square domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscfn2 (ph: wff) (T H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr H J cssc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (cdm (cdm J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp T T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ssc1 and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem ssclem (ph: wff) (S H: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel H cvv) (wcel S cvv)) $;

--| Value of the subcategory subset relation when the arguments are known functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem isssc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (H J: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp T T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr H J cssc) (wa (wss S T) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J)) S) S))) $;

--| Infer subset relation on objects from the subcategory subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem ssc1 (ph: wff) (S T H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp T T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H J cssc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S T) $;

--| Infer subset relation on morphisms from the subcategory subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem ssc2 (ph: wff) (S H J X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H J cssc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y H) (co X Y J)) $;

--| Any function restricted to a square domain is a subcategory subset of the original. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscres (S T H V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn H (cxp S S)) (wcel S V)) (wbr (cres H (cxp T T)) H cssc) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is reflexive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem sscid (S H V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn H (cxp S S)) (wcel S V)) (wbr H H cssc) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is transitive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem ssctr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cssc) (wbr B2 C cssc)) (wbr A2 C cssc) $;

--| The subcategory subset relation is antisymmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem ssceq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cssc) (wbr B2 A2 cssc)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Value of the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem rescval (C D H V W: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (wcel H W)) (wceq D (co (co C (cdm (cdm H)) cress) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) csts)) $;

--| Value of the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem rescval2 (ph: wff) (C D S H V W: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co C S cress) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) csts)) $;

--| Base set of the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem rescbas (ph: wff) (B2 C D S H V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cfv D cbs)) $;

--| Hom-sets of the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem reschom (ph: wff) (B2 C D S H V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cfv D chom)) $;

--| Hom-sets of the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem reschomf (ph: wff) (B2 C D S H V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cfv D chomf)) $;

--| Composition in the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem rescco (ph: wff) (B2 C D S c_x H V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cfv D cco)) $;

--| Restriction absorption law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Nov-2024.)
theorem rescabs (ph: wff) (C S T H J V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp T T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C H cresc) J cresc) (co C J cresc)) $;

--| Restriction absorption law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem rescabs2 (ph: wff) (C S T J V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp T T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C S cress) J cresc) (co C J cresc)) $;

--| Elementhood in the set of subcategories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem issubc {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z f2 g2) (C S: class) (c_x c_1 H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2) (J: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cdm (cdm J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wa (wbr J H cssc) (wral x3 (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) J)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) J)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) J)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J)) S) S)) S))) $;

--| Elementhood in the set of subcategories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem issubc2 {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z f2 g2) (C S: class) (c_x c_1 H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2) (J: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wa (wbr J H cssc) (wral x3 (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) J)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wral g2 (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) J)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) J)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J)) S) S)) S))) $;

--| For any category ` C ` , the empty set is a subcategory subset of ` C ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2020.)
theorem _0ssc (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wbr c0 (cfv C chomf) cssc) $;

--| For any category ` C ` , the empty set is a (full) subcategory of ` C ` , see example 4.3(1.a) in [Adamek] p. 48. (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2020.)
theorem _0subcat (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wcel c0 (cfv C csubc)) $;

--| For any category ` C ` , ` C ` itself is a (full) subcategory of ` C ` , see example 4.3(1.b) in [Adamek] p. 48. (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2020.)
theorem catsubcat (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wcel (cfv C chomf) (cfv C csubc)) $;

--| An element in the set of subcategories is a subset of the category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcssc (ph: wff) (C H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr J H cssc) $;

--| An element in the set of subcategories is a binary function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcfn (ph: wff) (C S J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cdm (cdm J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $;

--| The objects of a subcategory are a subset of the objects of the original. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcss1 (ph: wff) (B2 C S J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $;

--| The morphisms of a subcategory are a subset of the morphisms of the original. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcss2 (ph: wff) (C S H J X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y J) (co X Y H)) $;

--| The identity of the original category is contained in each subcategory. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcidcl (ph: wff) (C S c_1 J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X c_1) (co X X J)) $;

--| A subcategory is closed under composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subccocl (ph: wff) (C S c_x F G J X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co X Z J)) $;

--| A subcategory is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subccatid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D S c_1 J: class):
  $ wceq D (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel D ccat) (wceq (cfv D ccid) (cmpt x3 S (cfv (cv x3) c_1)))) $;

--| The identity in a subcategory is the same as the original category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subcid (ph: wff) (C D S c_1 J X: class):
  $ wceq D (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) (cfv X (cfv D ccid))) $;

--| A subcategory is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem subccat (ph: wff) (C D J: class):
  $ wceq D (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $;

--| Alternate definition of a subcategory, as a subset of the category which is itself a category. The assumption that the identity be closed is necessary just as in the case of a monoid, ~ issubm2 , for the same reasons, since categories are a generalization of monoids. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem issubc3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D S: class) (c_1: class x3) (H J: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq D (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn J (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (w3a (wbr J H cssc) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) J)) S) (wcel D ccat))) $;

--| The full subcategory generated by a subset of objects is the category with these objects and the same morphisms as the original. The result is always a subcategory (and it is full, meaning that all morphisms of the original category between objects in the subcategory is also in the subcategory), see definition 4.1(2) of [Adamek] p. 48. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullsubc (ph: wff) (B2 C S H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres H (cxp S S)) (cfv C csubc)) $;

--| The category formed by structure restriction is the same as the category restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullresc (ph: wff) (B2 C D S E H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wceq D (co C S cress) $ >
  $ wceq E (co C (cres H (cxp S S)) cresc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cfv D chomf) (cfv E chomf)) (wceq (cfv D ccomf) (cfv E ccomf))) $;

--| A category restricted to a smaller set of objects is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resscat (C S V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wcel S V)) (wcel (co C S cress) ccat) $;

--| A subcategory of a subcategory is a subcategory. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem subsubc (C D H J: class):
  $ wceq D (co C H cresc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel H (cfv C csubc)) (wb (wcel J (cfv D csubc)) (wa (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wbr J H cssc))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all functors.
term cfunc: class;

--| Extend class notation with identity functor.
term cidfu: class;

--| Extend class notation with functor composition.
term ccofu: class;

--| Extend class notation to include restriction of a functor to a subcategory.
term cresf: class;

--| Function returning all the functors from a category ` t ` to a category ` u ` . Definition 3.17 of [Adamek] p. 29, and definition in [Lang] p. 62 ("covariant functor"). Intuitively a functor associates any morphism of ` t ` to a morphism of ` u ` , any object of ` t ` to an object of ` u ` , and respects the identity, the composition, the domain and the codomain. Here to capture the idea that a functor associates any object of ` t ` to an object of ` u ` we write it associates any identity of ` t ` to an identity of ` u ` which simplifies the definition. According to remark 3.19 in [Adamek] p. 30, "a functor F : A -> B is technically a family of functions; one from Ob(A) to Ob(B) [here: f, called "the object part" in the following], and for each pair (A,A') of A-objects, one from hom(A,A') to hom(FA, FA') [here: g, called "the morphism part" in the following]". (Contributed by FL, 10-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_func {x3 y1 z u t f2 g2 m n b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfunc (cmpo t u ccat ccat (copab f2 g2 (wsbc b (w3a (wf (cv b) (cfv (cv u) cbs) (cv f2)) (wcel (cv g2) (cixp z (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv z) c1st) (cv f2)) (cfv (cfv (cv z) c2nd) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv u) chom)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv t) chom)) cmap))) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) ccid)) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv g2))) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv u) ccid))) (wral y1 (wral z (wral m (wral n (wceq (cfv (co (cv n) (cv m) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cfv (cv t) cco))) (co (cv x3) (cv z) (cv g2))) (co (cfv (cv n) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv g2))) (cfv (cv m) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv g2))) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv u) cco)))) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv (cv t) chom))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) chom))) (cv b)) (cv b))) (cv b))) (cfv (cv t) cbs)))) $;

--| Define the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_idfu {z t b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cidfu (cmpt t ccat (csb b (cfv (cv t) cbs) (cop (cres cid (cv b)) (cmpt z (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cres cid (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv t) chom))))))) $;

--| Define the composition of two functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_cofu {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccofu (cmpo g2 f2 cvv cvv (cop (ccom (cfv (cv g2) c1st) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cmpo x3 y1 (cdm (cdm (cfv (cv f2) c2nd))) (cdm (cdm (cfv (cv f2) c2nd))) (ccom (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd)))))) $;

--| Define the restriction of a functor to a subcategory (analogue of ~ df-res ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_resf {x3 f2 h: setvar}:
  $ wceq cresf (cmpo f2 h cvv cvv (cop (cres (cfv (cv f2) c1st) (cdm (cdm (cv h)))) (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv h)) (cres (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv h)))))) $;

--| The set of functors is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem relfunc (D E: class):
  $ wrel (co D E cfunc) $;

--| Reverse closure for a functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcrcl (D E F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co D E cfunc)) (wa (wcel D ccat) (wcel E ccat)) $;

--| Value of the set of functors between two categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfunc {x3 y1 z m n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z m n) (D: class) (c_x c_1: class x3 y1 z m n) (E F G H: class) (I: class x3 y1 z m n) (J: class m n) (O: class x3 y1 z m n):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv E ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv E cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) (w3a (wf B2 C F) (wcel G (cixp z (cxp B2 B2) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv z) c1st) F) (cfv (cfv (cv z) c2nd) F) J) (cfv (cv z) H) cmap))) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) G)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (wral y1 (wral z (wral m (wral n (wceq (cfv (co (cv n) (cv m) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) G)) (co (cfv (cv n) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cfv (cv m) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (cfv (cv z) F) O))) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2)) B2))) $;

--| Deduce that an operation is a functor of categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfuncd {x3 y1 z m n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z m n) (D: class) (c_x c_1: class x3 y1 z m n) (E F G H: class) (I: class x3 y1 z m n) (J: class m n) (O: class x3 y1 z m n):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv E ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv E cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wf (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) J) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) G)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wa (wcel (cv m) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv n) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv n) (cv m) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) c_x)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) G)) (co (cfv (cv n) (co (cv y1) (cv z) G)) (cfv (cv m) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (cfv (cv z) F) O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $;

--| The object part of a functor is a function on objects. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcf1 (ph: wff) (B2 C D E F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C F) $;

--| The morphism part of a functor is a function on homsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcixp {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D E F G H J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cixp z (cxp B2 B2) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv z) c1st) F) (cfv (cfv (cv z) c2nd) F) J) (cfv (cv z) H) cmap))) $;

--| The morphism part of a functor is a function on homsets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcf2 (ph: wff) (B2 D E F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co X Y H) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J) (co X Y G)) $;

--| The morphism part of a functor is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcfn2 (ph: wff) (B2 D E F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| A functor maps each identity to the corresponding identity in the target category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcid (ph: wff) (B2 D c_1 E F G I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv E ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv X c_1) (co X X G)) (cfv (cfv X F) I)) $;

--| A functor maps composition in the source category to composition in the target. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcco (ph: wff) (B2 D c_x E F G H M N O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv E cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co N M (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co X Z G)) (co (cfv N (co Y Z G)) (cfv M (co X Y G)) (co (cop (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)) (cfv Z F) O))) $;

--| The image of a section under a functor is a section. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcsect (ph: wff) (B2 D S T E F G M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv D csect) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv E csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N (co X Y S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M (co X Y G)) (cfv N (co Y X G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) T)) $;

--| The image of an inverse under a functor is an inverse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcinv (ph: wff) (B2 D E F G I J M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D cinv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M (co X Y G)) (cfv N (co Y X G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J)) $;

--| The image of an isomorphism under a functor is an isomorphism. Proposition 3.21 of [Adamek] p. 32. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem funciso (ph: wff) (B2 D E F G I J M X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D ciso) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv E ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co X Y I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv M (co X Y G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J)) $;

--| A functor on categories yields a functor on the opposite categories (in the same direction), see definition 3.41 of [Adamek] p. 39. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcoppc (ph: wff) (C D P F G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv D coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (ctpos G) (co O P cfunc)) $;

--| Value of the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfuval {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C H: class) (I: class z):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (cop (cres cid B2) (cmpt z (cxp B2 B2) (cres cid (cfv (cv z) H))))) $;

--| Value of the morphism part of the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfu2nd (ph: wff) (B2 C H I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y (cfv I c2nd)) (cres cid (co X Y H))) $;

--| Value of the morphism part of the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfu2 (ph: wff) (B2 C F H I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F (co X Y (cfv I c2nd))) F) $;

--| Value of the object part of the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfu1st (ph: wff) (B2 C I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv I c1st) (cres cid B2)) $;

--| Value of the object part of the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfu1 (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv I c1st)) X) $;

--| The identity functor is a functor. Example 3.20(1) of [Adamek] p. 30. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem idfucl (C I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wcel I (co C C cfunc)) $;

--| Value of the composition of two functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofuval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C D E: class x3 y1) (F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ccofu) (cop (ccom (cfv G c1st) (cfv F c1st)) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (ccom (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv G c2nd)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv F c2nd)))))) $;

--| Value of the object part of the functor composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofu1st (ph: wff) (B2 C D E F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co G F ccofu) c1st) (ccom (cfv G c1st) (cfv F c1st))) $;

--| Value of the object part of the functor composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofu1 (ph: wff) (B2 C D E F G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv (co G F ccofu) c1st)) (cfv (cfv X (cfv F c1st)) (cfv G c1st))) $;

--| Value of the morphism part of the functor composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofu2nd (ph: wff) (B2 C D E F G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y (cfv (co G F ccofu) c2nd)) (ccom (co (cfv X (cfv F c1st)) (cfv Y (cfv F c1st)) (cfv G c2nd)) (co X Y (cfv F c2nd)))) $;

--| Value of the morphism part of the functor composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofu2 (ph: wff) (B2 C D R E F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R (co X Y (cfv (co G F ccofu) c2nd))) (cfv (cfv R (co X Y (cfv F c2nd))) (co (cfv X (cfv F c1st)) (cfv Y (cfv F c1st)) (cfv G c2nd)))) $;

--| Value of the composition of two functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofuval2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C D E: class x3 y1) (F G H K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H K (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop H K) (cop F G) ccofu) (cop (ccom H F) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (ccom (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) K) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G))))) $;

--| The composition of two functors is a functor. Proposition 3.23 of [Adamek] p. 33. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofucl (ph: wff) (C D E F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F ccofu) (co C E cfunc)) $;

--| Functor composition is associative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofuass (ph: wff) (C D E F G H K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co D E cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co E F cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co K H ccofu) G ccofu) (co K (co H G ccofu) ccofu)) $;

--| The identity functor is a left identity for composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofulid (ph: wff) (C D F I: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D cidfu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I F ccofu) F) $;

--| The identity functor is a right identity for composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofurid (ph: wff) (C D F I: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F I ccofu) F) $;

--| Value of the functor restriction operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resfval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F H: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F H cresf) (cop (cres (cfv F c1st) (cdm (cdm H))) (cmpt x3 (cdm H) (cres (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c2nd)) (cfv (cv x3) H))))) $;

--| Value of the functor restriction operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resfval2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (S: class) (F: class y1) (G H: class) (V W X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop F G) H cresf) (cop (cres F S) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cres (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H))))) $;

--| Value of the functor restriction operator on objects. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resf1st (ph: wff) (S F H V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F H cresf) c1st) (cres (cfv F c1st) S)) $;

--| Value of the functor restriction operator on morphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resf2nd (ph: wff) (S F H V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y (cfv (co F H cresf) c2nd)) (cres (co X Y (cfv F c2nd)) (co X Y H))) $;

--| A functor restricted to a subcategory is a functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcres (ph: wff) (C D F H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F H cresf) (co (co C H cresc) D cfunc)) $;

--| Condition for a functor to also be a functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcres2b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C D R: class) (S: class x3 y1) (F G H: class) (Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cfv D csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wf Y (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wbr F G (co C (co D R cresc) cfunc))) $;

--| A functor into a restricted category is also a functor into the whole category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcres2 (C D R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R (cfv D csubc)) (wss (co C (co D R cresc) cfunc) (co C D cfunc)) $;

--| The identity functor for a subcategory is an "inclusion functor" from the subcategory into its supercategory. (Contributed by AV, 29-Mar-2020.)
theorem idfusubc0 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C S: class) (I: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wceq S (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wceq I (cop (cres cid B2) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv S chom)))))) $;

--| The identity functor for a subcategory is an "inclusion functor" from the subcategory into its supercategory. (Contributed by AV, 29-Mar-2020.)
theorem idfusubc {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C S: class) (I: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wceq S (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cidfu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wceq I (cop (cres cid B2) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J))))) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the functor set operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem wunfunc (ph: wff) (C D U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C D cfunc) U) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcpropd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 chomf) (cfv B2 chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccomf) (cfv B2 ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cfunc) (co B2 D cfunc)) $;

--| Condition for a functor to also be a functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcres2c (ph: wff) (A2 C D S E F G V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (co D S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wbr F G (co C E cfunc))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all full functors.
term cful: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all faithful functors.
term cfth: class;

--| Function returning all the full functors from a category ` C ` to a category ` D ` . A full functor is a functor in which all the morphism maps ` G ( X , Y ) ` between objects ` X , Y e. C ` are surjections. Definition 3.27(3) in [Adamek] p. 34. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_full {x3 y1 f2 g2 c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cful (cmpo c d ccat ccat (copab f2 g2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cv c) (cv d) cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (crn (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv g2))) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv d) chom))) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cfv (cv c) cbs))))) $;

--| Function returning all the faithful functors from a category ` C ` to a category ` D ` . A faithful functor is a functor in which all the morphism maps ` G ( X , Y ) ` between objects ` X , Y e. C ` are injections. Definition 3.27(2) in [Adamek] p. 34. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_fth {x3 y1 f2 g2 c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfth (cmpo c d ccat ccat (copab f2 g2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cv c) (cv d) cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wfun (ccnv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv g2)))) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cfv (cv c) cbs))))) $;

--| A full functor is a functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullfunc (C D: class):
  $ wss (co C D cful) (co C D cfunc) $;

--| A faithful functor is a functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthfunc (C D: class):
  $ wss (co C D cfth) (co C D cfunc) $;

--| The set of full functors is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem relfull (C D: class):
  $ wrel (co C D cful) $;

--| The set of faithful functors is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem relfth (C D: class):
  $ wrel (co C D cfth) $;

--| Value of the set of full functors between two categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfull {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C D F G J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wb (wbr F G (co C D cful)) (wa (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (crn (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) J)) B2) B2)) $;

--| Equivalent condition for a full functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfull2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C D F G H J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wb (wbr F G (co C D cful)) (wa (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wfo (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) J) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) B2) B2)) $;

--| The morphism map of a full functor is a surjection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullfo (ph: wff) (B2 C D F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cful)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo (co X Y H) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J) (co X Y G)) $;

--| The morphism map of a full functor is a surjection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fulli {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2 C D R F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cful)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wceq R (cfv (cv f2) (co X Y G))) (co X Y H)) $;

--| Value of the set of faithful functors between two categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfth {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) (wa (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wfun (ccnv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G))) B2) B2)) $;

--| Equivalent condition for a faithful functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isfth2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C D F G H J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wb (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) (wa (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wf1 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) J) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) B2) B2)) $;

--| A fully faithful functor is a functor which is bijective on hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem isffth2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C D F G H J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wb (wbr F G (cin (co C D cful) (co C D cfth))) (wa (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wf1o (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) J) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) B2) B2)) $;

--| The morphism map of a faithful functor is an injection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthf1 (ph: wff) (B2 C D F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co X Y H) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J) (co X Y G)) $;

--| The morphism map of a faithful functor is an injection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthi (ph: wff) (B2 C D R S F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv R (co X Y G)) (cfv S (co X Y G))) (wceq R S)) $;

--| The morphism map of a fully faithful functor is a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem ffthf1o (ph: wff) (B2 C D F G H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cin (co C D cful) (co C D cfth))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co X Y H) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J) (co X Y G)) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same full functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullpropd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 chomf) (cfv B2 chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccomf) (cfv B2 ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cful) (co B2 D cful)) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same faithful functors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthpropd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 chomf) (cfv B2 chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccomf) (cfv B2 ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cfth) (co B2 D cfth)) $;

--| The opposite functor of a full functor is also full. Proposition 3.43(d) in [Adamek] p. 39. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fulloppc (ph: wff) (C D P F G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv D coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cful)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (ctpos G) (co O P cful)) $;

--| The opposite functor of a faithful functor is also faithful. Proposition 3.43(c) in [Adamek] p. 39. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthoppc (ph: wff) (C D P F G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv D coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (ctpos G) (co O P cfth)) $;

--| The opposite functor of a fully faithful functor is also full and faithful. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem ffthoppc (ph: wff) (C D P F G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv D coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cin (co C D cful) (co C D cfth))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (ctpos G) (cin (co O P cful) (co O P cfth))) $;

--| A faithful functor reflects sections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthsect (ph: wff) (B2 C D S T F G H M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co Y X H)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr M N (co X Y S)) (wbr (cfv M (co X Y G)) (cfv N (co Y X G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) T))) $;

--| A faithful functor reflects inverses. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthinv (ph: wff) (B2 C D F G H I J M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co Y X H)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr M N (co X Y I)) (wbr (cfv M (co X Y G)) (cfv N (co Y X G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J))) $;

--| A faithful functor reflects monomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthmon (ph: wff) (B2 C D R F G H M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv R (co X Y G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y M)) $;

--| A faithful functor reflects epimorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthepi (ph: wff) (B2 C D P R E F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv D cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv R (co X Y G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y E)) $;

--| A fully faithful functor reflects isomorphisms. Corollary 3.32 of [Adamek] p. 35. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem ffthiso (ph: wff) (B2 C D R F G H I J X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cful)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel R (co X Y I)) (wcel (cfv R (co X Y G)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) J))) $;

--| Condition for a faithful functor to also be a faithful functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthres2b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C D R: class) (S: class x3 y1) (F G H: class) (Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cfv D csubc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wf Y (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) R) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) (wbr F G (co C (co D R cresc) cfth))) $;

--| Condition for a faithful functor to also be a faithful functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthres2c (ph: wff) (A2 C D S E F G V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (co D S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co C D cfth)) (wbr F G (co C E cfth))) $;

--| A faithful functor into a restricted category is also a faithful functor into the whole category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem fthres2 (C D R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R (cfv D csubc)) (wss (co C (co D R cresc) cfth) (co C D cfth)) $;

--| The identity functor is a fully faithful functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem idffth (C I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cidfu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wcel I (cin (co C C cful) (co C C cfth))) $;

--| The composition of two full functors is full. Proposition 3.30(d) in [Adamek] p. 35. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofull (ph: wff) (C D E F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cful)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cful)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F ccofu) (co C E cful)) $;

--| The composition of two faithful functors is faithful. Proposition 3.30(c) in [Adamek] p. 35. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem cofth (ph: wff) (C D E F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D E cfth)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F ccofu) (co C E cfth)) $;

--| The composition of two fully faithful functors is fully faithful. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem coffth (ph: wff) (C D E F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cin (co C D cful) (co C D cfth))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cin (co D E cful) (co D E cfth))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F ccofu) (cin (co C E cful) (co C E cfth))) $;

--| The inclusion functor from a subcategory is a faithful functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem rescfth (C D I J: class):
  $ wceq D (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D cidfu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) (wcel I (co D C cfth)) $;

--| The inclusion functor from a full subcategory is a full and faithful functor, see also remark 4.4(2) in [Adamek] p. 49. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem ressffth (C D S I V: class):
  $ wceq D (co C S cress) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D cidfu) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wcel S V)) (wcel I (cin (co D C cful) (co D C cfth))) $;

--| Condition for a full functor to also be a full functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem fullres2c (ph: wff) (A2 C D S E F G V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (co D S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co C D cful)) (wbr F G (co C E cful))) $;

--| Condition for a fully faithful functor to also be a fully faithful functor into the restriction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem ffthres2c (ph: wff) (A2 C D S E F G V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (co D S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cin (co C D cful) (co C D cfth))) (wbr F G (cin (co C E cful) (co C E cfth)))) $;

--| The "inclusion functor" from a subcategory of a category into the category itself. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem inclfusubc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2 C S: class) (F G: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv C csubc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (co C J cresc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cres cid B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cv x3) (cv y1) J)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co S C cfunc)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the collection of natural transformations.
term cnat: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the functor category.
term cfuc: class;

--| Definition of a natural transformation between two functors. A natural transformation ` A : F --> G ` is a collection of arrows ` A ( x ) : F ( x ) --> G ( x ) ` , such that ` A ( y ) o. F ( h ) = G ( h ) o. A ( x ) ` for each morphism ` h : x --> y ` . Definition 6.1 in [Adamek] p. 83, and definition in [Lang] p. 65. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_nat {x3 y1 u t f2 g2 h s1 r a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnat (cmpo t u ccat ccat (cmpo f2 g2 (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc) (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc) (csb r (cfv (cv f2) c1st) (csb s1 (cfv (cv g2) c1st) (crab a (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral h (wceq (co (cfv (cv y1) (cv a)) (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd))) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv r))) (cfv (cv y1) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv u) cco))) (co (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv g2) c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1))) (cfv (cv y1) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv u) cco)))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) chom))) (cfv (cv t) cbs)) (cfv (cv t) cbs)) (cixp x3 (cfv (cv t) cbs) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv u) chom)))))))) $;

--| Definition of the category of functors between two fixed categories, with the objects being functors and the morphisms being natural transformations. Definition 6.15 in [Adamek] p. 87. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_fuc {x3 v u t f2 g2 h a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfuc (cmpo t u ccat ccat (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (co (cv t) (cv u) cnat)) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v h (cxp (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc) (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc)) (co (cv t) (cv u) cfunc) (csb f2 (cfv (cv v) c1st) (csb g2 (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cmpo b a (co (cv g2) (cv h) (co (cv t) (cv u) cnat)) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co (cv t) (cv u) cnat)) (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv t) cbs) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv b)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) c1st))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv h) c1st)) (cfv (cv u) cco))))))))))) $;

--| The ` FuncCat ` operation is a well-defined function on categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem fnfuc:
  $ wfn cfuc (cxp ccat ccat) $;

--| Value of the function giving natural transformations between two categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Mar-2024.)
theorem natfval {x3 y1 f2 g2 h s1 r a: setvar} (B2: class h) (C D: class) (c_x: class x3 y1 h) (H: class x3 y1) (J: class x3 y1 h) (N: class x3 y1 f2 g2 h s1 r a):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpo f2 g2 (co C D cfunc) (co C D cfunc) (csb r (cfv (cv f2) c1st) (csb s1 (cfv (cv g2) c1st) (crab a (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral h (wceq (co (cfv (cv y1) (cv a)) (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd))) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv r))) (cfv (cv y1) (cv s1)) c_x)) (co (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv g2) c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1))) (cfv (cv y1) (cv s1)) c_x))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2) (cixp x3 B2 (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1)) J)))))) $;

--| Property of being a natural transformation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem isnat {x3 y1 h: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class h) (C D: class) (c_x: class x3 y1 h) (F G: class) (H: class x3 y1) (J: class x3 y1 h) (K L: class) (N: class x3 y1 h):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr K L (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 (co (cop F G) (cop K L) N)) (wa (wcel A2 (cixp x3 B2 (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) K) J))) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral h (wceq (co (cfv (cv y1) A2) (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) G)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (cfv (cv y1) K) c_x)) (co (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) L)) (cfv (cv x3) A2) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) K)) (cfv (cv y1) K) c_x))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2))) $;

--| Property of being a natural transformation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem isnat2 {x3 y1 h: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class h) (C D: class) (c_x: class x3 y1 h) (F G: class) (H: class x3 y1) (J N: class x3 y1 h):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 (co F G N)) (wa (wcel A2 (cixp x3 B2 (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st)) J))) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral h (wceq (co (cfv (cv y1) A2) (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv F c2nd))) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv F c1st))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv G c1st)) c_x)) (co (cfv (cv h) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv G c2nd))) (cfv (cv x3) A2) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv G c1st)) c_x))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) B2) B2))) $;

--| The natural transformation set operation is a well-defined function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem natffn (C D N: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wfn N (cxp (co C D cfunc) (co C D cfunc)) $;

--| Reverse closure for a natural transformation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem natrcl (A2 C D F G N: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co F G N)) (wa (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) (wcel G (co C D cfunc))) $;

--| Rewrite the natural transformation predicate with separated functor parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem nat1st2nd (ph: wff) (A2 C D F G N: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cop (cfv F c1st) (cfv F c2nd)) (cop (cfv G c1st) (cfv G c2nd)) N)) $;

--| A natural transformation is a function from the objects of ` C ` to homomorphisms from ` F ( x ) ` to ` G ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem natixp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G J K L: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cop F G) (cop K L) N)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cixp x3 B2 (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) K) J))) $;

--| A component of a natural transformation is a morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem natcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G J K L N X: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cop F G) (cop K L) N)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X A2) (co (cfv X F) (cfv X K) J)) $;

--| A natural transformation is a function on the objects of ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem natfn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G K L N: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cop F G) (cop K L) N)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn A2 B2) $;

--| Naturality property of a natural transformation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem nati (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R c_x F G H K L N X Y: class):
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cop F G) (cop K L) N)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv Y A2) (cfv R (co X Y G)) (co (cop (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)) (cfv Y K) c_x)) (co (cfv R (co X Y L)) (cfv X A2) (co (cop (cfv X F) (cfv X K)) (cfv Y K) c_x))) $;

--| A weak universe is closed under the natural transformation operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem wunnat (ph: wff) (C D U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C D cnat) U) $;

--| A category structure is a structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catstr (c_x U H: class):
  $ wbr (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) U) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x)) (cop c1 (cdc c1 c5)) cstr $;

--| Value of the functor category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucval {x3 v f2 g2 h a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 v f2 g2 h a b) (B2: class x3 f2 g2 a b) (C D: class) (Q c_xb c_x N: class x3 v f2 g2 h a b):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co C D cfunc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (cmpo v h (cxp B2 B2) B2 (csb f2 (cfv (cv v) c1st) (csb g2 (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cmpo b a (co (cv g2) (cv h) N) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) N) (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv b)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) c1st))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv h) c1st)) c_x)))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) N) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_xb))) $;

--| Value of the functor category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuccofval {x3 v f2 g2 h a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 v f2 g2 h a b) (B2: class x3 f2 g2 a b) (C D: class) (Q c_xb c_x N: class x3 v f2 g2 h a b):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co C D cfunc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_xb (cmpo v h (cxp B2 B2) B2 (csb f2 (cfv (cv v) c1st) (csb g2 (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cmpo b a (co (cv g2) (cv h) N) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) N) (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv b)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a)) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) c1st))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv h) c1st)) c_x)))))))) $;

--| The objects of the functor category are functors from ` C ` to ` D ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucbas (C D Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq (co C D cfunc) (cfv Q cbs) $;

--| The morphisms in the functor category are natural transformations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem fuchom (C D Q N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Q chom) $;

--| Value of the composition of natural transformations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucco {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C D: class) (Q: class x3) (R S: class) (c_xb: class x3) (c_x F G H: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co G H N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S R (co (cop F G) H c_xb)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) S) (cfv (cv x3) R) (co (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv H c1st)) c_x)))) $;

--| Value of the functor category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuccoval (ph: wff) (A2 C D Q R S c_xb c_x F G H N X: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co G H N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co S R (co (cop F G) H c_xb))) (co (cfv X S) (cfv X R) (co (cop (cfv X (cfv F c1st)) (cfv X (cfv G c1st))) (cfv X (cfv H c1st)) c_x))) $;

--| The composition of two natural transformations is a natural transformation. Remark 6.14(a) in [Adamek] p. 87. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuccocl (ph: wff) (C D Q R S c_xb F G H N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co G H N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S R (co (cop F G) H c_xb)) (co F H N)) $;

--| The identity natural transformation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucidcl (ph: wff) (C D Q c_1 F N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom c_1 (cfv F c1st)) (co F F N)) $;

--| Left identity of natural transformations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuclid (ph: wff) (C D Q R c_xb c_1 F G N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (ccom c_1 (cfv G c1st)) R (co (cop F G) G c_xb)) R) $;

--| Right identity of natural transformations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucrid (ph: wff) (C D Q R c_xb c_1 F G N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co R (ccom c_1 (cfv F c1st)) (co (cop F F) G c_xb)) R) $;

--| Associativity of natural transformation composition. Remark 6.14(b) in [Adamek] p. 87. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucass (ph: wff) (C D Q R S c_xb T F G H K N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Q cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co G H N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (co H K N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co T S (co (cop G H) K c_xb)) R (co (cop F G) K c_xb)) (co T (co S R (co (cop F G) H c_xb)) (co (cop F H) K c_xb))) $;

--| The functor category is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuccatid {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D Q: class) (c_1: class f2):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel Q ccat) (wceq (cfv Q ccid) (cmpt f2 (co C D cfunc) (ccom c_1 (cfv (cv f2) c1st))))) $;

--| The functor category is a category. Remark 6.16 in [Adamek] p. 88. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuccat (ph: wff) (C D Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q ccat) $;

--| The identity morphism in the functor category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucid (ph: wff) (C D Q c_1 F I: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv Q ccid) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv D ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F I) (ccom c_1 (cfv F c1st))) $;

--| Two natural transformations are in a section iff all the components are in a section relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucsect {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C D Q: class) (S T: class x3) (U F G N V: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Q csect) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr U V (co F G S)) (w3a (wcel U (co F G N)) (wcel V (co G F N)) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) U) (cfv (cv x3) V) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st)) T)) B2))) $;

--| Two natural transformations are inverses of each other iff all the components are inverse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucinv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C D Q U F G I J N V: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv Q cinv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr U V (co F G I)) (w3a (wcel U (co F G N)) (wcel V (co G F N)) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) U) (cfv (cv x3) V) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st)) J)) B2))) $;

--| If ` V ( x ) ` is an inverse to ` U ( x ) ` for each ` x ` , and ` U ` is a natural transformation, then ` V ` is also a natural transformation, and they are inverse in the functor category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem invfuc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C D Q U F G I J N: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv Q cinv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co F G N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) U) X (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st)) J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr U (cmpt x3 B2 X) (co F G I)) $;

--| A natural transformation is an isomorphism of functors iff all its components are isomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem fuciso {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D Q F G I J N: class):
  $ wceq Q (co C D cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (co C D cnat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co C D cfunc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv Q ciso) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 (co F G I)) (wa (wcel A2 (co F G N)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) A2) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv F c1st)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G c1st)) J)) B2))) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same natural transformations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem natpropd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 chomf) (cfv B2 chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccomf) (cfv B2 ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cnat) (co B2 D cnat)) $;

--| If two categories have the same set of objects, morphisms, and compositions, then they have the same functor categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem fucpropd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 chomf) (cfv B2 chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccomf) (cfv B2 ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) (cfv D chomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ccomf) (cfv D ccomf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C cfuc) (co B2 D cfuc)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of initial objects of a category.
term cinito: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of terminal objects of a category.
term ctermo: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of zero objects of a category.
term czeroo: class;

--| An object A is said to be an initial object provided that for each object B there is exactly one morphism from A to B. Definition 7.1 in [Adamek] p. 101, or definition in [Lang] p. 57 (called "a universally repelling object" there). See ~ dfinito2 and ~ dfinito3 for alternate definitions depending on ~ df-termo . See ~ dfinito4 for an alternate definition using the universal property. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_inito {h a b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cinito (cmpt c ccat (crab a (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv a) (cv b) (cfv (cv c) chom)))) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cfv (cv c) cbs))) $;

--| An object A is called a terminal object provided that for each object B there is exactly one morphism from B to A. Definition 7.4 in [Adamek] p. 102, or definition in [Lang] p. 57 (called "a universally attracting object" there). See ~ dftermo2 and ~ dftermo3 for alternate definitions depending on ~ df-inito . See ~ dftermo4 for an alternate definition using the universal property. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_termo {h a b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctermo (cmpt c ccat (crab a (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv b) (cv a) (cfv (cv c) chom)))) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cfv (cv c) cbs))) $;

--| An object A is called a zero object provided that it is both an initial object and a terminal object. Definition 7.7 of [Adamek] p. 103. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_zeroo {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq czeroo (cmpt c ccat (cin (cfv (cv c) cinito) (cfv (cv c) ctermo))) $;

--| ` InitO ` is a function on ` Cat ` . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem initofn:
  $ wfn cinito ccat $;

--| ` TermO ` is a function on ` Cat ` . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem termofn:
  $ wfn ctermo ccat $;

--| ` ZeroO ` is a function on ` Cat ` . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem zeroofn:
  $ wfn czeroo ccat $;

--| Reverse closure for an initial object: If a class has an initial object, the class is a category. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem initorcl (C I: class):
  $ wi (wcel I (cfv C cinito)) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| Reverse closure for a terminal object: If a class has a terminal object, the class is a category. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem termorcl (C T: class):
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv C ctermo)) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| Reverse closure for a zero object: If a class has a zero object, the class is a category. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem zeroorcl (C Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z (cfv C czeroo)) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The value of the initial object function, i.e. the set of all initial objects of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoval {h a b: setvar} (ph: wff h a b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C cinito) (crab a (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv a) (cv b) H))) B2) B2)) $;

--| The value of the terminal object function, i.e. the set of all terminal objects of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem termoval {h a b: setvar} (ph: wff h a b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C ctermo) (crab a (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv b) (cv a) H))) B2) B2)) $;

--| The value of the zero object function, i.e. the set of all zero objects of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem zerooval (ph: wff) (B2 C H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C czeroo) (cin (cfv C cinito) (cfv C ctermo))) $;

--| The predicate "is an initial object" of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem isinito {h b: setvar} (ph: wff h b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h b) (I: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel I (cfv C cinito)) (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co I (cv b) H))) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a terminal object" of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem istermo {h b: setvar} (ph: wff h b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h b) (I: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel I (cfv C ctermo)) (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv b) I H))) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a zero object" of a category. (Contributed by AV, 3-Apr-2020.)
theorem iszeroo (ph: wff) (B2 C H I: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel I (cfv C czeroo)) (wa (wcel I (cfv C cinito)) (wcel I (cfv C ctermo)))) $;

--| Implication of a class being an initial object. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2020.)
theorem isinitoi {h b: setvar} (ph: wff h b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h b) (O: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel O (cfv C cinito))) (wa (wcel O B2) (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co O (cv b) H))) B2)) $;

--| Implication of a class being a terminal object. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem istermoi {h b: setvar} (ph: wff h b) (B2: class h) (C: class) (H: class h b) (O: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel O (cfv C ctermo))) (wa (wcel O B2) (wral b (weu h (wcel (cv h) (co (cv b) O H))) B2)) $;

--| For an initial object, the identity arrow is the one and only morphism of the object to the object itself. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoid (ph: wff) (B2 C H O: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel O (cfv C cinito))) (wceq (co O O H) (csn (cfv O (cfv C ccid)))) $;

--| For a terminal object, the identity arrow is the one and only morphism of the object to the object itself. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem termoid (ph: wff) (B2 C H O: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel O (cfv C ctermo))) (wceq (co O O H) (csn (cfv O (cfv C ccid)))) $;

--| An initial object is a terminal object in the opposite category. An alternate definition of ~ df-inito depending on ~ df-termo . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem dfinito2 {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cinito (cmpt c ccat (cfv (cfv (cv c) coppc) ctermo)) $;

--| A terminal object is an initial object in the opposite category. An alternate definition of ~ df-termo depending on ~ df-inito . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem dftermo2 {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctermo (cmpt c ccat (cfv (cfv (cv c) coppc) cinito)) $;

--| An alternate definition of ~ df-inito depending on ~ df-termo , without dummy variables. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem dfinito3:
  $ wceq cinito (ccom ctermo (cres coppc ccat)) $;

--| An alternate definition of ~ df-termo depending on ~ df-inito , without dummy variables. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 29-Aug-2024.)
theorem dftermo3:
  $ wceq ctermo (ccom cinito (cres coppc ccat)) $;

--| An initial object is an object. (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoo (C O: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wi (wcel O (cfv C cinito)) (wcel O (cfv C cbs))) $;

--| A terminal object is an object. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem termoo (C O: class):
  $ wi (wcel C ccat) (wi (wcel O (cfv C ctermo)) (wcel O (cfv C cbs))) $;

--| Implication of a class being a zero object. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem iszeroi (C O: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C ccat) (wcel O (cfv C czeroo))) (wa (wcel O (cfv C cbs)) (wa (wcel O (cfv C cinito)) (wcel O (cfv C ctermo)))) $;

--| Morphisms between two initial objects are inverses. (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2020.)
theorem _2initoinv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel G (co B2 A2 (cfv C chom))) (wcel F (co A2 B2 (cfv C chom)))) (wbr F G (co A2 B2 (cfv C cinv))) $;

--| Initial objects are essentially unique (strong form), i.e. there is a _unique_ isomorphism between two initial objects, see statement in [Lang] p. 58 ("... if P, P' are two universal objects [[...] then there exists a unique isomorphism between them.". (Proposed by BJ, 14-Apr-2020.) (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (weu f2 (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 B2 (cfv C ciso)))) $;

--| Initial objects are essentially unique (weak form), i.e. if A and B are initial objects, then A and B are isomorphic. Proposition 7.3 (1) of [Adamek] p. 102. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu1w (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Lemma 0 for ~ initoeu2 . (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu2lem0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G H I K X c_op: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa ph (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel D X))) (w3a (wcel K (co B2 A2 I)) (wcel F (co A2 D H)) (wcel G (co B2 D H))) (wceq (co (co F K (co (cop B2 A2) D c_op)) (cfv K (co B2 A2 (cfv C cinv))) (co (cop A2 B2) D c_op)) (co G (cfv K (co B2 A2 (cfv C cinv))) (co (cop A2 B2) D c_op)))) (wceq G (co F K (co (cop B2 A2) D c_op))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ initoeu2 . (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu2lem1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G H I K X c_op: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel D X)) (wa (wcel K (co B2 A2 I)) (wcel (co F K (co (cop B2 A2) D c_op)) (co B2 D H)))) (wi (w3a (weu f2 (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 D H))) (wcel F (co A2 D H)) (wcel G (co B2 D H))) (wceq G (co F K (co (cop B2 A2) D c_op)))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ initoeu2 . (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu2lem2 {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F H I K X c_op: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel D X)) (w3a (wcel K (co B2 A2 I)) (wcel F (co A2 D H)) (wcel (co F K (co (cop B2 A2) D c_op)) (co B2 D H)))) (wi (weu f2 (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 D H))) (weu g2 (wcel (cv g2) (co B2 D H)))) $;

--| Initial objects are essentially unique, if A is an initial object, then so is every object that is isomorphic to A. Proposition 7.3 (2) in [Adamek] p. 102. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2020.)
theorem initoeu2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C cinito)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 (cfv C ccic)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cinito)) $;

--| Morphisms between two terminal objects are inverses. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem _2termoinv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel G (co B2 A2 (cfv C chom))) (wcel F (co A2 B2 (cfv C chom)))) (wbr F G (co A2 B2 (cfv C cinv))) $;

--| Terminal objects are essentially unique (strong form), i.e. there is a _unique_ isomorphism between two terminal objects, see statement in [Lang] p. 58 ("... if P, P' are two universal objects [[...] then there exists a unique isomorphism between them.". (Proposed by BJ, 14-Apr-2020.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem termoeu1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (weu f2 (wcel (cv f2) (co A2 B2 (cfv C ciso)))) $;

--| Terminal objects are essentially unique (weak form), i.e. if A and B are terminal objects, then A and B are isomorphic. Proposition 7.6 of [Adamek] p. 103. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem termoeu1w (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C ctermo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 (cfv C ccic)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the domain extractor for an arrow.
term cdoma: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the codomain extractor for an arrow.
term ccoda: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the collection of all arrows of a category.
term carw: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the set of all arrows with a specific domain and codomain.
term choma: class;

--| Definition of the domain extractor for an arrow. (Contributed by FL, 24-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_doma:
  $ wceq cdoma (ccom c1st c1st) $;

--| Definition of the codomain extractor for an arrow. (Contributed by FL, 26-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_coda:
  $ wceq ccoda (ccom c2nd c1st) $;

--| Definition of the hom-set extractor for arrows, which tags the morphisms of the underlying hom-set with domain and codomain, which can then be extracted using ~ df-doma and ~ df-coda . (Contributed by FL, 6-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_homa {x3 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq choma (cmpt c ccat (cmpt x3 (cxp (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) chom))))) $;

--| Definition of the set of arrows of a category. We will use the term "arrow" to denote a morphism tagged with its domain and codomain, as opposed to ` Hom ` , which allows hom-sets for distinct objects to overlap. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_arw {c: setvar}:
  $ wceq carw (cmpt c ccat (cuni (crn (cfv (cv c) choma)))) $;

--| Reverse closure for an arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homarcl (C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| Value of the disjointified hom-set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homafval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (H J: class x3):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpt x3 (cxp B2 B2) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv x3) J)))) $;

--| Functionality of the disjointified hom-set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homaf (ph: wff) (B2 C H: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) (cpw (cxp (cxp B2 B2) cvv)) H) $;

--| Value of the disjointified hom-set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homaval (ph: wff) (B2 C H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (cxp (csn (cop X Y)) (co X Y J))) $;

--| Value of the disjointified hom-set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem elhoma (ph: wff) (B2 C F H J X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr Z F (co X Y H)) (wa (wceq Z (cop X Y)) (wcel F (co X Y J)))) $;

--| Produce an arrow from a morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem elhomai (ph: wff) (B2 C F H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop X Y) F (co X Y H)) $;

--| Produce an arrow from a morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem elhomai2 (ph: wff) (B2 C F H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp X Y F) (co X Y H)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the domain and codomain of an arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homarcl2 (B2 C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) $;

--| An arrow is an ordered pair. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homarel (C H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wrel (co X Y H) $;

--| The first component of an arrow is the ordered pair of domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homa1 (C F H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wbr Z F (co X Y H)) (wceq Z (cop X Y)) $;

--| The second component of an arrow is the corresponding morphism (without the domain/codomain tag). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homahom2 (C F H J X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi (wbr Z F (co X Y H)) (wcel F (co X Y J)) $;

--| The second component of an arrow is the corresponding morphism (without the domain/codomain tag). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homahom (C F H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wcel (cfv F c2nd) (co X Y J)) $;

--| The domain of an arrow with known domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homadm (C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wceq (cfv F cdoma) X) $;

--| The codomain of an arrow with known domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homacd (C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wceq (cfv F ccoda) Y) $;

--| Decompose an arrow into domain, codomain, and morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homadmcd (C F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wceq F (cotp X Y (cfv F c2nd))) $;

--| The set of arrows is the union of all the disjointified hom-sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwval (A2 C H: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn H)) $;

--| The first component of an arrow is the ordered pair of domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwrcl (A2 C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| An arrow is contained in the hom-set corresponding to its domain and codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwhoma (A2 C F H: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel F (co (cfv F cdoma) (cfv F ccoda) H)) $;

--| A hom-set is a subset of the collection of all arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homarw (A2 C H X Y: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wss (co X Y H) A2 $;

--| The domain of an arrow is an object. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwdm (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel (cfv F cdoma) B2) $;

--| The codomain of an arrow is an object. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwcd (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel (cfv F ccoda) B2) $;

--| The domain function is a function from arrows to objects. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem dmaf (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wf A2 B2 (cres cdoma A2) $;

--| The codomain function is a function from arrows to objects. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem cdaf (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wf A2 B2 (cres ccoda A2) $;

--| The second component of an arrow is the corresponding morphism (without the domain/codomain tag). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwhom (A2 C F J: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel (cfv F c2nd) (co (cfv F cdoma) (cfv F ccoda) J)) $;

--| Decompose an arrow into domain, codomain, and morphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwdmcd (A2 C F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wceq F (cotp (cfv F cdoma) (cfv F ccoda) (cfv F c2nd))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include identity for arrows.
term cida: class;

--| Extend class notation to include composition for arrows.
term ccoa: class;

--| Definition of the identity arrow, which is just the identity morphism tagged with its domain and codomain. (Contributed by FL, 26-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_ida {x3 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cida (cmpt c ccat (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv c) cbs) (cotp (cv x3) (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv c) ccid))))) $;

--| Definition of the composition of arrows. Since arrows are tagged with domain and codomain, this does not need to be a quinary operation like the regular composition in a category ` comp ` . Instead, it is a partial binary operation on arrows, which is defined when the domain of the first arrow matches the codomain of the second. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_coa {f2 g2 h c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccoa (cmpt c ccat (cmpo g2 f2 (cfv (cv c) carw) (crab h (wceq (cfv (cv h) ccoda) (cfv (cv g2) cdoma)) (cfv (cv c) carw)) (cotp (cfv (cv f2) cdoma) (cfv (cv g2) ccoda) (co (cfv (cv g2) c2nd) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd) (co (cop (cfv (cv f2) cdoma) (cfv (cv g2) cdoma)) (cfv (cv g2) ccoda) (cfv (cv c) cco)))))) $;

--| Value of the identity arrow function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idafval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (cmpt x3 B2 (cotp (cv x3) (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) c_1)))) $;

--| Value of the identity arrow function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idaval (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X I) (cotp X X (cfv X c_1))) $;

--| Morphism part of the identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem ida2 (ph: wff) (B2 C c_1 I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv X I) c2nd) (cfv X c_1)) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idahom (ph: wff) (B2 C H I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X I) (co X X H)) $;

--| Domain of the identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idadm (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv X I) cdoma) X) $;

--| Codomain of the identity arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idacd (ph: wff) (B2 C I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv X I) ccoda) X) $;

--| The identity arrow function is a function from objects to arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem idaf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 A2 I) $;

--| The value of the composition of arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem coafval {f2 g2 h: setvar} (A2 C: class) (c_xb c_x: class f2 g2 h):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo g2 f2 A2 (crab h (wceq (cfv (cv h) ccoda) (cfv (cv g2) cdoma)) A2) (cotp (cfv (cv f2) cdoma) (cfv (cv g2) ccoda) (co (cfv (cv g2) c2nd) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd) (co (cop (cfv (cv f2) cdoma) (cfv (cv g2) cdoma)) (cfv (cv g2) ccoda) c_xb)))) $;

--| A pair ` <. G , F >. ` is in the domain of the arrow composition, if the domain of ` G ` equals the codomain of ` F ` . (In this case we say ` G ` and ` F ` are composable.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem eldmcoa (A2 C c_x F G: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wb (wbr G F (cdm c_x)) (w3a (wcel F A2) (wcel G A2) (wceq (cfv F ccoda) (cfv G cdoma))) $;

--| The domain of composition is a collection of pairs of arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem dmcoass (A2 C c_x: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wss (cdm c_x) (cxp A2 A2) $;

--| If ` F : X --> Y ` and ` G : Y --> Z ` , then ` G ` and ` F ` are composable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem homdmcoa (ph: wff) (C c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G F (cdm c_x)) $;

--| Value of composition for composable arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem coaval (ph: wff) (C c_xb c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F c_x) (cotp X Z (co (cfv G c2nd) (cfv F c2nd) (co (cop X Y) Z c_xb)))) $;

--| The morphism part of arrow composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem coa2 (ph: wff) (C c_xb c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co G F c_x) c2nd) (co (cfv G c2nd) (cfv F c2nd) (co (cop X Y) Z c_xb))) $;

--| The composition of two composable arrows is an arrow. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem coahom (ph: wff) (C c_x F G H X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F c_x) (co X Z H)) $;

--| Composition of arrows is a partial binary operation on arrows. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem coapm (A2 C c_x: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv C carw) $ >
  $ wcel c_x (co A2 (cxp A2 A2) cpm) $;

--| Left identity of a category using arrow notation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwlid (ph: wff) (C c_x c_1 F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv Y c_1) F c_x) F) $;

--| Right identity of a category using arrow notation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwrid (ph: wff) (C c_x c_1 F H X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F (cfv X c_1) c_x) F) $;

--| Associativity of composition in a category using arrow notation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2017.)
theorem arwass (ph: wff) (C c_x c_1 F G H K W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv C choma) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C ccoa) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C cida) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co Z W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co K G c_x) F c_x) (co K (co G F c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category Set.
term csetc: class;

--| Definition of the category Set, relativized to a subset ` u ` . Example 3.3(1) of [Adamek] p. 22. This is the category of all sets in ` u ` and functions between these sets. Generally, we will take ` u ` to be a weak universe or Grothendieck universe, because these sets have closure properties as good as the real thing. (Contributed by FL, 8-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_setc {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq csetc (cmpt u cvv (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv u)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv u) (cv u) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmap))) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v z (cxp (cv u) (cv u)) (cv u) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cv z) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cmap) (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))))) $;

--| Value of the category of sets (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcval {x3 y1 z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (C c_x: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (H V: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 U U (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp U U) U (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cv z) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cmap) (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) U) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $;

--| Set of objects of the category of sets (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcbas (ph: wff) (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cfv C cbs)) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of sets (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setchomfval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 y1) (U: class) (H V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 U U (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmap))) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of sets (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setchom (ph: wff) (C U H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (co Y X cmap)) $;

--| A morphism of sets is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem elsetchom (ph: wff) (C U F H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wf X Y F)) $;

--| Composition in the category of sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setccofval {z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (C c_x: class z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (V: class z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp U U) U (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cv z) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cmap) (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Composition in the category of sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcco (ph: wff) (C c_x U F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y Z G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (ccom G F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ setccat . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setccatid {x3: setvar} (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wa (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt x3 U (cres cid (cv x3))))) $;

--| The category of sets is a category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setccat (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The identity arrow in the category of sets is the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcid (ph: wff) (C U c_1 V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) (cres cid X)) $;

--| A monomorphism of sets is an injection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcmon (ph: wff) (C U F M V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv C cmon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y M)) (wf1 X Y F)) $;

--| An epimorphism of sets is a surjection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcepi (ph: wff) (C U E F V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv C cepi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c2o U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y E)) (wfo X Y F)) $;

--| A section in the category of sets, written out. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcsect (ph: wff) (C S U F G V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y S)) (w3a (wf X Y F) (wf Y X G) (wceq (ccom G F) (cres cid X)))) $;

--| An inverse in the category of sets is the converse operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setcinv (ph: wff) (C U F G N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y N)) (wa (wf1o X Y F) (wceq G (ccnv F)))) $;

--| An isomorphism in the category of sets is a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem setciso (ph: wff) (C U F I V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y I)) (wf1o X Y F)) $;

--| The restriction of the category of sets to a subset is the category of sets in the subset. Thus, the ` SetCat `` U ` categories for different ` U ` are full subcategories of each other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resssetc (ph: wff) (C D U V W: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv V csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cfv (co C V cress) chomf) (cfv D chomf)) (wceq (cfv (co C V cress) ccomf) (cfv D ccomf))) $;

--| A functor into a smaller category of sets is a functor into the larger category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2017.)
theorem funcsetcres2 (ph: wff) (C D U E V W: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv V csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co E D cfunc) (co E C cfunc)) $;

--| ` (/) ` and ` 1o ` are distinct objects in ` ( SetCat `` 2o ) ` . This combined with ~ setc2ohom demonstrates that the category does not have pairwise disjoint hom-sets. See also ~ df-cat and ~ cat1 . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 24-Sep-2024.)
theorem setc2obas (B2 C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv c2o csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ w3a (wcel c0 B2) (wcel c1o B2) (wne c1o c0) $;

--| ` ( SetCat `` 2o ) ` is a category (provable from ~ setccat and ~ 2oex ) that does not have pairwise disjoint hom-sets, proved by this theorem combined with ~ setc2obas . Notably, the empty set ` (/) ` is simultaneously an object ( ~ setc2obas ), an identity morphism from ` (/) ` to ` (/) ` ( ~ setcid or ~ thincid ), and a non-identity morphism from ` (/) ` to ` 1o ` . See ~ cat1lem and ~ cat1 for a more general statement. This category is also thin ( ~ setc2othin ), and therefore is "equivalent" to a preorder (actually a partial order). See ~ prsthinc for more details on the "equivalence". (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 24-Sep-2024.)
theorem setc2ohom (C H: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv c2o csetc) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wcel c0 (cin (co c0 c0 H) (co c0 c1o H)) $;

--| The category of sets in a "universe" containing the empty set and another set does not have pairwise disjoint hom-sets as required in Axiom CAT 1 in [Lang] p. 53. Lemma for ~ cat1 . (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem cat1lem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (B2: class) (C U: class x3 y1 z w) (H: class) (V: class x3 y1 z w) (Y: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq C (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne c0 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (wa (wne (cin (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H) (co (cv z) (cv w) H)) c0) (wn (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))))) B2) B2) B2) B2) $;

--| The definition of category ~ df-cat does not impose pairwise disjoint hom-sets as required in Axiom CAT 1 in [Lang] p. 53. See ~ setc2obas and ~ setc2ohom for a counterexample. For a version with pairwise disjoint hom-sets, see ~ df-homa and its subsection. (Contributed by Zhi Wang, 15-Sep-2024.)
theorem cat1 {x3 y1 z w h b c: setvar}:
  $ wrex c (wsbc b (wsbc h (wn (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cin (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv h)) (co (cv z) (cv w) (cv h))) c0) (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b))) (cfv (cv c) chom)) (cfv (cv c) cbs)) ccat $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category Cat.
term ccatc: class;

--| Definition of the category Cat, which consists of all categories in the universe ` u ` (i.e., " ` u ` -small categories", see Definition 3.44. of [Adamek] p. 39), with functors as the morphisms ( ~ catchom , ~ elcatchom ). Definition 3.47 of [Adamek] p. 40. We do not introduce a specific definition for " ` u ` -large categories", which can be expressed as ` ( Cat \ u ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_catc {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 b: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccatc (cmpt u cvv (csb b (cin (cv u) ccat) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv b) (cv b) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cfunc))) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v z (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) cfunc) (cfv (cv v) cfunc) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) ccofu))))))) $;

--| Value of the category of categories (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcval {x3 y1 z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (H V: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cin U ccat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cfunc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) cfunc) (cfv (cv v) cfunc) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) ccofu)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $;

--| Set of objects of the category of categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcbas (ph: wff) (B2 C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cin U ccat)) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of categories (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catchomfval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (U: class) (H V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cfunc))) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of categories (in a universe). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catchom (ph: wff) (B2 C U H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (co X Y cfunc)) $;

--| Composition in the category of categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catccofval {z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x: class z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (V: class z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) cfunc) (cfv (cv v) cfunc) (co (cv g2) (cv f2) ccofu)))) $;

--| Composition in the category of categories. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcco (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x U F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z cfunc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (co G F ccofu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ catccat . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catccatid {x3: setvar} (B2 C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wa (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cidfu)))) $;

--| The identity arrow in the category of categories is the identity functor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcid (ph: wff) (B2 C U c_1 I V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv X cidfu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) I) $;

--| The category of categories is a category, see remark 3.48 in [Adamek] p. 40. (Clearly it cannot be an element of itself, hence it is " ` U ` -large".) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jan-2017.)
theorem catccat (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The restriction of the category of categories to a subset is the category of categories in the subset. Thus, the ` CatCat `` U ` categories for different ` U ` are full subcategories of each other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2017.)
theorem resscatc (ph: wff) (C D U V W: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv V ccatc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cfv (co C V cress) chomf) (cfv D chomf)) (wceq (cfv (co C V cress) ccomf) (cfv D ccomf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ catciso . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem catcisolem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 y1) (C R S: class) (U: class x3 y1) (F G: class) (H: class x3 y1) (I: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 S S (ccnv (co (cfv (cv x3) (ccnv F)) (cfv (cv y1) (ccnv F)) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cin (co X Y cful) (co X Y cfth))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o R S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop F G) (cop (ccnv F) H) (co X Y I)) $;

--| A functor is an isomorphism of categories if and only if it is full and faithful, and is a bijection on the objects. Remark 3.28(2) in [Adamek] p. 34. Note that "catciso.u" is redundant thanks to ~ elbasfv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem catciso (ph: wff) (B2 C R S U F I V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y I)) (wa (wcel F (cin (co X Y cful) (co X Y cfth))) (wf1o R S (cfv F c1st)))) $;

--| An element of the base set of the category of categories for a weak universe belongs to the weak universe. Formerly part of the proof for ~ catcoppccl . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcbascl (ph: wff) (B2 C U X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $;

--| A slot entry of an element of the base set of the category of categories for a weak universe belongs to the weak universe. Formerly part of the proof for ~ catcoppccl . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcslotelcl (ph: wff) (B2 C U E X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X E) U) $;

--| The base set of an element of the base set of the category of categories for a weak universe belongs to the weak universe. Formerly part of the proof for ~ catcoppccl . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcbaselcl (ph: wff) (B2 C U X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X cbs) U) $;

--| The Hom-set of an element of the base set of the category of categories for a weak universe belongs to the weak universe. Formerly part of the proof for ~ catcoppccl . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catchomcl (ph: wff) (B2 C U X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X chom) U) $;

--| The composition operation of an element of the base set of the category of categories for a weak universe belongs to the weak universe. Formerly part of the proof for ~ catcoppccl . (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcccocl (ph: wff) (B2 C U X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X cco) U) $;

--| The category of categories for a weak universe is closed under taking opposites. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcoppccl (ph: wff) (B2 C U O X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv X coppc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O B2) $;

--| The category of categories for a weak universe is closed under the functor category operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Oct-2024.)
theorem catcfuccl (ph: wff) (B2 C Q U X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co X Y cfuc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q B2) $;

--| The inverse images of the universal class ` _V ` under functions on the universal class ` _V ` are the universal class ` _V ` itself. (Proposed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2020.) (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem fncnvimaeqv (F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F cvv) (wceq (cima (ccnv F) cvv) cvv) $;

--| The inverse image of the universal class ` _V ` under the base function is the universal class ` _V ` itself. (Proposed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2020.) (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem bascnvimaeqv:
  $ wceq (cima (ccnv cbs) cvv) cvv $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category ExtStr.
term cestrc: class;

--| Definition of the category ExtStr of extensible structures. This is the category whose objects are all sets in a universe ` u ` regarded as extensible structures and whose morphisms are the functions between their base sets. If a set is not a real extensible structure, it is regarded as extensible structure with an empty base set. Because of ~ bascnvimaeqv we do not need to restrict the universe to sets which "have a base". Generally, we will take ` u ` to be a weak universe or Grothendieck universe, because these sets have closure properties as good as the real thing. (Proposed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Mar-2020.) (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
axiom df_estrc {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cestrc (cmpt u cvv (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv u)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv u) (cv u) (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap))) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v z (cxp (cv u) (cv u)) (cv u) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv z) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) cmap) (co (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c1st) cbs) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))))) $;

--| Value of the category of extensible structures (in a universe). (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrcval {x3 y1 z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (C c_x: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (H V: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 U U (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp U U) U (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv z) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) cmap) (co (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c1st) cbs) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) U) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $;

--| Set of objects of the category of extensible structures (in a universe). (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrcbas (ph: wff) (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cfv C cbs)) $;

--| Set of morphisms ("arrows") of the category of extensible structures (in a universe). (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrchomfval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 y1) (U: class) (H V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 U U (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap))) $;

--| The morphisms between extensible structures are mappings between their base sets. (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrchom (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C U H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $;

--| A morphism between extensible structures is a function between their base sets. (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem elestrchom (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C U F H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wf A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Composition in the category of extensible structures. (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrccofval {z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (C c_x: class z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (V: class z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp U U) U (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv z) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) cmap) (co (cfv (cfv (cv v) c2nd) cbs) (cfv (cfv (cv v) c1st) cbs) cmap) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Composition in the category of extensible structures. (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrcco (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_x U F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (ccom G F)) $;

--| An element of the base set of the base set of the category of extensible structures (i.e. the base set of an extensible structure) belongs to the considered weak universe. (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrcbasbas (ph: wff) (B2 C U E: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel E B2)) (wcel (cfv E cbs) U) $;

--| Lemma for ~ estrccat . (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrccatid {x3: setvar} (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wa (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt x3 U (cres cid (cfv (cv x3) cbs))))) $;

--| The category of extensible structures is a category. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrccat (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The identity arrow in the category of extensible structures is the identity function of base sets. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrcid (ph: wff) (C U c_1 V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) (cres cid (cfv X cbs))) $;

--| The Hom-set operation in the category of extensible structures (in a universe) is a function. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrchomfn (ph: wff) (C U H V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp U U)) $;

--| The functionalized Hom-set operation equals the Hom-set operation in the category of extensible structures (in a universe). (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrchomfeqhom (ph: wff) (C U H V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C chomf) H) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ estrres . (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem estrreslem1 (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv C cbs)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ estrres . (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2020.)
theorem estrreslem2 (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x H V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_x Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv cnx cbs) (cdm C)) $;

--| Any restriction of a category (as an extensible structure which is an unordered triple of ordered pairs) is an unordered triple of ordered pairs. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2020.) (Revised by AV, 3-Jul-2022.)
theorem estrres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C c_x G H V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_x Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C A2 cress) (cop (cfv cnx chom) G) csts) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) G) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C S U E F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv X cbs)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C S U E F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wcel (cfv X F) U) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (S U E F: class x3):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C F) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1) (M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2))) (wceq (co X Y G) (cres cid (co N M cmap))) $;

--| Lemma 6 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem6 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G H: class x3 y1) (M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel H (co N M cmap))) (wceq (cfv H (co X Y G)) H) $;

--| Lemma 7 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X (cfv E ccid)) (co X X G)) (cfv (cfv X F) (cfv S ccid))) $;

--| Lemma 8 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2))) (wf (co X Y (cfv E chom)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) (cfv S chom)) (co X Y G)) $;

--| Lemma 9 for ~ funcestrcsetc . (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetclem9 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G H K: class x3 y1) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wa (wcel H (co X Y (cfv E chom))) (wcel K (co Y Z (cfv E chom))))) (wceq (cfv (co K H (co (cop X Y) Z (cfv E cco))) (co X Z G)) (co (cfv K (co Y Z G)) (cfv H (co X Y G)) (co (cop (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)) (cfv Z F) (cfv S cco)))) $;

--| The "natural forgetful functor" from the category of extensible structures into the category of sets which sends each extensible structure to its base set, preserving the morphisms as mappings between the corresponding base sets. (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcestrcsetc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co E S cfunc)) $;

--| The "natural forgetful functor" from the category of extensible structures into the category of sets which sends each extensible structure to its base set is faithful. (Contributed by AV, 2-Apr-2020.)
theorem fthestrcsetc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co E S cfth)) $;

--| The "natural forgetful functor" from the category of extensible structures into the category of sets which sends each extensible structure to its base set is full. (Contributed by AV, 2-Apr-2020.)
theorem fullestrcsetc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (S U E F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co E S cful)) $;

--| The "natural forgetful functor" from the category of extensible structures into the category of sets which sends each extensible structure to its base set is an equivalence. According to definition 3.33 (1) of [Adamek] p. 36, "A functor F : A -> B is called an _equivalence_ provided that it is full, faithful, and isomorphism-dense in the sense that for any B-object B' there exists some A-object A' such that F(A') is isomorphic to B'.". Therefore, the category of sets and the category of extensible structures are equivalent, according to definition 3.33 (2) of [Adamek] p. 36, "Categories A and B are called _equivalent_ provided that there is an equivalence from A to B.". (Contributed by AV, 2-Apr-2020.)
theorem equivestrcsetc {x3 y1 i a b: setvar} (ph: wff i) (B2: class i) (C: class y1 i b) (S: class x3 y1 i) (U: class x3 y1 i a b) (E: class x3 y1 i) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class x3 y1 i):
  $ wceq E (cfv U cestrc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv E cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cfv (cv y1) cbs) (cfv (cv x3) cbs) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv cnx cbs) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wbr F G (co E S cfth)) (wbr F G (co E S cful)) (wral b (wrex a (wex i (wf1o (cv b) (cfv (cv a) F) (cv i))) B2) C)) $;

--| A constructed one-slot structure with the objects of the category of sets as base set in a weak universe. (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.)
theorem setc1strwun (ph: wff) (C S U X: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel com U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X C)) (wcel (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) X)) U) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ funcsetcestrc . (Contributed by AV, 27-Mar-2020.)
theorem funcsetcestrclem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (S U F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 C (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv x3))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X C)) (wceq (cfv X F) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cbs) X))) $;

