  $ wceq I (cfv R cinv) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpo f2 x3 (co O S cfunc) B2 (cmpt u (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cmpt y1 B2 (cmpt g2 (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv C chom)) (cfv (cv u) (cfv (cv g2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd)))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N (co Z E I)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ yonffth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem yonffthlem {x3 y1 u f2 g2 a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (Q: class y1) (R: class x3 y1 f2 g2 a) (S: class) (T: class x3 a) (U: class x3 y1 u f2 g2 a) (c_1: class u) (E: class x3) (H I: class x3 y1 u f2 g2 a) (M: class x3 f2 a) (N: class x3 y1 u f2 g2) (O: class) (V W: class x3 y1 u f2 g2 a) (Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cyon) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv V csetc) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co O S cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv Q chof) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q O cxpc) T cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq E (co O S cevlf) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co H (co (co (cop (cfv Y c1st) (ctpos (cfv Y c2nd))) (co Q O c2ndf) ccofu) (co Q O c1stf) cprf) ccofu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cfv C chomf)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun (crn (cfv Q chomf)) U) V) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo f2 x3 (co O S cfunc) B2 (cmpt a (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv Y c1st)) (cv f2) (co O S cnat)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))))) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinv) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpo f2 x3 (co O S cfunc) B2 (cmpt u (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) c1st)) (cmpt y1 B2 (cmpt g2 (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv C chom)) (cfv (cv u) (cfv (cv g2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd)))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cin (co C Q cful) (co C Q cfth))) $;

--| The Yoneda Lemma. There is a natural isomorphism between the functors ` Z ` and ` E ` , where ` Z ( F , X ) ` is the natural transformations from ` Yon ( X ) = Hom ( - , X ) ` to ` F ` , and ` E ( F , X ) = F ( X ) ` is the evaluation functor. Here we need two universes to state the claim: the smaller universe ` U ` is used for forming the functor category ` Q = C ` <HTML><sup>op</sup></HTML> ` -> SetCat ( U ) ` , which itself does not (necessarily) live in ` U ` but instead is an element of the larger universe ` V ` . (If ` U ` is a Grothendieck universe, then it will be closed under this "presheaf" operation, and so we can set ` U = V ` in this case.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem yoneda {x3 f2 a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C Q: class) (R: class x3 f2 a) (S: class) (T: class x3 a) (U: class x3 f2 a) (c_1: class) (E: class x3) (H I M: class x3 f2 a) (O: class) (V W: class x3 f2 a) (Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cyon) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv V csetc) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co O S cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv Q chof) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q O cxpc) T cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq E (co O S cevlf) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co H (co (co (cop (cfv Y c1st) (ctpos (cfv Y c2nd))) (co Q O c2ndf) ccofu) (co Q O c1stf) cprf) ccofu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cfv C chomf)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun (crn (cfv Q chomf)) U) V) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo f2 x3 (co O S cfunc) B2 (cmpt a (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv Y c1st)) (cv f2) (co O S cnat)) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c_1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv a))))) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co Z E I)) $;

--| The Yoneda Lemma. The Yoneda embedding, the curried Hom functor, is full and faithful, and hence is a representation of the category ` C ` as a full subcategory of the category ` Q ` of presheaves on ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2017.)
theorem yonffth (ph: wff) (C Q S U O V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cyon) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co O S cfuc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C ccat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cfv C chomf)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cin (co C Q cful) (co C Q cfth))) $;

--| If the codomain is recoverable from a hom-set, then the Yoneda embedding is injective on objects, and hence is an isomorphism from ` C ` into a full subcategory of a presheaf category. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem yoniso {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class) (D Q S U E: class x3 y1) (F: class x3) (I O V W X: class x3 y1) (Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cyon) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv C coppc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv V ccatc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv D ciso) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co O S cfuc) $ >
  $ wceq E (co Q (crn (cfv Y c1st)) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cfv C chomf)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cbs)) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv C cbs)))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv C chom)) F) (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co C E I)) $;

--| Class function defining dual orders.
term codu: class;

--| Define the dual of an ordered structure, which replaces the order component of the structure with its reverse. See ~ odubas , ~ oduleval , and ~ oduleg for its principal properties. _EDITORIAL_: likely usable to simplify many lattice proofs, as it allows for duality arguments to be formalized; for instance ~ latmass . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_odu {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq codu (cmpt w cvv (co (cv w) (cop (cfv cnx cple) (ccnv (cfv (cv w) cple))) csts)) $;

--| Value of an order dual structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduval (D c_le O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv O cple) $ >
  $ wceq D (co O (cop (cfv cnx cple) (ccnv c_le)) csts) $;

--| Value of the less-equal relation in an order dual structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduleval (D c_le O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv O cple) $ >
  $ wceq (ccnv c_le) (cfv D cple) $;

--| Truth of the less-equal relation in an order dual structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduleg (A2 B2 D G c_le O V W: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv O cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 G) (wbr B2 A2 c_le)) $;

--| Base set of an order dual structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem odubas (B2 D O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv O cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv D cbs) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all prosets.
term cproset: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all directed sets.
term cdrs: class;

--| Define the class of preordered sets, or prosets. A proset is a set equipped with a preorder, that is, a transitive and reflexive relation. Preorders are a natural generalization of partial orders which need not be antisymmetric: there may be pairs of elements such that each is "less than or equal to" the other, so that both elements have the same order-theoretic properties (in some sense, there is a "tie" among them). If a preorder is required to be antisymmetric, that is, there is no such "tie", then one obtains a partial order. If a preorder is required to be symmetric, that is, all comparable elements are tied, then one obtains an equivalence relation. Every preorder naturally factors into these two notions: the "tie" relation on a proset is an equivalence relation, and the quotient under that equivalence relation is a partial order. (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_proset {x3 y1 z f2 r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cproset (cab f2 (wsbc b (wsbc r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv r)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv r))) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) (cv r)))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv f2) cple)) (cfv (cv f2) cbs))) $;

--| Define the class of directed sets. A directed set is a nonempty preordered set where every pair of elements have some upper bound. Note that it is not required that there exist a _least_ upper bound. There is no consensus in the literature over whether directed sets are allowed to be empty. It is slightly more convenient for us if they are not. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_drs {x3 y1 z f2 r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdrs (crab f2 (wsbc b (wsbc r (wa (wne (cv b) c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv r))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b))) (cfv (cv f2) cple)) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) cproset) $;

--| Property of being a preordered set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem isprs {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cproset) (wa (wcel K cvv) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_le) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le))) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prsref and ~ prstr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem prslem (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cproset) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wa (wbr X X c_le) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr X Z c_le))) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" is reflexive in a proset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem prsref (B2 K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cproset) (wcel X B2)) (wbr X X c_le) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" is transitive in a proset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem prstr (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cproset) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le))) (wbr X Z c_le) $;

--| Being a proset is a self-dual property. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem oduprs (D K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv K codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wcel D cproset) $;

--| Property of being a directed set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem isdrs {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cdrs) (w3a (wcel K cproset) (wne B2 c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| Direction of a directed set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem drsdir {z: setvar} (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cdrs) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wrex z (wa (wbr X (cv z) c_le) (wbr Y (cv z) c_le)) B2) $;

--| A directed set is a proset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem drsprs (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cdrs) (wcel K cproset) $;

--| The base of a directed set is not empty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem drsbn0 (B2 K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cdrs) (wne B2 c0) $;

--| Any _finite_ number of elements in a directed set have a common upper bound. Here is where the nonemptiness constraint in ~ df-drs first comes into play; without it we would need an additional constraint that ` X ` not be empty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem drsdirfi {y1 z: setvar} (B2 K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cdrs) (wss X B2) (wcel X cfn)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) X) B2) $;

--| Directed sets may be defined in terms of finite subsets. Again, without nonemptiness we would need to restrict to nonempty subsets here. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem isdrs2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cdrs) (wa (wcel K cproset) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) (cv x3)) B2) (cin (cpw B2) cfn))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of posets.
term cpo: class;

--| Extend class notation with less-than for posets.
term cplt: class;

--| Extend class notation with poset least upper bound.
term club: class;

--| Extend class notation with poset greatest lower bound.
term cglb: class;

--| Extend class notation with poset join.
term cjn: class;

--| Extend class notation with poset meet.
term cmee: class;

--| Define the class of partially ordered sets (posets). A poset is a set equipped with a partial order, that is, a binary relation which is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive. Unlike a total order, in a partial order there may be pairs of elements where neither precedes the other. Definition of poset in [Crawley] p. 1. Note that Crawley-Dilworth require that a poset base set be nonempty, but we follow the convention of most authors who don't make this a requirement. In our formalism of extensible structures, the base set of a poset ` f ` is denoted by ` ( Base `` f ) ` and its partial order by ` ( le `` f ) ` (for "less than or equal to"). The quantifiers ` E. b E. r ` provide a notational shorthand to allow to refer to the base and ordering relation as ` b ` and ` r ` in the definition rather than having to repeat ` ( Base `` f ) ` and ` ( le `` f ) ` throughout. These quantifiers can be eliminated with ~ ceqsex2v and related theorems. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.)
axiom df_poset {x3 y1 z f2 r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpo (cab f2 (wex b (wex r (w3a (wceq (cv b) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) (wceq (cv r) (cfv (cv f2) cple)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (w3a (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv r)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv r))) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) (cv r)))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)))))) $;

--| The predicate "is a poset". (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Nov-2013.)
theorem ispos {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2: class) (K: class x3 y1 z) (c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cpo) (wa (wcel K cvv) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (w3a (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_le) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le))) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| A poset is an antisymmetric proset. _EDITORIAL_: could become the definition of poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem ispos2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cpo) (wa (wcel K cproset) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) B2)) $;

--| A poset is a proset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem posprs (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cpo) (wcel K cproset) $;

--| Lemma for poset properties. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2011.)
theorem posi (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (w3a (wbr X X c_le) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y X c_le)) (wceq X Y)) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr X Z c_le))) $;

--| A poset ordering is reflexive. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2011.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem posref (B2 K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2)) (wbr X X c_le) $;

--| A poset ordering is asymmetric. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem posasymb (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y X c_le)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| A poset ordering is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2011.)
theorem postr (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr X Z c_le)) $;

--| Technical lemma to simplify the statement of ~ ipopos . The empty set is (rather pathologically) a poset under our definitions, since it has an empty base set ( ~ str0 ) and any relation partially orders an empty set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2024.)
theorem _0pos:
  $ wcel c0 cpo $;

--| Properties that determine a poset (implicit structure version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 26-Apr-2024.)
theorem isposd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_le (cfv K cple)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_le) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $;

--| Properties that determine a poset (implicit structure version). (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2011.)
theorem isposi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2: class) (K: class x3 y1 z) (c_le: class):
  $ wcel K cvv $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_le) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) $ >
  $ wcel K cpo $;

--| Properties that determine a poset (explicit structure version). Note that the numeric indices of the structure components are not mentioned explicitly in either the theorem or its proof. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2012.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Oct-2024.)
theorem isposix {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2: class) (K: class x3 y1 z) (c_le: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel c_le cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) c_le) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) $ >
  $ wcel K cpo $;

--| Posethood is determined only by structure components and only by the value of the relation within the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem pospropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cple)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cple))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cpo) (wcel L cpo)) $;

--| Being a poset is a self-dual property. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odupos (D O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O cpo) (wcel D cpo) $;

--| Being a poset is a self-dual property. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduposb (D O V: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wb (wcel O cpo) (wcel D cpo)) $;

--| Define less-than ordering for posets and related structures. Unlike ~ df-base and ~ df-ple , this is a derived component extractor and not an extensible structure component extractor that defines the poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_plt {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cplt (cmpt p cvv (cdif (cfv (cv p) cple) cid)) $;

--| Value of the less-than relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem pltfval (A2 c_lt K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K A2) (wceq c_lt (cdif c_le cid)) $;

--| Less-than relation. ( ~ df-pss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2011.)
theorem pltval (A2 B2 C c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K A2) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y C)) (wb (wbr X Y c_lt) (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wne X Y))) $;

--| "Less than" implies "less than or equal to". ( ~ pssss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2011.)
theorem pltle (A2 B2 C c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K A2) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y C)) (wi (wbr X Y c_lt) (wbr X Y c_le)) $;

--| The "less than" relation is not reflexive. ( ~ df-pss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2011.)
theorem pltne (A2 B2 C c_lt K X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K A2) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y C)) (wi (wbr X Y c_lt) (wne X Y)) $;

--| The "less than" relation is not reflexive. ( ~ pssirr analog.) (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2012.)
theorem pltirr (A2 B2 c_lt K X: class):
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K A2) (wcel X B2)) (wn (wbr X X c_lt)) $;

--| One direction of ~ pleval2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem pleval2i (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wi (wbr X Y c_le) (wo (wbr X Y c_lt) (wceq X Y))) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" in terms of "less than". ( ~ sspss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem pleval2 (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wo (wbr X Y c_lt) (wceq X Y))) $;

--| "Less than" implies not converse "less than or equal to". (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2011.)
theorem pltnle (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr X Y c_lt)) (wn (wbr Y X c_le)) $;

--| Alternate expression for the "less than" relation. ( ~ dfpss3 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2011.)
theorem pltval3 (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_lt) (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wn (wbr Y X c_le)))) $;

--| The less-than relation implies the negation of its inverse. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2011.)
theorem pltnlt (B2 c_lt K X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr X Y c_lt)) (wn (wbr Y X c_lt)) $;

--| The less-than relation has no 2-cycle loops. ( ~ pssn2lp analog.) (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2011.)
theorem pltn2lp (B2 c_lt K X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wn (wa (wbr X Y c_lt) (wbr Y X c_lt))) $;

--| The less-than relation is transitive. ( ~ psstr analog.) (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2011.)
theorem plttr (B2 c_lt K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_lt) (wbr Y Z c_lt)) (wbr X Z c_lt)) $;

--| Transitive law for chained "less than" and "less than or equal to". ( ~ psssstr analog.) (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2011.)
theorem pltletr (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_lt) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr X Z c_lt)) $;

--| Transitive law for chained "less than or equal to" and "less than". ( ~ sspsstr analog.) (Contributed by NM, 2-May-2012.)
theorem plelttr (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_lt)) (wbr X Z c_lt)) $;

--| Write a poset structure in terms of the proper-class poset predicate (strict less than version). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem pospo (B2 c_lt K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wb (wcel K cpo) (wa (wpo B2 c_lt) (wss (cres cid B2) c_le))) $;

--| Define the least upper bound (LUB) of a set of (poset) elements. The domain is restricted to exclude sets ` s ` for which the LUB doesn't exist uniquely. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_lub {x3 y1 z s1 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq club (cmpt p cvv (cres (cmpt s1 (cpw (cfv (cv p) cbs)) (crio x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) cple))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cab s1 (wreu x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) cple))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))))) $;

--| Define the greatest lower bound (GLB) of a set of (poset) elements. The domain is restricted to exclude sets ` s ` for which the GLB doesn't exist uniquely. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_glb {x3 y1 z s1 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cglb (cmpt p cvv (cres (cmpt s1 (cpw (cfv (cv p) cbs)) (crio x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) (cfv (cv p) cple))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cab s1 (wreu x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) (cfv (cv p) cple)) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) (cfv (cv p) cple))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (cfv (cv p) cbs))))) $;

--| Define poset join. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2015.)
axiom df_join {x3 y1 z p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cjn (cmpt p cvv (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) club)))) $;

--| Define poset meet. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 8-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_meet {x3 y1 z p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmee (cmpt p cvv (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cfv (cv p) cglb)))) $;

--| Value of the least upper bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubfval {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z s1) (B2: class y1) (U: class x3 y1 z s1) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cres (cmpt s1 (cpw B2) (crio x3 ps B2)) (cab s1 (wreu x3 ps B2)))) $;

--| Domain of the least upper bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubdm {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z s1) (B2: class y1) (U: class x3 y1 z s1) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm U) (crab s1 (wreu x3 ps B2) (cpw B2))) $;

--| The LUB is a function. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubfun (U K: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wfun U $;

--| Member of the domain of the least upper bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubeldm {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (U: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S (cdm U)) (wa (wss S B2) (wreu x3 ps B2))) $;

--| A member of the domain of the least upper bound function is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubelss (ph: wff) (B2 S U K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $;

--| Unique existence proper of a member of the domain of the least upper bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubeu {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (U: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 ps B2) $;

--| Value of the least upper bound function of a poset. Out-of-domain arguments (those not satisfying ` S e. dom U ` ) are allowed for convenience, evaluating to the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (U: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S U) (crio x3 ps B2)) $;

--| The least upper bound function value belongs to the base set. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubcl (ph: wff) (B2 S U K V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S U) B2) $;

--| Properties of greatest lower bound of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubprop {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S U K: class) (c_le: class z) (V: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cfv S U) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cfv S U) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| The greatest lower bound is the least element. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem luble (ph: wff) (B2 S U K c_le V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (cfv S U) c_le) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lublecl and ~ lubid . (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2018.)
theorem lublecllem {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (x3: setvar) (B2: class) (U: class y1 z w) (K: class y1) (c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wb (wa (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le) (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) X c_le) B2)) (wral w (wi (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv w) c_le) (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) X c_le) B2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv w) c_le)) B2)) (wceq (cv x3) X)) $;

--| The set of all elements less than a given element has an LUB. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2018.)
theorem lublecl {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2: class) (U K: class y1) (c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) X c_le) B2) (cdm U)) $;

--| The LUB of elements less than or equal to a fixed value equals that value. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem lubid {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2: class) (U K: class y1) (c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) X c_le) B2) U) X) $;

--| Value of the greatest lower function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbfval {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z s1) (B2: class y1) (G: class x3 y1 z s1) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cres (cmpt s1 (cpw B2) (crio x3 ps B2)) (cab s1 (wreu x3 ps B2)))) $;

--| Domain of the greatest lower bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbdm {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z s1) (B2: class y1) (G: class x3 y1 z s1) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (cv s1)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) (cv s1)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm G) (crab s1 (wreu x3 ps B2) (cpw B2))) $;

--| The GLB is a function. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbfun (G K: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wfun G $;

--| Member of the domain of the greatest lower bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbeldm {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S (cdm G)) (wa (wss S B2) (wreu x3 ps B2))) $;

--| A member of the domain of the greatest lower bound function is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbelss (ph: wff) (B2 S G K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $;

--| Unique existence proper of a member of the domain of the greatest lower bound function of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbeu {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 ps B2) $;

--| Value of the greatest lower bound function of a poset. Out-of-domain arguments (those not satisfying ` S e. dom U ` ) are allowed for convenience, evaluating to the empty set on both sides of the equality. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (c_le V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S G) (crio x3 ps B2)) $;

--| The least upper bound function value belongs to the base set. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbcl (ph: wff) (B2 S G K V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S G) B2) $;

--| Properties of greatest lower bound of a poset. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glbprop {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (B2: class y1) (S U K: class) (c_le: class z) (V: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cfv S U) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cfv S U) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| The greatest lower bound is the least element. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem glble (ph: wff) (B2 S U K c_le V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv S U) X c_le) $;

--| Value of join function for a poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.) TODO: prove ~ joinfval2 first to reduce net proof size (existence part)?
theorem joinfval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (U: class x3 y1) (c_or: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_or (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) U))) $;

--| Value of join function for a poset-type structure. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem joinfval2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (U: class x3 y1) (c_or: class x3 y1 z) (K: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_or (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cdm U)) (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) U))))) $;

--| Domain of join function for a poset-type structure. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2018.)
theorem joindm {x3 y1: setvar} (U c_or: class x3 y1) (K: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq (cdm c_or) (copab x3 y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cdm U)))) $;

--| Two ways to say that a join is defined. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem joindef (ph: wff) (U c_or K V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) (wcel (cpr X Y) (cdm U))) $;

--| Join value. Since both sides evaluate to ` (/) ` when they don't exist, for convenience we drop the ` { X , Y } e. dom U ` requirement. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem joinval (ph: wff) (U c_or K V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_or) (cfv (cpr X Y) U)) $;

--| Closure of join of elements in the domain. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem joincl (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_or) B2) $;

--| Subset property of domain of join. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem joindmss (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm c_or) (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ joinval2 and ~ joineu . (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.) TODO: combine this through ~ joineu into ~ joinlem ?
theorem joinval2lem {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (x3: setvar) (B2 c_or: class y1) (K: class) (c_le: class z) (V: class y1 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) (cpr X Y)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) (cpr X Y)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) (wa (wa (wbr X (cv x3) c_le) (wbr Y (cv x3) c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr X (cv z) c_le) (wbr Y (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2))) $;

--| Value of join for a poset with LUB expanded. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem joinval2 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (B2 c_or K: class) (c_le V: class x3 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_or) (crio x3 (wa (wa (wbr X (cv x3) c_le) (wbr Y (cv x3) c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr X (cv z) c_le) (wbr Y (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) B2)) $;

--| Uniqueness of join of elements in the domain. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem joineu {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (B2 c_or K: class) (c_le V: class x3 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wa (wa (wbr X (cv x3) c_le) (wbr Y (cv x3) c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr X (cv z) c_le) (wbr Y (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) B2) $;

--| Lemma for join properties. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem joinlem {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (B2 c_or K: class) (c_le V: class z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wbr X (co X Y c_or) c_le) (wbr Y (co X Y c_or) c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr X (cv z) c_le) (wbr Y (cv z) c_le)) (wbr (co X Y c_or) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| A join's first argument is less than or equal to the join. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.)
theorem lejoin1 (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K c_le V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (co X Y c_or) c_le) $;

--| A join's second argument is less than or equal to the join. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.)
theorem lejoin2 (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K c_le V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y (co X Y c_or) c_le) $;

--| A join is less than or equal to a third value iff each argument is less than or equal to the third value. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.)
theorem joinle (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wbr X Z c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr (co X Y c_or) Z c_le)) $;

--| Value of meet function for a poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.) TODO: prove ~ meetfval2 first to reduce net proof size (existence part)?
theorem meetfval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (G: class x3 y1) (K: class) (c_an V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_an (coprab x3 y1 z (wbr (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) G))) $;

--| Value of meet function for a poset. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetfval2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (G: class x3 y1) (K: class) (c_an V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_an (coprab x3 y1 z (wa (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cdm G)) (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) G))))) $;

--| Domain of meet function for a poset-type structure. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetdm {x3 y1: setvar} (G: class x3 y1) (K: class) (c_an V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq (cdm c_an) (copab x3 y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cdm G)))) $;

--| Two ways to say that a meet is defined. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetdef (ph: wff) (G K c_an V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) (wcel (cpr X Y) (cdm G))) $;

--| Meet value. Since both sides evaluate to ` (/) ` when they don't exist, for convenience we drop the ` { X , Y } e. dom G ` requirement. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetval (ph: wff) (G K c_an V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_an) (cfv (cpr X Y) G)) $;

--| Closure of meet of elements in the domain. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetcl (ph: wff) (B2 K c_an V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_an) B2) $;

--| Subset property of domain of meet. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetdmss (ph: wff) (B2 K c_an V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm c_an) (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ meetval2 and ~ meeteu . (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.) TODO: combine this through ~ meeteu into ~ meetlem ?
theorem meetval2lem {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (x3: setvar) (B2: class y1) (K: class) (c_le: class z) (c_an: class y1) (V: class y1 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (cpr X Y)) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) (cpr X Y)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) (wa (wa (wbr (cv x3) X c_le) (wbr (cv x3) Y c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr (cv z) X c_le) (wbr (cv z) Y c_le)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2))) $;

--| Value of meet for a poset with LUB expanded. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetval2 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (B2 K: class) (c_le: class x3 z) (c_an: class) (V: class x3 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_an) (crio x3 (wa (wa (wbr (cv x3) X c_le) (wbr (cv x3) Y c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr (cv z) X c_le) (wbr (cv z) Y c_le)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) B2)) $;

--| Uniqueness of meet of elements in the domain. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meeteu {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (B2 K: class) (c_le: class x3 z) (c_an: class) (V: class x3 z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wa (wa (wbr (cv x3) X c_le) (wbr (cv x3) Y c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr (cv z) X c_le) (wbr (cv z) Y c_le)) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) B2) $;

--| Lemma for meet properties. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetlem {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (B2 K: class) (c_le: class z) (c_an: class) (V: class z) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wbr (co X Y c_an) X c_le) (wbr (co X Y c_an) Y c_le)) (wral z (wi (wa (wbr (cv z) X c_le) (wbr (cv z) Y c_le)) (wbr (cv z) (co X Y c_an) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| A meet's first argument is less than or equal to the meet. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem lemeet1 (ph: wff) (B2 K c_le c_an V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co X Y c_an) X c_le) $;

--| A meet's second argument is less than or equal to the meet. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem lemeet2 (ph: wff) (B2 K c_le c_an V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co X Y c_an) Y c_le) $;

--| A meet is less than or equal to a third value iff each argument is less than or equal to the third value. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetle (ph: wff) (B2 K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wbr Z X c_le) (wbr Z Y c_le)) (wbr Z (co X Y c_an) c_le)) $;

--| The join of a poset is commutative. (This may not be a theorem under other definitions of meet.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem joincomALT (B2 c_or K V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K V) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_or) (co Y X c_or)) $;

--| The join of a poset is commutative. (The antecedent ` <. X , Y >. e. dom .\/ /\ <. Y , X >. e. dom .\/ ` i.e., "the joins exist" could be omitted as an artifact of our particular join definition, but other definitions may require it.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem joincom (B2 c_or K X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) (wcel (cop Y X) (cdm c_or)))) (wceq (co X Y c_or) (co Y X c_or)) $;

--| The meet of a poset is commutative. (This may not be a theorem under other definitions of meet.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem meetcomALT (B2 K c_an V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K V) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_an) (co Y X c_an)) $;

--| The meet of a poset is commutative. (The antecedent ` <. X , Y >. e. dom ./\ /\ <. Y , X >. e. dom ./\ ` i.e., "the meets exist" could be omitted as an artifact of our particular join definition, but other definitions may require it.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem meetcom (B2 K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel K cpo) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an)) (wcel (cop Y X) (cdm c_an)))) (wceq (co X Y c_an) (co Y X c_an)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ odumeet . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem join0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cjn) c0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ odujoin . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.) TODO ( ~ df-riota update): This proof increased from 152 bytes to 547 bytes after the ~ df-riota change. Any way to shorten it? ~ join0 also.
theorem meet0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cmee) c0 $;

--| Least upper bounds in a dual order are greatest lower bounds in the original order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odulub (D L O V: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv O cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq L (cfv D club)) $;

--| Joins in a dual order are meets in the original. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odujoin (D c_an O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv O cmee) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv D cjn) $;

--| Greatest lower bounds in a dual order are least upper bounds in the original order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduglb (D U O V: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv O club) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq U (cfv D cglb)) $;

--| Meets in a dual order are joins in the original. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odumeet (D c_or O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv O cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv D cmee) $;

--| Least upper bounds in a poset are unique if they exist. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) (Revised by NM, 16-Jun-2017.)
theorem poslubmo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 S K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (wss S B2)) (wrmo x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) B2) $;

--| Greatest lower bounds in a poset are unique if they exist. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2018.)
theorem posglbmo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 S K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cpo) (wss S B2)) (wrmo x3 (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) c_le)) B2)) B2) $;

--| Properties which determine the least upper bound in a poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem poslubd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 S T U K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wbr (cv x3) T c_le) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S)) (wbr T (cv y1) c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S U) T) $;

--| Properties which determine the least upper bound in a poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem poslubdg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 S T U K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cfv K club)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wbr (cv x3) T c_le) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S)) (wbr T (cv y1) c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S U) T) $;

--| Properties which determine the greatest lower bound in a poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem posglbdg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 S T G K c_le: class):
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cfv K cglb)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wbr T (cv x3) c_le) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le) S)) (wbr (cv y1) T c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S G) T) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all tosets.
term ctos: class;

--| Define the class of totally ordered sets (tosets). (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_toset {x3 y1 f2 r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctos (crab f2 (wsbc b (wsbc r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv f2) cple)) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) cpo) $;

--| The predicate "is a toset". (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem istos {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (K: class x3 y1) (c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K ctos) (wa (wcel K cpo) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) B2) B2)) $;

--| Write the totally ordered set structure predicate in terms of the proper class strict order predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem tosso (B2 c_lt K c_le V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wb (wcel K ctos) (wa (wor B2 c_lt) (wss (cres cid B2) c_le))) $;

--| A Toset is a Poset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jan-2018.)
theorem tospos (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F ctos) (wcel F cpo) $;

--| In a Toset, any two elements are comparable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem tleile (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ctos) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wo (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y X c_le)) $;

--| In a Toset, "less than" is equivalent to the negation of the converse of "less than or equal to", see ~ pltnle . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem tltnle (B2 c_lt K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ctos) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_lt) (wn (wbr Y X c_le))) $;

--| Extend class notation with poset zero.
term cp0: class;

--| Extend class notation with poset unit.
term cp1: class;

--| Define poset zero. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2011.)
axiom df_p0 {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cp0 (cmpt p cvv (cfv (cfv (cv p) cbs) (cfv (cv p) cglb))) $;

--| Define poset unit. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.)
axiom df_p1 {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cp1 (cmpt p cvv (cfv (cfv (cv p) cbs) (cfv (cv p) club))) $;

--| Value of poset zero. (Contributed by NM, 12-Oct-2011.)
theorem p0val (B2 G K V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv K cp0) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_0 (cfv B2 G)) $;

--| Value of poset zero. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem p1val (B2 U c_1 K V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv K cp1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wceq c_1 (cfv B2 U)) $;

--| Any element is less than or equal to a poset's upper bound (if defined). (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem p0le (ph: wff) (B2 G K c_le V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv K cp0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c_0 X c_le) $;

--| Any element is less than or equal to a poset's upper bound (if defined). (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.) (Revised by NM, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem ple1 (ph: wff) (B2 U c_1 K c_le V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv K cp1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X c_1 c_le) $;

--| The restriction of a Poset is a Poset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jan-2018.)
theorem resspos (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F cpo) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co F A2 cress) cpo) $;

--| The restriction of a Toset is a Toset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jan-2018.)
theorem resstos (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F ctos) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co F A2 cress) ctos) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all lattices.
term clat: class;

--| Define the class of all lattices. A lattice is a poset in which the join and meet of any two elements always exists. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_lat {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq clat (crab p (wa (wceq (cdm (cfv (cv p) cjn)) (cxp (cfv (cv p) cbs) (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (wceq (cdm (cfv (cv p) cmee)) (cxp (cfv (cv p) cbs) (cfv (cv p) cbs)))) cpo) $;

--| The predicate "is a lattice". (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem islat (B2 c_or K c_an: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K clat) (wa (wcel K cpo) (wa (wceq (cdm c_or) (cxp B2 B2)) (wceq (cdm c_an) (cxp B2 B2)))) $;

--| Being a lattice is self-dual. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odulatb (D O V: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wb (wcel O clat) (wcel D clat)) $;

--| Being a lattice is self-dual. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem odulat (D O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O clat) (wcel D clat) $;

--| The join and meet of any two elements exist. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem latcl2 (ph: wff) (B2 c_or K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K clat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_or)) (wcel (cop X Y) (cdm c_an))) $;

--| Lemma for lattice properties. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem latlem (B2 c_or K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel (co X Y c_or) B2) (wcel (co X Y c_an) B2)) $;

--| A lattice is a poset. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem latpos (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K clat) (wcel K cpo) $;

--| Closure of join operation in a lattice. ( ~ chjcom analog.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem latjcl (B2 c_or K X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_or) B2) $;

--| Closure of meet operation in a lattice. ( ~ incom analog.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem latmcl (B2 K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_an) B2) $;

--| A lattice ordering is reflexive. ( ~ ssid analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2011.)
theorem latref (B2 K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2)) (wbr X X c_le) $;

--| A lattice ordering is asymmetric. ( ~ eqss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2011.)
theorem latasymb (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y X c_le)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| A lattice ordering is asymmetric. ( ~ eqss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2011.)
theorem latasym (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y X c_le)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| A lattice ordering is transitive. ( ~ sstr analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Nov-2011.)
theorem lattr (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr X Z c_le)) $;

--| Deduce equality from lattice ordering. ( ~ eqssd analog.) (Contributed by NM, 18-Nov-2011.)
theorem latasymd (ph: wff) (B2 K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K clat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y X c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X Y) $;

--| A lattice ordering is transitive. Deduction version of ~ lattr . (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2012.)
theorem lattrd (ph: wff) (B2 K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K clat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y Z c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Z c_le) $;

--| The join of a lattice commutes. ( ~ chjcom analog.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2011.)
theorem latjcom (B2 c_or K X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_or) (co Y X c_or)) $;

--| A join's first argument is less than or equal to the join. ( ~ chub1 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem latlej1 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr X (co X Y c_or) c_le) $;

--| A join's second argument is less than or equal to the join. ( ~ chub2 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem latlej2 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr Y (co X Y c_or) c_le) $;

--| A join is less than or equal to a third value iff each argument is less than or equal to the third value. ( ~ chlub analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem latjle12 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wa (wbr X Z c_le) (wbr Y Z c_le)) (wbr (co X Y c_or) Z c_le)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" in terms of join. ( ~ chlejb1 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem latleeqj1 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wceq (co X Y c_or) Y)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" in terms of join. ( ~ chlejb2 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2011.)
theorem latleeqj2 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wceq (co Y X c_or) Y)) $;

--| Add join to both sides of a lattice ordering. ( ~ chlej1i analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latjlej1 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr (co X Z c_or) (co Y Z c_or) c_le)) $;

--| Add join to both sides of a lattice ordering. ( ~ chlej2i analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latjlej2 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr (co Z X c_or) (co Z Y c_or) c_le)) $;

--| Add join to both sides of a lattice ordering. ( ~ chlej12i analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latjlej12 (B2 c_or K c_le W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel Z B2) (wcel W B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Z W c_le)) (wbr (co X Z c_or) (co Y W c_or) c_le)) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-2012.)
theorem latnlej (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wa (wne X Y) (wne X Z)) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2012.)
theorem latnlej1l (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wne X Y) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2012.)
theorem latnlej1r (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wne X Z) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-2012.)
theorem latnlej2 (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wa (wn (wbr X Y c_le)) (wn (wbr X Z c_le))) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2012.)
theorem latnlej2l (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wn (wbr X Y c_le)) $;

--| An idiom to express that a lattice element differs from two others. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2012.)
theorem latnlej2r (B2 c_or K c_le X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wn (wbr X (co Y Z c_or) c_le))) (wn (wbr X Z c_le)) $;

--| Lattice join is idempotent. Analogue of ~ unidm . (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-2011.)
theorem latjidm (B2 c_or K X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X X c_or) X) $;

--| The join of a lattice commutes. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2011.)
theorem latmcom (B2 K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_an) (co Y X c_an)) $;

--| A meet is less than or equal to its first argument. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem latmle1 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr (co X Y c_an) X c_le) $;

--| A meet is less than or equal to its second argument. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem latmle2 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wbr (co X Y c_an) Y c_le) $;

--| An element is less than or equal to a meet iff the element is less than or equal to each argument of the meet. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem latlem12 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr X Z c_le)) (wbr X (co Y Z c_an) c_le)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" in terms of meet. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2011.)
theorem latleeqm1 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wceq (co X Y c_an) X)) $;

--| "Less than or equal to" in terms of meet. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2011.)
theorem latleeqm2 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wceq (co Y X c_an) X)) $;

--| Add meet to both sides of a lattice ordering. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2011.)
theorem latmlem1 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr (co X Z c_an) (co Y Z c_an) c_le)) $;

--| Add meet to both sides of a lattice ordering. ( ~ sslin analog.) (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2011.)
theorem latmlem2 (B2 K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr (co Z X c_an) (co Z Y c_an) c_le)) $;

--| Add join to both sides of a lattice ordering. ( ~ ss2in analog.) (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2011.)
theorem latmlem12 (B2 K c_le c_an W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel Z B2) (wcel W B2))) (wi (wa (wbr X Y c_le) (wbr Z W c_le)) (wbr (co X Z c_an) (co Y W c_an) c_le)) $;

--| Negation of "less than or equal to" expressed in terms of meet and less-than. ( ~ nssinpss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 5-Feb-2012.)
theorem latnlemlt (B2 c_lt K c_le c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wn (wbr X Y c_le)) (wbr (co X Y c_an) X c_lt)) $;

--| Equivalent expressions for "not less than" in a lattice. ( ~ chnle analog.) (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2011.)
theorem latnle (B2 c_lt c_or K c_le X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv K cplt) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wn (wbr Y X c_le)) (wbr X (co X Y c_or) c_lt)) $;

--| Lattice meet is idempotent. Analogue of ~ inidm . (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latmidm (B2 K c_an X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X X c_an) X) $;

--| Lattice absorption law. From definition of lattice in [Kalmbach] p. 14. ( ~ chabs1 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latabs1 (B2 c_or K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X (co X Y c_an) c_or) X) $;

--| Lattice absorption law. From definition of lattice in [Kalmbach] p. 14. ( ~ chabs2 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2011.)
theorem latabs2 (B2 c_or K c_an X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X (co X Y c_or) c_an) X) $;

--| An ortholattice is distributive in one ordering direction. ( ~ ledi analog.) (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2011.)
theorem latledi (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (co (co X Y c_an) (co X Z c_an) c_or) (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_an) c_le) $;

--| Ordering of a meet and join with a common variable. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2012.)
theorem latmlej11 (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (co X Y c_an) (co X Z c_or) c_le) $;

--| Ordering of a meet and join with a common variable. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2012.)
theorem latmlej12 (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (co X Y c_an) (co Z X c_or) c_le) $;

--| Ordering of a meet and join with a common variable. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2012.)
theorem latmlej21 (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (co Y X c_an) (co X Z c_or) c_le) $;

--| Ordering of a meet and join with a common variable. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2012.)
theorem latmlej22 (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (co Y X c_an) (co Z X c_or) c_le) $;

--| The least upper bound of a singleton. ( ~ chsupsn analog.) (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2011.)
theorem lubsn (B2 U K X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (csn X) U) X) $;

--| Lattice join is associative. Lemma 2.2 in [MegPav2002] p. 362. ( ~ chjass analog.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2011.)
theorem latjass (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_or) (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Swap 1st and 2nd members of lattice join. ( ~ chj12 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2012.)
theorem latj12 (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_or) (co Y (co X Z c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Swap 2nd and 3rd members of lattice join. Lemma 2.2 in [MegPav2002] p. 362. (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2011.)
theorem latj32 (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_or) (co (co X Z c_or) Y c_or)) $;

--| Swap 1st and 3rd members of lattice join. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2012.)
theorem latj13 (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_or) (co Z (co Y X c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Swap 2nd and 3rd members of lattice join. Lemma 2.2 in [MegPav2002] p. 362. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jun-2012.)
theorem latj31 (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_or) (co (co Z Y c_or) X c_or)) $;

--| Rotate lattice join of 3 classes. (Contributed by NM, 23-Jul-2012.)
theorem latjrot (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_or) (co (co Z X c_or) Y c_or)) $;

--| Rearrangement of lattice join of 4 classes. ( ~ chj4 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Jun-2012.)
theorem latj4 (B2 c_or K W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel Z B2) (wcel W B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) (co Z W c_or) c_or) (co (co X Z c_or) (co Y W c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Rotate lattice join of 4 classes. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jul-2012.)
theorem latj4rot (B2 c_or K W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K clat) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wa (wcel Z B2) (wcel W B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) (co Z W c_or) c_or) (co (co W X c_or) (co Y Z c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Lattice join distributes over itself. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2012.)
theorem latjjdi (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_or) (co (co X Y c_or) (co X Z c_or) c_or)) $;

--| Lattice join distributes over itself. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2012.)
theorem latjjdir (B2 c_or K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_or) (co (co X Z c_or) (co Y Z c_or) c_or)) $;

--| The weak direction of the modular law (e.g., ~ pmod1i , ~ atmod1i1 ) that holds in any lattice. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2012.)
theorem mod1ile (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr X Z c_le) (wbr (co X (co Y Z c_an) c_or) (co (co X Y c_or) Z c_an) c_le)) $;

--| The weak direction of the modular law (e.g., ~ pmod2iN ) that holds in any lattice. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2012.)
theorem mod2ile (B2 c_or K c_le c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wi (wbr Z X c_le) (wbr (co (co X Y c_an) Z c_or) (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_an) c_le)) $;

--| Lattice meet is associative. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem latmass (B2 K c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K clat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_an) Z c_an) (co X (co Y Z c_an) c_an)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ latdisd . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem latdisdlem {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (B2 c_or K c_an: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K clat) (wi (wral u (wral v (wral w (wceq (co (cv u) (co (cv v) (cv w) c_an) c_or) (co (co (cv u) (cv v) c_or) (co (cv u) (cv w) c_or) c_an)) B2) B2) B2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_or) c_an) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_an) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_an) c_or)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| In a lattice, joins distribute over meets if and only if meets distribute over joins; the distributive property is self-dual. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem latdisd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 c_or K c_an: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K clat) (wb (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_an) c_or) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_or) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_or) c_an)) B2) B2) B2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_or) c_an) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_an) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_an) c_or)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| Extend class notation with complete lattices.
term ccla: class;

--| Define the class of all complete lattices, where every subset of the base set has an LUB and a GLB. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_clat {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccla (crab p (wa (wceq (cdm (cfv (cv p) club)) (cpw (cfv (cv p) cbs))) (wceq (cdm (cfv (cv p) cglb)) (cpw (cfv (cv p) cbs)))) cpo) $;

--| The predicate "is a complete lattice". (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2012.) (Revised by NM, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem isclat (B2 U G K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K ccla) (wa (wcel K cpo) (wa (wceq (cdm U) (cpw B2)) (wceq (cdm G) (cpw B2)))) $;

--| A complete lattice is a poset. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2018.)
theorem clatpos (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K ccla) (wcel K cpo) $;

--| Lemma for properties of a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem clatlem (B2 S U G K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wa (wcel (cfv S U) B2) (wcel (cfv S G) B2)) $;

--| Any subset of the base set has an LUB in a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem clatlubcl (B2 S U K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wcel (cfv S U) B2) $;

--| Any subset of the base set has an LUB in a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem clatlubcl2 (B2 S U K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wcel S (cdm U)) $;

--| Any subset of the base set has a GLB in a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2011.)
theorem clatglbcl (B2 S G K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wcel (cfv S G) B2) $;

--| Any subset of the base set has a GLB in a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem clatglbcl2 (B2 S G K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wcel S (cdm G)) $;

--| Being a complete lattice is self-dual. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem oduclatb (D O: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv O codu) $ >
  $ wb (wcel O ccla) (wcel D ccla) $;

--| A complete lattice is a lattice. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2011.) TODO: use ~ eqrelrdv2 to shorten proof and eliminate ~ joindmss and ~ meetdmss ?
theorem clatl (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K ccla) (wcel K clat) $;

--| Properties that determine the greatest lower bound of a complete lattice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2014.)
theorem isglbd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 y1) (H K: class) (c_le: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wbr H (cv y1) c_le) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le) S)) (wbr (cv x3) H c_le) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ccla) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S G) H) $;

--| Lemma for the least upper bound properties in a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2011.)
theorem lublem {y1 z: setvar} (B2: class y1) (S U K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cfv S U) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) c_le) S) (wbr (cfv S U) (cv z) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| The LUB of a complete lattice subset is an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2011.)
theorem lubub (B2 S U K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2) (wcel X S)) (wbr X (cfv S U) c_le) $;

--| The LUB of a complete lattice subset is the least bound. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2011.)
theorem lubl {y1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (S U K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2) (wcel X B2)) (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) X c_le) S) (wbr (cfv S U) X c_le)) $;

--| Subset law for least upper bounds. ( ~ chsupss analog.) (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2011.)
theorem lubss (B2 S T U K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss T B2) (wss S T)) (wbr (cfv S U) (cfv T U) c_le) $;

--| An element of a set is less than or equal to the least upper bound of the set. (Contributed by NM, 21-Oct-2011.)
theorem lubel (B2 S U K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wcel X S) (wss S B2)) (wbr X (cfv S U) c_le) $;

--| The LUB of a union. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2012.)
theorem lubun (B2 S T U c_or K: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv K club) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2) (wss T B2)) (wceq (cfv (cun S T) U) (co (cfv S U) (cfv T U) c_or)) $;

--| Properties of greatest lower bound of a complete lattice. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2011.)
theorem clatglb {y1 z: setvar} (B2 S G K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2)) (wa (wral y1 (wbr (cfv S G) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wral z (wi (wral y1 (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le) S) (wbr (cv z) (cfv S G) c_le)) B2)) $;

--| The greatest lower bound is the least element. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2011.)
theorem clatglble (B2 S G K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss S B2) (wcel X S)) (wbr (cfv S G) X c_le) $;

--| Two ways of expressing "less than or equal to the greatest lower bound." (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2011.)
theorem clatleglb {y1: setvar} (B2 S G K c_le X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wcel X B2) (wss S B2)) (wb (wbr X (cfv S G) c_le) (wral y1 (wbr X (cv y1) c_le) S)) $;

--| Subset law for greatest lower bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2014.)
theorem clatglbss (B2 S T G K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv K cple) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv K cglb) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ccla) (wss T B2) (wss S T)) (wbr (cfv T G) (cfv S G) c_le) $;

--| The class of distributive lattices.
term cdlat: class;

--| A _distributive lattice_ is a lattice in which meets distribute over joins, or equivalently ( ~ latdisd ) joins distribute over meets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_dlat {x3 y1 z j k m b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdlat (crab k (wsbc b (wsbc j (wsbc m (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv j)) (cv m)) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (co (cv x3) (cv z) (cv m)) (cv j))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv k) cmee)) (cfv (cv k) cjn)) (cfv (cv k) cbs)) clat) $;

--| Property of being a distributive lattice. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem isdlat {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 c_or K c_an: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cdlat) (wa (wcel K clat) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_or) c_an) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_an) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_an) c_or)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| In a distributive lattice, meets distribute over joins. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem dlatmjdi (B2 c_or K c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cdlat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_or) c_an) (co (co X Y c_an) (co X Z c_an) c_or)) $;

--| A distributive lattice is a lattice. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem dlatl (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cdlat) (wcel K clat) $;

--| The dual of a distributive lattice is a distributive lattice and conversely. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem odudlatb (D K V: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv K codu) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wb (wcel K cdlat) (wcel D cdlat)) $;

--| In a distributive lattice, joins distribute over meets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem dlatjmdi (B2 c_or K c_an X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_or (cfv K cjn) $ >
  $ wceq c_an (cfv K cmee) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cdlat) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_an) c_or) (co (co X Y c_or) (co X Z c_or) c_an)) $;

--| Class function defining inclusion posets.
term cipo: class;

--| For any family of sets, define the poset of that family ordered by inclusion. See ~ ipobas , ~ ipolerval , and ~ ipole for its contract. _EDITORIAL_: I'm not thrilled with the name. Any suggestions? (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ipo {x3 y1 f2 o: setvar}:
  $ wceq cipo (cmpt f2 cvv (csb o (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv f2)) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv f2)) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (cv o) cordt))) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cple) (cv o)) (cop (cfv cnx coc) (cmpt x3 (cv f2) (cuni (crab y1 (wceq (cin (cv y1) (cv x3)) c0) (cv f2))))))))) $;

--| The structure of ~ df-ipo is a structure defining indices up to 11. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipostr (B2 J c_le c_pe: class):
  $ wbr (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx coc) c_pe))) (cop c1 (cdc c1 c1)) cstr $;

--| Value of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ipoval {x3 y1: setvar} (F I: class) (c_le: class x3 y1) (V: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) F) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq I (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) F) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv c_le cordt))) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cple) c_le) (cop (cfv cnx coc) (cmpt x3 F (cuni (crab y1 (wceq (cin (cv y1) (cv x3)) c0) F))))))) $;

--| Base set of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipobas (F I V: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq F (cfv I cbs)) $;

--| Relation of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ipolerval {x3 y1: setvar} (F I V: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) F) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (cfv I cple)) $;

--| Topology of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipotset (F I c_le V: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv I cple) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (cfv c_le cordt) (cfv I cts)) $;

--| Weak order condition of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ipole (F I c_le V X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv I cple) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel X F) (wcel Y F)) (wb (wbr X Y c_le) (wss X Y)) $;

--| Strict order condition of the inclusion poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ipolt (c_lt F I V X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv I cplt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel X F) (wcel Y F)) (wb (wbr X Y c_lt) (wpss X Y)) $;

--| The inclusion poset on a family of sets is actually a poset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 30-Jan-2015.)
theorem ipopos (F I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv F cipo) $ >
  $ wcel I cpo $;

--| Condition for a family of sets to be directed by inclusion. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isipodrs {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cfv A2 cipo) cdrs) (w3a (wcel A2 cvv) (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wss (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z)) A2) A2) A2)) $;

--| Direction by inclusion as used here implies sethood. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem ipodrscl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cfv A2 cipo) cdrs) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Finite upper bound property for directed collections of sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem ipodrsfi {z: setvar} (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cfv A2 cipo) cdrs) (wss X A2) (wcel X cfn)) (wrex z (wss (cuni X) (cv z)) A2) $;

--| The finite subsets of any set are directed by inclusion. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem fpwipodrs (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cfv (cin (cpw A2) cfn) cipo) cdrs) $;

--| The monotone image of a directed set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem ipodrsima {v u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class v u) (F: class) (V: class v u):
  $ wi ph (wfn F (cpw A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wss (cv u) (cv v)) (wss (cv v) A2))) (wss (cfv (cv u) F) (cfv (cv v) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 cipo) cdrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cpw A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima F B2) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cima F B2) cipo) cdrs) $;

--| An algebraic closure system satisfies ~ isacs3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs3lem {s1: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv s1) cipo) cdrs) (wcel (cuni (cv s1)) C)) (cpw C))) $;

--| An algebraic closure system contains all directed unions of closed sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsdrsel (C X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wss Y C) (wcel (cfv Y cipo) cdrs)) (wcel (cuni Y) C) $;

--| In a closure system in which directed unions of closed sets are closed, closure commutes with directed unions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs4lem {t s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv s1) cipo) cdrs) (wcel (cuni (cv s1)) C)) (cpw C))) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral t (wi (wcel (cfv (cv t) cipo) cdrs) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv t)) F) (cuni (cima F (cv t))))) (cpw (cpw X)))) $;

--| If closure commutes with directed unions, then the closure of a set is the closure of its finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs5lem {t s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral t (wi (wcel (cfv (cv t) cipo) cdrs) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv t)) F) (cuni (cima F (cv t))))) (cpw (cpw X)))) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) F) (cuni (cima F (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn)))) (cpw X))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, closure commutes with directed unions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsdrscl (C F X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wss Y (cpw X)) (wcel (cfv Y cipo) cdrs)) (wceq (cfv (cuni Y) F) (cuni (cima F Y))) $;

--| A closure in an algebraic closure system is the union of the closures of finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acsficl (C S F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S F) (cuni (cima F (cin (cpw S) cfn)))) $;

--| A closure system is algebraic iff the closure of a generating set is the union of the closures of its finite subsets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs5 {s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) F) (cuni (cima F (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn)))) (cpw X))) $;

--| A closure system is algebraic iff closure commutes with directed unions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs4 {s1: setvar} (C F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv s1) cipo) cdrs) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv s1)) F) (cuni (cima F (cv s1))))) (cpw (cpw X)))) $;

--| A closure system is algebraic iff directed unions of closed sets are closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem isacs3 {s1: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv X cacs)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wral s1 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv s1) cipo) cdrs) (wcel (cuni (cv s1)) C)) (cpw C))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, the closure of a set is the union of the closures of its finite subsets. Deduction form of ~ acsficl . (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsficld (ph: wff) (A2 S N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cuni (cima N (cin (cpw S) cfn)))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, an element is in the closure of a set if and only if it is in the closure of a finite subset. Alternate form of ~ acsficl . Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsficl2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (S N: class) (X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel Y (cfv S N)) (wrex x3 (wcel Y (cfv (cv x3) N)) (cin (cpw S) cfn))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, a set is independent if and only if all its finite subsets are independent. Part of Proposition 4.1.3 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 83. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsfiindd (ph: wff) (A2 S I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel S I) (wss (cin (cpw S) cfn) I)) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, if ` T ` is contained in the closure of ` S ` , there is a map ` f ` from ` T ` into the set of finite subsets of ` S ` such that the closure of ` U. ran f ` contains ` T ` . This is proven by applying ~ acsficl2d to each element of ` T ` . See Section II.5 in [Cohn] p. 81 to 82. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-reg and ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem acsmapd {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class f2) (S T N: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv S N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wf T (cin (cpw S) cfn) (cv f2)) (wss T (cfv (cuni (crn (cv f2))) N)))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, if ` S ` and ` T ` have the same closure and ` S ` is independent, then there is a map ` f ` from ` T ` into the set of finite subsets of ` S ` such that ` S ` equals the union of ` ran f ` . This is proven by taking the map ` f ` from ~ acsmapd and observing that, since ` S ` and ` T ` have the same closure, the closure of ` U. ran f ` must contain ` S ` . Since ` S ` is independent, by ~ mrissmrcd , ` U. ran f ` must equal ` S ` . See Section II.5 in [Cohn] p. 81 to 82. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsmap2d {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class f2) (S T: class) (I: class f2) (N: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wf T (cin (cpw S) cfn) (cv f2)) (wceq S (cuni (crn (cv f2)))))) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, if ` S ` and ` T ` have the same closure and ` S ` is infinite independent, then ` T ` is infinite. This follows from applying ~ unirnffid to the map given in ~ acsmap2d . See Section II.5 in [Cohn] p. 81 to 82. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsinfd (ph: wff) (A2 S T I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel T cfn)) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, if ` S ` and ` T ` have the same closure and ` S ` is infinite independent, then ` T ` dominates ` S ` . This follows from applying ~ acsinfd and then applying ~ unirnfdomd to the map given in ~ acsmap2d . See Section II.5 in [Cohn] p. 81 to 82. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsdomd (ph: wff) (A2 S T I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cdom) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system, if two independent sets have equal closure and one is infinite, then they are equinumerous. This is proven by using ~ acsdomd twice with ~ acsinfd . See Section II.5 in [Cohn] p. 81 to 82. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsinfdimd (ph: wff) (A2 S T I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cen) $;

--| In an algebraic closure system whose closure operator has the exchange property, if two independent sets have equal closure, they are equinumerous. See ~ mreexfidimd for the finite case and ~ acsinfdimd for the infinite case. This is a special case of Theorem 4.2.2 in [FaureFrolicher] p. 87. (Contributed by David Moews, 1-May-2017.)
theorem acsexdimd {y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z s1) (S: class) (T: class y1 z s1) (I N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv X cacs)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 cmri) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv z))) N)) (cdif (cfv (cun (cv s1) (csn (cv y1))) N) (cfv (cv s1) N))) X) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S N) (cfv T N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S T cen) $;

--| Greatest lower bounds in a Moore space are realized by intersections. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) See ~ mrelatglbALT for an alternate proof.
theorem mrelatglb (C U G I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cipo) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv I cglb) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U C) (wne U c0)) (wceq (cfv U G) (cint U)) $;

--| The empty intersection in a Moore space is realized by the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrelatglb0 (C G I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cipo) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv I cglb) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wceq (cfv c0 G) X) $;

--| Least upper bounds in a Moore space are realized by the closure of the union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) See ~ mrelatlubALT for an alternate proof.
theorem mrelatlub (C U F I L X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cipo) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv C cmrc) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv I club) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wss U C)) (wceq (cfv U L) (cfv (cuni U) F)) $;

--| A Moore space is a complete lattice under inclusion. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) TODO ( ~ df-riota update): Reprove using ~ isclat instead of the isclatBAD. hypothesis. See commented-out mreclat above. See ~ mreclat for a good version.
theorem mreclatBAD {x3: setvar} (C I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv C cipo) $ >
  $ wb (wcel I ccla) (wa (wcel I cpo) (wal x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) (cfv I cbs)) (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv I club)) (cfv I cbs)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv I cglb)) (cfv I cbs)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv X cmre)) (wcel I ccla) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all posets.
term cps: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all totally ordered sets.
term ctsr: class;

--| Define the class of all posets (partially ordered sets) with weak ordering (e.g., "less than or equal to" instead of "less than"). A poset is a relation which is transitive, reflexive, and antisymmetric. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2008.)
axiom df_ps {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cps (cab r (w3a (wrel (cv r)) (wss (ccom (cv r) (cv r)) (cv r)) (wceq (cin (cv r) (ccnv (cv r))) (cres cid (cuni (cuni (cv r))))))) $;

--| Define the class of all totally ordered sets. (Contributed by FL, 1-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_tsr {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctsr (crab r (wss (cxp (cdm (cv r)) (cdm (cv r))) (cun (cv r) (ccnv (cv r)))) cps) $;

--| The predicate "is a poset" i.e. a transitive, reflexive, antisymmetric relation. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2008.)
theorem isps (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R A2) (wb (wcel R cps) (w3a (wrel R) (wss (ccom R R) R) (wceq (cin R (ccnv R)) (cres cid (cuni (cuni R)))))) $;

--| A poset is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2008.)
theorem psrel (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cps) (wrel A2) $;

--| A poset is antisymmetric and reflexive. (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem psref2 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wceq (cin R (ccnv R)) (cres cid (cuni (cuni R)))) $;

--| A poset is transitive. (Contributed by FL, 3-Aug-2009.)
theorem pstr2 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wss (ccom R R) R) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psref and others. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem pslem (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (w3a (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 C R)) (wbr A2 C R)) (wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cuni R))) (wbr A2 A2 R)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 A2 R)) (wceq A2 B2))) $;

--| The domain and range of a poset equal its field. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-2008.)
theorem psdmrn (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wa (wceq (cdm R) (cuni (cuni R))) (wceq (crn R) (cuni (cuni R)))) $;

--| A poset is reflexive. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-2008.)
theorem psref (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cps) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr A2 A2 R) $;

--| The range of a poset equals it domain. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jul-2008.)
theorem psrn (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wceq X (crn R)) $;

--| A poset is antisymmetric. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2008.)
theorem psasym (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cps) (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 A2 R)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| A poset is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem pstr (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cps) (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 C R)) (wbr A2 C R) $;

--| The converse of a poset is a poset. In the general case ` ( ``' R e. PosetRel -> R e. PosetRel ) ` is not true. See ~ cnvpsb for a special case where the property holds. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnvps (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wcel (ccnv R) cps) $;

--| The converse of a poset is a poset. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.)
theorem cnvpsb (R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wb (wcel R cps) (wcel (ccnv R) cps)) $;

--| Any subset of a partially ordered set is partially ordered. (Contributed by FL, 24-Jan-2010.)
theorem psss (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wcel (cin R (cxp A2 A2)) cps) $;

--| Field of a subposet. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem psssdm2 (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wceq (cdm (cin R (cxp A2 A2))) (cin X A2)) $;

--| Field of a subposet. (Contributed by FL, 19-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem psssdm (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cps) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cdm (cin R (cxp A2 A2))) A2) $;

--| The predicate is a toset. (Contributed by FL, 1-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem istsr (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R ctsr) (wa (wcel R cps) (wss (cxp X X) (cun R (ccnv R)))) $;

--| The predicate is a toset. (Contributed by FL, 1-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem istsr2 {x3 y1: setvar} (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R ctsr) (wa (wcel R cps) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X) X)) $;

--| A toset is a linear order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsrlin (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ctsr) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 A2 R)) $;

--| Two ways of saying a number is less than or equal to the maximum of two others. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsrlemax (A2 B2 C R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctsr) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wb (wbr A2 (cif (wbr B2 C R) C B2) R) (wo (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr A2 C R))) $;

--| A toset is a poset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsrps (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wcel R cps) $;

--| The converse of a toset is a toset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnvtsr (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wcel (ccnv R) ctsr) $;

--| Any subset of a totally ordered set is totally ordered. (Contributed by FL, 24-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Nov-2013.)
theorem tsrss (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wcel (cin R (cxp A2 A2)) ctsr) $;

--| The domain of ` <_ ` is ` RR* ` . (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2015.)
theorem ledm:
  $ wceq cxr (cdm cle) $;

--| The range of ` <_ ` is ` RR* ` . (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem lern:
  $ wceq cxr (crn cle) $;

--| The field of the 'less or equal to' relationship on the extended real. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2015.)
theorem lefld:
  $ wceq cxr (cuni (cuni cle)) $;

--| The "less than or equal to" relationship on the extended reals is a toset. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem letsr:
  $ wcel cle ctsr $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of directed sets.
term cdir: class;

--| Extend class notation with the tail function for directed sets.
term ctail: class;

--| Define the class of directed sets (the order relation itself is sometimes called a direction, and a directed set is a set equipped with a direction). (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_dir {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdir (cab r (wa (wa (wrel (cv r)) (wss (cres cid (cuni (cuni (cv r)))) (cv r))) (wa (wss (ccom (cv r) (cv r)) (cv r)) (wss (cxp (cuni (cuni (cv r))) (cuni (cuni (cv r)))) (ccom (ccnv (cv r)) (cv r)))))) $;

--| Define the tail function for directed sets. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_tail {x3 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctail (cmpt r cdir (cmpt x3 (cuni (cuni (cv r))) (cima (cv r) (csn (cv x3))))) $;

--| A condition for a relation to be a direction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem isdir (A2 R V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (cuni R)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wb (wcel R cdir) (wa (wa (wrel R) (wss (cres cid A2) R)) (wa (wss (ccom R R) R) (wss (cxp A2 A2) (ccom (ccnv R) R))))) $;

--| A direction is a relation. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem reldir (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cdir) (wrel R) $;

--| A direction's domain is equal to its field. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem dirdm (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cdir) (wceq (cdm R) (cuni (cuni R))) $;

--| A direction is reflexive. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem dirref (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdir) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr A2 A2 R) $;

--| A direction is transitive. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem dirtr (A2 B2 C R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cdir) (wcel C V)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr B2 C R))) (wbr A2 C R) $;

--| For any two elements of a directed set, there exists a third element greater than or equal to both. Note that this does not say that the two elements have a _least_ upper bound. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem dirge {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdir) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) R) (wbr B2 (cv x3) R)) X) $;

--| A totally ordered set is a directed set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Nov-2013.)
theorem tsrdir (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 ctsr) (wcel A2 cdir) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of (finite) chains.
term cchn (A2 c_lt: class): class;

--| Define the class of (finite) chains. A chain is defined to be a sequence of objects, where each object is less than the next one in the sequence. The term "chain" is usually used in order theory. In the context of algebra, chains are often called "towers", for example for fields, or "series", for example for subgroup or subnormal series. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
axiom df_chn {n c: setvar} (A2 c_lt: class):
  $ wceq (cchn A2 c_lt) (crab c (wral n (wbr (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) (cv c)) (cfv (cv n) (cv c)) c_lt) (cdif (cdm (cv c)) (csn cc0))) (cword A2)) $;

--| Property of being a chain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem ischn {n: setvar} (A2 C c_lt: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) (wa (wcel C (cword A2)) (wral n (wbr (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) C) (cfv (cv n) C) c_lt) (cdif (cdm C) (csn cc0)))) $;

--| A chain is an ordered sequence, i.e. a word. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnwrd (ph: wff) (A2 C c_lt: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cword A2)) $;

--| Basic property of a chain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnltm1 (ph: wff) (A2 C c_lt N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cdif (cdm C) (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co N c1 cmin) C) (cfv N C) c_lt) $;

--| A prefix of a chain is still a chain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem pfxchn (ph: wff) (A2 C c_lt L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co cc0 (cfv C chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C L cpfx) (cchn A2 c_lt)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for chain collection constructor. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem nfchnd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 c_lt: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cchn A2 c_lt)) $;

--| Equality theorem for chains. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem chneq1 (A2 R c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wceq c_lt R) (wceq (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn A2 R)) $;

--| Equality theorem for chains. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem chneq2 (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn B2 c_lt)) $;

--| Equality theorem for chains. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem chneq12 (A2 B2 R c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq c_lt R) (wceq A2 B2)) (wceq (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn B2 R)) $;

--| Chains under a relation are also chains under any superset relation. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnrss (A2 R c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wss c_lt R) (wss (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn A2 R)) $;

--| Chains with an alphabet are also chains with any superset alphabet. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chndss (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn B2 c_lt)) $;

--| Subset theorem for chains. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnrdss (A2 B2 R c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss c_lt R) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (cchn A2 c_lt) (cchn B2 R)) $;

--| Chains with a set given for range form a set. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 21-Nov-2024.) (Revised by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnexg (A2 c_lt V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cchn A2 c_lt) cvv) $;

--| Empty set is an increasing chain for every range and every relation. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 19-Nov-2024.) (Revised by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem nulchn (A2 c_lt: class):
  $ wcel c0 (cchn A2 c_lt) $;

--| A singleton word is always a chain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem s1chn (ph: wff) (A2 c_lt X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cs1 X) (cchn A2 c_lt)) $;

--| Induction over a chain. See ~ nnind for an explanation about the hypotheses. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnind {x3 c d: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff c) (ch: wff x3 c d) (th ta et: wff x3 d) (A2: class) (C: class x3 d) (c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) c0) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv d)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (co (cv d) (cs1 (cv x3)) cconcat)) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) C) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv d) (cchn A2 c_lt))) (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wo (wceq (cv d) c0) (wbr (cfv (cv d) clsw) (cv x3) c_lt))) th) ta $ >
  $ wi ph et $;

--| In a chain, the last element is an upper bound. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnub (ph: wff) (A2 C c_lt I: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 (co (cfv C chash) c1 cmin) cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv I C) (cfv C clsw) c_lt) $;

--| Compare any two elements in a chain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnlt (ph: wff) (A2 C c_lt I J: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 (cfv C chash) cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 J cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv I C) (cfv J C) c_lt) $;

--| A chain induces a total order. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem chnso (A2 C c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wa (wpo A2 c_lt) (wcel C (cchn A2 c_lt))) (wor (crn C) c_lt) $;

--| Extend a chain with a single element. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem chnccats1 (ph: wff) (A2 c_lt T X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq T c0) (wbr (cfv T clsw) X c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co T (cs1 X) cconcat) (cchn A2 c_lt)) $;

--| Concatenate two chains. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnccat (ph: wff) (A2 c_lt T U: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3o (wceq T c0) (wceq U c0) (wbr (cfv T clsw) (cfv cc0 U) c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co T U cconcat) (cchn A2 c_lt)) $;

--| Reverse of a chain is chain under the converse relation and same domain. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnrev (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) (wcel (cfv B2 creverse) (cchn A2 (ccnv c_lt))) $;

--| There is a finite number of chains with fixed length over finite alphabet. Trivially holds for invalid lengths as there're no matching sequences. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 5-Jan-2025.) (Revised by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnflenfi {a: setvar} (A2 c_lt T: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (crab a (wceq (cfv (cv a) chash) T) (cchn A2 c_lt)) cfn) $;

--| A chain is a zero-based finite sequence with a recoverable upper limit. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnf (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) (wf (co cc0 (cfv B2 chash) cfzo) A2 B2) $;

--| A chain under relation which orders the alphabet is a one-to-one function from its domain to alphabet. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnpof1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co cc0 (cfv B2 chash) cfzo) A2 B2) $;

--| A chain under relation which orders the alphabet cannot have more elements than the alphabet itself. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnpoadomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_lt V: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co cc0 (cfv B2 chash) cfzo) A2 cdom) $;

--| A chain under relation which orders the alphabet has at most alphabet's size elements in it. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnpolleha (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_lt V: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 chash) (cfv A2 chash) cle) $;

--| Provided that chain's relation is a partial order, the chain length is restricted to a specific integer range. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnpolfz (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi ph (wpo A2 c_lt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cchn A2 c_lt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 chash) (co cc0 (cfv A2 chash) cfz)) $;

--| There is a finite number of chains over finite domain, as long as the relation orders it. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnfi (A2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wpo A2 c_lt)) (wcel (cchn A2 c_lt) cfn) $;

--| There is an infinite number of chains for any infinite alphabet and any relation. For instance, all the singletons of alphabet characters match. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chninf (A2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wnel A2 cfn) (wnel (cchn A2 c_lt) cfn) $;

--| Given a partial order, the set of chains is finite iff the alphabet is finite. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 20-Jan-2026.)
theorem chnfibg (A2 c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wpo A2 c_lt) (wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cchn A2 c_lt) cfn)) $;

--| Example: a doubleton of twos is a valid chain under the identity relation and domain of integers. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem ex_chn1:
  $ wcel (cs2 c2 c2) (cchn cz cid) $;

--| Example: sequence <" ZZ NN QQ "> is a valid chain under the equinumerosity relation in universal domain. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 17-Jan-2026.)
theorem ex_chn2:
  $ wcel (cs3 cz cn cq) (cchn cvv cen) $;

--| Extend class notation with group addition as a function.
term cplusf: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of all magmas.
term cmgm: class;

--| Define group addition function. Usually we will use ` +g ` directly instead of ` +f ` , and they have the same behavior in most cases. The main advantage of ` +f ` for any magma is that it is a guaranteed function ( ~ mgmplusf ), while ` +g ` only has closure ( ~ mgmcl ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_plusf {x3 y1 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cplusf (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)))) $;

--| A _magma_ is a set equipped with an everywhere defined internal operation. Definition 1 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 1, or definition of a groupoid in section I.1 of [Bruck] p. 1. Note: The term "groupoid" is now widely used to refer to other objects: (small) categories all of whose morphisms are invertible, or groups with a partial function replacing the binary operation. Therefore, we will only use the term "magma" for the present notion in set.mm. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_mgm {x3 y1 g2 o b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmgm (cab g2 (wsbc b (wsbc o (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv o)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs))) $;

--| The predicate "is a magma". (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem ismgm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 M: class) (V: class x3 y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wb (wcel M cmgm) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a magma" for a structure with a nonempty base set. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem ismgmn0 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wcel M cmgm) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| Closure of the operation of a magma. (Contributed by FL, 14-Sep-2010.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgmcl (B2 M X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cmgm) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_op) B2) $;

--| A condition for a structure not to be a magma. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by NM, 5-Feb-2020.)
theorem isnmgm (B2 M X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wnel (co X Y c_op) B2)) (wnel M cmgm) $;

--| If the base set of a magma is contained in the base set of another magma, and the group operation of the magma is the restriction of the group operation of the other magma to its base set, then the base set of the magma is closed under the group operation of the other magma. Formerly part of proof of ~ grpissubg . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem mgmsscl (B2 S G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cmgm) (wcel H cmgm)) (wa (wss S B2) (wceq (cfv H cplusg) (cres (cfv G cplusg) (cxp S S)))) (wa (wcel X S) (wcel Y S))) (wcel (co X Y (cfv G cplusg)) S) $;

--| The group addition operation as a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Mar-2024.)
theorem plusffval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_pd: class x3 y1) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) $;

--| The group addition operation as a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem plusfval (B2 c_pl c_pd G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_pd) (co X Y c_pl)) $;

--| If the addition operation is already a function, the functionalization of it is equal to the original operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem plusfeq (B2 c_pl c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wfn c_pl (cxp B2 B2)) (wceq c_pd c_pl) $;

--| The group addition operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem plusffn (B2 c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wfn c_pd (cxp B2 B2) $;

--| The group addition function of a magma is a function into its base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Revisd by AV, 28-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgmplusf (B2 c_pd M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv M cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pd) $;

--| The restriction of the group operation of a magma to its base set is a function if the base set does not contain the empty set. Excluding the empty set from the base set is necessary because of the specific definition of an undefined operation value (see also ~ ndmovcl and ~ ndmovrcl ). (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmn0plusgf (ph: wff) (B2 P c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel c0 B2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cres c_pl (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 P) $;

--| The group addition function of a magma is the restriction of its group operation to its base set if the base set does not contain the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmn0plusgplusf (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel c0 B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pd (cres c_pl (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| If two structures have the same (nonempty) base set, and the values of their group (addition) operations are equal for all pairs of elements of the base set, one is a magma iff the other one is. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 y1) (K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cmgm) (wcel L cmgm)) $;

--| Deduce a magma from its properties. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem ismgmd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (G: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $;

--| Characterize a substructure as submagma by closure properties. (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem issstrmgm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 y1) (H V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel H V) (wss S B2)) (wb (wcel H cmgm) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) S) S)) $;

--| The internal operation for a set is the trivial operation iff the set is a singleton. Formerly part of proof of ~ ring1zr . (Contributed by FL, 13-Feb-2010.) (Revised by AV, 23-Jan-2020.)
theorem intopsn (B2 c_op Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_op) (wcel Z B2)) (wb (wceq B2 (csn Z)) (wceq c_op (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z)))) $;

--| The only magma with a base set consisting of one element is the trivial magma (at least if its operation is an internal binary operation). (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2020.) (Revised by AV, 7-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmb1mgm1 (B2 c_pl M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cmgm) (wcel Z B2) (wfn c_pl (cxp B2 B2))) (wb (wceq B2 (csn Z)) (wceq c_pl (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z)))) $;

--| Any set with an empty base set and any group operation is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem mgm0 (M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M cbs) c0)) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| The structure with an empty base set and any group operation is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem mgm0b (O: class):
  $ wcel (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) c0) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) O)) cmgm $;

--| The structure with one element and the only closed internal operation for a singleton is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 10-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgm1 (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| A structure with a group addition operation expressed by a conditional operator is a magma if both values of the conditional operator are contained in the base set. (Contributed by AV, 9-Feb-2020.)
theorem opifismgm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1) (B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1) (M: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cif ps C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| A two-sided identity element is unique (if it exists) in any magma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem mgmidmo {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class):
  $ wrmo u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2 $;

--| Uniqueness of the left and right identity element of a magma when it exists. (Contributed by FL, 12-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 18-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmideud {x3 u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 u) (B2 c_pl: class):
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| The value of the group identity element. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem grpidval {x3 e: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 e) (G: class) (c_0: class x3 e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cio e (wa (wcel (cv e) B2) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2))) $;

--| The unique value of the group identity element. (Contributed by FL, 12-Dec-2009.) (Revised by AV, 24-Aug-2026.)
theorem idvalriota {x3 e: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 e) (G: class) (c_0: class x3 e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (crio e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| If two structures have the same base set, and the values of their group (addition) operations are equal for all pairs of elements of the base set, they have the same identity element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Nov-2014.)
theorem grpidpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K c0g) (cfv L c0g)) $;

--| The group identity extractor is a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem fn0g:
  $ wfn c0g cvv $;

--| The identity element function evaluates to the empty set on an empty structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem _0g0:
  $ wceq c0 (cfv c0 c0g) $;

--| Conditions for a class to be the identity element of a magma, if it exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem ismgmid {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3 e) (B2 c_pl U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel U B2) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co U (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) U c_pl) (cv x3))) B2)) (wceq c_0 U)) $;

--| The identity element of a magma, if it exists, belongs to the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem mgmidcl {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3 e) (B2 c_pl G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $;

--| The identity element of a magma, if it exists, is a left and right identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem mgmlrid {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3 e) (B2 c_pl G: class) (X: class e) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X B2)) (wa (wceq (co c_0 X c_pl) X) (wceq (co X c_0 c_pl) X)) $;

--| In a structure with an identity element, the group identity element is an identity element of the structure. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 8-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 11-Aug-2026.)
theorem _0gisid {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3 e) (B2 c_pl G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel c_0 B2) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3))) B2)) $;

--| Show that a given element is the identity element of a magma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem ismgmid2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co U (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) U c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U c_0) $;

--| If there is a left and right identity element for any binary operation (group operation) ` .+ ` , both identity elements are equal. Generalization of statement in [Lang] p. 3: it is sufficient that "e" is a left identity element and "e``" is a right identity element instead of both being (two-sided) identity elements. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2023.)
theorem lidrideqd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 c_pl R L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel L B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (co L (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) R c_pl) (cv x3)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L R) $;

--| If there is a left and right identity element for any binary operation (group operation) ` .+ ` , the left identity element (and therefore also the right identity element according to ~ lidrideqd ) is equal to the two-sided identity element. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2023.)
theorem lidrididd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 c_pl R: class) (G: class x3) (L: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel L B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (co L (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) R c_pl) (cv x3)) B2) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L c_0) $;

--| Deduce the identity element of a magma from its properties. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grpidd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl: class x3) (G c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g)) $;

--| Property of the set of identities of ` G ` . Either ` G ` has no identities, and ` O = (/) ` , or it has one and this identity is unique and identified by the ` 0g ` function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem mgmidsssn0 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class) (O: class x3 y1) (V: class y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab x3 (wral y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wss O (csn c_0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ grpinva . (Contributed by NM, 9-Aug-2013.)
theorem grpinvalem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 z) (B2 c_pl O: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co O (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) O) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co X X c_pl) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq X O) $;

--| Deduce right inverse from left inverse and left identity in an associative structure (such as a group). (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grpinva {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 z) (B2 c_pl: class) (N: class x3) (O: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co O (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) O) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel N B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co N X c_pl) O) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq (co X N c_pl) O) $;

--| Deduce right identity from left inverse and left identity in an associative structure (such as a group). (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2013.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grprida {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl O: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co O (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) O) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) O c_pl) (cv x3)) $;

--| The operation of a magma with identity as a function is an onto function. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 16-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmidpfod {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 e) (c_pd: class) (G: class x3 e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pd) $;

--| Range of an operation with a left and right identity element. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 16-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmidprnd {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 e) (c_pd: class) (G: class x3 e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn c_pd) B2) $;

--| The operation of a magma with identity is an onto function (assuming it is a function). (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 16-Aug-2026.)
theorem mgmfod {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 e) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn c_pl (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pl) $;

--| The restriction of a structure with an identity element to a subset containing the identity element has an identity element. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 8-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 11-Aug-2026.)
theorem idressidex0 {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 e) (B2 C c_pl: class) (S: class x3 e) (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 A2) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) C) C) $;

--| The restriction of a structure with an identity element to a subset containing the identity element has an identity element. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 8-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 11-Aug-2026.)
theorem idressidex {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 c_pl S G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) A2) A2) $;

--| The restriction of a structure with an identity element to a subset containing the identity element has the same identity element. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 8-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.) (Revised by AV, 12-Aug-2026.)
theorem idressid {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl S G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv S c0g) c_0) $;

--| The image structure of a unital magma is a unital magma. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Aug-2026.)
theorem imasmgm2 {x3 y1 q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 y1 a b) (c_pl: class a b) (R: class x3 y1 a b) (U F V: class) (W: class x3 y1 q p a b) (c_0: class y1 a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cmgm) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| Prove that a quotient structure is a unital magma. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Aug-2026.)
theorem qusmgm {x3 y1 q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_sm: class) (R: class x3 y1 a b) (U V: class) (X: class x3 y1 q p a b) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cmgm) (wceq (cec c_0 c_sm) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| Expand out the substitutions in ~ df-gsum . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem gsumvalx {x3 t f2 m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff t s1) (A2: class x3 t f2 m n s1) (B2 c_pl: class f2 m n) (F G O: class t s1) (V W X c_0: class x3 t f2 m n s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab s1 (wral t (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) c_pl) (cv t)) (wceq (co (cv t) (cv s1) c_pl) (cv t))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cvv O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (cif (wss (crn F) O) c_0 (cif (wcel A2 (crn cfz)) (cio x3 (wex m (wrex n (wa (wceq A2 (co (cv m) (cv n) cfz)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl F (cv m))))) (cfv (cv m) cuz)))) (cio x3 (wex f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (cfv W chash) cfz) W (cv f2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cfv W chash) (cseq c_pl (ccom F (cv f2)) c1))))))))) $;

--| Expand out the substitutions in ~ df-gsum . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumval {x3 t f2 m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff t s1) (A2: class x3 t f2 m n s1) (B2 c_pl: class f2 m n) (F G O: class t s1) (V W X c_0: class x3 t f2 m n s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab s1 (wral t (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) c_pl) (cv t)) (wceq (co (cv t) (cv s1) c_pl) (cv t))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cvv O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (cif (wss (crn F) O) c_0 (cif (wcel A2 (crn cfz)) (cio x3 (wex m (wrex n (wa (wceq A2 (co (cv m) (cv n) cfz)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl F (cv m))))) (cfv (cv m) cuz)))) (cio x3 (wex f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (cfv W chash) cfz) W (cv f2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cfv W chash) (cseq c_pl (ccom F (cv f2)) c1))))))))) $;

--| The group sum depends only on the base set and additive operation. Note that for entirely unrestricted functions, there can be dependency on out-of-domain values of the operation, so this is somewhat weaker than ~ mndpropd etc. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem gsumpropd (ph: wff) (F G H V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cplusg) (cfv H cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gsumpropd2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jun-2017.)
theorem gsumpropd2lem {t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class t s1) (F G H: class) (V W X: class t s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cfv G cbs)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cbs)))) (wcel (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cfv G cbs)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cbs)))) (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv H cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cvv (crab s1 (wral t (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (cv t)) (wceq (co (cv t) (cv s1) (cfv G cplusg)) (cv t))) (cfv G cbs)) (cfv G cbs)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cvv (crab s1 (wral t (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv H cplusg)) (cv t)) (wceq (co (cv t) (cv s1) (cfv H cplusg)) (cv t))) (cfv H cbs)) (cfv H cbs)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| A stronger version of ~ gsumpropd , working for magma, where only the closure of the addition operation on a common base is required, see ~ gsummgmpropd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jun-2017.)
theorem gsumpropd2 {t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H: class) (V W X: class t s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cfv G cbs)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cbs)))) (wcel (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cfv G cbs)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cbs)))) (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv H cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| A stronger version of ~ gsumpropd if at least one of the involved structures is a magma, see ~ gsumpropd2 . (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem gsummgmpropd {t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H: class) (V W X: class t s1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cfv G cbs)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cbs)))) (wceq (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv G cplusg)) (co (cv s1) (cv t) (cfv H cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| The group sum in a substructure is the same as the group sum in the original structure. The only requirement on the substructure is that it contain the identity element; neither ` G ` nor ` H ` need be groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem gsumress {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G H: class) (V X: class x3) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wa (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| Value of the group sum operation when every element being summed is an identity of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumval1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2 c_pl: class) (F G O V W c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab x3 (wral y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 O F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) c_0) $;

--| Value of the empty group sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsum0 (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c0 cgsu) c_0 $;

--| Value of the group sum operation over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumval2a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class) (M N O: class x3 y1) (V: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co M N cfz) B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab x3 (wral y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wss (crn F) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (cfv N (cseq c_pl F M))) $;

--| Value of the group sum operation over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumval2 (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl F G M N V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co M N cfz) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (cfv N (cseq c_pl F M))) $;

--| Splitting off the rightmost summand of a group sum. This corresponds to the (inductive) definition of a (finite) product in [Lang] p. 4, first formula. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2023.)
theorem gsumsplit1r (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl F G M N V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co (co G (cres F (co M N cfz)) cgsu) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) c_pl)) $;

--| Value of the group sum operation over a pair of sequential integers. (Contributed by AV, 14-Dec-2018.)
theorem gsumprval (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl F G M N V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co M c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpr M N) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co (cfv M F) (cfv N F) c_pl)) $;

--| Value of the group sum operation over the pair ` { 1 , 2 } ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Dec-2018.)
theorem gsumpr12val (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl F G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpr c1 c2) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co (cfv c1 F) (cfv c2 F) c_pl)) $;

--| Hom-set generator class for magmas.
term cmgmhm: class;

--| Class function taking a magma to its lattice of submagmas.
term csubmgm: class;

--| A magma homomorphism is a function on the base sets which preserves the binary operation. (Contributed by AV, 24-Feb-2020.)
axiom df_mgmhm {x3 y1 t f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmgmhm (cmpo s1 t cmgm cmgm (crab f2 (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv s1) cplusg)) (cv f2)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) cplusg))) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)) (co (cfv (cv t) cbs) (cfv (cv s1) cbs) cmap))) $;

--| A submagma is a subset of a magma which is closed under the operation. Such subsets are themselves magmas. (Contributed by AV, 24-Feb-2020.)
axiom df_submgm {x3 y1 t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csubmgm (cmpt s1 cmgm (crab t (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv s1) cplusg)) (cv t)) (cv t)) (cv t)) (cpw (cfv (cv s1) cbs)))) $;

--| Reverse closure of a magma homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 24-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmrcl (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wa (wcel S cmgm) (wcel T cmgm)) $;

--| Reverse closure for submagmas. (Contributed by AV, 24-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmrcl (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| Property of a magma homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem ismgmhm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (C c_pl c_pd: class x3 y1) (S T F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cmgm) (wcel T cmgm)) (wa (wf B2 C F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) B2) B2))) $;

--| A magma homomorphism is a function. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmf (B2 C S T F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wf B2 C F) $;

--| Magma homomorphism depends only on the operation of structures. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C J K L M: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv J cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv J cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) C) (wcel (cv y1) C))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv M cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J K cmgmhm) (co L M cmgmhm)) $;

--| A magma homomorphism preserves the binary operation. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmlin (B2 c_pl c_pd S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_pl) F) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_pd)) $;

--| A magma homomorphism is bijective iff its converse is also a magma homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmf1o (B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cmgmhm)) (wb (wf1o B2 C F) (wcel (ccnv F) (co S R cmgmhm))) $;

--| The identity homomorphism on a magma. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem idmgmhm (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wcel (cres cid B2) (co M M cmgmhm)) $;

--| Expand definition of a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem issubmgm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class x3 y1) (S M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wb (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wa (wss S B2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) S) S))) $;

--| Submagmas are subsets that are also magmas. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem issubmgm2 (B2 S H M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wb (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wa (wss S B2) (wcel H cmgm))) $;

--| Deduction for proving that a restricted class abstraction is a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem rabsubmgmd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff z) (th ta et: wff x3 y1) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (M: class z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmgm) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wa th ta))) et $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab z ps B2) (cfv M csubmgm)) $;

--| Submagmas are subsets of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmss (B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wss S B2) $;

--| Every magma is trivially a submagma of itself. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmid (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wcel B2 (cfv M csubmgm)) $;

--| Submagmas are closed under the magma operation. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmcl (c_pl S M X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) S) $;

--| Submagmas are themselves magmas under the given operation. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmmgm (S H M: class):
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wcel H cmgm) $;

--| The base set of a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmbas (S H M: class):
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmgm)) (wceq S (cfv H cbs)) $;

--| A submagma of a submagma is a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem subsubmgm (A2 S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubmgm)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv H csubmgm)) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv G csubmgm)) (wss A2 S))) $;

--| Restriction of a magma homomorphism to a submagma is a homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem resmgmhm (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co S X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wcel X (cfv S csubmgm))) (wcel (cres F X) (co U T cmgmhm)) $;

--| One direction of ~ resmgmhm2b . (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem resmgmhm2 (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S U cmgmhm)) (wcel X (cfv T csubmgm))) (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) $;

--| Restriction of the codomain of a homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2020.)
theorem resmgmhm2b (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv T csubmgm)) (wss (crn F) X)) (wb (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wcel F (co S U cmgmhm))) $;

--| The composition of magma homomorphisms is a homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cmgmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cmgmhm))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cmgmhm)) $;

--| The homomorphic image of a submagma is a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmima (F M N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co M N cmgmhm)) (wcel X (cfv M csubmgm))) (wcel (cima F X) (cfv N csubmgm)) $;

--| The equalizer of two magma homomorphisms is a submagma. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem mgmhmeql (S T F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cmgmhm))) (wcel (cdm (cin F G)) (cfv S csubmgm)) $;

--| Submagmas are an algebraic closure system. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem submgmacs (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmgm) (wcel (cfv G csubmgm) (cfv B2 cacs)) $;

--| Extend class notation with class of all semigroups.
term csgrp: class;

--| A _semigroup_ is a set equipped with an everywhere defined internal operation (so, a magma, see ~ df-mgm ), whose operation is associative. Definition in section II.1 of [Bruck] p. 23, or of an "associative magma" in definition 5 of [BourbakiAlg1] p. 4 . (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_sgrp {x3 y1 z g2 o b: setvar}:
  $ wceq csgrp (crab g2 (wsbc b (wsbc o (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv o)) (cv z) (cv o)) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv o)) (cv o))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) cmgm) $;

--| The predicate "is a semigroup". (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem issgrp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel M csgrp) (wa (wcel M cmgm) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) B2) B2) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a semigroup" for a structure which is a set. (Contributed by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem issgrpv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 M: class) (V: class x3 y1 z) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wb (wcel M csgrp) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) B2) (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) B2)) B2) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a semigroup" for a structure with a nonempty base set. (Contributed by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem issgrpn0 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wcel M csgrp) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) B2) (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) B2)) B2) B2)) $;

--| A condition for a structure not to be a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jan-2020.)
theorem isnsgrp (B2 M X Y c_op Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wi (wne (co (co X Y c_op) Z c_op) (co X (co Y Z c_op) c_op)) (wnel M csgrp)) $;

--| A semigroup is a magma. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrpmgm (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M csgrp) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| A semigroup operation is associative. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrpass (B2 G X Y c_op Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G csgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_op) Z c_op) (co X (co Y Z c_op) c_op)) $;

--| Closure of the operation of a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem sgrpcl (B2 G X Y c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G csgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_op) B2) $;

--| Any set with an empty base set and any group operation is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem sgrp0 (M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M cbs) c0)) (wcel M csgrp) $;

--| The structure with an empty base set and any group operation is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem sgrp0b (O: class):
  $ wcel (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) c0) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) O)) csgrp $;

--| The structure with one element and the only closed internal operation for a singleton is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 10-Feb-2020.)
theorem sgrp1 (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel M csgrp) $;

--| Deduce a semigroup from its properties. (Contributed by AV, 13-Feb-2025.)
theorem issgrpd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1 z) (G: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G csgrp) $;

--| If two structures are sets, have the same base set, and the values of their group (addition) operations are equal for all pairs of elements of the base set, one is a semigroup iff the other one is. (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem sgrppropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K csgrp) (wcel L csgrp)) $;

--| Structure product pointwise sums are closed when the factors are semigroups. (Contributed by AV, 21-Feb-2025.)
theorem prdsplusgsgrpcl (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R S F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I csgrp R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G c_pl) B2) $;

--| The product of a family of semigroups is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 21-Feb-2025.)
theorem prdssgrpd (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I csgrp R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y csgrp) $;

--| Extend class notation with class of all monoids.
term cmnd: class;

--| A _monoid_ is a semigroup, which has a two-sided neutral element. Definition 2 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 12. In other words (according to the definition in [Lang] p. 3), a monoid is a set equipped with an everywhere defined internal operation (see ~ mndcl ), whose operation is associative (see ~ mndass ) and has a two-sided neutral element (see ~ mndid ), see also ~ ismnd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
axiom df_mnd {x3 e g2 p b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmnd (crab g2 (wsbc b (wsbc p (wrex e (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv x3) (cv p)) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv e) (cv p)) (cv x3))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) csgrp) $;

--| The predicate "is a monoid", corresponding 1-to-1 to the definition. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem ismnddef {e a: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (G: class e a):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cmnd) (wa (wcel G csgrp) (wrex e (wral a (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv a) c_pl) (cv a)) (wceq (co (cv a) (cv e) c_pl) (cv a))) B2) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a monoid". This is the defining theorem of a monoid by showing that a set is a monoid if and only if it is a set equipped with a closed, everywhere defined internal operation (so, a magma, see ~ mndcl ), whose operation is associative (so, a semigroup, see also ~ mndass ) and has a two-sided neutral element (see ~ mndid ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem ismnd {e a b c: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (G: class e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cmnd) (wa (wral a (wral b (wa (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) B2) (wral c (wceq (co (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) (cv c) c_pl) (co (cv a) (co (cv b) (cv c) c_pl) c_pl)) B2)) B2) B2) (wrex e (wral a (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv a) c_pl) (cv a)) (wceq (co (cv a) (cv e) c_pl) (cv a))) B2) B2)) $;

--| The predicate "is a monoid". This is the defining theorem of a monoid by showing that a set is a monoid if and only if it is a set equipped with a closed, everywhere defined internal operation (so, a magma, see ~ mndcl ), whose operation is associative (so, a semigroup, see also ~ mndass ) and has a two-sided neutral element (see ~ mndid ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem ismnd_b1 {e a c: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cmnd) (wa (wral a (wral e (wa (wcel (co (cv a) (cv e) c_pl) B2) (wral c (wceq (co (co (cv a) (cv e) c_pl) (cv c) c_pl) (co (cv a) (co (cv e) (cv c) c_pl) c_pl)) B2)) B2) B2) (wrex e (wral a (wa (wceq (co (cv e) (cv a) c_pl) (cv a)) (wceq (co (cv a) (cv e) c_pl) (cv a))) B2) B2)) $;

--| A condition for a structure not to be a monoid: every element of the base set is not a left identity for at least one element of the base set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem isnmnd {x3 z: setvar} (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wral z (wrex x3 (wne (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_op) (cv x3)) B2) B2) (wnel M cmnd) $;

--| A semigroup with an identity element which is not the empty set is a monoid. Of course there could be monoids with the empty set as identity element (see, for example, the monoid of the power set of a class under union, ~ pwmnd and ~ pwmndid ), but these cannot be proven to be monoids with this theorem. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2024.)
theorem sgrpidmnd {e: setvar} (B2 G: class) (c_0: class e):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G csgrp) (wrex e (wa (wne (cv e) c0) (wceq (cv e) c_0)) B2)) (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| A monoid is a semigroup. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Feb-2020.)
theorem mndsgrp (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wcel G csgrp) $;

--| A monoid is a magma. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Feb-2020.)
theorem mndmgm (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| Closure of the operation of a monoid. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Feb-2020.)
theorem mndcl (B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) B2) $;

--| A monoid operation is associative. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2011.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Feb-2020.)
theorem mndass (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_pl) (co X (co Y Z c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| A monoid has a two-sided identity element. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by NM, 16-Aug-2011.)
theorem mndid {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wrex u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| The two-sided identity element of a monoid is unique. Lemma 2.2.1(a) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Dec-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Aug-2026.)
theorem mndideu {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wreu u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ mndideu as of 18-Aug-2026. The two-sided identity element of a monoid is unique. Lemma 2.2.1(a) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Dec-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mndideuOLD {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wreu u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for monoids, with an explicit commutativity hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem mnd32g (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Y Z c_pl) (co Z Y c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_pl) (co (co X Z c_pl) Y c_pl)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for monoids, with an explicit commutativity hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem mnd12g (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_pl) c_pl) (co Y (co X Z c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law for commutative monoids, with an explicit commutativity hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem mnd4g (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Y Z c_pl) (co Z Y c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) (co Z W c_pl) c_pl) (co (co X Z c_pl) (co Y W c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| The identity element of a monoid belongs to the monoid. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem mndidcl (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wcel c_0 B2) $;

--| The base set of a monoid is not empty. Statement in [Lang] p. 3. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2023.)
theorem mndbn0 (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wne B2 c0) $;

--| A finite monoid has positive integer size. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem hashfinmndnn (ph: wff) (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 chash) cn) $;

--| The group addition operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem mndplusf (B2 c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pd) $;

--| A monoid's identity element is a two-sided identity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2011.)
theorem mndlrid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel X B2)) (wa (wceq (co c_0 X c_pl) X) (wceq (co X c_0 c_pl) X)) $;

--| The identity element of a monoid is a left identity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2011.)
theorem mndlid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co c_0 X c_pl) X) $;

--| The identity element of a monoid is a right identity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2011.)
theorem mndrid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X c_0 c_pl) X) $;

--| Deduce a monoid from its properties. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem ismndd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1 z) (G: class) (c_0: class y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| The addition operation of a monoid as a function is an onto function. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.) (Revised by AV, 23-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem mndpfo (B2 c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pd) $;

--| The addition operation of a monoid is an onto function (assuming it is a function). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem mndfo (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wfn c_pl (cxp B2 B2))) (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pl) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ mndpfo as of 17-Aug-2026. The addition operation of a monoid as a function is an onto function. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.) (Revised by AV, 23-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mndpfoOLD (B2 c_pd G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pd) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ mndfo as of 17-Aug-2026. The addition operation of a monoid is an onto function (assuming it is a function). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.) (Proof shortened by AV, 23-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mndfoOLD (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wfn c_pl (cxp B2 B2))) (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pl) $;

--| If two structures have the same base set, and the values of their group (addition) operations are equal for all pairs of elements of the base set, one is a monoid iff the other one is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem mndpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cmnd) (wcel L cmnd)) $;

--| If two structures have the same group components (properties), one is a monoid iff the other one is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem mndprop (K L: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K cplusg) (cfv L cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cmnd) (wcel L cmnd) $;

--| Characterize a submonoid by closure properties. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem issubmnd {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S)) (wb (wcel H cmnd) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) S) S)) $;

--| ` 0g ` is unaffected by restriction. This is a bit more generic than ~ submnd0 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Aug-2026.)
theorem ress0g (A2 B2 R S c_0: class):
  $ wceq S (co R A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cmnd) (wcel c_0 A2) (wss A2 B2)) (wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ ress0g as of 12-Aug-2026. ` 0g ` is unaffected by restriction. This is a bit more generic than ~ submnd0 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2017.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ress0gOLD (A2 B2 R S c_0: class):
  $ wceq S (co R A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cmnd) (wcel c_0 A2) (wss A2 B2)) (wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g)) $;

--| The zero of a submonoid is the same as the zero in the parent monoid. (Note that we must add the condition that the zero of the parent monoid is actually contained in the submonoid, because it is possible to have "subsets that are monoids" which are not submonoids because they have a different identity element. See, for example, ~ smndex1mnd and ~ smndex1n0mnd ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem submnd0 (B2 S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel H cmnd)) (wa (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S))) (wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ submnd0 as of 12-Aug-2026. The zero of a submonoid is the same as the zero in the parent monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem submnd0OLD (B2 S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel H cmnd)) (wa (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S))) (wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| Uniqueness of an inverse element in a monoid, if it exists. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem mndinvmod {w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl: class) (G: class w) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrmo w (wa (wceq (co (cv w) A2 c_pl) c_0) (wceq (co A2 (cv w) c_pl) c_0)) B2) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is a subset of the support of the function of scalars. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.)
theorem mndpsuppss (A2 B2 R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel V X)) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel B2 (co R V cmap)))) (wss (co (co A2 B2 (cof (cfv M cplusg))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) (cun (co A2 (cfv M c0g) csupp) (co B2 (cfv M c0g) csupp))) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is finite if the support of the function of scalars is finite. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.)
theorem mndpsuppfi (A2 B2 R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel V X)) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel B2 (co R V cmap))) (wa (wcel (co A2 (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn) (wcel (co B2 (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn))) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 (cof (cfv M cplusg))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn) $;

--| A mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is finitely supported if the function of scalars is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2019.)
theorem mndpfsupp (A2 B2 R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel V X)) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel B2 (co R V cmap))) (wa (wbr A2 (cfv M c0g) cfsupp) (wbr B2 (cfv M c0g) cfsupp))) (wbr (co A2 B2 (cof (cfv M cplusg))) (cfv M c0g) cfsupp) $;

--| Structure product pointwise sums are closed when the factors are monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsplusgcl (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R S F G I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cmnd R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G c_pl) B2) $;

--| Characterization of identity in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsidlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R S I V W Y: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cmnd R) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (ccom c0g R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel c_0 B2) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3))) B2)) $;

--| The product of a family of monoids is a monoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsmndd (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cmnd R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cmnd) $;

--| The identity in a product of monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prds0g (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cmnd R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom c0g R) (cfv Y c0g)) $;

--| The structure power of a monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsmnd (R I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel I V)) (wcel Y cmnd) $;

--| The identity in a structure power of a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pws0g (R I V Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel I V)) (wceq (cxp I (csn c_0)) (cfv Y c0g)) $;

--| The image structure of a monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmnd2 {x3 y1 z q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 y1 z a b) (c_pl: class z a b) (R: class x3 y1 z a b) (U F V: class) (W: class x3 y1 z q p a b) (c_0: class y1 z a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V) (wcel (cv z) V))) (wceq (cfv (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cmnd) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| The image structure of a monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmnd {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R: class a b) (U F V: class) (c_0: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cmnd) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cmnd) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| The image of a monoid under an injection is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem imasmndf1 (B2 R U F V: class):
  $ wceq U (co F R cimas) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf1 V B2 F) (wcel R cmnd)) (wcel U cmnd) $;

--| The binary product of monoids is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsmnd (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel S cmnd)) (wcel T cmnd) $;

--| The identity element of a binary product of monoids. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2025.)
theorem xpsmnd0 (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel S cmnd)) (wceq (cfv T c0g) (cop (cfv R c0g) (cfv S c0g))) $;

--| The (smallest) structure representing a _trivial monoid_ consists of one element. (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem mnd1 (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel M cmnd) $;

--| The singleton element of a _trivial monoid_ is its identity element. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 23-Jan-2020.)
theorem mnd1id (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv M c0g) I) $;

--| Prove that a quotient structure is a monoid. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Aug-2026.)
theorem qusmnd {x3 y1 z q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl: class z) (c_sm: class) (R: class x3 y1 z a b) (U V: class) (X: class x3 y1 z q p a b) (c_0: class y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V) (wcel (cv z) V))) (wbr (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co (cv x3) c_0 c_pl) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cmnd) (wceq (cec c_0 c_sm) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| Hom-set generator class for monoids.
term cmhm: class;

--| Class function taking a monoid to its lattice of submonoids.
term csubmnd: class;

--| A monoid homomorphism is a function on the base sets which preserves the binary operation and the identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_mhm {x3 y1 t f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmhm (cmpo s1 t cmnd cmnd (crab f2 (wa (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv s1) cplusg)) (cv f2)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) cplusg))) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv s1) c0g) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) c0g))) (co (cfv (cv t) cbs) (cfv (cv s1) cbs) cmap))) $;

--| A submonoid is a subset of a monoid which contains the identity and is closed under the operation. Such subsets are themselves monoids with the same identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_submnd {x3 y1 t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csubmnd (cmpt s1 cmnd (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv (cv s1) c0g) (cv t)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv s1) cplusg)) (cv t)) (cv t)) (cv t))) (cpw (cfv (cv s1) cbs)))) $;

--| Property of a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem ismhm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (C c_pl c_pd: class x3 y1) (S T F: class) (Y c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cmnd) (wcel T cmnd)) (w3a (wf B2 C F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) B2) B2) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) Y))) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ ismhm . (Contributed by SN, 27-Jul-2024.)
theorem ismhmd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C c_pl c_pd: class x3 y1) (S T F: class) (c_0 Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c_0 F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) $;

--| Reverse closure of a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem mhmrcl1 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel S cmnd) $;

--| Reverse closure of a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem mhmrcl2 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel T cmnd) $;

--| A monoid homomorphism is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem mhmf (B2 C S T F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wf B2 C F) $;

--| Property of a monoid homomorphism, expressed by a magma homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 17-Apr-2020.)
theorem ismhm0 (B2 C c_pl c_pd S T F Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cmnd) (wcel T cmnd)) (wa (wcel F (co S T cmgmhm)) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) Y))) $;

--| Each monoid homomorphism is a magma homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 29-Feb-2020.)
theorem mhmismgmhm (R S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cmhm)) (wcel F (co R S cmgmhm)) $;

--| Monoid homomorphism depends only on the monoidal attributes of structures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2015.)
theorem mhmpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C J K L M: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv J cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv J cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) C) (wcel (cv y1) C))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv M cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J K cmhm) (co L M cmhm)) $;

--| A monoid homomorphism commutes with composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem mhmlin (B2 c_pl c_pd S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_pl) F) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) c_pd)) $;

--| A monoid homomorphism preserves zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem mhm0 (S T F Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) Y) $;

--| The identity homomorphism on a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2020.)
theorem idmhm (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wcel (cres cid B2) (co M M cmhm)) $;

--| A monoid homomorphism is bijective iff its converse is also a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2019.)
theorem mhmf1o (B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cmhm)) (wb (wf1o B2 C F) (wcel (ccnv F) (co S R cmhm))) $;

--| Tuple-wise additive closure in monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndvcl (B2 c_pl I M X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cmnd) (wcel X (co B2 I cmap)) (wcel Y (co B2 I cmap))) (wcel (co X Y (cof c_pl)) (co B2 I cmap)) $;

--| Tuple-wise associativity in monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndvass (B2 c_pl I M X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (w3a (wcel X (co B2 I cmap)) (wcel Y (co B2 I cmap)) (wcel Z (co B2 I cmap)))) (wceq (co (co X Y (cof c_pl)) Z (cof c_pl)) (co X (co Y Z (cof c_pl)) (cof c_pl))) $;

--| Tuple-wise left identity in monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndvlid (B2 c_pl I M X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel X (co B2 I cmap))) (wceq (co (cxp I (csn c_0)) X (cof c_pl)) X) $;

--| Tuple-wise right identity in monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndvrid (B2 c_pl I M X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel X (co B2 I cmap))) (wceq (co X (cxp I (csn c_0)) (cof c_pl)) X) $;

--| Tuple extension of monoid homomorphisms. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem mhmvlin (B2 c_pl c_pd F I M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv N cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co M N cmhm)) (wcel X (co B2 I cmap)) (wcel Y (co B2 I cmap))) (wceq (ccom F (co X Y (cof c_pl))) (co (ccom F X) (ccom F Y) (cof c_pd))) $;

--| Reverse closure for submonoids. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem submrcl (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wcel M cmnd) $;

--| Expand definition of a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem issubm {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class x3 y1) (S M: class) (c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wb (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (w3a (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) S) S))) $;

--| Submonoids are subsets that are also monoids with the same zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem issubm2 (B2 S H M c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wb (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (w3a (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S) (wcel H cmnd))) $;

--| The submonoid predicate. Analogous to ~ issubg . (Contributed by AV, 1-Feb-2024.)
theorem issubmndb (B2 S G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wa (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel (co G S cress) cmnd)) (wa (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S))) $;

--| Deduction for proving a submonoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem issubmd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff z) (ch th ta et: wff x3 y1) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (M: class z) (c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wa th ta))) et $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) c_0) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab z ps B2) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| If the base set of a monoid is contained in the base set of another monoid, and the group operation of the monoid is the restriction of the group operation of the other monoid to its base set, and the identity element of the other monoid is contained in the base set of the monoid, then the (base set of the) monoid is a submonoid of the other monoid. Analogous to ~ grpissubg . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem mndissubm (B2 S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel H cmnd)) (wi (w3a (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S) (wceq (cfv H cplusg) (cres (cfv G cplusg) (cxp S S)))) (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd))) $;

--| If the base set of a monoid is contained in the base set of another monoid, and the group operation of the monoid is the restriction of the group operation of the other monoid to its base set, and the identity element of the other monoid is contained in the base set of the monoid, then the other monoid restricted to the base set of the monoid is a monoid. Analogous to ~ resgrpisgrp . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem resmndismnd (B2 S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel H cmnd)) (wi (w3a (wss S B2) (wcel c_0 S) (wceq (cfv H cplusg) (cres (cfv G cplusg) (cxp S S)))) (wcel (co G S cress) cmnd)) $;

--| Submonoids are subsets of the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem submss (B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wss S B2) $;

--| Every monoid is trivially a submonoid of itself. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem submid (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wcel B2 (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| Submonoids contain zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem subm0cl (S M c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wcel c_0 S) $;

--| Submonoids are closed under the monoid operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem submcl (c_pl S M X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) S) $;

--| Submonoids are closed under the monoid operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-May-2025.)
theorem submcld (ph: wff) (c_pl S M X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) S) $;

--| Submonoids are themselves monoids under the given operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem submmnd (S H M: class):
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wcel H cmnd) $;

--| The base set of a submonoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem submbas (S H M: class):
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wceq S (cfv H cbs)) $;

--| Submonoids have the same identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem subm0 (S H M c_0: class):
  $ wceq H (co M S cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv M csubmnd)) (wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| A submonoid of a submonoid is a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem subsubm (A2 S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv H csubmnd)) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv G csubmnd)) (wss A2 S))) $;

--| The zero submonoid of an arbitrary monoid. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem _0subm (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wcel (csn c_0) (cfv G csubmnd)) $;

--| The intersection of two submonoids is a submonoid. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2024.)
theorem insubm (A2 B2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv M csubmnd)) (wcel B2 (cfv M csubmnd))) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| The constant zero linear function between two monoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem _0mhm (B2 M N c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv N c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel N cmnd)) (wcel (cxp B2 (csn c_0)) (co M N cmhm)) $;

--| Restriction of a monoid homomorphism to a submonoid is a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem resmhm (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co S X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel X (cfv S csubmnd))) (wcel (cres F X) (co U T cmhm)) $;

--| One direction of ~ resmhm2b . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem resmhm2 (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S U cmhm)) (wcel X (cfv T csubmnd))) (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) $;

--| Restriction of the codomain of a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem resmhm2b (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv T csubmnd)) (wss (crn F) X)) (wb (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel F (co S U cmhm))) $;

--| The composition of monoid homomorphisms is a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem mhmco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cmhm))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cmhm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mhmima and similar theorems, formerly part of proof for ~ mhmima . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 16-Feb-2025.)
theorem mhmimalem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (c_pl: class) (c_po: class x3 y1 z) (F: class) (M N: class x3 y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co M N cmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_po (cfv M cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv N cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv z) X) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_po) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cima F X)) (cima F X)) (cima F X)) $;

--| The homomorphic image of a submonoid is a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Mar-2025.)
theorem mhmima (F M N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co M N cmhm)) (wcel X (cfv M csubmnd))) (wcel (cima F X) (cfv N csubmnd)) $;

--| The equalizer of two monoid homomorphisms is a submonoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem mhmeql (S T F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cmhm))) (wcel (cdm (cin F G)) (cfv S csubmnd)) $;

--| Submonoids are an algebraic closure system. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem submacs (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wcel (cfv G csubmnd) (cfv B2 cacs)) $;

--| Induction in a monoid. In this theorem, ` ps ( x ) ` is the "generic" proposition to be be proved (the first four hypotheses tell its values at y, y+z, 0, A respectively). The two induction hypotheses mndind.i1 and mndind.i2 tell that it is true at 0, that if it is true at y then it is true at y+z (provided z is in ` G ` ). The hypothesis mndind.k tells that ` G ` is generating. (Contributed by SO, 14-Jul-2018.)
theorem mndind {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (th ta et: wff y1 z) (A2: class y1 z) (B2: class x3) (c_pl: class) (G: class x3) (M: class x3 y1 z) (c_0: class y1 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c_0) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G (cfv (cfv M csubmnd) cmrc))) $ >
  $ wi ph ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) G)) ch) th $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph et $;

--| A projection from a product of monoids to one of the factors is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem prdspjmhm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class) (S I V X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cmnd R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv A2 (cv x3))) (co Y (cfv A2 R) cmhm)) $;

--| A projection from a structure power of a monoid to the monoid itself is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem pwspjmhm {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R I V: class) (Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cmnd) (wcel I V) (wcel A2 I)) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv A2 (cv x3))) (co Y R cmhm)) $;

--| Diagonal monoid homomorphism into a structure power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem pwsdiagmhm {x3: setvar} (B2 R: class) (F: class x3) (I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cxp I (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel I W)) (wcel F (co R Y cmhm)) $;

--| Right composition with a function on the index sets yields a monoid homomorphism of structure powers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem pwsco1mhm {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class g2) (C: class) (R: class g2) (F: class) (V W: class g2) (Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R A2 cpws) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co R B2 cpws) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt g2 C (ccom (cv g2) F)) (co Z Y cmhm)) $;

--| Left composition with a monoid homomorphism yields a monoid homomorphism of structure powers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem pwsco2mhm {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class g2) (B2: class) (R S: class g2) (F: class) (V: class g2) (Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R A2 cpws) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co S A2 cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S cmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt g2 B2 (ccom F (cv g2))) (co Y Z cmhm)) $;

--| Lemma for properties of the set of identities of ` G ` . The set of identities of a monoid is exactly the unique identity element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumvallem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class) (O: class x3 y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab x3 (wral y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wceq O (csn c_0)) $;

--| Evaluate a group sum in a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumsubm (ph: wff) (A2 S F G H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F cgsu) (co H F cgsu)) $;

--| Value of a group sum over the zero element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem gsumz {k: setvar} (A2 G V: class) (c_0: class k):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co G (cmpt k A2 c_0) cgsu) c_0) $;

--| Closure of the composite in any submonoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem gsumwsubmcl (S G W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wcel W (cword S))) (wcel (co G W cgsu) S) $;

--| A singleton composite recovers the initial symbol. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem gsumws1 (B2 S G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S B2) (wceq (co G (cs1 S) cgsu) S) $;

--| Closure of the composite of a word in a structure ` G ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem gsumwcl (B2 G W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel W (cword B2))) (wcel (co G W cgsu) B2) $;

--| Homomorphic property of not empty composites of a group sum over a semigroup. Formerly part of proof for ~ gsumccat . (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2023.)
theorem gsumsgrpccat (B2 c_pl G W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G csgrp) (wa (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel X (cword B2))) (wa (wne W c0) (wne X c0))) (wceq (co G (co W X cconcat) cgsu) (co (co G W cgsu) (co G X cgsu) c_pl)) $;

--| Homomorphic property of composites. Second formula in [Lang] p. 4. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Dec-2023.)
theorem gsumccat (B2 c_pl G W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel X (cword B2))) (wceq (co G (co W X cconcat) cgsu) (co (co G W cgsu) (co G X cgsu) c_pl)) $;

--| Valuation of a pair in a monoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem gsumws2 (B2 c_pl S T G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel S B2) (wcel T B2)) (wceq (co G (cs2 S T) cgsu) (co S T c_pl)) $;

--| Homomorphic property of composites with a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsumccatsn (B2 c_pl G W Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel Z B2)) (wceq (co G (co W (cs1 Z) cconcat) cgsu) (co (co G W cgsu) Z c_pl)) $;

--| The primary purpose of the splice construction is to enable local rewrites. Thus, in any monoidal valuation, if a splice does not cause a local change it does not cause a global change. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem gsumspl (ph: wff) (B2 S T F M X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cword B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc0 T cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (co cc0 (cfv S chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cword B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cword B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M X cgsu) (co M Y cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M (co S (cotp F T X) csplice) cgsu) (co M (co S (cotp F T Y) csplice) cgsu)) $;

--| Behavior of homomorphisms on finite monoidal sums. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem gsumwmhm (B2 H M N W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel H (co M N cmhm)) (wcel W (cword B2))) (wceq (cfv (co M W cgsu) H) (co N (ccom H W) cgsu)) $;

--| The submonoid generated by a set of elements is precisely the set of elements which can be expressed as finite products of the generator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem gsumwspan {w: setvar} (B2 G K M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv M csubmnd) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wss G B2)) (wceq (cfv G K) (crn (cmpt w (cword G) (co M (cv w) cgsu)))) $;

--| Extend class definition with the free monoid construction.
term cfrmd: class;

--| Extend class notation with free monoid injection.
term cvrmd: class;

--| Define a free monoid over a set ` i ` of generators, defined as the set of finite strings on ` I ` with the operation of concatenation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_frmd {i: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfrmd (cmpt i cvv (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cword (cv i))) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cres cconcat (cxp (cword (cv i)) (cword (cv i))))))) $;

--| Define a free monoid over a set ` i ` of generators, defined as the set of finite strings on ` I ` with the operation of concatenation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_vrmd {i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvrmd (cmpt i cvv (cmpt j (cv i) (cs1 (cv j)))) $;

--| Value of the free monoid construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmdval (B2 c_pl I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq B2 (cword I)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cres cconcat (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl))) $;

--| The base set of a free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem frmdbas (B2 I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq B2 (cword I)) $;

--| An element of the base set of a free monoid is a string on the generators. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem frmdelbas (B2 I M X: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wcel X (cword I)) $;

--| The monoid operation of a free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Nov-2024.)
theorem frmdplusg (B2 c_pl I M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cres cconcat (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| Value of the monoid operation of the free monoid construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmdadd (B2 c_pl I M X Y: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co X Y cconcat)) $;

--| The canonical injection from the generating set ` I ` to the base set of the free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem vrmdfval {j: setvar} (U: class j) (I V: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq U (cmpt j I (cs1 (cv j)))) $;

--| The value of the generating elements of a free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem vrmdval (A2 U I V: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel A2 I)) (wceq (cfv A2 U) (cs1 A2)) $;

--| The mapping from the index set to the generators is a function into the free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem vrmdf (U I V: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wf I (cword I) U) $;

--| A free monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem frmdmnd (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel M cmnd) $;

--| The identity of the free monoid is the empty word. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmd0 (I M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq c0 (cfv M c0g) $;

--| The set of words taking values in a subset is a (free) submonoid of the free monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem frmdsssubm (I J M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wss J I)) (wcel (cword J) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| Any word in a free monoid can be expressed as the sum of the singletons composing it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmdgsum (U I M V W: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel W (cword I))) (wceq (co M (ccom U W) cgsu) W) $;

--| A subset of generators is contained in a submonoid iff the set of words on the generators is in the submonoid. This can be viewed as an elementary way of saying "the monoidal closure of ` J ` is ` Word J ` ". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem frmdss2 (A2 U I J M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wss J I) (wcel A2 (cfv M csubmnd))) (wb (wss (cima U J) A2) (wss (cword J) A2)) $;

--| Any assignment of the generators to target elements can be extended (uniquely) to a homomorphism from a free monoid to an arbitrary other monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmdup1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (E: class x3) (G I: class) (M X: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cword I) (co G (ccom A2 (cv x3)) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co M G cmhm)) $;

--| The evaluation map has the intended behavior on the generators. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem frmdup2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (U E: class x3) (G I: class) (M X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cword I) (co G (ccom A2 (cv x3)) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I B2 A2) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv Y U) E) (cfv Y A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ frmdup3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem frmdup3lem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 U F G I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel I V) (wf I B2 A2)) (wa (wcel F (co M G cmhm)) (wceq (ccom F U) A2))) (wceq F (cmpt x3 (cword I) (co G (ccom A2 (cv x3)) cgsu))) $;

--| Universal property of the free monoid by existential uniqueness. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem frmdup3 {m: setvar} (A2 B2 U G I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv I cfrmd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv I cvrmd) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel I V) (wf I B2 A2)) (wreu m (wceq (ccom (cv m) U) A2) (co M G cmhm)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of monoids of endofunctions.
term cefmnd: class;

--| Define the monoid of endofunctions on set ` x ` . We represent the monoid as the set of functions from ` x ` to itself ( ` ( x ^m x ) ` ) under function composition, and topologize it as a function space assuming the set is discrete. Analogous to the former definition of ` SymGrp ` , see ~ df-symg and ~ symgvalstruct . (Contributed by AV, 25-Jan-2024.)
axiom df_efmnd {x3 f2 g2 b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cefmnd (cmpt x3 cvv (csb b (co (cv x3) (cv x3) cmap) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv b) (cv b) (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2)))) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (cxp (cv x3) (csn (cpw (cv x3)))) cpt))))) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on set ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmnd {f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 c_pl G J V: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 A2 cmap) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (cxp A2 (csn (cpw A2))) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq G (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J))) $;

--| The base set of the monoid of endofunctions on class ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndbas (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 A2 cmap) $;

--| The base set of the monoid of endofunctions on class ` A ` is the set of functions from ` A ` into itself. (Contributed by AV, 29-Mar-2024.)
theorem efmndbasabf {f2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 G: class f2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab f2 (wf A2 A2 (cv f2))) $;

--| Two ways of saying a function is a mapping of ` A ` to itself. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem elefmndbas (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel F B2) (wf A2 A2 F)) $;

--| Two ways of saying a function is a mapping of ` A ` to itself. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Mar-2024.)
theorem elefmndbas2 (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F B2) (wf A2 A2 F)) $;

--| Elements in the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` are functions from ` A ` into itself. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndbasf (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wf A2 A2 F) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on ` n ` objects has cardinality ` n ^ n ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndhash (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) (co (cfv A2 chash) (cfv A2 chash) cexp)) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a finite set ` A ` is finite. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndbasfi (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| The function value of an endofunction. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndfv (A2 B2 F G X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel X A2)) (wcel (cfv X F) A2) $;

--| The topology of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . This component is defined on a larger set than the true base - the product topology is defined on the set of all functions, not just endofunctions - but the definition of ` TopOpen ` ensures that it is trimmed down before it gets use. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndtset (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cxp A2 (csn (cpw A2))) cpt) (cfv G cts)) $;

--| The group operation of a monoid of endofunctions is the function composition. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndplusg {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (c_pl G: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $;

--| The value of the group operation of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndov (A2 B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (ccom X Y)) $;

--| The group operation of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` is closed. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndcl (A2 B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) B2) $;

--| The topology of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndtopn (B2 G V X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv X cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (co (cfv (cxp X (csn (cpw X))) cpt) B2 crest) (cfv G ctopn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ symggrp and ~ efmndsgrp . Conditions for an operation to be associative. Formerly part of proof for ~ symggrp . (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2024.)
theorem symggrplem {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl X Y: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (ccom (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_pl) (co X (co Y Z c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a class ` A ` is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndmgm (A2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel G cmgm $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a class ` A ` is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndsgrp (A2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel G csgrp $;

--| The identity function restricted to a set ` A ` is an element of the base set of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem ielefmnd (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cres cid A2) (cfv G cbs)) $;

--| The identity function restricted to a set ` A ` is the identity element of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndid (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cres cid A2) (cfv G c0g)) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a set ` A ` is actually a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmndmnd (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| Even the monoid of endofunctions on the empty set is actually a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmnd0nmnd:
  $ wcel (cfv c0 cefmnd) cmnd $;

--| The base set of the monoid of endofunctions on the empty set is the singleton containing the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 31-Mar-2024.)
theorem efmndbas0:
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv c0 cefmnd) cbs) (csn c0) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a singleton has cardinality ` 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmnd1hash (A2 B2 G I V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (csn I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) c1) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a singleton consists of the identity only. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2024.)
theorem efmnd1bas (A2 B2 G I V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (csn I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq B2 (csn (csn (cop I I)))) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a (proper) pair has cardinality ` 4 ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem efmnd2hash (A2 B2 G I J V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr I J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel J W) (wne I J)) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) c4) $;

--| If the base set of a monoid is contained in the base set of the monoid of endofunctions on a set ` A ` , contains the identity function and has the function composition as group operation, then its base set is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on set ` A ` . Analogous to ~ pgrpsubgsymg . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem submefmnd {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S: class f2 g2) (F: class) (M V c_0: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wa (w3a (wcel S cmnd) (wss F B2) (wcel c_0 F)) (wceq (cfv S cplusg) (cmpo f2 g2 F F (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))))) (wcel F (cfv M csubmnd))) $;

--| The set of surjective endofunctions on a set ` A ` is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2024.)
theorem sursubmefmnd {h: setvar} (A2: class) (M V: class h):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cab h (wfo A2 A2 (cv h))) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| The set of injective endofunctions on a set ` A ` is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2024.)
theorem injsubmefmnd {h: setvar} (A2: class) (M V: class h):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cab h (wf1 A2 A2 (cv h))) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| The singleton containing only the identity function restricted to a set is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on this set. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem idressubmefmnd (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (csn (cres cid A2)) (cfv G csubmnd)) $;

--| The structure with the singleton containing only the identity function restricted to a set ` A ` as base set and the function composition as group operation, constructed by (structure) restricting the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` to that singleton, is a monoid whose base set is a subset of the base set of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem idresefmnd (A2 E G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq E (co G (csn (cres cid A2)) cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wa (wcel E cmnd) (wss (cfv E cbs) (cfv G cbs))) $;

--| The modulo function ` I ` is an endofunction on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1ibas {x3: setvar} (I M N: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wcel I (cfv M cbs) $;

--| The modulo function ` I ` is idempotent. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1iidm {x3: setvar} (I M: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq (ccom I I) I $;

--| The constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` are endofunctions on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-rep and shorten proof. (Revised by GG, 2-Apr-2026.)
theorem smndex1gbas {x3 n: setvar} (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel (cfv K G) (cfv M cbs)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ smndex1gbas as of 2-Apr-2026. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem smndex1gbasOLD {x3 n: setvar} (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel (cfv K G) (cfv M cbs)) $;

--| The composition of a constant function ` ( G `` K ) ` with another endofunction on ` NN0 ` results in ` ( G `` K ) ` itself. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by GG, 2-Apr-2026.)
theorem smndex1gid {x3 n: setvar} (F: class n) (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv M cbs)) (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (ccom (cfv K G) F) (cfv K G)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ smndex1gid as of 2-Apr-2026. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem smndex1gidOLD {x3 n: setvar} (F: class n) (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv M cbs)) (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (ccom (cfv K G) F) (cfv K G)) $;

--| The composition of the modulo function ` I ` and a constant function ` ( G `` K ) ` results in ` ( G `` K ) ` itself. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.) Avoid ~ ax-rep . (Revised by GG, 2-Apr-2026.)
theorem smndex1igid {x3 n: setvar} (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wceq (ccom I (cfv K G)) (cfv K G)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ smndex1igid as of 2-Apr-2026. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem smndex1igidOLD {x3 n: setvar} (G I: class x3 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wceq (ccom I (cfv K G)) (cfv K G)) $;

--| The modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` are endofunctions on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1basss {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (G: class x3) (I: class x3 n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wss B2 (cfv M cbs) $;

--| The base set of the monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` restricted to the modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1bas {x3 n: setvar} (B2 S: class x3 n) (G: class x3) (I: class x3 n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv S cbs) B2 $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` restricted to the modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1mgm {x3 n: setvar} (B2 S: class x3 n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wcel S cmgm $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` restricted to the modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1sgrp {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wcel S csgrp $;

--| Lemma for ~ smndex1mnd and ~ smndex1id . (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1mndlem {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wa (wceq (ccom I X) X) (wceq (ccom X I) X)) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` restricted to the modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` is a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1mnd {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wcel S cmnd $;

--| The modulo function ` I ` is the identity of the monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` restricted to the modulo function ` I ` and the constant functions ` ( G `` K ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1id {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S c0g) $;

--| The identity of the monoid ` M ` of endofunctions on set ` NN0 ` is not contained in the base set of the constructed monoid ` S ` . (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex1n0mnd {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wnel (cfv M c0g) B2 $;

--| The base set ` B ` of the constructed monoid ` S ` is not a submonoid of the monoid ` M ` of endofunctions on set ` NN0 ` , although ` M e. Mnd ` and ` S e. Mnd ` and ` B C_ ( Base `` M ) ` hold. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2024.)
theorem nsmndex1 {x3 n: setvar} (B2: class x3 n) (S: class n) (G I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) N cmo)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n (co cc0 N cfzo) (cmpt x3 cn0 (cv n))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cun (csn I) (ciun n (co cc0 N cfzo) (csn (cfv (cv n) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co M B2 cress) $ >
  $ wnel B2 (cfv M csubmnd) $;

--| The doubling function ` D ` is an endofunction on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex2dbas {x3: setvar} (B2 D M c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cn0 (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wcel D B2 $;

--| The doubling function ` D ` has no right inverse in the monoid of endofunctions on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex2dnrinv {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 D M c_0: class x3 f2):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cn0 (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wral f2 (wne (ccom D (cv f2)) c_0) B2 $;

--| The halving functions ` H ` are endofunctions on ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex2hbas {x3: setvar} (B2 D H M N c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cn0 (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cn0 (cif (wbr c2 (cv x3) cdvds) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) N)) $ >
  $ wcel H B2 $;

--| The halving functions ` H ` are left inverses of the doubling function ` D ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem smndex2dlinvh {x3: setvar} (B2 D H M: class x3) (N: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq M (cfv cn0 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cn0 (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cn0 (cif (wbr c2 (cv x3) cdvds) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) N)) $ >
  $ wceq (ccom H D) c_0 $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ mgm2nsgrp : ` M ` is a magma, even if ` A = B ` ( ` M ` is the trivial magma in this case, see ~ mgmb1mgm1 ). (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2nsgrplem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ mgm2nsgrp . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2nsgrplem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co (co A2 A2 c_op) B2 c_op) A2) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ mgm2nsgrp . (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2nsgrplem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co A2 (co A2 B2 c_op) c_op) B2) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ mgm2nsgrp : M is not a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2nsgrplem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wnel M csgrp) $;

--| A small magma (with two elements) which is not a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2nsgrp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wa (wcel M cmgm) (wnel M csgrp)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ sgrp2nmnd : ` M ` is a magma, even if ` A = B ` ( ` M ` is the trivial magma in this case, see ~ mgmb1mgm1 ). (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmndlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ sgrp2nmnd . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmndlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C S: class) (M: class y1) (c_op: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel C S)) (wceq (co A2 C c_op) A2) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ sgrp2nmnd . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmndlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C S: class) (M: class y1) (c_op: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C S) (wcel B2 S) (wne A2 B2)) (wceq (co B2 C c_op) B2) $;

--| A small semigroup (with two elements) with two right identities which are different if ` A =/= B ` . (Contributed by AV, 10-Feb-2020.)
theorem sgrp2rid2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M V W: class y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_op) (cv y1)) S) S) $;

--| A small semigroup (with two elements) with two right identities which are different. (Contributed by AV, 10-Feb-2020.)
theorem sgrp2rid2ex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1 z) (c_op: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wrex x3 (wrex z (wral y1 (w3a (wne (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_op) (cv y1)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) (cv y1))) S) S) S) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ sgrp2nmnd : M is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmndlem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class) (M: class y1):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wcel M csgrp) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ sgrp2nmnd : M is not a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmndlem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wnel M cmnd) $;

--| A small semigroup (with two elements) which is not a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2nmnd {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wceq S (cpr A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) S $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 S S (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv S chash) c2) (wa (wcel M csgrp) (wnel M cmnd)) $;

--| There is a magma which is not a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgmnsgrpex {m: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wnel (cv m) csgrp) cmgm $;

--| There is a semigroup which is not a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrpnmndex {m: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wnel (cv m) cmnd) csgrp $;

--| The class of all semigroups is a proper subclass of the class of all magmas. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrpssmgm:
  $ wpss csgrp cmgm $;

--| The class of all monoids is a proper subclass of the class of all semigroups. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem mndsssgrp:
  $ wpss cmnd csgrp $;

--| The domain of the operation of a degenerate magma. (Contributed by AV, 18-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgmopdm (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c0) c1o)))) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm (cfv M cplusg)) (cxp (csn c1o) (ctp c0 c1o c2o)) $;

--| The base set of a degenerate magma. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgmbas (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c0) c1o)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr c0 c1o) $;

--| The operation of a degenerate magma is not a function on its base set. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgmnfn (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c0) c1o)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wn (wfn (cfv M cplusg) (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| A degenerate magma: although the operation is not defined for all pairs of elements of the base set ( ` ( (/) ( +g `` M ) 1o ) ` and ` ( (/) ( +g `` M ) (/) ) ` are not defined, and therefore are ` (/) ` by definition, which is contained in the base set), and its domain is not (a subset of) the base set ( ` 2o ` is in the domain of the operation, but not in the base set) , the structure ` M ` is still a magma according to our definition. (Contributed by AV, 18-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgm (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c0) c1o)))) $ >
  $ wcel M cmgm $;

--| The domain of the operation of a second degenerate magma. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgm2opdm (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c2o)))) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm (cfv M cplusg)) (cxp (csn c1o) (cpr c1o c2o)) $;

--| The operation of a second degenerate magma is not a function. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgm2nfun (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c2o)))) $ >
  $ wn (wfun (cfv M cplusg)) $;

--| A degenerate magma: although the operation is not defined for all pairs of elements of the base set, and its domain is not (a subset of) the base set, and the operation is not a function (see ~ degenmgm2nfun ), the structure ` M ` is still a magma according to our definition. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2026.)
theorem degenmgm2 (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr c0 c1o)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctp (cop (cop c1o c1o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c1o) (cop (cop c1o c2o) c2o)))) $ >
  $ wcel M cmgm $;

--| The operation of the monoid of the power set of a class ` A ` under union. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2024.)
theorem pwmndgplus {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (M: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) (cpw A2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 (cpw A2) (cpw A2) (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cpw A2)) (wcel Y (cpw A2))) (wceq (co X Y (cfv M cplusg)) (cun X Y)) $;

--| The identity of the monoid of the power set of a class ` A ` under union is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2024.)
theorem pwmndid {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (M: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) (cpw A2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 (cpw A2) (cpw A2) (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M c0g) c0 $;

--| The power set of a class ` A ` is a monoid under union. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2024.)
theorem pwmnd {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (M: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) (cpw A2) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 (cpw A2) (cpw A2) (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wcel M cmnd $;

--| Extend class notation with class of all groups.
term cgrp: class;

--| Extend class notation with inverse of group element.
term cminusg: class;

--| Extend class notation with group subtraction (or division) operation.
term csg: class;

--| Define class of all groups. A group is a monoid ( ~ df-mnd ) whose internal operation is such that every element admits a left inverse (which can be proven to be a two-sided inverse). Thus, a group ` G ` is an algebraic structure formed from a base set of elements (notated ` ( Base `` G ) ` per ~ df-base ) and an internal group operation (notated ` ( +g `` G ) ` per ~ df-plusg ). The operation combines any two elements of the group base set and must satisfy the 4 group axioms: closure (the result of the group operation must always be a member of the base set, see ~ grpcl ), associativity (so ` ( ( a +g b ) +g c ) = ( a +g ( b +g c ) ) ` for any a, b, c, see ~ grpass ), identity (there must be an element ` e = ( 0g `` G ) ` such that ` e +g a = a +g e = a ` for any a), and inverse (for each element a in the base set, there must be an element ` b = invg a ` in the base set such that ` a +g b = b +g a = e ` ). It can be proven that the identity element is unique ( ~ grpideu ). Groups need not be commutative; a commutative group is an Abelian group (see ~ df-abl ). Subgroups can often be formed from groups, see ~ df-subg . An example of an (Abelian) group is the set of complex numbers ` CC ` over the group operation ` + ` (addition), as proven in ~ cnaddablx ; an Abelian group is a group as proven in ~ ablgrp . Other structures include groups, including unital rings ( ~ df-ring ) and fields ( ~ df-field ). (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_grp {g2 m a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgrp (crab g2 (wral a (wrex m (wceq (co (cv m) (cv a) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cfv (cv g2) c0g)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) cmnd) $;

--| Define inverse of group element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
axiom df_minusg {x3 w g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cminusg (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (crio w (wceq (co (cv w) (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cfv (cv g2) c0g)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)))) $;

--| Define group subtraction (also called division for multiplicative groups). (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
axiom df_sbg {x3 y1 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq csg (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv g2) cminusg)) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)))) $;

--| The predicate "is a group". (This theorem demonstrates the use of symbols as variable names, first proposed by FL in 2010.) (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem isgrp {m a: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class m a) (G: class) (c_0: class m a):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wral a (wrex m (wceq (co (cv m) (cv a) c_pl) c_0) B2) B2)) $;

--| A group is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grpmnd (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| Closure of the operation of a group. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpcl (B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) B2) $;

--| A group operation is associative. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpass (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_pl) (co X (co Y Z c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| Every member of a group has a left inverse. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grpinvex {y1: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class y1) (G X: class) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) X c_pl) c_0) B2) $;

--| The two-sided identity element of a group is unique. Lemma 2.2.1(a) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpideu {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class) (c_0: class u):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wreu u (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) B2) B2) $;

--| A group operation is associative. (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grpassd (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_pl) (co X (co Y Z c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| A group is a monoid. (Contributed by SN, 1-Jun-2024.)
theorem grpmndd (ph: wff) (G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| Closure of the operation of a group. (Contributed by SN, 29-Jul-2024.)
theorem grpcld (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) B2) $;

--| The group addition operation is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grpplusf (B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 F) $;

--| The group addition operation is a function onto the base set/set of group elements. (Contributed by NM, 30-Oct-2006.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem grpplusfo (B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wfo (cxp B2 B2) B2 F) $;

--| The underlying set of a group operation which is a restriction of a structure. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Mar-2008.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem resgrpplusfrn (B2 S F G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv H cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel H cgrp) (wss S B2)) (wceq S (crn F)) $;

--| If two structures have the same group components (properties), one is a group iff the other one is. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem grppropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cgrp) (wcel L cgrp)) $;

--| If two structures have the same group components (properties), one is a group iff the other one is. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem grpprop (K L: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K cplusg) (cfv L cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cgrp) (wcel L cgrp) $;

--| Generalize a specific 2-element group ` L ` to show that any set ` K ` with the same (relevant) properties is also a group. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grppropstr (B2 c_pl K L: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K cplusg) c_pl $ >
  $ wceq L (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cgrp) (wcel L cgrp) $;

--| Show that a structure extending a constructed group (e.g., a ring) is also a group. This allows to prove that a constructed potential ring ` R ` is a group before we know that it is also a ring. (Theorem ~ ringgrp , on the other hand, requires that we know in advance that ` R ` is a ring.) (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem grpss (B2 c_pl R G: class):
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wss G R $ >
  $ wfun R $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (wcel R cgrp) $;

--| Deduce a group from its properties. In this version of ~ isgrpd2 , we don't assume there is an expression for the inverse of ` x ` . (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2013.)
theorem isgrpd2e {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| Deduce a group from its properties. ` N ` (negative) is normally dependent on ` x ` i.e. read it as ` N ( x ) ` . Note: normally we don't use a ` ph ` antecedent on hypotheses that name structure components, since they can be eliminated with ~ eqid , but we make an exception for theorems such as ~ isgrpd2 , ~ ismndd , and ~ islmodd since theorems using them often rewrite the structure components. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2013.)
theorem isgrpd2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G: class) (N c_0: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel N B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| Deduce a group from its properties. In this version of ~ isgrpd , we don't assume there is an expression for the inverse of ` x ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 1-Dec-2009.) (Revised by NM, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem isgrpde {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| Deduce a group from its properties. Unlike ~ isgrpd2 , this one goes straight from the base properties rather than going through ` Mnd ` . ` N ` (negative) is normally dependent on ` x ` i.e. read it as ` N ( x ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem isgrpd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G: class) (N: class x3 z) (c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel N B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| Properties that determine a group. ` N ` (negative) is normally dependent on ` x ` i.e. read it as ` N ( x ) ` . (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2011.)
theorem isgrpi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class) (N: class x3 z) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wcel c_0 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel N B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) $ >
  $ wcel G cgrp $;

--| A group is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem grpsgrp (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G csgrp) $;

--| A group is a magma, deduction form. (Contributed by SN, 14-Apr-2025.)
theorem grpmgmd (ph: wff) (G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmgm) $;

--| Alternate definition of a group as semigroup with a left identity and a left inverse for each element. This "definition" is weaker than ~ df-grp , based on the definition of a monoid which provides a left and a right identity. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem dfgrp2 {x3 i n: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wcel G csgrp) (wrex n (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv n) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wrex i (wceq (co (cv i) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv n)) B2)) B2) B2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of a group as a set with a closed, associative operation, a left identity and a left inverse for each element. Alternate definition in [Lang] p. 7. (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem dfgrp2e {x3 y1 z i n: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) B2)) B2) B2) (wrex n (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv n) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wrex i (wceq (co (cv i) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv n)) B2)) B2) B2)) $;

--| Properties that determine a group. Read ` N ` as ` N ( x ) ` . Note: This theorem has hard-coded structure indices for demonstration purposes. It is not intended for general use. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2011.)
theorem isgrpix {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class) (N: class x3 z) (c_0: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel c_pl cvv $ >
  $ wceq G (cpr (cop c1 B2) (cop c2 c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wcel c_0 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel N B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) $ >
  $ wcel G cgrp $;

--| The identity element of a group belongs to the group. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpidcl (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel c_0 B2) $;

--| The base set of a group is not empty. (Contributed by Szymon Jaroszewicz, 3-Apr-2007.)
theorem grpbn0 (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wne B2 c0) $;

--| The identity element of a group is a left identity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2011.)
theorem grplid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co c_0 X c_pl) X) $;

--| The identity element of a group is a right identity. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2011.)
theorem grprid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X c_0 c_pl) X) $;

--| The identity element of a group is a left identity. Deduction associated with ~ grplid . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grplidd (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c_0 X c_pl) X) $;

--| The identity element of a group is a right identity. Deduction associated with ~ grprid . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grpridd (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X c_0 c_pl) X) $;

--| A group is not empty. (Contributed by Szymon Jaroszewicz, 3-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpn0 (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wne G c0) $;

--| A finite group has positive integer size. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem hashfingrpnn (ph: wff) (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 chash) cn) $;

--| Right cancellation law for groups. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grprcan (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wceq (co X Z c_pl) (co Y Z c_pl)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| The left inverse element of a group is unique. Lemma 2.2.1(b) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpinveu {y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wreu y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) X c_pl) c_0) B2) $;

--| Two ways of saying that an element of a group is the identity element. Provides a convenient way to compute the value of the identity element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpid (B2 c_pl G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wb (wceq (co X X c_pl) X) (wceq c_0 X)) $;

--| Properties showing that an element ` Z ` is the identity element of a group. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-2013.)
theorem isgrpid2 (B2 c_pl G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wb (wa (wcel Z B2) (wceq (co Z Z c_pl) Z)) (wceq c_0 Z)) $;

--| Deduce the identity element of a group from its properties. Useful in conjunction with ~ isgrpd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem grpidd2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl: class) (G: class x3) (c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g)) $;

--| The inverse function of a group. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-rep , see ~ grpinvfvalALT . (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2013.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-rep . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem grpinvfval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class y1) (G: class) (N: class x3 y1) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt x3 B2 (crio y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) B2)) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ grpinvfval using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2013.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grpinvfvalALT {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class y1) (G: class) (N: class x3 y1) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt x3 B2 (crio y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) B2)) $;

--| The inverse of a group element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2013.)
theorem grpinvval {y1: setvar} (B2: class) (c_pl: class y1) (G: class) (N: class y1) (X: class) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (cfv X N) (crio y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) X c_pl) c_0) B2)) $;

--| Functionality of the group inverse function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem grpinvfn (B2 G N: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wfn N B2 $;

--| The group inverse function is compatible with identity-function protection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem grpinvfvi (G N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv (cfv G cid) cminusg) $;

--| Group subtraction (division) operation. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-rep , see ~ grpsubfvalALT . (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-rep . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 17-Aug-2023.) (Proof shortened by AV, 19-Feb-2024.)
theorem grpsubfval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl G I: class) (c_mi: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) I) c_pl)) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ grpsubfval using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 19-Feb-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem grpsubfvalALT {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl G I: class) (c_mi: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) I) c_pl)) $;

--| Group subtraction (division) operation. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpsubval (B2 c_pl G I c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_mi) (co X (cfv Y I) c_pl)) $;

--| The group inversion operation is a function on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2015.)
theorem grpinvf (B2 G N: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wf B2 B2 N) $;

--| A group element's inverse is a group element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2015.)
theorem grpinvcl (B2 G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wcel (cfv X N) B2) $;

--| A group element's inverse is a group element. (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grpinvcld (ph: wff) (B2 G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X N) B2) $;

--| The left inverse of a group element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grplinv (B2 c_pl G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (cfv X N) X c_pl) c_0) $;

--| The right inverse of a group element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2015.)
theorem grprinv (B2 c_pl G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X (cfv X N) c_pl) c_0) $;

--| The inverse of a group element expressed in terms of the identity element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpinvid1 (B2 c_pl G N X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wceq (cfv X N) Y) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) c_0)) $;

--| The inverse of a group element expressed in terms of the identity element. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpinvid2 (B2 c_pl G N X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wceq (cfv X N) Y) (wceq (co Y X c_pl) c_0)) $;

--| Properties showing that a function ` M ` is the inverse function of a group. (Contributed by NM, 7-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem isgrpinv {x3: setvar} (B2 c_pl G M N c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wb (wa (wf B2 B2 M) (wral x3 (wceq (co (cfv (cv x3) M) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) B2)) (wceq N M)) $;

--| The left inverse of a group element. Deduction associated with ~ grplinv . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grplinvd (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv X N) X c_pl) c_0) $;

--| The right inverse of a group element. Deduction associated with ~ grprinv . (Contributed by SN, 29-Jan-2025.)
theorem grprinvd (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X (cfv X N) c_pl) c_0) $;

--| In a group, every member has a left and right inverse. (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2021.)
theorem grplrinv {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class x3) (G: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) c_0) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) c_0)) B2) B2) $;

--| A group's properties using the explicit identity element. (Contributed by NM, 5-Feb-2010.) (Revised by AV, 1-Sep-2021.)
theorem grpidinv2 {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 c_pl G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 B2)) (wa (wa (wceq (co c_0 A2 c_pl) A2) (wceq (co A2 c_0 c_pl) A2)) (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv y1) A2 c_pl) c_0) (wceq (co A2 (cv y1) c_pl) c_0)) B2)) $;

--| A group has a left and right identity element, and every member has a left and right inverse. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2006.) (Revised by AV, 1-Sep-2021.)
theorem grpidinv {x3 y1 u: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class x3) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wrex u (wral x3 (wa (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv u) c_pl) (cv x3))) (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv u)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv u))) B2)) B2) B2) $;

--| The inverse of the identity element of a group. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2011.)
theorem grpinvid (G N c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (cfv c_0 N) c_0) $;

--| Left cancellation law for groups. (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-2011.)
theorem grplcan (B2 c_pl G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wceq (co Z X c_pl) (co Z Y c_pl)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| An associative cancellation law for groups. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem grpasscan1 (B2 c_pl G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X (co (cfv X N) Y c_pl) c_pl) Y) $;

--| An associative cancellation law for groups. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem grpasscan2 (B2 c_pl G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co (co X (cfv Y N) c_pl) Y c_pl) X) $;

--| If right adding an element of a group to an arbitrary element of the group results in this element, the added element is the identity element and vice versa. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2019.)
theorem grpidrcan (B2 c_pl G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wb (wceq (co X Z c_pl) X) (wceq Z c_0)) $;

--| If left adding an element of a group to an arbitrary element of the group results in this element, the added element is the identity element and vice versa. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2019.)
theorem grpidlcan (B2 c_pl G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Z B2)) (wb (wceq (co Z X c_pl) X) (wceq Z c_0)) $;

--| Double inverse law for groups. Lemma 2.2.1(c) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpinvinv (B2 G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X N) N) X) $;

--| The group inverse is its own inverse function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grpinvcnv (B2 G N: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (ccnv N) N) $;

--| The group inverse is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 22-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by SN, 8-Jul-2025.)
theorem grpinv11 (ph: wff) (B2 G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv X N) (cfv Y N)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| The group inverse is a one-to-one onto function. (Contributed by NM, 22-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grpinvf1o (ph: wff) (B2 G N: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 B2 N) $;

--| The inverse of a nonzero group element is not zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem grpinvnz (B2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wne X c_0)) (wne (cfv X N) c_0) $;

--| The inverse of a nonzero group element is a nonzero group element. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem grpinvnzcl (B2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X (cdif B2 (csn c_0)))) (wcel (cfv X N) (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) $;

--| Subtraction of an inverse. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem grpsubinv (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X (cfv Y N) c_mi) (co X Y c_pl)) $;

--| Left multiplication by a group element is a bijection on any group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem grplmulf1o {x3: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3) (G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (co X (cv x3) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wf1o B2 B2 F) $;

--| Right addition by a group element is a bijection on any group. (Contributed by SN, 28-Apr-2012.)
theorem grpraddf1o {x3: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3) (G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (co (cv x3) X c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wf1o B2 B2 F) $;

--| If two structures have the same group components (properties), they have the same group inversion function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem grpinvpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cminusg) (cfv L cminusg)) $;

--| If the base set of a group is contained in the base set of another group, and the group operation of the group is the restriction of the group operation of the other group to its base set, then both groups have the same identity element. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2019.)
theorem grpidssd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2 S M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv M cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv S cplusg))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M c0g) (cfv S c0g)) $;

--| If the base set of a group is contained in the base set of another group, and the group operation of the group is the restriction of the group operation of the other group to its base set, then the elements of the first group have the same inverses in both groups. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2019.)
theorem grpinvssd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2 S M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv M cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv S cplusg))) B2) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (cfv X (cfv S cminusg)) (cfv X (cfv M cminusg)))) $;

--| The inverse of the group operation reverses the arguments. Lemma 2.2.1(d) of [Herstein] p. 55. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2006.)
theorem grpinvadd (B2 c_pl G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_pl) N) (co (cfv Y N) (cfv X N) c_pl)) $;

--| Functionality of group subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2014.)
theorem grpsubf (B2 G c_mi: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_mi) $;

--| Closure of group subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpsubcl (B2 G c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_mi) B2) $;

--| Right cancellation law for group subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpsubrcan (B2 G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wceq (co X Z c_mi) (co Y Z c_mi)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Inverse of a group subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2014.)
theorem grpinvsub (B2 G c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_mi) N) (co Y X c_mi)) $;

--| A ~ df-neg -like equation for inverse in terms of group subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem grpinvval2 (B2 G c_mi N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X N) (co c_0 X c_mi)) $;

--| Subtraction of a group element from itself. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpsubid (B2 G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X X c_mi) c_0) $;

--| Subtraction of the identity from a group element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.)
theorem grpsubid1 (B2 G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co X c_0 c_mi) X) $;

--| If the difference between two group elements is zero, they are equal. ( ~ subeq0 analog.) (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jan-2011.) (Revised by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpsubeq0 (B2 G c_mi X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wb (wceq (co X Y c_mi) c_0) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Subtraction expressed as addition of the difference of the identity element and the subtrahend. (Contributed by AV, 9-Nov-2019.)
theorem grpsubadd0sub (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_mi) (co X (co c_0 Y c_mi) c_pl)) $;

--| Relationship between group subtraction and addition. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.)
theorem grpsubadd (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wb (wceq (co X Y c_mi) Z) (wceq (co Z Y c_pl) X)) $;

--| Double group subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpsubsub (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co X (co Y Z c_mi) c_mi) (co X (co Z Y c_mi) c_pl)) $;

--| Associative-type law for group subtraction and addition. (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2014.)
theorem grpaddsubass (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Z c_mi) (co X (co Y Z c_mi) c_pl)) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction ( ~ pncan analog). (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2014.)
theorem grppncan (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co (co X Y c_pl) Y c_mi) X) $;

--| Cancellation law for subtraction ( ~ npcan analog). (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem grpnpcan (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co (co X Y c_mi) Y c_pl) X) $;

--| Double group subtraction ( ~ subsub4 analog). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpsubsub4 (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_mi) Z c_mi) (co X (co Z Y c_pl) c_mi)) $;

--| Cancellation law for mixed addition and subtraction. ( ~ pnpcan2 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grppnpcan2 (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Z c_pl) (co Y Z c_pl) c_mi) (co X Y c_mi)) $;

--| Cancellation law for group subtraction. ( ~ npncan analog.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpnpncan (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_mi) (co Y Z c_mi) c_pl) (co X Z c_mi)) $;

--| Cancellation law for group subtraction ( ~ npncan2 analog). (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2019.)
theorem grpnpncan0 (B2 c_pl G c_mi X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2))) (wceq (co (co X Y c_mi) (co Y X c_mi) c_pl) c_0) $;

--| Cancellation law for group subtraction. ( ~ nnncan2 analog.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem grpnnncan2 (B2 G c_mi X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wceq (co (co X Z c_mi) (co Y Z c_mi) c_mi) (co X Y c_mi)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfgrp3 . (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem dfgrp3lem {x3 y1 u i r a l: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G csgrp) (wne B2 c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wrex l (wceq (co (cv l) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2) (wrex r (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv r) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2)) B2) B2)) (wrex u (wral a (wa (wceq (co (cv u) (cv a) c_pl) (cv a)) (wrex i (wceq (co (cv i) (cv a) c_pl) (cv u)) B2)) B2) B2) $;

--| Alternate definition of a group as semigroup (with at least one element) which is also a quasigroup, i.e. a magma in which solutions ` x ` and ` y ` of the equations ` ( a .+ x ) = b ` and ` ( x .+ a ) = b ` exist. Theorem 3.2 of [Bruck] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem dfgrp3 {x3 y1 r l: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel G csgrp) (wne B2 c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wrex l (wceq (co (cv l) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2) (wrex r (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv r) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2)) B2) B2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of a group as a set with a closed, associative operation, for which solutions ` x ` and ` y ` of the equations ` ( a .+ x ) = b ` and ` ( x .+ a ) = b ` exist. Exercise 1 of [Herstein] p. 57. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Aug-2021.)
theorem dfgrp3e {x3 y1 z r l: setvar} (B2 c_pl G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wne B2 c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (w3a (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) B2) (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) B2) (wa (wrex l (wceq (co (cv l) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2) (wrex r (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv r) c_pl) (cv y1)) B2))) B2) B2)) $;

--| The left group action of element ` A ` of group ` G ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Mar-2008.)
theorem grplactfval {g2 a: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class g2 a) (G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cmpt a X (co A2 (cv a) c_pl))) $;

--| The value of the left group action of element ` A ` of group ` G ` at ` B ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Mar-2008.)
theorem grplactval {g2 a: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (c_pl: class) (F: class g2 a) (G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv A2 F)) (co A2 B2 c_pl)) $;

--| The left group action of element ` A ` of group ` G ` maps the underlying set ` X ` of ` G ` one-to-one onto itself. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grplactcnv {g2 a: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class g2 a) (G I X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wf1o X X (cfv A2 F)) (wceq (ccnv (cfv A2 F)) (cfv (cfv A2 I) F))) $;

--| The left group action of element ` A ` of group ` G ` maps the underlying set ` X ` of ` G ` one-to-one onto itself. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grplactf1o {g2 a: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class g2 a) (G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wf1o X X (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| Weak property deduction for the group subtraction operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem grpsubpropd (ph: wff) (G H: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cplusg) (cfv H cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G csg) (cfv H csg)) $;

--| Strong property deduction for the group subtraction operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem grpsubpropd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 G H: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv G cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv H cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G csg) (cfv H csg)) $;

--| The (smallest) structure representing a _trivial group_. According to Wikipedia ("Trivial group", 28-Apr-2019, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trivial_group ) "In mathematics, a _trivial group_ is a group consisting of a single element. All such groups are isomorphic, so one often speaks of _the_ trivial group. The single element of the trivial group is the identity element". (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Apr-2019.)
theorem grp1 (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel M cgrp) $;

--| The inverse function of the trivial group. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 26-Aug-2021.)
theorem grp1inv (I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv M cminusg) (cres cid (csn I))) $;

--| Characterization of inverses in a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsinvlem {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class y1) (R S F I: class) (N: class y1) (V W Y: class) (c_0: class y1):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cgrp R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (ccom c0g R) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt y1 I (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cfv (cv y1) R) cminusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel N B2) (wceq (co N F c_pl) c_0)) $;

--| The product of a family of groups is a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsgrpd (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cgrp R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cgrp) $;

--| Negation in a product of groups. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem prdsinvgd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S I: class) (N V W: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cgrp R) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Y cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X N) (cmpt x3 I (cfv (cfv (cv x3) X) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) R) cminusg)))) $;

--| A structure power of a group is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsgrp (R I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cgrp) (wcel I V)) (wcel Y cgrp) $;

--| Negation in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsinvg (B2 R I M N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Y cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cgrp) (wcel I V) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X N) (ccom M X)) $;

--| Subtraction in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwssub (B2 R F G I M c_mi V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Y csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cgrp) (wcel I V)) (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel G B2))) (wceq (co F G c_mi) (co F G (cof M))) $;

--| The image structure of a group is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem imasgrp2 {x3 y1 z q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class y1 z a b) (c_pl: class z a b) (R: class x3 y1 z a b) (U F: class) (N: class x3 y1 z q a b) (V: class) (W: class x3 y1 z q p a b) (c_0: class y1 z a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V) (wcel (cv z) V))) (wceq (cfv (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wcel N V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (cfv (co N (cv x3) c_pl) F) (cfv c_0 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cgrp) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| The image structure of a group is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem imasgrp {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl R: class a b) (U F V: class) (c_0: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V)) (wa (wcel (cv p) V) (wcel (cv q) V))) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv p) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv q) F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) F) (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cgrp) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cgrp) (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| The image of a group under an injection is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasgrpf1 (B2 R U F V: class):
  $ wceq U (co F R cimas) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf1 V B2 F) (wcel R cgrp)) (wcel U cgrp) $;

--| Prove that a quotient structure is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem qusgrp2 {x3 y1 z q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl: class z) (c_sm: class) (R: class x3 y1 z a b) (U: class) (N: class x3 y1 z q a b) (V: class) (X: class x3 y1 z q p a b) (c_0: class y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R c_sm cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wer V c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv p) c_sm) (wbr (cv b) (cv q) c_sm)) (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl) (co (cv p) (cv q) c_pl) c_sm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv y1) V) (wcel (cv z) V))) (wbr (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co c_0 (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wcel N V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr (co N (cv x3) c_pl) c_0 c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U cgrp) (wceq (cec c_0 c_sm) (cfv U c0g))) $;

--| The binary product of groups is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsgrp (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cgrp) (wcel S cgrp)) (wcel T cgrp) $;

--| Value of the negation operation in a binary structure product. (Contributed by AV, 18-Mar-2025.)
theorem xpsinv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S T I M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv S cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv T cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) I) (cop (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 N))) $;

--| Value of the subtraction operation in a binary structure product of groups. (Contributed by AV, 24-Mar-2025.)
theorem xpsgrpsub (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S T c_x c_xp c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv T csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) c_mi) (cop (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 D c_xp))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mhmmnd and ~ ghmgrp . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2020.)
theorem mhmlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (c_pl c_pd F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) F) (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) c_pd)) $;

--| A surjective monoid morphism preserves identity element. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2020.)
theorem mhmid {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_pd F G H X Y c_0: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c_0 F) (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| The image of a monoid ` G ` under a monoid homomorphism ` F ` is a monoid. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2020.)
theorem mhmmnd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_pd F G H X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cmnd) $;

--| The function fulfilling the conditions of ~ mhmmnd is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem mhmfmhm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_pd F G H X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cmhm)) $;

--| The image of a group ` G ` under a group homomorphism ` F ` is a group. This is a stronger result than that usually found in the literature, since the target of the homomorphism (operator ` O ` in our model) need not have any of the properties of a group as a prerequisite. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2020.)
theorem ghmgrp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_pd F G H X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cgrp) $;

--| Extend class notation with a function mapping a group operation to the multiple/power operation for the magma/group.
term cmg: class;

--| Define the group multiple function, also known as group exponentiation when viewed multiplicatively. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_mulg {x3 g2 n s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmg (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpo n x3 cz (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) (cfv (cv g2) c0g) (csb s1 (cseq (cfv (cv g2) cplusg) (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv n) clt) (cfv (cv n) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cneg (cv n)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv g2) cminusg))))))) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-rep , see ~ mulgfvalALT . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-rep . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 17-Aug-2023.)
theorem mulgfval {x3 n: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_x: class x3 n) (G I c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo n x3 cz B2 (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) c_0 (cif (wbr cc0 (cv n) clt) (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) (cfv (cfv (cneg (cv n)) (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) I)))) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ mulgfval using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulgfvalALT {x3 n: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_x: class x3 n) (G I c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo n x3 cz B2 (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) c_0 (cif (wbr cc0 (cv n) clt) (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) (cfv (cfv (cneg (cv n)) (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) I)))) $;

--| Value of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgval (B2 c_pl S c_x G I N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn X)) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co N X c_x) (cif (wceq N cc0) c_0 (cif (wbr cc0 N clt) (cfv N S) (cfv (cfv (cneg N) S) I)))) $;

--| Functionality of the group multiple operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mulgfn (B2 c_x G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wfn c_x (cxp cz B2) $;

--| The group multiple operation is compatible with identity-function protection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem mulgfvi (c_x G: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv (cfv G cid) cmg) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulg0 (B2 c_x G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (co cc0 X c_x) c_0) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a positive integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn (B2 c_pl S c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq c_pl (cxp cn (csn X)) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co N X c_x) (cfv N S)) $;

--| Values for the group multiple function in a restricted structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Jun-2017.)
theorem ressmulgnn (A2 G H I c_as N X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wss A2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (co N X (cfv H cmg)) (co N X c_as)) $;

--| Values for the group multiple function in a restricted structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jun-2017.)
theorem ressmulgnn0 (A2 G H I c_as N X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wss A2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G c0g) (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (co N X (cfv H cmg)) (co N X c_as)) $;

--| Values for the group multiple function in a restricted structure, a deduction version. (Contributed by metakunt, 14-May-2025.)
theorem ressmulgnnd (ph: wff) (A2 G H N X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co N X (cfv H cmg)) (co N X (cfv G cmg))) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a positive integer expressed by a group sum. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem mulgnngsum {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (c_x F G: class x3) (N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co N X c_x) (co G F cgsu)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a nonnegative integer expressed by a group sum. This corresponds to the definition in [Lang] p. 6, second formula. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem mulgnn0gsum {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (c_x F G: class x3) (N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co N X c_x) (co G F cgsu)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulg1 (B2 c_x G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (co c1 X c_x) X) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnnp1 (B2 c_pl c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (co N c1 caddc) X c_x) (co (co N X c_x) X c_pl)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at two. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem mulg2 (B2 c_pl c_x G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (co c2 X c_x) (co X X c_pl)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a negative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnegnn (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (cneg N) X c_x) (cfv (co N X c_x) I)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a successor, extended to ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn0p1 (B2 c_pl c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (co N c1 caddc) X c_x) (co (co N X c_x) X c_pl)) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation in a submagma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem mulgnnsubcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl S: class) (c_x: class y1) (G N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel N cn) (wcel X S)) (wcel (co N X c_x) S) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation in a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem mulgnn0subcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl S: class) (c_x: class y1) (G N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class) (c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel N cn0) (wcel X S)) (wcel (co N X c_x) S) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem mulgsubcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl S: class) (c_x: class y1) (G: class) (I: class y1) (N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class) (c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 S) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) I) S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel N cz) (wcel X S)) (wcel (co N X c_x) S) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation for a positive multiplier in a magma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgnncl (B2 c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmgm) (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2)) (wcel (co N X c_x) B2) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation for a nonnegative multiplier in a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn0cl (B2 c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2)) (wcel (co N X c_x) B2) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgcl (B2 c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wcel (co N X c_x) B2) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a negative integer. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgneg (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (cneg N) X c_x) (cfv (co N X c_x) I)) $;

--| The inverse of a negative group multiple is the positive group multiple. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgnegneg (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (co (cneg N) X c_x) I) (co N X c_x)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at negative one. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgm1 (B2 c_x G I X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (cneg c1) X c_x) (cfv X I)) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation for a nonnegative multiplier in a monoid. Deduction associated with ~ mulgnn0cl . (Contributed by SN, 1-Feb-2025.)
theorem mulgnn0cld (ph: wff) (B2 c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co N X c_x) B2) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ mulgcl . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem mulgcld (ph: wff) (B2 c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co N X c_x) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mulgaddcom . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 31-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgaddcomlem (y1: setvar) (B2 c_pl c_x G X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (cv y1) cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (co (cv y1) X c_x) X c_pl) (co X (co (cv y1) X c_x) c_pl))) (wceq (co (co (cneg (cv y1)) X c_x) X c_pl) (co X (co (cneg (cv y1)) X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| The group multiple operator commutes with the group operation. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 31-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgaddcom (B2 c_pl c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (co N X c_x) X c_pl) (co X (co N X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| The group multiple operator commutes with the group inverse function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 31-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulginvcom (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co N (cfv X I) c_x) (cfv (co N X c_x) I)) $;

--| The group multiple operator commutes with the group inverse function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 31-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulginvinv (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (co N (cfv X I) c_x) I) (co N X c_x)) $;

--| A group multiple of the identity, for nonnegative multiple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn0z (B2 c_x G N c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co N c_0 c_x) c_0) $;

--| A group multiple of the identity, for integer multiple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgz (B2 c_x G N c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co N c_0 c_x) c_0) $;

--| Sum of group multiples, for positive multiples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgnndir (B2 c_pl c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G csgrp) (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N caddc) X c_x) (co (co M X c_x) (co N X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Sum of group multiples, generalized to ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn0dir (B2 c_pl c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N caddc) X c_x) (co (co M X c_x) (co N X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mulgdir . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgdirlem (B2 c_pl c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wcel (co M N caddc) cn0)) (wceq (co (co M N caddc) X c_x) (co (co M X c_x) (co N X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Sum of group multiples, generalized to ` ZZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgdir (B2 c_pl c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N caddc) X c_x) (co (co M X c_x) (co N X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a successor, extended to ` ZZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgp1 (B2 c_pl c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (co N c1 caddc) X c_x) (co (co N X c_x) X c_pl)) $;

--| Group multiple (exponentiation) operation at a negative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgneg2 (B2 c_x G I N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (co (cneg N) X c_x) (co N (cfv X I) c_x)) $;

--| Product of group multiples, for positive multiples in a semigroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 29-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgnnass (B2 c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G csgrp) (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N cmul) X c_x) (co M (co N X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Product of group multiples, generalized to ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgnn0ass (B2 c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N cmul) X c_x) (co M (co N X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Product of group multiples, generalized to ` ZZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgass (B2 c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N cmul) X c_x) (co M (co N X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Reversed product of group multiples. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgassr (B2 c_x G M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co N M cmul) X c_x) (co M (co N X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Casting out multiples of the identity element leaves the group multiple unchanged. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Apr-2009.) (Revised by AV, 30-Aug-2021.)
theorem mulgmodid (B2 c_x G M N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel M cn)) (wa (wcel X B2) (wceq (co M X c_x) c_0))) (wceq (co (co N M cmo) X c_x) (co N X c_x)) $;

--| Distribution of group multiples over subtraction for group elements, ~ subdir analog. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Dec-2014.)
theorem mulgsubdir (B2 c_x G M c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2))) (wceq (co (co M N cmin) X c_x) (co (co M X c_x) (co N X c_x) c_mi)) $;

--| A homomorphism of monoids preserves group multiples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem mhmmulg (B2 c_x c_xp F G H N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co G H cmhm)) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (co N X c_x) F) (co N (cfv X F) c_xp)) $;

--| Two structures with the same group-nature have the same group multiple function. ` K ` is expected to either be ` _V ` (when strong equality is available) or ` B ` (when closure is available). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mulgpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_x c_xp: class x3 y1) (G H K: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv y1) K))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv G cplusg)) K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv y1) K))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv G cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv H cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x c_xp) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation in a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem submmulgcl (S c_xb G N X: class):
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X S)) (wcel (co N X c_xb) S) $;

--| A group multiple is the same if evaluated in a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem submmulg (S c_xb c_x G H N X: class):
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X S)) (wceq (co N X c_xb) (co N X c_x)) $;

--| Value of a group multiple in a structure power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem pwsmulg (A2 B2 R c_xb c_x I N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cmnd) (wcel I V)) (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel X B2) (wcel A2 I))) (wceq (cfv A2 (co N X c_xb)) (co N (cfv A2 X) c_x)) $;

--| Extend class notation with all subgroups of a group.
term csubg: class;

--| Extend class notation with all normal subgroups of a group.
term cnsg: class;

--| Quotient group equivalence class.
term cqg: class;

--| Define a subgroup of a group as a set of elements that is a group in its own right. Equivalently ( ~ issubg2 ), a subgroup is a subset of the group that is closed for the group internal operation (see ~ subgcl ), contains the neutral element of the group (see ~ subg0 ) and contains the inverses for all of its elements (see ~ subginvcl ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_subg {w s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csubg (cmpt w cgrp (crab s1 (wcel (co (cv w) (cv s1) cress) cgrp) (cpw (cfv (cv w) cbs)))) $;

--| Define the equivalence relation in a quotient ring or quotient group (where ` i ` is a two-sided ideal or a normal subgroup). For non-normal subgroups this generates the left cosets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
axiom df_nsg {x3 y1 w s1 p b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnsg (cmpt w cgrp (crab s1 (wsbc b (wsbc p (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv p)) (cv s1)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv p)) (cv s1))) (cv b)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv w) cplusg)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cfv (cv w) csubg))) $;

--| Define the equivalence relation in a group generated by a subgroup. More precisely, if ` G ` is a group and ` H ` is a subgroup, then ` G ~QG H ` is the equivalence relation on ` G ` associated with the left cosets of ` H ` . A typical application of this definition is the construction of the quotient group (resp. ring) of a group (resp. ring) by a normal subgroup (resp. two-sided ideal). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
axiom df_eqg {x3 y1 i r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqg (cmpo r i cvv cvv (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cfv (cv r) cbs)) (wcel (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv r) cminusg)) (cv y1) (cfv (cv r) cplusg)) (cv i))))) $;

--| The subgroup predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem issubg (B2 S G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wss S B2) (wcel (co G S cress) cgrp)) $;

--| A subgroup is a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgss (B2 S G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wss S B2) $;

--| A group is a subgroup of itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgid (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel B2 (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| A subgroup is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subggrp (S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel H cgrp) $;

--| The base of the restricted group in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgbas (S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wceq S (cfv H cbs)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the subgroup predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgrcl (S G: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| A subgroup of a group must have the same identity as the group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem subg0 (S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| The inverse of an element in a subgroup is the same as the inverse in the larger group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subginv (S G H I J X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X S)) (wceq (cfv X I) (cfv X J)) $;

--| The group identity is an element of any subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subg0cl (S G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel c_0 S) $;

--| The inverse of an element is closed in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subginvcl (S G I X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X S)) (wcel (cfv X I) S) $;

--| A subgroup is closed under group operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgcl (c_pl S G X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) S) $;

--| A subgroup is closed under group operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2025.)
theorem subgcld (ph: wff) (c_pl S G X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) S) $;

--| A subgroup is closed under group subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem subgsubcl (S G c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S)) (wcel (co X Y c_mi) S) $;

--| The subtraction of elements in a subgroup is the same as subtraction in the group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem subgsub (S G H c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv H csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S)) (wceq (co X Y c_mi) (co X Y N)) $;

--| Closure of the group multiple (exponentiation) operation in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem subgmulgcl (S c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel N cz) (wcel X S)) (wcel (co N X c_x) S) $;

--| A group multiple is the same if evaluated in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem subgmulg (S c_xb c_x G H N X: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel N cz) (wcel X S)) (wceq (co N X c_x) (co N X c_xb)) $;

--| Characterize the subgroups of a group by closure properties. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
theorem issubg2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class x3 y1) (c_pl S G I: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wb (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (w3a (wss S B2) (wne S c0) (wral x3 (wa (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) S) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) I) S)) S))) $;

--| Prove a subgroup by closure (definition version). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem issubgrpd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (D c_pl S I c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co I D cress)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv I c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv I cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cfv I cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv I cminusg)) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv I csubg)) $;

--| Prove a subgroup by closure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem issubgrpd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (D c_pl S I c_0: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co I D cress)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_0 (cfv I c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv I cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cfv I cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) D)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv I cminusg)) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $;

--| A subgroup is a symmetric submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem issubg3 {x3: setvar} (S G I: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wb (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) I) S) S))) $;

--| A subgroup is a nonempty subset of the group closed under subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem issubg4 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 S G c_mi: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wb (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (w3a (wss S B2) (wne S c0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) S) S) S))) $;

--| If the base set of a group is contained in the base set of another group, and the group operation of the group is the restriction of the group operation of the other group to its base set, then the (base set of the) group is subgroup of the other group. (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2019.)
theorem grpissubg (B2 S G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel H cgrp)) (wi (wa (wss S B2) (wceq (cfv H cplusg) (cres (cfv G cplusg) (cxp S S)))) (wcel S (cfv G csubg))) $;

--| If the base set of a group is contained in the base set of another group, and the group operation of the group is the restriction of the group operation of the other group to its base set, then the other group restricted to the base set of the group is a group. (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2019.)
theorem resgrpisgrp (B2 S G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel H cgrp)) (wi (wa (wss S B2) (wceq (cfv H cplusg) (cres (cfv G cplusg) (cxp S S)))) (wcel (co G S cress) cgrp)) $;

--| A subgroup is a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem subgsubm (S G: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) $;

--| A subgroup of a subgroup is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subsubg (A2 S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv H csubg)) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv G csubg)) (wss A2 S))) $;

--| The intersection of a nonempty collection of subgroups is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Dec-2014.)
theorem subgint (S G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S (cfv G csubg)) (wne S c0)) (wcel (cint S) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The zero subgroup of an arbitrary group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Dec-2014.) (Proof shortened by SN, 31-Jan-2025.)
theorem _0subg (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (csn c_0) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The only subgroup of a trivial group is itself. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem trivsubgd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| The only subgroup of a trivial group is itself. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem trivsubgsnd (ph: wff) (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G csubg) (csn B2)) $;

--| Property of being a normal subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem isnsg {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X)) $;

--| Weaken the condition of ~ isnsg to only one side of the implication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem isnsg2 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X)) $;

--| Defining property of a normal subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem nsgbi (A2 B2 c_pl S G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wcel (co A2 B2 c_pl) S) (wcel (co B2 A2 c_pl) S)) $;

--| A normal subgroup is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem nsgsubg (S G: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The conjugation of an element of a normal subgroup is in the subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem nsgconj (A2 B2 c_pl S G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) A2 c_mi) S) $;

--| A subgroup is normal iff the conjugation of all the elements of the subgroup is in the subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem isnsg3 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi) S) S) X)) $;

--| Subgroups are an algebraic closure system. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem subgacs (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (cfv G csubg) (cfv B2 cacs)) $;

--| Normal subgroups form an algebraic closure system. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem nsgacs (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (cfv G cnsg) (cfv B2 cacs)) $;

--| Elementhood in the normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem elnmz {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1) (c_pl S: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 N) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral z (wb (wcel (co A2 (cv z) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv z) A2 c_pl) S)) X)) $;

--| Defining property of the normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem nmzbi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3 y1) (c_pl S: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 N) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wcel (co A2 B2 c_pl) S) (wcel (co B2 A2 c_pl) S)) $;

--| The normalizer N_G(S) of a subset ` S ` of the group is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem nmzsubg {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| A subgroup is a subset of its normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem ssnmz {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wss S N) $;

--| A subgroup is normal iff its normalizer is the entire group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem isnsg4 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wceq N X)) $;

--| Any subgroup is a normal subgroup of its normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem nmznsg {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S G: class) (H N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G N cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel S (cfv H cnsg)) $;

--| The zero subgroup is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem _0nsg (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (csn c_0) (cfv G cnsg)) $;

--| The whole group is a normal subgroup of itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem nsgid (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel B2 (cfv G cnsg)) $;

--| The whole group and the zero subgroup are normal subgroups of a group. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem _0idnsgd (ph: wff) (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cpr (csn c_0) B2) (cfv G cnsg)) $;

--| The only normal subgroup of a trivial group is itself. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem trivnsgd (ph: wff) (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cnsg) (csn B2)) $;

--| A trivial group has exactly one normal subgroup. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem triv1nsgd (ph: wff) (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G cnsg) c1o cen) $;

--| A group with exactly one normal subgroup is trivial. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem _1nsgtrivd (ph: wff) (B2 G c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G cnsg) c1o cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (csn c_0)) $;

--| The left coset equivalence relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem releqg (R S G: class):
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wrel R $;

--| The equivalence class of a cartesian product is the whole set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2024.)
theorem ecxpid (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X A2) (wceq (cec X (cxp A2 A2)) A2) $;

--| The quotient set of a cartesian product is trivial. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2024.)
theorem qsxpid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wceq (cqs A2 (cxp A2 A2)) (csn A2)) $;

--| Value of the subgroup left coset equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem eqgfval {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S G N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wss S X)) (wceq R (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) X) (wcel (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cv y1) c_pl) S)))) $;

--| Value of the subgroup left coset equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem eqgval (A2 B2 c_pl R S G N V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wss S X)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel (co (cfv A2 N) B2 c_pl) S))) $;

--| The subgroup coset equivalence relation is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem eqger (c_sm G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wer X c_sm) $;

--| A left coset can be expressed as the image of a left action. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem eqglact {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl c_sm G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wss Y X) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cec A2 c_sm) (cima (cmpt x3 X (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl)) Y)) $;

--| The left coset containing the identity is the original subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem eqgid (c_sm G X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (cec c_0 c_sm) Y) $;

--| Each coset is equipotent to the subgroup itself (which is also the coset containing the identity). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem eqgen (A2 c_sm G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 (cqs X c_sm))) (wbr Y A2 cen) $;

--| The subgroup coset equivalence relation is compatible with addition when the subgroup is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem eqgcpbl (A2 B2 C D c_pl c_sm G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 C c_sm) (wbr B2 D c_sm)) (wbr (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl) c_sm)) $;

--| The Group quotient equivalence relation for the whole group is the cartesian product, i.e. all elements are in the same equivalence class. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2024.)
theorem qusxpid (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (co G B2 cqg) (cxp B2 B2)) $;

--| The quotient of a group ` G ` by itself is trivial. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2024.)
theorem qustriv (B2 Q G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G B2 cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (cfv Q cbs) (csn B2)) $;

--| Converse of ~ qustriv . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2024.)
theorem qustrivr (B2 Q G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G H cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (cfv Q cbs) (csn H))) (wceq H B2) $;

--| Equivalence class of a quotient group for a subgroup. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2024.)
theorem eqg0el (c_sm G H X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel H (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wceq (cec X c_sm) H) (wcel X H)) $;

--| Membership in the base set of a quotient group. (Contributed by AV, 1-Mar-2025.)
theorem quselbas {x3: setvar} (B2 c_sm: class) (S U G V W: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel X W)) (wb (wcel X (cfv U cbs)) (wrex x3 (wceq X (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) B2)) $;

--| Closure of the quotient map for a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.) Generalization of ~ quseccl for arbitrary sets ` G ` . (Revised by AV, 24-Feb-2025.)
theorem quseccl0 (B2 C c_sm S G H V X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel X C)) (wcel (cec X c_sm) B2) $;

--| If ` Y ` is a normal subgroup of ` G ` , then ` H = G / Y ` is a group, called the quotient of ` G ` by ` Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem qusgrp (S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel H cgrp) $;

--| Closure of the quotient map for a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Mar-2025.)
theorem quseccl (B2 S G H V X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel X V)) (wcel (cec X (co G S cqg)) B2) $;

--| Value of the group operation in a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qusadd (c_pl c_pb S G H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cec X (co G S cqg)) (cec Y (co G S cqg)) c_pb) (cec (co X Y c_pl) (co G S cqg))) $;

--| Value of the group identity operation in a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qus0 (S G H c_0: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wceq (cec c_0 (co G S cqg)) (cfv H c0g)) $;

--| Value of the group inverse operation in a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qusinv (S G H I N V X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv H cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel X V)) (wceq (cfv (cec X (co G S cqg)) N) (cec (cfv X I) (co G S cqg))) $;

--| Value of the group subtraction operation in a quotient group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qussub (S G H c_mi N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G S cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv H csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (co (cec X (co G S cqg)) (cec Y (co G S cqg)) N) (cec (co X Y c_mi) (co G S cqg))) $;

--| Addition of equivalence classes in a quotient group. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2025.)
theorem ecqusaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C Q c_sm R I: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cfv R cnsg)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co R I cqg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co R c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel C B2))) (wceq (cec (co A2 C (cfv R cplusg)) c_sm) (co (cec A2 c_sm) (cec C c_sm) (cfv Q cplusg))) $;

--| Closure of the addition in a quotient group. (Contributed by AV, 24-Feb-2025.)
theorem ecqusaddcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C Q c_sm R I: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cfv R cnsg)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co R I cqg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co R c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel C B2))) (wcel (co (cec A2 c_sm) (cec C c_sm) (cfv Q cplusg)) (cfv Q cbs)) $;

--| Lagrange's theorem for finite groups. Call the "order" of a group the cardinal number of the basic set of the group, and "index of a subgroup" the cardinal number of the set of left (or right, this is the same) cosets of this subgroup. Then the order of the group is the (cardinal) product of the order of any of its subgroups by the index of this subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem lagsubg2 (ph: wff) (c_sm G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G Y cqg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X chash) (co (cfv (cqs X c_sm) chash) (cfv Y chash) cmul)) $;

--| Lagrange's theorem for Groups: the order of any subgroup of a finite group is a divisor of the order of the group. This is Metamath 100 proof #71. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2015.)
theorem lagsubg (G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X cfn)) (wbr (cfv Y chash) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $;

--| The coset equivalence relation for the trivial (zero) subgroup of a group is the identity relation restricted to the base set of the group. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2025.)
theorem eqg0subg (B2 R S G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq S (csn c_0) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq R (cres cid B2)) $;

--| The equivalence classes modulo the coset equivalence relation for the trivial (zero) subgroup of a group are singletons. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2025.)
theorem eqg0subgecsn (B2 R S G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq S (csn c_0) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cec X R) (csn X)) $;

--| The base set of a quotient of a group by the trivial (zero) subgroup. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2025.)
theorem qus0subgbas {x3 u: setvar} (B2 c_sm: class) (S U: class x3 u) (G: class) (c_0: class x3 u):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq S (csn c_0) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (cfv U cbs) (cab u (wrex x3 (wceq (cv u) (csn (cv x3))) B2))) $;

--| The addition in a quotient of a group by the trivial (zero) subgroup. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2025.)
theorem qus0subgadd {a b: setvar} (B2: class a) (c_sm S U: class a b) (G: class) (c_0: class a b):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq S (csn c_0) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wral a (wral b (wceq (co (csn (cv a)) (csn (cv b)) (cfv U cplusg)) (csn (co (cv a) (cv b) (cfv G cplusg)))) B2) B2) $;

--| Characterization of an element of the set of nonnegative integer powers of an element ` A ` . Although this theorem holds for any class ` G ` , the definition of ` F ` is only meaningful if ` G ` is a monoid or at least a unital magma. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem cycsubmel {x3 i: setvar} (A2: class i) (B2 C: class x3 i) (c_x: class i) (F: class x3) (G: class x3 i) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn F) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X C) (wrex i (wceq X (co (cv i) A2 c_x)) cn0) $;

--| The set of nonnegative integer powers of an element ` A ` contains ` A ` . Although this theorem holds for any class ` G ` , the definition of ` F ` is only meaningful if ` G ` is a monoid or at least a unital magma. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem cycsubmcl {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (c_x: class) (F G: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wcel A2 C) $;

--| The set of nonnegative integer powers of an element ` A ` of a monoid forms a submonoid containing ` A ` (see ~ cycsubmcl ), called the cyclic monoid generated by the element ` A ` . This corresponds to the statement in [Lang] p. 6. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2023.)
theorem cycsubm {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn F) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 B2)) (wcel C (cfv G csubmnd)) $;

--| Condition for an operation to be commutative. Lemma for ~ cycsubmcom and ~ cygabl . Formerly part of proof for ~ cygabl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem cyccom {x3 m n c: setvar} (ph: wff m n c) (A2: class) (C: class x3 m n) (c_pl: class c) (c_x: class) (X Y: class m n) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wral c (wrex x3 (wceq (cv c) (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) Z) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral m (wral n (wceq (co (co (cv m) (cv n) caddc) A2 c_x) (co (co (cv m) A2 c_x) (co (cv n) A2 c_x) c_pl)) Z) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)) $;

--| The operation of a monoid is commutative over the set of nonnegative integer powers of an element ` A ` of the monoid. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2024.)
theorem cycsubmcom {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C c_pl: class x3) (c_x: class) (F G X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cn0 (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn F) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 B2)) (wa (wcel X C) (wcel Y C))) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)) $;

--| The cyclic subgroup generated by ` A ` includes its generator. Although this theorem holds for any class ` G ` , the definition of ` F ` is only meaningful if ` G ` is a group. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem cycsubggend {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 c_x F G: class n):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cz (co (cv n) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (crn F)) $;

--| The set of integer powers of an element ` A ` of a group forms a subgroup containing ` A ` , called the cyclic group generated by the element ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cycsubgcl {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wcel (crn F) (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 (crn F))) $;

--| The cyclic subgroup generated by an element ` A ` is a subset of any subgroup containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cycsubgss {x3: setvar} (A2 S c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 S)) (wss (crn F) S) $;

--| The cyclic group generated by ` A ` is the smallest subgroup containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cycsubg {x3 s1: setvar} (A2: class) (c_x: class s1) (F: class x3) (G: class) (X: class s1):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (crn F) (cint (crab s1 (wcel A2 (cv s1)) (cfv G csubg)))) $;

--| The cyclic subgroup generated by ` A ` is a subgroup. Deduction related to ~ cycsubgcl . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem cycsubgcld {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2 c_x: class) (F: class n) (G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cz (co (cv n) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The subgroup generated by an element is exhausted by its multiples. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cycsubg2 {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G: class) (K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) K) (crn F)) $;

--| Any multiple of an element is contained in the generated cyclic subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem cycsubg2cl (A2 c_x G K N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co N A2 c_x) (cfv (csn A2) K)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the generator of group hom-sets.
term cghm: class;

--| A homomorphism of groups is a map between two structures which preserves the group operation. Requiring both sides to be groups simplifies most theorems at the cost of complicating the theorem which pushes forward a group structure. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_ghm {x3 y1 w t g2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cghm (cmpo s1 t cgrp cgrp (cab g2 (wsbc w (wa (wf (cv w) (cfv (cv t) cbs) (cv g2)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv s1) cplusg)) (cv g2)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv t) cplusg))) (cv w)) (cv w))) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)))) $;

--| Lemma for group homomorphisms. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem reldmghm:
  $ wrel (cdm cghm) $;

--| Property of being a homomorphism of groups. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.) (Proof shortened by SN, 5-Jun-2025.)
theorem isghm {v u: setvar} (c_pl c_pd S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cgrp) (wcel T cgrp)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral u (wral v (wceq (cfv (co (cv u) (cv v) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv u) F) (cfv (cv v) F) c_pd)) X) X))) $;

--| Property of a group homomorphism, similar to ~ ismhm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem isghm3 {v u: setvar} (c_pl c_pd S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cgrp) (wcel T cgrp)) (wb (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral u (wral v (wceq (cfv (co (cv u) (cv v) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv u) F) (cfv (cv v) F) c_pd)) X) X))) $;

--| A group homomorphism is only defined when the domain is a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmgrp1 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel S cgrp) $;

--| A group homomorphism is only defined when the codomain is a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmgrp2 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel T cgrp) $;

--| A group homomorphism is a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmf (S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wf X Y F) $;

--| A homomorphism of groups is linear. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmlin (c_pl c_pd S T U F V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel U X) (wcel V X)) (wceq (cfv (co U V c_pl) F) (co (cfv U F) (cfv V F) c_pd)) $;

--| A homomorphism of groups preserves the identity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmid (S T F Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wceq (cfv Y F) c_0) $;

--| A homomorphism of groups preserves inverses. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghminv (B2 S T F M N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv S cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv T cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X M) F) (cfv (cfv X F) N)) $;

--| Linearity of subtraction through a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmsub (B2 S T U F c_mi N V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv T csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel U B2) (wcel V B2)) (wceq (cfv (co U V c_mi) F) (co (cfv U F) (cfv V F) N)) $;

--| Deduction for a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem isghmd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_pd S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_pd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $;

--| A group homomorphism is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem ghmmhm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel F (co S T cmhm)) $;

--| Group homomorphisms and monoid homomorphisms coincide. (Thus, ` GrpHom ` is somewhat redundant, although its stronger reverse closure properties are sometimes useful.) (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.)
theorem ghmmhmb (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cgrp) (wcel T cgrp)) (wceq (co S T cghm) (co S T cmhm)) $;

--| A group homomorphism preserves group multiples. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem ghmmulg (B2 c_x c_xp F G H N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co G H cghm)) (wcel N cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (co N X c_x) F) (co N (cfv X F) c_xp)) $;

--| The range of a homomorphism is a subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmrn (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel (crn F) (cfv T csubg)) $;

--| The constant zero linear function between two groups. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem _0ghm (B2 M N c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv N c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cgrp) (wcel N cgrp)) (wcel (cxp B2 (csn c_0)) (co M N cghm)) $;

--| The identity homomorphism on a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem idghm (B2 G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (cres cid B2) (co G G cghm)) $;

--| Restriction of a homomorphism to a subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem resghm (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co S X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel X (cfv S csubg))) (wcel (cres F X) (co U T cghm)) $;

--| One direction of ~ resghm2b . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem resghm2 (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S U cghm)) (wcel X (cfv T csubg))) (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $;

--| Restriction of the codomain of a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem resghm2b (S T U F X: class):
  $ wceq U (co T X cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv T csubg)) (wss (crn F) X)) (wb (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel F (co S U cghm))) $;

--| A group homomorphism from ` G ` to ` H ` is also a group homomorphism from ` G ` to its image in ` H ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Revised by AV, 26-Aug-2021.)
theorem ghmghmrn (S T U F: class):
  $ wceq U (co T (crn F) cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel F (co S U cghm)) $;

--| The composition of group homomorphisms is a homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem ghmco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cghm)) (wcel G (co S T cghm))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cghm)) $;

--| The image of a subgroup under a homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmima (S T U F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel U (cfv S csubg))) (wcel (cima F U) (cfv T csubg)) $;

--| The inverse image of a subgroup under a homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmpreima (S T F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel V (cfv T csubg))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) V) (cfv S csubg)) $;

--| The equalizer of two group homomorphisms is a subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem ghmeql (S T F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel G (co S T cghm))) (wcel (cdm (cin F G)) (cfv S csubg)) $;

--| The image of a normal subgroup under a surjective homomorphism is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem ghmnsgima (S T U F Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel U (cfv S cnsg)) (wceq (crn F) Y)) (wcel (cima F U) (cfv T cnsg)) $;

--| The inverse image of a normal subgroup under a homomorphism is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem ghmnsgpreima (S T F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel V (cfv T cnsg))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) V) (cfv S cnsg)) $;

--| The kernel of a homomorphism is a normal subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem ghmker (S T F c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) (cfv S cnsg)) $;

--| Two source points map to the same destination point under a group homomorphism iff their difference belongs to the kernel. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Dec-2014.)
theorem ghmeqker (B2 S T U F K c_mi V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel U B2) (wcel V B2)) (wb (wceq (cfv U F) (cfv V F)) (wcel (co U V c_mi) K)) $;

--| Diagonal homomorphism into a structure power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem pwsdiagghm {x3: setvar} (B2 R: class) (F: class x3) (I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cxp I (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cgrp) (wcel I W)) (wcel F (co R Y cghm)) $;

--| If a group homomorphism ` F ` is injective, it maps the zero of one group (and only the zero) to the zero of the other group. (Contributed by AV, 24-Oct-2019.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 13-May-2023.)
theorem f1ghm0to0 (A2 B2 R S F N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co R S cghm)) (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wcel X A2)) (wb (wceq (cfv X F) c_0) (wceq X N)) $;

--| Two ways of saying a group homomorphism is 1-1 into its codomain. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 4-Apr-2025.)
theorem ghmf1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R S F N c_0: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cghm)) (wb (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) c_0) (wceq (cv x3) N)) A2)) $;

--| A group homomorphism ` F ` is injective if and only if its kernel is the singleton ` { N } ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 16-Jun-2011.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Oct-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Oct-2019.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 13-May-2023.)
theorem kerf1ghm (A2 B2 R S F N c_0: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cghm)) (wb (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wceq (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) (csn N))) $;

--| A bijective group homomorphism is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem ghmf1o (S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wb (wf1o X Y F) (wcel (ccnv F) (co T S cghm))) $;

--| Conjugation is an automorphism of the group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjghm {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3) (G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wcel F (co G G cghm)) (wf1o X X F)) $;

--| A conjugated subgroup is also a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjsubg {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (crn F) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| A conjugated subgroup is equinumerous to the original subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjsubgen {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr S (crn F) cen) $;

--| A subgroup is unchanged under conjugation by an element of its normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjnmz {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G: class) (c_mi: class z) (N: class y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab y1 (wral z (wb (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv z) (cv y1) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 N)) (wceq S (crn F)) $;

--| Alternative condition for elementhood in the normalizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjnmzb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G: class) (c_mi: class z) (N: class y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab y1 (wral z (wb (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) S) (wcel (co (cv z) (cv y1) c_pl) S)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wb (wcel A2 N) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wceq S (crn F)))) $;

--| A normal subgroup is unchanged under conjugation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem conjnsg {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl S: class) (F: class x3) (G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (co (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl) A2 c_mi)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq S (crn F)) $;

--| If ` Y ` is a normal subgroup of ` G ` , then the "natural map" from elements to their cosets is a group homomorphism from ` G ` to ` G / Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qusghm {x3: setvar} (F: class x3) (G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G (co G Y cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cec (cv x3) (co G Y cqg))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $;

--| Group homomorphism depends only on the group attributes of structures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem ghmpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C J K L M: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv J cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv J cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) C) (wcel (cv y1) C))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv M cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J K cghm) (co L M cghm)) $;

--| The class of group isomorphism sets.
term cgim: class;

--| The class of the group isomorphism relation.
term cgic: class;

--| An isomorphism of groups is a homomorphism which is also a bijection, i.e. it preserves equality as well as the group operation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_gim {t g2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgim (cmpo s1 t cgrp cgrp (crab g2 (wf1o (cfv (cv s1) cbs) (cfv (cv t) cbs) (cv g2)) (co (cv s1) (cv t) cghm))) $;

--| Two groups are said to be isomorphic iff they are connected by at least one isomorphism. Isomorphic groups share all global group properties, but to relate local properties requires knowledge of a specific isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_gic:
  $ wceq cgic (cima (ccnv cgim) (cdif cvv c1o)) $;

--| The group isomorphism function is a well-defined function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem gimfn:
  $ wfn cgim (cxp cgrp cgrp) $;

--| An isomorphism of groups is a bijective homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.)
theorem isgim (B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wa (wcel F (co R S cghm)) (wf1o B2 C F)) $;

--| An isomorphism of groups is a bijection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem gimf1o (B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wf1o B2 C F) $;

--| An isomorphism of groups is a homomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem gimghm (R S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wcel F (co R S cghm)) $;

--| A group isomorphism is a homomorphism whose converse is also a homomorphism. Characterization of isomorphisms similar to ~ ishmeo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem isgim2 (R S F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wa (wcel F (co R S cghm)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co S R cghm))) $;

--| Behavior of subgroups under isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 21-Jan-2015.)
theorem subggim (A2 B2 R S F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wss A2 B2)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv R csubg)) (wcel (cima F A2) (cfv S csubg))) $;

--| The converse of a group isomorphism is a group isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem gimcnv (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cgim)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co T S cgim)) $;

--| The composition of group isomorphisms is a group isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem gimco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cgim)) (wcel G (co S T cgim))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cgim)) $;

--| A group isomorphism maps the zero of one group (and only the zero) to the zero of the other group. (Contributed by AV, 24-Oct-2019.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2023.)
theorem gim0to0 (A2 B2 R S F N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wcel X A2)) (wb (wceq (cfv X F) N) (wceq X c_0)) $;

--| The relation "is isomorphic to" for groups. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem brgic (R S: class):
  $ wb (wbr R S cgic) (wne (co R S cgim) c0) $;

--| Prove isomorphic by an explicit isomorphism. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem brgici (R S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cgim)) (wbr R S cgic) $;

--| Isomorphism is reflexive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem gicref (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cgrp) (wbr R R cgic) $;

--| Isomorphism implies the left side is a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem giclcl (R S: class):
  $ wi (wbr R S cgic) (wcel R cgrp) $;

--| Isomorphism implies the right side is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem gicrcl (R S: class):
  $ wi (wbr R S cgic) (wcel S cgrp) $;

--| Isomorphism is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem gicsym (R S: class):
  $ wi (wbr R S cgic) (wbr S R cgic) $;

--| Isomorphism is transitive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem gictr (R S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr R S cgic) (wbr S T cgic)) (wbr R T cgic) $;

--| Isomorphism is an equivalence relation on groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem gicer:
  $ wer cgrp cgic $;

--| Isomorphic groups have equinumerous base sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem gicen (B2 C R S: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wbr R S cgic) (wbr B2 C cen) $;

--| A less trivial example of a group invariant: cardinality of the subgroup lattice. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem gicsubgen (R S: class):
  $ wi (wbr R S cgic) (wbr (cfv R csubg) (cfv S csubg) cen) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ghmqusnsg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-May-2025.)
theorem ghmqusnsglem1 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G: class) (H J: class q) (K N X: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G N cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv G cnsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cec X (co G N cqg)) J) (cfv X F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ghmqusnsg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-May-2025.)
theorem ghmqusnsglem2 {x3 q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F: class x3) (G: class) (H J: class x3 q) (K N Y: class) (c_0: class x3 q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G N cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv G cnsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv Q cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv Y J) (cfv (cv x3) F)) Y) $;

--| The mapping ` H ` induced by a surjective group homomorphism ` F ` from the quotient group ` Q ` over a normal subgroup ` N ` of ` F ` 's kernel ` K ` is a group isomorphism. In this case, one says that ` F ` factors through ` Q ` , which is also called the factor group. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-May-2025.)
theorem ghmqusnsg {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G H J K N: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G N cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv G cnsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co Q H cghm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ghmqusker . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2025.)
theorem ghmquskerlem1 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G H J K X: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cec X (co G K cqg)) J) (cfv X F)) $;

--| In the case of theorem ~ ghmqusker , the composition of the natural homomorphism ` L ` with the constructed homomorphism ` J ` equals the original homomorphism ` F ` . One says that ` F ` factors through ` Q ` . (Proposed by Saveliy Skresanov, 15-Feb-2025.) (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem ghmquskerco {x3 q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 Q F G H J K: class) (L c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt x3 B2 (cec (cv x3) (co G K cqg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (ccom J L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ghmqusker . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2025.)
theorem ghmquskerlem2 {x3 q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G H J K Y: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv Q cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv Y J) (cfv (cv x3) F)) Y) $;

--| The mapping ` H ` induced by a surjective group homomorphism ` F ` from the quotient group ` Q ` over ` F ` 's kernel ` K ` is a group isomorphism. In this case, one says that ` F ` factors through ` Q ` , which is also called the factor group. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Mar-2025.)
theorem ghmquskerlem3 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G H J K: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co Q H cghm)) $;

--| A surjective group homomorphism ` F ` from ` G ` to ` H ` induces an isomorphism ` J ` from ` Q ` to ` H ` , where ` Q ` is the factor group of ` G ` by ` F ` 's kernel ` K ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem ghmqusker {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (Q F G H J K: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt q (cfv Q cbs) (cuni (cima F (cv q)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co Q H cgim)) $;

--| The image ` H ` of a group homomorphism ` F ` is isomorphic with the quotient group ` Q ` over ` F ` 's kernel ` K ` . Together with ~ ghmker and ~ ghmima , this is sometimes called the first isomorphism theorem for groups. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Mar-2025.)
theorem gicqusker (ph: wff) (Q F G H K c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv H c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cima (ccnv F) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co G (co G K cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Q H cgic) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of group actions.
term cga: class;

--| Define the class of all group actions. A group ` G ` acts on a set ` S ` if a permutation on ` S ` is associated with every element of ` G ` in such a way that the identity permutation on ` S ` is associated with the neutral element of ` G ` , and the composition of the permutations associated with two elements of ` G ` is identical with the permutation associated with the composition of these two elements (in the same order) in the group ` G ` . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Aug-2009.)
axiom df_ga {x3 y1 z g2 m s1 b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cga (cmpo g2 s1 cgrp cvv (csb b (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (crab m (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cfv (cv g2) c0g) (cv x3) (cv m)) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv (cv g2) cplusg)) (cv x3) (cv m)) (co (cv y1) (co (cv z) (cv x3) (cv m)) (cv m))) (cv b)) (cv b))) (cv s1)) (co (cv s1) (cxp (cv b) (cv s1)) cmap)))) $;

--| The predicate "is a (left) group action". The group ` G ` is said to act on the base set ` Y ` of the action, which is not assumed to have any special properties. There is a related notion of right group action, but as the Wikipedia article explains, it is not mathematically interesting. The way actions are usually thought of is that each element ` g ` of ` G ` is a permutation of the elements of ` Y ` (see ~ gapm ). Since group theory was classically about symmetry groups, it is therefore likely that the notion of group action was useful even in early group theory. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem isga {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl: class x3 y1 z) (c_po G: class) (X: class x3) (Y: class) (c_0: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wa (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel Y cvv)) (wa (wf (cxp X Y) Y c_po) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c_0 (cv x3) c_po) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_po) (co (cv y1) (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_po) c_po)) X) X)) Y))) $;

--| The left argument of a group action is a group. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem gagrp (c_po G Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| The right argument of a group action is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem gaset (c_po G Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel Y cvv) $;

--| The identity of the group does not alter the base set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gagrpid (A2 c_po G Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq (co c_0 A2 c_po) A2) $;

--| The mapping of the group action operation. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaf (c_po G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wf (cxp X Y) Y c_po) $;

--| A group action is onto its base set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gafo (c_po G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wfo (cxp X Y) Y c_po) $;

--| An "associative" property for group actions. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaass (A2 B2 C c_pl c_po G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C Y))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) C c_po) (co A2 (co B2 C c_po) c_po)) $;

--| The action of a group on the empty set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem ga0 (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel c0 (co G c0 cga)) $;

--| The trivial action of a group on any set. Each group element corresponds to the identity permutation. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaid (S G V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel S V)) (wcel (cres c2nd (cxp X S)) (co G S cga)) $;

--| A subgroup acts on its parent group. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem subgga {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class) (H: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G Y cress) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 Y X (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wcel F (co H X cga)) $;

--| A subset of a group action is a group action iff it is closed under the group action operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem gass {x3 y1: setvar} (c_po G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wss Z Y)) (wb (wcel (cres c_po (cxp X Z)) (co G Z cga)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_po) Z) Z) X)) $;

--| The restriction of a group action to a subgroup is a group action. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem gasubg (c_po S G H Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel S (cfv G csubg))) (wcel (cres c_po (cxp S Y)) (co H Y cga)) $;

--| A group operation is a left group action of the group on itself. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaid2 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X X (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel F (co G X cga)) $;

--| The action of a particular group element is left-cancelable. (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem galcan (A2 B2 C c_po G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y) (wcel C Y))) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 c_po) (co A2 C c_po)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Group inverses cancel in a group action. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gacan (A2 B2 C c_po G N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y) (wcel C Y))) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 c_po) C) (wceq (co (cfv A2 N) C c_po) B2)) $;

--| The action of a particular group element is a permutation of the base set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gapm {x3: setvar} (A2 c_po: class) (F: class x3) (G X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 Y (co A2 (cv x3) c_po)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 X)) (wf1o Y Y F) $;

--| The orbit equivalence relation puts two points in the group action in the same equivalence class iff there is a group element that takes one element to the other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaorb {x3 y1 g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2 c_po: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 g2) (X: class) (Y: class g2):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 c_sm) (w3a (wcel A2 Y) (wcel B2 Y) (wrex h (wceq (co (cv h) A2 c_po) B2) X)) $;

--| The orbit equivalence relation is an equivalence relation on the target set of the group action. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem gaorber {x3 y1 g2: setvar} (c_po: class) (c_sm G: class x3 y1 g2) (X: class) (Y: class g2):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wer Y c_sm) $;

--| The stabilizer subgroup in a group action. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem gastacl {u: setvar} (A2 c_po G: class) (H: class u) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Write the coset relation for the stabilizer subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem gastacos {u: setvar} (A2 B2 C c_po: class) (c_sm: class u) (G: class) (H: class u) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wa (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wb (wbr B2 C c_sm) (wceq (co B2 A2 c_po) (co C A2 c_po))) $;

--| Existence and uniqueness for the function of ~ orbsta . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem orbstafun {u k: setvar} (A2 c_po: class) (c_sm: class u) (F: class u k) (G: class) (H: class u k) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt k X (cop (cec (cv k) c_sm) (co (cv k) A2 c_po)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wfun F) $;

--| Value of the function at a given equivalence class element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem orbstaval {u k: setvar} (A2 B2 c_po: class) (c_sm: class u) (F: class u k) (G: class) (H: class u k) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt k X (cop (cec (cv k) c_sm) (co (cv k) A2 c_po)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cec B2 c_sm) F) (co B2 A2 c_po)) $;

--| The Orbit-Stabilizer theorem. The mapping ` F ` is a bijection from the cosets of the stabilizer subgroup of ` A ` to the orbit of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem orbsta {x3 y1 u g2 k: setvar} (A2 c_po: class) (c_sm: class u) (F: class x3 y1 u g2 k) (G: class) (H: class u g2 k) (O: class x3 y1 u g2) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt k X (cop (cec (cv k) c_sm) (co (cv k) A2 c_po)))) $ >
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wf1o (cqs X c_sm) (cec A2 O) F) $;

--| Relation between the size of the orbit and the size of the stabilizer of a point in a finite group action. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem orbsta2 {x3 y1 u g2: setvar} (A2 c_po: class) (c_sm: class u) (G: class) (H: class u g2) (O: class x3 y1 u g2) (X: class) (Y: class u):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) A2 c_po) A2) X) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G H cqg) $ >
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wcel X cfn)) (wceq (cfv X chash) (co (cfv (cec A2 O) chash) (cfv H chash) cmul)) $;

--| Syntax for the centralizer of a set in a monoid.
term ccntz: class;

--| Syntax for the centralizer of a monoid.
term ccntr: class;

--| Define the _centralizer_ of a subset of a magma, which is the set of elements each of which commutes with each element of the given subset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_cntz {x3 y1 m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccntz (cmpt m cvv (cmpt s1 (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs)) (crab x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv m) cplusg)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) cplusg))) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv m) cbs)))) $;

--| Define the _center_ of a magma, which is the elements that commute with all others. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_cntr {m: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccntr (cmpt m cvv (cfv (cfv (cv m) cbs) (cfv (cv m) ccntz))) $;

--| Substitute definition of the center. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntrval (B2 M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv B2 Z) (cfv M ccntr) $;

--| First level substitution for a centralizer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzfval {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (c_pl M: class) (V Z: class x3 y1 s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wceq Z (cmpt s1 (cpw B2) (crab x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl)) (cv s1)) B2))) $;

--| Definition substitution for a centralizer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (c_pl S M: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wss S B2) (wceq (cfv S Z) (crab x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl)) S) B2)) $;

--| Elementhood in the centralizer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem elcntz {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (c_pl S M: class) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wss S B2) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv S Z)) (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wral y1 (wceq (co A2 (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) A2 c_pl)) S))) $;

--| Membership in a centralizer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzel {y1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (c_pl S M X: class) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S B2) (wcel X B2)) (wb (wcel X (cfv S Z)) (wral y1 (wceq (co X (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) X c_pl)) S)) $;

--| Special substitution for the centralizer of a singleton. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzsnval {x3: setvar} (B2 c_pl M Y: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y B2) (wceq (cfv (csn Y) Z) (crab x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) Y c_pl) (co Y (cv x3) c_pl)) B2)) $;

--| Value of the centralizer of a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Apr-2016.)
theorem elcntzsn (B2 c_pl M X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y B2) (wb (wcel X (cfv (csn Y) Z)) (wa (wcel X B2) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)))) $;

--| A centralizer expression for two sets elementwise commuting. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem sscntz {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class y1) (c_pl S T M: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S B2) (wss T B2)) (wb (wss S (cfv T Z)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl)) T) S)) $;

--| Reverse closure for elements of the centralizer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzrcl (B2 S M X Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv S Z)) (wa (wcel M cvv) (wss S B2)) $;

--| The centralizer is unconditionally a subset. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzssv (B2 S M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wss (cfv S Z) B2 $;

--| Membership in a centralizer (inference). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzi (c_pl S M X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv S Z)) (wcel Y S)) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)) $;

--| Elementhood in the center of a magma. (Contributed by SN, 21-Mar-2025.)
theorem elcntr {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 c_pl M: class) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntr) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 Z) (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wral y1 (wceq (co A2 (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) A2 c_pl)) B2)) $;

--| The center is a subset of the base field. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Aug-2023.)
theorem cntrss (B2 M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wss (cfv M ccntr) B2 $;

--| Defining property of the center of a magma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntri (B2 c_pl M X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X Z) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co Y X c_pl)) $;

--| Centralizer in a substructure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2015.)
theorem resscntz (A2 S G H V Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss S A2)) (wceq (cfv S Y) (cin (cfv S Z) A2)) $;

--| Centralizers are closed under the semigroup operation. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2025.)
theorem cntzsgrpcl {y1 z: setvar} (B2 C S M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M csgrp) (wss S B2)) (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cfv M cplusg)) C) C) C) $;

--| Centralizers reverse the subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2015.)
theorem cntz2ss (B2 S T M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S B2) (wss T S)) (wss (cfv S Z) (cfv T Z)) $;

--| Reciprocity relationship for centralizers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzrec (B2 S T M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S B2) (wss T B2)) (wb (wss S (cfv T Z)) (wss T (cfv S Z))) $;

--| Express any centralizer as an intersection of singleton centralizers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntziinsn {x3: setvar} (B2 S M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wss S B2) (wceq (cfv S Z) (cin B2 (ciin x3 S (cfv (csn (cv x3)) Z)))) $;

--| Centralizers in a monoid are submonoids. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem cntzsubm (B2 S M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wss S B2)) (wcel (cfv S Z) (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| Centralizers in a group are subgroups. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntzsubg (B2 S M Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cgrp) (wss S B2)) (wcel (cfv S Z) (cfv M csubg)) $;

--| If the elements of ` S ` commute, the elements of a subset ` T ` also commute. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Apr-2016.)
theorem cntzidss (S T G Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S (cfv S Z)) (wss T S)) (wss T (cfv T Z)) $;

--| Centralizers in a monoid are preserved by monoid homomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem cntzmhm (A2 S F G H Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co G H cmhm)) (wcel A2 (cfv S Z))) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv (cima F S) Y)) $;

--| Centralizers in a monoid are preserved by monoid homomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem cntzmhm2 (S T F G H Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv H ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co G H cmhm)) (wss S (cfv T Z))) (wss (cima F S) (cfv (cima F T) Y)) $;

--| A central subgroup is normal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntrsubgnsg (M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv M csubg)) (wss X Z)) (wcel X (cfv M cnsg)) $;

--| The center of a group is a normal subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem cntrnsg (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M ccntr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cgrp) (wcel Z (cfv M cnsg)) $;

--| The opposite group operation.
term coppg: class;

--| Define an opposite group, which is the same as the original group but with addition written the other way around. ~ df-oppr does the same thing for multiplication. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_oppg {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq coppg (cmpt w cvv (co (cv w) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctpos (cfv (cv w) cplusg))) csts)) $;

--| Value of the opposite group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Fan Zheng, 26-Jun-2016.)
theorem oppgval (c_pl R O: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq O (co R (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ctpos c_pl)) csts) $;

--| Value of the addition operation of an opposite group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Fan Zheng, 26-Jun-2016.)
theorem oppgplusfval (c_pl c_pb R O: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv O cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (ctpos c_pl) $;

--| Value of the addition operation of an opposite ring. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Fan Zheng, 26-Jun-2016.)
theorem oppgplus (c_pl c_pb R O X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv O cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq (co X Y c_pb) (co Y X c_pl) $;

--| The other components of an extensible structure remain unchanged if the ` +g ` component is set/substituted. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.) Generalisation of the former oppglem and mgplem. (Revised by AV, 18-Oct-2024.)
theorem setsplusg (R S E O: class):
  $ wceq O (co R (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) S) csts) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) (cfv cnx cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv R E) (cfv O E) $;

--| Base set of an opposite group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppgbas (B2 R O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv O cbs) $;

--| Topology of an opposite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgtset (R J O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R cts) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv O cts) $;

--| Topology of an opposite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgtopn (R J O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv O ctopn) $;

--| The opposite of a monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgmnd (R O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cmnd) (wcel O cmnd) $;

--| Bidirectional form of ~ oppgmnd . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppgmndb (R O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cmnd) (wcel O cmnd) $;

--| Zero in a monoid is a symmetric notion. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgid (R O c_0: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv O c0g) $;

--| The opposite of a group is a group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppggrp (R O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cgrp) (wcel O cgrp) $;

--| Bidirectional form of ~ oppggrp . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppggrpb (R O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cgrp) (wcel O cgrp) $;

--| Inverses in a group are a symmetric notion. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppginv (R I O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cgrp) (wceq I (cfv O cminusg)) $;

--| The inverse is an antiautomorphism on any group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem invoppggim (G I O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel I (co G O cgim)) $;

--| Every group is (naturally) isomorphic to its opposite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem oppggic (G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wbr G O cgic) $;

--| Being a submonoid is a symmetric property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgsubm (G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G csubmnd) (cfv O csubmnd) $;

--| Being a subgroup is a symmetric property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgsubg (G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G csubg) (cfv O csubg) $;

--| A centralizer in a group is the same as the centralizer in the opposite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem oppgcntz (A2 G O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 Z) (cfv A2 (cfv O ccntz)) $;

--| The center of a group is the same as the center of the opposite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem oppgcntr (G O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv O ccntr) $;

--| A sum in an opposite monoid is the regular sum of a reversed word. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2016.)
theorem gsumwrev (B2 M O W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv M coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmnd) (wcel W (cword B2))) (wceq (co O W cgsu) (co M (cfv W creverse) cgsu)) $;

--| less-than relation of an opposite group. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Apr-2018.)
theorem oppgle (R c_le O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv R cple) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cfv O cple) $;

--| less-than relation of an opposite group. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Apr-2018.)
theorem oppglt (R c_lt O V: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_lt (cfv R cplt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq c_lt (cfv O cplt)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of symmetric groups.
term csymg: class;

--| Define the symmetric group on set ` x ` . We represent the group as the set of one-to-one onto functions from ` x ` to itself under function composition, and topologize it as a function space assuming the set is discrete. This definition is based on the fact that a symmetric group is a restriction of the monoid of endofunctions. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 28-Mar-2024.)
axiom df_symg {x3 h: setvar}:
  $ wceq csymg (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cfv (cv x3) cefmnd) (cab h (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv h))) cress)) $;

--| The value of the symmetric group function at ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 28-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgval {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wf1o A2 A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co (cfv A2 cefmnd) B2 cress) $;

--| The base set of the symmetric group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgbas {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wf1o A2 A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| Two ways of saying a function is a 1-1-onto mapping of A to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2015.)
theorem elsymgbas2 (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F B2) (wf1o A2 A2 F)) $;

--| Two ways of saying a function is a 1-1-onto mapping of A to itself. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2015.)
theorem elsymgbas (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel F B2) (wf1o A2 A2 F)) $;

--| Elements in the symmetric group are 1-1 onto functions. (Contributed by SO, 9-Jul-2018.)
theorem symgbasf1o (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wf1o A2 A2 F) $;

--| A permutation (element of the symmetric group) is a function from a set into itself. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgbasf (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wf A2 A2 F) $;

--| A permutation (element of the symmetric group) is a mapping (or set exponentiation) from a set into itself. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgbasmap (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wcel F (co A2 A2 cmap)) $;

--| The symmetric group on ` n ` objects has cardinality ` n ! ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem symghash (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) (cfv (cfv A2 chash) cfa)) $;

--| The symmetric group on a finite index set is finite. (Contributed by SO, 9-Jul-2018.)
theorem symgbasfi (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel B2 cfn) $;

--| The function value of a permutation. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfv (A2 B2 F G X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel X A2)) (wcel (cfv X F) A2) $;

--| The function values of a permutation for different arguments are different. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfvne (A2 B2 F G X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F B2) (wcel X A2) (wcel Y A2)) (wi (wceq (cfv X F) Z) (wi (wne Y X) (wne (cfv Y F) Z))) $;

--| The symmetric group on ` A ` characterized as structure restriction of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` to its base set. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgressbas (A2 B2 G M: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq G (co M B2 cress) $;

--| The group operation of a symmetric group is the function composition. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 19-Feb-2024.) (Revised by AV, 29-Mar-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Aug-2024.)
theorem symgplusg {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (c_pl G: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 A2 cmap) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $;

--| The value of the group operation of the symmetric group on ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgov (A2 B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (ccom X Y)) $;

--| The group operation of the symmetric group on ` A ` is closed, i.e. a magma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2015.)
theorem symgcl (A2 B2 c_pl G X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) B2) $;

--| The identity function restricted to a set is a permutation of this set. (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2019.)
theorem idresperm (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cres cid A2) (cfv G cbs)) $;

--| For a permutation of a set, each element of the set replaces an(other) element of the set. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgmov1 {k n: setvar} (P: class k) (Q N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Q P) (wral n (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv n) Q) (cv k)) N) N) $;

--| For a permutation of a set, each element of the set is replaced by an(other) element of the set. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgmov2 {k n: setvar} (P: class k) (Q N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Q P) (wral n (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv k) Q) (cv n)) N) N) $;

--| The base set of the symmetric group on the empty set is the singleton containing the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2019.)
theorem symgbas0:
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv c0 csymg) cbs) (csn c0) $;

--| The symmetric group on a singleton has cardinality ` 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.)
theorem symg1hash (A2 B2 G I V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (csn I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) c1) $;

--| The symmetric group on a singleton is the symmetric group S_1 consisting of the identity only. (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.)
theorem symg1bas (A2 B2 G I V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (csn I) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq B2 (csn (csn (cop I I)))) $;

--| The symmetric group on a (proper) pair has cardinality ` 2 ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.)
theorem symg2hash (A2 B2 G I J V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr I J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel J W) (wne I J)) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) c2) $;

--| The symmetric group on a pair is the symmetric group S_2 consisting of the identity and the transposition. Notice that this statement is valid for proper pairs only. In the case that both elements are identical, i.e., the pairs are actually singletons, this theorem would be about S_1, see Theorem ~ symg1bas . (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Jun-2022.)
theorem symg2bas (A2 B2 G I J V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr I J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel J W)) (wceq B2 (cpr (cpr (cop I I) (cop J J)) (cpr (cop I J) (cop J I)))) $;

--| The symmetric group on the empty set is identical with the monoid of endofunctions on the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem _0symgefmndeq:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cefmnd) (cfv c0 csymg) $;

--| The symmetric group on a singleton ` A ` is identical with the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2024.)
theorem snsymgefmndeq (A2 X: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 (csn X)) (wceq (cfv A2 cefmnd) (cfv A2 csymg)) $;

--| For a set ` A ` with more than one element, the symmetric group on ` A ` is a proper subset of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgpssefmnd (A2 G M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wbr c1 (cfv A2 chash) clt)) (wpss (cfv G cbs) (cfv M cbs)) $;

--| The value of the symmetric group function at ` A ` represented as extensible structure with three slots. This corresponds to the former definition of ` SymGrp ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Mar-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Nov-2024.)
theorem symgvalstruct {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 c_pl G J: class f2 g2) (M: class x3) (V: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wf1o A2 A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq M (co A2 A2 cmap) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cmpo f2 g2 M M (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (cxp A2 (csn (cpw A2))) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq G (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J))) $;

--| The symmetric group on a set ` A ` is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.)
theorem symgsubmefmnd (A2 B2 G M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| The topology of the symmetric group on ` A ` . This component is defined on a larger set than the true base - the product topology is defined on the set of all functions, not just bijections - but the definition of ` TopOpen ` ensures that it is trimmed down before it gets use. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.) (Proof revised by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgtset (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cxp A2 (csn (cpw A2))) cpt) (cfv G cts)) $;

--| The symmetric group on a set ` A ` is a group. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Jan-2024.)
theorem symggrp (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| The group identity element of the symmetric group on a set ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Apr-2024.)
theorem symgid (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cres cid A2) (cfv G c0g)) $;

--| The group inverse in the symmetric group corresponds to the functional inverse. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem symginv (A2 B2 F G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wceq (cfv F N) (ccnv F)) $;

--| The symmetric group on a set ` A ` is a submonoid of the monoid of endofunctions on ` A ` . Alternate proof based on ~ issubmndb and not on ~ injsubmefmnd and ~ sursubmefmnd . (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2024.) (Revised by AV, 30-Mar-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem symgsubmefmndALT (A2 B2 G M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 (cfv M csubmnd)) $;

--| The currying of a group action is a group homomorphism between the group ` G ` and the symmetric group ` ( SymGrp `` Y ) ` . (Contributed by FL, 17-May-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem galactghm {x3 y1: setvar} (c_po: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class) (H: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv Y csymg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_po))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $;

--| The converse of ~ galactghm . The uncurrying of a homomorphism into ` ( SymGrp `` Y ) ` is a group action. Thus, group actions and group homomorphisms into a symmetric group are essentially equivalent notions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem lactghmga {x3 y1: setvar} (c_po: class x3 y1) (F G H X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv Y csymg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cghm)) (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) $;

--| The topology of the symmetric group on ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem symgtopn (B2 G V X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (co (cfv (cxp X (csn (cpw X))) cpt) B2 crest) (cfv G ctopn)) $;

--| The symmetric group induces a group action on its base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem symgga {x3 f2: setvar} (B2: class) (F: class x3 f2) (G V X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo f2 x3 B2 X (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel F (co G X cga)) $;

--| Every permutation group is a subgroup of the corresponding symmetric group. (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2019.)
theorem pgrpsubgsymgbi (A2 B2 P G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel P (cfv G csubg)) (wa (wss P B2) (wcel (co G P cress) cgrp))) $;

--| Every permutation group is a subgroup of the corresponding symmetric group. (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem pgrpsubgsymg {f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (P: class f2 g2) (F: class) (G V: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (w3a (wcel P cgrp) (wss F B2) (wceq (cfv P cplusg) (cmpo f2 g2 F F (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))))) (wcel F (cfv G csubg))) $;

--| The singleton containing only the identity function restricted to a set is a subgroup of the symmetric group of this set. (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2019.)
theorem idressubgsymg (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (csn (cres cid A2)) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The structure with the singleton containing only the identity function restricted to a set as base set and the function composition as group operation (constructed by (structure) restricting the symmetric group to that singleton) is a permutation group (group consisting of permutations). (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2019.)
theorem idrespermg (A2 E G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq E (co G (csn (cres cid A2)) cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wa (wcel E cgrp) (wss (cfv E cbs) (cfv G cbs))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cayley . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cayleylem1 {g2 a: setvar} (c_pl: class) (S F: class g2 a) (G: class) (H: class a) (X: class) (c_0: class g2):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel F (co G H cghm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cayley . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cayleylem2 {g2 a: setvar} (c_pl: class) (S F: class g2 a) (G: class) (H: class a) (X: class) (c_0: class g2):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wf1 X S F) $;

--| Cayley's Theorem (constructive version): given group ` G ` , ` F ` is an isomorphism between ` G ` and the subgroup ` S ` of the symmetric group ` H ` on the underlying set ` X ` of ` G ` . See also Theorem 3.15 in [Rotman] p. 42. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cayley {g2 a: setvar} (c_pl: class) (S F: class g2 a) (G: class) (H: class a) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 X (cmpt a X (co (cv g2) (cv a) c_pl))) $ >
  $ wceq S (crn F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (w3a (wcel S (cfv H csubg)) (wcel F (co G (co H S cress) cghm)) (wf1o X S F)) $;

--| Cayley's Theorem (existence version): every group ` G ` is isomorphic to a subgroup of the symmetric group on the underlying set of ` G ` . (For any group ` G ` there exists an isomorphism ` f ` between ` G ` and a subgroup ` h ` of the symmetric group on the underlying set of ` G ` .) See also Theorem 3.15 in [Rotman] p. 42. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 3-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jan-2015.)
theorem cayleyth {f2 s1: setvar} (G H X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv X csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wrex s1 (wrex f2 (wf1o X (cv s1) (cv f2)) (co G (co H (cv s1) cress) cghm)) (cfv H csubg)) $;

--| If a permutation does not move a certain element of a set to a second element, there is a third element which is moved to the second element. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfix2 {k q: setvar} (P: class k) (Q K L: class) (N: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel L N) (wi (wcel Q (cdif P (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) L) P))) (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv k) Q) L) (cdif N (csn K)))) $;

--| The extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, is a function. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextf {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wf N N E) $;

--| The function value of the extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, for elements in the original domain. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextfv {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N X Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wi (wcel X (cdif N (csn K))) (wceq (cfv X E) (cfv X Z))) $;

--| The function value of the extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, for the additional element. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextfve {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N X Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K N) (wi (wceq X K) (wceq (cfv X E) K)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ symgextf1 . (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextf1lem {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wi (wa (wcel X (cdif N (csn K))) (wcel Y (csn K))) (wne (cfv X E) (cfv Y E))) $;

--| The extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, is a 1-1 function. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextf1 {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wf1 N N E) $;

--| The extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, is an onto function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextfo {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wfo N N E) $;

--| The extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextf1o {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wf1o N N E) $;

--| The extension of a permutation is an element of the extended symmetric group. (Contributed by AV, 9-Mar-2019.)
theorem symgextsymg {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N: class) (V: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N V) (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wcel E (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs)) $;

--| The restriction of the extension of a permutation, fixing the additional element, to the original domain. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgextres {x3: setvar} (S: class) (E: class x3) (K N Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wceq (cres E (cdif N (csn K))) Z) $;

--| Homomorphic property of composites of permutations with a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsumccatsymgsn (A2 B2 G V W Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel Z B2)) (wceq (co G (co W (cs1 Z) cconcat) cgsu) (ccom (co G W cgsu) Z)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gsmsymgrfix . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmsymgrfixlem1 {i: setvar} (B2 P: class) (S: class i) (K N W: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel P B2)) (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel K N)) (wi (wral i (wceq (cfv K (cfv (cv i) W)) K) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wceq (cfv K (co S W cgsu)) K))) (wi (wral i (wceq (cfv K (cfv (cv i) (co W (cs1 P) cconcat))) K) (co cc0 (co (cfv W chash) c1 caddc) cfzo)) (wceq (cfv K (co S (co W (cs1 P) cconcat) cgsu)) K)) $;

--| The composition of permutations fixing one element also fixes this element. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmsymgrfix {i: setvar} (B2: class) (S: class i) (K N W: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel K N) (wcel W (cword B2))) (wi (wral i (wceq (cfv K (cfv (cv i) W)) K) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wceq (cfv K (co S W cgsu)) K)) $;

--| The values of two compositions of permutations are equal if the values of the composed permutations are pairwise equal. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jan-2019.)
theorem fvcosymgeq {n: setvar} (B2 P S: class n) (F G H I K: class) (M N: class n) (X: class) (Z: class n):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M csymg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cin N M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G B2) (wcel K P)) (wi (w3a (wcel X I) (wceq (cfv X G) (cfv X K)) (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (cv n) H)) I)) (wceq (cfv X (ccom F G)) (cfv X (ccom H K)))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gsmsymgreq . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmsymgreqlem1 {n: setvar} (B2: class n) (C: class) (P: class n) (R S I J: class) (M N: class n) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M csymg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cin N M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel M cfn) (wcel J I)) (w3a (wa (wcel X (cword B2)) (wcel C B2)) (wa (wcel Y (cword P)) (wcel R P)) (wceq (cfv X chash) (cfv Y chash)))) (wi (wa (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S X cgsu)) (cfv (cv n) (co Z Y cgsu))) I) (wceq (cfv J C) (cfv J R))) (wceq (cfv J (co S (co X (cs1 C) cconcat) cgsu)) (cfv J (co Z (co Y (cs1 R) cconcat) cgsu)))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gsmsymgreq . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmsymgreqlem2 {i n: setvar} (B2 C P R: class) (S: class i) (I: class) (M: class i) (N X Y: class) (Z: class i):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M csymg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cin N M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel M cfn)) (w3a (wa (wcel X (cword B2)) (wcel C B2)) (wa (wcel Y (cword P)) (wcel R P)) (wceq (cfv X chash) (cfv Y chash)))) (wi (wi (wral i (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) X)) (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) Y))) I) (co cc0 (cfv X chash) cfzo)) (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S X cgsu)) (cfv (cv n) (co Z Y cgsu))) I)) (wi (wral i (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) (co X (cs1 C) cconcat))) (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) (co Y (cs1 R) cconcat)))) I) (co cc0 (cfv (co X (cs1 C) cconcat) chash) cfzo)) (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S (co X (cs1 C) cconcat) cgsu)) (cfv (cv n) (co Z (co Y (cs1 R) cconcat) cgsu))) I))) $;

--| Two combination of permutations moves an element of the intersection of the base sets of the permutations to the same element if each pair of corresponding permutations moves such an element to the same element. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmsymgreq {i n: setvar} (B2 P: class) (S: class i) (U I: class) (M: class i) (N W: class) (Z: class i):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M csymg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cin N M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel M cfn)) (w3a (wcel W (cword B2)) (wcel U (cword P)) (wceq (cfv W chash) (cfv U chash)))) (wi (wral i (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) W)) (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) U))) I) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S W cgsu)) (cfv (cv n) (co Z U cgsu))) I)) $;

--| A permutation of a set fixing an element of the set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixelq {q: setvar} (P: class) (Q F: class q) (K: class) (N V: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F Q) (wa (wf1o N N F) (wceq (cfv K F) K))) $;

--| The restriction of a permutation to a set with one element removed is an element of the restricted symmetric group if the restriction is a 1-1 onto function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixels {q: setvar} (D: class q) (P: class) (Q S F: class q) (K: class) (N V: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif N (csn K)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel (cres F D) S) (wf1o D D (cres F D))) $;

--| The restriction of a permutation fixing an element to the set with this element removed is an element of the restricted symmetric group. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixelsi {q: setvar} (D: class q) (P: class) (Q S F: class q) (K: class) (N: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif N (csn K)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K N) (wcel F Q)) (wcel (cres F D) S) $;

--| The mapping of a permutation of a set fixing an element to a permutation of the set without the fixed element is a function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixf {q: setvar} (P Q S: class) (H: class q) (K N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt q Q (cres (cv q) (cdif N (csn K)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K N) (wf Q S H) $;

--| The mapping of a permutation of a set fixing an element to a permutation of the set without the fixed element is a 1-1 function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixf1 {q: setvar} (P Q S: class) (H: class q) (K N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt q Q (cres (cv q) (cdif N (csn K)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K N) (wf1 Q S H) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ symgfixfo . (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixfolem1 {x3 q: setvar} (P Q: class x3) (S: class) (E: class q) (H: class x3 q) (K N: class) (V Z: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt q Q (cres (cv q) (cdif N (csn K)))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 N (cif (wceq (cv x3) K) K (cfv (cv x3) Z))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N V) (wcel K N) (wcel Z S)) (wcel E Q) $;

--| The mapping of a permutation of a set fixing an element to a permutation of the set without the fixed element is an onto function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixfo {q: setvar} (P Q S: class) (H: class q) (K N: class) (V: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt q Q (cres (cv q) (cdif N (csn K)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wcel K N)) (wfo Q S H) $;

--| The mapping of a permutation of a set fixing an element to a permutation of the set without the fixed element is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2019.)
theorem symgfixf1o {q: setvar} (P Q S: class) (H: class q) (K N: class) (V: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wceq (cfv K (cv q)) K) P) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt q Q (cres (cv q) (cdif N (csn K)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wcel K N)) (wf1o Q S H) $;

--| Syntax for the transposition generator function.
term cpmtr: class;

--| Define a function that generates the transpositions on a set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_pmtr {y1 z p d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpmtr (cmpt d cvv (cmpt p (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) c2o cen) (cpw (cv d))) (cmpt z (cv d) (cif (wcel (cv z) (cv p)) (cuni (cdif (cv p) (csn (cv z)))) (cv z))))) $;

--| A permutation of any class moves a point which is moved to a different point which is moved. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1omvdmvd (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf1o A2 A2 F) (wcel X (cdm (cdif F cid)))) (wcel (cfv X F) (cdif (cdm (cdif F cid)) (csn X))) $;

--| A permutation and its inverse move the same points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1omvdcnv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf1o A2 A2 F) (wceq (cdm (cdif (ccnv F) cid)) (cdm (cdif F cid))) $;

--| Composing two permutations moves at most the union of the points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem mvdco (F G: class):
  $ wss (cdm (cdif (ccom F G) cid)) (cun (cdm (cdif F cid)) (cdm (cdif G cid))) $;

--| Conjugation of a permutation takes the image of the moved subclass. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1omvdconj (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 A2 F) (wf1o A2 A2 G)) (wceq (cdm (cdif (ccom (ccom G F) (ccnv G)) cid)) (cima G (cdm (cdif F cid)))) $;

--| A transposition is characterized by the points it moves. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1otrspeq (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wf1o A2 A2 F) (wf1o A2 A2 G)) (wa (wbr (cdm (cdif F cid)) c2o cen) (wceq (cdm (cdif G cid)) (cdm (cdif F cid))))) (wceq F G) $;

--| If exactly one of two permutations is limited to a set of points, then the composition will not be. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1omvdco2 (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1o A2 A2 F) (wf1o A2 A2 G) (wxo (wss (cdm (cdif F cid)) X) (wss (cdm (cdif G cid)) X))) (wn (wss (cdm (cdif (ccom F G) cid)) X)) $;

--| If a point is moved by exactly one of two permutations, then it will be moved by their composite. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem f1omvdco3 (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf1o A2 A2 F) (wf1o A2 A2 G) (wxo (wcel X (cdm (cdif F cid))) (wcel X (cdm (cdif G cid))))) (wcel X (cdm (cdif (ccom F G) cid))) $;

--| The function generating transpositions on a set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfval {y1 z p: setvar} (D T: class) (V: class y1 p):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wceq T (cmpt p (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) c2o cen) (cpw D)) (cmpt z D (cif (wcel (cv z) (cv p)) (cuni (cdif (cv p) (csn (cv z)))) (cv z))))) $;

--| A generated transposition, expressed in a symmetric form. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrval {z: setvar} (D P T V: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wceq (cfv P T) (cmpt z D (cif (wcel (cv z) P) (cuni (cdif P (csn (cv z)))) (cv z)))) $;

--| General value of mapping a point under a transposition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfv (D P T V Z: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D V) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wcel Z D)) (wceq (cfv Z (cfv P T)) (cif (wcel Z P) (cuni (cdif P (csn Z))) Z)) $;

--| In a transposition of two given points, each maps to the other. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrprfv (D T V X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D V) (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wne X Y))) (wceq (cfv X (cfv (cpr X Y) T)) Y) $;

--| In a transposition of two given points, all other points are mapped to themselves. (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2019.)
theorem pmtrprfv3 (D T V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wcel Z D)) (w3a (wne X Y) (wne X Z) (wne Y Z))) (wceq (cfv Z (cfv (cpr X Y) T)) Z) $;

--| Functionality of a transposition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrf (D P T V: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wf D D (cfv P T)) $;

--| A transposition moves precisely the transposed points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrmvd (D P T V: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wceq (cdm (cdif (cfv P T) cid)) P) $;

--| Transposing two points gives a transposition function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrrn (D P R T V: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wcel (cfv P T) R) $;

--| A transposition (as a kind of function) is the function transposing the two points it moves. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfrn (D P R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wceq P (cdm (cdif F cid)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wa (w3a (wcel D cvv) (wss P D) (wbr P c2o cen)) (wceq F (cfv P T))) $;

--| Mapping of a point under a transposition function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrffv (D P R T F Z: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wceq P (cdm (cdif F cid)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F R) (wcel Z D)) (wceq (cfv Z F) (cif (wcel Z P) (cuni (cdif P (csn Z))) Z)) $;

--| For any transposition there are two points it is transposing. (Contributed by SO, 15-Jul-2018.)
theorem pmtrrn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq F (cfv (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) T))) D) D) $;

--| A transposition function is an involution. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfinv (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wceq (ccom F F) (cres cid D)) $;

--| A transposition moves at least one point. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfmvdn0 (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wne (cdm (cdif F cid)) c0) $;

--| A transposition function is a permutation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrff1o (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wf1o D D F) $;

--| A transposition function is its own inverse. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfcnv (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F R) (wceq (ccnv F) F) $;

--| An intrinsic characterization of the transposition permutations. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfb (D R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F R) (w3a (wcel D cvv) (wf1o D D F) (wbr (cdm (cdif F cid)) c2o cen)) $;

--| Any conjugate of a transposition is a transposition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pmtrfconj (D R T F G: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn T) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F R) (wf1o D D G)) (wcel (ccom (ccom G F) (ccnv G)) R) $;

--| The symmetric group has subgroups restricting the set of non-fixed points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem symgsssg {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (D: class x3) (G: class) (V: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wcel (crab x3 (wss (cdm (cdif (cv x3) cid)) X) B2) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The symmetric group has a subgroup of permutations that move finitely many points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem symgfisg {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (D: class x3) (G: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wcel (crab x3 (wcel (cdm (cdif (cv x3) cid)) cfn) B2) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Transpositions are elements of the symmetric group. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem symgtrf (B2 D T G: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wss T B2 $;

--| The span of the transpositions is the subgroup that moves finitely many points. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem symggen {x3: setvar} (B2 D T G K V: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubmnd) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wceq (cfv T K) (crab x3 (wcel (cdm (cdif (cv x3) cid)) cfn) B2)) $;

--| A finite permutation group is generated by the transpositions, see also Theorem 3.4 in [Rotman] p. 31. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem symggen2 (B2 D T G K: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubmnd) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D cfn) (wceq (cfv T K) B2) $;

--| To invert a permutation represented as a sequence of transpositions, reverse the sequence. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem symgtrinv (D T G I V W: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D V) (wcel W (cword T))) (wceq (cfv (co G W cgsu) I) (co G (cfv W creverse) cgsu)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ pmtr3ncom . (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2018.)
theorem pmtr3ncomlem1 (D T F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv (cpr X Y) T) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cpr Y Z) T) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wcel Z D)) (w3a (wne X Y) (wne X Z) (wne Y Z))) (wne (cfv X (ccom G F)) (cfv X (ccom F G))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ pmtr3ncom . (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2018.)
theorem pmtr3ncomlem2 (D T F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv (cpr X Y) T) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cpr Y Z) T) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D V) (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wcel Z D)) (w3a (wne X Y) (wne X Z) (wne Y Z))) (wne (ccom G F) (ccom F G)) $;

--| Transpositions over sets with at least 3 elements are not commutative, see also the remark in [Rotman] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 21-Mar-2019.)
theorem pmtr3ncom {f2 g2: setvar} (D T: class) (V: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq T (cfv D cpmtr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D V) (wbr c3 (cfv D chash) cle)) (wrex f2 (wrex g2 (wne (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2)) (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) (crn T)) (crn T)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ pmtrdifel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifellem1 (Q R S T K N: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cdm (cdif Q cid)) (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Q T) (wcel S R) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ pmtrdifel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifellem2 (Q R S T K N: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cdm (cdif Q cid)) (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Q T) (wceq (cdm (cdif S cid)) (cdm (cdif Q cid))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ pmtrdifel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifellem3 {x3: setvar} (Q: class) (R S: class x3) (T: class) (K N: class x3):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cdm (cdif Q cid)) (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Q T) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) Q) (cfv (cv x3) S)) (cdif N (csn K))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ pmtrdifel . (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifellem4 (Q R S T K N: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cdm (cdif Q cid)) (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Q T) (wcel K N)) (wceq (cfv K S) K) $;

--| A transposition of elements of a set without a special element corresponds to a transposition of elements of the set. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifel {x3 t r: setvar} (R: class x3 t) (T: class t r) (K: class x3 t) (N: class t):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wral t (wrex r (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv t)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r))) (cdif N (csn K))) R) T $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ pmtrdifwrdel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdellem1 {x3: setvar} (R T: class) (U K: class x3) (N W: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (cfv (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv x3) W) cid)) (cfv N cpmtr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cword T)) (wcel U (cword R)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ pmtrdifwrdel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdellem2 {x3: setvar} (R T: class) (U K: class x3) (N W: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (cfv (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv x3) W) cid)) (cfv N cpmtr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cword T)) (wceq (cfv W chash) (cfv U chash)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ pmtrdifwrdel . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdellem3 {x3 i n: setvar} (R: class i n) (T: class) (U K: class x3 i n) (N: class i n) (W: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (cfv (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv x3) W) cid)) (cfv N cpmtr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cword T)) (wral i (wral n (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) W)) (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv i) U))) (cdif N (csn K))) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ pmtrdifwrdel2 . (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdel2lem1 {x3 i: setvar} (R: class i) (T: class) (U: class x3 i) (K: class x3) (N W: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo) (cfv (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv x3) W) cid)) (cfv N cpmtr))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W (cword T)) (wcel K N)) (wral i (wceq (cfv K (cfv (cv i) U)) K) (co cc0 (cfv W chash) cfzo)) $;

--| A sequence of transpositions of elements of a set without a special element corresponds to a sequence of transpositions of elements of the set. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdel {x3 w u i: setvar} (R: class x3 w) (T: class w u) (K: class x3 w i) (N: class w):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wral w (wrex u (wa (wceq (cfv (cv w) chash) (cfv (cv u) chash)) (wral i (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) (cv w))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) (cv u)))) (cdif N (csn K))) (co cc0 (cfv (cv w) chash) cfzo))) (cword R)) (cword T) $;

--| A sequence of transpositions of elements of a set without a special element corresponds to a sequence of transpositions of elements of the set not moving the special element. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2019.)
theorem pmtrdifwrdel2 {x3 w u i: setvar} (R: class x3 w) (T: class w u) (K: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv (cdif N (csn K)) cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K N) (wral w (wrex u (wa (wceq (cfv (cv w) chash) (cfv (cv u) chash)) (wral i (wa (wceq (cfv K (cfv (cv i) (cv u))) K) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) (cv w))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) (cv u)))) (cdif N (csn K)))) (co cc0 (cfv (cv w) chash) cfzo))) (cword R)) (cword T)) $;

--| The transpositions on a pair. (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.)
theorem pmtrprfval {z p: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv (cpr c1 c2) cpmtr) (cmpt p (csn (cpr c1 c2)) (cmpt z (cpr c1 c2) (cif (wceq (cv z) c1) c2 c1))) $;

--| The range of the transpositions on a pair is actually a singleton: the transposition of the two elements of the pair. (Contributed by AV, 9-Dec-2018.)
theorem pmtrprfvalrn:
  $ wceq (crn (cfv (cpr c1 c2) cpmtr)) (csn (cpr (cop c1 c2) (cop c2 c1))) $;

--| Syntax for the sign of a permutation.
term cpsgn: class;

--| Syntax for even permutations.
term cevpm: class;

--| Define a function which takes the value ` 1 ` for even permutations and ` -u 1 ` for odd. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_psgn {x3 w s1 p d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpsgn (cmpt d cvv (cmpt x3 (crab p (wcel (cdm (cdif (cv p) cid)) cfn) (cfv (cfv (cv d) csymg) cbs)) (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv d) csymg) (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword (crn (cfv (cv d) cpmtr))))))) $;

--| Define the set of even permutations on a given set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Jul-2018.)
axiom df_evpm {d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cevpm (cmpt d cvv (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv d) cpsgn)) (csn c1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psgnuni . Given two consequtive transpositions in a representation of a permutation, either they are equal and therefore equivalent to the identity, or they are not and it is possible to commute them such that a chosen point in the left transposition is preserved in the right. By repeating this process, a point can be removed from a representation of the identity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnunilem1 {s1 r: setvar} (ph: wff s1 r) (A2: class) (D: class s1 r) (P Q T: class) (V: class s1 r):
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif P cid))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq (ccom P Q) (cres cid D)) (wrex r (wrex s1 (w3a (wceq (ccom P Q) (ccom (cv r) (cv s1))) (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cv s1) cid))) (wn (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cv r) cid))))) T) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psgnuni . It is impossible to shift a transposition off the end because if the active transposition is at the right end, it is the only transposition moving ` A ` in contradiction to this being a representation of the identity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Oct-2022.)
theorem psgnunilem5 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (D T: class k) (G I: class) (L V: class k) (W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G W cgsu) (cres cid D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 L cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv I W) cid))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wn (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv k) W) cid)))) (co cc0 I cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co I c1 caddc) (co cc0 L cfzo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psgnuni . Induction step for moving a transposition as far to the right as possible. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Feb-2016.)
theorem psgnunilem2 {x3 w j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w k) (A2: class x3) (D T: class k) (G I: class) (L: class j k) (V: class x3 w j k) (W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G W cgsu) (cres cid D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 L cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv I W) cid))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wn (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv k) W) cid)))) (co cc0 I cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex x3 (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) (co L c2 cmin)) (wceq (co G (cv x3) cgsu) (cres cid D))) (cword T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wa (wa (wceq (co G (cv w) cgsu) (cres cid D)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) chash) L)) (w3a (wcel (co I c1 caddc) (co cc0 L cfzo)) (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv (co I c1 caddc) (cv w)) cid))) (wral j (wn (wcel A2 (cdm (cdif (cfv (cv j) (cv w)) cid)))) (co cc0 (co I c1 caddc) cfzo)))) (cword T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psgnuni . Any nonempty representation of the identity can be incrementally transformed into a representation two shorter. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnunilem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (D T G L: class) (V: class x3) (W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv W chash) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G W cgsu) (cres cid D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex x3 (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) (co L c2 cmin)) (wceq (co G (cv x3) cgsu) (cres cid D))) (cword T))) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ psgnuni . An odd-length representation of the identity is impossible, as it could be repeatedly shortened to a length of 1, but a length 1 permutation must be a transposition. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnunilem4 (ph: wff) (D T G V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G W cgsu) (cres cid D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cneg c1) (cfv W chash) cexp) c1) $;

--| Addition and subtraction of parities are the same. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem m1expaddsub (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cz) (wcel Y cz)) (wceq (co (cneg c1) (co X Y cmin) cexp) (co (cneg c1) (co X Y caddc) cexp)) $;

--| If the same permutation can be written in more than one way as a product of transpositions, the parity of those products must agree; otherwise the product of one with the inverse of the other would be an odd representation of the identity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnuni (ph: wff) (D T G V W X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cword T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G W cgsu) (co G X cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cneg c1) (cfv W chash) cexp) (co (cneg c1) (cfv X chash) cexp)) $;

--| Function definition of the permutation sign function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnfval {x3 w s1 p: setvar} (B2: class x3 w s1) (D: class p) (T: class x3 s1 p) (F: class w s1 p) (G N: class x3 w s1 p):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (crab p (wcel (cdm (cdif (cv p) cid)) cfn) B2) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt x3 F (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T)))) $;

--| Functionality and domain of the permutation sign function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnfn {p: setvar} (B2: class) (D F G N: class p):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (crab p (wcel (cdm (cdif (cv p) cid)) cfn) B2) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wfn N F $;

--| The finitary permutations are a subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgndmsubg (D G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wcel (cdm N) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Property of being a finitary permutation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgneldm (B2 D P G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel P (cdm N)) (wa (wcel P B2) (wcel (cdm (cdif P cid)) cfn)) $;

--| The finitary permutations are the span of the transpositions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgneldm2 {w: setvar} (D P T G N: class) (V: class w):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D V) (wb (wcel P (cdm N)) (wrex w (wceq P (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (cword T))) $;

--| A sequence of transpositions describes a finitary permutation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgneldm2i (D T G N V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D V) (wcel W (cword T))) (wcel (co G W cgsu) (cdm N)) $;

--| A finitary permutation has exactly one parity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgneu {w s1: setvar} (D P T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P (cdm N)) (weu s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq P (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T))) $;

--| Value of the permutation sign function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnval {w s1: setvar} (D P T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P (cdm N)) (wceq (cfv P N) (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq P (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T)))) $;

--| A finitary permutation has at least one representation for its parity. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnvali {w: setvar} (D P T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P (cdm N)) (wrex w (wa (wceq P (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cfv P N) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T)) $;

--| Any representation of a permutation is length matching the permutation sign. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnvalii (D T G N V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D V) (wcel W (cword T))) (wceq (cfv (co G W cgsu) N) (co (cneg c1) (cfv W chash) cexp)) $;

--| All transpositions are odd. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Aug-2015.)
theorem psgnpmtr (D P T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P T) (wceq (cfv P N) (cneg c1)) $;

--| The permutation sign function for an empty set at an empty set is 1. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2019.)
theorem psgn0fv0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 (cfv c0 cpsgn)) c1 $;

--| The class of non-fixed points of a permutation of a finite set is finite. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2019.)
theorem sygbasnfpfi (B2 D P G: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D cfn) (wcel P B2)) (wcel (cdm (cdif P cid)) cfn) $;

--| Function definition of the permutation sign function for permutations of finite sets. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2019.)
theorem psgnfvalfi {x3 w s1: setvar} (B2: class w s1) (D: class) (T: class x3 s1) (G N: class x3 w s1):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D cfn) (wceq N (cmpt x3 B2 (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T))))) $;

--| Value of the permutation sign function for permutations of finite sets. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2019.)
theorem psgnvalfi {w s1: setvar} (B2: class w s1) (D P T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D cfn) (wcel P B2)) (wceq (cfv P N) (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq P (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T)))) $;

--| The range of the permutation sign function for finite permutations. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jan-2019.)
theorem psgnran (P Q S N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel Q P)) (wcel (cfv Q S) (cpr c1 (cneg c1))) $;

--| The group sum of transpositions of a finite set is a permutation, see also ~ psgneldm2i . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jan-2019.)
theorem gsmtrcl (B2 S T N W: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel W (cword T))) (wcel (co S W cgsu) B2) $;

--| A permutation of a finite set is generated by transpositions. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2019.)
theorem psgnfitr {w: setvar} (B2: class w) (Q T G: class) (N: class w):
  $ wceq G (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cfn) (wb (wcel Q B2) (wrex w (wceq Q (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (cword T))) $;

--| A permutation of a finite set has exactly one parity. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2019.)
theorem psgnfieu {w s1: setvar} (B2: class w s1) (Q T G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv N cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel Q B2)) (weu s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq Q (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T))) $;

--| The value of the transposition generator function for a singleton is empty, i.e. there is no transposition for a singleton. This also holds for ` A e/ _V ` , i.e. for the empty set ` { A } = (/) ` resulting in ` ( pmTrsp `` (/) ) = (/) ` . (Contributed by AV, 6-Aug-2019.)
theorem pmtrsn (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (csn A2) cpmtr) c0 $;

--| The permutation sign function for a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 6-Aug-2019.)
theorem psgnsn (A2 B2 D G N V X: class):
  $ wceq D (csn A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv X N) c1) $;

--| The permutation sign function for a pair. (Contributed by AV, 10-Dec-2018.)
theorem psgnprfval {w s1: setvar} (B2: class w s1) (D T G N X: class):
  $ wceq D (cpr c1 c2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X B2) (wceq (cfv X N) (cio s1 (wrex w (wa (wceq X (co G (cv w) cgsu)) (wceq (cv s1) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (cv w) chash) cexp))) (cword T)))) $;

--| The permutation sign of the identity for a pair. (Contributed by AV, 11-Dec-2018.)
theorem psgnprfval1 (B2 D T G N: class):
  $ wceq D (cpr c1 c2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c2 c2)) N) c1 $;

--| The permutation sign of the transposition for a pair. (Contributed by AV, 10-Dec-2018.)
theorem psgnprfval2 (B2 D T G N: class):
  $ wceq D (cpr c1 c2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv D csymg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (crn (cfv D cpmtr)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cpr (cop c1 c2) (cop c2 c1)) N) (cneg c1) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the order function on the elements of a group.
term cod: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the order function on the elements of a group.
term cgex: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of all p-groups.
term cpgp: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of all Sylow p-subgroups of a group.
term cslw: class;

--| Define the order of an element in a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
axiom df_od {x3 g2 i n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cod (cmpt g2 cvv (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv g2) cbs) (csb i (crab n (wceq (co (cv n) (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) cmg)) (cfv (cv g2) c0g)) cn) (cif (wceq (cv i) c0) cc0 (cinf (cv i) cr clt))))) $;

--| Define the exponent of a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 4-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_gex {x3 g2 i n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgex (cmpt g2 cvv (csb i (crab n (wral x3 (wceq (co (cv n) (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) cmg)) (cfv (cv g2) c0g)) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)) cn) (cif (wceq (cv i) c0) cc0 (cinf (cv i) cr clt)))) $;

--| Define the set of p-groups, which are groups such that every element has a power of ` p ` as its order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
axiom df_pgp {x3 g2 n p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpgp (copab p g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv p) cprime) (wcel (cv g2) cgrp)) (wral x3 (wrex n (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) cod)) (co (cv p) (cv n) cexp)) cn0) (cfv (cv g2) cbs)))) $;

--| Define the set of Sylow p-subgroups of a group ` g ` . A Sylow p-subgroup is a p-group that is not a subgroup of any other p-groups in ` g ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_slw {g2 h k p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cslw (cmpo p g2 cprime cgrp (crab h (wral k (wb (wa (wss (cv h) (cv k)) (wbr (cv p) (co (cv g2) (cv k) cress) cpgp)) (wceq (cv h) (cv k))) (cfv (cv g2) csubg)) (cfv (cv g2) csubg))) $;

--| Value of the order function. For a shorter proof using ~ ax-rep , see ~ odfvalALT . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-rep . (Revised by Rohan Ridenour, 17-Aug-2023.)
theorem odfval {x3 y1 i: setvar} (c_x: class) (G: class i) (O: class x3 y1 i) (X: class y1 i) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt x3 X (csb i (crab y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_0) cn) (cif (wceq (cv i) c0) cc0 (cinf (cv i) cr clt)))) $;

--| Shorter proof of ~ odfval using ~ ax-rep . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem odfvalALT {x3 y1 i: setvar} (c_x: class) (G: class i) (O: class x3 y1 i) (X: class y1 i) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt x3 X (csb i (crab y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_0) cn) (cif (wceq (cv i) c0) cc0 (cinf (cv i) cr clt)))) $;

--| Second substitution for the group order definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
theorem odval {y1: setvar} (A2 c_x G: class) (I O X: class y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq I (crab y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) A2 c_x) c_0) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (cif (wceq I c0) cc0 (cinf I cr clt))) $;

--| The group element order is either zero or a nonzero multiplier that annihilates the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
theorem odlem1 {y1: setvar} (A2 c_x G: class) (I O X: class y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq I (crab y1 (wceq (co (cv y1) A2 c_x) c_0) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wo (wa (wceq (cfv A2 O) cc0) (wceq I c0)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) I)) $;

--| The order of a group element is always a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem odcl (A2 G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn0) $;

--| Functionality of the group element order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
theorem odf (G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wf X cn0 O $;

--| Any element to the power of its order is the identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem odid (A2 c_x G O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (co (cfv A2 O) A2 c_x) c_0) $;

--| Any positive annihilator of a group element is an upper bound on the (positive) order of the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
theorem odlem2 (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cn) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Reduce the argument of a group multiple by modding out the order of the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmodnn0 (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wceq (co (co N (cfv A2 O) cmo) A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mndodcong . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndodconglem (ph: wff) (A2 c_x G M N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (cfv A2 O) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N (cfv A2 O) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr M N cle)) (wceq M N) $;

--| If two multipliers are congruent relative to the base point's order, the corresponding multiples are the same. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndodcong (A2 c_x G M N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 O) (co M N cmin) cdvds) (wceq (co M A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x))) $;

--| If two multipliers are congruent relative to the base point's order, the corresponding multiples are the same. For monoids, the reverse implication is false for elements with infinite order. For example, the powers of ` 2 mod 1 0 ` are 1,2,4,8,6,2,4,8,6,... so that the identity 1 never repeats, which is infinite order by our definition, yet other numbers like 6 appear many times in the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem mndodcongi (A2 c_x G M N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 X) (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0))) (wi (wbr (cfv A2 O) (co M N cmin) cdvds) (wceq (co M A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x))) $;

--| The only multiples of ` A ` that are equal to the identity are the multiples of the order of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem oddvdsnn0 (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cn0)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 O) N cdvds) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0)) $;

--| If a nonzero multiple of an element is zero, the element has positive order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem odnncl (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wne N cc0) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0))) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn) $;

--| Reduce the argument of a group multiple by modding out the order of the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmod (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wceq (co (co N (cfv A2 O) cmo) A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x)) $;

--| The only multiples of ` A ` that are equal to the identity are the multiples of the order of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem oddvds (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 O) N cdvds) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0)) $;

--| Any group element is annihilated by any multiple of its order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem oddvdsi (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wbr (cfv A2 O) N cdvds)) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0) $;

--| If two multipliers are congruent relative to the base point's order, the corresponding multiples are the same. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem odcong (A2 c_x G M N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz))) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 O) (co M N cmin) cdvds) (wceq (co M A2 c_x) (co N A2 c_x))) $;

--| The ~ oddvds property uniquely defines the group order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odeq {y1: setvar} (A2 c_x G N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cn0)) (wb (wceq N (cfv A2 O)) (wral y1 (wb (wbr N (cv y1) cdvds) (wceq (co (cv y1) A2 c_x) c_0)) cn0)) $;

--| A non-conditional definition of the group order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odval2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (c_x: class x3) (G O X: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (crio x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cdvds) (wceq (co (cv y1) A2 c_x) c_0)) cn0) cn0)) $;

--| The order of a group element is always a nonnegative integer, deduction form of ~ odcl . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem odcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 G O: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn0) $;

--| The (order-1)th multiple of an element is its inverse. (Contributed by SN, 31-Jan-2025.)
theorem odm1inv (ph: wff) (A2 c_x G I O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (cfv A2 O) c1 cmin) A2 c_x) (cfv A2 I)) $;

--| A relationship between the order of a multiple and the order of the basepoint. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmulgid (A2 c_x G K N O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wbr (cfv (co N A2 c_x) O) K cdvds) (wbr (cfv A2 O) (co K N cmul) cdvds)) $;

--| The order of a multiple divides the order of the base point. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmulg2 (A2 c_x G N O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wbr (cfv (co N A2 c_x) O) (cfv A2 O) cdvds) $;

--| Relationship between the order of an element and that of a multiple. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmulg (A2 c_x G N O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (co (co N (cfv A2 O) cgcd) (cfv (co N A2 c_x) O) cmul)) $;

--| A multiple of a point of finite order only has the same order if the multiplier is relatively prime. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem odmulgeq (A2 c_x G N O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wb (wceq (cfv (co N A2 c_x) O) (cfv A2 O)) (wceq (co N (cfv A2 O) cgcd) c1)) $;

--| If ` N ` is coprime to the order of ` A ` , there is a modular inverse ` x ` to cancel multiplication by ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2016.)
theorem odbezout {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x G N O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co N (cfv A2 O) cgcd) c1)) (wrex x3 (wceq (co (cv x3) (co N A2 c_x) c_x) A2) cz) $;

--| The order of the group identity is one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem od1 (G O c_0: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (cfv c_0 O) c1) $;

--| The group identity is the unique element of a group with order one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem odeq1 (A2 G O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 O) c1) (wceq A2 c_0)) $;

--| The order of the inverse of a group element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem odinv (A2 G I O X: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 I) O) (cfv A2 O)) $;

--| The multiples of an element with infinite order form an infinite cyclic subgroup of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2015.)
theorem odf1 {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 O) cc0) (wf1 cz X F)) $;

--| The multiples of an element with infinite order form an infinite cyclic subgroup of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.)
theorem odinf {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 O) cc0)) (wn (wcel (crn F) cfn)) $;

--| An alternative definition of the order of a group element is as the cardinality of the cyclic subgroup generated by the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem dfod2 {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3) (G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (cif (wcel (crn F) cfn) (cfv (crn F) chash) cc0)) $;

--| The order of an element of a finite group is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.)
theorem odcl2 (A2 G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn) $;

--| The order of an element of a finite group divides the order (cardinality) of the group. Corollary of Lagrange's theorem for the order of a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.)
theorem oddvds2 (A2 G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr (cfv A2 O) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $;

--| A submonoid whose elements have finite order is a subgroup. (Contributed by SN, 31-Jan-2025.)
theorem finodsubmsubg {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (S G: class) (O: class a):
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wcel (cfv (cv a) O) cn) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| A shorter proof of ~ 0subg using ~ df-od . (Contributed by SN, 31-Jan-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _0subgALT (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cgrp) (wcel (csn c_0) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The order of an element is the same in a submonoid. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-Oct-2020.)
theorem submod (A2 P G H O Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G Y cress) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv H cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Y (cfv G csubmnd)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (cfv A2 P)) $;

--| The order of an element is the same in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem subgod (A2 P G H O Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G Y cress) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv H cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel Y (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 Y)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (cfv A2 P)) $;

--| The order of an element of a subgroup divides the order of the subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem odsubdvds (A2 S G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel S cfn) (wcel A2 S)) (wbr (cfv A2 O) (cfv S chash) cdvds) $;

--| An element with zero order has infinitely many multiples. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odf1o1 {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 O) cc0)) (wf1o cz (cfv (csn A2) K) (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) A2 c_x))) $;

--| An element with nonzero order has as many multiples as its order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem odf1o2 {x3: setvar} (A2 c_x G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wf1o (co cc0 (cfv A2 O) cfzo) (cfv (csn A2) K) (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv A2 O) cfzo) (co (cv x3) A2 c_x))) $;

--| An element of zero order generates an infinite subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem odhash (A2 G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 O) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (csn A2) K) chash) cpnf) $;

--| If an element has nonzero order, it generates a subgroup with size equal to the order. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem odhash2 (A2 G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (csn A2) K) chash) (cfv A2 O)) $;

--| An element which generates a finite subgroup has order the size of that subgroup. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem odhash3 (A2 G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel (cfv (csn A2) K) cfn)) (wceq (cfv A2 O) (cfv (cfv (csn A2) K) chash)) $;

--| A cyclic subgroup of size ` ( O `` A ) ` has ` ( phi `` ( O `` A ) ) ` generators. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem odngen {x3: setvar} (A2 G K O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv G csubg) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn)) (wceq (cfv (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) O) (cfv A2 O)) (cfv (csn A2) K)) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 O) cphi)) $;

--| Value of the exponent of a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2016.) (Revised by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem gexval {x3 y1: setvar} (c_x: class) (E: class x3 y1) (G: class) (I: class x3 y1) (V: class) (X: class y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq I (crab y1 (wral x3 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_0) X) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wceq E (cif (wceq I c0) cc0 (cinf I cr clt))) $;

--| The group element order is either zero or a nonzero multiplier that annihilates the element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem gexlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_x: class) (E: class x3 y1) (G: class) (I: class x3 y1) (V: class) (X: class y1) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq I (crab y1 (wral x3 (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_0) X) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wo (wa (wceq E cc0) (wceq I c0)) (wcel E I)) $;

--| The exponent of a group is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexcl (E G V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wcel E cn0) $;

--| Any element to the power of the group exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexid (A2 c_x E G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (co E A2 c_x) c_0) $;

--| Any positive annihilator of all the group elements is an upper bound on the group exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem gexlem2 {x3: setvar} (c_x E G N V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G V) (wcel N cn) (wral x3 (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_x) c_0) X)) (wcel E (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Any group element is annihilated by any multiple of the group exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexdvdsi (A2 c_x E G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X) (wbr E N cdvds)) (wceq (co N A2 c_x) c_0) $;

--| The only ` N ` that annihilate all the elements of the group are the multiples of the group exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexdvds {x3: setvar} (c_x E G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wbr E N cdvds) (wral x3 (wceq (co N (cv x3) c_x) c_0) X)) $;

--| An integer divides the group exponent iff it divides all the group orders. In other words, the group exponent is the LCM of the orders of all the elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexdvds2 {x3: setvar} (E G N: class) (O: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wbr E N cdvds) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) O) N cdvds) X)) $;

--| Any group element is annihilated by any multiple of the group exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexod (A2 E G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr (cfv A2 O) E cdvds) $;

--| If the order of every group element is bounded by ` N ` , the group has finite exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexcl3 {x3: setvar} (E G N: class) (O: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) O) (co c1 N cfz)) X)) (wcel E cn) $;

--| Every group element has finite order if the exponent is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexnnod (A2 E G O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel E cn) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cfv A2 O) cn) $;

--| The exponent of a finite group is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexcl2 (E G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn)) (wcel E cn) $;

--| The exponent of a finite group divides the order (cardinality) of the group. Corollary of Lagrange's theorem for the order of a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gexdvds3 (E G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn)) (wbr E (cfv X chash) cdvds) $;

--| A group or monoid has exponent 1 iff it is trivial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2016.)
theorem gex1 (E G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cgex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wb (wceq E c1) (wbr X c1o cen)) $;

--| A group is a ` P ` -group if every element has some power of ` P ` as its order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem ispgp {x3 n: setvar} (P G: class) (O: class x3 n) (X: class n):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wb (wbr P G cpgp) (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wcel G cgrp) (wral x3 (wrex n (wceq (cfv (cv x3) O) (co P (cv n) cexp)) cn0) X)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the first argument of ` pGrp ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgpprm (P G: class):
  $ wi (wbr P G cpgp) (wcel P cprime) $;

--| Reverse closure for the second argument of ` pGrp ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgpgrp (P G: class):
  $ wi (wbr P G cpgp) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| A finite group with order a power of a prime ` P ` is a ` P ` -group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgpfi1 (P G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel P cprime) (wcel N cn0)) (wi (wceq (cfv X chash) (co P N cexp)) (wbr P G cpgp)) $;

--| The identity subgroup is a ` P ` -group for every prime ` P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgp0 (P G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel P cprime)) (wbr P (co G (csn c_0) cress) cpgp) $;

--| A subgroup of a p-group is a p-group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2016.)
theorem subgpgp (P S G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr P G cpgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg))) (wbr P (co G S cress) cpgp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow1 . The p-adic valuation of the size of ` S ` is equal to the number of excess powers of ` P ` in ` ( # `` X ) / ( P ^ N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1lem1 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (P c_pl: class) (S: class s1) (G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cfv S chash) cn) (wceq (co P (cfv S chash) cpc) (co (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) N cmin))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow1 . The function ` .(+) ` is a group action on ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1lem2 {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (P c_pl: class) (c_po S: class s1) (G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X S (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co G S cga)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow1 . One of the orbits of the group action has p-adic valuation less than the prime count of the set ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1lem3 {x3 y1 z w g2 s1: setvar} (ph: wff w g2 s1) (P: class) (c_pl: class g2) (c_po: class s1) (c_sm: class x3 y1 g2 s1) (S: class s1) (G: class w) (N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X S (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) S) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wbr (co P (cfv (cec (cv w) c_sm) chash) cpc) (co (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) N cmin) cle) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow1 . The stabilizer subgroup of any element of ` S ` is at most ` P ^ N ` in size. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1lem4 {x3 y1 z u g2 s1: setvar} (ph: wff g2 s1) (B2 P: class) (c_pl: class g2) (c_po: class s1) (c_sm: class x3 y1 u g2 s1) (S: class s1) (G: class) (H: class z u s1) (N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X S (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) S) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) B2 c_po) B2) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv H chash) (co P N cexp) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow1 . Using Lagrange's theorem and the orbit-stabilizer theorem, show that there is a subgroup with size exactly ` P ^ N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1lem5 {x3 y1 z u g2 h s1: setvar} (ph: wff g2 h s1) (B2: class h) (P: class) (c_pl: class g2 h) (c_po: class h s1) (c_sm: class x3 y1 u g2 h s1) (S: class h s1) (G: class) (H: class z u s1) (N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab s1 (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X S (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) S) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) B2 c_po) B2) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P (cfv (cec B2 c_sm) chash) cpc) (co (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) N cmin) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex h (wceq (cfv (cv h) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Sylow's first theorem. If ` P ^ N ` is a prime power that divides the cardinality of ` G ` , then ` G ` has a supgroup with size ` P ^ N ` . This is part of Metamath 100 proof #72. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow1 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (P G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P N cexp) (cfv X chash) cdvds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wceq (cfv (cv g2) chash) (co P N cexp)) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Cauchy's theorem for the order of an element in a group. A finite group whose order divides a prime ` P ` contains an element of order ` P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem odcau {g2: setvar} (P G: class) (O: class g2) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv G cod) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn) (wcel P cprime)) (wbr P (cfv X chash) cdvds)) (wrex g2 (wceq (cfv (cv g2) O) P) X) $;

--| The converse to ~ pgpfi1 . A finite group is a ` P ` -group iff it has size some power of ` P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgpfi {n: setvar} (P G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn)) (wb (wbr P G cpgp) (wa (wcel P cprime) (wrex n (wceq (cfv X chash) (co P (cv n) cexp)) cn0))) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ pgpfi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2016.)
theorem pgpfi2 (P G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn)) (wb (wbr P G cpgp) (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (cfv X chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)))) $;

--| The order of a p-group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2016.)
theorem pgphash (P G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr P G cpgp) (wcel X cfn)) (wceq (cfv X chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)) $;

--| The property of being a Sylow subgroup. A Sylow ` P ` -subgroup is a ` P ` -group which has no proper supersets that are also ` P ` -groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem isslw {k: setvar} (P G H: class):
  $ wb (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) (wral k (wb (wa (wss H (cv k)) (wbr P (co G (cv k) cress) cpgp)) (wceq H (cv k))) (cfv G csubg))) $;

--| Reverse closure for the first argument of a Sylow ` P ` -subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2015.)
theorem slwprm (P G H: class):
  $ wi (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wcel P cprime) $;

--| A Sylow ` P ` -subgroup is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwsubg (P G H: class):
  $ wi (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Defining property of a Sylow ` P ` -subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwispgp (P S G H K: class):
  $ wceq S (co G K cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wcel K (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wa (wss H K) (wbr P S cpgp)) (wceq H K)) $;

--| A proper superset of a Sylow subgroup is not a ` P ` -group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwpss (P S G H K: class):
  $ wceq S (co G K cress) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wcel K (cfv G csubg)) (wpss H K)) (wn (wbr P S cpgp)) $;

--| A Sylow ` P ` -subgroup is a ` P ` -group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwpgp (P S G H: class):
  $ wceq S (co G H cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wbr P S cpgp) $;

--| Every ` P ` -subgroup is contained in a Sylow ` P ` -subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem pgpssslw {x3 y1 k: setvar} (P S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G H: class) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (co G H cress) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crab y1 (wa (wbr P (co G (cv y1) cress) cpgp) (wss H (cv y1))) (cfv G csubg)) (cfv (cv x3) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X cfn) (wbr P S cpgp)) (wrex k (wss H (cv k)) (co P G cslw)) $;

--| Every finite group contains a Sylow ` P ` -subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwn0 (P G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn) (wcel P cprime)) (wne (co P G cslw) c0) $;

--| A Sylow subgroup that is contained in a larger subgroup is also Sylow with respect to the subgroup. (The converse need not be true.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subgslw (P S G H K: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel K (co P G cslw)) (wss K S)) (wcel K (co P H cslw)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow2a . An equivalence class of fixed points is a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2alem1 {x3 y1 u g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 u g2) (A2: class) (P: class x3 y1 u g2 h) (c_po: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 u g2) (G: class u h) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1 u g2 h):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P G cpgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cfn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab u (wral h (wceq (co (cv h) (cv u) c_po) (cv u)) X) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 Z)) (wceq (cec A2 c_sm) (csn A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow2a . All the orbits which are not for fixed points have size ` | G | / | G x | ` (where ` G x ` is the stabilizer subgroup) and thus are powers of ` P ` . And since they are all nontrivial (because any orbit which is a singleton is a fixed point), they all divide ` P ` , and so does the sum of all of them. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2alem2 {x3 y1 z u g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 u g2) (P: class x3 y1 u g2 h) (c_po: class z) (c_sm: class x3 y1 u g2) (G: class z u h) (X: class z) (Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1 u g2 h):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P G cpgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cfn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab u (wral h (wceq (co (cv h) (cv u) c_po) (cv u)) X) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P (csu z (cdif (cqs Y c_sm) (cpw Z)) (cfv (cv z) chash)) cdvds) $;

--| A named lemma of Sylow's second and third theorems. If ` G ` is a finite ` P ` -group that acts on the finite set ` Y ` , then the set ` Z ` of all points of ` Y ` fixed by every element of ` G ` has cardinality equivalent to the cardinality of ` Y ` , ` mod P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2a {x3 y1 u g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 u g2) (P: class x3 y1 u g2 h) (c_po: class) (c_sm: class x3 y1 u g2) (G: class u h) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1 u g2 h):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co G Y cga)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P G cpgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cfn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab u (wral h (wceq (co (cv h) (cv u) c_po) (cv u)) X) Y) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wss (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) Y) (wrex g2 (wceq (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_po) (cv y1)) X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P (co (cfv Y chash) (cfv Z chash) cmin) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow2b . Evaluate the group action on a left coset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2blem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (B2 C c_pl c_sm c_x G H K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G K cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo x3 y1 H (cqs X c_sm) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel B2 H) (wcel C X)) (wceq (co B2 (cec C c_sm) c_x) (cec (co B2 C c_pl) c_sm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow2b . Left multiplication in a subgroup ` H ` is a group action on the set of all left cosets of ` K ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2blem2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (c_pl c_sm c_x G H K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G K cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo x3 y1 H (cqs X c_sm) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_x (co (co G H cress) (cqs X c_sm) cga)) $;

--| Sylow's second theorem. Putting together the results of ~ sylow2a and the orbit-stabilizer theorem to show that ` P ` does not divide the set of all fixed points under the group action, we get that there is a fixed point of the group action, so that there is some ` g e. X ` with ` h g K = g K ` for all ` h e. H ` . This implies that ` invg ( g ) h g e. K ` , so ` h ` is in the conjugated subgroup ` g K invg ( g ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2blem3 {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (P: class x3 y1 z g2) (c_pl c_sm c_x G H K: class) (c_mi: class y1 g2) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G K cqg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cmpo x3 y1 H (cqs X c_sm) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P (co G H cress) cpgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wss H (crn (cmpt x3 K (co (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv g2) c_mi)))) X) $;

--| Sylow's second theorem. Any ` P ` -group ` H ` is a subgroup of a conjugated ` P ` -group ` K ` of order ` P ^ n || ( # `` X ) ` with ` n ` maximal. This is usually stated under the assumption that ` K ` is a Sylow subgroup, but we use a slightly different definition, whose equivalence to this one requires this theorem. This is part of Metamath 100 proof #72. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2b {x3 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (P: class x3 g2) (c_pl G H K: class) (c_mi: class g2) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P (co G H cress) cpgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wss H (crn (cmpt x3 K (co (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv g2) c_mi)))) X) $;

--| A sylow subgroup has cardinality equal to the maximum power of ` P ` dividing the group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem slwhash (ph: wff) (P G H X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv H chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)) $;

--| The sylow subgroups of a finite group are exactly the groups which have cardinality equal to the maximum power of ` P ` dividing the group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem fislw (P G H X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel X cfn) (wcel P cprime)) (wb (wcel H (co P G cslw)) (wa (wcel H (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (cfv H chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp)))) $;

--| Sylow's second theorem. See also ~ sylow2b for the "hard" part of the proof. Any two Sylow ` P ` -subgroups are conjugate to one another, and hence the same size, namely ` P ^ ( P pCnt | X | ) ` (see ~ fislw ). This is part of Metamath 100 proof #72. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow2 {x3 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (P: class x3 g2) (c_pl G H K: class) (c_mi: class g2) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wceq H (crn (cmpt x3 K (co (co (cv g2) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv g2) c_mi)))) X) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , first part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co G (co P G cslw) cga)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , first part. The stabilizer of a given Sylow subgroup ` K ` in the group action ` .(+) ` acting on all of ` G ` is the normalizer N_G(K). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem2 {x3 y1 z u: setvar} (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po G: class) (H: class z u) (K c_mi: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) K c_po) K) X) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) K) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) K)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H N) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , first part. The number of Sylow subgroups is the same as the index (number of cosets) of the normalizer of the Sylow subgroup ` K ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem3 {x3 y1 z u: setvar} (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po G: class) (H: class z u) (K c_mi: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) K c_po) K) X) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) K) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) K)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co P G cslw) chash) (cfv (cqs X (co G N cqg)) chash)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , first part. The number of Sylow subgroups is a divisor of the size of ` G ` reduced by the size of a Sylow subgroup of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem4 {x3 y1 z u: setvar} (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po G: class) (H: class z u) (K c_mi: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 X (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab u (wceq (co (cv u) K c_po) K) X) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) K) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) K)) X) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co P G cslw) chash) (co (cfv X chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp) cdiv) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , second part. Reduce the group action of ~ sylow3lem1 to a given Sylow subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem5 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po G K c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 K (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_po (co (co G K cress) (co P G cslw) cga)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sylow3 , second part. Using the lemma ~ sylow2a , show that the number of sylow subgroups is equivalent ` mod P ` to the number of fixed points under the group action. But ` K ` is the unique element of the set of Sylow subgroups that is fixed under the group action, so there is exactly one fixed point and so ` ( ( # `` ( P pSyl G ) ) mod P ) = 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3lem6 {x3 y1 z s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (c_pl: class s1) (c_po G K: class) (c_mi: class s1) (N: class x3 y1 s1) (X: class s1):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co P G cslw)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cmpo x3 y1 K (co P G cslw) (crn (cmpt z (cv y1) (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (cv x3) c_mi)))) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv s1)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl) (cv s1))) X) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P G cslw) chash) P cmo) c1) $;

--| Sylow's third theorem. The number of Sylow subgroups is a divisor of ` | G | / d ` , where ` d ` is the common order of a Sylow subgroup, and is equivalent to ` 1 mod P ` . This is part of Metamath 100 proof #72. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem sylow3 (ph: wff) (P G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv (co P G cslw) chash) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr N (co (cfv X chash) (co P (co P (cfv X chash) cpc) cexp) cdiv) cdvds) (wceq (co N P cmo) c1)) $;

--| Extend class notation with subgroup sum.
term clsm: class;

--| Extend class notation with left projection.
term cpj1: class;

--| Define subgroup sum (inner direct product of subgroups). (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2014.)
axiom df_lsm {x3 y1 w u t: setvar}:
  $ wceq clsm (cmpt w cvv (cmpo t u (cpw (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cpw (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cv t) (cv u) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cplusg)))))) $;

--| Define the left projection function, which takes two subgroups ` t , u ` with trivial intersection and returns a function mapping the elements of the subgroup sum ` t + u ` to their projections onto ` t ` . (The other projection function can be obtained by swapping the roles of ` t ` and ` u ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_pj1 {x3 y1 z w u t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpj1 (cmpt w cvv (cmpo t u (cpw (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cpw (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cmpt z (co (cv t) (cv u) (cfv (cv w) clsm)) (crio x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cplusg))) (cv u)) (cv t))))) $;

--| The subgroup sum function (for a group or vector space). (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmfval {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_po: class x3 y1 u t) (G: class) (V: class x3 y1 u t):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wceq c_po (cmpo t u (cpw B2) (cpw B2) (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cv t) (cv u) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl))))) $;

--| Subspace sum value (for a group or vector space). Extended domain version of ~ lsmval . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmvalx {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_po: class x3 y1) (T U G: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wceq (co T U c_po) (crn (cmpo x3 y1 T U (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)))) $;

--| Subspace sum membership (for a group or vector space). Extended domain version of ~ lsmelval . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelvalx {y1 z: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_po: class y1 z) (T U G: class) (V: class y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wb (wcel X (co T U c_po)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq X (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl)) U) T)) $;

--| Subspace sum membership (for a group or vector space). (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelvalix (B2 c_pl c_po T U G V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wa (wcel X T) (wcel Y U))) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| The subspace sum operation in the opposite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Mar-2024.)
theorem oppglsm (c_po T U G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq (co T U (cfv O clsm)) (co U T c_po) $;

--| Subgroup sum is a subset of the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmssv (B2 c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wss (co T U c_po) B2) $;

--| Subset implies subgroup sum subset (extended domain version). (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmless1x (B2 c_po R T U G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wss R T)) (wss (co R U c_po) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subset implies subgroup sum subset (extended domain version). (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmless2x (B2 c_po R T U G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G V) (wss R B2) (wss U B2)) (wss T U)) (wss (co R T c_po) (co R U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is an upper bound of its arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmub1x (B2 c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss T B2) (wcel U (cfv G csubmnd))) (wss T (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is an upper bound of its arguments. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmub2x (B2 c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubmnd)) (wss U B2)) (wss U (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum value (for a left module or left vector space). (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class) (c_po: class x3 y1) (T U G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wceq (co T U c_po) (crn (cmpo x3 y1 T U (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)))) $;

--| Subgroup sum membership (for a left module or left vector space). (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelval {y1 z: setvar} (c_pl: class) (c_po: class y1 z) (T U G X: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wcel X (co T U c_po)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq X (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl)) U) T)) $;

--| Subgroup sum membership (for a left module or left vector space). (Contributed by NM, 4-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelvali (c_pl c_po T U G X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wa (wcel X T) (wcel Y U))) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum membership analogue of ~ lsmelval using vector subtraction. TODO: any way to shorten proof? (Contributed by NM, 16-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelvalm {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_po: class y1 z) (T U G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X (co T U c_po)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wceq X (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_mi)) U) T)) $;

--| Membership of vector subtraction in subgroup sum. (Contributed by NM, 27-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmelvalmi (ph: wff) (c_po T U G c_mi X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_mi) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| The sum of two commuting submonoids is a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmsubm (c_po T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubmnd)) (wcel U (cfv G csubmnd)) (wss T (cfv U Z))) (wcel (co T U c_po) (cfv G csubmnd)) $;

--| The sum of two commuting subgroups is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmsubg (c_po T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss T (cfv U Z))) (wcel (co T U c_po) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| Subgroup sum commutes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmcom2 (c_po T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss T (cfv U Z))) (wceq (co T U c_po) (co U T c_po)) $;

--| The direct product is idempotent for submonoids. (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2023.)
theorem smndlsmidm (c_po U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv G csubmnd)) (wceq (co U U c_po) U) $;

--| Subgroup sum is an upper bound of its arguments. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmub1 (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wss T (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is an upper bound of its arguments. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmub2 (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wss U (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Union of subgroups is a subset of subgroup sum. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2014.)
theorem lsmunss (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wss (cun T U) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subset implies subgroup sum subset. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmless1 (c_po S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss S T)) (wss (co S U c_po) (co T U c_po)) $;

--| Subset implies subgroup sum subset. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmless2 (c_po S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss T U)) (wss (co S T c_po) (co S U c_po)) $;

--| Subset implies subgroup sum subset. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmless12 (c_po R S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wa (wss R S) (wss T U))) (wss (co R T c_po) (co S U c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is idempotent. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Dec-2023.)
theorem lsmidm (c_po U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (co U U c_po) U) $;

--| The least upper bound property of subgroup sum. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2014.)
theorem lsmlub (c_po S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wa (wss S U) (wss T U)) (wss (co S T c_po) U)) $;

--| Subgroup sum with a subset. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmss1 (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss T U)) (wceq (co T U c_po) U) $;

--| Subgroup sum with a subset. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmss1b (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wss T U) (wceq (co T U c_po) U)) $;

--| Subgroup sum with a subset. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmss2 (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) (wss U T)) (wceq (co T U c_po) T) $;

--| Subgroup sum with a subset. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmss2b (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wb (wss U T) (wceq (co T U c_po) T)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is associative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmass (c_po R T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R (cfv G csubg)) (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wceq (co (co R T c_po) U c_po) (co R (co T U c_po) c_po)) $;

--| Subgroup sum is idempotent for monoids. This corresponds to the observation in [Lang] p. 6. (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2023.)
theorem mndlsmidm (B2 c_po G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cmnd) (wceq (co B2 B2 c_po) B2) $;

--| Subgroup sum with the zero subgroup. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsm01 (c_po G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (co X (csn c_0) c_po) X) $;

--| Subgroup sum with the zero subgroup. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsm02 (c_po G X c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv G csubg)) (wceq (co (csn c_0) X c_po) X) $;

--| The subgroup sum evaluated within a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2016.)
theorem subglsm (A2 c_po S T U G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv H clsm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wss T S) (wss U S)) (wceq (co T U c_po) (co T U A2)) $;

--| Equivalent expressions for "not less than". ( ~ chnlei analog.) (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lssnle (ph: wff) (c_po T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn (wss U T)) (wpss T (co T U c_po))) $;

--| The modular law holds for subgroup sum. Similar to part of Theorem 16.9 of [MaedaMaeda] p. 70. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmmod (c_po S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wss S U)) (wceq (co S (cin T U) c_po) (cin (co S T c_po) U)) $;

--| Modular law dual for subgroup sum. Similar to part of Theorem 16.9 of [MaedaMaeda] p. 70. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmmod2 (c_po S T U G: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel U (cfv G csubg))) (wss U S)) (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (co (cin S T) U c_po)) $;

--| If two structures have the same components (properties), they have the same subspace structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Apr-2024.)
theorem lsmpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K clsm) (cfv L clsm)) $;

--| Commute the "subgroups commute" predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem cntzrecd (ph: wff) (T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (cfv T Z)) $;

--| The "subgroups commute" predicate applied to a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmcntz (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (co S T c_po) (cfv U Z)) (wa (wss S (cfv U Z)) (wss T (cfv U Z)))) $;

--| The "subgroups commute" predicate applied to a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmcntzr (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss S (cfv (co T U c_po) Z)) (wa (wss S (cfv T Z)) (wss S (cfv U Z)))) $;

--| Disjointness from a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cin S U) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0))) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj2 (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T (co S U c_po)) (csn c_0)) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj3 (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cfv T Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) $;

--| Disjointness from a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisjr (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S U) (csn c_0))) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj2r (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co S U c_po) T) (csn c_0)) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj3r (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj2a (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0))) (wa (wceq (cin T (co S U c_po)) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S U) (csn c_0)))) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj2b (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wceq (cin (co S U c_po) T) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S U) (csn c_0))) (wa (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)))) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj3a (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cfv T Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0))) (wa (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)))) $;

--| Association of the disjointness constraint in a subgroup sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmdisj3b (ph: wff) (c_po S T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wceq (cin (co S T c_po) U) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin S T) (csn c_0))) (wa (wceq (cin S (co T U c_po)) (csn c_0)) (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)))) $;

--| Vectors belonging to disjoint commuting subgroups are uniquely determined by their sum. (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem subgdisj1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| Vectors belonging to disjoint commuting subgroups are uniquely determined by their sum. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem subgdisj2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 D) $;

--| Vectors belonging to disjoint commuting subgroups are uniquely determined by their sum. Analogous to ~ opth , this theorem shows a way of representing a pair of vectors. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem subgdisjb (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| The left projection function (for a direct product of group subspaces). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem pj1fval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (c_po T U G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wceq (co T U P) (cmpt z (co T U c_po) (crio x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) U) T))) $;

--| The left projection function (for a direct product of group subspaces). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem pj1val {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (P c_pl: class x3 y1) (c_po T U G V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel G V) (wss T B2) (wss U B2)) (wcel X (co T U c_po))) (wceq (cfv X (co T U P)) (crio x3 (wrex y1 (wceq X (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) U) T)) $;

--| Uniqueness of a left projection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj1eu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl c_po T U G X: class) (c_0 Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co T U c_po))) (wreu x3 (wrex y1 (wceq X (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl)) U) T) $;

--| The left projection function maps a direct subspace sum onto the left factor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj1f (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co T U c_po) T (co T U P)) $;

--| The right projection function maps a direct subspace sum onto the right factor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj2f (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co T U c_po) U (co U T P)) $;

--| Any element of a direct subspace sum can be decomposed into projections onto the left and right factors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem pj1id (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co T U c_po))) (wceq X (co (cfv X (co T U P)) (cfv X (co U T P)) c_pl)) $;

--| Any element of a direct subspace sum can be decomposed uniquely into projections onto the left and right factors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj1eq (ph: wff) (B2 C P c_pl c_po T U G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co T U c_po)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq X (co B2 C c_pl)) (wa (wceq (cfv X (co T U P)) B2) (wceq (cfv X (co U T P)) C))) $;

--| The left projection function is the identity on the left subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj1lid (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X T)) (wceq (cfv X (co T U P)) X) $;

--| The left projection function is the zero operator on the right subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem pj1rid (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X U)) (wceq (cfv X (co T U P)) c_0) $;

--| The left projection function is a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem pj1ghm (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co T U P) (co (co G (co T U c_po) cress) G cghm)) $;

--| The left projection function is a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem pj1ghm2 (ph: wff) (P c_pl c_po T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv G cpj1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co T U P) (co (co G (co T U c_po) cress) (co G T cress) cghm)) $;

--| The order of the direct product of groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem lsmhash (ph: wff) (c_po T U G c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq c_po (cfv G clsm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv G ccntz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv G csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin T U) (csn c_0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (cfv U Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co T U c_po) chash) (co (cfv T chash) (cfv U chash) cmul)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the free group equivalence relation.
term cefg: class;

--| Extend class notation with the free group construction.
term cfrgp: class;

--| Extend class notation with free group injection.
term cvrgp: class;

--| Define the free group equivalence relation, which is the smallest equivalence relation ` ~~ ` such that for any words ` A , B ` and formal symbol ` x ` with inverse ` invg x ` , ` A B ~~ A x ( invg x ) B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_efg {x3 y1 z i n r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cefg (cmpt i cvv (cint (cab r (wa (wer (cword (cxp (cv i) c2o)) (cv r)) (wral x3 (wral n (wral y1 (wral z (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv x3) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z))))) csplice) (cv r)) c2o) (cv i)) (co cc0 (cfv (cv x3) chash) cfz)) (cword (cxp (cv i) c2o))))))) $;

--| Define the free group on a set ` I ` of generators, defined as the quotient of the free monoid on ` I X. 2o ` (representing the generator elements and their formal inverses) by the free group equivalence relation ~ df-efg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_frgp {i: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfrgp (cmpt i cvv (co (cfv (cxp (cv i) c2o) cfrmd) (cfv (cv i) cefg) cqus)) $;

--| Define the canonical injection from the generating set ` I ` into the base set of the free group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_vrgp {i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvrgp (cmpt i cvv (cmpt j (cv i) (cec (cs1 (cop (cv j) c0)) (cfv (cv i) cefg)))) $;

--| Value of the formal inverse operation for the generating set of a free group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgmval {y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class y1 z) (I: class) (M: class y1 z):
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 I) (wcel B2 c2o)) (wceq (co A2 B2 M) (cop A2 (cdif c1o B2))) $;

--| The formal inverse operation is an endofunction on the generating set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgmf {y1 z: setvar} (I: class) (M: class y1 z):
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wf (cxp I c2o) (cxp I c2o) M $;

--| The inversion function on the generators is an involution. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgmnvl {y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1 z) (I: class) (M: class y1 z):
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cxp I c2o)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 M) M) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efgval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem efgrcl (A2 I W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 W) (wa (wcel I cvv) (wceq W (cword (cxp I c2o)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efgval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efglem {x3 y1 z n r: setvar} (I W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wex r (wa (wer W (cv r)) (wral x3 (wral n (wral y1 (wral z (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv x3) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z))))) csplice) (cv r)) c2o) I) (co cc0 (cfv (cv x3) chash) cfz)) W)) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgval {x3 y1 z n r: setvar} (c_sm: class n) (I W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cint (cab r (wa (wer W (cv r)) (wral x3 (wral n (wral y1 (wral z (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv x3) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z))))) csplice) (cv r)) c2o) I) (co cc0 (cfv (cv x3) chash) cfz)) W)))) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem efger (c_sm I W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wer W c_sm $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem efgi (A2 c_sm I J K N W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 W) (wcel N (co cc0 (cfv A2 chash) cfz))) (wa (wcel J I) (wcel K c2o))) (wbr A2 (co A2 (cotp N N (cs2 (cop J K) (cop J (cdif c1o K)))) csplice) c_sm) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem efgi0 (A2 c_sm I J N W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 W) (wcel N (co cc0 (cfv A2 chash) cfz)) (wcel J I)) (wbr A2 (co A2 (cotp N N (cs2 (cop J c0) (cop J c1o))) csplice) c_sm) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2016.)
theorem efgi1 (A2 c_sm I J N W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 W) (wcel N (co cc0 (cfv A2 chash) cfz)) (wcel J I)) (wbr A2 (co A2 (cotp N N (cs2 (cop J c1o) (cop J c0))) csplice) c_sm) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgtf {y1 z w v n a b: setvar} (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class) (X: class y1 z w v n):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X W) (wa (wceq (cfv X T) (cmpo a b (co cc0 (cfv X chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co X (cotp (cv a) (cv a) (cs2 (cv b) (cfv (cv b) M))) csplice))) (wf (cxp (co cc0 (cfv X chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o)) W (cfv X T))) $;

--| Value of the extension function, which maps a word (a representation of the group element as a sequence of elements and their inverses) to its direct extensions, defined as the original representation with an element and its inverse inserted somewhere in the string. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgtval {y1 z w v n: setvar} (A2: class y1 z w v n) (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (N: class y1 z w v n) (W: class) (X: class y1 z w v n):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X W) (wcel N (co cc0 (cfv X chash) cfz)) (wcel A2 (cxp I c2o))) (wceq (co N A2 (cfv X T)) (co X (cotp N N (cs2 A2 (cfv A2 M))) csplice)) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgval2 {x3 y1 z w v n r: setvar} (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cint (cab r (wa (wer W (cv r)) (wral x3 (wss (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)) (cec (cv x3) (cv r))) W)))) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgi2 {y1 z w v n: setvar} (A2 B2: class y1 z w v n) (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 W) (wcel B2 (crn (cfv A2 T)))) (wbr A2 B2 c_sm) $;

--| Value of the free group construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgtlen {y1 z w v n: setvar} (A2: class y1 z w v n) (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class) (X: class y1 z w v n):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X W) (wcel A2 (crn (cfv X T)))) (wceq (cfv A2 chash) (co (cfv X chash) c2 caddc)) $;

--| The inverse of the reverse of a word composed with the word relates to the identity. (This provides an explicit expression for the representation of the group inverse, given a representative of the free group equivalence class.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efginvrel2 {y1 z w v n: setvar} (A2: class y1 z w v n) (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 W) (wbr (co A2 (ccom M (cfv A2 creverse)) cconcat) c0 c_sm) $;

--| The inverse of the reverse of a word composed with the word relates to the identity. (This provides an explicit expression for the representation of the group inverse, given a representative of the free group equivalence class.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efginvrel1 {y1 z w v n: setvar} (A2: class y1 z w v n) (c_sm: class w v n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class) (M: class y1 z) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 W) (wbr (co (ccom M (cfv A2 creverse)) A2 cconcat) c0 c_sm) $;

--| Value of the auxiliary function ` S ` defining a sequence of extensions starting at some irreducible word. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgsf {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wf (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) W S $;

--| Elementhood in the domain of ` S ` , the set of sequences of extensions starting at an irreducible word. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgsdm {x3 y1 z w v t i k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (cdm S)) (w3a (wcel F (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (wcel (cfv cc0 F) D) (wral i (wcel (cfv (cv i) F) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 cmin) F) T))) (co c1 (cfv F chash) cfzo))) $;

--| Value of the auxiliary function ` S ` defining a sequence of extensions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgsval {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm S)) (wceq (cfv F S) (cfv (co (cfv F chash) c1 cmin) F)) $;

--| Property of the last link in the chain of extensions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgsdmi {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm S)) (wcel (co (cfv F chash) c1 cmin) cn)) (wcel (cfv F S) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (co (cfv F chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) F) T))) $;

--| Value of the auxiliary function ` S ` defining a sequence of extensions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgsval2 {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cword W)) (wcel B2 W) (wcel (co A2 (cs1 B2) cconcat) (cdm S))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (cs1 B2) cconcat) S) B2) $;

--| The start and end of any extension sequence are related (i.e. evaluate to the same element of the quotient group to be created). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgsrel {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm S)) (wbr (cfv cc0 F) (cfv F S) c_sm) $;

--| A singleton of an irreducible word is an extension sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgs1 {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 D) (wcel (cs1 A2) (cdm S)) $;

--| Every extension sequence ending in an irreducible word is trivial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgs1b {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm S)) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 S) D) (wceq (cfv A2 chash) c1)) $;

--| If ` F ` is an extension sequence and ` A ` is an extension of the last element of ` F ` , then ` F + <" A "> ` is an extension sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgsp1 {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm S)) (wcel A2 (crn (cfv (cfv F S) T)))) (wcel (co F (cs1 A2) cconcat) (cdm S)) $;

--| An initial segment of an extension sequence is an extension sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Nov-2022.)
theorem efgsres {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (F: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (N: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm S)) (wcel N (co c1 (cfv F chash) cfz))) (wcel (cres F (co cc0 N cfzo)) (cdm S)) $;

--| For any word, there is a sequence of extensions starting at a reduced word and ending at the target word, such that each word in the chain is an extension of the previous (inserting an element and its inverse at adjacent indices somewhere in the sequence). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem efgsfo {x3 y1 z w v t k m n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wfo (cdm S) W S $;

--| The reduced word that forms the base of the sequence in ~ efgsval is uniquely determined, given the ending representation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem efgredlema {x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral b (wi (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv a) S) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 S) chash) clt) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) S) (cfv (cv b) S)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv a)) (cfv cc0 (cv b))))) (cdm S)) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) cn) (wcel (co (cfv B2 chash) c1 cmin) cn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efgredleme . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2016.)
theorem efgredlemf {x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (I: class k) (K L: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (M: class y1 z k) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral b (wi (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv a) S) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 S) chash) clt) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) S) (cfv (cv b) S)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv a)) (cfv cc0 (cv b))))) (cdm S)) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (co (cfv B2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cfv K A2) W) (wcel (cfv L B2) W)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efgred . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2016.)
theorem efgredlemg {x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (P Q: class x3 k m a b) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (U: class x3 t k m a b) (I: class k) (K L: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (M: class y1 z k) (V: class x3 t k m a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral b (wi (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv a) S) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 S) chash) clt) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) S) (cfv (cv b) S)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv a)) (cfv cc0 (cv b))))) (cdm S)) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (co (cfv B2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co cc0 (cfv (cfv K A2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (co cc0 (cfv (cfv L B2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (co P U (cfv (cfv K A2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 S) (co Q V (cfv (cfv L B2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv K A2) chash) (cfv (cfv L B2) chash)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efgred . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Oct-2022.)
theorem efgredleme {x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (C: class) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (P Q: class x3 k m a b) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (U: class x3 t k m a b) (I: class k) (K L: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (M: class y1 z k) (V: class x3 t k m a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral b (wi (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv a) S) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 S) chash) clt) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) S) (cfv (cv b) S)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv a)) (cfv cc0 (cv b))))) (cdm S)) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (co (cfv B2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co cc0 (cfv (cfv K A2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (co cc0 (cfv (cfv L B2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (co P U (cfv (cfv K A2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 S) (co Q V (cfv (cfv L B2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv K A2) (cfv L B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv (co Q c2 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C S) (co (co (cfv L B2) Q cpfx) (co (cfv K A2) (cop (co Q c2 caddc) (cfv (cfv K A2) chash)) csubstr) cconcat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cfv K A2) (crn (cfv (cfv C S) T))) (wcel (cfv L B2) (crn (cfv (cfv C S) T)))) $;

--| The reduced word that forms the base of the sequence in ~ efgsval is uniquely determined, given the ending representation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Oct-2022.)
theorem efgredlemd {x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w v t k m n a b) (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (C: class) (D: class x3 y1 z w v k n) (P Q: class x3 k m a b) (c_sm: class w v k n) (S: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (T: class y1 z w v n) (U: class x3 t k m a b) (I: class k) (K L: class x3 y1 z w v t k m n) (M: class y1 z k) (V: class x3 t k m a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv (cword (cxp I c2o)) cid) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv I cefg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo y1 z I c2o (cop (cv y1) (cdif c1o (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt v W (cmpo n w (co cc0 (cfv (cv v) chash) cfz) (cxp I c2o) (co (cv v) (cotp (cv n) (cv n) (cs2 (cv w) (cfv (cv w) M))) csplice))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif W (ciun x3 W (crn (cfv (cv x3) T)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m (crab t (wa (wcel (cfv cc0 (cv t)) D) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv t)) (crn (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv t)) T))) (co c1 (cfv (cv t) chash) cfzo))) (cdif (cword W) (csn c0))) (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) chash) c1 cmin) (cv m))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral a (wral b (wi (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv a) S) chash) (cfv (cfv A2 S) chash) clt) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) S) (cfv (cv b) S)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv a)) (cfv cc0 (cv b))))) (cdm S)) (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (co (cfv B2 chash) c1 cmin) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co cc0 (cfv (cfv K A2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (co cc0 (cfv (cfv L B2) chash) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cxp I c2o)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 S) (co P U (cfv (cfv K A2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 S) (co Q V (cfv (cfv L B2) T))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq (cfv K A2) (cfv L B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv (co Q c2 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C S) (co (co (cfv L B2) Q cpfx) (co (cfv K A2) (cop (co Q c2 caddc) (cfv (cfv K A2) chash)) csubstr) cconcat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (cfv cc0 B2)) $;

