--| The Cayley-Hamilton theorem: A matrix over a commutative ring "satisfies its own characteristic equation". This version of ~ cayleyhamilton provides definitions not used in the theorem itself, but in its proof to make it clearer, more readable and shorter compared with a proof without them (see ~ cayleyhamiltonALT )! (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2019.) (Revised by AV, 15-Dec-2019.)
theorem cayleyhamilton0 {n s1 b: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class s1 b) (P R T: class) (c_xp U c_1 E: class s1 b) (c_ex: class) (G: class s1 b) (c_as: class) (K: class n) (M c_mi N: class) (W: class s1 b) (Y: class) (c_0: class n) (Z: class s1 b):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv A2 c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv A2 cur) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv A2 cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv A2 cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq C (co N R cchpmat) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv M C) cco1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq Y (co N P cmat) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Y csg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv Y c0g) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv (cfv Y cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N R cmat2pmat) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn0 (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) (co Z (co (cfv M T) (cfv (cfv cc0 (cv b)) T) c_xp) c_mi) (cif (wceq (cv n) (co (cv s1) c1 caddc)) (cfv (cfv (cv s1) (cv b)) T) (cif (wbr (co (cv s1) c1 caddc) (cv n) clt) Z (co (cfv (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) (cv b)) T) (co (cfv M T) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv b)) T) c_xp) c_mi))))) $ >
  $ wceq U (co N R ccpmat2mat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2)) (wceq (co A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) K) (co (cv n) M c_ex) c_as)) cgsu) c_0) $;

--| The Cayley-Hamilton theorem: A matrix over a commutative ring "satisfies its own characteristic equation", see theorem 7.8 in [Roman] p. 170 (without proof!), or theorem 3.1 in [Lang] p. 561. In other words, a matrix over a commutative ring "inserted" into its characteristic polynomial results in zero. This is Metamath 100 proof #49. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-Nov-2019.)
theorem cayleyhamilton {n: setvar} (A2 B2 C R c_ex c_as: class) (K: class n) (M N: class) (c_0: class n):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv A2 c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (co N R cchpmat) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv M C) cco1) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv A2 cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv A2 cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2)) (wceq (co A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) K) (co (cv n) M c_ex) c_as)) cgsu) c_0) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ cayleyhamilton , the Cayley-Hamilton theorem. This proof does not use ~ cayleyhamilton0 directly, but has the same structure as the proof of ~ cayleyhamilton0 . In contrast to the proof of ~ cayleyhamilton0 , only the definitions required to formulate the theorem itself are used, causing the definitions used in the lemmas being expanded, which makes the proof longer and more difficult to read. (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem cayleyhamiltonALT {n: setvar} (A2 B2 C R c_ex c_as: class) (K: class n) (M N: class) (c_0: class n):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv A2 c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (co N R cchpmat) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv M C) cco1) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv A2 cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv A2 cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2)) (wceq (co A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) K) (co (cv n) M c_ex) c_as)) cgsu) c_0) $;

--| The Cayley-Hamilton theorem: A matrix over a commutative ring "satisfies its own characteristic equation", or, in other words, a matrix over a commutative ring "inserted" into its characteristic polynomial results in zero. In this variant of ~ cayleyhamilton , the meaning of "inserted" is made more transparent: If the characteristic polynomial is a polynomial with coefficients ` ( F `` n ) ` , then a matrix over a commutative ring "inserted" into its characteristic polynomial is the sum of these coefficients multiplied with the corresponding power of the matrix. (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2019.)
theorem cayleyhamilton1 {n: setvar} (A2 B2 C P R c_x E c_ex F c_as: class) (K: class n) (L M N X: class) (c_0: class n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv A2 c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (co N R cchpmat) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv M C) cco1) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv A2 cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv A2 cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv (cfv P cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel N cfn) (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2)) (wa (wcel F (co L cn0 cmap)) (wbr F Z cfsupp))) (wi (wceq (cfv M C) (co P (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cv n) X E) c_x)) cgsu)) (wceq (co A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cv n) M c_ex) c_as)) cgsu) c_0)) $;

--| Syntax for the class of topologies.
term ctop: class;

--| Define the class of topologies. It is a proper class (see ~ topnex ). See ~ istopg and ~ istop2g for the corresponding characterizations, using respectively binary intersections like in this definition and nonempty finite intersections. The final form of the definition is due to Bourbaki (Def. 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.1), while the idea of defining a topology in terms of its open sets is due to Aleksandrov. For the convoluted history of the definitions of these notions, see Gregory H. Moore, _The emergence of open sets, closed sets, and limit points in analysis and topology_, Historia Mathematica 35 (2008) 220--241. (Contributed by NM, 3-Mar-2006.) (Revised by BJ, 20-Oct-2018.)
axiom df_top {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctop (cab x3 (wa (wral y1 (wcel (cuni (cv y1)) (cv x3)) (cpw (cv x3))) (wral y1 (wral z (wcel (cin (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv x3)) (cv x3)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` J ` is a topology". See ~ istop2g for another characterization using nonempty finite intersections instead of binary intersections. Note: In the literature, a topology is often represented by a calligraphic letter T, which resembles the letter J. This confusion may have led to J being used by some authors (e.g., K. D. Joshi, _Introduction to General Topology_ (1983), p. 114) and it is convenient for us since we later use ` T ` to represent linear transformations (operators). (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 3-Mar-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem istopg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wb (wcel J ctop) (wa (wal x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) J) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) J))) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) J) J) J))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` J ` is a topology" using nonempty finite intersections instead of binary intersections as in ~ istopg . (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem istop2g {x3: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wb (wcel J ctop) (wa (wal x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) J) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) J))) (wal x3 (wi (w3a (wss (cv x3) J) (wne (cv x3) c0) (wcel (cv x3) cfn)) (wcel (cint (cv x3)) J))))) $;

--| The union of a subset of a topology (that is, the union of any family of open sets of a topology) is an open set. (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 27-Feb-2006.)
theorem uniopn (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 J)) (wcel (cuni A2) J) $;

--| The indexed union of a subset of a topology is an open set. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.)
theorem iunopn {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wcel B2 J) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) J) $;

--| The intersection of two open sets of a topology is an open set. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem inopn (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) J) $;

--| A topology is closed under finite intersections. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Oct-2009.)
theorem fitop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J cfi) J) $;

--| The intersection of a nonempty finite family of open sets is open. (Contributed by FL, 20-Apr-2012.)
theorem fiinopn (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wi (w3a (wss A2 J) (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (cint A2) J)) $;

--| The intersection of a nonempty finite family of open sets is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem iinopn {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wcel B2 J) A2))) (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) J) $;

--| The union of two open sets is open. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem unopn (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) J) $;

--| The empty set is an open subset of any topology. (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 27-Feb-2006.)
theorem _0opn (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel c0 J) $;

--| The empty set is not a topology. (Contributed by FL, 1-Jun-2008.)
theorem _0ntop:
  $ wn (wcel c0 ctop) $;

--| The underlying set of a topology is an open set. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem topopn (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel X J) $;

--| A member of a topology is a subset of its underlying set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.)
theorem eltopss (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J)) (wss A2 X) $;

--| A finite indexed relative intersection of open sets is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem riinopn {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel B2 J) A2)) (wcel (cin X (ciin x3 A2 B2)) J) $;

--| A finite relative intersection of open sets is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem rintopn (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 J) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (cin X (cint A2)) J) $;

--| Syntax for the function of topologies on sets.
term ctopon: class;

--| Define the function that associates with a set the set of topologies on it. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
axiom df_topon {j b: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctopon (cmpt b cvv (crab j (wceq (cv b) (cuni (cv j))) ctop)) $;

--| Property of being a topology with a given base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem istopon (B2 J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wceq B2 (cuni J))) $;

--| A topology on a given base set is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topontop (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The base set of a topology on a given base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem toponuni (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wceq B2 (cuni J)) $;

--| A topology on a given base set is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topontopi (B2 J: class):
  $ wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon) $ >
  $ wcel J ctop $;

--| The base set of a topology on a given base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem toponunii (B2 J: class):
  $ wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni J) $;

--| Alternative definition of ` Top ` in terms of ` TopOn ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem toptopon (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ctop) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| A topology is the same thing as a topology on the union of its open sets. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.)
theorem toptopon2 (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J ctop) (wcel J (cfv (cuni J) ctopon)) $;

--| A topology on a set is a topology on the union of its open sets. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.)
theorem topontopon (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel J (cfv (cuni J) ctopon)) $;

--| The class ` TopOn ` is a function. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Apr-2021.)
theorem funtopon:
  $ wfun ctopon $;

--| Given a topology on a set, restricting it to that same set has no effect. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Jul-2022.)
theorem toponrestid (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctopon) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co A2 B2 crest) $;

--| The set of topologies on a set is included in the double power set of that set. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Apr-2021.)
theorem toponsspwpw (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfv A2 ctopon) (cpw (cpw A2)) $;

--| The domain of ` TopOn ` is the universal class ` _V ` . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Apr-2021.)
theorem dmtopon:
  $ wceq (cdm ctopon) cvv $;

--| The class ` TopOn ` is a function with domain the universal class ` _V ` . Analogue for topologies of ~ fnmre for Moore collections. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Apr-2021.)
theorem fntopon:
  $ wfn ctopon cvv $;

--| A topology is the same thing as a topology on a set (variable-free version). (Contributed by BJ, 27-Apr-2021.)
theorem toprntopon:
  $ wceq ctop (cuni (crn ctopon)) $;

--| The base set of a topology is an open set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem toponmax (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wcel B2 J) $;

--| A member of a topology is a subset of its underlying set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem toponss (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 J)) (wss A2 X) $;

--| If ` K ` is a topology on the base set of topology ` J ` , then ` J ` is a topology on the base of ` K ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem toponcom (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K (cfv (cuni J) ctopon))) (wcel J (cfv (cuni K) ctopon)) $;

--| Biconditional form of ~ toponcom . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Dec-2021.)
theorem toponcomb (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wb (wcel J (cfv (cuni K) ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv (cuni J) ctopon))) $;

--| The topologies over the same set have the greatest element (the discrete topology) and the least element (the indiscrete topology). (Contributed by FL, 18-Apr-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem topgele (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wa (wss (cpr c0 X) J) (wss J (cpw X))) $;

--| The only topology on a singleton is the discrete topology (which is also the indiscrete topology by ~ pwsn ). (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem topsn (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv (csn A2) ctopon)) (wceq J (cpw (csn A2))) $;

--| Syntax for the class of topological spaces.
term ctps: class;

--| Define the class of topological spaces (as extensible structures). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_topsp {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctps (cab f2 (wcel (cfv (cv f2) ctopn) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cbs) ctopon))) $;

--| Express the predicate "is a topological space." (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem istps (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K ctps) (wcel J (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| Express the predicate "is a topological space." (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.)
theorem istps2 (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K ctps) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wceq A2 (cuni J))) $;

--| The base set of a topological space. (Contributed by FL, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem tpsuni (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K ctps) (wceq A2 (cuni J)) $;

--| The topology extractor on a topological space is a topology. (Contributed by FL, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem tpstop (J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K ctps) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A topological space depends only on the base and topology components. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tpspropd (ph: wff) (K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K ctps) (wcel L ctps)) $;

--| A topological space depends only on the base and topology components. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tpsprop2d (ph: wff) (K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cts) (cfv L cts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K ctps) (wcel L ctps)) $;

--| Express the predicate "is a topological space." (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem topontopn (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K cts) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv A2 ctopon)) (wceq J (cfv K ctopn)) $;

--| If the topology component is already correctly truncated, then it forms a topological space (with the topology extractor function coming out the same as the component). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tsettps (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K cts) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv A2 ctopon)) (wcel K ctps) $;

--| Properties that determine a topological space. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.)
theorem istpsi (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K ctopn) J $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni J) $ >
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| Properties that determine a topological space from a construction (using no explicit indices). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem eltpsg (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv A2 ctopon)) (wcel K ctps) $;

--| Properties that determine a topological space from a construction (using no explicit indices). (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem eltpsi (A2 J K: class):
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni J) $ >
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| Syntax for the class of topological bases.
term ctb: class;

--| Define the class of topological bases. Equivalent to definition of basis in [Munkres] p. 78 (see ~ isbasis2g ). Note that "bases" is the plural of "basis". (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
axiom df_bases {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctb (cab x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wss (cin (cv y1) (cv z)) (cuni (cin (cv x3) (cpw (cin (cv y1) (cv z)))))) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) $;

--| Express the predicate "the set ` B ` is a basis for a topology". (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem isbasisg {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wcel B2 ctb) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cin B2 (cpw (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) B2) B2)) $;

--| Express the predicate "the set ` B ` is a basis for a topology". (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem isbasis2g {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wcel B2 ctb) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wss (cv w) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)))) B2) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) B2)) $;

--| Express the predicate "the set ` B ` is a basis for a topology". Definition of basis in [Munkres] p. 78. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem isbasis3g {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wcel B2 C) (wb (wcel B2 ctb) (w3a (wral x3 (wss (cv x3) (cuni B2)) B2) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) (cuni B2)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wss (cv w) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)))) B2) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) B2))) $;

--| Property of a basis. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.)
theorem basis1 (B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel C B2) (wcel D B2)) (wss (cin C D) (cuni (cin B2 (cpw (cin C D))))) $;

--| Property of a basis. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem basis2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel C B2)) (wa (wcel D B2) (wcel A2 (cin C D)))) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) (cin C D))) B2) $;

--| If a set is closed under finite intersection, then it is a basis for a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem fiinbas {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 C) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) B2) B2)) (wcel B2 ctb) $;

--| A basis is not affected by the addition or removal of the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem basdif0 (B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cdif B2 (csn c0)) ctb) (wcel B2 ctb) $;

--| A disjoint system of sets is a basis for a topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem baspartn {x3 y1: setvar} (P: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P V) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0)) P) P)) (wcel P ctb) $;

--| The topology generated by a basis. See also ~ tgval2 and ~ tgval3 . (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem tgval {x3: setvar} (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) (cab x3 (wss (cv x3) (cuni (cin B2 (cpw (cv x3))))))) $;

--| Definition of a topology generated by a basis in [Munkres] p. 78. Later we show (in ~ tgcl ) that ` ( topGen `` B ) ` is indeed a topology (on ` U. B ` , see ~ unitg ). See also ~ tgval and ~ tgval3 . (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem tgval2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) (cab x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cuni B2)) (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cv z) (cv x3))) B2) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Membership in a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem eltg (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wss A2 (cuni (cin B2 (cpw A2))))) $;

--| Membership in a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem eltg2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wa (wss A2 (cuni B2)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wss (cv y1) A2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| Membership in a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem eltg2b {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wss (cv y1) A2)) B2) A2)) $;

--| An open set in a topology generated by a basis is the union of all basic open sets contained in it. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem eltg4i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wceq A2 (cuni (cin B2 (cpw A2)))) $;

--| The union of a set of basic open sets is in the generated topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem eltg3i (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wss A2 B2)) (wcel (cuni A2) (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| Membership in a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by NM, 15-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem eltg3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) B2) (wceq A2 (cuni (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Alternate expression for the topology generated by a basis. Lemma 2.1 of [Munkres] p. 80. See also ~ tgval and ~ tgval2 . (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgval3 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) (cab x3 (wex y1 (wa (wss (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) (cuni (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Property of a member of a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2006.)
theorem tg1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wss A2 (cuni B2)) $;

--| Property of a member of a topology generated by a basis. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2006.)
theorem tg2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv B2 ctg)) (wcel C A2)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel C (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) A2)) B2) $;

--| A member of a basis is a subset of the topology it generates. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem bastg (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wss B2 (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| The topology generated by a basis ` B ` is a topology on ` U. B ` . Importantly, this theorem means that we don't have to specify separately the base set for the topological space generated by a basis. In other words, any member of the class ` TopBases ` completely specifies the basis it corresponds to. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem unitg (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cuni (cfv B2 ctg)) (cuni B2)) $;

--| Subset relation for generated topologies. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.)
theorem tgss (B2 C V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C V) (wss B2 C)) (wss (cfv B2 ctg) (cfv C ctg)) $;

--| Show that a basis generates a topology. Remark in [Munkres] p. 79. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem tgcl (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel (cfv B2 ctg) ctop) $;

--| The property ~ tgcl can be reversed: if the topology generated by ` B ` is actually a topology, then ` B ` must be a topological basis. This yields an alternative definition of ` TopBases ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgclb (B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel (cfv B2 ctg) ctop) $;

--| A basis generates a topology on ` U. B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgtopon (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel (cfv B2 ctg) (cfv (cuni B2) ctopon)) $;

--| A topology is its own basis. (Contributed by NM, 17-Jul-2006.)
theorem topbas (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel J ctb) $;

--| A topology is its own basis. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.)
theorem tgtop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J ctg) J) $;

--| Membership in a topology, expressed without quantifiers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem eltop (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wss A2 (cuni (cin J (cpw A2))))) $;

--| Membership in a topology. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem eltop2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wss (cv y1) A2)) J) A2)) $;

--| Membership in a topology. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem eltop3 {x3: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) J) (wceq A2 (cuni (cv x3)))))) $;

--| A collection of finite intersections is a basis. The initial set is a subbasis for the topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Nov-2013.)
theorem fibas (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cfi) ctb $;

--| A space has no more open sets than subsets of a basis. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem tgdom (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wbr (cfv B2 ctg) (cpw B2) cdom) $;

--| The indexed union of a set of basic open sets is in the generated topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgiun {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wral x3 (wcel C B2) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 C) (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| The topology generator function is idempotent. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgidm (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv (cfv B2 ctg) ctg) (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| Two ways to express that a basis is a topology. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.)
theorem bastop (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 ctb) (wb (wcel B2 ctop) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) B2)) $;

--| The topology generation function is one-to-one when applied to completed topologies. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2006.)
theorem tgtop11 (J K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop) (wceq (cfv J ctg) (cfv K ctg))) (wceq J K) $;

--| The singleton of the empty set is the only topology possible for an empty underlying set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2006.)
theorem _0top (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wceq (cuni J) c0) (wceq J (csn c0))) $;

--| ` { (/) } ` is the only topology with one element. (Contributed by FL, 18-Aug-2008.)
theorem en1top (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wbr J c1o cen) (wceq J (csn c0))) $;

--| If a topology has two elements, it is the indiscrete topology. (Contributed by FL, 11-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem en2top (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wbr J c2o cen) (wa (wceq J (cpr c0 X)) (wne X c0))) $;

--| A criterion for determining whether one topology is finer than another. Lemma 2.2 of [Munkres] p. 80 using abbreviations. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgss3 (B2 C V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel C W)) (wb (wss (cfv B2 ctg) (cfv C ctg)) (wss B2 (cfv C ctg))) $;

--| A criterion for determining whether one topology is finer than another, based on a comparison of their bases. Lemma 2.2 of [Munkres] p. 80. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgss2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 C: class) (V: class z):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cuni B2) (cuni C))) (wb (wss (cfv B2 ctg) (cfv C ctg)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wss (cv z) (cv y1))) C)) B2) (cuni B2))) $;

--| Given a topology ` J ` , show that a subset ` B ` satisfying the third antecedent is a basis for it. Lemma 2.3 of [Munkres] p. 81 using abbreviations. (Contributed by NM, 22-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem basgen (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss B2 J) (wss J (cfv B2 ctg))) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) J) $;

--| Given a topology ` J ` , show that a subset ` B ` satisfying the third antecedent is a basis for it. Lemma 2.3 of [Munkres] p. 81. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem basgen2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss B2 J) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cv z) (cv x3))) B2) (cv x3)) J)) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) J) $;

--| Conditions that determine the equality of two generated topologies. (Contributed by NM, 8-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem _2basgen (B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 C) (wss C (cfv B2 ctg))) (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) (cfv C ctg)) $;

--| If a subbase is included into a topology, so is the generated topology. (Contributed by FL, 20-Apr-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2015.)
theorem tgfiss (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 J)) (wss (cfv (cfv A2 cfi) ctg) J) $;

--| A generated topology is not affected by the addition or removal of the empty set from the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgdif0 (B2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cdif B2 (csn c0)) ctg) (cfv B2 ctg) $;

--| A subset of a topology is a basis for the topology iff every member of the topology is a union of members of the basis. We use the idiom " ` ( topGen `` B ) = J ` " to express " ` B ` is a basis for topology ` J ` " since we do not have a separate notation for this. Definition 15.35 of [Schechter] p. 428. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem bastop1 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss B2 J)) (wb (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) J) (wral x3 (wex y1 (wa (wss (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) (cuni (cv y1))))) J)) $;

--| A version of ~ bastop1 that doesn't have ` B C_ J ` in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 3-Feb-2008.)
theorem bastop2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wceq (cfv B2 ctg) J) (wa (wss B2 J) (wral x3 (wex y1 (wa (wss (cv y1) B2) (wceq (cv x3) (cuni (cv y1))))) J))) $;

--| The discrete topology on a set ` A ` . Part of Example 2 in [Munkres] p. 77. (Contributed by FL, 17-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem distop (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cpw A2) ctop) $;

--| The class of all topologies is a proper class. The proof uses discrete topologies and ~ pwnex ; an alternate proof uses indiscrete topologies (see ~ indistop ) and the analogue of ~ pwnex with pairs ` { (/) , x } ` instead of power sets ` ~P x ` (that analogue is also a consequence of ~ abnex ). (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2021.)
theorem topnex:
  $ wnel ctop cvv $;

--| The discrete topology on a set ` A ` , with base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem distopon (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cpw A2) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The singleton of the empty set is a topology on the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem sn0topon:
  $ wcel (csn c0) (cfv c0 ctopon) $;

--| The singleton of the empty set is a topology. (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 3-Mar-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem sn0top:
  $ wcel (csn c0) ctop $;

--| A lemma to eliminate some sethood hypotheses when dealing with the indiscrete topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indislem (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cpr c0 (cfv A2 cid)) (cpr c0 A2) $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` . Part of Example 2 in [Munkres] p. 77. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem indistopon (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cpr c0 A2) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` . Part of Example 2 in [Munkres] p. 77. (Contributed by FL, 16-Jul-2006.) (Revised by Stefan Allan, 6-Nov-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem indistop (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cpr c0 A2) ctop $;

--| The base set of the indiscrete topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indisuni (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 cid) (cuni (cpr c0 A2)) $;

--| The finite complement topology on a set ` A ` . Example 3 in [Munkres] p. 77. (Contributed by FL, 15-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem fctop {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wo (wcel (cdif A2 (cv x3)) cfn) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The finite complement topology on a set ` A ` . Example 3 in [Munkres] p. 77. (This version of ~ fctop requires the Axiom of Infinity.) (Contributed by FL, 20-Aug-2006.)
theorem fctop2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wo (wbr (cdif A2 (cv x3)) com csdm) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The countable complement topology on a set ` A ` . Example 4 in [Munkres] p. 77. (Contributed by FL, 23-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem cctop {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 (wo (wbr (cdif A2 (cv x3)) com cdom) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The particular point topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ppttop {x3: setvar} (A2 P V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel P A2)) (wcel (crab x3 (wo (wcel P (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The particular point topology is generated by a basis consisting of pairs ` { x , P } ` for each ` x e. A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem pptbas {x3: setvar} (A2 P V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel P A2)) (wceq (crab x3 (wo (wcel P (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) (cpw A2)) (cfv (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cpr (cv x3) P))) ctg)) $;

--| The excluded point topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem epttop {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel P A2)) (wcel (crab x3 (wi (wcel P (cv x3)) (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space, using explicit structure component references. Compare with ~ indistps and ~ indistps2 . The advantage of this version is that the actual function for the structure is evident, and ~ df-ndx is not needed, nor any other special definition outside of basic set theory. The disadvantage is that if the indices of the component definitions ~ df-base and ~ df-tset are changed in the future, this theorem will also have to be changed. Note: This theorem has hard-coded structure indices for demonstration purposes. It is not intended for general use; use ~ indistps instead. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by FL, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem indistpsx (A2 K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop c1 A2) (cop c9 (cpr c0 A2))) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space, using implicit structure indices. The advantage of this version over ~ indistpsx is that it is independent of the indices of the component definitions ~ df-base and ~ df-tset , and if they are changed in the future, this theorem will not be affected. The advantage over ~ indistps2 is that it is easy to eliminate the hypotheses with ~ eqid and ~ vtoclg to result in a closed theorem. Theorems ~ indistpsALT and ~ indistps2ALT show that the two forms can be derived from each other. (Contributed by FL, 19-Jul-2006.)
theorem indistps (A2 K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cpr c0 A2))) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space, using direct component assignments. Compare with ~ indistps . The advantage of this version is that it is the shortest to state and easiest to work with in most situations. Theorems ~ indistpsALT and ~ indistps2ALT show that the two forms can be derived from each other. (Contributed by NM, 24-Oct-2012.)
theorem indistps2 (A2 K: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cpr c0 A2) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space. Here we show how to derive the structural version ~ indistps from the direct component assignment version ~ indistps2 . (Contributed by NM, 24-Oct-2012.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem indistpsALT (A2 K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cpr c0 A2))) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| The indiscrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space, using direct component assignments. Here we show how to derive the direct component assignment version ~ indistps2 from the structural version ~ indistps . (Contributed by NM, 24-Oct-2012.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem indistps2ALT (A2 K: class):
  $ wceq (cfv K cbs) A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cpr c0 A2) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| The discrete topology on a set ` A ` expressed as a topological space. (Contributed by FL, 20-Aug-2006.)
theorem distps (A2 K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cpw A2))) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| Extend class notation with the set of closed sets of a topology.
term ccld: class;

--| Extend class notation with interior of a subset of a topology base set.
term cnt: class;

--| Extend class notation with closure of a subset of a topology base set.
term ccl: class;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is the set of closed sets of the topology. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2006.)
axiom df_cld {x3 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccld (cmpt j ctop (crab x3 (wcel (cdif (cuni (cv j)) (cv x3)) (cv j)) (cpw (cuni (cv j))))) $;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is the interior function on the subsets of the base set. See ~ ntrval . (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2006.)
axiom df_ntr {x3 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnt (cmpt j ctop (cmpt x3 (cpw (cuni (cv j))) (cuni (cin (cv j) (cpw (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is the closure function on the subsets of the base set. See ~ clsval . (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2006.)
axiom df_cls {x3 y1 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccl (cmpt j ctop (cmpt x3 (cpw (cuni (cv j))) (cint (crab y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cfv (cv j) ccld))))) $;

--| The closed-set generator is a well-behaved function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem fncld:
  $ wfn ccld ctop $;

--| The set of closed sets of a topology. (Note that the set of open sets is just the topology itself, so we don't have a separate definition.) (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem cldval {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J ccld) (crab x3 (wcel (cdif X (cv x3)) J) (cpw X))) $;

--| The interior function on the subsets of a topology's base set. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ntrfval {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J cnt) (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (cuni (cin J (cpw (cv x3)))))) $;

--| The closure function on the subsets of a topology's base set. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem clsfval {x3 y1: setvar} (J: class) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J ccl) (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (cint (crab y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cfv J ccld))))) $;

--| Reverse closure of the closed set operation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem cldrcl (C J: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` S ` is a closed set". (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem iscld (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wa (wss S X) (wcel (cdif X S) J))) $;

--| A subset of the underlying set of a topology is closed iff its complement is open. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2006.)
theorem iscld2 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wcel (cdif X S) J)) $;

--| A closed set is a subset of the underlying set of a topology. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem cldss (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wss S X) $;

--| The set of closed sets is contained in the powerset of the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2014.)
theorem cldss2 (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wss (cfv J ccld) (cpw X) $;

--| The complement of a closed set is open. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem cldopn (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wcel (cdif X S) J) $;

--| A subset of the underlying set of a topology is open iff its complement is closed. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2006.)
theorem isopn2 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S J) (wcel (cdif X S) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| The complement of an open set is closed. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2006.)
theorem opncld (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel S J)) (wcel (cdif X S) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The difference of a closed set with an open set is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2014.)
theorem difopn (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 J) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) J) $;

--| The underlying set of a topology is closed. Part of Theorem 6.1(1) of [Munkres] p. 93. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2006.)
theorem topcld (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel X (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The interior of a subset of a topology's base set is the union of all the open sets it includes. Definition of interior of [Munkres] p. 94. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ntrval (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) (cuni (cin J (cpw S)))) $;

--| The closure of a subset of a topology's base set is the intersection of all the closed sets that include it. Definition of closure of [Munkres] p. 94. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem clsval {x3: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cint (crab x3 (wss S (cv x3)) (cfv J ccld)))) $;

--| The empty set is closed. Part of Theorem 6.1(1) of [Munkres] p. 93. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2006.)
theorem _0cld (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel c0 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The indexed intersection of a collection ` B ( x ) ` of closed sets is closed. Theorem 6.1(2) of [Munkres] p. 93. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem iincld {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) A2)) (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The intersection of a set of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.)
theorem intcld (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wss A2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cint A2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The union of two closed sets is closed. Equivalent to Theorem 6.1(3) of [Munkres] p. 93. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2006.)
theorem uncld (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A closed subset equals its own closure. (Contributed by NM, 15-Mar-2007.)
theorem cldcls (S J: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) S) $;

--| The intersection of two closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem incld (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| An indexed relative intersection of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem riincld {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) A2)) (wcel (cin X (ciin x3 A2 B2)) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A finite indexed union of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem iuncld {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) A2)) (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A finite union of closed sets is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem unicld (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cuni A2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The closure of a subset of a topology's underlying set is closed. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2006.)
theorem clscld (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wcel (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The closure function is a function from subsets of the base to closed sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.)
theorem clsf (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wf (cpw X) (cfv J ccld) (cfv J ccl)) $;

--| The interior of a subset of a topology's underlying set is open. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ntropn (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wcel (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) J) $;

--| Express closure in terms of interior. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem clsval2 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cdif X (cfv (cdif X S) (cfv J cnt)))) $;

--| Interior expressed in terms of closure. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntrval2 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) (cdif X (cfv (cdif X S) (cfv J ccl)))) $;

--| An interior of a complement is the complement of the closure. This set is also known as the exterior of ` A ` . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Aug-2009.)
theorem ntrdif (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cdif X A2) (cfv J cnt)) (cdif X (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)))) $;

--| A closure of a complement is the complement of the interior. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Aug-2009.)
theorem clsdif (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cdif X A2) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif X (cfv A2 (cfv J cnt)))) $;

--| Subset relationship for closure. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.)
theorem clsss (S T J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wss T S)) (wss (cfv T (cfv J ccl)) (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| Subset relationship for interior. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntrss (S T J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wss T S)) (wss (cfv T (cfv J cnt)) (cfv S (cfv J cnt))) $;

--| A subset of a topology's underlying set is included in its closure. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2007.)
theorem sscls (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss S (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| A subset includes its interior. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ntrss2 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) S) $;

--| An open subset of a set is a subset of the set's interior. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ssntr (S J O X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wa (wcel O J) (wss O S))) (wss O (cfv S (cfv J cnt))) $;

--| The closure of a subset of a topological space is included in the space. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem clsss3 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) X) $;

--| The interior of a subset of a topological space is included in the space. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntrss3 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) X) $;

--| A pairwise intersection of interiors is the interior of the intersection. This does not always hold for arbitrary intersections. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Aug-2009.)
theorem ntrin (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X) (wss B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) (cfv J cnt)) (cin (cfv A2 (cfv J cnt)) (cfv B2 (cfv J cnt)))) $;

--| The complement of a closure is open. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2006.)
theorem cmclsopn (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wcel (cdif X (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) J) $;

--| The complement of an interior is closed. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 3-Jul-2020.)
theorem cmntrcld (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wcel (cdif X (cfv S (cfv J cnt))) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A subset is closed iff it equals its own closure. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem iscld3 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) S)) $;

--| A subset is closed iff it contains its own closure. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2008.)
theorem iscld4 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) S)) $;

--| A subset is open iff it equals its own interior. (Contributed by NM, 9-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem isopn3 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S J) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) S)) $;

--| The closure operation is idempotent. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.)
theorem clsidm (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| The interior operation is idempotent. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntridm (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) (cfv J cnt)) (cfv S (cfv J cnt))) $;

--| The closure of a topology's underlying set is the entire set. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 11-Mar-2023.)
theorem clstop (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv X (cfv J ccl)) X) $;

--| The interior of a topology's underlying set is the entire set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.)
theorem ntrtop (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv X (cfv J cnt)) X) $;

--| A subset with an empty interior cannot cover a whole (nonempty) topology. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.)
theorem _0ntr (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wne X c0)) (wa (wss S X) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) c0))) (wne (cdif X S) c0) $;

--| If a subset is included in a closed set, so is the subset's closure. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2007.)
theorem clsss2 (C S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) (wss S C)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) C) $;

--| Membership in a closure. Theorem 6.5(a) of [Munkres] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2007.)
theorem elcls {x3: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wral x3 (wi (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cin (cv x3) S) c0)) J)) $;

--| Membership in a closure. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2007.)
theorem elcls2 {x3: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wa (wcel P X) (wral x3 (wi (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cin (cv x3) S) c0)) J))) $;

--| Any open set containing a point that belongs to the closure of a subset intersects the subset. One direction of Theorem 6.5(a) of [Munkres] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem clsndisj (P S U J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl)))) (wa (wcel U J) (wcel P U))) (wne (cin U S) c0) $;

--| A subset whose closure has an empty interior also has an empty interior. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntrcls0 (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wceq (cfv (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv J cnt)) c0)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) c0) $;

--| Two ways to say that a subset has an empty interior. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem ntreq0 {x3: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) c0) (wral x3 (wi (wss (cv x3) S) (wceq (cv x3) c0)) J)) $;

--| The closed sets of a topology comprise a Moore system on the points of the topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem cldmre (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel (cfv J ccld) (cfv X cmre)) $;

--| Moore closure generalizes closure in a topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mrccls (F J: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv (cfv J ccld) cmrc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J ccl) F) $;

--| The closure of the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by Jim Kingdon, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem cls0 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv c0 (cfv J ccl)) c0) $;

--| The interior of the empty set. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2007.)
theorem ntr0 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv c0 (cfv J cnt)) c0) $;

--| An open subset equals its own interior. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem isopn3i (S J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel S J)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnt)) S) $;

--| Membership in a closure in terms of the members of a basis. Theorem 6.5(b) of [Munkres] p. 95. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem elcls3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 P S: class) (J X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv B2 ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 ctb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wral x3 (wi (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cin (cv x3) S) c0)) B2)) $;

--| A bijection useful for converting statements about open sets to statements about closed sets and vice versa. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem opncldf1 {x3 u: setvar} (F: class u) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt u J (cdif X (cv u))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wa (wf1o J (cfv J ccld) F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt x3 (cfv J ccld) (cdif X (cv x3))))) $;

--| The values of the open-closed bijection. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem opncldf2 {u: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class u) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt u J (cdif X (cv u))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cdif X A2)) $;

--| The values of the converse/inverse of the open-closed bijection. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem opncldf3 {u: setvar} (B2 F: class u) (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt u J (cdif X (cv u))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) (wceq (cfv B2 (ccnv F)) (cdif X B2)) $;

--| A set ` A ` is clopen iff for every point ` x ` in the space there is a neighborhood ` y ` such that all the points in ` y ` are in ` A ` iff ` x ` is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem isclo {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 (cin J (cfv J ccld))) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv z) A2)) (cv y1))) J) X)) $;

--| A set ` A ` is clopen iff for every point ` x ` in the space there is a neighborhood ` y ` of ` x ` which is either disjoint from ` A ` or contained in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.)
theorem isclo2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 (cin J (cfv J ccld))) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) A2) (wss (cv y1) A2)) (cv y1))) J) X)) $;

--| The open sets of a discrete topology are closed and its closed sets are open. (Contributed by FL, 7-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem discld (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (cpw A2) ccld) (cpw A2)) $;

--| The closed sets of the topology ` { (/) } ` . (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.)
theorem sn0cld:
  $ wceq (cfv (csn c0) ccld) (csn c0) $;

--| The closed sets of an indiscrete topology. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indiscld (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cpr c0 A2) ccld) (cpr c0 A2) $;

--| A Moore collection which is closed under finite unions called _topological_; such a collection is the closed sets of a canonically associated topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem mretopd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (J: class z) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv B2 cmre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 M) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) M) (wcel (cv y1) M)) (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) M) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wcel (cdif B2 (cv z)) M) (cpw B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wceq M (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| The topologies over a given base set form a Moore collection: the intersection of any family of them is a topology, including the empty (relative) intersection which gives the discrete topology ~ distop . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2015.)
theorem toponmre (B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wcel (cfv B2 ctopon) (cfv (cpw B2) cmre)) $;

--| The closed sets of a topology over a set are a Moore collection over the same set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem cldmreon (B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ccld) (cfv B2 cmre)) $;

--| A family is the closed sets of a topology iff it is a Moore collection and closed under finite union. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem iscldtop {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 K: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (cima ccld (cfv B2 ctopon))) (w3a (wcel K (cfv B2 cmre)) (wcel c0 K) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) K) K) K)) $;

--| The closed subspaces of a topology-bearing module form a complete lattice. Demonstration for ~ mreclatBAD . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 31-Jan-2015.) TODO ( ~ df-riota update): This proof uses the old ~ df-clat and references the required instance of ~ mreclatBAD as a hypothesis. When ~ mreclatBAD is corrected to become mreclat, delete this theorem and uncomment the mreclatdemo below.
theorem mreclatdemoBAD (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cin (cfv W clss) (cfv (cfv W ctopn) ccld)) (cfv (cuni (cfv W ctopn)) cmre)) (wcel (cfv (cin (cfv W clss) (cfv (cfv W ctopn) ccld)) cipo) ccla) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cin ctps clmod)) (wcel (cfv (cin (cfv W clss) (cfv (cfv W ctopn) ccld)) cipo) ccla) $;

--| Extend class notation with neighborhood relation for topologies.
term cnei: class;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is a map from a subset to its neighborhoods. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-2007.)
axiom df_nei {x3 y1 g2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnei (cmpt j ctop (cmpt x3 (cpw (cuni (cv j))) (crab y1 (wrex g2 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) (cv y1))) (cv j)) (cpw (cuni (cv j)))))) $;

--| Value of the neighborhood function on the subsets of the base set of a topology. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem neifval {x3 v g2: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J cnei) (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (crab v (wrex g2 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) (cv v))) J) (cpw X)))) $;

--| The neighborhood function is a function from the set of the subsets of the base set of a topology. (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem neif (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wfn (cfv J cnei) (cpw X)) $;

--| A set with a neighborhood is a subset of the base set of a topology. (This theorem depends on a function's value being empty outside of its domain, but it will make later theorems simpler to state.) (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2007.)
theorem neiss2 (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wss S X) $;

--| Value of the set of neighborhoods of a subset of the base set of a topology. (Contributed by NM, 11-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem neival {v g2: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J cnei)) (crab v (wrex g2 (wa (wss S (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) (cv v))) J) (cpw X))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` N ` is a neighborhood of ` S ` ". (Contributed by FL, 25-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem isnei {g2: setvar} (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) (wa (wss N X) (wrex g2 (wa (wss S (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) N)) J))) $;

--| An intuitive definition of a neighborhood in terms of interior. (Contributed by Szymon Jaroszewicz, 18-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem neiint (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wss N X)) (wb (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) (wss S (cfv N (cfv J cnt)))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` N ` is a neighborhood of point ` P ` ". (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem isneip {g2: setvar} (P J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) (wa (wss N X) (wrex g2 (wa (wcel P (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) N)) J))) $;

--| A neighborhood is included in the topology's base set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2007.)
theorem neii1 (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wss N X) $;

--| The neighborhoods of any set are subsets of the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem neisspw (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wss (cfv S (cfv J cnei)) (cpw X)) $;

--| Property of a neighborhood. (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2007.)
theorem neii2 {g2: setvar} (S J N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wrex g2 (wa (wss S (cv g2)) (wss (cv g2) N)) J) $;

--| Any neighborhood of a set ` S ` is also a neighborhood of any subset ` R C_ S ` . Similar to Proposition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.2. (Contributed by FL, 25-Sep-2006.)
theorem neiss (R S J N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) (wss R S)) (wcel N (cfv R (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| A set is included in any of its neighborhoods. Generalization to subsets of ~ elnei . (Contributed by FL, 16-Nov-2006.)
theorem ssnei (S J N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wss S N) $;

--| A point belongs to any of its neighborhoods. Property V_iii of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.3. (Contributed by FL, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem elnei (A2 P J N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel P A2) (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)))) (wcel P N) $;

--| The empty set is not a neighborhood of a nonempty set. (Contributed by FL, 18-Sep-2007.)
theorem _0nnei (S J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wne S c0)) (wn (wcel c0 (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| A neighborhood of a set is a neighborhood of every point in the set. Proposition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.2. (Contributed by FL, 16-Nov-2006.)
theorem neips {p: setvar} (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wne S c0)) (wb (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) (wral p (wcel N (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv J cnei))) S)) $;

--| An open set is a neighborhood of any of its subsets. (Contributed by FL, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem opnneissb (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel N J) (wss S X)) (wb (wss S N) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| Any superset of an open set is a neighborhood of it. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-2007.)
theorem opnssneib (S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel S J) (wss N X)) (wb (wss S N) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| Any subset ` M ` of ` X ` containing a neighborhood ` N ` of a set ` S ` is a neighborhood of this set. Generalization to subsets of Property V_i of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.3. (Contributed by FL, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem ssnei2 (S J M N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wa (wss N M) (wss M X))) (wcel M (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| Any neighborhood of an element in the closure of a subset intersects the subset. Part of proof of Theorem 6.6 of [Munkres] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem neindisj (P S J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wa (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))))) (wne (cin N S) c0) $;

--| An open set is a neighborhood of any of its subsets. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2007.)
theorem opnneiss (S J N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel N J) (wss S N)) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| An open set is a neighborhood of any of its members. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem opnneip (P J N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel N J) (wcel P N)) (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| A set is open iff it is a neighborhood of all of its points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 15-Sep-2009.)
theorem opnnei {x3: setvar} (S J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel S J) (wral x3 (wcel S (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei))) S)) $;

--| The underlying set of a topology is a neighborhood of any of its subsets. Special case of ~ opnneiss . (Contributed by FL, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem tpnei (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wss S X) (wcel X (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| The union of the neighborhoods of a set equals the topology's underlying set. (Contributed by FL, 18-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem neiuni (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq X (cuni (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| A point ` P ` belongs to the closure of a set ` S ` iff every neighborhood of ` P ` meets ` S ` . (Contributed by FL, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem neindisj2 {n: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wral n (wne (cin (cv n) S) c0) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| A finer topology has more neighborhoods. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem topssnei (S J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop) (wceq X Y)) (wss J K)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J cnei)) (cfv S (cfv K cnei))) $;

--| The intersection of two neighborhoods of a set is also a neighborhood of the set. Generalization to subsets of Property V_ii of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.3 for binary intersections. (Contributed by FL, 28-Sep-2006.)
theorem innei (S J M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) (wcel M (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wcel (cin N M) (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| Only an open set is a neighborhood of itself. (Contributed by FL, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem opnneiid (J N: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel N (cfv N (cfv J cnei))) (wcel N J)) $;

--| For any neighborhood ` N ` of ` S ` , there is a neighborhood ` x ` of ` S ` such that ` N ` is a neighborhood of all subsets of ` x ` . Generalization to subsets of Property V_iv of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.3. (Contributed by FL, 2-Oct-2006.)
theorem neissex {x3 y1: setvar} (S J N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel N (cfv S (cfv J cnei)))) (wrex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wss (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel N (cfv (cv y1) (cfv J cnei))))) (cfv S (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| The empty set is a neighborhood of itself. (Contributed by FL, 10-Dec-2006.)
theorem _0nei (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel c0 (cfv c0 (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ neiptopreu . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jan-2018.)
theorem neipeltop {p a: setvar} (C: class) (J: class p a) (N X: class p):
  $ wceq J (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C J) (wa (wss C X) (wral p (wcel C (cfv (cv p) N)) C)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ neiptopreu . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jan-2018.)
theorem neiptopuni {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff q a b) (J: class q b) (N: class q p b) (X: class q b):
  $ wceq J (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cpw (cpw X)) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cv a) (cv b)) (wss (cv b) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv p) N) cfi) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv p) (cv a)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wrex b (wral q (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv q) N)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel X (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni J)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ neiptopreu . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2018.)
theorem neiptoptop {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff q) (J: class q b) (N: class q p) (X: class q):
  $ wceq J (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cpw (cpw X)) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cv a) (cv b)) (wss (cv b) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv p) N) cfi) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv p) (cv a)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wrex b (wral q (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv q) N)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel X (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $;

--| Lemma for ~ neiptopreu . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2018.)
theorem neiptopnei {q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (J: class q b) (N X: class):
  $ wceq J (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cpw (cpw X)) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cv a) (cv b)) (wss (cv b) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv p) N) cfi) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv p) (cv a)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wrex b (wral q (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv q) N)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel X (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (cmpt p X (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| If, to each element ` P ` of a set ` X ` , we associate a set ` ( N `` P ) ` fulfilling Properties V_i, V_ii, V_iii and Property V_iv of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.2. , corresponding to ~ ssnei , ~ innei , ~ elnei and ~ neissex , then there is a unique topology ` j ` such that for any point ` p ` , ` ( N `` p ) ` is the set of neighborhoods of ` p ` . Proposition 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.3. This can be used to build a topology from a set of neighborhoods. Note that ~ innei uses binary intersections whereas Property V_ii mentions finite intersections (which includes the empty intersection of subsets of ` X ` , which is equal to ` X ` ), so we add the hypothesis that ` X ` is a neighborhood of all points. TODO: when ~ df-fi includes the empty intersection, remove that extra hypothesis. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jan-2018.)
theorem neiptopreu {j q p a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (J: class q) (N X: class):
  $ wceq J (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cpw (cpw X)) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cv a) (cv b)) (wss (cv b) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv p) N) cfi) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv p) (cv a)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wrex b (wral q (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv q) N)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel X (cfv (cv p) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu j (wceq N (cmpt p X (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv (cv j) cnei)))) (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the limit point function for topologies.
term clp: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all perfect spaces.
term cperf: class;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is the set of limit points of the subsets of the base set. See ~ lpval . (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.)
axiom df_lp {x3 y1 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq clp (cmpt j ctop (cmpt x3 (cpw (cuni (cv j))) (cab y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv y1))) (cfv (cv j) ccl)))))) $;

--| Define the class of all perfect spaces. A perfect space is one for which every point in the set is a limit point of the whole space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_perf {j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cperf (crab j (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) clp)) (cuni (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| The limit point function on the subsets of a topology's base set. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem lpfval {x3 y1: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv J clp) (cmpt x3 (cpw X) (cab y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) (csn (cv y1))) (cfv J ccl)))))) $;

--| The set of limit points of a subset of the base set of a topology. Alternate definition of limit point in [Munkres] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem lpval {x3: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J clp)) (cab x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) (cfv J ccl))))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` P ` is a limit point of ` S ` ". (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.)
theorem islp (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp))) (wcel P (cfv (cdif S (csn P)) (cfv J ccl)))) $;

--| The limit points of a subset are included in the subset's closure. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem lpsscls (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J clp)) (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| The limit points of a subset are included in the base set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2007.)
theorem lpss (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J clp)) X) $;

--| ` P ` is a limit point of ` S ` iff it is a limit point of ` S \ { P } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem lpdifsn (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp))) (wcel P (cfv (cdif S (csn P)) (cfv J clp)))) $;

--| Subset relationship for limit points. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem lpss3 (S T J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wss T S)) (wss (cfv T (cfv J clp)) (cfv S (cfv J clp))) $;

--| The predicate " ` P ` is a limit point of ` S ` " in terms of neighborhoods. Definition of limit point in [Munkres] p. 97. Although Munkres uses open neighborhoods, it also works for our more general neighborhoods. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem islp2 {n: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp))) (wral n (wne (cin (cv n) (cdif S (csn P))) c0) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)))) $;

--| The predicate " ` P ` is a limit point of ` S ` " in terms of open sets. see ~ islp2 , ~ elcls , ~ islp . (Contributed by FL, 31-Jul-2009.)
theorem islp3 {x3: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp))) (wral x3 (wi (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cin (cv x3) (cdif S (csn P))) c0)) J)) $;

--| A point is a limit point of the whole space iff the singleton of the point is not open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem maxlp (P J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel P (cfv X (cfv J clp))) (wa (wcel P X) (wn (wcel (csn P) J)))) $;

--| The closure of a subset of a topological space is the subset together with its limit points. Theorem 6.6 of [Munkres] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem clslp (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cun S (cfv S (cfv J clp)))) $;

--| A point belonging to a set's closure but not the set itself is a limit point. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.)
theorem islpi (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wa (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wn (wcel P S)))) (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp))) $;

--| A subset of a topological space is closed iff it contains all its limit points. Corollary 6.7 of [Munkres] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 26-Feb-2007.)
theorem cldlp (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wss (cfv S (cfv J clp)) S)) $;

--| Definition of a perfect space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem isperf (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cperf) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cfv X (cfv J clp)) X)) $;

--| Definition of a perfect space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem isperf2 (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cperf) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss X (cfv X (cfv J clp)))) $;

--| A perfect space is a topology which has no open singletons. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem isperf3 {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cperf) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (csn (cv x3)) J)) X)) $;

--| The limit points of a perfect space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem perflp (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J cperf) (wceq (cfv X (cfv J clp)) X) $;

--| Property of a perfect space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem perfi (P J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cperf) (wcel P X)) (wn (wcel (csn P) J)) $;

--| A perfect space is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem perftop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cperf) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for the subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem restrcl (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel (co J A2 crest) ctop) (wa (wcel J cvv) (wcel A2 cvv)) $;

--| A subspace topology basis is a basis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem restbas (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel (co B2 A2 crest) ctb) $;

--| A subspace can be generated by restricted sets from a basis for the original topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgrest (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel A2 W)) (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 crest) ctg) (co (cfv B2 ctg) A2 crest)) $;

--| A subspace topology is a topology. Definition of subspace topology in [Munkres] p. 89. ` A ` is normally a subset of the base set of ` J ` . (Contributed by FL, 15-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem resttop (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) ctop) $;

--| A subspace topology is a topology on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem resttopon (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss A2 X)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) (cfv A2 ctopon)) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace topology. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem restuni (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wceq A2 (cuni (co J A2 crest))) $;

--| The topological space built with a subspace topology. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem stoig (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wcel (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) A2) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (co J A2 crest))) ctps) $;

--| Composition of subspaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem restco (A2 B2 J V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wcel A2 W) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co (co J A2 crest) B2 crest) (co J (cin A2 B2) crest)) $;

--| Equivalence of being a subspace of a subspace and being a subspace of the original. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem restabs (S T J V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wss S T) (wcel T W)) (wceq (co (co J T crest) S crest) (co J S crest)) $;

--| When the subspace region is not a subset of the base of the topology, the resulting set is the same as the subspace restricted to the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem restin (A2 J V W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel A2 W)) (wceq (co J A2 crest) (co J (cin A2 X) crest)) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem restuni2 (A2 J V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (cin A2 X) (cuni (co J A2 crest))) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem resttopon2 (A2 J V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) (cfv (cin A2 X) ctopon)) $;

--| The subspace topology induced by the topology ` J ` on the empty set. (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem rest0 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq (co J c0 crest) (csn c0)) $;

--| The only subspace topology induced by the topology ` { (/) } ` . (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem restsn (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (co (csn c0) A2 crest) (csn c0)) $;

--| The subspace topology induced by a singleton. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem restsn2 (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (co J (csn A2) crest) (cpw (csn A2))) $;

--| A closed set of a subspace topology is a closed set of the original topology intersected with the subset. (Contributed by FL, 11-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem restcld {x3: setvar} (A2 S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv (co J S crest) ccld)) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cin (cv x3) S)) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| A closed set is closed in the subspace topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem restcldi (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 X) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cfv (co J A2 crest) ccld)) $;

--| A set which is closed in the subspace topology induced by a closed set is closed in the original topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem restcldr (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) (wcel B2 (cfv (co J A2 crest) ccld))) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| If ` B ` is an open subset of the subspace base set ` A ` , then any subset of ` B ` is open iff it is open in ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem restopnb (A2 B2 C J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 V)) (w3a (wcel B2 J) (wss B2 A2) (wss C B2))) (wb (wcel C J) (wcel C (co J A2 crest))) $;

--| If ` K ` is a finer topology than ` J ` , then the subspace topologies induced by ` A ` maintain this relationship. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem ssrest (A2 J K V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K V) (wss J K)) (wss (co J A2 crest) (co K A2 crest)) $;

--| If ` A ` is open, then ` B ` is open in ` A ` iff it is an open subset of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem restopn2 (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J)) (wb (wcel B2 (co J A2 crest)) (wa (wcel B2 J) (wss B2 A2))) $;

--| A subspace of a discrete topology is discrete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem restdis (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2)) (wceq (co (cpw A2) B2 crest) (cpw B2)) $;

--| The restriction of the set of finite subsets of ` A ` is the set of finite subsets of ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem restfpw (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2)) (wceq (co (cin (cpw A2) cfn) B2 crest) (cin (cpw B2) cfn)) $;

--| The neighborhood of a trace is the trace of the neighborhood. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jan-2018.)
theorem neitr (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X) (wss B2 A2)) (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv (co J A2 crest) cnei)) (co (cfv B2 (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest)) $;

--| A closure in a subspace topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem restcls (S J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss Y X) (wss S Y)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv K ccl)) (cin (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) Y)) $;

--| An interior in a subspace topology. Willard in _General Topology_ says that there is no analogue of ~ restcls for interiors. In some sense, that is true. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 23-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem restntr (S J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss Y X) (wss S Y)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv K cnt)) (cin (cfv (cun S (cdif X Y)) (cfv J cnt)) Y)) $;

--| The limit points of a subset restrict naturally in a subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem restlp (S J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss Y X) (wss S Y)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv K clp)) (cin (cfv S (cfv J clp)) Y)) $;

--| Perfection of a subspace. Note that the term "perfect set" is reserved for _closed_ sets which are perfect in the subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem restperf (J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss Y X)) (wb (wcel K cperf) (wss Y (cfv Y (cfv J clp)))) $;

--| An open subset of a perfect space is perfect. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem perfopn (J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cperf) (wcel Y J)) (wcel K cperf) $;

--| The topology of a restricted structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem resstopn (A2 H J K: class):
  $ wceq H (co K A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq (co J A2 crest) (cfv H ctopn) $;

--| A restricted topological space is a topological space. Note that this theorem would not be true if ` TopSp ` was defined directly in terms of the ` TopSet ` slot instead of the ` TopOpen ` derived function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem resstps (A2 K V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ctps) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co K A2 cress) ctps) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtbas . In a total order, unbounded-above intervals are closed under intersection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtbaslem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wceq (cfv A2 cfi) A2) $;

--| Value of the order topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq (cfv R cordt) (cfv (cfv (cun (csn X) (cun A2 B2)) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| Value of the order topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtuni {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq X (cuni (cun (csn X) (cun A2 B2)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtbas . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtbas2 {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3 y1 a b) (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn (cmpo a b X X (crab y1 (wa (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv a) R)) (wn (wbr (cv b) (cv y1) R))) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) cfi) (cun (cun A2 B2) C)) $;

--| In a total order, the finite intersections of the open rays generates the set of open intervals, but no more - these four collections form a subbasis for the order topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtbas {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3 y1 a b) (R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) X))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn (cmpo a b X X (crab y1 (wa (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv a) R)) (wn (wbr (cv b) (cv y1) R))) X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wceq (cfv (cun (csn X) (cun A2 B2)) cfi) (cun (cun (csn X) (cun A2 B2)) C)) $;

--| Value of the order topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordttopon (R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wcel (cfv R cordt) (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| An upward ray ` ( P , +oo ) ` is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtopn1 {x3: setvar} (P R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel P X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wn (wbr (cv x3) P R)) X) (cfv R cordt)) $;

--| A downward ray ` ( -oo , P ) ` is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtopn2 {x3: setvar} (P R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel P X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wn (wbr P (cv x3) R)) X) (cfv R cordt)) $;

--| An open interval ` ( A , B ) ` is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtopn3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wa (wn (wbr (cv x3) A2 R)) (wn (wbr B2 (cv x3) R))) X) (cfv R cordt)) $;

--| A downward ray ` ( -oo , P ] ` is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtcld1 {x3: setvar} (P R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel P X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) P R) X) (cfv (cfv R cordt) ccld)) $;

--| An upward ray ` [ P , +oo ) ` is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtcld2 {x3: setvar} (P R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel P X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr P (cv x3) R) X) (cfv (cfv R cordt) ccld)) $;

--| A closed interval ` [ A , B ] ` is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtcld3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) R) (wbr (cv x3) B2 R)) X) (cfv (cfv R cordt) ccld)) $;

--| The order topology is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordttop (R V: class):
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wcel (cfv R cordt) ctop) $;

--| The order dual generates the same topology as the original order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtcnv (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wceq (cfv (ccnv R) cordt) (cfv R cordt)) $;

--| The subspace topology of an order topology is in general finer than the topology generated by the restricted order, but we do have inclusion in one direction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtrest (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cps) (wcel A2 V)) (wss (cfv (cin R (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) (co (cfv R cordt) A2 crest)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtrest2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtrest2lem {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctsr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wss (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R)) X) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral v (wcel (cin (cv v) A2) (cfv (cin R (cxp A2 A2)) cordt)) (crn (cmpt z X (crab w (wn (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R)) X)))) $;

--| An interval-closed set ` A ` in a total order has the same subspace topology as the restricted order topology. (An interval-closed set is the same thing as an open or half-open or closed interval in ` RR ` , but in other sets like ` QQ ` there are interval-closed sets like ` ( _pi , +oo ) i^i QQ ` that are not intervals.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtrest2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctsr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wss (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R)) X) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin R (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) (co (cfv R cordt) A2 crest)) $;

--| The topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem letopon:
  $ wcel (cfv cle cordt) (cfv cxr ctopon) $;

--| The topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem letop:
  $ wcel (cfv cle cordt) ctop $;

--| The topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem letopuni:
  $ wceq cxr (cuni (cfv cle cordt)) $;

--| The topology component of the extended real number structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrstopn:
  $ wceq (cfv cle cordt) (cfv cxrs ctopn) $;

--| The extended real number structure is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrstps:
  $ wcel cxrs ctps $;

--| Lemma for ~ leordtval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem leordtvallem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc))) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle)) cxr))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ leordtval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem leordtvallem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co cmnf (cv x3) cico))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle)) cxr))) $;

--| The topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem leordtval2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co cmnf (cv x3) cico))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cle cordt) (cfv (cfv (cun A2 B2) cfi) ctg) $;

--| The topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem leordtval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpt x3 cxr (co cmnf (cv x3) cico))) $ >
  $ wceq C (crn cioo) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cle cordt) (cfv (cun (cun A2 B2) C) ctg) $;

--| A closed interval is closed in the order topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem iccordt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cfv cle cordt) ccld) $;

--| An unbounded above open interval is open in the order topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem iocpnfordt (A2: class):
  $ wcel (co A2 cpnf cioc) (cfv cle cordt) $;

--| An unbounded above open interval is open in the order topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem icomnfordt (A2: class):
  $ wcel (co cmnf A2 cico) (cfv cle cordt) $;

--| An open interval is open in the order topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem iooordt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv cle cordt) $;

--| The real numbers are an open set in the topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem reordt:
  $ wcel cr (cfv cle cordt) $;

--| The set of closed intervals forms a closed subbasis for the topology on the extended reals. Since our definition of a basis is in terms of open sets, we express this by showing that the complements of closed intervals form an open subbasis for the topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem lecldbas {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cicc) (cdif cxr (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cle cordt) (cfv (cfv (crn F) cfi) ctg) $;

--| A neighborhood of ` +oo ` contains an unbounded interval based at a real number. Together with ~ xrtgioo (which describes neighborhoods of ` RR ` ) and ~ mnfnei , this gives all "negative" topological information ensuring that it is not too fine (and of course ~ iooordt and similar ensure that it has all the sets we want). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem pnfnei {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv cle cordt)) (wcel cpnf A2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc) A2) cr) $;

--| A neighborhood of ` -oo ` contains an unbounded interval based at a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem mnfnei {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv cle cordt)) (wcel cmnf A2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co cmnf (cv x3) cico) A2) cr) $;

--| The restriction of the less than order to an interval gives the same topology as the subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtrestixx {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wss A2 cxr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc) A2) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv cle cordt) A2 crest) (cfv (cin cle (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) $;

--| The restriction of the less than order to a closed interval gives the same topology as the subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtresticc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co (cfv cle cordt) (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) (cfv (cin cle (cxp (co A2 B2 cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc))) cordt) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of continuous functions between topologies.
term ccn: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of functions between topologies continuous at a given point.
term ccnp: class;

--| Extend class notation with a function on topological spaces whose value is the convergence relation for limit sequences in the space.
term clm: class;

--| Define a function on two topologies whose value is the set of continuous mappings from the first topology to the second. Based on definition of continuous function in [Munkres] p. 102. See ~ iscn for the predicate form. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.)
axiom df_cn {y1 f2 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccn (cmpo j k ctop ctop (crab f2 (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv j)) (cv k)) (co (cuni (cv k)) (cuni (cv j)) cmap))) $;

--| Define a function on two topologies whose value is the set of continuous mappings at a specified point in the first topology. Based on Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.)
axiom df_cnp {x3 y1 f2 g2 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccnp (cmpo j k ctop ctop (cmpt x3 (cuni (cv j)) (crab f2 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (wrex g2 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv g2)) (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cv y1))) (cv j))) (cv k)) (co (cuni (cv k)) (cuni (cv j)) cmap)))) $;

--| Define a function on topologies whose value is the convergence relation for sequences into the given topological space. Although ` f ` is typically a sequence (a function from an upperset of integers) with values in the topological space, it need not be. Note, however, that the limit property concerns only values at integers, so that the real-valued function ` ( x e. RR |-> ( sin `` ( _pi x. x ) ) ) ` converges to zero (in the standard topology on the reals) with this definition. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2006.)
axiom df_lm {x3 y1 u f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq clm (cmpt j ctop (copab f2 x3 (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (co (cuni (cv j)) cc cpm)) (wcel (cv x3) (cuni (cv j))) (wral u (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv u)) (wrex y1 (wf (cv y1) (cv u) (cres (cv f2) (cv y1))) (crn cuz))) (cv j))))) $;

--| The topological space convergence relation is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmrel (J: class):
  $ wrel (cfv J clm) $;

--| Reverse closure for the convergence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem lmrcl (P F J: class):
  $ wi (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The relation "sequence ` f ` converges to point ` y ` " in a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem lmfval {x3 y1 u f2: setvar} (J: class) (X: class u):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wceq (cfv J clm) (copab f2 x3 (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (co X cc cpm)) (wcel (cv x3) X) (wral u (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv u)) (wrex y1 (wf (cv y1) (cv u) (cres (cv f2) (cv y1))) (crn cuz))) J)))) $;

--| The set of all continuous functions from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfval {y1 f2: setvar} (J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wceq (co J K ccn) (crab f2 (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cv y1)) J) K) (co Y X cmap))) $;

--| The function mapping the points in a topology ` J ` to the set of all functions from ` J ` to topology ` K ` continuous at that point. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpfval {x3 w v f2: setvar} (J: class) (K X Y: class v):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wceq (co J K ccnp) (cmpt x3 X (crab f2 (wral w (wi (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv w)) (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv v)) (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv v)) (cv w))) J)) K) (co Y X cmap)))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` ". Definition of continuous function in [Munkres] p. 102. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscn {y1: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) J) K))) $;

--| The set of all functions from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` that are continuous at a point ` P ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem cnpval {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (P J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel P X)) (wceq (cfv P (co J K ccnp)) (crab f2 (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv P (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv x3)) (cv y1))) J)) K) (co Y X cmap))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` at point ` P ` ". Based on Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 17-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscnp {x3 y1: setvar} (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv P F) (cv y1)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cima F (cv x3)) (cv y1))) J)) K))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` ". Definition of continuous function in [Munkres] p. 102. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscn2 {y1: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) J) K))) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` at point ` P ` ". Based on Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscnp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop) (wcel P X)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv P F) (cv y1)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cima F (cv x3)) (cv y1))) J)) K))) $;

--| Reverse closure for a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cntop1 (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cntop2 (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel K ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for a function continuous at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnptop1 (P F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for a function continuous at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnptop2 (P F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel K ctop) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` at point ` P ` ". (Contributed by NM, 15-May-2007.)
theorem iscnp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv P F) (cv y1)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)))) J)) K))) $;

--| Reverse closure for a function continuous at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnprcl (P F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel P X) $;

--| A continuous function is a mapping. (Contributed by FL, 8-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnf (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wf X Y F) $;

--| A continuous function at point ` P ` is a mapping. (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpf (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wf X Y F) $;

--| The value of a continuous function from ` J ` to ` K ` at point ` P ` belongs to the underlying set of topology ` K ` . (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpcl (A2 P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cfv A2 F) Y) $;

--| A continuous function is a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnf2 (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wf X Y F) $;

--| A continuous function at point ` P ` is a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpf2 (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp)))) (wf X Y F) $;

--| Reverse closure for a function continuous at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnprcl2 (P F J K X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp)))) (wcel P X) $;

--| The continuity predicate when the range is given by a basis for a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgcn {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv B2 ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) J) B2))) $;

--| The "continuous at a point" predicate when the range is given by a basis for a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgcnp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2 P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv B2 ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cfv P F) (cv y1)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cima F (cv x3)) (cv y1))) J)) B2))) $;

--| The continuity predicate when the range is given by a subbasis for a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem subbascn {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (B2 F J K: class) (V: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv (cfv B2 cfi) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) J) B2))) $;

--| The identity function is a continuous function from one topology to another topology on the same set iff the domain is finer than the codomain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem ssidcn (J K X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon))) (wb (wcel (cres cid X) (co J K ccn)) (wss K J)) $;

--| Property of a function continuous at a point. (Contributed by FL, 31-Dec-2006.)
theorem cnpimaex {x3: setvar} (A2 P F J K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel A2 K) (wcel (cfv P F) A2)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cima F (cv x3)) A2)) J) $;

--| A restricted identity function is a continuous function. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem idcn (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cres cid X) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a topological space. Definition 1.4-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 25. The condition ` F C_ ( CC X. X ) ` allows to use objects more general than sequences when convenient; see the comment in ~ df-lm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmbr {y1 u: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (P: class y1) (F J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex y1 (wf (cv y1) (cv u) (cres F (cv y1))) (crn cuz))) J))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmbr2 {u j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P F J: class) (M: class u k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cv u))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z)) J))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. This version of ~ lmbr2 presupposes that ` F ` is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmbrf {u j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class u j k) (P F J: class) (M: class u k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wa (wcel P X) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex j (wral k (wcel A2 (cv u)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z)) J))) $;

--| A constant sequence converges to its value. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmconst (P J M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel P X) (wcel M cz)) (wbr (cxp Z (csn P)) P (cfv J clm)) $;

--| Convergence property of a converging sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem lmcvg {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P U F J: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) U) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The predicate "the class ` F ` is a continuous function from topology ` J ` to topology ` K ` at point ` P ` " in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by FL, 18-Jul-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem iscnp4 {x3 y1: setvar} (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wrex x3 (wss (cima F (cv x3)) (cv y1)) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) (cfv (csn (cfv P F)) (cfv K cnei))))) $;

--| A condition for continuity at a point in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Sep-2009.)
theorem cnpnei {y1: setvar} (A2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop) (wf X Y F)) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))) (cfv (csn (cfv A2 F)) (cfv K cnei)))) $;

--| An open subset of the codomain of a continuous function has an open preimage. (Contributed by FL, 15-Dec-2006.)
theorem cnima (A2 F J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel A2 K)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) J) $;

--| The composition of two continuous functions is a continuous function. (Contributed by FL, 8-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnco (F G J K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel G (co K L ccn))) (wcel (ccom G F) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| The composition of a function ` F ` continuous at ` P ` with a function continuous at ` ( F `` P ) ` is continuous at ` P ` . Proposition 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.9. (Contributed by FL, 16-Nov-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem cnpco (P F G J K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel G (cfv (cfv P F) (co K L ccnp)))) (wcel (ccom G F) (cfv P (co J L ccnp))) $;

--| A closed subset of the codomain of a continuous function has a closed preimage. (Contributed by NM, 15-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnclima (A2 F J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel A2 (cfv K ccld))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A characterization of a continuity function using closed sets. Theorem 1(d) of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.9. (Contributed by FL, 19-Nov-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscncl {y1: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv y1)) (cfv J ccld)) (cfv K ccld)))) $;

--| Property of the preimage of a closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncls2i (S F J K Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wss S Y)) (wss (cfv (cima (ccnv F) S) (cfv J ccl)) (cima (ccnv F) (cfv S (cfv K ccl)))) $;

--| Property of the preimage of an interior. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnntri (S F J K Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wss S Y)) (wss (cima (ccnv F) (cfv S (cfv K cnt))) (cfv (cima (ccnv F) S) (cfv J cnt))) $;

--| Property of the image of a closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnclsi (S F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wss S X)) (wss (cima F (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (cfv (cima F S) (cfv K ccl))) $;

--| Continuity in terms of closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncls2 {x3: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral x3 (wss (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)) (cfv J ccl)) (cima (ccnv F) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv K ccl)))) (cpw Y)))) $;

--| Continuity in terms of closure. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncls {x3: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral x3 (wss (cima F (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl))) (cfv (cima F (cv x3)) (cfv K ccl))) (cpw X)))) $;

--| Continuity in terms of interior. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Oct-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnntr {x3: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral x3 (wss (cima (ccnv F) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv K cnt))) (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)) (cfv J cnt))) (cpw Y)))) $;

--| If the topology ` K ` is finer than ` J ` , then there are more continuous functions from ` K ` than from ` J ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnss1 (J K L X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wss (co J L ccn) (co K L ccn)) $;

--| If the topology ` K ` is finer than ` J ` , then there are fewer continuous functions into ` K ` than into ` J ` from some other space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnss2 (J K L Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) (wss L K)) (wss (co J K ccn) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| A continuous function is continuous at all points. One direction of Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 20-Nov-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncnpi (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) $;

--| The set of continuous functions is a subset of the set of continuous functions at a point. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 21-Oct-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnsscnp (P J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P X) (wss (co J K ccn) (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) $;

--| A continuous function is continuous at all points. Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 15-May-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncnp {x3: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral x3 (wcel F (cfv (cv x3) (co J K ccnp))) X))) $;

--| A continuous function is continuous at all points. Theorem 7.2(g) of [Munkres] p. 107. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 20-Nov-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncnp2 {x3: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wne X c0) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wral x3 (wcel F (cfv (cv x3) (co J K ccnp))) X)) $;

--| Continuity in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jan-2018.)
theorem cnnei {w v p: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop) (wf X Y F)) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wral p (wral w (wrex v (wss (cima F (cv v)) (cv w)) (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv J cnei))) (cfv (csn (cfv (cv p) F)) (cfv K cnei))) X)) $;

--| A constant function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnconst2 (B2 J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel B2 Y)) (wcel (cxp X (csn B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| A constant function is continuous. (Contributed by FL, 15-Jan-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnconst (B2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wa (wcel B2 Y) (wf X (csn B2) F))) (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of a restriction from a subspace. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnrest (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wss A2 X)) (wcel (cres F A2) (co (co J A2 crest) K ccn)) $;

--| Equivalence of continuity in the parent topology and continuity in a subspace. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnrest2 (B2 F J K Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wss (crn F) B2) (wss B2 Y)) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel F (co J (co K B2 crest) ccn))) $;

--| Equivalence of continuity in the parent topology and continuity in a subspace. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnrest2r (B2 J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K ctop) (wss (co J (co K B2 crest) ccn) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| One direction of ~ cnprest under the weaker condition that the point is in the subset rather than the interior of the subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem cnpresti (A2 P F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 X) (wcel P A2) (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp)))) (wcel (cres F A2) (cfv P (co (co J A2 crest) K ccnp))) $;

--| Equivalence of continuity at a point and continuity of the restricted function at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.)
theorem cnprest (A2 P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wa (wcel P (cfv A2 (cfv J cnt))) (wf X Y F))) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel (cres F A2) (cfv P (co (co J A2 crest) K ccnp)))) $;

--| Equivalence of point-continuity in the parent topology and point-continuity in a subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnprest2 (B2 P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ctop) (wf X B2 F) (wss B2 Y)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel F (cfv P (co J (co K B2 crest) ccnp)))) $;

--| Every function is continuous when the domain is discrete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cndis (A2 J V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon))) (wceq (co (cpw A2) J ccn) (co X A2 cmap)) $;

--| Every function is continuous when the codomain is indiscrete (trivial). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnindis (A2 J V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co J (cpr c0 A2) ccn) (co A2 X cmap)) $;

--| If ` A ` is an isolated point in ` X ` (or equivalently, the singleton ` { A } ` is open in ` X ` ), then every function is continuous at ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnpdis (A2 J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (csn A2) J)) (wceq (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp)) (co Y X cmap)) $;

--| Pasting lemma. If ` A ` and ` B ` are closed sets in ` X ` with ` A u. B = X ` , then any function whose restrictions to ` A ` and ` B ` are continuous is continuous on all of ` X ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem paste (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cun A2 B2) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F A2) (co (co J A2 crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F B2) (co (co J B2 crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| If ` F ` converges, then ` F ` is a partial function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmfpm (P F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wbr F P (cfv J clm))) (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) $;

--| Inclusion of a function having a limit (used to ensure the limit relation is a set, under our definition). (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmfss (P F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wbr F P (cfv J clm))) (wss F (cxp cc X)) $;

--| Closure of a limit. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmcl (P F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wbr F P (cfv J clm))) (wcel P X) $;

--| Limit on a subspace. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmss (ph: wff) (P F J K M V Y Z: class):
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wbr F P (cfv K clm))) $;

--| A finer topology has fewer convergent sequences (but the sequences that do converge, converge to the same value). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2015.)
theorem sslm (J K X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wss (cfv K clm) (cfv J clm)) $;

--| A function converges iff its restriction to an upper integers set converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmres (ph: wff) (P F J M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wbr (cres F (cfv M cuz)) P (cfv J clm))) $;

--| If ` F ` converges, there is some upper integer set on which ` F ` is a total function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmff {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (cfv J clm))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) X (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $;

--| Any convergent sequence of points in a subset of a topological space converges to a point in the closure of the subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmcls {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P S F J M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| Any convergent sequence of points in a closed subset of a topological space converges to a point in the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmcld {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P S F J M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P S) $;

--| The image of a convergent sequence under a continuous map is convergent to the image of the original point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2014.)
theorem lmcnp (ph: wff) (P F G J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom G F) (cfv P G) (cfv K clm)) $;

--| The image of a convergent sequence under a continuous map is convergent to the image of the original point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2014.)
theorem lmcn (ph: wff) (P F G J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom G F) (cfv P G) (cfv K clm)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all T0 spaces.
term ct0: class;

--| Extend class notation to include T_1 spaces (also called Fr&eacute;chet spaces).
term ct1: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all Hausdorff spaces.
term cha: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all regular topologies.
term creg: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all normal topologies.
term cnrm: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all completely normal topologies.
term ccnrm: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all perfectly normal topologies.
term cpnrm: class;

--| Define T_0 or Kolmogorov spaces. A T_0 space satisfies a kind of "topological extensionality" principle (compare ~ ax-ext ): any two points which are members of the same open sets are equal, or in contraposition, for any two distinct points there is an open set which contains one point but not the other. This differs from T_1 spaces (see ~ ist1-2 ) in that in a T_1 space you can choose which point will be in the open set and which outside; in a T_0 space you only know that one of the two points is in the set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
axiom df_t0 {x3 y1 j o: setvar}:
  $ wceq ct0 (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral o (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv o))) (cv j)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cuni (cv j))) (cuni (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| The class of all T_1 spaces, also called Fr&eacute;chet spaces. Morris, Topology without tears, p. 30 ex. 3. (Contributed by FL, 18-Jun-2007.)
axiom df_t1 {x3 a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ct1 (crab x3 (wral a (wcel (csn (cv a)) (cfv (cv x3) ccld)) (cuni (cv x3))) ctop) $;

--| Define the class of all Hausdorff (or T_2) spaces. A Hausdorff space is a topology in which distinct points have disjoint open neighborhoods. Definition of Hausdorff space in [Munkres] p. 98. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
axiom df_haus {x3 y1 j m n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cha (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex n (wrex m (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv n)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv m)) (wceq (cin (cv n) (cv m)) c0)) (cv j)) (cv j))) (cuni (cv j))) (cuni (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| Define regular spaces. A space is regular if a point and a closed set can be separated by neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
axiom df_reg {x3 y1 z j: setvar}:
  $ wceq creg (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv j) ccl)) (cv x3))) (cv j)) (cv x3)) (cv j)) ctop) $;

--| Define normal spaces. A space is normal if disjoint closed sets can be separated by neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
axiom df_nrm {x3 y1 z j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnrm (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wss (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv j) ccl)) (cv x3))) (cv j)) (cin (cfv (cv j) ccld) (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv j)) ctop) $;

--| Define completely normal spaces. A space is completely normal if all its subspaces are normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_cnrm {x3 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccnrm (crab j (wral x3 (wcel (co (cv j) (cv x3) crest) cnrm) (cpw (cuni (cv j)))) ctop) $;

--| Define perfectly normal spaces. A space is perfectly normal if it is normal and every closed set is a G_&delta; set, meaning that it is a countable intersection of open sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_pnrm {f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpnrm (crab j (wss (cfv (cv j) ccld) (crn (cmpt f2 (co (cv j) cn cmap) (cint (crn (cv f2)))))) cnrm) $;

--| The predicate "is a T_0 space". Every pair of distinct points is topologically distinguishable. For the way this definition is usually encountered, see ~ ist0-3 . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem ist0 {x3 y1 o: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ct0) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral o (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv o))) J) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) X) X)) $;

--| The predicate "is a T_1 space". (Contributed by FL, 18-Jun-2007.)
theorem ist1 {a: setvar} (J: class) (X: class a):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ct1) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral a (wcel (csn (cv a)) (cfv J ccld)) X)) $;

--| The predicate "is a Hausdorff space". (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem ishaus {x3 y1 m n: setvar} (J: class) (X: class m n):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cha) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex n (wrex m (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv n)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv m)) (wceq (cin (cv n) (cv m)) c0)) J) J)) X) X)) $;

--| The property of being completely or hereditarily normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscnrm {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ccnrm) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cnrm) (cpw X))) $;

--| Any two topologically indistinguishable points in a T_0 space are identical. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem t0sep {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct0) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wi (wral x3 (wb (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel B2 (cv x3))) J) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Any two distinct points in a T_0 space are topologically distinguishable. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t0dist {o: setvar} (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct0) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wne A2 B2))) (wrex o (wn (wb (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wcel B2 (cv o)))) J) $;

--| In a T_1 space, singletons are closed. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t1sncld (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct1) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (csn A2) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| In a T_1 space, finite sets are closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem t1ficld (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ct1) (wss A2 X) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| Neighborhood property of a Hausdorff space. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem hausnei {m n: setvar} (P Q J: class) (X: class m n):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (w3a (wcel P X) (wcel Q X) (wne P Q))) (wrex n (wrex m (w3a (wcel P (cv n)) (wcel Q (cv m)) (wceq (cin (cv n) (cv m)) c0)) J) J) $;

--| A T_0 space is a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t0top (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ct0) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A T_1 space is a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t1top (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ct1) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A Hausdorff space is a topology. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2007.)
theorem haustop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The predicate "is a regular space". In a regular space, any open neighborhood has a closed subneighborhood. Note that some authors require the space to be Hausdorff (which would make it the same as T_3), but we reserve the phrase "regular Hausdorff" for that as many topologists do. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem isreg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J creg) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv z) (cfv J ccl)) (cv x3))) J) (cv x3)) J)) $;

--| A regular space is a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem regtop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J creg) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| In a regular space, every neighborhood of a point contains a closed subneighborhood. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem regsep {x3: setvar} (A2 U J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J creg) (wcel U J) (wcel A2 U)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl)) U)) J) $;

--| The predicate "is a normal space." Much like the case for regular spaces, normal does not imply Hausdorff or even regular. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem isnrm {x3 y1 z: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cnrm) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wa (wss (cv y1) (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv z) (cfv J ccl)) (cv x3))) J) (cin (cfv J ccld) (cpw (cv x3)))) J)) $;

--| A normal space is a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem nrmtop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cnrm) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A completely normal space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnrmtop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ccnrm) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The property of being completely or hereditarily normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem iscnrm2 {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ccnrm) (wral x3 (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cnrm) (cpw X))) $;

--| The property of being perfectly normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ispnrm {f2: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cpnrm) (wa (wcel J cnrm) (wss (cfv J ccld) (crn (cmpt f2 (co J cn cmap) (cint (crn (cv f2))))))) $;

--| A perfectly normal space is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem pnrmnrm (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cpnrm) (wcel J cnrm) $;

--| A perfectly normal space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem pnrmtop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cpnrm) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A closed set in a perfectly normal space is a countable intersection of open sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem pnrmcld {f2: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cpnrm) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld))) (wrex f2 (wceq A2 (cint (crn (cv f2)))) (co J cn cmap)) $;

--| An open set in a perfectly normal space is a countable union of closed sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem pnrmopn {f2: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cpnrm) (wcel A2 J)) (wrex f2 (wceq A2 (cuni (crn (cv f2)))) (co (cfv J ccld) cn cmap)) $;

--| The predicate "is a T_0 space". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist0_2 {x3 y1 o: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral o (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv o))) J) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) X) X)) $;

--| The predicate "is a T_0 space" expressed in more familiar terms. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem ist0_3 {x3 y1 o: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct0) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex o (wo (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv o)))) (wa (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv o))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv o)))) J)) X) X)) $;

--| The preimage of a T_0 topology under an injective map is T_0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnt0 (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ct0) (wf1 X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel J ct0) $;

--| An alternate characterization of T_1 spaces. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist1_2 {x3 y1 o: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct1) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral o (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv o))) J) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) X) X)) $;

--| A T_1 space is a T_0 space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t1t0 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ct1) (wcel J ct0) $;

--| A space is T_1 iff every point is the only point in the intersection of all open sets containing that point. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist1_3 {x3 o: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct1) (wral x3 (wceq (cint (crab o (wcel (cv x3) (cv o)) J)) (csn (cv x3))) X)) $;

--| The preimage of a T_1 topology under an injective map is T_1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnt1 (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ct1) (wf1 X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel J ct1) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` J ` is a Hausdorff space." (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem ishaus2 {x3 y1 m n: setvar} (J: class) (X: class m n):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J cha) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex n (wrex m (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cv n)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv m)) (wceq (cin (cv n) (cv m)) c0)) J) J)) X) X)) $;

--| A Hausdorff space is a T_1 space. (Contributed by FL, 11-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem haust1 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wcel J ct1) $;

--| The Hausdorff condition still holds if one considers general neighborhoods instead of open sets. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.)
theorem hausnei2 {x3 y1 v u: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J cha) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex u (wrex v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cfv (csn (cv y1)) (cfv J cnei))) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)))) X) X)) $;

--| The preimage of a Hausdorff topology under an injective map is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnhaus (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cha) (wf1 X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel J cha) $;

--| In a normal space, given a closed set ` B ` inside an open set ` A ` , there is an open set ` x ` such that ` B C_ x C_ cls ( x ) C_ A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmsep3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cnrm) (w3a (wcel A2 J) (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) (wss B2 A2))) (wrex x3 (wa (wss B2 (cv x3)) (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl)) A2)) J) $;

--| In a normal space, any two disjoint closed sets have the property that each one is a subset of an open set whose closure is disjoint from the other. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmsep2 {x3: setvar} (C D J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cnrm) (w3a (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) (wcel D (cfv J ccld)) (wceq (cin C D) c0))) (wrex x3 (wa (wss C (cv x3)) (wceq (cin (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl)) D) c0)) J) $;

--| In a normal space, disjoint closed sets are separated by open sets. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem nrmsep {x3 y1: setvar} (C D J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cnrm) (w3a (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) (wcel D (cfv J ccld)) (wceq (cin C D) c0))) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (w3a (wss C (cv x3)) (wss D (cv y1)) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0)) J) J) $;

--| An alternate characterization of normality. This is the important property in the proof of Urysohn's lemma. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem isnrm2 {o c d: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cnrm) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral c (wral d (wi (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (wrex o (wa (wss (cv c) (cv o)) (wceq (cin (cfv (cv o) (cfv J ccl)) (cv d)) c0)) J)) (cfv J ccld)) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| A topological space is normal iff any two disjoint closed sets are separated by open sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem isnrm3 {x3 y1 c d: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cnrm) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral c (wral d (wi (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (w3a (wss (cv c) (cv x3)) (wss (cv d) (cv y1)) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0)) J) J)) (cfv J ccld)) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| A subspace of a completely normal space is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnrmi (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccnrm) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) cnrm) $;

--| A completely normal space is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnrmnrm (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ccnrm) (wcel J cnrm) $;

--| A subspace of a completely normal space is completely normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem restcnrm (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccnrm) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) ccnrm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resthaus and similar theorems. If the topological property ` A ` is preserved under injective preimages, then property ` A ` passes to subspaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem resthauslem (A2 S J V: class):
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J A2) (wf1 (cin S (cuni J)) (cin S (cuni J)) (cres cid (cin S (cuni J)))) (wcel (cres cid (cin S (cuni J))) (co (co J S crest) J ccn))) (wcel (co J S crest) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J A2) (wcel S V)) (wcel (co J S crest) A2) $;

--| The limit points of the closure of a subset are the same as the limit points of the set in a T_1 space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem lpcls (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct1) (wss S X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv J clp)) (cfv S (cfv J clp))) $;

--| A subset of a perfect space is perfect iff its closure is perfect (and the closure is an actual perfect set, since it is both closed and perfect in the subspace topology). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem perfcls (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct1) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel (co J S crest) cperf) (wcel (co J (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) crest) cperf)) $;

--| A subspace of a T_0 topology is T_0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem restt0 (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct0) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) ct0) $;

--| A subspace of a T_1 topology is T_1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem restt1 (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct1) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) ct1) $;

--| A subspace of a Hausdorff topology is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem resthaus (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) cha) $;

--| Any two points in a T_1 space which have no separation are equal. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t1sep2 {o: setvar} (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ct1) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wi (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wcel B2 (cv o))) J) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Any two distinct points in a T_1 space are separated by an open set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.)
theorem t1sep {o: setvar} (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ct1) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wne A2 B2))) (wrex o (wa (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wn (wcel B2 (cv o)))) J) $;

--| A singleton is closed in a Hausdorff space. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem sncld (P J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (wcel P X)) (wcel (csn P) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sshaus and similar theorems. If the topological property ` A ` is preserved under injective preimages, then a topology finer than one with property ` A ` also has property ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem sshauslem (A2 J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J A2) (wf1 X X (cres cid X)) (wcel (cres cid X) (co K J ccn))) (wcel K A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J A2) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wcel K A2) $;

--| A topology finer than a T_0 topology is T_0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem sst0 (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ct0) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wcel K ct0) $;

--| A topology finer than a T_1 topology is T_1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem sst1 (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ct1) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wcel K ct1) $;

--| A topology finer than a Hausdorff topology is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem sshaus (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cha) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wcel K cha) $;

--| In a regular space, a closed set is separated by open sets from a point not in it. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem regsep2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 C J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J creg) (w3a (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) (wcel A2 X) (wn (wcel A2 C)))) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (w3a (wss C (cv x3)) (wcel A2 (cv y1)) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0)) J) J) $;

--| A topological space is regular if any closed set is separated from any point not in it by neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Feb-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem isreg2 {x3 o p c: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J creg) (wral c (wral x3 (wi (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv c))) (wrex o (wrex p (w3a (wss (cv c) (cv o)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv p)) (wceq (cin (cv o) (cv p)) c0)) J) J)) X) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| If a continuous mapping to a T_1 space is constant on a dense subset, it is constant on the entire space. Note that ` ( ( cls `` J ) `` A ) = X ` means " ` A ` is dense in ` X ` " and ` A C_ ( ``' F " { P } ) ` means " ` F ` is constant on ` A ` " (see ~ funconstss ). (Contributed by NM, 15-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem dnsconst (A2 P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel K ct1) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (w3a (wcel P Y) (wss A2 (cima (ccnv F) (csn P))) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) X))) (wf X (csn P) F) $;

--| The order topology is T_1 for any poset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtt1 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cps) (wcel (cfv R cordt) ct1) $;

--| A sequence in a Hausdorff space converges to at most one limit. Part of Lemma 1.4-2(a) of [Kreyszig] p. 26. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmo (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F B2 (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| The convergence relation is function-like in a Hausdorff space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2013.)
theorem lmfun (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wfun (cfv J clm)) $;

--| A discrete topology is Hausdorff. Morris, Topology without tears, p.72, ex. 13. (Contributed by FL, 24-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2015.)
theorem dishaus (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cpw A2) cha) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordthaus . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordthauslem {m n: setvar} (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ctsr) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 R) (wi (wne A2 B2) (wrex m (wrex n (w3a (wcel A2 (cv m)) (wcel B2 (cv n)) (wceq (cin (cv m) (cv n)) c0)) (cfv R cordt)) (cfv R cordt)))) $;

--| The order topology of a total order is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordthaus (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctsr) (wcel (cfv R cordt) cha) $;

--| The topology of the extended reals is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Mar-2017.)
theorem xrhaus:
  $ wcel (cfv cle cordt) cha $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all compact spaces.
term ccmp: class;

--| Definition of a compact topology. A topology is compact iff any open covering of its underlying set contains a finite subcovering (Heine-Borel property). Definition C''' of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.59. Note: Bourbaki uses the term "quasi-compact" (saving "compact" for "compact Hausdorff"), but it is not the modern usage (which we follow). (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.)
axiom df_cmp {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccmp (crab x3 (wral y1 (wi (wceq (cuni (cv x3)) (cuni (cv y1))) (wrex z (wceq (cuni (cv x3)) (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw (cv y1)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))) ctop) $;

--| The predicate "is a compact topology". (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem iscmp {y1 z: setvar} (J: class) (X: class y1 z):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ccmp) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral y1 (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (wrex z (wceq X (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw (cv y1)) cfn))) (cpw J))) $;

--| An open cover of a compact topology has a finite subcover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 29-Jun-2009.)
theorem cmpcov {s1: setvar} (S J: class) (X: class s1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wss S J) (wceq X (cuni S))) (wrex s1 (wceq X (cuni (cv s1))) (cin (cpw S) cfn)) $;

--| Rewrite ~ cmpcov for the cover ` { y e. J | ph } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem cmpcov2 {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (J: class) (X: class y1 s1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) J) X)) (wrex s1 (wa (wceq X (cuni (cv s1))) (wral y1 ph (cv s1))) (cin (cpw J) cfn)) $;

--| Combine ~ cmpcov with ~ ac6sfi to show the existence of a function that indexes the elements that are generating the open cover. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem cmpcovf {x3 y1 z f2 s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (ps: wff x3 y1 f2) (A2: class) (J: class f2) (X: class y1 z f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wrex z ph A2)) J) X)) (wrex s1 (wa (wceq X (cuni (cv s1))) (wex f2 (wa (wf (cv s1) A2 (cv f2)) (wral y1 ps (cv s1))))) (cin (cpw J) cfn)) $;

--| Compactness is respected by a continuous onto map. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cncmp (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wfo X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel K ccmp) $;

--| A finite topology is compact. (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.)
theorem fincmp (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cin ctop cfn)) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| The singleton of the empty set is compact. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.)
theorem _0cmp:
  $ wcel (csn c0) ccmp $;

--| A compact topology is a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 29-Jun-2009.)
theorem cmptop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ccmp) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The image of a compact set under a continuous function is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem rncmp (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel (co K (crn F) crest) ccmp) $;

--| The image of a compact set under a continuous function is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem imacmp (A2 F J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) ccmp)) (wcel (co K (cima F A2) crest) ccmp) $;

--| A discrete topology is compact iff the base set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem discmp (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cpw A2) ccmp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cmpsub . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jun-2009.)
theorem cmpsublem {t s1 c d: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wi (wral c (wi (wss S (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wss S (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw J)) (wral s1 (wi (wceq (cuni (co J S crest)) (cuni (cv s1))) (wrex t (wceq (cuni (co J S crest)) (cuni (cv t))) (cin (cpw (cv s1)) cfn))) (cpw (co J S crest)))) $;

--| Two equivalent ways of describing a compact subset of a topological space. Inspired by Sue E. Goodman's _Beginning Topology_. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Jun-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem cmpsub {c d: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel (co J S crest) ccmp) (wral c (wi (wss S (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wss S (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw J))) $;

--| A topology generated by a basis is compact iff open covers drawn from the basis have finite subcovers. (See also ~ alexsub , which further specializes to subbases, assuming the ultrafilter lemma.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgcmp {y1 z: setvar} (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 ctb) (wceq X (cuni B2))) (wb (wcel (cfv B2 ctg) ccmp) (wral y1 (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (wrex z (wceq X (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw (cv y1)) cfn))) (cpw B2))) $;

--| A closed subset of a compact space is compact. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 29-Jun-2009.)
theorem cmpcld (S J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wcel S (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (co J S crest) ccmp) $;

--| The union of two compact sets is compact. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Jan-2010.)
theorem uncmp (S T J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wceq X (cun S T))) (wa (wcel (co J S crest) ccmp) (wcel (co J T crest) ccmp))) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| A finite union of compact sets is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem fiuncmp {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (co J B2 crest) ccmp) A2)) (wcel (co J (ciun x3 A2 B2) crest) ccmp) $;

--| A subset of a compact topology (i.e. a coarser topology) is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem sscmp (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K ccmp) (wss J K)) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hauscmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Nov-2013.)
theorem hauscmplem {y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S J: class) (O: class y1 w) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab y1 (wrex w (wa (wcel A2 (cv w)) (wss (cfv (cv w) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif X (cv y1)))) J) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J S crest) ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif X S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wa (wcel A2 (cv z)) (wss (cfv (cv z) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif X S))) J) $;

--| A compact subspace of a T2 space is closed. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 16-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.)
theorem hauscmp (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cha) (wss S X) (wcel (co J S crest) ccmp)) (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| If a topology is compact and a collection of closed sets has the finite intersection property, its intersection is nonempty. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 25-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem cmpfi {x3: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel J ccmp) (wral x3 (wi (wn (wcel c0 (cfv (cv x3) cfi))) (wne (cint (cv x3)) c0)) (cpw (cfv J ccld)))) $;

--| In a compact topology, a system of closed sets with nonempty finite intersections has a nonempty intersection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem cmpfii (J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wss X (cfv J ccld)) (wn (wcel c0 (cfv X cfi)))) (wne (cint X) c0) $;

--| The glorious Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem. The first general topology theorem ever proved. The first mention of this theorem can be found in a course by Weierstrass from 1865. In his course Weierstrass called it a lemma. He didn't know how famous this theorem would be. He used a Euclidean space instead of a general compact space. And he was not aware of the Heine-Borel property. But the concepts of neighborhood and limit point were already there although not precisely defined. Cantor was one of his students. He published and used the theorem in an article from 1872. The rest of the general topology followed from that. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.) Revised by BL to significantly shorten the proof and avoid infinity, regularity, and choice. (Revised by Brendan Leahy, 26-Dec-2018.)
theorem bwth {x3: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wss A2 X) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wrex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv A2 (cfv J clp))) X) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all connected topologies.
term cconn: class;

--| Topologies are connected when only ` (/) ` and ` U. j ` are both open and closed. (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2008.)
axiom df_conn {j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cconn (crab j (wceq (cin (cv j) (cfv (cv j) ccld)) (cpr c0 (cuni (cv j)))) ctop) $;

--| The predicate ` J ` is a connected topology . (Contributed by FL, 17-Nov-2008.)
theorem isconn (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cconn) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wceq (cin J (cfv J ccld)) (cpr c0 X))) $;

--| The predicate ` J ` is a connected topology . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem isconn2 (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cconn) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss (cin J (cfv J ccld)) (cpr c0 X))) $;

--| The only nonempty clopen set of a connected topology is the whole space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem connclo (ph: wff) (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 X) $;

--| If a topology is connected, its underlying set can't be partitioned into two nonempty non-overlapping open sets. (Contributed by FL, 16-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem conndisj (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cun A2 B2) X) $;

--| A connected topology is a topology. (Contributed by FL, 22-Dec-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.)
theorem conntop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cconn) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The indiscrete topology (or trivial topology) on any set is connected. (Contributed by FL, 5-Jan-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indisconn (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cpr c0 A2) cconn $;

--| An alternate definition of connectedness. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem dfconn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J cconn) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (w3a (wne (cv x3) c0) (wne (cv y1) c0) (wceq (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0)) (wne (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) X)) J) J)) $;

--| Connectedness for a subspace. See ~ connsub . (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem connsuba {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (w3a (wne (cin (cv x3) A2) c0) (wne (cin (cv y1) A2) c0) (wceq (cin (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) c0)) (wne (cin (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) A2)) J) J)) $;

--| Two equivalent ways of saying that a subspace topology is connected. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem connsub {x3 y1: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel (co J S crest) cconn) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (w3a (wne (cin (cv x3) S) c0) (wne (cin (cv y1) S) c0) (wss (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cdif X S))) (wn (wss S (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))))) J) J)) $;

--| Connectedness is respected by a continuous onto map. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnconn (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cconn) (wfo X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel K cconn) $;

--| Disconnectedness for a subspace. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem nconnsubb (ph: wff) (A2 U J V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cin U A2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cin V A2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (cin U V) A2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cun U V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn)) $;

--| If a clopen set meets a connected subspace, it must contain the entire subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem connsubclo (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cin B2 A2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $;

--| The image of a connected set is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem connima (ph: wff) (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co K (cima F A2) crest) cconn) $;

--| A continuous function from a connected topology with one point in a clopen set must lie entirely within the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem conncn (ph: wff) (A2 U F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv K ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 F) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X U F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iunconn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2014.)
theorem iunconnlem {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (P U J V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss B2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cin V (ciun k A2 B2)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cin U V) (cdif X (ciun k A2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciun k A2 B2) (cun U V)) $ >
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel P U)) $;

--| The indexed union of connected overlapping subspaces sharing a common point is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2014.)
theorem iunconn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (P J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss B2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J (ciun k A2 B2) crest) cconn) $;

--| The union of two connected overlapping subspaces is connected. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jun-2014.)
theorem unconn (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wa (wss A2 X) (wss B2 X)) (wne (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wi (wa (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn) (wcel (co J B2 crest) cconn)) (wcel (co J (cun A2 B2) crest) cconn)) $;

--| The closure of a connected set is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem clsconn (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss A2 X) (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn)) (wcel (co J (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) crest) cconn) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` contains ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem conncompid {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel A2 S) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem conncompconn {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (co J S crest) cconn) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` is a superset of any other connected set containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem conncompss {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S T: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss T X) (wcel A2 T) (wcel (co J T crest) cconn)) (wss T S) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` is a closed set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem conncompcld {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` is a subset of any clopen set containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem conncompclo {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S T: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel T (cin J (cfv J ccld))) (wcel A2 T)) (wss S T) $;

--| A connected T_1 space is perfect, unless it is the topology of a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem t1connperf (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ct1) (wcel J cconn) (wn (wbr X c1o cen))) (wcel J cperf) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of all first-countable topologies.
term c1stc: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of all second-countable topologies.
term c2ndc: class;

--| Define the class of all first-countable topologies. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Aug-2009.)
axiom df_1stc {x3 y1 z j: setvar}:
  $ wceq c1stc (crab j (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) com cdom) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv x3) (cuni (cin (cv y1) (cpw (cv z)))))) (cv j))) (cpw (cv j))) (cuni (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| Define the class of all second-countable topologies. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Jan-2010.)
axiom df_2ndc {x3 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq c2ndc (cab j (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ctg) (cv j))) ctb)) $;

--| The predicate "is a first-countable topology." This can be described as "every point has a countable local basis" - that is, every point has a countable collection of open sets containing it such that every open set containing the point has an open set from this collection as a subset. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Aug-2009.)
theorem is1stc {x3 y1 z: setvar} (J: class) (X: class y1 z):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J c1stc) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) com cdom) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv x3) (cuni (cin (cv y1) (cpw (cv z)))))) J)) (cpw J)) X)) $;

--| An equivalent way of saying "is a first-countable topology." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Aug-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem is1stc2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (J: class w) (X: class y1 z w):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J c1stc) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) com cdom) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv w)) (wss (cv w) (cv z))) (cv y1))) J)) (cpw J)) X)) $;

--| A first-countable topology is a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Aug-2009.)
theorem _1stctop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J c1stc) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A property of points in a first-countable topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Aug-2009.)
theorem _1stcclb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J c1stc) (wcel A2 X)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wral y1 (wi (wcel A2 (cv y1)) (wrex z (wa (wcel A2 (cv z)) (wss (cv z) (cv y1))) (cv x3))) J)) (cpw J)) $;

--| For any point ` A ` in a first-countable topology, there is a function ` f : NN --> J ` enumerating neighborhoods of ` A ` which is decreasing and forms a local base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _1stcfb {y1 f2 k: setvar} (A2 J: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J c1stc) (wcel A2 X)) (wex f2 (w3a (wf cn J (cv f2)) (wral k (wa (wcel A2 (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))) (wss (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))) cn) (wral y1 (wi (wcel A2 (cv y1)) (wrex k (wss (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) cn)) J))) $;

--| The property of being second-countable. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem is2ndc {x3: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J c2ndc) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ctg) J)) ctb) $;

--| A second-countable topology is a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _2ndctop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J c2ndc) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A countable basis generates a second-countable topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _2ndci (B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 ctb) (wbr B2 com cdom)) (wcel (cfv B2 ctg) c2ndc) $;

--| Having a countable subbase is a sufficient condition for second-countability. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _2ndcsb {x3: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J c2ndc) (wex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg) J))) $;

--| A second-countable space has at most the cardinality of the continuum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2ndcredom (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J c2ndc) (wbr J cr cdom) $;

--| A second-countable space is first-countable. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Jan-2010.)
theorem _2ndc1stc (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J c2ndc) (wcel J c1stc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 1stcrest . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem _1stcrestlem {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wbr B2 com cdom) (wbr (crn (cmpt x3 B2 C)) com cdom) $;

--| A subspace of a first-countable space is first-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _1stcrest (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J c1stc) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) c1stc) $;

--| A subspace of a second-countable space is second-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _2ndcrest (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J c2ndc) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co J A2 crest) c2ndc) $;

--| An image set of a countable set is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.) (Moved to the main part of set.mm by Vincent Gonzalez, 19-Aug-2026.)
theorem abrexct {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wbr A2 com cdom) (wbr (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2)) com cdom) $;

--| If a topology is second-countable, every base has a countable subset which is a base. Exercise 16B2 in Willard. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _2ndcctbss {w v u b c: setvar} (B2: class) (S: class w v u b c) (J: class w v u):
  $ wceq J (cfv B2 ctg) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab u v (w3a (wcel (cv u) (cv c)) (wcel (cv v) (cv c)) (wrex w (wa (wss (cv u) (cv w)) (wss (cv w) (cv v))) B2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 ctb) (wcel J c2ndc)) (wrex b (w3a (wbr (cv b) com cdom) (wss (cv b) B2) (wceq J (cfv (cv b) ctg))) ctb) $;

--| Any disjoint family of open sets in a second-countable space is countable. (The sets are required to be nonempty because otherwise there could be many empty sets in the family.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem _2ndcdisj {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (J: class y1):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J c2ndc) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cdif J (csn c0))) A2) (wal y1 (wrmo x3 (wcel (cv y1) B2) A2))) (wbr A2 com cdom) $;

--| Any disjoint collection of open sets in a second-countable space is countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem _2ndcdisj2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (J: class y1):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J c2ndc) (wss A2 J) (wal y1 (wrmo x3 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) A2))) (wbr A2 com cdom) $;

--| A surjective continuous open map maps second-countable spaces to second-countable spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2ndcomap {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K: class) (Y: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J c2ndc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) J)) (wcel (cima F (cv x3)) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K c2ndc) $;

--| A second-countable topology is separable, which is to say it contains a countable dense subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2ndcsep {x3: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J c2ndc) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl)) X)) (cpw X)) $;

--| A discrete space is second-countable iff it is countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem dis2ndc (X: class):
  $ wb (wbr X com cdom) (wcel (cpw X) c2ndc) $;

--| A point belongs to the closure of a subset iff there is a sequence in the subset converging to it. Theorem 1.4-6(a) of [Kreyszig] p. 30. This proof uses countable choice ~ ax-cc . A space satisfying the conclusion of this theorem is called a sequential space, so the theorem can also be stated as "every first-countable space is a sequential space". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _1stcelcls {f2: setvar} (P S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J c1stc) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wex f2 (wa (wf cn S (cv f2)) (wbr (cv f2) P (cfv J clm))))) $;

--| A mapping is continuous at ` P ` in a first-countable space ` X ` iff it is sequentially continuous at ` P ` , meaning that the image under ` F ` of every sequence converging at ` P ` converges to ` F ( P ) ` . This proof uses ~ ax-cc , but only via ~ 1stcelcls , so it could be refactored into a proof that continuity and sequential continuity are the same in sequential spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem _1stccnp {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J c1stc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wal f2 (wi (wa (wf cn X (cv f2)) (wbr (cv f2) P (cfv J clm))) (wbr (ccom F (cv f2)) (cfv P F) (cfv K clm)))))) $;

--| A mapping ` X --> Y ` , where ` X ` is first-countable, is continuous iff it is sequentially continuous, meaning that for any sequence ` f ( n ) ` converging to ` x ` , its image under ` F ` converges to ` F ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem _1stccn {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J c1stc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wal f2 (wi (wf cn X (cv f2)) (wal x3 (wi (wbr (cv f2) (cv x3) (cfv J clm)) (wbr (ccom F (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv K clm))))))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the "locally ` A ` " predicate of a topological space.
term clly (A2: class): class;

--| Extend class notation with the "N-locally ` A ` " predicate of a topological space.
term cnlly (A2: class): class;

--| Define a space that is locally ` A ` , where ` A ` is a topological property like "compact", "connected", or "path-connected". A topological space is locally ` A ` if every neighborhood of a point contains an open subneighborhood that is ` A ` in the subspace topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_lly {x3 y1 u j: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (clly A2) (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv u)) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv u) crest) A2)) (cin (cv j) (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv x3)) (cv j)) ctop) $;

--| Define a space that is n-locally ` A ` , where ` A ` is a topological property like "compact", "connected", or "path-connected". A topological space is n-locally ` A ` if every neighborhood of a point contains a subneighborhood that is ` A ` in the subspace topology. The terminology "n-locally", where 'n' stands for "neighborhood", is not standard, although this is sometimes called "weakly locally ` A ` ". The reason for the distinction is that some notions only make sense for arbitrary neighborhoods (such as "locally compact", which is actually ` N-Locally Comp ` in our terminology - open compact sets are not very useful), while others such as "locally connected" are strictly weaker notions if the neighborhoods are not required to be open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_nlly {x3 y1 u j: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cnlly A2) (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (wcel (co (cv j) (cv u) crest) A2) (cin (cfv (csn (cv y1)) (cfv (cv j) cnei)) (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv x3)) (cv j)) ctop) $;

--| The property of being a locally ` A ` topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem islly {x3 y1 u: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J (clly A2)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv u)) (wcel (co J (cv u) crest) A2)) (cin J (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv x3)) J)) $;

--| The property of being an n-locally ` A ` topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem isnlly {x3 y1 u: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J (cnlly A2)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (wcel (co J (cv u) crest) A2) (cin (cfv (csn (cv y1)) (cfv J cnei)) (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv x3)) J)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the ` Locally A ` predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (clly A2) (clly B2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the ` Locally A ` predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllyeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cnlly A2) (cnlly B2)) $;

--| A locally ` A ` space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llytop (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (clly A2)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A locally ` A ` space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllytop (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cnlly A2)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The property of a locally ` A ` topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyi {u: setvar} (A2 P U J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (clly A2)) (wcel U J) (wcel P U)) (wrex u (w3a (wss (cv u) U) (wcel P (cv u)) (wcel (co J (cv u) crest) A2)) J) $;

--| The property of an n-locally ` A ` topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllyi {u: setvar} (A2 P U J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cnlly A2)) (wcel U J) (wcel P U)) (wrex u (wa (wss (cv u) U) (wcel (co J (cv u) crest) A2)) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| Eliminate the neighborhood symbol from ~ nllyi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nlly2i {u s1: setvar} (A2 P U J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cnlly A2)) (wcel U J) (wcel P U)) (wrex s1 (wrex u (w3a (wcel P (cv u)) (wss (cv u) (cv s1)) (wcel (co J (cv s1) crest) A2)) J) (cpw U)) $;

--| A locally ` A ` space is n-locally ` A ` : the "n-locally" predicate is the weaker notion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llynlly (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (clly A2)) (wcel J (cnlly A2)) $;

--| A locally ` A ` space is n-locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyssnlly (A2: class):
  $ wss (clly A2) (cnlly A2) $;

--| The "locally" predicate respects inclusion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (clly A2) (clly B2)) $;

--| The "n-locally" predicate respects inclusion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllyss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cnlly A2) (cnlly B2)) $;

--| The property of a subspace being locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem subislly {x3 y1 u: setvar} (A2 B2 J V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wcel (co J B2 crest) (clly A2)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (w3a (wss (cin (cv u) B2) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv u)) (wcel (co J (cin (cv u) B2) crest) A2)) J) (cin (cv x3) B2)) J)) $;

--| If the property ` A ` passes to open subspaces, then a space is n-locally ` A ` iff it is locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem restnlly {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv x3) (cv j)))) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv x3) crest) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cnlly A2) (clly A2)) $;

--| If the property ` A ` passes to open subspaces, then a space which is ` A ` is also locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem restlly {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv x3) (cv j)))) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv x3) crest) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (clly A2)) $;

--| An alternative expression for ` J e. Locally A ` when ` A ` passes to open subspaces: A space is locally ` A ` if every point is contained in an open neighborhood with property ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem islly2 {x3 y1 u j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 J: class) (X: class x3 j):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv x3) (cv j)))) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv x3) crest) A2) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel J (clly A2)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral y1 (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv u)) (wcel (co J (cv u) crest) A2)) J) X))) $;

--| An open subspace of a locally ` A ` space is also locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyrest (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (clly A2)) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) (clly A2)) $;

--| An open subspace of an n-locally ` A ` space is also n-locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllyrest (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cnlly A2)) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) (cnlly A2)) $;

--| If ` A ` is a local property, then both ` Locally A ` and ` N-Locally A ` simplify to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem loclly (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (clly A2) A2) (wceq (cnlly A2) A2) $;

--| Idempotence of the "locally" predicate, i.e. being "locally ` A ` " is a local property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem llyidm (A2: class):
  $ wceq (clly (clly A2)) (clly A2) $;

--| Idempotence of the "n-locally" predicate, i.e. being "n-locally ` A ` " is a local property. (Use ~ loclly to show ` N-Locally N-Locally A = N-Locally A ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem nllyidm (A2: class):
  $ wceq (clly (cnlly A2)) (cnlly A2) $;

--| A topology is locally a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem toplly:
  $ wceq (clly ctop) ctop $;

--| A topology is n-locally a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem topnlly:
  $ wceq (cnlly ctop) ctop $;

--| A Hausdorff space is locally Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem hauslly (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wcel J (clly cha)) $;

--| A Hausdorff space is n-locally Hausdorff iff it is locally Hausdorff (both notions are thus referred to as "locally Hausdorff"). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem hausnlly (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J (cnlly cha)) (wcel J (clly cha)) $;

--| A compact Hausdorff space is locally compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem hausllycmp (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (wcel J ccmp)) (wcel J (cnlly ccmp)) $;

--| A closed subspace of a locally compact space is also locally compact. (The analogous result for open subspaces follows from the more general ~ nllyrest .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem cldllycmp (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cnlly ccmp)) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (co J A2 crest) (cnlly ccmp)) $;

--| First-countability is a local property (unlike second-countability). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem lly1stc:
  $ wceq (clly c1stc) c1stc $;

--| The discrete space ` ~P X ` is locally ` A ` if and only if every singleton space has property ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem dislly {x3: setvar} (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel (cpw X) (clly A2)) (wral x3 (wcel (cpw (csn (cv x3))) A2) X)) $;

--| A discrete space is locally compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem disllycmp (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cpw X) (clly ccmp)) $;

--| A discrete space is first-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem dis1stc (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cpw X) c1stc) $;

--| If ` X ` is a first-countable Hausdorff space, then the cardinality of the closure of a set ` A ` is bounded by ` NN ` to the power ` A ` . In particular, a first-countable Hausdorff space with a dense subset ` A ` has cardinality at most ` A ^ NN ` , and a separable first-countable Hausdorff space has cardinality at most ` ~P NN ` . (Compare ~ hauspwpwdom to see a weaker result if the assumption of first-countability is omitted.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem hausmapdom (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cha) (wcel J c1stc) (wss A2 X)) (wbr (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (co A2 cn cmap) cdom) $;

--| Simplify the cardinal ` A ^ NN ` of ~ hausmapdom to ` ~P B = 2 ^ B ` when ` B ` is an infinite cardinal greater than ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem hauspwdom (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J cha) (wcel J c1stc) (wss A2 X)) (wa (wbr A2 (cpw B2) cdom) (wbr cn B2 cdom))) (wbr (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (cpw B2) cdom) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the refinement relation.
term cref: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of point-finite covers.
term cptfin: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the class of locally finite covers.
term clocfin: class;

--| Define the refinement relation. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.)
axiom df_ref {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cref (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv y1)) (cuni (cv x3))) (wral z (wrex w (wss (cv z) (cv w)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define "point-finite." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
axiom df_ptfin {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cptfin (cab x3 (wral y1 (wcel (crab z (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv x3)) cfn) (cuni (cv x3)))) $;

--| Define "locally finite." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
axiom df_locfin {x3 y1 n s1 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq clocfin (cmpt x3 ctop (cab y1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv x3)) (cuni (cv y1))) (wral p (wrex n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wcel (crab s1 (wne (cin (cv s1) (cv n)) c0) (cv y1)) cfn)) (cv x3)) (cuni (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Refinement is a relation. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem refrel:
  $ wrel cref $;

--| The property of being a refinement of a cover. Dr. Nyikos once commented in class that the term "refinement" is actually misleading and that people are inclined to confuse it with the notion defined in ~ isfne . On the other hand, the two concepts do seem to have a dual relationship. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem isref {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (C X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq X (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 C) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wa (wceq Y X) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) B2) A2))) $;

--| A refinement covers the same set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem refbas (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni B2) $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wceq Y X) $;

--| Every set in a refinement has a superset in the original cover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem refssex {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wcel S A2)) (wrex x3 (wss S (cv x3)) B2) $;

--| A subcover is a refinement of the original cover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem ssref (A2 B2 C X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 C) (wss A2 B2) (wceq X Y)) (wbr A2 B2 cref) $;

--| Reflexivity of refinement. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.)
theorem refref (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wbr A2 A2 cref) $;

--| Refinement is transitive. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem reftr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wbr B2 C cref)) (wbr A2 C cref) $;

--| Adding the empty set preserves refinements. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem refun0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wne B2 c0)) (wbr (cun A2 (csn c0)) B2 cref) $;

--| The statement "is a point-finite cover." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem isptfin {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq X (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wb (wcel A2 cptfin) (wral x3 (wcel (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) cfn) X)) $;

--| The statement "is a locally finite cover." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem islocfin {x3 n s1: setvar} (A2: class) (J: class s1) (X: class n s1) (Y: class x3 n s1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wceq X Y) (wral x3 (wrex n (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv n)) (wcel (crab s1 (wne (cin (cv s1) (cv n)) c0) A2) cfn)) J) X)) $;

--| A finite cover is a point-finite cover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem finptfin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel A2 cptfin) $;

--| A point covered by a point-finite cover is only covered by finitely many elements. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem ptfinfin {x3: setvar} (A2 P X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cptfin) (wcel P X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wcel P (cv x3)) A2) cfn) $;

--| A finite cover of a topological space is a locally finite cover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem finlocfin (A2 J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq X Y)) (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) $;

--| A locally finite cover covers a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem locfintop (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A locally finite cover must cover the base set of its corresponding topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem locfinbas (A2 J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni A2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (wceq X Y) $;

--| A point covered by a locally finite cover has a neighborhood which intersects only finitely many elements of the cover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.)
theorem locfinnei {n s1: setvar} (A2: class) (P J: class s1) (X: class n s1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (wcel P X)) (wrex n (wa (wcel P (cv n)) (wcel (crab s1 (wne (cin (cv s1) (cv n)) c0) A2) cfn)) J) $;

--| A locally finite cover is point-finite. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem lfinpfin (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (wcel A2 cptfin) $;

--| Adding a finite set preserves locally finite covers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem lfinun (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv J clocfin)) (wcel B2 cfn) (wss (cuni B2) (cuni J))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cfv J clocfin)) $;

--| For a compact space, the locally finite covers are precisely the finite covers. Sadly, this property does not properly characterize all compact spaces. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem locfincmp (C J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ccmp) (wb (wcel C (cfv J clocfin)) (wa (wcel C cfn) (wceq X Y))) $;

--| Taking the union of the set of singletons recovers the initial set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Jan-2020.)
theorem unisngl {x3 u: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wceq C (cab u (wrex x3 (wceq (cv u) (csn (cv x3))) X)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni C) $;

--| The set of singletons is a refinement of any open covering of the discrete topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Jan-2020.)
theorem dissnref {x3 u: setvar} (C V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cab u (wrex x3 (wceq (cv u) (csn (cv x3))) X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cuni Y) X)) (wbr C Y cref) $;

--| The set of singletons is locally finite in the discrete topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Jan-2020.)
theorem dissnlocfin {x3 u: setvar} (C V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cab u (wrex x3 (wceq (cv u) (csn (cv x3))) X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel C (cfv (cpw X) clocfin)) $;

--| The locally finite covers of a discrete space are precisely the point-finite covers. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem locfindis (C X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni C) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (cfv (cpw X) clocfin)) (wa (wcel C cptfin) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| A locally finite cover in a coarser topology is locally finite in a finer topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 22-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem locfincf (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wss (cfv J clocfin) (cfv K clocfin)) $;

--| A space where every open cover has a point-finite subcover is compact. This is significant in part because it shows half of the proposition that if only half the generalization in the definition of metacompactness (and consequently paracompactness) is performed, one does not obtain any more spaces. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jan-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem comppfsc {c d: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel J ccmp) (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wa (wss (cv d) (cv c)) (wceq X (cuni (cv d)))) cptfin)) (cpw J))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the compact generator operation.
term ckgen: class;

--| Define the "compact generator", the functor from topological spaces to compactly generated spaces. Also known as the k-ification operation. A set is k-open, i.e. ` x e. ( kGen `` j ) ` , iff the preimage of ` x ` is open in all compact Hausdorff spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_kgen {x3 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ckgen (cmpt j ctop (crab x3 (wral k (wi (wcel (co (cv j) (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv k)) (co (cv j) (cv k) crest))) (cpw (cuni (cv j)))) (cpw (cuni (cv j))))) $;

--| Value of the compact generator. (The "k" in ` kGen ` comes from the name "k-space" for these spaces, after the German word kompakt.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenval {x3 k: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wceq (cfv J ckgen) (crab x3 (wral k (wi (wcel (co J (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv k)) (co J (cv k) crest))) (cpw X)) (cpw X))) $;

--| Value of the compact generator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem elkgen {k: setvar} (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv J ckgen)) (wa (wss A2 X) (wral k (wi (wcel (co J (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wcel (cin A2 (cv k)) (co J (cv k) crest))) (cpw X)))) $;

--| Property of the open sets in the compact generator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgeni (A2 J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv J ckgen)) (wcel (co J K crest) ccmp)) (wcel (cin A2 K) (co J K crest)) $;

--| The compact generator generates a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem kgentopon (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckgen) (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| The base set of the compact generator is the same as the original topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenuni (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq X (cuni (cfv J ckgen))) $;

--| The compact generator generates a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenftop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel (cfv J ckgen) ctop) $;

--| The compact generator is a function on topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenf:
  $ wf ctop ctop ckgen $;

--| A compactly generated space is a topology. (Note: henceforth we will use the idiom " ` J e. ran kGen ` " to denote " ` J ` is compactly generated", since as we will show a space is compactly generated iff it is in the range of the compact generator.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgentop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The compact generator generates a finer topology than the original. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenss (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wss J (cfv J ckgen)) $;

--| The compact generator generates another Hausdorff topology given a Hausdorff topology to start from. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenhaus (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wcel (cfv J ckgen) cha) $;

--| The compact generator topology is the same as the original topology on compact subspaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgencmp (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel (co J K crest) ccmp)) (wceq (co J K crest) (co (cfv J ckgen) K crest)) $;

--| The compact generator topology has the same compact sets as the original topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgencmp2 (J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wb (wcel (co J K crest) ccmp) (wcel (co (cfv J ckgen) K crest) ccmp)) $;

--| The compact generator is idempotent on compactly generated spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgenidm (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wceq (cfv J ckgen) J) $;

--| A space is compactly generated iff it contains its image under the compact generator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem iskgen2 (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss (cfv J ckgen) J)) $;

--| Derive the usual definition of "compactly generated". A topology is compactly generated if every subset of ` X ` that is open in every compact subset is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem iskgen3 {x3 k: setvar} (J: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wi (wral k (wi (wcel (co J (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv k)) (co J (cv k) crest))) (cpw X)) (wcel (cv x3) J)) (cpw X))) $;

--| A locally compact space is compactly generated. (This variant of ~ llycmpkgen uses the weaker definition of locally compact, "every point has a compact neighborhood", instead of "every point has a local base of compact neighborhoods".) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem llycmpkgen2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (J: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wrex k (wcel (co J (cv k) crest) ccmp) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (crn ckgen)) $;

--| A compact space is compactly generated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem cmpkgen (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ccmp) (wcel J (crn ckgen)) $;

--| A locally compact space is compactly generated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem llycmpkgen (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cnlly ccmp)) (wcel J (crn ckgen)) $;

--| The one-point compactification of ` NN ` is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _1stckgenlem (ph: wff) (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J (cun (crn F) (csn A2)) crest) ccmp) $;

--| A first-countable space is compactly generated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem _1stckgen (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J c1stc) (wcel J (crn ckgen)) $;

--| The compact generator preserves the subset (fineness) relationship on topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgen2ss (J K X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) (wss J K)) (wss (cfv J ckgen) (cfv K ckgen)) $;

--| A function from a compactly generated space is continuous iff it is continuous "on compacta". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgencn {k: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co (cfv J ckgen) K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral k (wi (wcel (co J (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wcel (cres F (cv k)) (co (co J (cv k) crest) K ccn))) (cpw X)))) $;

--| A function ` F : J --> K ` from a compactly generated space is continuous iff for all compact spaces ` z ` and continuous ` g : z --> J ` , the composite ` F o. g : z --> K ` is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgencn2 {z g2: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co (cfv J ckgen) K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral z (wral g2 (wcel (ccom F (cv g2)) (co (cv z) K ccn)) (co (cv z) J ccn)) ccmp))) $;

--| The set of continuous functions from ` J ` to ` K ` is unaffected by k-ification of ` K ` , if ` J ` is already compactly generated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgencn3 (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wcel K ctop)) (wceq (co J K ccn) (co J (cfv K ckgen) ccn)) $;

--| A continuous function is also continuous with the domain and codomain replaced by their compact generator topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem kgen2cn (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel F (co (cfv J ckgen) (cfv K ckgen) ccn)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the binary topological product operation.
term ctx: class;

--| Extend class notation with a function whose value is the compact-open topology.
term cxko: class;

--| Define the binary topological product, which is homeomorphic to the general topological product over a two element set, but is more convenient to use. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
axiom df_tx {x3 y1 s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctx (cmpo r s1 cvv cvv (cfv (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cv r) (cv s1) (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ctg)) $;

--| Define the compact-open topology, which is the natural topology on the set of continuous functions between two topological spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_xko {x3 v f2 k s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxko (cmpo s1 r ctop ctop (cfv (cfv (crn (cmpo k v (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv r) (cv x3) crest) ccmp) (cpw (cuni (cv r)))) (cv s1) (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv k)) (cv v)) (co (cv r) (cv s1) ccn)))) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| Value of the binary topological product operation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem txval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class x3 y1) (R S: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 R S (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel S W)) (wceq (co R S ctx) (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| The underlying set of the product of two topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem txuni2 {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class x3 y1) (R S X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 R S (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni B2) $;

--| The basis for the product topology is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txbasex {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class x3 y1) (R S: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 R S (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel S W)) (wcel B2 cvv) $;

--| The set of Cartesian products of elements from two topological bases is a basis. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2015.)
theorem txbas {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class x3 y1) (R S: class):
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 R S (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctb) (wcel S ctb)) (wcel B2 ctb) $;

--| A set in a product is open iff each point is surrounded by an open rectangle. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.)
theorem eltx {x3 y1 p: setvar} (S J K: class) (V W: class x3 y1 p):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel K W)) (wb (wcel S (co J K ctx)) (wral p (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv p) (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wss (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)) S)) K) J) S)) $;

--| The product of two topologies is a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem txtop (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wcel (co R S ctx) ctop) $;

--| The value of the product topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptval {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wfn F A2)) (wceq (cfv F cpt) (cfv B2 ctg)) $;

--| The preimage of a projection function can be expressed as an indexed cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptpjpre1 {w k: setvar} (A2 U F I V: class) (X: class k):
  $ wceq X (cixp k A2 (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wa (wcel I A2) (wcel U (cfv I F)))) (wceq (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv I (cv w)))) U) (cixp k A2 (cif (wceq (cv k) I) U (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))))) $;

--| Elementhood in the bases of a product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem elpt {x3 y1 z w g2 h: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z w g2 h) (S: class y1 z w) (F: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S B2) (wex h (wa (w3a (wfn (cv h) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv h)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex w (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv h)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv w))) cfn)) (wceq S (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv h)))))) $;

--| A basic open set in the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem elptr {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F: class) (G: class z) (V: class) (W: class x3 z g2):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wa (wfn G A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) G) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2)) (wa (wcel W cfn) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) G) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 W)))) (wcel (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) G)) B2) $;

--| A basic open set in the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem elptr2 {x3 y1 z g2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z g2) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (S: class z k) (F V: class) (W: class x3 z g2):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel S (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cdif A2 W))) (wceq S (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp k A2 S) B2) $;

--| The base set of the product topology is a basic open set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptbasid {x3 y1 z g2 k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wcel (cixp k A2 (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) B2) $;

--| The base set for the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptuni2 {x3 y1 z g2 k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wceq (cixp k A2 (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) (cuni B2)) $;

--| The basis for a product topology is closed under intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptbasin {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class) (X: class y1) (Y: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wa (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2))) (wcel (cin X Y) B2) $;

--| The basis for a product topology is closed under intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem ptbasin2 {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wceq (cfv B2 cfi) B2) $;

--| The basis for a product topology is a basis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptbas {x3 y1 z g2: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z g2) (F V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wcel B2 ctb) $;

--| The basis for a product topology is a basis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptpjpre2 {x3 y1 z w g2 n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z w g2) (U: class z) (F: class) (I: class z) (V: class) (X: class y1 n):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wa (wcel I A2) (wcel U (cfv I F)))) (wcel (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv I (cv w)))) U) B2) $;

--| The basis for the product topology can also be written as the set of finite intersections of "cylinder sets", the preimages of projections into one factor from open sets in the factor. (We have to add ` X ` itself to the list because if ` A ` is empty we get ` ( fi `` (/) ) = (/) ` while ` B = { (/) } ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptbasfi {x3 y1 z w u g2 k n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 z w g2) (F V: class) (X: class y1 n):
  $ wceq B2 (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) A2) (wral y1 (wcel (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) F)) A2) (wrex z (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cdif A2 (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wceq B2 (cfv (cun (csn X) (crn (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))))) cfi)) $;

--| The product topology is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem pttop (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wcel (cfv F cpt) ctop) $;

--| A basic open set in the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptopn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (S: class k) (F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel S (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cdif A2 W))) (wceq S (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp k A2 S) (cfv F cpt)) $;

--| A sub-basic open set in the product topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptopn2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (O: class k) (V Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O (cfv Y F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp k A2 (cif (wceq (cv k) Y) O (cuni (cfv (cv k) F)))) (cfv F cpt)) $;

--| Functionality of function ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkotf {x3 v f2 k: setvar} (R S: class) (T: class x3 v f2 k) (K: class x3 f2) (X: class v f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wcel (co R (cv x3) crest) ccmp) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo k v K S (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv k)) (cv v)) (co R S ccn))) $ >
  $ wf (cxp K S) (cpw (co R S ccn)) T $;

--| Alternative expression for the subbase of the compact-open topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkobval {x3 v f2 k s1: setvar} (R S: class) (T: class x3 v f2 k) (K: class x3 f2) (X: class v f2 s1):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wcel (co R (cv x3) crest) ccmp) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo k v K S (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv k)) (cv v)) (co R S ccn))) $ >
  $ wceq (crn T) (cab s1 (wrex k (wrex v (wa (wcel (co R (cv k) crest) ccmp) (wceq (cv s1) (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv k)) (cv v)) (co R S ccn)))) S) (cpw X))) $;

--| Value of the compact-open topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoval {x3 v f2 k: setvar} (R S: class) (T: class x3 v f2 k) (K: class x3 f2) (X: class v f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wcel (co R (cv x3) crest) ccmp) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpo k v K S (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) (cv k)) (cv v)) (co R S ccn))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wceq (co S R cxko) (cfv (cfv (crn T) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| The compact-open topology is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkotop (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wcel (co S R cxko) ctop) $;

--| A basic open set of the compact-open topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoopn {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2 R S U: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co R A2 crest) ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab f2 (wss (cima (cv f2) A2) U) (co R S ccn)) (co S R cxko)) $;

--| The product of two topologies is a topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
theorem txtopi (R S: class):
  $ wcel R ctop $ >
  $ wcel S ctop $ >
  $ wcel (co R S ctx) ctop $;

--| The underlying set of the product of two topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txtopon (R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel (co R S ctx) (cfv (cxp X Y) ctopon)) $;

--| The underlying set of the product of two topologies. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem txuni (R S X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni (co R S ctx))) $;

--| The underlying set of the product of two topologies. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
theorem txunii (R S X Y: class):
  $ wcel R ctop $ >
  $ wcel S ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni (co R S ctx)) $;

--| The base set for the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptuni {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class) (J: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 (cuni (cfv (cv x3) F))) (cuni J)) $;

--| Base set of a product topology given by substitution. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptunimpt {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (J K V: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 K) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel K ctop) A2)) (wceq (cixp x3 A2 (cuni K)) (cuni J)) $;

--| The base set for the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pttopon {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 J K V: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 K) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral x3 (wcel K (cfv B2 ctopon)) A2)) (wcel J (cfv (cixp x3 A2 B2) ctopon)) $;

--| The base set for a product topology when all factors are the same. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem pttoponconst (A2 R J V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cxp A2 (csn R)) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel R (cfv X ctopon))) (wcel J (cfv (co X A2 cmap) ctopon)) $;

--| The base set for a product topology when all factors are the same. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptuniconst (A2 R J V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cxp A2 (csn R)) cpt) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel R ctop)) (wceq (co X A2 cmap) (cuni J)) $;

--| The base set of the compact-open topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkouni (R S J: class):
  $ wceq J (co S R cxko) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wceq (co R S ccn) (cuni J)) $;

--| The base set of the compact-open topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkotopon (R S J: class):
  $ wceq J (co S R cxko) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wcel J (cfv (co R S ccn) ctopon)) $;

--| The value of the product topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptval2 {w u k: setvar} (A2 F: class) (G J: class w u k) (V: class) (X: class u k):
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F)) (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cun (csn X) (crn G)) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| The product of two open sets is open in the product topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem txopn (A2 B2 R S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R V) (wcel S W)) (wa (wcel A2 R) (wcel B2 S))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (co R S ctx)) $;

--| The product of two closed sets is closed in the product topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem txcld (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv R ccld)) (wcel B2 (cfv S ccld))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (cfv (co R S ctx) ccld)) $;

--| Closure of a rectangle in the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem txcls (A2 B2 R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wa (wss A2 X) (wss B2 Y))) (wceq (cfv (cxp A2 B2) (cfv (co R S ctx) ccl)) (cxp (cfv A2 (cfv R ccl)) (cfv B2 (cfv S ccl)))) $;

--| Subset property of the topological product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txss12 (A2 B2 C D V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 V) (wcel D W)) (wa (wss A2 B2) (wss C D))) (wss (co A2 C ctx) (co B2 D ctx)) $;

--| It is sufficient to consider products of the bases for the topologies in the topological product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem txbasval (R S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel S W)) (wceq (co (cfv R ctg) (cfv S ctg) ctx) (co R S ctx)) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two neighborhoods is a neighborhood in the product topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jan-2018.)
theorem neitx (A2 B2 C D J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv C (cfv J cnei))) (wcel B2 (cfv D (cfv K cnei))))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (cfv (cxp C D) (cfv (co J K ctx) cnei))) $;

--| Continuity of a two-argument function at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem txcnpi {v u: setvar} (ph: wff v u) (A2 B2 U F J K: class) (L X Y: class v u):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv (cop A2 B2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 F) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wrex v (w3a (wcel A2 (cv u)) (wcel B2 (cv v)) (wss (cxp (cv u) (cv v)) (cima (ccnv F) U))) K) J) $;

--| Continuity of the first projection map of a topological product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem tx1cn (R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel (cres c1st (cxp X Y)) (co (co R S ctx) R ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of the second projection map of a topological product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem tx2cn (R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel (cres c2nd (cxp X Y)) (co (co R S ctx) S ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of a projection map into a topological product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptpjcn {x3: setvar} (A2 F I: class) (J: class x3) (V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F) (wcel I A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 Y (cfv I (cv x3))) (co J (cfv I F) ccn)) $;

--| The projection map is an open map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem ptpjopn {x3: setvar} (A2 U F I: class) (J: class x3) (V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F) (wcel I A2)) (wcel U J)) (wcel (cima (cmpt x3 Y (cfv I (cv x3))) U) (cfv I F)) $;

--| A closed box in the product topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptcld {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (C: class k) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ctop F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp k A2 C) (cfv (cfv F cpt) ccld)) $;

--| A closed box in the product topology. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptcldmpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (C J V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cixp k A2 C) (cfv (cfv (cmpt k A2 J) cpt) ccld)) $;

--| The closure of a box in the product topology is the box formed from the closures of the factors. The proof uses the axiom of choice; the last hypothesis is the choice assumption. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem ptclsg {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (R S J V X: class k):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cmpt k A2 R) cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun k A2 S) (wacn A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cixp k A2 S) (cfv J ccl)) (cixp k A2 (cfv S (cfv R ccl)))) $;

--| The closure of a box in the product topology is the box formed from the closures of the factors. This theorem is an AC equivalent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem ptcls {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (R S J V X: class k):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cmpt k A2 R) cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cixp k A2 S) (cfv J ccl)) (cixp k A2 (cfv S (cfv R ccl)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfac14 . By equipping ` S u. { P } ` for some ` P e/ S ` with the particular point topology, we can show that ` P ` is in the closure of ` S ` ; hence the sequence ` P ( x ) ` is in the product of the closures, and we can utilize this instance of ~ ptcls to extract an element of the closure of ` X_ k e. I S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfac14lem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (P: class x3) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class x3) (I: class y1) (J V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wne S c0) $ >
  $ wceq P (cpw (cuni S)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab y1 (wi (wcel P (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cun S (csn P)))) (cpw (cun S (csn P)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (cmpt x3 I R) cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cixp x3 I S) (cfv J ccl)) (cixp x3 I (cfv S (cfv R ccl)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cixp x3 I S) c0) $;

--| Theorem ~ ptcls is an equivalent of the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfac14 {f2 k s1: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal f2 (wi (wf (cdm (cv f2)) ctop (cv f2)) (wral s1 (wceq (cfv (cixp k (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv s1))) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cpt) ccl)) (cixp k (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) ccl)))) (cixp k (cdm (cv f2)) (cpw (cuni (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))))))) $;

--| The "constant function" function which maps ` x e. Y ` to the constant function ` z e. X |-> x ` is a continuous function from ` X ` into the space of continuous functions from ` Y ` to ` X ` . This can also be understood as the currying of the first projection function. (The currying of the second projection function is ` x e. Y |-> ( z e. X |-> z ) ` , which we already know is continuous because it is a constant function.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoccn {x3: setvar} (R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel (cmpt x3 Y (cxp X (csn (cv x3)))) (co S (co S R cxko) ccn)) $;

--| If two functions are continuous at ` D ` , then the ordered pair of them is continuous at ` D ` into the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem txcnp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (D: class) (J K L: class x3) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (cfv D (co J K ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (cfv D (co J L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cop A2 B2)) (cfv D (co J (co K L ctx) ccnp))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcnp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcnplem {x3 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 z k) (A2: class x3 k) (D F: class) (G: class k) (I: class) (J: class x3) (K: class x3 k) (V: class z) (W: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) I)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (cfv D (co J (cfv (cv k) F) ccnp))) $ >
  $ wnf k ps $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wfn G I) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wcel (cv k) I)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel W cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wcel (cv k) (cdif I W))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cfv D (cmpt x3 X (cmpt k I A2))) (cixp k I (cfv (cv k) G))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wrex z (wa (wcel D (cv z)) (wss (cima (cmpt x3 X (cmpt k I A2)) (cv z)) (cixp k I (cfv (cv k) G)))) J) $;

--| If every projection of a function is continuous at ` D ` , then the function itself is continuous at ` D ` into the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcnp {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (D F I: class) (J: class x3) (K: class x3 k) (V X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) I)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (cfv D (co J (cfv (cv k) F) ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt k I A2)) (cfv D (co J K ccnp))) $;

--| Universal property of the Cartesian product considered as a categorical product in the category of sets. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem upxp {h: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class) (P Q: class h) (F G: class):
  $ wceq P (cres c1st (cxp B2 C)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cres c2nd (cxp B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 D) (wf A2 B2 F) (wf A2 C G)) (weu h (w3a (wf A2 (cxp B2 C) (cv h)) (wceq F (ccom P (cv h))) (wceq G (ccom Q (cv h))))) $;

--| A map into the product of two topological spaces is continuous if both of its projections are continuous. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem txcnmpt {x3: setvar} (R S U F G: class) (H: class x3) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni U) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 W (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co U R ccn)) (wcel G (co U S ccn))) (wcel H (co U (co R S ctx) ccn)) $;

--| Universal property of the binary topological product. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem uptx {h: setvar} (P Q R S T U F G X Y: class) (Z: class h):
  $ wceq T (co R S ctx) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cxp X Y) $ >
  $ wceq P (cres c1st Z) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cres c2nd Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co U R ccn)) (wcel G (co U S ccn))) (wreu h (wa (wceq F (ccom P (cv h))) (wceq G (ccom Q (cv h)))) (co U T ccn)) $;

--| A map into the product of two topological spaces is continuous iff both of its projections are continuous. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem txcn (P Q R S U F W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cxp X Y) $ >
  $ wceq W (cuni U) $ >
  $ wceq P (cres c1st Z) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cres c2nd Z) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop) (wf W Z F)) (wb (wcel F (co U (co R S ctx) ccn)) (wa (wcel (ccom P F) (co U R ccn)) (wcel (ccom Q F) (co U S ccn)))) $;

--| If every projection of a function is continuous, then the function itself is continuous into the product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptcn {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (F I: class) (J: class x3) (K: class k) (V X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctop F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) I)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J (cfv (cv k) F) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt k I A2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Topology of a structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdstopn (ph: wff) (R S I O V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R I) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv Y ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (cfv (ccom ctopn R) cpt)) $;

--| A structure product of topological spaces is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdstps (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctps R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y ctps) $;

--| A structure power of a topological space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem pwstps (R I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctps) (wcel I V)) (wcel Y ctps) $;

--| The subspace of a topological product space induced by a subset with a Cartesian product representation is a topological product of the subspaces induced by the subspaces of the terms of the products. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txrest (A2 B2 R S V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R V) (wcel S W)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y))) (wceq (co (co R S ctx) (cxp A2 B2) crest) (co (co R A2 crest) (co S B2 crest) ctx)) $;

--| The topological product of discrete spaces is discrete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem txdis (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co (cpw A2) (cpw B2) ctx) (cpw (cxp A2 B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ txindis . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem txindislem (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cxp (cfv A2 cid) (cfv B2 cid)) (cfv (cxp A2 B2) cid) $;

--| The topological product of indiscrete spaces is indiscrete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem txindis (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co (cpr c0 A2) (cpr c0 B2) ctx) (cpr c0 (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| A function is jointly continuous on a discrete left topology iff it is continuous as a function of its right argument, for each fixed left value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem txdis1cn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (F: class) (J: class y1) (K: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F (cxp X Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (cmpt y1 Y (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F)) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co (cpw X) J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| If the property ` A ` is preserved under topological products, then so is the property of being locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem txlly {j k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class j):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv k) ctx) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (clly A2)) (wcel S (clly A2))) (wcel (co R S ctx) (clly A2)) $;

--| If the property ` A ` is preserved under topological products, then so is the property of being n-locally ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem txnlly {j k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class j):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co (cv j) (cv k) ctx) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cnlly A2)) (wcel S (cnlly A2))) (wcel (co R S ctx) (cnlly A2)) $;

--| The product of a collection of Hausdorff spaces is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem pthaus (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cha F)) (wcel (cfv F cpt) cha) $;

--| Restriction is a continuous function on product topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptrescn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (J: class x3) (K V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cres F B2) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ctop F) (wss B2 A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cres (cv x3) B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The "tube lemma". If ` X ` is compact and there is an open set ` U ` containing the line ` X X. { A } ` , then there is a "tube" ` X X. u ` for some neighborhood ` u ` of ` A ` which is entirely contained within ` U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem txtube {u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S U X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co R S ctx)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cxp X (csn A2)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wa (wcel A2 (cv u)) (wss (cxp X (cv u)) U)) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ txcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem txcmplem1 {v u: setvar} (ph: wff v) (A2 R: class v) (S W X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss W (co R S ctx)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wa (wcel A2 (cv u)) (wrex v (wss (cxp X (cv u)) (cuni (cv v))) (cin (cpw W) cfn))) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ txcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem txcmplem2 {v: setvar} (ph: wff v) (R: class v) (S W X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss W (co R S ctx)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex v (wceq (cxp X Y) (cuni (cv v))) (cin (cpw W) cfn)) $;

--| The topological product of two compact spaces is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem txcmp (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ccmp) (wcel S ccmp)) (wcel (co R S ctx) ccmp) $;

--| The topological product of two nonempty topologies is compact iff the component topologies are both compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem txcmpb (R S X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wa (wne X c0) (wne Y c0))) (wb (wcel (co R S ctx) ccmp) (wa (wcel R ccmp) (wcel S ccmp))) $;

--| A topology is Hausdorff iff the diagonal set is closed in the topology's product with itself. _EDITORIAL_: very clumsy proof, can probably be shortened substantially. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem hausdiag (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cha) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel (cres cid X) (cfv (co J J ctx) ccld))) $;

--| In a Hausdorff topology, the equalizer of two continuous functions is closed (thus, two continuous functions which agree on a dense set agree everywhere). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Jan-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hauseqlcld (ph: wff) (F G J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdm (cin F G)) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The topological product of two Hausdorff spaces is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem txhaus (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cha) (wcel S cha)) (wcel (co R S ctx) cha) $;

--| Two sequences converge iff the sequence of their ordered pairs converges. Proposition 14-2.6 of [Gleason] p. 230. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem txlm {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S: class n) (F G: class) (H: class n) (J K: class) (M: class n) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z Y G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n Z (cop (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (cv n) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wbr F R (cfv J clm)) (wbr G S (cfv K clm))) (wbr H (cop R S) (cfv (co J K ctx) clm))) $;

--| The image of a convergent sequence under a continuous map is convergent to the image of the original point. Binary operation version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2014.)
theorem lmcn2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S: class n) (F G: class) (H: class n) (J K: class) (M N: class n) (O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z Y G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F R (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G S (cfv K clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O (co (co J K ctx) N ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n Z (co (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (cv n) G) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co R S O) (cfv N clm)) $;

--| The topological product of two first-countable spaces is first-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem tx1stc (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R c1stc) (wcel S c1stc)) (wcel (co R S ctx) c1stc) $;

--| The topological product of two second-countable spaces is second-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem tx2ndc (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R c2ndc) (wcel S c2ndc)) (wcel (co R S ctx) c2ndc) $;

--| The topological product of a locally compact space and a compactly generated Hausdorff space is compactly generated. (The condition on ` S ` can also be replaced with either "compactly generated weak Hausdorff (CGWH)" or "compact Hausdorff-ly generated (CHG)", where WH means that all images of compact Hausdorff spaces are closed and CHG means that a set is open iff it is open in all compact Hausdorff spaces.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem txkgen (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cnlly ccmp)) (wcel S (cin (crn ckgen) cha))) (wcel (co R S ctx) (crn ckgen)) $;

--| If the codomain space is Hausdorff, then the compact-open topology of continuous functions is also Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkohaus (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S cha)) (wcel (co S R cxko) cha) $;

--| The compact-open topology is finer than the product topology restricted to continuous functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoptsub (R S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (cxp X (csn S)) cpt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop)) (wss (co J (co R S ccn) crest) (co S R cxko)) $;

--| The compact-open topology on a discrete set coincides with the product topology where all the factors are the same. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem xkopt (A2 R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctop) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co R (cpw A2) cxko) (cfv (cxp A2 (csn R)) cpt)) $;

--| Continuity of a projection map from the space of continuous functions. (This theorem can be strengthened, to joint continuity in both ` f ` and ` A ` as a function on ` ( S ^ko R ) tX R ` , but not without stronger assumptions on ` R ` ; see ~ xkofvcn .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkopjcn {f2: setvar} (A2 R S X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ctop) (wcel S ctop) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cmpt f2 (co R S ccn) (cfv A2 (cv f2))) (co (co S R cxko) S ccn)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a continuous function, then ` g |-> g o. F ` is a continuous function on function spaces. (The reason we prove this and ~ xkoco2cn independently of the more general ~ xkococn is because that requires some inconvenient extra assumptions on ` S ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoco1cn {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel T ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt g2 (co S T ccn) (ccom (cv g2) F)) (co (co T S cxko) (co T R cxko) ccn)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a continuous function, then ` g |-> F o. g ` is a continuous function on function spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoco2cn {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt g2 (co R S ccn) (ccom F (cv g2))) (co (co S R cxko) (co T R cxko) ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of the composition operation as a function on continuous function spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkococnlem {z f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff z f2 g2 h) (A2 B2: class f2 g2 h) (R: class) (S: class h) (T: class) (F: class f2 g2 h) (K V: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq F (cmpo f2 g2 (co S T ccn) (co R S ccn) (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K (cuni R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co R K crest) ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co S T ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co R S ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima (ccom A2 B2) K) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wa (wcel (cop A2 B2) (cv z)) (wss (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (crab h (wss (cima (cv h) K) V) (co R T ccn))))) (co (co T S cxko) (co S R cxko) ctx)) $;

--| Continuity of the composition operation as a function on continuous function spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkococn {f2 g2: setvar} (R S T: class) (F: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq F (cmpo f2 g2 (co S T ccn) (co R S ccn) (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ctop) (wcel S (cnlly ccmp)) (wcel T ctop)) (wcel F (co (co (co T S cxko) (co S R cxko) ctx) (co T R cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The identity function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cv x3)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| A constant function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X P) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt11 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt y1 Y B2) (co K L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X C) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt11f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (F J K L X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co K L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cfv A2 F)) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1t {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (J K L X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cop A2 B2)) (co J (co K L ctx) ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt12f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (F J K L M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co K L ctx) M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 F)) (co J M ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt12 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class y1 z) (D: class x3) (J K L: class z) (M X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo y1 z Y Z C) (co (co K L ctx) M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv y1) A2) (wceq (cv z) B2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X D) (co J M ccn)) $;

--| The projection onto the first coordinate is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1st {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (J K: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cv x3)) (co (co J K ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| The projection onto the second coordinate is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2nd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (J K: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cv y1)) (co (co J K ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| A constant function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2c {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (J K: class x3 y1) (L X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y P) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt21 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class z) (C J K: class x3 y1) (L M X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt z Z B2) (co L M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y C) (co (co J K ctx) M ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt21f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (F: class) (J K: class x3 y1) (L M X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co L M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cfv A2 F)) (co (co J K ctx) M ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2t {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J K: class x3 y1) (L M X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co J K ctx) M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cop A2 B2)) (co (co J K ctx) (co L M ctx) ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt22 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (C: class z w) (D: class x3 y1) (J K: class x3 y1 w) (L M N W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co J K ctx) M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv W ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo z w Z W C) (co (co L M ctx) N ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y D) (co (co J K ctx) N ccn)) $;

--| The composition of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt22f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (F: class) (J K: class x3 y1) (L M N X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co J K ctx) M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co L M ctx) N ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 F)) (co (co J K ctx) N ccn)) $;

--| The restriction of a continuous function to a subset is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnmpt1res {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 J K L: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 Y A2) (co K L ccn)) $;

--| The restriction of a continuous function to a subset is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnmpt2res {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 J K L M N: class x3 y1) (W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq K (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y X) $ >
  $ wceq N (co M W crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss W Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Z A2) (co (co J M ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 Y W A2) (co (co K N ctx) L ccn)) $;

--| The argument converse of a continuous function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnmptcom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 J K: class x3 y1) (L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo y1 x3 Y X A2) (co (co K J ctx) L ccn)) $;

--| The curried first projection function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptkc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y (cv x3))) (co J (co J K cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The evaluation of the inner function in a curried function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptkp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class) (C: class x3) (J K L X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X C) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| The composition of a curried function with a one-arg function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnmptk1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class z) (C: class x3 y1) (J K L M X Y: class z) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt z Z B2) (co L M ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y C)) (co J (co M K cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The composition of a one-arg function with a curried function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1k {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1 z) (C: class x3 y1) (J K L M: class z) (W: class x3 y1 z) (X Y: class z) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv W ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co J L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt y1 Y (cmpt z Z B2)) (co K (co M L cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt y1 Y (cmpt x3 X C)) (co K (co M J cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The composition of two curried functions is jointly continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptkk {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class x3 z) (C: class x3 y1) (J K L M: class y1 z) (W: class x3 y1 z) (X: class z) (Y: class x3 z) (Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv W ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt z Z B2)) (co J (co M L cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y C)) (co J (co M K cxko) ccn)) $;

--| Joint continuity of the function value operation as a function on continuous function spaces. (Compare ~ xkopjcn .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkofvcn {x3 f2: setvar} (R S: class) (F: class x3 f2) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni R) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo f2 x3 (co R S ccn) X (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cnlly ccmp)) (wcel S ctop)) (wcel F (co (co (co S R cxko) R ctx) S ccn)) $;

--| The evaluation of a curried function by a one-arg function is jointly continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptk1p {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2 C: class x3) (J K L: class y1) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X C) (co J L ccn)) $;

--| The uncurrying of a curried function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmptk2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (J K L: class y1) (X Y: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of "injection", i.e. currying, as a function on continuous function spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem xkoinjcn {x3 y1: setvar} (R S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel S (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel F (co R (co (co S R ctx) S cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The currying of a two-argument function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2k {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 J K: class x3 y1) (L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y A2)) (co J (co L K cxko) ccn)) $;

--| The topological product of two connected spaces is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem txconn (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cconn) (wcel S cconn)) (wcel (co R S ctx) cconn) $;

--| If a relation graph is open, then an image set of a singleton is also open. Corollary of Proposition 4 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.26. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jan-2018.)
theorem imasnopn (A2 R J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wcel R (co J K ctx)) (wcel A2 X))) (wcel (cima R (csn A2)) K) $;

--| If a relation graph is closed, then an image set of a singleton is also closed. Corollary of Proposition 4 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.26. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jan-2018.)
theorem imasncld (A2 R J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wcel R (cfv (co J K ctx) ccld)) (wcel A2 X))) (wcel (cima R (csn A2)) (cfv K ccld)) $;

--| If a relation graph is closed, then an image set of a singleton is also closed. Corollary of Proposition 4 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.26. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jan-2018.)
theorem imasncls (A2 R J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wss R (cxp X Y)) (wcel A2 X))) (wss (cfv (cima R (csn A2)) (cfv K ccl)) (cima (cfv R (cfv (co J K ctx) ccl)) (csn A2))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Kolmogorov quotient function.
term ckq: class;

--| Define the Kolmogorov quotient. This is a function on topologies which maps a topology to its quotient under the topological distinguishability map, which takes a point to the set of open sets that contain it. Two points are mapped to the same image under this function iff they are topologically indistinguishable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_kq {x3 y1 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ckq (cmpt j ctop (co (cv j) (cmpt x3 (cuni (cv j)) (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv j))) cqtop)) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopval {s1: setvar} (F J V: class) (W: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel F W)) (wceq (co J F cqtop) (crab s1 (wcel (cin (cima (ccnv F) (cv s1)) X) J) (cpw (cima F X)))) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function when ` F ` is a function on the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopval2 {s1: setvar} (F J V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wfo Z Y F) (wss Z X)) (wceq (co J F cqtop) (crab s1 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv s1)) J) (cpw Y))) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem elqtop (A2 F J V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wfo Z Y F) (wss Z X)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cqtop)) (wa (wss A2 Y) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) J))) $;

--| The quotient topology is unaffected by restriction to the base set. This property makes it slightly more convenient to use, since we don't have to require that ` F ` be a function with domain ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopres (F J V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (co J F cqtop) (co J (cres F X) cqtop)) $;

--| The quotient topology is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtoptop2 (F J V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel F V) (wfun F)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) ctop) $;

--| The quotient topology is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtoptop (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) ctop) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem elqtop2 (A2 F J V X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wfo X Y F)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cqtop)) (wa (wss A2 Y) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) J))) $;

--| The base set of the quotient topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopuni (F J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wfo X Y F)) (wceq Y (cuni (co J F cqtop))) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem elqtop3 (A2 F J X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wfo X Y F)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cqtop)) (wa (wss A2 Y) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) J))) $;

--| The base set of the quotient topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem qtoptopon (F J X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wfo X Y F)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) (cfv Y ctopon)) $;

--| A quotient map is a continuous function into its quotient topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopid (F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wfn F X)) (wcel F (co J (co J F cqtop) ccn)) $;

--| The quotient topology induced by the identity function is the original topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2015.)
theorem idqtop (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wceq (co J (cres cid X) cqtop) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qtopcmp and ~ qtopconn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopcmplem (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J A2) (wfo X (cuni (co J F cqtop)) F) (wcel F (co J (co J F cqtop) ccn))) (wcel (co J F cqtop) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J A2) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) A2) $;

--| A quotient of a compact space is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopcmp (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) ccmp) $;

--| A quotient of a connected space is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopconn (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cconn) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) cconn) $;

--| A quotient of a compactly generated space is compactly generated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem qtopkgen (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (crn ckgen)) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) (crn ckgen)) $;

--| An injection maps bases to bases. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem basqtop (F J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctb) (wf1o X Y F)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) ctb) $;

--| An injection maps generated topologies to each other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgqtop (F J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctb) (wf1o X Y F)) (wceq (co (cfv J ctg) F cqtop) (cfv (co J F cqtop) ctg)) $;

--| The property of being a closed set in the quotient topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopcld (A2 F J X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wfo X Y F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv (co J F cqtop) ccld)) (wa (wss A2 Y) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) (cfv J ccld)))) $;

--| Universal property of a quotient map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopcn (F G J K X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Z ctopon))) (wa (wfo X Y F) (wf Y Z G))) (wb (wcel G (co (co J F cqtop) K ccn)) (wcel (ccom G F) (co J K ccn))) $;

--| A surjective continuous function from ` J ` to ` K ` induces a topology ` J qTop F ` on the base set of ` K ` . This topology is in general finer than ` K ` . Together with ~ qtopid , this implies that ` J qTop F ` is the finest topology making ` F ` continuous, i.e. the final topology with respect to the family ` { F } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopss (F J K Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wceq (crn F) Y)) (wss K (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| Universal property of the quotient topology. If ` G ` is a function from ` J ` to ` K ` which is equal on all equivalent elements under ` F ` , then there is a unique continuous map ` f : ( J / F ) --> K ` such that ` G = f o. F ` , and we say that ` G ` "passes to the quotient". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopeu {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (J K: class y1) (X: class f2) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cv y1) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu f2 (wceq G (ccom (cv f2) F)) (co (co J F cqtop) K ccn)) $;

--| If ` A ` is a saturated open or closed set (where saturated means that ` A = ( ``' F " U ) ` for some ` U ` ), then the restriction of the quotient map ` F ` to ` A ` is a quotient map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem qtoprest (ph: wff) (A2 U F J X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cima (ccnv F) U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel A2 J) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co J F cqtop) U crest) (co (co J A2 crest) (cres F A2) cqtop)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a surjective continuous open map, then it is a quotient map. (An open map is a function that maps open sets to open sets.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopomap {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) J)) (wcel (cima F (cv x3)) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a surjective continuous closed map, then it is a quotient map. (A closed map is a function that maps closed sets to closed sets.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtopcmap {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cima F (cv x3)) (cfv K ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| The topology of an image structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem imastopn (ph: wff) (B2 R U F J O V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| The image of a topological space under a function is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem imastps (ph: wff) (B2 R U F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ctps) $;

--| A quotient structure is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem qustps (ph: wff) (R U E V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co R E cqus)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ctps) $;

--| Value of the function appearing in ~ df-kq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqfval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (crab y1 (wcel A2 (cv y1)) J)) $;

--| Two points in the Kolmogorov quotient are equal iff the original points are topologically indistinguishable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqfeq {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) (wral y1 (wb (wcel A2 (cv y1)) (wcel B2 (cv y1))) J)) $;

--| The topological indistinguishability map is a function on the base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqffn {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J V) (wfn F X) $;

--| Value of the quotient topology function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqval {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wceq (cfv J ckq) (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient is a topology on the quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqtopon {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) (cfv (crn F) ctopon)) $;

--| The topological indistinguishability map is a continuous function into the Kolmogorov quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqid {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (co J (cfv J ckq) ccn)) $;

--| The topological indistinguishability map is injective iff the space is T_0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist0_4 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct0) (wf1 X cvv F)) $;

--| When the image set is open, the quotient map satisfies a partial converse to ~ fnfvima , which is normally only true for injective functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqfvima {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U J) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 U) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cima F U))) $;

--| Any open set is saturated with respect to the topological indistinguishability map (in the terminology of ~ qtoprest ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqsat {x3 y1: setvar} (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U J)) (wceq (cima (ccnv F) (cima F U)) U) $;

--| A version of ~ imain for the topological indistinguishability map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqdisj {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U J)) (wceq (cin (cima F U) (cima F (cdif A2 U))) c0) $;

--| Any closed set is saturated with respect to the topological indistinguishability map (in the terminology of ~ qtoprest ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqcldsat {x3 y1: setvar} (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U (cfv J ccld))) (wceq (cima (ccnv F) (cima F U)) U) $;

--| The topological indistinguishability map is an open map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqopn {x3 y1: setvar} (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U J)) (wcel (cima F U) (cfv J ckq)) $;

--| The topological indistinguishability map is a closed map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqcld {x3 y1: setvar} (U F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U (cfv J ccld))) (wcel (cima F U) (cfv (cfv J ckq) ccld)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kqt0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem kqt0lem {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct0) $;

--| The property " ` J ` is an R_0 space". A space is R_0 if any two topologically distinguishable points are separated (there is an open set containing each one and disjoint from the other). Or in contraposition, if every open set which contains ` x ` also contains ` y ` , so there is no separation, then ` x ` and ` y ` are members of the same open sets. We have chosen not to give this definition a name, because it turns out that a space is R_0 if and only if its Kolmogorov quotient is T_1, so that is what we prove here. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem isr0 {x3 y1 z w o: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1) (wral z (wral w (wi (wral o (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv o)) (wcel (cv w) (cv o))) J) (wral o (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv o)) (wcel (cv w) (cv o))) J)) X) X)) $;

--| The analogue of the T_1 axiom (singletons are closed) for an R_0 space. In an R_0 space the set of all points topologically indistinguishable from ` A ` is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem r0cld {x3 y1 z o: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (crab z (wral o (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv o)) (wcel A2 (cv o))) J) X) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ regr1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem regr1lem {x3 y1 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 m n) (A2 B2: class) (U: class x3 y1 m n) (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J creg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex m (wrex n (w3a (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cv m)) (wcel (cfv B2 F) (cv n)) (wceq (cin (cv m) (cv n)) c0)) (cfv J ckq)) (cfv J ckq))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U)) $;

--| A Kolmogorov quotient of a regular space is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem regr1lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel J creg)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cha) $;

--| A Kolmogorov quotient of a regular space is regular. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqreglem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel J creg)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) creg) $;

--| If the Kolmogorov quotient of a space is regular then so is the original space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqreglem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) creg)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| A Kolmogorov quotient of a normal space is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqnrmlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel J cnrm)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cnrm) $;

--| If the Kolmogorov quotient of a space is normal then so is the original space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqnrmlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cnrm)) (wcel J cnrm) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient is a topology on the quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqtop (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J ctop) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ctop) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient is T_0 even if the original topology is not. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqt0 (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J ctop) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct0) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient is a topology on the quotient set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqf:
  $ wf ctop ct0 ckq $;

--| The separation property of an R_0 space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem r0sep {o: setvar} (A2 B2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wi (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wcel B2 (cv o))) J) (wral o (wb (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wcel B2 (cv o))) J)) $;

--| A normal R_0 space is also regular. These spaces are usually referred to as normal regular spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmr0reg (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cnrm) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| A regular space is R_1, which means that any two topologically distinct points can be separated by neighborhoods. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem regr1 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J creg) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cha) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient of a regular space is regular. By ~ regr1 it is also Hausdorff, so we can also say that a space is regular iff the Kolmogorov quotient is regular Hausdorff (T_3). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqreg (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J creg) (wcel (cfv J ckq) creg) $;

--| The Kolmogorov quotient of a normal space is normal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqnrm (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cnrm) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cnrm) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all homeomorphisms.
term chmeo: class;

--| Extend class notation with the relation "is homeomorphic to.".
term chmph: class;

--| Function returning all the homeomorphisms from topology ` j ` to topology ` k ` . (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.)
axiom df_hmeo {f2 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq chmeo (cmpo j k ctop ctop (crab f2 (wcel (ccnv (cv f2)) (co (cv k) (cv j) ccn)) (co (cv j) (cv k) ccn))) $;

--| Definition of the relation ` x ` is homeomorphic to ` y ` . (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.)
axiom df_hmph:
  $ wceq chmph (cima (ccnv chmeo) (cdif cvv c1o)) $;

--| The set of homeomorphisms is a function on topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeofn:
  $ wfn chmeo (cxp ctop ctop) $;

--| The set of all the homeomorphisms between two topologies. (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeofval {f2: setvar} (J K: class):
  $ wceq (co J K chmeo) (crab f2 (wcel (ccnv (cv f2)) (co K J ccn)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The predicate F is a homeomorphism between topology ` J ` and topology ` K ` . Criterion of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.2. (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem ishmeo (F J K: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wa (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co K J ccn))) $;

--| A homeomorphism is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocn (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The converse of a homeomorphism is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocnvcn (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co K J ccn)) $;

--| The converse of a homeomorphism is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by FL, 5-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocnv (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co K J chmeo)) $;

--| A homeomorphism is a 1-1-onto mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeof1o2 (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel F (co J K chmeo))) (wf1o X Y F) $;

--| A homeomorphism is a 1-1-onto mapping. (Contributed by FL, 5-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2014.)
theorem hmeof1o (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wf1o X Y F) $;

--| The image of an open set by a homeomorphism is an open set. (Contributed by FL, 5-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeoima (A2 F J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel (cima F A2) K) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve openness. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeoopn (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wcel (cima F A2) K)) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve closedness. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocld (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) (wcel (cima F A2) (cfv K ccld))) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve closures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocls (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cima F A2) (cfv K ccl)) (cima F (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)))) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve interiors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeontr (A2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cima F A2) (cfv K cnt)) (cima F (cfv A2 (cfv J cnt)))) $;

--| The function mapping open sets to their images under a homeomorphism is a bijection of topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmeoimaf1o {x3: setvar} (F: class) (G: class x3) (J K: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 J (cima F (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wf1o J K G) $;

--| The restriction of a homeomorphism is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeores (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wss Y X)) (wcel (cres F Y) (co (co J Y crest) (co K (cima F Y) crest) chmeo)) $;

--| The composite of two homeomorphisms is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by FL, 9-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeoco (F G J K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wcel G (co K L chmeo))) (wcel (ccom G F) (co J L chmeo)) $;

--| The identity function is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem idhmeo (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel (cres cid X) (co J J chmeo)) $;

--| The converse of a homeomorphism is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by FL, 5-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeocnvb (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wrel F) (wb (wcel (ccnv F) (co J K chmeo)) (wcel F (co K J chmeo))) $;

--| A homeomorphism is a quotient map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmeoqtop (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wceq K (co J F cqtop)) $;

--| Express the predicate ` J ` is homeomorphic to ` K ` . (Contributed by FL, 14-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmph (J K: class):
  $ wb (wbr J K chmph) (wne (co J K chmeo) c0) $;

--| If there is a homeomorphism between spaces, then the spaces are homeomorphic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphi (F J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wbr J K chmph) $;

--| Reverse closure for the homeomorphic predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphtop (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) $;

--| The relation "being homeomorphic to" implies the operands are topologies. (Contributed by FL, 23-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphtop1 (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The relation "being homeomorphic to" implies the operands are topologies. (Contributed by FL, 23-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphtop2 (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wcel K ctop) $;

--| "Is homeomorphic to" is reflexive. (Contributed by FL, 25-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphref (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wbr J J chmph) $;

--| "Is homeomorphic to" is symmetric. (Contributed by FL, 8-Mar-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2014.)
theorem hmphsym (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wbr K J chmph) $;

--| "Is homeomorphic to" is transitive. (Contributed by FL, 9-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmphtr (J K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr J K chmph) (wbr K L chmph)) (wbr J L chmph) $;

--| "Is homeomorphic to" is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by FL, 22-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem hmpher:
  $ wer ctop chmph $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve the cardinality of the topologies. (Contributed by FL, 1-Jun-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmphen (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wbr J K cen) $;

--| "Is homeomorphic to" is symmetric. (Contributed by FL, 22-Feb-2007.)
theorem hmphsymb (J K: class):
  $ wb (wbr J K chmph) (wbr K J chmph) $;

--| Lemma for ~ haushmph and similar theorems. If the topological property ` A ` is preserved under injective preimages, then property ` A ` is preserved under homeomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem haushmphlem {f2: setvar} (A2 J K: class):
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J A2) (wf1 (cuni K) (cuni J) (cv f2)) (wcel (cv f2) (co K J ccn))) (wcel K A2) $ >
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J A2) (wcel K A2)) $;

--| Compactness is a topological property-that is, for any two homeomorphic topologies, either both are compact or neither is. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Jun-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem cmphmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J ccmp) (wcel K ccmp)) $;

--| Connectedness is a topological property. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Jul-2009.)
theorem connhmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J cconn) (wcel K cconn)) $;

--| T_0 is a topological property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem t0hmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J ct0) (wcel K ct0)) $;

--| T_1 is a topological property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem t1hmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J ct1) (wcel K ct1)) $;

--| Hausdorff-ness is a topological property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem haushmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J cha) (wcel K cha)) $;

--| Regularity is a topological property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem reghmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J creg) (wcel K creg)) $;

--| Normality is a topological property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmhmph (J K: class):
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wi (wcel J cnrm) (wcel K cnrm)) $;

--| A topology homeomorphic to the empty set is empty. (Contributed by FL, 18-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmph0 (J: class):
  $ wb (wbr J (csn c0) chmph) (wceq J (csn c0)) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve topological discreteness. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmphdis (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wbr J (cpw A2) chmph) (wceq J (cpw X)) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve topological indiscreteness. (Contributed by FL, 18-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmphindis (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wbr J (cpr c0 A2) chmph) (wceq J (cpr c0 X)) $;

--| Equinumerous sets equipped with their indiscrete topologies are homeomorphic (which means in that particular case that a segment is homeomorphic to a circle contrary to what Wikipedia claims). (Contributed by FL, 17-Aug-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem indishmph (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wbr (cpr c0 A2) (cpr c0 B2) chmph) $;

--| Homeomorphisms preserve the cardinality of the underlying sets. (Contributed by FL, 17-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.)
theorem hmphen2 (J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wbr J K chmph) (wbr X Y cen) $;

--| A continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem cmphaushmeo (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wcel K cha) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wb (wcel F (co J K chmeo)) (wf1o X Y F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordthmeo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordthmeolem (R S F V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cdm S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R V) (wcel S W) (wiso X Y R S F)) (wcel F (co (cfv R cordt) (cfv S cordt) ccn)) $;

--| An order isomorphism is a homeomorphism on the respective order topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordthmeo (R S F V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm R) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cdm S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R V) (wcel S W) (wiso X Y R S F)) (wcel F (co (cfv R cordt) (cfv S cordt) chmeo)) $;

--| Lift a pair of homeomorphisms on the factors to a homeomorphism of product topologies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txhmeo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G J K L M X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J L chmeo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K M chmeo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) G))) (co (co J K ctx) (co L M ctx) chmeo)) $;

--| Show inverse for the "swap components" operation on a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem txswaphmeolem {x3 y1: setvar} (X Y: class):
  $ wceq (ccom (cmpo y1 x3 Y X (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cop (cv y1) (cv x3)))) (cres cid (cxp X Y)) $;

--| There is a homeomorphism from ` X X. Y ` to ` Y X. X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem txswaphmeo {x3 y1: setvar} (J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cop (cv y1) (cv x3))) (co (co J K ctx) (co K J ctx) chmeo)) $;

--| The canonical homeomorphism from a topological product on a singleton to the topology of the factor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Apr-2021.)
theorem pt1hmeo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 J K: class) (V: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv (csn (cop A2 J)) cpt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (csn (cop A2 (cv x3)))) (co J K chmeo)) $;

--| Exhibit the converse function of the map ` G ` which joins two product topologies on disjoint index sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptuncnv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G: class x3 y1) (J K L: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni K) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni L) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cres F A2) cpt) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (cres F B2) cpt) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccnv G) (cmpt z (cuni J) (cop (cres (cv z) A2) (cres (cv z) B2)))) $;

--| Define a homeomorphism from a binary product of indexed product topologies to an indexed product topology on the union of the index sets. This is the topological analogue of ` ( A ^ B ) x. ( A ^ C ) = A ^ ( B + C ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptunhmeo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G: class x3 y1) (J K L: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni K) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni L) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cres F A2) cpt) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (cres F B2) cpt) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cun (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C ctop F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co K L ctx) J chmeo)) $;

--| The function ` F ` used in ~ xpsval is a homeomorphism from the binary product topology to the indexed product topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem xpstopnlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class x3 y1) (J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J K ctx) (cfv (cpr (cop c0 J) (cop c1o K)) cpt) chmeo)) $;

--| A binary product of topologies is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpstps (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctps) (wcel S ctps)) (wcel T ctps) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xpstopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpstopnlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (R S: class) (T F: class x3 y1) (J K: class) (O: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctps) (wcel S ctps)) (wceq O (co J K ctx)) $;

--| The topology on a binary product of topological spaces, as we have defined it (transferring the indexed product topology on functions on ` { (/) , 1o } ` to ` ( X X. Y ) ` by the canonical bijection), coincides with the usual topological product (generated by a base of rectangles). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpstopn (R S T J K O: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ctps) (wcel S ctps)) (wceq O (co J K ctx)) $;

--| A topological product of finitely many compact spaces is compact. This weak version of Tychonoff's theorem does not require the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Feb-2015.)
theorem ptcmpfi (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wf A2 ccmp F)) (wcel (cfv F cpt) ccmp) $;

--| The inverse of the "currying" function ` F ` is the uncurrying function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem xkocnv {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt f2 (co (co J K ctx) L ccn) (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt g2 (co J (co L K cxko) ccn) (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)))))) $;

--| The Exponential Law for topological spaces. The "currying" function ` F ` is a homeomorphism on function spaces when ` J ` and ` K ` are exponentiable spaces (by ~ xkococn , it is sufficient to assume that ` J , K ` are locally compact to ensure exponentiability). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xkohmeo {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt f2 (co (co J K ctx) L ccn) (cmpt x3 X (cmpt y1 Y (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly ccmp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co L (co J K ctx) cxko) (co (co L K cxko) J cxko) chmeo)) $;

--| If a quotient map is injective, then it is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem qtopf1 (ph: wff) (F J X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J (co J F cqtop) chmeo)) $;

--| If two functions on a base topology ` J ` make the same identifications in order to create quotient spaces ` J qTop F ` and ` J qTop G ` , then not only are ` J qTop F ` and ` J qTop G ` homeomorphic, but there is a unique homeomorphism that makes the diagram commute. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem qtophmeo {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G J: class) (X: class f2) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cv y1) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu f2 (wceq G (ccom (cv f2) F)) (co (co J F cqtop) (co J G cqtop) chmeo)) $;

--| A topological space is T_0 iff the quotient map is a homeomorphism onto the space's Kolmogorov quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem t0kq {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ct0) (wcel F (co J (cfv J ckq) chmeo))) $;

--| A topological space is T_0 iff it is homeomorphic to its Kolmogorov quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem kqhmph (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J ct0) (wbr J (cfv J ckq) chmph) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ist1-5 and similar theorems. If ` A ` is a topological property which implies T_0, such as T_1 or T_2, the property can be "decomposed" into T_0 and a non-T_0 version of property ` A ` (which is defined as stating that the Kolmogorov quotient of the space has property ` A ` ). For example, if ` A ` is T_1, then the theorem states that a space is T_1 iff it is T_0 and its Kolmogorov quotient is T_1 (we call this property R_0). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist1_5lem (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J A2) (wcel J ct0) $ >
  $ wi (wbr J (cfv J ckq) chmph) (wi (wcel J A2) (wcel (cfv J ckq) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wbr (cfv J ckq) J chmph) (wi (wcel (cfv J ckq) A2) (wcel J A2)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J A2) (wa (wcel J ct0) (wcel (cfv J ckq) A2)) $;

--| A T_1 space is R_0. That is, the Kolmogorov quotient of a T_1 space is also T_1 (because they are homeomorphic). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem t1r0 (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ct1) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1) $;

--| A topological space is T_1 iff it is both T_0 and R_0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem ist1_5 (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J ct1) (wa (wcel J ct0) (wcel (cfv J ckq) ct1)) $;

--| A topological space is Hausdorff iff it is both T_0 and R_1 (where R_1 means that any two topologically distinct points are separated by neighborhoods). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem ishaus3 (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cha) (wa (wcel J ct0) (wcel (cfv J ckq) cha)) $;

--| A normal T_1 space is regular Hausdorff. In other words, a T_4 space is T_3 . One can get away with slightly weaker assumptions; see ~ nrmr0reg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmreg (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cnrm) (wcel J ct1)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| A regular T_0 space is Hausdorff. In other words, a T_3 space is T_2 . A regular Hausdorff or T_0 space is also known as a T_3 space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem reghaus (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J creg) (wb (wcel J cha) (wcel J ct0)) $;

--| A T_1 normal space is Hausdorff. A Hausdorff or T_1 normal space is also known as a T_4 space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem nrmhaus (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cnrm) (wb (wcel J cha) (wcel J ct1)) $;

--| Membership in a one-parameter class of sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem elmptrab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (D: class y1) (F: class x3 y1) (V X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 D (crab y1 ph B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wb (wcel Y (cfv X F)) (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y C) ps) $;

--| Membership in a one-parameter class of sets, indexed by arbitrary base sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.) (Revised by AV, 26-Mar-2021.)
theorem elmptrab2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (F: class x3 y1) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cvv (crab y1 ph B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq (cv y1) Y)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y C) (wcel X W) $ >
  $ wb (wcel Y (cfv X F)) (wa (wcel Y C) ps) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a filter base." Note that some authors require filter bases to be closed under pairwise intersections, but that is not necessary under our definition. One advantage of this definition is that tails in a directed set form a filter base under our meaning. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem isfbas {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wb (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wa (wss F (cpw B2)) (w3a (wne F c0) (wnel c0 F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wne (cin F (cpw (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)))) c0) F) F)))) $;

--| There are no empty filter bases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbasne0 (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wne F c0) $;

--| No filter base contains the empty set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem _0nelfb (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wn (wcel c0 F)) $;

--| A filter base on a set is a subset of the power set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbsspw (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wss F (cpw B2)) $;

--| An element of the filter base is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbelss (B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wcel X F)) (wss X B2) $;

--| The domain of a filter base is nonempty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbdmn0 (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wne B2 c0) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a filter base." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem isfbas2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 A2) (wb (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wa (wss F (cpw B2)) (w3a (wne F c0) (wnel c0 F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wss (cv z) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1))) F) F) F)))) $;

--| A filter base contains subsets of its pairwise intersections. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Jeff Hankins, 1-Dec-2010.)
theorem fbasssin {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 F)) (wrex x3 (wss (cv x3) (cin A2 B2)) F) $;

--| A filter base contains subsets of its finite intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbssfi {x3: setvar} (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel A2 (cfv F cfi))) (wrex x3 (wss (cv x3) A2) F) $;

--| A filter base contains subsets of its finite intersections. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbssint {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wss A2 F) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wrex x3 (wss (cv x3) (cint A2)) F) $;

--| A filter base does not contain complements of its elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbncp (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel A2 F)) (wn (wcel (cdif B2 A2) F)) $;

--| A necessary and sufficient condition for the union of two filter bases to also be a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbun {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel G (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wcel (cun F G) (cfv X cfbas)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wss (cv z) (cin (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cun F G)) G) F)) $;

--| No filter base containing a finite element is free. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fbfinnfr (B2 S F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv B2 cfbas)) (wcel S F) (wcel S cfn)) (wne (cint F) c0) $;

--| The collection of open supersets of a nonempty set in a topology is a neighborhoods of the set, one of the motivations for the filter concept. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem opnfbas {x3: setvar} (S J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wss S X) (wne S c0)) (wcel (crab x3 (wss S (cv x3)) J) (cfv X cfbas)) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of a filter base ` F ` to be a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem trfbas2 (A2 F Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv Y cfbas)) (wss A2 Y)) (wb (wcel (co F A2 crest) (cfv A2 cfbas)) (wn (wcel c0 (co F A2 crest)))) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of a filter base ` F ` to be a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem trfbas {v: setvar} (A2 F: class) (Y: class v):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv Y cfbas)) (wss A2 Y)) (wb (wcel (co F A2 crest) (cfv A2 cfbas)) (wral v (wne (cin (cv v) A2) c0) F)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the set of filters on a set.
term cfil: class;

--| The set of filters on a set. Definition 1 (axioms FI, FIIa, FIIb, FIII) of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. Filters are used to define the concept of limit in the general case. They are a generalization of the idea of neighborhoods. Suppose you are in ` RR ` . With neighborhoods you can express the idea of a variable that tends to a specific number but you can't express the idea of a variable that tends to infinity. Filters relax the "axioms" of neighborhoods and then succeed in expressing the idea of something that tends to infinity. Filters were invented by Cartan in 1937 and made famous by Bourbaki in his treatise. A notion similar to the notion of filter is the concept of net invented by Moore and Smith in 1922. (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
axiom df_fil {x3 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfil (cmpt z cvv (crab f2 (wral x3 (wi (wne (cin (cv f2) (cpw (cv x3))) c0) (wcel (cv x3) (cv f2))) (cpw (cv z))) (cfv (cv z) cfbas))) $;

--| The predicate "is a filter." (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem isfil {x3: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cin F (cpw (cv x3))) c0) (wcel (cv x3) F)) (cpw X))) $;

--| A filter is a filter base. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filfbas (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) $;

--| The empty set doesn't belong to a filter. (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem _0nelfil (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wn (wcel c0 F)) $;

--| An element of a filter is nonempty. (Contributed by FL, 24-May-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem fileln0 (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F)) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| A filter is a subset of the power set of the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filsspw (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss F (cpw X)) $;

--| An element of a filter is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filelss (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F)) (wss A2 X) $;

--| A filter is closed under taking supersets. (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filss (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (w3a (wcel A2 F) (wss B2 X) (wss A2 B2))) (wcel B2 F) $;

--| A filter is closed under taking intersections. (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filin (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 F)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) F) $;

--| The underlying set belongs to the filter. (Contributed by FL, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filtop (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel X F) $;

--| Derive the standard axioms of a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem isfil2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (w3a (w3a (wss F (cpw X)) (wn (wcel c0 F)) (wcel X F)) (wral x3 (wi (wrex y1 (wss (cv y1) (cv x3)) F) (wcel (cv x3) F)) (cpw X)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) F) F) F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isfild . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2013.)
theorem isfildlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) F) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel B2 F) (wa (wss B2 A2) (wsbc x3 ps B2))) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a set of the form ` { x e. ~P A | ph } ` to be a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 10-Apr-2024.)
theorem isfild {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 z) (A2 F: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) F) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wsbc x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wsbc x3 ps c0)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv y1) A2) (wss (cv z) (cv y1))) (wi (wsbc x3 ps (cv z)) (wsbc x3 ps (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv y1) A2) (wss (cv z) A2)) (wi (wa (wsbc x3 ps (cv y1)) (wsbc x3 ps (cv z))) (wsbc x3 ps (cin (cv y1) (cv z)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv A2 cfil)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a set of the form ` { x e. ~P A | ph } ` to be a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 10-Apr-2024.)
theorem isfild_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2 F: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) F) (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wsbc x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wsbc x3 ps c0)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv y1) A2) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wi (wsbc x3 ps (cv x3)) (wsbc x3 ps (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv y1) A2) (wss (cv x3) A2)) (wi (wa (wsbc x3 ps (cv y1)) (wsbc x3 ps (cv x3))) (wsbc x3 ps (cin (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv A2 cfil)) $;

--| A filter is closed under taking intersections. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filfi (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wceq (cfv F cfi) F) $;

--| The intersection of two elements of a filter can't be empty. (Contributed by FL, 16-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filinn0 (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 F)) (wne (cin A2 B2) c0) $;

--| A filter has the finite intersection property. Remark below Definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by FL, 20-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filintn0 (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (w3a (wss A2 F) (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 cfn))) (wne (cint A2) c0) $;

--| The empty set is not a filter. Remark below Definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by FL, 30-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem filn0 (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wne F c0) $;

--| The intersection of two filters is a filter. Use ~ fiint to extend this property to the intersection of a finite set of filters. Paragraph 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by FL, 17-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem infil (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel G (cfv X cfil))) (wcel (cin F G) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| A singleton is a filter. Example 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by FL, 16-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem snfil (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (csn A2) (cfv A2 cfil)) $;

--| A filter base on any set is also a filter base on any larger set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbasweak (F V X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wss F (cpw Y)) (wcel Y V)) (wcel F (cfv Y cfbas)) $;

--| Condition for a singleton to be a filter base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem snfbas (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 B2) (wne A2 c0) (wcel B2 V)) (wcel (csn A2) (cfv B2 cfbas)) $;

--| A condition for a set to generate a filter base. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fsubbas (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 cfi) (cfv X cfbas)) (w3a (wss A2 (cpw X)) (wne A2 c0) (wn (wcel c0 (cfv A2 cfi))))) $;

--| A filter base has the finite intersection property. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbasfip (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wn (wcel c0 (cfv F cfi))) $;

--| A helpful lemma for showing that certain sets generate filters. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbunfip {x3 y1: setvar} (F G X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel G (cfv Y cfbas))) (wb (wn (wcel c0 (cfv (cun F G) cfi))) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wne (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) c0) G) F)) $;

--| The filter generating class gives a filter for every filter base. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fgval {x3: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wceq (co X F cfg) (crab x3 (wne (cin F (cpw (cv x3))) c0) (cpw X))) $;

--| A condition for elements of a generated filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfg {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wb (wcel A2 (co X F cfg)) (wa (wss A2 X) (wrex x3 (wss (cv x3) A2) F))) $;

--| A filter base is a subset of its generated filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem ssfg (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wss F (co X F cfg)) $;

--| A bigger base generates a bigger filter. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fgss (F G X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel G (cfv X cfbas)) (wss F G)) (wss (co X F cfg) (co X G cfg)) $;

--| A condition for a filter to be finer than another involving their filter bases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fgss2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel G (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wss (co X F cfg) (co X G cfg)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cv y1) (cv x3)) G) F)) $;

--| A filter generates itself. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fgfil (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wceq (co X F cfg) F) $;

--| An element belongs to a filter iff any element below it does. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfilss {t: setvar} (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel A2 F) (wrex t (wss (cv t) A2) F)) $;

--| No filter containing a finite element is free. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filfinnfr (S F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel S F) (wcel S cfn)) (wne (cint F) c0) $;

--| A generated filter is a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem fgcl (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wcel (co X F cfg) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Absorption law for filter generation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem fgabs (F X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv Y cfbas)) (wss Y X)) (wceq (co X (co Y F cfg) cfg) (co X F cfg)) $;

--| The neighborhoods of a nonempty set is a filter. Example 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by FL, 18-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem neifil (S J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss S X) (wne S c0)) (wcel (cfv S (cfv J cnei)) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Recover the base set from a filter. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filunibas (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wceq (cuni F) X) $;

--| Two ways to express a filter on an unspecified base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filunirn (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cuni (crn cfil))) (wcel F (cfv (cuni F) cfil)) $;

--| A filter gives rise to a connected topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 6-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filconn (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel (cun F (csn c0)) cconn) $;

--| Given a filter on a domain, produce a filter on the range. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbasrn {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3) (F V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (cima F (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv X cfbas)) (wf X Y F) (wcel Y V)) (wcel C (cfv Y cfbas)) $;

--| The union of a nonempty set of filters with a common base and closed under pairwise union is a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filuni {f2 g2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss F (cfv X cfil)) (wne F c0) (wral f2 (wral g2 (wcel (cun (cv f2) (cv g2)) F) F) F)) (wcel (cuni F) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of a filter ` L ` to be a filter. (Contributed by FL, 2-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem trfil1 (A2 L Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wss A2 Y)) (wceq A2 (cuni (co L A2 crest))) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of a filter ` L ` to be a filter. (Contributed by FL, 2-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem trfil2 {v: setvar} (A2 L Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wss A2 Y)) (wb (wcel (co L A2 crest) (cfv A2 cfil)) (wral v (wne (cin (cv v) A2) c0) L)) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of a filter ` L ` to be a filter. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem trfil3 (A2 L Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wss A2 Y)) (wb (wcel (co L A2 crest) (cfv A2 cfil)) (wn (wcel (cdif Y A2) L))) $;

--| If ` A ` is a member of the filter, then the filter truncated to ` A ` is a subset of the original filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem trfilss (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F)) (wss (co F A2 crest) F) $;

--| If ` A ` is a member of the filter, then truncating ` F ` to ` A ` and regenerating the behavior outside ` A ` using ` filGen ` recovers the original filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem fgtr (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel A2 F)) (wceq (co X (co F A2 crest) cfg) F) $;

--| The trace operation and the ` filGen ` operation are inverses to one another in some sense, with ` filGen ` growing the base set and ` |``t ` shrinking it. See ~ fgtr for the converse cancellation law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem trfg (A2 F V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv A2 cfil)) (wss A2 X) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co (co X F cfg) A2 crest) F) $;

--| The trace, over a set ` A ` , of the filter of the neighborhoods of a point ` P ` is a filter iff ` P ` belongs to the closure of ` A ` . (This is ~ trfil2 applied to a filter of neighborhoods.) (Contributed by FL, 15-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem trnei (A2 P J Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv Y ctopon)) (wss A2 Y) (wcel P Y)) (wb (wcel P (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl))) (wcel (co (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) (cfv A2 cfil))) $;

--| Relative complements of the finite parts of an infinite set is a filter. When ` A = NN ` the set of the relative complements is called Frechet's filter and is used to define the concept of limit of a sequence. (Contributed by FL, 14-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem cfinfil {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wss A2 X) (wn (wcel A2 cfn))) (wcel (crab x3 (wcel (cdif A2 (cv x3)) cfn) (cpw X)) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| The set of all elements whose complement is dominated by the base set is a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem csdfil {x3: setvar} (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cdm ccrd)) (wbr com X cdom)) (wcel (crab x3 (wbr (cdif X (cv x3)) X csdm) (cpw X)) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| The supersets of a nonempty set which are also subsets of a given base set form a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem supfil {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2) (wne B2 c0)) (wcel (crab x3 (wss B2 (cv x3)) (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 cfil)) $;

--| The set of upper sets of integers is a filter base on ` ZZ ` , which corresponds to convergence of sequences on ` ZZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem zfbas:
  $ wcel (crn cuz) (cfv cz cfbas) $;

--| The restriction of the set of upper sets of integers to an upper set of integers is the set of upper sets of integers based at a point above the cutoff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem uzrest (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (co (crn cuz) Z crest) (cima cuz Z)) $;

--| The set of upper sets of integers based at a point in a fixed upper integer set like ` NN ` is a filter base on ` NN ` , which corresponds to convergence of sequences on ` NN ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem uzfbas (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wcel (cima cuz Z) (cfv Z cfbas)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the ultrafilters-on-a-set function.
term cufil: class;

--| Extend class notation with the ultrafilter lemma.
term cufl: class;

--| Define the set of ultrafilters on a set. An ultrafilter is a filter that gives a definite result for every subset. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_ufil {x3 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cufil (cmpt g2 cvv (crab f2 (wral x3 (wo (wcel (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wcel (cdif (cv g2) (cv x3)) (cv f2))) (cpw (cv g2))) (cfv (cv g2) cfil))) $;

--| Define the class of base sets for which the ultrafilter lemma ~ filssufil holds. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_ufl {x3 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cufl (cab x3 (wral f2 (wrex g2 (wss (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv x3) cufil)) (cfv (cv x3) cfil))) $;

--| The property of being an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem isufil {x3: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral x3 (wo (wcel (cv x3) F) (wcel (cdif X (cv x3)) F)) (cpw X))) $;

--| An ultrafilter is a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem ufilfil (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| For any subset of the base set of an ultrafilter, either the set is in the ultrafilter or the complement is. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem ufilss (S F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wss S X)) (wo (wcel S F) (wcel (cdif X S) F)) $;

--| The complement is in an ultrafilter iff the set is not. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem ufilb (S F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wss S X)) (wb (wn (wcel S F)) (wcel (cdif X S) F)) $;

--| Any filter finer than an ultrafilter is actually equal to it. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem ufilmax (F G X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wcel G (cfv X cfil)) (wss F G)) (wceq F G) $;

--| The maximal property of an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 30-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem isufil2 {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral f2 (wi (wss F (cv f2)) (wceq F (cv f2))) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| An ultrafilter is a prime filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufprim (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wss A2 X) (wss B2 X)) (wb (wo (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 F)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) F)) $;

--| Conditions for the trace of an ultrafilter ` L ` to be an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem trufil (A2 L Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv Y cufil)) (wss A2 Y)) (wb (wcel (co L A2 crest) (cfv A2 cufil)) (wcel A2 L)) $;

--| A filter is contained in some ultrafilter. This version of ~ filssufil contains the choice as a hypothesis (in the assumption that ` ~P ~P X ` is well-orderable). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filssufilg {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel (cpw (cpw X)) (cdm ccrd))) (wrex f2 (wss F (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| A filter is contained in some ultrafilter. (Requires the Axiom of Choice, via ~ numth3 .) (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 2-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem filssufil {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wrex f2 (wss F (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| Define the (strong) ultrafilter lemma, parameterized over base sets. A set ` X ` satisfies the ultrafilter lemma if every filter on ` X ` is a subset of some ultrafilter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem isufl {f2 g2: setvar} (V: class f2 g2) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel X cufl) (wral f2 (wrex g2 (wss (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cfv X cufil)) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| Property of a set that satisfies the ultrafilter lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufli {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cufl) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wrex f2 (wss F (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| Consequence of ~ filssufilg : a set whose double powerset is well-orderable satisfies the ultrafilter lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem numufl (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cpw (cpw X)) (cdm ccrd)) (wcel X cufl) $;

--| A finite set satisfies the ultrafilter lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fiufl (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X cfn) (wcel X cufl) $;

--| The axiom of choice implies the ultrafilter lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem acufl:
  $ wi wac (wceq cufl cvv) $;

--| If ` Y ` is a subset of ` X ` and filters extend to ultrafilters in ` X ` , then they still do in ` Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ssufl (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cufl) (wss Y X)) (wcel Y cufl) $;

--| If the ultrafilter containing a given filter is unique, the filter is an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 3-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ufileu {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wb (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wreu f2 (wss F (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil))) $;

--| A filter is equal to the intersection of the ultrafilters containing it. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem filufint {f2: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wceq (cint (crab f2 (wss F (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil))) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fixufil and ~ uffixfr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffix {x3: setvar} (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wcel (csn (csn A2)) (cfv X cfbas)) (wceq (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (cpw X)) (co X (csn (csn A2)) cfg))) $;

--| The condition describing a fixed ultrafilter always produces an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Jul-2015.)
theorem fixufil {x3: setvar} (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (cpw X)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| An ultrafilter is either fixed or free. A fixed ultrafilter is called principal (generated by a single element ` A ` ), and a free ultrafilter is called nonprincipal (having empty intersection). Note that examples of free ultrafilters cannot be defined in ZFC without some form of global choice. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffixfr {x3: setvar} (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wb (wcel A2 (cint F)) (wceq F (crab x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (cpw X)))) $;

--| A classification of fixed ultrafilters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffix2 {x3 y1: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wb (wne (cint F) c0) (wrex x3 (wceq F (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cpw X))) X)) $;

--| The singleton of the generator of a fixed ultrafilter is in the filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffixsn (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wcel A2 (cint F))) (wcel (csn A2) F) $;

--| An ultrafilter is generated by at most one element (because free ultrafilters have no generators and fixed ultrafilters have exactly one). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-May-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufildom1 (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wbr (cint F) c1o cdom) $;

--| An ultrafilter containing a finite element is fixed. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffinfix {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class x3 y1) (F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wcel S F) (wcel S cfn)) (wrex x3 (wceq F (crab y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cpw X))) X) $;

--| An ultrafilter is free iff it contains the Fr&eacute;chet filter ~ cfinfil as a subset. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 2-Aug-2015.)
theorem cfinufil {x3: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wb (wceq (cint F) c0) (wss (crab x3 (wcel (cdif X (cv x3)) cfn) (cpw X)) F)) $;

--| An infinite subset is contained in a free ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 6-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Dec-2013.)
theorem ufinffr {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X B2) (wss A2 X) (wbr com A2 cdom)) (wrex f2 (wa (wcel A2 (cv f2)) (wceq (cint (cv f2)) c0)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| Any infinite set has an ultrafilter on it whose elements are of the same cardinality as the set. Any such ultrafilter is necessarily free. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufilen {x3 f2: setvar} (X: class):
  $ wi (wbr com X cdom) (wrex f2 (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) X cen) (cv f2)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| An ultrafilter gives rise to a connected door topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 6-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 3-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufildr (F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cun F (csn c0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wceq (cun J (cfv J ccld)) (cpw X)) $;

--| There are no definable free ultrafilters in ZFC. However, there are free ultrafilters in some choice-denying constructions. Here we show that given an amorphous set (a.k.a. a Ia-finite I-infinite set) ` X ` , the set of infinite subsets of ` X ` is a free ultrafilter on ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2015.)
theorem fin1aufil (F X: class):
  $ wceq F (cdif (cpw X) cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (cdif cfin1a cfn)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wceq (cint F) c0)) $;

--| Extend class definition to include the neighborhood filter mapping function.
term cfm: class;

--| Extend class notation with a function returning the limit of a filter.
term cflim: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the function for filter-based function limits.
term cflf: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the cluster point function on filters.
term cfcls: class;

--| Extend class definition to include the function for cluster points of a function.
term cfcf: class;

--| Define a function that takes a filter to a neighborhood filter of the range. (Since we now allow filter bases to have support smaller than the base set, the function has to come first to ensure that curryings are sets.) (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Jul-2015.)
axiom df_fm {x3 y1 t f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfm (cmpo x3 f2 cvv cvv (cmpt y1 (cfv (cdm (cv f2)) cfbas) (co (cv x3) (crn (cmpt t (cv y1) (cima (cv f2) (cv t)))) cfg))) $;

--| Define a function (indexed by a topology ` j ` ) whose value is the limits of a filter ` f ` . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.)
axiom df_flim {x3 f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cflim (cmpo j f2 ctop (cuni (crn cfil)) (crab x3 (wa (wss (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv j) cnei)) (cv f2)) (wss (cv f2) (cpw (cuni (cv j))))) (cuni (cv j)))) $;

--| Define a function that gives the limits of a function ` f ` in the filter sense. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Oct-2009.)
axiom df_flf {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cflf (cmpo x3 y1 ctop (cuni (crn cfil)) (cmpt f2 (co (cuni (cv x3)) (cuni (cv y1)) cmap) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (co (cuni (cv x3)) (cv f2) cfm)) cflim))) $;

--| Define a function that takes a filter in a topology to its set of cluster points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_fcls {x3 f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfcls (cmpo j f2 ctop (cuni (crn cfil)) (cif (wceq (cuni (cv j)) (cuni (cv f2))) (ciin x3 (cv f2) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) ccl))) c0)) $;

--| Define a function that gives the cluster points of a function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Nov-2009.)
axiom df_fcf {f2 g2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfcf (cmpo j f2 ctop (cuni (crn cfil)) (cmpt g2 (co (cuni (cv j)) (cuni (cv f2)) cmap) (co (cv j) (cfv (cv f2) (co (cuni (cv j)) (cv g2) cfm)) cfcls))) $;

--| Introduce a function that takes a function from a filtered domain to a set and produces a filter which consists of supersets of images of filter elements. The functions which are dealt with by this function are similar to nets in topology. For example, suppose we have a sequence filtered by the filter generated by its tails under the usual positive integer ordering. Then the elements of this filter are precisely the supersets of tails of this sequence. Under this definition, it is not too difficult to see that the limit of a function in the filter sense captures the notion of convergence of a sequence. As a result, the notion of a filter generalizes many ideas associated with sequences, and this function is one way to make that relationship precise in Metamath. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmval {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) (co X (crn (cmpt y1 B2 (cima F (cv y1)))) cfg)) $;

--| A mapping filter is a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfil (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Pushing-forward via a function induces a mapping on filters. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmf (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2) (wf Y X F)) (wf (cfv Y cfbas) (cfv X cfil) (co X F cfm)) $;

--| A finer filter produces a finer image filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 16-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmss (A2 B2 C F X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wcel C (cfv Y cfbas))) (wa (wf Y X F) (wss B2 C))) (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) (cfv C (co X F cfm))) $;

--| An element of a mapping filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 8-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfm {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X C) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 (co X F cfm))) (wa (wss A2 X) (wrex x3 (wss (cima F (cv x3)) A2) B2))) $;

--| An element of a mapping filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 26-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfm2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (co Y B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X C) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 (co X F cfm))) (wa (wss A2 X) (wrex x3 (wss (cima F (cv x3)) A2) L))) $;

--| The image filter of a filter base is the same as the image filter of its generated filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfg (B2 C F L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (co Y B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X C) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) (cfv L (co X F cfm))) $;

--| An alternate formulation of elementhood in a mapping filter that requires ` F ` to be onto. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 1-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfm3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (co Y B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wfo Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv B2 (co X F cfm))) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (cima F (cv x3))) L)) $;

--| An image of a filter element is in the image filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem imaelfm (A2 B2 S F L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (co Y B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel S L)) (wcel (cima F S) (cfv B2 (co X F cfm))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rnelfm . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Nov-2009.)
theorem rnelfmlem {x3: setvar} (A2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel Y A2) (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel (crn F) L)) (wcel (crn (cmpt x3 L (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)))) (cfv Y cfbas)) $;

--| A condition for a filter to be an image filter for a given function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem rnelfm (A2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel Y A2) (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel L (crn (co X F cfm))) (wcel (crn F) L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmfnfm . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 18-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfnfmlem1 (ph: wff) (t: setvar) (B2 F L X Y: class) (s1: setvar):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (cv s1) (cfv B2 cfi)) (wi (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv t)) (wi (wss (cv t) X) (wcel (cv t) L)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmfnfm . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 19-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfnfmlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (t: setvar) (B2 F L X Y: class) (s1: setvar):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wrex x3 (wceq (cv s1) (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3))) L) (wi (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv t)) (wi (wss (cv t) X) (wcel (cv t) L)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmfnfm . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 19-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfnfmlem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (crn (cmpt x3 L (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)))) cfi) (crn (cmpt x3 L (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmfnfm . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 19-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfnfmlem4 {x3 s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (t: setvar) (B2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv t) L) (wa (wss (cv t) X) (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv t)) (cfv (cun B2 (crn (cmpt x3 L (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3))))) cfi)))) $;

--| A filter finer than an image filter is an image filter of the same function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmfnfm {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wa (wss B2 (cv f2)) (wceq L (cfv (cv f2) (co X F cfm)))) (cfv Y cfil)) $;

--| An image filter of an ultrafilter is an ultrafilter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmufil (A2 F L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X A2) (wcel L (cfv Y cufil)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel (cfv L (co X F cfm)) (cfv X cufil)) $;

--| The filter map applied to the identity. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 8-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmid (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wceq (cfv F (co X (cres cid X) cfm)) F) $;

--| Composition of image filters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem fmco (B2 F G V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y W) (wcel B2 (cfv Z cfbas))) (wa (wf Y X F) (wf Z Y G))) (wceq (cfv B2 (co X (ccom F G) cfm)) (cfv (cfv B2 (co Y G cfm)) (co X F cfm))) $;

--| The ultrafilter lemma property is a cardinal invariant, so since it transfers to subsets it also transfers over set dominance. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufldom (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cufl) (wbr Y X cdom)) (wcel Y cufl) $;

--| The set of limit points of a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimval {x3: setvar} (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel F (cuni (crn cfil)))) (wceq (co J F cflim) (crab x3 (wa (wss (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) F) (wss F (cpw X))) X)) $;

--| The predicate "is a limit point of a filter." (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem elflim2 (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wa (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel F (cuni (crn cfil))) (wss F (cpw X))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wss (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) F))) $;

--| Reverse closure for the limit point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimtop (A2 F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A filter contains the neighborhood filter as a subfilter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimneiss (A2 F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wss (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) F) $;

--| A filter contains all of the neighborhoods of its limit points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem flimnei (A2 F J N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wcel N (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)))) (wcel N F) $;

--| A limit point of a filter belongs to its base set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem flimelbas (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wcel A2 X) $;

--| Reverse closure for the limit point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimfil (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the limit point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimtopon (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wb (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| The predicate "is a limit point of a filter." (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem elflim (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wss (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) F))) $;

--| A limit point of a filter is a limit point of a finer filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimss2 (F G J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss G F)) (wss (co J G cflim) (co J F cflim)) $;

--| A limit point of a filter is a limit point in a coarser topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimss1 (F J K X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss J K)) (wss (co K F cflim) (co J F cflim)) $;

--| A point is a limit point of its neighborhood filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem neiflim (A2 J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel A2 (co J (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) cflim)) $;

--| The condition for being a limit point of a filter still holds if one only considers open neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem flimopn {x3: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral x3 (wi (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) F)) J))) $;

--| A condition for a filter to converge to a point involving one of its bases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 4-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbflim {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq F (co X B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel B2 (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral x3 (wi (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wrex y1 (wss (cv y1) (cv x3)) B2)) J))) $;

--| A condition for a filter base ` B ` to converge to a point ` A ` . Use neighborhoods instead of open neighborhoods. Compare ~ fbflim . (Contributed by FL, 4-Jul-2011.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem fbflim2 {x3 n: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class n) (J X: class):
  $ wceq F (co X B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel B2 (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral n (wrex x3 (wss (cv x3) (cv n)) B2) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))))) $;

--| The convergent points of a filter are a subset of the closure of any of the filter sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimclsi (S F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel S F) (wss (co J F cflim) (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| If ` A ` and ` B ` are both limits of the same filter, then all neighborhoods of ` A ` and ` B ` intersect. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem hausflimlem (A2 B2 U F J V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wcel B2 (co J F cflim))) (wa (wcel U J) (wcel V J)) (wa (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 V))) (wne (cin U V) c0) $;

--| One direction of ~ hausflim . A filter in a Hausdorff space has at most one limit. (Contributed by FL, 14-Nov-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem hausflimi {x3: setvar} (F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cha) (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co J F cflim))) $;

--| A condition for a topology to be Hausdorff in terms of filters. A topology is Hausdorff iff every filter has at most one limit point. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 5-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem hausflim {x3 f2: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cha) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral f2 (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co J (cv f2) cflim))) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| Fineness is properly characterized by the property that every limit point of a filter in the finer topology is a limit point in the coarser topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimcf {f2: setvar} (J K X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon))) (wb (wss J K) (wral f2 (wss (co K (cv f2) cflim) (co J (cv f2) cflim)) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| The set of limit points in a restricted topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem flimrest (F J X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel Y F)) (wceq (co (co J Y crest) (co F Y crest) cflim) (cin (co J F cflim) Y)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ flimcls . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimclslem (A2 S F J X: class):
  $ wceq F (co X (cfv (cun (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) (csn S)) cfi) cfg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss S X) (wcel A2 (cfv S (cfv J ccl)))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel S F) (wcel A2 (co J F cflim))) $;

--| Closure in terms of filter convergence. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimcls {f2: setvar} (A2 S J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wrex f2 (wa (wcel S (cv f2)) (wcel A2 (co J (cv f2) cflim))) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| If ` A ` is a limit point of the filter ` F ` , then all the points which specialize ` A ` (in the specialization preorder) are also limit points. Thus, the set of limit points is a union of closed sets (although this is only nontrivial for non-T1 spaces). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem flimsncls (A2 F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wss (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J ccl)) (co J F cflim)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hauspwpwdom . Points in the closure of a set in a Hausdorff space are characterized by the open neighborhoods they extend into the generating set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem hauspwpwf1 {x3 j a: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 j a) (J: class) (X: class a):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (cab a (wrex j (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv j)) (wceq (cv a) (cin (cv j) A2))) J))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (wss A2 X)) (wf1 (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (cpw (cpw A2)) F) $;

--| If ` X ` is a Hausdorff space, then the cardinality of the closure of a set ` A ` is bounded by the double powerset of ` A ` . In particular, a Hausdorff space with a dense subset ` A ` has cardinality at most ` ~P ~P A ` , and a separable Hausdorff space has cardinality at most ` ~P ~P NN ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2015.)
theorem hauspwpwdom (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cha) (wss A2 X)) (wbr (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (cpw (cpw A2)) cdom) $;

--| Given a topology and a filtered set, return the convergence function on the functions from the filtered set to the base set of the topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Oct-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flffval {f2: setvar} (J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil))) (wceq (co J L cflf) (cmpt f2 (co X Y cmap) (co J (cfv L (co X (cv f2) cfm)) cflim))) $;

--| Given a function from a filtered set to a topological space, define the set of limit points of the function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 8-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flfval (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wceq (cfv F (co J L cflf)) (co J (cfv L (co X F cfm)) cflim)) $;

--| The property of being a limit point of a function in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flfnei {n s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral n (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv n)) L) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))))) $;

--| A neighborhood of a limit point of a function contains the image of a filter element. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem flfneii {s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (F J L N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wcel N (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)))) (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) N) L) $;

--| The property of being a limit point of a function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 8-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem isflf {o s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv o)) L)) J))) $;

--| A limit point of a function is in the topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem flfelbas (A2 F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf)))) (wcel A2 X) $;

--| Limit points of a function can be defined using filter bases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 9-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem flffbas {o s1: setvar} (A2 B2 F J L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (co Y B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv o)) B2)) J))) $;

--| Limit points of a function can be defined using topological bases. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem flftg {o s1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class s1) (F: class) (J: class o) (L: class) (X Y: class o):
  $ wceq J (cfv B2 ctg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wrex s1 (wss (cima F (cv s1)) (cv o)) L)) B2))) $;

--| If a function has its values in a Hausdorff space, then it has at most one limit value. (Contributed by FL, 14-Nov-2010.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem hausflf {x3: setvar} (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cha) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv F (co J L cflf)))) $;

--| If a convergent function has its values in a Hausdorff space, then it has a unique limit. (Contributed by FL, 14-Nov-2010.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Aug-2015.)
theorem hausflf2 (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J cha) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wne (cfv F (co J L cflf)) c0)) (wbr (cfv F (co J L cflf)) c1o cen) $;

--| Forward direction of ~ cnpflf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpflfi (A2 F J K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co J L cflim)) (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp)))) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv F (co K L cflf))) $;

--| ` F ` is continuous at point ` A ` iff a limit of ` F ` when ` x ` tends to ` A ` is ` ( F `` A ) ` . Proposition 9 of [BourbakiTop1] p. TG I.50. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.)
theorem cnpflf2 (A2 F J K L X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv F (co K L cflf))))) $;

--| Continuity of a function at a point in terms of filter limits. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 7-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpflf {f2: setvar} (A2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral f2 (wi (wcel A2 (co J (cv f2) cflim)) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv F (co K (cv f2) cflf)))) (cfv X cfil)))) $;

--| A function is continuous iff it respects filter limits. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 6-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 7-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnflf {x3 f2: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv F (co K (cv f2) cflf))) (co J (cv f2) cflim)) (cfv X cfil)))) $;

--| A function is continuous iff it respects filter limits. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnflf2 {f2: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral f2 (wss (cima F (co J (cv f2) cflim)) (cfv F (co K (cv f2) cflf))) (cfv X cfil)))) $;

--| A continuous function preserves filter limits. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem flfcnp (A2 F G J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wcel G (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))))) (wcel (cfv A2 G) (cfv (ccom G F) (co K L cflf))) $;

--| The topological limit relation on functions can be written in terms of the filter limit along the filter generated by the upper integer sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem lmflf (P F J L M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq L (co Z (cima cuz Z) cfg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel M cz) (wf Z X F)) (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wcel P (cfv F (co J L cflf)))) $;

--| Two sequences converge in a filter iff the sequence of their ordered pairs converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem txflf {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S: class n) (F G: class) (H J K L: class n) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z Y G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n Z (cop (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (cv n) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cop R S) (cfv H (co (co J K ctx) L cflf))) (wa (wcel R (cfv F (co J L cflf))) (wcel S (cfv G (co K L cflf))))) $;

--| The image of a convergent sequence under a continuous map is convergent to the image of the original point. Binary operation version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem flfcnp2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S J K L N: class x3) (O X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z cfil)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cfv (cmpt x3 Z A2) (co J L cflf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv (cmpt x3 Z B2) (co K L cflf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O (cfv (cop R S) (co (co J K ctx) N ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co R S O) (cfv (cmpt x3 Z (co A2 B2 O)) (co N L cflf))) $;

--| The set of all cluster points of a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsval {t: setvar} (F J: class) (X Y: class t):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel F (cfv Y cfil))) (wceq (co J F cfcls) (cif (wceq X Y) (ciin t F (cfv (cv t) (cfv J ccl))) c0)) $;

--| A cluster point of a filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem isfcls {s1: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral s1 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv s1) (cfv J ccl))) F)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the cluster point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsfil (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Reverse closure for the cluster point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclstop (A2 F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for the cluster point predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclstopon (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wb (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| A cluster point of a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem isfcls2 {s1: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wral s1 (wcel A2 (cfv (cv s1) (cfv J ccl))) F)) $;

--| Write the cluster point condition in terms of open sets. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 10-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsopn {o s1: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wral s1 (wne (cin (cv o) (cv s1)) c0) F)) J))) $;

--| An open neighborhood of a cluster point of a filter intersects any element of that filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsopni (A2 S U F J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (w3a (wcel U J) (wcel A2 U) (wcel S F))) (wne (cin U S) c0) $;

--| A cluster point is in the base set. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclselbas (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wcel A2 X) $;

--| A neighborhood of a cluster point of a filter intersects any element of that filter. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsneii (A2 S F J N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wcel N (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))) (wcel S F)) (wne (cin N S) c0) $;

--| The set of cluster points is a subset of the closure of any filter element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclssscls (S F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel S F) (wss (co J F cfcls) (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| Cluster points in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsnei {n s1: setvar} (A2 F J: class) (X: class n):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral n (wral s1 (wne (cin (cv n) (cv s1)) c0) F) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))))) $;

--| The filter of supersets of ` X \ U ` does not cluster at any point of the open set ` U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem supnfcls {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (U J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel U J) (wcel A2 U)) (wn (wcel A2 (co J (crab x3 (wss (cdif X U) (cv x3)) (cpw X)) cfcls))) $;

--| Cluster points in terms of filter bases. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsbas {o s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (B2: class) (F J X: class s1):
  $ wceq F (co X B2 cfg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel B2 (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wral s1 (wne (cin (cv o) (cv s1)) c0) B2)) J))) $;

--| A finer topology has fewer cluster points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsss1 (F J K X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss J K)) (wss (co K F cfcls) (co J F cfcls)) $;

--| A finer filter has fewer cluster points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsss2 (F G J X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wss F G)) (wss (co J G cfcls) (co J F cfcls)) $;

--| The set of cluster points in a restricted topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem fclsrest (F J X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel Y F)) (wceq (co (co J Y crest) (co F Y crest) cfcls) (cin (co J F cfcls) Y)) $;

--| Characterization of fineness of topologies in terms of cluster points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclscf {f2: setvar} (J K X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv X ctopon))) (wb (wss J K) (wral f2 (wss (co K (cv f2) cfcls) (co J (cv f2) cfcls)) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| A limit point is a cluster point. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimfcls (F J: class):
  $ wss (co J F cflim) (co J F cfcls) $;

--| A filter clusters at a point iff a finer filter converges to it. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclsfnflim {g2: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cfcls)) (wrex g2 (wa (wss F (cv g2)) (wcel A2 (co J (cv g2) cflim))) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| A filter converges to a point iff every finer filter clusters there. Along with ~ fclsfnflim , this theorem illustrates the duality between convergence and clustering. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem flimfnfcls {g2: setvar} (A2 F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wb (wcel A2 (co J F cflim)) (wral g2 (wi (wss F (cv g2)) (wcel A2 (co J (cv g2) cfcls))) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| Forward direction of ~ fclscmp . Every filter clusters in a compact space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclscmpi (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ccmp) (wcel F (cfv X cfil))) (wne (co J F cfcls) c0) $;

--| A space is compact iff every filter clusters. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 20-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 8-Aug-2015.)
theorem fclscmp {f2: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wb (wcel J ccmp) (wral f2 (wne (co J (cv f2) cfcls) c0) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| The cluster points of an ultrafilter are its limit points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffclsflim (F J X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) (wceq (co J F cfcls) (co J F cflim)) $;

--| A space is compact iff every ultrafilter converges. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 11-Dec-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ufilcmp {f2: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cufl) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon))) (wb (wcel J ccmp) (wral f2 (wne (co J (cv f2) cflim) c0) (cfv X cufil))) $;

--| The set of cluster points of a function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem fcfval (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wceq (cfv F (co J L cfcf)) (co J (cfv L (co X F cfm)) cfcls)) $;

--| The property of being a cluster point of a function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem isfcf {o s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cfcf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral o (wi (wcel A2 (cv o)) (wral s1 (wne (cin (cv o) (cima F (cv s1))) c0) L)) J))) $;

--| The property of being a cluster point of a function in terms of neighborhoods. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 26-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem fcfnei {n s1: setvar} (A2: class s1) (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cfcf))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wral n (wral s1 (wne (cin (cv n) (cima F (cv s1))) c0) L) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))))) $;

--| A cluster point of a function is in the base set of the topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 26-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem fcfelbas (A2 F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cfcf)))) (wcel A2 X) $;

--| A neighborhood of a cluster point of a function contains a function value from every tail. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem fcfneii (A2 S F J L N X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv F (co J L cfcf))) (wcel N (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J cnei))) (wcel S L))) (wne (cin N (cima F S)) c0) $;

--| A limit point of a function is a cluster point of the function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem flfssfcf (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cfil)) (wf Y X F)) (wss (cfv F (co J L cflf)) (cfv F (co J L cfcf))) $;

--| If the domain filter is an ultrafilter, the cluster points of the function are the limit points. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 12-Dec-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem uffcfflf (F J L X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel L (cfv Y cufil)) (wf Y X F)) (wceq (cfv F (co J L cfcf)) (cfv F (co J L cflf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnpfcf . If a function is continuous at a point, it respects clustering there. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 20-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpfcfi (A2 F J K L: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K ctop) (wcel A2 (co J L cfcls)) (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp)))) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv F (co K L cfcf))) $;

--| A function ` F ` is continuous at point ` A ` iff ` F ` respects cluster points there. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnpfcf {f2: setvar} (A2 F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral f2 (wi (wcel A2 (co J (cv f2) cfcls)) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cfv F (co K (cv f2) cfcf)))) (cfv X cfil)))) $;

--| Continuity of a function in terms of cluster points of a function. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Nov-2009.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 9-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfcf {x3 f2: setvar} (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv F (co K (cv f2) cfcf))) (co J (cv f2) cfcls)) (cfv X cfil)))) $;

--| A continuous function's value is always in the trace of its filter limit. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Aug-2020.)
theorem flfcntr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K X: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J A2 crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X F) (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn X) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alexsub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem alexsublem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 F J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cufl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv (cfv B2 cfi) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wss (cv x3) B2) (wceq X (cuni (cv x3))))) (wrex y1 (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv X cufil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J F cflim) c0) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| The Alexander Subbase Theorem: If ` B ` is a subbase for the topology ` J ` , and any cover taken from ` B ` has a finite subcover, then the generated topology is compact. This proof uses the ultrafilter lemma; see ~ alexsubALT for a proof using Zorn's lemma. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem alexsub {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cufl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv (cfv B2 cfi) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wss (cv x3) B2) (wceq X (cuni (cv x3))))) (wrex y1 (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| Biconditional form of the Alexander Subbase Theorem ~ alexsub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem alexsubb {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cufl) (wceq X (cuni B2))) (wb (wcel (cfv (cfv B2 cfi) ctg) ccmp) (wral x3 (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv x3))) (wrex y1 (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn))) (cpw B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alexsubALT . A compact space has a subbase such that every cover taken from it has a finite subcover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Jan-2010.)
theorem alexsubALTlem1 {x3 c d: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ccmp) (wex x3 (wa (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg)) (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wceq X (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alexsubALT . Every subset of a base which has no finite subcover is a subset of a maximal such collection. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 27-Jan-2010.)
theorem alexsubALTlem2 {z v u b c d: setvar} (x3: setvar) (J X: class) (a: setvar):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg)) (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wceq X (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))) (wcel (cv a) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi)))) (wral b (wn (wceq X (cuni (cv b)))) (cin (cpw (cv a)) cfn))) (wrex u (wral v (wn (wpss (cv u) (cv v))) (cun (crab z (wa (wss (cv a) (cv z)) (wral b (wn (wceq X (cuni (cv b)))) (cin (cpw (cv z)) cfn))) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi))) (csn c0))) (cun (crab z (wa (wss (cv a) (cv z)) (wral b (wn (wceq X (cuni (cv b)))) (cin (cpw (cv z)) cfn))) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi))) (csn c0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alexsubALT . If a point is covered by a collection taken from the base with no finite subcover, a set from the subbase can be added that covers the point so that the resulting collection has no finite subcover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.)
theorem alexsubALTlem3 {x3 y1 w u n s1 b c d: setvar} (t: setvar) (J X: class) (a: setvar):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (w3a (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg)) (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wceq X (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))) (wcel (cv a) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi)))) (wa (wcel (cv u) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi))) (wa (wss (cv a) (cv u)) (wral b (wn (wceq X (cuni (cv b)))) (cin (cpw (cv u)) cfn))))) (wcel (cv w) (cv u))) (wa (wa (wcel (cv t) (cin (cpw (cv x3)) cfn)) (wceq (cv w) (cint (cv t)))) (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cuni (cin (cv x3) (cv u)))))))) (wrex s1 (wral n (wn (wceq X (cuni (cv n)))) (cin (cpw (cun (cv u) (csn (cv s1)))) cfn)) (cv t)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ alexsubALT . If any cover taken from a subbase has a finite subcover, any cover taken from the corresponding base has a finite subcover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Dec-2013.)
theorem alexsubALTlem4 {x3 a b c d: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg)) (wi (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wceq X (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))) (wral a (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv a))) (wrex b (wceq X (cuni (cv b))) (cin (cpw (cv a)) cfn))) (cpw (cfv (cv x3) cfi)))) $;

--| The Alexander Subbase Theorem: a space is compact iff it has a subbase such that any cover taken from the subbase has a finite subcover. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 24-Jan-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem alexsubALT {x3 c d: setvar} (J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J ccmp) (wex x3 (wa (wceq J (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfi) ctg)) (wral c (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv c))) (wrex d (wceq X (cuni (cv d))) (cin (cpw (cv c)) cfn))) (cpw (cv x3))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmplem1 {w u k n: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2: class) (S: class w) (F V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ccmp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq X (cuni (cun (crn S) (csn X)))) (wceq (cfv F cpt) (cfv (cfv (cun (crn S) (csn X)) cfi) ctg))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmplem2 {z w u k n: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2: class) (S: class w) (U: class w n) (F V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ccmp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (crn S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex z (wceq X (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun k (crab n (wn (wbr (cuni (cfv (cv n) F)) c1o cen)) A2) (cuni (cfv (cv k) F))) (cdm ccrd)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmplem3 {z w u f2 k n: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2: class) (S: class w f2) (U: class w f2 n) (F: class) (K: class z w k n) (V: class f2) (X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ccmp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (crn S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex z (wceq X (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn))) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab u (wcel (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u)) U) (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cdif (cuni (cfv (cv k) F)) (cuni K))) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmplem4 {z w u k n: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2: class) (S: class w) (U: class w n) (F: class) (K: class z w k n) (V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ccmp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (crn S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex z (wceq X (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn))) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab u (wcel (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u)) U) (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ ptcmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmplem5 {w u k n: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2: class) (S: class w) (F V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpo k u A2 (cfv (cv k) F) (cima (ccnv (cmpt w X (cfv (cv k) (cv w)))) (cv u))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cixp n A2 (cuni (cfv (cv n) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 ccmp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F cpt) ccmp) $;

--| Tychonoff's theorem: The product of compact spaces is compact. The choice principles needed are encoded in the last hypothesis: the base set of the product must be well-orderable and satisfy the ultrafilter lemma. Both these assumptions are satisfied if ` ~P ~P X ` is well-orderable, so if we assume the Axiom of Choice we can eliminate them (see ~ ptcmp ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmpg (A2 F J V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv F cpt) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ccmp F) (wcel X (cin cufl (cdm ccrd)))) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| Tychonoff's theorem: The product of compact spaces is compact. The proof uses the Axiom of Choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem ptcmp (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 ccmp F)) (wcel (cfv F cpt) ccmp) $;

--| Extend class notation with the continuous extension operation.
term ccnext: class;

--| Define the continuous extension of a given function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.)
axiom df_cnext {x3 f2 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccnext (cmpo j k ctop ctop (cmpt f2 (co (cuni (cv k)) (cuni (cv j)) cpm) (ciun x3 (cfv (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (cv j) ccl)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv f2) (co (cv k) (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv j) cnei)) (cdm (cv f2)) crest) cflf)))))) $;

--| The function applying continuous extension to a given function ` f ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextval {x3 f2: setvar} (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wceq (co J K ccnext) (cmpt f2 (co (cuni K) (cuni J) cpm) (ciun x3 (cfv (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv J ccl)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv (cv f2) (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) (cdm (cv f2)) crest) cflf)))))) $;

--| The continuous extension of a given function ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextfval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wss A2 X))) (wceq (cfv F (co J K ccnext)) (ciun x3 (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf))))) $;

--| In the general case, a continuous extension is a relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextrel (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wss A2 C))) (wrel (cfv F (co J K ccnext))) $;

--| If the target space is Hausdorff, a continuous extension is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextfun (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K cha)) (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wss A2 C))) (wfun (cfv F (co J K ccnext))) $;

--| The value of the continuous extension of a given function ` F ` at a point ` X ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextfvval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K X: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wne (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf)) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X C)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv F (co J K ccnext))) (cuni (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn X) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf)))) $;

--| Extension by continuity. The extension by continuity is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wne (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C B2 (cfv F (co J K ccnext))) $;

--| Extension by continuity. Theorem 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.57. Given a topology ` J ` on ` C ` , a subset ` A ` dense in ` C ` , this states a condition for ` F ` from ` A ` to a regular space ` K ` to be extensible by continuity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jan-2018.)
theorem cnextcn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wne (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K creg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co J K ccnext)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| ` F ` and its extension by continuity agree on the domain of ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jan-2018.)
theorem cnextfres1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wne (cfv F (co K (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) cflf)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K creg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J A2 crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv F (co J K ccnext)) A2) F) $;

--| ` F ` and its extension by continuity agree on the domain of ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem cnextfres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K X: class):
  $ wceq C (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J A2 crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv F (co J K ccnext))) (cfv X F)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all topological monoids.
term ctmd: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all topological groups.
term ctgp: class;

--| Define the class of all topological monoids. A topological monoid is a monoid whose operation is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_tmd {f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctmd (crab f2 (wsbc j (wcel (cfv (cv f2) cplusf) (co (co (cv j) (cv j) ctx) (cv j) ccn)) (cfv (cv f2) ctopn)) (cin cmnd ctps)) $;

--| Define the class of all topological groups. A topological group is a group whose operation and inverse function are continuous. (Contributed by FL, 18-Apr-2010.)
axiom df_tgp {f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctgp (crab f2 (wsbc j (wcel (cfv (cv f2) cminusg) (co (cv j) (cv j) ccn)) (cfv (cv f2) ctopn)) (cin cgrp ctmd)) $;

--| The predicate "is a topological monoid". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem istmd (F G J: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G ctmd) (w3a (wcel G cmnd) (wcel G ctps) (wcel F (co (co J J ctx) J ccn))) $;

--| A topological monoid is a monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdmnd (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ctmd) (wcel G cmnd) $;

--| A topological monoid is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdtps (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ctmd) (wcel G ctps) $;

--| The predicate "is a topological group". Definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. III.1. (Contributed by FL, 18-Apr-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem istgp (G I J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G ctgp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G ctmd) (wcel I (co J J ccn))) $;

--| A topological group is a group. (Contributed by FL, 18-Apr-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgpgrp (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| A topological group is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgptmd (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel G ctmd) $;

--| A topological group is a topological space. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgptps (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel G ctps) $;

--| The topology of a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tmdtopon (G J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctmd) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| The topology of a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgptopon (G J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| In a topological monoid, the operation ` F ` representing the functionalization of the operator slot ` +g ` is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdcn (F G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctmd) (wcel F (co (co J J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| In a topological group, the operation ` F ` representing the functionalization of the operator slot ` +g ` is continuous. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgpcn (F G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel F (co (co J J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| In a topological group, the inverse function is continuous. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by FL, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem tgpinv (G I J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel I (co J J ccn)) $;

--| The inverse function in a topological group is a homeomorphism from the group to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem grpinvhmeo (G I J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel I (co J J chmeo)) $;

--| Continuity of the group sum; analogue of ~ cnmpt12f which cannot be used directly because ` +g ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1plusg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl: class x3) (G J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 c_pl)) (co K J ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of the group sum; analogue of ~ cnmpt22f which cannot be used directly because ` +g ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2plusg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl: class x3 y1) (G J: class) (K: class y1) (L: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 c_pl)) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| Write out the definition of continuity of ` +g ` explicitly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdcn2 {x3 y1 v u: setvar} (B2 c_pl: class x3 y1 v u) (U G: class) (J X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G ctmd) (wcel U J)) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel (co X Y c_pl) U))) (wrex u (wrex v (w3a (wcel X (cv u)) (wcel Y (cv v)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) U) (cv v)) (cv u))) J) J) $;

--| In a topological group, the "subtraction" (or "division") is continuous. Axiom GT' of [BourbakiTop1] p. III.1. (Contributed by FL, 21-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem tgpsubcn (G J c_mi: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel c_mi (co (co J J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| A group with a topology is a topological group iff the subtraction operation is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem istgp2 (G J c_mi: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G ctgp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G ctps) (wcel c_mi (co (co J J ctx) J ccn))) $;

--| In a topological monoid, the n-times group multiple function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdmulg {x3: setvar} (B2 c_x G J N: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctmd) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 (co N (cv x3) c_x)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| In a topological group, the n-times group multiple function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpmulg {x3: setvar} (B2 c_x G J N: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 (co N (cv x3) c_x)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| In a topological monoid, the group multiple function is jointly continuous (although this is not saying much as one of the factors is discrete). Use ~ zdis to write the left topology as a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpmulg2 (c_x G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel c_x (co (co (cpw cz) J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| In a topological monoid, the group sum operation is a continuous function from the function space to the base topology. This theorem is not true when ` A ` is infinite, because in this case for any basic open set of the domain one of the factors will be the whole space, so by varying the value of the functions to sum at this index, one can achieve any desired sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tmdgsum {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ccmn) (wcel G ctmd) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (co B2 A2 cmap) (co G (cv x3) cgsu)) (co (co J (cpw A2) cxko) J ccn)) $;

--| For any neighborhood ` U ` of ` n X ` , there is a neighborhood ` u ` of ` X ` such that any sum of ` n ` elements in ` u ` sums to an element of ` U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmdgsum2 {u f2: setvar} (ph: wff u f2) (A2 B2: class) (c_x: class u f2) (U G J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cfv A2 chash) X c_x) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex u (wa (wcel X (cv u)) (wral f2 (wcel (co G (cv f2) cgsu) U) (co (cv u) A2 cmap))) J) $;

--| The opposite of a topological monoid is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgtmd (G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctmd) (wcel O ctmd) $;

--| The opposite of a topological group is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem oppgtgp (G O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv G coppg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel O ctgp) $;

--| Any group equipped with the discrete topology is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem distgp (B2 G J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wceq J (cpw B2))) (wcel G ctgp) $;

--| Any group equipped with the indiscrete topology is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indistgp (B2 G J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wceq J (cpr c0 B2))) (wcel G ctgp) $;

--| The monoid of endofunctions on a set ` A ` is a topological monoid. Formerly part of proof for ~ symgtgp . (Contributed by AV, 23-Feb-2024.)
theorem efmndtmd (A2 M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv A2 cefmnd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel M ctmd) $;

--| The left group action of element ` A ` in a topological monoid ` G ` is a continuous function. (Contributed by FL, 18-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem tmdlactcn {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3) (G J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctmd) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel F (co J J ccn)) $;

--| The left group action of element ` A ` in a topological group ` G ` is a homeomorphism from the group to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem tgplacthmeo {x3: setvar} (A2 c_pl: class) (F: class x3) (G J X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (co A2 (cv x3) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel F (co J J chmeo)) $;

--| A submonoid of a topological monoid is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem submtmd (S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctmd) (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd))) (wcel H ctmd) $;

--| A subgroup of a topological group is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem subgtgp (S G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg))) (wcel H ctgp) $;

--| The symmetric group is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Mar-2024.)
theorem symgtgp (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel G ctgp) $;

--| A subgroup of a topological group with nonempty interior is open. Alternatively, dual to ~ clssubg , the interior of a subgroup is either a subgroup, or empty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem subgntr (A2 S G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel A2 (cfv S (cfv J cnt)))) (wcel S J) $;

--| An open subgroup of a topological group is also closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem opnsubg (S G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel S J)) (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The closure of a subgroup in a topological group is a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem clssubg (S G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg))) (wcel (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv G csubg)) $;

--| The closure of a normal subgroup is a normal subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem clsnsg (S G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G cnsg))) (wcel (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) (cfv G cnsg)) $;

--| A subgroup of finite index is closed iff it is open. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem cldsubg (R S G J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G csubg)) (wcel (cqs X R) cfn)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wcel S J)) $;

--| The connected component containing ` A ` is the left coset of the identity component containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpconncompeqg {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (c_sm S: class x3) (G J X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel c_0 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cec A2 c_sm) (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X)))) $;

--| The identity component, the connected component containing the identity element, is a closed ( ~ conncompcld ) normal subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpconncomp {x3: setvar} (S: class x3) (G J X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel c_0 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wcel S (cfv G cnsg)) $;

--| The identity component is a subset of any open subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpconncompss {x3: setvar} (S T: class x3) (G J X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cuni (crab x3 (wa (wcel c_0 (cv x3)) (wcel (co J (cv x3) crest) cconn)) (cpw X))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel T (cfv G csubg)) (wcel T J)) (wss S T) $;

--| A group homomorphism on topological groups is continuous everywhere if it is continuous at any point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Oct-2015.)
theorem ghmcnp (A2 F G H J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv H ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctmd) (wcel H ctmd) (wcel F (co G H cghm))) (wb (wcel F (cfv A2 (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel F (co J K ccn)))) $;

--| The coset of the closure of the identity is the closure of a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem snclseqg (A2 c_sm S G J X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co G S cqg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (csn c_0) (cfv J ccl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cec A2 c_sm) (cfv (csn A2) (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| A topological group is Hausdorff iff the identity subgroup is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgphaus (G J c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wb (wcel J cha) (wcel (csn c_0) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| Hausdorff and T1 are equivalent for topological groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpt1 (G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wb (wcel J cha) (wcel J ct1)) $;

--| Hausdorff and T0 are equivalent for topological groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgpt0 (G J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G ctgp) (wb (wcel J cha) (wcel J ct0)) $;

--| A quotient map in a topological group is an open map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qustgpopn {x3: setvar} (S: class) (F: class x3) (G H J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G Y cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv H ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cec (cv x3) (co G Y cqg))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel S J)) (wcel (cima F S) K) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qustgp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem qustgplem {x3 z w: setvar} (F: class x3) (G H J K: class) (c_mi: class x3 z w) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G Y cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv H ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cec (cv x3) (co G Y cqg))) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cmpo z w X X (cec (co (cv z) (cv w) (cfv G csg)) (co G Y cqg))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg))) (wcel H ctgp) $;

--| The quotient of a topological group is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Sep-2015.)
theorem qustgp (G H Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G Y cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G ctgp) (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg))) (wcel H ctgp) $;

--| The quotient of a topological group by a closed normal subgroup is a Hausdorff topological group. In particular, the quotient by the closure of the identity is a Hausdorff topological group, isomorphic to both the Kolmogorov quotient and the Hausdorff quotient operations on topological spaces (because T0 and Hausdorff coincide for topological groups). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem qustgphaus (G H J K Y: class):
  $ wceq H (co G (co G Y cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv H ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G ctgp) (wcel Y (cfv G cnsg)) (wcel Y (cfv J ccld))) (wcel K cha) $;

--| The product of a family of topological monoids is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdstmdd (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctmd R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y ctmd) $;

--| The product of a family of topological groups is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdstgpd (ph: wff) (R S I V W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I ctgp R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y ctgp) $;

--| Extend class notation to include infinite group sums in a topological group.
term ctsu: class;

--| Define the set of limit points of an infinite group sum for the topological group ` G ` . If ` G ` is Hausdorff, then there will be at most one element in this set and ` U. ( W tsums F ) ` selects this unique element if it exists. ` ( W tsums F ) ~~ 1o ` is a way to say that the sum exists and is unique. Note that unlike ` sum_ ` ( ~ df-sum ) and ` gsum ` ( ~ df-gsum ), this does not return the sum itself, but rather the set of all such sums, which is usually either empty or a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_tsms {y1 z w f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctsu (cmpo w f2 cvv cvv (csb s1 (cin (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) cfn) (cfv (cmpt y1 (cv s1) (co (cv w) (cres (cv f2) (cv y1)) cgsu)) (co (cfv (cv w) ctopn) (co (cv s1) (crn (cmpt z (cv s1) (crab y1 (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (cv s1)))) cfg) cflf)))) $;

--| The collection of all sets of the form ` F ( z ) = { y e. S | z C_ y } ` , which can be read as the set of all finite subsets of ` A ` which contain ` z ` as a subset, for each finite subset ` z ` of ` A ` , form a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsfbas {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1 z) (A2: class y1) (S: class) (F L W: class y1 z):
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z S (crab y1 (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) S)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crn F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv S cfbas)) $;

--| The finite partial sums of a function ` F ` are defined in a commutative monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmslem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F G W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X S)) (wcel (co G (cres F X) cgsu) B2) $;

--| Definition of the topological group sum(s) of a collection ` F ( x ) ` of values in the group with index set ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsval2 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class y1 z) (S: class z) (F G: class) (J L V W: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (crn (cmpt z S (crab y1 (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) S))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (cfv (cmpt y1 S (co G (cres F (cv y1)) cgsu)) (co J (co S L cfg) cflf))) $;

--| Definition of the topological group sum(s) of a collection ` F ( x ) ` of values in the group with index set ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsval {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class y1 z) (S: class z) (F G: class) (J L V W: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (crn (cmpt z S (crab y1 (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) S))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (cfv (cmpt y1 S (co G (cres F (cv y1)) cgsu)) (co J (co S L cfg) cflf))) $;

--| The group sum depends only on the base set, additive operation, and topology components. Note that for entirely unrestricted functions, there can be dependency on out-of-domain values of the operation, so this is somewhat weaker than ~ mndpropd etc. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmspropd (ph: wff) (F G H V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cbs) (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cplusg) (cfv H cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G ctopn) (cfv H ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (co H F ctsu)) $;

--| The property of being a sum of the sequence ` F ` in the topological commutative monoid ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem eltsms {y1 z u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 u) (B2: class z) (C: class y1 z) (S F G: class) (J: class y1) (V: class y1 z u):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co G F ctsu)) (wa (wcel C B2) (wral u (wi (wcel C (cv u)) (wrex z (wral y1 (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wcel (co G (cres F (cv y1)) cgsu) (cv u))) S) S)) J))) $;

--| The property of being a sum of the sequence ` F ` in the topological commutative monoid ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsi {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (B2: class z) (C: class y1 z) (S U F G: class) (J: class y1) (V: class y1 z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wral y1 (wi (wss (cv z) (cv y1)) (wcel (co G (cres F (cv y1)) cgsu) U)) S) S) $;

--| A sum in a topological group is an element of the group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmscl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G F ctsu) B2) $;

--| In a Hausdorff topological group, a sum has at most one limit point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem haustsms {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co G F ctsu))) $;

--| In a Hausdorff topological group, a sum has at most one limit point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem haustsms2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) (wceq (co G F ctsu) (csn X))) $;

--| One half of ~ tgptsmscls , true in any commutative monoid topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmscls (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv (csn X) (cfv J ccl)) (co G F ctsu)) $;

--| The convergent points of a finite topological group sum are the closure of the finite group sum operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsmsgsum (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (cfv (csn (co G F cgsu)) (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| If a sum is finite, the usual sum is always a limit point of the topological sum (although it may not be the only limit point). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsmsid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F cgsu) (co G F ctsu)) $;

--| In a Hausdorff topological group, a finite sum sums to exactly the usual number with no extraneous limit points. By setting the topology to the discrete topology (which is Hausdorff), this theorem can be used to turn any ` tsums ` theorem into a ` gsum ` theorem, so that the infinite group sum operation can be viewed as a generalization of the finite group sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem haustsmsid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (csn (co G F cgsu))) $;

--| The sum of zero is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsms0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 G V c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 (co G (cmpt x3 A2 c_0) ctsu)) $;

--| Evaluate an infinite group sum in a submonoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmssubm (ph: wff) (A2 S F G H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv G csubmnd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co H F ctsu) (cin (co G F ctsu) S)) $;

--| Extend an infinite group sum by padding outside with zeroes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsmsres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F c_0 csupp) W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G (cres F W) ctsu) (co G F ctsu)) $;

--| Re-index an infinite group sum using a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsf1o (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G H V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (co G (ccom F H) ctsu)) $;

--| Apply a continuous group homomorphism to an infinite group sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsmhm (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G H J K V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv H ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co G H cmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X C) (co H (ccom C F) ctsu)) $;

--| The sum of two infinite group sums. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsmsadd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl F G H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co G H ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) (co G (co F H (cof c_pl)) ctsu)) $;

--| Inverse of an infinite group sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsinv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G I V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X I) (co G (ccom I F) ctsu)) $;

--| The difference of two infinite group sums. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmssub (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G H c_mi V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co G H ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_mi) (co G (co F H (cof c_mi)) ctsu)) $;

--| A sum in a topological group is uniquely determined up to a coset of ` cls ( { 0 } ) ` , which is a normal subgroup by ~ clsnsg , ~ 0nsg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tgptsmscls (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G F ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (cfv (csn X) (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| The set of limit points to an infinite sum in a topological group is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2015.)
theorem tgptsmscld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G J V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G F ctsu) (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| Split a topological group sum into two parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Jul-2019.)
theorem tsmssplit (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl F G V X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co G (cres F C) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co G (cres F D) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin C D) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cun C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) (co G F ctsu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tsmsxp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsxplem1 {x3 j k n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 j n) (C: class x3) (D: class) (c_pl: class x3 j k n) (F G H: class) (J: class x3 j k n) (K: class) (L c_mi: class k) (V W: class x3 j k n) (c_0: class x3 j n):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 C) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cv j) H) (co G (cmpt k C (co (cv j) (cv k) F)) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cin (cpw A2) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm D) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cin (cpw (cxp A2 C)) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wa (wss (crn D) (cv n)) (wral x3 (wcel (co (cfv (cv x3) H) (co G (cres F (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (cv n))) cgsu) c_mi) L) K)) (cin (cpw C) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tsmsxp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsxplem2 {x3 g2 j k c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c d) (A2: class x3 c d) (B2: class x3 j c d) (C: class x3 c d) (D: class c d) (c_pl: class x3 j k) (S: class x3 g2 j k d) (T: class x3 j k) (U: class x3 g2 j k) (F G: class) (H: class c) (J: class x3 g2 j k c d) (K: class) (L: class k c d) (c_mi: class k c) (N: class j k) (V W: class x3 g2 j k c d) (c_0: class x3 j c d):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 C) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cv j) H) (co G (cmpt k C (co (cv j) (cv k) F)) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_0 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cin (cpw A2) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral c (wral d (wcel (co (cv c) (cv d) c_pl) U) T) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cin (cpw C) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cxp K N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel (co (cfv (cv x3) H) (co G (cres F (cxp (csn (cv x3)) N)) cgsu) c_mi) L) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G (cres F (cxp K N)) cgsu) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral g2 (wcel (co G (cv g2) cgsu) T) (co L K cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co G (cres H K) cgsu) U) $;

--| Write a sum over a two-dimensional region as a double sum. This infinite group sum version of ~ gsumxp is also known as Fubini's theorem. The converse is not necessarily true without additional assumptions. See ~ tsmsxplem1 for the main proof; this part mostly sets up the local assumptions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2015.)
theorem tsmsxp {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j) (C F G H: class) (V W: class j k):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ctgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp A2 C) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cv j) H) (co G (cmpt k C (co (cv j) (cv k) F)) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G F ctsu) (co G H ctsu)) $;

--| The class of all topological division rings.
term ctrg: class;

--| The class of all topological division rings.
term ctdrg: class;

--| The class of all topological modules.
term ctlm: class;

--| The class of all topological vector spaces.
term ctvc: class;

--| Define a topological ring, which is a ring such that the addition is a topological group operation and the multiplication is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_trg {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctrg (crab r (wcel (cfv (cv r) cmgp) ctmd) (cin ctgp crg)) $;

--| Define a topological division ring (which differs from a topological field only in being potentially noncommutative), which is a division ring and topological ring such that the unit group of the division ring (which is the set of nonzero elements) is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_tdrg {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctdrg (crab r (wcel (co (cfv (cv r) cmgp) (cfv (cv r) cui) cress) ctgp) (cin ctrg cdr)) $;

--| Define a topological left module, which is just what its name suggests: instead of a group over a ring with a scalar product connecting them, it is a topological group over a topological ring with a continuous scalar product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_tlm {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctlm (crab w (wa (wcel (cfv (cv w) csca) ctrg) (wcel (cfv (cv w) cscaf) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv w) csca) ctopn) (cfv (cv w) ctopn) ctx) (cfv (cv w) ctopn) ccn))) (cin ctmd clmod)) $;

--| Define a topological left vector space, which is a topological module over a topological division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_tvc {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctvc (crab w (wcel (cfv (cv w) csca) ctdrg) ctlm) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` R ` is a topological ring". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem istrg (R M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R ctrg) (w3a (wcel R ctgp) (wcel R crg) (wcel M ctmd)) $;

--| The multiplicative monoid of a topological ring is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trgtmd (R M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel M ctmd) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` R ` is a topological ring". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem istdrg (R U M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R ctdrg) (w3a (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R cdr) (wcel (co M U cress) ctgp)) $;

--| The unit group of a topological division ring is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgunit (R U M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel (co M U cress) ctgp) $;

--| A topological ring is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trgtgp (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R ctgp) $;

--| A topological ring is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trgtmd2 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R ctmd) $;

--| A topological ring is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trgtps (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R ctps) $;

--| A topological ring is a ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trgring (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R crg) $;

--| A topological ring is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem trggrp (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R cgrp) $;

--| A topological division ring is a topological ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgtrg (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel R ctrg) $;

--| A topological division ring is a division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgdrng (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel R cdr) $;

--| A topological division ring is a ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgring (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel R crg) $;

--| A topological division ring is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgtmd (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel R ctmd) $;

--| A topological division ring is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tdrgtps (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel R ctps) $;

--| A topological-ring division ring is a topological division ring iff the group of nonzero elements is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem istdrg2 (B2 R M c_0: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R ctdrg) (w3a (wcel R ctrg) (wcel R cdr) (wcel (co M (cdif B2 (csn c_0)) cress) ctgp)) $;

--| The functionalization of the ring multiplication operation is a continuous function in a topological ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem mulrcn (R T J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (cfv R cmgp) cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctrg) (wcel T (co (co J J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| The multiplicative inverse function is a continuous function from the unit group (that is, the nonzero numbers) to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem invrcn2 (R U I J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel I (co (co J U crest) (co J U crest) ccn)) $;

--| The multiplicative inverse function is a continuous function from the unit group (that is, the nonzero numbers) to the field. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem invrcn (R U I J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel I (co (co J U crest) J ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of ring multiplication; analogue of ~ cnmpt12f which cannot be used directly because ` .r ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1mulr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (R: class) (c_x: class x3) (J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 c_x)) (co K J ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of ring multiplication; analogue of ~ cnmpt22f which cannot be used directly because ` .r ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2mulr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (R: class) (c_x: class x3 y1) (J: class) (K: class y1) (L: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 c_x)) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| The division function is continuous in a topological field. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem dvrcn (c_dv R U J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ctdrg) (wcel c_dv (co (co J (co J U crest) ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| The predicate " ` W ` is a topological left module". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem istlm (c_x F J K W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cscaf) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W ctlm) (wa (w3a (wcel W ctmd) (wcel W clmod) (wcel F ctrg)) (wcel c_x (co (co K J ctx) J ccn))) $;

--| The scalar multiplication is continuous in a topological module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem vscacn (c_x F J K W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cscaf) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel c_x (co (co K J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| A topological module is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmtmd (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel W ctmd) $;

--| A topological module is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmtps (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel W ctps) $;

--| A topological module is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmlmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| The scalar ring of a topological module is a topological ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmtrg (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel F ctrg) $;

--| The scalar ring of a topological module is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmscatps (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel F ctps) $;

--| A topological vector space is a topological module over a topological division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem istvc (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W ctvc) (wa (wcel W ctlm) (wcel F ctdrg)) $;

--| The scalar field of a topological vector space is a topological division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tvctdrg (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ctvc) (wcel F ctdrg) $;

--| Continuity of scalar multiplication; analogue of ~ cnmpt12f which cannot be used directly because ` .s ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1vsca {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_x: class x3) (F J K L W X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co L K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co L J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 c_x)) (co L J ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of scalar multiplication; analogue of ~ cnmpt22f which cannot be used directly because ` .s ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2vsca {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_x: class x3 y1) (F J K: class) (L: class y1) (M: class x3 y1) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co L M ctx) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co L M ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 c_x)) (co (co L M ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| A topological vector space is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tlmtgp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctlm) (wcel W ctgp) $;

--| A topological vector space is a topological module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tvctlm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctvc) (wcel W ctlm) $;

--| A topological vector space is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tvclmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctvc) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| A topological vector space is a vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem tvclvec (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ctvc) (wcel W clvec) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class function of uniform structures.
term cust: class;

--| Definition of a uniform structure. Definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.1. A uniform structure is used to give a generalization of the idea of Cauchy's sequence. This definition is analogous to ` TopOn ` . Elements of an uniform structure are called entourages. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_ust {x3 w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cust (cmpt x3 cvv (cab u (w3a (wss (cv u) (cpw (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wcel (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)) (cv u)) (wral v (w3a (wral w (wi (wss (cv v) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv u))) (cpw (cxp (cv x3) (cv x3)))) (wral w (wcel (cin (cv v) (cv w)) (cv u)) (cv u)) (w3a (wss (cres cid (cv x3)) (cv v)) (wcel (ccnv (cv v)) (cv u)) (wrex w (wss (ccom (cv w) (cv w)) (cv v)) (cv u)))) (cv u))))) $;

--| The defined uniform structure as a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustfn:
  $ wfn cust cvv $;

--| The class of all uniform structures for a base ` X ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem ustval {w v u: setvar} (V: class w v u) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X cust) (cab u (w3a (wss (cv u) (cpw (cxp X X))) (wcel (cxp X X) (cv u)) (wral v (w3a (wral w (wi (wss (cv v) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv u))) (cpw (cxp X X))) (wral w (wcel (cin (cv v) (cv w)) (cv u)) (cv u)) (w3a (wss (cres cid X) (cv v)) (wcel (ccnv (cv v)) (cv u)) (wrex w (wss (ccom (cv w) (cv w)) (cv v)) (cv u)))) (cv u))))) $;

--| The predicate " ` U ` is a uniform structure with base ` X ` ". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem isust {w v: setvar} (U: class) (V: class w v) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (w3a (wss U (cpw (cxp X X))) (wcel (cxp X X) U) (wral v (w3a (wral w (wi (wss (cv v) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) U)) (cpw (cxp X X))) (wral w (wcel (cin (cv v) (cv w)) U) U) (w3a (wss (cres cid X) (cv v)) (wcel (ccnv (cv v)) U) (wrex w (wss (ccom (cv w) (cv w)) (cv v)) U))) U))) $;

--| Entourages are subsets of the Cartesian product of the base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustssxp (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wss V (cxp X X)) $;

--| A uniform structure is upward closed. Condition F_I of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem ustssel (U V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wss W (cxp X X))) (wi (wss V W) (wcel W U)) $;

--| The full set is always an entourage. Condition F_IIb of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustbasel (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cxp X X) U) $;

--| A uniform structure is closed under finite intersection. Condition F_II of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.36. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustincl (U V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wcel W U)) (wcel (cin V W) U) $;

--| The diagonal set is included in any entourage, i.e. any point is ` V ` -close to itself. Condition U_I of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustdiag (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wss (cres cid X) V) $;

--| If ` V ` is an entourage, so is its inverse. Condition U_II of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustinvel (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wcel (ccnv V) U) $;

--| For each entourage ` V ` there is an entourage ` w ` that is "not more than half as large". Condition U_III of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustexhalf {w: setvar} (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wrex w (wss (ccom (cv w) (cv w)) V) U) $;

--| The elements of uniform structures, called entourages, are relations. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustrel (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wrel V) $;

--| A uniform structure on a nonempty base is a filter. Remark 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.2. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem ustfilxp (U X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel U (cfv X cust))) (wcel U (cfv (cxp X X) cfil)) $;

--| A uniform structure cannot be empty. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustne0 (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wne U c0) $;

--| In an uniform structure, any entourage ` V ` is a subset of its composition with itself. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustssco (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wss V (ccom V V)) $;

--| In an uniform structure, for any entourage ` V ` , there exists a smaller symmetrical entourage. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustexsym {w: setvar} (U V: class) (X: class w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wrex w (wa (wceq (ccnv (cv w)) (cv w)) (wss (cv w) V)) U) $;

--| In an uniform structure, for any entourage ` V ` , there exists a symmetrical entourage smaller than half ` V ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustex2sym {w: setvar} (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wrex w (wa (wceq (ccnv (cv w)) (cv w)) (wss (ccom (cv w) (cv w)) V)) U) $;

--| In an uniform structure, for any entourage ` V ` , there exists a symmetrical entourage smaller than a third of ` V ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustex3sym {w: setvar} (U V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U)) (wrex w (wa (wceq (ccnv (cv w)) (cv w)) (wss (ccom (cv w) (ccom (cv w) (cv w))) V)) U) $;

--| Any element of the base set is "near" itself, i.e. entourages are reflexive. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustref (A2 U V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr A2 A2 V) $;

--| The unique uniform structure of the empty set is the empty set. Remark 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.2. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2017.)
theorem ust0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cust) (csn (csn c0)) $;

--| The empty set is not an uniform structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustn0:
  $ wn (wcel c0 (cuni (crn cust))) $;

--| If two intersecting sets ` A ` and ` B ` are both small in ` V ` , their union is small in ` ( V ^ 2 ) ` . Proposition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.12. This proposition actually does not require any axiom of the definition of uniform structures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustund (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (cxp A2 A2) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cxp B2 B2) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cxp (cun A2 B2) (cun A2 B2)) (ccom V V)) $;

--| Any point ` A ` is near enough to itself. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustelimasn (A2 U V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel A2 (cima V (csn A2))) $;

--| For a point ` A ` in ` X ` , ` ( V " { A } ) ` is small enough in ` ( V o. ``' V ) ` . This proposition actually does not require any axiom of the definition of uniform structures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustneism (A2 V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss V (cxp X X)) (wcel A2 X)) (wss (cxp (cima V (csn A2)) (cima V (csn A2))) (ccom V (ccnv V))) $;

--| Second direction for ~ ustbas . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustbas2 (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq X (cdm (cuni U))) $;

--| The set union of a uniform structure is the Cartesian product of its base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustuni (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq (cuni U) (cxp X X)) $;

--| Recover the base of an uniform structure ` U ` . ` U. ran UnifOn ` is to ` UnifOn ` what ` Top ` is to ` TopOn ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem ustbas (U X: class):
  $ wceq X (cdm (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel U (cuni (crn cust))) (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem ustimasn (P U V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wcel P X)) (wss (cima V (csn P)) X) $;

--| The trace of a uniform structure ` U ` on a subset ` A ` is a uniform structure on ` A ` . Definition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.9. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Dec-2017.)
theorem trust (A2 U X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wss A2 X)) (wcel (co U (cxp A2 A2) crest) (cfv A2 cust)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the function inducing a topology from a uniform structure.
term cutop: class;

--| Definition of a topology induced by a uniform structure. Definition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_utop {x3 v u a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cutop (cmpt u (cuni (crn cust)) (crab a (wral x3 (wrex v (wss (cima (cv v) (csn (cv x3))) (cv a)) (cv u)) (cv a)) (cpw (cdm (cuni (cv u)))))) $;

--| The topology induced by a uniform structure ` U ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Nov-2017.)
theorem utopval {x3 v a: setvar} (U: class) (X: class v):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq (cfv U cutop) (crab a (wral x3 (wrex v (wss (cima (cv v) (csn (cv x3))) (cv a)) U) (cv a)) (cpw X))) $;

--| Open sets in the topology induced by an uniform structure ` U ` on ` X ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Nov-2017.)
theorem elutop {x3 v: setvar} (A2 U: class) (X: class v):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv U cutop)) (wa (wss A2 X) (wral x3 (wrex v (wss (cima (cv v) (csn (cv x3))) A2) U) A2))) $;

--| The topology induced by a uniform structure ` U ` is a topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Nov-2017.)
theorem utoptop (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cfv U cutop) ctop) $;

--| The base of the topology induced by a uniform structure ` U ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem utopbas (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq X (cuni (cfv U cutop))) $;

--| Topology induced by a uniform structure ` U ` with its base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jan-2018.)
theorem utoptopon (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cfv U cutop) (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| Restriction of a topology induced by an uniform structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Dec-2017.)
theorem restutop (A2 U X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wss A2 X)) (wss (co (cfv U cutop) A2 crest) (cfv (co U (cxp A2 A2) crest) cutop)) $;

--| The restriction of the topology induced by an uniform structure to an open set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem restutopopn (A2 U X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel A2 (cfv U cutop))) (wceq (co (cfv U cutop) A2 crest) (cfv (co U (cxp A2 A2) crest) cutop)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtoplem {w v p: setvar} (A2: class v p) (P: class p) (U: class) (N V: class w v p) (X: class w):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel P X)) (wcel A2 V)) (wb (wcel A2 (cfv P N)) (wrex w (wceq A2 (cima (cv w) (csn P))) U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop0 {v p: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wf X (cpw (cpw X)) N) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop , similar to ~ ssnei2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop1 {v p a b: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cv a) (cv b)) (wss (cv b) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv p) N)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop2 {v p: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cv p) X)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv p) N) cfi) (cfv (cv p) N)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop , similar to ~ elnei . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop3 {v p a: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wcel (cv p) (cv a)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop4 {v q p a b: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N))) (wrex b (wral q (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv q) N)) (cv b)) (cfv (cv p) N)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ustuqtop . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop5 {v p: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cv p) X)) (wcel X (cfv (cv p) N)) $;

--| For a given uniform structure ` U ` on a set ` X ` , there is a unique topology ` j ` such that the set ` ran ( v e. U |-> ( v " { p } ) ) ` is the filter of the neighborhoods of ` p ` for that topology. Proposition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.3. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem ustuqtop {v j p: setvar} (U: class) (N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wreu j (wral p (wceq (cfv (cv p) N) (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv (cv j) cnei))) X) (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| The neighborhoods of a point ` P ` for the topology induced by an uniform space ` U ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jan-2018.)
theorem utopsnneiplem {v p a: setvar} (P: class a) (U: class) (J: class v p a) (K N: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab a (wral p (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv p) N)) (cv a)) (cpw X)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt p X (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel P X)) (wceq (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)) (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn P))))) $;

--| The neighborhoods of a point ` P ` for the topology induced by an uniform space ` U ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jan-2018.)
theorem utopsnneip {v: setvar} (P U: class) (J: class v) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel P X)) (wceq (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)) (crn (cmpt v U (cima (cv v) (csn P))))) $;

--| Images of singletons by entourages ` V ` are neighborhoods of those singletons. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jan-2018.)
theorem utopsnnei (P U J V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V U) (wcel P X)) (wcel (cima V (csn P)) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| For any symmetrical entourage ` V ` and any relation ` M ` , build a neighborhood of ` M ` . First part of proposition 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jan-2018.)
theorem utop2nei (U J M V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wa (wcel V U) (wceq (ccnv V) V)) (wss M (cxp X X))) (wcel (ccom V (ccom M V)) (cfv M (cfv (co J J ctx) cnei))) $;

--| Relation between a topological closure and a symmetric entourage in an uniform space. Second part of proposition 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jan-2018.)
theorem utop3cls (U J M V X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wss M (cxp X X))) (wa (wcel V U) (wceq (ccnv V) V))) (wss (cfv M (cfv (co J J ctx) ccl)) (ccom V (ccom M V))) $;

--| All Hausdorff uniform spaces are regular. Proposition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.5. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2018.)
theorem utopreg (U J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel J cha)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Uniform Structure extractor function.
term cuss: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of uniform spaces.
term cusp: class;

--| Extend class notation with the function mapping a uniform structure to a uniform space.
term ctus: class;

--| Define the uniform structure extractor function. Similarly with ~ df-topn this differs from ~ df-unif when a structure has been restricted using ~ df-ress ; in this case the ` UnifSet ` component will still have a uniform set over the larger set, and this function fixes this by restricting the uniform set as well. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.)
axiom df_uss {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cuss (cmpt f2 cvv (co (cfv (cv f2) cunif) (cxp (cfv (cv f2) cbs) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) crest)) $;

--| Definition of a uniform space, i.e. a base set with an uniform structure and its induced topology. Derived from definition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_usp {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cusp (cab f2 (wa (wcel (cfv (cv f2) cuss) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cbs) cust)) (wceq (cfv (cv f2) ctopn) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cuss) cutop)))) $;

--| Define the function mapping a uniform structure to a uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_tus {u: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctus (cmpt u (cuni (crn cust)) (co (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cdm (cuni (cv u)))) (cop (cfv cnx cunif) (cv u))) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (cv u) cutop)) csts)) $;

--| The uniform structure on uniform space ` W ` . This proof uses a trick with ~ fvprc to avoid requiring ` W ` to be a set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem ussval (B2 U W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cunif) $ >
  $ wceq (co U (cxp B2 B2) crest) (cfv W cuss) $;

--| In case the base of the ` UnifSt ` element of the uniform space is the base of its element structure, then ` UnifSt ` does not restrict it further. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem ussid (B2 U W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cunif) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cxp B2 B2) (cuni U)) (wceq U (cfv W cuss)) $;

--| The predicate ` W ` is a uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem isusp (B2 U J W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cuss) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cusp) (wa (wcel U (cfv B2 cust)) (wceq J (cfv U cutop))) $;

--| Value of the uniform structure of a restricted space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Dec-2017.)
theorem ressuss (A2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (co W A2 cress) cuss) (co (cfv W cuss) (cxp A2 A2) crest)) $;

--| The uniform structure of a restricted space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jan-2018.)
theorem ressust (A2 T W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (co W A2 cress) cuss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cusp) (wss A2 X)) (wcel T (cfv A2 cust)) $;

--| The restriction of a uniform topological space to an open set is a uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem ressusp (A2 B2 J W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cusp) (wcel W ctps) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel (co W A2 cress) cusp) $;

--| The value of the uniform space mapping function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem tusval (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq (cfv U ctus) (co (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cdm (cuni U))) (cop (cfv cnx cunif) U)) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv U cutop)) csts)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tusbas , ~ tusunif , and ~ tustopn . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Oct-2024.)
theorem tuslem (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (w3a (wceq X (cfv K cbs)) (wceq U (cfv K cunif)) (wceq (cfv U cutop) (cfv K ctopn))) $;

--| The base set of a constructed uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem tusbas (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq X (cfv K cbs)) $;

--| The uniform structure of a constructed uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem tusunif (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq U (cfv K cunif)) $;

--| The uniform structure of a constructed uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem tususs (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq U (cfv K cuss)) $;

--| The topology induced by a constructed uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem tustopn (U J K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U cutop) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wceq J (cfv K ctopn)) $;

--| A constructed uniform space is an uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem tususp (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel K cusp) $;

--| A constructed uniform space is a topological space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2018.)
theorem tustps (U K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctus) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel K ctps) $;

--| If a uniform space is Hausdorff, it is regular. Proposition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.5. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jan-2018.)
theorem uspreg (J W: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cusp) (wcel J cha)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| Extend class notation with the uniform continuity operation.
term cucn: class;

--| Define a function on two uniform structures which value is the set of uniformly continuous functions from the first uniform structure to the second. A function ` f ` is uniformly continuous if, roughly speaking, it is possible to guarantee that ` ( f `` x ) ` and ` ( f `` y ) ` be as close to each other as we please by requiring only that ` x ` and ` y ` are sufficiently close to each other; unlike ordinary continuity, the maximum distance between ` ( f `` x ) ` and ` ( f `` y ) ` cannot depend on ` x ` and ` y ` themselves. This formulation is the definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_ucn {x3 y1 v u f2 s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cucn (cmpo u v (cuni (crn cust)) (cuni (crn cust)) (crab f2 (wral s1 (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv s1))) (cdm (cuni (cv u)))) (cdm (cuni (cv u)))) (cv u)) (cv v)) (co (cdm (cuni (cv v))) (cdm (cuni (cv u))) cmap))) $;

--| The set of all uniformly continuous function from uniform space ` U ` to uniform space ` V ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem ucnval {x3 y1 f2 s1 r: setvar} (U: class) (V: class y1) (X: class) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V (cfv Y cust))) (wceq (co U V cucn) (crab f2 (wral s1 (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv s1))) X) X) U) V) (co Y X cmap))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a uniformly continuous function from uniform space ` U ` to uniform space ` V ` ". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem isucn {x3 y1 s1 r: setvar} (U F: class) (V: class y1) (X: class) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel V (cfv Y cust))) (wb (wcel F (co U V cucn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral s1 (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) (cv s1))) X) X) U) V))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a uniformly continuous function from uniform space ` U ` to uniform space ` V ` ", expressed with filter bases for the entourages. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2018.)
theorem isucn2 {x3 y1 s1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (R: class s1) (S: class r) (U F: class) (V: class y1 r) (X: class) (Y: class r):
  $ wceq U (co (cxp X X) R cfg) $ >
  $ wceq V (co (cxp Y Y) S cfg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cfv (cxp X X) cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv (cxp Y Y) cfbas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co U V cucn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral s1 (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) (cv s1))) X) X) R) S))) $;

--| Reformulate the ` G ` function as a mapping with one variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem ucnimalem {x3 y1 p: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 p) (U: class x3 y1 p) (F: class) (G V W: class x3 y1 p) (X: class) (Y: class x3 y1 p):
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co U V cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt p (cxp X X) (cop (cfv (cfv (cv p) c1st) F) (cfv (cfv (cv p) c2nd) F))) $;

--| An equivalent statement of the definition of uniformly continuous function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem ucnima {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (U F: class) (G: class r) (V: class y1) (W X: class) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co U V cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wss (cima G (cv r)) W) U) $;

--| The preimage by a uniformly continuous function ` F ` of an entourage ` W ` of ` Y ` is an entourage of ` X ` . Note of the definition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem ucnprima {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (U F G: class) (V: class y1) (W X: class) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co U V cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv G) W) U) $;

--| The identity is uniformly continuous from a uniform structure to itself. Example 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem iducn (U X: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel (cres cid X) (co U U cucn)) $;

--| A constant function is uniformly continuous. Deduction form. Example 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
theorem cstucnd (ph: wff) (A2 U V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp X (csn A2)) (co U V cucn)) $;

--| Uniform continuity implies continuity. Deduction form. Proposition 1 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Nov-2017.)
theorem ucncn (ph: wff) (R S F J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cfv R cuss) (cfv S cuss) cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the set of Cauchy filter bases.
term ccfilu: class;

--| Define the set of Cauchy filter bases on a uniform space. A Cauchy filter base is a filter base on the set such that for every entourage ` v ` , there is an element ` a ` of the filter "small enough in ` v ` " i.e. such that every pair ` { x , y } ` of points in ` a ` is related by ` v ` ". Definition 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.13. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Nov-2017.)
axiom df_cfilu {v u f2 a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccfilu (cmpt u (cuni (crn cust)) (crab f2 (wral v (wrex a (wss (cxp (cv a) (cv a)) (cv v)) (cv f2)) (cv u)) (cfv (cdm (cuni (cv u))) cfbas))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a Cauchy filter base on uniform space ` U ` ". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Nov-2017.)
theorem iscfilu {v a: setvar} (U: class a) (F: class) (X: class v a):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wb (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) (wral v (wrex a (wss (cxp (cv a) (cv a)) (cv v)) F) U))) $;

--| A Cauchy filter base is a filter base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem cfilufbas (U F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu))) (wcel F (cfv X cfbas)) $;

--| For a Cauchy filter base and any entourage ` V ` , there is an element of the filter small in ` V ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem cfiluexsm {a: setvar} (U: class a) (F V: class) (X: class a):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu)) (wcel V U)) (wrex a (wss (cxp (cv a) (cv a)) V) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmucnd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2017.)
theorem fmucndlem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F X) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (cima (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) (cxp A2 A2)) (cxp (cima F A2) (cima F A2))) $;

--| The image of a Cauchy filter base by an uniformly continuous function is a Cauchy filter base. Deduction form. Proposition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.13. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Nov-2017.)
theorem fmucnd {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D: class) (U: class a) (F V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv X cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv Y cust)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co U V cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv U ccfilu)) $ >
  $ wceq D (crn (cmpt a C (cima F (cv a)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv V ccfilu)) $;

--| The filter generated by a Cauchy filter base is still a Cauchy filter base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2018.)
theorem cfilufg (U F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu))) (wcel (co X F cfg) (cfv U ccfilu)) $;

--| Condition for the trace of a Cauchy filter base to be a Cauchy filter base for the restricted uniform structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2018.)
theorem trcfilu (A2 U F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wa (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu)) (wn (wcel c0 (co F A2 crest)))) (wss A2 X)) (wcel (co F A2 crest) (cfv (co U (cxp A2 A2) crest) ccfilu)) $;

--| A Cauchy filter base is also a Cauchy filter base on any coarser uniform structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2018.)
theorem cfiluweak (A2 U F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel U (cfv X cust)) (wss A2 X) (wcel F (cfv (co U (cxp A2 A2) crest) ccfilu))) (wcel F (cfv U ccfilu)) $;

--| In an uniform space, a neighboring filter is a Cauchy filter base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2018.)
theorem neipcfilu (P U J W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cuss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cusp) (wcel W ctps) (wcel P X)) (wcel (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)) (cfv U ccfilu)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all complete uniform spaces.
term ccusp: class;

--| Define the class of all complete uniform spaces. Definition 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.15. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.)
axiom df_cusp {w c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccusp (crab w (wral c (wi (wcel (cv c) (cfv (cfv (cv w) cuss) ccfilu)) (wne (co (cfv (cv w) ctopn) (cv c) cflim) c0)) (cfv (cfv (cv w) cbs) cfil)) cusp) $;

--| The predicate " ` W ` is a complete uniform space." (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem iscusp {c: setvar} (W: class):
  $ wb (wcel W ccusp) (wa (wcel W cusp) (wral c (wi (wcel (cv c) (cfv (cfv W cuss) ccfilu)) (wne (co (cfv W ctopn) (cv c) cflim) c0)) (cfv (cfv W cbs) cfil))) $;

--| A complete uniform space is an uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem cuspusp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccusp) (wcel W cusp) $;

--| In a complete uniform space, any Cauchy filter ` C ` has a limit. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem cuspcvg (B2 C J W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccusp) (wcel C (cfv (cfv W cuss) ccfilu)) (wcel C (cfv B2 cfil))) (wne (co J C cflim) c0) $;

--| The predicate " ` W ` is a complete uniform space." (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem iscusp2 {c: setvar} (B2 U J: class c) (W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cuss) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W ccusp) (wa (wcel W cusp) (wral c (wi (wcel (cv c) (cfv U ccfilu)) (wne (co J (cv c) cflim) c0)) (cfv B2 cfil))) $;

--| Extension by continuity. Proposition 11 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.20. Given a topology ` J ` on ` X ` , a subset ` A ` dense in ` X ` , this states a condition for ` F ` from ` A ` to a space ` Y ` Hausdorff and complete to be extensible by continuity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnextucn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (U: class x3) (F J K: class) (V W X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cfv V cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv V ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cuss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (cfv (co (cfv (csn (cv x3)) (cfv J cnei)) A2 crest) (co Y F cfm)) (cfv U ccfilu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co J K ccnext)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Extension by continuity. Theorem 2 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.20. Given an uniform space on a set ` X ` , a subset ` A ` dense in ` X ` , and a function ` F ` uniformly continuous from ` A ` to ` Y ` , that function can be extended by continuity to the whole ` X ` , and its extension is uniformly continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2018.)
theorem ucnextcn (ph: wff) (A2 S T U F J K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv V cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv V ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv V cuss) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (co V A2 cress) cuss) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W cuss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V cusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co T U cucn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co J K ccnext)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` D ` is a pseudometric." (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem ispsmet {x3 y1 z: setvar} (D: class) (V: class x3 y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wa (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv x3) D) cc0) (wral y1 (wral z (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) cxad) cle) X) X)) X))) $;

--| Recover the base set from a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetdmdm (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq X (cdm (cdm D))) $;

--| The distance function of a pseudometric as a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetf (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) $;

--| Closure of the distance function of a pseudometric space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetcl (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cxr) $;

--| The distance function of a pseudometric space is zero if its arguments are equal. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmet0 (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (co A2 A2 D) cc0) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmettri2 (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co C A2 D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| The distance function of a pseudometric is symmetrical. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetsym (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co B2 A2 D)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a pseudometric space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmettri (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| The distance function of a pseudometric space is nonnegative. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetge0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The distance function of a pseudometric space is a function into the nonnegative extended real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetxrge0 (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wf (cxp X X) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) D) $;

--| Restriction of a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem psmetres2 (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wss R X)) (wcel (cres D (cxp R R)) (cfv R cpsmet)) $;

--| Real closure of an extended metric value that is upper bounded by a real. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem psmetlecl (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) C cle))) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cr) $;

--| A set ` X ` together with a (distance) function ` D ` which is a pseudometric is a _distance space_ (according to E. Deza, M.M. Deza: "Dictionary of Distances", Elsevier, 2006), i.e. a (base) set ` X ` equipped with a _distance_ ` D ` , which is a mapping of two elements of the base set to the (extended) reals and which is nonnegative, symmetric and equal to 0 if the two elements are equal. (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2021.) (Revised by AV, 5-Jul-2022.)
theorem distspace (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wa (wa (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) (wceq (co A2 A2 D) cc0)) (wa (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) cle) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co B2 A2 D)))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of extended metric spaces.
term cxms: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of metric spaces.
term cms: class;

--| Extend class notation with the function mapping a metric to the metric space it defines.
term ctms: class;

--| Define the (proper) class of extended metric spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_xms {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxms (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cv f2) ctopn) (cfv (cres (cfv (cv f2) cds) (cxp (cfv (cv f2) cbs) (cfv (cv f2) cbs))) cmopn)) ctps) $;

--| Define the (proper) class of metric spaces. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.)
axiom df_ms {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cms (crab f2 (wcel (cres (cfv (cv f2) cds) (cxp (cfv (cv f2) cbs) (cfv (cv f2) cbs))) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cbs) cmet)) cxms) $;

--| Define the function mapping a metric to the metric space which it defines. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_tms {d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctms (cmpt d (cuni (crn cxmet)) (co (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cdm (cdm (cv d)))) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (cv d))) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (cv d) cmopn)) csts)) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` D ` is a metric." (Contributed by NM, 25-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem ismet {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X A2) (wb (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wa (wf (cxp X X) cr D) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wral z (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) caddc) cle) X)) X) X))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` D ` is an extended metric." (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem isxmet {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X A2) (wb (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wa (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wral z (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) cxad) cle) X)) X) X))) $;

--| Properties that determine a metric. (Contributed by NM, 17-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem ismeti {x3 y1 z: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wf (cxp X X) cr D $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv X cmet) $;

--| Properties that determine an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Apr-2024.)
theorem isxmetd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class) (V: class x3 y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| It is safe to only require the triangle inequality when the values are real (so that we can use the standard addition over the reals), but in this case the nonnegativity constraint cannot be deduced and must be provided separately. (Counterexample: ` D ( x , y ) = if ( x = y , 0 , -oo ) ` satisfies all hypotheses except nonnegativity.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem isxmet2d {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class) (V: class x3 y1 z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr cc0 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wb (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0 cle) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X) (wcel (cv z) X)) (wa (wcel (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) cr) (wcel (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) cr))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metf and others. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem metflem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wa (wf (cxp X X) cr D) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wral z (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) D) (co (cv z) (cv y1) D) caddc) cle) X)) X) X)) $;

--| Mapping of the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetf (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wf (cxp X X) cxr D) $;

--| Mapping of the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.)
theorem metf (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wf (cxp X X) cr D) $;

--| Closure of the distance function of a metric space. Part of Property M1 of [Kreyszig] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.)
theorem xmetcl (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cxr) $;

--| Closure of the distance function of a metric space. Part of Property M1 of [Kreyszig] p. 3. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.)
theorem metcl (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cr) $;

--| An extended metric is a metric exactly when it takes real values for all values of the arguments. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem ismet2 (D X: class):
  $ wb (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wf (cxp X X) cr D)) $;

--| A metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem metxmet (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| Recover the base set from an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetdmdm (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq X (cdm (cdm D))) $;

--| Recover the base set from a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem metdmdm (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wceq X (cdm (cdm D))) $;

--| Two ways to express an extended metric on an unspecified base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmetunirn (D: class):
  $ wb (wcel D (cuni (crn cxmet))) (wcel D (cfv (cdm (cdm D)) cxmet)) $;

--| The value of an extended metric is zero iff its arguments are equal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmeteq0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 D) cc0) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The value of a metric is zero iff its arguments are equal. Property M2 of [Kreyszig] p. 4. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.)
theorem meteq0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 D) cc0) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmettri2 (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co C A2 D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem mettri2 (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co C A2 D) (co C B2 D) caddc) cle) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is zero if its arguments are equal. Definition 14-1.1(a) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmet0 (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (co A2 A2 D) cc0) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is zero if its arguments are equal. Definition 14-1.1(a) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.)
theorem met0 (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (co A2 A2 D) cc0) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetge0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem metge0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| Real closure of an extended metric value that is upper bounded by a real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetlecl (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) C cle))) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cr) $;

--| The distance function of an extended metric space is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetsym (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co B2 A2 D)) $;

--| An extended metric is a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem xmetpsmet (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) $;

--| The distance function of an extended metric space is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmettpos (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq (ctpos D) D) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is symmetric. Definition 14-1.1(c) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem metsym (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co B2 A2 D)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. Definition 14-1.1(d) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmettri (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. Definition 14-1.1(d) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.)
theorem mettri (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co C B2 D) caddc) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmettri3 (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2007.)
theorem mettri3 (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) caddc) cle) $;

--| One half of the reverse triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xmetrtri (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co (co A2 C D) (cxne (co B2 C D)) cxad) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The reverse triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. In order to express the "extended absolute value function", we use the distance function ~ xrsdsval defined on the extended real structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xmetrtri2 (A2 B2 C D K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) K) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| Reverse triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem metrtri (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) cmin) cabs) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The distance function of an extended metric space is positive for unequal points. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetgt0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wne A2 B2) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) clt)) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is positive for unequal points. Definition 14-1.1(b) of [Gleason] p. 223 and its converse. (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.)
theorem metgt0 (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wne A2 B2) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) clt)) $;

--| A metric space is nonempty iff its base set is nonempty. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem metn0 (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wb (wne D c0) (wne X c0)) $;

--| Restriction of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetres2 (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss R X)) (wcel (cres D (cxp R R)) (cfv R cxmet)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metres . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem metreslem (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cdm D) (cxp X X)) (wceq (cres D (cxp R R)) (cres D (cxp (cin X R) (cin X R)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metres . (Contributed by FL, 12-Oct-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem metres2 (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wss R X)) (wcel (cres D (cxp R R)) (cfv R cmet)) $;

--| A restriction of an extended metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetres (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel (cres D (cxp R R)) (cfv (cin X R) cxmet)) $;

--| A restriction of a metric is a metric. (Contributed by NM, 26-Aug-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem metres (D R X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel (cres D (cxp R R)) (cfv (cin X R) cmet)) $;

--| The empty metric. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem _0met:
  $ wcel c0 (cfv c0 cmet) $;

--| The product metric is a function into the nonnegative extended reals. In general this means that it is not a metric but rather an *extended* metric (even when all the factors are metrics), but it will be a metric when restricted to regions where it does not take infinite values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsdsf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D R S E: class x3) (I: class) (V W X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) D) $;

--| The product metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsxmetlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class) (R S E: class x3) (I: class) (V W X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cxmet)) $;

--| The product metric is an extended metric. Eliminate disjoint variable conditions from ~ prdsxmetlem . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Sep-2015.)
theorem prdsxmet {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D R S E: class x3) (I: class) (V W X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cxmet)) $;

--| The product metric is a metric when the index set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsmet {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D R S E: class x3) (I: class) (V W Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel E (cfv V cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cmet)) $;

--| Restriction of a product metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem ressprdsds {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R S T U E H: class x3) (I: class) (V W X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (co T (cmpt x3 I (co R A2 cress)) cprds)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel A2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cres D (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| Restriction of a power metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2015.)
theorem resspwsds (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R E H I V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq Y (co R I cpws)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (co (co R A2 cress) I cpws)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cres D (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasdsf1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem imasdsf1olem {g2 h i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class g2 h i n) (R: class) (S: class h i n) (T U: class g2 h i n) (E: class h) (F V: class) (W: class g2 h i n) (X Y: class) (Z: class g2 h i n):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wceq W (co cxrs (cdif cxr (csn cmnf)) cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab h (w3a (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c1 (cv h)) c1st) F) (cfv X F)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv Y F)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) c2nd) F) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv h)) c1st) F)) (co c1 (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz))) (co (cxp V V) (co c1 (cv n) cfz) cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq T (ciun n cn (crn (cmpt g2 S (co cxrs (ccom E (cv g2)) cgsu)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) D) (co X Y E)) $;

--| The distance function is transferred across an image structure under a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasdsf1o (ph: wff) (B2 D R U E F V X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) D) (co X Y E)) $;

--| The image of an extended metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasf1oxmet (ph: wff) (B2 D R U E F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cxmet)) $;

--| The image of a metric is a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasf1omet (ph: wff) (B2 D R U E F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv V cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cmet)) $;

--| Closure of the metric in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsdsfn (ph: wff) (P R S T V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn P (cxp (cxp X Y) (cxp X Y))) $;

--| Closure of the metric in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsdsfn2 (ph: wff) (P R S T V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn P (cxp (cfv T cbs) (cfv T cbs))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xpsxmet . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsxmetlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (P: class x3 y1) (R S: class y1) (T: class x3 y1) (M N: class y1) (V: class x3 y1) (W: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq M (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cres (cfv S cds) (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (co (cfv R csca) (cpr (cop c0 R) (cop c1o S)) cprds) cds) (cfv (crn (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cpr (cop c0 (cv x3)) (cop c1o (cv y1))))) cxmet)) $;

--| A product metric of extended metrics is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsxmet (ph: wff) (P R S T M N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq M (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cres (cfv S cds) (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv (cxp X Y) cxmet)) $;

--| Value of the metric in a binary structure product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsdsval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P R S T M N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq M (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cres (cfv S cds) (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) P) (csup (cpr (co A2 C M) (co B2 D N)) cxr clt)) $;

--| The direct product of two metric spaces. Definition 14-1.5 of [Gleason] p. 225. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsmet (ph: wff) (P R S T M N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq M (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cres (cfv S cds) (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv (cxp X Y) cmet)) $;

--| The value of the ball function. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem blfvalps {x3 y1 r: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq (cfv D cbl) (cmpo x3 r X cxr (crab y1 (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (cv r) clt) X))) $;

--| The value of the ball function. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem blfval {x3 y1 r: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq (cfv D cbl) (cmpo x3 r X cxr (crab y1 (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (cv r) clt) X))) $;

--| The ball around a point ` P ` is the set of all points whose distance from ` P ` is less than the ball's radius ` R ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blvalps {x3: setvar} (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wceq (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (crab x3 (wbr (co P (cv x3) D) R clt) X)) $;

--| The ball around a point ` P ` is the set of all points whose distance from ` P ` is less than the ball's radius ` R ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem blval {x3: setvar} (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wceq (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (crab x3 (wbr (co P (cv x3) D) R clt) X)) $;

--| Membership in a ball. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem elblps (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wbr (co P A2 D) R clt))) $;

--| Membership in a ball. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Nov-2013.)
theorem elbl (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wbr (co P A2 D) R clt))) $;

--| Membership in a ball. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem elbl2ps (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr (co P A2 D) R clt)) $;

--| Membership in a ball. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-2007.)
theorem elbl2 (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr (co P A2 D) R clt)) $;

--| Membership in a ball, with reversed distance function arguments. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2007.)
theorem elbl3ps (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr (co A2 P D) R clt)) $;

--| Membership in a ball, with reversed distance function arguments. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2007.)
theorem elbl3 (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr (co A2 P D) R clt)) $;

--| Commute the arguments to the ball function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blcomps (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wcel P (co A2 R (cfv D cbl)))) $;

--| Commute the arguments to the ball function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2014.)
theorem blcom (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr)) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wcel P (co A2 R (cfv D cbl)))) $;

--| The infinity ball in an extended metric is the set of all points that are a finite distance from the center. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem xblpnfps (A2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel A2 (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel (co P A2 D) cr))) $;

--| The infinity ball in an extended metric is the set of all points that are a finite distance from the center. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xblpnf (A2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel A2 (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel (co P A2 D) cr))) $;

--| The infinity ball in a standard metric is just the whole space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem blpnf (D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel P X)) (wceq (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl)) X) $;

--| Two balls are disjoint if the center-to-center distance is more than the sum of the radii. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.)
theorem bldisj (D P Q R S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel Q X)) (w3a (wcel R cxr) (wcel S cxr) (wbr (co R S cxad) (co P Q D) cle))) (wceq (cin (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q S (cfv D cbl))) c0) $;

--| A nonempty ball implies that the radius is positive. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem blgt0 (A2 D P R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel A2 (co P R (cfv D cbl)))) (wbr cc0 R clt) $;

--| Two balls are disjoint if they don't overlap. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem bl2in (D P Q R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel Q X)) (wa (wcel R cr) (wbr R (co (co P Q D) c2 cdiv) cle))) (wceq (cin (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q R (cfv D cbl))) c0) $;

--| One ball is contained in another if the center-to-center distance is less than the difference of the radii. In this version of ~ blss2 for extended metrics, we have to assume the balls are a finite distance apart, or else ` P ` will not even be in the infinity ball around ` Q ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem xblss2ps (ph: wff) (D P Q R S X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co P Q D) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P Q D) (co S (cxne R) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| One ball is contained in another if the center-to-center distance is less than the difference of the radii. In this version of ~ blss2 for extended metrics, we have to assume the balls are a finite distance apart, or else ` P ` will not even be in the infinity ball around ` Q ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xblss2 (ph: wff) (D P Q R S X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co P Q D) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co P Q D) (co S (cxne R) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| One ball is contained in another if the center-to-center distance is less than the difference of the radii. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blss2ps (D P Q R S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel Q X)) (w3a (wcel R cr) (wcel S cr) (wbr (co P Q D) (co S R cmin) cle))) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| One ball is contained in another if the center-to-center distance is less than the difference of the radii. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem blss2 (D P Q R S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel Q X)) (w3a (wcel R cr) (wcel S cr) (wbr (co P Q D) (co S R cmin) cle))) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co Q S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball of radius ` R / 2 ` is contained in a ball of radius ` R ` centered at any point inside the smaller ball. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2014.)
theorem blhalf (R M X Y Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel Y X)) (wa (wcel R cr) (wcel Z (co Y (co R c2 cdiv) (cfv M cbl))))) (wss (co Y (co R c2 cdiv) (cfv M cbl)) (co Z R (cfv M cbl))) $;

--| Mapping of a ball. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blfps (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wf (cxp X cxr) (cpw X) (cfv D cbl)) $;

--| Mapping of a ball. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem blf (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wf (cxp X cxr) (cpw X) (cfv D cbl)) $;

--| Membership in the range of the ball function. Note that ` ran ( ball `` D ) ` is the collection of all balls for metric ` D ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blrnps {x3 r: setvar} (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wb (wcel A2 (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wrex x3 (wrex r (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl))) cxr) X)) $;

--| Membership in the range of the ball function. Note that ` ran ( ball `` D ) ` is the collection of all balls for metric ` D ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blrn {x3 r: setvar} (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel A2 (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wrex x3 (wrex r (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl))) cxr) X)) $;

--| A ball contains its center. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem xblcntrps (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wa (wcel R cxr) (wbr cc0 R clt))) (wcel P (co P R (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball contains its center. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem xblcntr (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wa (wcel R cxr) (wbr cc0 R clt))) (wcel P (co P R (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball contains its center. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blcntrps (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R crp)) (wcel P (co P R (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball contains its center. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blcntr (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R crp)) (wcel P (co P R (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball is nonempty iff the radius is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xbln0 (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wb (wne (co P R (cfv D cbl)) c0) (wbr cc0 R clt)) $;

--| A ball is not empty. (Contributed by NM, 6-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem bln0 (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R crp)) (wne (co P R (cfv D cbl)) c0) $;

--| A ball belongs to the set of balls of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blelrnps (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball belongs to the set of balls of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blelrn (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball is a subset of the base set of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blssm (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) X) $;

--| The union of the set of balls of a metric space is its base set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem unirnblps (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq (cuni (crn (cfv D cbl))) X) $;

--| The union of the set of balls of a metric space is its base set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem unirnbl (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq (cuni (crn (cfv D cbl))) X) $;

--| The intersection of two balls with the same center is the smaller of them. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blin (D P R S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wa (wcel R cxr) (wcel S cxr))) (wceq (cin (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co P S (cfv D cbl))) (co P (cif (wbr R S cle) R S) (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| The size of a ball increases monotonically with its radius. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem ssblps (D P R S X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X)) (wa (wcel R cxr) (wcel S cxr)) (wbr R S cle)) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co P S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| The size of a ball increases monotonically with its radius. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ssbl (D P R S X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wa (wcel R cxr) (wcel S cxr)) (wbr R S cle)) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (co P S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| Any point ` P ` in a ball ` B ` can be centered in another ball that is a subset of ` B ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blssps {x3: setvar} (B2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel B2 (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wcel P B2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) B2) crp) $;

--| Any point ` P ` in a ball ` B ` can be centered in another ball that is a subset of ` B ` . (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem blss {x3: setvar} (B2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel B2 (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wcel P B2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) B2) crp) $;

--| Two ways to express the existence of a ball subset. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blssexps {x3 r: setvar} (A2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) A2)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wrex r (wss (co P (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) A2) crp)) $;

--| Two ways to express the existence of a ball subset. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blssex {x3 r: setvar} (A2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) A2)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wrex r (wss (co P (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) A2) crp)) $;

--| A nested ball exists whose radius is less than any desired amount. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem ssblex {x3: setvar} (D P R S X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wa (wcel R crp) (wcel S crp))) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) R clt) (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) (co P S (cfv D cbl)))) crp) $;

--| Given any two balls and a point in their intersection, there is a ball contained in the intersection with the given center point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blin2 {x3: setvar} (B2 C D P X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P (cin B2 C))) (wa (wcel B2 (crn (cfv D cbl))) (wcel C (crn (cfv D cbl))))) (wrex x3 (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) (cin B2 C)) crp) $;

--| The balls of a metric space form a basis for a topology. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2014.)
theorem blbas (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel (crn (cfv D cbl)) ctb) $;

--| A ball in a restricted metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2014.)
theorem blres (C D P R X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cres D (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P (cin X Y)) (wcel R cxr)) (wceq (co P R (cfv C cbl)) (cin (co P R (cfv D cbl)) Y)) $;

--| Value of the "finitely separated" relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmeterval (A2 B2 D c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cima (ccnv D) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 c_sm) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cr))) $;

--| The "finitely separated" relation is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmeter (D c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cima (ccnv D) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wer X c_sm) $;

--| The equivalence classes under the finite separation equivalence relation are infinity balls. Thus, by ~ erdisj , infinity balls are either identical or disjoint, quite unlike the usual situation with Euclidean balls which admit many kinds of overlap. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetec (D P c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cima (ccnv D) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wceq (cec P c_sm) (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A ball centered at ` P ` is contained in the set of points finitely separated from ` P ` . This is just an application of ~ ssbl to the infinity ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem blssec (D P c_sm S X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cima (ccnv D) cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel S cxr)) (wss (co P S (cfv D cbl)) (cec P c_sm)) $;

--| The infinity ball in an extended metric acts like an ultrametric ball in that every point in the ball is also its center. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem blpnfctr (A2 D P X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel A2 (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl)))) (wceq (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl)) (co A2 cpnf (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| An extended metric restricted to any ball (in particular the infinity ball) is a proper metric. Together with ~ xmetec , this shows that any extended metric space can be "factored" into the disjoint union of proper metric spaces, with points in the same region measured by that region's metric, and points in different regions being distance ` +oo ` from each other. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmetresbl (B2 D P R X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (co P R (cfv D cbl)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel (cres D (cxp B2 B2)) (cfv B2 cmet)) $;

--| An open set is a subset of a metric space which includes a ball around each of its points. Definition 1.3-2 of [Kreyszig] p. 18. The object ` ( MetOpen `` D ) ` is the family of all open sets in the metric space determined by the metric ` D ` . By ~ mopntop , the open sets of a metric space form a topology ` J ` , whose base set is ` U. J ` by ~ mopnuni . (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopnval (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq J (cfv (crn (cfv D cbl)) ctg)) $;

--| The set of open sets of a metric space ` X ` is a topology on ` X ` . Remark in [Kreyszig] p. 19. This theorem connects the two concepts and makes available the theorems for topologies for use with metric spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem mopntopon (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $;

--| The set of open sets of a metric space is a topology. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopntop (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The union of all open sets in a metric space is its underlying set. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopnuni (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq X (cuni J)) $;

--| The defining property of an open set of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 1-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem elmopn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D: class) (J: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wa (wss A2 X) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wss (cv y1) A2)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) A2))) $;

--| The family of open sets of a metric space is a collection of subsets of the base set. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopnfss (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss J (cpw X)) $;

--| The base set of a metric space is open. Part of Theorem T1 of [Kreyszig] p. 19. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopnm (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel X J) $;

--| A defining property of an open set of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 5-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem elmopn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D: class) (J: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel A2 J) (wa (wss A2 X) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv D cbl)) A2) crp) A2))) $;

--| An open set of a metric space is a subspace of its base set. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2006.)
theorem mopnss (A2 D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 J)) (wss A2 X) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` <. X , D >. ` is an extended metric space" with underlying set ` X ` and distance function ` D ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem isxms (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cxms) (wa (wcel K ctps) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` <. X , D >. ` is an extended metric space" with underlying set ` X ` and distance function ` D ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem isxms2 (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cxms) (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` <. X , D >. ` is a metric space" with underlying set ` X ` and distance function ` D ` . (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem isms (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cms) (wa (wcel K cxms) (wcel D (cfv X cmet))) $;

--| Express the predicate " ` <. X , D >. ` is a metric space" with underlying set ` X ` and distance function ` D ` . (Contributed by NM, 27-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem isms2 (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel K cms) (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn))) $;

--| The topology component of an extended metric space coincides with the topology generated by the metric component. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmstopn (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cxms) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| The topology component of a metric space coincides with the topology generated by the metric component. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem mstopn (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv K ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cms) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| An extended metric space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem xmstps (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cxms) (wcel M ctps) $;

--| A metric space is an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem msxms (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wcel M cxms) $;

--| A metric space is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem mstps (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wcel M ctps) $;

--| The distance function, suitably truncated, is an extended metric on ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem xmsxmet (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cxms) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| The distance function, suitably truncated, is a metric on ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem msmet (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| The distance function of a metric space is a function into the real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem msf (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wf (cxp X X) cr D) $;

--| The distance function, suitably truncated, is an extended metric on ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmsxmet2 (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cxms) (wcel (cres D (cxp X X)) (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| The distance function, suitably truncated, is a metric on ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem msmet2 (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wcel (cres D (cxp X X)) (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| Closure of the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mscl (A2 B2 D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cms) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cr) $;

--| Closure of the distance function of an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmscl (A2 B2 D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cxms) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (co A2 B2 D) cxr) $;

--| The distance function in an extended metric space is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmsge0 (A2 B2 D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cxms) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The distance between two points in an extended metric space is zero iff the two points are identical. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmseq0 (A2 B2 D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cxms) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 D) cc0) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The distance function in an extended metric space is symmetric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmssym (A2 B2 D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cxms) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co B2 A2 D)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmstri2 (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cxms) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co C A2 D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mstri2 (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cms) (w3a (wcel C X) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co C A2 D) (co C B2 D) caddc) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. Definition 14-1.1(d) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmstri (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cxms) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co C B2 D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. Definition 14-1.1(d) of [Gleason] p. 223. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mstri (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cms) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co C B2 D) caddc) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmstri3 (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cxms) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) cxad) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem mstri3 (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cms) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) caddc) cle) $;

--| Reverse triangle inequality for the distance function of a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem msrtri (A2 B2 C D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cms) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 C D) (co B2 C D) cmin) cabs) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| Property deduction for an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem xmspropd (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp B2 B2)) (cres (cfv L cds) (cxp B2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cxms) (wcel L cxms)) $;

--| Property deduction for a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem mspropd (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp B2 B2)) (cres (cfv L cds) (cxp B2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cms) (wcel L cms)) $;

--| The base set of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem setsmsbas (ph: wff) (D K M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv K cbs)) $;

--| The distance function of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem setsmsds (ph: wff) (D K M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M cds) (cfv K cds)) $;

--| The topology of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem setsmstset (ph: wff) (D K M V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv D cmopn) (cfv K cts)) $;

--| The topology of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem setsmstopn (ph: wff) (D K M V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv D cmopn) (cfv K ctopn)) $;

--| The constructed metric space is a metric space iff the provided distance function is a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem setsxms (ph: wff) (D K M V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cxms) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) $;

--| The constructed metric space is a metric space iff the provided distance function is a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem setsms (ph: wff) (D K M V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cfv M cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cms) (wcel D (cfv X cmet))) $;

--| For any metric there is an associated metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsval (D K M X: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) X) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq K (co M (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv D cmopn)) csts)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tmsbas , ~ tmsds , and ~ tmstopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmslem (D K M X: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) X) (cop (cfv cnx cds) D)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wceq X (cfv K cbs)) (wceq D (cfv K cds)) (wceq (cfv D cmopn) (cfv K ctopn))) $;

--| The base set of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsbas (D K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq X (cfv K cbs)) $;

--| The metric of a constructed metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsds (D K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq D (cfv K cds)) $;

--| The topology of a constructed metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmstopn (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq J (cfv K ctopn)) $;

--| The constructed metric space is an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsxms (D K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel K cxms) $;

--| The constructed metric space is a metric space given a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsms (D K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv D ctms) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel K cms) $;

--| The image of a metric space ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasf1obl (ph: wff) (B2 D P R S U E F V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv P F) S (cfv D cbl)) (cima F (co P S (cfv E cbl)))) $;

--| The image of a metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasf1oxms (ph: wff) (B2 R U F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cxms) $;

--| The image of a metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem imasf1oms (ph: wff) (B2 R U F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co F R cimas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o V B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cms) $;

--| A ball in the product metric for finite index set is the Cartesian product of balls in all coordinates. For infinite index set this is no longer true; instead the correct statement is that a *closed ball* is the product of closed balls in each coordinate (where closed ball means a set of the form in ~ blcld ) - for a counterexample the point ` p ` in ` RR ^ NN ` whose ` n ` -th coordinate is ` 1 - 1 / n ` is in ` X_ n e. NN ball ( 0 , 1 ) ` but is not in the ` 1 ` -ball of the product (since ` d ( 0 , p ) = 1 ` ). The last assumption, ` 0 < A ` , is needed only in the case ` I = (/) ` , when the right side evaluates to ` { (/) } ` and the left evaluates to ` (/) ` if ` A <_ 0 ` and ` { (/) } ` if ` 0 < A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsbl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P: class) (R S E: class x3) (I: class) (V W Y Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Y (co S (cmpt x3 I R) cprds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel R Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel E (cfv V cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co P A2 (cfv D cbl)) (cixp x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) P) A2 (cfv E cbl)))) $;

--| An open set of a metric space includes a ball around each of its points. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopni {x3: setvar} (A2 D P J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 J) (wcel P A2)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wss (cv x3) A2)) (crn (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| An open set of a metric space includes a ball around each of its points. (Contributed by NM, 2-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopni2 {x3: setvar} (A2 D P J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 J) (wcel P A2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) A2) crp) $;

--| An open set of a metric space includes an arbitrarily small ball around each of its points. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem mopni3 {x3: setvar} (A2 D P R J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 J) (wcel P A2)) (wcel R crp)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) R clt) (wss (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) A2)) crp) $;

--| The balls of a metric space are open sets. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem blssopn (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss (crn (cfv D cbl)) J) $;

--| The union of a collection of open sets of a metric space is open. Theorem T2 of [Kreyszig] p. 19. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem unimopn (A2 D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss A2 J)) (wcel (cuni A2) J) $;

--| The intersection of two open sets of a metric space is open. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem mopnin (A2 B2 D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 J) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) J) $;

--| The empty set is an open set of a metric space. Part of Theorem T1 of [Kreyszig] p. 19. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2006.)
theorem mopn0 (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel c0 J) $;

--| A ball of a metric space is an open set. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2006.)
theorem rnblopn (B2 D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel B2 (crn (cfv D cbl)))) (wcel B2 J) $;

--| A ball of a metric space is an open set. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem blopn (D P R J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel (co P R (cfv D cbl)) J) $;

--| The neighborhoods around a point ` P ` of a metric space are those subsets containing a ball around ` P ` . Definition of neighborhood in [Kreyszig] p. 19. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem neibl {r: setvar} (D P J N X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) (wa (wss N X) (wrex r (wss (co P (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) N) crp))) $;

--| A ball around a point is a neighborhood of the point. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem blnei (D P R J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R crp)) (wcel (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei))) $;

--| Every ball around a limit point ` P ` of a subset ` S ` includes a member of ` S ` (even if ` P e/ S ` ). (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem lpbl {x3: setvar} (D P R S J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X) (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J clp)))) (wcel R crp)) (wrex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co P R (cfv D cbl))) S) $;

--| A smaller closed ball is contained in a larger open ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2014.)
theorem blsscls2 {z: setvar} (D P R: class) (S: class z) (T: class) (J: class z) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (co P (cv z) D) R cle) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (w3a (wcel R cxr) (wcel T cxr) (wbr R T clt))) (wss S (co P T (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| A "closed ball" in a metric space is actually closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem blcld {z: setvar} (D P R: class) (S J: class z) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (co P (cv z) D) R cle) X) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The closure of an open ball in a metric space is contained in the corresponding closed ball. (Equality need not hold; for example, with the discrete metric, the closed ball of radius 1 is the whole space, but the open ball of radius 1 is just a point, whose closure is also a point.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2013.)
theorem blcls {z: setvar} (D P R: class) (S J: class z) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (co P (cv z) D) R cle) X) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R cxr)) (wss (cfv (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) S) $;

--| If two concentric balls have different radii, the closure of the smaller one is contained in the larger one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2014.)
theorem blsscls (D P R S J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (w3a (wcel R cxr) (wcel S cxr) (wbr R S clt))) (wss (cfv (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (co P S (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| Two ways of saying that metric ` D ` generates a finer topology than metric ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem metss {x3 s1 r: setvar} (C D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wb (wss J K) (wral x3 (wral r (wrex s1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv D cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv C cbl))) crp) crp) X)) $;

--| Two ways of saying that two metrics generate the same topology. Two metrics satisfying the right-hand side are said to be (topologically) equivalent. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jun-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem metequiv {x3 s1 r a b: setvar} (C D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wb (wceq J K) (wral x3 (wa (wral r (wrex s1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv D cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv C cbl))) crp) crp) (wral a (wrex b (wss (co (cv x3) (cv b) (cfv C cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv a) (cfv D cbl))) crp) crp)) X)) $;

--| Two ways of saying that two metrics generate the same topology. Two metrics satisfying the right-hand side are said to be (topologically) equivalent. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 21-Jun-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Nov-2013.)
theorem metequiv_b {x3 s1 r: setvar} (C D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wb (wceq J K) (wral x3 (wa (wral r (wrex s1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv D cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv C cbl))) crp) crp) (wral r (wrex s1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv C cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl))) crp) crp)) X)) $;

--| If there is a sequence of radii approaching zero for which the balls of both metrics coincide, then the generated topologies are equivalent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem metequiv2 {x3 s1 r: setvar} (C D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wi (wral x3 (wral r (wrex s1 (wa (wbr (cv s1) (cv r) cle) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv C cbl)) (co (cv x3) (cv s1) (cfv D cbl)))) crp) crp) X) (wceq J K)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metss2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2015.)
theorem metss2lem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D: class) (R S: class x3) (J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (co R (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel S crp))) (wss (co (cv x3) (co S R cdiv) (cfv D cbl)) (co (cv x3) S (cfv C cbl))) $;

--| If the metric ` D ` is "strongly finer" than ` C ` (meaning that there is a positive real constant ` R ` such that ` C ( x , y ) <_ R x. D ( x , y ) ` ), then ` D ` generates a finer topology. (Using this theorem twice in each direction states that if two metrics are strongly equivalent, then they generate the same topology.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2015.)
theorem metss2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D: class) (R: class x3) (J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (co R (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss J K) $;

--| The composition of an extended metric with a monotonic subadditive function is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem comet {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (D F: class) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cxr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 cpnf cicc))) (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)))) (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cxad) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom F D) (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| Value of the standard bounded metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem stdbdmetval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (R: class) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R cle) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cif (wbr (co A2 B2 C) R cle) (co A2 B2 C) R)) $;

--| The standard bounded metric is an extended metric given an extended metric and a positive extended real cutoff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem stdbdxmet {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (R X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R cle) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr) (wbr cc0 R clt)) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $;

--| The standard bounded metric is a proper metric given an extended metric and a positive real cutoff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem stdbdmet {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (R X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R cle) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R crp)) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| The standard bounded metric corresponding to ` C ` generates the same balls as ` C ` for radii less than ` R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem stdbdbl {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (P R: class) (S: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R cle) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr) (wbr cc0 R clt)) (w3a (wcel P X) (wcel S cxr) (wbr S R cle))) (wceq (co P S (cfv D cbl)) (co P S (cfv C cbl))) $;

--| The standard bounded metric corresponding to ` C ` generates the same topology as ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem stdbdmopn {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class) (D: class x3 y1) (R: class) (J: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R cle) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) R)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel R cxr) (wbr cc0 R clt)) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| The topology generated by an extended metric can also be generated by a true metric. Thus, "metrizable topologies" can equivalently be defined in terms of metrics or extended metrics. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem mopnex {d: setvar} (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wrex d (wceq J (cfv (cv d) cmopn)) (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| The topology generated by a metric space is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Aug-2015.)
theorem methaus (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J cha) $;

--| The topology generated by a metric space is first-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem met1stc (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J c1stc) $;

--| A separable metric space (a metric space with a countable dense subset) is second-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem met2ndci (A2 D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (w3a (wss A2 X) (wbr A2 com cdom) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) X))) (wcel J c2ndc) $;

--| A metric space is second-countable iff it is separable (has a countable dense subset). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2015.)
theorem met2ndc {x3: setvar} (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel J c2ndc) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cfv J ccl)) X)) (cpw X))) $;

--| Two alternate formulations of a subspace topology of a metric space topology. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 19-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2014.)
theorem metrest (C D J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq D (cres C (cxp Y Y)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wss Y X)) (wceq (co J Y crest) K) $;

--| The restriction of a metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ressxms (A2 K V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cxms) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co K A2 cress) cxms) $;

--| The restriction of a metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2015.)
theorem ressms (A2 K V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cms) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co K A2 cress) cms) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prdsms . The distance function of a product structure is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsmslem1 (ph: wff) (B2 D R S I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cms R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cmet)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prdsms . The distance function of a product structure is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsxmslem1 (ph: wff) (B2 D R S I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cxms R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv B2 cxmet)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ prdsxms . The topology generated by the supremum metric is the same as the product topology, when the index set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsxmslem2 {x3 z g2 k: setvar} (ph: wff z) (B2: class x3 z) (C: class x3 z g2 k) (D: class z) (R: class) (S: class z) (E: class x3 z k) (I: class) (J K V: class x3 z g2 k) (W Y: class z):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cds) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cxms R) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv Y ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv (cfv (cv k) R) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres (cfv (cfv (cv k) R) cds) (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv (cv k) R) ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab x3 (wex g2 (wa (w3a (wfn (cv g2) I) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv k) (ccom ctopn R))) I) (wrex z (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) (cuni (cfv (cv k) (ccom ctopn R)))) (cdif I (cv z))) cfn)) (wceq (cv x3) (cixp k I (cfv (cv k) (cv g2))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| The indexed product structure is an extended metric space when the index set is finite. (Although the extended metric is still valid when the index set is infinite, it no longer agrees with the product topology, which is not metrizable in any case.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsxms (R S I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S W) (wcel I cfn) (wf I cxms R)) (wcel Y cxms) $;

--| The indexed product structure is a metric space when the index set is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem prdsms (R S I W Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co S R cprds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S W) (wcel I cfn) (wf I cms R)) (wcel Y cms) $;

--| A power of an extended metric space is an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem pwsxms (R I Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cxms) (wcel I cfn)) (wcel Y cxms) $;

--| A power of a metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem pwsms (R I Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co R I cpws) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cms) (wcel I cfn)) (wcel Y cms) $;

--| A binary product of metric spaces is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsxms (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cxms) (wcel S cxms)) (wcel T cxms) $;

--| A binary product of metric spaces is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem xpsms (R S T: class):
  $ wceq T (co R S cxps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cms) (wcel S cms)) (wcel T cms) $;

--| Express the product of two metrics as another metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsxps (ph: wff) (P M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (co (cfv M ctms) (cfv N ctms) cxps) cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv (cxp X Y) cxmet)) $;

--| Express the product of two metrics as another metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsxpsmopn (ph: wff) (P J K L M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (co (cfv M ctms) (cfv N ctms) cxps) cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv M cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv N cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv P cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L (co J K ctx)) $;

--| Value of the product of two metrics. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsxpsval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (co (cfv M ctms) (cfv N ctms) cxps) cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) P) (csup (cpr (co A2 C M) (co B2 D N)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Value of the product of two metrics. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem tmsxpsval2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv (co (cfv M ctms) (cfv N ctms) cxps) cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) P) (cif (wbr (co A2 C M) (co B2 D N) cle) (co B2 D N) (co A2 C M))) $;

--| Two ways to express that ` F ` is continuous at ` P ` for metric spaces. Proposition 14-4.2 of [Gleason] p. 240. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem metcnp3 {y1 z: setvar} (C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wrex z (wss (cima F (co P (cv z) (cfv C cbl))) (co (cfv P F) (cv y1) (cfv D cbl))) crp) crp))) $;

--| Two ways to say a mapping from metric ` C ` to metric ` D ` is continuous at point ` P ` . (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem metcnp {y1 z w: setvar} (C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (co P (cv w) C) (cv z) clt) (wbr (co (cfv P F) (cfv (cv w) F) D) (cv y1) clt)) X) crp) crp))) $;

--| Two ways to say a mapping from metric ` C ` to metric ` D ` is continuous at point ` P ` . The distance arguments are swapped compared to ~ metcnp (and Munkres' ~ metcn ) for compatibility with ~ df-lm . Definition 1.3-3 of [Kreyszig] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem metcnp2 {y1 z w: setvar} (C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel P X)) (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (co (cv w) P C) (cv z) clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv P F) D) (cv y1) clt)) X) crp) crp))) $;

--| Two ways to say a mapping from metric ` C ` to metric ` D ` is continuous. Theorem 10.1 of [Munkres] p. 127. The second biconditional argument says that for every positive "epsilon" ` y ` there is a positive "delta" ` z ` such that a distance less than delta in ` C ` maps to a distance less than epsilon in ` D ` . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem metcn {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (C D F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet))) (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv w) C) (cv z) clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv w) F) D) (cv y1) clt)) X) crp) crp) X))) $;

--| Epsilon-delta property of a continuous metric space function, with function arguments as in ~ metcnp . (Contributed by NM, 17-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem metcnpi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet))) (wa (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel A2 crp))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co P (cv y1) C) (cv x3) clt) (wbr (co (cfv P F) (cfv (cv y1) F) D) A2 clt)) X) crp) $;

--| Epsilon-delta property of a continuous metric space function, with swapped distance function arguments as in ~ metcnp2 . (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem metcnpi2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet))) (wa (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel A2 crp))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co (cv y1) P C) (cv x3) clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv P F) D) A2 clt)) X) crp) $;

--| Epsilon-delta property of a metric space function continuous at ` P ` . A variation of ~ metcnpi2 with non-strict ordering. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem metcnpi3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet))) (wa (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wcel A2 crp))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co (cv y1) P C) (cv x3) cle) (wbr (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv P F) D) A2 cle)) X) crp) $;

--| Continuity of a binary operation on metric spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txmetcnp {z w v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C D E F J K: class) (L: class v u) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel E (cfv Z cxmet))) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y))) (wb (wcel F (cfv (cop A2 B2) (co (co J K ctx) L ccnp))) (wa (wf (cxp X Y) Z F) (wral z (wrex w (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (co A2 (cv u) C) (cv w) clt) (wbr (co B2 (cv v) D) (cv w) clt)) (wbr (co (co A2 B2 F) (co (cv u) (cv v) F) E) (cv z) clt)) Y) X) crp) crp))) $;

--| Continuity of a binary operation on metric spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem txmetcn {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (C D E F J K: class) (L: class v u) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel E (cfv Z cxmet))) (wb (wcel F (co (co J K ctx) L ccn)) (wa (wf (cxp X Y) Z F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wrex w (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv u) C) (cv w) clt) (wbr (co (cv y1) (cv v) D) (cv w) clt)) (wbr (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (co (cv u) (cv v) F) E) (cv z) clt)) Y) X) crp) crp) Y) X))) $;

--| Value of the uniform structure generated by metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metuval {a: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq (cfv D cmetu) (co (cxp X X) (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) cfg)) $;

--| Define a filter base ` F ` generated by a metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustel {a: setvar} (B2 D: class) (F: class a) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wb (wcel B2 F) (wrex a (wceq B2 (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico))) crp)) $;

--| Range of the elements of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustss {a: setvar} (A2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 F)) (wss A2 (cxp X X)) $;

--| Elements of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` are relations. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustrel {a: setvar} (A2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 F)) (wrel A2) $;

--| Any two elements of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` can be compared, like for RR+ (i.e. it's totally ordered). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustto {a: setvar} (A2 B2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 F)) (wo (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 A2)) $;

--| The identity diagonal is included in all elements of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem metustid {a: setvar} (A2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 F)) (wss (cres cid X) A2) $;

--| Elements of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` are symmetric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustsym {a: setvar} (A2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 F)) (wceq (ccnv A2) A2) $;

--| For any element ` A ` of the filter base generated by the metric ` D ` , the half element (corresponding to half the distance) is also in this base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metustexhalf {v a: setvar} (A2 D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wcel A2 F)) (wrex v (wss (ccom (cv v) (cv v)) A2) F) $;

--| The filter base generated by a metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem metustfbas {a: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wcel F (cfv (cxp X X) cfbas)) $;

--| The uniform structure generated by a metric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metust {a: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wcel (co (cxp X X) F cfg) (cfv X cust)) $;

--| Given a metric ` D ` and a uniform structure generated by that metric, Cauchy filter bases on that uniform structure are exactly the filter bases which contain balls of any pre-chosen size. See ~ iscfil . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem cfilucfil {x3 y1 a: setvar} (C D F X: class):
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wb (wcel C (cfv (co (cxp X X) F cfg) ccfilu)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cfbas)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cima D (cxp (cv y1) (cv y1))) (co cc0 (cv x3) cico)) C) crp))) $;

--| The uniform structure generated by metric ` D ` is a uniform structure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metuust (D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wcel (cfv D cmetu) (cfv X cust)) $;

--| Given a metric ` D ` and a uniform structure generated by that metric, Cauchy filter bases on that uniform structure are exactly the filter bases which contain balls of any pre-chosen size. See ~ iscfil . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem cfilucfil2 {x3 y1: setvar} (C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wb (wcel C (cfv (cfv D cmetu) ccfilu)) (wa (wcel C (cfv X cfbas)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cima D (cxp (cv y1) (cv y1))) (co cc0 (cv x3) cico)) C) crp))) $;

--| The ball around a point ` P ` , alternative definition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem blval2 (D P R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel P X) (wcel R crp)) (wceq (co P R (cfv D cbl)) (cima (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 R cico)) (csn P))) $;

--| Membership in a ball, alternative definition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem elbl4 (A2 B2 D R X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel R crp)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wb (wcel B2 (co A2 R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr B2 A2 (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 R cico)))) $;

--| Elementhood in the uniform structure generated by a metric ` D ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metuel {w a: setvar} (D: class) (V: class a) (X: class w):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wb (wcel V (cfv D cmetu)) (wa (wss V (cxp X X)) (wrex w (wss (cv w) V) (crn (cmpt a crp (cima (ccnv D) (co cc0 (cv a) cico))))))) $;

--| Elementhood in the uniform structure generated by a metric ` D ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metuel2 {x3 y1 d: setvar} (D: class) (U: class x3 y1 d) (V X: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv D cmetu) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wb (wcel V U) (wa (wss V (cxp X X)) (wrex d (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (cv d) clt) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) V)) X) X) crp))) $;

--| The "section" image of an entourage at a point ` P ` always contains a ball (centered on this point). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Dec-2017.)
theorem metustbl {a: setvar} (D P V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel V (cfv D cmetu)) (wcel P X)) (wrex a (wa (wcel P (cv a)) (wss (cv a) (cima V (csn P)))) (crn (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| The topology induced by a uniform structure generated by a metric ` D ` is generated by that metric's open balls. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem psmetutop (D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet))) (wceq (cfv (cfv D cmetu) cutop) (cfv (crn (cfv D cbl)) ctg)) $;

--| The topology induced by a uniform structure generated by an extended metric ` D ` is that metric's open sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem xmetutop (D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wceq (cfv (cfv D cmetu) cutop) (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| If the uniform set of a metric space is the uniform structure generated by its metric, then it is a uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Dec-2017.)
theorem xmsusp (D U F X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv F cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv F cuss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wne X c0) (wcel F cxms) (wceq U (cfv D cmetu))) (wcel F cusp) $;

--| The uniform structure generated by the restriction of a metric is its trace. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Dec-2017.)
theorem restmetu (A2 D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wne A2 c0) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wss A2 X)) (wceq (co (cfv D cmetu) (cxp A2 A2) crest) (cfv (cres D (cxp A2 A2)) cmetu)) $;

--| Uniform continuity in metric spaces. Compare the order of the quantifiers with ~ metcn . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metucn {x3 y1 c d: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D: class) (U: class c d) (F: class) (V: class y1 c d) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv C cmetu) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv D cmetu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cpsmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv Y cpsmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co U V cucn)) (wa (wf X Y F) (wral d (wrex c (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (cv c) clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) D) (cv d) clt)) X) X) crp) crp))) $;

--| The discrete metric on any set ` X ` . Definition 1.1-8 of [Kreyszig] p. 8. (Contributed by FL, 12-Oct-2006.)
theorem dscmet {x3 y1: setvar} (D V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| The discrete metric generates the discrete topology. In particular, the discrete topology is metrizable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem dscopn {x3 y1: setvar} (D V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv D cmopn) (cpw X)) $;

--| Show that a group norm generates a metric. Part of Definition 2.2-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 58. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrmmetd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (G: class x3 y1) (c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom F c_mi) (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| An absolute value ` F ` generates a metric defined by ` d ( x , y ) = F ( x - y ) ` , analogously to ~ cnmet . (In fact, the ring structure is not needed at all; the group properties ~ abveq0 and ~ abvtri , ~ abvneg are sufficient.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem abvmet (A2 R F c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cabv) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv R csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel (ccom F c_mi) (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| Norm of a normed ring.
term cnm: class;

--| The class of all normed groups.
term cngp: class;

--| Make a normed group from a norm and a group.
term ctng: class;

--| Normed ring.
term cnrg: class;

--| Normed module.
term cnlm: class;

--| Normed vector space.
term cnvc: class;

--| Define the norm on a group or ring (when it makes sense) in terms of the distance to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nm {x3 w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnm (cmpt w cvv (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv w) cbs) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) c0g) (cfv (cv w) cds)))) $;

--| Define a normed group, which is a group with a right-translation-invariant metric. This is not a standard notion, but is helpful as the most general context in which a metric-like norm makes sense. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_ngp {g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cngp (crab g2 (wss (ccom (cfv (cv g2) cnm) (cfv (cv g2) csg)) (cfv (cv g2) cds)) (cin cgrp cms)) $;

--| Define a function that fills in the topology and metric components of a structure given a group and a norm on it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_tng {f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctng (cmpo g2 f2 cvv cvv (co (co (cv g2) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (ccom (cv f2) (cfv (cv g2) csg))) csts) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (ccom (cv f2) (cfv (cv g2) csg)) cmopn)) csts)) $;

--| A normed ring is a ring with an induced topology and metric such that the metric is translation-invariant and the norm (distance from 0) is an absolute value on the ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nrg {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnrg (crab w (wcel (cfv (cv w) cnm) (cfv (cv w) cabv)) cngp) $;

--| A normed (left) module is a module which is also a normed group over a normed ring, such that the norm distributes over scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nlm {x3 y1 w f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnlm (crab w (wsbc f2 (wa (wcel (cv f2) cnrg) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cvsca)) (cfv (cv w) cnm)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv f2) cnm)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cnm)) cmul)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)) (cfv (cv f2) cbs))) (cfv (cv w) csca)) (cin cngp clmod)) $;

--| A normed vector space is a normed module which is also a vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nvc:
  $ wceq cnvc (cin cnlm clvec) $;

--| The value of the norm function as the distance to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmfval {x3: setvar} (D: class) (N: class x3) (W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt x3 X (co (cv x3) c_0 D)) $;

--| The value of the norm as the distance to zero. Problem 1 of [Kreyszig] p. 63. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmval (A2 D N W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 N) (co A2 c_0 D)) $;

--| The value of the norm function on a structure containing a zero as the distance restricted to the elements of the base set to zero. Examples of structures containing a "zero" are groups (see ~ nmfval2 proved from this theorem and ~ grpidcl ) or more generally monoids (see ~ mndidcl ), or pointed sets). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) Extract this result from the proof of ~ nmfval2 . (Revised by BJ, 27-Aug-2024.)
theorem nmfval0 {x3: setvar} (D: class) (E N: class x3) (W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres D (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel c_0 X) (wceq N (cmpt x3 X (co (cv x3) c_0 E))) $;

--| The value of the norm function on a group as the distance restricted to the elements of the base set to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmfval2 {x3: setvar} (D: class) (E N: class x3) (W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres D (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cgrp) (wceq N (cmpt x3 X (co (cv x3) c_0 E))) $;

--| The value of the norm on a group as the distance restricted to the elements of the base set to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmval2 (A2 D E N W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres D (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cgrp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv A2 N) (co A2 c_0 E)) $;

--| The norm on a metric group is a function from the base set into the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmf2 (D E N W X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres D (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cgrp) (wcel E (cfv X cmet))) (wf X cr N) $;

--| Weak property deduction for a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmpropd (ph: wff) (K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cbs) (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cplusg) (cfv L cplusg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cds) (cfv L cds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cnm) (cfv L cnm)) $;

--| Strong property deduction for a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmpropd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cgrp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp B2 B2)) (cres (cfv L cds) (cxp B2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K cnm) (cfv L cnm)) $;

--| The property of being a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem isngp (D G c_mi N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G cms) (wss (ccom N c_mi) D)) $;

--| The property of being a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem isngp2 (D E G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cres D (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G cms) (wceq (ccom N c_mi) E)) $;

--| The property of being a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem isngp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (D G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G cms) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) N)) X) X)) $;

--| A normed group is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpgrp (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| A normed group is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpms (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cms) $;

--| A normed group is an extended metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpxms (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cxms) $;

--| A normed group is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngptps (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel G ctps) $;

--| The (induced) metric of a normed group is a metric. Part of Definition 2.2-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 58. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 14-Oct-2021.)
theorem ngpmet (D G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv G cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| Value of the distance function in terms of the norm of a normed group. Equation 1 of [Kreyszig] p. 59. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpds (A2 B2 D G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N)) $;

--| Value of the distance function in terms of the norm of a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpdsr (A2 B2 D G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cfv (co B2 A2 c_mi) N)) $;

--| Write the distance between two points in terms of distance from zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpds2 (A2 B2 D G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co (co A2 B2 c_mi) c_0 D)) $;

--| Write the distance between two points in terms of distance from zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpds2r (A2 B2 D G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co (co B2 A2 c_mi) c_0 D)) $;

--| Write the distance between two points in terms of distance from zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpds3 (A2 B2 D G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co c_0 (co A2 B2 c_mi) D)) $;

--| Write the distance between two points in terms of distance from zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpds3r (A2 B2 D G c_mi X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (co c_0 (co B2 A2 c_mi) D)) $;

--| Cancel right addition inside a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngprcan (A2 B2 C D c_pl G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wceq (co (co A2 C c_pl) (co B2 C c_pl) D) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| Cancel left addition inside a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngplcan (A2 B2 C D c_pl G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cabl)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wceq (co (co C A2 c_pl) (co C B2 c_pl) D) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| Express the property of being a normed group purely in terms of right-translation invariance of the metric instead of using the definition of norm (which itself uses the metric). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Oct-2015.)
theorem isngp4 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (D: class) (c_pl: class x3 y1) (G X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel G cgrp) (wcel G cms) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) D) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D)) X) X) X)) $;

--| Two elements are the same distance apart as their inverses. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpinvds (A2 B2 D G I X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cabl)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wceq (co (cfv A2 I) (cfv B2 I) D) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| Cancel right subtraction inside a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngpsubcan (A2 B2 C D G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wceq (co (co A2 C c_mi) (co B2 C c_mi) D) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| The norm on a normed group is a function into the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmf (G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wf X cr N) $;

--| The norm of a normed group is closed in the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmcl (A2 G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cfv A2 N) cr) $;

--| The norm of a normed group is nonnegative. Second part of Problem 2 of [Kreyszig] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmge0 (A2 G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 N) cle) $;

--| The identity is the only element of the group with zero norm. First part of Problem 2 of [Kreyszig] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmeq0 (A2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 N) cc0) (wceq A2 c_0)) $;

--| The norm of a nonzero element is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmne0 (A2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wne A2 c_0)) (wne (cfv A2 N) cc0) $;

--| The norm of a nonzero element is a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmrpcl (A2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wne A2 c_0)) (wcel (cfv A2 N) crp) $;

--| The norm of a negated element is the same as the norm of the original element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nminv (A2 G I N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 I) N) (cfv A2 N)) $;

--| The triangle inequality for the norm of a subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmmtri (A2 B2 G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) caddc) cle) $;

--| The norm of the difference between two elements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmsub (A2 B2 G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) (cfv (co B2 A2 c_mi) N)) $;

--| Reverse triangle inequality for the norm of a subtraction. Problem 3 of [Kreyszig] p. 64. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmrtri (A2 B2 G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) cle) $;

--| Inequality for the difference of norms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nm2dif (A2 B2 G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) cle) $;

--| The triangle inequality for the norm of a sum. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmtri (A2 B2 c_pl G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) caddc) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the norm of two subtractions. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem nmtri2 (A2 B2 C G c_mi N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wbr (cfv (co A2 C c_mi) N) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) (cfv (co B2 C c_mi) N) caddc) cle) $;

--| The properties of a normed group, which is a group accompanied by a norm. (Contributed by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem ngpi {x3 y1: setvar} (c_mi N: class x3 y1) (V: class x3) (W: class) (c_0: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cngp) (w3a (wcel W cgrp) (wf V cr N) (wral x3 (wa (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) N) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N) caddc) cle) V)) V)) $;

--| Norm of the identity element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nm0 (G N c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wceq (cfv c_0 N) cc0) $;

--| The norm of a nonzero element is a positive real. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2007.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem nmgt0 (A2 G N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wne A2 c_0) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 N) clt)) $;

--| The induced metric on a subgroup is the induced metric on the parent group equipped with a norm. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 19-Oct-2021.)
theorem sgrim (D S T U E X: class):
  $ wceq X (co T U cress) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv X cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U S) (wceq E D) $;

--| The induced metric on a subgroup in terms of the induced metric on the parent normed group. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 19-Oct-2021.)
theorem sgrimval (A2 B2 D S T U E G N X: class):
  $ wceq X (co T U cress) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv X cds) $ >
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T csubg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel U S)) (wa (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 U))) (wceq (co A2 B2 E) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| The norm in a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem subgnm (A2 G H M N: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv H cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv G csubg)) (wceq M (cres N A2)) $;

--| A substructure assigns the same values to the norms of elements of a subgroup. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem subgnm2 (A2 G H M N X: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv H cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv G csubg)) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X M) (cfv X N)) $;

--| A normed group restricted to a subgroup is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem subgngp (A2 G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel A2 (cfv G csubg))) (wcel H cngp) $;

--| A normed abelian group is a topological group (with the topology induced by the metric induced by the norm). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngptgp (G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cngp) (wcel G cabl)) (wcel G ctgp) $;

--| Property deduction for a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem ngppropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv K cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv L cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp B2 B2)) (cres (cfv L cds) (cxp B2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K cngp) (wcel L cngp)) $;

--| The function ` toNrmGrp ` is a two-argument function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem reldmtng:
  $ wrel (cdm ctng) $;

--| Value of the function which augments a given structure ` G ` with a norm ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngval (D T G J c_mi N V W: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq D (ccom N c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel N W)) (wceq T (co (co G (cop (cfv cnx cds) D) csts) (cop (cfv cnx cts) J) csts)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tngbas and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tnglem (T E G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) (cfv cnx cts) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) (cfv cnx cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (cfv G E) (cfv T E)) $;

--| The base set of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngbas (B2 T G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq B2 (cfv T cbs)) $;

--| The group addition of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngplusg (c_pl T G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq c_pl (cfv T cplusg)) $;

--| The group identity of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tng0 (T G N V c_0: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g)) $;

--| The ring multiplication of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngmulr (T c_x G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq c_x (cfv T cmulr)) $;

--| The scalar ring of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngsca (T F G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq F (cfv T csca)) $;

--| The scalar multiplication of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngvsca (T c_x G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq c_x (cfv T cvsca)) $;

--| The inner product operation of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngip (T G c_xi N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv G cip) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq c_xi (cfv T cip)) $;

--| The metric function of a structure augmented with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Oct-2024.)
theorem tngds (T G c_mi N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (ccom N c_mi) (cfv T cds)) $;

--| The topology generated by a normed structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngtset (D T G J N V W: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel N W)) (wceq J (cfv T cts)) $;

--| The topology generated by a normed structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngtopn (D T G J N V W: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G V) (wcel N W)) (wceq J (cfv T ctopn)) $;

--| The topology generated by a normed structure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngnm (A2 T G N X: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wf X A2 N)) (wceq N (cfv T cnm)) $;

--| A norm turns a group into a normed group iff the generated metric is in fact a metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngngp2 (D T G N X: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wi (wf X cr N) (wb (wcel T cngp) (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)))) $;

--| Derive the axioms for a normed group from the axioms for a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngngpd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (T: class) (G: class x3 y1) (c_mi N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgrp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cr N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) N) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cngp) $;

--| Derive the axioms for a normed group from the axioms for a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngngp {x3 y1: setvar} (T: class) (G: class x3 y1) (c_mi N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wf X cr N) (wb (wcel T cngp) (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wral x3 (wa (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) N) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N) caddc) cle) X)) X))) $;

--| If a structure equipped with a norm is a normed group, the structure itself must be a group. (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2021.)
theorem tnggrpr (T G N V: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wcel T cngp)) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| Alternate definition of a normed group (i.e., a group equipped with a norm) without using the properties of a metric space. This corresponds to the definition in N. H. Bingham, A. J. Ostaszewski: "Normed versus topological groups: dichotomy and duality", 2010, Dissertationes Mathematicae 472, pp. 1-138 and E. Deza, M.M. Deza: "Dictionary of Distances", Elsevier, 2006. (Contributed by AV, 16-Oct-2021.)
theorem tngngp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl T G I N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wf X cr N) (wb (wcel T cngp) (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wral x3 (w3a (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) N) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cfv (cv x3) N)) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N) caddc) cle) X)) X))) $;

--| The augmentation of a normed group by its own norm has the same distance function as the normed group (restricted to the base set). (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2021.)
theorem nrmtngdist (T G X: class):
  $ wceq T (co G (cfv G cnm) ctng) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wceq (cfv T cds) (cres (cfv G cds) (cxp X X))) $;

--| The augmentation of a normed group by its own norm is a normed group with the same norm. (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2021.)
theorem nrmtngnrm (T G: class):
  $ wceq T (co G (cfv G cnm) ctng) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wa (wcel T cngp) (wceq (cfv T cnm) (cfv G cnm))) $;

--| The induced metric of a normed group is a function. (Contributed by AV, 19-Oct-2021.)
theorem tngngpim (D T G N X: class):
  $ wceq T (co G N ctng) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wf (cxp X X) cr D) $;

--| A normed ring is a ring with a norm that makes it into a normed group, and such that the norm is an absolute value on the ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnrg (A2 R N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cabv) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cnrg) (wa (wcel R cngp) (wcel N A2)) $;

--| The norm of a normed ring is an absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgabv (A2 R N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cabv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel N A2) $;

--| A normed ring is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgngp (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cngp) $;

--| A normed ring is a ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgring (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R crg) $;

--| The norm of a product in a normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmmul (A2 B2 R c_x N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cnrg) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_x) N) (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) cmul)) $;

--| Distribute a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgdsdi (A2 B2 C D R c_x N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnrg) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wceq (co (cfv A2 N) (co B2 C D) cmul) (co (co A2 B2 c_x) (co A2 C c_x) D)) $;

--| Distribute a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgdsdir (A2 B2 C D R c_x N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnrg) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 D) (cfv C N) cmul) (co (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 C c_x) D)) $;

--| The norm of one in a nonzero normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nm1 (R c_1 N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cnzr)) (wceq (cfv c_1 N) c1) $;

--| The norm of a unit is nonzero in a nonzero normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem unitnmn0 (A2 R U N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cnzr) (wcel A2 U)) (wne (cfv A2 N) cc0) $;

--| The norm of an inverse in a nonzero normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nminvr (A2 R U I N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cnzr) (wcel A2 U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 I) N) (co c1 (cfv A2 N) cdiv)) $;

--| The norm of a division in a nonzero normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmdvr (A2 B2 c_dv R U N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cnzr)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 U))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_dv) N) (co (cfv A2 N) (cfv B2 N) cdiv)) $;

--| A nonzero normed ring is a domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgdomn (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wb (wcel R cdomn) (wcel R cnzr)) $;

--| A normed ring is a topological group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgtgp (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R ctgp) $;

--| A normed ring restricted to a subring is a normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem subrgnrg (A2 G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co G A2 cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cnrg) (wcel A2 (cfv G csubrg))) (wcel H cnrg) $;

--| Given any absolute value over a ring, augmenting the ring with the absolute value produces a normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem tngnrg (A2 R T F: class):
  $ wceq T (co R F ctng) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cabv) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wcel T cnrg) $;

--| A normed (left) module is a module which is also a normed group over a normed ring, such that the norm distributes over scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnlm {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 c_x: class) (F: class x3 y1) (K: class y1) (N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cnm) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cnlm) (wa (w3a (wcel W cngp) (wcel W clmod) (wcel F cnrg)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_x) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) A2) (cfv (cv y1) N) cmul)) V) K)) $;

--| Defining property of a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmvs (A2 c_x F K N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cnm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cnlm) (wcel X K) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_x) N) (co (cfv X A2) (cfv Y N) cmul)) $;

--| A normed module is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmngp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel W cngp) $;

--| A normed module is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmlmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| The scalar component of a left module is a normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmnrg (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel F cnrg) $;

--| The scalar component of a left module is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmngp2 (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel F cngp) $;

--| Distribute a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmdsdi (A2 D c_x F K V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cnlm) (w3a (wcel X K) (wcel Y V) (wcel Z V))) (wceq (co (cfv X A2) (co Y Z D) cmul) (co (co X Y c_x) (co X Z c_x) D)) $;

--| Distribute a distance calculation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmdsdir (D c_x E F K N V W X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv F cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cnlm) (w3a (wcel X K) (wcel Y K) (wcel Z V))) (wceq (co (co X Y E) (cfv Z N) cmul) (co (co X Z c_x) (co Y Z c_x) D)) $;

--| If a scalar product is zero, one of its factors must be zero. (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmmul0or (A2 B2 c_x F K O V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv F c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cnlm) (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 c_x) c_0) (wo (wceq A2 O) (wceq B2 c_0))) $;

--| The subring algebra over a normed ring is a normed left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem sranlm (A2 S W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv S (cfv W csra)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cnrg) (wcel S (cfv W csubrg))) (wcel A2 cnlm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nlmvscn . Compare this proof with the similar elementary proof ~ mulcn2 for continuity of multiplication on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmvscnlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R T c_x U E F K N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv F cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv B2 A2) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv X N) T caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cnlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 C E) U clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co X Y D) T clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co B2 X c_x) (co C Y c_x) D) R clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nlmvscn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmvscnlem1 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2: class x3 y1 r) (B2 D R: class x3 y1) (T c_x U: class) (E: class x3 y1) (F: class) (K: class x3) (N: class x3 y1 r) (V: class x3 y1) (W: class) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv F cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv B2 A2) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv X N) T caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cnlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wbr (co B2 (cv x3) E) (cv r) clt) (wbr (co X (cv y1) D) (cv r) clt)) (wbr (co (co B2 X c_x) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_x) D) R clt)) V) K) crp) $;

--| The scalar multiplication of a normed module is continuous. Lemma for ~ nrgtrg and ~ nlmtlm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmvscn (c_x F J K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cscaf) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel c_x (co (co K J ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| The ring module over a normed ring is a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem rlmnlm (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel (cfv R crglmod) cnlm) $;

--| The norm function in the ring module. (Contributed by AV, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem rlmnm (R: class):
  $ wceq (cfv R cnm) (cfv (cfv R crglmod) cnm) $;

--| A normed ring is a topological ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 31-Oct-2024.)
theorem nrgtrg (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R ctrg) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nrginvrcn . Compare this proof with ~ reccn2 , the elementary proof of continuity of division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrginvrcnlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 D: class y1) (R T U I: class) (N X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnzr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cif (wbr c1 (co (cfv A2 N) B2 cmul) cle) c1 (co (cfv A2 N) B2 cmul)) (co (cfv A2 N) c2 cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (co A2 (cv y1) D) (cv x3) clt) (wbr (co (cfv A2 I) (cfv (cv y1) I) D) B2 clt)) U) crp) $;

--| The ring inverse function is continuous in a normed ring. (Note that this is true even in rings which are not division rings.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrginvrcn (R U I J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cnrg) (wcel I (co (co J U crest) (co J U crest) ccn)) $;

--| A normed division ring is a topological division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nrgtdrg (R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cdr)) (wcel R ctdrg) $;

--| A normed module is a topological module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Oct-2015.)
theorem nlmtlm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnlm) (wcel W ctlm) $;

--| A normed vector space is just a normed module which is algebraically a vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnvc (W: class):
  $ wb (wcel W cnvc) (wa (wcel W cnlm) (wcel W clvec)) $;

--| A normed vector space is a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nvcnlm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnvc) (wcel W cnlm) $;

--| A normed vector space is a left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nvclvec (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnvc) (wcel W clvec) $;

--| A normed vector space is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nvclmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnvc) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| A normed vector space is just a normed module whose scalar ring is a division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnvc2 (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cnvc) (wa (wcel W cnlm) (wcel F cdr)) $;

--| A normed vector space is a topological vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nvctvc (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cnvc) (wcel W ctvc) $;

--| A subspace of a normed module is a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem lssnlm (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cnlm) (wcel U S)) (wcel X cnlm) $;

--| A subspace of a normed vector space is a normed vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem lssnvc (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cnvc) (wcel U S)) (wcel X cnvc) $;

--| The ring module over a normed division ring is a normed vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem rlmnvc (R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cdr)) (wcel (cfv R crglmod) cnvc) $;

--| Membership of an off-center vector in a ball in a normed module. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-2007.) (Revised by AV, 14-Oct-2021.)
theorem ngpocelbl (A2 D P c_pl R G N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv G cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cnlm) (wcel R cxr) (wa (wcel P X) (wcel A2 X))) (wb (wcel (co P A2 c_pl) (co P R (cfv D cbl))) (wbr (cfv A2 N) R clt)) $;

--| The operator norm function.
term cnmo: class;

--| The class of normed group homomorphisms.
term cnghm: class;

--| The class of normed module homomorphisms.
term cnmhm: class;

--| Define the norm of an operator between two normed groups (usually vector spaces). This definition produces an operator norm function for each pair of groups ` <. s , t >. ` . Equivalent to the definition of linear operator norm in [AkhiezerGlazman] p. 39. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_nmo {x3 t f2 s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnmo (cmpo s1 t cngp cngp (cmpt f2 (co (cv s1) (cv t) cghm) (cinf (crab r (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) cnm)) (co (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv s1) cnm)) cmul) cle) (cfv (cv s1) cbs)) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) cxr clt))) $;

--| Define the set of normed group homomorphisms between two normed groups. A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism which additionally bounds the increase of norm by a fixed real operator. In vector spaces these are also known as bounded linear operators. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nghm {t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnghm (cmpo s1 t cngp cngp (cima (ccnv (co (cv s1) (cv t) cnmo)) cr)) $;

--| Define a normed module homomorphism, also known as a bounded linear operator. This is a module homomorphism (a linear function) such that the operator norm is finite, or equivalently there is a constant ` c ` such that... (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_nmhm {t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnmhm (cmpo s1 t cnlm cnlm (cin (co (cv s1) (cv t) clmhm) (co (cv s1) (cv t) cnghm))) $;

--| The function producing operator norm functions is a function on normed groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmoffn:
  $ wfn cnmo (cxp cngp cngp) $;

--| Lemma for normed group homomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem reldmnghm:
  $ wrel (cdm cnghm) $;

--| Lemma for module homomorphisms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem reldmnmhm:
  $ wrel (cdm cnmhm) $;

--| Value of the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmofval {x3 f2 r: setvar} (S T L M: class) (N: class f2) (V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp)) (wceq N (cmpt f2 (co S T cghm) (cinf (crab r (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) M) (co (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) L) cmul) cle) V) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) cxr clt))) $;

--| Value of the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmoval {x3 r: setvar} (S T F L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wceq (cfv F N) (cinf (crab r (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) (co (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) L) cmul) cle) V) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Property of the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmogelb {x3 r: setvar} (A2 S T F L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wcel A2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 (cfv F N) cle) (wral r (wi (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) (co (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) L) cmul) cle) V) (wbr A2 (cv r) cle)) (co cc0 cpnf cico))) $;

--| Any upper bound on the values of a linear operator translates to an upper bound on the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmolb {x3: setvar} (A2 S T F L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wi (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) (co A2 (cfv (cv x3) L) cmul) cle) V) (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle)) $;

--| Any upper bound on the values of a linear operator at nonzero vectors translates to an upper bound on the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmolb2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S T F L M N V: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cngp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cngp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) V) (wne (cv x3) c_0))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) (co A2 (cfv (cv x3) L) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) $;

--| The operator norm is a function into the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Sep-2020.)
theorem nmof (S T N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp)) (wf (co S T cghm) cxr N) $;

--| The operator norm of an operator is an extended real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmocl (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wcel (cfv F N) cxr) $;

--| The operator norm of an operator is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoge0 (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wbr cc0 (cfv F N) cle) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism with bounded norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmfval (S T N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq (co S T cnghm) (cima (ccnv N) cr) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism with bounded norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnghm (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp)) (wa (wcel F (co S T cghm)) (wcel (cfv F N) cr))) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism with bounded norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnghm2 (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wb (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel (cfv F N) cr)) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism with bounded norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnghm3 (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wb (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wbr (cfv F N) cpnf clt)) $;

--| A bounded group homomorphism is a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem bddnghm (A2 S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle))) (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism has a real operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmcl (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel (cfv F N) cr) $;

--| The operator norm achieves the minimum of the set of upper bounds, if the operator is bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoi (S T F L M N V X: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel X V)) (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) M) (co (cfv F N) (cfv X L) cmul) cle) $;

--| The operator norm is a bound on the size of an operator, even when it is infinite (using extended real multiplication). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoix (S T F L M N V X: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wcel X V)) (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) M) (co (cfv F N) (cfv X L) cxmu) cle) $;

--| The operator norm is a bound on the growth of a vector under the action of the operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoi2 (S T F L M N V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wa (wcel X V) (wne X c_0))) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv X F) M) (cfv X L) cdiv) (cfv F N) cle) $;

--| The operator norm, defined as an infimum of upper bounds, can also be defined as a supremum of norms of ` F ( x ) ` away from zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoleub {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S T F L M N V: class) (c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cngp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cngp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cv x3) c_0) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) (cfv (cv x3) L) cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| Reverse closure for a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmrcl1 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel S cngp) $;

--| Reverse closure for a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmrcl2 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel T cngp) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmghm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $;

--| The operator norm of the zero operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmo0 (S T N V c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp)) (wceq (cfv (cxp V (csn c_0)) N) cc0) $;

--| The operator norm is zero only for the zero operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoeq0 (S T F N V c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp) (wcel F (co S T cghm))) (wb (wceq (cfv F N) cc0) (wceq F (cxp V (csn c_0)))) $;

--| An upper bound on the operator norm of a composition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoco (S T U F G L M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S U cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq L (co T U cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq M (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cnghm)) (wcel G (co S T cnghm))) (wbr (cfv (ccom F G) N) (co (cfv F L) (cfv G M) cmul) cle) $;

--| The composition of normed group homomorphisms is a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cnghm)) (wcel G (co S T cnghm))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cnghm)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for the operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmotri (c_pl S T F G N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T cabl) (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel G (co S T cnghm))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof c_pl)) N) (co (cfv F N) (cfv G N) caddc) cle) $;

--| The sum of two bounded linear operators is bounded linear. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmplusg (c_pl S T F G: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T cabl) (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel G (co S T cnghm))) (wcel (co F G (cof c_pl)) (co S T cnghm)) $;

--| The zero operator is a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem _0nghm (S T V c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cngp) (wcel T cngp)) (wcel (cxp V (csn c_0)) (co S T cnghm)) $;

--| The operator norm of the identity function on a nontrivial group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoid (S N V c_0: class):
  $ wceq N (co S S cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cngp) (wpss (csn c_0) V)) (wceq (cfv (cres cid V) N) c1) $;

--| The identity operator is a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem idnghm (S V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S cngp) (wcel (cres cid V) (co S S cnghm)) $;

--| Upper bound for the distance between the values of a bounded linear operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmods (A2 B2 C D S T F N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wbr (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) D) (co (cfv F N) (co A2 B2 C) cmul) cle) $;

--| A normed group homomorphism is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nghmcn (S T F J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism is a left module homomorphism which is also a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnmhm (S T F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wa (wa (wcel S cnlm) (wcel T cnlm)) (wa (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) (wcel F (co S T cnghm)))) $;

--| Reverse closure for a normed module homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmrcl1 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel S cnlm) $;

--| Reverse closure for a normed module homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmrcl2 (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel T cnlm) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism is a left module homomorphism (a linear operator). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmlmhm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism is a normed group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmnghm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel F (co S T cnghm)) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism is a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmghm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel F (co S T cghm)) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism is a left module homomorphism with bounded norm (a bounded linear operator). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem isnmhm2 (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cnlm) (wcel T cnlm) (wcel F (co S T clmhm))) (wb (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel (cfv F N) cr)) $;

--| A normed module homomorphism has a real operator norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmcl (S T F N: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel (cfv F N) cr) $;

--| The identity operator is a bounded linear operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem idnmhm (S V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S cnlm) (wcel (cres cid V) (co S S cnmhm)) $;

--| The zero operator is a bounded linear operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem _0nmhm (S T F G V c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv T c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv T csca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cnlm) (wcel T cnlm) (wceq F G)) (wcel (cxp V (csn c_0)) (co S T cnmhm)) $;

--| The composition of bounded linear operators is a bounded linear operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cnmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cnmhm))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cnmhm)) $;

--| The sum of two bounded linear operators is bounded linear. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmplusg (c_pl S T F G: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv T cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wcel G (co S T cnmhm))) (wcel (co F G (cof c_pl)) (co S T cnmhm)) $;

--| The set of open intervals with endpoints in a subset forms a basis for a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem qtopbaslem (S: class):
  $ wss S cxr $ >
  $ wcel (cima cioo (cxp S S)) ctb $;

--| The set of open intervals with rational endpoints forms a basis for a topology. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem qtopbas:
  $ wcel (cima cioo (cxp cq cq)) ctb $;

--| A basis for the standard topology on the reals. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem retopbas:
  $ wcel (crn cioo) ctb $;

--| The standard topology on the reals. (Contributed by FL, 4-Jun-2007.)
theorem retop:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ctop $;

--| The underlying set of the standard topology on the reals is the reals. (Contributed by FL, 4-Jun-2007.)
theorem uniretop:
  $ wceq cr (cuni (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is a topology on the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem retopon:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (cfv cr ctopon) $;

--| The standard topological space on the reals. (Contributed by NM, 19-Oct-2012.)
theorem retps (K: class):
  $ wceq K (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cr) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cfv (crn cioo) ctg))) $ >
  $ wcel K ctps $;

--| Open intervals are open sets of the standard topology on the reals . (Contributed by FL, 18-Jun-2007.)
theorem iooretop (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $;

--| Closed intervals are closed sets of the standard topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by FL, 14-Sep-2007.)
theorem icccld (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccld)) $;

--| Right-unbounded closed intervals are closed sets of the standard topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem icopnfcld (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co A2 cpnf cico) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccld)) $;

--| Left-unbounded closed intervals are closed sets of the standard topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem iocmnfcld (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co cmnf A2 cioc) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccld)) $;

--| ` QQ ` is dense in the standard topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-2007.)
theorem qdensere:
  $ wceq (cfv cq (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccl)) cr $;

--| Value of the distance function of the metric space of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem cnmetdval (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs)) $;

--| The absolute value metric determines a metric space on the complex numbers. This theorem provides a link between complex numbers and metrics spaces, making metric space theorems available for use with complex numbers. (Contributed by FL, 9-Oct-2006.)
theorem cnmet:
  $ wcel (ccom cabs cmin) (cfv cc cmet) $;

--| The absolute value metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnxmet:
  $ wcel (ccom cabs cmin) (cfv cc cxmet) $;

--| Two ways to write the open ball centered at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnbl0 (D R: class):
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cxr) (wceq (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) (co cc0 R (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| Two ways to write the closed ball centered at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnblcld {x3: setvar} (D R: class):
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cxr) (wceq (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cicc)) (crab x3 (wbr (co cc0 (cv x3) D) R cle) cc)) $;

--| The complex number field is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfldms:
  $ wcel ccnfld cms $;

--| The complex number field is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfldxms:
  $ wcel ccnfld cxms $;

--| The complex number field is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfldtps:
  $ wcel ccnfld ctps $;

--| The norm of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnfldnm:
  $ wceq cabs (cfv ccnfld cnm) $;

--| The complex numbers form a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnngp:
  $ wcel ccnfld cngp $;

--| The complex numbers form a normed ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnnrg:
  $ wcel ccnfld cnrg $;

--| The topology of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem cnfldtopn (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cmopn) $;

--| The topology of the complex numbers is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnfldtopon (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J (cfv cc ctopon) $;

--| The topology of the complex numbers is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnfldtop (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J ctop $;

--| The topology of the complex numbers is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnfldhaus (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J cha $;

--| The underlying set of the standard topology on the complex numbers is the set of complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem unicntop:
  $ wceq cc (cuni (cfv ccnfld ctopn)) $;

--| The set of complex numbers is open with respect to the standard topology on complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cnopn:
  $ wcel cc (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $;

--| The set of nonzero complex numbers is open with respect to the standard topology on complex numbers. (Contributed by SN, 7-Oct-2025.)
theorem cnn0opn:
  $ wcel (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $;

--| The ring of integers is a normed ring. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem zringnrg:
  $ wcel czring cnrg $;

--| Value of the distance function of the metric space of real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 16-May-2007.)
theorem remetdval (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs)) $;

--| The absolute value metric determines a metric space on the reals. (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-2007.)
theorem remet (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv cr cmet) $;

--| The absolute value metric is an extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem rexmet (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv cr cxmet) $;

--| A ball in terms of an open interval of reals. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem bl2ioo (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 (cfv D cbl)) (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 caddc) cioo)) $;

--| An open interval of reals in terms of a ball. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Aug-2015.)
theorem ioo2bl (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| An open interval of reals in terms of a ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem ioo2blex (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cioo) (crn (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| The balls of the standard real metric space are included in the open real intervals. (Contributed by NM, 8-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem blssioo (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wss (crn (cfv D cbl)) (crn cioo) $;

--| The topology generated by open intervals of reals is the same as the open sets of the standard metric space on the reals. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Nov-2013.)
theorem tgioo (D J: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) J $;

--| ` QQ ` is dense in ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2007.)
theorem qdensere2 (D J: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cq (cfv J ccl)) cr $;

--| An open ball in the complex numbers is a convex set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem blcvx (A2 B2 P R S T: class):
  $ wceq S (co P R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel P cc) (wcel R cxr)) (w3a (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wcel (co (co T A2 cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) B2 cmul) caddc) S) $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is Hausdorff. (Contributed by NM, 8-Mar-2007.)
theorem rehaus:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cha $;

--| The topology generated by open intervals of reals with rational endpoints is the same as the open sets of the standard metric space on the reals. In particular, this proves that the standard topology on the reals is second-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem tgqioo (Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (cfv (cima cioo (cxp cq cq)) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) Q $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is second-countable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Mar-2015.)
theorem re2ndc:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) c2ndc $;

--| The subspace topology induced by a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem resubmet (A2 R J: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp A2 A2)) cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq J (co R A2 crest)) $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is a subspace of the complex metric topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem tgioo2 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co J cr crest) $;

--| The subspace topology induced by a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem rerest (A2 R J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq (co J A2 crest) (co R A2 crest)) $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is a subspace of the complex metric topology. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem tgioo4:
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) cr crest) $;

--| The standard topology on the reals is a subspace of the complex metric topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem tgioo3 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv crefld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) J $;

--| The topology on the extended reals coincides with the standard topology on the reals, when restricted to ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrtgioo (J: class):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) J $;

--| The subspace topology induced by a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrrest (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv cle cordt) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq (co X A2 crest) (co R A2 crest)) $;

--| The subspace topology induced by a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrrest2 (A2 J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv cle cordt) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq (co J A2 crest) (co X A2 crest)) $;

--| The metric on the extended reals is a proper extended metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrsxmet (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv cxr cxmet) $;

--| The metric on the extended reals coincides with the usual metric on the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrsdsre (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq (cres D (cxp cr cr)) (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $;

--| Any ball of the metric of the extended reals centered on an element of ` RR ` is entirely contained in ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrsblre (D P R: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cr) (wcel R cxr)) (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) cr) $;

--| The metric on the extended reals generates a topology, but this does not match the order topology on ` RR* ` ; for example ` { +oo } ` is open in the metric topology, but not the order topology. However, the metric topology is finer than the order topology, meaning that all open intervals are open in the metric topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrsmopn (D J: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wss (cfv cle cordt) J $;

--| The integers are a closed set in the topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem zcld (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wcel cz (cfv J ccld) $;

--| The real numbers are a closed set in the topology on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem recld2 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel cr (cfv J ccld) $;

--| The integers are a closed set in the topology on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem zcld2 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel cz (cfv J ccld) $;

--| The integers are a discrete set in the topology on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2015.)
theorem zdis (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq (co J cz crest) (cpw cz) $;

--| Every subset of the integers are closed in the topology on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem sszcld (A2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cz) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A subset of the real numbers that is closed under addition with real numbers is perfect. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem reperflem {v u: setvar} (S: class) (J: class v):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv u) S) (wcel (cv v) cr)) (wcel (co (cv u) (cv v) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wss S cc $ >
  $ wcel (co J S crest) cperf $;

--| The real numbers are a perfect subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem reperf (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel (co J cr crest) cperf $;

--| The complex numbers are a perfect space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnperf (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J cperf $;

--| The interior of a closed interval in the standard topology on ` RR ` is the corresponding open interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem iccntr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cnt)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ icccmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem icccmplem1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (A2 B2: class) (D: class y1 z) (S: class x3 z) (T: class y1) (U: class) (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wrex z (wss (co A2 (cv x3) cicc) (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 S) (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ icccmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2014.)
theorem icccmplem2 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 z) (D: class z) (R S: class x3 z) (T U: class) (G: class x3 z) (J: class x3) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wrex z (wss (co A2 (cv x3) cicc) (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co G C (cfv D cbl)) V) $ >
  $ wceq G (csup S cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq R (cif (wbr (co G (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) B2 cle) (co G (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ icccmp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2014.)
theorem icccmplem3 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (A2 B2: class) (D: class z) (S: class x3 z) (T U: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wrex z (wss (co A2 (cv x3) cicc) (cuni (cv z))) (cin (cpw U) cfn)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $;

--| A closed interval in ` RR ` is compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2014.)
theorem icccmp (A2 B2 T J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel T ccmp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ reconn . Connectedness in the reals-easy direction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem reconnlem1 (A2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) A2 crest) cconn)) (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y A2))) (wss (co X Y cicc) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ reconn . (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Aug-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem reconnlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (S U V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc) A2) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cin U A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cin V A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cin U V) (cdif cr A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (cin U (co B2 C cicc)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wss A2 (cun U V))) $;

--| A subset of the reals is connected iff it has the interval property. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 15-Jul-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem reconn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wb (wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) A2 crest) cconn) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc) A2) A2) A2)) $;

--| Corollary of ~ reconn . The set of real numbers is connected. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Aug-2009.)
theorem retopconn:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cconn $;

--| A closed interval is connected. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Aug-2009.)
theorem iccconn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) cconn) $;

--| Every nonempty open set is uncountable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem opnreen (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) (wne A2 c0)) (wbr A2 (cpw cn) cen) $;

--| A countable subset of the reals has empty interior. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem rectbntr0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wbr A2 cn cdom)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cnt)) c0) $;

--| A finite sum in the nonnegative extended reals is monotonic in the support. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrge0gsumle (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cin (cpw A2) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co G (cres F C) cgsu) (co G (cres F B2) cgsu) cle) $;

--| Any finite or infinite sum in the nonnegative extended reals is uniquely convergent to the supremum of all finite sums. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Jul-2019.)
theorem xrge0tsms {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (S: class s1) (F G: class) (V: class s1):
  $ wceq G (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (crn (cmpt s1 (cin (cpw A2) cfn) (co G (cres F (cv s1)) cgsu))) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F ctsu) (csn S)) $;

--| Any finite or infinite sum in the nonnegative extended reals is convergent. This is a rather unique property of the set ` [ 0 , +oo ] ` ; a similar theorem is not true for ` RR* ` or ` RR ` or ` [ 0 , +oo ) ` . It is true for ` NN0 u. { +oo } ` , however, or more generally any additive submonoid of ` [ 0 , +oo ) ` with ` +oo ` adjoined. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrge0tsms2 (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wceq G (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F)) (wbr (co G F ctsu) c1o cen) $;

--| The metric function of a metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdcnlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R J K X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 Y D) (co R c2 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 Z D) (co R c2 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 B2 D) (co Y Z D) C) R clt) $;

--| The metric function of an extended metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. In this variation of ~ xmetdcn we use the metric topology instead of the order topology on ` RR* ` , which makes the theorem a bit stronger. Since ` +oo ` is an isolated point in the metric topology, this is saying that for any points ` A , B ` which are an infinite distance apart, there is a product neighborhood around ` <. A , B >. ` such that ` d ( a , b ) = +oo ` for any ` a ` near ` A ` and ` b ` near ` B ` , i.e., the distance function is locally constant ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xmetdcn2 (C D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| The metric function of an extended metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem xmetdcn (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv cle cordt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel D (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| The metric function of a metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdcn2 (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel D (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| The metric function of a metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdcn (D J K X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel D (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| The metric function of a metric space is always continuous in the topology generated by it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem msdcn (D J K M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv M cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv M ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cms) (wcel (cres D (cxp X X)) (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of the metric function; analogue of ~ cnmpt12f which cannot be used directly because ` D ` is not necessarily a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1ds {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (D R G J K X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 D)) (co K R ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of the metric function; analogue of ~ cnmpt22f which cannot be used directly because ` D ` is not necessarily a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2ds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (D R G J: class) (K: class y1) (L: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 D)) (co (co K L ctx) R ccn)) $;

--| The norm of a normed group is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmcn (G J K N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel N (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The norm of a normed group is a continuous function to ` CC ` . (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-2007.) (Revised by AV, 17-Oct-2021.)
theorem ngnmcncn (G J K N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cngp) (wcel N (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The absolute value function on complex numbers is continuous. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2014.)
theorem abscn (J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wcel cabs (co J K ccn) $;

--| Value of the "distance to a set" function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem metdsval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 X) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co A2 (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $;

--| The distance from a point to a set is a nonnegative extended real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem metdsf {x3 y1: setvar} (D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wf X (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $;

--| The distance from the point ` A ` to the set ` S ` is greater than ` R ` iff the ` R ` -ball around ` A ` misses ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem metdsge {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel R cxr)) (wb (wbr R (cfv A2 F) cle) (wceq (cin S (co A2 R (cfv D cbl))) c0)) $;

--| If a point is in a set, its distance to the set is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metds0 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X) (wcel A2 S)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $;

--| A generalization of the triangle inequality to the point-set distance function. Under the usual notation where the same symbol ` d ` denotes the point-point and point-set distance functions, this theorem would be written ` d ( a , S ) <_ d ( a , b ) + d ( b , S ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdstri {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (cfv A2 F) (co (co A2 B2 D) (cfv B2 F) cxad) cle) $;

--| The distance from a point to a set is bounded by the distance to any member of the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdsle {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 X))) (wbr (cfv B2 F) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| The distance from a point to a nonempty set in a proper metric space is a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdsre {x3 y1: setvar} (D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wss S X) (wne S c0)) (wf X cr F) $;

--| The distance from a point to a set is zero iff the point is in the closure set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem metdseq0 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X) (wcel A2 X)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) (wcel A2 (cfv S (cfv J ccl)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metdscn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdscnlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (K: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 D) R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv A2 F) (cxne (cfv B2 F)) cxad) R clt) $;

--| The function ` F ` which gives the distance from a point to a set is a continuous function into the metric topology of the extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdscn {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (K: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv cxrs cds) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The function ` F ` which gives the distance from a point to a nonempty set in a metric space is a continuous function into the topology of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem metdscn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (D S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (K: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wss S X) (wne S c0)) (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metnrm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metnrmlem1a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 D S T: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 T)) (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 F) clt) (wcel (cif (wbr c1 (cfv A2 F) cle) c1 (cfv A2 F)) crp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metnrm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Sep-2015.)
theorem metnrmlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 D S T: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 T))) (wbr (cif (wbr c1 (cfv B2 F) cle) c1 (cfv B2 F)) (co A2 B2 D) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metnrm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem metnrmlem2 {x3 y1 t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (D S T: class) (U: class x3 y1 t) (F: class x3 y1) (J: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) c0) $ >
  $ wceq U (ciun t T (co (cv t) (co (cif (wbr c1 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) c1 (cfv (cv t) F)) c2 cdiv) (cfv D cbl))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U J) (wss T U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ metnrm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem metnrmlem3 {x3 y1 z w t s1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (D S T: class) (U: class x3 y1 z t) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class x3 y1 z w) (J: class x3) (V: class x3 y1 t s1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S T) c0) $ >
  $ wceq U (ciun t T (co (cv t) (co (cif (wbr c1 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) c1 (cfv (cv t) F)) c2 cdiv) (cfv D cbl))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 X (cinf (crn (cmpt y1 T (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D))) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq V (ciun s1 S (co (cv s1) (co (cif (wbr c1 (cfv (cv s1) G) cle) c1 (cfv (cv s1) G)) c2 cdiv) (cfv D cbl))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wrex w (w3a (wss S (cv z)) (wss T (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) J) J) $;

--| A metric space is normal. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 31-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem metnrm (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J cnrm) $;

--| A metric space is regular. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem metreg (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel J creg) $;

--| Lemma for ~ addcn , ~ subcn , and ~ mulcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem addcnlem {y1 z v u a b c: setvar} (c_pl J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wf (cxp cc cc) cc c_pl $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv a) crp) (wcel (cv b) cc) (wcel (cv c) cc)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cv u) (cv b) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cv v) (cv c) cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) c_pl) (co (cv b) (cv c) c_pl) cmin) cabs) (cv a) clt)) cc) cc) crp) crp) $ >
  $ wcel c_pl (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Complex number addition is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem addcn (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel caddc (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Complex number subtraction is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem subcn (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel cmin (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Complex number multiplication is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-mulf . Use ~ mpomulcn instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mulcn (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel cmul (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Complex number multiplication is a continuous function. Version of ~ mulcn using maps-to notation, which does not require ~ ax-mulf . (Contributed by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem mpomulcn {x3 y1: setvar} (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul)) (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Complex number division is a continuous function, when the second argument is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem divcn (J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J (cdif cc (csn cc0)) crest) $ >
  $ wcel cdiv (co (co J K ctx) J ccn) $;

--| The complex numbers form a topological group under addition, with the standard topology induced by the absolute value metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2015.)
theorem cnfldtgp:
  $ wcel ccnfld ctgp $;

--| A finite sum of functions to complex numbers from a common topological space is continuous. The class expression for ` B ` normally contains free variables ` k ` and ` x ` to index it. (Contributed by NM, 8-Aug-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem fsumcn {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 k) (J K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (csu k A2 B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Version of ~ fsumcn for two-argument mappings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2014.)
theorem fsum2cn {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1 k) (J K: class) (L: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co J L ctx) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (csu k A2 B2)) (co (co J L ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| The power function on complex numbers, for fixed exponent ` N ` , is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem expcn {x3: setvar} (J N: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) N cexp)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| Division by a nonzero constant is a continuous operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem divccn {x3: setvar} (A2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 cdiv)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| The square function on complex numbers is continuous. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem sqcn {x3: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) c2 cexp)) (co J J ccn) $;

--| Extend class notation with the unit interval.
term cii: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the operation which returns a class of continuous complex functions.
term ccncf: class;

--| Define the unit interval with the Euclidean topology. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
axiom df_ii:
  $ wceq cii (cfv (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) cmopn) $;

--| Define the operation whose value is a class of continuous complex functions. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Oct-2007.)
axiom df_cncf {x3 y1 e f2 a b d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccncf (cmpo a b (cpw cc) (cpw cc) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wral e (wrex d (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (cv d) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) cmin) cabs) (cv e) clt)) (cv a)) crp) crp) (cv a)) (co (cv b) (cv a) cmap))) $;

--| The unit interval is a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem iitopon:
  $ wcel cii (cfv (co cc0 c1 cicc) ctopon) $;

--| The unit interval is a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iitop:
  $ wcel cii ctop $;

--| The base set of the unit interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jan-2014.)
theorem iiuni:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cuni cii) $;

--| Alternate definition of the unit interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem dfii2:
  $ wceq cii (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co cc0 c1 cicc) crest) $;

--| Alternate definition of the unit interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfii3 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq cii (co J (co cc0 c1 cicc) crest) $;

--| Alternate definition of the unit interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfii4 (I: class):
  $ wceq I (co ccnfld (co cc0 c1 cicc) cress) $ >
  $ wceq cii (cfv I ctopn) $;

--| The unit interval expressed as an order topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfii5:
  $ wceq cii (cfv (cin cle (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) cordt) $;

--| The unit interval is compact. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2014.)
theorem iicmp:
  $ wcel cii ccmp $;

--| The unit interval is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem iiconn:
  $ wcel cii cconn $;

--| The value of the continuous complex function operation is the set of continuous functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Nov-2013.)
theorem cncfval {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccncf) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv w) cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv w) (cv f2)) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt)) A2) crp) crp) A2) (co B2 A2 cmap))) $;

--| Membership in the set of continuous complex functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Nov-2013.)
theorem elcncf {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv w) cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv w) F) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt)) A2) crp) crp) A2))) $;

--| Version of ~ elcncf with arguments commuted. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2014.)
theorem elcncf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wb (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv w) (cv x3) cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt)) A2) crp) crp) A2))) $;

--| Reverse closure of the continuous function predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncfrss (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wss A2 cc) $;

--| Reverse closure of the continuous function predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncfrss2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wss B2 cc) $;

--| A continuous complex function's domain and codomain. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncff (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| Defining property of a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncfi {z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C R F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wcel C A2) (wcel R crp)) (wrex z (wral w (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv w) C cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) F) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) R clt)) A2) crp) $;

--| Membership in the set of continuous complex functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.)
theorem elcncf1di {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wcel Z crp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv w) A2)) (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv w) cmin) cabs) Z clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv w) F) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf))) $;

--| Membership in the set of continuous complex functions from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.)
theorem elcncf1ii {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wf A2 B2 F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wcel Z crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv w) A2)) (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv w) cmin) cabs) Z clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv w) F) cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous complex function restricted to a subset is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem rescncf (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wss C A2) (wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) (wcel (cres F C) (co C B2 ccncf))) $;

--| Change the codomain of a continuous complex function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem cncfcdm (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss C cc) (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf))) (wb (wcel F (co A2 C ccncf)) (wf A2 C F)) $;

--| The set of continuous functions is expanded when the codomain is expanded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncfss (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss B2 C) (wss C cc)) (wss (co A2 B2 ccncf) (co A2 C ccncf)) $;

--| Image of a limit under a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem climcncf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G D cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom F G) (cfv D F) cli) $;

--| Absolute value is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2014.)
theorem abscncf:
  $ wcel cabs (co cc cr ccncf) $;

--| Real part is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2014.)
theorem recncf:
  $ wcel cre (co cc cr ccncf) $;

--| Imaginary part is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2014.)
theorem imcncf:
  $ wcel cim (co cc cr ccncf) $;

--| Complex conjugate is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2014.)
theorem cjcncf:
  $ wcel ccj (co cc cc ccncf) $;

--| Multiplication by a constant is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 28-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.)
theorem mulc1cncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Division by a constant is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 28-Nov-2007.)
theorem divccncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| The composition of two continuous maps on complex numbers is also continuous. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncfco (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co B2 C ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom G F) (co A2 C ccncf)) $;

--| Composition of continuous functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cncfcompt2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class y1) (C: class) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (T: class x3) (E: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 R) (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt y1 C S) (co C E ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) R) (wceq S T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 T) (co A2 E ccncf)) $;

--| Relate complex function continuity to metric space continuity. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem cncfmet (A2 B2 C D J K: class):
  $ wceq C (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp A2 A2)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccncf) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Relate complex function continuity to topological continuity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem cncfcn (A2 B2 J K L: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J A2 crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (co J B2 crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccncf) (co K L ccn)) $;

--| Relate complex function continuity to topological continuity. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 28-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem cncfcn1 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq (co cc cc ccncf) (co J J ccn) $;

--| A constant function is a continuous function on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem cncfmptc {x3: setvar} (A2 S T: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 T) (wss S cc) (wss T cc)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S A2) (co S T ccncf)) $;

--| The identity function is a continuous function on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem cncfmptid {x3: setvar} (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S T) (wss T cc)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S (cv x3)) (co S T ccncf)) $;

--| Composition of continuous functions. ` -cn-> ` analogue of ~ cnmpt11f . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem cncfmpt1f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cfv A2 F)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Composition of continuous functions. ` -cn-> ` analogue of ~ cnmpt12f . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem cncfmpt2f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J J ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 F)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Composition of continuous functions in a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem cncfmpt2ss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel F (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X S ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X S ccncf)) $ >
  $ wss S cc $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (co A2 B2 F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 F)) (co X S ccncf)) $;

--| Adding a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem addccncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| The identity function is a continuous function on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Moved into main set.mm as ~ cncfmptid and may be deleted by mathbox owner, JM. --MC 12-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem idcncf {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wcel F (co cc cc ccncf) $;

--| Subtracting a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem sub1cncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Subtraction from a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem sub2cncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Division with a constant numerator is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem cdivcncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co A2 (cv x3) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (co (cdif cc (csn cc0)) cc ccncf)) $;

--| The negative function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem negcncf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cc) (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| The negative of a continuous complex function is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem negfcncf {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) (wcel G (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| Absolute value is continuous. Alternate proof of ~ abscncf . (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem abscncfALT:
  $ wcel cabs (co cc cr ccncf) $;

--| Rewrite ~ cmphaushmeo for functions on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem cncfcnvcn (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J X crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ccmp) (wcel F (co X Y ccncf))) (wb (wf1o X Y F) (wcel (ccnv F) (co Y X ccncf))) $;

--| The power function on complex numbers, for fixed exponent N, is continuous. Similar to ~ expcn . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem expcncf {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) N cexp)) (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iirevcn and related functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnmptre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (R F J K: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) A2 crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) B2 crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 cc F) (co R R ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 F) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Piecewise definition of a continuous function on a real interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.)
theorem cnmpopc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D R E J: class x3 y1) (K: class) (M N O: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq M (co R (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq N (co R (co B2 C cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq O (co R (co A2 C cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co A2 C cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wceq D E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 (co A2 B2 cicc) X D) (co (co M J ctx) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 (co B2 C cicc) X E) (co (co N J ctx) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 (co A2 C cicc) X (cif (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle) D E)) (co (co O J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| Reverse the unit interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iirev (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (co c1 X cmin) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| The reversion function is a continuous map of the unit interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.)
theorem iirevcn {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin)) (co cii cii ccn) $;

--| Map the first half of ` II ` into ` II ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iihalf1 (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (co cc0 (co c1 c2 cdiv) cicc)) (wcel (co c2 X cmul) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| The first half function is a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem iihalf1cn {x3: setvar} (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co cc0 (co c1 c2 cdiv) cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (co c1 c2 cdiv) cicc) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) (co J cii ccn) $;

--| Map the second half of ` II ` into ` II ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iihalf2 (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) c1 cicc)) (wcel (co (co c2 X cmul) c1 cmin) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| The second half function is a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jun-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem iihalf2cn {x3: setvar} (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) c1 cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) c1 cicc) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 cmin)) (co J cii ccn) $;

--| Divide the unit interval into two pieces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem elii1 (X: class):
  $ wb (wcel X (co cc0 (co c1 c2 cdiv) cicc)) (wa (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wbr X (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Divide the unit interval into two pieces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem elii2 (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wn (wbr X (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle))) (wcel X (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) c1 cicc)) $;

--| The unit interval is closed under multiplication. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iimulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| Multiplication is a continuous function on the unit interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem iimulcn {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul)) (co (co cii cii ctx) cii ccn) $;

--| A half-open interval starting at ` A ` is open in the closed interval from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem icoopnst (A2 B2 C J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp (co A2 B2 cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc))) cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) (wcel (co A2 C cico) J)) $;

--| A half-open interval ending at ` B ` is open in the closed interval from ` A ` to ` B ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Dec-2013.)
theorem iocopnst (A2 B2 C J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp (co A2 B2 cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc))) cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) (wcel (co C B2 cioc) J)) $;

--| The natural bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` to an arbitrary nontrivial closed interval ` [ A , B ] ` is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem icchmeo {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmul) (co (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) A2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel F (co cii (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) chmeo)) $;

--| Define a bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ) ` to ` [ 0 , +oo ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem icopnfcnv {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cico) (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wa (wf1o (co cc0 c1 cico) (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 (co cc0 cpnf cico) (co (cv y1) (co c1 (cv y1) caddc) cdiv))) $;

--| The defined bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ) ` to ` [ 0 , +oo ) ` is an order isomorphism and a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem icopnfhmeo {x3: setvar} (F: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cico) (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wa (wiso (co cc0 c1 cico) (co cc0 cpnf cico) clt clt F) (wcel F (co (co J (co cc0 c1 cico) crest) (co J (co cc0 cpnf cico) crest) chmeo)) $;

--| Define a bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` to ` [ 0 , +oo ] ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem iccpnfcnv {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c1) cpnf (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wa (wf1o (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cpnf) c1 (co (cv y1) (co c1 (cv y1) caddc) cdiv)))) $;

--| The defined bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` to ` [ 0 , +oo ] ` is an order isomorphism and a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem iccpnfhmeo {x3: setvar} (F K: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c1) cpnf (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wa (wiso (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) clt clt F) (wcel F (co cii K chmeo)) $;

--| The bijection from ` [ -u 1 , 1 ] ` to the extended reals is an order isomorphism and a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrhmeo {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3) (G: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c1) cpnf (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv y1) cle) (cfv (cv y1) F) (cxne (cfv (cneg (cv y1)) F)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wa (wiso (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc) cxr clt clt G) (wcel G (co (co J (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc) crest) (cfv cle cordt) chmeo)) $;

--| The extended reals are homeomorphic to the interval ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrhmph:
  $ wbr cii (cfv cle cordt) chmph $;

--| The topology of the extended reals is compact. Since the topology of the extended reals extends the topology of the reals (by ~ xrtgioo ), this means that ` RR* ` is a compactification of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrcmp:
  $ wcel (cfv cle cordt) ccmp $;

--| The topology of the extended reals is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrconn:
  $ wcel (cfv cle cordt) cconn $;

--| A linear combination of two reals lies in the interval between them. Equivalently, a closed interval is a convex set. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem icccvx (A2 B2 C D T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wi (w3a (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel D (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (co (co c1 T cmin) C cmul) (co T D cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| Restriction to the first part of a piecewise defined function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem oprpiece1res1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (R S F G: class x3 y1) (K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 cle $ >
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wcel K (co A2 B2 cicc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (co A2 B2 cicc) C (cif (wbr (cv x3) K cle) R S)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 (co A2 K cicc) C R) $ >
  $ wceq (cres F (cxp (co A2 K cicc) C)) G $;

--| Restriction to the second part of a piecewise defined function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.)
theorem oprpiece1res2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P Q: class y1) (R S F G: class x3 y1) (K: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 cle $ >
  $ wcel R cvv $ >
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wcel K (co A2 B2 cicc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (co A2 B2 cicc) C (cif (wbr (cv x3) K cle) R S)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) K) (wceq R P) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) K) (wceq S Q) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) C) (wceq P Q) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 (co K B2 cicc) C S) $ >
  $ wceq (cres F (cxp (co K B2 cicc) C)) G $;

--| The canonical bijection from ` ( RR X. RR ) ` to ` CC ` described in ~ cnref1o is in fact a homeomorphism of the usual topologies on these sets. (It is also an isometry, if ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is metrized with the l<SUP>2</SUP> norm.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem cnrehmeo {x3 y1: setvar} (F J: class x3 y1) (K: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel F (co (co J J ctx) K chmeo) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnheibor . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem cnheiborlem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R: class x3 y1) (T: class) (F: class x3 y1) (J X: class) (Y: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J X crest) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cima F (cxp (co (cneg R) R cicc) (co (cneg R) R cicc))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cfv J ccld)) (wa (wcel R cr) (wral z (wbr (cfv (cv z) cabs) R cle) X))) (wcel T ccmp) $;

--| Heine-Borel theorem for complex numbers. A subset of ` CC ` is compact iff it is closed and bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem cnheibor {x3 r: setvar} (T J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J X crest) $ >
  $ wi (wss X cc) (wb (wcel T ccmp) (wa (wcel X (cfv J ccld)) (wrex r (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) (cv r) cle) X) cr))) $;

--| The topology on the complex numbers is locally compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnllycmp (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J (cnlly ccmp) $;

--| The topology on the reals is locally compact. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem rellycmp:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (cnlly ccmp) $;

--| The Boundedness Theorem. A continuous function from a compact topological space to the reals is bounded (above). (Boundedness below is obtained by applying this theorem to ` -u F ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem bndth {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J: class) (K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cv x3) cle) X) cr) $;

--| The Extreme Value Theorem. A continuous function from a nonempty compact topological space to the reals attains its maximum at some point in the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem evth {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J: class) (K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) X) X) $;

--| The Extreme Value Theorem, minimum version. A continuous function from a nonempty compact topological space to the reals attains its minimum at some point in the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem evth2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J: class) (K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle) X) X) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lebnum . The function ` F ` measures the sum of all of the distances to escape the sets of the cover. Since by assumption it is a cover, there is at least one set which covers a given point, and since it is open, the point is a positive distance from the edge of the set. Thus, the sum is a strictly positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem lebnumlem1 {y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D U: class) (F: class y1 z k) (J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel X U)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 X (csu k U (cinf (crn (cmpt z (cdif X (cv k)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) D))) cxr clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X crp F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lebnum . As a finite sum of point-to-set distance functions, which are continuous by ~ metdscn , the function ` F ` is also continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem lebnumlem2 {y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D U: class) (F: class y1 z k) (J: class) (K: class y1 z k) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel X U)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 X (csu k U (cinf (crn (cmpt z (cdif X (cv k)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) D))) cxr clt))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lebnum . By the previous lemmas, ` F ` is continuous and positive on a compact set, so it has a positive minimum ` r ` . Then setting ` d = r / # ( U ) ` , since for each ` u e. U ` we have ` ball ( x , d ) C_ u ` iff ` d <_ d ( x , X \ u ) ` , if ` -. ball ( x , d ) C_ u ` for all ` u ` then summing over ` u ` yields ` sum_ u e. U d ( x , X \ u ) = F ( x ) < sum_ u e. U d = r ` , in contradiction to the assumption that ` r ` is the minimum of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem lebnumlem3 {x3 y1 z u k d: setvar} (ph: wff u) (D U: class) (F: class y1 z u k d) (J: class u) (K: class y1 z u k d) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel X U)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 X (csu k U (cinf (crn (cmpt z (cdif X (cv k)) (co (cv y1) (cv z) D))) cxr clt))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral x3 (wrex u (wss (co (cv x3) (cv d) (cfv D cbl)) (cv u)) U) X) crp) $;

--| The Lebesgue number lemma, or Lebesgue covering lemma. If ` X ` is a compact metric space and ` U ` is an open cover of ` X ` , then there exists a positive real number ` d ` such that every ball of size ` d ` (and every subset of a ball of size ` d ` , including every subset of diameter less than ` d ` ) is a subset of some member of the cover. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Sep-2020.)
theorem lebnum {x3 u d: setvar} (ph: wff u) (D U: class) (J: class u) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral x3 (wrex u (wss (co (cv x3) (cv d) (cfv D cbl)) (cv u)) U) X) crp) $;

--| Generalize ~ lebnum to extended metrics. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem xlebnum {x3 u d: setvar} (ph: wff d) (D U: class) (J: class x3 u d) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral x3 (wrex u (wss (co (cv x3) (cv d) (cfv D cbl)) (cv u)) U) X) crp) $;

--| Specialize the Lebesgue number lemma ~ lebnum to the closed unit interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem lebnumii {u k n: setvar} (U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss U cii) (wceq (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cuni U))) (wrex n (wral k (wrex u (wss (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) (cv n) cdiv) (co (cv k) (cv n) cdiv) cicc) (cv u)) U) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) cn) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of homotopies between two continuous functions.
term chtpy: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of path homotopies between two continuous functions.
term cphtpy: class;

--| Extend class notation with the path homotopy relation.
term cphtpc: class;

--| Define the function which takes topological spaces ` X , Y ` and two continuous functions ` F , G : X --> Y ` and returns the class of homotopies from ` F ` to ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_htpy {x3 y1 f2 g2 h s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq chtpy (cmpo x3 y1 ctop ctop (cmpo f2 g2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) ccn) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) ccn) (crab h (wral s1 (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) cc0 (cv h)) (cfv (cv s1) (cv f2))) (wceq (co (cv s1) c1 (cv h)) (cfv (cv s1) (cv g2)))) (cuni (cv x3))) (co (co (cv x3) cii ctx) (cv y1) ccn)))) $;

--| Define the class of path homotopies between two paths ` F , G : II --> X ` ; these are homotopies (in the sense of ~ df-htpy ) which also preserve both endpoints of the paths throughout the homotopy. Definition of [Hatcher] p. 25. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
axiom df_phtpy {x3 f2 g2 h s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cphtpy (cmpt x3 ctop (cmpo f2 g2 (co cii (cv x3) ccn) (co cii (cv x3) ccn) (crab h (wral s1 (wa (wceq (co cc0 (cv s1) (cv h)) (cfv cc0 (cv f2))) (wceq (co c1 (cv s1) (cv h)) (cfv c1 (cv f2)))) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (co cii (cv x3) chtpy))))) $;

--| Membership in the class of homotopies between two continuous functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem ishtpy {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H J: class) (K: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel H (co F G (co J K chtpy))) (wa (wcel H (co (co J cii ctx) K ccn)) (wral s1 (wa (wceq (co (cv s1) cc0 H) (cfv (cv s1) F)) (wceq (co (cv s1) c1 H) (cfv (cv s1) G))) X))) $;

--| A homotopy is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem htpycn (ph: wff) (F G J K X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F G (co J K chtpy)) (co (co J cii ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| A homotopy evaluated at its endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem htpyi (ph: wff) (A2 F G H J K X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co J K chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 X)) (wa (wceq (co A2 cc0 H) (cfv A2 F)) (wceq (co A2 c1 H) (cfv A2 G))) $;

--| Deduction for membership in the class of homotopies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem ishtpyd {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H J: class) (K: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co (co J cii ctx) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) X)) (wceq (co (cv s1) cc0 H) (cfv (cv s1) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) X)) (wceq (co (cv s1) c1 H) (cfv (cv s1) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co J K chtpy))) $;

--| Given a homotopy from ` F ` to ` G ` , produce a homotopy from ` G ` to ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem htpycom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class x3 y1) (H J K: class) (M: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpo x3 y1 X (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv y1) cmin) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co J K chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co G F (co J K chtpy))) $;

--| A homotopy from a function to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem htpyid {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (G: class x3 y1) (J K X: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 X (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co F F (co J K chtpy))) $;

--| Compose a homotopy with a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem htpyco1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (F G: class x3 y1) (H J K L: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpo x3 y1 X (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (cfv (cv x3) P) (cv y1) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co K L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co K L chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co (ccom F P) (ccom G P) (co J L chtpy))) $;

--| Compose a homotopy with a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem htpyco2 (ph: wff) (P F G H J K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co K L ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co J K chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom P H) (co (ccom P F) (ccom P G) (co J L chtpy))) $;

--| Concatenate two homotopies. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem htpycc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H: class x3 y1) (J K L M: class) (N: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq N (cmpo x3 y1 X (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv y1) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) L) (co (cv x3) (co (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) c1 cmin) M))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F G (co J K chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co G H (co J K chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co F H (co J K chtpy))) $;

--| Membership in the class of path homotopies between two continuous functions. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem isphtpy {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) (wa (wcel H (co F G (co cii J chtpy))) (wral s1 (wa (wceq (co cc0 (cv s1) H) (cfv cc0 F)) (wceq (co c1 (cv s1) H) (cfv c1 F))) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) $;

--| A path homotopy is a homotopy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpyhtpy (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F G (cfv J cphtpy)) (co F G (co cii J chtpy))) $;

--| A path homotopy is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpycn (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F G (cfv J cphtpy)) (co (co cii cii ctx) J ccn)) $;

--| Membership in the class of path homotopies between two continuous functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpyi (ph: wff) (A2 F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wa (wceq (co cc0 A2 H) (cfv cc0 F)) (wceq (co c1 A2 H) (cfv c1 F))) $;

--| Two path-homotopic paths have the same start and end point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpy01 (ph: wff) (F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G)) (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv c1 G))) $;

--| Deduction for membership in the class of path homotopies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem isphtpyd {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (co cii J chtpy))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co cc0 (cv s1) H) (cfv cc0 F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co c1 (cv s1) H) (cfv c1 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Deduction for membership in the class of path homotopies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem isphtpy2d {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co (co cii cii ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co (cv s1) cc0 H) (cfv (cv s1) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co (cv s1) c1 H) (cfv (cv s1) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co cc0 (cv s1) H) (cfv cc0 F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (co c1 (cv s1) H) (cfv c1 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Given a homotopy from ` F ` to ` G ` , produce a homotopy from ` G ` to ` F ` . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpycom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class x3 y1) (H J: class) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (cv x3) (co c1 (cv y1) cmin) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co G F (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| A homotopy from a path to itself. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpyid {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (G: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co F F (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Compose a path homotopy with a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem phtpyco2 (ph: wff) (P F G H J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom P H) (co (ccom P F) (ccom P G) (cfv K cphtpy))) $;

--| Concatenate two path homotopies. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem phtpycc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H: class x3 y1) (J K L: class) (M: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq M (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv y1) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) K) (co (cv x3) (co (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) c1 cmin) L))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co F G (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co G H (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co F H (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Define the function which takes a topology and returns the path homotopy relation on that topology. Definition of [Hatcher] p. 25. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_phtpc {x3 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cphtpc (cmpt x3 ctop (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) (co cii (cv x3) ccn)) (wne (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cfv (cv x3) cphtpy)) c0)))) $;

--| The path homotopy relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.)
theorem phtpcrel (J: class):
  $ wrel (cfv J cphtpc) $;

--| The relation "is path homotopic to". (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem isphtpc (F G J: class):
  $ wb (wbr F G (cfv J cphtpc)) (w3a (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) (wne (co F G (cfv J cphtpy)) c0)) $;

--| Path homotopy is an equivalence relation. Proposition 1.2 of [Hatcher] p. 26. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem phtpcer (J: class):
  $ wer (co cii J ccn) (cfv J cphtpc) $;

--| Path homotopic paths have the same endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem phtpc01 (F G J: class):
  $ wi (wbr F G (cfv J cphtpc)) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G)) (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv c1 G))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ reparpht . (Contributed by NM, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem reparphti {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (H: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii cii ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 G) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co (co (co c1 (cv y1) cmin) (cfv (cv x3) G) cmul) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmul) caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co (ccom F G) F (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Reparametrization lemma. The reparametrization of a path by any continuous map ` G : II --> II ` with ` G ( 0 ) = 0 ` and ` G ( 1 ) = 1 ` is path-homotopic to the original path. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem reparpht (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii cii ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 G) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom F G) F (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| Compose a path homotopy with a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.)
theorem phtpcco2 (ph: wff) (P F G J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv J cphtpc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom P F) (ccom P G) (cfv K cphtpc)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the concatenation operation for paths in a topological space.
term cpco: class;

--| Extend class notation with the loop space.
term comi: class;

--| Extend class notation with the higher loop spaces.
term comn: class;

--| Extend class notation with the fundamental group.
term cpi1: class;

--| Extend class notation with the higher homotopy groups.
term cpin: class;

--| Define the concatenation of two paths in a topological space ` J ` . For simplicity of definition, we define it on all paths, not just those whose endpoints line up. Definition of [Hatcher] p. 26. Hatcher denotes path concatenation with a square dot; other authors, such as Munkres, use a star. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
axiom df_pco {x3 f2 g2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpco (cmpt j ctop (cmpo f2 g2 (co cii (cv j) ccn) (co cii (cv j) ccn) (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (cfv (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) (cv f2)) (cfv (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 cmin) (cv g2)))))) $;

--| Define the loop space of a topological space, with a magma structure on it given by concatenation of loops. This structure is not a group, but the operation is compatible with homotopy, which allows the homotopy groups to be defined based on this operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
axiom df_om1 {y1 f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq comi (cmpo j y1 ctop (cuni (cv j)) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv y1))) (co cii (cv j) ccn))) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cfv (cv j) cpco)) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (co (cv j) cii cxko)))) $;

--| Define the n-th iterated loop space of a topological space. Unlike ` Om1 ` this is actually a _pointed_ topological space, which is to say a tuple of a topological space (a member of ` TopSp ` , not ` Top ` ) and a point in the space. Higher loop spaces select the constant loop at the point from the lower loop space for the distinguished point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
axiom df_omn {x3 y1 j p: setvar}:
  $ wceq comn (cmpo j y1 ctop (cuni (cv j)) (cseq (ccom (cmpo x3 p cvv cvv (cop (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) ctopn) (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) comi) (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv (cv x3) c2nd))))) c1st) (cop (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cuni (cv j))) (cop (cfv cnx cts) (cv j))) (cv y1)) cc0)) $;

--| Define the fundamental group, whose operation is given by concatenation of homotopy classes of loops. Definition of [Hatcher] p. 26. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_pi1 {y1 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpi1 (cmpo j y1 ctop (cuni (cv j)) (co (co (cv j) (cv y1) comi) (cfv (cv j) cphtpc) cqus)) $;

--| Define the n-th homotopy group, which is formed by taking the ` n ` -th loop space and forming the quotient under the relation of path homotopy equivalence in the base space of the ` n ` -th loop space, which is the ` n - 1 ` -th loop space. For ` n = 0 ` , since this is not well-defined we replace this relation with the path-connectedness relation, so that the ` 0 ` -th homotopy group is the set of path components of ` X ` . (Since the ` 0 ` -th loop space does not have a group operation, neither does the ` 0 ` -th homotopy group, but the rest are genuine groups.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_pin {x3 y1 f2 j n p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpin (cmpo j p ctop (cuni (cv j)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) (co (cv j) (cv p) comn)) c1st) (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) (copab x3 y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv y1))) (co cii (cv j) ccn))) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) (co (cv j) (cv p) comn)) c1st) ctopn) cphtpc)) cqus))) $;

--| The value of the path concatenation function on a topological space. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Mar-2024.)
theorem pcofval {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wceq (cfv J cpco) (cmpo f2 g2 (co cii J ccn) (co cii J ccn) (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (cfv (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) (cv f2)) (cfv (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 cmin) (cv g2))))) $;

--| The concatenation of two paths. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem pcoval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cfv J cpco)) (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (cfv (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) F) (cfv (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 cmin) G)))) $;

--| Evaluate the concatenation of two paths. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcovalg (ph: wff) (F G J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (cif (wbr X (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (cfv (co c2 X cmul) F) (cfv (co (co c2 X cmul) c1 cmin) G))) $;

--| Evaluate the concatenation of two paths on the first half. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcoval1 (ph: wff) (F G J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co cc0 (co c1 c2 cdiv) cicc))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (cfv (co c2 X cmul) F)) $;

--| The starting point of a path concatenation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
theorem pco0 (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (cfv cc0 F)) $;

--| The ending point of a path concatenation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.)
theorem pco1 (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (cfv c1 G)) $;

--| Evaluate the concatenation of two paths on the second half. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcoval2 (ph: wff) (F G J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) c1 cicc))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (cfv (co (co c2 X cmul) c1 cmin) G)) $;

--| The concatenation of two paths is a path. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcocn (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cfv J cpco)) (co cii J ccn)) $;

--| The composition of a concatenation of paths with a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem copco (ph: wff) (F G H J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom H (co F G (cfv J cpco))) (co (ccom H F) (ccom H G) (cfv K cpco))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pcohtpy . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcohtpylem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class x3 y1) (F G H J K M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F H (cfv J cphtpc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G K (cfv J cphtpc)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) (cv y1) M) (co (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 cmin) (cv y1) N))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co F H (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G K (cfv J cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co (co F G (cfv J cpco)) (co H K (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpy))) $;

--| Homotopy invariance of path concatenation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 15-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcohtpy (ph: wff) (F G H J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F H (cfv J cphtpc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G K (cfv J cphtpc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co F G (cfv J cpco)) (co H K (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| A constant function is a path from ` Y ` to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem pcoptcl (P J X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel Y X)) (w3a (wcel P (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 P) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 P) Y)) $;

--| Concatenation with a point does not affect homotopy class. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem pcopt (P F J Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 F) Y)) (wbr (co P F (cfv J cpco)) F (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| Concatenation with a point does not affect homotopy class. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcopt2 (P F J Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv c1 F) Y)) (wbr (co F P (cfv J cpco)) F (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| Order of concatenation does not affect homotopy class. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcoass {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class x3) (F G H J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 G) (cfv cc0 H)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (cif (wbr (cv x3) (co c1 c4 cdiv) cle) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) (co (cv x3) (co c1 c4 cdiv) caddc)) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co F G (cfv J cpco)) H (cfv J cpco)) (co F (co G H (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| Closure for a reversed path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcorevcl {x3: setvar} (F: class) (G: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (w3a (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 G) (cfv c1 F)) (wceq (cfv c1 G) (cfv cc0 F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pcorev . Prove continuity of the homotopy function. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2014.)
theorem pcorevlem {x3 t s1: setvar} (P F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 t s1) (J: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv c1 F))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpo s1 t (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (cif (wbr (cv s1) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) (co c1 (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (co c2 (cv s1) cmul) cmul) cmin) (co c1 (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv s1) cmul) c1 cmin) cmin) cmul) cmin)) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wa (wcel H (co (co G F (cfv J cpco)) P (cfv J cphtpy))) (wbr (co G F (cfv J cpco)) P (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Concatenation with the reverse path. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem pcorev {x3: setvar} (P F: class) (G: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv c1 F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wbr (co G F (cfv J cpco)) P (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| Concatenation with the reverse path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcorev2 {x3: setvar} (P: class x3) (F: class) (G: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wbr (co F G (cfv J cpco)) P (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| The path homotopy equivalence relation on two paths ` F , G ` with the same start and end point can be written in terms of the loop ` F - G ` formed by concatenating ` F ` with the inverse of ` G ` . Thus, all the homotopy information in ` ~=ph `` J ` is available if we restrict our attention to closed loops, as in the definition of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pcophtb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (P F: class x3) (G: class) (H: class x3) (J: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) G)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv c1 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co F H (cfv J cpco)) P (cfv J cphtpc)) (wbr F G (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| The definition of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem om1val {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl: class f2) (J: class) (K O X: class f2) (Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) Y)) (co cii J ccn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_pl (cfv J cpco)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co J cii cxko)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq O (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) c_pl) (cop (cfv cnx cts) K))) $;

--| The base set of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem om1bas {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2) (J: class) (O: class f2) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) Y)) (co cii J ccn))) $;

--| Elementhood in the base set of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem om1elbas (ph: wff) (B2 F J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F B2) (w3a (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 F) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 F) Y))) $;

--| Closure of the group operation of the loop space. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem om1addcl (ph: wff) (B2 H J K O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co H K (cfv J cpco)) B2) $;

--| The group operation (which isn't much more than a magma) of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem om1plusg (ph: wff) (J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J cpco) (cfv O cplusg)) $;

--| The topology of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem om1tset (ph: wff) (J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J cii cxko) (cfv O cts)) $;

--| The topology of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem om1opn (ph: wff) (B2 J K O X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv O ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (co (co J cii cxko) B2 crest)) $;

--| The definition of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1val (ph: wff) (G J O X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (co O (cfv J cphtpc) cqus)) $;

--| The base set of the fundamental group of a topological space at a given base point. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1bas (ph: wff) (B2 G J K O X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cqs K (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pi1buni . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1blem (ph: wff) (B2 G J K O X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss (cima (cfv J cphtpc) K) K) (wss K (co cii J ccn))) $;

--| Another way to write the loop space base in terms of the base of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1buni (ph: wff) (B2 G J K O X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq K (cfv O cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni B2) K) $;

--| The base set of the fundamental group, written self-referentially. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1bas2 (ph: wff) (B2 G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cqs (cuni B2) (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Elementhood in the base set of the loop space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1eluni (ph: wff) (B2 F G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cuni B2)) (w3a (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 F) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 F) Y))) $;

--| The base set of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1bas3 (ph: wff) (B2 R G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cin (cfv J cphtpc) (cxp (cuni B2) (cuni B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cqs (cuni B2) R)) $;

--| The group operation, loop concatenation, is compatible with homotopy equivalence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1cpbl (ph: wff) (B2 P c_pl Q R G J M N O X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv G cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cin (cfv J cphtpc) (cxp (cuni B2) (cuni B2))) $ >
  $ wceq O (co J Y comi) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv O cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wbr M N R) (wbr P Q R)) (wbr (co M P c_pl) (co N Q c_pl) R)) $;

--| The elements of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem elpi1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 F G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F B2) (wrex f2 (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) Y) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) Y)) (wceq F (cec (cv f2) (cfv J cphtpc)))) (co cii J ccn))) $;

--| The elements of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem elpi1i (ph: wff) (B2 F G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cec F (cfv J cphtpc)) B2) $;

--| The group operation of ` pi1 ` is a binary operation. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1addf (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pl) $;

--| The concatenation of two path-homotopy classes in the fundamental group. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2015.)
theorem pi1addval (ph: wff) (B2 c_pl G J M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cec M (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec N (cfv J cphtpc)) c_pl) (cec (co M N (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pi1grp . (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 11-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.)
theorem pi1grplem (ph: wff) (B2 G J X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel G cgrp) (wceq (cec c_0 (cfv J cphtpc)) (cfv G c0g))) $;

--| The fundamental group is a group. Proposition 1.3 of [Hatcher] p. 26. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.)
theorem pi1grp (G J X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel Y X)) (wcel G cgrp) $;

--| The identity element of the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.)
theorem pi1id (G J X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn Y)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) (wcel Y X)) (wceq (cec c_0 (cfv J cphtpc)) (cfv G c0g)) $;

--| An inverse in the fundamental group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.)
theorem pi1inv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (I: class x3) (J: class) (N X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq G (co J Y cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) Y) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cec F (cfv J cphtpc)) N) (cec I (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Functionality of the loop transfer function on the equivalence class of a path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1xfrf {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P Q F: class) (G: class g2) (I J: class) (X: class g2):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 I) (cfv cc0 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 (cfv Q cbs) G) $;

--| The value of the loop transfer function on the equivalence class of a path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1xfrval {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P Q F: class) (G: class g2) (I J: class) (X: class g2):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv cc0 I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 I) (cfv cc0 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cec A2 (cfv J cphtpc)) G) (cec (co I (co A2 F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))) $;

--| Given a path ` F ` and its inverse ` I ` between two basepoints, there is an induced group homomorphism on the fundamental groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pi1xfr {x3 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P Q F: class) (G: class x3 g2) (I J: class) (X: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co P Q cghm)) $;

--| Given a path ` F ` between two basepoints, there is an induced group homomorphism on the fundamental groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1xfrcnvlem {x3 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P Q F: class) (G: class x3 g2) (H: class x3 g2 h) (I J: class) (X: class x3 g2 h):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crn (cmpt h (cuni (cfv Q cbs)) (cop (cec (cv h) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co F (co (cv h) I (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ccnv G) H) $;

--| Given a path ` F ` between two basepoints, there is an induced group homomorphism on the fundamental groups. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1xfrcnv {x3 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P Q F: class) (G: class x3 g2) (H: class x3 g2 h) (I J: class) (X: class x3 g2 h):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crn (cmpt h (cuni (cfv Q cbs)) (cop (cec (cv h) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co F (co (cv h) I (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (ccnv G) H) (wcel (ccnv G) (co Q P cghm))) $;

--| The mapping ` G ` between fundamental groups is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pi1xfrgim {x3 g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 P Q F: class) (G: class x3 g2) (I J: class) (X: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq P (co J (cfv cc0 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J (cfv c1 F) cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni B2) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (co I (co (cv g2) F (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cpco)) (cfv J cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cfv (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co P Q cgim)) $;

--| Functionality of the loop transfer function on the equivalence class of a path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1cof {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (P Q F: class) (G: class g2) (J K V: class) (X: class g2):
  $ wceq P (co J A2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co K B2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni V) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (ccom F (cv g2)) (cfv K cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf V (cfv Q cbs) G) $;

--| The value of the loop transfer function on the equivalence class of a path. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1coval {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (P Q T F: class) (G: class g2) (J K V: class) (X: class g2):
  $ wceq P (co J A2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co K B2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni V) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (ccom F (cv g2)) (cfv K cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel T (cuni V))) (wceq (cfv (cec T (cfv J cphtpc)) G) (cec (ccom F T) (cfv K cphtpc))) $;

--| The mapping ` G ` between fundamental groups is a group homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem pi1coghm {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (P Q F: class) (G: class g2) (J K V: class) (X: class g2):
  $ wceq P (co J A2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co K B2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq G (crn (cmpt g2 (cuni V) (cop (cec (cv g2) (cfv J cphtpc)) (cec (ccom F (cv g2)) (cfv K cphtpc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co P Q cghm)) $;

--| Syntax for the class of subcomplex modules.
term cclm: class;

--| Define the class of subcomplex modules, which are left modules over a subring of the field of complex numbers ` CCfld ` , which allows to use the complex addition, multiplication, etc. in theorems about subcomplex modules. Since the field of complex numbers is commutative and so are its subrings (see ~ subrgcrng ), left modules over such subrings are the same as right modules, see ~ rmodislmod . Therefore, we drop the word "left" from "subcomplex left module". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_clm {w f2 k: setvar}:
  $ wceq cclm (crab w (wsbc f2 (wsbc k (wa (wceq (cv f2) (co ccnfld (cv k) cress)) (wcel (cv k) (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) (cfv (cv w) csca)) clmod) $;

--| A subcomplex module is a left module over a subring of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem isclm (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel W clmod) (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) $;

--| The ring of scalars ` F ` of a subcomplex module is the restriction of the field of complex numbers to the base set of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsca (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $;

--| The base set of the ring of scalars of a subcomplex module is the base set of a subring of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsubrg (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) $;

--| A subcomplex module is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmlmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| A subcomplex module is an additive group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmgrp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel W cgrp) $;

--| A subcomplex module is an abelian group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmabl (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel W cabl) $;

--| The scalar ring of a subcomplex module is a ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmring (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel F crg) $;

--| The scalar ring of a subcomplex module is a group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmfgrp (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel F cgrp) $;

--| The zero of the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clm0 (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq cc0 (cfv F c0g)) $;

--| The identity of the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clm1 (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq c1 (cfv F cur)) $;

--| The addition of the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmadd (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq caddc (cfv F cplusg)) $;

--| The multiplication of the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmmul (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq cmul (cfv F cmulr)) $;

--| The conjugation of the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmcj (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wceq ccj (cfv F cstv)) $;

--| Reverse direction of ~ isclm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Oct-2015.)
theorem isclmi (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W clmod) (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) (wcel W cclm) $;

--| The scalar ring of a subcomplex module contains the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmzss (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wss cz K) $;

--| The scalar ring of a subcomplex module is a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsscn (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wss K cc) $;

--| Subtraction in the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsub (A2 B2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 (cfv F csg))) $;

--| Negation in the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmneg (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 K)) (wceq (cneg A2) (cfv A2 (cfv F cminusg))) $;

--| Minus one is in the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by AV, 28-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmneg1 (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wcel (cneg c1) K) $;

--| Norm in the scalar ring of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmabs (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 K)) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv A2 (cfv F cnm))) $;

--| Closure of ring addition for a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmacl (F K W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel X K) (wcel Y K)) (wcel (co X Y caddc) K) $;

--| Closure of ring multiplication for a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmmcl (F K W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel X K) (wcel Y K)) (wcel (co X Y cmul) K) $;

--| Closure of ring subtraction for a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsubcl (F K W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel X K) (wcel Y K)) (wcel (co X Y cmin) K) $;

--| The domain of a linear operator is a subcomplex module iff the range is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Oct-2015.)
theorem lmhmclm (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) (wb (wcel S cclm) (wcel T cclm)) $;

--| Closure of scalar product for a subcomplex module. Analogue of ~ lmodvscl . (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmvscl (Q c_x F K V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel Q K) (wcel X V)) (wcel (co Q X c_x) V) $;

--| Scalar multiplication is a semigroup action. Analogue of ~ lmodvsass . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmvsass (Q R c_x F K V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel Q K) (wcel R K) (wcel X V))) (wceq (co (co Q R cmul) X c_x) (co Q (co R X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Commutative law for the scalar product. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem clmvscom (Q R c_x F K V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel Q K) (wcel R K) (wcel X V))) (wceq (co Q (co R X c_x) c_x) (co R (co Q X c_x) c_x)) $;

--| Distributive law for scalar product (right-distributivity). ( ~ lmodvsdir analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmvsdir (c_pl Q R c_x F K V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel Q K) (wcel R K) (wcel X V))) (wceq (co (co Q R caddc) X c_x) (co (co Q X c_x) (co R X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Distributive law for scalar product (left-distributivity). ( ~ lmodvsdi analog.) (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmvsdi (A2 c_pl c_x F K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel X V) (wcel Y V))) (wceq (co A2 (co X Y c_pl) c_x) (co (co A2 X c_x) (co A2 Y c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Scalar product with ring unity. ( ~ lmodvs1 analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmvs1 (c_x V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co c1 X c_x) X) $;

--| A vector plus itself is two times the vector. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.) (Revised by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmvs2 (A2 c_pl c_x V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co A2 A2 c_pl) (co c2 A2 c_x)) $;

--| Zero times a vector is the zero vector. Equation 1a of [Kreyszig] p. 51. ( ~ lmod0vs analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clm0vs (c_x F V W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co cc0 X c_x) c_0) $;

--| The (functionalized) operations of addition and multiplication by a scalar of a subcomplex module cannot be identical. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2008.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2021.)
theorem clmopfne (c_pl c_x W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cscaf) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cclm) (wne c_pl c_x) $;

--| The predicate "is a subcomplex module". (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2008.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem isclmp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl S c_x K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cclm) (wa (w3a (wcel W cgrp) (wceq S (co ccnfld K cress)) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c1 (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (w3a (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) V) (wral z (wceq (co (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_x) c_pl)) V) (wral z (wa (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) caddc) (cv x3) c_x) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmul) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv z) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_x))) K)) K)) V)) $;

--| Properties that determine a subcomplex module. (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem isclmi0 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl S c_x K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (co ccnfld K cress) $ >
  $ wcel W cgrp $ >
  $ wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) V) (wceq (co c1 (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv z) V)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_x) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv z) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) caddc) (cv x3) c_x) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv z) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmul) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv z) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_x)) $ >
  $ wcel W cclm $;

--| Minus 1 times a vector is the negative of the vector. Equation 2 of [Kreyszig] p. 51. ( ~ lmodvneg1 analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmvneg1 (c_x F N V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co (cneg c1) X c_x) (cfv X N)) $;

--| Multiplication of a vector by a negated scalar. ( ~ lmodvsneg analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmvsneg (ph: wff) (B2 R c_x F K N W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cclm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co R X c_x) N) (co (cneg R) X c_x)) $;

--| The group multiple function matches the scalar multiplication function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmmulg (A2 B2 c_xb c_x V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv W cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xb) (co A2 B2 c_x)) $;

--| Scalar multiplication distributive law for subtraction. ( ~ lmodsubdir analog.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmsubdir (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_x F K c_mi V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cclm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) X c_x) (co (co A2 X c_x) (co B2 X c_x) c_mi)) $;

--| Subtractive distributive law for the scalar product of a subcomplex module. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2007.) (Revised by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmpm1dir (A2 B2 C c_pl c_x K V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (cfv W csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C c_x) (co (co A2 C c_x) (co (cneg c1) (co B2 C c_x) c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Double negative of a vector. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-2007.) (Revised by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmnegneg (A2 c_pl c_x V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co (cneg c1) (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) c_x) A2) $;

--| Distribution of negative over vector subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-2007.) (Revised by AV, 29-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmnegsubdi2 (A2 B2 c_pl c_x V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co (cneg c1) (co A2 (co (cneg c1) B2 c_x) c_pl) c_x) (co B2 (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a mixed vector addition and subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-2007.) (Revised by AV, 29-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmsub4 (A2 B2 C D c_pl c_x V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co (cneg c1) (co C D c_pl) c_x) c_pl) (co (co A2 (co (cneg c1) C c_x) c_pl) (co B2 (co (cneg c1) D c_x) c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| A vector minus itself. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmvsrinv (A2 c_pl c_x V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co A2 (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) c_pl) c_0) $;

--| Minus a vector plus itself. (Contributed by NM, 4-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2021.)
theorem clmvslinv (A2 c_pl c_x V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) A2 c_pl) c_0) $;

--| Value of vector subtraction in terms of addition in a subcomplex module. Analogue of ~ lmodvsubval2 . (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-2014.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem clmvsubval (A2 B2 c_pl c_x F c_mi V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_mi) (co A2 (co (cneg c1) B2 c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| Value of vector subtraction on a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Nov-2013.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem clmvsubval2 (A2 B2 c_pl c_x F c_mi V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_mi) (co (co (cneg c1) B2 c_x) A2 c_pl)) $;

--| Two ways to express the negative of a vector. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem clmvz (A2 c_x c_mi V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co c_0 A2 c_mi) (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x)) $;

--| The ` ZZ ` -module operation turns an arbitrary abelian group into a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Oct-2015.)
theorem zlmclm (G W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G cabl) (wcel W cclm) $;

--| The scalar product of a subcomplex module matches the scalar product of the derived ` ZZ ` -module, which implies, together with ~ zlmbas and ~ zlmplusg , that any module over ` ZZ ` is structure-equivalent to the canonical ` ZZ ` -module ` ZMod `` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Oct-2015.)
theorem clmzlmvsca (A2 B2 G W X: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cclm) (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 X))) (wceq (co A2 B2 (cfv G cvsca)) (co A2 B2 (cfv W cvsca))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nmoleub2a and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoleub2lem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2 R S: class) (T: class x3) (F: class) (G K: class x3 y1) (L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wral x3 (wi ps (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph (wral x3 (wi ps (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) (wcel A2 cr)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) V) (wne (cv y1) (cfv S c0g)))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) M) (co A2 (cfv (cv y1) L) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wi ps (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi ps (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nmoleub2a and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Sep-2021.)
theorem nmoleub2lem3 {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class) (S T c_x: class r) (F: class) (G K: class r) (L M: class) (N V: class r):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss cq K) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv S cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 (cfv S c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (co (cv r) B2 c_x) V) (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv r) B2 c_x) L) R clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (co (cv r) B2 c_x) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv (cfv B2 F) M) (co A2 (cfv B2 L) cmul) cle)) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Lemma for ~ nmoleub2a and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoleub2lem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S: class) (T: class x3) (F: class) (G K: class x3) (L M N: class) (O: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss cq K) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) L) cr) (wcel R cr)) (wi (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R O) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv (cv x3) L) cr) (wcel R cr)) (wi (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R clt) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R O) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| The operator norm is the supremum of the value of a linear operator in the closed unit ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoleub2a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S: class) (T: class x3) (F: class) (G K: class x3) (L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss cq K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R cle) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| The operator norm is the supremum of the value of a linear operator in the open unit ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmoleub2b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S: class) (T: class x3) (F: class) (G K: class x3) (L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss cq K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cfv (cv x3) L) R clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| The operator norm is the supremum of the value of a linear operator on the unit sphere. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Sep-2021.)
theorem nmoleub3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S: class) (T: class x3) (F: class) (G K: class x3) (L M N V: class):
  $ wceq N (co S T cnmo) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv S cnm) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv T cnm) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss cr K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F N) A2 cle) (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv x3) L) R) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) M) R cdiv) A2 cle)) V)) $;

--| A linear operator over a normed subcomplex module is bounded iff it is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmhmcn (B2 S T F G J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv S csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cin cnlm cclm)) (wcel T (cin cnlm cclm)) (wss cq B2)) (wb (wcel F (co S T cnmhm)) (wa (wcel F (co S T clmhm)) (wcel F (co J K ccn)))) $;

--| The powers of ` _i ` belong to the scalar subring of a subcomplex module if ` _i ` belongs to the scalar subring . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem cmodscexp (F K N W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel ci K)) (wcel N cn)) (wcel (co ci N cexp) K) $;

--| The scalar product of a vector with powers of ` _i ` belongs to the base set of a subcomplex module if the scalar subring of th subcomplex module contains ` _i ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem cmodscmulexp (B2 c_x F K N W X: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cclm) (w3a (wcel ci K) (wcel B2 X) (wcel N cn))) (wcel (co (co ci N cexp) B2 c_x) X) $;

--| Syntax for the class of subcomplex vector spaces.
term ccvs: class;

--| Define the class of subcomplex vector spaces, which are the subcomplex modules which are also vector spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
axiom df_cvs:
  $ wceq ccvs (cin cclm clvec) $;

--| A subcomplex vector space is a (left) vector space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvslvec (ph: wff) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccvs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W clvec) $;

--| A subcomplex vector space is a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsclm (ph: wff) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccvs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cclm) $;

--| A subcomplex vector space is a subcomplex module over a division ring. For example, the subcomplex modules over the rational or real or complex numbers are subcomplex vector spaces. (Contributed by AV, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem iscvs (W: class):
  $ wb (wcel W ccvs) (wa (wcel W cclm) (wcel (cfv W csca) cdr)) $;

--| The predicate "is a subcomplex vector space". (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2008.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem iscvsp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl S c_x K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W ccvs) (wa (w3a (wcel W cgrp) (wa (wcel S cdr) (wceq S (co ccnfld K cress))) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c1 (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (w3a (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) V) (wral z (wceq (co (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_x) c_pl)) V) (wral z (wa (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) caddc) (cv x3) c_x) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmul) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv z) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_x))) K)) K)) V)) $;

--| Properties that determine a subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2021.)
theorem iscvsi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl S c_x K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wcel W cgrp $ >
  $ wceq S (co ccnfld K cress) $ >
  $ wcel S cdr $ >
  $ wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) V) (wceq (co c1 (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) V) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv z) V)) (wceq (co (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_x) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv z) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) caddc) (cv x3) c_x) (co (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv y1) K) (wcel (cv z) K) (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wceq (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmul) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv z) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) c_x)) $ >
  $ wcel W ccvs $;

--| The properties of a subcomplex vector space, which is an Abelian group (i.e. the vectors, with the operation of vector addition) accompanied by a scalar multiplication operation on the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem cvsi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (c_pl: class x3 y1 z) (S: class x3 y1) (c_xb c_x: class x3 y1 z) (W: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cfv W csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv W cscaf) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccvs) (w3a (wcel W cabl) (wa (wss S cc) (wf (cxp S X) X c_xb)) (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co c1 (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wa (wral z (wceq (co (cv y1) (co (cv x3) (cv z) c_pl) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_x) c_pl)) X) (wral z (wa (wceq (co (co (cv y1) (cv z) caddc) (cv x3) c_x) (co (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (wceq (co (co (cv y1) (cv z) cmul) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv y1) (co (cv z) (cv x3) c_x) c_x))) S)) S)) X)) $;

--| Unit group of the scalar ring of a subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsunit (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccvs) (wceq (cdif K (csn cc0)) (cfv F cui)) $;

--| Division of the scalar ring of a subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsdiv (A2 B2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccvs) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co A2 B2 (cfv F cdvr))) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex vector space is closed under division. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsdivcl (A2 B2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccvs) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K) (wne B2 cc0))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) K) $;

--| An equality involving ratios in a subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsmuleqdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_x F K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccvs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 X c_x) (co B2 Y c_x)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co (co B2 A2 cdiv) Y c_x)) $;

--| An equality involving ratios in a subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2019.)
theorem cvsdiveqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_x F K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccvs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) Y c_x)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co B2 A2 cdiv) X c_x) Y) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ cnlmod . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnlmodlem1 (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W cbs) cc $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ cnlmod . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnlmodlem2 (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W cplusg) caddc $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ cnlmod . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnlmodlem3 (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W csca) ccnfld $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ cnlmod . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnlmod4 (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W cvsca) cmul $;

--| The set of complex numbers is a left module over itself. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnlmod (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wcel W clmod $;

--| The set of complex numbers is a subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 20-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnstrcvs (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cun (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cc) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx csca) ccnfld) (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) cmul))) $ >
  $ wcel W ccvs $;

--| The set of complex numbers is the base set of the complex left module of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnrbas (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv C cbs) cc $;

--| The complex left module of complex numbers is a left module. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnrlmod (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel C clmod $;

--| The complex left module of complex numbers is a left vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem cnrlvec (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel C clvec $;

--| The complex left module of complex numbers is a subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 21-Sep-2021.)
theorem cncvs (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel C ccvs $;

--| The field of the real numbers as left module over itself is a subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2021.) (Proof shortened by SN, 23-Nov-2024.)
theorem recvs (R: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv crefld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel R ccvs $;

--| The field of rational numbers as left module over itself is a subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2021.)
theorem qcvs (Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (cfv (co ccnfld cq cress) crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel Q ccvs $;

--| The ring of integers as left module over itself is a subcomplex module, but not a subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2021.)
theorem zclmncvs (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv czring crglmod) $ >
  $ wa (wcel Z cclm) (wnel Z ccvs) $;

--| A normed subcomplex vector space is a subcomplex vector space which is a normed group with a positively homogeneous norm. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jun-2008.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem isncvsngp {x3 k: setvar} (c_x F K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (w3a (wcel W ccvs) (wcel W cngp) (wral x3 (wral k (wceq (cfv (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) N) (co (cfv (cv k) cabs) (cfv (cv x3) N) cmul)) K) V)) $;

--| Properties that determine a normed subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by NM, 15-Apr-2007.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem isncvsngpd {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_x F K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccvs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cngp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) V) (wcel (cv k) K))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) N) (co (cfv (cv k) cabs) (cfv (cv x3) N) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) $;

--| The properties of a normed subcomplex vector space, which is a vector space accompanied by a norm. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvsi {x3 y1 k: setvar} (c_x F K: class y1) (c_mi: class x3 y1 k) (N: class y1) (V W: class) (c_0: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (w3a (wcel W ccvs) (wf V cr N) (wral x3 (w3a (wb (wceq (cfv (cv x3) N) cc0) (wceq (cv x3) c_0)) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_mi) N) (co (cfv (cv x3) N) (cfv (cv y1) N) caddc) cle) V) (wral k (wceq (cfv (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) N) (co (cfv (cv k) cabs) (cfv (cv x3) N) cmul)) K)) V)) $;

--| Proportionality property of the norm of a scalar product in a normed subcomplex vector space. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvsprp (A2 B2 c_x F K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_x) N) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 N) cmul)) $;

--| The norm of a scalar product with a nonnegative real. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2008.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvsge0 (A2 B2 c_x F K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wa (wcel A2 (cin K cr)) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_x) N) (co A2 (cfv B2 N) cmul)) $;

--| The norm of the opposite of a vector. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvsm1 (A2 c_x N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (cfv (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) N) (cfv A2 N)) $;

--| The norm of the difference between two vectors. (Contributed by NM, 1-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvsdif (A2 B2 c_pl c_x N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co (cneg c1) B2 c_x) c_pl) N) (cfv (co B2 (co (cneg c1) A2 c_x) c_pl) N)) $;

--| The norm of a vector plus the imaginary scalar product of another. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2007.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvspi (A2 B2 c_pl c_x F K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wcel ci K)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_pl) N) (cfv (co B2 (co (cneg ci) A2 c_x) c_pl) N)) $;

--| From any nonzero vector of a normed subcomplex vector space, construct a collinear vector whose norm is one. (Contributed by NM, 6-Dec-2007.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvs1 (A2 c_x F G K N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wne A2 c_0)) (wcel (co c1 (cfv A2 N) cdiv) K)) (wceq (cfv (co (co c1 (cfv A2 N) cdiv) A2 c_x) N) c1) $;

--| The module of complex numbers (as a module over itself) is a normed vector space over itself. The vector operation is ` + ` , and the scalar product is ` x. ` , and the norm function is ` abs ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem cnrnvc (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel C cnvc $;

--| The module of complex numbers (as a module over itself) is a normed subcomplex vector space. The vector operation is ` + ` , the scalar product is ` x. ` , and the norm is ` abs ` (see ~ cnnm ) . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 3-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem cnncvs (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wcel C (cin cnvc ccvs) $;

--| The norm of the normed subcomplex vector space of complex numbers is the absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-2008.) (Revised by AV, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem cnnm (C: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld crglmod) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv C cnm) cabs $;

--| Value of the distance function in terms of the norm of a normed subcomplex vector space. Equation 1 of [Kreyszig] p. 59. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2006.) (Revised by AV, 13-Oct-2021.)
theorem ncvspds (A2 B2 D c_pl c_x G N X: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G (cin cnvc ccvs)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) (cfv (co A2 (co (cneg c1) B2 c_x) c_pl) N)) $;

--| The metric induced on the complex numbers. ~ cnmet proves that it is a metric. The induced metric is identical with the original metric on the complex numbers, see ~ cnfldds and also ~ cnmet . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 5-Dec-2006.) (Revised by AV, 17-Oct-2021.)
theorem cnindmet (T: class):
  $ wceq T (co ccnfld cabs ctng) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv T cds) (ccom cabs cmin) $;

--| Derive the associative law for complex number addition ~ addass to demonstrate the use of the properties of a normed subcomplex vector space for the complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-2008.) (Revised by AV, 9-Oct-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem cnncvsaddassdemo (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Derive the associative law for complex number multiplication ~ mulass interpreted as scalar multiplication to demonstrate the use of the properties of a normed subcomplex vector space for the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 9-Oct-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem cnncvsmulassdemo (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C cmul) (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Derive the absolute value of a negative complex number ~ absneg to demonstrate the use of the properties of a normed subcomplex vector space for the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 9-Oct-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem cnncvsabsnegdemo (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of subcomplex pre-Hilbert spaces.
term ccph: class;

--| Function to put a norm on a pre-Hilbert space.
term ctcph: class;

--| Define the class of subcomplex pre-Hilbert spaces. By restricting the scalar field to a subfield of ` CCfld ` closed under square roots of nonnegative reals, we have enough structure to define a norm, with the associated connection to a metric and topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_cph {x3 w f2 k: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccph (crab w (wsbc f2 (wsbc k (w3a (wceq (cv f2) (co ccnfld (cv k) cress)) (wss (cima csqrt (cin (cv k) (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (cv k)) (wceq (cfv (cv w) cnm) (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv w) cbs) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cip)) csqrt)))) (cfv (cv f2) cbs)) (cfv (cv w) csca)) (cin cphl cnlm)) $;

--| Define a function to augment a pre-Hilbert space with a norm. No extra parameters are needed, but some conditions must be satisfied to ensure that this in fact creates a normed subcomplex pre-Hilbert space (see ~ tcphcph ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_tcph {x3 w: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctcph (cmpt w cvv (co (cv w) (cmpt x3 (cfv (cv w) cbs) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cip)) csqrt)) ctng)) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is exactly a pre-Hilbert space over a subfield of the field of complex numbers closed under square roots of nonnegative reals equipped with a norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscph {x3: setvar} (F c_xi K N V: class x3) (W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W ccph) (w3a (w3a (wcel W cphl) (wcel W cnlm) (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress))) (wss (cima csqrt (cin K (co cc0 cpnf cico))) K) (wceq N (cmpt x3 V (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) csqrt)))) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphphl (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W cphl) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnlm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W cnlm) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphngp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W cngp) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphlmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a left vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphlvec (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W clvec) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a normed vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnvc (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W cnvc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cphsubrg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsubrglem (ph: wff) (A2 F K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld A2 cress)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cdr) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) (wceq K (cin A2 cc)) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cphreccl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphreccllem (ph: wff) (A2 F K X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld A2 cress)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cdr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X K) (wne X cc0)) (wcel (co c1 X cdiv) K) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a vector space over a subfield of ` CCfld ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsca (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a subring of ` CCfld ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsubrg (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphreccl (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 K) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphdivcl (A2 B2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K) (wne B2 cc0))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under conjugation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphcjcl (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 K)) (wcel (cfv A2 ccj) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under square roots of nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsqrtcl (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle))) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under the absolute value operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphabscl (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 K)) (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is closed under square roots of all numbers except possibly the negative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsqrtcl2 (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 K) (wn (wcel (cneg A2) crp))) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) K) $;

--| If the scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space contains the imaginary unit ` _i ` , then it is closed under square roots (i.e., it is quadratically closed). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsqrtcl3 (A2 F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K) (wcel A2 K)) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space contains the rational numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphqss (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wss cq K) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a subcomplex module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphclm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel W cclm) $;

--| Norm of a scalar product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnmvs (c_x F K N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel X K) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y c_x) N) (co (cfv X cabs) (cfv Y N) cmul)) $;

--| An inner product is a member of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphipcl (A2 B2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wcel (co A2 B2 c_xi) cc) $;

--| The value of the norm in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is the square root of the inner product of a vector with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnmfval {x3: setvar} (c_xi: class) (N: class x3) (V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wceq N (cmpt x3 V (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) csqrt))) $;

--| The square of the norm is the norm of an inner product in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnm (A2 c_xi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (cfv A2 N) (cfv (co A2 A2 c_xi) csqrt)) $;

--| The square of the norm is the norm of an inner product in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. Equation I4 of [Ponnusamy] p. 362. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmsq (A2 c_xi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co (cfv A2 N) c2 cexp) (co A2 A2 c_xi)) $;

--| The norm of a vector is a member of the scalar field in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnmf (F c_xi K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wf V K N) $;

--| The norm of a vector is a member of the scalar field in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphnmcl (A2 F c_xi K N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (cfv A2 N) K) $;

--| An inner product of an element with itself is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem reipcl (A2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co A2 A2 c_xi) cr) $;

--| The inner product in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is positive definite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipge0 (A2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 A2 c_xi) cle) $;

--| Conjugate of an inner product in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. Complex version of ~ ipcj . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphipcj (A2 B2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 c_xi) ccj) (co B2 A2 c_xi)) $;

--| An inner product times its conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-2007.) (Revised by AV, 19-Oct-2021.)
theorem cphipipcj (A2 B2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co B2 A2 c_xi) cmul) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_xi) cabs) c2 cexp)) $;

--| Orthogonality (meaning inner product is 0) is commutative. Complex version of ~ iporthcom . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphorthcom (A2 B2 c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xi) cc0) (wceq (co B2 A2 c_xi) cc0)) $;

--| Inner product with a zero first argument. Part of proof of Theorem 6.44 of [Ponnusamy] p. 361. Complex version of ~ ip0l . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphip0l (A2 c_xi V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co c_0 A2 c_xi) cc0) $;

--| Inner product with a zero second argument. Complex version of ~ ip0r . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphip0r (A2 c_xi V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co A2 c_0 c_xi) cc0) $;

--| The inner product of a vector with itself is zero iff the vector is zero. Part of Definition 3.1-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 129. Complex version of ~ ipeq0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphipeq0 (A2 c_xi V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V)) (wb (wceq (co A2 A2 c_xi) cc0) (wceq A2 c_0)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product (right-distributivity). Equation I3 of [Ponnusamy] p. 362. Complex version of ~ ipdir . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphdir (A2 B2 C c_pl c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) C c_xi) (co (co A2 C c_xi) (co B2 C c_xi) caddc)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product (left-distributivity). Complex version of ~ ipdi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphdi (A2 B2 C c_pl c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C c_pl) c_xi) (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co A2 C c_xi) caddc)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product. Complex version of ~ ip2di . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cph2di (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_pl c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_pl) (co C D c_pl) c_xi) (co (co (co A2 C c_xi) (co B2 D c_xi) caddc) (co (co A2 D c_xi) (co B2 C c_xi) caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product subtraction. Complex version of ~ ipsubdir . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsubdir (A2 B2 C c_xi c_mi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_mi) C c_xi) (co (co A2 C c_xi) (co B2 C c_xi) cmin)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product subtraction. Complex version of ~ ipsubdi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphsubdi (A2 B2 C c_xi c_mi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C c_mi) c_xi) (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co A2 C c_xi) cmin)) $;

--| Distributive law for inner product subtraction. Complex version of ~ ip2subdi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cph2subdi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D c_xi c_mi V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_mi) (co C D c_mi) c_xi) (co (co (co A2 C c_xi) (co B2 D c_xi) caddc) (co (co A2 D c_xi) (co B2 C c_xi) caddc) cmin)) $;

--| Associative law for inner product. Equation I2 of [Ponnusamy] p. 363. See ~ ipass , ~ his5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphass (A2 B2 C c_x F c_xi K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 c_x) C c_xi) (co A2 (co B2 C c_xi) cmul)) $;

--| "Associative" law for second argument of inner product (compare ~ cphass ). See ~ ipassr , his52 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphassr (A2 B2 C c_x F c_xi K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (w3a (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C V))) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 C c_x) c_xi) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (co B2 C c_xi) cmul)) $;

--| Move scalar multiplication to outside of inner product. See ~ his35 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem cph2ass (A2 B2 C D c_x F c_xi K V W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wa (wcel A2 K) (wcel B2 K)) (wa (wcel C V) (wcel D V))) (wceq (co (co A2 C c_x) (co B2 D c_x) c_xi) (co (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) (co C D c_xi) cmul)) $;

--| Associative law for the first argument of an inner product with scalar ` _ i ` . (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2021.)
theorem cphassi (A2 B2 c_x F c_xi K W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co (co ci B2 c_x) A2 c_xi) (co ci (co B2 A2 c_xi) cmul)) $;

--| "Associative" law for the second argument of an inner product with scalar ` _ i ` . (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2021.)
theorem cphassir (A2 B2 c_x F c_xi K W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_xi) (co (cneg ci) (co A2 B2 c_xi) cmul)) $;

--| The pythagorean theorem for a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. The square of the norm of the sum of two orthogonal vectors (i.e., whose inner product is 0) is the sum of the squares of their norms. Problem 2 in [Kreyszig] p. 135. This is Metamath 100 proof #4. (Contributed by NM, 17-Apr-2008.) (Revised by SN, 22-Sep-2024.)
theorem cphpyth (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl c_xi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xi) cc0)) (wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv A2 N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 N) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tcphbas and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphex {x3: setvar} (c_xi: class x3) (V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 V (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) csqrt)) cvv $;

--| Define a function to augment a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphval {x3: setvar} (G c_xi V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq G (co W (cmpt x3 V (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) csqrt)) ctng) $;

--| The base set of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphbas (G V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cbs) $;

--| The addition operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tchplusg (c_pl G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $;

--| The subtraction operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem tcphsub (G c_mi W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv G csg) $;

--| The ring operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphmulr (c_x G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cmulr) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphsca (F G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv G csca) $;

--| The scalar multiplication of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphvsca (c_x G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cvsca) $;

--| The inner product of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphip (c_x G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cip) $;

--| The topology of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphtopn (D G J V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W V) (wceq J (cfv D cmopn)) $;

--| Augmentation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space with a norm does not affect whether it is still a pre-Hilbert space (because all the original components are the same). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphphl (G W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cphl) (wcel G cphl) $;

--| The norm of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tchnmfval {x3: setvar} (G c_xi: class) (N: class x3) (V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cgrp) (wceq N (cmpt x3 V (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) csqrt))) $;

--| The norm of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphnmval (G c_xi N V W X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cgrp) (wcel X V)) (wceq (cfv X N) (cfv (co X X c_xi) csqrt)) $;

--| The norm of a norm-augmented subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is the same as the original norm on it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem cphtcphnm (G N W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wceq N (cfv G cnm)) $;

--| The distance of a pre-Hilbert space augmented with norm. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem tcphds (G c_mi N W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cgrp) (wceq (ccom N c_mi) (cfv G cds)) $;

--| A pre-Hilbert space whose field of scalars is a restriction of the field of complex numbers is a subcomplex module. TODO: redundant hypotheses. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Oct-2015.)
theorem phclm (ph: wff) (F G K V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cclm) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tcphcph : real closure of an inner product of a vector with itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphcphlem3 (ph: wff) (F G c_xi K V W X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X V)) (wcel (co X X c_xi) cr) $;

--| The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space built from a pre-Hilbert space with certain properties. The main theorem is ~ ipcau . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipcau2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F G c_xi: class) (K N: class x3) (V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr cc0 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) cle) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co Y X c_xi) (co Y Y c_xi) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co X Y c_xi) cabs) (co (cfv X N) (cfv Y N) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tcphcph : the triangle inequality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphcphlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G c_xi: class) (K: class x3) (c_mi V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr cc0 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) cle) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co X Y c_mi) (co X Y c_mi) c_xi) csqrt) (co (cfv (co X X c_xi) csqrt) (cfv (co Y Y c_xi) csqrt) caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ tcphcph : homogeneity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphcphlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_x F G c_xi: class) (K: class x3) (V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr cc0 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) cle) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co (co X Y c_x) (co X Y c_x) c_xi) csqrt) (co (cfv X cabs) (cfv (co Y Y c_xi) csqrt) cmul)) $;

--| The standard definition of a norm turns any pre-Hilbert space over a subfield of ` CCfld ` closed under square roots of nonnegative reals into a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space (which allows access to a norm, metric, and topology). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem tcphcph {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G c_xi: class) (K: class x3) (V W: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv W ctcph) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cphl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co ccnfld K cress)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) K) (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) K) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) V)) (wbr cc0 (co (cv x3) (cv x3) c_xi) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccph) $;

--| The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. Part of Lemma 3.2-1(a) of [Kreyszig] p. 137. This is Metamath 100 proof #78. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipcau (c_xi N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) (wbr (cfv (co X Y c_xi) cabs) (co (cfv X N) (cfv Y N) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nmpar . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmparlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pl F c_xi K c_mi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (co (co (cfv A2 N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 N) c2 cexp) caddc) cmul)) $;

--| A subcomplex pre-Hilbert space satisfies the parallelogram law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem nmpar (A2 B2 c_pl c_mi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wceq (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (co (co (cfv A2 N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 N) c2 cexp) caddc) cmul)) $;

--| Value of the inner product expressed by the norm defined by it. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2007.) (Revised by AV, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem cphipval2 (A2 B2 c_pl c_x F c_xi K c_mi N W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co (co (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) c2 cexp) cmin) (co ci (co (co (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_mi) N) c2 cexp) cmin) cmul) caddc) c4 cdiv)) $;

--| Four times the inner product value ~ cphipval2 . (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem _4cphipval2 (A2 B2 c_pl c_x F c_xi K c_mi N W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co c4 (co A2 B2 c_xi) cmul) (co (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 B2 c_mi) N) c2 cexp) cmin) (co ci (co (co (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_pl) N) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 c_x) c_mi) N) c2 cexp) cmin) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Value of the inner product expressed by a sum of terms with the norm defined by the inner product. Equation 6.45 of [Ponnusamy] p. 361. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2007.) (Revised by AV, 18-Oct-2021.)
theorem cphipval {k: setvar} (A2 B2 c_pl c_x: class) (F c_xi: class k) (K N W X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel ci K)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co (csu k (co c1 c4 cfz) (co (co ci (cv k) cexp) (co (cfv (co A2 (co (co ci (cv k) cexp) B2 c_x) c_pl) N) c2 cexp) cmul)) c4 cdiv)) $;

--| The inner product operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipcnlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R T U c_xi N V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 N) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv B2 N) T caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 X D) U clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 Y D) T clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co X Y c_xi) cmin) cabs) R clt) $;

--| The inner product operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipcnlem1 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2 B2 D R: class x3 y1) (T U: class) (c_xi: class x3 y1) (N: class x3 y1 r) (V: class x3) (W: class x3 y1 r):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 N) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (co R c2 cdiv) (co (cfv B2 N) T caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wbr (co A2 (cv x3) D) (cv r) clt) (wbr (co B2 (cv y1) D) (cv r) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_xi) cmin) cabs) R clt)) V) V) crp) $;

--| The inner product operation of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem ipcn (c_xi J K W: class):
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cipf) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W ccph) (wcel c_xi (co (co J J ctx) K ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of inner product; analogue of ~ cnmpt12f which cannot be used directly because ` .i ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt1ip {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (c_xi: class x3) (J K W X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 c_xi)) (co K C ccn)) $;

--| Continuity of inner product; analogue of ~ cnmpt22f which cannot be used directly because ` .i ` is not a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cnmpt2ip {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (C: class) (c_xi: class x3 y1) (J: class) (K: class y1) (L: class x3 y1) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y A2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y B2) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (co A2 B2 c_xi)) (co (co K L ctx) C ccn)) $;

--| A "closed subspace" in a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is actually closed in the topology induced by the norm, thus justifying the terminology "closed subspace". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem csscld (C S J W: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel S C)) (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The orthogonal complement of the closure of a subset is the same as the orthogonal complement of the subset itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem clsocv (S J O V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W cocv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wss S V)) (wceq (cfv (cfv S (cfv J ccl)) O) (cfv S O)) $;

--| A subspace of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2008.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2022.)
theorem cphsscph (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W ccph) (wcel U S)) (wcel X ccph) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of Cauchy filters.
term ccfil: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of Cauchy sequences.
term ccau: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of complete metrics.
term ccmet: class;

--| Define the set of Cauchy filters on a given extended metric space. A Cauchy filter is a filter on the set such that for every ` 0 < x ` there is an element of the filter whose metric diameter is less than ` x ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_cfil {x3 y1 f2 d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccfil (cmpt d (cuni (crn cxmet)) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cima (cv d) (cxp (cv y1) (cv y1))) (co cc0 (cv x3) cico)) (cv f2)) crp) (cfv (cdm (cdm (cv d))) cfil))) $;

--| Define the set of Cauchy sequences on a given extended metric space. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.)
axiom df_cau {x3 f2 j d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccau (cmpt d (cuni (crn cxmet)) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) (co (cfv (cv j) (cv f2)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv d) cbl)) (cres (cv f2) (cfv (cv j) cuz))) cz) crp) (co (cdm (cdm (cv d))) cc cpm))) $;

--| Define the set of complete metrics on a given set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
axiom df_cmet {x3 f2 d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccmet (cmpt x3 cvv (crab d (wral f2 (wne (co (cfv (cv d) cmopn) (cv f2) cflim) c0) (cfv (cv d) ccfil)) (cfv (cv x3) cmet))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space. Definition 1.4-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 25. The condition ` F C_ ( CC X. X ) ` allows to use objects more general than sequences when convenient; see the comment in ~ df-lm . (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmbr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (D P F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wf (cv y1) (co P (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) (cres F (cv y1))) (crn cuz)) crp))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space. Definition 1.4-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 25. The condition ` F C_ ( CC X. X ) ` allows to use objects more general than sequences when convenient; see the comment in ~ df-lm . (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmbr2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P F: class) (J: class j k) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (w3a (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) X) (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) P D) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmbr3 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P F: class) (J: class j k) (M: class x3 k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (w3a (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) X) (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) P D) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Convergence property of a converging sequence. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmcvg {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class j k) (D P R F: class) (J: class j k) (M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel A2 X) (wbr (co A2 P D) R clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. This version of ~ lmmbr2 presupposes that ` F ` is a function. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmmbrf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 j k) (D P F: class) (J: class j k) (M: class x3 k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wa (wcel P X) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co A2 P D) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| A condition that implies convergence. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmnn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P F: class) (J: class k) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn X F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) P D) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $;

--| The set of Cauchy filters on a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilfval {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq (cfv D ccfil) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cima D (cxp (cv y1) (cv y1))) (co cc0 (cv x3) cico)) (cv f2)) crp) (cfv X cfil))) $;

--| The property of being a Cauchy filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscfil {x3 y1: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wss (cima D (cxp (cv y1) (cv y1))) (co cc0 (cv x3) cico)) F) crp))) $;

--| The property of being a Cauchy filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscfil2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wral w (wbr (co (cv z) (cv w) D) (cv x3) clt) (cv y1)) (cv y1)) F) crp))) $;

--| A Cauchy filter is a filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilfil (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccfil))) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) $;

--| Property of a Cauchy filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfili {x3 y1 z: setvar} (D R F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wcel R crp)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wbr (co (cv y1) (cv z) D) R clt) (cv x3)) (cv x3)) F) $;

--| A Cauchy filter contains balls of any pre-chosen size. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfil3i {x3: setvar} (D R F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wcel R crp)) (wrex x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) R (cfv D cbl)) F) X) $;

--| A filter finer than a Cauchy filter is Cauchy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilss (D F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccfil))) (wa (wcel G (cfv X cfil)) (wss F G))) (wcel G (cfv D ccfil)) $;

--| The Cauchy filter condition for a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem fgcfil {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel B2 (cfv X cfbas))) (wb (wcel (co X B2 cfg) (cfv D ccfil)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wral w (wbr (co (cv z) (cv w) D) (cv x3) clt) (cv y1)) (cv y1)) B2) crp)) $;

--| The Cauchy filter condition for a filter map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem fmcfil {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (B2 D F X: class) (Y: class w):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel B2 (cfv Y cfbas)) (wf Y X F)) (wb (wcel (cfv B2 (co X F cfm)) (cfv D ccfil)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wral w (wbr (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv w) F) D) (cv x3) clt) (cv y1)) (cv y1)) B2) crp)) $;

--| A filter is Cauchy iff it contains a ball of any chosen size. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscfil3 {x3 r: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wa (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wral r (wrex x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) F) X) crp))) $;

--| Similar to ultrafilters ( ~ uffclsflim ), the cluster points and limit points of a Cauchy filter coincide. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilfcls (D F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq X (cdm (cdm D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wceq (co J F cfcls) (co J F cflim)) $;

--| The set of Cauchy sequences on a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2015.)
theorem caufval {x3 f2 k: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wceq (cfv D ccau) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wrex k (wf (cfv (cv k) cuz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) (cres (cv f2) (cfv (cv k) cuz))) cz) crp) (co X cc cpm))) $;

--| Express the property " ` F ` is a Cauchy sequence of metric ` D ` ". Part of Definition 1.4-3 of [Kreyszig] p. 28. The condition ` F C_ ( CC X. X ) ` allows to use objects more general than sequences when convenient; see the comment in ~ df-lm . (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem iscau {x3 k: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wa (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wral x3 (wrex k (wf (cfv (cv k) cuz) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) (cfv D cbl)) (cres F (cfv (cv k) cuz))) cz) crp))) $;

--| Express the property " ` F ` is a Cauchy sequence of metric ` D ` " using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem iscau2 {x3 j k: setvar} (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wa (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (w3a (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) X) (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) D) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| Express the Cauchy sequence property in the more conventional three-quantifier form. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Nov-2013.)
theorem iscau3 {x3 j k m: setvar} (ph: wff m) (D F: class) (M: class x3 k m) (X: class) (Z: class m):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wa (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (w3a (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) X) (wral m (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv m) F) D) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Express the property " ` F ` is a Cauchy sequence of metric ` D ` " using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem iscau4 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 j k) (D F: class) (M: class x3 k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv j) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wa (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (w3a (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel A2 X) (wbr (co A2 B2 D) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Express the property " ` F ` is a Cauchy sequence of metric ` D ` " presupposing ` F ` is a function. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Dec-2013.)
theorem iscauf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 j k) (D F: class) (M: class x3 k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv j) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co B2 A2 D) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| A metric with a Cauchy sequence cannot be empty. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2013.)
theorem caun0 (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccau))) (wne X c0) $;

--| Inclusion of a Cauchy sequence, under our definition. (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2013.)
theorem caufpm (D F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccau))) (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) $;

--| A Cauchy sequence predicate can be expressed in terms of the Cauchy filter predicate for a suitably chosen filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem caucfil (D F L M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (cima cuz Z) (co X F cfm)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel M cz) (wf Z X F)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wcel L (cfv D ccfil))) $;

--| The property " ` D ` is a complete metric." meaning all Cauchy filters converge to a point in the space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscmet {f2: setvar} (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wral f2 (wne (co J (cv f2) cflim) c0) (cfv D ccfil))) $;

--| The convergence of a Cauchy filter in a complete metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmetcvg (D F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccfil))) (wne (co J F cflim) c0) $;

--| A complete metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jan-2014.)
theorem cmetmet (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| A complete metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by NM, 26-Oct-2007.)
theorem cmetmeti (D X: class):
  $ wcel D (cfv X ccmet) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv X cmet) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cmetcau . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmetcaulem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P F: class) (G: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cn (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cdm F)) (cfv (cv x3) F) P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (cfv J clm))) $;

--| The convergence of a Cauchy sequence in a complete metric space. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmetcau (D F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wcel F (cfv D ccau))) (wcel F (cdm (cfv J clm))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iscmet3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscmet3lem3 {j k: setvar} (R M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel R crp)) (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (cv k) cexp) R clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iscmet3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscmet3lem1 {v u k n: setvar} (ph: wff v u) (D S F: class) (J M X Z: class v u):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wral u (wral v (wbr (co (cv u) (cv v) D) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (cv k) cexp) clt) (cfv (cv k) S)) (cfv (cv k) S)) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wral n (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv n) S)) (co M (cv k) cfz)) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iscmet3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscmet3lem2 {v u k n: setvar} (ph: wff v u) (D S F: class) (G: class v u n) (J M X Z: class v u):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wral u (wral v (wbr (co (cv u) (cv v) D) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (cv k) cexp) clt) (cfv (cv k) S)) (cfv (cv k) S)) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wral n (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv n) S)) (co M (cv k) cfz)) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv X cfil)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cz G S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (cfv J clm))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co J G cflim) c0) $;

--| The property " ` D ` is a complete metric" expressed in terms of functions on ` NN ` (or any other upper integer set). Thus, we only have to look at functions on ` NN ` , and not all possible Cauchy filters, to determine completeness. (The proof uses countable choice.) (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem iscmet3 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D J M X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wral f2 (wi (wf Z X (cv f2)) (wcel (cv f2) (cdm (cfv J clm)))) (cfv D ccau))) $;

--| A metric ` D ` is complete iff all Cauchy sequences converge to a point in the space. The proof uses countable choice. Part of Definition 1.4-3 of [Kreyszig] p. 28. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscmet2 (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wb (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wss (cfv D ccau) (cdm (cfv J clm)))) $;

--| A Cauchy filter on a metric subspace extends to a Cauchy filter in the larger space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilresi (D F X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv (cres D (cxp Y Y)) ccfil))) (wcel (co X F cfg) (cfv D ccfil)) $;

--| Cauchy filter on a metric subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cfilres (D F X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel F (cfv X cfil)) (wcel Y F)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) (wcel (co F Y crest) (cfv (cres D (cxp Y Y)) ccfil))) $;

--| Cauchy sequence on a metric subspace. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.)
theorem caussi (D X Y: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss (cfv (cres D (cxp Y Y)) ccau) (cfv D ccau)) $;

--| Cauchy sequence on a metric subspace. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2013.)
theorem causs (D F X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wf cn Y F)) (wb (wcel F (cfv D ccau)) (wcel F (cfv (cres D (cxp Y Y)) ccau))) $;

--| If the metric ` D ` is "strongly finer" than ` C ` (meaning that there is a positive real constant ` R ` such that ` C ( x , y ) <_ R x. D ( x , y ) ` ), all the ` D ` -Cauchy filters are also ` C ` -Cauchy. (Using this theorem twice in each direction states that if two metrics are strongly equivalent, then they have the same Cauchy sequences.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2015.)
theorem equivcfil {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (co R (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv D ccfil) (cfv C ccfil)) $;

--| If the metric ` D ` is "strongly finer" than ` C ` (meaning that there is a positive real constant ` R ` such that ` C ( x , y ) <_ R x. D ( x , y ) ` ), all the ` D ` -Cauchy sequences are also ` C ` -Cauchy. (Using this theorem twice in each direction states that if two metrics are strongly equivalent, then they have the same Cauchy sequences.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2015.)
theorem equivcau {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (co R (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv D ccau) (cfv C ccau)) $;

--| If the distance from each member of a converging sequence to a given point is less than or equal to a given amount, so is the convergence value. (Contributed by NM, 23-Dec-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmle {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P Q R F J: class) (M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (co Q (cfv (cv k) F) D) R cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co Q P D) R cle) $;

--| If the norm of each member of a converging sequence is less than or equal to a given amount, so is the norm of the convergence value. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2007.) (Revised by AV, 16-Oct-2021.)
theorem nglmle {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P R F G J: class) (N: class k) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv G cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cngp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) N) R cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv P N) R cle) $;

--| Relate a limit on the metric space of complex numbers to our complex number limit notation. (Contributed by NM, 9-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmclim (P F J M Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wss Z (cdm F))) (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wa (wcel F (co cc cc cpm)) (wbr F P cli))) $;

--| Relate a limit on the metric space of complex numbers to our complex number limit notation. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jul-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem lmclimf (P F J M Z: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wf Z cc F)) (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wbr F P cli)) $;

--| A point belongs to the closure of a subset iff there is a sequence in the subset converging to it. Theorem 1.4-6(a) of [Kreyszig] p. 30. This proof uses countable choice ~ ax-cc . The statement can be generalized to first-countable spaces, not just metrizable spaces. (Contributed by NM, 8-Nov-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2015.)
theorem metelcls {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P S J: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv J ccl))) (wex f2 (wa (wf cn S (cv f2)) (wbr (cv f2) P (cfv J clm))))) $;

--| A subset of a metric space is closed iff every convergent sequence on it converges to a point in the subset. Theorem 1.4-6(b) of [Kreyszig] p. 30. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem metcld {x3 f2: setvar} (D S J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wal x3 (wal f2 (wi (wa (wf cn S (cv f2)) (wbr (cv f2) (cv x3) (cfv J clm))) (wcel (cv x3) S))))) $;

--| A subset of a metric space is closed iff every convergent sequence on it converges to a point in the subset. Theorem 1.4-6(b) of [Kreyszig] p. 30. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem metcld2 (D S J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss S X)) (wb (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wss (cima (cfv J clm) (co S cn cmap)) S)) $;

--| Sufficient condition to ensure a sequence of nested balls is Cauchy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem caubl {n r: setvar} (ph: wff) (D F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cxp X crp) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wss (cfv (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv D cbl))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral r (wrex n (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) (cv r) clt) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom c1st F) (cfv D ccau)) $;

--| The convergent point of a sequence of nested balls is in the closures of any of the balls (i.e. it is in the intersection of the closures). Indeed, it is the only point in the intersection because a metric space is Hausdorff, but we don't prove this here. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jan-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem caublcls {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class n) (D: class) (P: class n) (F: class) (J: class n) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cxp X crp) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wss (cfv (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv D cbl))) cn) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (ccom c1st F) P (cfv J clm)) (wcel A2 cn)) (wcel P (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 F) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl))) $;

--| Two ways to say a mapping from metric ` C ` to metric ` D ` is continuous at point ` P ` . Theorem 14-4.3 of [Gleason] p. 240. (Contributed by NM, 17-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2014.)
theorem metcnp4 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D P F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cfv P (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf X Y F) (wal f2 (wi (wa (wf cn X (cv f2)) (wbr (cv f2) P (cfv J clm))) (wbr (ccom F (cv f2)) (cfv P F) (cfv K clm)))))) $;

--| Two ways to say a mapping from metric ` C ` to metric ` D ` is continuous. Theorem 10.3 of [Munkres] p. 128. (Contributed by NM, 13-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2014.)
theorem metcn4 {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv C cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv Y cxmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co J K ccn)) (wal f2 (wi (wf cn X (cv f2)) (wal x3 (wi (wbr (cv f2) (cv x3) (cfv J clm)) (wbr (ccom F (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv K clm))))))) $;

--| Properties that determine a complete metric space. (Contributed by NM, 15-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem iscmet3i {f2: setvar} (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv X cmet) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv f2) (cfv D ccau)) (wf cn X (cv f2))) (wcel (cv f2) (cdm (cfv J clm))) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv X ccmet) $;

--| Every convergent sequence in a metric space is a Cauchy sequence. Theorem 1.4-5 of [Kreyszig] p. 28. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2014.)
theorem lmcau (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wss (cdm (cfv J clm)) (cfv D ccau)) $;

--| Every convergent filter in a metric space is a Cauchy filter. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem flimcfil (A2 D F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel A2 (co J F cflim))) (wcel F (cfv D ccfil)) $;

--| A complete subspace of a metric space is closed in the parent space. Formerly part of proof for ~ cmetss . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 9-Oct-2022.)
theorem metsscmetcld (D J X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel (cres D (cxp Y Y)) (cfv Y ccmet))) (wcel Y (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| A subspace of a complete metric space is complete iff it is closed in the parent space. Theorem 1.4-7 of [Kreyszig] p. 30. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Oct-2022.)
theorem cmetss (D J X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wb (wcel (cres D (cxp Y Y)) (cfv Y ccmet)) (wcel Y (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| If two metrics are strongly equivalent, one is complete iff the other is. Unlike ~ equivcau , ~ metss2 , this theorem does not have a one-directional form - it is possible for a metric ` C ` that is strongly finer than the complete metric ` D ` to be incomplete and vice versa. Consider ` D = ` the metric on ` RR ` induced by the usual homeomorphism from ` ( 0 , 1 ) ` against the usual metric ` C ` on ` RR ` and against the discrete metric ` E ` on ` RR ` . Then both ` C ` and ` E ` are complete but ` D ` is not, and ` C ` is strongly finer than ` D ` , which is strongly finer than ` E ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2015.)
theorem equivcmet {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C D R S X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) (co R (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X))) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) (co S (co (cv x3) (cv y1) C) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (cfv X ccmet)) (wcel D (cfv X ccmet))) $;

--| If ` D ` is a metric space such that all the balls of some fixed size are relatively compact, then ` D ` is complete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem relcmpcmet {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (D R J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (co J (cfv (co (cv x3) R (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) crest) ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $;

--| A compact metric space is complete. One half of ~ heibor . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmpcmet (ph: wff) (D J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $;

--| Given a metric ` D ` and a uniform structure generated by that metric, Cauchy filter bases on that uniform structure are exactly the Cauchy filters for the metric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem cfilucfil3 (C D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet))) (wb (wa (wcel C (cfv X cfil)) (wcel C (cfv (cfv D cmetu) ccfilu))) (wcel C (cfv D ccfil))) $;

--| Given a metric ` D ` and a uniform structure generated by that metric, Cauchy filter bases on that uniform structure are exactly the Cauchy filters for the metric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem cfilucfil4 (C D X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X cxmet)) (wcel C (cfv X cfil))) (wb (wcel C (cfv (cfv D cmetu) ccfilu)) (wcel C (cfv D ccfil))) $;

--| The set of complex numbers is a complete metric space under the absolute value metric. (Contributed by NM, 20-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cncmet (D: class):
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv cc ccmet) $;

--| The real numbers are a complete metric space. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem recmet:
  $ wcel (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) (cfv cr ccmet) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bcth . Substitutions for the function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcthlem1 {x3 z k r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class z k) (D F J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo k z cn (cxp X crp) (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv r) crp)) (wa (wbr (cv r) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cv k) M))))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 (cxp X crp)))) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 F)) (w3a (wcel C (cxp X crp)) (wbr (cfv C c2nd) (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (cfv C (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv B2 (cfv D cbl)) (cfv A2 M))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bcth . The balls in the sequence form an inclusion chain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcthlem2 {x3 z g2 k n r: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (C: class z g2 k n) (D: class) (R: class z g2 k n r) (F J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo k z cn (cxp X crp) (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv r) crp)) (wa (wbr (cv r) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cv k) M))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cxp X crp) (cv g2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) (cop C R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wcel (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wss (cfv (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cfv D cbl))) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bcth . The limit point of the centers in the sequence is in the intersection of every ball in the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcthlem3 {x3 z g2 k r: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (A2: class g2) (C: class z g2 k) (D: class) (R: class z g2 k r) (F J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo k z cn (cxp X crp) (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv r) crp)) (wa (wbr (cv r) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cv k) M))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cxp X crp) (cv g2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) (cop C R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wcel (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) cn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (ccom c1st (cv g2)) (cv x3) (cfv J clm)) (wcel A2 cn)) (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cfv A2 (cv g2)) (cfv D cbl))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bcth . Given any open ball ` ( C ( ball `` D ) R ) ` as starting point (and in particular, a ball in ` int ( U. ran M ) ` ), the limit point ` x ` of the centers of the induced sequence of balls ` g ` is outside ` U. ran M ` . Note that a set ` A ` has empty interior iff every nonempty open set ` U ` contains points outside ` A ` , i.e. ` ( U \ A ) =/= (/) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcthlem4 {x3 z g2 k r: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (C: class z g2 k) (D: class) (R: class z g2 k r) (F J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo k z cn (cxp X crp) (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv r) crp)) (wa (wbr (cv r) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cv k) M))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cxp X crp) (cv g2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) (cop C R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wcel (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) F)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cdif (co C R (cfv D cbl)) (cuni (crn M))) c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bcth . The proof makes essential use of the Axiom of Dependent Choice ~ axdc4uz , which in the form used here accepts a "selection" function ` F ` from each element of ` K ` to a nonempty subset of ` K ` , and the result function ` g ` maps ` g ( n + 1 ) ` to an element of ` F ( n , g ( n ) ) ` . The trick here is thus in the choice of ` F ` and ` K ` : we let ` K ` be the set of all tagged nonempty open sets (tagged here meaning that we have a point and an open set, in an ordered pair), and ` F ( k , <. x , z >. ) ` gives the set of all balls of size less than ` 1 / k ` , tagged by their centers, whose closures fit within the given open set ` z ` and miss ` M ( k ) ` . Since ` M ( k ) ` is closed, ` z \ M ( k ) ` is open and also nonempty, since ` z ` is nonempty and ` M ( k ) ` has empty interior. Then there is some ball contained in it, and hence our function ` F ` is valid (it never maps to the empty set). Now starting at a point in the interior of ` U. ran M ` , DC gives us the function ` g ` all whose elements are constrained by ` F ` acting on the previous value. (This is all proven in this lemma.) Now ` g ` is a sequence of tagged open balls, forming an inclusion chain (see ~ bcthlem2 ) and whose sizes tend to zero, since they are bounded above by ` 1 / k ` . Thus, the centers of these balls form a Cauchy sequence, and converge to a point ` x ` (see ~ bcthlem4 ). Since the inclusion chain also ensures the closure of each ball is in the previous ball, the point ` x ` must be in all these balls (see ~ bcthlem3 ) and hence misses each ` M ( k ) ` , contradicting the fact that ` x ` is in the interior of ` U. ran M ` (which was the starting point). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcthlem5 {x3 z k r: setvar} (ph: wff) (D F J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo k z cn (cxp X crp) (copab x3 r (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv r) crp)) (wa (wbr (cv r) (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) clt) (wss (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv r) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv J ccl)) (cdif (cfv (cv z) (cfv D cbl)) (cfv (cv k) M))))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) M) (cfv J cnt)) c0) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cuni (crn M)) (cfv J cnt)) c0) $;

--| Baire's Category Theorem. If a nonempty metric space is complete, it is nonmeager in itself. In other words, no open set in the metric space can be the countable union of rare closed subsets (where rare means having a closure with empty interior), so some subset ` M `` k ` must have a nonempty interior. Theorem 4.7-2 of [Kreyszig] p. 247. (The terminology "meager" and "nonmeager" is used by Kreyszig to replace Baire's "of the first category" and "of the second category." The latter terms are going out of favor to avoid confusion with category theory.) See ~ bcthlem5 for an overview of the proof. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcth {k: setvar} (D J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) (wne (cfv (cuni (crn M)) (cfv J cnt)) c0)) (wrex k (wne (cfv (cfv (cv k) M) (cfv J cnt)) c0) cn) $;

--| Baire's Category Theorem, version 2: If countably many closed sets cover ` X ` , then one of them has an interior. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcth2 {k: setvar} (D J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wne X c0)) (wa (wf cn (cfv J ccld) M) (wceq (cuni (crn M)) X))) (wrex k (wne (cfv (cfv (cv k) M) (cfv J cnt)) c0) cn) $;

--| Baire's Category Theorem, version 3: The intersection of countably many dense open sets is dense. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jan-2014.)
theorem bcth3 {k: setvar} (D J M X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wf cn J M) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) M) (cfv J ccl)) X) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cint (crn M)) (cfv J ccl)) X) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of complete metric spaces.
term ccms: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of Banach spaces.
term cbn: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of subcomplex Hilbert spaces.
term chl: class;

--| Define the class of complete metric spaces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_cms {w b: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccms (crab w (wsbc b (wcel (cres (cfv (cv w) cds) (cxp (cv b) (cv b))) (cfv (cv b) ccmet)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)) cms) $;

--| Define the class of all Banach spaces. A Banach space is a normed vector space such that both the vector space and the scalar field are complete under their respective norm-induced metrics. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
axiom df_bn {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cbn (crab w (wcel (cfv (cv w) csca) ccms) (cin cnvc ccms)) $;

--| Define the class of all subcomplex Hilbert spaces. A subcomplex Hilbert space is a Banach space which is also an inner product space over a subfield of the field of complex numbers closed under square roots of nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Apr-2007.)
axiom df_hl:
  $ wceq chl (cin cbn ccph) $;

--| A Banach space is a normed vector space with a complete induced metric. (Contributed by NM, 5-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem isbn (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W cbn) (w3a (wcel W cnvc) (wcel W ccms) (wcel F ccms)) $;

--| The scalar field of a Banach space is complete. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bnsca (F W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel F ccms) $;

--| A Banach space is a normed vector space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bnnvc (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel W cnvc) $;

--| A Banach space is a normed module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bnnlm (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel W cnlm) $;

--| A Banach space is a normed group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bnngp (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel W cngp) $;

--| A Banach space is a left module. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bnlmod (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel W clmod) $;

--| A Banach space is a complete metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bncms (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W cbn) (wcel W ccms) $;

--| A complete metric space is a metric space with a complete metric. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem iscms (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel M ccms) (wa (wcel M cms) (wcel D (cfv X ccmet))) $;

--| The induced metric on a complete normed group is complete. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmscmet (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M ccms) (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $;

--| The induced metric on Banach space is complete. (Contributed by NM, 8-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem bncmet (D M X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv M cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cbn) (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) $;

--| A complete metric space is a metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmsms (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G ccms) (wcel G cms) $;

--| Property deduction for a complete metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmspropd (ph: wff) (B2 K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv K cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv L cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cfv K cds) (cxp B2 B2)) (cres (cfv L cds) (cxp B2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K ctopn) (cfv L ctopn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel K ccms) (wcel L ccms)) $;

--| The restriction of a metric space is closed if it is complete. (Contributed by AV, 9-Oct-2022.)
theorem cmssmscld (A2 J K M X: class):
  $ wceq K (co M A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv M ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cms) (wss A2 X) (wcel K ccms)) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| The restriction of a complete metric space is complete iff it is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cmsss (A2 J K M X: class):
  $ wceq K (co M A2 cress) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv M ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M ccms) (wss A2 X)) (wb (wcel K ccms) (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| A subspace of a Banach space is a Banach space iff it is closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem lssbn (S U J W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W cbn) (wcel U S)) (wb (wcel X cbn) (wcel U (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| If the uniform set of a complete metric space is the uniform structure generated by its metric, then it is a complete uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem cmetcusp1 (D U F X: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv F cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv F cuss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wne X c0) (wcel F ccms) (wceq U (cfv D cmetu))) (wcel F ccusp) $;

--| The uniform space generated by a complete metric is a complete uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Dec-2017.)
theorem cmetcusp (D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne X c0) (wcel D (cfv X ccmet))) (wcel (cfv (cfv D cmetu) ctus) ccusp) $;

--| The field of complex numbers is a complete metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cncms:
  $ wcel ccnfld ccms $;

--| The uniform structure of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem cnflduss (U: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv ccnfld cuss) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cmetu) $;

--| The field of complex numbers is a complete uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem cnfldcusp:
  $ wcel ccnfld ccusp $;

--| The real numbers are a subset of any complete subfield in the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem resscdrg (F K: class):
  $ wceq F (co ccnfld K cress) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wcel F cdr) (wcel F ccms)) (wss cr K) $;

--| The only complete subfields of the complex numbers are ` RR ` and ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem cncdrg (F K: class):
  $ wceq F (co ccnfld K cress) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wcel F cdr) (wcel F ccms)) (wcel K (cpr cr cc)) $;

--| The subring algebra over a complete normed ring is a Banach space iff the subring is a closed division ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem srabn (A2 S J W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv S (cfv W csra)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W cnrg) (wcel W ccms) (wcel S (cfv W csubrg))) (wb (wcel A2 cbn) (wa (wcel S (cfv J ccld)) (wcel (co W S cress) cdr))) $;

--| The ring module over a complete normed division ring is a Banach space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem rlmbn (R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cdr) (wcel R ccms)) (wcel (cfv R crglmod) cbn) $;

--| The predicate "is a subcomplex Hilbert space". A Hilbert space is a Banach space which is also an inner product space, i.e. whose norm satisfies the parallelogram law. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem ishl (W: class):
  $ wb (wcel W chl) (wa (wcel W cbn) (wcel W ccph)) $;

--| Every subcomplex Hilbert space is a Banach space. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Apr-2007.)
theorem hlbn (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel W cbn) $;

--| Every subcomplex Hilbert space is a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlcph (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel W ccph) $;

--| Every subcomplex Hilbert space is an inner product space (also called a pre-Hilbert space). (Contributed by NM, 28-Apr-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlphl (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel W cphl) $;

--| Every subcomplex Hilbert space is a complete metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlcms (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel W ccms) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hlpr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlprlem (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (w3a (wcel K (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wcel (co ccnfld K cress) cdr) (wcel (co ccnfld K cress) ccms)) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex Hilbert space contains ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlress (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wss cr K) $;

--| The scalar field of a subcomplex Hilbert space is either ` RR ` or ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlpr (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel K (cpr cr cc)) $;

--| A Hilbert space is a complete subcomplex pre-Hilbert space over ` RR ` or ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem ishl2 (F K W: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W chl) (w3a (wcel W ccms) (wcel W ccph) (wcel K (cpr cr cc))) $;

--| A Banach subspace of a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a subcomplex Hilbert space. (Contributed by NM, 11-Apr-2008.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2022.)
theorem cphssphl (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel U S) (wcel X cbn)) (wcel X chl) $;

--| A complete linear subspace of a normed vector space is a Banach space. We furthermore have to assume that the field of scalars is complete since this is a requirement in the current definition of Banach spaces ~ df-bn . (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem cmslssbn (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel W cnvc) (wcel (cfv W csca) ccms)) (wa (wcel X ccms) (wcel U S))) (wcel X cbn) $;

--| A closed subspace of a complete metric space which is also a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a complete metric space. Remark: the assumption that the Banach space must be a (subcomplex) pre-Hilbert space is required because the definition of ` ClSubSp ` is based on an inner product. If ` ClSubSp ` was generalized to arbitrary topological spaces (or at least topological modules), this assumption could be omitted. (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem cmscsscms (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel W ccms) (wcel W ccph)) (wcel U S)) (wcel X ccms) $;

--| A closed subspace of a Banach space which is also a subcomplex pre-Hilbert space is a Banach space. Remark: the assumption that the Banach space must be a (subcomplex) pre-Hilbert space is required because the definition of ` ClSubSp ` is based on an inner product. If ` ClSubSp ` was generalized for arbitrary topological spaces, this assuption could be omitted. (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem bncssbn (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel W cbn) (wcel W ccph)) (wcel U S)) (wcel X cbn) $;

--| A complete subspace of a normed vector space with a complete scalar field is a Banach space. Remark: In contrast to ` ClSubSp ` , a complete subspace is defined by "a linear subspace in which all Cauchy sequences converge to a point in the subspace". This is closer to the original, but deprecated definition ` CH ` ( ~ df-ch ) of closed subspaces of a Hilbert space. It may be superseded by ~ cmslssbn . (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2008.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2022.)
theorem cssbn (D S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv W cds) (cxp U U)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel W cnvc) (wcel (cfv W csca) ccms) (wcel U S)) (wss (cfv D ccau) (cdm (cfv (cfv D cmopn) clm)))) (wcel X cbn) $;

--| A complete subspace of a complex pre-Hilbert space is a complex Hilbert space. Remarks: (a) In contrast to ` ClSubSp ` , a complete subspace is defined by "a linear subspace in which all Cauchy sequences converge to a point in the subspace". This is closer to the original, but deprecated definition ` CH ` ( ~ df-ch ) of closed subspaces of a Hilbert space. (b) This theorem does not hold for arbitrary subcomplex (pre-)Hilbert spaces, because the scalar field as restriction of the field of the complex numbers need not be closed. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2008.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2022.)
theorem csschl (D S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv W cds) (cxp U U)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv W csca) ccnfld $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W ccph) (wcel U S) (wss (cfv D ccau) (cdm (cfv (cfv D cmopn) clm)))) (wa (wcel X chl) (wceq (cfv X csca) ccnfld)) $;

--| A complete linear subspace of a subcomplex Hilbert space is a subcomplex Hilbert space. (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem cmslsschl (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W chl) (wcel X ccms) (wcel U S)) (wcel X chl) $;

--| A closed subspace of a subcomplex Hilbert space is a subcomplex Hilbert space. (Contributed by NM, 10-Apr-2008.) (Revised by AV, 8-Oct-2022.)
theorem chlcsschl (S U W X: class):
  $ wceq X (co W U cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W chl) (wcel U S)) (wcel X chl) $;

--| The topology of the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem retopn:
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (cfv crefld ctopn) $;

--| The real numbers form a complete metric space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Nov-2017.)
theorem recms:
  $ wcel crefld ccms $;

--| The Uniform structure of the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2018.)
theorem reust:
  $ wceq (cfv crefld cuss) (cfv (cres (cfv crefld cds) (cxp cr cr)) cmetu) $;

--| The real numbers form a complete uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem recusp:
  $ wcel crefld ccusp $;

--| Extend class notation with generalized real Euclidean spaces.
term crrx: class;

--| Extend class notation with real Euclidean spaces.
term cehl: class;

--| Define the function associating with a set the free real vector space on that set, equipped with the natural inner product and norm. This is the direct sum of copies of the field of real numbers indexed by that set. We call it here a "generalized real Euclidean space", but note that it need not be complete (for instance if the given set is infinite countable). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_rrx {i: setvar}:
  $ wceq crrx (cmpt i cvv (cfv (co crefld (cv i) cfrlm) ctcph)) $;

--| Define a function generating the real Euclidean spaces of finite dimension. The case ` n = 0 ` corresponds to a space of dimension 0, that is, limited to a neutral element (see ~ ehl0 ). Members of this family of spaces are Hilbert spaces, as shown in - ehlhl . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_ehl {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cehl (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo) crrx)) $;

--| Value of the generalized Euclidean space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxval (H I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq H (cfv (co crefld I cfrlm) ctcph)) $;

--| The base of the generalized real Euclidean space is the set of functions with finite support. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 22-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxbase {f2: setvar} (B2: class) (H: class f2) (I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq B2 (crab f2 (wbr (cv f2) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap))) $;

--| Expand the definition of the generalized real Euclidean spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxprds (B2 H I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq H (cfv (co (co crefld (cxp I (csn (cfv cr (cfv crefld csra)))) cprds) B2 cress) ctcph)) $;

--| The inner product of the generalized real Euclidean spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxip {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (B2: class) (H: class x3 f2 g2) (I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cmpo f2 g2 (co cr I cmap) (co cr I cmap) (co crefld (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) cmul)) cgsu)) (cfv H cip)) $;

--| The norm of the generalized real Euclidean spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxnm {x3 f2: setvar} (B2: class) (H: class x3 f2) (I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cmpt f2 B2 (cfv (co crefld (cmpt x3 I (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c2 cexp)) cgsu) csqrt)) (cfv H cnm)) $;

--| Generalized Euclidean real spaces are subcomplex pre-Hilbert spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jun-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 22-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxcph (B2 H I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel H ccph) $;

--| The distance over generalized Euclidean spaces. Compare with ~ df-rrn . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jun-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 20-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxds {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (B2: class) (H: class x3 f2 g2) (I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cfv (co crefld (cmpt x3 I (co (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp)) cgsu) csqrt)) (cfv H cds)) $;

--| The scalar product over generalized Euclidean spaces is the componentwise real number multiplication. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxvsca (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_xb H I J V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv H cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv H cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co A2 X c_xb)) (co A2 (cfv J X) cmul)) $;

--| The result of the addition combined with scalar multiplication in a generalized Euclidean space is defined by its coordinate-wise operations. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxplusgvscavalb {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class i) (C: class) (c_pb c_xb H: class i) (I: class) (V: class i) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv H cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv H cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq Z (co (co A2 X c_xb) (co C Y c_xb) c_pb)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) Z) (co (co A2 (cfv (cv i) X) cmul) (co C (cfv (cv i) Y) cmul) caddc)) I)) $;

--| The field of real numbers is the scalar field of the generalized real Euclidean space. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxsca (H I V: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv H csca) crefld) $;

--| The zero ("origin") in a generalized real Euclidean space. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx0 (H I V c_0: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp I (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (cfv H c0g) c_0) $;

--| The zero ("origin") in a generalized real Euclidean space is an element of its base set. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx0el (P I V c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp I (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel c_0 P) $;

--| Cauchy-Schwarz-Bunjakovsky inequality for R^n. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem csbren {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (csu k A2 (co B2 C cmul)) c2 cexp) (co (csu k A2 (co B2 c2 cexp)) (csu k A2 (co C c2 cexp)) cmul) cle) $;

--| Triangle inequality in R^n. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem trirn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (csu k A2 (co (co B2 C caddc) c2 cexp)) csqrt) (co (cfv (csu k A2 (co B2 c2 cexp)) csqrt) (cfv (csu k A2 (co C c2 cexp)) csqrt) caddc) cle) $;

--| Euclidean vectors as functions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxf {h: setvar} (ph: wff h) (F I: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf I cr F) $;

--| Euclidean vectors are of finite support. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxfsupp {h: setvar} (ph: wff h) (F I: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F cc0 csupp) cfn) $;

--| Support of Euclidean vectors. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxsuppss {h: setvar} (ph: wff h) (F I: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co F cc0 csupp) I) $;

--| Support of the function used for building the distance . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxmvallem {h k: setvar} (F G I V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wss (co (cmpt k I (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp)) cc0 csupp) (cun (co F cc0 csupp) (co G cc0 csupp))) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean metric. Compare with ~ rrnmval . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxmval {h k: setvar} (D: class h k) (F G I V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wceq (co F G D) (cfv (csu k (cun (co F cc0 csupp) (co G cc0 csupp)) (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt)) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean metric. Compare with ~ rrnval . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxmfval {f2 g2 h k: setvar} (D: class h k) (I V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 X X (cfv (csu k (cun (co (cv f2) cc0 csupp) (co (cv g2) cc0 csupp)) (co (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rrxmet . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxmetlem {h k: setvar} (ph: wff h) (A2: class h) (D: class h k) (F G I V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun (co F cc0 csupp) (co G cc0 csupp)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k (cun (co F cc0 csupp) (co G cc0 csupp)) (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp)) (csu k A2 (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp))) $;

--| Euclidean space is a metric space. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem rrxmet {h: setvar} (D: class h) (I V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) $;

--| The distance between two points in Euclidean space is greater than the distance between the projections onto one coordinate. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jul-2019.)
theorem rrxdstprj1 {h: setvar} (A2 D: class h) (F G I: class) (M: class h) (V: class) (X: class h):
  $ wceq X (crab h (wbr (cv h) cc0 cfsupp) (co cr I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wceq M (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp cr cr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel I V) (wcel A2 I)) (wa (wcel F X) (wcel G X))) (wbr (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv A2 G) M) (co F G D) cle) $;

--| The base of the generalized real Euclidean space, when the dimension of the space is finite. This justifies the use of ` ( RR ^m X ) ` for the development of the Lebesgue measure theory for n-dimensional real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem rrxbasefi (ph: wff) (B2 H X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv X crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv H cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (co cr X cmap)) $;

--| The distance over generalized Euclidean spaces. Finite dimensional case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem rrxdsfi {f2 g2 k: setvar} (B2: class f2 g2) (H I: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I cfn) (wceq (cfv H cds) (cmpo f2 g2 B2 B2 (cfv (csu k I (co (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt))) $;

--| Euclidean space is a metric space. Finite dimensional version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem rrxmetfi (D I: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I cfn) (wcel D (cfv (co cr I cmap) cmet)) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean distance function in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxdsfival {k: setvar} (D: class k) (F G I X: class):
  $ wceq X (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv I crrx) cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I cfn) (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wceq (co F G D) (cfv (csu k I (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt)) $;

--| Value of the Euclidean space of dimension ` N ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ehlval (E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv N cehl) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq E (cfv (co cc0 N cfzo) crrx)) $;

--| The base of the Euclidean space is the set of n-tuples of real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2019.)
theorem ehlbase (E N: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv N cehl) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co cr (co cc0 N cfzo) cmap) (cfv E cbs)) $;

--| The base of the Euclidean space of dimension 0 consists only of one element, the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2023.)
theorem ehl0base (E: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv cc0 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv E cbs) (csn c0) $;

--| The Euclidean space of dimension 0 consists of the neutral element only. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2023.)
theorem ehl0 (E c_0: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv cc0 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv E c0g) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv E cbs) (csn c_0) $;

--| The Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of finite dimension. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehleudis {f2 g2 k: setvar} (D E: class f2 g2 k) (I: class) (N: class f2 g2 k) (X: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv N cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 X X (cfv (csu k I (co (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv k) (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt))) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of finite dimension. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehleudisval {k: setvar} (D E: class k) (F G I: class) (N: class k) (X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv N cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wceq (co F G D) (cfv (csu k I (co (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) c2 cexp)) csqrt)) $;

--| The Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of dimension 1. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehl1eudis {f2 g2: setvar} (D E X: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq E (cfv c1 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (csn cc0) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 X X (cfv (co (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv cc0 (cv g2)) cmin) cabs)) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of dimension 1. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehl1eudisval (D E F G X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv c1 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (csn cc0) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wceq (co F G D) (cfv (co (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G) cmin) cabs)) $;

--| The Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehl2eudis {f2 g2: setvar} (D E X: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq E (cfv c2 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpo f2 g2 X X (cfv (co (co (co (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv cc0 (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv g2)) cmin) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| The value of the Euclidean distance function in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2023.)
theorem ehl2eudisval (D E F G X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv c2 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F X) (wcel G X)) (wceq (co F G D) (cfv (co (co (co (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G) cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv c1 F) (cfv c1 G) cmin) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The set of all distances from points of ` Y ` to ` A ` are a nonempty set of nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem1 {y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wss R cr) (wne R c0) (wral w (wbr cc0 (cv w) cle) R)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The infimum of the distances to ` A ` is a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minveclem4c {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . Any two points ` K ` and ` L ` in ` Y ` are close to each other if they are close to the infimum of distance to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minveclem2 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (D R S U J K L c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 K D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) B2 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 L D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) B2 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co K L D) c2 cexp) (co c4 B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . ` D ` is a complete metric when restricted to ` Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem3a {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R S U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres D (cxp Y Y)) (cfv Y ccmet)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The set of vectors within a fixed distance of the infimum forms a filter base. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minveclem3b {y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (R: class r) (S: class) (U F: class r) (J: class) (c_mi N: class r) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt r crp (crab y1 (wbr (co (co A2 (cv y1) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) (cv r) caddc) cle) Y))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv Y cfbas)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The filter formed by taking elements successively closer to the infimum is Cauchy. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem3 {y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (R: class r) (S: class) (U F: class r) (J: class) (c_mi N: class r) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt r crp (crab y1 (wbr (co (co A2 (cv y1) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) (cv r) caddc) cle) Y))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co Y F cfg) (cfv (cres D (cxp Y Y)) ccfil)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . ` F ` converges to a point ` P ` in ` Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem4a {y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (P R: class r) (S: class) (U F: class r) (J: class) (c_mi N: class r) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt r crp (crab y1 (wbr (co (co A2 (cv y1) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) (cv r) caddc) cle) Y))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cuni (co J (co X F cfg) cflim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cin (co J (co X F cfg) cflim) Y)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The convergent point of the Cauchy sequence ` F ` is a member of the base space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem4b {y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (P R: class r) (S: class) (U F: class r) (J: class) (c_mi N: class r) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt r crp (crab y1 (wbr (co (co A2 (cv y1) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) (cv r) caddc) cle) Y))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cuni (co J (co X F cfg) cflim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . The convergent point of the Cauchy sequence ` F ` attains the minimum distance, and so is closer to ` A ` than any other point in ` Y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minveclem4 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (P R: class r) (S: class) (T: class x3) (U F: class r) (J: class) (c_mi N: class r) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt r crp (crab y1 (wbr (co (co A2 (cv y1) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) (cv r) caddc) cle) Y))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cuni (co J (co X F cfg) cflim)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (co (co (co A2 P D) S caddc) c2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co S c2 cexp) cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N) cle) Y) Y) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . Discharge the assumptions in ~ minveclem4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R S U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N) cle) Y) Y) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . Any minimal point is less than ` S ` away from ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minveclem6 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R S U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Y)) (wb (wbr (co (co A2 (cv x3) D) c2 cexp) (co (co S c2 cexp) cc0 caddc) cle) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N) cle) Y)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ minvec . Since any two minimal points are distance zero away from each other, the minimal point is unique. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem minveclem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D R S U J c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cmpt y1 Y (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv U cds) (cxp X X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N) cle) Y) Y) $;

--| Minimizing vector theorem, or the Hilbert projection theorem. There is exactly one vector in a complete subspace ` W ` that minimizes the distance to an arbitrary vector ` A ` in a parent inner product space. Theorem 3.3-1 of [Kreyszig] p. 144, specialized to subspaces instead of convex subsets. (Contributed by NM, 11-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem minvec {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 U c_mi N X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U cnm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U ccph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv U clss)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co U Y cress) ccms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) c_mi) N) cle) Y) Y) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pjth . (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Jul-2022.)
theorem pjthlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (c_pl: class x3) (T U: class) (c_xi L: class x3) (c_mi N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W chl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cfv A2 N) (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) c_mi) N) cle) U) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co A2 B2 c_xi) (co (co B2 B2 c_xi) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 c_xi) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pjth . (Contributed by NM, 10-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-May-2014.)
theorem pjthlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 c_pl c_po U c_xi J L c_mi N O V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv W cnm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv W csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xi (cfv W cip) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W chl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv W clsm) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W cocv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co U (cfv U O) c_po)) $;

--| Projection Theorem: Any Hilbert space vector ` A ` can be decomposed uniquely into a member ` x ` of a closed subspace ` H ` and a member ` y ` of the complement of the subspace. Theorem 3.7(i) of [Beran] p. 102 (existence part). (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-May-2014.)
theorem pjth (c_po U J L O V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv W clsm) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W cocv) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W chl) (wcel U L) (wcel U (cfv J ccld))) (wceq (co U (cfv U O) c_po) V) $;

--| Projection Theorem with abbreviations: A topologically closed subspace is a projection subspace. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem pjth2 (U J K L W: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv W cpj) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W chl) (wcel U L) (wcel U (cfv J ccld))) (wcel U (cdm K)) $;

--| Corollary of the Projection Theorem: A topologically closed subspace is algebraically closed in Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem cldcss (C U J L V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wb (wcel U C) (wa (wcel U L) (wcel U (cfv J ccld)))) $;

--| Corollary of the Projection Theorem: A topologically closed subspace is algebraically closed in Hilbert space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem cldcss2 (C J L V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv W ccss) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wceq C (cin L (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| Corollary of the Projection Theorem: A subcomplex Hilbert space is a Hilbert space (in the algebraic sense, meaning that all algebraically closed subspaces have a projection decomposition). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Oct-2015.)
theorem hlhil (W: class):
  $ wi (wcel W chl) (wcel W chil) $;

--| The addition of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem addcncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 caddc)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| The subtraction of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem subcncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cmin)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| The multiplication of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem mulcncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cmul)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| The quotient of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem divcncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X A2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X (cdif cc (csn cc0)) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cdiv)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pmltpc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2014.)
theorem pmltpclem1 {a b c: setvar} (ph: wff a b c) (A2: class) (B2: class a) (C: class a b) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) clt) (wbr (cfv C F) (cfv B2 F) clt)) (wa (wbr (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) clt) (wbr (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F) clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (w3a (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wbr (cv b) (cv c) clt) (wo (wa (wbr (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt)) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv a) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv c) F) clt)))) S) S) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pmltpc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2014.)
theorem pmltpclem2 {a b c: setvar} (ph: wff a b c) (A2 U F: class) (V: class a) (W: class) (X: class a):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cr cr cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr U V cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr W X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv U F) (cfv V F) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv X F) (cfv W F) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (w3a (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wbr (cv b) (cv c) clt) (wo (wa (wbr (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt)) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv a) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv c) F) clt)))) A2) A2) A2) $;

--| Any function on the reals is either increasing, decreasing, or has a triple of points in a vee formation. (This theorem was created on demand by Mario Carneiro for the 6PCM conference in Bialystok, 1-Jul-2014.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2014.)
theorem pmltpc {x3 y1 a b c: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co cr cr cpm)) (wss A2 (cdm F))) (w3o (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle)) A2) A2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle)) A2) A2) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (w3a (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wbr (cv b) (cv c) clt) (wo (wa (wbr (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv c) F) (cfv (cv b) F) clt)) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv a) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv c) F) clt)))) A2) A2) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ivth . The set ` S ` of all ` x ` values with ` ( F `` x ) ` less than ` U ` is lower bounded by ` A ` and upper bounded by ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ivthlem1 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z) (B2 D S U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) U clt) (wbr U (cfv B2 F) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) U cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 S) (wral z (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ivth . Show that the supremum of ` S ` cannot be less than ` U ` . If it was, continuity of ` F ` implies that there are points just above the supremum that are also less than ` U ` , a contradiction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ivthlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D S U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) U clt) (wbr U (cfv B2 F) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) U cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq C (csup S cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv C F) U clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ivth , the intermediate value theorem. Show that ` ( F `` C ) ` cannot be greater than ` U ` , and so establish the existence of a root of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ivthlem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D S U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) U clt) (wbr U (cfv B2 F) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) U cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq C (csup S cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wceq (cfv C F) U)) $;

--| The intermediate value theorem, increasing case. This is Metamath 100 proof #79. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jan-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.)
theorem ivth {x3 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) U clt) (wbr U (cfv B2 F) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv c) F) U) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| The intermediate value theorem, decreasing case. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.)
theorem ivth2 {x3 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv B2 F) U clt) (wbr U (cfv A2 F) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv c) F) U) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| The intermediate value theorem with weak inequality, increasing case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem ivthle {x3 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv A2 F) U cle) (wbr U (cfv B2 F) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv c) F) U) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| The intermediate value theorem with weak inequality, decreasing case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem ivthle2 {x3 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv B2 F) U cle) (wbr U (cfv A2 F) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv c) F) U) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| The interval between any two points of a continuous real function is contained in the range of the function. Equivalently, the range of a continuous real function is convex. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem ivthicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cfv M F) (cfv N F) cicc) (crn F)) $;

--| Specialization of the Extreme Value Theorem to a closed interval of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem evthicc {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wrex z (wral w (wbr (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv w) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| Specialization of the Extreme Value Theorem to a closed interval of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem evthicc_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| Specialization of the Extreme Value Theorem to a closed interval of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem evthicc_b1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wrex z (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| Combine ~ ivthicc with ~ evthicc to exactly describe the image of a closed interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem evthicc2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (crn F) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc)) cr) cr) $;

--| A continuous function on a closed interval is bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem cniccbdd {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf))) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cr) $;

--| Extend class notation with the outer Lebesgue measure.
term covol: class;

--| Extend class notation with the Lebesgue measure.
term cvol: class;

--| Define the outer Lebesgue measure for subsets of the reals. Here ` f ` is a function from the positive integers to pairs ` <. a , b >. ` with ` a <_ b ` , and the outer volume of the set ` x ` is the infimum over all such functions such that the union of the open intervals ` ( a , b ) ` covers ` x ` of the sum of ` b - a ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_ovol {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq covol (cmpt x3 (cpw cr) (cinf (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss (cv x3) (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) cxr clt)) $;

--| Define the Lebesgue measure, which is just the outer measure with a peculiar domain of definition. The property of being Lebesgue-measurable can be expressed as ` A e. dom vol ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
axiom df_vol {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvol (cres covol (cab x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) covol) (co (cfv (cin (cv y1) (cv x3)) covol) (cfv (cdif (cv y1) (cv x3)) covol) caddc)) (cima (ccnv covol) cr)))) $;

--| Closure for the interval endpoint function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolfcl (F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wcel N cn)) (w3a (wcel (cfv (cfv N F) c1st) cr) (wcel (cfv (cfv N F) c2nd) cr) (wbr (cfv (cfv N F) c1st) (cfv (cfv N F) c2nd) cle)) $;

--| Unpack the interval covering property of the outer measure definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolfioo {z n: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F)) (wb (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) (wral z (wrex n (wa (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cv z) (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) clt)) cn) A2)) $;

--| Unpack the interval covering property using closed intervals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolficc {z n: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F)) (wb (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F)))) (wral z (wrex n (wa (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) (cv z) cle) (wbr (cv z) (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) cle)) cn) A2)) $;

--| Any (closed) interval covering is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovolficcss (F: class):
  $ wi (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wss (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F))) cr) $;

--| The value of the interval length function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolfsval (F G N: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (cfv N G) (co (cfv (cfv N F) c2nd) (cfv (cfv N F) c1st) cmin)) $;

--| Closure for the interval length function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolfsf (F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) $ >
  $ wi (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) G) $;

--| Closure for the partial sums of the interval length function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolsf (S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wi (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) S) $;

--| The value of the outer measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem ovolval {y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (M: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) (cinf M cxr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ elovolm and related theorems. (Contributed by BJ, 23-Jul-2022.)
theorem elovolmlem (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (co (cin A2 (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) (wf cn (cin A2 (cxp cr cr)) F) $;

--| Elementhood in the set ` M ` of approximations to the outer measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem elovolm {y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class f2) (B2: class) (M: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wb (wcel B2 M) (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq B2 (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for elementhood in the set ` M ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem elovolmr {y1 f2: setvar} (A2 S: class) (F: class y1) (M: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))))) (wcel (csup (crn S) cxr clt) M) $;

--| The set ` M ` is composed of nonnegative extended real numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolmge0 {y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class f2) (B2: class) (M: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 M) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $;

--| The volume of a set is an extended real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cxr) $;

--| The outer volume is a lower bound on the sum of all interval coverings of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovollb (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))))) (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The outer volume is the greatest lower bound on the sum of all interval coverings of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolgelb {g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S: class g2):
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv g2)) c1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wrex g2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv g2))))) (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) B2 caddc) cle)) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $;

--| The volume of a set is always nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 covol) cle) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the outer volume function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem ovolf:
  $ wf (cpw cr) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) covol $;

--| If an outer volume is bounded above, then it is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovollecl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr (cfv A2 covol) B2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolss . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem ovolsslem {y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (M N: class y1 f2):
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss B2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 cr)) (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) cle) $;

--| The volume of a set is monotone with respect to set inclusion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 cr)) (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) cle) $;

--| If a set is contained in another of bounded measure, it too is bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolsscl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $;

--| A subset of a nullset is null. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolssnul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 cr) (wceq (cfv B2 covol) cc0)) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovollb2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovollb2lem {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (T: class n) (F: class) (G: class n):
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) (co (co B2 c2 cdiv) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cmin) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) (co (co B2 c2 cdiv) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) caddc))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (co (csup (crn S) cxr clt) B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| It is often more convenient to do calculations with *closed* coverings rather than open ones; here we show that it makes no difference (compare ~ ovollb ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovollb2 (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F))))) (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The volume of a denumerable set is 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovolctb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wbr A2 cn cen)) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $;

--| The rational numbers have 0 outer Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolq:
  $ wceq (cfv cq covol) cc0 $;

--| The volume of a countable set is 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolctb2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wbr A2 cn cdom)) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $;

--| The empty set has 0 outer Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovol0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 covol) cc0 $;

--| A finite set has 0 outer Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ovolfi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss A2 cr)) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $;

--| A singleton has 0 outer Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2014.)
theorem ovolsn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) covol) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem ovolunlem1a {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (S T U: class n) (F G: class) (H: class n):
  $ wi ph (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv B2 covol) (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) cn) (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) G) (cfv (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) U) (co (co (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) caddc) C caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolunlem1 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (S T U: class n) (F G: class) (H: class n):
  $ wi ph (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv B2 covol) (co C c2 cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) cn) (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) G) (cfv (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cun A2 B2) covol) (co (co (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) caddc) C caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolunlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cun A2 B2) covol) (co (co (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) caddc) C caddc) cle) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is finitely sub-additive. (Unlike the stronger ~ ovoliun , this does not require any choice principles.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr)) (wa (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr))) (wbr (cfv (cun A2 B2) covol) (co (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol) caddc) cle) $;

--| Adding a nullset does not change the measure of a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovolunnul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wss B2 cr) (wceq (cfv B2 covol) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) covol) (cfv A2 covol)) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is finitely sub-additive. Finite sum version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolfiniun {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral k (wa (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) A2)) (wbr (cfv (ciun k A2 B2) covol) (csu k A2 (cfv B2 covol)) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovoliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem ovoliunlem1 {w k n: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2: class w n) (B2: class w) (S: class w n) (T: class w) (U: class w k n) (F G: class w) (H: class w k) (J: class) (K: class k) (L: class):
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 covol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cfv (cv n) F)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt k cn (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv k) J) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv k) J) c1st) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cxp cn cn) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cfv (cv n) F))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) (co B2 (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral w (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv w) J) c1st) L cle) (co c1 K cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv K U) (co (csup (crn T) cxr clt) B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovoliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovoliunlem2 {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2: class) (S: class n) (T: class) (U: class k n) (F G: class) (H: class k) (J: class):
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 covol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cfv (cv n) F)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt k cn (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv k) J) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv k) J) c1st) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cxp cn cn) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cfv (cv n) F))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) (co B2 (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (ciun n cn A2) covol) (co (csup (crn T) cxr clt) B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovoliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovoliunlem3 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2 T G: class):
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 covol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (ciun n cn A2) covol) (co (csup (crn T) cxr clt) B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is countably sub-additive. (Many books allow ` +oo ` as a value for one of the sets in the sum, but in our setup we can't do arithmetic on infinity, and in any case the volume of a union containing an infinitely large set is already infinitely large by monotonicity ~ ovolss , so we need not consider this case here, although we do allow the sum itself to be infinite.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovoliun {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 T G: class n):
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 covol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (ciun n cn A2) covol) (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is countably sub-additive. (This version is a little easier to read, but does not allow infinite values like ~ ovoliun .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovoliun2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 T G: class n):
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 covol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (ciun n cn A2) covol) (csu n cn (cfv A2 covol)) cle) $;

--| A countable union of nullsets is null. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2015.)
theorem ovoliunnul {n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class n):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 cn cdom) (wral n (wa (wss B2 cr) (wceq (cfv B2 covol) cc0)) A2)) (wceq (cfv (ciun n A2 B2) covol) cc0) $;

--| If ` B ` is a shift of ` A ` by ` C ` , then ` A ` is a shift of ` B ` by ` -u C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem shft2rab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) C cmin) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (crab y1 (wcel (co (cv y1) (cneg C) cmin) B2) cr)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolshft . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolshftlem1 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (S: class x3 y1 f2 n) (F: class y1 f2) (G: class x3) (M: class x3 y1 f2 n):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) C cmin) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss B2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) C caddc) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) C caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn S) cxr clt) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolshft . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolshftlem2 {x3 y1 z f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (M: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) C cmin) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss B2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crab z (wrex g2 (wa (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv g2))))) (wceq (cv z) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv g2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) M) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is shift-invariant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem ovolshft {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) C cmin) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) (cfv B2 covol)) $;

--| If ` B ` is a scale of ` A ` by ` C ` , then ` A ` is a scale of ` B ` by ` 1 / C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.)
theorem sca2rab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co C (cv x3) cmul) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (crab y1 (wcel (co (co c1 C cdiv) (cv y1) cmul) B2) cr)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolsca . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem ovolscalem1 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (R S: class n) (F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co C (cv x3) cmul) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) C cdiv) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) C cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv A2 covol) (co C R cmul) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 covol) (co (co (cfv A2 covol) C cdiv) R caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolshft . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.)
theorem ovolscalem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co C (cv x3) cmul) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 covol) (co (cfv A2 covol) C cdiv) cle) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function respects scaling of sets by positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem ovolsca {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (crab x3 (wcel (co C (cv x3) cmul) A2) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 covol) (co (cfv A2 covol) C cdiv)) $;

--| The measure of a closed interval is lower bounded by its length. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovolicc1 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cif (wceq (cv n) c1) (cop A2 B2) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) covol) (co B2 A2 cmin) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolicc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc2lem1 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (P S: class t) (U F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cin (cpw (crn (ccom cioo F))) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf U cn G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) (ccom cioo F)) (cv t)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X U)) (wb (wcel P X) (w3a (wcel P cr) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv X G) F) c1st) P clt) (wbr P (cfv (cfv (cfv X G) F) c2nd) clt))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolicc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc2lem2 {u t n: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2 B2: class) (C: class u) (S: class u t n) (T: class u) (U: class) (F G: class u) (H: class u n) (K N: class) (W: class u t):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cin (cpw (crn (ccom cioo F))) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf U cn G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) (ccom cioo F)) (cv t)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab u (wne (cin (cv u) (co A2 B2 cicc)) c0) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T T H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wcel (cif (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2 cle) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2) (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wceq K (cseq (ccom H c1st) (cxp cn (csn C)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab n (wcel B2 (cfv (cv n) K)) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel N cn) (wn (wcel N W)))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv N K) G) F) c2nd) B2 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolicc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc2lem3 {u t m n: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2 B2: class) (C: class u) (P S: class u t m n) (T: class u m) (U: class m) (F G: class u m) (H: class u m n) (K N: class m) (W: class u t):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cin (cpw (crn (ccom cioo F))) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf U cn G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) (ccom cioo F)) (cv t)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab u (wne (cin (cv u) (co A2 B2 cicc)) c0) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T T H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wcel (cif (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2 cle) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2) (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wceq K (cseq (ccom H c1st) (cxp cn (csn C)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab n (wcel B2 (cfv (cv n) K)) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel N (crab n (wral m (wbr (cv n) (cv m) cle) W) cn)) (wcel P (crab n (wral m (wbr (cv n) (cv m) cle) W) cn)))) (wb (wceq N P) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv N K) G) F) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv P K) G) F) c2nd))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolicc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.) (Revised by AV, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem ovolicc2lem4 {u t n: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2 B2: class) (C: class u) (S: class u t n) (T: class u) (U: class) (F G: class u) (H: class u n) (K: class) (M: class u) (W: class u t):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cin (cpw (crn (ccom cioo F))) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf U cn G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) (ccom cioo F)) (cv t)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab u (wne (cin (cv u) (co A2 B2 cicc)) c0) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T T H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wcel (cif (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2 cle) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) F) c2nd) B2) (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wceq K (cseq (ccom H c1st) (cxp cn (csn C)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab n (wcel B2 (cfv (cv n) K)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq M (cinf W cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmin) (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ovolicc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc2lem5 {u t: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2 B2: class) (S: class u t) (T: class u) (U: class) (F G: class u):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cin (cpw (crn (ccom cioo F))) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf U cn G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) U)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv t) G) (ccom cioo F)) (cv t)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab u (wne (cin (cv u) (co A2 B2 cicc)) c0) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmin) (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The measure of a closed interval is upper bounded by its length. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc2 {y1 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (M: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq M (crab y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cuni (crn (ccom cioo (cv f2))))) (wceq (cv y1) (csup (crn (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) (cv f2)) c1)) cxr clt))) (co (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) cn cmap)) cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) covol) cle) $;

--| The measure of a closed interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) covol) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| The measure of a right-unbounded interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolicopnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (co A2 cpnf cico) covol) cpnf) $;

--| The measure of the real numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem ovolre:
  $ wceq (cfv cr covol) cpnf $;

--| The predicate " ` A ` is Lebesgue-measurable". A set is measurable if it splits every other set ` x ` in a "nice" way, that is, if the measure of the pieces ` x i^i A ` and ` x \ A ` sum up to the measure of ` x ` (assuming that the measure of ` x ` is a real number, so that this addition makes sense). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem ismbl {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wa (wss A2 cr) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv x3) covol) cr) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) covol) (co (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) covol) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) A2) covol) caddc))) (cpw cr))) $;

--| From ~ ovolun , it suffices to show that the measure of ` x ` is at least the sum of the measures of ` x i^i A ` and ` x \ A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbl2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wa (wss A2 cr) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cfv (cv x3) covol) cr) (wbr (co (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) covol) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) A2) covol) caddc) (cfv (cv x3) covol) cle)) (cpw cr))) $;

--| A self-referencing abbreviated definition of the Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2014.)
theorem volres:
  $ wceq cvol (cres covol (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the Lebesgue measure function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2014.)
theorem volf:
  $ wf (cdm cvol) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cvol $;

--| The volume of a measurable set is the same as its outer volume. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem mblvol (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wceq (cfv A2 cvol) (cfv A2 covol)) $;

--| A measurable set is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem mblss (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wss A2 cr) $;

--| The defining property of measurability. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Mar-2014.)
theorem mblsplit (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wss B2 cr) (wcel (cfv B2 covol) cr)) (wceq (cfv B2 covol) (co (cfv (cin B2 A2) covol) (cfv (cdif B2 A2) covol) caddc)) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure is monotone with respect to set inclusion. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Oct-2017.)
theorem volss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) (wss A2 B2)) (wbr (cfv A2 cvol) (cfv B2 cvol) cle) $;

--| The complement of a measurable set is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem cmmbl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cdif cr A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A nullset is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem nulmbl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0)) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A set of outer measure zero is measurable. The term "outer measure zero" here is slightly different from "nullset/negligible set"; a nullset has ` vol* ( A ) = 0 ` while "outer measure zero" means that for any ` x ` there is a ` y ` containing ` A ` with volume less than ` x ` . Assuming AC, these notions are equivalent (because the intersection of all such ` y ` is a nullset) but in ZF this is a strictly weaker notion. Proposition 563Gb of [Fremlin5] p. 193. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem nulmbl2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wss A2 (cv y1)) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) covol) (cv x3) cle)) (cdm cvol)) crp) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A union of measurable sets is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem unmbl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol))) (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A shift of a measurable set is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Mar-2014.)
theorem shftmbl {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) A2) cr) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The empty set is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem _0mbl:
  $ wcel c0 (cdm cvol) $;

--| The set of all real numbers is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem rembl:
  $ wcel cr (cdm cvol) $;

--| The union of the Lebesgue measurable sets is ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem unidmvol:
  $ wceq (cuni (cdm cvol)) cr $;

--| An intersection of measurable sets is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem inmbl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol))) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A difference of measurable sets is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem difmbl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol))) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A finite union of measurable sets is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem finiunmbl {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral k (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) A2)) (wcel (ciun k A2 B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure function is finitely additive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem volun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wa (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel (cfv B2 cvol) cr))) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) cvol) (co (cfv A2 cvol) (cfv B2 cvol) caddc)) $;

--| Addition of non-disjoint sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem volinun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol))) (wa (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel (cfv B2 cvol) cr))) (wceq (co (cfv A2 cvol) (cfv B2 cvol) caddc) (co (cfv (cin A2 B2) cvol) (cfv (cun A2 B2) cvol) caddc)) $;

--| The volume of a disjoint finite union of measurable sets is the sum of the measures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem volfiniun {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wral k (wa (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv B2 cvol) cr)) A2) (wdisj k A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv (ciun k A2 B2) cvol) (csu k A2 (cfv B2 cvol))) $;

--| Rewrite a countable union as a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem iundisj {k n: setvar} (A2: class n) (B2: class k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv k)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (ciun n cn A2) (ciun n cn (cdif A2 (ciun k (co c1 (cv n) cfzo) B2))) $;

--| A disjoint union is disjoint. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem iundisj2 {k n: setvar} (A2: class n) (B2: class k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv k)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wdisj n cn (cdif A2 (ciun k (co c1 (cv n) cfzo) B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ voliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem voliunlem1 {i k n: setvar} (ph: wff i) (E: class i) (F: class) (H: class i n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm cvol) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj i cn (cfv (cv i) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (cin E (cfv (cv n) F)) covol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) (cseq caddc H c1)) (cfv (cdif E (cuni (crn F))) covol) caddc) (cfv E covol) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ voliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem voliunlem2 {x3 i n: setvar} (ph: wff i) (F: class) (H: class x3 i n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm cvol) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj i cn (cfv (cv i) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (cin (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) covol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (crn F)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ voliun . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Mar-2014.)
theorem voliunlem3 {x3 i n: setvar} (ph: wff i) (S: class i n) (F: class) (G H: class x3 i n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm cvol) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj i cn (cfv (cv i) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (cin (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) covol)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv i) F) cvol) cr) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cuni (crn F)) cvol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt)) $;

--| The measurable sets are closed under countable union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem iunmbl {n: setvar} (A2: class n):
  $ wi (wral n (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) cn) (wcel (ciun n cn A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure function is countably additive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem voliun {n: setvar} (A2 S G: class n):
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc G c1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cfv A2 cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wral n (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr)) cn) (wdisj n cn A2)) (wceq (cfv (ciun n cn A2) cvol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ volsup . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem volsuplem {n: setvar} (A2 B2: class n) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wral n (wss (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F)) cn) (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)))) (wss (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| The volume of the limit of an increasing sequence of measurable sets is the limit of the volumes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem volsup {n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cdm cvol) F) (wral n (wss (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F)) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cuni (crn F)) cvol) (csup (cima cvol (crn F)) cxr clt)) $;

--| The measurable sets are closed under countable union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem iunmbl2 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class n):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 cn cdom) (wral n (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) A2)) (wcel (ciun n A2 B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ioombl1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem ioombl1lem1 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2 C: class) (P Q: class n) (S: class) (T U: class n) (E F G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q) Q)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cop P (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) H)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ioombl1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem ioombl1lem2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2 C: class) (P Q: class n) (S: class) (T U: class n) (E F G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q) Q)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cop P (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn S) cxr clt) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ioombl1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Aug-2014.)
theorem ioombl1lem3 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2 C: class) (P Q: class n) (S: class) (T U: class n) (E F G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q) Q)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cop P (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wceq (co (cfv (cv n) (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G)) (cfv (cv n) (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H)) caddc) (cfv (cv n) (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ioombl1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem ioombl1lem4 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2 C: class) (P Q: class n) (S: class) (T U: class n) (E F G H: class):
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) H) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn S) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c1st) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) c2nd) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cop (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q) Q)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cop P (cif (wbr (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q cle) (cif (wbr P A2 cle) A2 P) Q))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E B2) covol) (cfv (cdif E B2) covol) caddc) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $;

--| An open right-unbounded interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem ioombl1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel (co A2 cpnf cioo) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A closed unbounded-above interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem icombl1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co A2 cpnf cico) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A closed-below, open-above real interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem icombl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cico) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| An open real interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem ioombl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (co A2 B2 cioo) (cdm cvol) $;

--| A closed real interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem iccmbl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cicc) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A closed real interval has finite volume. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iccvolcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) cvol) cr) $;

--| The measure of an open interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem ovolioo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) covol) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| The measure of an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem volioo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) cvol) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| An open real interval has finite volume. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem ioovolcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) cvol) cr) $;

--| Alternative expression for the interval length function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem ovolfs2 (F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) $ >
  $ wi (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) (wceq G (ccom (ccom covol cioo) F)) $;

--| An open interval with finite volume has real endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem ioorcl2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne (co A2 B2 cioo) c0) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) covol) cr)) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) $;

--| Define a function from open intervals to their endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem ioorf {x3: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wf (crn cioo) (cin cle (cxp cxr cxr)) F $;

--| Define a function from open intervals to their endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem ioorval {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (crn cioo)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cif (wceq A2 c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf A2 cxr clt) (csup A2 cxr clt)))) $;

--| The function ` F ` is an "inverse" of sorts to the open interval function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem ioorinv2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wi (wne (co A2 B2 cioo) c0) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) F) (cop A2 B2)) $;

--| The function ` F ` is an "inverse" of sorts to the open interval function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem ioorinv {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (crn cioo)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 F) cioo) A2) $;

--| The function ` F ` does not always return real numbers, but it does on intervals of finite volume. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem ioorcl {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (crn cioo)) (wcel (cfv A2 covol) cr)) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (cin cle (cxp cr cr))) $;

--| A union of closed intervals differs from the equivalent union of open intervals by a nullset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniiccdif (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F)))) (wceq (cfv (cdif (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F))) (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F)))) covol) cc0)) $;

--| A disjoint union of open intervals has volume equal to the sum of the volume of the intervals. (This proof does not use countable choice, unlike ~ voliun .) Lemma 565Ca of [Fremlin5] p. 213. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioovol {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) covol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt)) $;

--| An almost-disjoint union of closed intervals (disjoint interiors) has volume equal to the sum of the volume of the intervals. (This proof does not use countable choice, unlike ~ voliun .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniiccvol {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F))) covol) (csup (crn S) cxr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3) (T: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem2a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (z: setvar) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3) (T: class) (E: class x3) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel J cn)) (wcel (cv z) cn)) (wcel (cin (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) cioo) (cfv (cfv J G) cioo)) (crn cioo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem uniioombllem2 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3 z) (T: class) (E: class x3 z) (F G H J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt z cn (cin (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) cioo) (cfv (cfv J G) cioo))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (crn cioo) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c0) (cop cc0 cc0) (cop (cinf (cv x3) cxr clt) (csup (cv x3) cxr clt)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel J cn)) (wbr (cseq caddc (ccom covol H) c1) (cfv (cin (cfv (cfv J G) cioo) A2) covol) cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem3a {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3 j) (T: class) (E: class x3 j) (F G K M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv M T) (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cmin) cabs) C clt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cuni (cima (ccom cioo G) (co c1 M cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq K (ciun j (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cfv (cv j) G) cioo))) (wcel (cfv K covol) cr)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3) (T: class) (E: class x3) (F G K M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv M T) (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cmin) cabs) C clt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cuni (cima (ccom cioo G) (co c1 M cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E A2) covol) (cfv (cdif E A2) covol) caddc) (co (co (cfv (cin K A2) covol) (cfv (cdif K A2) covol) caddc) (co C C caddc) caddc) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem4 {x3 i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i) (C: class) (S: class x3 i j) (T: class) (E: class x3 i j) (F G: class) (K: class i) (L: class x3 i j) (M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv M T) (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cmin) cabs) C clt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cuni (cima (ccom cioo G) (co c1 M cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral j (wbr (cfv (co (csu i (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cin (cfv (cfv (cv i) F) cioo) (cfv (cfv (cv j) G) cioo)) covol)) (cfv (cin (cfv (cfv (cv j) G) cioo) A2) covol) cmin) cabs) (co C M cdiv) clt) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cuni (cima (ccom cioo F) (co c1 N cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cin K A2) covol) (co (cfv (cin K L) covol) C caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem uniioombllem5 {x3 i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i) (C: class) (S: class x3 i j) (T: class) (E: class x3 i j) (F G: class) (K: class i) (L: class x3 i j) (M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv M T) (csup (crn T) cxr clt) cmin) cabs) C clt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cuni (cima (ccom cioo G) (co c1 M cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral j (wbr (cfv (co (csu i (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cin (cfv (cfv (cv i) F) cioo) (cfv (cfv (cv j) G) cioo)) covol)) (cfv (cin (cfv (cfv (cv j) G) cioo) A2) covol) cmin) cabs) (co C M cdiv) clt) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cuni (cima (ccom cioo F) (co c1 N cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E A2) covol) (cfv (cdif E A2) covol) caddc) (co (cfv E covol) (co c4 C cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uniioombl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioombllem6 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C: class) (S: class x3) (T: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv E covol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (cuni (crn (ccom cioo G)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) G) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (crn T) cxr clt) (co (cfv E covol) C caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E A2) covol) (cfv (cdif E A2) covol) caddc) (co (cfv E covol) (co c4 C cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| A disjoint union of open intervals is measurable. (This proof does not use countable choice, unlike ~ iunmbl .) Lemma 565Ca of [Fremlin5] p. 214. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniioombl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (crn (ccom cioo F))) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| An almost-disjoint union of closed intervals is measurable. (This proof does not use countable choice, unlike ~ iunmbl .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem uniiccmbl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cseq caddc (ccom (ccom cabs cmin) F) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (crn (ccom cicc F))) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The function ` F ` returns the endpoints of a dyadic rational covering of the real line. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadf {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wf (cxp cz cn0) (cin cle (cxp cr cr)) F $;

--| Value of the dyadic rational function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) (cop (co A2 (co c2 B2 cexp) cdiv) (co (co A2 c1 caddc) (co c2 B2 cexp) cdiv))) $;

--| Volume of a dyadic rational interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadovol {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co A2 B2 F) cicc) covol) (co c1 (co c2 B2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Two closed dyadic rational intervals are either in a subset relationship or are almost disjoint (the interiors are disjoint). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2016.)
theorem dyadss {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel C cn0) (wcel D cn0))) (wi (wss (cfv (co A2 C F) cicc) (cfv (co B2 D F) cicc)) (wbr D C cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dyaddisj . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyaddisjlem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel C cn0) (wcel D cn0))) (wbr C D cle)) (w3o (wss (cfv (co A2 C F) cicc) (cfv (co B2 D F) cicc)) (wss (cfv (co B2 D F) cicc) (cfv (co A2 C F) cicc)) (wceq (cin (cfv (co A2 C F) cioo) (cfv (co B2 D F) cioo)) c0)) $;

--| Two closed dyadic rational intervals are either in a subset relationship or are almost disjoint (the interiors are disjoint). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyaddisj {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (crn F)) (wcel B2 (crn F))) (w3o (wss (cfv A2 cicc) (cfv B2 cicc)) (wss (cfv B2 cicc) (cfv A2 cicc)) (wceq (cin (cfv A2 cioo) (cfv B2 cioo)) c0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dyadmax . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadmaxlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr D C clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv (co A2 C F) cicc) (cfv (co B2 D F) cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) $;

--| Any nonempty set of dyadic rational intervals has a maximal element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadmax {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 (crn F)) (wne A2 c0)) (wrex z (wral w (wi (wss (cfv (cv z) cicc) (cfv (cv w) cicc)) (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) A2) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dyadmbl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadmbllem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class) (G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab z (wral w (wi (wss (cfv (cv z) cicc) (cfv (cv w) cicc)) (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (cima cicc A2)) (cuni (cima cicc G))) $;

--| Any union of dyadic rational intervals is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem dyadmbl {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class) (G: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab z (wral w (wi (wss (cfv (cv z) cicc) (cfv (cv w) cicc)) (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (cima cicc A2)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ opnmbl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem opnmbllem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cz cn0 (cop (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| All open sets are measurable. This proof, via ~ dyadmbl and ~ uniioombl , shows that it is possible to avoid choice for measurability of open sets and hence continuous functions, which extends the choice-free consequences of Lebesgue measure considerably farther than would otherwise be possible. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem opnmbl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| All open sets are measurable. This alternative proof of ~ opnmbl is significantly shorter, at the expense of invoking countable choice ~ ax-cc . (This was also the original proof before the current ~ opnmbl was discovered.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem opnmblALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Sets which are open in a measurable subspace are measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem subopnmbl (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 J)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The volume of ` A ` is the supremum of the sequence ` vol* `` ( A i^i ( -u n [,] n ) ) ` of volumes of bounded sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem volsup2 {n: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr B2 (cfv A2 cvol) clt)) (wrex n (wbr B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co (cneg (cv n)) (cv n) cicc)) cvol) clt) cn) $;

--| The function formed by restricting a measurable set to a closed interval with a varying endpoint produces an increasing continuous function on the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem volcn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (cin A2 (co B2 (cv x3) cicc)) cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel F (co cr cr ccncf)) $;

--| The Intermediate Value Theorem for the Lebesgue volume function. For any positive ` B <_ ( vol `` A ) ` , there is a measurable subset of ` A ` whose volume is ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem volivth {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 (cfv A2 cvol) cicc))) (wrex x3 (wa (wss (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cvol) B2)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vitali . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-May-2021.)
theorem vitalilem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (c_sm: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cq))) $ >
  $ wer (co cc0 c1 cicc) c_sm $;

--| Lemma for ~ vitali . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem vitalilem2 {x3 y1 z m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 s1) (c_sm: class) (S: class x3 y1 m n s1) (T: class y1 z n s1) (F G: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cq))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cqs (co cc0 c1 cicc) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cin cq (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) G) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (crab s1 (wcel (co (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) G) cmin) (crn F)) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (crn F) (cdif (cpw cr) (cdm cvol)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wss (crn F) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wss (co cc0 c1 cicc) (ciun m cn (cfv (cv m) T))) (wss (ciun m cn (cfv (cv m) T)) (co (cneg c1) c2 cicc))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vitali . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem vitalilem3 {x3 y1 z m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 s1) (c_sm: class) (S: class x3 y1 m n s1) (T: class y1 z n s1) (F G: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cq))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cqs (co cc0 c1 cicc) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cin cq (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) G) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (crab s1 (wcel (co (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) G) cmin) (crn F)) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (crn F) (cdif (cpw cr) (cdm cvol)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj m cn (cfv (cv m) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vitali . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem vitalilem4 {x3 y1 z m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 s1) (c_sm: class) (S: class x3 y1 m n s1) (T: class y1 z n s1) (F G: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cq))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cqs (co cc0 c1 cicc) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cin cq (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) G) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (crab s1 (wcel (co (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) G) cmin) (crn F)) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (crn F) (cdif (cpw cr) (cdm cvol)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) covol) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vitali . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem vitalilem5 {x3 y1 z n s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 s1) (c_sm: class) (S: class x3 y1 n s1) (T: class y1 z n s1) (F G: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cq))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cqs (co cc0 c1 cicc) c_sm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o cn (cin cq (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) G) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (crab s1 (wcel (co (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) G) cmin) (crn F)) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (crn F) (cdif (cpw cr) (cdm cvol)))) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| If the reals can be well-ordered, then there are non-measurable sets. The proof uses "Vitali sets", named for Giuseppe Vitali (1905). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem vitali (c_lt: class):
  $ wi (wwe cr c_lt) (wpss (cdm cvol) (cpw cr)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of measurable functions.
term cmbf: class;

--| Extend class notation with the Lebesgue integral for simple functions.
term citg1: class;

--| Extend class notation with the Lebesgue integral for nonnegative functions.
term citg2: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of integrable functions.
term cibl: class;

--| Extend class notation with the general Lebesgue integral.
term citg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3): class;

--| Define the class of measurable functions on the reals. A real function is measurable if the preimage of every open interval is a measurable set (see ~ ismbl ) and a complex function is measurable if the real and imaginary parts of the function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_mbf {x3 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmbf (crab f2 (wral x3 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (ccom cre (cv f2))) (cv x3)) (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cima (ccnv (ccom cim (cv f2))) (cv x3)) (cdm cvol))) (crn cioo)) (co cc cr cpm)) $;

--| Define the Lebesgue integral for simple functions. A simple function is a finite linear combination of indicator functions for finitely measurable sets, whose assigned value is the sum of the measures of the sets times their respective weights. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_itg1 {x3 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq citg1 (cmpt f2 (crab g2 (w3a (wf cr cr (cv g2)) (wcel (crn (cv g2)) cfn) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (cdif cr (csn cc0))) cvol) cr)) cmbf) (csu x3 (cdif (crn (cv f2)) (csn cc0)) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) cvol) cmul))) $;

--| Define the Lebesgue integral for nonnegative functions. A nonnegative function's integral is the supremum of the integrals of all simple functions that are less than the input function. Note that this may be ` +oo ` for functions that take the value ` +oo ` on a set of positive measure or functions that are bounded below by a positive number on a set of infinite measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_itg2 {x3 f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq citg2 (cmpt f2 (co (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cr cmap) (csup (cab x3 (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) (cv f2) (cofr cle)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1))) cxr clt)) $;

--| Define the class of integrable functions on the reals. A function is integrable if it is measurable and the integrals of the pieces of the function are all finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_ibl {x3 y1 f2 k: setvar}:
  $ wceq cibl (crab f2 (wral k (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (csb y1 (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre) (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdm (cv f2))) (wbr cc0 (cv y1) cle)) (cv y1) cc0))) citg2) cr) (co cc0 c3 cfz)) cmbf) $;

--| Define the full Lebesgue integral, for complex-valued functions to ` RR ` . The syntax is designed to be suggestive of the standard notation for integrals. For example, our notation for the integral of ` x ^ 2 ` from ` 0 ` to ` 1 ` is ` S. ( 0 [,] 1 ) ( x ^ 2 ) _d x = ( 1 / 3 ) ` . The only real function of this definition is to break the integral up into nonnegative real parts and send it off to ~ df-itg2 for further processing. Note that this definition cannot handle integrals which evaluate to infinity, because addition and multiplication are not currently defined on extended reals. (You can use ~ df-itg2 directly for this use-case.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_itg {x3 y1 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (csu k (co cc0 c3 cfz) (co (co ci (cv k) cexp) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (csb y1 (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre) (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cv y1) cle)) (cv y1) cc0))) citg2) cmul)) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a measurable function". This is more naturally stated for functions on the reals, see ~ ismbf and ~ ismbfcn for the decomposition of the real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbf1 {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F cmbf) (wa (wcel F (co cc cr cpm)) (wral x3 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (ccom cre F)) (cv x3)) (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cima (ccnv (ccom cim F)) (cv x3)) (cdm cvol))) (crn cioo))) $;

--| A measurable function is a function into the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbff (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F cmbf) (wf (cdm F) cc F) $;

--| The domain of a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfdm (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F cmbf) (wcel (cdm F) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfconst and related theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfconstlem (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel C cr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv (cxp A2 (csn C))) B2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a measurable function". A function is measurable iff the preimages of all open intervals are measurable sets in the sense of ~ ismbl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbf {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 cr F) (wb (wcel F cmbf) (wral x3 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)) (cdm cvol)) (crn cioo))) $;

--| A complex function is measurable iff the real and imaginary components of the function are measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbfcn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 cc F) (wb (wcel F cmbf) (wa (wcel (ccom cre F) cmbf) (wcel (ccom cim F) cmbf))) $;

--| Definitional property of a measurable function: the preimage of an open right-unbounded interval is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfima (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wf A2 cr F)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co B2 C cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The preimage of any closed interval under a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfimaicc (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wf A2 cr F)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co B2 C cicc)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The preimage of a point under a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfimasn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wf A2 cr F) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (csn B2)) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A constant function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfconst (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (cxp A2 (csn B2)) cmbf) $;

--| The empty function is measurable. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 28-Mar-2018.)
theorem mbf0:
  $ wcel c0 cmbf $;

--| The identity function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cres cid A2) cmbf) $;

--| Lemma for the ` MblFn ` predicate applied to a mapping operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfmptcl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $;

--| The domain of a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfdm2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $;

--| A complex function is measurable iff the real and imaginary components of the function are measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ismbfcn2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cre)) cmbf) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cim)) cmbf))) $;

--| Deduction to prove measurability of a real function. The third hypothesis is not necessary, but the proof of this requires countable choice, so we derive this separately as ~ ismbf3d . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbfd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cxr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co (cv x3) cpnf cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cxr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co cmnf (cv x3) cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| Deduction to prove measurability of a real function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbf2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co (cv x3) cpnf cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co cmnf (cv x3) cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfeqalem2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 19-Aug-2022.)
theorem mbfeqalem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (y1: setvar) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif (cima (ccnv (cmpt x3 B2 C)) (cv y1)) (cima (ccnv (cmpt x3 B2 D)) (cv y1))) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfeqa . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 19-Aug-2022.)
theorem mbfeqalem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cmbf) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 D) cmbf)) $;

--| If two functions are equal almost everywhere, then one is measurable iff the other is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfeqa {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cmbf) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 D) cmbf)) $;

--| The restriction of a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfres (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol))) (wcel (cres F A2) cmbf) $;

--| Measurability of a piecewise function: if ` F ` is measurable on subsets ` B ` and ` C ` of its domain, and these pieces make up all of ` A ` , then ` F ` is measurable on the whole domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfres2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cun B2 C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| Change the domain of a measurability predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cmbf) $;

--| Multiplication by a constant preserves measurability. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfmulc2lem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) F (cof cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| Multiplication by a constant preserves measurability. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfmulc2re (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) F (cof cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| The maximum of two functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem mbfmax {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) cle) (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cmbf) $;

--| The negative of a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.)
theorem mbfneg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2)) cmbf) $;

--| The positive part of a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.)
theorem mbfpos {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 B2 cle) B2 cc0)) cmbf) $;

--| Converse to ~ mbfpos . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfposr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 B2 cle) B2 cc0)) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle) (cneg B2) cc0)) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $;

--| A function is measurable iff its positive and negative parts are measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfposb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 B2 cle) B2 cc0)) cmbf) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle) (cneg B2) cc0)) cmbf))) $;

--| Simplified form of ~ ismbfd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem ismbf3d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (co (cv x3) cpnf cioo)) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfimaopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfimaopnlem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 F G J: class) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cima cioo (cxp cq cq)) $ >
  $ wceq K (crn (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The preimage of any open set (in the complex topology) under a measurable function is measurable. (See also ~ cncombf , which explains why ` A e. dom vol ` is too weak a condition for this theorem.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfimaopn (A2 F J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The preimage of any set open in the subspace topology of the range of the function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfimaopn2 (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J B2 crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wf A2 B2 F) (wss B2 cc)) (wcel C K)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) C) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| The composition of a continuous function with a measurable function is measurable. (More generally, ` G ` can be a Borel-measurable function, but notably the condition that ` G ` be only measurable is too weak, the usual counterexample taking ` G ` to be the Cantor function and ` F ` the indicator function of the ` G ` -image of a nonmeasurable set, which is a subset of the Cantor set and hence null and measurable.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cncombf (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel G (co B2 cc ccncf))) (wcel (ccom G F) cmbf) $;

--| A continuous function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnmbf (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf))) (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| The sum of two measurable functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfaddlem (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) cmbf) $;

--| The sum of two measurable functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfadd (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) cmbf) $;

--| The difference of two measurable functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfsub (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmin)) cmbf) $;

--| A complex constant times a measurable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfmulc2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co C B2 cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| The supremum of a sequence of measurable, real-valued functions is measurable. Note that in this and related theorems, ` B ( n , x ) ` is a function of both ` n ` and ` x ` , since it is an ` n ` -indexed sequence of functions on ` x ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfsup {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 n) (G M: class x3 y1 n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (csup (crn (cmpt n Z B2)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) Z) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $;

--| The infimum of a sequence of measurable, real-valued functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem mbfinf {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 n) (G M: class x3 y1 n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cinf (crn (cmpt n Z B2)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) Z) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $;

--| The limit supremum of a sequence of measurable real-valued functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem mbflimsup {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff m) (A2: class m) (B2: class x3 n) (G H: class x3 m n) (M: class x3 n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (cmpt n Z B2) clsp)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt m cr (csup (cin (cima (cmpt n Z B2) (co (cv m) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cmpt n Z B2) clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) Z) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $;

--| The pointwise limit of a sequence of measurable real-valued functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbflimlem {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C M: class x3 n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr (cmpt n Z B2) C cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) Z) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cmbf) $;

--| The pointwise limit of a sequence of measurable functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbflim {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C M V: class x3 n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr (cmpt n Z B2) C cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) Z) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cmbf) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the zero polynomial.
term c0p: class;

--| Define the zero polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
axiom df_0p:
  $ wceq c0p (cxp cc (csn cc0)) $;

--| The zero function evaluates to zero at every point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem _0pval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 c0p) cc0) $;

--| Two ways to say that the function ` F ` on the reals is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem _0plef (F: class):
  $ wb (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) (wa (wf cr cr F) (wbr c0p F (cofr cle))) $;

--| Adjust the domain of the left argument to match the right, which works better in our theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem _0pledm (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr c0p F (cofr cle)) (wbr (cxp A2 (csn cc0)) F (cofr cle))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a simple function". A simple function is a finite nonnegative linear combination of indicator functions for finitely measurable sets. We use the idiom ` F e. dom S.1 ` to represent this concept because ` S.1 ` is the first preparation function for our final definition ` S. ` (see ~ df-itg ); unlike that operator, which can integrate any function, this operator can only integrate simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem isi1f (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wa (wcel F cmbf) (w3a (wf cr cr F) (wcel (crn F) cfn) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cr (csn cc0))) cvol) cr))) $;

--| Simple functions are measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fmbf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| A simple function is a function on the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1ff (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wf cr cr F) $;

--| A simple function has finite range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1frn (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel (crn F) cfn) $;

--| Any preimage of a simple function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fima (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Any preimage of a simple function not containing zero has finite measure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fima2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wn (wcel cc0 A2))) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) A2) cvol) cr) $;

--| Preimage of a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fima2sn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel A2 (cdif B2 (csn cc0)))) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn A2)) cvol) cr) $;

--| A simplified set of assumptions to show that a given function is simple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif (crn F) (csn cc0)))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif (crn F) (csn cc0)))) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $;

--| Any simple function takes the value zero on a set of unbounded measure, so in particular this set is not empty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1f0rn (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel cc0 (crn F)) $;

--| The value of the integral on simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1val {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wceq (cfv F citg1) (csu x3 (cdif (crn F) (csn cc0)) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) cvol) cmul))) $;

--| The value of the integral on simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1val2 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wss (cdif (crn F) (csn cc0)) A2) (wss A2 (cdif cr (csn cc0))))) (wceq (cfv F citg1) (csu x3 A2 (co (cv x3) (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) cvol) cmul))) $;

--| Closure of the integral on simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1cl (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel (cfv F citg1) cr) $;

--| Closure of the integral on positive simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1ge0 (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wbr c0p F (cofr cle))) (wbr cc0 (cfv F citg1) cle) $;

--| The zero function is simple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1f0:
  $ wcel (cxp cr (csn cc0)) (cdm citg1) $;

--| The zero function has zero integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg10:
  $ wceq (cfv (cxp cr (csn cc0)) citg1) cc0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ i1f1 and ~ itg11 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1f1lem {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wa (wf cr (cpr cc0 c1) F) (wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wceq (cima (ccnv F) (csn c1)) A2)) $;

--| Base case simple functions are indicator functions of measurable sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1f1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr)) (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The integral of an indicator function is the volume of the set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg11 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr)) (wceq (cfv F citg1) (cfv A2 cvol)) $;

--| Decompose a preimage, which is always a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem itg1addlem1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F: class) (X Y: class k):
  $ wi ph (wf X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss B2 (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv k)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cfv B2 cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (ciun k A2 B2) cvol) (csu k A2 (cfv B2 cvol))) $;

--| Decompose the preimage of a sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1faddlem {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 cc)) (wceq (cima (ccnv (co F G (cof caddc))) (csn A2)) (ciun y1 (crn G) (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (co A2 (cv y1) cmin))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Decompose the preimage of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fmullem {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wceq (cima (ccnv (co F G (cof cmul))) (csn A2)) (ciun y1 (cdif (crn G) (csn cc0)) (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv y1)))))) $;

--| The sum of two simple functions is a simple function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fadd (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The pointwise product of two simple functions is a simple function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2014.)
theorem i1fmul (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg1add . The function ` I ` represents the pieces into which we will break up the domain of the sum. Since it is infinite only when both ` i ` and ` j ` are zero, we arbitrarily define it to be zero there to simplify the sums that are manipulated in ~ itg1addlem4 and ~ itg1addlem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1addlem2 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (I: class i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo i j cr cr (cif (wa (wceq (cv i) cc0) (wceq (cv j) cc0)) cc0 (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv i))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv j)))) cvol))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp cr cr) cr I) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg1add . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1addlem3 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class) (I: class i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo i j cr cr (cif (wa (wceq (cv i) cc0) (wceq (cv j) cc0)) cc0 (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv i))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv j)))) cvol))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wn (wa (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0)))) (wceq (co A2 B2 I) (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn A2)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn B2))) cvol)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg1add . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Proof shortened by SN, 3-Oct-2024.)
theorem itg1addlem4 {y1 z i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class i j) (F G: class) (I: class z i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo i j cr cr (cif (wa (wceq (cv i) cc0) (wceq (cv j) cc0)) cc0 (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv i))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv j)))) cvol))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cres caddc (cxp (crn F) (crn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) citg1) (csu y1 (crn F) (csu z (crn G) (co (co (cv y1) (cv z) caddc) (co (cv y1) (cv z) I) cmul)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg1add . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1addlem5 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class i j) (F G: class) (I: class i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo i j cr cr (cif (wa (wceq (cv i) cc0) (wceq (cv j) cc0)) cc0 (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv i))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cv j)))) cvol))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cres caddc (cxp (crn F) (crn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) citg1) (co (cfv F citg1) (cfv G citg1) caddc)) $;

--| The integral of a sum of simple functions is the sum of the integrals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1add (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) citg1) (co (cfv F citg1) (cfv G citg1) caddc)) $;

--| Decompose the preimage of a constant times a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fmulclem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cima (ccnv (co (cxp cr (csn A2)) F (cof cmul))) (csn B2)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (co B2 A2 cdiv)))) $;

--| A nonnegative constant times a simple function gives another simple function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fmulc (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cxp cr (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The integral of a constant times a simple function is the constant times the original integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg1mulc (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co (cxp cr (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) citg1) (co A2 (cfv F citg1) cmul)) $;

--| The "restriction" of a simple function to a measurable subset is simple. (It's not actually a restriction because it is zero instead of undefined outside ` A ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fres {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel A2 (cdm cvol))) (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The positive part of a simple function is simple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem i1fpos {x3: setvar} (F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ i1fposd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem i1fposd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 cr A2) (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wbr cc0 A2 cle) A2 cc0)) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The difference of two simple functions is a simple function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem i1fsub (F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel G (cdm citg1))) (wcel (co F G (cof cmin)) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| The integral of a difference of two simple functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg1sub (F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel G (cdm citg1))) (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof cmin)) citg1) (co (cfv F citg1) (cfv G citg1) cmin)) $;

--| The integral of a simple function supported on a nullset is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg10a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F citg1) cc0) $;

--| The integral of an almost positive simple function is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg1ge0a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv F citg1) cle) $;

--| Approximate version of ~ itg1le . If ` F <_ G ` for almost all ` x ` , then ` S.1 F <_ S.1 G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg1lea {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F citg1) (cfv G citg1) cle) $;

--| If one simple function dominates another, then the integral of the larger is also larger. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg1le (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel G (cdm citg1)) (wbr F G (cofr cle))) (wbr (cfv F citg1) (cfv G citg1) cle) $;

--| Restricting the simple function ` F ` to the increasing sequence ` A ( n ) ` of measurable sets whose union is ` RR ` yields a sequence of simple functions whose integrals approach the integral of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg1climres {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (G: class x3 n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm cvol) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss (cfv (cv n) A2) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (crn A2)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) A2)) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv G citg1)) (cfv F citg1) cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem1 {y1 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (J: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp cn cr) (co cc0 cpnf cico) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem2 {x3 y1 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (G: class y1 m) (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg (cv m)) (cv m) cicc)) (cif (wbr (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m) cle) (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m)) cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (cfv A2 G) (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg A2) A2 cicc)) (cif (wbr (co A2 (cv x3) J) A2 cle) (co A2 (cv x3) J) A2) cc0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem3 {x3 y1 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (G: class y1 m) (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg (cv m)) (cv m) cicc)) (cif (wbr (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m) cle) (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m)) cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 cn)) (wf cr (crn (cmpt m (co cc0 (co A2 (co c2 A2 cexp) cmul) cfz) (co (cv m) (co c2 A2 cexp) cdiv))) (cfv A2 G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . This lemma is not as interesting as it is long - it is simply checking that ` G ` is in fact a sequence of simple functions, by verifying that its range is in ` ( 0 ... n 2 ^ n ) / ( 2 ^ n ) ` (which is to say, the numbers from ` 0 ` to ` n ` in increments of ` 1 / ( 2 ^ n ) ` ), and also that the preimage of each point ` k ` is measurable, because it is equal to ` ( -u n [,] n ) i^i ( ``' F " ( k [,) k + 1 / ( 2 ^ n ) ) ) ` for ` k < n ` and ` ( -u n [,] n ) i^i ( ``' F " ( k [,) +oo ) ) ` for ` k = n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem4 {x3 y1 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (G: class y1 m) (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg (cv m)) (cv m) cicc)) (cif (wbr (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m) cle) (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m)) cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . Verify that ` G ` describes an increasing sequence of positive functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem5 {x3 y1 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (G: class y1 m) (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg (cv m)) (cv m) cicc)) (cif (wbr (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m) cle) (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m)) cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 cn)) (wa (wbr c0p (cfv A2 G) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 G) (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) G) (cofr cle))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1fseq . Verify that ` G ` converges pointwise to ` F ` , and wrap up the existential quantifier. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseqlem6 {x3 y1 g2 m n: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (F: class) (G: class y1 m) (J: class x3 y1 g2 n):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo m y1 cn cr (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cmul) cfl) (co c2 (cv m) cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co (cneg (cv m)) (cv m) cicc)) (cif (wbr (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m) cle) (co (cv m) (cv x3) J) (cv m)) cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (w3a (wf cn (cdm citg1) (cv g2)) (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (cofr cle))) cn) (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr))) $;

--| A characterization of measurability in terms of simple functions (this is an if and only if for nonnegative functions, although we don't prove it). Any nonnegative measurable function is the limit of an increasing sequence of nonnegative simple functions. This proof is an example of a poor de Bruijn factor - the formalized proof is much longer than an average hand proof, which usually just describes the function ` G ` and "leaves the details as an exercise to the reader". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem mbfi1fseq {x3 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (w3a (wf cn (cdm citg1) (cv g2)) (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv g2)) (cofr cle))) cn) (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfi1flim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfi1flimlem {x3 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (wa (wf cn (cdm citg1) (cv g2)) (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr))) $;

--| Any real measurable function has a sequence of simple functions that converges to it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfi1flim {x3 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex g2 (wa (wf cn (cdm citg1) (cv g2)) (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfmullem2 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 P Q F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) Q) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) Q))) (cfv (cv x3) G) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mbfmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfmullem (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| The product of two measurable functions is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem mbfmul (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) cmbf) $;

--| The set of lower sums is a set of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2lcl {x3 g2: setvar} (F: class) (L: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq L (cab x3 (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1))) $ >
  $ wss L cxr $;

--| Value of the integral on nonnegative real functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2val {x3 g2: setvar} (F: class) (L: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq L (cab x3 (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1))) $ >
  $ wi (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wceq (cfv F citg2) (csup L cxr clt)) $;

--| Elementhood in the set ` L ` of lower sums of the integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2l {x3 g2: setvar} (A2 F: class) (L: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq L (cab x3 (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 L) (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wceq A2 (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for elementhood in the set ` L ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2lr {x3 g2: setvar} (F G: class) (L: class x3 g2):
  $ wceq L (cab x3 (wrex g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) citg1))) (cdm citg1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G (cdm citg1)) (wbr G F (cofr cle))) (wcel (cfv G citg1) L) $;

--| A real function is a nonnegative extended real function if all its values are greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem xrge0f (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cr cr F) (wbr c0p F (cofr cle))) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative real function is an extended real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2cl (F: class):
  $ wi (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wcel (cfv F citg2) cxr) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative real function ` F ` is an upper bound on the integrals of all simple functions ` G ` dominated by ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2ub (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wcel G (cdm citg1)) (wbr G F (cofr cle))) (wbr (cfv G citg1) (cfv F citg2) cle) $;

--| Any upper bound on the integrals of all simple functions ` G ` dominated by ` F ` is greater than ` ( S.2 `` F ) ` , the least upper bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2leub {g2: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wcel A2 cxr)) (wb (wbr (cfv F citg2) A2 cle) (wral g2 (wi (wbr (cv g2) F (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv g2) citg1) A2 cle)) (cdm citg1))) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative real function is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2ge0 (F: class):
  $ wi (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wbr cc0 (cfv F citg2) cle) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative simple function using ` S.2 ` is the same as its value under ` S.1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2itg1 (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wbr c0p F (cofr cle))) (wceq (cfv F citg2) (cfv F citg1)) $;

--| The integral of the zero function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg20:
  $ wceq (cfv (cxp cr (csn cc0)) citg2) cc0 $;

--| If an ` S.2 ` integral is bounded above, then it is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2lecl (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv F citg2) A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $;

--| If one function dominates another, then the integral of the larger is also larger. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itg2le (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) G) (wbr F G (cofr cle))) (wbr (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) cle) $;

--| Integral of a constant function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2const {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) B2 cc0)) citg2) (co B2 (cfv A2 cvol) cmul)) $;

--| When the base set of a constant function has infinite volume, the integral is also infinite and vice-versa. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2const2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) B2 cc0)) citg2) cr)) $;

--| Definitional property of the ` S.2 ` integral: for any function ` F ` there is a countable sequence ` g ` of simple functions less than ` F ` whose integrals converge to the integral of ` F ` . (This theorem is for the most part unnecessary in lieu of ~ itg2i1fseq , but unlike that theorem this one doesn't require ` F ` to be measurable.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2seq {g2 n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wex g2 (w3a (wf cn (cdm citg1) (cv g2)) (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) F (cofr cle)) cn) (wceq (cfv F citg2) (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) citg1))) cxr clt)))) $;

--| Approximate version of ~ itg2ub . If ` F ` approximately dominates ` G ` , then ` S.1 G <_ S.2 F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2uba {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) G) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G citg1) (cfv F citg2) cle) $;

--| Approximate version of ~ itg2le . If ` F <_ G ` for almost all ` x ` , then ` S.2 F <_ S.2 G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2lea {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) cle) $;

--| Approximate equality of integrals. If ` F = G ` for almost all ` x ` , then ` S.2 F = S.2 G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2eqa {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cicc) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cr A2))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2mulc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2014.)
theorem itg2mulclem (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cxp cr (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) citg2) (co A2 (cfv F citg2) cmul) cle) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative constant times a function is the constant times the integral of the original function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2mulc (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co (cxp cr (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) citg2) (co A2 (cfv F citg2) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2split . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2splitlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (U: class) (F G H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) U)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) B2) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) U) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv H citg2) (co (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) caddc) cle) $;

--| The ` S.2 ` integral splits under an almost disjoint union. The proof avoids the use of ~ itg2add , which requires countable choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2split {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (U: class) (F G H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) U)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) B2) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) U) C cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv H citg2) (co (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2mono . We show that for any constant ` t ` less than one, ` t x. S.1 H ` is less than ` S ` , and so ` S.1 H <_ S ` , which is one half of the equality in ~ itg2mono . Consider the sequence ` A ( n ) = { x | t x. H <_ F ( n ) } ` . This is an increasing sequence of measurable sets whose union is ` RR ` , and so ` H |`` A ( n ) ` has an integral which equals ` S.1 H ` in the limit, by ~ itg1climres . Then by taking the limit in ` ( t x. H ) |`` A ( n ) <_ F ( n ) ` , we get ` t x. S.1 H <_ S ` as desired. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2monolem1 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 n) (S T F G H: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv (cv n) F) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) (cfv (cv n) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) (cv y1) cle) cn) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) citg2))) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H G (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (crab x3 (wbr (co T (cfv (cv x3) H) cmul) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) cle) cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co T (cfv H citg1) cmul) S cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2mono . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2monolem2 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (P S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv (cv n) F) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) (cfv (cv n) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) (cv y1) cle) cn) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) citg2))) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P G (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv P citg1) S cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2mono . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2monolem3 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (P S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv (cv n) F) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) (cfv (cv n) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) (cv y1) cle) cn) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) citg2))) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdm citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P G (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv P citg1) S cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv P citg1) S cle) $;

--| The Monotone Convergence Theorem for nonnegative functions. If ` { ( F `` n ) : n e. NN } ` is a monotone increasing sequence of positive, measurable, real-valued functions, and ` G ` is the pointwise limit of the sequence, then ` ( S.2 `` G ) ` is the limit of the sequence ` { ( S.2 `` ( F `` n ) ) : n e. NN } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2mono {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv (cv n) F) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) (cfv (cv n) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cofr cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wrex y1 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) F)) (cv y1) cle) cn) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) F) citg2))) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G citg2) S) $;

--| Subject to the conditions coming from ~ mbfi1fseq , the sequence of simple functions are all less than the target function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2i1fseqle {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (P F: class) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) P) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) P) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) P) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M cn)) (wbr (cfv M P) F (cofr cle)) $;

--| Subject to the conditions coming from ~ mbfi1fseq , the integral of the sequence of simple functions converges to the integral of the target function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2i1fseq {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (P: class) (S: class x3 m n) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) P) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) P) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) P) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m cn (cfv (cfv (cv m) P) citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F citg2) (csup (crn S) cxr clt)) $;

--| In an extension to the results of ~ itg2i1fseq , if there is an upper bound on the integrals of the simple functions approaching ` F ` , then ` S.2 F ` is real and the standard limit relation applies. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2i1fseq2 {x3 k m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (P: class) (S: class x3 m n) (F: class) (M: class x3 m):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) P) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) P) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) P) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m cn (cfv (cfv (cv m) P) citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) P) citg1) M cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cfv F citg2) cli) $;

--| Special case of ~ itg2i1fseq2 : if the integral of ` F ` is a real number, then the standard limit relation holds on the integrals of simple functions approaching ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2i1fseq3 {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (P: class) (S: class x3 m n) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) P) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) P) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) P) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m cn (cfv (cfv (cv m) P) citg1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cfv F citg2) cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itg2add . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2addlem {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (P Q F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) P) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) P) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) P) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) P))) (cfv (cv x3) F) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdm citg1) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wa (wbr c0p (cfv (cv n) Q) (cofr cle)) (wbr (cfv (cv n) Q) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) Q) (cofr cle))) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) Q))) (cfv (cv x3) G) cli) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) citg2) (co (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) caddc)) $;

--| The ` S.2 ` integral is linear. (Measurability is an essential component of this theorem; otherwise consider the characteristic function of a nonmeasurable set and its complement.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2add (ph: wff) (F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) citg2) (co (cfv F citg2) (cfv G citg2) caddc)) $;

--| If the function ` F ` is strictly positive on a set of positive measure, then the integral of the function is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2gt0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cvol) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv x3) F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv F citg2) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2cnlem1 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cv n) cle) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) citg2))) cxr clt) (cfv F citg2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg2cnlem2 {x3 u d: setvar} (ph: wff d) (C F M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) M cle) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) citg2) (co (cfv F citg2) (co C c2 cdiv) cmin) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral u (wi (wbr (cfv (cv u) cvol) (cv d) clt) (wbr (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv u)) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) citg2) C clt)) (cdm cvol)) crp) $;

--| A sort of absolute continuity of the Lebesgue integral (this is the core of ~ ftc1a which is about actual absolute continuity). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem itg2cn {x3 u d: setvar} (ph: wff d) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral u (wi (wbr (cfv (cv u) cvol) (cv d) clt) (wbr (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cv u)) (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0)) citg2) C clt)) (cdm cvol)) crp) $;

--| Conditioned equality theorem for the if statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.)
theorem ibllem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) B2 cc0) (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 C cle)) C cc0)) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is integrable". The "integrable" predicate corresponds roughly to the range of validity of ` S. A B _d x ` , which is to say that the expression ` S. A B _d x ` doesn't make sense unless ` ( x e. A |-> B ) e. L^1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem isibl {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (T: class x3 k) (F: class) (G: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 T cle)) T cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq T (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm F) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F cibl) (wa (wcel F cmbf) (wral k (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) (co cc0 c3 cfz)))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is integrable" when ` F ` is a mapping operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem isibl2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (T G: class x3 k) (V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 T cle)) T cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq T (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wral k (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) (co cc0 c3 cfz)))) $;

--| An integrable function is measurable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2014.)
theorem iblmbf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F cibl) (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| If a function is integrable, then the ` S.2 ` integrals of the function's decompositions all exist. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblitg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 T G: class x3) (K V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 T cle)) T cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq T (cfv (co B2 (co ci K cexp) cdiv) cre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K cz)) (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr) $;

--| Evaluate the class substitution in ~ df-itg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem dfitg {x3 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (T: class x3 k):
  $ wceq T (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre) $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (csu k (co cc0 c3 cfz) (co (co ci (cv k) cexp) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 T cle)) T cc0)) citg2) cmul)) $;

--| An integral is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wcel (citg x3 A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.) Avoid axioms. (Revised by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq1f {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgeq1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem nfitg1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 (citg x3 A2 B2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for an integral: if ` y ` is (effectively) not free in ` A ` and ` B ` , it is not free in ` S. A B _d x ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem nfitg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 (citg x3 A2 B2) $;

--| Change bound variable in an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem cbvitg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wnfc y1 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg y1 A2 C) $;

--| Change bound variable in an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem cbvitgv {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg y1 A2 C) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgeq2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wceq B2 C) A2) (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C)) $;

--| The domain of an integral only matters in its intersection with ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgresr {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 (cin A2 cr) B2) $;

--| The integral of anything on the empty set is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem itg0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq (citg x3 c0 A2) cc0 $;

--| The integral of zero on any set is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgz {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 cc0) cc0 $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2014.)
theorem itgeq2dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Change bound variable in an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg y1 A2 (cfv (cv y1) (cmpt x3 A2 B2)))) $;

--| The integral of an integrable function is a complex number. This is Metamath 100 proof #86. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jun-2014.)
theorem itgcl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (citg x3 A2 B2) cc) $;

--| Substitution lemma. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgvallem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 T: class x3) (k: setvar) (K: class):
  $ wceq (co ci K cexp) T $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) K) (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre) cle)) (cfv (co B2 (co ci (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cre) cc0)) citg2) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv (co B2 T cdiv) cre) cle)) (cfv (co B2 T cdiv) cre) cc0)) citg2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgposval and ~ itgreval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgvallem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) B2 cc0)) citg2) cc0) $;

--| The zero function is integrable on any measurable set. (Unlike ~ iblconst , this does not require ` A ` to have finite measure.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem ibl0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cxp A2 (csn cc0)) cibl) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iblcnlem . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblcnlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 R S T U: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cre) cle)) (cfv B2 cre) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cim) cle)) (cfv B2 cim) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (w3a (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wa (wcel R cr) (wcel S cr)) (wa (wcel T cr) (wcel U cr)))) $;

--| Expand out the universal quantifier in ~ isibl2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblcnlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 R S T U: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cre) cle)) (cfv B2 cre) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cim) cle)) (cfv B2 cim) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (w3a (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wa (wcel R cr) (wcel S cr)) (wa (wcel T cr) (wcel U cr)))) $;

--| Expand out the sum in ~ dfitg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgcnlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 R S T U: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cre) cle)) (cfv B2 cre) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cre)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cim) cle)) (cfv B2 cim) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cle)) (cneg (cfv B2 cim)) cc0)) citg2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (co (co R S cmin) (co ci (co T U cmin) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Integrability of a real function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblrelem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (w3a (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) B2 cc0)) citg2) cr) (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle)) (cneg B2) cc0)) citg2) cr))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iblpos . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblposlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle)) (cneg B2) cc0)) citg2) cc0) $;

--| Integrability of a nonnegative function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblpos {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) B2 cc0)) citg2) cr))) $;

--| Integrability of a real function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 B2 cle) B2 cc0)) cibl) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle) (cneg B2) cc0)) cibl))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgposval and ~ itgreval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgrevallem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (co (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) B2 cc0)) citg2) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle)) (cneg B2) cc0)) citg2) cmin)) $;

--| The integral of a nonnegative function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgposval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) B2 cc0)) citg2)) $;

--| Decompose the integral of a real function into positive and negative parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jul-2014.)
theorem itgreval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (co (citg x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 B2 cle) B2 cc0)) (citg x3 A2 (cif (wbr cc0 (cneg B2) cle) (cneg B2) cc0)) cmin)) $;

--| Real closure of an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgrecl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (citg x3 A2 B2) cr) $;

--| Integrability of a complex function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblcn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cre)) cibl) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cim)) cibl))) $;

--| Decompose the integral of a complex function into real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgcnval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (co (citg x3 A2 (cfv B2 cre)) (co ci (citg x3 A2 (cfv B2 cim)) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Real part of an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (citg x3 A2 B2) cre) (citg x3 A2 (cfv B2 cre))) $;

--| Imaginary part of an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgim {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (citg x3 A2 B2) cim) (citg x3 A2 (cfv B2 cim))) $;

--| The negative of an integrable function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblneg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2)) cibl) $;

--| Negation of an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgneg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cneg (citg x3 A2 B2)) (citg x3 A2 (cneg B2))) $;

--| A subset of an integrable function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $;

--| Change the domain of an integrability predicate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblss2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $;

--| Transfer an integral using ` S.2 ` to an equivalent integral using ` S. ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgitg2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 cr A2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 cr A2) (cfv (cmpt x3 cr A2) citg2)) $;

--| A simple function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem i1fibl (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wcel F cibl) $;

--| Transfer an integral using ` S.1 ` to an equivalent integral using ` S. ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgitg1 {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdm citg1)) (wceq (citg x3 cr (cfv (cv x3) F)) (cfv F citg1)) $;

--| Monotonicity of an integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgle {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) cle) $;

--| The integral of a positive function is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgge0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (citg x3 A2 B2) cle) $;

--| Expand the set of an integral by adding zeroes outside the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Expand the set of an integral by adding zeroes outside the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgss2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) C cc0))) $;

--| Approximate equality of integrals. If ` C ( x ) = D ( x ) ` for almost all ` x ` , then ` S. B C ( x ) _d x = S. B D ( x ) _d x ` and one is integrable iff the other is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgeqa {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 D) cibl)) (wceq (citg x3 B2 C) (citg x3 B2 D))) $;

--| Expand the set of an integral by a nullset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgss3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cdif B2 A2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wb (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl)) (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C))) $;

--| Equality of integrals on open and closed intervals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgioo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) C) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) C)) $;

--| Expand the integral of a nonnegative function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgless {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C) cle) $;

--| A constant function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblconst (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (cxp A2 (csn B2)) cibl) $;

--| Integral of a constant function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgconst {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (co B2 (cfv A2 cvol) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ibladd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem ibladdlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq D (co B2 C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) B2 cc0)) citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 C cle)) C cc0)) citg2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wbr cc0 D cle)) D cc0)) citg2) cr) $;

--| Add two integrals over the same domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem ibladd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C caddc)) cibl) $;

--| Subtract two integrals over the same domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblsub {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cmin)) cibl) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgadd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgaddlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 (co B2 C caddc)) (co (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgadd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgaddlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 (co B2 C caddc)) (co (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) caddc)) $;

--| Add two integrals over the same domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgadd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 (co B2 C caddc)) (co (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) caddc)) $;

--| Subtract two integrals over the same domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgsub {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 (co B2 C cmin)) (co (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) cmin)) $;

--| Take a finite sum of integrals over the same domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgfsum {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C V: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv k) B2))) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (csu k B2 C)) cibl) (wceq (citg x3 A2 (csu k B2 C)) (csu k B2 (citg x3 A2 C)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iblabs . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblabslem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 F G V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) A2) (cfv (cfv B2 F) cabs) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 F)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cfv B2 F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel G cmbf) (wcel (cfv G citg2) cr)) $;

--| The absolute value of an integrable function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblabs {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cabs)) cibl) $;

--| A measurable function is integrable iff its absolute value is integrable. (See ~ iblabs for the forward implication.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblabsr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 cabs)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $;

--| Multiply an integral by a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem iblmulc2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co C B2 cmul)) cibl) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgmulc2 : positive real case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgmulc2lem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (citg x3 A2 B2) cmul) (citg x3 A2 (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgmulc2 : real case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgmulc2lem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (citg x3 A2 B2) cmul) (citg x3 A2 (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Multiply an integral by a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgmulc2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (citg x3 A2 B2) cmul) (citg x3 A2 (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| The triangle inequality for integrals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgabs {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (citg x3 A2 B2) cabs) (citg x3 A2 (cfv B2 cabs)) cle) $;

--| The ` S. ` integral splits under an almost disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgsplit {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (U V: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) U)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 U C) (co (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C) caddc)) $;

--| The ` S. ` integral splits on closed intervals with matching endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem itgspliticc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co A2 C cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 C cicc))) (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co B2 C cicc) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 C cicc) D) (co (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) D) (citg x3 (co B2 C cicc) D) caddc)) $;

--| The ` S. ` integral splits on open intervals with matching endpoints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgsplitioo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co A2 C cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 C cioo))) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co B2 C cioo) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 C cioo) D) (co (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) D) (citg x3 (co B2 C cioo) D) caddc)) $;

--| A bounded function times an integrable function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem bddmulibl {x3 y1: setvar} (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wcel G cibl) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (cdm F)) cr)) (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) cibl) $;

--| A bounded function is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem bddibl {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wcel (cfv (cdm F) cvol) cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (cdm F)) cr)) (wcel F cibl) $;

--| A continuous function on a closed bounded interval is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem cniccibl (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf))) (wcel F cibl) $;

--| Choice-free proof of ~ bddibl . (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Nov-2017.) (Revised by Brendan Leahy, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bddiblnc {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F cmbf) (wcel (cfv (cdm F) cvol) cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (cdm F)) cr)) (wcel F cibl) $;

--| Choice-free proof of ~ cniccibl . (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 2-Nov-2017.)
theorem cnicciblnc (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf))) (wcel F cibl) $;

--| The integral of a strictly positive function is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Aug-2014.)
theorem itggt0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cvol) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (citg x3 A2 B2) clt) $;

--| Transfer ~ itg2cn to the full Lebesgue integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgcn {x3 u d: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (V: class x3 u d):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex d (wral u (wi (wa (wss (cv u) A2) (wbr (cfv (cv u) cvol) (cv d) clt)) (wbr (citg x3 (cv u) (cfv B2 cabs)) C clt)) (cdm cvol)) crp) $;

--| Extend class notation with the directed integral.
term cdit {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3): class;

--| Define the directed integral, which is just a regular integral but with a sign change when the limits are interchanged. The ` A ` and ` B ` here are the lower and upper limits of the integral, usually written as a subscript and superscript next to the integral sign. We define the region of integration to be an open interval instead of closed so that we can use ` +oo , -oo ` for limits and also integrate up to a singularity at an endpoint. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_ditg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) C) (cneg (citg x3 (co B2 A2 cioo) C))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgeq1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cdit x3 A2 C D) (cdit x3 B2 C D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgeq2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cdit x3 C A2 D) (cdit x3 C B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. (The domain of the equality here is very rough; for more precise bounds one should decompose it with ~ ditgpos first and use the equality theorems for ~ df-itg .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgeq3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D E: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wceq D E) cr) (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) (cdit x3 A2 B2 E)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgeq3dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (D E: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq D E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) (cdit x3 A2 B2 E)) $;

--| A directed integral is a set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem ditgex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wcel (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) cvv $;

--| Value of the directed integral from a point to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditg0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 A2 B2) cc0 $;

--| Change bound variable in a directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem cbvditg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wnfc y1 C $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (cdit y1 A2 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable in a directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem cbvditgv {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (cdit y1 A2 B2 D) $;

--| Value of the directed integral in the forward direction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgpos {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) C)) $;

--| Value of the directed integral in the backward direction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgneg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 B2 A2 C) (cneg (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) C))) $;

--| Closure of a directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgcl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co X Y cioo))) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) cc) $;

--| Reverse a directed integral. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgswap {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co X Y cioo))) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 B2 A2 C) (cneg (cdit x3 A2 B2 C))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ditgsplit . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgsplitlem {x3: setvar} (ph ps th: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3) (V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co X Y cioo))) (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wb (wa ps th) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) th) (wceq (cdit x3 A2 C D) (co (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) (cdit x3 B2 C D) caddc)) $;

--| This theorem is the raison d'&ecirc;tre for the directed integral, because unlike ~ itgspliticc , there is no constraint on the ordering of the points ` A , B , C ` in the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Aug-2014.)
theorem ditgsplit {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D: class x3) (V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co X Y cioo))) (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) D) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 C D) (co (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) (cdit x3 B2 C D) caddc)) $;

--| The limit operator.
term climc: class;

--| The derivative operator.
term cdv: class;

--| The ` n ` -th derivative operator.
term cdvn: class;

--| The set of ` n ` -times continuously differentiable functions.
term ccpn: class;

--| Define the set of limits of a complex function at a point. Under normal circumstances, this will be a singleton or empty, depending on whether the limit exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_limc {x3 y1 z f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq climc (cmpo f2 x3 (co cc cc cpm) cc (cab y1 (wsbc j (wcel (cmpt z (cun (cdm (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) (cif (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv y1) (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)))) (cfv (cv x3) (co (co (cv j) (cun (cdm (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) crest) (cv j) ccnp))) (cfv ccnfld ctopn)))) $;

--| Define the derivative operator. This acts on functions to produce a function that is defined where the original function is differentiable, with value the derivative of the function at these points. The set ` s ` here is the ambient topological space under which we are evaluating the continuity of the difference quotient. Although the definition is valid for any subset of ` CC ` and is well-behaved when ` s ` contains no isolated points, we will restrict our attention to the cases ` s = RR ` or ` s = CC ` for the majority of the development, these corresponding respectively to real and complex differentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_dv {x3 z f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdv (cmpo s1 f2 (cpw cc) (co cc (cv s1) cpm) (ciun x3 (cfv (cdm (cv f2)) (cfv (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) (cv s1) crest) cnt)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (co (cmpt z (cdif (cdm (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) (co (co (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) cmin) (co (cv z) (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) (cv x3) climc)))) $;

--| Define the ` n ` -th derivative operator on functions on the complex numbers. This just iterates the derivative operation according to the last argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_dvn {x3 f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdvn (cmpo s1 f2 (cpw cc) (co cc (cv s1) cpm) (cseq (ccom (cmpt x3 cvv (co (cv s1) (cv x3) cdv)) c1st) (cxp cn0 (csn (cv f2))) cc0)) $;

--| Define the set of ` n ` -times continuously differentiable functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.)
axiom df_cpn {x3 f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccpn (cmpt s1 (cpw cc) (cmpt x3 cn0 (crab f2 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (co (cv s1) (cv f2) cdvn)) (co (cdm (cv f2)) cc ccncf)) (co cc (cv s1) cpm)))) $;

--| The derivative function is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem reldv (S F: class):
  $ wrel (co S F cdv) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ellimc . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcvallem {z: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class) (G J: class z) (K: class):
  $ wceq J (co K (cun A2 (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z (cun A2 (csn B2)) (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) C (cfv (cv z) F))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wi (wcel G (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp))) (wcel C cc)) $;

--| Value and set bounds on the limit operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcfval {y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (J: class z) (K: class):
  $ wceq J (co K (cun A2 (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wceq (co F B2 climc) (cab y1 (wcel (cmpt z (cun A2 (csn B2)) (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) (cv y1) (cfv (cv z) F))) (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp))))) (wss (co F B2 climc) cc)) $;

--| Value of the limit predicate. ` C ` is the limit of the function ` F ` at ` B ` if the function ` G ` , formed by adding ` B ` to the domain of ` F ` and setting it to ` C ` , is continuous at ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem ellimc {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G J: class z) (K: class):
  $ wceq J (co K (cun A2 (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z (cun A2 (csn B2)) (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) C (cfv (cv z) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co F B2 climc)) (wcel G (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp)))) $;

--| Reverse closure for the limit operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcrcl (B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (co F B2 climc)) (w3a (wf (cdm F) cc F) (wss (cdm F) cc) (wcel B2 cc)) $;

--| Closure of the limit operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limccl (B2 F: class):
  $ wss (co F B2 climc) cc $;

--| It suffices to consider functions which are not defined at ` B ` to define the limit of a function. In particular, the value of the original function ` F ` at ` B ` does not affect the limit of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcdif (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F B2 climc) (co (cres F (cdif A2 (csn B2))) B2 climc)) $;

--| Write the definition of a limit directly in terms of open sets of the topology on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem ellimc2 {w u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co F B2 climc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wral u (wi (wcel C (cv u)) (wrex w (wa (wcel B2 (cv w)) (wss (cima F (cin (cv w) (cdif A2 (csn B2)))) (cv u))) K)) K))) $;

--| If ` B ` is not a limit point of the domain of the function ` F ` , then every point is a limit of ` F ` at ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcnlp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv K clp)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F B2 climc) cc) $;

--| Write the epsilon-delta definition of a limit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem ellimc3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co F B2 climc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wa (wne (cv z) B2) (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) B2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) F) C cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) A2) crp) crp))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ limcflf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcflflem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv K clp))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cdif A2 (csn B2)) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (cfv (csn B2) (cfv K cnei)) C crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv C cfil)) $;

--| The limit operator can be expressed as a filter limit, from the filter of neighborhoods of ` B ` restricted to ` A \ { B } ` , to the topology of the complex numbers. (If ` B ` is not a limit point of ` A ` , then it is still formally a filter limit, but the neighborhood filter is not a proper filter in this case.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcflf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F K L: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv K clp))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cdif A2 (csn B2)) $ >
  $ wceq L (co (cfv (csn B2) (cfv K cnei)) C crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F B2 climc) (cfv (cres F C) (co K L cflf))) $;

--| If ` B ` is a limit point of the domain of the function ` F ` , then there is at most one limit value of ` F ` at ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv K clp))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co F B2 climc))) $;

--| Express the limit operator for a function defined by a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcmpt {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D J K: class z):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) A2)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K (cun A2 (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co (cmpt z A2 D) B2 climc)) (wcel (cmpt z (cun A2 (csn B2)) (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) C D)) (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp)))) $;

--| Express the limit operator for a function defined by a mapping. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcmpt2 {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (D J K: class z):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv z) A2) (wne (cv z) B2))) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K A2 crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co (cmpt z (cdif A2 (csn B2)) D) B2 climc)) (wcel (cmpt z A2 (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) C D)) (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp)))) $;

--| Any limit of ` F ` is also a limit of the restriction of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcresi (B2 C F: class):
  $ wss (co F B2 climc) (co (cres F C) B2 climc) $;

--| If ` B ` is an interior point of ` C u. { B } ` relative to the domain ` A ` , then a limit point of ` F |`` C ` extends to a limit of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcres (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K (cun A2 (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cun C (csn B2)) (cfv J cnt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cres F C) B2 climc) (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| A function is continuous at ` B ` iff its limit at ` B ` equals the value of the function there. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnplimc (A2 B2 F J K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wcel B2 A2)) (wb (wcel F (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp))) (wa (wf A2 cc F) (wcel (cfv B2 F) (co F B2 climc)))) $;

--| ` F ` is a continuous function iff the limit of the function at each point equals the value of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnlimc {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cc) (wb (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) (wa (wf A2 cc F) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) (co F (cv x3) climc)) A2))) $;

--| If ` F ` is a continuous function, then the limit of the function at any point equals its value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnlimci (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 D ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 F) (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a continuous function, then the limit of the function at any point equals its value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem cnmptlimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 D: class) (X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 X) (co A2 D ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wceq X Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cmpt x3 A2 X) B2 climc)) $;

--| If the limit of ` F ` at ` B ` is ` C ` and ` G ` is continuous at ` C ` , then the limit of ` G o. F ` at ` B ` is ` G ( C ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem limccnp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 D F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cc) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K D crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv C (co J K ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C G) (co (ccom G F) B2 climc)) $;

--| The image of a convergent sequence under a continuous map is convergent to the image of the original point. Binary operation version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem limccnp2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class) (R S: class x3) (H: class) (J K: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel S Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y cc) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (co K K ctx) (cxp X Y) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt x3 A2 R) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co (cmpt x3 A2 S) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv (cop C D) (co J K ccnp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C D H) (co (cmpt x3 A2 (co R S H)) B2 climc)) $;

--| Composition of two limits. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcco {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (B2 C D: class) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (T: class x3) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wne R C))) (wcel R B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt x3 A2 R) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co (cmpt y1 B2 S) C climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) R) (wceq S T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq R C))) (wceq T D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co (cmpt x3 A2 T) X climc)) $;

--| A point is a limit of ` F ` on the finite union ` U_ x e. A B ( x ) ` iff it is the limit of the restriction of ` F ` to each ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem limciun {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wss B2 cc) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (ciun x3 A2 B2) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C climc) (cin cc (ciin x3 A2 (co (cres F B2) C climc)))) $;

--| A point is a limit of ` F ` on ` A u. B ` iff it is the limit of the restriction of ` F ` to ` A ` and to ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem limcun (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cun A2 B2) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C climc) (cin (co (cres F A2) C climc) (co (cres F B2) C climc))) $;

--| Closure for a difference quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf D cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (cdif D (csn B2)))) (wcel (co (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) cmin) (co A2 B2 cmin) cdiv) cc) $;

--| Value and set bounds on the derivative operator. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvfval {x3 z: setvar} (A2 S: class) (T: class z) (F K: class):
  $ wceq T (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cc) (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 S)) (wa (wceq (co S F cdv) (ciun x3 (cfv A2 (cfv T cnt)) (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (co (cmpt z (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) (co (cv z) (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) (cv x3) climc)))) (wss (co S F cdv) (cxp (cfv A2 (cfv T cnt)) cc))) $;

--| The differentiable predicate. A function ` F ` is differentiable at ` B ` with derivative ` C ` iff ` F ` is defined in a neighborhood of ` B ` and the difference quotient has limit ` C ` at ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem eldv {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 C S: class) (T: class z) (F: class) (G: class z) (K: class):
  $ wceq T (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv B2 F) cmin) (co (cv z) B2 cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr B2 C (co S F cdv)) (wa (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv T cnt))) (wcel C (co G B2 climc)))) $;

--| The derivative function takes values in the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr B2 C (co S F cdv))) (wcel C cc) $;

--| The set of differentiable points is a subset of the interior of the domain of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvbssntr (ph: wff) (A2 S F J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm (co S F cdv)) (cfv A2 (cfv J cnt))) $;

--| The set of differentiable points is a subset of the domain of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvbss (ph: wff) (A2 S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm (co S F cdv)) A2) $;

--| The set of differentiable points is a subset of the ambient topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvbsss (S F: class):
  $ wss (cdm (co S F cdv)) S $;

--| The derivative is a function, whenever it is defined relative to a perfect subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem perfdvf (S F K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (co K S crest) cperf) (wf (cdm (co S F cdv)) cc (co S F cdv)) $;

--| Both ` RR ` and ` CC ` are subsets of ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem recnprss (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wss S cc) $;

--| Both ` RR ` and ` CC ` are perfect subsets of ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem recnperf (S K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel (co K S crest) cperf) $;

--| Explicitly write out the functionality condition on derivative for ` S = RR ` and ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvfg (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wf (cdm (co S F cdv)) cc (co S F cdv)) $;

--| The derivative is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvf (F: class):
  $ wf (cdm (co cr F cdv)) cc (co cr F cdv) $;

--| The derivative is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvfcn (F: class):
  $ wf (cdm (co cc F cdv)) cc (co cc F cdv) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvres . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.) Commute the consequent and shorten proof. (Revised by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem dvreslem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 S T F: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) (co (cv z) (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv y1) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co S (cres F B2) cdv)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv B2 (cfv T cnt))) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co S F cdv)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvres2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvres2lem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 S T F: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z (cdif A2 (csn (cv x3))) (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) (co (cv z) (cv x3) cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv y1) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co S F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (co B2 (cres F B2) cdv)) $;

--| Restriction of a derivative. Note that our definition of derivative ~ df-dv would still make sense if we demanded that ` x ` be an element of the domain instead of an interior point of the domain, but then it is possible for a non-differentiable function to have two different derivatives at a single point ` x ` when restricted to different subsets containing ` x ` ; a classic example is the absolute value function restricted to ` [ 0 , +oo ) ` and ` ( -oo , 0 ] ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvres (A2 B2 S T F K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq T (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss S cc) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wss A2 S) (wss B2 S))) (wceq (co S (cres F B2) cdv) (cres (co S F cdv) (cfv B2 (cfv T cnt)))) $;

--| Restriction of the base set of a derivative. The primary application of this theorem says that if a function is complex-differentiable then it is also real-differentiable. Unlike ~ dvres , there is no simple reverse relation relating real-differentiable functions to complex differentiability, and indeed there are functions like ` Re ( x ) ` which are everywhere real-differentiable but nowhere complex-differentiable.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvres2 (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wss S cc) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wss A2 S) (wss B2 S))) (wss (cres (co S F cdv) B2) (co B2 (cres F B2) cdv)) $;

--| Restriction of a complex differentiable function to the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvres3 (A2 S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wss A2 cc) (wss S (cdm (co cc F cdv))))) (wceq (co S (cres F S) cdv) (cres (co cc F cdv) S)) $;

--| Restriction of a complex differentiable function to the reals. This version of ~ dvres3 assumes that ` F ` is differentiable on its domain, but does not require ` F ` to be differentiable on the whole real line. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvres3a (A2 S F J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wcel A2 J) (wceq (cdm (co cc F cdv)) A2))) (wceq (co S (cres F S) cdv) (cres (co cc F cdv) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvid and ~ dvconst . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvidlem {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cc cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) cc) (wcel (cv z) cc) (wne (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) (co (cv z) (cv x3) cmin) cdiv) B2) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) (cxp cc (csn B2))) $;

--| Derivative of a function restricted to a closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvmptresicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C D: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cc)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) (cmpt x3 cc B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cc)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co C D cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co C D cioo) B2)) $;

--| Derivative of a constant function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvconst (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc (cxp cc (csn A2)) cdv) (cxp cc (csn cc0))) $;

--| Derivative of the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvid:
  $ wceq (co cc (cres cid cc) cdv) (cxp cc (csn c1)) $;

--| The difference quotient is continuous at ` B ` when the original function is differentiable at ` B ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvcnp {z: setvar} (A2 B2 S F: class) (G: class z) (J K: class):
  $ wceq J (co K A2 crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt z A2 (cif (wceq (cv z) B2) (cfv B2 (co S F cdv)) (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv B2 F) cmin) (co (cv z) B2 cmin) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 S)) (wcel B2 (cdm (co S F cdv)))) (wcel G (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp))) $;

--| A function is continuous at each point for which it is differentiable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem dvcnp2 (A2 B2 S F J K: class):
  $ wceq J (co K A2 crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss S cc) (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 S)) (wcel B2 (cdm (co S F cdv)))) (wcel F (cfv B2 (co J K ccnp))) $;

--| A differentiable function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2015.)
theorem dvcn (A2 S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss S cc) (wf A2 cc F) (wss A2 S)) (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) A2)) (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| Value of the iterated derivative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnfval {x3: setvar} (S F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cvv (co S (cv x3) cdv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel F (co cc S cpm))) (wceq (co S F cdvn) (cseq (ccom G c1st) (cxp cn0 (csn F)) cc0)) $;

--| The iterated derivative is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnff (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc S cpm))) (wf cn0 (co cc (cdm F) cpm) (co S F cdvn)) $;

--| Zero times iterated derivative. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvn0 (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel F (co cc S cpm))) (wceq (cfv cc0 (co S F cdvn)) F) $;

--| Successor iterated derivative. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnp1 (S F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cc) (wcel F (co cc S cpm)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (co S F cdvn)) (co S (cfv N (co S F cdvn)) cdv)) $;

--| One times iterated derivative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvn1 (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel F (co cc S cpm))) (wceq (cfv c1 (co S F cdvn)) (co S F cdv)) $;

--| The N-times derivative is a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnf (S F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc S cpm)) (wcel N cn0)) (wf (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn))) cc (cfv N (co S F cdvn))) $;

--| The set of N-times differentiable points is a subset of the domain of the function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnbss (S F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc S cpm)) (wcel N cn0)) (wss (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn))) (cdm F)) $;

--| The ` N ` -th derivative of the ` M ` -th derivative of ` F ` is the same as the ` M + N ` -th derivative of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnadd (S F M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc S cpm))) (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0))) (wceq (cfv N (co S (cfv M (co S F cdvn)) cdvn)) (cfv (co M N caddc) (co S F cdvn))) $;

--| An N-times differentiable point is an M-times differentiable point, if ` M <_ N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvn2bss (S F M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc S cpm)) (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz))) (wss (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn))) (cdm (cfv M (co S F cdvn)))) $;

--| Multiple derivative version of ~ dvres3a . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvnres (S F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (co cc cc cpm)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cdm (cfv N (co cc F cdvn))) (cdm F))) (wceq (cfv N (co S (cres F S) cdvn)) (cres (cfv N (co cc F cdvn)) S)) $;

--| Condition for n-times continuous differentiability. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cpnfval {f2 n: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wss S cc) (wceq (cfv S ccpn) (cmpt n cn0 (crab f2 (wcel (cfv (cv n) (co S (cv f2) cdvn)) (co (cdm (cv f2)) cc ccncf)) (co cc S cpm)))) $;

--| The ` C^n ` object is a function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem fncpn (S: class):
  $ wi (wss S cc) (wfn (cfv S ccpn) cn0) $;

--| Condition for n-times continuous differentiability. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem elcpn (S F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wb (wcel F (cfv N (cfv S ccpn))) (wa (wcel F (co cc S cpm)) (wcel (cfv N (co S F cdvn)) (co (cdm F) cc ccncf)))) $;

--| ` C^n ` conditions are ordered by strength. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cpnord (S M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel M cn0) (wcel N (cfv M cuz))) (wss (cfv N (cfv S ccpn)) (cfv M (cfv S ccpn))) $;

--| A ` C^n ` function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cpncn (S F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (cfv N (cfv S ccpn)))) (wcel F (co (cdm F) cc ccncf)) $;

--| The restriction of a ` C^n ` function is ` C^n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cpnres (S F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel F (cfv N (cfv cc ccpn)))) (wcel (cres F S) (cfv N (cfv S ccpn))) $;

--| The sum rule for derivatives at a point. For the (simpler but more limited) function version, see ~ dvadd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.) Remove unnecessary hypotheses. (Revised by GG, 10-Apr-2025.)
theorem dvaddbr (ph: wff) (C S F G J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C K (co S F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C L (co S G cdv)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (co K L caddc) (co S (co F G (cof caddc)) cdv)) $;

--| The product rule for derivatives at a point. For the (simpler but more limited) function version, see ~ dvmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf and remove unnecessary hypotheses. (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem dvmulbr (ph: wff) (C S F G J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C K (co S F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C L (co S G cdv)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (co (co K (cfv C G) cmul) (co L (cfv C F) cmul) caddc) (co S (co F G (cof cmul)) cdv)) $;

--| The sum rule for derivatives at a point. For the (more general) relation version, see ~ dvaddbr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvadd (ph: wff) (C S F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co S F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co S G cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C (co S (co F G (cof caddc)) cdv)) (co (cfv C (co S F cdv)) (cfv C (co S G cdv)) caddc)) $;

--| The product rule for derivatives at a point. For the (more general) relation version, see ~ dvmulbr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmul (ph: wff) (C S F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co S F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co S G cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C (co S (co F G (cof cmul)) cdv)) (co (co (cfv C (co S F cdv)) (cfv C G) cmul) (co (cfv C (co S G cdv)) (cfv C F) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The sum rule for everywhere-differentiable functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvaddf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S G cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (co F G (cof caddc)) cdv) (co (co S F cdv) (co S G cdv) (cof caddc))) $;

--| The product rule for everywhere-differentiable functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmulf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S G cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (co F G (cof cmul)) cdv) (co (co (co S F cdv) G (cof cmul)) (co (co S G cdv) F (cof cmul)) (cof caddc))) $;

--| The product rule when one argument is a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcmul (ph: wff) (A2 C S F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co S F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C (co S (co (cxp S (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) cdv)) (co A2 (cfv C (co S F cdv)) cmul)) $;

--| The product rule when one argument is a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcmulf (ph: wff) (A2 S F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (co (cxp S (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) cdv) (co (cxp S (csn A2)) (co S F cdv) (cof cmul))) $;

--| The chain rule for derivatives at a point. For the (simpler but more limited) function version, see ~ dvco . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf and remove unnecessary hypotheses. (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem dvcobr (ph: wff) (C S T F G J K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv C G) K (co S F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C L (co T G cdv)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (co K L cmul) (co T (ccom F G) cdv)) $;

--| The chain rule for derivatives at a point. For the (more general) relation version, see ~ dvcobr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvco (ph: wff) (C S T F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C G) (cdm (co S F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co T G cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C (co T (ccom F G) cdv)) (co (cfv (cfv C G) (co S F cdv)) (cfv C (co T G cdv)) cmul)) $;

--| The chain rule for everywhere-differentiable functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcof (ph: wff) (S T F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y X G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co T G cdv)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T (ccom F G) cdv) (co (ccom (co S F cdv) G) (co T G cdv) (cof cmul))) $;

--| The derivative of the conjugate of a function. For the (simpler but more limited) function version, see ~ dvcj . (This doesn't follow from ~ dvcobr because ` * ` is not a function on the reals, and even if we used complex derivatives, ` * ` is not complex-differentiable.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcjbr (ph: wff) (C F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (cfv (cfv C (co cr F cdv)) ccj) (co cr (ccom ccj F) cdv)) $;

--| The derivative of the conjugate of a function. For the (more general) relation version, see ~ dvcjbr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcj (F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf X cc F) (wss X cr)) (wceq (co cr (ccom ccj F) cdv) (ccom ccj (co cr F cdv))) $;

--| The derivative of a real function is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem dvfre (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 cr F) (wss A2 cr)) (wf (cdm (co cr F cdv)) cr (co cr F cdv)) $;

--| The ` N ` -th derivative of a real function is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvnfre (A2 F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cr F) (wss A2 cr) (wcel N cn0)) (wf (cdm (cfv N (co cr F cdvn))) cr (cfv N (co cr F cdvn))) $;

--| Derivative of a power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvexp {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) N cexp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (co N (co (cv x3) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| Derivative of an exponential, possibly zero power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvexp2 {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) N cexp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (cif (wceq N cc0) cc0 (co N (co (cv x3) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Derivative of the reciprocal function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvrec {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co A2 (cv x3) cdiv)) cdv) (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cneg (co A2 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cdiv)))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: restrict a derivative to a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptres3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S J V: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin S X) Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 Y A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 Y B2)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: derivative of the identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 S (cv x3)) cdv) (cmpt x3 S c1)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: derivative of a constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S cc0)) $;

--| Closure lemma for ~ dvmptcmul and other related theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptcl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 cc) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, addition rule. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptadd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (S V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X C) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C caddc)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co B2 D caddc))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, product rule. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptmul {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (S V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X C) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cmul)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co (co B2 C cmul) (co D A2 cmul) caddc))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: restrict a derivative to a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptres2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S: class) (J K: class x3) (V X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z X) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z (cfv J cnt)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 Z A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 Y B2)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: restrict a derivative to an open subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptres {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S: class) (J K: class x3) (V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y X) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 Y A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 Y B2)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, product rule for constant multiplier. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptcmul {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co C A2 cmul)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co C B2 cmul))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, division rule for constant divisor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvmptdivc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cdiv)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co B2 C cdiv))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, product rule for negatives. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptneg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (cneg A2)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (cneg B2))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, subtraction rule. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptsub {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (S V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X C) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cmin)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co B2 D cmin))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, conjugate rule. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptcj {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X (cfv A2 ccj)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (cfv B2 ccj))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, real part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem dvmptre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X (cfv A2 cre)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (cfv B2 cre))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, imaginary part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem dvmptim {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 X (cfv A2 cim)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (cfv B2 cim))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: expand the function from an open set to its closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvmptntr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S J K: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv J cnt)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (co S (cmpt x3 Y A2) cdv)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, chain rule. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem dvmptco {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 y1) (C D: class y1) (S: class x3 y1) (T E F: class x3) (V: class y1) (W: class x3) (X: class y1) (Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) Y)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) Y)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T (cmpt y1 Y C) cdv) (cmpt y1 Y D)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) A2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) A2) (wceq D F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X E) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co F B2 cmul))) $;

--| Derivative of the reciprocal of a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvrecg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X B2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cdiv)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (cneg (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 c2 cexp) cdiv)))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, quotient rule. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvmptdiv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (S V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X C) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cdiv)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co (co (co B2 C cmul) (co D A2 cmul) cmin) (co C c2 cexp) cdiv))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, finite sums rule. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
theorem dvmptfsum {x3 i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 i) (S I: class) (J K: class x3 i) (X: class):
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) I) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) I) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) I)) (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (csu i I A2)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (csu i I B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvcnvre . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem dvcnvlem (ph: wff) (C S F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J S crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccnv F) (co Y X ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co S F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv C F) (co c1 (cfv C (co S F cdv)) cdiv) (co S (ccnv F) cdv)) $;

--| A weak version of ~ dvcnvre , valid for both real and complex domains but under the hypothesis that the inverse function is already known to be continuous, and the image set is known to be open. A more advanced proof can show that these conditions are unnecessary. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem dvcnv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F J: class) (K X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J S crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccnv F) (co Y X ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co S F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (ccnv F) cdv) (cmpt x3 Y (co c1 (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (ccnv F)) (co S F cdv)) cdiv))) $;

--| Derivative of an exponential of integer exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvexp3 {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co (cv x3) N cexp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co N (co (cv x3) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| Derivative of the exponential function at 0. The key step in the proof is ~ eftlub , to show that ` abs ( exp ( x ) - 1 - x ) <_ abs ( x ) ^ 2 x. ( 3 / 4 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dveflem:
  $ wbr cc0 c1 (co cc ce cdv) $;

--| Derivative of the exponential function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvef:
  $ wceq (co cc ce cdv) ce $;

--| Derivative of the sine and cosine functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem dvsincos {x3: setvar}:
  $ wa (wceq (co cc csin cdv) ccos) (wceq (co cc ccos cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (cneg (cfv (cv x3) csin)))) $;

--| Derivative of the sine function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem dvsin:
  $ wceq (co cc csin cdv) ccos $;

--| Derivative of the cosine function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem dvcos {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cc ccos cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (cneg (cfv (cv x3) csin))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvferm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvferm1lem {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 S: class) (T: class y1) (U F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co U B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wa (wne (cv z) U) (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) U cmin) cabs) T clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv U F) cmin) (co (cv z) U cmin) cdiv) (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cmin) cabs) (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) clt)) (cdif X (csn U))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co U (cif (wbr B2 (co U T caddc) cle) B2 (co U T caddc)) caddc) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| One-sided version of ~ dvferm . A point ` U ` which is the local maximum of its right neighborhood has derivative at most zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvferm1 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co U B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cc0 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvferm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvferm2lem {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2 S: class) (T: class y1) (U F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co A2 U cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wi (wa (wne (cv z) U) (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) U cmin) cabs) T clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv U F) cmin) (co (cv z) U cmin) cdiv) (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cmin) cabs) (cneg (cfv U (co cr F cdv))) clt)) (cdif X (csn U))) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co (cif (wbr A2 (co U T cmin) cle) (co U T cmin) A2) U caddc) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| One-sided version of ~ dvferm . A point ` U ` which is the local maximum of its left neighborhood has derivative at least zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvferm2 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co A2 U cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cle) $;

--| Fermat's theorem on stationary points. A point ` U ` which is a local maximum has derivative equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem dvferm {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv U (co cr F cdv)) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rolle . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem rollelem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 U F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv U F) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel U (cpr A2 B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cc0) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Rolle's theorem. If ` F ` is a real continuous function on ` [ A , B ] ` which is differentiable on ` ( A , B ) ` , and ` F ( A ) = F ( B ) ` , then there is some ` x e. ( A , B ) ` such that ` ( RR _D F ) `` x = 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem rolle {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cc0) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Cauchy's Mean Value Theorem. If ` F , G ` are real continuous functions on ` [ A , B ] ` differentiable on ` ( A , B ) ` , then there is some ` x e. ( A , B ) ` such that ` F ` ' ` ( x ) / G ` ' ` ( x ) = ` ` ( F ( A ) - F ( B ) ) / ( G ( A ) - G ( B ) ) ` . (We express the condition without division, so that we need no nonzero constraints.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem cmvth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr G cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (co (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) cmin) (cfv (cv x3) (co cr G cdv)) cmul) (co (co (cfv B2 G) (cfv A2 G) cmin) (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cmul)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| The Mean Value Theorem. If ` F ` is a real continuous function on ` [ A , B ] ` which is differentiable on ` ( A , B ) ` , then there is some ` x e. ( A , B ) ` such that ` ( RR _D F ) `` x ` is equal to the average slope over ` [ A , B ] ` . This is Metamath 100 proof #75. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem mvth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) (co (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) cmin) (co B2 A2 cmin) cdiv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| A function with derivative bounded by ` M ` is ` M `-Lipschitz continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvlip {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M: class) (X Y: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) M cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) cmin) cabs) (co M (cfv (co X Y cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) $;

--| A complex function with derivative bounded by ` M ` on an open ball is ` M `-Lipschitz continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvlipcn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class) (R: class x3) (F M: class) (X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cdm (co cc F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cc F cdv)) cabs) M cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv Y F) (cfv Z F) cmin) cabs) (co M (cfv (co Y Z cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) $;

--| Combine the results of ~ dvlip and ~ dvlipcn into one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem dvlip2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S F J M: class) (X: class x3) (Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 R (cfv J cbl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cdm (co S F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co S F cdv)) cabs) M cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv Y F) (cfv Z F) cmin) cabs) (co M (cfv (co Y Z cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ c1lip1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem c1liplem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cr cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres (co cr F cdv) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq K (csup (cima cabs (cima (co cr F cdv) (co A2 B2 cicc))) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K cr) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) cabs) (co K (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmin) cabs) cmul) cle)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| C^1 functions are Lipschitz continuous on closed intervals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem c1lip1 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cr cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres (co cr F cdv) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) cabs) (co (cv k) (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cr) $;

--| C^1 functions are Lipschitz continuous on closed intervals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 6-May-2015.)
theorem c1lip2 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv c1 (cfv cr ccpn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) cabs) (co (cv k) (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cr) $;

--| C^1 functions are Lipschitz continuous on closed intervals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem c1lip3 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F cr) (cfv c1 (cfv cr ccpn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima F cr) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmin) cabs) (co (cv k) (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmin) cabs) cmul) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cr) $;

--| If a continuous function has zero derivative at all points on the interior of a closed interval, then it must be a constant function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem dveq0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr F cdv) (cxp (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cxp (co A2 B2 cicc) (csn (cfv A2 F)))) $;

--| Two functions defined on a ball whose derivatives are the same and which are equal at any given point ` C ` in the ball must be equal everywhere. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem dv11cn (ph: wff) (A2 C R F G X: class):
  $ wceq X (co A2 R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cc F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) (co cc G cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C F) (cfv C G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvgt0 and ~ dvlt0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvgt0lem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) S (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cicc)))) (wbr X Y clt)) (wcel (co (co (cfv Y F) (cfv X F) cmin) (co Y X cmin) cdiv) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvgt0 and ~ dvlt0 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvgt0lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (S: class x3 y1) (F O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) S (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co A2 B2 cicc)))) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co A2 B2 cicc) (crn F) clt O F) $;

--| A function on a closed interval with positive derivative is increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvgt0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) crp (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co A2 B2 cicc) (crn F) clt clt F) $;

--| A function on a closed interval with negative derivative is decreasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvlt0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) (co cmnf cc0 cioo) (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co A2 B2 cicc) (crn F) clt (ccnv clt) F) $;

--| A function on a closed interval with nonnegative derivative is weakly increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2016.)
theorem dvge0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) (co cc0 cpnf cico) (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) cle) $;

--| If ` A ( x ) , C ( x ) ` are differentiable functions and ` A `` <_ C `` ` , then for ` x <_ y ` , ` A ( y ) - A ( x ) <_ C ( y ) - C ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-May-2016.)
theorem dvle {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (P Q R S M N X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co M N cicc) A2) (co (co M N cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co M N cicc) C) (co (co M N cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) C) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cioo))) (wbr B2 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co M N cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co M N cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq A2 P) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq C Q) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq A2 R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co R P cmin) (co S Q cmin) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvivth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvivthlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G: class y1) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cfv N (co cr F cdv)) (cfv M (co cr F cdv)) cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co C (cv y1) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) C) (co M N cicc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvivth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvivthlem2 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (G: class y1) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cfv N (co cr F cdv)) (cfv M (co cr F cdv)) cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (co C (cv y1) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (crn (co cr F cdv))) $;

--| Darboux' theorem, or the intermediate value theorem for derivatives. A differentiable function's derivative satisfies the intermediate value property, even though it may not be continuous (so that ~ ivthicc does not directly apply). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvivth (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cfv M (co cr F cdv)) (cfv N (co cr F cdv)) cicc) (crn (co cr F cdv))) $;

--| A function on a closed interval with nonzero derivative is either monotone increasing or monotone decreasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvne0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wiso (co A2 B2 cicc) (crn F) clt clt F) (wiso (co A2 B2 cicc) (crn F) clt (ccnv clt) F)) $;

--| A function on a closed interval with nonzero derivative is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvne0f1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co A2 B2 cicc) cr F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lhop1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem lhop1lem {z t r: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (B2: class t) (C: class) (D: class z r) (R: class t r) (E: class z) (F G: class) (X: class t):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr G cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co G A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr G cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) (co cr F cdv)) (cfv (cv z) (co cr G cdv)) cdiv)) A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 D cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (co (cfv (cv t) (co cr F cdv)) (cfv (cv t) (co cr G cdv)) cdiv) C cmin) cabs) E clt) (co A2 D cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq R (co A2 (co (cv r) c2 cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co (cfv X F) (cfv X G) cdiv) C cmin) cabs) (co c2 E cmul) clt) $;

--| L'H&ocirc;pital's Rule for limits from the right. If ` F ` and ` G ` are differentiable real functions on ` ( A , B ) ` , and ` F ` and ` G ` both approach 0 at ` A ` , and ` G ( x ) ` and ` G ` ' ` ( x ) ` are not zero on ` ( A , B ) ` , and the limit of ` F ` ' ` ( x ) / G ` ' ` ( x ) ` at ` A ` is ` C ` , then the limit ` F ( x ) / G ( x ) ` at ` A ` also exists and equals ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem lhop1 {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr G cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co G A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr G cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) (co cr F cdv)) (cfv (cv z) (co cr G cdv)) cdiv)) A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv z) G) cdiv)) A2 climc)) $;

--| L'H&ocirc;pital's Rule for limits from the left. If ` F ` and ` G ` are differentiable real functions on ` ( A , B ) ` , and ` F ` and ` G ` both approach 0 at ` B ` , and ` G ( x ) ` and ` G ` ' ` ( x ) ` are not zero on ` ( A , B ) ` , and the limit of ` F ` ' ` ( x ) / G ` ' ` ( x ) ` at ` B ` is ` C ` , then the limit ` F ( x ) / G ( x ) ` at ` B ` also exists and equals ` C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem lhop2 {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr G cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co G B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr G cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) (co cr F cdv)) (cfv (cv z) (co cr G cdv)) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv z) G) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $;

--| L'H&ocirc;pital's Rule. If ` I ` is an open set of the reals, ` F ` and ` G ` are real functions on ` A ` containing all of ` I ` except possibly ` B ` , which are differentiable everywhere on ` I \ { B } ` , ` F ` and ` G ` both approach 0, and the limit of ` F ` ' ` ( x ) / G ` ' ` ( x ) ` at ` B ` is ` C ` , then the limit ` F ( x ) / G ( x ) ` at ` B ` also exists and equals ` C ` . This is Metamath 100 proof #64. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem lhop {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z) (B2 C D F G I: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 I) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif I (csn B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cdm (co cr F cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cdm (co cr G cdv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co G B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (cima G D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (cima (co cr G cdv) D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z D (co (cfv (cv z) (co cr F cdv)) (cfv (cv z) (co cr G cdv)) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cmpt z D (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv (cv z) G) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvcnvre . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcnvrelem1 (ph: wff) (C R F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co C R cmin) (co C R caddc) cicc) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C F) (cfv (cima F (co (co C R cmin) (co C R caddc) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cnt))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvcnvre . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem dvcnvrelem2 (ph: wff) (C R T F J M N X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co C R cmin) (co C R caddc) cicc) X) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq M (co J X crest) $ >
  $ wceq N (co J Y crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cfv C F) (cfv Y (cfv T cnt))) (wcel (ccnv F) (cfv (cfv C F) (co N M ccnp)))) $;

--| The derivative rule for inverse functions. If ` F ` is a continuous and differentiable bijective function from ` X ` to ` Y ` which never has derivative ` 0 ` , then ` ``' F ` is also differentiable, and its derivative is the reciprocal of the derivative of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcnvre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (crn (co cr F cdv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o X Y F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (ccnv F) cdv) (cmpt x3 Y (co c1 (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (ccnv F)) (co cr F cdv)) cdiv))) $;

--| A real function with strictly increasing derivative is strictly convex. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem dvcvx (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C T F W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co A2 B2 cioo) W clt clt (co cr F cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co T A2 cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) B2 cmul) caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv C F) (co (co T (cfv A2 F) cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) (cfv B2 F) cmul) caddc) clt) $;

--| Compare a finite sum to an integral (the integral here is given as a function with a known derivative). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-May-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem dvfsumle {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 k) (C D: class k) (M N: class) (V: class x3 k) (X: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co M N cicc) A2) (co (co M N cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cioo))) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) M) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) N) (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo)) (wcel (cv x3) (co (cv k) (co (cv k) c1 caddc) cioo)))) (wbr X B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csu k (co M N cfzo) X) (co D C cmin) cle) $;

--| Compare a finite sum to an integral (the integral here is given as a function with a known derivative). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumge {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 k) (C D: class k) (M N: class) (V X: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co M N cicc) A2) (co (co M N cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cioo))) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) M) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) N) (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo)) (wcel (cv x3) (co (cv k) (co (cv k) c1 caddc) cioo)))) (wbr B2 X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co D C cmin) (csu k (co M N cfzo) X) cle) $;

--| Compare a finite sum to an integral (the integral here is given as a function with a known derivative). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumabs {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 k) (C D: class k) (M N: class) (V X Y: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co M N cicc) A2) (co (co M N cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cioo))) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co M N cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) M) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) N) (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo)) (wcel (cv x3) (co (cv k) (co (cv k) c1 caddc) cioo)))) (wbr (cfv (co X B2 cmin) cabs) Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (csu k (co M N cfzo) X) (co D C cmin) cmin) cabs) (csu k (co M N cfzo) Y) cle) $;

--| Real closure of a derivative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvmptrecl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (S: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss S cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvfsumrlim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumrlimf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (G: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cr G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvfsumrlim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumlem1 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (U: class) (H: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (X Y: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cxr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (w3a (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle) (wbr (cv k) U cle))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 S (co (co (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cmin) B2 cmul) (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y U cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y (co (cfv X cfl) c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y H) (co (co (co (co Y (cfv X cfl) cmin) (csb x3 Y B2) cmul) (csb x3 Y A2) cmin) (csu k (co M (cfv X cfl) cfz) C) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvfsumrlim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem dvfsumlem2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (U: class) (H: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (X Y: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
