  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) U cle))) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y U cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv Y G) (cfv X G) cmin) cabs) (csb x3 X B2) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvfsumrlim . Satisfy the assumption of ~ dvfsumlem4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumrlimge0 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (G: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (wa (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 S B2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wbr D (cv x3) cle))) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $;

--| Compare a finite sum to an integral (the integral here is given as a function with a known derivative). The statement here says that if ` x e. S |-> B ` is a decreasing function with antiderivative ` A ` converging to zero, then the difference between ` sum_ k e. ( M ... ( |_ `` x ) ) B ( k ) ` and ` A ( x ) = S. u e. ( M [,] x ) B ( u ) _d u ` converges to a constant limit value, with the remainder term bounded by ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumrlim {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (G: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (wa (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 S B2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm crli)) $;

--| Compare a finite sum to an integral (the integral here is given as a function with a known derivative). The statement here says that if ` x e. S |-> B ` is a decreasing function with antiderivative ` A ` converging to zero, then the difference between ` sum_ k e. ( M ... ( |_ `` x ) ) B ( k ) ` and ` S. u e. ( M [,] x ) B ( u ) _d u = A ( x ) ` converges to a constant limit value, with the remainder term bounded by ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumrlim2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (G L: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (wa (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 S B2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D X cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr G L crli)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv X G) L cmin) cabs) (csb x3 X B2) cle) $;

--| Conjoin the statements of ~ dvfsumrlim and ~ dvfsumrlim2 . (This is useful as a target for lemmas, because the hypotheses to this theorem are complex, and we don't want to repeat ourselves.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsumrlim3 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T E: class k) (G L: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class x3 k) (X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (wa (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 S B2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wf S cr G) (wcel G (cdm crli)) (wi (w3a (wbr G L crli) (wcel X S) (wbr D X cle)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv X G) L cmin) cabs) E cle))) $;

--| The reverse of ~ dvfsumrlim , when comparing a finite sum of _increasing_ terms to an integral. In this case there is no point in stating the limit properties, because the terms of the sum aren't approaching zero, but there is nevertheless still a natural asymptotic statement that can be made. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2016.)
theorem dvfsum2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D S: class) (T: class k) (U: class) (E: class k) (G: class x3 k) (M: class) (V: class k) (X Y: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wceq S (co T cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (co D c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 S A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 S B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv k) S)) (w3a (wbr D (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cv k) cle) (wbr (cv k) U cle))) (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 S (co (csu k (co M (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) C) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wbr D (cv x3) cle))) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y U cle) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv Y G) (cfv X G) cmin) cabs) E cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1a and ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2014.)
theorem ftc1lem1 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class) (G: class x3 t) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr X Y cle)) (wceq (co (cfv Y G) (cfv X G) cmin) (citg t (co X Y cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Aug-2014.)
theorem ftc1lem2 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class) (G: class x3 t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc G) $;

--| The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, part one. The function ` G ` formed by varying the right endpoint of an integral of ` F ` is continuous if ` F ` is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc1a {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class) (G: class x3 t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem ftc1lem3 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F: class) (G J K: class x3 t) (L: class t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv C (co K L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co L cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co L D crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cc F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2014.)
theorem ftc1lem4 {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class) (R: class x3 z t) (E: class x3 z) (F: class) (G: class x3 t) (H: class x3 z t) (J K: class x3 y1 z t) (L: class t) (X: class y1) (Y: class y1 z):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv C (co K L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co L cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co L D crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt z (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (csn C)) (co (co (cfv (cv z) G) (cfv C G) cmin) (co (cv z) C cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv y1) C cmin) cabs) R clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) E clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co X C cmin) cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co Y C cmin) cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr X Y clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (co (cfv Y G) (cfv X G) cmin) (co Y X cmin) cdiv) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) E clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem ftc1lem5 {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class) (R: class x3 z t) (E: class x3 z) (F: class) (G: class x3 t) (H: class x3 z t) (J K: class x3 y1 z t) (L: class t) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv C (co K L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co L cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co L D crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt z (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (csn C)) (co (co (cfv (cv z) G) (cfv C G) cmin) (co (cv z) C cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) D)) (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv y1) C cmin) cabs) R clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) E clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co X C cmin) cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wne X C)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv X H) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) E clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem ftc1lem6 {x3 z t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F: class) (G: class x3 t) (H J K: class x3 z t) (L: class t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv C (co K L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co L cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co L D crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt z (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (csn C)) (co (co (cfv (cv z) G) (cfv C G) cmin) (co (cv z) C cmin) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C F) (co H C climc)) $;

--| The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, part one. The function formed by varying the right endpoint of an integral is differentiable at ` C ` with derivative ` F ( C ) ` if the original function is continuous at ` C ` . This is part of Metamath 100 proof #15. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc1 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F: class) (G J K: class x3 t) (L: class t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv C (co K L ccnp))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co L cr crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (co L D crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C (cfv C F) (co cr G cdv)) $;

--| Strengthen the assumptions of ~ ftc1 to when the function ` F ` is continuous on the entire interval ` ( A , B ) ` ; in this case we can calculate ` _D G ` exactly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc1cn {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (G: class x3 t):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (citg t (co A2 (cv x3) cioo) (cfv (cv t) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr G cdv) F) $;

--| The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, part two. If ` F ` is a function continuous on ` [ A , B ] ` and continuously differentiable on ` ( A , B ) ` , then the integral of the derivative of ` F ` is equal to ` F ( B ) - F ( A ) ` . This is part of Metamath 100 proof #15. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc2 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg t (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cv t) (co cr F cdv))) (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) cmin)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ftc2ditg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc2ditglem {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co X Y cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cdit t A2 B2 (cfv (cv t) (co cr F cdv))) (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) cmin)) $;

--| Directed integral analogue of ~ ftc2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftc2ditg {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr F cdv) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co X Y cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit t A2 B2 (cfv (cv t) (co cr F cdv))) (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) cmin)) $;

--| Integration by parts. If ` B ( x ) ` is the derivative of ` A ( x ) ` and ` D ( x ) ` is the derivative of ` C ( x ) ` , and ` E = ( A x. B ) ( X ) ` and ` F = ( A x. B ) ( Y ) ` , then under suitable integrability and differentiability assumptions, the integral of ` A x. D ` from ` X ` to ` Y ` is equal to ` F - E ` minus the integral of ` B x. C ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgparts {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (E F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) (co (co X Y cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) C) (co (co X Y cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2) (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) D) (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) (co A2 D cmul)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) (co B2 C cmul)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) C) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) X)) (wceq (co A2 C cmul) E) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) Y)) (wceq (co A2 C cmul) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co X Y cioo) (co A2 D cmul)) (co (co F E cmin) (citg x3 (co X Y cioo) (co B2 C cmul)) cmin)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itgsubst . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgsubstlem {x3 u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 u) (C: class u) (E: class x3) (K L M N W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) (co (co X Y cicc) (co Z W cioo) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2) (cin (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf) cibl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt u (co Z W cioo) C) (co (co Z W cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv u) A2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq A2 K) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co Z W cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co Z W cioo)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co X Y cicc))) (wcel A2 (co M N cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit u K L C) (cdit x3 X Y (co E B2 cmul))) $;

--| Integration by ` u ` -substitution. If ` A ( x ) ` is a continuous, differentiable function from ` [ X , Y ] ` to ` ( Z , W ) ` , whose derivative is continuous and integrable, and ` C ( u ) ` is a continuous function on ` ( Z , W ) ` , then the integral of ` C ( u ) ` from ` K = A ( X ) ` to ` L = A ( Y ) ` is equal to the integral of ` C ( A ( x ) ) _D A ( x ) ` from ` X ` to ` Y ` . In this part of the proof we discharge the assumptions in ~ itgsubstlem , which use the fact that ` ( Z , W ) ` is open to shrink the interval a little to ` ( M , N ) ` where ` Z < M < N < W ` - this is possible because ` A ( x ) ` is a continuous function on a closed interval, so its range is in fact a closed interval, and we have some wiggle room on the edges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Sep-2014.)
theorem itgsubst {x3 u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 u) (C: class u) (E: class x3) (K L W X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) (co (co X Y cicc) (co Z W cioo) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2) (cin (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf) cibl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt u (co Z W cioo) C) (co (co Z W cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv u) A2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq A2 K) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit u K L C) (cdit x3 X Y (co E B2 cmul))) $;

--| The integral of a monomial on a closed bounded interval of the real line. Co-authors TA and MC. (Contributed by Jon Pennant, 31-May-2019.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jun-2019.)
theorem itgpowd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cv x3) N cexp)) (co (co (co B2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) (co A2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) cmin) (co N c1 caddc) cdiv)) $;

--| Multivariate polynomial degree.
term cmdg: class;

--| Univariate polynomial degree.
term cdg1: class;

--| Define the degree of a polynomial. Note (SO): as an experiment I am using a definition which makes the degree of the zero polynomial ` -oo ` , contrary to the convention used in ~ df-dgr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_mdeg {f2 h i r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmdg (cmpo i r cvv cvv (cmpt f2 (cfv (co (cv i) (cv r) cmpl) cbs) (csup (crn (cmpt h (co (cv f2) (cfv (cv r) c0g) csupp) (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu))) cxr clt))) $;

--| Define the degree of a univariate polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_deg1 {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdg1 (cmpt r cvv (co c1o (cv r) cmdg)) $;

--| Multivariate degree is a binary operation. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem reldmmdeg:
  $ wrel (cdm cmdg) $;

--| Functionality of the total degree helper function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.) Remove sethood antecedent. (Revised by SN, 7-Aug-2024.)
theorem tdeglem1 {h m: setvar} (A2: class m) (H: class h m) (I: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wf A2 cn0 H $;

--| Additivity of the total degree helper function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.) Remove a sethood antecedent. (Revised by SN, 7-Aug-2024.)
theorem tdeglem3 {h m: setvar} (A2: class m) (H: class h m) (I X Y: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y A2)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y (cof caddc)) H) (co (cfv X H) (cfv Y H) caddc)) $;

--| There is only one multi-index with total degree 0. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) Remove a sethood antecedent. (Revised by SN, 7-Aug-2024.)
theorem tdeglem4 {h m: setvar} (A2: class m) (H: class h m) (I X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X A2) (wb (wceq (cfv X H) cc0) (wceq X (cxp I (csn cc0)))) $;

--| Simplification of total degree for the univariate case. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem tdeglem2 {h: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cmpt h (co cn0 c1o cmap) (cfv c0 (cv h))) (cmpt h (co cn0 c1o cmap) (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $;

--| Value of the multivariate degree function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2019.)
theorem mdegfval {f2 h m: setvar} (A2: class f2 m) (B2: class h m) (D P: class f2 h m) (R: class h m) (H: class f2 h m) (I: class h) (c_0: class f2 m):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt f2 B2 (csup (cima H (co (cv f2) c_0 csupp)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Value of the multivariate degree function at some particular polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2019.)
theorem mdegval {h m: setvar} (A2: class m) (B2 D P R F H: class h m) (I: class h) (c_0: class m):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wceq (cfv F D) (csup (cima H (co F c_0 csupp)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Property of being of limited degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegleb {x3 h m: setvar} (A2: class x3 m) (B2: class h m) (D P: class x3 h m) (R F G H: class h m) (I: class x3) (c_0: class m):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel G cxr)) (wb (wbr (cfv F D) G cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr G (cfv (cv x3) H) clt) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) c_0)) A2)) $;

--| If there is an upper limit on the degree of a polynomial that is lower than the degree of some exponent bag, then that exponent bag is unrepresented in the polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdeglt {h m: setvar} (ph: wff h m) (A2: class m) (B2 D P R F H: class h m) (I: class) (X: class h m) (c_0: class m):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) (cfv X H) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X F) c_0) $;

--| A nonzero polynomial has some coefficient which witnesses its degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegldg {x3 h m: setvar} (A2: class m) (B2 D: class h m) (P: class x3 h m) (R F H: class h m) (I: class x3) (Y: class x3 h m) (c_0: class m):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab m (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv m)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt h A2 (co ccnfld (cv h) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wne F Y)) (wrex x3 (wa (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) c_0) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) H) (cfv F D))) A2) $;

--| Closure of polynomial degree in the extended reals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdegxrcl (B2 D P R F I: class):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wcel (cfv F D) cxr) $;

--| Functionality of polynomial degree in the extended reals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdegxrf (B2 D P R I: class):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wf B2 cxr D $;

--| Sharp closure for multivariate polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegcl (B2 D P R F I: class):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wcel (cfv F D) (cun cn0 (csn cmnf))) $;

--| Degree of the zero polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdeg0 (D P R I V c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel R crg)) (wceq (cfv c_0 D) cmnf) $;

--| Degree of a nonzero polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegnn0cl (B2 D P R F I c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wceq P (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0)) (wcel (cfv F D) cn0) $;

--| Theorem on arithmetic of extended reals useful for degrees. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem degltlem1 (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cun cn0 (csn cmnf))) (wcel Y cz)) (wb (wbr X Y clt) (wbr X (co Y c1 cmin) cle)) $;

--| Theorem on arithmetic of extended reals useful for degrees. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem degltp1le (X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (cun cn0 (csn cmnf))) (wcel Y cz)) (wb (wbr X (co Y c1 caddc) clt) (wbr X Y cle)) $;

--| The degree of a sum is at most the maximum of the degrees of the factors. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegaddle (ph: wff) (B2 D c_pl R F G I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_pl) D) (cif (wbr (cfv F D) (cfv G D) cle) (cfv G D) (cfv F D)) cle) $;

--| The degree of a scalar multiple of a polynomial is at most the degree of the original polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegvscale (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x F G I K V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (cfv G D) cle) $;

--| The degree of a scalar multiple of a polynomial is exactly the degree of the original polynomial when the multiple is a nonzero-divisor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdegvsca (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x E F G I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (cfv G D)) $;

--| A polynomial has nonpositive degree iff it is a constant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegle0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R F I V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv Y cascl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F D) cc0 cle) (wceq F (cfv (cfv (cxp I (csn cc0)) F) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mdegmulle2 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegmullem {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2: class a) (B2 D: class a b) (R: class a) (c_x F G H: class a b) (I: class) (J K: class a b) (V: class a) (Y: class a b):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) J cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) K cle) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab a (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv a)) cn) cfn) (co cn0 I cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt b A2 (co ccnfld (cv b) cgsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (co J K caddc) cle) $;

--| The multivariate degree of a product of polynomials is at most the sum of the degrees of the polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem mdegmulle2 (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x F G I J K V Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (co I R cmpl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co I R cmdg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) J cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) K cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (co J K caddc) cle) $;

--| Relate univariate polynomial degree to multivariate. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem deg1fval (D R: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq D (co c1o R cmdg) $;

--| Functionality of univariate polynomial degree, weak range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1xrf (B2 D P R: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wf B2 cxr D $;

--| Closure of univariate polynomial degree in extended reals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1xrcl (B2 D P R F: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wcel (cfv F D) cxr) $;

--| Sharp closure of univariate polynomial degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1cl (B2 D P R F: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wcel (cfv F D) (cun cn0 (csn cmnf))) $;

--| Property deduction for polynomial degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem mdegpropd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S: class) (I: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv S cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv R cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv S cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I R cmdg) (co I S cmdg)) $;

--| Univariate polynomial degree respects protection. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1fvi (R: class):
  $ wceq (cfv R cdg1) (cfv (cfv R cid) cdg1) $;

--| Property deduction for polynomial degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1propd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 R S: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv S cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv R cplusg)) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv S cplusg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cdg1) (cfv S cdg1)) $;

--| Degree of the zero univariate polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1z (D P R c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv c_0 D) cmnf) $;

--| Degree of a nonzero univariate polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Oct-2015.)
theorem deg1nn0cl (B2 D P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0)) (wcel (cfv F D) cn0) $;

--| Degree image of a set of polynomials which does not include zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1n0ima (B2 D P R c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wss (cima D (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) cn0) $;

--| A polynomial is nonzero iff it has definite degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1nn0clb (B2 D P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2)) (wb (wne F c_0) (wcel (cfv F D) cn0)) $;

--| A polynomial is zero iff it has negative degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem deg1lt0 (B2 D P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2)) (wb (wbr (cfv F D) cc0 clt) (wceq F c_0)) $;

--| A nonzero univariate polynomial always has a nonzero leading coefficient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1ldg (A2 B2 D P R F Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0)) (wne (cfv (cfv F D) A2) Y) $;

--| An index at which a polynomial is zero, cannot be its degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1ldgn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P R F X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X A2) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F D) X) $;

--| A nonzero univariate polynomial over a domain always has a nonzero-divisor leading coefficient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1ldgdomn (A2 B2 D P R E F c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdomn) (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0)) (wcel (cfv (cfv F D) A2) E) $;

--| Property of being of limited degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1leb {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 D P R F: class x3) (G c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel G cxr)) (wb (wbr (cfv F D) G cle) (wral x3 (wi (wbr G (cv x3) clt) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) A2) c_0)) cn0)) $;

--| Value of the univariate degree as a supremum. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jul-2019.)
theorem deg1val (A2 B2 D P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F B2) (wceq (cfv F D) (csup (co A2 c_0 csupp) cxr clt)) $;

--| If the degree of a univariate polynomial is less than some index, then that coefficient must be zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1lt (A2 B2 D P R F G c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F B2) (wcel G cn0) (wbr (cfv F D) G clt)) (wceq (cfv G A2) c_0) $;

--| Conversely, a nonzero coefficient sets a lower bound on the degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1ge (A2 B2 D P R F G c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F B2) (wcel G cn0) (wne (cfv G A2) c_0)) (wbr G (cfv F D) cle) $;

--| The coefficient vector of multiplication in the univariate polynomial ring, at indices high enough that at most one component can be active in the sum. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem coe1mul3 (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_xb c_x F G I J Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) I cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) J cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co I J caddc) (cfv (co F G c_xb) cco1)) (co (cfv I (cfv F cco1)) (cfv J (cfv G cco1)) c_x)) $;

--| Value of the "leading" coefficient of a product of two nonzero polynomials. This will fail to actually be the leading coefficient only if it is zero (requiring the basic ring to contain zero divisors). (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Mar-2015.)
theorem coe1mul4 (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_xb c_x F G Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv Y c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co (cfv F D) (cfv G D) caddc) (cfv (co F G c_xb) cco1)) (co (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) (cfv (cfv G D) (cfv G cco1)) c_x)) $;

--| The degree of a sum is at most the maximum of the degrees of the factors. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1addle (ph: wff) (B2 D c_pl R F G Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_pl) D) (cif (wbr (cfv F D) (cfv G D) cle) (cfv G D) (cfv F D)) cle) $;

--| If both factors have degree bounded by ` L ` , then the sum of the polynomials also has degree bounded by ` L ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem deg1addle2 (ph: wff) (B2 D c_pl R F G L Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) L cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) L cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_pl) D) L cle) $;

--| Exact degree of a sum of two polynomials of unequal degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1add (ph: wff) (B2 D c_pl R F G Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Y cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) (cfv F D) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_pl) D) (cfv F D)) $;

--| The degree of a scalar times a polynomial is at most the degree of the original polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1vscale (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x F G K Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (cfv G D) cle) $;

--| The degree of a scalar times a polynomial is exactly the degree of the original polynomial when the scalar is not a zero divisor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1vsca (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x E F G Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (cfv G D)) $;

--| The degree of the negated polynomial is the same as the original. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1invg (ph: wff) (B2 D R F N Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv Y cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv F N) D) (cfv F D)) $;

--| The degree of a difference of polynomials is bounded by the maximum of degrees. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1suble (ph: wff) (B2 D R F G c_mi Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Y csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_mi) D) (cif (wbr (cfv F D) (cfv G D) cle) (cfv G D) (cfv F D)) cle) $;

--| Exact degree of a difference of two polynomials of unequal degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1sub (ph: wff) (B2 D R F G c_mi Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Y csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) (cfv F D) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_mi) D) (cfv F D)) $;

--| Produce a bound on the product of two univariate polynomials given bounds on the factors. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1mulle2 (ph: wff) (B2 D R c_x F G J K Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv Y cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) J cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) K cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (co J K caddc) cle) $;

--| Subtraction of two polynomials limited to the same degree with the same leading coefficient gives a polynomial with a smaller degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1sublt (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P R F G L c_mi: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F D) L cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv G D) L cle) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv G cco1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv L (cfv F cco1)) (cfv L (cfv G cco1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_mi) D) L clt) $;

--| A polynomial has nonpositive degree iff it is a constant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1le0 (A2 B2 D P R F: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2)) (wb (wbr (cfv F D) cc0 cle) (wceq F (cfv (cfv cc0 (cfv F cco1)) A2))) $;

--| A scalar polynomial has nonpositive degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1sclle (A2 D P R F K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F K)) (wbr (cfv (cfv F A2) D) cc0 cle) $;

--| A nonzero scalar polynomial has zero degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1scl (A2 D P R F K c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F K) (wne F c_0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv F A2) D) cc0) $;

--| Degree of multiplication of two nonzero polynomials when the first leads with a nonzero-divisor coefficient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1mul2 (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_x E F G c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (co (cfv F D) (cfv G D) caddc)) $;

--| Degree of multiplication of two nonzero polynomials in a domain. (Contributed by metakunt, 6-May-2025.)
theorem deg1mul (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_x F G c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdomn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G c_x) D) (co (cfv F D) (cfv G D) caddc)) $;

--| Degree of multiplication of a polynomial on the left by a nonzero-dividing scalar. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Jul-2019.)
theorem deg1mul3 (A2 B2 D P R c_x E F G: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F E) (wcel G B2)) (wceq (cfv (co (cfv F A2) G c_x) D) (cfv G D)) $;

--| Degree of multiplication of a polynomial on the left by a scalar. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem deg1mul3le (A2 B2 D P R c_x F G K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F K) (wcel G B2)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv F A2) G c_x) D) (cfv G D) cle) $;

--| Limiting degree of a polynomial term. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1tmle (C D P R c_x c_ex F K N X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv N cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel C K) (wcel F cn0)) (wbr (cfv (co C (co F X c_ex) c_x) D) F cle) $;

--| Exact degree of a polynomial term. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1tm (C D P R c_x c_ex F K N X c_0: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv N cmg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wa (wcel C K) (wne C c_0)) (wcel F cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co C (co F X c_ex) c_x) D) F) $;

--| Limiting degree of a variable power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem deg1pwle (D P R c_ex F N X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv N cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F cn0)) (wbr (cfv (co F X c_ex) D) F cle) $;

--| Exact degree of a variable power over a nontrivial ring. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem deg1pw (D P R c_ex F N X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv N cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnzr) (wcel F cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co F X c_ex) D) F) $;

--| Univariate polynomials over a nonzero ring are a nonzero ring. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1nz (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cnzr) (wcel P cnzr) $;

--| Univariate polynomials are nonzero iff the base is nonzero. Or in contraposition, the univariate polynomials over the zero ring are also zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2015.)
theorem ply1nzb (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wb (wcel R cnzr) (wcel P cnzr)) $;

--| Corollary of ~ deg1mul2 : the univariate polynomials over a domain are a domain. This is true for multivariate but with a much more complicated proof. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1domn (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cdomn) (wcel P cdomn) $;

--| The ring of univariate polynomials over an integral domain is itself an integral domain. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1idom (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cidom) (wcel P cidom) $;

--| Monic polynomials.
term cmn1: class;

--| Unitic polynomials.
term cuc1p: class;

--| Univariate polynomial quotient.
term cq1p: class;

--| Univariate polynomial remainder.
term cr1p: class;

--| Univariate polynomial ideal generator.
term cig1p: class;

--| Define the set of monic univariate polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_mon1 {f2 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmn1 (cmpt r cvv (crab f2 (wa (wne (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) c0g)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv f2) (cfv (cv r) cdg1)) (cfv (cv f2) cco1)) (cfv (cv r) cur))) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) cbs))) $;

--| Define the set of unitic univariate polynomials, as the polynomials with an invertible leading coefficient. This is not a standard concept but is useful to us as the set of polynomials which can be used as the divisor in the polynomial division theorem ~ ply1divalg . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_uc1p {f2 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cuc1p (cmpt r cvv (crab f2 (wa (wne (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) c0g)) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv f2) (cfv (cv r) cdg1)) (cfv (cv f2) cco1)) (cfv (cv r) cui))) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) cbs))) $;

--| Define the quotient of two univariate polynomials, which is guaranteed to exist and be unique by ~ ply1divalg . We actually use the reversed version for better harmony with our divisibility ~ df-dvdsr . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_q1p {f2 g2 r q p b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cq1p (cmpt r cvv (csb p (cfv (cv r) cpl1) (csb b (cfv (cv p) cbs) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv b) (cv b) (crio q (wbr (cfv (co (cv f2) (co (cv q) (cv g2) (cfv (cv p) cmulr)) (cfv (cv p) csg)) (cfv (cv r) cdg1)) (cfv (cv g2) (cfv (cv r) cdg1)) clt) (cv b)))))) $;

--| Define the remainder after dividing two univariate polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_r1p {f2 g2 r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cr1p (cmpt r cvv (csb b (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) cbs) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv b) (cv b) (co (cv f2) (co (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cfv (cv r) cq1p)) (cv g2) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) cmulr)) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) csg))))) $;

--| Define a choice function for generators of ideals over a division ring; this is the unique monic polynomial of minimal degree in the ideal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
axiom df_ig1p {g2 i r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cig1p (cmpt r cvv (cmpt i (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) clidl) (cif (wceq (cv i) (csn (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) c0g))) (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) c0g) (crio g2 (wceq (cfv (cv g2) (cfv (cv r) cdg1)) (cinf (cima (cfv (cv r) cdg1) (cdif (cv i) (csn (cfv (cfv (cv r) cpl1) c0g)))) cr clt)) (cin (cv i) (cfv (cv r) cmn1)))))) $;

--| Uniqueness of a quotient in a polynomial division. For polynomials ` F , G ` such that ` G =/= 0 ` and the leading coefficient of ` G ` is not a zero divisor, there is at most one polynomial ` q ` which satisfies ` F = ( G x. q ) + r ` where the degree of ` r ` is less than the degree of ` G ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 26-Mar-2015.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem ply1divmo {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class) (P R: class q) (c_xb: class) (E: class q) (F G c_mi: class) (c_0: class q):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv G D) (cfv G cco1)) E) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R crlreg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrmo q (wbr (cfv (co F (co G (cv q) c_xb) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ply1divalg : existence part. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1divex {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_xb c_x: class) (c_1: class q) (F G I: class) (K: class q) (c_mi c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (cfv G D) (cfv G cco1)) I c_x) c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex q (wbr (cfv (co F (co G (cv q) c_xb) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2) $;

--| The division algorithm for univariate polynomials over a ring. For polynomials ` F , G ` such that ` G =/= 0 ` and the leading coefficient of ` G ` is a unit, there are unique polynomials ` q ` and ` r = F - ( G x. q ) ` such that the degree of ` r ` is less than the degree of ` G ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1divalg {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_xb: class) (U: class q) (F G c_mi c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv G D) (cfv G cco1)) U) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu q (wbr (cfv (co F (co G (cv q) c_xb) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2) $;

--| Reverse the order of multiplication in ~ ply1divalg via the opposite ring. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1divalg2 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_xb: class) (U: class q) (F G c_mi c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv G D) (cfv G cco1)) U) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu q (wbr (cfv (co F (co (cv q) G c_xb) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2) $;

--| Value of the set of unitic polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pval {f2: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class f2) (D: class) (P: class f2) (R U c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wne (cv f2) c_0) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv f2) D) (cfv (cv f2) cco1)) U)) B2) $;

--| Being a unitic polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem isuc1p (B2 C D P R U F c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F C) (w3a (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0) (wcel (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) U)) $;

--| Value of the set of monic polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem mon1pval {f2: setvar} (B2 D: class) (P: class f2) (R c_1: class) (M: class f2) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq M (crab f2 (wa (wne (cv f2) c_0) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv f2) D) (cfv (cv f2) cco1)) c_1)) B2) $;

--| Being a monic polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem ismon1p (B2 D P R c_1 F M c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F M) (w3a (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0) (wceq (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) c_1)) $;

--| Unitic polynomials are polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pcl (B2 C P R F: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F C) (wcel F B2) $;

--| Monic polynomials are polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem mon1pcl (B2 P R F M: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F M) (wcel F B2) $;

--| Unitic polynomials are not zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pn0 (C P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F C) (wne F c_0) $;

--| Monic polynomials are not zero. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem mon1pn0 (P R F M c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F M) (wne F c_0) $;

--| Unitic polynomials have nonnegative degrees. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pdeg (C D R F: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel F C)) (wcel (cfv F D) cn0) $;

--| Unitic polynomials have unit leading coefficients. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pldg (C D R U F: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F C) (wcel (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) U) $;

--| Unitic polynomials have one leading coefficients. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem mon1pldg (D R c_1 F M: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F M) (wceq (cfv (cfv F D) (cfv F cco1)) c_1) $;

--| Monic polynomials are unitic. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem mon1puc1p (C R M X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel X M)) (wcel X C) $;

--| Make a unitic polynomial monic by multiplying a factor to normalize the leading coefficient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem uc1pmon1p (A2 C D P R c_x I M X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel X C)) (wcel (co (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv X D) (cfv X cco1)) I) A2) X c_x) M) $;

--| The difference of two monic polynomials of the same degree is a polynomial of lesser degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem deg1submon1p (ph: wff) (D P R F G c_mi O X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F D) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G D) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co F G c_mi) D) X clt) $;

--| Monicity and degree of the unit polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem mon1pid (D P R c_1 M: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv P cur) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cnzr) (wa (wcel c_1 M) (wceq (cfv c_1 D) cc0)) $;

--| Value of the univariate polynomial quotient function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem q1pval {q: setvar} (B2: class) (D: class q) (P: class) (Q: class q) (R: class) (c_x: class q) (F G: class) (c_mi: class q):
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel G B2)) (wceq (co F G Q) (crio q (wbr (cfv (co F (co (cv q) G c_x) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2)) $;

--| Characterizing property of the polynomial quotient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem q1peqb (B2 C D P Q R c_x F G c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wb (wa (wcel X B2) (wbr (cfv (co F (co X G c_x) c_mi) D) (cfv G D) clt)) (wceq (co F G Q) X)) $;

--| Closure of the quotient by a unitic polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem q1pcl (B2 C P Q R F G: class):
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wcel (co F G Q) B2) $;

--| Value of the polynomial remainder function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem r1pval (B2 P Q R c_x E F G c_mi: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F B2) (wcel G B2)) (wceq (co F G E) (co F (co (co F G Q) G c_x) c_mi)) $;

--| Closure of remainder following division by a unitic polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem r1pcl (B2 C P R E F G: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wcel (co F G E) B2) $;

--| The remainder has a degree less than the divisor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem r1pdeglt (B2 C D P R E F G: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wbr (cfv (co F G E) D) (cfv G D) clt) $;

--| Express the original polynomial ` F ` as ` F = ( q x. G ) + r ` using the quotient and remainder functions for ` q ` and ` r ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2015.)
theorem r1pid (B2 C P c_pl Q R c_x E F G: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv P cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wceq F (co (co (co F G Q) G c_x) (co F G E) c_pl)) $;

--| Identity law for polynomial remainder operation: it leaves a polynomial ` A ` unchanged iff the degree of ` A ` is less than the degree of the divisor ` B ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Apr-2025.) Generalize to domains. (Revised by SN, 21-Jun-2025.)
theorem r1pid2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P R U E N: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdomn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 E) A2) (wbr (cfv A2 D) (cfv B2 D) clt)) $;

--| Divisibility in a polynomial ring is witnessed by the quotient. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvdsq1p (B2 C c_pa P Q R c_x F G: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_pa (cfv P cdsr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv R cq1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wb (wbr G F c_pa) (wceq F (co (co F G Q) G c_x))) $;

--| Divisibility in a polynomial ring in terms of the remainder. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 28-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvdsr1p (B2 C c_pa P R E F G c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_pa (cfv P cdsr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F B2) (wcel G C)) (wb (wbr G F c_pa) (wceq (co F G E) c_0)) $;

--| A term of the form ` x - N ` is linear, monic, and has exactly one zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem ply1remlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P R U G K c_mi N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv N A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnzr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N K) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel G U) (wceq (cfv G D) c1) (wceq (cima (ccnv (cfv G O)) (csn c_0)) (csn N))) $;

--| The polynomial remainder theorem, or little B&eacute;zout's theorem (by contrast to the regular B&eacute;zout's theorem ~ bezout ). If a polynomial ` F ` is divided by the linear factor ` x - A ` , the remainder is equal to ` F ( A ) ` , the evaluation of the polynomial at ` A ` (interpreted as a constant polynomial). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem ply1rem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P R E F G K c_mi N O X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv N A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnzr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cr1p) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G E) (cfv (cfv N (cfv F O)) A2)) $;

--| The factor theorem and its converse. A polynomial ` F ` has a root at ` A ` iff ` G = x - A ` is a factor of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem facth1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 c_pa P R F G K c_mi N O X c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv N A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnzr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pa (cfv P cdsr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr G F c_pa) (wceq (cfv N (cfv F O)) c_0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta1g . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem fta1glem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P R T F G K c_mi N O W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cidom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv T A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F D) (co N c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cima (ccnv (cfv F O)) (csn W))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co F G (cfv R cq1p)) D) N) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta1g . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem fta1glem2 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (A2: class g2) (B2 D P R: class) (T: class g2) (F G: class) (K c_mi: class g2) (N O W: class) (X c_0: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cidom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv T A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F D) (co N c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cima (ccnv (cfv F O)) (csn W))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral g2 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv g2) D) N) (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv g2) O)) (csn W)) chash) (cfv (cv g2) D) cle)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cfv F O)) (csn W)) chash) (cfv F D) cle) $;

--| The one-sided fundamental theorem of algebra. A polynomial of degree ` n ` has at most ` n ` roots. Unlike the real fundamental theorem ~ fta , which is only true in ` CC ` and other algebraically closed fields, this is true in any integral domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem fta1g (ph: wff) (B2 D P R F O W c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cidom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cfv F O)) (csn W)) chash) (cfv F D) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta1b . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2015.)
theorem fta1blem (ph: wff) (B2 D P R c_x c_xp K M N O W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N c_xp) W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel (co M X c_x) (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cfv (co M X c_x) O)) (csn W)) chash) (cfv (co M X c_x) D) cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N W) $;

--| The assumption that ` R ` be a domain in ~ fta1g is necessary. Here we show that the statement is strong enough to prove that ` R ` is a domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem fta1b {f2: setvar} (B2 D P R O W c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cidom) (w3a (wcel R ccrg) (wcel R cnzr) (wral f2 (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv f2) O)) (csn W)) chash) (cfv (cv f2) D) cle) (cdif B2 (csn c_0)))) $;

--| No element of an integral domain can have more than ` N ` ` N ` -th roots. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem idomrootle {y1: setvar} (B2 R: class) (c_ex: class y1) (N X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv R cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cidom) (wcel X B2) (wcel N cn)) (wbr (cfv (crab y1 (wceq (co N (cv y1) c_ex) X) B2) chash) N cle) $;

--| Over a division ring, all nonzero polynomials are unitic. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem drnguc1p (B2 C P R F c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdr) (wcel F B2) (wne F c_0)) (wcel F C) $;

--| There is a unique monic polynomial of minimal degree in any nonzero ideal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1peu {g2: setvar} (D P R U I M c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdr) (wcel I U) (wne I (csn c_0))) (wreu g2 (wceq (cfv (cv g2) D) (cinf (cima D (cdif I (csn c_0))) cr clt)) (cin I M)) $;

--| Substitutions for the polynomial ideal generator function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1pval {g2: setvar} (D P: class g2) (R: class) (U G: class g2) (I M: class) (V c_0: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R V) (wcel I U)) (wceq (cfv I G) (cif (wceq I (csn c_0)) c_0 (crio g2 (wceq (cfv (cv g2) D) (cinf (cima D (cdif I (csn c_0))) cr clt)) (cin I M)))) $;

--| Generator of the zero ideal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1pval2 (P R G c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv (csn c_0) G) c_0) $;

--| Characterizing properties of the monic generator of a nonzero ideal of polynomials. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Revised by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1pval3 (D P R U G I M c_0: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmn1) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdr) (wcel I U) (wne I (csn c_0))) (w3a (wcel (cfv I G) I) (wcel (cfv I G) M) (wceq (cfv (cfv I G) D) (cinf (cima D (cdif I (csn c_0))) cr clt))) $;

--| The monic generator of an ideal is always in the ideal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1pcl (P R U G I: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wcel I U)) (wcel (cfv I G) I) $;

--| The monic generator of an ideal divides all elements of the ideal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Sep-2020.)
theorem ig1pdvds (c_pa P R U G I X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wceq c_pa (cfv P cdsr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdr) (wcel I U) (wcel X I)) (wbr (cfv I G) X c_pa) $;

--| Any ideal of polynomials over a division ring is generated by the ideal's canonical generator. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem ig1prsp (P R U G I K: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv R cig1p) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv P clidl) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv P crsp) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wcel I U)) (wceq I (cfv (csn (cfv I G)) K)) $;

--| The ring of polynomials over a division ring has the principal ideal property. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1lpir (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cdr) (wcel P clpir) $;

--| The polynomials over a field are a PID. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 29-Mar-2015.)
theorem ply1pid (P R: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cfield) (wcel P cpid) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the set of complex polynomials.
term cply: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the identity polynomial.
term cidp: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the coefficient function on polynomials.
term ccoe: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the degree function on polynomials.
term cdgr: class;

--| Define the set of polynomials on the complex numbers with coefficients in the given subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_ply {x3 z f2 k n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cply (cmpt x3 (cpw cc) (cab f2 (wrex n (wrex a (wceq (cv f2) (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) (co (cun (cv x3) (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) cn0))) $;

--| Define the identity polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_idp:
  $ wceq cidp (cres cid cc) $;

--| Define the coefficient function for a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_coe {z f2 k n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccoe (cmpt f2 (cfv cc cply) (crio a (wrex n (wa (wceq (cima (cv a) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wceq (cv f2) (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) cn0) (co cc cn0 cmap))) $;

--| Define the degree of a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_dgr {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdgr (cmpt f2 (cfv cc cply) (csup (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv f2) ccoe)) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) cn0 clt)) $;

--| Two ways to say that a function on the nonnegative integers has finite support. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyco0 {k: setvar} (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wf cn0 cc A2)) (wb (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wral k (wi (wne (cfv (cv k) A2) cc0) (wbr (cv k) N cle)) cn0)) $;

--| Value of the polynomial set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyval {z f2 k n a: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wss S cc) (wceq (cfv S cply) (cab f2 (wrex n (wrex a (wceq (cv f2) (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) cn0))) $;

--| Reverse closure of the parameter ` S ` of the polynomial set function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plybss (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wss S cc) $;

--| Definition of a polynomial with coefficients in ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem elply {z k n a: setvar} (S: class) (F: class z k):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wa (wss S cc) (wrex n (wrex a (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) cn0)) $;

--| The coefficient function can be assumed to have zeroes outside ` 0 ... n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem elply2 {z k n a: setvar} (S: class) (F: class z k):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wa (wss S cc) (wrex n (wrex a (wa (wceq (cima (cv a) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) cn0)) $;

--| The set of polynomials is unaffected by the addition of zero. (This is built into the definition because all higher powers of a polynomial are effectively zero, so we require that the coefficient field contain zero to simplify some of our closure theorems.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyun0 (S: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (cun S (csn cc0)) cply) (cfv S cply) $;

--| A polynomial is a function on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyf (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wf cc cc F) $;

--| The polynomial set function preserves the subset relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyss (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S T) (wss T cc)) (wss (cfv S cply) (cfv T cply)) $;

--| Every polynomial ring is contained in the ring of polynomials over ` CC ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyssc (S: class):
  $ wss (cfv S cply) (cfv cc cply) $;

--| Sufficient condition for elementhood in the set of polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem elplyr {z k: setvar} (A2 S N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cc) (wcel N cn0) (wf cn0 S A2)) (wcel (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for elementhood in the set of polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem elplyd {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (S N: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co A2 (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ply1term . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem ply1termlem {z k: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cc (co A2 (co (cv z) N cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cif (wceq (cv k) N) A2 cc0) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| A one-term polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem ply1term {z: setvar} (A2 S: class) (F: class z) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cc (co A2 (co (cv z) N cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cc) (wcel A2 S) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $;

--| A power is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plypow {z: setvar} (S N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss S cc) (wcel c1 S) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cmpt z cc (co (cv z) N cexp)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| A constant function is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyconst (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cxp cc (csn A2)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| A test to show that a polynomial is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem ne0p (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (cfv A2 F) cc0)) (wne F c0p) $;

--| The zero function is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem ply0 (S: class):
  $ wi (wss S cc) (wcel c0p (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The identity function is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyid (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss S cc) (wcel c1 S)) (wcel cidp (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Value of the identity polynomial. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2026.)
theorem idpfv (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cidp) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plyeq0 . If ` A ` is the coefficient function for a nonzero polynomial such that ` P ( z ) = sum_ k e. NN0 A ( k ) x. z ^ k = 0 ` for every ` z e. CC ` and ` A ( M ) ` is the nonzero leading coefficient, then the function ` F ( z ) = P ( z ) / z ^ M ` is a sum of powers of ` 1 / z ` , and so the limit of this function as ` z ~~> +oo ` is the constant term, ` A ( M ) ` . But ` F ( z ) = 0 ` everywhere, so this limit is also equal to zero so that ` A ( M ) = 0 ` , a contradiction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyeq0lem {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class) (M: class z) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c0p (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (csup (cima (ccnv A2) (cdif S (csn cc0))) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cima (ccnv A2) (cdif S (csn cc0))) c0) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| If a polynomial is zero at every point (or even just zero at the positive integers), then all the coefficients must be zero. This is the basis for the method of equating coefficients of equal polynomials, and ensures that ~ df-coe is well-defined. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyeq0 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S N: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c0p (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cxp cn0 (csn cc0))) $;

--| Write the set of complex polynomials in a subring in terms of the abstract polynomial construction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Sep-2019.)
theorem plypf1 (A2 P R S E: class):
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld S cress) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv ccnfld ce1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wceq (cfv S cply) (cima E A2)) $;

--| Derive the coefficient function for the sum of two polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyaddlem1 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z k) (B2: class z) (S F G: class z k) (M N: class z):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof caddc)) (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (co A2 B2 (cof caddc))) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Derive the coefficient function for the product of two polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem plymullem1 {z k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z k) (B2: class z) (S F G: class z k n) (M N: class z):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (cof cmul)) (cmpt z cc (csu n (co cc0 (co M N caddc) cfz) (co (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv k) cmin) B2) cmul)) (co (cv z) (cv n) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plyadd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyaddlem {x3 y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (F G: class k) (M N: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plymul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jul-2014.)
theorem plymullem {x3 y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (F G: class k) (M N: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The sum of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyadd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The product of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jul-2014.)
theorem plymul {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The difference of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jul-2014.)
theorem plysub {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmin)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The sum of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyaddcl (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| The product of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plymulcl (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| The difference of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plysubcl (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wcel (co F G (cof cmin)) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| Value of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeval {z k n a: setvar} (S F: class z k):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cfv F ccoe) (crio a (wrex n (wa (wceq (cima (cv a) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) cn0) (co cc cn0 cmap))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ coeeu . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeeulem {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (S F: class z k) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co cc cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Uniqueness of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem coeeu {z k n a: setvar} (S F: class z k):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wreu a (wrex n (wa (wceq (cima (cv a) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) cn0) (co cc cn0 cmap)) $;

--| Lemma for properties of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem coelem {z k n: setvar} (S: class z k) (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wa (wcel (cfv F ccoe) (co cc cn0 cmap)) (wrex n (wa (wceq (cima (cfv F ccoe) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (cfv F ccoe)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) cn0)) $;

--| If ` A ` satisfies the properties of the coefficient function, it must be equal to the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem coeeq {z k: setvar} (ph: wff z k) (A2: class) (S F: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F ccoe) A2) $;

--| Value of the degree function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrval (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cfv F cdgr) (csup (cima (ccnv A2) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) cn0 clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dgrcl and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrlem {x3 n: setvar} (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wa (wf cn0 (cun S (csn cc0)) A2) (wrex n (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv n) cle) (cima (ccnv A2) (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) cz)) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coef (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wf cn0 (cun S (csn cc0)) A2) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coef2 (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel cc0 S)) (wf cn0 S A2) $;

--| The domain and codomain of the coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coef3 (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wf cn0 cc A2) $;

--| The degree of any polynomial is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrcl (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel (cfv F cdgr) cn0) $;

--| If the ` M ` -th coefficient of ` F ` is nonzero, then the degree of ` F ` is at least ` M ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrub (A2 S F M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel M cn0) (wne (cfv M A2) cc0)) (wbr M N cle) $;

--| All the coefficients above the degree of ` F ` are zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrub2 (A2 S F N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $;

--| If all the coefficients above ` M ` are zero, then the degree of ` F ` is at most ` M ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrlb (A2 S F M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel M cn0) (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0))) (wbr N M cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ coeid . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeidlem {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (F: class z) (M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cun S (csn cc0)) cn0 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima B2 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Reconstruct a polynomial as an explicit sum of the coefficient function up to the degree of the polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeid {z k: setvar} (A2 S F N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Reconstruct a polynomial as an explicit sum of the coefficient function up to the degree of the polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeid2 {k: setvar} (A2 S F N X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel X cc)) (wceq (cfv X F) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co X (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| Reconstruct a polynomial as an explicit sum of the coefficient function up to at least the degree of the polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeid3 {k: setvar} (A2 S F M N X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel M (cfv N cuz)) (wcel X cc)) (wceq (cfv X F) (csu k (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co X (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| The composition of two polynomials is a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem plyco {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom F G) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Compute the coefficient function given a sum expression for the polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeeq2 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (S F: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co A2 (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F ccoe) (cmpt k cn0 (cif (wbr (cv k) N cle) A2 cc0))) $;

--| Given an explicit expression for a polynomial, the degree is at most the highest term in the sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrle {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (S F: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co A2 (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F cdgr) N cle) $;

--| If the highest term in a polynomial expression is nonzero, then the polynomial's degree is completely determined. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgreq {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (S F: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv N A2) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F cdgr) N) $;

--| A constant function has degree 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem _0dgr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cxp cc (csn A2)) cdgr) cc0) $;

--| A function has degree zero iff it is a constant function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem _0dgrb (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wb (wceq (cfv F cdgr) cc0) (wceq F (cxp cc (csn (cfv cc0 F))))) $;

--| A nonzero polynomial with a root has positive degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Nov-2014.)
theorem dgrnznn (A2 P S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel P (cfv S cply)) (wne P c0p)) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 P) cc0))) (wcel (cfv P cdgr) cn) $;

--| The result of evaluating a polynomial at zero is the constant term. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coefv0 (A2 S F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ coeadd and ~ dgradd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeaddlem (A2 B2 S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wa (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) ccoe) (co A2 B2 (cof caddc))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) cdgr) (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ coemul and ~ dgrmul . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coemullem {k n: setvar} (A2 B2 S F G: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wa (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) ccoe) (cmpt n cn0 (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv k) cmin) B2) cmul)))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) cdgr) (co M N caddc) cle)) $;

--| The coefficient function of a sum is the sum of coefficients. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeadd (A2 B2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) ccoe) (co A2 B2 (cof caddc))) $;

--| A coefficient of a product of polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coemul {k: setvar} (A2 B2 S F G N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv N (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) ccoe)) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cfv (co N (cv k) cmin) B2) cmul))) $;

--| The coefficient function is one-to-one, so if the coefficients are equal then the functions are equal and vice-versa. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem coe11 (A2 B2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wb (wceq F G) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The leading coefficient of a product of polynomials. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coemulhi (A2 B2 S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wceq (cfv (co M N caddc) (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) ccoe)) (co (cfv M A2) (cfv N B2) cmul)) $;

--| The coefficient function is linear under scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coemulc (A2 S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F (cfv S cply))) (wceq (cfv (co (cxp cc (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) ccoe) (co (cxp cn0 (csn A2)) (cfv F ccoe) (cof cmul))) $;

--| The coefficients of the zero polynomial are zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem coe0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0p ccoe) (cxp cn0 (csn cc0)) $;

--| The coefficient function of a sum is the sum of coefficients. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coesub (A2 B2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof cmin)) ccoe) (co A2 B2 (cof cmin))) $;

--| The coefficient function of a monomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2014.)
theorem coe1termlem {z n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cc (co A2 (co (cv z) N cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wa (wceq (cfv F ccoe) (cmpt n cn0 (cif (wceq (cv n) N) A2 cc0))) (wi (wne A2 cc0) (wceq (cfv F cdgr) N))) $;

--| The coefficient function of a monomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem coe1term {z: setvar} (A2: class) (F M: class z) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cc (co A2 (co (cv z) N cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0) (wcel M cn0)) (wceq (cfv M (cfv F ccoe)) (cif (wceq M N) A2 cc0)) $;

--| The degree of a monomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgr1term {z: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class z) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cc (co A2 (co (cv z) N cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv F cdgr) N) $;

--| A polynomial is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem plycn (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| The degree of the zero polynomial is zero. Note: this differs from some other definitions of the degree of the zero polynomial, such as ` -u 1 , -oo ` or undefined. But it is convenient for us to define it this way, so that we have ~ dgrcl , ~ dgreq0 and ~ coeid without having to special-case zero, although ~ plydivalg is a little more complicated as a result. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgr0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0p cdgr) cc0 $;

--| The coefficients of the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem coeidp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv cidp ccoe)) (cif (wceq A2 c1) c1 cc0)) $;

--| The degree of the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrid:
  $ wceq (cfv cidp cdgr) c1 $;

--| The leading coefficient of a polynomial is nonzero, unless the entire polynomial is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.) (Proof shortened by Fan Zheng, 21-Jun-2016.)
theorem dgreq0 (A2 S F N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wb (wceq F c0p) (wceq (cfv N A2) cc0)) $;

--| Two ways to say that the degree of ` F ` is strictly less than ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrlt (A2 S F M N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel M cn0)) (wb (wo (wceq F c0p) (wbr N M clt)) (wa (wbr N M cle) (wceq (cfv M A2) cc0))) $;

--| The degree of a sum of polynomials is at most the maximum of the degrees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgradd (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) cdgr) (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) cle) $;

--| The degree of a sum of polynomials of unequal degrees is the degree of the larger polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgradd2 (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wbr M N clt)) (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof caddc)) cdgr) N) $;

--| The degree of a product of polynomials is at most the sum of degrees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrmul2 (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) cdgr) (co M N caddc) cle) $;

--| The degree of a product of nonzero polynomials is the sum of degrees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrmul (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wne F c0p)) (wa (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wne G c0p))) (wceq (cfv (co F G (cof cmul)) cdgr) (co M N caddc)) $;

--| Scalar multiplication by a nonzero constant does not change the degree of a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrmulc (A2 S F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel F (cfv S cply))) (wceq (cfv (co (cxp cc (csn A2)) F (cof cmul)) cdgr) (cfv F cdgr)) $;

--| The degree of a difference of polynomials is at most the maximum of the degrees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem dgrsub (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wbr (cfv (co F G (cof cmin)) cdgr) (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) cle) $;

--| The degree of a composition of a monomial with a polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem dgrcolem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3) (G M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 cc (co (cfv (cv x3) G) M cexp)) cdgr) (co M N cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dgrco . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem dgrcolem2 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D: class) (S: class f2) (F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co D c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wi (wbr (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) D cle) (wceq (cfv (ccom (cv f2) G) cdgr) (co (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) N cmul))) (cfv cc cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (ccom F G) cdgr) (co M N cmul)) $;

--| The degree of a composition of two polynomials is the product of the degrees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem dgrco (ph: wff) (S F G M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (ccom F G) cdgr) (co M N cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plycj and ~ coecj . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plycjlem {z k: setvar} (A2 S F: class) (G: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom ccj F) ccj) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) (ccom ccj A2)) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| The double conjugation of a polynomial is a polynomial. (The single conjugation is not because our definition of polynomial includes only holomorphic functions, i.e. no dependence on ` ( * `` z ) ` independently of ` z ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plycj {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom ccj F) ccj) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) ccj) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Double conjugation of a polynomial causes the coefficients to be conjugated. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem coecj (A2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (ccom (ccom ccj F) ccj) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cfv G ccoe) (ccom ccj A2)) $;

--| A polynomial with real coefficients distributes under conjugation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyrecj (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv cr cply)) (wcel A2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 F) ccj) (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) F)) $;

--| Polynomial multiplication has no zero divisors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem plymul0or (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply))) (wb (wceq (co F G (cof cmul)) c0p) (wo (wceq F c0p) (wceq G c0p))) $;

--| The set of roots of a product is the union of the roots of the terms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem ofmulrt (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G)) (wceq (cima (ccnv (co F G (cof cmul))) (csn cc0)) (cun (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn cc0)))) $;

--| Product of a polynomial with the zero polynomial. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Sep-2018.)
theorem plymul02 (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wceq (co c0p F (cof cmul)) c0p) $;

--| Coefficients of a non-zero polynomial multiplied by the identity polynomial. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Sep-2018.)
theorem plyn0mulidp {n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cdif (cfv cr cply) (csn c0p))) (wceq (cfv (co F cidp (cof cmul)) ccoe) (cmpt n cn0 (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) cc0 (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) (cfv F ccoe))))) $;

--| Coefficients of a polynomial multiplied by the identity polynomial. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Sep-2018.)
theorem plymulidp {n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv cr cply)) (wceq (cfv (co F cidp (cof cmul)) ccoe) (cmpt n cn0 (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) cc0 (cfv (co (cv n) c1 cmin) (cfv F ccoe))))) $;

--| Real-coefficient polynomials restrict to real functions. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem plyreres (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv cr cply)) (wf cr cr (cres F cr)) $;

--| Derivative of a polynomial, explicit sum version. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 13-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvply1 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F G: class z k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt z cc (csu k (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (co (cv z) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt k cn0 (co (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) A2) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) G) $;

--| The derivative of a polynomial with coefficients in a subring is a polynomial with coefficients in the same ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 30-Apr-2025.)
theorem dvply2g (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wcel F (cfv S cply))) (wcel (co cc F cdv) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| The derivative of a polynomial is a polynomial. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvply2 (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel (co cc F cdv) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| Polynomials have polynomials as derivatives of all orders. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvnply2 (S F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cfv N (co cc F cdvn)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Polynomials have polynomials as derivatives of all orders. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvnply (S F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cfv N (co cc F cdvn)) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| Polynomials are smooth. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem plycpn (S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel F (cint (crn (cfv cc ccpn)))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the quotient of a polynomial division.
term cquot: class;

--| Define the quotient function on polynomials. This is the ` q ` of the expression ` f = g x. q + r ` in the division algorithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_quot {f2 g2 r q: setvar}:
  $ wceq cquot (cmpo f2 g2 (cfv cc cply) (cdif (cfv cc cply) (csn c0p)) (crio q (wsbc r (wo (wceq (cv r) c0p) (wbr (cfv (cv r) cdgr) (cfv (cv g2) cdgr) clt)) (co (cv f2) (co (cv g2) (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin))) (cfv cc cply))) $;

--| Value of the quotient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotval {q: setvar} (R S: class q) (F G: class):
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wne G c0p)) (wceq (co F G cquot) (crio q (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) (cfv cc cply))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivalg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivalg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivlem2 {x3 y1 q: setvar} (ph: wff q) (R: class q) (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv q) (cfv S cply))) (wcel R (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivex . Base case of induction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivlem3 {x3 y1 q: setvar} (ph: wff q) (R: class q) (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq F c0p) (wbr (co (cfv F cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) cmin) cc0 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex q (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivex . Induction step. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivlem4 {x3 y1 z f2 q p: setvar} (ph: wff f2 q) (A2 B2: class f2 q p) (D: class x3 y1 q p) (R: class z q) (S: class) (U: class x3 y1 z f2 q p) (F G H: class) (M: class f2 q p) (N: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cmin) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c0p) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cv f2) (co G (cv p) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt z cc (co (co (cfv M A2) (cfv N B2) cdiv) (co (cv z) D cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wi (wo (wceq (cv f2) c0p) (wbr (co (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) N cmin) D clt)) (wrex p (wo (wceq U c0p) (wbr (cfv U cdgr) N clt)) (cfv S cply))) (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex q (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) N clt)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivalg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivex {x3 y1 q: setvar} (ph: wff q) (R: class q) (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex q (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ plydivalg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydiveu {x3 y1 q p: setvar} (ph: wff q p) (R: class q) (S: class) (T: class q p) (F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv q) (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co F (co G (cv p) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv p) (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq T c0p) (wbr (cfv T cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cv p) (cv q)) $;

--| The division algorithm on polynomials over a subfield ` S ` of the complex numbers. If ` F ` and ` G =/= 0 ` are polynomials over ` S ` , then there is a unique quotient polynomial ` q ` such that the remainder ` F - G x. q ` is either zero or has degree less than ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem plydivalg {x3 y1 q: setvar} (ph: wff) (R: class q) (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (cv q) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu q (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Lemma for properties of the polynomial quotient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (co F G cquot) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (co F G cquot) (cfv S cply)) (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt))) $;

--| The quotient of two polynomials in a field ` S ` is also in the field. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wne (cv x3) cc0))) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg c1) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne G c0p) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G cquot) (cfv S cply)) $;

--| Closure of the quotient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotcl2 (S F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wne G c0p)) (wcel (co F G cquot) (cfv cc cply)) $;

--| Remainder property of the quotient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotdgr (R S F G: class):
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (co F G cquot) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wne G c0p)) (wo (wceq R c0p) (wbr (cfv R cdgr) (cfv G cdgr) clt)) $;

--| Closure of a linear factor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyremlem (A2 G: class):
  $ wceq G (co cidp (cxp cc (csn A2)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (w3a (wcel G (cfv cc cply)) (wceq (cfv G cdgr) c1) (wceq (cima (ccnv G) (csn cc0)) (csn A2))) $;

--| The polynomial remainder theorem, or little B&eacute;zout's theorem (by contrast to the regular B&eacute;zout's theorem ~ bezout ). If a polynomial ` F ` is divided by the linear factor ` x - A ` , the remainder is equal to ` F ( A ) ` , the evaluation of the polynomial at ` A ` (interpreted as a constant polynomial). This is part of Metamath 100 proof #89. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem plyrem (A2 R S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (co cidp (cxp cc (csn A2)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq R (co F (co G (co F G cquot) (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel A2 cc)) (wceq R (cxp cc (csn (cfv A2 F)))) $;

--| The factor theorem. If a polynomial ` F ` has a root at ` A ` , then ` G = x - A ` is a factor of ` F ` (and the other factor is ` F quot G ` ). This is part of Metamath 100 proof #89. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem facth (A2 S F G: class):
  $ wceq G (co cidp (cxp cc (csn A2)) (cof cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0)) (wceq F (co G (co F G cquot) (cof cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem fta1lem {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (A2 D: class) (R: class g2) (F: class):
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdif (cfv cc cply) (csn c0p))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F cdgr) (co D c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral g2 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv g2) cdgr) D) (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (csn cc0)) cfn) (wbr (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (csn cc0)) chash) (cfv (cv g2) cdgr) cle))) (cdif (cfv cc cply) (csn c0p))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel R cfn) (wbr (cfv R chash) (cfv F cdgr) cle)) $;

--| The easy direction of the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: A nonzero polynomial has at most ` deg ( F ) ` roots. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem fta1 (R S F: class):
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wne F c0p)) (wa (wcel R cfn) (wbr (cfv R chash) (cfv F cdgr) cle)) $;

--| The range of a nonconstant polynomial is not a finite set. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2026.)
theorem rnplynfin (ph: wff) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F cdgr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (crn F) cfn)) $;

--| The composition of a nonzero and a nonconstant polynomial is a nonzero polynomial. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2026.)
theorem plyconz (ph: wff) (S F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne F c0p) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv G cdgr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (ccom F G) c0p) $;

--| Exact division with a multiple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jul-2014.)
theorem quotcan (S F G H: class):
  $ wceq H (co F G (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel G (cfv S cply)) (wne G c0p)) (wceq (co H G cquot) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vieta1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem vieta1lem1 {x3 z f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class x3) (D: class x3 z) (Q: class z) (R: class) (S: class x3 z f2) (F: class x3 z) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R chash) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co D c1 caddc) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wi (wa (wceq D (cfv (cv f2) cdgr)) (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn cc0)) chash) (cfv (cv f2) cdgr))) (wceq (csu x3 (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn cc0)) (cv x3)) (cneg (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) c1 cmin) (cfv (cv f2) ccoe)) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) (cfv (cv f2) ccoe)) cdiv)))) (cfv cc cply)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co F (co cidp (cxp cc (csn (cv z))) (cof cmin)) cquot) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) R)) (wa (wcel Q (cfv cc cply)) (wceq D (cfv Q cdgr))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ vieta1 : inductive step. Let ` z ` be a root of ` F ` . Then ` F = ( Xp - z ) x. Q ` for some ` Q ` by the factor theorem, and ` Q ` is a degree- ` D ` polynomial, so by the induction hypothesis ` sum_ x e. ( ``' Q " 0 ) x = -u ( coeff `` Q ) `` ( D - 1 ) / ` ` ( coeff `` Q ) `` D ` , so ` sum_ x e. R x = z - ( coeff `` Q ) `` ` ` ( D - 1 ) / ( coeff `` Q ) `` D ` . Now the coefficients of ` F ` are ` A `` ( D + 1 ) = ( coeff `` Q ) `` D ` and ` A `` D = ` ` sum_ k e. ( 0 ... D ) ( coeff `` Xp - z ) `` k x. ( coeff `` Q ) ` ` `` ( D - k ) ` , which works out to ` -u z x. ( coeff `` Q ) `` D ` ` + ( coeff `` Q ) `` ( D - 1 ) ` , so putting it all together we have ` sum_ x e. R x = -u A `` D / A `` ( D + 1 ) ` as we wanted to show. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem vieta1lem2 {x3 z f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class x3) (D: class x3 z) (Q: class z) (R: class) (S: class x3 z f2) (F: class x3 z) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R chash) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co D c1 caddc) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wi (wa (wceq D (cfv (cv f2) cdgr)) (wceq (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn cc0)) chash) (cfv (cv f2) cdgr))) (wceq (csu x3 (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn cc0)) (cv x3)) (cneg (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) c1 cmin) (cfv (cv f2) ccoe)) (cfv (cfv (cv f2) cdgr) (cfv (cv f2) ccoe)) cdiv)))) (cfv cc cply)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co F (co cidp (cxp cc (csn (cv z))) (cof cmin)) cquot) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 R (cv x3)) (cneg (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) A2) (cfv N A2) cdiv))) $;

--| The first-order Vieta's formula (see ~ http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vieta%27s_formulas ). If a polynomial of degree ` N ` has ` N ` distinct roots, then the sum over these roots can be calculated as ` -u A ( N - 1 ) / A ( N ) ` . (If the roots are not distinct, then this formula is still true but must double-count some of the roots according to their multiplicities.) See also ~ vieta for the case of polynomials over a generic ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem vieta1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (R: class) (S F N: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wceq R (cima (ccnv F) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R chash) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 R (cv x3)) (cneg (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) A2) (cfv N A2) cdiv))) $;

--| An infinite set of values can be extended to a polynomial in at most one way. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Nov-2014.)
theorem plyexmo {p: setvar} (D S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss D cc) (wn (wcel D cfn))) (wmo p (wa (wcel (cv p) (cfv S cply)) (wceq (cres (cv p) D) F))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the set of algebraic numbers.
term caa: class;

--| Define the set of algebraic numbers. An algebraic number is a root of a nonzero polynomial over the integers. Here we construct it as the union of all kernels (preimages of ` { 0 } ` ) of all polynomials in ` ( Poly `` ZZ ) ` , except the zero polynomial ` 0p ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_aa {f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq caa (ciun f2 (cdif (cfv cz cply) (csn c0p)) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn cc0))) $;

--| Elementhood in the set of algebraic numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem elaa {f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 caa) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wrex f2 (wceq (cfv A2 (cv f2)) cc0) (cdif (cfv cz cply) (csn c0p)))) $;

--| An algebraic number is a complex number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem aacn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 caa) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| The algebraic numbers are a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem aasscn:
  $ wss caa cc $;

--| Lemma for ~ elqaa . The function ` N ` represents the denominators of the rational coefficients ` B ` . By multiplying them all together to make ` R ` , we get a number big enough to clear all the denominators and make ` R x. F ` an integer polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem elqaalem1 {k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class) (R: class n) (F: class k n) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdif (cfv cq cply) (csn c0p))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt k cn0 (cinf (crab n (wcel (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (cv n) cmul) cz) cn) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv F cdgr) (cseq cmul N cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K cn0)) (wa (wcel (cfv K N) cn) (wcel (co (cfv K B2) (cfv K N) cmul) cz)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ elqaa . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem elqaalem2 {x3 y1 k n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 n) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (P: class x3 y1 k n) (R: class x3 y1 n) (F: class x3 y1 k n) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdif (cfv cq cply) (csn c0p))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt k cn0 (cinf (crab n (wcel (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (cv n) cmul) cz) cn) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv F cdgr) (cseq cmul N cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) (cfv K N) cmo)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K (co cc0 (cfv F cdgr) cfz))) (wceq (co R (cfv K N) cmo) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ elqaa . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem elqaalem3 {k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class) (R: class n) (F: class k n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdif (cfv cq cply) (csn c0p))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt k cn0 (cinf (crab n (wcel (co (cfv (cv k) B2) (cv n) cmul) cz) cn) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv F cdgr) (cseq cmul N cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 caa) $;

--| The set of numbers generated by the roots of polynomials in the _rational numbers_ is the same as the set of algebraic numbers, which by ~ elaa are defined only in terms of polynomials over the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem elqaa {f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 caa) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wrex f2 (wceq (cfv A2 (cv f2)) cc0) (cdif (cfv cq cply) (csn c0p)))) $;

--| An element of the preimage of the algebraic numbers by a nonconstant polynomial is an algebraic number. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2026.)
theorem preimaaa (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cq cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F cdgr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 F) caa) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 caa) $;

--| Every rational number is algebraic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem qaa (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel A2 caa) $;

--| The rational numbers are contained in the algebraic numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem qssaa:
  $ wss cq caa $;

--| Zero is algebraic. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem _0aa:
  $ wcel cc0 caa $;

--| One is algebraic. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem _1aa:
  $ wcel c1 caa $;

--| The imaginary unit is algebraic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.)
theorem iaa:
  $ wcel ci caa $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ iaa as of 30-Aug-2026. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jul-2014.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem iaaOLD:
  $ wcel ci caa $;

--| The reciprocal of an algebraic number is algebraic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem aareccl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 caa) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) caa) $;

--| The conjugate of an algebraic number is algebraic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jul-2014.)
theorem aacjcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 caa) (wcel (cfv A2 ccj) caa) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aannen . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aannenlem1 {e a b c d: setvar} (A2: class) (H: class e a b c d):
  $ wceq H (cmpt a cn0 (crab b (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv b) (cv c)) cc0) (crab d (w3a (wne (cv d) c0p) (wbr (cfv (cv d) cdgr) (cv a) cle) (wral e (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv d) ccoe)) cabs) (cv a) cle) cn0)) (cfv cz cply))) cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel (cfv A2 H) cfn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aannen . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aannenlem2 {e a b c d: setvar} (H: class e a b c d):
  $ wceq H (cmpt a cn0 (crab b (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv b) (cv c)) cc0) (crab d (w3a (wne (cv d) c0p) (wbr (cfv (cv d) cdgr) (cv a) cle) (wral e (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv d) ccoe)) cabs) (cv a) cle) cn0)) (cfv cz cply))) cc)) $ >
  $ wceq caa (cuni (crn H)) $;

--| The algebraic numbers are countable. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aannenlem3 {e a b c d: setvar} (H: class e a b c d):
  $ wceq H (cmpt a cn0 (crab b (wrex c (wceq (cfv (cv b) (cv c)) cc0) (crab d (w3a (wne (cv d) c0p) (wbr (cfv (cv d) cdgr) (cv a) cle) (wral e (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv d) ccoe)) cabs) (cv a) cle) cn0)) (cfv cz cply))) cc)) $ >
  $ wbr caa cn cen $;

--| The algebraic numbers are countable. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aannen:
  $ wbr caa cn cen $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . An integer polynomial cannot inflate the denominator of a rational by more than its degree. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Nov-2014.)
theorem aalioulem1 (ph: wff) (F X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cfv (co X Y cdiv) F) (co Y (cfv F cdgr) cexp) cmul) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Sep-2020.)
theorem aalioulem2 {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class x3 q p):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wi (wceq (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) F) cc0) (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) N cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) cle))) cn) cz) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Nov-2014.)
theorem aalioulem3 {x3 r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class x3 r):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral r (wi (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv r) cmin) cabs) c1 cle) (wbr (co (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv r) F) cabs) cmul) (cfv (co A2 (cv r) cmin) cabs) cle)) cr) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aalioulem4 {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class x3 q p):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wi (wa (wne (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) F) cc0) (wbr (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) c1 cle)) (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) N cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) cle))) cn) cz) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aalioulem5 {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class q p):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wi (wne (cfv (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) F) cc0) (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) N cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) cle))) cn) cz) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aalioulem6 {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class q p):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) N cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) cle)) cn) cz) crp) $;

--| Liouville's theorem on diophantine approximation: Any algebraic number, being a root of a polynomial ` F ` in integer coefficients, is not approximable beyond order ` N = deg ( F ) ` by rational numbers. In this form, it also applies to rational numbers themselves, which are not well approximable by other rational numbers. This is Metamath 100 proof #18. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou {x3 q p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (N: class q p):
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv cz cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) N cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) clt)) cn) cz) crp) $;

--| Geometric series convergence with arbitrary shift, radix, and multiplicative constant. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem geolim3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt k (cfv A2 cuz) (co C (co B2 (co (cv k) A2 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc F A2) (co C (co c1 B2 cmin) cdiv) cli) $;

--| Liouville's approximation theorem for algebraic numbers per se. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou2 {x3 k q p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin caa cr)) (wrex k (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) (cv k) cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) clt)) cn) cz) crp) cn) $;

--| Liouville's approximation theorem extended to complex ` A ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou2b {x3 k q p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 caa) (wrex k (wrex x3 (wral p (wral q (wo (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv)) (wbr (co (cv x3) (co (cv q) (cv k) cexp) cdiv) (cfv (co A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cdiv) cmin) cabs) clt)) cn) cz) crp) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem1 {c: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (G: class c):
  $ wceq G (cmpt c (cfv A2 cuz) (co (co c2 (cneg (cfv A2 cfa)) cexp) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (co (cv c) A2 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz))) (wcel (cfv B2 G) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem2 {a c: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class a) (G: class c):
  $ wceq G (cmpt c (cfv A2 cuz) (co (co c2 (cneg (cfv A2 cfa)) cexp) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (co (cv c) A2 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz))) (wcel (cfv B2 F) (co cc0 (cfv B2 G) cioc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem3 {a b c: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class a) (G: class c):
  $ wceq G (cmpt c (cfv A2 cuz) (co (co c2 (cneg (cfv A2 cfa)) cexp) (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (co (cv c) A2 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (w3a (wcel (cseq caddc F A2) (cdm cli)) (wcel (csu b (cfv A2 cuz) (cfv (cv b) F)) crp) (wbr (csu b (cfv A2 cuz) (cfv (cv b) F)) (co c2 (co c2 (cneg (cfv A2 cfa)) cexp) cmul) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem8 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 crp)) (wrex x3 (wbr (co c2 (co c2 (cneg (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) cfa)) cexp) cmul) (co B2 (co (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) cfa) cexp) A2 cexp) cdiv) cle) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem4 {a b c: setvar} (F: class a) (H: class a b c) (L: class a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csu b cn (cfv (cv b) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt c cn (csu b (co c1 (cv c) cfz) (cfv (cv b) F))) $ >
  $ wcel L cr $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem5 {a b c: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class a) (H: class a b c) (L: class a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csu b cn (cfv (cv b) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt c cn (csu b (co c1 (cv c) cfz) (cfv (cv b) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (cfv A2 H) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem6 {a b c: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class a) (H: class a b c) (L: class a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csu b cn (cfv (cv b) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt c cn (csu b (co c1 (cv c) cfz) (cfv (cv b) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (co (cfv A2 H) (co c2 (cfv A2 cfa) cexp) cmul) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ aaliou3 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem7 {a b c: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class a) (H: class a b c) (L: class a b):
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csu b cn (cfv (cv b) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt c cn (csu b (co c1 (cv c) cfz) (cfv (cv b) F))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wa (wne (cfv A2 H) L) (wbr (cfv (co L (cfv A2 H) cmin) cabs) (co c2 (co c2 (cneg (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cfa)) cexp) cmul) cle)) $;

--| Example of a "Liouville number", a very simple definable transcendental real. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 20-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3lem9 {a b c: setvar} (F: class a) (H: class a b c) (L: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv a) cfa)) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csu b cn (cfv (cv b) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt c cn (csu b (co c1 (cv c) cfz) (cfv (cv b) F))) $ >
  $ wn (wcel L caa) $;

--| Example of a "Liouville number", a very simple definable transcendental real. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Nov-2014.)
theorem aaliou3 {k: setvar}:
  $ wnel (csu k cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv k) cfa)) cexp)) caa $;

--| The sum presented above is convergent, which means that the "Liouville number" is indeed real. (Contributed by Ender Ting, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem aaliou3r {k: setvar}:
  $ wcel (csu k cn (co c2 (cneg (cfv (cv k) cfa)) cexp)) cr $;

--| Taylor polynomial of a function.
term ctayl: class;

--| The class of analytic functions.
term cana: class;

--| Define the Taylor polynomial or Taylor series of a function. TODO-AV: ` n e. ( NN0 u. { +oo } ) ` should be replaced by ` n e. NN0* ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_tayl {x3 f2 k n s1 a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctayl (cmpo s1 f2 (cpr cr cc) (co cc (cv s1) cpm) (cmpo n a (cun cn0 (csn cpnf)) (ciin k (cin (co cc0 (cv n) cicc) cz) (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co (cv s1) (cv f2) cdvn)))) (ciun x3 cc (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (co ccnfld (cmpt k (cin (co cc0 (cv n) cicc) cz) (co (co (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv k) (co (cv s1) (cv f2) cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) (cv a) cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)) ctsu))))) $;

--| Define the set of analytic functions, which are functions such that the Taylor series of the function at each point converges to the function in some neighborhood of the point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_ana {x3 f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cana (cmpt s1 (cpr cr cc) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (cdm (cin (cv f2) (co cpnf (cv x3) (co (cv s1) (cv f2) ctayl)))) (cfv (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) (cv s1) crest) cnt))) (cdm (cv f2))) (co cc (cv s1) cpm))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylfval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylfvallem1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel X cc)) (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co X B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul) cc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylfval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylfvallem {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X cc)) (wss (co ccnfld (cmpt k (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz) (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co X B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)) ctsu) cc) $;

--| Define the Taylor polynomial of a function. The constant ` Tayl ` is a function of five arguments: ` S ` is the base set with respect to evaluate the derivatives (generally ` RR ` or ` CC ` ), ` F ` is the function we are approximating, at point ` B ` , to order ` N ` . The result is a polynomial function of ` x ` . This "extended" version of ~ taylpfval additionally handles the case ` N = +oo ` , in which case this is not a polynomial but an infinite series, the Taylor series of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylfval {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (ciun x3 cc (cxp (csn (cv x3)) (co ccnfld (cmpt k (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz) (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)) ctsu)))) $;

--| Value of the Taylor series as a relation (elementhood in the domain here expresses that the series is convergent). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem eltayl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N X: class) (Y: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr X Y T) (wa (wcel X cc) (wcel Y (co ccnfld (cmpt k (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz) (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co X B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)) ctsu)))) $;

--| The Taylor series defines a function on a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylf {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm T) cc T) $;

--| The Taylor series is always defined at the basepoint, with value equal to the value of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem tayl0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel B2 (cdm T)) (wceq (cfv B2 T) (cfv B2 F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylpfval and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylplem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class) (k: setvar) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cin (co cc0 N cicc) cz))) (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylpfval and similar theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylplem2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel X cc)) (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co X B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul) cc) $;

--| Define the Taylor polynomial of a function. The constant ` Tayl ` is a function of five arguments: ` S ` is the base set with respect to evaluate the derivatives (generally ` RR ` or ` CC ` ), ` F ` is the function we are approximating, at point ` B ` , to order ` N ` . The result is a polynomial function of ` x ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylpfval {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (cmpt x3 cc (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| The Taylor polynomial is a function on the complex numbers (even if the base set of the original function is the reals). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylpf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S T F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cc cc T) $;

--| Value of the Taylor polynomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylpval {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 S: class) (T: class k) (F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X T) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) (co (co X B2 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| The Taylor polynomial is a polynomial of degree (at most) ` N ` . This version of ~ taylply shows that the coefficients of ` T ` are in a subring of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 30-Apr-2025.)
theorem taylply2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 D S: class) (T: class k) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv ccnfld csubrg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel (co (cfv B2 (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel T (cfv D cply)) (wbr (cfv T cdgr) N cle)) $;

--| The Taylor polynomial is a polynomial of degree (at most) ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem taylply (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S T F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel T (cfv cc cply)) (wbr (cfv T cdgr) N cle)) $;

--| The derivative of the Taylor polynomial is the Taylor polynomial of the derivative of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem dvtaylp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc (co (co N c1 caddc) B2 (co S F ctayl)) cdv) (co N B2 (co S (co S F cdv) ctayl))) $;

--| The ` M ` -th derivative of the Taylor polynomial is the Taylor polynomial of the ` M ` -th derivative of the function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvntaylp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv (co N M caddc) (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M (co cc (co (co N M caddc) B2 (co S F ctayl)) cdvn)) (co N B2 (co S (cfv M (co S F cdvn)) ctayl))) $;

--| The first ` N ` derivatives of the Taylor polynomial at ` B ` match the derivatives of the function from which it is derived. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem dvntaylp0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S T F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv M (co cc T cdvn))) (cfv B2 (cfv M (co S F cdvn)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylth . This is the main part of Taylor's theorem, except for the induction step, which is supposed to be proven using L'H&ocirc;pital's rule. However, since our proof of L'H&ocirc;pital assumes that ` S = RR ` , we can only do this part generically, and for ~ taylth itself we must restrict to ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem taylthlem1 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (R: class x3 y1 n) (S T F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (cfv N (co S F cdvn))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co S F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) T) cmin) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) N cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) (co c1 N cfzo)) (wcel cc0 (co (cmpt y1 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (co N (cv n) cmin) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (co N (cv n) cmin) (co cc T cdvn))) cmin) (co (co (cv y1) B2 cmin) (cv n) cexp) cdiv)) B2 climc)))) (wcel cc0 (co (cmpt x3 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cmin) (co S F cdvn))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cmin) (co cc T cdvn))) cmin) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cexp) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co R B2 climc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ taylth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem taylthlem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (cfv N (co cr F cdvn))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co cr F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c1 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co (cmpt x3 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N M cmin) (co cr F cdvn))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N M cmin) (co cc T cdvn))) cmin) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) M cexp) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co (cmpt x3 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N (co M c1 caddc) cmin) (co cr F cdvn))) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N (co M c1 caddc) cmin) (co cc T cdvn))) cmin) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) (co M c1 caddc) cexp) cdiv)) B2 climc)) $;

--| Taylor's theorem. The Taylor polynomial of a ` N ` -times differentiable function is such that the error term goes to zero faster than ` ( x - B ) ^ N ` . This is Metamath 100 proof #35. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem taylth {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (R: class x3) (T F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (cfv N (co cr F cdvn))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (co N B2 (co cr F ctayl)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (cdif A2 (csn B2)) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) T) cmin) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmin) N cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co R B2 climc)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the uniform convergence predicate.
term culm: class;

--| Define the uniform convergence of a sequence of functions. Here ` F ( ~~>u `` S ) G ` if ` F ` is a sequence of functions ` F ( n ) , n e. NN ` defined on ` S ` and ` G ` is a function on ` S ` , and for every ` 0 < x ` there is a ` j ` such that the functions ` F ( k ) ` for ` j <_ k ` are all uniformly within ` x ` of ` G ` on the domain ` S ` . Compare with ~ df-clim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_ulm {x3 y1 z f2 j k n s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq culm (cmpt s1 cvv (copab f2 y1 (wrex n (w3a (wf (cfv (cv n) cuz) (co cc (cv s1) cmap) (cv f2)) (wf (cv s1) cc (cv y1)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))) (cfv (cv z) (cv y1)) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) crp)) cz))) $;

--| The uniform limit relation is a relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmrel (S: class):
  $ wrel (cfv S culm) $;

--| Closure of the base set in a uniform limit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmscl (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wcel S cvv) $;

--| Express the predicate: The sequence of functions ` F ` converges uniformly to ` G ` on ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmval {x3 z j k n: setvar} (S F G: class) (V: class x3 z j k):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wb (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wrex n (w3a (wf (cfv (cv n) cuz) (co cc S cmap) F) (wf S cc G) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv z) G) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) crp)) cz)) $;

--| Closure of a uniform limit of functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmcl (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wf S cc G) $;

--| Closure of a uniform limit of functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmf {n: setvar} (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wrex n (wf (cfv (cv n) cuz) (co cc S cmap) F) cz) $;

--| Closure of a uniform limit of functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmpm (S F G: class):
  $ wi (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wcel F (co (co cc S cmap) cz cpm)) $;

--| Closure of a uniform limit of functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmf2 (S F G Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F Z) (wbr F G (cfv S culm))) (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $;

--| Simplify ~ ulmval when ` F ` and ` G ` are known to be functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulm2 {x3 z j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z) (B2: class z j k) (S F G M: class) (V: class x3 z j k) (Z: class z k):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) Z) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) S)) (wceq (cfv (cv z) G) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| The uniform limit property. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmi {z j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class z) (B2: class z j k) (C S F G M: class) (Z: class z k):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) Z) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) S)) (wceq (cfv (cv z) G) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) C clt) S) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A uniform limit of functions converges pointwise. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmclm {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S F G H M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv k) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (cfv A2 G) cli) $;

--| A sequence of functions converges iff the tail of the sequence converges (for any finite cutoff). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmres (ph: wff) (S F G M N W Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wbr (cres F W) G (cfv S culm))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ulmshft . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmshftlem {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G H: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (W: class) (Z: class n):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpt n W (cfv (co (cv n) K cmin) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wbr H G (cfv S culm))) $;

--| A sequence of functions converges iff the shifted sequence converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmshft {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G H: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (W: class) (Z: class n):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpt n W (cfv (co (cv n) K cmin) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wbr H G (cfv S culm))) $;

--| Every function converges uniformly on the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulm0 (ph: wff) (S F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq S c0)) (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $;

--| A sequence of functions uniformly converges to at most one limit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2017.)
theorem ulmuni (S F G H: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) (wbr F H (cfv S culm))) (wceq G H) $;

--| Two ways to express that a function has a limit. (The expression ` ( ( ~~>u `` S ) `` F ) ` is sometimes useful as a shorthand for "the unique limit of the function ` F ` "). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jul-2017.)
theorem ulmdm (S F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cdm (cfv S culm))) (wbr F (cfv F (cfv S culm)) (cfv S culm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ulmcau and ~ ulmcau2 : show the equivalence of the four- and five-quantifier forms of the Cauchy convergence condition. Compare ~ cau3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmcaulem {x3 z j k m: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (M: class x3 m) (V: class x3 z j k m) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv j) F)) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral m (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv m) F)) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| A sequence of functions converges uniformly iff it is uniformly Cauchy, which is to say that for every ` 0 < x ` there is a ` j ` such that for all ` j <_ k ` the functions ` F ( k ) ` and ` F ( j ) ` are uniformly within ` x ` of each other on ` S ` . This is the four-quantifier version, see ~ ulmcau2 for the more conventional five-quantifier version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmcau {x3 z j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (M: class x3) (V: class x3 z j k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm (cfv S culm))) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv j) F)) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| A sequence of functions converges uniformly iff it is uniformly Cauchy, which is to say that for every ` 0 < x ` there is a ` j ` such that for all ` j <_ k , m ` the functions ` F ( k ) ` and ` F ( m ) ` are uniformly within ` x ` of each other on ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmcau2 {x3 z j k m: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (M: class x3 m) (V: class x3 z j k m) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm (cfv S culm))) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wral m (wral z (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv m) F)) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) S) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| A uniform limit of functions is still a uniform limit if restricted to a subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (S T: class) (G M W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 Z A2) G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 Z (cres A2 T)) (cres G T) (cfv T culm)) $;

--| A uniform limit of bounded functions is bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmbdd {x3 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G: class) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wrex x3 (wral z (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) cabs) (cv x3) cle) S) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral z (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) cabs) (cv x3) cle) S) cr) $;

--| A uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmcn (ph: wff) (S F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co S cc ccncf) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co S cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ulmdv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem ulmdvlem1 {x3 z k m: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 z k m) (C: class x3 m) (R: class z k) (S: class) (U: class x3 z k m) (F: class) (G H: class x3 k m) (M: class z m) (N: class z) (W: class x3 z k m) (X: class) (Y: class x3 m) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc X cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F))) (cfv (cv z) G) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co S (cfv (cv k) F) cdv)) H (cfv X culm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel U crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel W crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr U W clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wss (co C U (cfv (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp S S)) cbl)) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cfv (co Y C cmin) cabs) U clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wral m (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) (co S (cfv N F) cdv)) (cfv (cv x3) (co S (cfv (cv m) F) cdv)) cmin) cabs) (co (co R c2 cdiv) c2 cdiv) clt) X) (cfv N cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv C (co S (cfv N F) cdv)) (cfv C H) cmin) cabs) (co R c2 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wne Y C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi (wbr (cfv (co Y C cmin) cabs) W clt) (wbr (cfv (co (co (co (cfv Y (cfv N F)) (cfv C (cfv N F)) cmin) (co Y C cmin) cdiv) (cfv C (co S (cfv N F) cdv)) cmin) cabs) (co (co R c2 cdiv) c2 cdiv) clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cfv (co (co (co (cfv Y G) (cfv C G) cmin) (co Y C cmin) cdiv) (cfv C H) cmin) cabs) R clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ulmdv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2015.)
theorem ulmdvlem2 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G H: class k) (M: class z) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc X cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F))) (cfv (cv z) G) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co S (cfv (cv k) F) cdv)) H (cfv X culm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cdm (co S (cfv (cv k) F) cdv)) X) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ulmdv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2015.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem ulmdvlem3 {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G H: class k) (M: class z) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc X cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F))) (cfv (cv z) G) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co S (cfv (cv k) F) cdv)) H (cfv X culm)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (cv z) (cfv (cv z) H) (co S G cdv)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a sequence of differentiable functions on ` X ` which converge pointwise to ` G ` , and the derivatives of ` F ( n ) ` converge uniformly to ` H ` , then ` G ` is differentiable with derivative ` H ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem ulmdv {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class) (G H: class k) (M: class z) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc X cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wbr (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F))) (cfv (cv z) G) cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co S (cfv (cv k) F) cdv)) H (cfv X culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S G cdv) H) $;

--| The Weierstrass M-test. If ` F ` is a sequence of functions which are uniformly bounded by the convergent sequence ` M ( k ) ` , then the series generated by the sequence ` F ` converges uniformly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem mtest {z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F M N: class) (V W: class z k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) M) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) Z) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) cabs) (cfv (cv k) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc M N) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq (cof caddc) F N) (cdm (cfv S culm))) $;

--| Given the hypotheses of the Weierstrass M-test, the convergent function of the sequence is uniformly bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem mtestbdd {x3 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class) (T: class k) (F M N: class) (V W: class x3 z k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z (co cc S cmap) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) M) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv k) Z) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) (cfv (cv k) F)) cabs) (cfv (cv k) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc M N) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq (cof caddc) F N) T (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral z (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) T) cabs) (cv x3) cle) S) cr) $;

--| A uniform limit of measurable functions is measurable. (This is just a corollary of the fact that a pointwise limit of measurable functions is measurable, see ~ mbflim .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem mbfulm (ph: wff) (S F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cmbf F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cmbf) $;

--| A uniform limit of integrable functions is integrable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.)
theorem iblulm (ph: wff) (S F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cibl F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cibl) $;

--| A uniform limit of integrals of integrable functions converges to the integral of the limit function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem itgulm {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cibl F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (citg x3 S (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F)))) (citg x3 S (cfv (cv x3) G)) cli) $;

--| A uniform limit of integrals of integrable functions converges to the integral of the limit function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem itgulm2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 k) (S: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S A2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (cmpt x3 S A2)) (cmpt x3 S B2) (cfv S culm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 S B2) cibl) (wbr (cmpt k Z (citg x3 S A2)) (citg x3 S B2) cli)) $;

--| Value of the function ` G ` that gives the sequence of monomials of a power series. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem pserval {x3 m n: setvar} (A2: class) (G X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X cc) (wceq (cfv X G) (cmpt m cn0 (co (cfv (cv m) A2) (co X (cv m) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| Value of the function ` G ` that gives the sequence of monomials of a power series. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem pserval2 {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class) (G: class x3 n) (N: class x3) (X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv N (cfv X G)) (co (cfv N A2) (co X N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| The sequence of terms in the infinite sequence defining a power series for fixed ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem psergf {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2: class) (G X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc (cfv X G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ radcnvlt1 , ~ radcnvle . If ` X ` is a point closer to zero than ` Y ` and the power series converges at ` Y ` , then it converges absolutely at ` X ` , even if the terms in the sequence are multiplied by ` n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem radcnvlem1 {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2: class) (G H X Y: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) (cfv Y cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv Y G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt m cn0 (co (cv m) (cfv (cfv (cv m) (cfv X G)) cabs) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc H cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ radcnvlt1 , ~ radcnvle . If ` X ` is a point closer to zero than ` Y ` and the power series converges at ` Y ` , then it converges absolutely at ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnvlem2 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2: class) (G X Y: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) (cfv Y cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv Y G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (ccom cabs (cfv X G)) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ radcnvlt1 , ~ radcnvle . If ` X ` is a point closer to zero than ` Y ` and the power series converges at ` Y ` , then it converges at ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem radcnvlem3 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2: class) (G X Y: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) (cfv Y cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv Y G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv X G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Zero is always a convergent point for any power series. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnv0 {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (G: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr)) $;

--| The radius of convergence ` R ` of an infinite series is a nonnegative extended real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnvcl {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 n r) (G: class x3 n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $;

--| If ` X ` is within the open disk of radius ` R ` centered at zero, then the infinite series converges absolutely at ` X ` , and also converges when the series is multiplied by ` n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnvlt1 {x3 m n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 m n r) (G: class x3 n) (H X: class x3 n r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt m cn0 (co (cv m) (cfv (cfv (cv m) (cfv X G)) cabs) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cseq caddc H cc0) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cseq caddc (ccom cabs (cfv X G)) cc0) (cdm cli))) $;

--| If ` X ` is within the open disk of radius ` R ` centered at zero, then the infinite series converges at ` X ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnvlt2 {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 n r) (G: class x3 n) (X: class x3 n r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv X G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| If ` X ` is a convergent point of the infinite series, then ` X ` is within the closed disk of radius ` R ` centered at zero. Or, by contraposition, the series diverges at any point strictly more than ` R ` from the origin. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem radcnvle {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 n r) (G: class x3 n) (X: class x3 n r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv X G) cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) R cle) $;

--| The radius of convergence of the (formal) derivative ` H ` of the power series ` G ` is at least as large as the radius of convergence of ` G ` . (In fact they are equal, but we don't have as much use for the negative side of this claim.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvradcnv {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 n r) (G: class x3 n) (H: class x3 n r) (X: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) A2) cmul) (co X (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc H cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| If ` S ` is a region contained in a circle of radius ` M < R ` , then the sequence of partial sums of the infinite series converges uniformly on ` S ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Feb-2015.)
theorem pserulm {x3 y1 i j n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class i) (R: class x3 y1 i j n r) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 i j n r) (G: class x3 n) (H M: class x3 n r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i cn0 (cmpt y1 S (cfv (cv i) (cseq caddc (cfv (cv y1) G) cc0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 M cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H F (cfv S culm)) $;

--| Since by ~ pserulm the series converges uniformly, it is also continuous by ~ ulmcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Mar-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem psercn2 {x3 y1 i j n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class i) (R: class x3 y1 i j n r) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 i j n r) (G: class x3 n) (H M: class x3 n r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i cn0 (cmpt y1 S (cfv (cv i) (cseq caddc (cfv (cv y1) G) cc0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 M cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psercn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem psercnlem2 {x3 y1 j n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (R: class x3 y1 j n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) S)) (w3a (wcel M crp) (wbr (cfv (cv a) cabs) M clt) (wbr M R clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) S)) (w3a (wcel (cv a) (co cc0 M (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl))) (wss (co cc0 M (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 M cicc))) (wss (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 M cicc)) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ psercn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2015.)
theorem psercnlem1 {x3 y1 j n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (R: class x3 y1 j n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) S)) (w3a (wcel M crp) (wbr (cfv (cv a) cabs) M clt) (wbr M R clt)) $;

--| An infinite series converges to a continuous function on the open disk of radius ` R ` , where ` R ` is the radius of convergence of the series. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Mar-2015.)
theorem psercn {x3 y1 j n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (R: class x3 y1 j n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pserdv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem pserdvlem1 {x3 y1 j n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (R: class x3 y1 j n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) S)) (w3a (wcel (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) crp) (wbr (cfv (cv a) cabs) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) R clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pserdv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem pserdvlem2 {x3 y1 j k n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (B2: class n r a) (R: class x3 y1 j k n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j k n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co cc0 (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) S)) (wceq (co cc (cres F B2) cdv) (cmpt y1 B2 (csu k cn0 (co (co (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) A2) cmul) (co (cv y1) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| The derivative of a power series on its region of convergence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem pserdv {x3 y1 j k n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (B2: class n r a) (R: class x3 y1 j k n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j k n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co cc0 (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) (cmpt y1 S (csu k cn0 (co (co (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) A2) cmul) (co (cv y1) (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| The derivative of a power series on its region of convergence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem pserdv2 {x3 y1 j k n r a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class) (B2: class n r a) (R: class x3 y1 j k n r a) (S: class x3 n r) (F: class x3 y1 j k n r) (G: class x3 n a) (M: class x3 n r a):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 S (csu j cn0 (cfv (cv j) (cfv (cv y1) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cif (wcel R cr) (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) R caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co cc0 (co (co (cfv (cv a) cabs) M caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc F cdv) (cmpt y1 S (csu k cn (co (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) A2) cmul) (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem1 {z n r: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) (cmpt z cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv z) (cv n) cexp) cmul)))) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . The peculiar region ` S ` , known as a _Stolz angle_ , is a teardrop-shaped subset of the closed unit ball containing ` 1 ` . Indeed, except for ` 1 ` itself, the rest of the Stolz angle is enclosed in the open unit ball. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem abelthlem2 {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel c1 S) (wss (cdif S (csn c1)) (co cc0 c1 (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem abelthlem3 {z n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X S)) (wcel (cseq caddc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co X (cv n) cexp) cmul)) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem abelthlem4 {x3 z n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem5 {x3 z k n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z k n) (M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc A2 cc0) cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co cc0 c1 (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)))) (wcel (cseq caddc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) (cseq caddc A2 cc0)) (co X (cv k) cexp) cmul)) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem6 {x3 z n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc A2 cc0) cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdif S (csn c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X F) (co (co c1 X cmin) (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) (cseq caddc A2 cc0)) (co X (cv n) cexp) cmul)) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2015.)
theorem abelthlem7a {x3 z n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc A2 cc0) cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdif S (csn c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel X cc) (wbr (cfv (co c1 X cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv X cabs) cmin) cmul) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem7 {x3 z k n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 R: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z k n) (M: class) (N: class x3 z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc A2 cc0) cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdif S (csn c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cseq caddc A2 cc0)) cabs) R clt) (cfv N cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co c1 X cmin) cabs) (co R (co (csu n (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cseq caddc A2 cc0)) cabs)) c1 caddc) cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) cabs) (co (co M c1 caddc) R cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem8 {x3 y1 z w n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 R: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc A2 cc0) cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel R crp)) (wrex w (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (cv w) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv c1 F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmin) cabs) R clt)) S) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ abelth . By adjusting the constant term, we can assume that the entire series converges to ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelthlem9 {x3 y1 z w n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 R: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel R crp)) (wrex w (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (cv w) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv c1 F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmin) cabs) R clt)) S) crp) $;

--| Abel's theorem. If the power series ` sum_ n e. NN0 A ( n ) ( x ^ n ) ` is convergent at ` 1 ` , then it is equal to the limit from "below", along a Stolz angle ` S ` (note that the ` M = 1 ` case of a Stolz angle is the real line ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` ). (Continuity on ` S \ { 1 } ` follows more generally from ~ psercn .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem abelth {x3 z n: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (S: class z) (F: class x3 z n) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M cle) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab z (wbr (cfv (co c1 (cv z) cmin) cabs) (co M (co c1 (cfv (cv z) cabs) cmin) cmul) cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 S (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S cc ccncf)) $;

--| Abel's theorem, restricted to the ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem abelth2 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3 n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc A2 cc0) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csu n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) cc ccncf)) $;

--| The exponential function is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem efcn:
  $ wcel ce (co cc cc ccncf) $;

--| Sine is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 28-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem sincn:
  $ wcel csin (co cc cc ccncf) $;

--| Cosine is continuous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 28-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2014.)
theorem coscn:
  $ wcel ccos (co cc cc ccncf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ reeff1o . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2014.)
theorem reeff1olem {x3: setvar} (U: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel U cr) (wbr c1 U clt)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ce) U) cr) $;

--| The real exponential function is one-to-one onto. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Nov-2013.)
theorem reeff1o:
  $ wf1o cr crp (cres ce cr) $;

--| The exponential function on the reals determines an isomorphism from reals onto positive reals. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Mar-2014.)
theorem reefiso:
  $ wiso cr crp clt clt (cres ce cr) $;

--| The exponential function on the reals is a strictly convex function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem efcvx (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cioo))) (wbr (cfv (co (co T A2 cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) B2 cmul) caddc) ce) (co (co T (cfv A2 ce) cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) (cfv B2 ce) cmul) caddc) clt) $;

--| The exponential function is a group isomorphism from the group of reals under addition to the group of positive reals under multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem reefgim (P: class):
  $ wceq P (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) crp cress) $ >
  $ wcel (cres ce cr) (co crefld P cgim) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pire , ~ pigt2lt4 and ~ sinpi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem pilem1 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cin crp (cima (ccnv csin) (csn cc0)))) (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wceq (cfv A2 csin) cc0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pire , ~ pigt2lt4 and ~ sinpi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2014.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.)
theorem pilem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co c2 c4 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 csin) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 csin) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co cpi A2 caddc) c2 cdiv) B2 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ pire , ~ pigt2lt4 and ~ sinpi . Existence part. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.) (Revised by AV, 14-Sep-2020.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 30-Jun-2022.)
theorem pilem3:
  $ wa (wcel cpi (co c2 c4 cioo)) (wceq (cfv cpi csin) cc0) $;

--| ` _pi ` is between 2 and 4. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem pigt2lt4:
  $ wa (wbr c2 cpi clt) (wbr cpi c4 clt) $;

--| The sine of ` _pi ` is 0. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem sinpi:
  $ wceq (cfv cpi csin) cc0 $;

--| ` _pi ` is a real number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem pire:
  $ wcel cpi cr $;

--| ` ( 2 x. _pi ) ` is a real number. (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _2pire:
  $ wcel (co c2 cpi cmul) cr $;

--| ` _pi ` is a complex number. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 6-Dec-2018.)
theorem picn:
  $ wcel cpi cc $;

--| ` ( 2 x. _pi ) ` is a complex number. (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem _2picn:
  $ wcel (co c2 cpi cmul) cc $;

--| ` _pi ` is positive. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem pipos:
  $ wbr cc0 cpi clt $;

--| ` _pi ` is nonnegative. (Contributed by Umit Teoman Dogan, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem pige0:
  $ wbr cc0 cpi cle $;

--| ` _pi ` is nonzero. (Contributed by SN, 25-Apr-2025.)
theorem pine0:
  $ wne cpi cc0 $;

--| ` _pi ` is a positive real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem pirp:
  $ wcel cpi crp $;

--| ` -u _pi ` is a complex number. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem negpicn:
  $ wcel (cneg cpi) cc $;

--| Lemma for ~ sinhalfpi and ~ coshalfpi . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem sinhalfpilem:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co cpi c2 cdiv) csin) c1) (wceq (cfv (co cpi c2 cdiv) ccos) cc0) $;

--| ` _pi / 2 ` is real. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem halfpire:
  $ wcel (co cpi c2 cdiv) cr $;

--| ` -u _pi / 2 ` is real. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem neghalfpire:
  $ wcel (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) cr $;

--| ` -u _pi / 2 ` is an extended real. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem neghalfpirx:
  $ wcel (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) cxr $;

--| Adding ` _pi / 2 ` to itself gives ` _pi ` . See ~ 2halves . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem pidiv2halves:
  $ wceq (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) (co cpi c2 cdiv) caddc) cpi $;

--| The sine of ` _pi / 2 ` is 1. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem sinhalfpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co cpi c2 cdiv) csin) c1 $;

--| The cosine of ` _pi / 2 ` is 0. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem coshalfpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co cpi c2 cdiv) ccos) cc0 $;

--| The cosine of ` -u _pi / 2 ` is zero. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem cosneghalfpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) ccos) cc0 $;

--| The exponential of ` _i _pi / 2 ` is ` _i ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem efhalfpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co ci (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) ce) ci $;

--| The cosine of ` _pi ` is ` -u 1 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem cospi:
  $ wceq (cfv cpi ccos) (cneg c1) $;

--| The exponential of ` _i x. _pi ` is ` -u 1 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem efipi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co ci cpi cmul) ce) (cneg c1) $;

--| Euler's identity. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem eulerid:
  $ wceq (co (cfv (co ci cpi cmul) ce) c1 caddc) cc0 $;

--| The sine of ` 2 _pi ` is 0. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem sin2pi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co c2 cpi cmul) csin) cc0 $;

--| The cosine of ` 2 _pi ` is 1. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.)
theorem cos2pi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co c2 cpi cmul) ccos) c1 $;

--| The exponential of ` 2 _pi _i ` is ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem ef2pi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) ce) c1 $;

--| If ` K ` is an integer, then the exponential of ` 2 K _pi _i ` is ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem ef2kpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) K cmul) ce) c1) $;

--| The exponential function is periodic. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem efper (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) K cmul) caddc) ce) (cfv A2 ce)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sinper and ~ cosper . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem sinperlem (A2 D F K O: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (co (co (cfv (co ci A2 cmul) ce) (cfv (co (cneg ci) A2 cmul) ce) O) D cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) cc) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) F) (co (co (cfv (co ci (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) cmul) ce) (cfv (co (cneg ci) (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) cmul) ce) O) D cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| The sine function is periodic. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem sinper (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) csin) (cfv A2 csin)) $;

--| The cosine function is periodic. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem cosper (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) caddc) ccos) (cfv A2 ccos)) $;

--| If ` K ` is an integer, then the sine of ` 2 K _pi ` is 0. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem sin2kpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) csin) cc0) $;

--| If ` K ` is an integer, then the cosine of ` 2 K _pi ` is 1. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem cos2kpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co K (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) ccos) c1) $;

--| Sine of a number subtracted from ` 2 x. _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem sin2pim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co c2 cpi cmul) A2 cmin) csin) (cneg (cfv A2 csin))) $;

--| Cosine of a number subtracted from ` 2 x. _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem cos2pim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co c2 cpi cmul) A2 cmin) ccos) (cfv A2 ccos)) $;

--| Sine of a number less ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem sinmpi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co A2 cpi cmin) csin) (cneg (cfv A2 csin))) $;

--| Cosine of a number less ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem cosmpi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co A2 cpi cmin) ccos) (cneg (cfv A2 ccos))) $;

--| Sine of a number plus ` _pi ` . (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-2008.)
theorem sinppi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co A2 cpi caddc) csin) (cneg (cfv A2 csin))) $;

--| Cosine of a number plus ` _pi ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2008.)
theorem cosppi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co A2 cpi caddc) ccos) (cneg (cfv A2 ccos))) $;

--| The exponential function at ` _i ` times a real number less ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem efimpi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co ci (co A2 cpi cmin) cmul) ce) (cneg (cfv (co ci A2 cmul) ce))) $;

--| The sine of ` _pi / 2 ` plus a number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sinhalfpip (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) A2 caddc) csin) (cfv A2 ccos)) $;

--| The sine of ` _pi / 2 ` minus a number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sinhalfpim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) A2 cmin) csin) (cfv A2 ccos)) $;

--| The cosine of ` _pi / 2 ` plus a number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem coshalfpip (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) A2 caddc) ccos) (cneg (cfv A2 csin))) $;

--| The cosine of ` _pi / 2 ` minus a number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem coshalfpim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) A2 cmin) ccos) (cfv A2 csin)) $;

--| Ptolemy's Theorem. This theorem is named after the Greek astronomer and mathematician Ptolemy (Claudius Ptolemaeus). This particular version is expressed using the sine function. It is proved by expanding all the multiplication of sines to a product of cosines of differences using ~ sinmul , then using algebraic simplification to show that both sides are equal. This formalization is based on the proof in "Trigonometry" by Gelfand and Saul. This is Metamath 100 proof #95. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 31-May-2015.)
theorem ptolemy (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) caddc) cpi)) (wceq (co (co (cfv A2 csin) (cfv B2 csin) cmul) (co (cfv C csin) (cfv D csin) cmul) caddc) (co (cfv (co B2 C caddc) csin) (cfv (co A2 C caddc) csin) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sincosq1sgn . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq1lem (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr A2 (co cpi c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csin) clt) $;

--| The signs of the sine and cosine functions in the first quadrant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq1sgn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csin) clt) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 ccos) clt)) $;

--| The signs of the sine and cosine functions in the second quadrant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq2sgn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) cpi cioo)) (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csin) clt) (wbr (cfv A2 ccos) cc0 clt)) $;

--| The signs of the sine and cosine functions in the third quadrant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq3sgn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cpi (co c3 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) cioo)) (wa (wbr (cfv A2 csin) cc0 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 ccos) cc0 clt)) $;

--| The signs of the sine and cosine functions in the fourth quadrant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 24-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq4sgn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (co c3 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) (co c2 cpi cmul) cioo)) (wa (wbr (cfv A2 csin) cc0 clt) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 ccos) clt)) $;

--| Location of the zeroes of cosine in ` ( 0 [,] _pi ) ` . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem coseq00topi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccos) cc0) (wceq A2 (co cpi c2 cdiv))) $;

--| Location of the zeroes of cosine in ` ( -u _pi (,] _pi ) ` . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem coseq0negpitopi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccos) cc0) (wcel A2 (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv))))) $;

--| Positive real closure of the tangent function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.)
theorem tanrpcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wcel (cfv A2 ctan) crp) $;

--| The tangent function is greater than its argument on positive reals in its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.)
theorem tangtx (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 ctan) clt) $;

--| The tangent function is greater than or equal to its argument in absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem tanabsge (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (cfv A2 ctan) cabs) cle) $;

--| The sine of a number strictly between ` 0 ` and ` _pi ` is positive. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 15-Mar-2008.)
theorem sinq12gt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cioo)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csin) clt) $;

--| The sine of a number between ` 0 ` and ` _pi ` is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem sinq12ge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csin) cle) $;

--| The sine of a number strictly between ` _pi ` and ` 2 x. _pi ` is negative. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.)
theorem sinq34lt0t (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cpi (co c2 cpi cmul) cioo)) (wbr (cfv A2 csin) cc0 clt) $;

--| The cosine of a number strictly between ` -u _pi / 2 ` and ` _pi / 2 ` is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem cosq14gt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 ccos) clt) $;

--| The cosine of a number between ` -u _pi / 2 ` and ` _pi / 2 ` is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem cosq14ge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 ccos) cle) $;

--| Complementarity of the sine and cosine functions in the first quadrant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincosq1eq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) c1)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) csin) (cfv (co B2 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) ccos)) $;

--| The sine and cosine of ` _pi / 4 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Jan-2008.)
theorem sincos4thpi:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co cpi c4 cdiv) csin) (co c1 (cfv c2 csqrt) cdiv)) (wceq (cfv (co cpi c4 cdiv) ccos) (co c1 (cfv c2 csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| The tangent of ` _pi / 4 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by SN, 2-Sep-2025.)
theorem tan4thpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (co cpi c4 cdiv) ctan) c1 $;

--| The sine and cosine of ` _pi / 6 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 24-Sep-2020.)
theorem sincos6thpi:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co cpi c6 cdiv) csin) (co c1 c2 cdiv)) (wceq (cfv (co cpi c6 cdiv) ccos) (co (cfv c3 csqrt) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The sine and cosine of ` _pi / 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem sincos3rdpi:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co cpi c3 cdiv) csin) (co (cfv c3 csqrt) c2 cdiv)) (wceq (cfv (co cpi c3 cdiv) ccos) (co c1 c2 cdiv)) $;

--| ` _pi ` is greater than 3. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 21-Aug-2020.)
theorem pigt3:
  $ wbr c3 cpi clt $;

--| ` _pi ` is greater than or equal to 3. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem pige3:
  $ wbr c3 cpi cle $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ pige3 . This proof is based on the geometric observation that a hexagon of unit side length has perimeter 6, which is less than the unit-radius circumcircle, of perimeter ` 2 _pi ` . We translate this to algebra by looking at the function ` _e ^ ( _i x ) ` as ` x ` goes from ` 0 ` to ` _pi / 3 ` ; it moves at unit speed and travels distance ` 1 ` , hence ` 1 <_ _pi / 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem pige3ALT:
  $ wbr c3 cpi cle $;

--| The absolute value of sine has period ` _pi ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.)
theorem abssinper (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co A2 (co K cpi cmul) caddc) csin) cabs) (cfv (cfv A2 csin) cabs)) $;

--| The sine of an integer multiple of ` _pi ` is 0. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-2008.)
theorem sinkpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co K cpi cmul) csin) cc0) $;

--| The absolute value of the cosine of an integer multiple of ` _pi ` is 1. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2008.)
theorem coskpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co K cpi cmul) ccos) cabs) c1) $;

--| A complex number whose sine is zero is an integer multiple of ` _pi ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem sineq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csin) cc0) (wcel (co A2 cpi cdiv) cz)) $;

--| A complex number whose cosine is one is an integer multiple of ` 2 _pi ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem coseq1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccos) c1) (wcel (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) cz)) $;

--| Cosine is less than one between zero and ` 2 x. _pi ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Mar-2024.)
theorem cos02pilt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co c2 cpi cmul) cioo)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccos) c1 clt) $;

--| Cosine is less than one in the third and fourth quadrants. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Mar-2024.)
theorem cosq34lt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cpi (co c2 cpi cmul) cico)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccos) c1 clt) $;

--| A complex number whose exponential is one is an integer multiple of ` 2 _pi _i ` . (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem efeq1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ce) c1) (wcel (co A2 (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) cdiv) cz)) $;

--| The cosine function has no zeroes within the vertical strip of the complex plane between real part ` -u _pi / 2 ` and ` _pi / 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem cosne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wne (cfv A2 ccos) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cosord . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem cosordlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 ccos) (cfv A2 ccos) clt) $;

--| Cosine is decreasing over the closed interval from ` 0 ` to ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem cosord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpi cicc))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv B2 ccos) (cfv A2 ccos) clt)) $;

--| Cosine is between minus one and one on the open interval between zero and ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-May-2024.)
theorem cos0pilt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cioo)) (wcel (cfv A2 ccos) (co (cneg c1) c1 cioo)) $;

--| Cosine is one-to-one over the closed interval from ` 0 ` to ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2014.)
theorem cos11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpi cicc)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpi cicc))) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cfv A2 ccos) (cfv B2 ccos))) $;

--| Sine is increasing over the closed interval from ` -u ( _pi / 2 ) ` to ` ( _pi / 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.)
theorem sinord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) (wcel B2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 csin) (cfv B2 csin) clt)) $;

--| The cosine function is a bijection when restricted to its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem recosf1o:
  $ wf1o (co cc0 cpi cicc) (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc) (cres ccos (co cc0 cpi cicc)) $;

--| The sine function is a bijection when restricted to its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem resinf1o:
  $ wf1o (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc) (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc) (cres csin (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) $;

--| The tangent function is strictly increasing on the nonnegative part of its principal domain. (Lemma for ~ tanord .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.) Revised to replace an OLD theorem. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 20-Sep-2020.)
theorem tanord1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cico)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cico))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 ctan) (cfv B2 ctan) clt)) $;

--| The tangent function is strictly increasing on its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Apr-2015.)
theorem tanord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) (wcel B2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 ctan) (cfv B2 ctan) clt)) $;

--| The real part of the tangent of a complex number with real part in the open interval ` ( 0 (,) ( _pi / 2 ) ) ` is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem tanregt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cfv A2 ctan) cre) clt) $;

--| The interval ` ( -u _pi (,] _pi ) ` is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem negpitopissre:
  $ wss (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc) cr $;

--| The exponential function of a scaled complex number is a group homomorphism from the group of complex numbers under addition to the set of complex numbers under multiplication. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem efgh {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel X (cfv ccnfld csubg))) (wcel B2 X) (wcel C X)) (wceq (cfv (co B2 C caddc) F) (co (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efif1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem efif1olem1 (x3 y1: setvar) (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (co A2 (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) caddc) cioc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (co c2 cpi cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efif1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem efif1olem2 {y1: setvar} (z: setvar) (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (co A2 (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) caddc) cioc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cv z) cr)) (wrex y1 (wcel (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmin) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) cz) D) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efif1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-May-2015.)
theorem efif1olem3 {w: setvar} (ph: wff) (x3: setvar) (C D: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (cmpt w D (cfv (co ci (cv w) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cim) (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) $;

--| The exponential function of an imaginary number maps any interval of length ` 2 _pi ` one-to-one onto the unit circle. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem efif1olem4 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class z) (D: class) (S: class x3 w) (F: class z w):
  $ wceq F (cmpt w D (cfv (co ci (cv w) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (co c2 cpi cmul) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) cr)) (wrex y1 (wcel (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmin) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) cz) D) $ >
  $ wceq S (cres csin (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o D C F) $;

--| The exponential function of an imaginary number maps any open-below, closed-above interval of length ` 2 _pi ` one-to-one onto the unit circle. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem efif1o {w: setvar} (A2 C D: class) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (cmpt w D (cfv (co ci (cv w) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wceq D (co A2 (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) caddc) cioc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wf1o D C F) $;

--| The exponential function of an imaginary number maps the reals onto the unit circle. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem efifo {z: setvar} (C: class) (F: class z):
  $ wceq F (cmpt z cr (cfv (co ci (cv z) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wfo cr C F $;

--| The exponential function maps the set ` S ` , of complex numbers with imaginary part in a real interval of length ` 2 x. _pi ` , one-to-one onto the nonzero complex numbers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem eff1olem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class) (S: class z w) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (cmpt w D (cfv (co ci (cv w) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cim) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (co c2 cpi cmul) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) cr)) (wrex y1 (wcel (co (co (cv z) (cv y1) cmin) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) cz) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o S (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cres ce S)) $;

--| The exponential function maps the set ` S ` , of complex numbers with imaginary part in the closed-above, open-below interval from ` -u _pi ` to ` _pi ` one-to-one onto the nonzero complex numbers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 16-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem eff1o (S: class):
  $ wceq S (cima (ccnv cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)) $ >
  $ wf1o S (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cres ce S) $;

--| The image of a subgroup of the group ` + ` , under the exponential function of a scaled complex number, is an Abelian group. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem efabl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) (crn F) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv ccnfld csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cabl) $;

--| The image of a subgroup of the group ` + ` , under the exponential function of a scaled complex number is a submonoid of the multiplicative group of ` CCfld ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem efsubm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) (crn F) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv ccnfld csubg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) (cfv (cfv ccnfld cmgp) csubmnd)) $;

--| The circle group ` T ` is an Abelian group. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Mar-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem circgrp (C T: class):
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) C cress) $ >
  $ wcel T cabl $;

--| The circle group ` T ` is a submonoid of the multiplicative group of ` CCfld ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2020.)
theorem circsubm (C T: class):
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) C cress) $ >
  $ wcel C (cfv (cfv ccnfld cmgp) csubmnd) $;

--| Extend class notation with the natural logarithm function on complex numbers.
term clog: class;

--| Extend class notation with the complex power function.
term ccxp: class;

--| Define the natural logarithm function on complex numbers. It is defined as the principal value, that is, the inverse of the exponential whose imaginary part lies in the interval (-pi, pi]. See ~ http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_logarithm and ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_logarithm . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
axiom df_log:
  $ wceq clog (ccnv (cres ce (cima (ccnv cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)))) $;

--| Define the power function on complex numbers. Note that the value of this function when ` x = 0 ` and ` ( Re `` y ) <_ 0 , y =/= 0 ` should properly be undefined, but defining it by convention this way simplifies the domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
axiom df_cxp {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccxp (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cc0) c1 cc0) (cfv (co (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmul) ce))) $;

--| The range of the natural logarithm function, also the principal domain of the exponential function. This allows to write the longer class expression as simply ` ran log ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem logrn:
  $ wceq (crn clog) (cima (ccnv cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)) $;

--| Write out the property ` A e. ran log ` explicitly. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem ellogrn (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (crn clog)) (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cneg cpi) (cfv A2 cim) clt) (wbr (cfv A2 cim) cpi cle)) $;

--| The natural logarithm function in terms of the exponential function restricted to its principal domain. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem dflog2:
  $ wceq clog (ccnv (cres ce (crn clog))) $;

--| The range of the natural logarithm function includes the real numbers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem relogrn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel A2 (crn clog)) $;

--| The range of the natural logarithm function is a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem logrncn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (crn clog)) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| The exponential function restricted to its principal domain maps one-to-one onto the nonzero complex numbers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem eff1o2:
  $ wf1o (crn clog) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cres ce (crn clog)) $;

--| The natural logarithm function maps the nonzero complex numbers one-to-one onto its range. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem logf1o:
  $ wf1o (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (crn clog) clog $;

--| The natural logarithm function on the positive reals in terms of the real exponential function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem dfrelog:
  $ wceq (cres clog crp) (ccnv (cres ce cr)) $;

--| The natural logarithm function maps the positive reals one-to-one onto the real numbers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem relogf1o:
  $ wf1o crp cr (cres clog crp) $;

--| Closure of the natural logarithm function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem logrncl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (cfv A2 clog) (crn clog)) $;

--| Closure of the natural logarithm function. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem logcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (cfv A2 clog) cc) $;

--| Closure of the imaginary part of the logarithm function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem logimcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wbr (cneg cpi) (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cpi cle)) $;

--| The logarithm of a nonzero complex number is a complex number. Deduction form of ~ logcl . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem logcld (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X clog) cc) $;

--| The imaginary part of the logarithm is in ` ( -u _pi (,] _pi ) ` . Deduction form of ~ logimcl . Compare ~ logimclad . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem logimcld (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cneg cpi) (cfv (cfv X clog) cim) clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv X clog) cim) cpi cle)) $;

--| The imaginary part of the logarithm is in ` ( -u _pi (,] _pi ) ` . Alternate form of ~ logimcld . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem logimclad (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv X clog) cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)) $;

--| The imaginary part of the logarithm function has absolute value less than pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem abslogimle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cabs) cpi cle) $;

--| The range of the natural logarithm is closed under addition with reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem logrnaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (crn clog)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) (crn clog)) $;

--| Closure of the natural logarithm function on positive reals. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cfv A2 clog) cr) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem eflog (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clog) ce) A2) $;

--| If the logarithm of a number is 0, the number must be 1. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 22-Jul-2017.)
theorem logeq0im1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wceq (cfv A2 clog) cc0)) (wceq A2 c1) $;

--| The logarithm isn't 0 if its argument isn't 0 or 1. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Jul-2017.)
theorem logccne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wne (cfv A2 clog) cc0) $;

--| Logarithm of a non-1 positive real number is not zero and thus suitable as a divisor. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem logne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wne A2 c1)) (wne (cfv A2 clog) cc0) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem reeflog (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clog) ce) A2) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem logef (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (crn clog)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ce) clog) A2) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogef (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ce) clog) A2) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem logeftb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 (crn clog))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 clog) B2) (wceq (cfv B2 ce) A2)) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogeftb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 clog) B2) (wceq (cfv B2 ce) A2)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of ` 1 ` . One case of Property 1a of [Cohen] p. 301. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem log1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 clog) cc0 $;

--| The natural logarithm of ` _e ` . One case of Property 1b of [Cohen] p. 301. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem loge:
  $ wceq (cfv ceu clog) c1 $;

--| The natural logarithm of ` _i ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Apr-2020.)
theorem logi:
  $ wceq (cfv ci clog) (co ci (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) $;

--| The natural logarithm of a negative real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem logneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (co ci cpi cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of negative ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2014.)
theorem logm1:
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg c1) clog) (co ci cpi cmul) $;

--| If a number has imaginary part equal to ` _pi ` , then it is on the negative real axis and vice-versa. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem lognegb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wb (wcel (cneg A2) crp) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cpi)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ relogmul and ~ relogdiv . Remark of [Cohen] p. 301 ("The proof of Property 3 is quite similar to the proof given for Property 2"). (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogoprlem (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv A2 clog) cc) (wcel (cfv B2 clog) cc)) (wceq (cfv (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) F) ce) (co (cfv (cfv A2 clog) ce) (cfv (cfv B2 clog) ce) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cfv A2 clog) cr) (wcel (cfv B2 clog) cr)) (wcel (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 G) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) F)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of the product of two positive real numbers is the sum of natural logarithms. Property 2 of [Cohen] p. 301, restricted to natural logarithms. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) caddc)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of the quotient of two positive real numbers is the difference of natural logarithms. Exercise 72(a) and Property 3 of [Cohen] p. 301, restricted to natural logarithms. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogdiv (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) cmin)) $;

--| Exponentiation of a nonzero complex number to an integer power. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Apr-2008.)
theorem explog (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (cfv (co N (cfv A2 clog) cmul) ce)) $;

--| Exponentiation of a positive real number to an integer power. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem reexplog (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (cfv (co N (cfv A2 clog) cmul) ce)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of positive ` A ` raised to an integer power. Property 4 of [Cohen] p. 301-302, restricted to natural logarithms and integer powers ` N ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogexp (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) clog) (co N (cfv A2 clog) cmul)) $;

--| Real part of a logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem relog (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cre) (cfv (cfv A2 cabs) clog)) $;

--| The natural logarithm function on positive reals determines an isomorphism from the positive reals onto the reals. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem relogiso:
  $ wiso crp cr clt clt (cres clog crp) $;

--| The natural logarithm is a group isomorphism from the group of positive reals under multiplication to the group of reals under addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jun-2019.)
theorem reloggim (P: class):
  $ wceq P (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) crp cress) $ >
  $ wcel (cres clog crp) (co P crefld cgim) $;

--| The natural logarithm function on positive reals is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem logltb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) clt)) $;

--| The logarithm of a factorial can be expressed as a finite sum of logs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem logfac {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (cfv N cfa) clog) (csu k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) clog))) $;

--| Solve an equation involving an exponential. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem eflogeq {n: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ce) B2) (wrex n (wceq A2 (co (cfv B2 clog) (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) (cv n) cmul) caddc)) cz)) $;

--| Natural logarithm preserves ` <_ ` . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.)
theorem logleb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) cle)) $;

--| Closure of the logarithm function in the positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem rplogcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wcel (cfv A2 clog) crp) $;

--| The logarithm of a number greater than 1 is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem logge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 clog) cle) $;

--| The natural logarithm distributes under conjugation away from the branch cut. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (cfv A2 cim) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) clog) (cfv (cfv A2 clog) ccj)) $;

--| The exponential of the "arg" function ` Im o. log ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem efiarg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (cfv (co ci (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cmul) ce) (co A2 (cfv A2 cabs) cdiv)) $;

--| The cosine of the argument is the quotient of the real part and the absolute value. Compare to ~ efiarg . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem cosargd (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv X clog) cim) ccos) (co (cfv X cre) (cfv X cabs) cdiv)) $;

--| The cosine of the argument is zero precisely on the imaginary axis. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem cosarg0d (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv X clog) cim) ccos) cc0) (wceq (cfv X cre) cc0)) $;

--| Closure of the argument of a complex number with positive real part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem argregt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cre) clt)) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) $;

--| Closure of the argument of a complex number with nonnegative real part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem argrege0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cre) cle)) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) $;

--| Closure of the argument of a complex number with positive imaginary part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem argimgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cim) clt)) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) (co cc0 cpi cioo)) $;

--| Closure of the argument of a complex number with negative imaginary part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem argimlt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cim) cc0 clt)) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) (co (cneg cpi) cc0 cioo)) $;

--| Multiplying a number by ` _i ` increases the logarithm of the number by ` _i _pi / 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Apr-2015.)
theorem logimul (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cre) cle)) (wceq (cfv (co ci A2 cmul) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (co ci (co cpi c2 cdiv) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The logarithm of the negative of a number with positive imaginary part is ` _i x. _pi ` less than the original. (Compare ~ logneg .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem logneg2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cim) clt)) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (co ci cpi cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Generalization of ~ relogmul to a complex left argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem logmul2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) caddc)) $;

--| Generalization of ~ relogdiv to a complex left argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem logdiv2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) cmin)) $;

--| Bound on the magnitude of the complex logarithm function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem abslogle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cabs) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 cabs) clog) cabs) cpi caddc) cle) $;

--| The basic relation between the "arg" function ` Im o. log ` and the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem tanarg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (cfv A2 cre) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) ctan) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv A2 cre) cdiv)) $;

--| The ` log x / x ` function is strictly decreasing on the reals greater than ` _e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.)
theorem logdivlti (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr ceu A2 cle)) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wbr (co (cfv B2 clog) B2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 clog) A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| The ` log x / x ` function is strictly decreasing on the reals greater than ` _e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.)
theorem logdivlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr ceu A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr ceu B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co (cfv B2 clog) B2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 clog) A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The ` log x / x ` function is decreasing on the reals greater than ` _e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2016.)
theorem logdivle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr ceu A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr ceu B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co (cfv B2 clog) B2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 clog) A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Closure of the natural logarithm function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem relogcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 clog) cr) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem reeflogd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clog) ce) A2) $;

--| The natural logarithm of the product of two positive real numbers is the sum of natural logarithms. Property 2 of [Cohen] p. 301, restricted to natural logarithms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem relogmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) caddc)) $;

--| The natural logarithm of the quotient of two positive real numbers is the difference of natural logarithms. Exercise 72(a) and Property 3 of [Cohen] p. 301, restricted to natural logarithms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem relogdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) cmin)) $;

--| Natural logarithm preserves ` <_ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem logled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cfv A2 clog) (cfv B2 clog) cle)) $;

--| Relationship between the natural logarithm function and the exponential function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem relogefd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ce) clog) A2) $;

--| Closure of the logarithm function in the positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem rplogcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 clog) crp) $;

--| The logarithm of a number greater than 1 is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem logge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 clog) cle) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is nonnegative iff the number is greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem logge0b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wb (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 clog) cle) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is positive iff the number is greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem loggt0b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wb (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 clog) clt) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is less than or equal to 1 iff the number is less than or equal to Euler's constant. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem logle1b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 clog) c1 cle) (wbr A2 ceu cle)) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is less than 1 iff the number is less than Euler's constant. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem loglt1b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 clog) c1 clt) (wbr A2 ceu clt)) $;

--| The inverse logarithm function converges to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem divlogrlim {x3: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt x3 (co c1 cpnf cioo) (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) clog) cdiv)) cc0 crli $;

--| The logarithm function is not eventually bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2016.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem logno1 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wcel (cmpt x3 crp (cfv (cv x3) clog)) co1) $;

--| The derivative of the real logarithm function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvrelog {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cr (cres clog crp) cdv) (cmpt x3 crp (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv)) $;

--| The real logarithm function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem relogcn:
  $ wcel (cres clog crp) (co crp cr ccncf) $;

--| Elementhood in the "continuous domain" of the complex logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2015.)
theorem ellogdm (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 D) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel A2 crp))) $;

--| A number in the continuous domain of ` log ` is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2015.)
theorem logdmn0 (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 D) (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| A number in the continuous domain of ` log ` is not a strictly negative number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2015.)
theorem logdmnrp (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 D) (wn (wcel (cneg A2) crp)) $;

--| The continuity domain of ` log ` is a subset of the regular domain of ` log ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem logdmss (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wss D (cdif cc (csn cc0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ logcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcnlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 D R S T: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cif (wcel A2 crp) A2 (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv A2 cabs) (co R (co c1 R caddc) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cif (wbr S T cle) S T) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ logcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcnlem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R S T: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cif (wcel A2 crp) A2 (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv A2 cabs) (co R (co c1 R caddc) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cif (wbr S T cle) S T) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cneg cpi) (co (cfv (cfv B2 clog) cim) (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cmin) clt) (wbr (co (cfv (cfv B2 clog) cim) (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cmin) cpi cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ logcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcnlem4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R S T: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cif (wcel A2 crp) A2 (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv A2 cabs) (co R (co c1 R caddc) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cif (wbr S T cle) S T) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) (cfv (cfv B2 clog) cim) cmin) cabs) R clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ logcn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcnlem5 {x3: setvar} (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cfv (cv x3) clog) cim)) (co D cr ccncf) $;

--| The logarithm function is continuous away from the branch cut at negative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem logcn (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wcel (cres clog D) (co D cc ccncf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvlog . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvloglem (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wcel (cima clog D) (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $;

--| The "continuous domain" of ` log ` is an open set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem logdmopn (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wcel D (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $;

--| The logarithm maps its continuous domain bijectively onto the set of numbers with imaginary part ` -u _pi < Im ( z ) < _pi ` . The negative reals are mapped to the numbers with imaginary part equal to ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2015.)
theorem logf1o2 (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wf1o D (cima (ccnv cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo)) (cres clog D) $;

--| The derivative of the complex logarithm function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvlog {x3: setvar} (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq (co cc (cres clog D) cdv) (cmpt x3 D (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dvlog2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvlog2lem (S: class):
  $ wceq S (co c1 c1 (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wss S (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $;

--| The derivative of the complex logarithm function on the open unit ball centered at ` 1 ` , a sometimes easier region to work with than the ` CC \ ( -oo , 0 ] ` of ~ dvlog . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem dvlog2 {x3: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wceq S (co c1 c1 (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wceq (co cc (cres clog S) cdv) (cmpt x3 S (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv)) $;

--| The antiderivative of the logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem advlog {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 crp (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv x3) clog) c1 cmin) cmul)) cdv) (cmpt x3 crp (cfv (cv x3) clog)) $;

--| The antiderivative of a power of the logarithm. (Set ` A = 1 ` and multiply by ` ( -u 1 ) ^ N x. N ! ` to get the antiderivative of ` log ( x ) ^ N ` itself.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-May-2016.)
theorem advlogexp {x3 k: setvar} (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 crp (co (cv x3) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cdiv) clog) (cv k) cexp) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv)) cmul)) cdv) (cmpt x3 crp (co (co (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cdiv) clog) N cexp) (cfv N cfa) cdiv))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem efopnlem1 (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R crp) (wbr R cpi clt)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)))) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs) cpi clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ efopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2015.)
theorem efopnlem2 (R J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crp) (wbr R cpi clt)) (wcel (cima ce (co cc0 R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl))) J) $;

--| The exponential map is an open map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem efopn (S J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S J) (wcel (cima ce S) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ logtayl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem logtayllem {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wcel (cseq caddc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) cc0 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv)) (co A2 (cv n) cexp) cmul)) cc0) (cdm cli)) $;

--| The Taylor series for ` -u log ( 1 - A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem logtayl {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wbr (cseq caddc (cmpt k cn (co (co A2 (cv k) cexp) (cv k) cdiv)) c1) (cneg (cfv (co c1 A2 cmin) clog)) cli) $;

--| The Taylor series for ` -u log ( 1 - A ) ` , as an infinite sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem logtaylsum {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wceq (csu k cn (co (co A2 (cv k) cexp) (cv k) cdiv)) (cneg (cfv (co c1 A2 cmin) clog))) $;

--| Power series expression for the logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem logtayl2 {k: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class k):
  $ wceq S (co c1 c1 (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) (wbr (cseq caddc (cmpt k cn (co (co (co (cneg c1) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) (cv k) cdiv) (co (co A2 c1 cmin) (cv k) cexp) cmul)) c1) (cfv A2 clog) cli) $;

--| The natural logarithm function on the reals is a strictly concave function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem logccv (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cioo))) (wbr (co (co T (cfv A2 clog) cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) (cfv B2 clog) cmul) caddc) (cfv (co (co T A2 cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) B2 cmul) caddc) clog) clt) $;

--| Value of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpval (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (cif (wceq A2 cc0) (cif (wceq B2 cc0) c1 cc0) (cfv (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul) ce))) $;

--| Value of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpef (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (cfv (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul) ce)) $;

--| Value of the complex power function when the first argument is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem _0cxp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co cc0 A2 ccxp) cc0) $;

--| Relate the complex power function to the integer power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpexpz (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 cz)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 B2 cexp)) $;

--| Relate the complex power function to the integer power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpexp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 B2 cexp)) $;

--| Logarithm of a complex power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem logcxp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) clog) (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul)) $;

--| Value of the complex power function when the second argument is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxp0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 cc0 ccxp) c1) $;

--| Value of the complex power function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c1 ccxp) A2) $;

--| Value of the complex power function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem _1cxp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co c1 A2 ccxp) c1) $;

--| Write the exponential function as an exponent to the power ` _e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem ecxp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co ceu A2 ccxp) (cfv A2 ce)) $;

--| Closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) cc) $;

--| Real closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem recxpcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) cr) $;

--| Positive real closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem rpcxpcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) crp) $;

--| Complex exponentiation is nonzero if its base is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpne0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 cc)) (wne (co A2 B2 ccxp) cc0) $;

--| Complex exponentiation is zero iff the base is zero and the exponent is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem cxpeq0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) cc0) (wa (wceq A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0))) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(a) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpadd (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cmul)) $;

--| Value of a nonzero complex number raised to a complex power plus one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpp1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpneg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg B2) ccxp) (co c1 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Exponent subtraction law for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxpsub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmin) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Nonnegative exponentiation with a real exponent is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpge0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wcel B2 cr)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mulcxp . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem mulcxplem (ph: wff) (A2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co cc0 C ccxp) cmul)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a product. Proposition 10-4.2(c) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem mulcxp (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cmul)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxprec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2 ccxp) (co c1 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2014.)
theorem divcxp (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 crp) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(b) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpmul (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C ccxp)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Variation on ~ cxpmul with more general conditions on ` A ` and ` B ` when ` C ` is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpmul2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C cexp)) $;

--| The complex power function allows to write n-th roots via the idiom ` A ^c ( 1 / N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem cxproot (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co (co A2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) N cexp) A2) $;

--| Generalize ~ cxpmul2 to negative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem cxpmul2z (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cz))) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C cexp)) $;

--| Absolute value of a power, when the base is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem abscxp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) cabs) (co A2 (cfv B2 cre) ccxp)) $;

--| Absolute value of a power, when the exponent is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem abscxp2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) B2 ccxp)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxplt (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr))) (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxple (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr))) (wb (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxplea (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cle) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxple2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wcel C crp)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxplt2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wcel C crp)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxple2a (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 C cle)) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cle) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem cxplt3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wbr A2 c1 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr))) (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co A2 B2 ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem cxple3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wbr A2 c1 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr))) (wb (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co A2 B2 ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cxpsqrt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpsqrtlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wceq (co A2 (co c1 c2 cdiv) ccxp) (cneg (cfv A2 csqrt)))) (wcel (co ci (cfv A2 csqrt) cmul) cr) $;

--| The complex exponential function with exponent ` 1 / 2 ` exactly matches the complex square root function (the branch cut is in the same place for both functions), and thus serves as a suitable generalization to other ` n ` -th roots and irrational roots. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpsqrt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 (co c1 c2 cdiv) ccxp) (cfv A2 csqrt)) $;

--| Logarithm of a square root. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2016.)
theorem logsqrt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 csqrt) clog) (co (cfv A2 clog) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| Value of the complex power function when the second argument is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxp0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 cc0 ccxp) c1) $;

--| Value of the complex power function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxp1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c1 ccxp) A2) $;

--| Value of the complex power function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem _1cxpd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 A2 ccxp) c1) $;

--| Closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) cc) $;

--| Product of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Variation on ~ cxpmul with more general conditions on ` A ` and ` B ` when ` C ` is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpmul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C cexp)) $;

--| Value of the complex power function when the first argument is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem _0cxpd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 A2 ccxp) cc0) $;

--| Relate the complex power function to the integer power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpexpzd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 B2 cexp)) $;

--| Value of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpefd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 ccxp) (cfv (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul) ce)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation is nonzero if its base is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpne0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co A2 B2 ccxp) cc0) $;

--| Value of a nonzero complex number raised to a complex power plus one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpp1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpnegd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (cneg B2) ccxp) (co c1 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Generalize ~ cxpmul2 to negative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpmul2zd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C cexp)) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(a) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cmul)) $;

--| Exponent subtraction law for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpsubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmin) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpled (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxplead (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 ccxp) (co A2 C ccxp) cle) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a quotient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem divcxpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Positive real closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem recxpcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) cr) $;

--| Nonnegative exponentiation with a real exponent is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpge0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cle) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxple2ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cle) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxplt2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxple2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a product. Proposition 10-4.2(c) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem mulcxpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) cmul)) $;

--| Complex exponentiation on positive real numbers is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Apr-2025.)
theorem recxpf1lem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp))) $;

--| Square root theorem over the complex numbers for the complex power function. Theorem I.35 of [Apostol] p. 29. Compare with ~ sqrtth . (Contributed by AV, 23-Dec-2022.)
theorem cxpsqrtth (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) c2 ccxp) A2) $;

--| There exist irrational numbers ` a ` and ` b ` such that ` ( a ^c b ) ` is rational. Statement in the Metamath book, section 1.1.5, footnote 27 on page 17, and the "classical proof" for theorem 1.2 of [Bauer], p. 483. This proof is not acceptable in intuitionistic logic, since it is based on the law of excluded middle: Either ` ( ( sqrt `` 2 ) ^c ( sqrt `` 2 ) ) ` is rational, in which case ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` , being irrational (see ~ sqrt2irr ), can be chosen for both ` a ` and ` b ` , or ` ( ( sqrt `` 2 ) ^c ( sqrt `` 2 ) ) ` is irrational, in which case ` ( ( sqrt `` 2 ) ^c ( sqrt `` 2 ) ) ` can be chosen for ` a ` and ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` for ` b ` , since ` ( ( ( sqrt `` 2 ) ^c ( sqrt `` 2 ) ) ^c ( sqrt `` 2 ) ) = 2 ` is rational. For an alternate proof, which can be used in intuitionistic logic, see ~ 2irrexpqALT . (Contributed by AV, 23-Dec-2022.)
theorem _2irrexpq {a b: setvar}:
  $ wrex a (wrex b (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) ccxp) cq) (cdif cr cq)) (cdif cr cq) $;

--| Complex exponentiation of a reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxprecd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2 ccxp) (co c1 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cdiv)) $;

--| Positive real closure of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem rpcxpcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 ccxp) crp) $;

--| Logarithm of a complex power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem logcxpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) clog) (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxplt3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co A2 B2 ccxp) clt)) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxple3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr (co A2 C ccxp) (co A2 B2 ccxp) cle)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for complex exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(b) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem cxpmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C ccxp)) $;

--| A positive real raised to a real power is positive. (Contributed by SN, 6-Apr-2023.)
theorem cxpgt0d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 N ccxp) clt) $;

--| Commutative law for real exponentiation. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2022.)
theorem cxpcom (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 ccxp) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) B2 ccxp)) $;

--| The derivative of a complex power with respect to the first argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcxp1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 crp (co (cv x3) A2 ccxp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 crp (co A2 (co (cv x3) (co A2 c1 cmin) ccxp) cmul))) $;

--| The derivative of a complex power with respect to the second argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Feb-2015.)
theorem dvcxp2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) ccxp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (co (cfv A2 clog) (co A2 (cv x3) ccxp) cmul))) $;

--| The derivative of the real square root function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2016.)
theorem dvsqrt {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 crp (cfv (cv x3) csqrt)) cdv) (cmpt x3 crp (co c1 (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Derivative of complex power with respect to first argument on the complex plane. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 18-Dec-2018.)
theorem dvcncxp1 {x3: setvar} (A2 D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 D (co (cv x3) A2 ccxp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 D (co A2 (co (cv x3) (co A2 c1 cmin) ccxp) cmul))) $;

--| Derivative of square root function. (Contributed by Brendan Leahy, 18-Dec-2018.)
theorem dvcnsqrt {x3: setvar} (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cv x3) csqrt)) cdv) (cmpt x3 D (co c1 (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Domain of continuity of the complex power function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2016.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem cxpcn {x3 y1: setvar} (D J K: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J D crest) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 D cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) ccxp)) (co (co K J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Continuity of the complex power function, when the base is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2016.)
theorem cxpcn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (J: class) (K: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J crp crest) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 crp cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) ccxp)) (co (co K J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cxpcn3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem cxpcn3lem {a b d: setvar} (A2 D T: class) (U: class a b d) (E: class) (J: class a b) (K L: class):
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cre) crp) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J (co cc0 cpnf cico) crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (co J D crest) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cif (wbr (cfv A2 cre) c1 cle) (cfv A2 cre) c1) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq T (cif (wbr U (co E (co c1 U cdiv) ccxp) cle) U (co E (co c1 U cdiv) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 D) (wcel E crp)) (wrex d (wral a (wral b (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (cv a) cabs) (cv d) clt) (wbr (cfv (co A2 (cv b) cmin) cabs) (cv d) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv a) (cv b) ccxp) cabs) E clt)) D) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) crp) $;

--| Extend continuity of the complex power function to a base of zero, as long as the exponent has strictly positive real part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem cxpcn3 {x3 y1: setvar} (D J: class) (K L: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cre) crp) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J (co cc0 cpnf cico) crest) $ >
  $ wceq L (co J D crest) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 (co cc0 cpnf cico) D (co (cv x3) (cv y1) ccxp)) (co (co K L ctx) J ccn) $;

--| Continuity of the real square root function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem resqrtcn:
  $ wcel (cres csqrt (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (co (co cc0 cpnf cico) cr ccncf) $;

--| Continuity of the square root function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtcn (D: class):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wcel (cres csqrt D) (co D cc ccncf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cxpaddle . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem cxpaddlelem (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 ccxp) cle) $;

--| Ordering property for complex exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxpaddle (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C ccxp) (co (co A2 C ccxp) (co B2 C ccxp) caddc) cle) $;

--| Bound on the absolute value of a complex power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem abscxpbnd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 cre) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) M cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) cabs) (co (co M (cfv B2 cre) ccxp) (cfv (co (cfv B2 cabs) cpi cmul) ce) cmul) cle) $;

--| Property of an ` N ` -th root of unity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem root1id (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 N cdiv) ccxp) N cexp) c1) $;

--| The only powers of an ` N ` -th root of unity that equal ` 1 ` are the multiples of ` N ` . In other words, ` -u 1 ^c ( 2 / N ) ` has order ` N ` in the multiplicative group of nonzero complex numbers. (In fact, these and their powers are the only elements of finite order in the complex numbers.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem root1eq1 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wceq (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 N cdiv) ccxp) K cexp) c1) (wbr N K cdvds)) $;

--| Within the ` N ` -th roots of unity, the conjugate of the ` K ` -th root is the ` N - K ` -th root. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem root1cj (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (cfv (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 N cdiv) ccxp) K cexp) ccj) (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 N cdiv) ccxp) (co N K cmin) cexp)) $;

--| Solve an equation involving an ` N ` -th power. The expression ` -u 1 ^c ( 2 / N ) = exp ( 2 _pi _i / N ) ` is a way to write the primitive ` N ` -th root of unity with the smallest positive argument. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem cxpeq {n: setvar} (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 N cexp) B2) (wrex n (wceq A2 (co (co B2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 N cdiv) ccxp) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| If the ` N ` -th root of an integer ` A ` is rational, that root must be an integer. Generalization of ~ zsqrtelqelz to positive integer roots. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 6-Apr-2023.)
theorem zrtelqelz (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn) (wcel (co A2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) cq)) (wcel (co A2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) cz) $;

--| A positive integer root divides its integer. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 6-Apr-2023.)
theorem zrtdvds (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn) (wcel (co A2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) cn)) (wbr (co A2 (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) A2 cdvds) $;

--| The root of a prime number is irrational. (Contributed by Steven Nguyen, 6-Apr-2023.)
theorem rtprmirr (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz))) (wcel (co P (co c1 N cdiv) ccxp) (cdif cr cq)) $;

--| An upper bound on the logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Aug-2021.)
theorem loglesqrt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clog) (cfv A2 csqrt) cle) $;

--| Symmetry of the natural logarithm range by negation. Lemma for ~ logrec . (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem logreclem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (crn clog)) (wn (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cpi))) (wcel (cneg A2) (crn clog)) $;

--| Logarithm of a reciprocal changes sign. (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem logrec (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne (cfv (cfv A2 clog) cim) cpi)) (wceq (cfv A2 clog) (cneg (cfv (co c1 A2 cdiv) clog))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the logarithm generalized to an arbitrary base.
term clogb: class;

--| Define the ` logb ` operator. This is the logarithm generalized to an arbitrary base. It can be used as ` ( B logb X ) ` for "log base B of X". In the most common traditional notation, base B is a subscript of "log". The definition is according to Wikipedia "Complex logarithm": ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_logarithm#Logarithms_to_other_bases (10-Jun-2020). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 21-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_logb {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clogb (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co (cfv (cv y1) clog) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cdiv)) $;

--| Define the value of the ` logb ` function, the logarithm generalized to an arbitrary base, when used as infix. Most Metamath statements select variables in order of their use, but to make the order clearer we use "B" for base and "X" for the argument of the logarithm function here. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 21-Jan-2017.) (Revised by David A. Wheeler, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem logbval (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wcel X (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wceq (co B2 X clogb) (co (cfv X clog) (cfv B2 clog) cdiv)) $;

--| General logarithm closure. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Jul-2017.)
theorem logbcl (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wcel X (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wcel (co B2 X clogb) cc) $;

--| General logarithm is 1 when base and arg match. Property 1(a) of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by David A. Wheeler, 22-Jul-2017.)
theorem logbid1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wceq (co A2 A2 clogb) c1) $;

--| The logarithm of ` 1 ` to an arbitrary base ` B ` is 0. Property 1(b) of [Cohen4] p. 361. See ~ log1 . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem logb1 (B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0) (wne B2 c1)) (wceq (co B2 c1 clogb) cc0) $;

--| The general logarithm of a number to the base being Euler's constant is the natural logarithm of the number. Put another way, using ` _e ` as the base in ` logb ` is the same as ` log ` . Definition in [Cohen4] p. 352. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 17-Oct-2017.) (Revised by David A. Wheeler and AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem elogb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0))) (wceq (co ceu A2 clogb) (cfv A2 clog)) $;

--| Change of base for logarithms. Property in [Cohen4] p. 367. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem logbchbase (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (w3a (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0) (wne B2 c1)) (wcel X (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wceq (co A2 X clogb) (co (co B2 X clogb) (co B2 A2 clogb) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of the general logarithm with integer base. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem relogbval (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp)) (wceq (co B2 X clogb) (co (cfv X clog) (cfv B2 clog) cdiv)) $;

--| Closure of the general logarithm with a positive real base on positive reals. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem relogbcl (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 crp) (wcel X crp) (wne B2 c1)) (wcel (co B2 X clogb) cr) $;

--| Closure of the general logarithm with integer base on positive reals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbzcl (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp)) (wcel (co B2 X clogb) cr) $;

--| Power law for the general logarithm for real powers: The logarithm of a positive real number to the power of a real number is equal to the product of the exponent and the logarithm of the base of the power. Property 4 of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbreexp (B2 C E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wcel C crp) (wcel E cr)) (wceq (co B2 (co C E ccxp) clogb) (co E (co B2 C clogb) cmul)) $;

--| Power law for the general logarithm for integer powers: The logarithm of a positive real number to the power of an integer is equal to the product of the exponent and the logarithm of the base of the power. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbzexp (B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wcel C crp) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co B2 (co C N cexp) clogb) (co N (co B2 C clogb) cmul)) $;

--| The logarithm of the product of two positive real numbers is the sum of logarithms. Property 2 of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem relogbmul (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel C crp))) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 C cmul) clogb) (co (co B2 A2 clogb) (co B2 C clogb) caddc)) $;

--| The logarithm of the product of a positive real and a positive real number to the power of a real number is the sum of the logarithm of the first real number and the scaled logarithm of the second real number. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem relogbmulexp (A2 B2 C E: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel C crp) (wcel E cr))) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 (co C E ccxp) cmul) clogb) (co (co B2 A2 clogb) (co E (co B2 C clogb) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The logarithm of the quotient of two positive real numbers is the difference of logarithms. Property 3 of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem relogbdiv (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel C crp))) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 C cdiv) clogb) (co (co B2 A2 clogb) (co B2 C clogb) cmin)) $;

--| Identity law for general logarithm: the logarithm of a power to the base is the exponent. Property 6 of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbexp (B2 M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 crp) (wne B2 c1) (wcel M cz)) (wceq (co B2 (co B2 M cexp) clogb) M) $;

--| Identity law for general logarithm with integer base. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem nnlogbexp (B2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel M cz)) (wceq (co B2 (co B2 M cexp) clogb) M) $;

--| Logarithm of a reciprocal changes sign. See ~ logrec . Particular case of Property 3 of [Cohen4] p. 361. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem logbrec (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 crp)) (wceq (co B2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clogb) (cneg (co B2 A2 clogb))) $;

--| The general logarithm function is monotone/increasing. See ~ logleb . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Oct-2014.) (Revised by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem logbleb (B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp) (wcel Y crp)) (wb (wbr X Y cle) (wbr (co B2 X clogb) (co B2 Y clogb) cle)) $;

--| The general logarithm function is strictly monotone/increasing. Property 2 of [Cohen4] p. 377. See ~ logltb . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 19-Oct-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem logblt (B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp) (wcel Y crp)) (wb (wbr X Y clt) (wbr (co B2 X clogb) (co B2 Y clogb) clt)) $;

--| Identity law for the general logarithm for real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2020.)
theorem relogbcxp (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif crp (csn c1))) (wcel X cr)) (wceq (co B2 (co B2 X ccxp) clogb) X) $;

--| Identity law for the general logarithm. (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2020.)
theorem cxplogb (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) (wcel X (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wceq (co B2 (co B2 X clogb) ccxp) X) $;

--| The logarithm is the inverse of the exponentiation. Observation in [Cohen4] p. 348. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbcxpb (B2 X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel B2 crp) (wne B2 c1)) (wcel X crp) (wcel Y cr)) (wb (wceq (co B2 X clogb) Y) (wceq (co B2 Y ccxp) X)) $;

--| The general logarithm to a fixed base regarded as mapping. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem logbmpt {y1: setvar} (B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0) (wne B2 c1)) (wceq (cfv B2 (ccur clogb)) (cmpt y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (co (cfv (cv y1) clog) (cfv B2 clog) cdiv))) $;

--| The general logarithm to a fixed base regarded as function. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem logbf (B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0) (wne B2 c1)) (wf (cdif cc (csn cc0)) cc (cfv B2 (ccur clogb))) $;

--| The general logarithm of a complex number to a fixed base. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem logbfval (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0) (wne B2 c1)) (wcel X (cdif cc (csn cc0)))) (wceq (cfv X (cfv B2 (ccur clogb))) (co B2 X clogb)) $;

--| The general logarithm to a real base greater than 1 regarded as function restricted to the positive integers. Property in [Cohen4] p. 349. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem relogbf (B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 crp) (wbr c1 B2 clt)) (wf crp cr (cres (cfv B2 (ccur clogb)) crp)) $;

--| The general logarithm to the base being Euler's constant regarded as function is the natural logarithm. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem logblog:
  $ wceq (cfv ceu (ccur clogb)) clog $;

--| The logarithm of a positive real number to a real base greater than 1 is positive iff the number is greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2022.)
theorem logbgt0b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel B2 crp) (wbr c1 B2 clt))) (wb (wbr cc0 (co B2 A2 clogb) clt) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) $;

--| The logarithm of an integer greater than 1 to an integer base greater than 1 is an irrational number if the argument and the base are relatively prime. For example, ` ( 2 logb 9 ) e. ( RR \ QQ ) ` (see ~ 2logb9irr ). (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2022.)
theorem logbgcd1irr (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (co X B2 cgcd) c1)) (wcel (co B2 X clogb) (cdif cr cq)) $;

--| Example for ~ logbgcd1irr . The logarithm of nine to base two is irrational. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2022.)
theorem _2logb9irr:
  $ wcel (co c2 c9 clogb) (cdif cr cq) $;

--| The logarithm of a prime to a different prime base is an irrational number. For example, ` ( 2 logb 3 ) e. ( RR \ QQ ) ` (see ~ 2logb3irr ). (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2022.)
theorem logbprmirr (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X cprime) (wcel B2 cprime) (wne X B2)) (wcel (co B2 X clogb) (cdif cr cq)) $;

--| Example for ~ logbprmirr . The logarithm of three to base two is irrational. (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2022.)
theorem _2logb3irr:
  $ wcel (co c2 c3 clogb) (cdif cr cq) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 2logb9irr : The logarithm of nine to base two is irrational. (Contributed by AV, 31-Dec-2022.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2logb9irrALT:
  $ wcel (co c2 c9 clogb) (cdif cr cq) $;

--| The square root of two to the power of the logarithm of nine to base two is three. ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` and ` ( 2 logb 9 ) ` are irrational numbers (see ~ sqrt2irr0 resp. ~ 2logb9irr ), satisfying the statement in ~ 2irrexpqALT . (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2022.)
theorem sqrt2cxp2logb9e3:
  $ wceq (co (cfv c2 csqrt) (co c2 c9 clogb) ccxp) c3 $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 2irrexpq : There exist irrational numbers ` a ` and ` b ` such that ` ( a ^ b ) ` is rational. Statement in the Metamath book, section 1.1.5, footnote 27 on page 17, and the "constructive proof" for theorem 1.2 of [Bauer], p. 483. In contrast to ~ 2irrexpq , this is a constructive proof because it is based on two explicitly named irrational numbers ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` and ` ( 2 logb 9 ) ` , see ~ sqrt2irr0 , ~ 2logb9irr and ~ sqrt2cxp2logb9e3 . Therefore, this proof is also acceptable/usable in intuitionistic logic. (Contributed by AV, 23-Dec-2022.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _2irrexpqALT {a b: setvar}:
  $ wrex a (wrex b (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) ccxp) cq) (cdif cr cq)) (cdif cr cq) $;

--| Define the angle function, which takes two complex numbers, treated as vectors from the origin, and returns the angle between them, in the range ` ( - _pi , _pi ] ` . To convert from the geometry notation, ` m A B C ` , the measure of the angle with legs ` A B ` , ` C B ` where ` C ` is more counterclockwise for positive angles, is represented by ` ( ( C - B ) F ( A - B ) ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem angval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) (cfv (cfv (co B2 A2 cdiv) clog) cim)) $;

--| Cancel a constant multiplier in the angle function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem angcan {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wne C cc0))) (wceq (co (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Cancel a negative sign in the angle function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem angneg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co (cneg A2) (cneg B2) F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| The (signed) angle between two vectors is the argument of their quotient. Deduction form of ~ angval . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angvald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y F) (cfv (cfv (co Y X cdiv) clog) cim)) $;

--| The (signed) angle between two vectors is in ` ( -u _pi (,] _pi ) ` . Deduction form. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angcld {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y F) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc)) $;

--| Two vectors are at a right angle iff their quotient is purely imaginary. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angrteqvd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (co X Y F) (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)))) (wceq (cfv (co Y X cdiv) cre) cc0)) $;

--| The cosine of the angle between ` X ` and ` -u Y ` is the negative of that between ` X ` and ` Y ` . If A, B and C are collinear points, this implies that the cosines of DBA and DBC sum to zero, i.e., that DBA and DBC are supplementary. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem cosangneg2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X (cneg Y) F) ccos) (cneg (cfv (co X Y F) ccos))) $;

--| Perpendicularity of two vectors does not change under rescaling the second. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angrtmuld {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F: class x3 y1) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Z cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co Z Y cdiv) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (co X Y F) (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)))) (wcel (co X Z F) (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ang180 . Show that the "revolution number" ` N ` is an integer, using ~ efeq1 to show that since the product of the three arguments ` A , 1 / ( 1 - A ) , ( A - 1 ) / A ` is ` -u 1 ` , the sum of the logarithms must be an integer multiple of ` 2 _pi _i ` away from ` _pi _i = log ( -u 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180lem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (T F N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (cfv (co c1 (co c1 A2 cmin) cdiv) clog) (cfv (co (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 cdiv) clog) caddc) (cfv A2 clog) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co (co T ci cdiv) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel (co T ci cdiv) cr)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ang180 . Show that the revolution number ` N ` is strictly between ` -u 2 ` and ` 1 ` . Both bounds are established by iterating using the bounds on the imaginary part of the logarithm, ~ logimcl , but the resulting bound gives only ` N <_ 1 ` for the upper bound. The case ` N = 1 ` is not ruled out here, but it is in some sense an "edge case" that can only happen under very specific conditions; in particular we show that all the angle arguments ` A , 1 / ( 1 - A ) , ( A - 1 ) / A ` must lie on the negative real axis, which is a contradiction because clearly if ` A ` is negative then the other two are positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (T F N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (cfv (co c1 (co c1 A2 cmin) cdiv) clog) (cfv (co (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 cdiv) clog) caddc) (cfv A2 clog) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co (co T ci cdiv) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wa (wbr (cneg c2) N clt) (wbr N c1 clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ang180 . Since ~ ang180lem1 shows that ` N ` is an integer and ~ ang180lem2 shows that ` N ` is strictly between ` -u 2 ` and ` 1 ` , it follows that ` N e. { -u 1 , 0 } ` , and these two cases correspond to the two possible values for ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180lem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (T F N: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (cfv (co c1 (co c1 A2 cmin) cdiv) clog) (cfv (co (co A2 c1 cmin) A2 cdiv) clog) caddc) (cfv A2 clog) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co (co T ci cdiv) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wcel T (cpr (cneg (co ci cpi cmul)) (co ci cpi cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ang180 . Reduce the statement to one variable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180lem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wne A2 c1)) (wcel (co (co (co (co c1 A2 cmin) c1 F) (co A2 (co A2 c1 cmin) F) caddc) (co c1 A2 F) caddc) (cpr (cneg cpi) cpi)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ang180 : Reduce the statement to two variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180lem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wne A2 B2)) (wcel (co (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) A2 F) (co B2 (co B2 A2 cmin) F) caddc) (co A2 B2 F) caddc) (cpr (cneg cpi) cpi)) $;

--| The sum of angles ` m A B C + m B C A + m C A B ` in a triangle adds up to either ` _pi ` or ` -u _pi ` , i.e. 180 degrees. (The sign is due to the two possible orientations of vertex arrangement and our signed notion of angle). This is Metamath 100 proof #27. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Sep-2014.)
theorem ang180 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne A2 C))) (wcel (co (co (co (co C B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmin) F) (co (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 C cmin) F) caddc) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) F) caddc) (cpr (cneg cpi) cpi)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lawcos . Here we prove the law for a point at the origin and two distinct points U and V, using an expanded version of the signed angle expression on the complex plane. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 11-Jun-2015.)
theorem lawcoslem1 (ph: wff) (U V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne U cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne V cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co U V cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (co (cfv U cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv V cabs) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (co (co (cfv U cabs) (cfv V cabs) cmul) (co (cfv (co U V cdiv) cre) (cfv (co U V cdiv) cabs) cdiv) cmul) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Law of cosines (also known as the Al-Kashi theorem or the generalized Pythagorean theorem, or the cosine formula or cosine rule). Given three distinct points A, B, and C, prove a relationship between their segment lengths. This theorem is expressed using the complex number plane as a plane, where ` F ` is the signed angle construct (as used in ~ ang180 ), ` X ` is the distance of line segment BC, ` Y ` is the distance of line segment AC, ` Z ` is the distance of line segment AB, and ` O ` is the signed angle m/__ BCA on the complex plane. We translate triangle ABC to move C to the origin (C-C), B to U=(B-C), and A to V=(A-C), then use lemma ~ lawcoslem1 to prove this algebraically simpler case. The Metamath convention is to use a signed angle; in this case the sign doesn't matter because we use the cosine of the angle (see ~ cosneg ). The Pythagorean theorem ~ pythag is a special case of the law of cosines. The theorem's expression and approach were suggested by Mario Carneiro. This is Metamath 100 proof #94. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 12-Jun-2015.)
theorem lawcos {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F O X Y Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq O (co (co B2 C cmin) (co A2 C cmin) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wa (wne A2 C) (wne B2 C))) (wceq (co Z c2 cexp) (co (co (co X c2 cexp) (co Y c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (co (co X Y cmul) (cfv O ccos) cmul) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Pythagorean theorem. Given three distinct points A, B, and C that form a right triangle (with the right angle at C), prove a relationship between their segment lengths. This theorem is expressed using the complex number plane as a plane, where ` F ` is the signed angle construct (as used in ~ ang180 ), ` X ` is the distance of line segment BC, ` Y ` is the distance of line segment AC, ` Z ` is the distance of line segment AB (the hypotenuse), and ` O ` is the signed right angle m/__ BCA. We use the law of cosines ~ lawcos to prove this, along with simple trigonometry facts like ~ coshalfpi and ~ cosneg . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Jun-2015.)
theorem pythag {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F O X Y Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq O (co (co B2 C cmin) (co A2 C cmin) F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wa (wne A2 C) (wne B2 C)) (wcel O (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv))))) (wceq (co Z c2 cexp) (co (co X c2 cexp) (co Y c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isosctr . (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 30-Dec-2016.)
theorem isosctrlem1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) c1) (wn (wceq c1 A2))) (wne (cfv (cfv (co c1 A2 cmin) clog) cim) cpi) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isosctr . Corresponds to the case where one vertex is at 0, another at 1 and the third lies on the unit circle. (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 31-Dec-2016.)
theorem isosctrlem2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) c1) (wn (wceq c1 A2))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co c1 A2 cmin) clog) cim) (cfv (cfv (co (cneg A2) (co c1 A2 cmin) cdiv) clog) cim)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isosctr . Corresponds to the case where one vertex is at 0. (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem isosctrlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (w3a (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0) (wne A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs))) (wceq (co (cneg A2) (co B2 A2 cmin) F) (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (cneg B2) F)) $;

--| Isosceles triangle theorem. This is Metamath 100 proof #65. (Contributed by Saveliy Skresanov, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem isosctr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (w3a (wne A2 C) (wne B2 C) (wne A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs))) (wceq (co (co C A2 cmin) (co B2 A2 cmin) F) (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) F)) $;

--| If two triangles have equal sides, one angle in one triangle has the same cosine as the corresponding angle in the other triangle. This is a partial form of the SSS congruence theorem. This theorem is proven by using ~ lawcos on both triangles to express one side in terms of the other two, and then equating these expressions and reducing this algebraically to get an equality of cosines of angles. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem ssscongptld {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D E: class) (F: class x3 y1) (G: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne E G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co D E cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co E G cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co C A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co G D cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) F) ccos) (cfv (co (co D E cmin) (co G E cmin) F) ccos)) $;

--| Equivalence between two ways of expressing ` B ` as an affine combination of ` A ` and ` C ` . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem affineequiv (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq B2 (co (co D A2 cmul) (co (co c1 D cmin) C cmul) caddc)) (wceq (co C B2 cmin) (co D (co C A2 cmin) cmul))) $;

--| Equivalence between two ways of expressing ` B ` as an affine combination of ` A ` and ` C ` . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem affineequiv2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq B2 (co (co D A2 cmul) (co (co c1 D cmin) C cmul) caddc)) (wceq (co B2 A2 cmin) (co (co c1 D cmin) (co C A2 cmin) cmul))) $;

--| Equivalence between two ways of expressing ` A ` as an affine combination of ` B ` and ` C ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem affineequiv3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 (co (co (co c1 D cmin) B2 cmul) (co D C cmul) caddc)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) (co D (co C B2 cmin) cmul))) $;

--| Equivalence between two ways of expressing ` A ` as an affine combination of ` B ` and ` C ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem affineequiv4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 (co (co (co c1 D cmin) B2 cmul) (co D C cmul) caddc)) (wceq A2 (co (co D (co C B2 cmin) cmul) B2 caddc))) $;

--| Equivalence between two ways of expressing ` A ` as an affine combination of ` B ` and ` C ` if ` B ` and ` C ` are not equal. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem affineequivne (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq A2 (co (co (co c1 D cmin) B2 cmul) (co D C cmul) caddc)) (wceq D (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) cdiv))) $;

--| Equivalence used in the proof of ~ angpieqvd . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angpieqvdlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cneg (co (co C B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmin) cdiv)) crp) (wcel (co (co C B2 cmin) (co C A2 cmin) cdiv) (co cc0 c1 cioo))) $;

--| Equivalence used in ~ angpieqvd . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angpieqvdlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cneg (co (co C B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 cmin) cdiv)) crp) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) F) cpi)) $;

--| If the angle at ABC is ` _pi ` , then ` A ` is not equal to ` C ` . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angpined {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) F) cpi) (wne A2 C)) $;

--| The angle ABC is ` _pi ` iff ` B ` is a nontrivial convex combination of ` A ` and ` C ` , i.e., iff ` B ` is in the interior of the segment AC. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem angpieqvd {x3 y1 w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin) F) cpi) (wrex w (wceq B2 (co (co (cv w) A2 cmul) (co (co c1 (cv w) cmin) C cmul) caddc)) (co cc0 c1 cioo))) $;

--| If ` M ` is the midpoint of AB and AQ = BQ, then QMB is a right angle. The proof uses ~ ssscongptld to observe that, since AMQ and BMQ have equal sides, the angles QMB and QMA must be equal. Since they are supplementary, both must be right angles. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthmlem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 Q: class) (F: class x3 y1) (M: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Q M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co Q M cmin) (co B2 M cmin) F) (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)))) $;

--| If M is the midpoint of AB, AQ = BQ, and P is on the line AB, then QMP is a right angle. This is proven by reduction to the special case ~ chordthmlem , where P = B, and using ~ angrtmuld to observe that QMP is right iff QMB is. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthmlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 P Q: class) (F: class x3 y1) (M: class) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co X A2 cmul) (co (co c1 X cmin) B2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Q M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co Q M cmin) (co P M cmin) F) (cpr (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)))) $;

--| If M is the midpoint of AB, AQ = BQ, and P is on the line AB, then PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> = QM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` + ` PM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. This follows from ~ chordthmlem2 and the Pythagorean theorem ( ~ pythag ) in the case where P and Q are unequal to M. If either P or Q equals M, the result is trivial. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthmlem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P Q M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co X A2 cmul) (co (co c1 X cmin) B2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P Q cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv (co Q M cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co P M cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| If P is on the segment AB and M is the midpoint of AB, then PA ` x. ` PB = BM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. If all lengths are reexpressed as fractions of AB, this reduces to the identity ` X x. ( 1 - X ) = ( 1 / 2 ) `<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ( ( 1 / 2 ) - X ) `<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthmlem4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co X A2 cmul) (co (co c1 X cmin) B2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P B2 cmin) cabs) cmul) (co (co (cfv (co B2 M cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co P M cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) cmin)) $;

--| If P is on the segment AB and AQ = BQ, then PA ` x. ` PB = BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. This follows from two uses of ~ chordthmlem3 to show that PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> = QM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` + ` PM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> and BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> = QM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` + ` BM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>, so BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> = (QM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` + ` BM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>) ` - ` (QM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` + ` PM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>) = BM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PM<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>, which equals PA ` x. ` PB by ~ chordthmlem4 . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthmlem5 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P Q X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co X A2 cmul) (co (co c1 X cmin) B2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P B2 cmin) cabs) cmul) (co (co (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (co P Q cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) cmin)) $;

--| The intersecting chords theorem. If points A, B, C, and D lie on a circle (with center Q, say), and the point P is on the interior of the segments AB and CD, then the two products of lengths PA ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD are equal. The Euclidean plane is identified with the complex plane, and the fact that P is on AB and on CD is expressed by the hypothesis that the angles APB and CPD are equal to ` _pi ` . The result is proven by using ~ chordthmlem5 twice to show that PA ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD both equal BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. This is similar to the proof of the theorem given in Euclid's _Elements_, where it is Proposition III.35. This is Metamath 100 proof #55. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem chordthm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D P: class) (Q F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 P cmin) (co B2 P cmin) F) cpi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C P cmin) (co D P cmin) F) cpi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co C Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co D Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P B2 cmin) cabs) cmul) (co (cfv (co P C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P D cmin) cabs) cmul)) $;

--| Heron's formula gives the area of a triangle given only the side lengths. If points A, B, C form a triangle, then the area of the triangle, represented here as ` ( 1 / 2 ) x. X x. Y x. abs ( sin O ) ` , is equal to the square root of ` S x. ( S - X ) x. ( S - Y ) x. ( S - Z ) ` , where ` S = ( X + Y + Z ) / 2 ` is half the perimeter of the triangle. Based on work by Jon Pennant. This is Metamath 100 proof #57. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2019.)
theorem heron {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C: class) (S F O X Y Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) $ >
  $ wceq O (co (co B2 C cmin) (co A2 C cmin) F) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co (co X Y caddc) Z caddc) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (co X Y cmul) cmul) (cfv (cfv O csin) cabs) cmul) (cfv (co (co S (co S X cmin) cmul) (co (co S Y cmin) (co S Z cmin) cmul) cmul) csqrt)) $;

--| The quadratic equation, without specifying the particular branch ` D ` to the square root. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem quad2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co D c2 cexp) (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co A2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 X cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) (wo (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) D caddc) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)) (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) D cmin) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)))) $;

--| The quadratic equation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem quad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co A2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 X cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) (wo (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) (cfv D csqrt) caddc) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)) (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) (cfv D csqrt) cmin) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)))) $;

--| The cube roots of unity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem _1cubrlem:
  $ wa (wceq (co (cneg c1) (co c2 c3 cdiv) ccxp) (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv)) (wceq (co (co (cneg c1) (co c2 c3 cdiv) ccxp) c2 cexp) (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) cmin) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The cube roots of unity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem _1cubr (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp c1 (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) cmin) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 R) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c3 cexp) c1)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dcubic1 and ~ dcubic2 : simplify the cubic equation under the substitution ` X = U - M / U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem dcubic1lem (ph: wff) (P Q T U G M N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co G N cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co M c3 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co P c3 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co Q c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne U cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co U (co M U cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co P X cmul) Q caddc) caddc) cc0) (wceq (co (co (co U c3 cexp) c2 cexp) (co (co Q (co U c3 cexp) cmul) (co M c3 cexp) cmin) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| Reverse direction of ~ dcubic . Given a solution ` U ` to the "substitution" quadratic equation ` X = U - M / U ` , show that ` X ` is in the desired form. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Apr-2015.)
theorem dcubic2 {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q T U: class) (G: class r) (M: class) (N: class r) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co G N cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co M c3 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co P c3 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co Q c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne U cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co U (co M U cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co P X cmul) Q caddc) caddc) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wa (wceq (co (cv r) c3 cexp) c1) (wceq X (co (co (cv r) T cmul) (co M (co (cv r) T cmul) cdiv) cmin))) cc) $;

--| Forward direction of ~ dcubic : the claimed formula produces solutions to the cubic equation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Apr-2015.)
theorem dcubic1 (ph: wff) (P Q T G M N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co G N cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co M c3 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co P c3 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co Q c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co T (co M T cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co P X cmul) Q caddc) caddc) cc0) $;

--| Solutions to the depressed cubic, a special case of ~ cubic . (The definitions of ` M , N , G , T ` here differ from ~ mcubic by scale factors of ` -u 9 ` , ` 5 4 ` , ` 5 4 ` and ` -u 2 7 ` respectively, to simplify the algebra and presentation.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem dcubic {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q T: class) (G: class r) (M: class) (N: class r) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co G N cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co M c3 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co P c3 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co Q c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co P X cmul) Q caddc) caddc) cc0) (wrex r (wa (wceq (co (cv r) c3 cexp) c1) (wceq X (co (co (cv r) T cmul) (co M (co (cv r) T cmul) cdiv) cmin))) cc)) $;

--| Solutions to a monic cubic equation, a special case of ~ cubic . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2015.)
theorem mcubic {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C D: class r) (T: class) (G: class r) (M N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co (co N G caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co c4 (co M c3 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c3 C cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co (co (co c2 (co B2 c3 cexp) cmul) (co c9 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c2 c7) D cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co X c3 cexp) (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) cc0) (wrex r (wa (wceq (co (cv r) c3 cexp) c1) (wceq X (cneg (co (co (co B2 (co (cv r) T cmul) caddc) (co M (co (cv r) T cmul) cdiv) caddc) c3 cdiv)))) cc)) $;

--| The solution to the general cubic equation, for arbitrary choices ` G ` and ` T ` of the square and cube roots. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Apr-2015.)
theorem cubic2 {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class r) (T: class) (G: class r) (M N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co T c3 cexp) (co (co N G caddc) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G c2 cexp) (co (co N c2 cexp) (co c4 (co M c3 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c3 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co (co (co c2 (co B2 c3 cexp) cmul) (co (co c9 A2 cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) D cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co A2 (co X c3 cexp) cmul) (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) cc0) (wrex r (wa (wceq (co (cv r) c3 cexp) c1) (wceq X (cneg (co (co (co B2 (co (cv r) T cmul) caddc) (co M (co (cv r) T cmul) cdiv) caddc) (co c3 A2 cmul) cdiv)))) cc)) $;

--| The cubic equation, which gives the roots of an arbitrary (nondegenerate) cubic function. Use ~ rextp to convert the existential quantifier to a triple disjunction. This is Metamath 100 proof #37. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2015.)
theorem cubic {r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D R: class r) (T: class) (G: class r) (M N X: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp c1 (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv) (co (co (cneg c1) (co ci (cfv c3 csqrt) cmul) cmin) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (co (co (co N (cfv G csqrt) caddc) c2 cdiv) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (co (co N c2 cexp) (co c4 (co M c3 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c3 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co (co (co c2 (co B2 c3 cexp) cmul) (co (co c9 A2 cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) D cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co A2 (co X c3 cexp) cmul) (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) cc0) (wrex r (wceq X (cneg (co (co (co B2 (co (cv r) T cmul) caddc) (co M (co (cv r) T cmul) cdiv) caddc) (co c3 A2 cmul) cdiv))) R)) $;

--| Work out a quartic binomial. (You would think that by this point it would be faster to use ~ binom , but it turns out to be just as much work to put it into this form after clearing all the sums and calculating binomial coefficients.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem binom4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c4 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c4 cexp) (co c4 (co (co A2 c3 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul) caddc) (co (co c6 (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmul) (co (co c4 (co A2 (co B2 c3 cexp) cmul) cmul) (co B2 c4 cexp) caddc) caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dquart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem dquartlem1 (ph: wff) (B2 C S I M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co c2 S cmul) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I c2 cexp) (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co B2 c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co C c4 cdiv) S cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co X c2 cexp) (co (co M B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) caddc) (co (co (co (co M c2 cdiv) X cmul) (co C c4 cdiv) cmin) S cdiv) caddc) cc0) (wo (wceq X (co (cneg S) I caddc)) (wceq X (co (cneg S) I cmin)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dquart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem dquartlem2 (ph: wff) (B2 C D S I M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co c2 S cmul) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I c2 cexp) (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co B2 c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co C c4 cdiv) S cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co M c3 cexp) (co (co c2 B2 cmul) (co M c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 D cmul) cmin) M cmul) (cneg (co C c2 cexp)) caddc) caddc) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co (co M B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co (co (co C c2 cexp) c4 cdiv) M cdiv) cmin) D) $;

--| Solve a depressed quartic equation. To eliminate ` S ` , which is the square root of a solution ` M ` to the resolvent cubic equation, apply ~ cubic or one of its variants. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem dquart (ph: wff) (B2 C D S I J M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (co (co c2 S cmul) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I c2 cexp) (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co B2 c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co C c4 cdiv) S cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co M c3 cexp) (co (co c2 B2 cmul) (co M c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 D cmul) cmin) M cmul) (cneg (co C c2 cexp)) caddc) caddc) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J c2 cexp) (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co B2 c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co C c4 cdiv) S cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co X c4 cexp) (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) cc0) (wo (wo (wceq X (co (cneg S) I caddc)) (wceq X (co (cneg S) I cmin))) (wo (wceq X (co S J caddc)) (wceq X (co S J cmin))))) $;

--| Closure lemmas for ~ quart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem quart1cl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel P cc) (wcel Q cc) (wcel R cc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ quart1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem quart1lem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Y (co X (co A2 c4 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co (co (co A2 c4 cexp) (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co P (co (co A2 c4 cdiv) c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co Q (co A2 c4 cdiv) cmul) R caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Depress a quartic equation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem quart1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Y (co X (co A2 c4 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co X c4 cexp) (co A2 (co X c3 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) caddc) (co (co (co Y c4 cexp) (co P (co Y c2 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co Q Y cmul) R caddc) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ quart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem quartlem1 (ph: wff) (P Q R U V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co (co P c2 cexp) (co (cdc c1 c2) R cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (co (co (cneg (co c2 (co P c3 cexp) cmul)) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co Q c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c7 c2) (co P R cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq U (co (co (co c2 P cmul) c2 cexp) (co c3 (co (co P c2 cexp) (co c4 R cmul) cmin) cmul) cmin)) (wceq V (co (co (co c2 (co (co c2 P cmul) c3 cexp) cmul) (co c9 (co (co c2 P cmul) (co (co P c2 cexp) (co c4 R cmul) cmin) cmul) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c2 c7) (cneg (co Q c2 cexp)) cmul) caddc))) $;

--| Closure lemmas for ~ quart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem quartlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R U E V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cneg (co A2 c4 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co (co P c2 cexp) (co (cdc c1 c2) R cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (co (co (cneg (co c2 (co P c3 cexp) cmul)) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co Q c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c7 c2) (co P R cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cfv (co (co V c2 cexp) (co c4 (co U c3 cexp) cmul) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel U cc) (wcel V cc) (wcel W cc)) $;

--| Closure lemmas for ~ quart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem quartlem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R S T U E M V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cneg (co A2 c4 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co (co P c2 cexp) (co (cdc c1 c2) R cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (co (co (cneg (co c2 (co P c3 cexp) cmul)) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co Q c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c7 c2) (co P R cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cfv (co (co V c2 cexp) (co c4 (co U c3 cexp) cmul) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co (cfv M csqrt) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (cneg (co (co (co (co c2 P cmul) T caddc) (co U T cdiv) caddc) c3 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (co (co (co V W caddc) c2 cdiv) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel S cc) (wcel M cc) (wcel T cc)) $;

--| Closure lemmas for ~ quart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem quartlem4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R S T U E I J M V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cneg (co A2 c4 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co (co P c2 cexp) (co (cdc c1 c2) R cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (co (co (cneg (co c2 (co P c3 cexp) cmul)) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co Q c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c7 c2) (co P R cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cfv (co (co V c2 cexp) (co c4 (co U c3 cexp) cmul) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co (cfv M csqrt) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (cneg (co (co (co (co c2 P cmul) T caddc) (co U T cdiv) caddc) c3 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (co (co (co V W caddc) c2 cdiv) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (cfv (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co P c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co Q c4 cdiv) S cdiv) caddc) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co P c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co Q c4 cdiv) S cdiv) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wne S cc0) (wcel I cc) (wcel J cc)) $;

--| The quartic equation, writing out all roots using square and cube root functions so that only direct substitutions remain, and we can actually claim to have a "quartic equation". Naturally, this theorem is ridiculously long (see ~ quartfull ) if all the substitutions are performed. This is Metamath 100 proof #46. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem quart (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P Q R S T U E I J M V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E (cneg (co A2 c4 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co B2 (co (co c3 c8 cdiv) (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (co (co C (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co A2 c3 cexp) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co (co D (co (co C A2 cmul) c4 cdiv) cmin) (co (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) cdiv) (co (co c3 (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) cdiv) (co A2 c4 cexp) cmul) cmin) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (co (co P c2 cexp) (co (cdc c1 c2) R cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq V (co (co (cneg (co c2 (co P c3 cexp) cmul)) (co (cdc c2 c7) (co Q c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) (co (cdc c7 c2) (co P R cmul) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq W (cfv (co (co V c2 cexp) (co c4 (co U c3 cexp) cmul) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (co (cfv M csqrt) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M (cneg (co (co (co (co c2 P cmul) T caddc) (co U T cdiv) caddc) c3 cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (co (co (co V W caddc) c2 cdiv) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I (cfv (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co P c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co Q c4 cdiv) S cdiv) caddc) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (cfv (co (co (cneg (co S c2 cexp)) (co P c2 cdiv) cmin) (co (co Q c4 cdiv) S cdiv) cmin) csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co (co (co X c4 cexp) (co A2 (co X c3 cexp) cmul) caddc) (co (co B2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co C X cmul) D caddc) caddc) caddc) cc0) (wo (wo (wceq X (co (co E S cmin) I caddc)) (wceq X (co (co E S cmin) I cmin))) (wo (wceq X (co (co E S caddc) J caddc)) (wceq X (co (co E S caddc) J cmin))))) $;

--| The arcsine function.
term casin: class;

--| The arccosine function.
term cacos: class;

--| The arctangent function.
term catan: class;

--| Define the arcsine function. Because ` sin ` is not a one-to-one function, the literal inverse ` ``' sin ` is not a function. Rather than attempt to find the right domain on which to restrict ` sin ` in order to get a total function, we just define it in terms of ` log ` , which we already know is total (except at ` 0 ` ). There are branch points at ` -u 1 ` and ` 1 ` (at which the function is defined), and branch cuts along the real line not between ` -u 1 ` and ` 1 ` , which is to say ` ( -oo , -u 1 ) u. ( 1 , +oo ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_asin {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq casin (cmpt x3 cc (co (cneg ci) (cfv (co (co ci (cv x3) cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) clog) cmul)) $;

--| Define the arccosine function. See also remarks for ~ df-asin . Since we define ` arccos ` in terms of ` arcsin ` , it shares the same branch points and cuts, namely ` ( -oo , -u 1 ) u. ( 1 , +oo ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_acos {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cacos (cmpt x3 cc (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cfv (cv x3) casin) cmin)) $;

--| Define the arctangent function. See also remarks for ~ df-asin . Unlike ` arcsin ` and ` arccos ` , this function is not defined everywhere, because ` tan ( z ) =/= +- _i ` for all ` z e. CC ` . For all other ` z ` , there is a formula for ` arctan ( z ) ` in terms of ` log ` , and we take that as the definition. Branch points are at ` +- _i ` ; branch cuts are on the pure imaginary axis not between ` -u _i ` and ` _i ` , which is to say ` { z e. CC | ( _i x. z ) e. ( -oo , -u 1 ) u. ( 1 , +oo ) } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
axiom df_atan {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq catan (cmpt x3 (cdif cc (cpr (cneg ci) ci)) (co (co ci c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci (cv x3) cmul) cmin) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci (cv x3) cmul) caddc) clog) cmin) cmul)) $;

--| The argument to the logarithm in ~ df-asin is always nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem asinlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wne (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) cc0) $;

--| The argument to the logarithm in ~ df-asin has the property that replacing ` A ` with ` -u A ` in the expression gives the reciprocal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinlem2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) (co (co ci (cneg A2) cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co (cneg A2) c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) cmul) c1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ asinlem3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinlem3a (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cim) cc0 cle)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) cre) cle) $;

--| The argument to the logarithm in ~ df-asin has nonnegative real part. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinlem3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (cfv (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) cre) cle) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the arcsin function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem asinf:
  $ wf cc cc casin $;

--| Closure for the arcsin function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem asincl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 casin) cc) $;

--| Domain and codoamin of the arccos function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem acosf:
  $ wf cc cc cacos $;

--| Closure for the arccos function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem acoscl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cacos) cc) $;

--| Since the property is a little lengthy, we abbreviate ` A e. CC /\ A =/= -u _i /\ A =/= _i ` as ` A e. dom arctan ` . This is the necessary precondition for the definition of ` arctan ` to make sense. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atandm (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 (cneg ci)) (wne A2 ci)) $;

--| This form of ~ atandm is a bit more useful for showing that the logarithms in ~ df-atan are well-defined. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atandm2 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) cc0) (wne (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| A compact form of ~ atandm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atandm3 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (co A2 c2 cexp) (cneg c1))) $;

--| A compact form of ~ atandm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem atandm4 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| Domain and codoamin of the arctan function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atanf:
  $ wf (cdif cc (cpr (cneg ci) ci)) cc catan $;

--| Closure for the arctan function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atancl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wcel (cfv A2 catan) cc) $;

--| Value of the arcsin function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem asinval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 casin) (co (cneg ci) (cfv (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc) clog) cmul)) $;

--| Value of the arccos function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem acosval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cacos) (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) (cfv A2 casin) cmin)) $;

--| Value of the arctan function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atanval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv A2 catan) (co (co ci c2 cdiv) (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) clog) cmin) cmul)) $;

--| A real number is in the domain of the arctangent function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atanre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) $;

--| The arcsine function is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) casin) (cneg (cfv A2 casin))) $;

--| The negative symmetry relation of the arccosine. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acosneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cacos) (co cpi (cfv A2 cacos) cmin)) $;

--| The exponential of the arcsine function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem efiasin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (co ci (cfv A2 casin) cmul) ce) (co (co ci A2 cmul) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt) caddc)) $;

--| The arcsine function is an inverse to ` sin ` . This is the main property that justifies the notation ` arcsin ` or ` sin ^ -u 1 ` . Because ` sin ` is not an injection, the other converse identity ~ asinsin is only true under limited circumstances. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem sinasin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 casin) csin) A2) $;

--| The arccosine function is an inverse to ` cos ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem cosacos (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cacos) ccos) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ asinsin . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinsinlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cfv (co ci A2 cmul) ce) cre) clt) $;

--| The arcsine function composed with ` sin ` is equal to the identity. This plus ~ sinasin allow to view ` sin ` and ` arcsin ` as inverse operations to each other. For ease of use, we have not defined precisely the correct domain of correctness of this identity; in addition to the main region described here it is also true for _some_ points on the branch cuts, namely when ` A = ( _pi / 2 ) - _i y ` for nonnegative real ` y ` and also symmetrically at ` A = _i y - ( _pi / 2 ) ` . In particular, when restricted to reals this identity extends to the closed interval ` [ -u ( _pi / 2 ) , ( _pi / 2 ) ] ` , not just the open interval (see ~ reasinsin ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinsin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 csin) casin) A2) $;

--| The arccosine function is an inverse to ` cos ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acoscos (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co cc0 cpi cioo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccos) cacos) A2) $;

--| The arcsine of ` 1 ` is ` _pi / 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asin1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 casin) (co cpi c2 cdiv) $;

--| The arccosine of ` 1 ` is ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acos1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cacos) cc0 $;

--| The arcsine function composed with ` sin ` is equal to the identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem reasinsin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 csin) casin) A2) $;

--| Relationship between sine and arcsine. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinsinb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel (cfv B2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 casin) B2) (wceq (cfv B2 csin) A2)) $;

--| Relationship between cosine and arccosine. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acoscosb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel (cfv B2 cre) (co cc0 cpi cioo))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cacos) B2) (wceq (cfv B2 ccos) A2)) $;

--| The arcsine function has range within a vertical strip of the complex plane with real part between ` -u _pi / 2 ` and ` _pi / 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 casin) cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) $;

--| The arccosine function has range within a vertical strip of the complex plane with real part between ` 0 ` and ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acosbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 cacos) cre) (co cc0 cpi cicc)) $;

--| Bounds on the arcsine function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinrebnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) (wcel (cfv A2 casin) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cicc)) $;

--| The arcsine function is real in its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem asinrecl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) (wcel (cfv A2 casin) cr) $;

--| The arccosine function is real in its principal domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem acosrecl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg c1) c1 cicc)) (wcel (cfv A2 cacos) cr) $;

--| The cosine of the arcsine of ` A ` is ` sqrt ( 1 - A ^ 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem cosasin (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 casin) ccos) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt)) $;

--| The sine of the arccosine of ` A ` is ` sqrt ( 1 - A ^ 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem sinacos (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cacos) csin) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmin) csqrt)) $;

--| The domain of the arctangent function is closed under negatives. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem atandmneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wcel (cneg A2) (cdm catan)) $;

--| The arctangent function is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) catan) (cneg (cfv A2 catan))) $;

--| The arctangent of zero is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atan0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 catan) cc0 $;

--| The arctangent function distributes under conjugation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atandmcj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wcel (cfv A2 ccj) (cdm catan)) $;

--| The arctangent function distributes under conjugation. (The condition that ` Re ( A ) =/= 0 ` is necessary because the branch cuts are chosen so that the negative imaginary line "agrees with" neighboring values with negative real part, while the positive imaginary line agrees with values with positive real part. This makes ~ atanneg true unconditionally but messes up conjugation symmetry, and it is impossible to have both in a single-valued function. The claim is true on the imaginary line between ` -u 1 ` and ` 1 ` , though.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atancj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (cfv A2 cre) cc0)) (wa (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 catan) ccj) (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) catan))) $;

--| The arctangent function is real for all real inputs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atanrecl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 catan) cr) $;

--| Value of the exponential of an arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem efiatan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (co ci (cfv A2 catan) cmul) ce) (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) csqrt) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ atanlogadd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanlogaddlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cre) cle)) (wcel (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) clog) caddc) (crn clog)) $;

--| The rule ` sqrt ( z w ) = ( sqrt z ) ( sqrt w ) ` is not always true on the complex numbers, but it is true when the arguments of ` z ` and ` w ` sum to within the interval ` ( -u _pi , _pi ] ` , so there are some cases such as this one with ` z = 1 + _i A ` and ` w = 1 - _i A ` which are true unconditionally. This result can also be stated as " ` sqrt ( 1 + z ) + sqrt ( 1 - z ) ` is analytic". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanlogadd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wcel (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) clog) caddc) (crn clog)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ atanlogsub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanlogsublem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cre) clt)) (wcel (cfv (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) clog) cmin) cim) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo)) $;

--| A variation on ~ atanlogadd , to show that ` sqrt ( 1 + _i z ) / sqrt ( 1 - _i z ) = ` ` sqrt ( ( 1 + _i z ) / ( 1 - _i z ) ) ` under more limited conditions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanlogsub (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wne (cfv A2 cre) cc0)) (wcel (co (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) clog) (cfv (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) clog) cmin) (crn clog)) $;

--| Value of the exponential of an arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem efiatan2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (co ci (cfv A2 catan) cmul) ce) (co (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of the exponential of an arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem _2efiatan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (co c2 (co ci (cfv A2 catan) cmul) cmul) ce) (co (co (co c2 ci cmul) (co A2 ci caddc) cdiv) c1 cmin)) $;

--| The arctangent function is an inverse to ` tan ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem tanatan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 catan) ctan) A2) $;

--| The tangent function has range contained in the domain of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem atandmtan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne (cfv A2 ccos) cc0)) (wcel (cfv A2 ctan) (cdm catan)) $;

--| The cosine of an arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem cosatan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 catan) ccos) (co c1 (cfv (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| The arctangent function has range contained in the domain of the tangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Apr-2015.)
theorem cosatanne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wne (cfv (cfv A2 catan) ccos) cc0) $;

--| The arctangent function is an inverse to ` tan ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem atantan (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ctan) catan) A2) $;

--| Relationship between tangent and arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem atantanb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel (cfv B2 cre) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 catan) B2) (wceq (cfv B2 ctan) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ atanbnd . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanbndlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cfv A2 catan) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) $;

--| The arctangent function is bounded by ` _pi / 2 ` on the reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 catan) (co (cneg (co cpi c2 cdiv)) (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) $;

--| The arctangent function is strictly increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem atanord (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 catan) (cfv B2 catan) clt)) $;

--| The arctangent of ` 1 ` is ` _pi / 4 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Apr-2015.)
theorem atan1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 catan) (co cpi c4 cdiv) $;

--| A point in the open unit disk is in the domain of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Apr-2015.)
theorem bndatandm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wcel A2 (cdm catan)) $;

--| The "domain of continuity" of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atans {y1: setvar} (A2 D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 S) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) caddc) D)) $;

--| It suffices to show that ` 1 - _i A ` and ` 1 + _i A ` are in the continuity domain of ` log ` to show that ` A ` is in the continuity domain of arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atans2 {y1: setvar} (A2 D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 S) (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) cmin) D) (wcel (co c1 (co ci A2 cmul) caddc) D)) $;

--| The domain of continuity of the arctangent is an open set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atansopn {y1: setvar} (D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wcel S (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $;

--| The domain of continuity of the arctangent is a subset of the actual domain of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atansssdm {y1: setvar} (D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wss S (cdm catan) $;

--| The real number line is a subset of the domain of continuity of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem ressatans {y1: setvar} (D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wss cr S $;

--| The derivative of the arctangent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem dvatan {x3 y1: setvar} (D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wceq (co cc (cres catan S) cdv) (cmpt x3 S (co c1 (co c1 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) caddc) cdiv)) $;

--| The arctangent is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atancn {y1: setvar} (D: class) (S: class y1):
  $ wceq D (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wcel (co c1 (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc) D) cc) $ >
  $ wcel (cres catan S) (co S cc ccncf) $;

--| The Taylor series for ` arctan ( A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem atantayl {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co ci (co (co (cneg ci) (cv n) cexp) (co ci (cv n) cexp) cmin) cmul) c2 cdiv) (co (co A2 (cv n) cexp) (cv n) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) (cfv A2 catan) cli) $;

--| The Taylor series for ` arctan ( A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Apr-2015.)
theorem atantayl2 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds) cc0 (co (co (cneg c1) (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) (co (co A2 (cv n) cexp) (cv n) cdiv) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) (cfv A2 catan) cli) $;

--| The Taylor series for ` arctan ( A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem atantayl3 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (cneg c1) (cv n) cexp) (co (co A2 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) c1 clt)) (wbr (cseq caddc F cc0) (cfv A2 catan) cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ leibpi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by Steven Nguyen, 23-Mar-2023.)
theorem leibpilem1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wa (wn (wceq N cc0)) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)))) (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn0)) $;

--| The Leibniz formula for ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem leibpilem2 {k n: setvar} (A2 F: class k n) (G: class k):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (cneg c1) (cv n) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cn0 (cif (wo (wceq (cv k) cc0) (wbr c2 (cv k) cdvds)) cc0 (co (co (cneg c1) (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) (cv k) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cseq caddc F cc0) A2 cli) (wbr (cseq caddc G cc0) A2 cli) $;

--| The Leibniz formula for ` _pi ` . This proof depends on three main facts: (1) the series ` F ` is convergent, because it is an alternating series ( ~ iseralt ). (2) Using ~ leibpilem2 to rewrite the series as a power series, it is the ` x = 1 ` special case of the Taylor series for ` arctan ` ( ~ atantayl2 ). (3) Although we cannot directly plug ` x = 1 ` into ~ atantayl2 , Abel's theorem ( ~ abelth2 ) says that the limit along any sequence converging to ` 1 ` , such as ` 1 - 1 / n ` , of the power series converges to the power series extended to ` 1 ` , and then since ` arctan ` is continuous at ` 1 ` ( ~ atancn ) we get the desired result. This is Metamath 100 proof #26. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem leibpi {n: setvar} (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (cneg c1) (cv n) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr (cseq caddc F cc0) (co cpi c4 cdiv) cli $;

--| The Leibniz formula for ` _pi ` . This version of ~ leibpi looks nicer but does not assert that the series is convergent so is not as practically useful. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem leibpisum {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (csu n cn0 (co (co (cneg c1) (cv n) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) (co cpi c4 cdiv) $;

--| Using the Taylor series for ` arctan ( _i / 3 ) ` , produce a rapidly convergent series for ` log 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem log2cnv {n: setvar} (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co c2 (co (co c3 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr (cseq caddc F cc0) (cfv c2 clog) cli $;

--| Bound the error term in the series of ~ log2cnv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.)
theorem log2tlbnd {n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (cfv c2 clog) (csu n (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co c2 (co (co c3 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) cmin) (co cc0 (co c3 (co (co c4 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 N cexp) cmul) cdiv) cicc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ log2ub . The proof of ~ log2ub , which is simply the evaluation of ~ log2tlbnd for ` N = 4 ` , takes the form of the addition of five fractions and showing this is less than another fraction. We could just perform exact arithmetic on these fractions, get a large rational number, and just multiply everything to verify the claim, but as anyone who uses decimal numbers for this task knows, it is often better to pick a common denominator ` d ` (usually a large power of ` 1 0 ` ) and work with the closest approximations of the form ` n / d ` for some integer ` n ` instead. It turns out that for our purposes it is sufficient to take ` d = ( 3 ^ 7 ) x. 5 x. 7 ` , which is also nice because it shares many factors in common with the fractions in question. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem log2ublem1 (A2 B2 C D E F G: class):
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) A2 cmul) B2 cle $ >
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel D cn0 $ >
  $ wcel E cn $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel F cn0 $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 (co D E cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 F caddc) G $ >
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) D cmul) (co E F cmul) cle $ >
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) C cmul) G cle $;

--| Lemma for ~ log2ub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem log2ublem2 {n: setvar} (B2 F G: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) (csu n (co cc0 K cfz) (co c2 (co (co c3 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) cmul) (co c2 B2 cmul) cle $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel F cn0 $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co N c1 cmin) K $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 F caddc) G $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co M N caddc) c3 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c5 c7 cmul) (co c9 M cexp) cmul) (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) F cmul) $ >
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) (csu n (co cc0 N cfz) (co c2 (co (co c3 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) cmul) (co c2 G cmul) cle $;

--| Lemma for ~ log2ub . In decimal, this is a proof that the first four terms of the series for ` log 2 ` is less than ` 5 3 0 5 6 / 7 6 5 4 5 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem log2ublem3 {n: setvar}:
  $ wbr (co (co (co c3 c7 cexp) (co c5 c7 cmul) cmul) (csu n (co cc0 c3 cfz) (co c2 (co (co c3 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) (co c9 (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c5 c3) cc0) c5) c6) cle $;

--| ` log 2 ` is less than ` 2 5 3 / 3 6 5 ` . If written in decimal, this is because ` log 2 = ` 0.693147... is less than 253/365 = 0.693151... , so this is a very tight bound, at five decimal places. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem log2ub:
  $ wbr (cfv c2 clog) (co (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c3) (cdc (cdc c3 c6) c5) cdiv) clt $;

--| ` log 2 ` is less than ` 1 ` . This is just a weaker form of ~ log2ub when no tight upper bound is required. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem log2le1:
  $ wbr (cfv c2 clog) c1 clt $;

--| Lemma for ~ birthday . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem birthdaylem1 {f2: setvar} (S T: class f2) (K N: class):
  $ wceq S (cab f2 (wf (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cab f2 (wf1 (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ w3a (wss T S) (wcel S cfn) (wi (wcel N cn) (wne S c0)) $;

--| For general ` N ` and ` K ` , count the fraction of injective functions from ` 1 ... K ` to ` 1 ... N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem birthdaylem2 {f2 k: setvar} (S T: class f2 k) (K N: class):
  $ wceq S (cab f2 (wf (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cab f2 (wf1 (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz))) (wceq (co (cfv T chash) (cfv S chash) cdiv) (cfv (csu k (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz) (cfv (co c1 (co (cv k) N cdiv) cmin) clog)) ce)) $;

--| For general ` N ` and ` K ` , upper-bound the fraction of injective functions from ` 1 ... K ` to ` 1 ... N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem birthdaylem3 {f2: setvar} (S T: class f2) (K N: class):
  $ wceq S (cab f2 (wf (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cab f2 (wf1 (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wcel N cn)) (wbr (co (cfv T chash) (cfv S chash) cdiv) (cfv (cneg (co (co (co (co K c2 cexp) K cmin) c2 cdiv) N cdiv)) ce) cle) $;

--| The Birthday Problem. There is a more than even chance that out of 23 people in a room, at least two of them have the same birthday. Mathematically, this is asserting that for ` K = 2 3 ` and ` N = 3 6 5 ` , fewer than half of the set of all functions from ` 1 ... K ` to ` 1 ... N ` are injective. This is Metamath 100 proof #93. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem birthday {f2: setvar} (S T: class f2) (K N: class):
  $ wceq S (cab f2 (wf (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cab f2 (wf1 (co c1 K cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cdc c2 c3) $ >
  $ wceq N (cdc (cdc c3 c6) c5) $ >
  $ wbr (co (cfv T chash) (cfv S chash) cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) clt $;

--| Area of regions in the complex plane.
term carea: class;

--| Define the area of a subset of ` RR X. RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
axiom df_area {x3 t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq carea (cmpt s1 (crab t (wa (wral x3 (wcel (cima (cv t) (csn (cv x3))) (cima (ccnv cvol) cr)) cr) (wcel (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (cima (cv t) (csn (cv x3))) cvol)) cibl)) (cpw (cxp cr cr))) (citg x3 cr (cfv (cima (cv s1) (csn (cv x3))) cvol))) $;

--| The domain of the area function is the set of finitely measurable subsets of ` RR X. RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem dmarea {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm carea)) (w3a (wss A2 (cxp cr cr)) (wral x3 (wcel (cima A2 (csn (cv x3))) (cima (ccnv cvol) cr)) cr) (wcel (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (cima A2 (csn (cv x3))) cvol)) cibl)) $;

--| The fibers of a measurable region are finitely measurable subsets of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areambl (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cdm carea)) (wcel A2 cr)) (wa (wcel (cima S (csn A2)) (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv (cima S (csn A2)) cvol) cr)) $;

--| A measurable region is a subset of ` RR X. RR ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areass (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cdm carea)) (wss S (cxp cr cr)) $;

--| Rewrite ~ df-area self-referentially. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem dfarea {x3 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq carea (cmpt s1 (cdm carea) (citg x3 cr (cfv (cima (cv s1) (csn (cv x3))) cvol))) $;

--| Area measurement is a function whose values are nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areaf:
  $ wf (cdm carea) (co cc0 cpnf cico) carea $;

--| The area of a measurable region is a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areacl (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cdm carea)) (wcel (cfv S carea) cr) $;

--| The area of a measurable region is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areage0 (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cdm carea)) (wbr cc0 (cfv S carea) cle) $;

--| The area of a measurable region is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem areaval {x3: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cdm carea)) (wceq (cfv S carea) (citg x3 cr (cfv (cima S (csn (cv x3))) cvol))) $;

--| Relate a limit of a real-valued sequence at infinity to the continuity of the function ` S ( y ) = R ( 1 / y ) ` at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem rlimcnp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (J K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel R cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) cc0) (wceq R C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co c1 (cv y1) cdiv)) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cmpt y1 B2 S) C crli) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 R) (cfv cc0 (co K J ccnp)))) $;

--| Relate a limit of a real-valued sequence at infinity to the continuity of the function ` S ( y ) = R ( 1 / y ) ` at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem rlimcnp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (J K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wb (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel (co c1 (cv y1) cdiv) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv)) (wceq S R) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cmpt y1 B2 S) C crli) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) C R)) (cfv cc0 (co K J ccnp)))) $;

--| Relate a limit of a real-valued sequence at infinity to the continuity of the function ` S ( y ) = R ( 1 / y ) ` at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem rlimcnp3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (R: class x3) (S: class y1) (J K: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv)) (wceq S R) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J (co cc0 cpnf cico) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cmpt y1 crp S) C crli) (wcel (cmpt x3 (co cc0 cpnf cico) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) C R)) (cfv cc0 (co K J ccnp)))) $;

--| Relate a limit of a real-valued sequence at infinity to the continuity of the corresponding extended real function at ` +oo ` . Since any ` ~~>r ` limit can be written in the form on the left side of the implication, this shows that real limits are a special case of topological continuity at a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem xrlimcnp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R J K: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cun B2 (csn cpnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel R cc) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) cpnf) (wceq R C) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (cfv cle cordt) A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cmpt x3 B2 R) C crli) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 R) (cfv cpnf (co K J ccnp)))) $;

--| The limit of the sequence ` ( 1 + A / k ) ^ k ` is the exponential function. This is often taken as an alternate definition of the exponential function (see also ~ dfef2 ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem efrlim {k: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class k):
  $ wceq S (co cc0 (co c1 (co (cfv A2 cabs) c1 caddc) cdiv) (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cmpt k crp (co (co c1 (co A2 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) (cv k) ccxp)) (cfv A2 ce) crli) $;

--| The limit of the sequence ` ( 1 + A / k ) ^ k ` as ` k ` goes to ` +oo ` is ` ( exp `` A ) ` . This is another common definition of ` _e ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Mar-2015.)
theorem dfef2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (co (co c1 (co A2 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) (cv k) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cfv A2 ce) cli) $;

--| A power to a negative exponent goes to zero as the base becomes large. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem cxplim {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cmpt n crp (co c1 (co (cv n) A2 ccxp) cdiv)) cc0 crli) $;

--| The inverse square root function converges to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtlim {n: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt n crp (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) cc0 crli $;

--| Any power to a positive exponent of a converging sequence also converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2014.)
theorem rlimcxp {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class n) (C: class) (V: class n):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n A2 B2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n A2 (co B2 C ccxp)) cc0 crli) $;

--| An eventually bounded function taken to a nonnegative power is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem o1cxp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv C cre) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) co1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C ccxp)) co1) $;

--| A linear factor grows slower than any exponential with base greater than ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxp2limlem {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wbr (cmpt n crp (co (cv n) (co A2 (cv n) ccxp) cdiv)) cc0 crli) $;

--| Any power grows slower than any exponential with base greater than ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxp2lim {n: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr c1 B2 clt)) (wbr (cmpt n crp (co (co (cv n) A2 ccxp) (co B2 (cv n) ccxp) cdiv)) cc0 crli) $;

--| The logarithm grows slower than any positive power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2014.)
theorem cxploglim {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cmpt n crp (co (cfv (cv n) clog) (co (cv n) A2 ccxp) cdiv)) cc0 crli) $;

--| Every power of the logarithm grows slower than any positive power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2016.)
theorem cxploglim2 {n: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 crp)) (wbr (cmpt n crp (co (co (cfv (cv n) clog) A2 ccxp) (co (cv n) B2 ccxp) cdiv)) cc0 crli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ divsqrsum and ~ divsqrtsum2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem divsqrtsumlem {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class) (F L: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ w3a (wf crp cr F) (wcel F (cdm crli)) (wi (wa (wbr F L crli) (wcel A2 crp)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 F) L cmin) cabs) (co c1 (cfv A2 csqrt) cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The function ` F ` used in ~ divsqrsum is a real function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2016.)
theorem divsqrsumf {x3 n: setvar} (F: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wf crp cr F $;

--| The sum ` sum_ n <_ x ( 1 / sqrt n ) ` is asymptotic to ` 2 sqrt x + L ` with a finite limit ` L ` . (In fact, this limit is ` zeta ( 1 / 2 ) ~~ -u 1 . 4 6 ... ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2016.)
theorem divsqrsum {x3 n: setvar} (F: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wcel F (cdm crli) $;

--| A bound on the distance of the sum ` sum_ n <_ x ( 1 / sqrt n ) ` from its asymptotic value ` 2 sqrt x + L ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem divsqrtsum2 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (F L: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F L crli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 crp)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 F) L cmin) cabs) (co c1 (cfv A2 csqrt) cdiv) cle) $;

--| The sum ` sum_ n <_ x ( 1 / sqrt n ) ` has the asymptotic expansion ` 2 sqrt x + L + O ( 1 / sqrt x ) ` , for some ` L ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2016.)
theorem divsqrtsumo1 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (F L: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) csqrt) cdiv)) (co c2 (cfv (cv x3) csqrt) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F L crli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt y1 crp (co (co (cfv (cv y1) F) L cmin) (cfv (cv y1) csqrt) cmul)) co1) $;

--| Closure of a 0-1 linear combination in a convex set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem cvxcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class) (T: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D))) (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wcel (co (co T X cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) Y cmul) caddc) D) $;

--| A strictly convex function is convex. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem scvxcvx {x3 y1 t a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class) (T: class x3 y1 a b) (F: class a b) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv a) D) (wcel (cv b) D))) (wss (co (cv a) (cv b) cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D) (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) clt)) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cioo))) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) F) (co (co (cv t) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmul) caddc) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel X D) (wcel Y D) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (co T X cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) Y cmul) caddc) F) (co (co T (cfv X F) cmul) (co (co c1 T cmin) (cfv Y F) cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ jensen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2016.)
theorem jensenlem1 {x3 y1 z t a b: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class z) (B2: class) (D S T F: class z) (L: class z a b) (X: class z):
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv a) D) (wcel (cv b) D))) (wss (co (cv a) (cv b) cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co ccnfld T cgsu) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) F) (co (co (cv t) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmul) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cv z) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun B2 (csn (cv z))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq S (co ccnfld (cres T B2) cgsu) $ >
  $ wceq L (co ccnfld (cres T (cun B2 (csn (cv z)))) cgsu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L (co S (cfv (cv z) T) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ jensen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem jensenlem2 {x3 y1 z t a b: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class z) (B2: class) (D S T F: class z) (L: class z a b) (X: class z):
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv a) D) (wcel (cv b) D))) (wss (co (cv a) (cv b) cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co ccnfld T cgsu) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) F) (co (co (cv t) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmul) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cv z) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun B2 (csn (cv z))) A2) $ >
  $ wceq S (co ccnfld (cres T B2) cgsu) $ >
  $ wceq L (co ccnfld (cres T (cun B2 (csn (cv z)))) cgsu) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T X (cof cmul)) B2) cgsu) S cdiv) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T X (cof cmul)) B2) cgsu) S cdiv) F) (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T (ccom F X) (cof cmul)) B2) cgsu) S cdiv) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T X (cof cmul)) (cun B2 (csn (cv z)))) cgsu) L cdiv) D) (wbr (cfv (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T X (cof cmul)) (cun B2 (csn (cv z)))) cgsu) L cdiv) F) (co (co ccnfld (cres (co T (ccom F X) (cof cmul)) (cun B2 (csn (cv z)))) cgsu) L cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Jensen's inequality, a finite extension of the definition of convexity (the last hypothesis). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem jensen {x3 y1 t a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D T F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv a) D) (wcel (cv b) D))) (wss (co (cv a) (cv b) cicc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 D X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co ccnfld T cgsu) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv y1) D) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) F) (co (co (cv t) (cfv (cv x3) F) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv y1) F) cmul) caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (co (co ccnfld (co T X (cof cmul)) cgsu) (co ccnfld T cgsu) cdiv) D) (wbr (cfv (co (co ccnfld (co T X (cof cmul)) cgsu) (co ccnfld T cgsu) cdiv) F) (co (co ccnfld (co T (ccom F X) (cof cmul)) cgsu) (co ccnfld T cgsu) cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ amgm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem amgmlem (ph: wff) (A2 F M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv ccnfld cmgp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 crp F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co M F cgsu) (co c1 (cfv A2 chash) cdiv) ccxp) (co (co ccnfld F cgsu) (cfv A2 chash) cdiv) cle) $;

--| Inequality of arithmetic and geometric means. Here ` ( M gsum F ) ` calculates the group sum within the multiplicative monoid of the complex numbers (or in other words, it multiplies the elements ` F ( x ) , x e. A ` together), and ` ( CCfld gsum F ) ` calculates the group sum in the additive group (i.e. the sum of the elements). This is Metamath 100 proof #38. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem amgm (A2 F M: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv ccnfld cmgp) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0) (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico) F)) (wbr (co (co M F cgsu) (co c1 (cfv A2 chash) cdiv) ccxp) (co (co ccnfld F cgsu) (cfv A2 chash) cdiv) cle) $;

--| The Euler-Mascheroni constant. (The label abbreviates Euler-Mascheroni.)
term cem: class;

--| Define the Euler-Mascheroni constant, ` gamma ` = 0.57721.... This is the limit of the series ` sum_ k e. ( 1 ... m ) ( 1 / k ) - ( log `` m ) ` , with a proof that the limit exists in ~ emcl . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_em {k: setvar}:
  $ wceq cem (csu k cn (co (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) clog) cmin)) $;

--| Bound on the difference of logs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-May-2016.)
theorem logdifbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (co (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clog) (cfv A2 clog) cmin) (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Lower bound on the difference of logs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem logdiflbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (co c1 (co A2 c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clog) (cfv A2 clog) cmin) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . The series ` F ` and ` G ` are sequences of real numbers that approach ` gamma ` from above and below, respectively. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem1 {m n: setvar} (F G: class m n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wa (wf cn cr F) (wf cn cr G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . ` F ` is increasing, and ` G ` is decreasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem2 {m n: setvar} (F G: class m n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wa (wbr (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) (cfv N F) cle) (wbr (cfv N G) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) G) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . The function ` H ` is the difference between ` F ` and ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem3 {m n: setvar} (F G: class m n) (H: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N H) (co (cfv N F) (cfv N G) cmin)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . The difference between series ` F ` and ` G ` tends to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem4 {m n: setvar} (F G: class m n) (H: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog)) $ >
  $ wbr H cc0 cli $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . The partial sums of the series ` T ` , which is used in Definition ~ df-em , is in fact the same as ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem5 {m n: setvar} (T: class) (F G: class m n) (H: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (co (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cseq caddc T c1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl . By the previous lemmas, ` F ` and ` G ` must approach a common limit, which is ` gamma ` by definition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcllem6 {m n: setvar} (T: class) (F G: class m n) (H: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (co (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wa (wbr F cem cli) (wbr G cem cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ emcl and ~ harmonicbnd . Derive bounds on ` gamma ` as ` F ( 1 ) ` and ` G ( 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem emcllem7 {m n: setvar} (T: class) (F G: class m n) (H: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (cv n) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (csu m (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n cn (co (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) (cfv (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ w3a (wcel cem (co (co c1 (cfv c2 clog) cmin) c1 cicc)) (wf cn (co cem c1 cicc) F) (wf cn (co (co c1 (cfv c2 clog) cmin) cem cicc) G) $;

--| Closure and bounds for the Euler-Mascheroni constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emcl:
  $ wcel cem (co (co c1 (cfv c2 clog) cmin) c1 cicc) $;

--| A bound on the harmonic series, as compared to the natural logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem harmonicbnd {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co (csu m (co c1 N cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv N clog) cmin) (co cem c1 cicc)) $;

--| A bound on the harmonic series, as compared to the natural logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem harmonicbnd2 {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co (csu m (co c1 N cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) clog) cmin) (co (co c1 (cfv c2 clog) cmin) cem cicc)) $;

--| The Euler-Mascheroni constant is a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emre:
  $ wcel cem cr $;

--| The Euler-Mascheroni constant is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Jul-2014.)
theorem emgt0:
  $ wbr cc0 cem clt $;

--| A bound on the harmonic series, as compared to the natural logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem harmonicbnd3 {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (csu m (co c1 N cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) clog) cmin) (co cc0 cem cicc)) $;

--| A bound on the harmonic series, as compared to the natural logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem harmoniclbnd {m: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cfv A2 clog) (csu m (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) cle) $;

--| A bound on the harmonic series, as compared to the natural logarithm. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem harmonicubnd {m: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr (csu m (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (co (cfv A2 clog) c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| The asymptotic behavior of ` sum_ m <_ A , 1 / m = log A + gamma + O ( 1 / A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-May-2016.)
theorem harmonicbnd4 {m: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cfv (co (csu m (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co c1 (cv m) cdiv)) (co (cfv A2 clog) cem caddc) cmin) cabs) (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Bound a finite sum based on the harmonic series, where the "strong" bound ` C ` only applies asymptotically, and there is a "weak" bound ` R ` for the remaining values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.)
theorem fsumharmonic {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class n) (R T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel T cr) (wbr c1 T cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel R cr) (wbr cc0 R cle)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wbr T (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) cle)) (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (co C (cv n) cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wbr (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) T clt)) (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) R cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co B2 (cv n) cdiv)) cabs) (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) C) (co R (co (cfv T clog) c1 caddc) cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| The Riemann zeta function.
term czeta: class;

--| Define the Riemann zeta function. This definition uses a series expansion of the alternating zeta function ~~? zetaalt that is convergent everywhere except ` 1 ` , but going from the alternating zeta function to the regular zeta function requires dividing by ` 1 - 2 ^ ( 1 - s ) ` , which has zeroes other than ` 1 ` . To extract the correct value of the zeta function at these points, we extend the divided alternating zeta function by continuity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_zeta {f2 k n s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq czeta (crio f2 (wral s1 (wceq (co (co c1 (co c2 (co c1 (cv s1) cmin) ccxp) cmin) (cfv (cv s1) (cv f2)) cmul) (csu n cn0 (co (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (co (co (cneg c1) (cv k) cexp) (co (cv n) (cv k) cbc) cmul) (co (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv s1) ccxp) cmul)) (co c2 (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cexp) cdiv))) (cdif cc (csn c1))) (co (cdif cc (csn c1)) cc ccncf)) $;

--| The zeta series is convergent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2014.)
theorem zetacvg {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 (cfv S cre) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv k) (cneg S) ccxp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc F c1) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Logarithm of the Gamma function.
term clgam: class;

--| The Gamma function.
term cgam: class;

--| The inverse Gamma function.
term cigam: class;

--| Define the log-Gamma function. We can work with this form of the gamma function a bit easier than the equivalent expression for the gamma function itself, and moreover this function is not actually equal to ` log ( _G ( x ) ) ` because the branch cuts are placed differently (we do have ` exp ( log_G ( x ) ) = _G ( x ) ` , though). This definition is attributed to Euler, and unlike the usual integral definition is defined on the entire complex plane except the nonpositive integers ` ZZ \ NN ` , where the function has simple poles. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_lgam {z m: setvar}:
  $ wceq clgam (cmpt z (cdif cc (cdif cz cn)) (co (csu m cn (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) (cfv (cv z) clog) cmin)) $;

--| Define the Gamma function. See ~ df-lgam for more information about the reason for this definition in terms of the log-gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jul-2014.)
axiom df_gam:
  $ wceq cgam (ccom ce clgam) $;

--| Define the inverse Gamma function, which is defined everywhere, unlike the Gamma function itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
axiom df_igam {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cigam (cmpt x3 cc (cif (wcel (cv x3) (cdif cz cn)) cc0 (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cgam) cdiv))) $;

--| Elementhood in the set of non-nonpositive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jul-2014.)
theorem eldmgm (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wn (wcel (cneg A2) cn0))) $;

--| If ` A ` is not a nonpositive integer, then ` A + N ` is nonzero for any nonnegative integer ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jul-2014.)
theorem dmgmaddn0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wcel N cn0)) (wne (co A2 N caddc) cc0) $;

--| If ` A ` is in the continuous domain of the logarithm, then it is in the domain of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem dmlogdmgm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc))) (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $;

--| A positive real number is in the domain of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem rpdmgm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $;

--| If ` A ` is not a nonpositive integer, then ` A ` is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem dmgmn0 (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| If ` A ` is not a nonpositive integer and ` N ` is a nonnegative integer, then ` A + N ` is also not a nonpositive integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem dmgmaddnn0 (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N caddc) (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamf . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem dmgmdivn0 (ph: wff) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co (co A2 M cdiv) c1 caddc) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamgulm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem1 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (U: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamgulm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 R: class) (U: class x3 k) (N: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c2 R cmul) N cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 N cdiv) (cfv (co (co A2 N cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin) cabs) (co R (co (co c1 (co N R cmin) cdiv) (co c1 N cdiv) cmin) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamgulm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem3 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 R: class) (U: class x3 k) (N: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c2 R cmul) N cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co N c1 caddc) N cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 N cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin) cabs) (co R (co (co c2 (co R c1 caddc) cmul) (co N c2 cexp) cdiv) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamgulm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem4 {x3 z k m: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (T: class x3 z k m) (U: class x3 k) (G: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt m cn (cif (wbr (co c2 R cmul) (cv m) cle) (co R (co (co c2 (co R c1 caddc) cmul) (co (cv m) c2 cexp) cdiv) cmul) (co (co R (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (co (cfv (co (co R c1 caddc) (cv m) cmul) clog) cpi caddc) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc T c1) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamgulm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem5 {x3 z k m: setvar} (ph: wff k) (y1: setvar) (R: class) (T: class x3 z k m) (U: class x3 k) (n: setvar) (G: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt m cn (cif (wbr (co c2 R cmul) (cv m) cle) (co R (co (co c2 (co R c1 caddc) cmul) (co (cv m) c2 cexp) cdiv) cmul) (co (co R (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (co (cfv (co (co R c1 caddc) (cv m) cmul) clog) cpi caddc) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) cn) (wcel (cv y1) U))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv n) G)) cabs) (cfv (cv n) T) cle) $;

--| The series ` G ` is uniformly convergent on the compact region ` U ` , which describes a circle of radius ` R ` with holes of size ` 1 / R ` around the poles of the gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulmlem6 {x3 z k m r: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (T: class x3 z k m) (U: class x3 k) (G O: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt m cn (cif (wbr (co c2 R cmul) (cv m) cle) (co R (co (co c2 (co R c1 caddc) cmul) (co (cv m) c2 cexp) cdiv) cmul) (co (co R (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (co (cfv (co (co R c1 caddc) (cv m) cmul) clog) cpi caddc) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cseq (cof caddc) G c1) (cdm (cfv U culm))) (wi (wbr (cseq (cof caddc) G c1) (cmpt z U O) (cfv U culm)) (wrex r (wral z (wbr (cfv O cabs) (cv r) cle) U) cr))) $;

--| The series ` G ` is uniformly convergent on the compact region ` U ` , which describes a circle of radius ` R ` with holes of size ` 1 / R ` around the poles of the gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulm {x3 z k m: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (U: class x3 k) (G: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq (cof caddc) G c1) (cdm (cfv U culm))) $;

--| Rewrite the limit of the sequence ` G ` in terms of the log-Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamgulm2 {x3 z k m: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (U: class x3 k) (G: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wral z (wcel (cfv (cv z) clgam) cc) U) (wbr (cseq (cof caddc) G c1) (cmpt z U (co (cfv (cv z) clgam) (cfv (cv z) clog) caddc)) (cfv U culm))) $;

--| The log-Gamma function is bounded on the region ` U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgambdd {x3 z k m r: setvar} (ph: wff k) (R: class) (U: class x3 k) (G: class x3 z k m):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) R cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 R cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (cmpt z U (co (co (cv z) (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co (cv z) (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral z (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) clgam) cabs) (cv r) cle) U) cr) $;

--| The ` U ` regions used in the proof of ~ lgamgulm have interiors which cover the entire domain of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamucov {x3 k r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (U J: class x3 k r):
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) (cv r) cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 (cv r) cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wcel A2 (cfv U (cfv J cnt))) cn) $;

--| The ` U ` regions used in the proof of ~ lgamgulm have interiors which cover the entire domain of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamucov2 {x3 k r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (U: class x3 k r):
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) (cv r) cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 (cv r) cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wcel A2 U) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgamf and ~ lgamcvg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamcvglem {x3 k m r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (U: class x3 k r) (G: class x3 k m):
  $ wceq U (crab x3 (wa (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) (cv r) cle) (wral k (wbr (co c1 (cv r) cdiv) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) cabs) cle) cn0)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cfv A2 clgam) cc) (wbr (cseq caddc G c1) (co (cfv A2 clgam) (cfv A2 clog) caddc) cli)) $;

--| The log-Gamma function is a complex function defined on the whole complex plane except for the negative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wcel (cfv A2 clgam) cc) $;

--| The log-Gamma function is a complex function defined on the whole complex plane except for the negative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamf:
  $ wf (cdif cc (cdif cz cn)) cc clgam $;

--| The Gamma function is a complex function defined on the whole complex plane except for the negative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamf:
  $ wf (cdif cc (cdif cz cn)) cc cgam $;

--| The exponential of the log-Gamma function is the Gamma function (by definition). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wcel (cfv A2 cgam) cc) $;

--| The exponential of the log-Gamma function is the Gamma function (by definition). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2017.)
theorem eflgam (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 clgam) ce) (cfv A2 cgam)) $;

--| The Gamma function is never zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wne (cfv A2 cgam) cc0) $;

--| Value of the inverse Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cigam) (cif (wcel A2 (cdif cz cn)) cc0 (co c1 (cfv A2 cgam) cdiv))) $;

--| Value of the inverse Gamma function on nonpositive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamz (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cz cn)) (wceq (cfv A2 cigam) cc0) $;

--| Value of the inverse Gamma function in terms of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamgam (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv A2 cigam) (co c1 (cfv A2 cgam) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of the inverse Gamma function in terms of the log-Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamlgam (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv A2 cigam) (cfv (cneg (cfv A2 clgam)) ce)) $;

--| Closure of the inverse Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamf:
  $ wf cc cc cigam $;

--| Closure of the inverse Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem igamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cigam) cc) $;

--| The Gamma function is the inverse of the inverse Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamigam (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv A2 cgam) (co c1 (cfv A2 cigam) cdiv)) $;

--| The series ` G ` converges to ` log_G ( A ) + log ( A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamcvg {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (G: class m):
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc G c1) (co (cfv A2 clgam) (cfv A2 clog) caddc) cli) $;

--| The series ` G ` converges to ` log_G ( A + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamcvg2 {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (G: class m):
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc G c1) (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clgam) cli) $;

--| The pointwise exponential of the series ` G ` converges to ` _G ( A ) x. A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamcvg {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (G: class m):
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom ce (cseq caddc G c1)) (co (cfv A2 cgam) A2 cmul) cli) $;

--| The functional equation of the (log) Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgamp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clgam) (co (cfv A2 clgam) (cfv A2 clog) caddc)) $;

--| The functional equation of the Gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cgam) (co (cfv A2 cgam) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gamcvg2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamcvg2lem {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F G: class m):
  $ wceq F (cmpt m cn (co (co (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) A2 ccxp) (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt m cn (co (co A2 (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) clog) cmul) (cfv (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) clog) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom ce (cseq caddc G c1)) (cseq cmul F c1)) $;

--| An infinite product expression for the gamma function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamcvg2 {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class m):
  $ wceq F (cmpt m cn (co (co (co (co (cv m) c1 caddc) (cv m) cdiv) A2 ccxp) (co (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq cmul F c1) (co (cfv A2 cgam) A2 cmul) cli) $;

--| The Gamma function is real for real input. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem regamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cr (cdif cz cn))) (wcel (cfv A2 cgam) cr) $;

--| The log-Gamma function is real for positive real input. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem relgamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cfv A2 clgam) cr) $;

--| The log-Gamma function is positive real for positive real input. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem rpgamcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cfv A2 cgam) crp) $;

--| The log-Gamma function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem lgam1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 clgam) cc0 $;

--| The log-Gamma function at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem gam1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cgam) c1 $;

--| The Gamma function generalizes the factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem facgam (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N cfa) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cgam)) $;

--| The Gamma function generalizes the factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2017.)
theorem gamfac (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cgam) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa)) $;

--| The only elements that are equal to their own inverses in the multiplicative group of nonzero elements in ` ZZ / P ZZ ` are ` 1 ` and ` -u 1 == P - 1 ` . (Note that from ~ prmdiveq , ` ( N ^ ( P - 2 ) ) mod P ` is the modular inverse of ` N ` in ` ZZ / P ZZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.)
theorem wilthlem1 (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel N (co c1 (co P c1 cmin) cfz))) (wb (wceq N (co (co N (co P c2 cmin) cexp) P cmo)) (wo (wceq N c1) (wceq N (co P c1 cmin)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wilth : induction step. The "hand proof" version of this theorem works by writing out the list of all numbers from ` 1 ` to ` P - 1 ` in pairs such that a number is paired with its inverse. Every number has a unique inverse different from itself except ` 1 ` and ` P - 1 ` , and so each pair multiplies to ` 1 ` , and ` 1 ` and ` P - 1 == -u 1 ` multiply to ` -u 1 ` , so the full product is equal to ` -u 1 ` . Here we make this precise by doing the product pair by pair. The induction hypothesis says that every subset ` S ` of ` 1 ... ( P - 1 ) ` that is closed under inverse (i.e. all pairs are matched up) and contains ` P - 1 ` multiplies to ` -u 1 mod P ` . Given such a set, we take out one element ` z =/= P - 1 ` . If there are no such elements, then ` S = { P - 1 } ` which forms the base case. Otherwise, ` S \ { z , z ^ -u 1 } ` is also closed under inverse and contains ` P - 1 ` , so the induction hypothesis says that this equals ` -u 1 ` ; and the remaining two elements are either equal to each other, in which case ~ wilthlem1 gives that ` z = 1 ` or ` P - 1 ` , and we've already excluded the second case, so the product gives ` 1 ` ; or ` z =/= z ^ -u 1 ` and their product is ` 1 ` . In either case the accumulated product is unaffected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem wilthlem2 {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (A2 P S T: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv ccnfld cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (wa (wcel (co P c1 cmin) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (co (co (cv y1) (co P c2 cmin) cexp) P cmo) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw (co c1 (co P c1 cmin) cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral s1 (wi (wpss (cv s1) S) (wceq (co (co T (cres cid (cv s1)) cgsu) P cmo) (co (cneg c1) P cmo))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co T (cres cid S) cgsu) P cmo) (co (cneg c1) P cmo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ wilth . Here we round out the argument of ~ wilthlem2 with the final step of the induction. The induction argument shows that every subset of ` 1 ... ( P - 1 ) ` that is closed under inverse and contains ` P - 1 ` multiplies to ` -u 1 mod P ` , and clearly ` 1 ... ( P - 1 ) ` itself is such a set. Thus, the product of all the elements is ` -u 1 ` , and all that is left is to translate the group sum notation (which we used for its unordered summing capabilities) into an ordered sequence to match the definition of the factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Jul-2019.)
theorem wilthlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 P T: class):
  $ wceq T (cfv ccnfld cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (wa (wcel (co P c1 cmin) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wcel (co (co (cv y1) (co P c2 cmin) cexp) P cmo) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw (co c1 (co P c1 cmin) cfz))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wbr P (co (cfv (co P c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) cdvds) $;

--| Wilson's theorem. A number is prime iff it is greater than or equal to ` 2 ` and ` ( N - 1 ) ! ` is congruent to ` -u 1 ` , ` mod N ` , or alternatively if ` N ` divides ` ( N - 1 ) ! + 1 ` . In this part of the proof we show the relatively simple reverse implication; see ~ wilthlem3 for the forward implication. This is Metamath 100 proof #51. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jan-2015.) (Proof shortened by Fan Zheng, 16-Jun-2016.)
theorem wilth (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cprime) (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr N (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) cdvds)) $;

--| The forward implication of Wilson's theorem ~ wilth (see ~ wilthlem3 ), expressed using the modulo operation: For any prime ` p ` we have ` ( p - 1 ) ! == - 1 ` (mod ` p ` ), see theorem 5.24 in [ApostolNT] p. 116. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2021.)
theorem wilthimp (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co (cfv (co P c1 cmin) cfa) P cmo) (co (cneg c1) P cmo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta : "growth lemma". There exists some ` r ` such that ` F ` is arbitrarily close in proportion to its dominant term. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftalem1 {x3 k r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2: class) (S: class x3 r) (T: class) (E: class x3 k) (F N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (csu k (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (cfv (cfv (cv k) A2) cabs)) E cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) cabs) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (co (cfv N A2) (co (cv x3) N cexp) cmul) cmin) cabs) (co E (co (cfv (cv x3) cabs) N cexp) cmul) clt)) cc) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta . There exists some ` r ` such that ` F ` has magnitude greater than ` F ( 0 ) ` outside the closed ball B(0,r). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftalem2 {x3 s1 r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2: class) (S: class x3 r) (T U: class s1) (F N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cif (wbr (cif (wbr c1 (cv s1) cle) (cv s1) c1) T cle) T (cif (wbr c1 (cv s1) cle) (cv s1) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) (co (cfv (cfv N A2) cabs) c2 cdiv) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv r) (cfv (cv x3) cabs) clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta . There exists a global minimum of the function ` abs o. F ` . The proof uses a circle of radius ` r ` where ` r ` is the value coming from ~ ftalem1 ; since this is a compact set, the minimum on this disk is achieved, and this must then be the global minimum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftalem3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1 z) (D: class y1) (R: class z) (S: class x3 y1 z) (F: class) (J: class y1) (N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) cabs) R cle) cc) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wi (wbr R (cfv (cv x3) cabs) clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) clt)) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) cle) cc) cc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta : Closure of the auxiliary variables for ~ ftalem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2020.)
theorem ftalem4 {k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class) (S T: class n) (U: class k n) (F K N X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv cc0 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (cinf (crab n (wne (cfv (cv n) A2) cc0) cn) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cneg (co (cfv cc0 F) (cfv K A2) cdiv)) (co c1 K cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) (co (csu k (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz) (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co T (cv k) cexp) cmul) cabs)) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq X (cif (wbr c1 U cle) c1 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wcel K cn) (wne (cfv K A2) cc0)) (w3a (wcel T cc) (wcel U crp) (wcel X crp))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta : Main proof. We have already shifted the minimum found in ~ ftalem3 to zero by a change of variables, and now we show that the minimum value is zero. Expanding in a series about the minimum value, let ` K ` be the lowest term in the polynomial that is nonzero, and let ` T ` be a ` K ` -th root of ` -u F ( 0 ) / A ( K ) ` . Then an evaluation of ` F ( T X ) ` where ` X ` is a sufficiently small positive number yields ` F ( 0 ) ` for the first term and ` -u F ( 0 ) x. X ^ K ` for the ` K ` -th term, and all higher terms are bounded because ` X ` is small. Thus, ` abs ( F ( T X ) ) <_ abs ( F ( 0 ) ) ( 1 - X ^ K ) ` ` < abs ( F ( 0 ) ) ` , in contradiction to our choice of ` F ( 0 ) ` as the minimum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.) (Revised by AV, 28-Sep-2020.)
theorem ftalem5 {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class) (S: class x3 n) (T: class n) (U: class k n) (F: class) (K: class x3) (N X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv cc0 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (cinf (crab n (wne (cfv (cv n) A2) cc0) cn) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cneg (co (cfv cc0 F) (cfv K A2) cdiv)) (co c1 K cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) (co (csu k (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz) (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (co T (cv k) cexp) cmul) cabs)) c1 caddc) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq X (cif (wbr c1 U cle) c1 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) clt) cc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta : Discharge the auxiliary variables in ~ ftalem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Sep-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Sep-2020.)
theorem ftalem6 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (S: class x3) (F N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv cc0 F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv cc0 F) cabs) clt) cc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fta . Shift the minimum away from zero by a change of variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Sep-2014.)
theorem ftalem7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (S: class x3) (F N X: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv F ccoe) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv F cdgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv S cply)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv X F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) cle) cc)) $;

--| The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra. Any polynomial with positive degree (i.e. non-constant) has a root. This is Metamath 100 proof #2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem fta {z: setvar} (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv S cply)) (wcel (cfv F cdgr) cn)) (wrex z (wceq (cfv (cv z) F) cc0) cc) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . Closure of the sequence of roots. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jul-2014.) Replace OLD theorem. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 18-Sep-2020.)
theorem basellem1 (K M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn) (wcel K (co c1 M cfz))) (wcel (co (co K cpi cmul) N cdiv) (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . Show that ` P ` is a polynomial of degree ` M ` , and compute its coefficient function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem2 {t j n: setvar} (P: class t j) (M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t cc (csu j (co cc0 M cfz) (co (co (co N (co c2 (cv j) cmul) cbc) (co (cneg c1) (co M (cv j) cmin) cexp) cmul) (co (cv t) (cv j) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv cc cply)) (wceq (cfv P cdgr) M) (wceq (cfv P ccoe) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co N (co c2 (cv n) cmul) cbc) (co (cneg c1) (co M (cv n) cmin) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . Using the binomial theorem and de Moivre's formula, we have the identity ` _e ^ _i N x / ( sin x ) ^ n = ` ` sum_ m e. ( 0 ... N ) ( N _C m ) ( _i ^ m ) ( cot x ) ^ ( N - m ) ` , so taking imaginary parts yields ` sin ( N x ) / ( sin x ) ^ N = ` ` sum_ j e. ( 0 ... M ) ( N _C 2 j ) ( -u 1 ) ^ ( M - j ) ` ` ( cot x ) ^ ( -u 2 j ) = P ( ( cot x ) ^ 2 ) ` , where ` N = 2 M + 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem3 {t j: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class t j) (M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t cc (csu j (co cc0 M cfz) (co (co (co N (co c2 (cv j) cmul) cbc) (co (cneg c1) (co M (cv j) cmin) cexp) cmul) (co (cv t) (cv j) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn) (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co cpi c2 cdiv) cioo))) (wceq (cfv (co (cfv A2 ctan) (cneg c2) cexp) P) (co (cfv (co N A2 cmul) csin) (co (cfv A2 csin) N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . By ~ basellem3 , the expression ` P ( ( cot x ) ^ 2 ) = sin ( N x ) / ( sin x ) ^ N ` goes to zero whenever ` x = n _pi / N ` for some ` n e. ( 1 ... M ) ` , so this function enumerates ` M ` distinct roots of a degree- ` M ` polynomial, which must therefore be all the roots by ~ fta1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem4 {t j n: setvar} (P: class t j) (T: class t j n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t cc (csu j (co cc0 M cfz) (co (co (co N (co c2 (cv j) cmul) cbc) (co (cneg c1) (co M (cv j) cmin) cexp) cmul) (co (cv t) (cv j) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n (co c1 M cfz) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) cpi cmul) N cdiv) ctan) (cneg c2) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wf1o (co c1 M cfz) (cima (ccnv P) (csn cc0)) T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . Using ~ vieta1 , we can calculate the sum of the roots of ` P ` as the quotient of the top two coefficients, and since the function ` T ` enumerates the roots, we are left with an equation that sums the ` cot ^ 2 ` function at the ` M ` different roots. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem5 {t j k n: setvar} (P: class t j) (T: class t j n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t cc (csu j (co cc0 M cfz) (co (co (co N (co c2 (cv j) cmul) cbc) (co (cneg c1) (co M (cv j) cmin) cexp) cmul) (co (cv t) (cv j) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n (co c1 M cfz) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) cpi cmul) N cdiv) ctan) (cneg c2) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wceq (csu k (co c1 M cfz) (co (cfv (co (co (cv k) cpi cmul) N cdiv) ctan) (cneg c2) cexp)) (co (co (co c2 M cmul) (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 cmin) cmul) c6 cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . The function ` G ` goes to zero because it is bounded by ` 1 / n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem6 {n: setvar} (G: class n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr G cc0 cli $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . The function ` 1 + A x. G ` for any fixed ` A ` goes to ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem7 {n: setvar} (A2 G: class n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wbr (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) (co (cxp cn (csn A2)) G (cof cmul)) (cof caddc)) c1 cli $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . The function ` F ` of partial sums of the inverse squares is bounded below by ` J ` and above by ` K ` , obtained by summing the inequality ` cot ^ 2 x <_ 1 / x ^ 2 <_ ` ` csc ^ 2 x = cot ^ 2 x + 1 ` over the ` M ` roots of the polynomial ` P ` , and applying the identity ~ basellem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem8 {n: setvar} (F: class) (G H: class n) (J: class) (K: class n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cseq caddc (cmpt n cn (co (cv n) (cneg c2) cexp)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cxp cn (csn (co (co cpi c2 cexp) c6 cdiv))) (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) G (cof cmin)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq J (co H (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) (co (cxp cn (csn (cneg c2))) G (cof cmul)) (cof caddc)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq K (co H (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) G (cof caddc)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co c2 M cmul) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wa (wbr (cfv M J) (cfv M F) cle) (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv M K) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ basel . Since by ~ basellem8 ` F ` is bounded by two expressions that tend to ` _pi ^ 2 / 6 ` , ` F ` must also go to ` _pi ^ 2 / 6 ` by the squeeze theorem ~ climsqz . But the series ` F ` is exactly the partial sums of ` k ^ -u 2 ` , so it follows that this is also the value of the infinite sum ` sum_ k e. NN ( k ^ -u 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jul-2014.)
theorem basellem9 {k n: setvar} (F: class) (G H: class n) (J: class) (K: class n):
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cseq caddc (cmpt n cn (co (cv n) (cneg c2) cexp)) c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cxp cn (csn (co (co cpi c2 cexp) c6 cdiv))) (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) G (cof cmin)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq J (co H (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) (co (cxp cn (csn (cneg c2))) G (cof cmul)) (cof caddc)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq K (co H (co (cxp cn (csn c1)) G (cof caddc)) (cof cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq (csu k cn (co (cv k) (cneg c2) cexp)) (co (co cpi c2 cexp) c6 cdiv) $;

--| The sum of the inverse squares is ` _pi ^ 2 / 6 ` . This is commonly known as the Basel problem, with the first known proof attributed to Euler. See ~ http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basel_problem . This particular proof approach is due to Cauchy (1821). This is Metamath 100 proof #14. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jul-2014.)
theorem basel {k: setvar}:
  $ wceq (csu k cn (co (cv k) (cneg c2) cexp)) (co (co cpi c2 cexp) c6 cdiv) $;

--| Extend class notation with the first Chebyshev function.
term ccht: class;

--| Extend class notation with the von Mangoldt function.
term cvma: class;

--| Extend class notation with the second Chebyshev function.
term cchp: class;

--| Extend class notation with the prime-counting function pi.
term cppi: class;

--| Extend class notation with the M&ouml;bius function.
term cmu: class;

--| Extend class notation with the divisor function.
term csgm: class;

--| Define the first Chebyshev function, which adds up the logarithms of all primes less than ` x ` , see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 75. The symbol used to represent this function is sometimes the variant greek letter theta shown here and sometimes the greek letter psi, &psi;; however, this notation can also refer to the second Chebyshev function, which adds up the logarithms of prime powers instead, see ~ df-chp . See ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev_function for a discussion of the two functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
axiom df_cht {x3 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccht (cmpt x3 cr (csu p (cin (co cc0 (cv x3) cicc) cprime) (cfv (cv p) clog))) $;

--| Define the von Mangoldt function, which gives the logarithm of the prime at a prime power, and is zero elsewhere, see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 32. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
axiom df_vma {x3 s1 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cvma (cmpt x3 cn (csb s1 (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv x3) cdvds) cprime) (cif (wceq (cfv (cv s1) chash) c1) (cfv (cuni (cv s1)) clog) cc0))) $;

--| Define the second Chebyshev function, which adds up the logarithms of the primes corresponding to the prime powers less than ` x ` , see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 75. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
axiom df_chp {x3 n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cchp (cmpt x3 cr (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (cfv (cv n) cvma))) $;

--| Define the prime &pi; function, which counts the number of primes less than or equal to ` x ` , see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 8. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
axiom df_ppi {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cppi (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (cin (co cc0 (cv x3) cicc) cprime) chash)) $;

--| Define the M&ouml;bius function, which is zero for non-squarefree numbers and is ` -u 1 ` or ` 1 ` for squarefree numbers according as to the number of prime divisors of the number is even or odd, see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 24. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
axiom df_mu {x3 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmu (cmpt x3 cn (cif (wrex p (wbr (co (cv p) c2 cexp) (cv x3) cdvds) cprime) cc0 (co (cneg c1) (cfv (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv x3) cdvds) cprime) chash) cexp))) $;

--| Define the sum of positive divisors function ` ( x sigma n ) ` , which is the sum of the xth powers of the positive integer divisors of n, see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 38. For ` x = 0 ` , ` ( x sigma n ) ` counts the number of divisors of ` n ` , i.e. ` ( 0 sigma n ) ` is _the_ divisor function, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 38. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
axiom df_sgm {x3 k n p: setvar}:
  $ wceq csgm (cmpo x3 n cc cn (csu k (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv n) cdvds) cn) (co (cv k) (cv x3) ccxp))) $;

--| Finite sum closure in the log-integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem efnnfsumcl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cfv B2 ce) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (csu k A2 B2) ce) cn) $;

--| The set of primes less than ` A ` expressed using a finite set of integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppisval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) (cin (co c2 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) cprime)) $;

--| The set of primes less than ` A ` expressed using a finite set of integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppisval2 (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel c2 (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) (cin (co M (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) cprime)) $;

--| The set of primes less than ` A ` is a finite set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppifi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) cfn) $;

--| The set of prime divisors of a number is a finite set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem prmdvdsfi {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (crab p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cprime) cfn) $;

--| Domain and codoamin of the Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtf:
  $ wf cr cr ccht $;

--| Real closure of the Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 ccht) cr) $;

--| Value of the Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtval {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 ccht) (csu p (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) (cfv (cv p) clog))) $;

--| The Chebyshev function is closed in the log-integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem efchtcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 ccht) ce) cn) $;

--| The Chebyshev function is always positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 ccht) cle) $;

--| Value of the von Mangoldt function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmaval {p: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class p):
  $ wceq S (crab p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cprime) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (cfv A2 cvma) (cif (wceq (cfv S chash) c1) (cfv (cuni S) clog) cc0)) $;

--| Two ways to say that ` A ` is a prime power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem isppw {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wne (cfv A2 cvma) cc0) (wreu p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cprime)) $;

--| Two ways to say that ` A ` is a prime power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem isppw2 {k p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wne (cfv A2 cvma) cc0) (wrex p (wrex k (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv k) cexp)) cn) cprime)) $;

--| Value of the von Mangoldt function at a prime power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmappw (P K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel K cn)) (wceq (cfv (co P K cexp) cvma) (cfv P clog)) $;

--| Value of the von Mangoldt function at a prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmaprm (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wceq (cfv P cvma) (cfv P clog)) $;

--| Closure for the von Mangoldt function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmacl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (cfv A2 cvma) cr) $;

--| Functionality of the von Mangoldt function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmaf:
  $ wf cn cr cvma $;

--| The von Mangoldt is closed in the log-integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem efvmacl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 cvma) ce) cn) $;

--| The von Mangoldt function is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmage0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cvma) cle) $;

--| Value of the second Chebyshev function, or summary von Mangoldt function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpval {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cchp) (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (cfv (cv n) cvma))) $;

--| Functionality of the second Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpf:
  $ wf cr cr cchp $;

--| Closure for the second Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 cchp) cr) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function is closed in the log-integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem efchpcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 cchp) ce) cn) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cchp) cle) $;

--| Value of the prime-counting function pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppival (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cppi) (cfv (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) chash)) $;

--| Value of the prime-counting function pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppival2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (cfv A2 cppi) (cfv (cin (co c2 A2 cfz) cprime) chash)) $;

--| Value of the prime-counting function pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppival2g (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel c2 (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv A2 cppi) (cfv (cin (co M A2 cfz) cprime) chash)) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the prime-counting function pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppif:
  $ wf cr cn0 cppi $;

--| Real closure of the prime-counting function pi. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppicl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cfv A2 cppi) cn0) $;

--| The value of the M&ouml;bius function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem muval {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (cfv A2 cmu) (cif (wrex p (wbr (co (cv p) c2 cexp) A2 cdvds) cprime) cc0 (co (cneg c1) (cfv (crab p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cprime) chash) cexp))) $;

--| The value of the M&ouml;bius function at a non-squarefree number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem muval1 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel P (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr (co P c2 cexp) A2 cdvds)) (wceq (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) $;

--| The value of the M&ouml;bius function at a squarefree number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.)
theorem muval2 {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0)) (wceq (cfv A2 cmu) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (crab p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cprime) chash) cexp)) $;

--| Two ways to say that a number is not squarefree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.)
theorem isnsqf {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) (wrex p (wbr (co (cv p) c2 cexp) A2 cdvds) cprime)) $;

--| Two ways to say that a number is squarefree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.)
theorem issqf {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wb (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) (wral p (wbr (co (cv p) A2 cpc) c1 cle) cprime)) $;

--| The prime count of a squarefree number is at most 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem sqfpc (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) (wcel P cprime)) (wbr (co P A2 cpc) c1 cle) $;

--| A divisor of a squarefree number is squarefree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem dvdssqf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wbr B2 A2 cdvds)) (wi (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) (wne (cfv B2 cmu) cc0)) $;

--| A squarefree number is completely determined by the set of its prime divisors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem sqf11 {p: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cn) (wne (cfv B2 cmu) cc0))) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wral p (wb (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) (wbr (cv p) B2 cdvds)) cprime)) $;

--| The M&ouml;bius function is a function into the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem muf:
  $ wf cn cz cmu $;

--| Closure of the M&ouml;bius function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem mucl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (cfv A2 cmu) cz) $;

--| The value of the divisor function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem sgmval {k p: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (co A2 B2 csgm) (csu k (crab p (wbr (cv p) B2 cdvds) cn) (co (cv k) A2 ccxp))) $;

--| The value of the divisor function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem sgmval2 {k p: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (co A2 B2 csgm) (csu k (crab p (wbr (cv p) B2 cdvds) cn) (co (cv k) A2 cexp))) $;

--| The value of the sum-of-divisors function, usually denoted &sigma;<SUB>0</SUB>(<i>n</i>). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem _0sgm {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (co cc0 A2 csgm) (cfv (crab p (wbr (cv p) A2 cdvds) cn) chash)) $;

--| The divisor function is a function into the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem sgmf:
  $ wf (cxp cc cn) cc csgm $;

--| Closure of the divisor function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem sgmcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 csgm) cc) $;

--| Closure of the divisor function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jun-2015.)
theorem sgmnncl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 csgm) cn) $;

--| The M&ouml;bius function takes on values in magnitude at most ` 1 ` . (Together with ~ mucl , this implies that it takes a value in ` { -u 1 , 0 , 1 } ` for every positive integer.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem mule1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 cmu) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| The Chebyshev function does not change off the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtfl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) ccht) (cfv A2 ccht)) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function does not change off the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpfl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cchp) (cfv A2 cchp)) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` at a prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppiprm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cprime)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cppi) (co (cfv A2 cppi) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` at a non-prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppinprm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wn (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cprime))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cppi) (cfv A2 cppi)) $;

--| The Chebyshev function at a prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtprm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cprime)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) ccht) (co (cfv A2 ccht) (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) clog) caddc)) $;

--| The Chebyshev function at a non-prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtnprm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wn (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) cprime))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) ccht) (cfv A2 ccht)) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function at a successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cchp) (co (cfv A2 cchp) (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cvma) caddc)) $;

--| The Chebyshev function is weakly increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtwordi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 ccht) (cfv B2 ccht) cle) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function is weakly increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpwordi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 cchp) (cfv B2 cchp) cle) $;

--| The difference of the Chebyshev function at two points sums the logarithms of the primes in an interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtdif {p: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co (cfv N ccht) (cfv M ccht) cmin) (csu p (cin (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz) cprime) (cfv (cv p) clog))) $;

--| The exponentiated Chebyshev function forms a divisibility chain between any two points. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem efchtdvds (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 ccht) ce) (cfv (cfv B2 ccht) ce) cdvds) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` does not change off the integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppifl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cppi) (cfv A2 cppi)) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` cannot locally increase faster than the identity function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppip1le (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cppi) (co (cfv A2 cppi) c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` is weakly increasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppiwordi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 cppi) (cfv B2 cppi) cle) $;

--| The difference of the prime-counting function ` ppi ` at two points counts the number of primes in an interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppidif (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co (cfv N cppi) (cfv M cppi) cmin) (cfv (cin (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz) cprime) chash)) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` at ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppi1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cppi) cc0 $;

--| The Chebyshev function at ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem cht1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 ccht) cc0 $;

--| The von Mangoldt function at ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem vma1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cvma) cc0 $;

--| The second Chebyshev function at ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem chp1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cchp) cc0 $;

--| Inference form of ~ ppiprm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppi1i (K M N: class):
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq N (co M c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cppi) K $ >
  $ wcel N cprime $ >
  $ wceq (cfv N cppi) (co K c1 caddc) $;

--| Inference form of ~ ppinprm . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppi2i (K M N: class):
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq N (co M c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cppi) K $ >
  $ wn (wcel N cprime) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv N cppi) K $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` at ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppi2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cppi) c1 $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` at ` 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppi3:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 cppi) c2 $;

--| The Chebyshev function at ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem cht2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 ccht) (cfv c2 clog) $;

--| The Chebyshev function at ` 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem cht3:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 ccht) (cfv c6 clog) $;

--| Closure of the prime-counting function ` ppi ` in the positive integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppinncl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c2 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 cppi) cn) $;

--| Closure of the Chebyshev function in the positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtrpcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c2 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 ccht) crp) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` is zero iff its argument is less than ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppieq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cppi) cc0) (wbr A2 c2 clt)) $;

--| The prime-counting function ` ppi ` is strictly less than the identity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem ppiltx (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cfv A2 cppi) A2 clt) $;

--| Relate the primorial (product of the primes up to ` A ` ) to the Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem prmorcht {n: setvar} (A2 F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (cv n) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccht) ce) (cfv A2 (cseq cmul F c1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mumul . A multiple of a non-squarefree number is non-squarefree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.)
theorem mumullem1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (cfv A2 cmu) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cmu) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mumul . The product of two coprime squarefree numbers is squarefree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.)
theorem mumullem2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wceq (co A2 B2 cgcd) c1)) (wa (wne (cfv A2 cmu) cc0) (wne (cfv B2 cmu) cc0))) (wne (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cmu) cc0) $;

--| The M&ouml;bius function is a multiplicative function. This is one of the primary interests of the M&ouml;bius function as an arithmetic function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Timothy J. O'Malley, 9-Oct-2026.)
theorem mumul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wceq (co A2 B2 cgcd) c1)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cmu) (co (cfv A2 cmu) (cfv B2 cmu) cmul)) $;

--| There is a bijection from the squarefree divisors of a number ` N ` to the powerset of the prime divisors of ` N ` . Among other things, this implies that a number has ` 2 ^ k ` squarefree divisors where ` k ` is the number of prime divisors, and a squarefree number has ` 2 ^ k ` divisors (because all divisors of a squarefree number are squarefree). The inverse function to ` F ` takes the product of all the primes in some subset of prime divisors of ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jul-2015.)
theorem sqff1o {x3 n p: setvar} (S: class x3) (F: class x3 n p) (G N: class):
  $ wceq S (crab x3 (wa (wne (cfv (cv x3) cmu) cc0) (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n S (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv n) cdvds) cprime)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (cmpt p cprime (co (cv p) (cv n) cpc))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wf1o S (cpw (crab p (wbr (cv p) N cdvds) cprime)) F) $;

--| A "diagonal commutation" of divisor sums analogous to ~ fsum0diag . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem fsumdvdsdiaglem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (j k: setvar) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv j) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn)) (wcel (cv k) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv j) cdiv) cdvds) cn))) (wa (wcel (cv k) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn)) (wcel (cv j) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv k) cdiv) cdvds) cn)))) $;

--| A "diagonal commutation" of divisor sums analogous to ~ fsum0diag . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem fsumdvdsdiag {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 j k) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn)) (wcel (cv k) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv j) cdiv) cdvds) cn)))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu j (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) (csu k (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv j) cdiv) cdvds) cn) A2)) (csu k (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) (csu j (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv k) cdiv) cdvds) cn) A2))) $;

--| A double commutation of divisor sums based on ~ fsumdvdsdiag . Note that ` A ` depends on both ` j ` and ` k ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-May-2016.)
theorem fsumdvdscom {x3 j k m: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 j k) (B2: class x3 k m) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) (cv m) cmul)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn)) (wcel (cv k) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv j) cdvds) cn)))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu j (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) (csu k (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv j) cdvds) cn) A2)) (csu k (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) (csu m (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co N (cv k) cdiv) cdvds) cn) B2))) $;

--| A bijection between the divisors of a prime power and the integers less than or equal to the exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2016.)
theorem dvdsppwf1o {x3 n: setvar} (A2 P: class) (F: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n (co cc0 A2 cfz) (co P (cv n) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel A2 cn0)) (wf1o (co cc0 A2 cfz) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co P A2 cexp) cdvds) cn) F) $;

--| A bijection from the numbers less than ` N / A ` to the multiples of ` A ` less than ` N ` . Useful for some sum manipulations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2016.)
theorem dvdsflf1o {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (F: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n (co c1 (cfv (co A2 N cdiv) cfl) cfz) (co N (cv n) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co c1 (cfv (co A2 N cdiv) cfl) cfz) (crab x3 (wbr N (cv x3) cdvds) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) F) $;

--| A sum commutation from ` sum_ n <_ A , sum_ d || n , B ( n , d ) ` to ` sum_ d <_ A , sum_ m <_ A / d , B ( n , d m ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dvdsflsumcom {x3 m n d: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 n d) (C: class x3 m d):
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (co (cv d) (cv m) cmul)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) (wcel (cv d) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv n) cdvds) cn)))) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (csu d (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv n) cdvds) cn) B2)) (csu d (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (csu m (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) cfl) cfz) C))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fsumfldivdiag . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-May-2016.)
theorem fsumfldivdiaglem (ph: wff) (A2: class) (m n: setvar):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) (wcel (cv m) (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) cfl) cfz))) (wa (wcel (cv m) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) cfl) cfz)))) $;

--| The right-hand side of ~ dvdsflsumcom is commutative in the variables, because it can be written as the manifestly symmetric sum over those ` <. m , n >. ` such that ` m x. n <_ A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem fsumfldivdiag {m n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class m n):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv n) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) (wcel (cv m) (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) cfl) cfz)))) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (csu m (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) cfl) cfz) B2)) (csu m (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (csu n (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv m) cdiv) cfl) cfz) B2))) $;

--| The sum of the M&ouml;bius function over the divisors of ` N ` gives one if ` N = 1 ` , but otherwise always sums to zero. Theorem 2.1 in [ApostolNT] p. 25. This makes the M&ouml;bius function useful for inverting divisor sums; see also ~ muinv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.)
theorem musum {k n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (csu k (crab n (wbr (cv n) N cdvds) cn) (cfv (cv k) cmu)) (cif (wceq N c1) c1 cc0)) $;

--| Evaluate a collapsing sum over the M&ouml;bius function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem musumsum {k m n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class n) (B2: class m n) (C: class k n):
  $ wi (wceq (cv m) c1) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c1 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu m A2 (csu k (crab n (wbr (cv n) (cv m) cdvds) cn) (co (cfv (cv k) cmu) B2 cmul))) C) $;

--| The M&ouml;bius inversion formula. If ` G ( n ) = sum_ k || n F ( k ) ` for every ` n e. NN ` , then ` F ( n ) = sum_ k || n ` ` mmu ( k ) G ( n / k ) = sum_ k || n mmu ( n / k ) G ( k ) ` , i.e. the M&ouml;bius function is the Dirichlet convolution inverse of the constant function ` 1 ` . Theorem 2.9 in [ApostolNT] p. 32. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.)
theorem muinv {x3 j k m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (F: class x3) (G: class x3 j k m n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpt n cn (csu k (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv n) cdvds) cn) (cfv (cv k) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt m cn (csu j (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cv m) cdvds) cn) (co (cfv (cv j) cmu) (cfv (co (cv m) (cv j) cdiv) G) cmul)))) $;

--| If ` M ` and ` N ` are two coprime integers, multiplication forms a bijection from the set of pairs ` <. j , k >. ` where ` j || M ` and ` k || N ` , to the set of divisors of ` M x. N ` . Version of ~ dvdsmulf1o using maps-to notation, which does not require ~ ax-mulf . (Contributed by GG, 18-Apr-2025.)
theorem mpodvdsmulf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (M N: class) (X Y Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wceq X (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) M cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co M N cmul) cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp X Y) Z (cres (cmpo x3 y1 cc cc (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul)) (cxp X Y))) $;

--| Product of two divisor sums. (This is also the main part of the proof that " ` sum_ k || N F ( k ) ` is a multiplicative function if ` F ` is".) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 18-Apr-2025.)
theorem fsumdvdsmul {x3 i j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 i j) (B2: class x3 i k) (C: class x3 i) (D: class x3 j k) (M N: class i j k) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wceq X (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) M cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co M N cmul) cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Y)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) X) (wcel (cv k) Y))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv i) (co (cv j) (cv k) cmul)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csu j X A2) (csu k Y B2) cmul) (csu i Z C)) $;

--| If ` M ` and ` N ` are two coprime integers, multiplication forms a bijection from the set of pairs ` <. j , k >. ` where ` j || M ` and ` k || N ` , to the set of divisors of ` M x. N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.)
theorem dvdsmulf1o {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (M N: class) (X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wceq X (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) M cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) N cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co M N cmul) cdvds) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cxp X Y) Z (cres cmul (cxp X Y))) $;

--| The value of the divisor function at a prime power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem sgmppw {k: setvar} (A2 P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel P cprime) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co P N cexp) csgm) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co P A2 ccxp) (cv k) cexp))) $;

--| A prime power ` P ^ K ` has ` K + 1 ` divisors. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem _0sgmppw (P K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel K cn0)) (wceq (co cc0 (co P K cexp) csgm) (co K c1 caddc)) $;

--| The sum of divisors for a prime is ` P + 1 ` because the only divisors are ` 1 ` and ` P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem _1sgmprm (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co c1 P csgm) (co P c1 caddc)) $;

--| The sum of the divisors of ` 2 ^ ( N - 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem _1sgm2ppw (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co c1 (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) csgm) (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin)) $;

--| The divisor function for fixed parameter ` A ` is a multiplicative function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2015.)
theorem sgmmul (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn) (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1))) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) csgm) (co (co A2 M csgm) (co A2 N csgm) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ppiub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.)
theorem ppiublem1 (P M N: class):
  $ wa (wbr N c6 cle) (wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wbr c4 P cle)) (wi (wcel (co P c6 cmo) (co N c5 cfz)) (wcel (co P c6 cmo) (cpr c1 c5)))) $ >
  $ wcel M cn0 $ >
  $ wceq N (co M c1 caddc) $ >
  $ w3o (wbr c2 M cdvds) (wbr c3 M cdvds) (wcel M (cpr c1 c5)) $ >
  $ wa (wbr M c6 cle) (wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wbr c4 P cle)) (wi (wcel (co P c6 cmo) (co M c5 cfz)) (wcel (co P c6 cmo) (cpr c1 c5)))) $;

--| A prime greater than ` 3 ` does not divide ` 2 ` or ` 3 ` , so its residue ` mod 6 ` is ` 1 ` or ` 5 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.)
theorem ppiublem2 (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wbr c4 P cle)) (wcel (co P c6 cmo) (cpr c1 c5)) $;

--| An upper bound on the prime-counting function ` ppi ` , which counts the number of primes less than ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem ppiub (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cr) (wbr cc0 N cle)) (wbr (cfv N cppi) (co (co N c3 cdiv) c2 caddc) cle) $;

--| The von Mangoldt function is less than the natural log. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmalelog (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr (cfv A2 cvma) (cfv A2 clog) cle) $;

--| The first Chebyshev function is less than the second. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chtlepsi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv A2 ccht) (cfv A2 cchp) cle) $;

--| Closure of the second Chebyshev function in the positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem chprpcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c2 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 cchp) crp) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function is zero iff its argument is less than ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpeq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cchp) cc0) (wbr A2 c2 clt)) $;

--| The first Chebyshev function is zero iff its argument is less than ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Apr-2016.)
theorem chteq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccht) cc0) (wbr A2 c2 clt)) $;

--| Upper bound on the ` theta ` function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtleppi (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (cfv A2 ccht) (co (cfv A2 cppi) (cfv A2 clog) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ chtub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem chtublem (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 cmin) ccht) (co (cfv N ccht) (co (cfv c4 clog) (co N c1 cmin) cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| An upper bound on the Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.) (Revised 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtub (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cr) (wbr c2 N clt)) (wbr (cfv N ccht) (co (cfv c2 clog) (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cmin) cmul) clt) $;

--| Rewrite a sum over the von Mangoldt function as a sum over prime powers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Apr-2016.)
theorem fsumvma {x3 k p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class k p) (P: class) (K: class p):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv p) (cv k) cexp)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel (cv p) P) (wcel (cv k) K)) (wa (wa (wcel (cv p) cprime) (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel (co (cv p) (cv k) cexp) A2))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cvma) cc0))) (wceq B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 A2 B2) (csu p P (csu k K C))) $;

--| Apply ~ fsumvma for the common case of all numbers less than a real number ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2016.)
theorem fsumvma2 {x3 k p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class k p):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv p) (cv k) cexp)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz))) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cvma) cc0))) (wceq B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) B2) (csu p (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) (csu k (co c1 (cfv (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv (cv p) clog) cdiv) cfl) cfz) C))) $;

--| The logarithmic analogue of ~ pcprod . The sum of the logarithms of the primes dividing ` A ` multiplied by their powers yields the logarithm of ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Apr-2016.)
theorem pclogsum {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (csu p (cin (co c1 A2 cfz) cprime) (co (co (cv p) A2 cpc) (cfv (cv p) clog) cmul)) (cfv A2 clog)) $;

--| The sum of the von Mangoldt function over the divisors of ` n ` . Equation 9.2.4 of [Shapiro], p. 328 and theorem 2.10 in [ApostolNT] p. 32. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmasum {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (csu n (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cdvds) cn) (cfv (cv n) cvma)) (cfv A2 clog)) $;

--| Another expression for the logarithm of a factorial, in terms of the von Mangoldt function. Equation 9.2.7 of [Shapiro], p. 329. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Apr-2016.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2016.)
theorem logfac2 {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cfa) clog) (csu k (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) cvma) (cfv (co A2 (cv k) cdiv) cfl) cmul))) $;

--| Express the second Chebyshev function directly as a sum over the primes less than ` A ` (instead of indirectly through the von Mangoldt function). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpval2 {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cchp) (csu p (cin (co cc0 A2 cicc) cprime) (co (cfv (cv p) clog) (cfv (co (cfv A2 clog) (cfv (cv p) clog) cdiv) cfl) cmul))) $;

--| The second Chebyshev function is the sum of the theta function at arguments quickly approaching zero. (This is usually stated as an infinite sum, but after a certain point, the terms are all zero, and it is easier for us to use an explicit finite sum.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpchtsum {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cchp) (csu k (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (cfv (co A2 (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) ccxp) ccht))) $;

--| An upper bound on the second Chebyshev function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpub (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 cchp) (co (cfv A2 ccht) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv A2 clog) cmul) caddc) cle) $;

--| A simple upper bound on the logarithm of a factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2016.)
theorem logfacubnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cfa) clog) (co A2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul) cle) $;

--| A lower bound on the logarithm of a factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2016.)
theorem logfaclbnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (co A2 (co (cfv A2 clog) c2 cmin) cmul) (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cfa) clog) cle) $;

--| Show the stronger statement ` log ( x ! ) = x log x - x + O ( log x ) ` alluded to in ~ logfacrlim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-May-2016.)
theorem logfacbnd3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cfa) clog) (co A2 (co (cfv A2 clog) c1 cmin) cmul) cmin) cabs) (co (cfv A2 clog) c1 caddc) cle) $;

--| Combine the estimates ~ logfacubnd and ~ logfaclbnd , to get ` log ( x ! ) = x log x + O ( x ) ` . Equation 9.2.9 of [Shapiro], p. 329. This is a weak form of the even stronger statement, ` log ( x ! ) = x log x - x + O ( log x ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2016.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2016.)
theorem logfacrlim {x3: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) clog) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfa) clog) (cv x3) cdiv) cmin)) c1 crli $;

--| The sum ` sum_ n <_ x , log ^ N ( x / n ) ` has the asymptotic expansion ` ( N ! ) x + o ( x ) ` . (More precisely, the omitted term has order ` O ( log ^ N ( x ) / x ) ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-May-2016.)
theorem logexprlim {x3 n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv n) cdiv) clog) N cexp)) (cv x3) cdiv)) (cfv N cfa) crli) $;

--| Write out ~ logfacrlim as a sum of logs. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-May-2016.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-May-2016.)
theorem logfacrlim2 {x3 n: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt x3 crp (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv n) cdiv) clog) (cv x3) cdiv))) c1 crli $;

--| A Mersenne prime is a prime number of the form ` 2 ^ P - 1 ` . This theorem shows that the ` P ` in this expression is necessarily also prime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem mersenne (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cz) (wcel (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cprime)) (wcel P cprime) $;

--| Euclid's contribution to the Euclid-Euler theorem. A number of the form ` 2 ^ ( p - 1 ) x. ( 2 ^ p - 1 ) ` is a perfect number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem perfect1 (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cz) (wcel (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cprime)) (wceq (co c1 (co (co c2 (co P c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cmul) csgm) (co (co c2 P cexp) (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ perfect . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem perfectlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 B2 cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) csgm) (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) cn) (wcel (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin) cn) (wcel (co B2 (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin) cdiv) cn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ perfect . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 17-Sep-2020.)
theorem perfectlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 B2 cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) csgm) (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel B2 cprime) (wceq B2 (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin))) $;

--| The Euclid-Euler theorem, or Perfect Number theorem. A positive even integer ` N ` is a perfect number (that is, its divisor sum is ` 2 N ` ) if and only if it is of the form ` 2 ^ ( p - 1 ) x. ( 2 ^ p - 1 ) ` , where ` 2 ^ p - 1 ` is prime (a Mersenne prime), and therefore ` p ` is also prime, see ~ mersenne . This is Metamath 100 proof #70. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.)
theorem perfect {p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wbr c2 N cdvds)) (wb (wceq (co c1 N csgm) (co c2 N cmul)) (wrex p (wa (wcel (co (co c2 (cv p) cexp) c1 cmin) cprime) (wceq N (co (co c2 (co (cv p) c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv p) cexp) c1 cmin) cmul))) cz)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the group of Dirichlet characters.
term cdchr: class;

--| The group of Dirichlet characters ` mod n ` is the set of monoid homomorphisms from ` ZZ / n ZZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of the complex numbers, equipped with the group operation of pointwise multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
axiom df_dchr {x3 z n b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdchr (cmpt n cn (csb z (cfv (cv n) czn) (csb b (crab x3 (wss (cxp (cdif (cfv (cv z) cbs) (cfv (cv z) cui)) (csn cc0)) (cv x3)) (co (cfv (cv z) cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm)) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cres (cof cmul) (cxp (cv b) (cv b)))))))) $;

--| Value of the group of Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3) (U: class) (G: class x3) (N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (crab x3 (wss (cxp (cdif B2 U) (csn cc0)) (cv x3)) (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) D) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cres (cof cmul) (cxp D D))))) $;

--| Base set of the group of Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrbas {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3) (U: class) (G: class x3) (N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (crab x3 (wss (cxp (cdif B2 U) (csn cc0)) (cv x3)) (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm))) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism from the multiplicative monoid on ` Z/nZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of ` CC ` , which is zero off the group of units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrelbas (ph: wff) (B2 D U G N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X D) (wa (wcel X (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm)) (wss (cxp (cdif B2 U) (csn cc0)) X))) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism from the multiplicative monoid on ` Z/nZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of ` CC ` , which is zero off the group of units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrelbas2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3) (U: class) (G: class x3) (N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X D) (wa (wcel X (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm)) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cfv (cv x3) X) cc0) (wcel (cv x3) U)) B2))) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism from the multiplicative monoid on ` Z/nZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of ` CC ` , which is zero off the group of units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrelbas3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3 y1) (U: class) (G: class x3 y1) (N: class y1) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X D) (wa (wf B2 cc X) (w3a (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv Z cmulr)) X) (co (cfv (cv x3) X) (cfv (cv y1) X) cmul)) U) U) (wceq (cfv (cfv Z cur) X) c1) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cfv (cv x3) X) cc0) (wcel (cv x3) U)) B2)))) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism from the multiplicative monoid on ` Z/nZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of ` CC ` , which is zero off the group of units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrelbasd {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class x3 y1 k) (U: class) (E: class x3 y1) (G: class x3 y1 k) (N: class y1 k) (X: class k) (Y: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv x3)) (wceq X A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv y1)) (wceq X C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv Z cmulr))) (wceq X E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cfv Z cur)) (wceq X Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) U)) (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) U) (wcel (cv y1) U))) (wceq E (co A2 C cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Y c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt k B2 (cif (wcel (cv k) U) X cc0)) D) $;

--| Reverse closure for a Dirichlet character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2016.)
theorem dchrrcl (D G N X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X D) (wcel N cn) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrmhm (D G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wss D (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrf (ph: wff) (B2 D G N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cc X) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is a monoid homomorphism from the multiplicative monoid on ` Z/nZ ` to the multiplicative monoid of ` CC ` , which is zero off the group of units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrelbas4 {x3: setvar} (D: class) (G: class x3) (L N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X D) (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel X (co (cfv Z cmgp) (cfv ccnfld cmgp) cmhm)) (wral x3 (wi (wbr c1 (co (cv x3) N cgcd) clt) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) L) X) cc0)) cz)) $;

--| Value of a Dirichlet character at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrzrh1 (ph: wff) (D G L N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv c1 L) X) c1) $;

--| A Dirichlet character takes values in the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2016.)
theorem dchrzrhcl (ph: wff) (A2 D G L N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 L) X) cc) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is completely multiplicative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrzrhmul (ph: wff) (A2 C D G L N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (co A2 C cmul) L) X) (co (cfv (cfv A2 L) X) (cfv (cfv C L) X) cmul)) $;

--| Group operation on the group of Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrplusg (ph: wff) (D c_x G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cres (cof cmul) (cxp D D))) $;

--| Group operation on the group of Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrmul (ph: wff) (D c_x G N X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_x) (co X Y (cof cmul))) $;

--| Closure of the group operation on Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrmulcl (ph: wff) (D c_x G N X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_x) D) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is nonzero on the units of ` Z/nZ ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrn0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D U G N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wne (cfv A2 X) cc0) (wcel A2 U)) $;

--| Closure of the principal Dirichlet character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchr1cl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class k) (U: class) (c_1 G: class k) (N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cmpt k B2 (cif (wcel (cv k) U) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_1 D) $;

--| Left identity for the principal Dirichlet character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrmullid {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D c_x: class k) (U: class) (c_1 G: class k) (N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cmpt k B2 (cif (wcel (cv k) U) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c_1 X c_x) X) $;

--| Closure of the group inverse operation on Dirichlet characters. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrinvcl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D c_x: class k) (U: class) (c_1 G K: class k) (N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cmpt k B2 (cif (wcel (cv k) U) c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt k B2 (cif (wcel (cv k) U) (co c1 (cfv (cv k) X) cdiv) cc0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K D) (wceq (co K X c_x) c_1)) $;

--| The set of Dirichlet characters is an Abelian group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrabl (G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel G cabl) $;

--| The group of Dirichlet characters is a finite group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrfi (D G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel D cfn) $;

--| A Dirichlet character restricted to the unit group of ` Z/nZ ` is a group homomorphism into the multiplicative group of nonzero complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrghm (ph: wff) (D U G H M N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cfv Z cmgp) U cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) cress) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres X U) (co H M cghm)) $;

--| Value of the principal Dirichlet character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchr1 (ph: wff) (A2 U c_1 G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 c_1) c1) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is determined by its values on the unit group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchreq {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class k) (U: class) (G N: class k) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq X Y) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) X) (cfv (cv k) Y)) U)) $;

--| A Dirichlet character is determined by its values on the unit group. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrresb (ph: wff) (D U G N X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cres X U) (cres Y U)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| A Dirichlet character takes values on the unit circle. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrabs (ph: wff) (A2 D U G N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 X) cabs) c1) $;

--| The inverse of a Dirichlet character is the conjugate (which is also the multiplicative inverse, because the values of ` X ` are unimodular). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrinv (ph: wff) (D G I N X: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X I) (ccom ccj X)) $;

--| A Dirichlet character takes values inside the unit circle. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2016.)
theorem dchrabs2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D G N X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 X) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| The principal Dirichlet character is a real character. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchr1re (ph: wff) (B2 c_1 G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cr c_1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrpt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrptlem1 {u h k m n: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2: class) (B2: class u h k m n) (C: class k n) (D: class u h k m n) (P: class k n) (S: class) (T: class k n) (c_x: class) (U: class k n) (c_1: class u) (G: class u h k m n) (H: class) (I: class n) (M: class u k n) (N O: class u h k m n) (W: class) (X: class u h k m n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c_1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cfv Z cmgp) U cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k (cdm W) (crn (cmpt n cz (co (cv n) (cfv (cv k) W) c_x)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H S (cdm cdprd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co H S cdprd) U) $ >
  $ wceq P (co H S cdpj) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv H cod) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cneg c1) (co c2 (cfv (cfv I W) O) cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 (cfv I P)) c_1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpt u U (cio h (wrex m (wa (wceq (cfv (cv u) (cfv I P)) (co (cv m) (cfv I W) c_x)) (wceq (cv h) (co T (cv m) cexp))) cz))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel C U)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wceq (cfv C (cfv I P)) (co M (cfv I W) c_x)))) (wceq (cfv C X) (co T M cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrpt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrptlem2 {x3 u h k m n: setvar} (ph: wff u) (A2: class) (B2 D: class u h k m n) (P: class x3 k n) (S: class) (T: class x3 k n) (c_x: class) (U: class k n) (c_1: class u) (G: class u h k m n) (H: class) (I: class x3 n) (N: class u h k m n) (O: class x3 u h k m n) (W: class) (X: class u h k m n) (Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c_1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cfv Z cmgp) U cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k (cdm W) (crn (cmpt n cz (co (cv n) (cfv (cv k) W) c_x)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H S (cdm cdprd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co H S cdprd) U) $ >
  $ wceq P (co H S cdpj) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv H cod) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (cneg c1) (co c2 (cfv (cfv I W) O) cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cdm W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 (cfv I P)) c_1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpt u U (cio h (wrex m (wa (wceq (cfv (cv u) (cfv I P)) (co (cv m) (cfv I W) c_x)) (wceq (cv h) (co T (cv m) cexp))) cz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wne (cfv A2 (cv x3)) c1) D) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrpt . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrptlem3 {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 D: class k n) (S c_x: class) (U: class k n) (c_1: class) (G: class k n) (H: class) (N: class k n) (W Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c_1) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (cfv Z cmgp) U cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv H cmg) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k (cdm W) (crn (cmpt n cz (co (cv n) (cfv (cv k) W) c_x)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H S (cdm cdprd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co H S cdprd) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wne (cfv A2 (cv x3)) c1) D) $;

--| For any element other than 1, there is a Dirichlet character that is not one at the given element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D c_1 G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wne (cfv A2 (cv x3)) c1) D) $;

--| An orthogonality relation for Dirichlet characters: the sum of all the values of a Dirichlet character ` X ` is ` 0 ` if ` X ` is non-principal and ` phi ( n ) ` otherwise. Part of Theorem 6.5.1 of [Shapiro] p. 230. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrsum2 {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class a) (U c_1: class) (G N: class a) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu a U (cfv (cv a) X)) (cif (wceq X c_1) (cfv N cphi) cc0)) $;

--| An orthogonality relation for Dirichlet characters: the sum of all the values of a Dirichlet character ` X ` is ` 0 ` if ` X ` is non-principal and ` phi ( n ) ` otherwise. Part of Theorem 6.5.1 of [Shapiro] p. 230. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrsum {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class a) (c_1: class) (G N: class a) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu a B2 (cfv (cv a) X)) (cif (wceq X c_1) (cfv N cphi) cc0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sumdchr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem sumdchr2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (D c_1 G N: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 D (cfv A2 (cv x3))) (cif (wceq A2 c_1) (cfv D chash) cc0)) $;

--| There are exactly ` phi ( N ) ` Dirichlet characters modulo ` N ` . Part of Theorem 6.5.1 of [Shapiro] p. 230. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchrhash (D G N: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv D chash) (cfv N cphi)) $;

--| An orthogonality relation for Dirichlet characters: the sum of ` x ( A ) ` for fixed ` A ` and all ` x ` is ` 0 ` if ` A = 1 ` and ` phi ( n ) ` otherwise. Theorem 6.5.1 of [Shapiro] p. 230. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem sumdchr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (D c_1 G N: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv Z cur) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 D (cfv A2 (cv x3))) (cif (wceq A2 c_1) (cfv N cphi) cc0)) $;

--| An orthogonality relation for Dirichlet characters: the sum of ` X ( a ) x. * Y ( a ) ` over all ` a ` is nonzero only when ` X = Y ` . Part of Theorem 6.5.2 of [Shapiro] p. 232. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem dchr2sum {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class a) (G: class) (N: class a) (X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu a B2 (co (cfv (cv a) X) (cfv (cfv (cv a) Y) ccj) cmul)) (cif (wceq X Y) (cfv N cphi) cc0)) $;

--| An orthogonality relation for Dirichlet characters: the sum of ` x ( A ) ` for fixed ` A ` and all ` x ` is ` 0 ` if ` A = 1 ` and ` phi ( n ) ` otherwise. Part of Theorem 6.5.2 of [Shapiro] p. 232. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem sum2dchr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (U: class x3) (G N Z: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Z cui) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu x3 D (co (cfv A2 (cv x3)) (cfv (cfv C (cv x3)) ccj) cmul)) (cif (wceq A2 C) (cfv N cphi) cc0)) $;

--| Value of the central binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem bcctr (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) (co (cfv (co c2 N cmul) cfa) (co (cfv N cfa) (cfv N cfa) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Prime count of a central binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.)
theorem pcbcctr {k: setvar} (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel P cprime)) (wceq (co P (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) (csu k (co c1 (co c2 N cmul) cfz) (co (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) (co P (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cfl) (co c2 (cfv (co N (co P (cv k) cexp) cdiv) cfl) cmul) cmin))) $;

--| The binomial coefficient is monotone in its second argument, up to the midway point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Mar-2014.)
theorem bcmono (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)) (wbr B2 (co N c2 cdiv) cle)) (wbr (co N A2 cbc) (co N B2 cbc) cle) $;

--| The binomial coefficient takes its maximum value at the center. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Mar-2014.)
theorem bcmax (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel K cz)) (wbr (co (co c2 N cmul) K cbc) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cle) $;

--| Ratio of two central binomial coefficients. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2014.)
theorem bcp1ctr (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cmul) (co N c1 caddc) cbc) (co (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) (co c2 (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) (co N c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul) cmul)) $;

--| A bound on the binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Mar-2014.)
theorem bclbnd (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wbr (co (co c4 N cexp) N cdiv) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) clt) $;

--| Convert a bound on a power to a bound on the exponent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Mar-2014.)
theorem efexple (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wcel N cz) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wbr (co A2 N cexp) B2 cle) (wbr N (cfv (co (cfv B2 clog) (cfv A2 clog) cdiv) cfl) cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.)
theorem bpos1lem {p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (A2 B2: class p) (P N: class):
  $ wi (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime) ph $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv P cuz)) ph $ >
  $ wcel P cprime $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c2 cmul) B2 $ >
  $ wbr A2 P clt $ >
  $ wo (wbr P B2 clt) (wceq P B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv A2 cuz)) ph $;

--| Bertrand's postulate, checked numerically for ` N <_ 6 4 ` , using the prime sequence ` 2 , 3 , 5 , 7 , 1 3 , 2 3 , 4 3 , 8 3 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Apr-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem bpos1 {p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wbr N (cdc c6 c4) cle)) (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime) $;

--| An upper bound on the prime powers dividing a central binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem1 (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel P cprime)) (wbr (co P (co P (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cexp) (co c2 N cmul) cle) $;

--| There are no odd primes in the range ` ( 2 N / 3 , N ] ` dividing the ` N ` -th central binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem2 (ph: wff) (P N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c2 P clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cdiv) P clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P N cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co P (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . Since the binomial coefficient does not have any primes in the range ` ( 2 N / 3 , N ] ` or ` ( 2 N , +oo ) ` by ~ bposlem2 and ~ prmfac1 , respectively, and it does not have any in the range ` ( N , 2 N ] ` by hypothesis, the product of the primes up through ` 2 N / 3 ` must be sufficient to compose the whole binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem3 {n p: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class n) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cv n) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K (cseq cmul F c1)) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem4 {n p: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cv n) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (cfv (co c2 N cmul) csqrt) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c3 K cfz)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . Bound the product of all small primes in the binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem bposlem5 {n p: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cv n) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (cfv (co c2 N cmul) csqrt) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M (cseq cmul F c1)) (co (co c2 N cmul) (co (co (cfv (co c2 N cmul) csqrt) c3 cdiv) c2 caddc) ccxp) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . By using the various bounds at our disposal, arrive at an inequality that is false for ` N ` large enough. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem bposlem6 {n p: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cv n) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (co (co c2 N cmul) c3 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (cfv (co c2 N cmul) csqrt) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co c4 N cexp) N cdiv) (co (co (co c2 N cmul) (co (co (cfv (co c2 N cmul) csqrt) c3 cdiv) c2 caddc) ccxp) (co c2 (co (co (co c4 N cmul) c3 cdiv) c5 cmin) ccxp) cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . The function ` F ` is strictly decreasing for arguments greater than 7. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem7 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 n) (G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (cfv c2 csqrt) (cfv (cfv (cv n) csqrt) G) cmul) (co (co c9 c4 cdiv) (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) G) cmul) caddc) (co (cfv c2 clog) (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) clog) (cv x3) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co ceu c2 cexp) A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co ceu c2 cexp) B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv B2 F) (cfv A2 F) clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . Show that ` F ( 6 4 ) ` is less than ` log 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.)
theorem bposlem8 {x3 n: setvar} (F G: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (cfv c2 csqrt) (cfv (cfv (cv n) csqrt) G) cmul) (co (co c9 c4 cdiv) (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) G) cmul) caddc) (co (cfv c2 clog) (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) clog) (cv x3) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wa (wcel (cfv (cdc c6 c4) F) cr) (wbr (cfv (cdc c6 c4) F) (cfv c2 clog) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bpos . Derive a contradiction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem bposlem9 {x3 n p: setvar} (ph: wff x3 p) (ps: wff x3 n p) (F: class x3 n p) (G: class x3 p) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (cfv c2 csqrt) (cfv (cfv (cv n) csqrt) G) cmul) (co (co c9 c4 cdiv) (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) G) cmul) caddc) (co (cfv c2 clog) (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) clog) (cv x3) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cdc c6 c4) N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Bertrand's postulate: there is a prime between ` N ` and ` 2 N ` for every positive integer ` N ` . This proof follows Erd&#337;s's method, for the most part, but with some refinements due to Shigenori Tochiori to save us some calculations of large primes. See ~ http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_of_Bertrand%27s_postulate for an overview of the proof strategy. This is Metamath 100 proof #98. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Mar-2014.)
theorem bpos {p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wrex p (wa (wbr N (cv p) clt) (wbr (cv p) (co c2 N cmul) cle)) cprime) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Legendre symbol function.
term clgs: class;

--| Define the Legendre symbol (actually the Kronecker symbol, which extends the Legendre symbol to all integers, and also the Jacobi symbol, which restricts the Kronecker symbol to positive odd integers). See definition in [ApostolNT] p. 179 resp. definition in [ApostolNT] p. 188. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_lgs {m n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq clgs (cmpo a n cz cz (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) (cif (wceq (co (cv a) c2 cexp) c1) c1 cc0) (co (cif (wa (wbr (cv n) cc0 clt) (wbr (cv a) cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) (cfv (cfv (cv n) cabs) (cseq cmul (cmpt m cn (cif (wcel (cv m) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv m) c2) (cif (wbr c2 (cv a) cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co (cv a) c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co (cv a) (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv m) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv m) (cv n) cpc) cexp) c1)) c1)) cmul))) $;

--| ` { -u 1 , 0 , 1 } ` is the set of all integers with absolute value at most ` 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-Jul-2021.)
theorem zabsle1 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cz) (wb (wcel Z (ctp (cneg c1) cc0 c1)) (wbr (cfv Z cabs) c1 cle)) $;

--| When ` a ` is coprime to the prime ` p ` , ` a ^ ( ( p - 1 ) / 2 ) ` is equivalent ` mod p ` to ` 1 ` or ` -u 1 ` , and so adding ` 1 ` makes it equivalent to ` 0 ` or ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgslem1 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wn (wbr P A2 cdvds))) (wcel (co (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) P cmo) (cpr cc0 c2)) $;

--| The set ` Z ` of all integers with absolute value at most ` 1 ` contains ` { -u 1 , 0 , 1 } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgslem2 {x3: setvar} (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ w3a (wcel (cneg c1) Z) (wcel cc0 Z) (wcel c1 Z) $;

--| The set ` Z ` of all integers with absolute value at most ` 1 ` is closed under multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgslem3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 Z) (wcel B2 Z)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsfcl2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 19-Mar-2022.)
theorem lgslem4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (P Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wcel (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) P cmo) c1 cmin) Z) $;

--| Value of the Legendre symbol at an arbitrary integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 N clgs) (cif (wceq N cc0) (cif (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) c1) c1 cc0) (co (cif (wa (wbr N cc0 clt) (wbr A2 cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) (cfv (cfv N cabs) (cseq cmul F c1)) cmul))) $;

--| Value of the function ` F ` which defines the Legendre symbol at the primes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsfval {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wceq (cfv M F) (cif (wcel M cprime) (co (cif (wceq M c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co M c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) M cmo) c1 cmin)) (co M N cpc) cexp) c1)) $;

--| The function ` F ` is closed in integers with absolute value less than ` 1 ` (namely ` { -u 1 , 0 , 1 } ` , see ~ zabsle1 ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsfcl2 {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wf cn Z F) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is an element of ` Z ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgscllem {x3 n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N clgs) Z) $;

--| Closure of the function ` F ` which defines the Legendre symbol at the primes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsfcl {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wf cn cz F) $;

--| The function ` F ` has magnitude less or equal to ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsfle1 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (M N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wcel M cn)) (wbr (cfv (cfv M F) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsval2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval2lem {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cprime)) (wceq (co A2 N clgs) (cif (wceq N c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) N cmo) c1 cmin))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsval4 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval4lem {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (cif (wceq (cv n) c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co (cv n) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo) c1 cmin)) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (co A2 (cv n) clgs) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1))) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is an integer with absolute value less than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgscl2 {x3: setvar} (A2 N: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c1 cle) cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N clgs) Z) $;

--| The Legendre symbol when the second argument is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgs0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (co A2 cc0 clgs) (cif (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) c1) c1 cc0)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is an integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgscl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N clgs) cz) $;

--| The Legendre symbol has absolute value less than or equal to 1. Together with ~ lgscl this implies that it takes values in ` { -u 1 , 0 , 1 } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsle1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 N clgs) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at a prime (this is the traditional domain of the Legendre symbol, except for the addition of prime ` 2 ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval2 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P cprime)) (wceq (co A2 P clgs) (cif (wceq P c2) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1))) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) P cmo) c1 cmin))) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgs2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (co A2 c2 clgs) (cif (wbr c2 A2 cdvds) cc0 (cif (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) c1 (cneg c1)))) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at an odd prime (this is the traditional domain of the Legendre symbol). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval3 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wceq (co A2 P clgs) (co (co (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) c1 caddc) P cmo) c1 cmin)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is equivalent to ` a ^ ( ( p - 1 ) / 2 ) ` , ` mod p ` . This theorem is also called "Euler's criterion", see theorem 9.2 in [ApostolNT] p. 180, or a representation of Euler's criterion using the Legendre symbol, see also ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsvalmod (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wceq (co (co A2 P clgs) P cmo) (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo)) $;

--| Restate ~ lgsval for nonzero ` N ` , where the function ` F ` has been abbreviated into a self-referential expression taking the value of ` /L ` on the primes as given. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval4 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (co A2 (cv n) clgs) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wceq (co A2 N clgs) (co (cif (wa (wbr N cc0 clt) (wbr A2 cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) (cfv (cfv N cabs) (cseq cmul F c1)) cmul)) $;

--| Closure of the function ` F ` which defines the Legendre symbol at the primes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsfcl3 {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (co A2 (cv n) clgs) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wf cn cz F) $;

--| Same as ~ lgsval4 for positive ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsval4a {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (co A2 (cv n) clgs) (co (cv n) N cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co A2 N clgs) (cfv N (cseq cmul F c1))) $;

--| The value of the Legendre symbol is either -1 or 0 or 1. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jul-2021.)
theorem lgscl1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N clgs) (ctp (cneg c1) cc0 c1)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is either even or odd under negation with respect to the second parameter according to the sign of the first. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsneg (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg N) clgs) (co (cif (wbr A2 cc0 clt) (cneg c1) c1) (co A2 N clgs) cmul)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol for nonnegative first parameter is unchanged by negation of the second. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsneg1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg N) clgs) (co A2 N clgs)) $;

--| The Legendre (Jacobi) symbol is preserved under reduction ` mod n ` when ` n ` is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsmod (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds))) (wceq (co (co A2 N cmo) N clgs) (co A2 N clgs)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdi and ~ lgsdir : the sign part of the Legendre symbol is multiplicative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdilem (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (cif (wa (wbr N cc0 clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) (co (cif (wa (wbr N cc0 clt) (wbr A2 cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) (cif (wa (wbr N cc0 clt) (wbr B2 cc0 clt)) (cneg c1) c1) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdir2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2lem1:
  $ wa (wa (wceq (co c1 c8 cmo) c1) (wceq (co (cneg c1) c8 cmo) c7)) (wa (wceq (co c3 c8 cmo) c3) (wceq (co (cneg c3) c8 cmo) c5)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdir2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2lem2 (A2 S K M N: class):
  $ w3a (wcel K cz) (wbr c2 (co K c1 caddc) cdvds) (wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wn (wbr c2 A2 cdvds))) (wi (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (co cc0 K cfz)) (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) S))) $ >
  $ wceq M (co K c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq N (co M c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wcel N S $ >
  $ w3a (wcel N cz) (wbr c2 (co N c1 caddc) cdvds) (wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wn (wbr c2 A2 cdvds))) (wi (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) S))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdir2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2lem3 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wn (wbr c2 A2 cdvds))) (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cun (cpr c1 c7) (cpr c3 c5))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdir2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2lem4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7))) (wb (wcel (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) (wcel (co B2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdir2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2lem5 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel (co A2 c8 cmo) (cpr c3 c5)) (wcel (co B2 c8 cmo) (cpr c3 c5)))) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is completely multiplicative at ` 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 clgs) (co (co A2 c2 clgs) (co B2 c2 clgs) cmul)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is completely multiplicative at the primes. See theorem 9.3 in [ApostolNT] p. 180. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Mar-2022.)
theorem lgsdirprm (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel P cprime)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) P clgs) (co (co A2 P clgs) (co B2 P clgs) cmul)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is completely multiplicative in its left argument. Generalization of theorem 9.9(a) in [ApostolNT] p. 188 (which assumes that ` A ` and ` B ` are odd positive integers). Together with ~ lgsqr this implies that the product of two quadratic residues or nonresidues is a residue, and the product of a residue and a nonresidue is a nonresidue. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdir (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) N clgs) (co (co A2 N clgs) (co B2 N clgs) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsdi . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdilem2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class) (F: class n) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne N cc0) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (co (co A2 (cv n) clgs) (co (cv n) M cpc) cexp) c1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv M cabs) (cseq cmul F c1)) (cfv (cfv (co M N cmul) cabs) (cseq cmul F c1))) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is completely multiplicative in its right argument. Generalization of theorem 9.9(b) in [ApostolNT] p. 188 (which assumes that ` M ` and ` N ` are odd positive integers). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsdi (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wne M cc0) (wne N cc0))) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) clgs) (co (co A2 M clgs) (co A2 N clgs) cmul)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is nonzero (and hence equal to ` 1 ` or ` -u 1 ` ) precisely when the arguments are coprime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsne0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wne (co A2 N clgs) cc0) (wceq (co A2 N cgcd) c1)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol is nonzero (and hence equal to ` 1 ` or ` -u 1 ` ) precisely when the arguments are coprime. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgsabs1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wceq (cfv (co A2 N clgs) cabs) c1) (wceq (co A2 N cgcd) c1)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at a square is equal to ` 1 ` . Together with ~ lgsmod this implies that the Legendre symbol takes value ` 1 ` at every quadratic residue. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem lgssq (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel N cz) (wceq (co A2 N cgcd) c1)) (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) N clgs) c1) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at a square is equal to ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Feb-2015.)
theorem lgssq2 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn) (wceq (co A2 N cgcd) c1)) (wceq (co A2 (co N c2 cexp) clgs) c1) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at any prime (even at 2) is ` 0 ` iff the prime does not divide the first argument. See definition in [ApostolNT] p. 179. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem lgsprme0 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P cprime)) (wb (wceq (co A2 P clgs) cc0) (wceq (co A2 P cmo) cc0)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at ` 1 ` . See example 1 in [ApostolNT] p. 180. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem _1lgs (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (co c1 N clgs) c1) $;

--| The Legendre symbol at ` 1 ` . See definition in [ApostolNT] p. 188. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem lgs1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (co A2 c1 clgs) c1) $;

--| The Legendre (Jacobi) symbol is preserved under reduction ` mod n ` when ` n ` is odd. Theorem 9.9(c) in [ApostolNT] p. 188. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem lgsmodeq (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wa (wcel N cn) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)))) (wi (wceq (co A2 N cmo) (co B2 N cmo)) (wceq (co A2 N clgs) (co B2 N clgs))) $;

--| The Legendre (Jacobi) symbol is preserved under multiplication with a square of an integer coprime to the second argument. Theorem 9.9(d) in [ApostolNT] p. 188. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem lgsmulsqcoprm (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wne A2 cc0)) (wa (wcel B2 cz) (wne B2 cc0)) (wa (wcel N cz) (wceq (co A2 N cgcd) c1))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) N clgs) (co B2 N clgs)) $;

--| Variation on ~ lgsdir valid for all ` A , B ` but only for positive ` N ` . (The exact location of the failure of this law is for ` A = 0 ` , ` B < 0 ` , ` N = -u 1 ` in which case ` ( 0 /L -u 1 ) = 1 ` but ` ( B /L -u 1 ) = -u 1 ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem lgsdirnn0 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) N clgs) (co (co A2 N clgs) (co B2 N clgs) cmul)) $;

--| Variation on ~ lgsdi valid for all ` M , N ` but only for positive ` A ` . (The exact location of the failure of this law is for ` A = -u 1 ` , ` M = 0 ` , and some ` N ` in which case ` ( -u 1 /L 0 ) = 1 ` but ` ( -u 1 /L N ) = -u 1 ` when ` -u 1 ` is not a quadratic residue mod ` N ` .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem lgsdinn0 (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) clgs) (co (co A2 M clgs) (co A2 N clgs) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqrlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D P S T c_1 c_ex L c_mi O X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv Y ce1) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv S cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Y cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) X c_ex) c_1 c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo) (co c1 P cmo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 L) (cfv T O)) (cfv Y c0g)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqrlem2 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 D: class y1) (P: class) (S: class y1) (T: class) (c_1 c_ex G: class y1) (L: class) (c_mi: class y1) (O: class) (X: class y1) (Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv Y ce1) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv S cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Y cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) X c_ex) c_1 c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cima (ccnv (cfv T O)) (csn (cfv Y c0g))) G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqrlem3 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D: class y1) (P: class) (S: class y1) (T: class) (c_1 c_ex G: class y1) (L: class) (c_mi: class y1) (O: class) (X: class y1) (Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv Y ce1) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv S cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Y cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) X c_ex) c_1 c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 L) (cima (ccnv (cfv T O)) (csn (cfv Y c0g)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqrlem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (B2 D: class x3 y1) (P: class) (S: class x3 y1) (T: class x3) (c_1 c_ex: class x3 y1) (G: class y1) (L: class) (c_mi: class x3 y1) (O: class x3) (X: class x3 y1) (Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv Y cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Y cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv Y ce1) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv S cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Y cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq T (co (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) X c_ex) c_1 c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) cdvds) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsqr . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqrlem5 {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1)) (wrex x3 (wbr P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) cdvds) cz) $;

--| The Legendre symbol for odd primes is ` 1 ` iff the number is not a multiple of the prime (in which case it is ` 0 ` , see ~ lgsne0 ) and the number is a quadratic residue ` mod P ` (it is ` -u 1 ` for nonresidues by the process of elimination from ~ lgsabs1 ). Given our definition of the Legendre symbol, this theorem is equivalent to Euler's criterion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsqr {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wb (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1) (wa (wn (wbr P A2 cdvds)) (wrex x3 (wbr P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) cdvds) cz))) $;

--| If the Legendre symbol of an integer for an odd prime is ` 1 ` , then the number is a quadratic residue ` mod P ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2021.)
theorem lgsqrmod {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wi (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1) (wrex x3 (wceq (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) P cmo) (co A2 P cmo)) cz)) $;

--| If the Legendre symbol of an integer ` A ` for an odd prime is ` 1 ` , then the number is a quadratic residue ` mod P ` with a solution ` x ` of the congruence ` ( x ^ 2 ) == A ` (mod ` P ` ) which is not divisible by the prime. (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2021.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Mar-2022.)
theorem lgsqrmodndvds {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wi (wceq (co A2 P clgs) c1) (wrex x3 (wa (wceq (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) P cmo) (co A2 P cmo)) (wn (wbr P (cv x3) cdvds))) cz)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol function ` X ( m ) = ( m /L N ) ` , where ` N ` is an odd positive number, is a Dirichlet character modulo ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem lgsdchrval {y1 h m: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class h m) (D G: class y1 h m) (L N: class) (X Z: class h m):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpt y1 B2 (cio h (wrex m (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv m) L)) (wceq (cv h) (co (cv m) N clgs))) cz))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N cn) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds))) (wcel A2 cz)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 L) X) (co A2 N clgs)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol function ` X ( m ) = ( m /L N ) ` , where ` N ` is an odd positive number, is a real Dirichlet character modulo ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2016.)
theorem lgsdchr {y1 h m: setvar} (B2: class h m) (D G: class y1 h m) (L N: class) (X Z: class h m):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Z cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpt y1 B2 (cio h (wrex m (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv m) L)) (wceq (cv h) (co (cv m) N clgs))) cz))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds))) (wa (wcel X D) (wf B2 cr X)) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 1 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0a (ph: wff) (P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cn) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 2 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0b (ph: wff) (P H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cn) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 3 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 13-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0c (ph: wff) (P H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv H cfa) P cgcd) c1) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 4 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0d (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 5 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0e (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 6 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0f (ph: wff) (P H M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co M c1 caddc) H cle) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 7 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0g (ph: wff) (P H M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M H cle) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 8 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0h (ph: wff) (P H M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $;

--| Auxiliary lemma 9 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem0i (ph: wff) (P H M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (co (co c2 P clgs) P cmo) (co (co (cneg c1) N cexp) P cmo)) (wceq (co c2 P clgs) (co (cneg c1) N cexp))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gausslemma2dlem1 . (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem1a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn R) (co c1 H cfz)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem1 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class k) (R: class x3) (H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv H cfa) (cprod k (co c1 H cfz) (cfv (cv k) R))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class k) (R: class x3) (H: class) (M: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) R) (co (cv k) c2 cmul)) (co c1 M cfz)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem3 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class k) (R: class x3) (H: class) (M: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) R) (co P (co (cv k) c2 cmul) cmin)) (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz)) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem4 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv H cfa) (co (cprod k (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv k) R)) (cprod k (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz) (cfv (cv k) R)) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gausslemma2dlem5 . (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem5a {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cprod k (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz) (cfv (cv k) R)) P cmo) (co (cprod k (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz) (co (cneg c1) (co (cv k) c2 cmul) cmul)) P cmo)) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem5 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class) (N: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cprod k (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz) (cfv (cv k) R)) P cmo) (co (co (co (cneg c1) N cexp) (cprod k (co (co M c1 caddc) H cfz) (co (cv k) c2 cmul)) cmul) P cmo)) $;

--| Lemma 6 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem6 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv H cfa) P cmo) (co (co (co (co (cneg c1) N cexp) (co c2 H cexp) cmul) (cfv H cfa) cmul) P cmo)) $;

--| Lemma 7 for ~ gausslemma2d . (Contributed by AV, 13-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2dlem7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co (cneg c1) N cexp) (co c2 H cexp) cmul) P cmo) c1) $;

--| Gauss' Lemma (see also theorem 9.6 in [ApostolNT] p. 182) for integer ` 2 ` : Let p be an odd prime. Let S = {2, 4, 6, ..., p - 1}. Let n denote the number of elements of S whose least positive residue modulo p is greater than p/2. Then ( 2 | p ) = (-1)^n. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2021.)
theorem gausslemma2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (R: class x3) (H M: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt x3 (co c1 H cfz) (cif (wbr (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P c2 cdiv) clt) (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) (co P (co (cv x3) c2 cmul) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq N (co H M cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c2 P clgs) (co (cneg c1) N cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgseisen . If ` R ( u ) = ( Q x. u ) mod P ` and ` M ( u ) = ( -u 1 ^ R ( u ) ) x. R ( u ) ` , then for any even ` 1 <_ u <_ P - 1 ` , ` M ( u ) ` is also an even integer ` 1 <_ M ( u ) <_ P - 1 ` . To simplify these statements, we divide all the even numbers by ` 2 ` , so that it becomes the statement that ` M ( x / 2 ) = ( -u 1 ^ R ( x / 2 ) ) x. R ( x / 2 ) / 2 ` is an integer between ` 1 ` and ` ( P - 1 ) / 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgseisenlem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q: class) (R M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co (co (co (co (cneg c1) R cexp) R cmul) P cmo) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgseisen . The function ` M ` is an injection (and hence a bijection by the pigeonhole principle). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgseisenlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class y1) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co (co (co (co (cneg c1) R cexp) R cmul) P cmo) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co Q (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgseisen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Jun-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lgseisenlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S G L: class y1) (M: class x3) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co (co (co (co (cneg c1) R cexp) R cmul) P cmo) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co Q (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv Y cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G (cmpt x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (co (cneg c1) R cexp) Q cmul) L)) cgsu) (cfv Y cur)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgseisen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Jun-2019.)
theorem lgseisenlem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S G L: class y1) (M: class x3) (Y: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (co (co Q (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (co (co (co (co (cneg c1) R cexp) R cmul) P cmo) c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co Q (co c2 (cv y1) cmul) cmul) P cmo) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv P czn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv Y cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Y czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co Q (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo) (co (co (cneg c1) (csu x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (co Q P cdiv) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) cfl)) cexp) P cmo)) $;

--| Eisenstein's lemma, an expression for ` ( P /L Q ) ` when ` P , Q ` are distinct odd primes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgseisen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Q P clgs) (co (cneg c1) (csu x3 (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz) (cfv (co (co Q P cdiv) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) cmul) cfl)) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsquad . Count the members of ` S ` with odd coordinates. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquadlem1 {x3 y1 z u: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q S M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co Q c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co c1 M cfz)) (wcel (cv y1) (co c1 N cfz))) (wbr (co (cv y1) P cmul) (co (cv x3) Q cmul) clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cneg c1) (csu u (co (co (cfv (co M c2 cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) M cfz) (cfv (co (co Q P cdiv) (co c2 (cv u) cmul) cmul) cfl)) cexp) (co (cneg c1) (cfv (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cfv (cv z) c1st) cdvds)) S) chash) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsquad . Count the members of ` S ` with even coordinates, and combine with ~ lgsquadlem1 to get the total count of lattice points in ` S ` (up to parity). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquadlem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q S M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co Q c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co c1 M cfz)) (wcel (cv y1) (co c1 N cfz))) (wbr (co (cv y1) P cmul) (co (cv x3) Q cmul) clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Q P clgs) (co (cneg c1) (cfv S chash) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsquad . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquadlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (P Q S M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne P Q) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co Q c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq S (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (co c1 M cfz)) (wcel (cv y1) (co c1 N cfz))) (wbr (co (cv y1) P cmul) (co (cv x3) Q cmul) clt))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co P Q clgs) (co Q P clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co M N cmul) cexp)) $;

--| The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity, see also theorem 9.8 in [ApostolNT] p. 185. If ` P ` and ` Q ` are distinct odd primes, then the product of the Legendre symbols ` ( P /L Q ) ` and ` ( Q /L P ) ` is the parity of ` ( ( P - 1 ) / 2 ) x. ( ( Q - 1 ) / 2 ) ` . This uses Eisenstein's proof, which also has a nice geometric interpretation - see ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proofs_of_quadratic_reciprocity . This is Metamath 100 proof #7. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquad (P Q: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wcel Q (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wne P Q)) (wceq (co (co P Q clgs) (co Q P clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co Q c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsquad2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquad2lem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 N clgs) (co N A2 clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co A2 c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co B2 N clgs) (co N B2 clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co B2 c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co M N clgs) (co N M clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lgsquad2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquad2lem2 {x3 k m: setvar} (ph: wff k) (ps: wff x3 k m) (M: class x3 k) (N: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv m) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wceq (co (cv m) N cgcd) c1))) (wceq (co (co (cv m) N clgs) (co N (cv m) clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $ >
  $ wb ps (wral x3 (wi (wceq (co (cv x3) (co c2 N cmul) cgcd) c1) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) N clgs) (co N (cv x3) clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp))) (co c1 (cv k) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co M N clgs) (co N M clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $;

--| Extend ~ lgsquad to coprime odd integers (the domain of the Jacobi symbol). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquad2 (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M N cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co M N clgs) (co N M clgs) cmul) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp)) $;

--| Extend ~ lgsquad2 to integers which share a factor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem lgsquad3 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cn) (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds))) (wa (wcel N cn) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)))) (wceq (co M N clgs) (co (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co M c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cmul) cexp) (co N M clgs) cmul)) $;

--| The first supplement to the law of quadratic reciprocity. Negative one is a square mod an odd prime ` P ` iff ` P == 1 ` (mod ` 4 ` ). See first case of theorem 9.4 in [ApostolNT] p. 181. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem m1lgs (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wb (wceq (co (cneg c1) P clgs) c1) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgslem1a . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1a1 {i: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wral i (wceq (co (cv i) c2 cmul) (co (co (cv i) c2 cmul) P cmo)) (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgslem1a . (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1a2 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel I cz)) (wb (wbr (cfv (co N c4 cdiv) cfl) I clt) (wbr (co N c2 cdiv) (co I c2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgslem1 . (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1a {x3 i: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wceq (crab x3 (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) c2 cmul)) (wbr (co P c2 cdiv) (co (cv x3) P cmo) clt)) (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz)) cz) (crab x3 (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) c2 cmul)) (co (co (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz)) cz)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgslem1 . (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1b {x3 i j: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3 i j) (I: class):
  $ wceq I (co A2 B2 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j I (co (cv j) c2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wf1o I (crab x3 (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) c2 cmul)) I) cz) F $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2lgslem1 . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1c (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wbr (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgs . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem1 {x3 i: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wceq (cfv (crab x3 (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) c2 cmul)) (wbr (co P c2 cdiv) (co (cv x3) P cmo) clt)) (co c1 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfz)) cz) chash) (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgs . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem2 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wcel N cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2lgslem3a1 . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3a (P K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wceq P (co (co c8 K cmul) c1 caddc))) (wceq N (co c2 K cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2lgslem3b1 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3b (P K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wceq P (co (co c8 K cmul) c3 caddc))) (wceq N (co (co c2 K cmul) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2lgslem3c1 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3c (P K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wceq P (co (co c8 K cmul) c5 caddc))) (wceq N (co (co c2 K cmul) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2lgslem3d1 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3d (P K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wceq P (co (co c8 K cmul) c7 caddc))) (wceq N (co (co c2 K cmul) c2 caddc)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgslem3 . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3a1 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wceq (co P c8 cmo) c1)) (wceq (co N c2 cmo) cc0) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgslem3 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3b1 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wceq (co P c8 cmo) c3)) (wceq (co N c2 cmo) c1) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2lgslem3 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3c1 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wceq (co P c8 cmo) c5)) (wceq (co N c2 cmo) c1) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ 2lgslem3 . (Contributed by AV, 15-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3d1 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wceq (co P c8 cmo) c7)) (wceq (co N c2 cmo) cc0) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2lgs . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem3 (P N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co P c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cn) (wn (wbr c2 P cdvds))) (wceq (co N c2 cmo) (cif (wcel (co P c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) cc0 c1)) $;

--| The Legendre symbol for ` 2 ` at ` 2 ` is ` 0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgs2:
  $ wceq (co c2 c2 clgs) cc0 $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ 2lgs : special case of ~ 2lgs for ` P = 2 ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2lgslem4:
  $ wb (wceq (co c2 c2 clgs) c1) (wcel (co c2 c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7)) $;

--| The second supplement to the law of quadratic reciprocity (for the Legendre symbol extended to arbitrary primes as second argument). Two is a square modulo a prime ` P ` iff ` P == +- 1 ` (mod ` 8 ` ), see first case of theorem 9.5 in [ApostolNT] p. 181. This theorem justifies our definition of ` ( N /L 2 ) ` ( ~ lgs2 ) to some degree, by demanding that reciprocity extend to the case ` Q = 2 ` . (Proposed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.) (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgs (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co c2 P clgs) c1) (wcel (co P c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgsoddprm . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem1 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wceq N (co (co c8 A2 cmul) B2 caddc))) (wceq (co (co (co N c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) (co (co (co c8 (co A2 c2 cexp) cmul) (co c2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cmul) caddc) (co (co (co B2 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) caddc)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgsoddprm . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem2 (R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cz) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) (wceq R (co N c8 cmo))) (wb (wbr c2 (co (co (co N c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cdvds) (wbr c2 (co (co (co R c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cdvds)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2lgsoddprmlem3 . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem3a:
  $ wceq (co (co (co c1 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cc0 $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2lgsoddprmlem3 . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem3b:
  $ wceq (co (co (co c3 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) c1 $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2lgsoddprmlem3 . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem3c:
  $ wceq (co (co (co c5 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) c3 $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ 2lgsoddprmlem3 . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem3d:
  $ wceq (co (co (co c7 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) (co c2 c3 cmul) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2lgsoddprm . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem3 (R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cz) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) (wceq R (co N c8 cmo))) (wb (wbr c2 (co (co (co R c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cdvds) (wcel R (cpr c1 c7))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ 2lgsoddprm . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprmlem4 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds))) (wb (wbr c2 (co (co (co N c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cdvds) (wcel (co N c8 cmo) (cpr c1 c7))) $;

--| The second supplement to the law of quadratic reciprocity for odd primes (common representation, see theorem 9.5 in [ApostolNT] p. 181): The Legendre symbol for ` 2 ` at an odd prime is minus one to the power of the square of the odd prime minus one divided by eight ( ` ( 2 /L P ) ` = -1^(((P^2)-1)/8) ). (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem _2lgsoddprm (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wceq (co c2 P clgs) (co (cneg c1) (co (co (co P c2 cexp) c1 cmin) c8 cdiv) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem1 {x3 w: setvar} (A2 S: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 S) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) cabs) c2 cexp)) cgz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem2 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2 S: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 S) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc)) cz) cz) $;

--| Fibonacci's identity (actually due to Diophantus). The product of two sums of two squares is also a sum of two squares. We can take advantage of Gaussian integers here to trivialize the proof. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem mul2sq {w: setvar} (A2 B2 S: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqlem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem3 {w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2 C D P S N: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co N P cmul) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co C c2 cexp) (co D c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr P (co (co C B2 cmul) (co A2 D cmul) caddc) cdvds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqlem5 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem4 {w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2 C D P S N: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co N P cmul) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co C c2 cexp) (co D c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . If a number that is a sum of two squares is divisible by a prime that is a sum of two squares, then the quotient is a sum of two squares. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem5 {w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (P S N: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co N P cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . If a number that is a sum of two squares is divisible by a number whose prime divisors are all sums of two squares, then the quotient is a sum of two squares. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem6 {w p: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2: class w p) (B2 S: class w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral p (wi (wbr (cv p) B2 cdvds) (wcel (cv p) S)) cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem7 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (S: class w) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wss Y (cin S cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqlem8 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2016.)
theorem _2sqlem8a {x3 y1 z w a b: setvar} (ph: wff z w a b) (A2: class w b) (B2: class z w) (C D: class y1 z w a b) (S M: class w) (N: class w a b) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wral a (wi (wbr (cv b) (cv a) cdvds) (wcel (cv b) S)) Y) (co c1 (co M c1 cmin) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N cdvds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co (co A2 (co M c2 cdiv) caddc) M cmo) (co M c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (co B2 (co M c2 cdiv) caddc) M cmo) (co M c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C D cgcd) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem8 {x3 y1 z w a b: setvar} (ph: wff z w a b) (A2: class w b) (B2: class z w) (C D: class y1 z w a b) (S: class w) (E F: class w b) (M: class w) (N: class w a b) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wral a (wi (wbr (cv b) (cv a) cdvds) (wcel (cv b) S)) Y) (co c1 (co M c1 cmin) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N cdvds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cgcd) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co (co A2 (co M c2 cdiv) caddc) M cmo) (co M c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (co B2 (co M c2 cdiv) caddc) M cmo) (co M c2 cdiv) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (co C (co C D cgcd) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq F (co D (co C D cgcd) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem9 {x3 y1 z w a b: setvar} (ph: wff z w a b) (S M: class w) (N: class w a b) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wral a (wi (wbr (cv b) (cv a) cdvds) (wcel (cv b) S)) Y) (co c1 (co M c1 cmin) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N cdvds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . Every factor of a "proper" sum of two squares (where the summands are coprime) is a sum of two squares. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem10 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class w) (B2: class z w) (S: class w) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 Y) (wcel B2 cn) (wbr B2 A2 cdvds)) (wcel B2 S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqlem11 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (P: class z w) (S: class w) (Y: class z w):
  $ wceq S (crn (cmpt w cgz (co (cfv (cv w) cabs) c2 cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cgcd) c1) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc))) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wcel P S) $;

--| All primes of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` are sums of two squares. This is Metamath 100 proof #20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sq {x3 y1: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc)) cz) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqb . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqblem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel P cprime) (wne P c2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel X cz) (wcel Y cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co X c2 cexp) (co Y c2 cexp) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co P Y cgcd) (co (co P A2 cmul) (co Y B2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) $;

--| The converse to ~ 2sq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Jun-2015.)
theorem _2sqb {x3 y1: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc)) cz) cz) (wo (wceq P c2) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1))) $;

--| ` 2 ` is the sum of squares of two nonnegative integers iff the two integers are ` 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wb (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) c2) (wa (wceq A2 c1) (wceq B2 c1))) $;

--| If the sum of two squares is prime, none of the original number is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2sqn0 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| If the sum of two squares is prime, the two original numbers are coprime. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2sqcoprm (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cgcd) c1) $;

--| Given two decompositions of a prime as a sum of two squares, show that they are equal. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2sqmod (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C c2 cexp) (co D c2 cexp) caddc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| There exists at most one decomposition of a prime as a sum of two squares. See ~ 2sqb for the existence of such a decomposition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2sqmo {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wrmo a (wrex b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn0) cn0) $;

--| All primes of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` are sums of squares of two nonnegative integers. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqnn0 {x3 y1: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc)) cn0) cn0) $;

--| All primes of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` are sums of squares of two positive integers. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqnn {x3 y1: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq P (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) caddc)) cn) cn) $;

--| For each complex number ` C ` , there exists a unique complex number ` a ` added to the square of a unique another complex number ` b ` resulting in the given complex number ` C ` . The unique complex number ` a ` is ` C ` , and the unique another complex number ` b ` is ` 0 ` . Remark: This, together with ~ addsqnreup , is an example showing that the pattern ` E! a e. A E! b e. B ph ` does not necessarily mean "There are unique sets ` a ` and ` b ` fulfilling ` ph ` ). See also comments for ~ df-eu and ~ 2eu4 . For more details see comment for ~ addsqnreup . (Contributed by AV, 21-Jun-2023.)
theorem addsq2reu {a b: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wreu a (wreu b (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc) cc) $;

--| For each complex number ` C ` , there does not exist a unique complex number ` b ` , squared and added to a unique another complex number ` a ` resulting in the given complex number ` C ` . Actually, for each complex number ` b ` , ` a = ( C - ( b ^ 2 ) ) ` is unique. Remark: This, together with ~ addsq2reu , shows that commutation of two unique quantifications need not be equivalent, and provides an evident justification of the fact that considering the pair of variables is necessary to obtain what we intuitively understand as "double unique existence". (Proposed by GL, 23-Jun-2023.). (Contributed by AV, 23-Jun-2023.)
theorem addsqn2reu {a b: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wn (wreu b (wreu a (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc) cc)) $;

--| For each complex number, there exists a complex number to which the square of more than one (or no) other complex numbers can be added to result in the given complex number. Remark: This theorem, together with ~ addsq2reu , shows that there are cases in which there is a set together with a not unique other set fulfilling a wff, although there is a unique set fulfilling the wff together with another unique set (see ~ addsq2reu ). For more details see comment for ~ addsqnreup . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2023.)
theorem addsqrexnreu {a b: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wrex a (wn (wreu b (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc)) cc) $;

--| There is no unique decomposition of a complex number as a sum of a complex number and a square of a complex number. Remark: This theorem, together with ~ addsq2reu , is a real life example (about a numerical property) showing that the pattern ` E! a e. A E! b e. B ph ` does not necessarily mean "There are unique sets ` a ` and ` b ` fulfilling ` ph ` "). See also comments for ~ df-eu and ~ 2eu4 . In the case of decompositions of complex numbers as a sum of a complex number and a square of a complex number, the only/unique complex number to which the square of a unique complex number is added yields in the given complex number is the given number itself, and the unique complex number to be squared is ` 0 ` (see comment for ~ addsq2reu ). There are, however, complex numbers to which the square of more than one other complex numbers can be added to yield the given complex number (see ~ addsqrexnreu ). For example, ` <. 1 , ( sqrt `` ( C - 1 ) ) >. ` and ` <. 1 , -u ( sqrt `` ( C - 1 ) ) >. ` are two different decompositions of ` C ` (if ` C =/= 1 ` ). Therefore, there is no unique decomposition of any complex number as a sum of a complex number and a square of a complex number, as generally proved by this theorem. As a consequence, a theorem must claim the existence of a unique pair of sets to express "There are unique ` a ` and ` b ` so that .." (more formally ` E! p e. ( A X. B ) ph ` with ` p = <. a , b >. ` ), or by showing ` ( E! x e. A E. y e. B ph /\ E! y e. B E. x e. A ph ) ` (see ~ 2reu4 resp. ~ 2eu4 ). These two representations are equivalent (see ~ opreu2reurex ). An analogon of this theorem using the latter variant is given in ~ addsqn2reurex2 . In some cases, however, the variant with (ordered!) pairs may be possible only for ordered sets (like ` RR ` or ` Prime ` ) and claiming that the first component is less than or equal to the second component (see, for example, ~ 2sqreunnltb and ~ 2sqreuopb ). Alternatively, (proper) unordered pairs can be used: ` E! p e ~P A ( ( # `` p ) = 2 /\ ph ) ` , or, using the definition of proper pairs: ` E! p e. ( PrPairs `` A ) ph ` (see, for example, ~ inlinecirc02preu ). (Contributed by AV, 21-Jun-2023.)
theorem addsqnreup {p: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wn (wreu p (wceq (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) C) (cxp cc cc))) $;

--| For each complex number ` C ` , there is no unique complex number ` a ` added to the square of another complex number ` b ` resulting in the given complex number ` C ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem addsq2nreurex {a b: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wn (wreu a (wrex b (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc) cc)) $;

--| For each complex number ` C ` , there does not uniquely exist two complex numbers ` a ` and ` b ` , with ` b ` squared and added to ` a ` resulting in the given complex number ` C ` . Remark: This, together with ~ addsq2reu , is an example showing that the pattern ` E! a e. A E! b e. B ph ` does not necessarily mean "There are unique sets ` a ` and ` b ` fulfilling ` ph ` ), as it is the case with the pattern ` ( E! a e. A E. b e. B ph /\ E! b e. B E. a e. A ph ` . See also comments for ~ df-eu and ~ 2eu4 . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem addsqn2reurex2 {a b: setvar} (C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C cc) (wn (wa (wreu a (wrex b (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc) cc) (wreu b (wrex a (wceq (co (cv a) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) C) cc) cc))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2sqreu . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreulem1 {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn0) cn0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqreult . (Contributed by AV, 8-Jun-2023.) (Proposed by GL, 8-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreultlem {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn0) cn0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqreultb . (Contributed by AV, 10-Jun-2023.) The prime need not be odd, as observed by WL. (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreultblem {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn0) cn0)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 2sqreunn . (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnlem1 {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqreunnlt . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2023.) Specialization to different integers, proposed by GL. (Revised by AV, 11-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnltlem {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ 2sqreunnltb . (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2023.) The prime need not be odd, as observed by WL. (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnltblem {a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wreu a (wreu b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) cn) cn)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2sqreu etc. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreulem2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wi (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co C c2 cexp) caddc)) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ 2sqreu etc. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreulem3 (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wa (wcel B2 cn0) (wcel C cn0))) (wi (wa (wa ph (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (wa ps (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co C c2 cexp) caddc) P))) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ 2sqreu et. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreulem4 {a b: setvar} (ph ps: wff a b) (P: class a):
  $ wb ph (wa ps (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wral a (wrmo b ph cn0) cn0 $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 2sqreunn . (Contributed by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnlem2 {a b: setvar} (ph ps: wff a b) (P: class a):
  $ wb ph (wa ps (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wral a (wrmo b ph cn) cn $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two nonnegative integers. See ~ 2sqnn0 for the existence of such a decomposition. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2023.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreu {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn0) cn0) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn0) cn0)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two positive integers. See ~ 2sqnn for the existence of such a decomposition. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2023.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunn {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn) cn) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn) cn)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different nonnegative integers. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jun-2023.) (Proposed by GL, 8-Jun-2023.) (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreult {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn0) cn0) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn0) cn0)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different nonnegative integers iff ` P == 1 ` (mod 4). (Contributed by AV, 10-Jun-2023.) The prime need not be odd, as observed by WL. (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreultb {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn0) cn0) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn0) cn0))) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two different positive integers. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2023.) Specialization to different integers, proposed by GL. (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnlt {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn) cn) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn) cn)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different positive integers iff the prime is of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2023.) The prime need not be odd, as observed by WL. (Revised by AV, 25-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2sqreunnltb {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (P: class):
  $ wb ph (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wa (wreu a (wrex b ph cn) cn) (wreu b (wrex a ph cn) cn))) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two nonnegative integers. Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreu . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuop {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) cle) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn0 cn0)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two positive integers. Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreunn . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuopnn {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) cle) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn cn)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different nonnegative integers. Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreult . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuoplt {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) clt) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn0 cn0)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different nonnegative integers iff ` P == 1 ` (mod 4). Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreultb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuopltb {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) clt) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn0 cn0))) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` as a sum of squares of two different positive integers. Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreunnlt . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuopnnlt {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1)) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) clt) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn cn)) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different positive integers iff the prime is of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` . Ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreunnltb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuopnnltb {p: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wreu p (wa (wbr (cfv (cv p) c1st) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) clt) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv p) c1st) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c2 cexp) caddc) P)) (cxp cn cn))) $;

--| There exists a unique decomposition of a prime as a sum of squares of two different positive integers iff the prime is of the form ` 4 k + 1 ` . Alternate ordered pair variant of ~ 2sqreunnltb . (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2sqreuopb {p a b: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c1) (wreu p (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) P))))) (cxp cn cn))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ chebbnd1 : show a lower bound on ` ppi ( x ) ` at even integers using similar techniques to those used to prove ~ bpos . (Note that the expression ` K ` is actually equal to ` 2 x. N ` , but proving that is not necessary for the proof, and it's too much work.) The key to the proof is ~ bposlem1 , which shows that each term in the expansion ` ( ( 2 x. N ) _C N ) = prod_ p e. Prime ` ` ( p ^ ( p pCnt ( ( 2 x. N ) _C N ) ) ) ` is at most ` 2 x. N ` , so that the sum really only has nonzero elements up to ` 2 x. N ` , and since each term is at most ` 2 x. N ` , after taking logs we get the inequality ` ppi ( 2 x. N ) x. log ( 2 x. N ) <_ ` ` log ( ( 2 x. N ) _C N ) ` , and ~ bclbnd finishes the proof. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Apr-2016.)
theorem chebbnd1lem1 (K N: class):
  $ wceq K (cif (wbr (co c2 N cmul) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc) cle) (co c2 N cmul) (co (co c2 N cmul) N cbc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wbr (cfv (co (co c4 N cexp) N cdiv) clog) (co (cfv (co c2 N cmul) cppi) (cfv (co c2 N cmul) clog) cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ chebbnd1 : Show that ` log ( N ) / N ` does not change too much between ` N ` and ` M = |_ ( N / 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chebbnd1lem2 (M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cr) (wbr c8 N cle)) (wbr (co (cfv (co c2 M cmul) clog) (co c2 M cmul) cdiv) (co c2 (co (cfv N clog) N cdiv) cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ chebbnd1 : get a lower bound on ` ppi ( N ) / ( N / log ( N ) ) ` that is independent of ` N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2014.)
theorem chebbnd1lem3 (M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cr) (wbr c8 N cle)) (wbr (co (co (cfv c2 clog) (co c1 (co c2 ceu cmul) cdiv) cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (cfv N cppi) (co (cfv N clog) N cdiv) cmul) clt) $;

--| The Chebyshev bound: The function ` ppi ( x ) ` is eventually lower bounded by a positive constant times ` x / log ( x ) ` . Alternatively stated, the function ` ( x / log ( x ) ) / ppi ( x ) ` is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chebbnd1 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co c2 cpnf cico) (co (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cdiv) (cfv (cv x3) cppi) cdiv)) co1 $;

--| Lemma for ~ chtppilim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtppilimlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co c2 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co N A2 ccxp) (cfv N cppi) cdiv) (co c1 A2 cmin) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co (cfv N cppi) (cfv N clog) cmul) cmul) (cfv N ccht) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ chtppilim . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtppilimlem2 {x3 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) cle) (wbr (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv x3) cppi) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmul) cmul) (cfv (cv x3) ccht) clt)) (co c2 cpnf cico)) cr) $;

--| The ` theta ` function is asymptotic to ` ppi ( x ) log ( x ) ` , so it is sufficient to prove ` theta ( x ) / x ~~>r 1 ` to establish the PNT. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chtppilim {x3: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt x3 (co c2 cpnf cico) (co (cfv (cv x3) ccht) (co (cfv (cv x3) cppi) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmul) cdiv)) c1 crli $;

--| The ` theta ` function is upper bounded by a linear term. Corollary of ~ chtub . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chto1ub {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) ccht) (cv x3) cdiv)) co1 $;

--| The Chebyshev bound, part 2: The function ` ppi ( x ) ` is eventually upper bounded by a positive constant times ` x / log ( x ) ` . Alternatively stated, the function ` ppi ( x ) / ( x / log ( x ) ) ` is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Sep-2014.)
theorem chebbnd2 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co c2 cpnf cico) (co (cfv (cv x3) cppi) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cdiv) cdiv)) co1 $;

--| The ` theta ` function is lower bounded by a linear term. Corollary of ~ chebbnd1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem chto1lb {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 (co c2 cpnf cico) (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) ccht) cdiv)) co1 $;

--| The ` psi ` and ` theta ` functions are asymptotic to each other, so is sufficient to prove either ` theta ( x ) / x ~~>r 1 ` or ` psi ( x ) / x ~~>r 1 ` to establish the PNT. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpchtlim {x3: setvar}:
  $ wbr (cmpt x3 (co c2 cpnf cico) (co (cfv (cv x3) cchp) (cfv (cv x3) ccht) cdiv)) c1 crli $;

--| The ` psi ` function is upper bounded by a linear term. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2016.)
theorem chpo1ub {x3: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 crp (co (cfv (cv x3) cchp) (cv x3) cdiv)) co1 $;

--| The ` psi ` function is upper bounded by a linear term. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-May-2016.)
theorem chpo1ubb {x3 c: setvar}:
  $ wrex c (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) cchp) (co (cv c) (cv x3) cmul) cle) crp) crp $;

--| The sum of the von Mangoldt function over ` n ` is asymptotic to ` log x + O(1) ` . Equation 9.2.13 of [Shapiro], p. 331. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Apr-2016.)
theorem vmadivsum {x3 n: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cv n) cdiv)) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmin)) co1 $;

--| Give a total bound on the von Mangoldt sum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem vmadivsumb {x3 n c: setvar}:
  $ wrex c (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cv n) cdiv)) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmin) cabs) (cv c) cle) (co c1 cpnf cico)) crp $;

--| Lemma for ~ rplogsum . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem rplogsumlem1 {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wbr (csu n (co c2 A2 cfz) (co (cfv (cv n) clog) (co (cv n) (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cmul) cdiv)) c2 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rplogsum . Equation 9.2.14 of [Shapiro], p. 331. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem rplogsumlem2 {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wbr (csu n (co c1 A2 cfz) (co (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cif (wcel (cv n) cprime) (cfv (cv n) clog) cc0) cmin) (cv n) cdiv)) c2 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrisum0lem1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem dchrisum0lem1a (ph: wff) (D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel X crp)) (wcel D (co c1 (cfv X cfl) cfz))) (wa (wbr X (co (co X c2 cexp) D cdiv) cle) (wcel (cfv (co (co X c2 cexp) D cdiv) cfl) (cfv (cfv X cfl) cuz))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rpvmasum . Calculate the "trivial case" estimate ` sum_ n <_ x ( .1. ( n ) Lam ( n ) / n ) = log x + O(1) ` , where ` .1. ( x ) ` is the principal Dirichlet character. Equation 9.4.7 of [Shapiro], p. 376. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem rpvmasumlem {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (D c_1: class) (G: class x3 n) (L N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) c_1) (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cv n) cdiv) cmul)) (cfv (cv x3) clog) cmin)) co1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrisum . Lemma 9.4.1 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchrisumlema {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2: class x3) (D c_1 F: class) (G: class x3 n) (I L M N X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv x3)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) crp)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv n) crp) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) (wbr (cv n) (cv x3) cle))) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n crp A2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) A2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wi (wcel I crp) (wcel (csb n I A2) cr)) (wi (wcel I (co M cpnf cico)) (wbr cc0 (csb n I A2) cle))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrisum . Lemma 9.4.1 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchrisumlem1 {x3 u n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class u n) (B2: class x3 u) (D: class u) (R U: class) (c_1: class u) (F: class) (G: class x3 u n) (L M N X: class) (Z: class u):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv x3)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) crp)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv n) crp) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) (wbr (cv n) (cv x3) cle))) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n crp A2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) A2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral u (wbr (cfv (csu n (co cc0 (cv u) cfzo) (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X)) cabs) R cle) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel U cn0)) (wbr (cfv (csu n (co cc0 U cfzo) (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X)) cabs) R cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrisum . Lemma 9.4.1 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchrisumlem2 {x3 u n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class u n) (B2: class x3 u) (D: class u) (R U: class) (c_1: class u) (F: class) (G: class x3 u n) (I J L M N X: class) (Z: class u):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv x3)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) crp)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv n) crp) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) (wbr (cv n) (cv x3) cle))) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n crp A2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) A2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral u (wbr (cfv (csu n (co cc0 (cv u) cfzo) (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X)) cabs) R cle) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M U cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr U (co I c1 caddc) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv I cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv J (cseq caddc F c1)) (cfv I (cseq caddc F c1)) cmin) cabs) (co (co c2 R cmul) (csb n U A2) cmul) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrisum . Lemma 9.4.1 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchrisumlem3 {x3 u t n c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class u n) (B2: class x3 u t) (D: class u) (R: class t) (c_1: class u) (F: class) (G: class x3 u t n c) (L: class) (M: class t) (N X: class) (Z: class u t c):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv x3)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) crp)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv n) crp) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) (wbr (cv n) (cv x3) cle))) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n crp A2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) A2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral u (wbr (cfv (csu n (co cc0 (cv u) cfzo) (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X)) cabs) R cle) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex t (wrex c (wa (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) (cv t) cli) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfl) (cseq caddc F c1)) (cv t) cmin) cabs) (co (cv c) B2 cmul) cle) (co M cpnf cico))) (co cc0 cpnf cico))) $;

--| If ` n e. [ M , +oo ) |-> A ( n ) ` is a positive decreasing function approaching zero, then the infinite sum ` sum_ n , X ( n ) A ( n ) ` is convergent, with the partial sum ` sum_ n <_ x , X ( n ) A ( n ) ` within ` O ( A ( M ) ) ` of the limit ` T ` . Lemma 9.4.1 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-May-2016.)
theorem dchrisum {x3 t n c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2: class x3 t) (D c_1 F: class) (G: class x3 t n c) (L: class) (M: class t) (N X: class) (Z: class t c):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv x3)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) crp)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa (wcel (cv n) crp) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) (wbr (cv n) (cv x3) cle))) (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n crp A2) cc0 crli) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) A2 cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex t (wrex c (wa (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) (cv t) cli) (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) cfl) (cseq caddc F c1)) (cv t) cmin) cabs) (co (cv c) B2 cmul) cle) (co M cpnf cico))) (co cc0 cpnf cico))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrmusum and ~ dchrisumn0 . Apply ~ dchrisum for the function ` 1 / y ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrmusumlema {y1 t a c: setvar} (ph: wff y1 a) (D c_1 F: class a) (G: class y1 t a c) (L: class) (N: class a) (X: class) (Z: class t a c):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv a) L) X) (cv a) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex t (wrex c (wa (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) (cv t) cli) (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv y1) cfl) (cseq caddc F c1)) (cv t) cmin) cabs) (co (cv c) (cv y1) cdiv) cle) (co c1 cpnf cico))) (co cc0 cpnf cico))) $;

--| The sum of the M&ouml;bius function multiplied by a non-principal Dirichlet character, divided by ` n ` , is bounded, provided that ` T =/= 0 ` . Lemma 9.4.2 of [Shapiro], p. 380. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrmusum2 {x3 y1 a d: setvar} (ph: wff y1 a) (C: class a) (D: class a d) (T: class a) (c_1: class a d) (F: class a) (G: class x3 y1 a d) (L: class) (N: class a d) (X: class) (Z: class a d):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt a cn (co (cfv (cfv (cv a) L) X) (cv a) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) T cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv y1) cfl) (cseq caddc F c1)) T cmin) cabs) (co C (cv y1) cdiv) cle) (co c1 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 crp (co (csu d (co c1 (cfv (cv x3) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv d) L) X) (co (cfv (cv d) cmu) (cv d) cdiv) cmul)) T cmul)) co1) $;

--| An alternative expression for a Dirichlet-weighted von Mangoldt sum in terms of the M&ouml;bius function. Equation 9.4.11 of [Shapiro], p. 377. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2016.)
theorem dchrvmasumlem1 {m n d: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (D c_1: class d) (G: class m n d) (L: class) (N: class d) (X: class) (Z: class d):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cv n) cdiv) cmul)) (csu d (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv d) L) X) (co (cfv (cv d) cmu) (cv d) cdiv) cmul) (csu m (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) L) X) (co (cfv (cv m) clog) (cv m) cdiv) cmul)) cmul))) $;

--| Give an expression for ` log x ` remarkably similar to ` sum_ n <_ x ( X ( n ) Lam ( n ) / n ) ` given in ~ dchrvmasumlem1 . Part of Lemma 9.4.3 of [Shapiro], p. 380. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrvmasum2lem {m d: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (D c_1: class d) (G: class m d) (L: class) (N: class d) (X: class) (Z: class d):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 clog) (csu d (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv d) L) X) (co (cfv (cv d) cmu) (cv d) cdiv) cmul) (csu m (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) L) X) (co (cfv (co (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) (cv m) cdiv) clog) (cv m) cdiv) cmul)) cmul))) $;

--| Combine the results of ~ dchrvmasumlem1 and ~ dchrvmasum2lem inside a conditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrvmasum2if {m n d: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff n) (A2: class) (D c_1: class d) (G: class m n d) (L: class) (N: class d) (X: class) (Z: class d):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csu n (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv n) L) X) (co (cfv (cv n) cvma) (cv n) cdiv) cmul)) (cif ps (cfv A2 clog) cc0) caddc) (csu d (co c1 (cfv A2 cfl) cfz) (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv d) L) X) (co (cfv (cv d) cmu) (cv d) cdiv) cmul) (csu m (co c1 (cfv (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) cfl) cfz) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) L) X) (co (cfv (cif ps (co A2 (cv d) cdiv) (cv m)) clog) (cv m) cdiv) cmul)) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dchrvmasum . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-May-2016.)
theorem dchrvmasumlem2 {x3 m d: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (D: class d) (R T: class) (c_1: class d) (F: class m) (G: class x3 m d) (K: class x3 d) (L: class) (N: class d) (X: class) (Z: class d):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv Z czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv N cdchr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c_1) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) crp)) (wcel F cc) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv m) (co (cv x3) (cv d) cdiv)) (wceq F K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
