--| Technical lemma for eliminating the hypothesis of ~ constr0 and co. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrcbvlem {x3 y1 z t e f2 i j k m o s1 r q p a b c d l: setvar}:
  $ wceq (crdg (cmpt z cvv (crab y1 (w3o (wrex i (wrex j (wrex k (wrex l (wrex o (wrex p (w3a (wceq (cv y1) (co (cv i) (co (cv o) (co (cv j) (cv i) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cv y1) (co (cv k) (co (cv p) (co (cv l) (cv k) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wne (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv j) (cv i) cmin) ccj) (co (cv l) (cv k) cmin) cmul) cim) cc0)) cr) cr) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (wrex i (wrex j (wrex k (wrex m (wrex q (wrex o (wa (wceq (cv y1) (co (cv i) (co (cv o) (co (cv j) (cv i) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv k) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv m) (cv q) cmin) cabs))) cr) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (wrex i (wrex j (wrex k (wrex l (wrex m (wrex q (w3a (wne (cv i) (cv l)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv i) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv j) (cv k) cmin) cabs)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv l) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv m) (cv q) cmin) cabs))) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z)) (cv z))) cc)) (cpr cc0 c1)) (crdg (cmpt s1 cvv (crab x3 (w3o (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex t (wrex r (w3a (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv a) (co (cv t) (co (cv b) (cv a) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv c) (co (cv r) (co (cv d) (cv c) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wne (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv b) (cv a) cmin) ccj) (co (cv d) (cv c) cmin) cmul) cim) cc0)) cr) cr) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex e (wrex f2 (wrex t (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv a) (co (cv t) (co (cv b) (cv a) cmin) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv c) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv e) (cv f2) cmin) cabs))) cr) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex e (wrex f2 (w3a (wne (cv a) (cv d)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv a) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv b) (cv c) cmin) cabs)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv d) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv e) (cv f2) cmin) cabs))) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) cc)) (cpr cc0 c1)) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under line-line intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrllcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R T G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co A2 (co T (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co G (co R (co D G cmin) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv (co (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) ccj) (co D G cmin) cmul) cim) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under line-circle intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrlccl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T E F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co A2 (co T (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X G cmin) cabs) (cfv (co E F cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under circle-circle intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrcccl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X D cmin) cabs) (cfv (co E F cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $;

--| If a constructible number generates some subfield ` L ` of ` CC ` , then the degree of the extension of ` L ` over ` QQ ` is a power of two. Theorem 7.12 of [Stewart] p. 98. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem constrext2chn {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 Q S L: class):
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq L (co ccnfld S cress) $ >
  $ wceq S (co ccnfld (cun cq (csn A2)) cfldgen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wceq (co L Q cextdg) (co c2 (cv n) cexp)) cn0) $;

--| Constructible numbers are complex numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrcn (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $;

--| Nonnegative integers are constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem nn0constr (ph: wff) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cconstr) $;

--| Constructive numbers are closed under complex addition. Item (1) of Theorem 7.10 of [Stewart] p. 96. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constraddcl (ph: wff) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y caddc) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under additive inverse. Item (2) of Theorem 7.10 of [Stewart] p. 96. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrnegcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg X) cconstr) $;

--| Integers are constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Nov-2025.)
theorem zconstr (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under taking the point on the unit circle having the same argument. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrdircl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (cfv X cabs) cdiv) cconstr) $;

--| The imaginary unit ` _i ` is constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem iconstr:
  $ wcel ci cconstr $;

--| If two real numbers ` X ` and ` Y ` are constructible, then, so is their product. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrremulcl (ph: wff) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y cmul) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under complex conjugate. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrcjcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X ccj) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under taking the real part. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrrecl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X cre) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under taking the imaginary part. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrimcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X cim) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under complex multiplication. Item (3) of Theorem 7.10 of [Stewart] p. 96. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrmulcl (ph: wff) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y cmul) cconstr) $;

--| If a real number ` X ` is constructible, then, so is its inverse. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrreinvcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 X cdiv) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under complex inverse. Item (4) of Theorem 7.10 of [Stewart] p. 96. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrinvcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 X cdiv) cconstr) $;

--| Contradiction of constructibility: If a complex number ` A ` has minimal polynomial ` F ` over ` QQ ` of a degree that is not a power of ` 2 ` , then ` A ` is not constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem constrcon {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (D F M: class n):
  $ wceq D (cfv (co ccnfld cq cress) cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cq cminply) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cfv A2 M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F D) cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn0)) (wne (cfv F D) (co c2 (cv n) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cconstr)) $;

--| Constructible numbers form a subfield of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrsdrg:
  $ wcel cconstr (cfv ccnfld csdrg) $;

--| The constructible numbers form a field. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrfld:
  $ wcel (co ccnfld cconstr cress) cfield $;

--| If a positive real number ` X ` is constructible, then, so is its square root. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrresqrtcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 X cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X csqrt) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under absolute value (modulus). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrabscl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X cabs) cconstr) $;

--| Constructible numbers are closed under taking the square root. This is not generally the case for the cubic root operation, see ~ 2sqr3nconstr . Item (5) of Theorem 7.10 of [Stewart] p. 96 (Proposed by Saveliy Skresanov, 3-Nov-2025.) (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem constrsqrtcl (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cconstr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X csqrt) cconstr) $;

--| The polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) - 2 ) ` is the minimal polynomial for ` ( 2 ^c ( 1 / 3 ) ) ` over ` QQ ` , and its degree is ` 3 ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Jun-2025.)
theorem _2sqr3minply (A2 D P Q c_ex F K M c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv P cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Q cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Q cv1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Q cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq F (co (co c3 X c_ex) (cfv c2 K) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co c2 (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cq cminply) $ >
  $ wa (wceq F (cfv A2 M)) (wceq (cfv F D) c3) $;

--| Doubling the cube is an impossible construction, i.e. the cube root of ` 2 ` is not constructible with straightedge and compass. Given a cube of edge of length one, a cube of double volume would have an edge of length ` ( 2 ^c ( 1 / 3 ) ) ` , however that number is not constructible. This is the first part of Metamath 100 proof #8. Theorem 7.13 of [Stewart] p. 99. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux and Saveliy Skresanov, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem _2sqr3nconstr:
  $ wnel (co c2 (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) cconstr $;

--| The polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) + ( ( -u 3 x. ( X ^ 2 ) ) + 1 ) ) ` has no integer roots. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem1 (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co (cneg c3) (co X c2 cexp) cmul) c1 caddc) caddc) cc0) $;

--| The polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) + ( ( -u 3 x. X ) + 1 ) ) ` has no rational roots. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem2 (ph: wff) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cq) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co (co X c3 cexp) (co (co (cneg c3) X cmul) c1 caddc) caddc) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cos9thpiminply . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem3 (O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co O c2 cexp) (co O c1 caddc) caddc) cc0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ cos9thpiminply . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem4 (O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co Z c6 cexp) (co Z c3 cexp) caddc) (cneg c1) $;

--| The constructed complex number ` A ` is a root of the polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) + ( ( -u 3 x. X ) + 1 ) ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem5 (A2 O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co Z (co c1 Z cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 c3 cexp) (co (co (cneg c3) A2 cmul) c1 caddc) caddc) cc0 $;

--| Evaluation of the polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) + ( ( -u 3 x. X ) + 1 ) ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminplylem6 (ph: wff) (A2 D P c_pl Q c_x c_ex F K O X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co Z (co c1 Z cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv P cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv P cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Q cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Q cv1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Q cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq F (co (co c3 X c_ex) (co (co (cfv (cneg c3) K) X c_x) (cfv c1 K) c_pl) c_pl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y (cfv F (co ccnfld cq ces1))) (co (co Y c3 cexp) (co (co (cneg c3) Y cmul) c1 caddc) caddc)) $;

--| The polynomial ` ( ( X ^ 3 ) + ( ( -u 3 x. X ) + 1 ) ) ` is the minimal polynomial for ` A ` over ` QQ ` , and its degree is ` 3 ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpiminply (A2 D P c_pl Q c_x c_ex F K M O X Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co Z (co c1 Z cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv P cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv (cfv P cmgp) cmg) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv Q cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv Q cv1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv Q cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq F (co (co c3 X c_ex) (co (co (cfv (cneg c3) K) X c_x) (cfv c1 K) c_pl) c_pl) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cq cminply) $ >
  $ wa (wceq F (cfv A2 M)) (wceq (cfv F D) c3) $;

--| The complex number ` O ` , representing an angle of ` ( 2 x. _pi ) / 3 ` , is constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpinconstrlem1 (O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wcel O cconstr $;

--| The complex number ` A ` is not constructible. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpinconstrlem2 (A2 O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co Z (co c1 Z cdiv) caddc) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 cconstr) $;

--| Trisecting an angle is an impossible construction. Given for example ` O = ( exp `` ( ( _i x. ( 2 x. _pi ) ) / 3 ) ) ` , which represents an angle of ` ( ( 2 x. _pi ) / 3 ) ` , the cube root of ` O ` is not constructible with straightedge and compass, while ` O ` itself is constructible. This is the second part of Metamath 100 proof #8. Theorem 7.14 of [Stewart] p. 99. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux and Saveliy Skresanov, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem cos9thpinconstr (O Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv (co (co ci (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) c3 cdiv) ce) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co O (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) $ >
  $ wa (wcel O cconstr) (wnel Z cconstr) $;

--| Not all angles can be trisected. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem trisecnconstr {o: setvar}:
  $ wn (wral o (wcel (co (cv o) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) cconstr) cconstr) $;

--| Syntax for a function generating submatrices.
term csmat: class;

--| Define a function generating submatrices of an integer-indexed matrix. The function maps an index in ` ( ( 1 ... M ) X. ( 1 ... N ) ) ` into a new index in ` ( ( 1 ... ( M - 1 ) ) X. ( 1 ... ( N - 1 ) ) ) ` . A submatrix is obtained by deleting a row and a column of the original matrix. Because this function re-indexes the matrix, the resulting submatrix still has the same index set for rows and columns, and its determinent is defined, unlike the current ~ df-subma . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Aug-2020.)
axiom df_smat {i j k m l: setvar}:
  $ wceq csmat (cmpt m cvv (cmpo k l cn cn (ccom (cv m) (cmpo i j cn cn (cop (cif (wbr (cv i) (cv k) clt) (cv i) (co (cv i) c1 caddc)) (cif (wbr (cv j) (cv l) clt) (cv j) (co (cv j) c1 caddc))))))) $;

--| Value of the submatrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatfval {i j: setvar} (K L: class) (M V: class i j):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn) (wcel L cn) (wcel M V)) (wceq (co K L (cfv M csmat)) (ccom M (cmpo i j cn cn (cop (cif (wbr (cv i) K clt) (cv i) (co (cv i) c1 caddc)) (cif (wbr (cv j) L clt) (cv j) (co (cv j) c1 caddc)))))) $;

--| Closure of the rectangular submatrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatrcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S K L M N: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co B2 (cxp (co c1 (co M c1 cmin) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) cmap)) $;

--| Lemma for the next theorems. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S I J K L M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cif (wbr I K clt) I (co I c1 caddc)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cif (wbr J L clt) J (co J c1 caddc)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J S) (co X Y A2)) $;

--| Entries of a submatrix, top left. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smattl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S I J K L M N: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 K cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 L cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J S) (co I J A2)) $;

--| Entries of a submatrix, top right. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smattr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S I J K L M N: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co K M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 L cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J S) (co (co I c1 caddc) J A2)) $;

--| Entries of a submatrix, bottom left. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatbl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S I J K L M N: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 K cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co L N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J S) (co I (co J c1 caddc) A2)) $;

--| Entries of a submatrix, bottom right. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatbr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S I J K L M N: class):
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv A2 csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 (cxp (co c1 M cfz) (co c1 N cfz)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co K M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co L N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J S) (co (co I c1 caddc) (co J c1 caddc) A2)) $;

--| Closure of the square submatrix: if ` M ` is a square matrix of dimension ` N ` with indices in ` ( 1 ... N ) ` , then a submatrix of ` M ` is of dimension ` ( N - 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem smatcl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R S K L M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmat) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (co K L (cfv M csmat)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S C) $;

--| Write a square matrix as a mapping operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Aug-2020.)
theorem matmpo {i j: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (R: class i j) (M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M B2) (wceq M (cmpo i j N N (co (cv i) (cv j) M))) $;

--| The submatrix of the identity matrix obtained by removing the ith row and the ith column is an identity matrix. Cf. ~ 1marepvsma1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem _1smat1 (ph: wff) (R c_1 I N: class):
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) cur) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I I (cfv c_1 csmat)) (cfv (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmat) cur)) $;

--| One case where the submatrix with integer indices, ` subMat1 ` , and the general submatrix ` subMat ` , agree. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem submat1n (A2 B2 R M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel M B2)) (wceq (co N N (cfv M csmat)) (co N N (cfv M (co (co c1 N cfz) R csubma)))) $;

--| Special case where the submatrix is a restriction of the initial matrix, and no renumbering occurs. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Aug-2020.)
theorem submatres (A2 B2 R M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel M B2)) (wceq (co N N (cfv M csmat)) (cres M (cxp (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ submateq . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Aug-2020.)
theorem submateqlem1 (ph: wff) (K M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr K M cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel M (co K N cfz)) (wcel (co M c1 caddc) (cdif (co c1 N cfz) (csn K)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ submateq . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Aug-2020.)
theorem submateqlem2 (ph: wff) (K M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M K clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel M (co c1 K cfzo)) (wcel M (cdif (co c1 N cfz) (csn K)))) $;

--| Sufficient condition for two submatrices to be equal. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Aug-2020.)
theorem submateq {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R: class i j) (E F I J N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) (cdif (co c1 N cfz) (csn I))) (wcel (cv j) (cdif (co c1 N cfz) (csn J)))) (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) E) (co (cv i) (cv j) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J (cfv E csmat)) (co I J (cfv F csmat))) $;

--| If we take a submatrix by removing the row ` I ` and column ` J ` , then the result is the same on the matrix with row ` I ` and column ` J ` modified by the ` minMatR1 ` operator. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Aug-2020.)
theorem submatminr1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R E I J M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wceq E (co I J (cfv M (co (co c1 N cfz) R cminmar1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J (cfv M csmat)) (co I J (cfv E csmat))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the literal matrix conversion function.
term clmat: class;

--| Define a function converting words of words into matrices. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2020.)
axiom df_lmat {i j m: setvar}:
  $ wceq clmat (cmpt m cvv (cmpo i j (co c1 (cfv (cv m) chash) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cfv cc0 (cv m)) chash) cfz) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv m))))) $;

--| Value of the literal matrix conversion function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem lmatval {i j: setvar} (M: class) (V: class i j):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M clmat) (cmpo i j (co c1 (cfv M chash) cfz) (co c1 (cfv (cfv cc0 M) chash) cfz) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 cmin) M)))) $;

--| Entries of a literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem lmatfval {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (I J M N: class) (V: class i) (W: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv W clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword (cword V))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) W) chash) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J M) (cfv (co J c1 cmin) (cfv (co I c1 cmin) W))) $;

--| Useful lemma to extract literal matrix entries. Suggested by Mario Carneiro. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmatfvlem {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (I J: class) (K L: class i) (M N: class) (V: class i) (W: class) (X Y: class i):
  $ wceq M (cfv W clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword (cword V))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) W) chash) N) $ >
  $ wcel K cn0 $ >
  $ wcel L cn0 $ >
  $ wbr I N cle $ >
  $ wbr J N cle $ >
  $ wceq (co K c1 caddc) I $ >
  $ wceq (co L c1 caddc) J $ >
  $ wceq (cfv K W) X $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv L X) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J M) Y) $;

--| Closure of the literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmatcl {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P R: class i) (M N: class) (O V: class i) (W: class) (X: class i):
  $ wceq M (cfv W clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cword (cword V))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W chash) N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) W) chash) N) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq O (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv O cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M P) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lmat22e11 and co. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem lmat22lem {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D M: class) (V: class i):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 c2 cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D))) chash) c2) $;

--| Entry of a 2x2 literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2020.)
theorem lmat22e11 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 c1 M) A2) $;

--| Entry of a 2x2 literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmat22e12 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 c2 M) B2) $;

--| Entry of a 2x2 literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmat22e21 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c2 c1 M) C) $;

--| Entry of a 2x2 literal matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmat22e22 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c2 c2 M) D) $;

--| The determinant of a literal 2x2 complex matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2020.)
theorem lmat22det (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R c_x J M c_mi V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv (cs2 (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 C D)) clmat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv R csg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (co c1 c2 cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M J) (co (co A2 D c_x) (co C B2 c_x) c_mi)) $;

--| The determinant of a matrix with permuted rows is the determinant of the original matrix multiplied by the sign of the permutation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem mdetpmtr1 {i j: setvar} (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P R: class) (S c_x E: class i j) (G M N: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpo i j N N (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cv j) M)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R ccrg) (wcel N cfn)) (wa (wcel M B2) (wcel P G))) (wceq (cfv M D) (co (cfv P (ccom Z S)) (cfv E D) c_x)) $;

--| The determinant of a matrix with permuted columns is the determinant of the original matrix multiplied by the sign of the permutation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem mdetpmtr2 {i j: setvar} (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P R: class) (S c_x E: class i j) (G M N: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpo i j N N (co (cv i) (cfv (cv j) P) M)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R ccrg) (wcel N cfn)) (wa (wcel M B2) (wcel P G))) (wceq (cfv M D) (co (cfv P (ccom Z S)) (cfv E D) c_x)) $;

--| The determinant of a matrix with permuted rows and columns is the determinant of the original matrix multiplied by the product of the signs of the permutations. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem mdetpmtr12 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P Q R: class) (S c_x E: class i j) (G M N: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cfv N csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpo i j N N (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (cv j) Q) M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M D) (co (cfv (co (cfv P S) (cfv Q S) cmul) Z) (cfv E D) c_x)) $;

--| A Laplace expansion of the determinant of a matrix, using the adjunct (cofactor) matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Aug-2020.)
theorem mdetlap1 {j: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D: class j) (R c_x I K M N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co N R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2) (wcel I N)) (wceq (cfv M D) (co R (cmpt j N (co (co I (cv j) M) (co (cv j) I (cfv M K)) c_x)) cgsu)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ madjusmdet . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem madjusmdetlem1 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P Q R: class) (S c_x: class i j) (U: class) (E: class i j) (G I J: class) (K: class i j) (M N W: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cfv (co c1 N cfz) csymg) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv (co c1 N cfz) cpsgn) $ >
  $ wceq U (co I J (cfv M (co (co c1 N cfz) R cminmar1))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cmpo i j (co c1 N cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (cv j) Q) U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N P) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N Q) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J (cfv U csmat)) (co N N (cfv W csmat))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J I (cfv M K)) (co (cfv (co (cfv P S) (cfv Q S) cmul) Z) (cfv (co I J (cfv M csmat)) E) c_x)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ madjusmdet . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Aug-2020.)
theorem madjusmdetlem2 {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i) (B2: class) (D: class i) (P R S: class) (c_x E: class i) (I J: class) (K: class i) (M N: class) (X Z: class i):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) I (cif (wbr (cv i) I cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) N (cif (wbr (cv i) N cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wceq (cif (wbr X I clt) X (co X c1 caddc)) (cfv X (ccom P (ccnv S)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ madjusmdet . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Aug-2020.)
theorem madjusmdetlem3 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P Q R S T: class) (c_x: class i j) (U: class) (E: class i j) (I J: class) (K: class i j) (M N W: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) I (cif (wbr (cv i) I cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) N (cif (wbr (cv i) N cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt j (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv j) c1) J (cif (wbr (cv j) J cle) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cv j)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt j (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv j) c1) N (cif (wbr (cv j) N cle) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cv j)))) $ >
  $ wceq W (cmpo i j (co c1 N cfz) (co c1 N cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) (ccom P (ccnv S))) (cfv (cv j) (ccom Q (ccnv T))) U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co I J (cfv U csmat)) (co N N (cfv W csmat))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ madjusmdet . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Aug-2020.)
theorem madjusmdetlem4 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i j) (B2: class) (D: class i j) (P Q R S T: class) (c_x E: class i j) (I J: class) (K: class i j) (M N: class) (Z: class i j):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) I (cif (wbr (cv i) I cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt i (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv i) c1) N (cif (wbr (cv i) N cle) (co (cv i) c1 cmin) (cv i)))) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt j (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv j) c1) J (cif (wbr (cv j) J cle) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cv j)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt j (co c1 N cfz) (cif (wceq (cv j) c1) N (cif (wbr (cv j) N cle) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) (cv j)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J I (cfv M K)) (co (cfv (co (cneg c1) (co I J caddc) cexp) Z) (cfv (co I J (cfv M csmat)) E) c_x)) $;

--| Express the cofactor of the matrix, i.e. the entries of its adjunct matrix, using determinant of submatrices. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Aug-2020.)
theorem madjusmdet (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D R c_x E I J K M N Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co J I (cfv M K)) (co (cfv (co (cneg c1) (co I J caddc) cexp) Z) (cfv (co I J (cfv M csmat)) E) c_x)) $;

--| Laplace expansion of the determinant of a square matrix. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2020.)
theorem mdetlap {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (D: class j) (R c_x: class) (E: class j) (I J K M N: class) (Z: class j):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wceq K (co (co c1 N cfz) R cmadu) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) R cmdat) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M D) (co R (cmpt j (co c1 N cfz) (co (cfv (co (cneg c1) (co I (cv j) caddc) cexp) Z) (co (co I (cv j) M) (cfv (co I (cv j) (cfv M csmat)) E) c_x) c_x)) cgsu)) $;

--| The predicate "is a T_0 space", using closed sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Aug-2020.)
theorem ist0cld {x3 y1 d: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class d) (D: class x3 y1) (J: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cuni J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel J ct0) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral d (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv d)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv d))) D) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) B2))) $;

--| Given two open maps ` F ` and ` G ` , ` H ` mapping pairs of sets, is also an open map for the product topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2019.)
theorem txomap {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F G H J K L M X Y: class) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wf X Z F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y T G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv Y ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (cfv Z ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv T ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) J)) (wcel (cima F (cv x3)) L) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) K)) (wcel (cima G (cv y1)) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co J K ctx)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 X Y (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima H A2) (co L M ctx)) $;

--| If every equivalence class is closed, then the quotient space is T_1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jan-2020.)
theorem qtopt1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J: class) (X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ct1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Y)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J F cqtop) ct1) $;

--| If an open map's graph in the product space ` ( J tX J ) ` is closed, then its quotient topology is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jan-2020.)
theorem qtophaus {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_sm F H J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (ccom (ccnv F) F) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo X Y F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) J)) (wcel (cima F (cv x3)) (co J F cqtop)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c_sm (cfv (co J J ctx) ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J F cqtop) cha) $;

--| The topology of the unit circle is generated by open intervals of the polar coordinate. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jan-2020.)
theorem circtopn {x3: setvar} (C: class) (F I J: class x3):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 (co c2 cpi cmul) cicc) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (co ci (cv x3) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wceq (co J F cqtop) (cfv (co F crefld cimas) ctopn) $;

--| The function gluing the real line into the unit circle is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jan-2020.)
theorem circcn {x3: setvar} (C: class) (F I J: class x3):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 (co c2 cpi cmul) cicc) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (co ci (cv x3) cmul) ce)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cima (ccnv cabs) (csn c1)) $ >
  $ wcel F (co J (co J F cqtop) ccn) $;

--| For any cover refinement, there exists a function associating with each set in the refinement a set in the original cover containing it. This is sometimes used as a definition of refinement. Note that this definition uses the axiom of choice through ~ ac6sg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Jan-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-reg and ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem reff {v f2: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cref) (wa (wss (cuni B2) (cuni A2)) (wex f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wral v (wss (cv v) (cfv (cv v) (cv f2))) A2))))) $;

--| A locally finite refinement of an open cover induces a locally finite open cover with the original index set. This is fact 2 of ~ http://at.yorku.ca/p/a/c/a/02.pdf , it is expressed by exposing a function ` f ` from the original cover ` U ` , which is taken as the index set. The solution is constructed by building unions, so the same method can be used to prove a similar theorem about closed covers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Jan-2020.)
theorem locfinreflem {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (U J V: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr V U cref) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv J clocfin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (w3a (wfun (cv f2)) (wss (cdm (cv f2)) U) (wss (crn (cv f2)) J)) (wa (wbr (crn (cv f2)) U cref) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) (cfv J clocfin))))) $;

--| A locally finite refinement of an open cover induces a locally finite open cover with the original index set. This is fact 2 of ~ http://at.yorku.ca/p/a/c/a/02.pdf , it is expressed by exposing a function ` f ` from the original cover ` U ` , which is taken as the index set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem locfinref {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (U J V: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (cuni U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss V J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr V U cref) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv J clocfin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (w3a (wf U J (cv f2)) (wbr (crn (cv f2)) U cref) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) (cfv J clocfin)))) $;

--| The "every open cover has an ` A ` refinement" predicate.
term ccref (A2: class): class;

--| Define a statement "every open cover has an ` A ` refinement" , where ` A ` is a property for refinements like "finite", "countable", "point finite" or "locally finite". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_cref {y1 z j: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (ccref A2) (crab j (wral y1 (wi (wceq (cuni (cv j)) (cuni (cv y1))) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cref) (cin (cpw (cv j)) A2))) (cpw (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| The property that every open cover has an ` A ` refinement for the topological space ` J ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem iscref {y1 z: setvar} (A2 J: class) (X: class y1 z):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J (ccref A2)) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral y1 (wi (wceq X (cuni (cv y1))) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cref) (cin (cpw J) A2))) (cpw J))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the "every open cover has an A refinement" predicate. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem crefeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (ccref A2) (ccref B2)) $;

--| A space where every open cover has an ` A ` refinement is a topological space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem creftop (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J (ccref A2)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The property that every open cover has an ` A ` refinement for the topological space ` J ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem crefi {z: setvar} (A2 C J: class) (X: class z):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J (ccref A2)) (wss C J) (wceq X (cuni C))) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) C cref) (cin (cpw J) A2)) $;

--| A formulation of ~ crefi easier to use for definitions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem crefdf {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (B2: class z) (C J: class) (X: class z):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (ccref A2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) A2) ph $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J B2) (wss C J) (wceq X (cuni C))) (wrex z (wa ph (wbr (cv z) C cref)) (cpw J)) $;

--| The "every open cover has an ` A ` refinement" predicate respects inclusion. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem crefss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (ccref A2) (ccref B2)) $;

--| Equivalent definition of compact space in terms of open cover refinements. Compact spaces are topologies with finite open cover refinements. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-reg and ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem cmpcref:
  $ wceq ccmp (ccref cfn) $;

--| Every open cover of a Compact space has a finite refinement. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem cmpfiref {v: setvar} (U J: class) (X: class v):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ccmp) (wss U J) (wceq X (cuni U))) (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv v) cfn) (wbr (cv v) U cref)) (cpw J)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all Lindel&ouml;f spaces.
term cldlf: class;

--| Definition of a Lindel&ouml;f space. A Lindel&ouml;f space is a topological space in which every open cover has a countable subcover. Definition 1 of [BourbakiTop2] p. 195. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_ldlf {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cldlf (ccref (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) com cdom))) $;

--| Every open cover of a Lindel&ouml;f space has a countable refinement. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Feb-2020.)
theorem ldlfcntref {v: setvar} (U J: class) (X: class v):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cldlf) (wss U J) (wceq X (cuni U))) (wrex v (wa (wbr (cv v) com cdom) (wbr (cv v) U cref)) (cpw J)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of all paracompact topologies.
term cpcmp: class;

--| Definition of a paracompact topology. A topology is said to be paracompact iff every open cover has an open refinement that is locally finite. The definition 6 of [BourbakiTop1] p. I.69. also requires the topology to be Hausdorff, but this is dropped here. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_pcmp {j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpcmp (cab j (wcel (cv j) (ccref (cfv (cv j) clocfin)))) $;

--| The predicate "is a paracompact topology". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem ispcmp (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J cpcmp) (wcel J (ccref (cfv J clocfin))) $;

--| Every compact space is paracompact. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem cmppcmp (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ccmp) (wcel J cpcmp) $;

--| Every discrete space is paracompact. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jan-2020.)
theorem dispcmp (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel (cpw X) cpcmp) $;

--| Given a paracompact topology ` J ` and an open cover ` U ` , there exists an open refinement ` v ` that is locally finite. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem pcmplfin {v: setvar} (U J: class) (X: class v):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cpcmp) (wss U J) (wceq X (cuni U))) (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv v) (cfv J clocfin)) (wbr (cv v) U cref)) (cpw J)) $;

--| Given a paracompact topology ` J ` and an open cover ` U ` , there exists an open refinement ` ran f ` that is locally finite, using the same index as the original cover ` U ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem pcmplfinf {f2: setvar} (U J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cpcmp) (wss U J) (wceq X (cuni U))) (wex f2 (w3a (wf U J (cv f2)) (wbr (crn (cv f2)) U cref) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) (cfv J clocfin)))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the spectrum of a ring.
term crspec: class;

--| Define the spectrum of a ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2024.)
axiom df_rspec {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq crspec (cmpt r crg (co (cfv (cv r) cidlsrg) (cfv (cv r) cprmidl) cress)) $;

--| Value of the spectrum of the ring ` R ` . Notation 1.1.1 of [EGA] p. 80. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jun-2024.)
theorem rspecval (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv R crspec) (co (cfv R cidlsrg) (cfv R cprmidl) cress)) $;

--| The prime ideals form the base of the spectrum of a ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jun-2024.)
theorem rspecbas (R S: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv R cprmidl) (cfv S cbs)) $;

--| Topology component of the spectrum of a ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jun-2024.)
theorem rspectset {i j: setvar} (R: class) (S: class i j) (I: class) (J: class i j):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq J (crn (cmpt i I (crab j (wn (wss (cv i) (cv j))) I))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq J (cfv S cts)) $;

--| The topology component of the spectrum of a ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2024.)
theorem rspectopn {i j: setvar} (P R: class) (S: class i j) (I: class) (J: class i j):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq J (crn (cmpt i I (crab j (wn (wss (cv i) (cv j))) P))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq J (cfv S ctopn)) $;

--| The closure of the identity ideal in the Zariski topology. Proposition 1.1.2(i) of [EGA] p. 80. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarcls0 {i j: setvar} (P: class j) (R: class) (V: class i j) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv (csn c_0) V) P) $;

--| The unit ideal ` B ` is the only ideal whose closure in the Zariski topology is the empty set. Stronger form of the Proposition 1.1.2(i) of [EGA] p. 80. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarcls1 {i j: setvar} (B2 R I: class) (V: class i j):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ccrg) (wcel I (cfv R clidl))) (wb (wceq (cfv I V) c0) (wceq I B2)) $;

--| The union of two closed sets of the Zariski topology is closed. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarclsun {i j: setvar} (R: class) (V: class i j) (X Y: class j):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ccrg) (wcel X (crn V)) (wcel Y (crn V))) (wcel (cun X Y) (crn V)) $;

--| In a Zariski topology, the intersection of the closures of a family of ideals is the closure of the span of their union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarclsiin {i j l: setvar} (R T K: class) (V: class i j):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R crsp) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wss T (cfv R clidl)) (wne T c0)) (wceq (ciin l T (cfv (cv l) V)) (cfv (cfv (cuni T) K) V)) $;

--| The intersection of a family of closed sets is closed in the Zariski topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarclsint {i j: setvar} (R: class) (S V: class j):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R ccrg) (wss S (crn V)) (wne S c0)) (wcel (cint S) (crn V)) $;

--| The closed points of Zariski topology are the maximal ideals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarclssn {i j: setvar} (B2 R M V: class):
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R ccrg) (wcel M B2)) (wb (wceq (csn M) (cfv M V)) (wcel M (cfv R cmxidl))) $;

--| The open sets of the Zariski topology are the complements of the closed sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarcls {i j s1: setvar} (P R: class) (S J: class i j s1) (V: class i j):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq J (crab s1 (wcel (cdif P (cv s1)) (crn V)) (cpw P))) $;

--| The Zariski topology is a topology, and its closed sets are images by ` V ` of the ideals of ` R ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zartopn {i j: setvar} (P R: class) (S J: class i j) (V: class j):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) P)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wa (wcel J (cfv P ctopon)) (wceq (crn V) (cfv J ccld))) $;

--| The Zariski topology is a topology. Proposition 1.1.2 of [EGA] p. 80. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zartop (R S J: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The points of the Zariski topology are the prime ideals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zartopon (P R S J: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel J (cfv P ctopon)) $;

--| The Zariski Topology of the trivial ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jul-2024.)
theorem zar0ring (B2 R S J: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wceq (cfv B2 chash) c1)) (wceq J (csn c0)) $;

--| The Zariski topology is T_0 . Corollary 1.1.8 of [EGA] p. 81. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zart0 (R S J: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel J ct0) $;

--| The Zariski topology restricted to maximal ideals is T_1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem zarmxt1 (R S T J M: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmxidl) $ >
  $ wceq T (co J M crest) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel T ct1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ zarcmp . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jul-2024.)
theorem zarcmplem {i j: setvar} (R: class) (S: class i j) (J V: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (cfv R clidl) (crab j (wss (cv i) (cv j)) (cfv R cprmidl))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| The Zariski topology is compact. Proposition 1.1.10(ii) of [EGA], p. 82. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jul-2024.)
theorem zarcmp (R S J: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv S ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel J ccmp) $;

--| The spectrum of a ring ` R ` is a topological space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2024.)
theorem rspectps (R S: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wcel S ctps) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rhmpreimacn . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Jul-2024.)
theorem rhmpreimacnlem {i j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T U: class i j k) (F: class) (G: class j k) (I: class) (J: class k) (K: class i j k) (V: class k) (W: class i k):
  $ wceq T (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv S crspec) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt i B2 (cima (ccnv F) (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S crh)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cfv S cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (cfv R clidl)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt j (cfv R clidl) (crab k (wss (cv j) (cv k)) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cmpt j (cfv S clidl) (crab k (wss (cv j) (cv k)) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cima F I) W) (cima (ccnv G) (cfv I V))) $;

--| The function mapping a prime ideal to its preimage by a surjective ring homomorphism is continuous, when considering the Zariski topology. Corollary 1.2.3 of [EGA], p. 83. Notice that the direction of the continuous map ` G ` is reverse: the original ring homomorphism ` F ` goes from ` R ` to ` S ` , but the continuous map ` G ` goes from ` B ` to ` A ` . This mapping is also called "induced map on prime spectra" or "pullback on primes". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Jul-2024.)
theorem rhmpreimacn {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S: class) (T U: class i) (F G J: class) (K: class i):
  $ wceq T (cfv R crspec) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv S crspec) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv S cprmidl) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv U ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt i B2 (cima (ccnv F) (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S crh)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cfv S cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K J ccn)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of metric identifications.
term cmetid: class;

--| Extend class notation with the metric induced by a pseudometric.
term cpstm: class;

--| Define the metric identification relation for a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
axiom df_metid {x3 y1 d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmetid (cmpt d (cuni (crn cpsmet)) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdm (cdm (cv d)))) (wcel (cv y1) (cdm (cdm (cv d))))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv d)) cc0)))) $;

--| Define the metric induced by a pseudometric. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
axiom df_pstm {x3 y1 z a b d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpstm (cmpt d (cuni (crn cpsmet)) (cmpo a b (cqs (cdm (cdm (cv d))) (cfv (cv d) cmetid)) (cqs (cdm (cdm (cv d))) (cfv (cv d) cmetid)) (cuni (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv d))) (cv b)) (cv a)))))) $;

--| Value of the metric identification relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem metidval {x3 y1: setvar} (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq (cfv D cmetid) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D) cc0)))) $;

--| As a relation, the metric identification is a subset of a Cartesian product. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem metidss (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wss (cfv D cmetid) (cxp X X)) $;

--| ` A ` and ` B ` identify by the metric ` D ` if their distance is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem metidv (A2 B2 D X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 (cfv D cmetid)) (wceq (co A2 B2 D) cc0)) $;

--| Basic property of the metric identification relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem metideq (A2 B2 D E F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 (cfv D cmetid)) (wbr E F (cfv D cmetid)))) (wceq (co A2 E D) (co B2 F D)) $;

--| The metric identification is an equivalence relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem metider (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wer X (cfv D cmetid)) $;

--| Value of the metric induced by a pseudometric ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2018.)
theorem pstmval {x3 y1 z a b: setvar} (D c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv D cmetid) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wceq (cfv D cpstm) (cmpo a b (cqs X c_sm) (cqs X c_sm) (cuni (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) D)) (cv b)) (cv a)))))) $;

--| Function value of the metric induced by a pseudometric ` D ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem pstmfval (A2 B2 D c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv D cmetid) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (co (cec A2 c_sm) (cec B2 c_sm) (cfv D cpstm)) (co A2 B2 D)) $;

--| The metric induced by a pseudometric is a full-fledged metric on the equivalence classes of the metric identification. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem pstmxmet (D c_sm X: class):
  $ wceq c_sm (cfv D cmetid) $ >
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) (wcel (cfv D cpstm) (cfv (cqs X c_sm) cxmet)) $;

--| In a Hausdorff topology, two continuous functions which agree on a dense set agree everywhere. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2017.)
theorem hauseqcn (ph: wff) (A2 F G J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cha) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres F A2) (cres G A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv J ccl)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $;

--| An element of the closed unit interval is positive. Useful lemma for manipulating probabilities within the closed unit interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2016.)
theorem elunitge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| The closed unit interval is a subset of the set of the extended nonnegative reals. Useful lemma for manipulating probabilities within the closed unit interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Dec-2016.)
theorem unitssxrge0:
  $ wss (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) $;

--| Necessary conditions for a quotient to be in the closed unit interval. (somewhat too strong, it would be sufficient that A and B are in RR+) (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2016.)
theorem unitdivcld (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wne B2 cc0)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co cc0 c1 cicc))) $;

--| The closed unit interval forms a topological monoid under multiplication. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Mar-2017.)
theorem iistmd (I: class):
  $ wceq I (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) (co cc0 c1 cicc) cress) $ >
  $ wcel I ctmd $;

--| The union of the closed set is the underlying set of the topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem unicls (J X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (cfv J ccld)) X $;

--| The usual topology on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is the product topology of the usual topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem tpr2tp (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wcel (co J J ctx) (cfv (cxp cr cr) ctopon) $;

--| The usual topology on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is the product topology of the usual topology on ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem tpr2uni (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (co J J ctx)) (cxp cr cr) $;

--| Rewrite the cartesian product of two sets as the intersection of their preimage by ` 1st ` and ` 2nd ` , the projections on the first and second elements. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem xpinpreima (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cxp A2 B2) (cin (cima (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) A2) (cima (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) B2)) $;

--| Rewrite the cartesian product of two sets as the intersection of their preimage by ` 1st ` and ` 2nd ` , the projections on the first and second elements. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem xpinpreima2 (A2 B2 E F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 E) (wss B2 F)) (wceq (cxp A2 B2) (cin (cima (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp E F))) A2) (cima (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp E F))) B2))) $;

--| The complex square of side ` D ` is a subset of the complex circle of radius ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Sep-2017.)
theorem sqsscirc1 (D X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel X cr) (wbr cc0 X cle)) (wa (wcel Y cr) (wbr cc0 Y cle))) (wcel D crp)) (wi (wa (wbr X (co D c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr Y (co D c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co X c2 cexp) (co Y c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt) D clt)) $;

--| The complex square of side ` D ` is a subset of the complex disc of radius ` D ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Sep-2017.)
theorem sqsscirc2 (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel D crp)) (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cre) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cim) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) D clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnre2csqima . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem cnre2csqlem {x3 y1: setvar} (D: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class y1) (H X Y: class):
  $ wceq (cres G (cxp cr cr)) (ccom H F) $ >
  $ wfn F (cxp cr cr) $ >
  $ wfn G cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cxp cr cr)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) (crn F)) (wcel (cv y1) (crn F))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) H) (cfv (cv y1) H) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X (cxp cr cr)) (wcel Y (cxp cr cr)) (wcel D crp)) (wi (wcel Y (cima (ccnv (cres G (cxp cr cr))) (co (co (cfv X G) D cmin) (co (cfv X G) D caddc) cioo))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (co (cfv Y F) (cfv X F) cmin) H) cabs) D clt)) $;

--| Image of a centered square by the canonical bijection from ` ( RR X. RR ) ` to ` CC ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Sep-2017.)
theorem cnre2csqima {x3 y1: setvar} (D F X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X (cxp cr cr)) (wcel Y (cxp cr cr)) (wcel D crp)) (wi (wcel Y (cxp (co (co (cfv X c1st) D cmin) (co (cfv X c1st) D caddc) cioo) (co (co (cfv X c2nd) D cmin) (co (cfv X c2nd) D caddc) cioo))) (wa (wbr (cfv (cfv (co (cfv Y F) (cfv X F) cmin) cre) cabs) D clt) (wbr (cfv (cfv (co (cfv Y F) (cfv X F) cmin) cim) cabs) D clt))) $;

--| For any point of an open set of the usual topology on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` there is an open square which contains that point and is entirely in the open set. This is square is actually a ball by the ` ( l ^ +oo ) ` norm ` X ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem tpr2rico {x3 y1 v u r: setvar} (A2: class y1 v u) (B2: class x3 y1 v u) (G J: class y1 v u r) (X: class v u):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpo u v cr cr (co (cv u) (co ci (cv v) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (crn cioo) (crn cioo) (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co J J ctx)) (wcel X A2)) (wrex r (wa (wcel X (cv r)) (wss (cv r) A2)) B2) $;

--| The restriction of the 'greater than' order to an interval gives the same topology as the subspace topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Apr-2017.)
theorem cnvordtrestixx {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wss A2 cxr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wss (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cicc) A2) $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv cle cordt) A2 crest) (cfv (cin (ccnv cle) (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) $;

--| Domain of the relation of a proset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem prsdm (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wceq (cdm c_le) B2) $;

--| Range of the relation of a proset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem prsrn (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wceq (crn c_le) B2) $;

--| Relation of a subproset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem prsss (A2 B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cproset) (wss A2 B2)) (wceq (cin c_le (cxp A2 A2)) (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp A2 A2))) $;

--| Domain of a subproset relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Sep-2018.)
theorem prsssdm (A2 B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cproset) (wss A2 B2)) (wceq (cdm (cin c_le (cxp A2 A2))) A2) $;

--| Value of the order topology for a proset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem ordtprsval {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (E F: class x3 y1) (K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq E (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le)) B2))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wceq (cfv c_le cordt) (cfv (cfv (cun (csn B2) (cun E F)) cfi) ctg)) $;

--| Value of the order topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtprsuni {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (E F: class x3 y1) (K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq E (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) c_le)) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq F (crn (cmpt x3 B2 (crab y1 (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) c_le)) B2))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wceq B2 (cuni (cun (csn B2) (cun E F)))) $;

--| The order dual generates the same topology as the original order. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtcnvNEW (B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cproset) (wceq (cfv (ccnv c_le) cordt) (cfv c_le cordt)) $;

--| The subspace topology of an order topology is in general finer than the topology generated by the restricted order, but we do have inclusion in one direction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtrestNEW (A2 B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cproset) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (cfv (cin c_le (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) (co (cfv c_le cordt) A2 crest)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ordtrest2NEW . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtrest2NEWlem {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (ph: wff w v) (A2 B2: class) (K: class z w v) (c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctos) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wss (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral v (wcel (cin (cv v) A2) (cfv (cin c_le (cxp A2 A2)) cordt)) (crn (cmpt z B2 (crab w (wn (wbr (cv w) (cv z) c_le)) B2)))) $;

--| An interval-closed set ` A ` in a total order has the same subspace topology as the restricted order topology. (An interval-closed set is the same thing as an open or half-open or closed interval in ` RR ` , but in other sets like ` QQ ` there are interval-closed sets like ` ( _pi , +oo ) i^i QQ ` that are not intervals.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtrest2NEW {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctos) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2))) (wss (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) c_le) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) c_le)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin c_le (cxp A2 A2)) cordt) (co (cfv c_le cordt) A2 crest)) $;

--| Connectedness in the order topology of a toset. This is the "easy" direction of ~ ordtconn . See also ~ reconnlem1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtconnlem1 {x3 y1 r: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (J: class x3 y1) (K: class) (c_le: class r):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv c_le cordt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K ctos) (wss A2 B2)) (wi (wcel (co J A2 crest) cconn) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral r (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv r) c_le) (wbr (cv r) (cv y1) c_le)) (wcel (cv r) A2)) B2) A2) A2)) $;

--| Connectedness in the order topology of a complete uniform totally ordered space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2018.)
theorem ordtconn (B2 J K c_le: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_le (cin (cfv K cple) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv c_le cordt) $ >
  $ wtru $;

--| A mapping that is both a homeomorphism and a monoid homomorphism preserves the "continuousness" of the operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Mar-2017.)
theorem mndpluscn {x3 y1: setvar} (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (c_pl F c_as: class) (J K: class x3 y1):
  $ wcel F (co J K chmeo) $ >
  $ wf (cxp B2 B2) B2 c_pl $ >
  $ wf (cxp C C) C c_as $ >
  $ wcel J (cfv B2 ctopon) $ >
  $ wcel K (cfv C ctopon) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) c_as)) $ >
  $ wcel c_pl (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $ >
  $ wcel c_as (co (co K K ctx) K ccn) $;

--| Deduce a Topological Monoid using mapping that is both a homeomorphism and a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2017.)
theorem mhmhmeotmd (S T F: class):
  $ wcel F (co S T cmhm) $ >
  $ wcel F (co (cfv S ctopn) (cfv T ctopn) chmeo) $ >
  $ wcel S ctmd $ >
  $ wcel T ctps $ >
  $ wcel T ctmd $;

--| Multiplication by a real constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2017.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 16-Mar-2025.)
theorem rmulccn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) C cmul)) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| Addition in the real numbers is a continuous function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2017.)
theorem raddcn {x3 y1: setvar} (J: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wcel (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc)) (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| The operation multiplying an extended real number by a nonnegative constant is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jul-2017.)
theorem xrmulc1cn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv cle cordt) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cxr (co (cv x3) C cxmu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J J ccn)) $;

--| The image of a Cauchy filter by a continuous filter map is a Cauchy filter. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Nov-2017.)
theorem fmcncfil (B2 D E F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv D cmopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv E cmopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel D (cfv X ccmet)) (wcel E (cfv Y cxmet)) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel B2 (cfv D ccfil))) (wcel (cfv B2 (co Y F cfm)) (cfv E ccfil)) $;

--| The extended nonnegative reals are homeomorphic to the closed unit interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Mar-2017.)
theorem xrge0hmph:
  $ wbr cii (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) chmph $;

--| Define a bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` onto ` [ 0 , +oo ] ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Mar-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifcnv {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wa (wf1o (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cpnf) cc0 (cfv (cneg (cv y1)) ce)))) $;

--| The defined function's value in the real. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Apr-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifcv {x3: setvar} (F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cioc)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cneg (cfv X clog))) $;

--| The defined bijection from the closed unit interval onto the extended nonnegative reals is an order isomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Mar-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifiso {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wiso (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) clt (ccnv clt) F $;

--| Expose a homeomorphism from the closed unit interval to the extended nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Apr-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifhmeo {x3: setvar} (F: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wcel F (co cii J chmeo) $;

--| The defined function from the closed unit interval to the extended nonnegative reals is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Apr-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifhom {x3: setvar} (F: class) (J: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wcel Y (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wceq (cfv (co X Y cmul) F) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) cxad)) $;

--| Condition for the defined function, ` -u ( log `` x ) ` to be a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jun-2017.)
theorem xrge0iif1 {x3: setvar} (F: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c1 F) cc0 $;

--| The defined function from the closed unit interval to the extended nonnegative reals is a monoid homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2017.)
theorem xrge0iifmhm {x3: setvar} (F: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wcel F (co (co (cfv ccnfld cmgp) (co cc0 c1 cicc) cress) (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) cmhm) $;

--| The addition operation of the extended nonnegative real numbers monoid is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Mar-2017.)
theorem xrge0pluscn {x3: setvar} (c_pl: class x3) (F: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cc0) cpnf (cneg (cfv (cv x3) clog)))) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cres cxad (cxp (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (co cc0 cpnf cicc))) $ >
  $ wcel c_pl (co (co J J ctx) J ccn) $;

--| The operation multiplying a nonnegative real numbers by a nonnegative constant is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem xrge0mulc1cn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (C: class) (F J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (co (cv x3) C cxmu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J J ccn)) $;

--| The extended nonnegative real numbers monoid forms a topological space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2017.)
theorem xrge0tps:
  $ wcel (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctps $;

--| The topology of the extended nonnegative real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jun-2017.)
theorem xrge0topn:
  $ wceq (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $;

--| The topology of the extended nonnegative real numbers is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jul-2017.)
theorem xrge0haus:
  $ wcel (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) cha $;

--| The extended nonnegative real numbers monoid is a topological monoid. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Mar-2017.) (Proof Shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2017.)
theorem xrge0tmd:
  $ wcel (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctmd $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ xrge0tmd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Mar-2017.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem xrge0tmdALT:
  $ wcel (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctmd $;

--| Relate a limit in a given topology to a complex number limit, provided that topology agrees with the common topology on ` CC ` on the required subset. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jul-2017.)
theorem lmlim (ph: wff) (P F J X Y: class):
  $ wcel J (cfv Y ctopon) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wceq (co J X crest) (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) X crest) $ >
  $ wss X cc $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wbr F P cli)) $;

--| Relate a limit in the nonnegative extended reals to a complex limit, provided the considered function is a real function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jul-2017.)
theorem lmlimxrge0 (ph: wff) (P F J X: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn X F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wss X (co cc0 cpnf cico) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (wbr F P cli)) $;

--| Implication of convergence for a nonnegative series. This could be used to shorten ~ prmreclem6 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jul-2017.)
theorem rge0scvg (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) (wcel (cseq caddc F c1) (cdm cli))) (wcel (csup (crn (cseq caddc F c1)) cr clt) cr) $;

--| A serie with finite support is a finite sum, and therefore converges. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Sep-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem fsumcvg4 (ph: wff) (S F M: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc F M) (cdm cli)) $;

--| A neighborhood of ` +oo ` contains an unbounded interval based at a real number. See ~ pnfnei . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jul-2017.)
theorem pnfneige0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 J) (wcel cpnf A2)) (wrex x3 (wss (co (cv x3) cpnf cioc) A2) cr) $;

--| Express "sequence ` F ` converges to plus infinity" (i.e. diverges), for a sequence of nonnegative extended real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Aug-2017.)
theorem lmxrge0 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2: class k) (F: class) (J: class j):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf (cfv J clm)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) A2 clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) cr)) $;

--| If a monotonic sequence of real numbers diverges, it is unbounded. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Aug-2017.)
theorem lmdvg {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel F (cdm cli))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) cr) $;

--| If a monotonic real number sequence ` F ` diverges, it converges in the extended real numbers and its limit is plus infinity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Aug-2017.)
theorem lmdvglim {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel F (cdm cli))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cpnf (cfv J clm)) $;

--| A univariate polynomial is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem pl1cn (ph: wff) (B2 P R E F J K: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F E) (co J J ccn)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Hausdorff uniform completion relation.
term chcmp: class;

--| Definition of the Hausdorff completion. In this definition, a structure ` w ` is a Hausdorff completion of a uniform structure ` u ` if ` w ` is a complete uniform space, in which ` u ` is dense, and which admits the same uniform structure. Theorem 3 of [BourbakiTop1] p. II.21. states the existence and uniqueness of such a completion. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Mar-2018.)
axiom df_hcmp {w u: setvar}:
  $ wceq chcmp (copab u w (w3a (wa (wcel (cv u) (cuni (crn cust))) (wcel (cv w) ccusp)) (wceq (co (cfv (cv w) cuss) (cdm (cuni (cv u))) crest) (cv u)) (wceq (cfv (cdm (cuni (cv u))) (cfv (cfv (cv w) ctopn) ccl)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)))) $;

--| The norm (function) for a ring of integers is the absolute value function (restricted to the integers). (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem zringnm:
  $ wceq (cfv czring cnm) (cres cabs cz) $;

--| The norm of the ring of the integers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem zzsnm (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (cfv M cabs) (cfv M (cfv czring cnm))) $;

--| Zero of a ` ZZ ` -module. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zlm0 (G W c_0: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $;

--| Unity element of a ` ZZ ` -module (if present). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zlm1 (c_1 G W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv G cur) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv W cur) $;

--| Distance in a ` ZZ ` -module (if present). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem zlmds (D G V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv G cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wceq D (cfv W cds)) $;

--| Topology in a ` ZZ ` -module (if present). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Nov-2024.)
theorem zlmtset (G J V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv G cts) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wceq J (cfv W cts)) $;

--| Norm of a ` ZZ ` -module (if present). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zlmnm (G N V W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv G cnm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G V) (wceq N (cfv W cnm)) $;

--| The ` ZZ ` -module built from a normed ring is also a normed ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zhmnrg (G W: class):
  $ wceq W (cfv G czlm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cnrg) (wcel W cnrg) $;

--| The norm of a group product, provided the ` ZZ ` -module is normed. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem nmmulg (B2 R c_x M N X Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel Z cnlm) (wcel M cz) (wcel X B2)) (wceq (cfv (co M X c_x) N) (co (cfv M cabs) (cfv X N) cmul)) $;

--| The norm of the image by ` ZRHom ` of an integer in a normed ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zrhnm (B2 R L M N Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel Z cnlm) (wcel Z cnrg) (wcel R cnzr)) (wcel M cz)) (wceq (cfv (cfv M L) N) (cfv M cabs)) $;

--| The ` ZZ ` -module of ` CC ` is a normed module. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Feb-2018.)
theorem cnzh:
  $ wcel (cfv ccnfld czlm) cnlm $;

--| The ` ZZ ` -module of ` RR ` is a normed module. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2018.)
theorem rezh:
  $ wcel (cfv crefld czlm) cnlm $;

--| Map the rationals into a field.
term cqqh: class;

--| Define the canonical homomorphism from the rationals into any field. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Oct-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_qqh {x3 y1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqqh (cmpt r cvv (crn (cmpo x3 y1 cz (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv r) czrh)) (cfv (cv r) cui)) (cop (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cdiv) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv r) czrh)) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv r) czrh)) (cfv (cv r) cdvr)))))) $;

--| Value of the canonical homormorphism from the rational number to a field. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhval {x3 y1: setvar} (c_dv: class x3 y1) (R: class) (c_1: class x3 y1) (L: class x3):
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R cvv) (wceq (cfv R cqqh) (crn (cmpo x3 y1 cz (cima (ccnv L) (cfv R cui)) (cop (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cdiv) (co (cfv (cv x3) L) (cfv (cv y1) L) c_dv))))) $;

--| The kernel of the homomorphism from the integers to a ring, if it is injective. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Oct-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2023.)
theorem zrhf1ker (B2 R L c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wb (wf1 cz B2 L) (wceq (cima (ccnv L) (csn c_0)) (csn cc0))) $;

--| The kernel of the homomorphism from the integers to a ring is injective if and only if the ring has characteristic 0 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zrhchr (B2 R L c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wb (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) (wf1 cz B2 L)) $;

--| The kernel of the homomorphism from the integers to a ring with characteristic 0. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem zrhker (B2 R L c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wb (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) (wceq (cima (ccnv L) (csn c_0)) (csn cc0))) $;

--| The preimage by ` ZRHom ` of the units of a division ring is ` ( ZZ \ { 0 } ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem zrhunitpreima (B2 R L c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wceq (cima (ccnv L) (cfv R cui)) (cdif cz (csn cc0))) $;

--| Condition for the image by ` ZRHom ` to be a unit. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Oct-2017.)
theorem elzrhunit (B2 R L M c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wne M cc0))) (wcel (cfv M L) (cfv R cui)) $;

--| The canonical homomorphism from the integers to a ring ` R ` maps additive inverses to additive inverses. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Oct-2025.)
theorem zrhneg (ph: wff) (R I L N: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cneg N) L) (cfv (cfv N L) I)) $;

--| The canonical representation of an integer ` N ` in a ring ` R ` is in the centralizer of the ring's multiplicative monoid. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Oct-2025.)
theorem zrhcntr (ph: wff) (C R L M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv M ccntr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv N L) C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qqhval2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Oct-2017.)
theorem elzdif0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cdif cz (csn cc0))) (wo (wcel M cn) (wcel (cneg M) cn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qqhval2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhval2lem (B2 c_dv R L X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (w3a (wcel X cz) (wcel Y cz) (wne Y cc0))) (wceq (co (cfv (cfv (co X Y cdiv) cnumer) L) (cfv (cfv (co X Y cdiv) cdenom) L) c_dv) (co (cfv X L) (cfv Y L) c_dv)) $;

--| Value of the canonical homormorphism from the rational number when the target ring is a division ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhval2 {q: setvar} (B2 c_dv R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wceq (cfv R cqqh) (cmpt q cq (co (cfv (cfv (cv q) cnumer) L) (cfv (cfv (cv q) cdenom) L) c_dv))) $;

--| Value of the canonical homormorphism from the rational number when the target ring is a division ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhvval (B2 c_dv Q R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wcel Q cq)) (wceq (cfv Q (cfv R cqqh)) (co (cfv (cfv Q cnumer) L) (cfv (cfv Q cdenom) L) c_dv)) $;

--| The image of ` 0 ` by the ` QQHom ` homomorphism is the ring's zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqh0 (B2 c_dv R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv R cqqh)) (cfv R c0g)) $;

--| The image of ` 1 ` by the ` QQHom ` homomorphism is the ring unity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqh1 (B2 c_dv R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cfv R cqqh)) (cfv R cur)) $;

--| ` QQHom ` as a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhf (B2 c_dv R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wf cq B2 (cfv R cqqh)) $;

--| The image of a quotient by the ` QQHom ` homomorphism. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhvq (B2 c_dv R L X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (w3a (wcel X cz) (wcel Y cz) (wne Y cc0))) (wceq (cfv (co X Y cdiv) (cfv R cqqh)) (co (cfv X L) (cfv Y L) c_dv)) $;

--| The ` QQHom ` homomorphism is a group homomorphism if the target structure is a division ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Nov-2017.)
theorem qqhghm (B2 c_dv Q R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdr) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wcel (cfv R cqqh) (co Q R cghm)) $;

--| The ` QQHom ` homomorphism is a ring homomorphism if the target structure is a field. If the target structure is a division ring, it is a group homomorphism, but not a ring homomorphism, because it does not preserve the ring multiplication operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhrhm (B2 c_dv Q R L: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_dv (cfv R cdvr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R czrh) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cfield) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wcel (cfv R cqqh) (co Q R crh)) $;

--| The norm of the image by ` QQHom ` of a rational number in a topological division ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Nov-2017.)
theorem qqhnm (Q R N Z: class):
  $ wceq N (cfv R cnm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel R (cin cnrg cdr)) (wcel Z cnlm) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wcel Q cq)) (wceq (cfv (cfv Q (cfv R cqqh)) N) (cfv Q cabs)) $;

--| The ` QQHom ` homomorphism is a continuous function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Nov-2017.)
theorem qqhcn (Q R J K Z: class):
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv Q ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R (cin cnrg cdr)) (wcel Z cnlm) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wcel (cfv R cqqh) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The ` QQHom ` homomorphism is uniformly continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jan-2018.)
theorem qqhucn (ph: wff) (B2 Q R U V Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co ccnfld cq cress) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv Q cuss) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp B2 B2)) cmetu) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cnlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv R cqqh) (co U V cucn)) $;

--| Map the real numbers into a complete field.
term crrh: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of extension fields of ` RR ` .
term crrext: class;

--| Define the canonical homomorphism from the real numbers to any complete field, as the extension by continuity of the canonical homomorphism from the rational numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Oct-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_rrh {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq crrh (cmpt r cvv (cfv (cfv (cv r) cqqh) (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (cfv (cv r) ctopn) ccnext))) $;

--| Value of the canonical homormorphism from the real numbers to a complete space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2017.)
theorem rrhval (R J K V: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq (cfv R crrh) (cfv (cfv R cqqh) (co J K ccnext))) $;

--| If the topology of ` R ` is Hausdorff, and ` R ` is a complete uniform space, then the canonical homomorphism from the real numbers to ` R ` is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jan-2018.)
theorem rrhcn (ph: wff) (B2 D R J K Z: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cnlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cuss) (cfv D cmetu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv R crrh) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| If the topology of ` R ` is Hausdorff, Cauchy sequences have at most one limit, i.e. the canonical homomorphism of ` RR ` into ` R ` is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Nov-2017.)
theorem rrhf (ph: wff) (B2 D R J K Z: class):
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cnrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cnlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccusp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv R cuss) (cfv D cmetu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr B2 (cfv R crrh)) $;

--| Define the class of extensions of ` RR ` . This is a shorthand for listing the necessary conditions for a structure to admit a canonical embedding of ` RR ` into it. Interestingly, this is not coming from a mathematical reference, but was from the necessary conditions to build the embedding at each step ( ` ZZ ` , ` QQ ` and ` RR ` ). It would be interesting see if this is formally treated in the literature. See ~ isrrext for a better readable version. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
axiom df_rrext {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq crrext (crab r (wa (wa (wcel (cfv (cv r) czlm) cnlm) (wceq (cfv (cv r) cchr) cc0)) (wa (wcel (cv r) ccusp) (wceq (cfv (cv r) cuss) (cfv (cres (cfv (cv r) cds) (cxp (cfv (cv r) cbs) (cfv (cv r) cbs))) cmetu)))) (cin cnrg cdr)) $;

--| Express the property " ` R ` is an extension of ` RR ` ". (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem isrrext (B2 D R Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R crrext) (w3a (wa (wcel R cnrg) (wcel R cdr)) (wa (wcel Z cnlm) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0)) (wa (wcel R ccusp) (wceq (cfv R cuss) (cfv D cmetu)))) $;

--| An extension of ` RR ` is a normed ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextnrg (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel R cnrg) $;

--| An extension of ` RR ` is a division ring. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextdrg (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel R cdr) $;

--| The norm of an extension of ` RR ` is absolutely homogeneous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextnlm (R Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv R czlm) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel Z cnlm) $;

--| The ring characteristic of an extension of ` RR ` is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextchr (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wceq (cfv R cchr) cc0) $;

--| An extension of ` RR ` is a complete uniform space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextcusp (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel R ccusp) $;

--| An extension of ` RR ` is a topological space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem rrexttps (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel R ctps) $;

--| The topology of an extension of ` RR ` is Hausdorff. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Sep-2018.)
theorem rrexthaus (R K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel K cha) $;

--| The uniformity of an extension of ` RR ` is the uniformity generated by its distance. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrextust (B2 D R: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv R cds) (cxp B2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wceq (cfv R cuss) (cfv D cmetu)) $;

--| The field of the real numbers is an extension of the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rerrext:
  $ wcel crefld crrext $;

--| The field of the complex numbers is an extension of the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem cnrrext:
  $ wcel ccnfld crrext $;

--| The topology of the field of the rational numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Aug-2020.)
theorem qqtopn:
  $ wceq (co (cfv crefld ctopn) cq crest) (cfv (co ccnfld cq cress) ctopn) $;

--| If ` R ` is an extension of ` RR ` , then the canonical homomorphism of ` RR ` into ` R ` is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrhfe (B2 R: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wf cr B2 (cfv R crrh)) $;

--| If ` R ` is an extension of ` RR ` , then the canonical homomorphism of ` RR ` into ` R ` is continuous. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2018.)
theorem rrhcne (R J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wcel (cfv R crrh) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The ` RRHom ` homomorphism leaves rational numbers unchanged. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2018.)
theorem rrhqima (Q R: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crrext) (wcel Q cq)) (wceq (cfv Q (cfv R crrh)) (cfv Q (cfv R cqqh))) $;

--| The image of ` 0 ` by the ` RRHom ` homomorphism is the ring's zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem rrh0 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crrext) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv R crrh)) (cfv R c0g)) $;

--| Map the extended real numbers into a complete lattice.
term cxrh: class;

--| Define an embedding from the extended real number into a complete lattice. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
axiom df_xrh {x3 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxrh (cmpt r cvv (cmpt x3 cxr (cif (wcel (cv x3) cr) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv r) crrh)) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cpnf) (cfv (cima (cfv (cv r) crrh) cr) (cfv (cv r) club)) (cfv (cima (cfv (cv r) crrh) cr) (cfv (cv r) cglb)))))) $;

--| The value of the embedding from the extended real numbers into a complete lattice. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem xrhval {x3: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R: class) (U L V: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cima (cfv R crrh) cr) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv R cglb) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R club) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R V) (wceq (cfv R cxrh) (cmpt x3 cxr (cif (wcel (cv x3) cr) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv R crrh)) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cpnf) (cfv B2 U) (cfv B2 L))))) $;

--| The ` ZRHom ` homomorphism for the real number structure is the identity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Oct-2017.)
theorem zrhre:
  $ wceq (cfv crefld czrh) (cres cid cz) $;

--| The ` QQHom ` homomorphism for the real number structure is the identity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Oct-2017.)
theorem qqhre:
  $ wceq (cfv crefld cqqh) (cres cid cq) $;

--| The ` RRHom ` homomorphism for the real numbers structure is the identity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem rrhre:
  $ wceq (cfv crefld crrh) (cres cid cr) $;

--| The class of n-manifold topologies.
term cmntop: class;

--| Define the class of ` N ` -manifold topologies, as second countable Hausdorff topologies locally homeomorphic to a ball of the Euclidean space of dimension ` N ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Dec-2019.)
axiom df_mntop {j n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmntop (copab n j (wa (wcel (cv n) cn0) (w3a (wcel (cv j) c2ndc) (wcel (cv j) cha) (wcel (cv j) (clly (cec (cfv (cfv (cv n) cehl) ctopn) chmph)))))) $;

--| Manifold is a relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2019.)
theorem relmntop:
  $ wrel cmntop $;

--| Property of being a manifold. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2019.)
theorem ismntoplly (J N V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel J V)) (wb (wbr N J cmntop) (w3a (wcel J c2ndc) (wcel J cha) (wcel J (clly (cec (cfv (cfv N cehl) ctopn) chmph))))) $;

--| Property of being a manifold. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jan-2020.)
theorem ismntop {x3 y1 u: setvar} (J N: class) (V: class x3 y1 u):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel J V)) (wb (wbr N J cmntop) (w3a (wcel J c2ndc) (wcel J cha) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv u)) (wbr (co J (cv u) crest) (cfv (cfv N cehl) ctopn) chmph)) (cin J (cpw (cv x3)))) (cv x3)) J))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include infinite summations.
term cesum {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k): class;

--| Define a short-hand for the possibly infinite sum over the extended nonnegative reals. ` sum* ` is relying on the properties of the ` tsums ` , developed by Mario Carneiro. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_esum {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cuni (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k A2 B2) ctsu)) $;

--| An extended sum is a set by definition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Sep-2017.)
theorem esumex {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wcel (cesum k A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Closure for extended sum in the extended positive reals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem esumcl {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral k (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) A2)) (wcel (cesum k A2 B2) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Mar-2017.)
theorem esumeq12dvaf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C D: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 D)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Feb-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumeq12dva {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 D)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Feb-2017.)
theorem esumeq12d {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Feb-2017.)
theorem esumeq1 {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class k):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.)
theorem esumeq1d {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Dec-2016.)
theorem esumeq2 {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class k):
  $ wi (wral k (wceq B2 C) A2) (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2016.)
theorem esumeq2d {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wceq B2 C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem esumeq2dv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Equality deduction for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem esumeq2sdv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.)
theorem nfesum1 {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k (cesum k A2 B2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2020.)
theorem nfesum2 {x3 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 k):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cesum k A2 B2) $;

--| Change bound variable in an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2017.)
theorem cbvesum {j k: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class j k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc j A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wnfc j C $ >
  $ wceq (cesum j A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) $;

--| Change bound variable in an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2017.)
theorem cbvesumv {j k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class j) (C: class k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (cesum j A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) $;

--| Identify the extended sum as any limit points of the infinite sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-May-2017.)
theorem esumid {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C V: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k A2 B2) ctsu)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) C) $;

--| A finite extended sum is the group sum over the extended nonnegative real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Apr-2020.)
theorem esumgsum {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k A2 B2) cgsu)) $;

--| Develop the value of the extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem esumval {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k) (C V: class x3 k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin (cpw A2) cfn))) (wceq (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k (cv x3) B2) cgsu) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (csup (crn (cmpt x3 (cin (cpw A2) cfn) C)) cxr clt)) $;

--| The extended sum is a limit point of the corresponding infinite group sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Mar-2017.)
theorem esumel {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cesum k A2 B2) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k A2 B2) ctsu)) $;

--| Extended sum over the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2017.)
theorem esumnul {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wceq (cesum x3 c0 A2) cc0 $;

--| Extended sum of zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Mar-2017.)
theorem esum0 {k: setvar} (A2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cesum k A2 cc0) cc0) $;

--| Re-index an extended sum using a bijection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Apr-2017.)
theorem esumf1o {k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class n) (B2: class k n) (C: class n) (D F: class k n) (G: class n) (V: class k n):
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wnfc n B2 $ >
  $ wnfc k D $ >
  $ wnfc n A2 $ >
  $ wnfc n C $ >
  $ wnfc n F $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) G) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) C)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) F) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum n C D)) $;

--| Convert from the collection form to the class-variable form of a sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-May-2017.)
theorem esumc {y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff z k) (A2: class k) (B2: class z k) (C: class k) (D V W: class y1 z k):
  $ wnfc k D $ >
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) C) (wceq D B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (ccnv (cmpt k A2 C))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum y1 (cab z (wrex k (wceq (cv z) C) A2)) D)) $;

--| Rewrite an extended sum into a sum on the range of a mapping function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-May-2020.)
theorem esumrnmpt {y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k) (C: class y1) (D: class k) (V: class y1 k) (W: class y1):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel D (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (cdif W (csn c0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj k A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum y1 (crn (cmpt k A2 B2)) C) (cesum k A2 D)) $;

--| Split an extended sum into two parts. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-May-2017.)
theorem esumsplit {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (cun A2 B2) C) (co (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 C) cxad)) $;

--| Extended sum is monotonic. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.)
theorem esummono {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class) (V: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) C)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k C B2) cle) $;

--| Extend an extended sum by padding outside with zeroes. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-May-2020.)
theorem esumpad {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (V: class) (W: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (cun A2 B2) C) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Remove zeroes from an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jun-2020.)
theorem esumpad2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (V: class) (W: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (cdif A2 B2) C) (cesum k A2 C)) $;

--| Addition of infinite sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Mar-2017.)
theorem esumadd {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k) (V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 (co B2 C cxad)) (co (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) cxad)) $;

--| If all of the terms of an extended sums compare, so do the sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumle {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k) (V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) cle) $;

--| Relate a group sum on ` ( RR*s |``s ( 0 [,] +oo ) ) ` to a finite extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Dec-2019.)
theorem gsumesum {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cmpt k A2 B2) cgsu) (cesum k A2 B2)) $;

--| The extended sum is the lowest upper bound for the partial sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Dec-2019.)
theorem esumlub {k a: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (V: class k a) (X: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (cesum k A2 B2) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex a (wbr X (cesum k (cv a) B2) clt) (cin (cpw A2) cfn)) $;

--| Addition of infinite sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumaddf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 (co B2 C cxad)) (co (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) cxad)) $;

--| If all of the terms of an extended sums compare, so do the sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumlef {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k A2 B2) (cesum k A2 C) cle) $;

--| The extended sum of a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Mar-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Jul-2017.)
theorem esumcst {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc))) (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (co (cfv A2 chash) B2 cxmu)) $;

--| The extended sum of a singleton is the term. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2020.)
theorem esumsnf {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k) (M: class) (V: class k):
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) M)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (csn M) A2) B2) $;

--| The extended sum of a singleton is the term. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.) (Shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 2-May-2020.)
theorem esumsn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2 M V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) M)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (csn M) A2) B2) $;

--| Extended sum over a pair. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem esumpr {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (D E V W: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) B2)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (cpr A2 B2) C) (co D E cxad)) $;

--| Extended sum over a pair, with a relaxed condition compared to ~ esumpr . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.)
theorem esumpr2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class k) (D E V W: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) B2)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq A2 B2) (wo (wceq D cc0) (wceq D cpnf))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k (cpr A2 B2) C) (co D E cxad)) $;

--| Rewrite an extended sum into a sum on the range of a mapping function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-May-2020.)
theorem esumrnmpt2 {y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class y1) (D: class k) (V: class y1 k) (W: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel D (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wceq B2 c0)) (wceq D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj k A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum y1 (crn (cmpt k A2 B2)) C) (cesum k A2 D)) $;

--| Formulating a partial extended sum over integers using the recursive sequence builder. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Oct-2017.)
theorem esumfzf {k: setvar} (F: class k) (N: class):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (cesum k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) F)) (cfv N (cseq cxad F c1))) $;

--| Formulating an extended sum over integers using the recursive sequence builder. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Oct-2017.)
theorem esumfsup {k: setvar} (F: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cicc) F) (wceq (cesum k cn (cfv (cv k) F)) (csup (crn (cseq cxad F c1)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Formulating an extended sum over integers using the recursive sequence builder. This version is limited to real-valued functions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2017.)
theorem esumfsupre {k: setvar} (F: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi (wf cn (co cc0 cpnf cico) F) (wceq (cesum k cn (cfv (cv k) F)) (csup (crn (cseq caddc F c1)) cxr clt)) $;

--| Change the index set to a subset by adding zeroes. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumss {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C V: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cdif B2 A2))) (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 C) (cesum k B2 C)) $;

--| The value of the extended sum of nonnegative terms, with at least one infinite term. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumpinfval {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wceq B2 cpnf) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) cpnf) $;

--| Lemma for ~ esumpfinval . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumpfinvallem (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico) F)) (wceq (co ccnfld F cgsu) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) F cgsu)) $;

--| The value of the extended sum of a finite set of nonnegative finite terms. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Jun-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Jul-2019.)
theorem esumpfinval {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (csu k A2 B2)) $;

--| Same as ~ esumpfinval , minus distinct variable restrictions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Aug-2017.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-Jul-2019.)
theorem esumpfinvalf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) (csu k A2 B2)) $;

--| The value of the extended sum of infinitely many terms greater than one. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumpinfsum {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k) (M V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cfn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wbr M B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 M clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k A2 B2) cpnf) $;

--| The value of the extended sum when the corresponding series sum is convergent. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jul-2017.)
theorem esumpcvgval {k n l: setvar} (ph: wff l) (A2: class k) (B2: class l):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv l)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt n cn (csu k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) A2)) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k cn A2) (csu k cn A2)) $;

--| The partial sums in an extended sum form a monotonic sequence. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Aug-2017.)
theorem esumpmono {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k (co c1 M cfz) A2) (cesum k (co c1 N cfz) A2) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ esummulc2 and co. Composing with a continuous function preserves extended sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem esumcocn {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class x3 y1 k) (C: class) (J: class x3 y1 k) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv cle cordt) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co J J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 C) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 cpnf cicc))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cxad) C) (co (cfv (cv x3) C) (cfv (cv y1) C) cxad)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cesum k A2 B2) C) (cesum k A2 (cfv B2 C))) $;

--| An extended sum multiplied by a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem esummulc1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cesum k A2 B2) C cxmu) (cesum k A2 (co B2 C cxmu))) $;

--| An extended sum multiplied by a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem esummulc2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (cesum k A2 B2) cxmu) (cesum k A2 (co C B2 cxmu))) $;

--| An extended sum divided by a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2017.)
theorem esumdivc {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cesum k A2 B2) C cxdiv) (cesum k A2 (co B2 C cxdiv))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ hasheuni . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2016.)
theorem hashf2:
  $ wf cvv (co cc0 cpnf cicc) chash $;

--| The cardinality of a disjoint union, not necessarily finite. cf. ~ hashuni . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Nov-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Jun-2017.)
theorem hasheuni {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wdisj x3 A2 (cv x3))) (wceq (cfv (cuni A2) chash) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) chash))) $;

--| The sequence of partial sums of an extended sum converges to the whole sum. cf. ~ fsumcvg2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Sep-2017.)
theorem esumcvg {k m n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (B2: class m) (F: class) (J: class m):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cesum k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv m)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cesum k cn A2) (cfv J clm)) $;

--| Simpler version of ~ esumcvg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Sep-2017.)
theorem esumcvg2 {k m n l: setvar} (ph: wff m) (A2: class k) (B2: class m l) (C: class m) (J: class m l):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv l)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv m)) (wceq A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n cn (cesum k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) A2)) (cesum k cn A2) (cfv J clm)) $;

--| The value of the extended sum when the corresponding sum is convergent. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Oct-2019.)
theorem esumcvgsum {i k: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2: class k) (B2 F: class i) (L: class i k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv i)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) L cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k cn A2) (csu k cn A2)) $;

--| Express an extended sum as a supremum of extended sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-May-2020.)
theorem esumsup {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum k cn A2) (csup (crn (cmpt n cn (cesum k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) A2))) cxr clt)) $;

--| "Send ` n ` to ` +oo ` " in an inequality with an extended sum. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-May-2020.)
theorem esumgect {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wbr (cesum k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) A2) B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k cn A2) B2 cle) $;

--| All terms of a converging extended sum shall be finite. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Sep-2019.)
theorem esumcvgre {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (V: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cesum k A2 B2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ esum2d (finite case). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Sep-2021.)
theorem esum2dlem {z j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j) (C: class j k) (F: class z k) (V: class z j k) (W: class z):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv j) (cv k))) (wceq F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv k) B2))) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum j A2 (cesum k B2 C)) (cesum z (ciun j A2 (cxp (csn (cv j)) B2)) F)) $;

--| Write a double extended sum as a sum over a two-dimensional region. Note that ` B ( j ) ` is a function of ` j ` . This can be seen as "slicing" the relation ` A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem esum2d {z j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j) (C: class j k) (F: class z k) (V: class z j k) (W: class z):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv j) (cv k))) (wceq F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv j) A2) (wcel (cv k) B2))) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cesum j A2 (cesum k B2 C)) (cesum z (ciun j A2 (cxp (csn (cv j)) B2)) F)) $;

--| Sum over a nonnecessarily disjoint indexed union. The inequality is strict in the case where the sets B(x) overlap. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2019.)
theorem esumiun {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j) (C: class k) (V: class j k) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel C (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum k (ciun j A2 B2) C) (cesum j A2 (cesum k B2 C)) cle) $;

--| Extend class notation to include mapping of an operation to an operation for a function and a constant.
term cofc (R: class): class;

--| Define the function/constant operation map. The definition is designed so that if ` R ` is a binary operation, then ` oFC R ` is the analogous operation on functions and constants. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_ofc {x3 f2 c: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (cofc R) (cmpo f2 c cvv cvv (cmpt x3 (cdm (cv f2)) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv c) R))) $;

--| Equality theorem for function/constant operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofceq (R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cofc R) (cofc S)) $;

--| Value of an operation applied to a function and a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C R F: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C R))) $;

--| Evaluate a function/constant operation at a point. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R F V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv X (co F C (cofc R))) (co B2 C R)) $;

--| The function operation produces a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfn (ph: wff) (A2 C R F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (co F C (cofc R)) A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for function/constant operation value. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfeqd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class x3) (C P R F: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wceq (co (cv y1) C R) (co (cv y1) C P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (co F C (cofc P))) $;

--| General value of ` ( F oFC R C ) ` with no assumptions on functionality of ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfval3 {x3: setvar} (C R F: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel C W)) (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) C R))) $;

--| The function/constant operation produces a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3) (R S T U F: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) T))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 U (co F C (cofc R))) $;

--| The function operation expressed as a mapping. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfval2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C R F: class) (V W X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C R))) $;

--| The function/constant operation expressed as an operation composition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcfval4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (B2 C R F: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (ccom (cmpt x3 B2 (co (cv x3) C R)) F)) $;

--| Left operation by a constant on a mixed operation with a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem ofcc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cxp A2 (csn B2)) C (cofc R)) (cxp A2 (csn (co B2 C R)))) $;

--| Relate function operation with operation with a constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Oct-2018.)
theorem ofcof (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F C (cofc R)) (co F (cxp A2 (csn C)) (cof R))) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the function giving the sigma-algebras on a given base set.
term csiga: class;

--| Define a sigma-algebra, i.e. a set closed under complement and countable union. Literature usually uses capital greek sigma and omega letters for the algebra set, and the base set respectively. We are using ` S ` and ` O ` as a parallel. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_siga {x3 o s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csiga (cmpt o cvv (cab s1 (wa (wss (cv s1) (cpw (cv o))) (w3a (wcel (cv o) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif (cv o) (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ issiga and ~ isrnsiga . The class of sigma-algebras with base set ` o ` is a set. Note: a more generic version with ` ( O e. _V -> ... ) ` could be useful for ~ sigaval . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Oct-2016.)
theorem sigaex {x3 o s1: setvar}:
  $ wcel (cab s1 (wa (wss (cv s1) (cpw (cv o))) (w3a (wcel (cv o) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif (cv o) (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))))) cvv $;

--| The set of sigma-algebra with a given base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Sep-2016.)
theorem sigaval {x3 s1: setvar} (O: class):
  $ wi (wcel O cvv) (wceq (cfv O csiga) (cab s1 (wa (wss (cv s1) (cpw O)) (w3a (wcel O (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1))))))) $;

--| An alternative definition of the sigma-algebra, for a given base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2016.)
theorem issiga {x3: setvar} (S O: class):
  $ wi (wcel S cvv) (wb (wcel S (cfv O csiga)) (wa (wss S (cpw O)) (w3a (wcel O S) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) S) S) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) S)) (cpw S))))) $;

--| The property of being a sigma-algebra on an indefinite base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2016.)
theorem isrnsiga {x3 o: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wb (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wa (wcel S cvv) (wex o (wa (wss S (cpw (cv o))) (w3a (wcel (cv o) S) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif (cv o) (cv x3)) S) S) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) S)) (cpw S)))))) $;

--| A sigma-algebra contains the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Sep-2016.)
theorem _0elsiga (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel c0 S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra contains its base universe set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Oct-2016.)
theorem baselsiga (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv A2 csiga)) (wcel A2 S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is a set of subset of the base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jan-2017.)
theorem sigasspw (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv A2 csiga)) (wss S (cpw A2)) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under countable union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigaclcu (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 (cpw S)) (wbr A2 com cdom)) (wcel (cuni A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under countable union: indexed union version. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Jun-2017.)
theorem sigaclcuni {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k) (S: class):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wral k (wcel B2 S) A2) (wbr A2 com cdom)) (wcel (ciun k A2 B2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under finite union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigaclfu (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 (cpw S)) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (cuni A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under countable union - indexing on ` NN ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigaclcu2 {k: setvar} (A2: class k) (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wral k (wcel A2 S) cn)) (wcel (ciun k cn A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under finite union - indexing on ` ( 1 ..^ N ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigaclfu2 {k: setvar} (A2: class k) (S N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wral k (wcel A2 S) (co c1 N cfzo))) (wcel (ciun k (co c1 N cfzo) A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under countable or finite union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Mar-2017.)
theorem sigaclcu3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (S M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq N cn) (wceq N (co c1 M cfzo))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) N)) (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun k N A2) S) $;

--| Property of being a sigma-algebra with a given base set, noting that the base set of a sigma-algebra is actually its union set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Oct-2016.)
theorem issgon (S O: class):
  $ wb (wcel S (cfv O csiga)) (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wceq O (cuni S))) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is a sigma on its union set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jun-2017.)
theorem sgon (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel S (cfv (cuni S) csiga)) $;

--| An element of a sigma-algebra is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jun-2017.)
theorem elsigass (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S)) (wss A2 (cuni S)) $;

--| Dropping the base information off a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2017.)
theorem elrnsiga (S O: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cfv O csiga)) (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| The property of being a sigma-algebra, universe is the union set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Nov-2016.)
theorem isrnsigau {x3: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wa (wss S (cpw (cuni S))) (w3a (wcel (cuni S) S) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif (cuni S) (cv x3)) S) S) (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) S)) (cpw S)))) $;

--| A sigma-algebra contains its universe set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2017.) (Shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jun-2017.)
theorem unielsiga (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel (cuni S) S) $;

--| Lebesgue-measurable subsets of ` RR ` form a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Oct-2016.)
theorem dmvlsiga:
  $ wcel (cdm cvol) (cfv cr csiga) $;

--| Any power set forms a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Oct-2016.)
theorem pwsiga (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wcel (cpw O) (cfv O csiga)) $;

--| The smallest possible sigma-algebra containing ` O ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2016.)
theorem prsiga (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wcel (cpr c0 O) (cfv O csiga)) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under countable intersections. Deduction version. The proof uses ~ abrexct rather than ~ abrexdom2jm , and so does not require ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Vincent Gonzalez, 19-Aug-2026.)
theorem sigaclci (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 (cpw S))) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wne A2 c0))) (wcel (cint A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under complement relative to its base set. This is immediate from the definition, see ~ issiga , but the library states it nowhere in this form. (Contributed by Vincent Gonzalez, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem difunielsiga (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cdif (cuni S) A2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under pairwise unions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Dec-2016.)
theorem unelsiga (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under class differences. The proof goes through ~ difunielsiga and ~ unelsiga rather than countable intersection, and so does not use ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Sep-2016.) (Proof shortened by Vincent Gonzalez, 17-Aug-2026.)
theorem difelsiga (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) S) $;

--| A sigma-algebra is closed under pairwise intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Dec-2016.)
theorem inelsiga (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) S) $;

--| Building a sigma-algebra from intersections with a given set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigainb (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cin S (cpw A2)) (cfv A2 csiga)) $;

--| The intersection of a collection of sigma-algebras of same base is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem insiga (A2 O: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wcel A2 (cpw (cfv O csiga)))) (wcel (cint A2) (cfv O csiga)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the sigma-algebra generator.
term csigagen: class;

--| Define the sigma-algebra generated by a given collection of sets as the intersection of all sigma-algebra containing that set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_sigagen {x3 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csigagen (cmpt x3 cvv (cint (crab s1 (wss (cv x3) (cv s1)) (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) csiga)))) $;

--| Value of the generated sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigagenval {s1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class s1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv A2 csigagen) (cint (crab s1 (wss A2 (cv s1)) (cfv (cuni A2) csiga)))) $;

--| A generated sigma-algebra is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem sigagensiga (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cfv A2 csigagen) (cfv (cuni A2) csiga)) $;

--| A generated sigma-algebra is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem sgsiga (ph: wff) (A2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 csigagen) (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| The sigma-algebra generated by a collection ` A ` is a sigma-algebra on ` U. A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem unisg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cuni (cfv A2 csigagen)) (cuni A2)) $;

--| A sigma-algebra can be generated from any set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem dmsigagen:
  $ wceq (cdm csigagen) cvv $;

--| A set is a subset of the sigma-algebra it generates. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2017.)
theorem sssigagen (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wss A2 (cfv A2 csigagen)) $;

--| A subset of the generating set is also a subset of the generated sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem sssigagen2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2)) (wss B2 (cfv A2 csigagen)) $;

--| Any element of a set is also an element of the sigma-algebra that set generates. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2017.)
theorem elsigagen (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel B2 (cfv A2 csigagen)) $;

--| Any countable union of elements of a set is also in the sigma-algebra that set generates. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2017.)
theorem elsigagen2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wss B2 A2) (wbr B2 com cdom)) (wcel (cuni B2) (cfv A2 csigagen)) $;

--| The generated sigma-algebra is a subset of all sigma-algebras containing the generating set, i.e. the generated sigma-algebra is the smallest sigma-algebra containing the generating set, here ` A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2017.)
theorem sigagenss (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cfv (cuni A2) csiga)) (wss A2 S)) (wss (cfv A2 csigagen) S) $;

--| Sufficient condition for inclusion of sigma-algebras. This is used to prove equality of sigma-algebras. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem sigagenss2 (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq (cuni A2) (cuni B2)) (wss A2 (cfv B2 csigagen)) (wcel B2 V)) (wss (cfv A2 csigagen) (cfv B2 csigagen)) $;

--| The sigma-algebra generated by a sigma-algebra is itself. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2017.)
theorem sigagenid (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wceq (cfv S csigagen) S) $;

--| The property of being a pi-system. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem ispisys {s1: setvar} (P: class s1) (S O: class):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S P) (wa (wcel S (cpw (cpw O))) (wss (cfv S cfi) S)) $;

--| The property of being a pi-system, expanded version. Pi-systems are closed under finite intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem ispisys2 {x3 s1: setvar} (P: class x3 s1) (S O: class):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S P) (wa (wcel S (cpw (cpw O))) (wral x3 (wcel (cint (cv x3)) S) (cdif (cin (cpw S) cfn) (csn c0)))) $;

--| Pi-systems are closed under pairwise intersections. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jul-2020.)
theorem inelpisys {s1: setvar} (A2 B2 P: class s1) (S O: class):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S P) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) S) $;

--| All sigma-algebras are pi-systems. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sigapisys {s1: setvar} (P: class s1) (O: class):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wss (cfv O csiga) P $;

--| The property of being a lambda-system or Dynkin system. Lambda-systems contain the empty set, are closed under complement, and closed under countable disjoint union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem isldsys {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (S: class y1) (L: class x3 y1 s1) (O: class y1):
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S L) (wa (wcel S (cpw (cpw O))) (w3a (wcel c0 S) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) S) S) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) S)) (cpw S)))) $;

--| The power set of the universe set ` O ` is always a lambda-system. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem pwldsys {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (L: class x3 y1 s1) (O: class y1) (V: class y1 s1):
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wcel (cpw O) L) $;

--| Lambda-systems are closed under disjoint set unions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem unelldsys {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 s1) (A2 B2: class x3 s1) (S: class y1) (L: class x3 y1 s1) (O: class y1):
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) S) $;

--| All sigma-algebras are lambda-systems. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sigaldsys {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (L: class x3 y1 s1) (O: class y1):
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wss (cfv O csiga) L $;

--| The intersection of all lambda-systems containing a given collection of sets ` A ` , which is called the lambda-system generated by ` A ` , is itself also a lambda-system. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem ldsysgenld {x3 y1 t s1: setvar} (ph: wff y1 s1) (A2 L O: class y1) (V: class y1 t s1):
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cpw O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cint (crab t (wss A2 (cv t)) L)) L) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sigapildsys . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sigapildsyslem {x3 y1 t n s1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 t n s1) (A2: class x3 y1 t s1) (B2: class y1 t n s1) (P L: class s1) (N: class y1 t s1) (O: class y1 n):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv t) (cin P L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cv t)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) N)) (wcel B2 (cv t)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif A2 (ciun n N B2)) (cv t)) $;

--| Sigma-algebra are exactly classes which are both lambda and pi-systems. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sigapildsys {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (P L: class s1) (O: class y1):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv O csiga) (cin P L) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ldgenpisys . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Jun-2020.)
theorem ldgenpisyslem1 {x3 y1 t s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff s1 b) (A2: class) (P: class s1 b) (T: class b) (E: class) (L: class b) (O: class) (V: class y1 t s1 b):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cint (crab t (wss T (cv t)) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab b (wcel (cin A2 (cv b)) E) (cpw O)) L) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ldgenpisys . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem ldgenpisyslem2 {x3 y1 t s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff s1 b) (A2: class) (P: class s1 b) (T: class b) (E: class) (L: class b) (O: class) (V: class y1 t s1 b):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cint (crab t (wss T (cv t)) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (crab b (wcel (cin A2 (cv b)) E) (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (crab b (wcel (cin A2 (cv b)) E) (cpw O))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ldgenpisys . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem ldgenpisyslem3 {x3 y1 t s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (A2: class) (P: class s1 b) (T E: class) (L: class b) (O: class) (V: class y1 t s1 b):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cint (crab t (wss T (cv t)) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss E (crab b (wcel (cin A2 (cv b)) E) (cpw O))) $;

--| The lambda system ` E ` generated by a pi-system ` T ` is also a pi-system. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem ldgenpisys {x3 y1 t s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (P: class s1) (T E L O: class) (V: class y1 t s1):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cint (crab t (wss T (cv t)) L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E P) $;

--| Dynkin's lambda-pi theorem: if a lambda-system contains a pi-system, it also contains the sigma-algebra generated by that pi-system. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem dynkin {x3 y1 u s1: setvar} (ph: wff u s1) (P: class u s1) (S: class x3 y1 u s1) (T L O: class) (V: class y1 u s1):
  $ wceq P (crab s1 (wss (cfv (cv s1) cfi) (cv s1)) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab s1 (w3a (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wcel (cdif O (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cint (crab u (wss T (cv u)) (cfv O csiga))) S) $;

--| The property of being a rings of sets, i.e. containing the empty set, and closed under finite union and set complement. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem isros {x3 y1 v u s1: setvar} (Q: class x3 y1 v u s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1 v u):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S Q) (w3a (wcel S (cpw (cpw O))) (wcel c0 S) (wral u (wral v (wa (wcel (cun (cv u) (cv v)) S) (wcel (cdif (cv u) (cv v)) S)) S) S)) $;

--| A ring of sets is a collection of subsets of ` O ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem rossspw {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (Q: class x3 y1 s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S Q) (wss S (cpw O)) $;

--| A ring of sets contains the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem _0elros {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (Q: class x3 y1 s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S Q) (wcel c0 S) $;

--| A ring of sets is closed under union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem unelros {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (A2 B2 Q: class x3 y1 s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S Q) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) S) $;

--| A ring of sets is closed under set complement. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem difelros {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (A2 B2 Q: class x3 y1 s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S Q) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cdif A2 B2) S) $;

--| A ring of sets is closed under intersection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem inelros {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (A2 B2 Q: class x3 y1 s1) (S: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S Q) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) S) $;

--| A ring of sets is closed under finite union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem fiunelros {x3 y1 k s1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 s1) (B2 Q: class x3 y1 k s1) (S: class) (N: class x3 y1 s1) (O: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co c1 N cfzo))) (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun k (co c1 N cfzo) B2) S) $;

--| The property of being a semirings of sets, i.e., collections of sets containing the empty set, closed under finite intersection, and where complements can be written as finite disjoint unions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem issros {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel S N) (w3a (wcel S (cpw (cpw O))) (wcel c0 S) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) S) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw S))) S) S)) $;

--| A semiring of sets is a collection of subsets of ` O ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem srossspw {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S N) (wss S (cpw O)) $;

--| A semiring of sets contains the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem _0elsros {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S N) (wcel c0 S) $;

--| A semiring of sets is closed under union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem inelsros {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (A2: class t s1) (B2: class x3 t s1) (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S N) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) S) $;

--| In semiring of sets, complements can be written as finite disjoint unions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem diffiunisros {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (A2: class t s1) (B2: class x3 t s1) (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S N) (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif A2 B2) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw S)) $;

--| Rings of sets are semirings of sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem rossros {x3 y1 z t s1: setvar} (Q: class z t s1) (S: class t) (N: class x3 y1 z t s1) (O: class x3 y1 z t):
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wceq N (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cin (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wrex z (w3a (wcel (cv z) cfn) (wdisj t (cv z) (cv t)) (wceq (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cuni (cv z)))) (cpw (cv s1)))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S Q) (wcel S N) $;

--| The Borel Algebra on real numbers, usually a gothic B
term cbrsiga: class;

--| A Borel Algebra is defined as a sigma-algebra generated by a topology. 'The' Borel sigma-algebra here refers to the sigma-algebra generated by the topology of open intervals on real numbers. The Borel algebra of a given topology ` J ` is the sigma-algebra generated by ` J ` , ` ( sigaGen `` J ) ` , so there is no need to introduce a special constant function for Borel sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
axiom df_brsiga:
  $ wceq cbrsiga (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) csigagen) $;

--| The Borel Algebra on real numbers is a Borel sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem brsiga:
  $ wcel cbrsiga (cima csigagen ctop) $;

--| The Borel Algebra is a sigma-algebra on the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Dec-2016.)
theorem brsigarn:
  $ wcel cbrsiga (cfv cr csiga) $;

--| The Borel Algebra is a set of subsets of the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jan-2017.)
theorem brsigasspwrn:
  $ wss cbrsiga (cpw cr) $;

--| The union of the Borel Algebra is the set of real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem unibrsiga:
  $ wceq (cuni cbrsiga) cr $;

--| A Borel Algebra contains all closed sets of its base topology. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2017.)
theorem cldssbrsiga (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wss (cfv J ccld) (cfv J csigagen)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the product sigma-algebra operation.
term csx: class;

--| Define the product sigma-algebra operation, analogous to ~ df-tx . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jun-2017.)
axiom df_sx {x3 y1 t s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csx (cmpo s1 t cvv cvv (cfv (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cv s1) (cv t) (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) csigagen)) $;

--| Value of the product sigma-algebra operation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jun-2017.)
theorem sxval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (S T: class) (V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 (crn (cmpo x3 y1 S T (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel T W)) (wceq (co S T csx) (cfv A2 csigagen)) $;

--| A product sigma-algebra is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jun-2017.)
theorem sxsiga (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga)))) (wcel (co S T csx) (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| A product sigma-algebra is a sigma-algebra on the product of the bases. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jun-2017.)
theorem sxsigon (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga)))) (wcel (co S T csx) (cfv (cxp (cuni S) (cuni T)) csiga)) $;

--| The base set of a product sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Jun-2017.)
theorem sxuni (S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga)))) (wceq (cxp (cuni S) (cuni T)) (cuni (co S T csx))) $;

--| The cartesian product of two open sets is an element of the product sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2017.)
theorem elsx (A2 B2 S T V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S V) (wcel T W)) (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 T))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (co S T csx)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of measures.
term cmeas: class;

--| Define a measure as a nonnegative countably additive function over a sigma-algebra onto ` ( 0 [,] +oo ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_meas {x3 y1 m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmeas (cmpt s1 (cuni (crn csiga)) (cab m (w3a (wf (cv s1) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cv m)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv m)) cc0) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) (cv m)) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv m))))) (cpw (cv s1)))))) $;

--| The base set of a measure is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem measbase (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| The value of the ` measures ` function applied on a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Oct-2016.)
theorem measval {x3 y1 m: setvar} (S: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wceq (cfv S cmeas) (cab m (w3a (wf S (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cv m)) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv m)) cc0) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) (cv m)) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv m))))) (cpw S))))) $;

--| The property of being a measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Oct-2016.)
theorem ismeas {x3 y1: setvar} (S: class y1) (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wb (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (w3a (wf S (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)))) (cpw S)))) $;

--| The property of being a measure on an undefined base sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem isrnmeas {x3 y1: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wa (wcel (cdm M) (cuni (crn csiga))) (w3a (wf (cdm M) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)))) (cpw (cdm M))))) $;

--| The domain of a measure is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem dmmeas (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wcel (cdm M) (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| The base set of a measure is its domain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem measbasedom (M: class):
  $ wb (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wcel M (cfv (cdm M) cmeas)) $;

--| A measure is a function over its base to the positive extended reals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem measfrge0 (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wf S (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $;

--| A measure is a function on its base sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2017.)
theorem measfn (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wfn M S) $;

--| The values of a measure are positive extended reals. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem measvxrge0 (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cfv A2 M) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $;

--| The measure of the empty set is always zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem measvnul (S M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $;

--| A measure is nonnegative. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Mar-2018.)
theorem measge0 (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 M) cle) $;

--| If the measure of a given set is bounded by zero, it is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem measle0 (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S) (wbr (cfv A2 M) cc0 cle)) (wceq (cfv A2 M) cc0) $;

--| The measure of a countable disjoint union is the sum of the measures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Dec-2016.)
theorem measvun {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3) (M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 (cpw S)) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 A2 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cfv (cuni A2) M) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) M))) $;

--| The measure the union of two complementary sets is the sum of their measures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Mar-2017.)
theorem measxun2 (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wss B2 A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 M) (co (cfv B2 M) (cfv (cdif A2 B2) M) cxad)) $;

--| The measure the union of two disjoint sets is the sum of their measures. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Mar-2017.)
theorem measun (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) M) (co (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 M) cxad)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ measvuni . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Mar-2017.)
theorem measvunilem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (S M: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (cdif S (csn c0))) A2) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 A2 B2))) (wceq (cfv (ciun x3 A2 B2) M) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv B2 M))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ measvuni . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Mar-2017.)
theorem measvunilem0 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (S M: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wral x3 (wcel B2 (csn c0)) A2) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 A2 B2))) (wceq (cfv (ciun x3 A2 B2) M) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv B2 M))) $;

--| The measure of a countable disjoint union is the sum of the measures. This theorem uses a collection rather than a set of subsets of ` S ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Mar-2017.)
theorem measvuni {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wral x3 (wcel B2 S) A2) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 A2 B2))) (wceq (cfv (ciun x3 A2 B2) M) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv B2 M))) $;

--| A measure is monotone with respect to set inclusion. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem measssd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 M) cle) $;

--| A measure is sub-additive with respect to union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem measunl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cun A2 B2) M) (co (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 M) cxad) cle) $;

--| The measure of the union of a collection of sets, expressed as the sum of a disjoint set. This is used as a lemma for both ~ measiun and ~ meascnbl . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jan-2017.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2017.)
theorem measiuns {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class n) (B2: class k n) (S I: class) (M: class k) (N: class):
  $ wnfc n B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv n) (cv k)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo (wceq N cn) (wceq N (co c1 I cfzo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) N)) (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (ciun n N A2) M) (cesum n N (cfv (cdif A2 (ciun k (co c1 (cv n) cfzo) B2)) M))) $;

--| A measure is sub-additive. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Dec-2016.) (Proof shortened by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Feb-2017.)
theorem measiun {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class n) (S M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel B2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (ciun n cn B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 M) (cesum n cn (cfv B2 M)) cle) $;

--| A measure is continuous from below. Cf. ~ volsup . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jul-2017.)
theorem meascnbl {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (S F J M: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn S F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wss (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom M F) (cfv (cuni (crn F)) M) (cfv J clm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ measinb . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Jun-2017.)
theorem measinblem {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel B2 (cpw S))) (wa (wbr B2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 B2 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cfv (cin (cuni B2) A2) M) (cesum x3 B2 (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) M))) $;

--| Building a measure restricted to the intersection with a given set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem measinb {x3: setvar} (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) M)) (cfv S cmeas)) $;

--| Building a measure restricted to a smaller sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem measres (S T M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) (wss T S)) (wcel (cres M T) (cfv T cmeas)) $;

--| Building a measure restricted to the intersection with a given set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem measinb2 {x3: setvar} (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (cin S (cpw A2)) (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) M)) (cfv (cin S (cpw A2)) cmeas)) $;

--| Division of a measure by a positive constant is a measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem measdivcst (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 crp)) (wcel (co M A2 (cofc cxdiv)) (cfv S cmeas)) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ measdivcst . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem measdivcstALTV {x3: setvar} (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 crp)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S (co (cfv (cv x3) M) A2 cxdiv)) (cfv S cmeas)) $;

--| The Counting measure is a measure on any sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem cntmeas (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) (wcel (cres chash S) (cfv S cmeas)) $;

--| The counting measure is a measure on any power set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2017.)
theorem pwcntmeas (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wcel (cres chash (cpw O)) (cfv (cpw O) cmeas)) $;

--| Counting and Lebesgue measures are different. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Jan-2017.)
theorem cntnevol (O: class):
  $ wne (cres chash (cpw O)) cvol $;

--| The Lebesgue measure function is countably additive. This formulation on the extended reals, allows for ` +oo ` for the measure of any set in the sum. Cf. ~ ovoliun and ~ voliun . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Oct-2017.)
theorem voliune {n: setvar} (A2: class n):
  $ wi (wa (wral n (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) cn) (wdisj n cn A2)) (wceq (cfv (ciun n cn A2) cvol) (cesum n cn (cfv A2 cvol))) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure function is countably additive. This theorem is to ~ volfiniun what ~ voliune is to ~ voliun . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Oct-2017.)
theorem volfiniune {n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class n):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wral n (wcel B2 (cdm cvol)) A2) (wdisj n A2 B2)) (wceq (cfv (ciun n A2 B2) cvol) (cesum n A2 (cfv B2 cvol))) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure is a measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Oct-2017.)
theorem volmeas:
  $ wcel cvol (cfv (cdm cvol) cmeas) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the Dirac delta measure.
term cdde: class;

--| Define the Dirac delta measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2018.)
axiom df_dde {a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdde (cmpt a (cpw cr) (cif (wcel cc0 (cv a)) c1 cc0)) $;

--| Value of the delta measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ddeval1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wcel cc0 A2)) (wceq (cfv A2 cdde) c1) $;

--| Value of the delta measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ddeval0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wn (wcel cc0 A2))) (wceq (cfv A2 cdde) cc0) $;

--| The Dirac delta measure is a measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ddemeas:
  $ wcel cdde (cfv (cpw cr) cmeas) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the 'almost everywhere' relation.
term cae: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the 'almost everywhere' builder.
term cfae: class;

--| Define 'almost everywhere' with regard to a measure ` M ` . A property holds almost everywhere if the measure of the set where it does not hold has measure zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_ae {m a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cae (copab a m (wceq (cfv (cdif (cuni (cdm (cv m))) (cv a)) (cv m)) cc0)) $;

--| 'almost everywhere' is a relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem relae:
  $ wrel cae $;

--| 'almost everywhere' relation for a measure and a measurable set ` A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem brae (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wcel A2 (cdm M))) (wb (wbr A2 M cae) (wceq (cfv (cdif (cuni (cdm M)) A2) M) cc0)) $;

--| 'almost everywhere' relation for a measure ` M ` and a property ` ph ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem braew {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (M: class x3) (O: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (cdm M)) O $ >
  $ wi (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wb (wbr (crab x3 ph O) M cae) (wceq (cfv (crab x3 (wn ph) O) M) cc0)) $;

--| A truth holds almost everywhere. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem truae {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (M: class x3) (O: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (cdm M)) O $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (crab x3 ps O) M cae) $;

--| A conjunction holds almost everywhere if and only if both its terms do. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Oct-2017.)
theorem aean {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (M: class x3) (O: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (cdm M)) O $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wcel (crab x3 (wn ph) O) (cdm M)) (wcel (crab x3 (wn ps) O) (cdm M))) (wb (wbr (crab x3 (wa ph ps) O) M cae) (wa (wbr (crab x3 ph O) M cae) (wbr (crab x3 ps O) M cae))) $;

--| Define a builder for an 'almost everywhere' relation between functions, from relations between function values. In this definition, the range of ` f ` and ` g ` is enforced in order to ensure the resulting relation is a set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_fae {x3 f2 g2 m r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfae (cmpo r m cvv (cuni (crn cmeas)) (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cdm (cv r)) (cuni (cdm (cv m))) cmap)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cdm (cv r)) (cuni (cdm (cv m))) cmap))) (wbr (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) (cv r)) (cuni (cdm (cv m)))) (cv m) cae)))) $;

--| Value of the 'almost everywhere' relation for a given relation and measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem faeval {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (R M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cvv) (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas)))) (wceq (co R M cfae) (copab f2 g2 (wa (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cdm R) (cuni (cdm M)) cmap)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cdm R) (cuni (cdm M)) cmap))) (wbr (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv g2)) R) (cuni (cdm M))) M cae)))) $;

--| The 'almost everywhere' builder for functions produces relations. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem relfae (R M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cvv) (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas)))) (wrel (co R M cfae)) $;

--| 'almost everywhere' relation for two functions ` F ` and ` G ` with regard to the measure ` M ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
theorem brfae {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (D: class x3) (R F G M: class):
  $ wceq (cdm R) D $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co D (cuni (cdm M)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D (cuni (cdm M)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co R M cfae)) (wbr (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) R) (cuni (cdm M))) M cae)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the measurable functions builder.
term cmbfm: class;

--| Define the measurable function builder, which generates the set of measurable functions from a measurable space to another one. Here, the measurable spaces are given using their sigma-algebras ` s ` and ` t ` , and the spaces themselves are recovered by ` U. s ` and ` U. t ` . Note the similarities between the definition of measurable functions in measure theory, and of continuous functions in topology. This is the definition for the generic measure theory. For the specific case of functions from ` RR ` to ` CC ` , see ~ df-mbf . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_mbfm {x3 t f2 s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmbfm (cmpo s1 t (cuni (crn csiga)) (cuni (crn csiga)) (crab f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv t)) (co (cuni (cv t)) (cuni (cv s1)) cmap))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a measurable function from the measurable space ` S ` to the measurable space ` T ` ". Cf. ~ ismbf . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jan-2017.)
theorem ismbfm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) (wa (wcel F (co (cuni T) (cuni S) cmap)) (wral x3 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)) S) T))) $;

--| The property of being a measurable function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jan-2017.)
theorem elunirnmbfm {x3 t s1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cuni (crn cmbfm))) (wrex s1 (wrex t (wa (wcel F (co (cuni (cv t)) (cuni (cv s1)) cmap)) (wral x3 (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv x3)) (cv s1)) (cv t))) (cuni (crn csiga))) (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| A measurable function is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2017.)
theorem mbfmfun (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cuni (crn cmbfm))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $;

--| A measurable function as a function with domain and codomain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem mbfmf (ph: wff) (S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cuni S) (cuni T) F) $;

--| The preimage by a measurable function is a measurable set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Jan-2017.)
theorem mbfmcnvima (ph: wff) (A2 S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima (ccnv F) A2) S) $;

--| The predicate to be a measurable function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.) Remove hypotheses. (Revised by SN, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem isanmbfm (ph: wff) (S T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cuni (crn cmbfm))) $;

--| A measurable function to a Borel Set is measurable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2017.) Remove hypotheses. (Revised by SN, 13-Jan-2025.)
theorem mbfmbfm (ph: wff) (F J M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cdm M) (cfv J csigagen) cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cuni (crn cmbfm))) $;

--| A constant function is measurable. Cf. ~ mbfconst . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem mbfmcst {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S T: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 (cuni S) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cuni T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) $;

--| The first projection map is measurable with regard to the product sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2017.)
theorem _1stmbfm (ph: wff) (S T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres c1st (cxp (cuni S) (cuni T))) (co (co S T csx) S cmbfm)) $;

--| The second projection map is measurable with regard to the product sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2017.)
theorem _2ndmbfm (ph: wff) (S T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres c2nd (cxp (cuni S) (cuni T))) (co (co S T csx) T cmbfm)) $;

--| If the sigma-algebra in the range of a given function is generated by a collection of basic sets ` K ` , then to check the measurability of that function, we need only consider inverse images of basic sets ` a ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2017.)
theorem imambfm {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (S T F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel K cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (cfv K csigagen)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) (wa (wf (cuni S) (cuni T) F) (wral a (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (cv a)) S) K))) $;

--| A continuous function is measurable with respect to the Borel Algebra of its domain and range. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Jun-2017.)
theorem cnmbfm (ph: wff) (S T F J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq S (cfv J csigagen)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (cfv K csigagen)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co S T cmbfm)) $;

--| The composition of two measurable functions is measurable. See ~ cnmpt11 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2017.)
theorem mbfmco (ph: wff) (R S T F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co S T cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom G F) (co R T cmbfm)) $;

--| The pair building of two measurable functions is measurable. ( cf. ~ cnmpt1t ). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Jun-2017.)
theorem mbfmco2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S T F G H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co R S cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co R T cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (cuni R) (cop (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co R (co S T csx) cmbfm)) $;

--| Measurable functions with respect to the Lebesgue measure are real-valued functions on the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Mar-2017.)
theorem mbfmvolf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co (cdm cvol) cbrsiga cmbfm)) (wf cr cr F) $;

--| Measurable functions with respect to the Lebesgue measure. We only have the inclusion, since ` MblFn ` includes complex-valued functions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Jan-2017.)
theorem elmbfmvol2 (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co (cdm cvol) cbrsiga cmbfm)) (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| All functions are measurable with respect to the counting measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Jan-2017.)
theorem mbfmcnt (O V: class):
  $ wi (wcel O V) (wceq (co (cpw O) cbrsiga cmbfm) (co cr O cmap)) $;

--| The base set for the generator of the Borel sigma-algebra on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is indeed ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem br2base {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cuni (crn (cmpo x3 y1 cbrsiga cbrsiga (cxp (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (cxp cr cr) $;

--| An upper bound for a dyadic number. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2ub (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R crp) (wbr (co c1 (co c2 (cfv (co c1 (co c2 R clogb) cmin) cfl) cexp) cdiv) R clt) $;

--| The closed half-spaces of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` cover ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem0 {e f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cuni (cun (crn (cmpt e cr (cxp (co (cv e) cpnf cico) cr))) (crn (cmpt f2 cr (cxp cr (co (cv f2) cpnf cico)))))) (cxp cr cr) $;

--| The sigma-algebra generated by the closed half-spaces of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is a subset of the sigma-algebra generated by the closed sets of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem3 {e f2: setvar} (J: class e f2):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wss (cfv (cun (crn (cmpt e cr (cxp (co (cv e) cpnf cico) cr))) (crn (cmpt f2 cr (cxp cr (co (cv f2) cpnf cico))))) csigagen) (cfv (cfv (co J J ctx) ccld) csigagen) $;

--| The function ` I ` returns closed-below open-above dyadic rational intervals covering the real line. This is the same construction as in ~ dyadmbl . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocival {x3 n: setvar} (I J N X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel X cz)) (wceq (co X N I) (co (co X (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) (co (co X c1 caddc) (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) cico)) $;

--| Dyadic intervals are subsets of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocress {x3 n: setvar} (I J N X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel X cz)) (wss (co X N I) cr) $;

--| Dyadic intervals are Borel sets of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocbrsiga {x3 n: setvar} (I J N X: class x3 n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel X cz)) (wcel (co X N I) cbrsiga) $;

--| Dyadic intervals are Borel sets of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Oct-2017.)
theorem dya2icobrsiga {x3 n: setvar} (I J: class x3 n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wss (crn I) cbrsiga $;

--| For any point and any closed-below, open-above interval of ` RR ` centered on that point, there is a closed-below open-above dyadic rational interval which contains that point and is included in the original interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2icoseg {x3 n b: setvar} (D: class x3 n) (I: class n) (J: class x3 n b) (N X: class n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv (co c1 (co c2 D clogb) cmin) cfl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel D crp)) (wrex b (wa (wcel X (cv b)) (wss (cv b) (co (co X D cmin) (co X D caddc) cioo))) (crn I)) $;

--| For any point and any open interval of ` RR ` containing that point, there is a closed-below open-above dyadic rational interval which contains that point and is included in the original interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Oct-2017.)
theorem dya2icoseg2 {x3 n b: setvar} (E I: class n) (J: class x3 n b) (X: class n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X cr) (wcel E (crn cioo)) (wcel X E)) (wrex b (wa (wcel X (cv b)) (wss (cv b) E)) (crn I)) $;

--| The function returning dyadic square covering for a given size has domain ` ( ran I X. ran I ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocrfn {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class x3 v u n) (I: class n) (J: class x3 v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wfn R (cxp (crn I) (crn I)) $;

--| The dyadic rectangle set is countable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Sep-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem dya2iocct {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class x3 v u n) (I: class n) (J: class x3 v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wbr (crn R) com cdom $;

--| For any point of an open rectangle in ` ( RR X. RR ) ` , there is a closed-below open-above dyadic rational square which contains that point and is included in the rectangle. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Oct-2017.)
theorem dya2iocnrect {x3 v u e f2 n b: setvar} (A2: class x3 v u n) (B2: class x3 v u e f2 n b) (R: class x3 v u n) (I: class e f2 n b) (J: class x3 v u e f2 n b) (X: class v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crn (cmpo e f2 (crn cioo) (crn cioo) (cxp (cv e) (cv f2)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X (cxp cr cr)) (wcel A2 B2) (wcel X A2)) (wrex b (wa (wcel X (cv b)) (wss (cv b) A2)) (crn R)) $;

--| For any point of an open set of the usual topology on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` there is a closed-below open-above dyadic rational square which contains that point and is entirely in the open set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocnei {x3 v u n b: setvar} (A2 R: class x3 v u n) (I: class n b) (J: class x3 v u n b) (X: class v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co J J ctx)) (wcel X A2)) (wrex b (wa (wcel X (cv b)) (wss (cv b) A2)) (crn R)) $;

--| Every open set of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is a union of closed-below open-above dyadic rational rectangular subsets of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . This union must be a countable union by ~ dya2iocct . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocuni {x3 v u n c: setvar} (A2: class x3 n) (R: class x3 v u n) (I: class n c) (J: class x3 v u n c):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co J J ctx)) (wrex c (wceq (cuni (cv c)) A2) (cpw (crn R))) $;

--| The dyadic rectangular set collection covers ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Sep-2017.)
theorem dya2iocucvr {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class x3 v u n) (I: class n) (J: class x3 v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (crn R)) (cxp cr cr) $;

--| The Borel algebra on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is a subset of the sigma-algebra generated by the dyadic closed-below, open-above rectangular subsets of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . This is a step of the proof of Proposition 1.1.5 of [Cohn] p. 4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem1 {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class v u) (I: class n) (J: class v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wss (cfv (co J J ctx) csigagen) (cfv (crn R) csigagen) $;

--| The sigma-algebra generated by the dyadic closed-below, open-above rectangular subsets of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is a subset of the sigma-algebra generated by the closed half-spaces of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . The proof goes by noting the fact that the dyadic rectangles are intersections of a 'vertical band' and an 'horizontal band', which themselves are differences of closed half-spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem2 {x3 v u e f2 n: setvar} (R: class v u e f2) (I: class e f2 n) (J: class v u e f2 n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wss (cfv (crn R) csigagen) (cfv (cun (crn (cmpt e cr (cxp (co (cv e) cpnf cico) cr))) (crn (cmpt f2 cr (cxp cr (co (cv f2) cpnf cico))))) csigagen) $;

--| The Borel algebra on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` is generated by the dyadic closed-below, open-above rectangular subsets of ` ( RR X. RR ) ` . Proposition 1.1.5 of [Cohn] p. 4 . Note that the interval used in this formalization are closed-below, open-above instead of open-below, closed-above in the proof as they are ultimately generated by the floor function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem4 {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class v u) (I: class n) (J: class v u n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co J J ctx) csigagen) (cfv (crn R) csigagen) $;

--| First direction for ~ sxbrsiga . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Sep-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Oct-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem5 {x3 v u n: setvar} (R: class v u) (I J: class n):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpo x3 n cz cz (co (co (cv x3) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) cico)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpo u v (crn I) (crn I) (cxp (cv u) (cv v))) $ >
  $ wss (cfv (co J J ctx) csigagen) (co cbrsiga cbrsiga csx) $;

--| First direction for ~ sxbrsiga , same as sxbrsigalem6, dealing with the antecedents. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem sxbrsigalem6 (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wss (cfv (co J J ctx) csigagen) (co cbrsiga cbrsiga csx) $;

--| The product sigma-algebra ` ( BrSiga sX BrSiga ) ` is the Borel algebra on ` ( RR X. RR ) ` See example 5.1.1 of [Cohn] p. 143 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Oct-2017.)
theorem sxbrsiga (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq (co cbrsiga cbrsiga csx) (cfv (co J J ctx) csigagen) $;

--| Class declaration for the outer measure construction function.
term coms: class;

--| Define a function constructing an outer measure. See ~ omsval for its value. Definition 1.5 of [Bogachev] p. 16. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
axiom df_oms {x3 y1 z r a: setvar}:
  $ wceq coms (cmpt r cvv (cmpt a (cpw (cuni (cdm (cv r)))) (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 (crab z (wa (wss (cv a) (cuni (cv z))) (wbr (cv z) com cdom)) (cpw (cdm (cv r)))) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) (cv r))))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) clt))) $;

--| Value of the function mapping a content function to the corresponding outer measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem omsval {x3 y1 z a: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cvv) (wceq (cfv R coms) (cmpt a (cpw (cuni (cdm R))) (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 (crab z (wa (wss (cv a) (cuni (cv z))) (wbr (cv z) com cdom)) (cpw (cdm R))) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) R)))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) clt))) $;

--| Value of the outer measure evaluated for a given set ` A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem omsfval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 Q R V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel Q V) (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) (wss A2 (cuni Q))) (wceq (cfv A2 (cfv R coms)) (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 (crab z (wa (wss A2 (cuni (cv z))) (wbr (cv z) com cdom)) (cpw (cdm R))) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) R)))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) clt)) $;

--| A closure lemma for the constructed outer measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2019.)
theorem omscl {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 Q R V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel Q V) (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) (wcel A2 (cpw (cuni (cdm R))))) (wss (crn (cmpt x3 (crab z (wa (wss A2 (cuni (cv z))) (wbr (cv z) com cdom)) (cpw (cdm R))) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) R)))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $;

--| A constructed outer measure is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem omsf (Q R V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel Q V) (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R)) (wf (cpw (cuni (cdm R))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (cfv R coms)) $;

--| A constructed outer measure evaluates to zero for the empty set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem oms0 (ph: wff) (Q R M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R coms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 (cdm R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 R) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $;

--| A constructed outer measure is monotone. Note in Example 1.5.2 of [Bogachev] p. 17. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem omsmon (ph: wff) (A2 B2 Q R M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R coms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cuni Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 M) cle) $;

--| For any small margin ` E ` , we can find a covering approaching the outer measure of a set ` A ` by that margin. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.)
theorem omssubaddlem {x3 z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 Q R: class) (E M: class z w) (V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R coms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 M) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr (cesum w (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) R)) (co (cfv A2 M) E caddc) clt) (crab z (wa (wss A2 (cuni (cv z))) (wbr (cv z) com cdom)) (cpw (cdm R)))) $;

--| A constructed outer measure is countably sub-additive. Lemma 1.5.4 of [Bogachev] p. 17. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2019.) (Revised by AV, 4-Oct-2020.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-reg . (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem omssubadd {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (Q R: class) (M: class y1) (V X: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R coms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) X)) (wss A2 (cuni Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (ciun y1 X A2) M) (cesum y1 X (cfv A2 M)) cle) $;

--| Class declaration for the Caratheodory sigma-Algebra construction.
term ccarsg: class;

--| Define a function constructing Caratheodory measurable sets for a given outer measure. See ~ carsgval for its value. Definition 1.11.2 of [Bogachev] p. 41. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
axiom df_carsg {e m a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccarsg (cmpt m cvv (crab a (wral e (wceq (co (cfv (cin (cv e) (cv a)) (cv m)) (cfv (cdif (cv e) (cv a)) (cv m)) cxad) (cfv (cv e) (cv m))) (cpw (cuni (cdm (cv m))))) (cpw (cuni (cdm (cv m)))))) $;

--| Value of the Caratheodory sigma-Algebra construction function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem carsgval {e a: setvar} (ph: wff) (M O: class) (V: class e a):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M ccarsg) (crab a (wral e (wceq (co (cfv (cin (cv e) (cv a)) M) (cfv (cdif (cv e) (cv a)) M) cxad) (cfv (cv e) M)) (cpw O)) (cpw O))) $;

--| Closure of the Caratheodory measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem carsgcl (ph: wff) (M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv M ccarsg) (cpw O)) $;

--| Property of being a Caratheodory measurable set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem elcarsg {e: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 M O: class) (V: class e):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) (wa (wss A2 O) (wral e (wceq (co (cfv (cin (cv e) A2) M) (cfv (cdif (cv e) A2) M) cxad) (cfv (cv e) M)) (cpw O)))) $;

--| The universe set, ` O ` , is Caratheodory measurable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem baselcarsg (ph: wff) (M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The empty set is Caratheodory measurable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-May-2020.)
theorem _0elcarsg (ph: wff) (M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The union of all Caratheodory measurable sets is the universe. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2020.)
theorem carsguni (ph: wff) (M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (cfv M ccarsg)) O) $;

--| Caratheodory measurable sets are subsets of the universe. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-May-2020.)
theorem elcarsgss (ph: wff) (A2 M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 O) $;

--| The Caratheodory measurable sets are closed under complement. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem difelcarsg (ph: wff) (A2 M O V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif O A2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The Caratheodory measurable sets are closed under intersection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 18-May-2020.)
theorem inelcarsg {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M O: class) (V: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv a) (cpw O)) (wcel (cv b) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cun (cv a) (cv b)) M) (co (cfv (cv a) M) (cfv (cv b) M) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cin A2 B2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The Caratheodory-measurable sets are closed under pairwise unions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-May-2020.)
theorem unelcarsg {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M O: class) (V: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv a) (cpw O)) (wcel (cv b) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cun (cv a) (cv b)) M) (co (cfv (cv a) M) (cfv (cv b) M) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The Caratheodory-measurable sets are closed under class difference. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-May-2020.)
theorem difelcarsg2 {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M O: class) (V: class a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv a) (cpw O)) (wcel (cv b) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cun (cv a) (cv b)) M) (co (cfv (cv a) M) (cfv (cv b) M) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif A2 B2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| Utility lemma: Apply monotony. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-May-2020.)
theorem carsgmon {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cpw O)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 M) (cfv B2 M) cle) $;

--| Lemma for the following theorems. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2020.)
theorem carsgsigalem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (e f2: setvar) (M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv e) (cpw O)) (wcel (cv f2) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cun (cv e) (cv f2)) M) (co (cfv (cv e) M) (cfv (cv f2) M) cxad) cle) $;

--| The Caratheodory measurable sets are closed under finite union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2020.)
theorem fiunelcarsg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ carsgclctun . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-May-2020.)
theorem carsgclctunlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 E M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj y1 A2 (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cpw O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin E (cuni A2)) M) (cesum y1 A2 (cfv (cin E (cv y1)) M))) $;

--| The outer measure is countably superadditive on Caratheodory measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-May-2020.)
theorem carsggect {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 M O: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel c0 A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj y1 A2 (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cesum z A2 (cfv (cv z) M)) (cfv (cuni A2) M) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ carsgclctun . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-May-2020.)
theorem carsgclctunlem2 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (E M O: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj k cn A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn)) (wcel A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cpw O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv E M) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E (ciun k cn A2)) M) (cfv (cdif E (ciun k cn A2)) M) cxad) (cfv E M) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ carsgclctun . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-May-2020.)
theorem carsgclctunlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 E M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cpw O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cin E (cuni A2)) M) (cfv (cdif E (cuni A2)) M) cxad) (cfv E M) cle) $;

--| The Caratheodory measurable sets are closed under countable union. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-May-2020.)
theorem carsgclctun {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cfv M ccarsg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) (cfv M ccarsg)) $;

--| The Caratheodory measurable sets constructed from outer measures form a Sigma-algebra. Statement (iii) of Theorem 1.11.4 of [Bogachev] p. 42. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem carsgsiga {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (M O: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel O V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cpw O) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 M) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) M) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) M)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw O))) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cv y1) M) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv M ccarsg) (cfv O csiga)) $;

--| The restriction of a constructed outer measure to Caratheodory measurable sets is a measure. This theorem allows to construct measures from pre-measures with the required characteristics, as for the Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-May-2020.)
theorem omsmeas (ph: wff) (Q R S M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R coms) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M ccarsg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Q (co cc0 cpnf cicc) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 (cdm R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 R) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres M S) (cfv S cmeas)) $;

--| This theorem's hypotheses define a pre-measure. A pre-measure is monotone. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem pmeasmono {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P R: class):
  $ wi ph (wf R (co cc0 cpnf cicc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 P) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) R) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) P) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cdif B2 A2) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 P) (cfv B2 P) cle) $;

--| A premeasure on a ring of sets is additive on disjoint countable collections. This is called sigma-additivity. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem pmeasadd {x3 y1 k s1: setvar} (ph: wff s1) (A2: class s1) (B2: class k s1) (P: class s1) (Q: class x3 y1 k s1) (R: class s1) (O: class x3 y1 k s1):
  $ wi ph (wf R (co cc0 cpnf cicc) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 P) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wbr (cv x3) com cdom) (wss (cv x3) R) (wdisj y1 (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cv x3)) P) (cesum y1 (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) P))) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab s1 (wa (wcel c0 (cv s1)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wa (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1)) (wcel (cdif (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv s1))) (cv s1)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cpw O))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj k A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (ciun k A2 B2) P) (cesum k A2 (cfv B2 P))) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2017.)
theorem itgeq12dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 D)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the (measure) Bochner integral.
term citgm: class;

--| Extend class notation with the integral metric for simple functions.
term csitm: class;

--| Extend class notation with the integral of simple functions.
term csitg: class;

--| Define the integral of simple functions from a measurable space ` dom m ` to a generic space ` w ` equipped with the right scalar product. ` w ` will later be required to be a Banach space. These simple functions are required to take finitely many different values: this is expressed by ` ran g e. Fin ` in the definition. Moreover, for each ` x ` , the pre-image ` ( ``' g " { x } ) ` is requested to be measurable, of finite measure. In this definition, ` ( sigaGen `` ( TopOpen `` w ) ) ` is the Borel sigma-algebra on ` w ` , and the functions ` g ` range over the measurable functions over that Borel algebra. Definition 2.4.1 of [Bogachev] p. 118. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_sitg {x3 w f2 g2 m: setvar}:
  $ wceq csitg (cmpo w m cvv (cuni (crn cmeas)) (cmpt f2 (crab g2 (wa (wcel (crn (cv g2)) cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (csn (cv x3))) (cv m)) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (cdif (crn (cv g2)) (csn (cfv (cv w) c0g))))) (co (cdm (cv m)) (cfv (cfv (cv w) ctopn) csigagen) cmbfm)) (co (cv w) (cmpt x3 (cdif (crn (cv f2)) (csn (cfv (cv w) c0g))) (co (cfv (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) (cv m)) (cfv (cfv (cv w) csca) crrh)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cvsca))) cgsu))) $;

--| Define the integral metric for simple functions, as the integral of the distances between the function values. Since distances take nonnegative values in ` RR* ` , the range structure for this integral is ` ( RR*s |``s ( 0 [,] +oo ) ) ` . See definition 2.3.1 of [Bogachev] p. 116. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Oct-2017.)
axiom df_sitm {w f2 g2 m: setvar}:
  $ wceq csitm (cmpo w m cvv (cuni (crn cmeas)) (cmpo f2 g2 (cdm (co (cv w) (cv m) csitg)) (cdm (co (cv w) (cv m) csitg)) (cfv (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cof (cfv (cv w) cds))) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) (cv m) csitg)))) $;

--| Value of the simple function integral builder for a given space ` W ` and measure ` M ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2018.)
theorem sitgval {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 f2 g2) (B2: class x3 g2) (S: class x3) (c_x H: class x3 g2) (J: class x3 f2 g2) (M: class) (V: class x3 f2 g2) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co W M csitg) (cmpt f2 (crab g2 (wa (wcel (crn (cv g2)) cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) (csn (cv x3))) M) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (cdif (crn (cv g2)) (csn c_0)))) (co (cdm M) S cmbfm)) (co W (cmpt x3 (cdif (crn (cv f2)) (csn c_0)) (co (cfv (cfv (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (csn (cv x3))) M) H) (cv x3) c_x)) cgsu))) $;

--| The predicate " ` F ` is a simple function" relative to the Bochner integral. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem issibf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 S c_x: class x3) (F: class) (H J: class x3) (M: class) (V: class x3) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) (w3a (wcel F (co (cdm M) S cmbfm)) (wcel (crn F) cfn) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) M) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (cdif (crn F) (csn c_0))))) $;

--| The constant zero function is a simple function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Mar-2018.)
theorem sibf0 (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp (cuni (cdm M)) (csn c_0)) (cdm (co W M csitg))) $;

--| A simple function is measurable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem sibfmbl (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cdm M) S cmbfm)) $;

--| A simple function is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem sibff (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cuni (cdm M)) (cuni J) F) $;

--| A simple function has finite range. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem sibfrn (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) cfn) $;

--| Any preimage of a singleton by a simple function is measurable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Feb-2018.)
theorem sibfima (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (cdif (crn F) (csn c_0)))) (wcel (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn A2)) M) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $;

--| The measure of the intersection of any two preimages by simple functions is a real number. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Mar-2018.)
theorem sibfinima (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F G H J M V W X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ct1) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph (wcel X (crn F)) (wcel Y (crn G))) (wo (wne X c_0) (wne Y c_0))) (wcel (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn X)) (cima (ccnv G) (csn Y))) M) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $;

--| Applying function operations on simple functions results in simple functions with regard to the destination space, provided the operation fulfills a simple condition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Mar-2018.)
theorem sibfof (ph: wff) (B2 C c_pl S c_x F G H J K M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv K cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp B2 B2) C c_pl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ct1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c_0 c_0 c_pl) (cfv K c0g)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof c_pl)) (cdm (co K M csitg))) $;

--| Value of the Bochner integral for a simple function ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2018.)
theorem sitgfval {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x: class x3) (F: class) (H J: class x3) (M: class) (V: class x3) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F (co W M csitg)) (co W (cmpt x3 (cdif (crn F) (csn c_0)) (co (cfv (cfv (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv x3))) M) H) (cv x3) c_x)) cgsu)) $;

--| Closure of the Bochner integral on simple functions, generic version. See ~ sitgclbn for the version for Banach spaces. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Feb-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Dec-2019.)
theorem sitgclg {x3 m: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (D: class x3 m) (S c_x: class x3) (F: class) (G H: class x3) (J: class x3 m) (M: class) (V: class x3 m) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (cfv G cds) (cxp (cfv G cbs) (cfv G cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv W csca) crrext) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv m) (cima H (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel (co (cv m) (cv x3) c_x) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co W M csitg)) B2) $;

--| Closure of the Bochner integral on a simple function. This version is specific to Banach spaces, with additional conditions on its scalar field. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Feb-2018.)
theorem sitgclbn (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cbn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv W csca) crrext) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co W M csitg)) B2) $;

--| Closure of the Bochner integral on a simple function. This version is specific to Banach spaces on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Feb-2018.)
theorem sitgclcn (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cbn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W csca) ccnfld) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co W M csitg)) B2) $;

--| Closure of the Bochner integral on a simple function. This version is specific to Banach spaces on the real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Feb-2018.)
theorem sitgclre (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x F H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cbn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv W csca) crefld) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F (co W M csitg)) B2) $;

--| The integral of the constant zero function is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Mar-2018.)
theorem sitg0 (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x H J M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cxp (cuni (cdm M)) (csn c_0)) (co W M csitg)) c_0) $;

--| The integral for simple functions is itself a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2018.)
theorem sitgf {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 S c_x H: class) (J: class f2) (M: class) (V: class f2) (W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) (cdm (co W M csitg)))) (wcel (cfv (cv f2) (co W M csitg)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm (co W M csitg)) B2 (co W M csitg)) $;

--| Lemma for * sitgadd . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Mar-2019.)
theorem sitgaddlemb {p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (B2 c_pl S c_x F G H J M V W c_0: class p):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv W ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv J csigagen) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv W c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv (cfv W csca) crrh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W ctps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co W (cima H (co cc0 cpnf cico)) cresv) cslmd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ct1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv W csca) crrext) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv p) (cdif (cxp (crn F) (crn G)) (csn (cop c_0 c_0))))) (wcel (co (cfv (cfv (cin (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv p) c1st))) (cima (ccnv G) (csn (cfv (cv p) c2nd)))) M) H) (cfv (cv p) c2nd) c_x) B2) $;

--| Value of the simple function integral metric for a given space ` W ` and measure ` M ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2018.)
theorem sitmval {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (D: class f2 g2) (M: class) (V: class f2 g2) (W: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co W M csitm) (cmpo f2 g2 (cdm (co W M csitg)) (cdm (co W M csitg)) (cfv (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cof D)) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) M csitg)))) $;

--| Value of the integral distance between two simple functions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2018.)
theorem sitmfval (ph: wff) (D F G M V W: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F G (co W M csitm)) (cfv (co F G (cof D)) (co (co cxrs (co cc0 cpnf cicc) cress) M csitg))) $;

--| Closure of the integral distance between two simple functions, for an extended metric space. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2018.)
theorem sitmcl (ph: wff) (F G M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel W cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cxms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (cdm (co W M csitg))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (co W M csitm)) (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $;

--| The integral metric as a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Mar-2018.)
theorem sitmf (ph: wff) (M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel W cmnd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cxms) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cuni (crn cmeas))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cxp (cdm (co W M csitg)) (cdm (co W M csitg))) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (co W M csitm)) $;

--| Define the Bochner integral as the extension by continuity of the Bochnel integral for simple functions. Bogachev first defines 'fundamental in the mean' sequences, in definition 2.3.1 of [Bogachev] p. 116, and notes that those are actually Cauchy sequences for the pseudometric ` ( w sitm m ) ` . He then defines the Bochner integral in chapter 2.4.4 in [Bogachev] p. 118. The definition of the Lebesgue integral, ~ df-itg . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2018.)
axiom df_itgm {w m: setvar}:
  $ wceq citgm (cmpo w m cvv (cuni (crn cmeas)) (cfv (co (cv w) (cv m) csitg) (co (cfv (co (cv w) (cv m) csitm) cmetu) (cfv (cv w) cuss) ccnext))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . The function ` F ` that decomposes a number into its "odd" and "even" parts, which is to say the largest power of two and largest odd divisor of a number, is a bijection from pairs of a nonnegative integer and an odd number to positive integers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Aug-2017.)
theorem oddpwdc {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class x3 y1 z) (J: class z):
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp J cn0) cn F $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : value of the ` F ` function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Sep-2017.)
theorem oddpwdcv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class x3 y1 z) (J W: class z):
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W (cxp J cn0)) (wceq (cfv W F) (co (co c2 (cfv W c2nd) cexp) (cfv W c1st) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . Value of the sum of a partition ` A ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemsv1 {f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2 k):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wceq (cfv A2 S) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cv k) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemelr {f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2 k):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wa (wf cn cn0 A2) (wcel (cima (ccnv A2) cn) cfn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . Value of the sum of a finite partition ` A ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemsv2 {f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2 k):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wceq (cfv A2 S) (csu k (cima (ccnv A2) cn) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cv k) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemsf {f2 k: setvar} (R: class) (S: class f2 k):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wf (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) cn0 S $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Aug-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem eulerpartlems {t f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2 k):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (co (cfv A2 S) c1 caddc) cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv t) A2) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . Value of the sum of a finite partition ` A ` (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemsv3 {f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wceq (cfv A2 S) (csu k (co c1 (cfv A2 S) cfz) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cv k) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemgc {t f2 k: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class f2) (n: setvar):
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R)) (wa (wcel (cv t) cn) (wcel (cv n) (cfv (cfv (cv t) A2) cbits)))) (wbr (co (co c2 (cv n) cexp) (cv t) cmul) (cfv A2 S) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2015.)
theorem eulerpartleme {f2 k: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class f2 k) (N: class k):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 P) (w3a (wf cn cn0 A2) (wcel (cima (ccnv A2) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cv k) cmul)) N)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemv {f2 k: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 P) (w3a (wf cn cn0 A2) (wcel (cima (ccnv A2) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k (cima (ccnv A2) cn) (co (cfv (cv k) A2) (cv k) cmul)) N)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : ` O ` is the set of odd partitions of ` N ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Aug-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemo {f2 g2 k n: setvar} (A2: class f2 k) (D: class f2 g2 k n) (P: class f2 k n) (N O: class f2 g2 k n):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 O) (wa (wcel A2 P) (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv A2) cn))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : ` D ` is the set of distinct part. of ` N ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Aug-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemd {f2 g2 k n: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class f2 g2 k n) (P: class f2 k) (N: class g2 k n) (O: class f2 g2 k n):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 D) (wa (wcel A2 P) (wss (cima A2 cn) (cpr cc0 c1))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Aug-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem eulerpartlem1 {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r: setvar} (D P F: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (J: class z f2 g2 k n) (M N O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wf1o H (cin (cpw (cxp J cn0)) cfn) M $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . The set of all partitions of ` N ` is finite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Jan-2015.)
theorem eulerpartlemb {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k n r) (F H J M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (N: class y1 z k n r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wcel P cfn $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemt0 {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (D P R T F H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (J: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (M N O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (w3a (wcel A2 (co cn0 cn cmap)) (wcel (cima (ccnv A2) cn) cfn) (wss (cima (ccnv A2) cn) J)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : Odd partitions are zero for even numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Sep-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemf {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r: setvar} (t: setvar) (A2: class x3 y1 z r) (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k n r) (R T F H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (J: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (N: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wcel (cv t) (cdif cn J))) (wceq (cfv (cv t) A2) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Sep-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemt {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m n r: setvar} (D P: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m n r) (R T: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (F H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m n r) (J: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (M N O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m n r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq (cin (co cn0 J cmap) R) (crn (cmpt m (cin T R) (cres (cv m) J))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : The ` G ` function is a bijection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 27-Aug-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem eulerpartgbij {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k n o r) (R T: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (F: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (G: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (J: class z g2 k n) (M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (N: class y1 z k n o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wf1o (cin T R) (cin (co (cpr cc0 c1) cn cmap) R) G $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : value of the function ` G ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Nov-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemgv {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (D P: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (R T F: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (G H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (J M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (N O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wceq (cfv A2 G) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres A2 J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Nov-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemr {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k o r) (R T F G H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (J: class x3 y1 z g2 k o r) (M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (N: class x3 y1 z g2 k n o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wceq O (cin (cin T R) P) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Aug-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem eulerpartlemmf {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z g2 k n) (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k o r) (R T F: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (G: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o) (J: class z g2 k) (M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (N: class x3 y1 z g2 k n o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wcel (ccom cbits (cres A2 J)) H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : value of the function ` G ` evaluated. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemgvv {x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class z g2 k) (B2: class z f2 g2 k o r) (D: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z t f2 k o r) (R T: class z f2 g2 k) (F: class z f2 g2 k r) (G: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r) (H: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o) (J: class z g2 k) (M: class z f2 g2 k r) (N: class x3 y1 z t g2 k n o r) (O: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (cfv B2 (cfv A2 G)) (cif (wrex t (wrex n (wceq (co (co c2 (cv n) cexp) (cv t) cmul) B2) (cfv (cfv (cv t) A2) cbits)) cn) c1 cc0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : Rewriting the ` U ` set for an odd partition Note that interestingly, this proof reuses ~ marypha2lem2 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemgu {x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z o r) (D: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z t f2 n o r) (R: class x3 y1 z g2 o r) (T: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k o r) (U F G H: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r) (J: class x3 y1 z g2 k o r) (M: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k n o r) (N: class x3 y1 z g2 o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wceq U (ciun t (cin (cima (ccnv A2) cn) J) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cfv (cfv (cv t) A2) cbits))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wceq U (copab t n (wa (wcel (cv t) (cin (cima (ccnv A2) cn) J)) (wcel (cv n) (cfv (cv t) (ccom cbits A2)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : The ` F ` function is a bijection on the ` U ` subsets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemgh {x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k m n o r: setvar} (A2: class y1 z o r) (D: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k m n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 m n o r) (R: class x3 y1 z g2 m o r) (T: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m o r) (U: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k m n o r) (F: class y1 z f2 g2 k m o r) (G H: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k m n o r) (J: class z g2 k m o r) (M: class x3 y1 z t f2 g2 k m n o r) (N: class x3 y1 z g2 m o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k m o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wceq U (ciun t (cin (cima (ccnv A2) cn) J) (cxp (csn (cv t)) (cfv (cfv (cv t) A2) cbits))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wf1o U (crab m (wrex t (wrex n (wceq (co (co c2 (cv n) cexp) (cv t) cmul) (cv m)) (cfv (cfv (cv t) A2) cbits)) cn) cn) (cres F U)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : Images under ` G ` have finite support. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemgf {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z g2 k n) (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 k o r) (R T: class x3 y1 z g2 k n r) (F: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n r) (G: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (J: class z g2 k) (M: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (N: class y1 z k n o r) (O: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wcel (cima (ccnv (cfv A2 G)) cn) cfn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart : The ` G ` function also preserves partition sums. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Sep-2017.)
theorem eulerpartlemgs2 {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (A2: class z) (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 o r) (R: class z g2) (S: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (T: class z g2) (F: class z f2 g2 k r) (G: class x3 y1 z g2 n o r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (J: class z g2 k) (M: class z f2 g2 k) (N: class y1 z o r) (O: class z f2 g2 k o):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin T R)) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 G) S) (cfv A2 S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ eulerpart . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Aug-2018.)
theorem eulerpartlemn {x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (P: class x3 y1 z f2 o r) (R: class z g2) (S: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n o r) (T: class z g2) (F: class z f2 g2 r) (G: class x3 y1 z g2 n r) (H: class x3 y1 z f2 g2 k n) (J: class z g2) (M: class z f2 g2) (N: class y1 z r) (O: class z f2 g2 k o):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq J (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 J cn0 (co (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) (cv x3) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab r (wcel (co (cv r) c0 csupp) cfn) (co (cin (cpw cn0) cfn) J cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt r H (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) J) (wcel (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) (cv r)))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (cab f2 (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn)) $ >
  $ wceq T (crab f2 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) J) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt o (cin T R) (cfv (cima F (cfv (ccom cbits (cres (cv o) J)) M)) (cfv cn cind))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt f2 (cin (co cn0 cn cmap) R) (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul))) $ >
  $ wf1o O D (cres G O) $;

--| Euler's theorem on partitions, also known as a special case of Glaisher's theorem. Let ` P ` be the set of all partitions of ` N ` , represented as multisets of positive integers, which is to say functions from ` NN ` to ` NN0 ` where the value of the function represents the number of repetitions of an individual element, and the sum of all the elements with repetition equals ` N ` . Then the set ` O ` of all partitions that only consist of odd numbers and the set ` D ` of all partitions which have no repeated elements have the same cardinality. This is Metamath 100 proof #45. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Aug-2018.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Sep-2019.)
theorem eulerpart {f2 g2 k n: setvar} (D: class f2 k n) (P: class f2) (N: class) (O: class f2 k):
  $ wceq P (crab f2 (wa (wcel (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) cn) cfn) (wceq (csu k cn (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv k) cmul)) N)) (co cn0 cn cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq O (crab g2 (wral n (wn (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds)) (cima (ccnv (cv g2)) cn)) P) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab g2 (wral n (wbr (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)) c1 cle) cn) P) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv O chash) (cfv D chash) $;

--| Sequences defined by strong recursion.
term csseq: class;

--| Define a builder for sequences by strong recursion, i.e., by computing the value of the n-th element of the sequence from all preceding elements and not just the previous one. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Apr-2019.)
axiom df_sseq {x3 y1 f2 m: setvar}:
  $ wceq csseq (cmpo m f2 cvv cvv (cun (cv m) (ccom clsw (cseq (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (co (cv x3) (cs1 (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2))) cconcat)) (cxp cn0 (csn (co (cv m) (cs1 (cfv (cv m) (cv f2))) cconcat))) (cfv (cv m) chash))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sseqp1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem subiwrd (ph: wff) (S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co cc0 N cfzo)) (cword S)) $;

--| Length of a subword of an infinite word. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem subiwrdlen (ph: wff) (S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F (co cc0 N cfzo)) chash) N) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sseqp1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 14-Oct-2022.)
theorem iwrdsplit (ph: wff) (S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres F (co cc0 (co N c1 caddc) cfzo)) (co (cres F (co cc0 N cfzo)) (cs1 (cfv N F)) cconcat)) $;

--| Value of the strong sequence builder function. The set ` W ` represents here the words of length greater than or equal to the lenght of the initial sequence ` M ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3 y1) (F M: class) (W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M F csseq) (cun M (ccom clsw (cseq (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (co (cv x3) (cs1 (cfv (cv x3) F)) cconcat)) (cxp cn0 (csn (co M (cs1 (cfv M F)) cconcat))) (cfv M chash))))) $;

--| Value of the strong sequence builder function at one of its initial values. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqfv1 (ph: wff) (S F M N W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co cc0 (cfv M chash) cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co M F csseq)) (cfv N M)) $;

--| A strong recursive sequence is a function over the nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqfn (ph: wff) (S F M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (co M F csseq) cn0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sseqf amd ~ sseqp1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqmw (ph: wff) (S F M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $;

--| A strong recursive sequence is a function over the nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 7-Mar-2022.)
theorem sseqf (ph: wff) (S F M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 S (co M F csseq)) $;

--| The first elements in the strong recursive sequence are the sequence initializer. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqfres (ph: wff) (S F M W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (co M F csseq) (co cc0 (cfv M chash) cfzo)) M) $;

--| Value of the strong sequence builder function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqfv2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class x3 y1) (F M: class) (N: class x3 y1) (W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co M F csseq)) (cfv (cfv N (cseq (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (co (cv x3) (cs1 (cfv (cv x3) F)) cconcat)) (cxp cn0 (csn (co M (cs1 (cfv M F)) cconcat))) (cfv M chash))) clsw)) $;

--| Value of the strong sequence builder function at a successor. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Apr-2019.)
theorem sseqp1 (ph: wff) (S F M N W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cword S)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cin (cword S) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf W S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cfv M chash) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co M F csseq)) (cfv (cres (co M F csseq) (co cc0 N cfzo)) F)) $;

--| The Fibonacci sequence.
term cfib: class;

--| Define the Fibonacci sequence, where that each element is the sum of the two preceding ones, starting from 0 and 1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
axiom df_fib {w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfib (co (cs2 cc0 c1) (cmpt w (cin (cword cn0) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv c2 cuz))) (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) chash) c2 cmin) (cv w)) (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) chash) c1 cmin) (cv w)) caddc)) csseq) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fib0 , ~ fib1 and ~ fibp1 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fiblem {w: setvar}:
  $ wf (cin (cword cn0) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv (cfv (cs2 cc0 c1) chash) cuz))) cn0 (cmpt w (cin (cword cn0) (cima (ccnv chash) (cfv c2 cuz))) (co (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) chash) c2 cmin) (cv w)) (cfv (co (cfv (cv w) chash) c1 cmin) (cv w)) caddc)) $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 0. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cfib) cc0 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cfib) c1 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at higher indices. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fibp1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cfib) (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfib) (cfv N cfib) caddc)) $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 2. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cfib) c1 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 3. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib3:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 cfib) c2 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 4. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib4:
  $ wceq (cfv c4 cfib) c3 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 5. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib5:
  $ wceq (cfv c5 cfib) c5 $;

--| Value of the Fibonacci sequence at index 6. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem fib6:
  $ wceq (cfv c6 cfib) c8 $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of probability measures.
term cprb: class;

--| Define the class of probability measures as the set of measures with total measure 1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2016.)
axiom df_prob {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprb (crab p (wceq (cfv (cuni (cdm (cv p))) (cv p)) c1) (cuni (crn cmeas))) $;

--| The property of being a probability measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Dec-2016.)
theorem elprob (P: class):
  $ wb (wcel P cprb) (wa (wcel P (cuni (crn cmeas))) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cdm P)) P) c1)) $;

--| A probability measure is a measure on its domain. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem domprobmeas (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wcel P (cfv (cdm P) cmeas)) $;

--| The domain of a probability measure is a sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem domprobsiga (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wcel (cdm P) (cuni (crn csiga))) $;

--| The probability of the universe set is 1. Second axiom of Kolmogorov. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Dec-2016.)
theorem probtot (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wceq (cfv (cuni (cdm P)) P) c1) $;

--| A probability is an element of [ 0 , 1 ]. First axiom of Kolmogorov. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem prob01 (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P))) (wcel (cfv A2 P) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| The probability of the empty event set is 0. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem probnul (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wceq (cfv c0 P) cc0) $;

--| The universe is an element of the domain of the probability, the universe (entire probability space) being ` U. dom P ` in our construction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jan-2017.)
theorem unveldomd (ph: wff) (P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni (cdm P)) (cdm P)) $;

--| The universe is an element of the domain of the probability, the universe (entire probability space) being ` U. dom P ` in our construction. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jan-2017.)
theorem unveldom (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wcel (cuni (cdm P)) (cdm P)) $;

--| The empty set is an element of the domain of the probability. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Jan-2017.)
theorem nuleldmp (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprb) (wcel c0 (cdm P)) $;

--| The probability of the union of a countable disjoint set of events is the sum of their probabilities. (Third axiom of Kolmogorov) Here, the ` sum_ ` construct cannot be used as it can handle infinite indexing set only if they are subsets of ` ZZ ` , which is not the case here. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem probcun {x3: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cpw (cdm P))) (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wdisj x3 A2 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cfv (cuni A2) P) (cesum x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) P))) $;

--| The probability of the union two incompatible events is the sum of their probabilities. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem probun (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wi (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) (wceq (cfv (cun A2 B2) P) (co (cfv A2 P) (cfv B2 P) caddc))) $;

--| The probability of the difference of two event sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Dec-2016.)
theorem probdif (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wceq (cfv (cdif A2 B2) P) (co (cfv A2 P) (cfv (cin A2 B2) P) cmin)) $;

--| A probability law is increasing with regard to event set inclusion. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Feb-2017.)
theorem probinc (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wss A2 B2)) (wbr (cfv A2 P) (cfv B2 P) cle) $;

--| The probability of the complement of a set. That is, the probability that the event ` A ` does not occur. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Dec-2016.)
theorem probdsb (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P))) (wceq (cfv (cdif (cuni (cdm P)) A2) P) (co c1 (cfv A2 P) cmin)) $;

--| A probability measure is a measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2017.)
theorem probmeasd (ph: wff) (P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cuni (crn cmeas))) $;

--| The value of a probability is a real number. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2017.)
theorem probvalrnd (ph: wff) (A2 P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 P) cr) $;

--| The probability of the universe set is finite. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Feb-2017.)
theorem probtotrnd (ph: wff) (P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cuni (cdm P)) P) cr) $;

--| Law of total probability, deduction form. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem totprobd {b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cpw (cdm P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni B2) (cuni (cdm P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj b B2 (cv b)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 P) (cesum b B2 (cfv (cin (cv b) A2) P))) $;

--| Law of total probability. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem totprob {b: setvar} (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (w3a (wceq (cuni B2) (cuni (cdm P))) (wcel B2 (cpw (cdm P))) (wa (wbr B2 com cdom) (wdisj b B2 (cv b))))) (wceq (cfv A2 P) (cesum b B2 (cfv (cin (cv b) A2) P))) $;

--| Build a probability measure from a finite measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 31-Jan-2017.)
theorem probfinmeasb (S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel (cfv (cuni S) M) crp)) (wcel (co M (cfv (cuni S) M) (cofc cxdiv)) cprb) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ probfinmeasb . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Dec-2016.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem probfinmeasbALTV {x3: setvar} (S M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel (cfv (cuni S) M) crp)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S (co (cfv (cv x3) M) (cfv (cuni S) M) cxdiv)) cprb) $;

--| Build a probability from a measure and a set with finite measure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Dec-2016.)
theorem probmeasb {x3: setvar} (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M (cfv S cmeas)) (wcel A2 S) (wcel (cfv A2 M) crp)) (wcel (cmpt x3 S (co (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) M) (cfv A2 M) cdiv)) cprb) $;

--| Extends class notation with the conditional probability builder.
term ccprob: class;

--| Define the conditional probability. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_cndprob {p a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccprob (cmpt p cprb (cmpo a b (cdm (cv p)) (cdm (cv p)) (co (cfv (cin (cv a) (cv b)) (cv p)) (cfv (cv b) (cv p)) cdiv))) $;

--| The value of the conditional probability , i.e. the probability for the event ` A ` , given ` B ` , under the probability law ` P ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprobval (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) (cfv P ccprob)) (co (cfv (cin A2 B2) P) (cfv B2 P) cdiv)) $;

--| An identity linking conditional probability and intersection. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprobin (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wne (cfv B2 P) cc0)) (wceq (co (cfv (cop A2 B2) (cfv P ccprob)) (cfv B2 P) cmul) (cfv (cin A2 B2) P)) $;

--| The conditional probability has values in ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprob01 (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wne (cfv B2 P) cc0)) (wcel (cfv (cop A2 B2) (cfv P ccprob)) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| The conditional probability given a certain event is one. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprobtot (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wne (cfv A2 P) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cop (cuni (cdm P)) A2) (cfv P ccprob)) c1) $;

--| The conditional probability given empty event is zero. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprobnul (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wne (cfv A2 P) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cop c0 A2) (cfv P ccprob)) cc0) $;

--| The conditional probability defines a probability law. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem cndprobprob {a: setvar} (B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel B2 (cdm P)) (wne (cfv B2 P) cc0)) (wcel (cmpt a (cdm P) (cfv (cop (cv a) B2) (cfv P ccprob))) cprb) $;

--| Bayes Theorem. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Dec-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem bayesth (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel P cprb) (wcel A2 (cdm P)) (wcel B2 (cdm P))) (wne (cfv A2 P) cc0) (wne (cfv B2 P) cc0)) (wceq (cfv (cop A2 B2) (cfv P ccprob)) (co (co (cfv (cop B2 A2) (cfv P ccprob)) (cfv A2 P) cmul) (cfv B2 P) cdiv)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of real-valued random variables.
term crrv: class;

--| In its generic definition, a random variable is a measurable function from a probability space to a Borel set. Here, we specifically target real-valued random variables, i.e. measurable function from a probability space to the Borel sigma-algebra on the set of real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 20-Sep-2016.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_rrv {p: setvar}:
  $ wceq crrv (cmpt p cprb (co (cdm (cv p)) cbrsiga cmbfm)) $;

--| A real-valued random variable is a measurable function from its sample space to the Borel sigma-algebra. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvmbfm (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) (wcel X (co (cdm P) cbrsiga cmbfm))) $;

--| Elementhood to the set of real-valued random variables with respect to the probability ` P ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem isrrvv {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) (wa (wf (cuni (cdm P)) cr X) (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv X) (cv y1)) (cdm P)) cbrsiga))) $;

--| A real-valued random variable is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvvf (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cuni (cdm P)) cr X) $;

--| A real-valued random variable is a function over the universe. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvfn (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn X (cuni (cdm P))) $;

--| The domain of a random variable is the universe. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvdm (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm X) (cuni (cdm P))) $;

--| The range of a random variable as a subset of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Feb-2017.)
theorem rrvrnss (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn X) cr) $;

--| A real-valued random variable is a function. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvf2 (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm X) cr X) $;

--| The domain of a random variable. This is useful to shorten proofs. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvdmss (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (cdm P)) (cdm X)) $;

--| For a real-value random variable ` X ` , any open interval in ` RR ` is the image of a measurable set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Jan-2017.)
theorem rrvfinvima {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wcel (cima (ccnv X) (cv y1)) (cdm P)) cbrsiga) $;

--| The constant function equal to zero is a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 16-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 30-Jan-2017.)
theorem _0rrv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (P: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (cuni (cdm P)) cc0) (cfv P crrv)) $;

--| The sum of two random variables is a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Jun-2017.)
theorem rrvadd (ph: wff) (P X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y (cof caddc)) (cfv P crrv)) $;

--| A random variable multiplied by a constant is a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Jan-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2017.)
theorem rrvmulc (ph: wff) (C P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X C (cofc cmul)) (cfv P crrv)) $;

--| An indexed sum of random variables is a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-May-2017.)
theorem rrvsum (ph: wff) (P S N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cfv P crrv) X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wceq S (cfv N (cseq (cof caddc) X c1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wcel S (cfv P crrv)) $;

--| Boole's inequality (union bound). For any finite or countable collection of events, the probability of their union is at most the sum of their probabilities. (Suggested by DeepSeek R1.) (Contributed by Ender Ting, 30-Apr-2025.)
theorem boolesineq {n: setvar} (A2 P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprb) (wf cn (cdm P) A2)) (wbr (cfv (ciun n cn (cfv (cv n) A2)) P) (cesum n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) P)) cle) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the preimage set mapping operator.
term corvc (R: class): class;

--| Define the preimage set mapping operator. In probability theory, the notation ` P ( X = A ) ` denotes the probability that a random variable ` X ` takes the value ` A ` . We introduce here an operator which enables to write this in Metamath as ` ( P `` ( X oRVC _I A ) ) ` , and keep a similar notation. Because with this notation ` ( X oRVC _I A ) ` is a set, we can also apply it to conditional probabilities, like in ` ( P `` ( X oRVC _I A ) | ( Y oRVC _I B ) ) ) ` . The oRVC operator transforms a relation ` R ` into an operation taking a random variable ` X ` and a constant ` C ` , and returning the preimage through ` X ` of the equivalence class of ` C ` . The most commonly used relations are: - equality: ` { X = A } ` as ` ( X oRVC _I A ) ` cf. ~ ideq - elementhood: ` { X e. A } ` as ` ( X oRVC _E A ) ` cf. ~ epel - less-than: ` { X <_ A } ` as ` ( X oRVC <_ A ) ` Even though it is primarily designed to be used within probability theory and with random variables, this operator is defined on generic functions, and could be used in other fields, e.g., for continuous functions. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
axiom df_orvc {x3 y1 a: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (corvc R) (cmpo x3 a (cab x3 (wfun (cv x3))) cvv (cima (ccnv (cv x3)) (cab y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv a) R)))) $;

--| Value of the preimage mapping operator applied on a given random variable and constant. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jan-2017.)
theorem orvcval {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class) (V W: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X A2 (corvc R)) (cima (ccnv X) (cab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R)))) $;

--| Another way to express the value of the preimage mapping operator. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 19-Jan-2017.)
theorem orvcval2 {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 R: class) (V W: class z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X A2 (corvc R)) (crab z (wbr (cfv (cv z) X) A2 R) (cdm X))) $;

--| Elementhood of a preimage. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem elorvc {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 R: class) (V W: class z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) (cdm X))) (wb (wcel (cv z) (co X A2 (corvc R))) (wbr (cfv (cv z) X) A2 R)) $;

--| The value of the preimage mapping operator can be restricted to preimages in the base set of the topology. Cf. ~ orvcval . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orvcval4 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class) (S: class y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co S (cfv J csigagen) cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X A2 (corvc R)) (cima (ccnv X) (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) (cuni J)))) $;

--| If the relation produces open sets, preimage maps by a measurable function are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orvcoel {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class) (S: class y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co S (cfv J csigagen) cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) (cuni J)) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc R)) S) $;

--| If the relation produces closed sets, preimage maps by a measurable function are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orvccel {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class) (S: class y1) (J: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cuni (crn csiga))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co S (cfv J csigagen) cmbfm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) (cuni J)) (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc R)) S) $;

--| Elementhood of a preimage for a real-valued random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem elorrvc {z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (P: class z) (R: class) (V: class z) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) (cuni (cdm P)))) (wb (wcel (cv z) (co X A2 (corvc R))) (wbr (cfv (cv z) X) A2 R)) $;

--| The value of the preimage mapping operator can be restricted to preimages of subsets of ` RR ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orrvcval4 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (P: class y1) (R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X A2 (corvc R)) (cima (ccnv X) (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) cr))) $;

--| If the relation produces open sets, preimage maps of a random variable are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orrvcoel {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (P: class y1) (R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) cr) (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc R)) (cdm P)) $;

--| If the relation produces closed sets, preimage maps are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem orrvccel {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (P: class y1) (R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab y1 (wbr (cv y1) A2 R) cr) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc R)) (cdm P)) $;

--| Preimage maps produced by the "greater than or equal to" relation are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Feb-2017.)
theorem orvcgteel (ph: wff) (A2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc (ccnv cle))) (cdm P)) $;

--| Preimage maps produced by the membership relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 6-Feb-2017.)
theorem orvcelval (ph: wff) (A2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cbrsiga) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X A2 (corvc cep)) (cima (ccnv X) A2)) $;

--| Preimage maps produced by the membership relation are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Feb-2017.)
theorem orvcelel (ph: wff) (A2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cbrsiga) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc cep)) (cdm P)) $;

--| The value of the distribution of a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem dstrvval {a: setvar} (ph: wff a) (A2: class) (D: class a) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpt a cbrsiga (cfv (co X (cv a) (corvc cep)) P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cbrsiga) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 D) (cfv (cima (ccnv X) A2) P)) $;

--| The distribution of a random variable is a probability law. (TODO: could be shortened using ~ dstrvval ). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Feb-2017.)
theorem dstrvprob {a: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (cmpt a cbrsiga (cfv (co X (cv a) (corvc cep)) P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cprb) $;

--| Preimage maps produced by the "less than or equal to" relation are measurable sets. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 4-Feb-2017.)
theorem orvclteel (ph: wff) (A2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X A2 (corvc cle)) (cdm P)) $;

--| Elementhood of preimage maps produced by the "less than or equal to" relation. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 13-Feb-2017.)
theorem dstfrvel (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cuni (cdm P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 X) A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co X A2 (corvc cle))) $;

--| The limit of all preimage maps by the "less than or equal to" relation is the universe. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Feb-2017.)
theorem dstfrvunirn {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (crn (cmpt n cn (co X (cv n) (corvc cle))))) (cuni (cdm P))) $;

--| Preimage maps produced by the "less than or equal to" relation are increasing. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2017.)
theorem orvclteinc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X A2 (corvc cle)) (co X B2 (corvc cle))) $;

--| A cumulative distribution function is nondecreasing. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Feb-2017.)
theorem dstfrvinc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P: class) (F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (co X (cv x3) (corvc cle)) P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) cle) $;

--| The limit of the cumulative distribution function is one. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Feb-2017.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 11-Jul-2017.)
theorem dstfrvclim1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprb) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv P crrv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (co X (cv x3) (corvc cle)) P))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c1 cli) $;

--| Division in the extended real numbers can be used for the coin-flip example. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinfliplem (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cxdiv)) $;

--| The ` P ` we defined for coin-flip is a probability law. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinflipprob (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wcel P cprb $;

--| The space of our coin-flip probability. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinflipspace (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm P) (cpw (cpr H T)) $;

--| The universe of our coin-flip probability is ` { H , T } ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinflipuniv (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (cdm P)) (cpr H T) $;

--| The ` X ` we defined for coin-flip is a random variable. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinfliprv (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wcel X (cfv P crrv) $;

--| The probability of heads is one-half. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 15-Jan-2017.)
theorem coinflippv (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csn H) P) (co c1 c2 cdiv) $;

--| The probability of tails is one-half. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 5-Feb-2017.)
theorem coinflippvt (P T H X: class):
  $ wcel H cvv $ >
  $ wcel T cvv $ >
  $ wne H T $ >
  $ wceq P (co (cres chash (cpw (cpr H T))) c2 (cofc cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq X (cpr (cop H c1) (cop T cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (csn T) P) (co c1 c2 cdiv) $;

--| ` O ` is a set. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Dec-2016.)
theorem ballotlemoex {c: setvar} (M N O: class):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wcel O cvv $;

--| The size of the universe is a binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlem1 {c: setvar} (M N O: class):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv O chash) (co (co M N caddc) M cbc) $;

--| Elementhood in ` O ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 17-Apr-2017.)
theorem ballotlemelo {c: setvar} (C: class c) (M N O: class):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C O) (wa (wss C (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) (wceq (cfv C chash) M)) $;

--| The probability that the first vote picked in a count is a B. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlem2 {x3 c: setvar} (P: class x3 c) (M N: class x3) (O: class):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (crab c (wn (wcel c1 (cv c))) O) P) (co N (co M N caddc) cdiv) $;

--| The value of ` F ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfval {x3 i c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c) (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i c) (F: class x3) (J: class x3 c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (cfv C F)) (co (cfv (cin (co c1 J cfz) C) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 J cfz) C) chash) cmin)) $;

--| ` ( F `` C ) ` has values in ` ZZ ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfelz {x3 i c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c) (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i c) (F: class x3) (J: class x3 c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv J (cfv C F)) cz) $;

--| If the ` J ` th ballot is for A, ` ( F `` C ) ` goes up 1. If the ` J ` th ballot is for B, ` ( F `` C ) ` goes down 1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfp1 {x3 i c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c) (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i c) (F: class x3) (J: class x3 c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wi (wn (wcel J C)) (wceq (cfv J (cfv C F)) (co (cfv (co J c1 cmin) (cfv C F)) c1 cmin))) (wi (wcel J C) (wceq (cfv J (cfv C F)) (co (cfv (co J c1 cmin) (cfv C F)) c1 caddc)))) $;

--| ` F ` takes value 0 between negative and positive values. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 24-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfc0 {x3 i k c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c) (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i k c) (F: class x3) (J: class x3 c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cfv C F)) cc0 cle) (co c1 J cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv J (cfv C F)) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cfv C F)) cc0) (co c1 J cfz)) $;

--| ` F ` takes value 0 between positive and negative values. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 2-Apr-2017.)
theorem ballotlemfcc {x3 i k c: setvar} (ph: wff x3 c) (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i k c) (F: class x3) (J: class x3 c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C O) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv C F)) cle) (co c1 J cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv J (cfv C F)) cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cfv C F)) cc0) (co c1 J cfz)) $;

--| ` ( F `` C ) ` finishes counting at ` ( M - N ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfmpn {x3 i c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C O) (wceq (cfv (co M N caddc) (cfv C F)) (co M N cmin)) $;

--| ` ( F `` C ) ` always starts counting at 0 . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlemfval0 {x3 i c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C O) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv C F)) cc0) $;

--| Elements of ` E ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Dec-2016.)
theorem ballotleme {x3 i c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P E: class x3 i c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq E (crab c (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv c) F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) O) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C E) (wa (wcel C O) (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv C F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $;

--| Elements of ` ( O \ E ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 7-Dec-2016.)
theorem ballotlemodife {x3 i c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P E: class x3 i c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq E (crab c (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv c) F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) O) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (cdif O E)) (wa (wcel C O) (wrex i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cfv C F)) cc0 cle) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $;

--| If the first pick is a vote for B, A is not ahead throughout the count. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Nov-2016.)
theorem ballotlem4 {x3 i c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P E: class x3 i c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq E (crab c (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv c) F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) O) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C O) (wi (wn (wcel c1 C)) (wn (wcel C E))) $;

--| If A is not ahead throughout, there is a ` k ` where votes are tied. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2016.)
theorem ballotlem5 {x3 i k c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i k c) (E: class x3 c) (F M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq E (crab c (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv c) F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) O) $ >
  $ wbr N M clt $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cdif O E)) (wrex k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cfv C F)) cc0) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) $;

--| Value of ` I ` for a given counting ` C ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem ballotlemi {x3 i k c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i k c) (E F: class x3) (I: class x3 i c) (M N O: class x3):
  $ wcel M cn $ >
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wceq O (crab c (wceq (cfv (cv c) chash) M) (cpw (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt x3 (cpw O) (co (cfv (cv x3) chash) (cfv O chash) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt c O (cmpt i cz (co (cfv (cin (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) (cfv (cdif (co c1 (cv i) cfz) (cv c)) chash) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq E (crab c (wral i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv c) F)) clt) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) O) $ >
  $ wbr N M clt $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt c (cdif O E) (cinf (crab k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cfv (cv c) F)) cc0) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C (cdif O E)) (wceq (cfv C I) (cinf (crab k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (cfv C F)) cc0) (co c1 (co M N caddc) cfz)) cr clt)) $;

--| Properties of ` ( I `` C ) ` . (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 12-Dec-2016.) (Revised by AV, 6-Oct-2020.)
theorem ballotlemiex {x3 i k c: setvar} (C: class x3 c) (P: class x3 i k c) (E F: class x3) (I: class x3 i c) (M N O: class x3):
