--| Lemma for derangements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem deranglem {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel (cab f2 (wa (wf1o A2 A2 (cv f2)) ph)) cfn) $;

--| Define the derangement function, which counts the number of bijections from a set to itself such that no element is mapped to itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangval {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq (cfv A2 D) (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o A2 A2 (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) A2))) chash)) $;

--| The derangement number is a function from finite sets to nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangf {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wf cfn cn0 D $;

--| The derangement number of the empty set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem derang0 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c0 D) c1 $;

--| The derangement number of a singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangsn {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) D) cc0) $;

--| One half of ~ derangen . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangenlem {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wbr (cfv A2 D) (cfv B2 D) cle) $;

--| The derangement number is a cardinal invariant, i.e. it only depends on the size of a set and not on its contents. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangen {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cen) (wcel B2 cfn)) (wceq (cfv A2 D) (cfv B2 D)) $;

--| The subfactorial is defined as the number of derangements (see ~ derangval ) of the set ` ( 1 ... N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacval {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N S) (cfv (co c1 N cfz) D)) $;

--| Write the derangement number in terms of the subfactorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangen2 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wceq (cfv A2 D) (cfv (cfv A2 chash) S)) $;

--| The subfactorial is a function from nonnegative integers to nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacf {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wf cn0 cn0 S $;

--| The subfactorial is less than the factorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfaclefac {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cfv N S) (cfv N cfa) cle) $;

--| The subfactorial at zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfac0 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 S) c1 $;

--| The subfactorial at one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfac1 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c1 S) cc0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . The set ` K ` together with ` { 1 , M } ` partitions the set ` 1 ... ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem1 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 n) (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wceq (cin K (cpr c1 M)) c0) (wceq (cun K (cpr c1 M)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) (wceq (cfv K chash) (co N c1 cmin))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . Properties of a bijection on ` K ` augmented with the two-element flip to get a bijection on ` K u. { 1 , M } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem2a {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 n) (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (F: class n) (G: class x3 y1 f2 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cun G (cpr (cop c1 M) (cop M c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o K K G) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) F) (wceq (cfv c1 F) M) (wceq (cfv M F) c1)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . Properties of a bijection on ` K ` augmented with the two-element flip to get a bijection on ` K u. { 1 , M } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem2b {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 n) (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (F: class n) (G: class x3 y1 f2 n) (K: class) (M: class n) (N: class) (X: class x3 y1 f2 n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cun G (cpr (cop c1 M) (cop M c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o K K G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X K)) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv X G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . In ~ subfacp1lem6 we cut up the set of all derangements on ` 1 ... ( N + 1 ) ` first according to the value at ` 1 ` , and then by whether or not ` ( f `` ( f `` 1 ) ) = 1 ` . In this lemma, we show that the subset of all ` N + 1 ` derangements that satisfy this for fixed ` M = ( f `` 1 ) ` is in bijection with ` N - 1 ` derangements, by simply dropping the ` x = 1 ` and ` x = M ` points from the function to get a derangement on ` K = ( 1 ... ( N - 1 ) ) \ { 1 , M } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem3 {x3 y1 f2 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2 n) (A2: class) (B2: class n) (C: class f2 g2 n) (D: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (S: class f2 g2) (K: class g2) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab g2 (wa (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) M) (wceq (cfv M (cv g2)) c1)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab f2 (wa (wf1o K K (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) K))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 chash) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . The function ` F ` , which swaps ` 1 ` with ` M ` and leaves all other elements alone, is a bijection of order ` 2 ` , i.e. it is its own inverse. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem4 {x3 y1 f2 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2 n) (A2: class) (B2: class n) (D: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (S: class f2 g2) (F: class n) (K: class g2) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab g2 (wa (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) M) (wne (cfv M (cv g2)) c1)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid K) (cpr (cop c1 M) (cop M c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccnv F) F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . In ~ subfacp1lem6 we cut up the set of all derangements on ` 1 ... ( N + 1 ) ` first according to the value at ` 1 ` , and then by whether or not ` ( f `` ( f `` 1 ) ) = 1 ` . In this lemma, we show that the subset of all ` N + 1 ` derangements with ` ( f `` ( f `` 1 ) ) =/= 1 ` for fixed ` M = ( f `` 1 ) ` is in bijection with derangements of ` 2 ... ( N + 1 ) ` , because pre-composing with the function ` F ` swaps ` 1 ` and ` M ` and turns the function into a bijection with ` ( f `` 1 ) = 1 ` and ` ( f `` x ) =/= x ` for all other ` x ` , so dropping the point at ` 1 ` yields a derangement on the ` N ` remaining points. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem5 {x3 y1 f2 g2 n: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2 n) (A2: class) (B2: class n) (C: class f2 g2 n) (D: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (S: class f2 g2) (F: class n) (K: class g2) (M: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $ >
  $ wcel M cvv $ >
  $ wceq K (cdif (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn M)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab g2 (wa (wceq (cfv c1 (cv g2)) M) (wne (cfv M (cv g2)) c1)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid K) (cpr (cop c1 M) (cop M c1))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 chash) (cfv N S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ subfacp1 . By induction, we cut up the set of all derangements on ` N + 1 ` according to the ` N ` possible values of ` ( f `` 1 ) ` (since ` ( f `` 1 ) =/= 1 ` ), and for each set for fixed ` M = ( f `` 1 ) ` , the subset of derangements with ` ( f `` M ) = 1 ` has size ` S ( N - 1 ) ` (by ~ subfacp1lem3 ), while the subset with ` ( f `` M ) =/= 1 ` has size ` S ( N ) ` (by ~ subfacp1lem5 ). Adding it all up yields the desired equation ` N ( S ( N ) + S ( N - 1 ) ) ` for the number of derangements on ` N + 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1lem6 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) S) (co N (co (cfv N S) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) S) caddc) cmul)) $;

--| A two-term recurrence for the subfactorial. This theorem allows to forget the combinatorial definition of the derangement number in favor of the recursive definition provided by this theorem and ~ subfac0 , ~ subfac1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacp1 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) S) (co N (co (cfv N S) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) S) caddc) cmul)) $;

--| A closed-form expression for the subfactorial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacval2 {x3 y1 f2 k n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2 k) (S: class f2 k) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N S) (co (cfv N cfa) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cneg c1) (cv k) cexp) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv)) cmul)) $;

--| The subfactorial converges rapidly to ` N ! / _e ` . This is part of Metamath 100 proof #88. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfaclim {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cfv N cfa) ceu cdiv) (cfv N S) cmin) cabs) (co c1 N cdiv) clt) $;

--| Another closed form expression for the subfactorial. The expression ` |_ `` ( x + 1 / 2 ) ` is a way of saying "rounded to the nearest integer". (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem subfacval3 {x3 y1 f2 n: setvar} (D: class x3 y1 f2) (S: class f2) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (cfv (co c1 (cv n) cfz) D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N S) (cfv (co (co (cfv N cfa) ceu cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) cfl)) $;

--| The derangements formula, which expresses the number of derangements of a finite nonempty set in terms of the factorial. The expression ` |_ `` ( x + 1 / 2 ) ` is a way of saying "rounded to the nearest integer". This is part of Metamath 100 proof #88. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Jan-2015.)
theorem derangfmla {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (A2: class) (D: class f2):
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 cfn (cfv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cv x3) (cv x3) (cv f2)) (wral y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1)) (cv x3)))) chash)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wceq (cfv A2 D) (cfv (co (co (cfv (cfv A2 chash) cfa) ceu cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) cfl)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem1 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class y1) (F O X: class):
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 A2 cfz))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X S) (w3a (wss X (co c1 A2 cfz)) (wiso X (cima F X) clt O (cres F X)) (wcel A2 X)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem2 {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class y1) (F O: class):
  $ wceq S (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 A2 cfz))) $ >
  $ wa (wcel (cima chash S) cfn) (wss (cima chash S) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co c1 N cfz)) (wceq (cfv A2 K) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 A2 cfz)))) cr clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (co c1 N cfz))) (wcel (csn A2) (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 A2 cfz)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 (co c1 N cfz))) (wcel (cfv A2 K) (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel A2 (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 A2 cfz))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem6 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) cn K) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem7 {x3 y1 s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel (cfv A2 K) (co c1 (co R c1 cmin) cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex s1 (wa (wbr R (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt O (cres F (cv s1)))) (cpw (co c1 N cfz))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (K: class x3 y1) (N O: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt O (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wor cr O $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cfv A2 K) (cfv B2 K)) (wn (wbr (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F) O))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem9 {x3 y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (T: class x3 y1 n) (F I J N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt clt (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n (co c1 N cfz) (cop (cfv (cv n) I) (cfv (cv n) J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) (cxp cn cn) T) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem10 {x3 y1 m n: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S: class n) (T: class x3 y1 n) (F: class) (I J: class m) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt clt (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n (co c1 N cfz) (cop (cfv (cv n) I) (cfv (cv n) J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex m (wo (wn (wcel (cfv (cv m) I) (co c1 (co R c1 cmin) cfz))) (wn (wcel (cfv (cv m) J) (co c1 (co S c1 cmin) cfz)))) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdszelem11 {x3 y1 n s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S: class n) (T: class x3 y1 n) (F I J N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt clt (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csup (cima chash (crab y1 (wa (wiso (cv y1) (cima F (cv y1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv y1))) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cpw (co c1 (cv x3) cfz)))) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cmpt n (co c1 N cfz) (cop (cfv (cv n) I) (cfv (cv n) J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex s1 (wo (wa (wbr R (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt clt (cres F (cv s1)))) (wa (wbr S (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv s1))))) (cpw (co c1 N cfz))) $;

--| The Erd&#337;s-Szekeres theorem. For any injective sequence ` F ` on the reals of length at least ` ( R - 1 ) x. ( S - 1 ) + 1 ` , there is either a subsequence of length at least ` R ` on which ` F ` is increasing (i.e. a ` < , < ` order isomorphism) or a subsequence of length at least ` S ` on which ` F ` is decreasing (i.e. a ` < , ``' < ` order isomorphism, recalling that ` ``' < ` is the "greater than" relation). This is part of Metamath 100 proof #73. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdsze {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (R S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) N clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex s1 (wo (wa (wbr R (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt clt (cres F (cv s1)))) (wa (wbr S (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv s1))))) (cpw (co c1 N cfz))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdsze2lem1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N (cfv A2 chash) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wf1 (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) A2 (cv f2)) (wiso (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (crn (cv f2)) clt clt (cv f2)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ erdsze2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdsze2lem2 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S F G N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N (cfv A2 chash) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (crn G) clt clt G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex s1 (wo (wa (wbr R (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt clt (cres F (cv s1)))) (wa (wbr S (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv s1))))) (cpw A2)) $;

--| Generalize the statement of the Erd&#337;s-Szekeres theorem ~ erdsze to "sequences" indexed by an arbitrary subset of ` RR ` , which can be infinite. This is part of Metamath 100 proof #73. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jan-2015.)
theorem erdsze2 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 R S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co R c1 cmin) (co S c1 cmin) cmul) (cfv A2 chash) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex s1 (wo (wa (wbr R (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt clt (cres F (cv s1)))) (wa (wbr S (cfv (cv s1) chash) cle) (wiso (cv s1) (cima F (cv s1)) clt (ccnv clt) (cres F (cv s1))))) (cpw A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem1 (A2 T K N X: class):
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wcel (cdif X A2) T $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 K) T $ >
  $ wi (wceq N A2) (wa (wss N X) (wss (cpr (cdif X N) (cfv N K)) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Write interior in terms of closure and complement: ` i A = c k c A ` where ` c ` is complement and ` k ` is closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem2 (A2 I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 I) (cdif X (cfv (cdif X A2) K)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . A closure is a subset of the base set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem3 (A2 I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wss (cfv A2 K) X $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Complementation is an involution on the set of subsets of a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem4 (A2 I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq (cdif X (cdif X A2)) A2 $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Closure is an idempotent operation in the set of subsets of a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem5 (A2 I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 K) K) (cfv A2 K) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . If ` k ` is the complementation operator and ` k ` is the closure operator, this expresses the identity ` k c k A = k c k c k c k A ` for any subset ` A ` of the topological space. This is the key result that lets us cut down long enough sequences of ` c k c k ... ` that arise when applying closure and complement repeatedly to ` A ` , and explains why we end up with a number as large as ` 1 4 ` , yet no larger. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem6 (A2 B2 I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cdif X (cfv A2 K)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv B2 K) I) K) (cfv B2 K) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 : main proof. The set ` T ` here contains all the distinct combinations of ` k ` and ` c ` that can arise, and we prove here that applying ` k ` or ` c ` to any element of ` T ` yields another element of ` T ` . In operator shorthand, we have ` T = { A , c A , k A ` ` , c k A , k c A , c k c A , k c k A , c k c k A , k c k c A , ` ` c k c k c A , k c k c k A , c k c k c k A , k c k c k c A , ` ` c k c k c k c A } ` . From the identities ` c c A = A ` and ` k k A = k A ` , we can reduce any operator combination containing two adjacent identical operators, which is why the list only contains alternating sequences. The reason the sequences don't keep going after a certain point is due to the identity ` k c k A = k c k c k c k A ` , proved in ~ kur14lem6 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem7 (A2 B2 C D T I J K N X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cdif X (cfv A2 K)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv (cdif X A2) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv A2 K) I) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cun (cun (ctp A2 (cdif X A2) (cfv A2 K)) (ctp B2 C (cfv A2 I))) (ctp (cfv B2 K) D (cfv (cfv A2 I) K))) (cun (ctp (cfv C I) (cfv D K) (cfv (cfv B2 K) I)) (cpr (cfv (cfv C I) K) (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 I) K) I)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N T) (wa (wss N X) (wss (cpr (cdif X N) (cfv N K)) T)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Show that the set ` T ` contains at most ` 1 4 ` elements. (It could be less if some of the operators take the same value for a given set, but Kuratowski showed that this upper bound of ` 1 4 ` is tight in the sense that there exist topological spaces and subsets of these spaces for which all ` 1 4 ` generated sets are distinct, and indeed the real numbers form such a topological space.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem8 (A2 B2 C D T I J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cdif X (cfv A2 K)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv (cdif X A2) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv A2 K) I) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cun (cun (ctp A2 (cdif X A2) (cfv A2 K)) (ctp B2 C (cfv A2 I))) (ctp (cfv B2 K) D (cfv (cfv A2 I) K))) (cun (ctp (cfv C I) (cfv D K) (cfv (cfv B2 K) I)) (cpr (cfv (cfv C I) K) (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 I) K) I)))) $ >
  $ wa (wcel T cfn) (wbr (cfv T chash) (cdc c1 c4) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Since the set ` T ` is closed under closure and complement, it contains the minimal set ` S ` as a subset, so ` S ` also has at most ` 1 4 ` elements. (Indeed ` S = T ` , and it's not hard to prove this, but we don't need it for this proof.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem9 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 C D S: class x3 y1) (T: class) (I J: class x3 y1) (K: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cdif X (cfv A2 K)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv (cdif X A2) K) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv (cfv A2 K) I) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cun (cun (ctp A2 (cdif X A2) (cfv A2 K)) (ctp B2 C (cfv A2 I))) (ctp (cfv B2 K) D (cfv (cfv A2 I) K))) (cun (ctp (cfv C I) (cfv D K) (cfv (cfv B2 K) I)) (cpr (cfv (cfv C I) K) (cfv (cfv (cfv A2 I) K) I)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cint (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wss (cpr (cdif X (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) K)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw (cpw X)))) $ >
  $ wa (wcel S cfn) (wbr (cfv S chash) (cdc c1 c4) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ kur14 . Discharge the set ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14lem10 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3 y1) (J K X: class):
  $ wcel J ctop $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq S (cint (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wss (cpr (cdif X (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) K)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw (cpw X)))) $ >
  $ wss A2 X $ >
  $ wa (wcel S cfn) (wbr (cfv S chash) (cdc c1 c4) cle) $;

--| Kuratowski's closure-complement theorem. There are at most 14 sets which can be obtained by the application of the closure and complement operations to a set in a topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem kur14 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (S: class x3 y1) (J: class) (K: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq S (cint (crab x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wss (cpr (cdif X (cv y1)) (cfv (cv y1) K)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw (cpw X)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wss A2 X)) (wa (wcel S cfn) (wbr (cfv S chash) (cdc c1 c4) cle)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the retract relation.
term cretr: class;

--| Define the set of retractions on two topological spaces. We say that ` R ` is a retraction from ` J ` to ` K ` . or ` R e. ( J Retr K ) ` iff there is an ` S ` such that ` R : J --> K , S : K --> J ` are continuous functions called the retraction and section respectively, and their composite ` R o. S ` is homotopic to the identity map. If a retraction exists, we say ` J ` is a retract of ` K ` . (This terminology is borrowed from HoTT and appears to be nonstandard, although it has similaries to the concept of retract in the category of topological spaces and to a deformation retract in general topology.) Two topological spaces that are retracts of each other are called homotopy equivalent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_retr {j k s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cretr (cmpo j k ctop ctop (crab r (wrex s1 (wne (co (ccom (cv r) (cv s1)) (cres cid (cuni (cv j))) (co (cv j) (cv j) chtpy)) c0) (co (cv k) (cv j) ccn)) (co (cv j) (cv k) ccn))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of path-connected topologies.
term cpconn: class;

--| Extend class notation with the class of simply connected topologies.
term csconn: class;

--| Define the class of path-connected topologies. A topology is path-connected if there is a path (a continuous function from the closed unit interval) that goes from ` x ` to ` y ` for any points ` x , y ` in the space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_pconn {x3 y1 f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpconn (crab j (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv y1))) (co cii (cv j) ccn)) (cuni (cv j))) (cuni (cv j))) ctop) $;

--| Define the class of simply connected topologies. A topology is simply connected if it is path-connected and every loop (continuous path with identical start and endpoint) is contractible to a point (path-homotopic to a constant function). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_sconn {f2 j: setvar}:
  $ wceq csconn (crab j (wral f2 (wi (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv f2))) (wbr (cv f2) (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 (cv f2)))) (cfv (cv j) cphtpc))) (co cii (cv j) ccn)) cpconn) $;

--| The property of being a path-connected topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem ispconn {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (J: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel J cpconn) (wa (wcel J ctop) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv y1))) (co cii J ccn)) X) X)) $;

--| The property of being a path-connected topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem pconncn {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 J: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cpconn) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wrex f2 (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) A2) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) B2)) (co cii J ccn)) $;

--| A simply connected space is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem pconntop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cpconn) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| The property of being a simply connected topological space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem issconn {f2: setvar} (J: class):
  $ wb (wcel J csconn) (wa (wcel J cpconn) (wral f2 (wi (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv f2))) (wbr (cv f2) (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 (cv f2)))) (cfv J cphtpc))) (co cii J ccn))) $;

--| A simply connected space is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem sconnpconn (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J csconn) (wcel J cpconn) $;

--| A simply connected space is a topology. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem sconntop (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J csconn) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A closed path in a simply connected space is contractible to a point. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem sconnpht (F J: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J csconn) (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv c1 F))) (wbr F (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 F))) (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| An image of a path-connected space is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnpconn (F J K X Y: class):
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cpconn) (wfo X Y F) (wcel F (co J K ccn))) (wcel K cpconn) $;

--| A path-connected space is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem pconnconn (J: class):
  $ wi (wcel J cpconn) (wcel J cconn) $;

--| The topological product of two path-connected spaces is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem txpconn (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cpconn) (wcel S cpconn)) (wcel (co R S ctx) cpconn) $;

--| The topological product of a collection of path-connected spaces is path-connected. The proof uses the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.) Remove dependency on ~ ax-reg and ~ ax-inf2 . (Revised by Timothy J. O'Malley, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem ptpconn (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cpconn F)) (wcel (cfv F cpt) cpconn) $;

--| The indiscrete topology (or trivial topology) on any set is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem indispconn (A2: class):
  $ wcel (cpr c0 A2) cpconn $;

--| A connected and locally path-connected space is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.)
theorem connpconn (J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cconn) (wcel J (cnlly cpconn))) (wcel J cpconn) $;

--| A quotient of a path-connected space is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Mar-2015.)
theorem qtoppconn (F J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cpconn) (wfn F X)) (wcel (co J F cqtop) cpconn) $;

--| All fundamental groups in a path-connected space are isomorphic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem pconnpi1 (A2 B2 P Q S T J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq P (co J A2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq Q (co J B2 cpi1) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv Q cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J cpconn) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wbr P Q cgic) $;

--| Any two paths in a simply connected space with the same start and end point are path-homotopic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem sconnpht2 (ph: wff) (F G J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J csconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cfv c1 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (cfv J cphtpc)) $;

--| A path-connected topological space is simply connected iff its fundamental group is trivial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem sconnpi1 (J X Y: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cpconn) (wcel Y X)) (wb (wcel J csconn) (wbr (cfv (co J Y cpi1) cbs) c1o cen)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ txsconn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem txsconnlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S F G H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel R ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cii (co R S ctx) ccn)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (ccom (cres c1st (cxp (cuni R) (cuni S))) F) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (ccom (cres c2nd (cxp (cuni R) (cuni S))) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co A2 (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 A2))) (cfv R cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co B2 (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 B2))) (cfv S cphtpy))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cfv cc0 F))) (cfv (co R S ctx) cphtpc)) $;

--| The topological product of two simply connected spaces is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem txsconn (R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel R csconn) (wcel S csconn)) (wcel (co R S ctx) csconn) $;

--| A convex subset of the complex numbers is path-connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem cvxpconn {x3 y1 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class y1) (J: class x3 y1) (K: class t):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wcel (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J S crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cpconn) $;

--| A convex subset of the complex numbers is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem cvxsconn {x3 y1 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class) (J: class x3 y1) (K: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv t) (co cc0 c1 cicc)))) (wcel (co (co (cv t) (cv x3) cmul) (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv y1) cmul) caddc) S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J S crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K csconn) $;

--| An open ball in the complex numbers is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Feb-2015.)
theorem blsconn (P R S J K: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wceq S (co P R (cfv (ccom cabs cmin) cbl)) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J S crest) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cc) (wcel R cxr)) (wcel K csconn) $;

--| The topology of the complex numbers is locally simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Mar-2015.)
theorem cnllysconn (J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wcel J (clly csconn) $;

--| A subset of ` RR ` is simply connected iff it is connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem resconn (A2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) A2 crest) $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wb (wcel J csconn) (wcel J cconn)) $;

--| An open interval is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem ioosconn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co A2 B2 cioo) crest) csconn $;

--| A closed interval is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem iccsconn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) csconn) $;

--| The real numbers are simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem retopsconn:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) csconn $;

--| A closed interval is locally simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem iccllysconn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) (clly csconn)) $;

--| The real numbers are locally simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem rellysconn:
  $ wcel (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (clly csconn) $;

--| The unit interval is simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Mar-2015.)
theorem iisconn:
  $ wcel cii csconn $;

--| The unit interval is locally simply connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem iillysconn:
  $ wcel cii (clly csconn) $;

--| The unit interval is locally connected. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Jul-2015.)
theorem iinllyconn:
  $ wcel cii (cnlly cconn) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of covering maps.
term ccvm: class;

--| Define the class of covering maps on two topological spaces. A function ` f : c --> j ` is a covering map if it is continuous and for every point ` x ` in the target space there is a neighborhood ` k ` of ` x ` and a decomposition ` s ` of the preimage of ` k ` as a disjoint union such that ` f ` is a homeomorphism of each set ` u e. s ` onto ` k ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
axiom df_cvm {x3 v u f2 j k s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccvm (cmpo c j ctop ctop (crab f2 (wral x3 (wrex k (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv k)) (wrex s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres (cv f2) (cv u)) (co (co (cv c) (cv u) crest) (co (cv j) (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw (cv c)) (csn c0)))) (cv j)) (cuni (cv j))) (co (cv c) (cv j) ccn))) $;

--| Lemma for covering maps. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem fncvm:
  $ wfn ccvm (cxp ctop ctop) $;

--| Change bound variables in the set of even coverings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmscbv {v u k s1 a b c d: setvar} (C: class v d) (S: class v u k s1 a b c d) (F J: class v d):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt a J (crab b (wa (wceq (cuni (cv b)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv a))) (wral c (wa (wral d (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (cdif (cv b) (csn (cv c)))) (wcel (cres F (cv c)) (co (co C (cv c) crest) (co J (cv a) crest) chmeo))) (cv b))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $;

--| The property of being a covering map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem iscvm {x3 v u k s1: setvar} (C: class v) (S: class x3 v u k s1) (F J: class v) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wb (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wa (w3a (wcel C ctop) (wcel J ctop) (wcel F (co C J ccn))) (wral x3 (wrex k (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv k)) (wne (cfv (cv k) S) c0)) J) X)) $;

--| Reverse closure for a covering map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmtop1 (C F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel C ctop) $;

--| Reverse closure for a covering map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmtop2 (C F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel J ctop) $;

--| A covering map is a continuous function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmcn (C F J: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel F (co C J ccn)) $;

--| Property of a covering map. In order to make the covering property more manageable, we define here the set ` S ( k ) ` of all even coverings of an open set ` k ` in the range. Then the covering property states that every point has a neighborhood which has an even covering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmcov {x3 v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (P: class v u s1) (S: class v u k s1) (F J: class) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel P X)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cfv (cv x3) S) c0)) J) $;

--| Reverse closure for an even covering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsrcl {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel U J) $;

--| One direction of ~ cvmsval . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsi {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv U S)) (w3a (wcel U J) (wa (wss T C) (wne T c0)) (wa (wceq (cuni T) (cima (ccnv F) U)) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif T (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J U crest) chmeo))) T))) $;

--| Elementhood in the set ` S ` of all even coverings of an open set in ` J ` . ` S ` is an even covering of ` U ` if it is a nonempty collection of disjoint open sets in ` C ` whose union is the preimage of ` U ` , such that each set ` u e. S ` is homeomorphic under ` F ` to ` U ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsval {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J V: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel T (cfv U S)) (w3a (wcel U J) (wa (wss T C) (wne T c0)) (wa (wceq (cuni T) (cima (ccnv F) U)) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif T (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J U crest) chmeo))) T)))) $;

--| An even covering is a subset of the topology of the domain (i.e. a collection of open sets). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsss {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wss T C) $;

--| An even covering is nonempty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsn0 {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wne T c0) $;

--| An even covering of ` U ` has union equal to the preimage of ` U ` by ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsuni {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wceq (cuni T) (cima (ccnv F) U)) $;

--| An even covering of ` U ` is a disjoint union. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsdisj {v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 T) (wcel B2 T)) (wo (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0)) $;

--| Every element of an even covering of ` U ` is homeomorphic to ` U ` via ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmshmeo {v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel (cres F A2) (co (co C A2 crest) (co J U crest) chmeo)) $;

--| ` F ` , localized to an element of an even covering of ` U ` , is a bijection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsf1o {v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 T)) (wf1o A2 U (cres F A2)) $;

--| The sets of an even covering are clopen in the subspace topology on ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmscld {v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 T)) (wcel A2 (cfv (co C (cima (ccnv F) U) crest) ccld)) $;

--| An open subset of an evenly covered set is evenly covered. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.)
theorem cvmsss2 {v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (U F J V: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel V J) (wss V U)) (wi (wne (cfv U S) c0) (wne (cfv V S) c0)) $;

--| The covering map property can be restricted to an open subset. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jul-2015.)
theorem cvmcov2 {x3 v u k s1: setvar} (C: class) (P: class v u s1) (S: class v u k s1) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel U J) (wcel P U)) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wne (cfv (cv x3) S) c0)) (cpw U)) $;

--| Every element in ` U. T ` is a member of a unique element of ` T ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmseu {x3 v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (w3a (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 F) U))) (wreu x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) T) $;

--| Identify the unique element of ` T ` containing ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmsiota {x3 v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (T: class k) (U F J: class) (W: class x3 u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq W (crio x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) T) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (w3a (wcel T (cfv U S)) (wcel A2 B2) (wcel (cfv A2 F) U))) (wa (wcel W T) (wcel A2 W)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmopn . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem cvmopnlem {v u k s1: setvar} (A2: class k s1) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (F J: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel A2 C)) (wcel (cima F A2) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmfo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmfolem {v u k s1: setvar} (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k s1) (F J: class) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wfo B2 X F) $;

--| A covering map is an open map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem cvmopn (A2 C F J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel A2 C)) (wcel (cima F A2) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmliftmo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem cvmliftmolem1 {v u k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (ps: wff v u k s1) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (Q R: class v u k s1) (S: class v u k) (T: class k) (U F: class) (I: class v u k s1) (J: class) (K: class v u k) (M: class) (N: class v u k) (O: class v u k s1) (W: class k s1) (X: class v u k s1) (Y: class v u k):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom F M) (ccom F N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv O M) (cfv O N)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel T (cfv U S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel W T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wss I (cima (ccnv M) W)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (co K I crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel X I) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel Q I) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel R I) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cfv (cfv X M) F) U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wi (wcel Q (cdm (cin M N))) (wcel R (cdm (cin M N)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmliftmo . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem cvmliftmolem2 {v u k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (B2: class u k s1) (C: class) (S: class v u k) (F J: class) (K: class v u k) (M: class) (N: class v u k) (O: class v u k s1) (Y: class v u k):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom F M) (ccom F N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv O M) (cfv O N)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M N) $;

--| A lift of a continuous function from a connected and locally connected space over a covering map is unique when it exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem cvmliftmoi (ph: wff) (B2 C F J K M N O Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co K C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom F M) (ccom F N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv O M) (cfv O N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq M N) $;

--| A lift of a continuous function from a connected and locally connected space over a covering map is unique when it exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Mar-2015.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem cvmliftmo {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class f2) (C P F G: class) (J: class f2) (K O: class) (Y: class f2):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv O G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrmo f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv O (cv f2)) P)) (co K C ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . In ~ cvmliftlem15 , we picked an ` N ` large enough so that the sections ` ( G " [ ( k - 1 ) / N , k / N ] ) ` are all contained in an even covering, and the function ` T ` enumerates these even coverings. So ` 1st `` ( T `` M ) ` is a neighborhood of ` ( G " [ ( M - 1 ) / N , M / N ] ) ` , and ` 2nd `` ( T `` M ) ` is an even covering of ` 1st `` ( T `` M ) ` , which is to say a disjoint union of open sets in ` C ` whose image is ` 1st `` ( T `` M ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem1 {v u j k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (ps: wff v u j k s1) (B2: class u j k s1) (C: class) (P: class j s1) (S T F G J: class) (L: class v u j k s1) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel M (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cfv (cfv M T) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cfv M T) c1st) S)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . ` W = [ ( k - 1 ) / N , k / N ] ` is a subset of ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` for each ` M e. ( 1 ... N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem2 {v u j k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (ps: wff v u j k s1) (B2: class u j k s1) (C: class) (P: class j s1) (S T F G J: class) (L: class v u j k s1) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel M (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wss W (co cc0 c1 cicc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Since ` 1st `` ( T `` M ) ` is a neighborhood of ` ( G " W ) ` , every element ` A e. W ` satisfies ` ( G `` A ) e. ( 1st `` ( T `` M ) ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem3 {v u j k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (ps: wff v u j k s1) (A2: class v u j k s1) (B2: class u j k s1) (C: class) (P: class j s1) (S T F G J: class) (L: class v u j k s1) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1) (X: class v u k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel M (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cfv A2 G) (cfv (cfv M T) c1st)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . The function ` Q ` will be our lifted path, defined piecewise on each section ` [ ( M - 1 ) / N , M / N ] ` for ` M e. ( 1 ... N ) ` . For ` M = 0 ` , it is a "seed" value which makes the rest of the recursion work, a singleton function mapping ` 0 ` to ` P ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem4 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (N: class j s1) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 Q) (csn (cop cc0 P)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Definition of ` Q ` at a successor. This is a function defined on ` W ` as ` ``' ( T |`` I ) o. G ` where ` I ` is the unique covering set of ` 2nd `` ( T `` M ) ` that contains ` Q ( M - 1 ) ` evaluated at the last defined point, namely ` ( M - 1 ) / N ` (note that for ` M = 1 ` this is using the seed value ` Q ( 0 ) ( 0 ) = P ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem5 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1 b) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M cn)) (wceq (cfv M Q) (cmpt z W (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co M c1 cmin) Q)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv M T) c2nd))))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Induction step for ~ cvmliftlem7 . Assuming that ` Q ( M - 1 ) ` is defined at ` ( M - 1 ) / N ` and is a preimage of ` G ( ( M - 1 ) / N ) ` , the next segment ` Q ( M ) ` is also defined and is a function on ` W ` which is a lift ` G ` for this segment. This follows explicitly from the definition ` Q ( M ) = ``' ( F |`` I ) o. G ` since ` G ` is in ` 1st `` ( F `` M ) ` for the entire interval so that ` ``' ( F |`` I ) ` maps this into ` I ` and ` F o. Q ` maps back to ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem6 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (ps: wff x3 v u j k m s1 b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1 b) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel M (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wcel (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co M c1 cmin) Q)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) G)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wa (wf W B2 (cfv M Q)) (wceq (ccom F (cfv M Q)) (cres G W))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Prove by induction that every ` Q ` function is well-defined (we can immediately follow this theorem with ~ cvmliftlem6 to show functionality and lifting of ` Q ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem7 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1 b) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M (co c1 N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co M c1 cmin) Q)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) G)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . The functions ` Q ` are continuous functions because they are defined as ` ``' ( F |`` I ) o. G ` where ` G ` is continuous and ` ( F |`` I ) ` is a homeomorphism. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem8 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (W: class v u j s1 b) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq W (co (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co M N cdiv) cicc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M (co c1 N cfz))) (wcel (cfv M Q) (co (co L W crest) C ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . The ` Q ( M ) ` functions are defined on almost disjoint intervals, but they overlap at the edges. Here we show that at these points the ` Q ` functions agree on their common domain. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem9 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (M: class) (N: class j s1) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel M (co c1 N cfz))) (wceq (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv M Q)) (cfv (co (co M c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co M c1 cmin) Q))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . The function ` K ` is going to be our complete lifted path, formed by unioning together all the ` Q ` functions (each of which is defined on one segment ` [ ( M - 1 ) / N , M / N ] ` of the interval). Here we prove by induction that ` K ` is a continuous function and a lift of ` G ` by applying ~ cvmliftlem6 , ~ cvmliftlem7 (to show it is a function and a lift), ~ cvmliftlem8 (to show it is continuous), and ~ cvmliftlem9 (to show that different ` Q ` functions agree on the intersection of their domains, so that the pasting lemma ~ paste gives that ` K ` is well-defined and continuous). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem10 {x3 z v u j k m n s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (ch: wff x3 z v u j k m n s1 b) (B2: class x3 u j k m n s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T: class n) (F G: class) (J: class m) (K: class x3 z v u j k m n s1 b) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (N: class j s1) (X: class x3 z v u k m n s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (ciun k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q)) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wcel (cv n) cn) (wcel (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (co c1 N cfz))) (wa (wcel (ciun k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q)) (co (co L (co cc0 (co (cv n) N cdiv) cicc) crest) C ccn)) (wceq (ccom F (ciun k (co c1 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q))) (cres G (co cc0 (co (cv n) N cdiv) cicc))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K (co (co L (co cc0 (co N N cdiv) cicc) crest) C ccn)) (wceq (ccom F K) (cres G (co cc0 (co N N cdiv) cicc)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem11 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (K: class x3 z v u j k m s1 b) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (N: class j s1) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (ciun k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K (co cii C ccn)) (wceq (ccom F K) G)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . The initial value of ` K ` is ` P ` because ` Q ( 1 ) ` is a subset of ` K ` which takes value ` P ` at ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem13 {x3 z v u j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P Q: class j s1) (S: class m) (T F G: class) (J: class m) (K: class x3 z v u j k m s1 b) (L: class x3 v u j k m s1 b) (N: class j s1) (X: class x3 z v u k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (ciun k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 K) P) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Putting the results of ~ cvmliftlem11 , ~ cvmliftlem13 and ~ cvmliftmo together, we have that ` K ` is a continuous function, satisfies ` F o. K = G ` and ` K ( 0 ) = P ` , and is equal to any other function which also has these properties, so it follows that ` K ` is the unique lift of ` G ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem14 {x3 z v u f2 j k m s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff v u k m b) (B2: class x3 u f2 j k m s1) (C: class x3 m) (P: class j s1) (Q: class f2 j s1) (S: class m) (T: class f2) (F G: class) (J: class m) (K: class x3 z v u j k m s1 b) (L: class x3 v u f2 j k m s1 b) (N: class f2 j s1) (X: class x3 z v u f2 k m s1 b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 N cfz) (ciun j J (cxp (csn (cv j)) (cfv (cv j) S))) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wss (cima G (co (co (co (cv k) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv k) N cdiv) cicc)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) T) c1st)) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cseq (cmpo x3 m cvv cn (cmpt z (co (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (co (cv m) N cdiv) cicc) (cfv (cfv (cv z) G) (ccnv (cres F (crio b (wcel (cfv (co (co (cv m) c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cv x3)) (cv b)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) T) c2nd))))))) (cun (cres cid cn) (csn (cop cc0 (csn (cop cc0 P))))) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq K (ciun k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) P)) (co cii C ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift . Discharge the assumptions of ~ cvmliftlem14 . The set of all open subsets ` u ` of the unit interval such that ` G " u ` is contained in an even covering of some open set in ` J ` is a cover of ` II ` by the definition of a covering map, so by the Lebesgue number lemma ~ lebnumii , there is a subdivision of the closed unit interval into ` N ` equal parts such that each part is entirely contained within one such open set of ` J ` . Then using finite choice ~ ac6sfi to uniformly select one such subset and one even covering of each subset, we are ready to finish the proof with ~ cvmliftlem14 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftlem15 {v u f2 k s1: setvar} (ph: wff v u k) (B2: class u f2 k s1) (C: class) (P: class s1) (S F G J: class) (X: class v u f2 k s1):
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral u (wa (wral v (wceq (cin (cv u) (cv v)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv u)))) (wcel (cres F (cv u)) (co (co C (cv u) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) P)) (co cii C ccn)) $;

--| One of the important properties of covering maps is that any path ` G ` in the base space "lifts" to a path ` f ` in the covering space such that ` F o. f = G ` , and given a starting point ` P ` in the covering space this lift is unique. The proof is contained in ~ cvmliftlem1 thru ~ cvmliftlem15 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmlift {f2: setvar} (B2 C P F G J: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wcel G (co cii J ccn))) (wa (wcel P B2) (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)))) (wreu f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) P)) (co cii C ccn)) $;

--| A covering map is an onto function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmfo (B2 C F J X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) (wfo B2 X F) $;

--| Write out a function ` H ` that is the unique lift of ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2015.)
theorem cvmliftiota {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2: class f2) (C P F G: class) (H J: class f2):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq H (crio f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) P)) (co cii C ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co cii J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv cc0 G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel H (co cii C ccn)) (wceq (ccom F H) G) (wceq (cfv cc0 H) P)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jun-2015.)
theorem cvmlift2lem1 {y1 u t: setvar} (x3: setvar) (M: class t):
  $ wi (wral y1 (wrex u (wb (wss (cxp (cv u) (csn (cv x3))) M) (wss (cxp (cv u) (csn (cv t))) M)) (cfv (csn (cv y1)) (cfv cii cnei))) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wi (wss (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cv x3))) M) (wss (cxp (co cc0 c1 cicc) (csn (cv t))) M)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift2 and ~ cvmlift3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem cvmlift2lem9a {k s1 c d: setvar} (ph: wff k s1 c d) (A2: class) (B2: class k s1 c d) (C: class) (S: class k s1 c d) (T: class k) (F: class) (H: class k s1 c d) (J: class) (K M: class k s1 c d) (W: class k s1) (X Y: class k s1 c d):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral c (wa (wral d (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv c)))) (wcel (cres F (cv c)) (co (co C (cv c) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Y B2 H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccom F H) (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv A2 S)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel W T) (wcel (cfv X H) W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss M Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima H M) W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres H M) (co (co K M crest) C ccn)) $;

