theorem cvmlift3lem8 {x3 z f2 g2 k s1 c d: setvar} (ph: wff z g2 k s1 c d) (B2: class k s1 c) (C: class) (P: class k s1) (S: class z g2 k s1 c d) (F: class) (G: class s1) (H: class k s1) (J: class z) (K O: class k s1 d) (Y: class k s1 c d):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K csconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cpconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv O G)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 Y (crio z (wrex f2 (w3a (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) O) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv c1 (crio g2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv g2)) (ccom G (cv f2))) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv g2)) P)) (co cii C ccn))) (cv z))) (co cii K ccn)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral c (wa (wral d (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv c)))) (wcel (cres F (cv c)) (co (co C (cv c) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (co K C ccn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvmlift2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-May-2015.)
theorem cvmlift3lem9 {x3 z f2 g2 k s1 c d: setvar} (ph: wff z g2 k s1 c d) (B2: class k s1 c) (C: class) (P: class k s1) (S: class z g2 k s1 c d) (F: class) (G: class s1) (H: class k s1) (J: class z) (K O: class k s1 d) (Y: class k s1 c d):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K csconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cpconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv O G)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 Y (crio z (wrex f2 (w3a (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) O) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wceq (cfv c1 (crio g2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv g2)) (ccom G (cv f2))) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv g2)) P)) (co cii C ccn))) (cv z))) (co cii K ccn)) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt k J (crab s1 (wa (wceq (cuni (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv F) (cv k))) (wral c (wa (wral d (wceq (cin (cv c) (cv d)) c0) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv c)))) (wcel (cres F (cv c)) (co (co C (cv c) crest) (co J (cv k) crest) chmeo))) (cv s1))) (cdif (cpw C) (csn c0)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv O (cv f2)) P)) (co K C ccn)) $;

--| A general version of ~ cvmlift . If ` K ` is simply connected and weakly locally path-connected, then there is a unique lift of functions on ` K ` which commutes with the covering map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2015.)
theorem cvmlift3 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C P F G J K O Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cuni C) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C J ccvm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K csconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cnlly cpconn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co K J ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv P F) (cfv O G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu f2 (wa (wceq (ccom F (cv f2)) G) (wceq (cfv O (cv f2)) P)) (co K C ccn)) $;

--| The function ` F ` from ~ snmlval is a mapping from positive integers to real numbers in the range ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem snmlff {k n: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class k) (F: class k n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 (co R (cv k) cexp) cmul) R cmo) cfl) B2) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wf cn (co cc0 c1 cicc) F $;

--| The function ` F ` from ~ snmlval maps ` N ` to the relative density of ` B ` in the first ` N ` digits of the digit string of ` A ` in base ` R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem snmlfval {k n: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class k) (F: class k n) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 (co R (cv k) cexp) cmul) R cmo) cfl) B2) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N F) (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 (co R (cv k) cexp) cmul) R cmo) cfl) B2) (co c1 N cfz)) chash) N cdiv)) $;

--| The property " ` A ` is simply normal in base ` R ` ". A number is simply normal if each digit ` 0 <_ b < R ` occurs in the base- ` R ` digit string of ` A ` with frequency ` 1 / R ` (which is consistent with the expectation in an infinite random string of numbers selected from ` 0 ... R - 1 ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem snmlval {x3 k n r b: setvar} (A2: class r) (R: class) (S: class x3 k n r b):
  $ wceq S (cmpt r (cfv c2 cuz) (crab x3 (wral b (wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co (cv x3) (co (cv r) (cv k) cexp) cmul) (cv r) cmo) cfl) (cv b)) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) (co c1 (cv r) cdiv) cli) (co cc0 (co (cv r) c1 cmin) cfz)) cr)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cfv R S)) (w3a (wcel R (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 cr) (wral b (wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 (co R (cv k) cexp) cmul) R cmo) cfl) (cv b)) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) (co c1 R cdiv) cli) (co cc0 (co R c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| If ` A ` is simply normal, then the function ` F ` of relative density of ` B ` in the digit string converges to ` 1 / R ` , i.e. the set of occurrences of ` B ` in the digit string has natural density ` 1 / R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.)
theorem snmlflim {x3 k n r b: setvar} (A2: class r) (B2: class x3 r) (R: class) (S: class x3 k n r b) (F: class x3 k n r):
  $ wceq S (cmpt r (cfv c2 cuz) (crab x3 (wral b (wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co (cv x3) (co (cv r) (cv k) cexp) cmul) (cv r) cmo) cfl) (cv b)) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) (co c1 (cv r) cdiv) cli) (co cc0 (co (cv r) c1 cmin) cfz)) cr)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (crab k (wceq (cfv (co (co A2 (co R (cv k) cexp) cmul) R cmo) cfl) B2) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) chash) (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv R S)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 (co R c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr F (co c1 R cdiv) cli) $;

--| The Godel-set of membership.
term cgoe: class;

--| The Godel-set for the Sheffer stroke.
term cgna: class;

--| The Godel-set of universal quantification. (Note that this is not a wff.)
term cgol (U N: class): class;

--| The satisfaction function.
term csat: class;

--| The formula set predicate.
term cfmla: class;

--| The ` e. ` -satisfaction function.
term csate: class;

--| The "proves" relation.
term cprv: class;

--| Define the Godel-set of membership. Here the arguments ` x = <. N , P >. ` correspond to v_N and v_P , so ` ( (/) e.g 1o ) ` actually means v_0 ` e. ` v_1 , not ` 0 e. 1 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goel {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgoe (cmpt x3 (cxp com com) (cop c0 (cv x3))) $;

--| Define the Godel-set for the Sheffer stroke NAND. Here the arguments ` x = <. U , V >. ` are also Godel-sets corresponding to smaller formulas. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gona {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgna (cmpt x3 (cxp cvv cvv) (cop c1o (cv x3))) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of universal quantification. Here ` N e. _om ` corresponds to v_N , and ` U ` represents another formula, and this expression is ` [ A. x ph ] = A.g N U ` where ` x ` is the ` N ` -th variable, ` U = [ ph ] ` is the code for ` ph ` . Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goal (U N: class):
  $ wceq (cgol U N) (cop c2o (cop N U)) $;

--| Define the satisfaction predicate. This recursive construction builds up a function over wff codes (see ~ satff ) and simultaneously defines the set of assignments to all variables from ` M ` that makes the coded wff true in the model ` M ` , where ` e. ` is interpreted as the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` . The interpretation of the statement ` S e. ( ( ( M Sat E ) `` n ) `` U ) ` is that for the model ` <. M , E >. ` , ` S : _om --> M ` is a valuation of the variables (v_0 ` = ( S `` (/) ) ` , v_1 ` = ( S `` 1o ) ` , etc.) and ` U ` is a code for a wff using ` e. , -/\ , A. ` that is true under the assignment ` S ` . The function is defined by finite recursion; ` ( ( M Sat E ) `` n ) ` only operates on wffs of depth at most ` n e. _om ` , and ` ( ( M Sat E ) `` _om ) = U_ n e. _om ( ( M Sat E ) `` n ) ` operates on all wffs. The coding scheme for the wffs is defined so that <HTML><ul> <li>v<sub>i</sub> ` e. ` v<sub>j</sub> is coded as ` <. (/) , <. i , j >. >. ` ,</li> <li> ` ( ph -/\ ps ) ` is coded as ` <. 1o , <. ph , ps >. >. ` , and</li> <li> ` A. ` v<sub>i</sub> ` ph ` is coded as ` <. 2o , <. i , ph >. >. ` .</li></ul></HTML> (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_sat {x3 y1 z v u e f2 i j m a: setvar}:
  $ wceq csat (cmpo m e cvv cvv (cres (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co (cv m) com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) (cv m)) (co (cv m) com cmap)))) com)) (cv f2))))) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) (cv e)) (co (cv m) com cmap)))) com) com))) (csuc com))) $;

--| A simplified version of the satisfaction predicate, using the standard membership relation and eliminating the extra variable ` n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_sate {u m: setvar}:
  $ wceq csate (cmpo m u cvv cvv (cfv (cv u) (cfv com (co (cv m) (cin cep (cxp (cv m) (cv m))) csat)))) $;

--| Define the predicate which defines the set of valid Godel formulas. The parameter ` n ` defines the maximum height of the formulas: the set ` ( Fmla `` (/) ) ` is all formulas of the form ` x e. y ` (which in our coding scheme is the set ` ( { (/) } X. ( _om X. _om ) ) ` ; see ~ df-sat for the full coding scheme), see ~ fmla0 , and each extra level adds to the complexity of the formulas in ` ( Fmla `` n ) ` , see ~ fmlasuc . Remark: it is sufficient to have atomic formulas of the form ` x e. y ` only, because equations (formulas of the form ` x = y ` ), which are required as (atomic) formulas, can be introduced as a defined notion in terms of ` e.g ` , see ~ df-goeq . ` ( Fmla `` _om ) = U_ n e. _om ( Fmla `` n ) ` is the set of all valid formulas, see ~ fmla . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_fmla {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfmla (cmpt n (csuc com) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (co c0 c0 csat)))) $;

--| Define the "proves" relation on a set. A wff is true in a model ` M ` if for every valuation ` s e. ( M ^m _om ) ` , the interpretation of the wff using the membership relation on ` M ` is true. Since ` |= ` is defined in terms of the interpretations making the given formula true, it is not defined on the empty "model" ` M = (/) ` , since there are no interpretations. In particular, the empty set on the LHS of ` |= ` should not be interpreted as the empty model. Statement ~ prv0 shows that our definition yields ` (/) |= U ` for all formulas, though of course the formula ` E. x x = x ` is not satisfied on the empty model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_prv {u m: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprv (copab m u (wceq (co (cv m) (cv u) csate) (co (cv m) com cmap))) $;

--| A "Godel-set of membership". The variables are identified by their indices (which are natural numbers), and the membership v_i ` e. ` v_j is coded as ` <. (/) , <. i , j >. >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2023.)
theorem goel (I J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wceq (co I J cgoe) (cop c0 (cop I J))) $;

--| A "Godel-set of membership" is a member of a doubled Cartesian product. (Contributed by AV, 16-Sep-2023.)
theorem goelel3xp (I J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wcel (co I J cgoe) (cxp com (cxp com com))) $;

--| Two "Godel-set of membership" codes for two variables are equal iff the two corresponding variables are equal. (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2023.)
theorem goeleq12bg (I J M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M com) (wcel N com)) (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com))) (wb (wceq (co I J cgoe) (co M N cgoe)) (wa (wceq I M) (wceq J N))) $;

--| The "Godel-set for the Sheffer stroke NAND" for two formulas ` A ` and ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 16-Oct-2023.)
theorem gonafv (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cgna) (cop c1o (cop A2 B2))) $;

--| Equality of the "Godel-set of universal quantification". (Contributed by AV, 18-Sep-2023.)
theorem goaleq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq M N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cgol A2 M) (cgol B2 N)) $;

--| The Godel-set for the Sheffer stroke NAND is not equal to the Godel-set of universal quantification. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem gonanegoal {a b: setvar} (u i: setvar):
  $ wne (co (cv a) (cv b) cgna) (cgol (cv u) (cv i)) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf {x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a: setvar} (E: class z) (M: class) (V W: class x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (co M E csat) (cres (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cv f2))))) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E) (co M com cmap)))) com) com))) (csuc com))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate for wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` at an element of the successor of ` _om ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Sep-2023.)
theorem satfsucom {x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a: setvar} (E: class z) (M: class) (N V W: class x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N (csuc com))) (wceq (cfv N (co M E csat)) (cfv N (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cv f2))))) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E) (co M com cmap)))) com) com))))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate for wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` is a function over ` suc _om ` . (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfn (E M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wfn (co M E csat) (csuc com)) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate for wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` at omega ( ` _om ` ). (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2023.)
theorem satom {n: setvar} (E M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (cfv com (co M E csat)) (ciun n com (cfv (cv n) (co M E csat)))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at a successor of ` _om ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Sep-2023.)
theorem satfvsucom {x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a: setvar} (S: class x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a) (E: class z) (M: class) (N V W: class x3 y1 z v u f2 i j a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N (csuc com))) (wceq (cfv N S) (cfv N (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cv f2))))) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E) (co M com cmap)))) com) com))))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at ` (/) ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfv0 {x3 y1 i j a: setvar} (S: class x3 y1 i j a) (E M: class) (V W: class x3 y1 i j a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (cfv c0 S) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E) (co M com cmap)))) com) com))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ satfvsuc . (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfvsuclem1 {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (S: class y1 z i a) (E M: class) (N: class z i a) (V W: class x3 z v i a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cfv N S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cfv N S)) (wcel (cv y1) (cpw (co M com cmap))))) cvv) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ satfvsuc . (Contributed by AV, 8-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfvsuclem2 {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (S: class y1 z i a) (E M: class) (N: class z i a) (V W: class x3 z v i a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cfv N S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cfv N S))) cvv) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at a successor. (Contributed by AV, 10-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfvsuc {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (S: class z i a) (E M: class) (N: class z i a) (V W: class x3 z v i a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wceq (cfv (csuc N) S) (cun (cfv N S) (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cfv N S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cfv N S))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ satfv1 . (Contributed by AV, 9-Nov-2023.)
theorem satfv1lem {z a b: setvar} (E: class z a) (I J: class) (M: class a) (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N com) (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wceq (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop N (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn N)))) (crab b (wbr (cfv I (cv b)) (cfv J (cv b)) E) (co M com cmap))) M) (co M com cmap)) (crab a (wral z (wif (wceq I N) (wif (wceq J N) (wbr (cv z) (cv z) E) (wbr (cv z) (cfv J (cv a)) E)) (wif (wceq J N) (wbr (cfv I (cv a)) (cv z) E) (wbr (cfv I (cv a)) (cfv J (cv a)) E))) M) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes of height 1. (Contributed by AV, 9-Nov-2023.)
theorem satfv1 {x3 y1 z i j k n a l: setvar} (S: class z i j k n a l) (E M: class) (V W: class z i j k n a l):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (cfv c1o S) (cun (cfv c0 S) (copab x3 y1 (wrex i (wrex j (wo (wrex k (wrex l (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe) (co (cv k) (cv l) cgoe) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wo (wn (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E)) (wn (wbr (cfv (cv k) (cv a)) (cfv (cv l) (cv a)) E))) (co M com cmap)))) com) com) (wrex n (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe) (cv n))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab a (wral z (wif (wceq (cv i) (cv n)) (wif (wceq (cv j) (cv n)) (wbr (cv z) (cv z) E) (wbr (cv z) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E)) (wif (wceq (cv j) (cv n)) (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cv z) E) (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv a)) (cfv (cv j) (cv a)) E))) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) com) com)))) $;

--| The binary relation of a satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes is an increasing chain (with respect to inclusion). (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfsschain (A2 B2 S E M V W: class):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com))) (wi (wss B2 A2) (wss (cfv B2 S) (cfv A2 S))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes of height ` ( N + 1 ) ` , expressed by the minimally necessary satisfaction predicates as function over wff codes of height ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfvsucsuc {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1 z v u i a) (S: class z i a) (E M: class) (N: class z i a) (V W: class z v i a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wceq (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) S) (cun (cfv (csuc N) S) (copab x3 y1 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cfv (csuc N) S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) com)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (wrex u (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (cfv N S)))))) $;

--| The binary relation of a satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at a successor. (Contributed by AV, 13-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfbrsuc {z v u f2 i: setvar} (A2 B2: class z f2) (P: class z u f2 i) (S: class z f2 i) (E M: class) (N: class z f2 i) (V W: class z v f2 i) (X Y: class z v u f2 i):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv N S) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wcel N com) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 (cfv (csuc N) S)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 P) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq A2 (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq B2 (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) P) (wrex i (wa (wceq A2 (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq B2 (crab f2 (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv f2) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) P))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at a natural number is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 12-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfrel (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wrel (cfv N (co M E csat))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ satfdm . (Contributed by AV, 12-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfdmlem {v u i a b: setvar} (x3: setvar) (E F M N V W Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel Y com)) (wceq (cdm (cfv Y (co M E csat))) (cdm (cfv Y (co N F csat))))) (wi (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (cfv Y (co M E csat))) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cfv Y (co M E csat))) (wrex a (wo (wrex b (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv a) c1st) (cfv (cv b) c1st) cgna)) (cfv Y (co N F csat))) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv a) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cfv Y (co N F csat)))) $;

--| The domain of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes does not depend on the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfdm {n: setvar} (E F M N V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wa (wcel N X) (wcel F Y))) (wral n (wceq (cdm (cfv (cv n) (co M E csat))) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (co N F csat)))) com) $;

--| The range of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in any model ` M ` and any binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` for a natural number ` N ` is a subset of the power set of all mappings from the natural numbers into the model ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfrnmapom (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wss (crn (cfv N (co M E csat))) (cpw (co M com cmap))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at ` (/) ` is a function. (Contributed by AV, 15-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfv0fun (E M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wfun (cfv c0 (co M E csat))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0 {x3 y1 v u f2 i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co c0 c0 csat) (cres (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cv f2)))))) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex i (wrex j (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) com) com)))) (csuc com)) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation at a successor of ` _om ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0sucom {x3 y1 v u f2 i j: setvar} (N: class x3 y1 v u f2 i j):
  $ wi (wcel N (csuc com)) (wceq (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat)) (cfv N (crdg (cmpt f2 cvv (cun (cv f2) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (cv f2)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cv f2)))))) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex i (wrex j (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) com) com)))))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation at ` (/) ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf00 {x3 y1 i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 (co c0 c0 csat)) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex i (wrex j (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) com) com))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ satf0suc , ~ sat1el2xp and ~ fmlasuc0 . (Contributed by AV, 19-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0suclem {x3 y1 w v u: setvar} (B2 C: class y1 w v u) (U: class w v) (V W: class x3 w v) (X: class w v) (Y: class w) (Z: class v):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X U) (wcel Y V) (wcel Z W)) (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) B2) Y) (wrex w (wceq (cv x3) C) Z)) X))) cvv) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model and the empty binary relation at a successor. (Contributed by AV, 19-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0suc {x3 y1 v u i: setvar} (S N: class i):
  $ wceq S (co c0 c0 csat) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wceq (cfv (csuc N) S) (cun (cfv N S) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) c0) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (cfv N S)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cfv N S)))))) $;

--| An element of a value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model and the empty binary relation expressed as ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 19-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0op {x3: setvar} (S N X: class):
  $ wceq S (co c0 c0 csat) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wb (wcel X (cfv N S)) (wex x3 (wa (wceq X (cop (cv x3) c0)) (wcel (cop (cv x3) c0) (cfv N S))))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model and the empty binary relation does not contain the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 19-Sep-2023.)
theorem satf0n0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wnel c0 (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat))) $;

--| The first component of an element of the value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation is a member of a doubled Cartesian product. (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2023.)
theorem sat1el2xp {w a b: setvar} (N: class a b):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wral w (wex a (wex b (wcel (cfv (cv w) c1st) (cxp com (cxp (cv a) (cv b)))))) (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat))) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height ` N ` is the domain of the value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation at ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmlafv (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (csuc com)) (wceq (cfv N cfmla) (cdm (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat)))) $;

--| The set of all valid Godel formulas. (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmla {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv com cfmla) (ciun n com (cfv (cv n) cfmla)) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height 0 is the set of all formulas of the form v_i ` e. ` v_j ("Godel-set of membership") coded as ` <. (/) , <. i , j >. >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmla0 {x3 i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cfmla) (crab x3 (wrex i (wrex j (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe)) com) com) cvv) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height 0 is the set of all formulas of the form v_i ` e. ` v_j ("Godel-set of membership") coded as ` <. (/) , <. i , j >. >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 15-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmla0xp:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cfmla) (cxp (csn c0) (cxp com com)) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height ` ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 18-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmlasuc0 {x3 v u i: setvar} (N: class i):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wceq (cfv (csuc N) cfmla) (cun (cfv N cfmla) (cab x3 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat))) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) com)) (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat)))))) $;

--| A class is a valid Godel formula of height ` N ` iff it is the first component of a member of the value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the empty model with an empty binary relation at ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmlafvel (F N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wb (wcel F (cfv N cfmla)) (wcel (cop F c0) (cfv N (co c0 c0 csat)))) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height ` ( N + 1 ) ` , expressed by the valid Godel formulas of height ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmlasuc {x3 v u i: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wceq (cfv (csuc N) cfmla) (cun (cfv N cfmla) (cab x3 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna)) (cfv N cfmla)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cv u) (cv i))) com)) (cfv N cfmla))))) $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height 1 is the set of all formulas of the form ` ( a |g b ) ` and ` A.g k a ` with atoms ` a ` , ` b ` of the form ` x e. y ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2023.)
theorem fmla1 {x3 i j k l: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c1o cfmla) (cun (cxp (csn c0) (cxp com com)) (cab x3 (wrex i (wrex j (wrex k (wo (wrex l (wceq (cv x3) (co (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe) (co (cv k) (cv l) cgoe) cgna)) com) (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (co (cv i) (cv j) cgoe) (cv k)))) com) com) com))) $;

--| The characterization of a Godel formula of height at least 1. (Contributed by AV, 14-Oct-2023.)
theorem isfmlasuc {v u i: setvar} (F N: class) (V: class v u i):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N com) (wcel F V)) (wb (wcel F (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) (wo (wcel F (cfv N cfmla)) (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq F (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna)) (cfv N cfmla)) (wrex i (wceq F (cgol (cv u) (cv i))) com)) (cfv N cfmla)))) $;

--| The Godel formulas of height ` N ` are a subset of the Godel formulas of height ` N + 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem fmlasssuc (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wss (cfv N cfmla) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) $;

--| The empty set is not a Godel formula of any height. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem fmlaomn0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wnel c0 (cfv N cfmla)) $;

--| The empty set is not a Godel formula. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem fmlan0:
  $ wnel c0 (cfv com cfmla) $;

--| The "Godel-set of NAND" is a Godel formula of at least height 1. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem gonan0 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wcel (co A2 B2 cgna) (cfv N cfmla)) (wne N c0) $;

--| The "Godel-set of universal quantification" is a Godel formula of at least height 1. (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2023.)
theorem goaln0 (A2: class) (i: setvar) (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cgol A2 (cv i)) (cfv N cfmla)) (wne N c0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gonar (induction step). (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem gonarlem {a b: setvar} (N: class a b):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wi (wi (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) cgna) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) (wa (wcel (cv a) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)))) (wi (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) cgna) (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) cfmla)) (wa (wcel (cv a) (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) cfmla)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) cfmla))))) $;

--| If the "Godel-set of NAND" applied to classes is a Godel formula, the classes are also Godel formulas. Remark: The reverse is not valid for ` A ` or ` B ` being of the same height as the "Godel-set of NAND". (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2023.)
theorem gonar {a b: setvar} (N: class a b):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N com) (wcel (co (cv a) (cv b) cgna) (cfv N cfmla))) (wa (wcel (cv a) (cfv N cfmla)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cfmla))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ goalr (induction step). (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2023.)
theorem goalrlem {i a: setvar} (N: class a):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wi (wi (wcel (cgol (cv a) (cv i)) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla))) (wi (wcel (cgol (cv a) (cv i)) (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) cfmla)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) cfmla)))) $;

--| If the "Godel-set of universal quantification" applied to a class is a Godel formula, the class is also a Godel formula. Remark: The reverse is not valid for ` A ` being of the same height as the "Godel-set of universal quantification". (Contributed by AV, 22-Oct-2023.)
theorem goalr {i a: setvar} (N: class a):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N com) (wcel (cgol (cv a) (cv i)) (cfv N cfmla))) (wcel (cv a) (cfv N cfmla)) $;

--| The set of valid Godel formulas of height 0 is disjoint with the formulas constructed from Godel-sets for the Sheffer stroke NAND and Godel-set of universal quantification. (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem fmla0disjsuc {x3 v u i: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cin (cfv c0 cfmla) (cab x3 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna)) (cfv c0 cfmla)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cv u) (cv i))) com)) (cfv c0 cfmla)))) c0 $;

--| The valid Godel formulas of height ` ( N + 1 ) ` is disjoint with the difference ` ( ( Fmla `` suc suc N ) \ ( Fmla `` suc N ) ) ` , expressed by formulas constructed from Godel-sets for the Sheffer stroke NAND and Godel-set of universal quantification based on the valid Godel formulas of height ` ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2023.)
theorem fmlasucdisj {x3 v u i: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N com) (wceq (cin (cfv (csuc N) cfmla) (cab x3 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna)) (cfv (csuc N) cfmla)) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cv u) (cv i))) com)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) cfmla) (cfv N cfmla))) (wrex u (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) cfmla) (cfv N cfmla))) (cfv N cfmla))))) c0) $;

--| The domain of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in any model ` M ` and any binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` for a natural number ` N ` is the set of valid Godel formulas of height ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfdmfmla (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wceq (cdm (cfv N (co M E csat))) (cfv N cfmla)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ satffunlem1lem1 and ~ satffunlem2lem1 . (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem (x3 y1 w v u: setvar) (M Z: class) (s1 r: setvar):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wfun Z) (wa (wcel (cv s1) Z) (wcel (cv r) Z)) (wa (wcel (cv u) Z) (wcel (cv v) Z))) (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv s1) c1st) (cfv (cv r) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv s1) c2nd) (cfv (cv r) c2nd))))) (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv w) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd)))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ satffunlem1 . (Contributed by AV, 17-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem1lem1 {x3 y1 v u f2 i k: setvar} (E M N: class f2 k):
  $ wi (wfun (cfv N (co M E csat))) (wfun (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cfv N (co M E csat))) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab f2 (wral k (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv k))) (cres (cv f2) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cfv N (co M E csat))))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ satffunlem1 . (Contributed by AV, 23-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem1lem2 {x3 y1 v u f2 i j: setvar} (E M: class j) (V W: class y1 j):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wceq (cin (cdm (cfv c0 (co M E csat))) (cdm (copab x3 y1 (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))))) (cfv c0 (co M E csat))) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) (crab f2 (wral j (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv j))) (cres (cv f2) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)))) com)) (cfv c0 (co M E csat)))))) c0) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ satffunlem2 . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem2lem1 {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 z v u i a) (S: class z a) (E: class x3 y1 z v u i a) (M: class x3 y1 z a) (N: class z a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun (cfv (csuc N) S)) (wss (cfv N S) (cfv (csuc N) S))) (wfun (copab x3 y1 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cfv (csuc N) S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) com)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (wrex u (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (cfv N S))))) $;

--| The domain of an ordered pair class abstraction with three nested restricted existential quantifiers with differences. (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2023.)
theorem dmopab3rexdif {x3 y1 v u i: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class v u i) (S U: class i) (I: class v u) (W X: class x3 y1 v u i):
  $ wi (wa (wral u (wa (wral v (wcel B2 X) U) (wral i (wcel D W) I)) U) (wss S U)) (wceq (cdm (copab x3 y1 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) U) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq (cv y1) D)) I)) (cdif U S)) (wrex u (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) (cdif U S)) S)))) (cab x3 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) A2) U) (wrex i (wceq (cv x3) C) I)) (cdif U S)) (wrex u (wrex v (wceq (cv x3) A2) (cdif U S)) S)))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ satffunlem2 . (Contributed by AV, 27-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem2lem2 {x3 y1 z v u i a: setvar} (A2: class z v u a) (B2: class z v u i a) (S: class z a) (E: class y1 z a) (M: class y1 z) (N: class z a) (V W: class y1 z a):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cdif (co M com cmap) (cin (cfv (cv u) c2nd) (cfv (cv v) c2nd))) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab a (wral z (wcel (cun (csn (cop (cv i) (cv z))) (cres (cv a) (cdif com (csn (cv i))))) (cfv (cv u) c2nd)) M) (co M com cmap)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N com) (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W))) (wfun (cfv (csuc N) S))) (wceq (cin (cdm (cfv (csuc N) S)) (cdm (copab x3 y1 (wo (wrex u (wo (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cfv (csuc N) S)) (wrex i (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cgol (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cv i))) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) com)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (wrex u (wrex v (wa (wceq (cv x3) (co (cfv (cv u) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c1st) cgna)) (wceq (cv y1) A2)) (cdif (cfv (csuc N) S) (cfv N S))) (cfv N S)))))) c0) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ satffun : induction basis. (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem1 (E M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wfun (cfv (csuc c0) (co M E csat))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ satffun : induction step. (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffunlem2 (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N com) (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W))) (wi (wfun (cfv (csuc N) (co M E csat))) (wfun (cfv (csuc (csuc N)) (co M E csat)))) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes at a natural number is a function. (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem satffun (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wfun (cfv N (co M E csat))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2023.)
theorem satff (E M N V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel N com)) (wf (cfv N cfmla) (cpw (co M com cmap)) (cfv N (co M E csat))) $;

--| The satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfun (E M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wf (cfv com cfmla) (cpw (co M com cmap)) (cfv com (co M E csat))) $;

--| An element of the value of the satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the model ` M ` and the binary relation ` E ` on ` M ` at the code ` U ` for a wff using ` e. , -/\ , A. ` is a valuation ` S : _om --> M ` of the variables (v_0 ` = ( S `` (/) ) ` , v_1 ` = ( S `` 1o ) ` , etc.) so that ` U ` is true under the assignment ` S ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2023.)
theorem satfvel (S U E M V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wcel U (cfv com cfmla)) (wcel S (cfv U (cfv com (co M E csat))))) (wf com M S) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate as function over a wff code at ` (/) ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2023.)
theorem satfv0fvfmla0 {a: setvar} (S: class a) (E M: class) (V W: class a) (X: class):
  $ wceq S (co M E csat) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel E W) (wcel X (cfv c0 cfmla))) (wceq (cfv X (cfv c0 S)) (crab a (wbr (cfv (cfv (cfv X c2nd) c1st) (cv a)) (cfv (cfv (cfv X c2nd) c2nd) (cv a)) E) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| The simplified satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes in the model ` M ` at the code ` U ` . (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2023.)
theorem satefv (U M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel U W)) (wceq (co M U csate) (cfv U (cfv com (co M (cin cep (cxp M M)) csat)))) $;

--| The simplified satisfaction predicate for any wff code over an empty model. (Contributed by AV, 6-Oct-2023.) (Revised by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem sate0 (U V: class):
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wceq (co c0 U csate) (cfv U (cfv com (co c0 c0 csat)))) $;

--| The simplified satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes over an empty model. (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2023.)
theorem satef (S U M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel U (cfv com cfmla)) (wcel S (co M U csate))) (wf com M S) $;

--| A simplified satisfaction predicate as function over wff codes over an empty model is an empty set. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2023.)
theorem sate0fv0 (S U: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv com cfmla)) (wi (wcel S (co c0 U csate)) (wceq S c0)) $;

--| The simplified satisfaction predicate for wff codes of height 0. (Contributed by AV, 4-Nov-2023.)
theorem satefvfmla0 {a: setvar} (M V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel X (cfv c0 cfmla))) (wceq (co M X csate) (crab a (wcel (cfv (cfv (cfv X c2nd) c1st) (cv a)) (cfv (cfv (cfv X c2nd) c2nd) (cv a))) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| Characterization of a valuation ` S ` of a simplified satisfaction predicate for a Godel-set of membership. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem sategoelfvb (A2 B2 S E M V: class):
  $ wceq E (co M (co A2 B2 cgoe) csate) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com))) (wb (wcel S E) (wa (wcel S (co M com cmap)) (wcel (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)))) $;

--| Condition of a valuation ` S ` of a simplified satisfaction predicate for a Godel-set of membership: The sets in model ` M ` corresponding to the variables ` A ` and ` B ` under the assignment of ` S ` are in a membership relation in ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem sategoelfv (A2 B2 S E M V: class):
  $ wceq E (co M (co A2 B2 cgoe) csate) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wcel S E)) (wcel (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $;

--| Example of a valuation of a simplified satisfaction predicate for a Godel-set of membership. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem ex_sategoelel {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S E: class x3) (M Z: class):
  $ wceq E (co M (co A2 B2 cgoe) csate) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 com (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) Z (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) (cpw Z) c0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cwun) (wcel Z M)) (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wne A2 B2))) (wcel S E) $;

--| Instance of ~ sategoelfv for the example of a valuation of a simplified satisfaction predicate for a Godel-set of membership. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem ex_sategoel {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (S E: class x3) (M Z: class):
  $ wceq E (co M (co A2 B2 cgoe) csate) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 com (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) Z (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) (cpw Z) c0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cwun) (wcel Z M)) (w3a (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com) (wne A2 B2))) (wcel (cfv A2 S) (cfv B2 S)) $;

--| The value of the satisfaction predicate at two Godel-sets of membership combined with a Godel-set for NAND. (Contributed by AV, 17-Nov-2023.)
theorem satfv1fvfmla1 {a: setvar} (E I J K L M: class) (V W X: class a):
  $ wceq X (co (co I J cgoe) (co K L cgoe) cgna) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M V) (wcel E W)) (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wa (wcel K com) (wcel L com))) (wceq (cfv X (cfv c1o (co M E csat))) (crab a (wo (wn (wbr (cfv I (cv a)) (cfv J (cv a)) E)) (wn (wbr (cfv K (cv a)) (cfv L (cv a)) E))) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| Two Godel-sets of membership combined with a Godel-set for NAND is a Godel formula of height 1. (Contributed by AV, 17-Nov-2023.)
theorem _2goelgoanfmla1 (I J K L X: class):
  $ wceq X (co (co I J cgoe) (co K L cgoe) cgna) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wa (wcel K com) (wcel L com))) (wcel X (cfv c1o cfmla)) $;

--| The simplified satisfaction predicate at two Godel-sets of membership combined with a Godel-set for NAND. (Contributed by AV, 17-Nov-2023.)
theorem satefvfmla1 {a: setvar} (I J K L M V: class) (X: class a):
  $ wceq X (co (co I J cgoe) (co K L cgoe) cgna) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wa (wcel I com) (wcel J com)) (wa (wcel K com) (wcel L com))) (wceq (co M X csate) (crab a (wo (wn (wcel (cfv I (cv a)) (cfv J (cv a)))) (wn (wcel (cfv K (cv a)) (cfv L (cv a))))) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| Example of a valuation of a simplified satisfaction predicate over the ordinal numbers as model for a Godel-set of membership using the properties of a successor: ` ( S `` 2o ) = Z e. suc Z = ( S `` 2o ) ` . Remark: the indices ` 1o ` and ` 2o ` are intentionally reversed to distinguish them from elements of the model: ` ( 2o e.g 1o ) ` should not be confused with ` 2o e. 1o ` , which is false. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem ex_sategoelelomsuc {x3: setvar} (S: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 com (cif (wceq (cv x3) c2o) Z (csuc Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Z com) (wcel S (co com (co c2o c1o cgoe) csate)) $;

--| Example of a valuation of a simplified satisfaction predicate over a proper pair (of ordinal numbers) as model for a Godel-set of membership using the properties of a successor: ` ( S `` 2o ) = 1o e. 2o = ( S `` 2o ) ` . Remark: the indices ` 1o ` and ` 2o ` are intentionally reversed to distinguish them from elements of the model: ` ( 2o e.g 1o ) ` should not be confused with ` 2o e. 1o ` , which is false. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem ex_sategoelel12 {x3: setvar} (S: class x3):
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 com (cif (wceq (cv x3) c2o) c1o c2o)) $ >
  $ wcel S (co (cpr c1o c2o) (co c2o c1o cgoe) csate) $;

--| The "proves" relation on a set. A wff encoded as ` U ` is true in a model ` M ` iff for every valuation ` s e. ( M ^m _om ) ` , the interpretation of the wff using the membership relation on ` M ` is true. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem prv (U M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel U W)) (wb (wbr M U cprv) (wceq (co M U csate) (co M com cmap))) $;

--| The wff ` ( A e. B -/\ B e. A ) ` encoded as ` ( ( A e.g B ) ` ` |g ( B e.g A ) ) ` is true in any model ` M ` . This is the model theoretic proof of ~ elnanel . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2023.)
theorem elnanelprv (A2 B2 M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M V) (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wbr M (co (co A2 B2 cgoe) (co B2 A2 cgoe) cgna) cprv) $;

--| Every wff encoded as ` U ` is true in an "empty model" ( ` M = (/) ` ). Since ` |= ` is defined in terms of the interpretations making the given formula true, it is not defined on the "empty model", since there are no interpretations. In particular, the empty set on the LHS of ` |= ` should not be interpreted as the empty model, because ` E. x x = x ` is not satisfied on the empty model. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem prv0 (U: class):
  $ wi (wcel U (cfv com cfmla)) (wbr c0 U cprv) $;

--| No wff encoded as a Godel-set of membership is true in a model with only one element. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2023.)
theorem prv1n (I J V X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I com) (wcel J com) (wcel X V)) (wn (wbr (csn X) (co I J cgoe) cprv)) $;

--| The Godel-set of negation. (Note that this is not a wff.)
term cgon (U: class): class;

--| The Godel-set of conjunction.
term cgoa: class;

--| The Godel-set of implication.
term cgoi: class;

--| The Godel-set of disjunction.
term cgoo: class;

--| The Godel-set of equivalence.
term cgob: class;

--| The Godel-set of equality.
term cgoq: class;

--| The Godel-set of existential quantification. (Note that this is not a wff.)
term cgox (U N: class): class;

--| Define the Godel-set of negation. Here the argument ` U ` is also a Godel-set corresponding to smaller formulas. Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gonot (U: class):
  $ wceq (cgon U) (co U U cgna) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of conjunction. Here the arguments ` U ` and ` V ` are also Godel-sets corresponding to smaller formulas. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goan {v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgoa (cmpo u v cvv cvv (cgon (co (cv u) (cv v) cgna))) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of implication. Here the arguments ` U ` and ` V ` are also Godel-sets corresponding to smaller formulas. Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goim {v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgoi (cmpo u v cvv cvv (co (cv u) (cgon (cv v)) cgna)) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of disjunction. Here the arguments ` U ` and ` V ` are also Godel-sets corresponding to smaller formulas. Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goor {v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgoo (cmpo u v cvv cvv (co (cgon (cv u)) (cv v) cgoi)) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of equivalence. Here the arguments ` U ` and ` V ` are also Godel-sets corresponding to smaller formulas. Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gobi {v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgob (cmpo u v cvv cvv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) cgoi) (co (cv v) (cv u) cgoi) cgoa)) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of equality. Here the arguments ` x = <. N , P >. ` correspond to v_N and v_P , so ` ( (/) =g 1o ) ` actually means v_0 ` = ` v_1 , not ` 0 = 1 ` . Here we use the trick mentioned in ~ ax-ext to introduce equality as a defined notion in terms of ` e.g ` . The expression ` suc ( u u. v ) = ` max ` ( u , v ) + 1 ` here is a convenient way of getting a dummy variable distinct from ` u ` and ` v ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goeq {w v u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgoq (cmpo u v com com (csb w (csuc (cun (cv u) (cv v))) (cgol (co (co (cv w) (cv u) cgoe) (co (cv w) (cv v) cgoe) cgob) (cv w)))) $;

--| Define the Godel-set of existential quantification. Here ` N e. _om ` corresponds to v_N , and ` U ` represents another formula, and this expression is ` [ E. x ph ] = E.g N U ` where ` x ` is the ` N ` -th variable, ` U = [ ph ] ` is the code for ` ph ` . Note that this is a _class_ expression, not a wff. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_goex (U N: class):
  $ wceq (cgox U N) (cgon (cgol (cgon U) N)) $;

--| The Axiom of Extensionality.
term cgze: class;

--| The Axiom Scheme of Replacement.
term cgzr: class;

--| The Axiom of Power Sets.
term cgzp: class;

--| The Axiom of Unions.
term cgzu: class;

--| The Axiom of Regularity.
term cgzg: class;

--| The Axiom of Infinity.
term cgzi: class;

--| The set of models of ZF.
term cgzf: class;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom of Extensionality. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzext:
  $ wceq cgze (co (cgol (co (co c2o c0 cgoe) (co c2o c1o cgoe) cgob) c2o) (co c0 c1o cgoq) cgoi) $;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom Scheme of Replacement. Since this is a scheme and not a single axiom, it manifests as a function on wffs, each giving rise to a different axiom. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzrep {u: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgzr (cmpt u (cfv com cfmla) (co (cgol (cgox (cgol (co (cgol (cv u) c1o) (co c2o c1o cgoq) cgoi) c2o) c1o) c3o) (cgol (cgol (co (co c2o c1o cgoe) (cgox (co (co c3o c0 cgoe) (cgol (cv u) c1o) cgoa) c3o) cgob) c2o) c1o) cgoi)) $;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom of Power Sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzpow:
  $ wceq cgzp (cgox (cgol (co (cgol (co (co c1o c2o cgoe) (co c1o c0 cgoe) cgob) c1o) (co c2o c1o cgoe) cgoi) c2o) c1o) $;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom of Unions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzun:
  $ wceq cgzu (cgox (cgol (co (cgox (co (co c2o c1o cgoe) (co c1o c0 cgoe) cgoa) c1o) (co c2o c1o cgoe) cgoi) c2o) c1o) $;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom of Regularity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzreg:
  $ wceq cgzg (co (cgox (co c1o c0 cgoe) c1o) (cgox (co (co c1o c0 cgoe) (cgol (co (co c2o c1o cgoe) (cgon (co c2o c0 cgoe)) cgoi) c2o) cgoa) c1o) cgoi) $;

--| The Godel-set version of the Axiom of Infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzinf:
  $ wceq cgzi (cgox (co (co c0 c1o cgoe) (cgol (co (co c2o c1o cgoe) (cgox (co (co c2o c0 cgoe) (co c0 c1o cgoe) cgoa) c0) cgoi) c2o) cgoa) c1o) $;

--| Define the class of all (transitive) models of ZF. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2013.)
axiom df_gzf {u m: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgzf (cab m (w3a (w3a (wtr (cv m)) (wbr (cv m) cgze cprv) (wbr (cv m) cgzp cprv)) (w3a (wbr (cv m) cgzu cprv) (wbr (cv m) cgzg cprv) (wbr (cv m) cgzi cprv)) (wral u (wbr (cv m) (cfv (cv u) cgzr) cprv) (cfv com cfmla)))) $;

--| The set of constants.
term cmcn: class;

--| The set of variables.
term cmvar: class;

--| The type function.
term cmty: class;

--| The set of variable typecodes.
term cmvt: class;

--| The set of typecodes.
term cmtc: class;

--| The set of axioms.
term cmax: class;

--| The set of raw expressions.
term cmrex: class;

--| The set of expressions.
term cmex: class;

--| The set of distinct variables.
term cmdv: class;

--| The variables in an expression.
term cmvrs: class;

--| The set of raw substitutions.
term cmrsub: class;

--| The set of substitutions.
term cmsub: class;

--| The set of variable hypotheses.
term cmvh: class;

--| The set of pre-statements.
term cmpst: class;

--| The reduct of a pre-statement.
term cmsr: class;

--| The set of statements.
term cmsta: class;

--| The set of formal systems.
term cmfs: class;

--| The closure of a set of statements.
term cmcls: class;

--| The set of provable pre-statements.
term cmpps: class;

--| The set of theorems.
term cmthm: class;

--| Define the set of constants in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mcn:
  $ wceq cmcn (cslot c1) $;

--| Define the set of variables in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvar:
  $ wceq cmvar (cslot c2) $;

--| Define the type function in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mty:
  $ wceq cmty (cslot c3) $;

--| Define the set of typecodes in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mtc:
  $ wceq cmtc (cslot c4) $;

--| Define the set of axioms in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mmax:
  $ wceq cmax (cslot c5) $;

--| Define the set of variable typecodes in a Metamath formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvt {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmvt (cmpt t cvv (crn (cfv (cv t) cmty))) $;

--| Define the set of "raw expressions", which are expressions without a typecode attached. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mrex {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmrex (cmpt t cvv (cword (cun (cfv (cv t) cmcn) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)))) $;

--| Define the set of expressions, which are strings of constants and variables headed by a typecode constant. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mex {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmex (cmpt t cvv (cxp (cfv (cv t) cmtc) (cfv (cv t) cmrex))) $;

--| Define the set of distinct variable conditions, which are pairs of distinct variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mdv {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmdv (cmpt t cvv (cdif (cxp (cfv (cv t) cmvar) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) cid)) $;

--| Define the set of variables in an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvrs {t e: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmvrs (cmpt t cvv (cmpt e (cfv (cv t) cmex) (cin (crn (cfv (cv e) c2nd)) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)))) $;

--| Define a substitution of raw expressions given a mapping from variables to expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mrsub {v t e f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmrsub (cmpt t cvv (cmpt f2 (co (cfv (cv t) cmrex) (cfv (cv t) cmvar) cpm) (cmpt e (cfv (cv t) cmrex) (co (cfv (cun (cfv (cv t) cmcn) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) cfrmd) (ccom (cmpt v (cun (cfv (cv t) cmcn) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) (cif (wcel (cv v) (cdm (cv f2))) (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) (cs1 (cv v)))) (cv e)) cgsu)))) $;

--| Define a substitution of expressions given a mapping from variables to expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msub {t e f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmsub (cmpt t cvv (cmpt f2 (co (cfv (cv t) cmrex) (cfv (cv t) cmvar) cpm) (cmpt e (cfv (cv t) cmex) (cop (cfv (cv e) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv e) c2nd) (cfv (cv f2) (cfv (cv t) cmrsub))))))) $;

--| Define the mapping from variables to their variable hypothesis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvh {v t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmvh (cmpt t cvv (cmpt v (cfv (cv t) cmvar) (cop (cfv (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmty)) (cs1 (cv v))))) $;

--| Define the set of all pre-statements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mpst {t d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmpst (cmpt t cvv (cxp (cxp (crab d (wceq (ccnv (cv d)) (cv d)) (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmdv))) (cin (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmex)) cfn)) (cfv (cv t) cmex))) $;

--| Define the reduct of a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msr {z t h s1 a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmsr (cmpt t cvv (cmpt s1 (cfv (cv t) cmpst) (csb h (cfv (cfv (cv s1) c1st) c2nd) (csb a (cfv (cv s1) c2nd) (cotp (cin (cfv (cfv (cv s1) c1st) c1st) (csb z (cuni (cima (cfv (cv t) cmvrs) (cun (cv h) (csn (cv a))))) (cxp (cv z) (cv z)))) (cv h) (cv a)))))) $;

--| Define the set of all statements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msta {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmsta (cmpt t cvv (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsr))) $;

--| Define the set of all formal systems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mfs {v t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmfs (cab t (wa (wa (wceq (cin (cfv (cv t) cmcn) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) c0) (wf (cfv (cv t) cmvar) (cfv (cv t) cmtc) (cfv (cv t) cmty))) (wa (wss (cfv (cv t) cmax) (cfv (cv t) cmsta)) (wral v (wn (wcel (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv t) cmty)) (csn (cv v))) cfn)) (cfv (cv t) cmvt))))) $;

--| Define the closure of a set of statements relative to a set of disjointness constraints. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mcls {x3 y1 t h m o s1 p c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmcls (cmpt t cvv (cmpo d h (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmdv)) (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmex)) (cint (cab c (wa (wss (cun (cv h) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmvh))) (cv c)) (wal m (wal o (wal p (wi (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wral s1 (wi (wa (wss (cima (cv s1) (cun (cv o) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmvh)))) (cv c)) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs))) (cv d)))))) (wcel (cfv (cv p) (cv s1)) (cv c))) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsub)))))))))))) $;

--| Define the set of provable pre-statements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mpps {t h a d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmpps (cmpt t cvv (coprab d h a (wa (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) (cfv (cv t) cmpst)) (wcel (cv a) (co (cv d) (cv h) (cfv (cv t) cmcls)))))) $;

--| Define the set of theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mthm {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmthm (cmpt t cvv (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv t) cmsr)) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmsr) (cfv (cv t) cmpps)))) $;

--| The set of variable typecodes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvtval (T V Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvt) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wceq V (crn Y) $;

--| The set of "raw expressions", which are expressions without a typecode, that is, just sequences of constants and variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrexval (C R T V W: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wceq R (cword (cun C V))) $;

--| The set of expressions, which are pairs whose first element is a typecode, and whose second element is a raw expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mexval (R T E K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv T cmtc) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq E (cxp K R) $;

--| The set of expressions, which are pairs whose first element is a typecode, and whose second element is a list of constants and variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mexval2 (C T E K V: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv T cmtc) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq E (cxp K (cword (cun C V))) $;

--| The set of disjoint variable conditions, which are pairs of distinct variables. (This definition differs from appendix C, which uses unordered pairs instead. We use ordered pairs, but all sets of disjoint variable conditions of interest will be symmetric, so it does not matter.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mdvval (D T V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif (cxp V V) cid) $;

--| The set of variables in an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvrsval (T E V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X E) (wceq (cfv X W) (cin (crn (cfv X c2nd)) V)) $;

--| The set of variables in an expression is a finite subset of ` V ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvrsfpw (T E V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X E) (wcel (cfv X W) (cin (cpw V) cfn)) $;

--| The substitution of some variables for expressions in a raw expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubffval {v e f2: setvar} (C R: class) (S: class v e f2) (T: class) (G: class v) (V: class) (W: class v e f2):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cun C V) cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wceq S (cmpt f2 (co R V cpm) (cmpt e R (co G (ccom (cmpt v (cun C V) (cif (wcel (cv v) (cdm (cv f2))) (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) (cs1 (cv v)))) (cv e)) cgsu)))) $;

--| The substitution of some variables for expressions in a raw expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubfval {v e: setvar} (A2 C R: class) (S: class v e) (T F: class) (G: class v) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cun C V) cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V)) (wceq (cfv F S) (cmpt e R (co G (ccom (cmpt v (cun C V) (cif (wcel (cv v) A2) (cfv (cv v) F) (cs1 (cv v)))) (cv e)) cgsu))) $;

--| The substitution of some variables for expressions in a raw expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubval {v: setvar} (A2 C R: class) (S: class v) (T F: class) (G: class v) (V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv (cun C V) cfrmd) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X R)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv F S)) (co G (ccom (cmpt v (cun C V) (cif (wcel (cv v) A2) (cfv (cv v) F) (cs1 (cv v)))) X) cgsu)) $;

--| The value of a substituted singleton. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubcv (A2 C R S T F V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X (cun C V))) (wceq (cfv (cs1 X) (cfv F S)) (cif (wcel X A2) (cfv X F) (cs1 X))) $;

--| The value of a substituted variable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubvr (A2 R S T F V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cfv (cs1 X) (cfv F S)) (cfv X F)) $;

--| A substitution is a function from ` R ` to ` R ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubff (R S T V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wf (co R V cpm) (co R R cmap) S) $;

--| Although it is defined for partial mappings of variables, every partial substitution is a substitution on some complete mapping of the variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubrn (R S T V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq (crn S) (cima S (co R V cmap)) $;

--| When restricted to complete mappings, the substitution-producing function is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubff1 (R S T V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wf1 (co R V cmap) (co R R cmap) (cres S (co R V cmap))) $;

--| When restricted to complete mappings, the substitution-producing function is bijective to the set of all substitutions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubff1o (R S T V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wf1o (co R V cmap) (crn S) (cres S (co R V cmap))) $;

--| The value of the substituted empty string. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsub0 (S T F: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (crn S)) (wceq (cfv c0 F) c0) $;

--| A substitution is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubf (R S T F: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (crn S)) (wf R R F) $;

--| Substitution distributes over concatenation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubccat (R S T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel X R) (wcel Y R)) (wceq (cfv (co X Y cconcat) F) (co (cfv X F) (cfv Y F) cconcat)) $;

--| A substitution does not change the value of constant substrings. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubcn (C R S T F V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel X (cdif C V))) (wceq (cfv (cs1 X) F) (cs1 X)) $;

--| Characterization of the substitutions as functions from expressions to expressions that distribute under concatenation and map constants to themselves. (The constant part uses ` ( C \ V ) ` because we don't know that ` C ` and ` V ` are disjoint until we get to ~ ismfs .) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmrsubrn {x3 y1 c: setvar} (C: class) (R: class c) (S: class) (T: class c) (F V: class) (W: class c):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wb (wcel F (crn S)) (w3a (wf R R F) (wral c (wceq (cfv (cs1 (cv c)) F) (cs1 (cv c))) (cdif C V)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cconcat) F) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cconcat)) R) R))) $;

--| The composition of two substitutions is a substitution. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubco (S T F G: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel G (crn S))) (wcel (ccom F G) (crn S)) $;

--| The set of variables in a substitution is the union, indexed by the variables in the original expression, of the variables in the substitution to that variable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mrsubvrs {x3: setvar} (R: class x3) (S T F V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel X R)) (wceq (cin (crn (cfv X F)) V) (ciun x3 (cin (crn X) V) (cin (crn (cfv (cs1 (cv x3)) F)) V))) $;

--| A substitution applied to an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubffval {e f2: setvar} (R: class) (S: class e f2) (T E O V: class) (W: class e f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wceq S (cmpt f2 (co R V cpm) (cmpt e E (cop (cfv (cv e) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv e) c2nd) (cfv (cv f2) O)))))) $;

--| A substitution applied to an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubfval {e: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class e) (T E F O V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V)) (wceq (cfv F S) (cmpt e E (cop (cfv (cv e) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv e) c2nd) (cfv F O))))) $;

--| A substitution applied to an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubval (A2 R S T E F O V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X E)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv F S)) (cop (cfv X c1st) (cfv (cfv X c2nd) (cfv F O)))) $;

--| A substitution applied to an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubrsub (A2 R S T E F O V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X E)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X (cfv F S)) c2nd) (cfv (cfv X c2nd) (cfv F O))) $;

--| The type of a substituted expression is the same as the original type. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubty (A2 R S T E F V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 R F) (wss A2 V) (wcel X E)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X (cfv F S)) c1st) (cfv X c1st)) $;

--| Characterization of substitution in terms of raw substitution, without reference to the generating functions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmsubrn {e f2: setvar} (S: class e f2) (T: class f2) (E O: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv T cmrsub) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq (crn S) (crn (cmpt f2 (crn O) (cmpt e E (cop (cfv (cv e) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv e) c2nd) (cv f2)))))) $;

--| Although it is defined for partial mappings of variables, every partial substitution is a substitution on some complete mapping of the variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubrn (R S T V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq (crn S) (cima S (co R V cmap)) $;

--| A substitution is a function from ` E ` to ` E ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubff (R S T E V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wf (co R V cpm) (co E E cmap) S) $;

--| The composition of two substitutions is a substitution. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubco (S T F G: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel G (crn S))) (wcel (ccom F G) (crn S)) $;

--| A substitution is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubf (S T E F: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (crn S)) (wf E E F) $;

--| Value of the function mapping variables to their corresponding variable expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvhfval {v: setvar} (T: class) (H: class v) (V Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt v V (cop (cfv (cv v) Y) (cs1 (cv v)))) $;

--| Value of the function mapping variables to their corresponding variable expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvhval (T H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X H) (cop (cfv X Y) (cs1 X))) $;

--| A pre-statement is an ordered triple, whose first member is a symmetric set of disjoint variable conditions, whose second member is a finite set of expressions, and whose third member is an expression. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mpstval {d: setvar} (P: class d) (T: class) (E: class d) (V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq P (cxp (cxp (crab d (wceq (ccnv (cv d)) (cv d)) (cpw V)) (cin (cpw E) cfn)) E) $;

--| Property of being a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmpst (A2 D P T E H V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cotp D H A2) P) (w3a (wa (wss D V) (wceq (ccnv D) D)) (wa (wss H E) (wcel H cfn)) (wcel A2 E)) $;

--| Value of the reduct of a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrfval {z h s1 a: setvar} (P: class) (R: class z h s1 a) (T: class z) (V: class h s1 a):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt s1 P (csb h (cfv (cfv (cv s1) c1st) c2nd) (csb a (cfv (cv s1) c2nd) (cotp (cin (cfv (cfv (cv s1) c1st) c1st) (csb z (cuni (cima V (cun (cv h) (csn (cv a))))) (cxp (cv z) (cv z)))) (cv h) (cv a))))) $;

--| Value of the reduct of a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrval (A2 D P R T H V Z: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cuni (cima V (cun H (csn A2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cotp D H A2) P) (wceq (cfv (cotp D H A2) R) (cotp (cin D (cxp Z Z)) H A2)) $;

--| A pre-statement is an ordered triple. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mpstssv (P T: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wss P (cxp (cxp cvv cvv) cvv) $;

--| Decompose a pre-statement into a triple of values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mpst123 (P T X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X P) (wceq X (cotp (cfv (cfv X c1st) c1st) (cfv (cfv X c1st) c2nd) (cfv X c2nd))) $;

--| The elements of a pre-statement are sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mpstrcl (A2 D P T H: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cotp D H A2) P) (w3a (wcel D cvv) (wcel H cvv) (wcel A2 cvv)) $;

--| The reduct of a pre-statement is a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrf (P R T: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wf P P R $;

--| If ` X ` and ` Y ` have the same reduct, then one is a pre-statement iff the other is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrrcl (P R T X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv X R) (cfv Y R)) (wb (wcel X P) (wcel Y P)) $;

--| Value of the set of statements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mstaval (R S T: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wceq S (crn R) $;

--| The reduct of a statement is itself. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrid (R S T X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X S) (wceq (cfv X R) X) $;

--| The reduct of a pre-statement is a statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msrfo (P R S T: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wfo P S R $;

--| A statement is a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mstapst (P S T: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wss S P $;

--| Property of being a statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmsta (A2 D P S T H V Z: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cuni (cima V (cun H (csn A2)))) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cotp D H A2) S) (wa (wcel (cotp D H A2) P) (wss D (cxp Z Z))) $;

--| A formal system is a tuple ` <. mCN , mVR , mType , mVT , mTC , mAx >. ` such that: ` mCN ` and ` mVR ` are disjoint; ` mType ` is a function from ` mVR ` to ` mVT ` ; ` mVT ` is a subset of ` mTC ` ; ` mAx ` is a set of statements; and for each variable typecode, there are infinitely many variables of that type. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem ismfs {v: setvar} (A2 C S: class v) (T F: class) (K V W Y: class v):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv T cmvt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv T cmtc) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T W) (wb (wcel T cmfs) (wa (wa (wceq (cin C V) c0) (wf V K Y)) (wa (wss A2 S) (wral v (wn (wcel (cima (ccnv Y) (csn (cv v))) cfn)) F)))) $;

--| The constants and variables of a formal system are disjoint. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mfsdisj (C T V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wceq (cin C V) c0) $;

--| The type function maps variables to typecodes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mtyf2 (T K V Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv T cmtc) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf V K Y) $;

--| The type function maps variables to variable typecodes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mtyf (T F V Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv T cmvt) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf V F Y) $;

--| The set of variable typecodes is a subset of all typecodes. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvtss (T F K: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv T cmvt) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv T cmtc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wss F K) $;

--| An axiom is a statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem maxsta (A2 S T: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wss A2 S) $;

--| Each variable typecode has infinitely many variables. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvtinf (T F X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cfv T cmvt) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv T cmty) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T cmfs) (wcel X F)) (wn (wcel (cima (ccnv Y) (csn X)) cfn)) $;

--| When restricted to complete mappings, the substitution-producing function is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubff1 (R S T E V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf1 (co R V cmap) (co E E cmap) (cres S (co R V cmap))) $;

--| When restricted to complete mappings, the substitution-producing function is bijective to the set of all substitutions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubff1o (R S T V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmrex) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf1o (co R V cmap) (crn S) (cres S (co R V cmap))) $;

--| The function mapping variables to variable expressions is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvhf (T E H V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf V E H) $;

--| The function mapping variables to variable expressions is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mvhf1 (T E H V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wf1 V E H) $;

--| The set of variables in a substitution is the union, indexed by the variables in the original expression, of the variables in the substitution to that variable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem msubvrs {x3: setvar} (S: class x3) (T E F: class) (H: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel T cmfs) (wcel F (crn S)) (wcel X E)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X F) V) (ciun x3 (cfv X V) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) F) V))) $;

--| Reverse closure for the closure function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsrcl (A2 B2 C D T E K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co K B2 C)) (w3a (wcel T cvv) (wss K D) (wss B2 E)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mclsssv . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsssvlem {x3 y1 m o s1 p c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class) (C: class y1 c) (D: class x3 y1 m o s1 p c) (S: class m o p) (T: class) (E: class x3 y1) (H: class y1) (K: class) (V: class y1 m o s1 p):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cint (cab c (wa (wss (cun B2 (crn H)) (cv c)) (wal m (wal o (wal p (wi (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) A2) (wral s1 (wi (wa (wss (cima (cv s1) (cun (cv o) (crn H))) (cv c)) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) (cv s1)) V) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) (cv s1)) V)) K))))) (wcel (cfv (cv p) (cv s1)) (cv c))) (crn S))))))))) E) $;

--| The function mapping variables to variable expressions is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsval {x3 y1 m o s1 p c: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class) (C: class y1 c) (D: class x3 y1 m o s1 p c) (S: class m o p) (T: class) (E: class x3 y1) (H: class y1) (K: class) (V: class y1 m o s1 p):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co K B2 C) (cint (cab c (wa (wss (cun B2 (crn H)) (cv c)) (wal m (wal o (wal p (wi (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) A2) (wral s1 (wi (wa (wss (cima (cv s1) (cun (cv o) (crn H))) (cv c)) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) (cv s1)) V) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) (cv s1)) V)) K))))) (wcel (cfv (cv p) (cv s1)) (cv c))) (crn S)))))))))) $;

--| The closure of a set of expressions is a set of expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsssv (ph: wff) (B2 C D T E K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co K B2 C) E) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ssmcls . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem ssmclslem (ph: wff) (B2 C D T E H K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun B2 (crn H)) (co K B2 C)) $;

--| All variable hypotheses are in the closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem vhmcls (ph: wff) (B2 C D T E H K V X: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X H) (co K B2 C)) $;

--| The original expressions are also in the closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem ssmcls (ph: wff) (B2 C D T E K: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (co K B2 C)) $;

--| The closure is monotonic under subsets of the original set of expressions and the set of disjoint variable conditions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem ss2mcls (ph: wff) (B2 C D T E K X Y: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co X Y C) (co K B2 C)) $;

--| The closure is closed under axiom application. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsax {x3 y1 v a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1 v a b) (B2: class a b) (C: class y1 a b) (D: class x3 y1 v a b) (P: class v a b) (S: class) (T: class v a b) (E: class x3 y1 a b) (H: class y1) (K: class) (L: class v a b) (M: class v) (O: class v a b) (V: class y1 a b) (W: class y1 v):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp M O P) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (crn L)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) O)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) V)) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv v) H) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) M) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) S) W)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) S) W)))) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv P S) (co K B2 C)) $;

--| Induction theorem for closure: any other set ` Q ` closed under the axioms and the hypotheses contains all the elements of the closure. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsind {x3 y1 v m o s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1 v) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (D: class x3 y1 v m o s1 p) (Q: class x3 y1) (T: class v) (E: class x3 y1) (H: class y1) (K: class) (L: class v) (V: class y1 m o s1 p) (W: class y1 v):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv T cmax) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 Q) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) V)) (wcel (cfv (cv v) H) Q) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (w3a (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) A2) (wcel (cv s1) (crn L)) (wss (cima (cv s1) (cun (cv o) (crn H))) Q)) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) (cv s1)) W) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) (cv s1)) W)) K))))) (wcel (cfv (cv p) (cv s1)) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co K B2 C) Q) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mppspst . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mppspstlem {h a d: setvar} (C P T: class) (J: class h a d):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wss (coprab d h a (wa (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) P) (wcel (cv a) (co (cv d) (cv h) C)))) P $;

--| Definition of a provable pre-statement, essentially just a reorganization of the arguments of df-mcls . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mppsval {h a d: setvar} (C P T: class) (J: class h a d):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wceq J (coprab d h a (wa (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) P) (wcel (cv a) (co (cv d) (cv h) C)))) $;

--| Definition of a provable pre-statement, essentially just a reorganization of the arguments of df-mcls . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmpps (A2 C D P T H J: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cotp D H A2) J) (wa (wcel (cotp D H A2) P) (wcel A2 (co D H C))) $;

--| A provable pre-statement is a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mppspst (P T J: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wss J P $;

--| A theorem is a pre-statement, whose reduct is also the reduct of a provable pre-statement. Unlike the difference between pre-statement and statement, this application of the reduct is not necessarily trivial: there are theorems that are not themselves provable but are provable once enough "dummy variables" are introduced. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmval (R T U J: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wceq U (cima (ccnv R) (cima R J)) $;

--| A theorem is a pre-statement, whose reduct is also the reduct of a provable pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem elmthm {x3: setvar} (R T: class) (U: class x3) (J X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X U) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) R) (cfv X R)) J) $;

--| A statement whose reduct is the reduct of a provable pre-statement is a theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmi (R T U J X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X J) (wceq (cfv X R) (cfv Y R))) (wcel Y U) $;

--| A theorem is a pre-statement. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmsta (S T U: class):
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv T cmpst) $ >
  $ wss U S $;

--| A provable pre-statement is a theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mppsthm (T U J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wss J U $;

--| Lemma for ~ mthmb . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmblem (R T U X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv X R) (cfv Y R)) (wi (wcel X U) (wcel Y U)) $;

--| If two statements have the same reduct then one is a theorem iff the other is. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmb (R T U X Y: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv X R) (cfv Y R)) (wb (wcel X U) (wcel Y U)) $;

--| Given a theorem, there is an explicitly definable witnessing provable pre-statement for the provability of the theorem. (However, this pre-statement requires infinitely many disjoint variable conditions, which is sometimes inconvenient.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mthmpps (A2 C D R T U H J M V Z: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv T cmsr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv T cmthm) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cuni (cima V (cun H (csn A2)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cun C (cdif D (cxp Z Z))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T cmfs) (wb (wcel (cotp C H A2) U) (wa (wcel (cotp M H A2) J) (wceq (cfv (cotp M H A2) R) (cfv (cotp C H A2) R)))) $;

--| The closure is closed under application of provable pre-statements. (Compare ~ mclsax .) This theorem is what justifies the treatment of theorems as "equivalent" to axioms once they have been proven: the composition of one theorem in the proof of another yields a theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclsppslem {x3 y1 z w v m o s1 p a b: setvar} (ph: wff z w m o s1 p) (B2: class z w) (C: class w) (D P: class x3 y1 z w v m o s1 p a b) (S: class z w) (T: class) (E: class x3 y1 z w a b) (H: class) (J: class x3 y1 z w v m o s1 p a b) (K: class z w) (L M: class) (O: class y1 a b) (V: class x3 y1 w m o s1 p a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp M O P) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (crn L)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) O)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) V)) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv v) H) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) M) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) S) W)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) S) W)))) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) (cfv T cmax)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv s1) (crn L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima (cv s1) (cun (cv o) (crn H))) (cima (ccnv S) (co K B2 C))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal z (wal w (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv w) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv z) H) (cv s1)) W) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv w) H) (cv s1)) W)) M)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cv p) (cv s1)) (cima (ccnv S) (co K B2 C))) $;

--| The closure is closed under application of provable pre-statements. (Compare ~ mclsax .) This theorem is what justifies the treatment of theorems as "equivalent" to axioms once they have been proven: the composition of one theorem in the proof of another yields a theorem. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2016.)
theorem mclspps {x3 y1 v a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (D P: class x3 y1 v a b) (S T: class) (E: class x3 y1 a b) (H: class) (J: class x3 y1 v a b) (K L M: class) (O: class y1 a b) (V: class x3 y1 a b) (W: class):
  $ wceq D (cfv T cmdv) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv T cmex) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv T cmcls) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cmfs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss K D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 E) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv T cmpps) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv T cmsub) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv T cmvar) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv T cmvh) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv T cmvrs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cotp M O P) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (crn L)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) O)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv v) V)) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cv v) H) S) (co K B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) M) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) H) S) W)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) H) S) W)))) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv P S) (co K B2 C)) $;

--| Mapping expressions to statements.
term cm0s: class;

--| The set of syntax axioms.
term cmsa: class;

--| The set of weakly grammatical formal systems.
term cmwgfs: class;

--| The syntax typecode function.
term cmsy: class;

--| The syntax typecode function for expressions.
term cmesy: class;

--| The set of grammatical formal systems.
term cmgfs: class;

--| The set of proof trees.
term cmtree: class;

--| The set of syntax trees.
term cmst: class;

--| The indexing set for a syntax axiom.
term cmsax: class;

--| The set of unambiguous formal systems.
term cmufs: class;

--| Define a function mapping expressions to statements. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_m0s {a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cm0s (cmpt a cvv (cotp c0 c0 (cv a))) $;

--| Define the set of syntax axioms. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msa {t a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmsa (cmpt t cvv (crab a (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv a) cm0s) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wcel (cfv (cv a) c1st) (cfv (cv t) cmvt)) (wfun (cres (ccnv (cfv (cv a) c2nd)) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)))) (cfv (cv t) cmex))) $;

--| Define the set of weakly grammatical formal systems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mwgfs {t h s1 a d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmwgfs (crab t (wal d (wal h (wal a (wi (wa (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wcel (cfv (cv a) c1st) (cfv (cv t) cmvt))) (wrex s1 (wcel (cv a) (cima (cv s1) (cfv (cv t) cmsa))) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsub))))))) cmfs) $;

--| Define the syntax typecode function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msyn:
  $ wceq cmsy (cslot c6) $;

--| Define the syntax typecode function for expressions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2023.)
axiom df_mesyn {t e c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmesy (cmpt t cvv (cmpo c e (cfv (cv t) cmtc) (cfv (cv t) cmrex) (co (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv t) cmsy)) (cv e) cm0s))) $;

--| Define the set of grammatical formal systems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Jun-2023.)
axiom df_mgfs {t e h a c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmgfs (crab t (w3a (wf (cfv (cv t) cmtc) (cfv (cv t) cmvt) (cfv (cv t) cmsy)) (wral c (wceq (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv t) cmsy)) (cv c)) (cfv (cv t) cmvt)) (wal d (wal h (wal a (wi (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wral e (wcel (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmesy)) (cfv (cv t) cmpps)) (cun (cv h) (csn (cv a))))))))) cmwgfs) $;

--| Define the set of proof trees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mtree {x3 y1 t e h m o s1 r p d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmtree (cmpt t cvv (cmpo d h (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmdv)) (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmex)) (cint (cab r (w3a (wral e (wbr (cv e) (cop (cfv (cv e) cm0s) c0) (cv r)) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmvh))) (wral e (wbr (cv e) (cop (cfv (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv e)) (cfv (cv t) cmsr)) c0) (cv r)) (cv h)) (wal m (wal o (wal p (wi (wcel (cotp (cv m) (cv o) (cv p)) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wral s1 (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv m)) (wss (cxp (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs))) (cv d))))) (wss (cxp (csn (cfv (cv p) (cv s1))) (cixp e (cun (cv o) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmvh) (cuni (cima (cfv (cv t) cmvrs) (cun (cv o) (csn (cv p))))))) (cima (cv r) (csn (cfv (cv e) (cv s1)))))) (cv r))) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsub)))))))))))) $;

--| Define the function mapping syntax expressions to syntax trees. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mst {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmst (cmpt t cvv (cres (co c0 c0 (cfv (cv t) cmtree)) (cres (cfv (cv t) cmex) (cfv (cv t) cmvt)))) $;

--| Define the indexing set for a syntax axiom's representation in a tree. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_msax {t p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmsax (cmpt t cvv (cmpt p (cfv (cv t) cmsa) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmvh) (cfv (cv p) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs))))) $;

--| Define the set of unambiguous formal systems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mufs {t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmufs (crab t (wfun (cfv (cv t) cmst)) cmgfs) $;

--| The universe of a model.
term cmuv: class;

--| The set of valuations.
term cmvl: class;

--| Substitution for a valuation.
term cmvsb: class;

--| The freshness relation of a model.
term cmfsh: class;

--| The set of freshness relations.
term cmfr: class;

--| The evaluation function of a model.
term cmevl: class;

--| The set of models.
term cmdl: class;

--| The syntax function applied to elements of the model.
term cusyn: class;

--| The set of models in a grammatical formal system.
term cgmdl: class;

--| The interpretation function of the model.
term cmitp: class;

--| The evaluation function derived from the interpretation.
term cmfitp: class;

--| Define the universe of a model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_muv:
  $ wceq cmuv (cslot c7) $;

--| Define the freshness relation of a model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mfsh:
  $ wceq cmfsh (cslot (cdc c1 c9)) $;

--| Define the evaluation function of a model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mevl:
  $ wceq cmevl (cslot (cdc c2 cc0)) $;

--| Define the set of valuations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvl {v t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmvl (cmpt t cvv (cixp v (cfv (cv t) cmvar) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmty)))))) $;

--| Define substitution applied to a valuation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mvsb {x3 v t m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmvsb (cmpt t cvv (coprab s1 m x3 (w3a (wa (wcel (cv s1) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsub))) (wcel (cv m) (cfv (cv t) cmvl))) (wral v (wbr (cv m) (cfv (cfv (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cdm (cfv (cv t) cmevl))) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) (wceq (cv x3) (cmpt v (cfv (cv t) cmvar) (co (cv m) (cfv (cfv (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv s1)) (cfv (cv t) cmevl))))))) $;

--| Define the set of freshness relations. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mfrel {w v t r c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmfr (cmpt t cvv (crab r (wa (wceq (ccnv (cv r)) (cv r)) (wral c (wral w (wrex v (wss (cv w) (cima (cv r) (csn (cv v)))) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cv c)))) (cin (cpw (cfv (cv t) cmuv)) cfn)) (cfv (cv t) cmvt))) (cpw (cxp (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (cfv (cv t) cmuv))))) $;

--| Define the set of models of a formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mdl {x3 y1 z v u t e f2 h m n s1 p a d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmdl (crab t (wsbc u (wsbc x3 (wsbc v (wsbc n (wsbc f2 (wa (w3a (wss (cv u) (cxp (cfv (cv t) cmtc) cvv)) (wcel (cv f2) (cfv (cv t) cmfr)) (wcel (cv n) (co (cv u) (cxp (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmex)) cpm))) (wral m (wa (w3a (wral e (wss (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cv e)))) (cima (cv u) (csn (cfv (cv e) c1st)))) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wbr (cop (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) cmvh))) (cfv (cv y1) (cv m)) (cv n)) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) (wal d (wal h (wal a (wi (wcel (cotp (cv d) (cv h) (cv a)) (cfv (cv t) cmax)) (wi (wa (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) (cv d)) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) (cv m)) (cfv (cv z) (cv m)) (cv f2))))) (wss (cv h) (cima (cdm (cv n)) (csn (cv m))))) (wbr (cv m) (cv a) (cdm (cv n))))))))) (w3a (wral s1 (wral e (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cop (cv s1) (cv m)) (cv y1) (cfv (cv t) cmvsb)) (wceq (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cfv (cv e) (cv s1))))) (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv y1) (cv e))))))) (cfv (cv t) cmex)) (crn (cfv (cv t) cmsub))) (wral p (wral e (wi (wceq (cres (cv m) (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs))) (cres (cv p) (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)))) (wceq (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cv e)))) (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv p) (cv e)))))) (cv x3)) (cv v)) (wral y1 (wral e (wi (wss (cima (cv m) (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs))) (cima (cv f2) (csn (cv y1)))) (wss (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cv e)))) (cima (cv f2) (csn (cv y1))))) (cv x3)) (cv u)))) (cv v))) (cfv (cv t) cmfsh)) (cfv (cv t) cmevl)) (cfv (cv t) cmvl)) (cfv (cv t) cmex)) (cfv (cv t) cmuv)) cmfs) $;

--| Define the syntax typecode function for the model universe. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_musyn {v t: setvar}:
  $ wceq cusyn (cmpt t cvv (cmpt v (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (cop (cfv (cfv (cv v) c1st) (cfv (cv t) cmsy)) (cfv (cv v) c2nd)))) $;

--| Define the set of models of a grammatical formal system. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_gmdl {w v t e m c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgmdl (crab t (w3a (wral c (wss (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cv c))) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv c) (cfv (cv t) cmsy))))) (cfv (cv t) cmtc)) (wral v (wral w (wb (wbr (cv v) (cv w) (cfv (cv t) cmfsh)) (wbr (cv v) (cfv (cv w) (cfv (cv t) cusyn)) (cfv (cv t) cmfsh))) (cfv (cv c) cmuv)) (cfv (cv c) cmuv)) (wral m (wral e (wceq (cima (cfv (cv t) cmevl) (csn (cop (cv m) (cv e)))) (cin (cima (cfv (cv t) cmevl) (csn (cop (cv m) (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmesy))))) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv e) c1st))))) (cfv (cv t) cmex)) (cfv (cv t) cmvl))) (cin cmgfs cmdl)) $;

--| Define the interpretation function for a model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mitp {x3 t g2 i m a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmitp (cmpt t cvv (cmpt a (cfv (cv t) cmsa) (cmpt g2 (cixp i (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv t) cmty))))) (cio x3 (wrex m (wa (wceq (cv g2) (cres (cv m) (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)))) (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv m) (cv a) (cfv (cv t) cmevl)))) (cfv (cv t) cmvl)))))) $;

--| Define a function that produces the evaluation function, given the interpretation function for a model. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2016.)
axiom df_mfitp {v t e f2 g2 i m n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmfitp (cmpt t cvv (cmpt f2 (cixp a (cfv (cv t) cmsa) (co (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv t) c1st)))) (cixp i (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv t) cmty))))) cmap)) (crio n (wral m (w3a (wral v (wbr (cop (cv m) (cfv (cv v) (cfv (cv t) cmvh))) (cfv (cv v) (cv m)) (cv n)) (cfv (cv t) cmvar)) (wal e (wal a (wal g2 (wi (wbr (cv e) (cop (cv a) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv t) cmst)) (wbr (cop (cv m) (cv e)) (cfv (cmpt i (cfv (cv a) (cfv (cv t) cmvrs)) (co (cv m) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv t) cmvh)) (cv g2)) (cv n))) (cv f2)) (cv n)))))) (wral e (wceq (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cv e)))) (cin (cima (cv n) (csn (cop (cv m) (cfv (cv e) (cfv (cv t) cmesy))))) (cima (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (csn (cfv (cv e) c1st))))) (cfv (cv t) cmex))) (cfv (cv t) cmvl)) (co (cfv (cv t) cmuv) (cxp (cfv (cv t) cmvl) (cfv (cv t) cmex)) cpm)))) $;

--| Completion of a metric space.
term ccpms: class;

--| Embeddings for a direct limit.
term chlb: class;

--| Direct limit structure.
term chlim: class;

--| Polynomial extension field.
term cpfl: class;

--| Splitting field for a single polynomial (auxiliary).
term csf1: class;

--| Splitting field for a finite set of polynomials.
term csf: class;

--| Splitting field for a sequence of polynomials.
term cpsl: class;

--| A function which completes the given metric space. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_cplmet {x3 y1 z w v e f2 g2 j r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccpms (cmpt w cvv (csb r (co (co (cv w) cn cpws) (cfv (cfv (cv w) cds) ccau) cress) (csb v (cfv (cv r) cbs) (csb e (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cv v)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) (co (cfv (cv j) (cv g2)) (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv w) cds) cbl)) (cres (cv f2) (cfv (cv j) cuz))) cz) crp))) (co (co (cv r) (cv e) cqus) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cds) (coprab x3 y1 z (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cec (cv p) (cv e))) (wceq (cv y1) (cec (cv q) (cv e)))) (wbr (co (cv p) (cv q) (cof (cfv (cv r) cds))) (cv z) cli)) (cv v)) (cv v))))) csts))))) $;

--| The input to this function is a sequence (on ` NN ` ) of homomorphisms ` F ( n ) : R ( n ) --> R ( n + 1 ) ` . The resulting structure is the direct limit of the direct system so defined. This function returns the pair ` <. S , G >. ` where ` S ` is the terminal object and ` G ` is a sequence of functions such that ` G ( n ) : R ( n ) --> S ` and ` G ( n ) = F ( n ) o. G ( n + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_homlimb {x3 v e f2 n s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq chlb (cmpt f2 cvv (csb v (ciun n cn (cxp (csn (cv n)) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))))) (csb e (cint (cab s1 (wa (wer (cv v) (cv s1)) (wss (cmpt x3 (cv v) (cop (co (cfv (cv x3) c1st) c1 caddc) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cv f2))))) (cv s1))))) (cop (cqs (cv v) (cv e)) (cmpt n cn (cmpt x3 (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) (cec (cop (cv n) (cv x3)) (cv e)))))))) $;

--| The input to this function is a sequence (on ` NN ` ) of structures ` R ( n ) ` and homomorphisms ` F ( n ) : R ( n ) --> R ( n + 1 ) ` . The resulting structure is the direct limit of the direct system so defined, and maintains any structures that were present in the original objects. TODO: generalize to directed sets? (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_homlim {x3 y1 v e f2 g2 n s1 r: setvar}:
  $ wceq chlim (cmpo r f2 cvv cvv (csb e (cfv (cv f2) chlb) (csb v (cfv (cv e) c1st) (csb g2 (cfv (cv e) c2nd) (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv v)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (ciun n cn (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cop (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv r)) cplusg)) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))))))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (ciun n cn (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cop (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv r)) cmulr)) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))))))))) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx ctopn) (crab s1 (wral n (wcel (cima (ccnv (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cv s1)) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv r)) ctopn)) cn) (cpw (cv v)))) (cop (cfv cnx cds) (ciun n cn (crn (cmpo x3 y1 (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cdm (cfv (cv n) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (cop (cop (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (cfv (cv y1) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv r)) cds))))))) (cop (cfv cnx cple) (ciun n cn (ccom (ccnv (cfv (cv n) (cv g2))) (ccom (cfv (cfv (cv n) (cv r)) cple) (cfv (cv n) (cv g2)))))))))))) $;

--| Define the field extension that augments a field with the root of the given irreducible polynomial, and extends the norm if one exists and the extension is unique. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux and Steven Nguyen, 21-Jun-2025.)
axiom df_plfl {z t f2 g2 i n s1 r q p c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpfl (cmpo r p cvv cvv (csb s1 (cfv (cv r) cpl1) (csb i (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv (cv s1) crsp)) (csb f2 (cmpt c (cfv (cv r) cbs) (cec (co (cv c) (cfv (cv s1) cur) (cfv (cv s1) cvsca)) (co (cv s1) (cv i) cqg))) (cop (csb t (co (cv s1) (co (cv s1) (cv i) cqg) cqus) (co (co (cv t) (crio n (wceq (ccom (cv n) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv r) cnm)) (cfv (cv t) cabv)) ctng) (cop (cfv cnx cple) (csb g2 (cmpt z (cfv (cv t) cbs) (crio q (wceq (co (cv q) (cv p) (cfv (cv r) cr1p)) (cv q)) (cv z))) (ccom (ccnv (cv g2)) (ccom (cfv (cv s1) cple) (cv g2))))) csts)) (cv f2)))))) $;

--| Transfer existential quantification from a variable ` x ` to another variable ` y ` contained in expression ` A ` . (Contributed by SN, 20-Jun-2025.)
theorem rexxfr3d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) X) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 ps A2) (wrex y1 ch B2)) $;

--| Longer proof of ~ rexxfr3d using ~ ax-11 instead of ~ ax-12 , without the disjoint variable condition ` A x y ` . (Contributed by SN, 19-Jun-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rexxfr3dALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class y1) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) X) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 ps A2) (wrex y1 ch B2)) $;

--| The span of a set of ring elements is a set of ring elements. (Contributed by SN, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem rspssbasd (ph: wff) (B2 R G K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv R crsp) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv G K) B2) $;

--| Membership in a left coset in a quotient of a ring by the span of a singleton (that is, by the ideal generated by an element). This characterization comes from ~ eqglact and ~ elrspsn . (Contributed by SN, 19-Jun-2025.)
theorem ellcsrspsn {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (c_pl: class x3) (c_sm: class y1 z) (R c_x: class x3 z) (U: class x3 y1 z) (I: class x3) (M: class x3 z) (X: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv R cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_sm (co R I cqg) $ >
  $ wceq U (co R c_sm cqus) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (csn M) (cfv R crsp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv U cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wceq X (cec (cv x3) c_sm)) (wceq X (cab z (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) M c_x) c_pl)) B2)))) B2) $;

--| Uniqueness of polynomial remainder: convert the subtraction in ~ ply1divalg2 to addition. (Contributed by SN, 20-Jun-2025.)
theorem ply1divalg3 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class q) (D P c_pl R c_xb F G: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv P cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu q (wbr (cfv (co F (co (cv q) G c_xb) c_pl) D) (cfv G D) clt) B2) $;

--| Uniqueness of polynomial remainder in terms of a quotient structure in the sense of the right hand side of ~ r1pid2 . (Contributed by SN, 21-Jun-2025.)
theorem r1peuqusdeg1 {q: setvar} (ph: wff) (D P: class) (Q: class q) (R: class) (T: class q) (F I: class) (N: class q) (Z: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (csn F) (cfv P crsp)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co P (co P I cqg) cqus) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv T cbs) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cuc1p) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv R cdg1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cdomn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wreu q (wbr (cfv (cv q) D) (cfv F D) clt) Z) $;

--| Temporary construction for the splitting field of a polynomial. The inputs are a field ` r ` and a polynomial ` p ` that we want to split, along with a tuple ` j ` in the same format as the output. The output is a tuple ` <. S , F >. ` where ` S ` is the splitting field and ` F ` is an injective homomorphism from the original field ` r ` . The function works by repeatedly finding the smallest monic irreducible factor, and extending the field by that factor using the ` polyFld ` construction. We keep track of a total order in each of the splitting fields so that we can pick an element definably without needing global choice. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_sfl1 {t f2 g2 h j m s1 r p b: setvar}:
  $ wceq csf1 (cmpo r j cvv cvv (cmpt p (cfv (cv r) cpl1) (cfv (cfv (co c1 (co (cv r) (cv p) cdg1) cfz) ccrd) (crdg (cmpo s1 f2 cvv cvv (csb m (cfv (cv s1) cpl1) (csb b (crab g2 (wa (wbr (cv g2) (ccom (cv p) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv m) cdsr)) (wbr c1 (co (cv s1) (cv g2) cdg1) clt)) (cin (cfv (cv s1) cmn1) (cfv (cv m) cir))) (cif (wo (wceq (ccom (cv p) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv m) c0g)) (wceq (cv b) c0)) (cop (cv s1) (cv f2)) (csb h (cfv (cv b) cglb) (csb t (co (cv s1) (cv h) cpfl) (cop (cfv (cv t) c1st) (ccom (cv f2) (cfv (cv t) c2nd))))))))) (cv j))))) $;

--| Define the splitting field of a finite collection of polynomials, given a total ordered base field. The output is a tuple ` <. S , F >. ` where ` S ` is the totally ordered splitting field and ` F ` is an injective homomorphism from the original field ` r ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_sfl {x3 e f2 g2 r p: setvar}:
  $ wceq csf (cmpo r p cvv cvv (cio x3 (wex f2 (wa (wiso (co c1 (cfv (cv p) chash) cfz) (cv p) clt (cfv (cv r) cplt) (cv f2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv p) chash) (cseq (cmpo e g2 cvv cvv (cfv (cv g2) (co (cv r) (cv e) csf1))) (cun (cv f2) (csn (cop cc0 (cop (cv r) (cres cid (cfv (cv r) cbs)))))) cc0))))))) $;

--| Define the direct limit of an increasing sequence of fields produced by pasting together the splitting fields for each sequence of polynomials. That is, given a ring ` r ` , a strict order on ` r ` , and a sequence ` p : NN --> ( ~P r i^i Fin ) ` of finite sets of polynomials to split, we construct the direct limit system of field extensions by splitting one set at a time and passing the resulting construction to ` HomLim ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_psl {x3 e f2 g2 s1 r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cpsl (cmpo r p cvv (co (cin (cpw (cfv (cv r) cbs)) cfn) cn cmap) (csb f2 (ccom c1st (cseq (cmpo g2 q cvv cvv (csb e (cfv (cv g2) c1st) (csb s1 (cfv (cv e) c1st) (csb f2 (co (cv s1) (crn (cmpt x3 (cv q) (ccom (cv x3) (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)))) csf) (cop (cv f2) (ccom (cfv (cv g2) c2nd) (cfv (cv f2) c2nd))))))) (cun (cv p) (csn (cop cc0 (cop (cop (cv r) c0) (cres cid (cfv (cv r) cbs)))))) cc0)) (co (ccom c1st (co (cv f2) c1 cshi)) (ccom c2nd (cv f2)) chlim))) $;

--| Integral elements of a ring.
term czr: class;

--| Galois finite field.
term cgf: class;

--| Galois limit field.
term cgfo: class;

--| Equivalence relation for ~ df-qp .
term ceqp: class;

--| Equivalence relation representatives for ~ df-qp .
term crqp: class;

--| The set of ` p ` -adic rational numbers.
term cqp: class;

--| The set of ` p ` -adic integers. (Not to be confused with ~ czn .)
term czp: class;

--| Algebraic completion of the ` p ` -adic rational numbers.
term cqpa: class;

--| Metric completion of ` _Qp ` .
term ccp: class;

--| Define the subring of integral elements in a ring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_zrng {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq czr (cmpt r cvv (co (cv r) (crn (cfv (cv r) czrh)) cirng)) $;

--| Define the Galois finite field of order ` p ^ n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_gf {x3 n s1 r p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgf (cmpo p n cprime cn (csb r (cfv (cv p) czn) (cfv (co (cv r) (csn (csb s1 (cfv (cv r) cpl1) (csb x3 (cfv (cv r) cv1) (co (co (co (cv p) (cv n) cexp) (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv s1) cmgp) cmg)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv s1) csg))))) csf) c1st))) $;

--| Define the Galois field of order ` p ^ +oo ` , as a direct limit of the Galois finite fields. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_gfoo {x3 n s1 r p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgfo (cmpt p cprime (csb r (cfv (cv p) czn) (co (cv r) (cmpt n cn (csn (csb s1 (cfv (cv r) cpl1) (csb x3 (cfv (cv r) cv1) (co (co (co (cv p) (cv n) cexp) (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv s1) cmgp) cmg)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv s1) csg)))))) cpsl))) $;

--| Define an equivalence relation on ` ZZ ` -indexed sequences of integers such that two sequences are equivalent iff the difference is equivalent to zero, and a sequence is equivalent to zero iff the sum ` sum_ k <_ n f ( k ) ( p ^ k ) ` is a multiple of ` p ^ ( n + 1 ) ` for every ` n ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_eqp {f2 g2 k n p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceqp (cmpt p cprime (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) (co cz cz cmap)) (wral n (wcel (csu k (cfv (cneg (cv n)) cuz) (co (co (cfv (cneg (cv k)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cneg (cv k)) (cv g2)) cmin) (co (cv p) (co (cv k) (co (cv n) c1 caddc) caddc) cexp) cdiv)) cz) cz)))) $;

--| There is a unique element of ` ( ZZ ^m ( 0 ... ( p - 1 ) ) ) ` ` ~Qp ` -equivalent to any element of ` ( ZZ ^m ZZ ) ` , if the sequences are zero for sufficiently large negative values; this function selects that element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_rqp {x3 y1 f2 p: setvar}:
  $ wceq crqp (cmpt p cprime (cin ceqp (csb y1 (crab f2 (wrex x3 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cdif cz (csn cc0))) (cv x3)) (crn cuz)) (co cz cz cmap)) (cxp (cv y1) (cin (cv y1) (co cz (co cc0 (co (cv p) c1 cmin) cfz) cmap)))))) $;

--| Define the ` p ` -adic completion of the rational numbers, as a normed field structure with a total order (that is not compatible with the operations). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
axiom df_qp {x3 f2 g2 h k n p b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqp (cmpt p cprime (csb b (crab h (wrex x3 (wss (cima (ccnv (cv h)) (cdif cz (csn cc0))) (cv x3)) (crn cuz)) (co cz (co cc0 (co (cv p) c1 cmin) cfz) cmap)) (co (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv b) (cv b) (cfv (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cof caddc)) (cfv (cv p) crqp)))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo f2 g2 (cv b) (cv b) (cfv (cmpt n cz (csu k cz (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv k) cmin) (cv g2)) cmul))) (cfv (cv p) crqp))))) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cple) (copab f2 g2 (wa (wss (cpr (cv f2) (cv g2)) (cv b)) (wbr (csu k cz (co (cfv (cneg (cv k)) (cv f2)) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cneg (cv k)) cexp) cmul)) (csu k cz (co (cfv (cneg (cv k)) (cv g2)) (co (co (cv p) c1 caddc) (cneg (cv k)) cexp) cmul)) clt)))))) (cmpt f2 (cv b) (cif (wceq (cv f2) (cxp cz (csn cc0))) cc0 (co (cv p) (cneg (cinf (cima (ccnv (cv f2)) (cdif cz (csn cc0))) cr clt)) cexp))) ctng))) $;

--| Define the ` p ` -adic integers, as a subset of the ` p ` -adic rationals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_zp:
  $ wceq czp (ccom czr cqp) $;

--| Define the completion of the ` p ` -adic rationals. Here we simply define it as the splitting field of a dense sequence of polynomials (using as the ` n ` -th set the collection of polynomials with degree less than ` n ` and with coefficients ` < ( p ^ n ) ` ). Krasner's lemma will then show that all monic polynomials have splitting fields isomorphic to a sufficiently close Eisenstein polynomial from the list, and unramified extensions are generated by the polynomial ` x ^ ( p ^ n ) - x ` , which is in the list. Thus, every finite extension of ` Qp ` is a subfield of this field extension, so it is algebraically closed. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_qpa {f2 n r p d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cqpa (cmpt p cprime (csb r (cfv (cv p) cqp) (co (cv r) (cmpt n cn (crab f2 (wa (wbr (co (cv r) (cv f2) cdg1) (cv n) cle) (wral d (wss (cima (ccnv (cv d)) (cdif cz (csn cc0))) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz)) (crn (cfv (cv f2) cco1)))) (cfv (cv r) cpl1))) cpsl))) $;

--| Define the metric completion of the algebraic completion of the ` p ` -adic rationals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Dec-2014.)
axiom df_cp:
  $ wceq ccp (ccom ccpms cqpa) $;

--| Practice problem 1. Clues: ~ 5p4e9 ~ 3p2e5 ~ eqtri ~ oveq1i . (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 16-Mar-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem problem1:
  $ wceq (co (co c3 c2 caddc) c4 caddc) c9 $;

--| Practice problem 2. Clues: ~ oveq12i ~ adddiri ~ add4i ~ mulcli ~ recni ~ 2re ~ 3eqtri ~ 10re ~ 5re ~ 1re ~ 4re ~ eqcomi ~ 5p4e9 ~ oveq1i ~ df-3 . (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 16-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem problem2:
  $ wceq (co (co (co c2 (cdc c1 cc0) cmul) c5 caddc) (co (co c1 (cdc c1 cc0) cmul) c4 caddc) caddc) (co (co c3 (cdc c1 cc0) cmul) c9 caddc) $;

--| Practice problem 3. Clues: ~ eqcomi ~ eqtri ~ subaddrii ~ recni ~ 4re ~ 3re ~ 1re ~ df-4 ~ addcomi . (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 16-Mar-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem problem3 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c3 caddc) c4 $ >
  $ wceq A2 c1 $;

--| Practice problem 4. Clues: ~ pm3.2i ~ eqcomi ~ eqtri ~ subaddrii ~ recni ~ 7re ~ 6re ~ ax-1cn ~ df-7 ~ ax-mp ~ oveq1i ~ 3cn ~ 2cn ~ df-3 ~ mullidi ~ subdiri ~ mp3an ~ mulcli ~ subadd23 ~ oveq2i ~ oveq12i ~ 3t2e6 ~ mulcomi ~ subcli ~ biimpri ~ subadd2i . (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 16-Mar-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem problem4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) c3 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c3 A2 cmul) (co c2 B2 cmul) caddc) c7 $ >
  $ wa (wceq A2 c1) (wceq B2 c2) $;

--| Practice problem 5. Clues: ~ 3brtr3i ~ mpbi ~ breqtri ~ ltaddsubi ~ remulcli ~ 2re ~ 3re ~ 9re ~ eqcomi ~ mvlladdi 3cn ~ 6cn ~ eqtr3i ~ 6p3e9 ~ addcomi ~ ltdiv1ii ~ 6re ~ nngt0i ~ 2nn ~ divcan3i ~ recni ~ 2cn ~ 2ne0 ~ mpbir ~ eqtri ~ mulcomi ~ 3t2e6 ~ divmuli . (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 16-Mar-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem problem5 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr (co (co c2 A2 cmul) c3 caddc) c9 clt $ >
  $ wbr A2 c3 clt $;

--| Variant of quadratic equation with discriminant expanded. (Contributed by Filip Cernatescu, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem quad3 (A2 B2 C X: class):
  $ wcel X cc $ >
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wne A2 cc0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 (co X c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 X cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0 $ >
  $ wo (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) (cfv (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin) csqrt) caddc) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)) (wceq X (co (co (cneg B2) (cfv (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin) csqrt) cmin) (co c2 A2 cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Utility lemma to convert between ` m <_ k ` and ` k e. ( ZZ>= `` m ) ` in limit theorems. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Nov-2012.)
theorem climuzcnv (ph: wff) (k m: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel (cv m) cn) (wb (wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv (cv m) cuz)) ph) (wi (wcel (cv k) cn) (wi (wbr (cv m) (cv k) cle) ph))) $;

--| ` ( ( sin `` x ) / x ) ~~> 1 ` as (real) ` x ~~> 0 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Nov-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem sinccvglem {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (F: class) (G H M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf cn (cdif cr (csn cc0)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (cdif cr (csn cc0)) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv x3) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cc (co c1 (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) c3 cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs) c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (ccom G F) c1 cli) $;

--| ` ( ( sin `` x ) / x ) ~~> 1 ` as (real) ` x ~~> 0 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Nov-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem sinccvg {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cn (cdif cr (csn cc0)) F) (wbr F cc0 cli)) (wbr (ccom (cmpt x3 (cdif cr (csn cc0)) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv x3) cdiv)) F) c1 cli) $;

--| The circumference of a circle of radius ` R ` , defined as the limit as ` n ~~> +oo ` of the perimeter of an inscribed n-sided isogons, is ` ( ( 2 x. _pi ) x. R ) ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Nov-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem circum {n: setvar} (A2 P: class n) (R: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cv n) cdiv) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt n cn (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) (co R (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wcel R cr $ >
  $ wbr P (co (co c2 cpi cmul) R cmul) cli $;

--| Membership in a curtailed finite sequence of integers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Nov-2012.)
theorem elfzm12 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wi (wcel M (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) (wcel M (co c1 N cfz))) $;

--| A strictly-decreasing nonnegative integer sequence with initial term ` N ` reaches zero by the ` N ` th term. Inference version. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.)
theorem nn0seqcvg {k: setvar} (F N: class):
  $ wf cn0 cn0 F $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv cc0 F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) cn0) (wi (wne (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cc0) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv N F) cc0 $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem lediv2aALT (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wbr cc0 C cle))) (wi (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The absolute values of two numbers compare as their squares. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2sqlei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cle) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) cle) $;

--| The absolute values of two numbers compare as their squares. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2sqlti (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) clt) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| The absolute values of two numbers compare as their squares. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2sqle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cle) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) cle)) $;

--| The absolute values of two numbers compare as their squares. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2sqlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) clt) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) clt)) $;

--| Difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2difi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle $;

--| Absolute value of difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2difabsi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 2th . (Contributed by Hongxiu Chen, 29-Jun-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2thALT (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ orbi2i . (Contributed by Hongxiu Chen, 29-Jun-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem orbi2iALT (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wo ch ph) (wo ch ps) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ pm3.48 . (Contributed by Hongxiu Chen, 29-Jun-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pm3_48ALT (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ch th)) (wi (wo ph ch) (wo ps th)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ 3jcad . (Contributed by Hongxiu Chen, 29-Jun-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use 3jcad instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _3jcadALT (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (w3a ch th ta)) $;

--| Biconditional version of Curry's paradox. If some proposition ` ph ` amounts to the self-referential statement "This very statement is equivalent to ` ps ` ", then ` ps ` is true. See ~ bj-currypara in BJ's mathbox for the classical version. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 18-Mar-2025.)
theorem currybi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph (wb ph ps)) ps $;

--| Suppose ` ph ` , ` ps ` are distinct atomic propositional formulas, and let ` _G ` be the smallest class of formulas for which ` T. e. _G ` and ` ( ch -> ph ) ` , ` ( ch -> ps ) e. _G ` for ` ch e. _G ` . The present theorem is then an element of ` _G ` , and the implications occurring in the theorem are in one-to-one correspondence with the formulas in ` _G ` up to logical equivalence. In particular, the theorem itself is equivalent to ` T. e. _G ` . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 2-Oct-2025.)
theorem antnest (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi (wi wtru ph) ps) ps) ph) ps) ps $;

--| Lemma for ~ antnestlaw3 . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 5-Dec-2025.)
theorem antnestlaw3lem (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) ch)) (wn (wi (wi (wi ph ch) ps) ps)) $;

--| A law of nested antecedents. The converse direction is a subschema of ~ pm2.27 . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 5-Dec-2025.)
theorem antnestlaw1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| A law of nested antecedents. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 5-Dec-2025.)
theorem antnestlaw2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) ch) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) ps) ch) $;

--| A law of nested antecedents. Compare with ~ looinv . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 5-Dec-2025.)
theorem antnestlaw3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) ch) (wi (wi (wi ph ch) ps) ps) $;

--| Alternative proof of ~ antnest from the valid schema ` ( ( ( ( T. -> ph ) -> ph ) -> ps ) -> ps ) ` using laws of nested antecedents. Our proof uses only the laws ~ antnestlaw1 and ~ antnestlaw3 . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 5-Dec-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem antnestALT (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi (wi (wi wtru ph) ps) ps) ph) ps) ps $;

--| Relative version of ~ mirimanoff (which corresponds to the case ` V = _V ` ). Note that the definition of ` M ` presupposes that ` _om ` is available as a set ( ~ omex ), which is equivalent to assuming ~ ax-inf2 . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 13-Oct-2026.)
theorem mirimanoffr {x3 f2 n: setvar} (M V: class):
  $ wceq M (crab x3 (wn (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) com) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wral n (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) com)))) V) $ >
  $ wnel M V $;

--| Mirimanoff's paradox: The class of grounded sets is not a set. Here, a set ` x ` is grounded if there is no infinite membership-descending sequence of sets beginning with ` x ` . See Paragraph 1 of [Quine] p. 37 . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 11-Oct-2026.)
theorem mirimanoff {x3 f2 n: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cab x3 (wn (wex f2 (w3a (wfn (cv f2) com) (wceq (cfv c0 (cv f2)) (cv x3)) (wral n (wcel (cfv (csuc (cv n)) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv n) (cv f2))) com))))) $ >
  $ wnel M cvv $;

--| Relative version of ~ onprc . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem onprcr {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (word (cv x3)) V) $ >
  $ wtr A2 $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 V) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ onprc from ~ onprcr . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 15-Oct-2026.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem onprcALT:
  $ wn (wcel con0 cvv) $;

--| Relative version of ~ cardprc . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 15-Oct-2026.)
theorem cardprcr {x3: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cfv (cuni A2) char) V) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 V) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ cardprc from ~ cardprcr . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 15-Oct-2026.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cardprcALT {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) ccrd) (cv x3))) cvv $;

--| A class has an element just in case it has a proper subset. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 26-Oct-2026.)
theorem elpssex {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wex y1 (wpss (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Uniqueness of elements is equivalent to uniqueness of proper subsets. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 26-Oct-2026.)
theorem elpssmo {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wmo x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wmo y1 (wpss (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| A class has a unique element just in case it has a unique proper subset. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 26-Oct-2026.)
theorem elpsseu {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (weu x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (weu y1 (wpss (cv y1) A2)) $;

--| Syntax for the function of the class of operations on a set.
term ccloneop: class;

--| Define the function that sends a set to the class of clone-theoretic operations on the set. For convenience, we take an operation on ` a ` to be a function on finite sequences of elements of ` a ` (rather than tuples) with values in ` a ` . Following line 6 of [Szendrei] p. 11, the arity ` n ` of an operation (here, the length of the sequences at which the operation is defined) is always finite and nonzero, whence ` n ` is taken to be a nonzero finite ordinal. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 3-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_cloneop {x3 n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccloneop (cmpt a cvv (cab x3 (wrex n (wcel (cv x3) (co (cv a) (co (cv a) (cv n) cmap) cmap)) (cdif com c1o)))) $;

--| Syntax for the function of projections on sets.
term cprj: class;

--| Define the function that, for a set ` a ` , arity ` n ` , and index ` i ` , returns the ` i ` -th ` n ` -ary projection on ` a ` . This is the ` n ` -ary operation on ` a ` that, for any sequence of ` n ` elements of ` a ` , returns the element having index ` i ` . (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 3-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_prj {x3 i n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprj (cmpt a cvv (cmpo n i (cdif com c1o) (cv n) (cmpt x3 (co (cv a) (cv n) cmap) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3))))) $;

--| Syntax for the function of superpositions.
term csuppos: class;

--| Define the function that, when given an ` n ` -ary operation ` f ` and ` n ` many ` m ` -ary operations ` ( g `` (/) ) ` , ..., ` ( g `` U. n ) ` , returns the superposition of ` f ` with the ` ( g `` i ) ` , itself another ` m ` -ary operation on ` a ` . Given ` x ` (a sequence of ` m ` arguments in ` a ` ), the superposition effectively applies each of the ` ( g `` i ) ` to ` x ` , then applies ` f ` to the resulting sequence of ` n ` function values. This can be seen as a generalized version of function composition; see paragraph 3 of [Szendrei] p. 11. (Contributed by Adrian Ducourtial, 3-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_suppos {x3 f2 g2 i m n a: setvar}:
  $ wceq csuppos (cmpt a cvv (cmpo n m (cdif com c1o) (cdif com c1o) (cmpo f2 g2 (co (cv a) (co (cv a) (cv n) cmap) cmap) (co (co (cv a) (co (cv a) (cv m) cmap) cmap) (cv n) cmap) (cmpt x3 (co (cv a) (cv m) cmap) (cfv (cmpt i (cv n) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)))) (cv f2)))))) $;

--| ~ ax-ext without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axextprim {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wi (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))) (wi (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)))))) $;

--| ~ ax-rep without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axrepprim {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wi (wn (wal y1 (wn (wal z (wi ph (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))))) (wal z (wn (wi (wi (wal y1 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) (wn (wal x3 (wi (wal z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal y1 ph)))))) (wn (wi (wn (wal x3 (wi (wal z (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wn (wal y1 ph))))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))))))))))) $;

--| ~ ax-un without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axunprim {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wal y1 (wi (wn (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| ~ ax-pow without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axpowprim {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wn (wal y1 (wi (wal x3 (wi (wn (wal z (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wal y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv z))))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| ~ ax-reg without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axregprim {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))))))))) $;

--| ~ ax-inf without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2010.)
theorem axinfprim {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wn (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wn (wal z (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))))))))))))) $;

--| ~ ax-ac without distinct variable conditions or defined symbols. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2010.)
theorem axacprim {x3 y1 z w: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal x3 (wn (wal y1 (wal z (wi (wal x3 (wn (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv w)))))) (wn (wal w (wn (wal y1 (wn (wi (wi (wn (wal w (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))))))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) (wn (wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wn (wal w (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)) (wn (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))))))))))))))))))))) $;

--| We call a class "untanged" if all its members are not members of themselves. The term originates from Isbell (see citation in ~ dfon2 ). Using this concept, we can avoid a lot of the uses of the Axiom of Regularity. Here, we prove a series of properties of untanged classes. First, we prove that an untangled class is not a member of itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Feb-2011.)
theorem untelirr {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) A2) (wn (wcel A2 A2)) $;

--| The union of a class is untangled iff all its members are untangled. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Feb-2011.)
theorem untuni {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) (cuni A2)) (wral y1 (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) (cv y1)) A2) $;

--| If a class is untangled, then so is its successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Feb-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem untsucf {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1):
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) A2) (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) (csuc A2)) $;

--| The empty set is untangled. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 27-Aug-2011.)
theorem unt0 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) c0 $;

--| If there is an untangled element of a class, then the intersection of the class is untangled. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 1-Mar-2011.)
theorem untint {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) (cv x3)) A2) (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) (cint A2)) $;

--| If ` A ` is well-founded by ` _E ` , then it is untangled. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 1-Mar-2011.)
theorem efrunt {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wfr A2 cep) (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) A2) $;

--| A transitive class is untangled iff its elements are. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Mar-2011.)
theorem untangtr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wtr A2) (wb (wral x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3))) A2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv y1))) (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| Double deduction form of ~ 3jaoi . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem _3jaodd (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi th et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ta et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi (w3o ch th ta) et)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ 3ori . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem _3orit (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3o ph ps ch) (wi (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) ch) $;

--| A biconditional in the antecedent is the same as two implications. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Dec-2010.)
theorem biimpexp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wb ph ps) ch) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ph) ch)) $;

--| Two classes are unequal iff their intersection is a proper subset of one of them. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Feb-2011.)
theorem nepss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wne A2 B2) (wo (wpss (cin A2 B2) A2) (wpss (cin A2 B2) B2)) $;

--| Triple disjunction form of ~ ccased . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem _3ccased (ph ps ch th ta et ze si: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch et) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch ze) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ch si) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th et) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ze) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th si) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ta et) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ta ze) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ta si) ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (w3o ch th ta) (w3o et ze si)) ps) $;

--| Expansion of the definition of a strict order. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Jun-2016.)
theorem dfso3 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wor A2 R) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (w3a (wn (wbr (cv x3) (cv x3) R)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R)) (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R)) (w3o (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R))) A2) A2) A2) $;

--| A binary relation involving unordered triples. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem brtpid1 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 (ctp (cop A2 B2) C D) $;

--| A binary relation involving unordered triples. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem brtpid2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 (ctp C (cop A2 B2) D) $;

--| A binary relation involving unordered triples. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem brtpid3 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wbr A2 B2 (ctp C D (cop A2 B2)) $;

--| A method for computing iota. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2017.)
theorem iota5f {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 V)) (wb ps (wceq (cv x3) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (cio x3 ps) A2) $;

--| Closed form of ~ ja . Proved using the completeness script. (Proof modification is discouraged.) (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2021.)
theorem jath (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn ph) ch) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wi ph ps) ch)) $;

--| Cartesian product of two class abstractions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Aug-2024.)
theorem xpab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wceq (cxp (cab x3 ph) (cab y1 ps)) (copab x3 y1 (wa ph ps)) $;

--| The union of a finite ordinal is a finite ordinal. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2024.)
theorem nnuni (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel (cuni A2) com) $;

--| Distribution of square over division. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Jun-2013.)
theorem sqdivzi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 cc0) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| The supremum of a finite sequence of integers. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Aug-2013.)
theorem supfz (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (csup (co M N cfz) cz clt) N) $;

--| The infimum of a finite sequence of integers. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Aug-2013.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem inffz (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (cinf (co M N cfz) cz clt) M) $;

--| The sequence ` ( 0 ... ( N - 1 ) ) ` is empty iff ` N ` is zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-May-2014.)
theorem fz0n (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wceq (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) c0) (wceq N cc0)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem shftvalg (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co F A2 cshi)) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) F)) $;

--| Limit of the ratio of two linear functions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem divcnvlin {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M: class) (V: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (co (co (cv k) A2 caddc) (co (cv k) B2 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c1 cli) $;

--| Comparison of a constant to the limit of a sequence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Jan-2018.)
theorem climlec3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| ` _i ` raised to itself is real. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Apr-2020.)
theorem iexpire:
  $ wcel (co ci ci ccxp) cr $;

--| The binomial coefficient over negative one is zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-May-2020.)
theorem bcneg1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co N (cneg c1) cbc) cc0) $;

--| The proportion of one binomial coefficient to another with ` N ` decreased by 1. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Jun-2020.)
theorem bcm1nt (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wceq (co N K cbc) (co (co (co N c1 cmin) K cbc) (co N (co N K cmin) cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| A product identity for binomial coefficients. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Jun-2020.)
theorem bcprod {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cprod k (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co (co N c1 cmin) (cv k) cbc)) (cprod k (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co (cv k) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) N cmin) cexp))) $;

--| A column-sum rule for binomial coefficients. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Jun-2020.)
theorem bccolsum {k: setvar} (C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wceq (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (cv k) C cbc)) (co (co N c1 caddc) (co C c1 caddc) cbc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iprodefisum . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem iprodefisumlem (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cseq cmul (ccom ce F) M) (ccom ce (cseq caddc F M))) $;

--| Applying the exponential function to an infinite sum yields an infinite product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Feb-2018.)
theorem iprodefisum {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class k) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc F M) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k Z (cfv B2 ce)) (cfv (csu k Z B2) ce)) $;

--| An infinite product version of Euler's gamma function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Feb-2018.)
theorem iprodgam {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc (cdif cz cn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cgam) (co (cprod k cn (co (co (co c1 (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) A2 ccxp) (co c1 (co A2 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) cdiv)) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ faclim . Closed form for a particular sequence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem faclimlem1 {x3 n: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wceq (cseq cmul (cmpt n cn (co (co (co c1 (co M (cv n) cdiv) caddc) (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cmul) (co c1 (co (co M c1 caddc) (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cdiv)) c1) (cmpt x3 cn (co (co M c1 caddc) (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (co M c1 caddc) caddc) cdiv) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ faclim . Show a limit for the inductive step. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem faclimlem2 {n: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wbr (cseq cmul (cmpt n cn (co (co (co c1 (co M (cv n) cdiv) caddc) (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cmul) (co c1 (co (co M c1 caddc) (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cdiv)) c1) (co M c1 caddc) cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ faclim . Algebraic manipulation for the final induction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem faclimlem3 (B2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel B2 cn)) (wceq (co (co (co c1 (co c1 B2 cdiv) caddc) (co M c1 caddc) cexp) (co c1 (co (co M c1 caddc) B2 cdiv) caddc) cdiv) (co (co (co (co c1 (co c1 B2 cdiv) caddc) M cexp) (co c1 (co M B2 cdiv) caddc) cdiv) (co (co (co c1 (co M B2 cdiv) caddc) (co c1 (co c1 B2 cdiv) caddc) cmul) (co c1 (co (co M c1 caddc) B2 cdiv) caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| An infinite product expression relating to factorials. Originally due to Euler. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Nov-2017.)
theorem faclim {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co c1 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) A2 cexp) (co c1 (co A2 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wbr (cseq cmul F c1) (cfv A2 cfa) cli) $;

--| An infinite product expression for factorial. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Dec-2017.)
theorem iprodfac {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wceq (cfv A2 cfa) (cprod k cn (co (co (co c1 (co c1 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) A2 cexp) (co c1 (co A2 (cv k) cdiv) caddc) cdiv))) $;

--| Another factorial limit due to Euler. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Dec-2017.)
theorem faclim2 {n: setvar} (F: class n) (M: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) M cexp) cmul) (cfv (co (cv n) M caddc) cfa) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wbr F c1 cli) $;

--| Swap the second and third arguments of a gcd. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem gcd32 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cgcd) C cgcd) (co (co A2 C cgcd) B2 cgcd)) $;

--| Absorption law for gcd. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem gcdabsorb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cgcd) B2 cgcd) (co A2 B2 cgcd)) $;

--| A potential definition of transitivity for sets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Mar-2012.)
theorem dftr6 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wtr A2) (wcel A2 (cdif cvv (crn (cdif (ccom cep cep) cep)))) $;

--| Composition with the membership relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Feb-2013.)
theorem coep {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (ccom cep R)) (wrex x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) R) B2) $;

--| Composition with the converse membership relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Feb-2013.)
theorem coepr {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (ccom R (ccnv cep))) (wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) B2 R) A2) $;

--| A quantifier-free definition of a well-founded relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2026.)
theorem dffr5 (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wfr A2 R) (wss (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) (crn (cdif cep (ccom cep (ccnv R))))) $;

--| Quantifier-free definition of a strict order. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Feb-2013.)
theorem dfso2 (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wor A2 R) (wa (wpo A2 R) (wss (cxp A2 A2) (cun R (cun cid (ccnv R))))) $;

--| Substitution for an eight-place predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Sep-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem br8 {x3 e f2 g2 h q p a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 g2 h a b c d) (ps: wff x3 e f2 g2 h q p b c d) (ch: wff x3 e f2 g2 h q p a c d) (th: wff x3 e f2 g2 h q p a b d) (ta: wff x3 e f2 g2 h q p a b c) (et: wff x3 f2 g2 h q p a b c d) (ze: wff x3 e g2 h q p a b c d) (si: wff x3 e f2 h q p a b c d) (rh: wff q p) (A2 B2 C D: class) (P: class x3) (Q: class q p) (R: class x3 e f2 g2 h q p a b c d) (S E F G H: class) (X: class q p):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) C) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv d) D) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv e) E) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv f2) F) (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv g2) G) (wb ze si) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv h) H) (wb si rh) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab p q (wrex x3 (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex e (wrex f2 (wrex g2 (wrex h (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cop (cv e) (cv f2)) (cop (cv g2) (cv h)))) ph) P) P) P) P) P) P) P) P) S)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel X S) (wcel A2 Q) (wcel B2 Q)) (w3a (wcel C Q) (wcel D Q) (wcel E Q)) (w3a (wcel F Q) (wcel G Q) (wcel H Q))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) R) rh) $;

--| Substitution for a six-place predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem br6 {x3 e f2 q p a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 a b c d) (ps: wff x3 e f2 q p b c d) (ch: wff x3 e f2 q p a c d) (th: wff x3 e f2 q p a b d) (ta: wff x3 e f2 q p a b c) (et: wff x3 f2 q p a b c d) (ze: wff q p) (A2 B2 C D: class) (P: class x3) (Q: class q p) (R: class x3 e f2 q p a b c d) (S E F: class) (X: class q p):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) C) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv d) D) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv e) E) (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv f2) F) (wb et ze) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab p q (wrex x3 (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex e (wrex f2 (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cop (cv b) (cv c)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cv d) (cop (cv e) (cv f2)))) ph) P) P) P) P) P) P) S)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X S) (w3a (wcel A2 Q) (wcel B2 Q) (wcel C Q)) (w3a (wcel D Q) (wcel E Q) (wcel F Q))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) R) ze) $;

--| Substitution for a four-place predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem br4 {x3 q p a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff a b c d) (ps: wff x3 q p b c d) (ch: wff x3 q p a c d) (th: wff x3 q p a b d) (ta: wff q p) (A2 B2 C D: class) (P: class x3) (Q: class q p) (R: class x3 q p a b c d) (S: class) (X: class q p):
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) C) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv d) D) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq P Q) $ >
  $ wceq R (copab p q (wrex x3 (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cv c) (cv d))) ph) P) P) P) P) S)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X S) (wa (wcel A2 Q) (wcel B2 Q)) (wa (wcel C Q) (wcel D Q))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) R) ta) $;

--| Another distributive law of converse over class composition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem cnvco1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (ccnv (ccom (ccnv A2) B2)) (ccom (ccnv B2) A2) $;

--| Another distributive law of converse over class composition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem cnvco2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (ccnv (ccom A2 (ccnv B2))) (ccom B2 (ccnv A2)) $;

--| Quantifier-free definition of membership in a domain. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem eldm3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm B2)) (wne (cres B2 (csn A2)) c0) $;

--| Quantifier-free definition of membership in a range. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jan-2017.)
theorem elrn3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (crn B2)) (wne (cin B2 (cxp cvv (csn A2))) c0) $;

--| The converse of a partial ordering is still a partial ordering. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem pocnv (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wpo A2 R) (wpo A2 (ccnv R)) $;

--| The converse of a strict ordering is still a strict ordering. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem socnv (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (wor A2 R) (wor A2 (ccnv R)) $;

--| Membership in an intersection of function values. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Dec-2021.)
theorem elintfv {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wss B2 A2)) (wb (wcel X (cint (cima F B2))) (wral y1 (wcel X (cfv (cv y1) F)) B2)) $;

--| A condition for subset trichotomy for functions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Apr-2011.)
theorem funpsstri (F G H: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun H) (wa (wss F H) (wss G H)) (wo (wss (cdm F) (cdm G)) (wss (cdm G) (cdm F)))) (w3o (wpss F G) (wceq F G) (wpss G F)) $;

--| If a class ` F ` is a proper subset of a function ` G ` , then ` dom F C. dom G ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem fundmpss (F G: class):
  $ wi (wfun G) (wi (wpss F G) (wpss (cdm F) (cdm G))) $;

--| Given two functions with equal domains, equality only requires one direction of the subset relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Apr-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem funsseq (F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun F) (wfun G) (wceq (cdm F) (cdm G))) (wb (wceq F G) (wss F G)) $;

--| An equality condition for functions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Feb-2013.)
theorem funbreq (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wbr A2 B2 F)) (wb (wbr A2 C F) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Uniqueness condition for the binary relation ` 1st ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem br1steq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C c1st) (wceq C A2) $;

--| Uniqueness condition for the binary relation ` 2nd ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem br2ndeq (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C c2nd) (wceq C B2) $;

--| Definition of domain in terms of ` 1st ` and image. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem dfdm5 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cdm A2) (cima (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)) A2) $;

--| Definition of range in terms of ` 2nd ` and image. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem dfrn5 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (crn A2) (cima (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv)) A2) $;

--| Alternate way of saying that an ordered pair is in a composition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-May-2018.)
theorem opelco3 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cop A2 B2) (ccom C D)) (wcel B2 (cima C (cima D (csn A2)))) $;

--| Quantifier-free expression saying that a class is a member of an image. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-May-2018.)
theorem elima4 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cima R B2)) (wne (cin R (cxp B2 (csn A2))) c0) $;

--| The value of the converse of ` 1st ` restricted to a singleton. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jul-2020.)
theorem fv1stcnv (A2 V X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y V)) (wceq (cfv X (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp A2 (csn Y))))) (cop X Y)) $;

--| The value of the converse of ` 2nd ` restricted to a singleton. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jul-2020.)
theorem fv2ndcnv (A2 V X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y A2)) (wceq (cfv Y (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp (csn X) A2)))) (cop X Y)) $;

--| A class of transitive sets is partially ordered by ` _E ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2010.)
theorem elpotr {z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral z (wtr (cv z)) A2) (wpo A2 cep) $;

--| Given ~ ax-reg , an ordinal is a transitive class totally ordered by the membership relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Jan-2011.)
theorem dford5reg (A2: class):
  $ wb (word A2) (wa (wtr A2) (wor A2 cep)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Feb-2011.) (Proof shortened by SN, 5-Oct-2026.)
theorem dfon2lem1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wtr (cuni (cab x3 (w3a ph (wtr (cv x3)) ps))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wss (cuni (cab x3 (w3a (wss (cv x3) A2) ph ps))) A2 $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . All sets satisfying the new definition are transitive and untangled. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem3 {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wal x3 (wi (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wtr (cv x3))) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wa (wtr A2) (wral z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv z))) A2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . If two sets satisfy the new definition, then one is a subset of the other. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wi (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wtr (cv x3))) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) B2) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) B2)))) (wo (wss A2 B2) (wss B2 A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . Two sets satisfying the new definition also satisfy trichotomy with respect to ` e. ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wi (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wtr (cv x3))) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) B2) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) B2)))) (w3o (wcel A2 B2) (wceq A2 B2) (wcel B2 A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . A transitive class of sets satisfying the new definition satisfies the new definition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem6 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr S) (wral x3 (wal z (wi (wa (wpss (cv z) (cv x3)) (wtr (cv z))) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) S)) (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) S) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) S))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . All elements of a new ordinal are new ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem7 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wa (wpss (cv x3) A2) (wtr (cv x3))) (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wi (wcel B2 A2) (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) B2) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) B2)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . The intersection of a nonempty class ` A ` of new ordinals is itself a new ordinal and is contained within ` A ` (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem8 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne A2 c0) (wral x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) A2)) (wa (wal z (wi (wa (wpss (cv z) (cint A2)) (wtr (cv z))) (wcel (cv z) (cint A2)))) (wcel (cint A2) A2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfon2 . A class of new ordinals is well-founded by ` _E ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Mar-2011.)
theorem dfon2lem9 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) A2) (wfr A2 cep) $;

--| ` On ` consists of all sets that contain all its transitive proper subsets. This definition comes from J. R. Isbell, "A Definition of Ordinal Numbers", American Mathematical Monthly, vol 67 (1960), pp. 51-52. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Feb-2011.)
theorem dfon2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq con0 (cab x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wpss (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wtr (cv y1))) (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))))) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator at ` (/) ` when the base value is a proper class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem rdgprc0 (F I: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel I cvv)) (wceq (cfv c0 (crdg F I)) c0) $;

--| The value of the recursive definition generator when ` I ` is a proper class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem rdgprc (F I: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel I cvv)) (wceq (crdg F I) (crdg F c0)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the recursive function generator when ` I ` is a set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem dfrdg2 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (F I: class) (V: class x3 y1 f2):
  $ wi (wcel I V) (wceq (crdg F I) (cuni (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cif (wceq (cv y1) c0) I (cif (wlim (cv y1)) (cuni (cima (cv f2) (cv y1))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cv y1)) (cv f2)) F)))) (cv x3))) con0)))) $;

--| Generalization of ~ dfrdg2 to remove sethood requirement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem dfrdg3 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (F I: class):
  $ wceq (crdg F I) (cuni (cab f2 (wrex x3 (wa (wfn (cv f2) (cv x3)) (wral y1 (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cif (wceq (cv y1) c0) (cif (wcel I cvv) I c0) (cif (wlim (cv y1)) (cuni (cima (cv f2) (cv y1))) (cfv (cfv (cuni (cv y1)) (cv f2)) F)))) (cv x3))) con0))) $;

--| A version of ~ ax-ext for use with defined equality. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Dec-2010.)
theorem axextdfeq {x3 y1 z: setvar} (w: setvar):
  $ wex z (wi (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (wi (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wcel (cv z) (cv x3))) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv w)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv w))))) $;

--| A version of ~ ax-8 for use with defined equality. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Dec-2010.)
theorem ax8dfeq {z w: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wex z (wi (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)))) $;

--| ~ ax-ext with distinctors instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem axextdist {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wi (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| ~ axextb with distinctors instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem axextbdist {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))))) (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Version of ~ 19.12vv with not-free hypotheses, instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem _19_12b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wal y1 (wex x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| There is always a set not in ` y ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem exnel {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Distinctors in terms of membership. (NOTE: this only works with relations where we can prove ~ el and ~ elirrv .) (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Dec-2010.)
theorem distel {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wn (wal y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) (wn (wal y1 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| ~ axextnd as a biconditional. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Dec-2010.)
theorem axextndbi {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wex z (wb (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)))) $;

--| A more general form of ~ hbnt . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem hbntg {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ps))) (wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ph))) $;

--| A more general and closed form of ~ hbim . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem hbimtg {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch th: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wi ph (wal x3 ch))) (wi ps (wal x3 th))) (wi (wi ch ps) (wal x3 (wi ph th))) $;

--| A more general and closed form of ~ hbal . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem hbaltg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wal y1 ps))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 (wal x3 ps))) $;

--| A more general form of ~ hbn . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem hbng {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wal x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| A more general form of ~ hbim . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Dec-2010.)
theorem hbimg {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch th: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi ch (wal x3 th) $ >
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wal x3 (wi ph th)) $;

--| Declare the syntax for well-founded successor.
term cwsuc (A2 R X: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for well-founded limit class.
term cwlim (A2 R: class): class;

--| Define the concept of a successor in a well-founded set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
axiom df_wsuc (A2 R X: class):
  $ wceq (cwsuc A2 R X) (cinf (cpred A2 (ccnv R) X) A2 R) $;

--| Define the class of limit points of a well-founded set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
axiom df_wlim {x3: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wceq (cwlim A2 R) (crab x3 (wa (wne (cv x3) (cinf A2 A2 R)) (wceq (cv x3) (csup (cpred A2 R (cv x3)) A2 R))) A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wsuceq123 (A2 B2 R S X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq R S) (wceq A2 B2) (wceq X Y)) (wceq (cwsuc A2 R X) (cwsuc B2 S Y)) $;

--| Equality theorem for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem wsuceq1 (A2 R S X: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cwsuc A2 R X) (cwsuc A2 S X)) $;

--| Equality theorem for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem wsuceq2 (A2 B2 R X: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cwsuc A2 R X) (cwsuc B2 R X)) $;

--| Equality theorem for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.)
theorem wsuceq3 (A2 R X Y: class):
  $ wi (wceq X Y) (wceq (cwsuc A2 R X) (cwsuc A2 R Y)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfwsuc {x3: setvar} (A2 R X: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 X $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cwsuc A2 R X) $;

--| Equality theorem for the limit class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wlimeq12 (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq R S) (wceq A2 B2)) (wceq (cwlim A2 R) (cwlim B2 S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the limit class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.)
theorem wlimeq1 (A2 R S: class):
  $ wi (wceq R S) (wceq (cwlim A2 R) (cwlim A2 S)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the limit class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.)
theorem wlimeq2 (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cwlim A2 R) (cwlim B2 R)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the limit class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem nfwlim {x3: setvar} (A2 R: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cwlim A2 R) $;

--| Membership in the limit class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem elwlim (A2 R X: class):
  $ wb (wcel X (cwlim A2 R)) (w3a (wcel X A2) (wne X (cinf A2 A2 R)) (wceq X (csup (cpred A2 R X) A2 R))) $;

--| The zero of a well-founded set is a member of that set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wzel (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wwe A2 R) (wse A2 R) (wne A2 c0)) (wcel (cinf A2 A2 R) A2) $;

--| Lemma for the supremum properties of well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Revised by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wsuclem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 R: class) (V: class x3 y1 z w) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wbr X (cv w) R) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wral y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) (cpred A2 (ccnv R) X)) (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) (cpred A2 (ccnv R) X))) A2)) A2) $;

--| Existence theorem for well-founded successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wsucex (ph: wff) (A2 R X: class):
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cwsuc A2 R X) cvv) $;

--| If ` X ` is a set with an ` R ` successor in ` A ` , then its well-founded successor is a member of ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wsuccl {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 R: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wbr X (cv y1) R) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cwsuc A2 R X) A2) $;

--| A well-founded successor is a lower bound on points after ` X ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem wsuclb (ph: wff) (A2 R V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wse A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr Y (cwsuc A2 R X) R)) $;

--| The class of limit points is a subclass of the base class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jun-2018.)
theorem wlimss (A2 R: class):
  $ wss (cwlim A2 R) A2 $;

--| Declare the syntax for tail Cartesian product.
term ctxp (A2 B2: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for the parallel product.
term cpprod (R S: class): class;

--| Declare the subset relationship class.
term csset: class;

--| Declare the transitive set class.
term ctrans: class;

--| Declare the set union relationship.
term cbigcup: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the fixpoints of a class.
term cfix (A2: class): class;

--| Declare the class of limit ordinals.
term climits: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the class of all function.
term cfuns: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the singleton function.
term csingle: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the class of all singletons.
term csingles: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the image functor.
term cimage (A2: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for the cartesian function.
term ccart: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the image function.
term cimg: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the domain function.
term cdomain: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the range function.
term crange: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the application function.
term capply: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the cup function.
term ccup: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the cap function.
term ccap: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the successor function.
term csuccf: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the functional part functor.
term cfunpart (F: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for the full function functor.
term cfullfn (F: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for the restriction function.
term crestrict: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the upper bound relationship functor.
term cub (R: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for the lower bound relationship functor.
term clb (R: class): class;

--| Define the tail cross of two classes. Membership in this class is defined by ~ txpss3v and ~ brtxp . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
axiom df_txp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (ctxp A2 B2) (cin (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) A2) (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) B2)) $;

--| Define the parallel product of two classes. Membership in this class is defined by ~ pprodss4v and ~ brpprod . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_pprod (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cpprod A2 B2) (ctxp (ccom A2 (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom B2 (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv)))) $;

--| Define the subset class. For the value, see ~ brsset . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
axiom df_sset:
  $ wceq csset (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (crn (ctxp cep (cdif cvv cep)))) $;

--| Define the class of all transitive sets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
axiom df_trans:
  $ wceq ctrans (cdif cvv (crn (cdif (ccom cep cep) cep))) $;

--| Define the Bigcup function, which, per ~ fvbigcup , carries a set to its union. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
axiom df_bigcup:
  $ wceq cbigcup (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (ccom cep cep) cvv)))) $;

--| Define the class of all fixpoints of a relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
axiom df_fix (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfix A2) (cdm (cin A2 cid)) $;

--| Define the class of all limit ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
axiom df_limits:
  $ wceq climits (cdif (cin con0 (cfix cbigcup)) (csn c0)) $;

--| Define the class of all functions. See ~ elfuns for membership. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Feb-2013.)
axiom df_funs:
  $ wceq cfuns (cdif (cpw (cxp cvv cvv)) (cfix (ccom cep (ccom (ctxp c1st (ccom (cdif cvv cid) c2nd)) (ccnv cep))))) $;

--| Define the singleton function. See ~ brsingle for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_singleton:
  $ wceq csingle (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp cid cvv)))) $;

--| Define the class of all singletons. See ~ elsingles for membership. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
axiom df_singles:
  $ wceq csingles (crn csingle) $;

--| Define the image functor. This function takes a set ` A ` to a function ` x |-> ( A " x ) ` , providing that the latter exists. See ~ imageval for the derivation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Mar-2014.)
axiom df_image (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cimage A2) (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (ccom cep (ccnv A2)) cvv)))) $;

--| Define the cartesian product function. See ~ brcart for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_cart:
  $ wceq ccart (cdif (cxp (cxp cvv cvv) cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (cpprod cep cep) cvv)))) $;

--| Define the image function. See ~ brimg for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_img:
  $ wceq cimg (ccom (cimage (cres (ccom c2nd c1st) (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) ccart) $;

--| Define the domain function. See ~ brdomain for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_domain:
  $ wceq cdomain (cimage (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) $;

--| Define the range function. See ~ brrange for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_range:
  $ wceq crange (cimage (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) $;

--| Define the little cup function. See ~ brcup for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_cup:
  $ wceq ccup (cdif (cxp (cxp cvv cvv) cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (cun (ccom (ccnv c1st) cep) (ccom (ccnv c2nd) cep)) cvv)))) $;

--| Define the little cap function. See ~ brcap for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_cap:
  $ wceq ccap (cdif (cxp (cxp cvv cvv) cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (cin (ccom (ccnv c1st) cep) (ccom (ccnv c2nd) cep)) cvv)))) $;

--| Define the restriction function. See ~ brrestrict for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_restrict:
  $ wceq crestrict (ccom ccap (ctxp c1st (ccom ccart (ctxp c2nd (ccom crange c1st))))) $;

--| Define the successor function. See its alternate version ~ dfsuccf2 . See ~ brsuccf for its value. Cf. the equivalent ~ df-sucmap family. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_succf:
  $ wceq csuccf (ccom ccup (ctxp cid csingle)) $;

--| Define the application function. See ~ brapply for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_apply:
  $ wceq capply (ccom (ccom cbigcup cbigcup) (ccom (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp (cres cep csingles) cvv)))) (ccom (ccom csingle cimg) (cpprod cid csingle)))) $;

--| Define the functional part of a class ` F ` . This is the maximal part of ` F ` that is a function. See ~ funpartfun and ~ funpartfv for the meaning of this statement. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_funpart (F: class):
  $ wceq (cfunpart F) (cres F (cdm (cin (ccom (cimage F) csingle) (cxp cvv csingles)))) $;

--| Define the full function over ` F ` . This is a function with domain ` _V ` that always agrees with ` F ` for its value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.)
axiom df_fullfun (F: class):
  $ wceq (cfullfn F) (cun (cfunpart F) (cxp (cdif cvv (cdm (cfunpart F))) (csn c0))) $;

--| Define the upper bound relationship functor. See ~ brub for value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2018.)
axiom df_ub (R: class):
  $ wceq (cub R) (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) (ccom (cdif cvv R) (ccnv cep))) $;

--| Define the lower bound relationship functor. See ~ brlb for value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2018.)
axiom df_lb (R: class):
  $ wceq (clb R) (cub (ccnv R)) $;

--| A tail Cartesian product is a subset of the class of ordered triples. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem txpss3v (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (ctxp A2 B2) (cxp cvv (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| A tail Cartesian product is a relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem txprel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wrel (ctxp A2 B2) $;

--| Characterize a ternary relation over a tail Cartesian product. Together with ~ txpss3v , this completely defines membership in a tail cross. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem brtxp (A2 B2 X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wcel Y cvv $ >
  $ wcel Z cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr X (cop Y Z) (ctxp A2 B2)) (wa (wbr X Y A2) (wbr X Z B2)) $;

--| The binary relation over a tail cross when the second argument is not an ordered pair. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-May-2015.)
theorem brtxp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (ctxp R S)) (wex x3 (wex y1 (w3a (wceq B2 (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wbr A2 (cv x3) R) (wbr A2 (cv y1) S)))) $;

--| Expanded definition of parallel product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem dfpprod2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cpprod A2 B2) (cin (ccom (ccnv (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom A2 (cres c1st (cxp cvv cvv)))) (ccom (ccnv (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))) (ccom B2 (cres c2nd (cxp cvv cvv))))) $;

--| A converse law for parallel product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem pprodcnveq (R S: class):
  $ wceq (cpprod R S) (ccnv (cpprod (ccnv R) (ccnv S))) $;

--| The parallel product is a subclass of ` ( ( _V X. _V ) X. ( _V X. _V ) ) ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem pprodss4v (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (cpprod A2 B2) (cxp (cxp cvv cvv) (cxp cvv cvv)) $;

--| Characterize a quaternary relation over a tail Cartesian product. Together with ~ pprodss4v , this completely defines membership in a parallel product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brpprod (A2 B2 W X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wcel Y cvv $ >
  $ wcel Z cvv $ >
  $ wcel W cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop X Y) (cop Z W) (cpprod A2 B2)) (wa (wbr X Z A2) (wbr Y W B2)) $;

--| Condition for parallel product when the last argument is not an ordered pair. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brpprod3a {z w: setvar} (R S X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wcel Y cvv $ >
  $ wcel Z cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop X Y) Z (cpprod R S)) (wex z (wex w (w3a (wceq Z (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wbr X (cv z) R) (wbr Y (cv w) S)))) $;

--| Condition for parallel product when the first argument is not an ordered pair. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem brpprod3b {z w: setvar} (R S X Y Z: class):
  $ wcel X cvv $ >
  $ wcel Y cvv $ >
  $ wcel Z cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr X (cop Y Z) (cpprod R S)) (wex z (wex w (w3a (wceq X (cop (cv z) (cv w))) (wbr (cv z) Y R) (wbr (cv w) Z S)))) $;

--| The subset class is a binary relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem relsset:
  $ wrel csset $;

--| For sets, the ` SSet ` binary relation is equivalent to the subset relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem brsset (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csset) (wss A2 B2) $;

--| ` _I ` is equal to the intersection of ` SSet ` and its converse. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem idsset:
  $ wceq cid (cin csset (ccnv csset)) $;

--| Membership in the class of all transitive sets. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Mar-2012.)
theorem eltrans (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 ctrans) (wtr A2) $;

--| A quantifier-free definition of ` On ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Apr-2012.)
theorem dfon3:
  $ wceq con0 (cdif cvv (crn (cdif (cin csset (cxp ctrans cvv)) (cun cid cep)))) $;

--| Another quantifier-free definition of ` On ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-May-2014.)
theorem dfon4:
  $ wceq con0 (cdif cvv (cima (cdif csset (cun cid cep)) ctrans)) $;

--| Expansion of a common form used in quantifier-free definitions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brtxpsd {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wn (wbr A2 B2 (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp R cvv))))) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wbr (cv x3) A2 R))) $;

--| Another common abbreviation for quantifier-free definitions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Apr-2014.)
theorem brtxpsd2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C R: class x3) (S: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq R (cdif C (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp S cvv)))) $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 C $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wbr (cv x3) A2 S))) $;

--| A third common abbreviation for quantifier-free definitions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem brtxpsd3 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C R: class x3) (S X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq R (cdif C (crn (csymdif (ctxp cvv cep) (ctxp S cvv)))) $ >
  $ wbr A2 B2 C $ >
  $ wb (wcel (cv x3) X) (wbr (cv x3) A2 S) $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wceq B2 X) $;

--| The ` Bigcup ` relationship is a relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem relbigcup:
  $ wrel cbigcup $;

--| Binary relation over ` Bigcup ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem brbigcup (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cbigcup) (wceq (cuni A2) B2) $;

--| ` Bigcup ` using maps-to notation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem dfbigcup2 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cbigcup (cmpt x3 cvv (cuni (cv x3))) $;

--| ` Bigcup ` maps the universe onto itself. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem fobigcup:
  $ wfo cvv cvv cbigcup $;

--| ` Bigcup ` is a function over the universal class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem fnbigcup:
  $ wfn cbigcup cvv $;

--| For sets, ` Bigcup ` yields union. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem fvbigcup (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 cbigcup) (cuni A2) $;

--| Membership in the fixpoints of a class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem elfix (A2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cfix R)) (wbr A2 A2 R) $;

--| Alternative membership in the fixpoint of a class. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem elfix2 (A2 R: class):
  $ wrel R $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cfix R)) (wbr A2 A2 R) $;

--| The fixpoints of a class in terms of its range. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem dffix2 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfix A2) (crn (cin A2 cid)) $;

--| The fixpoints of a class are a subset of its domain. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem fixssdm (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfix A2) (cdm A2) $;

--| The fixpoints of a class are a subset of its range. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem fixssrn (A2: class):
  $ wss (cfix A2) (crn A2) $;

--| The fixpoints of a class are the same as those of its converse. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem fixcnv (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfix A2) (cfix (ccnv A2)) $;

--| The fixpoint operator distributes over union. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem fixun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cfix (cun A2 B2)) (cun (cfix A2) (cfix B2)) $;

--| Membership in the class of all limit ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem ellimits (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 climits) (wlim A2) $;

--| The class of all limit ordinals is a subclass of the class of all ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem limitssson:
  $ wss climits con0 $;

--| A quantifier-free definition of ` _om ` that does not depend on ~ ax-inf . (Note: label was changed from ~ dfom5 to ~ dfom5b to prevent naming conflict. NM, 12-Feb-2013.) (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2012.)
theorem dfom5b:
  $ wceq com (cin con0 (cint climits)) $;

--| A condition for subset and composition with identity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Apr-2018.)
theorem sscoid (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wss A2 (ccom cid B2)) (wa (wrel A2) (wss A2 B2)) $;

--| Another potential definition of functionality. Based on statements in ~ http://people.math.gatech.edu/~~belinfan/research/autoreas/otter/sum/fs/ . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Aug-2017.)
theorem dffun10 (F: class):
  $ wb (wfun F) (wss F (ccom cid (cdif cvv (ccom (cdif cvv cid) F)))) $;

--| Membership in the class of all functions. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Feb-2013.)
theorem elfuns (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel F cfuns) (wfun F) $;

--| Closed form of ~ elfuns . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-May-2014.)
theorem elfunsg (F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F cfuns) (wfun F)) $;

--| The binary relation form of the singleton function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brsingle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csingle) (wceq B2 (csn A2)) $;

--| Membership in the class of all singletons. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
theorem elsingles {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 csingles) (wex x3 (wceq A2 (csn (cv x3)))) $;

--| The singleton relationship is a function over the universe. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fnsingle:
  $ wfn csingle cvv $;

--| The value of the singleton function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Scott Fenton, 13-Apr-2018.)
theorem fvsingle (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 csingle) (csn A2) $;

--| Alternate definition of the class of all singletons. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem dfsingles2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csingles (cab x3 (wex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csn (cv y1))))) $;

--| A singleton is a member of the class of all singletons. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
theorem snelsingles (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (csn A2) csingles $;

--| A definition of iota using minimal quantifiers. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
theorem dfiota3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (cio x3 ph) (cuni (cuni (cin (csn (cab x3 ph)) csingles))) $;

--| Another quantifier-free definition of function value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Feb-2013.)
theorem dffv5 (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 F) (cuni (cuni (cin (csn (cima F (csn A2))) csingles))) $;

--| Express union of singleton in terms of ` if ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem unisnif (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cuni (csn A2)) (cif (wcel A2 cvv) A2 c0) $;

--| Binary relation form of the Image functor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brimage (A2 B2 R: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (cimage R)) (wceq B2 (cima R A2)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ brimage . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brimageg (A2 B2 R V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 (cimage R)) (wceq B2 (cima R A2))) $;

--| ` Image A ` is a function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funimage (A2: class):
  $ wfun (cimage A2) $;

--| ` Image R ` is a function over the set-like portion of ` R ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fnimage {x3: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wfn (cimage R) (cab x3 (wcel (cima R (cv x3)) cvv)) $;

--| The image functor in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem imageval {x3: setvar} (R: class):
  $ wceq (cimage R) (cmpt x3 cvv (cima R (cv x3))) $;

--| Value of the image functor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fvimage (A2 R V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cima R A2) W)) (wceq (cfv A2 (cimage R)) (cima R A2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the cartesian product operator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brcart (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C ccart) (wceq C (cxp A2 B2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the domain function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brdomain (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 cdomain) (wceq B2 (cdm A2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the range function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brrange (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 crange) (wceq B2 (crn A2)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ brdomain . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-May-2014.)
theorem brdomaing (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cdomain) (wceq B2 (cdm A2))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ brrange . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2014.)
theorem brrangeg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 crange) (wceq B2 (crn A2))) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Img ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem brimg (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C cimg) (wceq C (cima A2 B2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Apply ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem brapply (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C capply) (wceq C (cfv B2 A2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Cup ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brcup (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C ccup) (wceq C (cun A2 B2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Cap ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brcap (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C ccap) (wceq C (cin A2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for unfolding different forms of the ` Succ ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem lemsuccf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) (ctxp cid csingle)) (wbr (cv x3) B2 ccup))) (wceq B2 (csuc A2)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Succ ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2014.)
theorem brsuccf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 csuccf) (wceq B2 (csuc A2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of Scott Fenton's version of ` Succ ` , cf. ~ df-sucmap . (Contributed by Peter Mazsa, 6-Jan-2026.)
theorem dfsuccf2 {m n: setvar}:
  $ wceq csuccf (copab m n (wceq (csuc (cv m)) (cv n))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ funpartfun . Show membership in the restriction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem funpartlem {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm (cin (ccom (cimage F) csingle) (cxp cvv csingles)))) (wex x3 (wceq (cima F (csn A2)) (csn (cv x3)))) $;

--| The functional part of ` F ` is a function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem funpartfun (F: class):
  $ wfun (cfunpart F) $;

--| The functional part of ` F ` is a subset of ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funpartss (F: class):
  $ wss (cfunpart F) F $;

--| The function value of the functional part is identical to the original functional value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funpartfv (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 (cfunpart F)) (cfv A2 F) $;

--| The full functional part of ` F ` is a function over ` _V ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fullfunfnv (F: class):
  $ wfn (cfullfn F) cvv $;

--| The function value of the full function of ` F ` agrees with ` F ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fullfunfv (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cfv A2 (cfullfn F)) (cfv A2 F) $;

--| A binary relation form condition for the full function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brfullfun (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 (cfullfn F)) (wceq B2 (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the ` Restrict ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brrestrict (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) C crestrict) (wceq C (cres A2 B2)) $;

--| A quantifier-free definition of ` recs ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jul-2020.)
theorem dfrecs2 (F: class):
  $ wceq (crecs F) (cuni (cdif (cin cfuns (cima (ccnv cdomain) con0)) (cdm (cdif (ccom (ccnv cep) cdomain) (cfix (ccom (ccnv capply) (ccom (cfullfn F) crestrict))))))) $;

--| A quantifier-free definition of the recursive definition generator. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem dfrdg4 (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (crdg F A2) (cuni (cdif (cin cfuns (cima (ccnv cdomain) con0)) (cdm (cdif (ccom (ccnv cep) cdomain) (cfix (ccom (ccnv capply) (cun (cxp (cxp cvv (csn c0)) (csn (cuni (csn A2)))) (cun (cres (ccom cbigcup cimg) (cxp cvv climits)) (cres (ccom (cfullfn F) (ccom capply (cpprod cid cbigcup))) (cxp cvv (crn csuccf))))))))))) $;

--| Quantifier-free definition of class intersection. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Apr-2018.)
theorem dfint3 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cint A2) (cdif cvv (cima (ccnv (cdif cvv cep)) A2)) $;

--| The Image functor applied to the converse of the subset relationship yields a subset of the subset relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2018.)
theorem imagesset:
  $ wss (cimage (ccnv csset)) csset $;

--| Binary relation form of the upper bound functor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2018.)
theorem brub {x3: setvar} (A2 R S: class):
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr S A2 (cub R)) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 R) S) $;

--| Binary relation form of the lower bound functor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-May-2018.)
theorem brlb {x3: setvar} (A2 R S: class):
  $ wcel S cvv $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr S A2 (clb R)) (wral x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) R) S) $;

--| Alternate quantifier-free definition of a well-founded relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Aug-2026.)
theorem dffr7 (A2 R: class):
  $ wb (wfr A2 R) (wss (cdif (cpw A2) (csn c0)) (cfix (ccom cep (cdif cvv (ccom R (ccnv cep)))))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for an alternate ordered pair.
term caltop (A2 B2: class): class;

--| Declare the syntax for an alternate Cartesian product.
term caltxp (A2 B2: class): class;

--| An alternative definition of ordered pairs. This definition removes a hypothesis from its defining theorem (see ~ altopth ), making it more convenient in some circumstances. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
axiom df_altop (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (caltop A2 B2) (cpr (csn A2) (cpr A2 (csn B2))) $;

--| Define Cartesian products of alternative ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Mar-2012.)
axiom df_altxp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (caltxp A2 B2) (cab z (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv z) (caltop (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) A2)) $;

--| Alternative ordered pairs always exist. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel (caltop A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Two alternate ordered pairs are equal iff the singletons of their respective elements are equal. Note that this holds regardless of sethood of any of the elements. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Apr-2012.)
theorem altopthsn (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq (csn A2) (csn C)) (wceq (csn B2) (csn D))) $;

--| Equality for alternate ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopeq12 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D)) (wceq (caltop A2 C) (caltop B2 D)) $;

--| Equality for alternate ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopeq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (caltop A2 C) (caltop B2 C)) $;

--| Equality for alternate ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopeq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (caltop C A2) (caltop C B2)) $;

--| Equality of the first members of equal alternate ordered pairs, which holds regardless of the second members' sethood. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopth1 (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wceq A2 C)) $;

--| Equality of the second members of equal alternate ordered pairs, which holds regardless of the first members' sethood. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopth2 (A2 B2 C D V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wi (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair theorem. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopthg (A2 B2 C D V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair theorem. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2012.)
theorem altopthbg (A2 B2 C D V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel D W)) (wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| The alternate ordered pair theorem. If two alternate ordered pairs are equal, their first elements are equal and their second elements are equal. Note that ` C ` and ` D ` are not required to be a set due to a peculiarity of our specific ordered pair definition, as opposed to the regular ordered pairs used here, which (as in ~ opth ), requires ` D ` to be a set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopth (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair theorem with different sethood requirements. See ~ altopth for more comments. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2012.)
theorem altopthb (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair theorem with different sethood requirements. See ~ altopth for more comments. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2012.)
theorem altopthc (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair theorem with different sethood requirements. See ~ altopth for more comments. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Apr-2012.)
theorem altopthd (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel C cvv $ >
  $ wcel D cvv $ >
  $ wb (wceq (caltop A2 B2) (caltop C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D)) $;

--| Equality for alternate Cartesian products. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Mar-2012.)
theorem altxpeq1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (caltxp A2 C) (caltxp B2 C)) $;

--| Equality for alternate Cartesian products. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Mar-2012.)
theorem altxpeq2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (caltxp C A2) (caltxp C B2)) $;

--| Membership in alternate Cartesian products. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Mar-2012.)
theorem elaltxp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wb (wcel X (caltxp A2 B2)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq X (caltop (cv x3) (cv y1))) B2) A2) $;

--| Alternate ordered pair membership in a Cartesian product. Note that, unlike ~ opelxp , there is no sethood requirement here. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 22-Mar-2012.)
theorem altopelaltxp (A2 B2 X Y: class):
  $ wb (wcel (caltop X Y) (caltxp A2 B2)) (wa (wcel X A2) (wcel Y B2)) $;

--| An inclusion rule for alternate Cartesian products. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Mar-2012.)
theorem altxpsspw (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (caltxp A2 B2) (cpw (cpw (cun A2 (cpw B2)))) $;

--| The alternate Cartesian product of two sets is a set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Mar-2012.)
theorem altxpexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (caltxp A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Compute the rank of an alternate ordered pair. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Dec-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem rankaltopb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wceq (cfv (caltop A2 B2) crnk) (csuc (csuc (cun (cfv A2 crnk) (csuc (cfv B2 crnk)))))) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for alternate ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Sep-2015.)
theorem nfaltop {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (caltop A2 B2) $;

--| Distribution of class substitution over alternate ordered pairs. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Sep-2015.)
theorem sbcaltop {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (C D: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cvv) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (caltop C D)) (caltop (csb x3 A2 C) (csb x3 A2 D))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the outer five segment configuration.
term cofs: class;

--| The outer five segment configuration is an abbreviation for the conditions of the Five Segment Axiom ( ~ ax5seg ). See ~ brofs and ~ 5segofs for how it is used. Definition 2.10 of [Schwabhauser] p. 28. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Sep-2013.)
axiom df_ofs {x3 y1 z w n q p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cofs (copab p q (wrex n (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cop (cv z) (cv w)))) (w3a (wa (wbr (cv b) (cop (cv a) (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cv y1) (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv b) (cv c)) (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv d)) (cop (cv x3) (cv w)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv b) (cv d)) (cop (cv y1) (cv w)) ccgr)))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ axcgrrflx . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrrflx2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop B2 A2) ccgr) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ axcgrtr . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrtr4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ axcgrtr . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrtr4and (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr) $;

--| Reflexivity law for congruence. Theorem 2.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 27. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrrflx (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 B2) ccgr) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrrflx . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrrflxd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 B2) ccgr) $;

--| Congruence commutes on the two sides. Implication version. Theorem 2.2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 27. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrcomim (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) $;

--| Congruence commutes between the two sides. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrcom (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrcom . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrcomand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop C D) (cop A2 B2) ccgr) $;

--| Transitivity law for congruence. Theorem 2.3 of [Schwabhauser] p. 27. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Sep-2013.)
theorem cgrtr (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr)) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrtr . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrtrand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) $;

--| Transitivity law for congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrtr3 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr)) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrtr3 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrtr3and (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $;

--| Congruence commutes on the left. Biconditional version of Theorem 2.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 27. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrcoml (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 A2) (cop C D) ccgr)) $;

--| Congruence commutes on the right. Biconditional version of Theorem 2.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 27. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrcomr (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D C) ccgr)) $;

--| Congruence commutes on both sides. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrcomlr (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 A2) (cop D C) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrcoml . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrcomland (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop B2 A2) (cop C D) ccgr) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrcoml . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrcomrand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D C) ccgr) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrcomlr . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrcomlrand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop B2 A2) (cop D C) ccgr) $;

--| Degenerate segments are congruent. Theorem 2.8 of [Schwabhauser] p. 28. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrtriv (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr (cop A2 A2) (cop B2 B2) ccgr) $;

--| Identity law for congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrid2 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr (cop A2 A2) (cop B2 C) ccgr) (wceq B2 C)) $;

--| Two congruent segments are either both degenerate or both nondegenerate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrdegen (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wceq C D))) $;

--| Binary relation form of the outer five segment predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Sep-2013.)
theorem brofs (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cofs) (w3a (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr F (cop E G) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop F G) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop E H) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 D) (cop F H) ccgr)))) $;

--| Rephrase ~ ax5seg using the outer five segment predicate. Theorem 2.10 of [Schwabhauser] p. 28. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Sep-2013.)
theorem _5segofs (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cofs) (wne A2 B2)) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr)) $;

--| The outer five segment predicate commutes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Sep-2013.)
theorem ofscom (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cofs) (wbr (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) cofs)) $;

--| Link congruence over a pair of line segments. Theorem 2.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 29. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem cgrextend (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D F) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D E) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop E F) ccgr))) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop D F) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ cgrextend . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrextendand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr E (cop D F) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D E) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop E F) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop D F) ccgr) $;

--| Two points that satisfy the conclusion of ~ axsegcon are identical. Uniqueness portion of Theorem 2.12 of [Schwabhauser] p. 29. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem segconeq (A2 B2 C Q N X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel X (cfv N cee)) (wcel Y (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wne Q A2) (wa (wbr A2 (cop Q X) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 X) (cop B2 C) ccgr)) (wa (wbr A2 (cop Q Y) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 Y) (cop B2 C) ccgr))) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Existential uniqueness version of ~ segconeq . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem segconeu {r: setvar} (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee))) (wne C D))) (wreu r (wa (wbr D (cop C (cv r)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv r)) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) (cfv N cee)) $;

--| Betweenness always holds for the second endpoint. Theorem 3.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwntriv2 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr B2 (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) $;

--| Betweenness commutes. Implication version. Theorem 3.2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwncomim (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr A2 (cop C B2) cbtwn)) $;

--| Betweenness commutes. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwncom (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr A2 (cop C B2) cbtwn)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ btwncom . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwncomand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr A2 (cop C B2) cbtwn) $;

--| Betweenness always holds for the first endpoint. Theorem 3.3 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwntriv1 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr A2 (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) $;

--| If you can swap the first two arguments of a betweenness statement, then those arguments are identical. Theorem 3.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnswapid (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| If you can swap arguments one and three of a betweenness statement, then those arguments are identical. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnswapid2 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 A2) cbtwn)) (wceq A2 C)) $;

--| Inner transitivity law for betweenness. Left-hand side of Theorem 3.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnintr (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn)) $;

--| Exchange the first endpoint in betweenness. Left-hand side of Theorem 3.6 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnexch3 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ btwnexch3 . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnexch3and (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn) $;

--| Outer transitivity law for betweenness. Left-hand side of Theorem 3.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnouttr2 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wne B2 C) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) $;

--| Exchange the outer point of two betweenness statements. Right-hand side of Theorem 3.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnexch2 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) $;

--| Outer transitivity law for betweenness. Right-hand side of Theorem 3.7 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Jun-2013.)
theorem btwnouttr (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wne B2 C) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) $;

--| Outer transitivity law for betweenness. Right-hand side of Theorem 3.6 of [Schwabhauser] p. 30. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Sep-2013.)
theorem btwnexch (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ btwnexch . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnexchand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn) $;

--| There is always a ` c ` distinct from ` B ` such that ` B ` lies between ` A ` and ` c ` . Theorem 3.14 of [Schwabhauser] p. 32. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Sep-2013.)
theorem btwndiff {c: setvar} (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wrex c (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wne B2 (cv c))) (cfv N cee)) $;

--| A line segment between two sides of a triange intersects a segment crossing from the remaining side to the opposite vertex. Theorem 3.17 of [Schwabhauser] p. 33. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Sep-2013.)
theorem trisegint {q: setvar} (A2 B2 C D P E N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel P (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D C) cbtwn) (wbr P (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wrex q (wa (wbr (cv q) (cop P C) cbtwn) (wbr (cv q) (cop B2 E) cbtwn)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the segment transport function.
term ctransport: class;

--| Define the segment transport function. See ~ fvtransport for an explanation of the function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_transport {x3 n r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctransport (coprab p q x3 (wrex n (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) (cxp (cfv (cv n) cee) (cfv (cv n) cee))) (wcel (cv q) (cxp (cfv (cv n) cee) (cfv (cv n) cee))) (wne (cfv (cv q) c1st) (cfv (cv q) c2nd))) (wceq (cv x3) (crio r (wa (wbr (cfv (cv q) c2nd) (cop (cfv (cv q) c1st) (cv r)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cfv (cv q) c2nd) (cv r)) (cv p) ccgr)) (cfv (cv n) cee)))) cn)) $;

--| The ` TransportTo ` relationship is a function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funtransport:
  $ wfun ctransport $;

--| Calculate the value of the ` TransportTo ` function. This function takes four points, ` A ` through ` D ` , where ` C ` and ` D ` are distinct. It then returns the point that extends ` C D ` by the length of ` A B ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fvtransport {r: setvar} (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee))) (wne C D))) (wceq (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ctransport) (crio r (wa (wbr D (cop C (cv r)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv r)) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Closure law for segment transport. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem transportcl (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee))) (wne C D))) (wcel (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ctransport) (cfv N cee)) $;

--| Calculate the defining properties of the transport function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem transportprops (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee))) (wne C D))) (wa (wbr D (cop C (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ctransport)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (co (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ctransport)) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the inner five segment predicate.
term cifs: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the three place congruence predicate.
term ccgr3: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the colinearity predicate.
term ccolin: class;

--| Declare the syntax for the five segment predicate.
term cfs: class;

--| The colinearity predicate states that the three points in its arguments sit on one line. Definition 4.10 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_colinear {n a b c: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccolin (ccnv (coprab b c a (wrex n (wa (w3a (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv (cv n) cee))) (w3o (wbr (cv a) (cop (cv b) (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cv b) (cop (cv c) (cv a)) cbtwn) (wbr (cv c) (cop (cv a) (cv b)) cbtwn))) cn))) $;

--| The inner five segment configuration is an abbreviation for another congruence condition. See ~ brifs and ~ ifscgr for how it is used. Definition 4.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 34. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Sep-2013.)
axiom df_ifs {x3 y1 z w n q p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cifs (copab p q (wrex n (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cop (cv z) (cv w)))) (w3a (wa (wbr (cv b) (cop (cv a) (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cv y1) (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv c)) (cop (cv x3) (cv z)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv b) (cv c)) (cop (cv y1) (cv z)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv d)) (cop (cv x3) (cv w)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv d)) (cop (cv z) (cv w)) ccgr)))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn)) $;

--| The three place congruence predicate. This is an abbreviation for saying that all three pair in a triple are congruent with each other. Three place form of Definition 4.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 35. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_cgr3 {e f2 n q p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccgr3 (copab p q (wrex n (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex e (wrex f2 (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cop (cv b) (cv c)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cv d) (cop (cv e) (cv f2)))) (w3a (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv d) (cv e)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv c)) (cop (cv d) (cv f2)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv b) (cv c)) (cop (cv e) (cv f2)) ccgr))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn)) $;

--| The general five segment configuration is a generalization of the outer and inner five segment configurations. See ~ brfs and ~ fscgr for its use. Definition 4.15 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_fs {x3 y1 z w n q p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfs (copab p q (wrex n (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (wrex w (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv d)))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cop (cv z) (cv w)))) (w3a (wbr (cv a) (cop (cv b) (cv c)) ccolin) (wbr (cop (cv a) (cop (cv b) (cv c))) (cop (cv x3) (cop (cv y1) (cv z))) ccgr3) (wa (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv d)) (cop (cv x3) (cv w)) ccgr) (wbr (cop (cv b) (cv d)) (cop (cv y1) (cv w)) ccgr)))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the inner five segment predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Sep-2013.)
theorem brifs (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cifs) (w3a (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr F (cop E G) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop E G) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop F G) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop E H) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr)))) $;

--| Inner five segment congruence. Take two triangles, ` A D C ` and ` E H G ` , with ` B ` between ` A ` and ` C ` and ` F ` between ` E ` and ` G ` . If the other components of the triangles are congruent, then so are ` B D ` and ` F H ` . Theorem 4.2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 34. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Sep-2013.)
theorem ifscgr (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cifs) (wbr (cop B2 D) (cop F H) ccgr)) $;

--| Removing identical parts from the end of a line segment preserves congruence. Theorem 4.3 of [Schwabhauser] p. 35. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrsub (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D F) cbtwn)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop D F) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop E F) ccgr))) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D E) ccgr)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the three-place congruence predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem brcgr3 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (w3a (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D E) ccgr) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop D F) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop E F) ccgr))) $;

--| Permutation law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3permute3 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop B2 (cop C A2)) (cop E (cop F D)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Permutation law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3permute1 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop A2 (cop C B2)) (cop D (cop F E)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Permutation law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3permute2 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop B2 (cop A2 C)) (cop E (cop D F)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Permutation law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3permute4 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop C (cop A2 B2)) (cop F (cop D E)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Permutation law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3permute5 (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop C (cop B2 A2)) (cop F (cop E D)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Transitivity law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3tr4 (A2 B2 C D E F G H I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)) (wcel I (cfv N cee))))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop G (cop H I)) ccgr3)) (wbr (cop D (cop E F)) (cop G (cop H I)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Commutativity law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3com (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3) (wbr (cop D (cop E F)) (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) ccgr3)) $;

--| Identity law for three-place congruence. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgr3rflx (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) ccgr3) $;

--| A line segment can be divided at the same place as a congruent line segment is divided. Theorem 4.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 35. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem cgrxfr {e: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop D F) ccgr)) (wrex e (wa (wbr (cv e) (cop D F) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop (cv e) F)) ccgr3)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| A condition for extending betweenness to a new set of points based on congruence with another set of points. Theorem 4.6 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 4-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnxfr (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3)) (wbr E (cop D F) cbtwn)) $;

--| Colinearity is a relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem colinrel:
  $ wrel ccolin $;

--| Alternate colinearity binary relation. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem brcolinear2 {n: setvar} (P Q R: class) (V W: class n):
  $ wi (wa (wcel Q V) (wcel R W)) (wb (wbr P (cop Q R) ccolin) (wrex n (wa (w3a (wcel P (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel Q (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel R (cfv (cv n) cee))) (w3o (wbr P (cop Q R) cbtwn) (wbr Q (cop R P) cbtwn) (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn))) cn)) $;

--| The binary relation form of the colinearity predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem brcolinear (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (w3o (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop C A2) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn))) $;

--| The colinear predicate exists. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem colinearex:
  $ wcel ccolin cvv $;

--| If ` A ` is colinear with ` B ` and ` C ` , then ` A ` is in the same space as ` B ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem colineardim1 (A2 B2 C N V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C W))) (wi (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Permutation law for colinearity. Part of theorem 4.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearperm1 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr A2 (cop C B2) ccolin)) $;

--| Permutation law for colinearity. Part of theorem 4.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearperm3 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr B2 (cop C A2) ccolin)) $;

--| Permutation law for colinearity. Part of theorem 4.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearperm2 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) ccolin)) $;

--| Permutation law for colinearity. Part of theorem 4.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearperm4 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr C (cop A2 B2) ccolin)) $;

--| Permutation law for colinearity. Part of theorem 4.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 36. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearperm5 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr C (cop B2 A2) ccolin)) $;

--| Trivial case of colinearity. Theorem 4.12 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colineartriv1 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr A2 (cop A2 B2) ccolin) $;

--| Trivial case of colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem colineartriv2 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr A2 (cop B2 B2) ccolin) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwncolinear1 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin)) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwncolinear2 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr A2 (cop C B2) ccolin)) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwncolinear3 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) ccolin)) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwncolinear4 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop C A2) ccolin)) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwncolinear5 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 B2) ccolin)) $;

--| Betweenness implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwncolinear6 (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop B2 A2) ccolin)) $;

--| Transfer law for colinearity. Theorem 4.13 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem colinearxfr (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) ccolin) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E F)) ccgr3)) (wbr E (cop D F) ccolin)) $;

--| Extend a line with a missing point. Theorem 4.14 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem lineext {f2: setvar} (A2 B2 C D E N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop D E) ccgr)) (wrex f2 (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop D (cop E (cv f2))) ccgr3) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Change some conditions for outer five segment predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem brofs2 (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cofs) (w3a (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop E (cop F G)) ccgr3) (wa (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop E H) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 D) (cop F H) ccgr)))) $;

--| Change some conditions for inner five segment predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem brifs2 (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cifs) (w3a (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop E (cop F G)) ccgr3) (wa (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop E H) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr)))) $;

--| Binary relation form of the general five segment predicate. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem brfs (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cfs) (w3a (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wbr (cop A2 (cop B2 C)) (cop E (cop F G)) ccgr3) (wa (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop E H) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 D) (cop F H) ccgr)))) $;

--| Congruence law for the general five segment configuration. Theorem 4.16 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 5-Oct-2013.)
theorem fscgr (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (cop (cop E F) (cop G H)) cfs) (wne A2 B2)) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr)) $;

--| Congruence rule for lines. Theorem 4.17 of [Schwabhauser] p. 37. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 6-Oct-2013.)
theorem linecgr (A2 B2 C P Q N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 P) (cop A2 Q) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 P) (cop B2 Q) ccgr))) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C Q) ccgr)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ linecgr . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem linecgrand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C P Q N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 P) (cop A2 Q) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop B2 P) (cop B2 Q) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C Q) ccgr) $;

--| Identity law for points on lines. Theorem 4.18 of [Schwabhauser] p. 38. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem lineid (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin)) (wa (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop A2 D) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop B2 D) ccgr))) (wceq C D)) $;

--| Law for finding a point inside a segment. Theorem 4.19 of [Schwabhauser] p. 38. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem idinside (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop A2 D) ccgr) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop B2 D) ccgr)) (wceq C D)) $;

--| If ` A ` , ` B ` , and ` C ` fall in order on a line, and ` A B ` and ` A C ` are congruent, then ` C = B ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Oct-2013.)
theorem endofsegid (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 C) (cop A2 B2) ccgr)) (wceq C B2)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ endofsegid . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem endofsegidand (ph ps: wff) (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cfv N cee)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 C) ccgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wceq B2 C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . The next several lemmas introduce various properties of hypothetical points that end up eliminating alternatives to connectivity. We begin by showing a congruence property of those hypothetical points. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem1 (A2 B2 C D N X: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel X (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C)) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 X) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) X) (cop D B2) ccgr))))) (wbr (cop B2 (cv c)) (cop X C) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now, we show that two of the hypotheticals we introduced in the first lemma are identical. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem2 (A2 B2 C D N X: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel X (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C)) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 X) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) X) (cop D B2) ccgr))))) (wceq X (cv b)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Establish the next congruence in the series. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem3 (A2 B2 C D N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C)) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr))))) (wbr (cop B2 (cv d)) (cop (cv b) D) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Assuming ` C =/= c ` , we now attempt to force ` D = d ` from here out via a series of congruences. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem4 (A2 B2 C D N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr))))) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv c)) (cop D C) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now, we introduce ` E ` , the intersection of ` C c ` and ` D d ` . We begin by showing that it is the midpoint of ` C ` and ` c ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem5 (A2 B2 C D E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)))) (wbr (cop E C) (cop E (cv c)) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Next, we show that ` E ` is the midpoint of ` D ` and ` d ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem6 (A2 B2 C D E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)))) (wbr (cop E D) (cop E (cv d)) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Under our assumptions, ` C ` and ` d ` are distinct. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem7 (A2 B2 C D E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)))) (wne C (cv d)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now, we introduce the last three points used in the construction: ` P ` , ` Q ` , and ` R ` will turn out to be equal further down, and will provide us with the key to the final statement. We begin by establishing congruence of ` R P ` and ` E d ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem8 (A2 B2 C D P Q R E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)) (w3a (wa (wbr C (cop (cv c) P) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C (cv d)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop (cv d) R) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C R) (cop C E) ccgr)) (wa (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop R P) ccgr)))))) (wbr (cop R P) (cop E (cv d)) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now, a quick use of transitivity to establish congruence on ` R Q ` and ` E D ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem9 (A2 B2 C D P Q R E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)) (w3a (wa (wbr C (cop (cv c) P) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C (cv d)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop (cv d) R) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C R) (cop C E) ccgr)) (wa (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop R P) ccgr)))))) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop E D) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now we establish a congruence that will give us ` D = d ` when we compute ` P = Q ` later on. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem10 (A2 B2 C D P Q R E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)) (w3a (wa (wbr C (cop (cv c) P) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C (cv d)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop (cv d) R) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C R) (cop C E) ccgr)) (wa (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop R P) ccgr)))))) (wbr (cop (cv d) D) (cop P Q) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Now, we establish that ` D ` and ` Q ` are equidistant from ` C ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem11 (A2 B2 C D P Q R E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)) (w3a (wa (wbr C (cop (cv c) P) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C (cv d)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop (cv d) R) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C R) (cop C E) ccgr)) (wa (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop R P) ccgr)))))) (wbr (cop D C) (cop Q C) ccgr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Using a long string of invocations of ~ linecgr , we show that ` D = d ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem12 (A2 B2 C D P Q R E N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)))) (wa (w3a (wa (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C) (wne C (cv c))) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr)))) (wa (wa (wbr E (cop C (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop D (cv d)) cbtwn)) (w3a (wa (wbr C (cop (cv c) P) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C P) (cop C (cv d)) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop (cv d) R) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C R) (cop C E) ccgr)) (wa (wbr R (cop P Q) cbtwn) (wbr (cop R Q) (cop R P) ccgr)))))) (wceq D (cv d)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Begin back-filling and eliminating hypotheses. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem13 (A2 B2 C D N: class) (b c d: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv c) (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel (cv d) (cfv N cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv N cee))))) (w3a (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C)) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn))) (wa (wa (wbr D (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop D (cv c)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (wa (wbr C (cop A2 (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop C (cv d)) (cop C D) ccgr))) (wa (wa (wbr (cv c) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv c) (cv b)) (cop C B2) ccgr)) (wa (wbr (cv d) (cop A2 (cv b)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv d) (cv b)) (cop D B2) ccgr))))) (wo (wceq C (cv c)) (wceq D (cv d))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ btwnconn1 . Final statement of the theorem when ` B =/= C ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1lem14 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wa (wa (wne A2 B2) (wne B2 C)) (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn)))) (wo (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn) (wbr D (cop A2 C) cbtwn)) $;

--| Connectitivy law for betweenness. Theorem 5.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 39-41. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn1 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wo (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn) (wbr D (cop A2 C) cbtwn))) $;

--| Another connectivity law for betweenness. Theorem 5.2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 41. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn2 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wo (wbr C (cop B2 D) cbtwn) (wbr D (cop B2 C) cbtwn))) $;

--| Inner connectivity law for betweenness. Theorem 5.3 of [Schwabhauser] p. 41. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Oct-2013.)
theorem btwnconn3 (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 D) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 D) cbtwn)) (wo (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr C (cop A2 B2) cbtwn))) $;

--| If two points fall in the same place in the middle of a segment, then they are identical. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem midofsegid (A2 B2 D E N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wbr D (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr E (cop A2 B2) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 D) (cop A2 E) ccgr)) (wceq D E)) $;

--| Generalization of ~ axsegcon . This time, we generate an endpoint for a segment on the ray ` Q A ` congruent to ` B C ` and starting at ` Q ` , as opposed to ~ axsegcon , where the segment starts at ` A ` (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.) Remove unneeded inequality. (Revised by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.)
theorem segcon2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C Q N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wrex x3 (wa (wo (wbr A2 (cop Q (cv x3)) cbtwn) (wbr (cv x3) (cop Q A2) cbtwn)) (wbr (cop Q (cv x3)) (cop B2 C) ccgr)) (cfv N cee)) $;

--| Declare the constant for the segment less than or equal to relationship.
term csegle: class;

--| Define the segment length comparison relationship. This relationship expresses that the segment ` A B ` is no longer than ` C D ` . In this section, we establish various properties of this relationship showing that it is a transitive, reflexive relationship on pairs of points that is substitutive under congruence. Definition 5.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 41. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_segle {y1 n q p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq csegle (copab p q (wrex n (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (cv q) (cop (cv c) (cv d))) (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cop (cv c) (cv d)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (cop (cv c) (cv y1)) ccgr)) (cfv (cv n) cee))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn)) $;

--| Binary relation form of the segment comparison relationship. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem brsegle {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cop C D) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C (cv y1)) ccgr)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Alternate characterization of segment comparison. Theorem 5.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 41-42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem brsegle2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr B2 (cop A2 (cv x3)) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 (cv x3)) (cop C D) ccgr)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Substitution law for segment comparison under congruence. Theorem 5.6 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem seglecgr12im (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wi (w3a (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle)) (wbr (cop E F) (cop G H) csegle)) $;

--| Substitution law for segment comparison under congruence. Biconditional version. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem seglecgr12 (A2 B2 C D E F G H N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel F (cfv N cee)) (wcel G (cfv N cee)) (wcel H (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) ccgr) (wbr (cop C D) (cop G H) ccgr)) (wb (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wbr (cop E F) (cop G H) csegle))) $;

--| Segment comparison is reflexive. Theorem 5.7 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem seglerflx (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 B2) csegle) $;

--| Any segment is at least as long as a degenerate segment. Theorem 5.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem seglemin (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wbr (cop A2 A2) (cop B2 C) csegle) $;

--| Segment less than is transitive. Theorem 5.8 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem segletr (A2 B2 C D E F N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel D (cfv N cee)) (wcel E (cfv N cee)) (wcel F (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wbr (cop C D) (cop E F) csegle)) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop E F) csegle)) $;

--| Antisymmetry law for segment comparison. Theorem 5.9 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem segleantisym (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wbr (cop C D) (cop A2 B2) csegle)) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) ccgr)) $;

--| Linearity law for segment comparison. Theorem 5.10 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 14-Oct-2013.)
theorem seglelin (A2 B2 C D N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel D (cfv N cee)))) (wo (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop C D) csegle) (wbr (cop C D) (cop A2 B2) csegle)) $;

--| If ` B ` falls between ` A ` and ` C ` , then ` A B ` is no longer than ` A C ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwnsegle (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 C) csegle)) $;

--| Given three colinear points ` A ` , ` B ` , and ` C ` , ` B ` falls in the middle iff the two segments to ` B ` are no longer than ` A C ` . Theorem 5.12 of [Schwabhauser] p. 42. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem colinbtwnle (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr A2 (cop B2 C) ccolin) (wb (wbr B2 (cop A2 C) cbtwn) (wa (wbr (cop A2 B2) (cop A2 C) csegle) (wbr (cop B2 C) (cop A2 C) csegle)))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the outside of constant.
term coutsideof: class;

--| The outside of relationship. This relationship expresses that ` P ` , ` A ` , and ` B ` fall on a line, but ` P ` is not on the segment ` A B ` . This definition is taken from theorem 6.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 43, since it requires no dummy variables. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_outsideof:
  $ wceq coutsideof (cdif ccolin cbtwn) $;

--| Binary relation form of ` OutsideOf ` . Theorem 6.4 of [Schwabhauser] p. 43. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem broutsideof (A2 B2 P: class):
  $ wb (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wa (wbr P (cop A2 B2) ccolin) (wn (wbr P (cop A2 B2) cbtwn))) $;

--| Alternate form of ` OutsideOf ` . Definition 6.1 of [Schwabhauser] p. 43. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem broutsideof2 (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (w3a (wne A2 P) (wne B2 P) (wo (wbr A2 (cop P B2) cbtwn) (wbr B2 (cop P A2) cbtwn)))) $;

--| Outsideness implies inequality. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsidene1 (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wne A2 P)) $;

--| Outsideness implies inequality. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsidene2 (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wne B2 P)) $;

--| A principle linking outsideness to betweenness. Theorem 6.2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 43. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem btwnoutside (A2 B2 C P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel P (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (w3a (wne A2 P) (wne B2 P) (wne C P)) (wbr P (cop A2 C) cbtwn)) (wb (wbr P (cop B2 C) cbtwn) (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof))) $;

--| Characterization of outsideness in terms of relationship to a fourth point. Theorem 6.3 of [Schwabhauser] p. 43. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem broutsideof3 {c: setvar} (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (w3a (wne A2 P) (wne B2 P) (wrex c (w3a (wne (cv c) P) (wbr P (cop A2 (cv c)) cbtwn) (wbr P (cop B2 (cv c)) cbtwn)) (cfv N cee)))) $;

--| Reflexivity of outsideness. Theorem 6.5 of [Schwabhauser] p. 44. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideofrflx (A2 P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee))) (wi (wne A2 P) (wbr P (cop A2 A2) coutsideof)) $;

--| Commutativity law for outsideness. Theorem 6.6 of [Schwabhauser] p. 44. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideofcom (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wb (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wbr P (cop B2 A2) coutsideof)) $;

--| Transitivity law for outsideness. Theorem 6.7 of [Schwabhauser] p. 44. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideoftr (A2 B2 C P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel P (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wbr P (cop B2 C) coutsideof)) (wbr P (cop A2 C) coutsideof)) $;

--| Uniqueness law for ` OutsideOf ` . Analogue of ~ segconeq . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideofeq (A2 B2 C R N X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee))) (w3a (wcel C (cfv N cee)) (wcel X (cfv N cee)) (wcel Y (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wa (wbr A2 (cop X R) coutsideof) (wbr (cop A2 X) (cop B2 C) ccgr)) (wa (wbr A2 (cop Y R) coutsideof) (wbr (cop A2 Y) (cop B2 C) ccgr))) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Given a nondegenerate ray, there is a unique point congruent to the segment ` B C ` lying on the ray ` A R ` . Theorem 6.11 of [Schwabhauser] p. 44. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 23-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideofeu {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee))) (wa (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel C (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wa (wne R A2) (wne B2 C)) (wreu x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cop (cv x3) R) coutsideof) (wbr (cop A2 (cv x3)) (cop B2 C) ccgr)) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Relate ` OutsideOf ` to ` Seg<_ ` . Theorem 6.13 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 24-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsidele (A2 B2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)))) (wi (wbr P (cop A2 B2) coutsideof) (wb (wbr (cop P A2) (cop P B2) csegle) (wbr A2 (cop P B2) cbtwn))) $;

--| Outside of implies colinearity. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem outsideofcol (P Q R: class):
  $ wi (wbr P (cop Q R) coutsideof) (wbr P (cop Q R) ccolin) $;

--| Declare the constant for the line function.
term cline2: class;

--| Declare the constant for the ray function.
term cray: class;

--| Declare the constant for the set of all lines.
term clines2: class;

--| Define the ` Line ` function. This function generates the line passing through the distinct points ` a ` and ` b ` . Adapted from definition 6.14 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_line2 {n a b l: setvar}:
  $ wceq cline2 (coprab a b l (wrex n (wa (w3a (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel (cv b) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wne (cv a) (cv b))) (wceq (cv l) (cec (cop (cv a) (cv b)) (ccnv ccolin)))) cn)) $;

--| Define the ` Ray ` function. This function generates the set of all points that lie on the ray starting at ` p ` and passing through ` a ` . Definition 6.8 of [Schwabhauser] p. 44. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_ray {x3 n r p a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cray (coprab p a r (wrex n (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wcel (cv a) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (wne (cv p) (cv a))) (wceq (cv r) (crab x3 (wbr (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv x3)) coutsideof) (cfv (cv n) cee)))) cn)) $;

--| Define the set of all lines. Definition 6.14, part 2 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. See ~ ellines for membership. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.)
axiom df_lines2:
  $ wceq clines2 (crn cline2) $;

--| Show that the ` Ray ` relationship is a function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funray:
  $ wfun cray $;

--| Calculate the value of the ` Ray ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fvray {x3: setvar} (A2 P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wne P A2))) (wceq (co P A2 cray) (crab x3 (wbr P (cop A2 (cv x3)) coutsideof) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| Show that the ` Line ` relationship is a function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem funline:
  $ wfun cline2 $;

--| When ` Line ` is applied with the same argument, the result is the empty set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linedegen (A2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 A2 cline2) c0 $;

--| Calculate the value of the ` Line ` function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fvline {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wne A2 B2))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cline2) (cab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cop A2 B2) ccolin))) $;

--| A line is a subset of the space its two points lie in. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem liness (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wne A2 B2))) (wss (co A2 B2 cline2) (cfv N cee)) $;

--| Alternate definition of a line. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 25-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem fvline2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv N cee)) (wcel B2 (cfv N cee)) (wne A2 B2))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cline2) (crab x3 (wbr (cv x3) (cop A2 B2) ccolin) (cfv N cee))) $;

--| A line is composed of a point and the two rays emerging from it. Theorem 6.15 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 26-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem lineunray (P Q R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wcel R (cfv N cee))) (wa (wne P Q) (wne P R))) (wi (wbr P (cop Q R) cbtwn) (wceq (co P Q cline2) (cun (cun (co P Q cray) (csn P)) (co P R cray)))) $;

--| If ` S ` lies on ` P Q ` , then ` P Q = P S ` . Theorem 6.16 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem lineelsb2 (P Q S N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q)) (wa (wcel S (cfv N cee)) (wne P S))) (wi (wcel S (co P Q cline2)) (wceq (co P Q cline2) (co P S cline2))) $;

--| Reflexivity law for line membership. Part of theorem 6.17 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linerflx1 (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q))) (wcel P (co P Q cline2)) $;

--| Commutativity law for lines. Part of theorem 6.17 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linecom (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q))) (wceq (co P Q cline2) (co Q P cline2)) $;

--| Reflexivity law for line membership. Part of theorem 6.17 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linerflx2 (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q))) (wcel Q (co P Q cline2)) $;

--| Membership in the set of all lines. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem ellines {n q p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 clines2) (wrex n (wrex p (wrex q (wa (wne (cv p) (cv q)) (wceq A2 (co (cv p) (cv q) cline2))) (cfv (cv n) cee)) (cfv (cv n) cee)) cn) $;

--| If ` A ` is a line containing two distinct points ` P ` and ` Q ` , then ` A ` is the line through ` P ` and ` Q ` . Theorem 6.18 of [Schwabhauser] p. 45. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linethru (A2 P Q: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 clines2) (wa (wcel P A2) (wcel Q A2)) (wne P Q)) (wceq A2 (co P Q cline2)) $;

--| There is a line through any two distinct points. Hilbert's axiom I.1 for geometry. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem hilbert1_1 {x3: setvar} (P Q: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q))) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wcel Q (cv x3))) clines2) $;

--| There is at most one line through any two distinct points. Hilbert's axiom I.2 for geometry. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Oct-2013.) (Revised by NM, 17-Jun-2017.)
theorem hilbert1_2 {x3: setvar} (P Q: class):
  $ wi (wne P Q) (wrmo x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wcel Q (cv x3))) clines2) $;

--| There is a unique line going through any two distinct points. Theorem 6.19 of [Schwabhauser] p. 46. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem linethrueu {x3: setvar} (P Q: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel P (cfv N cee)) (wcel Q (cfv N cee)) (wne P Q))) (wreu x3 (wa (wcel P (cv x3)) (wcel Q (cv x3))) clines2) $;

--| Two distinct lines intersect in at most one point. Theorem 6.21 of [Schwabhauser] p. 46. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Oct-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem lineintmo {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 clines2) (wcel B2 clines2) (wne A2 B2)) (wmo x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the forward difference operator.
term cfwddif: class;

--| Define the forward difference operator. This is a discrete analogue of the derivative operator. Definition 2.42 of [GramKnuthPat], p. 47. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-May-2020.)
axiom df_fwddif {x3 y1 f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfwddif (cmpt f2 (co cc cc cpm) (cmpt x3 (crab y1 (wcel (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) (cdm (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))) (co (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) cmin))) $;

--| Declare the syntax for the nth forward difference operator.
term cfwddifn: class;

--| Define the nth forward difference operator. This works out to be the forward difference operator iterated ` n ` times. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-May-2020.)
axiom df_fwddifn {x3 y1 f2 k n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfwddifn (cmpo n f2 cn0 (co cc cc cpm) (cmpt x3 (crab y1 (wral k (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv k) caddc) (cdm (cv f2))) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz)) cc) (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (co (cv n) (cv k) cbc) (co (co (cneg c1) (co (cv n) (cv k) cmin) cexp) (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) (cv f2)) cmul) cmul)))) $;

--| Calculate the value of the forward difference operator at a point. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-May-2020.)
theorem fwddifval (ph: wff) (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X c1 caddc) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv F cfwddif)) (co (cfv (co X c1 caddc) F) (cfv X F) cmin)) $;

--| The value of the forward difference operator at a point. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-May-2020.)
theorem fwddifnval {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wcel (co X (cv k) caddc) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co N F cfwddifn)) (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co N (cv k) cbc) (co (co (cneg c1) (co N (cv k) cmin) cexp) (cfv (co X (cv k) caddc) F) cmul) cmul))) $;

--| The value of the n-iterated forward difference operator at zero is just the function value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-May-2020.)
theorem fwddifn0 (ph: wff) (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co cc0 F cfwddifn)) (cfv X F)) $;

--| The value of the n-iterated forward difference at a successor. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 28-May-2020.)
theorem fwddifnp1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 (co N c1 caddc) cfz))) (wcel (co X (cv k) caddc) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co (co N c1 caddc) F cfwddifn)) (co (cfv (co X c1 caddc) (co N F cfwddifn)) (cfv X (co N F cfwddifn)) cmin)) $;

--| The rank of the empty set is ` (/) ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jul-2015.)
theorem rank0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 crnk) c0 $;

--| The only set with rank ` 1o ` is the singleton of the empty set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jul-2015.)
theorem rankeq1o (A2: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 crnk) c1o) (wceq A2 (csn c0)) $;

--| The class of all hereditarily finite sets is transitive. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.)
theorem hftr:
  $ wtr chf $;

--| Extensionality for HF sets depends only on comparison of HF elements. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.)
theorem hfext {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wral x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) chf)) $;

--| ` _om ` is not hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2015.)
theorem hfninf:
  $ wn (wcel com chf) $;

--| Declare the syntax for natural multiplication.
term cnmul: class;

--| Define natural ordinal multiplication. This is the corresponding operation to ~ df-nadd . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jun-2026.)
axiom df_nmul {x3 y1 z m p a b c d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnmul (cfrecs (cxp con0 con0) (copab x3 y1 (w3a (wcel (cv x3) (cxp con0 con0)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp con0 con0)) (w3a (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) cep) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd))) (wne (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (cmpo p m cvv cvv (csb a (cfv (cv p) c1st) (csb b (cfv (cv p) c2nd) (cint (crab z (wral c (wral d (wcel (co (co (cv c) (cv b) (cv m)) (co (cv a) (cv d) (cv m)) cnadd) (co (cv z) (co (cv c) (cv d) (cv m)) cnadd)) (cv b)) (cv a)) con0)))))) $;

--| Natural multiplication is a function over pairs of ordinals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulfn:
  $ wfn cnmul (cxp con0 con0) $;

--| Show closure and value of natural multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 2-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulprop {x3 a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel (co A2 B2 cnmul) con0) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnmul) (cint (crab x3 (wral a (wral b (wcel (co (co (cv a) B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv b) cnmul) cnadd) (co (cv x3) (co (cv a) (cv b) cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2) con0)))) $;

--| Closure law for natural multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cnmul) con0) $;

--| Show the value of natural multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulval {x3 a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnmul) (cint (crab x3 (wral a (wral b (wcel (co (co (cv a) B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv b) cnmul) cnadd) (co (cv x3) (co (cv a) (cv b) cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2) con0))) $;

--| Closure law for natural multiplication. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 12-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cnmul) con0) $;

--| Natural multiplication is commutative. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulcom (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co B2 A2 cnmul)) $;

--| Natural multiplication by zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmulr0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c0 cnmul) c0) $;

--| Natural multiplication by zero. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2026.)
theorem nmull0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c0 A2 cnmul) c0) $;

--| Identity law for natural multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulrid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co A2 c1o cnmul) A2) $;

--| Identity law for natural multiplication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmullid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wceq (co c1o A2 cnmul) A2) $;

--| An element of an ordinal number is an ordinal number. Theorem 2.2(iii) of [BellMachover] p. 469. Lemma 1.3 of [Schloeder] p. 1. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jul-2026.)
theorem onelond (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $;

--| Transitive law for ordinal numbers. Exercise 3 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 40. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jul-2026.)
theorem ontr2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $;

--| An element of an ordinal number is a subset of the number. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jul-2026.)
theorem onelssd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $;

--| Natural multiplication by zero. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulr0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c0 cnmul) c0) $;

--| Natural multiplication by zero. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmull0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c0 A2 cnmul) c0) $;

--| Identity law for natural multiplication. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulridd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c1o cnmul) A2) $;

--| Identity law for natural multiplication. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmullidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1o A2 cnmul) A2) $;

--| Natural multiplication commutes. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co B2 A2 cnmul)) $;

--| Identity law for natural addition. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem naddridd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c0 cnadd) A2) $;

--| Identity law for natural addition. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem naddlidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c0 A2 cnadd) A2) $;

--| Natural addition commutes. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem naddcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cnadd) (co B2 A2 cnadd)) $;

--| Natural addition associates. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem naddassd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnadd)) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the last two terms in a triple sum. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 30-Jul-2026.)
theorem nadd32d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnadd) (co (co A2 C cnadd) B2 cnadd)) $;

--| Ordering relationship for natural ordinal operations. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmuladdel (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D B2))) (wcel (co (co C B2 cnmul) (co A2 D cnmul) cnadd) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co C D cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Ordering relationship for natural ordinal operations. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmuladdss (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0)) (wa (wcel C con0) (wcel D con0)) (wa (wss C A2) (wss D B2))) (wss (co (co C B2 cnmul) (co A2 D cnmul) cnadd) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co C D cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Natural multiplication preserves less-than or equal. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulss1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wss A2 B2)) (wss (co C A2 cnmul) (co C B2 cnmul)) $;

--| Natural multiplication by a non-zero number preserves less-than. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulel1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wne C c0))) (wcel (co C A2 cnmul) (co C B2 cnmul)) $;

--| Characterize less-than a natural product. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 15-Jul-2026.)
theorem ltnmul {b c: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wcel A2 (co B2 C cnmul)) (wrex b (wrex c (wss (co A2 (co (cv b) (cv c) cnmul) cnadd) (co (co (cv b) C cnmul) (co B2 (cv c) cnmul) cnadd)) C) B2)) $;

--| A condition for bounding a natural product above. Converse of ~ ltnmul . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Jul-2026.)
theorem nmulle {a b: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wss (co A2 B2 cnmul) C) (wral a (wral b (wcel (co (co (cv a) B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv b) cnmul) cnadd) (co C (co (cv a) (cv b) cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2)) $;

--| Condition for bounding a natural sum below. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem ltnadd {b c: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wcel A2 (co B2 C cnadd)) (wo (wrex b (wss A2 (co (cv b) C cnadd)) B2) (wrex c (wss A2 (co B2 (cv c) cnadd)) C))) $;

--| Condition for bounding natural addition above. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Jul-2026.)
theorem naddle {a b: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wb (wss (co A2 B2 cnadd) C) (wa (wral a (wcel (co (cv a) B2 cnadd) C) A2) (wral b (wcel (co A2 (cv b) cnadd) C) B2))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nadddi . Prove a subcase of the reverse implication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jul-2026.)
theorem nadddilem1 {f2 d: setvar} (ph: wff f2 d) (A2 B2 C: class) (Y: class f2 d):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wceq (co A2 (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel Y (co A2 C cnmul))) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) Y cnadd) (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nadddi . Prove the reverse implication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 31-Jul-2026.)
theorem nadddilem2 {e f2 d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 d) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wceq (co A2 (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 (cv e) cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wceq (co A2 (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral e (wceq (co (cv d) (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) (cv e) cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd) (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nadddi . Prove a subcase of the forward implication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2026.)
theorem nadddilem3 {e d: setvar} (ph: wff e d) (A2 B2 C X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co B2 C cnadd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Y (co Z C cnadd)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wceq (co A2 (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 (cv e) cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral e (wceq (co (cv d) (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) (cv e) cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (co X (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co A2 Y cnmul) cnadd) (co (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd) (co X Y cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nadddi . Prove the forward implication. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2026.)
theorem nadddilem4 {e f2 d: setvar} (ph: wff e f2 d) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wceq (co A2 (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 (cv e) cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wceq (co A2 (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral e (wceq (co (cv d) (co (cv e) C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) (cv e) cnmul) (co (cv d) C cnmul) cnadd)) B2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral d (wral f2 (wceq (co (cv d) (co B2 (cv f2) cnadd) cnmul) (co (co (cv d) B2 cnmul) (co (cv d) (cv f2) cnmul) cnadd)) C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Natural multiplication distributes over natural addition. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 27-Jul-2026.)
theorem nadddi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 con0) (wcel B2 con0) (wcel C con0)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Natural multiplication distributes over natural addition. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2026.)
theorem nadddid (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cnadd) cnmul) (co (co A2 B2 cnmul) (co A2 C cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Natural multiplication distributes over natural addition. Deduction form. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Aug-2026.)
theorem nadddird (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C con0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cnadd) C cnmul) (co (co A2 C cnmul) (co B2 C cnmul) cnadd)) $;

--| Equality inference for restricted at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem rmoeqi {x3: setvar} (ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wrmo x3 ps A2) (wrmo x3 ps B2) $;

--| Equality inference for restricted at-most-one quantifier. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem rmoeqbii {x3: setvar} (ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb (wrmo x3 ps A2) (wrmo x3 ch B2) $;

--| Equality inference for restricted existential uniqueness quantifier. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem reueqi {x3: setvar} (ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wreu x3 ps A2) (wreu x3 ps B2) $;

--| Equality inference for restricted existential uniqueness quantifier. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem reueqbii {x3: setvar} (ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb (wreu x3 ps A2) (wreu x3 ch B2) $;

--| Formula-building inference for class substitution. General version of ~ sbcbii . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem sbceqbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wsbc x3 ps B2) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint collection. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem disjeq1i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wdisj x3 A2 C) (wdisj x3 B2 C) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint collection. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem disjeq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wb (wdisj x3 A2 C) (wdisj x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality theorem for restricted class abstractions. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem rabeqbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wceq (crab x3 ph A2) (crab x3 ps B2) $;

--| Equality theorem for indexed union. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem iuneq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (ciun x3 A2 C) (ciun x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality theorem for indexed intersection. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem iineq1i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (ciin x3 A2 C) (ciin x3 B2 C) $;

--| Equality theorem for indexed intersection. Inference version. General version of ~ iineq1i . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem iineq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (ciin x3 A2 C) (ciin x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equivalent wff's and equal domains yield equal restricted iotas. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem riotaeqbii {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wceq (crio x3 ph A2) (crio x3 ps B2) $;

--| Equal domains yield equal restricted iotas. Inference version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem riotaeqi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (crio x3 ph A2) (crio x3 ph B2) $;

--| Equality inference for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ixpeq1i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 C) $;

--| Equality inference for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ixpeq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality inference for sum. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem sumeq2si {k: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (csu k A2 B2) (csu k A2 C) $;

--| Equality inference for sum. General version of ~ sumeq2si . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem sumeq12si {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (csu x3 A2 C) (csu x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality inference for product. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem prodeq2si {k: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (cprod k A2 B2) (cprod k A2 C) $;

--| Equality inference for product. General version of ~ prodeq2si . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem prodeq12si {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (cprod x3 A2 C) (cprod x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality inference for an integral. General version of ~ itgeq1i and ~ itgeq2i . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 D) $;

--| Equality inference for an integral. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq1i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C) $;

--| Equality inference for an integral. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq2i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C) $;

--| Equality inference for the directed integral. General version of ~ ditgeq12i and ~ ditgeq3i . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq123i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D E F: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq E F $ >
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 C E) (cdit x3 B2 D F) $;

--| Equality inference for the directed integral. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq12i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D E: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 C E) (cdit x3 B2 D E) $;

--| Equality inference for the directed integral. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq3i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wceq C D $ >
  $ wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) $;

--| Formula-building rule for restricted at-most-one quantifier. Deduction form. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem rmoeqdv {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrmo x3 ps A2) (wrmo x3 ps B2)) $;

--| Formula-building rule for restricted at-most-one quantifier. Deduction form. General version of ~ rmobidv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem rmoeqbidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrmo x3 ps A2) (wrmo x3 ch B2)) $;

--| Deduction substituting both sides of a biconditional. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem sbequbidv {x3 v u: setvar} (ph: wff v u) (ps ch: wff x3 v u):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cv u) (cv v)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wsb x3 ps u) (wsb x3 ch v)) $;

--| Equality theorem for disjoint collection. Deduction version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem disjeq12dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wdisj x3 A2 C) (wdisj x3 B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. Deduction version. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ixpeq12dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cixp x3 A2 C) (cixp x3 B2 D)) $;

--| Equality deduction for sum. General version of ~ sumeq2sdv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem sumeq12sdv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k A2 C) (csu k B2 D)) $;

--| Equality deduction for product. General version of ~ prodeq2sdv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem prodeq12sdv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k A2 C) (cprod k B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. Deduction form. General version of ~ itgeq1d and ~ itgeq2sdv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq12sdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 D)) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. Deduction form. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem itgeq2sdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) (citg x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. Deduction form. General version of ~ ditgeq3sdv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq123dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 C E) (cdit x3 B2 D F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. Deduction form. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq12d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D: class) (E: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 C E) (cdit x3 B2 D E)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. Deduction form. (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem ditgeq3sdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 C) (cdit x3 A2 B2 D)) $;

--| A proof of ~ ax-8 that does not rely on ~ ax-8 . It employs ~ df-in to perform alpha-renaming and eliminates disjoint variable conditions using ~ ax-9 . Since the nature of this result is unclear, usage of this theorem is discouraged, and this method should not be applied to eliminate axiom dependencies. (Contributed by GG, 1-Aug-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem in_ax8 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| A proof of ~ ax-8 that does not rely on ~ ax-8 . It employs ~ df-ss to perform alpha-renaming and eliminates disjoint variable conditions using ~ ax-9 . Contrary to ~ in-ax8 , this proof does not rely on ~ df-cleq , therefore using fewer axioms . This method should not be applied to eliminate axiom dependencies. (Contributed by GG, 30-Aug-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ss_ax8 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) (wcel (cv y1) (cv z))) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in the restricted universal quantifier, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvralvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 ph A2) (wral y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in the restricted existential quantifier, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvrexvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in the restricted at-most-one quantifier, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvrmovw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wrmo x3 ph A2) (wrmo y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in the restricted existential uniqueness quantifier, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvreuvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wreu x3 ph A2) (wreu y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change bound variable of a class substitution using implicit substitution. General version of ~ cbvsbcvw . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem cbvsbcvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wsbc y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change bound variable of a proper substitution into a class using implicit substitution. General version of ~ cbvcsbv . (Contributed by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem cbvcsbvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wceq (csb x3 A2 C) (csb y1 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in indexed unions, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbviunvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (ciun x3 A2 C) (ciun y1 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in an indexed intersection, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbviinvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (ciin x3 A2 C) (ciin y1 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in a maps-to function, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvmptvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpt x3 A2 C) (cmpt y1 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in a disjoint collection, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvdisjvw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (D: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wb (wdisj x3 A2 C) (wdisj y1 B2 D) $;

--| Change bound variable and domain in a restricted description binder, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvriotavw2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (crio x3 ph A2) (crio y1 ps B2) $;

--| Change the first bound variable in an operation abstraction, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvoprab1vw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (ch: wff y1 z w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z ps) (coprab w y1 z ch) $;

--| Change the second bound variable in an operation abstraction, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvoprab2vw {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z w):
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z ps) (coprab x3 w z ch) $;

--| Change all bound variables in an operation abstraction, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvoprab123vw {x3 y1 z w v u: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (ch: wff w v u):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv w)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv u))) (wceq (cv z) (cv v))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wceq (coprab x3 y1 z ps) (coprab w u v ch) $;

--| Change the second and third bound variables in an operation abstraction, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvoprab23vw {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1 z) (ch: wff x3 w v):
