--| Lemma for ~ lclkr . When the hypotheses of ~ lclkrlem2u and ~ lclkrlem2u are negated, the functional sum must be zero, so the kernel is the vector space. We make use of the law of excluded middle, ~ dochexmid , which requires the orthomodular law ~ dihoml4 (Lemma 3.3 of [Holland95] p. 214). (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrlem2v (ph: wff) (D c_pl c_po S c_x c_xp U E F G H I K L c_mi N c_pe V W X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U clspn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv U clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv E L) (cfv (csn X) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G L) (cfv (csn Y) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co E G c_pl)) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y (co E G c_pl)) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co E G c_pl) L) V) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lclkr . This is the same as ~ lclkrlem2u and ~ lclkrlem2u with the inequality hypotheses negated. When the sum of two functionals is zero at each generating vector, the kernel is the vector space and therefore closed. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrlem2w (ph: wff) (D c_pl c_po S c_x c_xp U E F G H I K L c_mi N c_pe V W X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv U csg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U clspn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq c_po (cfv U clsm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv E L) (cfv (csn X) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G L) (cfv (csn Y) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (co E G c_pl)) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y (co E G c_pl)) c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (co E G c_pl) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (co E G c_pl) L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lclkr . Eliminate by cases the hypotheses of ~ lclkrlem2u , ~ lclkrlem2u and ~ lclkrlem2w . (Contributed by NM, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrlem2x (ph: wff) (D c_pl U E F G H K L c_pe V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv E L) (cfv (csn X) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G L) (cfv (csn Y) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (co E G c_pl) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (co E G c_pl) L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lclkr . Restate the hypotheses for ` E ` and ` G ` to say their kernels are closed, in order to eliminate the generating vectors ` X ` and ` Y ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrlem2y (ph: wff) (D c_pl U E F G H K L c_pe W: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv E L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv E L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv G L)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (co E G c_pl) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (co E G c_pl) L)) $;

--| The set of functionals having closed kernels is closed under vector (functional) addition. Lemmas ~ lclkrlem2a through ~ lclkrlem2y are used for the proof. Here we express ~ lclkrlem2y in terms of membership in the set ` C ` of functionals with closed kernels. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrlem2 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (C D: class f2) (c_pl: class) (U: class f2) (E F G: class) (H K: class f2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co E G c_pl) C) $;

--| The set of functionals with closed kernels is a subspace. Part of proof of Theorem 3.6 of [Holland95] p. 218, line 20, stating "The f_M that arise this way generate a subspace F of E'". Our proof was suggested by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2015. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkr {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (C: class f2) (D: class) (S: class f2) (U F: class) (H K: class f2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C S) $;

--| Property of a functional with a closed kernel. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jan-2015.)
theorem lcfls1lem {f2: setvar} (C: class f2) (Q F G L c_pe: class):
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G C) (w3a (wcel G F) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv G L)) (wss (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) Q)) $;

--| Property of a functional with a closed kernel. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jan-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lcfls1N {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (C: class f2) (Q F G L c_pe: class):
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel G C) (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv G L)) (wss (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) Q))) $;

--| Property of a functional with a closed kernel. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2015.)
theorem lcfls1c {f2: setvar} (C D: class f2) (Q F G L c_pe: class):
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wb (wcel G C) (wa (wcel G D) (wss (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe) Q)) $;

--| The set of functionals having closed kernels and majorizing the orthocomplement of a given subspace ` Q ` is closed under scalar product. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrslem1 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2 C D: class f2) (Q: class) (R S: class f2) (c_x: class) (U: class f2) (F G: class) (H K: class f2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X G c_x) C) $;

--| The set of functionals having closed kernels and majorizing the orthocomplement of a given subspace ` Q ` is closed under scalar product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrslem2 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (B2 C D: class f2) (c_pl Q: class) (R S: class f2) (c_x: class) (U: class f2) (E F G: class) (H K: class f2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G C) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv D cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co E G c_pl) C) $;

--| The set of functionals having closed kernels and majorizing the orthocomplement of a given subspace ` R ` is a subspace of the dual space. TODO: This proof repeats large parts of the ~ lclkr proof. Do we achieve overall shortening by breaking them out as subtheorems? Or make ~ lclkr a special case of this? (Contributed by NM, 29-Jan-2015.)
theorem lclkrs {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (C: class f2) (D R: class) (S T: class f2) (U F: class) (H K: class f2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) R)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C T) $;

--| The set of functionals with closed kernels and majorizing the orthocomplement of a given subspace ` Q ` is a subspace of the dual space containing functionals with closed kernels. Note that ` R ` is the value given by ~ mapdval . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem lclkrs2 {f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (C: class f2 g2) (D Q: class f2) (R S T: class f2 g2) (U: class f2) (F: class) (H K: class f2 g2) (L c_pe: class) (W: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab g2 (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv g2) L)) (wss (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) c_pe) Q)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel R T) (wss R C)) $;

--| Reconstruction from the dual space span of a singleton. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem lcfrvalsnN {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D Q: class f2) (R: class) (U F: class f2) (G: class) (H K: class f2) (L: class) (N: class f2) (c_pe: class) (W: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv D clspn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wceq Q (ciun f2 R (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (csn G) N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Q (cfv (cfv G L) c_pe)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . Note that ` X ` is z in Mario's notes. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem1 (ph: wff) (D S c_x c_xp U E F G H I c_mi V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv D csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U clvec) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv X G) c_0) $ >
  $ wceq H (co E (co (co (cfv (cfv X G) I) (cfv X E) c_xp) G c_x) c_mi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X H) c_0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem2 (ph: wff) (D S c_x c_xp U E F G H I L c_mi V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv D csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U clvec) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv X G) c_0) $ >
  $ wceq H (co E (co (co (cfv (cfv X G) I) (cfv X E) c_xp) G c_x) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cin (cfv E L) (cfv G L)) (cfv H L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem3 (ph: wff) (D S c_x c_xp U E F G H I L c_mi V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv S cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv S cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv D cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv D csg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U clvec) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv X G) c_0) $ >
  $ wceq H (co E (co (co (cfv (cfv X G) I) (cfv X E) c_xp) G c_x) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv H L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem4 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D Q U E G H K L c_pe: class g2) (V: class) (W X: class g2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq E (ciun g2 G (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X V) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . The set of functionals having closed kernels and majorizing the orthocomplement of a given subspace ` Q ` is closed under scalar product. TODO: share hypotheses with others. Use more consistent variable names here or elsewhere when possible. (Contributed by NM, 5-Mar-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem5 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D Q R S: class f2) (c_x: class) (U F H K L c_pe V W: class f2) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R S) $ >
  $ wceq Q (ciun f2 R (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X Q) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 X c_x) Q) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . Closure of vector sum with colinear vectors. TODO: Move down ` N ` definition so top hypotheses can be shared. (Contributed by NM, 10-Mar-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem6 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class g2) (c_pl: class) (Q: class g2) (U: class) (E G H K L N c_pe W: class g2) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv U clspn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G Q) $ >
  $ wceq E (ciun g2 G (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (csn X) N) (cfv (csn Y) N)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) E) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . Closure of vector sum when one vector is zero. TODO: share hypotheses with others. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem7 {g2: setvar} (ph: wff) (D c_pl Q: class g2) (U: class) (E G H K L c_pe W X Y c_0: class g2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G Q) $ >
  $ wceq E (ciun g2 G (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) c_pe)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X E) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv U c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Y c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_pl) E) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcf1o and ~ lcfr . (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem8 {x3 w v f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w v f2 k) (C D: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pl: class w v f2 k) (Q: class x3 w v f2 k) (R: class w v f2 k) (S: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_x: class w v f2 k) (U F H J K L: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pe: class v f2 k) (V: class w f2 k) (W: class x3 w v f2 k) (X: class f2) (c_0: class w v f2 k):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv U c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cmpt v V (crio k (wrex w (wceq (cv v) (co (cv w) (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) c_pe)) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cdif V (csn c_0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X J) (cmpt v V (crio k (wrex w (wceq (cv v) (co (cv w) (co (cv k) X c_x) c_pl)) (cfv (csn X) c_pe)) R))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcf1o . (This part has undesirable $d's on ` J ` and ` ph ` that we remove in ~ lcf1o .) TODO: ugly proof; maybe have better subtheorems or abbreviate some ` iota_ k ` expansions with ` J `` z ` ? TODO: Some redundant $d's? (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem9 {x3 w v f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (C: class f2) (D: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pl: class) (Q: class f2) (R: class) (S: class f2) (c_x: class) (U: class v f2) (F: class x3 w v k) (H: class x3 w v f2 k) (J: class f2) (K: class x3 w v f2 k) (L c_pe V: class) (W: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_0: class f2):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv U c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cmpt v V (crio k (wrex w (wceq (cv v) (co (cv w) (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) c_pe)) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cdif C (csn Q)) J) $;

--| Define a function ` J ` that provides a bijection from nonzero vectors ` V ` to nonzero functionals with closed kernels ` C ` . (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcf1o {x3 w v f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w v f2 k) (C D: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pl: class) (Q: class x3 w v f2 k) (R: class w) (S: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_x: class) (U: class x3 w v f2 k) (F: class x3 w v k) (H J K: class x3 w v f2 k) (L: class x3 w v k) (c_pe: class) (V: class w k) (W: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_0: class w v f2 k):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv U c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cmpt v V (crio k (wrex w (wceq (cv v) (co (cv w) (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) c_pe)) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cdif C (csn Q)) J) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lcfr . (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2015.)
theorem lcfrlem10 {x3 w v f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w v f2 k) (C D: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pl: class f2) (Q: class x3 w v f2 k) (R: class w f2) (S: class x3 w v f2) (c_x: class f2) (U F H J K L: class x3 w v f2 k) (c_pe: class f2) (V: class w f2 k) (W: class x3 w v f2 k) (X: class f2) (c_0: class w v f2 k):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq c_pe (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv U cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv U cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv U cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv U c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cfv D c0g) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab f2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) c_pe) c_pe) (cfv (cv f2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 (cdif V (csn c_0)) (cmpt v V (crio k (wrex w (wceq (cv v) (co (cv w) (co (cv k) (cv x3) c_x) c_pl)) (cfv (csn (cv x3)) c_pe)) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
