  $ wi ph (wceq (crab f2 (wss (cfv (cfv (cv f2) L) O) Q) C) R) $;

--| Given a dual subspace ` R ` (of functionals with closed kernels), reconstruct the subspace ` Q ` that maps to it. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapdrval {g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff g2 h) (C D Q: class g2 h) (R: class g2) (S T: class g2 h) (U F: class h) (H K: class g2 h) (L: class) (M: class g2 h) (O: class) (W: class g2 h):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab g2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) O) O) (cfv (cv g2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss R C) $ >
  $ wceq Q (ciun h R (cfv (cfv (cv h) L) O)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Q M) R) $;

--| The map defined by ~ df-mapd is one-to-one and onto the set of dual subspaces of functionals with closed kernels. This shows ` M ` satisfies part of the definition of projectivity of [Baer] p. 40. TODO: change theorems leading to this ( ~ lcfr , ~ mapdrval , ~ lclkrs , ~ lclkr ,...) to use ` T i^i ~P C ` ? TODO: maybe get rid of $d's for ` g ` versus ` K U W ` ; propagate to ~ mapdrn and any others. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapd1o {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (C D S T: class g2) (U F: class) (H: class g2) (K L: class) (M: class g2) (O W: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab g2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) O) O) (cfv (cv g2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o S (cin T (cpw C)) M) $;

--| Range of the map defined by ~ df-mapd . (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapdrn {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (C D T: class g2) (U F: class) (H: class g2) (K L: class) (M: class g2) (O W: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv U cld) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv D clss) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab g2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) O) O) (cfv (cv g2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn M) (cin T (cpw C))) $;

--| Union of the range of the map defined by ~ df-mapd . (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2015.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mapdunirnN {g2: setvar} (ph: wff g2) (C: class g2) (U F: class) (H: class g2) (K L: class) (M: class g2) (O W: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv W (cfv K coch)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv U clfn) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv U clk) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab g2 (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv g2) L) O) O) (cfv (cv g2) L)) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (crn M)) C) $;

--| Range of the map defined by ~ df-mapd . TODO: this seems to be needed a lot in ~ hdmaprnlem3eN etc. Would it be better to change ~ df-mapd theorems to use ` LSubSp `` C ` instead of ` ran M ` ? (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapdrn2 (ph: wff) (C T H K M W: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv W (cfv K clcd)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cfv C clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn M) T) $;

--| Closure of the converse of the map defined by ~ df-mapd . (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2015.)
theorem mapdcnvcl (ph: wff) (S U H K M W X: class):
  $ wceq H (cfv K clh) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv W (cfv K cmpd)) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv W (cfv K cdvh)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U clss) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel K chlt) (wcel W H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn M)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X (ccnv M)) S) $;

--| Closure the value of the map defined by ~ df-mapd . (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2015.)
