  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpw B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cdif B2 S) (cfv X N)) (wn (wcel S (cfv X M)))) $;

--| The value of the neighborhoods (convergents) in terms of the convergents (neighborhoods) function. (Contributed by RP, 27-Jun-2021.)
theorem neicvgfv {i j k m n o s1 p l: setvar} (ph: wff m) (B2: class) (D: class s1) (P: class i j k m n o s1 p l) (F: class m s1) (G: class s1) (H: class i j k m n o s1 p l) (M: class m s1) (N: class) (O: class i j k m n o s1 p l) (X: class i j k n o p):
  $ wceq O (cmpo i j cvv cvv (cmpt k (co (cpw (cv j)) (cv i) cmap) (cmpt l (cv j) (crab m (wcel (cv l) (cfv (cv m) (cv k))) (cv i))))) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt n cvv (cmpt p (co (cpw (cv n)) (cpw (cv n)) cmap) (cmpt o (cpw (cv n)) (cdif (cv n) (cfv (cdif (cv n) (cv o)) (cv p)))))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv B2 P) $ >
  $ wceq F (co (cpw B2) B2 O) $ >
  $ wceq G (co B2 (cpw B2) O) $ >
  $ wceq H (ccom F (ccom D G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N M H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X N) (crab s1 (wn (wcel (cdif B2 (cv s1)) (cfv X M))) (cpw B2))) $;

--| The range of the interior function of a topology a subset of the open sets of the topology. (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem ntrrn (I J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wss (crn I) J) $;

--| The interior function of a topology is a map from the powerset of the base set to the open sets of the topology. (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem ntrf (I J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wf (cpw X) J I) $;

--| The interior function is a map from the powerset of the base set to itself. (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem ntrf2 (I J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wf (cpw X) (cpw X) I) $;

--| The interior function is a map from the powerset of the base set to itself. (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem ntrelmap (I J X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel I (co (cpw X) (cpw X) cmap)) $;

--| The closure function is a map from the powerset of the base set to itself. This is less precise than ~ clsf . (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem clsf2 (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wf (cpw X) (cpw X) K) $;

--| The closure function is a map from the powerset of the base set to itself. (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem clselmap (J K X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wcel K (co (cpw X) (cpw X) cmap)) $;

--| The interior and closure operators on a topology are duals of each other. See also ~ kur14lem2 . (Contributed by RP, 21-Apr-2021.)
theorem dssmapntrcls {f2 s1 b: setvar} (D I: class f2 s1 b) (J: class) (K: class b) (O: class f2 s1 b) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt b cvv (cmpt f2 (co (cpw (cv b)) (cpw (cv b)) cmap) (cmpt s1 (cpw (cv b)) (cdif (cv b) (cfv (cdif (cv b) (cv s1)) (cv f2)))))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv X O) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq I (cfv K D)) $;

--| The closure and interior operators on a topology are duals of each other. See also ~ kur14lem2 . (Contributed by RP, 22-Apr-2021.)
theorem dssmapclsntr {f2 s1 b: setvar} (D I: class f2 s1 b) (J: class) (K: class b) (O: class f2 s1 b) (X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv J ccl) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv J cnt) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt b cvv (cmpt f2 (co (cpw (cv b)) (cpw (cv b)) cmap) (cmpt s1 (cpw (cv b)) (cdif (cv b) (cfv (cdif (cv b) (cv s1)) (cv f2)))))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv X O) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wceq K (cfv I D)) $;

--| Each point ` p ` of the neighborhood space has at least one neighborhood; each neighborhood of ` p ` contains ` p ` . Axiom A of Seifert and Threlfall. (Contributed by RP, 5-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispa {n p: setvar} (J: class) (X: class p):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel J ctop) (wral p (wa (wne (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv J cnei)) c0) (wral n (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (cfv (csn (cv p)) (cfv J cnei)))) X) $;

--| Given a neighborhood ` N ` of ` P ` , each subset of the neighborhood space containing this neighborhood is also a neighborhood of ` P ` . Axiom B of Seifert and Threlfall. (Contributed by RP, 5-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispb {s1: setvar} (P J N X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel J ctop) (wcel P X) (wcel N (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)))) (wral s1 (wi (wss N (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (csn P) (cfv J cnei)))) (cpw X)) $;

--| The predicate that ` F ` is a (generic) Seifert and Threlfall neighborhood space. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class) (V: class f2 n s1 p):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F A2) (wa (wf (cdm F) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm F)) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) F) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) F))) (cpw (cdm F)))) (cfv (cv p) F)) (cdm F)))) $;

--| The predicate that ` F ` is a (generic) Seifert and Threlfall neighborhood space. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace3 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class) (V: class f2 n s1 p):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F A2) (wa (wa (wfun F) (wss (crn F) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm F)) (csn c0))) (csn c0)))) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) F))) (cpw (cdm F)))) (cfv (cv p) F)) (cdm F)))) $;

--| The predicate that ` F ` is a (generic) Seifert and Threlfall neighborhood space. (Contributed by RP, 14-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class) (V: class f2 n s1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F A2) (w3a (wfun F) (wss (crn F) (cpw (cpw (cdm F)))) (wral p (wa (wne (cfv (cv p) F) c0) (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) F))) (cpw (cdm F)))) (cfv (cv p) F))) (cdm F)))) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space is a function with a range that is a subset of the powerset of the powerset of its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacef {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wf (cdm F) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm F)) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) F) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space is a function with a range that is a subset of the powerset of the powerset of its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacef2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wf (cdm F) (cpw (cpw (cdm F))) F) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space is a function. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacefun {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wfun F) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space has a range that is a subset of the powerset of the powerset of its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacern {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wss (crn F) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm F)) (csn c0))) (csn c0))) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space has a range that is a subset of the powerset of the powerset of its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacern2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wss (crn F) (cpw (cpw (cdm F)))) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space has a nonempty set of neighborhoods for every point in its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace0nelrn {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wral p (wne (cfv (cv p) F) c0) (cdm F)) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space has a nonempty set of neighborhoods for every point in its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace0nelrn2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (P: class f2 s1) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F A2) (wcel P (cdm F))) (wne (cfv P F) c0) $;

--| A generic neighborhood space has a nonempty set of neighborhoods for every point in its domain. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispace0nelrn3 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wn (wcel c0 (crn F))) $;

--| Every neighborhood of a point in a generic neighborhood space contains that point. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispaceel {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wral p (wral n (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (cfv (cv p) F)) (cdm F)) $;

--| Every neighborhood of a point in a generic neighborhood space contains that point. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispaceel2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (P: class f2 s1) (F: class) (N: class f2 s1 p):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F A2) (wcel P (cdm F)) (wcel N (cfv P F))) (wcel P N) $;

--| All supersets of a neighborhood of a point (limited to the domain of the neighborhood space) are also neighborhoods of that point. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacess {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (F: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F A2) (wral p (wral n (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) F))) (cpw (cdm F))) (cfv (cv p) F)) (cdm F)) $;

--| All supersets of a neighborhood of a point (limited to the domain of the neighborhood space) are also neighborhoods of that point. (Contributed by RP, 15-Apr-2021.)
theorem gneispacess2 {f2 n s1 p: setvar} (A2: class f2 n s1 p) (P: class f2) (S: class f2 n p) (F: class) (N: class f2 p):
  $ wceq A2 (cab f2 (wa (wf (cdm (cv f2)) (cdif (cpw (cdif (cpw (cdm (cv f2))) (csn c0))) (csn c0)) (cv f2)) (wral p (wral n (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv n)) (wral s1 (wi (wss (cv n) (cv s1)) (wcel (cv s1) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2)))) (cpw (cdm (cv f2))))) (cfv (cv p) (cv f2))) (cdm (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel F A2) (wcel P (cdm F))) (w3a (wcel N (cfv P F)) (wcel S (cpw (cdm F))) (wss N S))) (wcel S (cfv P F)) $;

--| Application of ~ ssin to range of a function. (Contributed by RP, 1-Apr-2021.)
theorem k0004lem1 (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wi (wceq D (cin B2 C)) (wb (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wss (cima F A2) C)) (wf A2 D F)) $;

--| A mapping with a particular restricted range is also a mapping to that range. (Contributed by RP, 1-Apr-2021.)
theorem k0004lem2 (A2 B2 C U F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 V) (wss C B2)) (wb (wa (wcel F (co B2 A2 cmap)) (wss (cima F A2) C)) (wcel F (co C A2 cmap))) $;

--| When the value of a mapping on a singleton is known, the mapping is a completely known singleton. (Contributed by RP, 2-Apr-2021.)
theorem k0004lem3 (A2 B2 C U F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 V) (wcel C B2)) (wb (wa (wcel F (co B2 (csn A2) cmap)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) C)) (wceq F (csn (cop A2 C)))) $;

--| The topological simplex of dimension ` N ` is the set of real vectors where the components are nonnegative and sum to 1. (Contributed by RP, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem k0004val {t k n: setvar} (A2: class t k n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N A2) (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 N cfz) cmap))) $;

--| The topological simplex of dimension ` N ` is a subset of the real vectors of dimension ` ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by RP, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem k0004ss1 {t k n: setvar} (A2: class t k n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wss (cfv N A2) (co cr (co cc0 N cfz) cmap)) $;

--| The topological simplex of dimension ` N ` is a subset of the base set of a real vector space of dimension ` ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by RP, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem k0004ss2 {t k n: setvar} (A2: class t k n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wss (cfv N A2) (cfv (cfv (co cc0 N cfz) crrx) cbs)) $;

--| The topological simplex of dimension ` N ` is a subset of the base set of Euclidean space of dimension ` ( N + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by RP, 29-Mar-2021.)
theorem k0004ss3 {t k n: setvar} (A2: class t k n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wss (cfv N A2) (cfv (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cehl) cbs)) $;

--| The topological simplex of dimension 0 is a singleton. (Contributed by RP, 2-Apr-2021.)
theorem k0004val0 {t k n: setvar} (A2: class t k n):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (crab t (wceq (csu k (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv t))) c1) (co (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 A2) (csn (cs1 c1)) $;

--| Simple induction example. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem inductionexd (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr c3 (co (co c4 N cexp) c5 caddc) cdvds) $;

--| Natural deduction form of ~ lemul2d . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem wwlemuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Specialization of ~ breq1d to reals and less than. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem leeq1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $;

--| Specialization of ~ breq2d to reals and less than. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem leeq2d (ph: wff) (A2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 D cle) $;

--| Specialization of absmuld with ~ absidd . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem absmulrposd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cabs) (co A2 (cfv B2 cabs) cmul)) $;

--| Natural dduction form of one side of ~ imadisj . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem imadisjld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (cdm A2) B2) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima A2 B2) c0) $;

--| The image of a mapping from A is nonempty if A is nonempty. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem wnefimgd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cima F A2) c0) $;

--| Natural deduction form of ~ fco2 . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem fco2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 C (cres F B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C (ccom F G)) $;

--| The value of a function on its domain is in the image of the function. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem wfximgfd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C F) (cima F A2)) $;

--| If |f(x)| <= C for all x then it applies to all x in the image of |f(x)| (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem extoimad {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) C cle) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) C cle) (cima cabs (cima F cr))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imo72b2 . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem imo72b2lem0 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class y1) (F: class) (G: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) F) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) F) caddc) (co c2 (co (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 G) cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) c1 cle) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv (cfv A2 F) cabs) (cfv (cfv B2 G) cabs) cmul) (csup (cima cabs (cima F cr)) cr clt) cle) $;

--| Natural deduction form of specialized ~ suprleub . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem suprleubrd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imo72b2 . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem imo72b2lem2 {z: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral z (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) cabs) C cle) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup (cima cabs (cima F cr)) cr clt) C cle) $;

--| Natural deduction form of specialized ~ suprlub . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem suprlubrd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wbr B2 (cv z) clt) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (csup A2 cr clt) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imo72b2 . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem imo72b2lem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) c1 cle) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (csup (cima cabs (cima F cr)) cr clt) clt) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem lemuldiv3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmul) C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (co C A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem lemuldiv4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (co C A2 cdiv) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmul) C cle) $;

--| IMO 1972 B2. (14th International Mathematical Olympiad in Poland, problem B2). (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Mar-2020.)
theorem imo72b2 {x3 y1 v u: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral u (wral v (wceq (co (cfv (co (cv u) (cv v) caddc) F) (cfv (co (cv u) (cv v) cmin) F) caddc) (co c2 (co (cfv (cv u) F) (cfv (cv v) G) cmul) cmul)) cr) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) c1 cle) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wne (cfv (cv x3) F) cc0) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cfv B2 G) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| AdditionCommutativity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_addcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 C caddc) (co C A2 caddc)) $;

--| AdditionAssociativity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_addassocd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 (co C D caddc) caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) D caddc)) $;

--| AdditionSimplification generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_addsimpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq cc0 (co A2 B2 cmin)) $;

--| MultiplicationCommutativity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_mulcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 C cmul) (co C A2 cmul)) $;

--| MultiplicationAssociativity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_mulassocd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 (co C D cmul) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) D cmul)) $;

--| MultiplicationSimplification generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_mulsimpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c1 (co A2 B2 cdiv)) $;

--| AdditionMultiplicationLeftDistribution generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_leftdistd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C D caddc) B2 cmul) (co (co C A2 cmul) (co D A2 cmul) caddc)) $;

--| AdditionMultiplicationRightDistribution generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_rightdistd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 (co C D caddc) cmul) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co A2 D cmul) caddc)) $;

--| SquareDefinition generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_sqdefd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (co A2 c2 cexp)) $;

--| First MultiplicationOne generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_mul11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c1 cmul) B2) $;

--| Second MultiplicationOne generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_mul12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 A2 cmul) B2) $;

--| First AdditionZero generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_add01d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 cc0 caddc) B2) $;

--| Second AdditionZero generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_add02d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 A2 caddc) B2) $;

--| SquareGEQZero generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_sqgeq0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul) cle) $;

--| PrincipleOfEquality generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_eqprincd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 D caddc)) $;

--| EqualityTransitivity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_eqtransd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 C) $;

--| EquMoveTerm generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_eqmvtd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (co C D caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (co B2 D cmin)) $;

--| EquivalenceImpliesDoubleInequality generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_eqineqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $;

--| IneqMoveTerm generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_ineqmvtd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (co C D caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 D cmin) C cle) $;

--| FirstPrincipleOfInequality generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_ineq1stprincd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 D caddc) (co A2 C caddc) cle) $;

--| SecondPrincipleOfInequality generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_ineq2ndprincd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 C cmul) (co A2 C cmul) cle) $;

--| InequalityTransitivity generator rule. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem int_ineqtransd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C A2 cle) $;

--| Theorem used in conjunction with ~ decaddc to absorb carry when generating n-digit addition synthetic proofs. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 7-Apr-2020.)
theorem unitadd (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) F $ >
  $ wceq (co C c1 caddc) B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel C cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 C caddc) c1 caddc) F $;

--| Valuation of a length 3 word in a monoid. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 9-Sep-2020.)
theorem gsumws3 (B2 c_pl S T U G: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wa (wcel S B2) (wa (wcel T B2) (wcel U B2)))) (wceq (co G (cs3 S T U) cgsu) (co S (co T U c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| Valuation of a length 4 word in a monoid. (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 10-Sep-2020.)
theorem gsumws4 (B2 c_pl S T U G V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cmnd) (wa (wcel S B2) (wa (wcel T B2) (wa (wcel U B2) (wcel V B2))))) (wceq (co G (cs4 S T U V) cgsu) (co S (co T (co U V c_pl) c_pl) c_pl)) $;

--| Arithmetic-geometric mean inequality for ` n = 2 ` , derived from ~ amgmlem . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 8-Sep-2020.)
theorem amgm2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (co c1 c2 cdiv) ccxp) (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Arithmetic-geometric mean inequality for ` n = 3 ` . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 11-Sep-2020.)
theorem amgm3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co c1 c3 cdiv) ccxp) (co (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) c3 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Arithmetic-geometric mean inequality for ` n = 4 ` . (Contributed by Stanislas Polu, 11-Sep-2020.)
theorem amgm4d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 (co B2 (co C D cmul) cmul) cmul) (co c1 c4 cdiv) ccxp) (co (co A2 (co B2 (co C D caddc) caddc) caddc) c4 cdiv) cle) $;

--| ~ sp can be proven from the other classic axioms. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ sp instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem spALT {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Prove an existential. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 12-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_spce {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) A2)) ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Unpack a restricted existential antecedent while changing the variable with implicit substitution. The equivalent of this theorem without the bound variable change is ~ rexlimdvaa . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem rexlimdvaacbv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (th: wff y1) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) th)) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wrex x3 ps A2) ch) $;

--| Unpack a restricted existential assumption while changing the variable with implicit substitution. Similar to ~ rexlimdvaacbv . The equivalent of this theorem without the bound variable change is ~ rexlimddv . Version of ~ rexlimddvcbv with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.) (Revised by GG, 2-Apr-2024.)
theorem rexlimddvcbvw {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (th: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 th A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) ch)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Unpack a restricted existential assumption while changing the variable with implicit substitution. Similar to ~ rexlimdvaacbv . The equivalent of this theorem without the bound variable change is ~ rexlimddv . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 , see ~ rexlimddvcbvw for a weaker version that does not require it. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rexlimddvcbv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (th: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 th A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv y1) A2) ch)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Elementhood in an image set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_elrnmpt3d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) C)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (crn F)) $;

--| Equivalent of ~ php without negation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 3-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_phpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cen) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ tfinds . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 8-Aug-2023.)
theorem tfindsd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff x3 y1) (th: wff y1) (ta: wff x3 y1) (et: wff y1) (A2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps et) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) con0) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wlim (cv x3)) (wral y1 th (cv x3))) ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 con0) $ >
  $ wi ph et $;

--| Extend class notation with the monoid ring function.
term cmnring: class;

--| Define the monoid ring function. This takes a monoid ` M ` and a ring ` R ` and produces a free left module over ` R ` with a product extending the monoid function on ` M ` . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-May-2024.)
axiom df_mnring {x3 y1 v i m r a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmnring (cmpo r m cvv cvv (csb v (co (cv r) (cfv (cv m) cbs) cfrlm) (co (cv v) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv v) cbs) (cfv (cv v) cbs) (co (cv v) (cmpo a b (cfv (cv m) cbs) (cfv (cv m) cbs) (cmpt i (cfv (cv m) cbs) (cif (wceq (cv i) (co (cv a) (cv b) (cfv (cv m) cplusg))) (co (cfv (cv a) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv b) (cv y1)) (cfv (cv r) cmulr)) (cfv (cv r) c0g)))) cgsu))) csts))) $;

--| Value of the monoid ring function. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringvald {x3 y1 i a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 i a b) (A2 B2 c_pl: class x3 y1 i a b) (R: class) (c_x U F: class x3 y1 i a b) (M: class) (V W c_0: class x3 y1 i a b):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv V cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (co V (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co V (cmpo a b A2 A2 (cmpt i A2 (cif (wceq (cv i) (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl)) (co (cfv (cv a) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv b) (cv y1)) c_x) c_0))) cgsu))) csts)) $;

--| Components of a monoid ring other than its ring product match its underlying free module. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.) (Revised by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem mnringnmulrd (ph: wff) (A2 R U E F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq E (cslot (cfv cnx E)) $ >
  $ wne (cfv cnx E) (cfv cnx cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv V E) (cfv F E)) $;

--| The base set of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem mnringbased (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R U F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv V cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv F cbs)) $;

--| The base set of a monoid ring. Converse of ~ mnringbased . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringbaserd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R U F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cfv V cbs)) $;

--| Membership in the base set of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringelbased (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R U F M W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel X B2) (wa (wcel X (co C A2 cmap)) (wbr X c_0 cfsupp))) $;

--| Elements of a monoid ring are functions. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringbasefd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R U F M W X: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C X) $;

--| Elements of a monoid ring are finitely supported. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringbasefsuppd (ph: wff) (B2 R U F M W X c_0: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| The additive operation of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem mnringaddgd (ph: wff) (A2 R U F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv V cplusg) (cfv F cplusg)) $;

--| The additive identity of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnring0gd (ph: wff) (A2 R U F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R A2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv V c0g) (cfv F c0g)) $;

--| The additive identity of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnring0g2d (ph: wff) (A2 R F M W c_0: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxp A2 (csn c_0)) (cfv F c0g)) $;

--| The ring product of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringmulrd {x3 y1 i a b: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (A2: class i) (B2 c_pl: class x3 y1 i a b) (R: class) (c_x U F: class x3 y1 i a b) (M: class) (W c_0: class x3 y1 i a b):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co F (cmpo a b A2 A2 (cmpt i A2 (cif (wceq (cv i) (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl)) (co (cfv (cv a) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv b) (cv y1)) c_x) c_0))) cgsu)) (cfv F cmulr)) $;

--| The scalar ring of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem mnringscad (ph: wff) (R U F M W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cfv F csca)) $;

--| The scalar product of a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 1-Nov-2024.)
theorem mnringvscad (ph: wff) (B2 R U F M V W: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq V (co R B2 cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv V cvsca) (cfv F cvsca)) $;

--| Monoid rings are left modules. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringlmodd (ph: wff) (R U F M: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F clmod) $;

--| Value of multiplication in a monoid ring. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringmulrvald {i a b: setvar} (ph: wff i a b) (A2: class i) (B2 c_pl: class i a b) (R: class) (c_xb c_x U F: class i a b) (M: class) (W: class i a b) (X Y: class) (c_0b: class i a b):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0b (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv F cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y c_x) (co F (cmpo a b A2 A2 (cmpt i A2 (cif (wceq (cv i) (co (cv a) (cv b) c_pl)) (co (cfv (cv a) X) (cfv (cv b) Y) c_xb) c_0b))) cgsu)) $;

--| Monoid rings are closed under multiplication. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 14-May-2024.)
theorem mnringmulrcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R c_x U F M X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (co R M cmnring) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv F cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y c_x) B2) $;

--| A nonempty Grothendieck universe contains the empty set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem gru0eld (ph: wff) (A2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 G) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are closed under ordinal successor. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
theorem grusucd (ph: wff) (A2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csuc A2) G) $;

--| Any rank of the cumulative hierarchy is closed under the rank function. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem r1rankcld (ph: wff) (A2 R: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv R cr1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) (cfv R cr1)) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are closed under the cumulative hierarchy function. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 8-Aug-2023.)
theorem grur1cld (ph: wff) (A2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cr1) G) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are closed under the rank function. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
theorem grurankcld (ph: wff) (A2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) G) $;

--| If a Grothendieck universe contains a set's rank, it contains that set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.)
theorem grurankrcld (ph: wff) (A2 G V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 crnk) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $;

--| If a Grothendieck universe contains an element of a Scott's trick set, it contains the Scott's trick set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem gruscottcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cscott A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cscott A2) G) $;

--| Extend class notation with the collection operation.
term ccoll (A2 F: class): class;

--| Define the collection operation. This is similar to the image set operation ` " ` , but it uses Scott's trick to ensure the output is always a set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
axiom df_coll {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (ccoll A2 F) (ciun x3 A2 (cscott (cima F (csn (cv x3))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem dfcoll2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wceq (ccoll A2 F) (ciun x3 A2 (cscott (cab y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| Equality theorem for the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem colleq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccoll A2 F) (ccoll B2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem colleq1 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (ccoll A2 F) (ccoll A2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem colleq2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (ccoll A2 F) (ccoll B2 F)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem nfcoll {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (ccoll A2 F) $;

--| The output of the collection operation is a set if the second input is. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem collexd (ph: wff) (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccoll A2 F) cvv) $;

--| Property of the collection operation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem cpcolld {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (ccoll A2 F)) $;

--| ~ cpcolld with an extra existential quantifier. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 12-Aug-2023.)
theorem cpcoll2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (ccoll A2 F)) $;

--| A Grothendieck universe contains the output of a collection operation whenever its left input is a relation on the universe, and its right input is in the universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem grucollcld (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F (cxp G G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ccoll A2 F) G) $;

--| The hypothesis of this theorem defines a class M of sets that we temporarily call "minimal universes", and which will turn out in ~ grumnueq to be exactly Grothendicek universes. Minimal universes are sets which satisfy the predicate on ` y ` in ~ rr-groth , except for the ` x e. y ` clause. A minimal universe is closed under subsets ( ~ mnussd ), powersets ( ~ mnupwd ), and an operation which is similar to a combination of collection and union ( ~ mnuop3d ), from which closure under pairing ( ~ mnuprd ), unions ( ~ mnuunid ), and function ranges ( ~ mnurnd ) can be deduced, from which equivalence with Grothendieck universes ( ~ grumnueq ) can be deduced. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem ismnu {z w v u f2 i k m n r q p l: setvar} (U: class) (M V: class z w v u f2 i k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wb (wcel U M) (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) U) (wal f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) (cv f2))) U) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (cv f2))) (cv z))) U))) U)) $;

--| Operations of a minimal universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuop123d {w v u f2 i k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff w v u f2 i k m n r q p l) (A2: class v u k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class w v u f2 i k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wss (cpw A2) U) (wal f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw A2) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) (cv f2))) U) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (cv f2))) A2)) U))) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under subsets. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnussd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $;

--| ~ mnussd with arguments provided with an existential quantifier. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuss2d {x3 k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class x3 k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wss A2 (cv x3)) U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $;

--| A nonempty minimal universe contains the empty set. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnu0eld {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel c0 U) $;

--| Second and third operations of a minimal universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuop23d {w v u i k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff w v u i k m n r q p l) (A2: class v u k m n r q p l) (U: class) (F: class k m n r q p l) (M V: class w v u i k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw A2) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) F)) U) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) F)) A2)) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under powersets. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnupwd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpw A2) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under singletons. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnusnd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csn A2) U) $;

--| A minimal universe contains pairs of subsets of an element of the universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprssd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2 C: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| Special case of ~ mnuprssd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprss2d {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2 C: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wss A2 C $ >
  $ wss B2 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| Third operation of a minimal universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuop3d {w v u i k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff u k m n r q p l) (A2: class v u k m n r q p l) (U: class) (F: class k m n r q p l) (M: class w v u i k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss F U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wral i (wi (wrex v (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) F) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) F)) A2) U) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnuprd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprdlem1 {w u i: setvar} (ph: wff w u i) (A2 B2 U: class w u i) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (cpr (cpr c0 (csn A2)) (cpr (csn c0) (csn B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) F) (cpr c0 (csn c0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cv w)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnuprd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprdlem2 {w u i: setvar} (ph: wff w u i) (A2 B2 U: class w u i) (F: class w):
  $ wceq F (cpr (cpr c0 (csn A2)) (cpr (csn c0) (csn B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 c0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) F) (cpr c0 (csn c0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cv w)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnuprd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 11-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprdlem3 {v i: setvar} (ph: wff v i) (A2 B2: class v i) (F: class i):
  $ wceq F (cpr (cpr c0 (csn A2)) (cpr (csn c0) (csn B2))) $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wrex v (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) F) (cpr c0 (csn c0))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnuprd . General case. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprdlem4 {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (F M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cpr c0 (csn A2)) (cpr (csn c0) (csn B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wceq A2 c0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under pairing. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuprd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under union. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuunid {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are closed under binary unions. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnuund {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cun A2 B2) U) $;

--| Minimal universes contain the elements of their elements. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnutrcld {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2 B2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 U) $;

--| Minimal universes are transitive. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnutrd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wtr U) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnurnd . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 12-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnurndlem1 {w v u i a: setvar} (ph: wff w v u i a) (A2: class w) (U: class w v u i a) (F: class w):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 U F) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wi (wrex v (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (crn (cmpt a A2 (cpr (cv a) (cpr (cfv (cv a) F) A2))))) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (crn (cmpt a A2 (cpr (cv a) (cpr (cfv (cv a) F) A2)))))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) (cv w)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mnurnd . Deduction theorem input. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnurndlem2 {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (F M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 U F) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) U) $;

--| Minimal universes contain ranges of functions from an element of the universe to the universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnurnd {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (A2: class k m n r q p l) (U: class) (F M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 U F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn F) U) $;

--| Minimal universes are Grothendieck universes. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem mnugrud {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (U: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cgru) $;

--| Lemma for ~ grumnud . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem grumnudlem {z u f2 h i j k m n r q p b c d l: setvar} (ph: wff u k m n r q p b c d l) (F: class z f2 k m n r q p b c d l) (G: class b c d) (M: class z u f2 h i j k m n r q p b c d l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wceq F (cin (copab b c (wex d (w3a (wceq (cuni (cv d)) (cv c)) (wcel (cv d) (cv f2)) (wcel (cv b) (cv d))))) (cxp G G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv i) G) (wcel (cv h) G)) (wb (wbr (cv i) (cv h) F) (wex j (w3a (wceq (cuni (cv j)) (cv h)) (wcel (cv j) (cv f2)) (wcel (cv i) (cv j))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv h) (ccoll (cv z) F)) (w3a (wceq (cuni (cv j)) (cv h)) (wcel (cv j) (cv f2)) (wcel (cv i) (cv j)))) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wcel (cuni (cv u)) (ccoll (cv z) F))) (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G M) $;

--| Grothendieck universes are minimal universes. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 12-Aug-2023.)
theorem grumnud {k m n r q p l: setvar} (ph: wff k m n r q p l) (G: class) (M: class k m n r q p l):
  $ wceq M (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cgru) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G M) $;

--| The class of Grothendieck universes is equal to the class of minimal universes. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem grumnueq {k m n r q p l: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgru (cab k (wral l (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv k)) (wal m (wrex n (wa (wss (cpw (cv l)) (cv n)) (wral p (wi (wrex q (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv q)) (wcel (cv q) (cv m))) (cv k)) (wrex r (wa (wcel (cv p) (cv r)) (wss (cuni (cv r)) (cv n))) (cv m))) (cv l))) (cv k)))) (cv k))) $;

--| Expand conjunction to primitives. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expandan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wa ph ch) (wn (wi ps (wn th))) $;

--| Expand an existential quantifier to primitives while contracting a double negation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expandexn {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wn (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Expand a restricted universal quantifier to primitives. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expandral {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 ph A2) (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps)) $;

--| Expand a restricted existential quantifier to primitives while contracting a double negation. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expandrexn {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wn (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps))) $;

--| Expand a restricted existential quantifier to primitives. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expandrex {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wn (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wn ps)))) $;

--| Expand ` U. A C_ B ` to primitives. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem expanduniss {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wb (wss (cuni A2) B2) (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv y1) B2))))) $;

--| Express the predicate on ` U ` in ~ ismnu using only primitives. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem ismnuprim {z w v u t f2 i o s1: setvar} (U: class z u t i o s1):
  $ wb (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) U) (wal f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) (cv f2))) U) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (cv f2))) (cv z))) U))) U) (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) U) (wal f2 (wn (wal w (wi (wcel (cv w) U) (wn (wal v (wn (wi (wi (wal t (wi (wcel (cv t) (cv v)) (wcel (cv t) (cv z)))) (wn (wi (wcel (cv v) U) (wn (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))))) (wn (wal i (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv z)) (wi (wcel (cv v) U) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv f2)) (wn (wal u (wi (wcel (cv u) (cv f2)) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wn (wal o (wi (wcel (cv o) (cv u)) (wal s1 (wi (wcel (cv s1) (cv o)) (wcel (cv s1) (cv w))))))))))))))))))))))))))) $;

--| Express "every set is contained in a Grothendieck universe" using only primitives. The right side (without the outermost universal quantifier) is proven as ~ rr-grothprim . (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_grothprimbi {x3 y1 z w v u t f2 i o s1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wal x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) cgru)) (wal x3 (wn (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal f2 (wn (wal w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wn (wal v (wn (wi (wi (wal t (wi (wcel (cv t) (cv v)) (wcel (cv t) (cv z)))) (wn (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))))) (wn (wal i (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv z)) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv f2)) (wn (wal u (wi (wcel (cv u) (cv f2)) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wn (wal o (wi (wcel (cv o) (cv u)) (wal s1 (wi (wcel (cv s1) (cv o)) (wcel (cv s1) (cv w)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))) $;

--| Inaccessible levels of the cumulative hierarchy are Grothendieck universes. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem inagrud (ph: wff) (I: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel I cina) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv I cr1) cgru) $;

--| Assuming the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom, every ordinal is contained in an inaccessible ordinal. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem inaex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 con0) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) cina) $;

--| Assuming the Tarski-Grothendieck axiom, every set is contained in a Grothendieck universe. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem gruex {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) cgru $;

--| An equivalent of ~ ax-groth using only simple defined symbols. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_groth {x3 y1 z w v u f2 i: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv y1)) (wal f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) (cv f2))) (cv y1)) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (cv f2))) (cv z))) (cv y1)))) (cv y1))) $;

--| An equivalent of ~ ax-groth using only primitives. This uses only 123 symbols, which is significantly less than the previous record of 163 established by ~ grothprim (which uses some defined symbols, and requires 229 symbols if expanded to primitives). (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 13-Aug-2023.)
theorem rr_grothprim {x3 y1 z w v u t f2 i o s1: setvar}:
  $ wn (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wn (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wal f2 (wn (wal w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wn (wal v (wn (wi (wi (wal t (wi (wcel (cv t) (cv v)) (wcel (cv t) (cv z)))) (wn (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv v) (cv w)))))) (wn (wal i (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv z)) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv y1)) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wi (wcel (cv v) (cv f2)) (wn (wal u (wi (wcel (cv u) (cv f2)) (wi (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wn (wal o (wi (wcel (cv o) (cv u)) (wal s1 (wi (wcel (cv s1) (cv o)) (wcel (cv s1) (cv w)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))) $;

--| Express the predicate on ` U ` and ` z ` in ~ ismnu in a shorter form while avoiding complicated definitions. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 10-Oct-2024.)
theorem ismnushort {w v u f2 i: setvar} (z: setvar) (U: class):
  $ wb (wral f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cin U (cv w))) (wss (cin (cv z) (cuni (cv f2))) (cuni (cin (cv f2) (cpw (cpw (cv w))))))) U) (cpw U)) (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) U) (wal f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cv w)) (wral i (wi (wrex v (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv v)) (wcel (cv v) (cv f2))) U) (wrex u (wa (wcel (cv i) (cv u)) (wss (cuni (cv u)) (cv w))) (cv f2))) (cv z))) U))) $;

--| Alternative definition of ` Univ ` using only simple defined symbols. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 10-Oct-2024.)
theorem dfuniv2 {y1 z w f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgru (cab y1 (wral z (wral f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cin (cv y1) (cv w))) (wss (cin (cv z) (cuni (cv f2))) (cuni (cin (cv f2) (cpw (cpw (cv w))))))) (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1))) (cv y1))) $;

--| Express "every set is contained in a Grothendieck universe" in a short form while avoiding complicated definitions. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 8-Oct-2024.)
theorem rr_grothshortbi {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar}:
  $ wb (wal x3 (wrex y1 (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) cgru)) (wal x3 (wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wral f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cin (cv y1) (cv w))) (wss (cin (cv z) (cuni (cv f2))) (cuni (cin (cv f2) (cpw (cpw (cv w))))))) (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1))) (cv y1))))) $;

--| A shorter equivalent of ~ ax-groth than ~ rr-groth using a few more simple defined symbols. (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 8-Oct-2024.)
theorem rr_grothshort {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar}:
  $ wex y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wral z (wral f2 (wrex w (wa (wss (cpw (cv z)) (cin (cv y1) (cv w))) (wss (cin (cv z) (cuni (cv f2))) (cuni (cin (cv f2) (cpw (cpw (cv w))))))) (cv y1)) (cpw (cv y1))) (cv y1))) $;

--| 'nand' is equivalent to the equivalence of inclusive and exclusive or. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Feb-2020.)
theorem nanorxor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wnan ph ps) (wb (wo ph ps) (wxo ph ps)) $;

--| Union of two disjoint restricted class abstractions; compare ~ unrab . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Feb-2020.)
theorem undisjrab {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (wceq (cin (crab x3 ph A2) (crab x3 ps A2)) c0) (wceq (cun (crab x3 ph A2) (crab x3 ps A2)) (crab x3 (wxo ph ps) A2)) $;

--| The empty set is an ` R , S ` isomorphism from the empty set to the empty set. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 24-Oct-2015.)
theorem iso0 (R S: class):
  $ wiso c0 c0 R S c0 $;

--| ` RR ` is a subset of both ` RR ` and ` CC ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Nov-2015.)
theorem ssrecnpr (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wss cr S) $;

--| Let set ` S ` be the real or complex numbers. Then the exponential function restricted to ` S ` is a mapping from ` S ` to ` S ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 6-Nov-2015.)
theorem seff (ph: wff) (S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S S (cres ce S)) $;

--| The infinity ball in the absolute value metric is just the whole space. ` S ` analogue of ~ blpnf . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 8-Nov-2015.)
theorem sblpnf (ph: wff) (D P S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cres (ccom cabs cmin) (cxp S S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel P S)) (wceq (co P cpnf (cfv D cbl)) S) $;

--| The primes are unbounded. This generalizes ~ prmunb to real ` A ` with ~ arch and ~ lttrd : every real is less than some positive integer, itself less than some prime. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem prmunb2 {p: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wrex p (wbr A2 (cv p) clt) cprime) $;

--| Ratio test for divergence of a complex infinite series. See e.g. remark "if ` ( abs `` ( ( a `` ( n + 1 ) ) / ( a `` n ) ) ) >_ 1 ` for all large n..." in ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratio_test#The_test . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Feb-2020.)
theorem dvgrat {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N V W Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) W)) (wne (cfv (cv k) F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) W)) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cabs) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel (cseq caddc F M) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Ratio test for convergence and divergence of a complex infinite series. If the ratio ` R ` of the absolute values of successive terms in an infinite sequence ` F ` converges to less than one, then the infinite sum of the terms of ` F ` converges to a complex number; and if ` R ` converges _greater_ then the sum diverges. This combined form of ~ cvgrat and ~ dvgrat directly uses the limit of the ratio. (It also demonstrates how to use ~ climi2 and ~ absltd to transform a limit to an inequality cf. ~ https://math.stackexchange.com/q/2215191 , and how to use ~ r19.29a in a similar fashion to Mario Carneiro's proof sketch with ~ rexlimdva at ~ https://groups.google.com/g/metamath/c/2RPikOiXLMo .) (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Feb-2020.)
theorem cvgdvgrat {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (R F L M N: class) (V: class k) (W Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) W)) (wne (cfv (cv k) F) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt k W (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cdiv) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr R L cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne L c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr L c1 clt) (wcel (cseq caddc F M) (cdm cli))) $;

--| Let ` L ` be the limit, if one exists, of the ratio ` ( abs `` ( ( A `` ( k + 1 ) ) / ( A `` k ) ) ) ` (as in the ratio test ~ cvgdvgrat ) as ` k ` increases. Then the radius of convergence of power series ` sum_ n e. NN0 ( ( A `` n ) x. ( x ^ n ) ) ` is ` ( 1 / L ) ` if ` L ` is nonzero. Proof "The limit involved in the ratio test..." in ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radius_of_convergence &mdash;a few lines that evidently hide quite an involved process to confirm. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 8-Mar-2020.)
theorem radcnvrat {x3 k n r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2: class k r) (D: class x3 n r) (R: class x3 k n r) (G: class) (L: class n r) (M: class x3 n r) (Z: class x3 r):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt k cn0 (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) A2) (cfv (cv k) A2) cdiv) cabs)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wne (cfv (cv k) A2) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D L cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne L cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (co c1 L cdiv)) $;

--| The divides relation is in fact a relation. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem reldvds:
  $ wrel cdvds $;

--| All positive integers in the set of multiples of _n_, <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> are the absolute value of _n_ or greater. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem nznngen (ph: wff) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cin (cima cdvds (csn N)) cn) (cfv (cfv N cabs) cuz)) $;

--| The set of multiples of _m_, <HTML><i>m</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> is a subset of those of _n_, <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> iff _n_ divides _m_. Lemma 2.1(a) of ~ https://www.mscs.dal.ca/~~selinger/3343/handouts/ideals.pdf p. 5, with <HTML><i>m</i>&#x2124;</HTML> and <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;</HTML> as images of the divides relation under _m_ and _n_. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem nzss (ph: wff) (M N V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (cima cdvds (csn M)) (cima cdvds (csn N))) (wbr N M cdvds)) $;

--| The intersection of the set of multiples of _m_, <HTML><i>m</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> and those of _n_, <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> is the set of multiples of their least common multiple. Roughly Lemma 2.1(c) of ~ https://www.mscs.dal.ca/~~selinger/3343/handouts/ideals.pdf p. 5 and Problem 1(b) of ~ https://people.math.binghamton.edu/mazur/teach/40107/40107h16sol.pdf p. 1, with <HTML><i>m</i>&#x2124;</HTML> and <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;</HTML> as images of the divides relation under _m_ and _n_. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem nzin (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (cima cdvds (csn M)) (cima cdvds (csn N))) (cima cdvds (csn (co M N clcm)))) $;

--| Subtract one prime's multiples from an unequal prime's. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem nzprmdif (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cprime) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne M N) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdif (cima cdvds (csn M)) (cima cdvds (csn N))) (cdif (cima cdvds (csn M)) (cima cdvds (csn (co M N cmul))))) $;

--| Special case of ~ hashdvds : the count of multiples in <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;</HTML> restricted to an interval. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem hashnzfz (ph: wff) (J K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv (co J c1 cmin) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin (cima cdvds (csn N)) (co J K cfz)) chash) (co (cfv (co K N cdiv) cfl) (cfv (co (co J c1 cmin) N cdiv) cfl) cmin)) $;

--| Special case of ~ hashnzfz : the count of multiples in <HTML><i>n</i>&#x2124;,</HTML> _n_ greater than one, restricted to an interval starting at two. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem hashnzfz2 (ph: wff) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin (cima cdvds (csn N)) (co c2 K cfz)) chash) (cfv (co K N cdiv) cfl)) $;

--| As the upper bound ` K ` of the constraint interval ` ( J ... K ) ` in ~ hashnzfz increases, the resulting count of multiples tends to ` ( K / M ) ` &mdash;that is, there are approximately ` ( K / M ) ` multiples of ` M ` in a finite interval of integers. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem hashnzfzclim {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (J M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k (cfv (co J c1 cmin) cuz) (co (cfv (cin (cima cdvds (csn M)) (co J (cv k) cfz)) chash) (cv k) cdiv)) (co c1 M cdiv) cli) $;

--| Transfer a cancellation law like ~ mulcan to the function operation. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 16-Nov-2015.)
theorem caofcan {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 y1 z) (R S T F G H: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 T F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 S H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) T) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wb (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (co (cv x3) (cv z) R)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co F G (cof R)) (co F H (cof R))) (wceq G H)) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ subid . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 5-Nov-2015.)
theorem ofsubid (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F)) (wceq (co F F (cof cmin)) (cxp A2 (csn cc0))) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ mul12 . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 13-Nov-2015.)
theorem ofmul12 (A2 F G H V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wf A2 cc G) (wf A2 cc H))) (wceq (co F (co G H (cof cmul)) (cof cmul)) (co G (co F H (cof cmul)) (cof cmul))) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ divrec , a division analogue of ~ ofnegsub . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 3-Nov-2015.)
theorem ofdivrec (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) G)) (wceq (co F (co (cxp A2 (csn c1)) G (cof cdiv)) (cof cmul)) (co F G (cof cdiv))) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ divcan4 . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 4-Nov-2015.)
theorem ofdivcan4 (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) G)) (wceq (co (co F G (cof cmul)) G (cof cdiv)) F) $;

--| Function analogue of ~ divdiv2 . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 23-Nov-2015.)
theorem ofdivdiv2 (A2 F G H V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wf A2 cc F)) (wa (wf A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) G) (wf A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) H))) (wceq (co F (co G H (cof cdiv)) (cof cdiv)) (co (co F H (cof cmul)) G (cof cdiv))) $;

--| Example of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, part two ( ~ ftc2 ): ` S. ( 1 (,) 2 ) ( ( x ^ 2 ) - 3 ) _d x = -u ( 2 / 3 ) ` . Section 4.4 example 1a of [LarsonHostetlerEdwards] p. 311. (The book teaches ~ ftc2 as simply the "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus", then ~ ftc1 as the "Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus".) (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 28-Oct-2015.) (Revised by Steve Rodriguez, 31-Oct-2015.)
theorem lhe4_4ex1a {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (citg x3 (co c1 c2 cioo) (co (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) c3 cmin)) (cneg (co c2 c3 cdiv)) $;

--| Derivative of a constant function on the real or complex numbers. The function may return a complex ` A ` even if ` S ` is ` RR ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 11-Nov-2015.)
theorem dvsconst (A2 S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wcel A2 cc)) (wceq (co S (cxp S (csn A2)) cdv) (cxp S (csn cc0))) $;

--| Derivative of the identity function on the real or complex numbers. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 11-Nov-2015.)
theorem dvsid (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wceq (co S (cres cid S) cdv) (cxp S (csn c1))) $;

--| Derivative of the exponential function on the real or complex numbers. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 12-Nov-2015.)
theorem dvsef (S: class):
  $ wi (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) (wceq (co S (cres ce S) cdv) (cres ce S)) $;

--| Exponential growth and decay model. See ~ expgrowth for more information. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 4-Nov-2015.)
theorem expgrowthi {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (C S K: class) (Y: class t):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpt t S (co C (cfv (co K (cv t) cmul) ce) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S Y cdv) (co (cxp S (csn K)) Y (cof cmul))) $;

--| The derivative of a function on ` S ` is zero iff it is a constant function. Roughly a biconditional ` S ` analogue of ~ dvconst and ~ dveq0 . Corresponds to integration formula " ` S. 0 _d x = C ` " in section 4.1 of [LarsonHostetlerEdwards] p. 278. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 11-Nov-2015.)
theorem dvconstbi {c: setvar} (ph: wff c) (S Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S Y cdv)) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co S Y cdv) (cxp S (csn cc0))) (wrex c (wceq Y (cxp S (csn (cv c)))) cc)) $;

--| Exponential growth and decay model. The derivative of a function _y_ of variable _t_ equals a constant _k_ times _y_ itself, iff _y_ equals some constant _C_ times the exponential of _kt_. This theorem and ~ expgrowthi illustrate one of the simplest and most crucial classes of _differential equations_, equations that relate functions to their derivatives. Section 6.3 of [Strang] p. 242 calls _y_' = _ky_ "the most important differential equation in applied mathematics". In the field of population ecology it is known as the _Malthusian growth model_ or _exponential law_, and _C_, _k_, and _t_ correspond to initial population size, growth rate, and time respectively ( ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malthusian_growth_model ); and in finance, the model appears in a similar role in _continuous compounding_ with _C_ as the initial amount of money. In _exponential decay_ models, _k_ is often expressed as the negative of a positive constant &lambda;. Here _y_' is given as ` ( S _D Y ) ` , _C_ as ` c ` , and _ky_ as ` ( ( S X. { K } ) oF x. Y ) ` . ` ( S X. { K } ) ` is the constant function that maps any real or complex input to _k_ and ` oF x. ` is multiplication as a function operation. The leftward direction of the biconditional is as given in ~ http://www.saylor.org/site/wp-content/uploads/2011/06/MA221-2.1.1.pdf pp. 1-2, which also notes the reverse direction ("While we will not prove this here, it turns out that these are the only functions that satisfy this equation."). The rightward direction is Theorem 5.1 of [LarsonHostetlerEdwards] p. 375 (which notes " _C_ is the _initial value_ of _y_, and _k_ is the _proportionality constant_. _Exponential growth_ occurs when _k_ > 0, and _exponential decay_ occurs when _k_ < 0."); its proof here closely follows the proof of _y_' = _y_ in ~ https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Exponential_Growth_Equation/Special_Case . Statements for this and ~ expgrowthi formulated by Mario Carneiro. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 24-Nov-2015.)
theorem expgrowth {t c: setvar} (ph: wff t c) (S K: class) (Y: class t):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S cc Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S Y cdv)) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co S Y cdv) (co (cxp S (csn K)) Y (cof cmul))) (wrex c (wceq Y (cmpt t S (co (cv c) (cfv (co K (cv t) cmul) ce) cmul))) cc)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the generalized binomial coefficient operation.
term cbcc: class;

--| Define a generalized binomial coefficient operation, which unlike ~ df-bc allows complex numbers for the first argument. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
axiom df_bcc {k c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cbcc (cmpo c k cc cn0 (co (co (cv c) (cv k) cfallfac) (cfv (cv k) cfa) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of the generalized binomial coefficient, ` C ` choose ` K ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccval (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C K cbcc) (co (co C K cfallfac) (cfv K cfa) cdiv)) $;

--| Closure of the generalized binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bcccl (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C K cbcc) cc) $;

--| The generalized binomial coefficient ` C ` choose ` K ` is zero iff ` C ` is an integer between zero and ` ( K - 1 ) ` inclusive. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bcc0 (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co C K cbcc) cc0) (wcel C (co cc0 (co K c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| Generalized binomial coefficient: ` C ` choose ` ( K + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccp1k (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (co K c1 caddc) cbcc) (co (co C K cbcc) (co (co C K cmin) (co K c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Generalized binomial coefficient: ` C ` choose ` ( K - 1 ) ` , when ` C ` is not ` ( K - 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccm1k (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C (cdif cc (csn (co K c1 cmin)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C (co K c1 cmin) cbcc) (co (co C K cbcc) (co (co C (co K c1 cmin) cmin) K cdiv) cdiv)) $;

--| Generalized binomial coefficient: ` C ` choose ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccn0 (ph: wff) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C cc0 cbcc) c1) $;

--| Generalized binomial coefficient: ` C ` choose ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccn1 (ph: wff) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C c1 cbcc) C) $;

--| The binomial coefficient and generalized binomial coefficient are equal when their arguments are nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccbc (ph: wff) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co N K cbcc) (co N K cbc)) $;

--| When ` F ` is a maps-to function on some set of upper integers ` Z ` that returns a set ` B ` , ` ( F shift N ) ` is another maps-to function on the shifted set of upper integers ` W ` . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem uzmptshftfval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class y1) (F M: class x3) (N: class) (W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 Z B2) $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) N cmin)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv (co M N caddc) cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F N cshi) (cmpt y1 W C)) $;

--| The radius of convergence of the (formal) derivative ` H ` of the power series ` G ` is (at least) as large as the radius of convergence of ` G ` . This version of ~ dvradcnv uses a shifted version of ` H ` to match the sum form of ` ( CC _D F ) ` in ~ pserdv2 (and shows how to use ~ uzmptshftfval to shift a maps-to function on a set of upper integers). (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvradcnv2 {x3 n r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n r) (A2: class r) (R: class x3 n r) (G: class x3 n) (H: class x3 n r) (X: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (co (cv x3) (cv n) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) G) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (co (co (cv n) (cfv (cv n) A2) cmul) (co X (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cc A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv X cabs) R clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc H c1) (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . The lemma in the Wikibooks proof. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemwb (ph: wff) (C K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co C K cmin) (co C K cbcc) cmul) (co (co C (co K c1 cmin) cmin) (co C (co K c1 cmin) cbcc) cmul) caddc) (co C (co C K cbcc) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . When ` C ` is a nonnegative integer, the binomial's finite sum value by the standard binomial theorem ~ binom equals this generalized infinite sum: the generalized binomial coefficient and exponentiation operators give exactly the same values in the standard index set ` ( 0 ... C ) ` , and when the index set is widened beyond ` C ` the additional values are just zeroes. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemnn0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel C cn0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C ccxp) (csu k cn0 (co (co C (cv k) cbcc) (co (co A2 (co C (cv k) cmin) ccxp) (co B2 (cv k) cexp) cmul) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . As ` k ` increases, this ratio's absolute value converges to one. Part of equation "Since continuity of the absolute value..." in the Wikibooks proof (proven for the inverse ratio, which we later show is no problem). (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemrat {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class k) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k cn0 (cfv (co (co C (cv k) cmin) (co (cv k) c1 caddc) cdiv) cabs)) c1 cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . ~ binomcxplemrat implies that when ` C ` is not a nonnegative integer, the absolute value of the ratio ` ( ( F `` ( k + 1 ) ) / ( F `` k ) ) ` converges to one. The rest of equation "Since continuity of the absolute value..." in the Wikibooks proof. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemfrat {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class j k) (C: class) (F: class j k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wcel C cn0))) (wbr (cmpt k cn0 (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cdiv) cabs)) c1 cli) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . By ~ binomcxplemfrat and ~ radcnvrat the radius of convergence of power series ` sum_ k e. NN0 ( ( F `` k ) x. ( b ^ k ) ) ` is one. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemradcnv {j k r b: setvar} (ph: wff r b) (A2 B2: class j k r b) (C: class r b) (R: class j k r b) (S: class j k b) (F: class j r):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv b) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) S) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wcel C cn0))) (wceq R c1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . By the power and chain rules, calculate the derivative of ` ( ( 1 + b ) ^c -u C ) ` , with respect to ` b ` in the disk of convergence ` D ` . We later multiply the derivative in the later ~ binomcxplemdvsum by this derivative to show that ` ( ( 1 + b ) ^c C ) ` (with a nonnegated ` C ` ) and the later sum, since both at ` b = 0 ` equal one, are the same. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemdvbinom {j k r b: setvar} (ph: wff r b) (A2 B2: class j k r b) (C: class r) (D R: class j k r b) (S: class j k b) (E: class j k r b) (F: class j r):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv b) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) S) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn (co (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) F) cmul) (co (cv b) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wcel C cn0))) (wceq (co cc (cmpt b D (co (co c1 (cv b) caddc) (cneg C) ccxp)) cdv) (cmpt b D (co (cneg C) (co (co c1 (cv b) caddc) (co (cneg C) c1 cmin) ccxp) cmul))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . The sum in ~ binomcxplemnn0 and its derivative (see the next theorem, ~ binomcxplemdvsum ) converge, as long as their base ` J ` is within the disk of convergence. Part of remark "This convergence allows us to apply term-by-term differentiation..." in the Wikibooks proof. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemcvg {j k r b: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2 B2 C D R: class j k r b) (S: class j k b) (E: class j k r b) (F: class j r) (J: class j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv b) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) S) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn (co (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) F) cmul) (co (cv b) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel J D)) (wa (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv J S) cc0) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv J E) c1) (cdm cli))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . The derivative of the generalized sum in ~ binomcxplemnn0 . Part of remark "This convergence allows to apply term-by-term differentiation..." in the Wikibooks proof. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemdvsum {j k r b: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2 B2: class j k r b) (C: class k r b) (D P R S E: class j k r b) (F: class j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv b) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) S) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn (co (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) F) cmul) (co (cv b) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt b D (csu k cn0 (cfv (cv k) (cfv (cv b) S)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc P cdv) (cmpt b D (csu k cn (cfv (cv k) (cfv (cv b) E))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ binomcxp . When ` C ` is not a nonnegative integer, the generalized sum in ~ binomcxplemnn0 &mdash;which we will call ` P ` &mdash;is a convergent power series: its base ` b ` is always of smaller absolute value than the radius of convergence. ~ pserdv2 gives the derivative of ` P ` , which by ~ dvradcnv also converges in that radius. When ` A ` is fixed at one, ` ( A + b ) ` times that derivative equals ` ( C x. P ) ` and fraction ` ( P / ( ( A + b ) ^c C ) ) ` is always defined with derivative zero, so the fraction is a constant&mdash;specifically one, because ` ( ( 1 + 0 ) ^c C ) = 1 ` . Thus ` ( ( 1 + b ) ^c C ) = ( P `` b ) ` . Finally, let ` b ` be ` ( B / A ) ` , and multiply both the binomial ` ( ( 1 + ( B / A ) ) ^c C ) ` and the sum ` ( P `` ( B / A ) ) ` by ` ( A ^c C ) ` to get the result. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxplemnotnn0 {j k r b: setvar} (ph: wff r) (A2 B2: class j) (C: class r) (D: class r b) (P R: class j k r b) (S: class j b) (E: class r b) (F: class j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn0 (co C (cv j) cbcc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn0 (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cv b) (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq R (csup (crab r (wcel (cseq caddc (cfv (cv r) S) cc0) (cdm cli)) cr) cxr clt) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt b cc (cmpt k cn (co (co (cv k) (cfv (cv k) F) cmul) (co (cv b) (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cima (ccnv cabs) (co cc0 R cico)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt b D (csu k cn0 (cfv (cv k) (cfv (cv b) S)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn (wcel C cn0))) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C ccxp) (csu k cn0 (co (co C (cv k) cbcc) (co (co A2 (co C (cv k) cmin) ccxp) (co B2 (cv k) cexp) cmul) cmul))) $;

--| Generalize the binomial theorem ~ binom to positive real summand ` A ` , real summand ` B ` , and complex exponent ` C ` . Proof in ~ https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Advanced_Calculus ; see also ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_series , ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem (sections "Newton's generalized binomial theorem" and "Future generalizations"), and proof "General Binomial Theorem" in ~ https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Binomial_Theorem . (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 22-Apr-2020.)
theorem binomcxp {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C ccxp) (csu k cn0 (co (co C (cv k) cbcc) (co (co A2 (co C (cv k) cmin) ccxp) (co B2 (cv k) cexp) cmul) cmul))) $;

--| Theorem *10.12 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 146. In *10, this is treated as an axiom, and the proofs in *10 are based on this theorem. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm10_12 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Theorem *10.14 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 146. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm10_14 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) (wa (wsb x3 ph y1) (wsb x3 ps y1)) $;

--| Theorem *10.251 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 149. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm10_251 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wn ph)) (wn (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Theorem *10.252 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 149. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem pm10_252 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wn (wex x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| Theorem *10.253 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 149. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm10_253 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wn (wal x3 ph)) (wex x3 (wn ph)) $;

--| Theorem *10.301 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 151. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem albitr {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wal x3 (wb ps ch))) (wal x3 (wb ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *10.42 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 155. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm10_42 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wo (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 ps)) (wex x3 (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *10.52 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 155. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_52 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) (wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) ps) $;

--| Theorem *10.53 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 155. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_53 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *10.541 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 155. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_541 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph (wo ch ps))) (wo ch (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *10.542 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 156. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_542 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi ph (wi ch ps))) (wi ch (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *10.55 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 156. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_55 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (wa (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) (wa (wex x3 ph) (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *10.56 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 156. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_56 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ch))) (wex x3 (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *10.57 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 156. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm10_57 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph (wo ps ch))) (wo (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wex x3 (wa ph ch))) $;

--| Removes two universal quantifiers from a statement. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2alanimi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) (wal x3 (wal y1 ch)) $;

--| Removes two universal quantifiers from a statement. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2al2imi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ps)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ch))) $;

--| Theorem *11.11 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 159. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm11_11 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wal z (wal w (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z)) $;

--| Theorem *11.12 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 159. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 17-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm11_12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wo ph ps))) (wo ph (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Compare Theorem *11.3 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 161. Special case of theorem 19.21 of [Margaris] p. 90 with two quantifiers. See ~ 19.21v . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_21vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ps ph))) (wi ps (wal x3 (wal y1 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *11.32 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 162. Theorem 19.20 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2alim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.33 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 162. Theorem 19.15 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2albi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wb ph ps))) (wb (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.34 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 162. Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2exim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.341 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 162. Theorem 19.18 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _2exbi {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wb ph ps))) (wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex x3 (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.36 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 162. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem spsbce_2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w):
  $ wi (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph w) z) (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| Theorem *11.421 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 163. Theorem 19.33 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_33_2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wo (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wal y1 ps))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wo ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.43 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 163. Theorem 19.36 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem _19_36vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wi ph ps))) (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) ps) $;

--| Theorem *11.44 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 163. Theorem 19.31 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_31vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wo ph ps))) (wo (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) ps) $;

--| Theorem *11.46 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. Theorem 19.37 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_37vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wi ps ph))) (wi ps (wex x3 (wex y1 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *11.47 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. Theorem 19.28 of [Margaris] p. 90 with 2 quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem _19_28vv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wa ps ph))) (wa ps (wal x3 (wal y1 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *11.52 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 164. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_52 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph ps))) (wn (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wn ps))))) $;

--| Theorem *11.56 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 165. Special case of ~ aaan . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem aaanv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wb (wa (wal x3 ph) (wal y1 ps)) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.57 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 165. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_57 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)))) $;

--| Theorem *11.58 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 165. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_58 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 ph) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)))) $;

--| Theorem *11.59 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 165. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_59 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wal y1 (wal x3 (wi (wa ph (wsb x3 ph y1)) (wa ps (wsb x3 ps y1))))) $;

--| Theorem *11.6 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 165. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_6 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1) (ch: wff x3):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wa (wex y1 (wa ph ps)) ch)) (wex y1 (wa (wex x3 (wa ph ch)) ps)) $;

--| Theorem *11.61 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 166. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_61 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wex y1 (wal x3 (wi ph ps))) (wal x3 (wi ph (wex y1 ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.62 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 166. Importation combined with the rearrangement with quantifiers. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_62 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph ps) ch))) (wal x3 (wi ph (wal y1 (wi ps ch)))) $;

--| Theorem *11.63 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 166. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_63 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wn (wex x3 (wex y1 ph))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *11.7 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 166. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wex y1 (wo ph ph))) (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| Theorem *11.71 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 166. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 24-May-2011.)
theorem pm11_71 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch th: wff y1):
  $ wi (wa (wex x3 ph) (wex y1 ch)) (wb (wa (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wal y1 (wi ch th))) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph ch) (wa ps th))))) $;

--| If ` x = y ` always implies ` x = z ` , then ` y = z ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 2-Jun-2011.)
theorem sbeqal1 {x3 y1: setvar} (z: setvar):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) $;

--| Suppose you know ` x = y ` implies ` x = z ` , assuming ` x ` and ` z ` are distinct. Then, ` y = z ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem sbeqal1i {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) $ >
  $ wceq (cv y1) (cv z) $;

--| If ` x = y ` implies ` x = z ` , then we can infer ` z = y ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem sbeqal2i {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) $ >
  $ wceq (cv z) (cv y1) $;

--| Proof of a theorem that can act as a sole axiom for pure predicate calculus with ~ ax-gen as the inference rule. This proof extends the idea of ~ axc5c711 and related theorems. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.)
theorem axc5c4c711 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wn (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wal y1 ph) ps)))))) (wi ph (wal y1 (wi (wal y1 ph) ps)))) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal y1 ps)) $;

--| Proof of a theorem that can act as a sole axiom for pure predicate calculus with ~ ax-gen as the inference rule. This proof extends the idea of ~ axc5c711 and related theorems. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.)
theorem axc5c4c711_b {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wi (wal x3 (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 (wal x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) ps)))))) (wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) ps)))) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Rederivation of ~ sp from ~ axc5c4c711 . Note that ~ ax6 is used for the rederivation. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.) Revised to use ~ ax6v instead of ~ ax6 , so that this rederivation requires only ~ ax6v and propositional calculus. (Revised by BJ, 14-Sep-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc5c4c711toc5 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) ph $;

--| Rederivation of ~ axc4 from ~ axc5c4c711 . Note that only propositional calculus is required for the rederivation. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc5c4c711toc4 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wal x3 ph) ps)) (wi (wal x3 ph) (wal x3 ps)) $;

--| Rederivation of ~ axc7 from ~ axc5c4c711 . Note that neither ~ axc7 nor ~ ax-11 are required for the rederivation. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc5c4c711toc7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wn (wal x3 ph)))) ph $;

--| Rederivation of ~ ax-11 from ~ axc5c4c711 . Note that ~ ax-11 is not required for the rederivation. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc5c4c711to11 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| This theorem shows that, given ~ axextb , we can derive a version of ~ axc11n . However, it is weaker than ~ axc11n because it has a distinct variable requirement. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem axc11next {x3 z: setvar}:
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv z))) (wal z (wceq (cv z) (cv x3))) $;

--| One result of theorem *13.13 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 178. A note on the section - to make the theorems more usable, and because inequality is notation for set theory (it is not defined in the predicate calculus section), this section will use classes instead of sets. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_13a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) A2)) (wsbc x3 ph A2) $;

--| Theorem *13.13 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 178 with different variable substitution. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_13b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wceq (cv x3) A2)) ph $;

--| Theorem *13.14 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 178. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_14 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wn ph)) (wne (cv x3) A2) $;

--| Theorem *13.192 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.) (Revised by NM, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem pm13_192 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wa (wal x3 (wb (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ph)) (wsbc y1 ph A2) $;

--| Theorem *13.193 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_193 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wa (wsb x3 ph y1) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Theorem *13.194 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_194 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wb (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (w3a (wsb x3 ph y1) ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Theorem *13.195 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. This theorem is very similar to ~ sbc5 . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.) (Revised by NM, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem pm13_195 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) A2) ph)) (wsbc y1 ph A2) $;

--| Theorem *13.196 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. The only difference is the position of the substituted variable. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm13_196a {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wn ph) (wal y1 (wi (wsb x3 ph y1) (wne (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| Theorem *13.21 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem _2sbc6g {z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wb (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2)) ph))) (wsbc z (wsbc w ph B2) A2)) $;

--| Theorem *13.22 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 179. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem _2sbc5g {z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wb (wex z (wex w (wa (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2)) ph))) (wsbc z (wsbc w ph B2) A2)) $;

--| The iota class exists. This theorem does not require ~ ax-nul for its proof. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotaexeu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wcel (cio x3 ph) cvv) $;

--| Definition *14.01 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 184. In Principia Mathematica, Russell and Whitehead define ` iota ` in terms of a function of ` ( iota x ph ) ` . Their definition differs in that a function of ` ( iota x ph ) ` evaluates to "false" when there isn't a single ` x ` that satisfies ` ph ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotasbc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wsbc y1 ps (cio x3 ph)) (wex y1 (wa (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) ps))) $;

--| Theorem *14.111 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 184. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotasbc2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa (weu x3 ph) (weu x3 ps)) (wb (wsbc y1 (wsbc z ch (cio x3 ps)) (cio x3 ph)) (wex y1 (wex z (w3a (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wal x3 (wb ps (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)))) ch)))) $;

--| Theorem *14.12 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 184. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem pm14_12 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa ph (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Theorem *14.122 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_122a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wa (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wsbc x3 ph A2))) $;

--| Theorem *14.122 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_122b {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wa (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wsbc x3 ph A2)) (wa (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.122 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_122c {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wa (wal x3 (wi ph (wceq (cv x3) A2))) (wex x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.123 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_123a {z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wal z (wal w (wb ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wa (wal z (wal w (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wsbc z (wsbc w ph B2) A2))) $;

--| Theorem *14.123 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_123b {z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wa (wal z (wal w (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wsbc z (wsbc w ph B2) A2)) (wa (wal z (wal w (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wex z (wex w ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *14.123 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 185. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 9-Jun-2011.)
theorem pm14_123c {z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (A2 B2: class) (V W: class z w):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wal z (wal w (wb ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wa (wal z (wal w (wi ph (wa (wceq (cv z) A2) (wceq (cv w) B2))))) (wex z (wex w ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *14.18 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 189. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem pm14_18 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wi (wal x3 ps) (wsbc x3 ps (cio x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.2 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 189. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotaequ {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cio x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv y1) $;

--| Theorem *14.202 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 189. A biconditional version of ~ iotaval . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotavalb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cio x3 ph) (cv y1))) $;

--| Theorem *14.205 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 190. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotasbc5 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wsbc y1 ps (cio x3 ph)) (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cio x3 ph)) ps))) $;

--| Theorem *14.24 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 191. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 12-Jul-2011.)
theorem pm14_24 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wal y1 (wb (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) (wceq (cv y1) (cio x3 ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *14.242 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotavalsb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 z) (ps: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wb (wsbc z ps (cv y1)) (wsbc z ps (cio x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.25 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 12-Jul-2011.)
theorem sbiota1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wsbc x3 ps (cio x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.26 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 192. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 12-Jul-2011.)
theorem sbaniota {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wb (wex x3 (wa ph ps)) (wsbc x3 ps (cio x3 ph))) $;

--| Theorem *14.272 in [WhiteheadRussell] p. 193. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 11-Jul-2011.)
theorem iotasbcq {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph ps)) (wb (wsbc y1 ch (cio x3 ph)) (wsbc y1 ch (cio x3 ps))) $;

--| Any set that contains one element less than the universe is not equal to it. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 16-Jun-2011.)
theorem elnev {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cvv) (wne (cab x3 (wn (wceq (cv x3) A2))) cvv) $;

--| A version of Russell's paradox which is proven using proper substitution. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 18-Jun-2011.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem rusbcALT {x3: setvar}:
  $ wnel (cab x3 (wnel (cv x3) (cv x3))) cvv $;

--| Equality between two ways of saying "the complement of ` A ` ". (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2011.)
theorem compeq {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cdif cvv A2) (cab x3 (wn (wcel (cv x3) A2))) $;

--| The complement of ` A ` is not equal to ` A ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by BJ, 11-Nov-2021.)
theorem compne (A2: class):
  $ wne (cdif cvv A2) A2 $;

--| Two ways of saying "the complement of a class abstraction". (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 11-Dec-2016.)
theorem compab {z: setvar} (ph: wff z):
  $ wceq (cdif cvv (cab z ph)) (cab z (wn ph)) $;

--| Contrapositive law for subsets. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2011.)
theorem conss2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wss A2 (cdif cvv B2)) (wss B2 (cdif cvv A2)) $;

--| Contrapositive law for subsets. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2011.)
theorem conss1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wss (cdif cvv A2) B2) (wss (cdif cvv B2) A2) $;

--| More general form of ~ ralbida . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem ralbidar {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wral x3 ph A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 ps A2) (wral x3 ch A2)) $;

--| More general form of ~ rexbida . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem rexbidar {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wral x3 ph A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 ps A2) (wrex x3 ch A2)) $;

--| Theorem to aid use of the distinctor reduction theorem with ordered pair class abstraction. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem dropab1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (copab x3 z ph) (copab y1 z ph)) $;

--| Theorem to aid use of the distinctor reduction theorem with ordered pair class abstraction. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem dropab2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq (copab z x3 ph) (copab z y1 ph)) $;

--| If the identity relation partially orders any class, then that class is the null class. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem ipo0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wpo A2 cid) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| A class that is founded by the identity relation is null. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 25-Jul-2011.)
theorem ifr0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wfr A2 cid) (wceq A2 c0) $;

--| Explicit substitution of a value of a function into a wff. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 1-Aug-2011.)
theorem fvsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) (wb (wsbc x3 ph (cfv A2 F)) (wex x3 (wa (wal y1 (wb (wbr A2 (cv y1) F) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) ph))) $;

--| Implicit substitution of a value of a function into a wff. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 1-Aug-2011.)
theorem fveqsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cfv A2 F)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) (wb ps (wex x3 (wa (wal y1 (wb (wbr A2 (cv y1) F) (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)))) ph))) $;

--| A Cartesian product exists iff its converse does. Corollary 6.9(1) in [TakeutiZaring] p. 26. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 13-Nov-2011.)
theorem xpexb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel (cxp A2 B2) cvv) (wcel (cxp B2 A2) cvv) $;

--| An element of a transitive set is a proper subset of it. Theorem 7.2 in [TakeutiZaring] p. 35. Unlike ~ tz7.2 , ~ ax-reg is required for its proof. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 13-Nov-2011.)
theorem trelpss (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wpss B2 A2) $;

--| Generalization of commutative law for addition. Simplifies proofs dealing with vectors. However, it is dependent on our particular definition of ordered pair. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 28-Jan-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-May-2015.)
theorem addcomgi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc) $;

--| Introduce the operation of vector addition.
term cplusr: class;

--| Introduce the operation of vector subtraction.
term cminusr: class;

--| Introduce the operation of scalar multiplication.
term ctimesr: class;

--| ` PtDf ` is a predicate that is crucial for the definition of lines as well as proving a number of important theorems.
term cptdfc (A2 B2: class): class;

--| ` RR3 ` is a class.
term crr3c: class;

--| ` line3 ` is a class.
term cline3: class;

--| Define the operation of vector addition. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
axiom df_addr {x3 y1 v: setvar}:
  $ wceq cplusr (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt v cr (co (cfv (cv v) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv v) (cv y1)) caddc))) $;

--| Define the operation of vector subtraction. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
axiom df_subr {x3 y1 v: setvar}:
  $ wceq cminusr (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt v cr (co (cfv (cv v) (cv x3)) (cfv (cv v) (cv y1)) cmin))) $;

--| Define the operation of scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
axiom df_mulv {x3 y1 v: setvar}:
  $ wceq ctimesr (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (cmpt v cr (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv v) (cv y1)) cmul))) $;

--| Value of the operation of vector addition. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem addrval {v: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class v):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cplusr) (cmpt v cr (co (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv (cv v) B2) caddc))) $;

--| Value of the operation of vector subtraction. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem subrval {v: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class v):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cminusr) (cmpt v cr (co (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv (cv v) B2) cmin))) $;

--| Value of the operation of scalar multiplication. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem mulvval {v: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C D: class v):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 ctimesr) (cmpt v cr (co A2 (cfv (cv v) B2) cmul))) $;

--| Vector addition at a value. The operation takes each vector ` A ` and ` B ` and forms a new vector whose values are the sum of each of the values of ` A ` and ` B ` . (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem addrfv (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 E) (wcel B2 D) (wcel C cr)) (wceq (cfv C (co A2 B2 cplusr)) (co (cfv C A2) (cfv C B2) caddc)) $;

--| Vector subtraction at a value. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem subrfv (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 E) (wcel B2 D) (wcel C cr)) (wceq (cfv C (co A2 B2 cminusr)) (co (cfv C A2) (cfv C B2) cmin)) $;

--| Scalar multiplication at a value. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem mulvfv (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 E) (wcel B2 D) (wcel C cr)) (wceq (cfv C (co A2 B2 ctimesr)) (co A2 (cfv C B2) cmul)) $;

--| Vector addition produces a function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem addrfn (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wfn (co A2 B2 cplusr) cr) $;

--| Vector subtraction produces a function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem subrfn (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wfn (co A2 B2 cminusr) cr) $;

--| Scalar multiplication producees a function. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 27-Jan-2012.)
theorem mulvfn (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wfn (co A2 B2 ctimesr) cr) $;

--| Vector addition is commutative. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 28-Jan-2012.)
theorem addrcom (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 D)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cplusr) (co B2 A2 cplusr)) $;

--| Define the predicate ` PtDf ` , which is a utility definition used to shorten definitions and simplify proofs. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2012.)
axiom df_ptdf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cptdfc A2 B2) (cmpt x3 cr (cima (co (co (cv x3) (co B2 A2 cminusr) ctimesr) A2 cpv) (ctp c1 c2 c3))) $;

--| Define the set of all points ` RR3 ` . We define each point ` A ` as a function to allow the use of vector addition and subtraction as well as scalar multiplication in our proofs. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2012.)
axiom df_rr3:
  $ wceq crr3c (co cr (ctp c1 c2 c3) cmap) $;

--| Define the set of all lines. A line is an infinite subset of ` RR3 ` that satisfies a ` PtDf ` property. (Contributed by Andrew Salmon, 15-Jul-2012.)
axiom df_line3 {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cline3 (crab x3 (wa (wbr c2o (cv x3) cdom) (wral y1 (wral z (wi (wne (cv z) (cv y1)) (wceq (crn (cptdfc (cv y1) (cv z))) (cv x3))) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (cpw crr3c)) $;

--| Placeholder for ~ idi . Though unnecessary, this theorem is sometimes used in proofs in this mathbox for pedagogical purposes. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem idiALT (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Exportation implication also converting the consequent from a biconditional to an implication. Derived automatically from ~ exbirVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem exbir (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wa ph ps) (wb ch th)) (wi ph (wi ps (wi th ch))) $;

--| Version of ~ 3impexp where in addition the consequent is commuted. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem _3impexpbicom (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wb (wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wb th ta)) (wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wb ta th)))) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ 3impexpbicom . Derived automatically from ~ 3impexpbicomiVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.)
theorem _3impexpbicomi (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch (wb ta th))) $;

--| Importation inference similar to ~ imp , except the outermost implication of the hypothesis is a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi1imp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Importation inference similar to ~ imp , except both implications of the hypothesis are biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi2imp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Similar to ~ 3impb with implication in hypothesis replaced by biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi3impb (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Similar to ~ 3impa with implication in hypothesis replaced by biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi3impa (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3impib with the inner implication of the hypothesis a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi23impib (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3impib with the outer implication of the hypothesis a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi13impib (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wi (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3impib with the implications of the hypothesis biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi123impib (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3impia with the outer implication of the hypothesis a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi13impia (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3impia with the implications of the hypothesis biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi123impia (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3imp with innermost implication of the hypothesis a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi33imp12 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| ~ 3imp with outermost implication of the hypothesis a biconditional. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi13imp23 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Similar to ~ 3imp except the outermost and innermost implications are biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi13imp2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Similar to ~ 3imp except all but innermost implication are biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi12imp3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Similar to ~ 3imp except all but outermost implication are biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi23imp1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| Similar to ~ 3imp except all implications are biconditionals. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 6-Nov-2017.)
theorem bi123imp0 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (wb ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) th $;

--| A single hypothesis unification deduction with an assertion which is an implication with a 4-right-nested conjunction antecedent. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 30-May-2018.)
theorem _4animp1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wb ta th) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts for a 4-right-nested conjunction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 30-May-2018.)
theorem _4an31 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ch ps) ph) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts for a 4-right-nested conjunction. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 30-May-2018.)
theorem _4an4132 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa th ch) ps) ph) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| Biconditional form of ~ expcomd . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2012.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem expcomdg (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) th)) (wi ph (wi ch (wi ps th))) $;

--| ~ idn3 without virtual deduction connectives. Special theorem needed for the Virtual Deduction translation tool. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 23-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem iidn3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ch)) $;

--| ~ e222 without virtual deduction connectives. Special theorem needed for the Virtual Deduction translation tool. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 7-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee222 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $ >
  $ wi ch (wi th (wi ta et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps et) $;

--| Right-biconditional form of ~ e3 without virtual deduction connectives. Special theorem needed for the Virtual Deduction translation tool. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee3bir (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wb ta th $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $;

--| ~ e13 without virtual deduction connectives. Special theorem needed for the Virtual Deduction translation tool. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 28-Oct-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee13 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wi th ta)) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wi th et)) $;

--| ~ e121 without virtual deductions. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 13-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee121 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ta $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th (wi ta et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch et) $;

--| ~ e122 without virtual deductions. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 13-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee122 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch ta) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi th (wi ta et)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch et) $;

--| ~ e333 without virtual deductions. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 17-Jul-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee333 (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ta)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch et)) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta (wi et ze)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ze)) $;

--| ~ e323 without virtual deductions. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 17-Apr-2012.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ee323 (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch et)) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta (wi et ze)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi ch ze)) $;

--| If the second and third disjuncts of a true triple disjunction are false, then the first disjunct is true. Automatically derived from ~ 3ornot23VD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _3ornot23 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wi (w3o ch ph ps) ch) $;

--| ~ orbi1 with order of disjuncts reversed. Derived from ~ orbi1rVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem orbi1r (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wo ch ph) (wo ch ps)) $;

--| ~ pm4.39 with a 3-conjunct antecedent. This proof is ~ 3orbi123VD automatically translated and minimized. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _3orbi123 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (w3a (wb ph ps) (wb ch th) (wb ta et)) (wb (w3o ph ch ta) (w3o ps th et)) $;

--| Closed form of ~ syl5 . Derived automatically from ~ syl5impVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 31-Dec-2011.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem syl5imp (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi th ps) (wi ph (wi th ch))) $;

