--| A set equals the union of its singleton. Theorem 8.2 of [Quine] p. 53. The User manually input on a mmj2 Proof Worksheet, without labels, all steps of ~ unisnALT except 1, 11, 15, 21, and 30. With execution of the mmj2 unification command, mmj2 could find labels for all steps except for 2, 12, 16, 22, and 31 (and the then non-existing steps 1, 11, 15, 21, and 30). mmj2 could not find reference theorems for those five steps because the hypothesis field of each of these steps was empty and none of those steps unifies with a theorem in set.mm. Each of these five steps is a semantic variation of a theorem in set.mm and is 2-step provable. mmj2 does not have the ability to automatically generate the semantic variation in set.mm of a theorem in a mmj2 Proof Worksheet unless the theorem in the Proof Worksheet is labeled with a 1-hypothesis deduction whose hypothesis is a theorem in set.mm which unifies with the theorem in the Proof Worksheet. The stepprover.c program, which invokes mmj2, has this capability. stepprover.c automatically generated steps 1, 11, 15, 21, and 30, labeled all steps, and generated the RPN proof of ~ unisnALT . Roughly speaking, stepprover.c added to the Proof Worksheet a labeled duplicate step of each non-unifying theorem for each label in a text file, labels.txt, containing a list of labels provided by the User. Upon mmj2 unification, stepprover.c identified a label for each of the five theorems which 2-step proves it. For ~ unisnALT , the label list is a list of all 1-hypothesis propositional calculus deductions in set.mm. stepproverp.c is the same as stepprover.c except that it intermittently pauses during execution, allowing the User to observe the changes to a text file caused by the execution of particular statements of the program. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 19-Aug-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem unisnALT (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq (cuni (csn A2)) A2 $;

--| Converse of double negation. Theorem *2.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 102. Proof derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 11-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem notnotrALT2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wn ph)) ph $;

--| If a class is a subclass of another class, then its power class is a subclass of that other class's power class. Left-to-right implication of Exercise 18 of [TakeutiZaring] p. 18. Proof derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. The User's Proof in html format is displayed in ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/sspwimpaltvd.html . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 11-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sspwimpALT2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wss (cpw A2) (cpw B2)) $;

--| Absorption of an existential quantifier of a double existential quantifier of non-distinct variables. The proof is derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. The User's Proof in html format is displayed in ~ e2ebindVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 11-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem e2ebindALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| If at least two sets exist ( ~ dtru ), then the same is true expressed in an alternate form similar to the form of ~ ax6e . The proof is derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. The User's Proof in html format is displayed in ~ ax6e2ndVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 11-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax6e2ndALT {x3 y1 v u: setvar}:
  $ wi (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv u)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv v))))) $;

--| "At least two sets exist" expressed in the form of ~ dtru is logically equivalent to the same expressed in a form similar to ~ ax6e if ~ dtru is false implies ` u = v ` . Proof derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. The User's Proof in html format is displayed in ~ ax6e2ndeqVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 11-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ax6e2ndeqALT {x3 y1 v u: setvar}:
  $ wb (wo (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv u)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv v))))) $;

--| Equivalence for double substitution ~ 2sb5 without distinct ` x ` , ` y ` requirement. ~ 2sb5nd is derived from ~ 2sb5ndVD . The proof is derived by completeusersproof.c from User's Proof in VirtualDeductionProofs.txt. The User's Proof in html format is displayed in ~ 2sb5ndVD . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 19-Sep-2016.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem _2sb5ndALT {x3 y1 v u: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 v u):
  $ wi (wo (wn (wal x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (cv u) (cv v))) (wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph v) u) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv u)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv v))) ph)))) $;

--| The intersecting chords theorem. If points A, B, C, and D lie on a circle (with center Q, say), and the point P is on the interior of the segments AB and CD, then the two products of lengths PA ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD are equal. The Euclidean plane is identified with the complex plane, and the fact that P is on AB and on CD is expressed by the hypothesis that the angles APB and CPD are equal to ` _pi ` . The result is proven by using ~ chordthmlem5 twice to show that PA ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD both equal BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - ` PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>. This is similar to the proof of the theorem given in Euclid's _Elements_, where it is Proposition III.35. Proven by David Moews on 28-Feb-2017 as ~ chordthm . ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/chordthmaltvd.html is a Virtual Deduction User's Proof transcription of ~ chordthm . That VD User's Proof was input into completeusersproof, automatically generating this ~ chordthmALT Metamath proof. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 19-Sep-2017.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem chordthmALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2 C D P: class) (Q F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cdif cc (csn cc0)) (cfv (cfv (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cdiv) clog) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 P cmin) (co B2 P cmin) F) cpi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co C P cmin) (co D P cmin) F) cpi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co C Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 Q cmin) cabs) (cfv (co D Q cmin) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv (co P A2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P B2 cmin) cabs) cmul) (co (cfv (co P C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co P D cmin) cabs) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isosctr . This proof was automatically derived by completeusersproof from its Virtual Deduction proof counterpart ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/isosctrlem1altvd.html . As it is verified by the Metamath program, ~ isosctrlem1ALT verifies ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/isosctrlem1altvd.html . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Apr-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem isosctrlem1ALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) c1) (wn (wceq c1 A2))) (wne (cfv (cfv (co c1 A2 cmin) clog) cim) cpi) $;

--| The indexed union of connected overlapping subspaces sharing a common point is connected. This proof was automatically derived by completeusersproof from its Virtual Deduction proof counterpart ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/iunconlem2vd.html . As it is verified by the Metamath program, ~ iunconnlem2 verifies ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/iunconlem2vd.html . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Apr-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem iunconnlem2 {v u k: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff v u k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (P: class v u) (J X: class):
  $ wb ps (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv u) J)) (wcel (cv v) J)) (wne (cin (cv u) (ciun k A2 B2)) c0)) (wne (cin (cv v) (ciun k A2 B2)) c0)) (wss (cin (cv u) (cv v)) (cdif X (ciun k A2 B2)))) (wss (ciun k A2 B2) (cun (cv u) (cv v)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss B2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J (ciun k A2 B2) crest) cconn) $;

--| The indexed union of connected overlapping subspaces sharing a common point is connected. This proof was automatically derived by completeusersproof from its Virtual Deduction proof counterpart ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/iunconaltvd.html . As it is verified by the Metamath program, ~ iunconnALT verifies ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/iunconaltvd.html . (Contributed by Alan Sare, 22-Apr-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem iunconnALT {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (P J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wss B2 X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel P B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (co J B2 crest) cconn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co J (ciun k A2 B2) crest) cconn) $;

--| A complex number whose sine is zero is an integer multiple of ` _pi ` . The Virtual Deduction form of the proof is ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/sineq0altvd.html . The Metamath form of the proof is ~ sineq0ALT . The Virtual Deduction proof is based on Mario Carneiro's revision of Norm Megill's proof of ~ sineq0 . The Virtual Deduction proof is verified by automatically transforming it into the Metamath form of the proof using completeusersproof, which is verified by the Metamath program. The proof of ~ https://us.metamath.org/other/completeusersproof/sineq0altro.html is a form of the completed proof which preserves the Virtual Deduction proof's step numbers and their ordering. (Contributed by Alan Sare, 13-Jun-2018.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sineq0ALT (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csin) cc0) (wcel (co A2 cpi cdiv) cz)) $;

--| Restricted quantifier version of ~ spesbcd . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem rspesbcd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wsbc x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 ps B2) $;

--| Nonempty existential quantification of a theorem is true. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem rext0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| Cancellation law for composition. Suggested by BJ. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 30-Sep-2026.)
theorem cocanss2 (ph: wff) (F H K: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm H) (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (ccom H F) (ccom K F)) (wss H K)) $;

--| Cancellation law for composition. A version of ~ cocan2 with weaker hypotheses. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem cocan2g (ph: wff) (F H K: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm H) (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm K) (crn F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (ccom H F) (ccom K F)) (wceq H K)) $;

--| Cancellation law for composition. Suggested by BJ. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 30-Sep-2026.)
theorem cocanss1 (ph: wff) (F H K: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun (ccnv F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn H) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (ccom F H) (ccom F K)) (wss H K)) $;

--| Cancellation law for composition. A version of ~ cocan1 with weaker hypotheses. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem cocan1g (ph: wff) (F H K: class):
  $ wi ph (wfun (ccnv F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn H) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrel K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn K) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (ccom F H) (ccom F K)) (wceq H K)) $;

--| The domain of an extensible structure is a subset of ` NN ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem dmstructnn (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wss (cdm F) cn) $;

--| The domain of an extensible structure is finite. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem dmstructfi (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wcel (cdm F) cfn) $;

--| The range of an extensible structure is finite. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem rnstructfi (F X: class):
  $ wi (wbr F X cstr) (wcel (crn F) cfn) $;

--| Version of ~ dfbi1ALT using ` T. ` for step 2 and shortened using ~ a1i , ~ a2i , and ~ con4i . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 22-Oct-2025.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dfbi1ALTa (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ simprim . Proved exactly as step 11 is obtained from step 4 in ~ dfbi1ALTa . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 22-Oct-2025.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem simprimi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wi ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Further shorten ~ dfbi1ALTa using ~ simprimi . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 22-Oct-2025.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem dfbi1ALTb (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wi ps ph)))) $;

--| Extend the definition of a wff to include the relation-preserving property. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
term wrelp (A2 B2 R S H: class): wff;

--| Define the relation-preserving predicate. This is a viable notion of "homomorphism" corresponding to ~ df-isom . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
axiom df_relp {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 R S H: class):
  $ wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wa (wf A2 B2 H) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) H) (cfv (cv y1) H) S)) A2) A2)) $;

--| Equality theorem for relation-preserving functions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpeq1 (A2 B2 R S G H: class):
  $ wi (wceq H G) (wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wrelp A2 B2 R S G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for relation-preserving functions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpeq2 (A2 B2 R S T H: class):
  $ wi (wceq R T) (wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wrelp A2 B2 T S H)) $;

--| Equality theorem for relation-preserving functions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpeq3 (A2 B2 R S T H: class):
  $ wi (wceq S T) (wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wrelp A2 B2 R T H)) $;

--| Equality theorem for relation-preserving functions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpeq4 (A2 B2 C R S H: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 C) (wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wrelp C B2 R S H)) $;

--| Equality theorem for relation-preserving functions. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpeq5 (A2 B2 C R S H: class):
  $ wi (wceq B2 C) (wb (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wrelp A2 C R S H)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for a relation-preserving function. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem nfrelp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 R S H: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 H $ >
  $ wnfc x3 R $ >
  $ wnfc x3 S $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) $;

--| A relation-preserving function is a function. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpf (A2 B2 R S H: class):
  $ wi (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wf A2 B2 H) $;

--| A relation-preserving function preserves the relation. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relprel (A2 B2 C D R S H: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wa (wcel C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wi (wbr C D R) (wbr (cfv C H) (cfv D H) S)) $;

--| A preimage of a minimal element under a relation-preserving function is minimal. Essentially one half of ~ isomin . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpmin (A2 B2 C D R S H: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wa (wss C A2) (wcel D A2))) (wi (wceq (cin (cima H C) (cima (ccnv S) (csn (cfv D H)))) c0) (wceq (cin C (cima (ccnv R) (csn D))) c0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ relpfr . Proved without using the Axiom of Replacement. This is ~ isofrlem with weaker hypotheses. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpfrlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S H: class):
  $ wi ph (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima H (cv x3)) cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wfr B2 S) (wfr A2 R)) $;

--| If the image of a set under a relation-preserving function is well-founded, so is the set. See ~ isofr for a bidirectional statement. A more general version of Lemma I.9.9 of [Kunen2] p. 47. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem relpfr (A2 B2 R S H: class):
  $ wi (wrelp A2 B2 R S H) (wi (wfr B2 S) (wfr A2 R)) $;

--| Orbits exist. Given a set ` A ` and a function ` F ` , the orbit of ` A ` under ` F ` is the smallest set ` Z ` such that ` A e. Z ` and ` Z ` is closed under ` F ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem orbitex (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel (cima (crdg F A2) com) cvv $;

--| A set is contained in its orbit. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem orbitinit (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel A2 (cima (crdg F A2) com)) $;

--| The orbit under a function is closed under the function. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem orbitcl (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cima (crdg F A2) com)) (wcel (cfv B2 F) (cima (crdg F A2) com)) $;

--| Version of ~ orbitcl using maps-to notation. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem orbitclmpt {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D V Z: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wceq Z (cima (crdg (cmpt x3 cvv C) A2) com) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 Z) (wcel D V)) (wcel D Z) $;

--| The class of well-founded sets is transitive. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 9-Sep-2025.)
theorem trwf:
  $ wtr (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $;

--| The rank function preserves ` e. ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem rankrelp:
  $ wrelp (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) con0 cep cep crnk $;

--| The class of well-founded sets is well-founded. Lemma I.9.24(2) of [Kunen2] p. 53. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Oct-2025.)
theorem wffr:
  $ wfr (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) cep $;

--| A transitive class well-founded by ` e. ` is a subclass of the class of well-founded sets. Part of Lemma I.9.21 of [Kunen2] p. 53. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem trfr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr A2) (wfr A2 cep)) (wss A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A set is well-founded if and only if its transitive closure is well-founded by ` e. ` . This characterization of well-founded sets is that in Definition I.9.20 of [Kunen2] p. 53. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Oct-2025.)
theorem tcfr (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wfr (cfv A2 ctc) cep) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two well-founded sets is well-founded. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem xpwf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel B2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0)))) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| The domain of a well-founded set is well-founded. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem dmwf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (cdm A2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| The range of a well-founded set is well-founded. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem rnwf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (crn A2) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) $;

--| A relation is a well-founded set iff its domain and range are. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem relwf (R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wb (wcel R (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wa (wcel (cdm R) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wcel (crn R) (cuni (cima cr1 con0))))) $;

--| Simplification of restricted quantification in a transitive class. When ` ph ` is quantifier-free, this shows that the formula ` A. x e. y ph ` is absolute for transitive models, which is a particular case of Lemma I.16.2 of [Kunen2] p. 95. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem ralabso {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wral x3 ph A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph) M)) $;

--| Simplification of restricted quantification in a transitive class. When ` ph ` is quantifier-free, this shows that the formula ` E. x e. y ph ` is absolute for transitive models, which is a particular case of Lemma I.16.2 of [Kunen2] p. 95. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem rexabso {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph) M)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ ralabso . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem ralabsod {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wtr M) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wral x3 ps A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) M)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ rexabso . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem rexabsod {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wtr M) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wrex x3 ps A2) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) M)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for proving absoluteness results. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem ralabsobidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wtr M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wral x3 ps A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ch) M)) $;

--| Formula-building lemma for proving absoluteness results. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem rexabsobidv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3) (A2 M: class):
  $ wi ph (wtr M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wrex x3 ps A2) (wrex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ch) M)) $;

--| The notion " ` x ` is a subset of ` y ` " is absolute for transitive models. Compare Example I.16.3 of [Kunen2] p. 96 and the following discussion. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem ssabso {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wss A2 B2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) M)) $;

--| Disjointness is absolute for transitive models. Compare Example I.16.3 of [Kunen2] p. 96 and the following discussion. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem disjabso {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wn (wcel (cv x3) B2))) M)) $;

--| Nonemptiness is absolute for transitive models. Compare Example I.16.3 of [Kunen2] p. 96 and the following discussion. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem n0abso {x3: setvar} (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel A2 M)) (wb (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2) M)) $;

--| A transitive class models the Axiom of Extensionality ~ ax-ext . Lemma II.2.4(1) of [Kunen2] p. 111. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem traxext {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wtr M) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) M) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) M) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ modelaxrep . We show that ` M ` is closed under taking subsets. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem modelaxreplem1 {x3 f2: setvar} (ps: wff x3 f2) (A2: class x3 f2) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ps (wss (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal f2 (wi (w3a (wfun (cv f2)) (wcel (cdm (cv f2)) M) (wss (crn (cv f2)) M)) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) M))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel c0 M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wss A2 (cv x3) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel A2 M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ modelaxrep . We define a class ` F ` and show that the antecedent of Replacement implies that ` F ` is a function. We use Replacement (in the form of ~ funex ) to show that ` F ` exists. Then we show that, under our hypotheses, the range of ` F ` is a member of ` M ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem modelaxreplem2 {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w f2) (F: class x3 y1 z w) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ps (wss (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal f2 (wi (w3a (wfun (cv f2)) (wcel (cdm (cv f2)) M) (wss (crn (cv f2)) M)) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) M))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel c0 M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wnf w ps $ >
  $ wnf z ps $ >
  $ wnfc z F $ >
  $ wceq F (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wa (wcel (cv z) M) (wal y1 ph)))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wcel (cv w) M) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) M) M)) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel (crn F) M) $;

--| Lemma for ~ modelaxrep . We show that the consequent of Replacement is satisfied with ` ran F ` as the value of ` y ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem modelaxreplem3 {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w f2) (F: class x3 y1 z w) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ps (wss (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal f2 (wi (w3a (wfun (cv f2)) (wcel (cdm (cv f2)) M) (wss (crn (cv f2)) M)) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) M))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel c0 M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel (cv x3) M) $ >
  $ wnf w ps $ >
  $ wnf z ps $ >
  $ wnfc z F $ >
  $ wceq F (copab w z (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wa (wcel (cv z) M) (wal y1 ph)))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wi (wcel (cv w) M) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) M) M)) $ >
  $ wi ps (wrex y1 (wral z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wal y1 ph)) M)) M) M) $;

--| Conditions which guarantee that a class models the Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep . Similar to Lemma II.2.4(6) of [Kunen2] p. 111. The first two hypotheses are those in Kunen. The reason for the third hypothesis that our version of Replacement is different from Kunen's (which is ~ zfrep6 ). If we assumed Regularity, we could eliminate this extra hypothesis, since under Regularity, the empty set is a member of every non-empty transitive class. Note that, to obtain the relativization of an instance of Replacement to ` M ` , the formula ` A. y ph ` would need to be replaced with ` A. y e. M ch ` , where ` ch ` is ` ph ` with all quantifiers relativized to ` M ` . However, we can obtain this by using ` y e. M /\ ch ` for ` ph ` in this theorem, so it does establish that all instances of Replacement hold in ` M ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem modelaxrep {x3 y1 z w f2: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z w f2) (M: class):
  $ wi ps (wtr M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wal f2 (wi (w3a (wfun (cv f2)) (wcel (cdm (cv f2)) M) (wss (crn (cv f2)) M)) (wcel (crn (cv f2)) M))) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel c0 M) $ >
  $ wi ps (wral x3 (wi (wral w (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) M) M) M) (wrex y1 (wral z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wal y1 ph)) M)) M) M)) M) $;

--| A class that is closed under subsets models the Axiom of Separation ~ ax-sep . Lemma II.2.4(3) of [Kunen2] p. 111. Note that, to obtain the relativization of an instance of Separation to ` M ` , the formula ` ph ` would need to be replaced with its relativization to ` M ` . However, this new formula is a valid substitution for ` ph ` , so this theorem does establish that all instances of Separation hold in ` M ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem ssclaxsep {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wi (wral z (wss (cpw (cv z)) M) M) (wral z (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)) M) M) M) $;

--| A class that contains the empty set models the Null Set Axiom ~ ax-nul . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem _0elaxnul {x3 y1: setvar} (M: class y1):
  $ wi (wcel c0 M) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) M) M) $;

--| Suppose ` M ` is a transitive class that is closed under power sets intersected with ` M ` . Then, ` M ` models the Axiom of Power Sets ~ ax-pow . One direction of Lemma II.2.8 of [Kunen2] p. 113. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem pwclaxpow {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wral x3 (wcel (cin (cpw (cv x3)) M) M) M)) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wral w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) M) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) M) M) M) $;

--| A class that is closed under the pairing operation models the Axiom of Pairing ~ ax-pr . Lemma II.2.4(4) of [Kunen2] p. 111. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem prclaxpr {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (M: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) M) M) M) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wo (wceq (cv w) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))) (wcel (cv w) (cv z))) M) M) M) M) $;

--| A class that is closed under the union operation models the Axiom of Union ~ ax-un . Lemma II.2.4(5) of [Kunen2] p. 111. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem uniclaxun {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (M: class x3 z w):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel (cuni (cv x3)) M) M) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) M) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) M) M) M) $;

--| A subclass of the class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg . Lemma II.2.4(2) of [Kunen2] p. 111. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem sswfaxreg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wss M (cuni (cima cr1 con0))) (wral x3 (wi (wrex y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) M) (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) M)) M)) M) $;

--| A class that contains all ordinals up to and including ` _om ` models the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 . The antecedent of this theorem is not enough to guarantee that the class models the alternate axiom ~ ax-inf . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem omssaxinf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (M: class w):
  $ wi (wa (wss com M) (wcel com M)) (wrex x3 (wa (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wral z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) M)) M) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wral w (wb (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wo (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))) M)) M)) M)) M) $;

--| A transitive class that contains ` _om ` models the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 . Lemma II.2.11(7) of [Kunen2] p. 114. Kunen has the additional hypotheses that the Extensionality, Separation, Pairing, and Union axioms are true in ` M ` . This, apparently, is because Kunen's statement of the Axiom of Infinity uses the defined notions ` (/) ` and ` suc ` , and these axioms guarantee that these notions are well-defined. When we state the axiom using primitives only, the need for these hypotheses disappears. The antecedent of this theorem is not enough to guarantee that the class models the alternate axiom ~ ax-inf . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem omelaxinf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (M: class w):
  $ wi (wa (wtr M) (wcel com M)) (wrex x3 (wa (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wral z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) M)) M) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wral w (wb (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wo (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))) M)) M)) M)) M) $;

--| ~ dfac5 expanded into primitives. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem dfac5prim {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wb wac (wal x3 (wi (wa (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex w (wcel (cv w) (cv z))))) (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)))))))))) (wex y1 (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex w (wal v (wb (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv v) (cv w)))))))))) $;

--| ~ ac8 expanded into primitives. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem ac8prim {x3 y1 z w v: setvar}:
  $ wi (wa (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex w (wcel (cv w) (cv z))))) (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)))))))))) (wex y1 (wal z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wex w (wal v (wb (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv v) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| If ` M ` is a transitive class, then the following are equivalent. (1) Every nonempty set ` x e. M ` of pairwise disjoint nonempty sets has a choice set in ` M ` . (2) The class ` M ` models the Axiom of Choice, in the form ~ ac8prim . Lemma II.2.11(7) of [Kunen2] p. 114. Kunen has the additional hypotheses that the Extensionality, Separation, Pairing, and Union axioms are true in ` M ` . This, apparently, is because Kunen's statement of the Axiom of Choice uses defined notions, including ` (/) ` and ` i^i ` , and these axioms guarantee that these notions are well-defined. When we state the axiom using primitives only, the need for these hypotheses disappears. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem modelac8prim {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wtr M) (wb (wral x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wne (cv z) c0) (cv x3)) (wral z (wral w (wi (wne (cv z) (cv w)) (wceq (cin (cv z) (cv w)) c0)) (cv x3)) (cv x3))) (wrex y1 (wral z (weu v (wcel (cv v) (cin (cv z) (cv y1)))) (cv x3)) M)) M) (wral x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wrex w (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) M)) M) (wral z (wral w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)))) M))) M) M)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wrex w (wral v (wb (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv v) (cv w))) M) M)) M) M)) M)) $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Extensionality ~ ax-ext . Part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112. This is the first of a series of theorems showing that all the axioms of ZFC hold in the class of well-founded sets, which we here denote by ` W ` . More precisely, for each axiom of ZFC, we obtain a provable statement if we restrict all quantifiers to ` W ` (including implicit universal quantifiers on free variables). None of these proofs use the Axiom of Regularity. In particular, the Axiom of Regularity itself is proved to hold in ` W ` without using Regularity. Further, the Axiom of Choice is used only in the proof that Choice holds in ` W ` . This has the consequence that any theorem of ZF (possibly proved using Regularity) can be proved, without using Regularity, to hold in ` W ` . This gives us a relative consistency result: If ZF without Regularity is consistent, so is ZF itself. Similarly, if ZFC without Regularity is consistent, so is ZFC itself. These consistency results are metatheorems and are part of Theorem II.2.13 of [Kunen2] p. 114. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 11-Sep-2025.) (Revised by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem wfaxext {x3 y1 z: setvar} (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wral z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) W) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep . Actually, our statement is stronger, since it is an instance of Replacement only when all quantifiers in ` A. y ph ` are relativized to ` W ` . Essentially part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112, but note that our Replacement is different from Kunen's. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem wfaxrep {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wi (wral w (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1))) W) W) W) (wrex y1 (wral z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)) (wal y1 ph)) W)) W) W)) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Separation ~ ax-sep . Actually, our statement is stronger, since it is an instance of Separation only when all quantifiers in ` ph ` are relativized to ` W ` . Part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem wfaxsep {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (W: class x3 z):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral z (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv z)) ph)) W) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Null Set Axiom ~ ax-nul . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfaxnul {x3 y1: setvar} (W: class y1):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3))) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axioms of Power Sets. Part of Corollary II.2.9 of [Kunen2] p. 113. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfaxpow {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wral w (wi (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) W) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) W) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Pairing ~ ax-pr . Part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem wfaxpr {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (W: class x3 w):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wral w (wi (wo (wceq (cv w) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))) (wcel (cv w) (cv z))) W) W) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Union ~ ax-un . Part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfaxun {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (W: class x3 z w):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wrex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) W) (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) W) W) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg . Part of Corollary II.2.5 of [Kunen2] p. 112. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfaxreg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wi (wrex y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) W) (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)))) W)) W)) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets models the Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 . Part of Corollary II.2.12 of [Kunen2] p. 114. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfaxinf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (W: class w):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wrex x3 (wa (wrex y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wral z (wn (wcel (cv z) (cv y1))) W)) W) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wrex z (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wral w (wb (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) (wo (wcel (cv w) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))) W)) W)) W)) W $;

--| The class of well-founded sets ` W ` models the Axiom of Choice. Since the previous theorems show that all the ZF axioms hold in ` W ` , we may use any statement that ZF proves is equivalent to Choice to prove this. We use ~ ac8prim . Part of Corollary II.2.12 of [Kunen2] p. 114. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 19-Oct-2025.)
theorem wfac8prim {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (W: class):
  $ wceq W (cuni (cima cr1 con0)) $ >
  $ wral x3 (wi (wa (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wrex w (wcel (cv w) (cv z)) W)) W) (wral z (wral w (wi (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3))) (wi (wn (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) (wral y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv z)) (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv w)))) W))) W) W)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wcel (cv z) (cv x3)) (wrex w (wral v (wb (wa (wcel (cv v) (cv z)) (wcel (cv v) (cv y1))) (wceq (cv v) (cv w))) W) W)) W) W)) W $;

--| The membership relation in a permutation model. We use a permutation ` F ` of the universe to define a relation ` R ` that serves as the membership relation in our model. The conclusion of this theorem is Definition II.9.1 of [Kunen2] p. 148. All the axioms of ZFC except for Regularity hold in permutation models, and Regularity will be false if ` F ` is chosen appropriately. Thus, permutation models can be used to show that Regularity does not follow from the other axioms (with the usual proviso that the axioms are consistent). (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem brpermmodel (A2 B2 R F: class):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 B2 R) (wcel A2 (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Ordinary membership expressed in terms of the permutation model's membership relation. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem brpermmodelcnv (A2 B2 R F: class):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 (cfv B2 (ccnv F)) R) (wcel A2 B2) $;

--| The Axiom of Extensionality ~ ax-ext holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxext {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 z) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| The Axiom of Replacement ~ ax-rep holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. Note that, to prove that an instance of Replacement holds in the model, ` ph ` would need have all instances of ` e. ` replaced with ` R ` . But this still results in an instance of this theorem, so we do establish that Replacement holds. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxrep {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (R: class x3 z w) (F: class x3):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wal w (wex y1 (wal z (wi (wal y1 ph) (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)))))) (wex y1 (wal z (wb (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) (wex w (wa (wbr (cv w) (cv x3) R) (wal y1 ph)))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Separation ~ ax-sep holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. Note that, to prove that an instance of Separation holds in the model, ` ph ` would need have all instances of ` e. ` replaced with ` R ` . But this still results in an instance of this theorem, so we do establish that Separation holds. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxsep {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (R: class x3 z) (F: class z):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex y1 (wal x3 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) ph))) $;

--| The Null Set Axiom ~ ax-nul holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxnul {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R))) $;

--| The Axiom of Power Sets ~ ax-pow holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxpow {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (R: class x3 z w) (F: class x3):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wal w (wi (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv w) (cv x3) R))) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))) $;

--| The Axiom of Pairing ~ ax-pr holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxpr {z w: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar) (R: class z w) (F: class):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex z (wal w (wi (wo (wceq (cv w) (cv x3)) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1))) (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R))) $;

--| The Axiom of Union ~ ax-un holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxun {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 z) (F: class x3):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wi (wex w (wa (wbr (cv z) (cv w) R) (wbr (cv w) (cv x3) R))) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ permaxinf2 . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxinf2lem {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 w v) (F: class) (Z: class w v):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cima (crdg (cmpt v cvv (cfv (cun (cfv (cv v) F) (csn (cv v))) (ccnv F))) (cfv c0 (ccnv F))) com) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wal z (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))))) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wex z (wa (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wal w (wb (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wo (wbr (cv w) (cv y1) R) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))))))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Infinity ~ ax-inf2 holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem permaxinf2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 w) (F: class):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wa (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wal z (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))))) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wex z (wa (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wal w (wb (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R) (wo (wbr (cv w) (cv y1) R) (wceq (cv w) (cv y1)))))))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Choice ~ ac8prim holds in permutation models. Part of Exercise II.9.3 of [Kunen2] p. 149. Note that ~ ax-ac requires Regularity for its derivation from the usual Axiom of Choice and does not necessarily hold in permutation models. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 16-Nov-2025.)
theorem permac8prim {x3 y1 z w v: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 z w v) (F: class x3):
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wal z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wex w (wbr (cv w) (cv z) R)))) (wal z (wal w (wi (wa (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wbr (cv w) (cv x3) R)) (wi (wn (wceq (cv z) (cv w))) (wal y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) R) (wn (wbr (cv y1) (cv w) R))))))))) (wex y1 (wal z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wex w (wal v (wb (wa (wbr (cv v) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv v) (cv y1) R)) (wceq (cv v) (cv w)))))))) $;

--| Define a permutation ` F ` used to produce a model in which ~ ax-reg is false. The permutation swaps ` (/) ` and ` { (/) } ` and leaves the rest of ` V ` fixed. This is an example given after Exercise II.9.2 of [Kunen2] p. 148. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 16-Nov-2025.)
theorem nregmodelf1o (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid (cdif cvv (cpr c0 (csn c0)))) (cpr (cop c0 (csn c0)) (cop (csn c0) c0))) $ >
  $ wf1o cvv cvv F $;

--| Lemma for ~ nregmodel . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 16-Nov-2025.)
theorem nregmodellem {x3: setvar} (R F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid (cdif cvv (cpr c0 (csn c0)))) (cpr (cop c0 (csn c0)) (cop (csn c0) c0))) $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cv x3) c0 R) (wcel (cv x3) (csn c0)) $;

--| The Axiom of Regularity ~ ax-reg is false in the permutation model defined from ` F ` . Since the other axioms of ZFC hold in all permutation models ( ~ permaxext through ~ permac8prim ), we can conclude that Regularity does not follow from those axioms, assuming ZFC is consistent. (If we could prove Regularity from the other axioms, we could prove it in the permutation model and thus obtain a contradiction with this theorem.) Since we also know that Regularity is consistent with the other axioms ( ~ wfaxext through ~ wfac8prim ), Regularity is neither provable nor disprovable from the other axioms; i.e., it is independent of them. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 16-Nov-2025.)
theorem nregmodel {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R: class y1 z) (F: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid (cdif cvv (cpr c0 (csn c0)))) (cpr (cop c0 (csn c0)) (cop (csn c0) c0))) $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wn (wal x3 (wi (wex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) (wex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R) (wal z (wi (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R) (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R)))))))) $;

--| The Axiom of Extensionality ~ ax-ext is true in the permutation model defined from ` F ` . This theorem is an immediate consequence of the fact that ~ ax-ext holds in all permutation models and is provided as an illustration. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 16-Nov-2025.)
theorem nregmodelaxext {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R: class x3 y1 z) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cun (cres cid (cdif cvv (cpr c0 (csn c0)))) (cpr (cop c0 (csn c0)) (cop (csn c0) c0))) $ >
  $ wceq R (ccom (ccnv F) cep) $ >
  $ wi (wal z (wb (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) R))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| The ` # ` function on ` _om ` preserves addition. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashnna (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 coa) chash) (co (cfv A2 chash) (cfv B2 chash) caddc)) $;

--| The ` # ` function on ` _om ` turns successor into adding 1. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashnnsuc (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) chash) (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The ` # ` function on ` _om ` preserves multiplication. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashnnm (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 comu) chash) (co (cfv A2 chash) (cfv B2 chash) cmul)) $;

--| The ` # ` function on ` _om ` preserves the ordering. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashnnlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 A2)) (wbr (cfv B2 chash) (cfv A2 chash) clt) $;

--| The ` # ` function on ` _om ` preserves the ordering. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashnnltb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 chash) (cfv B2 chash) clt)) $;

--| The ` # ` function yields a bijection from ` _om ` to ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashomf1o:
  $ wf1o com cn0 (cres chash com) $;

--| The ` # ` function yields an order isomorphism between ` _om ` and ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 7-Jul-2026.)
theorem hashomiso:
  $ wiso com cn0 cep clt (cres chash com) $;

--| A pair whose members are hereditarily finite sets is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfpr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) chf) $;

--| The Cartesian product of two hereditarily finite sets is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfxp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 chf) (wcel B2 chf)) (wcel (cxp A2 B2) chf) $;

--| The domain of a hereditarily finite set is hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfdm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (cdm A2) chf) $;

--| The range of a hereditarily finite set is hereditarily finite. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfrn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 chf) (wcel (crn A2) chf) $;

--| A relation is a hereditarily finite set iff its domain and range are. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfrel (R: class):
  $ wi (wrel R) (wb (wcel R chf) (wa (wcel (cdm R) chf) (wcel (crn R) chf))) $;

--| Finite ordinals are hereditarily finite sets. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem omhf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel A2 chf) $;

--| The class of finite ordinals is included in the class of hereditarily finite sets. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 26-Sep-2026.)
theorem omsshf:
  $ wss com chf $;

--| Extend class notation with the set of structures whose components are hereditarily finite.
term chfstruct: class;

--| Define the class of extensible structures whose components are all hereditarily finite. We will call these "HFStructs". Ideally, an HFStruct would itself be a hereditarily finite set, but this is not possible because the domain of a structure is, in the current formulation, a subset of ` NN ` . To get around this, we adjust the domain to be a subset of ` _om ` , obtaining the "converted form" ` ( F o. ( # |`` _om ) ) ` of an HFStruct ` F ` . This is indeed hereditarily finite by ~ hfstructhf . Also, unlike general extensible structures, we do not allow ` (/) ` to be a member of an HFStruct. This restriction gives us the property that two HFStructs are equal if and only if their converted forms are equal ( ~ hfstructcan ). (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
axiom df_hfstruct:
  $ wceq chfstruct (cin (cdm cstr) (cpw (cxp cvv chf))) $;

--| An HFStruct is an extensible structure. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfstructstruct {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F chfstruct) (wex x3 (wbr F (cv x3) cstr)) $;

--| An HFStruct is a function. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfstructfun (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F chfstruct) (wfun F) $;

--| The range of an HFStruct consists of hereditarily finite sets. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem rnhfstructsshf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F chfstruct) (wss (crn F) chf) $;

--| Conditions that imply that ` F ` is an HFStruct. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem ishfstruct (F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr F X cstr) (wrel F) (wss (crn F) chf)) (wcel F chfstruct) $;

--| The range of an HFStruct is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem rnhfstructhf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F chfstruct) (wcel (crn F) chf) $;

--| A converted HFStruct is a hereditarily finite set. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfstructhf (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F chfstruct) (wcel (ccom F (cres chash com)) chf) $;

--| Two HFStructs are equal if and only if their converted forms are equal. (Contributed by Eric Schmidt, 29-Sep-2026.)
theorem hfstructcan (F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F chfstruct) (wcel G chfstruct)) (wb (wceq (ccom F (cres chash com)) (ccom G (cres chash com))) (wceq F G)) $;

--| A version of ~ evth2 using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem evth2f {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F J K X: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc y1 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 X $ >
  $ wnfc y1 X $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) cle) X) X) $;

--| A version of ~ eluni using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem elunif {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni B2)) (wex x3 (wa (wcel A2 (cv x3)) (wcel (cv x3) B2))) $;

--| A version of ~ rzal using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rzalf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 (wceq A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wceq A2 c0) (wral x3 ph A2) $;

--| A version of ~ fvelrnb using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fvelrnbf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wb (wcel B2 (crn F)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| If F is a continuous function with respect to the standard topology, then the preimage A of the values greater than a given extended real B is an open set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rfcnpre1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F J K X: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (wbr B2 (cfv (cv x3) F) clt) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $;

--| If U belongs to A and U is an upper bound, then U is the sup of A. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem ubelsupr {x3: setvar} (A2 U: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel U A2) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) U cle) A2)) (wceq U (csup A2 cr clt)) $;

--| A finite sum of functions to complex numbers from a common topological space is continuous, without disjoint var constraint x ph. The class expression for B normally contains free variables k and x to index it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fsumcnf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 k) (J K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (csu k A2 B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The product of a negative and a positive number is negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem mulltgt0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) cc0 clt) $;

--| A version of ~ rspcev using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rspcegf {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) ps) (wrex x3 ph B2) $;

--| A version of ~ rabexg using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rabexgf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 ph A2) cvv) $;

--| A function continuous with respect to the standard topology, is a real mapping. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fcnre (ph: wff) (C T F J K: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr F) $;

--| A sum of a singleton is the term. The deduction version of ~ sumsn . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem sumsnd {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k) (M: class) (V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wnfc k B2) $ >
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) M)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k (csn M) A2) B2) $;

--| A version of ~ evth using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem evthf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (F J K X: class x3 y1):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc y1 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 X $ >
  $ wnfc y1 X $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne X c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) X) X) $;

--| The class of continuous functions between two topologies is a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem cnfex (J K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel K ctop)) (wcel (co J K ccn) cvv) $;

--| For a finite set, a choice function exists, without using the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fnchoice {x3 f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cfn) (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wi (wne (cv x3) c0) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3))) A2))) $;

--| A finite sum of continuous real functions, from a common topological space, is continuous. The class expression for B normally contains free variables k and x to index it. See ~ fsumcn for the analogous theorem on continuous complex functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem refsumcn {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 k) (J: class) (K: class x3 k) (X: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (csu k A2 B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a continuous function with respect to the standard topology, then the preimage A of the values smaller than a given extended real ` B ` , is an open set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rfcnpre2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F J K X: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) B2 clt) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $;

--| When the hypothesis for the extreme value theorem hold, then the sup of the range of the function belongs to the range, it is real and it an upper bound of the range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem cncmpmax {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (T F J K: class):
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel (csup (crn F) cr clt) (crn F)) (wcel (csup (crn F) cr clt) cr) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (csup (crn F) cr clt) cle) T)) $;

--| If F is a continuous function with respect to the standard topology, then the preimage A of the values greater than or equal to a given real B is a closed set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rfcnpre3 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (B2 T: class) (F J K: class t):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab t (wbr B2 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| If F is a continuous function with respect to the standard topology, then the preimage A of the values less than or equal to a given real B is a closed set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rfcnpre4 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (B2 T: class) (F J K: class t):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) B2 cle) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv J ccld)) $;

--| Sum of two distinct complex values. The class expression for ` A ` and ` B ` normally contain free variable ` k ` to index it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem sumpair {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D E V W: class k):
  $ wi ph (wnfc k D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc k E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) A2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) B2)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k (cpr A2 B2) C) (co D E caddc)) $;

--| Given a real continuous function ` F ` defined on a compact topological space, there is always a positive integer that is a strict upper bound of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem rfcnnnub {t n: setvar} (ph: wff t n) (C: class t n) (T: class) (F: class t) (J K: class n):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (cv n) clt) T) cn) $;

--| This is the core Lemma for ~ refsum2cn : the sum of two continuous real functions (from a common topological space) is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem refsum2cnlem1 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 k) (F G: class x3) (J K X: class):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 G $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt k (cpr c1 c2) (cif (wceq (cv k) c1) F G)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) caddc)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| The sum of two continuus real functions (from a common topological space) is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem refsum2cn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (F G: class x3) (J K X: class):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 G $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) caddc)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem adantlllr (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa ph et) ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _3adantlr3 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (w3a ps ch et)) th) ta $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _3adantll2 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a ph et ps) ch) th) ta $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _3adantll3 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a ph ps et) ch) th) ta $;

--| If not element of a set, then not element of a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ssnel (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wn (wcel C B2))) (wn (wcel C A2)) $;

--| A singleton is closed w.r.t. the standard topology on the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sncldre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (csn A2) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccld)) $;

--| A polynomial with a nonzero coefficient is not the zero polynomial. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem n0p (P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P (cfv cz cply)) (wcel N cn0) (wne (cfv N (cfv P ccoe)) cc0)) (wne P c0p) $;

--| Inference rule for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem pm2_65ni (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wn ps) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Equality deduction for indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem iuneq2df {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciun x3 A2 B2) (ciun x3 A2 C)) $;

--| There exists a mapping from ` NN ` onto any (nonempty) countable set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnfoctb {f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wne A2 c0)) (wex f2 (wfo cn A2 (cv f2))) $;

--| An element of the powerset of ` B ` intersected with anything, is a subset of ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem elpwinss (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (cpw B2) C)) (wss A2 B2) $;

--| If ` F ` is a set, then ` U. dom F ` is a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem unidmex (ph: wff) (F V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq X (cuni (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cvv) $;

--| A non-disjointness condition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem ndisj2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wb (wn (wdisj x3 A2 B2)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wne (cin B2 C) c0)) A2) A2) $;

--| The set of integer numbers is equinumerous to omega (the set of finite ordinal numbers). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem zenom:
  $ wbr cz com cen $;

--| Well-ordering principle: any nonempty subset of an upper set of integers has the least element. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem uzwo4 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (ps: wff j k) (S: class) (M: class j):
  $ wnf j ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss S (cfv M cuz)) (wrex j ph S)) (wrex j (wa ph (wral k (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) clt) (wn ps)) S)) S) $;

--| The union of the singleton of the empty set is the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem unisn0:
  $ wceq (cuni (csn c0)) c0 $;

--| If two classes are disjoint, two respective subclasses are disjoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem ssin0 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq (cin A2 B2) c0) (wss C A2) (wss D B2)) (wceq (cin C D) c0) $;

--| Absorption law for intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem inabs3 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wss C B2) (wceq (cin (cin A2 B2) C) (cin A2 C)) $;

--| Relationship between power class and union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem pwpwuni (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wcel A2 (cpw (cpw B2))) (wcel (cuni A2) (cpw B2))) $;

--| In a disjoint collection, an indexed union is disjoint from an additional term. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem disjiun2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D E: class):
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cdif A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq B2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (ciun x3 C B2) E) c0) $;

--| The empty set is in any power set, and it's finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem _0pwfi (A2: class):
  $ wcel c0 (cin (cpw A2) cfn) $;

--| Intersection preserves subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem ssinss2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cin A2 B2) C) $;

--| The set of integer numbers is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem zct:
  $ wbr cz com cdom $;

--| A finite set always belongs to a power class. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem pwfin0 (A2: class):
  $ wne (cin (cpw A2) cfn) c0 $;

--| An upper integer set is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem uzct (N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wbr Z com cdom $;

--| A singleton index picks out an instance of an indexed union's argument. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem iunxsnf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq (ciun x3 (csn A2) B2) C $;

--| If a set is closed under the union of two sets, then it is closed under finite indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem fiiuncl {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class y1 z) (B2: class x3) (D: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv y1) D) (wcel (cv z) D)) (wcel (cun (cv y1) (cv z)) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciun x3 A2 B2) D) $;

--| The addition of the next set to a union indexed by a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem iunp1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class k) (M N: class):
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (co N c1 caddc)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciun k (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) A2) (cun (ciun k (co M N cfz) A2) B2)) $;

--| If a set is closed under the union of two sets, then it is closed under finite union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem fiunicl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wcel (cun (cv x3) (cv y1)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni A2) A2) $;

--| Equality theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem ixpeq2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| The sets of a cartesian product are disjoint if the sets in the first argument are disjoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem disjxp1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 (cxp B2 C)) $;

--| The sets in the cartesian product of singletons with other sets, are disjoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem disjsnxp {j: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class j):
  $ wdisj j A2 (cxp (csn (cv j)) B2) $;

--| Membership in indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem eliind {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) K) (wb (wcel A2 C) (wcel A2 D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 D) $;

--| Restricted existential specialization, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem rspcef {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) ps) (wrex x3 ph B2) $;

--| An infinite Cartesian product is a subset of set exponentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem ixpssmapc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (co C A2 cmap)) $;

--| Membership in class intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem elintd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel A2 (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cint B2)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem ssdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $;

--| Substitution of equal classes into the negation of a binary relation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem brneqtrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C R: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 B2 R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 C R)) $;

--| A set containing an uncountable set is itself uncountable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem ssnct (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 com cdom)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 com cdom)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for being a subclass of the union of an intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem ssuniint {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel A2 (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni (cint B2))) $;

--| Membership in class intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem elintdv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel A2 (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cint B2)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem ssd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $;

--| Introducing any antecedent in a restricted universal quantification. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem ralimralim {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 ph A2) (wral x3 (wi ps ph) A2) $;

--| Membership of the element in the range of a constant map. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem snelmap {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxp A2 (csn (cv x3))) (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) $;

--| An extended real that is neither real nor minus infinity, is plus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem xrnmnfpnf (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cmnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cpnf) $;

--| Equality theorem for indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.) Remove DV conditions. (Revised by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem iuneq1i {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (ciun x3 A2 C) (ciun x3 B2 C) $;

--| Inclusion relation for a monotonic sequence of sets. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem ssinc {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) (co M N cfzo))) (wss (cfv (cv m) F) (cfv (co (cv m) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv M F) (cfv N F)) $;

--| Inclusion relation for a monotonic sequence of sets. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem ssdec {m: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) (co M N cfzo))) (wss (cfv (co (cv m) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv N F) (cfv M F)) $;

--| Membership in an infinite Cartesian product of a constant ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem elixpconstg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wb (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wf A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Equality theorem for indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem iineq1d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciin x3 A2 C) (ciin x3 B2 C)) $;

--| A metric is a pseudometric. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem metpsmet (D X: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (cfv X cmet)) (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) $;

--| Subclass theorem for infinite Cartesian product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem ixpssixp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cixp x3 A2 B2) (cixp x3 A2 C)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a ` ball ` being a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem ballss3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 D P R: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv X cpsmet)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cxr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wbr (co P (cv x3) D) R clt)) (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co P R (cfv D cbl)) A2) $;

--| Given a sequence of increasing sets, the union of a finite subsequence, is its last element. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem iunincfi {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co M N cfzo))) (wss (cfv (cv n) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciun n (co M N cfz) (cfv (cv n) F)) (cfv N F)) $;

--| If it's not a subclass, it's not a subclass of a smaller one. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem nsstr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wn (wss A2 B2)) (wss C B2)) (wn (wss A2 C)) $;

--| Combine two different upper integer properties into one, for a single integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem rexanuz3 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (ps ch: wff k) (th ta: wff j) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k ch (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k ps (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv j)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv j)) (wb ps ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wa th ta) Z) $;

--| Rule to change the second bound variable in a maps-to function, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem cbvmpo2 {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 w) (B2: class x3) (C E: class x3 y1 w):
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc w A2 $ >
  $ wnfc w C $ >
  $ wnfc y1 E $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (cmpo x3 w A2 B2 E) $;

--| Rule to change the first bound variable in a maps-to function, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem cbvmpo1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 C E: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc z B2 $ >
  $ wnfc z C $ >
  $ wnfc x3 E $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (cmpo z y1 A2 B2 E) $;

--| Indexed union of indexed intersections. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliuniin {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 C D: class x3 y1) (V: class y1) (Z: class):
  $ wceq A2 (ciun x3 B2 (ciin y1 C D)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Z V) (wb (wcel Z A2) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wcel Z D) C) B2)) $;

--| Subclass of a class abstraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ssabf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wss A2 (cab x3 ph)) (wal x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)) $;

--| A proper subclass does not include the other class. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem pssnssi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wpss A2 B2 $ >
  $ wn (wss B2 A2) $;

--| Membership in a restricted abstraction, implication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem rabidim2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (crab x3 ph A2)) ph $;

--| Membership in class union. Restricted quantifier version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eluni2f {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cuni B2)) (wrex x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3)) B2) $;

--| Membership in indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliin2f {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wne B2 c0) (wb (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) (wral x3 (wcel A2 C) B2)) $;

--| Negation of subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem nssd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wss A2 B2)) $;

--| Equality deduction for indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.) Remove DV conditions. (Revised by GG, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem iineq12dv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciin x3 A2 C) (ciin x3 B2 D)) $;

--| The supremum of an arbitrary set of extended reals is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem supxrcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup A2 cxr clt) cxr) $;

--| A sufficient condition for being an open set of a subspace topology. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem elrestd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X J) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cin X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co J B2 crest)) $;

--| Counterexample to show that the additional conditions in ~ eliuniin and ~ eliuniin2 are actually needed. Notice that the definition of ` A ` is not even needed (it can be any class). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliuniincex {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1) (B2: class y1) (C: class x3) (D: class x3 y1) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (csn c0) $ >
  $ wceq C c0 $ >
  $ wceq D c0 $ >
  $ wceq Z cvv $ >
  $ wn (wb (wcel Z A2) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wcel Z D) C) B2)) $;

--| Counterexample to show that the additional conditions in ~ eliin and ~ eliin2 are actually needed. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliincex {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq B2 c0 $ >
  $ wn (wb (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) (wral x3 (wcel A2 C) B2)) $;

--| Membership in an indexed intersection implies membership in any intersected set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliinid {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel A2 C) $;

--| Class abstraction in a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem abssf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wss (cab x3 ph) A2) (wal x3 (wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2))) $;

--| A member of a set of extended reals is less than or equal to the set's supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem supxrubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup A2 cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 S cle) $;

--| Subclass of a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ssrabf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wss B2 (crab x3 ph A2)) (wa (wss B2 A2) (wral x3 ph B2)) $;

--| Subclass of a restricted class abstraction (deduction form). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem ssrabdf {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (crab x3 ps A2)) $;

--| Membership in indexed intersection. See ~ eliincex for a counterexample showing that the precondition ` B =/= (/) ` cannot be simply dropped. ~ eliin uses an alternative precondition (and it doesn't have a disjoint var constraint between ` B ` and ` x ` ; see ~ eliin2f ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliin2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wne B2 c0) (wb (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) (wral x3 (wcel A2 C) B2)) $;

--| Subclass relation for a restricted class. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ssrab2f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wss (crab x3 ph A2) A2 $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace induced by the subspace operator ` |``t ` . The result can be applied, for instance, to topologies and sigma-algebras. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem restuni3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (co A2 B2 crest)) (cin (cuni A2) B2)) $;

--| Restricted class abstraction in a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem rabssf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wss (crab x3 ph A2) B2) (wral x3 (wi ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) A2) $;

--| Indexed union of indexed intersections. See ~ eliincex for a counterexample showing that the precondition ` C =/= (/) ` cannot be simply dropped. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem eliuniin2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class x3) (D: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq A2 (ciun x3 B2 (ciin y1 C D)) $ >
  $ wi (wne C c0) (wb (wcel Z A2) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wcel Z D) C) B2)) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace induced by the ` |``t ` operator. The result can be applied, for instance, to topologies and sigma-algebras. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem restuni4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cuni A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (co A2 B2 crest)) B2) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace topology. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem restuni6 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cuni (co A2 B2 crest)) (cin (cuni A2) B2)) $;

--| The underlying set of a subspace induced by the ` |``t ` operator. The result can be applied, for instance, to topologies and sigma-algebras. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem restuni5 (A2 J V X: class):
  $ wceq X (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel J V) (wss A2 X)) (wceq A2 (cuni (co J A2 crest))) $;

--| The union of an elementwise intersection is a subset of the underlying set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem unirestss (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cuni (co A2 B2 crest)) (cuni A2)) $;

--| Indexed intersection of intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iniin1 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wceq (cin (ciin x3 A2 C) B2) (ciin x3 A2 (cin C B2))) $;

--| Indexed intersection of intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iniin2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wceq (cin B2 (ciin x3 A2 C)) (ciin x3 A2 (cin B2 C))) $;

--| A more general version of ~ cbvrabv . Usage of this theorem is discouraged because it depends on ~ ax-13 . Use of ~ cbvrabv2w is preferred. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cbvrabv2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (crab x3 ph A2) (crab y1 ps B2) $;

--| A more general version of ~ cbvrabv . Version of ~ cbvrabv2 with a disjoint variable condition, which does not require ~ ax-13 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.) (Revised by GG, 14-Aug-2025.)
theorem cbvrabv2w {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (A2: class x3) (B2: class y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wceq (crab x3 ph A2) (crab y1 ps B2) $;

--| Subset implication for an indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iinssiin {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciin x3 A2 B2) (ciin x3 A2 C)) $;

--| Membership in indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem eliind2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (ciin x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Subset implication for an indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iinssd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciin x3 A2 B2) C) $;

--| Equivalent wff's yield equal restricted class abstractions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rabbida2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crab x3 ps A2) (crab x3 ch B2)) $;

--| The existence of an indexed union. ` x ` is normally a free-variable parameter in ` B ` , which should be read ` B ( x ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iinexd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (ciin x3 A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Separation Scheme in terms of a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rabexf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 V $ >
  $ wcel (crab x3 ph A2) cvv $;

--| Equivalent wff's yield equal restricted class abstractions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rabbida3 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crab x3 ps A2) (crab x3 ch B2)) $;

--| Restricted quantifier version of one direction of ~ 19.36 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem r19_36vf {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wi ph ps) A2) (wi (wral x3 ph A2) ps) $;

--| Equality deduction for restricted universal quantifier. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem raleqd {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 ps A2) (wral x3 ps B2)) $;

--| Subset implication for an indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iinssf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wss B2 C) A2) (wss (ciin x3 A2 B2) C) $;

--| Subset implication for an indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iinssdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D X: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 X $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciin x3 A2 B2) C) $;

--| Absorption law for restriction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem resabs2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wss B2 C $ >
  $ wceq (cres (cres A2 B2) C) (cres A2 B2) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem ssdf2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $;

--| Restricted class abstraction in a subclass relationship. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem rabssd {x3: setvar} (ph ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) ch) (wcel (cv x3) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crab x3 ch A2) B2) $;

--| Minus a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem rexnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxne A2) (cneg A2)) $;

--| * Inference from Theorem 19.23 of [Margaris] p. 90 (restricted quantifier version). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem rexlimd3 {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wrex x3 ps A2) ch) $;

--| Membership in an intersection implies membership in the first set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem nel1nelini (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wn (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 (cin B2 C)) $;

--| Membership in an intersection implies membership in the second set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem nel2nelini (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wn (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wn (wcel A2 (cin B2 C)) $;

--| Membership in indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem eliunid {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel C B2)) (wcel C (ciun x3 A2 B2)) $;

--| Deduction from Theorem 19.22 of [Margaris] p. 90. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem reximdd {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 ch A2) $;

--| The intersection of two open sets of a topology is an open set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem inopnd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cin A2 B2) J) $;

--| Deduction of restricted abstraction subclass from implication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem ss2rabdf {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crab x3 ps A2) (crab x3 ch A2)) $;

--| If ` A ` is open, then ` A ` is open in the restriction to itself. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem restopn3 (A2 J: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J ctop) (wcel A2 J)) (wcel A2 (co J A2 crest)) $;

--| A topology restricted to an open set is a subset of the original topology. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem restopnssd (ph: wff) (A2 J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co J A2 crest) J) $;

--| A subset belongs in the space it generates via restriction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem restsubel (ph: wff) (A2 J V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cuni J) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co J A2 crest)) $;

--| A subset is open in the topology it generates via restriction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem toprestsubel (ph: wff) (A2 J: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni J)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co J A2 crest)) $;

--| An "identity" law of concretion for restricted abstraction. Special case of Definition 2.1 of [Quine] p. 16. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem rabidd {x3: setvar} (ph ch: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) (crab x3 ch A2)) $;

--| Subset theorem for an indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem iunssdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciun x3 A2 B2) C) $;

--| Subset implication for an indexed intersection. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem iinss2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciin x3 A2 B2) C) $;

--| Restricted quantification of wff not containing quantified variable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem r19_3rzf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wb ph (wral x3 ph A2)) $;

--| Restricted quantifier version of Theorem 19.28 of [Margaris] p. 90. It is valid only when the domain of quantification is not empty. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem r19_28zf {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wb (wral x3 (wa ph ps) A2) (wa ph (wral x3 ps A2))) $;

--| Indexed intersection of class difference. Generalization of half of theorem "De Morgan's laws". (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem iindif2f {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wne A2 c0) (wceq (ciin x3 A2 (cdif B2 C)) (cdif B2 (ciun x3 A2 C))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2024.)
theorem ralfal {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wb (wceq A2 c0) (wral x3 wfal A2) $;

--| Archimedean property of real numbers. For any real number, there is an integer greater than it. Theorem I.29 of [Apostol] p. 26. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 1-Feb-2025.)
theorem archd {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wbr A2 (cv n) clt) cn) $;

--| If an implication is false, the biconditional is false. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem nimnbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wn (wb ph ps) $;

--| If an implication is false, the biconditional is false. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem nimnbi2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wn (wb ph ps) $;

--| Commutative law for the negation of a biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem notbicom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wn (wb ps ph) $;

--| Equality inference for restricted existential quantifier. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem rexeqif {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex x3 ph B2) $;

--| Restricted existential specialization, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem rspced {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 ps B2) $;

--| A function does not change when restricted to a set that contains its domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fnresdmss (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wss A2 B2)) (wceq (cres F B2) F) $;

--| Maps-to notation and Cartesian product for a singleton function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fmptsnxp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wceq (cmpt x3 (csn A2) B2) (cxp (csn A2) (csn B2))) $;

--| Value of a function given by the maps-to notation. Deduction version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fvmpt2bd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel B2 C)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $;

--| The range of a function with finite domain is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem rnmptfi {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt x3 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wcel B2 cfn) (wcel (crn A2) cfn) $;

--| Restriction of a function with respect to the intersection with its domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fresin2 (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wceq (cres F (cin C A2)) (cres F C)) $;

--| A function with finite domain is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ffi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel F cfn) $;

--| An explicit bound for the range of a bounded function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem suprnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class y1) (B2 C: class x3 y1) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C (csup (crn F) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel C cr) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| The range of a function with finite domain is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem rnffi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 cfn)) (wcel (crn F) cfn) $;

--| A function in maps-to notation is a partial map . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem mptelpm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (D V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (co C D cpm)) $;

--| Range of a function defined on an unordered pair. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem rnmptpr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D E: class) (F V W: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (cpr A2 B2) C) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) B2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (cpr D E)) $;

--| Restriction of the mapping operation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem resmpti {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wss B2 A2 $ >
  $ wceq (cres (cmpt x3 A2 C) B2) (cmpt x3 B2 C) $;

--| Union expressed as an indexed union, when a map onto is given. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem founiiun {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfo A2 B2 F) (wceq (cuni B2) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| Distribution law for range of a restriction over a union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem rnresun (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq (crn (cres F (cun A2 B2))) (cun (crn (cres F A2)) (crn (cres F B2))) $;

--| The range of a function in maps-to notation. Same as ~ elrnmpt , but using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem elrnmptf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel C (crn F)) (wrex x3 (wceq C B2) A2)) $;

--| Inclusion relation for two ranges expressed in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem rnmptssrn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3) (C D: class y1) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wceq B2 D) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) (crn (cmpt y1 C D))) $;

--| A 1 to 1 mapping built from disjoint, nonempty sets. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem disjf1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 F: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 A2 V F) $;

--| The range of a function whose domain is a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem rnsnf (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (csn A2) B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) (csn (cfv A2 F))) $;

--| Given a function ` F ` on a well-ordered domain ` A ` there exists a subset of ` A ` such that ` F ` restricted to such subset is injective and onto the range of ` F ` (without using the axiom of choice). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem wessf1ornlem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class y1 z) (R F: class) (G V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (crn F) (crio x3 (wral z (wn (wbr (cv z) (cv x3) R)) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv y1)))) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wf1o (cv x3) (crn F) (cres F (cv x3))) (cpw A2)) $;

--| Given a function ` F ` on a well-ordered domain ` A ` there exists a subset of ` A ` such that ` F ` restricted to such subset is injective and onto the range of ` F ` (without using the axiom of choice). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem wessf1orn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 R F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wwe A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wf1o (cv x3) (crn F) (cres F (cv x3))) (cpw A2)) $;

--| If ` A ` is not in the range, it is not in the range of any restriction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem nelrnres (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (crn B2))) (wn (wcel A2 (crn (cres B2 C)))) $;

--| Disjointness of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem disjrnmpt2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3 y1) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wdisj x3 A2 B2) (wdisj y1 (crn F) (cv y1)) $;

--| Elementhood in an image set. Same as ~ elrnmpt1s , but using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem elrnmpt1sf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (D: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) D) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel D A2) (wcel C V)) (wcel C (crn F)) $;

--| Union expressed as an indexed union, when a map onto is given. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem founiiun0 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfo A2 (cun B2 (csn c0)) F) (wceq (cuni B2) (ciun x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| A bijection built from disjoint sets. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem disjf1o {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (F: class x3) (V: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab x3 (wne B2 c0) A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (cdif (crn F) (csn c0)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C D (cres F C)) $;

--| Only a finite number of disjoint sets can have a nonempty intersection with a finite set ` C ` . The proof uses ~ fodomfi rather than ~ fodomg , and so does not require ~ ax-ac . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.) (Revised by Vincent Gonzalez, 19-Aug-2026.)
theorem disjinfi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C V: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crab x3 (wne (cin B2 C) c0) A2) cfn) $;

--| Value of the composition of an operator, with a given function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem fvovco (ph: wff) (F O V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf X (cxp V W) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y (ccom O F)) (co (cfv (cfv Y F) c1st) (cfv (cfv Y F) c2nd) O)) $;

--| There exists a bijection between a subset of ` NN ` and a given nonempty countable set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem ssnnf1octb {f2: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 com cdom) (wne A2 c0)) (wex f2 (wa (wss (cdm (cv f2)) cn) (wf1o (cdm (cv f2)) A2 (cv f2)))) $;

--| There is a bijection between the set of positive integers and the Cartesian product of the set of positive integers with itself. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem nnf1oxpnn {f2: setvar}:
  $ wex f2 (wf1o cn (cxp cn cn) (cv f2)) $;

--| A biijection from a set to a projection in a two dimensional space. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem projf1o {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cop A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o B2 (cxp (csn A2) B2) F) $;

--| Function value for a member of a set exponentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem fvmap (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 B2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv C F) A2) $;

--| Projection of a factor of an indexed Cartesian product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem fvixp2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cixp x3 A2 B2)) (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $;

--| For a finite set, a choice function exists, without using the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem choicefi {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (W: class x3 f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) B2) A2))) $;

--| The exponentiation of a countable set to a finite set is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem mpct (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cmap) com cdom) $;

--| Value of the distance function of the metric space of complex numbers, composed with a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem cnmetcoval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F: class):
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cc cc) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 (ccom D F)) (cfv (co (cfv (cfv B2 F) c1st) (cfv (cfv B2 F) c2nd) cmin) cabs)) $;

--| Express composition of two functions as a maps-to applying both in sequence. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fcomptss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom G F) (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) G))) $;

--| Membership in a set exponentiated to a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem elmapsnd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wfn F (csn A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co B2 (csn A2) cmap)) $;

--| Subset inheritance for set exponentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem mapss2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap))) $;

--| Difference of two sets exponentiations. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem difmap (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cdif A2 B2) C cmap) (cdif (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap))) $;

--| Given a subset of a set exponentiation, the base set can be restricted. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem unirnmap (ph: wff) (A2 B2 V X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X (co B2 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X (co (crn (cuni X)) A2 cmap)) $;

--| Intersection of two sets exponentiations. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem inmap (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap)) (co (cin A2 B2) C cmap)) $;

--| Composition of two mappings. Similar to ~ fco , but with a weaker condition on the domain of ` F ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fcoss (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D C G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D B2 (ccom F G)) $;

--| Equality condition for two functions defined on a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fsneqrn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (csn A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq F G) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (crn G))) $;

--| Difference of two sets exponentiatiated to a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem difmapsn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdif (co A2 (csn C) cmap) (co B2 (csn C) cmap)) (co (cdif A2 B2) (csn C) cmap)) $;

--| Subset inheritance for set exponentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem mapssbi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss A2 B2) (wss (co A2 C cmap) (co B2 C cmap))) $;

--| Equality theorem for a subset of a set exponentiation, where the exponent is a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem unirnmapsn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C V W X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq C (csn A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X (co B2 C cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq X (co (crn (cuni X)) C cmap)) $;

--| The indexed union of set exponentiations is a subset of the set exponentiation of the indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem iunmapss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (ciun x3 A2 (co B2 C cmap)) (co (ciun x3 A2 B2) C cmap)) $;

--| A subset ` C ` of a set exponentiation to a singleton, is its projection ` D ` exponentiated to the singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem ssmapsn {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class f2) (C: class) (D V: class f2):
  $ wnfc f2 D $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (co B2 (csn A2) cmap)) $ >
  $ wceq D (ciun f2 C (crn (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (co D (csn A2) cmap)) $;

--| The indexed union of set exponentiations to a singleton is equal to the set exponentiation of the indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem iunmapsn {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (V W Z: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciun x3 A2 (co B2 (csn C) cmap)) (co (ciun x3 A2 B2) (csn C) cmap)) $;

--| Mapping domain and codomain of the absolute value function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem absfico:
  $ wf cc (co cc0 cpnf cico) cabs $;

--| The set of left-closed right-open intervals of extended reals maps to subsets of extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem icof:
  $ wf (cxp cxr cxr) (cpw cxr) cico $;

--| The range of a partial function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem elpmrn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wss (crn F) A2) $;

--| The image of a set is a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.) (Proof shortened by SN, 27-Apr-2025.)
theorem imaexi (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wcel A2 V $ >
  $ wcel (cima A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Relax the constraint on ax-cc to dominance instead of equinumerosity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem axccdom {z f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr X com cdom) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) X)) (wne (cv z) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wa (wfn (cv f2) X) (wral z (wcel (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv z)) X))) $;

--| The domain of the mapping operation, deduction form. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem dmmptdff {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt x3 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm A2) B2) $;

--| The domain of the mapping operation, deduction form. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem dmmptdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (B2: class) (C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt x3 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm A2) B2) $;

--| The domain of a partial function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem elpmi2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co A2 B2 cpm)) (wss (cdm F) B2) $;

--| A relation preserving function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem dmrelrnrel {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (R S F: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F) S)) A2) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F) S) $;

--| Membership in the range of an operation class abstraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem elrnmpoid {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C F V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel C V) B2) A2)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) (crn F)) $;

--| An alternative version of the axiom of countable choice. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem axccd {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cen) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wne (cv x3) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| An alternative version of the axiom of countable choice. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem axccd2 {x3 f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 com cdom) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wne (cv x3) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex f2 (wral x3 (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| Express a restricted function as a mapping. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem feqresmptf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres F C) (cmpt x3 C (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| The domain of a mapping is a subset of its base class. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem dmmptssf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) A2 $;

--| The domain of the mapping operation, deduction form. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem dmmptdf2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt x3 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm A2) B2) $;

--| Domain of the upper integers function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem dmuz:
  $ wceq (cdm cuz) cz $;

--| Domain and codomain of the mapping operation; deduction form. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem fmptd2f {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $;

--| An equality theorem for the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.) (Proof shortened by SN, 11-Nov-2024.)
theorem mpteq1df {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cmpt x3 A2 C) (cmpt x3 B2 C)) $;

--| If the domain of a function given by maps-to notation is a set, the function is a set. Inference version of ~ mptexg . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem mptexf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Value of a function given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem fvmpt4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel B2 C)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) B2) $;

--| Functionality of the mapping operation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem fmptf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wcel C B2) A2) (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| The image of a restriction is a subset of the original image. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem resimass (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wss (cima (cres A2 B2) C) (cima A2 C) $;

--| The mapping operation expressed with its actual domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem mptssid {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wceq C (crab x3 (wcel B2 cvv) A2) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (cmpt x3 C B2) $;

--| The maps-to notation defines a function with domain. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2013.) (Revised by Thierry Arnoux, 10-May-2017.)
theorem mptfnd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (cmpt x3 A2 B2) A2) $;

--| Boundness below of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptlb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr) $;

--| Boundness of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbddlem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr) $;

--| Boundness of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbdd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr) $;

--| Membership relation for the values of a function whose image is a subclass. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem funimaeq {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 F G: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cdm G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima F A2) (cima G A2)) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptssf {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2) (wss (crn F) C) $;

--| Boundness below of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbd2lem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3 y1) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr)) $;

--| Boundness below of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbd2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv y1) (cv z) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr)) $;

--| The indexed infimum of real numbers is the negative of the indexed supremum of the negative values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infnsuprnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) (cneg (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2))) cr clt))) $;

--| Closure of the indexed supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals. Range of a function in maps-to notation can be used, to express an indexed supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem suprclrnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) cr) $;

--| A member of a nonempty indexed set of reals is less than or equal to the set's upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem suprubrnmpt2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) cle) $;

--| A member of a nonempty indexed set of reals is less than or equal to the set's upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem suprubrnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr B2 (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) cle) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptssdf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) C) $;

--| Boundness above of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbdlem {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr)) $;

--| Boundness above of the range of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptbd {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2 B2: class x3) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle) (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) cr)) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rnmptss2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn (cmpt x3 A2 C)) (crn (cmpt x3 B2 C))) $;

--| The image of a function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem elmptima {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel C V) (wb (wcel C (cima (cmpt x3 A2 B2) D)) (wrex x3 (wceq C B2) (cin A2 D))) $;

--| A restricted quantifier over an image set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem ralrnmpt3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1) (ch: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral y1 ps (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2))) (wral x3 ch A2)) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem rnmptssbi {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (crn F) C) (wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2)) $;

--| Subset theorem for image. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem imass2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cima C A2) (cima C B2)) $;

--| Membership relation for the values of a function whose image is a subclass. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem imassmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin A2 C))) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (cima F C) D) (wral x3 (wcel B2 D) (cin A2 C))) $;

--| A total function is a partial function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem fpmd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co B2 A2 cpm)) $;

--| An alternative way to express a constant function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem fconst7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cxp A2 (csn B2))) $;

--| Functionality and domain of an ordered-pair class abstraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem fnmptif {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wfn F A2 $;

--| Domain of the mapping operation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem dmmptif {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm F) A2 $;

--| Slightly more general equality inference for the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem mpteq2dfa {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (cmpt x3 A2 C)) $;

--| The domain of the mapping operation, deduction form. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem dmmpt1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (B2 C V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (cmpt x3 B2 C)) B2) $;

--| Functionality of the mapping operation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem fmptff {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wcel C B2) A2) (wf A2 B2 F) $;

--| The value of a function at a point of its domain belongs to its codomain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem fvmptelcdmf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $;

--| A version of ~ fmptd using bound-variable hypothesis instead of a distinct variable condition for ` ph ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Jan-2025.)
theorem fmptdff {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 C F) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ fvmpt2 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem fvmpt2df {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) B2) $;

--| The range of a function with a first-countable domain is itself first-countable. This is a variation of ~ 1stcrestlem , with a not-free hypothesis replacing a disjoint variable constraint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem rn1st {x3: setvar} (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wi (wbr B2 com cdom) (wbr (crn (cmpt x3 B2 C)) com cdom) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem rnmptssff {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel B2 C) A2) (wss (crn F) C) $;

--| The range of a function given by the maps-to notation as a subset. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem rnmptssdff {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C F: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) C) $;

--| Value of a function given by the maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem fvmpt4d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) B2) $;

--| Subtracting from a number, twice the number itself, gives negative the number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sub2times (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 (co c2 A2 cmul) cmin) (cneg A2)) $;

--| A natural number is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem nnxrd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| A natural number is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Jan-2025.)
theorem nnxr (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel N cxr) $;

--| The distance of two distinct complex number is a strictly positive real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem abssubrp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne A2 B2)) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) crp) $;

--| Relationship between membership in a half-open finite set of sequential integers and membership in a finite set of sequential intergers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem elfzfzo (A2 M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co M N cfzo)) (wa (wcel A2 (co M N cfz)) (wbr A2 N clt)) $;

--| Odd number representation by using the floor function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem oddfl (K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cz) (wne (co K c2 cmo) cc0)) (wceq K (co (co c2 (cfv (co K c2 cdiv) cfl) cmul) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Bound for the absolute value of the cosine of a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem abscosbd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 ccos) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| Commutative/associative law that swaps the first and the third factor in a triple product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem mul13d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co C (co B2 A2 cmul) cmul)) $;

--| Negative ` _pi ` is negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem negpilt0:
  $ wbr (cneg cpi) cc0 clt $;

--| A complex number ` A ` that is not real, has a distance from the reals that is strictly larger than ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dstregt0 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdif cc cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) cmin) cabs) clt) cr) crp) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 terms in a mixed addition and subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem subadd4b (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cmin) caddc) (co (co A2 D cmin) (co C B2 cmin) caddc)) $;

--| Not equal and not larger implies smaller. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem xrlttri5d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $;

--| If an integer ` N ` is less than or equal to a real, and we subtract a quantity less than ` 1 ` , then ` N ` is less than or equal to the result. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem zltlesub (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N (co A2 B2 cmin) cle) $;

--| The ratio of a negative numerator and a positive denominator is negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem divlt0gt0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) cc0 clt) $;

--| Swap subtrahend and result of subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem subsub23d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 cmin) C) (wceq (co A2 C cmin) B2)) $;

--| Double of a positive real is larger than the real itself. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _2timesgt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr A2 (co c2 A2 cmul) clt) $;

--| The reals are open with respect to the standard topology. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem reopn:
  $ wcel cr (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $;

--| Swap the first and third terms in a double subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sub31 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 C cmin) cmin) (co C (co B2 A2 cmin) cmin)) $;

--| A positive integer which is not 1 is greater than or equal to 2. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem nnne1ge2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wne N c1)) (wbr c2 N cle) $;

--| A closed enough, smaller real ` C ` has the same floor of ` A ` when both are divided by ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lefldiveq (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (co A2 (co A2 B2 cmo) cmin) A2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cfl) (cfv (co C B2 cdiv) cfl)) $;

--| Distribution of negative over subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem negsubdi3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cneg (co A2 B2 cmin)) (co (cneg A2) (cneg B2) cmin)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ltdiv2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| Bound for the absolute value of the sine of a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem abssinbd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 csin) cabs) c1 cle) $;

--| Floor of ` ( 1 / 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem halffl:
  $ wceq (cfv (co c1 c2 cdiv) cfl) cc0 $;

--| Ordering relation for a strictly monotonic sequence, increasing case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem monoords {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F I J M N: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co M N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co M N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr I J clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv I F) (cfv J F) clt) $;

--| The size of a subset of a finite set is less than the size of the containing set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) TODO (NM): usage (2 times) should be replaced by ~ hashss , and ~ hashssle should be deleted afterwards.
theorem hashssle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wss B2 A2)) (wbr (cfv B2 chash) (cfv A2 chash) cle) $;

--| Not equal and not larger implies smaller. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lttri5d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $;

--| A finite ordered set has a unique order isomorphism to a generic finite sequence of integers. This theorem generalizes ~ fz1iso for the base index and also states the uniqueness condition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fzisoeu {f2: setvar} (ph: wff f2) (H M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel H cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor H clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) (co M c1 cmin) caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (weu f2 (wiso (co M N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $;

--| If three real numbers are less than a fourth real number, the sum of the three real numbers is less than three times the third real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lt3addmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) (co c3 D cmul) clt) $;

--| Triangular inequality, combined with cancellation law for subtraction (applied twice). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem absnpncan2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 D cmin) cabs) (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) caddc) (cfv (co C D cmin) cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| A function with period ` T ` is also periodic with period nonnegative multiple of ` T ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fperiodmullem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (T F: class) (N: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X (co N T cmul) caddc) F) (cfv X F)) $;

--| A function with period T is also periodic with period multiple of T. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fperiodmul {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (T F N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co X (co N T cmul) caddc) F) (cfv X F)) $;

--| Choice of an upper bound for a nonempty bunded set (image set version). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem upbdrech {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (csup (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) B2) A2)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel C cr) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| If four real numbers are less than a fifth real number, the sum of the four real numbers is less than four times the fifth real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lt4addmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C caddc) D caddc) (co c4 E cmul) clt) $;

--| Triangular inequality, combined with cancellation law for subtraction (applied three times). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem absnpncan3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 E cmin) cabs) (co (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 C cmin) cabs) caddc) (cfv (co C D cmin) cabs) caddc) (cfv (co D E cmin) cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Choice of an upper bound for a possibly empty bunded set (image set version). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem upbdrech2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cif (wceq A2 c0) cc0 (csup (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) B2) A2)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel C cr) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| A finite union of bounded sets is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ssfiunibd {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class z) (C: class y1 z w):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) (cuni A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) (cv x3)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (cuni A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex w (wral z (wbr B2 (cv w) cle) C) cr) $;

--| A finite union of bounded sets is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ssfiunibd_b1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class z) (C: class y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) (cuni A2))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) (cv x3)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (cuni A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral z (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) C) cr) $;

--| Remove a successor from the end of a finite set of sequential integers. Similar to ~ fzdifsuc , but with a weaker condition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzdifsuc2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 cmin) cuz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cdif (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn (co N c1 caddc)))) $;

--| A finite sequence of integers is a set of complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzsscn (M N: class):
  $ wss (co M N cfz) cc $;

--| A cancellation law for division. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem divcan8d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co B2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Cancellation law for division and multiplication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dmmcand (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co B2 C cmul) cmul) (co A2 C cmul)) $;

--| A binomial coefficient, in its extended domain, is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem bccld (ph: wff) (K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co N K cbc) cn0) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers that is a subset of ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzssnn0 (N: class):
  $ wss (co cc0 N cfz) cn0 $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xreqle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wceq A2 B2)) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| ` 0 ` is a left identity for extended real addition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xaddlidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 A2 cxad) A2) $;

--| A number is less than or equal to itself plus a nonnegative extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xadd0ge (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 cxad) cle) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' and 'not equals' implies 'less than', for extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xrleneltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $;

--| The extended real addition operation is commutative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xaddcomd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 cxad) (co B2 A2 cxad)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty set of reals, is real if and only if it is bounded-above . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem supxrre3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0)) (wb (wcel (csup A2 cxr clt) cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) $;

--| For any finite subset of the upper integers, there is a finite set of sequential integers that includes it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem uzfissfz {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wss A2 (co M (cv k) cfz)) Z) $;

--| Addition of extended reals preserves the "less than or equal to" relation, in the right slot. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xleadd2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 cxad) (co C B2 cxad) cle) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals can be approximated from below by elements of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem suprltrp {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z) (A2: class) (X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wbr (co (csup A2 cr clt) X cmin) (cv z) clt) A2) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded set of reals can be approximated from below by elements of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem suprltrp_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wbr (co (csup A2 cr clt) X cmin) (cv y1) clt) A2) $;

--| Addition of extended reals preserves the "less than or equal to" relation, in the left slot. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xleadd1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cxad) (co B2 C cxad) cle) $;

--| Equality implies 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xreqled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| An extended real greater than or equal to ` +oo ` is ` +oo ` (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xrgepnfd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cpnf A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cpnf) $;

--| A nonnegative extended real is not minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xrge0nemnfd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cmnf) $;

--| If a real number can be approximated from below by members of a set, then it is less than or equal to the supremum of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem supxrgere {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wbr (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) (cv y1) clt) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (csup A2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iuneqfzuz : here, inclusion is proven; aiuneqfzuz uses this lemma twice, to prove equality. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem iuneqfzuzlem {m n: setvar} (A2 B2: class n) (N: class m) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wral m (wceq (ciun n (co N (cv m) cfz) A2) (ciun n (co N (cv m) cfz) B2)) Z) (wss (ciun n Z A2) (ciun n Z B2)) $;

--| If two unions indexed by upper integers are equal if they agree on any partial indexed union. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem iuneqfzuz {m n: setvar} (A2 B2: class n) (N: class m) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wral m (wceq (ciun n (co N (cv m) cfz) A2) (ciun n (co N (cv m) cfz) B2)) Z) (wceq (ciun n Z A2) (ciun n Z B2)) $;

--| Adding both side of two inequalities. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xle2addd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cxad) (co C D cxad) cle) $;

--| If an extended real number can be approximated from below by members of a set, then it is less than or equal to the supremum of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem supxrgelem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wbr B2 (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cxad) clt) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (csup A2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| If an extended real number can be approximated from below by members of a set, then it is less than or equal to the supremum of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem supxrge {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wbr B2 (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cxad) cle) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (csup A2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| If any element of ` A ` can be approximated from below by members of ` B ` , then the supremum of ` A ` is less than or equal to the supremum of ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem suplesup {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral y1 (wrex z (wbr (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) (cv z) clt) B2) crp) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup A2 cxr clt) (csup B2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The infimum of a set of extended reals is less than an extended real if and only if the set contains a smaller number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem infxrglb {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr (cinf A2 cxr clt) B2 clt) (wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) B2 clt) A2)) $;

--| A number is less than or equal to itself plus a nonnegative extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem xadd0ge2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co B2 A2 cxad) cle) $;

--| An extended real that is not ` +oo ` is less than ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem nepnfltpnf (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cpnf clt) $;

--| Addition to both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem ltadd12dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co C D caddc) clt) $;

--| An extended real that is not minus infinity, is larger than minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem nemnftgtmnft (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wne A2 cmnf)) (wbr cmnf A2 clt) $;

--| 'Greater than' is a strict ordering on the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem xrgtso:
  $ wor cxr (ccnv clt) $;

--| The positive reals form a set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem rpex:
  $ wcel crp cvv $;

--| A nonnegative extended real is nonnegative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem xrge0ge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| A subset of extended reals that does not contain ` +oo ` and ` -oo ` is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem xrssre (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cpnf A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cmnf A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $;

--| A finite subset of the upper integers is a subset of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem ssuzfz (ph: wff) (A2 M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co M (csup A2 cr clt) cfz)) $;

--| The absolute value is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem absfun:
  $ wfun cabs $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty, bounded subset of extended reals can be approximated from above by an element of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem infrpge {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (co (cinf A2 cxr clt) B2 cxad) cle) A2) $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty, bounded subset of extended reals can be approximated from above by an element of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem infrpge_b1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2: class) (B2: class y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr (cv x3) (co (cinf A2 cxr clt) B2 cxad) cle) A2) $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty, bounded subset of extended reals can be approximated from above by an element of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem infrpge_b2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (co (cinf A2 cxr clt) B2 cxad) cle) A2) $;

--| If an extended real number ` A ` can be approximated from above, adding positive reals to ` B ` , then ` A ` is less than or equal to ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem xrlexaddrp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wbr A2 (co B2 (cv x3) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| The supremum function distributes over subtraction in a sense similar to that in ~ supaddc . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem supsubc {x3 y1 z v: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z v):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (cab z (wrex v (wceq (cv z) (co (cv v) B2 cmin)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csup A2 cr clt) B2 cmin) (csup C cr clt)) $;

--| Show that ` A ` is less than ` B ` by showing that there is no positive bound on the difference. A variant on ~ xralrple . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem xralrple2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wral x3 (wbr A2 (co (co c1 (cv x3) caddc) B2 cmul) cle) crp)) $;

--| The first ` N ` elements of the set of nonnegative integers are distinct from any later members. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem nnuzdisj (N: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) c0 $;

--| Divsion by a number greater than 1. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem ltdivgt1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr c1 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem xrltned (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 B2) $;

--| Express the set of positive integers as the disjoint (see ~ nnuzdisj ) union of the first ` N ` values and the rest. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem nnsplit (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq cn (cun (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz))) $;

--| Division into a fraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem divdiv3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cdiv) (co A2 (co C B2 cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Comparison of the square of two numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem abslt2sqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| The set of rational numbers is equinumerous to omega (the set of finite ordinal numbers). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem qenom:
  $ wbr cq com cen $;

--| The set of rational numbers is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem qct:
  $ wbr cq com cdom $;

--| 'less than or equal to' but not 'less than' implies 'equal' . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem lenlteq (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| An extended real that is neither minus infinity, nor plus infinity, is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem xrred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cmnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| The cartesian square of ` RR ` is a subset of the cartesian square of ` CC ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem rr2sscn2:
  $ wss (cxp cr cr) (cxp cc cc) $;

--| The infimum of a set of extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infxr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wn (wbr (cv x3) B2 clt)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wi (wbr B2 (cv x3) clt) (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) A2)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cinf A2 cxr clt) B2) $;

--| The infimum of an unbounded-below set of extended reals is minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infxrunb2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wb (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) A2) cr) (wceq (cinf A2 cxr clt) cmnf)) $;

--| The infimum of a bounded-below set of extended reals is greater than minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infxrbnd2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wb (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wbr cmnf (cinf A2 cxr clt) clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infleinf , case ` B =/= (/) /\ -oo < inf ( B , RR* , < ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infleinflem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 W X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (co (cinf B2 cxr clt) (co W c2 cdiv) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Z (co X (co W c2 cdiv) cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf A2 cxr clt) (co (cinf B2 cxr clt) W cxad) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ infleinf , when ` inf ( B , RR* , < ) = -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infleinflem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (co R c2 cmin) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Z (co X c1 cxad) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Z R clt) $;

--| If any element of ` B ` can be approximated from above by members of ` A ` , then the infimum of ` A ` is less than or equal to the infimum of ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem infleinf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) crp)) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cxad) cle) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf A2 cxr clt) (cinf B2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| Show that ` A ` is less than ` B ` by showing that there is no positive bound on the difference. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem xralrple4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wral x3 (wbr A2 (co B2 (co (cv x3) N cexp) caddc) cle) crp)) $;

--| Show that ` A ` is less than ` B ` by showing that there is no positive bound on the difference. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem xralrple3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wral x3 (wbr A2 (co B2 (co C (cv x3) cmul) caddc) cle) crp)) $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is an integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem eluzelzd (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $;

--| If any element of ` A ` is less than or equal to an element in ` B ` , then the supremum of ` A ` is less than or equal to the supremum of ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem suplesup2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csup A2 cxr clt) (csup B2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| ` N ` is a natural number large enough that its reciprocal is smaller than the given positive ` E ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem recnnltrp (E N: class):
  $ wceq N (co (cfv (co c1 E cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wcel E crp) (wa (wcel N cn) (wbr (co c1 N cdiv) E clt)) $;

--| The set of positive integers is nonempty. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem nnn0:
  $ wne cn c0 $;

--| A finite set of sequential integer is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fzct (M N: class):
  $ wbr (co N M cfz) com cdom $;

--| Any positive real number is greater than the reciprocal of a positive integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem rpgtrecnn {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wrex n (wbr (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) A2 clt) cn) $;

--| A half-open integer interval is a subset of an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fzossuz (M N: class):
  $ wss (co M N cfzo) (cfv M cuz) $;

--| The real and extended real infima match when the set is finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem infxrrefi (A2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wcel A2 cfn) (wne A2 c0)) (wceq (cinf A2 cxr clt) (cinf A2 cr clt)) $;

--| Show that ` A ` is less than ` B ` by showing that there is no positive bound on the difference. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem xrralrecnnle {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wral n (wbr A2 (co B2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cle) cn)) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integer is countable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fzoct (M N: class):
  $ wbr (co N M cfzo) com cdom $;

--| A function taking real values, is a function taking extended real values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem frexr (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive natural number is a positive real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem nnrecrp (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co c1 N cdiv) crp) $;

--| The reciprocal of a negative number is negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem reclt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc0 clt) $;

--| If a number is negative, its negative is positive. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem lt0neg1dd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cneg A2) clt) $;

--| The infimum of an arbitrary set of extended reals is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem infxrcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cinf A2 cxr clt) cxr) $;

--| Show that ` A ` is less than ` B ` by showing that there is no positive bound on the difference. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem xrralrecnnge {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wral n (wbr (co A2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) cmin) B2 cle) cn)) $;

--| The reciprocal of a negative number is negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem reclt0 (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 cc0 clt) (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) cc0 clt)) $;

--| Multiplying by a negative number, swaps the order. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ltmulneg (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co B2 C cmul) (co A2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| For all but finitely many. Some authors say "cofinitely many". Some authors say "ultimately". Compare with ~ eliuniin and ~ eliuniin2 (here, the precondition can be dropped; see ~ eliuniincex ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem allbutfi {m n: setvar} (A2 B2 M: class m n) (X: class) (Z: class m n):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) B2)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X A2) (wrex n (wral m (wcel X B2) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than', when both are negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ltdiv23neg (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt)) $;

--| A consequence of trichotomy. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem xreqnltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| Minus infinity is not a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem mnfnre2:
  $ wn (wcel cmnf cr) $;

--| The integers are a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem zssxr:
  $ wss cz cxr $;

--| A nonempty finite indexed set contains its supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem fisupclrnmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C R: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wor A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csup (crn (cmpt x3 B2 C)) A2 R) A2) $;

--| The supremum of an unbounded-above set of extended reals is plus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supxrunb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wb (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) cpnf)) $;

--| A nonempty finite set of real numbers is bounded (image set version). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem fimaxre4 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $;

--| The set of reals is nonempty. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem ren0:
  $ wne cr c0 $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is an integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem eluzelz2 (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wcel N cz) $;

--| Absorption law for restriction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem resabs2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cres A2 B2) C) (cres A2 B2)) $;

--| Membership of the least member in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzid2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (cfv N cuz)) (wcel M (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded indexed set of extended reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supxrleubrnmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) C cle) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| An upper set of integers is a subset of the Reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzssre2 (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wss Z cr $;

--| Subset relationship for two sets of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzssd (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv N cuz) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem eluzd (ph: wff) (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M N cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $;

--| A member of a nonempty indexed set of reals is greater than or equal to the set's lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infxrlbrnmpt2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) C) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) D cle) $;

--| An extended real is real iff it is not an infinty. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem xrre4 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wne A2 cmnf) (wne A2 cpnf))) $;

--| The upper integers function applied to a non-integer, is the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uz0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel M cz)) (wceq (cfv M cuz) c0) $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is an integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem eluzelz2d (ph: wff) (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $;

--| If any element in ` B ` is greater than or equal to an element in ` A ` , then the infimum of ` A ` is less than or equal to the infimum of ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infleinf2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) B2)) (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf A2 cxr clt) (cinf B2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| "Unbounded below" expressed with ` < ` and with ` <_ ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem unb2ltle {x3 y1 w: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wb (wral w (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv w) clt) A2) cr) (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr)) $;

--| Membership of the least member in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzidd2 (ph: wff) (M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M Z) $;

--| Subset relationship for two sets of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzssd2 (ph: wff) (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cfv N cuz) Z) $;

--| An indexed set of absolute values of real numbers is bounded if and only if the original values are bounded above and below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rexabslelem {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wa (wrex w (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv w) cle) A2) cr) (wrex z (wral x3 (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) A2) cr))) $;

--| An indexed set of absolute values of real numbers is bounded if and only if the original values are bounded above and below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rexabsle {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wa (wrex w (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv w) cle) A2) cr) (wrex z (wral x3 (wbr (cv z) B2 cle) A2) cr))) $;

--| An indexed set of absolute values of real numbers is bounded if and only if the original values are bounded above and below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rexabsle_b {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wa (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr))) $;

--| Given a "for all but finitely many" condition, the condition holds from ` N ` on. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem allbutfiinf {m n: setvar} (ph: wff m n) (A2: class m n) (B2: class m) (M N: class m n) (X Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wceq N (cinf (crab n (wral m (wcel X B2) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) Z) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel N Z) (wral m (wcel X B2) (cfv N cuz))) $;

--| The real and extended real indexed suprema match when the indexed real supremum exists. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supxrrernmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded indexed set of reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem suprleubrnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) C cle) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| An indexed infimum of extended reals is smaller than another indexed infimum of extended reals, when every indexed element is smaller than the corresponding one. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infrnmptle {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 C)) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The infimum of an unbounded-below set of extended reals is minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infxrunb3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wb (wral x3 (wrex y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) (wceq (cinf A2 cxr clt) cmnf)) $;

--| The upper integers are all nonempty. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzn0d (ph: wff) (M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Z c0) $;

--| Subset relationship for two sets of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzssd3 (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wss (cfv N cuz) Z) $;

--| An indexed set of absolute values of real numbers is bounded if and only if the original values are bounded above and below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem rexabsle2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wa (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr))) $;

--| The infimum of an unbounded-below set of extended reals is minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem infxrunb3rnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral y1 (wrex x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wceq (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) cmnf)) $;

--| The indexed supremum of a nonempty set of reals, is real if and only if it is bounded-above . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supxrre3rnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) cr) (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) $;

--| A set of reals, indexed by upper integers, is bound if and only if any upper part is bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzublem {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j) (B2: class j) (K M: class x3) (W: class x3 j) (X: class j) (Y: class x3 j) (Z: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j X $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wceq W (csup (crn (cmpt j (co M K cfz) B2)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq X (cif (wbr W Y cle) Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral j (wbr B2 Y cle) (cfv K cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) Z) cr) $;

--| A set of reals, indexed by upper integers, is bound if and only if any upper part is bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzub {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (B2: class j) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) Z) cr)) $;

--| A subset of the reals is a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem ssrexr (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $;

--| Removing minus infinity from a set does not affect its supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supxrmnf2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wceq (csup (cdif A2 (csn cmnf)) cxr clt) (csup A2 cxr clt)) $;

--| The supremum of an arbitrary set of extended reals is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supxrcli (A2: class):
  $ wss A2 cxr $ >
  $ wcel (csup A2 cxr clt) cxr $;

--| Membership of the least member in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzid3 (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wcel N (cfv N cuz)) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty set is greater than or equal to the infimum. The second condition is needed, see ~ supxrltinfxr . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infxrlesupxr (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cinf A2 cxr clt) (csup A2 cxr clt) cle) $;

--| Equality of two extended numbers with ` -e ` in front of them. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegeqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxne A2) (cxne B2)) $;

--| The extended real negative of a real number is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegrecl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cxne A2) cr) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ negneg . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegnegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cxr $ >
  $ wceq (cxne (cxne A2)) A2 $;

--| Equality of two extended numbers with ` -e ` in front of them. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegeqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wceq (cxne A2) (cxne B2) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ nfxneg . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem nfxnegd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnfc x3 (cxne A2)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ negnegd . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cxne (cxne A2)) A2) $;

--| An upper integer is a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzred (ph: wff) (A2 M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| Closure of extended real negative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cxr $ >
  $ wcel (cxne A2) cxr $;

--| The indexed supremum of a bounded-above set of reals is the negation of the indexed infimum of that set's image under negation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supminfrnmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cr clt) (cneg (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2))) cr clt))) $;

--| Adding plus infinity to a set does not affect its infimum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infxrpnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wceq (cinf (cun A2 (csn cpnf)) cxr clt) (cinf A2 cxr clt)) $;

--| The infimum of an arbitrary indexed set of extended reals is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infxrrnmptcl {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) cxr) $;

--| Negative of one side of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem leneg2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 (cneg B2) cle) (wbr B2 (cneg A2) cle)) $;

--| The supremum of the empty set is strictly smaller than the infimum of the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supxrltinfxr:
  $ wbr (csup c0 cxr clt) (cinf c0 cxr clt) clt $;

--| A number is less than or equal to the maximum of it and another. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem max1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) B2 A2) cle) $;

--| The supremum of a nonempty bounded indexed set of extended reals is less than or equal to an upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supxrleubrnmptf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 C $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) C cle) (wral x3 (wbr B2 C cle) A2)) $;

--| 'Not less than or equal to' implies 'grater than'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem nleltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $;

--| An integer is an extended real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem zxrd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| The infimum of an indexed set of extended reals is greater than or equal to a lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infxrgelbrnmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr C (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) cle) (wral x3 (wbr C B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| Half of a positive real is less than the original number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem rphalfltd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 c2 cdiv) A2 clt) $;

--| An upper set of integers is a subset of all integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzssz2 (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wss Z cz $;

--| Negative of one side of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem leneg3d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cneg A2) B2 cle) (wbr (cneg B2) A2 cle)) $;

--| A number is less than or equal to the maximum of it and another. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem max2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) B2 A2) cle) $;

--| The upper integers function needs to be applied to an integer, in order to return a nonempty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzn0bi (M: class):
  $ wb (wne (cfv M cuz) c0) (wcel M cz) $;

--| If the extended real negative is real, then the number itself is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegrecl2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel (cxne A2) cr)) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for the negative of an extended real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem nfxneg {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cxne A2) $;

--| An upper integer is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzxrd (ph: wff) (A2 M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| Removing plus infinity from a set does not affect its infimum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infxrpnf2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cxr) (wceq (cinf (cdif A2 (csn cpnf)) cxr clt) (cinf A2 cxr clt)) $;

--| The extended real suprema of a set of reals is the extended real negative of the extended real infima of that set's image under negation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supminfxr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) (cxne (cinf (crab x3 (wcel (cneg (cv x3)) A2) cr) cxr clt))) $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty, bounded below, indexed subset of extended reals can be approximated from above by an element of the set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem infrpgernmpt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle) A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wbr B2 (co (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) C cxad) cle) A2) $;

--| An extended real is real if and only if its extended negative is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cxne A2) cr)) $;

--| If the extended real negative is real, then the number itself is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegrecl2d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxne A2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| An upper integer is an extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzxr (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv M cuz)) (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| The extended real suprema of a set of extended reals is the extended real negative of the extended real infima of that set's image under extended real negation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supminfxr2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) (cxne (cinf (crab x3 (wcel (cxne (cv x3)) A2) cxr) cxr clt))) $;

--| An extended real is real if and only if its extended negative is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem xnegred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (cxne A2) cr)) $;

--| The indexed supremum of a set of reals is the negation of the indexed infimum of that set's image under negation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem supminfxrrnmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup (crn (cmpt x3 A2 B2)) cxr clt) (cxne (cinf (crn (cmpt x3 A2 (cxne B2))) cxr clt))) $;

--| The minimum of two numbers is less than or equal to the first. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem min1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) A2 B2) A2 cle) $;

--| The minimum of two numbers is less than or equal to the second. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem min2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) A2 B2) B2 cle) $;

--| An extended real that is neither real nor plus infinity, is minus infinity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xrnpnfmnf (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cmnf) $;

--| An upper set of integers is a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem uzsscn (M: class):
  $ wss (cfv M cuz) cc $;

--| The absolute value of the imaginary part of a non-real, complex number, is strictly positive. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem absimnre (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs) crp) $;

--| An upper set of integers is a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem uzsscn2 (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wss Z cc $;

--| The standard topologies on the extended reals and on the complex numbers, coincide when restricted to the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xrtgcntopre:
  $ wceq (co (cfv cle cordt) cr crest) (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) cr crest) $;

--| The absolute value of the imaginary part of a complex number is a lower bound of the distance to any real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem absimlere (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) cle) $;

--| The positive reals are a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem rpssxr:
  $ wss crp cxr $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, increasing case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 13-Feb-2022.)
theorem monoordxrv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv N F) cle) $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, increasing case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 13-Feb-2022.)
theorem monoordxr {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class k) (M N: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv N F) cle) $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, decreasing case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 13-Feb-2022.)
theorem monoord2xrv {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N F) (cfv M F) cle) $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, decreasing case. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 13-Feb-2022.)
theorem monoord2xr {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class k) (M N: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N F) (cfv M F) cle) $;

--| An extended real is plus infinity iff it's larger than all real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 13-Feb-2022.)
theorem xrpnf {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wceq A2 cpnf) (wral x3 (wbr (cv x3) A2 cle) cr)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ lenegcon1 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlenegcon1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr (cxne A2) B2 cle) (wbr (cxne B2) A2 cle)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ lenegcon2 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlenegcon2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 (cxne B2) cle) (wbr B2 (cxne A2) cle)) $;

--| The preimage of a set of extended reals that does not contain a value ` C ` is the union of the preimage of the elements smaller than ` C ` and the preimage of the subset of elements larger than ` C ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Dec-2024.)
theorem pimxrneun {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crab x3 (wne B2 C) A2) (cun (crab x3 (wbr B2 C clt) A2) (crab x3 (wbr C B2 clt) A2))) $;

--| A function is convergent if and only if it is Cauchy. Theorem 12-5.3 of [Gleason] p. 180. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem caucvgbf {x3 j k: setvar} (F M: class j k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F V)) (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| A convergent function is Cauchy. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem cvgcau {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F M V: class j k) (X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) X clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A convergent function is Cauchy. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem cvgcaule {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F M V: class j k) (X Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) X cle)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A simple counterexample related to theorem ~ rexanuz2 , demonstrating the necessity of its disjoint variable constraints. Here, ` j ` appears free in ` ph ` , showing that without these constraints, ~ rexanuz2 and similar theorems would not hold (see ~ rexanre and ~ rexanuz ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 15-Feb-2025.)
theorem rexanuz2nf {j k: setvar} (ph ps: wff j k) (Z: class j k):
  $ wceq Z cn0 $ >
  $ wb ph (wa (wceq (cv j) cc0) (wbr (cv j) (cv k) cle)) $ >
  $ wb ps (wbr cc0 (cv k) clt) $ >
  $ wn (wb (wrex j (wral k (wa ph ps) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wa (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wrex j (wral k ps (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z))) $;

--| A real number larger than the upper bound of a left-open right-closed interval is not an element of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem gtnelioc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc))) $;

--| An open interval is a subset of its right closure. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioossioc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co A2 B2 cioc) $;

--| A condition for two open intervals not to be disjoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioondisj2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wcel D cxr) (wbr C D clt))) (wa (wbr A2 D clt) (wbr D B2 cle))) (wne (cin (co A2 B2 cioo) (co C D cioo)) c0) $;

--| A condition for two open intervals not to be disjoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioondisj1 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wcel D cxr) (wbr C D clt))) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 clt))) (wne (cin (co A2 B2 cioo) (co C D cioo)) c0) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is greater than its lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioogtlb (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Extreme Value Theorem on y closed interval, for the absolute value of y continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem evthiccabs {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wrex z (wral w (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) cabs) (cfv (cfv (cv w) F) cabs) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| A real number smaller than the lower bound of a closed interval is not an element of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ltnelicc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| Membership in an open real interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliood (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| An upper bound for the distance from the center of an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iooabslt (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 caddc) cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) B2 clt) $;

--| A real number greater than the upper bound of a closed interval is not an element of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem gtnelicc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| An open interval has empty intersection with its bounds. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iooinlbub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cioo) (cpr A2 B2)) c0 $;

--| An element of a left-open right-closed interval is larger than its lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iocgtlb (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc))) (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| An element of a left-open right-closed interval is smaller than or equal to its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iocleub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc))) (wbr C B2 cle) $;

--| Membership in a closed real interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliccd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| A member of a closed interval of reals is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliccre (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel C cr) $;

--| Element of an open interval shifted by a displacement. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliooshift (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 (co B2 C cioo)) (wcel (co A2 D caddc) (co (co B2 D caddc) (co C D caddc) cioo))) $;

--| Membership in a left-open right-closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliocd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) $;

--| An element of a left-closed right-open interval is less than its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem icoltub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico))) (wbr C B2 clt) $;

--| A member of a left-open right-closed interval of reals is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliocre (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc))) (wcel C cr) $;

--| An element of an open interval is less than its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iooltub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wbr C B2 clt) $;

--| The interior of an interval in the standard topology on ` RR ` is the open interval itself. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioontr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) cnt)) (co A2 B2 cioo) $;

--| The closure of one end of an open real interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem snunioo1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wceq (cun (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn A2)) (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| A left-open right-closed interval does not contain its left endpoint. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lbioc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 cioc)) $;

--| The midpoint is an element of the open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioomidp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| If the open inverval is removed from the closed interval, only the bounds are left. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iccdifioo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (co A2 B2 cioo)) (cpr A2 B2)) $;

--| An open interval is the closed interval without the bounds. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iccdifprioo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (cpr A2 B2)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Biconditional form of ~ ioossioo . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioossioobi (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wss (co C D cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 cle))) $;

--| A closed interval shifted by a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iccshift {z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (crab w (wrex z (wceq (cv w) (co (cv z) T caddc)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cc)) $;

--| An upper bound to the distance of two elements in a closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iccsuble (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C D cmin) (co B2 A2 cmin) cle) $;

--| A left-open right-closed interval is an open set of the standard topology restricted to an interval that contains the original interval and has the same upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iocopn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K (co A2 B2 cioc) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co C B2 cioc) J) $;

--| Membership in a closed interval and in a left-open right-closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliccelioc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wne C A2))) $;

--| An open interval shifted by a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iooshift {z w: setvar} (ph: wff w) (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cioo) (crab w (wrex z (wceq (cv w) (co (cv z) T caddc)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cc)) $;

--| Intersection of two adiacent closed intervals is a singleton. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iccintsng (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle))) (wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cicc) (co B2 C cicc)) (csn B2)) $;

--| Left-closed right-open interval gotten by a closed iterval taking away the upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem icoiccdif (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cico) (cdif (co A2 B2 cicc) (csn B2))) $;

--| A left-closed right-open interval is an open set of the standard topology restricted to an interval that contains the original interval and has the same lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem icoopn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C J K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K (co A2 B2 cico) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 C cico) J) $;

--| A left-closed, right-open interval does not contain its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem icoub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wn (wcel B2 (co A2 B2 cico))) $;

--| Membership in a closed real interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem eliccxrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| ` +oo ` is a nonnegative extended real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem pnfel0pnf:
  $ wcel cpnf (co cc0 cpnf cicc) $;

--| An element of a closed interval that is not a member of the left-closed right-open interval, must be the upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem eliccnelico (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C B2) $;

--| A member of a closed interval that is not the upper bound, is a member of the left-closed, right-open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem eliccelicod (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| A nonnegative extended real that is not ` +oo ` is a real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem ge0xrre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wne A2 cpnf)) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A nonnegative extended Real number smaller than or equal to a Real number, is a Real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem ge0lere (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $;

--| Membership in a left-closed, right-open interval with real bounds. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem elicores {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (crn (cres cico (cxp cr cr)))) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cico)) cr) cr) $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty set, included in a closed interval, is a member of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem inficc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne S c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cinf S cxr clt) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| The rational numbers are dense in ` RR ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem qinioo (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cin cq (co A2 B2 cioo)) c0) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| A real number smaller than or equal to the lower bound of a left-open right-closed interval is not an element of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem lenelioc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc))) $;

--| A nonempty open interval is uncountable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem ioonct (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 B2 cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr C com cdom)) $;

--| A real number greater than the upper bound of a closed interval is not an element of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem xrgtnelicc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) $;

--| The difference of two closed intervals with the same lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem iccdificc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdif (co A2 C cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co B2 C cioc)) $;

--| A nonempty left-open, right-closed interval is uncountable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem iocnct (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 B2 cioc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr C com cdom)) $;

--| A closed interval, with more than one element is uncountable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Jan-2021.)
theorem iccnct (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 B2 cicc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr C com cdom)) $;

--| A closed interval expressed as the indexed intersection of open intervals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem iooiinicc {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciin n cn (co (co A2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) cmin) (co B2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cioo)) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is more than or equal to its lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem iccgelbd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $;

--| An element of an open interval is less than its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem iooltubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 clt) $;

--| An element of a left-closed right-open interval is less than its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem icoltubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 clt) $;

--| The rational numbers are dense in ` RR* ` : any two extended real numbers have a rational between them. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem qelioo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cq) $;

--| Every open set of reals is the (countable) union of open interval with rational bounds. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem tgqioo2 {q: setvar} (ph: wff q) (A2: class) (J: class q):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex q (wa (wss (cv q) (cima cioo (cxp cq cq))) (wceq A2 (cuni (cv q))))) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is less than or equal to its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem iccleubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $;

--| A member of an open interval of reals is a real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem elioored (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co B2 C cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is greater than its lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ioogtlbd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| ` (,) ` is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ioofun:
  $ wfun cioo $;

--| A left-closed, right-open, interval of extended reals, intersected with the Reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem icomnfinre (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin (co cmnf A2 cico) cr) (co cmnf A2 cioo)) $;

--| The square compared with its argument. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem sqrlearg (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) A2 cle) (wcel A2 (co cc0 c1 cicc))) $;

--| If the supremum belongs to a set of reals, the set is a subset of the unbounded below, right-closed interval, with upper bound equal to the supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ressiocsup (ph: wff) (A2 S I: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup A2 cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S A2) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cmnf S cioc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 I) $;

--| If the supremum does not belong to a set of reals, the set is a subset of the unbounded below, right-open interval, with upper bound equal to the supremum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ressioosup (ph: wff) (A2 S I: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (csup A2 cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S A2)) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cmnf S cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 I) $;

--| A left-open, right-closed interval expressed as the indexed intersection of open intervals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem iooiinioc {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ciin n cn (co A2 (co B2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) cioo)) (co A2 B2 cioc)) $;

--| If the infimum does not belong to a set of reals, the set is a subset of the unbounded above, left-open interval, with lower bound equal to the infimum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem ressiooinf (ph: wff) (A2 S I: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cinf A2 cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel S A2)) $ >
  $ wceq I (co S cpnf cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 I) $;

--| An element of a left-open right-closed interval is smaller than or equal to its upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem iocleubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C B2 cle) $;

--| An upper interval of integers is the intersection of the integers with an upper part of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzinico (ph: wff) (M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Z (cin cz (co M cpnf cico))) $;

--| Preimage of a right-closed interval, unbounded below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem preimaiocmnf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima (ccnv F) (co cmnf B2 cioc)) (crab x3 (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) B2 cle) A2)) $;

--| An upper interval of integers is the intersection of a larger upper interval of integers with an upper part of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem uzinico2 (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N cuz) (cin (cfv M cuz) (co N cpnf cico))) $;

--| An upper interval of integers doesn't change when it's intersected with a left-closed, unbounded above interval, with the same lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzinico3 (ph: wff) (M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq Z (cin Z (co M cpnf cico))) $;

--| The domain of the closed-below, open-above interval function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem dmico:
  $ wceq (cdm cico) (cxp cxr cxr) $;

--| The closed-below, open-above interval function's value is empty outside of its domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem ndmico (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cico) c0) $;

--| The upper integers are unbounded above. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzubioo {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (M X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wcel (cv k) Z) (co X cpnf cioo)) $;

--| The upper integers are unbounded above. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzubico {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (M X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wcel (cv k) Z) (co X cpnf cico)) $;

--| The upper integers are unbounded above. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzubioo2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 k) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex k (wcel (cv k) Z) (co (cv x3) cpnf cioo)) cr) $;

--| The upper integers are unbounded above. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem uzubico2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 k) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex k (wcel (cv k) Z) (co (cv x3) cpnf cico)) cr) $;

--| An element of a left-open right-closed interval is larger than its lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem iocgtlbd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| The topology on the extended reals coincides with the standard topology on the reals, when restricted to ` RR ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xrtgioo2:
  $ wceq (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (co (cfv cle cordt) cr crest) $;

--| Closure of a finite sum of complex numbers ` A ( k ) ` . A version of ~ fsummulc1 using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fsummulc1f {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (csu k A2 B2) C cmul) (csu k A2 (co B2 C cmul))) $;

--| Closure of a nonempty, finite sum of positive integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fsumnncl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wne A2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csu k A2 B2) cn) $;

--| The finite sum of nonnegative reals is a nonnegative real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem fsumge0cl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csu k A2 B2) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $;

--| Re-index a finite sum using a bijection. Same as ~ fsumf1o , but using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem fsumf1of {k n: setvar} (ph: wff k n) (A2: class n) (B2 C: class k) (D: class n) (F: class k) (G: class n):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) G) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o C A2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) C)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) F) G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k A2 B2) (csu n C D)) $;

--| Sum over a disjoint indexed union, intersected with a finite set ` D ` . Similar to ~ fsumiun , but here ` A ` and ` B ` need not be finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 17-Aug-2020.)
theorem fsumiunss {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class k) (D: class) (V: class k):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k (ciun x3 A2 (cin B2 D)) C) (csu x3 (crab x3 (wne (cin B2 D) c0) A2) (csu k (cin B2 D) C))) $;

--| Closure of a finite sum of reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem fsumreclf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csu k A2 B2) cr) $;

--| A shorter sum of nonnegative terms is smaller than a longer one. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 21-Nov-2020.)
theorem fsumlessf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (csu k C B2) (csu k A2 B2) cle) $;

--| Finite sum of function values, for a function of finite support. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 24-Dec-2020.)
theorem fsumsupp0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k (co F cc0 csupp) (cfv (cv k) F)) (csu k A2 (cfv (cv k) F))) $;

--| A finite sum expressed in terms of a partial sum of an infinite series. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fsumsermpt {k n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class k) (F G: class k n) (M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n Z (csu k (co M (cv n) cfz) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cseq caddc (cmpt k Z A2) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $;

--| Multiplying a finite number of values in [ 0 , 1 ] , gives the final product itself a number in [ 0 , 1 ]. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmul01 {i: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2 B2 K: class i) (L M: class):
  $ wnfc i B2 $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cseq cmul B2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv L cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co L M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv i) B2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) B2) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) B2) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv K A2) cle) (wbr (cfv K A2) c1 cle)) $;

--| If ' Y ' is closed under the multiplication of two functions, then Y is closed under the multiplication ( ' X ' ) of a finite number of functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmulcl {t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (P: class t f2 g2) (T: class) (U M N X: class t f2 g2) (Y: class t):
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv N (cseq P U c1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) Y) (wcel (cv g2) Y)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X Y) $;

--| induction step for the proof of ~ fmuldfeq . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmuldfeqlem1 {t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t f2 g2 i) (P: class t f2 g2 i) (T: class i) (U: class g2) (F: class t f2 g2 i) (M: class t f2 g2) (N: class g2 i) (Y: class t i):
  $ wnf f2 ph $ >
  $ wnf g2 ph $ >
  $ wnfc t Y $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) Y) (wcel (cv g2) Y)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co N c1 caddc) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cv t) (cfv N (cseq P U c1))) (cfv N (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) Y)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wceq (cfv (cv t) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (cseq P U c1))) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $;

--| X and Z are two equivalent definitions of the finite product of real functions. Y is a set of real functions from a common domain T, Y is closed under function multiplication and U is a finite sequence of functions in Y. M is the number of functions multiplied together. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmuldfeq {t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t i) (P: class t f2 g2 i) (T U: class) (F: class t i) (M: class t) (X: class t f2 g2 i) (Y: class t i) (Z: class t f2 g2 i):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnfc t Y $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M (cseq P U c1)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt t T (cfv M (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) Y)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) Y) (wcel (cv g2) Y)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wceq (cfv (cv t) X) (cfv (cv t) Z)) $;

--| Given a finite multiplication of values between 0 and 1, a value larger than its first element is larger the whole multiplication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmul01lt1lem1 {i: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2 B2 E: class i) (L M: class):
  $ wnfc i B2 $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cseq cmul B2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv L cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv i) B2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) B2) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) B2) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv L B2) E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M A2) E clt) $;

--| Given a finite multiplication of values between 0 and 1, a value ` E ` larger than any multiplicand, is larger than the whole multiplication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmul01lt1lem2 {i: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2 B2 E: class i) (J L M: class):
  $ wnfc i B2 $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cseq cmul B2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv L cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv i) B2) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) B2) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co L M cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) B2) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co L M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv J B2) E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M A2) E clt) $;

--| Given a finite multiplication of values between 0 and 1, a value E larger than any multiplicand, is larger than the whole multiplication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem fmul01lt1 {i j: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2 B2: class i j) (E M: class):
  $ wnfc i B2 $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnfc j A2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cseq cmul B2 c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) cr B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv i) B2) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) B2) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wbr (cfv (cv j) B2) E clt) (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M A2) E clt) $;

--| A continuous complex function restricted to a subset is continuous, using maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem cncfmptss {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 C (cfv (cv x3) F)) (co C B2 ccncf)) $;

--| The positive reals are a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem rrpsscn:
  $ wss crp cc $;

--| A version of ~ mulc1cncf using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem mulc1cncfg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 (cfv (cv x3) F) cmul)) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| The infimum of a nonempty bounded set of reals is the greatest lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Sep-2020.)
theorem infrglb {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wne A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (cinf A2 cr clt) B2 clt) (wrex z (wbr (cv z) B2 clt) A2)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a complex continuous function and N is a fixed number, then F^N is continuous too. A generalization of ~ expcncf . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem expcnfg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) F) N cexp)) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| Equality deduction for product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem prodeq2ad {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k):
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k A2 B2) (cprod k A2 C)) $;

--| Separate out a term in a finite product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fprodsplit1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) C)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k A2 B2) (co D (cprod k (cdif A2 (csn C)) B2) cmul)) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation of a finite product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fprodexp {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (N: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k A2 (co B2 N cexp)) (co (cprod k A2 B2) N cexp)) $;

--| The absolute value of a finite product . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fprodabs2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cprod k A2 B2) cabs) (cprod k A2 (cfv B2 cabs))) $;

--| A finite product with a zero term is zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fprod0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2 C: class k) (K: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k C $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) K) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cprod k A2 B2) cc0) $;

--| * Induction step for ~ mccl . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem mccllem {k b: setvar} (ph: wff b) (A2: class b) (B2 C: class) (D: class b):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cdif A2 C)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cn0 (cun C (csn D)) cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral b (wcel (co (cfv (csu k C (cfv (cv k) (cv b))) cfa) (cprod k C (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv b)) cfa)) cdiv) cn) (co cn0 C cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cfv (csu k (cun C (csn D)) (cfv (cv k) B2)) cfa) (cprod k (cun C (csn D)) (cfv (cfv (cv k) B2) cfa)) cdiv) cn) $;

--| A multinomial coefficient, in its standard domain, is a positive integer. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem mccl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class k):
  $ wnfc k B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co cn0 A2 cmap)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cfv (csu k A2 (cfv (cv k) B2)) cfa) (cprod k A2 (cfv (cfv (cv k) B2) cfa)) cdiv) cn) $;

--| A finite product of functions to complex numbers from a common topological space is continuous. Induction step. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.) Avoid ~ ax-mulf . (Revised by GG, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem fprodcnlem {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 k) (J K W X: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (cdif A2 Z)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cprod k Z B2)) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cprod k (cun Z (csn W)) B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| A finite product of functions to complex numbers from a common topological space is continuous. The class expression for ` B ` normally contains free variables ` k ` and ` x ` to index it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodcn {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 k) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 k) (J K: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (cprod k A2 B2)) (co J K ccn)) $;

--| A class of sequences of fractions that converge to 1. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem clim1fr1 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (co A2 (cv n) cmul) B2 caddc) (co A2 (cv n) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c1 cli) $;

--| Negation of a converging sum. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem isumneg {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (csu k Z A2) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc F M) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csu k Z (cneg A2)) (cneg (csu k Z A2))) $;

--| Limit of the reciprocal of a converging sequence. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climrec {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 G H: class) (M W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co c1 (cfv (cv k) G) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co c1 A2 cdiv) cli) $;

--| A version of ~ climmul using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climmulf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 F G H M X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wnfc k H $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 cmul) cli) $;

--| The limit of natural powers, is the natural power of the limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climexp {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F H M: class k) (N: class) (V: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k H $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) N cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 N cexp) cli) $;

--| A bounded monotonic nonincreasing sequence converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Sep-2020.)
theorem climinf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cinf (crn F) cr clt) cli) $;

--| The subsequence index ` I ` has the expected properties: it belongs to the same upper integers as the original index, and it is always greater than or equal to the original index. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climsuselem1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (I: class) (K: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv M I) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) I) (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) I) c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K Z)) (wcel (cfv K I) (cfv K cuz)) $;

--| A subsequence ` G ` of a converging sequence ` F ` , converges to the same limit. ` I ` is the strictly increasing and it is used to index the subsequence. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climsuse {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F G I M X Y: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wnfc k I $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv M I) Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) I) (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) I) c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G Y) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) I) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $;

--| A version of ~ climrec using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climrecf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 G H M W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wnfc k H $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co c1 (cfv (cv k) G) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co c1 A2 cdiv) cli) $;

--| Complex limit of the negative of a sequence. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climneg {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (cneg (cfv (cv k) F))) (cneg A2) cli) $;

--| A version of ~ climinf using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Sep-2020.)
theorem climinff {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cinf (crn F) cr clt) cli) $;

--| Limit of the ratio of two converging sequences. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem climdivf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 F G H M X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wnfc k H $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 cdiv) cli) $;

--| If ` F ` is a real function, then ` F ` converges to ` A ` with respect to the standard topology on the reals if and only if it converges to ` A ` with respect to the standard topology on complex numbers. In the theorem, ` R ` is defined to be convergence w.r.t. the standard topology on the reals and then ` F R A ` represents the statement " ` F ` converges to ` A ` , with respect to the standard topology on the reals". Notice that there is no need for the hypothesis that ` A ` is a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem climreeq (ph: wff) (A2 R F M Z: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) clm) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 R) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| An explicit value for the limit, when the limit exists at a limit point. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ellimciota {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv K clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co F B2 climc) c0) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cio x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co F B2 climc))) (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| A version of ~ climadd using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem climaddf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 F G H M X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wnfc k H $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 caddc) cli) $;

--| Limit of the product of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem mullimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (D: class) (F G H: class x3) (X: class) (Y: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co G D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y cmul) (co H D climc)) $;

--| An equivalent condition for being a limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ellimcabssub0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class) (D F G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel C (co F D climc)) (wcel cc0 (co G D climc))) $;

--| If a function has empty domain, every complex number is a limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcdm0 (ph: wff) (B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf c0 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F B2 climc) cc) $;

--| An equivalence condition for a limit point w.r.t. the standard topology on the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem islptre {a b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv J clp))) (wral a (wral b (wi (wcel B2 (co (cv a) (cv b) cioo)) (wne (cin (co (cv a) (cv b) cioo) (cdif A2 (csn B2))) c0)) cxr) cxr)) $;

--| Limit of the composition of two functions. If the limit of ` F ` at ` A ` is ` B ` and the limit of ` G ` at ` B ` is ` C ` , then the limit of ` G o. F ` at ` A ` is ` C ` . With respect to ~ limcco and ~ limccnp , here we drop continuity assumptions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limccog (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wss (crn F) (cdif (cdm G) (csn B2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co G B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (ccom G F) A2 climc)) $;

--| The limit of a function at the lower bound of a closed interval only depends on the values in the inner open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limciccioolb (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) A2 climc) (co F A2 climc)) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . Similar to ~ clim , but without the disjoint var constraint ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem climf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (V: class x3 j k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| Limit of the multiplication of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem mullimcf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D F G: class) (H: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc G) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co G D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co B2 C cmul) (co H D climc)) $;

--| Limit of constant function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem constlimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co F C climc)) $;

--| Inference removing two restricted quantifiers. Same as ~ rexlimdvv , but with bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable restrictions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem rexlim2d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2)) (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wrex x3 (wrex y1 ps B2) A2) ch) $;

--| Limit of the identity function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem idlimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co F X climc)) $;

--| The sequence of reciprocals of positive integers, multiplied by the factor ` A ` , converges to zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem divcnvg {n: setvar} (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel M cn)) (wbr (cmpt n (cfv M cuz) (co A2 (cv n) cdiv)) cc0 cli) $;

--| If ` F ` is a periodic function with period ` T ` , the limit doesn't change if we shift the limiting point by ` T ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcperiod {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (C D T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm F) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) T caddc)) A2) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) A2)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cres F A2) D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cres F B2) (co D T caddc) climc)) $;

--| If ` F ` is a real-valued function, ` B ` is a limit point of its domain, and the limit of ` F ` at ` B ` exists, then this limit is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcrecl (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F L: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv A2 (cfv (cfv ccnfld ctopn) clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $;

--| A series indexed by ` NN ` with only odd terms. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sumnnodd {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class k) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cc F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv k) cn) (wcel (co (cv k) c2 cdiv) cn)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc F c1) B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cseq caddc (cmpt k cn (cfv (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) F)) c1) B2 cli) (wceq (csu k cn (cfv (cv k) F)) (csu k cn (cfv (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) F)))) $;

--| The upper bound of an open interval is a limit point of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lptioo2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv J clp))) $;

--| The lower bound of an open interval is a limit point of the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lptioo1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv J clp))) $;

--| The domain of a maps-to function with a limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcmptdm {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` , with more general quantifier restrictions than ~ clim . Similar to ~ clim2 , but without the disjoint var constraint ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem clim2f {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| The limit of a function at the upper bound of a closed interval only depends on the values in the inner open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcicciooub (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) B2 climc) (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a "less than" relationship for the ` mod ` operator. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ltmod (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (co A2 (co A2 B2 cmo) cmin) A2 cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C B2 cmo) (co A2 B2 cmo) clt) $;

--| A characterization for a limit point for the standard topology on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem islpcn {x3 e: setvar} (ph: wff) (P S: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel P (cfv S (cfv (cfv ccnfld ctopn) clp))) (wral e (wrex x3 (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) P cmin) cabs) (cv e) clt) (cdif S (csn P))) crp)) $;

--| If a set in the real line has a limit point than it contains two distinct points that are closer than a given distance. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lptre2pt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 E J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 (cfv J clp)) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) E clt)) A2) A2) $;

--| If a sequence is bounded, then the limsup is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem limsupre {j k b: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class b) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup B2 cxr clt) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv j) F) cabs) (cv b) cle)) B2) cr) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $;

--| The left limit doesn't change if the function is restricted to a smaller open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcresiooub (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co B2 C cioo) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cres F (co B2 C cioo)) C climc) (co (cres F (co D C cioo)) C climc)) $;

--| The right limit doesn't change if the function is restricted to a smaller open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcresioolb (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co B2 C cioo) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cres F (co B2 C cioo)) B2 climc) (co (cres F (co B2 D cioo)) B2 climc)) $;

--| If the left and the right limits are equal, the limit of the function exits and the three limits coincide. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limcleqr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F J K L: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co (cres F (co cmnf B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cres F (co B2 cpnf cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| The upper bound of an open interval is a limit point of the interval, wirth respect to the standard topology on complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lptioo2cn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv J clp))) $;

--| The lower bound of an open interval is a limit point of the interval, wirth respect to the standard topology on complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem lptioo1cn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 J: class):
  $ wceq J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv J clp))) $;

--| Limit of the negative function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem neglimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D F G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cneg C) (co G D climc)) $;

--| Sum of two limits. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem addlimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D E F G H I: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co G D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co E I caddc) (co H D climc)) $;

--| If the numerator converges to 0 and the denominator converges to a nonzero number, then the fraction converges to 0. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _0ellimcdiv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D E F G H: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co F E climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co G E climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel cc0 (co H E climc)) $;

--| Express the predicate ` F ` converges to ` A ` . Similar to ~ clim2 , but without the disjoint var constraint ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem clim2cf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| For a limit point, both from the left and from the right, of the domain, the limit of the function exits only if the left and the right limits are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limclner (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F J K L: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co cmnf B2 cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co B2 cpnf cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co (cres F (co cmnf B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cres F (co B2 cpnf cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne L R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co F B2 climc) c0) $;

--| Subtraction of two limits. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sublimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C D E F G H I: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co G D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co E I cmin) (co H D climc)) $;

--| Limit of the reciprocal of a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem reclimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (F G: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (co c1 B2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 C cdiv) (co G D climc)) $;

--| Express the predicate ` F ` converges to ` 0 ` . Similar to ~ clim , but without the disjoint var constraint ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem clim0cf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cc0 cli) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| For a limit point, both from the left and from the right, of the domain, the limit of the function exits only if the left and the right limits are equal. In this case, the three limits coincide. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem limclr (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F J K L: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co cmnf B2 cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co B2 cpnf cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co (cres F (co cmnf B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cres F (co B2 cpnf cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wb (wne (co F B2 climc) c0) (wceq L R)) (wi (wceq L R) (wcel L (co F B2 climc)))) $;

--| Limit of the quotient of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem divlimc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2 C: class x3) (D: class) (F G H: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 B2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 C cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel C (cdif cc (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co F D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co G D climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne Y cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X Y cdiv) (co H D climc)) $;

--| Factorial grows faster than exponential. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem expfac {n: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co A2 (cv n) cexp) (cfv (cv n) cfa) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr F cc0 cli) $;

--| A constant sequence converges to its value. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem climconstmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt x3 Z A2) A2 cli) $;

--| A function restricted to upper integers converges iff the original function converges. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem climresmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 F G M: class x3) (N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (cfv N cuz) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr G B2 cli) (wbr F B2 cli)) $;

--| Limit of the difference of two converging sequences. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem climsubmpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C D M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z A2) C cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z B2) D cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co A2 B2 cmin)) (co C D cmin) cli) $;

--| Limit of the difference of two converging sequences. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem climsubc2mpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (B2: class) (C M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z A2) C cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co A2 B2 cmin)) (co C B2 cmin) cli) $;

--| Limit of the difference of two converging sequences. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem climsubc1mpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2 C M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z B2) C cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (co A2 B2 cmin)) (co A2 C cmin) cli) $;

--| The value of the limit function ` G ` at any point of its domain ` D ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimfv {x3 m: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m) (D F G: class x3 m) (X: class x3) (Z: class m):
  $ wnfc x3 D $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X G) (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $;

--| The limit of a convergent real sequence is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climreclf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal, either both are convergent or both are divergent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climeldmeq {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wcel G (cdm cli))) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . Similar to ~ clim , but without the disjoint var constraint ` ph k ` and ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climf2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (V: class x3 j k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| The sequence of function values converges to the value of the limit function ` G ` at any point of its domain ` D ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimcnv {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m n) (D F G: class x3 m n) (X: class x3 n) (Z: class m n):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq D (crab x3 (wcel (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) (cdm cli)) (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt m Z (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F))) (cfv X G) cli) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal, either both are convergent or both are divergent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climeldmeqmpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class) (D R S M: class k) (V W Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) C)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt k A2 B2) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cmpt k C D) (cdm cli))) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climfveq {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F cli) (cfv G cli)) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` , with more general quantifier restrictions than ~ clim . Similar to ~ clim2 , but without the disjoint var constraint ` F k ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem clim2f2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climfveqmpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class) (D R S M: class k) (V W Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) C)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k A2 B2) cli) (cfv (cmpt k C D) cli)) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climd {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class j k) (F M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) X clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is eventually approximated. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem clim2d {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class j k) (F M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) X clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The limit function of real functions, applied to elements in its domain, evaluates to Real values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimfvre {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m) (D: class x3 m n) (F: class x3 m) (M: class x3 m n) (X Z: class):
  $ wnf m ph $ >
  $ wnfc m F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) Z)) (wf (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)) cr (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab x3 (wcel (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) (cdm cli)) (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F))) cli) cr) $;

--| Given a sequence of real-valued functions, and ` X ` that belongs to all but finitely many domains, then its function value is ultimately a real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem allbutfifvre {m n: setvar} (ph: wff m) (D F M: class m n) (X: class) (Z: class n):
  $ wnf m ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) Z)) (wf (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)) cr (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wceq D (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral m (wcel (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F)) cr) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is eventually approximated. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem climleltrp {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (C: class j k) (F: class k) (M: class j k) (N X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv N cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co C X caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The limit function of real functions, applied to elements in its domain, evaluates to Real values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimfvre2 {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m) (D: class x3 m n) (F: class x3 m) (G M: class x3 m n) (X Z: class):
  $ wnf m ph $ >
  $ wnfc m F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) Z)) (wf (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)) cr (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab x3 (wcel (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) (cdm cli)) (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X G) cr) $;

--| The limit function of real functions, is a real-valued function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimf {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m) (D: class x3) (F: class x3 m) (G M: class x3 m n) (Z: class):
  $ wnf m ph $ >
  $ wnfc m F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) Z)) (wf (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)) cr (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab x3 (wcel (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) (cdm cli)) (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr G) $;

--| A sequence of function values, approximates the corresponding limit function value, all but finitely many times. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem fnlimabslt {x3 m n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m) (D: class x3 m n) (F G M: class x3 m) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wnf m ph $ >
  $ wnfc m F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv m) Z)) (wf (cdm (cfv (cv m) F)) cr (cfv (cv m) F)) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab x3 (wcel (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) (cdm cli)) (ciun n Z (ciin m (cfv (cv n) cuz) (cdm (cfv (cv m) F))))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (cmpt m Z (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) F))) cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral m (wa (wcel (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F)) cr) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv X (cfv (cv m) F)) (cfv X G) cmin) cabs) Y clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climfveqf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F G M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F cli) (cfv G cli)) $;

--| Exhibit a function ` G ` with the same convergence properties as the not-quite-function ` F ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climmptf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F G M V: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr G A2 cli)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. TODO: this is more general than ~ climfveqmpt and should replace it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climfveqmpt3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class) (D: class k) (U: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k A2 B2) cli) (cfv (cmpt k C D) cli)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal, either both are convergent or both are divergent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climeldmeqf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F G M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wcel G (cdm cli))) $;

--| The limit of B convergent real sequence is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climreclmpt {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z A2) B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $;

--| If a sequence is bounded, then the limsup is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupref {j k b: setvar} (ph: wff j k b) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv j) F) cabs) (cv b) cle)) A2) cr) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $;

--| If a sequence is eventually at most ` A ` , then the limsup is also at most ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupbnd1f {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (A2 B2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) A2 cle)) B2) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsp) A2 cle) $;

--| A converging sequence of complex numbers is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climbddf {x3 k: setvar} (F: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wral k (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) Z)) (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) Z) cr) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climeqf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 F G M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr G A2 cli)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal, either both are convergent or both are divergent. TODO: this is more general than ~ climeldmeqmpt and should replace it. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climeldmeqmpt3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (C: class) (D: class k) (U: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt k A2 B2) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cmpt k C D) (cdm cli))) $;

--| Closure of the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupcld (ph: wff) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cxr) $;

--| The limit of a convergent sequence, expressed as the function value of the convergence relation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climfv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wbr F A2 cli) (wceq A2 (cfv F cli)) $;

--| The superior limit of an infinite sequence ` F ` of extended real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupval3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (csup (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cinf (crn G) cxr clt)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climfveqmpt2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class) (C U M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k A2 C) cli) (cfv (cmpt k B2 C) cli)) $;

--| The superior limit of the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsup0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 clsp) cmnf $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal, either both are convergent or both are divergent. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climeldmeqmpt2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class) (C U M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cmpt k A2 C) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cmpt k B2 C) (cdm cli))) $;

--| The supremum limit of a function only depends on the real part of its domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupresre (ph: wff) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F cr) clsp) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climeqmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C D U M V W: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Z B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel C U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cmpt x3 A2 C) D cli) (wbr (cmpt x3 B2 C) D cli)) $;

--| The limit of a convergent sequence, expressed as the function value of the convergence relation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climfvd (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cfv F cli)) $;

--| An upper bound for the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsuplesup (ph: wff) (F K V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsp) (csup (cin (cima F (co K cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The superior limit doesn't change when a function is restricted to the upper part of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupresico (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co M cpnf cico) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsp) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| If the restriction of a function to every upper interval is unbounded above, its ` limsup ` is ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsuppnfdlem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (G: class x3 j k):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (csup (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $;

--| If the restriction of a function to every upper interval is unbounded above, its ` limsup ` is ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsuppnfd {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $;

--| If the real part of the domain of a function is a subset of the integers, the superior limit doesn't change when the function is restricted to an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupresuz (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm (cres F cr)) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsp) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| If the limsup is not ` +oo ` , then the function is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupub {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle)) A2) cr) cr) $;

--| The superior limit of a restriction is less than or equal to the original superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupres (ph: wff) (C F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cres F C) clsp) (cfv F clsp) cle) $;

--| A convergent, nonincreasing sequence, converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climinf2lem {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cinf (crn F) cxr clt) cli) $;

--| A convergent, nonincreasing sequence, converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climinf2 {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cinf (crn F) cxr clt) cli) $;

--| The superior limit, when the domain of the function is a set of upper integers (the first condition is needed, otherwise the l.h.s. would be ` -oo ` and the r.h.s. would be ` +oo ` ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupvaluz {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cinf (crn (cmpt k Z (csup (crn (cres F (cfv (cv k) cuz))) cxr clt))) cxr clt)) $;

--| If the domain of a function is a subset of the integers, the superior limit doesn't change when the function is restricted to an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupresuz2 (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm F) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsp) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| If the restriction of a function to every upper interval is unbounded above, its ` limsup ` is ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsuppnflem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cpnf) (wral x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr)) $;

--| If the restriction of a function to every upper interval is unbounded above, its ` limsup ` is ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsuppnf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cpnf) (wral x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr)) $;

--| If the limsup is not ` +oo ` , then the function is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupubuzlem {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j) (F: class j) (K: class x3 j) (M N: class x3) (W: class x3 j) (X: class j) (Y: class x3 j) (Z: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j X $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral j (wi (wbr K (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) Y cle)) Z) $ >
  $ wceq N (cif (wbr (cfv K cceil) M cle) M (cfv K cceil)) $ >
  $ wceq W (csup (crn (cmpt j (co M N cfz) (cfv (cv j) F))) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq X (cif (wbr W Y cle) Y W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) Z) cr) $;

--| For a real-valued function on a set of upper integers, if the superior limit is not ` +oo ` , then the function is bounded above. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupubuz {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j) (F M: class j) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) Z) cr) $;

--| A bounded below, monotonic nonincreasing sequence converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climinf2mpt {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (B2: class k) (C: class j) (M: class j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv j)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv k) Z) (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) c1 caddc))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt k Z B2) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z B2) (cinf (crn (cmpt k Z B2)) cxr clt) cli) $;

--| A bounded below, monotonic nonincreasing sequence converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climinfmpt {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (B2: class k) (C: class x3 j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) (cv j)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv k) Z) (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) c1 caddc))) (wbr C B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral k (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z B2) (cinf (crn (cmpt k Z B2)) cxr clt) cli) $;

--| A convergent, nonincreasing sequence, converges to the infimum of its range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem climinf3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (cinf (crn F) cxr clt) cli) $;

--| The superior limit, when the domain of the function is a set of upper integers (the first condition is needed, otherwise the l.h.s. would be ` -oo ` and the r.h.s. would be ` +oo ` ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupvaluzmpt {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (B2: class j) (M: class j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j Z B2) clsp) (cinf (crn (cmpt k Z (csup (crn (cmpt j (cfv (cv k) cuz) B2)) cxr clt))) cxr clt)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequzmpt2 {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2 B2 C: class j) (K: class) (M N V: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j A2 $ >
  $ wnfc j B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (cfv K cuz))) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j A2 C) clsp) (cfv (cmpt j B2 C) clsp)) $;

--| If the limsup is not ` +oo ` , then the function is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupubuzmpt {x3 j: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j) (B2: class j) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv (cmpt j Z B2) clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) Z) cr) $;

--| The superior limit of a function is ` -oo ` if and only if every real number is the upper bound of the restriction of the function to an upper interval of real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupmnflem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 k) (F: class) (G: class x3 j k):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (csup (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle)) A2) cr) cr)) $;

--| The superior limit of a function is ` -oo ` if and only if every real number is the upper bound of the restriction of the function to an upper interval of real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupmnf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle)) A2) cr) cr)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequzlem {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F G K M N: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G (cfv N cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv K cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cfv G clsp)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequz {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F G: class k) (K: class) (M N: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wnfc k G $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn G (cfv N cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv K cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cfv G clsp)) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is smaller than the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually larger than the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre2lem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) clt)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) clt)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is smaller than the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually larger than the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) clt)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) clt)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| The superior limit of a function is ` -oo ` if and only if every real number is the upper bound of the restriction of the function to a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupmnfuzlem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| The superior limit of a function is ` -oo ` if and only if every real number is the upper bound of the restriction of the function to a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupmnfuz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequzmptlem {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2 B2: class) (C: class j) (K M N: class) (V W: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) B2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wceq K (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j A2 C) clsp) (cfv (cmpt j B2 C) clsp)) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequzmpt {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2 B2: class) (C: class j) (M N: class) (V W: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) B2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j A2 C) clsp) (cfv (cmpt j B2 C) clsp)) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is smaller than the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually larger than the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre2mpt {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 k) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsp) cr) (wa (wrex y1 (wral k (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv y1) B2 clt)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex y1 (wrex k (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv x3) cle) (wbr B2 (cv y1) clt)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupequzmptf {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2 B2 C M N: class j) (V W: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j A2 $ >
  $ wnfc j B2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) A2)) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) B2)) (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j A2 C) clsp) (cfv (cmpt j B2 C) clsp)) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre3lem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre3 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, at some point, in any upper part of the reals; 2. there is a real number that is eventually greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre3mpt {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 k) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsp) cr) (wa (wrex y1 (wral k (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv k) (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv y1) B2 cle)) A2) cr) cr) (wrex y1 (wrex k (wral x3 (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv x3) cle) (wbr B2 (cv y1) cle)) A2) cr) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is eventually greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre3uzlem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class j) (M: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the extended reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is eventually greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupre3uz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the reals, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupreuz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) Z) cr))) $;

--| The superior limit, when the domain of a real-valued function is a set of upper integers, and the superior limit is real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupvaluz2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cinf (crn (cmpt k Z (csup (crn (cres F (cfv (cv k) cuz))) cxr clt))) cr clt)) $;

--| Given a function on the reals, defined on a set of upper integers, its supremum limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is less than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is greater than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem limsupreuzmpt {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (B2: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv (cmpt j Z B2) clsp) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) Z) cr))) $;

--| If a function on a set of upper integers has a real superior limit, the supremum of the rightmost parts of the function, converges to that superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supcnvlimsup {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (csup (crn (cres F (cfv (cv k) cuz))) cxr clt)) (cfv F clsp) cli) $;

--| If a function on a set of upper integers has a real superior limit, the supremum of the rightmost parts of the function, converges to that superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Oct-2021.)
theorem supcnvlimsupmpt {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (B2: class j) (M: class j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv (cmpt j Z B2) clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k Z (csup (crn (cmpt j (cfv (cv k) cuz) B2)) cxr clt)) (cfv (cmpt j Z B2) clsp) cli) $;

--| If ` (/) ` is a complex number, then it converges to itself. See ~ 0ncn and its comment; see also the comment in ~ climlimsupcex . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem _0cnv:
  $ wi (wcel c0 cc) (wbr c0 c0 cli) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climuzlem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climuz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class) (F: class k) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem lmbr3v {u j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (P F J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cv u))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz)) J))) $;

--| If a sequence converges to an isolated point (w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers) then the sequence eventually becomes that point. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climisp {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv k) Z) (wne (cfv (cv k) F) A2)) (wbr X (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) A2 cmin) cabs) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " in a metric space using an arbitrary upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem lmbr3 {u j k: setvar} (ph: wff u j k) (P: class j k) (F: class k) (J: class j k) (X: class u j k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (cfv X ctopon)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P (cfv J clm)) (w3a (wcel F (co X cc cpm)) (wcel P X) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cv u))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz)) J))) $;

--| A sequence converging w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers, eventually becomes a sequence of complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climrescn {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (F: class) (M: class j) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) cc (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $;

--| If a sequence ranging over the extended reals converges w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers, then there exists an upper set of the integers over which the function is real-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climxrrelem {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 D F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel cpnf cc)) (wbr D (cfv (co cpnf A2 cmin) cabs) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel cmnf cc)) (wbr D (cfv (co cmnf A2 cmin) cabs) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) cr (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $;

--| If a sequence ranging over the extended reals converges w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers, then there exists an upper set of the integers over which the function is real-valued (the weaker hypothesis ` F e. dom ~~> ` is probably not enough, since in principle we could have ` +oo e. CC ` and ` -oo e. CC ` ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climxrre {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) cr (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the liminf function. (actually, it makes sense for any extended real function defined on a subset of RR which is not upper-bounded)
term clsi: class;

--| Define the inferior limit of a sequence of extended real numbers. (Contributed by GS, 2-Jan-2022.)
axiom df_liminf {x3 k: setvar}:
  $ wceq clsi (cmpt x3 cvv (csup (crn (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima (cv x3) (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt))) cxr clt)) $;

--| The defining property of the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsuplt2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F clsp) A2 clt) (wrex k (wbr (csup (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt) A2 clt) cr)) $;

--| Ordering property of the inferior limit function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfgord (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cinf (cin (cima F (co A2 cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt) (cinf (cin (cima F (co B2 cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt) cle) $;

--| The superior limit of a sequence ` F ` of extended real numbers is the infimum of the set of suprema of all restrictions of ` F ` to an upperset of reals . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupvald {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (csup (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) (cinf (crn G) cxr clt)) $;

--| The superior limit doesn't change when a function is restricted to the upper part of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupresicompt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 M V: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co M cpnf cico) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsp) (cfv (cmpt x3 (cin A2 Z) B2) clsp)) $;

--| Closure of the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupcli (F V: class):
  $ wcel F V $ >
  $ wcel (cfv F clsp) cxr $;

--| Closure of the inferior limit function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfgf {k: setvar} (F G: class k):
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wf cr cxr G $;

--| The inferior limit of a set ` F ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfval {k: setvar} (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (csup (crn G) cxr clt)) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if it converges to its superior limit. The first hypothesis is needed (see ~ climlimsupcex for a counterexample). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climlimsup (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wbr F (cfv F clsp) cli)) $;

--| The defining property of the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupge {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf B2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 (cfv F clsp) cle) (wral k (wbr A2 (csup (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt) cle) cr)) $;

--| Value of the inferior limit function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfgval {k: setvar} (F: class) (G: class k) (M: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cr) (wceq (cfv M G) (cinf (cin (cima F (co M cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $;

--| Closure of the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfcl (F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wcel (cfv F clsi) cxr) $;

--| The inferior limit of a set ` F ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvald {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (csup (crn G) cxr clt)) $;

--| The inferior limit of an infinite sequence ` F ` of extended real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfval5 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (csup (crn G) cxr clt)) $;

--| The superior limit of a function only depends on the restriction of that function to the preimage of the set of extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupresxr (ph: wff) (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cima (ccnv F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F A2) clsp) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| The inferior limit of a function only depends on the preimage of the extended real part. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresxr (ph: wff) (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun F) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cima (ccnv F) cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F A2) clsi) (cfv F clsi)) $;

--| The superior limit, relativized to an unbounded set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfval2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (F: class) (G V: class k):
  $ wceq G (cmpt k cr (cinf (cin (cima F (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cxr) cxr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (csup (cima G A2) cxr clt)) $;

--| Counterexample for ~ climlimsup , showing that the first hypothesis is needed, if the empty set is a complex number (see ~ 0ncn and its comment). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climlimsupcex (F M Z: class):
  $ wn (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq F c0 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c0 cc) (wn (wcel cmnf cc))) (w3a (wf Z cr F) (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wn (wbr F (cfv F clsp) cli))) $;

--| Closure of the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfcld (ph: wff) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsi) cxr) $;

--| The inferior limit doesn't change when a function is restricted to an upperset of reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresico (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co M cpnf cico) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsi) (cfv F clsi)) $;

--| The range of the given function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsup10exlem {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class n) (K: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cima F (co K cpnf cico)) (cpr cc0 c1)) $;

--| The superior limit of a function that alternates between two values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsup10ex {n: setvar} (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv F clsp) c1 $;

--| The inferior limit of a function that alternates between two values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminf10ex {n: setvar} (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv F clsi) cc0 $;

--| The superior limit is greater than or equal to the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflelimsuplem {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class) (V: class j k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wrex j (wne (cin (cima F (co (cv j) cpnf cico)) cxr) c0) (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp) cle) $;

--| The superior limit is greater than or equal to the inferior limit. The second hypothesis is needed (see ~ liminflelimsupcex for a counterexample). The inequality can be strict, see ~ liminfltlimsupex . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflelimsup {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class) (V: class j k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wrex j (wne (cin (cima F (co (cv j) cpnf cico)) cxr) c0) (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp) cle) $;

--| For any positive real, the superior limit of F is larger than any of its values at large enough arguments, up to that positive real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupgtlem {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class j k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) X cmin) (cfv F clsp) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Given a sequence of real numbers, there exists an upper part of the sequence that's appxoximated from below by the superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupgt {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class k) (M: class j k) (X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co (cfv (cv k) F) X cmin) (cfv F clsp) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The inferior limit of a function only depends on the real part of its domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresre (ph: wff) (F V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F cr) clsi) (cfv F clsi)) $;

--| The inferior limit doesn't change when a function is restricted to the upper part of the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresicompt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 M V: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co M cpnf cico) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 (cin A2 Z) B2) clsi) (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsi)) $;

--| An example where the ` liminf ` is strictly smaller than the ` limsup ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfltlimsupex {n: setvar} (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (cif (wbr c2 (cv n) cdvds) cc0 c1)) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp) clt $;

--| The inferior limit is greater than or equal to the superior limit if and only if they are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfgelimsup {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class) (V: class j k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wrex j (wne (cin (cima F (co (cv j) cpnf cico)) cxr) c0) (co (cv k) cpnf cico)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` when ` F ` is an extended real-valued function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvalxr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (F V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 (cxne (cfv (cv x3) F))) clsp))) $;

--| If the real part of the domain of a function is a subset of the integers, the inferior limit doesn't change when the function is restricted to an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresuz (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm (cres F cr)) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsi) (cfv F clsi)) $;

--| The superior limit is greater than or equal to the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflelimsupuz (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp) cle) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` when ` F ` is an extended real-valued function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvalxrmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2 V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 (cxne B2)) clsp))) $;

--| If the domain of a function is a subset of the integers, the inferior limit doesn't change when the function is restricted to an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfresuz2 (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm F) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cres F Z) clsi) (cfv F clsi)) $;

--| The inferior limit is greater than or equal to the superior limit if and only if they are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfgelimsupuz (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` when the given function is eventually real-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfval4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (M: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin A2 (co M cpnf cico)))) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2)) clsp))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` when the given function is eventually extended real-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfval3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (M: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin A2 (co M cpnf cico)))) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 (cxne B2)) clsp))) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfequzmpt2 {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2 B2 C: class j) (K: class) (M N V: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j A2 $ >
  $ wnfc j B2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (cfv K cuz))) (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt j A2 C) clsi) (cfv (cmpt j B2 C) clsi)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for an extended real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvaluz {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (B2: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k Z B2) clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cxne B2)) clsp))) $;

--| The inferior limit of the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminf0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 clsi) cpnf $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` when the given a function is eventually extended real-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupval4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (M: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cin A2 (co M cpnf cico)))) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 B2) clsp) (cxne (cfv (cmpt x3 A2 (cxne B2)) clsi))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for a real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvaluz2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (B2: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k Z B2) clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cneg B2)) clsp))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for an extended real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvaluz3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cxne (cfv (cv k) F))) clsp))) $;

--| A counterexample for ~ liminflelimsup , showing that the second hypothesis is needed. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflelimsupcex:
  $ wbr (cfv c0 clsp) (cfv c0 clsi) clt $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for an extended real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupvaluz3 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (B2: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k Z B2) clsp) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cxne B2)) clsi))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for a real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfvaluz4 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (F: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cneg (cfv (cv k) F))) clsp))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` liminf ` for a real-valued function, defined on a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem limsupvaluz4 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (B2: class k) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cmpt k Z B2) clsp) (cxne (cfv (cmpt k Z (cneg B2)) clsi))) $;

--| If a sequence of real numbers converges, its inferior limit and its superior limit are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminflimsupd (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp)) $;

--| Given a function on the reals, its inferior limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is greater than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is smaller than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfreuzlem {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class j) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) Z) cr))) $;

--| Given a function on the reals, its inferior limit is real if and only if two condition holds: 1. there is a real number that is greater than or equal to the function, infinitely often; 2. there is a real number that is smaller than or equal to the function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfreuz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) (wa (wrex x3 (wral k (wrex j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wrex x3 (wral j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) Z) cr))) $;

--| Given a sequence of real numbers, there exists an upper part of the sequence that's approximated from above by the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminfltlem {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class j) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv F clsi) (co (cfv (cv k) F) X caddc) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Given a sequence of real numbers, there exists an upper part of the sequence that's approximated from above by the inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflt {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class k) (M: class j k) (X: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv F clsi) (co (cfv (cv k) F) X caddc) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if it converges to its inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminf (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wbr F (cfv F clsi) cli)) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if its inferior limit is real, and it is greater than or equal to the superior limit (in such a case, they are actually equal, see ~ liminflelimsupuz ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem liminflimsupclim (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if its inferior limit is real and it is greater than or equal to its superior limit (in such a case, they are actually equal, see ~ liminfgelimsupuz ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminflimsup (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wa (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle))) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if its superior limit is real and it is less than or equal to its inferior limit (in such a case, they are actually equal, see ~ liminfgelimsupuz ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminflimsup2 (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wa (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle))) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if its inferior limit is real and equal to its superior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminflimsup3 (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wa (wcel (cfv F clsi) cr) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp)))) $;

--| A sequence of real numbers converges if and only if its superior limit is real and equal to its inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Jan-2022.)
theorem climliminflimsup4 (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wa (wcel (cfv F clsp) cr) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp)))) $;

--| A extended real valued function, with limsup that is not ` +oo ` , is eventually less than ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem limsupub2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2: class) (F: class j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral j (wi (wbr (cv k) (cv j) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) cpnf clt)) A2) cr) $;

--| A sequence with values in the extended reals, and with limsup that is not ` +oo ` , is eventually less than ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem limsupubuz2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (F: class j) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cfv (cv j) F) cpnf clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A sequence converges to ` +oo ` if and only if its negation converges to ` -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimpnfxnegmnf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class j):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cxne (cfv (cv j) F)) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A sequence with values in the extended reals, and with liminf that is not ` -oo ` , is eventually greater than ` -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem liminflbuz2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (F: class j) (M Z: class):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsi) cmnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wral j (wbr cmnf (cfv (cv j) F) clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) $;

--| The inferior limit of a function is ` +oo ` if and only if every real number is the lower bound of the restriction of the function to a set of upper integers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem liminfpnfuz {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class j) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv F clsi) cpnf) (wral x3 (wrex k (wral j (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv j) F) cle) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A sequence with values in the extended reals, and with real liminf and limsup, is eventually real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem liminflimsupxrre {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsp) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv F clsi) cmnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wf (cfv (cv k) cuz) cr (cres F (cfv (cv k) cuz))) Z) $;

--| Extend class notation with convergence relation for limits in the extended real numbers.
term clsxlim: class;

--| Define the convergence relation for extended real sequences. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
axiom df_xlim:
  $ wceq clsxlim (cfv (cfv cle cordt) clm) $;

--| The limit on extended reals is a relation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimrel:
  $ wrel clsxlim $;

--| A function converges iff its restriction to an upper integers set converges. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimres (ph: wff) (A2 F M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cxr cc cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (wbr (cres F (cfv M cuz)) A2 clsxlim)) $;

--| The limit of a sequence of extended real numbers is an extended real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimcl (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| Restricted existential elimination rule of natural deduction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem rexlimddv2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Given a sequence of reals, it converges to a real number ` A ` w.r.t. the standard topology on the reals, if and only if it converges to ` A ` w.r.t. to the standard topology on the extended reals (see ~ climreeq ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimclim (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| A constant sequence converges to its value, w.r.t. the standard topology on the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimconst {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (F M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn F Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $;

--| A converging sequence in the reals is a converging sequence in the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climxlim (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $;

--| Express the binary relation "sequence ` F ` converges to point ` P ` " w.r.t. the standard topology on the extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimbr {u j k: setvar} (ph: wff u j k) (P: class j k) (F: class k) (J: class j k) (M: class k) (Z: class u):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv cle cordt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F P clsxlim) (wa (wcel P cxr) (wral u (wi (wcel P (cv u)) (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cv k) (cdm F)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) (cv u))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z)) J))) $;

--| A function mapping from an upper set of integers to the extended reals is a partial map on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem fuzxrpmcn (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cxr cc cpm)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cnrefiisp (some local definitions are used). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem cnrefiisplem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C: class y1) (D: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun cr B2) $ >
  $ wceq D (cun (csn (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs)) (ciun y1 (cdif (cin B2 cc) (csn A2)) (csn (cfv (co (cv y1) A2 cmin) cabs)))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cinf D cxr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cc) (wne (cv y1) A2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (co (cv y1) A2 cmin) cabs) cle)) C) crp) $;

--| A non-real, complex number is an isolated point w.r.t. the union of the reals with any finite set (the extended reals is an example of such a union). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem cnrefiisp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel A2 cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wceq C (cun cr B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cc) (wne (cv y1) A2)) (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (co (cv y1) A2 cmin) cabs) cle)) C) crp) $;

--| If a sequence ranging over the extended reals converges w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers, then there exists an upper set of the integers over which the function is real-valued (the weaker hypothesis ` F e. dom ~~> ` is probably not enough, since in principle we could have ` +oo e. CC ` and ` -oo e. CC ` ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimxrre {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) cr (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xlimmnfv : the "only if" part of the biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimmnfvlem1 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) X cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xlimmnf : the "if" part of the biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimmnfvlem2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) $;

--| A function converges to minus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) smaller than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimmnfv {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A sequence that eventually becomes constant, converges to its constant value (w.r.t. the standard topology on the extended reals). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimconst2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (F M: class k) (N: class) (Z: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv N cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xlimpnfv : the "only if" part of the biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimpnfvlem1 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class k) (X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr X (cfv (cv k) F) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xlimpnfv : the "if" part of the biconditional. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimpnfvlem2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) $;

--| A function converges to plus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) larger than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimpnfv {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ xlimclim2 . Here it is additionally assumed that the sequence will eventually become (and stay) real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimclim2lem {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (M Z: class j):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wf (cfv (cv j) cuz) cr (cres F (cfv (cv j) cuz))) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| Given a sequence of extended reals, it converges to a real number ` A ` w.r.t. the standard topology on the reals (see ~ climreeq ), if and only if it converges to ` A ` w.r.t. to the standard topology on the extended reals. In order for the first part of the statement to even make sense, the sequence will of course eventually become (and stay) real: showing this, is the key step of the proof. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimclim2 (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| A function converges to minus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) smaller than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimmnf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A function converges to plus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) larger than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimpnf {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A function converges to plus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) larger than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimmnfmpt {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (B2: class k) (F M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt k Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr B2 (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| A function converges to plus infinity if it eventually becomes (and stays) larger than any given real number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem xlimpnfmpt {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (B2: class k) (F M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt k Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) $;

--| In this lemma for ~ climxlim2 there is the additional assumption that the converging function is complex-valued on the whole domain. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climxlim2lem (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals, converging w.r.t. the standard topology on the complex numbers is a converging sequence w.r.t. the standard topology on the extended reals. This is non-trivial, because ` +oo ` and ` -oo ` could, in principle, be complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem climxlim2 (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $;

--| An alternative definition for the convergence relation in the extended real numbers. This resembles what's found in most textbooks: three distinct definitions for the same symbol (limit of a sequence). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem dfxlim2v {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (F: class) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (w3o (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wceq A2 cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) (wa (wceq A2 cpnf) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)))) $;

--| An alternative definition for the convergence relation in the extended real numbers. This resembles what's found in most textbooks: three distinct definitions for the same symbol (limit of a sequence). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Feb-2022.)
theorem dfxlim2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j k) (A2: class x3 j k) (F: class k) (M: class x3 j k) (Z: class k):
  $ wnfc k F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 clsxlim) (w3o (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wceq A2 cmnf) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)) (wa (wceq A2 cpnf) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr)))) $;

--| A function restricted to upper integers converges iff the original function converges. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem climresd (ph: wff) (A2 F M V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cres F (cfv M cuz)) A2 cli) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| A real function converges iff its restriction to an upper integers set converges. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem climresdm (ph: wff) (F M V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wcel (cres F (cfv M cuz)) (cdm cli))) $;

--| A real valued sequence that converges w.r.t. the topology on the complex numbers, converges w.r.t. the topology on the extended reals (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem dmclimxlim (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm clsxlim)) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals converges to ` -oo ` if and only if its superior limit is also ` -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimmnflimsup2 (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf)) $;

--| An infinite sequence converges to at most one limit (w.r.t. to the standard topology on the extended reals). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimuni (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F B2 clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals that converges to a real w.r.t. the standard topology on the extended reals, also converges w.r.t. to the standard topology on the complex numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimclimdm (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $;

--| The convergence relation on the extended reals is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimfun:
  $ wfun clsxlim $;

--| If a sequence of extended reals converges to ` -oo ` then its superior limit is also ` -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimmnflimsup (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cmnf clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsp) cmnf) $;

--| Two ways to express that a function has a limit. (The expression ` ( ~~>* `` F ) ` is sometimes useful as a shorthand for "the unique limit of the function ` F ` "). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimdm (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cdm clsxlim)) (wbr F (cfv F clsxlim) clsxlim) $;

--| A sequence converges to ` +oo ` if and only if its negation converges to ` -oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimpnfxnegmnf2 {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (F M: class j) (Z: class):
  $ wnfc j F $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) (wbr (cmpt j Z (cxne (cfv (cv j) F))) cmnf clsxlim)) $;

--| A function converges in the extended reals iff its restriction to an upper integers set converges. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimresdm (ph: wff) (F M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cxr cc cpm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm clsxlim)) (wcel (cres F (cfv M cuz)) (cdm clsxlim))) $;

--| If a sequence of extended reals converges to ` +oo ` then its superior limit is also ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimpnfliminf (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F clsi) cpnf) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals converges to ` +oo ` if and only if its superior limit is also ` +oo ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimpnfliminf2 (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cpnf clsxlim) (wceq (cfv F clsi) cpnf)) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals converges if and only if its inferior limit and its superior limit are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 23-Apr-2023.)
theorem xlimliminflimsup (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm clsxlim)) (wceq (cfv F clsi) (cfv F clsp))) $;

--| A sequence of extended reals converges if and only if its superior limit is smaller than or equal to its inferior limit. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 2-Dec-2023.)
theorem xlimlimsupleliminf (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cxr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm clsxlim)) (wbr (cfv F clsp) (cfv F clsi) cle)) $;

--| A complex number whose cosine is zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem coseq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccos) cc0) (wcel (co (co A2 cpi cdiv) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) cz)) $;

--| Multiplication formula for sine and cosine. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sinmulcos (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csin) (cfv B2 ccos) cmul) (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) csin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) csin) caddc) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The cosine of an integer multiple of negative ` _pi ` is either ` 1 ` or negative ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem coskpi2 (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co K cpi cmul) ccos) (cif (wbr c2 K cdvds) c1 (cneg c1))) $;

--| The cosine of negative ` _pi ` is negative ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cosnegpi:
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg cpi) ccos) (cneg c1) $;

--| If ` A ` in ` ( 0 , 2 _pi ) ` then ` sin ( A / 2 ) ` is not ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem sinaover2ne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co c2 cpi cmul) cioo)) (wne (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cc0) $;

--| The cosine of an integer multiple of negative ` _pi ` is either ` 1 ` or negative ` 1 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cosknegpi (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wceq (cfv (co K (cneg cpi) cmul) ccos) (cif (wbr c2 K cdvds) c1 (cneg c1))) $;

--| The multiplication of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem mulcncff (ph: wff) (F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmul)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous complex function restricted to a subset is continuous, using maps-to notation. This theorem generalizes ~ cncfmptss because it allows to establish a subset for the codomain also. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfmptssg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D: class) (E F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel E D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 C E) (co C D ccncf)) $;

--| A constant function is a continuous function on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem constcncfg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (co A2 C ccncf)) $;

--| The identity function is a continuous function on ` CC ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem idcncfg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cv x3)) (co A2 B2 ccncf)) $;

--| A periodic continuous function stays continuous if the domain is shifted a period. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfshift {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T: class) (F: class y1) (G: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) T caddc)) A2) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (co (cv x3) T cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co B2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| ` sin ` restricted to reals is continuous from reals to reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem resincncf:
  $ wcel (cres csin cr) (co cr cr ccncf) $;

--| Adding a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem addccncf2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (co B2 (cv x3) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| The empty set is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem _0cnf:
  $ wcel c0 (co (csn c0) (csn c0) ccn) $;

--| The finite sum of continuous complex function is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fsumcncf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 k) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss X cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 X B2) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 X (csu k A2 B2)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| A periodic continuous function stays continuous if the domain is shifted a period. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfperiod {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (crab x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) T caddc)) A2) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm F) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F A2) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F B2) (co B2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| The subtraction of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem subcncff (ph: wff) (F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cmin)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| The opposite of a continuous function is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem negcncfg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cneg B2)) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| A function with a singleton domain is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cnfdmsn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (csn A2) B2) (co (cpw (csn A2)) (cpw (csn B2)) ccn)) $;

--| Composition of continuous functions. A generalization of ~ cncfmpt1f to arbitrary domains. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfcompt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3) (C D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) (co A2 C ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C D ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv B2 F)) (co A2 D ccncf)) $;

--| The sum of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem addcncff (ph: wff) (F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof caddc)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Limit at the upper bound of a continuous function defined on a left-open right-closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ioccncflimc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv B2 F) (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $;

--| A complex function on a subset of the complex numbers is continuous if its domain is the union of relatively open subsets over which the function is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfuni {b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cuni B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wcel (cin A2 (cv b)) (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) A2 crest)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv b) B2)) (wcel (cres F (cv b)) (co (cin A2 (cv b)) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous function on a closed interval can be extended to a continuous function on the whole real line. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem icccncfext {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (F G J K Y: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cuni K) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc)) (cfv (cv x3) F) (cif (wbr (cv x3) A2 clt) (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K ctop) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co J (co A2 B2 cicc) crest) K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel G (co J (co K (crn F) crest) ccn)) (wceq (cres G (co A2 B2 cicc)) F)) $;

--| A the absolute value of a continuous function on a closed interval, that is never 0, has a strictly positive lower bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncficcgt0 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 B2: class) (C F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) (cdif cr (csn cc0)) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cv y1) (cfv C cabs) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) crp) $;

--| Limit at the lower bound, of a continuous function defined on a left-closed right-open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem icocncflimc (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cico) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 F) (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) A2 climc)) $;

--| A complex function with a singleton domain is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfdmsn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (csn A2) B2) (co (csn A2) (csn B2) ccncf)) $;

--| The quotient of two continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem divcncff (ph: wff) (F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co X (cdif cc (csn cc0)) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co F G (cof cdiv)) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| A periodic continuous function stays continuous if the domain is an open interval that is shifted a period. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfshiftioo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D T F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 B2 cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co C cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 D (cfv (co (cv x3) T cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co D cc ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous function ` F ` on an open interval ` ( A (,) B ) ` can be extended to a continuous function ` G ` on the corresponding closed interval, if it has a finite right limit ` R ` in ` A ` and a finite left limit ` L ` in ` B ` . ` F ` can be complex-valued. This lemma assumes ` A < B ` , the invoking theorem drops this assumption. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfiooicclem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 R F: class) (G: class x3) (L: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous function ` F ` on an open interval ` ( A (,) B ) ` can be extended to a continuous function ` G ` on the corresponding closed interval, if it has a finite right limit ` R ` in ` A ` and a finite left limit ` L ` in ` B ` . ` F ` can be complex-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfiooicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F: class) (G: class x3) (L: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $;

--| A continuous function ` F ` on an open interval ` ( A (,) B ) ` can be extended to a continuous function ` G ` on the corresponding closed interval, if it has a finite right limit ` R ` in ` A ` and a finite left limit ` L ` in ` B ` . ` F ` is assumed to be real-valued. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfiooiccre {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F: class) (G: class x3) (L: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cr ccncf)) $;

--| ` G ` actually extends ` F ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfioobdlem {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (R: class x3) (F: class) (G L V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) V F) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C G) (cfv C F)) $;

--| A continuous function ` F ` on an open interval ` ( A (,) B ) ` with a finite right limit ` R ` in ` A ` and a finite left limit ` L ` in ` B ` is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cncfioobd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $;

--| Jump discontinuity or discontinuity of the first kind: if the left and the right limit don't match, the function is discontinuous at the point. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem jumpncnp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F J K L: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co cmnf B2 cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv (cin A2 (co B2 cpnf cioo)) (cfv J clp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co (cres F (co cmnf B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cres F (co B2 cpnf cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne L R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel F (cfv B2 (co J (cfv ccnfld ctopn) ccnp)))) $;

--| The complex power function is continuous with respect to its second argument. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem cxpcncf2 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif cc (co cmnf cc0 cioc))) (wcel (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) ccxp)) (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| The finite product of continuous complex functions is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem fprodcncf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 k):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) B2)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 (cprod k B2 C)) (co A2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| Addition to a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem add1cncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Addition to a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem add2cncf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Subtracting a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem sub1cncfd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cv x3) A2 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| Subtraction from a constant is a continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem sub2cncfd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cv x3) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| ` F ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodsub2cncf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F: class x3 k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (cprod k A2 (co B2 (cv x3) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| ` F ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodadd2cncf {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (F: class x3 k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (cprod k A2 (co B2 (cv x3) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co cc cc ccncf)) $;

--| The sequence ` S ` of finite products, where every factor is subtracted an "always smaller" amount, converges to the finite product of the factors. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodsubrecnncnvlem {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class n) (B2: class k n) (S: class x3 k n) (F G: class x3 k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn (cprod k A2 (co B2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (cprod k A2 (co B2 (cv x3) cmin))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cprod k A2 B2) cli) $;

--| The sequence ` S ` of finite products, where every factor is subtracted an "always smaller" amount, converges to the finite product of the factors. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodsubrecnncnv {k n: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (S: class k n) (X: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn (cprod k X (co A2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) cmin))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cprod k X A2) cli) $;

--| The sequence ` S ` of finite products, where every factor is added an "always smaller" amount, converges to the finite product of the factors. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodaddrecnncnvlem {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class n) (B2: class k n) (S: class x3 k n) (F G: class x3 k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn (cprod k A2 (co B2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (cprod k A2 (co B2 (cv x3) caddc))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cprod k A2 B2) cli) $;

--| The sequence ` S ` of finite products, where every factor is added an "always smaller" amount, converges to the finite product of the factors. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem fprodaddrecnncnv {k n: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2: class k) (S: class k n) (X: class):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn (cprod k X (co A2 (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cprod k X A2) cli) $;

--| The derivative of sin^N . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem dvsinexp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 cc (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (co (co N (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul) (cfv (cv x3) ccos) cmul))) $;

--| The real derivative of the cosine. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem dvcosre {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 cr (cfv (cv x3) ccos)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cr (cneg (cfv (cv x3) csin))) $;

--| Derivative exercise: the derivative with respect to y of sin(Ay), given a constant ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvsinax {y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc (cmpt y1 cc (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) cmul) csin)) cdv) (cmpt y1 cc (co A2 (cfv (co A2 (cv y1) cmul) ccos) cmul))) $;

--| The subtraction rule for everywhere-differentiable functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvsubf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S G cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (co F G (cof cmin)) cdv) (co (co S F cdv) (co S G cdv) (cof cmin))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: derivative of a constant. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvmptconst {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cdv) (cmpt x3 A2 cc0)) $;

--| From complex differentiation to real differentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvcnre (F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf cc cc F) (wss cr (cdm (co cc F cdv)))) (wceq (co cr (cres F cr) cdv) (cres (co cc F cdv) cr)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: derivative of the identity. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvmptidg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 A2 (cv x3)) cdv) (cmpt x3 A2 c1)) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative: expand the function from an open set to its closure. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvresntr (ph: wff) (S F J K X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wss S cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (cfv J cnt)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S F cdv) (co S (cres F Y) cdv)) $;

--| The derivative of a periodic function is periodic. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fperdvper {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (T F: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdm G))) (wa (wcel (co (cv x3) T caddc) (cdm G)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) G) (cfv (cv x3) G))) $;

--| Derivative exercise: the derivative with respect to y of A x sin(By), given two constants ` A ` and ` B ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvasinbx {y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co cc (cmpt y1 cc (co A2 (cfv (co B2 (cv y1) cmul) csin) cmul)) cdv) (cmpt y1 cc (co (co A2 B2 cmul) (cfv (co B2 (cv y1) cmul) ccos) cmul))) $;

--| Restriction of a derivative to an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvresioo (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wf A2 cc F)) (wceq (co cr (cres F (co B2 C cioo)) cdv) (cres (co cr F cdv) (co B2 C cioo))) $;

--| The quotient rule for everywhere-differentiable functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvdivf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cdif cc (csn cc0)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S F cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co S G cdv)) X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (co F G (cof cdiv)) cdv) (co (co (co (co S F cdv) G (cof cmul)) (co (co S G cdv) F (cof cmul)) (cof cmin)) (co G G (cof cmul)) (cof cdiv))) $;

--| A sufficient condition for the derivative to be bounded, for the quotient of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvdivbd {x3 b: setvar} (ph: wff b) (A2 B2 C D: class x3 b) (Q R: class) (S: class b) (T U E: class) (F: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X C)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wbr (cfv C cabs) U cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) R cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wbr (cfv D cabs) T cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) Q cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X B2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr E (cfv B2 cabs) cle) X) $ >
  $ wceq F (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cdiv)) cdv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) (cv b) cle) X) cr) $;

--| A sufficient condition for the derivative of a product to be continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvsubcncf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S F cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S G cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S (co F G (cof cmin)) cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for the derivative of a product to be continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvmulcncf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S F cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S G cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S (co F G (cof cmul)) cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Derivative exercise: the derivative with respect to x of cos(Ax), given a constant ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvcosax {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co cc (cmpt x3 cc (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) ccos)) cdv) (cmpt x3 cc (co A2 (cneg (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) csin)) cmul))) $;

--| A sufficient condition for the derivative of a quotient to be continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvdivcncf (ph: wff) (S F G X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf X (cdif cc (csn cc0)) G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S F cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S G cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co S (co F G (cof cdiv)) cdv) (co X cc ccncf)) $;

--| Given a function with bounded derivative, on an open interval, here is an absolute bound to the difference of the image of two points in the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvbdfbdioolem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) K cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cfv D F) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) (co K (co D C cmin) cmul) cle) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv D F) (cfv C F) cmin) cabs) (co K (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) cle)) $;

--| A function on an open interval, with bounded derivative, is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvbdfbdioolem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F K: class) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) K cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (cfv (cfv (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) F) cabs) (co K (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) M cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| A function on an open interval, with bounded derivative, is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dvbdfbdioo {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 b) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex a (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv a) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) (cv b) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $;

--| If ` F ` has bounded derivative on ` ( A (,) B ) ` then a sequence of points in its image converges to its ` limsup ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem ioodvbdlimc1lem1 {x3 y1 z i j k: setvar} (ph: wff z) (A2 B2: class j) (R: class x3 z) (S: class y1 z j) (F: class) (K: class x3 z) (M: class y1 z):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cfv M cuz) (co A2 B2 cioo) R) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt j (cfv M cuz) (cfv (cfv (cv j) R) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cinf (crab k (wral i (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv i) R) (cfv (cv k) R) cmin) cabs) (co (cv x3) (co (csup (crn (cmpt z (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cfv (cv z) (co cr F cdv)) cabs))) cr clt) c1 caddc) cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz)) (cfv M cuz)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr S (cfv S clsp) cli) $;

--| Limit at the lower bound of an open interval, for a function with bounded derivative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem ioodvbdlimc1lem2 {x3 y1 z j: setvar} (ph: wff) (ch: wff x3 y1 z j) (A2 B2: class) (R: class z) (S F: class) (M: class z) (N: class x3 y1) (Y: class y1 z j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wceq Y (csup (crn (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs))) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (cfv (co c1 (co B2 A2 cmin) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt j (cfv M cuz) (cfv (co A2 (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt j (cfv M cuz) (co A2 (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cif (wbr M (co (cfv (co Y (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) cle) (co (cfv (co Y (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) M) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wcel (cv j) (cfv N cuz))) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv S clsp) cmin) cabs) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) clt)) (wcel (cv z) (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S clsp) (co F A2 climc)) $;

--| A real function with bounded derivative, has a limit at the upper bound of an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem ioodvbdlimc1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co F A2 climc) c0) $;

--| Limit at the upper bound of an open interval, for a function with bounded derivative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem ioodvbdlimc2lem {x3 y1 z j: setvar} (ph: wff) (ch: wff x3 y1 z j) (A2 B2: class) (R: class z) (S F: class) (M: class z) (N: class x3 y1) (Y: class y1 z j):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wceq Y (csup (crn (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs))) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (cfv (co c1 (co B2 A2 cmin) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt j (cfv M cuz) (cfv (co B2 (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt j (cfv M cuz) (co B2 (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cif (wbr M (co (cfv (co Y (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) cle) (co (cfv (co Y (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) M) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wcel (cv j) (cfv N cuz))) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv S clsp) cmin) cabs) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) clt)) (wcel (cv z) (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) B2 cmin) cabs) (co c1 (cv j) cdiv) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv S clsp) (co F B2 climc)) $;

--| A real function with bounded derivative, has a limit at the upper bound of an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Oct-2020.)
theorem ioodvbdlimc2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm (co cr F cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (co cr F cdv)) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co F B2 climc) c0) $;

--| ` X ` is a subset of ` CC ` . This statement is very often used when computing derivatives. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvdmsscn (ph: wff) (S X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X cc) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, product rule. A version of ~ dvmptmul using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvmptmulf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C D S: class x3) (V W X: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel D W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X C) cdv) (cmpt x3 X D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cmul)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co (co B2 C cmul) (co D A2 cmul) caddc))) $;

--| Function-builder for iterated derivative, division rule for constant divisor. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnmptdivc {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 n) (C: class n) (S M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) X) (wcel (cv n) (co cc0 M cfz))) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co cc0 M cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne C cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co cc0 M cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 C cdiv)) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (co B2 C cdiv))) $;

--| If ` K ` divides ` N ` but ` K ` does not divide ` M ` , then ` K ` does not divide ` ( M + N ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvdsn1add (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wi (wa (wn (wbr K M cdvds)) (wbr K N cdvds)) (wn (wbr K (co M N caddc) cdvds))) $;

--| Derivative of the polynomial ` ( x + A ) ^ K ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvxpaek {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S K X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (co (co (cv x3) A2 caddc) K cexp)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (co K (co (co (cv x3) A2 caddc) (co K c1 cmin) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| The ` N ` -th derivative of a constant function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnmptconst {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class) (N: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X cc0)) $;

--| The ` n ` -th derivative of the polynomial ` ( x + A ) ^ K ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnxpaek {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 S: class) (F: class x3) (K N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (co (co (cv x3) A2 caddc) K cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv N (co S F cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (cif (wbr K N clt) cc0 (co (co (cfv K cfa) (cfv (co K N cmin) cfa) cdiv) (co (co (cv x3) A2 caddc) (co K N cmin) cexp) cmul)))) $;

--| Function-builder for the ` N ` -th derivative, product rule. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnmul {x3 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C D: class k) (S: class) (F G: class x3) (N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 X B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf X cc (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf X cc (cfv (cv k) (co S G cdvn))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpt k (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) (co S F cdvn))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt k (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (cv k) (co S G cdvn))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co S (cmpt x3 X (co A2 B2 cmul)) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co N (cv k) cbc) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv k) C)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (co N (cv k) cmin) D)) cmul) cmul)))) $;

--| Induction step for ~ dvmptfprod . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvmptfprodlem {x3 i j: setvar} (ph: wff x3 i j) (A2: class x3 i) (C: class x3 i j) (D: class) (S: class x3 i j) (E: class) (F: class x3 i) (G: class x3 i j) (I: class x3 j) (X: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnfc i F $ >
  $ wnfc j G $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) I) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel E D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun D (csn E)) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel (cv j) D)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (cprod i D A2)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (csu j D (co C (cprod i (cdif D (csn (cv j))) A2) cmul)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X F) cdv) (cmpt x3 X G)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv i) E) (wceq A2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) E) (wceq C G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (cprod i (cun D (csn E)) A2)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (csu j (cun D (csn E)) (co C (cprod i (cdif (cun D (csn E)) (csn (cv j))) A2) cmul)))) $;

--| Function-builder for derivative, finite product rule. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvmptfprod {x3 i j: setvar} (ph: wff i j) (A2: class x3 i) (B2: class x3 i j) (C: class x3 j) (S I: class) (J K: class x3 i j) (X: class):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wceq J (co K S crest) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv ccnfld ctopn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cfn) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) I) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) I) (wcel (cv x3) X)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) I)) (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 X B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv i) (cv j)) (wceq B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co S (cmpt x3 X (cprod i I A2)) cdv) (cmpt x3 X (csu j I (co C (cprod i (cdif I (csn (cv j))) A2) cmul)))) $;

--| ` D ` is bijective. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnprodlem1 {t k n s1 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class t n s1) (D: class k n s1) (R: class) (T: class k n c) (J: class s1) (Z: class):
  $ wceq C (cmpt s1 (cpw T) (cmpt n cn0 (crab c (wceq (csu t (cv s1) (cfv (cv t) (cv c))) (cv n)) (co (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cv s1) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cn0) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt c (cfv J (cfv (cun R (csn Z)) C)) (cop (co J (cfv Z (cv c)) cmin) (cres (cv c) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel Z R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cun R (csn Z)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (cfv J (cfv (cun R (csn Z)) C)) (ciun k (co cc0 J cfz) (cxp (csn (cv k)) (cfv (cv k) (cfv R C)))) D) $;

--| Induction step for ~ dvnprodlem2 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnprodlem2 {x3 t j k n s1 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 j n s1) (D: class x3 j k n s1) (R: class j) (S: class n s1) (T: class x3 k n c) (H: class n s1) (J: class) (N: class x3 k n s1 c) (X: class n s1) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wf X cc (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv t) T) (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf X cc (cfv (cv j) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpt s1 (cpw T) (cmpt n cn0 (crab c (wceq (csu t (cv s1) (cfv (cv t) (cv c))) (cv n)) (co (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cv s1) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss R T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z (cdif T R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral k (wceq (cfv (cv k) (co S (cmpt x3 X (cprod t R (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) H)))) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (csu c (cfv (cv k) (cfv R C)) (co (co (cfv (cv k) cfa) (cprod t R (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) cfa)) cdiv) (cprod t R (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn)))) cmul)))) (co cc0 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt c (cfv J (cfv (cun R (csn Z)) C)) (cop (co J (cfv Z (cv c)) cmin) (cres (cv c) R))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv J (co S (cmpt x3 X (cprod t (cun R (csn Z)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) H)))) cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (csu c (cfv J (cfv (cun R (csn Z)) C)) (co (co (cfv J cfa) (cprod t (cun R (csn Z)) (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) cfa)) cdiv) (cprod t (cun R (csn Z)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn)))) cmul)))) $;

--| The multinomial formula for the ` k ` -th derivative of a finite product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnprodlem3 {x3 t j n s1 c: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 t j n s1) (D S T: class) (F: class x3 t j n c) (H N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wf X cc (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv t) T) (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf X cc (cfv (cv j) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cprod t T (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) H)))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt s1 (cpw T) (cmpt n cn0 (crab c (wceq (csu t (cv s1) (cfv (cv t) (cv c))) (cv n)) (co (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (cv s1) cmap)))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpt n cn0 (crab c (wceq (csu t T (cfv (cv t) (cv c))) (cv n)) (co (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) T cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co S F cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (csu c (cfv N C) (co (co (cfv N cfa) (cprod t T (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) cfa)) cdiv) (cprod t T (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn)))) cmul)))) $;

--| The multinomial formula for the ` N ` -th derivative of a finite product. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem dvnprod {x3 t k n c: setvar} (ph: wff c) (C: class x3 t k n) (S T: class) (F: class x3 t k n c) (H N: class) (X: class c):
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cpr cr cc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) S crest)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wf X cc (cfv (cv t) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv t) T) (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf X cc (cfv (cv k) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 X (cprod t T (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cv t) H)))) $ >
  $ wceq C (cmpt n cn0 (crab c (wceq (csu t T (cfv (cv t) (cv c))) (cv n)) (co (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) T cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (co S F cdvn)) (cmpt x3 X (csu c (cfv N C) (co (co (cfv N cfa) (cprod t T (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) cfa)) cdiv) (cprod t T (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cfv (cv t) (cv c)) (co S (cfv (cv t) H) cdvn)))) cmul)))) $;

--| Calculation of the integral for sine on the (0,&pi;) interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem itgsin0pilem1 {x3 t: setvar} (C: class t):
  $ wceq C (cmpt t (co cc0 cpi cicc) (cneg (cfv (cv t) ccos))) $ >
  $ wceq (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (cfv (cv x3) csin)) c2 $;

--| sin^n on a closed interval is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem ibliccsinexp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp)) cibl) $;

--| Calculation of the integral for sine on the (0,&pi;) interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem itgsin0pi {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (cfv (cv x3) csin)) c2 $;

--| sin^n on an open integral is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem iblioosinexp {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp)) cibl) $;

--| Integration by parts is applied to integrate sin^(N+1). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem itgsinexplem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H I L M: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 cc (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cc (cneg (cfv (cv x3) ccos))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cc (co (co N (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul) (cfv (cv x3) ccos) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cc (co (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp) (cfv (cv x3) csin) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt x3 cc (co (co (co N (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul) (cfv (cv x3) ccos) cmul) (cneg (cfv (cv x3) ccos)) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 cc (co (co (cfv (cv x3) ccos) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) N cexp) (cfv (cv x3) csin) cmul)) (co N (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (co (cfv (cv x3) ccos) c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cmul)) cmul)) $;

--| A recursive formula for the integral of sin^N on the interval (0,&pi;) . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem itgsinexp {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (I: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N I) (co (co (co N c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co N c2 cmin) I) cmul)) $;

--| A constant function is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblconstmpt {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) (wcel B2 cc)) (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) $;

--| Equality theorem for an integral. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgeq1d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 A2 C) (citg x3 B2 C)) $;

--| Measurability of a piecewise function: if ` F ` is measurable on subsets ` B ` and ` C ` of its domain, and these pieces make up all of ` A ` , then ` F ` is measurable on the whole domain. Similar to ~ mbfres2 but here the theorem is extended to complex-valued functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem mbfres2cn (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F B2) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cmbf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cun B2 C) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cmbf) $;

--| The measure of the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem vol0:
  $ wceq (cfv c0 cvol) cc0 $;

--| A function ` F ` on an open interval, has the same directed integral as its extension ` G ` on the closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ditgeqiooicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (R F G L: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 (cfv (cv x3) F)) (cdit x3 A2 B2 (cfv (cv x3) G))) $;

--| The volume of a set is always nonnegative. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem volge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cvol) cle) $;

--| A continuous bounded function is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem cnbdibl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cvol) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (cdm F)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $;

--| A singleton is measurable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem snmbl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (csn A2) (cdm cvol)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the directed integral. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ditgeq3d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D E: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wceq D E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit x3 A2 B2 D) (cdit x3 A2 B2 E)) $;

--| The empty function is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblempty:
  $ wcel c0 cibl $;

--| The union of two integrable functions is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblsplit {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (U: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) U)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 U C) cibl) $;

--| A singleton has 0 Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem volsn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv (csn A2) cvol) cc0) $;

--| If the domani is negligible, the function is integrable and the integral is 0. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgvol0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 B2) cibl) (wceq (citg x3 A2 B2) cc0)) $;

--| Exercise: the integral of ` x |-> cos a x ` on an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgcoscmulx {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co B2 C cioo) (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) ccos)) (co (co (cfv (co A2 C cmul) csin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csin) cmin) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| A version of ~ iblsplit using bound-variable hypotheses instead of distinct variable conditions". (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblsplitf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (U: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq U (cun A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) U)) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 A2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 B2 C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 U C) cibl) $;

--| If a function is integrable on an open interval, it is integrable on the corresponding closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem ibliooicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) C) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) C) cibl) $;

--| The measure of a left-open right-closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem volioc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioc) cvol) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| If a function is integrable on any interval of a partition, then it is integrable on the whole interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblspltprt {t i: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (P M N: class):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv i) P) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co M N cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) (co (cfv M P) (cfv N P) cicc))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel (cmpt t (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cicc) A2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t (co (cfv M P) (cfv N P) cicc) A2) cibl) $;

--| Exercise: the integral of ` x |-> sin a x ` on an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgsincmulx {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co B2 C cioo) (cfv (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) csin)) (co (co (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) ccos) (cfv (co A2 C cmul) ccos) cmin) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| lemma for ~ itgsubsticc . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgsubsticclem {x3 u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C: class x3 u) (E: class x3) (F: class x3 u) (G: class u) (K L X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt u (co K L cicc) C) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt u cr (cif (wcel (cv u) (co K L cicc)) (cfv (cv u) F) (cif (wbr (cv u) K clt) (cfv K F) (cfv L F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) (co (co X Y cicc) (co K L cicc) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2) (cin (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf) cibl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co K L cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr K L cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv u) A2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq A2 K) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit u K L C) (cdit x3 X Y (co E B2 cmul))) $;

--| Integration by u-substitution. The main difference with respect to ~ itgsubst is that here we consider the range of ` A ( x ) ` to be in the closed interval ` ( K [,] L ) ` . If ` A ( x ) ` is a continuous, differentiable function from ` [ X , Y ] ` to ` ( Z , W ) ` , whose derivative is continuous and integrable, and ` C ( u ) ` is a continuous function on ` ( Z , W ) ` , then the integral of ` C ( u ) ` from ` K = A ( X ) ` to ` L = A ( Y ) ` is equal to the integral of ` C ( A ( x ) ) _D A ( x ) ` from ` X ` to ` Y ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgsubsticc {x3 u: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 u) (C: class u) (E: class x3) (K L X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) (co (co X Y cicc) (co K L cicc) ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt u (co K L cicc) C) (co (co K L cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2) (cin (co (co X Y cioo) cc ccncf) cibl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt x3 (co X Y cicc) A2) cdv) (cmpt x3 (co X Y cioo) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv u) A2) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq A2 K) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) Y) (wceq A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit u K L C) (cdit x3 X Y (co E B2 cmul))) $;

--| The integral of a piecewise continuous function ` F ` on an open interval is equal to the integral of the continuous function ` G ` , in the corresponding closed interval. ` G ` is equal to ` F ` on the open interval, but it is continuous at the two boundaries, also. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgioocnicc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F: class) (G: class x3) (L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm F) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel G cibl) (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) G)) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| A continuous function ` F ` on an open interval ` ( A (,) B ) ` with a finite right limit ` R ` in ` A ` and a finite left limit ` L ` in ` B ` is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem iblcncfioo (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F L: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $;

--| The ` S. ` integral splits on a given partition ` P ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgspltprt {t i: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class t) (P M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co M N cfz) cr P) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co M N cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) (co (cfv M P) (cfv N P) cicc))) (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel (cmpt t (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cicc) A2) cibl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg t (co (cfv M P) (cfv N P) cicc) A2) (csu i (co M N cfzo) (citg t (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cicc) A2))) $;

--| The integral of a function, ` F ` stays the same if its closed interval domain is shifted by ` T ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgiccshift {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (cfv (co (cv x3) T cmin) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (cfv (cv x3) G)) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| The integral of a periodic function, with period ` T ` stays the same if the domain of integration is shifted. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgperiod {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T F: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co A2 B2 cicc)) (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| Integral substitution, adding a constant to the function's argument. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem itgsbtaddcnst {t s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdit s1 (co A2 X cmin) (co B2 X cmin) (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F)) (cdit t A2 B2 (cfv (cv t) F))) $;

--| The measure of left-closed, right-open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Oct-2020.)
theorem volico (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cico) cvol) (cif (wbr A2 B2 clt) (co B2 A2 cmin) cc0)) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure of a left-closed, right-open interval is greater than or equal to the difference of the two bounds. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem sublevolico (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co B2 A2 cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cico) cvol) cle) $;

--| Lebesgue measurable sets are subsets of Real numbers. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem dmvolss:
  $ wss (cdm cvol) (cpw cr) $;

--| The predicate " ` A ` is Lebesgue-measurable". Similar to ~ ismbl2 , but here ` +e ` is used, and the precondition ` ( vol* `` x ) e. RR ` can be dropped. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem ismbl3 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wa (wss A2 cr) (wral x3 (wbr (co (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) covol) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) A2) covol) cxad) (cfv (cv x3) covol) cle) (cpw cr))) $;

--| The function that assigns the Lebesgue measure to open intervals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem volioof:
  $ wf (cxp cxr cxr) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (ccom cvol cioo) $;

--| The Lebesgue outer measure function is finitely sub-additive: application to a set split in two parts, using addition for extended reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem ovolsplit (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 covol) (co (cfv (cin A2 B2) covol) (cfv (cdif A2 B2) covol) cxad) cle) $;

--| The function value of the Lebesgue measure of an open interval composed with a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fvvolioof (ph: wff) (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cxr cxr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (ccom (ccom cvol cioo) F)) (cfv (co (cfv (cfv X F) c1st) (cfv (cfv X F) c2nd) cioo) cvol)) $;

--| The measure of an open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem volioore (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) cvol) (cif (wbr A2 B2 cle) (co B2 A2 cmin) cc0)) $;

--| The function value of the Lebesgue measure of a left-closed right-open interval composed with a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem fvvolicof (ph: wff) (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cxr cxr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X (ccom (ccom cvol cico) F)) (cfv (co (cfv (cfv X F) c1st) (cfv (cfv X F) c2nd) cico) cvol)) $;

--| An open interval and a left-closed, right-open interval with the same real bounds, have the same Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem voliooico (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cioo) cvol) (cfv (co A2 B2 cico) cvol)) $;

--| The predicate " ` A ` is Lebesgue-measurable". Similar to ~ ismbl , but here ` +e ` is used, and the precondition ` ( vol* `` x ) e. RR ` can be dropped. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem ismbl4 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) (wa (wss A2 cr) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) covol) (co (cfv (cin (cv x3) A2) covol) (cfv (cdif (cv x3) A2) covol) cxad)) (cpw cr))) $;

--| ` ( ( vol o. (,) ) o. F ) ` expressed in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem volioofmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cxr cxr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom (ccom cvol cioo) F) (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) c2nd) cioo) cvol))) $;

--| ` ( ( vol o. [,) ) o. F ) ` expressed in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem volicoff (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cr cxr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (ccom (ccom cvol cico) F)) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure of open intervals is the same as the Lebesgue measure of left-closed right-open intervals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem voliooicof (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cr cr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom (ccom cvol cioo) F) (ccom (ccom cvol cico) F)) $;

--| ` ( ( vol o. [,) ) o. F ) ` expressed in maps-to notation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 3-Mar-2021.)
theorem volicofmpt {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 (cxp cr cxr) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom (ccom cvol cico) F) (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) c1st) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) c2nd) cico) cvol))) $;

--| The Lebesgue measure of a closed interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem volicc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) cvol) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| A closed interval and a left-closed, right-open interval with the same real bounds, have the same Lebesgue measure. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 8-Apr-2021.)
theorem voliccico (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cicc) cvol) (cfv (co A2 B2 cico) cvol)) $;

--| The domain of a measurable function is a subset of the Reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 26-Jun-2021.)
theorem mbfdmssre (F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F cmbf) (wss (cdm F) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid . This lemma is used by Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90; the key step uses Bernoulli's inequality ~ bernneq . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 D K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp) (co c1 (co (co K D cmul) N cexp) cdiv) cle) $;

--| lemma for ~ stoweid : here we prove that the subalgebra of continuous functions, which contains constant functions, is closed under scaling. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem2 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (T: class) (E: class g2) (F: class x3):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t T (co E (cfv (cv t) F) cmul)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid : if ` A ` is positive and all ` M ` terms of a finite product are larger than ` A ` , then the finite product is larger than ` A ^ M ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem3 {i: setvar} (ph: wff i) (A2: class) (F: class i) (M: class) (X: class i):
  $ wnfc i F $ >
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wceq X (cseq cmul F c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wbr A2 (cfv (cv i) F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 M cexp) (cfv M X) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid : a class variable replaces a setvar variable, for constant functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem4 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (B2: class) (T: class t):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T B2) A2) $;

--| There exists a &delta; as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: 0 < &delta; < 1 , p >= &delta; on ` T \ U ` . Here ` D ` is used to represent &delta; in the paper and ` Q ` to represent ` T \ U ` in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem5 {t d: setvar} (ph: wff t d) (C: class t d) (D: class) (P Q: class t) (T: class t d):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq D (cif (wbr C (co c1 c2 cdiv) cle) C (co c1 c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss Q T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr C (cfv (cv t) P) cle) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex d (w3a (wcel (cv d) crp) (wbr (cv d) c1 clt) (wral t (wbr (cv d) (cfv (cv t) P) cle) Q))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid : two class variables replace two setvar variables, for multiplication of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem6 {t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2 T F: class t) (G: class t f2):
  $ wnf t (wceq (cv f2) F) $ >
  $ wnf t (wceq (cv g2) G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel F A2) (wcel G A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) G) cmul)) A2) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that q_n as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91, (at the top of page 91), is such that q_n < &epsilon; on ` T \ U ` , and q_n > 1 - &epsilon; on ` V ` . Here it is proven that, for ` n ` large enough, 1-(k*&delta;/2)^n > 1 - &epsilon; , and 1/(k*&delta;)^n < &epsilon;. The variable ` A ` is used to represent (k*&delta;) in the paper, and ` B ` is used to represent (k*&delta;/2). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem7 {i n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 E: class) (F G: class i):
  $ wceq F (cmpt i cn0 (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) (cv i) cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt i cn0 (co B2 (cv i) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wa (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (co c1 (co B2 (cv n) cexp) cmin) clt) (wbr (co c1 (co A2 (cv n) cexp) cdiv) E clt)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid : two class variables replace two setvar variables, for the sum of two functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem8 {t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2 T F: class t) (G: class t f2):
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel F A2) (wcel G A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) G) caddc)) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid : here the Stone Weierstrass theorem is proven for the trivial case, T is the empty set. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem9 {t g2: setvar} (ph: wff t g2) (A2: class t) (T: class) (E F: class t):
  $ wi ph (wceq T c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t T c1) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid . This lemma is used by Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90, this lemma is an application of Bernoulli's inequality. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem10 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr A2 c1 cle)) (wbr (co c1 (co N A2 cmul) cmin) (co (co c1 A2 cmin) N cexp) cle) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that there is a function ` g ` as in the proof of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 92 (at the top of page 92): this lemma proves that g(t) < ( j + 1 / 3 ) * &epsilon;. Here ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem11 {t i j: setvar} (ph: wff t j) (T: class i j) (E N: class j) (X: class i j):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv t) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv j) (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf T cr (cfv (cv i) X)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co (cv j) N cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) (co E N cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cmpt t T (csu i (co cc0 N cfz) (co E (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cmul)))) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid . This Lemma is used by other three Lemmas. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem12 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (P Q: class t) (T: class) (K N: class t):
  $ wceq Q (cmpt t T (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wceq (cfv (cv t) Q) (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid . This lemma is used to prove the statement abs( f(t) - g(t) ) < 2 epsilon, in the last step of the proof in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 92. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem13 {j: setvar} (ph: wff j) (E X Y: class j):
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv j) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) X clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) Y clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr Y (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co Y X cmin) cabs) (co c2 E cmul) clt) $;

--| There exists a ` k ` as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: ` k ` is an integer and 1 < k * &delta; < 2. ` D ` is used to represent &delta; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem14 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (A2: class j) (D: class):
  $ wceq A2 (crab j (wbr (co c1 D cdiv) (cv j) clt) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex k (wa (wbr c1 (co (cv k) D cmul) clt) (wbr (co (co (cv k) D cmul) c2 cdiv) c1 clt)) cn) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function ` p ` as in Lemma 1 from [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: ` p ` is in the subalgebra, such that 0 &le; p &le; 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Here ` ( G `` I ) ` is used to represent p__(t_i) in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem15 {t f2 h: setvar} (ph: wff t h) (A2: class t) (Q S: class t f2 h) (T G I: class) (M: class t f2 h) (Z: class t f2):
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel I (co c1 M cfz))) (wcel S T)) (w3a (wcel (cfv S (cfv I G)) cr) (wbr cc0 (cfv S (cfv I G)) cle) (wbr (cfv S (cfv I G)) c1 cle)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ stoweid . The subset ` Y ` of functions in the algebra ` A ` , with values in [ 0 , 1 ], is closed under multiplication. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem16 {t f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff t g2 h) (A2: class) (T: class g2) (H: class t f2 g2) (Y: class t f2 g2 h):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) Y) (wcel (cv g2) Y)) (wcel H Y) $;

--| This lemma proves that the function ` g ` (as defined in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91, at the end of page 91) belongs to the subalgebra. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem17 {x3 t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t i) (T E: class) (N: class x3 f2 g2) (X: class x3):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 N cfz) A2 X) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t T (csu i (co cc0 N cfz) (co E (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cmul))) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 92 when A is empty, the trivial case. Here D is used to denote the set A of Lemma 2, because the variable A is used for the subalgebra. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem18 {x3 t a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t) (A2: class t) (B2 D: class t a) (T: class) (E F: class t a) (J: class x3 t a):
  $ wnfc t D $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T c1) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv a)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) D) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) B2)) A2) $;

--| If a set of real functions is closed under multiplication and it contains constants, then it is closed under finite exponentiation. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem19 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2 T: class) (F: class x3 t) (N: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) N cexp)) A2) $;

--| If a set A of real functions from a common domain T is closed under the sum of two functions, then it is closed under the sum of a finite number of functions, indexed by G. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem20 {t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t i) (T: class) (F: class t f2 g2 i) (G: class) (M: class f2 g2):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) A2 G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F A2) $;

--| Once the Stone Weierstrass theorem has been proven for approximating nonnegative functions, then this lemma is used to extend the result to functions with (possibly) negative values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem21 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (S: class t f2) (T: class) (E F G H: class x3 t):
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wnfc t H $ >
  $ wnfc t S $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) H) S caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral f2 (wf T cr (cv f2)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) H) (co (cfv (cv t) F) S cmin) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) A2) $;

--| If a set of real functions from a common domain is closed under addition, multiplication and it contains constants, then it is closed under subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem22 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2 T: class) (F G: class x3 t) (H: class x3 t f2 g2) (I: class x3 t) (L: class x3 t f2):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) G) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt t T (cneg c1)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) I) (cfv (cv t) G) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel F A2) (wcel G A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) G) cmin)) A2) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p_t as in the beginning of Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: for all t in T - U, there exists a function p in the subalgebra, such that p_t ( t_0 ) = 0 , p_t ( t ) > 0, and 0 <= p_t <= 1. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem23 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t) (S: class x3 f2 g2) (T: class) (G: class t) (H: class x3 t f2 g2) (Z: class f2):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) G) (cfv Z G) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv S G) (cfv Z G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel H A2) (wne (cfv S H) (cfv Z H)) (wceq (cfv Z H) cc0)) $;

--| This lemma proves that for ` n ` sufficiently large, q_n( t ) > ( 1 - epsilon ), for all ` t ` in ` V ` : see Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90, (at the bottom of page 90). ` Q ` is used to represent q_n in the paper, ` N ` to represent ` n ` in the paper, ` K ` to represent ` k ` , ` D ` to represent &delta;, and ` E ` to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem24 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (D P Q: class t) (T: class) (E K N V: class t):
  $ wceq V (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) T) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt t T (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (co c1 (co (co (co K D cmul) c2 cdiv) N cexp) cmin) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) V)) (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) Q) clt) $;

--| This lemma proves that for n sufficiently large, q_n( t ) < &epsilon;, for all ` t ` in ` T \ U ` : see Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91 (at the top of page 91). ` Q ` is used to represent q_n in the paper, ` N ` to represent n in the paper, ` K ` to represent k, ` D ` to represent &delta;, ` P ` to represent p, and ` E ` to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem25 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (D P Q: class t) (T: class) (U E K N: class t):
  $ wceq Q (cmpt t T (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr D (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 (co (co K D cmul) N cexp) cdiv) E clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) (cdif T U))) (wbr (cfv (cv t) Q) E clt) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that there is a function ` g ` as in the proof of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 92: this lemma proves that g(t) > ( j - 4 / 3 ) * &epsilon;. Here ` L ` is used to represent j in the paper, ` D ` is used to represent A in the paper, ` S ` is used to represent t, and ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem26 {t i j: setvar} (ph: wff t j) (B2 D: class t i j) (S: class j) (T: class i) (E: class) (F: class t i) (L N: class) (X: class i j):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co c1 N cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdif (cfv L D) (cfv (co L c1 cmin) D))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf T cr (cfv (cv i) X)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) T)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv i) B2))) (wbr (co c1 (co E N cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co L (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) (cfv S (cmpt t T (csu i (co cc0 N cfz) (co E (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cmul)))) clt) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Here ` ( q `` i ) ` is used to represent p__(t_i) in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem27 {w t h i q l: setvar} (ph: wff w t h q) (Q: class t h i) (T: class t i l) (U: class t h i l) (F: class l) (G: class w t h q) (M: class t h l) (X: class t h q l) (Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt w X (crab h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn Y (crn G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn G) cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv l) (crn G))) (wcel (cfv (cv l) Y) (cv l)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co c1 M cfz) (crn G) F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdif T U) (cuni X)) $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnfc h Q $ >
  $ wi ph (wex q (wa (wcel M cn) (wa (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q (cv q)) (wral t (wrex i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv q))) clt) (co c1 M cfz)) (cdif T U))))) $;

--| There exists a &delta; as in Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: 0 < delta < 1 and p >= delta on ` T \ U ` . Here ` d ` is used to represent &delta; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem28 {t d: setvar} (ph: wff t d) (P T: class) (U: class t) (J: class d) (K: class t d):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) clt) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex d (w3a (wcel (cv d) crp) (wbr (cv d) c1 clt) (wral t (wbr (cv d) (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (cdif T U)))) $;

--| When the hypothesis for the extreme value theorem hold, then the inf of the range of the function belongs to the range, it is real and it a lower bound of the range. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem stoweidlem29 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (T: class) (F: class t) (J K: class):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel (cinf (crn F) cr clt) (crn F)) (wcel (cinf (crn F) cr clt) cr) (wral t (wbr (cinf (crn F) cr clt) (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T)) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Z is used for t_0, P is used for p, ` ( G `` i ) ` is used for p__(t_i). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem30 {t f2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff t h) (A2: class t i) (P Q: class t f2 h i) (S: class t f2 h) (T: class) (G: class i) (M: class f2 h) (Z: class t f2 i):
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t T (co (co c1 M cdiv) (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel S T)) (wceq (cfv S P) (co (co c1 M cdiv) (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv S (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that there exists a function x as in the proof of Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91: assuming that ` R ` is a finite subset of ` V ` , ` x ` indexes a finite set of functions in the subalgebra (of the Stone Weierstrass theorem), such that for all ` i ` ranging in the finite indexing set, 0 &le; x_i &le; 1, x_i < &epsilon; / m on V(t_i), and x_i > 1 - &epsilon; / m on ` B ` . Here M is used to represent m in the paper, ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper, v_i is used to represent V(t_i). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem31 {x3 w v t e h i: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w v t e h) (A2: class x3 v i) (B2: class w v t e h i) (R: class x3 v e i) (T U: class x3 v t i) (E: class v i) (G: class w v t e h) (J: class x3 w v t e h i) (M: class v) (V: class x3 w v t e h i) (Y: class v t e h i):
  $ wnf h ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq V (crab w (wral e (wrex h (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) (cv e) clt) (cv w)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp) J) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt w R (crab h (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) (co E M cdiv) clt) (cv w)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E M cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss R V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co c1 M cfz) R (cv v)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn G) cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wa (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y (cv x3)) (wral i (wa (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3))) (co E M cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv i) (cv v))) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E M cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3))) clt) B2)) (co c1 M cfz)))) $;

--| If a set A of real functions from a common domain T is a subalgebra and it contains constants, then it is closed under the sum of a finite number of functions, indexed by G and finally scaled by a real Y. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem32 {x3 t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class t i) (P: class x3 t f2 g2 i) (T: class) (F: class x3 t i) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 t f2 i) (M: class x3 f2 g2) (Y: class f2 g2 i):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t T (co Y (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G)))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) A2 G) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P A2) $;

--| If a set of real functions from a common domain is closed under addition, multiplication and it contains constants, then it is closed under subtraction. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem33 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2 T: class) (F G: class x3 t):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel F A2) (wcel G A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) G) cmin)) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that for all ` t ` in ` T ` there is a ` j ` as in the proof of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91 (at the bottom of page 91 and at the top of page 92): (j-4/3) * &epsilon; < f(t) <= (j-1/3) * &epsilon; , g(t) < (j+1/3) * &epsilon;, and g(t) > (j-4/3) * &epsilon;. Here ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem34 {t i j: setvar} (ph: wff t j) (B2: class t i j) (D: class t j) (T E: class) (F: class t i) (J: class t j) (N: class) (X: class i):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt t T (crab j (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv j) D)) (co c1 N cfz))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) T)) (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co N c1 cmin) E cmul) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz))) (wf T cr (cfv (cv j) X)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) T)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) X)) cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) T)) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) X)) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv j) D))) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) X)) (co E N cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (cv t) (cfv (cv j) B2))) (wbr (co c1 (co E N cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) X)) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wrex j (wa (wa (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle)) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cmpt t T (csu i (co cc0 N cfz) (co E (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cmul)))) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) clt) (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) (cmpt t T (csu i (co cc0 N cfz) (co E (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) X)) cmul)))) clt))) cr) T) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Here ` ( q `` i ) ` is used to represent p__(t_i) in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem35 {w t h i m q: setvar} (ph: wff w t h q) (A2: class w i m q) (Q: class t h i m) (T: class t i m) (U: class t h i m) (G: class w t h q) (J W: class w t h i m q) (X: class m q) (Z: class w h i m q):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnf h ph $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wrex h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q) J) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt w X (crab h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss X W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdif T U) (cuni X)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cdif T U) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex m (wex q (wa (wcel (cv m) cn) (wa (wf (co c1 (cv m) cfz) Q (cv q)) (wral t (wrex i (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv q))) clt) (co c1 (cv m) cfz)) (cdif T U)))))) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p_t as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90 (at the beginning of Lemma 1): for all t in T - U, there exists a function p in the subalgebra, such that p_t ( t_0 ) = 0 , p_t ( t ) > 0, and 0 <= p_t <= 1. Z is used for t_0 , S is used for t e. T - U , h is used for p_t . G is used for (h_t)^2 and the final h is a normalized version of G ( divided by its norm, see the variable N ). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem36 {x3 t f2 g2 h: setvar} (ph: wff t h) (A2: class t) (Q: class x3 t f2 g2 h) (S: class x3 f2 g2) (T: class) (F G: class x3 t h) (H: class x3 t f2 g2) (J K: class x3 t f2 g2 h) (N: class f2 h) (Z: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wnfc h Q $ >
  $ wnfc t H $ >
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnfc t G $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cfv (cv t) F) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq N (csup (crn G) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) G) N cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv S F) (cfv Z F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z F) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex h (wa (wcel (cv h) Q) (wbr cc0 (cfv S (cv h)) clt))) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Z is used for t_0, P is used for p, ` ( G `` i ) ` is used for p__(t_i). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem37 {t f2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff t h) (A2: class t i) (P Q: class t f2 h i) (T: class) (G: class i) (M: class f2 h) (Z: class f2):
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t T (co (co c1 M cdiv) (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z P) cc0) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Z is used for t_0, P is used for p, ` ( G `` i ) ` is used for p__(t_i). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem38 {t f2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff t h) (A2: class t i) (P Q: class t f2 h i) (S: class t f2 h) (T: class) (G: class i) (M: class f2 h) (Z: class t f2 i):
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t T (co (co c1 M cdiv) (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q G) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel S T)) (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv S P) cle) (wbr (cfv S P) c1 cle)) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that there exists a function x as in the proof of Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91: assuming that ` r ` is a finite subset of ` W ` , ` x ` indexes a finite set of functions in the subalgebra (of the Stone Weierstrass theorem), such that for all i ranging in the finite indexing set, 0 &le; x_i &le; 1, x_i < &epsilon; / m on V(t_i), and x_i > 1 - &epsilon; / m on ` B ` . Here ` D ` is used to represent A in the paper's Lemma 2 (because ` A ` is used for the subalgebra), ` M ` is used to represent m in the paper, ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon;, and v_i is used to represent V(t_i). ` W ` is just a local definition, used to shorten statements. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem39 {x3 w v t e h i m r: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w t e h r) (A2: class v m r) (B2: class w v t e h i m r) (D: class x3 w v t e h i m r) (T U: class v t m r) (E: class v m r) (J W: class x3 w v t e h i m r) (Y: class v t e h i m r):
  $ wnf h ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wceq U (cdif T B2) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wral e (wrex h (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) (cv e) clt) (cv w)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv r) (cin (cpw W) cfn)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cuni (cv r))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex m (wex v (w3a (wf (co c1 (cv m) cfz) W (cv v)) (wss D (cuni (crn (cv v)))) (wex x3 (wa (wf (co c1 (cv m) cfz) Y (cv x3)) (wral i (wa (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3))) (co E (cv m) cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv i) (cv v))) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E (cv m) cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3))) clt) B2)) (co c1 (cv m) cfz)))))) cn) $;

--| This lemma proves that q_n is in the subalgebra, as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90. Q is used to represent q_n in the paper, N is used to represent n in the paper, and M is used to represent k^n in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem40 {x3 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (P: class x3 t) (Q: class x3 t f2 g2) (T: class) (F G H: class x3 t) (M N: class x3 f2 g2):
  $ wnfc t P $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt t T (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) M cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt t T c1) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q A2) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that there exists x as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: 0 <= x(t) <= 1 for all t in T, x(t) < epsilon for all t in V, x(t) > 1 - epsilon for all t in T \ U. Here we prove the very last step of the proof of Lemma 1: "The result follows from taking x = 1 - q_n". Here ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper, and ` y ` to represent q_n in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem41 {x3 y1 w t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 t) (A2 T: class y1) (U E: class y1 w t f2 g2) (F: class x3 y1 w t) (V X: class y1 w t f2 g2):
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq X (cmpt t T (co c1 (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T c1) $ >
  $ wss V T $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cv y1) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv w) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv w)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) clt) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) E clt) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) V) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that ` x ` built as in Lemma 2 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91, is such that x > 1 - &epsilon; on B. Here ` X ` is used to represent ` x ` in the paper, and E is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem42 {t f2 g2 i: setvar} (ph: wff t i) (B2: class t f2 g2) (P: class t f2 g2 i) (T U: class) (E: class t f2 g2) (F: class t i) (M: class t) (X: class t f2 g2 i) (Y: class t i) (Z: class t f2 g2 i):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnfc t Y $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M (cseq P U c1)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt t T (cfv M (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E M cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)) clt) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) Y)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) Y) (wcel (cv g2) Y)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) X) clt) B2) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p_t as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90 (at the beginning of Lemma 1): for all t in T - U, there exists a function p_t in the subalgebra, such that p_t( t_0 ) = 0 , p_t ( t ) > 0, and 0 <= p_t <= 1. Hera Z is used for t_0 , S is used for t e. T - U , h is used for p_t. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem43 {x3 t f2 g2 h r l: setvar} (ph: wff t g2 h) (A2: class) (Q: class x3 t g2 h r l) (S: class) (T: class g2) (U J K: class x3 t f2 g2 h r l) (Z: class r):
  $ wnf g2 ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnfc h Q $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv l) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv l)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv l) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv l)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex g2 (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex h (wa (wcel (cv h) Q) (wbr cc0 (cfv S (cv h)) clt))) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function p as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Z is used to represent t_0 in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem44 {x3 t f2 g2 h i j p: setvar} (ph: wff t h j p) (A2: class t i j) (P: class x3 t f2 g2 h i j) (Q: class x3 t f2 g2 h i j p) (T: class) (U: class x3 t f2 g2 h i) (G: class x3 p) (J K: class x3 t f2 g2 h i j p) (M: class h p) (Z: class x3 f2 g2 j):
  $ wnf j ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt t T (co (co c1 M cdiv) (csu i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) G))) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Q G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wrex j (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) G)) clt) (co c1 M cfz)) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex p (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) c1 cle)) T) (wceq (cfv Z (cv p)) cc0) (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that, given an appropriate ` K ` (in another theorem we prove such a ` K ` exists), there exists a function q_n as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91 ( at the top of page 91): 0 <= q_n <= 1 , q_n < &epsilon; on T \ U, and q_n > 1 - &epsilon; on ` V ` . We use y to represent the final q_n in the paper (the one with n large enough), ` N ` to represent ` n ` in the paper, ` K ` to represent ` k ` , ` D ` to represent &delta;, ` E ` to represent &epsilon;, and ` P ` to represent ` p ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem45 {x3 y1 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff y1 t) (A2: class) (D: class x3 y1 t f2 g2) (P: class x3 y1 t) (Q: class x3 t f2 g2) (T: class) (U E: class x3 t f2 g2) (K: class x3 y1 f2 g2) (N: class x3 y1) (V: class x3 t f2 g2):
  $ wnfc t P $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq V (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) T) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt t T (co (co c1 (co (cfv (cv t) P) N cexp) cmin) (co K N cexp) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr D (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (co c1 (co (co (co K D cmul) c2 cdiv) N cexp) cmin) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 (co (co K D cmul) N cexp) cdiv) E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) clt) V) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) E clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that sets U(t) as defined in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90, are a cover of T \ U. Using this lemma, in a later theorem we will prove that a finite subcover exists. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem46 {x3 w t f2 g2 h r q: setvar} (ph: wff w t h q) (A2: class w) (Q: class x3 t h r q) (T: class) (U: class w t h) (J: class x3 f2 g2 h r q) (K: class x3 w f2 g2 h r q) (W: class x3 w t f2 h r q) (Z: class w r):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnfc h Q $ >
  $ wnf q ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wrex h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q) J) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co J K ccn)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cdif T U) (cuni W)) $;

--| Subtracting a constant from a real continuous function gives another continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem47 {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (C S: class t) (T: class) (F G: class t) (J K: class):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnfc t S $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq G (cxp T (csn (cneg S))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ctop) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) S cmin)) C) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove that ` x ` built as in Lemma 2 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91, is such that x < &epsilon; on ` A ` . Here ` X ` is used to represent ` x ` in the paper, ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper, and ` D ` is used to represent ` A ` in the paper (because ` A ` is always used to represent the subalgebra). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem48 {t f2 g2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff t h i) (A2: class i) (D: class t f2 g2 h) (P: class t f2 g2 h i) (T U: class) (E: class t f2 g2 h) (F: class t h i) (M: class t h) (V: class t f2 g2 h i) (W: class t f2 g2 h) (X: class t f2 g2 h i) (Y: class t h i) (Z: class t f2 g2 h i):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M (cseq P U c1)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt t T (cfv M (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cuni (crn W))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)) E clt) (cfv (cv i) W)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) X) E clt) D) $;

--| There exists a function q_n as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91 (at the top of page 91): 0 <= q_n <= 1 , q_n < &epsilon; on ` T \ U ` , and q_n > 1 - &epsilon; on ` V ` . Here y is used to represent the final q_n in the paper (the one with n large enough), ` N ` represents ` n ` in the paper, ` K ` represents ` k ` , ` D ` represents &delta;, ` E ` represents &epsilon;, and ` P ` represents ` p ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem49 {x3 y1 t f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff y1 t) (A2: class) (D: class y1) (P: class x3 t) (T: class) (U: class x3 t f2 g2) (E: class) (V: class x3 t f2 g2):
  $ wnfc t P $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq V (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf T cr P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr D (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) clt) V) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) E clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that sets U(t) as defined in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90, contain a finite subcover of T \ U. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem50 {x3 w u t f2 g2 h r q: setvar} (ph: wff w t h) (A2: class w u) (C: class x3 w u t f2 g2 h r q) (Q: class x3 u t h r q) (T: class) (U: class w t h) (J: class x3 f2 g2 h r q) (K: class x3 w u f2 g2 h r q) (W: class x3 w t f2 h r q) (Z: class w u r):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wrex h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q) J) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex u (w3a (wcel (cv u) cfn) (wss (cv u) W) (wss (cdif T U) (cuni (cv u))))) $;

--| There exists a function x as in the proof of Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91. Here ` D ` is used to represent ` A ` in the paper, because here ` A ` is used for the subalgebra of functions. ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem51 {x3 w t f2 g2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff x3 w t h i) (A2: class w i) (B2 D: class w t f2 g2 h) (P: class x3 w t f2 g2 h i) (T: class) (U: class x3 w) (E: class w t f2 g2 h) (F: class x3 w t h i) (M: class x3 w) (V: class x3 w t f2 g2 h i) (W: class x3 t f2 g2 h) (X: class w t f2 g2 h i) (Y: class x3 w t h i) (Z: class x3 w t f2 g2 h i):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wnfc w V $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv M (cseq P U c1)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt t T (cfv M (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv w) V)) (wss (cv w) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cuni (crn W))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)) (co E M cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv i) W)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co c1 M cfz))) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E M cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) U)) clt) B2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) D) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) B2)))) $;

--| There exists a neighborhood V as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90. Here Z is used to represent t_0 in the paper, and v is used to represent V in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem52 {x3 v t e f2 g2 a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 v t) (A2: class e) (C: class x3 v t e f2 g2 a) (D: class x3 v e) (P: class x3 v t e a) (T: class e) (U: class t e) (J: class x3 t e f2 g2 a) (K: class x3 v t e f2 g2 a) (V: class t) (Z: class x3 e f2 g2 a):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnfc t P $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq V (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) T) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv a)) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr D c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) P) c1 cle)) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z P) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr D (cfv (cv t) P) cle) (cdif T U)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex v (wa (wa (wcel Z (cv v)) (wss (cv v) U)) (wral e (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) (cv e) clt) (cv v)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp)) J) $;

--| This lemma is used to prove the existence of a function ` p ` as in Lemma 1 of [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: ` p ` is in the subalgebra, such that ` 0 <_ p <_ 1 ` , p__(t_0) = 0, and ` 0 < p ` on ` T \ U ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem53 {x3 w t f2 g2 h r q p: setvar} (ph: wff t p) (A2: class w) (C: class x3 w t f2 g2 h r q p) (Q: class t h r p) (T: class) (U: class t) (J: class x3 f2 g2 r q p) (K: class x3 w f2 g2 h r q p) (W: class x3 w t f2 h r q p) (Z: class w r):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wrex h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q) J) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cdif T U) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex p (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) c1 cle)) T) (wceq (cfv Z (cv p)) cc0) (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| There exists a function ` x ` as in the proof of Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91. Here ` D ` is used to represent ` A ` in the paper, because here ` A ` is used for the subalgebra of functions. ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon; in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem54 {x3 y1 w t e f2 g2 h i: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 w t e h i) (A2: class w e i) (B2: class t e h) (D: class w t e f2 g2 h) (P: class y1 w t e f2 g2 h i) (T: class e) (U: class x3 y1 w t e f2 g2 h i) (E: class t e h) (F: class x3 y1 w t e h i) (J: class x3 y1 w t e f2 g2 h i) (M: class y1 e) (V: class x3 y1 w t e f2 g2 h i) (W Y: class x3 y1 t e h) (Z: class x3 y1 w t e f2 g2 h i):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf w ph $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpo f2 g2 Y Y (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt t T (cmpt i (co c1 M cfz) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt t T (cfv M (cseq cmul (cfv (cv t) F) c1))) $ >
  $ wceq V (crab w (wral e (wrex h (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) (cv e) clt) (cv w)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv f2) A2)) (wf T cr (cv f2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co c1 M cfz) V W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D (cuni (crn W))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex y1 (wa (wf (co c1 M cfz) Y (cv y1)) (wral i (wa (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1))) (co E M cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv i) W)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E M cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1))) clt) B2)) (co c1 M cfz)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cvv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) D) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) B2)) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves the existence of a function p as in the proof of Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90: p is in the subalgebra, such that 0 <= p <= 1, p__(t_0) = 0, and p > 0 on T - U. Here Z is used to represent t_0 in the paper. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem55 {x3 w t f2 g2 h r q p: setvar} (ph: wff t p) (A2: class w) (C: class x3 w t f2 g2 h r q p) (Q: class t h r p) (T: class) (U: class t) (J: class x3 f2 g2 r q p) (K: class x3 w f2 g2 h r q p) (W: class x3 w t f2 h r q p) (Z: class w r):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wceq Q (crab h (wa (wceq (cfv Z (cv h)) cc0) (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T)) A2) $ >
  $ wceq W (crab w (wrex h (wceq (cv w) (crab t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) T)) Q) J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex p (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) c1 cle)) T) (wceq (cfv Z (cv p)) cc0) (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv p)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves Lemma 1 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 90. Here ` Z ` is used to represent t_0 in the paper, ` v ` is used to represent ` V ` in the paper, and ` e ` is used to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem56 {x3 y1 v t e f2 g2 r q: setvar} (ph: wff x3 v t) (A2: class) (C: class x3 y1 v t e f2 g2 r q) (T: class) (U: class t) (J: class x3 y1 e f2 r q) (K: class x3 y1 v e f2 g2 r q) (Z: class x3 r):
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv y1)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex v (wa (wa (wcel Z (cv v)) (wss (cv v) U)) (wral e (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) (cv e) clt) (cv v)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp)) J) $;

--| There exists a function x as in the proof of Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91. In this theorem, it is proven the non-trivial case (the closed set D is nonempty). Here D is used to represent A in the paper, because the variable A is used for the subalgebra of functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem57 {x3 w t e f2 g2 h r q a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t) (A2: class) (B2: class t e h q a) (C: class x3 w t e f2 g2 h r q a) (D: class t) (T: class) (U: class x3 t) (E: class q a) (J: class x3 e f2 r q a) (K: class x3 w e f2 g2 h r q a) (V: class x3 w t e h q a) (Y: class x3 t e h q a):
  $ wnfc t D $ >
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab h (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq V (crab w (wral e (wrex h (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) (cv e) clt) (cv w)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (cv e) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv h)) clt) (cdif T U))) A2) crp) J) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wceq U (cdif T B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv a)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin B2 D) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne D c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) D) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) B2)) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves Lemma 2 in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91. Here D is used to represent the set A of Lemma 2, because here the variable A is used for the subalgebra of functions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem58 {x3 t f2 g2 r q a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 t) (A2: class) (B2: class t q a) (C: class x3 t f2 g2 r q a) (D: class t) (T: class) (U: class x3 t) (E: class q a) (J: class x3 q a) (K: class x3 f2 g2 r q a):
  $ wnfc t D $ >
  $ wnfc t U $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv a) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv a)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv J ccld)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin B2 D) c0) $ >
  $ wceq U (cdif T B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) E clt) D) (wral t (wbr (co c1 E cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv x3)) clt) B2)) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that there exists a function ` x ` as in the proof in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91, after Lemma 2: x_j is in the subalgebra, 0 <= x_j <= 1, x_j < &epsilon; / n on A_j (meaning A in the paper), x_j > 1 - \epsilon / n on B_j. Here ` D ` is used to represent A in the paper (because A is used for the subalgebra of functions), ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem59 {x3 y1 t f2 g2 j r q: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class j) (B2: class t j) (C: class x3 y1 t f2 g2 j r q) (D: class t j) (T: class j) (E: class j q) (F: class x3 y1 t f2 g2 j r q) (H: class y1 t f2 g2 j r q) (J: class x3 y1 j q) (K: class x3 y1 f2 g2 j r q) (N: class) (Y: class y1 t f2 g2 r q):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab t (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (crab y1 (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) c1 cle)) T) A2) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt j (co cc0 N cfz) (crab y1 (wa (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) (co E N cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv j) D)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E N cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cv y1)) clt) (cfv (cv j) B2))) Y)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv y1)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wa (wf (co cc0 N cfz) A2 (cv x3)) (wral j (w3a (wral t (wa (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) (cv x3))) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) (cv x3))) c1 cle)) T) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) (cv x3))) (co E N cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv j) D)) (wral t (wbr (co c1 (co E N cdiv) cmin) (cfv (cv t) (cfv (cv j) (cv x3))) clt) (cfv (cv j) B2))) (co cc0 N cfz)))) $;

--| This lemma proves that there exists a function g as in the proof in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 91 (this parte of the proof actually spans through pages 91-92): g is in the subalgebra, and for all ` t ` in ` T ` , there is a ` j ` such that (j-4/3)*&epsilon; < f(t) <= (j-1/3)*&epsilon; and (j-4/3)*&epsilon; < g(t) < (j+1/3)*&epsilon;. Here ` F ` is used to represent f in the paper, and ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem60 {y1 t f2 g2 j n r q: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (B2: class t j n) (C: class y1 t f2 g2 j n r q) (D: class t j n) (T: class) (E: class q) (F: class y1 t f2 r q) (J: class y1 j n q) (K: class y1 f2 g2 j n r q):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt j (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (crab t (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt j (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (crab t (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv y1)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wral t (wrex j (wa (wa (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) F) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv t) F) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) cle)) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) (co (co (cv j) (co c1 c3 cdiv) caddc) E cmul) clt) (wbr (co (co (cv j) (co c4 c3 cdiv) cmin) E cmul) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) clt))) cr) T) A2) $;

--| This lemma proves that there exists a function ` g ` as in the proof in [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 92: ` g ` is in the subalgebra, and for all ` t ` in ` T ` , abs( f(t) - g(t) ) < 2*&epsilon;. Here ` F ` is used to represent f in the paper, and ` E ` is used to represent &epsilon;. For this lemma there's the further assumption that the function ` F ` to be approximated is nonnegative (this assumption is removed in a later theorem). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweidlem61 {x3 t f2 g2 r q: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (C: class x3 t f2 g2 r q) (T E: class) (F: class t) (J: class x3 q) (K: class x3 f2 g2 r q):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral t (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv t) F) cle) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex g2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) (co c2 E cmul) clt) T) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves the Stone Weierstrass theorem for the non-trivial case in which T is nonempty. The proof follows [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 89 (through page 92). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.) (Revised by AV, 13-Sep-2020.)
theorem stoweidlem62 {x3 t f2 g2 r q: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (C: class x3 t f2 g2 r q) (T E: class) (F: class t r q) (H: class t) (J: class x3 g2 q) (K: class x3 f2 g2 r q):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf f2 ph $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) F) (cinf (crn F) cr clt) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex q (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv q)) (cfv (cv t) (cv q))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne T c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (co c1 c3 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves the Stone-Weierstrass theorem for real-valued functions: let ` J ` be a compact topology on ` T ` , and ` C ` be the set of real continuous functions on ` T ` . Assume that ` A ` is a subalgebra of ` C ` (closed under addition and multiplication of functions) containing constant functions and discriminating points (if ` r ` and ` t ` are distinct points in ` T ` , then there exists a function ` h ` in ` A ` such that h(r) is distinct from h(t) ). Then, for any continuous function ` F ` and for any positive real ` E ` , there exists a function ` f ` in the subalgebra ` A ` , such that ` f ` approximates ` F ` up to ` E ` ( ` E ` represents the usual &epsilon; value). As a classical example, given any a, b reals, the closed interval ` T = [ a , b ] ` could be taken, along with the subalgebra ` A ` of real polynomials on ` T ` , and then use this theorem to easily prove that real polynomials are dense in the standard metric space of continuous functions on ` [ a , b ] ` . The proof and lemmas are written following [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 89 (through page 92). Some effort is put in avoiding the use of the axiom of choice. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stoweid {x3 t f2 g2 h r: setvar} (ph: wff t) (A2: class) (C: class x3 t f2 g2 h r) (T E: class) (F: class t) (J: class x3 g2 h) (K: class x3 f2 g2 h r):
  $ wnfc t F $ >
  $ wnf t ph $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J ccmp) $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t)))) (wrex h (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv h)) (cfv (cv t) (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex f2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) A2) $;

--| This theorem proves the Stone-Weierstrass theorem for real-valued functions: let ` J ` be a compact topology on ` T ` , and ` C ` be the set of real continuous functions on ` T ` . Assume that ` A ` is a subalgebra of ` C ` (closed under addition and multiplication of functions) containing constant functions and discriminating points (if ` r ` and ` t ` are distinct points in ` T ` , then there exists a function ` h ` in ` A ` such that h(r) is distinct from h(t) ). Then, for any continuous function ` F ` and for any positive real ` E ` , there exists a function ` f ` in the subalgebra ` A ` , such that ` f ` approximates ` F ` up to ` E ` ( ` E ` represents the usual &epsilon; value). As a classical example, given any a, b reals, the closed interval ` T = [ a , b ] ` could be taken, along with the subalgebra ` A ` of real polynomials on ` T ` , and then use this theorem to easily prove that real polynomials are dense in the standard metric space of continuous functions on ` [ a , b ] ` . The proof and lemmas are written following [BrosowskiDeutsh] p. 89 (through page 92). Some effort is put in avoiding the use of the axiom of choice. The deduction version of this theorem is ~ stoweid : often times it will be better to use ~ stoweid in other proofs (but this version is probably easier to be read and understood). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 20-Apr-2017.)
theorem stowei {x3 t f2 g2 h r: setvar} (A2: class) (C: class x3 t f2 g2 h r) (T E F: class) (J: class x3 g2 h) (K: class x3 f2 g2 h r):
  $ wceq K (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wcel J ccmp $ >
  $ wceq T (cuni J) $ >
  $ wceq C (co J K ccn) $ >
  $ wss A2 C $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) caddc)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv f2) A2) (wcel (cv g2) A2)) (wcel (cmpt t T (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) (cv g2)) cmul)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wcel (cmpt t T (cv x3)) A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv r) T) (wcel (cv t) T) (wne (cv r) (cv t))) (wrex h (wne (cfv (cv r) (cv h)) (cfv (cv t) (cv h))) A2) $ >
  $ wcel F C $ >
  $ wcel E crp $ >
  $ wrex f2 (wral t (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv t) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv t) F) cmin) cabs) E clt) T) A2 $;

--| ` I ` is monotone: increasing the exponent, the integral decreases. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispilem1 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (I: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co N c1 caddc) I) (cfv N I) cle) $;

--| A first set of properties for the sequence ` I ` that will be used in the proof of the Wallis product formula. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispilem2 {x3 n: setvar} (I: class x3 n) (N: class):
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ w3a (wceq (cfv cc0 I) cpi) (wceq (cfv c1 I) c2) (wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv N I) (co (co (co N c1 cmin) N cdiv) (cfv (co N c2 cmin) I) cmul))) $;

--| I maps to real values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispilem3 {x3 n: setvar} (I N: class x3 n):
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N I) crp) $;

--| ` F ` maps to explicit expression for the ratio of two consecutive values of ` I ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispilem4 {z k n: setvar} (F: class k n) (G H I: class z k n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg z (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv z) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) I) (cfv (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) I) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) (cseq cmul F c1)) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq G H $;

--| The sequence ` H ` converges to 1. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispilem5 {x3 k n: setvar} (F: class k n) (G: class x3 n) (H I: class x3 k n) (L: class x3 n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt n cn0 (citg x3 (co cc0 cpi cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) csin) (cv n) cexp))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) I) (cfv (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) I) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (co (co cpi c2 cdiv) (co c1 (cfv (cv n) (cseq cmul F c1)) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt n cn (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cv n) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr H c1 cli $;

--| Wallis' formula for &pi; : Wallis' product converges to &pi; / 2 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispi {k n: setvar} (F: class k) (W: class k n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cmpt n cn (cfv (cv n) (cseq cmul F c1))) $ >
  $ wbr W (co cpi c2 cdiv) cli $;

--| An intermediate step between the first version of the Wallis' formula for &pi; and the second version of Wallis' formula. This second version will then be used to prove Stirling's approximation formula for the factorial. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispi2lem1 {k: setvar} (N: class k):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N (cseq cmul (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmul)) c1)) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (cfv N (cseq cmul (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c4 cexp) (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cmul) c2 cexp) cdiv)) c1)) cmul)) $;

--| Two expressions are proven to be equal, and this is used to complete the proof of the second version of Wallis' formula for &pi; . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispi2lem2 {k: setvar} (N: class k):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N (cseq cmul (cmpt k cn (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c4 cexp) (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 cmin) cmul) c2 cexp) cdiv)) c1)) (co (co (co c2 (co c4 N cmul) cexp) (co (cfv N cfa) c4 cexp) cmul) (co (cfv (co c2 N cmul) cfa) c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| An alternative version of Wallis' formula for &pi; ; this second formula uses factorials and it is later used to prove Stirling's approximation formula. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem wallispi2 {n: setvar} (V: class n):
  $ wceq V (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c2 (co c4 (cv n) cmul) cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) c4 cexp) cmul) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) cfa) c2 cexp) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr V (co cpi c2 cdiv) cli $;

--| A simple limit of fractions is computed. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 30-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem1 {n: setvar} (F G H L: class n):
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (co (co (cv n) c2 cexp) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt n cn (co c1 (cv n) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wbr H (co c1 c2 cdiv) cli $;

--| ` A ` maps to positive reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem2 {n: setvar} (A2 N: class n):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (cfv N A2) crp) $;

--| Long but simple algebraic transformations are applied to show that ` V ` , the Wallis formula for &pi; , can be expressed in terms of ` A ` , the Stirling's approximation formula for the factorial, up to a constant factor. This will allow (in a later theorem) to determine the right constant factor to be put into the ` A ` , in order to get the exact Stirling's formula. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem3 {n: setvar} (A2 D E V: class n):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c2 (co c4 (cv n) cmul) cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) c4 cexp) cmul) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) cfa) c2 cexp) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (cfv (cv n) A2) c4 cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) D) c2 cexp) cdiv) (co (co (cv n) c2 cexp) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) cdiv) cmul)) $;

--| Algebraic manipulation of ` ( ( B ` n ) - ( B ` ( n + 1 ) ) ) ` . It will be used in other theorems to show that ` B ` is decreasing. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem4 {n: setvar} (A2 B2 J: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c1 (co c2 (cv n) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv n) cdiv) clog) cmul) c1 cmin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co (cfv N B2) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) B2) cmin) (cfv N J)) $;

--| If ` T ` is between ` 0 ` and ` 1 ` , then a series (without alternating negative and positive terms) is given that converges to log((1+T)/(1-T)). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem5 {j: setvar} (ph: wff) (D: class j) (T: class) (E F G H: class j):
  $ wceq D (cmpt j cn (co (co (cneg c1) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co T (cv j) cexp) (cv j) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt j cn (co (co T (cv j) cexp) (cv j) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt j cn (co (co (co (cneg c1) (co (cv j) c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co T (cv j) cexp) (cv j) cdiv) cmul) (co (co T (cv j) cexp) (cv j) cdiv) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt j cn0 (co c2 (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv j) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co T (co (co c2 (cv j) cmul) c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt j cn0 (co (co c2 (cv j) cmul) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv T cabs) c1 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc H cc0) (cfv (co (co c1 T caddc) (co c1 T cmin) cdiv) clog) cli) $;

--| A series that converges to ` log ( ( N + 1 ) / N ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem6 {j: setvar} (H: class j) (N: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt j cn0 (co c2 (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv j) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv j) cmul) c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cseq caddc H cc0) (cfv (co (co N c1 caddc) N cdiv) clog) cli) $;

--| Algebraic manipulation of the formula for J(n). (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem7 {k n: setvar} (H: class k) (J: class k n) (K: class k) (N: class):
  $ wceq J (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c1 (co c2 (cv n) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv n) cdiv) clog) cmul) c1 cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt k cn (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co c2 (cv k) cmul) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt k cn0 (co c2 (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cseq caddc K c1) (cfv N J) cli) $;

--| If ` A ` converges to ` C ` , then ` F ` converges to C^2 . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem8 {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 C D F L M: class n):
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wnfc n A2 $ >
  $ wnfc n D $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn crp A2) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (cfv (cv n) A2) c4 cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) D) c2 cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) A2) c4 cexp)) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) D) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cfv (cv n) D) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F (co C c2 cexp) cli) $;

--| ` ( ( B `` N ) - ( B `` ( N + 1 ) ) ) ` is expressed as a limit of a series. This result will be used both to prove that ` B ` is decreasing and to prove that ` B ` is bounded (below). It will follow that ` B ` converges in the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem9 {k n: setvar} (A2 B2 J: class k n) (K: class k) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c1 (co c2 (cv n) cmul) caddc) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cv n) cdiv) clog) cmul) c1 cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt k cn (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co c2 (cv k) cmul) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cseq caddc K c1) (co (cfv N B2) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) B2) cmin) cli) $;

--| A bound for any B(N)-B(N + 1) that will allow to find a lower bound for the whole ` B ` sequence. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem10 {k n: setvar} (A2 B2: class k n) (K L: class k) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt k cn (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co c2 (cv k) cmul) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt k cn (co (co c1 (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) c2 cexp) cdiv) (cv k) cexp)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (co (cfv N B2) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) B2) cmin) (co (co c1 c4 cdiv) (co c1 (co N (co N c1 caddc) cmul) cdiv) cmul) cle) $;

--| ` B ` is decreasing. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem11 {k n: setvar} (A2 B2: class k n) (K: class k) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt k cn (co (co c1 (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co (co c1 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv) (co c2 (cv k) cmul) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (cfv (co N c1 caddc) B2) (cfv N B2) clt) $;

--| The sequence ` B ` is bounded below. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem12 {n: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class n) (N: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co c1 (co (cv n) (co (cv n) c1 caddc) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr (co (cfv c1 B2) (co c1 c4 cdiv) cmin) (cfv N B2) cle) $;

--| ` B ` is decreasing and has a lower bound, then it converges. Since ` B ` is ` log A ` , in another theorem it is proven that ` A ` converges as well. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem13 {n d: setvar} (A2: class n d) (B2: class n):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wrex d (wbr B2 (cv d) cli) cr $;

--| The sequence ` A ` converges to a positive real. This proves that the Stirling's formula converges to the factorial, up to a constant. In another theorem, using Wallis' formula for &pi;& , such constant is exactly determined, thus proving the Stirling's formula. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem14 {n c: setvar} (A2: class n) (B2: class n c):
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (cfv (cfv (cv n) A2) clog)) $ >
  $ wrex c (wbr A2 (cv c) cli) crp $;

--| The Stirling's formula is proven using a number of local definitions. The main theorem ~ stirling will use this final lemma, but it will not expose the local definitions. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlinglem15 {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2: class n) (C: class) (D S E F H V: class n):
  $ wnf n ph $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt n cn (co (co (co (co c2 (co c4 (cv n) cmul) cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) c4 cexp) cmul) (co (cfv (co c2 (cv n) cmul) cfa) c2 cexp) cdiv) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn (co (co (cfv (cv n) A2) c4 cexp) (co (cfv (cv n) D) c2 cexp) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt n cn (co (co (cv n) c2 cexp) (co (cv n) (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (cfv (cv n) S) cdiv)) c1 cli) $;

--| Stirling's approximation formula for ` n ` factorial. The proof follows two major steps: first it is proven that ` S ` and ` n ` factorial are asymptotically equivalent, up to an unknown constant. Then, using Wallis' formula for &pi; it is proven that the unknown constant is the square root of &pi; and then the exact Stirling's formula is established. This is Metamath 100 proof #90. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirling {n: setvar} (S: class n):
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (cfv (cv n) S) cdiv)) c1 cli $;

--| Stirling's approximation formula for ` n ` factorial: here convergence is expressed with respect to the standard topology on the reals. The main theorem ~ stirling is proven for convergence in the topology of complex numbers. The variable ` R ` is used to denote convergence with respect to the standard topology on the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem stirlingr {n: setvar} (R S: class n):
  $ wceq S (cmpt n cn0 (co (cfv (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cv n) cmul) csqrt) (co (co (cv n) ceu cdiv) (cv n) cexp) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) clm) $ >
  $ wbr (cmpt n cn (co (cfv (cv n) cfa) (cfv (cv n) S) cdiv)) c1 R $;

--| The N_th Dirichlet kernel. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerval {n s1: setvar} (D: class n s1) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N D) (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $;

--| The Dirichlet kernel value is a real if the argument is not a multiple of &pi; . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirker2re (S N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel S cr)) (wn (wceq (co S (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0))) (wcel (co (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) S cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co S c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cr) $;

--| The Dirichlet kernel denominator is never ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerdenne0 (S: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cr) (wn (wceq (co S (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0))) (wne (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co S c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cc0) $;

--| The N_th Dirichlet kernel evaluated at a specific point ` S ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerval2 {n s1: setvar} (D S: class n s1) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel S cr)) (wceq (cfv S (cfv N D)) (cif (wceq (co S (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) S cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co S c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $;

--| The Dirichlet kernel at any point evaluates to a real. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerre {n s1: setvar} (D S: class n s1) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel S cr)) (wcel (cfv S (cfv N D)) cr) $;

--| the Dirichlet kernel has period ` 2 _pi ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerper {y1 n: setvar} (x3: setvar) (D T: class y1 n) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co c2 cpi cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) (cfv N D)) (cfv (cv x3) (cfv N D))) $;

--| For any natural number ` N ` , the Dirichlet kernel ` ( D `` N ) ` is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkerf {y1 n: setvar} (D: class y1 n) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wf cr cr (cfv N D)) $;

--| Sum of an even number of alternating cos values. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkertrigeqlem1 {n: setvar} (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wceq (csu n (co c1 (co c2 K cmul) cfz) (cfv (co (cv n) cpi cmul) ccos)) cc0) $;

--| Trigonometric equality lemma for the Dirichlet kernel trigonometric equality. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkertrigeqlem2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 csin) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (csu n (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co (cv n) A2 cmul) ccos)) caddc) cpi cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) A2 cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Trigonometric equality lemma for the Dirichlet kernel trigonometric equality. Here we handle the case for an angle that's an odd multiple of ` _pi ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkertrigeqlem3 {n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 K: class n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co (co c2 K cmul) c1 caddc) cpi cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (csu n (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co (cv n) A2 cmul) ccos)) caddc) cpi cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) A2 cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Trigonometric equality for the Dirichlet kernel. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkertrigeq {k n s1: setvar} (ph: wff n) (D F H: class k n s1) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv N D) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 cr (co (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (csu k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co (cv k) (cv s1) cmul) ccos)) caddc) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F H) $;

--| Trigonometric equality for the Dirichlet kernel. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkertrigeq_b {k s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (D F H: class k s1) (N: class):
  $ wceq D (cmpt k cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv k) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv k) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv N D) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 cr (co (co (co c1 c2 cdiv) (csu k (co c1 N cfz) (cfv (co (cv k) (cv s1) cmul) ccos)) caddc) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F H) $;

--| The definite integral of the Dirichlet kernel. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkeritg {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff x3 n) (A2 B2: class n) (D: class x3 k n) (F: class k n) (G: class x3 k n) (N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt x3 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv x3) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv x3) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv N D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (co (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) (csu k (co c1 N cfz) (co (cfv (co (cv k) (cv x3) cmul) csin) (cv k) cdiv)) caddc) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (co (cfv B2 G) (cfv A2 G) cmin)) $;

--| If ` Y ` is a multiple of ` _pi ` then it belongs to an open inerval ` ( A (,) B ) ` such that for any other point ` y ` in the interval, cos y/2 and sin y/2 are nonzero. Such an interval is needed to apply De L'Hopital theorem. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkercncflem1 {y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class y1) (Y: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co Y cpi cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co Y cpi caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Y (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wral y1 (wa (wne (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cc0) (wne (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) ccos) cc0)) (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)))) $;

--| Lemma used to prove that the Dirichlet kernel is continuous at ` Y ` points that are multiples of ` ( 2 x. _pi ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkercncflem2 {y1 w n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class n) (D: class w n) (F G H I: class n) (L: class w n) (N Y: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)))) (wne (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt y1 (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)) (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) ccos) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt y1 (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)) (co cpi (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) ccos) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt w (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv w) cmul) ccos) cmul) (co cpi (cfv (co (cv w) c2 cdiv) ccos) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Y (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv y1) (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y)))) (wne (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) ccos) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv Y (cfv N D)) (co (cres (cfv N D) (cdif (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn Y))) Y climc)) $;

--| The Dirichlet kernel is continuous at ` Y ` points that are multiples of ` ( 2 x. _pi ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkercncflem3 {y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2 D: class n) (F G: class y1 n) (N Y: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co Y cpi cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co Y cpi caddc) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Y (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv Y (cfv N D)) (co (cfv N D) Y climc)) $;

--| The Dirichlet kernel is continuos at points that are not multiple of 2 &pi; . This is the easier condition, for the proof of the continuity of the Dirichlet kernel. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkercncflem4 {y1 n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 B2: class y1 n) (C D E N Y: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co Y (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv (co Y (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) cfl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co A2 c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq C (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) $ >
  $ wceq E (co B2 (co c2 cpi cmul) cmul) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv N D) (cfv Y (co (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) ccnp))) $;

--| For any natural number ` N ` , the Dirichlet kernel ` ( D `` N ) ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem dirkercncf {y1 n: setvar} (D N: class n):
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt y1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv y1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv y1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (cfv N D) (co cr cr ccncf)) $;

--| A partition interval is a subset of the partitioned interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 Q I M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| Membership in a partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem2 {i m p: setvar} (A2 B2: class i) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wb (wcel Q (cfv M P)) (wa (wcel Q (co cr (co cc0 M cfz) cmap)) (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 Q) A2) (wceq (cfv M Q) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) clt) (co cc0 M cfzo))))) $;

--| Membership in a partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem3 {i m p: setvar} (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wb (wcel Q (cfv M P)) (wa (wcel Q (co (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co cc0 M cfz) cmap)) (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 Q) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv M Q) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) clt) (co cc0 M cfzo))))) $;

--| ` E ` is a function that maps any point to a periodic corresponding point in ` ( A , B ] ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T: class) (E: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr (co A2 B2 cioc) E) $;

--| ` S ` is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem5 {x3: setvar} (S: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq S (cmpt x3 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co X (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv x3) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X cr) (wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr S) $;

--| ` X ` is in the periodic partition, when the considered interval is centered at ` X ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem6 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T I J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr I J clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (co I T cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (co J T cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq J (co I c1 caddc)) $;

--| The difference between the periodic sawtooth function and the identity function is decreasing. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem7 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 T: class) (E: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr X Y cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv Y E) Y cmin) (co (cfv X E) X cmin) cle) $;

--| A partition interval is a subset of the partitioned interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem8 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 Q I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| ` H ` is a complex function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem9 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (F H W X Y: class s1):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr H) $;

--| Condition on the bounds of a nonempty subinterval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem10 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co C D cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 cle)) $;

--| If there is a partition, than the lower bound is strictly less than the upper bound. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem11 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| A point of a partition is not an element of any open interval determined by the partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem12 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class) (X: class i m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wn (wcel X (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) $;

--| Value of ` V ` in terms of value of ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem13 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P Q: class i m p) (I: class m p) (M: class) (V: class m) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co A2 X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co B2 X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cfv I Q) (co (cfv I V) X cmin)) (wceq (cfv I V) (co X (cfv I Q) caddc))) $;

--| Given the partition ` V ` , ` Q ` is the partition shifted to the left by ` X ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem14 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class) (O: class i m p) (V: class m) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co A2 X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co B2 X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M O)) $;

--| The range of the partition is between its starting point and its ending point. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem15 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) Q) $;

--| The coefficients of the fourier series are integrable and reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem16 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 n) (C F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cibl) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) ccos) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wcel (cfv N A2) cr) (wcel (cmpt x3 C (cfv (cv x3) F)) cibl)) (wcel (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co N (cv x3) cmul) ccos) cmul)) cr)) $;

--| The defined ` L ` is actually a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem17 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (L: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) A2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioc) (co A2 B2 cicc) L) $;

--| The function ` S ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem18 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S: class s1) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S (co (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr ccncf)) $;

--| If two elements of ` D ` have the same periodic image in ` ( A (,] B ) ` then they are equal. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem19 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff y1 k) (A2 B2: class k) (C D: class x3 y1 k) (T: class y1 k) (E: class x3 y1 k) (W: class y1 k) (X: class x3 k) (Z: class y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq D (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) C) cz) (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioc)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Z E) (cfv W E)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wbr W Z clt)) $;

--| Every interval in the partition ` S ` is included in an interval of the partition ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem20 {i k: setvar} (ph: wff i k) (A2 B2: class i k) (Q S: class) (T: class i k) (I: class k) (J M: class) (N: class i k):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) cr Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv cc0 Q) A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (cfv M Q) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cpr A2 B2) (cin (crn Q) (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) T clt clt S) $ >
  $ wceq I (csup (crab k (wbr (cfv (cv k) Q) (cfv J S) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex i (wss (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $;

--| The coefficients of the fourier series are integrable and reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem21 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 n) (C F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cibl) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wcel (cfv N B2) cr) (wcel (cmpt x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co N (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cibl)) (wcel (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co N (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cr)) $;

--| The coefficients of the fourier series are integrable and reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem22 {x3 n: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 n) (C F: class n):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cibl) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) ccos) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wi (wcel (cv n) cn0) (wcel (cfv (cv n) A2) cr)) (wi (wcel (cv n) cn) (wcel (cfv (cv n) B2) cr))) $;

--| If ` F ` is continuous and ` X ` is constant, then ` ( F `` ( X + s ) ) ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem23 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co A2 cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) B2)) (wcel (co X (cv s1) caddc) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt s1 B2 (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F)) (co B2 cc ccncf)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for module being nonzero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem24 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (csn cc0))) (wne (co A2 (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) $;

--| If ` C ` is not in the range of the partition, then it is in an open interval induced by the partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem25 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (C Q I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) cr Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cfv cc0 Q) (cfv M Q) cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel C (crn Q))) $ >
  $ wceq I (csup (crab k (wbr (cfv (cv k) Q) C clt) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wcel C (co (cfv (cv j) Q) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $;

--| Periodic image of a point ` Y ` that's in the period that begins with the point ` X ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem26 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 T: class) (E: class x3) (X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X E) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co X (co X T caddc) cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv Y E) (co A2 (co Y X cmin) caddc)) $;

--| A partition open interval is a subset of the partitioned open interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem27 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 Q I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Derivative of ` ( F `` ( X + s ) ) ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem28 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq D (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo) cr D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F)) cdv) (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) D))) $;

--| Explicit function value for ` K ` applied to ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem29 {s1: setvar} (A2: class) (K: class s1):
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) (wceq (cfv A2 K) (cif (wceq A2 cc0) c1 (co A2 (co c2 (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $;

--| Sum of three small pieces is less than &epsilon;. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem30 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class x3) (R: class) (E F G: class x3) (I: class) (X Y Z: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 I (co F (cneg G) cmul)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel F cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv A2 cabs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv (citg x3 I (co F (cneg G) cmul)) cabs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (co (co X Y caddc) Z caddc) E cdiv) c1 caddc) R cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv D cabs) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (co (co A2 (cneg (co B2 R cdiv)) cmul) (co C (cneg (co D R cdiv)) cmul) cmin) (citg x3 I (co F (cneg (co G R cdiv)) cmul)) cmin) cabs) E clt) $;

--| If ` A ` is finite and for any element in ` A ` there is a number ` m ` such that a property holds for all numbers larger than ` m ` , then there is a number ` n ` such that the property holds for all numbers larger than ` n ` and for all elements in ` A ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 29-Sep-2020.)
theorem fourierdlem31 {i m n r: setvar} (ph: wff i m n r) (ch: wff i r) (A2: class) (M: class i m n r) (N: class i m r) (V: class i m n r):
  $ wnf i ph $ >
  $ wnf r ph $ >
  $ wnfc i V $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wrex m (wral r ch (co (cv m) cpnf cioo)) cn) A2) $ >
  $ wceq M (crab m (wral r ch (co (cv m) cpnf cioo)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt i A2 (cinf M cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq N (csup (crn V) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex n (wral r (wral i ch A2) (co (cv n) cpnf cioo)) cn) $;

--| Limit of a continuous function on an open subinterval. Lower bound version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem32 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R F J Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co C D cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cif (wceq C A2) R (cfv C F)) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) (co A2 B2 cico) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co C D cioo)) C climc)) $;

--| Limit of a continuous function on an open subinterval. Upper bound version. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem33 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F J L Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co C D cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cif (wceq D B2) L (cfv D F)) $ >
  $ wceq J (co (cfv ccnfld ctopn) (cun (co A2 B2 cioo) (csn B2)) crest) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co C D cioo)) D climc)) $;

--| A partition is one to one. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem34 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 (co cc0 M cfz) cr Q) $;

--| There is a single point in ` ( A (,] B ) ` that's distant from ` X ` a multiple integer of ` T ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem35 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 T I J X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (co I T cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co X (co J T cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq I J) $;

--| ` F ` is an isomorphism. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem36 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) A2 clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv A2 chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) A2 clt clt F) $;

--| ` I ` is a function that maps any real point to the point that in the partition that immediately precedes the corresponding periodic point in the interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem37 {x3 y1 i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class i) (P: class x3 y1 i m p) (Q: class x3 y1 m) (T: class y1 i m p) (E: class x3 m p) (I: class x3 y1 i m p) (L: class x3 y1 m p) (M: class y1):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) L) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wf cr (co cc0 M cfzo) I) (wi (wcel (cv x3) cr) (wcel (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) L) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt) (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) L) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo))))) $;

--| The function ` F ` is continuous on every interval induced by the partition ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem38 {i n p: setvar} (ph: wff n p) (A2 P: class i n p) (Q: class n) (F: class n p) (H: class i n p) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cdm F) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt n cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun A2 (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cdm F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn Q) H) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $;

--| Integration by parts of ` S. ( A (,) B ) ( ( F `` x ) x. ( sin `` ( R x. x ) ) ) _d x ` (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem39 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (co A2 B2 cicc) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) G) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (co A2 B2 cioo)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co R (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) (co (co (co (cfv B2 F) (cneg (co (cfv (co R B2 cmul) ccos) R cdiv)) cmul) (co (cfv A2 F) (cneg (co (cfv (co R A2 cmul) ccos) R cdiv)) cmul) cmin) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) G) (cneg (co (cfv (co R (cv x3) cmul) ccos) R cdiv)) cmul)) cmin)) $;

--| ` H ` is a continuous function on any partition interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem40 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F: class) (H: class s1) (W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioo)) (co (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres H (co A2 B2 cioo)) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $;

--| Lemma used to prove that every real is a limit point for the domain of the derivative of the periodic function to be approximated. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem41 {x3 y1 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2: class y1 i k) (B2: class) (D: class m p) (P: class x3 y1 i k m p) (Q: class x3 m) (T: class i m p) (E: class x3 m p) (M: class x3) (X: class m p) (Z: class i m p):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt x3 cr (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) Z) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wrex y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) X clt) (wss (co (cv y1) X cioo) D)) cr) (wrex y1 (wa (wbr X (cv y1) clt) (wss (co X (cv y1) cioo) D)) cr)) $;

--| The set of points in a moved partition are finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 29-Sep-2020.)
theorem fourierdlem42 {x3 j k a b: setvar} (ph: wff j k) (ps: wff x3 a b) (A2: class) (B2 C D R: class j k a b) (T: class) (E: class x3) (H: class j k) (I: class j k a b) (J: class x3 j k) (K: class j k) (X Y: class j k a b):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co C B2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co B2 C cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C A2) $ >
  $ wceq D (ccom cabs cmin) $ >
  $ wceq I (cdif (cxp A2 A2) cid) $ >
  $ wceq R (crn (cres D I)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cinf R cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv (crn cioo) ctg) $ >
  $ wceq K (co J (co X Y cicc) crest) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab x3 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) A2) cz) (co X Y cicc)) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv a) cr) (wcel (cv b) cr) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt))) (wrex j (wrex k (wa (wcel (co (cv a) (co (cv j) T cmul) caddc) A2) (wcel (co (cv b) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) A2)) cz) cz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cfn) $;

--| ` K ` is a real function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem43 {s1: setvar} (K: class s1):
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr K $;

--| A condition for having ` ( sin `` ( A / 2 ) ) ` nonzero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem44 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) (wne A2 cc0)) (wne (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) csin) cc0) $;

--| The function ` F ` has a limit at the bounds of every interval induced by the partition ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem46 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3) (Q F: class) (H: class x3) (I: class) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co (cdm F) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cico) (cdm F)))) (wne (co (cres F (co (cv x3) cpnf cioo)) (cv x3) climc) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioc) (cdm F)))) (wne (co (cres F (co cmnf (cv x3) cioo)) (cv x3) climc) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso (co cc0 M cfz) H clt clt Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) H Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cioo) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (ctp (cneg cpi) cpi C) (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cdm F))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn Q) H) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wne (co (cres F (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv I Q) climc) c0) (wne (co (cres F (co (cfv I Q) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) Q) climc) c0)) $;

--| For ` r ` large enough, the final expression is less than the given positive ` E ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem47 {x3 m r: setvar} (ph: wff m) (A2 B2 C D E F G: class x3 r) (I: class r) (M: class) (X Y Z: class x3 m r):
  $ wi ph (wcel (cmpt x3 I F) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv r) cr)) (wcel (cmpt x3 I (co F (cneg G) cmul)) cibl) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel F cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel (cv r) cc)) (wcel G cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) I)) (wcel (cv r) cr)) (wbr (cfv G cabs) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv A2 cabs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv C cabs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (citg x3 I (cfv F cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv r) cc)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv r) cr)) (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv r) cc)) (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv r) cr)) (wbr (cfv D cabs) c1 cle) $ >
  $ wceq M (co (cfv (co (co (co (co X Y caddc) Z caddc) E cdiv) c1 caddc) cfl) c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex m (wral r (wbr (cfv (co (co (co A2 (cneg (co B2 (cv r) cdiv)) cmul) (co C (cneg (co D (cv r) cdiv)) cmul) cmin) (citg x3 I (co F (cneg (co G (cv r) cdiv)) cmul)) cmin) cabs) E clt) (co (cv m) cpnf cioo)) cn) $;

--| The given periodic function ` F ` has a right limit at every point in the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem48 {x3 y1 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (ch: wff y1 i k m p) (A2: class y1 k) (B2: class y1) (D: class y1 i m p) (P: class x3 y1 i k m p) (Q: class m) (R: class x3 y1 i k m p) (T: class m p) (E: class x3 m p) (F: class m p) (M: class x3) (X: class m p) (Z: class y1 i k m p):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt x3 cr (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) Z) caddc)) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cv y1) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cico))) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cv y1) (co X (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc) c0) $;

--| The given periodic function ` F ` has a left limit at every point in the reals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem49 {x3 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2: class k) (B2: class) (D: class i m p) (P: class x3 i k m p) (Q: class m) (T: class i m p) (E F: class m p) (L: class x3 i k m p) (M: class) (X: class m p) (Z: class i m p):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf D cr F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) D) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) D) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt x3 cr (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) Z) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (co (cres F (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc) c0) $;

--| Continuity of ` O ` and its limits with respect to the ` S ` partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem50 {f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (ch: wff f2 i k m p) (A2 B2 P: class f2 i k m p) (Q: class f2 m p) (S: class m p) (T: class i k m p) (U: class f2 k m p) (J: class f2 m p) (M: class f2) (N: class i k m p) (V: class f2 m) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cpr A2 B2) (cin (crn Q) (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv T chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) T clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq U (crio i (wss (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) (wcel (cv k) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv k) Q) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel U (co cc0 M cfzo)) (wss (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv U Q) (cfv (co U c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) $;

--| ` X ` is in the periodic partition, when the considered interval is centered at ` X ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem51 {x3 y1 f2 k: setvar} (ph: wff y1) (A2 B2 C: class f2) (D: class x3 y1 k) (T: class f2) (E: class y1 f2) (F: class x3 y1 k) (H: class y1 f2 k) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss C (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv X E) C) $ >
  $ wceq D (cun (cpr (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc)) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) C) cz) (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 (co (cfv D chash) c1 cmin) cfz) D clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq H (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) C) cz) (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn F)) $;

--| d16:d17,d18:jca |- ( ph -> ( ( S ` 0 ) <_ A /\ A <_ ( S ` 0 ) ) ) . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem52 {f2: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class f2) (S T N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel T cfn) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv T chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) T clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss T (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wf (co cc0 N cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) S) (wceq (cfv cc0 S) A2)) (wceq (cfv N S) B2)) $;

--| The limit of ` F ( s ) ` at ` ( X + D ) ` is the limit of ` F ( X + s ) ` at ` D ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem53 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class s1) (D F: class) (G: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 A2 (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wcel (co X (cv s1) caddc) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wne (cv s1) D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cres F B2) (co X D caddc) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co G D climc)) $;

--| Given a partition ` Q ` and an arbitrary interval ` [ C , D ] ` , a partition ` S ` on ` [ C , D ] ` is built such that it preserves any periodic function piecewise continuous on ` Q ` will be piecewise continuous on ` S ` , with the same limits. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem54 {x3 f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff x3 m p) (A2 B2: class x3 f2 k) (C D: class f2 i k) (P: class x3 f2 i k m p) (Q: class f2 m) (S: class k m) (T: class f2 m p) (H: class i k m p) (M: class x3 f2 k) (N: class k) (O: class x3 f2 i k m p):
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab x3 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel S (cfv N O))) (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt S)) $;

--| ` U ` is a real function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem55 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (U F H K W X Y: class s1):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr U) $;

--| Derivative of the ` K ` function on an interval not containing ' 0 '. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem56 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (K: class s1):
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wne (cv s1) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cr (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (cv s1) K)) cdv) (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (co (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) (co (co (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) ccos) c2 cdiv) (cv s1) cmul) cmin) (co (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) c2 cexp) cdiv) c2 cdiv))) $;

--| The derivative of ` O ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem57 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (O: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo) cr (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wa (wi ph (wa (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr (co cr O cdv)) (wceq (co cr O cdv) (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv)) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cmul) (co (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) ccos) (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) cmul) cmin) (co (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) c2 cexp) cdiv))))) (wceq (co cr (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul)) cdv) (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) ccos))) $;

--| The derivative of ` K ` is continuous on the given interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem58 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (K: class s1):
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 A2 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cfv (crn cioo) ctg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr K cdv) (co A2 cr ccncf)) $;

--| The derivative of ` H ` is continuous on the given interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem59 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class) (H: class s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv) (co (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (cv s1) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr H cdv) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $;

--| Given a differentiable function ` F ` , with finite limit of the derivative at ` A ` the derived function ` H ` has a limit at ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem60 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D E F G: class) (H: class s1) (N Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm G) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co G B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (co A2 B2 cmin) cc0 cioo) (co (co (cfv (co B2 (cv s1) caddc) F) Y cmin) (cv s1) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt s1 (co (co A2 B2 cmin) cc0 cioo) (co (cfv (co B2 (cv s1) caddc) F) Y cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt s1 (co (co A2 B2 cmin) cc0 cioo) (cv s1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co H cc0 climc)) $;

--| Given a differentiable function ` F ` , with finite limit of the derivative at ` A ` the derived function ` H ` has a limit at ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem61 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 D E F G: class) (H: class s1) (N Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cioo) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cdm G) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co G A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co cc0 (co B2 A2 cmin) cioo) (co (co (cfv (co A2 (cv s1) caddc) F) Y cmin) (cv s1) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt s1 (co cc0 (co B2 A2 cmin) cioo) (co (cfv (co A2 (cv s1) caddc) F) Y cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt s1 (co cc0 (co B2 A2 cmin) cioo) (cv s1)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co H cc0 climc)) $;

--| The function ` K ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem62 {y1: setvar} (K: class y1):
  $ wceq K (cmpt y1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cc0) c1 (co (cv y1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv y1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wcel K (co (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr ccncf) $;

--| The upper bound of intervals in the moved partition are mapped to points that are not greater than the corresponding upper bounds in the original partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem63 {x3 f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2 C D: class f2 k) (P: class x3 f2 i k m p) (Q: class f2 m) (S: class m) (T: class f2 m p) (E: class f2 i m p) (H: class i k m p) (J K: class f2 i m p) (M: class x3 f2 k) (N: class k) (O X: class x3 f2 i k m p) (Y: class f2 i m p):
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab x3 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K (co cc0 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cico)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv Y E) (cfv K Q) clt) $ >
  $ wceq X (co (cfv K Q) (co (cfv Y E) Y cmin) cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) (cfv K Q) cle) $;

--| The partition ` V ` is finer than ` Q ` , when ` Q ` is moved on the same interval where ` V ` lies. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem64 {y1 f2 i j k m p l: setvar} (ph: wff y1 m p l) (A2 B2: class y1 f2 j k l) (C D: class f2 i j k l) (P: class y1 f2 i j k m p l) (Q: class f2 m) (T: class f2 m p) (H: class i k m p l) (I: class f2 m p) (J: class y1 f2 m p) (L: class f2 i m p) (M: class y1 f2 l) (N: class k l) (V: class m):
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq V (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq L (csup (crab k (wbr (co (cfv cc0 Q) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (cfv J V) cle) cz) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq I (csup (crab j (wbr (co (cfv (cv j) Q) (co L T cmul) caddc) (cfv J V) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) (wcel L cz)) (wrex i (wrex l (wss (co (cfv J V) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) V) cioo) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (co (cv l) T cmul) caddc) (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) (co (cv l) T cmul) caddc) cioo)) cz) (co cc0 M cfzo))) $;

--| The distance of two adjacent points in the moved partition is preserved in the original partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem65 {x3 y1 f2 i j k m p: setvar} (ph: wff j m p) (A2 B2: class j) (C D: class j k) (P: class x3 y1 f2 i j k m p) (Q S: class j m) (T E: class f2 j m p) (L: class x3 y1 f2 i j k m p) (M: class x3 y1 f2 j k) (N: class j k) (O: class x3 y1 f2 i j k m p) (Z: class x3 f2 j m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C cpnf cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) (co B2 A2 cmin) cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co (cfv (cv j) S) (co B2 (cfv (cfv (cv j) S) E) cmin) caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wceq (co (cfv (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) E) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv j) S) E) L) cmin) (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cv j) S) cmin)) $;

--| Value of the ` G ` function when the argument is not zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem66 {n s1: setvar} (ph: wff n) (A2 D S U F G H K W X Y: class n s1):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wceq (cfv (cv s1) G) (co cpi (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cfv (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) D)) cmul) cmul)) $;

--| ` G ` is a function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem67 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (S U F G H K: class s1) (N: class) (W X Y: class s1):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cr G) $;

--| The derivative of ` O ` is bounded on the given interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem68 {t s1 b: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C: class t) (D E F: class) (O: class t s1 b) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo) cr (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) F) cabs) D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv t) (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo))) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) (co cr (cres F (co (co X A2 caddc) (co X B2 caddc) cioo)) cdv)) cabs) E cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wceq (cdm (co cr O cdv)) (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wrex b (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) (co cr O cdv)) cabs) (cv b) cle) (cdm (co cr O cdv))) cr)) $;

--| A piecewise continuous function is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem69 {i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (P: class i m p) (Q: class m) (R: class i m p) (F: class m p) (L: class i m p) (M: class):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cibl) $;

--| A piecewise continuous function is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem70 {x3 i s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3 s1) (Q: class) (R: class x3 i) (F I: class) (L: class x3 i) (M: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) cr Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 Q) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M Q) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfzo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) F) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (co A2 B2 cicc)) cr) $;

--| A periodic piecewise continuous function, possibly undefined on a finite set in each periodic interval, is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem71 {x3 y1 i k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class i k) (B2: class i) (Q: class) (R: class y1 i k) (T: class i) (E: class x3 y1 i k) (F: class) (I: class k) (L: class y1 i k) (M: class y1):
  $ wi ph (wss (cdm F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (cdm F) cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) cr Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 Q) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M Q) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdm F))) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cdm F))) (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfzo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (cdm F)) cr) $;

--| The derivative of ` O ` is continuous on the given interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem72 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2 C: class i m p) (P Q: class i m s1 p) (U: class m s1 p) (F: class m p) (H K: class i m p) (M: class s1) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m s1) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres (co cr F cdv) (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv U Q) (cfv (co U c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (cv s1) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co cr O cdv) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $;

--| A version of the Riemann Lebesgue lemma: as ` r ` increases, the integral in ` S ` goes to zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem73 {x3 y1 e i n r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class y1 e i n r) (D: class e i n) (Q: class e) (R: class y1 e i n r) (S: class x3 y1 e i n r) (F: class y1 e r) (G: class e i n r) (L: class y1 e i n r) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) Q) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 Q) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M Q) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres G (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv x3) G) cabs) (cv y1) cle) (cdm G)) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt r crp (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv r) (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul))) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt x3 (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) Q)) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q)) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex n (wral r (wbr (cfv (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cioo) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv r) (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cabs) (cv e) clt) (co (cv n) cpnf cioo)) cn) crp) $;

--| Given a piecewise smooth function ` F ` , the derived function ` H ` has a limit at the upper bound of each interval of the partition ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem74 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 P: class i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R E F: class i m p) (G: class i m s1 p) (H: class i m p) (M: class) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m) (W: class i m p) (X: class) (Y: class i m p):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (co (cres F (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wf (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cr (cres G (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co (cres G (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cif (wceq (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) X) E (co (co R (cif (wbr (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) X clt) W Y) cmin) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel A2 (co (cres H (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $;

--| Given a piecewise smooth function ` F ` , the derived function ` H ` has a limit at the lower bound of each interval of the partition ` Q ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem75 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 P: class i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R E F: class i m p) (G: class i m s1 p) (H: class i m p) (M: class) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m) (W: class i m p) (X: class) (Y: class i m p):
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wf (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc (cres G (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co (cres G (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cif (wceq (cfv (cv i) V) X) E (co (co R (cif (wbr (cfv (cv i) V) X clt) W Y) cmin) (cfv (cv i) Q) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel A2 (co (cres H (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $;

--| Continuity of ` O ` and its limits with respect to the ` S ` partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem76 {f2 i j m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (ch: wff f2 i j m p) (A2 B2 C: class f2 i j m p) (D P: class f2 i j m s1 p) (Q R: class f2 i j m p) (S: class i j m p) (T: class i j m s1 p) (E: class f2 i j m s1 p) (F L: class f2 i j m p) (M: class f2 s1) (N: class i j m s1 p) (O: class f2 i j m s1 p) (V: class f2 m) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (cv s1) cdiv) (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cpr A2 B2) (cin (crn Q) (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv T chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) T clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (co (cif (wceq (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q)) L (cfv (co X (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) caddc) F)) C cmin) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (co c2 (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co (co (cif (wceq (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (cv i) Q)) R (cfv (co X (cfv (cv j) S) caddc) F)) C cmin) (cfv (cv j) S) cdiv) (co (cfv (cv j) S) (co c2 (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) S) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo))) (wa (wa (wcel D (co (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) climc)) (wcel E (co (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv (cv j) S) climc))) (wcel (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) cc ccncf))) $;

--| If ` H ` is bounded, then ` U ` is bounded. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem77 {s1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff b) (U: class s1) (F H: class s1 a b) (K: class a) (W X Y: class s1 a b):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex a (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) H) cabs) (cv a) cle) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) U) cabs) (cv b) cle) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) crp) $;

--| ` G ` is continuous when restricted on an interval not containing ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem78 {s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (S U: class s1) (F: class) (G H K: class s1) (N W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioo)) (co (co (co A2 X caddc) (co B2 X caddc) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres G (co A2 B2 cioo)) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cr ccncf)) $;

--| ` E ` projects every interval of the partition induced by ` S ` on ` H ` into a corresponding interval of the partition induced by ` Q ` on ` [ A , B ] ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem79 {x3 y1 f2 i j k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2: class f2 k) (B2: class j) (C D: class y1 f2 j k) (P: class x3 y1 f2 i j k m p) (Q: class y1 m) (S: class j m) (T: class y1 f2 j m p) (E: class j m p) (H: class y1 i j k m p) (I: class y1 j m p) (L: class y1 f2 j k m p) (M: class y1 f2 k) (N: class j) (O: class x3 y1 f2 i j k m p) (Z: class y1 f2 i j m p):
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D clt) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab x3 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv x3) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq Z (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cif (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cv j) S) cmin) (co (cfv c1 Q) A2 cmin) clt) (co (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cv j) S) cmin) c2 cdiv) (co (co (cfv c1 Q) A2 cmin) c2 cdiv)) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) L) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv j) S) E) L) (cfv (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) E) cioo) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv j) S) I) Q) (cfv (co (cfv (cfv (cv j) S) I) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) $;

--| The derivative of ` O ` is bounded on the given interval. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem80 {z w t j k s1 r b: setvar} (ph: wff t k) (ch: wff z w j k r b) (A2 B2 C: class z w j k) (S: class) (F: class j k) (I: class t j k s1 r b) (N: class t) (O: class t k s1) (X: class z w j k) (Y: class z w t j k r b):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq I (co (co X (cfv (cv j) S) caddc) (co X (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) caddc) cioo) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wrex w (wral t (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) F) cabs) (cv w) cle) I) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wrex z (wral t (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) (co cr (cres F I) cdv)) cabs) (cv z) cle) I) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 N cfz) (co A2 B2 cicc) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wbr (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wss (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv r) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wn (wcel (cv r) (crn S)))) (wrex k (wcel (cv r) (co (cfv (cv k) S) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wf I cr (co cr (cres F I) cdv)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cmpt s1 (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wcel (cv w) cr)) (wcel (cv z) cr)) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) F) cabs) (cv w) cle) I)) (wral t (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv t) (co cr (cres F I) cdv)) cabs) (cv z) cle) I)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex b (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) (co cr O cdv)) cabs) (cv b) cle) (cdm (co cr O cdv))) cr) $;

--| The integral of a piecewise continuous periodic function ` F ` is unchanged if the domain is shifted by its period ` T ` . In this lemma, ` T ` is assumed to be strictly positive. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem81 {x3 i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (P: class x3 i m p) (Q: class m) (R: class i m p) (S T F: class m p) (G: class i m p) (H: class x3 i m p) (L: class i m p) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) T caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv i) Q)) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q)) L (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 (co (cfv (cv i) S) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) S) cicc) (cfv (co (cv x3) T cmin) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| Integral by substitution, adding a constant to the function's argument, for a function on an open interval with finite limits ad boundary points. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem82 {x3 t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (R F: class t) (G: class x3) (L: class t) (X: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cif (wceq (cv x3) A2) R (cif (wceq (cv x3) B2) L (cfv (cv x3) (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co A2 B2 cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co A2 B2 cioo)) (co (co A2 B2 cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L (co F B2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R (co F A2 climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg t (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv t) F)) (citg t (co (co A2 X cmin) (co B2 X cmin) cicc) (cfv (co X (cv t) caddc) F))) $;

--| The fourier partial sum for ` F ` rewritten as an integral. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem83 {x3 m n s1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 s1) (B2: class x3 n s1) (C: class m) (D: class m n) (S: class x3 m n s1) (F: class m s1) (N X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (cneg cpi) cpi cioo) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F C) cibl) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cmpt n cn0 (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) ccos) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cmpt n cn (co (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv x3) cmul) csin) cmul)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt m cn (co (co (cfv cc0 A2) c2 cdiv) (csu n (co c1 (cv m) cfz) (co (co (cfv (cv n) A2) (cfv (co (cv n) X cmul) ccos) cmul) (co (cfv (cv n) B2) (cfv (co (cv n) X cmul) csin) cmul) caddc)) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N S) (citg x3 C (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (co (cv x3) X cmin) (cfv N D)) cmul))) $;

--| If ` F ` is piecewise continuous and ` D ` is continuous, then ` G ` is continuous. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem84 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (D: class i m p) (P: class i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R F: class i m p) (G: class m s1 p) (L: class i m p) (M: class) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m) (X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co A2 X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co B2 X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co cr cr ccncf)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cfv (cv s1) D) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cibl) $;

--| Limit of the function ` G ` at the lower bounds of the partition intervals. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem85 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 P: class i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R S: class i m p) (U: class i m s1 p) (E F: class i m p) (G: class i m s1 p) (H: class i m p) (I: class i m s1 p) (K: class i m p) (M: class) (N: class i m p) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m) (W: class i m p) (X: class) (Y: class i m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wf (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc (cres I (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (co (cres I (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (co (cif (wceq (cfv (cv i) V) X) E (co (co R (cif (wbr (cfv (cv i) V) X clt) W Y) cmin) (cfv (cv i) Q) cdiv)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) Q) K) cmul) (cfv (cfv (cv i) Q) S) cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel A2 (co (cres G (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $;

--| Continuity of ` O ` and its limits with respect to the ` S ` partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem86 {f2 i j m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class f2 i j m p) (C: class f2 j m p) (D P: class f2 i j m s1 p) (Q: class f2 j m p) (R: class f2 i j m p) (S: class j m p) (T: class i j m s1 p) (U: class f2 j m s1 p) (E: class f2 i j m s1 p) (F: class f2 j m p) (L: class f2 i j m p) (M: class f2) (N: class j m p) (O: class f2 j m s1 p) (V: class f2 m) (X: class f2):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wcel cc0 (co A2 B2 cicc))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt s1 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) C cmin) (cv s1) cdiv) (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq T (cun (cpr A2 B2) (cin (crn Q) (co A2 B2 cioo))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv T chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) T clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (co (cif (wceq (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (cfv (co U c1 caddc) Q)) (csb i U L) (cfv (co X (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) caddc) F)) C cmin) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) (co c2 (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wceq E (co (co (co (cif (wceq (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv U Q)) (csb i U R) (cfv (co X (cfv (cv j) S) caddc) F)) C cmin) (cfv (cv j) S) cdiv) (co (cfv (cv j) S) (co c2 (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) S) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv) cmul) $ >
  $ wceq U (crio i (wss (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv j) (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wa (wa (wcel D (co (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) climc)) (wcel E (co (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv (cv j) S) climc))) (wcel (cres O (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv j) S) (cfv (co (cv j) c1 caddc) S) cioo) cc ccncf))) $;

--| The integral of ` G ` goes uniformly ( with respect to ` n ` ) to zero if the measure of the domain of integration goes to zero. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem87 {x3 u e k n s1 a d: setvar} (ph: wff e k) (ch: wff x3 u e k n a d) (D: class x3 e k s1 a) (S: class x3 u e k n s1 a d) (U: class u e s1 d) (F: class x3 u e k n s1 a d) (G: class x3 e k n) (H: class x3 u e k n s1 a d) (K: class x3 u e k n d) (W X Y: class x3 u e k n s1 a d):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W cr) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) H) cabs) (cv x3) cle) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wcel G cibl) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (cv e) c3 cdiv) (cv a) cdiv) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa (wa (wa (w3a (wa ph (wcel (cv e) crp)) (wcel (cv a) crp) (wral n (wral s1 (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv s1) G) cabs) (cv a) cle) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) cn)) (wcel (cv u) (cdm cvol))) (wa (wss (cv u) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) (wbr (cfv (cv u) cvol) D cle))) (wcel (cv n) cn)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv e) crp)) (wrex d (wral u (wi (wa (wss (cv u) (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc)) (wbr (cfv (cv u) cvol) (cv d) cle)) (wral k (wbr (cfv (citg s1 (cv u) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (co (co (cv k) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) cmul)) cabs) (co (cv e) c2 cdiv) clt) cn)) (cdm cvol)) crp) $;

--| Given a piecewise continuous function ` F ` , a continuous function ` K ` and a continuous function ` S ` , the function ` G ` is integrable. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem88 {i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (C D: class i m p) (P: class i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R S: class i m p) (U: class i m s1 p) (F: class i m p) (G: class m p) (H: class i m p) (I: class i m s1 p) (K L: class i m p) (M: class) (N: class i m p) (O: class i m s1 p) (V: class m) (W: class i m p) (X: class) (Y: class i m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (co (cres F (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co N (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq Q (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) V) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wf (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cr (cres I (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cres I (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co (cres I (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cibl) $;

--| Given a piecewise continuous function and changing the interval and the partition, the limit at the lower bound of each interval of the moved partition is still finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem89 {x3 y1 f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class k) (P: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (Q: class m) (R: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (S: class m) (T: class m p) (U: class f2 i k m p) (E: class m p) (F: class f2 k m p) (H: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (I: class m p) (J: class f2 k m p) (M: class y1 f2 k) (N: class) (O: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (V: class f2 i k m p) (Z: class f2 k m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C cpnf cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) Z) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfzo) R) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cif (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) E) Z) (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) I) Q)) (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) I) V) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) E) Z) F)) (co (cres F (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv J S) climc)) $;

--| Given a piecewise continuous function, it is still continuous with respect to an open interval of the moved partition. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem90 {x3 y1 f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class k) (P: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (Q: class m) (R: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (S: class m) (T: class f2 m p) (U: class x3 f2 i k m p) (E: class m p) (F: class f2 k m p) (G: class x3 f2 i k m p) (H: class i k m p) (I: class y1 m p) (J L: class f2 k m p) (M: class y1 f2 k) (N: class) (O: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C cpnf cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq L (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres F (co (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) E) L) (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cmpt y1 (co (co (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) E) L) U caddc) (co (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) U caddc) cioo) (cfv (co (cv y1) U cmin) G)) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) L) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres F (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (co (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo) cc ccncf)) $;

--| Given a piecewise continuous function and changing the interval and the partition, the limit at the upper bound of each interval of the moved partition is still finite. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem91 {x3 y1 f2 i k m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class k) (P: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (Q S: class m) (T: class m p) (U: class f2 i k m p) (E: class m p) (F: class f2 k m p) (H: class y1 i k m p) (I: class m p) (J: class f2 k m p) (L: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (M: class y1 f2 k) (N: class) (O: class x3 y1 f2 i k m p) (W: class f2 i k m p) (Z: class f2 k m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C cpnf cioo)) $ >
  $ wceq O (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) C) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) D)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) $ >
  $ wceq N (co (cfv H chash) c1 cmin) $ >
  $ wceq S (cio f2 (wiso (co cc0 N cfz) H clt clt (cv f2))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 cr (co (cv x3) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmin) T cdiv) cfl) T cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cioc) (cif (wceq (cv y1) B2) A2 (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq I (cmpt x3 cr (csup (crab i (wbr (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (cfv (cv x3) E) Z) cle) (co cc0 M cfzo)) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wceq W (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfzo) L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cif (wceq (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) (cfv (co (cfv (cfv J S) I) c1 caddc) Q)) (cfv (cfv (cfv J S) I) W) (cfv (cfv (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) E) F)) (co (cres F (co (cfv J S) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) cioo)) (cfv (co J c1 caddc) S) climc)) $;

--| The integral of a piecewise continuous periodic function ` F ` is unchanged if the domain is shifted by its period ` T ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem92 {x3 i m p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (A2 B2: class) (P: class x3 i m p) (Q: class m) (R: class i m p) (S: class m) (T: class) (F: class m p) (H: class x3 i m p) (L: class i m p) (M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) T caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co A2 T caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co B2 T caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cr cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg x3 (co (co A2 T caddc) (co B2 T caddc) cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F)) (citg x3 (co A2 B2 cicc) (cfv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| Integral by substitution (the domain is shifted by ` X ` ) for a piecewise continuous function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem93 {t i m s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m p) (P: class t i m s1 p) (Q: class m) (R: class i m s1 p) (F H: class m p) (L: class i m s1 p) (M: class) (X: class m p):
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) X cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) cc F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (citg t (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (cv t) F)) (citg s1 (co (co (cneg cpi) X cmin) (co cpi X cmin) cicc) (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F))) $;

--| For a piecewise smooth function, the left and the right limits exist at any point. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem94 {x3 i n p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (P: class x3 i n p) (Q T: class) (F: class p) (M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq T (co c2 cpi cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt n cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cneg cpi)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) (cv p)) cpi)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres (co cr F cdv) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wne (co (cres (co cr F cdv) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc) c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wne (co (cres (co cr F cdv) (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) climc) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wne (co (cres F (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc) c0) (wne (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc) c0)) $;

--| Algebraic manipulation of integrals, used by other lemmas. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem95 {i m n s1 p: setvar} (ph: wff m n p) (A2 B2 C D: class i m n p) (P: class i m n s1 p) (R S: class i m n p) (U E: class i m n s1 p) (F: class i m n p) (G: class m n p) (H: class i m n p) (I: class i m n s1 p) (K L: class i m n p) (M: class n) (O: class i m n p) (V: class m n) (W: class i m n p) (X: class n) (Y: class i m n p):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X cr) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (cneg cpi) X caddc)) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) (co cpi X caddc))) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel L (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) climc)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) cc0 (co (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) cmin) (cv s1) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq K (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cif (wceq (cv s1) cc0) c1 (co (cv s1) (co c2 (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv))) $ >
  $ wceq U (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) H) (cfv (cv s1) K) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (co (cfv (cv s1) U) (cfv (cv s1) S) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cr F cdv) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wf (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo) cr (cres I (co (cfv (cv i) V) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) V) cioo))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (co (cres I (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C (co (cres I (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y (co (cres F (co X cpnf cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W (co (cres F (co cmnf X cioo)) X climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 (cdm cvol)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 (cdif (co (cneg cpi) cpi cicc) (csn cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt n cn (co (citg s1 A2 (cfv (cv s1) G)) cpi cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cmpt n cn (cmpt s1 cr (cif (wceq (co (cv s1) (co c2 cpi cmul) cmo) cc0) (co (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) (co c2 cpi cmul) cdiv) (co (cfv (co (co (cv n) (co c1 c2 cdiv) caddc) (cv s1) cmul) csin) (co (co c2 cpi cmul) (cfv (co (cv s1) c2 cdiv) csin) cmul) cdiv)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel O cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv s1) A2)) (wceq (cif (wbr cc0 (cv s1) clt) Y W) O) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wceq (citg s1 A2 (cfv (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) D))) (co c1 c2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) cn)) (wceq (co (cfv (cv n) E) (co O c2 cdiv) caddc) (citg s1 A2 (co (cfv (co X (cv s1) caddc) F) (cfv (cv s1) (cfv (cv n) D)) cmul))) $;

--| limit for ` F ` at the lower bound of an interval for the moved partition ` V ` . (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.)
theorem fourierdlem96 {x3 y1 v u g2 h i j k m p: setvar} (ph: wff y1 v u g2 h j k m p) (A2: class v g2 h k) (B2: class g2 h k) (C D: class v u h j k) (P: class x3 y1 v u g2 h i j k m p) (Q: class v u) (R: class y1 v u g2 h i j k m p) (T: class u) (F J: class y1 v u g2 h j k m p) (M: class v u g2 h k) (V: class y1 v u g2 h j k m):
  $ wi ph (wf cr cr F) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt m cn (crab p (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) A2) (wceq (cfv (cv m) (cv p)) B2)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo))) (co cr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $ >
  $ wceq T (co B2 A2 cmin) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Q (cfv M P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) cr)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) T caddc) F) (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (co (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo) cc ccncf)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wcel R (co (cres F (co (cfv (cv i) Q) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) Q) cioo)) (cfv (cv i) Q) climc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (co C cpnf cioo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel J (co cc0 (co (cfv (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) chash) c1 cmin) cfzo)) $ >
  $ wceq V (cio g2 (wiso (co cc0 (co (cfv (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex k (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv k) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) chash) c1 cmin) cfz) (cun (cpr C D) (crab y1 (wrex h (wcel (co (cv y1) (co (cv h) T cmul) caddc) (crn Q)) cz) (co C D cicc))) clt clt (cv g2))) $ >
