  $ la $ >
  $ ka $ >
  $ jph $ >
  $ jps $ >
  $ jch $ >
  $ jth $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) la) ka) jph) jps) jch) jth $;

--| CONTRADICTION PROVED AT 1 + 1 = 2 . Given the right hypotheses we can prove a dandysum of 2+2=4. The qed step is the value '4' in Decimal BEING IMPLIED by the hypotheses. Note: Values that when added would exceed a 4bit value are not supported. Note: Digits begin from left (least) to right (greatest). E.g., 1000 would be '1', 0100 would be '2', 0010 would be '4'. How to perceive the hypotheses' bits in order: ( th <-> F. ), ( ta <-> F. ) Would be input value X's first bit, and input value Y's first bit. ( et <-> F ), ( ze <-> F. ) would be input value X's second bit, and input value Y's second bit. (Contributed by Jarvin Udandy, 6-Sep-2016.)
theorem dandysum2p2e4 (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la ka jph jps jch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa th ta) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa et ze) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa si rh) $ >
  $ wb th wfal $ >
  $ wb ta wfal $ >
  $ wb et wtru $ >
  $ wb ze wtru $ >
  $ wb si wfal $ >
  $ wb rh wfal $ >
  $ wb mu wfal $ >
  $ wb la wfal $ >
  $ wb ka (wxo (wxo th ta) (wa th ta)) $ >
  $ wb jph (wo (wxo et ze) ph) $ >
  $ wb jps (wo (wxo si rh) ps) $ >
  $ wb jch (wo (wxo mu la) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wb ph (wa th ta)) (wb ps (wa et ze))) (wb ch (wa si rh))) (wb th wfal)) (wb ta wfal)) (wb et wtru)) (wb ze wtru)) (wb si wfal)) (wb rh wfal)) (wb mu wfal)) (wb la wfal)) (wb ka (wxo (wxo th ta) (wa th ta)))) (wb jph (wo (wxo et ze) ph))) (wb jps (wo (wxo si rh) ps))) (wb jch (wo (wxo mu la) ch))) (wa (wa (wa (wb ka wfal) (wb jph wfal)) (wb jps wtru)) (wb jch wfal)) $;

--| CONTRADICTION PROVED AT 1 + 1 = 2 . Luckily Mario Carneiro did a successful version of his own. See Mario's Relevant Work: Half adder and full adder in propositional calculus. Given the right hypotheses we can prove a dandysum of 2+2=4. The qed step is the value '4' in Decimal BEING IMPLIED by the hypotheses. Note: Values that when added would exceed a 4bit value are not supported. Note: Digits begin from left (least) to right (greatest). E.g., 1000 would be '1', 0100 would be '2'. 0010 would be '4'. How to perceive the hypotheses' bits in order: ( th <-> F. ), ( ta <-> F. ) Would be input value X's first bit, and input value Y's first bit. ( et <-> F. ), ( ze <-> F. ) would be input value X's second bit, and input value Y's second bit. In mdandysum2p2e4, one might imagine what jth or jta could be then do the math with their truths. Also limited to the restriction jth, jta are having opposite truths equivalent to the stated truth constants. (Contributed by Jarvin Udandy, 6-Sep-2016.)
theorem mdandysum2p2e4 (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la ka jph jps jch jth jta: wff):
  $ wb jth wfal $ >
  $ wb jta wtru $ >
  $ wb ph (wa th ta) $ >
  $ wb ps (wa et ze) $ >
  $ wb ch (wa si rh) $ >
  $ wb th jth $ >
  $ wb ta jth $ >
  $ wb et jta $ >
  $ wb ze jta $ >
  $ wb si jth $ >
  $ wb rh jth $ >
  $ wb mu jth $ >
  $ wb la jth $ >
  $ wb ka (wxo (wxo th ta) (wa th ta)) $ >
  $ wb jph (wo (wxo et ze) ph) $ >
  $ wb jps (wo (wxo si rh) ps) $ >
  $ wb jch (wo (wxo mu la) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wb ph (wa th ta)) (wb ps (wa et ze))) (wb ch (wa si rh))) (wb th wfal)) (wb ta wfal)) (wb et wtru)) (wb ze wtru)) (wb si wfal)) (wb rh wfal)) (wb mu wfal)) (wb la wfal)) (wb ka (wxo (wxo th ta) (wa th ta)))) (wb jph (wo (wxo et ze) ph))) (wb jps (wo (wxo si rh) ps))) (wb jch (wo (wxo mu la) ch))) (wa (wa (wa (wb ka wfal) (wb jph wfal)) (wb jps wtru)) (wb jch wfal)) $;

--| Replacement of a nested antecedent with an outer antecedent. Commuted simplificated form of elimination of a nested antecedent. Also holds intuitionistically. Polish prefix notation: CCCpqrCsCqr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem adh_jarrsc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi th (wi ps ch)) $;

--| A single axiom for minimal implicational calculus, due to Meredith. Other single axioms of the same length are known, but it is thought to be the minimal length. This is the axiom from Carew Arthur Meredith, _A single axiom of positive logic_, The Journal of Computing Systems, volume 1, issue 3, July 1953, pages 169--170. A two-line review by Alonzo Church of this article can be found in The Journal of Symbolic Logic, volume 19, issue 2, June 1954, page 144, ~ https://doi.org/10.2307/2268914 . Known as "HI-1" on Dolph Edward "Ted" Ulrich's web page. In the next 6 lemmas and 3 theorems, ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 are derived from this single axiom in 16 detachments (instances of ~ ax-mp ) in total. Polish prefix notation: CCCpqrCsCCqCrtCqt . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.)
theorem adh_minim (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi th (wi (wi ps (wi ch ta)) (wi ps ta))) $;

--| First lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CpCCqCCrCCsCqtCstuCqu . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax1_ax2_lem1 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps (wi (wi ch (wi (wi th (wi ps ta)) (wi th ta))) et)) (wi ps et)) $;

--| Second lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpCCqCCrCpsCrstCpt . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax1_ax2_lem2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi (wi ps (wi (wi ch (wi ph th)) (wi ch th))) ta)) (wi ph ta) $;

--| Third lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpCqrCqCsCpr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax1_ax2_lem3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi th (wi ph ch))) $;

--| Fourth lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCCpqrCCqCrsCqs . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax1_ax2_lem4 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wi ps (wi ch th)) (wi ps th)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-1 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Carew Arthur Meredith derived ~ ax-1 in _A single axiom of positive logic_, The Journal of Computing Systems, volume 1, issue 3, July 1953, pages 169--170. However, here we follow the shortened derivation by Ivo Thomas, _On Meredith's sole positive axiom_, Notre Dame Journal of Formal Logic, volume XV, number 3, July 1974, page 477. Polish prefix notation: CpCqp . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Fifth lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CpCCCqrsCCrCstCrt . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax2_lem5 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi (wi ps ch) th) (wi (wi ch (wi th ta)) (wi ch ta))) $;

--| Sixth lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpCCCCqrsCCrCstCrtuCpu . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax2_lem6 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi (wi (wi (wi ps ch) th) (wi (wi ch (wi th ta)) (wi ch ta))) et)) (wi ph et) $;

--| Derivation of a commuted form of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpqCCpCqrCpr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax2c (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minim and ~ ax-mp . Carew Arthur Meredith derived ~ ax-2 in _A single axiom of positive logic_, The Journal of Computing Systems, volume 1, issue 3, July 1953, pages 169--170. However, here we follow the shortened derivation by Ivo Thomas, _On Meredith's sole positive axiom_, Notre Dame Journal of Formal Logic, volume XV, number 3, July 1974, page 477. Polish prefix notation: CCpCqrCCpqCpr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_ax2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ id (reflexivity of implication, PM *2.08 WhiteheadRussell p. 101) from ~ adh-minim-ax1 , ~ adh-minim-ax2 , and ~ ax-mp . It uses the derivation written DD211 in D-notation. (See head comment for an explanation.) Polish prefix notation: Cpp . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_idALT (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph ph $;

--| Derivation of ~ pm2.43 WhiteheadRussell p. 106 (also called "hilbert" or "W") from ~ adh-minim-ax1 , ~ adh-minim-ax2 , and ~ ax-mp . It uses the derivation written DD22D21 in D-notation. (See head comment for an explanation.) (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minim_pm2_43 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Another single axiom for minimal implicational calculus, due to Meredith. Other single axioms of the same length are known, but it is thought to be the minimal length. Among single axioms of this length, it is the one with simplest antecedents (i.e., in the corresponding ordering of binary trees which first compares left subtrees, it is the first one). Known as "HI-2" on Dolph Edward "Ted" Ulrich's web page. In the next 4 lemmas and 5 theorems, ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 are derived from this other single axiom in 20 detachments (instances of ~ ax-mp ) in total. Polish prefix notation: CpCCqrCCCsqCrtCqt ; or CtCCpqCCCspCqrCpr in Carew Arthur Meredith and Arthur Norman Prior, _Notes on the axiomatics of the propositional calculus_, Notre Dame Journal of Formal Logic, volume IV, number 3, July 1963, pages 171--187, on page 180. (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.)
theorem adh_minimp (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wi (wi th ps) (wi ch ta)) (wi ps ta))) $;

--| First lemma for the derivation of ~ jarr , ~ imim1 , and a commuted form of ~ ax-2 , and indirectly ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 , from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpqCCCrpCqsCps . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_jarr_imim1_ax2c_lem1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ch ph) (wi ps th)) (wi ph th)) $;

--| Second lemma for the derivation of ~ jarr , and indirectly ~ ax-1 , a commuted form of ~ ax-2 , and ~ ax-2 proper, from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCCpqCCCrsCCCtrCsuCruvCqv . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_jarr_lem2 (ph ps ch th ta et ze: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ch th) (wi (wi (wi ta ch) (wi th et)) (wi ch et))) ze)) (wi ps ze) $;

--| Third lemma for the derivation of ~ jarr and a commuted form of ~ ax-2 , and indirectly ~ ax-1 and ~ ax-2 proper , from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCCCpqCCCrpCqsCpstt . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_jarr_ax2c_lem3 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wi ch ph) (wi ps th)) (wi ph th))) ta) ta $;

--| Derivation of ~ jarr (also called "syll-simp") from ~ minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCCpqrCqr . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_sylsimp (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi ps ch) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-1 from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CpCqp . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_ax1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ph) $;

--| Derivation of ~ imim1 ("left antimonotonicity of implication", theorem *2.06 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100) from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpqCCqrCpr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_imim1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of a commuted form of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpqCCpCqrCpr . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_ax2c (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Fourth lemma for the derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CpCCqCprCqr . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_ax2_lem4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wi ps (wi ph ch)) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ ax-2 from ~ adh-minimp and ~ ax-mp . Polish prefix notation: CCpCqrCCpqCpr . (Contributed by BJ, 4-Apr-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_ax2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Derivation of ~ id (reflexivity of implication, PM *2.08 WhiteheadRussell p. 101) from ~ adh-minimp-ax1 , ~ adh-minimp-ax2 , and ~ ax-mp . It uses the derivation written DD211 in D-notation. (See head comment for an explanation.) Polish prefix notation: Cpp . (Contributed by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_idALT (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph ph $;

--| Derivation of ~ pm2.43 WhiteheadRussell p. 106 (also called "hilbert" or "W") from ~ adh-minimp-ax1 , ~ adh-minimp-ax2 , and ~ ax-mp . It uses the derivation written DD22D21 in D-notation. (See head comment for an explanation.) Polish prefix notation: CCpCpqCpq . (Contributed by BJ, 31-May-2021.) (Revised by ADH, 10-Nov-2023.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem adh_minimp_pm2_43 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| If a class with one element is not a singleton, there is at least another element in this class. (Contributed by AV, 6-Mar-2025.)
theorem n0nsn2el {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wne B2 (csn A2))) (wrex x3 (wne (cv x3) A2) B2) $;

--| There is a unique element of a singleton which is equal to another singleton. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem eusnsn {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ weu x3 (wceq (csn (cv x3)) (csn (cv y1))) $;

--| If the class abstraction ` { x | ph } ` associated with the wff ` ph ` is a singleton, the wff is true for the singleton element. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem absnsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv y1))) (wsb x3 ph y1) $;

--| Another way to express existential uniqueness of a wff ` ph ` : its associated class abstraction ` { x | ph } ` is a singleton. Variant of ~ euabsn2 using existential uniqueness for the singleton element instead of existence only. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem euabsneu {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (weu y1 (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv y1)))) $;

--| An element of a proper unordered pair is the first element iff it is not the second element. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem elprneb (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cpr B2 C)) (wne B2 C)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wne A2 C)) $;

--| Equality for ordered pairs implies equality of unordered pairs with the same elements. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2023.)
theorem oppr (A2 B2 C D V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wi (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D))) $;

--| Equality for unordered pairs corresponds to equality of unordered pairs with the same elements. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2023.)
theorem opprb (A2 B2 C D V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wa (wcel C X) (wcel D Y))) (wb (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D)) (wo (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop C D)) (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop D C)))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ or2expropbi and ~ ich2exprop . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2023.)
theorem or2expropbilem1 {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2 B2: class a b) (X: class x3 y1 a b):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wi (wa (wceq A2 (cv a)) (wceq B2 (cv b))) (wi ph (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wsbc b (wsbc a ph (cv x3)) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ or2expropbi and ~ ich2exprop . (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2023.)
theorem or2expropbilem2 {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wsbc b (wsbc a ph (cv x3)) (cv y1))))) $;

--| If two classes are strictly ordered, there is an ordered pair of both classes fulfilling a wff iff there is an unordered pair of both classes fulfilling the wff. (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2023.)
theorem or2expropbi {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2 B2 R V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X V) (wor X R)) (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wbr A2 B2 R))) (wb (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) R) ph)))) (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) R) ph))))) $;

--| If there is a unique set which is related to a class, then the class must be a set. (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem eubrv {b: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (weu b (wbr A2 (cv b) R)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| If there is a unique set which is related to a class, then the class is an element of the domain of the relation. (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem eubrdm {b: setvar} (A2 R: class):
  $ wi (weu b (wbr A2 (cv b) R)) (wcel A2 (cdm R)) $;

--| Element of the domain of a restriction to a singleton. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem eldmressn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cdm (cres F (csn A2)))) (wceq B2 A2) $;

--| Example for a defined iota being the empty set, i.e., ` A. y x C_ y ` is a wff satisfied by a unique value ` x ` , namely ` x = (/) ` (the empty set is the one and only set which is a subset of every set). (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem iota0def {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cio x3 (wal y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) c0 $;

--| Example for an undefined iota being the empty set, i.e., ` A. y y e. x ` is a wff not satisfied by a (unique) value ` x ` (there is no set, and therefore certainly no unique set, which contains every set). (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem iota0ndef {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cio x3 (wal y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) c0 $;

--| If a function's value at an argument is the universal class (which can never be the case because of ~ fvex ), the function's value at this argument is any set (especially the empty set). In short "If a function's value is a proper class, it is a set", which sounds strange/contradictory, but which is a consequence of that a contradiction implies anything (see ~ pm2.21i ). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem fveqvfvv (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cfv A2 F) cvv) (wceq (cfv A2 F) B2) $;

--| Composition of two functions, similar to ~ fnco . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-Jul-2017.)
theorem fnresfnco (B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn (cres F (crn G)) (crn G)) (wfn G B2)) (wfn (ccom F G) B2) $;

--| A composition restricted to a singleton is a function under certain conditions. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-Jul-2017.)
theorem funcoressn (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cfv X G) (cdm F)) (wfun (cres F (csn (cfv X G))))) (wa (wfn G A2) (wcel X A2))) (wfun (cres (ccom F G) (csn X))) $;

--| A restriction to a singleton with a function value is a function under certain conditions. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-Jul-2017.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem funressnfv (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel X (cdm (ccom F G))) (wfun (cres (ccom F G) (csn X)))) (wa (wfn G A2) (wcel X A2))) (wfun (cres F (csn (cfv X G)))) $;

--| The value of a function ` F ` at a set ` A ` is in the range of the function ` F ` if ` A ` is in the domain of the function ` F ` . It is sufficient that ` F ` is a function at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressndmfvrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun (cres F (csn A2))) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| A function restricted to a singleton has at most one value for the singleton element as argument. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressnvmo {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3):
  $ wi (wfun (cres F (csn (cv x3)))) (wmo y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) F)) $;

--| A function restricted to a singleton has at most one value for the singleton element as argument. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressnmo {y1: setvar} (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wfun (cres F (csn A2)))) (wmo y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) $;

--| There is exactly one value of a class which is a function restricted to a singleton, analogous to ~ funeu . ` A e. _V ` is required because otherwise ` E! y A F y ` , see ~ brprcneu . (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressneu {y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (F V: class) (W: class y1):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wfun (cres F (csn A2))) (wbr A2 B2 F)) (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) $;

--| Conditions for a restriction to be an onto function. Part of ~ fresf1o . (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2024.)
theorem fresfo (C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wss C (crn F))) (wfo (cima (ccnv F) C) C (cres F (cima (ccnv F) C))) $;

--| The class of all functions from a (proper) singleton into ` B ` is the union of all the singletons of (proper) ordered pairs over the elements of ` B ` as second component. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsniunop {f2 b: setvar} (B2 S: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq (cab f2 (wf (csn S) B2 (cv f2))) (ciun b B2 (csn (csn (cop S (cv b)))))) $;

--| The class of all functions from a (proper) singleton into ` B ` is the class of all the singletons of (proper) ordered pairs over the elements of ` B ` as second component. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetabsnop {y1 f2 b: setvar} (B2 S: class) (V: class y1 f2):
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq (cab f2 (wf (csn S) B2 (cv f2))) (cab y1 (wrex b (wceq (cv y1) (csn (cop S (cv b)))) B2))) $;

--| The mapping of an element of a class to a singleton function is a function. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsnf {x3 y1 b: setvar} (A2: class y1 b) (B2 S: class) (F: class x3 y1 b) (V: class y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab y1 (wrex b (wceq (cv y1) (csn (cop S (cv b)))) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (csn (cop S (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wf B2 A2 F) $;

--| The mapping of an element of a class to a singleton function is an injection. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsnf1 {x3 y1 b: setvar} (A2: class y1 b) (B2 S: class) (F: class x3 y1 b) (V: class y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab y1 (wrex b (wceq (cv y1) (csn (cop S (cv b)))) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (csn (cop S (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wf1 B2 A2 F) $;

--| The mapping of an element of a class to a singleton function is a surjection. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsnfo {x3 y1 b: setvar} (A2: class y1 b) (B2 S: class) (F: class x3 y1 b) (V: class y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab y1 (wrex b (wceq (cv y1) (csn (cop S (cv b)))) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (csn (cop S (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wfo B2 A2 F) $;

--| The mapping of an element of a class to a singleton function is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsnf1o {x3 y1 b: setvar} (A2: class y1 b) (B2 S: class) (F: class x3 y1 b) (V: class y1):
  $ wceq A2 (cab y1 (wrex b (wceq (cv y1) (csn (cop S (cv b)))) B2)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (csn (cop S (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wf1o B2 A2 F) $;

--| The class of all functions from a (proper) singleton into a proper class ` B ` is not a set. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fsetsnprcnex {f2: setvar} (B2 S: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wnel B2 cvv)) (wnel (cab f2 (wf (csn S) B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| The class of constant functions is a subclass of the class of functions. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetssfset {z f2 b: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class z f2 b):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wss F (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) $;

--| The function value of the mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetfv {x3 z f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3 z f2 b) (B2 F: class x3 z f2 g2 a b) (G: class x3 z f2 a b) (H: class x3 z f2 g2 a b) (V: class x3 z f2 a b) (X: class x3 z f2 b) (Y: class x3 z f2 a b):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel X G)) (wceq (cfv X H) (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y X))) $;

--| The function value of the mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetfv_b {x3 z g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3 z b) (B2 F: class x3 z g2 a b) (G: class x3 z a b) (H: class x3 z g2 a b) (V: class x3 z a b) (X: class x3 z b) (Y: class x3 z a b):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel X G)) (wceq (cfv X H) (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y X))) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a function. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf {x3 z f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class z g2) (F: class x3 z f2 a b) (G: class x3 f2) (H: class x3 z f2 g2 a b) (V: class x3 f2) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a function. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf_b {x3 z g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class z g2) (F: class x3 z a b) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 z g2 a b) (V: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is an injection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf1 {x3 z f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class z g2) (F: class x3 z f2 a b) (G: class x3 f2) (H: class x3 z f2 g2 a b) (V: class x3 f2) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf1 G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is an injection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf1_b {x3 z g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class z g2) (F: class x3 z a b) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 z g2 a b) (V: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf1 G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a surjection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetfo {x3 z f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class g2) (F: class x3 z f2 a b) (G: class x3 f2) (H: class x3 z f2 g2 a) (V: class x3 f2) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wfo G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a surjection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetfo_b {x3 z g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (F: class x3 z a b) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 z g2 a) (V: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wfo G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf1o {x3 z f2 g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class g2) (F: class x3 z f2 a b) (G: class x3 f2) (H: class x3 z f2 g2 a) (V: class x3 f2) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab f2 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv f2)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv f2)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf1o G F H) $;

--| The mapping of the class of singleton functions into the class of constant functions is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.)
theorem cfsetsnfsetf1o_b {x3 z g2 a b: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class g2) (F: class x3 z a b) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 z g2 a) (V: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cab x3 (wa (wf A2 B2 (cv x3)) (wrex b (wral z (wceq (cfv (cv z) (cv x3)) (cv b)) A2) B2))) $ >
  $ wceq G (cab x3 (wf (csn Y) B2 (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt g2 G (cmpt a A2 (cfv Y (cv g2)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel Y A2)) (wf1o G F H) $;

--| First version of proof for ~ fsetprcnex , which was much more complicated. (Contributed by AV, 14-Sep-2024.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fsetprcnexALT {f2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V: class f2):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wne A2 c0)) (wnel B2 cvv)) (wnel (cab f2 (wf A2 B2 (cv f2))) cvv) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ fcores . (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoreslem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C P E F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (cima (ccnv F) E)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ fcores . (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoreslem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C P E F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn X) E) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ fcores . (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoreslem3 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C P E F X: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo P E X) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ fcores . (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoreslem4 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (ccom Y X) P) $;

--| Every composite function ` ( G o. F ) ` can be written as composition of restrictions of the composed functions (to their minimum domains). (Contributed by GL and AV, 17-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcores (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (ccom G F) (ccom Y X)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fcoresf1 . (Contributed by AV, 18-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoresf1lem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel Z P)) (wceq (cfv Z (ccom G F)) (cfv (cfv Z X) Y)) $;

--| If a composition is injective, then the restrictions of its components to the minimum domains are injective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 18-Sep-2024.) (Revised by AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem fcoresf1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1 P D (ccom G F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wf1 P E X) (wf1 E D Y)) $;

--| A composition is injective iff the restrictions of its components to the minimum domains are injective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem fcoresf1b (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wf1 P D (ccom G F)) (wa (wf1 P E X) (wf1 E D Y))) $;

--| If a composition is surjective, then the restriction of its first component to the minimum domain is surjective. (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2024.)
theorem fcoresfo (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo P D (ccom G F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfo E D Y) $;

--| A composition is surjective iff the restriction of its first component to the minimum domain is surjective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem fcoresfob (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wfo P D (ccom G F)) (wfo E D Y)) $;

--| A composition is bijective iff the restriction of its first component to the minimum domain is bijective and the restriction of its second component to the minimum domain is injective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem fcoresf1ob (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wf1o P D (ccom G F)) (wa (wf1 P E X) (wf1o E D Y))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ f1cof1b and ~ focofob . (Contributed by AV, 18-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1cof1blem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D P E F G X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wceq E (cin (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wceq P (cima (ccnv F) C) $ >
  $ wceq X (cres F P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf C D G) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cres G E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn F) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wa (wceq P A2) (wceq E C)) (wa (wceq X F) (wceq Y G))) $;

--| The composition of three bijections as bijection from the image of the domain onto the image of the range of the middle bijection. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2025.)
theorem _3f1oss1 (A2 B2 C D E F G H I: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wf1o C D G) (wf1o E I H)) (wa (wss C A2) (wss D E))) (wf1o (cima F C) (cima H D) (ccom (ccom H G) (ccnv F))) $;

--| The composition of three bijections as bijection from the image of the converse of the domain onto the image of the converse of the range of the middle bijection. (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2025.)
theorem _3f1oss2 (A2 B2 C D E F G H I: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wf1o A2 B2 F) (wf1o C D G) (wf1o E I H)) (wa (wss C B2) (wss D I))) (wf1o (cima (ccnv F) C) (cima (ccnv H) D) (ccom (ccom (ccnv H) G) F)) $;

--| If the range of ` F ` equals the domain of ` G ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is injective iff ` F ` and ` G ` are both injective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 19-Sep-2024.)
theorem f1cof1b (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wf C D G) (wceq (crn F) C)) (wb (wf1 A2 D (ccom G F)) (wa (wf1 A2 B2 F) (wf1 C D G))) $;

--| If the domain of a function ` G ` is a subset of the range of a function ` F ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is surjective iff ` G ` is surjective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 29-Sep-2024.)
theorem funfocofob (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfun F) (wf A2 B2 G) (wss A2 (crn F))) (wb (wfo (cima (ccnv F) A2) B2 (ccom G F)) (wfo A2 B2 G)) $;

--| If the domain of a function ` G ` equals the range of a function ` F ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is surjective iff ` G ` is surjective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 29-Sep-2024.)
theorem fnfocofob (A2 B2 C F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wf B2 C G) (wceq (crn F) B2)) (wb (wfo A2 C (ccom G F)) (wfo B2 C G)) $;

--| If the domain of a function ` G ` equals the range of a function ` F ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is surjective iff ` G ` and ` F ` as function to the domain of ` G ` are both surjective. Symmetric version of ~ fnfocofob including the fact that ` F ` is a surjection onto its range. (Contributed by GL and AV, 20-Sep-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 29-Sep-2024.)
theorem focofob (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wf C D G) (wceq (crn F) C)) (wb (wfo A2 D (ccom G F)) (wa (wfo A2 C F) (wfo C D G))) $;

--| If the range of ` F ` equals the domain of ` G ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is bijective iff ` F ` and ` G ` are both bijective. (Contributed by GL and AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem f1ocof1ob (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wf C D G) (wceq (crn F) C)) (wb (wf1o A2 D (ccom G F)) (wa (wf1 A2 C F) (wf1o C D G))) $;

--| If the range of ` F ` equals the domain of ` G ` , then the composition ` ( G o. F ) ` is bijective iff ` F ` and ` G ` are both bijective. Symmetric version of ~ f1ocof1ob including the fact that ` F ` is a surjection onto its range. (Contributed by GL and AV, 20-Sep-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 7-Oct-2024.)
theorem f1ocof1ob2 (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wf C D G) (wceq (crn F) C)) (wb (wf1o A2 D (ccom G F)) (wa (wf1o A2 C F) (wf1o C D G))) $;

--| Extend class notation with an alternative for Russell's definition of a description binder (inverted iota).
term caiota {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3): class;

--| Soundness justification theorem for ~ df-aiota . (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiotajust {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (cint (cab y1 (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv y1))))) (cint (cab z (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv z))))) $;

--| Alternate version of Russell's definition of a description binder, which can be read as "the unique ` x ` such that ` ph ` ", where ` ph ` ordinarily contains ` x ` as a free variable. Our definition is meaningful only when there is exactly one ` x ` such that ` ph ` is true (see ~ aiotaval ); otherwise, it is not a set (see ~ aiotaexb ), or even more concrete, it is the universe ` _V ` (see ~ aiotavb ). Since this is an alternative for ~ df-iota , we call this symbol ` iota' ` _alternate iota_ in the following. The advantage of this definition is the clear distinguishability of the defined and undefined cases: the alternate iota over a wff is defined iff it is a set (see ~ aiotaexb ). With the original definition, there is no corresponding theorem ` ( E! x ph <-> ( iota x ph ) =/= (/) ) ` , because ` (/) ` can be a valid unique set satisfying a wff (see, for example, ~ iota0def ). Only the right to left implication would hold, see (negated) ~ iotanul . See also ~ aiotaexim which requires sethood instead of not being empty. For defined cases, however, both definitions ~ df-iota and ~ df-aiota are equivalent, see ~ reuaiotaiota . (Proposed by BJ, 13-Aug-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
axiom df_aiota {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (caiota x3 ph) (cint (cab y1 (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv y1))))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the alternate version of Russell's definition of a description binder. Definition 8.18 in [Quine] p. 56. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem dfaiota2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (caiota x3 ph) (cint (cab y1 (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| The iota and the alternate iota over a wff ` ph ` are equal iff there is a unique satisfying value of ` { x | ph } = { y } ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem reuabaiotaiota {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu y1 (wceq (cab x3 ph) (csn (cv y1)))) (wceq (cio x3 ph) (caiota x3 ph)) $;

--| The iota and the alternate iota over a wff ` ph ` are equal iff there is a unique value ` x ` satisfying ` ph ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem reuaiotaiota {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wceq (cio x3 ph) (caiota x3 ph)) $;

--| The alternate iota over a wff ` ph ` is a set iff there is a unique value ` x ` satisfying ` ph ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiotaexb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (weu x3 ph) (wcel (caiota x3 ph) cvv) $;

--| The alternate iota over a wff ` ph ` is the universe iff there is no unique value ` x ` satisfying ` ph ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiotavb {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wn (weu x3 ph)) (wceq (caiota x3 ph) cvv) $;

--| This is to ~ df-aiota what ~ iotauni is to ~ df-iota (it uses intersection like ~ df-aiota , similar to ~ iotauni using union like ~ df-iota ; we could also prove an analogous result using union here too, in the same way that we have ~ iotaint ). (Contributed by BJ, 31-Aug-2024.)
theorem aiotaint {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (weu x3 ph) (wceq (caiota x3 ph) (cint (cab x3 ph))) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` iota' ` , using the ` if ` operator: this is to ~ df-aiota what ~ dfiota4 is to ~ df-iota . It is simpler than ~ df-aiota and uses no dummy variables, so it would be the preferred definition if ` iota' ` becomes the description binder used in set.mm. (Contributed by BJ, 31-Aug-2024.)
theorem dfaiota3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wceq (caiota x3 ph) (cif (weu x3 ph) (cint (cab x3 ph)) cvv) $;

--| If the iota over a wff ` ph ` is not empty, the alternate iota over ` ph ` is a set. (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem iotan0aiotaex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wne (cio x3 ph) c0) (wcel (caiota x3 ph) cvv) $;

--| The alternate iota over a wff ` ph ` is a set iff the iota and the alternate iota over ` ph ` are equal. (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiotaexaiotaiota {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wcel (caiota x3 ph) cvv) (wceq (cio x3 ph) (caiota x3 ph)) $;

--| Theorem 8.19 in [Quine] p. 57. This theorem is the fundamental property of (alternate) iota. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiotaval {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb ph (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)))) (wceq (caiota x3 ph) (cv y1)) $;

--| Example for a defined alternate iota being the empty set, i.e., ` A. y x C_ y ` is a wff satisfied by a unique value ` x ` , namely ` x = (/) ` (the empty set is the one and only set which is a subset of every set). This corresponds to ~ iota0def . (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiota0def {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq (caiota x3 (wal y1 (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))) c0 $;

--| Example for an undefined alternate iota being no set, i.e., ` A. y y e. x ` is a wff not satisfied by a (unique) value ` x ` (there is no set, and therefore certainly no unique set, which contains every set). This is different from ~ iota0ndef , where the iota still is a set (the empty set). (Contributed by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem aiota0ndef {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wnel (caiota x3 (wal y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) cvv $;

--| Representation of "the unique element such that ` ph ` " with a class expression ` A ` which is a set (that means that "the unique element such that ` ph ` " exists). Analog to ~ iotan0 which requires ` A ` to be nonempty instead of being a set. That gives a more natural statement, closer to statements like ~ spcgv . (Proposed by BJ, 18-Oct-2026.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Oct-2026.)
theorem aiotaexim {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wceq A2 (caiota x3 ph))) ps $;

--| Theorem 19.32 of [Margaris] p. 90 with restricted quantifiers, analogous to ~ r19.32v . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem r19_32 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wo ph ps) A2) (wo ph (wral x3 ps A2)) $;

--| An equivalent expression for restricted existence, analogous to ~ exsb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jul-2017.)
theorem rexsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph)) A2) $;

--| An equivalent expression for restricted existence, analogous to ~ exsb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jul-2017.)
theorem rexrsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) ph) A2) A2) $;

--| An equivalent expression for double restricted existence, analogous to ~ rexsb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jul-2017.)
theorem _2rexsb {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wb (wrex x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wrex z (wrex w (wal x3 (wal y1 (wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ph))) B2) A2) $;

--| An equivalent expression for double restricted existence, analogous to ~ 2exsb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jul-2017.)
theorem _2rexrsb {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wb (wrex x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wrex z (wrex w (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ph) B2) A2) B2) A2) $;

--| Change bound variables of double restricted universal quantification, using implicit substitution, analogous to ~ cbvral2v . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem cbvral2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class y1 w) (B2: class):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf w ch $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wral y1 ph B2) A2) (wral z (wral w ps B2) A2) $;

--| Change bound variables of double restricted universal quantification, using implicit substitution, analogous to ~ cbvrex2v . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem cbvrex2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1 z w) (A2: class y1 w) (B2: class):
  $ wnf z ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf w ch $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wb ch ps) $ >
  $ wb (wrex x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wrex z (wrex w ps B2) A2) $;

--| Example for a theorem about a restricted universal quantification in which the restricting class depends on (actually is) the bound variable: All sets containing themselves contain the universal class. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jun-2023.)
theorem ralndv1 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wral x3 (wcel cvv (cv x3)) (cv x3) $;

--| Second example for a theorem about a restricted universal quantification in which the restricting class depends on the bound variable: all subsets of a set are sets. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jun-2023.)
theorem ralndv2 {x3: setvar}:
  $ wral x3 (wcel (cv x3) cvv) (cpw (cv x3)) $;

--| There is exactly one element in each of two isomorphic sets. Variant of ~ reuf1od with no distinct variable condition for ` ch ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Mar-2023.)
theorem reuf1odnf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff z) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff x3 y1 z) (th: wff y1 z) (B2 C F: class z):
  $ wi ph (wf1o C B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) F))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu x3 ps B2) (wreu y1 ch C)) $;

--| There is exactly one element in each of two isomorphic sets. (Contributed by AV, 19-Mar-2023.)
theorem reuf1od {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (ch: wff y1) (B2 C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o C B2 F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv y1) F))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu x3 ps B2) (wreu y1 ch C)) $;

--| There is a set which is equal to one of two other sets iff the other sets are equal. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jan-2023.)
theorem euoreqb {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wreu x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv x3) B2)) V) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Double restricted existential uniqueness, analogous to ~ 2eu3 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem _2reu3 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wral y1 (wo (wrmo x3 ph A2) (wrmo y1 ph B2)) B2) A2) (wb (wa (wreu x3 (wreu y1 ph B2) A2) (wreu y1 (wreu x3 ph A2) B2)) (wa (wreu x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wreu y1 (wrex x3 ph A2) B2))) $;

--| Two equivalent expressions for double restricted existential uniqueness, analogous to ~ 2eu7 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem _2reu7 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wreu x3 (wrex y1 ph B2) A2) (wreu y1 (wrex x3 ph A2) B2)) (wreu x3 (wreu y1 (wa (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 ph B2)) B2) A2) $;

--| Two equivalent expressions for double restricted existential uniqueness, analogous to ~ 2eu8 . Curiously, we can put ` E! ` on either of the internal conjuncts but not both. We can also commute ` E! x e. A E! y e. B ` using ~ 2reu7 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Jul-2017.)
theorem _2reu8 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wreu x3 (wreu y1 (wa (wrex x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 ph B2)) B2) A2) (wreu x3 (wreu y1 (wa (wreu x3 ph A2) (wrex y1 ph B2)) B2) A2) $;

--| Implication of a double restricted existential uniqueness in terms of restricted existential quantification and restricted universal quantification, see also ~ 2reu8 . The involved wffs depend on the setvar variables as follows: ph(x,y), ta(v,y), ch(x,w), th(v,w), et(x,b), ps(a,b), ze(a,w). (Contributed by AV, 1-Apr-2023.)
theorem _2reu8i {x3 y1 w v a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff v a b) (ch: wff x3 w) (th ta: wff y1 w v) (et: wff x3 v a b) (ze: wff y1 w v a b) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv v)) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv v)) (wb ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv b)) (wb ph et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv a)) (wb ch ze) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wi ch (wceq (cv y1) (cv w))) ze) (wceq (cv y1) (cv w)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv a)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv b))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wreu x3 (wreu y1 ph B2) A2) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wa ph (wral a (wral b (wi et (wa (wceq (cv b) (cv y1)) (wi ps (wceq (cv a) (cv x3))))) B2) A2)) B2) A2) $;

--| Implication of a double restricted existential uniqueness in terms of restricted existential quantification and restricted universal quantification. The involved wffs depend on the setvar variables as follows: ph(a,b), th(a,c), ch(d,b), ta(d,c), et(a,e), ps(a,f) (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem _2reuimp0 {e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff f2 a b) (ps: wff e f2 a b d) (ch: wff b c d) (th: wff a c) (ta: wff b c d) (et: wff e a c d) (V: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv c)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ph et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb th ps) $ >
  $ wi (wreu a (wreu b ph V) V) (wrex a (wral d (wral b (wrex e (wral f2 (wa (wa et (wi (wa ch (wral c (wi ta (wceq (cv b) (cv c))) V)) (wceq (cv a) (cv d)))) (wi ps (wceq (cv e) (cv f2)))) V) V) V) V) V) $;

--| Implication of a double restricted existential uniqueness in terms of restricted existential quantification and restricted universal quantification if the class of the quantified elements is not empty. (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem _2reuimp {e f2 a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff f2 a b) (ps: wff e f2 a b d) (ch: wff b d) (th: wff a c) (ta: wff b c d) (et: wff e a d) (V: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv c)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv b) (cv e)) (wb ph et) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv c) (cv f2)) (wb th ps) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wne V c0) (wreu a (wreu b ph V) V)) (wrex a (wral d (wral b (wrex e (wral f2 (wrex c (wi (wa ch (wi ta (wceq (cv b) (cv c)))) (wi ps (wa et (wa (wceq (cv a) (cv d)) (wceq (cv e) (cv f2)))))) V) V) V) V) V) V) $;

--| Extend the definition of a wff to include the "defined at" predicate. Read: "(the function) ` F ` is defined at (the argument) ` A ` ". In a previous version, the token "def@" was used. However, since the @ is used (informally) as a replacement for $ in commented out sections that may be deleted some day. While there is no violation of any standard to use the @ in a token, it could make the search for such commented-out sections slightly more difficult. (See remark of Norman Megill at ~ https://groups.google.com/g/metamath/c/cteNUppB6A4 ).
term wdfat (A2 F: class): wff;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the value of a function. Read: "the value of ` F ` at ` A ` " or " ` F ` of ` A ` ". In a previous version, the symbol " ' " was used. However, since the similarity with the symbol ` `` ` used for the current definition of a function's value (see ~ df-fv ), which, by the way, was intended to visualize that in many cases ` `` ` and " ' " are exchangeable, makes reading the theorems, especially those which use both definitions as ~ dfafv2 , very difficult, 3 apostrophes ` ''' ` are used now so that it's easier to distinguish from ~ df-fv and ~ df-ima . And not three backticks ( three times ` `` ` ) since that would be annoying to escape in a comment. (See remark of Norman Megill and Gerard Lang at ~ https://groups.google.com/g/metamath/c/cteNUppB6A4 ).
term cafv (A2 F: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include the value of an operation ` F ` (such as ` + ` ) for two arguments ` A ` and ` B ` . Note that the syntax is simply three class symbols in a row surrounded by a pair of parentheses in contrast to the current definition, see ~ df-ov .
term caov (A2 B2 F: class): class;

--| Definition of the predicate that determines if some class ` F ` is defined as function for an argument ` A ` or, in other words, if the function value for some class ` F ` for an argument ` A ` is defined. We say that ` F ` is defined at ` A ` if a ` F ` is a function restricted to the member ` A ` of its domain. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
axiom df_dfat (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wdfat A2 F) (wa (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wfun (cres F (csn A2)))) $;

--| Alternative definition of the value of a function, ` ( F ''' A ) ` , also known as function application. In contrast to ` ( F `` A ) = (/) ` (see ~ df-fv and ~ ndmfv ), ` ( F ''' A ) = _V ` if F is not defined for A! (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.) (Revised by BJ/AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
axiom df_afv {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cafv A2 F) (caiota x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)) $;

--| Define the value of an operation. In contrast to ~ df-ov , the alternative definition for a function value (see ~ df-afv ) is used. By this, the value of the operation applied to two arguments is the universal class if the operation is not defined for these two arguments. There are still no restrictions of any kind on what those class expressions may be, although only certain kinds of class expressions - a binary operation ` F ` and its arguments ` A ` and ` B ` - will be useful for proving meaningful theorems. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
axiom df_aov (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (cafv (cop A2 B2) F) $;

--| Elemination of a restricted universal quantification under certain conditions. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Aug-2017.)
theorem ralbinrald {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3) (th: wff) (A2 X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel X A2) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv x3) X) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 ps A2) th) $;

--| If a statement holds for all sets, there is not a unique set for which the statement holds. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 28-Nov-2017.)
theorem alneu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wal x3 ph) (wn (weu x3 ph)) $;

--| If there is a unique second component in an ordered pair contained in a given set, the first component must be a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Nov-2017.)
theorem eu2ndop1stv {y1: setvar} (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (weu y1 (wcel (cop A2 (cv y1)) V)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Equality deduction for "defined at". (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem dfateq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wdfat A2 F) (wdfat B2 G)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for "defined at". To prove a deduction version of this theorem is not easily possible because many deduction versions for bound-variable hypothesis builder for constructs the definition of "defined at" is based on are not available (e.g., for Fun/Rel, dom, ` C_ ` , etc.). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem nfdfat {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 (wdfat A2 F) $;

--| Alternate definition of the predicate "defined at" not using the ` Fun ` predicate. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Jul-2017.) (Proof shortened by Peter Mazsa, 2-Oct-2022.)
theorem dfdfat2 {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wdfat A2 F) (wa (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F))) $;

--| A function is defined at any element of its domain. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem fundmdfat (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wdfat A2 F) $;

--| A function is not defined at a proper class. (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatprc (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wn (wdfat A2 F)) $;

--| The value of a function ` F ` at a set ` A ` is in the range of the function ` F ` if ` F ` is defined at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatelrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wcel (cfv A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| Alternative definition of ` ( F ''' A ) ` using ` ( F `` A ) ` directly. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Jul-2017.) (Revised by AV, 25-Aug-2022.)
theorem dfafv2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cafv A2 F) (cif (wdfat A2 F) (cfv A2 F) cvv) $;

--| Equality deduction for function value, analogous to ~ fveq12d . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem afveq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cafv B2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function value, analogous to ~ fveq1 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Jul-2017.)
theorem afveq1 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cafv A2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function value, analogous to ~ fveq1 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Jul-2017.)
theorem afveq2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cafv B2 F)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for function value, analogous to ~ nffv . To prove a deduction version of this analogous to ~ nffvd is not easily possible because a deduction version of ~ nfdfat cannot be shown easily. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem nfafv {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cafv A2 F) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of a function value, analogous to ~ csbfv12 , with a direct proof proposed by Mario Carneiro, analogous to ~ csbov123 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Jul-2017.)
theorem csbafv12g {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (cafv B2 F)) (cafv (csb x3 A2 B2) (csb x3 A2 F))) $;

--| If a class is a function restricted to a member of its domain, then the function value for this member is equal for both definitions. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvfundmfveq (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| If a set is not in the domain of a class or the class is not a function restricted to the set, then the function value for this set is the universe. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem afvnfundmuv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wdfat A2 F)) (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv) $;

--| The value of a class outside its domain is the universe, compare with ~ ndmfv . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem ndmafv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv) $;

--| If the function value of a class for an argument is a set, the argument is contained in the domain of the class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvvdm (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cafv A2 F) B2) (wcel A2 (cdm F)) $;

--| If the restriction of a class to a singleton is not a function, its value is the universe, compare with ~ nfunsn . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem nfunsnafv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wfun (cres F (csn A2)))) (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv) $;

--| If the function value of a class for an argument is a set, the class restricted to the singleton of the argument is a function. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvvfunressn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cafv A2 F) B2) (wfun (cres F (csn A2))) $;

--| A function's value at a proper class is the universe, compare with ~ fvprc . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvprc (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv) $;

--| If a function's value at an argument is a set, the argument is also a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvvv (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cafv A2 F) B2) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| If the value of the alternative function at an argument is the universe, the function's value at this argument is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvpcfv0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv) (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) $;

--| The value of the alternative function at a set as argument equals the function's value at this argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvnufveq (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (cafv A2 F) cvv) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| The value of the alternative function at a set as argument equals the function's value at this argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvvfveq (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cafv A2 F) B2) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| If the value of the alternative function at an argument is the empty set, the function's value at this argument is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afv0fv0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cafv A2 F) c0) (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) $;

--| If the function's value at an argument is not the empty set, it equals the value of the alternative function at this argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvfvn0fveq (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (cfv A2 F) c0) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| The function's value at an argument is an element of a set if and only if the value of the alternative function at this argument is an element of that set, if the set does not contain the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afv0nbfvbi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wnel c0 B2) (wb (wcel (cafv A2 F) B2) (wcel (cfv A2 F) B2)) $;

--| The function's value at an argument is the empty set if and only if the value of the alternative function at this argument is either the empty set or the universe. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvfv0bi (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) (wo (wceq (cafv A2 F) c0) (wceq (cafv A2 F) cvv)) $;

--| The value of a function at a unique point, analogous to ~ fveu . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Nov-2017.)
theorem afveu {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (weu x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cuni (cab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and binary relation, analogous to ~ fnbrfvb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem fnbrafvb (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wb (wceq (cafv B2 F) C) (wbr B2 C F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and ordered pair membership, analogous to ~ fnopfvb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem fnopafvb (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wb (wceq (cafv B2 F) C) (wcel (cop B2 C) F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and binary relation, analogous to ~ funbrfvb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem funbrafvb (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wb (wceq (cafv A2 F) B2) (wbr A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and ordered pair membership, analogous to ~ funopfvb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem funopafvb (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wb (wceq (cafv A2 F) B2) (wcel (cop A2 B2) F)) $;

--| The second argument of a binary relation on a function is the function's value, analogous to ~ funbrfv . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem funbrafv (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wi (wbr A2 B2 F) (wceq (cafv A2 F) B2)) $;

--| Function value in terms of a binary relation, analogous to ~ funbrfv2b . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem funbrafv2b (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wb (wbr A2 B2 F) (wa (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wceq (cafv A2 F) B2))) $;

--| Representation of a function in terms of its values, analogous to ~ dffn5 (only one direction of implication!). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem dfafn5a {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cafv (cv x3) F))) $;

--| Representation of a function in terms of its values, analogous to ~ dffn5 (only if it is assumed that the function value for each x is a set). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem dfafn5b {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wcel (cafv (cv x3) F) V) A2) (wb (wfn F A2) (wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 (cafv (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| The range of a function expressed as a collection of the function's values, analogous to ~ fnrnfv . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem fnrnafv {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wceq (crn F) (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cv y1) (cafv (cv x3) F)) A2))) $;

--| A member of a function's range is a value of the function, analogous to ~ fvelrnb with the additional requirement that the member must be a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvelrnb {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 V)) (wb (wcel B2 (crn F)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cafv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| A member of a function's range is a value of the function, only one direction of implication of ~ fvelrnb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jun-2017.)
theorem afvelrnb0 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wi (wcel B2 (crn F)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cafv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the image of a function, analogous to ~ dfimafn . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem dfaimafn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wss A2 (cdm F))) (wceq (cima F A2) (cab y1 (wrex x3 (wceq (cafv (cv x3) F) (cv y1)) A2))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the image of a function as an indexed union of singletons of function values, analogous to ~ dfimafn2 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem dfaimafn2 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wss A2 (cdm F))) (wceq (cima F A2) (ciun x3 A2 (csn (cafv (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| Function value in an image, analogous to ~ fvelima . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvelima {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cima F B2))) (wrex x3 (wceq (cafv (cv x3) F) A2) B2) $;

--| A function's value belongs to its range, analogous to ~ fvelrn . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem afvelrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wcel (cafv A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| A function's value belongs to its range, analogous to ~ fnfvelrn . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem fnafvelrn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel (cafv B2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| A function's value belongs to its codomain, analogous to ~ ffvelcdm . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem fafvelcdm (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cafv C F) B2) $;

--| A function maps to a class to which all values belong, analogous to ~ ffnfv . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2017.)
theorem ffnafv {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wb (wf A2 B2 F) (wa (wfn F A2) (wral x3 (wcel (cafv (cv x3) F) B2) A2)) $;

--| The value of a restricted function, analogous to ~ fvres . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Jul-2017.)
theorem afvres (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wceq (cafv A2 (cres F B2)) (cafv A2 F)) $;

--| Function value. Theorem 6.12(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27, analogous to ~ tz6.12 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Nov-2017.)
theorem tz6_12_afv {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cop A2 (cv y1)) F) (weu y1 (wcel (cop A2 (cv y1)) F))) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cv y1)) $;

--| Function value (Theorem 6.12(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27, analogous to ~ tz6.12-1 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Nov-2017.)
theorem tz6_12_1_afv {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 (cv y1) F) (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F))) (wceq (cafv A2 F) (cv y1)) $;

--| Domains of a function composition, analogous to ~ dmfco . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Jul-2017.)
theorem dmfcoafv (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun G) (wcel A2 (cdm G))) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm (ccom F G))) (wcel (cafv A2 G) (cdm F))) $;

--| Value of a function composition, analogous to ~ fvco2 . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Jul-2017.)
theorem afvco2 (A2 F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn G A2) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cafv X (ccom F G)) (cafv (cafv X G) F)) $;

--| Two ways to express that a function has a limit, analogous to ~ rlimdm . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 27-Nov-2017.)
theorem rlimdmafv (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm crli)) (wbr F (cafv F crli) crli)) $;

--| Equality deduction for operation value, analogous to ~ oveq123d . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aoveq123d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (caov A2 C F) (caov B2 D G)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for operation value, analogous to ~ nfov . To prove a deduction version of this analogous to ~ nfovd is not quickly possible because many deduction versions for bound-variable hypothesis builder for constructs the definition of alternative operation values is based on are not available (see ~ nfafv ). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem nfaov {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (caov A2 B2 F) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of an operation. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem csbaovg {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C D F: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 D) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (caov B2 C F)) (caov (csb x3 A2 B2) (csb x3 A2 C) (csb x3 A2 F))) $;

--| If a class is a function restricted to an ordered pair of its domain, then the value of the operation on this pair is equal for both definitions. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovfundmoveq (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat (cop A2 B2) F) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| If an ordered pair is not in the domain of a class or the class is not a function restricted to the ordered pair, then the operation value for this pair is the universal class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovnfundmuv (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wdfat (cop A2 B2) F)) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) $;

--| The value of an operation outside its domain, analogous to ~ ndmafv . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaov (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel (cop A2 B2) (cdm F))) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) $;

--| The value of an operation outside its domain, analogous to ~ ndmovg . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovg (A2 B2 R S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cdm F) (cxp R S)) (wn (wa (wcel A2 R) (wcel B2 S)))) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) $;

--| If the operation value of a class for an ordered pair is a set, the ordered pair is contained in the domain of the class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovvdm (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) (wcel (cop A2 B2) (cdm F)) $;

--| If the restriction of a class to a singleton is not a function, its operation value is the universal class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem nfunsnaov (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wfun (cres F (csn (cop A2 B2))))) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) $;

--| If the operation value of a class for an argument is a set, the class restricted to the singleton of the argument is a function. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovvfunressn (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) (wfun (cres F (csn (cop A2 B2)))) $;

--| The value of an operation when the one of the arguments is a proper class, analogous to ~ ovprc . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovprc (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wrel (cdm F) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv))) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) $;

--| Reverse closure for an operation value, analogous to ~ afvvv . In contrast to ~ ovrcl , elementhood of the operation's value in a set is required, not containing an element. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovrcl (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wrel (cdm F) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) $;

--| If the alternative value of the operation on an ordered pair is the universal class, the operation's value at this ordered pair is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovpcov0 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) c0) $;

--| The alternative value of the operation on an ordered pair equals the operation's value at this ordered pair. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovnuoveq (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (caov A2 B2 F) cvv) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| The alternative value of the operation on an ordered pair equals the operation's value on this ordered pair. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovvoveq (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| If the alternative value of the operation on an ordered pair is the empty set, the operation's value at this ordered pair is the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aov0ov0 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) c0) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) c0) $;

--| If the operation's value at an argument is not the empty set, it equals the value of the alternative operation at this argument. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovovn0oveq (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (co A2 B2 F) c0) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (co A2 B2 F)) $;

--| The operation's value on an ordered pair is an element of a set if and only if the alternative value of the operation on this ordered pair is an element of that set, if the set does not contain the empty set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aov0nbovbi (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wnel c0 C) (wb (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) (wcel (co A2 B2 F) C)) $;

--| The operation's value on an ordered pair is the empty set if and only if the alternative value of the operation on this ordered pair is either the empty set or the universal class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovov0bi (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 B2 F) c0) (wo (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) c0) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) cvv)) $;

--| A frequently used special case of ~ rspc2ev for operation values, analogous to ~ rspceov . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem rspceaov {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2 C: class) (D: class x3) (S F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel C A2) (wcel D B2) (wceq S (caov C D F))) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq S (caov (cv x3) (cv y1) F)) B2) A2) $;

--| Equivalence of operation value and ordered triple membership, analogous to ~ fnopfvb . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem fnotaovb (A2 B2 C D R F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wcel C A2) (wcel D B2)) (wb (wceq (caov C D F) R) (wcel (cotp C D R) F)) $;

--| An operation maps to a class to which all values belong, analogous to ~ ffnov . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ffnaov {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wb (wf (cxp A2 B2) C F) (wa (wfn F (cxp A2 B2)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (caov (cv x3) (cv y1) F) C) B2) A2)) $;

--| Closure law for an operation, analogous to ~ fovcl . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem faovcl (A2 B2 C R S F: class):
  $ wf (cxp R S) C F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 R) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) C) $;

--| Value of a function given by the maps-to notation, analogous to ~ ovmpt4g . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aovmpt4g {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3 y1) (V: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2) (wcel C V)) (wceq (caov (cv x3) (cv y1) F) C) $;

--| Domain of closure of an operation. In contrast to ~ oprssdm , no additional property for S ( ` -. (/) e. S ` ) is required! (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem aoprssdm {x3 y1: setvar} (S F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wcel (caov (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wss (cxp S S) (cdm F) $;

--| The "closure" of an operation outside its domain, when the operation's value is a set in contrast to ~ ndmovcl where it is required that the domain contains the empty set ( ` (/) e. S ` ). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovcl (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) S) $ >
  $ wcel (caov A2 B2 F) cvv $ >
  $ wcel (caov A2 B2 F) S $;

--| Reverse closure law, in contrast to ~ ndmovrcl where it is required that the operation's domain doesn't contain the empty set ( ` -. (/) e. S ` ), no additional asumption is required. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovrcl (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (caov A2 B2 F) S) (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S)) $;

--| Any operation is commutative outside its domain, analogous to ~ ndmovcom . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovcom (A2 B2 S F: class):
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S))) (wceq (caov A2 B2 F) (caov B2 A2 F)) $;

--| Any operation is associative outside its domain. In contrast to ~ ndmovass where it is required that the operation's domain doesn't contain the empty set ( ` -. (/) e. S ` ), no additional assumption is required. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovass (A2 B2 C S F: class):
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wi (wn (w3a (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S) (wcel C S))) (wceq (caov (caov A2 B2 F) C F) (caov A2 (caov B2 C F) F)) $;

--| Any operation is distributive outside its domain. In contrast to ~ ndmovdistr where it is required that the operation's domain doesn't contain the empty set ( ` -. (/) e. S ` ), no additional assumption is required. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 26-May-2017.)
theorem ndmaovdistr (A2 B2 C S F G: class):
  $ wceq (cdm F) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wceq (cdm G) (cxp S S) $ >
  $ wi (wn (w3a (wcel A2 S) (wcel B2 S) (wcel C S))) (wceq (caov A2 (caov B2 C F) G) (caov (caov A2 B2 G) (caov A2 C G) F)) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the alternate function value. Read: "the value of ` F ` at ` A ` " or " ` F ` of ` A ` ". For using several apostrophes as a symbol see comment for ~ cafv .
term cafv2 (A2 F: class): class;

--| Alternate definition of the value of a function, ` ( F '''' A ) ` , also known as function application (and called "alternate function value" in the following). In contrast to ` ( F `` A ) = (/) ` (see comment of ~ df-fv , and especially ~ ndmfv ), ` ( F '''' A ) ` is guaranteed not to be in the range of ` F ` if ` F ` is not defined at ` A ` (whereas ` (/) ` can be a member of ` ran F ` ). (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
axiom df_afv2 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cif (wdfat A2 F) (cio x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)) (cpw (cuni (crn F)))) $;

--| If a function is defined at a class ` A ` the alternate function value at ` A ` is the unique value assigned to ` A ` by the function (analogously to ` ( F `` A ) ` ). (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatafv2iota {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cio x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F))) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` if the function is not defined at this set ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem ndfatafv2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wdfat A2 F)) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cpw (cuni (crn F)))) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is undefined if the function, whose range is a set, is not defined at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem ndfatafv2undef (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (crn F) V) (wn (wdfat A2 F))) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cfv (crn F) cund)) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is always a set if the function/class ` F ` is defined at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatafv2ex (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) cvv) $;

--| The alternate function value is always a set if the range of the function is a set. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2ex (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel (crn F) V) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) cvv) $;

--| Equality deduction for function value, analogous to ~ fveq12d . (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2eq12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq F G) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cafv2 B2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function value, analogous to ~ fveq1 . (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2eq1 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cafv2 A2 G)) $;

--| Equality theorem for function value, analogous to ~ fveq2 . (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2eq2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cafv2 B2 F)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for function value, analogous to ~ nffv . To prove a deduction version of this analogous to ~ nffvd is not easily possible because a deduction version of ~ nfdfat cannot be shown easily. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem nfafv2 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cafv2 A2 F) $;

--| Move class substitution in and out of a function value, analogous to ~ csbfv12 , with a direct proof proposed by Mario Carneiro, analogous to ~ csbov123 . (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem csbafv212g {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (csb x3 A2 (cafv2 B2 F)) (cafv2 (csb x3 A2 B2) (csb x3 A2 F))) $;

--| The alternate function value is always a set if the function (resp. the domain of the function) is a set. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem fexafv2ex (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) cvv) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` at which the function is not defined is undefined, i.e., not in the range of the function. (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem ndfatafv2nrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wdfat A2 F)) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| The value of a class outside its domain is not in the range, compare with ~ ndmfv . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem ndmafv2nrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is defined, i.e., in the range of the function if the function is defined at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressndmafv2rn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| Two ways to say that an alternate function value is not defined. (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2ndefb (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cpw (cuni (crn F)))) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| If the restriction of a class to a singleton is not a function, its value at the singleton element is undefined, compare with ~ nfunsn . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem nfunsnafv2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wfun (cres F (csn A2)))) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| A function's value at a proper class is not defined, compare with ~ fvprc . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2prc (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (wcel A2 cvv)) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is defined, i.e. in the range of the function, iff the function is defined at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatafv2rnb (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wdfat A2 F) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| If a set is in the range of a function, the alternate function value at a class ` A ` equals this set or is not in the range of the function iff the alternate function value at the class ` A ` either equals this set or is not in the range of the function. If ` B e/ ran F ` , both disjuncts of the exclusive or can be true: ` ( F '''' A ) = B ` ` -> ( F '''' A ) e/ ran F ` . (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2orxorb (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (crn F)) (wb (wo (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F))) (wxo (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)))) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is defined, i.e., in the range of the function, iff ` A ` is in the domain of the function. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem dmafv2rnb (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfun (cres F (csn A2))) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F))) $;

--| The alternate function value at a class ` A ` is defined, i.e., in the range of the function iff ` A ` is in the domain of the function. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem fundmafv2rnb (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm F)) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F))) $;

--| An alternate function value belongs to the range of the function, analogous to ~ fvelrn . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2elrn (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| If the alternate function value at an argument is the empty set, the function is defined at this argument. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv20defat (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) c0) (wdfat A2 F) $;

--| An alternate function value belongs to the range of the function, analogous to ~ fnfvelrn . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem fnafv2elrn (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wcel (cafv2 B2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| An alternate function value belongs to the codomain of the function, analogous to ~ ffvelcdm . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2022.)
theorem fafv2elcdm (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel C A2)) (wcel (cafv2 C F) B2) $;

--| An alternate function value is defined, i.e., belongs to the range of the function, iff its argument is in the domain of the function. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem fafv2elrnb (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (wb (wcel C A2) (wcel (cafv2 C F) (crn F))) $;

--| If the codomain of a function is a set, the alternate function value is always also a set. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2022.)
theorem fcdmvafv2v (A2 B2 C F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel B2 V)) (wcel (cafv2 C F) cvv) $;

--| Function value when ` F ` is (locally) not a function. Theorem 6.12(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27, analogous to ~ tz6.12-2 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem tz6_12_2_afv2 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wn (weu x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F))) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) $;

--| The value of a function at a unique point, analogous to ~ fveu . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2eu {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (weu x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cuni (cab x3 (wbr A2 (cv x3) F)))) $;

--| The value of a restricted function for an argument at which the function is defined. Analog to ~ fvres . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2res (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat A2 F) (wcel A2 B2)) (wceq (cafv2 A2 (cres F B2)) (cafv2 A2 F)) $;

--| Function value (Theorem 6.12(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27), analogous to ~ tz6.12 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem tz6_12_afv2 {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cop A2 (cv y1)) F) (weu y1 (wcel (cop A2 (cv y1)) F))) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cv y1)) $;

--| Function value (Theorem 6.12(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27), analogous to ~ tz6.12-1 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem tz6_12_1_afv2 {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr A2 (cv y1) F) (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F))) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cv y1)) $;

--| Corollary of Theorem 6.12(1) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27, analogous to ~ tz6.12c . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem tz6_12c_afv2 {y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (weu y1 (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) (wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cv y1)) (wbr A2 (cv y1) F)) $;

--| Corollary of Theorem 6.12(2) of [TakeutiZaring] p. 27. analogous to ~ tz6.12i . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem tz6_12i_afv2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (crn F)) (wi (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wbr A2 B2 F)) $;

--| The second argument of a binary relation on a function is the function's value, analogous to ~ funbrfv . (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2022.)
theorem funressnbrafv2 (A2 B2 F V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wfun (cres F (csn A2)))) (wi (wbr A2 B2 F) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and binary relation, analogous to ~ fnbrfvb or ~ funbrfvb . ` B e. _V ` is required, because otherwise ` A F B <-> (/) e. F ` can be true, but ` ( F '''' A ) = B ` is always false (because of ~ dfatafv2ex ). (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatbrafv2b (A2 B2 F W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat A2 F) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wbr A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and ordered pair membership, analogous to ~ fnopfvb or ~ funopfvb . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatopafv2b (A2 B2 F W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat A2 F) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wcel (cop A2 B2) F)) $;

--| The second argument of a binary relation on a function is the function's value, analogous to ~ funbrfv . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem funbrafv2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfun F) (wi (wbr A2 B2 F) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and binary relation, analogous to ~ fnbrfvb . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem fnbrafv2b (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wb (wceq (cafv2 B2 F) C) (wbr B2 C F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and ordered pair membership, analogous to ~ fnopfvb . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem fnopafv2b (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel B2 A2)) (wb (wceq (cafv2 B2 F) C) (wcel (cop B2 C) F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and binary relation, analogous to ~ funbrfvb . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem funbrafv22b (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wbr A2 B2 F)) $;

--| Equivalence of function value and ordered pair membership, analogous to ~ funopfvb . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem funopafv2b (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel A2 (cdm F))) (wb (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) B2) (wcel (cop A2 B2) F)) $;

--| Singleton of function value, analogous to ~ fnsnfv . (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatsnafv2 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wceq (csn (cafv2 A2 F)) (cima F (csn A2))) $;

--| A definition of function value in terms of iota, analogous to ~ dffv3 . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfafv23 {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cio x3 (wcel (cv x3) (cima F (csn A2))))) $;

--| Domain of a function composition, analogous to ~ dmfco . (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatdmfcoafv2 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 G) (wb (wcel A2 (cdm (ccom F G))) (wcel (cafv2 A2 G) (cdm F))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dfatco . (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatcolem {y1: setvar} (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat X G) (wdfat (cafv2 X G) F)) (weu y1 (wbr X (cv y1) (ccom F G))) $;

--| The predicate "defined at" for a function composition. (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatco (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat X G) (wdfat (cafv2 X G) F)) (wdfat X (ccom F G)) $;

--| Value of a function composition, analogous to ~ fvco2 . (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2co2 (F G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wdfat X G) (wdfat (cafv2 X G) F)) (wceq (cafv2 X (ccom F G)) (cafv2 (cafv2 X G) F)) $;

--| Two ways to express that a function has a limit, analogous to ~ rlimdm . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2022.)
theorem rlimdmafv2 (ph: wff) (A2 F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf A2 cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (csup A2 cxr clt) cpnf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (cdm crli)) (wbr F (cafv2 F crli) crli)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` ( F '''' A ) ` using ` ( F `` A ) ` directly. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfafv22 (A2 F: class):
  $ wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cif (wdfat A2 F) (cfv A2 F) (cpw (cuni (crn F)))) $;

--| If the alternate function value at an argument is undefined, i.e., not in the range of the function, the function's value at this argument is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2ndeffv0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) $;

--| If a function is defined at a class ` A ` , the alternate function value equals the function's value at ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem dfatafv2eqfv (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wdfat A2 F) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| If the alternate function value is defined, i.e., in the range of the function, the alternate function value equals the function's value. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2rnfveq (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| If the alternate function value at an argument is the empty set, the function's value at this argument is the empty set. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv20fv0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) c0) (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) $;

--| If the function's value at an argument is not the empty set, it equals the alternate function value at this argument. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2fvn0fveq (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (cfv A2 F) c0) (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| If the function's value at an argument is the empty set, then the alternate function value at this argument is the empty set or undefined. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2fv0 (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) (wo (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) c0) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F))) $;

--| The function's value at an argument is the empty set if and only if the alternate function value at this argument is the empty set or undefined. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2fv0b (A2 F: class):
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) (wo (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) c0) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F))) $;

--| If a set is in the range of a function, the function's value at an argument is the empty set if and only if the alternate function value at this argument is either the empty set or undefined. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2022.)
theorem afv2fv0xorb (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wcel c0 (crn F)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 F) c0) (wxo (wceq (cafv2 A2 F) c0) (wnel (cafv2 A2 F) (crn F)))) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts: second and forth positions interchanged. (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2022.)
theorem an4com24 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph th) (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Commutative law for a conjunction with a triple conjunction: second and forth positions interchanged. (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2022.)
theorem _3an4ancom24 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) (wa (w3a ph th ch) ps) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts with a triple conjunction. (Contributed by AV, 4-Mar-2022.)
theorem _4an21 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (w3a (wa ph ps) ch th) (wa ps (w3a ph ch th)) $;

--| Extend wff notation to include the 'not element of' relation.
term cnelbr: class;

--| Define negated membership as binary relation. Analogous to ~ df-eprel (the membership relation). (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
axiom df_nelbr {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnelbr (copab x3 y1 (wn (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the negated membership as binary relation. (Proposed by BJ, 27-Dec-2021.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2021.)
theorem dfnelbr2:
  $ wceq cnelbr (cdif (cxp cvv cvv) cep) $;

--| The binary relation of a set not being a member of another set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem nelbr (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cnelbr) (wn (wcel A2 B2))) $;

--| If a set is related to another set by the negated membership relation, then it is not a member of the other set. The other direction of the implication is not generally true, because if ` A ` is a proper class, then ` -. A e. B ` would be true, but not ` A e// B ` . (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem nelbrim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cnelbr) (wn (wcel A2 B2)) $;

--| A set is related to another set by the negated membership relation iff it is not a member of the other set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem nelbrnel (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cnelbr) (wnel A2 B2)) $;

--| If a set is related to another set by the negated membership relation, then it is not a member of the other set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem nelbrnelim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cnelbr) (wnel A2 B2) $;

--| Selecting one of two alternatives within a restricted generalization if one of the alternatives is false. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Oct-2018.)
theorem ralralimp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (th: wff x3) (ta: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wne A2 c0)) (wi (wral x3 (wa (wi ph (wo th ta)) (wn th)) A2) ta) $;

--| The union of singletons consisting of ordered triples which have distinct first and third components are disjunct. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 10-Mar-2018.)
theorem otiunsndisjX {a c: setvar} (B2 V W X: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 X) (wdisj a V (ciun c W (csn (cotp (cv a) B2 (cv c))))) $;

--| Equality of function values with conditional arguments, see also ~ fvif . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-May-2018.)
theorem fvifeq (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 (cif ph B2 C)) (wceq (cfv A2 F) (cif ph (cfv B2 F) (cfv C F))) $;

--| The range of a one-to-one function ` F ` of an unordered pair into a set is the unordered pair of the function values. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Feb-2018.)
theorem rnfdmpr (F V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y W)) (wi (wfn F (cpr X Y)) (wceq (crn F) (cpr (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)))) $;

--| The image of the range of a function ` F ` under a function ` E ` if ` F ` is a function from a pair into the domain of ` E ` . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 2-Feb-2018.)
theorem imarnf1pr (A2 B2 R E F V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y W)) (wi (wa (wa (wf (cpr X Y) (cdm E) F) (wf (cdm E) R E)) (wa (wceq (cfv (cfv X F) E) A2) (wceq (cfv (cfv Y F) E) B2))) (wceq (cima E (crn F)) (cpr A2 B2))) $;

--| A function is an ordered pair iff it is a singleton of an ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 20-Sep-2020.) (Avoid depending on this detail.)
theorem funop1 {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wi (wex x3 (wex y1 (wceq F (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))))) (wb (wfun F) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wceq F (csn (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))))))) $;

--| A function with a domain containing (at least) two different elements is not an ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 21-Sep-2020.) (Avoid depending on this detail.)
theorem fun2dmnopgexmpl (G: class):
  $ wi (wceq G (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1))) (wn (wcel G (cxp cvv cvv))) $;

--| A collection of ordered pairs, the class of all possible second components being a set, with a restriction of a binary relation is a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jan-2021.)
theorem opabresex0d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps th: wff x3 y1) (C: class) (R V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel (cab y1 th) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) ps)) cvv) $;

--| A collection of ordered pairs, the class of all possible second components being a set, is a set. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2021.)
theorem opabbrfex0d {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (th: wff x3 y1) (C: class) (R V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel (cab y1 th) V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) cvv) $;

--| A collection of ordered pairs, the second component being a function, with a restriction of a binary relation is a set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Nov-2017.) (Revised by AV, 15-Jan-2021.)
theorem opabresexd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff x3 y1) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (R U V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wf A2 B2 (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) ps)) cvv) $;

--| A collection of ordered pairs, the second component being a function, is a set. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jan-2021.)
theorem opabbrfexd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (R U V W: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wcel (cv x3) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) (wf A2 B2 (cv y1)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel A2 U) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) C)) (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R)) cvv) $;

--| Build a bijection by restricting the domain of a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2022.)
theorem f1oresf1orab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ch: wff y1) (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (D: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D A2) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) C)) (wb ch (wcel (cv x3) D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o D (crab y1 ch B2) (cres F D)) $;

--| Build a bijection by restricting the domain of a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2022.)
theorem f1oresf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ch: wff y1) (A2 B2: class y1) (D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cv y1)) D) (wa (wcel (cv y1) B2) ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o D (crab y1 ch B2) (cres F D)) $;

--| Build a bijection by restricting the domain of a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2022.)
theorem f1oresf1o2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ch: wff y1) (A2 B2: class y1) (D F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf1o A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss D A2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv y1) (cfv (cv x3) F))) (wb (wcel (cv x3) D) ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o D (crab y1 ch B2) (cres F D)) $;

--| Value of a function mapping a set to a class abstraction restricting a class depending on the argument of the function. More general version of ~ fvmptrabfv , but relying on the fact that out-of-domain arguments evaluate to the empty set, which relies on set.mm's particular encoding. (Contributed by AV, 14-Feb-2022.)
theorem fvmptrab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1) (F: class x3 y1) (M: class x3) (N: class) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (crab y1 ph M)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wceq M N) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wcel N cvv) $ >
  $ wi (wnel X V) (wceq N c0) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv X F) (crab y1 ps N) $;

--| Value of a function mapping a set to a class abstraction restricting the value of another function. See also ~ fvmptrabfv . (Suggested by BJ, 18-Feb-2022.) (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2022.)
theorem fvmptrabdm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1) (F: class y1) (G: class) (V: class y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (crab y1 ph (cfv Y G))) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) X) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel Y (cdm G)) (wcel X (cdm F)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv X F) (crab y1 ps (cfv Y G)) $;

--| ((a-b)+c)-a = c-a holds for complex numbers a,b,c. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Mar-2018.)
theorem cnambpcma (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C caddc) A2 cmin) (co C B2 cmin)) $;

--| ((a+b)-c)+d = ((a+d)+b)-c holds for complex numbers a,b,c,d. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Mar-2018.)
theorem cnapbmcpd (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cc))) (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmin) D caddc) (co (co (co A2 D caddc) B2 caddc) C cmin)) $;

--| The sum of two complex numbers is equal to the difference of these two complex numbers iff the subtrahend is 0. (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2023.)
theorem addsubeq0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 B2 cmin)) (wceq B2 cc0)) $;

--| Addition and subtraction on one side of "less than or equal to". (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Mar-2018.)
theorem leaddsuble (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr (co (co A2 B2 caddc) C cmin) A2 cle)) $;

--| If two real numbers are less than a third real number, the sum of the real numbers is less than twice the third real number. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-May-2018.)
theorem _2leaddle2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr (co A2 B2 caddc) (co c2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| Variant of trichotomy law for 'less than'. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem ltnltne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wa (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)) (wn (wceq B2 A2)))) $;

--| A real number increasd by 1 is less than or equal to the number increased by 2. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem p1lep2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cr) (wbr (co N c1 caddc) (co N c2 caddc) cle) $;

--| If the result of subtracting two numbers is greater than a number, the result of adding one of these subtracted numbers to the number is less than the result of subtracting the other subtracted number only. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 9-Jun-2018.)
theorem ltsubsubaddltsub (J L M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cr) (w3a (wcel L cr) (wcel M cr) (wcel N cr))) (wb (wbr J (co (co L M cmin) N cmin) clt) (wbr (co J M caddc) (co L N cmin) clt)) $;

--| An integer minus 1 is positive under certain circumstances. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 9-Jun-2018.)
theorem zm1nn (J L N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel L cz)) (wi (w3a (wcel J cr) (wbr cc0 J cle) (wbr J (co (co L N cmin) c1 cmin) clt)) (wcel (co L c1 cmin) cn)) $;

--| The sum of a real number and an imaginary number is not a real number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem readdcnnred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdif cc cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel (co A2 B2 caddc) cr) $;

--| The difference of a real number and an imaginary number is not a real number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem resubcnnred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdif cc cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel (co A2 B2 cmin) cr) $;

--| The product of a real number and an imaginary number is not a real number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem recnmulnred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdif cc cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel (co A2 B2 cmul) cr) $;

--| The quotient of an imaginary number and a real number is not a real number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem cndivrenred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cdif cc cr)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel (co B2 A2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| The square root of a negative number is not a real number. (Contributed by AV, 28-Feb-2023.)
theorem sqrtnegnre (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cr) (wbr X cc0 clt)) (wnel (cfv X csqrt) cr) $;

--| Closure law for subtraction of reals, restricted to nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 6-Apr-2018.)
theorem nn0resubcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) cr) $;

--| If an integer is between another integer and its successor, the integer is equal to the other integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem zgeltp1eq (A2 I: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cz) (wcel A2 cz)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 I cle) (wbr I (co A2 c1 caddc) clt)) (wceq I A2)) $;

--| 11 is 1 times 10 to the power of 1, plus 1. (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem _1t10e1p1e11:
  $ wceq (cdc c1 c1) (co (co c1 (co (cdc c1 cc0) c1 cexp) cmul) c1 caddc) $;

--| Add 1 to a 2 digit number with carry. This is a special case of ~ decsucc , but in closed form. As observed by ML, this theorem allows for carrying the 1 down multiple decimal constructors, so we can carry the 1 multiple times down a multi-digit number, e.g., by applying this theorem three times we get ` ( ; ; 9 9 9 + 1 ) = ; ; ; 1 0 0 0 ` . (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2020.) (Revised by ML, 8-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Sep-2021.)
theorem deccarry (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wceq (co (cdc A2 c9) c1 caddc) (cdc (co A2 c1 caddc) cc0)) $;

--| If an integer is greater than or equal to a nonnegative integer, then it is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 27-Aug-2018.)
theorem eluzge0nn0 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wi (wbr cc0 M cle) (wcel N cn0)) $;

--| Negated extended trichotomy law for 'less than' and 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem nltle2tri (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wn (w3a (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C cle) (wbr C A2 cle))) $;

--| Subset relationship for finite sets of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 16-Mar-2018.)
theorem ssfz12 (K L M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel L cz) (wbr K L cle)) (wi (wss (co K L cfz) (co M N cfz)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr L N cle))) $;

--| Membership of an integer in a finite set of sequential integers starting at 0. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 25-May-2018.)
theorem elfz2z (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wa (wbr cc0 K cle) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| If there are two elements in a finite set of sequential integers starting at 0, these two elements as well as the upper bound are nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 7-Apr-2018.)
theorem _2elfz3nn0 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 N cfz))) (w3a (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0) (wcel N cn0)) $;

--| The addition of two members of a finite set of sequential integers starting at 0 is commutative. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-May-2018.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 9-Jun-2018.)
theorem fz0addcom (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 N cfz))) (wceq (co A2 B2 caddc) (co B2 A2 caddc)) $;

--| If the sum of two integers of a 0-based finite set of sequential integers is greater than the upper bound, the difference between one of the integers and the difference between the upper bound and the other integer is in the 0-based finite set of sequential integers with the first integer as upper bound. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 7-Apr-2018.) (Revised by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-May-2018.)
theorem _2elfz2melfz (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 N cfz))) (wi (wbr N (co A2 B2 caddc) clt) (wcel (co B2 (co N A2 cmin) cmin) (co cc0 A2 cfz))) $;

--| The sum of two integers in 0-based finite sets of sequential integers is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem fz0addge0 (A2 B2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 M cfz)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 N cfz))) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 caddc) cle) $;

--| Membership of an integer in a finite set of sequential integers with the integer as upper bound and a lower bound less than or equal to the integer. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2018.)
theorem elfzlble (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel M cn0)) (wcel N (co (co N M cmin) N cfz)) $;

--| Membership of an element of a finite set of sequential integers in a finite set of sequential integers with the same upper bound and a lower bound less than the upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 21-Oct-2018.)
theorem elfzelfzlble (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wbr N (co M K caddc) clt)) (wcel K (co (co N M cmin) N cfz)) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integers starting at 2 is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem elfz2nn (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co c2 N cfz)) (wcel K cn) $;

--| Join a predecessor to the beginning of an open integer interval. Generalization of ~ fzo0sn0fzo1 . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem fzopred (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wbr M N clt)) (wceq (co M N cfzo) (cun (csn M) (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfzo))) $;

--| Join a predecessor and a successor to the beginning and the end of an open integer interval. This theorem holds even if ` N = M ` (then ` ( M ... N ) = { M } = ( { M } u. (/) ) u. { M } ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem fzopredsuc (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cun (cun (csn M) (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfzo)) (csn N))) $;

--| Join 0 and a successor to the beginning and the end of an open integer interval starting at 1. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem _1fzopredsuc (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co cc0 N cfz) (cun (cun (csn cc0) (co c1 N cfzo)) (csn N))) $;

--| An element of an open integer interval starting at 1 joined by 0 and a successor at the beginning and the end is either 0 or an element of the open integer interval or the successor. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem el1fzopredsuc (I N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wcel I (co cc0 N cfz)) (w3o (wceq I cc0) (wcel I (co c1 N cfzo)) (wceq I N))) $;

--| Subtracting a difference from a number which is not less than the difference results in a bounded nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-May-2018.)
theorem subsubelfzo0 (A2 I N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel I (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wn (wbr I (co N A2 cmin) clt))) (wcel (co I (co N A2 cmin) cmin) (co cc0 A2 cfzo)) $;

--| Two functions over a half-open range of nonnegative integers are equal if and only if their domains have the same length and the function values are the same at each position. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Jul-2018.)
theorem _2ffzoeq {i: setvar} (P F M: class) (N X Y: class i):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wa (wf (co cc0 M cfzo) X F) (wf (co cc0 N cfzo) Y P))) (wb (wceq F P) (wa (wceq M N) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) F) (cfv (cv i) P)) (co cc0 M cfzo)))) $;

--| A member of a half-open range of integers starting at 2 is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem elfzo2nn (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co c2 N cfzo)) (wcel K cn) $;

--| If one factor of a product of integers is at least 2 and less then the product, so is the second factor. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem nnmul2 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wcel B2 cn) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) N)) (wcel B2 (co c2 N cfzo)) $;

--| A factor of a product of integers is at least 2 and less then the product iff the second factor is at least 2 and less then the product. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem nnmul2b (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) N)) (wb (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wcel B2 (co c2 N cfzo))) $;

--| The ceiling of half of an integer greater than 2 is greater than or equal to 2. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2025.)
theorem _2ltceilhalf (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wbr c2 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cle) $;

--| The ceiling of half of an integer greater than two is greater than one. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem ceilhalfgt1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wbr c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) clt) $;

--| A positive integer less than (the ceiling of) half of another integer is in the half-open range of positive integers up to the other integer. (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem ceilhalfelfzo1 (J K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wi (wcel K J) (wcel K (co c1 N cfzo))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gpgedgvtx1 . (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgedgvtx1lem (I J N X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel X J)) (wcel X I) $;

--| Two times a positive integer less than (the ceiling of) half of another integer is less than the other integer. This theorem would hold even for integers less than 3, but then a corresponding ` K ` would not exist. (Contributed by AV, 9-Sep-2025.)
theorem _2tceilhalfelfzo1 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo))) (wbr (co c2 K cmul) N clt) $;

--| A condition equivalent to ceiling. Analogous to ~ flbi . (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem ceilbi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cz)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cceil) B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 (co A2 c1 caddc) clt))) $;

--| The ceiling of one half is one. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem ceilhalf1:
  $ wceq (cfv (co c1 c2 cdiv) cceil) c1 $;

--| Half of a real number greater than or equal to two is greater than or equal to one. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem rehalfge1 (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (co c2 cpnf cico)) (wbr c1 (co X c2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The ceiling of half of a positive integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem ceilhalfnn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cn) $;

--| 1 is in the half-open integer range from 1 to the ceiling of half of an integer greater than two is greater than one. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem _1elfzo1ceilhalf1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel c1 (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo)) $;

--| The floor of the reciprocal of an integer greater than 1 is 0. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2026.)
theorem nnge2recfl0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv (co c1 N cdiv) cfl) cc0) $;

--| The floor of an integer minus the reciprocal of a positive integer is the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2026.)
theorem flmrecm1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (cfv (co M (co c1 N cdiv) cmin) cfl) (co M c1 cmin)) $;

--| Expressing the floor of a division by the modulo operator. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem fldivmod (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cfl) (co (co A2 (co A2 B2 cmo) cmin) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Expressing the ceiling of a division by the modulo operator. (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem ceildivmod (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cceil) (co (co A2 (co (co B2 A2 cmin) B2 cmo) caddc) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| The ceiling of half of 5 is 3. (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem ceil5half3:
  $ wceq (cfv (co c5 c2 cdiv) cceil) c3 $;

--| Subtraction and addition modulo a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem submodaddmod (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz))) (wb (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) N cmo) (co (co A2 C cmin) N cmo)) (wceq (co (co A2 (co B2 C caddc) caddc) N cmo) (co A2 N cmo))) $;

--| Two nonnegative integers are not equal modulo a positive modulus if their difference is greater than 0 and less than the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem difltmodne (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wbr c1 (co A2 B2 cmin) cle) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmin) N clt))) (wne (co A2 N cmo) (co B2 N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is not itself plus a positive integer modulo an integer greater than 1 and the positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem zplusmodne (A2 K N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 cz) (wcel K (co c1 N cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 K caddc) N cmo) (co A2 N cmo)) $;

--| The sum of a nonnegative integer and a positive integer modulo a number greater than both integers is not equal to the nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 27-Aug-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem addmodne (A2 B2 M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn) (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wbr A2 M clt)) (wa (wcel B2 cn) (wbr B2 M clt))) (wne (co (co A2 B2 caddc) M cmo) A2) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is not itself plus a positive integer less than 5 modulo 5. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem plusmod5ne (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 c5 cfzo)) (wcel K (co c1 c5 cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 K caddc) c5 cmo) A2) $;

--| An integer is not itself plus 1 modulo an integer greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem zp1modne (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 cz)) (wne (co (co A2 c1 caddc) N cmo) (co A2 N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is not itself plus 1 modulo an integer greater than 1 and the nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem p1modne (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 c1 caddc) N cmo) A2) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is not itself minus 1 modulo an integer greater than 1 and the nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem m1modne (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmo) A2) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is not itself minus a positive integer less than 5 modulo 5. (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem minusmod5ne (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 c5 cfzo)) (wcel K (co c1 c5 cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 K cmin) c5 cmo) A2) $;

--| The difference of an element of a half-open range of nonnegative integers and the upper bound of this range modulo an integer greater than the upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 1-Sep-2025.)
theorem submodlt (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) (wbr B2 N clt)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) N cmo) (co (co N A2 caddc) B2 cmin)) $;

--| An integer minus ` B ` is not itself plus ` C ` modulo an integer greater than the sum of ` B ` and ` C ` . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem submodneaddmod (A2 B2 C N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz)) (wa (wbr c1 (co B2 C caddc) cle) (wbr (co B2 C caddc) N clt))) (wne (co (co A2 B2 caddc) N cmo) (co (co A2 C cmin) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer minus 1 is not itself plus 2 modulo an integer greater than 3 and the nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem m1modnep2mod (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wcel A2 cz)) (wne (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmo) (co (co A2 c2 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer minus a positive integer 1 or 2 is not itself plus 2 times the positive integer modulo 5. (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2025.)
theorem minusmodnep2tmod (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 (co c1 c3 cfzo))) (wne (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c5 cmo) (co (co A2 (co c2 B2 cmul) caddc) c5 cmo)) $;

--| An integer decreased by 1 is 0 modulo a positive integer iff the integer is 1 modulo the same modulus. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem m1mod0mod1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cr) (wbr c1 N clt)) (wb (wceq (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmo) cc0) (wceq (co A2 N cmo) c1)) $;

--| An integer modulo 2 is either 0 or 1. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 3-Jul-2020.)
theorem elmod2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wcel (co N c2 cmo) (cpr cc0 c1)) $;

--| If an integer is 0 modulo a positive integer, this integer must be a multiple of the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem mod0mul {x3: setvar} (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn)) (wi (wceq (co A2 N cmo) cc0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) N cmul)) cz)) $;

--| If an integer is not 0 modulo a positive integer, this integer must be the sum of a multiple of the modulus and a positive integer less than the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem modn0mul {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn)) (wi (wne (co A2 N cmo) cc0) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (co (cv x3) N cmul) (cv y1) caddc)) (co c1 N cfzo)) cz)) $;

--| An integer decreased by 1 modulo a positive integer minus the integer modulo the same modulus is either -1 or the modulus minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem m1modmmod (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmo) (co A2 N cmo) cmin) (cif (wceq (co A2 N cmo) cc0) (co N c1 cmin) (cneg c1))) $;

--| The difference between an integer modulo a positive integer and the integer decreased by 1 modulo the same modulus is less than the modulus decreased by 1 (if the modulus is greater than 2). This theorem would not be valid for an odd ` A ` and ` N = 2 ` , since ` ( ( A mod N ) - ( ( A - 1 ) mod N ) ) ` would be ` ( 1 - 0 ) = 1 ` which is not less than ` ( N - 1 ) = 1 ` . (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2012.) (Proof shortened by SN, 27-Nov-2025.)
theorem difmodm1lt (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn) (wbr c2 N clt)) (wbr (co (co A2 N cmo) (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmo) cmin) (co N c1 cmin) clt) $;

--| 8 modulo 5 is 3. (Contributed by AV, 20-Nov-2025.)
theorem _8mod5e3:
  $ wceq (co c8 c5 cmo) c3 $;

--| If an integer minus a constant equals another integer plus the constant modulo ` N ` , then the first integer plus the constant equals the second integer minus the constant modulo ` N ` iff the fourfold of the constant is a multiple of ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem modmkpkne (K N X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel X cz) (wcel Y cz) (wcel K cz))) (wi (wceq (co (co Y K cmin) N cmo) (co (co X K caddc) N cmo)) (wb (wceq (co (co Y K caddc) N cmo) (co (co X K cmin) N cmo)) (wceq (co (co c4 K cmul) N cmo) cc0))) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than the modulus plus/minus a positive integer less than (the ceiling of) half of the modulus are not equal modulo the modulus. For this theorem, it is essential that ` K < ( N / 2 ) ` ! (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2025.) (Revised by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem modmknepk (I J K N Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel Y I) (wcel K J)) (wne (co (co Y K cmin) N cmo) (co (co Y K caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| An integer with an absolute value less than a positive integer is 0 modulo the positive integer iff it is 0. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2025.)
theorem modlt0b (N X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel X cz) (wbr (cfv X cabs) N clt)) (wb (wceq (co X N cmo) cc0) (wceq X cc0)) $;

--| The sums of a nonnegative integer less than the modulus and two integers whose difference is less than the modulus are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem mod2addne (A2 B2 I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (w3a (wcel X I) (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co c1 N cfzo))) (wne (co (co X A2 caddc) N cmo) (co (co X B2 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than a modulus greater than 2 plus/minus one are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem modm1nep1 (I N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel Y I)) (wne (co (co Y c1 cmin) N cmo) (co (co Y c1 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than a modulus greater than 4 plus one/minus two are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem modm2nep1 (I N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel Y I)) (wne (co (co Y c2 cmin) N cmo) (co (co Y c1 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than a modulus greater than 4 plus one/plus two are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem modp2nep1 (I N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel Y I)) (wne (co (co Y c2 caddc) N cmo) (co (co Y c1 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than a modulus greater than 4 plus one/minus two are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem modm1nep2 (I N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel Y I)) (wne (co (co Y c1 cmin) N cmo) (co (co Y c2 caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer less than a modulus greater than 4 minus one/minus two are not equal modulo the modulus. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem modm1nem2 (I N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel Y I)) (wne (co (co Y c1 cmin) N cmo) (co (co Y c2 cmin) N cmo)) $;

--| If an integer minus one equals another integer plus one modulo an integer greater than 4, then the first integer plus one is not equal to the second integer minus one modulo the same modulus. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem modm1p1ne (I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel X I) (wcel Y I)) (wi (wceq (co (co Y c1 cmin) N cmo) (co (co X c1 caddc) N cmo)) (wne (co (co Y c1 caddc) N cmo) (co (co X c1 cmin) N cmo))) $;

--| Ordering relation for a strictly monotonic sequence, increasing case. Analogous to ~ monoord (except that the case ` M = N ` must be excluded). Duplicate of ~ monoords ? (Contributed by AV, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem smonoord {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv N F) clt) $;

--| Two times an integer greater than 2 is less than the square of the integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2026.)
theorem _2timesltsq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv c3 cuz)) (wbr (co c2 A2 cmul) (co A2 c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| Two times an integer greater than 2 is less than the square of the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem _2timesltsqm1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv c3 cuz)) (wbr (co c2 A2 cmul) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) clt) $;

--| A finite sum with one of its integer summands removed is a real number. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Aug-2018.)
theorem fsummsndifre {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k) (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cfn) (wral k (wcel B2 cz) A2)) (wcel (csu k (cdif A2 (csn X)) B2) cr) $;

--| Separate out a term in a finite sum by splitting the sum into two parts. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Aug-2018.)
theorem fsumsplitsndif {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k) (X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel X A2) (wral k (wcel B2 cz) A2)) (wceq (csu k A2 B2) (co (csu k (cdif A2 (csn X)) B2) (csb k X B2) caddc)) $;

--| A finite sum of summands modulo a positive number with one of its summands removed is a real number. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Aug-2018.)
theorem fsummmodsndifre {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k) (N X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel N cn) (wral k (wcel B2 cz) A2)) (wcel (csu k (cdif A2 (csn X)) (co B2 N cmo)) cr) $;

--| A finite sum of summands modulo a positive number with an additional summand is an integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Sep-2018.)
theorem fsummmodsnunz {k: setvar} (z: setvar) (A2: class) (B2: class k) (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cfn) (wcel N cn) (wral k (wcel B2 cz) (cun A2 (csn (cv z))))) (wcel (csu k (cun A2 (csn (cv z))) (co B2 N cmo)) cz) $;

--| Definition of the divides relation for divisors greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem nndivides2 {n: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (co c2 N cfzo)) (wcel N cn)) (wb (wbr M N cdvds) (wrex n (wceq (co (cv n) M cmul) N) (co c2 N cfzo))) $;

--| The factorial of a nonnegative integer divides the factorial of an integer which is greater than or equal to the first integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2026.)
theorem facnn0dvdsfac (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) (wbr (cfv M cfa) (cfv N cfa) cdvds) $;

--| The product of two different positive integers divides the factorial of the bigger integer. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2026.)
theorem muldvdsfacgt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co c1 B2 cfzo)) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) (cfv B2 cfa) cdvds) $;

--| The product of two different positive integers less than a third integer divides the factorial of the third integer decreased by 1. By assumption, the third integer must be greater than 3. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2026.)
theorem muldvdsfacm1 (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co c1 B2 cfzo)) (wcel B2 (co c1 N cfzo))) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) cdvds) $;

--| The injected slot is an element of the structure with replacement. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsidel (ph: wff) (A2 B2 R S V W: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq R (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop A2 B2) R) $;

--| The injected slot is an element of the structure with replacement. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsnidel (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D R S V W X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 W) $ >
  $ wceq R (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop C D) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop C D) R) $;

--| The value of the structure replacement function is a set. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2021.)
theorem setsv (A2 B2 S V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (co S (cop A2 B2) csts) cvv) $;

--| The preimage of a function value at ` X ` contains ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2024.)
theorem preimafvsnel (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel X A2)) (wcel X (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv X F)))) $;

--| The preimage of a function value is not empty. (Contributed by AV, 7-Mar-2024.)
theorem preimafvn0 (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel X A2)) (wne (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv X F))) c0) $;

--| The union of the image of a subset ` S ` of the domain of a function with elements having the same function value is the function value at one of the elements of ` S ` . (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem uniimafveqt {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3) (S F X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 B2 F) (wss S A2) (wcel X S)) (wi (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv X F)) S) (wceq (cuni (cima F S)) (cfv X F))) $;

--| The union of the image of the preimage of a function value is the function value. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2024.)
theorem uniimaprimaeqfv (A2 F X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel X A2)) (wceq (cuni (cima F (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv X F))))) (cfv X F)) $;

--| The class ` P ` of all preimages of function values is a set. (Contributed by AV, 10-Mar-2024.)
theorem setpreimafvex {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (F: class) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel P cvv) $;

--| The characterization of an element of the class ` P ` of all preimages of function values. (Contributed by AV, 10-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafvb {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wb (wcel S P) (wrex x3 (wceq S (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $;

--| An element of the class ` P ` of all preimages of function values. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S P) (wrex x3 (wceq S (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2) $;

--| An element of the class ` P ` of all preimages of function values is a subset of the domain of the function. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafvssdm {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel S P)) (wss S A2) $;

--| There is an element in a preimage ` S ` of function values so that ` S ` is the preimage of the function value at this element. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2024.)
theorem fvelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel S P)) (wrex x3 (wceq S (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) S) $;

--| The preimage of a function value is an element of the class ` P ` of all preimages of function values. (Contributed by AV, 10-Mar-2024.)
theorem preimafvelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (F: class) (V: class x3 z) (X: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 V) (wcel X A2)) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv X F))) P) $;

--| The class ` P ` of all preimages of function values is a subset of the power set of the domain of the function. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem preimafvsspwdm {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wss P (cpw A2)) $;

--| The empty set is not an element of the class ` P ` of all preimages of function values. (Contributed by AV, 6-Mar-2024.)
theorem _0nelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wnel c0 P) $;

--| An element of the preimage of a function value is an element of the domain of the function with the same value as another element of the preimage. (Contributed by AV, 9-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafvbi {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class) (X Y: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel S P) (wcel X S)) (wb (wcel Y S) (wa (wcel Y A2) (wceq (cfv Y F) (cfv X F)))) $;

--| The elements of the preimage of a function value have the same function values. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafveqfv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class) (X Y: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (w3a (wcel S P) (wcel X S) (wcel Y S))) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)) $;

--| If an element of the domain of the function has the same function value as an element of the preimage of a function value, then it is an element of the same preimage. (Contributed by AV, 9-Mar-2024.)
theorem eqfvelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class) (X Y: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel S P) (wcel X S)) (wi (wa (wcel Y A2) (wceq (cfv Y F) (cfv X F))) (wcel Y S)) $;

--| An element of the preimage of a function value expressed as a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 9-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafvrab {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (S F: class) (X: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel S P) (wcel X S)) (wceq S (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv X F)) A2)) $;

--| The image of an element of the preimage of a function value is the singleton consisting of the function value at one of its elements. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem imaelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (S F: class) (X: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel S P) (wcel X S)) (wceq (cima F S) (csn (cfv X F))) $;

--| The union of the image of an element of the preimage of a function value is an element of the range of the function. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.) (Revised by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem uniimaelsetpreimafv {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (S F: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel S P)) (wcel (cuni (cima F S)) (crn F)) $;

--| If two preimages of function values contain elements with identical function values, then both preimages are equal. (Contributed by AV, 8-Mar-2024.)
theorem elsetpreimafveq {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (R S F: class) (X Y: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wa (wcel S P) (wcel R P)) (wa (wcel X S) (wcel Y R))) (wi (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv Y F)) (wceq S R)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ fundcmpsurinj . (Contributed by AV, 4-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjlem1 {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (F: class) (G: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wceq (crn G) P $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ fundcmpsurinj . (Contributed by AV, 4-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjlem2 {x3 z: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class x3 z) (F: class) (G: class x3 z) (V: class z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 V)) (wfo A2 P G) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ fundcmpsurinj . (Contributed by AV, 3-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjlem3 {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class p) (P: class x3 z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p) (X: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfun F) (wcel X P)) (wceq (cfv X H) (cuni (cima F X))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasetpreimafvbij : the mapping ` H ` is a function into the range of function ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbijlemf {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wf P (cima F A2) H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasetpreimafvbij : the value of the mapping ` H ` at a preimage of a value of function ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbijlemfv {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p) (X: class z) (Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wfn F A2) (wcel Y P) (wcel X Y)) (wceq (cfv Y H) (cfv X F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasetpreimafvbij : for a preimage of a value of function ` F ` there is an element of the preimage so that the value of the mapping ` H ` at this preimage is the function value at this element. (Contributed by AV, 5-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbijlemfv1 {x3 y1 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 y1 z p) (X: class):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel X P)) (wrex y1 (wceq (cfv X H) (cfv (cv y1) F)) X) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasetpreimafvbij : the mapping ` H ` is an injective function into the range of function ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Mar-2024.) (Revised by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbijlemf1 {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wfn F A2) (wf1 P (cima F A2) H) $;

--| Lemma for ~ imasetpreimafvbij : the mapping ` H ` is a function onto the range of function ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbijlemfo {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 V)) (wfo P (cima F A2) H) $;

--| The mapping ` H ` is a bijective function between the set ` P ` of all preimages of values of function ` F ` and the range of ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem imasetpreimafvbij {x3 z p: setvar} (A2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (H: class x3 z p) (V: class x3 z):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt p P (cuni (cima F (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F A2) (wcel A2 V)) (wf1o P (cima F A2) H) $;

--| Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective function onto ` P ` , a bijective function from ` P ` and an injective function into the codomain of ` F ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurbijinjpreimafv {x3 z g2 h i: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (V: class z g2 h i):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V)) (wex g2 (wex h (wex i (wa (w3a (wfo A2 P (cv g2)) (wf1o P (cima F A2) (cv h)) (wf1 (cima F A2) B2 (cv i))) (wceq F (ccom (ccom (cv i) (cv h)) (cv g2))))))) $;

--| Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective function onto ` P ` and an injective function from ` P ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 22-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjpreimafv {x3 z g2 h: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (P: class z) (F: class) (V: class z h):
  $ wceq P (cab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (cima (ccnv F) (csn (cfv (cv x3) F)))) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V)) (wex g2 (wex h (w3a (wfo A2 P (cv g2)) (wf1 P B2 (cv h)) (wceq F (ccom (cv h) (cv g2)))))) $;

--| Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective and an injective function. (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinj {g2 h p: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V: class h p):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V)) (wex g2 (wex h (wex p (w3a (wfo A2 (cv p) (cv g2)) (wf1 (cv p) B2 (cv h)) (wceq F (ccom (cv h) (cv g2))))))) $;

--| Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective, a bijective and an injective function. (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurbijinj {g2 h i q p: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V: class h i q p):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V)) (wex g2 (wex h (wex i (wex p (wex q (wa (w3a (wfo A2 (cv p) (cv g2)) (wf1o (cv p) (cv q) (cv h)) (wf1 (cv q) B2 (cv i))) (wceq F (ccom (ccom (cv i) (cv h)) (cv g2))))))))) $;

--| Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective function onto the image ` ( F " A ) ` of the domain of ` F ` and an injective function from the image ` ( F " A ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 17-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjimaid {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (G: class x3) (H I: class):
  $ wceq I (cima F A2) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 A2 (cfv (cv x3) F)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres cid I) $ >
  $ wi (wf A2 B2 F) (w3a (wfo A2 I G) (wf1 I B2 H) (wceq F (ccom H G))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ fundcmpsurinj , based on ~ fundcmpsurinjimaid : Every function ` F : A --> B ` can be decomposed into a surjective and an injective function. (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2024.)
theorem fundcmpsurinjALT {g2 h p: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (V: class g2 h p):
  $ wi (wa (wf A2 B2 F) (wcel A2 V)) (wex g2 (wex h (wex p (w3a (wfo A2 (cv p) (cv g2)) (wf1 (cv p) B2 (cv h)) (wceq F (ccom (cv h) (cv g2))))))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the partitions of a closed interval of extended reals.
term ciccp: class;

--| Define partitions of a closed interval of extended reals. Such partitions are finite increasing sequences of extended reals. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2020.)
axiom df_iccp {i m p: setvar}:
  $ wceq ciccp (cmpt m cn (crab p (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 (cv m) cfzo)) (co cxr (co cc0 (cv m) cfz) cmap))) $;

--| Partition consisting of a fixed number ` M ` of parts. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpval {i p: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wceq (cfv M ciccp) (crab p (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) clt) (co cc0 M cfzo)) (co cxr (co cc0 M cfz) cmap))) $;

--| A special partition. Corresponds to ~ fourierdlem2 in GS's mathbox. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpart {i: setvar} (P M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wb (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) (wa (wcel P (co cxr (co cc0 M cfz) cmap)) (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) clt) (co cc0 M cfzo)))) $;

--| Implications for a class being a partition. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartimp (P I M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn) (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wa (wcel P (co cxr (co cc0 M cfz) cmap)) (wbr (cfv I P) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) P) clt)) $;

--| The restriction of a partition is a partition. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartres (P M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn) (wcel P (cfv (co M c1 caddc) ciccp))) (wcel (cres P (co cc0 M cfz)) (cfv M ciccp)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and bounds are extended real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartxr (ph: wff) (P I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv I P) cxr) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the upper bound are strictly greater than the preceeding intermediate points or lower bound. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartgtprec (ph: wff) (P I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 M cfz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co I c1 cmin) P) (cfv I P) clt) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points are real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartipre (ph: wff) (P I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I (co c1 M cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv I P) cr) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points are strictly less than the upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartiltu {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv M P) clt) (co c1 M cfzo)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points are strictly greater than the lower bound. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartigtl {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv (cv i) P) clt) (co c1 M cfzo)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then the lower bound is strictly less than the upper bound. Corresponds to ~ fourierdlem11 in GS's mathbox. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartlt (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) clt) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the lower bound are strictly less than the upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartltu {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv M P) clt) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the upper bound are strictly greater than the lower bound. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartgtl {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv (cv i) P) clt) (co c1 M cfz)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the bounds are strictly ordered. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartgt {i j: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wral j (wi (wbr (cv i) (cv j) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (cv j) P) clt)) (co cc0 M cfz)) (co cc0 M cfz)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the lower and the upper bound are less than or equal to the upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartleu {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv M P) cle) (co cc0 M cfz)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and the upper and the lower bound are greater than or equal to the lower bound. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartgel {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (wbr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv (cv i) P) cle) (co cc0 M cfz)) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and bounds are contained in a closed interval of extended reals. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartrn (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (crn P) (co (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) cicc)) $;

--| The range of the partition is between its starting point and its ending point. Corresponds to ~ fourierdlem15 in GS's mathbox. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartf (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 M cfz) (co (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) cicc) P) $;

--| If there is a partition, then all intermediate points and bounds are contained in a closed interval of extended reals. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartel (ph: wff) (P I M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfz))) (wcel (cfv I P) (co (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) cicc)) $;

--| An element of any partitioned half-open interval of extended reals is an element of a part of this partition. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccelpart {i p: setvar} (M X: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn) (wral p (wi (wcel X (co (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cfv M (cv p)) cico)) (wrex i (wcel X (co (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv p)) cico)) (co cc0 M cfzo))) (cfv M ciccp)) $;

--| A half-open interval of extended reals is the union of the parts of its partition. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartiun {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) cico) (ciun i (co cc0 M cfzo) (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cico))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ icceuelpart . (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem icceuelpartlem (ph: wff) (P I J M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo)) (wcel J (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wi (wbr I J clt) (wbr (cfv (co I c1 caddc) P) (cfv J P) cle))) $;

--| An element of a partitioned half-open interval of extended reals is an element of exactly one part of the partition. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem icceuelpart {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co (cfv cc0 P) (cfv M P) cico))) (wreu i (wcel X (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cico)) (co cc0 M cfzo)) $;

--| The segments of a partitioned half-open interval of extended reals are a disjoint collection. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartdisj {i: setvar} (ph: wff) (P M: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wdisj i (co cc0 M cfzo) (co (cfv (cv i) P) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) P) cico)) $;

--| A point of a partition is not an element of any open interval determined by the partition. Corresponds to ~ fourierdlem12 in GS's mathbox. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 8-Jul-2020.)
theorem iccpartnel (ph: wff) (P I M X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (cfv M ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (crn P)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel I (co cc0 M cfzo))) (wn (wcel X (co (cfv I P) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) P) cioo))) $;

--| If a class is a function, then the values of the "shifted function" correspond to the function values of the class. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Nov-2017.)
theorem fargshiftfv {x3: setvar} (E F: class) (G N: class x3) (X: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wf (co c1 N cfz) (cdm E) F)) (wi (wcel X (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wceq (cfv X G) (cfv (co X c1 caddc) F))) $;

--| If a class is a function, then also its "shifted function" is a function. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Nov-2017.)
theorem fargshiftf {x3: setvar} (E F: class) (G N: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wf (co c1 N cfz) (cdm E) F)) (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cdm E) G) $;

--| If a function is 1-1, then also the shifted function is 1-1. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Nov-2017.)
theorem fargshiftf1 {x3: setvar} (E F: class) (G N: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wf1 (co c1 N cfz) (cdm E) F)) (wf1 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cdm E) G) $;

--| If a function is onto, then also the shifted function is onto. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 24-Nov-2017.)
theorem fargshiftfo {x3: setvar} (E F: class) (G: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wfo (co c1 N cfz) (cdm E) F)) (wfo (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cdm E) G) $;

--| The values of a shifted function correspond to the value of the original function. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 24-Nov-2017.)
theorem fargshiftfva {x3 k l: setvar} (P: class x3) (E F: class) (G: class x3 l) (N: class):
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wf (co c1 N cfz) (cdm E) F)) (wi (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) E) (csb x3 (cv k) P)) (co c1 N cfz)) (wral l (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv l) G) E) (csb x3 (co (cv l) c1 caddc) P)) (co cc0 N cfzo))) $;

--| The last symbol of a nonempty word exists. The empty set must be excluded as symbol, because otherwise, it cannot be distinguished between valid cases ( ` (/) ` is the last symbol) and invalid cases ( ` (/) ` means that no last symbol exists). This is because of the special definition of a function in set.mm. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Mar-2018.)
theorem lswn0 (V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W (cword V)) (wnel c0 V) (wne (cfv W chash) cc0)) (wne (cfv W clsw) c0) $;

--| Extend wff notation to include the property of a wff ` ph ` that the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable. Read this notation as " ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable in wff ` ph ` ".
term wich {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1): wff;

--| Define the property of a wff ` ph ` that the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable. For an alternate definition using implicit substitution and a temporary setvar variable see ~ ichcircshi . Another, equivalent definition using two temporary setvar variables is provided in ~ dfich2 . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
axiom df_ich {x3 y1 a: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wich x3 y1 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 (wb (wsb a (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph a) y1) x3) ph))) $;

--| Define the property of a wff ` ph ` that the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable. For an alternate definition using implicit substitution and a temporary setvar variable see ~ ichcircshi . Another, equivalent definition using two temporary setvar variables is provided in ~ dfich2 . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
axiom df_ich_b {x3 a: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wich x3 x3 ph) (wal x3 (wal x3 (wb (wsb a (wsb x3 (wsb x3 ph a) x3) x3) ph))) $;

--| The first interchangeable setvar variable is not free. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2023.)
theorem nfich1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 (wich x3 y1 ph) $;

--| The second interchangeable setvar variable is not free. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2023.)
theorem nfich2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 (wich x3 y1 ph) $;

--| Setvar variables are interchangeable in a wff they do not appear in. (Contributed by SN, 23-Nov-2023.)
theorem ichv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wich x3 y1 ph $;

--| Setvar variables are interchangeable in a wff they are not free in. (Contributed by SN, 23-Nov-2023.)
theorem ichf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wich x3 y1 ph $;

--| A setvar variable is always interchangeable with itself. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem ichid {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wich x3 x3 ph $;

--| A theorem is interchangeable. (Contributed by SN, 4-May-2024.)
theorem icht {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wich x3 y1 ph $;

--| Formula building rule for interchangeability (deduction). (Contributed by SN, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ichbidv {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wich x3 y1 ps) (wich x3 y1 ch)) $;

--| The setvar variables are interchangeable if they can be circularily shifted using a third setvar variable, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem ichcircshi {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps: wff y1 z) (ch: wff x3 z):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv x3)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cv y1)) (wb ch ph) $ >
  $ wich x3 y1 ph $;

--| If two setvar variables are interchangeable in two wffs, then they are interchangeable in the conjunction of these two wffs. Notice that the reverse implication is not necessarily true. Corresponding theorems will hold for other commutative operations, too. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2023.) Use ~ df-ich instead of ~ dfich2 to reduce axioms. (Revised by SN, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ichan {a b: setvar} (ph ps: wff a b):
  $ wi (wa (wich a b ph) (wich a b ps)) (wich a b (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Negation does not affect interchangeability. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2023.)
theorem ichn {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b):
  $ wb (wich a b ph) (wich a b (wn ph)) $;

--| Formula building rule for implication in interchangeability. (Contributed by SN, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ichim {a b: setvar} (ph ps: wff a b):
  $ wi (wa (wich a b ph) (wich a b ps)) (wich a b (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the property of a wff ` ph ` that the setvar variables ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable. (Contributed by AV and WL, 6-Aug-2023.)
theorem dfich2 {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wich x3 y1 ph) (wal a (wal b (wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph b) a) (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph a) b)))) $;

--| The interchangeability of setvar variables is commutative. (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2023.)
theorem ichcom {x3 y1: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wb (wich x3 y1 ps) (wich y1 x3 ps) $;

--| Equivalence for interchangeable setvar variables. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem ichbi12i {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ps: wff x3 y1) (ch: wff a b):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv a)) (wceq (cv y1) (cv b))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wb (wich x3 y1 ps) (wich a b ch) $;

--| In an equality for the same setvar variable, the setvar variable is interchangeable by itself. Special case of ~ ichid and ~ icheq without distinct variables restriction. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem icheqid {x3: setvar}:
  $ wich x3 x3 (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| In an equality of setvar variables, the setvar variables are interchangeable. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem icheq {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wich x3 y1 (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ichnfim : A substitution for a nonfree variable has no effect. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-Aug-2023.) Avoid ~ ax-13 . (Revised by GG, 1-May-2024.)
theorem ichnfimlem {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a b):
  $ wi (wal y1 (wnf x3 ph)) (wb (wsb x3 (wsb y1 ph b) a) (wsb y1 ph b)) $;

--| If in an interchangeability context ` x ` is not free in ` ph ` , the same holds for ` y ` . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-Aug-2023.) (Revised by AV, 23-Sep-2023.)
theorem ichnfim {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wal y1 (wnf x3 ph)) (wich x3 y1 ph)) (wal x3 (wnf y1 ph)) $;

--| If ` x ` and ` y ` are interchangeable in ` ph ` , they are both free or both not free in ` ph ` . (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 6-Aug-2023.) (Revised by AV, 23-Sep-2023.)
theorem ichnfb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wich x3 y1 ph) (wb (wal x3 (wnf y1 ph)) (wal y1 (wnf x3 ph))) $;

--| Move a universal quantifier inside interchangeability. (Contributed by SN, 30-Aug-2023.)
theorem ichal {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 a b):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wich a b ph)) (wich a b (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Two setvar variables are always interchangeable when there are two universal quantifiers. (Contributed by SN, 23-Nov-2023.)
theorem ich2al {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wich x3 y1 (wal x3 (wal y1 ph)) $;

--| Two setvar variables are always interchangeable when there are two existential quantifiers. (Contributed by SN, 23-Nov-2023.)
theorem ich2ex {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wich x3 y1 (wex x3 (wex y1 ph)) $;

--| Example for interchangeable setvar variables in a statement of predicate calculus with equality. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2023.)
theorem ichexmpl1 {a b c: setvar}:
  $ wich a b (wex a (wex b (wex c (w3a (wceq (cv a) (cv b)) (wne (cv a) (cv c)) (wne (cv b) (cv c)))))) $;

--| Example for interchangeable setvar variables in an arithmetic expression. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2023.)
theorem ichexmpl2 {a b: setvar} (c: setvar):
  $ wich a b (wi (w3a (wcel (cv a) cc) (wcel (cv b) cc) (wcel (cv c) cc)) (wceq (co (co (cv a) c2 cexp) (co (cv b) c2 cexp) caddc) (co (cv c) c2 cexp))) $;

--| If the setvar variables are interchangeable in a wff, there is an ordered pair fulfilling the wff iff there is an unordered pair fulfilling the wff. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jul-2023.)
theorem ich2exprop {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2 B2 X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X) (wich a b ph)) (wb (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ph)))) $;

--| If the setvar variables are interchangeable in a wff, there is never a unique ordered pair with different components fulfilling the wff (because if ` <. a , b >. ` fulfils the wff, then also ` <. b , a >. ` fulfils the wff). (Contributed by AV, 27-Aug-2023.)
theorem ichnreuop {p a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (X: class a b):
  $ wi (wich a b ph) (wn (wreu p (wex a (wex b (w3a (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) (wne (cv a) (cv b)) ph))) (cxp X X))) $;

--| If the setvar variables are interchangeable in a wff, and there is a unique ordered pair fulfilling the wff, then both setvar variables must be equal. (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2023.)
theorem ichreuopeq {p a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (X: class):
  $ wi (wich a b ph) (wi (wreu p (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (cxp X X)) (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv a) (cv b)) ph)))) $;

--| Two identical representations of the class of all unordered pairs. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprid {p a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cab p (wrex a (wrex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) cvv) cvv)) (cab p (wex a (wex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))))) $;

--| An unordered pair is an element of all unordered pairs. At least one of the two elements of the unordered pair must be a set. Otherwise, the unordered pair would be the empty set, see ~ prprc , which is not an element of all unordered pairs, see ~ spr0nelg . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem elsprel {p a b: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (V W: class p a b):
  $ wi (wo (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) (cab p (wex a (wex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))))))) $;

--| The empty set is not an element of all unordered pairs. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem spr0nelg {p a b: setvar}:
  $ wnel c0 (cab p (wex a (wex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))))) $;

--| Extend class notation with set of pairs.
term cspr: class;

--| Define the function which maps a set ` v ` to the set of pairs consisting of elements of the set ` v ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
axiom df_spr {v p a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cspr (cmpt v cvv (cab p (wrex a (wrex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (cv v)) (cv v)))) $;

--| The set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprval {p a b: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cspr) (cab p (wrex a (wrex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) V) V))) $;

--| The set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` , expressed by a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprvalpw {p a b: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cspr) (crab p (wrex a (wrex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) V) V) (cpw V))) $;

--| The set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` is a subset of the set of all unordered pairs. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprssspr {p a b: setvar} (V: class):
  $ wss (cfv V cspr) (cab p (wex a (wex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))))) $;

--| The empty set is not an unordered pair over any set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem spr0el (V: class):
  $ wnel c0 (cfv V cspr) $;

--| The set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` , expressed by a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprvalpwn0 {p a b: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cspr) (crab p (wrex a (wrex b (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) V) V) (cdif (cpw V) (csn c0)))) $;

--| An element of the set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` is a pair of elements of the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprel {a b: setvar} (V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv V cspr)) (wrex a (wrex b (wceq X (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) V) V) $;

--| An element of a subset of the set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` , is a pair of elements of the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2021.)
theorem prssspr {a b: setvar} (P: class a b) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss P (cfv V cspr)) (wcel X P)) (wrex a (wrex b (wceq X (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) V) V) $;

--| An unordered pair of elements of a fixed set ` V ` belongs to the set of all unordered pairs over the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem prelspr (V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel V W) (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y V))) (wcel (cpr X Y) (cfv V cspr)) $;

--| The elements of a pair from the set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` are elements of the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2021.)
theorem prsprel (U V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cpr X Y) (cfv V cspr)) (wa (wcel X U) (wcel Y W))) (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) $;

--| The elements of a pair from a subset of the set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` are elements of the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2021.)
theorem prsssprel (P U V W X Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss P (cfv V cspr)) (wcel (cpr X Y) P) (wa (wcel X U) (wcel Y W))) (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y V)) $;

--| The set of all unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` , expressed by a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprvalpwle2 {p: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cspr) (crab p (wbr (cfv (cv p) chash) c2 cle) (cdif (cpw V) (csn c0)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sprsymrelf and ~ sprsymrelfv . (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelfvlem {x3 y1 c: setvar} (P V: class):
  $ wi (wss P (cfv V cspr)) (wcel (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) P)) (cpw (cxp V V))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sprsymrelf1 . (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelf1lem {x3 y1 c: setvar} (V: class x3 y1) (a b: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wss (cv a) (cfv V cspr)) (wss (cv b) (cfv V cspr))) (wi (wceq (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv a))) (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv b)))) (wss (cv a) (cv b))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ sprsymrelfo . (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelfolem1 {q a b: setvar} (Q R: class q a b) (V: class a b):
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wral a (wral b (wi (wceq (cv q) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) R)) V) V) (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wcel Q (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ sprsymrelfo . (Contributed by AV, 23-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelfolem2 {x3 y1 q a b c: setvar} (Q: class x3 y1 q a b) (R V: class) (W: class x3 y1 q):
  $ wceq Q (crab q (wral a (wral b (wi (wceq (cv q) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wbr (cv a) (cv b) R)) V) V) (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel V W) (wss R (cxp V V)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) R)) V) V)) (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) R) (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) Q)) $;

--| The value of the function ` F ` which maps a subset of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` to the relation relating two elements of the set ` V ` iff they are in a pair of the subset. (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelfv {x3 y1 r p c: setvar} (P: class x3 y1 r c) (R F: class x3 y1 r p c) (V: class r p) (X: class r):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X P) (wceq (cfv X F) (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) X))) $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a function from the subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` into the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelf {x3 y1 r p c: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r c) (F: class x3 y1 r p c) (V: class p):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wf P R F $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a one-to-one function from the subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` into the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelf1 {x3 y1 r p c: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r c) (F: class x3 y1 r p c) (V: class p):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wf1 P R F $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a function from the subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` onto the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelfo {x3 y1 r p c: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r c) (F: class x3 y1 r p c) (V: class p) (W: class r p):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wfo P R F) $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a bijection between the subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` into the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelf1o {x3 y1 r p c: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r c) (F: class x3 y1 r p c) (V: class p) (W: class r p):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (copab x3 y1 (wrex c (wceq (cv c) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (cv p)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wf1o P R F) $;

--| There is a bijection between the subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` and the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprbisymrel {x3 y1 f2 r: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r) (V: class f2) (W: class f2 r):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wex f2 (wf1o P R (cv f2))) $;

--| The class ` P ` of subsets of the set of pairs over a fixed set ` V ` and the class ` R ` of symmetric relations on the fixed set ` V ` are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 27-Nov-2021.)
theorem sprsymrelen {x3 y1 r: setvar} (P R: class x3 y1 r) (V: class) (W: class r):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wbr P R cen) $;

--| Characterization of a proper pair: A class is a proper pair iff it consists of exactly two different sets. (Contributed by AV, 11-Mar-2023.)
theorem prpair {x3 a b: setvar} (P: class x3 a b) (V X: class):
  $ wceq P (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X P) (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wceq X (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wne (cv a) (cv b))) V) V) $;

--| Lemma 0 for ~ prproropf1o . Remark: ` O ` , the set of ordered ordered pairs, i.e., ordered pairs in which the first component is less than the second component, can alternatively be written as ` O = { x e. ( V X. V ) | ( 1st `` x ) R ( 2nd `` x ) } ` or even as ` O = { x e. ( V X. V ) | <. ( 1st `` x ) , ( 2nd `` x ) >. e. R } ` , by which the relationship between ordered and unordered pair is immediately visible. (Contributed by AV, 18-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1olem0 (R O V W: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel W O) (w3a (wceq W (cop (cfv W c1st) (cfv W c2nd))) (wa (wcel (cfv W c1st) V) (wcel (cfv W c2nd) V)) (wbr (cfv W c1st) (cfv W c2nd) R)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ prproropf1o . (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1olem1 {p: setvar} (P R O: class p) (V W: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wor V R) (wcel W O)) (wcel (cpr (cfv W c1st) (cfv W c2nd)) P) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ prproropf1o . (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1olem2 {p: setvar} (P R O: class p) (V X: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wor V R) (wcel X P)) (wcel (cop (cinf X V R) (csup X V R)) O) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ prproropf1o . (Contributed by AV, 13-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1olem3 {p: setvar} (P R: class) (F: class p) (O V W: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (cop (cinf (cv p) V R) (csup (cv p) V R))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wor V R) (wcel W O)) (wceq (cfv (cpr (cfv W c1st) (cfv W c2nd)) F) (cop (cfv W c1st) (cfv W c2nd))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ prproropf1o . (Contributed by AV, 14-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1olem4 {p: setvar} (P R: class) (F: class p) (O V W Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (cop (cinf (cv p) V R) (csup (cv p) V R))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wor V R) (wcel W P) (wcel Z P)) (wi (wceq (cfv Z F) (cfv W F)) (wceq Z W)) $;

--| There is a bijection between the set of proper pairs and the set of ordered ordered pairs, i.e., ordered pairs in which the first component is less than the second component. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropf1o {p: setvar} (P R: class) (F: class p) (O V: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt p P (cop (cinf (cv p) V R) (csup (cv p) V R))) $ >
  $ wi (wor V R) (wf1o P O F) $;

--| The set of proper pairs and the set of ordered ordered pairs, i.e., ordered pairs in which the first component is less than the second component, are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 15-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropen {p: setvar} (P R O V W: class):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel V W) (wor V R)) (wbr O P cen) $;

--| There is exactly one ordered ordered pair fulfilling a wff iff there is exactly one proper pair fulfilling an equivalent wff. (Contributed by AV, 20-Mar-2023.)
theorem prproropreud {x3 y1 z p: setvar} (ph: wff z) (ps: wff x3 p) (ch th: wff y1 z p) (P R O V: class z):
  $ wceq O (cin R (cxp V V)) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wor V R) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cop (cinf (cv y1) V R) (csup (cv y1) V R))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu x3 ps O) (wreu y1 ch P)) $;

--| Two equivalent representations of the existence of a unique proper pair. (Contributed by AV, 1-Mar-2023.)
theorem pairreueq {x3 p: setvar} (ph: wff x3 p) (P: class x3 p) (V: class p):
  $ wceq P (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wb (wreu p ph P) (wreu p (wa (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) ph) (cpw V)) $;

--| Two sets are not equal iff there is exactly one proper pair whose elements are either one of these sets. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2023.)
theorem paireqne {x3 p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P: class) (V: class p):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wceq P (crab x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) chash) c2) (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu p (wral x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv x3) B2)) (cv p)) P) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Extend class notation with set of proper unordered pairs.
term cprpr: class;

--| Define the function which maps a set ` v ` to the set of proper unordered pairs consisting of exactly two (different) elements of the set ` v ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
axiom df_prpr {v p a b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprpr (cmpt v cvv (cab p (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wne (cv a) (cv b)) (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))) (cv v)) (cv v)))) $;

--| The set of all proper unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprval {p a b: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cprpr) (cab p (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wne (cv a) (cv b)) (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))) V) V))) $;

--| The set of all proper unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` , expressed by a restricted class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprvalpw {p a b: setvar} (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wceq (cfv V cprpr) (crab p (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wne (cv a) (cv b)) (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))) V) V) (cpw V))) $;

--| An element of the set of all proper unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` is a subset of ` V ` of size two. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprelb (P V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wb (wcel P (cfv V cprpr)) (wa (wcel P (cpw V)) (wceq (cfv P chash) c2))) $;

--| A set is an element of the set of all proper unordered pairs over a given set ` X ` iff it is a pair of different elements of the set ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2023.)
theorem prprelprb {a b: setvar} (P X: class):
  $ wb (wcel P (cfv X cprpr)) (wa (wcel X cvv) (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wceq P (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wne (cv a) (cv b))) X) X)) $;

--| The set of all proper unordered pairs over a given set ` V ` is the set of all unordered pairs over that set of size two. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprspr2 {p: setvar} (V: class):
  $ wceq (cfv V cprpr) (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cfv V cspr)) $;

--| There is a unique proper unordered pair over a given set ` V ` fulfilling a wff iff there is a unique unordered pair over ` V ` of size two fulfilling this wff. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprsprreu {p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wb (wreu p ph (cfv V cprpr)) (wreu p (wa (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) ph) (cfv V cspr))) $;

--| There is a unique proper unordered pair over a given set ` V ` fulfilling a wff iff there is a unique subset of ` V ` of size two fulfilling this wff. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2023.)
theorem prprreueq {p: setvar} (ph: wff p) (V W: class):
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wb (wreu p ph (cfv V cprpr)) (wreu p (wa (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) ph) (cpw V))) $;

--| The proper substitution of an unordered pair for a setvar variable corresponds to a proper substitution of each of its elements. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2023.)
theorem sbcpr {x3 y1 p a b: setvar} (ph: wff p a b) (ps: wff x3 y1 a b):
  $ wi (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wb (wsbc p ph (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wsbc y1 (wsbc x3 ps (cv a)) (cv b)) $;

--| There is a unique unordered pair fulfilling a wff iff there are uniquely two sets fulfilling a corresponding wff. (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2023.)
theorem reupr {x3 y1 p a b: setvar} (ps: wff p) (ch th: wff x3 y1 a b) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wreu p ps (cfv X cspr)) (wrex a (wrex b (wa ch (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi th (wceq (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))) X) X)) X) X)) $;

--| There is a unique proper unordered pair fulfilling a wff iff there are uniquely two different sets fulfilling a corresponding wff. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2023.)
theorem reuprpr {x3 y1 p a b: setvar} (ps: wff p) (ch th: wff x3 y1 a b) (V X: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wreu p ps (cfv X cprpr)) (wrex a (wrex b (w3a (wne (cv a) (cv b)) ch (wral x3 (wral y1 (wi (wa (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) th) (wceq (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cpr (cv a) (cv b)))) X) X)) X) X)) $;

--| Equality for unordered pairs with partially ordered elements. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jul-2023.)
theorem poprelb (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wrel R) (wpo X R)) (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 R) (wbr C D R))) (wb (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr C D)) (wa (wceq A2 C) (wceq B2 D))) $;

--| The existence of an ordered pair fulfilling a wff implies the existence of an unordered pair fulfilling the wff. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2023.)
theorem _2exopprim {a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (A2 B2: class a b):
  $ wi (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cop A2 B2) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cpr A2 B2) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) $;

--| There is a unique unordered pair with ordered elements fulfilling a wff if there is a unique ordered pair fulfilling the wff. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2023.)
theorem reuopreuprim {p a b: setvar} (ph: wff a b) (V: class a b) (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wi (wreu p (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv p) (cop (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (cxp X X)) (wreu p (wex a (wex b (wa (wceq (cv p) (cpr (cv a) (cv b))) ph))) (cfv X cspr))) $;

--| Special factorization of a non-prime integer greater than 3. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmmul1 {a b: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wb (wnel N cprime) (wrex a (wrex b (wceq N (co (cv a) (cv b) cmul)) (co c2 N cfzo)) (co c2 N cfzo))) $;

--| Special factorization of a non-prime integer greater than 3. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmmul2 {a b: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wb (wnel N cprime) (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) cle) (wceq N (co (cv a) (cv b) cmul))) (co c2 N cfzo)) (co c2 N cfzo))) $;

--| Special factorization of a non-prime integer greater than 3. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmmul3 {a b: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wb (wnel N cprime) (wo (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wbr (cv a) (cv b) clt) (wceq N (co (cv a) (cv b) cmul))) (co c2 N cfzo)) (co c2 N cfzo)) (wrex a (wceq N (co (cv a) c2 cexp)) (co c2 N cfzo)))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Fermat numbers.
term cfmtno: class;

--| Define the function that enumerates the _Fermat numbers_, see definition in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
axiom df_fmtno {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfmtno (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (co c2 (cv n) cexp) cexp) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The ` N ` th Fermat number. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N cfmtno) (co (co c2 (co c2 N cexp) cexp) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Each Fermat number is greater than or equal to 3. (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnoge3 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N cfmtno) (cfv c3 cuz)) $;

--| Each Fermat number is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by AV, 4-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnonn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N cfmtno) cn) $;

--| A Fermat number minus one is a power of a power of two. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnom1nn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co (cfv N cfmtno) c1 cmin) (co c2 (co c2 N cexp) cexp)) $;

--| Each Fermat number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnoodd (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wn (wbr c2 (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) $;

--| A Fermat number is a function value of the enumeration of the Fermat numbers. (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnorn {n: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (crn cfmtno)) (wrex n (wceq (cfv (cv n) cfmtno) F) cn0) $;

--| The enumeration of the Fermat numbers is a one-one function into the positive integers. (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnof1:
  $ wf1 cn0 cn cfmtno $;

--| The set of Fermat numbers is infinite. (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnoinf:
  $ wnel (crn cfmtno) cfn $;

--| The first recurrence relation for Fermat numbers, see Wikipedia "Fermat number", ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties , 22-Jul-2021. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnorec1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cfmtno) (co (co (co (cfv N cfmtno) c1 cmin) c2 cexp) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The floor of the square root of 2 to the power of 2 to the power of a positive integer plus a bounded nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem sqrtpwpw2p (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel M cn0) (wbr M (co (co c2 (co (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) c1 caddc) cexp) c1 caddc) clt)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co (co c2 (co c2 N cexp) cexp) M caddc) csqrt) cfl) (co c2 (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cexp)) $;

--| The floor of the square root of a Fermat number. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnosqrt (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv N cfmtno) csqrt) cfl) (co c2 (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cexp)) $;

--| The ` 0 ` th Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cfmtno) c3 $;

--| The ` 1 ` st Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cfmtno) c5 $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmtnorec2 (induction step). (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnorec2lem {n: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cn0) (wi (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cfmtno) (co (cprod n (co cc0 (cv y1) cfz) (cfv (cv n) cfmtno)) c2 caddc)) (wceq (cfv (co (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) c1 caddc) cfmtno) (co (cprod n (co cc0 (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cfz) (cfv (cv n) cfmtno)) c2 caddc))) $;

--| The second recurrence relation for Fermat numbers, see ProofWiki "Product of Sequence of Fermat Numbers plus 2", 29-Jul-2021, ~ https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Product_of_Sequence_of_Fermat_Numbers_plus_2 or Wikipedia "Fermat number", 29-Jul-2021, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties . (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnorec2 {n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cfmtno) (co (cprod n (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (cv n) cfmtno)) c2 caddc)) $;

--| Any Fermat number divides a greater Fermat number minus 2. Corollary of ~ fmtnorec2 , see ProofWiki "Product of Sequence of Fermat Numbers plus 2/Corollary", 31-Jul-2021. (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnodvds (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel M cn)) (wbr (cfv N cfmtno) (co (cfv (co N M caddc) cfmtno) c2 cmin) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ goldbachth . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem goldbachthlem1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel M cn0) (wbr M N clt)) (wbr (cfv M cfmtno) (co (cfv N cfmtno) c2 cmin) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ goldbachth . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem goldbachthlem2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel M cn0) (wbr M N clt)) (wceq (co (cfv N cfmtno) (cfv M cfmtno) cgcd) c1) $;

--| Goldbach's theorem: Two different Fermat numbers are coprime. See ProofWiki "Goldbach's theorem", 31-Jul-2021, ~ https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Goldbach%27s_Theorem or Wikipedia "Fermat number", 31-Jul-2021, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem goldbachth (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wcel M cn0) (wne N M)) (wceq (co (cfv N cfmtno) (cfv M cfmtno) cgcd) c1) $;

--| The third recurrence relation for Fermat numbers, see Wikipedia "Fermat number", 31-Jul-2021, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties . (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2021.)
theorem fmtnorec3 {n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv N cfmtno) (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfmtno) (co (co c2 (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cexp) (cprod n (co cc0 (co N c2 cmin) cfz) (cfv (cv n) cfmtno)) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The fourth recurrence relation for Fermat numbers, see Wikipedia "Fermat number", 31-Jul-2021, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties . (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnorec4 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv N cfmtno) (co (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfmtno) c2 cexp) (co c2 (co (co (cfv (co N c2 cmin) cfmtno) c1 cmin) c2 cexp) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| The ` 2 ` nd Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cfmtno) (cdc c1 c7) $;

--| The ` 3 ` rd Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno3:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 cfmtno) (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c7) $;

--| The ` 4 ` th Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno4:
  $ wceq (cfv c4 cfmtno) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c7) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ fmtno5 . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5lem1:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) c6 cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c3 c9) c3) c2) c1) c6) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ fmtno5 . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5lem2:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) c5 cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c3 c2) c7) c6) c8) cc0) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ fmtno5 . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5lem3:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) c3 cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c1 c9) c6) c6) cc0) c8) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ fmtno5 . (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5lem4:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) c2 cexp) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 c2) c9) c4) c9) c6) c7) c2) c9) c6) $;

--| The ` 5 ` th Fermat number. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5:
  $ wceq (cfv c5 cfmtno) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 c2) c9) c4) c9) c6) c7) c2) c9) c7) $;

--| The ` 0 ` th Fermat number is a prime (_first Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno0prm:
  $ wcel (cfv cc0 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| The ` 1 ` st Fermat number is a prime (_second Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno1prm:
  $ wcel (cfv c1 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| The ` 2 ` nd Fermat number is a prime (_third Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 13-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno2prm:
  $ wcel (cfv c2 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| 257 is a prime number (the _fourth Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2021.)
theorem _257prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c7) cprime $;

--| The ` 3 ` rd Fermat number is a prime (_fourth Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2021.)
theorem fmtno3prm:
  $ wcel (cfv c3 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| Any power of two is coprime to any prime not being two. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2021.)
theorem odz2prm2pw (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2)))) (wa (wne (co (co c2 (co c2 N cexp) cexp) P cmo) c1) (wceq (co (co c2 (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) cexp) P cmo) c1))) (wceq (cfv c2 (cfv P codz)) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmtnoprmfac1 : The order of 2 modulo a prime that divides the n-th Fermat number is 2^(n+1). (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2021.) (Proof shortened by AV, 18-Mar-2022.)
theorem fmtnoprmfac1lem (P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr P (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wceq (cfv c2 (cfv P codz)) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp)) $;

--| Divisor of Fermat number (special form of Euler's result, see ~ fmtnofac1 ): Let F_n be a Fermat number. Let p be a prime divisor of F_n. Then p is in the form: k*2^(n+1)+1 where k is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnoprmfac1 {k: setvar} (P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel P cprime) (wbr P (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq P (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmtnoprmfac2 . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnoprmfac2lem1 (P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr P (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wceq (co (co c2 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo) c1) $;

--| Divisor of Fermat number (special form of Lucas' result, see ~ fmtnofac2 ): Let F_n be a Fermat number. Let p be a prime divisor of F_n. Then p is in the form: k*2^(n+2)+1 where k is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnoprmfac2 {k: setvar} (P N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel P cprime) (wbr P (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq P (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c2 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fmtnofac2 (Induction step). (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnofac2lem {k: setvar} (y1 z: setvar) (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (cv z) (cfv c2 cuz))) (wi (wa (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr (cv y1) (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq (cv y1) (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c2 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0)) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr (cv z) (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c2 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0))) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr (co (cv y1) (cv z) cmul) (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv z) cmul) (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c2 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0))) $;

--| Divisor of Fermat number (Euler's Result refined by Fran&ccedil;ois &Eacute;douard Anatole Lucas), see ~ fmtnofac1 : Let F_n be a Fermat number. Let m be divisor of F_n. Then m is in the form: k*2^(n+2)+1 where k is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnofac2 {k: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel M cn) (wbr M (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq M (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c2 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0) $;

--| Divisor of Fermat number (Euler's Result), see ProofWiki "Divisor of Fermat Number/Euler's Result", 24-Jul-2021, ~ https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Divisor_of_Fermat_Number/Euler's_Result ): "Let F_n be a Fermat number. Let m be divisor of F_n. Then m is in the form: k*2^(n+1)+1 where k is a positive integer." Here, however, k must be a nonnegative integer, because k must be 0 to represent 1 (which is a divisor of F_n ). Historical Note: In 1747, Leonhard Paul Euler proved that a divisor of a Fermat number F_n is always in the form kx2^(n+1)+1. This was later refined to k*2^(n+2)+1 by Fran&ccedil;ois &Eacute;douard Anatole Lucas, see ~ fmtnofac2 . (Contributed by AV, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnofac1 {k: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel M cn) (wbr M (cfv N cfmtno) cdvds)) (wrex k (wceq M (co (co (cv k) (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0) $;

--| The floor of the square root of the fourth Fermat number is 256. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno4sqrt:
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv (cfv c4 cfmtno) csqrt) cfl) (cdc (cdc c2 c5) c6) $;

--| If P was a (prime) factor of the fourth Fermat number less than the square root of the fourth Fermat number, it would be either 65 or 129 or 193. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno4prmfac (P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wbr P (cfv c4 cfmtno) cdvds) (wbr P (cfv (cfv (cfv c4 cfmtno) csqrt) cfl) cle)) (w3o (wceq P (cdc c6 c5)) (wceq P (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c9)) (wceq P (cdc (cdc c1 c9) c3))) $;

--| If P was a (prime) factor of the fourth Fermat number, it would be 193. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno4prmfac193 (P: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wbr P (cfv c4 cfmtno) cdvds) (wbr P (cfv (cfv (cfv c4 cfmtno) csqrt) cfl) cle)) (wceq P (cdc (cdc c1 c9) c3)) $;

--| 193 is not a (prime) factor of the fourth Fermat number. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno4nprmfac193:
  $ wn (wbr (cdc (cdc c1 c9) c3) (cfv c4 cfmtno) cdvds) $;

--| The ` 4 `-th Fermat number (65537) is a prime (the _fifth Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno4prm:
  $ wcel (cfv c4 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| 65537 is a prime number (the _fifth Fermat prime_). (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem _65537prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c7) cprime $;

--| The first five Fermat numbers are prime, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnofz04prm (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (co cc0 c4 cfz)) (wcel (cfv N cfmtno) cprime) $;

--| The first five Fermat numbers are prime. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtnole4prm (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wbr N c4 cle)) (wcel (cfv N cfmtno) cprime) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ fmtno5fac . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5faclem1:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c7) cc0) cc0) c4) c1) c7) c4 cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c6) c8) cc0) c1) c6) c6) c8) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ fmtno5fac . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5faclem2:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c7) cc0) cc0) c4) c1) c7) c6 cmul) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) c2) cc0) c2) c5) cc0) c2) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ fmtno5fac . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5faclem3:
  $ wceq (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 cc0) c2) cc0) c2) c5) cc0) c2) cc0) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c2 c6) c8) cc0) c1) c6) c6) c8) caddc) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c4 c2) c8) c8) c2) c6) c6) c8) c8) $;

--| The factorization of the ` 5 ` th Fermat number, see remark in [ApostolNT] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5fac:
  $ wceq (cfv c5 cfmtno) (co (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c7) cc0) cc0) c4) c1) c7) (cdc (cdc c6 c4) c1) cmul) $;

--| The ` 5 ` th Fermat number is a not a prime. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem fmtno5nprm:
  $ wnel (cfv c5 cfmtno) cprime $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ prmdvdsfmtnof1 . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2021.)
theorem prmdvdsfmtnof1lem1 {p: setvar} (F G: class) (I J: class p):
  $ wceq I (cinf (crab p (wbr (cv p) F cdvds) cprime) cr clt) $ >
  $ wceq J (cinf (crab p (wbr (cv p) G cdvds) cprime) cr clt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel G (cfv c2 cuz))) (wi (wceq I J) (w3a (wcel I cprime) (wbr I F cdvds) (wbr I G cdvds))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ prmdvdsfmtnof1 . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2021.)
theorem prmdvdsfmtnof1lem2 (F G I: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (crn cfmtno)) (wcel G (crn cfmtno))) (wi (w3a (wcel I cprime) (wbr I F cdvds) (wbr I G cdvds)) (wceq F G)) $;

--| The mapping of a Fermat number to its smallest prime factor is a function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2021.) (Proof shortened by II, 16-Feb-2023.)
theorem prmdvdsfmtnof {f2 p: setvar} (F: class f2 p):
  $ wceq F (cmpt f2 (crn cfmtno) (cinf (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv f2) cdvds) cprime) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wf (crn cfmtno) cprime F $;

--| The mapping of a Fermat number to its smallest prime factor is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2021.)
theorem prmdvdsfmtnof1 {f2 p: setvar} (F: class f2 p):
  $ wceq F (cmpt f2 (crn cfmtno) (cinf (crab p (wbr (cv p) (cv f2) cdvds) cprime) cr clt)) $ >
  $ wf1 (crn cfmtno) cprime F $;

--| The set of prime numbers is infinite. The proof of this variant of ~ prminf is based on Goldbach's theorem ~ goldbachth (via ~ prmdvdsfmtnof1 and ~ prmdvdsfmtnof1lem2 ), see Wikipedia "Fermat number", 4-Aug-2021, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number#Basic_properties . (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2021.)
theorem prminf2:
  $ wnel cprime cfn $;

--| For ` ( ( 2 ^ k ) + 1 ) ` to be prime, ` k ` must be a power of 2, see Wikipedia "Fermat number", section "Other theorems about Fermat numbers", ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number , 5-Aug-2021. (Contributed by AV, 7-Aug-2021.)
theorem _2pwp1prm {n: setvar} (K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn) (wcel (co (co c2 K cexp) c1 caddc) cprime)) (wrex n (wceq K (co c2 (cv n) cexp)) cn0) $;

--| Every prime number of the form ` ( ( 2 ^ k ) + 1 ) ` must be a Fermat number. (Contributed by AV, 7-Aug-2021.)
theorem _2pwp1prmfmtno {n: setvar} (P K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn) (wceq P (co (co c2 K cexp) c1 caddc)) (wcel P cprime)) (wrex n (wceq P (cfv (cv n) cfmtno)) cn0) $;

--| The second Mersenne number M_2 = 3 is a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem m2prm:
  $ wcel (co (co c2 c2 cexp) c1 cmin) cprime $;

--| The third Mersenne number M_3 = 7 is a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem m3prm:
  $ wcel (co (co c2 c3 cexp) c1 cmin) cprime $;

--| A condition equivalent to the floor of a square root. (Contributed by AV, 17-Aug-2021.)
theorem flsqrt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wb (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 csqrt) cfl) B2) (wa (wbr (co B2 c2 cexp) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (co B2 c1 caddc) c2 cexp) clt))) $;

--| The floor of the square root of a nonnegative number is 5 iff the number is between 25 and 35. (Contributed by AV, 17-Aug-2021.)
theorem flsqrt5 (X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X cr) (wbr cc0 X cle)) (wb (wa (wbr (cdc c2 c5) X cle) (wbr X (cdc c3 c6) clt)) (wceq (cfv (cfv X csqrt) cfl) c5)) $;

--| 3 does not divide 4. (Contributed by AV, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem _3ndvds4:
  $ wn (wbr c3 c4 cdvds) $;

--| 139 is a prime number. In contrast to ~ 139prm , the proof of this theorem uses ~ 3dvds2dec for checking the divisibility by 3. Although the proof using ~ 3dvds2dec is longer (regarding size: 1849 characters compared with 1809 for ~ 139prm ), the number of essential steps is smaller (301 compared with 327 for ~ 139prm ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Feb-2014.) (Revised by AV, 18-Aug-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _139prmALT:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c1 c3) c9) cprime $;

--| 31 is a prime number. In contrast to ~ 37prm , the proof of this theorem is not based on the "blanket" ~ prmlem2 , but on ~ isprm7 . Although the checks for non-divisibility by the primes 7 to 23 are not needed, the proof is much longer (regarding size) than the proof of ~ 37prm (1810 characters compared with 1213 for ~ 37prm ). The number of essential steps, however, is much smaller (138 compared with 213 for ~ 37prm ). (Contributed by AV, 17-Aug-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _31prm:
  $ wcel (cdc c3 c1) cprime $;

--| The fifth Mersenne number M_5 = 31 is a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 17-Aug-2021.)
theorem m5prm:
  $ wcel (co (co c2 c5 cexp) c1 cmin) cprime $;

--| 127 is a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.) (Proof shortened by AV, 16-Sep-2021.)
theorem _127prm:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c1 c2) c7) cprime $;

--| The seventh Mersenne number M_7 = 127 is a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem m7prm:
  $ wcel (co (co c2 c7 cexp) c1 cmin) cprime $;

--| The eleventh Mersenne number M_11 = 2047 is not a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem m11nprm:
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc c1 c1) cexp) c1 cmin) (co (cdc c8 c9) (cdc c2 c3) cmul) $;

--| If a number is ` 3 ` modulo ` 4 ` , twice the number plus ` 1 ` is ` 7 ` modulo ` 8 ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-Aug-2021.)
theorem mod42tp1mod8 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wceq (co N c4 cmo) c3)) (wceq (co (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) c8 cmo) c7) $;

--| If ` Q ` is a safe prime (i.e. ` Q = ( ( 2 x. P ) + 1 ) ` for a prime ` P ` ) with ` Q == 7 ` (mod ` 8 ` ), then ` Q ` divides the ` P `-th Mersenne number M_P. (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2021.)
theorem sfprmdvdsmersenne (P Q: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (w3a (wcel Q cprime) (wceq (co Q c8 cmo) c7) (wceq Q (co (co c2 P cmul) c1 caddc)))) (wbr Q (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cdvds) $;

--| If ` P ` is a Sophie Germain prime (i.e. ` Q = ( ( 2 x. P ) + 1 ) ` is also prime) with ` P == 3 ` (mod ` 4 ` ), then ` Q ` divides the ` P `-th Mersenne number M_P. (Contributed by AV, 20-Aug-2021.)
theorem sgprmdvdsmersenne (P Q: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel P cprime) (wceq (co P c4 cmo) c3)) (wa (wceq Q (co (co c2 P cmul) c1 caddc)) (wcel Q cprime))) (wbr Q (co (co c2 P cexp) c1 cmin) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ lighneal . (Contributed by AV, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem1 (P M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wceq P c2) (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn)) (wne (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin) (co P M cexp)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ lighneal . (Contributed by AV, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem2 (P M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn)) (wbr c2 N cdvds) (wceq (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin) (co P M cexp))) (wceq M c1) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ lighneal . (Contributed by AV, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem3 (P M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn)) (wa (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) (wbr c2 M cdvds)) (wceq (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin) (co P M cexp))) (wceq M c1) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ lighneallem4 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem4a (A2 S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel M (cfv c3 cuz)) (wceq S (co (co (co A2 M cexp) c1 caddc) (co A2 c1 caddc) cdiv))) (wbr c2 S cle) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ lighneallem4 . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem4b {k: setvar} (A2 M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel M (cfv c2 cuz)) (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds))) (wcel (csu k (co cc0 (co M c1 cmin) cfz) (co (co (cneg c1) (cv k) cexp) (co A2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)) (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ lighneal . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneallem4 (P M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn)) (wa (wn (wbr c2 N cdvds)) (wn (wbr c2 M cdvds))) (wceq (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin) (co P M cexp))) (wceq M c1) $;

--| If a power of a prime ` P ` (i.e. ` P ^ M ` ) is of the form ` 2 ^ N - 1 ` , then ` N ` must be prime and ` M ` must be ` 1 ` . Generalization of ~ mersenne (where ` M = 1 ` is a prerequisite). Theorem of S. Ligh and L. Neal (1974) "A note on Mersenne mumbers", Mathematics Magazine, 47:4, 231-233. (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem lighneal (P M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wcel M cn) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co (co c2 N cexp) c1 cmin) (co P M cexp))) (wa (wceq M c1) (wcel N cprime)) $;

--| The square of an integer which is -1 modulo a number greater than 1 is 1 modulo the same modulus. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2020.)
theorem modexp2m1d (ph: wff) (A2 E: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 E cmo) (co (cneg c1) E cmo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) E cmo) c1) $;

--| Lemma for ~ proththd . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2020.)
theorem proththdlem (ph: wff) (P K N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co K (co c2 N cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel P cn) (wbr c1 P clt) (wcel (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn)) $;

--| Proth's theorem (1878). If P is a _Proth number_, i.e. a number of the form k2^n+1 with k less than 2^n, and if there exists an integer x for which x^((P-1)/2) is -1 modulo P, then P is prime. Such a prime is called a _Proth prime_. Like Pocklington's theorem (see ~ pockthg ), Proth's theorem allows for a convenient method for verifying large primes. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2020.)
theorem proththd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (K: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq P (co (co K (co c2 N cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr K (co c2 N cexp) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo) (co (cneg c1) P cmo)) cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P cprime) $;

--| 5 times the cube of 2 is 40. (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2020.)
theorem _5tcu2e40:
  $ wceq (co c5 (co c2 c3 cexp) cmul) (cdc c4 cc0) $;

--| 3 to the fourth power is -1 modulo 41. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2020.)
theorem _3exp4mod41:
  $ wceq (co (co c3 c4 cexp) (cdc c4 c1) cmo) (co (cneg c1) (cdc c4 c1) cmo) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ 41prothprm . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jul-2020.)
theorem _41prothprmlem1 (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cdc c4 c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cdc c2 cc0) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ 41prothprm . (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2020.)
theorem _41prothprmlem2 (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cdc c4 c1) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c3 (co (co P c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cexp) P cmo) (co (cneg c1) P cmo) $;

--| 41 is a _Proth prime_. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jul-2020.)
theorem _41prothprm (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cdc c4 c1) $ >
  $ wa (wceq P (co (co c5 (co c2 c3 cexp) cmul) c1 caddc)) (wcel P cprime) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ nprmdvdsfacm1 . (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmdvdsfacm1lem1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wceq N (co A2 c2 cexp))) (wbr N (co A2 (co c2 A2 cmul) cmul) cdvds) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ nprmdvdsfacm1 . (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmdvdsfacm1lem2 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wceq N (co A2 c2 cexp))) (wbr c3 A2 cle) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ nprmdvdsfacm1 . (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmdvdsfacm1lem3 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wceq N (co A2 c2 cexp))) (wbr (co c2 A2 cmul) (co N c1 cmin) clt) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ nprmdvdsfacm1 . (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmdvdsfacm1lem4 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wcel A2 (co c2 N cfzo)) (wceq N (co A2 c2 cexp))) (wbr N (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) cdvds) $;

--| A non-prime integer greater than 5 divides the factorial of the integer decreased by 1 (see remark in [Ribenboim] p. 181). Note: not valid for ` N = 4 ` , but for ` N = 1 ` ! (Contributed by AV, 7-Apr-2026.)
theorem nprmdvdsfacm1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wnel N cprime)) (wbr N (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) cdvds) $;

--| Value of a term of the prime-counting function pi for positive integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;, for a prime number. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppivalnnprm (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wceq (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co P c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) P cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co P c1 cmin) cfa) P cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl) c1) $;

--| Value of a term of the prime-counting function pi for positive integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;, for a non-prime number greater than 4. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppivalnnnprmge6 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wnel N cprime)) (wceq (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) N cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) N cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl) cc0) $;

--| Value of the term of the prime-counting function pi for positive integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;, for 4. (Contributed by AV, 8-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppivalnn4:
  $ wceq (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co c4 c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) c4 cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co c4 c1 cmin) cfa) c4 cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl) cc0 $;

--| Value of a term of the prime-counting function pi for positive integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron, for a non-prime number greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 8-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppivalnnnprm (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wnel N cprime)) (wceq (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) N cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) cfa) N cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl) cc0) $;

--| An indicator function for prime numbers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2026.)
theorem indprm {k: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv cprime (cfv (cfv c2 cuz) cind)) (cmpt k (cfv c2 cuz) (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) (cv k) cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) (cv k) cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl)) $;

--| An indicator function for prime numbers in a finite interval of integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2026.)
theorem indprmfz {k: setvar} (A2: class k) (I: class):
  $ wceq I (co c2 A2 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cin I cprime) (cfv I cind)) (cmpt k I (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) (cv k) cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) (cv k) cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl)) $;

--| Value of the prime-counting function pi for 1, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppi1sum {k: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cppi) (csu k c0 (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) (cv k) cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) (cv k) cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl)) $;

--| Value of the prime-counting function pi for positive integers, according to J&aacute;n Min&aacute;&ccaron;, see statement in [Ribenboim], p. 181. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2026.)
theorem ppivalnn {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cppi) (csu k (co c2 N cfz) (cfv (co (co (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) c1 caddc) (cv k) cdiv) (cfv (co (cfv (co (cv k) c1 cmin) cfa) (cv k) cdiv) cfl) cmin) cfl))) $;

--| A condition for a quadratic equation with complex coefficients to have (exactly) one complex solution. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem quad1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu x3 (wceq (co (co A2 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) cc) (wceq D cc0)) $;

--| A condition for a quadratic equation with real coefficients to have (at least) one real solution. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jan-2023.)
theorem requad01 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex x3 (wceq (co (co A2 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) cr) (wbr cc0 D cle)) $;

--| A condition for a quadratic equation with real coefficients to have (exactly) one real solution. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jan-2023.)
theorem requad1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu x3 (wceq (co (co A2 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) cr) (wceq D cc0)) $;

--| A condition for a quadratic equation with real coefficients to have (exactly) two different real solutions. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2023.)
theorem requad2 {x3 p: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq D (co (co B2 c2 cexp) (co c4 (co A2 C cmul) cmul) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wreu p (wa (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (wral x3 (wceq (co (co A2 (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co B2 (cv x3) cmul) C caddc) caddc) cc0) (cv p))) (cpw cr)) (wbr cc0 D clt)) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the set of even numbers.
term ceven: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the set of odd numbers.
term codd: class;

--| Define the set of even numbers. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
axiom df_even {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceven (crab z (wcel (co (cv z) c2 cdiv) cz) cz) $;

--| Define the set of odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
axiom df_odd {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wcel (co (co (cv z) c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cz) cz) $;

--| The predicate "is an even number". An even number is an integer which is divisible by 2, i.e. the result of dividing the even integer by 2 is still an integer. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseven (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z ceven) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wcel (co Z c2 cdiv) cz)) $;

--| The predicate "is an odd number". An odd number is an integer which is not divisible by 2, i.e. the result of dividing the odd integer increased by 1 and then divided by 2 is still an integer. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem isodd (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z codd) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wcel (co (co Z c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cz)) $;

--| An even number is an integer. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem evenz (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wcel Z cz) $;

--| An odd number is an integer. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddz (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wcel Z cz) $;

--| The result of dividing an even number by 2 is an integer. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem evendiv2z (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wcel (co Z c2 cdiv) cz) $;

--| The result of dividing an odd number increased by 1 and then divided by 2 is an integer. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddp1div2z (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wcel (co (co Z c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cz) $;

--| The result of dividing an odd number decreased by 1 and then divided by 2 is an integer. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddm1div2z (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wcel (co (co Z c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cz) $;

--| The predicate "is an odd number". An odd number is an integer which is not divisible by 2, i.e. the result of dividing the odd number decreased by 1 and then divided by 2 is still an integer. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem isodd2 (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z codd) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wcel (co (co Z c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cz)) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfodd2 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wcel (co (co (cv z) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cz) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfodd6 {z i: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wrex i (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv i) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for even numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfeven4 {z i: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceven (crab z (wrex i (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv i) cmul)) cz) cz) $;

--| The predecessor of an even number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem evenm1odd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wcel (co Z c1 cmin) codd) $;

--| The successor of an even number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem evenp1odd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wcel (co Z c1 caddc) codd) $;

--| The successor of an odd number is even. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddp1eveni (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wcel (co Z c1 caddc) ceven) $;

--| The predecessor of an odd number is even. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jul-2020.)
theorem oddm1eveni (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wcel (co Z c1 cmin) ceven) $;

--| An even number is not an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem evennodd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wn (wcel Z codd)) $;

--| An odd number is not an even number. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddneven (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wn (wcel Z ceven)) $;

--| The negative of an even number is even. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem enege (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel (cneg A2) ceven) $;

--| The negative of an odd number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem onego (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 codd) (wcel (cneg A2) codd) $;

--| Exponentiation of -1 by an even power. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.) (Revised by AV, 6-Jul-2020.)
theorem m1expevenALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N ceven) (wceq (co (cneg c1) N cexp) c1) $;

--| Exponentiation of -1 by an odd power. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jul-2020.)
theorem m1expoddALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N codd) (wceq (co (cneg c1) N cexp) (cneg c1)) $;

--| Alternate definition for even numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfeven2 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceven (crab z (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfodd3 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cz) $;

--| The predicate "is an even number". An even number is an integer which is divisible by 2. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseven2 (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z ceven) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wbr c2 Z cdvds)) $;

--| The predicate "is an odd number". An odd number is an integer which is not divisible by 2. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem isodd3 (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z codd) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wn (wbr c2 Z cdvds))) $;

--| 2 divides an even number. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2dvdseven (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z ceven) (wbr c2 Z cdvds) $;

--| A multiple of 2 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem m2even (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cz) (wcel (co c2 Z cmul) ceven) $;

--| 2 does not divide an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2ndvdsodd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wn (wbr c2 Z cdvds)) $;

--| 2 divides an odd number increased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2dvdsoddp1 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wbr c2 (co Z c1 caddc) cdvds) $;

--| 2 divides an odd number decreased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2dvdsoddm1 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wbr c2 (co Z c1 cmin) cdvds) $;

--| Alternate definition for even numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfeven3 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceven (crab z (wceq (co (cv z) c2 cmo) cc0) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfodd4 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wceq (co (cv z) c2 cmo) c1) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem dfodd5 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wne (co (cv z) c2 cmo) cc0) cz) $;

--| The floor of an even number divided by 2 is equal to the even number divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem zefldiv2ALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N ceven) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (co N c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The floor of an odd number divided by 2 is equal to the odd number first decreased by 1 and then divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem zofldiv2ALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N codd) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| Odd number representation by using the floor function. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 11-Dec-2019.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddflALTV (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K codd) (wceq K (co (co c2 (cfv (co K c2 cdiv) cfl) cmul) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The predicate "is an even number". An even number and 2 have 2 as greatest common divisor. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem iseven5 (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z ceven) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wceq (co c2 Z cgcd) c2)) $;

--| The predicate "is an odd number". An odd number and 2 have 1 as greatest common divisor. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem isodd7 (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z codd) (wa (wcel Z cz) (wceq (co c2 Z cgcd) c1)) $;

--| Alternate definition for even numbers. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem dfeven5 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq ceven (crab z (wceq (co c2 (cv z) cgcd) c2) cz) $;

--| Alternate definition for odd numbers. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem dfodd7 {z: setvar}:
  $ wceq codd (crab z (wceq (co c2 (cv z) cgcd) c1) cz) $;

--| The greatest common divisor of an odd number and 2 is 1, i.e., 2 and any odd number are coprime. Remark: The proof using ~ dfodd7 is longer (see proof in comment)! (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem gcd2odd1 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z codd) (wceq (co Z c2 cgcd) c1) $;

--| No even integer equals an odd integer (i.e. no integer can be both even and odd). Exercise 10(a) of [Apostol] p. 28. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2004.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem zneoALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel B2 codd)) (wne A2 B2) $;

--| An integer is even or odd. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2006.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem zeoALTV (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cz) (wo (wcel Z ceven) (wcel Z codd)) $;

--| An integer is even or odd but not both. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.) (Revised by AV, 16-Jun-2020.)
theorem zeo2ALTV (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cz) (wb (wcel Z ceven) (wn (wcel Z codd))) $;

--| A positive integer is even or odd but not both. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2006.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem nneoALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wb (wcel N ceven) (wn (wcel N codd))) $;

--| A positive integer is even or odd but not both. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem nneoiALTV (N: class):
  $ wcel N cn $ >
  $ wb (wcel N ceven) (wn (wcel N codd)) $;

--| An integer is odd iff it is one plus twice another integer. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem odd2np1ALTV {n: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel N codd) (wrex n (wceq (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c1 caddc) N) cz)) $;

--| An integer is odd iff its predecessor is even. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2016.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddm1evenALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel N codd) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) ceven)) $;

--| An integer is odd iff its successor is even. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2016.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddp1evenALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel N codd) (wcel (co N c1 caddc) ceven)) $;

--| The exponential of the negative of a number, when the exponent is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Apr-2015.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 10-Jul-2022.)
theorem oexpnegALTV (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn) (wcel N codd)) (wceq (co (cneg A2) N cexp) (cneg (co A2 N cexp))) $;

--| The exponential of the negative of a number not being 0, when the exponent is odd. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem oexpnegnz (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel N codd)) (wceq (co (cneg A2) N cexp) (cneg (co A2 N cexp))) $;

--| Value of the zeroth bit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2016.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem bits0ALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel cc0 (cfv N cbits)) (wcel N codd)) $;

--| The zeroth bit of an even number is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2016.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem bits0eALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N ceven) (wn (wcel cc0 (cfv N cbits))) $;

--| The zeroth bit of an odd number is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Sep-2016.) (Revised by AV, 19-Jun-2020.)
theorem bits0oALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N codd) (wcel cc0 (cfv N cbits)) $;

--| Either ` A / ( A gcd B ) ` is odd or ` B / ( A gcd B ) ` is odd. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem divgcdoddALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel B2 cn)) (wo (wcel (co A2 (co A2 B2 cgcd) cdiv) codd) (wcel (co B2 (co A2 B2 cgcd) cdiv) codd)) $;

--| The sum of two odds is even. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem opoeALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 codd) (wcel B2 codd)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) ceven) $;

--| The sum of an odd and an even is odd. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem opeoALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 codd) (wcel B2 ceven)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) codd) $;

--| The difference of two odds is even. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem omoeALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 codd) (wcel B2 codd)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) ceven) $;

--| The difference of an odd and an even is odd. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 7-Apr-2014.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem omeoALTV (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 codd) (wcel B2 ceven)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) codd) $;

--| A prime not equal to ` 2 ` is odd. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Feb-2015.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem oddprmALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wcel N codd) $;

--| 0 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 17-Jun-2020.)
theorem _0evenALTV:
  $ wcel cc0 ceven $;

--| 0 is not an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 17-Jun-2020.)
theorem _0noddALTV:
  $ wnel cc0 codd $;

--| 1 is an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _1oddALTV:
  $ wcel c1 codd $;

--| 1 is not an even number. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _1nevenALTV:
  $ wnel c1 ceven $;

--| 2 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2evenALTV:
  $ wcel c2 ceven $;

--| 2 is not an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2020.)
theorem _2noddALTV:
  $ wnel c2 codd $;

--| An odd nonnegative integer is either 1 or greater than 2. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0o1gt2ALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel N codd)) (wo (wceq N c1) (wbr c2 N clt)) $;

--| An alternate characterization of an odd number greater than 1. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnoALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N codd)) (wcel (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn) $;

--| An alternate characterization of an odd nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2020.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0oALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel N codd)) (wcel (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn0) $;

--| An alternate characterization of an even nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0e (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel N ceven)) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0) $;

--| An alternate characterization of an even positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem nneven (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel N ceven)) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn) $;

--| For each odd nonnegative integer there is a nonnegative integer which, multiplied by 2 and increased by 1, results in the odd nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.) (Revised by AV, 22-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0onn0exALTV {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel N codd)) (wrex m (wceq N (co (co c2 (cv m) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0) $;

--| For each even nonnegative integer there is a nonnegative integer which, multiplied by 2, results in the even nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.) (Revised by AV, 22-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0enn0exALTV {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex m (wceq N (co c2 (cv m) cmul)) cn0) $;

--| For each even positive integer there is a positive integer which, multiplied by 2, results in the even positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem nnennexALTV {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex m (wceq N (co c2 (cv m) cmul)) cn) $;

--| 2 to the power of a positive integer is even. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnpw2evenALTV (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co c2 N cexp) ceven) $;

--| The sum of an even and an odd is odd. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jul-2020.)
theorem epoo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel B2 codd)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) codd) $;

--| The difference of an even and an odd is odd. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jul-2020.)
theorem emoo (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel B2 codd)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) codd) $;

--| The sum of two even numbers is even. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2020.)
theorem epee (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel B2 ceven)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) ceven) $;

--| The difference of two even numbers is even. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2020.)
theorem emee (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel B2 ceven)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) ceven) $;

--| If a summand is even, the other summand is even iff the sum is even. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2020.)
theorem evensumeven (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 ceven)) (wb (wcel A2 ceven) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) ceven)) $;

--| 3 is an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _3odd:
  $ wcel c3 codd $;

--| 4 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.)
theorem _4even:
  $ wcel c4 ceven $;

--| 5 is an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.)
theorem _5odd:
  $ wcel c5 codd $;

--| 6 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _6even:
  $ wcel c6 ceven $;

--| 7 is an odd number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _7odd:
  $ wcel c7 codd $;

--| 8 is an even number. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.)
theorem _8even:
  $ wcel c8 ceven $;

--| A prime number is even iff it is 2. (Contributed by AV, 21-Jul-2020.)
theorem evenprm2 (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wb (wcel P ceven) (wceq P c2)) $;

--| Every prime number not being 2 is an odd prime number. (Contributed by AV, 21-Aug-2021.)
theorem oddprmne2 (P: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel P codd)) (wcel P (cdif cprime (csn c2))) $;

--| A prime number which is odd is an integer greater than or equal to 3. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 21-Aug-2021.)
theorem oddprmuzge3 (P: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel P codd)) (wcel P (cfv c3 cuz)) $;

--| If an even number is greater than another even number, then it is greater than or equal to the other even number plus 2. (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2021.)
theorem evenltle (M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N ceven) (wcel M ceven) (wbr M N clt)) (wbr (co M c2 caddc) N cle) $;

--| If an odd number is the sum of two prime numbers, one of the prime numbers must be 2. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem odd2prm2 (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N codd) (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel Q cprime)) (wceq N (co P Q caddc))) (wo (wceq P c2) (wceq Q c2)) $;

--| If an even number is the sum of three prime numbers, one of the prime numbers must be 2. (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2021.)
theorem even3prm2 (P Q R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N ceven) (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wcel Q cprime) (wcel R cprime)) (wceq N (co (co P Q caddc) R caddc))) (w3o (wceq P c2) (wceq Q c2) (wceq R c2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ mogoldbb . (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem mogoldbblem {q p: setvar} (P Q R N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel P cprime) (wcel Q cprime) (wcel R cprime)) (wcel N ceven) (wceq (co N c2 caddc) (co (co P Q caddc) R caddc))) (wrex p (wrex q (wceq N (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc)) cprime) cprime) $;

--| Lemma for ~ perfectALTV . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem perfectALTVlem1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 codd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) csgm) (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (w3a (wcel (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) cn) (wcel (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin) cn) (wcel (co B2 (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin) cdiv) cn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ perfectALTV . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2020.)
theorem perfectALTVlem2 (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 codd) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co c1 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) csgm) (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel B2 cprime) (wceq B2 (co (co c2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cexp) c1 cmin))) $;

--| The Euclid-Euler theorem, or Perfect Number theorem. A positive even integer ` N ` is a perfect number (that is, its divisor sum is ` 2 N ` ) if and only if it is of the form ` 2 ^ ( p - 1 ) x. ( 2 ^ p - 1 ) ` , where ` 2 ^ p - 1 ` is prime (a Mersenne prime). (It follows from this that ` p ` is also prime.) This is Metamath 100 proof #70. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-May-2016.) (Revised by AV, 1-Jul-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem perfectALTV {p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel N ceven)) (wb (wceq (co c1 N csgm) (co c2 N cmul)) (wrex p (wa (wcel (co (co c2 (cv p) cexp) c1 cmin) cprime) (wceq N (co (co c2 (co (cv p) c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co c2 (cv p) cexp) c1 cmin) cmul))) cz)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the Fermat pseudoprimes.
term cfppr: class;

--| Define the function that maps a positive integer to the set of _Fermat pseudoprimes_ to the base of this positive integer. Since Fermat pseudoprimes shall be composite (positive) integers, they must be nonprime integers greater than or equal to 4 (we cannot use ` x e. NN ` ` /\ x e/ Prime ` because ` x = 1 ` would fulfil this requirement, but should not be regarded as "composite" integer). (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2023.)
axiom df_fppr {x3 n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfppr (cmpt n cn (crab x3 (wa (wnel (cv x3) cprime) (wbr (cv x3) (co (co (cv n) (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) cexp) c1 cmin) cdvds)) (cfv c4 cuz))) $;

--| The set of Fermat pseudoprimes to the base ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2023.)
theorem fppr {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cfppr) (crab x3 (wa (wnel (cv x3) cprime) (wbr (cv x3) (co (co N (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) cexp) c1 cmin) cdvds)) (cfv c4 cuz))) $;

--| The set of Fermat pseudoprimes to the base ` N ` , expressed by a modulo operation instead of the divisibility relation. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2023.)
theorem fpprmod {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cfppr) (crab x3 (wa (wnel (cv x3) cprime) (wceq (co (co N (co (cv x3) c1 cmin) cexp) (cv x3) cmo) c1)) (cfv c4 cuz))) $;

--| A Fermat pseudoprime to the base ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2023.)
theorem fpprel (N X: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wb (wcel X (cfv N cfppr)) (w3a (wcel X (cfv c4 cuz)) (wnel X cprime) (wceq (co (co N (co X c1 cmin) cexp) X cmo) c1))) $;

--| The base of a Fermat pseudoprime is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2023.)
theorem fpprbasnn (N X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv N cfppr)) (wcel N cn) $;

--| A Fermat pseudoprime to the base ` N ` is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2023.)
theorem fpprnn (N X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv N cfppr)) (wcel X cn) $;

--| A Fermat pseudoprime to the base 2 is odd. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem fppr2odd (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv c2 cfppr)) (wcel X codd) $;

--| 341 is the product of 11 and 31. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem _11t31e341:
  $ wceq (co (cdc c1 c1) (cdc c3 c1) cmul) (cdc (cdc c3 c4) c1) $;

--| Eight to the eighth power modulo nine is one. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem _2exp340mod341:
  $ wceq (co (co c2 (cdc (cdc c3 c4) cc0) cexp) (cdc (cdc c3 c4) c1) cmo) c1 $;

--| 341 is the (smallest) _Poulet number_ (Fermat pseudoprime to the base 2). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem _341fppr2:
  $ wcel (cdc (cdc c3 c4) c1) (cfv c2 cfppr) $;

--| 4 is the (smallest) Fermat pseudoprime to the base 1. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem _4fppr1:
  $ wcel c4 (cfv c1 cfppr) $;

--| Eight to the eighth power modulo nine is one. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2023.)
theorem _8exp8mod9:
  $ wceq (co (co c8 c8 cexp) c9 cmo) c1 $;

--| 9 is the (smallest) Fermat pseudoprime to the base 8. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2023.)
theorem _9fppr8:
  $ wcel c9 (cfv c8 cfppr) $;

--| Alternate definition of a _weak pseudoprime_ ` X ` , which fulfils ` ( N ^ X ) == N ` (modulo ` X ` ), see Wikipedia "Fermat pseudoprime", ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_pseudoprime , 29-May-2023. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2023.)
theorem dfwppr (N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X cn)) (wb (wceq (co (co N X cexp) X cmo) (co N X cmo)) (wbr X (co (co N X cexp) N cmin) cdvds)) $;

--| A Fermat pseudoprime to the base ` N ` is a _weak pseudoprime_ (see Wikipedia "Fermat pseudoprime", 29-May-2023, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_pseudoprime . (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2023.)
theorem fpprwppr (N X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X (cfv N cfppr)) (wceq (co (co N X cexp) X cmo) (co N X cmo)) $;

--| An integer ` X ` which is coprime with an integer ` N ` is a Fermat pseudoprime to the base ` N ` iff it is a weak pseudoprime to the base ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2023.)
theorem fpprwpprb (N X: class):
  $ wi (wceq (co X N cgcd) c1) (wb (wcel X (cfv N cfppr)) (wa (wa (wcel X (cfv c4 cuz)) (wnel X cprime)) (wa (wcel N cn) (wceq (co (co N X cexp) X cmo) (co N X cmo))))) $;

--| An alternate definition for a Fermat pseudoprime to the base 2. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem fpprel2 (X: class):
  $ wb (wcel X (cfv c2 cfppr)) (wa (w3a (wcel X (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X codd) (wnel X cprime)) (wceq (co (co c2 X cexp) X cmo) c2)) $;

--| Fermat's little theorem with base 8 reversed is not generally true: There is an integer ` p ` (for example 9, see ~ 9fppr8 ) so that " ` p ` is prime" does not follow from ` 8 ^ p == 8 ` (mod ` p ` ). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem nfermltl8rev {p: setvar}:
  $ wrex p (wn (wi (wceq (co (co c8 (cv p) cexp) (cv p) cmo) (co c8 (cv p) cmo)) (wcel (cv p) cprime))) (cfv c3 cuz) $;

--| Fermat's little theorem with base 2 reversed is not generally true: There is an integer ` p ` (for example 341, see ~ 341fppr2 ) so that " ` p ` is prime" does not follow from ` 2 ^ p == 2 ` (mod ` p ` ). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem nfermltl2rev {p: setvar}:
  $ wrex p (wn (wi (wceq (co (co c2 (cv p) cexp) (cv p) cmo) (co c2 (cv p) cmo)) (wcel (cv p) cprime))) (cfv c3 cuz) $;

--| Fermat's little theorem reversed is not generally true: There are integers ` a ` and ` p ` so that " ` p ` is prime" does not follow from ` a ^ p == a ` (mod ` p ` ). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2023.)
theorem nfermltlrev {p a: setvar}:
  $ wrex a (wrex p (wn (wi (wceq (co (co (cv a) (cv p) cexp) (cv p) cmo) (co (cv a) (cv p) cmo)) (wcel (cv p) cprime))) (cfv c3 cuz)) cz $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the set of even numbers which have a Goldbach partition.
term cgbe: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the set of odd numbers which can be written as a sum of three primes.
term cgbow: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the set of odd numbers which can be written as a sum of three odd primes.
term cgbo: class;

--| Define the set of (even) Goldbach numbers, which are positive even integers that can be expressed as the sum of two odd primes. By this definition, the binary Goldbach conjecture can be expressed as ` A. n e. Even ( 4 < n -> n e. GoldbachEven ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
axiom df_gbe {z q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgbe (crab z (wrex p (wrex q (w3a (wcel (cv p) codd) (wcel (cv q) codd) (wceq (cv z) (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc))) cprime) cprime) ceven) $;

--| Define the set of weak odd Goldbach numbers, which are positive odd integers that can be expressed as the sum of three primes. By this definition, the weak ternary Goldbach conjecture can be expressed as ` A. m e. Odd ( 5 < m -> m e. GoldbachOddW ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-Jun-2020.)
axiom df_gbow {z r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgbow (crab z (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime) codd) $;

--| Define the set of (strong) odd Goldbach numbers, which are positive odd integers that can be expressed as the sum of three _odd_ primes. By this definition, the strong ternary Goldbach conjecture can be expressed as ` A. m e. Odd ( 7 < m -> m e. GoldbachOdd ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
axiom df_gbo {z r q p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgbo (crab z (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) codd) (wcel (cv q) codd) (wcel (cv r) codd)) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc))) cprime) cprime) cprime) codd) $;

--| The predicate "is an even Goldbach number". An even Goldbach number is an even integer having a Goldbach partition, i.e. which can be written as a sum of two odd primes. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem isgbe {q p: setvar} (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z cgbe) (wa (wcel Z ceven) (wrex p (wrex q (w3a (wcel (cv p) codd) (wcel (cv q) codd) (wceq Z (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc))) cprime) cprime)) $;

--| The predicate "is a weak odd Goldbach number". A weak odd Goldbach number is an odd integer having a Goldbach partition, i.e. which can be written as a sum of three primes. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem isgbow {r q p: setvar} (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z cgbow) (wa (wcel Z codd) (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq Z (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime)) $;

--| The predicate "is an odd Goldbach number". An odd Goldbach number is an odd integer having a Goldbach partition, i.e. which can be written as sum of three odd primes. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem isgbo {r q p: setvar} (Z: class):
  $ wb (wcel Z cgbo) (wa (wcel Z codd) (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) codd) (wcel (cv q) codd) (wcel (cv r) codd)) (wceq Z (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc))) cprime) cprime) cprime)) $;

--| An even Goldbach number is even. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbeeven (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbe) (wcel Z ceven) $;

--| A weak odd Goldbach number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbowodd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbow) (wcel Z codd) $;

--| A (strong) odd Goldbach number is a weak Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbogbow (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbo) (wcel Z cgbow) $;

--| An odd Goldbach number is odd. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem gboodd (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbo) (wcel Z codd) $;

--| Any even Goldbach number is positive. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbepos (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbe) (wcel Z cn) $;

--| Any weak odd Goldbach number is positive. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbowpos (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbow) (wcel Z cn) $;

--| Any odd Goldbach number is positive. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbopos (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbo) (wcel Z cn) $;

--| Any even Goldbach number is greater than 5. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbegt5 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbe) (wbr c5 Z clt) $;

--| Any weak odd Goldbach number is greater than 5. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbowgt5 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbow) (wbr c5 Z clt) $;

--| Any weak odd Goldbach number is greater than or equal to 7. Because of ~ 7gbow , this bound is strict. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbowge7 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbow) (wbr c7 Z cle) $;

--| Any odd Goldbach number is greater than or equal to 9. Because of ~ 9gbo , this bound is strict. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem gboge9 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbo) (wbr c9 Z cle) $;

--| Any even Goldbach number is greater than or equal to 6. Because of ~ 6gbe , this bound is strict. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbege6 (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cgbe) (wbr c6 Z cle) $;

--| The Goldbach partition of 6. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbpart6:
  $ wceq c6 (co c3 c3 caddc) $;

--| The (weak) Goldbach partition of 7. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbpart7:
  $ wceq c7 (co (co c2 c2 caddc) c3 caddc) $;

--| The Goldbach partition of 8. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbpart8:
  $ wceq c8 (co c3 c5 caddc) $;

--| The (strong) Goldbach partition of 9. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbpart9:
  $ wceq c9 (co (co c3 c3 caddc) c3 caddc) $;

--| The (strong) Goldbach partition of 11. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2020.)
theorem gbpart11:
  $ wceq (cdc c1 c1) (co (co c3 c3 caddc) c5 caddc) $;

--| 6 is an even Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _6gbe:
  $ wcel c6 cgbe $;

--| 7 is a weak odd Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _7gbow:
  $ wcel c7 cgbow $;

--| 8 is an even Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem _8gbe:
  $ wcel c8 cgbe $;

--| 9 is an odd Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem _9gbo:
  $ wcel c9 cgbo $;

--| 11 is an odd Goldbach number. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2020.)
theorem _11gbo:
  $ wcel (cdc c1 c1) cgbo $;

--| If the strong ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then the weak ternary Goldbach conjecture holds, too. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2020.)
theorem stgoldbwt {n: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c7 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbo)) codd) (wral n (wi (wbr c5 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbow)) codd) $;

--| If the strong binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then the (weak) ternary Goldbach conjecture holds, too. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2020.)
theorem sbgoldbwt {m n: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral m (wi (wbr c5 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbow)) codd) $;

--| If the strong binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then the (strong) ternary Goldbach conjecture holds, too. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem sbgoldbst {m n: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral m (wi (wbr c7 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbo)) codd) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ sbgoldbalt : If an even number greater than 4 is the sum of two primes, one of the prime summands must be odd, i.e. not 2. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2020.)
theorem sbgoldbaltlem1 (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel Q cprime)) (wi (w3a (wcel N ceven) (wbr c4 N clt) (wceq N (co P Q caddc))) (wcel Q codd)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ sbgoldbalt : If an even number greater than 4 is the sum of two primes, the primes must be odd, i.e. not 2. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2020.)
theorem sbgoldbaltlem2 (P Q N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P cprime) (wcel Q cprime)) (wi (w3a (wcel N ceven) (wbr c4 N clt) (wceq N (co P Q caddc))) (wa (wcel P codd) (wcel Q codd))) $;

--| An alternate (related to the original) formulation of the binary Goldbach conjecture: Every even integer greater than 2 can be expressed as the sum of two primes. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jul-2020.)
theorem sbgoldbalt {n q p: setvar}:
  $ wb (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wi (wbr c2 (cv n) clt) (wrex p (wrex q (wceq (cv n) (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc)) cprime) cprime)) ceven) $;

--| If the strong binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, the binary Goldbach conjecture is valid. (Contributed by AV, 23-Dec-2021.)
theorem sbgoldbb {n q p: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wi (wbr c2 (cv n) clt) (wrex p (wrex q (wceq (cv n) (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc)) cprime) cprime)) ceven) $;

--| If the binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then an even integer greater than 5 can be expressed as the sum of three primes: Since ` ( N - 2 ) ` is even iff ` N ` is even, there would be primes ` p ` and ` q ` with ` ( N - 2 ) = ( p + q ) ` , and therefore ` N = ( ( p + q ) + 2 ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 24-Dec-2021.)
theorem sgoldbeven3prm {n r q p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wi (wa (wcel N ceven) (wbr c6 N cle)) (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq N (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime)) $;

--| If the strong binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, the modern version of the original formulation of the Goldbach conjecture also holds: Every integer greater than 5 can be expressed as the sum of three primes. (Contributed by AV, 24-Dec-2021.)
theorem sbgoldbm {n r q p: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq (cv n) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime) (cfv c6 cuz)) $;

--| If the modern version of the original formulation of the Goldbach conjecture is valid, the (weak) binary Goldbach conjecture also holds. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem mogoldbb {n r q p: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq (cv n) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime) (cfv c6 cuz)) (wral n (wi (wbr c2 (cv n) clt) (wrex p (wrex q (wceq (cv n) (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc)) cprime) cprime)) ceven) $;

--| The strong binary Goldbach conjecture and the modern version of the original formulation of the Goldbach conjecture are equivalent. (Contributed by AV, 26-Dec-2021.)
theorem sbgoldbmb {n r q p: setvar}:
  $ wb (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq (cv n) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) cprime) cprime) cprime) (cfv c6 cuz)) $;

--| If the strong binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, the original formulation of the Goldbach conjecture also holds: Every integer greater than 2 can be expressed as the sum of three "primes" with regarding 1 to be a prime (as Goldbach did). Original text: "Es scheint wenigstens, dass eine jede Zahl, die groesser ist als 2, ein aggregatum trium numerorum primorum sey." (Goldbach, 1742). (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2021.)
theorem sbgoldbo {n r q p: setvar} (P: class n):
  $ wceq P (cun (csn c1) cprime) $ >
  $ wi (wral n (wi (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wceq (cv n) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc)) P) P) P) (cfv c3 cuz)) $;

--| 4 is the sum of at most 3 (actually 2) primes. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum3primes4 {f2 k d: setvar}:
  $ wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c3 cle) (wceq c4 (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn $;

--| 4 is the sum of at most 4 (actually 2) primes. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primes4 {f2 k d: setvar}:
  $ wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq c4 (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn $;

--| Every prime is "the sum of at most 3" (actually one - the prime itself) primes. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 17-Apr-2021.)
theorem nnsum3primesprm {f2 k d: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c3 cle) (wceq P (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Every prime is "the sum of at most 4" (actually one - the prime itself) primes. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesprm {f2 k d: setvar} (P: class):
  $ wi (wcel P cprime) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq P (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Any even Goldbach number is the sum of at most 3 (actually 2) primes. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum3primesgbe {f2 k d: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cgbe) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c3 cle) (wceq N (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Any even Goldbach number is the sum of at most 4 (actually 2) primes. (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesgbe {f2 k d: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cgbe) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq N (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Every integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to 8 is the sum of at most 3 primes. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum3primesle9 {f2 k d: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr N c8 cle)) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c3 cle) (wceq N (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| Every integer greater than 1 and less than or equal to 8 is the sum of at most 4 primes. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesle9 {f2 k d: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wbr N c8 cle)) (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq N (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) $;

--| If the (weak) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every odd integer greater than 5 is the sum of 3 primes. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jul-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesodd {f2 k m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c5 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbow)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c6 cuz)) (wcel N codd)) (wrex f2 (wceq N (csu k (co c1 c3 cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))) (co cprime (co c1 c3 cfz) cmap))) $;

--| If the (strong) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every odd integer greater than 7 is the sum of 3 primes. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesoddALTV {f2 k m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c7 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbo)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c8 cuz)) (wcel N codd)) (wrex f2 (wceq N (csu k (co c1 c3 cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))) (co cprime (co c1 c3 cfz) cmap))) $;

--| If the (weak) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every even integer greater than 8 is the sum of an odd Goldbach number and 3. (Contributed by AV, 24-Jul-2020.)
theorem evengpop3 {m o: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c5 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbow)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c9 cuz)) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex o (wceq N (co (cv o) c3 caddc)) cgbow)) $;

--| If the (strong) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every even integer greater than 10 is the sum of an odd Goldbach number and 3. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 15-Sep-2021.)
theorem evengpoap3 {m o: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c7 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbo)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c2) cuz)) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex o (wceq N (co (cv o) c3 caddc)) cgbo)) $;

--| If the (weak) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every even integer greater than 8 is the sum of 4 primes. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primeseven {f2 k m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c5 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbow)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c9 cuz)) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex f2 (wceq N (csu k (co c1 c4 cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))) (co cprime (co c1 c4 cfz) cmap))) $;

--| If the (strong) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every even integer greater than 10 is the sum of 4 primes. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2020.)
theorem nnsum4primesevenALTV {f2 k m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c7 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbo)) codd) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c2) cuz)) (wcel N ceven)) (wrex f2 (wceq N (csu k (co c1 c4 cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)))) (co cprime (co c1 c4 cfz) cmap))) $;

--| If the (weak) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every integer greater than 1 is the sum of at most 4 primes, showing that Schnirelmann's constant would be less than or equal to 4. See corollary 1.1 in [Helfgott] p. 4. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2020.)
theorem wtgoldbnnsum4prm {f2 k m n d: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c5 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbow)) codd) (wral n (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq (cv n) (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| If the (strong) ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every integer greater than 1 is the sum of at most 4 primes. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2020.)
theorem stgoldbnnsum4prm {f2 k m n d: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c7 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbo)) codd) (wral n (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c4 cle) (wceq (cv n) (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| If the binary Goldbach conjecture is valid, then every integer greater than 1 is the sum of at most 3 primes, showing that Schnirelmann's constant would be equal to 3. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.)
theorem bgoldbnnsum3prm {f2 k m n d: setvar}:
  $ wi (wral m (wi (wbr c4 (cv m) clt) (wcel (cv m) cgbe)) ceven) (wral n (wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv d) c3 cle) (wceq (cv n) (csu k (co c1 (cv d) cfz) (cfv (cv k) (cv f2))))) (co cprime (co c1 (cv d) cfz) cmap)) cn) (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ bgoldbtbnd : the odd numbers between 7 and 13 (exclusive) are odd Goldbach numbers. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jul-2020.)
theorem bgoldbtbndlem1 (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N codd) (wbr c7 N clt) (wcel N (co c7 (cdc c1 c3) cico))) (wcel N cgbo) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ bgoldbtbnd . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2020.)
theorem bgoldbtbndlem2 {i n: setvar} (ph: wff i n) (D: class n) (S: class i n) (F I: class n) (M: class i n) (N: class n) (X: class i n):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) N clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv c3 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv i) F) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) (co N c4 cmin) clt) (wbr c4 (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) clt)) (co cc0 D cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) c7) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cdc c1 c3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (cfv D F) clt) $ >
  $ wceq S (co X (cfv (co I c1 cmin) F) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X codd) (wcel I (co c1 D cfzo))) (wi (wa (wcel X (co (cfv I F) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) F) cico)) (wbr (co X (cfv I F) cmin) c4 cle)) (w3a (wcel S ceven) (wbr S N clt) (wbr c4 S clt))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ bgoldbtbnd . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2020.)
theorem bgoldbtbndlem3 {i n: setvar} (ph: wff i n) (D: class n) (S: class i n) (F I: class n) (M: class i n) (N: class n) (X: class i n):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) N clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv c3 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv i) F) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) (co N c4 cmin) clt) (wbr c4 (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) clt)) (co cc0 D cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) c7) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cdc c1 c3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (cfv D F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv D F) cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (co X (cfv I F) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X codd) (wcel I (co c1 D cfzo))) (wi (wa (wcel X (co (cfv I F) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) F) cico)) (wbr c4 S clt)) (w3a (wcel S ceven) (wbr S N clt) (wbr c4 S clt))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ bgoldbtbnd . (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2020.)
theorem bgoldbtbndlem4 {i n r q p: setvar} (ph: wff i n) (D F I: class n) (M: class i n r q p) (N: class r q p) (X: class i n):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) N clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv c3 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv i) F) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) (co N c4 cmin) clt) (wbr c4 (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) clt)) (co cc0 D cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) c7) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cdc c1 c3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (cfv D F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv D F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wcel I (co c1 D cfzo))) (wcel X codd)) (wi (wa (wcel X (co (cfv I F) (cfv (co I c1 caddc) F) cico)) (wbr (co X (cfv I F) cmin) c4 cle)) (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) codd) (wcel (cv q) codd) (wcel (cv r) codd)) (wceq X (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc))) cprime) cprime) cprime)) $;

--| If the binary Goldbach conjecture is valid up to an integer ` N ` , and there is a series ("ladder") of primes with a difference of at most ` N ` up to an integer ` M ` , then the strong ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid up to ` M ` , see section 1.2.2 in [Helfgott] p. 4 with N = 4 x 10^18, taken from [OeSilva], and M = 8.875 x 10^30. (Contributed by AV, 1-Aug-2020.)
theorem bgoldbtbnd {i n: setvar} (ph: wff i) (D F: class n) (M: class i n) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (cdc c1 c1) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) N clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D (cfv c3 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cfv D ciccp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral i (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv i) F) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) (co N c4 cmin) clt) (wbr c4 (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv i) F) cmin) clt)) (co cc0 D cfzo)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv cc0 F) c7) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (cdc c1 c3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr M (cfv D F) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv D F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c7 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) M clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbo)) codd) $;

--| The binary Goldbach conjecture is valid for all even numbers less than or equal to 4x10^18, see section 2 in [OeSilva] p. 2042. Temporarily provided as "axiom". (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
axiom ax_bgbltosilva (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N ceven) (wbr c4 N clt) (wbr N (co c4 (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c1 c8) cexp) cmul) cle)) (wcel N cgbe) $;

--| Temporary duplicate of ~ tgoldbachgt , provided as "axiom" as long as this theorem is in the mathbox of Thierry Arnoux: Odd integers greater than ` ( ; 1 0 ^ ; 2 7 ) ` have at least a representation as a sum of three odd primes. Final statement in section 7.4 of [Helfgott] p. 70 , expressed using the set ` G ` of odd numbers which can be written as a sum of three odd primes. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 22-Dec-2021.)
axiom ax_tgoldbachgt {z m n r q p: setvar} (G: class z n r q p) (O: class n):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wn (wbr c2 (cv z) cdvds)) cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab z (wrex p (wrex q (wrex r (wa (w3a (wcel (cv p) O) (wcel (cv q) O) (wcel (cv r) O)) (wceq (cv z) (co (co (cv p) (cv q) caddc) (cv r) caddc))) cprime) cprime) cprime) O) $ >
  $ wrex m (wa (wbr (cv m) (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c2 c7) cexp) cle) (wral n (wi (wbr (cv m) (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) G)) O)) cn $;

--| Variant of Thierry Arnoux's ~ tgoldbachgt using the symbols ` Odd ` and ` GoldbachOdd ` : The ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid for large odd numbers (i.e. for all odd numbers greater than a fixed ` m ` ). This is proven by Helfgott (see section 7.4 in [Helfgott] p. 70) for ` m ` = 10^27. (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 15-Jan-2022.)
theorem tgoldbachgtALTV {m n: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wa (wbr (cv m) (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c2 c7) cexp) cle) (wral n (wi (wbr (cv m) (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbo)) codd)) cn $;

--| The binary Goldbach conjecture is valid for small even numbers (i.e. for all even numbers less than or equal to a fixed big ` m ` ). This is verified for m = 4 x 10^18 by Oliveira e Silva, see ~ ax-bgbltosilva . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem bgoldbachlt {m n: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wa (wbr (co c4 (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c1 c8) cexp) cmul) (cv m) cle) (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c4 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) (cv m) clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbe)) ceven)) cn $;

--| There is a partition ("ladder") of primes from 7 to 8.8 x 10^30 with parts ("rungs") having lengths of at least 4 and at most N - 4, see section 1.2.2 in [Helfgott] p. 4. Temporarily provided as "axiom". (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
axiom ax_hgprmladder {f2 i d: setvar}:
  $ wrex d (wrex f2 (wa (w3a (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) c7) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cdc c1 c3)) (wceq (cfv (cv d) (cv f2)) (co (cdc c8 c9) (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c2 c9) cexp) cmul))) (wral i (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv i) (cv f2)) (cdif cprime (csn c2))) (wbr (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv i) (cv f2)) cmin) (co (co c4 (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c1 c8) cexp) cmul) c4 cmin) clt) (wbr c4 (co (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv i) (cv f2)) cmin) clt)) (co cc0 (cv d) cfzo))) (cfv (cv d) ciccp)) (cfv c3 cuz) $;

--| The ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid for all odd numbers less than 8.8 x 10^30 (actually 8.875694 x 10^30, see section 1.2.2 in [Helfgott] p. 4, using ~ bgoldbachlt , ~ ax-hgprmladder and ~ bgoldbtbnd . (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem tgblthelfgott (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N codd) (wbr c7 N clt) (wbr N (co (cdc c8 c8) (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c2 c9) cexp) cmul) clt)) (wcel N cgbo) $;

--| The ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid for small odd numbers (i.e. for all odd numbers less than a fixed big ` m ` greater than 8 x 10^30). This is verified for m = 8.875694 x 10^30 by Helfgott, see ~ tgblthelfgott . (Contributed by AV, 4-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem tgoldbachlt {m n: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wa (wbr (co c8 (co (cdc c1 cc0) (cdc c3 cc0) cexp) cmul) (cv m) clt) (wral n (wi (wa (wbr c7 (cv n) clt) (wbr (cv n) (cv m) clt)) (wcel (cv n) cgbo)) codd)) cn $;

--| The ternary Goldbach conjecture is valid. Main theorem in [Helfgott] p. 2. This follows from ~ tgoldbachlt and ~ ax-tgoldbachgt . (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2020.) (Revised by AV, 9-Sep-2021.)
theorem tgoldbach {n: setvar}:
  $ wral n (wi (wbr c7 (cv n) clt) (wcel (cv n) cgbo)) codd $;

--| Extend class notation with closed neighborhoods (of a vertex in a graph).
term cclnbgr: class;

--| Define the closed _neighborhood_ resp. the class of all neighbors of a vertex (in a graph) and the vertex itself, see definition in section I.1 of [Bollobas] p. 3. The closed neighborhood of a vertex is the set of all vertices which are connected with this vertex by an edge and the vertex itself (in contrast to an open neighborhood, see ~ df-nbgr ). Alternatively, a closed neighborhood of a vertex could have been defined as its open neighborhood enhanced by the vertex itself, see ~ dfclnbgr4 . This definition is applicable even for arbitrary hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2025.)
axiom df_clnbgr {v e g2 n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cclnbgr (cmpo g2 v cvv (cfv (cv g2) cvtx) (cun (csn (cv v)) (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr (cv v) (cv n)) (cv e)) (cfv (cv g2) cedg)) (cfv (cv g2) cvtx)))) $;

--| The closed neighborhood is empty if the graph ` G ` or the vertex ` N ` are proper classes. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrprc0 (G N: class):
  $ wi (wn (wa (wcel G cvv) (wcel N cvv))) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) c0) $;

--| If a class ` X ` has at least one element in its closed neighborhood, this class must be a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrcl (G N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (co G X cclnbgr)) (wcel X V) $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex ` V ` in a graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrval {e n: setvar} (E: class n) (G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the closed neighborhood of a vertex breaking up the subset relationship of an unordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2025.)
theorem dfclnbgr2 {e n: setvar} (E: class n) (G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) (crab n (wrex e (wa (wcel N (cv e)) (wcel (cv n) (cv e))) E) V))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the closed neighborhood of a vertex as union of the vertex with its open neighborhood. (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2025.)
theorem dfclnbgr4 (G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) (co G N cnbgr))) $;

--| An element of the closed neighborhood of a vertex which is not the vertex itself is an element of the open neighborhood of the vertex. (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem elclnbgrelnbgr (G N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (co G N cclnbgr)) (wne X N)) (wcel X (co G N cnbgr)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the closed neighborhood of a vertex using the edge function instead of the edges themselves (see also ~ clnbgrval ). (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2025.)
theorem dfclnbgr3 {i n: setvar} (G: class i) (I N: class) (V: class i):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wfun I)) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) (crab n (wrex i (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cdm I)) V))) $;

--| If a class ` X ` is not a vertex of a graph ` G ` , then it has an empty closed neighborhood in ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrnvtx0 (G V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wnel X V) (wceq (co G X cclnbgr) c0) $;

--| Characterization of a member ` N ` of the closed neighborhood of a vertex ` X ` in a graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrel {e: setvar} (E G N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel N (co G X cclnbgr)) (wa (wa (wcel N V) (wcel X V)) (wo (wceq N X) (wrex e (wss (cpr X N) (cv e)) E))) $;

--| Every vertex ` K ` is a member of its closed neighborhood. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrvtxel (G K V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K V) (wcel K (co G K cclnbgr)) $;

--| Every member ` N ` of the closed neighborhood of a vertex ` K ` is a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrisvtx (G K N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (co G K cclnbgr)) (wcel N V) $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex ` K ` in a graph is a subset of all vertices of the graph. (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrssvtx (G K V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wss (co G K cclnbgr) V $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex is never empty. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrn0 (G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wne (co G N cclnbgr) c0) $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex in a pseudograph. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbupgr {n: setvar} (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cupgr) (wcel N V)) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) (crab n (wcel (cpr N (cv n)) E) V))) $;

--| A member of the closed neighborhood of a vertex in a pseudograph. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbupgrel (E G K N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cupgr) (wcel K V) (wcel N V)) (wb (wcel N (co G K cclnbgr)) (wo (wceq N K) (wcel (cpr N K) E))) $;

--| A member of the closed neighborhood of a vertex in a pseudograph. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2025.)
theorem clnbupgreli (E G K N: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cupgr) (wcel N (co G K cclnbgr))) (wo (wceq N K) (wcel (cpr N K) E)) $;

--| In a null graph (with no vertices), all closed neighborhoods are empty. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2020.)
theorem clnbgr0vtx (G K: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cfv G cvtx) c0) (wceq (co G K cclnbgr) c0) $;

--| In an empty graph (with no edges), all closed neighborhoods consists of a single vertex. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgr0edg (G K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cfv G cedg) c0) (wcel K (cfv G cvtx))) (wceq (co G K cclnbgr) (csn K)) $;

--| In a graph, the closed neighborhood relation is symmetric: a vertex ` N ` in a graph ` G ` is a neighbor of a second vertex ` K ` iff the second vertex ` K ` is a neighbor of the first vertex ` N ` . (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrsym (G K N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (co G K cclnbgr)) (wcel K (co G N cclnbgr)) $;

--| If a (not necessarily proper) unordered pair containing a vertex is an edge, the other vertex is in the closed neighborhood of the first vertex. (Contributed by AV, 23-Aug-2025.)
theorem predgclnbgrel (E G N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N V) (wcel X V) (wcel (cpr X N) E)) (wcel N (co G X cclnbgr)) $;

--| A vertex connected by an edge with another vertex is a neighbor of that vertex. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem clnbgredg (E G K N X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (w3a (wcel K E) (wcel X K) (wcel Y K))) (wcel Y N) $;

--| The vertices connected by an edge are a subset of the neighborhood of each of these vertices. (Contributed by AV, 26-May-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem clnbgrssedg (E G K N X: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel K E) (wcel X K)) (wss K N) $;

--| The size of the closed neighborhood of a vertex in a simple graph is finite iff the number of edges having this vertex as endpoint is finite. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem edgusgrclnbfin {e: setvar} (U E: class) (G V: class e):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel U V)) (wb (wcel (co G U cclnbgr) cfn) (wcel (crab e (wcel U (cv e)) E) cfn)) $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex in a simple graph with a finite number of edges is a finite set. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbusgrfi (U E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cusgr) (wcel E cfn) (wcel U V)) (wcel (co G U cclnbgr) cfn) $;

--| The closed neighborhood of a vertex in a finite simple graph is a finite set. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbfiusgrfi (G N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cfusgr) (wcel N (cfv G cvtx))) (wcel (co G N cclnbgr) cfn) $;

--| The size of the closed neighborhood of a vertex is at most the number of vertices of a graph. (Contributed by AV, 10-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrlevtx (U G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wbr (cfv (co G U cclnbgr) chash) (cfv V chash) cle $;

--| Alternate definition of the semiclosed neighborhood of a vertex breaking up the subset relationship of an unordered pair. A _semiclosed neighborhood_ ` S ` of a vertex ` N ` is the set of all vertices incident with edges which join the vertex ` N ` with a vertex. Therefore, a vertex is contained in its semiclosed neighborhood if it is connected with any vertex by an edge (see ~ sclnbgrelself ), even only with itself (i.e., by a loop). (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem dfsclnbgr2 {e n: setvar} (S E G: class e n) (N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wa (wcel N (cv e)) (wcel (cv n) (cv e))) E) V)) $;

--| Characterization of a member ` X ` of the semiclosed neighborhood of a vertex ` N ` in a graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem sclnbgrel {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E: class) (G: class e n) (N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel X S) (wa (wcel X V) (wrex e (wss (cpr N X) (cv e)) E)) $;

--| A vertex ` N ` is a member of its semiclosed neighborhood iff there is an edge joining the vertex with a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem sclnbgrelself {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E: class) (G: class e n) (N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel N S) (wa (wcel N V) (wrex e (wcel N (cv e)) E)) $;

--| Every member ` X ` of the semiclosed neighborhood of a vertex ` N ` is a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem sclnbgrisvtx {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E: class) (G: class e n) (N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X S) (wcel X V) $;

--| Alternate definition of the closed neighborhood of a vertex as union of the vertex with its semiclosed neighborhood. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem dfclnbgr5 {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) S)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the (open) neighborhood of a vertex as a semiclosed neighborhood without itself. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem dfnbgr5 {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cnbgr) (cdif S (csn N))) $;

--| Subset chain for different kinds of neighborhoods of a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem dfnbgrss {e n: setvar} (S: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wa (wss (co G N cnbgr) S) (wss S (co G N cclnbgr))) $;

--| Alternate definition of the semiopen neighborhood of a vertex breaking up the subset relationship of an unordered pair. A _semiopen neighborhood_ ` U ` of a vertex ` N ` is its open neighborhood together with itself if there is a loop at this vertex. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2025.)
theorem dfvopnbgr2 {e n: setvar} (U: class e n) (E G: class n) (N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq U (crab n (wrex e (wo (w3a (wne (cv n) N) (wcel N (cv e)) (wcel (cv n) (cv e))) (wa (wceq (cv n) N) (wceq (cv e) (csn (cv n))))) E) V)) $;

--| Characterization of a member ` X ` of the semiopen neighborhood of a vertex ` N ` in a graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem vopnbgrel {e n: setvar} (U: class e n) (E: class) (G: class n) (N V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wb (wcel X U) (wa (wcel X V) (wrex e (wo (w3a (wne X N) (wcel N (cv e)) (wcel X (cv e))) (wa (wceq X N) (wceq (cv e) (csn X)))) E))) $;

--| A vertex ` N ` is a member of its semiopen neighborhood iff there is a loop joining the vertex with itself. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem vopnbgrelself {e n: setvar} (U: class e n) (E: class) (G: class n) (N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wb (wcel N U) (wrex e (wceq (cv e) (csn N)) E)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the closed neighborhood of a vertex as union of the vertex with its semiopen neighborhood. (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2025.)
theorem dfclnbgr6 {e n: setvar} (U: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cclnbgr) (cun (csn N) U)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the (open) neighborhood of a vertex as a difference of its semiopen neighborhood and the singleton of itself. (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2025.)
theorem dfnbgr6 {e n: setvar} (U: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (co G N cnbgr) (cdif U (csn N))) $;

--| Alternate definition of a semiclosed neighborhood of a vertex as a union of a semiopen neighborhood and the vertex itself if there is a loop at this vertex. (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2025.)
theorem dfsclnbgr6 {e n: setvar} (S U: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq S (cun U (crab n (wrex e (wcel (cv n) (cv e)) E) (csn N)))) $;

--| Subset chain for different kinds of neighborhoods of a vertex. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2025.)
theorem dfnbgrss2 {e n: setvar} (S U: class e n) (E G N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq U (crab n (wo (wcel (cv n) (co G N cnbgr)) (wrex e (wa (wceq N (cv n)) (wceq (cv e) (csn N))) E)) V) $ >
  $ wceq S (crab n (wrex e (wss (cpr N (cv n)) (cv e)) E) V) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N V) (w3a (wss (co G N cnbgr) U) (wss U S) (wss S (co G N cclnbgr))) $;

--| Extend class notation with induced subgraphs.
term cisubgr: class;

--| Define the function mapping graphs and subsets of their vertices to their induced subgraphs. A _subgraph induced by a subset of vertices_ of a graph is a subgraph of the graph which contains all edges of the graph that join vertices of the subgraph (see section I.1 in [Bollobas] p. 2 or section 1.1 in [Diestel] p. 4). Although a graph may be given in any meaningful representation, its induced subgraphs are always ordered pairs of vertices and edges. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_isubgr {x3 v e g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cisubgr (cmpo g2 v cvv (cpw (cfv (cv g2) cvtx)) (cop (cv v) (csb e (cfv (cv g2) ciedg) (cres (cv e) (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cv e)) (cv v)) (cdm (cv e))))))) $;

--| The subgraph induced by a subset of vertices. (Contributed by AV, 12-May-2025.)
theorem isisubgr {x3: setvar} (S E G V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G W) (wss S V)) (wceq (co G S cisubgr) (cop S (cres E (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) E) S) (cdm E))))) $;

--| The edges of an induced subgraph. (Contributed by AV, 12-May-2025.)
theorem isubgriedg {x3: setvar} (S E G V: class) (W: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G W) (wss S V)) (wceq (cfv (co G S cisubgr) ciedg) (cres E (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) E) S) (cdm E)))) $;

--| The subgraph induced by the full set of vertices of a hypergraph. (Contributed by AV, 12-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrvtxuhgr (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wceq (co G V cisubgr) (cop V E)) $;

--| The edges of an induced subgraph of a graph are edges of the graph. (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgredgss (S E G H I V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cisubgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G W) (wss S V)) (wss I E) $;

--| An edge of an induced subgraph of a hypergraph is an edge of the hypergraph connecting vertices of the subgraph. (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgredg (S E G H I K V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (co G S cisubgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wss S V)) (wb (wcel K I) (wa (wcel K E) (wss K S))) $;

--| The vertices of an induced subgraph. (Contributed by AV, 12-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrvtx (S G V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G W) (wss S V)) (wceq (cfv (co G S cisubgr) cvtx) S) $;

--| An induced subgraph of a hypergraph is a hypergraph. (Contributed by AV, 13-May-2025.)
theorem isubgruhgr (S G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wss S V)) (wcel (co G S cisubgr) cuhgr) $;

--| An induced subgraph of a hypergraph is a subgraph of the hypergraph. (Contributed by AV, 14-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrsubgr (S G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wss S V)) (wbr (co G S cisubgr) G csubgr) $;

--| An induced subgraph of a pseudograph is a pseudograph. (Contributed by AV, 14-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrupgr (S G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cupgr) (wss S V)) (wcel (co G S cisubgr) cupgr) $;

--| An induced subgraph of a multigraph is a multigraph. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrumgr (S G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cumgr) (wss S V)) (wcel (co G S cisubgr) cumgr) $;

--| An induced subgraph of a simple graph is a simple graph. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrusgr (S G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wss S V)) (wcel (co G S cisubgr) cusgr) $;

--| The subgraph induced by an empty set of vertices of a hypergraph. (Contributed by AV, 13-May-2025.)
theorem isubgr0uhgr (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wceq (co G c0 cisubgr) (cop c0 c0)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the graph ispmorphisms as pair.
term cgrisom: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the graph ispmorphisms.
term cgrim: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the "is isomorphic to" relation for graphs.
term cgric: class;

--| Define the class of all isomorphisms between two graphs. In contrast to ` ( F GraphIso H ) ` , which is a set of functions between the vertices, ` ( F GraphIsom H ) ` is a set of pairs of functions: a function between the vertices, and a function between the (indices of the) edges. It is not clear if such a definition is useful. In the definition by [Diestel] p. 3, for example, the bijection between the vertices is called an isomorphism, as formalized in ~ df-grim . (Contributed by AV, 11-Dec-2022.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_grisom {x3 y1 f2 g2 i: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgrisom (cmpo x3 y1 cvv cvv (copab f2 g2 (w3a (wf1o (cfv (cv x3) cvtx) (cfv (cv y1) cvtx) (cv f2)) (wf1o (cdm (cfv (cv x3) ciedg)) (cdm (cfv (cv y1) ciedg)) (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) (cfv (cv x3) ciedg))) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) (cfv (cv y1) ciedg))) (cdm (cfv (cv x3) ciedg)))))) $;

--| An isomorphism between two graphs is a bijection between the sets of vertices of the two graphs that preserves adjacency, see definition in [Diestel] p. 3. (Contributed by AV, 19-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_grim {e f2 g2 h i j d: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgrim (cmpo g2 h cvv cvv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cfv (cv g2) cvtx) (cfv (cv h) cvtx) (cv f2)) (wex j (wsbc e (wsbc d (wa (wf1o (cdm (cv e)) (cdm (cv d)) (cv j)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv j)) (cv d)) (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) (cv e)))) (cdm (cv e)))) (cfv (cv h) ciedg)) (cfv (cv g2) ciedg)))))) $;

--| The graph isomorphism function is a well-defined function. (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2025.)
theorem grimfn:
  $ wfn cgrim (cxp cvv cvv) $;

--| The domain of the graph isomorphism function is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2025.)
theorem grimdmrel:
  $ wrel (cdm cgrim) $;

--| Two graphs are said to be isomorphic iff they are connected by at least one isomorphism, see definition in [Diestel] p. 3 and definition in [Bollobas] p. 3. Isomorphic graphs share all global graph properties like order and size. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 19-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_gric:
  $ wceq cgric (cima (ccnv cgrim) (cdif cvv c1o)) $;

--| An isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between their vertices that preserves adjacency. (Contributed by AV, 19-Apr-2025.)
theorem isgrim {i j: setvar} (D E: class i j) (F G H: class) (V W X Y Z: class i j):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G X) (wcel H Y) (wcel F Z)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wex j (wa (wf1o (cdm E) (cdm D) (cv j)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv j)) D) (cima F (cfv (cv i) E))) (cdm E)))))) $;

--| Properties of an isomorphism of graphs. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2025.)
theorem grimprop {i j: setvar} (D E: class i j) (F G H: class) (V W: class i j):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wex j (wa (wf1o (cdm E) (cdm D) (cv j)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv j)) D) (cima F (cfv (cv i) E))) (cdm E))))) $;

--| An isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between their vertices. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2025.)
theorem grimf1o (F G H V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wf1o V W F) $;

--| The identity relation restricted to the set of vertices of a graph is a graph isomorphism between the graph and a graph with the same vertices and edges. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2025.)
theorem grimidvtxedg (ph: wff) (G H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuhgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G cvtx) (cfv H cvtx)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G ciedg) (cfv H ciedg)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cres cid (cfv G cvtx)) (co G H cgrim)) $;

--| The identity relation restricted to the set of vertices of a graph is a graph isomorphism between the graph and itself. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2025.) (Prove shortened by AV, 5-May-2025.)
theorem grimid (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel (cres cid (cfv G cvtx)) (co G G cgrim)) $;

--| If there is a graph isomorphism between a hypergraph and a class with an edge function, the class is also a hypergraph. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2025.)
theorem grimuhgr (S T F: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel S cuhgr) (wcel F (co S T cgrim)) (wfun (cfv T ciedg))) (wcel T cuhgr) $;

--| The converse of a graph isomorphism is a graph isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 1-May-2025.)
theorem grimcnv (S T F: class):
  $ wi (wcel S cuhgr) (wi (wcel F (co S T cgrim)) (wcel (ccnv F) (co T S cgrim))) $;

--| The composition of graph isomorphisms is a graph isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 3-May-2025.)
theorem grimco (S T U F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co T U cgrim)) (wcel G (co S T cgrim))) (wcel (ccom F G) (co S U cgrim)) $;

--| An isomorphism between graphs preserves edges, i.e. if there is an edge in one graph connecting vertices then there is an edge in the other graph connecting the corresponding vertices. (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem uhgrimedgi (D E F G H K: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr)) (wa (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wcel K E))) (wcel (cima F K) D) $;

--| An isomorphism between graphs preserves edges, i.e. there is an edge in one graph connecting vertices iff there is an edge in the other graph connecting the corresponding vertices. (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem uhgrimedg (D E F G H K: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wss K (cfv G cvtx))) (wb (wcel K E) (wcel (cima F K) D)) $;

--| An isomorphism between hypergraphs is a bijection between their vertices that preserves adjacency for simple edges, i.e. there is a simple edge in one graph connecting one or two vertices iff there is a simple edge in the other graph connecting the vertices which are the images of the vertices. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.) (Revised by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem uhgrimprop {x3 y1: setvar} (D E: class x3 y1) (F G H: class) (V: class x3) (W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrim))) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) D)) V) V)) $;

--| An isomorphism of simple pseudographs is a bijection between their vertices which induces a bijection between their edges. (Contributed by AV, 21-Apr-2025.)
theorem isuspgrim0lem {x3 i: setvar} (D E F G H I J M: class) (N: class x3) (V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq M (cmpt x3 E (cima F (cv x3))) $ >
  $ wceq N (cmpt x3 (cdm I) (cfv (cfv (cfv (cv x3) I) M) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr) (wcel F X)) (wf1o V W F)) (wf1o E D M)) (wa (wf1o (cdm I) (cdm J) N) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) N) J) (cima F (cfv (cv i) I))) (cdm I))) $;

--| An isomorphism of simple pseudographs is a bijection between their vertices which induces a bijection between their edges. (Contributed by AV, 21-Apr-2025.)
theorem isuspgrim0 {e: setvar} (D E F G H V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr) (wcel F X)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wf1o E D (cmpt e E (cima F (cv e)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ isuspgrim . (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.)
theorem isuspgrimlem {x3 y1 e: setvar} (D E F G H V: class) (W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wf1o V W F)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) D)) V) V)) (wf1o E D (cmpt e E (cima F (cv e)))) $;

--| A class is an isomorphism of simple pseudographs iff it is a bijection between their vertices that preserves adjacency, i.e. there is an edge in one graph connecting one or two vertices iff there is an edge in the other graph connecting the vertices which are the images of the vertices. This corresponds to the formal definition in [Bollobas] p. 3 and the definition in [Diestel] p. 3. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.)
theorem isuspgrim {x3 y1: setvar} (D E F G H V: class) (W: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv y1) F)) D)) V) V))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ upgrimwlk and ~ upgrimwlklen . (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (E: class x3) (F: class) (G H I: class x3) (J: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv E chash) (cfv F chash)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ upgrimwlk . (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (E: class x3) (F: class) (G H I: class x3) (J: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cword (cdm J))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ upgrimwlk . (Contributed by AV, 25-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklem3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (E F: class) (G H: class x3) (I J N X: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co cc0 (cfv E chash) cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv X E) J) (cima N (cfv (cfv X F) I))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ upgrimwlk . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklem4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class x3) (E F: class) (G H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfz) (cfv G cvtx) P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (co cc0 (cfv E chash) cfz) (cfv H cvtx) (ccom N P)) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ upgrimwlk . (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklem5 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (i: setvar) (E: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv i) (co cc0 (cfv E chash) cfzo))) (wceq (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv i) F) I)) (cpr (cfv (cv i) (ccom N P)) (cfv (co (cv i) c1 caddc) (ccom N P)))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map walks onto walks. (Contributed by AV, 28-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlk {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H cwlks)) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map walks onto walks of the same length. (Contributed by AV, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem upgrimwlklen {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H cwlks)) (wceq (cfv E chash) (cfv F chash))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ upgrimtrls . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimtrlslem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J: class) (N X: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G ctrls)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (cdm F))) (wcel (cima N (cfv (cfv X F) I)) (cfv H cedg)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ upgrimtrls . (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimtrlslem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (y1: setvar) (P: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G ctrls)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cdm F)) (wcel (cv y1) (cdm F)))) (wi (wceq (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J)) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv y1) F) I)) (ccnv J))) (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map trails onto trails. (Contributed by AV, 29-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimtrls {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G ctrls)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H ctrls)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ upgrimpths . (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimpthslem1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J: class) (N: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cpths)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfun (ccnv (cres (ccom N P) (co c1 (cfv F chash) cfzo)))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ upgrimpths . (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimpthslem2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P: class) (E: class x3) (F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J: class) (N X: class x3):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cpths)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel X (co c1 (cfv F chash) cfzo))) (wa (wn (wceq (cfv X (ccom N P)) (cfv cc0 (ccom N P)))) (wn (wceq (cfv X (ccom N P)) (cfv (cfv F chash) (ccom N P))))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map paths onto paths. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimpths {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cpths)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H cpths)) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map simple paths onto simple paths. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimspths {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G cspths)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H cspths)) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map cycles onto cycles. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2025.)
theorem upgrimcycls {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (P E F G: class) (H: class x3) (I J N: class):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wceq E (cmpt x3 (cdm F) (cfv (cima N (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I)) (ccnv J))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G ccycls)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr E (ccom N P) (cfv H ccycls)) $;

--| The relation "is isomorphic to" for graphs. (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2025.)
theorem brgric (R S: class):
  $ wb (wbr R S cgric) (wne (co R S cgrim) c0) $;

--| Prove that two graphs are isomorphic by an explicit isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2025.)
theorem brgrici (R S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cgrim)) (wbr R S cgric) $;

--| Reverse closure of the "is isomorphic to" relation for graphs. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jun-2025.)
theorem gricrcl (S G: class):
  $ wi (wbr G S cgric) (wa (wcel G cvv) (wcel S cvv)) $;

--| Alternate, explicit definition of the "is isomorphic to" relation for two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 5-May-2025.)
theorem dfgric2 {f2 g2 i: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (I: class f2 g2) (J V W: class f2 g2 i) (X Y: class g2 i):
  $ wceq V (cfv A2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv B2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv B2 ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 Y)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o (cdm I) (cdm J) (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) (cdm I))))))) $;

--| Implications of two graphs being isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 5-May-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 12-Jun-2025.)
theorem gricbri {f2 g2 i: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (I: class f2 g2) (J V W: class f2 g2 i):
  $ wceq V (cfv A2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv B2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv A2 ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv B2 ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 B2 cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o (cdm I) (cdm J) (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) (cdm I)))))) $;

--| The "is isomorphic to" relation for two simple hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 28-Nov-2022.)
theorem gricushgr {e f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (E: class f2) (K: class e f2) (V W: class f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv A2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv B2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv A2 cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv B2 cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cushgr) (wcel B2 cushgr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o E K (cv g2)) (wral e (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) (cv g2))) E)))))) $;

--| The "is isomorphic to" relation for two simple pseudographs. This corresponds to the definition in [Bollobas] p. 3. (Contributed by AV, 1-Dec-2022.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-May-2025.)
theorem gricuspgr {f2 a b: setvar} (A2 B2 E: class) (K V W: class f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv A2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv B2 cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv A2 cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv B2 cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cuspgr) (wcel B2 cuspgr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wral a (wral b (wb (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv b)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv b) (cv f2))) K)) V) V)))) $;

--| The "is isomorphic to" relation for graphs is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 5-May-2025.)
theorem gricrel:
  $ wrel cgric $;

--| Graph isomorphism is reflexive for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 29-Apr-2025.)
theorem gricref (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wbr G G cgric) $;

--| Graph isomorphism is symmetric for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 3-May-2025.)
theorem gricsym (S G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wi (wbr G S cgric) (wbr S G cgric)) $;

--| Graph isomorphism is symmetric in both directions for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-May-2025.)
theorem gricsymb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cuhgr) (wcel B2 cuhgr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cgric) (wbr B2 A2 cgric)) $;

--| Graph isomorphism is transitive. (Contributed by AV, 5-Dec-2022.) (Revised by AV, 3-May-2025.)
theorem grictr (R S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr R S cgric) (wbr S T cgric)) (wbr R T cgric) $;

--| Isomorphism is an equivalence relation on hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 3-May-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Jul-2025.)
theorem gricer:
  $ wer cuhgr (cin cgric (cxp cuhgr cuhgr)) $;

--| Isomorphic graphs have equinumerous sets of vertices. (Contributed by AV, 3-May-2025.)
theorem gricen (B2 C R S: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wbr R S cgric) (wbr B2 C cen) $;

--| A graph represented as an extensible structure with vertices as base set and indexed edges is isomorphic to a hypergraph represented as ordered pair with the same vertices and edges. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.) (Revised by AV, 4-May-2025.)
theorem opstrgric (E G H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq H (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) V) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) E)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel V X) (wcel E Y)) (wbr G H cgric) $;

--| A simple hypergraph (with arbitrarily indexed edges) is isomorphic to a graph with the same vertices and the same edges, indexed by the edges themselves. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2022.)
theorem ushggricedg (E G H V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cop V (cres cid E)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cushgr) (wbr G H cgric) $;

--| Two simple pseudographs are not isomorphic if one has a cycle and the other has no cycle of the same length. (Contributed by AV, 6-Nov-2025.)
theorem cycldlenngric {f2 p: setvar} (G H N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wi (wa (wex p (wex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv p) (cfv G ccycls)) (wceq (cfv (cv f2) chash) N)))) (wn (wex p (wex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv p) (cfv H ccycls)) (wceq (cfv (cv f2) chash) N)))))) (wn (wbr G H cgric))) $;

--| Isomorphic subgraphs induced by subsets of vertices of two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2025.)
theorem isubgrgrim {x3 f2 g2 i: setvar} (T U: class x3) (G H: class) (I J: class f2 g2 i) (K L: class x3 f2 g2) (M N V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G U) (wcel H T)) (wa (wss N V) (wss M W))) (wb (wbr (co G N cisubgr) (co H M cisubgr) cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ uhgrimisgrgric . (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2025.)
theorem uhgrimisgrgriclem {i k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class i) (F G H I J: class) (N V W: class i):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wf1o V W F) (wf A2 (cpw V) G)) (wa (wss N V) (wf1o A2 B2 I)) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv i) I) H) (cima F (cfv (cv i) G))) A2)) (wb (wa (wcel J B2) (wss (cfv J H) (cima F N))) (wrex k (wa (wss (cfv (cv k) G) N) (wceq (cfv (cv k) I) J)) A2)) $;

--| For isomorphic hypergraphs, the induced subgraph of a subset of vertices of one graph is isomorphic to the subgraph induced by the image of the subset. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2025.)
theorem uhgrimisgrgric (F G H N V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wss N V)) (wbr (co G N cisubgr) (co H (cima F N) cisubgr) cgric) $;

--| Isomorphic subgraphs induced by closed neighborhoods of vertices of two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2025.)
theorem clnbgrisubgrgrim {x3 f2 g2 i: setvar} (T U: class x3) (G H: class) (I J: class f2 g2 i) (K L: class x3 f2 g2) (M N: class) (X Y: class x3 f2 g2 i):
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H Y cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G U) (wcel H T)) (wb (wbr (co G N cisubgr) (co H M cisubgr) cgric) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ clnbgrgrim : For two isomorphic hypergraphs, if there is an edge connecting the image of a vertex of the first graph with a vertex of the second graph, the vertex of the second graph is the image of a neighbor of the vertex of the first graph. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2025.)
theorem clnbgrgrimlem {n: setvar} (E F G H K V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) (wa (wcel X V) (wcel Y W))) (wi (wa (wcel K E) (wss (cpr (cfv X F) Y) K)) (wrex n (wceq (cfv (cv n) F) Y) (co G X cclnbgr))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms between hypergraphs map closed neighborhoods onto closed neighborhoods. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2025.)
theorem clnbgrgrim (F G H V X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrim))) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co H (cfv X F) cclnbgr) (cima F (co G X cclnbgr))) $;

--| For two isomorphic graphs, a set of vertices is an edge in one graph iff its image by a graph isomorphism is an edge of the other graph. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2025.)
theorem grimedg (E F G H I K V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrim))) (wb (wcel K I) (wa (wcel (cima F K) E) (wss K V))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms map edges onto the corresponding edges. (Contributed by AV, 30-Dec-2025.)
theorem grimedgi (E F G H I K V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrim))) (wi (wcel K I) (wcel (cima F K) E)) $;

--| Extend class notation with triangles (in a graph).
term cgrtri: class;

--| Definition of a triangles in a graph. A triangle in a graph is a set of three (different) vertices completely connected with each other. Such vertices induce a closed walk of length 3, see ~ grtriclwlk3 , and the vertices of a cycle of size 3 are a triangle in a graph, see ~ cycl3grtri . (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2025.)
axiom df_grtri {v t e f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgrtri (cmpt g2 cvv (csb v (cfv (cv g2) cvtx) (csb e (cfv (cv g2) cedg) (crab t (wex f2 (wa (wf1o (co cc0 c3 cfzo) (cv t) (cv f2)) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv f2))) (cv e)) (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) (cv e)) (wcel (cpr (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) (cv e))))) (cpw (cv v)))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ grtriprop . (Contributed by AV, 23-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtriproplem (x3 y1 z f2: setvar) (E: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf1o (co cc0 c3 cfzo) (ctp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)) (cv f2)) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv f2))) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) E))) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv z)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv y1) (cv z)) E)) $;

--| The triangles in a graph. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtri {t f2: setvar} (E G V: class) (W: class t f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G W) (wceq (cfv G cgrtri) (crab t (wex f2 (wa (wf1o (co cc0 c3 cfzo) (cv t) (cv f2)) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c1 (cv f2))) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv c1 (cv f2)) (cfv c2 (cv f2))) E)))) (cpw V))) $;

--| The properties of a triangle. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtriprop {x3 y1 z: setvar} (T E: class) (G: class x3 y1 z) (V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (w3a (wceq T (ctp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (cfv T chash) c3) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv z)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv y1) (cv z)) E))) V) V) V) $;

--| Any bijection onto a triangle preserves the edges of the triangle. (Contributed by AV, 25-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtrif1o (T E F G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) (wf1o (co cc0 c3 cfzo) T F)) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 F) (cfv c1 F)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 F) (cfv c2 F)) E) (wcel (cpr (cfv c1 F) (cfv c2 F)) E)) $;

--| A triangle in a graph. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jul-2025.)
theorem isgrtri {x3 y1 z: setvar} (T E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wrex z (w3a (wceq T (ctp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z))) (wceq (cfv T chash) c3) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv z)) E) (wcel (cpr (cv y1) (cv z)) E))) V) V) V) $;

--| A triangle is a subset of the vertices (of a graph). (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtrissvtx (T G V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) (wss T V) $;

--| A triangle induces a closed walk of length 3 . (Contributed by AV, 26-Jul-2025.)
theorem grtriclwlk3 (ph: wff) (P T G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o (co cc0 c3 cfzo) T P) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel P (co c3 G cclwwlkn)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cycl3grtri . (Contributed by AV, 5-Oct-2025.)
theorem cycl3grtrilem (P F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cupgr) (wbr F P (cfv G cpths))) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 P) (cfv (cfv F chash) P)) (wceq (cfv F chash) c3))) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv c1 P)) (cfv G cedg)) (wcel (cpr (cfv cc0 P) (cfv c2 P)) (cfv G cedg)) (wcel (cpr (cfv c1 P) (cfv c2 P)) (cfv G cedg))) $;

--| The vertices of a cycle of size 3 are a triangle in a graph. (Contributed by AV, 5-Oct-2025.)
theorem cycl3grtri (ph: wff) (P F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cupgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F P (cfv G ccycls)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv F chash) c3) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (crn P) (cfv G cgrtri)) $;

--| Conditions for mapping triangles onto triangles. Lemma for ~ grimgrtri and ~ grlimgrtri . (Contributed by AV, 23-Aug-2025.)
theorem grtrimap (T F V W: class) (a b c: setvar):
  $ wi (wf1 V W F) (wi (wa (w3a (wcel (cv a) V) (wcel (cv b) V) (wcel (cv c) V)) (wa (wceq T (ctp (cv a) (cv b) (cv c))) (wceq (cfv T chash) c3))) (w3a (w3a (wcel (cfv (cv a) F) W) (wcel (cfv (cv b) F) W) (wcel (cfv (cv c) F) W)) (wceq (cima F T) (ctp (cfv (cv a) F) (cfv (cv b) F) (cfv (cv c) F))) (wceq (cfv (cima F T) chash) c3))) $;

--| Graph isomorphisms map triangles onto triangles. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jul-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem grimgrtri (ph: wff) (T F G H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuhgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuhgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cgrim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cima F T) (cfv H cgrtri)) $;

--| Conditions for a simple graph to contain a triangle. (Contributed by AV, 7-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrgrtrirex {t a b c: setvar} (E G: class) (N: class a) (V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv a) cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G cusgr) (wb (wex t (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cgrtri))) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wa (wne (cv b) (cv c)) (wcel (cpr (cv b) (cv c)) E)) N) N) V)) $;

--| Extend class notation with star graphs.
term cstgr: class;

--| Definition of star graphs according to the first definition in Wikipedia, so that ` ( StarGr `` N ) ` has size ` N ` , and order ` N + 1 ` : ` ( StarGr `` 0 ) ` will be a single vertex (graph without edges), see ~ stgr0 , ` ( StarGr `` 1 ) ` will be a single edge (graph with two vertices connected by an edge), see ~ stgr1 , and ` ( StarGr `` 3 ) ` will be a 3-star or "claw" (a star with 3 edges). (Contributed by AV, 10-Sep-2025.)
axiom df_stgr {x3 e n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cstgr (cmpt n cn0 (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (co cc0 (cv n) cfz)) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (wceq (cv e) (cpr cc0 (cv x3))) (co c1 (cv n) cfz)) (cpw (co cc0 (cv n) cfz))))))) $;

--| The star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 10-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrfv {x3 e: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv N cstgr) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (co cc0 N cfz)) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (wceq (cv e) (cpr cc0 (cv x3))) (co c1 N cfz)) (cpw (co cc0 N cfz))))))) $;

--| The vertices of the star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrvtx (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cvtx) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| The indexed edges of the star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgriedg {x3 e: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (cfv N cstgr) ciedg) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (wceq (cv e) (cpr cc0 (cv x3))) (co c1 N cfz)) (cpw (co cc0 N cfz))))) $;

--| The edges of the star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgredg {x3 e: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg) (crab e (wrex x3 (wceq (cv e) (cpr cc0 (cv x3))) (co c1 N cfz)) (cpw (co cc0 N cfz)))) $;

--| An edge of the star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgredgel {x3: setvar} (E N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wcel E (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg)) (wa (wss E (co cc0 N cfz)) (wrex x3 (wceq E (cpr cc0 (cv x3))) (co c1 N cfz)))) $;

--| The edges of the star graph S_N as indexed union. (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgredgiun {x3: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg) (ciun x3 (co c1 N cfz) (csn (cpr cc0 (cv x3))))) $;

--| The star graph S_N is a simple graph. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrusgra (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N cstgr) cusgr) $;

--| The star graph S_0 consists of a single vertex without edges. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgr0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cstgr) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn cc0)) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) c0)) $;

--| The star graph S_1 consists of a single simple edge. (Contributed by AV, 11-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgr1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cstgr) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cpr cc0 c1)) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) (cres cid (csn (cpr cc0 c1))))) $;

--| The center ("internal node") of a star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrvtx0 (G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel cc0 V) $;

--| The order of a star graph S_N. (Contributed by AV, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrorder (G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (cfv V chash) (co N c1 caddc)) $;

--| All vertices of a star graph S_N except the center are in the (open) neighborhood of the center. (Contributed by AV, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrnbgr0 (G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co G cc0 cnbgr) (cdif V (csn cc0))) $;

--| All vertices of a star graph S_N are in the closed neighborhood of the center. (Contributed by AV, 12-Sep-2025.)
theorem stgrclnbgr0 (G N V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co G cc0 cclnbgr) V) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 16-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem1 (C R U F G H V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq F (cun H (csn (cop X Y))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wf1o U R H) (wcel X V) (wa (wcel Y W) (wnel Y R))) (wf1o C (cun R (csn Y)) F) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 16-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem2 {f2: setvar} (C S: class f2) (U: class) (G N V: class f2) (W: class) (X: class f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv U chash) N)) (wex f2 (wf1o U (cdif W (csn cc0)) (cv f2))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 17-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem3 {g2: setvar} (C: class) (S U G N V: class g2) (W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv U chash) N)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o C W (cv g2)) (wceq (cfv X (cv g2)) cc0))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem4 {z: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (S U E: class z) (F: class) (G: class z) (N: class) (V: class z) (W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wceq A2 X) (wa (wf1o C W F) (wceq (cfv X F) cc0)) (w3a (wne A2 B2) (wcel A2 C) (wcel B2 C))) (wrex z (wceq (cima F (cpr A2 B2)) (cpr cc0 (cv z))) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem5 {i: setvar} (C: class) (S U E: class i) (F: class) (G H: class i) (I: class) (N V: class i) (W: class) (X: class i) (Y: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (co G C cisubgr) cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i I (cima F (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf C W F) (wcel Y I)) (wceq (cfv Y H) (cima F Y)) $;

--| Lemma 6 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 24-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem6 {x3 y1 i: setvar} (C: class x3 y1) (S: class x3 y1 i) (U E: class) (F G: class x3 y1) (H: class x3 y1 i) (I N V W X: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (co G C cisubgr) cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i I (cima F (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V)) (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) N) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U))) (wa (wf1o C W F) (wceq (cfv X F) cc0))) (wf I (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg) H) $;

--| Lemma 7 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem7 {i: setvar} (C: class) (S: class i) (U E F G: class) (H: class i) (I: class) (J: class i) (N V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (co G C cisubgr) cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i I (cima F (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V)) (wa (wf1o C W F) (wceq (cfv X F) cc0)) (wcel J (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg))) (wcel (cima (ccnv F) J) I) $;

--| Lemma 8 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem8 {x3 y1 i: setvar} (C: class x3) (S: class x3 y1 i) (U E: class) (F G: class x3) (H: class x3 y1 i) (I N V W X: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (co G C cisubgr) cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i I (cima F (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V)) (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) N) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U))) (wa (wf1o C W F) (wceq (cfv X F) cc0))) (wf1o I (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg) H) $;

--| Lemma 9 for ~ isubgr3stgr . (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgrlem9 {x3 y1 e i: setvar} (C: class x3) (S: class x3 y1 e i) (U E: class) (F G: class x3) (H: class x3 y1 e i) (I: class x3 e) (N V W X: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv (co G C cisubgr) cedg) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt i I (cima F (cv i))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V)) (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) N) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U))) (wa (wf1o C W F) (wceq (cfv X F) cc0))) (wa (wf1o I (cfv (cfv N cstgr) cedg) H) (wral e (wceq (cima F (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) H)) I)) $;

--| If a vertex of a simple graph has exactly ` N ` (different) neighbors, and none of these neighbors are connected by an edge, then the (closed) neighborhood of this vertex induces a subgraph which is isomorphic to an ` N `-star. (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem isubgr3stgr {x3 y1: setvar} (C: class x3) (S: class x3 y1) (U E: class) (G: class x3) (N: class) (V W X: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq C (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv N cstgr) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cusgr) (wcel X V)) (wi (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) N) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U)) (wbr (co G C cisubgr) (cfv N cstgr) cgric)) $;

--| The class of graph local isomorphism sets.
term cgrlim: class;

--| The class of the graph local isomorphism relation.
term cgrlic: class;

--| A local isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between the sets of vertices of two graphs that preserves local adjacency, i.e. the subgraph induced by the closed neighborhood of a vertex of the first graph and the subgraph induced by the closed neighborhood of the associated vertex of the second graph are isomorphic. See the following chat in mathoverflow: ~ https://mathoverflow.net/questions/491133/locally-isomorphic-graphs . (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_grlim {v f2 g2 h: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgrlim (cmpo g2 h cvv cvv (cab f2 (wa (wf1o (cfv (cv g2) cvtx) (cfv (cv h) cvtx) (cv f2)) (wral v (wbr (co (cv g2) (co (cv g2) (cv v) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (co (cv h) (co (cv h) (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) cclnbgr) cisubgr) cgric) (cfv (cv g2) cvtx))))) $;

--| The graph local isomorphism function is a well-defined function. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2025.)
theorem grlimfn:
  $ wfn cgrlim (cxp cvv cvv) $;

--| The domain of the graph local isomorphism function is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2025.)
theorem grlimdmrel:
  $ wrel (cdm cgrlim) $;

--| Two graphs are said to be locally isomorphic iff they are connected by at least one local isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2025.)
axiom df_grlic:
  $ wceq cgrlic (cima (ccnv cgrlim) (cdif cvv c1o)) $;

--| A local isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between their vertices that preserves neighborhoods. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2025.)
theorem isgrlim {v: setvar} (F G H V: class) (W X Y Z: class v):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G X) (wcel H Y) (wcel F Z)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wbr (co G (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (co H (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) cisubgr) cgric) V))) $;

--| A local isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between their vertices that preserves neighborhoods. Definitions expanded. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2025.)
theorem isgrlim2 {x3 v f2 g2 i: setvar} (F: class x3 i) (G H: class) (I J: class v f2 g2 i) (K L: class x3 v f2 g2) (M N: class v) (V: class x3 f2 g2 i) (W: class x3 v f2 g2 i) (X Y: class x3) (Z: class x3 i):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G X) (wcel H Y) (wcel F Z)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K))))) V))) $;

--| Properties of a local isomorphism of graphs. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2025.)
theorem grlimprop {v: setvar} (F G H V: class) (W: class v):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wbr (co G (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (co H (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) cisubgr) cgric) V)) $;

--| A local isomorphism of graphs is a bijection between their vertices. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2025.)
theorem grlimf1o (F G H V W: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wf1o V W F) $;

--| Properties of a local isomorphism of graphs. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2025.)
theorem grlimprop2 {x3 v f2 g2 i: setvar} (F: class x3 i) (G H: class) (I J: class v f2 g2 i) (K L: class x3 v f2 g2) (M N: class v) (V: class x3 f2 g2 i) (W: class x3 v f2 g2 i):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K))))) V)) $;

--| An isomorphism of hypergraphs is a local isomorphism between the two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2025.)
theorem uhgrimgrlim (F G H: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrim))) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ uspgrlim . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2025.)
theorem uspgrlimlem1 {x3: setvar} (H J: class) (L: class x3) (M: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq M (co H X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel H cuspgr) (wceq L (cima (cfv H ciedg) (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv H ciedg)) M) (cdm (cfv H ciedg))))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ uspgrlim . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2025.)
theorem uspgrlimlem2 {x3: setvar} (H J L M: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq M (co H X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (wcel H cuspgr) (wceq (cima (ccnv (cfv H ciedg)) L) (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv H ciedg)) M) (cdm (cfv H ciedg)))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ uspgrlim . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2025.)
theorem uspgrlimlem3 {x3 v i: setvar} (R: class x3 v i) (e f2 h: setvar) (F: class x3 v i) (G H: class v) (I J: class v i) (K L: class x3 v i) (M: class v i) (N: class v) (V W: class x3 v i):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuspgr) (wf1o (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G ciedg)) N) (cdm (cfv G ciedg))) R (cv h)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) (cfv G ciedg))) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv h)) (cfv H ciedg))) (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) (cfv G ciedg)) N) (cdm (cfv G ciedg))))) (wi (wcel (cv e) K) (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) (ccom (ccom (cfv H ciedg) (cv h)) (ccnv (cfv G ciedg)))))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ uspgrlim . (Contributed by AV, 16-Aug-2025.)
theorem uspgrlimlem4 {x3 v e: setvar} (f2 g2 i: setvar) (F: class x3 v e) (G: class v) (H I J: class v e) (K: class v) (L M N: class v e) (V W: class x3 v e):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral e (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) (cv g2))) K))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv i) (cdm (cfv G ciedg))) (wss (cfv (cv i) (cfv G ciedg)) N)) (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cfv (cv i) (cfv G ciedg))) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (ccom (ccom (ccnv (cfv H ciedg)) (cv g2)) (cfv G ciedg))) (cfv H ciedg)))) $;

--| A local isomorphism of simple pseudographs is a bijection between their vertices that preserves neighborhoods, expressed by properties of their edges (not edge functions as in ~ isgrlim2 ). (Contributed by AV, 15-Aug-2025.)
theorem uspgrlim {x3 v e f2 g2: setvar} (F: class x3 e g2) (G H: class) (I J: class v e f2 g2) (K: class v f2) (L: class v e f2) (M N: class v) (V: class x3 e f2 g2) (W: class x3 v e f2 g2) (Z: class x3 e g2):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr) (wcel F Z)) (wb (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral e (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) (cv g2))) K))))) V))) $;

--| Properties of a local isomorphism of simple pseudographs. (Contributed by AV, 17-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrlimprop {x3 v e f2 g2: setvar} (F: class x3 e g2) (G H: class) (I J: class v e f2 g2) (K: class v f2) (L: class v e f2) (M N: class v) (V: class x3 e f2 g2) (W: class x3 v e f2 g2):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim))) (wa (wf1o V W F) (wral v (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral e (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv e)) (cfv (cv e) (cv g2))) K))))) V)) $;

--| An edge ` E ` containing a vertex ` A ` is an edge in the closed neighborhood of this vertex ` A ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2025.)
theorem clnbgrvtxedg {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (E: class) (G: class x3) (I: class) (K: class x3) (N: class):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel E I) (wcel A2 E)) (wcel E K) $;

--| For two locally isomorphic graphs ` G ` and ` H ` and a vertex ` A ` of ` G ` there are two bijections ` f ` and ` g ` mapping the closed neighborhood ` N ` of ` A ` onto the closed neighborhood ` M ` of ` ( F `` A ) ` and the edges between the vertices in ` N ` onto the edges between the vertices in ` M ` , so that the mapped vertices of an edge ` E ` containing the vertex ` A ` is an edge between the vertices in ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimedgclnbgr {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 E F G H I: class) (J: class f2) (K L M: class x3 f2 g2) (N: class f2 g2):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv A2 F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (wa (wcel E I) (wcel A2 E))) (wex f2 (wex g2 (w3a (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wceq (cima (cv f2) E) (cfv E (cv g2)))))) $;

--| For two locally isomorphic graphs ` G ` and ` H ` and a vertex ` A ` of ` G ` there are two bijections ` f ` and ` g ` mapping the closed neighborhood ` N ` of ` A ` onto the closed neighborhood ` M ` of ` ( F `` A ) ` and the edges between the vertices in ` N ` onto the edges between the vertices in ` M ` , so that the mapped vertices of an edge ` { A , B } ` containing the vertex ` A ` is an edge between the vertices in ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimprclnbgr {x3 f2 g2: setvar} (A2 B2 F G H I: class) (J: class f2) (K L M: class x3 f2 g2) (N: class f2 g2) (V W: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv A2 F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) I))) (wex f2 (wex g2 (w3a (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wceq (cpr (cfv A2 (cv f2)) (cfv B2 (cv f2))) (cfv (cpr A2 B2) (cv g2)))))) $;

--| For two locally isomorphic graphs ` G ` and ` H ` and a vertex ` A ` of ` G ` there is a bijection ` f ` mapping the closed neighborhood ` N ` of ` A ` onto the closed neighborhood ` M ` of ` ( F `` A ) ` , so that the mapped vertices of an edge ` { A , B } ` containing the vertex ` A ` is an edge between the vertices in ` M ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimprclnbgredg {x3 f2: setvar} (A2 B2 F G H I: class) (J: class f2) (K L M: class x3 f2) (N: class f2) (V W: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv A2 F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) I))) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wcel (cpr (cfv A2 (cv f2)) (cfv B2 (cv f2))) L))) $;

--| For two locally isomorphic graphs ` G ` and ` H ` and a vertex ` A ` of ` G ` there is a bijection ` f ` mapping the closed neighborhood ` N ` of ` A ` onto the closed neighborhood ` M ` of ` ( F `` A ) ` , so that the mapped vertices of an edge ` { A , B } ` containing the vertex ` A ` is an edge in ` H ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimpredg {x3 f2: setvar} (A2 B2 F G H I: class) (J: class f2) (K L: class x3 f2) (M N: class f2) (V W: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv A2 F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) I))) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wcel (cpr (cfv A2 (cv f2)) (cfv B2 (cv f2))) J))) $;

--| For two locally isomorphic graphs ` G ` and ` H ` and a vertex ` A ` of ` G ` there is a bijection ` f ` mapping the closed neighborhood ` N ` of ` A ` onto the closed neighborhood ` M ` of ` ( F `` A ) ` , so that the mapped vertices of an edge ` { A , B } ` containing the vertex ` A ` is an edge between the vertices in ` M ` containing the vertex ` ( F `` A ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimprclnbgrvtx {x3 f2: setvar} (A2 B2 F G H I: class) (J: class f2) (K L: class x3 f2) (M N: class f2) (V W: class x3):
  $ wceq N (co G A2 cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv A2 F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel G cuspgr) (wcel H cuspgr)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) I))) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wo (wcel (cpr (cfv A2 F) (cfv B2 (cv f2))) L) (wcel (cpr (cfv A2 F) (cfv A2 (cv f2))) L)))) $;

--| Local isomorphisms between simple pseudographs map an edge onto an edge with an endpoint being the image of one of the endpoints of the first edge under the local isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimgredgex {v: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 E F G H: class) (I: class v) (V: class) (X Y: class v):
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cpr A2 B2) I) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex v (wcel (cpr (cfv A2 F) (cv v)) E) V) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ grlimgrtri . (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimgrtrilem1 {x3 a b c: setvar} (G: class x3 a b c) (I: class a b c) (K: class x3 a b c) (N: class a b c) (V: class x3 a b c):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv a) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv b)) I) (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv c)) I) (wcel (cpr (cv b) (cv c)) I))) (w3a (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv b)) K) (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv c)) K) (wcel (cpr (cv b) (cv c)) K)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ grlimgrtri . (Contributed by AV, 23-Aug-2025.)
theorem grlimgrtrilem2 {x3 i a b c: setvar} (f2 g2: setvar) (F G H: class x3 i a b c) (I J: class i a b c) (K: class x3 a b c) (L M: class x3 i a b c) (N: class i a b c) (V: class x3 i a b c):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv a) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv a) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wf1o K L (cv g2))) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv i)) (cfv (cv i) (cv g2))) K) (wcel (cpr (cv b) (cv c)) K)) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv b) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv c) (cv f2))) J) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ grlimgrtri . (Contributed by AV, 23-Aug-2025.)
theorem grlimgrtrilem2_b {x3 i a c: setvar} (f2 g2: setvar) (F G H: class x3 i a c) (I J: class i a c) (K: class x3 a c) (L M: class x3 i a c) (N: class i a c) (V: class x3 i a c):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv a) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) N) I) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv a) F) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H cedg) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cv x3) M) J) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wf1o N M (cv f2)) (wf1o K L (cv g2))) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv f2) (cv i)) (cfv (cv i) (cv g2))) K) (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv c)) K)) (wcel (cpr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv c) (cv f2))) J) $;

--| If one of two locally isomorphic graphs has a triangle, so does the other. The triangle in the other graph is not necessarily the image ` ( F " T ) ` of the triangle ` T ` in the first graph. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem grlimgrtri {t: setvar} (ph: wff t) (T F G: class t) (H: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel G cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H cuspgr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T (cfv G cgrtri)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex t (wcel (cv t) (cfv H cgrtri))) $;

--| The relation "is locally isomorphic to" for graphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem brgrlic (R S: class):
  $ wb (wbr R S cgrlic) (wne (co R S cgrlim) c0) $;

--| Prove that two graphs are locally isomorphic by an explicit local isomorphism. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem brgrilci (R S F: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co R S cgrlim)) (wbr R S cgrlic) $;

--| The "is locally isomorphic to" relation for graphs is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicrel:
  $ wrel cgrlic $;

--| Reverse closure of the "is locally isomorphic to" relation for graphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicrcl (S G: class):
  $ wi (wbr G S cgrlic) (wa (wcel G cvv) (wcel S cvv)) $;

--| Alternate, explicit definition of the "is locally isomorphic to" relation for two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem dfgrlic2 {v f2: setvar} (G H: class) (V: class f2) (W: class v f2) (X Y: class v):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G X) (wcel H Y)) (wb (wbr G H cgrlic) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wral v (wbr (co G (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (co H (co H (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) cclnbgr) cisubgr) cgric) V)))) $;

--| Implications of two graphs being locally isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grilcbri {v f2: setvar} (G H: class) (V: class f2) (W: class v f2):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wbr G H cgrlic) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wral v (wbr (co G (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (co H (co H (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) cclnbgr) cisubgr) cgric) V))) $;

--| Alternate, explicit definition of the "is locally isomorphic to" relation for two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem dfgrlic3 {x3 v f2 g2 i j: setvar} (G H: class) (I J: class v f2 g2 j) (K L: class x3 v f2 g2 j) (M N: class v f2) (V: class x3 f2 g2 i j) (W: class x3 v f2 g2 i j) (X Y: class x3):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G (cv v) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv (cv v) (cv f2)) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel G X) (wcel H Y)) (wb (wbr G H cgrlic) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wral v (wex j (wa (wf1o N M (cv j)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv j) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K))))) V)))) $;

--| Implications of two graphs being locally isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grilcbri2 {x3 f2 g2 i j: setvar} (G H: class) (I J: class f2 g2 j) (K: class x3 f2 g2 j) (L M N V W: class x3 f2 g2 i j) (X: class i):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cfv H ciedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq M (co H (cfv X (cv f2)) cclnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq K (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) I) N) (cdm I)) $ >
  $ wceq L (crab x3 (wss (cfv (cv x3) J) M) (cdm J)) $ >
  $ wi (wbr G H cgrlic) (wex f2 (wa (wf1o V W (cv f2)) (wi (wcel X V) (wex j (wa (wf1o N M (cv j)) (wex g2 (wa (wf1o K L (cv g2)) (wral i (wceq (cima (cv j) (cfv (cv i) I)) (cfv (cfv (cv i) (cv g2)) J)) K)))))))) $;

--| Graph local isomorphism is reflexive for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicref (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wbr G G cgrlic) $;

--| Graph local isomorphism is symmetric for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicsym (S G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cuhgr) (wi (wbr G S cgrlic) (wbr S G cgrlic)) $;

--| Graph local isomorphism is symmetric in both directions for hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicsymb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cuhgr) (wcel B2 cuhgr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cgrlic) (wbr B2 A2 cgrlic)) $;

--| Graph local isomorphism is transitive. (Contributed by AV, 10-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlictr (R S T: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr R S cgrlic) (wbr S T cgrlic)) (wbr R T cgrlic) $;

--| Local isomorphism is an equivalence relation on hypergraphs. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicer:
  $ wer cuhgr (cin cgrlic (cxp cuhgr cuhgr)) $;

--| Locally isomorphic graphs have equinumerous sets of vertices. (Contributed by AV, 11-Jun-2025.)
theorem grlicen (B2 C R S: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wbr R S cgrlic) (wbr B2 C cen) $;

--| Isomorphic hypergraphs are locally isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 12-Jun-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Jul-2025.)
theorem gricgrlic (G H: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cuhgr) (wcel H cuhgr)) (wi (wbr G H cgric) (wbr G H cgrlic)) $;

--| If all (closed) neighborhoods of the vertices in two simple graphs with the same order induce a subgraph which is isomorphic to an ` N `-star, then any bijection between the vertices is a local isomorphism between the two graphs. (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2025.)
theorem clnbgr3stgrgrlim {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class) (G: class y1) (H N: class) (V: class y1) (W: class):
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel G cusgr) (wcel H cusgr) (wf1o V W F)) (wral x3 (wbr (co G (co G (cv x3) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (cfv N cstgr) cgric) V) (wral y1 (wbr (co H (co H (cv y1) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (cfv N cstgr) cgric) W)) (wcel F (co G H cgrlim)) $;

--| If all (closed) neighborhoods of the vertices in two simple graphs with the same order induce a subgraph which is isomorphic to an ` N `-star, then the two graphs are locally isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem clnbgr3stgrgrlic {x3 y1: setvar} (G H N: class) (V: class y1) (W: class):
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv H cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel G cusgr) (wcel H cusgr) (wbr V W cen)) (wral x3 (wbr (co G (co G (cv x3) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (cfv N cstgr) cgric) V) (wral y1 (wbr (co H (co H (cv y1) cclnbgr) cisubgr) (cfv N cstgr) cgric) W)) (wbr G H cgrlic) $;

--| Lemma for ~ usgrexmpl1 . (Contributed by AV, 2-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl1lem {e: setvar} (E: class e) (V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wf1 (cdm E) (crab e (wceq (cfv (cv e) chash) c2) (cpw V)) E $;

--| ` G ` is a simple graph of six vertices ` 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ` , with edges ` { 0 , 1 } , { 1 , 2 } , { 0 , 2 } , { 0 , 3 } , { 3 , 4 } , ` ` { 3 , 5 } , { 4 , 5 } ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl1 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wcel G cusgr $;

--| The vertices ` 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ` of the graph ` G = <. V , E >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl1vtx (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G cvtx) (cun (ctp cc0 c1 c2) (ctp c3 c4 c5)) $;

--| The edges ` { 0 , 1 } , { 1 , 2 } , { 0 , 2 } , { 0 , 3 } , ` ` { 3 , 4 } , { 3 , 5 } , { 4 , 5 } ` of the graph ` G = <. V , E >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl1edg (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G cedg) (cun (csn (cpr cc0 c3)) (cun (ctp (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2)) (ctp (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)))) $;

--| ` G ` contains a triangle ` 0 , 1 , 2 ` , with corresponding edges ` { 0 , 1 } , { 1 , 2 } , { 0 , 2 } ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl1tri (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wcel (ctp cc0 c1 c2) (cfv G cgrtri) $;

--| Lemma for ~ usgrexmpl2 . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2lem {e: setvar} (E: class e) (V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wf1 (cdm E) (crab e (wceq (cfv (cv e) chash) c2) (cpw V)) E $;

--| ` G ` is a simple graph of six vertices ` 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ` , with edges ` { 0 , 1 } , { 1 , 2 } , { 2 , 3 } , { 0 , 3 } , { 3 , 4 } , ` ` { 4 , 5 } , { 0 , 5 } ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wcel G cusgr $;

--| The vertices ` 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ` of the graph ` G = <. V , E >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2vtx (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G cvtx) (cun (ctp cc0 c1 c2) (ctp c3 c4 c5)) $;

--| The edges ` { 0 , 1 } , { 1 , 2 } , { 2 , 3 } , { 0 , 3 } , ` ` { 3 , 4 } , { 4 , 5 } , { 0 , 5 } ` of the graph ` G = <. V , E >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 3-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2edg (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv G cedg) (cun (csn (cpr cc0 c3)) (cun (ctp (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3)) (ctp (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c5)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ usgrexmpl2nb0 etc. (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nblem {n: setvar} (E: class n) (G K: class) (V: class n):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (cun (ctp cc0 c1 c2) (ctp c3 c4 c5))) (wceq (co G K cnbgr) (crab n (wcel (cpr K (cv n)) (cun (csn (cpr cc0 c3)) (cun (ctp (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3)) (ctp (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c5))))) (cun (ctp cc0 c1 c2) (ctp c3 c4 c5)))) $;

--| The neighborhood of the first vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb0 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G cc0 cnbgr) (ctp c1 c3 c5) $;

--| The neighborhood of the second vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb1 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c1 cnbgr) (cpr cc0 c2) $;

--| The neighborhood of the third vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb2 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c2 cnbgr) (cpr c1 c3) $;

--| The neighborhood of the forth vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb3 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c3 cnbgr) (ctp cc0 c2 c4) $;

--| The neighborhood of the fifth vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 9-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb4 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c4 cnbgr) (cpr c3 c5) $;

--| The neighborhood of the sixth vertex of graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2nb5 (E G V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq (co G c5 cnbgr) (cpr cc0 c4) $;

--| ` G ` is triangle-free. (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl2trifr {t: setvar} (E: class t) (G: class) (V: class t):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wn (wex t (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cgrtri))) $;

--| The graphs ` H ` and ` G ` are not isomorphic ( ` H ` contains a triangle, see ~ usgrexmpl1tri , whereas ` G ` does not, see ~ usgrexmpl2trifr . (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl12ngric (E G H K V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq K (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cop V K) $ >
  $ wn (wbr G H cgric) $;

--| The graphs ` H ` and ` G ` are not locally isomorphic ( ` H ` contains a triangle, see ~ usgrexmpl1tri , whereas ` G ` does not, see ~ usgrexmpl2trifr . (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2025.)
theorem usgrexmpl12ngrlic (E G H K V: class):
  $ wceq V (co cc0 c5 cfz) $ >
  $ wceq E (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr c2 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c4 c5) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr cc0 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cop V E) $ >
  $ wceq K (cs7 (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr cc0 c2) (cpr c1 c2) (cpr cc0 c3) (cpr c3 c4) (cpr c3 c5) (cpr c4 c5)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cop V K) $ >
  $ wn (wbr G H cgrlic) $;

--| Extend class notation with generalized Petersen graphs.
term cgpg: class;

--| Definition of generalized Petersen graphs according to Wikipedia "Generalized Petersen graph", 26-Aug-2025, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Petersen_graph : "In Watkins' notation, ` G ( n , k ) ` is a graph with vertex set { u_0, u_1, ... , u_n-1, v_0, v_1, ... , v_n-1 } and edge set { u_i u_i+1 , u_i v_i , v_i v_i+k | ` 0 <_ i <_ ( n - 1 ) ` } where subscripts are to be read modulo n and where ` k < ( n / 2 ) ` . Some authors use the notation GPG(n,k)." Instead of ` n e. NN ` , we could restrict the first argument to ` n e. ( ZZ>= `` 3 ) ` (i.e., ` 3 <_ n ` ), because for ` n <_ 2 ` , the definition is not meaningful (since then ` ( |^ `` ( n / 2 ) ) <_ 1 ` and therefore ` ( 1 ..^ ( |^ `` ( n / 2 ) ) ) = (/) ` , so that there would be no fitting second argument). (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
axiom df_gpg {x3 e k n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cgpg (cmpo n k cn (co c1 (cfv (co (cv n) c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo))) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (cv n) cmo)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) (cv k) caddc) (cv n) cmo))))) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo)) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo)))))))) $;

--| The generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgov {x3 e: setvar} (I: class) (J: class x3 e) (K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wceq (co N K cgpg) (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I)) (cop (cfv cnx cedgf) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I))))))) $;

--| The vertices of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtx (I J K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv (co N K cgpg) cvtx) (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I)) $;

--| The indexed edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgiedg {x3 e: setvar} (I: class) (J: class x3 e) (K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv (co N K cgpg) ciedg) (cres cid (crab e (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I))))) $;

--| The edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgedg {x3 e: setvar} (I: class) (J: class x3 e) (K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv (co N K cgpg) cedg) (crab e (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gpgiedgdmel and ~ gpgedgel . (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgiedgdmellem {x3: setvar} (I J K N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wi (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq Y (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq Y (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq Y (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I) (wcel Y (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I)))) $;

--| A vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtxel {x3 y1: setvar} (G: class x3 y1) (I: class) (J K N: class y1) (V: class x3 y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wb (wcel X V) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq X (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) I) (cpr cc0 c1))) $;

--| The second component of a vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtxel2 (G I J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wcel X V)) (wcel (cfv X c2nd) I) $;

--| An index of edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgiedgdmel {x3: setvar} (G: class x3) (I J K N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wb (wcel X (cdm (cfv G ciedg))) (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq X (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq X (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq X (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I)) $;

--| An edge in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Aug-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgedgel {x3: setvar} (E G: class x3) (I J K N Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wb (wcel Y E) (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq Y (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq Y (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq Y (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I)) $;

--| An index of edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,1). (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgriedgdmel {x3: setvar} (G: class x3) (I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wb (wcel X (cdm (cfv G ciedg))) (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq X (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq X (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq X (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo))))) I)) $;

--| A subset of the index of edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,1). (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgriedgdmss (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wss (cun (cpr (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop c1 cc0))) (cpr (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)))) (cdm (cfv (co N c1 cgpg) ciedg))) $;

--| The outside vertices in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtx0 (G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wcel X V)) (w3a (wcel (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 caddc) N cmo)) V) (wcel (cop cc0 (cfv X c2nd)) V) (wcel (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 cmin) N cmo)) V)) $;

--| The inside vertices in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtx1 (G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wcel X V)) (w3a (wcel (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K caddc) N cmo)) V) (wcel (cop c1 (cfv X c2nd)) V) (wcel (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K cmin) N cmo)) V)) $;

--| A vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` as ordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem opgpgvtx (G I J K N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wb (wcel (cop X Y) V) (wa (wo (wceq X cc0) (wceq X c1)) (wcel Y I))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ gpgusgra . (Contributed by AV, 27-Aug-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgusgralem {x3 e p: setvar} (I: class) (J K N: class p):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wss (crab e (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cv e) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) K caddc) N cmo))))) I) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I))) (crab p (wceq (cfv (cv p) chash) c2) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) I)))) $;

--| The generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K) is a simple graph. (Contributed by AV, 27-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgusgra (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo))) (wcel (co N K cgpg) cusgr) $;

--| The generalized Petersen graphs G(N,1), which are the N-prisms, are simple graphs. (Contributed by AV, 31-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgrusgra (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel (co N c1 cgpg) cusgr) $;

--| The order of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K). (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgorder (J K N: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co N K cgpg) cvtx) chash) (co c2 N cmul)) $;

--| The order of a generalized Petersen graph G(5,K), which is either the Petersen graph G(5,2) or the 5-prism G(5,1), is 10. (Contributed by AV, 26-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpg5order (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co c1 c2 cfz)) (wceq (cfv (cfv (co c5 K cgpg) cvtx) chash) (cdc c1 cc0)) $;

--| The edges starting at an outside vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgedgvtx0 (E G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0))) (w3a (wcel (cpr X (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 caddc) N cmo))) E) (wcel (cpr X (cop c1 (cfv X c2nd))) E) (wcel (cpr X (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 cmin) N cmo))) E)) $;

--| The edges starting at an inside vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgedgvtx1 (E G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c1))) (w3a (wcel (cpr X (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K caddc) N cmo))) E) (wcel (cpr X (cop cc0 (cfv X c2nd))) E) (wcel (cpr X (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K cmin) N cmo))) E)) $;

--| The edges starting at an outside vertex ` X ` in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgvtxedg0 (E G J K N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0) (wcel (cpr X Y) E)) (w3o (wceq Y (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 caddc) N cmo))) (wceq Y (cop c1 (cfv X c2nd))) (wceq Y (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 cmin) N cmo)))) $;

--| The edges starting at an inside vertex ` X ` in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgvtxedg1 (E G J K N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c1) (wcel (cpr X Y) E)) (w3o (wceq Y (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K caddc) N cmo))) (wceq Y (cop cc0 (cfv X c2nd))) (wceq Y (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K cmin) N cmo)))) $;

--| The edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K) between an inside and an outside vertex. (Contributed by AV, 11-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgedgiov (E G I J K N X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X I) (wcel Y I))) (wb (wcel (cpr (cop cc0 X) (cop c1 Y)) E) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| The edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K) between two outside vertices. (Contributed by AV, 15-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgedg2ov (E G I J K N X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X I) (wcel Y I))) (wb (wa (wcel (cpr (cop cc0 (co (co Y c1 cmin) N cmo)) (cop cc0 X)) E) (wcel (cpr (cop cc0 X) (cop cc0 (co (co Y c1 caddc) N cmo))) E)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| The edges of the generalized Petersen graph GPG(N,K) between two inside vertices. (Contributed by AV, 20-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgedg2iv (E G I J K N X Y: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz)) (wa (wcel X I) (wcel Y I)) (wa (wcel K J) (wne (co (co c4 K cmul) N cmo) cc0))) (wb (wa (wcel (cpr (cop c1 (co (co Y K cmin) N cmo)) (cop c1 X)) E) (wcel (cpr (cop c1 X) (cop c1 (co (co Y K caddc) N cmo))) E)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gpg5nbgrvtx03star . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx03starlem1 (E G J K N V W X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J) (wcel X W)) (wnel (cpr (cop cc0 (co (co X c1 caddc) N cmo)) (cop c1 X)) E) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gpg5nbgrvtx03star . (Contributed by AV, 6-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx03starlem2 (E G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wcel K J) (wcel X cz)) (wnel (cpr (cop cc0 (co (co X c1 caddc) N cmo)) (cop cc0 (co (co X c1 cmin) N cmo))) E) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ gpg5nbgrvtx03star . (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx03starlem3 (E G J K N V W X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J) (wcel X W)) (wnel (cpr (cop c1 X) (cop cc0 (co (co X c1 cmin) N cmo))) E) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gpg5nbgr3star . (Contributed by AV, 7-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx13starlem1 (E G J K N V W X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wceq N c5) (wcel K J)) (wcel X W)) (wnel (cpr (cop c1 (co (co X K caddc) N cmo)) (cop cc0 X)) E) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gpg5nbgr3star . (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx13starlem2 (E G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wceq N c5) (wcel K J)) (wcel X cz)) (wnel (cpr (cop c1 (co (co X K caddc) N cmo)) (cop c1 (co (co X K cmin) N cmo))) E) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ gpg5nbgr3star . (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx13starlem3 (E G J K N V W X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wceq N c5) (wcel K J)) (wcel X W)) (wnel (cpr (cop cc0 X) (cop c1 (co (co X K cmin) N cmo))) E) $;

--| The (open) neighborhood of an outside vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 28-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpgnbgrvtx0 (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0))) (wceq U (ctp (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 caddc) N cmo)) (cop c1 (cfv X c2nd)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) c1 cmin) N cmo)))) $;

--| The (open) neighborhood of an inside vertex in a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` . (Contributed by AV, 2-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgnbgrvtx1 (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c1))) (wceq U (ctp (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K caddc) N cmo)) (cop cc0 (cfv X c2nd)) (cop c1 (co (co (cfv X c2nd) K cmin) N cmo)))) $;

--| In a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` , every outside vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors. (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpg3nbgrvtx0 (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0))) (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) $;

--| In a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` , every outside vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors. (Contributed by AV, 30-Aug-2025.) The proof of ~ gpg3nbgrvtx0 can be shortened using ~ modmknepk , but then theorem ~ 2ltceilhalf is required which is based on an "example" ~ ex-ceil . If these theorems were moved to main, the "example" should also be moved up to become a full-fledged theorem. (Proof shortened by AV, 4-Sep-2025.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem gpg3nbgrvtx0ALT (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0))) (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) $;

--| In a generalized Petersen graph ` G ` , every inside vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpg3nbgrvtx1 (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) c1))) (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) $;

--| Every generalized Petersen graph is a _cubic_ graph, i.e., it is a 3-regular graph, i.e., every vertex has degree 3 (see ~ gpgvtxdg3 ), i.e., every vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors. (Contributed by AV, 3-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgcubic (U G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J) (wcel X V)) (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) $;

--| In a generalized Petersen graph G(N,K) of order greater than 8 ( ` 3 < N ` ), every outside vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors, and none of these neighbors are connected by an edge (i.e., the (closed) neighborhood of every outside vertex induces a subgraph which is isomorphic to a 3-star). (Contributed by AV, 31-Aug-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgrvtx03star {x3 y1: setvar} (U E: class) (G: class x3) (J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel N (cfv c4 cuz)) (wcel K J)) (wa (wcel X V) (wceq (cfv X c1st) cc0))) (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U)) $;

--| In a generalized Petersen graph G(N,K) of order 10 ( ` N = 5 ` ), these are the Petersen graph G(5,2) and the 5-prism G(5,1), every vertex has exactly three (different) neighbors, and none of these neighbors are connected by an edge (i.e., the (closed) neighborhood of every vertex induces a subgraph which is isomorphic to a 3-star). This does not hold for every generalized Petersen graph: for example, in the 3-prism G(3,1) (see gpg31grim3prism TODO) and the D&uuml;rer graph G(6,2) there are vertices which have neighborhoods containing triangles. In general, all generalized Petersen graphs G(N,K) with ` N = 3 x. K ` contain triangles, see ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5nbgr3star {x3 y1: setvar} (U E: class) (G: class x3) (J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq U (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wceq N c5) (wcel K J) (wcel X V)) (wa (wceq (cfv U chash) c3) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wnel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) E) U) U)) $;

--| Every vertex in a generalized Petersen graph has degree 3. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpgvtxdg3 (G J K N V X: class):
  $ wceq J (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel K J) (wcel X V)) (wceq (cfv X (cfv G cvtxdg)) c3) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem1 (K: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wbr K (cfv (co (co c3 K cmul) c2 cdiv) cceil) clt) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem2 (K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wceq (co (cneg K) N cmo) (co (co (co K N cmo) K caddc) N cmo)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem3 (K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem4 (K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wcel K (co c1 (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo)) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem5 (K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wne (co K N cmo) (co (cneg K) N cmo)) $;

--| Lemma 6 for ~ gpg3kgrtriex : ` E ` is an edge in the generalized Petersen graph G(N,K) with ` N = 3 x. K ` . (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriexlem6 (E G K N: class):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cpr (cop c1 (co K N cmo)) (cop c1 (co (cneg K) N cmo))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wcel E (cfv G cedg)) $;

--| All generalized Petersen graphs G(N,K) with ` N = 3 x. K ` contain triangles. (Contributed by AV, 1-Oct-2025.)
theorem gpg3kgrtriex {t: setvar} (G: class) (K N: class t):
  $ wceq N (co c3 K cmul) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N K cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K cn) (wex t (wcel (cv t) (cfv G cgrtri))) $;

--| Each closed neighborhood in a generalized Petersen graph G(N,K) of order 10 ( ` N = 5 ` ), which is either the Petersen graph G(5,2) or the 5-prism G(5,1), is isomorphic to a 3-star. (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2025.)
theorem gpg5gricstgr3 (G K V: class):
  $ wceq G (co c5 K cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (co c1 c2 cfz)) (wcel V (cfv G cvtx))) (wbr (co G (co G V cclnbgr) cisubgr) (cfv c3 cstgr) cgric) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about Petersen graphs. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2025.)
theorem pglem:
  $ wcel c2 (co c1 (cfv (co c5 c2 cdiv) cceil) cfzo) $;

--| A Petersen graph is a simple graph. (Contributed by AV, 10-Nov-2025.)
theorem pgjsgr:
  $ wcel (co c5 c2 cgpg) cusgr $;

--| A local isomorphism between the two generalized Petersen graphs G(N,K) of order 10 ( ` N = 5 ` ), which are the Petersen graph G(5,2) and the 5-prism G(5,1). (Contributed by AV, 28-Dec-2025.)
theorem gpg5grlim:
  $ wcel (cres cid (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) (co cc0 c5 cfzo))) (co (co c5 c1 cgpg) (co c5 c2 cgpg) cgrlim) $;

--| The two generalized Petersen graphs G(N,K) of order 10 ( ` N = 5 ` ), which are the Petersen graph G(5,2) and the 5-prism G(5,1), are locally isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 29-Sep-2025.) (Proof shortened by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpg5grlic:
  $ wbr (co c5 c1 cgpg) (co c5 c2 cgpg) cgrlic $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 : the cycle ` <. P , F >. ` consists of 4 edges (i.e., has length 4). (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem1 (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv F chash) c4 $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 : the cycle ` <. P , F >. ` is proper, i.e., it has no overlapping edges. (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem2 (F: class):
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wfun (ccnv F) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem3 {x3: setvar} (F: class x3) (N X: class):
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel X (co cc0 c4 cfzo))) (wa (wcel (cfv X F) (cpw (cxp (cpr cc0 c1) (co cc0 N cfzo)))) (wrex x3 (w3o (wceq (cfv X F) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo)))) (wceq (cfv X F) (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv x3)))) (wceq (cfv X F) (cpr (cop c1 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (co (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) N cmo))))) (co cc0 N cfzo))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 : the cycle ` <. P , F >. ` consists of 5 vertices (the first and the last vertex are identical, see ~ gpgprismgr4cycllem6 . (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem4 (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv P chash) c5 $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem5 (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wcel P (cword cvv) $;

--| Lemma 6 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 : the cycle ` <. P , F >. ` is closed, i.e., the first and the last vertex are identical. (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem6 (P: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 P) (cfv c4 P) $;

--| Lemma 7 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 : the cycle ` <. P , F >. ` is proper, i.e., it has no overlapping vertices, except the first and the last one. (Contributed by AV, 1-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem7 (P X Y: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X (co cc0 (cfv P chash) cfzo)) (wcel Y (co c1 c4 cfzo))) (wi (wne X Y) (wne (cfv X P) (cfv Y P))) $;

--| Lemma 8 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 2-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem8 (P F G N: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel F (cword (cdm (cfv G ciedg)))) $;

--| Lemma 9 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 3-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem9 (P F G N: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfz) (cfv G cvtx) P) $;

--| Lemma 10 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem10 (P F G N X: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel X (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (cfv X F) (cfv G ciedg)) (cpr (cfv X P) (cfv (co X c1 caddc) P))) $;

--| Lemma 11 for ~ gpgprismgr4cycl0 . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycllem11 (P F G N: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wbr F P (cfv G ccycls)) $;

--| The generalized Petersen graphs G(N,1), which are the N-prisms, have a cycle of length 4 starting at the vertex ` <. 0 , 0 >. ` . (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cycl0 (P F G N: class):
  $ wceq P (cs5 (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cs4 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop cc0 c1)) (cpr (cop cc0 c1) (cop c1 c1)) (cpr (cop c1 c1) (cop c1 cc0)) (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop cc0 cc0))) $ >
  $ wceq G (co N c1 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wa (wbr F P (cfv G ccycls)) (wceq (cfv F chash) c4)) $;

--| The generalized Petersen graphs G(N,1), which are the N-prisms, have (at least) one cycle of length 4. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpgprismgr4cyclex {f2 p: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wex p (wex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv p) (cfv (co N c1 cgpg) ccycls)) (wceq (cfv (cv f2) chash) c4)))) $;

--| An inside and an outside vertex not adjacent in a Petersen graph. (Contributed by AV, 21-Nov-2025.)
theorem pgnioedg1 (y1: setvar) (E G: class):
  $ wceq G (co c5 c2 cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) (co cc0 c5 cfzo)) (wn (wcel (cpr (cop c1 (co (co (cv y1) c2 cmin) c5 cmo)) (cop cc0 (co (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) c5 cmo))) E)) $;

