  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel K N) (wcel L N) (wne K L)) (wcel (cv b) (co cc0 c5 cfzo))) (wi (wa (wcel (cpr K (cop c1 (cv b))) E) (wcel (cpr (cop c1 (cv b)) L) E)) (wceq X (cop c1 (cv b)))) $;

--| In a Petersen graph, two different neighbors of a vertex have exactly one common neighbor, which is the vertex itself. (Contributed by AV, 9-Nov-2025.)
theorem pgnbgreunbgr {x3: setvar} (E: class) (G: class x3) (K L N V X: class):
  $ wceq G (co c5 c2 cgpg) $ >
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv G cedg) $ >
  $ wceq N (co G X cnbgr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K N) (wcel L N) (wne K L)) (wreu x3 (wss (cpr (cpr K (cv x3)) (cpr (cv x3) L)) E) V) $;

--| A cycle in a Petersen graph G(5,2) does not have length 4. (Contributed by AV, 9-Nov-2025.)
theorem pgn4cyclex (P F G: class):
  $ wceq G (co c5 c2 cgpg) $ >
  $ wi (wbr F P (cfv G ccycls)) (wne (cfv F chash) c4) $;

--| In the Petersen graph G(5,2), there is no cycle of length 4. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem pg4cyclnex {f2 p: setvar}:
  $ wn (wex p (wex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cv p) (cfv (co c5 c2 cgpg) ccycls)) (wceq (cfv (cv f2) chash) c4)))) $;

--| The two generalized Petersen graphs G(5,K) of order 10, which are the Petersen graph G(5,2) and the 5-prism G(5,1), are not isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 22-Nov-2025.)
theorem gpg5ngric:
  $ wn (wbr (co c5 c1 cgpg) (co c5 c2 cgpg) cgric) $;

--| There are two different locally isomorphic graphs which are not isomorphic. (Contributed by AV, 23-Nov-2025.)
theorem lgricngricex {g2 h: setvar}:
  $ wex g2 (wex h (wa (wbr (cv g2) (cv h) cgrlic) (wn (wbr (cv g2) (cv h) cgric)))) $;

--| Two consecutive (according to the numbering) inside vertices of the Petersen graph G(5,2) are not connected by an edge, but are connected by an edge in a 5-prism G(5,1). (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2025.)
theorem gpg5edgnedg:
  $ wa (wcel (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop c1 c1)) (cfv (co c5 c1 cgpg) cedg)) (wnel (cpr (cop c1 cc0) (cop c1 c1)) (cfv (co c5 c2 cgpg) cedg)) $;

--| In general, the image of an edge of a graph by a local isomprphism is not an edge of the other graph, proven by an example (see ~ gpg5edgnedg ). This theorem proves that the analogon ` ( ( ( G e. USPGraph /\ H e. USPGraph ) /\ ( F e. ( G GraphLocIso H ) ` ` /\ K e. I ) ) -> ( F " K ) e. E ) ` of ~ grimedgi for ordinarily isomorphic graphs does not hold in general. (Contributed by AV, 30-Dec-2025.)
theorem grlimedgnedg {f2 g2 h a b: setvar}:
  $ wrex g2 (wrex h (wrex f2 (wrex a (wrex b (wa (wcel (cpr (cv a) (cv b)) (cfv (cv g2) cedg)) (wnel (cpr (cfv (cv a) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv b) (cv f2))) (cfv (cv h) cedg))) (cfv (cv g2) cvtx)) (cfv (cv g2) cvtx)) (co (cv g2) (cv h) cgrlim)) cusgr) cusgr $;

--| A graph ` G ` with one hyperedge joining at least two vertices is a loop-free graph. (Contributed by AV, 23-Feb-2021.)
theorem _1hegrlfgr {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2 C: class x3) (E: class) (G: class x3) (V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E (cpw V)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv G ciedg) (csn (cop A2 E))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss (cpr B2 C) E) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf (csn A2) (crab x3 (wbr c2 (cfv (cv x3) chash) cle) (cpw V)) (cfv G ciedg)) $;

--| Extend class notation with walks (of a pseudograph).
term cupwlks: class;

--| Define the set of all walks (in a pseudograph), called "simple walks" in the following. According to Wikipedia ("Path (graph theory)", ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Path_(graph_theory) , 3-Oct-2017): "A walk of length k in a graph is an alternating sequence of vertices and edges, v0 , e0 , v1 , e1 , v2 , ... , v(k-1) , e(k-1) , v(k) which begins and ends with vertices. If the graph is undirected, then the endpoints of e(i) are v(i) and v(i+1)." According to Bollobas: " A walk W in a graph is an alternating sequence of vertices and edges x0 , e1 , x1 , e2 , ... , e(l) , x(l) where e(i) = x(i-1)x(i), 0<i<=l.", see Definition of [Bollobas] p. 4. Therefore, a walk can be represented by two mappings f from { 1 , ... , n } and p from { 0 , ... , n }, where f enumerates the (indices of the) edges, and p enumerates the vertices. So the walk is represented by the following sequence: p(0) e(f(1)) p(1) e(f(2)) ... p(n-1) e(f(n)) p(n). Although this definition is also applicable for arbitrary hypergraphs, it allows only walks consisting of not proper hyperedges (i.e. edges connecting at most two vertices). Therefore, it should be used for pseudographs only. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens and Mario Carneiro, 4-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 28-Dec-2020.)
axiom df_upwlks {f2 g2 k p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cupwlks (cmpt g2 cvv (copab f2 p (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (cword (cdm (cfv (cv g2) ciedg)))) (wf (co cc0 (cfv (cv f2) chash) cfz) (cfv (cv g2) cvtx) (cv p)) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cfv (cv g2) ciedg)) (cpr (cfv (cv k) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv p)))) (co cc0 (cfv (cv f2) chash) cfzo))))) $;

--| The set of simple walks (in an undirected graph). (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 28-Dec-2020.)
theorem upwlksfval {f2 k p: setvar} (G: class) (I: class k) (V: class f2 k) (W: class k p):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G W) (wceq (cfv G cupwlks) (copab f2 p (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (cword (cdm I))) (wf (co cc0 (cfv (cv f2) chash) cfz) V (cv p)) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) I) (cpr (cfv (cv k) (cv p)) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) (cv p)))) (co cc0 (cfv (cv f2) chash) cfzo))))) $;

--| Properties of a pair of functions to be a simple walk. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 20-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 28-Dec-2020.)
theorem isupwlk {k: setvar} (P: class) (U: class k) (F G: class) (I V W Z: class k):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G W) (wcel F U) (wcel P Z)) (wb (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks)) (w3a (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfz) V P) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) I) (cpr (cfv (cv k) P) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) P))) (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo)))) $;

--| Generalization of ~ isupwlk : Conditions for two classes to represent a simple walk. (Contributed by AV, 5-Nov-2021.)
theorem isupwlkg {k: setvar} (P F G: class) (I V W: class k):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel G W) (wb (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks)) (w3a (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfz) V P) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) I) (cpr (cfv (cv k) P) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) P))) (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo)))) $;

--| Basic properties of a simple walk. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 29-Dec-2020.)
theorem upwlkbprop (P F G I V: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks)) (w3a (wcel G cvv) (wcel F cvv) (wcel P cvv)) $;

--| A simple walk is a walk. (Contributed by AV, 30-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 27-Feb-2021.)
theorem upwlkwlk (P F G: class):
  $ wi (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks)) (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) $;

--| In a pseudograph, a walk is a simple walk. (Contributed by AV, 30-Dec-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Jan-2021.)
theorem upgrwlkupwlk (P F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G cupgr) (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks))) (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks)) $;

--| In a pseudograph, the definitions for a walk and a simple walk are equivalent. (Contributed by AV, 30-Dec-2020.)
theorem upgrwlkupwlkb (P F G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cupgr) (wb (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) (wbr F P (cfv G cupwlks))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ upgriswlk using the definition of ` UPGraph ` and related theorems. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jan-2021.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem upgrisupwlkALT {k: setvar} (P: class) (U: class k) (F G: class) (I V Z: class k):
  $ wceq V (cfv G cvtx) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv G ciedg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G cupgr) (wcel F U) (wcel P Z)) (wb (wbr F P (cfv G cwlks)) (w3a (wcel F (cword (cdm I))) (wf (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfz) V P) (wral k (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) I) (cpr (cfv (cv k) P) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) P))) (co cc0 (cfv F chash) cfzo)))) $;

--| The set of edges of a pseudograph is a subset of the set of unordered pairs of vertices. (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2021.)
theorem upgredgssspr (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G cupgr) (wss (cfv G cedg) (cfv (cfv G cvtx) cspr)) $;

--| The set ` G ` of "simple pseudographs" for a fixed set ` V ` of vertices is a subset of a Cartesian product. For more details about the class ` G ` of all "simple pseudographs" see comments on ~ uspgrbisymrel . (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgropssxp {v e q: setvar} (P: class) (G: class v e q) (V: class) (W: class q):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wss G (cxp W P)) $;

--| The value of the function ` F ` which maps a "simple pseudograph" for a fixed set ` V ` of vertices to the set of edges (i.e. range of the edge function) of the graph. Solely for ` G ` as defined here, the function ` F ` is a bijection between the "simple pseudographs" and the subsets of the set of pairs ` P ` over the fixed set ` V ` of vertices, see ~ uspgrbispr . (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrsprfv {v e g2 q: setvar} (P: class g2) (F: class v e g2 q) (G: class v e q) (V: class g2) (X: class v e q):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 G (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X G) (wceq (cfv X F) (cfv X c2nd)) $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a function from the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` into the subsets of the set of pairs over the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 24-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrsprf {v e g2 q: setvar} (P: class) (F: class v e g2 q) (G: class v e q) (V: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 G (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wf G P F $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a one-to-one function from the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` into the subsets of the set of pairs over the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrsprf1 {v e g2 q: setvar} (P: class) (F: class v e g2 q) (G: class v e q) (V: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 G (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wf1 G P F $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a function from the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` onto the subsets of the set of pairs over the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrsprfo {v e g2 q: setvar} (P: class) (F: class v e g2 q) (G: class v e q) (V W: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 G (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wfo G P F) $;

--| The mapping ` F ` is a bijection between the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` and the subsets of the set of pairs over the set ` V ` . See also the comments on ~ uspgrbisymrel . (Contributed by AV, 25-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrsprf1o {v e g2 q: setvar} (P: class) (F: class v e g2 q) (G: class v e q) (V W: class g2):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt g2 G (cfv (cv g2) c2nd)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wf1o G P F) $;

--| The class ` G ` of all "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` is a set. (Contributed by AV, 26-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrex {v e q: setvar} (P: class) (G: class v e q) (V W: class):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wcel G cvv) $;

--| There is a bijection between the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` and the subsets of the set of pairs over the set ` V ` . (Contributed by AV, 26-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrbispr {v e f2 q: setvar} (P: class) (G: class v e q) (V W: class f2):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wex f2 (wf1o G P (cv f2))) $;

--| The set ` G ` of the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` and the class ` P ` of subsets of the set of pairs over the fixed set ` V ` are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 27-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrspren {v e q: setvar} (P: class) (G: class v e q) (V W: class):
  $ wceq P (cpw (cfv V cspr)) $ >
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wbr G P cen) $;

--| The set ` G ` of the "simple pseudographs" with a fixed set of vertices ` V ` and the class ` R ` of the symmetric relations on the fixed set ` V ` are equinumerous. For more details about the class ` G ` of all "simple pseudographs" see comments on ~ uspgrbisymrel . (Contributed by AV, 27-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrymrelen {x3 y1 v e r q: setvar} (R G: class x3 y1 v e r q) (V: class) (W: class r):
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wbr G R cen) $;

--| There is a bijection between the "simple pseudographs" for a fixed set ` V ` of vertices and the class ` R ` of the symmetric relations on the fixed set ` V ` . The simple pseudographs, which are graphs without hyper- or multiedges, but which may contain loops, are expressed as ordered pairs of the vertices and the edges (as proper or improper unordered pairs of vertices, not as indexed edges!) in this theorem. That class ` G ` of such simple pseudographs is a set (if ` V ` is a set, see ~ uspgrex ) of equivalence classes of graphs abstracting from the index sets of their edge functions. Solely for this abstraction, there is a bijection between the "simple pseudographs" as members of ` G ` and the symmetric relations ` R ` on the fixed set ` V ` of vertices. This theorem would not hold for ` G = { g e. USPGraph | ( Vtx `` g ) = V } ` and even not for ` G = { <. v , e >. | ( v = V /\ <. v , e >. e. USPGraph ) } ` , because these are much bigger classes. (Proposed by Gerard Lang, 16-Nov-2021.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Nov-2021.)
theorem uspgrbisymrel {x3 y1 v e f2 r q: setvar} (R G: class x3 y1 v e r q) (V: class f2) (W: class f2 r):
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wex f2 (wf1o G R (cv f2))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ uspgrbisymrel not using the definition of equinumerosity. (Contributed by AV, 26-Nov-2021.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem uspgrbisymrelALT {x3 y1 v e f2 r q: setvar} (R G: class x3 y1 v e r q) (V: class) (W: class f2 r):
  $ wceq G (copab v e (wa (wceq (cv v) V) (wrex q (wa (wceq (cfv (cv q) cvtx) (cv v)) (wceq (cfv (cv q) cedg) (cv e))) cuspgr))) $ >
  $ wceq R (crab r (wral x3 (wral y1 (wb (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv r)) (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv r))) V) V) (cpw (cxp V V))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel V W) (wex f2 (wf1o G R (cv f2))) $;

--| If a class operation value for two operands is not the empty set, then the operands are contained in the domain of the class, and the class restricted to the operands is a function, analogous to ~ fvfundmfvn0 . (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem ovn0dmfun (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wne (co A2 B2 F) c0) (wa (wcel (cop A2 B2) (cdm F)) (wfun (cres F (csn (cop A2 B2))))) $;

--| A Cartesian product with a singleton expressed as ordered-pair class abstraction. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem xpsnopab {a b: setvar} (C X: class):
  $ wceq (cxp (csn X) C) (copab a b (wa (wceq (cv a) X) (wcel (cv b) C))) $;

--| A Cartesian product expressed as indexed union of ordered-pair class abstractions. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jan-2020.)
theorem xpiun {x3 a b: setvar} (B2: class a b) (C: class):
  $ wceq (cxp B2 C) (ciun x3 B2 (copab a b (wa (wceq (cv a) (cv x3)) (wcel (cv b) C)))) $;

--| The domain of the domain of a function over a Cartesian square. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
theorem fnxpdmdm (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wfn F (cxp A2 A2)) (wceq (cdm (cdm F)) A2) $;

--| The base set of a subring of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem cnfldsrngbas (R S: class):
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wss S cc) (wceq S (cfv R cbs)) $;

--| The group addition operation of a subring of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem cnfldsrngadd (R S V: class):
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq caddc (cfv R cplusg)) $;

--| The ring multiplication operation of a subring of the field of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem cnfldsrngmul (R S V: class):
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld S cress) $ >
  $ wi (wcel S V) (wceq cmul (cfv R cmulr)) $;

--| If the empty set is not contained in the range of the group addition function of an extensible structure (not necessarily a magma), the restriction of the addition operation to (the Cartesian square of) the base set is the functionalization of it. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2020.)
theorem plusfreseq (B2 c_pl c_pd M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv M cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wnel c0 (crn c_pd)) (wceq (cres c_pl (cxp B2 B2)) c_pd) $;

--| If the empty set is not contained in the base set of a magma, the restriction of the addition operation to (the Cartesian square of) the base set is the functionalization of it. (Contributed by AV, 28-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgmplusfreseq (B2 c_pl c_pd M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_pd (cfv M cplusf) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cmgm) (wnel c0 B2)) (wceq (cres c_pl (cxp B2 B2)) c_pd) $;

--| A set with an empty base set is always a magma. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2020.)
theorem _0mgm (M V: class):
  $ wceq (cfv M cbs) c0 $ >
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| A structure with a group addition operation in maps-to notation is a magma if the operation value is contained in the base set. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem opmpoismgm {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (M: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))) (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| A structure with a constant group addition operation is a semigroup if the constant is contained in the base set. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem copissgrp {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (M: class y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M csgrp) $;

--| A structure with a constant group addition operation and at least two elements is not a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem copisnmnd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2 C: class) (M: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv M cplusg) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 (cfv B2 chash) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnel M cmnd) $;

--| 0 is not an odd integer. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem _0nodd {x3 z: setvar} (O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wnel cc0 O $;

--| 1 is an odd integer. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem _1odd {x3 z: setvar} (O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wcel c1 O $;

--| 2 is not an odd integer. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2nodd {x3 z: setvar} (O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wnel c2 O $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ oddinmgm : The base set of M is the set of all odd integers. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem oddibas {x3 z: setvar} (M O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld O cress) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv M cbs) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ oddinmgm : The group addition operation of M is the addition of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem oddiadd {x3 z: setvar} (M O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld O cress) $ >
  $ wceq caddc (cfv M cplusg) $;

--| The structure of all odd integers together with the addition of complex numbers is not a magma. Remark: the structure of the complementary subset of the set of integers, the even integers, is a magma, actually an abelian group, see ~ 2zrngaabl , and even a non-unital ring, see ~ 2zrng . (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2020.)
theorem oddinmgm {x3 z: setvar} (M O: class x3 z):
  $ wceq O (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co (co c2 (cv x3) cmul) c1 caddc)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld O cress) $ >
  $ wnel M cmgm $;

--| The structure of positive integers together with the addition of complex numbers is a magma. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem nnsgrpmgm (M: class):
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cn cress) $ >
  $ wcel M cmgm $;

--| The structure of positive integers together with the addition of complex numbers is a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem nnsgrp (M: class):
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cn cress) $ >
  $ wcel M csgrp $;

--| The structure of positive integers together with the addition of complex numbers is not a monoid. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem nnsgrpnmnd (M: class):
  $ wceq M (co ccnfld cn cress) $ >
  $ wnel M cmnd $;

--| The set of nonnegative integers under (complex) addition is a monoid. Example in [Lang] p. 6. Remark: ` M ` could have also been written as ` ( CCfld |``s NN0 ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2023.)
theorem nn0mnd (M: class):
  $ wceq M (cpr (cop (cfv cnx cbs) cn0) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) caddc)) $ >
  $ wcel M cmnd $;

--| Split a group sum into two parts. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2019.)
theorem gsumsplit2f {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2 C D: class) (c_pl G V X c_0: class k):
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k A2 X) c_0 cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cin C D) c0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (cun C D)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G (cmpt k A2 X) cgsu) (co (co G (cmpt k C X) cgsu) (co G (cmpt k D X) cgsu) c_pl)) $;

--| Extract a summand from a finitely supported group sum. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2019.)
theorem gsumdifsndf {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (A2 B2: class) (c_pl: class k) (G M: class) (W X Y: class k):
  $ wnfc k Y $ >
  $ wnf k ph $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv G cplusg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cmpt k A2 X) (cfv G c0g) cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) A2)) (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv k) M)) (wceq X Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G (cmpt k A2 X) cgsu) (co (co G (cmpt k (cdif A2 (csn M)) X) cgsu) Y c_pl)) $;

--| A group sum of a family can be restricted to the support of that family without changing its value, provided that that support is finite. This corresponds to the definition of an (infinite) product in [Lang] p. 5, last two formulas. (Contributed by AV, 27-Dec-2023.)
theorem gsumfsupp (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G I V c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv G cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv G c0g) $ >
  $ wceq I (co F c_0 csupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G ccmn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 B2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G (cres F I) cgsu) (co G F cgsu)) $;

--| Extend class notation for the closure law.
term ccllaw: class;

--| Extend class notation for the associative law.
term casslaw: class;

--| Extend class notation for the commutative law.
term ccomlaw: class;

--| The closure law for binary operations, see definitions of laws A0. and M0. in section 1.1 of [Hall] p. 1, or definition 1 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 1: the value of a binary operation applied to two operands of a given sets is an element of this set. By this definition, the closure law is expressed as binary relation: a binary operation is related to a set by ` clLaw ` if the closure law holds for this binary operation regarding this set. Note that the binary operation need not to be a function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_cllaw {x3 y1 m o: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccllaw (copab o m (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv o)) (cv m)) (cv m)) (cv m))) $;

--| The commutative law for binary operations, see definitions of laws A2. and M2. in section 1.1 of [Hall] p. 1, or definition 8 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 7: the value of a binary operation applied to two operands equals the value of a binary operation applied to the two operands in reversed order. By this definition, the commutative law is expressed as binary relation: a binary operation is related to a set by ` comLaw ` if the commutative law holds for this binary operation regarding this set. Note that the binary operation needs neither to be closed nor to be a function. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_comlaw {x3 y1 m o: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccomlaw (copab o m (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv o)) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cv o))) (cv m)) (cv m))) $;

--| The associative law for binary operations, see definitions of laws A1. and M1. in section 1.1 of [Hall] p. 1, or definition 5 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 4: the value of a binary operation applied the value of the binary operation applied to two operands and a third operand equals the value of the binary operation applied to the first operand and the value of the binary operation applied to the second and third operand. By this definition, the associative law is expressed as binary relation: a binary operation is related to a set by ` assLaw ` if the associative law holds for this binary operation regarding this set. Note that the binary operation needs neither to be closed nor to be a function. (Contributed by FL, 1-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_asslaw {x3 y1 z m o: setvar}:
  $ wceq casslaw (copab o m (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv o)) (cv z) (cv o)) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) (cv o)) (cv o))) (cv m)) (cv m)) (cv m))) $;

--| The predicate "is a closed operation". (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
theorem iscllaw {x3 y1: setvar} (M: class) (V W: class x3 y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_op V) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr c_op M ccllaw) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) M) M) M)) $;

--| The predicate "is a commutative operation". (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem iscomlaw {x3 y1: setvar} (M: class) (V W: class x3 y1) (c_op: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_op V) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr c_op M ccomlaw) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_op)) M) M)) $;

--| Closure of a closed operation. (Contributed by FL, 14-Sep-2010.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jan-2020.)
theorem clcllaw (M X Y c_op: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wbr c_op M ccllaw) (wcel X M) (wcel Y M)) (wcel (co X Y c_op) M) $;

--| The predicate "is an associative operation". (Contributed by FL, 1-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jan-2020.)
theorem isasslaw {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M: class) (V W: class x3 y1 z) (c_op: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel c_op V) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr c_op M casslaw) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) M) M) M)) $;

--| Associativity of an associative operation. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 21-Jan-2020.)
theorem asslawass {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wbr c_op M casslaw) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) M) M) M) $;

--| Slot 2 (group operation) of a magma as extensible structure is a closed operation on the base set. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem mgmplusgiopALT (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cmgm) (wbr (cfv M cplusg) (cfv M cbs) ccllaw) $;

--| Slot 2 (group operation) of a semigroup as extensible structure is an associative operation on the base set. (Contributed by AV, 13-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem sgrpplusgaopALT (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G csgrp) (wbr (cfv G cplusg) (cfv G cbs) casslaw) $;

--| Extend class notation with class of internal (binary) operations for a set.
term cintop: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of closed operations for a set.
term cclintop: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of associative operations for a set.
term cassintop: class;

--| Function mapping a set to the class of all internal (binary) operations for this set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_intop {m n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cintop (cmpo m n cvv cvv (co (cv n) (cxp (cv m) (cv m)) cmap)) $;

--| Function mapping a set to the class of all closed (internal binary) operations for this set, see definition in section 1.2 of [Hall] p. 2, definition in section I.1 of [Bruck] p. 1, or definition 1 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 1, where it is called "a law of composition". (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_clintop {m: setvar}:
  $ wceq cclintop (cmpt m cvv (co (cv m) (cv m) cintop)) $;

--| Function mapping a set to the class of all associative (closed internal binary) operations for this set, see definition 5 in [BourbakiAlg1] p. 4, where it is called "an associative law of composition". (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_assintop {m o: setvar}:
  $ wceq cassintop (cmpt m cvv (crab o (wbr (cv o) (cv m) casslaw) (cfv (cv m) cclintop))) $;

--| The internal (binary) operations for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem intopval (M N V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M V) (wcel N W)) (wceq (co M N cintop) (co N (cxp M M) cmap)) $;

--| An internal (binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem intop (M N c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (co M N cintop)) (wf (cxp M M) N c_op) $;

--| The closed (internal binary) operations for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem clintopval (M V: class):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M cclintop) (co M (cxp M M) cmap)) $;

--| The associative (closed internal binary) operations for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintopval {o: setvar} (M: class) (V: class o):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M cassintop) (crab o (wbr (cv o) M casslaw) (cfv M cclintop))) $;

--| The associative (closed internal binary) operations for a set, expressed with set exponentiation. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintopmap {o: setvar} (M: class) (V: class o):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wceq (cfv M cassintop) (crab o (wbr (cv o) M casslaw) (co M (cxp M M) cmap))) $;

--| The predicate "is a closed (internal binary) operations for a set". (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem isclintop (M V c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wb (wcel c_op (cfv M cclintop)) (wf (cxp M M) M c_op)) $;

--| A closed (internal binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem clintop (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cclintop)) (wf (cxp M M) M c_op) $;

--| An associative (closed internal binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintop (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cassintop)) (wa (wf (cxp M M) M c_op) (wbr c_op M casslaw)) $;

--| The predicate "is an associative (closed internal binary) operations for a set". (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem isassintop {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M: class) (V: class x3 y1 z) (c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wb (wcel c_op (cfv M cassintop)) (wa (wf (cxp M M) M c_op) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) M) M) M))) $;

--| The closure law holds for a closed (internal binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem clintopcllaw (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cclintop)) (wbr c_op M ccllaw) $;

--| The closure low holds for an associative (closed internal binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintopcllaw (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cassintop)) (wbr c_op M ccllaw) $;

--| The associative low holds for a associative (closed internal binary) operation for a set. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintopasslaw (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cassintop)) (wbr c_op M casslaw) $;

--| An associative (closed internal binary) operation for a set is associative. (Contributed by FL, 2-Nov-2009.) (Revised by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
theorem assintopass {x3 y1 z: setvar} (M c_op: class):
  $ wi (wcel c_op (cfv M cassintop)) (wral x3 (wral y1 (wral z (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_op) (cv z) c_op) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_op) c_op)) M) M) M) $;

--| Extend class notation with class of all magmas.
term cmgm2: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of all commutative magmas.
term ccmgm2: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of all semigroups.
term csgrp2: class;

--| Extend class notation with class of all commutative semigroups.
term ccsgrp2: class;

--| A _magma_ is a set equipped with a closed operation. Definition 1 of [BourbakiAlg1] p. 1, or definition of a groupoid in section I.1 of [Bruck] p. 1. Note: The term "groupoid" is now widely used to refer to other objects: (small) categories all of whose morphisms are invertible, or groups with a partial function replacing the binary operation. Therefore, we will only use the term "magma" for the present notion in set.mm. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_mgm2 {m: setvar}:
  $ wceq cmgm2 (cab m (wbr (cfv (cv m) cplusg) (cfv (cv m) cbs) ccllaw)) $;

--| A _commutative magma_ is a magma with a commutative operation. Definition 8 of [BourbakiAlg1] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_cmgm2 {m: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccmgm2 (crab m (wbr (cfv (cv m) cplusg) (cfv (cv m) cbs) ccomlaw) cmgm2) $;

--| A _semigroup_ is a magma with an associative operation. Definition in section II.1 of [Bruck] p. 23, or of an "associative magma" in definition 5 of [BourbakiAlg1] p. 4, or of a semigroup in section 1.3 of [Hall] p. 7. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_sgrp2 {g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq csgrp2 (crab g2 (wbr (cfv (cv g2) cplusg) (cfv (cv g2) cbs) casslaw) cmgm2) $;

--| A _commutative semigroup_ is a semigroup with a commutative operation. (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.)
axiom df_csgrp2 {g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccsgrp2 (crab g2 (wbr (cfv (cv g2) cplusg) (cfv (cv g2) cbs) ccomlaw) csgrp2) $;

--| The predicate "is a magma". (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem ismgmALT (B2 M V c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wb (wcel M cmgm2) (wbr c_op B2 ccllaw)) $;

--| The predicate "is a commutative magma". (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem iscmgmALT (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel M ccmgm2) (wa (wcel M cmgm2) (wbr c_op B2 ccomlaw)) $;

--| The predicate "is a semigroup". (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem issgrpALT (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel M csgrp2) (wa (wcel M cmgm2) (wbr c_op B2 casslaw)) $;

--| The predicate "is a commutative semigroup". (Contributed by AV, 20-Jan-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem iscsgrpALT (B2 M c_op: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_op (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wb (wcel M ccsgrp2) (wa (wcel M csgrp2) (wbr c_op B2 ccomlaw)) $;

--| Equivalence of the two definitions of a magma. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2020.)
theorem mgm2mgm (M: class):
  $ wb (wcel M cmgm2) (wcel M cmgm) $;

--| Equivalence of the two definitions of a semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 16-Jan-2020.)
theorem sgrp2sgrp (M: class):
  $ wb (wcel M csgrp2) (wcel M csgrp) $;

--| If the scalar ring of a module is the zero ring, the module is the zero module, i.e. the base set of the module is the singleton consisting of the identity element only. (Contributed by AV, 17-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod0rng (M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wn (wcel (cfv M csca) cnzr))) (wceq (cfv M cbs) (csn (cfv M c0g))) $;

--| The additive inverse of the 1 in a nonzero ring is not zero ( -1 =/= 0 ). (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem nzrneg1ne0 (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cnzr) (wne (cfv (cfv R cur) (cfv R cminusg)) (cfv R c0g)) $;

--| If a (left) ideal (which is not the zero ideal) of a domain has a multiplicative identity element, the identity element is the identity of the domain. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2020.)
theorem lidldomn1 {x3: setvar} (R: class x3) (c_x U: class) (c_1: class x3) (I: class) (L c_0: class x3):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R cdomn) (wa (wcel U L) (wne U (csn c_0))) (wcel I U)) (wi (wral x3 (wa (wceq (co I (cv x3) c_x) (cv x3)) (wceq (co (cv x3) I c_x) (cv x3))) U) (wceq I c_1)) $;

--| A (left) ideal of a ring is an (additive) abelian group. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2020.)
theorem lidlabl (R U I L: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq I (co R U cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel U L)) (wcel I cabl) $;

--| A (left) ideal of a ring is a non-unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 11-Mar-2025.)
theorem lidlrng (R U I L: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq I (co R U cress) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel U L)) (wcel I crng) $;

--| The zero (left) ideal of a non-unital ring is a unital ring (the zero ring). (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem zlidlring (B2 R U I L c_0: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq I (co R U cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R crg) (wceq U (csn c_0))) (wcel I crg) $;

--| Only the zero (left) ideal or the unit (left) ideal of a domain is a unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2020.)
theorem uzlidlring (B2 R U I L c_0: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq I (co R U cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdomn) (wcel U L)) (wb (wcel I crg) (wo (wceq U (csn c_0)) (wceq U B2))) $;

--| A (left) ideal of a domain which is neither the zero ideal nor the unit ideal is not a unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 18-Feb-2020.)
theorem lidldomnnring (B2 R U I L c_0: class):
  $ wceq L (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wceq I (co R U cress) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cdomn) (w3a (wcel U L) (wne U (csn c_0)) (wne U B2))) (wnel I crg) $;

--| 0 is an even integer. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _0even {x3 z: setvar} (E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wcel cc0 E $;

--| 1 is not an even integer. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.)
theorem _1neven {x3 z: setvar} (E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wnel c1 E $;

--| 2 is an even integer. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2even {x3 z: setvar} (E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wcel c2 E $;

--| The even integers are a (left) ideal of the ring of integers. (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zlidl {x3 z: setvar} (U E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv czring clidl) $ >
  $ wcel E U $;

--| The ring of integers restricted to the even integers is a non-unital ring, the "ring of even integers". Remark: the structure of the complementary subset of the set of integers, the odd integers, is not even a magma, see ~ oddinmgm . (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrng {x3 z: setvar} (R U E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv czring clidl) $ >
  $ wceq R (co czring E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R crng $;

--| The base set of R is the set of all even integers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngbas {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $;

--| The group addition operation of R is the addition of complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngadd {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq caddc (cfv R cplusg) $;

--| The additive identity of R is the complex number 0. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrng0 {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq cc0 (cfv R c0g) $;

--| R is an (additive) magma. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngamgm {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R cmgm $;

--| R is an (additive) semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngasgrp {x3 z: setvar} (R: class) (E: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R csgrp $;

--| R is an (additive) monoid. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngamnd {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R cmnd $;

--| R is a commutative (additive) monoid. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngacmnd {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R ccmn $;

--| R is an (additive) group. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngagrp {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R cgrp $;

--| R is an (additive) abelian group. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngaabl {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wcel R cabl $;

--| The ring multiplication operation of R is the multiplication on complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngmul {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq cmul (cfv R cmulr) $;

--| R is a (multiplicative) magma. (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngmmgm {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wcel M cmgm $;

--| R is a (multiplicative) semigroup. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngmsgrp {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wcel M csgrp $;

--| The ring of integers restricted to the even integers is a non-unital ring, the "ring of even integers". Alternate version of ~ 2zrng , based on a restriction of the field of the complex numbers. The proof is based on the facts that the ring of even integers is an additive abelian group (see ~ 2zrngaabl ) and a multiplicative semigroup (see ~ 2zrngmsgrp ). (Contributed by AV, 11-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _2zrngALT {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wcel R crng $;

--| R has no multiplicative (left) identity. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngnmlid {x3 z a b: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wral b (wrex a (wne (co (cv b) (cv a) cmul) (cv a)) E) E $;

--| R has no multiplicative (right) identity. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngnmrid {x3 z a b: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wral a (wral b (wne (co (cv a) (cv b) cmul) (cv a)) E) (cdif E (csn cc0)) $;

--| R has no multiplicative (left) identity. (Contributed by AV, 12-Feb-2020.)
theorem _2zrngnmlid2 {x3 z a b: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wral a (wral b (wne (co (cv b) (cv a) cmul) (cv a)) E) (cdif E (csn cc0)) $;

--| R is not a unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jan-2020.)
theorem _2zrngnring {x3 z: setvar} (R E: class) (M: class x3 z):
  $ wceq E (crab z (wrex x3 (wceq (cv z) (co c2 (cv x3) cmul)) cz) cz) $ >
  $ wceq R (co ccnfld E cress) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wnel R crg $;

--| Lemma for ~ cznrng : The base set of the ring constructed from a ` Z/nZ ` structure by replacing the (multiplicative) ring operation by a constant operation is the base set of the ` Z/nZ ` structure. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem cznrnglem {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C N X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Y (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (co Y (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C)) csts) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv X cbs) $;

--| The ring constructed from a ` Z/nZ ` structure by replacing the (multiplicative) ring operation by a constant operation is an abelian group. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.)
theorem cznabel {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C N X Y: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq Y (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (co Y (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C)) csts) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel C B2)) (wcel X cabl) $;

--| The ring constructed from a ` Z/nZ ` structure by replacing the (multiplicative) ring operation by a constant operation is a non-unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2020.)
theorem cznrng {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C N: class) (X: class y1) (Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (co Y (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C)) csts) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv Y c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wceq C c_0)) (wcel X crng) $;

--| The ring constructed from a ` Z/nZ ` structure with ` 1 < n ` by replacing the (multiplicative) ring operation by a constant operation is not a unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 17-Feb-2020.)
theorem cznnring {x3 y1: setvar} (B2 C N: class) (X: class y1) (Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq Y (cfv N czn) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv Y cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (co Y (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 C)) csts) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv Y c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel C B2)) (wnel X crg) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category Rng. (New usage is discouraged.)
term crngcALTV: class;

--| Definition of the category Rng, relativized to a subset ` u ` . This is the category of all non-unital rings in ` u ` and homomorphisms between these rings. Generally, we will take ` u ` to be a weak universe or Grothendieck universe, because these sets have closure properties as good as the real thing. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
axiom df_rngcALTV {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 b: setvar}:
  $ wceq crngcALTV (cmpt u cvv (csb b (cin (cv u) crng) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv b) (cv b) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crnghm))) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v z (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) crnghm) (co (cfv (cv v) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) crnghm) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2)))))))) $;

--| Value of the category of non-unital rings (in a universe). (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngcvalALTV {x3 y1 z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (H V: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cin U crng)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crnghm))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) crnghm) (co (cfv (cv v) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) crnghm) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq C (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) B2) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) (cop (cfv cnx cco) c_x))) $;

--| Set of objects of the category of non-unital rings (in a universe). (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngcbasALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq B2 (cin U crng)) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of non-unital rings (in a universe). (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngchomfvalALTV {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (U: class) (H V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq H (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crnghm))) $;

--| Set of arrows of the category of non-unital rings (in a universe). (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngchomALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co X Y H) (co X Y crnghm)) $;

--| A morphism of non-unital rings is a function. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem elrngchomALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U F H V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq H (cfv C chom) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel F (co X Y H)) (wf (cfv X cbs) (cfv Y cbs) F)) $;

--| Composition in the category of non-unital rings. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngccofvalALTV {z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x: class z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (V: class z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq c_x (cmpo v z (cxp B2 B2) B2 (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) crnghm) (co (cfv (cv v) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) crnghm) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Composition in the category of non-unital rings. (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngccoALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C c_x U F G V X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv C cco) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (co X Y crnghm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G (co Y Z crnghm)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co G F (co (cop X Y) Z c_x)) (ccom G F)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ rngccatALTV . (New usage is discouraged.) (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.)
theorem rngccatidALTV {x3: setvar} (B2 C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wa (wcel C ccat) (wceq (cfv C ccid) (cmpt x3 B2 (cres cid (cfv (cv x3) cbs))))) $;

--| The category of non-unital rings is a category. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngccatALTV (C U V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel C ccat) $;

--| The identity arrow in the category of non-unital rings is the identity function. (Contributed by AV, 27-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngcidALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C S U c_1 V X: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv C ccid) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv X c_1) (cres cid S)) $;

--| A section in the category of non-unital rings, written out. (Contributed by AV, 28-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngcsectALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C S U E F G V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv X cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv C csect) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y S)) (w3a (wcel F (co X Y crnghm)) (wcel G (co Y X crnghm)) (wceq (ccom G F) (cres cid E)))) $;

--| An inverse in the category of non-unital rings is the converse operation. (Contributed by AV, 28-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngcinvALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U F G N V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv C cinv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F G (co X Y N)) (wa (wcel F (co X Y crngim)) (wceq G (ccnv F)))) $;

--| An isomorphism in the category of non-unital rings is a bijection. (Contributed by AV, 28-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngcisoALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U F I V X Y: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Y B2) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv C ciso) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel F (co X Y I)) (wcel F (co X Y crngim))) $;

--| The value of the functionalized Hom-set operation in the category of non-unital rings (in a universe) in maps-to notation for an operation. (Contributed by AV, 1-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngchomffvalALTV {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (U: class) (F V: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv C cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crnghm))) $;

--| The value of the functionalized Hom-set operation in the category of non-unital rings (in a universe) as restriction of the non-unital ring homomorphisms. (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngchomrnghmresALTV (ph: wff) (B2 C U F V: class):
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cin crng U) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv C chomf) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cres crnghm (cxp B2 B2))) $;

--| The category of non-unital rings (in a universe) restricted to the ring homomorphisms between unital rings (in the same universe). (Contributed by AV, 1-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rngcrescrhmALTV (ph: wff) (C R U H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co C H cresc) (co (co C R cress) (cop (cfv cnx chom) H) csts)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ rhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTVlem1 (ph: wff) (C R U H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn H (cxp R R)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ rhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTVlem2 (ph: wff) (C R U H V X Y: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wcel X R) (wcel Y R)) (wceq (co X Y H) (co X Y crh)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ rhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTVlem3 (ph: wff) (x3: setvar) (C R U H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) R)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cfv (cfv U crngcALTV) ccid)) (co (cv x3) (cv x3) H)) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ rhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTVlem4 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (y1 z: setvar) (C: class x3) (R: class) (U: class x3) (f2 g2: setvar) (H V: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) R)) (wa (wcel (cv y1) R) (wcel (cv z) R))) (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) H)) (wcel (cv g2) (co (cv y1) (cv z) H)))) (wcel (co (cv g2) (cv f2) (co (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) (cv z) (cfv (cfv U crngcALTV) cco))) (co (cv x3) (cv z) H)) $;

--| According to ~ df-subc , the subcategories ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` of a category ` C ` are subsets of the homomorphisms of ` C ` (see ~ subcssc and ~ subcss2 ). Therefore, the set of unital ring homomorphisms is a "subcategory" of the category of non-unital rings. (Contributed by AV, 2-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTV (ph: wff) (C R U H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H (cfv (cfv U crngcALTV) csubc)) $;

--| The restriction of the category of non-unital rings to the set of unital ring homomorphisms is a category. (Contributed by AV, 4-Mar-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem rhmsubcALTVcat (ph: wff) (C R U H V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv U crngcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq R (cin crg U)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cres crh (cxp R R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co (cfv U crngcALTV) H cresc) ccat) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the category Ring. (New usage is discouraged.)
term cringcALTV: class;

--| Definition of the category Ring, relativized to a subset ` u ` . This is the category of all rings in ` u ` and homomorphisms between these rings. Generally, we will take ` u ` to be a weak universe or Grothendieck universe, because these sets have closure properties as good as the real thing. (Contributed by AV, 13-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom df_ringcALTV {x3 y1 z v u f2 g2 b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cringcALTV (cmpt u cvv (csb b (cin (cv u) crg) (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (cv b)) (cop (cfv cnx chom) (cmpo x3 y1 (cv b) (cv b) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crh))) (cop (cfv cnx cco) (cmpo v z (cxp (cv b) (cv b)) (cv b) (cmpo g2 f2 (co (cfv (cv v) c2nd) (cv z) crh) (co (cfv (cv v) c1st) (cfv (cv v) c2nd) crh) (ccom (cv g2) (cv f2)))))))) $;

--| Value of the category of rings (in a universe). (Contributed by AV, 13-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem ringcvalALTV {x3 y1 z v f2 g2: setvar} (ph: wff f2 g2) (B2: class f2 g2) (C c_x: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2) (U: class f2 g2) (H V: class x3 y1 z v f2 g2):
  $ wceq C (cfv U cringcALTV) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U V) $ >
