--| The "natural forgetful functor" from the category of rings into the category of sets which sends each ring to its underlying set (base set) and the morphisms (ring homomorphisms) to mappings of the corresponding base sets. (Contributed by AV, 16-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem funcringcsetcALTV {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class) (C: class y1) (R S U F G: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq R (cfv U cringcALTV) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv U csetc) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel U cwun) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpt x3 B2 (cfv (cv x3) cbs))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq G (cmpo x3 y1 B2 B2 (cres cid (co (cv x3) (cv y1) crh)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F G (co R S cfunc)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ srhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem srhmsubcALTVlem1 {r: setvar} (C: class r) (S: class) (U V: class r) (X: class):
  $ wral r (wcel (cv r) crg) S $ >
  $ wceq C (cin U S) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U V) (wcel X C)) (wcel X (cfv (cfv U cringcALTV) cbs)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ srhmsubcALTV . (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem srhmsubcALTVlem2 {s1 r: setvar} (C: class) (S: class s1) (U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V X Y: class):
  $ wral r (wcel (cv r) crg) S $ >
  $ wceq C (cin U S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel U V) (wa (wcel X C) (wcel Y C))) (wceq (co X Y J) (co X Y (cfv (cfv U cringcALTV) chom))) $;

--| According to ~ df-subc , the subcategories ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` of a category ` C ` are subsets of the homomorphisms of ` C ` (see ~ subcssc and ~ subcss2 ). Therefore, the set of special ring homomorphisms (i.e., ring homomorphisms from a special ring to another ring of that kind) is a subcategory of the category of (unital) rings. (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem srhmsubcALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C: class) (S: class s1) (U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wral r (wcel (cv r) crg) S $ >
  $ wceq C (cin U S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel J (cfv (cfv U cringcALTV) csubc)) $;

--| The restriction of the category of (unital) rings to the set of special ring homomorphisms is a category. (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem sringcatALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C: class) (S: class s1) (U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wral r (wcel (cv r) crg) S $ >
  $ wceq C (cin U S) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel (co (cfv U cringcALTV) J cresc) ccat) $;

--| According to ~ df-subc , the subcategories ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` of a category ` C ` are subsets of the homomorphisms of ` C ` (see ~ subcssc and ~ subcss2 ). Therefore, the set of commutative ring homomorphisms (i.e. ring homomorphisms from a commutative ring to a commutative ring) is a "subcategory" of the category of (unital) rings. (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem crhmsubcALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U ccrg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel J (cfv (cfv U cringcALTV) csubc)) $;

--| The restriction of the category of (unital) rings to the set of commutative ring homomorphisms is a category, the "category of commutative rings". (Contributed by AV, 19-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem cringcatALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U ccrg) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel (co (cfv U cringcALTV) J cresc) ccat) $;

--| According to ~ df-subc , the subcategories ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` of a category ` C ` are subsets of the homomorphisms of ` C ` (see ~ subcssc and ~ subcss2 ). Therefore, the set of division ring homomorphisms is a "subcategory" of the category of (unital) rings. (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drhmsubcALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U cdr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel J (cfv (cfv U cringcALTV) csubc)) $;

--| The restriction of the category of (unital) rings to the set of division ring homomorphisms is a category, the "category of division rings". (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem drngcatALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C U: class) (J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U cdr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel (co (cfv U cringcALTV) J cresc) ccat) $;

--| The restriction of the category of (unital) rings to the set of field homomorphisms is a category, the "category of fields". (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fldcatALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C D U: class) (F J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U cdr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cin U cfield) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo r s1 D D (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel (co (cfv U cringcALTV) F cresc) ccat) $;

--| The restriction of the category of division rings to the set of field homomorphisms is a category, the "category of fields". (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fldcALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C D U: class) (F J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U cdr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cin U cfield) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo r s1 D D (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel (co (co (cfv U cringcALTV) J cresc) F cresc) ccat) $;

--| According to ~ df-subc , the subcategories ` ( Subcat `` C ) ` of a category ` C ` are subsets of the homomorphisms of ` C ` (see ~ subcssc and ~ subcss2 ). Therefore, the set of field homomorphisms is a "subcategory" of the category of division rings. (Contributed by AV, 20-Feb-2020.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem fldhmsubcALTV {s1 r: setvar} (C D U: class) (F J: class s1 r) (V: class):
  $ wceq C (cin U cdr) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpo r s1 C C (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wceq D (cin U cfield) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo r s1 D D (co (cv r) (cv s1) crh)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel U V) (wcel F (cfv (co (cfv U cringcALTV) J cresc) csubc)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of prime rings.
term cprmrng: class;

--| Define the class of prime rings. A ring is prime if the zero ideal is a prime ideal. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2026.)
axiom df_prmring {r: setvar}:
  $ wceq cprmrng (crab r (wcel (csn (cfv (cv r) c0g)) (cfv (cv r) cprmidl)) crg) $;

--| The predicate "is a prime ring". (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2026.)
theorem isprmrng (P R c_0: class):
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cprmidl) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cprmrng) (wa (wcel R crg) (wcel (csn c_0) P)) $;

--| A prime ring is a nonzero ring. (Contributed by AV, 26-Jun-2026.)
theorem prmringnzring (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cprmrng) (wcel R cnzr) $;

--| A prime ring is a ring. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2026.) (Proof shortened by AV, 26-Jun-2026.)
theorem prmrngring (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cprmrng) (wcel R crg) $;

--| A simple ring (a nonzero ring whose only ideals are ` .0. ` and ` R ` ) is a prime ring. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jan-2011.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2026.)
theorem smprngprmrng (B2 R U c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R clidl) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cnzr) (wceq U (cpr (csn c_0) B2))) (wcel R cprmrng) $;

--| A division ring is a prime ring. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jan-2011.) (Revised by AV, 18-Jun-2026.)
theorem drngprmrng (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R cdr) (wcel R cprmrng) $;

--| A commutative ring is a prime ring if and only if it is an integral domain. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2026.)
theorem crngprmringidom (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wb (wcel R cprmrng) (wcel R cidom)) $;

--| A commutative ring is a prime ring if and only if it is a domain. (Contributed by AV, 27-Jun-2026.)
theorem crngprmringdom (R: class):
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wb (wcel R cprmrng) (wcel R cdomn)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the class of integral domains. An integral domain is a commutative prime ring. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 27-Jun-2026.)
theorem dfidom2:
  $ wceq cidom (cin cprmrng ccrg) $;

--| The predicate "is an integral domain": An integral domain is a commutative prime ring. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 27-Jun-2026.)
theorem isidom2 (R: class):
  $ wb (wcel R cidom) (wa (wcel R cprmrng) (wcel R ccrg)) $;

--| The predicate "is a domain", alternate expression. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2026.)
theorem isidom3 {a b: setvar} (B2 R c_x: class) (c_1: class a b) (c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wb (wcel R cidom) (w3a (wcel R ccrg) (wne c_0 c_1) (wral a (wral b (wi (wceq (co (cv a) (cv b) c_x) c_0) (wo (wceq (cv a) c_0) (wceq (cv b) c_0))) B2) B2)) $;

--| A domain has no zero-divisors (besides zero). (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2010.) (Revised by AV, 12-Jul-2026.)
theorem idomnzd (B2 R c_x X Y c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel R cidom) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wceq (co X Y c_x) c_0))) (wo (wceq X c_0) (wceq Y c_0)) $;

--| Cancellation law for domains. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jan-2011.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jul-2026.)
theorem idomcanl (B2 R c_x X Y c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cidom) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wne X c_0)) (wi (wceq (co X Y c_x) (co X Z c_x)) (wceq Y Z)) $;

--| Cancellation law for domains. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jan-2011.) (Revised by AV, 13-Jul-2026.)
theorem idomcanr (B2 R c_x X Y c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel R cidom) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel Z B2))) (wne Z c_0)) (wi (wceq (co X Z c_x) (co Y Z c_x)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Membership in a union of Cartesian products over its second component, analogous to ~ eliunxp . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem eliunxp2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class y1) (C: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (ciun y1 B2 (cxp A2 (csn (cv y1))))) (wex x3 (wex y1 (wa (wceq C (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (cv y1) B2))))) $;

--| Express a two-argument function as a one-argument function, or vice-versa. In this version ` A ( y ) ` is not assumed to be constant w.r.t ` y ` , analogous to ~ mpomptx . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem mpomptx2 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2: class y1) (C: class z) (D: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv z) (cop (cv x3) (cv y1))) (wceq C D) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpt z (ciun y1 B2 (cxp A2 (csn (cv y1)))) C) (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 D) $;

--| Rule to change the bound variable in a maps-to function, using implicit substitution. This version of ~ cbvmpo allows ` A ` to be a function of ` y ` , analogous to ~ cbvmpox . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem cbvmpox2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1 z) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1 z w) (D: class y1 z w) (E: class x3 y1 z w):
  $ wnfc z A2 $ >
  $ wnfc y1 D $ >
  $ wnfc z C $ >
  $ wnfc w C $ >
  $ wnfc x3 E $ >
  $ wnfc y1 E $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wceq A2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv y1) (cv z)) (wceq (cv x3) (cv w))) (wceq C E) $ >
  $ wceq (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) (cmpo w z D B2 E) $;

--| The domain of a mapping is a subset of its base classes expressed as union of Cartesian products over its second component, analogous to ~ dmmpossx . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem dmmpossx2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (C F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wss (cdm F) (ciun y1 B2 (cxp A2 (csn (cv y1)))) $;

--| Existence of an operation class abstraction (version for dependent domains, i.e. the first base class may depend on the second base class), analogous to ~ mpoexxg . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem mpoexxg2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class y1) (B2: class) (C R S F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 A2 B2 C) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 R) (wral y1 (wcel A2 S) B2)) (wcel F cvv) $;

--| Value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, deduction form, with substitution of second argument, analogous to ~ ovmpodxf . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem ovmpordxf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (A2: class y1) (B2: class x3) (C D R S F L X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 C D R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) B2))) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv y1) B2)) (wceq C L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S X) $ >
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnfc y1 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 S $ >
  $ wnfc y1 S $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 F) S) $;

--| Value of an operation given by a maps-to rule, deduction form, with substitution of second argument, analogous to ~ ovmpodxf . (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem ovmpordx {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F L X: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 C D R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) B2))) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wceq (cv y1) B2)) (wceq C L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 L) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 B2 F) S) $;

--| The value of an operation class abstraction. Variant of ~ ovmpoga which does not require ` D ` and ` x ` to be distinct. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 10-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Dec-2013.)
theorem ovmpox2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (D: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (F: class x3 y1) (H L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wceq (cv y1) B2)) (wceq R S) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv y1) B2) (wceq C L) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 C D R) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 L) (wcel B2 D) (wcel S H)) (wceq (co A2 B2 F) S) $;

--| The restriction of a conditional mapping to function values of a function having a domain which is a difference with a singleton equals this function. (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2019.)
theorem fdmdifeqresdif {x3: setvar} (D R: class) (F: class x3) (G: class) (X: class x3) (Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 D (cif (wceq (cv x3) Y) X (cfv (cv x3) G))) $ >
  $ wi (wf (cdif D (csn Y)) R G) (wceq G (cres F (cdif D (csn Y)))) $;

--| The function operation applied to the addition for functions (with the same domain) into a monoid is a function (with the same domain) into the monoid. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2019.)
theorem ofaddmndmap (A2 B2 c_pl R M V Y: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cmnd) (wcel V Y) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel B2 (co R V cmap)))) (wcel (co A2 B2 (cof c_pl)) (co R V cmap)) $;

--| A singleton of an ordered pair as an element of the mapping operation. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem mapsnop (R F V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (csn (cop X Y)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel Y R) (wcel R W)) (wcel F (co R (csn X) cmap)) $;

--| A function with a domain of two elements as element of the mapping operator applied to a pair. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2024.)
theorem fprmappr (A2 B2 C D U V W X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (w3a (wcel A2 U) (wcel B2 W) (wne A2 B2)) (wa (wcel C X) (wcel D X))) (wcel (cpr (cop A2 C) (cop B2 D)) (co X (cpr A2 B2) cmap)) $;

--| An unordered pair containing two ordered pairs as an element of the mapping operation. (Contributed by AV, 16-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 2-Jun-2024.)
theorem mapprop (A2 B2 R F V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop X A2) (cop Y B2)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel X V) (wcel A2 R)) (wa (wcel Y V) (wcel B2 R)) (wa (wne X Y) (wcel R W))) (wcel F (co R (cpr X Y) cmap)) $;

--| A prime is not an integer multiple of another prime. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2019.)
theorem ztprmneprm (A2 B2 Z: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel Z cz) (wcel A2 cprime) (wcel B2 cprime)) (wi (wceq (co Z A2 cmul) B2) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| 2 times 6 minus 3 times 4 equals 0. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.)
theorem _2t6m3t4e0:
  $ wceq (co (co c2 c6 cmul) (co c3 c4 cmul) cmin) cc0 $;

--| For any finite subset of ` NN0 ` , find a superset in the form of a set of sequential integers, analogous to ~ ssnnssfz . (Contributed by AV, 30-Sep-2019.)
theorem ssnn0ssfz {n: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cin (cpw cn0) cfn)) (wrex n (wss A2 (co cc0 (cv n) cfz)) cn0) $;

--| If the sum of two nonnegative integers is less than a third integer, then one of the summands is already less than this third integer. (Contributed by AV, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem nn0sumltlt (a b c: setvar):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv a) cn0) (wcel (cv b) cn0) (wcel (cv c) cn0)) (wi (wbr (co (cv a) (cv b) caddc) (cv c) clt) (wbr (cv b) (cv c) clt)) $;

--| Pascal's rule for the binomial coefficient, generalized to all integers ` K ` , shifted down by 1. (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2019.)
theorem bcpascm1 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (co (co (co N c1 cmin) K cbc) (co (co N c1 cmin) (co K c1 cmin) cbc) caddc) (co N K cbc)) $;

--| The sum of binomial coefficients for a fixed positive ` N ` with alternating signs is zero. Notice that this is not valid for ` N = 0 ` (since ` ( ( -u 1 ^ 0 ) x. ( 0 _C 0 ) ) = ( 1 x. 1 ) = 1 ` ). For a proof using Pascal's rule ( ~ bcpascm1 ) instead of the binomial theorem ( ~ binom ), see ~ altgsumbcALT . (Contributed by AV, 13-Sep-2019.)
theorem altgsumbc {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cneg c1) (cv k) cexp) (co N (cv k) cbc) cmul)) cc0) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ altgsumbc , using Pascal's rule ( ~ bcpascm1 ) instead of the binomial theorem ( ~ binom ). (Contributed by AV, 8-Sep-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem altgsumbcALT {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (csu k (co cc0 N cfz) (co (co (cneg c1) (cv k) cexp) (co N (cv k) cbc) cmul)) cc0) $;

--| The ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` is a (left) module with the ring of integers as base set. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzlmod (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wa (wcel Z clmod) (wceq czring (cfv Z csca)) $;

--| An element of the (base set of the) ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` . (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzel (A2 B2 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wcel (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 B2)) (cfv Z cbs)) $;

--| The ` 0 ` of the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxz0 (c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv Z c0g) $;

--| The scalar multiplication of the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzscm (A2 B2 C c_xb Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Z cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz)) (wceq (co A2 (cpr (cop cc0 B2) (cop c1 C)) c_xb) (cpr (cop cc0 (co A2 B2 cmul)) (cop c1 (co A2 C cmul)))) $;

--| The addition of the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzadd (A2 B2 C D c_pl Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Z cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel C cz) (wcel D cz))) (wceq (co (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 C)) (cpr (cop cc0 B2) (cop c1 D)) c_pl) (cpr (cop cc0 (co A2 B2 caddc)) (cop c1 (co C D caddc)))) $;

--| The subtraction of the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` expressed as addition. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzsubm (A2 B2 C D c_mi Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Z csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel C cz) (wcel D cz))) (wceq (co (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 C)) (cpr (cop cc0 B2) (cop c1 D)) c_mi) (co (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 C)) (co (cneg c1) (cpr (cop cc0 B2) (cop c1 D)) (cfv Z cvsca)) (cfv Z cplusg))) $;

--| The subtraction of the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzsub (A2 B2 C D c_mi Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Z csg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wa (wcel C cz) (wcel D cz))) (wceq (co (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 C)) (cpr (cop cc0 B2) (cop c1 D)) c_mi) (cpr (cop cc0 (co A2 B2 cmin)) (cop c1 (co C D cmin)))) $;

--| Extract a summand/factor from the group sum for the multiplicative group of a unital ring. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2018.)
theorem mgpsumunsn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (R: class) (c_x: class k) (I M N X: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) N)) (wcel A2 (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) I) (wceq A2 X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M (cmpt k N A2) cgsu) (co (co M (cmpt k (cdif N (csn I)) A2) cgsu) X c_x)) $;

--| If the group sum for the multiplicative group of a unital ring contains a summand/factor that is the zero of the ring, the group sum itself is zero. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2018.)
theorem mgpsumz {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (R: class) (c_x: class k) (I M N c_0: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) N)) (wcel A2 (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) I) (wceq A2 c_0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M (cmpt k N A2) cgsu) c_0) $;

--| If the group sum for the multiplicative group of a unital ring contains a summand/factor that is the one of the ring, this summand/ factor can be removed from the group sum. (Contributed by AV, 29-Dec-2018.)
theorem mgpsumn {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class k) (R: class) (c_x: class k) (c_1 I M N: class):
  $ wceq M (cfv R cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cfn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel I N) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) N)) (wcel A2 (cfv R cbs)) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv k) I) (wceq A2 c_1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co M (cmpt k N A2) cgsu) (co M (cmpt k (cdif N (csn I)) A2) cgsu)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer to the power of itself is less than 6 if it is less than or equal to 2. (Contributed by AV, 16-Mar-2019.)
theorem exple2lt6 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wbr N c2 cle)) (wbr (co N N cexp) c6 clt) $;

--| Every symmetric group on a set with at most 2 elements is abelian. (Contributed by AV, 16-Mar-2019.)
theorem pgrple2abl (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wbr (cfv A2 chash) c2 cle)) (wcel G cabl) $;

--| Every symmetric group on a set with more than 2 elements is not abelian, see also the remark in [Rotman] p. 28. (Contributed by AV, 21-Mar-2019.)
theorem pgrpgt2nabl (A2 G V: class):
  $ wceq G (cfv A2 csymg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wbr c2 (cfv A2 chash) clt)) (wnel G cabl) $;

--| Identity for a multiplication with additive and multiplicative inverses in a ring. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2018.)
theorem invginvrid (B2 R c_x U I N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y U)) (wceq (co (cfv Y N) (co (cfv (cfv Y N) I) X c_x) c_x) X) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a multiplication of zero with a function into a ring is empty. (Contributed by AV, 10-Apr-2019.)
theorem rmsupp0 {v: setvar} (A2 C R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M crg) (wcel V X) (wceq C (cfv M c0g))) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap))) (wceq (co (cmpt v V (co C (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv M cmulr))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) c0) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a multiplication of a nonzero constant with a function into a (ring theoretic) domain equals the support of the function. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2019.)
theorem domnmsuppn0 {v: setvar} (A2 C R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M cdomn) (wcel V X)) (wa (wcel C R) (wne C (cfv M c0g))) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap))) (wceq (co (cmpt v V (co C (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv M cmulr))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) (co A2 (cfv M c0g) csupp)) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a multiplication of a constant with a function into a ring is a subset of the support of the function. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2019.)
theorem rmsuppss {v: setvar} (A2 C R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M crg) (wcel V X) (wcel C R)) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap))) (wss (co (cmpt v V (co C (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv M cmulr))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) (co A2 (cfv M c0g) csupp)) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is a subset of the support of the function of scalars. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.)
theorem scmsuppss {v: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class v) (M V: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs))) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap))) (wss (co (cmpt v V (co (cfv (cv v) A2) (cv v) (cfv M cvsca))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) (co A2 (cfv S c0g) csupp)) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a multiplication of a constant with a function into a ring is finite if the support of the function is finite. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2019.)
theorem rmsuppfi {v: setvar} (A2 C R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel M crg) (wcel V X) (wcel C R)) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel (co A2 (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn)) (wcel (co (cmpt v V (co C (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv M cmulr))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn) $;

--| A mapping of a multiplication of a constant with a function into a ring is finitely supported if the function is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2019.)
theorem rmfsupp {v: setvar} (A2 C R M V X: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel M crg) (wcel V X) (wcel C R)) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wbr A2 (cfv M c0g) cfsupp)) (wbr (cmpt v V (co C (cfv (cv v) A2) (cfv M cmulr))) (cfv M c0g) cfsupp) $;

--| The support of a mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is finite if the support of the function of scalars is finite. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.)
theorem scmsuppfi {v: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class v) (M V: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel (co A2 (cfv S c0g) csupp) cfn)) (wcel (co (cmpt v V (co (cfv (cv v) A2) (cv v) (cfv M cvsca))) (cfv M c0g) csupp) cfn) $;

--| A mapping of a scalar multiplication with a function of scalars is finitely supported if the function of scalars is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2019.)
theorem scmfsupp {v: setvar} (A2 R: class) (S: class v) (M V: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wbr A2 (cfv S c0g) cfsupp)) (wbr (cmpt v V (co (cfv (cv v) A2) (cv v) (cfv M cvsca))) (cfv M c0g) cfsupp) $;

--| The support of a mapping with value 0 except of one is finite. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem suppmptcfin {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class x3) (c_1 F M V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cif (wceq (cv x3) X) c_1 c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2)) (wcel X V)) (wcel (co F c_0 csupp) cfn) $;

--| A mapping with value 0 except of one is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 9-Jun-2019.)
theorem mptcfsupp {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class x3) (c_1 F M V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cif (wceq (cv x3) X) c_1 c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2)) (wcel X V)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| A mapping with a finite domain is finitely supported. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2019.)
theorem fsuppmptdmf {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V: class) (W Y Z: class x3):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 A2 Y) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cfn) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) A2)) (wcel Y V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F Z cfsupp) $;

--| Multiple distributive law for scalar product (left-distributivity). (Contributed by AV, 5-Sep-2019.)
theorem lmodvsmdi (R c_x E c_ex F K N V W X: class):
  $ wceq V (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv F cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv W cmg) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv F cmg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W clmod) (w3a (wcel R K) (wcel N cn0) (wcel X V))) (wceq (co R (co N X c_ex) c_x) (co (co N R E) X c_x)) $;

--| Closure of a group sum in a linear subspace: A (finitely supported) sum of scalar multiplications of vectors of a subset of a linear subspace is also contained in the linear subspace. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem gsumlsscl {v: setvar} (B2 R S F M V Z: class):
  $ wceq S (cfv M clss) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel Z S) (wss V Z)) (wi (wa (wcel F (co B2 V cmap)) (wbr F (cfv R c0g) cfsupp)) (wcel (co M (cmpt v V (co (cfv (cv v) F) (cv v) (cfv M cvsca))) cgsu) Z)) $;

--| The scalar 0 embedded into an associative algebra corresponds to the 0 of the associative algebra. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2019.)
theorem assaascl0 (ph: wff) (A2 F W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv W cascl) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W casa) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv F c0g) A2) (cfv W c0g)) $;

--| The scalar 1 embedded into an associative algebra corresponds to the 1 of the an associative algebra. (Contributed by AV, 31-Jul-2019.)
theorem assaascl1 (ph: wff) (A2 F W: class):
  $ wceq A2 (cfv W cascl) $ >
  $ wceq F (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel W casa) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv F cur) A2) (cfv W cur)) $;

--| The variable in a polynomial expressed as scaled monomial. (Contributed by AV, 12-Aug-2019.)
theorem ply1vr1smo (P R c_x c_1 c_ex G X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv G cmg) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R crg) (wceq (co c_1 (co c1 X c_ex) c_x) X) $;

--| The ring multiplication of a polynomial with a scalar polynomial is equal to the scalar multiplication of the polynomial with the corresponding scalar. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2019.)
theorem ply1sclrmsm (A2 P R c_x c_xp E c_ex F K N X Z: class):
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv N cmg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel F K) (wcel Z E)) (wceq (co (cfv F A2) Z c_xp) (co F Z c_x)) $;

--| The value of the coefficient vector of a polynomial multiplied on the left by a scalar. (Contributed by AV, 14-Aug-2019.)
theorem coe1sclmulval (A2 B2 P R S c_xb c_x K N Y Z: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wa (wcel Y K) (wcel Z B2)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv N (cfv (co Y Z S) cco1)) (co Y (cfv N (cfv Z cco1)) c_x)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ ply1mulgsum . (Contributed by AV, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem ply1mulgsumlem1 {n s1: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class n s1) (R: class) (c_x c_xp c_ex c_as: class n s1) (K L: class) (M X: class n s1):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv L cco1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv M cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel K B2) (wcel L B2)) (wrex s1 (wral n (wi (wbr (cv s1) (cv n) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv n) A2) (cfv R c0g)) (wceq (cfv (cv n) C) (cfv R c0g)))) cn0) cn0) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ ply1mulgsum . (Contributed by AV, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem ply1mulgsumlem2 {n s1 l: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class n s1 l) (R: class) (c_x c_xp c_ex: class n s1 l) (c_as: class n l) (K L: class) (M X: class n s1 l):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv L cco1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv M cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel K B2) (wcel L B2)) (wrex s1 (wral n (wi (wbr (cv s1) (cv n) clt) (wceq (co R (cmpt l (co cc0 (cv n) cfz) (co (cfv (cv l) A2) (cfv (co (cv n) (cv l) cmin) C) c_as)) cgsu) (cfv R c0g))) cn0) cn0) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ ply1mulgsum . (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2019.)
theorem ply1mulgsumlem3 {k l: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class k l) (R: class) (c_x c_xp c_ex: class k l) (c_as: class l) (K L: class) (M X: class k l):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv L cco1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv M cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel K B2) (wcel L B2)) (wbr (cmpt k cn0 (co R (cmpt l (co cc0 (cv k) cfz) (co (cfv (cv l) A2) (cfv (co (cv k) (cv l) cmin) C) c_as)) cgsu)) (cfv R c0g) cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ ply1mulgsum . (Contributed by AV, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem ply1mulgsumlem4 {k l: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class k l) (R: class) (c_x: class l) (c_xp: class k l) (c_ex c_as: class l) (K L: class) (M: class k l) (X: class l):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv L cco1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv M cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel K B2) (wcel L B2)) (wbr (cmpt k cn0 (co (co R (cmpt l (co cc0 (cv k) cfz) (co (cfv (cv l) A2) (cfv (co (cv k) (cv l) cmin) C) c_as)) cgsu) (co (cv k) X c_ex) c_x)) (cfv P c0g) cfsupp) $;

--| The product of two polynomials expressed as group sum of scaled monomials. (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2019.)
theorem ply1mulgsum {k l: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class l) (R: class) (c_x: class l) (c_xp: class k l) (c_ex: class l) (c_as K L: class) (M: class k l) (X: class l):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv K cco1) $ >
  $ wceq C (cfv L cco1) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_xp (cfv P cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv P cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_as (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq M (cfv P cmgp) $ >
  $ wceq c_ex (cfv M cmg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel R crg) (wcel K B2) (wcel L B2)) (wceq (co K L c_xp) (co P (cmpt k cn0 (co (co R (cmpt l (co cc0 (cv k) cfz) (co (cfv (cv l) A2) (cfv (co (cv k) (cv l) cmin) C) c_as)) cgsu) (co (cv k) X c_ex) c_x)) cgsu)) $;

--| Polynomial evaluation for the 0 scalar. (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2019.)
theorem evl1at0 (P R O c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv P c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wceq (cfv c_0 (cfv Z O)) c_0) $;

--| Polynomial evaluation for the 1 scalar. (Contributed by AV, 10-Aug-2019.)
theorem evl1at1 (P R c_1 I O: class):
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv P cur) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R ccrg) (wceq (cfv c_1 (cfv I O)) c_1) $;

--| A term of the form ` x - C ` is a (univariate) polynomial, also called "linear polynomial". (Part of ~ ply1remlem ). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem linply1 (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C P R G K c_mi X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv C A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R crg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G B2) $;

--| A term of the form ` x - C ` evaluated for ` x = V ` results in ` V - C ` (part of ~ ply1remlem ). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem lineval (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C P R G K c_mi O V X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv R cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv R cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv C A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C K) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv R ce1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel R ccrg) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel V K) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv V (cfv G O)) (co V C (cfv R csg))) $;

--| The polynomial ` x - 3 ` over ` ZZ ` evaluated for ` x = 5 ` results in 2. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jul-2019.)
theorem linevalexample (A2 B2 P G c_mi O X: class):
  $ wceq P (cfv czring cpl1) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv P cbs) $ >
  $ wceq X (cfv czring cv1) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv P csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cfv P cascl) $ >
  $ wceq G (co X (cfv c3 A2) c_mi) $ >
  $ wceq O (cfv czring ce1) $ >
  $ wceq (cfv c5 (cfv (co X (cfv c3 A2) c_mi) O)) c2 $;

--| Alternative notation for the algebra of diagonal matrices.
term cdmatalt: class;

--| Alternative notation for the algebra of scalar matrices.
term cscmatalt: class;

--| Define the set of n x n diagonal (square) matrices over a set (usually a ring) r, see definition in [Roman] p. 4 or Definition 3.12 in [Hefferon] p. 240. (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
axiom df_dmatalt {i j m n r a: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdmatalt (cmpo n r cfn cvv (csb a (co (cv n) (cv r) cmat) (co (cv a) (crab m (wral i (wral j (wi (wne (cv i) (cv j)) (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) (cv m)) (cfv (cv r) c0g))) (cv n)) (cv n)) (cfv (cv a) cbs)) cress))) $;

--| Define the algebra of n x n scalar matrices over a set (usually a ring) r, see definition in [Connell] p. 57: "A _scalar matrix_ is a diagonal matrix for which all the diagonal terms are equal, i.e., a matrix of the form cI_n". (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
axiom df_scmatalt {i j m n r a c: setvar}:
  $ wceq cscmatalt (cmpo n r cfn cvv (csb a (co (cv n) (cv r) cmat) (co (cv a) (crab m (wrex c (wral i (wral j (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) (cv m)) (cif (wceq (cv i) (cv j)) (cv c) (cfv (cv r) c0g))) (cv n)) (cv n)) (cfv (cv r) cbs)) (cfv (cv a) cbs)) cress))) $;

--| The algebra of ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over a ring ` R ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem dmatALTval {i j m: setvar} (A2: class i j m) (B2: class i j) (D: class i j m) (R N: class) (c_0: class i j m):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cdmatalt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel R cvv)) (wceq D (co A2 (crab m (wral i (wral j (wi (wne (cv i) (cv j)) (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) (cv m)) c_0)) N) N) B2) cress)) $;

--| The base set of the algebra of ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over a ring ` R ` , i.e. the set of all ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over the ring ` R ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem dmatALTbas {i j m: setvar} (A2: class i j m) (B2: class i j) (D: class i j m) (R N: class) (c_0: class i j m):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cdmatalt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel R cvv)) (wceq (cfv D cbs) (crab m (wral i (wral j (wi (wne (cv i) (cv j)) (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) (cv m)) c_0)) N) N) B2)) $;

--| An element of the base set of the algebra of ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over a ring ` R ` , i.e. an ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrix over the ring ` R ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem dmatALTbasel {i j: setvar} (A2 B2 D: class i j) (R M N: class) (c_0: class i j):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cdmatalt) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel R cvv)) (wb (wcel M (cfv D cbs)) (wa (wcel M B2) (wral i (wral j (wi (wne (cv i) (cv j)) (wceq (co (cv i) (cv j) M) c_0)) N) N))) $;

--| The set of all ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over (the ring) ` R ` is the base set of the algebra of ` N ` x ` N ` diagonal matrices over (the ring) ` R ` . (Contributed by AV, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem dmatbas (A2 B2 D R N V c_0: class):
  $ wceq A2 (co N R cmat) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv A2 cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq D (co N R cdmat) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cfn) (wcel R V)) (wceq D (cfv (co N R cdmatalt) cbs)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the operation constructing a linear combination (of vectors from a left module).
term clinc: class;

--| Extend class notation with the operation constructing a set of linear combinations (of vectors from a left module) with finite support.
term clinco: class;

--| Define the operation constructing a linear combination. Although this definition is taylored for linear combinations of vectors from left modules, it can be used for any structure having a ` Base ` , ` Scalar ` s and a scalar multiplication ` .s ` . (Contributed by AV, 29-Mar-2019.)
axiom df_linc {x3 v m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clinc (cmpt m cvv (cmpo s1 v (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cv v) cmap) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs)) (co (cv m) (cmpt x3 (cv v) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) cvsca))) cgsu))) $;

--| Define the operation constructing the set of all linear combinations for a set of vectors. (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
axiom df_lco {v m s1 c: setvar}:
  $ wceq clinco (cmpo m v cvv (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs)) (crab c (wrex s1 (wa (wbr (cv s1) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (cv c) (co (cv s1) (cv v) (cfv (cv m) clinc)))) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cv v) cmap)) (cfv (cv m) cbs))) $;

--| A linear combination as operation. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem lincop {x3 v s1: setvar} (M: class) (X: class x3 s1):
  $ wi (wcel M X) (wceq (cfv M clinc) (cmpo s1 v (co (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) (cv v) cmap) (cpw (cfv M cbs)) (co M (cmpt x3 (cv v) (co (cfv (cv x3) (cv s1)) (cv x3) (cfv M cvsca))) cgsu))) $;

--| The value of a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 30-Mar-2019.)
theorem lincval {x3: setvar} (S M V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M X) (wcel S (co (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) V cmap)) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wceq (co S V (cfv M clinc)) (co M (cmpt x3 V (co (cfv (cv x3) S) (cv x3) (cfv M cvsca))) cgsu)) $;

--| Alternative definition of linear combinations using the function operation. (Contributed by AV, 1-Apr-2019.)
theorem dflinc2 {v m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clinc (cmpt m cvv (cmpo s1 v (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cv v) cmap) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs)) (co (cv m) (co (cv s1) (cres cid (cv v)) (cof (cfv (cv m) cvsca))) cgsu))) $;

--| A linear combination as operation. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcoop {s1 c: setvar} (B2: class s1) (R: class) (S: class s1 c) (M V: class) (X: class s1 c):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M X) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wceq (co M V clinco) (crab c (wrex s1 (wa (wbr (cv s1) (cfv S c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (cv c) (co (cv s1) V (cfv M clinc)))) (co R V cmap)) B2)) $;

--| The value of a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 5-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcoval {s1: setvar} (B2: class s1) (C R: class) (S: class s1) (M V: class) (X: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M X) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wb (wcel C (co M V clinco)) (wa (wcel C B2) (wrex s1 (wa (wbr (cv s1) (cfv S c0g) cfsupp) (wceq C (co (cv s1) V (cfv M clinc)))) (co R V cmap)))) $;

--| A linear combination of vectors (with finite support) is a vector. (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincfsuppcl (B2 R S F M V W c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wa (wcel V W) (wss V B2)) (wa (wcel F (co S V cmap)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp))) (wcel (co F V (cfv M clinc)) B2) $;

--| A linear combination of vectors is a vector. (Contributed by AV, 31-Mar-2019.)
theorem linccl (B2 R S M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (w3a (wcel V cfn) (wss V B2) (wcel S (co R V cmap)))) (wcel (co S V (cfv M clinc)) B2) $;

--| The value of an empty linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincval0 (M X: class):
  $ wi (wcel M X) (wceq (co c0 c0 (cfv M clinc)) (cfv M c0g)) $;

--| The linear combination over a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.)
theorem lincvalsng (B2 R S c_x M V Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V B2) (wcel Y R)) (wceq (co (csn (cop V Y)) (csn V) (cfv M clinc)) (co Y V c_x)) $;

--| The linear combination over a singleton. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 25-May-2019.)
theorem lincvalsn (B2 R S c_x F M V Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq F (csn (cop V Y)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V B2) (wcel Y R)) (wceq (co F (csn V) (cfv M clinc)) (co Y V c_x)) $;

--| The linear combination over an unordered pair. (Contributed by AV, 16-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincvalpr (B2 c_pl R S c_x F M V W X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop V X) (cop W Y)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wne V W)) (wa (wcel V B2) (wcel X R)) (wa (wcel W B2) (wcel Y R))) (wceq (co F (cpr V W) (cfv M clinc)) (co (co X V c_x) (co Y W c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| The linear combination over a singleton mapping to 0. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincval1 (B2 R S F M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (csn (cop V (cfv S c0g))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V B2)) (wceq (co F (csn V) (cfv M clinc)) (cfv M c0g)) $;

--| Properties of a linear combination over a singleton mapping to 0. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcosn0 (B2 R S F M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (csn (cop V (cfv S c0g))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V B2)) (w3a (wcel F (co R (csn V) cmap)) (wbr F (cfv S c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (co F (csn V) (cfv M clinc)) (cfv M c0g))) $;

--| The linear combination where all scalars are 0. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincvalsc0 {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (S F: class x3) (M V c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V c_0) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wceq (co F V (cfv M clinc)) Z) $;

--| Properties of a linear combination where all scalars are 0. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcoc0 {x3: setvar} (B2 R: class) (S: class x3) (F M V c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V c_0) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (w3a (wcel F (co R V cmap)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co F V (cfv M clinc)) Z)) $;

--| If a set contains the zero element of a module, there is a linear combination being 0 where not all scalars are 0. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.)
theorem linc0scn0 {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (S: class x3) (c_1: class) (F: class x3) (M V c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cif (wceq (cv x3) Z) c_1 c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wceq (co F V (cfv M clinc)) Z) $;

--| A vector is a linear combination of a set containing this vector. (Contributed by AV, 21-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincdifsn (B2 c_pl R S c_x F G M V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2)) (wcel X V)) (wa (wcel F (co S V cmap)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp)) (wceq G (cres F (cdif V (csn X))))) (wceq (co F V (cfv M clinc)) (co (co G (cdif V (csn X)) (cfv M clinc)) (co (cfv X F) X c_x) c_pl)) $;

--| A vector is a linear combination of a set containing this vector. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem linc1 {x3: setvar} (B2: class) (S: class x3) (c_1: class) (F: class x3) (M V X c_0: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv S c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (cif (wceq (cv x3) X) c_1 c_0)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2)) (wcel X V)) (wceq (co F V (cfv M clinc)) X) $;

--| A linear combination of a subset of a linear subspace is also contained in the linear subspace. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincellss (S F M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cfv M clss)) (wss V S)) (wi (wa (wcel F (co (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) V cmap)) (wbr F (cfv (cfv M csca) c0g) cfsupp)) (wcel (co F V (cfv M clinc)) S)) $;

--| The set of empty linear combinations over a monoid is the singleton with the identity element of the monoid. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem lco0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cmnd) (wceq (co M c0 clinco) (csn (cfv M c0g))) $;

--| The zero vector is always a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcoel0 (M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wcel (cfv M c0g) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| The sum of two linear combinations is a linear combination, see also the proof in [Lang] p. 129. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincsum (A2 B2 c_pl c_pb R S M V X Y: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq X (co A2 V (cfv M clinc)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (co B2 V (cfv M clinc)) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pb (cfv S cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel B2 (co R V cmap))) (wa (wbr A2 (cfv S c0g) cfsupp) (wbr B2 (cfv S c0g) cfsupp))) (wceq (co X Y c_pl) (co (co A2 B2 (cof c_pb)) V (cfv M clinc))) $;

--| A linear combinations multiplied with a scalar is a linear combination, see also the proof in [Lang] p. 129. (Contributed by AV, 9-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincscm {x3: setvar} (A2 R S: class) (c_xb: class x3) (c_x: class) (F: class x3) (M V: class) (X: class x3):
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv (cfv M csca) cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq X (co A2 V (cfv M clinc)) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 V (co S (cfv (cv x3) A2) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wa (wcel A2 (co R V cmap)) (wcel S R)) (wbr A2 (cfv (cfv M csca) c0g) cfsupp)) (wceq (co S X c_xb) (co F V (cfv M clinc))) $;

--| The sum of two linear combinations is a linear combination, see also the proof in [Lang] p. 129. (Contributed by AV, 4-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincsumcl (C D c_pl M V: class):
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wa (wcel C (co M V clinco)) (wcel D (co M V clinco)))) (wcel (co C D c_pl) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| The multiplication of a linear combination with a scalar is a linear combination, see also the proof in [Lang] p. 129. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 28-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincscmcl (C D R c_x M V: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wcel C R) (wcel D (co M V clinco))) (wcel (co C D c_x) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| The sum of a linear combination and a multiplication of a linear combination with a scalar is a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 11-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincsumscmcl (B2 C D c_pl R c_x M V: class):
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv M cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (w3a (wcel C R) (wcel D (co M V clinco)) (wcel B2 (co M V clinco)))) (wcel (co (co C D c_x) B2 c_pl) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| According to the statement in [Lang] p. 129, the set ` ( LSubSp `` M ) ` of all linear combinations of a set of vectors V is a submodule (_generated_ by V) of the module M. The elements of V are called _generators_ of ` ( LSubSp `` M ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincolss (M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wcel (co M V clinco) (cfv M clss)) $;

--| Every linear combination of a subset of a linear subspace is also contained in the linear subspace. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem ellcoellss {x3: setvar} (S M V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cfv M clss)) (wss V S)) (wral x3 (wcel (cv x3) S) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| A set of vectors of a module is a subset of the set of all linear combinations of the set. (Contributed by AV, 18-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lcoss (M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wss V (co M V clinco)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lspeqlco . (Contributed by AV, 17-Apr-2019.)
theorem lspsslco (B2 M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wss (cfv V (cfv M clspn)) (co M V clinco)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ lspeqlco . (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.)
theorem lcosslsp (B2 M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wss (co M V clinco) (cfv V (cfv M clspn))) $;

--| Equivalence of a _span_ of a set of vectors of a left module defined as the intersection of all linear subspaces which each contain every vector in that set (see ~ df-lsp ) and as the set of all linear combinations of the vectors of the set with finite support. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.)
theorem lspeqlco (B2 M V: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel V (cpw B2))) (wceq (co M V clinco) (cfv V (cfv M clspn))) $;

--| Extend class notation with the relation between a module and its linearly independent subsets.
term clininds: class;

--| Extend class notation with the relation between a module and its linearly dependent subsets.
term clindeps: class;

--| Define the relation between a module and its linearly independent subsets. (Contributed by AV, 12-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 24-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
axiom df_lininds {x3 f2 m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clininds (copab s1 m (wa (wcel (cv s1) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs))) (wral f2 (wi (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) (cv s1) (cfv (cv m) clinc)) (cfv (cv m) c0g))) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g)) (cv s1))) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cv s1) cmap)))) $;

--| The class defining the relation between a module and its linearly independent subsets is a relation. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.)
theorem rellininds:
  $ wrel clininds $;

--| Define the relation between a module and its linearly dependent subsets. (Contributed by AV, 26-Apr-2019.)
axiom df_lindeps {m s1: setvar}:
  $ wceq clindeps (copab s1 m (wn (wbr (cv s1) (cv m) clininds))) $;

--| The classes of the module and its linearly independent subsets are sets. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.)
theorem linindsv (S M: class):
  $ wi (wbr S M clininds) (wa (wcel S cvv) (wcel M cvv)) $;

--| The property of being a linearly independent subset. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem islininds {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 R: class x3 f2) (S: class) (E: class x3) (M: class) (V W c_0 Z: class x3 f2):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr S M clininds) (wa (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wral f2 (wi (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co E S cmap)))) $;

--| The implications of being a linearly independent subset. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem linindsi {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 R: class x3 f2) (S: class) (E: class x3) (M: class) (c_0 Z: class x3 f2):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wbr S M clininds) (wa (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wral f2 (wi (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co E S cmap))) $;

--| The implications of being a linearly independent subset and a linear combination of this subset being 0. (Contributed by AV, 24-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem linindslinci {x3: setvar} (B2 R: class x3) (S: class) (E: class x3) (F M: class) (c_0 Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr S M clininds) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co F S (cfv M clinc)) Z))) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) c_0) S) $;

--| The property of being a linearly independent finite subset. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem islinindfis {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 R: class x3 f2) (S: class) (E: class x3) (M: class) (W c_0 Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S cfn) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr S M clininds) (wa (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wral f2 (wi (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co E S cmap)))) $;

--| The property of being a linearly independent finite subset. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem islinindfiss {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 R: class x3 f2) (S: class) (E: class x3) (M: class) (W c_0 Z: class x3):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M W) (wcel S cfn) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (wb (wbr S M clininds) (wral f2 (wi (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co E S cmap))) $;

--| A linearly independent set is a subset of (the base set of) a module. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.)
theorem linindscl (S M: class):
  $ wi (wbr S M clininds) (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs))) $;

--| A linearly dependent subset is not a linearly independent subset. (Contributed by AV, 26-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindepsnlininds (S M V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M W)) (wb (wbr S M clindeps) (wn (wbr S M clininds))) $;

--| The property of being a linearly dependent subset. (Contributed by AV, 26-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem islindeps {x3 f2: setvar} (B2 R: class x3 f2) (S: class) (E: class x3) (M: class) (W c_0 Z: class x3 f2):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M W) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (wb (wbr S M clindeps) (wrex f2 (w3a (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z) (wrex x3 (wne (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co E S cmap))) $;

--| Property 1 of an extension of a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lincext1 {z: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class z) (S E: class) (F: class z) (G M: class) (N: class z) (X Y: class) (c_0 Z: class z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z S (cif (wceq (cv z) X) (cfv Y N) (cfv (cv z) G))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (w3a (wcel Y E) (wcel X S) (wcel G (co E (cdif S (csn X)) cmap)))) (wcel F (co E S cmap)) $;

--| Property 2 of an extension of a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 20-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincext2 {z: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class z) (S E: class) (F: class z) (G M: class) (N: class z) (X Y: class) (c_0 Z: class z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z S (cif (wceq (cv z) X) (cfv Y N) (cfv (cv z) G))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (w3a (wcel Y E) (wcel X S) (wcel G (co E (cdif S (csn X)) cmap))) (wbr G c_0 cfsupp)) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| Property 3 of an extension of a linear combination. (Contributed by AV, 23-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincext3 {z: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class z) (S E: class) (F: class z) (G M N X Y: class) (c_0 Z: class z):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpt z S (cif (wceq (cv z) X) (cfv Y N) (cfv (cv z) G))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (w3a (wcel Y E) (wcel X S) (wcel G (co E (cdif S (csn X)) cmap))) (wa (wbr G c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co Y X (cfv M cvsca)) (co G (cdif S (csn X)) (cfv M clinc))))) (wceq (co F S (cfv M clinc)) Z) $;

--| Implication 1 for ~ lindslininds . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.) (Proof shortened by II, 16-Feb-2023.)
theorem lindslinindsimp1 {x3 y1 f2 s1: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R: class y1 s1) (S: class) (M: class x3) (V: class x3 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wi (wa (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs))) (wral f2 (wi (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co B2 S cmap))) (wa (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wral s1 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv s1) (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) (cfv M clspn)))) (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) S))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ lindslinindsimp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindslinindimp2lem1 {x3 f2: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R S: class) (G: class x3 f2) (M: class x3) (V Y: class x3 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv R cminusg)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (w3a (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv f2) (co B2 S cmap)))) (wcel Y B2) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ lindslinindsimp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindslinindimp2lem2 {x3 f2: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R S: class) (G: class x3 f2) (M: class x3) (V Y: class x3 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv R cminusg)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (w3a (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv f2) (co B2 S cmap)))) (wcel G (co B2 (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) cmap)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ lindslinindsimp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lindslinindimp2lem3 {x3 f2: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R S: class) (G: class x3 f2) (M: class x3) (V Y: class x3 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv R cminusg)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wa (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co B2 S cmap)) (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp))) (wbr G c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ lindslinindsimp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.) (Proof shortened by II, 16-Feb-2023.)
theorem lindslinindimp2lem4 {x3 y1 f2: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R: class y1) (S: class) (G: class x3 f2) (M: class x3) (V: class x3 f2) (Y: class x3 y1 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cfv (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) (cfv R cminusg)) $ >
  $ wceq G (cres (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wa (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wcel (cv x3) S)) (w3a (wcel (cv f2) (co B2 S cmap)) (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z))) (wceq (co M (cmpt y1 (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) (co (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (cv y1) (cfv M cvsca))) cgsu) (co Y (cv x3) (cfv M cvsca))) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ lindslinindsimp2 . (Contributed by AV, 25-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lindslinindsimp2lem5 {x3 y1 f2 g2: setvar} (B2: class x3) (R S: class) (M: class x3) (V: class x3 f2) (c_0: class) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wa (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wcel (cv x3) S))) (wi (wa (wcel (cv f2) (co B2 S cmap)) (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z))) (wi (wral y1 (wral g2 (wo (wn (wbr (cv g2) c_0 cfsupp)) (wn (wceq (co (cv y1) (cv x3) (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cv g2) (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) (cfv M clinc))))) (co B2 (cdif S (csn (cv x3))) cmap)) (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0))) $;

--| Implication 2 for ~ lindslininds . (Contributed by AV, 26-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lindslinindsimp2 {x3 y1 f2 s1: setvar} (B2 R S M V c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wi (wa (wss S (cfv M cbs)) (wral s1 (wral y1 (wn (wcel (co (cv y1) (cv s1) (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) (cfv M clspn)))) (cdif B2 (csn c_0))) S)) (wa (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs))) (wral f2 (wi (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) (cv f2)) c_0) S)) (co B2 S cmap)))) $;

--| Equivalence of definitions ~ df-linds and ~ df-lininds for (linear) independence for (left) modules. (Contributed by AV, 26-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lindslininds (S M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel S V) (wcel M clmod)) (wb (wbr S M clininds) (wcel S (cfv M clinds))) $;

--| The empty set is always a linearly independent subset. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem linds0 (M V: class):
  $ wi (wcel M V) (wbr c0 M clininds) $;

--| A set containing the zero element of a module is always linearly dependent, if the underlying ring has at least two elements. (Contributed by AV, 13-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem el0ldep (S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wbr c1 (cfv (cfv (cfv M csca) cbs) chash) clt)) (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs))) (wcel (cfv M c0g) S)) (wbr S M clindeps) $;

--| A set containing the zero element of a module over a nonzero ring is always linearly dependent. (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem el0ldepsnzr (S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel (cfv M csca) cnzr)) (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs))) (wcel (cfv M c0g) S)) (wbr S M clindeps) $;

--| Any subset of a module is always linearly independent if the underlying ring has at most one element. Since the underlying ring cannot be the empty set (see ~ lmodsn0 ), this means that the underlying ring has only one element, so it is a zero ring. (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindsrng01 (B2 R S E M: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wo (wceq (cfv E chash) cc0) (wceq (cfv E chash) c1)) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (wbr S M clininds) $;

--| Any subset of a module over a zero ring is always linearly independent. (Contributed by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindszr (S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wn (wcel (cfv M csca) cnzr)) (wcel S (cpw (cfv M cbs)))) (wbr S M clininds) $;

--| Lemma for ~ snlindsntor . (Contributed by AV, 15-Apr-2019.)
theorem snlindsntorlem {f2 s1: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class f2 s1) (S c_x M X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel X B2)) (wi (wral f2 (wi (wceq (co (cv f2) (csn X) (cfv M clinc)) Z) (wceq (cfv X (cv f2)) c_0)) (co S (csn X) cmap)) (wral s1 (wi (wceq (co (cv s1) X c_x) Z) (wceq (cv s1) c_0)) S)) $;

--| A singleton is linearly independent iff it does not contain a torsion element. According to Wikipedia ("Torsion (algebra)", 15-Apr-2019, ~ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torsion_(algebra) ): "An element m of a module M over a ring R is called a _torsion element_ of the module if there exists a regular element r of the ring (an element that is neither a left nor a right zero divisor) that annihilates m, i.e., ` ( r .x. m ) = 0 ` . In an integral domain (a commutative ring without zero divisors), every nonzero element is regular, so a torsion element of a module over an integral domain is one annihilated by a nonzero element of the integral domain." Analogously, the definition in [Lang] p. 147 states that "An element x of [a module] E [over a ring R] is called a _torsion element_ if there exists ` a e. R ` , ` a =/= 0 ` , such that ` a .x. x = 0 ` . This definition includes the zero element of the module. Some authors, however, exclude the zero element from the definition of torsion elements. (Contributed by AV, 14-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.)
theorem snlindsntor {s1: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class s1) (S c_x M X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (wcel X B2)) (wb (wral s1 (wne (co (cv s1) X c_x) Z) (cdif S (csn c_0))) (wbr (csn X) M clininds)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ldepspr . (Contributed by AV, 16-Apr-2019.)
theorem ldepsprlem (A2 B2 R S c_x c_1 M N X Y c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv R cur) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wcel A2 S))) (wi (wceq X (co A2 Y c_x)) (wceq (co (co c_1 X c_x) (co (cfv A2 N) Y c_x) (cfv M cplusg)) Z)) $;

--| If a vector is a scalar multiple of another vector, the (unordered pair containing the) two vectors are linearly dependent. (Contributed by AV, 16-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 27-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem ldepspr (A2 B2 R S c_x M X Y c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clmod) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wne X Y))) (wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wceq X (co A2 Y c_x))) (wbr (cpr X Y) M clindeps)) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ lincresunit3 . (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunit3lem3 (A2 B2 R c_x U E M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv M cvsca) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2)) (wcel A2 U)) (wb (wceq (co (cfv A2 N) X c_x) (co (cfv A2 N) Y c_x)) (wceq X Y)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for properties of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination containing a unit as scalar. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunitlem1 {s1: setvar} (B2 R S c_x U E F G I M N X c_0 Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (wa (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U))) (wcel (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) E) $;

--| Lemma for properties of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination containing a unit as scalar. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunitlem2 {s1: setvar} (B2 R S c_x U E F G I M N X Y c_0 Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (wa (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U))) (wcel Y S)) (wcel (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv Y F) c_x) E) $;

--| Property 1 of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination containing a unit as scalar. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunit1 {s1: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class s1) (S: class) (c_x: class s1) (U E F: class) (G I: class s1) (M: class) (N: class s1) (X: class) (c_0 Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (wa (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U))) (wcel G (co E (cdif S (csn X)) cmap)) $;

--| Property 2 of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination containing a unit as scalar. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunit2 {s1: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class s1) (S c_x U E F: class) (G: class s1) (I M N X c_0: class) (Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp))) (wbr G c_0 cfsupp) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ lincresunit3 . (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2019.)
theorem lincresunit3lem1 {s1: setvar} (z: setvar) (B2: class) (R: class s1) (S c_x U E F: class) (G: class s1) (I M N X: class) (c_0 Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U) (wcel (cv z) (cdif S (csn X))))) (wceq (co (cfv (cfv X F) N) (co (cfv (cv z) G) (cv z) (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z) (cfv M cvsca))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ lincresunit3 . (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincresunit3lem2 {z s1: setvar} (B2: class) (R: class s1) (S: class) (c_x: class z) (U E F: class) (G: class s1) (I: class z) (M N X c_0: class) (Z: class s1):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp))) (wceq (co (cfv (cfv X F) N) (co M (cmpt z (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cv z) G) (cv z) (cfv M cvsca))) cgsu) (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cres F (cdif S (csn X))) (cdif S (csn X)) (cfv M clinc))) $;

--| Property 3 of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination in a vector space. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincresunit3 {s1: setvar} (B2 R S c_x U E F G I M N X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clmod) (wcel X S)) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wcel (cfv X F) U) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp)) (wceq (co F S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wceq (co G (cdif S (csn X)) (cfv M clinc)) X) $;

--| Property 3 of a specially modified restriction of a linear combination in a vector space. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem lincreslvec3 {s1: setvar} (B2 R S c_x U E F G I M N X c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq U (cfv R cui) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv R cminusg) $ >
  $ wceq I (cfv R cinvr) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv R cmulr) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt s1 (cdif S (csn X)) (co (cfv (cfv (cfv X F) N) I) (cfv (cv s1) F) c_x)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel M clvec) (wcel X S)) (w3a (wcel F (co E S cmap)) (wne (cfv X F) c_0) (wbr F c_0 cfsupp)) (wceq (co F S (cfv M clinc)) Z)) (wceq (co G (cdif S (csn X)) (cfv M clinc)) X) $;

--| Conditions for being a linearly dependent subset of a (left) module over a nonzero ring. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem islindeps2 {f2 s1: setvar} (B2 R S E M c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel R cnzr)) (wi (wrex s1 (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) (cfv M clinc)) (cv s1))) (co E (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) cmap)) S) (wbr S M clindeps)) $;

--| Implication of being a linearly independent subset of a (left) module over a nonzero ring. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem islininds2 {f2 s1: setvar} (B2 R S E M c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clmod) (wcel S (cpw B2)) (wcel R cnzr)) (wi (wbr S M clininds) (wral s1 (wral f2 (wo (wn (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp)) (wne (co (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) (cfv M clinc)) (cv s1))) (co E (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) cmap)) S)) $;

--| Alternative definition of being a linearly dependent subset of a (left) vector space. In this case, the reverse implication of ~ islindeps2 holds, so that both definitions are equivalent (see theorem 1.6 in [Roman] p. 46 and the note in [Roman] p. 112: if a nontrivial linear combination of elements (where not all of the coefficients are 0) in an R-vector space is 0, then and only then each of the elements is a linear combination of the others. (Contributed by AV, 30-Apr-2019.) (Proof shortened by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem isldepslvec2 {f2 s1: setvar} (B2 R S E M c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv M cbs) $ >
  $ wceq Z (cfv M c0g) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfv M csca) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv R cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cfv R c0g) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M clvec) (wcel S (cpw B2))) (wb (wrex s1 (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) c_0 cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) (cfv M clinc)) (cv s1))) (co E (cdif S (csn (cv s1))) cmap)) S) (wbr S M clindeps)) $;

--| A singleton not containing the zero element of a vector space is always linearly independent. (Contributed by AV, 16-Apr-2019.) (Revised by AV, 28-Apr-2019.)
theorem lindssnlvec (S M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M clvec) (wcel S (cfv M cbs)) (wne S (cfv M c0g))) (wbr (csn S) M clininds) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ lmod1 . (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1lem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I: class) (M: class x3 y1) (V: class) (r: setvar):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel R crg) (wcel (cv r) (cfv R cbs))) (wcel (co (cv r) I (cfv M cvsca)) (csn I)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ lmod1 . (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1lem2 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I: class) (M: class x3 y1) (V: class) (r: setvar):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I V) (wcel R crg) (wcel (cv r) (cfv R cbs))) (wceq (co (cv r) (co I I (cfv M cplusg)) (cfv M cvsca)) (co (co (cv r) I (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cv r) I (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv M cplusg))) $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ lmod1 . (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1lem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I M V: class) (r q: setvar):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel I V) (wcel R crg)) (wa (wcel (cv q) (cfv R cbs)) (wcel (cv r) (cfv R cbs)))) (wceq (co (co (cv q) (cv r) (cfv (cfv M csca) cplusg)) I (cfv M cvsca)) (co (co (cv q) I (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cv r) I (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv M cplusg))) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ lmod1 . (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1lem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I M V: class) (r q: setvar):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel I V) (wcel R crg)) (wa (wcel (cv q) (cfv R cbs)) (wcel (cv r) (cfv R cbs)))) (wceq (co (co (cv q) (cv r) (cfv (cfv M csca) cmulr)) I (cfv M cvsca)) (co (cv q) (co (cv r) I (cfv M cvsca)) (cfv M cvsca))) $;

--| Lemma 5 for ~ lmod1 . (Contributed by AV, 28-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1lem5 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel R crg)) (wceq (co (cfv (cfv M csca) cur) I (cfv M cvsca)) I) $;

--| The (smallest) structure representing a _zero module_ over an arbitrary ring. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1 {x3 y1: setvar} (R I M V: class):
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv R cbs) (csn I) (cv y1))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel R crg)) (wcel M clmod) $;

--| The (smallest) structure representing a _zero module_ over a zero ring. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1zr (R I M V W Z: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn Z)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (csn (cop (cop Z I) I))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel Z W)) (wcel M clmod) $;

--| There is a (left) module (a _zero module_) which is not a (left) vector space. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmod1zrnlvec (R I M V W Z: class):
  $ wceq R (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn Z)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z))) (cop (cfv cnx cmulr) (csn (cop (cop Z Z) Z)))) $ >
  $ wceq M (cun (ctp (cop (cfv cnx cbs) (csn I)) (cop (cfv cnx cplusg) (csn (cop (cop I I) I))) (cop (cfv cnx csca) R)) (csn (cop (cfv cnx cvsca) (csn (cop (cop Z I) I))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I V) (wcel Z W)) (wnel M clvec) $;

--| Left modules exist. (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lmodn0:
  $ wne clmod c0 $;

--| Example of an equation within the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` (see example in [Roman] p. 112 for a linearly dependent set). (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzequa (A2 B2 c_xb c_mi c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Z cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Z csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 A2 c_xb) (co c3 B2 c_xb) c_mi) c_0 $;

--| Example of a linearly dependent set whose elements are not linear combinations of the others, see note in [Roman] p. 112). (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxznm {i: setvar} (A2 B2 c_xb c_mi c_0 Z: class i):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Z cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv Z csg) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wral i (wa (wne (co (cv i) A2 c_xb) B2) (wne (co (cv i) B2 c_xb) A2)) cz $;

--| A and B are not equal. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldeplem (A2 B2 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wne A2 B2 $;

--| Example of an equation within the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` (see example in [Roman] p. 112 for a linearly dependent set), written as a sum. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzequap (A2 B2 c_pl c_xb c_0 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cpr (cop cc0 cc0) (cop c1 cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv Z cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_xb (cfv Z cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c2 A2 c_xb) (co (cneg c3) B2 c_xb) c_pl) c_0 $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ zlmodzxzldep . (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldeplem1 (A2 B2 F Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop A2 c2) (cop B2 (cneg c3))) $ >
  $ wcel F (co cz (cpr A2 B2) cmap) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ zlmodzxzldep . (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldeplem2 (A2 B2 F Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop A2 c2) (cop B2 (cneg c3))) $ >
  $ wbr F cc0 cfsupp $;

--| Lemma 3 for ~ zlmodzxzldep . (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldeplem3 (A2 B2 F Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop A2 c2) (cop B2 (cneg c3))) $ >
  $ wceq (co F (cpr A2 B2) (cfv Z clinc)) (cfv Z c0g) $;

--| Lemma 4 for ~ zlmodzxzldep . (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldeplem4 {y1: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wceq F (cpr (cop A2 c2) (cop B2 (cneg c3))) $ >
  $ wrex y1 (wne (cfv (cv y1) F) cc0) (cpr A2 B2) $;

--| { A , B } is a linearly dependent set within the ` ZZ `-module ` ZZ X. ZZ ` (see example in [Roman] p. 112). (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem zlmodzxzldep (A2 B2 Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wbr (cpr A2 B2) Z clindeps $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ ldepsnlinc . (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem ldepsnlinclem1 (A2 B2 F Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co (cfv czring cbs) (csn B2) cmap)) (wne (co F (csn B2) (cfv Z clinc)) A2) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ ldepsnlinc . (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 10-Jun-2019.)
theorem ldepsnlinclem2 (A2 B2 F Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (co czring (cpr cc0 c1) cfrlm) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (cpr (cop cc0 c3) (cop c1 c6)) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (cpr (cop cc0 c2) (cop c1 c4)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co (cfv czring cbs) (csn A2) cmap)) (wne (co F (csn A2) (cfv Z clinc)) B2) $;

--| The class of all (left) vector spaces is a proper subclass of the class of all (left) modules. Although it is obvious (and proven by ~ lveclmod ) that every left vector space is a left module, there is (at least) one left module which is no left vector space, for example the zero module over the zero ring, see ~ lmod1zrnlvec . (Contributed by AV, 29-Apr-2019.)
theorem lvecpsslmod:
  $ wpss clvec clmod $;

--| The reverse implication of ~ islindeps2 does not hold for arbitrary (left) modules, see note in [Roman] p. 112: "... if a nontrivial linear combination of the elements ... in an R-module M is 0, ... where not all of the coefficients are 0, then we cannot conclude ... that one of the elements ... is a linear combination of the others." This means that there is at least one left module having a linearly dependent subset in which there is at least one element which is not a linear combination of the other elements of this subset. Such a left module can be constructed by using ~ zlmodzxzequa and ~ zlmodzxznm . (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem ldepsnlinc {v f2 m s1: setvar}:
  $ wrex m (wrex s1 (wa (wbr (cv s1) (cv m) clindeps) (wral v (wral f2 (wi (wbr (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g) cfsupp) (wne (co (cv f2) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) (cfv (cv m) clinc)) (cv v))) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) cmap)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs))) clmod $;

--| For (left) vector spaces, ~ isldepslvec2 provides an alternative definition of being a linearly dependent subset, whereas ~ ldepsnlinc indicates that there is not an analogous alternative definition for arbitrary (left) modules. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2019.) (Revised by AV, 30-Jul-2019.)
theorem ldepslinc {v f2 m s1: setvar}:
  $ wa (wral m (wral s1 (wb (wbr (cv s1) (cv m) clindeps) (wrex v (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) (cfv (cv m) clinc)) (cv v))) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) cmap)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs))) clvec) (wn (wral m (wral s1 (wb (wbr (cv s1) (cv m) clindeps) (wrex v (wrex f2 (wa (wbr (cv f2) (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) c0g) cfsupp) (wceq (co (cv f2) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) (cfv (cv m) clinc)) (cv v))) (co (cfv (cfv (cv m) csca) cbs) (cdif (cv s1) (csn (cv v))) cmap)) (cv s1))) (cpw (cfv (cv m) cbs))) clmod)) $;

--| If the range of a function does not contain the zero, the support of the function equals its domain. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.)
theorem suppdm (F V W Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wfun F) (wcel F V) (wcel Z W)) (wnel Z (crn F))) (wceq (co F Z csupp) (cdm F)) $;

--| An integer greater than 1 is a complex number not equal to 0 or 1. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem eluz2cnn0n1 (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel B2 (cdif cc (cpr cc0 c1))) $;

--| The ratio of a real number to a positive real number is greater than or equal to 1 iff the divisor (the positive real number) is less than or equal to the dividend (the real number). (Contributed by AV, 26-May-2020.)
theorem divge1b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr c1 (co B2 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| The ratio of a real number to a positive real number is greater than 1 iff the divisor (the positive real number) is less than the dividend (the real number). (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem divgt1b (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr c1 (co B2 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Equivalence for the "less than" relation between differences and sums. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem ltsubaddb (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 D cmin) clt) (wbr (co A2 D caddc) (co B2 C caddc) clt)) $;

--| Equivalence for the "less than" relation between differences. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem ltsubsubb (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr))) (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmin) (co B2 D cmin) clt) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cmin) clt)) $;

--| Equivalence for the "less than" relation between differences and sums. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem ltsubadd2b (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr))) (wb (wbr (co D C cmin) (co B2 A2 cmin) clt) (wbr (co A2 D caddc) (co B2 C caddc) clt)) $;

--| Distribution of division over subtraction by 1. (Contributed by AV, 6-Jun-2020.)
theorem divsub1dir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wne B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) c1 cmin) (co (co A2 B2 cmin) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| An integer greater than 1 to the power of a negative integer is in the closed-below, open-above interval between 0 and 1. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem expnegico01 (B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N cz) (wbr N cc0 clt)) (wcel (co B2 N cexp) (co cc0 c1 cico)) $;

--| An element of a half-open integer interval is either equal to the left bound of the interval or an element of a half-open integer interval with a lower bound increased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.)
theorem elfzolborelfzop1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wo (wceq K M) (wcel K (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfzo))) $;

--| 2 to the power of a positive integer decreased by 1 is less than or equal to 2 to the power of the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem pw2m1lepw2m1 (I: class):
  $ wi (wcel I cn) (wbr (co c2 (co I c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co c2 I cexp) c1 cmin) cle) $;

--| If an integer is between another integer and its predecessor, the integer is equal to the other integer. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem zgtp1leeq (A2 I: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cz) (wcel A2 cz)) (wi (wa (wbr (co A2 c1 cmin) I clt) (wbr I A2 cle)) (wceq I A2)) $;

--| An integer can be moved in and out of the floor of a difference. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem flsubz (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cmin) cfl) (co (cfv A2 cfl) N cmin)) $;

--| For each odd nonnegative integer there is a nonnegative integer which, multiplied by 2 and increased by 1, results in the odd nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nn0onn0ex {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wrex m (wceq N (co (co c2 (cv m) cmul) c1 caddc)) cn0) $;

--| For each even nonnegative integer there is a nonnegative integer which, multiplied by 2, results in the even nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nn0enn0ex {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wrex m (wceq N (co c2 (cv m) cmul)) cn0) $;

--| For each even positive integer there is a positive integer which, multiplied by 2, results in the even positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2023.)
theorem nnennex {m: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn)) (wrex m (wceq N (co c2 (cv m) cmul)) cn) $;

--| A positive integer is even or odd. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nneop (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wo (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn)) $;

--| A positive integer is even or odd. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nneom (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wo (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn) (wcel (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn0)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is even or odd. (Contributed by AV, 27-May-2020.)
theorem nn0eo (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wo (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) $;

--| 2 to the power of a positive integer is even. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnpw2even (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (co (co c2 N cexp) c2 cdiv) cn) $;

--| The floor of an even integer divided by 2 is equal to the integer divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem zefldiv2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cz)) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (co N c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The floor of an odd integer divided by 2 is equal to the integer first decreased by 1 and then divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem zofldiv2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cz)) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The floor of an odd nonnegative integer divided by 2 is equal to the integer first decreased by 1 and then divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jun-2020.) (Proof shortened by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0ofldiv2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The floor of a positive integer divided by 2 is greater than or equal to the integer decreased by 1 and then divided by 2. (Contributed by AV, 1-Jun-2020.)
theorem flnn0div2ge (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) cle) $;

--| The floor of the half of an odd positive integer is equal to the floor of the half of the integer decreased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 5-Jun-2012.)
theorem flnn0ohalf (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) (cfv (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cfl)) $;

--| Logarithm of a complex power. Generalization of ~ logcxp . (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2020.)
theorem logcxp0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdif cc (csn cc0))) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul) (crn clog))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 ccxp) clog) (co B2 (cfv A2 clog) cmul)) $;

--| The natural logarithm for a real number greater than 1 is greater than 0. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem regt1loggt0 (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (co c1 cpnf cioo)) (wbr cc0 (cfv B2 clog) clt) $;

--| Extend class notation with the division operator of two functions.
term cfdiv: class;

--| Define the division of two functions into the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2020.)
axiom df_fdiv {f2 g2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfdiv (cmpo f2 g2 cvv cvv (cres (co (cv f2) (cv g2) (cof cdiv)) (co (cv g2) cc0 csupp))) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2020.)
theorem fdivval (F G V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel G W)) (wceq (co F G cfdiv) (cres (co F G (cof cdiv)) (co G cc0 csupp))) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the complex numbers as mapping. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem fdivmpt {x3: setvar} (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G) (wcel A2 V)) (wceq (co F G cfdiv) (cmpt x3 (co G cc0 csupp) (co (cfv (cv x3) F) (cfv (cv x3) G) cdiv))) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the complex numbers is a function into the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem fdivmptf (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G) (wcel A2 V)) (wf (co G cc0 csupp) cc (co F G cfdiv)) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the real numbers is a function into the real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem refdivmptf (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cr F) (wf A2 cr G) (wcel A2 V)) (wf (co G cc0 csupp) cr (co F G cfdiv)) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the complex numbers is a partial function. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem fdivpm (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co F G cfdiv) (co cc A2 cpm)) $;

--| The quotient of two functions into the real numbers is a partial function. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem refdivpm (A2 F G V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wf A2 cr F) (wf A2 cr G) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel (co F G cfdiv) (co cr A2 cpm)) $;

--| The function value of a quotient of two functions into the complex numbers. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2020.)
theorem fdivmptfv (A2 F G V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wf A2 cc F) (wf A2 cc G) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel X (co G cc0 csupp))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G cfdiv)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv X G) cdiv)) $;

--| The function value of a quotient of two functions into the real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2020.)
theorem refdivmptfv (A2 F G V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wf A2 cr F) (wf A2 cr G) (wcel A2 V)) (wcel X (co G cc0 csupp))) (wceq (cfv X (co F G cfdiv)) (co (cfv X F) (cfv X G) cdiv)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of the "big-O" function.
term cbigo: class;

--| Define the function "big-O", mapping a real function g to the set of real functions "of order g(x)". Definition in section 1.1 of [AhoHopUll] p. 2. This is a generalization of "big-O of one", see ~ df-o1 and ~ df-lo1 . As explained in the comment of df-o1 , any big-O can be represented in terms of ` O(1) ` and division, see ~ elbigolo1 . (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2020.)
axiom df_bigo {x3 y1 f2 g2 m: setvar}:
  $ wceq cbigo (cmpt g2 (co cr cr cpm) (crab f2 (wrex x3 (wrex m (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) (cv g2)) cmul) cle) (cin (cdm (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) cpnf cico))) cr) cr) (co cr cr cpm))) $;

--| Set of functions of order G(x). (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2020.)
theorem bigoval {x3 y1 f2 m: setvar} (G: class):
  $ wi (wcel G (co cr cr cpm)) (wceq (cfv G cbigo) (crab f2 (wrex x3 (wrex m (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) (cv f2)) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) G) cmul) cle) (cin (cdm (cv f2)) (co (cv x3) cpnf cico))) cr) cr) (co cr cr cpm))) $;

--| Reverse closure of the "big-O" function. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem elbigofrcl (F G: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wcel G (co cr cr cpm)) $;

--| Properties of a function of order G(x). (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
theorem elbigo {x3 y1 m: setvar} (F G: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (w3a (wcel F (co cr cr cpm)) (wcel G (co cr cr cpm)) (wrex x3 (wrex m (wral y1 (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) G) cmul) cle) (cin (cdm F) (co (cv x3) cpnf cico))) cr) cr)) $;

--| Properties of a function of order G(x) under certain assumptions. (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2020.)
theorem elbigo2 {x3 y1 m: setvar} (A2 B2 F G: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wf A2 cr G) (wss A2 cr)) (wa (wf B2 cr F) (wss B2 A2))) (wb (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wrex x3 (wrex m (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) G) cmul) cle)) B2) cr) cr)) $;

--| Sufficient condition for a function to be of order G(x). (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2020.)
theorem elbigo2r {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F G M: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wf A2 cr G) (wss A2 cr)) (wa (wf B2 cr F) (wss B2 A2)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wcel M cr) (wral x3 (wi (wbr C (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) F) (co M (cfv (cv x3) G) cmul) cle)) B2))) (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) $;

--| A function of order G(x) is a function. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2020.)
theorem elbigof (F G: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wf (cdm F) cr F) $;

--| The domain of a function of order G(x) is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2020.)
theorem elbigodm (F G: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wss (cdm F) cr) $;

--| The defining property of a function of order G(x). (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2020.)
theorem elbigoimp {x3 y1 m: setvar} (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wf A2 cr F) (wss A2 (cdm G))) (wrex x3 (wrex m (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (wbr (cfv (cv y1) F) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv y1) G) cmul) cle)) A2) cr) cr) $;

--| A function (into the positive reals) is of order G(x) iff the quotient of the function and G(x) (also a function into the positive reals) is an eventually upper bounded function. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.) (Proof shortened by II, 16-Feb-2023.)
theorem elbigolo1 (A2 F G: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 cr) (wf A2 crp G) (wf A2 crp F)) (wb (wcel F (cfv G cbigo)) (wcel (co F G cfdiv) clo1)) $;

--| The general logarithm, with a real base greater than 1, for a real number greater than or equal to 1 is greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem rege1logbrege0 (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (co c1 cpnf cioo)) (wcel X (co c1 cpnf cico))) (wbr cc0 (co B2 X clogb) cle) $;

--| The general logarithm, with an integer base greater than 1, for a real number greater than or equal to 1 is greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem rege1logbzge0 (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X (co c1 cpnf cico))) (wbr cc0 (co B2 X clogb) cle) $;

--| A real number is between the base of a logarithm to the power of the floor of the logarithm of the number and the base of the logarithm to the power of the floor of the logarithm of the number plus one. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem fllogbd (ph: wff) (B2 E X: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv (co B2 X clogb) cfl) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wbr (co B2 E cexp) X cle) (wbr X (co B2 (co E c1 caddc) cexp) clt)) $;

--| The logarithm of the product of a positive real number and the base to the power of a real number is the logarithm of the positive real number plus the real number. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem relogbmulbexp (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif crp (csn c1))) (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel C cr))) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 (co B2 C ccxp) cmul) clogb) (co (co B2 A2 clogb) C caddc)) $;

--| The logarithm of the quotient of a positive real number and the base is the logarithm of the number minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem relogbdivb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cdif crp (csn c1))) (wcel A2 crp)) (wceq (co B2 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clogb) (co (co B2 A2 clogb) c1 cmin)) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is nonnegative iff the number is greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem logbge0b (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp)) (wb (wbr cc0 (co B2 X clogb) cle) (wbr c1 X cle)) $;

--| The logarithm of a number is less than 1 iff the number is less than the base of the logarithm. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem logblt1b (B2 X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel X crp)) (wb (wbr (co B2 X clogb) c1 clt) (wbr X B2 clt)) $;

--| The floor of a positive real number divided by 2 to the power of the floor of the logarithm to base 2 of the number is 1. (Contributed by AV, 26-May-2020.)
theorem fldivexpfllog2 (X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X crp) (wceq (cfv (co X (co c2 (cfv (co c2 X clogb) cfl) cexp) cdiv) cfl) c1) $;

--| A positive integer is 1 iff its binary logarithm is between 0 and 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nnlog2ge0lt1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wb (wceq N c1) (wa (wbr cc0 (co c2 N clogb) cle) (wbr (co c2 N clogb) c1 clt))) $;

--| The floor of the binary logarithm of 2 to the power of a positive integer minus 1 is equal to the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem logbpw2m1 (I: class):
  $ wi (wcel I cn) (wceq (cfv (co c2 (co (co c2 I cexp) c1 cmin) clogb) cfl) (co I c1 cmin)) $;

--| The floor of the binary logarithm of 2 to the power of an element of a half-open integer interval bounded by powers of 2 is equal to the integer. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem fllog2 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cn0) (wcel N (co (co c2 I cexp) (co c2 (co I c1 caddc) cexp) cfzo))) (wceq (cfv (co c2 N clogb) cfl) I) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of the binary length function.
term cblen: class;

--| Define the binary length of an integer. Definition in section 1.3 of [AhoHopUll] p. 12. Although not restricted to integers, this definition is only meaningful for ` n e. ZZ ` or even for ` n e. CC ` . (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
axiom df_blen {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cblen (cmpt n cvv (cif (wceq (cv n) cc0) c1 (co (cfv (co c2 (cfv (cv n) cabs) clogb) cfl) c1 caddc))) $;

--| The binary length of an integer. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.)
theorem blenval (N V: class):
  $ wi (wcel N V) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (cif (wceq N cc0) c1 (co (cfv (co c2 (cfv N cabs) clogb) cfl) c1 caddc))) $;

--| The binary length of 0. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.)
theorem blen0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cblen) c1 $;

--| The binary length of a "number" not being 0. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.)
theorem blenn0 (N V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wne N cc0)) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (co c2 (cfv N cabs) clogb) cfl) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of a positive real number. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2020.)
theorem blenre (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N crp) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (co c2 N clogb) cfl) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2020.)
theorem blennn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (co c2 N clogb) cfl) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of a positive integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem blennnelnn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel (cfv N cblen) cn) $;

--| The binary length of a nonnegative integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2020.)
theorem blennn0elnn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel (cfv N cblen) cn) $;

--| The binary length of a power of 2 is the exponent plus 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem blenpw2 (I: class):
  $ wi (wcel I cn0) (wceq (cfv (co c2 I cexp) cblen) (co I c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of a power of 2 minus 1 is the exponent. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem blenpw2m1 (I: class):
  $ wi (wcel I cn) (wceq (cfv (co (co c2 I cexp) c1 cmin) cblen) I) $;

--| A positive integer is between 2 to the power of its binary length minus 1 and 2 to the power of its binary length. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem nnpw2blen (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wa (wbr (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp) N cle) (wbr N (co c2 (cfv N cblen) cexp) clt)) $;

--| A positive integer is between 2 to the power of the binary length of the integer minus 1, and 2 to the power of the binary length of the integer. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnpw2blenfzo (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wcel N (co (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp) (co c2 (cfv N cblen) cexp) cfzo)) $;

--| A positive integer is either 2 to the power of the binary length of the integer minus 1, or between 2 to the power of the binary length of the integer minus 1, increased by 1, and 2 to the power of the binary length of the integer. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnpw2blenfzo2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wo (wceq N (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp)) (wcel N (co (co (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp) c1 caddc) (co c2 (cfv N cblen) cexp) cfzo))) $;

--| Every positive integer can be represented as the sum of a power of 2 and a "remainder" less than the power. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem nnpw2pmod (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq N (co (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp) (co N (co c2 (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) cexp) cmo) caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of 1. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2020.)
theorem blen1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cblen) c1 $;

--| The binary length of 2. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2020.)
theorem blen2:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cblen) c2 $;

--| Every positive integer can be represented as the sum of a power of 2 and a "remainder" less than the power. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem nnpw2p {i r: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wrex i (wrex r (wceq N (co (co c2 (cv i) cexp) (cv r) caddc)) (co cc0 (co c2 (cv i) cexp) cfzo)) cn0) $;

--| A number is a positive integer iff it can be represented as the sum of a power of 2 and a "remainder" less than the power. (Contributed by AV, 31-May-2020.)
theorem nnpw2pb {i r: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cn) (wrex i (wrex r (wceq N (co (co c2 (cv i) cexp) (cv r) caddc)) (co cc0 (co c2 (cv i) cexp) cfzo)) cn0) $;

--| The binary length of a nonnegative integer is 1 if the integer is 0 or 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2020.)
theorem blen1b (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wceq (cfv N cblen) c1) (wo (wceq N cc0) (wceq N c1))) $;

--| The binary length of a positive integer, doubled and increased by 1, is the binary length of the integer plus 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2010.)
theorem blennnt2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (cfv (co c2 N cmul) cblen) (co (cfv N cblen) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The floor of the binary logarithm of an odd integer greater than 1 is the floor of the binary logarithm of the integer decreased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 2-Jun-2020.)
theorem nnolog2flm1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn)) (wceq (cfv (co c2 N clogb) cfl) (cfv (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) clogb) cfl)) $;

--| The binary length of the half of an even positive integer is the binary length of the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 30-May-2010.)
theorem blennn0em1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cblen) (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin)) $;

--| The binary length of an odd integer greater than 1 is the binary length of the half of the integer decreased by 1, increased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2020.)
theorem blennngt2o2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cblen) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of an integer greater than 1 is the binary length of the integer divided by 2, increased by one. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2020.)
theorem blengt1fldiv2p1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cfl) cblen) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The binary length of an even positive integer is the binary length of the half of the integer, increased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 29-May-2020.)
theorem blennn0e2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (cfv N cblen) (co (cfv (co N c2 cdiv) cblen) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Extend class notation with the class of the digit extraction operation.
term cdig: class;

--| Definition of an operation to obtain the ` k ` th digit of a nonnegative real number ` r ` in the positional system with base ` b ` . ` k = - 1 ` corresponds to the first digit of the fractional part (for ` b = ` 10 the first digit after the decimal point), ` k = ` 0 corresponds to the last digit of the integer part (for ` b = ` 10 the first digit before the decimal point). See also ~ digit1 . Examples (not formal): ( 234.567 ( digit `` 10 ) 0 ) = 4; ( 2.567 ( digit `` 10 ) -2 ) = 6; ( 2345.67 ( digit `` 10 ) 2 ) = 3. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2020.)
axiom df_dig {k r b: setvar}:
  $ wceq cdig (cmpt b cn (cmpo k r cz (co cc0 cpnf cico) (co (cfv (co (co (cv b) (cneg (cv k)) cexp) (cv r) cmul) cfl) (cv b) cmo))) $;

--| Operation to obtain the ` k ` th digit of a nonnegative real number ` r ` in the positional system with base ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem digfval {k r: setvar} (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 cn) (wceq (cfv B2 cdig) (cmpo k r cz (co cc0 cpnf cico) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cneg (cv k)) cexp) (cv r) cmul) cfl) B2 cmo))) $;

--| The ` K ` th digit of a nonnegative real number ` R ` in the positional system with base ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem digval (B2 R K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cn) (wcel K cz) (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wceq (co K R (cfv B2 cdig)) (co (cfv (co (co B2 (cneg K) cexp) R cmul) cfl) B2 cmo)) $;

--| The ` K ` th digit of a nonnegative real number ` R ` in the positional system with base ` B ` is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2020.)
theorem digvalnn0 (B2 R K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cn) (wcel K cz) (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wcel (co K R (cfv B2 cdig)) cn0) $;

--| The ` K ` th digit of a nonnegative real number ` R ` in the positional system with base ` B ` . (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem nn0digval (B2 R K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cn) (wcel K cn0) (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wceq (co K R (cfv B2 cdig)) (co (cfv (co R (co B2 K cexp) cdiv) cfl) B2 cmo)) $;

--| The digits of the fractional part of a nonnegative integer are 0. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2020.)
theorem dignn0fr (B2 K N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 cn) (wcel K (cdif cz cn0)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co K N (cfv B2 cdig)) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ dignnld . (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem dignn0ldlem (B2 K N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N cn) (wcel K (cfv (co (cfv (co B2 N clogb) cfl) c1 caddc) cuz))) (wbr N (co B2 K cexp) clt) $;

--| The leading digits of a positive integer are 0. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem dignnld (B2 K N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N cn) (wcel K (cfv (co (cfv (co B2 N clogb) cfl) c1 caddc) cuz))) (wceq (co K N (cfv B2 cdig)) cc0) $;

--| The leading digits of a positive integer in a binary system are 0. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem dig2nn0ld (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel K (cfv (cfv N cblen) cuz))) (wceq (co K N (cfv c2 cdig)) cc0) $;

--| The first (relevant) digit of a positive integer in a binary system is 1. (Contributed by AV, 26-May-2020.)
theorem dig2nn1st (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co (co (cfv N cblen) c1 cmin) N (cfv c2 cdig)) c1) $;

--| All digits of 0 are 0. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem dig0 (B2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cn) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (co K cc0 (cfv B2 cdig)) cc0) $;

--| The ` K ` th digit of a power to the base is either 1 or 0. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem digexp (B2 K N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel K cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co K (co B2 N cexp) (cfv B2 cdig)) (cif (wceq K N) c1 cc0)) $;

--| All but one digits of 1 are 0. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem dig1 (B2 K: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel K cz)) (wceq (co K c1 (cfv B2 cdig)) (cif (wceq K cc0) c1 cc0)) $;

--| The ` 0 ` th digit of 1 is 1 in any positional system. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2020.)
theorem _0dig1 (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wceq (co cc0 c1 (cfv B2 cdig)) c1) $;

--| The integers 0 and 1 correspond to their last bit. (Contributed by AV, 28-May-2010.)
theorem _0dig2pr01 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cpr cc0 c1)) (wceq (co cc0 N (cfv c2 cdig)) N) $;

--| A digit of a nonnegative integer ` N ` in a binary system is either 0 or 1. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem dig2nn0 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel K cz)) (wcel (co K N (cfv c2 cdig)) (cpr cc0 c1)) $;

--| The last bit of an even integer is 0. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2010.)
theorem _0dig2nn0e (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (co cc0 N (cfv c2 cdig)) cc0) $;

--| The last bit of an odd integer is 1. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2010.)
theorem _0dig2nn0o (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wceq (co cc0 N (cfv c2 cdig)) c1) $;

--| The ` K ` th digit of a nonnegative integer ` N ` in a binary system is its ` K ` th bit. (Contributed by AV, 24-May-2020.)
theorem dig2bits (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel K cn0)) (wb (wceq (co K N (cfv c2 cdig)) c1) (wcel K (cfv N cbits))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ dignn0flhalf . (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2012.)
theorem dignn0flhalflem1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co (co A2 c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn) (wcel N cn)) (wbr (cfv (co (co A2 (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) c1 cmin) cfl) (cfv (co (co A2 c1 cmin) (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) cfl) clt) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ dignn0flhalf . (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2012.)
theorem dignn0flhalflem2 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co (co A2 c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co c2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) cdiv) cfl) (cfv (co (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) cfl) (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) cfl)) $;

--| The digits of the half of an even nonnegative integer are the digits of the integer shifted by 1. (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2010.)
theorem dignn0ehalf (A2 I: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (co A2 c2 cdiv) cn0) (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel I cn0)) (wceq (co (co I c1 caddc) A2 (cfv c2 cdig)) (co I (co A2 c2 cdiv) (cfv c2 cdig))) $;

--| The digits of the rounded half of a nonnegative integer are the digits of the integer shifted by 1. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2010.)
theorem dignn0flhalf (A2 I: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel I cn0)) (wceq (co (co I c1 caddc) A2 (cfv c2 cdig)) (co I (cfv (co A2 c2 cdiv) cfl) (cfv c2 cdig))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nn0sumshdig (induction step, even multiplier). (Contributed by AV, 3-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0sumshdiglemA {x3 k: setvar} (y1 a: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) cn) (wcel (co (cv a) c2 cdiv) cn)) (wcel (cv y1) cn)) (wi (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cblen) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (csu k (co cc0 (cv y1) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv x3) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) cn0) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) cblen) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wceq (cv a) (csu k (co cc0 (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv a) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ nn0sumshdig (induction step, odd multiplier). (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0sumshdiglemB {x3 k: setvar} (y1 a: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel (cv a) cn) (wcel (co (co (cv a) c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cn0)) (wcel (cv y1) cn)) (wi (wral x3 (wi (wceq (cfv (cv x3) cblen) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv x3) (csu k (co cc0 (cv y1) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv x3) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) cn0) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) cblen) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wceq (cv a) (csu k (co cc0 (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv a) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul))))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ nn0sumshdig (induction step). (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0sumshdiglem1 {k a: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) cn) (wi (wral a (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) cblen) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv a) (csu k (co cc0 (cv y1) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv a) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) cn0) (wral a (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) cblen) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wceq (cv a) (csu k (co cc0 (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv a) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) cn0)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ nn0sumshdig . (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0sumshdiglem2 {k a: setvar} (L: class):
  $ wi (wcel L cn) (wral a (wi (wceq (cfv (cv a) cblen) L) (wceq (cv a) (csu k (co cc0 L cfzo) (co (co (cv k) (cv a) (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul)))) cn0) $;

--| A nonnegative integer can be represented as sum of its shifted bits. (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0sumshdig {k: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wceq A2 (csu k (co cc0 (cfv A2 cblen) cfzo) (co (co (cv k) A2 (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul))) $;

--| Trivial algorithm to calculate the product of two nonnegative integers ` a ` and ` b ` by adding ` b ` to itself ` a ` times. (Contributed by AV, 17-May-2020.)
theorem nn0mulfsum {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (csu k (co c1 A2 cfz) B2)) $;

--| Standard algorithm (also known as "long multiplication" or "grade-school multiplication") to calculate the product of two nonnegative integers ` a ` and ` b ` by multiplying the multiplicand ` b ` by each digit of the multiplier ` a ` and then add up all the properly shifted results. Here, the binary representation of the multiplier ` a ` is used, i.e., the above mentioned "digits" are 0 or 1. This is a similar result as provided by ~ smumul . (Contributed by AV, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem nn0mullong {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) (csu k (co cc0 (cfv A2 cblen) cfzo) (co (co (co (cv k) A2 (cfv c2 cdig)) (co c2 (cv k) cexp) cmul) B2 cmul))) $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the n-ary functions.
term cnaryf: class;

--| Define the n-ary (endo)functions. (Contributed by AV, 11-May-2024.) (Revised by TA and SN, 7-Jun-2024.)
axiom df_naryf {x3 n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cnaryf (cmpo n x3 cn0 cvv (co (cv x3) (co (cv x3) (co cc0 (cv n) cfzo) cmap) cmap)) $;

--| The set of the n-ary (endo)functions on a class ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 13-May-2024.)
theorem naryfval (I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co N X cnaryf) (co X (co X I cmap) cmap)) $;

--| The set of the n-ary (endo)functions on a class ` X ` expressed with the notation of infinite Cartesian products. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem naryfvalixp {x3: setvar} (I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq (co N X cnaryf) (co X (cixp x3 I X) cmap)) $;

--| An n-ary (endo)function on a set ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-May-2024.)
theorem naryfvalel (F I N V X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel X V)) (wb (wcel F (co N X cnaryf)) (wf (co X I cmap) X F)) $;

--| Reverse closure for n-ary (endo)functions. (Contributed by AV, 14-May-2024.)
theorem naryrcl (F I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co N X cnaryf)) (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel X cvv)) $;

--| The value of an n-ary (endo)function on a set ` X ` is an element of ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 14-May-2024.)
theorem naryfvalelfv (A2 F I N X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 N cfzo) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F (co N X cnaryf)) (wf I X A2)) (wcel (cfv A2 F) X) $;

--| An n-ary (endo)function on a set ` X ` expressed as a function over the set of words on ` X ` of length ` n ` . (Contributed by AV, 4-Jun-2024.)
theorem naryfvalelwrdf {w: setvar} (F: class w) (N V X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel X V)) (wb (wcel F (co N X cnaryf)) (wf (crab w (wceq (cfv (cv w) chash) N) (cword X)) X F)) $;

--| A nullary (endo)function on a set ` X ` is a singleton of an ordered pair with the empty set as first component. A nullary function represents a constant: ` ( F `` (/) ) = C ` with ` C e. X ` , see also ~ 0aryfvalelfv . Instead of ` ( F `` (/) ) ` , nullary functions are usually written as ` F ( ) ` in literature. (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2024.)
theorem _0aryfvalel {x3: setvar} (F V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel F (co cc0 X cnaryf)) (wrex x3 (wceq F (csn (cop c0 (cv x3)))) X)) $;

--| The value of a nullary (endo)function on a set ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _0aryfvalelfv {x3: setvar} (F X: class):
  $ wi (wcel F (co cc0 X cnaryf)) (wrex x3 (wceq (cfv c0 F) (cv x3)) X) $;

--| A unary (endo)function on a set ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 15-May-2024.)
theorem _1aryfvalel (F V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel F (co c1 X cnaryf)) (wf (co X (csn cc0) cmap) X F)) $;

--| Closure of a unary (endo)function. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2024.)
theorem fv1arycl (A2 G X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel G (co c1 X cnaryf)) (wcel A2 X)) (wcel (cfv (csn (cop cc0 A2)) G) X) $;

--| A unary (endo)function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2024.)
theorem _1arympt1 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (F: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co X (csn cc0) cmap) (cfv (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wf X X A2)) (wcel F (co c1 X cnaryf)) $;

--| The value of a unary (endo)function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by AV, 16-May-2024.)
theorem _1arympt1fv {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3) (V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co X (csn cc0) cmap) (cfv (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (csn (cop cc0 B2)) F) (cfv B2 A2)) $;

--| The value of the mapping of unary (endo)functions. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2024.)
theorem _1arymaptfv {x3 h: setvar} (F: class) (H: class x3 h) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c1 X cnaryf) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co c1 X cnaryf)) (wceq (cfv F H) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) F))) $;

--| The mapping of unary (endo)functions is a function into the set of endofunctions. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2024.)
theorem _1arymaptf {x3 h: setvar} (H: class x3 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c1 X cnaryf) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf (co c1 X cnaryf) (co X X cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of unary (endo)functions is a one-to-one function into the set of endofunctions. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _1arymaptf1 {x3 h: setvar} (H: class x3 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c1 X cnaryf) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf1 (co c1 X cnaryf) (co X X cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of unary (endo)functions is a function onto the set of endofunctions. (Contributed by AV, 18-May-2024.)
theorem _1arymaptfo {x3 h: setvar} (H: class x3 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c1 X cnaryf) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wfo (co c1 X cnaryf) (co X X cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of unary (endo)functions is a one-to-one function onto the set of endofunctions. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _1arymaptf1o {x3 h: setvar} (H: class x3 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c1 X cnaryf) (cmpt x3 X (cfv (csn (cop cc0 (cv x3))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf1o (co c1 X cnaryf) (co X X cmap) H) $;

--| The set of unary (endo)functions and the set of endofunctions are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _1aryenef (X: class):
  $ wbr (co c1 X cnaryf) (co X X cmap) cen $;

--| The set of unary (endo)functions and the base set of the monoid of endofunctions are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _1aryenefmnd (X: class):
  $ wbr (co c1 X cnaryf) (cfv (cfv X cefmnd) cbs) cen $;

--| A binary (endo)function on a set ` X ` . (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2024.)
theorem _2aryfvalel (F V X: class):
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wb (wcel F (co c2 X cnaryf)) (wf (co X (cpr cc0 c1) cmap) X F)) $;

--| Closure of a binary (endo)function. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2024.)
theorem fv2arycl (A2 B2 G X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel G (co c2 X cnaryf)) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wcel (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 B2)) G) X) $;

--| A binary (endo)function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2024.)
theorem _2arympt {x3: setvar} (F: class x3) (O V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co X (cpr cc0 c1) cmap) (co (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv x3)) O)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X V) (wf (cxp X X) X O)) (wcel F (co c2 X cnaryf)) $;

--| The value of a binary (endo)function in maps-to notation. (Contributed by AV, 20-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymptfv {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3) (O V X: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co X (cpr cc0 c1) cmap) (co (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv x3)) O)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X V) (wcel A2 X) (wcel B2 X)) (wceq (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 A2) (cop c1 B2)) F) (co A2 B2 O)) $;

--| The value of the mapping of binary (endo)functions. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymaptfv {x3 y1 h: setvar} (F: class) (H: class x3 y1 h) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c2 X cnaryf) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F (co c2 X cnaryf)) (wceq (cfv F H) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) F))) $;

--| The mapping of binary (endo)functions is a function into the set of binary operations. (Contributed by AV, 21-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymaptf {x3 y1 h: setvar} (H: class x3 y1 h) (V: class y1) (X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c2 X cnaryf) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf (co c2 X cnaryf) (co X (cxp X X) cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of binary (endo)functions is a one-to-one function into the set of binary operations. (Contributed by AV, 22-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymaptf1 {x3 y1 h: setvar} (H: class x3 y1 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c2 X cnaryf) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf1 (co c2 X cnaryf) (co X (cxp X X) cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of binary (endo)functions is a function onto the set of binary operations. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymaptfo {x3 y1 h: setvar} (H: class x3 y1 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c2 X cnaryf) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wfo (co c2 X cnaryf) (co X (cxp X X) cmap) H) $;

--| The mapping of binary (endo)functions is a one-to-one function onto the set of binary operations. (Contributed by AV, 23-May-2024.)
theorem _2arymaptf1o {x3 y1 h: setvar} (H: class x3 y1 h) (V X: class):
  $ wceq H (cmpt h (co c2 X cnaryf) (cmpo x3 y1 X X (cfv (cpr (cop cc0 (cv x3)) (cop c1 (cv y1))) (cv h)))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X V) (wf1o (co c2 X cnaryf) (co X (cxp X X) cmap) H) $;

--| The set of binary (endo)functions and the set of binary operations are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 19-May-2024.)
theorem _2aryenef (X: class):
  $ wbr (co c2 X cnaryf) (co X (cxp X X) cmap) cen $;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include iterated functions.
term citco: class;

--| Extend the definition of a class to include the Ackermann function operator.
term cack: class;

--| Define a function (recursively) that returns the n-th iterate of a class (usually a function) with regard to composition. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Apr-2024.) (Revised by AV, 2-May-2024.)
axiom df_itco {f2 g2 i j: setvar}:
  $ wceq citco (cmpt f2 cvv (cseq (cmpo g2 j cvv cvv (ccom (cv f2) (cv g2))) (cmpt i cn0 (cif (wceq (cv i) cc0) (cres cid (cdm (cv f2))) (cv f2))) cc0)) $;

--| Define the Ackermann function (recursively). (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Apr-2024.) (Revised by AV, 2-May-2024.)
axiom df_ack {f2 i j n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cack (cseq (cmpo f2 j cvv cvv (cmpt n cn0 (cfv c1 (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cfv (cv f2) citco))))) (cmpt i cn0 (cif (wceq (cv i) cc0) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) c1 caddc)) (cv i))) cc0) $;

--| The value of the function that returns the n-th iterate of a class (usually a function) with regard to composition. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval {g2 i j: setvar} (F: class) (V: class g2 i j):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (cfv F citco) (cseq (cmpo g2 j cvv cvv (ccom F (cv g2))) (cmpt i cn0 (cif (wceq (cv i) cc0) (cres cid (cdm F)) F)) cc0)) $;

--| A function iterated zero times (defined as identity function). (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval0 (F V: class):
  $ wi (wcel F V) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv F citco)) (cres cid (cdm F))) $;

--| A function iterated once. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval1 (F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel F) (wcel F V)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cfv F citco)) F) $;

--| A function iterated twice. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval2 (F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel F) (wcel F V)) (wceq (cfv c2 (cfv F citco)) (ccom F F)) $;

--| A function iterated three times. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval3 (F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wrel F) (wcel F V)) (wceq (cfv c3 (cfv F citco)) (ccom F (ccom F F))) $;

--| A mapping iterated zero times (defined as identity function). (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcoval0mpt {n: setvar} (A2: class) (B2 F V W: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wral n (wcel B2 W) A2)) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n A2 (cv n))) $;

--| The value of the function that returns the n-th iterate of a function with regard to composition at a successor. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalsuc {g2 j: setvar} (F: class) (G V Y: class g2 j):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel Y cn0) (wceq (cfv Y (cfv F citco)) G)) (wceq (cfv (co Y c1 caddc) (cfv F citco)) (co G F (cmpo g2 j cvv cvv (ccom F (cv g2))))) $;

--| The value of the function that returns the n-th iterate of a function with regard to composition at a successor. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalsucov (F G V Y: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel Y cn0) (wceq (cfv Y (cfv F citco)) G)) (wceq (cfv (co Y c1 caddc) (cfv F citco)) (ccom F G)) $;

--| The n-th iterate of an endofunction is an endofunction. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalendof (ph: wff) (A2 F N V: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 A2 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf A2 A2 (cfv N (cfv F citco))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ itcovalpc : induction basis. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalpclem1 {n: setvar} (C: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C cn0) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) (co C cc0 cmul) caddc))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ itcovalpc : induction step. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalpclem2 {n: setvar} (y1: setvar) (C: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) (co C (cv y1) cmul) caddc))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) (co C (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cmul) caddc)))) $;

--| The value of the function that returns the n-th iterate of the "plus a constant" function with regard to composition. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalpc {n: setvar} (C: class) (F: class n) (I: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wceq (cfv I (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) (co C I cmul) caddc))) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ itcovalt2lem2 . (Contributed by AV, 6-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalt2lem2lem1 (C N Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel Y cn) (wcel C cn0)) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co (co (co N C caddc) Y cmul) C cmin) cn0) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ itcovalt2lem2 . (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalt2lem2lem2 (C N Y: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel Y cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co (co c2 (co (co (co N C caddc) (co c2 Y cexp) cmul) C cmin) cmul) C caddc) (co (co (co N C caddc) (co c2 (co Y c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) C cmin)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for ~ itcovalt2 : induction basis. (Contributed by AV, 5-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalt2lem1 {n: setvar} (C: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel C cn0) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (co (cv n) C caddc) (co c2 cc0 cexp) cmul) C cmin))) $;

--| Lemma 2 for ~ itcovalt2 : induction step. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalt2lem2 {n: setvar} (y1: setvar) (C: class) (F: class n):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv y1) cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv y1) (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (co (cv n) C caddc) (co c2 (cv y1) cexp) cmul) C cmin))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (co (cv n) C caddc) (co c2 (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) cexp) cmul) C cmin)))) $;

--| The value of the function that returns the n-th iterate of the "times 2 plus a constant" function with regard to composition. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem itcovalt2 {n: setvar} (C: class) (F: class n) (I: class):
  $ wceq F (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cn0) (wcel C cn0)) (wceq (cfv I (cfv F citco)) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co (co (cv n) C caddc) (co c2 I cexp) cmul) C cmin))) $;

--| The Ackermann function at a successor of the first argument as a mapping of the second argument. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Apr-2024.) (Revised by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackvalsuc1mpt {n: setvar} (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wceq (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cack) (cmpt n cn0 (cfv c1 (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) (cfv (cfv M cack) citco))))) $;

--| The Ackermann function at a successor of the first argument and an arbitrary second argument. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Apr-2024.) (Revised by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackvalsuc1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (cfv (cfv M cack) citco)))) $;

--| The Ackermann function at 0. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem ackval0 {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cack) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at 1. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackval1 {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cack) (cmpt n cn0 (co (cv n) c2 caddc)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at 2. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackval2 {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 cack) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (cv n) cmul) c3 caddc)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at 3. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem ackval3 {n: setvar}:
  $ wceq (cfv c3 cack) (cmpt n cn0 (co (co c2 (co (cv n) c3 caddc) cexp) c3 cmin)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at any nonnegative integer is an endofunction on the nonnegative integers. (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2024.)
theorem ackendofnn0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wf cn0 cn0 (cfv M cack)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at any nonnegative integer is a function on the nonnegative integers. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-May-2024.)
theorem ackfnnn0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wfn (cfv M cack) cn0) $;

--| The Ackermann function at 0 (for the first argument). This is the first equation of P&eacute;ter's definition of the Ackermann function. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackval0val (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wceq (cfv M (cfv cc0 cack)) (co M c1 caddc)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at a successor (of the first argument). This is the second equation of P&eacute;ter's definition of the Ackermann function. (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackvalsuc0val (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv M cack))) $;

--| The Ackermann function at the successors. This is the third equation of P&eacute;ter's definition of the Ackermann function. (Contributed by AV, 8-May-2024.)
theorem ackvalsucsucval (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cack)) (cfv (cfv N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cack)) (cfv M cack))) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (0,0), (0,1), (0,2). (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2024.)
theorem ackval0012:
  $ wceq (cotp (cfv cc0 (cfv cc0 cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv cc0 cack)) (cfv c2 (cfv cc0 cack))) (cotp c1 c2 c3) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (1,0), (1,1), (1,2). (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackval1012:
  $ wceq (cotp (cfv cc0 (cfv c1 cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv c1 cack)) (cfv c2 (cfv c1 cack))) (cotp c2 c3 c4) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (2,0), (2,1), (2,2). (Contributed by AV, 4-May-2024.)
theorem ackval2012:
  $ wceq (cotp (cfv cc0 (cfv c2 cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv c2 cack)) (cfv c2 (cfv c2 cack))) (cotp c3 c5 c7) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (3,0), (3,1), (3,2). (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2024.)
theorem ackval3012:
  $ wceq (cotp (cfv cc0 (cfv c3 cack)) (cfv c1 (cfv c3 cack)) (cfv c2 (cfv c3 cack))) (cotp c5 (cdc c1 c3) (cdc c2 c9)) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (4,0). (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval40:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv c4 cack)) (cdc c1 c3) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (4,1). (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval41a:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 (cfv c4 cack)) (co (co c2 (cdc c1 c6) cexp) c3 cmin) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (4,1). (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval41:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 (cfv c4 cack)) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c3) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (4,2). (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval42:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 (cfv c4 cack)) (co (co c2 (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c6) cexp) c3 cmin) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (4,2), expressed with powers of 2. (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval42a:
  $ wceq (cfv c2 (cfv c4 cack)) (co (co c2 (co c2 (co c2 (co c2 c2 cexp) cexp) cexp) cexp) c3 cmin) $;

--| The Ackermann function at (5,0). (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2024.)
theorem ackval50:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 (cfv c5 cack)) (cdc (cdc (cdc (cdc c6 c5) c5) c3) c3) $;

--| The function value of unordered pair of ordered pairs with first components 1 and 2 at 1. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2023.)
theorem fv1prop (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wceq (cfv c1 (cpr (cop c1 A2) (cop c2 B2))) A2) $;

--| The function value of unordered pair of ordered pairs with first components 1 and 2 at 1. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2023.)
theorem fv2prop (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 V) (wceq (cfv c2 (cpr (cop c1 A2) (cop c2 B2))) B2) $;

--| Transformation of a sum of a product of a difference and a product with the subtrahend of the difference. (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem submuladdmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) C cmul) (co B2 D cmul) caddc) (co (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 (co D C cmin) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Combination of two real affine combinations, one class variable resolved. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem affinecomb1 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C S E F: class) (G: class t):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cr) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co G F cmin) (co C B2 cmin) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex t (wa (wceq A2 (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) B2 cmul) (co (cv t) C cmul) caddc)) (wceq E (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) F cmul) (co (cv t) G cmul) caddc))) cr) (wceq E (co (co S (co A2 B2 cmin) cmul) F caddc))) $;

--| Combination of two real affine combinations, presented without fraction. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem affinecomb2 {t: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C E F G: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wrex t (wa (wceq A2 (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) B2 cmul) (co (cv t) C cmul) caddc)) (wceq E (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) F cmul) (co (cv t) G cmul) caddc))) cr) (wceq (co (co C B2 cmin) E cmul) (co (co (co G F cmin) A2 cmul) (co (co F C cmul) (co B2 G cmul) cmin) caddc))) $;

--| Identity of an affine combination. (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem affineid (ph: wff) (A2 T: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co (co c1 T cmin) A2 cmul) (co T A2 cmul) caddc) A2) $;

--| Subtract the reciprocal of 1 minus a number from 1 results in the number divided by the number minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2023.)
theorem _1subrec1sub (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 c1)) (wceq (co c1 (co c1 (co c1 A2 cmin) cdiv) cmin) (co A2 (co A2 c1 cmin) cdiv)) $;

--| The sum of two squares of real numbers is a real number. (Contributed by AV, 7-Feb-2023.)
theorem resum2sqcl (A2 B2 Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel Q cr) $;

--| The sum of the square of a nonzero real number and the square of another real number is greater than zero. (Contributed by AV, 7-Feb-2023.)
theorem resum2sqgt0 (A2 B2 Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wbr cc0 Q clt) $;

--| The sum of the square of a nonzero real number and the square of another real number is a positive real number. (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2023.)
theorem resum2sqrp (A2 B2 Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel Q crp) $;

--| The sum of the square of two real numbers is greater than zero if at least one of the real numbers is nonzero. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2023.)
theorem resum2sqorgt0 (A2 B2 Q: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wo (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0))) (wbr cc0 Q clt) $;

--| Membership in and outside of a closed real interval. (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2023.)
theorem reorelicc (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (w3o (wbr C A2 clt) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wbr B2 C clt)) $;

--| The x-coordinate of a point in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 is a real number. (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2pxel (P I X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X P) (wcel (cfv cc0 X) cr) $;

--| The y-coordinate of a point in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 is a real number. (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2pyel (P I X: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wcel X P) (wcel (cfv c1 X) cr) $;

--| A doubleton word of real numbers is a point in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2023.)
theorem prelrrx2 (A2 B2 P I: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wcel (cs2 A2 B2) P) $;

--| Equivalent condition for ` Z ` being a point in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2, determined by its coordinates. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 10-Oct-2026.)
theorem prelrrx2bd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 P I Z: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq Z (cs2 A2 B2)) (wa (wcel Z P) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 Z) A2) (wceq (cfv c1 Z) B2)))) $;

--| Equivalent condition for being one of a pair of points in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2, determined by its coordinates. (Contributed by AV, 7-May-2023.)
theorem prelrrx2b (A2 B2 P I X Y Z: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel Y cr))) (wb (wa (wcel Z P) (wo (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 Z) A2) (wceq (cfv c1 Z) B2)) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 Z) X) (wceq (cfv c1 Z) Y)))) (wcel Z (cpr (cs2 A2 B2) (cs2 X Y)))) $;

--| If two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 are different, then they are different at least at one coordinate. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2pnecoorneor (P I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wo (wne (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y)) (wne (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y))) $;

--| If two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 are different, then at least one difference of two corresponding coordinates is not 0. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2pnedifcoorneor (A2 B2 P I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co (cfv c1 Y) (cfv c1 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wo (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) $;

--| If two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 are different, then at least one difference of two corresponding coordinates is not 0. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2pnedifcoorneorr (A2 B2 P I X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wo (wne A2 cc0) (wne B2 cc0)) $;

--| There is a bijection between the set of ordered pairs of real numbers (the cartesian product of the real numbers) and the set of points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane (represented as mappings from ` ( 0 ..^ 2 ) ` to the real numbers). (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2xpref1o {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq R (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (cs2 (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp cr cr) R F $;

--| The set of points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane and the set of ordered pairs of real numbers (the cartesian product of the real numbers) are equinumerous. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2xpreen (R: class):
  $ wceq R (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wbr R (cxp cr cr) cen $;

--| The lexicographical ordering for points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane: a point is less than another point iff its first coordinate is less than the first coordinate of the other point, or the first coordinates of both points are equal and the second coordinate of the first point is less than the second coordinate of the other point: ` <. a , b >. <_ <. x , y >. ` iff ` ( a < x \/ ( a = x /\ b <_ y ) ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2plord {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (O: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) R) (wcel (cv y1) R)) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1)) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1))) (wbr (cfv c1 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv y1)) clt))))) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel X R) (wcel Y R)) (wb (wbr X Y O) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y)) (wbr (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y) clt)))) $;

--| The lexicographical ordering for points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane: a point is less than another point if its first coordinate is less than the first coordinate of the other point. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2plord1 {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (O: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) R) (wcel (cv y1) R)) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1)) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1))) (wbr (cfv c1 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv y1)) clt))))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X R) (wcel Y R) (wbr (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y) clt)) (wbr X Y O) $;

--| The lexicographical ordering for points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane: if the first coordinates of two points are equal, a point is less than another point iff the second coordinate of the point is less than the second coordinate of the other point. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2plord2 {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (O: class x3 y1) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) R) (wcel (cv y1) R)) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1)) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1))) (wbr (cfv c1 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv y1)) clt))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X R) (wcel Y R) (wceq (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y))) (wb (wbr X Y O) (wbr (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y) clt)) $;

--| The set of points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane with the lexicographical ordering is isomorphic to the cartesian product of the real numbers with the lexicographical ordering implied by the ordering of the real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2plordisom {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (T F O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) R) (wcel (cv y1) R)) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1)) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1))) (wbr (cfv c1 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv y1)) clt))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq F (cmpo x3 y1 cr cr (cs2 (cv x3) (cv y1))) $ >
  $ wceq T (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cxp cr cr)) (wcel (cv y1) (cxp cr cr))) (wo (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv (cv x3) c1st) (cfv (cv y1) c1st)) (wbr (cfv (cv x3) c2nd) (cfv (cv y1) c2nd) clt))))) $ >
  $ wiso (cxp cr cr) R T O F $;

--| The lexicographical ordering for points in the two dimensional Euclidean plane is a strict total ordering. (Contributed by AV, 12-Mar-2023.)
theorem rrx2plordso {x3 y1: setvar} (R: class) (O: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq O (copab x3 y1 (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) R) (wcel (cv y1) R)) (wo (wbr (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1)) clt) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv x3)) (cfv cc0 (cv y1))) (wbr (cfv c1 (cv x3)) (cfv c1 (cv y1)) clt))))) $ >
  $ wceq R (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wor R O $;

--| The Euclidean distance of a point to the origin in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 26-Feb-2023.)
theorem ehl2eudisval0 (D E F X c_0: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv c2 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp (co cc0 c2 cfzo) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel F X) (wceq (co F c_0 D) (cfv (co (co (cfv cc0 F) c2 cexp) (co (cfv c1 F) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| An upper bound of the Euclidean distance of a point to the origin in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 9-May-2023.)
theorem ehl2eudis0lt (D R E F X c_0: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv c2 cehl) $ >
  $ wceq X (co cr (co cc0 c2 cfzo) cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp (co cc0 c2 cfzo) (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel F X) (wcel R crp)) (wb (wbr (co F c_0 D) R clt) (wbr (co (co (cfv cc0 F) c2 cexp) (co (cfv c1 F) c2 cexp) caddc) (co R c2 cexp) clt)) $;

--| Declare the syntax for lines in generalized real Euclidean spaces.
term cline: class;

--| Declare the syntax for spheres in generalized real Euclidean spaces.
term csph: class;

--| Definition of lines passing through two different points in a left module (or any extended structure having a base set, an addition, and a scalar multiplication). (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
axiom df_line {x3 y1 w t p: setvar}:
  $ wceq cline (cmpt w cvv (cmpo x3 y1 (cfv (cv w) cbs) (cdif (cfv (cv w) cbs) (csn (cv x3))) (crab p (wrex t (wceq (cv p) (co (co (co (cfv (cfv (cv w) csca) cur) (cv t) (cfv (cfv (cv w) csca) csg)) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cvsca)) (co (cv t) (cv y1) (cfv (cv w) cvsca)) (cfv (cv w) cplusg))) (cfv (cfv (cv w) csca) cbs)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)))) $;

--| Definition of spheres for given centers and radii in a metric space (or more generally, in a distance space, see ~ distspace , or even in any extended structure having a base set and a distance function into the real numbers. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
axiom df_sph {x3 w r p: setvar}:
  $ wceq csph (cmpt w cvv (cmpo x3 r (cfv (cv w) cbs) (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (crab p (wceq (co (cv p) (cv x3) (cfv (cv w) cds)) (cv r)) (cfv (cv w) cbs)))) $;

--| The lines passing through two different points in a left module (or any extended structure having a base set, an addition, and a scalar multiplication). (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem lines {x3 y1 t p: setvar} (B2: class t) (c_pl: class x3 y1 t p) (S: class x3 y1 p) (c_x c_1: class x3 y1 t p) (K: class x3 y1 p) (L c_mi V: class x3 y1 t p) (W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W cline) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W V) (wceq L (cmpo x3 y1 B2 (cdif B2 (csn (cv x3))) (crab p (wrex t (wceq (cv p) (co (co (co c_1 (cv t) c_mi) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv t) (cv y1) c_x) c_pl)) K) B2))) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a left module (or any extended structure having a base set, an addition, and a scalar multiplication). (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem line {t p: setvar} (B2: class t) (c_pl: class t p) (S: class p) (c_x c_1: class t p) (K: class p) (L c_mi V: class t p) (W X Y: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv W cline) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csca) $ >
  $ wceq K (cfv S cbs) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv W cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv W cplusg) $ >
  $ wceq c_mi (cfv S csg) $ >
  $ wceq c_1 (cfv S cur) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel W V) (w3a (wcel X B2) (wcel Y B2) (wne X Y))) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wrex t (wceq (cv p) (co (co (co c_1 (cv t) c_mi) X c_x) (co (cv t) Y c_x) c_pl)) K) B2)) $;

--| Definition of lines passing through two different points in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxlines {x3 y1 t p: setvar} (P: class x3 y1 t) (c_pl c_x: class x3 y1 t p) (E I: class) (L: class x3 y1 t p):
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv E cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv E cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I cfn) (wceq L (cmpo x3 y1 P (cdif P (csn (cv x3))) (crab p (wrex t (wceq (cv p) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cv x3) c_x) (co (cv t) (cv y1) c_x) c_pl)) cr) P))) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrxline {t p: setvar} (P: class t) (c_pl c_x: class t p) (E I: class) (L: class t p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq c_x (cfv E cvsca) $ >
  $ wceq c_pl (cfv E cplusg) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cfn) (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y))) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wrex t (wceq (cv p) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) X c_x) (co (cv t) Y c_x) c_pl)) cr) P)) $;

--| Definition of lines passing through two different points in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension, expressed by their coordinates. (Contributed by AV, 13-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrxlinesc {x3 y1 t i p: setvar} (P: class x3 y1) (E: class i) (I: class) (L: class x3 y1 t i p):
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wi (wcel I cfn) (wceq L (cmpo x3 y1 P (cdif P (csn (cv x3))) (crab p (wrex t (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) I) cr) P))) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension, expressed by its coordinates. Remark: This proof is shorter and requires less distinct variables than the proof using ~ rrxlinesc . (Contributed by AV, 13-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrxlinec {t i p: setvar} (P: class) (E: class i) (I: class) (L: class t i p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel I cfn) (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y))) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wrex t (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv i) X) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv (cv i) Y) cmul) caddc)) I) cr) P)) $;

--| Lemma 1 for - eenglngeehlnm . (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2023.)
theorem eenglngeehlnmlem1 {t i k m l: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar) (N: class) (p: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel (cv x3) (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap)) (wcel (cv y1) (cdif (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap) (csn (cv x3))))) (wcel (cv p) (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap))) (wi (w3o (wrex k (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv k) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wrex l (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) (co (co (co c1 (cv l) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) cmul) (co (cv l) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cico)) (wrex m (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) (co (co (co c1 (cv m) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cioc))) (wrex t (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) cr)) $;

--| Lemma 2 for - eenglngeehlnm . (Contributed by AV, 15-Feb-2023.)
theorem eenglngeehlnmlem2 {t i k m l: setvar} (x3 y1: setvar) (N: class) (p: setvar):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel (cv x3) (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap)) (wcel (cv y1) (cdif (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap) (csn (cv x3))))) (wcel (cv p) (co cr (co c1 N cfz) cmap))) (wi (wrex t (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) cr) (w3o (wrex k (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv k) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv k) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wrex l (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) (co (co (co c1 (cv l) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) cmul) (co (cv l) (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cico)) (wrex m (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) (cv y1)) (co (co (co c1 (cv m) cmin) (cfv (cv i) (cv x3)) cmul) (co (cv m) (cfv (cv i) (cv p)) cmul) caddc)) (co c1 N cfz)) (co cc0 c1 cioc)))) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2. (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.) (Proof shortened by AV, 13-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2line {t p: setvar} (P E I: class) (L: class t p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wrex t (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv cc0 X) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv cc0 Y) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv p)) (co (co (co c1 (cv t) cmin) (cfv c1 X) cmul) (co (cv t) (cfv c1 Y) cmul) caddc))) cr) P)) $;

--| The vertical line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 in "standard form". (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2vlinest {p: setvar} (P E I: class) (L: class p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y)) (wne (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y)))) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cfv cc0 X)) P)) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 in "standard form". (Contributed by AV, 2-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2linest {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class p) (P E I: class) (L: class p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co (cfv c1 Y) (cfv c1 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv cc0 Y) cmul) (co (cfv cc0 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmul) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wceq (co A2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) (co (co B2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) C caddc)) P)) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2, expressed by the slope ` S ` between the two points ("point-slope form"), sometimes also written as ` ( ( p `` 1 ) - ( X `` 1 ) ) = ( S x. ( ( p `` 0 ) - ( X `` 0 ) ) ) ` . (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem rrx2linesl {p: setvar} (P: class) (S: class p) (E I: class) (L: class p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq S (co (co (cfv c1 Y) (cfv c1 X) cmin) (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) cdiv) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne (cfv cc0 X) (cfv cc0 Y))) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wceq (cfv c1 (cv p)) (co (co S (co (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) cmul) (cfv c1 X) caddc)) P)) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 in another "standard form" (usually with ` ( p `` 1 ) = x ` and ` ( p `` 2 ) = y ` ). (Contributed by AV, 23-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrx2linest2 {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class p) (P E I: class) (L: class p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv cc0 Y) cmul) (co (cfv cc0 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmul) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wceq (co X Y L) (crab p (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) P)) $;

--| The line passing through the two different points ` X ` and ` Y ` in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 in another "standard form" (usually with ` ( p `` 0 ) = x ` and ` ( p `` 1 ) = y ` ). (Contributed by AV, 23-Feb-2023.)
theorem elrrx2linest2 (A2 B2 C P E G I L X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq B2 (co (cfv cc0 Y) (cfv cc0 X) cmin) $ >
  $ wceq C (co (co (cfv c1 X) (cfv cc0 Y) cmul) (co (cfv cc0 X) (cfv c1 Y) cmul) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel X P) (wcel Y P) (wne X Y)) (wb (wcel G (co X Y L)) (wa (wcel G P) (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 G) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 G) cmul) caddc) C))) $;

--| The spheres for given centers and radii in a metric space (or any extensible structure having a base set and a distance function). (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem spheres {x3 r p: setvar} (B2: class) (D S V: class x3 r p) (W: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csph) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wi (wcel W V) (wceq S (cmpo x3 r B2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) (crab p (wceq (co (cv p) (cv x3) D) (cv r)) B2))) $;

--| A sphere with center ` X ` and radius ` R ` in a metric space (or any extensible structure having a base set and a distance function). (Contributed by AV, 22-Jan-2023.)
theorem sphere {p: setvar} (B2: class) (D: class p) (R: class) (S V: class p) (W X: class):
  $ wceq B2 (cfv W cbs) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv W csph) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv W cds) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel W V) (wcel X B2) (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cicc))) (wceq (co X R S) (crab p (wceq (co (cv p) X D) R) B2)) $;

--| The sphere with center ` M ` and radius ` R ` in a generalized real Euclidean space of finite dimension. Remark: this theorem holds also for the degenerate case ` R < 0 ` (negative radius): in this case, ` ( M S R ) ` is empty. (Contributed by AV, 5-Feb-2023.)
theorem rrxsphere {p: setvar} (D: class p) (P R: class) (S: class p) (E I M: class):
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq D (cfv E cds) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv E csph) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel I cfn) (wcel M P) (wcel R cr)) (wceq (co M R S) (crab p (wceq (co (cv p) M D) R) P)) $;

--| The sphere with center ` M ` and radius ` R ` in a two dimensional Euclidean space is a circle. (Contributed by AV, 5-Feb-2023.)
theorem _2sphere {p: setvar} (C: class p) (P R: class) (S: class p) (E I M: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv E csph) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab p (wceq (co (co (co (cfv cc0 (cv p)) (cfv cc0 M) cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv c1 (cv p)) (cfv c1 M) cmin) c2 cexp) caddc) (co R c2 cexp)) P) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M P) (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wceq (co M R S) C) $;

--| The sphere around the origin ` .0. ` (see ~ rrx0 ) with radius ` R ` in a two dimensional Euclidean space is a circle. (Contributed by AV, 5-Feb-2023.)
theorem _2sphere0 {p: setvar} (C: class p) (P R: class) (S: class p) (E I c_0: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq S (cfv E csph) $ >
  $ wceq c_0 (cxp I (csn cc0)) $ >
  $ wceq C (crab p (wceq (co (co (cfv cc0 (cv p)) c2 cexp) (co (cfv c1 (cv p)) c2 cexp) caddc) (co R c2 cexp)) P) $ >
  $ wi (wcel R (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (wceq (co c_0 R S) C) $;

--| Lemma for ~ line2y . This proof is based on counterexamples for the following cases: 1. ` C =/= 0 ` : p = (0,0) (LHS of biconditional is false, RHS is true); 2. ` C = 0 /\ B =/= 0 ` : p = (1,-A/B) (LHS of biconditional is true, RHS is false); 3. ` A = B = C = 0 ` : p = (1,1) (LHS of biconditional is true, RHS is false). (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2023.)
theorem line2ylem {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C P: class) (I: class p):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wral p (wb (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) (wceq (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cc0)) P) (w3a (wne A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0) (wceq C cc0))) $;

--| Example for a line ` G ` passing through two different points in "standard form". (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2023.)
theorem line2 {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C P E: class) (G: class p) (I: class) (L: class p) (X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab p (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) P) $ >
  $ wceq X (cs2 cc0 (co C B2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cs2 c1 (co (co C A2 cmin) B2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cr)) (wceq G (co X Y L)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ line2x . This proof is based on counterexamples for the following cases: 1. ` M =/= ( C / B ) ` : p = (0,C/B) (LHS of biconditional is true, RHS is false); 2. ` A =/= 0 /\ M = ( C / B ) ` : p = (1,C/B) (LHS of biconditional is false, RHS is true). (Contributed by AV, 4-Feb-2023.)
theorem line2xlem {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C P E: class) (G: class p) (I: class) (L: class p) (M X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab p (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) P) $ >
  $ wceq X (cs2 cc0 M) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cs2 c1 M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cr)) (wcel M cr)) (wi (wral p (wb (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) (wceq (cfv c1 (cv p)) M)) P) (wa (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq M (co C B2 cdiv)))) $;

--| Example for a horizontal line ` G ` passing through two different points in "standard form". (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2023.)
theorem line2x {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C P E: class) (G: class p) (I: class) (L: class p) (M X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab p (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) P) $ >
  $ wceq X (cs2 cc0 M) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cs2 c1 M) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel C cr)) (wcel M cr)) (wb (wceq G (co X Y L)) (wa (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq M (co C B2 cdiv)))) $;

--| Example for a vertical line ` G ` passing through two different points in "standard form". (Contributed by AV, 3-Feb-2023.)
theorem line2y {p: setvar} (A2 B2 C P E: class) (G: class p) (I: class) (L: class p) (M N X Y: class):
  $ wceq I (co cc0 c2 cfzo) $ >
  $ wceq E (cfv I crrx) $ >
  $ wceq P (co cr I cmap) $ >
  $ wceq L (cfv E cline) $ >
  $ wceq G (crab p (wceq (co (co A2 (cfv cc0 (cv p)) cmul) (co B2 (cfv c1 (cv p)) cmul) caddc) C) P) $ >
  $ wceq X (cs2 cc0 M) $ >
  $ wceq Y (cs2 cc0 N) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (w3a (wcel M cr) (wcel N cr) (wne M N))) (wb (wceq G (co X Y L)) (w3a (wne A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0) (wceq C cc0))) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about intersections of lines and circles in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 . (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2023.)
theorem itsclc0lem1 (S T U V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel S cr) (wcel T cr) (wcel U cr)) (wa (wcel V cr) (wbr cc0 V cle)) (wa (wcel W cr) (wne W cc0))) (wcel (co (co (co S U cmul) (co T (cfv V csqrt) cmul) caddc) W cdiv) cr) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about intersections of lines and circles in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 . (Contributed by AV, 3-May-2023.)
theorem itsclc0lem2 (S T U V W: class):
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wcel S cr) (wcel T cr) (wcel U cr)) (wa (wcel V cr) (wbr cc0 V cle)) (wa (wcel W cr) (wne W cc0))) (wcel (co (co (co S U cmul) (co T (cfv V csqrt) cmul) cmin) W cdiv) cr) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about intersections of lines and circles in a real Euclidean space of dimension 2 . (Contributed by AV, 2-May-2023.)
theorem itsclc0lem3 (A2 B2 C D Q R: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq D (co (co (co R c2 cexp) Q cmul) (co C c2 cexp) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wcel R cr)) (wcel D cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itsclc0 . Quadratic equation for the y-coordinate of the intersection points of a nonhorizontal line and a circle. (Contributed by AV, 6-Feb-2023.)
theorem itscnhlc0yqe (A2 B2 C Q R T U X Y: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
  $ wceq T (cneg (co c2 (co B2 C cmul) cmul)) $ >
  $ wceq U (co (co C c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co R c2 cexp) cmul) cmin) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wcel R crp) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel Y cr))) (wi (wa (wceq (co (co X c2 cexp) (co Y c2 cexp) caddc) (co R c2 cexp)) (wceq (co (co A2 X cmul) (co B2 Y cmul) caddc) C)) (wceq (co (co Q (co Y c2 cexp) cmul) (co (co T Y cmul) U caddc) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ itsclc0 . Quadratic equation for the y-coordinate of the intersection points of a horizontal line and a circle. (Contributed by AV, 25-Feb-2023.)
theorem itschlc0yqe (A2 B2 C Q R T U X Y: class):
  $ wceq Q (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $ >
